window.NPLEX_UNITS = {"CV-1":{"id":"CV-1","summary":"How a single heart tube folds, loops, and divides into four chambers with valves and outflow vessels, and how fetal circulation bypasses the lungs and liver. Exam questions link each embryonic structure to its adult derivative and to the congenital defect that results when a step fails.","notes":[{"h":"Heart tube and looping","points":["Heart forms from splanchnic mesoderm (cardiogenic field) in week 3.","Paired endocardial tubes fuse into one heart tube as the embryo folds.","Heart beats spontaneously by about day 22 (week 4); first functional organ.","Looping begins in week 4 and sets left-right position of the chambers.","Defective left-right dynein (primary ciliary dyskinesia, Kartagener syndrome) can cause dextrocardia with situs inversus."]},{"h":"Atrial septation","points":["Septum primum grows toward the endocardial cushions; the gap below it is the ostium primum.","Ostium secundum opens by apoptosis in the upper septum primum.","Septum secundum grows on the right side of septum primum and leaves an opening, the foramen ovale.","Septum primum acts as a one-way flap valve over the foramen ovale.","After birth, left atrial pressure rises above right and presses the flap shut; the scar is the fossa ovalis.","Failure of the two septa to fuse leaves a patent foramen ovale (about 1 in 4 adults)."]},{"h":"Ventricles, AV canal, and outflow tract","points":["Dorsal and ventral endocardial cushions fuse to split the AV canal into right and left channels.","Muscular interventricular septum grows up from the floor of the ventricle.","Membranous septum closes the last gap using endocardial cushion and outflow ridge tissue; most VSDs are membranous.","Neural crest cells form truncal and bulbar ridges that spiral to make the aorticopulmonary septum.","Mitral and tricuspid valves come from fused AV canal endocardial cushions; aortic and pulmonary valves come from outflow tract endocardial cushions."]},{"h":"Fetal circulation","points":["Umbilical vein carries the most oxygenated fetal blood from the placenta.","Ductus venosus lets most umbilical blood bypass the liver into the IVC.","IVC blood is directed through the foramen ovale to the left atrium, then to the head and upper body.","SVC blood goes RA to RV to pulmonary trunk, then through the ductus arteriosus to the descending aorta.","High fetal pulmonary vascular resistance drives the ductus shunt.","Two umbilical arteries (from the internal iliac arteries) return deoxygenated blood to the placenta."]},{"h":"Changes at birth","points":["First breaths drop pulmonary vascular resistance, so more blood returns to the left atrium.","Higher left atrial pressure closes the foramen ovale.","Rising O2 and falling prostaglandin E2 (placenta removed) constrict the ductus arteriosus.","Clamping the cord stops flow in the umbilical vessels and ductus venosus."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Embryonic heart structures and adult derivatives","cols":["Embryonic structure","Adult derivative"],"rows":[["Truncus arteriosus","Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk"],["Bulbus cordis","Smooth outflow parts of both ventricles (RV conus arteriosus, LV aortic vestibule)"],["Primitive ventricle","Trabeculated parts of left and right ventricles"],["Primitive atrium","Trabeculated parts of both atria (auricles)"],["Left horn of sinus venosus","Coronary sinus"],["Right horn of sinus venosus","Smooth part of right atrium (sinus venarum)"],["Primitive pulmonary vein","Smooth part of left atrium"],["Right common and anterior cardinal veins","Superior vena cava"]]},{"title":"Fetal shunts and their adult remnants","cols":["Fetal structure","What it does before birth","Adult remnant"],"rows":[["Umbilical vein","Brings oxygenated blood from placenta","Ligamentum teres hepatis (round ligament of liver)"],["Ductus venosus","Shunts umbilical blood past the liver to the IVC","Ligamentum venosum"],["Foramen ovale","Shunts RA blood to LA","Fossa ovalis"],["Ductus arteriosus","Shunts pulmonary trunk blood to the aorta","Ligamentum arteriosum"],["Umbilical arteries","Return blood to the placenta","Medial umbilical ligaments"]]},{"title":"Aortic (pharyngeal) arch artery derivatives","cols":["Arch","Adult derivative"],"rows":[["1st","Part of the maxillary artery"],["2nd","Stapedial and hyoid arteries"],["3rd","Common carotid artery and proximal internal carotid artery"],["4th","Left: part of the aortic arch. Right: proximal right subclavian artery"],["5th","Regresses"],["6th","Proximal pulmonary arteries; left side also forms the ductus arteriosus"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"1st arch is MAXimal, Second = Stapedial, C is the 3rd letter, 4 limbs = systemic, 6th = pulmonary","text":"Arch 1: maxillary artery. Arch 2: stapedial artery. Arch 3: common carotid. Arch 4: systemic vessels (aortic arch, right subclavian). Arch 6: pulmonary arteries and the pulmonary-to-systemic shunt (ductus arteriosus)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 5 Ts of early cyanosis","text":"Truncus arteriosus, Transposition of the great arteries, Tricuspid atresia, Tetralogy of Fallot, TAPVR. The first, second, and fourth are outflow tract (neural crest) septation errors."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the oxygen","text":"Trace one red cell from the placenta: umbilical vein, ductus venosus, IVC, foramen ovale, LA, LV, aorta to the brain. Every shunt exists to send the best blood to the brain and skip the airless lungs and busy liver."}],"highYield":["Neural crest failure in the outflow tract gives persistent truncus arteriosus, transposition, or tetralogy of Fallot.","22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge) is linked to truncus arteriosus and tetralogy of Fallot.","Endocardial cushion defects (AV septal defect) are classic in Down syndrome.","Patent foramen ovale can let a venous clot cross to the systemic side (paradoxical embolism).","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the ligamentum arteriosum (6th arch) and aortic arch; the right hooks under the right subclavian artery (4th arch).","Most VSDs are in the membranous septum."],"pitfalls":["Ligamentum venosum comes from the ductus venosus; ligamentum arteriosum comes from the ductus arteriosus.","Medial umbilical ligaments are old umbilical arteries; the median umbilical ligament is the urachus (allantois), which is not a vessel.","Ostium secundum forms in septum primum; the foramen ovale is the hole left in septum secundum.","Failure to form the aorticopulmonary septum gives truncus arteriosus; failure of the septum to spiral gives transposition."],"dumps":[{"t":"Fetal circulation map","p":"Draw the fetal circulation from the placenta and back. Label the three shunts, mark where blood is most and least oxygenated, and write each shunt's adult remnant beside it.","l":["Umbilical vein carries the most oxygenated blood","Ductus venosus bypasses the liver to the IVC (ligamentum venosum)","Foramen ovale moves blood RA to LA (fossa ovalis)","Ductus arteriosus moves blood pulmonary trunk to aorta (ligamentum arteriosum)","High pulmonary vascular resistance explains why lungs are bypassed","Two umbilical arteries from internal iliacs return blood (medial umbilical ligaments)","Umbilical vein becomes ligamentum teres hepatis"]},{"t":"Atrial septum step by step","p":"Draw four panels showing how the atrial septum forms, from septum primum to the closed foramen ovale after birth. Label each opening and septum.","l":["Septum primum grows toward endocardial cushions","Ostium primum is the gap below septum primum, then closes","Ostium secundum forms by apoptosis in septum primum","Septum secundum grows to the right of septum primum","Foramen ovale is the opening in septum secundum","Septum primum serves as the flap valve","At birth LA pressure exceeds RA pressure and closes the flap (fossa ovalis)"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Fetal and Neonatal Physiology (section on circulatory readjustments at birth)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on congenital heart disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Embryology | Development of the Heart ❤️","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RjiPx6Xi-68"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Fetal Circulation | Embryology | Dr Najeeb","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYVGjbzmAtg"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Fetal circulation right before birth | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-IRkisEtzsk"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-18-G","CV-18-G2","CV-18-G3","CV-18-G4"]},"CV-2":{"id":"CV-2","summary":"The microscopic structure of the heart wall, cardiac muscle cells, and the different types of blood vessels. Questions ask you to match a wall feature to its function and to know which layer is affected in common vascular diseases.","notes":[{"h":"Layers of the heart wall","points":["Endocardium: endothelium plus thin connective tissue; the subendocardial layer holds Purkinje fibers, nerves, and small vessels.","Myocardium: thickest layer, made of cardiac muscle cells; thickest in the left ventricle.","Epicardium: visceral layer of serous pericardium (mesothelium) over fat that carries the coronary vessels.","Cardiac skeleton: dense connective tissue rings around the valves; electrically insulates atria from ventricles except at the AV bundle.","Valve leaflets: dense connective tissue core covered by endothelium on both sides."]},{"h":"Cardiac muscle cells","points":["Striated, branched cells with one or two central nuclei.","Intercalated discs join cells end to end: fascia adherens and desmosomes hold, gap junctions pass current.","Gap junctions let the heart contract as a functional syncytium.","T tubules sit at the Z lines and pair with one SR cistern (diads).","Very rich in mitochondria; the heart depends on aerobic metabolism.","Atrial muscle cells contain granules of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP).","Adult cardiac muscle barely regenerates; dead muscle is replaced by fibrous scar."]},{"h":"Purkinje fibers","points":["Larger and paler than working myocytes, with fewer myofibrils and lots of glycogen.","Lie in the subendocardium and conduct impulses fast."]},{"h":"Blood vessel wall","points":["Tunica intima: endothelium, basal lamina, subendothelial connective tissue; arteries add an internal elastic lamina.","Tunica media: smooth muscle and elastic tissue; thickest layer in arteries.","Tunica adventitia: collagen-rich connective tissue; thickest layer in large veins.","Vasa vasorum supply the outer wall of large vessels and are more plentiful in veins.","Limb veins have valves (folds of intima) that stop backflow."]},{"h":"Endothelial products","points":["Nitric oxide and prostacyclin relax smooth muscle and resist clotting.","Endothelin-1 is a strong vasoconstrictor.","Weibel-Palade bodies store von Willebrand factor and P-selectin."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Vessel types under the microscope","cols":["Vessel","Key wall features","Examples","Main job"],"rows":[["Elastic artery","Media packed with fenestrated elastic laminae","Aorta, pulmonary trunk, their large branches","Stretch in systole and recoil in diastole to keep flow steady"],["Muscular artery","Thick smooth muscle media, clear internal elastic lamina","Coronary, radial, femoral arteries","Distribute blood to organs"],["Arteriole","Thin media of only a few smooth muscle layers","Inside every organ","Main site of vascular resistance; control capillary inflow"],["Capillary","Endothelium and basal lamina, with pericytes","All tissues","Exchange between blood and tissue"],["Postcapillary venule","Endothelium and pericytes; high endothelial venules in lymph nodes","All tissues","Main site where leukocytes leave blood"],["Medium and large vein","Thin media, thick adventitia, valves in limb veins","Great saphenous vein, IVC","Low-pressure return; hold most of the blood volume"]]},{"title":"Capillary types","cols":["Type","Structure","Where found"],"rows":[["Continuous","Unbroken endothelium and basal lamina, tight junctions","Muscle, lung, connective tissue, nervous tissue (blood-brain barrier)"],["Fenestrated","Endothelium with small pores","Kidney glomeruli, intestinal villi, endocrine glands"],["Sinusoidal (discontinuous)","Wide, irregular lumen with gaps in endothelium and basal lamina","Liver, spleen, bone marrow"]]},{"title":"Three muscle types compared","cols":["Feature","Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth"],"rows":[["Striations","Yes","Yes","No"],["Nuclei","Many, peripheral","One or two, central","One, central"],["Cell-to-cell links","No gap junctions between fibers","Intercalated discs with gap junctions","Gap junctions in single-unit smooth muscle"],["T tubule arrangement","Triads at A-I junctions","Diads at Z lines","No T tubules (caveolae instead)"],["Ca2+ binds to","Troponin C","Troponin C","Calmodulin"],["Regeneration","Limited, through satellite cells","Very little; heals by scar","Good; cells can divide"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Arteries flex, veins hold","text":"Arteries face high pressure, so their media is thick. Veins hold volume at low pressure, so their adventitia is the thickest layer and the lumen is wide."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Leakiness matches the job","text":"Where cells and large proteins must pass (liver, spleen, marrow) capillaries are sinusoidal. Where filtration or hormone release happens (kidney, gut, endocrine) they are fenestrated. Everywhere else they are continuous."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Disc parts by direction","text":"In an intercalated disc, the parts that run across the cell end (fascia adherens, desmosomes) hold cells together; the parts along the side (gap junctions) carry the electrical signal."}],"highYield":["Gap junctions in intercalated discs make the myocardium a functional syncytium.","Infarcted myocardium heals by fibrous scar because cardiac muscle cells barely divide.","Arterioles set most of the systemic vascular resistance.","Postcapillary venules are where neutrophils leave the blood in acute inflammation.","Continuous capillaries with tight junctions form the blood-brain barrier.","Atherosclerosis starts in the intima; Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis affects the media of muscular arteries."],"pitfalls":["Cardiac T tubules sit at Z lines (diads); skeletal T tubules sit at A-I junctions (triads).","Purkinje fibers are larger and paler than working myocytes; their wide diameter helps them conduct fast.","The epicardium is the same thing as the visceral serous pericardium.","In arteries the media is the thickest layer; in large veins the adventitia is."],"dumps":[{"t":"Artery versus vein","p":"Draw a cross section of a muscular artery next to its companion vein. Label all three tunics in each and add the elastic laminae and a valve.","l":["Intima with endothelium in both","Internal elastic lamina prominent in the artery","Thick smooth muscle media in the artery","Thin media in the vein","Thick adventitia in the vein","Wider, often collapsed lumen in the vein","Valve drawn as a fold of intima in the vein"]},{"t":"Cardiac muscle close up","p":"Draw two cardiac muscle cells joined end to end. Label the nucleus, intercalated disc parts, T tubule, SR, and mitochondria, and write what each part does.","l":["Branched cells with one or two central nuclei","Fascia adherens anchors actin at the cell end","Desmosomes resist pulling apart","Gap junctions on the lateral part pass ions between cells","T tubule at the Z line forming a diad with SR","Many mitochondria between myofibrils","Striations from sarcomeres"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Circulatory System"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue (section on cardiac muscle)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Histology of Cardiovascular System 🧑🏻‍⚕️","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UiMHnNHmgHM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Structures and Layers of the Heart","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dgAbpwp9gF8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Layers of the heart | Human anatomy and physiology | Health & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bm65xCS5ivo"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Arteries vs. veins-what's the difference? | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7b6LRebCgb4"}],"related":["CV-5","CV-11","CV-18-I3"]},"CV-3":{"id":"CV-3","summary":"Where the heart sits in the chest, which chambers form each surface and border, how the great vessels connect, and how the pericardium wraps it all. Exam questions use these relations to explain stab wounds, enlarged chambers, referred pain, and tamponade.","notes":[{"h":"Position","points":["Heart lies in the middle mediastinum inside the pericardium, about two thirds left of midline.","Apex sits behind the left 5th intercostal space at about the midclavicular line.","The plane through the sternal angle and the T4/T5 disc separates superior from inferior mediastinum.","Right ventricle is the most anterior chamber, right behind the sternum.","Left atrium is the most posterior chamber, right in front of the esophagus."]},{"h":"Pericardium","points":["Fibrous pericardium: tough outer sac fused to the central tendon of the diaphragm and to the adventitia of the great vessels.","Serous pericardium has a parietal layer (lines the fibrous sac) and a visceral layer (epicardium).","Pericardial cavity holds a thin film of serous fluid between the two serous layers.","Phrenic nerves (C3 to C5) carry pain from the fibrous and parietal layers; pain refers to the shoulder and supraclavicular skin.","Main blood supply is the pericardiacophrenic artery from the internal thoracic artery.","Transverse pericardial sinus lies behind the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk, in front of the SVC.","Oblique pericardial sinus is a blind pouch behind the left atrium, bounded by the pulmonary veins and IVC."]},{"h":"Great vessels","points":["SVC (formed by the two brachiocephalic veins) and IVC enter the right atrium; the azygos vein drains into the SVC.","Pulmonary trunk leaves the RV and divides into right and left pulmonary arteries under the aortic arch.","Usually four pulmonary veins enter the left atrium.","The coronary arteries are the only branches of the ascending aorta.","Aortic arch branches, right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian.","Ligamentum arteriosum links the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the arch; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the arch next to it."]},{"h":"Inside the chambers","points":["RA: crista terminalis separates smooth sinus venarum from pectinate muscle; fossa ovalis on the septum; coronary sinus opening.","RV: trabeculae carneae, three papillary muscles, smooth conus arteriosus leading to the pulmonary valve.","Moderator band (septomarginal trabecula) carries part of the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle.","LV wall is about two to three times thicker than the RV wall; two papillary muscles (anterior and posterior)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Heart chambers at a glance","cols":["Chamber","Receives blood from","Sends blood to","Key internal landmarks"],"rows":[["Right atrium","SVC, IVC, coronary sinus","RV through tricuspid valve","Crista terminalis, pectinate muscles, fossa ovalis, SA node near SVC opening"],["Right ventricle","Right atrium","Pulmonary trunk through pulmonary valve","Trabeculae carneae, moderator band, three papillary muscles, conus arteriosus"],["Left atrium","Pulmonary veins (usually four)","LV through mitral valve","Mostly smooth wall; pectinate muscles only in left auricle"],["Left ventricle","Left atrium","Ascending aorta through aortic valve","Thickest wall, two papillary muscles, smooth aortic vestibule"]]},{"title":"Which chamber forms each surface and border","cols":["Surface or border","Formed mainly by"],"rows":[["Anterior (sternocostal) surface","Right ventricle"],["Diaphragmatic (inferior) surface","Left ventricle, partly right ventricle"],["Base (posterior surface)","Left atrium, small part of right atrium"],["Apex","Left ventricle"],["Right border","Right atrium"],["Left border","Left ventricle, small part of left auricle"],["Inferior border","Right ventricle, small part of left ventricle"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive","text":"Phrenic nerve roots are C3 to C5. The same nerve carries pericardial pain, which is why pericarditis can be felt at the shoulder."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Front to back: RV first, LA last","text":"A knife through the sternum hits the RV. An enlarged LA pushes back on the esophagus (trouble swallowing) and can stretch the left recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Transverse sinus = finger behind the arteries","text":"Slide a finger behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk and in front of the SVC. Surgeons pass a clamp or ligature through this sinus to stop arterial outflow during bypass."}],"highYield":["Left atrial enlargement (for example from mitral stenosis) can cause dysphagia and hoarseness.","Cardiac tamponade: fluid in the pericardial sac compresses the heart; Beck triad is hypotension, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds.","Pericardial pain refers to the shoulder because of the phrenic nerve.","Phrenic nerve runs anterior to the lung root on the pericardium; vagus runs posterior to the lung root.","The fibrous pericardium does not stretch quickly, so even a small fast bleed can cause tamponade.","A stab wound just left of the sternum most likely enters the right ventricle."],"pitfalls":["The base of the heart is its posterior surface (mostly LA), and the apex points anteroinferiorly to the left.","Epicardium equals visceral serous pericardium; it is part of the heart wall.","Transverse sinus is behind the great arteries; oblique sinus is behind the left atrium.","The right border on a chest X-ray is the right atrium, while the left border is mostly the left ventricle."],"dumps":[{"t":"Anterior view of the heart","p":"Draw the heart as seen from the front. Label each chamber, the borders, the apex, and the great vessels leaving the top.","l":["RV forms most of the anterior surface","RA forms the right border","LV forms the left border and apex","Inferior border mostly RV","SVC entering RA from above","Pulmonary trunk from RV","Ascending aorta and arch with its three branches"]},{"t":"Pericardium and its sinuses","p":"Draw a cross section through the heart and pericardium. Label the fibrous and serous layers, the cavity, and both pericardial sinuses, then add the nerve and artery that supply the pericardium.","l":["Fibrous pericardium outermost, fused to central tendon of diaphragm","Parietal serous layer lines the fibrous sac","Visceral serous layer is the epicardium","Pericardial cavity with thin fluid film","Transverse sinus behind aorta and pulmonary trunk, in front of SVC","Oblique sinus behind LA, bounded by pulmonary veins and IVC","Phrenic nerve (C3 to C5) carries pain","Pericardiacophrenic artery supplies it"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, sections on the pericardium and the heart"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the superior mediastinum and great vessels"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Anatomy of the Heart | Heart Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jU9w6w8LwqM"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Introduction to Cardiac (Heart) Anatomy and the Chest Xray","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HYr2NiOvjZE"}],"related":["CV-4","CV-8","CV-18-F","CV-18-F2"]},"CV-4":{"id":"CV-4","summary":"Where the four heart valves sit, how pressure differences open and close them, and how the valve events line up with pressures, volumes, heart sounds, and the ECG in one cardiac cycle. This is the foundation for every murmur and valve disease question.","notes":[{"h":"Valve anatomy","points":["Tricuspid valve (RA to RV): anterior, posterior, and septal cusps.","Mitral valve (LA to LV): anterior and posterior cusps.","Chordae tendineae tie AV cusps to papillary muscles; papillary muscles tighten in systole so cusps do not flip into the atria.","Pulmonary valve: anterior, right, and left cusps.","Aortic valve: right, left, and posterior cusps; right and left aortic sinuses give rise to the coronary arteries.","All four valves open and close passively, driven by pressure differences."]},{"h":"Where to listen","points":["Aortic area: right 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border.","Pulmonic area: left 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border.","Tricuspid area: left lower sternal border.","Mitral area: apex, left 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line."]},{"h":"Pressures and volumes","points":["Aortic valve opens when LV pressure rises above aortic pressure (about 80 mmHg).","Normal aortic pressure about 120/80 mmHg; pulmonary artery about 25/8 mmHg.","End-diastolic volume about 110 to 120 mL, stroke volume about 70 mL, end-systolic volume about 40 to 50 mL.","Ejection fraction = SV / EDV, normally about 55 to 70%.","Most ventricular filling is passive; atrial contraction adds about the last 20%.","Dicrotic notch (incisura) on the aortic pressure curve marks aortic valve closure."]},{"h":"Jugular venous pulse waves","points":["a wave: right atrial contraction.","c wave: RV contraction bulges the closed tricuspid valve into the RA.","x descent: atrial relaxation and downward pull of the tricuspid ring during ejection.","v wave: RA fills against the closed tricuspid valve.","y descent: RA empties into the RV after the tricuspid valve opens."]},{"h":"Splitting of S2","points":["Inspiration lowers chest pressure, raises venous return to the right heart, lengthens RV ejection, and delays P2.","Normal (physiologic) split widens on inspiration."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Phases of the cardiac cycle (left heart)","cols":["Phase","Valve status","LV volume","Markers"],"rows":[["Atrial contraction","Mitral open, aortic closed","Rises to EDV","Follows the P wave; a wave; S4 if the ventricle is stiff"],["Isovolumetric contraction","All valves closed","Stays at EDV","Starts just after QRS onset with S1; highest myocardial O2 use"],["Ejection","Mitral closed, aortic open","Falls to ESV","During the ST segment and T wave"],["Isovolumetric relaxation","All valves closed","Stays at ESV","Starts with S2 (aortic closure); dicrotic notch"],["Rapid filling","Mitral open, aortic closed","Rises quickly","S3 if present; y descent"],["Reduced filling (diastasis)","Mitral open, aortic closed","Rises slowly","Flat stretch before the next P wave"]]},{"title":"Heart sounds","cols":["Sound","Cause","Timing","What it means"],"rows":[["S1","Mitral and tricuspid closure","Start of systole","Normal; loudest at the apex (mitral area)"],["S2","Aortic (A2) then pulmonic (P2) closure","End of ejection","Normal; loudest at left upper sternal border; splits on inspiration"],["S3","Rapid early filling into a dilated or volume-loaded ventricle","Early diastole, just after S2","Normal in children and pregnancy; in adults over about 40 suggests heart failure or volume overload"],["S4","Atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle","Late diastole, just before S1","Stiff or hypertrophied ventricle; absent in atrial fibrillation"]]},{"title":"Abnormal S2 splitting","cols":["Pattern","What happens","Classic causes"],"rows":[["Wide split","P2 is delayed more than normal; split still widens on inspiration","Pulmonic stenosis, right bundle branch block"],["Fixed split","Split stays the same through the breathing cycle","Atrial septal defect"],["Paradoxical split","A2 is delayed past P2; split narrows on inspiration and widens on expiration","Aortic stenosis, left bundle branch block"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"All Physicians Take Money","text":"Listening areas in order from the right upper chest to the apex: Aortic, Pulmonic, Tricuspid, Mitral."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Kentucky and Tennessee","text":"S3 follows S2 with the rhythm of Kentucky (S1, S2, S3). S4 comes before S1 with the rhythm of Tennessee (S4, S1, S2)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Valves are doors pushed by pressure","text":"A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it, and shuts when that reverses. Find which pressure line is on top in a Wiggers diagram and you know the valve status."}],"highYield":["Isovolumetric phases are the only times all four valves are closed.","Loss of the atrial kick in atrial fibrillation removes S4 and lowers filling in a stiff ventricle.","Fixed splitting of S2 points to an atrial septal defect.","Paradoxical splitting points to delayed LV emptying, such as aortic stenosis or left bundle branch block.","The c wave is the closed tricuspid valve bulging into the RA as the RV contracts; the x descent is atrial relaxation plus the tricuspid ring pulled down during ejection.","Papillary muscle rupture after MI lets mitral cusps prolapse and causes acute mitral regurgitation."],"pitfalls":["S1 marks the start of isovolumetric contraction, which comes before ejection; the aortic valve opens later.","Papillary muscles do not pull the AV valves open; they hold the cusps shut during systole.","S3 can be normal in a child or pregnant patient; the same sound in an older adult points to disease.","Wide split still varies with breathing; fixed split does not."],"dumps":[{"t":"Wiggers diagram","p":"Draw one cardiac cycle with aortic, LV, and LA pressure curves, the LV volume curve, the ECG, and heart sounds on a shared time axis. Mark when each valve opens and closes.","l":["Mitral closes where LV pressure crosses above LA pressure (S1)","Isovolumetric contraction with volume flat at EDV","Aortic valve opens where LV pressure crosses aortic pressure (about 80 mmHg)","Volume falls to ESV during ejection","Aortic closure at the dicrotic notch (S2)","Isovolumetric relaxation with volume flat at ESV","Mitral opens where LV pressure drops below LA pressure, then rapid filling","P wave before atrial kick; QRS before isovolumetric contraction; T wave near end of ejection"]},{"t":"Listening map","p":"Draw the front of the chest and mark the four auscultation areas. Next to each, write which valve it represents and which heart sound is loudest there.","l":["Aortic area at right 2nd intercostal space, sternal border","Pulmonic area at left 2nd intercostal space, sternal border","Tricuspid area at left lower sternal border","Mitral area at apex, left 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line","S1 loudest at the apex","S2 loudest at the left upper sternal border"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the heart valves and areas of auscultation"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Cardiac Cycle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xamYVlNF5Zo"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Lub Dub | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-4kGMI-qQ3I"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Cardiac Cycle | Systole & Diastole | Dr Najeeb Part 1/8","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XbivIaFPoQI"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Mastering Second Heart Sound | S2 Splitting🩺","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IdRb3OjFAtQ"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-6","CV-18-D","CV-18-D1","CV-18-D2"]},"CV-5":{"id":"CV-5","summary":"How an action potential in a ventricular muscle cell triggers calcium release and contraction, and how calcium handling sets the strength and speed of each beat. Expect questions on ion channels by phase, calcium-induced calcium release, and how sympathetic input raises contractility.","notes":[{"h":"Ventricular action potential","points":["Resting potential is about -90 mV, held by inward rectifier K+ channels.","Upstroke comes from fast voltage-gated Na+ channels.","The plateau comes from L-type Ca2+ influx balanced by K+ efflux.","The action potential lasts about 0.2 to 0.3 s, far longer than in skeletal muscle.","The long refractory period lasts almost as long as contraction, so the heart cannot be tetanized."]},{"h":"Excitation-contraction coupling","points":["The action potential spreads into T tubules and opens L-type Ca2+ channels (dihydropyridine receptors).","Entering Ca2+ opens ryanodine receptors (RyR2) on the SR: calcium-induced calcium release.","Ca2+ binds troponin C, tropomyosin moves, and myosin-binding sites on actin are exposed.","Cross-bridges cycle using ATP until Ca2+ falls.","Cardiac muscle needs extracellular Ca2+ to contract; skeletal muscle does not."]},{"h":"Relaxation","points":["SERCA pumps most Ca2+ back into the SR.","Phospholamban brakes SERCA; when PKA phosphorylates phospholamban, the brake comes off.","The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (3 Na+ in for 1 Ca2+ out) removes Ca2+ from the cell.","A sarcolemmal Ca2+-ATPase removes a smaller share.","Relaxation uses ATP, so ischemia impairs relaxation as well as contraction."]},{"h":"Controlling force","points":["Every cell contracts with every beat, so force is graded by Ca2+ and sarcomere length, with no motor unit recruitment.","Sympathetic β1 stimulation raises cAMP and activates PKA.","PKA phosphorylates L-type Ca2+ channels (more Ca2+ in), phospholamban (faster SR uptake, bigger SR store), and troponin I (faster relaxation).","Stretch (Frank-Starling) improves filament overlap and raises troponin C sensitivity to Ca2+.","Blocking the Na+/K+-ATPase raises cell Na+, slows Ca2+ removal by the exchanger, and strengthens contraction (cardiac glycoside mechanism)."]},{"h":"Energy","points":["Heart muscle depends almost entirely on aerobic metabolism and burns mostly fatty acids at rest.","It extracts a large share of delivered O2 even at rest, so more demand must be met by more coronary flow."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Ventricular myocyte action potential phases","cols":["Phase","What happens","Main current"],"rows":[["0","Rapid depolarization","Na+ in through fast voltage-gated Na+ channels"],["1","Brief early repolarization","Na+ channels inactivate; transient outward K+ current"],["2","Plateau","Ca2+ in through L-type channels balanced by K+ out"],["3","Rapid repolarization","L-type Ca2+ channels close; delayed rectifier K+ out"],["4","Resting potential (about -90 mV)","Inward rectifier K+ channels; Na+/K+-ATPase keeps gradients"]]},{"title":"Skeletal versus cardiac muscle coupling","cols":["Feature","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"rows":[["What starts contraction","Motor neuron releases ACh at the neuromuscular junction","Action potential spreads from neighbor cells through gap junctions"],["DHPR to RyR link","Mechanical: DHPR directly pulls RyR1 open","Ca2+ entering through L-type channels opens RyR2"],["Needs extracellular Ca2+","No","Yes"],["Action potential length","A few milliseconds","About 200 to 300 ms"],["Tetanus possible","Yes","No (long refractory period)"],["How force is graded","Recruiting motor units and firing rate","Amount of Ca2+ released and sarcomere length"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Phase story: Na, K, Ca, K, rest","text":"Phase 0 Na+ rushes in, phase 1 K+ starts out, phase 2 Ca2+ holds the plateau, phase 3 K+ pulls it down, phase 4 rests."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Phospholamban is a brake","text":"Unphosphorylated phospholamban slows SERCA. PKA phosphorylation releases the brake, so Ca2+ is pumped back faster: quicker relaxation and a bigger store for the next beat."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Calcium is the volume knob","text":"Anything that raises cytosolic Ca2+ during systole (β1 stimulation, slower Ca2+ removal) turns up force. Anything that lowers it turns force down."}],"highYield":["Calcium-induced calcium release through RyR2 is the core of cardiac excitation-contraction coupling.","The plateau (phase 2) depends on L-type Ca2+ channels.","A refractory period nearly as long as contraction prevents tetany and lets the heart refill.","β1 stimulation raises contractility and speeds relaxation through PKA phosphorylation of L-type channels, phospholamban, and troponin I.","High extracellular K+ makes the resting potential less negative, inactivates some fast Na+ channels, and slows conduction.","Inhibiting the Na+/K+-ATPase raises intracellular Ca2+ through reduced Na+/Ca2+ exchange."],"pitfalls":["Ventricular myocytes use Na+ for the upstroke; SA and AV nodal cells use Ca2+ (covered in CV-7).","In skeletal muscle the DHPR is linked mechanically to RyR1; in cardiac muscle Ca2+ entry is the trigger.","Phosphorylating phospholamban speeds SERCA; it does not block it.","Calcium binds troponin C in both striated muscles; calmodulin is the smooth muscle partner."],"dumps":[{"t":"One beat inside a myocyte","p":"Draw a cardiac muscle cell with its T tubule and SR. Trace Ca2+ from the moment the action potential arrives to full relaxation, numbering each step.","l":["Action potential travels down the T tubule","L-type Ca2+ channels open and Ca2+ enters","Ca2+ opens RyR2 and the SR releases more Ca2+","Ca2+ binds troponin C and tropomyosin shifts","Cross-bridge cycling shortens the sarcomere","SERCA pumps Ca2+ back into the SR (phospholamban regulates it)","Na+/Ca2+ exchanger moves Ca2+ out of the cell"]},{"t":"Action potential with labels","p":"Draw a ventricular myocyte action potential. Label phases 0 to 4, the voltage values, the ion moving in each phase, and the refractory period.","l":["Resting about -90 mV in phase 4","Steep phase 0 from fast Na+ influx","Small notch in phase 1 from transient K+ efflux","Long plateau in phase 2 from L-type Ca2+ in and K+ out","Phase 3 repolarization from delayed rectifier K+","Effective refractory period spans most of the action potential"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue (section on cardiac muscle)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Action potentials in cardiac myocytes | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rIVCuC-Etc0"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Calcium puts myosin to work | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vv6WBeqw2Nc"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Heart Physiology I (Cardiac Myocyte and Membrane Potential)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FlyKXrY5ak0"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Heart Physiology II (Muscle contraction and Pacemaker activity)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o7L0W3Xb5Eg"}],"related":["CV-2","CV-6","CV-7","CV-18-B"]},"CV-6":{"id":"CV-6","summary":"What sets cardiac output from beat to beat: heart rate plus the three stroke volume controls (preload, afterload, contractility), and the reflexes that adjust them. Exam questions ask you to predict how a change shifts stroke volume, ejection fraction, or a pressure-volume loop.","notes":[{"h":"Core equations","points":["Cardiac output (CO) = stroke volume (SV) x heart rate (HR); about 5 L/min in a resting adult.","Ejection fraction (EF) = SV / EDV.","Mean arterial pressure (MAP) = CO x total peripheral resistance.","MAP is about diastolic pressure plus one third of pulse pressure at resting heart rates.","Fick principle: CO = O2 consumption / (arterial O2 content minus venous O2 content).","Wall stress (Laplace) = pressure x radius / (2 x wall thickness)."]},{"h":"Preload","points":["Preload is ventricular stretch at end of diastole, best judged by end-diastolic volume.","Set mainly by venous return.","Frank-Starling: more filling gives a stronger beat and larger SV, so the heart pumps out what comes in.","Venous return rises with mean systemic filling pressure (about 7 mmHg) and falls as right atrial pressure rises."]},{"h":"Afterload","points":["Afterload is the wall stress the ventricle must overcome to eject.","For the LV it mostly tracks aortic pressure; it rises in hypertension and in aortic stenosis.","Higher afterload raises ESV and lowers SV.","Concentric hypertrophy thickens the wall and lowers wall stress back toward normal."]},{"h":"Contractility and heart rate","points":["Contractility is force at a given preload and afterload; it rises with sympathetic β1 input and falls with ischemia, acidosis, and hypoxia.","Intrinsic SA node rate after autonomic blockade is about 100 bpm, so resting rate shows vagal tone.","Fast heart rates shorten diastole much more than systole, which cuts filling time and LV coronary flow.","Myocardial O2 demand rises with heart rate, contractility, and wall stress."]},{"h":"Reflex control","points":["Carotid sinus baroreceptors signal through CN IX and respond to rises and falls in pressure.","Aortic arch baroreceptors signal through CN X, mainly to rises in pressure.","Both feed the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla.","Falling pressure lowers baroreceptor firing, so sympathetic output rises (faster, stronger beats, vasoconstriction) and vagal output falls.","Atrial stretch speeds heart rate (Bainbridge reflex)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"What each change does to the left ventricle","cols":["Change","EDV","ESV","Stroke volume","Pressure-volume loop"],"rows":[["Higher preload","Up","About the same","Up","Wider, shifts right"],["Higher afterload","About the same","Up","Down","Taller and narrower"],["Higher contractility","About the same","Down","Up","Wider, shifts left; steeper end-systolic line"]]},{"title":"Autonomic effects on the heart","cols":["Effect","Sympathetic (β1)","Parasympathetic (vagus, M2)"],"rows":[["SA node rate (chronotropy)","Faster: steeper phase 4","Slower: hyperpolarized cell, flatter phase 4"],["AV node conduction (dromotropy)","Faster; shorter PR interval","Slower; longer PR interval"],["Atrial contractility","Up","Down"],["Ventricular contractility (inotropy)","Up strongly","Small effect (vagal fibers go mainly to atria and nodes)"],["Relaxation (lusitropy)","Faster","Little direct effect"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Preload is volume, afterload is pressure","text":"Preload asks how full the ventricle is before it squeezes. Afterload asks how hard it must push to get blood out."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Read the loop by its corners","text":"The lower right corner is EDV (preload). The upper left corner is ESV and aortic pressure (afterload). The loop width is SV. Move one corner and see what happens to the width."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Baroreceptor direction","text":"Baroreceptors fire more when stretched. Low pressure means less firing, and the brain answers with more sympathetic and less vagal output."}],"highYield":["SV depends on preload, afterload, and contractility; CO = SV x HR.","Normal EF is about 55 to 70%; it falls when contractility falls.","Raising afterload raises ESV and lowers SV.","Tachycardia shortens diastole and reduces LV coronary perfusion.","Carotid sinus uses CN IX; aortic arch uses CN X.","Concentric LV hypertrophy is the adaptation that lowers wall stress in pressure overload."],"pitfalls":["Increasing preload raises SV with little change in ESV; increasing contractility raises SV by lowering ESV.","Contractility is independent of preload; a stronger beat from more filling is the Frank-Starling effect.","The vagus has little direct effect on ventricular force even though it slows the heart a lot.","MAP equals diastolic plus one third of pulse pressure only at normal resting rates."],"dumps":[{"t":"Three pressure-volume loops","p":"Draw a normal LV pressure-volume loop. Then overlay separate loops for higher preload, higher afterload, and higher contractility, and label the EDV, ESV, and SV changes.","l":["Normal loop with four labeled corners (mitral close, aortic open, aortic close, mitral open)","Width of the loop is SV","Higher preload loop extends right with larger SV","Higher afterload loop is taller with larger ESV and smaller SV","Higher contractility loop extends left with smaller ESV","End-systolic pressure-volume line steeper with higher contractility"]},{"t":"Baroreceptor reflex","p":"Trace the baroreceptor reflex after a sudden drop in blood pressure, from receptor to heart and vessels. Label each nerve and the brainstem center.","l":["Stretch receptors in carotid sinus and aortic arch","Less stretch means less firing","Carotid sinus afferents in CN IX","Aortic arch afferents in CN X","Nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla","Sympathetic output up: faster HR, more contractility, vasoconstriction","Vagal output down","Result: CO and TPR rise and MAP returns toward normal"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Output, Venous Return, and Their Regulation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Nervous Regulation of the Circulation and Rapid Control of Arterial Pressure"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves (section on regulation of heart pumping)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Cardiac Output","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0O3FfHPE9PU"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiovascular System Physiology - Cardiac Output (stroke volume, heart rate, preload and afterload)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hpQFToprlH8"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Cardiac Cycle | Cardiology | Systole & Diastole | Cardiovascular🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVvQaqFOJpY"}],"related":["CV-4","CV-5","CV-11","CV-12","CV-18-B"]},"CV-7":{"id":"CV-7","summary":"Where the heart's conduction system lies, how pacemaker cells fire on their own, how the autonomic nervous system speeds or slows them, and how the ECG records the result. Questions ask you to link a wave or interval to a cardiac event and a lead group to the wall and artery it views.","notes":[{"h":"Conduction pathway","points":["SA node: right atrial wall near the SVC opening, at the top of the crista terminalis.","AV node: lower interatrial septum near the opening of the coronary sinus.","AV bundle (His) crosses the fibrous skeleton, the only normal electrical link from atria to ventricles.","Bundle splits into right and left bundle branches; the left has anterior and posterior fascicles.","Right bundle branch reaches the anterior papillary muscle through the moderator band.","Purkinje fibers spread the impulse through the subendocardium."]},{"h":"Blood supply of the nodes","points":["SA nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 60% of people.","AV nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 80% of people (right-dominant hearts)."]},{"h":"Pacemaker action potential","points":["No true resting potential; lowest point about -60 mV.","Phase 4: slow climb from the funny current (If, Na+ in through HCN channels), then T-type Ca2+.","Phase 0: slow upstroke from L-type Ca2+ channels once threshold (about -40 mV) is reached.","Phase 3: K+ efflux repolarizes. There is no phase 1 or 2.","The fastest pacemaker (normally the SA node) suppresses slower ones (overdrive suppression)."]},{"h":"Autonomic control","points":["Norepinephrine on β1 receptors raises cAMP, increases If and Ca2+ currents, and steepens phase 4.","Acetylcholine on M2 receptors lowers cAMP and opens K+ channels, hyperpolarizes the cell, and flattens phase 4.","AV node delay (about 0.1 s) lets the atria finish filling the ventricles; sympathetic input shortens it and vagal input lengthens it."]},{"h":"Reading the ECG","points":["Standard paper at 25 mm/s: small box 0.04 s, large box 0.20 s; 10 mm = 1 mV.","Heart rate is about 300 divided by the number of large boxes between R waves.","Depolarization moving toward a positive electrode gives an upward deflection.","Septum depolarizes first (left to right), then apex, then free walls from endocardium to epicardium; the base is last.","Repolarization runs epicardium to endocardium, so the T wave normally points the same way as the QRS.","Normal QRS axis is about -30° to +90°."]},{"h":"AV blocks","points":["First degree: PR over 0.20 s, every P wave conducts.","Second degree Mobitz I (Wenckebach): PR lengthens beat by beat until a QRS is dropped; usually AV node.","Second degree Mobitz II: QRS dropped without PR lengthening; usually below the AV node and can progress.","Third degree: P waves and QRS complexes beat independently; an escape rhythm drives the ventricles."]}],"charts":[{"title":"ECG waves and intervals","cols":["Component","What it shows","Normal value"],"rows":[["P wave","Atrial depolarization","Under 0.12 s"],["PR interval","Atrial depolarization plus AV nodal delay","0.12 to 0.20 s"],["QRS complex","Ventricular depolarization (hides atrial repolarization)","Under 0.12 s (usually about 0.06 to 0.10 s)"],["ST segment","Ventricles fully depolarized (plateau phase)","Flat at baseline"],["T wave","Ventricular repolarization","Same direction as QRS in most leads"],["QT interval","Ventricular depolarization plus repolarization","Shortens as heart rate rises; read as rate-corrected QTc"],["U wave","Small wave after T","Prominent in hypokalemia"]]},{"title":"Pacemakers and conduction speed","cols":["Site","Intrinsic rate","Conduction speed"],"rows":[["SA node","About 60 to 100 bpm","Slow (nodal tissue)"],["Atrial muscle","None normally","About 0.3 m/s"],["AV node","About 40 to 60 bpm (junctional escape)","Slowest, gives about 0.1 s delay"],["Purkinje fibers","About 20 to 40 bpm (ventricular escape)","Fastest, about 1.5 to 4 m/s"],["Ventricular muscle","None normally","About 0.3 to 0.5 m/s"]]},{"title":"Limb and augmented lead angles","cols":["Lead","Electrodes","Angle"],"rows":[["I","Right arm (-) to left arm (+)","0°"],["II","Right arm (-) to left leg (+)","+60°"],["III","Left arm (-) to left leg (+)","+120°"],["aVR","Augmented, right arm positive","-150°"],["aVL","Augmented, left arm positive","-30°"],["aVF","Augmented, left leg positive","+90°"]]},{"title":"Lead groups, wall, and usual artery","cols":["Wall","Leads","Usual artery"],"rows":[["Septal","V1, V2","LAD"],["Anterior","V3, V4","LAD"],["Lateral","I, aVL, V5, V6","Left circumflex (V5 and V6 may be LAD)"],["Inferior","II, III, aVF","RCA in most people"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Park AT VENtura AVenue","text":"Conduction speed from fastest to slowest: Purkinje, Atria, Ventricles, AV node."},{"kind":"trick","label":"300, 150, 100, 75, 60, 50","text":"Find an R wave on a thick line. Count the next thick lines as 300, 150, 100, 75, 60, 50. Where the next R wave lands is the approximate heart rate."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Leads II, III, aVF look at the floor","text":"All three have their positive pole on the left leg, so they view the inferior wall, which usually gets its blood from the RCA."}],"highYield":["The AV node is the slowest part of the conduction system; that delay lets the atria finish ventricular filling.","SA nodal artery from the RCA in about 60% and AV nodal artery from the RCA in about 80%, so inferior MI often causes bradycardia or AV block.","Phase 0 in nodal cells is Ca2+ driven, so Ca2+ channel blockade slows AV conduction.","Einthoven's law: lead II = lead I + lead III.","Lyme carditis classically causes third-degree AV block.","Mobitz II is more dangerous than Mobitz I because the block is usually below the AV node.","Prominent U waves suggest hypokalemia."],"pitfalls":["The PR interval is measured from the start of P to the start of QRS; the PR segment is only from the end of P.","Atrial repolarization has no visible wave because it is hidden in the QRS.","The T wave is upright even though repolarization is the opposite of depolarization, because it travels in the opposite direction (epicardium to endocardium).","Mobitz I has progressive PR lengthening; Mobitz II has a constant PR before the dropped beat."],"dumps":[{"t":"Conduction system and pacemaker potential","p":"Draw the heart's conduction system in an opened heart, then beside it draw a pacemaker cell action potential. Label each structure and each ion current.","l":["SA node near SVC at the top of the crista terminalis","AV node near coronary sinus opening in the interatrial septum","Bundle of His through the fibrous skeleton","Right bundle branch through moderator band; left bundle with two fascicles","Purkinje fibers in subendocardium","Pacemaker phase 4 slow rise from If (HCN) then T-type Ca2+","Phase 0 slow upstroke from L-type Ca2+","Phase 3 K+ efflux; no plateau"]},{"t":"Label one ECG beat","p":"Draw one normal ECG beat on grid paper at standard speed. Label each wave, segment, and interval with its normal duration and the cardiac event it represents.","l":["Small box 0.04 s and large box 0.20 s","P wave is atrial depolarization","PR interval 0.12 to 0.20 s includes AV nodal delay","QRS under 0.12 s is ventricular depolarization","Atrial repolarization hidden in QRS","ST segment is the plateau, normally flat","T wave is ventricular repolarization","QT interval spans depolarization plus repolarization"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Rhythmical Excitation of the Heart"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Fundamentals of Electrocardiography"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the conducting system of the heart"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Electrophysiology | Intrinsic Cardiac Conduction System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1kX6Tp8CWFw"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Electrophysiology | Extrinsic Cardiac Conduction System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2L4FbEEsy1s"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Electrical system of the heart | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7K2icszdxQc"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Relationship of conduction system, ventricular contraction and ECG","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eOX_NmYLKOE"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"EKG or ECG Interpretation | Part 1/20","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ku96IY27CE0"}],"related":["CV-5","CV-6","CV-8","CV-18-C3","CV-18-K3"]},"CV-8":{"id":"CV-8","summary":"This unit covers where the coronary arteries arise, how they branch, which part of the heart each one feeds, and how the heart's veins drain back to the right atrium. The exam uses it to link a blocked artery to the wall that dies, the ECG leads that change, and the complications that follow.","notes":[{"h":"Origins and the left system","points":["Right coronary artery (RCA) arises from the right aortic sinus; left coronary artery (LCA) from the left aortic sinus.","Left main runs between the pulmonary trunk and left auricle, then splits into the LAD and the circumflex (LCx).","LAD (anterior interventricular artery) runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex.","LAD feeds the anterior LV wall, the apex, and the anterior two-thirds of the septum (septal perforators); diagonal branches feed the anterolateral wall.","LCx runs in the left atrioventricular (coronary) groove to the back; obtuse marginal branches feed the lateral LV wall."]},{"h":"The right system","points":["RCA runs in the right atrioventricular groove.","Right (acute) marginal branch feeds the right ventricle.","SA nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 60% of hearts and from the LCx in about 40%.","AV nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 80% of hearts.","In a right-dominant heart the RCA gives the posterior descending artery (PDA), which feeds the posterior one-third of the septum and the inferior LV wall."]},{"h":"Dominance and papillary muscles","points":["Dominance is defined by which artery gives the PDA.","Most hearts are right dominant (estimates run from about 67% to 85%); the rest are left dominant (PDA from LCx) or codominant.","Anterolateral papillary muscle has a double supply (LAD and LCx).","Posteromedial papillary muscle depends on the PDA alone, so it is the one that ruptures after an inferior MI."]},{"h":"Venous drainage","points":["Coronary sinus lies in the posterior atrioventricular groove and opens into the right atrium.","Great cardiac vein runs with the LAD, middle cardiac vein with the PDA, small cardiac vein with the right marginal artery; all three reach the coronary sinus.","Anterior cardiac veins drain the front of the RV straight into the right atrium.","Smallest cardiac veins (venae cordis minimae) open directly into the chambers."]},{"h":"How coronary flow works","points":["Resting coronary flow is about 225 mL/min, about 4 to 5% of cardiac output.","LV muscle is perfused mostly in diastole, because systole squeezes the intramyocardial vessels shut.","Tachycardia shortens diastole, so it cuts LV perfusion while raising demand.","The heart extracts about 70% of arterial oxygen at rest, so more demand must be met by more flow.","Adenosine and other local metabolites are the main coronary vasodilators.","The subendocardium has the highest wall stress and the least flow reserve, so it becomes ischemic first."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Coronary arteries and their territories","cols":["Artery","Course","Main territory","Typical infarct ECG leads"],"rows":[["LAD","Anterior interventricular groove to the apex","Anterior LV wall, apex, anterior two-thirds of septum","V1 to V4 (anterior and septal)"],["LCx","Left atrioventricular groove to the posterior heart","Lateral and posterolateral LV wall, left atrium","I, aVL, V5, V6 (lateral)"],["RCA","Right atrioventricular groove","Right atrium, most of the RV, SA node (about 60%), AV node (about 80%)","II, III, aVF (inferior), often with bradycardia or AV block"],["PDA (from RCA in right-dominant hearts)","Posterior interventricular groove","Posterior one-third of septum, inferior LV wall, posteromedial papillary muscle","II, III, aVF (inferior)"]]},{"title":"Cardiac veins","cols":["Vein","Runs with","Drains into"],"rows":[["Great cardiac vein","LAD","Coronary sinus"],["Middle cardiac vein","PDA","Coronary sinus"],["Small cardiac vein","Right marginal artery","Coronary sinus"],["Anterior cardiac veins","Over the anterior RV wall","Right atrium directly"],["Smallest cardiac veins","Within the myocardium","Heart chambers directly"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Who gives the PDA wins","text":"To name the dominant artery, ask only one question: which artery gives the posterior descending artery? Size of the vessel does not matter."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Veins shadow arteries","text":"Pair each vein with its artery: great with LAD, middle with PDA, small with right marginal. All three empty into the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Diastole feeds the left","text":"Anything that shortens diastole (fast heart rate) or lowers aortic diastolic pressure (aortic regurgitation, hypotension) starves the LV subendocardium first."}],"highYield":["LAD is the most commonly occluded coronary artery, followed by the RCA, then the LCx.","Inferior MI (II, III, aVF) usually means RCA occlusion and can come with bradycardia or AV block.","Posteromedial papillary muscle rupture after inferior MI causes sudden acute mitral regurgitation.","Coronary perfusion pressure is aortic diastolic pressure minus LV end-diastolic pressure.","Coronary sinus runs in the posterior atrioventricular groove and opens into the right atrium.","Adenosine is the classic local metabolite that dilates coronary arterioles when demand rises."],"pitfalls":["Dominance is about who supplies the PDA; a large LCx can still sit in a right-dominant heart.","The SA node is usually fed by the RCA (about 60%), but in about 40% it comes from the LCx, so an LCx lesion can still cause sinus node problems.","The anterolateral papillary muscle rarely ruptures because it has two arteries; the posteromedial one has only the PDA.","Anterior cardiac veins and smallest cardiac veins bypass the coronary sinus, so the coronary sinus does not carry all cardiac venous blood."],"dumps":[{"t":"Map the coronary arteries","p":"Draw the heart from the front and from below. Label the left main, LAD, diagonal branch, circumflex, obtuse marginal, RCA, right marginal, and PDA. Shade and name the wall each artery feeds.","l":["RCA from the right aortic sinus, LCA from the left aortic sinus","Left main splits into LAD and circumflex","LAD in the anterior interventricular groove reaching the apex","LAD territory: anterior LV wall, apex, anterior two-thirds of septum","Circumflex in the left atrioventricular groove feeding the lateral wall","RCA in the right atrioventricular groove with the right marginal branch to the RV","PDA in the posterior interventricular groove feeding the inferior wall and posterior one-third of septum","Note that the PDA source defines dominance"]},{"t":"Trace coronary blood","p":"Trace a red cell from the aorta through the LAD, across the anterior wall capillaries, and back to the right atrium. Name each vessel and say when in the cardiac cycle the flow is highest.","l":["Left aortic sinus to the left main coronary artery","Left main to LAD, then septal or diagonal branches","Capillaries of the anterior LV wall","Great cardiac vein running back up beside the LAD","Coronary sinus in the posterior atrioventricular groove","Opening into the right atrium","LV flow is highest in diastole because systole compresses intramyocardial vessels","About 70% of the oxygen is extracted on the way through"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax chapter, section on the heart: arterial supply and venous drainage"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on ischemic heart disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Circulatory System | Coronary Circulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TEcPhovaAg4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Anatomy of the Heart and Coronary Circulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7ERVxf5eD7k"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Coronary Blood Supply","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3wpT-4bSmoU"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-7","CV-18-C","CV-18-C3"]},"CV-9":{"id":"CV-9","summary":"This unit covers how arterial blood reaches the head, limbs, and abdominal organs, how venous blood returns, and how the portal system connects the gut to the liver. Exam questions name a vessel or a vertebral level and ask what it supplies, or describe a blockage and ask what tissue suffers.","notes":[{"h":"Aortic arch and thoracic aorta","points":["Arch branches, right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian.","Brachiocephalic trunk splits into the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries.","Common carotid divides at about the level of C4 (upper border of the thyroid cartilage) into internal and external carotids.","Descending thoracic aorta gives posterior intercostal, bronchial, and esophageal branches."]},{"h":"Abdominal aorta","points":["Enters the abdomen through the aortic hiatus at T12 and bifurcates into the common iliac arteries at L4.","Unpaired gut arteries: celiac trunk (T12), superior mesenteric artery (L1), inferior mesenteric artery (L3).","Paired branches: inferior phrenic, middle suprarenal, renal (about L1), gonadal (about L2), and four pairs of lumbar arteries.","Celiac trunk gives the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries.","Splenic flexure sits between SMA and IMA territories, a watershed zone for ischemic colitis."]},{"h":"Brain and limbs","points":["Brain gets anterior flow from the internal carotids and posterior flow from the vertebral arteries (branches of the subclavians), which join as the basilar artery.","Circle of Willis links them: anterior communicating, anterior cerebral, internal carotid, posterior communicating, and posterior cerebral arteries.","Upper limb: subclavian becomes axillary at the lateral border of the first rib, then brachial at the lower border of teres major; brachial splits into radial and ulnar in the cubital fossa.","Lower limb: external iliac becomes femoral under the inguinal ligament, then popliteal at the adductor hiatus; popliteal splits into anterior tibial and the tibiofibular trunk (posterior tibial and fibular).","Dorsalis pedis is the continuation of the anterior tibial artery."]},{"h":"Veins and the portal system","points":["SVC forms from the two brachiocephalic veins; IVC forms from the common iliac veins at about L5.","Left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the SMA; the left gonadal vein drains into it, while the right gonadal vein drains straight into the IVC.","Hepatic portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas from the superior mesenteric and splenic veins; the inferior mesenteric vein usually joins the splenic vein.","Portal blood passes through liver sinusoids, then leaves by the hepatic veins into the IVC."]},{"h":"Where cardiac output goes at rest","points":["Liver and gut together receive about a quarter of resting cardiac output, kidneys about a fifth.","Skeletal muscle gets about 15% at rest and the brain about 14%.","Heart muscle gets about 4 to 5% but uses oxygen at a very high rate."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Abdominal aorta landmarks","cols":["Branch or event","Vertebral level","Supplies"],"rows":[["Aorta passes the aortic hiatus","T12","Start of the abdominal aorta"],["Celiac trunk","T12","Foregut: stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, gallbladder, part of the pancreas, spleen"],["Superior mesenteric artery","L1","Midgut: distal duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon"],["Renal arteries","About L1","Kidneys"],["Gonadal arteries","About L2","Testes or ovaries"],["Inferior mesenteric artery","L3","Hindgut: distal one-third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal"],["Bifurcation into common iliacs","L4","Pelvis and lower limbs"]]},{"title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Sign in portal hypertension"],"rows":[["Lower esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins draining to the azygos system","Esophageal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial and inferior epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ABC'S","text":"Aortic arch gives the Brachiocephalic trunk, left common Carotid, and left Subclavian, in that order from right to left."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Bifourcation","text":"The abdominal aorta bifurcates at L4: bi-FOUR-cation."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One artery per gut segment","text":"Foregut goes with the celiac trunk, midgut with the SMA, hindgut with the IMA. Find which segment the organ came from and you have its artery."}],"highYield":["Celiac T12, SMA L1, renal about L1, gonadal about L2, IMA L3, bifurcation L4.","Left renal vein can be squeezed between the SMA and aorta (nutcracker phenomenon).","Left varicocele is more common because the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein.","Splenic flexure is a watershed area between SMA and IMA territories.","Portal hypertension opens anastomoses at the esophagus, umbilicus, and rectum.","The vertebral arteries come off the subclavians and join to form the basilar artery."],"pitfalls":["The spleen develops from mesoderm, yet it is supplied by the celiac trunk (splenic artery).","The pancreatic head has a double supply: superior pancreaticoduodenal (celiac) and inferior pancreaticoduodenal (SMA) arteries.","The popliteal artery starts at the adductor hiatus, while the femoral artery starts at the inguinal ligament; mixing these up shifts the level of a lesion.","The right gonadal vein drains into the IVC; only the left one drains into the renal vein."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw the abdominal aorta","p":"Draw the abdominal aorta from the diaphragm to its bifurcation. Mark the vertebral level of each branch and write what each branch supplies.","l":["Aortic hiatus at T12","Celiac trunk at T12 with left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic branches","SMA at L1 supplying the midgut","Renal arteries at about L1 and gonadal arteries at about L2","IMA at L3 supplying the hindgut","Bifurcation into common iliac arteries at L4","Left renal vein crossing anterior to the aorta below the SMA"]},{"t":"Draw the circle of Willis","p":"Draw the circle of Willis as seen from below the brain. Label every artery that forms the circle, the arteries that feed it, and the middle cerebral arteries.","l":["Internal carotid arteries entering on each side","Anterior cerebral arteries joined by the anterior communicating artery","Middle cerebral arteries coming off each internal carotid","Posterior communicating arteries linking internal carotid to posterior cerebral","Posterior cerebral arteries from the top of the basilar artery","Basilar artery formed by the two vertebral arteries","Vertebral arteries arising from the subclavian arteries"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen chapter, section on the abdominal aorta and the arterial supply of the abdominal viscera"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head chapter, section on the arterial supply of the brain"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Local and Humoral Control of Tissue Blood Flow"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Circulatory System | Arteries of the Thorax & Abdomen | Torso Anatomy Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L4vf12QCy70"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Circulatory System | Arteries & Veins of the Upper & Lower Limbs | Wire Man Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EmoOicZqXhI"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Arteries, arterioles, venules, and veins | Health & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iqRTd1NY-pU"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-11","CV-18-H3","CV-18-I6"]},"CV-10":{"id":"CV-10","summary":"This unit covers how lymph is formed, how it moves, where it drains, and how lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, and mucosal lymphoid tissue are built. The exam asks about drainage routes, the zones of each lymphoid organ, and what goes wrong when lymph flow is blocked.","notes":[{"h":"Lymph formation","points":["Lymphatic capillaries are blind-ended tubes with overlapping endothelial cells that act as one-way flap valves.","Anchoring filaments hold them open when the tissue swells.","They return filtered fluid and leaked plasma protein to the blood, about 2 to 3 L per day.","Lacteals in intestinal villi take up dietary fat as chylomicrons, which makes lymph milky (chyle)."]},{"h":"How lymph moves","points":["Collecting lymphatics have valves and smooth muscle that contract in segments (lymphangions).","Skeletal muscle contraction, breathing, nearby arterial pulsation, and outside compression all push lymph forward.","A rise in interstitial fluid pressure increases lymph flow, up to a maximum."]},{"h":"Drainage routes","points":["Thoracic duct starts at the cisterna chyli (about L1 to L2), passes through the aortic hiatus, and drains into the left venous angle (left internal jugular and left subclavian junction).","Thoracic duct drains everything except the right upper body.","Right lymphatic duct (or its trunks separately) drains the right head, neck, arm, and right side of the thorax into the right venous angle."]},{"h":"Lymphoid organs","points":["Primary lymphoid organs: bone marrow (B cells mature) and thymus (T cells mature).","Secondary lymphoid organs: lymph nodes, spleen, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (tonsils, Peyer patches in the ileum, appendix).","Lymph node path: afferent vessels, subcapsular sinus, cortical sinuses, medullary sinuses, efferent vessel at the hilum.","Spleen filters blood: red pulp removes old red cells; white pulp mounts immune responses to blood-borne antigens.","Thymus comes from the third pharyngeal pouch; positive selection happens in the cortex, negative selection in the medulla."]},{"h":"When lymph flow fails","points":["Lymphedema is protein-rich edema that becomes firm and nonpitting as fibrosis sets in.","Causes include lymph node removal or radiation (such as after breast cancer surgery), tumor, and filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti).","Thoracic duct injury leaks chyle into the pleural space (chylothorax)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Zones of lymphoid organs","cols":["Organ","Zone","Main cells","Job"],"rows":[["Lymph node","Cortex follicles","B cells; germinal centers in secondary follicles","B-cell activation and proliferation"],["Lymph node","Paracortex","T cells and dendritic cells; high endothelial venules","T-cell activation; entry point for circulating lymphocytes"],["Lymph node","Medulla","Plasma cells and macrophages in medullary cords","Antibody secretion and filtering of lymph"],["Spleen","White pulp, periarteriolar lymphoid sheath","T cells","T-cell responses to blood antigens"],["Spleen","White pulp, follicles","B cells","B-cell responses to blood antigens"],["Spleen","Red pulp (cords and sinusoids)","Macrophages","Removes old or damaged red cells"],["Thymus","Cortex","Immature T cells and cortical epithelial cells","Positive selection"],["Thymus","Medulla","Maturing T cells; Hassall corpuscles","Negative selection"]]},{"title":"First lymph node group by region","cols":["Region drained","First node group"],"rows":[["Upper limb and lateral breast","Axillary"],["Medial breast","Parasternal (internal thoracic)"],["Scrotum, vulva, anal canal below the pectinate line, most of the superficial lower limb","Superficial inguinal"],["Lateral foot and posterolateral calf","Popliteal"],["Testes and ovaries","Para-aortic (lumbar)"],["Stomach","Celiac"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Right upper corner","text":"Only the right upper corner of the body (right head, neck, arm, and chest) goes to the right lymphatic duct. Every other region drains to the thoracic duct."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SHiNE SKiS","text":"Encapsulated organisms that threaten people without a spleen: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis, Escherichia coli, Salmonella, Klebsiella pneumoniae, group B Streptococcus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Cortex positive, medulla negative","text":"In the thymus, the cortex keeps T cells that can bind self MHC (positive selection), and the medulla deletes T cells that bind self antigen too strongly (negative selection)."}],"highYield":["Testes drain to para-aortic nodes, while the scrotum drains to superficial inguinal nodes.","Paracortex is underdeveloped in DiGeorge syndrome because T cells are lacking.","Asplenia raises the risk of sepsis from encapsulated bacteria; Howell-Jolly bodies appear on the smear.","Thoracic duct empties into the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins.","Lymphedema fluid is protein rich, which drives fibrosis and nonpitting swelling.","Peyer patches sit in the ileum and contain M cells that sample gut antigens."],"pitfalls":["Anal canal below the pectinate line drains to superficial inguinal nodes; above it drains to internal iliac nodes.","Lymph node B cells sit in the cortex follicles, while T cells sit in the paracortex; in the spleen T cells sit in the periarteriolar sheath.","Lymphedema and cardiac edema both swell the legs, but lymphedema is protein rich and becomes nonpitting, while early edema from high venous pressure pits easily.","Bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs; the spleen is secondary."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw a lymph node","p":"Draw a lymph node in cross section. Label where lymph enters and leaves, every sinus it passes through, and the B-cell, T-cell, and plasma-cell zones.","l":["Afferent lymphatic vessels entering the convex surface","Capsule and subcapsular sinus","Cortex with primary and secondary follicles and germinal centers (B cells)","Paracortex with T cells and high endothelial venules","Medullary cords with plasma cells and macrophages","Medullary sinuses leading to the hilum","Efferent lymphatic vessel, artery, and vein at the hilum"]},{"t":"Trace lymph back to the blood","p":"Trace a drop of interstitial fluid from the left foot back into the blood. Then do the same for a drop from the right hand. Name each vessel and node group.","l":["Lymphatic capillary with overlapping endothelial flaps","Collecting vessels with valves","Left foot: superficial inguinal nodes, then deep inguinal, external iliac, common iliac, and lumbar nodes","Lumbar trunks to the cisterna chyli near L1 to L2","Thoracic duct through the aortic hiatus to the left venous angle","Right hand: axillary nodes, then the subclavian trunk","Right lymphatic duct (or the trunks directly) to the right venous angle"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Introduction to the Immune System, section on the tissues of the immune system (lymph nodes, spleen, mucosal lymphoid tissue)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Why we need a lymphatic system | Lymphatic system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_GinTV94hUk"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"How lymphatic vessels move fluid | Lymphatic system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CLneY6SoPi0"}],"related":["CV-11","CV-16","CV-18-H4"]},"CV-11":{"id":"CV-11","summary":"This unit covers the physics of blood flow (pressure, resistance, viscosity, turbulence), the Starling forces that move fluid across capillaries, and how tissues control their own flow. Board questions ask you to predict what happens to flow or pressure when one variable changes.","notes":[{"h":"Pressure, flow, and resistance","points":["Flow equals pressure difference divided by resistance (Q = ΔP / R).","For the whole circulation, mean arterial pressure is about cardiac output times total peripheral resistance.","Poiseuille: resistance = 8ηL / (πr⁴), so radius matters most.","Halving the radius raises resistance 16 times and cuts flow to one-sixteenth at the same pressure.","Resistances in series add; in parallel, 1/R total = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + and so on, so the total is lower than any single branch.","Organs sit in parallel, which lets each one change its own flow without changing the others much."]},{"h":"Velocity, viscosity, turbulence","points":["Velocity = flow divided by total cross-sectional area, so blood moves slowest in capillaries.","Hematocrit is the main determinant of viscosity; polycythemia raises viscosity and resistance, anemia lowers them.","Turbulence becomes more likely with high velocity, low viscosity, large diameter, and rough or narrowed vessels (Reynolds number).","Turbulence makes bruits and murmurs, such as flow murmurs in anemia."]},{"h":"Arterial pressure and compliance","points":["Pulse pressure = systolic minus diastolic; it widens with stiff arteries or a larger stroke volume.","MAP is about diastolic pressure plus one-third of pulse pressure at resting heart rates.","Veins are about 24 times more compliant than their matching arteries and hold about 64% of blood volume.","Law of Laplace: wall tension rises with pressure and radius, so a dilated ventricle or aneurysm faces more wall stress."]},{"h":"Capillary exchange and lymph","points":["Starling: net filtration = Kf × [(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)].","Edema comes from higher capillary pressure, lower plasma oncotic pressure, leakier capillaries, or blocked lymphatics.","Lymph returns the small net filtrate and leaked protein to the blood.","Venous return is helped by venous valves, the skeletal muscle pump, and the drop in thoracic pressure during inspiration."]},{"h":"Control of blood flow","points":["Metabolic control: adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, lactate, and low O₂ dilate arterioles in active tissue.","Myogenic control: stretch of arteriolar smooth muscle triggers contraction, which keeps flow steady when pressure rises (autoregulation).","Endothelium releases nitric oxide (from shear stress) and prostacyclin to dilate, and endothelin to constrict.","Sympathetic nerves constrict most arterioles through α1 receptors.","Constrictors in blood: norepinephrine, angiotensin II, vasopressin. Dilators: bradykinin, histamine, ANP, nitric oxide.","Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus (CN IX) and aortic arch (CN X) report pressure to the nucleus of the solitary tract in the medulla."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Vessel segments","cols":["Segment","Key feature","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Aorta and large arteries","Elastic, store energy in systole","Keep flow going during diastole"],["Arterioles","Largest share of resistance and the biggest pressure drop","Set total peripheral resistance and divide flow among organs"],["Capillaries","Largest total cross-sectional area, slowest velocity","Time for exchange with tissues"],["Venules and veins","Most compliant, hold about 64% of blood volume","Blood reservoir; venoconstriction raises venous return"]]},{"title":"Main control of flow by organ","cols":["Organ","Dominant control"],"rows":[["Heart","Local metabolites (adenosine, NO, CO₂) and low O₂"],["Brain","Arterial PCO₂ (through pH)"],["Kidney","Myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback"],["Lung","Hypoxia causes vasoconstriction"],["Skeletal muscle","Local metabolites during exercise; sympathetic tone at rest"],["Skin","Sympathetic nerves (temperature control)"]]},{"title":"Starling forces","cols":["Force","Pushes fluid","Changes that cause edema"],"rows":[["Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc)","Out of the capillary","Rises in heart failure, venous obstruction, volume overload"],["Plasma oncotic pressure (πc)","Into the capillary","Falls in nephrotic syndrome, liver failure, malnutrition"],["Interstitial hydrostatic pressure (Pi)","Into the capillary when positive (it is slightly negative in most loose tissues, which favors filtration)","Rarely a primary cause; it rises as edema builds and opposes further filtration"],["Interstitial oncotic pressure (πi)","Out of the capillary","Rises with leaky capillaries (burns, inflammation) or lymphatic blockage"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Blood follows Ohm's law","text":"ΔP = Q × R works like V = I × R. Pressure is voltage, flow is current, and resistance is resistance. MAP = CO × TPR is the same idea for the whole body."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Radius to the fourth","text":"When a question changes vessel radius, think in powers of two: half the radius gives 16 times the resistance; double the radius gives one-sixteenth."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Four ways to edema","text":"Push harder (high Pc), pull weaker (low πc), leak more (high Kf or πi), or drain less (lymph block). Sort any edema cause into one of the four."}],"highYield":["Arterioles are the main resistance vessels and the main site of blood pressure control.","Capillaries have the greatest total cross-sectional area and the slowest velocity.","Radius is raised to the fourth power in Poiseuille's law, so small changes in caliber cause large changes in flow.","Cerebral flow tracks PCO₂: hyperventilation lowers PCO₂ and constricts cerebral vessels.","Hypoxia dilates systemic vessels but constricts pulmonary vessels.","Nephrotic syndrome causes edema by lowering plasma oncotic pressure.","Isolated systolic hypertension in older adults reflects stiff, less compliant arteries."],"pitfalls":["Adding a parallel vascular bed lowers total resistance; adding a segment in series raises it.","Blood velocity is lowest in capillaries even though each capillary is tiny, because their combined area is the largest.","Pulmonary vessels constrict to hypoxia, the opposite of systemic vessels.","Higher hematocrit raises viscosity and resistance; anemia lowers them and favors turbulence."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pressure, area, and velocity","p":"Draw three stacked line graphs across the aorta, arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins, and vena cava: one for pressure, one for total cross-sectional area, one for velocity. Explain each shape.","l":["Pressure is highest in the aorta and pulsatile in large arteries","Steepest pressure drop across the arterioles","Pulsatility is mostly gone by the capillaries","Venous pressure is low and near zero at the right atrium","Cross-sectional area peaks at the capillaries","Velocity is the mirror image of area, lowest in capillaries","Velocity rises again as veins merge into the vena cava"]},{"t":"Starling forces and edema","p":"Build a table on blank paper of the four Starling forces. For each, write which way it pushes fluid and one disease that changes it to cause edema. Add the role of lymph.","l":["Capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out","Plasma oncotic pressure pulls fluid in","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure opposes filtration when positive","Interstitial oncotic pressure pulls fluid out","Heart failure raises capillary hydrostatic pressure","Nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis lowers plasma oncotic pressure","Burns or inflammation raise permeability","Lymph removes the net filtrate; blockage causes lymphedema"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on the overview of the circulation and the physics of pressure, flow, and resistance"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Local and Humoral Control of Tissue Blood Flow"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Fundamentals of Blood Pressure","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2g7pFPXG2eE"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Resistance in a tube | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8X7yEzgurP8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Flow and perfusion | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePwN6rHhNyU"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Hemodynamics | Circulatory System Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCyTYsxECLw"}],"related":["CV-6","CV-10","CV-18-A2","CV-18-H4"]},"CV-12":{"id":"CV-12","summary":"This unit covers how the heart and vessels respond during a single bout of exercise and how they adapt with regular training. Questions ask you to predict changes in heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, blood pressure, and resistance, and to tell an athlete's heart from disease.","notes":[{"h":"Starting exercise","points":["Central command from the motor cortex and reflexes from muscle mechanoreceptors and metaboreceptors raise sympathetic output.","The early rise in heart rate comes from vagal withdrawal; sympathetic drive takes over as intensity rises.","Venoconstriction, the skeletal muscle pump, and deeper breathing push more blood back to the heart."]},{"h":"Heart and output","points":["Heart rate rises roughly in step with workload.","Stroke volume rises from more filling (Frank-Starling) and stronger contraction; in many untrained people it levels off at moderate intensity.","Cardiac output can rise about four- to fivefold in hard exercise in untrained adults, and more in trained endurance athletes.","Oxygen uptake follows the Fick principle: VO₂ = cardiac output × arteriovenous O₂ difference.","The arteriovenous O₂ difference widens because active muscle extracts more oxygen."]},{"h":"Redistribution of flow","points":["Active muscle arterioles dilate to local metabolites (K⁺, adenosine, H⁺, CO₂, lactate) and nitric oxide.","Muscle blood flow can rise up to about 25 times in strenuous exercise.","Sympathetic constriction cuts flow to the gut, kidneys, and inactive muscle.","Skin flow rises as body temperature climbs, to shed heat.","Brain flow stays fairly constant; coronary flow rises with cardiac work."]},{"h":"Blood pressure in two kinds of exercise","points":["Dynamic (aerobic) exercise: systolic pressure rises, diastolic stays about the same or falls slightly, total peripheral resistance falls.","Static (isometric) exercise: contracting muscle squeezes its own vessels, so systolic and diastolic pressure both rise sharply.","Dynamic exercise is mainly a volume load on the heart; static exercise is mainly a pressure load."]},{"h":"Training adaptations","points":["Endurance training enlarges the LV cavity with proportional wall thickening (eccentric remodeling), so stroke volume rises.","Plasma and blood volume increase, which raises preload.","Resting heart rate falls, mostly from higher vagal tone; maximum heart rate changes little.","Maximal cardiac output and VO₂max rise.","Trained muscle has more capillaries and mitochondria, so it extracts more oxygen.","Regular exercise lowers resting blood pressure, raises HDL, lowers triglycerides, and improves insulin sensitivity and endothelial nitric oxide release."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Acute response: dynamic vs static exercise","cols":["Variable","Dynamic (running, cycling)","Static (heavy hold, handgrip)"],"rows":[["Heart rate","Rises a lot","Rises moderately"],["Stroke volume","Rises","Little change"],["Cardiac output","Rises a lot","Rises modestly"],["Systolic pressure","Rises","Rises sharply"],["Diastolic pressure","Same or slightly lower","Rises sharply"],["Total peripheral resistance","Falls","Same or rises"],["Main load on the LV","Volume","Pressure"]]},{"title":"Athlete's heart vs hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","cols":["Feature","Athlete's heart","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"rows":[["Wall thickening","Symmetric and mild","Often asymmetric, septum most"],["LV cavity","Normal or enlarged","Small"],["Diastolic filling","Normal","Impaired (stiff ventricle)"],["After a period of rest from training","Thickening regresses","No regression"],["Family history of sudden death","Usually absent","May be present (autosomal dominant inheritance)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Fick for exercise","text":"VO₂ = CO × (arterial minus venous O₂ content). Exercise raises both terms: the heart pumps more, and muscle pulls more oxygen out of each liter."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Run vs lift","text":"Running opens muscle vessels, so resistance falls and diastolic pressure holds steady. Lifting squeezes them shut, so both systolic and diastolic pressure climb."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Big tank, slow pump","text":"The trained heart has a bigger LV and more blood volume, so each beat moves more blood and the resting rate can be lower for the same output."}],"highYield":["In dynamic exercise total peripheral resistance falls because active muscle beds dilate.","Diastolic pressure stays about the same or falls slightly in dynamic exercise; it rises in static exercise.","Resting bradycardia in trained athletes is mainly from increased vagal tone.","The arteriovenous O₂ difference widens during exercise and after training.","Exercise shunts blood away from the gut and kidneys toward muscle, heart, and skin.","Athlete's heart regresses after training stops; hypertrophic cardiomyopathy does not."],"pitfalls":["Cardiac output at rest stays about the same after training; the lower heart rate is balanced by a larger stroke volume.","Maximum heart rate does not rise with training; the gains in maximal output come from stroke volume.","A rise in systolic pressure during running is normal; a fall in systolic pressure as workload increases is abnormal.","Brain blood flow stays fairly constant in exercise even though cardiac output rises several-fold."],"dumps":[{"t":"Rest vs hard exercise","p":"Build a table with two columns, rest and hard dynamic exercise. Fill in what happens to heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, systolic pressure, diastolic pressure, total peripheral resistance, arteriovenous O₂ difference, muscle flow, and gut and kidney flow.","l":["Heart rate up","Stroke volume up","Cardiac output up about four- to fivefold (more in trained athletes)","Systolic pressure up","Diastolic pressure about the same or slightly down","Total peripheral resistance down","Arteriovenous O₂ difference wider","Muscle flow up as much as about 25 times","Gut and kidney flow down from sympathetic constriction"]},{"t":"Trace the reflexes of a run","p":"Trace the steps from the moment someone starts running to the rise in cardiac output. Include the nerve signals, the heart, the veins, and the muscle arterioles.","l":["Central command from the motor cortex","Muscle mechanoreceptor and metaboreceptor input","Medullary cardiovascular centers respond","Vagal withdrawal raises heart rate first","Sympathetic drive raises heart rate and contractility","Venoconstriction and the muscle pump raise venous return","More filling raises stroke volume (Frank-Starling)","Local metabolites dilate active muscle arterioles while inactive beds constrict"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Sports Physiology"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Cardiac Output","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0O3FfHPE9PU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Autonomic nervous system effects on the heart | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KiouveG278Y"}],"related":["CV-6","CV-11","CV-15","CV-18-E1"]},"CV-13":{"id":"CV-13","summary":"This unit covers how lipids travel in the blood, how the heart makes its ATP, and which vitamins, minerals, and cofactors the heart and vessels depend on. The exam links a biochemical defect or deficiency to a heart or vessel problem, such as thiamine and high-output failure or homocysteine and thrombosis.","notes":[{"h":"Lipoprotein transport","points":["Exogenous path: intestine packs dietary triglyceride into chylomicrons (apo B-48), which reach the blood through lymph.","Lipoprotein lipase on capillary endothelium, activated by apo C-II, frees fatty acids from chylomicrons and VLDL.","Endogenous path: liver makes VLDL (apo B-100), which becomes IDL and then LDL as triglyceride is removed.","LDL delivers cholesterol to cells through the LDL receptor, which binds apo B-100.","HDL (apo A-I) carries cholesterol back to the liver (reverse cholesterol transport); apo A-I activates LCAT, which esterifies cholesterol.","CETP moves cholesteryl esters from HDL to VLDL, IDL, and LDL in exchange for triglyceride.","HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis."]},{"h":"Lipids and the vessel wall","points":["LDL that enters the intima can be oxidized; macrophages take it up through scavenger receptors that are not downregulated, becoming foam cells.","Lipoprotein(a) is an LDL-like particle with apo(a), which resembles plasminogen; its level is mostly genetic and it adds atherosclerotic risk.","Trans fats raise LDL and lower HDL; omega-3 fatty acids (EPA, DHA) lower triglycerides at higher intakes."]},{"h":"Cardiac energy metabolism","points":["The adult heart at rest makes most of its ATP (roughly 60 to 90%) from fatty acid beta-oxidation; glucose, lactate, and ketones supply the rest.","Mitochondria fill about a third of cardiomyocyte volume, so the heart depends on a steady O₂ supply.","Long-chain fatty acids need carnitine and carnitine palmitoyltransferase I to enter mitochondria; malonyl-CoA inhibits CPT-I.","Creatine kinase moves high-energy phosphate from mitochondria to myofibrils as creatine phosphate.","In ischemia the heart switches to anaerobic glycolysis, lactate builds up, and contraction fails within a minute or two.","Coenzyme Q10 (ubiquinone) carries electrons in the respiratory chain and is made from the same mevalonate pathway that statins block."]},{"h":"Vitamins","points":["Thiamine (B1), as thiamine pyrophosphate, is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase, and branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase.","Thiamine deficiency with heart failure (wet beriberi) is a high-output state with dilated heart and peripheral vasodilation; risk is high in alcohol use disorder and malnutrition.","Homocysteine is remethylated to methionine by methionine synthase (needs B12 and folate) or converted to cystathionine by cystathionine beta-synthase (needs B6).","Deficiency of B12, folate, or B6 raises homocysteine, which injures endothelium and favors thrombosis.","Vitamin C is needed to hydroxylate proline and lysine in collagen; scurvy causes fragile vessels, bleeding gums, and perifollicular hemorrhage.","Vitamin K is needed to gamma-carboxylate factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S."]},{"h":"Minerals","points":["Potassium (normal about 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L) sets resting membrane potential; both low and high levels cause arrhythmias.","Calcium (normal about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL) triggers contraction through L-type channels and calcium-induced calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Magnesium is needed for Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity; low magnesium increases renal potassium loss, so hypokalemia is hard to correct, and causes hypocalcemia by impairing PTH release and action.","Iron deficiency anemia causes a high-output, tachycardic state; iron overload (hemochromatosis) causes cardiomyopathy and conduction problems.","Selenium deficiency causes Keshan disease, a cardiomyopathy.","Copper is a cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen and elastin; deficiency (as in Menkes disease) weakens arteries."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Lipoproteins","cols":["Particle","Made in","Main cargo","Key apolipoproteins","Job"],"rows":[["Chylomicron","Intestine","Dietary triglyceride","B-48, C-II, E","Delivers dietary fat to muscle and adipose tissue; remnant goes to liver"],["VLDL","Liver","Hepatic triglyceride","B-100, C-II, E","Delivers liver-made triglyceride to tissues"],["IDL","From VLDL in plasma","Triglyceride and cholesterol","B-100, E","Taken up by liver or converted to LDL"],["LDL","From IDL in plasma","Cholesteryl ester","B-100","Delivers cholesterol to tissues through the LDL receptor"],["HDL","Liver and intestine","Cholesterol and phospholipid","A-I (also carries C-II and E)","Reverse cholesterol transport; apolipoprotein reservoir"]]},{"title":"Apolipoprotein functions","cols":["Apolipoprotein","Function"],"rows":[["A-I","Activates LCAT"],["B-48","Needed to assemble and secrete chylomicrons"],["B-100","Binds the LDL receptor"],["C-II","Cofactor for lipoprotein lipase"],["E","Mediates uptake of chylomicron remnants and IDL by the liver"]]},{"title":"Electrolytes and the ECG","cols":["Disturbance","ECG findings","Risk"],"rows":[["Hypokalemia","Flattened T waves, U waves","Arrhythmias; raises digoxin toxicity"],["Hyperkalemia","Peaked T waves, wide QRS, loss of P waves, sine wave pattern","Ventricular fibrillation or asystole"],["Hypocalcemia","Prolonged QT","Arrhythmias, tetany"],["Hypercalcemia","Shortened QT","Arrhythmias"],["Hypomagnesemia","Prolonged QT possible","Torsades de pointes; refractory hypokalemia"]]},{"title":"Homocysteine metabolism","cols":["Step","Enzyme","Cofactor needed","Lab clue when deficient"],"rows":[["Homocysteine to methionine","Methionine synthase","Vitamin B12 and folate (as N5-methyl-THF)","B12 deficiency: high homocysteine and high methylmalonic acid. Folate deficiency: high homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid"],["Homocysteine to cystathionine","Cystathionine beta-synthase","Vitamin B6","High homocysteine; inherited enzyme defect causes homocystinuria"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Thiamine has four enzymes","text":"List them as pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase, and transketolase. All energy production slows without B1, and the heart fails in a high-output pattern."},{"kind":"trick","label":"MMA splits B12 from folate","text":"Both deficiencies raise homocysteine. Only B12 deficiency also raises methylmalonic acid."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Apo letters by job","text":"C-II cuts (activates lipoprotein lipase), E is the entry ticket to the liver for remnants, B-100 docks on the LDL receptor, A-I activates LCAT."}],"highYield":["Wet beriberi: thiamine deficiency causing high-output heart failure.","Apo C-II activates lipoprotein lipase; apo B-100 binds the LDL receptor.","Oxidized LDL taken up by macrophage scavenger receptors forms foam cells.","The resting adult heart burns mostly fatty acids for ATP.","Hypomagnesemia causes refractory hypokalemia and raises the risk of torsades de pointes.","Hyperkalemia shows peaked T waves; hypokalemia shows U waves.","Selenium deficiency causes Keshan cardiomyopathy.","Homocystinuria (cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency) causes arterial and venous thrombosis."],"pitfalls":["LDL receptors on cells are downregulated when cells have enough cholesterol; scavenger receptors on macrophages are not, which is why foam cells overfill.","Lowering homocysteine with B vitamins has not clearly reduced heart attacks in trials, so treat high homocysteine as a marker of risk and a sign of vitamin status.","Thiamine deficiency heart failure is high output with low resistance, unlike the low-output failure of most cardiomyopathies.","Hypocalcemia lengthens QT and hypercalcemia shortens it; potassium changes show up mostly in T and U waves."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lipoprotein pathways","p":"Draw the exogenous and endogenous lipoprotein pathways from the gut and liver to the tissues and back. Label each particle, its main apolipoproteins, and each enzyme or receptor.","l":["Chylomicron from the intestine with B-48, entering blood through lymph","Lipoprotein lipase on capillary endothelium, activated by C-II","Chylomicron remnant taken up by the liver through apo E","VLDL from the liver with B-100","VLDL to IDL to LDL as triglyceride is removed","LDL taken up through the LDL receptor binding B-100","HDL with A-I activating LCAT and returning cholesterol to the liver","CETP swapping cholesteryl ester for triglyceride"]},{"t":"Nutrients the heart needs","p":"Build a table with three columns: nutrient, its biochemical job, and the heart or vessel problem when it is missing or in excess. Include thiamine, B12, folate, B6, vitamin C, potassium, magnesium, iron, and selenium.","l":["Thiamine: TPP for pyruvate dehydrogenase and related enzymes; wet beriberi high-output failure","B12 and folate: methionine synthase; high homocysteine","B6: cystathionine beta-synthase; high homocysteine","Vitamin C: collagen hydroxylation; fragile vessels in scurvy","Potassium: resting membrane potential; arrhythmias when low or high","Magnesium: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase; torsades and refractory hypokalemia","Iron: deficiency gives high-output state, overload gives cardiomyopathy","Selenium: deficiency gives Keshan cardiomyopathy"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on vitamins (water-soluble and fat-soluble micronutrients)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases, section on nutritional diseases"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Metabolism | Lipoprotein Metabolism | Chylomicrons, VLDL, IDL, LDL, & HDL","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wQY0xpwqPfQ"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hyperlipidemia | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gE9cIQ9vz0k"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Physiology of Lipoproteins Cholesterol","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkKH8lTxvzA"}],"related":["CV-5","CV-14","CV-18-I3","CV-18-I4","CV-18-C"]},"CV-14":{"id":"CV-14","summary":"This unit covers single-gene and chromosomal disorders that affect the heart and vessels, plus the gene-expression and cell-cycle ideas behind them. The exam asks you to match a gene or inheritance pattern to a cardiovascular finding and to explain the mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"Genetic principles seen in heart disease","points":["Most single-gene cardiovascular disorders are autosomal dominant (familial hypercholesterolemia, HCM, Marfan, most long QT).","Variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance are common, so relatives with the same variant can look very different.","Locus heterogeneity: one disease, many genes (familial hypercholesterolemia from LDLR, APOB, or PCSK9; long QT from many channel genes).","Pleiotropy: one gene, many organs (fibrillin-1 in Marfan affects aorta, eye, and skeleton).","Gene dosage matters: familial hypercholesterolemia homozygotes are far more severe than heterozygotes.","Mitochondrial DNA disorders can cause cardiomyopathy and conduction block (for example Kearns-Sayre syndrome); heteroplasmy explains variable severity."]},{"h":"Familial hypercholesterolemia","points":["Usually a mutation in the LDL receptor gene; APOB and gain-of-function PCSK9 variants cause the same picture.","Fewer working receptors means less LDL and IDL clearance, so more IDL becomes LDL.","Less cholesterol inside liver cells also takes the brake off cholesterol synthesis.","Heterozygotes have about two to three times normal plasma cholesterol; homozygotes about five to six times, often with MI before age 20.","Signs: tendon xanthomas (often Achilles), xanthelasma, early corneal arcus, premature coronary disease."]},{"h":"Cardiomyopathy and channel genes","points":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: sarcomere protein genes, most often MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) and MYBPC3 (myosin-binding protein C).","HCM shows myocyte disarray, asymmetric septal thickening, diastolic dysfunction, and a risk of sudden death in young athletes.","Congenital long QT comes from potassium channel loss (KCNQ1, KCNH2) or sodium channel gain (SCN5A), which delays repolarization and invites torsades de pointes.","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome is autosomal recessive long QT with sensorineural deafness; Romano-Ward syndrome is autosomal dominant without deafness.","Brugada syndrome: loss of function in SCN5A, ST elevation in V1 to V3 with a pseudo-right bundle branch block pattern, and sudden death risk.","Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy (dystrophin, X-linked) cause dilated cardiomyopathy; Friedreich ataxia (frataxin, GAA repeat) causes HCM."]},{"h":"Connective tissue disorders","points":["Marfan syndrome: FBN1 (fibrillin-1) on chromosome 15; faulty microfibrils weaken elastic tissue and increase TGF-beta signaling.","Marfan cardiovascular findings: aortic root dilation, aortic dissection, aortic regurgitation, mitral valve prolapse.","Loeys-Dietz syndrome (TGFBR1 or TGFBR2): aggressive aortic aneurysms, arterial tortuosity, bifid uvula, widely spaced eyes.","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (COL3A1, type III collagen): arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture."]},{"h":"Gene expression and the cell cycle in the heart","points":["Adult cardiomyocytes have largely left the cell cycle and renew very slowly, so dead myocardium is replaced by scar.","Under pressure or volume load, cardiomyocytes grow larger (hypertrophy) instead of dividing.","Hypertrophy reactivates fetal genes such as ANP, BNP, and beta-myosin heavy chain.","SREBP senses cell sterol levels and turns up LDL receptor and HMG-CoA reductase genes when cholesterol is low.","Smooth muscle cells in the intima proliferate in response to growth factors such as PDGF in atherosclerosis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Single-gene cardiovascular disorders","cols":["Disorder","Gene and protein","Inheritance","Key cardiovascular features"],"rows":[["Familial hypercholesterolemia","LDLR (most), APOB, or PCSK9 gain of function","Autosomal dominant","Very high LDL, xanthomas, premature coronary disease"],["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Sarcomere genes, most often MYH7 or MYBPC3","Autosomal dominant","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy, outflow obstruction, sudden death"],["Marfan syndrome","FBN1, fibrillin-1","Autosomal dominant","Aortic root dilation and dissection, mitral valve prolapse"],["Loeys-Dietz syndrome","TGFBR1 or TGFBR2","Autosomal dominant","Aortic aneurysm and dissection, arterial tortuosity"],["Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","COL3A1, type III collagen","Autosomal dominant","Arterial rupture"],["Duchenne muscular dystrophy","DMD, dystrophin","X-linked recessive","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],["Friedreich ataxia","FXN, frataxin (GAA repeat)","Autosomal recessive","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],["Homocystinuria (classic)","CBS, cystathionine beta-synthase","Autosomal recessive","Arterial and venous thrombosis"]]},{"title":"Congenital long QT types","cols":["Type","Gene","Channel effect","Classic trigger"],"rows":[["LQT1","KCNQ1","Loss of slow delayed rectifier K⁺ current (IKs)","Exercise, especially swimming"],["LQT2","KCNH2 (hERG)","Loss of rapid delayed rectifier K⁺ current (IKr)","Sudden loud noise or emotional stress"],["LQT3","SCN5A","Gain of function: persistent late Na⁺ current","Rest or sleep"]]},{"title":"Chromosomal syndromes and their heart lesions","cols":["Syndrome","Genetic cause","Typical cardiovascular lesion"],"rows":[["Down syndrome","Trisomy 21","Atrioventricular septal defect (endocardial cushion defect)"],["Turner syndrome","45,X","Bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation of the aorta"],["22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge)","Microdeletion at 22q11.2","Conotruncal defects: truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot"],["Williams syndrome","7q11.23 microdeletion including the elastin gene","Supravalvular aortic stenosis"],["Noonan syndrome","Often PTPN11","Pulmonary valve stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CATCH-22","text":"22q11.2 deletion: Cleft palate, Abnormal facies, Thymic aplasia, Cardiac defects (conotruncal), Hypocalcemia from parathyroid aplasia, on chromosome 22."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lens goes up in Marfan, down in homocystinuria","text":"Both can look tall and thin with a dislocated lens. Marfan lenses usually shift up and out; homocystinuria lenses shift down and in, and homocystinuria adds clots."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Receptor down, LDL up","text":"In familial hypercholesterolemia, picture the liver with half its LDL receptors. LDL piles up in plasma, and the cell, sensing too little cholesterol inside, keeps making more."}],"highYield":["Familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant; homozygotes can have MI before age 20.","MYH7 and MYBPC3 are the most common HCM genes; inheritance is autosomal dominant.","Marfan syndrome is caused by FBN1 mutations; aortic dissection is the main cause of death.","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome: long QT plus sensorineural deafness, autosomal recessive.","Down syndrome is most linked to atrioventricular septal defects.","Turner syndrome is linked to bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation.","Williams syndrome causes supravalvular aortic stenosis through elastin gene loss.","Hypertrophied myocytes switch back on fetal genes such as ANP, BNP, and beta-myosin heavy chain."],"pitfalls":["Marfan (FBN1) and Loeys-Dietz (TGFBR1 or TGFBR2) both cause aortic aneurysm; Loeys-Dietz adds bifid uvula, widely spaced eyes, and arterial tortuosity.","Long QT from KCNQ1 and KCNH2 is a loss of potassium current, while SCN5A long QT is a gain of sodium current; Brugada is a loss of SCN5A function.","Cardiomyocytes respond to load by hypertrophy, while vascular smooth muscle cells can still proliferate.","Gain-of-function PCSK9 raises LDL; loss-of-function PCSK9 lowers it, because PCSK9 sends LDL receptors to be degraded."],"dumps":[{"t":"Single-gene heart disorders","p":"Build a table on blank paper for familial hypercholesterolemia, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, Marfan syndrome, congenital long QT, and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. Give the gene, protein, inheritance, and main cardiovascular finding for each.","l":["Familial hypercholesterolemia: LDLR (or APOB, PCSK9), autosomal dominant, premature coronary disease","HCM: MYH7 or MYBPC3, autosomal dominant, asymmetric septal hypertrophy and sudden death","Marfan: FBN1, fibrillin-1, autosomal dominant, aortic root dilation and dissection","Long QT: KCNQ1, KCNH2, or SCN5A, mostly autosomal dominant, torsades de pointes","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos: COL3A1, type III collagen, autosomal dominant, arterial rupture","Notes variable expressivity or incomplete penetrance in at least one","Notes that FH homozygotes are much more severe than heterozygotes"]},{"t":"LDL receptor cycle","p":"Draw a liver cell taking up LDL through its receptor. Show what happens to the receptor and to cholesterol inside the cell. Then redraw it for a person with familial hypercholesterolemia.","l":["LDL apo B-100 binds the LDL receptor in clathrin-coated pits","Receptor and LDL are endocytosed and separate in the endosome","Receptor recycles to the cell surface","LDL is broken down in the lysosome, releasing free cholesterol","Free cholesterol lowers SREBP activity, reducing HMG-CoA reductase and LDL receptor synthesis","PCSK9 directs receptors to degradation","In FH, fewer working receptors means less LDL and IDL clearance and more cholesterol synthesis","Result: heterozygotes about two to three times normal cholesterol, homozygotes about five to six times"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on cardiomyopathies"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy - signs and symptoms, causes, pathophysiology, treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lqn71jvMhQM"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: Pathophysiology and diagnosis | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtqrKxJmBn4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiomyopathy | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Miz9kvrrqk"}],"related":["CV-13","CV-18-E1","CV-18-G","CV-18-I2","CV-18-I4"]},"CV-15":{"id":"CV-15","summary":"The right heart pumps its whole output through the lungs, and the left heart receives it back, so the two organs work as one circuit. Exam items test how the pulmonary circulation differs from the systemic one, how breathing changes venous return, and how disease in one organ overloads the other.","notes":[{"h":"Two circuits in series","points":["Right and left ventricular outputs are equal over time (about 5 L/min at rest).","Pulmonary artery pressure is about 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15 mmHg); systemic is about 120/80 mmHg.","Pulmonary vascular resistance is much lower than systemic, roughly one tenth.","Pulmonary capillary pressure is about 7 mmHg, which keeps fluid out of the alveoli.","Bronchial arteries (systemic) feed the airways; part of their venous blood drains into pulmonary veins, a small normal shunt."]},{"h":"How pulmonary vessels are controlled","points":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: low alveolar O2 constricts nearby arterioles and sends blood to better ventilated alveoli.","Systemic arterioles do the opposite and dilate in hypoxia.","Chronic global hypoxia (COPD, high altitude) constricts vessels throughout the lung and causes pulmonary hypertension.","When cardiac output rises, closed capillaries open (recruitment) and open ones widen (distension), so resistance falls.","Resistance is lowest near FRC; at high lung volume alveolar vessels are stretched and compressed, at low volume extra-alveolar vessels narrow."]},{"h":"Gravity and the zones of the lung","points":["Upright, the base gets more blood flow and more ventilation than the apex; flow rises more steeply.","V/Q is about 3 at the apex and about 0.6 at the base; the whole-lung average is about 0.8.","Zone 1 (no flow) is normally absent; it appears with low arterial pressure (hemorrhage) or high alveolar pressure (positive pressure ventilation)."]},{"h":"Breathing changes the heart","points":["Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, raises venous return to the right heart, and raises right ventricular stroke volume.","At the same time pulmonary veins hold more blood, so left ventricular filling and systolic pressure dip slightly (under 10 mmHg).","A drop over 10 mmHg is pulsus paradoxus: cardiac tamponade, severe asthma, or COPD.","Inspiration delays pulmonic valve closure, which gives normal (physiologic) splitting of S2.","Positive pressure ventilation raises intrathoracic pressure, lowers venous return, and can lower cardiac output."]},{"h":"When one organ fails, the other is loaded","points":["Left heart failure or mitral stenosis raises left atrial and pulmonary capillary pressure and causes pulmonary edema.","Alveolar edema appears once pulmonary capillary pressure exceeds plasma colloid osmotic pressure (about 28 mmHg).","Chronic lung disease or hypoxia raises pulmonary artery pressure and causes right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (cor pulmonale).","A large pulmonary embolus suddenly raises right ventricular afterload (acute cor pulmonale).","A right-to-left shunt sends venous blood past the lungs, so hypoxemia barely improves with 100% O2."]},{"h":"The lung as a metabolic organ","points":["ACE on pulmonary capillary endothelium converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II.","The same enzyme inactivates bradykinin.","The pulmonary capillary bed filters small clots and debris from venous blood before it reaches the systemic arteries."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pulmonary vs systemic circulation","cols":["Feature","Pulmonary circulation","Systemic circulation"],"rows":[["Arterial pressure","About 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15)","About 120/80 mmHg (mean about 93)"],["Resistance","Low","High (roughly 10 times pulmonary)"],["Artery wall","Thin, little smooth muscle, very compliant","Thick muscular media"],["Capillary hydrostatic pressure","About 7 mmHg","About 17 mmHg (average functional value)"],["Response to local hypoxia","Vasoconstriction (matches flow to ventilation)","Vasodilation (matches flow to tissue need)"],["Flow","Entire right ventricular output","Entire left ventricular output, shared among organs"]]},{"title":"Zones of the upright lung","cols":["Zone","Pressure order","Blood flow","Where"],"rows":[["Zone 1","Alveolar > arterial > venous","None (alveolar dead space)","Apex, only if arterial pressure falls or alveolar pressure rises"],["Zone 2","Arterial > alveolar > venous","Intermittent, set by arterial minus alveolar pressure","Upper and middle lung"],["Zone 3","Arterial > venous > alveolar","Continuous, set by arterial minus venous pressure","Lower lung (base)"]]},{"title":"Dead space vs shunt","cols":["Feature","Dead space","Shunt"],"rows":[["V/Q","High (ventilation with no perfusion)","Zero (perfusion with no ventilation)"],["Examples","Pulmonary embolism, zone 1","Atelectasis, lobar pneumonia, pulmonary edema, right-to-left cardiac shunt"],["Effect of 100% O2","Hypoxemia usually improves","Hypoxemia improves very little"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Hypoxia flips in the lung","text":"Everywhere else, low O2 dilates arterioles to bring in more blood. In the lung, low O2 constricts them so blood goes where the air is."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Alveolar pressure walks down the line","text":"Write arterial, venous, alveolar. Zone 1 puts alveolar first, zone 2 puts it second, zone 3 puts it last."},{"kind":"trick","label":"The 100% O2 test","text":"If extra oxygen fixes the hypoxemia, think V/Q mismatch or diffusion. If it barely helps, think shunt."}],"highYield":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is the main local control of pulmonary blood flow.","Chronic hypoxic lung disease leads to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.","V/Q is highest at the apex and lowest at the base.","Inspiration raises right heart filling and splits S2.","Pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg points to cardiac tamponade or severe obstructive lung disease.","Pulmonary ACE makes angiotensin II and breaks down bradykinin.","A right-to-left shunt causes hypoxemia that 100% O2 barely corrects."],"pitfalls":["Cor pulmonale means right heart disease caused by lung disease or pulmonary vessels; right heart failure from left heart failure does not count.","Pulmonary embolism is a dead-space (high V/Q) problem in the blocked area, yet hypoxemia comes from low V/Q in the overperfused areas.","Both blood flow and ventilation are greater at the base; V/Q is lower there because flow increases more than ventilation."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lung zones","p":"Draw an upright lung. Mark zones 1, 2, and 3, write the pressure order for each, and label where V/Q is highest and lowest.","l":["Zone 1: alveolar > arterial > venous, no flow","Zone 2: arterial > alveolar > venous, flow set by arterial minus alveolar pressure","Zone 3: arterial > venous > alveolar, continuous flow","Zone 1 normally absent; appears with hemorrhage or positive pressure ventilation","V/Q about 3 at apex","V/Q about 0.6 at base","Flow and ventilation both rise toward the base, flow more steeply"]},{"t":"From a sick lung to a failing right heart","p":"Trace the steps from chronic COPD to right heart failure. Then list the signs you would find on exam.","l":["Alveolar hypoxia","Widespread hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Vessel remodeling (medial hypertrophy) raises pulmonary vascular resistance","Pulmonary hypertension raises right ventricular afterload","Right ventricular hypertrophy, then dilation and failure (cor pulmonale)","Raised jugular venous pressure","Hepatomegaly and peripheral pitting edema","Loud P2 and right ventricular heave"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Principles of Gas Exchange; Diffusion of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide Through the Respiratory Membrane (section on ventilation-perfusion ratio)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Respiratory | External Respiration: Ventilation Perfusion Coupling","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yUtngVm93-c"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Respiratory System Physiology - Ventilation and Perfusion (V:Q Ratio) Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mL_NQ3pKnA"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJ1gu_WRx5o"}],"related":["CV-11","CV-18-A1","CV-18-B2","CV-18-I7"]},"CV-16":{"id":"CV-16","summary":"Many heart and vessel diseases are driven by the immune system: atherosclerosis is chronic inflammation, rheumatic fever is molecular mimicry, and most vasculitis is antibody, immune complex, or T-cell injury. The exam asks you to match each disease to its hypersensitivity type and immune cell.","notes":[{"h":"Atherosclerosis is chronic inflammation","points":["Injured endothelium expresses VCAM-1 and recruits monocytes and T cells into the intima.","LDL trapped in the intima is oxidized; macrophages take it up through scavenger receptors and become foam cells.","Scavenger receptors are not shut off by cell cholesterol, so foam cells keep filling.","Cholesterol crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome, which releases IL-1 beta.","Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages; PDGF draws smooth muscle cells in to build the fibrous cap.","Macrophage metalloproteinases thin the cap and make the plaque prone to rupture.","C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) is a liver-made marker of this inflammation and predicts cardiac risk."]},{"h":"Rheumatic fever: molecular mimicry","points":["Follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 3 weeks.","Antibodies and T cells against the streptococcal M protein cross-react with heart antigens such as cardiac myosin.","Review books classify it as type II hypersensitivity; CD4 T cells also drive the valve damage.","Acute lesion: Aschoff bodies with Anitschkow (caterpillar) cells; pancarditis.","ASO and anti-DNase B titers show the recent strep infection."]},{"h":"Vasculitis by immune mechanism","points":["Immune complex (type III): polyarteritis nodosa (often hepatitis B), IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis (hepatitis C), lupus vasculitis.","ANCA-associated (few immune deposits): granulomatosis with polyangiitis (PR3, c-ANCA), microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (MPO, p-ANCA).","ANCA bind neutrophil enzymes on primed neutrophils and trigger degranulation that injures the vessel wall.","Anti-GBM disease is type II: antibodies to type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes.","T-cell granulomatous arteritis (type IV): giant cell arteritis and Takayasu arteritis."]},{"h":"Heart transplant rejection","points":["Hyperacute: preformed antibodies (ABO or HLA) bind graft endothelium within minutes, causing thrombosis.","Acute cellular: CD8 and CD4 T cells attack the myocardium, weeks to months after transplant.","Acute antibody-mediated: antibodies to donor HLA injure graft vessels.","Chronic: cardiac allograft vasculopathy, a diffuse concentric intimal thickening of coronary arteries."]},{"h":"Other immune heart disease","points":["Viral myocarditis: direct viral injury, then a T-cell response that can lead to dilated cardiomyopathy.","Hypersensitivity myocarditis: drug reaction with eosinophils in the myocardium.","Dressler syndrome: autoimmune pericarditis weeks after MI, against myocardial antigens released by necrosis.","Libman-Sacks endocarditis in lupus: sterile vegetations on both sides of the valve, usually mitral.","Maternal anti-Ro (SSA) antibodies cross the placenta and cause congenital heart block.","Rare myocarditis after mRNA COVID-19 vaccines, mostly in young males within days of a dose."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypersensitivity types in cardiovascular disease","cols":["Type","Mechanism","Cardiovascular examples"],"rows":[["I (immediate)","IgE on mast cells; histamine causes vasodilation and capillary leak","Anaphylactic shock"],["II (antibody to fixed antigen)","IgG or IgM binds cell or matrix antigens; complement and phagocytes injure tissue","Acute rheumatic fever, hyperacute rejection, anti-GBM disease"],["III (immune complex)","Antigen-antibody complexes deposit in vessel walls, fix complement, draw neutrophils","Polyarteritis nodosa, IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, serum sickness, lupus vasculitis"],["IV (T-cell mediated)","CD4 Th1 cells activate macrophages; CD8 cells kill target cells","Acute cellular rejection, giant cell arteritis, Takayasu arteritis"]]},{"title":"Heart transplant rejection","cols":["Type","Timing","Main mechanism","Finding in the graft"],"rows":[["Hyperacute","Minutes to hours","Preformed anti-ABO or anti-HLA antibodies, complement","Graft thrombosis and ischemic necrosis"],["Acute cellular","Weeks to months","Donor-reactive CD8 and CD4 T cells","Lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte damage"],["Acute antibody-mediated","Weeks to months","New antibodies to donor HLA","Capillary injury with complement (C4d) deposits"],["Chronic","Months to years","T cells, antibodies, and cytokines acting on vessel walls","Cardiac allograft vasculopathy (concentric intimal thickening)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ACID","text":"Hypersensitivity types I to IV: Anaphylactic and Atopic (I), Cytotoxic antibody-mediated (II), Immune complex (III), Delayed cell-mediated (IV)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Throat vs skin","text":"Rheumatic fever follows strep throat only. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis can follow throat or skin infection."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Rejection clock","text":"Minutes means preformed antibody, weeks means T cells, years means vessel narrowing."}],"highYield":["Rheumatic fever: M protein antibodies cross-react with cardiac myosin (molecular mimicry).","Aschoff bodies are the hallmark of acute rheumatic carditis.","Foam cells form through scavenger receptor uptake of oxidized LDL.","Polyarteritis nodosa is linked to hepatitis B and spares the lungs.","c-ANCA (PR3) points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis; p-ANCA (MPO) to microscopic polyangiitis or EGPA.","Chronic heart transplant rejection appears as accelerated coronary artery narrowing.","Maternal anti-Ro antibodies cause neonatal complete heart block."],"pitfalls":["Rheumatic fever vegetations are small and sterile along the closure line; infective endocarditis vegetations are large, bulky, and contain organisms.","Dressler syndrome comes weeks after MI and is autoimmune; fibrinous pericarditis in the first few days comes from the necrosis itself.","ANCA vasculitis is called pauci-immune because few immune deposits are seen, which separates it from immune complex vasculitis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypersensitivity table","p":"On blank paper, build a four-row table for hypersensitivity types I to IV. For each, write the immune effector and two cardiovascular examples.","l":["Type I: IgE and mast cells; anaphylactic shock","Type II: IgG or IgM against fixed antigens","Type II examples: rheumatic fever, hyperacute rejection, anti-GBM","Type III: immune complexes deposit in vessel walls","Type III examples: polyarteritis nodosa, IgA vasculitis, serum sickness","Type IV: T cells and macrophages","Type IV examples: acute cellular rejection, giant cell arteritis"]},{"t":"Plaque as inflammation","p":"Draw a coronary artery wall and show how a plaque forms. Label each immune cell and molecule in the order it acts.","l":["Endothelial injury and VCAM-1 expression","Monocytes enter the intima and become macrophages","LDL is oxidized in the intima","Scavenger receptor uptake makes foam cells","IL-1 and the NLRP3 inflammasome","Th1 cells and IFN-gamma","PDGF draws smooth muscle cells to form the fibrous cap","Metalloproteinases thin the cap and promote rupture"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity: Disorders Caused by Immune Responses"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on transplant rejection"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (sections on atherosclerosis and vasculitis)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypersensitivity Type II (Cytotoxic reaction) - causes, pathophysiology, complement system","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Em0Wi07dj4E"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypersensitivity Type III (Immune complex hypersensitivity) - causes,  pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NKnAXcM5Ly0"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Vasculitis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xHRwhK835QE"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Rheumatic Fever & Heart Disease | Pathology | Dr Najeeb🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ivIE8ARRIgM"}],"related":["CV-18-D5","CV-18-I3","CV-18-I12","CV-18-E2","CV-17"]},"CV-17":{"id":"CV-17","summary":"This unit covers the organisms behind the listed cardiovascular infections: the bacteria of infective endocarditis, Trypanosoma cruzi, Borrelia burgdorferi, Rickettsia rickettsii, the hemorrhagic fever viruses, and the myocarditis viruses. Questions pair each organism with its shape, vector, life cycle, and the virulence factor that explains the disease.","notes":[{"h":"Infective endocarditis bacteria","points":["Staphylococcus aureus: gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase and coagulase positive; acute endocarditis on normal valves and in IV drug use.","S. aureus virulence: protein A binds the Fc end of IgG; clumping factor and fibronectin-binding proteins help it stick to valves.","Viridans streptococci: alpha-hemolytic, optochin resistant; subacute endocarditis on damaged valves after dental work.","Viridans strep make dextran, which binds fibrin-platelet clumps on damaged valves.","Enterococcus faecalis: grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile; endocarditis after GI or GU procedures.","Staphylococcus epidermidis: coagulase negative, makes biofilm on prosthetic valves and devices.","Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis) endocarditis is linked to colon cancer."]},{"h":"Trypanosoma cruzi (Chagas disease)","points":["Flagellated protozoan; spread by triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bugs in Latin America.","The bug defecates while feeding; trypomastigotes in the feces enter the bite wound or the conjunctiva (Romana sign).","Also spread by blood transfusion, organ transplant, mother to fetus, and contaminated food.","Trypomastigotes circulate in blood and do not divide; amastigotes multiply inside heart muscle, smooth muscle, and macrophages.","Chronic disease years later: dilated cardiomyopathy with apical aneurysm and conduction block, megaesophagus, and megacolon."]},{"h":"Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme disease)","points":["Spirochete; stains poorly with Gram stain, seen with Giemsa or Wright stain.","Vector: Ixodes ticks (black-legged deer tick); white-footed mouse is the main reservoir; deer feed the adult ticks.","Transmission usually needs about 36 to 48 hours of tick attachment.","Switches outer surface proteins: OspA in the tick gut, OspC when it moves to the host.","Antigenic variation of the VlsE surface protein helps it avoid antibodies.","Unusual genome: a linear chromosome plus many linear and circular plasmids.","Lyme carditis (early disseminated stage) causes AV block of varying degree."]},{"h":"Rickettsia rickettsii (Rocky Mountain spotted fever)","points":["Small gram-negative obligate intracellular bacterium; cannot make enough of its own CoA and NAD+.","Vector: Dermacentor ticks (American dog tick, Rocky Mountain wood tick); most US cases are in the southeast and south-central states.","Infects vascular endothelial cells and spreads cell to cell using host actin.","Endothelial infection causes vasculitis, leaky vessels, edema, low blood pressure, and thrombocytopenia.","Rash starts on wrists and ankles, spreads to the trunk, and involves palms and soles."]},{"h":"Viral hemorrhagic fever viruses","points":["Yellow fever and dengue: flaviviruses (enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA), spread by Aedes aegypti mosquitoes.","Yellow fever damages hepatocytes (Councilman bodies) and causes jaundice and bleeding; a live attenuated vaccine prevents it.","Dengue has four serotypes; a second infection with a different serotype raises the risk of severe dengue through antibody-dependent enhancement.","Ebola and Marburg: filoviruses (enveloped, filamentous, negative-sense single-stranded RNA); fruit bats are the likely reservoir; people spread it through body fluids.","Filoviruses infect macrophages, dendritic cells, and endothelium; Ebola VP35 blocks type I interferon.","Common end path: endothelial leak, cytokine storm, and consumptive coagulopathy (DIC)."]},{"h":"Myocarditis viruses","points":["Coxsackie B virus (enterovirus, picornavirus family) is the classic cause; spread mainly fecal-oral.","Coxsackie B enters cardiomyocytes through the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR); its protease 2A cleaves dystrophin.","Adenovirus uses the same CAR receptor and is common in children.","Parvovirus B19 and HHV-6 are now frequently found in heart biopsies; parvovirus infects endothelial cells."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infective endocarditis organisms","cols":["Organism","Morphology and lab clues","Typical setting","Key virulence factor or clue"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Gram-positive cocci in clusters; catalase and coagulase positive","Acute endocarditis, normal valves, IV drug use (tricuspid)","Protein A, clumping factor, fibronectin-binding proteins"],["Viridans streptococci","Gram-positive cocci in chains; alpha-hemolytic; optochin resistant","Subacute endocarditis on damaged valves after dental work","Dextran binds fibrin-platelet clumps"],["Enterococcus faecalis","Gram-positive cocci in pairs and chains; grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile","After GI or GU procedures; older adults","Intrinsic resistance to many antibiotics"],["Staphylococcus epidermidis","Gram-positive cocci in clusters; coagulase negative","Prosthetic valves and devices","Biofilm"],["Streptococcus gallolyticus","Gram-positive cocci; group D streptococcus; bile esculin positive","Older adults with colorectal neoplasia","Look for a colon tumor"],["HACEK group","Fastidious gram-negative rods","Culture-negative endocarditis","Slow growth delays diagnosis"]]},{"title":"Vector-borne and other systemic cardiovascular pathogens","cols":["Organism","Type and morphology","Vector or route","Life cycle or spread in the body","Cardiovascular effect"],"rows":[["Trypanosoma cruzi","Flagellated protozoan","Triatomine bug feces; transfusion; congenital","Trypomastigote in blood; amastigote divides inside cells","Dilated cardiomyopathy, apical aneurysm, conduction block"],["Borrelia burgdorferi","Spirochete","Ixodes tick","Spreads from bite site through skin and blood","AV block (Lyme carditis)"],["Rickettsia rickettsii","Gram-negative obligate intracellular rod","Dermacentor tick","Infects endothelium; spreads cell to cell by actin","Vasculitis, capillary leak, hypotension"],["Yellow fever virus","Flavivirus, enveloped, positive-sense ssRNA","Aedes aegypti mosquito","Replicates in liver and lymphoid tissue","Hemorrhage, shock"],["Dengue virus","Flavivirus, enveloped, positive-sense ssRNA (4 serotypes)","Aedes mosquitoes","Infects monocytes and dendritic cells","Plasma leakage, shock, bleeding"],["Ebola and Marburg viruses","Filovirus, enveloped, filamentous, negative-sense ssRNA","Bats to humans; body fluids person to person","Infects macrophages, dendritic cells, endothelium","Capillary leak, DIC, shock"]]},{"title":"Common myocarditis viruses","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Transmission","Notes"],"rows":[["Coxsackie B","Picornavirus (enterovirus); naked, positive-sense ssRNA","Fecal-oral, respiratory","Uses CAR; protease 2A cleaves dystrophin; also causes pericarditis"],["Adenovirus","Adenovirus; naked, double-stranded DNA","Respiratory droplets, fecal-oral","Uses CAR; common cause in children"],["Parvovirus B19","Parvovirus; naked, single-stranded DNA","Respiratory, blood, mother to fetus","Infects endothelial cells and red cell precursors"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HACEK","text":"Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter (formerly Actinobacillus), Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella: fastidious gram-negative causes of culture-negative endocarditis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Match the procedure to the bug","text":"Dental work brings viridans strep. GI or GU procedures bring Enterococcus. A new prosthetic valve brings S. epidermidis. IV drug use brings S. aureus to the tricuspid valve."},{"kind":"trick","label":"RMSF rash travels inward","text":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever starts at the wrists and ankles and moves to the trunk. Typhus rash does the reverse, starting on the trunk."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the most common cause of acute endocarditis and of endocarditis in IV drug users.","Viridans strep dextran lets it stick to fibrin-platelet clumps on damaged valves.","S. gallolyticus endocarditis calls for a colonoscopy to look for colon cancer.","Chagas disease: triatomine bug, Latin America, dilated cardiomyopathy plus megaesophagus and megacolon.","Lyme carditis presents as AV block.","R. rickettsii infects endothelium and causes a vasculitis with a rash on palms and soles.","Dengue: secondary infection with a different serotype causes severe disease through antibody-dependent enhancement.","Coxsackie B is the classic cause of viral myocarditis and pericarditis."],"pitfalls":["Viridans strep and S. pneumoniae are both alpha-hemolytic; S. pneumoniae is optochin sensitive and bile soluble, viridans strep is neither.","Ixodes ticks carry Borrelia; Dermacentor ticks carry Rickettsia rickettsii.","Despite the name, most RMSF cases in the US occur in the southeast and south-central states.","Flaviviruses (yellow fever, dengue) are positive-sense RNA; filoviruses (Ebola, Marburg) are negative-sense RNA."],"dumps":[{"t":"Chagas life cycle","p":"Draw the life cycle of Trypanosoma cruzi between the triatomine bug and a human. Label each form and where it lives.","l":["Triatomine bug takes a blood meal","Bug feces contain metacyclic trypomastigotes","Trypomastigotes enter through the bite wound or conjunctiva","Inside host cells they become amastigotes and divide","Cells burst and release trypomastigotes into the blood","A bug takes up trypomastigotes in a blood meal","In the bug gut they become epimastigotes and multiply","Target tissues: heart muscle, GI smooth muscle and nerve plexus"]},{"t":"Endocarditis organism table","p":"Build a table on blank paper for S. aureus, viridans strep, Enterococcus, and S. epidermidis. Include the lab clues, the typical patient, and one virulence factor for each.","l":["S. aureus: clusters, coagulase positive","S. aureus: acute, normal valves, IV drug use","S. aureus: protein A or coagulase","Viridans strep: alpha-hemolytic, optochin resistant","Viridans strep: damaged valves, dental work, dextran","Enterococcus: grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile; GI or GU procedures","S. epidermidis: coagulase negative; prosthetic valve; biofilm"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spirochetes and Other Spiral Microorganisms"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Rickettsia and Related Genera"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology (section on Trypanosoma cruzi)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Infective Endocarditis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iaO8110iSzI"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Staphylococcus: Aureus, Epidermidis, Saprophyticus","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6BkqWKOG8E0"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Infective Endocarditis | Cardiac Pathology | Cardiology🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lyiLcpxa2FU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is myocarditis and pericarditis? | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xL98lBuAn1A"}],"related":["CV-18-K","CV-18-D3","CV-18-E2","CV-16"]},"CV-18-A":{"id":"CV-18-A","summary":"Systemic hypertension loads the left ventricle and damages arterioles in the brain, kidney, retina, and heart. Pulmonary hypertension loads the right ventricle and ends in right heart failure. Know the definitions, the main causes, the vessel changes, and the organ damage for each.","notes":[{"h":"Systemic hypertension: definition and causes","points":["ACC/AHA 2017: stage 1 is 130 to 139 or 80 to 89 mmHg; stage 2 is 140/90 mmHg or higher.","Primary (essential) hypertension is about 90 to 95% of cases; it is polygenic with environmental triggers.","Risk factors: older age, family history, obesity, high sodium intake, excess alcohol, inactivity, diabetes.","Secondary causes: renal artery stenosis, kidney disease, primary aldosteronism, pheochromocytoma, Cushing syndrome, coarctation, sleep apnea.","Renal artery stenosis: atherosclerosis in older adults, fibromuscular dysplasia in younger women."]},{"h":"Systemic hypertension: vessel and heart changes","points":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis: pink glassy arteriole walls from plasma protein leak; seen in chronic hypertension and diabetes.","Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis: onion-skin wall layers, often with fibrinoid necrosis, in severe (malignant) hypertension.","Pressure overload causes concentric left ventricular hypertrophy (sarcomeres added in parallel).","The thick, stiff ventricle fills poorly (diastolic dysfunction), enlarging the left atrium and raising the risk of atrial fibrillation.","Hypertensive emergency: severe pressure (often 180/120 mmHg or higher) with acute organ damage."]},{"h":"Pulmonary hypertension: definition and groups","points":["Mean pulmonary artery pressure over 20 mmHg at rest by right heart catheterization (older cutoff 25 mmHg).","Left heart disease (group 2) is the most common cause.","Pulmonary arterial hypertension (group 1) includes idiopathic, heritable (BMPR2), drug-related, connective tissue disease, HIV, and portal hypertension.","BMPR2 loss-of-function mutations allow smooth muscle proliferation in small pulmonary arteries.","Endothelial imbalance: low NO and prostacyclin, high endothelin-1."]},{"h":"Pulmonary hypertension: pathology and findings","points":["Medial hypertrophy and intimal fibrosis of small arteries; plexiform lesions in severe pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Atherosclerosis can appear in the large pulmonary arteries.","Symptoms: exertional dyspnea, fatigue, chest pain, syncope.","Signs: loud P2, right ventricular heave, tricuspid regurgitation murmur, raised jugular venous pressure, edema.","ECG: right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy."]},{"h":"Complications","points":["Systemic: stroke (including intracerebral hemorrhage from Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms), coronary disease and MI, heart failure.","Systemic: aortic dissection, abdominal aortic aneurysm, chronic kidney disease (nephrosclerosis), retinopathy.","Pulmonary: right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (cor pulmonale), arrhythmias, sudden death."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypertensive heart diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Definition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Pulmonary hypertension","Mean pulmonary artery pressure over 20 mmHg at rest","Left heart disease, chronic lung disease and hypoxia, chronic thromboembolism, BMPR2 mutation, drugs, connective tissue disease","Vasoconstriction and remodeling of small pulmonary arteries raise resistance and right ventricular afterload","Exertional dyspnea, syncope, loud P2, right ventricular heave, raised JVP, edema","Cor pulmonale, right heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden death"],["Systemic hypertension","130/80 mmHg or higher (stage 1); 140/90 mmHg or higher (stage 2)","Primary in about 90 to 95%; secondary from kidney, adrenal, or vascular disease","Raised cardiac output or peripheral resistance with sodium retention and RAAS activity","Usually silent; S4, concentric LVH, retinal arteriolar narrowing and AV nicking","Stroke, MI, heart failure, aortic dissection, kidney disease, retinopathy"]]},{"title":"Pulmonary hypertension groups (WHO)","cols":["Group","Cause","Examples"],"rows":[["1","Pulmonary arterial hypertension","Idiopathic, heritable (BMPR2), drugs and toxins, systemic sclerosis, HIV, portal hypertension, congenital shunts"],["2","Left heart disease (most common)","Left ventricular failure, mitral or aortic valve disease"],["3","Lung disease or hypoxia","COPD, interstitial lung disease, sleep apnea, high altitude"],["4","Pulmonary artery obstruction","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension"],["5","Unclear or multiple mechanisms","Sarcoidosis, myeloproliferative disorders"]]},{"title":"Arteriolosclerosis in hypertension","cols":["Type","Look","Setting"],"rows":[["Hyaline","Homogeneous pink thickened wall, narrow lumen","Long-standing mild hypertension, diabetes, aging"],["Hyperplastic","Concentric onion-skin layers, often fibrinoid necrosis","Severe (malignant) hypertension"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Concentric means pressure","text":"Pressure overload (hypertension, aortic stenosis) thickens the wall inward. Volume overload (regurgitation) stretches the chamber outward, which is eccentric hypertrophy."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Order of PH groups","text":"List the groups in order: 1 disease of the pulmonary arteries themselves, 2 left heart disease, 3 lung disease or hypoxia, 4 clots blocking the arteries, 5 everything else. Group 2 is the most common."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Onion skin means emergency","text":"Hyaline arterioles mean long, mild pressure. Onion-skin arterioles with fibrinoid necrosis mean malignant hypertension."}],"highYield":["Essential hypertension makes up about 90 to 95% of cases.","Primary aldosteronism is the most common endocrine cause of secondary hypertension: high blood pressure, high aldosterone, low renin, and sometimes low potassium.","Hypertension is the main risk factor for aortic dissection and intracerebral hemorrhage.","Concentric LVH is the heart's response to chronic pressure overload.","BMPR2 mutations cause heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Plexiform lesions mark severe pulmonary arterial hypertension.","The most common cause of pulmonary hypertension is left heart disease."],"pitfalls":["Cor pulmonale is right heart disease from lung or pulmonary vascular disease; pulmonary hypertension from left heart failure is group 2.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis appears in both hypertension and diabetes; onion-skin change points to malignant hypertension.","Fibromuscular dysplasia (young women, string of beads) and atherosclerosis (older adults, ostial narrowing) both cause renal artery stenosis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypertension end-organ map","p":"Draw a body outline. Mark every organ that long-term systemic hypertension damages, and write the lesion found in each.","l":["Brain: ischemic stroke and intracerebral hemorrhage (Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms)","Eye: retinopathy with AV nicking, hemorrhages, cotton wool spots; papilledema if malignant","Heart: concentric LVH, diastolic dysfunction, coronary disease","Aorta: dissection and aneurysm","Kidney: hyaline arteriolosclerosis and nephrosclerosis","Malignant hypertension: hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis","Peripheral arteries: atherosclerosis and PAD"]},{"t":"Pulmonary hypertension pathway","p":"Trace how pulmonary arterial hypertension leads to right heart failure, starting with a BMPR2 mutation.","l":["BMPR2 loss of function","Endothelial imbalance: less NO and prostacyclin, more endothelin-1","Smooth muscle proliferation and medial hypertrophy","Intimal fibrosis and plexiform lesions narrow the lumen","Pulmonary vascular resistance rises","Right ventricular hypertrophy (pressure overload)","Right ventricular dilation and failure","Raised JVP, hepatomegaly, peripheral edema"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on hypertensive heart disease, systemic and pulmonary)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (section on hypertensive vascular disease)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Role of the Kidneys in Long-Term Control of Arterial Pressure and in Hypertension: The Integrated System for Arterial Pressure Regulation"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hypertension | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sNCtvsY3TS8"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Pulmonary Artery Hypertension | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S8ehpYrnX2w"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is hypertension? | Circulatory system diseases | Health & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FBoYigdnLKQ"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Pulmonary hypertension symptoms and causes | Respiratory system diseases | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZVl1M3D2B80"}],"related":["CV-15","CV-11","CV-18-B","CV-18-I2"]},"CV-18-B":{"id":"CV-18-B","summary":"Heart failure means the heart cannot pump enough blood for the body's needs, or can only do so at high filling pressures. Left-sided failure backs blood up into the lungs; right-sided failure backs it up into the body's veins. Know the causes, the compensations that turn harmful, and the classic findings on each side.","notes":[{"h":"Left-sided heart failure","points":["Main causes: ischemic heart disease, hypertension, aortic and mitral valve disease, cardiomyopathy.","Blood backs up into the pulmonary veins and capillaries and causes pulmonary congestion and edema.","Symptoms: dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea.","Signs: basal crackles, S3, displaced apex beat, pleural effusions.","Lungs show hemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells) from leaked red cells.","Low forward output reduces kidney perfusion and activates RAAS (prerenal azotemia); low brain perfusion causes confusion."]},{"h":"Right-sided heart failure","points":["Most common cause is left-sided heart failure.","Isolated causes: cor pulmonale (lung disease, pulmonary embolism, pulmonary hypertension), right ventricular infarct, tricuspid or pulmonic valve disease.","Blood backs up into the systemic veins: raised JVP, hepatojugular reflux, dependent pitting edema.","Liver: congestive hepatomegaly with nutmeg pattern (centrilobular congestion and necrosis); long-standing cases develop cardiac cirrhosis.","Also ascites, splenomegaly, and pleural and pericardial effusions."]},{"h":"Ejection fraction types","points":["HFrEF: ejection fraction 40% or lower; the ventricle contracts poorly (systolic failure).","HFpEF: ejection fraction 50% or higher; the stiff ventricle fills poorly (diastolic failure).","Ejection fraction 41 to 49% is called mildly reduced."]},{"h":"Compensation that turns harmful","points":["Frank-Starling: more filling stretches fibers and raises stroke volume until the curve flattens.","Sympathetic activation raises heart rate and contractility but increases O2 demand and arrhythmia risk.","RAAS and ADH retain salt and water, which raises preload and causes edema.","Angiotensin II and aldosterone promote fibrosis and remodeling.","Natriuretic peptides (ANP from atria, BNP from ventricles) are released with stretch and partly oppose RAAS; BNP is a diagnostic marker."]},{"h":"Complications","points":["Acute pulmonary edema and cardiogenic shock.","Arrhythmias (atrial fibrillation, ventricular tachycardia) and sudden death.","Mural thrombi in dilated chambers that can embolize.","Cardiorenal syndrome and cardiac cachexia."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Left vs right heart failure","cols":["Condition","Common causes","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Left-sided","Ischemic heart disease, hypertension, aortic or mitral valve disease, cardiomyopathy","Low forward output with back-pressure into pulmonary veins and capillaries","Dyspnea, orthopnea, PND, crackles, S3, pleural effusion, heart failure cells in lung","Pulmonary edema, cardiogenic shock, arrhythmia, mural thrombus, right heart failure"],["Right-sided","Left heart failure (most common), cor pulmonale, right ventricular infarct, tricuspid or pulmonic valve disease","Back-pressure into systemic veins","Raised JVP, hepatojugular reflux, pitting edema, nutmeg liver, ascites; lungs often clear in pure right failure","Cardiac cirrhosis, ascites, renal congestion, low output"]]},{"title":"HFrEF vs HFpEF","cols":["Feature","HFrEF (systolic)","HFpEF (diastolic)"],"rows":[["Ejection fraction","40% or lower","50% or higher"],["Core problem","Weak contraction","Stiff ventricle, poor relaxation and filling"],["Usual causes","MI, dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertension, older age, obesity, diabetes, hypertrophic or restrictive cardiomyopathy"],["Ventricle shape","Dilated (eccentric hypertrophy)","Thick walled (concentric hypertrophy)"],["Extra heart sound","S3","S4"]]},{"title":"Compensatory mechanisms","cols":["Mechanism","Short-term help","Long-term harm"],"rows":[["Frank-Starling","More stroke volume from more filling","Fails once the curve flattens; congestion"],["Sympathetic activation","Faster rate, stronger contraction, vasoconstriction","Higher O2 demand, arrhythmia, myocyte death"],["RAAS and ADH","Restore volume and blood pressure","Edema, higher afterload, fibrosis"],["Hypertrophy and remodeling","Spreads wall stress over more muscle","Stiffness, ischemia, dilation"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Left goes to the lungs","text":"Left-sided failure backs up into the lungs. Right-sided failure backs up into the rest of the body (neck veins, liver, legs)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"S3 floods, S4 stiffens","text":"S3 comes from blood rushing into an overfilled, dilated ventricle. S4 comes from the atrium kicking against a stiff, thick ventricle."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Why orthopnea","text":"Lying flat returns fluid from the legs to the chest, and a failing left ventricle cannot handle the extra volume, so the lungs congest."}],"highYield":["Left heart failure is the most common cause of right heart failure.","Heart failure cells are hemosiderin-laden macrophages in the alveoli.","Nutmeg liver shows centrilobular congestion from right heart failure.","BNP is released by stretched ventricular myocytes and is used to diagnose heart failure.","Ischemic heart disease is the most common cause of left-sided heart failure.","HFpEF is linked with hypertension and concentric hypertrophy."],"pitfalls":["Heart failure cells are found in the lungs, not in the heart.","Edema in right heart failure is pitting and dependent; lymphedema and myxedema are nonpitting.","A normal ejection fraction does not rule out heart failure; HFpEF has a normal or high ejection fraction."],"dumps":[{"t":"Two sides of failure","p":"Draw the heart and both circulations. Show where blood backs up in left-sided and in right-sided failure, and label the organ finding at each spot.","l":["Left ventricle fails and left atrial pressure rises","Pulmonary venous and capillary congestion","Pulmonary edema and heart failure cells","Pulmonary hypertension then loads the right ventricle","Right ventricle fails and systemic venous pressure rises","Raised JVP and hepatojugular reflux","Nutmeg liver and ascites","Dependent pitting edema"]},{"t":"Compensation loop","p":"Trace what happens after cardiac output falls in heart failure. Show each compensatory system and how it worsens the failure over time.","l":["Low cardiac output lowers blood pressure and kidney perfusion","Baroreceptors activate sympathetic nerves","Renin release activates angiotensin II and aldosterone","ADH release adds water retention","Salt and water retention raise preload and cause edema","Vasoconstriction raises afterload","Angiotensin II and aldosterone cause fibrosis and remodeling","BNP rises with stretch and partly opposes RAAS"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Failure"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on heart failure)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Congestive Heart Failure | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gsu8NT1yYes"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Heart failure with reduced and preserved ejection fraction, pathophysiology and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ItKGeEbzAis"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Heart Failure | CHF | Congestive Heart Failure | Pathophysiology🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi39GGJA16E"}],"related":["CV-6","CV-15","CV-18-A","CV-18-C","CV-18-E1"]},"CV-18-C":{"id":"CV-18-C","summary":"Ischemic heart disease happens when coronary blood flow cannot meet myocardial O2 demand, nearly always because of coronary atherosclerosis. It ranges from angina to infarction to slow ischemic heart failure. Expect questions on plaque events, the histologic timeline of an infarct, and which complication appears on which day.","notes":[{"h":"Causes and risk factors","points":["Coronary atherosclerosis causes over 90% of cases.","Risk factors: age, male sex, smoking, hypertension, diabetes, high LDL, low HDL, family history, obesity, inactivity.","Other causes: coronary vasospasm, emboli, vasculitis, and high demand states (tachycardia, LVH, severe aortic stenosis).","The subendocardium is most vulnerable because it is last on the coronary supply line and squeezed hardest in systole."]},{"h":"Angina pectoris","points":["Stable angina: fixed narrowing (about 70% or more) causes chest pain with exertion that eases with rest or nitroglycerin.","Stable angina shows ST depression during pain (subendocardial ischemia).","Unstable angina: plaque disruption with a partial clot causes pain at rest or worsening pain, without troponin rise.","Prinzmetal (variant) angina: coronary vasospasm at rest with transient ST elevation; triggers include smoking and cocaine."]},{"h":"Myocardial infarction","points":["Usual sequence: plaque rupture, platelet adhesion, and a thrombus that blocks the artery.","Irreversible myocyte injury begins after about 20 to 40 minutes of severe ischemia.","Transmural infarct (often STEMI) spans the full wall; subendocardial infarct (often NSTEMI) is limited to the inner third to half.","Artery involved: LAD about 40 to 50%, RCA about 30 to 40%, left circumflex about 15 to 20%.","ECG leads: LAD in V1 to V4, left circumflex in I, aVL, V5, V6, RCA in II, III, aVF.","Troponin rises within about 3 to 4 hours and stays elevated about 7 to 10 days; CK-MB returns to normal in about 2 to 3 days, so it helps detect reinfarction.","Reperfusion injury: contraction band necrosis and free radical damage when flow returns."]},{"h":"Chronic ischemic heart disease","points":["Progressive heart failure from ischemic myocardial damage, also called ischemic cardiomyopathy.","Usually follows one or more healed infarcts; scars replace muscle and the remaining muscle hypertrophies.","The left ventricle dilates; diffuse subendocardial myocyte vacuolization and fibrosis are seen.","Complications: progressive heart failure, arrhythmias, sudden cardiac death."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Ischemic heart disease compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Angina pectoris","Coronary atherosclerosis; vasospasm in variant angina","Transient ischemia without myocyte death","Pressure-like chest pain lasting minutes; ST depression (stable) or transient ST elevation (variant)","Progression to unstable angina or MI"],["Chronic ischemic heart disease","Prior MIs and long-standing coronary disease","Loss of muscle with scarring, hypertrophy of the rest, and chamber dilation","Slowly worsening heart failure, enlarged dilated left ventricle","Heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden cardiac death"],["Myocardial infarction (MI)","Plaque rupture with thrombus; same risk factors as atherosclerosis","Prolonged ischemia causes coagulative necrosis","Crushing chest pain over 20 minutes, diaphoresis, ST elevation or depression, rising troponin","Arrhythmia, shock, rupture, aneurysm, mural thrombus, pericarditis"]]},{"title":"Types of angina","cols":["Type","Mechanism","When pain occurs","ECG during pain","Troponin"],"rows":[["Stable","Fixed stenosis about 70% or more","Exertion; eases with rest","ST depression","Normal"],["Unstable","Plaque disruption with nonocclusive thrombus","At rest or with less effort; worsening","ST depression or T wave inversion","Normal"],["Prinzmetal (variant)","Coronary vasospasm","At rest, often early morning","Transient ST elevation","Normal"]]},{"title":"Timeline of an infarct","cols":["Time","Gross","Microscopic","Main risk"],"rows":[["0 to 4 hours","No change","Wavy fibers at the border may appear","Ventricular arrhythmia"],["4 to 24 hours","Dark mottling","Early coagulative necrosis, edema, contraction bands if reperfused","Arrhythmia, heart failure, shock"],["1 to 3 days","Mottled with yellow-tan center","Neutrophils enter, necrosis advances","Fibrinous pericarditis"],["3 to 7 days","Yellow-tan, soft, hyperemic border","Macrophages clear dead cells","Free wall, septal, or papillary muscle rupture"],["1 to 2 weeks","Red-gray depressed border","Granulation tissue","Rupture risk still present"],["2 weeks to 2 months","Gray-white scar","Dense collagen scar","Ventricular aneurysm, mural thrombus, Dressler syndrome"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cells arrive in order","text":"After an MI the cells arrive in the same order as in any acute inflammation: neutrophils first (days 1 to 3), macrophages next (days 3 to 7), then granulation tissue and scar."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Soft wall, rupture risk","text":"Rupture is most likely when macrophages have eaten the dead muscle and no scar has formed yet, roughly days 3 to 14."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Why the posteromedial papillary muscle","text":"The posteromedial papillary muscle usually has a single supply (posterior descending artery), so an inferior MI can rupture it and cause acute mitral regurgitation."}],"highYield":["Ventricular fibrillation is the main cause of death in the first hours after MI.","LAD occlusion is the most common cause of MI and affects the anterior wall and septum.","Contraction band necrosis is a sign of reperfusion.","Free wall rupture causes sudden cardiac tamponade; septal rupture causes a new left-to-right shunt.","Papillary muscle rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation and pulmonary edema.","Dressler syndrome is autoimmune pericarditis weeks after MI.","Troponin is the most specific marker of myocyte necrosis."],"pitfalls":["Unstable angina and NSTEMI both have rest pain; only NSTEMI has a troponin rise.","Stable angina shows ST depression; Prinzmetal angina shows transient ST elevation.","A true ventricular aneurysm is a scarred bulging wall; a pseudoaneurysm is a rupture sealed off by pericardium and has a narrow neck.","Early fibrinous pericarditis (days 1 to 3) comes from the necrosis itself; Dressler syndrome is a later immune reaction."],"dumps":[{"t":"Infarct timeline","p":"Draw a timeline from hour 0 to 2 months after an MI. Write the microscopic change and the main complication at each stage.","l":["0 to 4 hours: little visible change; arrhythmia risk highest","4 to 24 hours: coagulative necrosis begins; contraction bands if reperfused","1 to 3 days: neutrophils; fibrinous pericarditis","3 to 7 days: macrophages; rupture risk rises","1 to 2 weeks: granulation tissue","2 weeks to 2 months: collagen scar","Late: ventricular aneurysm, mural thrombus, Dressler syndrome"]},{"t":"Coronary territories","p":"Draw the heart with the LAD, left circumflex, and RCA. Label the walls each supplies and the ECG leads that show each infarct.","l":["LAD: anterior wall and anterior two thirds of septum","LAD infarct: V1 to V4","Left circumflex: lateral wall","Left circumflex infarct: I, aVL, V5, V6","RCA: inferior wall, right ventricle, usually SA and AV nodes","RCA infarct: II, III, aVF","Inferior MI can cause bradycardia or AV block and posteromedial papillary muscle rupture"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on ischemic heart disease)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Coronary Artery Disease | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=67tUtS3y_GA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Case Study #18: Myocardial Infarction","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JXJEeCZZdQ0"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Heart attack (myocardial infarction) pathophysiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T_b9U5gn_Zk"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Coronary Artery Disease | Ischemic Heart Disease | Angina🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oQ235E1gvrU"}],"related":["CV-8","CV-7","CV-18-I3","CV-18-B","CV-18-F3"]},"CV-18-D":{"id":"CV-18-D","summary":"Valve disease either narrows a valve (stenosis, a pressure load) or lets blood leak back (regurgitation, a volume load). The exam links each lesion to its cause, its murmur, and its effect on the chambers, and adds the special valve diseases: endocarditis, MVP, rheumatic heart disease, and carcinoid heart disease.","notes":[{"h":"Aortic valve","points":["Aortic stenosis: age-related calcification is most common; bicuspid valve causes it at a younger age; rheumatic disease less often.","AS: pressure overload, concentric LVH; syncope, angina, and dyspnea on exertion; weak delayed pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus).","AS can cause hemolysis with schistocytes and GI bleeding from angiodysplasia (Heyde syndrome).","Aortic regurgitation: aortic root dilation (hypertension, age, Marfan, syphilis), bicuspid valve, endocarditis, rheumatic disease.","AR: volume overload, eccentric LVH; wide pulse pressure and bounding pulses; acute AR from dissection or endocarditis causes sudden failure."]},{"h":"Mitral valve","points":["Mitral stenosis is almost always rheumatic.","MS raises left atrial pressure: atrial enlargement, atrial fibrillation, mural thrombi and emboli, pulmonary hypertension, right heart failure.","A large left atrium can compress the esophagus (dysphagia) or the left recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness).","Mitral regurgitation: MVP, ischemic papillary muscle damage or rupture, left ventricular dilation, rheumatic disease, endocarditis.","MR: volume overload of left atrium and ventricle; S3; atrial fibrillation."]},{"h":"Endocarditis","points":["Infective: acute (S. aureus, normal valves, destructive) or subacute (viridans strep, damaged valves, slower).","Mitral valve is involved most often overall; the tricuspid valve in IV drug use.","Nonbacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis: sterile clots on valves in cancer and hypercoagulable states.","Libman-Sacks endocarditis: sterile vegetations on both valve surfaces in lupus.","Signs: fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions (painless, palms and soles), Osler nodes (painful, fingers), Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages."]},{"h":"Mitral valve prolapse","points":["Myxomatous degeneration: excess proteoglycan in the spongiosa makes leaflets floppy and they balloon into the atrium in systole.","Common (about 2 to 3% of adults); linked to Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, and ADPKD.","Mid-systolic click then a late systolic murmur; standing or Valsalva moves the click earlier.","Usually benign; can lead to mitral regurgitation, endocarditis, arrhythmia, and rarely sudden death."]},{"h":"Rheumatic and carcinoid heart disease","points":["Rheumatic heart disease: repeated rheumatic fever scars valves; commissures fuse and chordae thicken and shorten.","The mitral valve is affected in nearly all cases; mitral plus aortic is next most common.","Classic chronic result is mitral stenosis with a fish-mouth valve.","Carcinoid heart disease: serotonin-secreting neuroendocrine tumor with liver metastases lets serotonin reach the right heart.","Plaque-like fibrous thickening of the right heart valves and endocardium causes tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonic stenosis.","The left heart is usually spared because the lungs break down serotonin (monoamine oxidase); urine 5-HIAA is high."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Valvular heart diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Aortic stenosis/insufficiency","AS: calcific aging, bicuspid valve, rheumatic. AI: aortic root dilation, bicuspid valve, endocarditis, rheumatic","AS: pressure overload, concentric LVH. AI: volume overload, eccentric LVH","AS: syncope, angina, dyspnea, slow weak pulse. AI: wide pulse pressure, bounding pulses","Left heart failure, sudden death (AS), hemolysis (AS)"],["Mitral stenosis/insufficiency","MS: rheumatic. MR: MVP, ischemic papillary muscle damage, LV dilation, endocarditis, rheumatic","MS: high left atrial pressure. MR: volume overload of left atrium and ventricle","MS: opening snap, diastolic rumble, dyspnea, hemoptysis. MR: holosystolic apical murmur","Atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus and emboli, pulmonary hypertension, heart failure"],["Endocarditis","Damaged or prosthetic valves, IV drug use, dental and GU procedures, cancer (marantic), lupus (Libman-Sacks)","Vegetations of platelets and fibrin, with organisms in infective endocarditis","Fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages","Valve destruction, heart failure, septic emboli, abscess, mycotic aneurysm, glomerulonephritis"],["Mitral valve prolapse (MVP)","Myxomatous degeneration; Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, ADPKD","Floppy leaflets balloon into the left atrium in systole","Mid-systolic click and late systolic murmur","Mitral regurgitation, endocarditis, arrhythmia, rare sudden death"],["Rheumatic heart disease","Repeated group A strep pharyngitis and rheumatic fever","Molecular mimicry, then valve fibrosis and commissural fusion","Mitral stenosis (fish-mouth valve); history of rheumatic fever","Atrial fibrillation, heart failure, infective endocarditis"],["Carcinoid heart disease","Serotonin-secreting neuroendocrine tumor with liver metastases","Serotonin drives plaque-like fibrosis of right heart valves","Flushing, diarrhea, wheezing, right heart failure; high urine 5-HIAA","Tricuspid regurgitation, pulmonic stenosis, right heart failure"]]},{"title":"Murmurs to know","cols":["Lesion","Timing","Where heard best","Features"],"rows":[["Aortic stenosis","Systolic, crescendo-decrescendo","Right upper sternal border, radiates to carotids","Ejection click possible; soft A2"],["Aortic regurgitation","Early diastolic, decrescendo","Left sternal border","Blowing; wide pulse pressure"],["Mitral stenosis","Mid to late diastolic rumble after an opening snap","Apex","Shorter S2 to snap interval means more severe"],["Mitral regurgitation","Holosystolic","Apex, radiates to axilla","Blowing; S3 possible"],["Mitral valve prolapse","Mid-systolic click, then late systolic murmur","Apex","Standing or Valsalva moves the click earlier"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SAD","text":"Aortic stenosis symptoms: Syncope, Angina, Dyspnea on exertion."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"J♥NES","text":"Major Jones criteria for rheumatic fever: Joints (migratory polyarthritis), heart (carditis), Nodules (subcutaneous), Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"TIPS","text":"Carcinoid heart disease hits the right side: Tricuspid Insufficiency, Pulmonic Stenosis."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Bacteria FROM JANE","text":"Infective endocarditis signs: Bacteremia, Fever, Roth spots, Osler nodes, Murmur, Janeway lesions, Anemia, Nail-bed (splinter) hemorrhages, Emboli."}],"highYield":["Most common cause of aortic stenosis is age-related calcification; bicuspid valve causes it earlier.","Mitral stenosis is almost always rheumatic.","MVP comes from myxomatous degeneration and is linked to Marfan syndrome.","S. aureus causes acute endocarditis; tricuspid involvement suggests IV drug use.","Carcinoid heart disease needs liver metastases and spares the left heart.","Left atrial enlargement in mitral stenosis leads to atrial fibrillation and stroke.","Standing or Valsalva makes the MVP click earlier and the HCM murmur louder."],"pitfalls":["Janeway lesions are painless and from septic emboli; Osler nodes are painful and from immune complexes.","Rheumatic fever vegetations are small and sterile; infective endocarditis vegetations are bulky and full of organisms.","Stenosis is a pressure load (concentric hypertrophy); regurgitation is a volume load (eccentric hypertrophy).","The opening snap belongs to mitral stenosis; the mid-systolic click belongs to MVP."],"dumps":[{"t":"Murmur map","p":"Draw the chest with the four listening areas. For aortic stenosis, aortic regurgitation, mitral stenosis, mitral regurgitation, and MVP, write the timing, location, and one extra clue.","l":["AS: systolic crescendo-decrescendo at right upper sternal border, radiates to carotids","AR: early diastolic decrescendo at left sternal border","AR: wide pulse pressure","MS: opening snap then diastolic rumble at apex","MR: holosystolic at apex, radiates to axilla","MVP: mid-systolic click then late systolic murmur","Standing or Valsalva moves the MVP click earlier"]},{"t":"From strep throat to a stiff valve","p":"Trace the path from group A strep pharyngitis to chronic mitral stenosis and its complications.","l":["Group A strep pharyngitis","M protein antibodies cross-react with heart antigens","Acute rheumatic fever about 2 to 3 weeks later with pancarditis and Aschoff bodies","Repeated attacks scar the valve","Commissural fusion and short, thick chordae make a fish-mouth mitral valve","Left atrial enlargement and atrial fibrillation","Mural thrombus and stroke","Pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on valvular heart disease)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Valvular Heart Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KiI9WMXBV_o"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Aortic stenosis and aortic regurgitation | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3t1n5szXriQ"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Mitral valve regurgitation and mitral valve prolapse | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bjnw_jwDt1Q"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Endocarditis | Cardiac Pathology🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Df-FzR7S4SM"}],"related":["CV-4","CV-16","CV-17","CV-18-G1","CV-18-K1"]},"CV-18-E":{"id":"CV-18-E","summary":"Primary myocardial diseases are problems of the heart muscle itself: the three cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive) and myocarditis. Exam questions hinge on telling them apart by chamber shape, systolic versus diastolic failure, and cause.","notes":[{"h":"Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM)","points":["Most common cardiomyopathy; all four chambers dilate and the walls thin out.","Systolic dysfunction: low ejection fraction, eccentric hypertrophy (sarcomeres added in series).","Causes: familial (often autosomal dominant, TTN titin gene most common), alcohol, prior myocarditis, doxorubicin, peripartum, Chagas, thiamine deficiency.","Findings: signs of heart failure, S3, mitral and tricuspid regurgitation from stretched valve rings, balloon-shaped heart on chest X-ray."]},{"h":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)","points":["Autosomal dominant sarcomere gene mutations, most often beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7) or myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3).","Thick, stiff left ventricle, often asymmetric septal hypertrophy; small cavity; myocyte disarray on histology.","Diastolic dysfunction; ejection fraction normal or high.","Left ventricular outflow obstruction from systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve against the septum.","Classic exam patient: young athlete with exertional syncope or sudden cardiac death from ventricular arrhythmia."]},{"h":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM)","points":["Stiff ventricle that resists filling; chamber size and systolic function near normal; both atria enlarge.","Causes: amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation fibrosis, endomyocardial fibrosis, Loeffler endocarditis.","Amyloid clue: low-voltage ECG despite a thick ventricular wall.","Right-sided failure signs dominate: raised JVP, Kussmaul sign, edema, hepatomegaly."]},{"h":"Myocarditis","points":["Inflammation of the myocardium with myocyte injury.","Most common cause in the US: viruses, classically coxsackie B; also adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, SARS-CoV-2.","Other causes: Chagas disease, Lyme disease, diphtheria toxin, drug hypersensitivity, SLE, giant cell myocarditis.","Viral form shows a lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis on biopsy.","Presents after a viral illness with chest pain, dyspnea, arrhythmia, raised troponin; can progress to DCM."]},{"h":"How each one fails","points":["DCM: weak squeeze (systolic failure).","HCM and RCM: poor filling (diastolic failure) with preserved ejection fraction.","All three cause arrhythmias and can cause sudden death."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Primary myocardial diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Familial (TTN), alcohol, prior viral myocarditis, doxorubicin, peripartum, Chagas, wet beriberi, cocaine","Myocyte loss and eccentric hypertrophy; dilated chambers; systolic dysfunction with low EF","Heart failure signs, S3, regurgitant murmurs, balloon-shaped heart on X-ray","Heart failure, arrhythmia, mural thrombus with emboli, sudden death"],["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Autosomal dominant sarcomere mutations (MYH7, MYBPC3); also seen in Friedreich ataxia","Myocyte disarray, thick septum, small stiff LV; diastolic dysfunction; dynamic LV outflow obstruction","S4, systolic murmur that gets louder with Valsalva or standing and softer with squatting; exertional syncope","Sudden cardiac death, atrial fibrillation, heart failure"],["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation, endomyocardial fibrosis, Loeffler endocarditis","Infiltration or fibrosis stiffens the wall; impaired filling with near-normal systolic function","Biatrial enlargement, raised JVP, Kussmaul sign, low-voltage ECG in amyloid","Heart failure (right-sided signs prominent), atrial fibrillation, conduction block"],["Myocarditis","Viruses (coxsackie B classic), Chagas, Lyme, diphtheria, drugs, autoimmune disease","Inflammatory infiltrate with myocyte necrosis; direct viral injury plus T-cell mediated damage","Recent viral illness, chest pain, dyspnea, arrhythmia, raised troponin","Acute heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden death, later dilated cardiomyopathy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ABCCCD (causes of dilated cardiomyopathy)","text":"Alcohol, wet Beriberi, Coxsackie B myocarditis, chronic Cocaine use, Chagas disease, Doxorubicin."},{"kind":"trick","label":"HCM murmur follows LV volume","text":"Anything that shrinks the LV (Valsalva, standing up) pulls the septum and mitral leaflet closer, so the murmur gets louder. Anything that fills the LV (squatting, handgrip, leg raise) makes it softer."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Squeeze versus fill","text":"Ask whether the heart cannot squeeze (DCM, low EF) or cannot fill (HCM and RCM, normal EF). Then use wall thickness and cavity size to split HCM from RCM."}],"highYield":["DCM is the most common cardiomyopathy, about 90 percent of cases.","HCM is a leading cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes.","HCM inheritance is autosomal dominant; screen first-degree relatives.","Amyloid heart: thick walls with low-voltage QRS complexes.","Coxsackie B is the classic viral cause of myocarditis and of later DCM.","Doxorubicin cardiotoxicity is dose-related and causes DCM."],"pitfalls":["HCM versus athlete heart: athlete heart has a normal-sized or larger cavity, normal diastolic function, and regresses with rest from training.","Restrictive cardiomyopathy versus constrictive pericarditis: both impair filling, but constriction shows a thickened or calcified pericardium and a pericardial knock.","Hemochromatosis can cause either a dilated or a restrictive pattern; do not lock it into one box."],"dumps":[{"t":"Three cardiomyopathies side by side","p":"Draw three hearts in cross-section showing dilated, hypertrophic, and restrictive cardiomyopathy. Label wall thickness, cavity size, ejection fraction, and two causes for each.","l":["DCM: thin walls, large cavity, low ejection fraction","HCM: thick (often septal) wall, small cavity, normal or high ejection fraction","RCM: near-normal wall and cavity, enlarged atria, normal or near-normal ejection fraction","DCM causes from ABCCCD or familial TTN","HCM cause: autosomal dominant sarcomere mutation (MYH7 or MYBPC3)","RCM causes: amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation","DCM is systolic failure; HCM and RCM are diastolic failure"]},{"t":"HCM outflow obstruction","p":"Trace why the HCM murmur gets louder when the patient stands up from squatting. Start with venous return and end with the murmur.","l":["Standing lowers venous return (preload)","Lower preload means a smaller LV cavity","Smaller cavity brings the thick septum and anterior mitral leaflet closer","Systolic anterior motion of the mitral leaflet narrows the outflow tract","More obstruction gives a louder systolic ejection murmur","Squatting raises preload and afterload and softens the murmur","Mitral regurgitation often accompanies the obstruction"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on cardiomyopathies and myocarditis"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Failure"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiomyopathy | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Miz9kvrrqk"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Dilated cardiomyopathy: Pathophysiology and diagnosis | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ou3a7Htbrvw"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: Pathophysiology and diagnosis | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtqrKxJmBn4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Myocarditis - causes, pathophysiology, investigation and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=INSa4QQcwFo"}],"related":["CV-18-B","CV-18-K6","CV-18-K2","CV-14"]},"CV-18-F":{"id":"CV-18-F","summary":"Pericardial disease covers fluid in the pericardial sac (effusion and tamponade), inflammation of the pericardium (pericarditis, primary or secondary), and tumor spread to the pericardium. Exams test how each one changes filling of the heart and what the ECG and exam look like.","notes":[{"h":"Pericardium basics","points":["Fibrous outer layer plus a serous sac (parietal and visceral layers).","Normal pericardial fluid is a small volume, about 15 to 50 mL.","The fibrous layer does not stretch quickly, so the rate of fluid build-up matters more than the total amount."]},{"h":"Pericarditis","points":["Primary (acute idiopathic) pericarditis is mostly viral, classically coxsackie B.","Secondary causes: MI (early fibrinous, or Dressler syndrome weeks later), uremia, SLE or RA, radiation, TB, bacterial infection, cancer, cardiac surgery.","Sharp chest pain worse lying flat and with inspiration, better sitting up and leaning forward.","Friction rub; ECG shows diffuse ST elevation with PR depression.","Patterns: fibrinous or serous (most common), purulent (bacterial), hemorrhagic (cancer, TB), caseous (TB)."]},{"h":"Effusion and tamponade","points":["Serous effusion: heart failure, low albumin. Bloody: trauma, cancer, TB, ruptured MI or aortic dissection.","Large effusion: muffled heart sounds, low-voltage QRS, electrical alternans, water-bottle heart on X-ray.","Tamponade: fluid pressure squeezes the heart so it cannot fill; cardiac output falls.","Beck triad: hypotension, distended neck veins, distant heart sounds.","Pulsus paradoxus: systolic pressure falls more than 10 mmHg with inspiration.","Diastolic pressures equalize across all four chambers."]},{"h":"Metastatic disease","points":["Metastases are far more common than primary tumors of the heart and pericardium.","Common sources: lung and breast carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma, leukemia.","Usually present as a bloody effusion, sometimes with tamponade; malignant cells can be found in the fluid."]},{"h":"Constrictive pericarditis","points":["Chronic scarring, sometimes calcified, encases the heart and limits filling.","Worldwide TB is a major cause; in developed countries it often follows viral pericarditis, cardiac surgery, or radiation.","Findings: Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock in early diastole, pericardial calcification on imaging."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pericardial diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Metastatic disease","Lung and breast carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma, leukemia","Direct extension or lymphatic and blood spread to the pericardium; tumor causes exudate and bleeding","Bloody effusion, malignant cells in fluid, new effusion in a cancer patient","Tamponade, constriction"],["Pericardial effusion","Heart failure, low albumin, kidney failure, infection, cancer, trauma, hypothyroidism","Fluid builds in the sac; fast build-up raises pressure and limits filling","Muffled heart sounds, low-voltage QRS, electrical alternans, water-bottle heart","Cardiac tamponade (Beck triad, pulsus paradoxus, equal diastolic pressures)"],["Pericarditis, primary","Viral (coxsackie B classic) or idiopathic","Fibrinous or serous inflammation of the pericardial layers","Positional pleuritic chest pain, friction rub, diffuse ST elevation with PR depression","Effusion, tamponade, recurrence, later constriction"],["Pericarditis, secondary","MI, Dressler syndrome, uremia, SLE or RA, radiation, TB, bacteria, cancer, cardiac surgery","Inflammation driven by the underlying disease (necrosis, toxins, immune complexes, infection, tumor)","Same pain, rub and ECG pattern plus signs of the underlying disease","Purulent or hemorrhagic effusion, tamponade, constrictive pericarditis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Beck triad as three Ds","text":"Decreased blood pressure, Distended neck veins, Distant heart sounds. Add a fourth clue, pulsus paradoxus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pericarditis ECG versus STEMI","text":"Pericarditis: ST elevation in almost every lead with PR depression and no reciprocal changes. STEMI: ST elevation in one coronary territory with reciprocal depression."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tamponade versus constriction","text":"Tamponade is a fluid problem (pulsus paradoxus, Kussmaul sign usually absent). Constriction is a scar problem (Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock, calcification)."}],"highYield":["Pain of pericarditis improves sitting up and leaning forward.","Diffuse ST elevation plus PR depression points to pericarditis.","Electrical alternans means a large effusion with the heart swinging in the fluid.","Rate of fluid build-up matters: a small fast bleed can cause tamponade while a slow large effusion may not.","Dressler syndrome is an autoimmune pericarditis weeks after an MI.","Uremic pericarditis is fibrinous and appears with kidney failure.","TB is a classic cause of caseous pericarditis and constrictive pericarditis."],"pitfalls":["Early post-MI fibrinous pericarditis (first few days, over the infarct) versus Dressler syndrome (weeks later, immune mediated).","Kussmaul sign (JVP rises on inspiration) suggests constriction or restrictive cardiomyopathy more than tamponade.","A primary cardiac tumor is rare; a pericardial mass in an adult with known cancer is most likely metastatic."],"dumps":[{"t":"Tamponade physiology","p":"Draw the heart inside a fluid-filled pericardial sac. Trace what happens to filling, stroke volume and blood pressure as pressure rises, and label the bedside signs.","l":["Rising pericardial pressure limits diastolic filling","Right heart is compressed first (thin walls)","Diastolic pressures equalize across the chambers","Stroke volume and cardiac output fall, so blood pressure drops","Neck veins distend as venous return backs up","Inspiration fills the RV, septum bows left, LV output falls: pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg","Heart sounds are muffled"]},{"t":"Causes of pericarditis table","p":"Build a table with columns for type of pericarditis (fibrinous, serous, purulent, hemorrhagic, caseous) and the cause that best fits each.","l":["Fibrinous: MI, uremia, rheumatic fever, radiation, SLE","Serous: viral, autoimmune disease, uremia","Purulent: bacterial infection","Hemorrhagic: malignancy and TB","Caseous: TB","Dressler syndrome is fibrinous and immune mediated weeks after MI","Classic virus: coxsackie B"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on pericardial disease"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease, section on pericardial disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Pericardial Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQDd9OgwSV4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiac Tamponade - pericardial effusion, causes, pathophysiology, investigations and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9FgpLaCi_KE"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Causes of pericarditis | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ij_ERo1Ashc"}],"related":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-E","CV-3","CV-18-K6"]},"CV-18-G":{"id":"CV-18-G","summary":"This unit covers the congenital heart conditions on the blueprint: bicuspid aortic valve, patent ductus arteriosus, atrial and ventricular septal defects, and tetralogy of Fallot. Expect questions that link the embryology to the shunt direction, the murmur, and the genetic syndrome.","notes":[{"h":"Shunt basics","points":["Left-to-right shunts (VSD, ASD, PDA) send extra blood to the lungs; no cyanosis at first.","Right-to-left shunts (TOF) send blue blood to the body; cyanosis early in life.","Untreated left-to-right shunt raises pulmonary pressure, the shunt reverses, and late cyanosis follows (Eisenmenger syndrome).","Eisenmenger signs: late cyanosis, clubbing, polycythemia, right ventricular hypertrophy."]},{"h":"Septal defects","points":["VSD is the most common congenital heart defect (not counting bicuspid aortic valve); the membranous septum is the usual site.","VSD: harsh holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border; small ones are loud and often close on their own.","Ostium secundum ASD is the most common ASD (septum primum over-resorbed or septum secundum too small).","Ostium primum ASD is an endocardial cushion defect, linked to Down syndrome.","ASD: fixed, widely split S2 and a systolic flow murmur at the left upper sternal border.","ASD allows paradoxical emboli (venous clot crossing to the systemic side)."]},{"h":"Patent ductus arteriosus","points":["Ductus arteriosus (from the sixth aortic arch) normally closes in the first days after birth as oxygen rises and prostaglandin E2 falls.","Risk factors: prematurity, congenital rubella.","Continuous machine-like murmur below the left clavicle; bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure.","With Eisenmenger: differential cyanosis, blue clubbed toes with pink fingers."]},{"h":"Tetralogy of Fallot","points":["Most common cyanotic congenital heart defect.","Cause: anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular (outflow) septum, a neural crest related defect.","Associated with 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge syndrome).","Degree of right ventricular outflow obstruction sets how blue the child is.","Tet spells improve with squatting (raises systemic resistance, less right-to-left flow); boot-shaped heart on X-ray."]},{"h":"Bicuspid aortic valve","points":["Most common congenital heart anomaly overall, about 1 to 2 percent of people; more common in males.","Associated with Turner syndrome and coarctation of the aorta.","Valve calcifies early, so aortic stenosis appears decades earlier than in a normal valve.","Also linked to aortic regurgitation, endocarditis, and dilation or dissection of the ascending aorta."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital heart conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Bicuspid aortic valve","Male sex, Turner syndrome, coarctation of the aorta, familial cases","Two cusps instead of three; higher leaflet stress leads to early calcification; aortic wall weakness","Ejection click, systolic ejection murmur, aortic stenosis at a younger age","Aortic stenosis or regurgitation, endocarditis, ascending aortic aneurysm and dissection"],["Patent ductus arteriosus","Prematurity, congenital rubella","Ductus stays open; left-to-right flow from aorta to pulmonary artery","Continuous machine-like murmur, bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure","Heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, Eisenmenger with differential cyanosis, endarteritis"],["Ventricular septal defect","Most common defect after bicuspid aortic valve; Down syndrome, fetal alcohol syndrome; part of TOF","Left-to-right shunt between ventricles; usually membranous septum","Holosystolic murmur at left lower sternal border","Heart failure, Eisenmenger syndrome, endocarditis"],["Atrial septal defect","Secundum most common; primum with Down syndrome; Holt-Oram syndrome; fetal alcohol syndrome","Left-to-right shunt between atria; right heart volume overload","Fixed widely split S2, systolic flow murmur at left upper sternal border","Paradoxical embolism, atrial fibrillation, right heart failure, late Eisenmenger"],["Tetralogy of Fallot","22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge syndrome), fetal alcohol exposure","Displaced infundibular septum causes pulmonary stenosis, RVH, overriding aorta, and VSD; right-to-left shunt","Early cyanosis, tet spells eased by squatting, boot-shaped heart, systolic ejection murmur","Hypoxic spells, polycythemia, paradoxical emboli and brain abscess, endocarditis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PROVe (tetralogy of Fallot)","text":"Pulmonary stenosis (the most important feature), RVH, Overriding aorta, VSD."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"eaRLy versus LateR cyanosis","text":"Right-to-Left shunts cause eaRLy cyanosis (blue babies). Left-to-Right shunts cause LateR cyanosis (blue kids) once Eisenmenger reversal sets in."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Frequency order","text":"Left-to-right shunts by frequency: VSD, then ASD, then PDA. Picture the shunt moving from the ventricles, up to the atria, then out to the great arteries."}],"highYield":["VSD is the most common congenital heart defect after bicuspid aortic valve; TOF is the most common cyanotic one.","Fixed split S2 is the ASD sound.","Continuous machine-like murmur means PDA.","Squatting helps TOF by raising systemic vascular resistance.","Bicuspid aortic valve plus Turner syndrome plus coarctation is a classic cluster.","Down syndrome links to endocardial cushion defects (AV septal defect, ostium primum ASD).","22q11.2 deletion links to TOF and truncus arteriosus."],"pitfalls":["Fixed split S2 (ASD) versus paradoxical split S2 (aortic stenosis or left bundle branch block).","PDA with Eisenmenger causes cyanosis of the lower body (blue toes, pink fingers), because the ductus enters the aorta beyond the left subclavian artery.","A small VSD makes a louder murmur than a large one, so loudness does not equal size.","Patent foramen ovale is a flap that failed to fuse; an ASD is missing septal tissue."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw the shunts","p":"Draw a four-chamber heart with the aorta and pulmonary artery. Add an ASD, a VSD and a PDA, draw arrows for blood flow through each, and label the murmur for each one.","l":["ASD arrow from left atrium to right atrium","VSD arrow from left ventricle to right ventricle","PDA arrow from aorta to pulmonary artery","ASD murmur: systolic flow murmur at left upper sternal border with fixed split S2","VSD murmur: holosystolic at left lower sternal border","PDA murmur: continuous machine-like below left clavicle","All three are left-to-right and can lead to Eisenmenger syndrome"]},{"t":"Tetralogy of Fallot from embryo to child","p":"Draw the heart in tetralogy of Fallot and label all four defects. Then explain why a child with TOF squats during a spell.","l":["Anterosuperior shift of the infundibular septum is the single root cause","Pulmonary (RV outflow) stenosis","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Overriding aorta sitting over the VSD","Ventricular septal defect","Squatting kinks leg arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance","Higher resistance pushes more RV blood to the lungs and less across the VSD","Associated with 22q11.2 deletion"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on congenital heart disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Acyanotic Congenital Heart Defects","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=orjnzBGSNx4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cyanotic Congenital Heart Defects","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m-PdQwT8mPU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Tetralogy of fallot | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Be3tuYMgA9I"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Patent ductus arteriosus | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U2fGKvbir24"}],"related":["CV-1","CV-14","CV-18-D1","CV-18-I2"]},"CV-18-H":{"id":"CV-18-H","summary":"Hemodynamic conditions are the general pathology of blood flow: thrombosis, embolism, infarction, hemorrhage, edema and shock. They link to each other in chains (clot, then embolus, then infarct) and show up in almost every organ system on the exam.","notes":[{"h":"Thrombosis","points":["Virchow triad: endothelial injury, abnormal flow (stasis or turbulence), hypercoagulability.","Arterial thrombi: platelet-rich, form on ruptured atherosclerotic plaque.","Venous thrombi: red, form in stasis, usually deep leg veins.","Lines of Zahn (layers of platelets and fibrin alternating with red cells) mark a clot formed in flowing blood before death.","Inherited hypercoagulability: factor V Leiden (most common), prothrombin G20210A, antithrombin, protein C or S deficiency.","Fates: propagation, embolization, dissolution, organization and recanalization."]},{"h":"Embolism","points":["Most emboli are thromboemboli.","Venous emboli (mostly from deep leg veins) lodge in the lungs.","Systemic arterial emboli mostly come from intracardiac mural thrombi and lodge in legs, brain, gut, kidney and spleen.","Fat embolism after long-bone fracture: low oxygen, confusion, petechial rash, about 1 to 3 days after injury.","Amniotic fluid embolism: sudden respiratory failure, shock and DIC during or after labor.","Gas embolism includes decompression sickness (nitrogen bubbles on fast ascent)."]},{"h":"Infarction","points":["Area of ischemic necrosis from blocked arterial supply or venous drainage.","White (pale) infarcts: solid organs with a single arterial supply (heart, kidney, spleen).","Red (hemorrhagic) infarcts: venous occlusion, loose tissue, dual blood supply (lung, small bowel), or reperfusion.","Coagulative necrosis in most organs; liquefactive necrosis in the brain.","Infarcts are often wedge-shaped with the blocked vessel at the apex."]},{"h":"Hemorrhage and edema","points":["Hemorrhage sizes: petechiae (1 to 2 mm), purpura (3 mm or more), ecchymoses (larger bruises), hematoma (collected mass of blood).","Acute loss of more than about 20 percent of blood volume can cause hypovolemic shock; chronic loss causes iron deficiency anemia.","Edema forms when Starling forces push fluid out faster than lymphatics remove it.","Transudate (low protein): high hydrostatic pressure or low oncotic pressure. Exudate (high protein): inflammation."]},{"h":"Shock","points":["Shock is widespread poor tissue perfusion from low cardiac output or low effective blood volume.","Types: hypovolemic, cardiogenic, obstructive, distributive (septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic).","Stages: nonprogressive (compensated), progressive (lactic acidosis), irreversible.","End-organ damage: acute tubular necrosis, ARDS, DIC, ischemic bowel, multiorgan failure."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hemodynamic conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Thrombosis","Immobility, surgery, cancer, pregnancy, estrogen, factor V Leiden, antiphospholipid syndrome, atherosclerosis","Virchow triad activates platelets and coagulation inside an intact vessel","Venous: swollen painful limb. Arterial: sudden ischemia of the supplied tissue","Embolism, infarction, chronic venous insufficiency"],["Embolism","Deep vein thrombosis, atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus, long-bone fracture, labor, rapid ascent from a dive","Detached mass (clot, fat, gas, amniotic fluid, tumor, bacteria) travels and blocks a downstream vessel","Depends on site: pulmonary embolism, stroke, cold pulseless limb, bowel ischemia","Infarction, sudden death, DIC (amniotic fluid)"],["Hemorrhage","Trauma, vessel rupture, aneurysm, coagulation or platelet disorders","Blood escapes the vessel into tissue, a body cavity, or outside the body","Petechiae, purpura, ecchymoses, hematoma","Hypovolemic shock (acute), iron deficiency anemia (chronic), mass effect in the skull"],["Infarction","Arterial thrombosis or embolism, venous occlusion, vasospasm, torsion","Ischemic necrosis of the tissue supplied","White wedge in heart, kidney, spleen; red infarct in lung, bowel, testis","Organ dysfunction, rupture, scar formation"],["Edema","Heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition, lymphatic blockage, inflammation","Raised hydrostatic pressure, lowered oncotic pressure, lymphatic obstruction, sodium retention, or leaky vessels","Pitting leg edema, pulmonary crackles, ascites, periorbital swelling","Pulmonary edema with hypoxemia, cerebral edema with herniation, skin breakdown"],["Shock","Bleeding or fluid loss, MI, tamponade, massive PE, sepsis, anaphylaxis, spinal cord injury","Systemic hypoperfusion leads to anaerobic metabolism and cell injury","Low blood pressure, fast pulse, low urine output, confusion, raised lactate","Acute tubular necrosis, ARDS, DIC, multiorgan failure, death"]]},{"title":"Types of shock by hemodynamics","cols":["Type","Example causes","Cardiac output","Systemic vascular resistance","Wedge pressure (PCWP)","Skin"],"rows":[["Hypovolemic","Hemorrhage, burns, vomiting, diarrhea","Low","High","Low","Cold, clammy"],["Cardiogenic","MI, arrhythmia, acute valve failure","Low","High","High","Cold, clammy"],["Obstructive","Tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive PE","Low","High","Varies (high in tamponade, low in massive PE)","Cold, clammy"],["Distributive: septic","Gram-negative or gram-positive sepsis","High early, may fall later","Low","Low","Warm early"],["Distributive: anaphylactic","IgE-mediated mast cell release (type I hypersensitivity)","High or normal","Low","Low","Warm, flushed, hives"],["Distributive: neurogenic","Spinal cord or brainstem injury","Low","Low","Low","Warm, dry, with slow heart rate"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"An embolus moves like a FAT BAT","text":"Types of emboli: Fat, Air, Thrombus, Bacteria, Amniotic fluid, Tumor."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Red or white infarct","text":"Red infarcts happen where blood can still get in or pool: veins blocked, loose tissue, two blood supplies, or reperfusion. Otherwise expect a white infarct."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Reading a shock profile","text":"Start with SVR. High SVR means the body is compensating for a pump or volume problem, so check PCWP (high in cardiogenic, low in hypovolemic). Low SVR means distributive shock."}],"highYield":["Factor V Leiden resists inactivation by activated protein C and is the most common inherited thrombophilia.","Lines of Zahn tell an antemortem clot from a postmortem clot.","Most pulmonary emboli start in deep veins of the thigh and pelvis.","Brain infarcts show liquefactive necrosis; other solid organs show coagulative necrosis.","Lung infarcts are red because of the dual bronchial and pulmonary supply.","Septic shock: low SVR, low PCWP, and high cardiac output early.","Neurogenic shock is the type with a slow heart rate."],"pitfalls":["Postmortem clots are gelatinous with a chicken-fat upper layer and no lines of Zahn; do not mistake them for antemortem thrombi.","Transudate versus exudate: inflammation leaks protein (exudate); heart failure and low albumin do not (transudate).","Obstructive shock looks like cardiogenic shock on SVR and output; the cause is outside the heart muscle.","Paradoxical embolism (venous clot causing a stroke) needs a right-to-left path such as a patent foramen ovale."],"dumps":[{"t":"Clot to infarct chain","p":"Trace a deep leg vein clot from formation to its effect on the lung. Label each step with the Virchow triad element or pathology term involved.","l":["Stasis from immobility starts the clot (Virchow triad)","Hypercoagulability or vessel injury adds risk","Venous thrombus forms in deep calf or thigh veins and propagates","Fragment breaks off and becomes a thromboembolus","Travels through the right heart to the pulmonary arteries","Blocks flow and creates dead space (V/Q mismatch)","Most do not infarct because of bronchial artery supply; an infarct is red and wedge-shaped"]},{"t":"Shock table","p":"Build a table on blank paper with rows for hypovolemic, cardiogenic, obstructive, septic and neurogenic shock, and columns for one cause, cardiac output, SVR, and PCWP.","l":["Hypovolemic: low CO, high SVR, low PCWP","Cardiogenic: low CO, high SVR, high PCWP","Obstructive: low CO, high SVR, PCWP depends on cause","Septic: high CO early, low SVR, low PCWP","Neurogenic: low CO, low SVR, low PCWP, slow heart rate","One correct cause for each type","Lactic acidosis marks progressive shock"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Circulatory Shock and Physiology of Its Treatment"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Shock | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=07N42Ragqas"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Approach to Shock - types, hypovolemic, cardiogenic, distributive, anaphylaxis, pathology, treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NqkdqJtxIfs"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Shock | Types And Mechanisms of Shock | Dr Najeeb🧠","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hvoYD2Qu_ms"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is shock? | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AvtS_IrlbYk"},{"channel":"Khan Academy","title":"Thromboemboli and thromboembolisms | Miscellaneous | Heatlh & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h0207xMD6b8"}],"related":["CV-11","CV-18-I7","CV-18-I10","CV-18-C3"]},"CV-18-I":{"id":"CV-18-I","summary":"Vascular conditions cover disease of the arteries (atherosclerosis, aneurysm, dissection, PAD, familial hypercholesterolemia), the veins (DVT, PE, varicose veins), and vessel inflammation or spasm (vasculitis, giant cell arteritis, Buerger disease, Raynaud). The exam favors risk factors, vessel size, and the classic patient for each.","notes":[{"h":"Arteriosclerosis and atherosclerosis","points":["Arteriosclerosis means hardening of arteries; three patterns: atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, and Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis: hypertension and diabetes. Hyperplastic (onion-skin): severe hypertension.","Monckeberg: calcification of the media in muscular arteries; does not narrow the lumen.","Atherosclerosis steps: endothelial injury, LDL entry and oxidation, monocytes become foam-cell macrophages, fatty streak, smooth muscle migration (PDGF), fibrous cap over a necrotic core.","Most affected: abdominal aorta, then coronary, popliteal and carotid arteries.","Modifiable risks: smoking, hypertension, high LDL, diabetes. Fixed: age, male sex, family history."]},{"h":"Familial hypercholesterolemia","points":["Autosomal dominant; most often LDL receptor (LDLR) mutation; also APOB or gain-of-function PCSK9.","Heterozygotes (about 1 in 250) have LDL often above 190 mg/dL and early coronary disease.","Homozygotes can have LDL above 500 mg/dL and MI in childhood or the teen years.","Tendon xanthomas (Achilles), xanthelasma, and early corneal arcus."]},{"h":"Aneurysm and dissection","points":["Abdominal aortic aneurysm: atherosclerosis; older male smokers; usually below the renal arteries; rupture risk rises with diameter.","Thoracic aortic aneurysm: cystic medial degeneration (hypertension, Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos); tertiary syphilis damages the vasa vasorum.","Berry aneurysms in the circle of Willis link to ADPKD and Ehlers-Danlos.","Aortic dissection: intimal tear lets blood split the media; hypertension is the main risk; also Marfan, bicuspid aortic valve, Turner, cocaine.","Dissection pain is sudden, tearing, radiates to the back; unequal arm pressures; widened mediastinum.","Stanford A involves the ascending aorta; Stanford B does not."]},{"h":"Peripheral arterial disease","points":["Atherosclerosis of leg arteries; smoking and diabetes are the strongest risks.","Claudication (calf pain with walking that stops with rest), then rest pain, ulcers and gangrene.","Weak pulses, shiny hairless skin, cool feet; ankle-brachial index 0.90 or lower (normal about 1.0 to 1.4).","Leriche syndrome (aortoiliac block): buttock claudication, absent femoral pulses, erectile dysfunction."]},{"h":"Veins: DVT, PE, varicose veins","points":["DVT: Virchow triad; one swollen, warm, painful leg, though many are silent.","PE: sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia with a raised A-a gradient; saddle embolus can cause sudden death.","Varicose veins: incompetent valves in superficial veins (great saphenous); risks are age, female sex, pregnancy, obesity, long standing.","Chronic venous disease: edema, stasis dermatitis, ulcers near the medial malleolus; superficial varicose veins rarely embolize."]},{"h":"Vasospasm and vasculitis","points":["Raynaud: cold or stress triggers white, then blue, then red fingers; primary is benign in young women, secondary goes with scleroderma, SLE, Buerger disease.","Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger): heavy smokers, often young men; segmental inflammation and thrombosis of small and medium arteries spreading to veins and nerves; stopping tobacco is the key step.","Giant cell arteritis: over age 50, women more than men; headache, jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, high ESR; can cause sudden blindness.","Half or so of GCA patients have polymyalgia rheumatica (shoulder and hip girdle stiffness).","Vasculitis is grouped by vessel size: large, medium, small (see chart)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Arterial conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Arteriosclerosis and atherosclerosis","Smoking, hypertension, high LDL, diabetes, age, male sex, family history","Endothelial injury, LDL oxidation, foam cells, smooth muscle proliferation, fibrous plaque","Often silent until narrowing or rupture; angina, claudication, TIA","MI, stroke, PAD, aneurysm, mesenteric and renal ischemia"],["Familial hypercholesterolemia","Autosomal dominant LDLR mutation (also APOB, PCSK9)","Poor LDL clearance by the liver raises plasma LDL from birth","Very high LDL, tendon xanthomas, xanthelasma, early corneal arcus","Premature coronary disease and MI"],["Aneurysm","Abdominal: atherosclerosis, smoking, male, older age. Thoracic: hypertension, Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, syphilis","Weakened wall (media damage or matrix defect) bulges outward","Pulsatile abdominal mass; often found by chance on imaging","Rupture, thrombosis, embolism, pressure on nearby structures"],["Aortic dissection","Hypertension, Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, bicuspid aortic valve, Turner syndrome, cocaine, pregnancy","Intimal tear; blood splits the media and forms a false lumen","Sudden tearing chest pain to the back, unequal arm pressures, widened mediastinum","Tamponade, aortic regurgitation, MI, stroke, organ ischemia, rupture"],["Peripheral arterial disease","Smoking, diabetes, hypertension, high LDL, age","Atherosclerotic narrowing of leg arteries limits flow during exercise","Claudication, weak pulses, hairless shiny skin, ABI 0.90 or lower","Rest pain, nonhealing ulcers, gangrene, amputation"]]},{"title":"Venous and embolic conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Thrombosis, deep vein (DVT)","Immobility, surgery, trauma, pregnancy, estrogen, cancer, inherited thrombophilia","Virchow triad: stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability","Unilateral leg swelling, pain, warmth; often silent","Pulmonary embolism, post-thrombotic syndrome"],["Pulmonary embolism (PE)","Source is usually a deep vein of the thigh or pelvis; same risks as DVT","Clot blocks pulmonary arteries, creating dead space and raising RV afterload","Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia, respiratory alkalosis","Acute right heart failure, sudden death, pulmonary infarct, chronic pulmonary hypertension"],["Varicose veins","Age, female sex, pregnancy, obesity, long standing, family history","Incompetent valves raise pressure in superficial veins, which dilate and twist","Visible tortuous leg veins, aching, edema","Stasis dermatitis, venous ulcers, superficial thrombophlebitis"]]},{"title":"Vasospastic and vasculitic conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Giant cell arteritis (temporal arteritis)","Age over 50, female, northern European ancestry; polymyalgia rheumatica","T-cell driven granulomatous inflammation of large and medium arteries (carotid branches); patchy skip lesions","Headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, visual symptoms, high ESR and CRP","Permanent blindness (ophthalmic artery), aortic aneurysm"],["Raynaud phenomenon (primary and secondary)","Primary: young women, no cause. Secondary: scleroderma, SLE, Buerger disease, some drugs","Exaggerated vasospasm of digital arteries and arterioles","White, then blue, then red fingers or toes on cold or stress","Primary: none serious. Secondary: digital ulcers and gangrene"],["Thromboangiitis obliterans","Heavy tobacco use; usually young men","Segmental inflammation and thrombosis of small and medium arteries, spreading to veins and nerves","Instep claudication, superficial migratory phlebitis, Raynaud, severe ischemic pain","Ulcers, gangrene, digit amputation"],["Vasculitis","Immune complexes (hepatitis B in PAN, hepatitis C in cryoglobulinemia), ANCA, T-cell granulomas, infection","Inflammation and necrosis of the vessel wall narrow or weaken the vessel","Fever, weight loss, palpable purpura (small vessel), organ ischemia by vessel size","Organ infarction, aneurysm, hemorrhage, glomerulonephritis"]]},{"title":"Vasculitis by vessel size","cols":["Vessel size","Disease","Classic patient and clues"],"rows":[["Large","Giant cell arteritis","Over 50, headache, jaw claudication, high ESR, granulomas"],["Large","Takayasu arteritis","Asian woman under 40, weak arm pulses (pulseless disease), granulomatous aortic arch disease"],["Medium","Polyarteritis nodosa","Hepatitis B link, spares the lungs, renal and mesenteric microaneurysms, transmural fibrinoid necrosis"],["Medium","Kawasaki disease","Child under 5, fever 5 days or more, rash, red eyes, strawberry tongue; coronary aneurysms"],["Medium","Thromboangiitis obliterans","Young heavy smoker, distal limb ischemia"],["Small","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA); sinus, lung and kidney necrotizing granulomas"],["Small","Microscopic polyangiitis","MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA); lung and kidney, no granulomas"],["Small","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Asthma, eosinophilia, MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA), neuropathy"],["Small","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura)","Child after an upper respiratory infection; palpable purpura on buttocks and legs, joint pain, abdominal pain, IgA nephropathy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CRASH and Burn (Kawasaki disease)","text":"Conjunctival injection, Rash, Adenopathy (cervical), Strawberry tongue, Hand and foot redness and swelling, and Burn (fever lasting 5 days or more)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Raynaud colors in order","text":"White (spasm, no flow), blue (deoxygenated blood sitting still), red (blood rushing back)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Arterial versus venous leg ulcer","text":"Arterial: toes or pressure points, punched out, painful, pale cold foot, weak pulses. Venous: above the medial malleolus, shallow, edema and brown skin, pulses present."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Smoker with a cold limb","text":"Older smoker with claudication and calf pain points to PAD. Young heavy smoker with digit ischemia and migrating phlebitis points to Buerger disease."}],"highYield":["Hypertension is the most important risk factor for aortic dissection.","Abdominal aortic aneurysm is mainly atherosclerotic; thoracic aneurysm is mainly hypertension or connective tissue disease.","Familial hypercholesterolemia: LDL receptor defect, tendon xanthomas, early MI.","Giant cell arteritis needs prompt treatment to prevent blindness; biopsy can miss skip lesions.","Most PEs come from proximal deep leg veins; sinus tachycardia is the most common ECG finding.","c-ANCA (PR3) goes with granulomatosis with polyangiitis; p-ANCA (MPO) with microscopic polyangiitis and EGPA.","PAN spares the lungs and links to hepatitis B.","Buerger disease is treated by stopping tobacco completely."],"pitfalls":["Monckeberg sclerosis calcifies the media without narrowing the lumen, so it gives falsely high ankle pressures; it differs from atherosclerosis.","Primary Raynaud is symmetric and benign; asymmetric attacks, ulcers, or an older patient suggest secondary Raynaud.","Aortic dissection versus MI: dissection gives tearing back pain and unequal arm pressures, and thrombolytics are dangerous.","Takayasu and giant cell arteritis share granulomas; age (under 40 versus over 50) is the main split."],"dumps":[{"t":"Atherosclerotic plaque","p":"Draw an artery wall in cross-section and build an atherosclerotic plaque step by step. Label each cell type and the order of events.","l":["Endothelial injury from smoking, hypertension, high LDL or high glucose","LDL enters the intima and is oxidized","Monocytes stick, enter, and become macrophages","Macrophages eat oxidized LDL and become foam cells (fatty streak)","Smooth muscle cells migrate from the media (PDGF) and lay down matrix","Fibrous cap over a necrotic lipid core","Cap rupture exposes contents and triggers thrombosis"]},{"t":"Vasculitis map","p":"Draw three vessels of decreasing size (aorta, medium artery, capillary). Write two vasculitides next to each, with one lab or clinical clue for each disease.","l":["Large: giant cell arteritis (over 50, jaw claudication, high ESR)","Large: Takayasu arteritis (young woman, weak arm pulses)","Medium: polyarteritis nodosa (hepatitis B, spares lung, microaneurysms)","Medium: Kawasaki disease (child, coronary aneurysm)","Small: granulomatosis with polyangiitis (PR3-ANCA, sinus, lung, kidney)","Small: IgA vasculitis (child, palpable purpura, IgA nephropathy)","Small vessel disease gives palpable purpura"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Aortic Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4QvHj5wXdWA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Venous Thromboembolism (DVT, PE) | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0SVP95BOUNI"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Vasculitis Overview - types and classification","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YwW_Wwkhxnk"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Giant cell Arteritis and Takayasu arteritis (Large Vessel Vasculitis) - signs, pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ck8W4GcYYe8"}],"related":["CV-18-H","CV-18-C","CV-13","CV-18-G1"]},"CV-18-J":{"id":"CV-18-J","summary":"Vascular neoplasms on the blueprint are hemangiomas (benign, very common, especially in infants) and Kaposi sarcoma (an HHV-8 driven tumor seen with immune suppression). Exam questions focus on the virus, the patient group, and telling these lesions from look-alikes.","notes":[{"h":"Hemangioma basics","points":["Benign tumors made of blood vessels; the most common tumors of infancy and childhood.","Most occur in the skin of the head and neck; cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign tumor of the liver.","Capillary type: small, thin-walled capillary-sized vessels in skin and mucosa.","Cavernous type: large dilated blood-filled channels; less well circumscribed; does not regress."]},{"h":"Infantile hemangioma","points":["Appears in the first weeks of life, grows fast for months, then slowly fades.","Most regress by about age 7.","Bright red raised lesion, often called a strawberry hemangioma.","GLUT1 positive on staining, which separates it from vascular malformations."]},{"h":"Other benign vascular lesions","points":["Pyogenic granuloma (lobular capillary hemangioma): fast-growing red polyp on skin, gums or mouth that bleeds easily, often after minor trauma.","Pregnancy tumor (granuloma gravidarum) is a pyogenic granuloma of the gingiva during pregnancy.","Cherry hemangiomas are small red papules that increase with age in adults."]},{"h":"Kaposi sarcoma","points":["Caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8, also called KSHV), a DNA virus.","Spindle cells form slit-like vascular spaces with red cells leaking between them.","Lesions progress from purple patches to plaques to nodules.","HHV-8 latent proteins push cell growth: a viral cyclin D homolog, and LANA, which interferes with p53 and Rb.","Immune suppression lets the infected cells grow; tumors may shrink when immunity improves.","HHV-8 also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease."]},{"h":"Four forms of Kaposi sarcoma","points":["Classic: older men of Mediterranean, Eastern European or Ashkenazi Jewish background; slow; lower legs.","Endemic African: children and young adults; children can have aggressive lymph node disease.","Transplant-associated: from immunosuppressive drugs; often regresses when the drugs are reduced.","AIDS-associated: an AIDS-defining illness; spreads widely to skin, mouth, gut, lungs and lymph nodes."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hemangiomas and Kaposi sarcoma compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Hemangiomas (capillary and cavernous)","Unknown cause; very common in infants and children","Benign proliferation of endothelial cells forming capillary or large cavernous channels","Red to blue skin lesion in head and neck; cavernous hemangioma as a liver mass on imaging","Usually none; large or deep ones can bleed, compress structures, or cause cosmetic or vision problems"],["Infantile (strawberry) hemangioma","Infants, more often girls and preterm babies","Rapid endothelial growth phase then involution; GLUT1 positive","Bright red raised lesion appearing in the first weeks of life","Ulceration, bleeding, airway or eye obstruction; most regress by about age 7"],["Pyogenic granuloma (lobular capillary hemangioma)","Minor trauma, pregnancy","Rapidly growing lobules of capillaries with inflamed, often ulcerated surface","Red polypoid lesion on skin, gums or lips that bleeds easily","Repeated bleeding; may recur after removal"],["Kaposi sarcoma","HHV-8 infection plus immune suppression (AIDS, transplant drugs) or older age in the classic form","HHV-8 latent genes drive proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vessels","Purple patches, plaques and nodules on skin; lesions in mouth, gut, lungs","GI bleeding, lung involvement with respiratory failure, lymphedema"]]},{"title":"Forms of Kaposi sarcoma","cols":["Form","Who gets it","Typical sites","Behavior"],"rows":[["Classic","Older men of Mediterranean, Eastern European or Ashkenazi Jewish background","Lower legs","Slow, rarely spreads to organs"],["Endemic African","Children and young adults in sub-Saharan Africa","Skin; lymph nodes in children","Can be aggressive in children"],["Transplant-associated","Organ transplant patients on immunosuppressive drugs","Skin, lymph nodes, organs","Often regresses when immunosuppression is reduced"],["AIDS-associated","People with untreated HIV and low CD4 counts","Skin, mouth, gut, lungs, lymph nodes","Widespread and aggressive; improves with antiretroviral therapy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Purple lesion in a patient with HIV","text":"Ask whether it responds to antibiotics. Bacillary angiomatosis (Bartonella) has neutrophils and clears with antibiotics. Kaposi sarcoma has lymphocytes and spindle cells and is driven by HHV-8."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Which hemangioma goes away","text":"Infantile hemangioma grows then shrinks on its own. Cavernous hemangioma and cherry hemangioma stay."},{"kind":"trick","label":"HHV-8 three diseases","text":"Link HHV-8 to three things: Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, and multicentric Castleman disease."}],"highYield":["Kaposi sarcoma is caused by HHV-8 (KSHV).","Kaposi sarcoma is an AIDS-defining illness.","Histology: spindle cells, slit-like vascular spaces, extravasated red cells.","Infantile hemangiomas grow fast and then regress in most children.","Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor.","Pyogenic granuloma bleeds easily and can appear in pregnancy."],"pitfalls":["Kaposi sarcoma versus bacillary angiomatosis: both are red-purple lesions in AIDS; bacillary angiomatosis has neutrophils and is caused by Bartonella.","Pyogenic granuloma is a vascular lesion and is neither infectious nor granulomatous, despite its name.","Angiosarcoma is a separate malignant endothelial tumor (linked to vinyl chloride, arsenic, and chronic lymphedema); do not confuse it with Kaposi sarcoma."],"dumps":[{"t":"HHV-8 to Kaposi sarcoma","p":"Trace how HHV-8 infection becomes a Kaposi sarcoma lesion in a person with untreated HIV. Draw the lesion stages and label the cell types.","l":["HHV-8 is a DNA herpesvirus that infects endothelial-lineage cells","Low CD4 count weakens T-cell control of the virus","Latent proteins drive growth: viral cyclin D homolog and LANA blocking p53 and Rb","Spindle cells form slit-like vessels with leaking red cells","Lesions progress: patch, plaque, nodule","Spread to mouth, gut, lungs and lymph nodes in the AIDS form","Restoring immunity with antiretroviral therapy can shrink lesions"]},{"t":"Vascular lesion comparison","p":"Build a table comparing infantile hemangioma, cavernous hemangioma, pyogenic granuloma, and Kaposi sarcoma. Use columns for patient, appearance, behavior, and cause.","l":["Infantile: infant, bright red raised lesion, grows then regresses","Cavernous: large dilated channels, skin or liver, does not regress","Pyogenic granuloma: red polyp that bleeds, after trauma or in pregnancy","Kaposi sarcoma: purple patches to nodules, HHV-8","Only Kaposi sarcoma is malignant","Kaposi sarcoma forms: classic, endemic, transplant, AIDS"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, section on vascular tumors"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses, section on human herpesvirus 8"}],"videos":[],"related":["CV-18-I","CV-16","CV-17"]},"CV-18-K":{"id":"CV-18-K","summary":"Infectious vascular diseases are infections that damage the heart or blood vessels: bacterial endocarditis, Chagas disease, Lyme disease, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, viral hemorrhagic fevers, and viral myocarditis. Exams test the organism, how it spreads, and the cardiovascular damage it causes.","notes":[{"h":"Bacterial endocarditis","points":["Acute: Staphylococcus aureus, high virulence, attacks normal valves; IV drug use favors the tricuspid valve.","Subacute: viridans streptococci, low virulence, attacks damaged valves, often after dental work; dextrans help them stick to fibrin-platelet clumps.","Prosthetic valves: Staphylococcus epidermidis. Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) suggests colon cancer.","Culture-negative causes: HACEK organisms, Coxiella, Bartonella.","Signs: fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages.","Complications: valve destruction, heart failure, septic emboli, glomerulonephritis, mycotic aneurysm."]},{"h":"Chagas disease","points":["Trypanosoma cruzi, a flagellated protozoan; Mexico, Central and South America.","Triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bug bites, defecates, and parasites in the feces enter the bite or mucosa.","Also spread by transfusion, transplant, mother to baby, and contaminated food.","Acute: often mild; Romana sign (one swollen eyelid) when entry is through the conjunctiva.","Chronic (in about 20 to 30 percent, years later): dilated cardiomyopathy with conduction block and apical aneurysm, megaesophagus, megacolon."]},{"h":"Lyme disease","points":["Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete; Ixodes tick; Northeast and upper Midwest US.","Early localized: erythema migrans (expanding bull's-eye rash) plus flu-like illness.","Early disseminated: facial nerve palsy (can be bilateral), meningitis, carditis with AV block.","Late: arthritis of large joints such as the knee, and neurologic disease."]},{"h":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever","points":["Rickettsia rickettsii, an obligate intracellular gram-negative bacterium; Dermacentor tick.","Most US cases are in the southeastern and south-central states despite the name.","Infects endothelial cells, causing vasculitis, leaky vessels, edema and microhemorrhage.","Fever, headache, and a rash that starts at wrists and ankles, spreads to the trunk, and involves palms and soles.","Untreated: shock, pulmonary edema, encephalitis, DIC, death."]},{"h":"Viral hemorrhagic fevers","points":["Enveloped RNA viruses that damage endothelium and clotting, causing leaky vessels, bleeding and shock.","Yellow fever: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito; liver necrosis with Councilman bodies, jaundice, black vomit.","Dengue: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito, four serotypes; fever, severe muscle and joint pain, low platelets.","Severe dengue is more likely with a second infection by a different serotype (antibody-dependent enhancement).","Filoviruses (Ebola, Marburg): filamentous negative-sense RNA viruses; spread by body fluids; fruit bats are the likely reservoir; high fatality."]},{"h":"Viral myocarditis","points":["Coxsackie B is the classic cause; also adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, influenza, SARS-CoV-2.","Coxsackie B and adenovirus enter myocytes through the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR).","Injury comes from direct viral damage plus cytotoxic T-cell attack.","Young adult after a cold or GI illness with chest pain, arrhythmia, raised troponin, heart failure."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infectious vascular diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Organism and spread","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Cardiovascular complications"],"rows":[["Bacterial endocarditis","S. aureus (acute, IV drug use), viridans strep (subacute, dental), S. epidermidis (prosthetic valve), enterococci; spread through the bloodstream","Bacteria colonize valve vegetations; acute forms destroy valve tissue","Fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages","Valve regurgitation, heart failure, septic emboli, mycotic aneurysm, ring abscess"],["Chagas disease","Trypanosoma cruzi (protozoan); triatomine bug feces, transfusion, transplant, congenital, food","Intracellular amastigotes in heart muscle and gut nerves; parasite persistence plus immune damage","Romana sign or chagoma early; decades later heart failure, megaesophagus, megacolon","Dilated cardiomyopathy, apical aneurysm, conduction block, arrhythmia, emboli"],["Lyme disease","Borrelia burgdorferi (spirochete); Ixodes tick","Spirochete spreads from the bite to skin, nerves, heart and joints","Erythema migrans, facial palsy, migratory or knee arthritis","Lyme carditis with AV block, myocarditis"],["Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Rickettsia rickettsii (obligate intracellular gram-negative); Dermacentor tick","Infects vascular endothelium causing vasculitis and leaky vessels","Fever, headache, rash from wrists and ankles inward including palms and soles","Hypotension and shock, edema, DIC, myocarditis"],["Viral hemorrhagic fever (yellow fever, dengue, filoviruses)","Yellow fever and dengue: flaviviruses, Aedes mosquito. Ebola and Marburg: filoviruses, body fluids","Endothelial and immune cell infection causes capillary leak and clotting failure","High fever, bleeding, low platelets; jaundice in yellow fever; bone-breaking pain in dengue","Hypovolemic shock, DIC, multiorgan failure"],["Viral myocarditis","Coxsackie B (classic), adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, SARS-CoV-2; respiratory or fecal-oral spread","Direct viral injury plus cytotoxic T-cell attack on myocytes","Recent viral illness, chest pain, arrhythmia, raised troponin","Acute heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden death, dilated cardiomyopathy"]]},{"title":"Viral hemorrhagic fevers compared","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Spread","Key features"],"rows":[["Yellow fever","Flavivirus; enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA","Aedes aegypti mosquito; monkeys in the jungle cycle","Fever, jaundice from midzonal liver necrosis, Councilman bodies, black vomit; live vaccine available"],["Dengue","Flavivirus; enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA; four serotypes","Aedes mosquitoes","Fever, retro-orbital pain, severe muscle and joint pain, rash, low platelets; severe dengue with plasma leak more likely on reinfection"],["Ebola and Marburg","Filoviruses; enveloped negative-sense single-stranded RNA, filament-shaped","Contact with blood or body fluids; fruit bats likely reservoir","Fever, vomiting and diarrhea, bleeding, DIC, high case fatality"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Bacteria FROM JANE (endocarditis signs)","text":"Fever, Roth spots, Osler nodes, Murmur, Janeway lesions, Anemia, Nail-bed (splinter) hemorrhages, Emboli."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"FAKE a Key Lyme pie (Lyme disease)","text":"Facial nerve palsy, Arthritis, Kardiac block, Erythema migrans."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"You drive CARS using your palms and soles","text":"Rashes on palms and soles: Coxsackie A (hand-foot-mouth disease), Rocky Mountain spotted fever, Syphilis (secondary)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Osler versus Janeway","text":"Osler nodes are Ouch: painful, on finger pads, immune complex. Janeway lesions are painless, on palms and soles, from septic microemboli."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the most common cause of endocarditis in IV drug users and attacks the tricuspid valve.","S. gallolyticus (bovis) endocarditis means look for colon cancer.","Chagas disease is a classic infectious cause of dilated cardiomyopathy in Latin America.","Lyme carditis usually shows AV block.","RMSF rash moves from wrists and ankles toward the trunk and involves palms and soles.","Second dengue infection with a new serotype raises the risk of severe dengue.","Yellow fever liver shows Councilman bodies (apoptotic hepatocytes).","Coxsackie B is the classic viral cause of myocarditis."],"pitfalls":["RMSF rash moves from the limbs inward (centripetal); typhus rash starts on the trunk and spreads outward and spares palms and soles.","Lyme erythema migrans appears at the bite site days after a tick bite; a small red bump right after the bite is a local reaction.","Libman-Sacks endocarditis in SLE is sterile, so it does not count as bacterial endocarditis.","Romana sign is unilateral eyelid swelling; bilateral eyelid swelling points to other causes such as nephrotic syndrome."],"dumps":[{"t":"Endocarditis pathway","p":"Trace subacute bacterial endocarditis from a dental procedure to a kidney complication. Draw the valve vegetation and label each step.","l":["Damaged valve (for example after rheumatic disease) has a sterile platelet-fibrin clump","Dental work causes transient bacteremia with viridans streptococci","Dextrans help the bacteria stick to the fibrin-platelet clump","Vegetation grows on the valve, usually the mitral valve","Pieces break off as septic emboli to brain, kidney, spleen","Immune complexes cause glomerulonephritis","Osler nodes and Roth spots reflect immune complex and embolic damage"]},{"t":"Tick and bug map","p":"Build a table of the vector-borne diseases in this unit. Columns: disease, organism, vector, main US or world region, and cardiovascular effect.","l":["Lyme: Borrelia burgdorferi, Ixodes tick, Northeast and upper Midwest, AV block","RMSF: Rickettsia rickettsii, Dermacentor tick, southeastern and south-central US, vasculitis and shock","Chagas: Trypanosoma cruzi, triatomine bug, Latin America, dilated cardiomyopathy","Yellow fever: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito, Africa and South America, hemorrhage and shock","Dengue: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito, tropics worldwide, plasma leak and shock","Ebola has no insect vector; spread by body fluids"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on infective endocarditis and myocarditis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spirochetes and Other Spiral Microorganisms"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Arthropod-Borne and Rodent-Borne Viral Diseases"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on Trypanosoma cruzi in the medical parasitology chapter"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Infective Endocarditis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iaO8110iSzI"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Myocarditis - causes, pathophysiology, investigation and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=INSa4QQcwFo"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is myocarditis and pericarditis? | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xL98lBuAn1A"}],"related":["CV-17","CV-16","CV-18-D3","CV-18-E2"]},"EN-1":{"id":"EN-1","summary":"Where each endocrine gland comes from in the embryo: the thyroid from the floor of the pharynx, the parathyroids and C cells from the pharyngeal pouches, the pituitary from two ectoderm sources, the adrenal from mesoderm plus neural crest, and the islets from foregut endoderm. Exam questions tie each origin to the defect, ectopic tissue, or tumor that results.","notes":[{"h":"Thyroid","points":["The thyroid is the first endocrine gland to form, starting around week 4 as an endodermal diverticulum in the floor of the pharynx.","Its origin is marked in the adult by the foramen cecum, a pit at the junction of the anterior two thirds and posterior third of the tongue.","The gland descends in the midline, anterior to the hyoid bone and laryngeal cartilages, and stays linked to the tongue by the thyroglossal duct.","It reaches its final place in front of the trachea by about week 7, and the duct normally disappears.","A leftover duct segment can form a thyroglossal duct cyst: a midline neck mass that rises when the tongue is protruded.","A lower remnant of the duct can persist as a pyramidal lobe.","Failed descent leaves a lingual thyroid at the base of the tongue, which may be the patient's only thyroid tissue."]},{"h":"Parathyroids and C cells","points":["Third pouch: dorsal wing forms the inferior parathyroids, ventral wing forms the thymus.","Fourth pouch: forms the superior parathyroids.","The inferior parathyroids travel down with the thymus, so they end up below the superior pair and can stop anywhere along that path, including in the thymus or anterior mediastinum.","The ultimobranchial body (from the caudal part of the fourth pouch) joins the thyroid and carries in neural crest cells that become parafollicular C cells (calcitonin)."]},{"h":"Pituitary","points":["Anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis): Rathke pouch, an upgrowth of oral ectoderm from the roof of the stomodeum.","Posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis): the infundibulum, a downgrowth of neuroectoderm from the floor of the diencephalon.","The two parts meet and Rathke pouch loses its connection to the mouth.","Remnants of Rathke pouch can give rise to craniopharyngioma: a calcified, cystic suprasellar mass, common in children (with a second peak in older adults), that can cause bitemporal hemianopia and hypopituitarism."]},{"h":"Adrenal gland","points":["Cortex: mesoderm (coelomic epithelium of the posterior abdominal wall), first as a large fetal cortex, then the adult cortex.","Medulla: neural crest cells that invade the cortex and become chromaffin cells, which act like postganglionic sympathetic neurons.","Neural crest tumors of the medulla: pheochromocytoma (mainly adults, episodic catecholamine excess) and neuroblastoma (children, the most common extracranial solid tumor of childhood)."]},{"h":"DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion)","points":["The third and fourth pharyngeal pouches fail to develop normally.","Absent or small parathyroids, so little PTH: neonatal hypocalcemia with tetany or seizures.","Absent or small thymus: T cell deficiency, recurrent viral and fungal infections, and no thymic shadow on chest X-ray.","Also conotruncal heart defects (truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot), cleft palate, and abnormal facies."]},{"h":"Pancreatic islets","points":["The pancreas forms from dorsal and ventral buds of foregut endoderm.","The ventral bud forms the uncinate process and part of the head; the dorsal bud forms the rest.","Islet cells arise from the same endoderm as the ducts and acini, appear around the third month, and begin secreting insulin around the fifth month."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Endocrine gland origins and defects","cols":["Structure","Embryonic origin","Related defect or tumor"],"rows":[["Thyroid follicular cells","Endoderm of pharyngeal floor (foramen cecum)","Thyroglossal duct cyst, lingual thyroid"],["Parafollicular C cells","Neural crest via ultimobranchial body","Medullary thyroid carcinoma"],["Inferior parathyroids","Third pharyngeal pouch (with thymus)","Ectopic gland in thymus or mediastinum; absent in DiGeorge"],["Superior parathyroids","Fourth pharyngeal pouch","Absent in DiGeorge"],["Anterior pituitary","Rathke pouch (oral ectoderm)","Craniopharyngioma from remnants"],["Posterior pituitary","Infundibulum (diencephalic neuroectoderm)","Central diabetes insipidus when damaged"],["Adrenal cortex","Mesoderm (coelomic epithelium)","Cortical adenoma, congenital adrenal hyperplasia"],["Adrenal medulla","Neural crest","Pheochromocytoma, neuroblastoma"],["Pancreatic islets","Foregut endoderm (dorsal and ventral buds)","Insulinoma and other islet tumors"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Three goes lower than four","text":"The third pouch gives the inferior parathyroids and the fourth gives the superior ones. The third pouch glands ride down with the thymus, so they pass the fourth pouch glands on the way."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CATCH-22","text":"Cardiac defects, Abnormal facies, Thymic aplasia, Cleft palate, Hypocalcemia from absent parathyroids, all from a deletion on chromosome 22 (22q11.2)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Mouth up, brain down","text":"The anterior pituitary grows up from the roof of the mouth (Rathke pouch) and the posterior pituitary grows down from the brain (infundibulum)."}],"highYield":["A midline neck mass that moves up when the tongue sticks out is a thyroglossal duct cyst; a lateral mass along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid points to a branchial cleft cyst.","Check for normal thyroid tissue in the neck before removing a lingual thyroid, since it may be the only functioning thyroid.","Hypocalcemic tetany in the first days of life with absent thymic shadow means DiGeorge syndrome.","A missing inferior parathyroid adenoma at neck surgery is often found in the thymus or anterior mediastinum.","Craniopharyngioma is the classic calcified suprasellar mass in a child and comes from Rathke pouch.","Both pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma make catecholamines because both come from the neural crest sympathoadrenal lineage."],"pitfalls":["The inferior parathyroids come from the third pouch and the superior ones from the fourth; the numbers run opposite to adult position.","C cells sit in the thyroid but come from neural crest, while follicular cells come from endoderm.","The adrenal cortex is mesoderm and the medulla is neural crest; only the medulla gives neuroblastoma and pheochromocytoma.","Posterior pituitary tissue is neural tissue that stores and releases hormones made in the hypothalamus."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pharyngeal pouch map","p":"Draw pharyngeal pouches 1 through 4 on the side of the embryonic pharynx. Label the adult structure each one forms, then draw arrows showing where the parathyroids and thymus move.","l":["First pouch: middle ear cavity and auditory tube","Second pouch: palatine tonsil","Third pouch dorsal wing: inferior parathyroid","Third pouch ventral wing: thymus","Fourth pouch: superior parathyroid","Ultimobranchial body: C cells from neural crest","Inferior parathyroids migrate with the thymus and end below the superior pair","DiGeorge: third and fourth pouches fail, causing hypocalcemia and T cell deficiency"]},{"t":"Endocrine origin table","p":"Make a table on blank paper with columns for gland, germ layer source, and the defect or tumor tied to that source. Fill in thyroid, C cells, parathyroids, both pituitary lobes, adrenal cortex, adrenal medulla, and islets.","l":["Thyroid from pharyngeal floor endoderm at the foramen cecum","Thyroglossal duct cyst and lingual thyroid from abnormal descent","Anterior pituitary from oral ectoderm (Rathke pouch), linked to craniopharyngioma","Posterior pituitary from diencephalic neuroectoderm","Adrenal cortex from mesoderm","Adrenal medulla from neural crest, linked to pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma","Islets from foregut endoderm of the pancreatic buds"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck (sections on pharyngeal pouches and the thyroid gland)"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Central Nervous System (section on the hypophysis)"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Section on the suprarenal glands"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-2","EN-3","EN-12-E1","EN-12-B3","EN-12-G4","EN-12-G6"]},"EN-2":{"id":"EN-2","summary":"What each endocrine gland looks like under the microscope, which cell makes which hormone, and which germ layer each cell type comes from. Exam questions show a slide description and ask for the hormone, the disease, or the stain that fits.","notes":[{"h":"Thyroid","points":["Follicles: a single layer of follicular cells surrounding colloid, which is stored thyroglobulin.","Resting gland: flat to cuboidal cells and large follicles full of colloid.","Stimulated gland (TSH or TSH receptor antibodies): tall columnar cells and scant colloid with a scalloped edge where it is being taken back into the cells.","Graves disease shows this active pattern, often with papillary infoldings and patchy lymphocytes.","Parafollicular C cells: pale cells between or beside the follicles that secrete calcitonin."]},{"h":"Adrenal gland","points":["Zona glomerulosa: thin outer layer of cells in rounded clusters; makes aldosterone.","Zona fasciculata: thickest layer, long straight cords of pale, lipid-filled cells (spongiocytes); makes cortisol.","Zona reticularis: innermost cortex, a branching network of smaller, darker cells; makes adrenal androgens (DHEA).","Medulla: chromaffin cells that secrete catecholamines, mostly epinephrine, under sympathetic preganglionic control."]},{"h":"Pituitary","points":["Acidophils (pink): somatotrophs (GH) and lactotrophs (prolactin).","Basophils (blue or purple): gonadotrophs (FSH, LH), corticotrophs (ACTH), thyrotrophs (TSH).","Chromophobes: pale cells with few granules, many of them degranulated hormone cells.","Posterior pituitary has no hormone-making cells: it holds unmyelinated axons from the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, glial pituicytes, and capillaries.","Herring bodies are swellings of those axon terminals packed with granules of ADH or oxytocin."]},{"h":"Parathyroid and islets","points":["Parathyroid chief cells: small, numerous cells that secrete PTH.","Oxyphil cells: larger cells with pink, mitochondria-rich cytoplasm that appear around puberty and increase with age; their function is unclear.","Islets of Langerhans: pale clusters of cells scattered among the darker exocrine acini.","Beta cells (insulin) are the most numerous; alpha cells make glucagon; delta cells make somatostatin."]},{"h":"Derivation and neuroendocrine stains","points":["Neural crest: adrenal chromaffin cells and thyroid C cells.","Endoderm: thyroid follicular cells, parathyroids, and islets.","Ectoderm: adenohypophysis (oral ectoderm) and neurohypophysis (neuroectoderm). Mesoderm: adrenal cortex.","Neuroendocrine cells store hormones in dense-core secretory granules that contain chromogranin and express synaptophysin.","Immunostains for chromogranin and synaptophysin mark neuroendocrine tumors such as pheochromocytoma, medullary thyroid carcinoma, carcinoid tumors, and islet cell tumors."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Adrenal layers","cols":["Layer","Histology","Main product","Main control"],"rows":[["Zona glomerulosa","Thin outer layer, rounded clusters","Aldosterone","Angiotensin II and potassium"],["Zona fasciculata","Thick, straight cords of lipid-filled pale cells","Cortisol","ACTH"],["Zona reticularis","Branching network of smaller cells","Adrenal androgens (DHEA)","ACTH"],["Medulla","Chromaffin cells in clusters around vessels","Epinephrine and norepinephrine","Sympathetic preganglionic fibers"]]},{"title":"Endocrine cells, products, and origin","cols":["Cell","Location","Hormone","Origin"],"rows":[["Follicular cell","Thyroid","T4 and T3","Endoderm"],["C cell","Thyroid","Calcitonin","Neural crest"],["Chief cell","Parathyroid","PTH","Endoderm"],["Acidophil","Anterior pituitary","GH, prolactin","Oral ectoderm"],["Basophil","Anterior pituitary","FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH","Oral ectoderm"],["Hypothalamic axon terminal (Herring body)","Posterior pituitary","ADH, oxytocin (stored)","Neuroectoderm"],["Alpha, beta, delta cells","Pancreatic islet","Glucagon, insulin, somatostatin","Endoderm"],["Chromaffin cell","Adrenal medulla","Epinephrine, norepinephrine","Neural crest"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"GFR: salt, sugar, sex","text":"Glomerulosa, Fasciculata, Reticularis from outside in, making salt (aldosterone), sugar (cortisol), and sex steroids (androgens). The deeper you go, the sweeter it gets."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"B-FLAT","text":"Basophils make FSH, LH, ACTH, and TSH. The acidophils are the rest: GH and prolactin."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Busy cells stand tall","text":"A working thyroid cell grows tall and eats its colloid, so tall cells with scalloped colloid mean stimulation, and flat cells with a lake of colloid mean rest."}],"highYield":["Graves disease histology: hyperplastic tall columnar follicular cells with scant, scalloped colloid.","Medullary thyroid carcinoma comes from C cells, makes calcitonin, and stains for chromogranin.","Pheochromocytoma cells are chromaffin cells and stain for chromogranin and synaptophysin.","Excess ACTH enlarges the fasciculata and reticularis of both adrenals; a cortisol-making adrenal tumor suppresses ACTH, so the other adrenal shrinks.","The posterior pituitary stores ADH and oxytocin made by hypothalamic neurons; it makes no hormone of its own.","Beta cells are the most numerous islet cells and are the cells destroyed in type 1 diabetes."],"pitfalls":["Basophils include ACTH cells, and acidophils include GH and prolactin cells; do not reverse them.","Parathyroid chief cells make PTH; oxyphil cells are a separate, larger pink cell type.","Herring bodies are axon terminal swellings; pituicytes are glial support cells, not secretory cells.","Adrenal medulla and C cells are neural crest, while thyroid follicular cells and parathyroids are endoderm."],"dumps":[{"t":"Adrenal cross section","p":"Draw a wedge of adrenal gland from capsule to medulla. Label each layer, its cell arrangement, its main product, and what controls it.","l":["Capsule on the outside","Zona glomerulosa in rounded clusters making aldosterone","Zona fasciculata in straight cords of pale lipid-filled cells making cortisol","Zona reticularis as a branching network making androgens","Medulla with chromaffin cells making mostly epinephrine","Angiotensin II and potassium control the glomerulosa; ACTH controls the fasciculata and reticularis","Medulla controlled by sympathetic preganglionic fibers"]},{"t":"Resting versus active thyroid","p":"Draw two thyroid follicles side by side: one resting and one stimulated, as in Graves disease. Label the cell height, colloid amount, and where a C cell sits.","l":["Resting follicle with flat to cuboidal cells","Resting follicle full of colloid","Active follicle with tall columnar cells","Active follicle with scant colloid and scalloped edges","Colloid labeled as stored thyroglobulin","C cell drawn between or beside follicles","C cell labeled as calcitonin-secreting and neural crest derived"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Endocrine Glands"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System (section on Graves disease)"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-1","EN-5","EN-12-A4","EN-12-G5","EN-12-G6"]},"EN-3":{"id":"EN-3","summary":"Where the endocrine glands sit in the body and what lies next to them. Exam questions use these neighbors to predict what goes wrong in surgery or with a growing tumor: hoarseness after thyroidectomy, hypocalcemia from removed parathyroids, and visual field loss from a pituitary mass.","notes":[{"h":"Thyroid","points":["Two lobes lie beside the larynx and trachea, joined by an isthmus over the second and third tracheal rings.","The gland is enclosed in the pretracheal layer of deep cervical fascia, which binds it to the larynx and trachea.","Because of that attachment, the thyroid and any mass within it rise with the larynx when the patient swallows."]},{"h":"Laryngeal nerves","points":["The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs up in the groove between the trachea and esophagus, close to the inferior thyroid artery near the lower pole.","It supplies every intrinsic laryngeal muscle except the cricothyroid.","Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury: hoarse, weak voice from a paralyzed vocal fold.","Bilateral injury: both folds can rest near the midline, causing stridor and airway obstruction.","The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve runs with the superior thyroid artery to the cricothyroid muscle.","Injury to the external branch causes a weak, monotone voice with loss of high pitch, because the cricothyroid tenses the vocal fold."]},{"h":"Parathyroids","points":["Usually four small glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes, outside the thyroid capsule.","The superior glands are fairly constant, near the level of the cricoid cartilage.","The inferior glands vary most, from the lower thyroid poles down into the thymus or mediastinum, because of their long embryonic migration.","Some people have more than four glands, so surgeons must find each one to spare them in thyroidectomy or to remove an adenoma."]},{"h":"Pituitary","points":["Sits in the hypophyseal fossa of the sella turcica in the sphenoid bone.","The diaphragma sellae, a dural fold, roofs the fossa and lets the stalk pass through.","Above: the optic chiasm, where nasal retinal fibers cross.","Lateral: the cavernous sinuses, containing the internal carotid artery and cranial nerves III, IV, V1, V2, and VI.","Below: the sphenoid sinus, the route for transsphenoidal surgery.","A tumor growing upward presses on the crossing fibers and causes bitemporal hemianopia; growth sideways can cause cranial nerve palsies such as double vision."]},{"h":"Adrenal glands","points":["Retroperitoneal, on the superomedial poles of the kidneys, enclosed in renal fascia with a fat layer separating them from the kidney.","Right adrenal: pyramidal, lying behind the inferior vena cava and touching the bare area of the liver.","Left adrenal: crescent-shaped, along the medial border of the left kidney above the hilum, behind the stomach and pancreas."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Nerve injury at thyroidectomy","cols":["Nerve","Course near thyroid","Muscle supplied","Result of injury"],"rows":[["Recurrent laryngeal (one side)","Tracheoesophageal groove, near the inferior thyroid artery","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","Hoarseness from vocal fold paralysis"],["Recurrent laryngeal (both sides)","Both tracheoesophageal grooves","Same muscles on both sides","Stridor and airway obstruction"],["External branch of superior laryngeal","Runs with the superior thyroid artery to the upper pole","Cricothyroid","Monotone voice, loss of high pitch, voice fatigue"]]},{"title":"Pituitary neighbors and mass effects","cols":["Direction","Structure","Effect of tumor extension"],"rows":[["Above","Optic chiasm","Bitemporal hemianopia"],["Above","Hypothalamus and stalk","Raised prolactin from lost dopamine; diabetes insipidus"],["Lateral","Cavernous sinus (CN III, IV, V1, V2, VI, internal carotid)","Double vision, ptosis, facial numbness"],["Below","Sphenoid sinus","Surgical access route"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"High pitch rides high","text":"The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve travels with the superior thyroid artery at the upper pole and controls high pitch. The recurrent nerve comes up from below and controls nearly everything else."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Wall versus middle","text":"In the cavernous sinus, CN III, IV, V1, and V2 run in the lateral wall, while CN VI runs through the middle of the sinus beside the internal carotid artery."}],"highYield":["Hoarseness after thyroid surgery means recurrent laryngeal nerve injury.","Loss of high notes after thyroid surgery in a singer means injury to the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve.","Bitemporal hemianopia means a mass compressing the optic chiasm from below, most often a pituitary adenoma.","Thyroid nodules move with swallowing; this helps place a neck mass in the thyroid.","A missing inferior parathyroid is often found low in the neck, in the thymus, or in the anterior mediastinum.","The right adrenal lies directly behind the inferior vena cava, which matters in right adrenalectomy."],"pitfalls":["A thyroid mass rises with swallowing; a thyroglossal duct cyst also rises with tongue protrusion.","Bitemporal hemianopia comes from the chiasm; a lesion of one optic tract gives homonymous hemianopia.","The cricothyroid is the one intrinsic laryngeal muscle supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve; the recurrent nerve supplies all the others.","The left adrenal sits along the medial border of the left kidney; only the right adrenal sits on top like a cap behind the IVC."],"dumps":[{"t":"Thyroid from behind","p":"Draw the thyroid and trachea as seen from behind. Add the four parathyroids, the recurrent laryngeal nerves, the superior and inferior thyroid arteries, and the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve.","l":["Isthmus over tracheal rings 2 and 3","Recurrent laryngeal nerves in the tracheoesophageal grooves","Inferior thyroid artery crossing near the recurrent laryngeal nerve","External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve beside the superior thyroid artery","Superior parathyroids near the cricoid level in a constant spot","Inferior parathyroids near the lower poles with a note that they vary","Note of voice change for each nerve injury"]},{"t":"Pituitary in the sella","p":"Draw a coronal section through the sella turcica. Label the pituitary, diaphragma sellae, optic chiasm, cavernous sinuses with their contents, and the sphenoid sinus.","l":["Pituitary in the hypophyseal fossa of the sphenoid","Diaphragma sellae as the roof with the stalk passing through","Optic chiasm directly above","Cavernous sinuses on each side","Internal carotid artery and CN VI inside the sinus","CN III, IV, V1, and V2 in the lateral wall","Sphenoid sinus below","Upward growth causes bitemporal hemianopia"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Neck (section on the thyroid and parathyroid glands)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the cranial cavity and cavernous sinus)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (section on the suprarenal glands)"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-1","EN-4","EN-12-B3","EN-12-G4","EN-12-E1"]},"EN-4":{"id":"EN-4","summary":"How blood reaches and leaves the thyroid, parathyroids, pituitary, adrenals, and islets. The exam uses blood supply to explain why the anterior pituitary infarcts after severe hemorrhage, why calcium falls after thyroid surgery, why the medulla makes epinephrine, and why the liver sees insulin first.","notes":[{"h":"Thyroid","points":["Superior thyroid artery: usually the first branch of the external carotid, enters the upper pole.","Inferior thyroid artery: from the thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian, enters the lower posterior gland.","Thyroid ima artery: present in a minority of people (about 10%), rising from the brachiocephalic trunk or aortic arch to the isthmus; it can bleed in a tracheostomy.","Veins: superior and middle thyroid veins drain to the internal jugular; inferior thyroid veins drain to the brachiocephalic veins."]},{"h":"Parathyroids","points":["Both pairs get their blood mainly from branches of the inferior thyroid artery.","Tying the inferior thyroid artery close to the gland, or removing parathyroid tissue, can leave the parathyroids without PTH output.","Result: hypocalcemia within a day or two of thyroidectomy, with perioral tingling, muscle cramps, and Chvostek and Trousseau signs."]},{"h":"Pituitary portal system","points":["Superior hypophyseal arteries (from the internal carotid) form a capillary plexus in the median eminence.","Hypothalamic releasing hormones enter this plexus and travel down the stalk in hypophyseal portal veins.","The portal veins open into a second capillary bed in the anterior pituitary.","The anterior lobe gets most of its blood through this low-pressure portal route, with little direct arterial supply.","The posterior lobe gets direct arterial blood from the inferior hypophyseal arteries.","Sheehan syndrome: the pituitary enlarges in pregnancy, and severe postpartum hemorrhage with hypotension infarcts the anterior lobe; failure to lactate is often the first sign."]},{"h":"Adrenal","points":["Three arteries on each side: superior suprarenal (from the inferior phrenic), middle suprarenal (from the aorta), inferior suprarenal (from the renal artery).","One central vein on each side: the right drains straight into the inferior vena cava, the left into the left renal vein.","Blood flows from the capsule through cortical sinusoids into the medulla, carrying high cortisol levels.","Cortisol induces PNMT in chromaffin cells, which converts norepinephrine to epinephrine; this is why the medulla mainly makes epinephrine."]},{"h":"Islets and the liver","points":["Pancreatic veins drain to the splenic and superior mesenteric veins, then to the hepatic portal vein.","The liver, the main target of both insulin and glucagon, receives them at much higher concentration than the rest of the body.","The liver removes about half of the insulin in portal blood on its first pass.","C-peptide is released in equal amounts with insulin but is not cleared by the liver much, so it tracks the pancreas's own insulin output better than insulin does.","Injected insulin enters the systemic circulation first, which reverses the normal portal-to-peripheral gradient."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Endocrine blood supply","cols":["Gland","Arteries","Venous drainage","Clinical link"],"rows":[["Thyroid","Superior thyroid (external carotid), inferior thyroid (thyrocervical trunk), sometimes thyroid ima","Superior and middle to internal jugular; inferior to brachiocephalic","Thyroid ima bleeding in tracheostomy"],["Parathyroids","Mainly inferior thyroid artery","Thyroid veins","Hypocalcemia after thyroidectomy"],["Anterior pituitary","Portal veins from the median eminence plexus (superior hypophyseal arteries)","Into the cavernous sinuses","Sheehan syndrome"],["Posterior pituitary","Inferior hypophyseal arteries","Into the cavernous sinuses","Usually spared in Sheehan syndrome"],["Adrenal","Superior (inferior phrenic), middle (aorta), inferior (renal)","Right to IVC; left to left renal vein","Cortisol-rich blood induces PNMT in medulla"],["Pancreatic islets","Branches of splenic, gastroduodenal, and superior mesenteric arteries","Splenic and superior mesenteric veins to portal vein","Hepatic first-pass removal of insulin"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Three in, one out","text":"Each adrenal has three arteries (phrenic, aorta, renal) but only one vein. The left vein joins the left renal vein, just like the left gonadal vein."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Blood flows inside out","text":"Adrenal blood runs from cortex to medulla, so the medulla bathes in cortisol, and cortisol switches on PNMT to finish epinephrine."}],"highYield":["Sheehan syndrome: postpartum hemorrhage, then no lactation, amenorrhea, and later hypothyroid and adrenal insufficiency signs.","The anterior pituitary is vulnerable to ischemia because its blood arrives mainly through low-pressure portal veins.","Tetany or perioral tingling a day after thyroidectomy points to parathyroid devascularization or removal.","The left adrenal vein drains into the left renal vein; the right adrenal vein enters the IVC directly.","C-peptide, not insulin, is the better measure of the pancreas's own insulin secretion.","Insulin given as a drug reaches muscle and fat before the liver, unlike secreted insulin."],"pitfalls":["The posterior pituitary has its own arterial supply and is usually spared in Sheehan syndrome; the anterior lobe is not.","The superior thyroid artery comes from the external carotid; the inferior comes from the subclavian via the thyrocervical trunk.","PNMT is induced by the high cortisol in blood coming from the cortex; chromaffin tissue outside the adrenal lacks this and makes mainly norepinephrine.","High portal insulin is normal physiology, so peripheral insulin levels underestimate actual pancreatic output."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypothalamic-pituitary portal system","p":"Draw the hypothalamus, stalk, and both pituitary lobes. Trace blood from the superior hypophyseal artery to the anterior lobe, and show the separate supply of the posterior lobe.","l":["Superior hypophyseal arteries from the internal carotid","Primary capillary plexus in the median eminence","Releasing hormones entering the primary plexus","Hypophyseal portal veins running down the stalk","Secondary capillary plexus in the anterior lobe","Inferior hypophyseal arteries to the posterior lobe","Note that the anterior lobe infarcts in Sheehan syndrome because of its portal supply"]},{"t":"Adrenal vessels","p":"Draw both kidneys, both adrenals, the aorta, the IVC, and the renal veins. Add the three adrenal arteries and the single vein on each side, then show the path of blood from cortex to medulla.","l":["Superior suprarenal from the inferior phrenic artery","Middle suprarenal from the aorta","Inferior suprarenal from the renal artery","Right adrenal vein into the IVC","Left adrenal vein into the left renal vein","Blood flows from capsule through cortex into medulla","Cortisol in this blood induces PNMT for epinephrine"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Neck (section on the thyroid and parathyroid glands)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (section on the suprarenal glands)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Endocrine Glands (section on the adrenal glands)"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-3","EN-5","EN-12-F1","EN-12-B3","EN-12-B4"]},"EN-5":{"id":"EN-5","summary":"How the hypothalamus and endocrine glands control each other and what signals switch each hormone on or off. The exam tests the hypothalamic hormones and pituitary stalk injury, ADH control, calcium and PTH, aldosterone, the steps of insulin release, and the hormones that defend blood glucose.","notes":[{"h":"Hypothalamus to anterior pituitary","points":["TRH raises TSH (and prolactin); CRH raises ACTH; pulsatile GnRH raises LH and FSH; GHRH raises GH.","Somatostatin lowers GH and TSH; dopamine lowers prolactin.","Prolactin is the only anterior pituitary hormone held down mainly by inhibition.","Pituitary stalk section cuts the portal flow: prolactin rises while GH, ACTH, TSH, LH, and FSH fall.","Stalk section also stops ADH from reaching the posterior lobe, so central diabetes insipidus can develop."]},{"h":"ADH","points":["Made mainly in the supraoptic (and paraventricular) nuclei and released from the posterior pituitary.","Main trigger: a rise in plasma osmolality (normal about 275 to 295 mOsm/kg) sensed by hypothalamic osmoreceptors; a change of about 1% is enough.","Second trigger: a fall in blood volume or pressure sensed by baroreceptors, which needs a larger change but gives a strong response.","ADH binds V2 receptors (Gs, cAMP) on collecting duct principal cells, which insert aquaporin-2 channels into the luminal membrane.","Water then moves out of the duct into the hypertonic medulla, and urine becomes concentrated."]},{"h":"Calcium and PTH","points":["Normal total serum calcium is about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL; only the ionized fraction is active and regulated.","The calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid chief cells lowers PTH release when ionized calcium rises.","Bone: PTH acts on osteoblasts to raise RANKL, which activates osteoclasts and releases calcium and phosphate.","Distal tubule: PTH increases calcium reabsorption.","Proximal tubule: PTH blocks phosphate reabsorption, causing phosphate loss in urine.","Proximal tubule: PTH stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase, raising calcitriol, which increases gut calcium and phosphate absorption.","Net effect of PTH: serum calcium up, serum phosphate down. Calcitonin from C cells inhibits osteoclasts and lowers calcium, a minor effect in adults."]},{"h":"Aldosterone","points":["Main stimuli: angiotensin II and high serum potassium; ACTH has a small, short-lived effect.","Principal cells: more ENaC sodium channels on the luminal side and more Na/K-ATPase on the blood side, so more sodium is reabsorbed.","The sodium movement makes the lumen negative and drives potassium secretion through luminal potassium channels.","Alpha intercalated cells: more H+ secretion through H+-ATPase.","So aldosterone excess causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis."]},{"h":"Insulin release","points":["Glucose enters the beta cell by GLUT2 and is trapped by glucokinase, the beta cell's glucose sensor.","Glucose metabolism raises ATP, which closes ATP-sensitive potassium channels.","The cell depolarizes, voltage-gated calcium channels open, and calcium influx triggers insulin exocytosis.","Oral glucose releases more insulin than the same glucose level given IV because gut incretins add to the signal.","Incretins: GLP-1 from intestinal L cells and GIP from duodenal and jejunal K cells raise beta cell cAMP and amplify glucose-driven release."]},{"h":"Glucose counterregulation","points":["As glucose falls, insulin secretion drops first, then glucagon and epinephrine rise.","Glucagon acts within minutes on the liver: glycogen breakdown, then gluconeogenesis.","Epinephrine acts within minutes: liver glycogen breakdown, lipolysis, suppression of insulin release, and the warning symptoms of hypoglycemia (tremor, sweating, palpitations).","Cortisol and GH act over hours: cortisol supplies gluconeogenic substrate and enzymes; GH raises lipolysis and reduces glucose uptake.","Long-standing type 1 diabetes often loses the glucagon response, so epinephrine becomes the main defense."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypothalamic control of the anterior pituitary","cols":["Hypothalamic hormone","Pituitary target","Effect","After stalk section"],"rows":[["TRH","Thyrotrophs (and lactotrophs)","Raises TSH (and prolactin)","TSH falls"],["CRH","Corticotrophs","Raises ACTH","ACTH falls"],["GnRH (pulsatile)","Gonadotrophs","Raises LH and FSH","LH and FSH fall"],["GHRH","Somatotrophs","Raises GH","GH falls"],["Somatostatin","Somatotrophs and thyrotrophs","Lowers GH and TSH","Loss of inhibition, outweighed by loss of GHRH and TRH"],["Dopamine","Lactotrophs","Lowers prolactin","Prolactin rises"]]},{"title":"PTH, calcitriol, and calcitonin","cols":["Hormone","Released when","Bone","Kidney","Gut","Net serum effect"],"rows":[["PTH","Ionized calcium falls","Raises resorption via RANKL","More calcium reabsorbed; phosphate excreted; more calcitriol made","Indirect, through calcitriol","Calcium up, phosphate down"],["Calcitriol","PTH high or phosphate low","Supports mineralization; with PTH can mobilize calcium","Some calcium and phosphate reabsorption","More calcium and phosphate absorbed","Calcium up, phosphate up"],["Calcitonin","Calcium rises","Inhibits osteoclasts","Minor","None","Calcium down (small effect)"]]},{"title":"Counterregulatory hormones","cols":["Hormone","Speed","Main glucose-raising actions"],"rows":[["Glucagon","Minutes","Liver glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis"],["Epinephrine","Minutes","Liver glycogenolysis, lipolysis, less insulin release"],["Cortisol","Hours","Gluconeogenesis, protein breakdown for substrate, less glucose uptake"],["Growth hormone","Hours","Lipolysis, less glucose uptake by muscle"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cut the stalk, free the prolactin","text":"Prolactin is the one hormone mostly held back by the hypothalamus. Cut the stalk and its brake (dopamine) is gone, so it alone goes up."},{"kind":"trick","label":"PTH: up calcium, out phosphate","text":"PTH raises calcium in blood and throws phosphate into urine. Calcitriol raises both because it acts on the gut."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sugar to spike","text":"Glucose in, ATP up, K channel shut, cell depolarized, calcium in, insulin out. Each step follows from the one before it."}],"highYield":["Stalk section or a large mass compressing the stalk: raised prolactin with low other pituitary hormones.","Plasma osmolality is the main signal for ADH; large blood volume loss can override it.","Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium, low phosphate, high PTH.","Hyperaldosteronism: hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis.","Sulfonylureas close beta cell K-ATP channels and raise insulin release; diazoxide opens them.","The incretin effect explains why oral glucose releases more insulin than IV glucose.","Glucagon and epinephrine are the fast defenses against hypoglycemia; cortisol and GH are slow."],"pitfalls":["ADH works on V2 receptors (Gs) in the kidney; its vasoconstricting effect is through V1 receptors (Gq).","PTH raises calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule but blocks phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule.","ACTH is a weak, short-term stimulus for aldosterone; angiotensin II and potassium are the main controls.","Glucokinase in beta cells and liver is a sensor with low affinity for glucose; hexokinase in other tissues works at low glucose."],"dumps":[{"t":"Insulin release in a beta cell","p":"Draw a pancreatic beta cell and number each step from glucose entry to insulin release. Add the incretin signal and show where sulfonylureas act.","l":["Glucose enters by GLUT2","Glucokinase phosphorylates glucose","Metabolism raises ATP","ATP closes K-ATP channels","Membrane depolarizes","Voltage-gated calcium channels open and calcium enters","Calcium triggers insulin granule exocytosis","GLP-1 and GIP raise cAMP and amplify release","Sulfonylureas close the K-ATP channel"]},{"t":"Calcium control loop","p":"Draw the parathyroid, bone, kidney, and small intestine. Trace what happens after ionized calcium falls, ending with calcium back to normal.","l":["Calcium-sensing receptor detects low ionized calcium","PTH released from chief cells","Bone: osteoblast RANKL activates osteoclasts","Distal tubule calcium reabsorption increases","Proximal tubule phosphate reabsorption decreases","1-alpha-hydroxylase makes calcitriol","Gut absorbs more calcium and phosphate","Rising calcium shuts off PTH"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Insulin, Glucagon, and Diabetes Mellitus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-6","EN-7","EN-12-B1","EN-12-A2","EN-12-A1","EN-12-D1"]},"EN-6":{"id":"EN-6","summary":"How hormones reach their targets and act on them, and how feedback loops let you locate a disorder from a pair of lab values. The exam asks which second messenger a hormone uses, why total and free hormone levels can disagree, what thyroid hormone, GH, and cortisol do, and why high prolactin shuts down the gonads.","notes":[{"h":"Receptor types","points":["Gs and cAMP: ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, PTH, glucagon (also CRH, GHRH, calcitonin, ADH at V2).","Gq and IP3 with calcium: GnRH, TRH, oxytocin (also ADH at V1, angiotensin II).","Intrinsic tyrosine kinase: insulin and IGF-1.","JAK/STAT (receptor-associated tyrosine kinase): GH and prolactin.","Intracellular receptors that act as transcription factors: steroid hormones, thyroid hormone, and calcitriol; their effects take hours."]},{"h":"Binding proteins and free hormone","points":["Thyroid hormone travels mostly bound to thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG); only a tiny free fraction (well under 1%) is active.","Cortisol travels mostly bound to cortisol-binding globulin (CBG); roughly 5 to 10% is free.","Only free hormone enters cells, acts on receptors, and feeds back on the pituitary.","Estrogen (pregnancy, oral contraceptives) raises TBG: total T4 rises, but free T4 and TSH stay normal and the patient is euthyroid.","Low TBG (nephrotic syndrome, liver failure, androgens) lowers total T4 with normal free T4."]},{"h":"Reading feedback pairs","points":["Target hormone and trophic hormone move in opposite directions: the problem is in the target gland (primary).","Both move in the same direction: the problem is above the gland, in the pituitary or hypothalamus.","Low free T4 with high TSH: primary hypothyroidism.","High cortisol with low ACTH: adrenal tumor or steroid medication; high cortisol with high ACTH: pituitary adenoma or ectopic ACTH.","Low cortisol with high ACTH: primary adrenal insufficiency; low with low: pituitary or hypothalamic disease.","A releasing-hormone stimulation test separates pituitary failure (no response) from hypothalamic failure (pituitary still responds)."]},{"h":"Thyroid hormone actions","points":["The thyroid secretes mostly T4; deiodinases in tissues remove one iodine to make T3, the more active form.","Raises basal metabolic rate, oxygen use, and heat production, partly by increasing Na/K-ATPase.","Increases beta-1 adrenergic receptors in the heart: higher heart rate, contractility, and cardiac output.","Needed for bone growth (with GH) and for brain maturation; lack in infancy causes intellectual disability and short stature."]},{"h":"GH and IGF-1","points":["GH is released in pulses, mostly in deep sleep, and rises with exercise, fasting, and hypoglycemia.","GHRH stimulates release; somatostatin and IGF-1 feedback suppress it; a glucose load suppresses it in healthy people.","Direct GH actions: lipolysis and insulin antagonism, which raise blood glucose.","Indirect actions through IGF-1 from the liver: linear bone growth at the growth plates and protein synthesis."]},{"h":"Cortisol and prolactin","points":["Cortisol raises glucose by gluconeogenesis, and supplies substrate by protein breakdown and lipolysis.","Cortisol is anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive, and it reduces collagen synthesis, so skin thins and wounds heal slowly.","Cortisol is permissive for catecholamine vasoconstriction, which helps maintain blood pressure.","ACTH and cortisol peak in the early morning around waking and are lowest near midnight.","Prolactin is held down by tonic dopamine from the hypothalamus and stimulated by TRH.","High prolactin suppresses GnRH, so LH and FSH fall: amenorrhea and infertility in women, low libido and erectile dysfunction in men."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hormone signaling pathways","cols":["Pathway","Hormones","Key point"],"rows":[["Gs, cAMP","ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, PTH, glucagon, CRH, GHRH, calcitonin, ADH (V2)","Protein kinase A phosphorylates target proteins"],["Gq, IP3 and calcium","GnRH, TRH, oxytocin, ADH (V1), angiotensin II","Phospholipase C makes IP3 (releases calcium) and DAG (activates protein kinase C)"],["Intrinsic tyrosine kinase","Insulin, IGF-1","Receptor phosphorylates itself"],["JAK/STAT","GH, prolactin","Receptor recruits JAK kinase; STAT enters nucleus"],["Intracellular receptor","Cortisol, aldosterone, sex steroids, thyroid hormone, calcitriol","Hormone-receptor complex binds DNA and changes transcription"]]},{"title":"Localizing by feedback","cols":["Pattern","Most likely site","Example"],"rows":[["Low free T4, high TSH","Thyroid (primary)","Hashimoto thyroiditis"],["Low free T4, low or normal TSH","Pituitary or hypothalamus","Sheehan syndrome"],["High free T4, low TSH","Thyroid (primary)","Graves disease"],["High cortisol, low ACTH","Adrenal or exogenous steroid","Adrenal adenoma"],["High cortisol, high ACTH","Pituitary or ectopic ACTH source","Pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)"],["Low cortisol, high ACTH","Adrenal (primary)","Addison disease"],["High total T4, normal free T4 and TSH","No disease; binding protein raised","Pregnancy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"FLAT ChAMP","text":"cAMP hormones: FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH, CRH, hCG, ADH (V2), MSH, PTH. Add calcitonin, GHRH, and glucagon."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"GOAT HAG","text":"Gq hormones and signals: GnRH, Oxytocin, ADH (V1), TRH, Histamine (H1), Angiotensin II, Gastrin."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"VETTT CAP","text":"Intracellular receptors: Vitamin D, Estrogen, Testosterone, T3, T4, Cortisol, Aldosterone, Progesterone."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Opposite means the gland","text":"If the gland's hormone and its pituitary driver move in opposite directions, the gland is the problem. If they move together, look above it."}],"highYield":["Pregnancy or estrogen use: high total T4, normal free T4, normal TSH.","Primary hypothyroidism raises TRH, which can raise prolactin and cause galactorrhea.","Dopamine D2 antagonists (antipsychotics, metoclopramide) raise prolactin.","Thyroid hormone upregulates cardiac beta-1 receptors, which explains the tachycardia of hyperthyroidism.","IGF-1 is the screening test for GH excess; failure of GH to fall after oral glucose confirms it.","Late-night salivary cortisol is high in Cushing syndrome because the normal night-time fall in cortisol is lost."],"pitfalls":["GH and prolactin use JAK/STAT, while the other anterior pituitary hormones (ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH) use cAMP.","Thyroid hormone is an amine but acts on a nuclear receptor like a steroid.","Total hormone changes with binding protein; free hormone and TSH tell you thyroid status.","High cortisol with high ACTH can be pituitary or ectopic; it is high cortisol with low ACTH that points to the adrenal."],"dumps":[{"t":"Feedback table","p":"Make a table with four rows: primary and secondary hypothyroidism, primary and secondary adrenal insufficiency. For each, write whether the target hormone and the trophic hormone are high or low, and give one cause.","l":["Primary hypothyroidism: low free T4, high TSH","Secondary hypothyroidism: low free T4, low or normal TSH","Primary adrenal insufficiency: low cortisol, high ACTH","Secondary adrenal insufficiency: low cortisol, low ACTH","Opposite directions point to the target gland","Same direction points to the pituitary or hypothalamus","A cause listed for each row, such as Hashimoto, Sheehan, Addison, or long-term steroid use"]},{"t":"Hormone action map","p":"Draw five boxes for the five receptor pathways (Gs, Gq, intrinsic tyrosine kinase, JAK/STAT, intracellular). Write at least two hormones in each box and one sentence on how the signal reaches the nucleus or enzyme.","l":["Gs: cAMP and protein kinase A, with ACTH, TSH, PTH, or glucagon","Gq: IP3, calcium, and protein kinase C, with GnRH, TRH, or oxytocin","Tyrosine kinase: insulin and IGF-1","JAK/STAT: GH and prolactin","Intracellular: steroids, thyroid hormone, calcitriol","Intracellular receptors change gene transcription and act slowly","Only free hormone reaches the receptor"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Introduction to Endocrinology"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"section on disorders of the hypothalamus and pituitary gland"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-5","EN-8","EN-12-A1","EN-12-B5","EN-12-G4","EN-12-A3"]},"EN-7":{"id":"EN-7","summary":"The biochemistry behind insulin, glucagon, ketones, and the nutrients the endocrine system depends on: iodine, selenium, vitamin D, and magnesium. It also covers leptin and ghrelin in appetite control. The exam links each nutrient or pathway step to the disease that follows when it fails.","notes":[{"h":"Fed state: insulin","points":["Insulin moves GLUT4 transporters to the cell surface in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue.","Liver and muscle: activates glycogen synthase, so glucose is stored as glycogen.","Speeds glycolysis and, in the liver, turns extra glucose into fatty acids (lipogenesis through acetyl-CoA carboxylase).","Adipose: raises lipoprotein lipase to take up triglyceride and blocks hormone-sensitive lipase.","Increases protein synthesis and drives potassium into cells."]},{"h":"Fasting state: glucagon","points":["Glucagon acts mainly on the liver through cAMP and protein kinase A; muscle has few glucagon receptors.","Activates glycogen phosphorylase for glycogenolysis; liver glycogen lasts about a day of fasting.","Lowers fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, turning off glycolysis and turning on gluconeogenesis.","Lowers malonyl-CoA, which releases CPT-1, so fatty acids enter mitochondria for oxidation and ketogenesis."]},{"h":"Diabetic ketoacidosis","points":["No insulin plus high glucagon: hormone-sensitive lipase runs freely and floods the liver with fatty acids.","Beta-oxidation makes more acetyl-CoA than the TCA cycle can use, since oxaloacetate is pulled into gluconeogenesis.","The liver converts the excess acetyl-CoA via HMG-CoA to acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate.","These ketoacids release H+, lower bicarbonate, and raise the anion gap (normal about 8 to 12 mEq/L).","Acetone forms from acetoacetate and gives a fruity breath odor; deep, rapid Kussmaul breathing compensates.","The liver makes ketones but cannot use them; brain, heart, and muscle can."]},{"h":"Iodine and selenium","points":["Iodide enters follicular cells by the sodium-iodide symporter and is attached to thyroglobulin by thyroid peroxidase.","Iodine deficiency lowers T4 and T3, so TSH rises and drives follicular cell growth: goiter.","Severe deficiency in pregnancy causes congenital hypothyroidism with intellectual disability.","The deiodinases that convert T4 to T3 contain selenium (as selenocysteine).","Selenium is also in glutathione peroxidase, which protects the thyroid from the peroxide it uses to make hormone."]},{"h":"Vitamin D","points":["UVB light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in skin to vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol); diet adds D2 and D3.","Liver 25-hydroxylase makes 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the storage form and the best measure of vitamin D status.","Kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylase (proximal tubule) makes 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), the active form; PTH and low phosphate stimulate it.","Deficiency: less calcium absorbed, PTH rises (secondary hyperparathyroidism), phosphate is lost in urine, and bone mineralizes poorly.","Children get rickets (bowed legs, rachitic rosary, wide growth plates); adults get osteomalacia (bone pain, pseudofractures).","Typical labs: low or low-normal calcium, low phosphate, high PTH, high alkaline phosphatase, low 25-hydroxyvitamin D."]},{"h":"Magnesium","points":["Severe low magnesium blocks PTH release and makes bone and kidney resistant to PTH.","The result is hypocalcemia that will not correct with calcium alone until magnesium is replaced.","Common causes: alcohol use disorder, chronic diarrhea, poor intake, long-term proton pump inhibitor use, and diuretics."]},{"h":"Leptin, ghrelin, and obesity","points":["Leptin comes from adipocytes in proportion to fat mass and signals satiety to the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus.","Leptin stimulates POMC neurons (alpha-MSH acting on MC4R) and inhibits NPY/AgRP neurons, lowering intake.","Ghrelin comes from the stomach, rises before meals, and stimulates NPY/AgRP neurons to cause hunger.","Common obesity has high leptin with leptin resistance; true leptin deficiency is rare.","Visceral fat releases fatty acids into the portal vein and inflammatory cytokines, and makes less adiponectin, which promotes insulin resistance."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Insulin and glucagon on fuel metabolism","cols":["Pathway","Insulin (fed)","Glucagon (fasting)"],"rows":[["Glucose uptake in muscle and fat","Up (GLUT4)","No direct effect"],["Glycogen","Synthesis up","Breakdown up (liver)"],["Glycolysis","Up","Down"],["Gluconeogenesis","Down","Up"],["Fatty acid synthesis","Up","Down"],["Lipolysis in adipose","Down","Up (driven mainly by low insulin and epinephrine)"],["Ketogenesis","Down","Up"],["Protein","Synthesis up","Liver takes up amino acids for gluconeogenesis"]]},{"title":"Vitamin D activation and deficiency","cols":["Step","Site","Enzyme or trigger","When it fails"],"rows":[["7-dehydrocholesterol to vitamin D3","Skin","UVB light","Deficiency with little sun exposure"],["Vitamin D to 25-hydroxyvitamin D","Liver","25-hydroxylase","Low levels in severe liver disease"],["25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol","Kidney proximal tubule","1-alpha-hydroxylase, raised by PTH and low phosphate","Chronic kidney disease, secondary hyperparathyroidism"],["Calcitriol action","Intestine and bone","Vitamin D receptor","Rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Insulin stores, glucagon spends","text":"Every pathway that builds storage (glycogen, fat, protein) is insulin's; every pathway that releases fuel (glycogen breakdown, gluconeogenesis, ketones) is glucagon's."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fix magnesium first","text":"If calcium stays low after calcium is given, check magnesium. Without it the parathyroids cannot release PTH and tissues cannot respond to it."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ghrelin growls","text":"Ghrelin is the hunger hormone from the stomach; leptin is the fat-made signal that you have eaten enough."}],"highYield":["DKA: high glucose, ketones, low bicarbonate, and a raised anion gap.","The urine nitroprusside test detects acetoacetate but not beta-hydroxybutyrate, the main ketone in DKA.","Iodine deficiency is the most common cause of goiter worldwide.","25-hydroxyvitamin D measures vitamin D stores; calcitriol does not, since PTH keeps it up until late.","Chronic kidney disease lowers 1-alpha-hydroxylase, causing low calcitriol and secondary hyperparathyroidism.","Hypocalcemia that does not respond to calcium points to hypomagnesemia.","Most people with obesity have high leptin levels, reflecting resistance to its signal."],"pitfalls":["Glucagon has little effect on skeletal muscle; epinephrine drives muscle glycogen breakdown.","The liver makes ketones but lacks the enzyme to use them, so it never consumes its own product.","Rickets and osteomalacia are the same mineralization defect at different ages; osteoporosis has normal mineralization with less bone.","Selenium helps convert T4 to T3; iodine is the building block of the hormone itself."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pathway to DKA","p":"Draw an adipocyte, a hepatocyte, and the blood between them. Trace the steps from insulin deficiency to anion gap metabolic acidosis.","l":["Low insulin and high glucagon","Hormone-sensitive lipase active, fatty acids released","Low malonyl-CoA frees CPT-1 for beta-oxidation","Excess acetyl-CoA as oxaloacetate goes to gluconeogenesis","HMG-CoA pathway makes acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate","Ketoacids lower bicarbonate and raise the anion gap","Acetone on the breath and Kussmaul breathing","Brain, heart, and muscle use ketones; liver does not"]},{"t":"Vitamin D and calcium chain","p":"Draw the skin, liver, kidney, gut, and parathyroid glands. Trace vitamin D activation, then trace what happens to calcium, phosphate, and PTH when vitamin D is deficient.","l":["UVB converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in skin","Liver makes 25-hydroxyvitamin D","Kidney makes calcitriol with 1-alpha-hydroxylase","PTH and low phosphate stimulate 1-alpha-hydroxylase","Calcitriol raises gut calcium and phosphate absorption","Deficiency lowers calcium absorption and raises PTH","High PTH causes phosphate loss in urine","Poor mineralization gives rickets or osteomalacia"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Metabolic Effects of Insulin and Glucagon"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Diabetes Mellitus"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Obesity"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-5","EN-8","EN-12-D1","EN-12-B5","EN-12-A2","EN-12-B3"]},"EN-8":{"id":"EN-8","summary":"How the body builds and breaks down its main hormones: thyroid hormone, adrenal steroids, catecholamines, insulin, and the posterior pituitary peptides. Exam questions name a blocked enzyme or an odd lab pattern and ask you to predict what builds up, what falls, and what the patient looks like.","notes":[{"h":"Thyroid hormone synthesis","points":["The sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) on the basolateral membrane pulls iodide into the follicular cell using the sodium gradient; TSH increases it.","Pendrin carries iodide across the apical membrane into the colloid.","Thyroid peroxidase (TPO) uses hydrogen peroxide to oxidize iodide and attach it to tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin (organification), forming MIT and DIT.","TPO also couples them: DIT plus DIT makes T4, and MIT plus DIT makes T3.","For release, TSH drives endocytosis of colloid; lysosomal proteases cleave thyroglobulin and free T4 (most of the output) and some T3.","Leftover MIT and DIT are deiodinated so the iodide is reused; peripheral 5'-deiodinase turns T4 into the more active T3."]},{"h":"Adrenal steroid synthesis","points":["ACTH drives cholesterol into mitochondria through StAR protein, the rate-limiting transport step.","Side-chain cleavage enzyme (desmolase, CYP11A1) converts cholesterol to pregnenolone, the parent of every steroid.","Zona glomerulosa lacks 17-alpha-hydroxylase, so it makes aldosterone: progesterone, then 11-deoxycorticosterone (21-hydroxylase), corticosterone (11-beta-hydroxylase), aldosterone (aldosterone synthase).","Zona fasciculata uses 17-alpha-hydroxylase: 17-hydroxyprogesterone, then 11-deoxycortisol (21-hydroxylase), then cortisol (11-beta-hydroxylase).","Zona reticularis uses 17,20-lyase to make DHEA and androstenedione.","Block rule: substrates above a blocked enzyme pile up and spill into the pathways that are still open, and low cortisol raises ACTH, which drives even more flow."]},{"h":"Catecholamine synthesis and breakdown","points":["Tyrosine hydroxylase (rate-limiting, needs tetrahydrobiopterin) converts tyrosine to L-DOPA.","DOPA decarboxylase (needs vitamin B6) makes dopamine; dopamine beta-hydroxylase (needs vitamin C and copper) makes norepinephrine inside vesicles.","PNMT adds a methyl group from S-adenosylmethionine to make epinephrine; cortisol arriving through the adrenal portal flow from cortex to medulla induces PNMT.","COMT converts epinephrine and norepinephrine to metanephrine and normetanephrine; MAO then makes vanillylmandelic acid (VMA). Dopamine breaks down to homovanillic acid (HVA).","Pheochromocytoma cells metabolize catecholamines to metanephrines continuously, so plasma free or urine fractionated metanephrines are the most sensitive tests even when catecholamine release is episodic."]},{"h":"Insulin processing and C-peptide","points":["Preproinsulin is made on the rough ER; removal of the signal peptide gives proinsulin (B chain, C-peptide, A chain), which folds and forms disulfide bonds.","In secretory granules, prohormone convertases cut out C-peptide, leaving insulin (A and B chains joined by two disulfide bonds).","Insulin and C-peptide are released in equal molar amounts; the liver clears much of the insulin on first pass, while C-peptide is cleared by the kidney and lasts longer.","Pharmaceutical insulin contains no C-peptide, so high insulin with low C-peptide means injected insulin."]},{"h":"ADH and oxytocin","points":["Both are made in magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic nucleus (mainly ADH) and paraventricular nucleus (mainly oxytocin).","Each is made as a larger precursor that includes its carrier protein, neurophysin; the pair travels down the axons of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract by axonal transport.","They are stored in axon terminals in the posterior pituitary and released by calcium-triggered exocytosis when the neurons fire. The posterior pituitary stores these hormones but does not make them."]},{"h":"11-beta-HSD2 and licorice","points":["The mineralocorticoid receptor binds cortisol about as well as aldosterone, and free cortisol circulates at far higher levels.","11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 in collecting duct cells converts cortisol to cortisone, which does not bind the receptor, so aldosterone stays the main signal.","Glycyrrhetinic acid in licorice inhibits this enzyme; cortisol then activates the receptor, causing sodium retention, hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with low renin and low aldosterone.","The same pattern appears with inherited loss of the enzyme (apparent mineralocorticoid excess) and when huge cortisol levels overwhelm it, as with ectopic ACTH."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Adrenal enzyme blocks: what builds up and what falls","cols":["Enzyme deficiency","Builds up","Falls","Sex steroids","Blood pressure and potassium"],"rows":[["21-hydroxylase","17-hydroxyprogesterone, progesterone","Cortisol, aldosterone, 11-deoxycorticosterone","Increased","Low pressure (salt wasting), high potassium"],["11-beta-hydroxylase","11-deoxycortisol, 11-deoxycorticosterone (a mineralocorticoid)","Cortisol, aldosterone, renin","Increased","High pressure, low potassium"],["17-alpha-hydroxylase","11-deoxycorticosterone, corticosterone","Cortisol, sex steroids, renin","Decreased","High pressure, low potassium"]]},{"title":"Insulin and C-peptide in hypoglycemia","cols":["Cause","Insulin","C-peptide","Extra clue"],"rows":[["Insulinoma","High","High","Sulfonylurea screen negative"],["Sulfonylurea ingestion","High","High","Sulfonylurea screen positive"],["Injected insulin","High","Low","Commercial insulin has no C-peptide"],["Non-insulin causes (liver failure, sepsis)","Low","Low","Insulin appropriately suppressed"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Starts with 1 means hypertension; ends with 1 means virilization","text":"11-beta-hydroxylase starts and ends with 1 (hypertension and virilization). 17-alpha starts with 1 (hypertension, no virilization). 21 ends with 1 (virilization, no hypertension)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Hydroxylate, decarboxylate, hydroxylate, methylate","text":"The four steps from tyrosine to epinephrine in order: tyrosine hydroxylase, DOPA decarboxylase, dopamine beta-hydroxylase, PNMT."},{"kind":"trick","label":"C-peptide only comes from the patient","text":"Any source that makes the patient's own beta cells secrete (tumor or sulfonylurea) raises C-peptide with insulin. Insulin from a vial raises insulin alone."}],"highYield":["TPO does both organification and coupling; antibodies against it mark Hashimoto thyroiditis.","T4 is the main secreted hormone; T3 is the active one, made mostly in peripheral tissues by 5'-deiodinase.","StAR transport of cholesterol into mitochondria is the rate-limiting step in steroid synthesis.","21-hydroxylase deficiency raises 17-hydroxyprogesterone, the newborn screening marker for congenital adrenal hyperplasia.","Tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme for catecholamines; cortisol induces PNMT.","Plasma free or urine fractionated metanephrines are the best screening tests for pheochromocytoma.","High insulin with low C-peptide means exogenous insulin.","Licorice causes a low-renin, low-aldosterone hypertension with hypokalemia by blocking 11-beta-HSD2."],"pitfalls":["11-beta-hydroxylase and 21-hydroxylase deficiency both virilize. Hypertension and low potassium point to 11-beta, because 11-deoxycorticosterone builds up and acts as a mineralocorticoid.","In 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency, the extra mineralocorticoid is mostly 11-deoxycorticosterone; aldosterone itself is often low because renin is suppressed.","Sulfonylurea use and insulinoma give the same insulin and C-peptide pattern; only a drug screen separates them.","Licorice and primary hyperaldosteronism both cause hypertension with hypokalemia, but aldosterone is low with licorice and high in primary hyperaldosteronism."],"dumps":[{"t":"Adrenal steroid map","p":"Draw the steroid pathway from cholesterol to aldosterone, cortisol, and androstenedione in three columns, one per adrenal zone. Label each enzyme on its arrow, then cross out 21-hydroxylase and circle what builds up.","l":["Cholesterol enters mitochondria by StAR, the rate-limiting step","Side-chain cleavage makes pregnenolone","Glomerulosa column ends in aldosterone through 11-deoxycorticosterone and corticosterone","Fasciculata column ends in cortisol through 17-hydroxyprogesterone and 11-deoxycortisol","Reticularis column makes DHEA and androstenedione by 17,20-lyase","21-hydroxylase sits on both the glomerulosa and fasciculata columns","With 21-hydroxylase blocked, 17-hydroxyprogesterone builds up and flows into androgens","Low cortisol raises ACTH, which drives more flow into the open pathway"]},{"t":"Thyroid follicle","p":"Draw one thyroid follicular cell with the bloodstream on one side and the colloid on the other. Trace iodide from blood to finished T4 and T3 leaving the cell, labeling each transporter and enzyme.","l":["NIS on the basolateral side brings iodide in with sodium","Pendrin moves iodide into the colloid","TPO oxidizes iodide and iodinates thyroglobulin tyrosines to MIT and DIT","TPO couples DIT plus DIT to T4 and MIT plus DIT to T3","TSH stimulates endocytosis of colloid","Lysosomal proteolysis of thyroglobulin frees T4 and T3","Deiodinase recycles iodide from leftover MIT and DIT","T4 is converted to T3 in peripheral tissues"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Insulin, Glucagon, and Diabetes Mellitus"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on catecholamine synthesis and degradation"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-5","EN-6","EN-9","EN-12-A1","EN-12-G2","EN-12-G6"]},"EN-9":{"id":"EN-9","summary":"The genetics behind endocrine disease: tumor syndromes, inherited enzyme defects, diabetes genetics, thyroid cancer mutations, and disorders of sex development. You also need to know how steroid and thyroid hormones act as transcription factors. Questions give a family history or a karyotype with hormone levels and ask for the gene, the inheritance, or the mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"MEN syndromes","points":["MEN1: loss-of-function mutation in the MEN1 tumor suppressor gene, which encodes menin.","A person with MEN1 inherits one bad copy in every cell; a tumor forms only after a second hit knocks out the normal copy in a gland cell.","MEN2A and MEN2B: gain-of-function mutation in the RET proto-oncogene, a receptor tyrosine kinase. One mutant copy is enough to drive growth.","Both are autosomal dominant: each child of an affected parent has a 50 percent chance of inheriting the mutation.","RET carriers are offered early prophylactic thyroidectomy because medullary thyroid carcinoma develops in nearly all of them."]},{"h":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and diabetes","points":["Congenital adrenal hyperplasia is autosomal recessive; carrier parents have a 25 percent risk of an affected child each pregnancy.","About 90 to 95 percent of cases are 21-hydroxylase deficiency from CYP21A2 mutations; the gene lies in the HLA region on chromosome 6.","Type 1 diabetes is linked to HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4 (and the DQ alleles inherited with them).","Type 2 diabetes is polygenic, with no HLA link, and has higher monozygotic twin concordance than type 1.","MODY is monogenic and autosomal dominant: young onset, not obese, no autoantibodies, not ketosis prone.","Glucokinase MODY resets the beta cell glucose sensor higher, giving mild, stable fasting hyperglycemia; HNF1A MODY (a common type) worsens over time."]},{"h":"Thyroid cancer mutations","points":["Papillary carcinoma: BRAF V600E point mutation (most common) or RET/PTC rearrangement (linked to radiation exposure); both switch on the MAP kinase pathway.","Follicular carcinoma: RAS point mutations or PAX8-PPARG fusion.","Medullary carcinoma: RET point mutations, germline in MEN2 and familial cases, somatic in many sporadic tumors.","Anaplastic carcinoma often adds TP53 loss on top of an earlier BRAF or RAS mutation."]},{"h":"Sex development and gonadal axis","points":["Androgen insensitivity: X-linked androgen receptor defect in a 46,XY person. Testes make testosterone and AMH, so there is no uterus, but the body cannot respond to androgen and the external genitalia are female.","In androgen insensitivity, testosterone and LH are high (no feedback); breasts develop from aromatized testosterone, and pubic and axillary hair is scant.","5-alpha-reductase deficiency: autosomal recessive, 46,XY, low DHT with normal testosterone; ambiguous external genitalia at birth, normal Wolffian structures, and virilization at puberty when testosterone rises.","Klinefelter syndrome 47,XXY (meiotic nondisjunction): small firm testes, infertility, gynecomastia, tall stature; low testosterone with high LH and FSH (primary hypogonadism).","Kallmann syndrome: GnRH neurons fail to migrate from the olfactory placode with the olfactory neurons, giving anosmia with low GnRH, low LH and FSH, and delayed puberty (secondary hypogonadism)."]},{"h":"Intracellular hormone receptors","points":["Steroid hormones and thyroid hormone are lipid soluble and cross the cell membrane to reach intracellular receptors.","Steroid receptors such as the glucocorticoid receptor wait in the cytoplasm bound to heat shock proteins; hormone binding releases them so the receptor can dimerize and move to the nucleus.","The thyroid hormone receptor sits on DNA in the nucleus as a heterodimer with the retinoid X receptor; T3 binding swaps corepressors for coactivators.","The receptor's zinc finger domain binds a hormone response element and turns genes on or off, so effects take hours to days and last longer than the seconds-to-minutes effects of peptide hormones acting through second messengers."]}],"charts":[{"title":"MEN syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Gene and type","Tumors and features"],"rows":[["MEN1","MEN1 (menin), tumor suppressor, loss of function","Parathyroid hyperplasia or adenomas, pancreatic and duodenal neuroendocrine tumors (gastrinoma, insulinoma), pituitary adenoma (often prolactinoma)"],["MEN2A","RET proto-oncogene, gain of function","Medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, parathyroid hyperplasia"],["MEN2B","RET proto-oncogene, gain of function","Medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus"]]},{"title":"Gonadal disorders by labs","cols":["Disorder","Karyotype and inheritance","Testosterone","DHT","LH and FSH","Key features"],"rows":[["Androgen insensitivity","46,XY, X-linked","High","Normal or high","LH high","Female external genitalia, testes, no uterus, scant body hair"],["5-alpha-reductase deficiency","46,XY, autosomal recessive","Normal","Low","Normal","Ambiguous genitalia at birth, virilizes at puberty"],["Klinefelter syndrome","47,XXY, nondisjunction","Low","Low","High","Small firm testes, gynecomastia, infertility, tall"],["Kallmann syndrome","46,XY or 46,XX, several genes","Low","Low","Low","Anosmia, delayed puberty, low GnRH"]]},{"title":"Diabetes genetics","cols":["Type","Genetic pattern","Twin concordance","Key point"],"rows":[["Type 1","Polygenic, HLA-DR3 and DR4","Lower than type 2","Autoimmune beta cell loss"],["Type 2","Polygenic, no HLA link","Higher than type 1","Strong family history, obesity"],["MODY","Monogenic, autosomal dominant (glucokinase, HNF1A)","Very high (single dominant gene)","Young, lean, antibody negative, several generations affected"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"MEN1 has 3 Ps, MEN2A has 2 Ps, MEN2B has 1 P","text":"MEN1: parathyroid, pituitary, pancreas. MEN2A: parathyroid and pheochromocytoma (plus medullary thyroid cancer). MEN2B: pheochromocytoma only (plus medullary thyroid cancer, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Where the problem is tells you LH and FSH","text":"If the testis fails (Klinefelter), the pituitary shouts: LH and FSH go up. If the signal never arrives (Kallmann), LH and FSH are low."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Receptor problem or enzyme problem","text":"In 46,XY with female or ambiguous genitalia, check DHT. Normal testosterone with low DHT is the enzyme (5-alpha-reductase). High testosterone, high LH, and normal DHT is the receptor (androgen insensitivity)."}],"highYield":["MEN1 is a tumor suppressor (two hits needed in the tumor cell); RET in MEN2 is a proto-oncogene (one hit is enough).","Both MEN syndromes are autosomal dominant.","Most congenital adrenal hyperplasia is autosomal recessive 21-hydroxylase deficiency from CYP21A2 mutations.","Type 1 diabetes: HLA-DR3 and DR4. Type 2: polygenic, with higher twin concordance than type 1.","BRAF V600E and RET/PTC point to papillary carcinoma; RAS and PAX8-PPARG point to follicular; RET points to medullary.","Kallmann syndrome is low GnRH with anosmia; Klinefelter is 47,XXY with high LH and FSH.","The thyroid hormone receptor pairs with the retinoid X receptor on DNA."],"pitfalls":["MEN1 is inherited as a dominant trait even though the gene is a tumor suppressor that acts recessively in the cell. Inheritance pattern and cellular mechanism are different questions.","Androgen insensitivity and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency are both 46,XY. Androgen insensitivity has no virilization at puberty and scant body hair; 5-alpha-reductase deficiency virilizes at puberty.","Twin concordance in diabetes runs opposite to what many students expect: it is higher in type 2 than in type 1.","Thyroid hormone acts through a nuclear receptor even though it is made from amino acids. Group it with steroids."],"dumps":[{"t":"Two-hit versus one-hit","p":"Draw two cells side by side, one from a person with MEN1 and one from a person with MEN2A. Show both copies of the relevant gene in each, mark which copy is inherited mutant, and show what must happen for a tumor to form. Under each, list the tumors.","l":["MEN1 cell shows one inherited mutant MEN1 copy","A second somatic hit to the normal copy is needed for a tumor","Menin is a tumor suppressor (loss of function)","MEN2A cell shows one mutant RET copy that is enough on its own","RET is a proto-oncogene encoding a receptor tyrosine kinase (gain of function)","Both are autosomal dominant","MEN1 tumors: parathyroid, pancreatic neuroendocrine, pituitary","MEN2A tumors: medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, parathyroid hyperplasia"]},{"t":"Steroid versus peptide signaling","p":"Draw a target cell receiving cortisol on the left side and glucagon on the right side. Trace each hormone from the blood to its effect and write how fast each effect starts.","l":["Cortisol crosses the membrane because it is lipid soluble","Cortisol binds a cytoplasmic receptor held by heat shock proteins","The hormone-receptor complex enters the nucleus and binds a hormone response element","Gene transcription and new protein synthesis follow, so onset takes hours","Glucagon binds a cell surface G protein-coupled receptor","Gs raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A","Second messenger effects start within seconds to minutes","Thyroid hormone works like cortisol but its receptor sits on DNA with the retinoid X receptor"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Introduction to Endocrinology"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-6","EN-8","EN-10","EN-12-A1","EN-12-D1","EN-12-G5","EN-12-G6"]},"EN-10":{"id":"EN-10","summary":"How the immune system attacks endocrine glands and how hormones and immunity talk to each other. The exam favors the mechanism behind Graves disease, Hashimoto thyroiditis, and type 1 diabetes, the polyendocrine syndromes, how cortisol suppresses inflammation, and why conjugate vaccines work.","notes":[{"h":"Graves disease","points":["IgG thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins bind the TSH receptor and switch it on, raising cAMP, gland growth, and hormone output.","This is a type II hypersensitivity (antibody against a cell surface receptor), here stimulating instead of destroying the cell.","TSH falls by negative feedback while T4 and T3 stay high, because the antibody does not respond to feedback.","Orbital fibroblasts and fat also express the TSH receptor; T cells and cytokines drive glycosaminoglycan buildup and swelling of orbital fat and extraocular muscles (ophthalmopathy).","IgG crosses the placenta, so a baby of a mother with Graves can have transient thyrotoxicosis that fades over weeks to a few months as maternal IgG is cleared. This can happen even after the mother's thyroid was removed."]},{"h":"Hashimoto thyroiditis","points":["Mainly T cell mediated: CD8 cytotoxic T cells kill follicular cells, and CD4 Th1 cells release interferon-gamma that activates macrophages.","Antibodies against thyroid peroxidase (most sensitive) and thyroglobulin are the diagnostic markers and add antibody-dependent cell killing.","Chronic lymphoid stimulation in the gland forms germinal centers and raises the risk of primary thyroid B cell lymphoma (extranodal marginal zone (MALT) or diffuse large B cell type)."]},{"h":"Type 1 diabetes and LADA","points":["Insulitis: islets fill with lymphocytes, mostly CD8 T cells, that selectively destroy beta cells and spare alpha cells.","Autoantibodies to GAD65, insulin, IA-2, and ZnT8 appear years before symptoms; they are markers of the attack, and having more of them predicts higher risk.","Symptoms begin after most beta cell mass is gone.","LADA (type 1.5) is slowly progressive autoimmune diabetes in adults: GAD65 antibody positive, at first managed without insulin, often mistaken for type 2, with C-peptide falling over time."]},{"h":"Autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes","points":["AIRE makes thymic medullary epithelial cells display tissue proteins so self-reactive T cells are deleted (central tolerance).","APS-1: autosomal recessive AIRE mutation; childhood chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and Addison disease.","The candidiasis in APS-1 comes from autoantibodies that neutralize IL-17 and IL-22, cytokines needed for mucosal defense against Candida.","APS-2: Addison disease with autoimmune thyroid disease, type 1 diabetes, or both; adult onset, more common in women, polygenic with HLA-DR3 and DR4."]},{"h":"Cytokines, cortisol, and vaccines","points":["IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha act on the hypothalamus to raise CRH, which raises ACTH and cortisol. Cortisol then dampens the inflammation, closing the loop.","Cortisol induces annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene production.","Cortisol blocks NF-kB signaling, cutting IL-1, IL-2, IL-6, and TNF-alpha output.","Cortisol lowers circulating lymphocytes and eosinophils and raises neutrophils (demargination).","Plain polysaccharide capsule is a T cell independent antigen: mostly IgM, little class switching, weak memory, and a poor response in children under 2 years.","Conjugating the polysaccharide to a protein carrier lets the B cell present carrier peptides on MHC II to helper T cells, giving CD40L help, class switching to IgG, affinity maturation, and memory, even in infants.","Meningococcal conjugate vaccines prevent invasive disease and so prevent Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Autoimmune endocrine disease","cols":["Disease","Main effector","Antibody markers","Effect on gland"],"rows":[["Graves disease","Stimulating IgG (type II)","TSH receptor stimulating antibody","Overactive, enlarged"],["Hashimoto thyroiditis","CD8 and Th1 T cells (type IV), with antibodies","Anti-TPO, anti-thyroglobulin","Destroyed, hypothyroid"],["Type 1 diabetes","CD8 T cells (insulitis)","GAD65, insulin, IA-2, ZnT8","Beta cells destroyed"],["Autoimmune Addison disease","T cells, with antibodies","21-hydroxylase antibodies","Cortex destroyed"]]},{"title":"APS-1 versus APS-2","cols":["Feature","APS-1","APS-2"],"rows":[["Genetics","AIRE mutation, autosomal recessive","Polygenic, HLA-DR3 and DR4"],["Onset","Childhood","Adulthood"],["Core features","Mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, Addison disease","Addison disease with autoimmune thyroid disease or type 1 diabetes"],["Mechanism","Failed central (thymic) tolerance","Shared susceptibility genes"]]},{"title":"Polysaccharide versus conjugate vaccine","cols":["Feature","Plain polysaccharide","Conjugate"],"rows":[["T cell help","No (T cell independent)","Yes (T cell dependent)"],["Main antibody","IgM","Class-switched IgG"],["Memory and boosting","Weak","Strong"],["Works under age 2","Poorly","Yes"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Graves stimulates, Hashimoto destroys","text":"Graves is an antibody that turns the TSH receptor on. Hashimoto is mostly T cells killing follicles, with anti-TPO as the marker."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Carrier protein brings the T cell","text":"The B cell sees the sugar but shows the protein. The protein peptide recruits a helper T cell, and that help is what buys class switching and memory."},{"kind":"trick","label":"APS-1 is candida plus two failed glands","text":"Chronic candidiasis, failed parathyroids, and failed adrenals. Think AIRE and the thymus, so it starts in childhood."}],"highYield":["Graves disease is a type II hypersensitivity with stimulating antibodies to the TSH receptor.","Transplacental IgG causes transient neonatal thyrotoxicosis in babies of mothers with Graves disease.","Anti-TPO antibody is the most sensitive marker of Hashimoto thyroiditis.","Hashimoto thyroiditis raises the risk of primary thyroid B cell lymphoma.","GAD65 antibodies mark type 1 diabetes and LADA.","APS-1 is an AIRE defect: candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, Addison disease.","Cortisol inhibits phospholipase A2 and NF-kB and lowers eosinophils and lymphocytes.","Conjugate vaccines turn a T cell independent response into a T cell dependent one with IgG and memory."],"pitfalls":["Graves and Hashimoto can both show antithyroid antibodies, but only Graves has stimulating TSH receptor antibodies.","Diabetes autoantibodies are markers of the attack; the beta cells are killed by T cells.","LADA looks like type 2 diabetes in an adult but is antibody positive and progresses to insulin need.","Cortisol raises the neutrophil count while lowering lymphocytes and eosinophils. Do not read the high white count as infection."],"dumps":[{"t":"Graves mother and baby","p":"Draw a pregnant woman with Graves disease, her thyroid, the placenta, and the fetal thyroid. Trace the antibody from where it is made to both thyroids and write the TSH and T4 result for mother and baby.","l":["B cells make IgG thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin","The antibody binds and activates the TSH receptor","Mother has high T4 and T3 with low TSH","IgG crosses the placenta (third trimester especially)","The antibody stimulates the fetal thyroid","The newborn can be thyrotoxic with low TSH","Neonatal disease is transient and fades as maternal IgG is cleared over weeks to months","This is type II hypersensitivity"]},{"t":"Conjugate vaccine","p":"Draw a B cell meeting a meningococcal polysaccharide joined to a carrier protein, and the helper T cell it works with. Label each step from antigen binding to memory B cell.","l":["B cell receptor binds the polysaccharide","The B cell takes in the whole conjugate","Carrier protein peptides are shown on MHC class II","A carrier-specific CD4 helper T cell recognizes the peptide","CD40L on the T cell binds CD40 on the B cell, with cytokines","Class switching to IgG and affinity maturation follow","Memory B cells form, so boosters work and infants respond","Antibody plus complement prevents invasive meningococcal disease and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on humoral immune responses to polysaccharide and protein antigens"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-9","EN-11","EN-12-A4","EN-12-B2","EN-12-C1","EN-12-D1","EN-12-H2"]},"EN-11":{"id":"EN-11","summary":"The microbes that damage endocrine glands: Neisseria meningitidis and the adrenal hemorrhage of Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome, bacteria and viruses that inflame the thyroid, and the infections that destroy the adrenal cortex. Questions describe the organism or the gland damage and ask you to connect the two.","notes":[{"h":"Neisseria meningitidis","points":["Gram-negative diplococcus (kidney-bean pairs), oxidase positive, ferments glucose and maltose, grows on chocolate agar.","Spread by respiratory droplets and close contact; it colonizes the nasopharynx, with outbreaks in dormitories and military barracks.","Virulence: polysaccharide capsule (blocks phagocytosis, defines serogroups), pili for attachment, IgA protease, and lipooligosaccharide (LOS) endotoxin.","The bacteria shed LOS-rich membrane blebs, so endotoxin levels in blood can be very high.","Higher risk with terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency and asplenia."]},{"h":"From endotoxin to adrenal hemorrhage","points":["LOS (lipid A) binds TLR4 on macrophages and triggers TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 release.","These cytokines injure endothelium and induce tissue factor, setting off DIC with microthrombi and consumption of platelets and clotting factors.","The adrenal has a rich arterial plexus draining into a limited venous outflow, so it is prone to hemorrhagic infarction when vessels clot and bleed.","Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage causes acute adrenal insufficiency and shock that does not respond to fluids, with a petechial or purpuric rash.","Other organisms that can cause it: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Pseudomonas, and staphylococci."]},{"h":"Thyroid infections","points":["Acute suppurative thyroiditis is rare because the gland has a capsule, rich blood and lymph flow, and high iodide content.","Usual bacteria: Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes, and Streptococcus pneumoniae.","In children, repeated left-sided infection points to a pyriform sinus fistula, a pharyngeal pouch remnant that lets bacteria track from the pharynx to the thyroid.","Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis follows a viral upper respiratory infection; coxsackievirus and mumps are among the viruses implicated.","Coxsackievirus is a nonenveloped positive-sense RNA picornavirus; mumps is an enveloped negative-sense RNA paramyxovirus spread by droplets."]},{"h":"Infectious adrenalitis","points":["Adrenal insufficiency appears only after about 90 percent of the cortex is destroyed, and infections usually hit both glands by blood spread.","Mycobacterium tuberculosis: acid-fast rod (mycolic acid wall) that forms caseating granulomas destroying cortex and medulla; healed glands often calcify.","Histoplasma capsulatum: dimorphic fungus from bird and bat droppings in the Ohio and Mississippi River valleys; small yeasts inside macrophages; disseminated disease often enlarges both adrenals.","Cytomegalovirus in AIDS: the most common adrenal infection at autopsy; cells show large intranuclear owl's eye inclusions."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Endocrine pathogens","cols":["Organism","Type and stain","Key feature","Endocrine disease"],"rows":[["Neisseria meningitidis","Gram-negative diplococcus","Capsule, LOS endotoxin, droplet spread","Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome"],["Staphylococcus aureus","Gram-positive cocci in clusters, coagulase positive","Abscess formation","Acute suppurative thyroiditis"],["Streptococcus pyogenes","Gram-positive cocci in chains, beta-hemolytic","Spreads from pharynx","Acute suppurative thyroiditis"],["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci, alpha-hemolytic","Polysaccharide capsule","Acute suppurative thyroiditis"],["Coxsackievirus, mumps virus","RNA viruses","Preceding upper respiratory illness","Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis"],["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Acid-fast rod","Caseating granulomas, adrenal calcification","Addison disease"],["Histoplasma capsulatum","Dimorphic fungus","Yeasts inside macrophages","Addison disease"],["Cytomegalovirus","Enveloped DNA herpesvirus","Owl's eye inclusions, AIDS","Adrenalitis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Fever, rash, shock, dead adrenals","text":"A young person with sudden fever, purpura, and shock that will not respond to fluids: think meningococcemia with bilateral adrenal hemorrhage."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Left side, again and again","text":"A child with a second left-sided thyroid abscess needs a search for a pyriform sinus fistula."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Painful thyroid: bacteria or virus","text":"Fluctuant, tender gland with normal thyroid function suggests bacteria. Tender gland with thyrotoxicosis after a cold suggests a virus (subacute thyroiditis)."}],"highYield":["Neisseria meningitidis: gram-negative diplococcus with a polysaccharide capsule and LOS endotoxin, spread by droplets.","Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is bilateral adrenal hemorrhage from sepsis and DIC, classically meningococcemia.","Terminal complement deficiency predisposes to recurrent Neisseria infection.","Acute suppurative thyroiditis: Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes, Streptococcus pneumoniae.","Recurrent left-sided thyroid infection in a child points to a pyriform sinus fistula.","Tuberculosis is the leading infectious cause of Addison disease worldwide.","Cytomegalovirus is the most common adrenal infection in AIDS."],"pitfalls":["Neisseria meningitidis ferments maltose and glucose; Neisseria gonorrhoeae ferments glucose only.","Subacute thyroiditis follows a virus, yet the gland shows granulomas with giant cells around colloid and no viral inclusions. Caseating granulomas point to tuberculosis instead.","Cytomegalovirus adrenalitis is common in AIDS but rarely destroys enough cortex to cause Addison disease on its own."],"dumps":[{"t":"Meningococcemia to adrenal crisis","p":"Trace the path from a meningococcus in the nasopharynx to shock from adrenal failure as a flow chart, writing the molecule or event at each step.","l":["Droplet spread and nasopharyngeal colonization using pili","Capsule lets the organism survive in blood","LOS endotoxin binds TLR4 on macrophages","TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 are released","Endothelial injury and tissue factor trigger DIC","Adrenal vascular anatomy favors hemorrhagic infarction","Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage causes acute adrenal insufficiency","Shock with petechial or purpuric rash"]},{"t":"Endocrine infections table","p":"Make a table with three rows (thyroid abscess, painful thyroid after a cold, destroyed adrenals) and columns for organisms, how to identify each organism, and what the gland shows.","l":["Thyroid abscess: Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes, Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus clusters versus streptococcal chains and lancet diplococci","Pyriform sinus fistula in recurrent left-sided cases","Painful thyroid after a cold: coxsackievirus or mumps","Subacute thyroiditis shows granulomas with giant cells","Adrenal destruction: tuberculosis, Histoplasma, cytomegalovirus in AIDS","Tuberculosis is acid-fast with caseating granulomas","Cytomegalovirus shows owl's eye inclusions"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on Neisseria meningitidis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on Mycobacterium tuberculosis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-1","EN-10","EN-12-B2","EN-12-C2","EN-12-H1","EN-12-H2"]},"EN-12-A":{"id":"EN-12-A","summary":"Diseases where a gland makes too much hormone: Cushing and Conn syndromes, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, hyperparathyroidism, GH excess, and hyperthyroidism, plus SIADH, familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, and precocious puberty. The exam asks you to find the source from paired hormone levels and to explain each sign from what the hormone does.","notes":[{"h":"Cushing syndrome","points":["Most common cause is exogenous glucocorticoids; the most common endogenous cause is an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease).","ACTH-dependent causes (pituitary adenoma, ectopic ACTH from small cell lung carcinoma) cause bilateral adrenal hyperplasia; ACTH-independent causes (adrenal adenoma or carcinoma, exogenous steroids) suppress ACTH, so the other adrenal atrophies with a tumor and both adrenals atrophy with exogenous steroids.","Screening: 24-hour urine free cortisol, late-night salivary cortisol, or failure of low-dose dexamethasone to suppress cortisol.","Then measure ACTH: low means adrenal or exogenous; normal or high means pituitary or ectopic.","High-dose dexamethasone suppresses a pituitary adenoma (some feedback is kept) but not ectopic ACTH.","Protein breakdown causes proximal muscle weakness, thin skin, wide purple striae, bruising, osteoporosis, and poor wound healing; fat redistribution causes central obesity, moon facies, and a dorsocervical fat pad.","Gluconeogenesis and insulin resistance cause hyperglycemia; hypertension comes partly from cortisol acting on mineralocorticoid receptors."]},{"h":"Hyperaldosteronism","points":["Primary: the adrenal makes aldosterone on its own, from bilateral adrenal hyperplasia (now the more common cause) or an aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn syndrome).","Findings: hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis (potassium and hydrogen ion loss in the collecting duct), with high aldosterone and suppressed renin.","No edema, because of aldosterone escape (pressure natriuresis and natriuretic peptides).","Secondary: high renin drives aldosterone, as in renal artery stenosis, heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, or diuretic use."]},{"h":"Parathyroid and calcium","points":["Primary hyperparathyroidism: single adenoma in about 85 percent; high calcium, low phosphate, and high or inappropriately normal PTH.","Effects: kidney stones and polyuria, bone pain, constipation, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, depression; most cases today are found on routine calcium tests.","Osteitis fibrosa cystica: osteoclastic resorption with fibrous replacement, subperiosteal resorption of the middle phalanges, and brown tumors (hemorrhage with osteoclast-type giant cells).","Secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD: phosphate retention and low calcitriol (lost 1-alpha-hydroxylase) lower calcium and stimulate PTH; calcium is low or normal and phosphate is high.","In vitamin D deficiency, calcium is low or normal, phosphate is low, and PTH is high.","Renal osteodystrophy is the bone disease of CKD; tertiary hyperparathyroidism is autonomous PTH secretion with hypercalcemia after long secondary stimulation, often seen after kidney transplant.","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia: autosomal dominant inactivating calcium-sensing receptor mutation; mild hypercalcemia, normal or mildly raised PTH, very low urine calcium, usually no symptoms, and surgery does not help."]},{"h":"GH excess","points":["Usually a somatotroph pituitary adenoma, often a macroadenoma at diagnosis.","Before epiphyses close: gigantism (excess linear growth). After closure: acromegaly (bones and soft tissues thicken).","Acromegaly: coarse facial features, frontal bossing, prognathism, enlarged hands and feet, macroglossia, sweating, carpal tunnel syndrome, and sleep apnea.","GH opposes insulin, causing insulin resistance and diabetes; cardiomyopathy and hypertension are the leading cause of death.","Diagnosis: raised IGF-1, then confirmation by failure of GH to suppress after an oral glucose load."]},{"h":"Hyperthyroidism","points":["Graves disease and toxic multinodular goiter both give low TSH and high free T4.","T3 raises Na-K-ATPase activity and metabolic rate (heat intolerance, sweating, weight loss with good appetite) and increases beta-adrenergic receptors in the heart (tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, tremor).","Excess thyroid hormone speeds bone turnover and causes bone loss.","Thyroid storm: fever, tachyarrhythmia, delirium, vomiting, and heart failure, triggered by stress such as infection, surgery, or delivery; it can be fatal."]},{"h":"SIADH and precocious puberty","points":["SIADH causes: small cell lung carcinoma, CNS disease (trauma, stroke, meningitis), lung disease (pneumonia), drugs (SSRIs, carbamazepine, cyclophosphamide).","SIADH labs: hyponatremia with low serum osmolality, urine osmolality inappropriately high, urine sodium high, and normal volume status.","Central precocious puberty: early GnRH activation with pubertal LH and FSH.","Peripheral precocious puberty: sex steroids from congenital adrenal hyperplasia, McCune-Albright syndrome (activating GNAS mutation), or a tumor; LH and FSH are suppressed."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Finding the source of Cushing syndrome","cols":["Cause","Plasma ACTH","High-dose dexamethasone","Clues"],"rows":[["Pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)","Normal or high","Cortisol suppresses","Most common endogenous cause"],["Ectopic ACTH (small cell lung carcinoma)","Very high","No suppression","Hypokalemia, hyperpigmentation, rapid course"],["Adrenal adenoma or carcinoma","Low","No suppression","Carcinoma often virilizes; other adrenal atrophies"],["Exogenous glucocorticoids","Low","No suppression","Drug history; both adrenals atrophic"]]},{"title":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","cols":["Deficiency","Blood pressure","Potassium","Sex steroids","Lab marker","Presentation"],"rows":[["21-hydroxylase","Low (salt wasting)","High","High","High 17-hydroxyprogesterone","Ambiguous genitalia in 46,XX; salt-wasting crisis in newborns"],["11-beta-hydroxylase","High","Low","High","High 11-deoxycortisol","Virilization with hypertension"],["17-alpha-hydroxylase","High","Low","Low","High 11-deoxycorticosterone","46,XY undervirilized; 46,XX no puberty"]]},{"title":"High calcium and PTH patterns","cols":["Condition","Calcium","Phosphate","PTH","Urine calcium"],"rows":[["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High","Low","High or inappropriately normal","Normal or high"],["Secondary (CKD)","Low or normal","High","High","Low"],["Secondary (vitamin D deficiency)","Low or normal","Low","High","Low"],["Tertiary hyperparathyroidism","High","Variable","Very high","Variable"],["Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia","Mildly high","Normal or low","Normal or mildly high","Very low"]]},{"title":"Graves disease versus toxic multinodular goiter","cols":["Feature","Graves disease","Toxic multinodular goiter"],"rows":[["Cause","TSH receptor stimulating antibody","Autonomous nodules in a long-standing goiter"],["Patient","Younger woman","Older adult"],["Gland","Diffuse goiter, often with bruit","Multiple nodules"],["Radioiodine uptake","Diffusely increased","Patchy, hot nodules with suppressed background"],["Ophthalmopathy and pretibial myxedema","Present","Absent"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Stones, bones, groans, thrones, and psychiatric overtones","text":"Kidney stones, bone pain, abdominal groans (constipation, ulcer, pancreatitis), polyuria, and depression or confusion from hypercalcemia in hyperparathyroidism."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Big dose, pituitary listens","text":"Only a pituitary adenoma still obeys feedback at a high dexamethasone dose. If cortisol falls, it is Cushing disease."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Renin splits the aldosterone cases","text":"High aldosterone with low renin is primary (the adrenal is acting on its own). High aldosterone with high renin is secondary (the kidney is asking for it)."}],"highYield":["Low ACTH means adrenal or exogenous Cushing; high ACTH with no suppression on high-dose dexamethasone means ectopic ACTH.","Primary hyperaldosteronism: hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, low renin, no edema.","21-hydroxylase deficiency: salt wasting, hyperkalemia, high 17-hydroxyprogesterone, virilization.","Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium, low phosphate, high PTH, usually a single adenoma.","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia has low urine calcium and needs no surgery.","Confirm acromegaly with high IGF-1 and failed GH suppression by oral glucose.","Ophthalmopathy and pretibial myxedema are specific to Graves disease.","SIADH: euvolemic hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine and high urine sodium."],"pitfalls":["Lid lag and stare happen in any thyrotoxicosis from sympathetic overactivity; true proptosis (ophthalmopathy) points to Graves disease.","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia can look like primary hyperparathyroidism. Check urine calcium before anyone operates.","Ectopic ACTH often lacks the classic Cushing body shape because it develops quickly; severe hypokalemia and hyperpigmentation are the clues.","In secondary hyperparathyroidism from CKD, phosphate is high; in vitamin D deficiency, phosphate is low.","A random GH level is not useful because GH is pulsatile; use IGF-1 and the glucose suppression test."],"dumps":[{"t":"Cushing workup","p":"Draw a decision tree for a patient with suspected Cushing syndrome, from the first screening test to each of the four causes. Write the test result on each branch.","l":["Screen with 24-hour urine free cortisol, late-night salivary cortisol, or low-dose dexamethasone","Measure plasma ACTH next","Low ACTH points to adrenal adenoma, adrenal carcinoma, or exogenous steroids","Normal or high ACTH points to pituitary or ectopic source","High-dose dexamethasone suppresses cortisol in pituitary adenoma","No suppression with high ACTH points to ectopic ACTH such as small cell lung carcinoma","Exogenous steroids are excluded by history"]},{"t":"Calcium disorders table","p":"Build a table for primary hyperparathyroidism, secondary hyperparathyroidism from CKD, secondary from vitamin D deficiency, tertiary hyperparathyroidism, and familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia. Fill in calcium, phosphate, PTH, and urine calcium, and write why PTH is high in each.","l":["Primary: high calcium, low phosphate, high PTH from an adenoma","CKD: phosphate retention and low calcitriol drive PTH up","CKD: calcium low or normal, phosphate high","Vitamin D deficiency: low gut calcium absorption drives PTH; phosphate low","Tertiary: glands become autonomous after long stimulation, with hypercalcemia","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia: calcium-sensing receptor set point is higher","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia has very low urine calcium"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on disorders of the adrenal cortex"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-5","EN-6","EN-8","EN-9","EN-10","EN-12-B","EN-12-G3","EN-12-G4"]},"EN-12-B":{"id":"EN-12-B","summary":"Diseases where a gland makes too little hormone, or the body cannot respond to it: diabetes insipidus, adrenal insufficiency, hypoparathyroidism, hypopituitarism, and hypothyroidism, plus short stature and euthyroid sick syndrome. Most questions hinge on reading paired trophic and target hormone levels to place the problem in the gland, the pituitary, or the target tissue.","notes":[{"h":"Diabetes insipidus","points":["Central: too little ADH, from head trauma, pituitary or hypothalamic surgery, tumors such as craniopharyngioma, or infiltrative disease.","Nephrogenic: the kidney does not respond to ADH, from V2 receptor (X-linked) or aquaporin-2 defects, lithium, hypercalcemia, or hypokalemia.","Both cause polyuria with dilute urine and thirst; serum sodium rises (hypernatremia) only when the patient cannot drink enough."]},{"h":"Adrenal insufficiency","points":["Primary (Addison disease): autoimmune adrenalitis in developed countries, tuberculosis worldwide, and metastases; all three cortical zones fail.","Low cortisol raises ACTH; POMC cleavage also releases MSH, causing hyperpigmentation of skin creases, scars, and buccal mucosa.","Low aldosterone causes sodium loss, hyperkalemia, volume depletion, and hypotension with high renin.","Low cortisol adds fatigue, weight loss, hypoglycemia, and eosinophilia.","Adrenal crisis: stress in a patient with chronic insufficiency, sudden stop of long-term glucocorticoids, or bilateral adrenal hemorrhage (anticoagulation, DIC, Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome).","Cortisol is needed for vessels to respond to catecholamines, so crisis causes shock that does not respond well to fluids and pressors.","Secondary: low ACTH from pituitary disease or suppression by exogenous glucocorticoids; no hyperpigmentation and no hyperkalemia because renin and angiotensin II still drive aldosterone."]},{"h":"Hypoparathyroidism","points":["Causes: surgical removal during thyroidectomy (most common), autoimmune (APS-1), DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion, absent third and fourth pharyngeal pouch derivatives), and severe hypomagnesemia.","Labs: low calcium, high phosphate, low PTH.","Low calcium makes nerves fire too easily: perioral and fingertip numbness, muscle cramps, carpopedal spasm, tetany, laryngospasm, and seizures.","Chvostek sign is facial twitching when the facial nerve is tapped; Trousseau sign is carpal spasm after a blood pressure cuff is inflated above systolic pressure. The ECG shows a long QT interval.","Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a: maternally inherited inactivating GNAS mutation causes PTH resistance; calcium is low and phosphate high, but PTH is high. Patients have Albright hereditary osteodystrophy (short stature, round face, short fourth and fifth metacarpals)."]},{"h":"Hypopituitarism and hypothalamic lesions","points":["Causes: nonfunctioning macroadenoma (most common in adults), Sheehan necrosis, pituitary apoplexy, surgery, radiation, and infiltrative disease.","Symptoms usually appear only after about 75 percent of the anterior pituitary is lost; GH and gonadotropins tend to fail before TSH and ACTH.","Primary empty sella: arachnoid and CSF herniate through a defective diaphragma sellae and flatten the pituitary; function is usually normal, but it can cause hypopituitarism or mild hyperprolactinemia.","Hypothalamic lesions (craniopharyngioma, sarcoidosis, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, radiation) lower releasing hormones, so anterior pituitary output falls.","They also cause central diabetes insipidus, and prolactin rises because dopamine no longer reaches the lactotrophs."]},{"h":"Hypothyroidism","points":["Iodine deficiency lowers T4 and T3, TSH rises, and the follicles grow, first as a diffuse goiter and later as a multinodular goiter.","Features: cold intolerance, weight gain, fatigue, bradycardia, constipation, dry skin, slow relaxation of deep tendon reflexes, and high LDL (fewer LDL receptors).","Myxedema: glycosaminoglycans build up in the dermis and soft tissue, giving nonpitting swelling, a puffy face, an enlarged tongue, and a hoarse voice.","In primary hypothyroidism, high TRH stimulates lactotrophs, raising prolactin; this can cause galactorrhea, menstrual disturbance, and infertility.","Congenital hypothyroidism: thyroid dysgenesis (most common), dyshormonogenesis, or maternal iodine deficiency; babies show lethargy, prolonged jaundice, large tongue, umbilical hernia, and intellectual disability if untreated.","Euthyroid sick syndrome: severe illness reduces peripheral T4 to T3 conversion, giving low T3, high reverse T3, and usually normal TSH."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Water deprivation test with desmopressin","cols":["Diagnosis","Urine after water deprivation","Urine after desmopressin","ADH level"],"rows":[["Normal","Concentrated","Little further rise","Rises appropriately"],["Primary polydipsia","Concentrated (may be less than normal)","Little further rise","Low at baseline, rises with deprivation"],["Central diabetes insipidus","Stays dilute","Concentrates (large rise, often over 50 percent)","Low"],["Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Stays dilute","Little or no rise","High"]]},{"title":"Primary versus secondary adrenal insufficiency","cols":["Feature","Primary (Addison disease)","Secondary"],"rows":[["Site of problem","Adrenal cortex","Pituitary (or glucocorticoid suppression)"],["ACTH","High","Low"],["Cortisol","Low","Low"],["Aldosterone","Low","Normal"],["Hyperpigmentation","Present","Absent"],["Potassium","High","Normal"]]},{"title":"Low calcium disorders","cols":["Condition","Calcium","Phosphate","PTH","Body type"],"rows":[["Hypoparathyroidism","Low","High","Low","Normal"],["Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a","Low","High","High","Albright hereditary osteodystrophy"],["Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism","Normal","Normal","Normal","Albright hereditary osteodystrophy"]]},{"title":"Thyroid function test patterns","cols":["Condition","TSH","Free T4","Other"],"rows":[["Primary hypothyroidism","High","Low","Prolactin may be high"],["Central hypothyroidism","Low or inappropriately normal","Low","Look for other pituitary deficits"],["TSH-secreting adenoma","High or inappropriately normal","High","Goiter with thyrotoxicosis"],["Euthyroid sick syndrome","Usually normal","Normal or low","Low T3, high reverse T3"]]},{"title":"Short stature","cols":["Cause","GH","IGF-1","Bone age","Clue"],"rows":[["GH deficiency","Low on stimulation","Low","Delayed","Slow growth, chubby build"],["Laron syndrome (GH receptor defect)","High","Low","Delayed","No response to GH"],["Constitutional growth delay","Normal","Normal for bone age","Delayed","Late puberty, normal adult height"],["Familial short stature","Normal","Normal","Normal","Short parents, normal growth rate"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 6 Ps of congenital hypothyroidism","text":"Pot-bellied, pale, puffy-faced child with a protruding umbilicus, protuberant tongue, and poor brain development."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Brown and salty means adrenal","text":"Hyperpigmentation plus hyperkalemia places adrenal insufficiency in the adrenal gland itself. Without them, think pituitary or steroid withdrawal."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Desmopressin answers the question","text":"If urine stays dilute after water deprivation, give desmopressin. If the kidney concentrates, the problem was missing ADH (central). If it still cannot, the kidney is deaf to ADH (nephrogenic)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Low calcium, then look at PTH","text":"Low calcium with low PTH is a gland problem (hypoparathyroidism). Low calcium with high PTH and short fourth and fifth knuckles is a receptor problem (pseudohypoparathyroidism)."}],"highYield":["Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus does not respond to desmopressin; central does.","Addison disease: hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hyperpigmentation from high ACTH and MSH.","Secondary adrenal insufficiency has no hyperpigmentation and no hyperkalemia.","Abrupt withdrawal of long-term glucocorticoids is a classic cause of adrenal crisis.","Hypoparathyroidism after thyroid surgery: low calcium, high phosphate, low PTH, Chvostek and Trousseau signs.","Pseudohypoparathyroidism has high PTH and short fourth and fifth metacarpals from a GNAS mutation.","Hypothalamic or stalk lesions raise prolactin because dopamine is cut off.","Euthyroid sick syndrome: low T3, high reverse T3, normal TSH."],"pitfalls":["Primary polydipsia also causes polyuria with dilute urine, but serum sodium tends to be low-normal and the urine concentrates with water deprivation.","Hyponatremia can occur in both primary and secondary adrenal insufficiency (low cortisol raises ADH), so use potassium and skin color to separate them.","Central hypothyroidism can show a normal TSH. A normal TSH with low free T4 is still abnormal.","Low magnesium causes hypocalcemia that will not correct until magnesium is replaced, because PTH release and action need magnesium.","Constitutional delay and familial short stature both grow at a normal rate. Bone age separates them: delayed in constitutional delay, normal in familial."],"dumps":[{"t":"Adrenal insufficiency map","p":"Draw the hypothalamus, pituitary, adrenal cortex, and kidney with arrows for CRH, ACTH, cortisol, renin, and aldosterone. Put an X on the adrenal for Addison disease and list the lab results, then move the X to the pituitary and list what changes.","l":["Addison: low cortisol and low aldosterone","Addison: high ACTH from lost feedback","High ACTH and MSH cause hyperpigmentation","Low aldosterone causes hyperkalemia, sodium loss, and high renin","Secondary: low ACTH and low cortisol","Secondary: renin and angiotensin II keep aldosterone normal","Secondary: no hyperpigmentation and no hyperkalemia","Exogenous glucocorticoids suppress CRH and ACTH and cause secondary insufficiency"]},{"t":"Water deprivation test","p":"Sketch a graph of urine osmolality over time during a water deprivation test, with desmopressin given near the end. Draw one line each for normal, primary polydipsia, central diabetes insipidus, and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, and label the cause of each pattern.","l":["Normal line rises with deprivation and barely changes after desmopressin","Primary polydipsia rises with deprivation, sometimes less than normal","Central diabetes insipidus stays low during deprivation","Central diabetes insipidus jumps after desmopressin","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus stays low throughout","Nephrogenic has high ADH levels","Lithium and hypercalcemia are acquired nephrogenic causes"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on disorders of the hypothalamus and pituitary gland"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-5","EN-6","EN-10","EN-11","EN-12-A","EN-12-C1","EN-12-F1","EN-12-H2"]},"EN-12-C":{"id":"EN-12-C","summary":"The inflammatory diseases of the thyroid: Hashimoto thyroiditis, subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis, painless and postpartum thyroiditis, and Riedel thyroiditis. Questions give you the feel of the gland, the thyroid tests, the radioiodine uptake, or a biopsy description and ask which disease it is and how it will progress.","notes":[{"h":"Hashimoto thyroiditis","points":["Most common cause of hypothyroidism where iodine intake is adequate; usually a middle-aged woman with a painless, firm, diffusely enlarged goiter.","Autoimmune loss of tolerance: cytotoxic T cells and Th1-activated macrophages destroy follicular cells; anti-TPO and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies are present.","Histology: dense lymphocyte and plasma cell infiltrate with germinal centers, small atrophic follicles, and Hurthle cells (pink granular cytoplasm packed with mitochondria).","Early follicle damage can leak stored hormone and cause brief thyrotoxicosis (hashitoxicosis).","The usual course is a slow slide through subclinical hypothyroidism (high TSH, normal free T4) to permanent hypothyroidism.","It travels with other autoimmune diseases: type 1 diabetes, pernicious anemia, Addison disease, celiac disease, and vitiligo.","It raises the risk of primary thyroid B cell lymphoma, which shows up as a rapidly enlarging thyroid in a patient with known Hashimoto."]},{"h":"Subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis","points":["Follows a viral upper respiratory infection, most often in women aged 30 to 50.","Painful, tender thyroid with pain spreading to the jaw or ear, fever, malaise, and a very high ESR.","Damaged follicles leak stored hormone, so T4 is high and TSH is low, but radioiodine uptake is low because the gland is not making new hormone.","Histology: follicle destruction with neutrophils early, then lymphocytes and macrophages, and granulomas with multinucleated giant cells around pools of colloid.","Course: thyrotoxic phase for weeks, often a transient hypothyroid phase as stores run out, then recovery to normal function in most patients within months."]},{"h":"Painless and Riedel thyroiditis","points":["Painless (lymphocytic) thyroiditis: painless, mildly enlarged, nontender gland; transient thyrotoxicosis with low uptake, then often a hypothyroid phase.","Postpartum thyroiditis is the same process within the first year after delivery; it is linked to anti-TPO antibodies and can recur with later pregnancies.","Histology of painless thyroiditis shows lymphocytes without the fibrosis and Hurthle cells of Hashimoto.","Riedel thyroiditis: IgG4-related dense fibrosis that extends beyond the capsule into neck structures, giving a rock-hard, fixed, painless thyroid.","Riedel can compress the trachea, esophagus, or recurrent laryngeal nerve and mimic anaplastic carcinoma; it may occur with other fibrosing diseases such as retroperitoneal fibrosis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Comparing the thyroiditides","cols":["Disease","Gland on exam","Thyroid function course","Radioiodine uptake","Histology"],"rows":[["Hashimoto thyroiditis","Painless, firm, diffuse goiter","Sometimes brief thyrotoxicosis, then permanent hypothyroidism","Variable; low when the gland is damaged","Lymphocytes with germinal centers, atrophic follicles, Hurthle cells"],["Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","Painful, tender","Thyrotoxic, then transient hypothyroid, then usually normal","Low in the thyrotoxic phase","Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells around colloid"],["Painless or postpartum thyroiditis","Painless, mildly enlarged","Thyrotoxic, then hypothyroid, usually recovers","Low in the thyrotoxic phase","Lymphocytes without fibrosis or Hurthle cells"],["Riedel thyroiditis","Rock-hard, fixed, painless","Often normal; hypothyroid if gland is replaced","Low or normal","Dense fibrosis with IgG4 plasma cells extending past the capsule"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Leaking versus making","text":"Thyrotoxic with high uptake means the gland is making hormone (Graves, toxic nodules). Thyrotoxic with low uptake means the gland is leaking stored hormone (subacute, painless, or postpartum thyroiditis)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sort by how the gland feels","text":"Painful and tender: subacute. Painless and rubbery: Hashimoto. Painless and mildly enlarged after a baby: postpartum. Rock-hard and stuck: Riedel."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Hurthle cells mean Hashimoto","text":"When the biopsy shows lymphoid germinal centers with Hurthle cells, it is Hashimoto. Giant cells around colloid point to subacute thyroiditis."}],"highYield":["Hashimoto thyroiditis: lymphocytes with germinal centers, Hurthle cells, anti-TPO, painless goiter.","Hashimoto thyroiditis raises the risk of primary thyroid B cell lymphoma.","Subacute thyroiditis: painful thyroid after a viral illness, high ESR, granulomas with giant cells.","Subacute thyroiditis is thyrotoxic with low radioiodine uptake and is self-limited.","Postpartum thyroiditis is painless, anti-TPO linked, and transient.","Riedel thyroiditis is IgG4-related fibrosis that makes the thyroid rock-hard and fixed."],"pitfalls":["Hashitoxicosis and Graves disease can both cause early thyrotoxicosis. Graves has high uptake and stimulating TSH receptor antibodies; hashitoxicosis has low uptake.","Subacute thyroiditis is painful and has a high ESR; painless thyroiditis has the same thyroid test course but no pain and a normal or mildly raised ESR.","A hard, fixed thyroid suggests cancer first, but Riedel thyroiditis is the inflammatory mimic. Biopsy shows fibrosis without malignant cells."],"dumps":[{"t":"Thyroiditis comparison table","p":"Build a table with rows for Hashimoto, subacute granulomatous, painless or postpartum, and Riedel thyroiditis. Fill in columns for pain, typical patient, thyroid function course, radioiodine uptake, histology, and one complication.","l":["Hashimoto: painless goiter in a middle-aged woman","Hashimoto: permanent hypothyroidism and B cell lymphoma risk","Hashimoto histology: germinal centers and Hurthle cells","Subacute: painful after a viral illness, high ESR","Subacute histology: granulomas with multinucleated giant cells","Subacute and painless: thyrotoxic with low uptake, then recovery","Postpartum: within a year of delivery, anti-TPO, recurs in later pregnancies","Riedel: rock-hard fixed gland from IgG4-related fibrosis"]},{"t":"Subacute thyroiditis timeline","p":"Draw a timeline across about four to six months for subacute thyroiditis. Plot free T4 and TSH, mark the phases, and note radioiodine uptake and symptoms in each phase.","l":["Starts after a viral upper respiratory infection","Thyrotoxic phase: high free T4, low TSH","Radioiodine uptake is low in the thyrotoxic phase","Pain, tenderness, fever, and high ESR early","Transient hypothyroid phase with high TSH as stores run out","Recovery to normal function in most patients","Histology shows granulomas with giant cells"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on thyroid disease"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-2","EN-10","EN-11","EN-12-A4","EN-12-B5","EN-12-H1"]},"EN-12-D":{"id":"EN-12-D","summary":"Diabetes mellitus is chronic hyperglycemia from too little insulin, too little insulin effect, or both. Boards ask you to separate type 1 from type 2, read the diagnostic cutoffs, tell diabetic ketoacidosis from hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state, and trace how high glucose damages vessels, nerves, kidneys, eyes, and lenses.","notes":[{"h":"Type 1 vs type 2","points":["Type 1: T cell mediated autoimmune destruction of beta cells, so insulin deficiency is absolute.","Type 1 markers: HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4, autoantibodies to GAD65, insulin, IA-2, and ZnT8.","Type 1 symptoms appear after most beta cells are gone; onset is usually in childhood or young adulthood, in thin patients.","Type 2: insulin resistance in muscle, fat, and liver plus progressive beta cell failure.","Type 2 islets show amyloid deposits made of amylin (islet amyloid polypeptide).","Type 2 is tied to obesity (especially visceral fat), inactivity, and family history; twin concordance is higher than in type 1.","Type 1 is ketosis prone; in type 2, the remaining insulin usually holds back lipolysis and ketone production."]},{"h":"Diagnosis and HbA1c","points":["Diabetes if any one is met: fasting glucose at least 126 mg/dL, 2-hour glucose at least 200 mg/dL on a 75 g oral glucose tolerance test, HbA1c at least 6.5%, or random glucose at least 200 mg/dL with classic symptoms.","Without symptoms, an abnormal result is confirmed with a repeat test.","Prediabetes: fasting 100 to 125 mg/dL, 2-hour 140 to 199 mg/dL, or HbA1c 5.7 to 6.4%.","HbA1c is hemoglobin that glucose has attached to nonenzymatically, so it rises with average glucose.","Red cells live about 120 days, so HbA1c reflects about the last 3 months.","HbA1c reads falsely low when red cells turn over fast (hemolysis, recent blood loss)."]},{"h":"Acute crises: DKA and HHS","points":["DKA: with no insulin, lipolysis runs unchecked; high glucagon then drives the liver to turn the fatty acids into beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate.","Ketoacids cause an anion gap metabolic acidosis; deep, rapid Kussmaul breathing blows off CO2 to compensate.","DKA also brings fruity (acetone) breath, abdominal pain, vomiting, and dehydration from osmotic diuresis.","Serum potassium is often high in DKA because insulin lack and hyperosmolality shift potassium out of cells, yet total body potassium is low from urinary loss.","HHS: usually older type 2 patients; glucose often above 600 mg/dL and osmolality above 320 mOsm/kg cause severe dehydration and confusion or coma.","HHS has little or no ketosis because a small amount of insulin is enough to block ketogenesis."]},{"h":"Why chronic hyperglycemia damages tissue","points":["Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) form when glucose binds proteins without enzymes; they cross-link collagen and trap LDL and albumin in vessel walls.","AGEs binding their receptor (RAGE) trigger inflammation, oxidative stress, and growth factor release, thickening basement membranes.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis: plasma proteins leak into small arteriole walls, giving a pink, glassy, narrowed lumen.","Polyol pathway: aldose reductase turns glucose into sorbitol in tissues that take up glucose without insulin (lens, nerves, retina, kidney).","Sorbitol is trapped and pulls in water (osmotic injury); the reaction uses NADPH, leaving less glutathione to fight oxidants.","Results: the polyol pathway gives cataracts (lens) and peripheral neuropathy (Schwann cells and nerves); AGEs and vessel injury speed up atherosclerosis, leading to MI, stroke, and peripheral artery disease."]},{"h":"Metabolic syndrome","points":["Any 3 of 5: abdominal obesity, triglycerides at least 150 mg/dL, low HDL (under 40 mg/dL in men, under 50 mg/dL in women), blood pressure at least 130/85 mm Hg, fasting glucose at least 100 mg/dL.","Waist cutoffs (US): over 102 cm (40 in) in men, over 88 cm (35 in) in women.","Insulin resistance is the common thread: visceral fat floods the liver with free fatty acids, so the liver makes more VLDL (high triglycerides, low HDL).","Acanthosis nigricans: velvety dark plaques in the neck folds and axillae; high insulin stimulates IGF-1 receptors on keratinocytes and fibroblasts."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes","cols":["Feature","Type 1","Type 2"],"rows":[["Main defect","Autoimmune beta cell destruction","Insulin resistance plus beta cell dysfunction"],["Insulin level","Absent or very low","High early, falls later"],["Typical patient","Young, thin","Older, obese (now also obese youth)"],["Genetics","HLA-DR3, HLA-DR4","Polygenic, strong family history"],["Islet histology","Insulitis (lymphocytes in islets)","Islet amyloid (amylin)"],["Autoantibodies","GAD65, insulin, IA-2, ZnT8","Absent"],["Acute crisis","DKA","HHS (DKA can still occur under severe stress)"]]},{"title":"DKA vs HHS","cols":["Feature","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state"],"rows":[["Usual patient","Type 1, often young","Type 2, often older"],["Glucose","Usually above 250 mg/dL","Often above 600 mg/dL"],["Ketones","Large","Little or none"],["Arterial pH and bicarbonate","pH under 7.30, low bicarbonate, high anion gap","pH above 7.30, near normal bicarbonate"],["Serum osmolality","Raised, variable","Above 320 mOsm/kg"],["Key signs","Kussmaul breathing, fruity breath, abdominal pain","Severe dehydration, confusion, seizures, coma"],["Potassium","Serum high or normal, total body low","Total body low"]]},{"title":"Chronic complications by organ","cols":["Organ","Mechanism","Classic findings"],"rows":[["Kidney","GBM thickening, mesangial expansion, hyaline arteriolosclerosis of afferent and efferent arterioles","Albuminuria progressing to nephrotic range proteinuria; Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (nodular glomerulosclerosis)"],["Retina","Pericyte loss and microvascular leak, then ischemia driving VEGF","Microaneurysms, dot-blot hemorrhages, hard exudates, then neovascularization"],["Lens","Sorbitol accumulation","Cataracts"],["Peripheral nerves","Sorbitol, oxidative stress, small vessel disease","Stocking-glove loss of sensation and vibration"],["Autonomic nerves","Same nerve injury","Gastroparesis, orthostatic hypotension, erectile dysfunction, bladder dysfunction"],["Large arteries","Accelerated atherosclerosis","MI (leading cause of death), stroke, peripheral artery disease"],["Feet","Neuropathy plus ischemia plus impaired healing","Painless ulcers, infection, gangrene"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"The potassium paradox","text":"In DKA the lab shows high potassium while the body is short of it. Insulin pushes potassium back into cells, so serum potassium falls fast once insulin is given."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Insulin-independent tissues get sorbitol","text":"Lens, nerves, retina, and kidney take up glucose without insulin, so high blood glucose floods them and aldose reductase makes sorbitol. That list matches the classic chronic complications."},{"kind":"trick","label":"A little insulin stops ketones","text":"It takes far less insulin to block lipolysis and ketogenesis than to move glucose into muscle. Type 2 patients keep that small amount, so they drift into HHS instead of DKA."}],"highYield":["HbA1c reflects about 3 months of glucose because red cells live about 120 days.","DKA: anion gap metabolic acidosis, Kussmaul breathing, high serum potassium with total body depletion.","HHS: glucose often above 600 mg/dL, osmolality above 320 mOsm/kg, altered mental status, little ketosis.","Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (nodular glomerulosclerosis) are highly characteristic of diabetic nephropathy; GBM thickening is the earliest change.","Albuminuria is the earliest clinical sign of diabetic nephropathy.","Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol in lens and nerve, causing cataracts and neuropathy.","Type 2 islets show amyloid from amylin; type 1 islets show insulitis.","Acanthosis nigricans in an obese patient points to insulin resistance."],"pitfalls":["A normal or high serum potassium in DKA does not mean body stores are normal; they are depleted.","A urine dipstick for ketones detects acetoacetate, while beta-hydroxybutyrate is the main ketone in DKA, so the dipstick can underestimate ketosis.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis occurs in both diabetes and hypertension; in diabetes it affects efferent as well as afferent arterioles.","Type 2 diabetes can present in obese teenagers, and type 1 can present in adults, so use antibodies and ketosis, along with age."],"dumps":[{"t":"DKA vs HHS table","p":"Make a two-column table comparing DKA and HHS. Include usual patient, cause, glucose, ketones, pH, osmolality, potassium, and key signs.","l":["DKA follows absolute insulin deficiency with high glucagon","DKA ketones are beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate","DKA gives an anion gap metabolic acidosis with Kussmaul breathing","Serum potassium high in DKA, total body potassium low","HHS glucose often above 600 mg/dL","HHS osmolality above 320 mOsm/kg with confusion or coma","HHS has little ketosis because some insulin remains","Both cause osmotic diuresis and dehydration"]},{"t":"From high glucose to organ damage","p":"Draw two pathways starting from high blood glucose: one through AGEs and one through the polyol (sorbitol) pathway. End each pathway at the organs it damages.","l":["AGEs form by nonenzymatic glycation of proteins","AGEs cross-link collagen, trap LDL and albumin, and activate RAGE","AGE path leads to basement membrane thickening and hyaline arteriolosclerosis","AGE path accelerates atherosclerosis (MI, stroke, peripheral artery disease)","Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol using NADPH","Sorbitol causes osmotic injury; NADPH loss reduces glutathione","Polyol path damages lens (cataracts), nerves (neuropathy), and retina","Kidney shows GBM thickening, albuminuria, and Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Insulin, Glucagon, and Diabetes Mellitus"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System, section on diabetes mellitus"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Endocrine Pancreas"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Diabetes Mellitus"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-6","EN-7","EN-10","EN-12-G2"]},"EN-12-E":{"id":"EN-12-E","summary":"A thyroglossal duct cyst is a leftover piece of the tract the thyroid follows as it descends from the tongue to the neck. Boards test where it sits, why it moves when the patient swallows or sticks out the tongue, how it differs from a branchial cleft cyst, and why the surgeon checks for a normal thyroid before removing it.","notes":[{"h":"How the cyst forms","points":["The thyroid starts as an endodermal outgrowth at the foramen cecum, between the front two-thirds and back third of the tongue.","It descends in the midline in front of the pharynx, the hyoid bone, and the laryngeal cartilages, reaching its final spot by about week 7.","It stays connected to the tongue by the thyroglossal duct, which normally disappears.","A pyramidal lobe is a normal remnant of the lower end of the duct.","If part of the duct persists, its lining secretes fluid and forms a cyst anywhere along the path."]},{"h":"Clinical picture","points":["Most common congenital neck mass; usually found in childhood, sometimes in adults.","Painless, smooth, midline mass, most often at or just below the hyoid bone.","Moves upward with swallowing (tied to the hyoid and larynx) and with tongue protrusion (tract runs to the tongue base).","Lined by respiratory or squamous epithelium; thyroid follicles may sit in the wall.","Complications: infection from mouth bacteria through the tract, abscess, a draining sinus or fistula, and rarely papillary thyroid carcinoma in the cyst."]},{"h":"Lateral look-alike: branchial cleft cyst","points":["Usually from the second branchial cleft (persistent cervical sinus).","Sits on the lateral neck along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid, below the angle of the jaw.","Does not move with swallowing or tongue protrusion.","Can also become infected, often after an upper respiratory infection."]},{"h":"Why check the thyroid first","points":["If the thyroid stopped descending, the mass may contain the only functioning thyroid tissue (ectopic thyroid).","Removing it would leave the patient permanently hypothyroid.","Neck ultrasound or a thyroid scan confirms a normally placed gland before surgery.","Standard surgery removes the cyst, the whole tract, and the middle of the hyoid body (Sistrunk procedure) to prevent recurrence."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Thyroglossal duct cyst vs branchial cleft cyst","cols":["Feature","Thyroglossal duct cyst","Branchial cleft cyst"],"rows":[["Origin","Persistent thyroglossal duct (thyroid descent path)","Persistent second branchial cleft (cervical sinus)"],["Position","Midline, near the hyoid bone","Lateral, anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid"],["Moves with swallowing","Yes","No"],["Moves with tongue protrusion","Yes","No"],["Lining","Respiratory or squamous epithelium, thyroid tissue in wall","Squamous or respiratory epithelium, lymphoid tissue in wall"],["Main complication","Infection, abscess, fistula","Infection, abscess, fistula"],["Before surgery","Confirm a normally placed thyroid","No thyroid check needed"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Middle moves, side stays","text":"A midline neck lump that rises when the patient sticks out the tongue is a thyroglossal duct cyst. A lump on the side, along the front edge of the sternocleidomastoid, that stays put is a branchial cleft cyst."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the thyroid's trip","text":"Trace the path from the foramen cecum down past the hyoid to the lower neck. A thyroglossal duct cyst or ectopic thyroid tissue (such as a lingual thyroid) sits somewhere on that midline path."}],"highYield":["Midline neck mass that moves up with swallowing and tongue protrusion: thyroglossal duct cyst.","The thyroid arises from the foramen cecum at the base of the tongue.","Infection is the most common complication, because the tract connects to the mouth.","Confirm a normally located thyroid before removal; the mass may be the only thyroid tissue.","Lateral neck cyst at the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid: branchial cleft cyst.","The pyramidal lobe is a normal remnant of the thyroglossal duct."],"pitfalls":["Moving with swallowing alone also happens with thyroid nodules; moving with tongue protrusion points to the thyroglossal tract.","A midline lump below the hyoid is not always a cyst; ectopic thyroid tissue looks the same, so image the neck first.","Branchial cleft cysts are lateral and do not rise with swallowing or tongue protrusion; position is the fastest way to tell the two apart."],"dumps":[{"t":"Path of thyroid descent","p":"Draw a side view of the tongue and neck. Trace the path of the thyroid from its origin to its final position and mark where a thyroglossal duct cyst can form.","l":["Origin at the foramen cecum of the tongue","Endodermal outgrowth","Path runs in the midline in front of the hyoid bone and larynx","Final position below the cricoid cartilage by about week 7","Thyroglossal duct normally disappears","Pyramidal lobe is a remnant of the lower duct","Cyst is most often at or just below the hyoid","Lingual thyroid forms if descent fails"]},{"t":"Midline vs lateral neck cysts","p":"Make a table comparing a thyroglossal duct cyst and a branchial cleft cyst: origin, location, movement, complications, and what to check before surgery.","l":["Thyroglossal: persistent thyroglossal duct","Branchial: persistent second branchial cleft","Thyroglossal is midline near the hyoid","Branchial is lateral at the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid","Thyroglossal moves with swallowing and tongue protrusion; branchial does not","Both can become infected and form abscesses or fistulas","Confirm a normal thyroid before removing a thyroglossal cyst"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck, section on the thyroid gland"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the thyroid gland and thyroglossal duct cysts"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, section on neck cysts"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-1","EN-3","EN-12-G5"]},"EN-12-F":{"id":"EN-12-F","summary":"The anterior pituitary depends on a low-pressure portal blood supply, so a sudden drop in blood flow can kill it. Boards test Sheehan syndrome after postpartum hemorrhage, why the posterior lobe is spared, which hormones are lost, and pituitary apoplexy, sudden bleeding into an adenoma.","notes":[{"h":"Why the anterior pituitary is vulnerable","points":["The anterior pituitary gets most of its blood from the hypophyseal portal veins, a low-pressure system fed by the superior hypophyseal arteries.","The posterior pituitary has a direct arterial supply from the inferior hypophyseal arteries, so it is usually spared.","In pregnancy, estrogen-driven lactotroph hyperplasia enlarges the anterior pituitary, raising its oxygen demand without a matching rise in blood supply."]},{"h":"Sheehan syndrome (postpartum pituitary necrosis)","points":["Severe postpartum hemorrhage causes hypotension and vasospasm, and the enlarged anterior lobe infarcts.","Necrosis must destroy most of the anterior pituitary before symptoms appear.","Onset can be right after delivery or months to years later.","Diabetes insipidus is uncommon because the posterior lobe usually survives."]},{"h":"Predicting the presentation","points":["Low prolactin: failure to breastfeed, often the first sign.","Low FSH and LH: amenorrhea (menses do not return) and infertility.","Low ACTH: fatigue, hypotension, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia; no hyperpigmentation and usually no hyperkalemia because aldosterone runs on renin-angiotensin.","Low TSH: cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, slow reflexes.","Low GH: hypoglycemia tendency and reduced muscle mass.","Loss of axillary and pubic hair from low adrenal and ovarian androgens."]},{"h":"Pituitary apoplexy","points":["Sudden hemorrhage or infarction into a pituitary adenoma, often a macroadenoma no one knew about.","Sudden severe headache from rapid expansion and meningeal irritation.","Visual loss, often bitemporal, from compression of the optic chiasm above.","Ophthalmoplegia from compression of the cavernous sinuses beside the sella (CN III, IV, VI).","Acute loss of ACTH causes adrenal crisis with shock and hypoglycemia; glucocorticoids are life-saving.","Triggers include anticoagulation, head trauma, pregnancy, and surgery."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Anterior pituitary hormone loss in Sheehan syndrome","cols":["Hormone lost","Target effect","Finding"],"rows":[["Prolactin","No milk production","Failure of lactation"],["FSH and LH","Low estrogen","Amenorrhea, infertility, vaginal atrophy"],["ACTH","Low cortisol and adrenal androgens","Fatigue, hypotension, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, pale skin"],["TSH","Low T4 and T3","Cold intolerance, weight gain, bradycardia"],["GH","Low IGF-1","Hypoglycemia tendency, less muscle mass"],["Androgen loss (ACTH plus FSH and LH)","Low adrenal and ovarian androgens","Loss of axillary and pubic hair"]]},{"title":"Sheehan syndrome vs pituitary apoplexy","cols":["Feature","Sheehan syndrome","Pituitary apoplexy"],"rows":[["Underlying gland","Normal gland enlarged by pregnancy","Pituitary adenoma"],["Event","Ischemic necrosis after postpartum hemorrhage","Hemorrhage or infarction inside the tumor"],["Onset","Can be gradual, even years later","Sudden"],["Headache and visual loss","Usually absent","Severe headache, visual loss, ophthalmoplegia"],["Shared danger","Adrenal insufficiency","Acute adrenal crisis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Portal means vulnerable","text":"The anterior lobe drinks second-hand, low-pressure portal blood, so it dies when pressure drops. The posterior lobe has its own arteries and keeps releasing ADH."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Start with the baby","text":"A new mother who cannot breastfeed after a big bleed is the classic opening. Then work down the hormone list: no periods, no thyroid hormone, no cortisol, no body hair."}],"highYield":["Failure of lactation after severe postpartum hemorrhage: Sheehan syndrome.","Posterior pituitary is spared because it has a direct arterial supply; diabetes insipidus is uncommon.","Pregnancy enlarges the anterior pituitary through lactotroph hyperplasia.","Secondary adrenal insufficiency causes no hyperpigmentation and usually no hyperkalemia.","Sudden headache, visual loss, and ophthalmoplegia in a patient with a pituitary adenoma: apoplexy.","Pituitary apoplexy causes acute adrenal crisis, which is the main threat to life."],"pitfalls":["Sheehan is ischemic and painless; apoplexy is sudden and painful with mass effect.","Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) gives hyperpigmentation and hyperkalemia; pituitary causes do not.","Ophthalmoplegia means lateral spread into the cavernous sinus, while bitemporal hemianopia means upward pressure on the chiasm.","Postpartum amenorrhea alone can be normal during breastfeeding; failure to lactate is what points to the pituitary."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pituitary blood supply","p":"Draw the hypothalamus, pituitary stalk, and both pituitary lobes. Label the arteries and the portal system, and mark which lobe dies in Sheehan syndrome and why.","l":["Superior hypophyseal arteries feed the primary capillary plexus in the median eminence","Hypophyseal portal veins carry blood down the stalk","Anterior lobe receives mainly low-pressure portal blood","Inferior hypophyseal arteries supply the posterior lobe directly","Pregnancy enlarges the anterior lobe through lactotroph hyperplasia","Hypotension after hemorrhage infarcts the anterior lobe","Posterior lobe and ADH are usually spared"]},{"t":"Hormone loss list","p":"List each anterior pituitary hormone and write the symptom a woman with Sheehan syndrome would have when that hormone is lost.","l":["Prolactin: failure of lactation","FSH and LH: amenorrhea and infertility","ACTH: fatigue, hypotension, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia","No hyperpigmentation because ACTH is low","TSH: hypothyroid symptoms such as cold intolerance","GH: hypoglycemia tendency","Androgen loss: loss of axillary and pubic hair"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System, section on hypopituitarism"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Hypothalamus and Pituitary Gland"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-4","EN-5","EN-12-B4","EN-12-G4"]},"EN-12-G":{"id":"EN-12-G","summary":"Endocrine tumors show up on the exam either by the hormone they make or by the space they take up. You need to recognize the main adrenal, pancreatic, parathyroid, pituitary, and thyroid tumors, the MEN syndromes that link them, pheochromocytoma, and the endocrine paraneoplastic syndromes.","notes":[{"h":"Adrenal tumors","points":["Cortical adenoma: small, well circumscribed, yellow; most are nonfunctional incidentalomas, some make cortisol or aldosterone.","A cortisol-secreting adenoma suppresses ACTH, so the rest of that gland and the other gland atrophy.","Cortical carcinoma: rare, large, invasive, often functional and virilizing; poor prognosis.","Metastases (lung, breast) to the adrenal are more common than primary adrenal carcinoma.","Neuroblastoma: adrenal medulla tumor of children under about 5, from neural crest; N-MYC amplification predicts a worse outcome.","Neuroblastoma shows Homer Wright rosettes, raises urine HVA and VMA, and can cause opsoclonus-myoclonus."]},{"h":"Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors and hypoglycemia","points":["Insulinoma: beta cell tumor, usually small, single, and benign; the most common functional islet cell tumor.","Whipple triad: hypoglycemic symptoms, low plasma glucose, and relief when glucose is given.","Insulinoma gives high insulin and high C-peptide during hypoglycemia; C-peptide is cut from proinsulin in equal amounts.","Injected insulin gives high insulin with low C-peptide, because commercial insulin has no C-peptide and it suppresses the patient's own release.","Glucagonoma (alpha cell): hyperglycemia, necrolytic migratory erythema, weight loss.","Somatostatinoma (delta cell): diabetes, gallstones, steatorrhea, achlorhydria, because somatostatin blocks insulin, CCK, pancreatic enzymes, and gastrin."]},{"h":"Parathyroid and pituitary tumors","points":["Parathyroid adenoma: about 85% of primary hyperparathyroidism; one enlarged gland of chief cells, other glands normal or shrunken.","Parathyroid hyperplasia involves all four glands and is the pattern in MEN1 and MEN2A.","Parathyroid carcinoma is rare; very high calcium, a firm neck mass, and invasion or metastasis define it.","Pituitary microadenoma is under 1 cm; macroadenoma is 1 cm or larger.","Prolactinoma is the most common functional pituitary adenoma; prolactin suppresses GnRH.","Nonfunctioning macroadenoma: bitemporal hemianopia (crossing nasal fibers in the chiasm), headache, hypopituitarism, mild hyperprolactinemia from stalk compression.","Craniopharyngioma: suprasellar tumor from Rathke pouch, calcified and cystic with oily fluid; causes headache, bitemporal hemianopia, diabetes insipidus, and growth failure in children."]},{"h":"Thyroid nodules and cancers","points":["Follicular adenoma and follicular carcinoma look alike on cytology; only capsular or vascular invasion on the removed nodule proves carcinoma.","Follicular carcinoma spreads through blood to bone and lung.","Papillary carcinoma is the most common thyroid cancer, linked to childhood radiation; it spreads by lymphatics to cervical nodes, with excellent prognosis.","Medullary carcinoma comes from calcitonin-secreting C cells; its amyloid stroma is made from calcitonin; RET mutations link it to MEN2.","Anaplastic carcinoma: older adults, rapidly growing invasive mass with dysphagia and hoarseness, TP53 mutations, nearly always fatal.","Euthyroid (nontoxic) diffuse or multinodular goiter: normal TSH, compression causes dysphagia, dyspnea, or stridor.","A cold nodule (no radioiodine uptake) carries a higher cancer risk than a hot nodule, though most cold nodules are still benign."]},{"h":"Pheochromocytoma and paraneoplastic syndromes","points":["Pheochromocytoma: chromaffin cell tumor of the adrenal medulla that makes catecholamines.","Episodic headache, palpitations, and sweating with hypertension that can be sustained or come in spells.","Diagnose with raised plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urine metanephrines.","Associated with MEN2A, MEN2B, von Hippel-Lindau disease, and neurofibromatosis type 1.","Small cell lung carcinoma can secrete ACTH (Cushing syndrome) or ADH (SIADH with hyponatremia).","PTH-related peptide from squamous cell lung carcinoma or renal cell carcinoma causes hypercalcemia with suppressed PTH."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Thyroid neoplasms","cols":["Tumor","Cell or genetics","Histology clue","Spread and prognosis"],"rows":[["Follicular adenoma","Follicular cell, RAS mutations in some","Encapsulated, no capsular or vascular invasion","Benign"],["Papillary carcinoma","Follicular cell, BRAF or RET/PTC; childhood radiation","Orphan Annie eye nuclei, nuclear grooves, psammoma bodies","Lymphatic to cervical nodes; excellent prognosis"],["Follicular carcinoma","Follicular cell, RAS or PAX8-PPARG","Capsular or vascular invasion","Blood to bone and lung; good prognosis"],["Medullary carcinoma","Parafollicular C cell, RET; MEN2","Sheets of cells in amyloid stroma from calcitonin","Nodes and blood; raised calcitonin"],["Anaplastic carcinoma","Undifferentiated, TP53; older adults","Pleomorphic or spindle cells","Local invasion; very poor prognosis"]]},{"title":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia","cols":["Syndrome","Gene","Tumors"],"rows":[["MEN1","MEN1 (menin, tumor suppressor)","Parathyroid hyperplasia, pituitary adenoma (often prolactinoma), pancreatic endocrine tumors (gastrinoma, insulinoma)"],["MEN2A","RET (gain of function)","Medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, parathyroid hyperplasia"],["MEN2B","RET (gain of function)","Medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus"]]},{"title":"Sorting out hypoglycemia","cols":["Cause","Insulin","C-peptide","Key clue"],"rows":[["Insulinoma","High","High","Whipple triad, MEN1 link, sulfonylurea screen negative"],["Sulfonylurea use","High","High","Drug screen positive"],["Surreptitious insulin","High","Low","Health care worker or family member with diabetes"],["Sepsis","Low","Low","High glucose use, poor gluconeogenesis"],["Liver failure","Low","Low","Little glycogen and impaired gluconeogenesis"]]},{"title":"Pituitary and adrenal tumor quick view","cols":["Tumor","Who","Hallmarks"],"rows":[["Prolactinoma","Adults","Galactorrhea, amenorrhea, infertility in women; low libido in men"],["Nonfunctioning macroadenoma","Adults","Bitemporal hemianopia, headache, hypopituitarism, mild high prolactin"],["Craniopharyngioma","Children (also older adults)","Calcified suprasellar cyst, headache, bitemporal hemianopia, diabetes insipidus, growth failure"],["Adrenal cortical adenoma","Adults","Small, often incidental; may cause Cushing or Conn syndrome"],["Adrenal cortical carcinoma","Adults","Large, invasive, often virilizing"],["Neuroblastoma","Young children","N-MYC, Homer Wright rosettes, high HVA and VMA, opsoclonus-myoclonus"],["Pheochromocytoma","Adults","Episodic hypertension, headache, sweating, palpitations; high metanephrines"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"MEN1 = 3 P's, MEN2A = 2 P's, MEN2B = 1 P","text":"MEN1: pituitary, parathyroid, pancreas. MEN2A: parathyroid and pheochromocytoma (plus medullary thyroid). MEN2B: pheochromocytoma only (plus medullary thyroid, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"C-peptide tells you who made the insulin","text":"The pancreas releases insulin and C-peptide together. High insulin with high C-peptide means the body made it (tumor or sulfonylurea); high insulin with low C-peptide means it came from a syringe."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Papillary goes by lymph, follicular goes by blood","text":"Papillary carcinoma spreads to neck nodes. Follicular carcinoma invades vessels and lands in bone and lung, which is why vascular invasion is what proves follicular carcinoma."}],"highYield":["Most common thyroid cancer: papillary carcinoma, with Orphan Annie eye nuclei and psammoma bodies.","Medullary thyroid carcinoma: C cells, calcitonin, amyloid stroma, RET, MEN2.","Follicular carcinoma is diagnosed by capsular or vascular invasion and spreads by blood.","Insulinoma: high insulin and high C-peptide during hypoglycemia; think MEN1.","Most common functional pituitary adenoma: prolactinoma.","Child with a calcified suprasellar mass and diabetes insipidus: craniopharyngioma.","Pheochromocytoma: episodic headache, sweating, palpitations; raised metanephrines; MEN2, VHL, NF1.","Hypercalcemia with low PTH in a smoker: PTH-related peptide from squamous cell lung carcinoma."],"pitfalls":["Fine needle aspiration cannot separate follicular adenoma from follicular carcinoma; the whole capsule must be examined.","Mild hyperprolactinemia with a large pituitary mass is usually stalk compression; a prolactinoma that size makes far more prolactin.","Neuroblastoma and pheochromocytoma both come from the adrenal medulla; neuroblastoma is a childhood tumor with HVA and VMA, pheochromocytoma is mostly an adult tumor with raised metanephrines.","Parathyroid adenoma is one big gland; hyperplasia (as in MEN) is all four.","Hypercalcemia of malignancy has suppressed PTH; primary hyperparathyroidism has high or inappropriately normal PTH."],"dumps":[{"t":"Thyroid cancer table","p":"Make a table of the four thyroid carcinomas (papillary, follicular, medullary, anaplastic). For each, write the cell of origin, key gene or risk factor, histology clue, and how it spreads.","l":["Papillary: most common, childhood radiation, BRAF or RET/PTC","Papillary histology: Orphan Annie eye nuclei, nuclear grooves, psammoma bodies","Papillary spreads by lymphatics to cervical nodes; excellent prognosis","Follicular: capsular or vascular invasion, blood spread to bone and lung","Medullary: C cells, calcitonin, amyloid stroma","Medullary: RET mutations, MEN2A and MEN2B","Anaplastic: older adults, TP53, rapid local invasion, very poor prognosis"]},{"t":"MEN syndromes and their tumors","p":"Draw three body outlines labeled MEN1, MEN2A, and MEN2B. Mark the tumors of each syndrome on the right organ and write the gene under each outline.","l":["MEN1: parathyroid hyperplasia","MEN1: pituitary adenoma, often prolactinoma","MEN1: pancreatic endocrine tumors such as gastrinoma and insulinoma","MEN1 gene encodes menin, a tumor suppressor","MEN2A: medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, parathyroid hyperplasia","MEN2B: medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus","MEN2A and MEN2B: RET gain-of-function mutation","All are autosomal dominant"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Adrenal Medulla"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Thyroid Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Insulin, Glucagon, and Diabetes Mellitus"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-9","EN-12-A1","EN-12-A2","NE-20-F3"]},"EN-12-H":{"id":"EN-12-H","summary":"Two infections hit endocrine glands hard enough to show up on the exam. Acute suppurative thyroiditis is a rare bacterial infection of the thyroid that usually leaves hormone levels normal, and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is bilateral adrenal hemorrhage in overwhelming sepsis, classically meningococcemia, that causes shock from acute adrenal insufficiency.","notes":[{"h":"Acute infectious (suppurative) thyroiditis","points":["Rare because the thyroid has a capsule, a rich blood and lymph supply, and high iodine content.","Usual organisms: Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and mouth anaerobes; fungi and mycobacteria in immunocompromised hosts.","Routes: blood or lymph spread, direct spread from the neck, or a piriform sinus fistula (left side, often recurrent in children).","Risk: preexisting thyroid nodules or goiter, immunosuppression, recent fine needle aspiration.","Fever, sudden anterior neck pain, a hot, red, tender thyroid lobe, and pain on swallowing.","Thyroid function is usually normal; leukocytosis and raised ESR are common.","Complications: abscess, spread into deep neck spaces or mediastinum, airway compromise, sepsis."]},{"h":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome: mechanism","points":["Bilateral massive hemorrhage into the adrenal glands during overwhelming sepsis.","Classic cause is Neisseria meningitidis, a gram-negative diplococcus; other causes include Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Pseudomonas, and staphylococci.","Meningococcal lipooligosaccharide (endotoxin) drives TNF and IL-1 release, endothelial injury, and DIC.","DIC forms microthrombi and consumes platelets and clotting factors, so the adrenals bleed and infarct.","Loss of cortisol removes vascular tone support (cortisol lets vessels respond to catecholamines), so shock does not respond to fluids alone.","Loss of aldosterone adds salt wasting, hyponatremia, and hyperkalemia."]},{"h":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome: clinical picture","points":["Usually a child or young adult with fever that progresses over hours.","Petechial rash that spreads into purpura (purpura fulminans) from DIC and vessel damage.","Hypotension and shock, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia.","Labs: low platelets, prolonged PT and PTT, low fibrinogen, high D-dimer.","Risk: terminal complement deficiency (C5 to C9), asplenia, crowded living such as dormitories.","Very high mortality; survival needs antibiotics plus glucocorticoid replacement."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Painful thyroid: infectious vs subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","cols":["Feature","Acute infectious thyroiditis","Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis"],"rows":[["Cause","Bacterial (Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, anaerobes)","Postviral inflammation"],["Onset","Sudden, high fever","Follows a viral illness, often low-grade fever"],["Gland","One lobe hot, red, sometimes fluctuant","Diffusely tender, firm"],["Thyroid function","Usually normal","Transient thyrotoxicosis, then hypothyroid phase"],["Radioiodine uptake","Usually normal overall","Low"],["Main complication","Abscess and deep neck spread","Permanent hypothyroidism in a minority"]]},{"title":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome: cause to finding","cols":["Step","Mechanism","Finding"],"rows":[["Bacteremia","Neisseria meningitidis releases lipooligosaccharide","High fever"],["Cytokine storm","TNF and IL-1 injure endothelium","Vasodilation, capillary leak"],["DIC","Microthrombi consume platelets and factors","Petechiae, purpura, bleeding, low platelets"],["Adrenal hemorrhage","Both adrenals bleed and infarct","Acute adrenal insufficiency"],["Cortisol and aldosterone loss","Vessels stop responding to catecholamines; salt wasting","Refractory shock, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Hot lobe, normal labs","text":"A febrile patient with one red, hot, tender thyroid lobe and normal thyroid hormone levels points to bacterial thyroiditis. Painful thyroiditis with abnormal levels and low radioiodine uptake points to subacute granulomatous thyroiditis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Rash plus shock that will not lift","text":"Fever, a spreading purpuric rash, and hypotension that fluids and pressors cannot fix suggest the adrenals are gone. Think meningococcemia with Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome."}],"highYield":["Acute suppurative thyroiditis: fever, painful tender thyroid lobe, normal thyroid function, risk of abscess.","Recurrent left-sided suppurative thyroiditis in a child suggests a piriform sinus fistula.","Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome: bilateral adrenal hemorrhage in sepsis, classically Neisseria meningitidis.","Meningococcal endotoxin triggers DIC, which drives the adrenal hemorrhage and the purpuric rash.","Shock in Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome resists treatment until cortisol is replaced.","Terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency raises the risk of Neisseria infections."],"pitfalls":["Painful thyroid with normal hormone levels favors infection; painful thyroid with thyrotoxicosis and low uptake favors subacute thyroiditis.","Waterhouse-Friderichsen is acute primary adrenal failure; Sheehan syndrome is pituitary failure causing secondary adrenal insufficiency.","Neisseria meningitidis endotoxin is lipooligosaccharide, which lacks the long O-antigen chains of the lipopolysaccharide on enteric gram-negative rods.","Hyperkalemia here comes from aldosterone loss in primary adrenal failure; pituitary causes spare aldosterone."],"dumps":[{"t":"Meningococcemia to adrenal crisis","p":"Draw a flow chart that starts with Neisseria meningitidis in the blood and ends with refractory shock. Label each step and the finding it causes.","l":["Gram-negative diplococcus releases lipooligosaccharide","TNF and IL-1 release injures endothelium","DIC with microthrombi and consumption of platelets and factors","Petechial rash progressing to purpura","Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage","Cortisol loss removes vascular response to catecholamines","Aldosterone loss causes hyponatremia and hyperkalemia","Refractory shock and hypoglycemia"]},{"t":"Painful thyroid comparison","p":"Make a table comparing acute infectious thyroiditis and subacute granulomatous thyroiditis: cause, onset, how the gland feels, thyroid function, radioiodine uptake, and complications.","l":["Infectious: bacteria such as Staphylococcus aureus and streptococci","Subacute: follows a viral illness","Infectious: one lobe hot, red, sometimes fluctuant","Infectious: thyroid function usually normal","Subacute: transient thyrotoxicosis with low radioiodine uptake","Infectious risk: piriform sinus fistula, nodular goiter, immunosuppression","Infectious complication: abscess and deep neck spread"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System, sections on thyroiditis and adrenocortical insufficiency"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on Neisseria meningitidis"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Adrenal Cortex"}],"videos":[],"related":["EN-11","EN-12-B2","EN-12-C2","HE-9-C1","NE-20-G8"]},"GI-1":{"id":"GI-1","summary":"How the gut tube is divided into foregut, midgut, and hindgut, how the midgut herniates and rotates, and how the liver, pancreas, and enteric nerves form. Exam questions link each step to the birth defect that results when it fails, and use the gut divisions to explain blood supply and referred pain.","notes":[{"h":"Three parts of the gut tube","points":["Foregut: pharynx to the second part of the duodenum (at the major papilla), plus liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. Artery: celiac trunk.","Midgut: distal duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. Artery: superior mesenteric (SMA).","Hindgut: distal third of the transverse colon to the anal canal above the pectinate line. Artery: inferior mesenteric (IMA).","Visceral pain is dull and poorly localized and refers to the midline by gut division: foregut to the epigastrium, midgut to the umbilicus, hindgut to the suprapubic region.","Early appendicitis hurts at the umbilicus (midgut, T10) before it moves to the right lower quadrant once the parietal peritoneum is irritated."]},{"h":"Midgut herniation and rotation","points":["In week 6 the midgut loop grows faster than the abdomen and herniates into the umbilical cord (physiologic herniation).","The loop rotates 270 degrees counterclockwise (viewed from the front) around the axis of the SMA: 90 degrees while herniated, 180 degrees as it returns.","The loops return to the abdomen by about week 10, and the cecum descends to the right lower quadrant.","Malrotation leaves the cecum high and Ladd bands crossing the duodenum, and the narrow mesentery can twist (midgut volvulus) with bilious vomiting in a newborn.","Omphalocele: herniated loops fail to return, so viscera sit at the umbilicus in a sac of peritoneum and amnion."]},{"h":"Foregut septation and glands","points":["In week 4 the respiratory diverticulum buds from the ventral foregut; the tracheoesophageal septum then separates trachea in front from esophagus behind.","A septum that deviates or fails gives esophageal atresia, tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF), or both.","Most common type: proximal esophageal atresia with a distal TEF, causing drooling, choking on first feeds, an NG tube that coils, and air in the stomach.","The hepatic diverticulum grows from the distal foregut into the septum transversum; endoderm forms hepatocytes and bile duct lining, and its caudal part forms the gallbladder and cystic duct.","Pancreas: dorsal bud forms most of the gland; ventral bud rotates behind the duodenum and forms the uncinate process and part of the head.","Annular pancreas: a bifid ventral bud wraps around the second part of the duodenum and can obstruct it.","Pancreas divisum: dorsal and ventral ducts fail to fuse, so most juice drains through the minor papilla."]},{"h":"Enteric nerves and duodenal lumen","points":["Neural crest cells migrate along the gut from cranial to caudal to form the myenteric and submucosal plexuses.","Hirschsprung disease: failed migration (often RET mutation) leaves a distal aganglionic segment that always includes the rectum and cannot relax.","Hirschsprung signs: no meconium in the first 48 hours, a dilated colon above the narrow segment, and an explosive stool after rectal exam; biopsy shows no ganglion cells.","The duodenal lumen is filled in by epithelial growth around weeks 5 to 6 and opens again (recanalizes) by the end of the embryonic period.","Failed recanalization gives duodenal atresia: bilious vomiting, a double bubble on X-ray, polyhydramnios, and a strong link to Down syndrome."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gut tube divisions","cols":["Division","Adult structures","Artery","Parasympathetic supply","Pain referred to"],"rows":[["Foregut","Esophagus, stomach, duodenum to the major papilla, liver, gallbladder, pancreas","Celiac trunk","Vagus nerve","Epigastrium"],["Midgut","Distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, proximal two thirds of transverse colon","Superior mesenteric artery","Vagus nerve","Periumbilical region"],["Hindgut","Distal third of transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, anal canal above the pectinate line","Inferior mesenteric artery","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4)","Suprapubic region"]]},{"title":"Developmental errors and their signs","cols":["Defect","What went wrong","Classic finding"],"rows":[["Esophageal atresia with distal TEF","Tracheoesophageal septum deviates","Drooling and choking on first feed; gas in the stomach"],["Malrotation with volvulus","Incomplete 270 degree rotation","Bilious vomiting; Ladd bands over the duodenum"],["Omphalocele","Midgut fails to return from the cord","Midline sac covered by peritoneum and amnion"],["Annular pancreas","Bifid ventral bud encircles the duodenum","Duodenal obstruction in a newborn"],["Pancreas divisum","Dorsal and ventral ducts fail to fuse","Usually silent; minor papilla drains most juice"],["Duodenal atresia","Duodenal lumen fails to recanalize","Double bubble; linked to Down syndrome"],["Hirschsprung disease","Neural crest fails to reach the distal colon","Delayed meconium; aganglionic rectum"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"90 out, 180 back","text":"The midgut turns 90 degrees counterclockwise while it is in the cord and 180 degrees more as it comes home, for 270 degrees in all around the SMA."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"VACTERL","text":"Vertebral defects, Anal atresia, Cardiac defects, TracheoEsophageal fistula, Renal anomalies, Limb anomalies. A baby with a TEF gets checked for the rest."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the artery to the pain","text":"Name the artery that feeds the organ and you know where its pain refers: celiac to the epigastrium, SMA to the umbilicus, IMA to the suprapubic area."}],"highYield":["Duodenal atresia with a double bubble points to Down syndrome; jejunal and ileal atresia come from a vascular accident instead.","Hirschsprung disease is also more common in Down syndrome, and the rectum is always involved.","Omphalocele is linked to trisomies 13 and 18 and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome; gastroschisis has no covering sac.","The H-type TEF (no atresia) shows up later as recurrent aspiration pneumonia.","An annular pancreas and duodenal atresia both obstruct the second part of the duodenum; the vomiting is bilious when the block lies below the major papilla, as it usually does.","The spleen comes from mesoderm in the dorsal mesogastrium yet is supplied by the celiac trunk (splenic artery)."],"pitfalls":["Omphalocele is midline and covered by a sac; gastroschisis is usually right of the umbilicus with bare bowel and no sac.","Malrotation is a rotation problem; omphalocele is a return problem. Both involve the midgut.","The foregut ends at the major duodenal papilla, so the duodenum above the papilla hurts like the stomach and the duodenum below it like the small bowel.","The ventral pancreatic bud forms the uncinate process; the dorsal bud forms the body and tail."],"dumps":[{"t":"Gut tube map","p":"Draw the gut tube from esophagus to anal canal. Mark where the foregut, midgut, and hindgut begin and end, and write each division's artery, nerve, and pain referral site beside it.","l":["Foregut ends at the major duodenal papilla","Midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two thirds and distal third of the transverse colon","Hindgut ends at the pectinate line","Celiac, SMA, and IMA matched to each division","Vagus to foregut and midgut; pelvic splanchnics to hindgut","Pain to epigastrium, umbilicus, and suprapubic region","Liver, gallbladder, and pancreas drawn as foregut buds"]},{"t":"Midgut rotation and its errors","p":"Draw the midgut loop in three panels: herniated into the cord at week 6, rotating, and back in the abdomen at week 10. Then list the defect that results when each step fails.","l":["SMA drawn as the axis of rotation","Herniation into the umbilical cord in week 6","90 degrees counterclockwise in the cord","180 degrees more during return, 270 in total","Return by about week 10 with the cecum moving to the right lower quadrant","Incomplete rotation gives malrotation with Ladd bands and volvulus risk","Failed return gives omphalocele in a peritoneal and amniotic sac"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract (section on congenital abnormalities)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Development & Embryology of the GI Tract: Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4it-uI9e_I8"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Development & Embryology of the GI Tract: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PAMaaKocIsk"}],"related":["GI-5","GI-13-H","GI-13-H1","GI-13-H3","GI-13-F5","GI-13-B"]},"GI-2":{"id":"GI-2","summary":"The microscopic structure of the gut wall, stomach glands, small and large intestine, liver, pancreas, and salivary glands. Exam questions show a cell or a section and ask what it secretes, where it is, and what happens when it is damaged.","notes":[{"h":"Four layers of the gut wall","points":["Mucosa: epithelium, lamina propria, and a thin muscularis mucosae.","Submucosa: dense connective tissue with vessels and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus, which controls secretion and local blood flow.","Muscularis externa: inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle, with the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between them controlling motility.","Outer layer: serosa (mesothelium) over intraperitoneal parts; adventitia over the thoracic esophagus and retroperitoneal parts."]},{"h":"Epithelium along the tube","points":["Esophagus: nonkeratinized stratified squamous to resist abrasion.","Stomach through colon: simple columnar for secretion and absorption.","Anal canal: columnar above the pectinate line and stratified squamous below it.","Barrett esophagus: chronic acid reflux turns distal esophageal squamous epithelium into intestinal-type columnar epithelium with goblet cells, which raises adenocarcinoma risk."]},{"h":"Gastric gland cells","points":["Parietal cells (body and fundus): pink, fried-egg look; secrete HCl and intrinsic factor.","Chief cells (base of body and fundus glands): basophilic; secrete pepsinogen.","Mucous neck cells: mucus; surface mucous cells also add bicarbonate.","G cells (antrum): gastrin. D cells: somatostatin. ECL cells: histamine.","Autoimmune gastritis destroys body and fundus mucosa, so acid, intrinsic factor, and pepsinogen all fall while the antrum is spared.","Without acid feedback, antral G cells keep releasing gastrin (hypergastrinemia), and lost intrinsic factor leads to vitamin B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia)."]},{"h":"Small intestine versus colon","points":["Duodenum: Brunner glands in the submucosa secrete alkaline mucus to neutralize acid.","Jejunum: tall, closely spaced plicae circulares and long villi; the main absorptive segment.","Ileum: Peyer patches (lymphoid follicles with overlying M cells) in the lamina propria and submucosa.","Small intestine crypts hold stem cells and Paneth cells (lysozyme and defensins).","Colon: no villi, straight crypts packed with goblet cells, and outer longitudinal muscle gathered into taeniae coli."]},{"h":"Liver lobule and acinus","points":["Classic lobule: a hexagon with a central vein in the middle and portal triads (portal vein branch, hepatic artery branch, bile duct) at the corners.","Blood runs from the triads through fenestrated sinusoids to the central vein; bile runs the other way in canaliculi toward the triad.","Space of Disse: the gap between hepatocytes and sinusoid lining, where plasma meets hepatocyte microvilli.","Kupffer cells are sinusoid macrophages; stellate (Ito) cells in the space of Disse store vitamin A and make collagen in fibrosis.","Acinus zones: zone 1 near the triad gets the most oxygen; zone 3 near the central vein gets the least and has the most cytochrome P450.","Zone 3 dies first in shock or heart failure and in acetaminophen overdose (toxic metabolite NAPQI made by P450)."]},{"h":"Pancreas and salivary glands","points":["Pancreatic acini: basophilic base and apical eosinophilic zymogen granules; centroacinar cells start the ducts.","Islets of Langerhans: pale clusters of endocrine cells scattered among the acini.","Serous acini stain dark and make watery protein-rich (amylase) secretion; mucous acini look pale and foamy.","Parotid is mainly serous, submandibular is mixed (mostly serous), and sublingual is mostly mucous."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gastric gland cells","cols":["Cell","Location","Product","Loss causes"],"rows":[["Parietal","Body and fundus","HCl, intrinsic factor","Achlorhydria, B12 deficiency"],["Chief","Body and fundus, gland base","Pepsinogen","Low pepsin activity"],["Mucous neck and surface","Throughout stomach","Mucus, bicarbonate","Weaker mucosal barrier"],["G cell","Antrum","Gastrin","Less acid drive"],["D cell","Antrum and body","Somatostatin","Less brake on acid"],["ECL cell","Body and fundus","Histamine","Less acid drive"]]},{"title":"Telling gut segments apart","cols":["Segment","Epithelium","Key feature"],"rows":[["Esophagus","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Skeletal muscle above, smooth muscle below"],["Stomach body","Simple columnar","Gastric pits and glands with parietal and chief cells"],["Duodenum","Simple columnar with villi","Brunner glands in the submucosa"],["Jejunum","Simple columnar with villi","Tall plicae circulares"],["Ileum","Simple columnar with villi","Peyer patches"],["Colon","Simple columnar, no villi","Crypts rich in goblet cells; taeniae coli"]]},{"title":"Liver acinus zones","cols":["Zone","Position","Oxygen","Most vulnerable to"],"rows":[["Zone 1","Periportal","Highest","Viral hepatitis, toxins that arrive in portal blood"],["Zone 2","Middle","Intermediate","Yellow fever"],["Zone 3","Around the central vein","Lowest","Ischemia, acetaminophen, alcohol"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Brunner in the bulb","text":"Brunner glands mean duodenum. Think B for bicarbonate-rich mucus that buffers acid as it leaves the stomach."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Zone 3 is last in line","text":"Blood reaches zone 3 last, after the oxygen is used up, and zone 3 holds the most P450. Low oxygen and toxic drug metabolites both hit it first."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Parotid is pure","text":"The parotid is almost entirely serous, so its acini all stain dark. Pale foamy mucous acini mean submandibular or sublingual."}],"highYield":["Autoimmune gastritis: anti-parietal cell and anti-intrinsic factor antibodies, achlorhydria, high gastrin, and B12 deficiency.","Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells and is the precursor of esophageal adenocarcinoma.","Stellate (Ito) cells drive liver fibrosis and cirrhosis by laying down collagen in the space of Disse.","Acetaminophen overdose causes centrilobular (zone 3) necrosis.","Peyer patches in the ileum are the entry point for some invasive bacteria (for example Salmonella Typhi) through M cells.","Paneth cells at crypt bases release defensins and lysozyme."],"pitfalls":["Meissner (submucosal) plexus controls secretion; Auerbach (myenteric) plexus controls motility.","Zone 1 is near the portal triad, and zone 3 is near the central vein; the lobule is drawn around the central vein while the acinus is drawn around the triad.","Autoimmune gastritis spares the antrum, so gastrin rises; H. pylori gastritis usually starts in the antrum.","Brunner glands sit in the submucosa; crypts of Lieberkuhn sit in the mucosa."],"dumps":[{"t":"Gut wall and gastric gland","p":"Draw a cross section of the stomach wall and enlarge one gastric gland beside it. Label the four layers, both nerve plexuses, and each gland cell with its product.","l":["Mucosa with epithelium, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosae","Submucosa with Meissner plexus","Muscularis externa with Auerbach plexus between layers","Serosa on the outside","Surface and mucous neck cells make mucus","Parietal cells make HCl and intrinsic factor","Chief cells at the base make pepsinogen","G cells in the antrum make gastrin"]},{"t":"Liver lobule and acinus","p":"Draw a hexagonal liver lobule. Label the central vein, portal triads, sinusoids, space of Disse, Kupffer cell, and stellate cell, then shade zones 1 to 3 of one acinus and write what injures each zone.","l":["Central vein in the center","Portal triad with portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct at the corners","Blood flows from triad to central vein","Bile flows from hepatocytes toward the triad","Space of Disse between hepatocytes and sinusoid lining","Kupffer cell in the sinusoid; stellate cell in the space of Disse","Zone 1 periportal, zone 3 around the central vein","Zone 3 hit by ischemia and acetaminophen"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder (section on patterns of hepatic injury)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Liver Histology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9CTyKLefn9E"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Hepatocytes and Portal Vein | Liver Histology | Dr Najeeb","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QEY_hCx-t4w"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Small intestine 1: Structure | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UgnF1w-H8vw"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hepatic lobule | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nJfB32MtqBA"}],"related":["GI-1","GI-6","GI-13-G2","GI-13-G7","GI-13-E1","GI-13-C"]},"GI-3":{"id":"GI-3","summary":"The gross anatomy of the stomach, duodenum, liver, biliary tree, colon, and salivary glands, and which organs lie behind the peritoneum. Exam questions use location to explain where a stone blocks, where an injury bleeds, and which nerve is at risk.","notes":[{"h":"Stomach and duodenum","points":["Stomach parts: cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus, with a lesser and a greater curvature.","Duodenum is about 25 cm long and wraps in a C around the head of the pancreas.","First (superior) part: the duodenal bulb, the most common site of duodenal ulcers.","Second (descending) part: the major duodenal papilla, where bile and pancreatic juice enter.","Third (horizontal) part: crossed in front by the superior mesenteric vessels, with the aorta behind.","Fourth (ascending) part: ends at the duodenojejunal flexure, held by the suspensory ligament (of Treitz)."]},{"h":"Intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal","points":["Intraperitoneal organs hang on a mesentery and are wrapped in visceral peritoneum: stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse and sigmoid colon, liver, spleen.","Retroperitoneal gut structures: duodenum parts 2 to 4, pancreas except the tail, ascending and descending colon, and part of the rectum.","Retroperitoneal organs are fixed to the back wall, so bleeding from them can collect without peritoneal signs.","The first part of the duodenum is mostly intraperitoneal; the pancreatic tail runs in the splenorenal ligament."]},{"h":"Liver and bile ducts","points":["Anatomic lobes: right and left (split by the falciform ligament), plus the caudate and quadrate lobes on the underside.","Bare area: the posterior surface touching the diaphragm, with no peritoneal cover.","Porta hepatis: the hilum where the portal vein and hepatic artery enter and the hepatic ducts leave.","Bile path: right and left hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, joined by the cystic duct to form the common bile duct.","The common bile duct runs behind the first part of the duodenum and through the pancreatic head, then joins the main pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater.","The sphincter of Oddi controls flow from the ampulla into the second part of the duodenum."]},{"h":"Colon and appendix","points":["Colon has three taeniae coli (bands of longitudinal muscle), haustra (sacculations), and omental appendices (fat tags); small intestine has none of these.","The taeniae converge at the base of the appendix, which surgeons follow to find it.","McBurney point: one third of the way from the right anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus, the surface mark for the appendix base."]},{"h":"Salivary glands","points":["Parotid: largest, in front of the ear over the masseter; its duct crosses the masseter, pierces the buccinator, and opens opposite the upper second molar.","Submandibular: under the mandible; its duct opens beside the lingual frenulum. Most salivary stones form here.","Sublingual: in the floor of the mouth, with many small ducts.","The facial nerve (CN VII) runs through the parotid and splits into its five branches there, so a parotid tumor or surgery can cause facial weakness."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Duodenum by part","cols":["Part","Peritoneal status","Key relation or feature"],"rows":[["First (superior)","Mostly intraperitoneal","Duodenal bulb; gastroduodenal artery behind it"],["Second (descending)","Retroperitoneal","Major papilla with the ampulla of Vater"],["Third (horizontal)","Retroperitoneal","Between the superior mesenteric vessels in front and the aorta behind"],["Fourth (ascending)","Retroperitoneal","Duodenojejunal flexure and suspensory ligament"]]},{"title":"Salivary glands","cols":["Gland","Secretion type","Duct opening","Parasympathetic supply"],"rows":[["Parotid","Serous","Opposite upper second molar","CN IX (via otic ganglion)"],["Submandibular","Mixed, mostly serous","Sublingual papilla beside the frenulum","CN VII (chorda tympani, submandibular ganglion)"],["Sublingual","Mixed, mostly mucous","Many small ducts in the floor of the mouth","CN VII (chorda tympani, submandibular ganglion)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SAD PUCKER","text":"Retroperitoneal structures: Suprarenal glands, Aorta and IVC, Duodenum (parts 2 to 4), Pancreas (except tail), Ureters, Colon (ascending and descending), Kidneys, Esophagus (lower part), Rectum (partly)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the bands","text":"Taeniae coli mean colon. Trace them down the cecum and they meet at the base of the appendix, wherever its tip has wandered."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One stone, two blocks","text":"A stone stuck at the ampulla blocks the common bile duct and the pancreatic duct at once, so jaundice and pancreatitis can arrive together."}],"highYield":["A stone in the common bile duct gives obstructive jaundice; a stone at the ampulla can also cause gallstone pancreatitis.","Pancreatic head cancer compresses the common bile duct and causes painless jaundice with a palpable gallbladder (Courvoisier sign).","Compression of the third part of the duodenum between the SMA and aorta (SMA syndrome) can follow rapid weight loss.","Facial nerve weakness with a parotid mass suggests a malignant tumor; the common pleomorphic adenoma rarely affects the nerve.","Blunt trauma can crush the retroperitoneal duodenum or pancreas against the spine.","The appendix base is constant at the taeniae meeting point; the tip is often retrocecal."],"pitfalls":["The major papilla (bile and main pancreatic duct) is in the second part of the duodenum; the minor papilla, slightly higher, drains the accessory duct.","Ascending and descending colon are retroperitoneal; transverse and sigmoid colon have mesenteries and are intraperitoneal.","The bare area of the liver touches the diaphragm directly, with no peritoneum between them.","The facial nerve passes through the parotid without supplying it; the parotid gets secretomotor fibers from CN IX."],"dumps":[{"t":"Biliary tree","p":"Draw the liver, gallbladder, duodenum, and pancreas. Trace bile from the hepatic ducts into the duodenum and label every duct, the ampulla, and the sphincter of Oddi.","l":["Right and left hepatic ducts join as the common hepatic duct","Cystic duct from the gallbladder","Common bile duct formed by common hepatic plus cystic duct","Common bile duct passes behind the first part of the duodenum","Runs through the head of the pancreas","Joins the main pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater","Opens at the major papilla in the second part of the duodenum","Sphincter of Oddi around the ampulla"]},{"t":"Peritoneal status table","p":"Build a two-column table: intraperitoneal on one side and retroperitoneal on the other. Place every gut organ, then circle the ones that change status partway along.","l":["Stomach, jejunum, ileum intraperitoneal","Transverse and sigmoid colon intraperitoneal","Liver and spleen intraperitoneal","Duodenum parts 2 to 4 retroperitoneal","Pancreas retroperitoneal except the tail","Ascending and descending colon retroperitoneal","Duodenum circled: first part mostly intraperitoneal","Rectum circled: upper and middle thirds covered in front, lower third below the peritoneum"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (sections on the abdominal viscera)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the parotid region)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Digestive System Anatomy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1-m3z_d531E"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Salivary glands - Anatomy and Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EYZPE5-7vZ8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Biliary tree | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jmfcACEGW-0"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Colon, rectum, and anus | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lzr9GGjh6YQ"}],"related":["GI-4","GI-5","GI-13-A1","GI-13-D2","GI-13-G1","GI-13-K6"]},"GI-4":{"id":"GI-4","summary":"Where the gut sits in the mouth, mediastinum, and abdominopelvic cavity: the path and narrowings of the esophagus, nerve supply of the tongue, peritoneal sacs and ligaments, the inguinal region, and the low spaces where fluid pools. Exam questions give a lesion or a collection and ask which structure or space is involved.","notes":[{"h":"Esophagus through the thorax","points":["Starts at the cricoid cartilage (C6) as a continuation of the pharynx.","Runs through the superior and then posterior mediastinum, behind the trachea and the left atrium.","Passes through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm at T10 with the vagal trunks.","Three narrowings: the cricopharyngeus (upper esophageal sphincter), the crossing of the aortic arch and left main bronchus, and the diaphragmatic hiatus.","Swallowed objects and pills tend to lodge at these narrowings, and an enlarged left atrium can press on the esophagus."]},{"h":"Tongue innervation","points":["Anterior two thirds: touch by CN V3 (lingual nerve); taste by CN VII (chorda tympani riding with the lingual nerve).","Posterior third: touch and taste by CN IX.","Motor: CN XII to all tongue muscles except palatoglossus, which is CN X.","A lower motor neuron CN XII lesion makes the protruded tongue deviate toward the injured side, with atrophy and fasciculations on that side.","Lingual nerve injury above where the chorda tympani joins it removes touch only; injury below the junction removes both touch and taste from the anterior two thirds."]},{"h":"Peritoneal sacs and ligaments","points":["Greater sac: most of the peritoneal cavity. Lesser sac (omental bursa): behind the stomach and lesser omentum.","The two sacs connect through the omental (epiploic) foramen, whose front edge is the hepatoduodenal ligament.","Lesser omentum: hepatogastric (gastrohepatic) plus hepatoduodenal ligaments. Greater omentum hangs from the greater curvature.","Hepatoduodenal ligament carries the portal triad: portal vein behind, proper hepatic artery in front on the left, common bile duct in front on the right.","Squeezing this ligament (Pringle maneuver) controls bleeding from the liver."]},{"h":"Inguinal region and hernias","points":["The inguinal canal runs from the deep ring (in transversalis fascia, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels) to the superficial ring (in external oblique aponeurosis).","Hesselbach triangle: inferior epigastric vessels laterally, rectus abdominis medially, inguinal ligament below.","Indirect hernia: enters the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels through a patent processus vaginalis and can reach the scrotum; most common type, often in young males.","Direct hernia: pushes through a weak Hesselbach triangle medial to the vessels; usually older men.","Femoral hernia: below the inguinal ligament through the femoral canal; more common in women and most likely to strangulate."]},{"h":"Where fluid collects","points":["Supine: the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) is the lowest part of the upper abdomen, so blood and pus pool there.","Upright: the rectouterine pouch (Douglas) in women or the rectovesical pouch in men is the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity.","Subphrenic spaces under the diaphragm collect abscesses after perforation or surgery, sometimes with shoulder-tip pain from phrenic nerve irritation.","The paracolic gutters let fluid travel between the pelvis and the upper abdomen."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Peritoneal ligaments and what they carry","cols":["Ligament","Connects","Contents"],"rows":[["Falciform","Liver to anterior abdominal wall","Ligamentum teres (old umbilical vein), paraumbilical veins"],["Hepatoduodenal","Liver to duodenum","Portal vein, proper hepatic artery, common bile duct"],["Gastrohepatic","Liver to lesser curvature","Left and right gastric vessels"],["Gastrocolic","Greater curvature to transverse colon","Gastroepiploic (gastro-omental) vessels"],["Gastrosplenic","Greater curvature to spleen","Short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels"],["Splenorenal","Spleen to posterior abdominal wall","Splenic artery and vein, tail of pancreas"]]},{"title":"Groin hernias","cols":["Type","Path","Relation to inferior epigastric vessels","Typical patient"],"rows":[["Indirect inguinal","Deep ring, canal, superficial ring, may reach scrotum","Lateral","Infants and young males; most common overall"],["Direct inguinal","Through Hesselbach triangle to superficial ring","Medial","Older men"],["Femoral","Femoral canal below the inguinal ligament","Below the inguinal ligament","Women; highest strangulation risk"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"I 8 10 EGGs AT 12","text":"Diaphragm openings: IVC at T8; Esophagus and vaGus at T10; Aorta, Thoracic duct, and azygos at T12."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"MDs don't LIe","text":"Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels is a Direct hernia; Lateral is Indirect."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lick your wounds","text":"A hypoglossal lesion pushes the tongue toward the hurt side, because the healthy genioglossus pushes forward alone."}],"highYield":["Gag reflex: afferent CN IX, efferent CN X.","An indirect inguinal hernia follows the path of testicular descent and is covered by all three layers of spermatic fascia.","In a supine trauma patient, free fluid on a FAST exam most often shows up first in the hepatorenal recess.","A ruptured appendix can track pus up the right paracolic gutter to the right subphrenic space.","The omental foramen lies behind the hepatoduodenal ligament and in front of the IVC.","Third molar extraction can injure the lingual nerve and cause loss of touch and taste on the front of the tongue."],"pitfalls":["Palatoglossus is the one tongue muscle supplied by CN X; every other tongue muscle is CN XII.","Taste and touch on the anterior two thirds travel in different nerves (VII and V3), while the posterior third uses CN IX for both.","Femoral hernias are more common in women than in men, yet indirect inguinal hernia is still the most common hernia in women.","The hepatorenal recess is lowest when lying flat; the rectouterine or rectovesical pouch is lowest when upright."],"dumps":[{"t":"Inguinal region","p":"Draw the inner surface of the lower anterior abdominal wall. Label Hesselbach triangle, the deep ring, the inferior epigastric vessels, the inguinal ligament, and the femoral canal, then draw arrows for direct, indirect, and femoral hernias.","l":["Inferior epigastric vessels as the lateral border of Hesselbach triangle","Rectus abdominis as the medial border","Inguinal ligament as the inferior border","Deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","Indirect arrow through the deep ring","Direct arrow through the triangle","Femoral arrow below the inguinal ligament, medial to the femoral vein"]},{"t":"Tongue nerve map","p":"Draw the tongue from above, divided into anterior two thirds and posterior third. Label the nerve for touch, taste, and movement in each region, and write the deficit from a CN XII lesion and a lingual nerve lesion.","l":["Anterior two thirds touch: CN V3 lingual nerve","Anterior two thirds taste: CN VII chorda tympani","Posterior third touch and taste: CN IX","Motor: CN XII to all but palatoglossus","Palatoglossus: CN X","CN XII lesion: deviation toward the lesion with atrophy","Lingual nerve lesion: loss of touch and taste on the anterior two thirds"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (sections on the inguinal region and the peritoneum)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax (section on the posterior mediastinum)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the oral region and tongue)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"The Oesophagus (Esophagus) - Clinical Anatomy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BZ0JBiPeek8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Esophagus | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wU9_daCwr_w"}],"related":["GI-3","GI-5","GI-13-F3","GI-13-I","GI-13-I2","GI-13-H2"]},"GI-5":{"id":"GI-5","summary":"The arteries that feed the gut, the portal vein that drains it, the shortcuts portal blood takes when the liver is blocked, and the two blood supplies of the anal canal and liver. Exam questions ask which vessel bleeds, which segment of colon dies in shock, and where varices form.","notes":[{"h":"Celiac trunk","points":["Leaves the aorta at about T12 and splits into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries.","Left gastric supplies the lesser curvature and lower esophagus.","Splenic runs along the top of the pancreas and gives short gastric, left gastroepiploic, and pancreatic branches.","Common hepatic gives the proper hepatic artery (to the liver and, through the cystic artery, the gallbladder) and the gastroduodenal artery.","The gastroduodenal artery runs behind the first part of the duodenum, so a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it and cause major bleeding."]},{"h":"Superior and inferior mesenteric arteries","points":["SMA (about L1) supplies the distal duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon through jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic branches.","IMA (about L3) supplies the distal transverse colon to the upper rectum through left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal branches.","The marginal artery runs along the colon and links SMA and IMA branches.","Watershed zones sit where two territories meet: the splenic flexure (SMA and IMA) and the rectosigmoid junction (sigmoid and superior rectal arteries).","Hypotension starves these watershed zones first and causes ischemic colitis: cramping pain followed by bloody diarrhea."]},{"h":"Portal venous system","points":["The superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein join behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein; the inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein.","The portal vein rises in the hepatoduodenal ligament to the porta hepatis.","Portal blood mixes with hepatic arterial blood in the sinusoids, drains to central veins, then hepatic veins, then the IVC.","Portal hypertension (often from cirrhosis) forces blood through portosystemic anastomoses back to the systemic veins."]},{"h":"Portosystemic anastomoses","points":["Esophagus: left gastric vein to esophageal veins draining to the azygos system. Result: esophageal varices that can bleed massively.","Umbilicus: paraumbilical veins to superficial veins of the abdominal wall. Result: caput medusae.","Rectum: superior rectal vein (portal) to middle and inferior rectal veins (systemic). Result: anorectal varices."]},{"h":"Anal canal above and below the pectinate line","points":["Above: columnar epithelium, superior rectal artery, portal drainage, internal iliac nodes, visceral (painless) nerves.","Below: stratified squamous epithelium, inferior rectal artery, systemic drainage, superficial inguinal nodes, somatic pudendal nerve (painful).","Internal hemorrhoids arise above the line and bleed without pain; external hemorrhoids arise below and hurt."]},{"h":"Two blood supplies to the liver","points":["About 75% of liver blood flow comes from the portal vein and about 25% from the hepatic artery; each supplies roughly half of the oxygen.","Because two sources feed the liver, a block in one rarely causes hepatic infarction.","Bile ducts depend on the hepatic artery alone, so hepatic artery thrombosis (for example after liver transplant) causes bile duct ischemia and strictures."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gut arteries","cols":["Artery","Aortic level","Main branches","Territory"],"rows":[["Celiac trunk","T12","Left gastric, splenic, common hepatic","Foregut: lower esophagus to the second part of the duodenum, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen"],["Superior mesenteric","L1","Inferior pancreaticoduodenal, jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic, middle colic","Midgut: distal duodenum to proximal two thirds of transverse colon"],["Inferior mesenteric","L3","Left colic, sigmoid, superior rectal","Hindgut: distal third of transverse colon to upper rectum"]]},{"title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Clinical sign"],"rows":[["Lower esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins to azygos","Esophageal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial and inferior epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices"]]},{"title":"Anal canal by the pectinate line","cols":["Feature","Above the line","Below the line"],"rows":[["Epithelium","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous"],["Artery","Superior rectal (from IMA)","Inferior rectal (from internal pudendal)"],["Venous drainage","Superior rectal to IMV to portal vein","Inferior rectal to internal iliac to IVC"],["Lymph drainage","Internal iliac nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes"],["Pain","Visceral, so internal hemorrhoids are painless","Somatic (pudendal), so external hemorrhoids hurt"],["Typical cancer","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Three exits for trapped portal blood","text":"Portal blood escapes at the top (esophagus), the middle (umbilicus), and the bottom (rectum) of the gut. Varices, caput medusae, and anorectal varices are the same problem at three sites."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Watershed = border towns","text":"Ischemia hits where two arterial territories meet and neither supplies much: splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Posterior bleeds, anterior perforates","text":"A posterior duodenal ulcer erodes the gastroduodenal artery and bleeds; an anterior ulcer perforates into the peritoneal cavity and gives free air."}],"highYield":["Massive upper GI bleeding from a posterior duodenal ulcer comes from the gastroduodenal artery.","A gastric ulcer on the lesser curvature can erode the left gastric artery.","Ischemic colitis after hypotension favors the splenic flexure; the rectum is spared by its dual supply.","Acute mesenteric ischemia (often an embolus to the SMA in atrial fibrillation) causes pain out of proportion to the exam.","Bleeding esophageal varices are a leading cause of death in cirrhosis.","Anal cancer below the pectinate line spreads to superficial inguinal nodes."],"pitfalls":["Anorectal varices come from portal hypertension; internal hemorrhoids are enlarged anal vascular cushions with other causes such as straining.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains into the splenic vein, while the SMV and splenic vein form the portal vein.","The portal vein brings most of the liver's blood flow, yet the hepatic artery is the only supply to the bile ducts.","The superior rectal artery comes from the IMA; the middle and inferior rectal arteries come from the internal iliac system."],"dumps":[{"t":"Arterial supply of the gut","p":"Draw the abdominal aorta with the celiac trunk, SMA, and IMA. Draw each main branch to its organ, then circle the two watershed zones of the colon.","l":["Celiac trunk at T12 with left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries","Gastroduodenal artery behind the first part of the duodenum","SMA at L1 with jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic arteries","IMA at L3 with left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal arteries","Marginal artery connecting SMA and IMA along the colon","Splenic flexure circled as a watershed zone","Rectosigmoid junction circled as a watershed zone"]},{"t":"Portal system and its escape routes","p":"Draw the portal vein forming behind the pancreas and entering the liver. Then draw the three portosystemic anastomoses and write the sign each one produces in portal hypertension.","l":["SMV and splenic vein join behind the pancreatic neck","IMV drains into the splenic vein","Portal vein in the hepatoduodenal ligament to the porta hepatis","Sinusoids to central veins to hepatic veins to IVC","Left gastric to esophageal veins to azygos: esophageal varices","Paraumbilical to abdominal wall veins: caput medusae","Superior rectal to middle and inferior rectal veins: anorectal varices"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (sections on the vasculature of the abdominal viscera and the hepatic portal system)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Liver Disease (section on portal hypertension)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on hepatic blood flow"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Liver Circulation & Portal Hypertension","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CqtQALXskDU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Liver | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rDjWrNRKfvg"}],"related":["GI-1","GI-4","GI-13-C5","GI-13-J1","GI-13-J2","GI-13-J3"]},"GI-6":{"id":"GI-6","summary":"How food is chewed, swallowed, moved, broken down, and absorbed, how acid and gut hormones are controlled, and how defecation and vomiting work. Exam questions give a meal, a drug, or a lesion and ask you to predict the hormone, secretion, or acid-base change that follows.","notes":[{"h":"Chewing and swallowing","points":["Masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids chew; all are supplied by CN V3.","Oral phase is voluntary: the tongue pushes the bolus into the pharynx.","Pharyngeal and esophageal phases are reflexes run by the swallowing center in the medulla: the soft palate seals the nose, the larynx rises, breathing pauses, and the upper esophageal sphincter opens.","Vagal inhibitory neurons release nitric oxide and VIP to relax the lower esophageal sphincter and the proximal stomach (receptive relaxation)."]},{"h":"Slow waves, peristalsis, and the MMC","points":["Interstitial cells of Cajal make slow waves that set the maximum contraction rate (about 3 per minute in the stomach, about 12 per minute in the duodenum).","Contraction happens only when spike potentials fire on top of a slow wave; ACh and stretch make this more likely.","Peristalsis needs the myenteric plexus: contraction above the bolus (ACh, substance P) and relaxation below it (VIP, nitric oxide).","Between meals, motilin from M cells drives the migrating motor complex about every 90 minutes to sweep the stomach and small bowel clean; eating stops it."]},{"h":"Gastric acid control","points":["Parietal cell stimulators: ACh (M3), gastrin (CCK-B), and histamine (H2); each makes the others stronger.","Gastrin also acts by making ECL cells release histamine.","Final step: the H+/K+ ATPase pumps H+ into the lumen in exchange for K+.","Somatostatin from D cells, released when antral pH falls, inhibits G cells and parietal cells.","Cephalic phase (about 30%): sight, smell, and chewing drive the vagus, which releases ACh and GRP (gastrin releasing peptide).","Gastric phase (about 60%): stretch and peptides in the stomach release gastrin.","Intestinal phase (about 10%): protein in the duodenum releases a little gastrin, but acid and fat in the duodenum mainly inhibit acid through secretin, GIP, and nerve reflexes."]},{"h":"Digestion of carbohydrate, protein, and fat","points":["Salivary and pancreatic amylase cut starch into maltose, maltotriose, and limit dextrins; brush border enzymes (lactase, sucrase, maltase) finish to monosaccharides.","Glucose and galactose enter by SGLT1 with sodium; fructose by GLUT5; all leave the cell by GLUT2.","Enteropeptidase on the duodenal brush border turns trypsinogen into trypsin, which activates the other pancreatic proteases.","Amino acids and di- and tripeptides are absorbed; peptides are split to amino acids inside the cell.","Bile salts emulsify fat; pancreatic lipase with colipase makes fatty acids and monoglycerides, which ride in micelles to the brush border.","Enterocytes rebuild triglycerides and pack them into chylomicrons that enter the lacteals (lymph)."]},{"h":"Defecation and the gastrocolic reflex","points":["Rectal stretch triggers reflex relaxation of the internal anal sphincter (smooth muscle, myenteric plexus) and the urge to defecate.","The external anal sphincter (skeletal muscle, pudendal nerve S2 to S4) stays closed by choice until the time is right.","Gastrocolic reflex: a filling stomach increases colonic mass movements, which is why the urge often comes after meals.","Pudendal nerve injury weakens the external sphincter and gives urge incontinence; internal sphincter damage gives passive leaking at rest."]},{"h":"Vomiting","points":["The chemoreceptor trigger zone sits in the area postrema, outside the blood-brain barrier, and senses drugs and toxins in blood (D2, 5-HT3, NK1 receptors).","It signals the vomiting center in the medulla, which also gets input from gut vagal afferents, the vestibular system, and the cortex.","Prolonged vomiting loses HCl, causing hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis.","Volume loss turns on aldosterone, which wastes K+ and H+ in the urine, causing hypokalemia and keeping the alkalosis going."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gut hormones","cols":["Hormone","Cell and site","Main stimulus","Main actions"],"rows":[["Gastrin","G cells, antrum and duodenum","Peptides and amino acids, stomach stretch, vagus (GRP)","Raises acid secretion, mucosal growth, gastric motility"],["Cholecystokinin (CCK)","I cells, duodenum and jejunum","Fatty acids and amino acids","Pancreatic enzyme release, gallbladder contraction, sphincter of Oddi relaxation, slower gastric emptying"],["Secretin","S cells, duodenum","Acid (and fatty acids) in the duodenum","Pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate, lower gastric acid"],["GIP","K cells, duodenum and jejunum","Glucose, fatty acids, amino acids","Insulin release, lower gastric acid"],["Motilin","M cells, duodenum and jejunum","Fasting","Migrating motor complex"],["Somatostatin","D cells, stomach and duodenum","Low pH","Inhibits gastrin, acid, and most gut hormones"]]},{"title":"Response to a meal that puts acid and fat into the duodenum","cols":["Signal","Change","Effect"],"rows":[["Secretin","Rises","Bicarbonate from pancreas neutralizes acid"],["CCK","Rises","Enzymes and bile arrive; stomach empties more slowly"],["GIP","Rises","Insulin release; less gastric acid"],["Gastric acid secretion","Falls","Less acid sent to the duodenum"],["Motilin and MMC","Fall","Fasting sweep is switched off"]]},{"title":"Digestion by nutrient","cols":["Nutrient","Key enzymes","Absorbed as","Into"],"rows":[["Carbohydrate","Salivary and pancreatic amylase, brush border disaccharidases","Glucose, galactose (SGLT1), fructose (GLUT5)","Portal blood"],["Protein","Pepsin, trypsin (activated by enteropeptidase), other pancreatic proteases, brush border peptidases","Amino acids, di- and tripeptides","Portal blood"],["Fat","Pancreatic lipase with colipase, aided by bile salts","Fatty acids and monoglycerides in micelles, packed into chylomicrons","Lacteals (lymph)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cell letters and their hormones","text":"G cells make Gastrin, S cells make Secretin, and M cells make Motilin, so those letters match. K cells make GIP and I cells make CCK, so those two need extra practice."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Three keys, one lock","text":"ACh, gastrin, and histamine are three keys that all open the same lock, the H+/K+ ATPase. Block the pump and all three lose their effect."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lose acid, lose volume, lose potassium","text":"Vomiting removes HCl (alkalosis, low chloride), shrinks volume (aldosterone), and aldosterone makes the kidney dump K+ and H+ (low potassium, worse alkalosis)."}],"highYield":["Achalasia: loss of inhibitory (NO and VIP) myenteric neurons, so the lower esophageal sphincter cannot relax and peristalsis is lost.","Erythromycin acts on motilin receptors and speeds gastric emptying.","Enteropeptidase deficiency leaves trypsinogen inactive and causes protein malabsorption.","Oral glucose releases more insulin than the same glucose given by vein because gut incretins (GIP and GLP-1) add to the insulin response.","Fat malabsorption causes steatorrhea and deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K.","Bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum; ileal resection causes bile salt loss and fat malabsorption.","In prolonged vomiting, urine chloride is low and the alkalosis corrects with saline."],"pitfalls":["Spike potentials riding on slow waves trigger contraction; a slow wave by itself only sets the rhythm.","Secretin brings bicarbonate; CCK brings enzymes and bile.","The internal anal sphincter relaxes by reflex; only the external sphincter is under voluntary control.","Most K+ lost in vomiting leaves through the kidney; gastric fluid itself holds little K+.","The intestinal phase is mostly inhibitory even though a small amount of gastrin comes from the duodenum."],"dumps":[{"t":"Parietal cell control","p":"Draw a parietal cell with its receptors and the H+/K+ ATPase. Add the G cell, ECL cell, D cell, and vagal fiber around it, and use arrows to show what turns acid on and off.","l":["M3 receptor for ACh from the vagus","CCK-B receptor for gastrin","H2 receptor for histamine from ECL cells","Gastrin also drives ECL histamine release","H+/K+ ATPase on the luminal side","Bicarbonate leaving the basolateral side (alkaline tide)","D cell somatostatin inhibits G cells and parietal cells","Vagal GRP stimulates G cells"]},{"t":"From meal to absorption","p":"Trace one meal with starch, protein, and fat from mouth to enterocyte. For each nutrient, write the enzymes, the hormones released, and how the product is absorbed.","l":["Salivary amylase starts starch digestion","Pancreatic amylase and brush border disaccharidases finish carbohydrate","SGLT1, GLUT5, and GLUT2 for monosaccharides","Enteropeptidase activates trypsinogen, trypsin activates the rest","Amino acids and small peptides absorbed","CCK releases enzymes and bile; secretin releases bicarbonate","Bile salts and lipase with colipase form micelles","Chylomicrons enter the lacteals"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Propulsion and Mixing of Food in the Alimentary Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Gastric Secretion: The Cephalic & Gastric Phase","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NIbclTo3duU"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | GI Motility of the Esophagus & Stomach","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DXPwCphPsmU"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Gastric Acid Physiology (Secretion, Ulcers, Acid Reflux and Treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rcb6I7gsl-Y"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Gastrointestinal Motility | GI Motility | Dr Najeeb🩺","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pWGhGx_mNXc"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Small intestine 2: Digestion | Gastrointestinal system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nLCNoT79_-o"}],"related":["GI-7","GI-8","GI-13-F1","GI-13-E","GI-13-E4","GI-13-K3"]},"GI-7":{"id":"GI-7","summary":"How the end products of digestion (monosaccharides, amino acids and small peptides, fatty acids and monoglycerides, vitamins, and minerals) cross the intestinal wall, where each is absorbed, and how bile acids recycle. The exam asks you to predict what goes wrong when a transporter is missing, a gut segment is diseased or removed, or a toxin turns on secretion.","notes":[{"h":"Carbohydrates","points":["Only monosaccharides are absorbed; brush border lactase, sucrase-isomaltase, and maltase finish digestion.","SGLT1 (apical) moves glucose and galactose in with 2 Na+ (secondary active transport).","GLUT5 (apical) lets fructose in by facilitated diffusion, no sodium needed.","GLUT2 (basolateral) carries all three monosaccharides out to portal blood.","Oral rehydration solution: glucose pulls Na+ in through SGLT1, and water follows osmotically.","SGLT1 keeps working in cholera because the toxin does not block it, so ORS still works."]},{"h":"Proteins","points":["Enteropeptidase (enterokinase) activates trypsinogen; trypsin activates the other pancreatic proteases.","Free amino acids enter enterocytes on several Na+-linked carriers grouped by amino acid class.","Dipeptides and tripeptides enter on PepT1 with H+, then cytosolic peptidases split them.","Hartnup disease: autosomal recessive defect of the neutral amino acid transporter in gut and proximal tubule.","Lost tryptophan means less niacin made, so pellagra-like rash, diarrhea, and ataxia appear; urine shows neutral aminoaciduria.","Dipeptide uptake on PepT1 is spared, which is why patients still absorb some tryptophan."]},{"h":"Fats","points":["Pancreatic lipase plus colipase splits triglyceride into 2-monoglyceride and free fatty acids.","Bile acids form micelles that carry these products to the brush border.","In the enterocyte smooth ER, they are re-esterified to triglyceride.","MTP loads triglyceride onto apoB-48 to make chylomicrons, which exit into lacteals and the thoracic duct.","Short and medium chain fatty acids skip chylomicrons and go straight to portal blood.","Abetalipoproteinemia (autosomal recessive MTP defect): no chylomicrons or VLDL, fat-filled enterocytes, steatorrhea.","Abetalipoproteinemia also gives acanthocytes and vitamin E deficiency with ataxia and retinal degeneration."]},{"h":"Where things are absorbed","points":["Duodenum: iron (Fe2+ on DMT1) and active calcium uptake.","Jejunum: folate and most sugars, amino acids, and fats.","Terminal ileum: vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor, and bile acids.","Duodenal disease (celiac disease) causes iron deficiency first.","Ileal resection or Crohn ileitis causes B12 deficiency and bile acid loss."]},{"h":"Enterohepatic circulation","points":["About 95% of bile acids are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum by the apical Na+-bile acid cotransporter (ASBT).","Bile acids return in portal blood and the liver re-secretes them; the pool cycles several times a day.","Short ileal loss (under about 100 cm): bile acids reach the colon and cause watery bile acid diarrhea.","Long ileal loss (over about 100 cm): the pool runs out and steatorrhea follows.","Fewer bile acids per unit of cholesterol in bile lets cholesterol crystallize, so cholesterol gallstones form."]},{"h":"Three kinds of diarrhea","points":["Secretory: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs, cAMP rises, PKA opens CFTR, and Cl- with water pours out.","Osmotic: unabsorbed solute (lactose in lactase deficiency) holds water in the lumen.","Inflammatory: mucosal damage with blood, leukocytes, and often fever (invasive bacteria, IBD).","Stool osmotic gap = 290 minus 2 x (stool Na+ plus K+), in mOsm/kg.","Gap below about 50 means secretory; gap above about 100 to 125 means osmotic.","Fasting stops osmotic diarrhea; secretory diarrhea continues."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Monosaccharide transporters","cols":["Transporter","Membrane","Carries","Mechanism"],"rows":[["SGLT1","Apical","Glucose, galactose","Secondary active, with 2 Na+"],["GLUT5","Apical","Fructose","Facilitated diffusion"],["GLUT2","Basolateral","Glucose, galactose, fructose","Facilitated diffusion to blood"]]},{"title":"Absorption sites and what happens when they fail","cols":["Nutrient","Main site","Deficiency after loss"],"rows":[["Iron","Duodenum","Microcytic anemia"],["Calcium","Duodenum","Low bone mineral, secondary hyperparathyroidism"],["Folate","Jejunum","Megaloblastic anemia with normal methylmalonic acid"],["Vitamin B12","Terminal ileum","Megaloblastic anemia with neuropathy"],["Bile acids","Terminal ileum","Bile acid diarrhea, then steatorrhea and fat-soluble vitamin loss"]]},{"title":"Diarrhea types","cols":["Type","Classic cause","Stool osmotic gap","With fasting","Stool findings"],"rows":[["Secretory","Cholera toxin, ETEC, VIPoma","Low (under about 50 mOsm/kg)","Continues","Large volume, watery"],["Osmotic","Lactase deficiency, laxatives","High (over about 100 to 125 mOsm/kg)","Stops","Acidic stool in lactose intolerance"],["Inflammatory","Shigella, Campylobacter, IBD","Variable","Often continues","Blood, leukocytes, mucus"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Iron Fist, Bro","text":"Iron in the duodenum, Folate in the jejunum, B12 in the ileum, going down the gut in order."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ride the sodium","text":"Glucose and sodium enter together on SGLT1, so giving both drags water in. That is the whole logic of oral rehydration."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fast them","text":"If the diarrhea stops when the patient stops eating, the cause is something in the food (osmotic). If it keeps going, the gut is secreting."}],"highYield":["Cholera toxin raises cAMP and opens CFTR; SGLT1 still works, so oral rehydration with glucose and sodium saves lives.","Hartnup disease: neutral amino acid transporter loss gives pellagra-like findings that respond to niacin.","Abetalipoproteinemia: steatorrhea, acanthocytes, very low cholesterol and triglyceride, and vitamin E deficiency.","Chylomicrons enter lacteals; medium chain triglycerides go to portal blood and help in fat malabsorption.","Terminal ileal resection: B12 deficiency, bile acid diarrhea or steatorrhea, and cholesterol gallstones.","Fat malabsorption also raises oxalate absorption, because free fatty acids bind calcium that would normally trap oxalate, leading to oxalate kidney stones."],"pitfalls":["GLUT5 brings fructose in; GLUT2 takes all three sugars out. Do not put GLUT2 on the apical side for exam purposes.","Folate is absorbed in the jejunum, B12 in the terminal ileum; ileal resection spares folate.","A small ileal resection causes watery bile acid diarrhea; a large one causes steatorrhea from a depleted pool.","Lactase deficiency stool is acidic with a high osmotic gap; cholera stool has a low gap."],"dumps":[{"t":"Enterocyte transport map","p":"Draw one enterocyte with its apical and basolateral sides. Show how glucose, galactose, fructose, amino acids, dipeptides, and fatty acids get in and out, labeling each transporter.","l":["SGLT1 apical with 2 Na+ for glucose and galactose","GLUT5 apical for fructose","GLUT2 basolateral exit for all monosaccharides","Na+/K+-ATPase basolateral keeps intracellular Na+ low","Na+-linked amino acid carriers and H+-linked PepT1 for di- and tripeptides","Micelles deliver monoglyceride and fatty acids; re-esterified in smooth ER","Chylomicrons with apoB-48 leave into the lacteal"]},{"t":"Ileal resection consequences","p":"A patient loses 120 cm of terminal ileum. Make a flowchart from the resection to every deficiency and complication that follows.","l":["Loss of intrinsic factor-B12 uptake leads to B12 deficiency","Megaloblastic anemia with high methylmalonic acid and neuropathy","Bile acids not reabsorbed, so the pool shrinks","Micelles fail, causing steatorrhea","Fat-soluble vitamin A, D, E, K deficiency","Bile supersaturated with cholesterol, so cholesterol gallstones","Calcium bound by fat frees oxalate, so oxalate kidney stones"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on malabsorption and diarrhea"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on digestion and absorption of dietary lipids"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Digestion & Absorption of Carbohydrates","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BXXxtEW5v3o"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Digestion & Absorption of Lipids","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ja-MWkFNvDg"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Diarrhea | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7ylgYbLCEA0"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Malabsorption | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kx1UgaqpYkc"}],"related":["GI-6","GI-8","GI-13-E4","GI-13-D2","GI-12"]},"GI-8":{"id":"GI-8","summary":"What the body must get from food (essential amino acids, essential fatty acids, vitamins, and minerals), what each vitamin does as a cofactor, and how the liver keeps energy flowing between meals. Questions give you a deficiency or toxicity picture and ask you to name the missing cofactor, the blocked enzyme, or the lab that tells two deficiencies apart.","notes":[{"h":"Essential nutrients and malnutrition","points":["Essential amino acids: phenylalanine, valine, threonine, tryptophan, isoleucine, methionine, histidine, leucine, lysine.","Essential fatty acids: linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3); lack causes scaly dermatitis and poor healing.","Kwashiorkor: protein lack with enough calories; low albumin gives edema and ascites.","Kwashiorkor fatty liver: too little apolipoprotein to export VLDL from hepatocytes.","Marasmus: total calorie lack; muscle wasting and lost subcutaneous fat, no edema."]},{"h":"Fat-soluble vitamins","points":["Fat malabsorption (pancreatic insufficiency, cholestasis, celiac disease) drops vitamins A, D, E, and K together.","A: night blindness, dry eyes with Bitot spots, follicular hyperkeratosis.","D: osteomalacia in adults, rickets in children; low calcium and phosphate, high PTH and alkaline phosphatase.","E: antioxidant loss gives hemolytic anemia, spinocerebellar ataxia, posterior column loss, and neuropathy.","K: no gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S; PT prolongs first because factor VII has the shortest half-life."]},{"h":"Vitamin B12 and folate","points":["Acid and pepsin free B12 from food protein; it binds haptocorrin (R binder) from saliva.","Pancreatic proteases digest haptocorrin in the duodenum, freeing B12 to bind intrinsic factor.","The IF-B12 complex is taken up in the terminal ileum; transcobalamin carries it in blood; liver stores last years.","B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.","B12 deficiency: homocysteine high and methylmalonic acid high, with subacute combined degeneration.","Folate deficiency: homocysteine high, methylmalonic acid normal, no neurologic findings."]},{"h":"Water-soluble vitamin cofactors","points":["Thiamine (B1) as TPP: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase.","Thiamine lack: Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia), dry beriberi (neuropathy), wet beriberi (high-output heart failure).","Niacin (B3) makes NAD+ and NADP+; lack gives pellagra: dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia.","Pyridoxine (B6) as PLP: ALA synthase (sideroblastic anemia), glutamate decarboxylase for GABA (seizures), transaminases.","Vitamin C: cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase; scurvy gives bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, poor wound healing."]},{"h":"Fed and fasting energy","points":["Fed: insulin drives glycogen synthesis, glycolysis, and fat synthesis in the liver.","Early fasting: glucagon drives hepatic glycogenolysis; liver glycogen is largely used up within about 24 hours.","Gluconeogenesis uses alanine (glucose-alanine cycle), lactate (Cori cycle), and glycerol from fat breakdown.","Even-chain fatty acids cannot make net glucose; acetyl-CoA cannot be turned back into pyruvate.","Prolonged fasting: liver makes acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate from fatty acid acetyl-CoA; the brain shifts to ketones over days.","The liver cannot use ketones because it lacks succinyl-CoA:acetoacetate CoA transferase (thiophorase)."]},{"h":"Minerals and toxicity","points":["Iron deficiency: microcytic hypochromic anemia, low ferritin, high TIBC.","Zinc deficiency: poor wound healing, dysgeusia, alopecia, hypogonadism, acrodermatitis around mouth and on hands and feet.","Copper deficiency: anemia, neutropenia, and a B12-like myelopathy with ataxia.","Excess zinc induces enterocyte metallothionein, which traps copper; gastric bypass also causes copper deficiency.","Vitamin A toxicity: raised intracranial pressure (pseudotumor cerebri), liver injury, dry skin, hair loss; it is a strong teratogen.","Vitamin D toxicity: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, kidney stones, with suppressed PTH."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Vitamin deficiencies by cofactor role","cols":["Vitamin","Active form or role","Deficiency findings"],"rows":[["A (retinol)","Retinal in rhodopsin; epithelial differentiation","Night blindness, Bitot spots, xerophthalmia"],["D","Calcitriol raises gut calcium and phosphate uptake","Rickets, osteomalacia"],["E","Antioxidant in cell membranes","Hemolytic anemia, ataxia, neuropathy"],["K","Gamma-carboxylation of II, VII, IX, X, C, S","Bleeding, prolonged PT"],["B1 (thiamine)","TPP for dehydrogenases and transketolase","Wernicke encephalopathy, beriberi"],["B3 (niacin)","NAD+ and NADP+","Pellagra"],["B6 (pyridoxine)","PLP for ALA synthase, transaminases, GABA synthesis","Sideroblastic anemia, seizures, neuropathy"],["B9 (folate)","THF carries one-carbon units","Megaloblastic anemia, high homocysteine only"],["B12 (cobalamin)","Methionine synthase, methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","Megaloblastic anemia, high homocysteine and MMA, neuropathy"],["C (ascorbate)","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase","Scurvy"]]},{"title":"Kwashiorkor and marasmus","cols":["Feature","Kwashiorkor","Marasmus"],"rows":[["Main lack","Protein","Total calories"],["Edema","Yes, from low albumin","No"],["Liver","Fatty, enlarged","Usually normal"],["Muscle and fat","Some muscle loss, fat partly kept","Severe wasting of both"]]},{"title":"Fuel sources across a fast","cols":["Phase","Main hormone","Main glucose or fuel source"],"rows":[["Fed","Insulin","Dietary glucose; liver stores glycogen and makes fat"],["Early fast (hours)","Glucagon","Hepatic glycogenolysis"],["Fast past about a day","Glucagon, cortisol","Gluconeogenesis from alanine, lactate, glycerol"],["Prolonged fast (days to weeks)","Glucagon","Ketone bodies for brain; less muscle protein breakdown"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PVT TIM HaLL","text":"Phenylalanine, Valine, Threonine, Tryptophan, Isoleucine, Methionine, Histidine, Leucine, Lysine: the essential amino acids."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Kwashiorkor is a protein-poor MEAL","text":"Malnutrition, Edema, Anemia, Liver (fatty)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 3 Ds of pellagra","text":"Dermatitis (sun-exposed skin), Diarrhea, Dementia; untreated it can lead to death."},{"kind":"trick","label":"B12 needs two jobs done","text":"B12 runs two enzymes, so both products back up (homocysteine and MMA). Folate runs only the methionine synthase step, so only homocysteine rises."}],"highYield":["Give thiamine before glucose in a malnourished patient with alcohol use disorder to avoid triggering Wernicke encephalopathy.","Isoniazid causes B6 deficiency, which can also cause pellagra because B6 is needed to make niacin from tryptophan.","Carcinoid syndrome and Hartnup disease both cause niacin deficiency by diverting or losing tryptophan.","Copper deficiency after gastric bypass or zinc supplements can look exactly like B12 subacute combined degeneration.","Vitamin K deficiency in fat malabsorption corrects with parenteral vitamin K; in liver failure it does not.","Isotretinoin (a vitamin A analog) is teratogenic; vitamin A excess can cause pseudotumor cerebri with papilledema."],"pitfalls":["High homocysteine alone does not tell B12 from folate deficiency; methylmalonic acid does.","Kwashiorkor has edema; marasmus does not.","Vitamin E deficiency neurologic signs mimic B12 deficiency; look for fat malabsorption and hemolysis.","Ketones are made in the liver but used by brain, heart, and muscle; red cells and liver cannot use them."],"dumps":[{"t":"Vitamin B12 path","p":"Draw the path of vitamin B12 from a bite of meat to a cell in bone marrow. Label each binding protein and the organ that makes it.","l":["Acid and pepsin in the stomach release B12 from food protein","Haptocorrin (R binder) from saliva binds B12 in the stomach","Intrinsic factor comes from gastric parietal cells","Pancreatic proteases cleave haptocorrin in the duodenum","B12 transfers to intrinsic factor","IF-B12 taken up in the terminal ileum","Transcobalamin carries B12 in blood; liver stores last years","Name one cause of failure at each step (pernicious anemia, pancreatic insufficiency, ileal resection)"]},{"t":"Fasting timeline","p":"Draw a timeline from a meal through 3 weeks of fasting. Under each phase, write the main hormone, the main source of blood glucose, and the main fuel used by the brain.","l":["Fed: insulin, dietary glucose, glycogen and fat storage","Hours after the meal: glucagon and hepatic glycogenolysis","Liver glycogen largely gone within about 24 hours","Gluconeogenesis from alanine, lactate, and glycerol","Even-chain fatty acids cannot give net glucose","Liver makes ketones from fatty acids but cannot use them (no thiophorase)","Brain adapts to ketones over days, sparing muscle protein"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on vitamins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on the feed/fast cycle"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Vitamin B12 Digestion and Absorption & Pernicious Anaemia","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aww5NiahRb8"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Metabolism | Gluconeogenesis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YpV96faQOZc"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Metabolism | Ketone Metabolism","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BlR9DkUAUkI"}],"related":["GI-7","GI-13-E","HE-9-A1","HE-9-C5"]},"GI-9":{"id":"GI-9","summary":"The liver's work beyond digestion: clearing bilirubin, transforming drugs and toxins, making albumin and clotting factors, and turning ammonia into urea, along with how saliva protects teeth and how the gallbladder concentrates bile. Questions hand you a jaundice lab panel, an overdose, or a failing liver and ask which step broke.","notes":[{"h":"Bilirubin pathway","points":["Macrophages (mainly spleen) break down heme: heme oxygenase makes biliverdin, CO, and iron.","Biliverdin reductase makes unconjugated bilirubin, which is fat-soluble and rides on albumin.","Hepatocytes take it up and UGT1A1 conjugates it with glucuronic acid, making it water-soluble.","MRP2 pumps conjugated bilirubin into the canaliculus; this ATP-dependent step is rate-limiting.","Gut bacteria turn it into urobilinogen; most becomes stercobilin (brown stool).","Some urobilinogen is reabsorbed; a little is excreted in urine, where it oxidizes to yellow urobilin."]},{"h":"Reading jaundice labs","points":["Only conjugated bilirubin can appear in urine; unconjugated is bound to albumin.","Hemolysis: unconjugated rises, no urine bilirubin, urine urobilinogen goes up.","Obstruction: conjugated rises, dark urine bilirubin, pale stool, urine urobilinogen low or absent.","Hepatocellular injury: both fractions rise, urine bilirubin present, urobilinogen normal or high.","Gilbert syndrome (mild UGT1A1 reduction) and Crigler-Najjar (absent or very low UGT1A1) raise unconjugated bilirubin.","Dubin-Johnson (MRP2 defect, black liver) and Rotor syndrome raise conjugated bilirubin."]},{"h":"Drug and toxin metabolism","points":["Phase I: cytochrome P450 oxidation, reduction, or hydrolysis in smooth ER; products can be more reactive.","Phase II: conjugation (glucuronidation, sulfation, glutathione, acetylation) makes a polar product to excrete.","Acetaminophen is mostly glucuronidated and sulfated; a small fraction goes through CYP2E1 to NAPQI.","Glutathione normally neutralizes NAPQI; in overdose glutathione runs out and NAPQI binds cell proteins.","Injury is centrilobular (zone 3) because zone 3 has the most CYP2E1 and the least oxygen.","N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione; chronic alcohol use raises risk by inducing CYP2E1 and lowering glutathione.","Carbon tetrachloride: CYP450 makes the trichloromethyl radical, which causes lipid peroxidation, fatty change, and centrilobular necrosis."]},{"h":"Synthesis and ammonia","points":["Liver makes albumin, most clotting factors, and proteins C and S.","PT prolongs early in liver failure because factor VII has a half-life of about 6 hours.","Albumin falls more slowly (half-life about 3 weeks) and causes edema and ascites.","The urea cycle in hepatocytes turns ammonia into urea; CPS I is the first committed step.","Liver failure or portosystemic shunting lets ammonia rise, causing hepatic encephalopathy with asterixis.","Factor VIII is made by endothelium, so it stays normal in liver disease."]},{"h":"Saliva and gallbladder","points":["Saliva bicarbonate buffers plaque acid; calcium and phosphate remineralize early enamel lesions.","Saliva defenses: secretory IgA, lysozyme (cleaves peptidoglycan), lactoferrin (binds iron).","Low salivary flow (Sjogren syndrome, anticholinergics, head and neck radiation) leads to rampant caries.","The gallbladder absorbs Na+ actively; Cl- and water follow, concentrating bile about 5-fold, up to about 20-fold.","CCK from duodenal I cells (after fat and protein) contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi.","Stasis (fasting, TPN, pregnancy, rapid weight loss) plus concentrated bile lets cholesterol crystals nucleate into stones."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Jaundice by mechanism","cols":["Type","Serum bilirubin","Urine bilirubin","Urine urobilinogen","Stool"],"rows":[["Hemolytic (prehepatic)","Unconjugated high","Absent","Increased","Normal or dark"],["Hepatocellular","Both high","Present","Normal or increased","Normal or pale"],["Obstructive (posthepatic)","Conjugated high","Present (dark urine)","Decreased or absent","Pale, clay-colored"]]},{"title":"Inherited hyperbilirubinemias","cols":["Syndrome","Defect","Bilirubin type","Key feature"],"rows":[["Gilbert","Mildly reduced UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Mild jaundice with fasting, stress, illness"],["Crigler-Najjar type I","Absent UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Kernicterus in infancy"],["Dubin-Johnson","MRP2 defect","Conjugated","Black liver"],["Rotor","Impaired hepatic uptake and storage","Conjugated","Normal liver color, milder"]]},{"title":"Liver failure findings and their cause","cols":["Finding","Lost function"],"rows":[["Prolonged PT, bleeding","Clotting factor synthesis"],["Edema, ascites","Albumin synthesis"],["Confusion, asterixis","Urea cycle disposal of ammonia"],["Hypoglycemia","Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis"],["Jaundice","Bilirubin conjugation and excretion"],["Gynecomastia, spider angiomas","Estrogen breakdown"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Only the conjugated pees","text":"Unconjugated bilirubin is stuck to albumin and cannot be filtered. If there is bilirubin in the urine, the conjugated fraction is up."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the urobilinogen","text":"More heme in means more urobilinogen out (hemolysis). A blocked duct means nothing reaches the gut, so urobilinogen vanishes and the stool turns pale."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Zone 3 takes the hit","text":"Zone 3 sits by the central vein, gets the least oxygen, and holds the most CYP2E1. Toxins activated by P450 (acetaminophen, CCl4) and ischemia all injure zone 3 first."}],"highYield":["Acetaminophen overdose: glutathione depletion, NAPQI, centrilobular necrosis; treat with N-acetylcysteine.","CCl4 is the classic model of free radical injury through lipid peroxidation.","Dark urine and pale stools mean obstruction; jaundice with no urine bilirubin means hemolysis or Gilbert.","PT is the fastest lab marker of hepatic synthetic failure; vitamin K does not correct it in liver failure.","Hyperammonemia from liver failure causes hepatic encephalopathy and asterixis.","Xerostomia from Sjogren syndrome or radiation causes caries at the gum line and root surfaces."],"pitfalls":["Heme oxygenase makes biliverdin; biliverdin reductase makes bilirubin. Do not reverse them.","Phase II conjugation usually detoxifies; phase I can create the toxic metabolite.","Albumin is a poor marker of acute liver failure because of its long half-life; use PT.","Gilbert syndrome is unconjugated; Dubin-Johnson and Rotor are conjugated."],"dumps":[{"t":"Bilirubin from red cell to stool","p":"Draw the path of bilirubin from an old red cell in the spleen to the stool and urine. Label every enzyme, carrier, and site.","l":["Heme oxygenase makes biliverdin, CO, and iron","Biliverdin reductase makes unconjugated bilirubin","Albumin carries unconjugated bilirubin in blood","UGT1A1 conjugates with glucuronic acid in the hepatocyte","MRP2 excretes into bile (rate-limiting)","Gut bacteria make urobilinogen; most becomes stercobilin","Some urobilinogen reabsorbed, a little excreted in urine as urobilin"]},{"t":"Acetaminophen overdose","p":"Draw a hepatocyte and show the three routes acetaminophen takes at a normal dose, then redraw it after an overdose. Mark where N-acetylcysteine acts.","l":["Most of a normal dose is glucuronidated and sulfated (phase II)","A small fraction goes through CYP2E1 to NAPQI (phase I)","Glutathione conjugates NAPQI to a safe product","In overdose, phase II routes saturate and more NAPQI forms","Glutathione runs out; NAPQI binds proteins and kills cells","Zone 3 centrilobular necrosis","N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on chemical (toxic) injury in cell injury"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on heme degradation and bilirubin"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Bilirubin Metabolism - unconjugated and conjugated bilirubin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_-JFqnBYmzc"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Liver Metabolism, Protein Synthesis & Storage","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wRkanE05ICo"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Bile Synthesis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-L4i_2EHWQU"}],"related":["GI-13-C1","GI-13-C2","GI-13-C3","GI-13-D2","GI-13-A"]},"GI-10":{"id":"GI-10","summary":"Single-gene diseases that damage the liver, pancreas, and colon: iron and copper overload, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, cystic fibrosis, and the inherited colon cancer and polyposis syndromes. Questions give you the gene or inheritance pattern and ask for the mechanism and the organs that pay for it, or give you the findings and ask for the gene.","notes":[{"h":"Hereditary hemochromatosis","points":["Autosomal recessive; most often homozygous HFE C282Y (chromosome 6).","Faulty iron sensing keeps hepcidin inappropriately low.","Low hepcidin leaves ferroportin active, so the gut absorbs too much iron for years.","Labs: high transferrin saturation and ferritin; liver iron stains with Prussian blue.","Iron free radicals cause cirrhosis, diabetes, skin bronzing, cardiomyopathy, arthropathy, and hypogonadism.","Cirrhosis carries a high risk of hepatocellular carcinoma; women present later because of menstrual iron loss."]},{"h":"Wilson disease","points":["Autosomal recessive ATP7B mutation (chromosome 13), a hepatocyte copper-transporting ATPase.","Copper is not loaded onto ceruloplasmin and not excreted into bile, so it builds up in the liver.","Spillover copper reaches brain (basal ganglia), cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings), and kidney.","Labs: low serum ceruloplasmin, high urinary copper, high liver copper; may have Coombs-negative hemolysis.","Presents in children and young adults with hepatitis, cirrhosis, or acute liver failure, plus tremor, parkinsonism, or psychiatric change."]},{"h":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","points":["SERPINA1 gene, codominant alleles; PiMM is normal, PiZZ is severe.","The Z protein misfolds and polymerizes in the hepatocyte ER instead of being secreted.","Retained protein shows as PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules; it causes neonatal hepatitis, cirrhosis, and HCC.","Low serum levels leave neutrophil elastase unopposed, causing panacinar emphysema at the lung bases, worse with smoking."]},{"h":"Colorectal cancer pathways","points":["Chromosomal instability (adenoma to carcinoma): APC loss, then KRAS activation, then TP53 loss.","APC loss frees beta-catenin, which turns on Wnt target genes and starts the adenoma.","Microsatellite instability: mismatch repair genes (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) fail, so repeats mutate.","Lynch syndrome is a germline mismatch repair mutation, autosomal dominant, with few polyps.","Lynch cancers tend to be right-sided and early; endometrial, ovarian, and gastric cancers also rise.","Sporadic MSI cancers usually come from MLH1 promoter hypermethylation."]},{"h":"Polyposis syndromes","points":["FAP: autosomal dominant APC (chromosome 5q), hundreds to thousands of adenomas, near-certain cancer without colectomy.","Gardner syndrome: FAP plus osteomas, desmoid tumors, epidermoid cysts, and extra teeth.","Turcot syndrome: polyposis plus a brain tumor (medulloblastoma or glioma).","MUTYH-associated polyposis: autosomal recessive base excision repair defect, tens to hundreds of adenomas.","Peutz-Jeghers: autosomal dominant STK11, hamartomatous polyps, dark spots on lips and buccal mucosa, intussusception, raised cancer risk."]},{"h":"Cystic fibrosis in the gut","points":["Autosomal recessive CFTR (chromosome 7); the common F508del protein misfolds and is degraded.","CFTR is a Cl- channel opened by cAMP and protein kinase A; without it, Cl- and bicarbonate secretion fall and secretions thicken.","Newborn: meconium ileus from thick meconium blocking the distal ileum.","Pancreatic ducts plug, acini are destroyed, and exocrine insufficiency causes steatorrhea and A, D, E, K deficiency.","Inspissated bile causes focal biliary cirrhosis; sweat chloride is high because the sweat duct cannot reabsorb Cl-."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Inherited liver diseases","cols":["Disease","Gene and inheritance","Mechanism","Key findings"],"rows":[["Hemochromatosis","HFE C282Y, autosomal recessive","Low hepcidin, excess iron absorption","Cirrhosis, diabetes, bronze skin, cardiomyopathy, high transferrin saturation"],["Wilson disease","ATP7B, autosomal recessive","No biliary copper excretion or ceruloplasmin loading","Low ceruloplasmin, Kayser-Fleischer rings, basal ganglia signs, hepatitis"],["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","SERPINA1 PiZZ, codominant","Misfolded protein trapped in ER; low serum level","PAS-positive globules, cirrhosis, panacinar emphysema"]]},{"title":"Hereditary colorectal syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Gene","Inheritance","Polyps","Extra features"],"rows":[["FAP","APC","Autosomal dominant","Hundreds to thousands of adenomas","Cancer by about age 40 without colectomy"],["Gardner","APC","Autosomal dominant","Many adenomas","Osteomas, desmoids, epidermoid cysts, dental anomalies"],["Turcot","APC or mismatch repair","Depends on the gene","Adenomas","Medulloblastoma or glioma"],["MUTYH-associated polyposis","MUTYH","Autosomal recessive","Tens to hundreds of adenomas","Base excision repair defect"],["Lynch","MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2","Autosomal dominant","Few","Right-sided cancer, endometrial and ovarian cancer"],["Peutz-Jeghers","STK11","Autosomal dominant","Hamartomas, mostly small bowel","Mucocutaneous pigmentation, intussusception"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Iron is absorbed, copper is excreted","text":"Hemochromatosis is a problem of taking in too much (gut absorption, hepcidin). Wilson disease is a problem of getting rid of too little (bile excretion, ATP7B)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"AKP in order","text":"Walk the adenoma to carcinoma sequence as A, K, P: APC first (polyp starts), KRAS next (polyp grows), p53 last (cancer)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Amsterdam 3-2-1","text":"Suspect Lynch syndrome when 3 relatives have Lynch cancers, across 2 generations, with 1 diagnosed before age 50."}],"highYield":["Hemochromatosis: high transferrin saturation is the best first screen; phlebotomy is the treatment.","Wilson disease: low ceruloplasmin and Kayser-Fleischer rings in a young patient with liver and movement problems.","PiZZ alpha-1 antitrypsin: liver disease from retained protein, lung disease from absent protein.","Lynch tumors show microsatellite instability and loss of mismatch repair protein staining.","Peutz-Jeghers: freckle-like spots on the lips with small bowel hamartomas and intussusception.","CF: meconium ileus at birth, then pancreatic insufficiency with fat-soluble vitamin deficiency."],"pitfalls":["Ceruloplasmin is low in Wilson disease even though total body copper is high.","FAP is autosomal dominant APC; MUTYH polyposis looks similar but is autosomal recessive.","Lynch syndrome has few polyps; FAP has hundreds.","Peutz-Jeghers polyps are hamartomas, yet cancer risk still rises in the gut and other organs."],"dumps":[{"t":"Iron and copper overload side by side","p":"Draw a hepatocyte for hemochromatosis and a second one for Wilson disease. Show where the metal comes in, where it should leave, and where the defect is. List the organs damaged by each.","l":["Hemochromatosis: HFE C282Y, autosomal recessive","Low hepcidin keeps ferroportin active, so gut absorbs excess iron","Iron damages liver, pancreas, heart, joints, skin, pituitary","Wilson: ATP7B, autosomal recessive","Copper not put into ceruloplasmin and not excreted into bile","Copper spills to brain basal ganglia, cornea, kidney","Labs: high transferrin saturation versus low ceruloplasmin"]},{"t":"Two roads to colon cancer","p":"Draw two parallel flowcharts, one for the adenoma to carcinoma pathway and one for the mismatch repair pathway. Name the genes at each step and the inherited syndrome tied to each road.","l":["APC loss raises beta-catenin and starts the adenoma","KRAS activation drives growth","TP53 loss allows invasion and carcinoma","FAP is germline APC","Mismatch repair genes MLH1, MSH2 lost","Microsatellite instability in repeat sequences","Lynch syndrome is germline mismatch repair, autosomal dominant","Sporadic MSI comes from MLH1 promoter methylation"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on colonic polyps and neoplastic disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Haemochromatosis (Iron Overload) - iron physiology, causes and pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hbs_GaWX5PI"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Colorectal Cancer | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HjIQy8FycKA"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Liver Cirrhosis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f-Fxlsb2dtQ"}],"related":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-K4","GI-13-K7","GI-13-B","GI-8"]},"GI-11":{"id":"GI-11","summary":"How the gut immune system tolerates food and flora but still fights pathogens, and how that balance fails in celiac disease, Crohn disease, autoimmune liver and stomach disease, MALT lymphoma, and hepatitis B. Questions ask which cell, antibody, HLA type, or immune mechanism explains the damage, and how to read hepatitis B serology.","notes":[{"h":"Mucosal immunity","points":["M cells over Peyer patches (ileum) sample luminal antigen and hand it to dendritic cells.","TGF-beta in the gut drives B cells to switch to IgA; lamina propria plasma cells make dimeric IgA with a J chain.","The polymeric Ig receptor carries dimeric IgA across the epithelium; its cleaved piece stays on as secretory component.","Secretory IgA blocks microbe attachment without activating much complement or inflammation.","Oral tolerance: regulatory T cells (IL-10, TGF-beta) and anergy keep reactions to food and flora quiet.","Selective IgA deficiency: most common primary immunodeficiency; often silent, but recurrent sinopulmonary and GI infections (Giardia) occur."]},{"h":"Celiac disease","points":["Gliadin (from gluten) resists digestion; tissue transglutaminase deamidates its glutamines to glutamate.","Deamidated gliadin binds HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 tightly and activates CD4 T cells, which release IFN-gamma.","Epithelial IL-15 activates intraepithelial CD8 T cells that kill enterocytes.","Biopsy: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytes, worst in the duodenum.","Serology: IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies; check total IgA because IgA deficiency gives false negatives.","Associations: dermatitis herpetiformis, type 1 diabetes, and enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma."]},{"h":"Crohn disease","points":["NOD2 is a cytosolic sensor of bacterial peptidoglycan (muramyl dipeptide) in Paneth cells and monocytes.","NOD2 variants impair handling of commensal bacteria, which then breach the mucosa.","Th1 (IFN-gamma) and Th17 (IL-17, sustained by IL-23) responses drive the inflammation.","Result: transmural inflammation, noncaseating granulomas, skip lesions, fistulas, and strictures."]},{"h":"Autoimmune gut and liver disease","points":["Autoimmune gastritis: antibodies to parietal cells (H+/K+-ATPase) and intrinsic factor; T cells destroy the body and fundus.","Autoimmune gastritis gives achlorhydria, high gastrin, and pernicious anemia.","Autoimmune hepatitis type 1: ANA and anti-smooth muscle antibodies; type 2 (often children): anti-LKM1.","Primary biliary cholangitis: antimitochondrial antibodies, middle-aged women, destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts."]},{"h":"MALT lymphoma","points":["Chronic H. pylori gastritis builds lymphoid tissue in a stomach that normally has little.","H. pylori-specific T cells keep stimulating B cells until a monoclonal marginal zone lymphoma emerges.","Early tumors still depend on that T cell help, so eradicating H. pylori can make them regress.","Tumors with t(11;18) usually no longer respond to eradication."]},{"h":"Hepatitis B immunity","points":["HBV itself barely harms hepatocytes; CD8 cytotoxic T cells kill infected cells displaying viral peptides on MHC class I.","A strong T cell response causes acute hepatitis and clearance; a weak one allows chronic infection.","Infection at birth becomes chronic about 90% of the time; adult infection only about 5%.","HBsAg-antibody immune complexes cause polyarteritis nodosa and membranous nephropathy (type III hypersensitivity).","The vaccine is recombinant HBsAg only, so a vaccinated person makes anti-HBs but never anti-HBc."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hepatitis B serology","cols":["State","HBsAg","Anti-HBc","Anti-HBs"],"rows":[["Vaccinated","Negative","Negative","Positive"],["Resolved infection","Negative","Positive (IgG)","Positive"],["Acute infection","Positive","Positive (IgM)","Negative"],["Window period","Negative","Positive (IgM)","Negative"],["Chronic infection","Positive over 6 months","Positive (IgG)","Negative"]]},{"title":"Autoantibodies in GI disease","cols":["Disease","Autoantibody","Target or note"],"rows":[["Celiac disease","IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase, anti-endomysial","Tissue transglutaminase"],["Autoimmune gastritis","Anti-parietal cell, anti-intrinsic factor","H+/K+-ATPase; intrinsic factor"],["Autoimmune hepatitis type 1","ANA, anti-smooth muscle","Smooth muscle antibody targets actin"],["Autoimmune hepatitis type 2","Anti-LKM1","CYP2D6"],["Primary biliary cholangitis","Antimitochondrial","Pyruvate dehydrogenase E2 subunit"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Core means caught","text":"Anti-HBc only appears after real infection because the vaccine has no core protein. Anti-HBs alone means vaccine; anti-HBs plus anti-HBc means recovered from infection."},{"kind":"trick","label":"The enzyme makes the antigen and the target","text":"In celiac disease tissue transglutaminase does both jobs: it modifies gliadin so DQ2 or DQ8 can present it, and it becomes the antibody target used in testing."},{"kind":"trick","label":"MALT lymphoma still listens","text":"Early gastric MALT lymphoma still needs H. pylori-driven T cell help to grow. Take away the bug and the help disappears, so antibiotics can cure an early lymphoma."}],"highYield":["Celiac disease is tied to HLA-DQ2 and DQ8; screen with IgA anti-tTG and check total IgA.","Selective IgA deficiency can cause anaphylaxis to blood products that contain IgA.","NOD2 variants are the classic genetic link to Crohn disease.","Antimitochondrial antibody points to primary biliary cholangitis; anti-LKM1 points to type 2 autoimmune hepatitis.","Hepatitis B liver damage is mostly immune-mediated by CD8 T cells.","HBsAg immune complexes cause polyarteritis nodosa, which spares the lungs."],"pitfalls":["A negative anti-tTG IgA does not rule out celiac disease in a patient with IgA deficiency.","IgM anti-HBc is the only marker positive in the window period; HBsAg and anti-HBs are both negative.","Autoimmune gastritis hits the body and fundus; H. pylori gastritis usually starts in the antrum.","Crohn granulomas are noncaseating; caseating granulomas suggest tuberculosis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Secretory IgA on the move","p":"Draw the intestinal lining over a Peyer patch. Show how an antigen is sampled and how secretory IgA is made and delivered to the lumen. Label each cell and molecule.","l":["M cell transports antigen across the epithelium","Dendritic cell presents antigen to T cells in the Peyer patch","TGF-beta drives B cell class switching to IgA","Plasma cell in lamina propria makes dimeric IgA joined by J chain","Polymeric Ig receptor binds IgA on the basolateral side and carries it across","Cleavage leaves secretory component attached in the lumen","Secretory IgA blocks microbial attachment"]},{"t":"Celiac disease mechanism","p":"Draw a flowchart from eating bread to villous atrophy in celiac disease. Mark where the HLA molecule, the enzyme, and each T cell type act, and where the test antibodies come from.","l":["Gliadin resists digestion","Tissue transglutaminase deamidates gliadin","HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 presents deamidated gliadin","CD4 T cells release IFN-gamma","IL-15 activates intraepithelial CD8 T cells that kill enterocytes","Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytes","IgA anti-tTG and anti-endomysial antibodies appear","Gluten-free diet reverses the lesion"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on mucosal immunity and secretory IgA"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on immunological tolerance and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on the gastrointestinal tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Coeliac (Celiac) Disease - Overview (signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, diagnosis, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hxd0msD954I"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Pathophysiology and Complications","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=chSyQPKsNk4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PBZZ_Cu64aQ"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hepatitis B | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e-P-L0oGXgo"}],"related":["GI-13-E2","GI-13-G9","GI-13-G7","GI-13-L7","IM-15-A3","IM-15-C3"]},"GI-12":{"id":"GI-12","summary":"The bacteria, viruses, and parasites that cause diarrhea, gastritis, and hepatitis, organized by how they cause disease: preformed toxin, secretory toxin, invasion, villus damage, or immune injury. Questions give you the food, the timing, the stool, or a lab clue and ask for the organism or its virulence factor.","notes":[{"h":"Toxin-mediated","points":["Staphylococcus aureus: preformed heat-stable enterotoxin in mayonnaise salads, custards, and meats; vomiting within about 1 to 6 hours.","Bacillus cereus emetic toxin: preformed in reheated fried rice; vomiting within about 1 to 6 hours.","Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs, locking adenylyl cyclase on; cAMP rises and rice-water stool follows.","ETEC heat-labile toxin works like cholera toxin (cAMP); its heat-stable toxin raises cGMP; classic traveler's diarrhea.","Clostridioides difficile: spore-forming gram-positive anaerobe; toxins A and B glucosylate Rho GTPases and kill colonocytes.","C. difficile follows antibiotics (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins) and forms pseudomembranes."]},{"h":"Invasive bacteria","points":["Shigella: nonmotile, no H2S, infectious dose as low as about 10 to 100 organisms; invades M cells and spreads cell to cell.","Salmonella: motile, H2S-producing, needs a large dose (acid sensitive); poultry, eggs, reptiles.","Salmonella Typhi causes typhoid fever with rose spots and can persist in the gallbladder.","Campylobacter jejuni: comma or S-shaped, grows at 42 °C (107.6 °F), from undercooked poultry; linked to Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Yersinia enterocolitica: pork and unpasteurized milk; mesenteric adenitis and ileitis mimic appendicitis.","EHEC O157:H7: Shiga toxin inactivates the 60S ribosome; bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef; hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Listeria monocytogenes: deli meats and soft cheese, grows in the cold; listeriolysin O and ActA actin tails for cell-to-cell spread."]},{"h":"Enteric viruses","points":["Rotavirus: reovirus, segmented double-stranded RNA, nonenveloped; destroys villus tip cells in infants and young children.","Norovirus: calicivirus, single-stranded RNA, nonenveloped; outbreaks on cruise ships and in nursing homes, low infectious dose.","Coxsackievirus and echovirus: picornaviruses (single-stranded RNA), fecal-oral, peak in summer and fall."]},{"h":"Hepatitis viruses","points":["HAV: picornavirus, fecal-oral, acute only, never chronic.","HBV: hepadnavirus with partially double-stranded DNA; it replicates through an RNA intermediate using reverse transcriptase.","HCV: flavivirus; error-prone RNA polymerase gives high antigenic variation, so no vaccine and frequent chronic infection.","HDV: defective RNA virus that needs HBsAg as its envelope; superinfection of an HBV carrier is the most severe.","HEV: fecal-oral from contaminated water; fulminant hepatitis with high mortality in pregnancy."]},{"h":"Helicobacter pylori","points":["Curved, flagellated, microaerophilic gram-negative rod; urease, catalase, and oxidase positive.","Urease splits urea to ammonia, buffering acid around the organism in the antral mucus.","Causes chronic gastritis, duodenal and gastric ulcers, gastric adenocarcinoma, and MALT lymphoma.","Active infection tests: urea breath test and stool antigen (both used for test of cure); biopsy with rapid urease test or histology.","Serology stays positive after cure, so it cannot confirm eradication."]},{"h":"Intestinal parasites","points":["Giardia: cysts in untreated water (campers); trophozoites stick to villi and cause foul, fatty diarrhea without blood.","Entamoeba histolytica: trophozoites with ingested red cells; flask-shaped colon ulcers, bloody diarrhea, liver abscess.","Cryptosporidium: acid-fast oocysts, chlorine resistant; severe watery diarrhea in advanced HIV, self-limited otherwise.","Cyclospora (contaminated produce) and Cystoisospora also have acid-fast oocysts.","Strongyloides: larvae penetrate skin, migrate through lungs, and live in the duodenum; autoinfection lets it persist for decades.","Corticosteroids or HTLV-1 trigger Strongyloides hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Toxin-mediated GI infections","cols":["Organism","Toxin and mechanism","Typical source","Onset and stool"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Preformed heat-stable enterotoxin","Mayonnaise salads, custards, meats","About 1 to 6 hours; vomiting predominant"],["Bacillus cereus (emetic)","Preformed heat-stable cereulide","Reheated fried rice","About 1 to 6 hours; vomiting"],["Vibrio cholerae","Cholera toxin activates Gs, raises cAMP","Contaminated water, shellfish","Rice-water stool, massive volume"],["ETEC","Heat-labile toxin raises cAMP; heat-stable toxin raises cGMP","Food and water in travel","Watery diarrhea"],["Clostridioides difficile","Toxins A and B inactivate Rho GTPases","Recent antibiotics, hospitals","Watery diarrhea, pseudomembranes"]]},{"title":"Invasive enteric bacteria","cols":["Organism","Key lab or structural clue","Source","Classic complication"],"rows":[["Shigella","Nonmotile, no H2S, very low infectious dose","Person to person, daycare","Bloody dysentery; HUS with S. dysenteriae"],["Salmonella (nontyphoidal)","Motile, H2S producing","Poultry, eggs, reptiles","Bacteremia, osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease"],["Campylobacter jejuni","Comma or S-shaped, grows at 42 °C (107.6 °F)","Undercooked poultry, raw milk","Guillain-Barre syndrome, reactive arthritis"],["Yersinia enterocolitica","Grows in the cold","Pork, raw milk","Pseudoappendicitis from mesenteric adenitis"],["EHEC O157:H7","Shiga toxin, does not ferment sorbitol","Undercooked beef, produce","Hemolytic uremic syndrome"],["Listeria monocytogenes","Gram-positive rod, tumbling motility, actin tails","Deli meats, soft cheese","Meningitis in newborns, elderly; fetal infection"]]},{"title":"Hepatitis viruses","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Spread","Chronic?","Key point"],"rows":[["HAV","Picornavirus, ssRNA","Fecal-oral","No","Travel, shellfish"],["HBV","Hepadnavirus, partially dsDNA","Blood, sex, birth","Yes","Reverse transcriptase; HCC risk"],["HCV","Flavivirus, ssRNA","Blood","Yes, most cases","High antigenic variation, no vaccine"],["HDV","Deltavirus, defective ssRNA","Blood, sex","Yes","Needs HBsAg to make particles"],["HEV","Hepevirus, ssRNA","Fecal-oral, water","No in healthy hosts","Severe in pregnancy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The vowels hit your bowels","text":"Hepatitis A and E spread by the fecal-oral route; B, C, and D spread through blood and body fluids."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the hours","text":"Vomiting within about 6 hours of eating means the toxin was already in the food (S. aureus, B. cereus). Symptoms a day or more later mean the organism had to grow in the gut first."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Acid-fast in the stool","text":"Acid-fast oocysts in diarrhea point to the coccidia: Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, and Cystoisospora, especially in advanced HIV."}],"highYield":["EHEC O157:H7 plus bloody diarrhea, then anemia, low platelets, and kidney failure means hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Rotavirus infects infants; norovirus causes adult outbreaks.","HDV can only replicate where HBV supplies HBsAg; vaccinating for HBV also prevents HDV.","Urea breath test and stool antigen detect active H. pylori; hold proton pump inhibitors beforehand to avoid false negatives.","Strongyloides hyperinfection follows corticosteroids; screen people from endemic areas before immunosuppression.","Entamoeba causes flask-shaped ulcers and a liver abscess; Giardia causes fatty diarrhea without invasion."],"pitfalls":["Shigella dysenteriae and EHEC both make Shiga or Shiga-like toxin; Shigella invades and is nonmotile, EHEC does not invade.","Salmonella is motile and makes H2S; Shigella is neither.","Cholera toxin and ETEC heat-labile toxin raise cAMP; ETEC heat-stable toxin raises cGMP.","HBV is a DNA virus but uses reverse transcriptase; do not file it with the RNA hepatitis viruses."],"dumps":[{"t":"Diarrhea by mechanism","p":"Build a comparison table on blank paper with rows for preformed toxin, secretory toxin, cytotoxin, invasive bacteria, virus, and parasite. For each row, name two organisms, the onset, and whether the stool is watery or bloody.","l":["Preformed toxin: S. aureus, B. cereus; onset about 1 to 6 hours; vomiting","Secretory toxin: V. cholerae, ETEC; cAMP rise; watery","Cytotoxin: C. difficile, EHEC; colon damage","Invasive: Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella; bloody with fever","Viral: rotavirus in infants, norovirus in outbreaks; watery","Parasite: Giardia fatty and nonbloody, Entamoeba bloody","EHEC links to hemolytic uremic syndrome"]},{"t":"Hepatitis virus map","p":"Draw five boxes, one for each hepatitis virus. In each, write the genome type, the virus family, how it spreads, whether it becomes chronic, and one fact the exam loves.","l":["HAV picornavirus, fecal-oral, never chronic","HBV hepadnavirus, partially dsDNA, reverse transcriptase","HCV flavivirus, high antigenic variation, often chronic","HDV defective, requires HBsAg","HEV fecal-oral, severe in pregnancy","A and E spread fecal-oral; B, C, D spread by blood and body fluids","HBV and HCV raise hepatocellular carcinoma risk"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on enteric gram-negative rods (Enterobacteriaceae)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Hepatitis Viruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on intestinal protozoa and nematodes"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Microbiology - Helicobacter Pylori (Ulcer)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y-shOXdsJeA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hepatitis | Pathophysiology of Viral Hepatitis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R4OG0wHkPcM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hepatitis | Diagnosis of Viral Hepatitis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0pFnLbo6eM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Case Study #16: Clostridium Difficile | C. Diff","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nn34qoj1hh0"}],"related":["GI-13-L1","GI-13-L3","GI-13-L7","GI-13-G7","GI-13-G5","GI-11"]},"GI-13-A":{"id":"GI-13-A","summary":"Parotitis is inflammation and swelling of the parotid gland. Exam questions ask you to separate viral mumps parotitis from bacterial parotitis using the patient, the duct findings, and the complications, and to explain why salivary stones favor the submandibular gland.","notes":[{"h":"Salivary gland basics","points":["Parotid gland is purely serous; its duct (Stensen duct) opens opposite the upper second molar.","Submandibular gland is mixed, mostly serous; its duct (Wharton duct) opens at the sublingual papilla beside the frenulum.","Sublingual gland is mostly mucous and drains by many small ducts into the floor of the mouth.","Parotid secretion is driven by CN IX through the otic ganglion; submandibular and sublingual by CN VII (chorda tympani) through the submandibular ganglion.","Serous cells make salivary amylase, so parotid inflammation raises serum amylase."]},{"h":"Mumps parotitis","points":["Caused by mumps virus, an enveloped negative-sense RNA paramyxovirus spread by respiratory droplets.","Fever and malaise, then painful parotid swelling that is often bilateral (it may start on one side).","Pain with chewing and an earache; the duct opening may be red, but no pus comes out.","Serum amylase is raised from the salivary gland; lipase stays normal unless the pancreas is involved.","Complications: orchitis after puberty (testicular atrophy, rarely sterility), oophoritis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis, sensorineural hearing loss.","Prevented by the live attenuated MMR vaccine."]},{"h":"Acute bacterial parotitis","points":["Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause.","Low salivary flow lets mouth bacteria climb up Stensen duct.","Risk: dehydration, older age, the days after surgery, poor oral hygiene, anticholinergic drugs, duct obstruction.","Usually one side: hot, red, very tender gland, fever, and leukocytosis.","Massaging the gland pushes pus out of the Stensen duct opening.","Can form an abscess and spread into the deep spaces of the neck."]},{"h":"Salivary stones (sialolithiasis)","points":["About 80 to 90 percent of stones form in the submandibular gland.","Its saliva is thicker (more mucin) and more alkaline, with more calcium and phosphate.","Wharton duct is long and runs upward against gravity, bending around the mylohyoid, so saliva pools.","Stone blocks flow, so the gland swells painfully at meals when secretion is stimulated, then settles.","Stagnant saliva gets infected, causing secondary bacterial sialadenitis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Mumps parotitis vs bacterial parotitis vs salivary stone","cols":["Feature","Mumps parotitis","Acute bacterial parotitis","Sialolithiasis"],"rows":[["Cause","Mumps virus (paramyxovirus)","Staphylococcus aureus most often","Calcium phosphate stone blocking a duct"],["Typical patient","Unvaccinated child or young adult","Dehydrated, older, or postoperative patient","Adult with meal-related swelling"],["Gland and side","Parotid, often both sides","Parotid, usually one side","Submandibular, one side"],["Duct opening","May be red, no pus","Pus on gland massage","Stone may be felt in floor of mouth"],["Pain pattern","Constant, worse with chewing","Constant, severe, with fever","Comes and goes with meals"],["Key labs","High amylase, normal lipase","Leukocytosis","Usually normal"],["Complications","Orchitis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis, deafness","Abscess, deep neck spread","Secondary bacterial sialadenitis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Mumps makes glands as big as POM-POMs","text":"Parotitis, Orchitis, aseptic Meningitis, and Pancreatitis are the classic sites of mumps disease."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Wharton duct flows uphill","text":"Thick saliva that has to climb a long duct against gravity sits still and crystallizes. That is why stones form in the submandibular gland."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pus means bacteria","text":"If pressing the parotid squeezes pus from the duct opening, think bacterial parotitis. Mumps gives a swollen gland with a clean duct."}],"highYield":["Unvaccinated young adult with bilateral parotid swelling and testicular pain: mumps orchitis.","High serum amylase with normal lipase in mumps comes from the salivary gland.","Older, dehydrated, or postoperative patient with one hot parotid and pus at the duct: Staphylococcus aureus parotitis.","Salivary stones form mostly in the submandibular gland because of thick saliva and an upward duct.","Gland swelling and pain that start with eating point to a duct stone.","Mumps can cause aseptic meningitis with a lymphocytic CSF."],"pitfalls":["A raised amylase in mumps does not prove pancreatitis; check lipase, which is pancreas-specific.","Mumps can begin on one side, so one-sided swelling does not rule it out; pus at the duct is what points to bacteria.","Stensen duct drains the parotid; Wharton duct drains the submandibular gland.","Stone pain is intermittent and tied to meals; constant pain with fever means the stagnant gland is now infected."],"dumps":[{"t":"Mumps vs bacterial parotitis","p":"Make a two-column table comparing mumps parotitis and acute bacterial parotitis. Include cause, who gets it, sides involved, duct findings, labs, and complications.","l":["Mumps is a paramyxovirus spread by droplets","Bacterial parotitis is usually Staphylococcus aureus","Mumps is often bilateral; bacterial is usually one side","Pus at Stensen duct means bacterial","Bacterial risk: dehydration, older age, after surgery, low saliva flow","Mumps raises serum amylase with normal lipase","Mumps complications: orchitis, oophoritis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis, hearing loss","Mumps is prevented by the MMR vaccine"]},{"t":"Salivary glands and their ducts","p":"Draw the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands with their ducts and where each duct opens in the mouth. Mark where stones usually form and write the reasons beside it.","l":["Stensen duct opens opposite the upper second molar","Wharton duct opens at the sublingual papilla beside the frenulum","Parotid is serous; submandibular is mixed; sublingual is mostly mucous","Parotid nerve supply is CN IX via the otic ganglion","About 80 to 90 percent of stones are submandibular","Reasons: thick alkaline saliva rich in calcium, long duct running upward","Blocked duct causes painful swelling at meals and can become infected"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, section on salivary glands"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on mumps virus (paramyxoviruses)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the parotid region and salivary glands"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Salivation: Parotid, Submandibular, & Sublingual Glands","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3tasuHt6jKg"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Salivary glands - Anatomy and Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EYZPE5-7vZ8"}],"related":["GI-3","GI-12","GI-13-B1"]},"GI-13-B":{"id":"GI-13-B","summary":"Pancreatitis is the pancreas digesting itself after its enzymes switch on too early. The exam tests the causes, the lab and exam findings of acute pancreatitis, its complications, and how chronic pancreatitis leads to malabsorption and diabetes.","notes":[{"h":"How acute pancreatitis starts","points":["Normally trypsinogen is switched on only in the duodenum, by enteropeptidase.","Safeguards: enzymes are made as inactive zymogens, SPINK1 inhibits stray trypsin, and trypsin can destroy itself.","Acinar injury or duct blockage activates trypsin inside the pancreas.","Trypsin then activates phospholipase A2, elastase, and other enzymes, and the gland digests itself.","Results: protein breakdown, fat necrosis, vessel damage with bleeding, and intense inflammation."]},{"h":"Causes","points":["Gallstones and alcohol cause most cases.","A gallstone at the ampulla blocks the duct and raises pressure in it.","Hypertriglyceridemia (usually above 1,000 mg/dL) and hypercalcemia (calcium activates trypsinogen).","After ERCP, after blunt abdominal trauma, and with mumps.","Drugs include thiazides, azathioprine, valproate, and sulfonamides."]},{"h":"Findings of acute pancreatitis","points":["Sudden epigastric pain boring through to the back, with nausea and vomiting; leaning forward may ease it.","Diagnosis needs two of three: typical pain, lipase or amylase above 3 times the upper limit of normal, typical imaging.","Lipase is more specific than amylase and stays high longer.","Lipase frees fatty acids that bind calcium and form chalky calcium soaps (fat necrosis, saponification).","Calcium used up in soaps causes hypocalcemia, a sign of severe disease.","Cullen sign (periumbilical bruising) and Grey Turner sign (flank bruising) mean blood is tracking from the retroperitoneum."]},{"h":"Complications","points":["Pseudocyst: enzyme-rich fluid walled off by granulation and fibrous tissue, with no epithelial lining, usually after about 4 weeks.","Pseudocysts can compress nearby organs, rupture, bleed, or become infected.","Necrotic pancreas can become infected by gut bacteria, forming an abscess.","ARDS: circulating enzymes (phospholipase A2 breaks down surfactant) and cytokines injure the lungs.","DIC from release of procoagulant tissue factors into the blood.","Hypovolemic shock from massive fluid loss into the retroperitoneum and from bleeding."]},{"h":"Chronic pancreatitis","points":["Repeated injury causes permanent fibrosis, loss of acini, and strictured, dilated ducts.","Calcifications on imaging are typical, especially with alcohol.","Causes: alcohol in adults, cystic fibrosis (thick secretions plug ducts) in children, duct obstruction.","Hereditary: PRSS1 gain-of-function (trypsin resists inactivation, autosomal dominant) and SPINK1 loss of function.","Exocrine failure (after about 90 percent loss): steatorrhea, weight loss, deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K.","Islet loss causes diabetes; lipase and amylase may be normal late in disease."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Acute vs chronic pancreatitis","cols":["Feature","Acute pancreatitis","Chronic pancreatitis"],"rows":[["Main lesion","Enzyme autodigestion, edema, fat necrosis, hemorrhage","Fibrosis, acinar loss, duct strictures, calcification"],["Leading causes","Gallstones, alcohol","Alcohol (adults), cystic fibrosis (children)"],["Pain","Sudden severe epigastric pain to the back","Recurrent or constant epigastric pain"],["Lipase and amylase","Above 3 times normal","Often normal"],["Reversible","Usually, if the cause is removed","No"],["Key complications","Pseudocyst, infected necrosis, ARDS, DIC, shock, hypocalcemia","Steatorrhea, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, diabetes, pancreatic cancer risk"]]},{"title":"Complications of acute pancreatitis and their mechanism","cols":["Complication","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Hypocalcemia","Calcium binds free fatty acids in fat necrosis (calcium soaps)"],["Pseudocyst","Leaked enzymes and debris walled off by granulation tissue, no epithelium"],["Infected necrosis or abscess","Gut bacteria seed dead pancreatic tissue"],["ARDS","Phospholipase A2 and cytokines damage alveoli and surfactant"],["DIC","Tissue factor and activated enzymes trigger clotting"],["Hypovolemic shock","Fluid shifts into the retroperitoneum plus bleeding"],["Cullen and Grey Turner signs","Retroperitoneal hemorrhage tracking to umbilicus or flanks"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"I GET SMASHED","text":"Idiopathic, Gallstones, Ethanol, Trauma, Steroids, Mumps, Autoimmune, Scorpion sting, Hypercalcemia or Hypertriglyceridemia, ERCP, Drugs (such as thiazides and azathioprine)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pseudo means no lining","text":"A true cyst is lined by epithelium. A pseudocyst is a walled-off pocket lined only by granulation and fibrous tissue."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Calcium goes into soap","text":"Picture lipase making fatty acids that grab calcium like soap scum. The blood calcium falls as it is locked away in dead fat."}],"highYield":["Epigastric pain to the back plus lipase above 3 times normal: acute pancreatitis.","Gallstones and alcohol are the two leading causes.","Triglycerides above about 1,000 mg/dL can cause pancreatitis.","Low calcium in pancreatitis comes from calcium soap formation in fat necrosis.","Pseudocyst wall is granulation tissue with no epithelium.","Cullen and Grey Turner signs mean retroperitoneal hemorrhage.","PRSS1 mutation makes trypsin resist inactivation, causing hereditary pancreatitis.","Chronic pancreatitis gives steatorrhea, vitamin A, D, E, K deficiency, and diabetes."],"pitfalls":["Amylase rises in salivary disease and some bowel disorders; lipase is the more specific pancreatic test.","Pseudocyst vs cystic tumor: pseudocyst follows pancreatitis and has no epithelial lining.","In chronic pancreatitis the enzymes may be normal because little gland is left; diagnosis relies on imaging and function.","Hypercalcemia causes pancreatitis, but pancreatitis causes hypocalcemia."],"dumps":[{"t":"Trypsin cascade gone wrong","p":"Draw an acinar cell and the duodenum. Show where trypsinogen is normally activated, the safeguards, and what happens when trypsin is activated inside the pancreas.","l":["Enteropeptidase in the duodenum normally activates trypsinogen","Safeguards: zymogens, SPINK1 inhibitor, trypsin self-destruction","Early activation inside the gland starts autodigestion","Trypsin activates phospholipase A2 and elastase","Lipase causes fat necrosis with calcium soaps","Elastase damages vessels, causing hemorrhage","PRSS1 mutation blocks trypsin inactivation"]},{"t":"Acute vs chronic pancreatitis table","p":"Build a table comparing acute and chronic pancreatitis: causes, tissue changes, labs, signs, and complications.","l":["Acute: gallstones and alcohol lead the causes","Acute: lipase above 3 times normal; chronic: enzymes often normal","Acute: fat necrosis, hypocalcemia, Cullen and Grey Turner signs","Acute complications: pseudocyst, infected necrosis, ARDS, DIC, shock","Chronic: fibrosis and calcifications","Chronic causes include alcohol, cystic fibrosis, PRSS1, SPINK1","Chronic: steatorrhea, fat-soluble vitamin loss, diabetes"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Pancreas"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Exocrine Pancreas"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract, section on pancreatic secretion"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Pancreatitis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9j9G3d2gRs4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Acute Pancreatitis - Overview (signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, investigations, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UVof2fZdfjY"}],"related":["GI-6","GI-7","GI-10","GI-13-D2","GI-13-E3","GI-13-K6"]},"GI-13-C":{"id":"GI-13-C","summary":"This unit covers the main noninfectious liver diseases: blocked bile flow, inherited bilirubin disorders, alcohol, fatty liver, autoimmune and drug hepatitis, and the cirrhosis and portal hypertension they can lead to. Exam questions give you liver tests and physical signs and ask you to name the process and predict what happens next.","notes":[{"h":"Cholestasis","points":["Intrahepatic causes: drugs (estrogens, anabolic steroids), pregnancy (third trimester itching), total parenteral nutrition, sepsis, primary biliary cholangitis.","Extrahepatic causes: common bile duct stone, pancreatic head cancer, cholangiocarcinoma, strictures; ultrasound shows dilated ducts.","Labs: conjugated bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, and GGT rise; GGT confirms the ALP comes from the liver.","Retained bile acids cause itching; high cholesterol causes xanthomas.","No bile reaches the gut: pale stools, dark urine (conjugated bilirubin), poor fat and vitamin K absorption."]},{"h":"Cirrhosis: how it forms","points":["Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells sit in the space of Disse and normally store vitamin A.","Chronic injury releases TGF-beta and other signals that turn stellate cells into collagen-making myofibroblasts.","Collagen in the space of Disse closes sinusoid fenestrations, so exchange with hepatocytes falls.","Bridging fibrous septa and regenerative nodules replace the normal lobules across the whole liver.","Main causes: alcohol, chronic HBV and HCV, fatty liver disease, and metabolic disease (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency)."]},{"h":"Cirrhosis: findings from liver failure","points":["Jaundice from failed conjugation and excretion of bilirubin.","Coagulopathy: the liver makes fewer clotting factors (II, V, VII, IX, X), so PT and INR rise and vitamin K does not correct them.","Low albumin causes edema and adds to ascites.","Poor estrogen breakdown: spider angiomas, palmar erythema, gynecomastia, testicular atrophy.","Hepatic encephalopathy: ammonia escapes the urea cycle and shunts past the liver; confusion and asterixis."]},{"h":"Portal hypertension","points":["Prehepatic: portal vein thrombosis; varices and splenomegaly with a normal liver.","Intrahepatic: cirrhosis (most common) and schistosomiasis (presinusoidal periportal fibrosis).","Posthepatic: Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein thrombosis) and right heart failure (nutmeg liver).","Findings: ascites, congestive splenomegaly with low platelets, varices, caput medusae."]},{"h":"Hepatorenal and hepatopulmonary syndromes","points":["Hepatorenal: splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial volume and triggers RAAS and sympathetic drive.","The kidneys clamp down (renal vasoconstriction), so GFR falls and urine sodium is very low, yet the kidneys look normal under the microscope.","Hepatopulmonary: nitric oxide widens lung capillaries, so oxygen cannot diffuse to blood in the middle of the vessel, causing hypoxemia.","Dilations are at the lung bases, so oxygen saturation drops on standing (orthodeoxia)."]},{"h":"Inherited bilirubin disorders","points":["Gilbert: mildly reduced UGT1A1; unconjugated bilirubin rises with fasting, stress, illness; benign.","Crigler-Najjar type I: absent UGT1A1; severe unconjugated jaundice and kernicterus in infancy.","Crigler-Najjar type II: partial deficiency, milder; bilirubin falls with phenobarbital, which induces UGT1A1.","Dubin-Johnson: MRP2 defect blocks secretion of conjugated bilirubin; black liver; benign.","Rotor: similar conjugated rise, milder, and the liver is not pigmented."]},{"h":"Noninfectious hepatitis","points":["Alcohol: steatosis (reversible) to alcoholic hepatitis to cirrhosis.","Alcoholic hepatitis: ballooned hepatocytes, Mallory-Denk bodies (clumped keratin), neutrophils; AST to ALT ratio above 2.","MASLD: insulin resistance and obesity flood the liver with fatty acids; ALT usually above AST.","Simple steatosis is fat only; MASH adds inflammation and ballooning and can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma.","Autoimmune hepatitis: young women, high IgG, ANA and anti-smooth muscle antibody, interface hepatitis with plasma cells.","Acetaminophen overdose: CYP2E1 makes NAPQI, glutathione runs out, zone 3 (centrilobular) necrosis; N-acetylcysteine replaces glutathione.","Isoniazid causes hepatitis that is more common with older age and alcohol use."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Primary biliary cholangitis vs primary sclerosing cholangitis","cols":["Feature","Primary biliary cholangitis","Primary sclerosing cholangitis"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Middle-aged woman","Young or middle-aged man"],["Ducts affected","Small intrahepatic ducts","Intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts"],["Histology","Granulomatous, lymphocytic destruction of small ducts","Onion-skin periductal fibrosis"],["Imaging","Usually normal large ducts","Beaded ducts (strictures and dilations) on cholangiography"],["Antibody","Antimitochondrial antibody","p-ANCA in many"],["Associations","Other autoimmune disease (Sjogren syndrome, thyroiditis)","Ulcerative colitis"],["Cancer risk","Hepatocellular carcinoma after cirrhosis","Cholangiocarcinoma"]]},{"title":"Inherited disorders of bilirubin","cols":["Disorder","Defect","Bilirubin type","Key features"],"rows":[["Gilbert syndrome","Mildly reduced UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Mild jaundice with fasting, stress, illness; benign"],["Crigler-Najjar type I","Absent UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Severe neonatal jaundice, kernicterus; no response to phenobarbital"],["Crigler-Najjar type II","Partial UGT1A1 deficiency","Unconjugated","Milder; bilirubin falls with phenobarbital"],["Dubin-Johnson syndrome","MRP2 canalicular transporter","Conjugated","Black pigmented liver; benign"],["Rotor syndrome","Hepatic bilirubin reuptake defect (OATP1B1 and OATP1B3)","Conjugated","Milder; liver not pigmented; benign"]]},{"title":"Causes of portal hypertension by site","cols":["Site","Examples","Notes"],"rows":[["Prehepatic","Portal vein thrombosis","Varices and splenomegaly; liver itself normal"],["Intrahepatic","Cirrhosis, schistosomiasis","Cirrhosis is the most common cause overall"],["Posthepatic","Budd-Chiari syndrome, right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis","Congested, enlarged liver (nutmeg liver)"]]},{"title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Result"],"rows":[["Esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins to azygos","Esophageal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices"]]},{"title":"Noninfectious hepatitis at a glance","cols":["Type","Key mechanism","Histology","Lab clue"],"rows":[["Alcoholic hepatitis","Alcohol metabolism injures hepatocytes and mitochondria","Ballooning, Mallory-Denk bodies, neutrophils","AST to ALT above 2"],["MASH","Insulin resistance and fatty acid overload","Steatosis, ballooning, inflammation","ALT usually above AST"],["Autoimmune hepatitis","T cell attack on hepatocytes","Interface hepatitis with plasma cells","High IgG, ANA, anti-smooth muscle antibody"],["Acetaminophen","NAPQI after glutathione is used up","Zone 3 (centrilobular) necrosis","Very high AST and ALT"],["Isoniazid","Toxic metabolite injury","Hepatitis pattern","Rising AST and ALT"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"ToAST","text":"In alcoholic liver disease AST runs higher than ALT, usually more than twice as high. Picture the liver toasted by alcohol."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Dubin-Johnson is dark","text":"Dubin-Johnson makes the liver black. Rotor is the look-alike with a normal colored liver."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Before, in, after the liver","text":"Sort portal hypertension by following blood: portal vein (before), sinusoids (in), hepatic veins and right heart (after)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Gilbert skips breakfast","text":"A healthy person who turns slightly yellow after fasting or a cold, with normal liver enzymes and no hemolysis, has Gilbert syndrome."}],"highYield":["Middle-aged woman, itching, high ALP, antimitochondrial antibody: primary biliary cholangitis.","Ulcerative colitis with beaded bile ducts: primary sclerosing cholangitis, with cholangiocarcinoma risk.","Stellate cells in the space of Disse make the collagen of cirrhosis.","Spider angiomas, palmar erythema, and gynecomastia come from high estrogen in liver failure.","Asterixis and confusion in cirrhosis: hepatic encephalopathy from ammonia.","Hepatorenal syndrome: kidney failure with very low urine sodium and normal kidney histology.","Acetaminophen overdose injures zone 3 first; N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione.","Mallory-Denk bodies plus AST to ALT above 2: alcoholic hepatitis."],"pitfalls":["Gilbert and Crigler-Najjar raise unconjugated bilirubin; Dubin-Johnson and Rotor raise conjugated bilirubin.","High PT from cholestasis corrects with vitamin K; high PT from liver failure does not.","PBC damages small intrahepatic ducts; PSC damages large and small ducts with beading.","Budd-Chiari is posthepatic (hepatic veins); portal vein thrombosis is prehepatic.","MASLD has ALT higher than AST; alcohol has AST higher than ALT."],"dumps":[{"t":"Cirrhosis from cell to patient","p":"Draw a liver sinusoid with a hepatocyte, the space of Disse, and a stellate cell. Show how injury leads to fibrosis, then list the signs from liver failure and from portal hypertension.","l":["Stellate cell in space of Disse stores vitamin A","TGF-beta activates it into a collagen-making myofibroblast","Collagen closes sinusoid fenestrations","Bridging fibrosis and regenerative nodules","Liver failure: jaundice, coagulopathy, low albumin, encephalopathy","High estrogen: spider angiomas, palmar erythema, gynecomastia","Portal hypertension: ascites, splenomegaly, varices, caput medusae","Complications include hepatorenal and hepatopulmonary syndromes"]},{"t":"Jaundice sorting table","p":"Make a table of Gilbert, Crigler-Najjar I and II, Dubin-Johnson, Rotor, and cholestasis. For each give the defect, bilirubin type, and one key finding.","l":["Gilbert: mild UGT1A1 drop, unconjugated, worse with fasting","Crigler-Najjar I: absent UGT1A1, kernicterus","Crigler-Najjar II: partial, responds to phenobarbital","Dubin-Johnson: MRP2 defect, conjugated, black liver","Rotor: conjugated, milder, liver not pigmented","Cholestasis: conjugated bilirubin with high ALP and GGT","Cholestasis signs: itching, pale stools, dark urine, xanthomas"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Liver Disease"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on bilirubin metabolism and jaundice"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cirrhosis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R1f1gWCyutA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Gastrointestinal | Types of Jaundice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2qgtG_KGz9A"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Liver Cirrhosis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f-Fxlsb2dtQ"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Approach to Jaundice - types and causes of hyperbilirubinemia, investigations","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdZFGEJ3bqQ"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Pathophysiology Of Jaundice | Dr Najeeb Lectures","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6tU6BS6x_cs"}],"related":["GI-9","GI-5","GI-13-I1","GI-13-J1","GI-13-K4","GI-13-L7","GI-13-G9"]},"GI-13-D":{"id":"GI-13-D","summary":"Gallstones are common, and where a stone ends up decides the disease it causes. The exam tests stone types and risk factors, acute cholecystitis and its calculous and acalculous forms, and complications such as cholangitis, gallstone pancreatitis, gallstone ileus, and Mirizzi syndrome.","notes":[{"h":"How stones form","points":["Bile salts and lecithin hold cholesterol in micelles.","Cholesterol stones form when bile has too much cholesterol or too few bile salts, plus gallbladder stasis.","Cholesterol stone risks: female sex, obesity, pregnancy, estrogen or oral contraceptives, rapid weight loss, older age, fibrates.","Estrogen increases cholesterol secretion into bile; progesterone slows gallbladder emptying.","Black pigment stones (calcium bilirubinate) form with chronic hemolysis such as sickle cell disease and hereditary spherocytosis.","Brown pigment stones form in infected bile ducts: bacterial beta-glucuronidase frees unconjugated bilirubin."]},{"h":"Where the stone goes, what it causes","points":["In the gallbladder: often silent.","Brief block of the cystic duct: biliary colic, pain after a fatty meal (CCK makes the gallbladder contract) that settles within hours, normal labs.","Stuck in the cystic duct: acute cholecystitis.","In the common bile duct (choledocholithiasis): obstructive jaundice with high conjugated bilirubin and ALP.","Common duct stone plus infection: ascending cholangitis with fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain (Charcot triad).","At the ampulla: gallstone pancreatitis.","Through a cholecystoenteric fistula: gallstone ileus, small bowel obstruction usually at the ileocecal valve, with air in the biliary tree."]},{"h":"Acute cholecystitis","points":["Calculous: a stone stays stuck in the cystic duct and the gallbladder distends.","First chemical inflammation (lysolecithin and prostaglandins irritate the wall), then bacterial infection by gut organisms such as E. coli.","Right upper quadrant pain lasting more than about 6 hours, fever, leukocytosis.","Murphy sign: the patient stops breathing in when the examiner presses under the right costal margin.","Acalculous: no stone; occurs in critically ill patients (sepsis, burns, trauma, major surgery, TPN) from stasis and ischemia.","Acalculous disease has a higher risk of gangrene and perforation."]},{"h":"Chronic cholecystitis and cancer risk","points":["Repeated attacks scar and thicken the gallbladder wall.","Calcium deposits in the scarred wall give a porcelain gallbladder.","Porcelain gallbladder and long-standing gallstones raise the risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma."]},{"h":"Mirizzi syndrome","points":["A stone stuck in the cystic duct or gallbladder neck presses on the common hepatic duct from outside.","Result: obstructive jaundice even though no stone sits in the common bile duct."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gallstone types","cols":["Type","Made of","Main cause or risk","Where it forms"],"rows":[["Cholesterol","Cholesterol (most common type)","Supersaturated bile and stasis: female, obesity, pregnancy, estrogen, rapid weight loss","Gallbladder"],["Black pigment","Calcium bilirubinate","Chronic hemolysis (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis)","Gallbladder"],["Brown pigment","Calcium bilirubinate with fatty acids","Biliary infection (E. coli, liver flukes)","Bile ducts"]]},{"title":"Gallstone complications by location","cols":["Stone location","Condition","Key findings"],"rows":[["Cystic duct, brief","Biliary colic","Postprandial pain, settles within hours, normal labs"],["Cystic duct, persistent","Acute cholecystitis","Pain over 6 hours, fever, Murphy sign, leukocytosis"],["Cystic duct or gallbladder neck pressing on common hepatic duct","Mirizzi syndrome","Obstructive jaundice"],["Common bile duct","Choledocholithiasis","Jaundice, high conjugated bilirubin and ALP"],["Common bile duct with infection","Ascending cholangitis","Fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain"],["Ampulla of Vater","Gallstone pancreatitis","Epigastric pain to back, high lipase"],["Ileocecal valve via fistula","Gallstone ileus","Small bowel obstruction, air in biliary tree"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 4 Fs","text":"Female, Fat (obese), Fertile (pregnancy, multiparity), Forty: classic risk factors for cholesterol gallstones."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Black is blood, brown is bugs","text":"Black pigment stones come from hemolysis. Brown pigment stones come from bacteria or parasites in the bile ducts."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Add one finding, change one diagnosis","text":"Pain under 6 hours with normal labs is biliary colic. Pain over 6 hours with fever and leukocytosis is cholecystitis. Jaundice points to a stone in the common bile duct, and fever with jaundice and right upper quadrant pain is cholangitis."}],"highYield":["Charcot triad (fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain) means ascending cholangitis.","Reynolds pentad adds hypotension and confusion to Charcot triad.","Air in the biliary tree with small bowel obstruction in an older woman: gallstone ileus.","Critically ill patient with gallbladder inflammation and no stones: acalculous cholecystitis.","Porcelain gallbladder is linked to gallbladder adenocarcinoma.","Hemolytic anemia in a child with gallstones: black pigment stones.","Mirizzi syndrome causes jaundice from outside compression of the common hepatic duct."],"pitfalls":["Biliary colic lasts a few hours with normal labs; cholecystitis lasts longer with fever and leukocytosis.","Plain cholecystitis rarely causes marked jaundice; jaundice suggests a duct stone, cholangitis, or Mirizzi syndrome.","Gallstone ileus is a mechanical obstruction of the small bowel, usually at the ileocecal valve.","Black stones form in the gallbladder; brown stones form in the bile ducts."],"dumps":[{"t":"Biliary tree stone map","p":"Draw the gallbladder, cystic duct, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, pancreatic duct, and duodenum. Place a stone at each site and write the condition it causes.","l":["Brief cystic duct block: biliary colic","Persistent cystic duct block: acute cholecystitis","Gallbladder neck stone pressing common hepatic duct: Mirizzi syndrome","Common bile duct: choledocholithiasis with obstructive jaundice","Duct stone plus infection: ascending cholangitis (Charcot triad)","Ampulla: gallstone pancreatitis","Fistula to bowel: gallstone ileus at the ileocecal valve"]},{"t":"Stone types table","p":"Make a table of cholesterol, black pigment, and brown pigment stones with what they are made of, the cause, and where they form.","l":["Cholesterol stones are the most common","Cholesterol stones need supersaturated bile and stasis","Risks: female, obesity, pregnancy, estrogen, rapid weight loss","Black stones: calcium bilirubinate from chronic hemolysis","Brown stones: infection, bacterial beta-glucuronidase","Brown stones form in the bile ducts"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder, section on gallbladder diseases"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract, section on bile secretion"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Biliary Tract Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSiifa2PR_Y"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Acute Cholecystitis - Overview (signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dGdQ-xWXOTE"}],"related":["GI-3","GI-6","GI-9","GI-13-B1","GI-13-C1","GI-13-F"]},"GI-13-E":{"id":"GI-13-E","summary":"Malabsorption happens when food is not broken down (enzyme or acid problems) or not taken up by a damaged or bypassed bowel. The exam asks you to match the stool, the labs, and the vitamin pattern to the cause: achlorhydria, celiac disease, pancreatic or brush border enzyme loss, lactase deficiency, short bowel, or bacterial overgrowth.","notes":[{"h":"Achlorhydria","points":["Causes: autoimmune atrophic gastritis (antibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor), chronic H. pylori pangastritis with atrophy, gastrectomy.","No acid means no feedback on antral G cells, so gastrin rises.","Gastrin drives ECL cell hyperplasia, which can progress to gastric carcinoid (neuroendocrine) tumors.","Iron needs acid to stay soluble and reduced (Fe2+), so iron deficiency follows.","Acid and pepsin free B12 from food; autoimmune gastritis also loses intrinsic factor, causing pernicious anemia.","Without the acid barrier, bacteria overgrow in the stomach and small bowel."]},{"h":"Celiac disease","points":["Tissue transglutaminase deamidates gliadin, which binds HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 and activates CD4 T cells.","Biopsy: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, more intraepithelial lymphocytes.","Worst in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum, where iron and folate are absorbed.","Iron deficiency anemia that does not respond to oral iron is a common first sign.","Antibodies: IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and antiendomysial; check total IgA because IgA deficiency is linked.","Dermatitis herpetiformis: itchy vesicles on elbows and knees with IgA in the dermal papillae.","Long-term risk of enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma."]},{"h":"Enzyme deficiency vs mucosal disease","points":["Pancreatic insufficiency (chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis) blocks digestion of fat and protein: steatorrhea, weight loss, low vitamins A, D, E, K.","Fecal elastase is low in pancreatic insufficiency.","D-xylose needs no digestion, so it tests the mucosa: normal in pancreatic disease, low in mucosal disease such as celiac.","Enteropeptidase (brush border) normally activates trypsinogen; congenital deficiency stops all protease activation.","Enteropeptidase deficiency causes protein malabsorption in infants: diarrhea, failure to thrive, low protein, edema."]},{"h":"Lactase deficiency","points":["Primary (adult type): lactase falls after childhood; common in people of East Asian, African, and Native American ancestry; villi are normal.","Secondary: villus tips are injured, as after viral enteritis or in celiac disease.","Unabsorbed lactose pulls water into the gut: osmotic diarrhea with a high stool osmotic gap and acidic stool.","Colon bacteria ferment lactose to gas and acids: bloating, cramps, flatulence after dairy.","Hydrogen breath test rises after a lactose load; diarrhea stops with fasting."]},{"h":"Short bowel and blind loop","points":["Short bowel after extensive resection: watery diarrhea, steatorrhea, weight loss.","Losing the terminal ileum loses B12 uptake and bile acid recycling, so B12 deficiency and fat malabsorption follow.","Unabsorbed fat binds calcium, leaving oxalate free to be absorbed in the colon: calcium oxalate kidney stones.","Blind loop after gastric surgery (stasis) lets bacteria overgrow.","Bacteria deconjugate bile acids (micelles fail, steatorrhea) and consume B12, while making folate (normal or high folate)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Sorting malabsorption","cols":["Condition","Main defect","D-xylose test","Key clue"],"rows":[["Pancreatic insufficiency","No pancreatic enzymes, no digestion","Normal","Low fecal elastase, steatorrhea"],["Celiac disease","Damaged villi in duodenum and jejunum","Low","Anti-tTG IgA, iron deficiency, dermatitis herpetiformis"],["Lactase deficiency","Missing brush border lactase","Normal (primary form)","Positive hydrogen breath test after lactose"],["Bacterial overgrowth","Bacteria deconjugate bile acids and use B12","Low, corrects with antibiotics","Low B12, normal or high folate"],["Terminal ileal resection","Loss of B12 and bile acid uptake","Normal if jejunum is intact","B12 deficiency, oxalate stones"],["Enteropeptidase deficiency","Trypsinogen never activated","Normal","Infant with protein malabsorption and edema"]]},{"title":"Consequences of achlorhydria","cols":["Loss of acid leads to","Why"],"rows":[["Hypergastrinemia","No acid feedback on antral G cells"],["ECL hyperplasia and carcinoid tumors","Gastrin is trophic to ECL cells"],["Iron deficiency","Iron needs acid to stay soluble and reduced"],["B12 deficiency","Acid and pepsin free B12 from food; intrinsic factor lost in autoimmune gastritis"],["Bacterial overgrowth","No acid barrier to swallowed bacteria"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"D-xylose skips digestion","text":"D-xylose is absorbed without enzymes, so it only tests the bowel wall. Low result means mucosal disease (or bacteria eating it); normal result points to the pancreas."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Bacteria eat B12 and make folate","text":"In bacterial overgrowth B12 falls and folate stays normal or rises. Two vitamins moving in opposite directions is the clue."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tips go first","text":"Lactase sits at the villus tips, so any mucosal injury (viral enteritis, celiac) knocks it out first."}],"highYield":["Autoimmune gastritis: achlorhydria, high gastrin, pernicious anemia, and carcinoid risk.","Celiac disease: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytes, worst in distal duodenum.","Iron deficiency anemia not fixed by oral iron should prompt testing for celiac disease.","Dermatitis herpetiformis is a skin sign of celiac disease.","Steatorrhea with normal D-xylose and low fecal elastase: pancreatic insufficiency.","Bloating and diarrhea after dairy with normal villi: primary lactase deficiency.","Ileal resection leads to B12 deficiency, bile acid loss, and calcium oxalate stones.","Blind loop: low B12 with normal or high folate."],"pitfalls":["Pernicious anemia lacks intrinsic factor; bacterial overgrowth has intrinsic factor but bacteria use up the B12.","Primary lactase deficiency has normal villi; secondary lactase deficiency follows mucosal injury.","Oxalate stones after bowel resection need the colon to be present, because oxalate is absorbed there.","Celiac IgA tests can be falsely negative in IgA deficiency, so total IgA is checked."],"dumps":[{"t":"Malabsorption sorting table","p":"Make a table of pancreatic insufficiency, celiac disease, lactase deficiency, and bacterial overgrowth. Give the defect, the D-xylose result, and the key test or clue for each.","l":["Pancreatic insufficiency: normal D-xylose, low fecal elastase","Celiac: low D-xylose, anti-tTG IgA, villous atrophy","Celiac distribution: distal duodenum and proximal jejunum","Lactase deficiency: osmotic diarrhea, positive hydrogen breath test","Primary lactase deficiency: normal villi","Bacterial overgrowth: deconjugated bile acids, steatorrhea","Bacterial overgrowth: low B12, normal or high folate"]},{"t":"Achlorhydria chain","p":"Draw the stomach with parietal cells, G cells, and ECL cells. Trace what happens when acid is lost, ending with each nutrient and tumor consequence.","l":["Causes: autoimmune gastritis, H. pylori pangastritis, gastrectomy","No acid feedback on G cells, gastrin rises","Gastrin causes ECL hyperplasia and carcinoid risk","Iron deficiency from loss of acid","B12 deficiency, plus intrinsic factor loss in autoimmune gastritis","Bacterial overgrowth from loss of the acid barrier"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Malabsorption | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kx1UgaqpYkc"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Coeliac (Celiac) Disease - Overview (signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, diagnosis, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hxd0msD954I"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is celiac disease | Gastrointestinal system diseases | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gZeliSjhkpg"}],"related":["GI-6","GI-7","GI-11","GI-13-B1","GI-13-G7"]},"GI-13-F":{"id":"GI-13-F","summary":"This unit covers disorders that block or stall movement through the gut, from the esophagus to the rectum. The exam asks you to tell a nerve or muscle failure (achalasia, ileus, Hirschsprung disease) from a mechanical block (hernia, intussusception, volvulus) and to predict the findings and complications of each.","notes":[{"h":"Achalasia and Zenker diverticulum","points":["Achalasia: loss of inhibitory (nitric oxide and VIP) neurons in the myenteric plexus.","Result: the LES cannot relax and the distal esophagus loses peristalsis.","Dysphagia to solids and liquids from the start, regurgitation, weight loss.","Barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus tapering to a bird beak; manometry shows incomplete LES relaxation and no peristalsis.","Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi) destroys the plexus and causes secondary achalasia.","Zenker diverticulum: a false diverticulum (mucosa and submucosa) pushing out above the cricopharyngeus.","Cricopharyngeal dysfunction raises pressure; regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis, aspiration in older adults."]},{"h":"Adynamic ileus vs mechanical obstruction","points":["Adynamic (paralytic) ileus: the bowel stops moving with no physical block.","Causes: abdominal surgery, peritonitis, hypokalemia, opioids.","Adynamic signs: distension, absent bowel sounds, gas spread through small and large bowel.","Mechanical obstruction causes: adhesions (most common in the small bowel), hernias, tumors, impaction.","Mechanical signs: colicky pain, vomiting, high-pitched bowel sounds early, dilated loops above a transition point with little gas below."]},{"h":"Hernias","points":["Sliding hiatal hernia: the GE junction slides up into the chest; most common type; linked to reflux.","Paraesophageal hiatal hernia: GE junction stays in place and the fundus rolls up beside the esophagus; can incarcerate or strangulate.","Incarcerated hernia: bowel is trapped and cannot be pushed back.","Strangulation: the tight neck squeezes thin-walled veins first, then swelling raises pressure until arterial inflow stops and the bowel infarcts.","Femoral hernias have the highest risk of strangulation."]},{"h":"Intussusception and volvulus","points":["Intussusception: one segment telescopes into the next, most often ileocolic in children about 6 months to 3 years old.","Lead points: hypertrophied Peyer patches after viral infection, Meckel diverticulum, polyps; in adults, a tumor.","Findings: bouts of colicky pain with legs drawn up, sausage-shaped mass, currant jelly stool (mucosal ischemia), target sign on ultrasound.","Volvulus: bowel twists on its mesentery, blocking the lumen and its blood supply.","Sigmoid volvulus is most common in older adults with chronic constipation (coffee bean sign).","Midgut volvulus occurs in infants with malrotation and presents with bilious vomiting."]},{"h":"Megacolon","points":["Hirschsprung disease: neural crest cells fail to migrate, so ganglion cells are missing from the rectum and a continuous segment above it.","The aganglionic segment stays contracted; colon above it dilates.","Newborn fails to pass meconium within 48 hours, distended abdomen, empty rectal vault, then explosive stool after a rectal exam.","Linked to RET mutations and Down syndrome; diagnosed by rectal suction biopsy.","Chagas disease destroys the myenteric plexus later in life (acquired megacolon and megaesophagus).","Toxic megacolon: acute colon dilation with systemic toxicity complicating ulcerative colitis or C. difficile colitis; risk of perforation."]},{"h":"Functional motility disorders","points":["Gastroparesis: delayed gastric emptying with no blockage, most often from diabetic autonomic (vagal) neuropathy.","Gastroparesis symptoms: early satiety, nausea, vomiting of undigested food hours after meals, bloating.","Irritable bowel syndrome: recurrent abdominal pain linked to defecation and a change in stool frequency or form.","IBS has no structural disease; weight loss, bleeding, or anemia suggest another diagnosis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Adynamic ileus vs mechanical obstruction","cols":["Feature","Adynamic (paralytic) ileus","Mechanical obstruction"],"rows":[["Problem","Bowel stops contracting","Physical block in or around the lumen"],["Common causes","Surgery, peritonitis, hypokalemia, opioids","Adhesions, hernias, tumors, impaction, volvulus"],["Pain","Mild, diffuse","Colicky"],["Bowel sounds","Absent or reduced","High-pitched and tinkling early, absent late"],["Imaging","Gas spread through small and large bowel","Dilated loops above a transition point, little gas below"]]},{"title":"Intussusception vs volvulus","cols":["Feature","Intussusception","Volvulus"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Segment telescopes into the next segment","Bowel twists around its mesentery"],["Typical patient","Child about 6 months to 3 years","Older adult (sigmoid) or infant with malrotation (midgut)"],["Usual site","Ileocolic","Sigmoid colon in adults; midgut in infants"],["Lead point or risk","Peyer patches, Meckel diverticulum, polyp, tumor in adults","Redundant sigmoid, chronic constipation, malrotation"],["Classic findings","Colicky pain, sausage mass, currant jelly stool, target sign","Distension, coffee bean sign, bilious vomiting in infants"]]},{"title":"Causes of megacolon","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Classic picture"],"rows":[["Hirschsprung disease","Failed neural crest migration, no ganglion cells in rectum and segment above","Newborn with delayed meconium, empty rectal vault"],["Chagas disease","Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the myenteric plexus","Adult from Latin America with megacolon, megaesophagus, cardiomyopathy"],["Toxic megacolon","Severe colitis paralyzes colon muscle","Ulcerative colitis or C. difficile colitis with fever, tachycardia, dilated colon"]]},{"title":"Hiatal hernia types","cols":["Feature","Sliding","Paraesophageal"],"rows":[["GE junction","Moves up into the chest","Stays below the diaphragm"],["What herniates","GE junction and cardia","Fundus beside the esophagus"],["Frequency","Most common","Less common"],["Main problem","Reflux","Incarceration, strangulation, volvulus"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"A-chalasia means no relaxing","text":"Chalasia means relaxation. Achalasia is the LES that cannot relax because its nitric oxide neurons are gone."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Solids first or both at once","text":"A mechanical narrowing (stricture, cancer) blocks solids first. A motility problem like achalasia blocks solids and liquids from the start."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Veins before arteries","text":"In strangulation, soft veins collapse first, the bowel swells, and only then are the arteries squeezed shut. That is why trapped bowel turns dark and swollen before it dies."}],"highYield":["Bird-beak esophagus with dysphagia to solids and liquids: achalasia.","Achalasia in a patient from Latin America: think Chagas disease.","Older adult with halitosis and regurgitation of old food: Zenker diverticulum.","Absent bowel sounds after surgery or with low potassium: adynamic ileus.","Currant jelly stool and a sausage-shaped mass in a toddler: intussusception.","Coffee bean sign in an older constipated patient: sigmoid volvulus.","Newborn with no meconium by 48 hours and an empty rectum: Hirschsprung disease.","Diabetic with early satiety and vomiting of food eaten hours ago: gastroparesis."],"pitfalls":["Hirschsprung always involves the rectum; the dilated part is the normal colon above the aganglionic segment.","Zenker is a false diverticulum (no muscle layer); Meckel diverticulum is a true diverticulum.","A sliding hernia moves the GE junction up; a paraesophageal hernia leaves it in place.","Ileus has diffuse gas with no transition point; mechanical obstruction has a transition point."],"dumps":[{"t":"Obstruction types table","p":"Make a table comparing adynamic ileus, intussusception, volvulus, and strangulated hernia. Include cause, typical patient, and key findings.","l":["Adynamic ileus: surgery, peritonitis, hypokalemia, opioids","Adynamic ileus: absent bowel sounds, diffuse gas","Intussusception: ileocolic in young children with a lead point","Intussusception: currant jelly stool, sausage mass, target sign","Volvulus: sigmoid in older adults, midgut with malrotation in infants","Strangulation: venous block, then swelling, then arterial block and infarction","Mechanical obstruction: high-pitched bowel sounds and a transition point"]},{"t":"Enteric nervous system failures","p":"Draw the gut wall layers and label the myenteric and submucosal plexuses. Then show what goes wrong in achalasia, Hirschsprung disease, Chagas disease, and gastroparesis.","l":["Myenteric plexus lies between the muscle layers; submucosal plexus in the submucosa","Achalasia: loss of nitric oxide and VIP inhibitory neurons","Achalasia: LES fails to relax, no peristalsis, bird beak","Hirschsprung: neural crest migration failure, no ganglion cells in rectum","Hirschsprung: delayed meconium, empty rectal vault","Chagas: Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the plexus, megaesophagus and megacolon","Gastroparesis: diabetic vagal neuropathy slows gastric emptying"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Physiology of Gastrointestinal Disorders"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Section on the neural crest and enteric nervous system"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the inguinal region and hernias"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Bowel Obstruction | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6GhdESRDkqY"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Esophageal Motility Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rc-ZnGg4QMA"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Achalasia (esophageal) - signs and symptoms, pathophysiology, investigations and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IthD_e6SIio"}],"related":["GI-1","GI-6","GI-13-G8","GI-13-G9","GI-13-H3","GI-13-I2"]},"GI-13-G":{"id":"GI-13-G","summary":"Inflammatory GI diseases run from reflux, esophagitis, gastritis, and ulcers in the upper tract to appendicitis, diverticular disease, and inflammatory bowel disease lower down. Exams test what injures the mucosa, where the lesion sits, and which complication follows.","notes":[{"h":"Reflux and Barrett esophagus","points":["GERD comes from transient LES relaxations, low resting LES tone, and hiatal hernia.","Risks: obesity and pregnancy (raised abdominal pressure; progesterone relaxes the LES), smoking, alcohol, fatty meals.","Symptoms: heartburn and regurgitation; aspiration and vagal reflexes add chronic cough, hoarseness, and wheeze.","Reflux esophagitis biopsy: basal cell hyperplasia, elongated lamina propria papillae, a few eosinophils.","Complications: peptic stricture with dysphagia, ulcer, bleeding, and Barrett esophagus.","Barrett: distal squamous epithelium replaced by intestinal-type columnar epithelium with goblet cells; then low-grade dysplasia, high-grade dysplasia, adenocarcinoma."]},{"h":"Other esophageal injury","points":["Eosinophilic esophagitis: atopic young men, dysphagia and food impaction, 15 or more eosinophils per high-power field, rings and linear furrows.","Pill esophagitis: doxycycline, NSAIDs, potassium chloride, bisphosphonates; a pill taken with little water while lying down sits and burns a focal ulcer.","Mallory-Weiss: longitudinal mucosal tear at the gastroesophageal junction after forceful retching (alcohol use, bulimia); hematemesis.","Boerhaave: full-thickness rupture, usually the distal left wall; severe chest pain, pneumomediastinum, subcutaneous emphysema, mediastinitis."]},{"h":"Gastritis","points":["NSAIDs block COX, so less prostaglandin: less mucus and bicarbonate, less mucosal blood flow, more acid.","Alcohol, uremia, and shock also cause acute erosive gastritis.","Curling ulcer: severe burns cause hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia. Cushing ulcer: brain injury raises vagal output, so acid rises.","H. pylori gastritis starts in the antrum; urease makes ammonia; leads to intestinal metaplasia, adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma, and duodenal ulcer.","Autoimmune gastritis hits the body and fundus; T cells and antibodies destroy parietal cells, giving achlorhydria, high gastrin, and pernicious anemia."]},{"h":"Peptic ulcers","points":["H. pylori and NSAIDs cause most ulcers; Zollinger-Ellison gives multiple, refractory, or distal ulcers.","H. pylori in the antrum lowers somatostatin, so gastrin and acid rise and the duodenum takes the hit.","Posterior duodenal ulcers erode the gastroduodenal artery; lesser curvature gastric ulcers erode the left gastric artery.","Anterior duodenal ulcers perforate: sudden severe pain, rigid abdomen, free air under the diaphragm on an upright film."]},{"h":"Appendicitis and diverticular disease","points":["Appendicitis: a fecalith (adults) or lymphoid hyperplasia (children) blocks the lumen; mucus builds, pressure squeezes veins then arteries, and bacteria invade the ischemic wall.","Early pain is visceral (T10 afferents), vague, and periumbilical; it moves to McBurney point once the parietal peritoneum is inflamed.","Untreated appendicitis leads to gangrene, perforation, abscess, and peritonitis.","Diverticula: a low-fiber diet makes small stools and high segmental pressure; mucosa and submucosa herniate where vasa recta pierce the muscle, mostly in the sigmoid.","Diverticulosis bleeds painlessly when a stretched vas rectum ruptures; diverticulitis gives LLQ pain, fever, leukocytosis, and can form an abscess, perforate, or make a colovesical fistula."]},{"h":"Inflammatory bowel disease","points":["Crohn: anywhere mouth to anus, usually terminal ileum; skip lesions, transmural, noncaseating granulomas, cobblestone mucosa.","Ulcerative colitis: rectum first, extends proximally, continuous; mucosa and submucosa only; crypt abscesses, pseudopolyps.","Crohn complications: fistulas, strictures, B12 and bile acid malabsorption, gallstones, calcium oxalate kidney stones.","UC complications: toxic megacolon, primary sclerosing cholangitis. Both: colorectal cancer, erythema nodosum, uveitis, arthritis."]},{"h":"Noninfectious enteritis and anorectal disease","points":["Microscopic colitis: older women, chronic watery diarrhea, normal-looking colonoscopy; only a biopsy shows it.","Collagenous colitis has a thick subepithelial collagen band; lymphocytic colitis has excess intraepithelial lymphocytes. NSAIDs, PPIs, and SSRIs are linked.","Radiation enteritis: acute crypt injury; later, obliterative endarteritis causes fibrosis, stricture, and bleeding.","Anal fissure: posterior midline tear, tearing pain with stools, bright red blood on the paper; an off-midline fissure suggests Crohn disease.","Anorectal abscess starts in an anal gland and can track to skin as a fistula (classic in Crohn). Proctitis: UC, radiation, sexually transmitted infection. Rectal prolapse: concentric rings, older women who strain, children with cystic fibrosis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Inflammatory GI diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Appendicitis","Fecalith in adults, lymphoid hyperplasia in children","Luminal obstruction, distension, ischemia, bacterial invasion","Periumbilical pain moving to McBurney point, fever, vomiting, leukocytosis","Gangrene, perforation, abscess, peritonitis"],["Barrett esophagus","Chronic GERD, obesity, white men, smoking","Intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells replaces distal squamous epithelium","Salmon-pink mucosa above the GE junction; may have no symptoms beyond reflux","Dysplasia, esophageal adenocarcinoma"],["Diverticular disease","Low-fiber diet, age, obesity","High segmental pressure pushes mucosa through weak spots at vasa recta (false diverticula)","Diverticulosis: painless hematochezia. Diverticulitis: LLQ pain, fever","Abscess, perforation, colovesical fistula, stricture"],["Enteritis (noninfectious)","Radiation, NSAIDs, microscopic colitis (drugs, autoimmune links)","Direct mucosal injury; radiation endarteritis; mucosal collagen or lymphocyte infiltration","Diarrhea; microscopic colitis: chronic watery diarrhea with normal-looking mucosa","Radiation strictures and bleeding; NSAID strictures"],["Esophageal, gastric, duodenal ulcers","H. pylori, NSAIDs, Zollinger-Ellison, smoking","Acid and pepsin overwhelm mucosal defense","Epigastric pain tied to meals","Hemorrhage, perforation with free air, gastric outlet obstruction"],["Esophagitis (noninfectious)","Reflux, atopy (eosinophilic), pills, corrosives","Acid, allergic (Th2) inflammation, or direct chemical injury","Heartburn, odynophagia, dysphagia, food impaction in eosinophilic type","Stricture, Barrett esophagus (reflux)"],["Gastritis","H. pylori, autoimmunity, NSAIDs, alcohol, burns, brain injury","Infection, parietal cell destruction, or loss of prostaglandin protection and ischemia","Dyspepsia, bleeding erosions; B12 deficiency in autoimmune type","Ulcer, adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma, pernicious anemia, carcinoid"],["GERD","Obesity, pregnancy, hiatal hernia, smoking, alcohol","Transient LES relaxation, low LES tone","Heartburn, regurgitation, chronic cough, hoarseness","Esophagitis, stricture, Barrett esophagus, adenocarcinoma"],["Inflammatory bowel disease","Genetic susceptibility (NOD2 in Crohn), abnormal immune response to gut flora; smoking raises Crohn risk and lowers UC risk","Crohn: transmural, Th1 and Th17. UC: mucosal","Crohn: RLQ pain, diarrhea, perianal disease. UC: bloody diarrhea, tenesmus","Fistula and stricture (Crohn), toxic megacolon and PSC (UC), colorectal cancer"]]},{"title":"Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","cols":["Feature","Crohn disease","Ulcerative colitis"],"rows":[["Location","Mouth to anus, terminal ileum most often, rectum often spared","Rectum always, extends proximally in the colon"],["Distribution","Skip lesions","Continuous"],["Depth","Transmural","Mucosa and submucosa"],["Gross look","Cobblestone mucosa, knife-like linear ulcers, creeping fat, strictures","Pseudopolyps, friable bleeding mucosa, loss of haustra"],["Microscopy","Noncaseating granulomas, lymphoid aggregates","Crypt abscesses, crypt distortion"],["Bowel complications","Fistulas, strictures, perianal abscess","Toxic megacolon, severe bleeding"],["Absorption","B12 and bile acid loss when ileum is diseased; gallstones; calcium oxalate stones","Malabsorption uncommon"],["Liver link","Primary sclerosing cholangitis less common","Primary sclerosing cholangitis (often p-ANCA positive)"],["Shared","Colorectal cancer risk, erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum, uveitis, arthritis","Same"]]},{"title":"Gastric versus duodenal ulcer","cols":["Feature","Gastric ulcer","Duodenal ulcer"],"rows":[["Usual site","Lesser curvature near the antrum","First part of the duodenum"],["Main mechanism","Weak mucosal defense (H. pylori, NSAIDs)","Too much acid (H. pylori raising gastrin, Zollinger-Ellison)"],["Pain and meals","Worse with eating; weight loss","Better with eating, returns 2 to 3 hours later"],["Malignancy","Can be malignant, so biopsy","Almost never malignant"],["Bleeding vessel","Left gastric artery","Gastroduodenal artery (posterior wall)"],["Perforation","Free air under the diaphragm","Anterior wall ulcer; free air under the diaphragm"]]},{"title":"Chronic gastritis types","cols":["Feature","H. pylori gastritis","Autoimmune gastritis"],"rows":[["Region","Antrum","Body and fundus"],["Cause","Infection with a urease-positive curved gram-negative rod","T cells and antibodies against parietal cells (H+/K+ ATPase) and intrinsic factor"],["Acid","Often raised (antral type)","Low to absent (achlorhydria)"],["Gastrin","Normal or raised","Very high (G-cell hyperplasia)"],["Key risks","Peptic ulcer, intestinal-type adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma","Pernicious anemia, adenocarcinoma, gastric carcinoid"]]},{"title":"Esophageal injury compared","cols":["Condition","Cause","Key finding","Typical patient"],"rows":[["Reflux esophagitis","Acid and pepsin reflux","Distal; basal cell hyperplasia, elongated papillae","Obese adult with heartburn"],["Eosinophilic esophagitis","Allergic (Th2) reaction to food antigens","15 or more eosinophils per high-power field; rings and furrows","Young atopic man with food impaction"],["Pill esophagitis","Doxycycline, NSAIDs, potassium chloride, bisphosphonates","Focal ulcer where the pill lodged","Took a pill at bedtime with little water"],["Mallory-Weiss syndrome","Forceful retching","Mucosal tear at the GE junction; hematemesis","Binge drinker or bulimia"],["Boerhaave syndrome","Forceful vomiting","Transmural rupture; pneumomediastinum, subcutaneous emphysema","Severe chest pain after vomiting"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ULCCCERS (ulcerative colitis)","text":"Ulcers, Large intestine, Continuous, Colorectal carcinoma, Crypt abscesses, Extends proximally, Red diarrhea, Sclerosing cholangitis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Meals sort the ulcer","text":"Gastric ulcer pain gets worse when food arrives, so patients eat less and lose weight. Duodenal ulcer pain eases with food because the meal buffers acid, then it returns hours later."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Appendicitis pain moves","text":"Visceral fibers from the appendix enter at T10, so early pain is vague and around the navel. When the inflamed tip touches the parietal peritoneum, which has somatic nerves, pain localizes to McBurney point."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tear versus rupture","text":"Mallory-Weiss tears the mucosa only, so blood comes up. Boerhaave goes all the way through, so air escapes into the mediastinum and under the skin."}],"highYield":["Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells and is the precursor of esophageal adenocarcinoma.","Autoimmune gastritis in the body and fundus causes achlorhydria, high gastrin, and pernicious anemia.","Posterior duodenal ulcers bleed from the gastroduodenal artery; anterior ones perforate.","Curling ulcer follows burns from ischemia; Cushing ulcer follows brain injury from vagal acid drive.","Colonic diverticula are false diverticula at sites where vasa recta pierce the muscle.","Noncaseating granulomas and transmural inflammation point to Crohn disease.","Primary sclerosing cholangitis and toxic megacolon go with ulcerative colitis.","Microscopic colitis causes chronic watery diarrhea with normal-looking mucosa on colonoscopy."],"pitfalls":["Calcium oxalate stones in Crohn disease come from fat binding calcium in the gut, which leaves oxalate free to be absorbed; they do not mean a calcium excess.","Eosinophils appear in both reflux and eosinophilic esophagitis; the eosinophilic type has 15 or more per high-power field and involves the upper esophagus too.","Diverticulosis bleeding is painless; pain with fever means diverticulitis.","Autoimmune gastritis has high gastrin because acid is absent; a high gastrin with high acid points to Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","A fissure off the posterior midline is atypical and should raise concern for Crohn disease or another cause."],"dumps":[{"t":"Crohn versus UC table","p":"On blank paper, build a two-column table comparing Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis. Rows: location, spread, depth, gross look, microscopy, complications, extraintestinal links.","l":["Crohn: mouth to anus, terminal ileum; UC: rectum extending proximally","Crohn: skip lesions; UC: continuous","Crohn: transmural; UC: mucosa and submucosa","Crohn: cobblestone, creeping fat, strictures; UC: pseudopolyps, loss of haustra","Crohn: noncaseating granulomas; UC: crypt abscesses","Crohn: fistulas, B12 loss, oxalate stones; UC: toxic megacolon","UC more linked to primary sclerosing cholangitis","Both: colorectal cancer, erythema nodosum, uveitis, arthritis"]},{"t":"Ulcer pathways","p":"Draw the stomach and duodenum. Mark where gastric and duodenal ulcers form, trace how H. pylori and NSAIDs each cause an ulcer, and label the artery that bleeds and the sign of perforation.","l":["Gastric ulcer on the lesser curvature; duodenal ulcer in the first part of the duodenum","H. pylori urease and inflammation damage the antral mucosa","H. pylori lowers somatostatin, raising gastrin and acid","NSAIDs block COX and cut prostaglandin protection (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow)","Left gastric artery bleeds from lesser curvature ulcers","Gastroduodenal artery bleeds from posterior duodenal ulcers","Perforation shows free air under the diaphragm on an upright film"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Physiology of Gastrointestinal Disorders"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Stomach Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mFOz0C6sI_I"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PBZZ_Cu64aQ"}],"related":["GI-13-K1","GI-13-K2","GI-13-K3","GI-13-L1","GI-11"]},"GI-13-H":{"id":"GI-13-H","summary":"Congenital GI disease comes from steps in gut, face, and abdominal wall development that fail or go wrong. Exams test the defect behind each condition, the age it shows up, and the clue that names it, such as bilious versus nonbilious vomiting.","notes":[{"h":"Esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula","points":["Faulty division of the foregut by the tracheoesophageal septum.","Most common type: blind upper esophageal pouch with a fistula from the trachea to the distal esophagus.","Fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid, so polyhydramnios.","Newborn drools, chokes, coughs, and turns cyanotic with the first feeds; a nasogastric tube coils in the pouch.","Air in the stomach on x-ray means a distal fistula; a gasless abdomen means atresia without a distal fistula.","Look for VACTERL defects (vertebral, anal, cardiac, TE fistula, renal, limb)."]},{"h":"Webs, rings, and Meckel diverticulum","points":["Esophageal web: thin mucosal fold in the upper esophagus; dysphagia to solids.","Plummer-Vinson: web plus iron deficiency anemia and dysphagia; raises squamous cell carcinoma risk.","Schatzki ring: lower esophageal ring at the squamocolumnar junction; intermittent dysphagia with solid food such as meat.","Meckel: persistent vitelline duct forming a true diverticulum (all wall layers) on the antimesenteric ileum.","Ectopic gastric mucosa secretes acid and ulcerates nearby ileum, giving painless rectal bleeding in a young child.","Meckel can also act as a lead point for intussusception, twist into volvulus, or inflame and mimic appendicitis."]},{"h":"Pyloric stenosis","points":["Hypertrophy of pyloric smooth muscle narrows the outlet.","Starts at 2 to 6 weeks: nonbilious projectile vomiting after feeds, hungry infant, olive-shaped epigastric mass.","Loss of gastric HCl gives hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis; volume loss drives aldosterone, so the kidney wastes potassium (hypokalemia).","Risks: first-born males, macrolide exposure (erythromycin, azithromycin) in early infancy."]},{"h":"Biliary atresia","points":["Progressive inflammatory obliteration of the extrahepatic bile ducts.","First weeks of life: persistent conjugated jaundice, acholic (pale) stools, dark urine, hepatomegaly.","Without surgery it progresses to biliary cirrhosis; it is the most common reason for liver transplant in children."]},{"h":"Abdominal wall and rotation","points":["Gastroschisis: full-thickness defect, usually right of the umbilicus; bowel lies free with no covering sac.","Omphalocele: midline; midgut fails to return after physiologic herniation; covered by peritoneum and amnion; linked to trisomy 13 and 18 and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome.","Malrotation: incomplete 270 degree counterclockwise rotation leaves the cecum in the upper right abdomen.","Ladd bands run from the misplaced cecum across the duodenum and obstruct it; the narrow mesentery can twist (midgut volvulus), causing bilious vomiting and bowel ischemia."]},{"h":"Cleft lip and palate","points":["Cleft lip: maxillary prominence fails to fuse with the medial nasal prominence.","Cleft palate: palatine shelves fail to fuse with each other or with the primary palate.","Infant cannot seal and suck well: poor feeding, nasal regurgitation, aspiration; later ear infections and speech problems."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital GI conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Defect","Age and presentation","Key clue"],"rows":[["Esophageal atresia with distal TE fistula","Incomplete division by the tracheoesophageal septum","Newborn; drooling, choking, cyanosis with feeds","Polyhydramnios; tube coils in pouch; air in stomach"],["Esophageal web","Mucosal fold, upper esophagus","Adult (often a woman) with dysphagia to solids","Iron deficiency anemia (Plummer-Vinson)"],["Schatzki ring","Mucosal ring at the squamocolumnar junction","Adult with intermittent solid food dysphagia","Lower esophagus; food bolus impaction"],["Meckel diverticulum","Persistent vitelline duct; true diverticulum","Child under about 2 years with painless rectal bleeding","Ectopic gastric mucosa; technetium-99m pertechnetate scan"],["Pyloric stenosis","Hypertrophy of pyloric smooth muscle","2 to 6 weeks; projectile nonbilious vomiting","Olive mass; hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis"],["Biliary atresia","Obliterated extrahepatic bile ducts","First weeks; jaundice persisting past 2 weeks","Conjugated bilirubin high; acholic stools"],["Gastroschisis","Full-thickness wall defect beside the umbilicus","At birth; exposed bowel","Right of umbilicus; no covering sac"],["Omphalocele","Midgut fails to return to the abdomen","At birth; midline sac with bowel or liver","Covered by peritoneum and amnion; trisomies, Beckwith-Wiedemann"],["Malrotation with Ladd bands","Incomplete midgut rotation","Often first month; bilious vomiting","Cecum in upper right abdomen; volvulus risk"],["Cleft lip","Maxillary and medial nasal prominences do not fuse","At birth; split upper lip","Feeding difficulty"],["Cleft palate","Palatine shelves do not fuse","At birth; opening into nasal cavity","Nasal regurgitation, ear infections"]]},{"title":"Vomiting in the newborn and infant","cols":["Vomit type","Where the block is","Examples"],"rows":[["Nonbilious","Above the ampulla of Vater","Pyloric stenosis, esophageal atresia (feeds come straight back)"],["Bilious","Below the ampulla of Vater","Malrotation with volvulus or Ladd bands, duodenal atresia, intestinal atresia"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Rule of 2s (Meckel diverticulum)","text":"About 2 percent of people, 2 feet from the ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, 2 types of ectopic tissue (gastric and pancreatic), often shows up by age 2, about 2 times more common in males."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"VACTERL","text":"Vertebral defects, Anal atresia, Cardiac defects, Tracheoesophageal fistula, Renal defects, Limb defects (often radial)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sac or no sac","text":"Omphalocele has a sac and sits in the midline, and it often comes with other defects. Gastroschisis has no sac and sits beside the cord, and the bowel is usually the only problem."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Green means emergency","text":"Bile in a newborn's vomit means the block is past the ampulla. Treat it as malrotation with volvulus until proven otherwise."}],"highYield":["The most common esophageal atresia has a distal tracheoesophageal fistula, so the stomach contains air.","Polyhydramnios points to a fetus that cannot swallow, as in esophageal atresia.","Pyloric stenosis gives hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis.","Meckel diverticulum is a true diverticulum; painless bleeding comes from acid made by ectopic gastric mucosa.","Plummer-Vinson syndrome: esophageal web, iron deficiency anemia, dysphagia, and squamous cell carcinoma risk.","Biliary atresia causes persistent conjugated jaundice with pale stools in the first weeks of life.","Omphalocele is covered by peritoneum and amnion and goes with trisomies and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome."],"pitfalls":["Physiologic newborn jaundice is unconjugated and fades in the first two weeks; jaundice that stays and is conjugated needs a biliary atresia workup.","Pyloric stenosis vomit has no bile because the block is above the ampulla; bilious vomit points to malrotation or atresia below it.","Esophageal web sits high (upper esophagus); Schatzki ring sits low at the squamocolumnar junction.","Cleft lip and cleft palate come from different fusion failures and can occur alone or together."],"dumps":[{"t":"TE fistula sketch","p":"Draw the most common form of esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula. Label the blind pouch, trachea, fistula, and stomach, and write the newborn findings beside it.","l":["Upper esophagus ends in a blind pouch","Fistula connects the trachea to the distal esophagus","Air reaches the stomach through the fistula","Polyhydramnios before birth","Drooling, choking, cyanosis with feeds","Nasogastric tube coils in the pouch","Screen for VACTERL defects"]},{"t":"Pyloric stenosis acid-base","p":"Trace how repeated vomiting in pyloric stenosis leads to low chloride, low potassium, and metabolic alkalosis. Draw arrows from the stomach to the kidney.","l":["Vomiting removes HCl from the stomach","Loss of H+ raises plasma bicarbonate","Loss of Cl- gives hypochloremia","Volume loss activates renin, angiotensin, and aldosterone","Aldosterone makes the kidney reabsorb sodium and secrete potassium and H+","Hypokalemia and paradoxical acidic urine sustain the alkalosis","Infant aged 2 to 6 weeks with an olive mass"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract, section on congenital anomalies"}],"videos":[],"related":["GI-1","GI-13-F4","GI-13-C1","GI-13-K1"]},"GI-13-I":{"id":"GI-13-I","summary":"The abdominal cavity can fill with fluid (ascites), become infected (peritonitis), or scar after injury (adhesions). Exams test why fluid builds in cirrhosis, how to read the ascitic fluid, and how to tell spontaneous from secondary peritonitis.","notes":[{"h":"Ascites in cirrhosis","points":["Portal hypertension raises hydrostatic pressure in the hepatic sinusoids and splanchnic capillaries.","Low albumin from liver failure lowers plasma oncotic pressure.","Splanchnic vasodilation (nitric oxide) makes arteries feel underfilled, so renin, angiotensin, aldosterone, ADH, and sympathetic tone rise.","The kidney retains sodium and water, and the extra fluid leaks into the peritoneum.","Exam: bulging flanks, shifting dullness, fluid wave."]},{"h":"Reading ascitic fluid","points":["SAAG = serum albumin minus ascitic fluid albumin.","SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more means portal hypertension: cirrhosis, heart failure, Budd-Chiari syndrome.","SAAG under 1.1 g/dL means another cause: peritoneal carcinomatosis, tuberculous peritonitis, nephrotic syndrome, pancreatitis.","Ascitic neutrophils of 250/mm3 or more mean infection."]},{"h":"Peritonitis","points":["Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP): infected cirrhotic ascites with no surgical source; gut bacteria cross the wall.","SBP organisms: usually one gram-negative enteric rod, E. coli or Klebsiella.","Low ascitic protein (under about 1.5 g/dL) means little complement and opsonin, which raises SBP risk.","SBP can be subtle: fever, mild diffuse tenderness, new confusion, or worsening kidney function.","Secondary peritonitis: perforated ulcer, appendix, or diverticulum; polymicrobial with anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis.","Secondary peritonitis gives a rigid, board-like abdomen, rebound, guarding, and free air on imaging."]},{"h":"Adhesions","points":["Surgery, infection, or blood injures the mesothelium, and a fibrin exudate forms.","Injured mesothelium makes less plasminogen activator, so fibrin persists and fibroblasts turn it into fibrous bands.","Main complication: small bowel obstruction with colicky pain, vomiting, distension, and no flatus.","Adhesions are the most common cause of small bowel obstruction in adults; a closed loop can strangulate."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Conditions of the abdominal cavity","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Ascites","Cirrhosis, heart failure, cancer, nephrotic syndrome","Portal hypertension, low albumin, renal sodium retention","Bulging flanks, shifting dullness, fluid wave","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, hepatorenal syndrome, hernia"],["Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Cirrhotic ascites with low protein","Bacterial translocation from the gut","Fever, mild tenderness, confusion; neutrophils 250/mm3 or more","Hepatorenal syndrome, sepsis, death"],["Secondary peritonitis","Perforated viscus, ruptured appendix or diverticulum","Gut contents spill into the peritoneum; polymicrobial","Rigid abdomen, rebound, free air","Abscess, sepsis, adhesions"],["Adhesions","Prior surgery, peritonitis, endometriosis","Persistent fibrin organized into fibrous bands","Often silent until obstruction","Small bowel obstruction, strangulation, female infertility"]]},{"title":"Serum-ascites albumin gradient","cols":["SAAG","Meaning","Causes"],"rows":[["1.1 g/dL or more","Portal hypertension present","Cirrhosis, heart failure, Budd-Chiari syndrome, massive liver metastases"],["Under 1.1 g/dL","Portal pressure normal","Peritoneal carcinomatosis, tuberculous peritonitis, nephrotic syndrome, pancreatitis"]]},{"title":"Spontaneous versus secondary peritonitis","cols":["Feature","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Secondary peritonitis"],"rows":[["Setting","Cirrhosis with ascites","Perforated or ruptured abdominal organ"],["Organisms","One organism, usually E. coli or Klebsiella","Several, including anaerobes"],["Abdomen","Soft or mildly tender","Rigid with rebound and guarding"],["Imaging","No free air","Free air if a viscus perforated"],["Treatment","Antibiotics","Surgery plus antibiotics"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"High gradient, high pressure","text":"A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more means fluid was pushed out by high portal pressure. A low SAAG means something on the peritoneum itself, such as tumor or infection, is leaking protein-rich fluid."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One bug or many","text":"One gram-negative rod in ascitic fluid fits spontaneous bacterial peritonitis. Many organisms, including anaerobes, mean a hole in the bowel."}],"highYield":["A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more means portal hypertension.","Ascitic neutrophils of 250/mm3 or more diagnose spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.","E. coli and Klebsiella are the usual causes of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.","Splanchnic vasodilation and RAAS activation make the kidney retain sodium in cirrhosis.","Secondary peritonitis is polymicrobial and causes a rigid abdomen.","Adhesions are the leading cause of small bowel obstruction in adults."],"pitfalls":["Cirrhosis has total body sodium overload even when serum sodium is low; the low serum sodium reflects ADH-driven water retention.","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis may show no rigidity; new confusion or kidney injury in a cirrhotic patient is enough to tap the ascites.","Heart failure ascites also has a high SAAG; a high ascitic protein helps separate it from cirrhosis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Ascites flowchart","p":"Draw a flowchart showing how cirrhosis leads to ascites. Start at the scarred liver and end at fluid in the peritoneal cavity.","l":["Scarred liver raises resistance to portal flow","Portal hypertension raises sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure","Low albumin lowers plasma oncotic pressure","Nitric oxide causes splanchnic vasodilation","Effective arterial underfilling activates RAAS, ADH, and sympathetic nerves","Kidney retains sodium and water","Fluid leaks into the peritoneum; SAAG 1.1 g/dL or more"]},{"t":"Peritonitis comparison","p":"Make a table comparing spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and secondary peritonitis. Rows: setting, organisms, exam, fluid findings, imaging, treatment.","l":["SBP: cirrhotic ascites; secondary: perforated organ","SBP: one gram-negative rod (E. coli, Klebsiella); secondary: polymicrobial with anaerobes","SBP: mild tenderness, fever, confusion; secondary: rigid abdomen","Both: neutrophils 250/mm3 or more in fluid","Secondary: free air on imaging","SBP: antibiotics; secondary: surgery plus antibiotics"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Liver Disease, section on cirrhosis and ascites"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"section on portal hypertension and ascites"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"section on the peritoneal cavity"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Ascites | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GdGEiVDvCtU"}],"related":["GI-13-C5","GI-13-C2","GI-4","GI-13-F3"]},"GI-13-J":{"id":"GI-13-J","summary":"GI vascular disease covers dilated veins (varices, hemorrhoids), dilated mucosal vessels (angiodysplasia), and lost blood supply (mesenteric ischemia, necrotizing enterocolitis). Exams test the vessel involved, where it bleeds or infarcts, and the setting that predicts it.","notes":[{"h":"Esophageal varices","points":["Portal hypertension opens portosystemic shunts.","Left gastric vein (portal) connects to esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system (systemic).","Distal esophageal submucosal veins dilate and can rupture.","Rupture causes painless, massive hematemesis, a major cause of death in cirrhosis.","Low platelets from splenic sequestration and low clotting factors make bleeding worse."]},{"h":"Hemorrhoids","points":["Dilated, sliding anal vascular cushions.","Internal: above the pectinate line, visceral nerves, so painless bright red bleeding and prolapse.","External: below the pectinate line, somatic nerves (inferior rectal), so painful when thrombosed.","Risks: straining, constipation, low-fiber diet, pregnancy, prolonged sitting."]},{"h":"Mesenteric ischemia and infarction","points":["Acute: embolus from atrial fibrillation or mural thrombus lodges in the superior mesenteric artery (SMA).","Pain out of proportion to the exam, then raised lactate, metabolic acidosis, bloody stool, and peritonitis.","Other acute causes: SMA thrombosis on atherosclerosis, low-flow states (shock), mesenteric vein thrombosis.","Mucosa dies first; full-thickness infarction leads to perforation and sepsis.","Chronic: atherosclerosis of the mesenteric arteries; pain soon after meals, food fear, weight loss.","Ischemic colitis: low flow to watershed zones (splenic flexure, rectosigmoid junction); older adult with crampy pain then bloody diarrhea."]},{"h":"Angiodysplasia","points":["Dilated, tortuous submucosal vessels, mostly in the cecum and right colon of adults over 60.","High wall tension in the wide cecum partly blocks submucosal veins during contraction, so they dilate over years.","Painless, intermittent lower GI bleeding and iron deficiency anemia.","Heyde syndrome: aortic stenosis shear breaks up large von Willebrand factor multimers, so angiodysplasia bleeds more.","Also linked to end-stage kidney disease."]},{"h":"Necrotizing enterocolitis","points":["Premature infant, usually after enteral (often formula) feeds start; breast milk lowers risk.","Immature mucosa, poor blood flow, and bacterial invasion cause mucosal necrosis, often terminal ileum and colon.","Feeding intolerance, abdominal distension, bloody stools.","X-ray: pneumatosis intestinalis (gas in the bowel wall), portal venous gas, free air if perforated."]}],"charts":[{"title":"GI vascular diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Esophageal varices","Portal hypertension, mostly cirrhosis","Left gastric vein backs up into esophageal veins draining to the azygos system","Painless massive hematemesis","Hemorrhagic shock, death, hepatic encephalopathy after bleeding"],["Hemorrhoids","Straining, constipation, low fiber, pregnancy","Anal vascular cushions dilate and slide down","Internal: painless bright red bleeding. External: painful lump when thrombosed","Prolapse, thrombosis, iron deficiency (rare)"],["Acute mesenteric ischemia","Atrial fibrillation, recent MI, atherosclerosis, hypercoagulable state, shock","Embolus or thrombus blocks the SMA","Pain out of proportion to exam, raised lactate","Transmural infarction, perforation, sepsis"],["Chronic mesenteric ischemia","Atherosclerosis of mesenteric arteries","Blood flow cannot rise after meals","Postprandial pain, food fear, weight loss","Acute thrombosis and infarction"],["Ischemic colitis","Older age, hypotension, vascular disease","Low flow to watershed zones","Crampy pain then bloody diarrhea, splenic flexure or rectosigmoid","Stricture, gangrene"],["Angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia)","Age over 60, aortic stenosis, end-stage kidney disease","Wall tension slowly dilates submucosal veins and capillaries","Painless intermittent bleeding from the cecum and right colon","Iron deficiency anemia, recurrent bleeding"],["Necrotizing enterocolitis","Prematurity, formula feeds","Mucosal ischemia plus bacterial invasion","Distension, bloody stools, pneumatosis intestinalis","Perforation, sepsis, short bowel"]]},{"title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Result in portal hypertension"],"rows":[["Distal esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins to the azygos system","Esophageal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial and inferior epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices"]]},{"title":"Internal versus external hemorrhoids","cols":["Feature","Internal","External"],"rows":[["Position","Above the pectinate line","Below the pectinate line"],["Covering","Rectal and transitional mucosa","Squamous anoderm and skin"],["Nerve supply","Visceral (autonomic)","Somatic (inferior rectal nerve)"],["Pain","Painless","Painful when thrombosed"],["Main sign","Bright red bleeding, prolapse","Tender bluish perianal lump"],["Venous drainage","Superior rectal vein to the portal system","Inferior rectal vein to the internal iliac (systemic)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Pectinate line sorts pain","text":"Above the line has visceral nerves, so internal hemorrhoids do not hurt. Below the line has somatic nerves, so external hemorrhoids hurt when they clot."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Watershed zones of the colon","text":"The splenic flexure sits between SMA and IMA territory, and the rectosigmoid sits between IMA and internal iliac territory. Low flow injures these edges first."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Heyde: murmur plus bleeding","text":"An older patient with an aortic stenosis murmur and iron deficiency from painless GI bleeding has Heyde syndrome until proven otherwise."}],"highYield":["Esophageal varices form where the left gastric vein meets esophageal veins draining to the azygos system.","Variceal bleeding is painless massive hematemesis in a patient with cirrhosis.","Internal hemorrhoids bleed painlessly; thrombosed external hemorrhoids hurt.","Acute mesenteric ischemia gives pain out of proportion to the exam; atrial fibrillation is the classic embolic source.","Chronic mesenteric ischemia gives postprandial pain and weight loss from food fear.","Angiodysplasia is in the cecum and right colon and links to aortic stenosis (Heyde syndrome).","Pneumatosis intestinalis in a premature infant means necrotizing enterocolitis."],"pitfalls":["Hemorrhoids are not caused by portal hypertension; portal hypertension makes anorectal varices, a different lesion.","Mallory-Weiss tears also cause hematemesis after retching, but the bleed is usually smaller and the patient may have no liver disease.","Early acute mesenteric ischemia has a soft abdomen; waiting for peritoneal signs means the bowel is already dying.","Ischemic colitis affects the colon watershed areas and usually recovers; acute mesenteric ischemia involves the SMA and small bowel and is more lethal."],"dumps":[{"t":"Portal-systemic map","p":"Draw the portal vein and its tributaries. Mark the three main portosystemic anastomoses and label what forms at each one in portal hypertension.","l":["Portal vein forms from the superior mesenteric and splenic veins","Inferior mesenteric vein usually drains to the splenic vein","Left gastric to esophageal veins (azygos): esophageal varices","Paraumbilical to epigastric veins: caput medusae","Superior rectal to middle and inferior rectal veins: anorectal varices","Variceal rupture causes painless massive hematemesis"]},{"t":"Gut ischemia table","p":"Build a table comparing acute mesenteric ischemia, chronic mesenteric ischemia, ischemic colitis, and necrotizing enterocolitis. Columns: patient, cause, key symptom, key test finding.","l":["Acute: older adult with atrial fibrillation; SMA embolus; pain out of proportion; raised lactate","Chronic: atherosclerosis; postprandial pain and food fear; weight loss","Ischemic colitis: low flow to watershed zones; crampy pain then bloody diarrhea","Splenic flexure and rectosigmoid are the watershed zones","Necrotizing enterocolitis: premature infant after feeds; bloody stools","Necrotizing enterocolitis: pneumatosis intestinalis on x-ray"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract, section on vascular disorders of the bowel"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, section on the hepatic portal venous system"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum, section on the anal canal"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Intestinal Ischemia | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y6k7OEtoSXQ"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"GI Bleed | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zERmfobGF70"}],"related":["GI-5","GI-13-C5","GI-13-G5","GI-13-I1"]},"GI-13-K":{"id":"GI-13-K","summary":"GI neoplasms run from the mouth and salivary glands to the anus, plus the liver, pancreas, and bile ducts. Exams test each tumor's risk factors and precursor lesion, where it grows, its marker, and the sign that shows it has spread.","notes":[{"h":"Esophagus and stomach","points":["Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma: upper and middle third; alcohol, tobacco, achalasia, caustic injury, Plummer-Vinson.","Esophageal adenocarcinoma: distal third; GERD, Barrett esophagus, obesity.","Both cause progressive dysphagia, solids then liquids, with weight loss.","Intestinal-type gastric cancer: H. pylori, nitrosamines in smoked foods, atrophic gastritis; forms glands.","Diffuse-type gastric cancer: signet ring cells, thick rigid stomach (linitis plastica), loss of E-cadherin (CDH1).","Spread: Virchow node (left supraclavicular), Sister Mary Joseph nodule (umbilicus), Krukenberg tumor (ovaries).","MALT lymphoma: extranodal marginal zone B cell lymphoma driven by H. pylori; early lesions can regress when H. pylori is cleared.","GIST: KIT (CD117) mutant tumor of the interstitial cells of Cajal, most often in the stomach."]},{"h":"Neuroendocrine tumors of the gut","points":["Zollinger-Ellison: gastrinoma in the duodenum or pancreas; about a quarter occur in MEN1.","Gastrin drives acid: multiple, refractory, or distal (jejunal) ulcers.","Acid inactivates pancreatic lipase and precipitates bile acids, so diarrhea and steatorrhea.","Secretin normally lowers gastrin; in gastrinoma it paradoxically raises it.","Small bowel carcinoid makes serotonin; the liver breaks it down, so carcinoid syndrome appears mainly after liver metastasis.","Carcinoid syndrome: flushing, diarrhea, wheezing, right-sided valve fibrosis; raised urinary 5-HIAA."]},{"h":"Liver","points":["Hepatocellular carcinoma risks: chronic HBV (even without cirrhosis), HCV, alcohol, hemochromatosis, aflatoxin B1 (TP53 mutation).","Findings: raised alpha-fetoprotein, enlarging liver, weight loss; spreads through blood vessels (portal and hepatic veins).","Metastases are the most common malignancy in the liver and on the peritoneum.","Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor.","Hepatocellular adenoma: oral contraceptive or anabolic steroid use; can rupture and bleed."]},{"h":"Mouth and salivary glands","points":["Oral leukoplakia: white plaque that does not scrape off; may hide dysplasia, so biopsy.","Oral squamous cell carcinoma: tobacco and alcohol. Oropharyngeal carcinoma: HPV-16.","Pleomorphic adenoma: most common salivary tumor, usually parotid, epithelial plus stromal tissue; recurs if incompletely removed.","Warthin tumor: parotid, smokers, cysts with lymphoid stroma. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma: most common salivary malignancy."]},{"h":"Pancreas, bile ducts, and anus","points":["Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma: KRAS mutation; risks smoking and chronic pancreatitis.","Head tumors: painless jaundice with a palpable nontender gallbladder (Courvoisier sign); also migratory thrombophlebitis, weight loss, raised CA 19-9.","Cholangiocarcinoma: primary sclerosing cholangitis, liver flukes such as Clonorchis sinensis; painless obstructive jaundice.","Gallbladder adenocarcinoma: gallstones. Anal squamous cell carcinoma: below the pectinate line, HPV-related."]},{"h":"Colon","points":["Right-sided cancer: bulky mass, occult bleeding, iron deficiency anemia.","Left-sided cancer: napkin-ring narrowing, change in bowel habit, obstruction, visible blood.","Risks: age, IBD, familial adenomatous polyposis (APC), Lynch syndrome (mismatch repair genes).","CEA tracks recurrence after surgery; it is not a screening test.","Adenoma malignancy risk rises with size and villous component."]}],"charts":[{"title":"GI neoplasms by site","cols":["Site","Main tumors","Key risk factors","Classic findings","Marker or spread"],"rows":[["Esophageal","Squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma","SCC: alcohol, tobacco, achalasia, caustics. Adeno: GERD, Barrett, obesity","Progressive dysphagia, weight loss","Local invasion; lymph nodes"],["Gastric","Adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma, GIST","H. pylori, nitrosamines, atrophic gastritis, CDH1 loss, KIT mutation","Early satiety, weight loss, epigastric mass","Virchow node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule, Krukenberg tumor"],["Intestinal (gastrinoma, carcinoid)","Gastrinoma, carcinoid (neuroendocrine) tumor","MEN1 for gastrinoma","Refractory ulcers and diarrhea; flushing, wheeze, diarrhea","Paradoxical gastrin rise with secretin; urinary 5-HIAA"],["Liver","Metastases, hepatocellular carcinoma, hemangioma, adenoma","HBV, HCV, alcohol, hemochromatosis, aflatoxin; oral contraceptives for adenoma","Hepatomegaly, weight loss, decompensated cirrhosis","Alpha-fetoprotein; hematogenous spread"],["Oral (leukoplakia)","Squamous cell carcinoma arising from leukoplakia","Tobacco, alcohol; HPV-16 for oropharynx","White plaque that does not scrape off; nonhealing ulcer","Cervical lymph nodes"],["Pancreas","Ductal adenocarcinoma","Smoking, chronic pancreatitis, KRAS mutation","Painless jaundice, Courvoisier sign, back pain, weight loss","CA 19-9; migratory thrombophlebitis"],["Colorectal","Adenocarcinoma from adenomas or serrated polyps","Age, IBD, FAP, Lynch syndrome","Right: anemia. Left: obstruction, change in bowel habit","CEA for recurrence; liver metastases"]]},{"title":"Esophageal cancer types","cols":["Feature","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"rows":[["Location","Upper and middle third","Distal third"],["Precursor","Squamous dysplasia","Barrett esophagus"],["Risks","Alcohol, tobacco, hot liquids, achalasia, caustic strictures, Plummer-Vinson","Chronic GERD, obesity, tobacco"],["Where most common","Worldwide","United States and other Western countries"]]},{"title":"Gastric tumors","cols":["Tumor","Cell or origin","Driver","Key feature"],"rows":[["Intestinal-type adenocarcinoma","Gastric epithelium forming glands","H. pylori, nitrosamines, atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia","Ulcerated mass, often lesser curvature"],["Diffuse-type adenocarcinoma","Signet ring cells infiltrating the wall","Loss of E-cadherin (CDH1)","Linitis plastica (leather-bottle stomach)"],["MALT lymphoma","Marginal zone B cells","Chronic H. pylori antigen stimulation","May regress after H. pylori is cleared"],["GIST","Interstitial cells of Cajal","Activating KIT (CD117) mutation","Submucosal mass, stomach most often"]]},{"title":"Colonic polyps","cols":["Polyp","Features","Malignant potential"],"rows":[["Hyperplastic","Most common non-neoplastic polyp; small, rectosigmoid; serrated crypts","Very low"],["Tubular adenoma","Most common adenoma; tubular glands","Present; lowest of the adenomas"],["Tubulovillous adenoma","Mixed tubular and villous parts","Intermediate"],["Villous adenoma","Finger-like villi; can secrete mucus and potassium","Highest of the adenomas; rises with size"],["Sessile serrated lesion","Flat, right colon; BRAF mutation and CpG island methylation","Yes, through the serrated pathway"],["Juvenile polyp","Hamartoma; child, rectum, bleeding","Solitary: very low; juvenile polyposis syndrome: raised"],["Peutz-Jeghers polyp","Hamartoma; STK11; lip and mouth hyperpigmentation; small bowel intussusception","Raised risk of GI and other cancers"]]},{"title":"Liver masses","cols":["Lesion","Who gets it","Key features"],"rows":[["Metastases","Patients with colon, breast, lung, or pancreatic cancer","Most common liver malignancy; multiple nodules"],["Hepatocellular carcinoma","Chronic HBV or HCV, cirrhosis, aflatoxin exposure","Raised alpha-fetoprotein; invades veins"],["Cavernous hemangioma","Any adult","Most common benign tumor; avoid needle biopsy (bleeding)"],["Hepatocellular adenoma","Young women on oral contraceptives; anabolic steroid users","Can rupture and bleed, especially in pregnancy"]]},{"title":"White lesions of the mouth","cols":["Lesion","Cause","Scrapes off?","Clue"],"rows":[["Leukoplakia","Tobacco, alcohol; may hide dysplasia","No","Biopsy to exclude dysplasia or carcinoma"],["Oral candidiasis","Candida albicans","Yes, leaving a red base","Infants, antibiotics, inhaled steroids, HIV"],["Hairy leukoplakia","Epstein-Barr virus","No","Lateral tongue, immunosuppression, not premalignant"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Squamous up top, adeno at the bottom","text":"Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma sits high where the lining is squamous and the irritants pass. Adenocarcinoma sits low where acid makes Barrett glandular lining."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Why carcinoid needs the liver","text":"Gut serotonin goes to the liver through the portal vein and is broken down there. Once tumor is in the liver, serotonin reaches the systemic circulation, and the lungs clear it before the left heart, so the right-sided valves scar."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Right bleeds, left blocks","text":"The right colon is wide with liquid stool, so tumors grow large and bleed slowly. The left colon is narrow with formed stool, so tumors obstruct."}],"highYield":["Barrett esophagus leads to distal esophageal adenocarcinoma; alcohol and tobacco lead to upper and middle squamous cell carcinoma.","Signet ring cells and linitis plastica mean diffuse gastric cancer with CDH1 loss.","Gastric MALT lymphoma is driven by H. pylori; GIST carries a KIT (CD117) mutation.","Gastrinoma raises gastrin after secretin and links to MEN1.","Carcinoid syndrome needs liver metastasis and raises urinary 5-HIAA.","Aflatoxin B1 causes TP53 mutation and hepatocellular carcinoma; HBV can cause it without cirrhosis.","Courvoisier sign: painless jaundice with a palpable gallbladder points to a tumor blocking the distal bile duct, classically a pancreatic head cancer.","Villous adenomas carry the highest malignant risk of colon adenomas."],"pitfalls":["Metastases outnumber primary tumors in the liver; a liver mass in a patient with colon cancer is a metastasis until proven otherwise.","Leukoplakia and candidiasis both look white; candidiasis scrapes off and leukoplakia does not.","CEA helps follow colon cancer after surgery and should not be used to screen.","Hyperplastic polyps are usually harmless; sessile serrated lesions look similar but are premalignant.","Warthin tumor is benign and occurs in smokers; mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the most common salivary malignancy."],"dumps":[{"t":"GI tumor map","p":"Draw the GI tract from mouth to anus with the liver and pancreas. At each site, write the main tumor, its biggest risk factor, and one sign or marker.","l":["Mouth: squamous cell carcinoma, tobacco and alcohol; leukoplakia precursor","Upper esophagus: squamous cell carcinoma; distal esophagus: adenocarcinoma from Barrett","Stomach: adenocarcinoma with H. pylori; Virchow node, Krukenberg tumor","Small bowel: carcinoid with 5-HIAA after liver metastasis","Liver: hepatocellular carcinoma with HBV or HCV; alpha-fetoprotein","Pancreatic head: adenocarcinoma with KRAS; Courvoisier sign, CA 19-9","Colon: adenocarcinoma from adenomas; CEA for recurrence","Anus below the pectinate line: squamous cell carcinoma with HPV"]},{"t":"Polyp to cancer","p":"Draw a colonic polyp progressing to invasive cancer. Label each step of the adenoma-carcinoma sequence and list which polyps carry the most risk.","l":["Loss of APC starts the adenoma","KRAS mutation helps the adenoma grow","TP53 loss allows invasive carcinoma","Risk rises with size and villous component","Villous adenoma has the highest adenoma risk; tubular the lowest","Sessile serrated lesions follow a separate pathway with BRAF and methylation","Lynch syndrome tumors have mismatch repair loss and microsatellite instability"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"section on hepatic neoplasms"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Pancreas"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Neoplasia"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Esophageal and Gastric Cancer | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ahvwhYMk3Ng"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Colorectal Cancer | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HjIQy8FycKA"}],"related":["GI-10","GI-13-G2","GI-13-G7","GI-13-L7","GI-13-C2"]},"GI-13-L":{"id":"GI-13-L","summary":"Infectious GI diseases include diarrheal illness, infections of the mouth and esophagus, viral hepatitis, and liver abscess. Exams link each organism to its setting, the way it causes damage, and the complication it is known for.","notes":[{"h":"Clostridioides difficile colitis","points":["Antibiotics (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins) wipe out normal colonic flora.","Spore-forming gram-positive anaerobe; spores survive alcohol hand rubs, so wash with soap and water.","Toxin A (enterotoxin) draws fluid and neutrophils; toxin B (cytotoxin) breaks down the actin cytoskeleton.","Yellow-white pseudomembranes of fibrin, mucus, and neutrophils; watery diarrhea, cramps, high white count.","Diagnosis: stool toxin or toxin gene (PCR) testing. Severe cases cause toxic megacolon."]},{"h":"Bloody and watery diarrhea","points":["Bloody (invasive or cytotoxic): Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, EHEC, Entamoeba histolytica.","EHEC O157:H7 (undercooked beef): Shiga toxin damages renal endothelium, causing hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Campylobacter (poultry) can trigger Guillain-Barre syndrome by molecular mimicry.","Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, and Yersinia can trigger HLA-B27 reactive arthritis.","Watery: rotavirus (unvaccinated infants), norovirus (cruise ships, institutions), ETEC (traveler's diarrhea).","Cholera toxin keeps Gs on, cAMP rises, and CFTR pumps chloride and water out: rice-water stool.","Bacillus cereus emetic toxin is preformed in reheated rice and causes vomiting within hours."]},{"h":"Mouth and esophagus","points":["Plaque biofilm at the gum line causes gingivitis: red, swollen gums that bleed; reversible.","Periodontitis: Porphyromonas gingivalis and host inflammation destroy periodontal ligament and alveolar bone.","Streptococcus mutans ferments sugar to acid, dissolving enamel (caries) and leading to tooth abscess.","Oral thrush: Candida albicans; white plaques that scrape off; infants, inhaled steroids, antibiotics, diabetes, HIV.","Herpetic gingivostomatitis (primary HSV-1): fever with vesicles on gums and lips. Herpangina (coxsackie A): vesicles on the soft palate and posterior pharynx.","Hand-foot-and-mouth disease: coxsackie A16, mouth ulcers plus palm and sole vesicles. Aphthous ulcers: Crohn disease, Behcet disease.","Infectious esophagitis in immunosuppression: Candida (white plaques), HSV-1 (punched-out ulcers), CMV (linear ulcers)."]},{"h":"Viral hepatitis and liver abscess","points":["HBV chronic: most infected neonates, few infected adults. HCV chronic: most infected adults.","HBV and HCV lead to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma; HBV can cause carcinoma without cirrhosis.","HCV causes mixed cryoglobulinemia (vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, purpura).","Pyogenic liver abscess: biliary or portal spread, often E. coli or Klebsiella.","Amebic liver abscess: Entamoeba histolytica, travel history, anchovy paste fluid."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infectious GI diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Main organisms","Setting or risk","Key mechanism or finding","Complications"],"rows":[["Enterocolitis","C. difficile, Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, EHEC, Entamoeba","Antibiotics, contaminated food or water","Toxins or invasion damage the colonic mucosa; bloody or watery diarrhea","Toxic megacolon, HUS, Guillain-Barre, reactive arthritis"],["Esophagitis","Candida, HSV-1, CMV","HIV, transplant, chemotherapy, steroids","White plaques, punched-out ulcers, or linear ulcers","Odynophagia, poor intake, bleeding"],["Gastroenteritis","Rotavirus, norovirus, ETEC, Vibrio cholerae, Bacillus cereus","Infants, outbreaks, travel, bad water, reheated rice","Watery diarrhea from toxins or villous injury","Dehydration, electrolyte loss"],["Gingivitis and periodontitis","Plaque bacteria, Porphyromonas gingivalis; Streptococcus mutans for caries","Poor oral hygiene, smoking, diabetes","Biofilm inflammation; bone and ligament loss","Tooth loss, tooth abscess"],["Oral thrush","Candida albicans","Infants, inhaled steroids, antibiotics, diabetes, HIV","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae; plaques scrape off","Esophageal spread in immunosuppression"],["Stomatitis","HSV-1, coxsackie A, coxsackie A16; noninfectious aphthae","Young children; Crohn or Behcet disease for aphthae","Vesicles and painful ulcers","Dehydration from painful swallowing; HSV latency"],["Viral hepatitis","HAV, HBV, HCV, HDV, HEV","Fecal-oral (A, E); blood, sex, birth (B, C, D)","Immune attack on infected hepatocytes","Chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, carcinoma, cryoglobulinemia"]]},{"title":"Hepatitis B serology","cols":["Stage","HBsAg","Anti-HBs","Anti-HBc"],"rows":[["Acute infection","Positive","Negative","IgM"],["Window period","Negative","Negative","IgM"],["Chronic infection","Positive for over 6 months","Negative","IgG"],["Resolved infection","Negative","Positive","IgG"],["Vaccinated","Negative","Positive","Negative"]]},{"title":"Bloody diarrhea organisms","cols":["Organism","Source","Key feature","Known complication"],"rows":[["Shigella","Person to person, very low infectious dose","Invades M cells, spreads cell to cell","Reactive arthritis, seizures in children"],["Campylobacter jejuni","Undercooked poultry, raw milk","Curved gram-negative rod","Guillain-Barre syndrome, reactive arthritis"],["Nontyphoidal Salmonella","Poultry, eggs, reptiles","Invades mucosa; flagellated","Bacteremia, osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease"],["EHEC O157:H7","Undercooked ground beef, produce","Shiga toxin; usually little fever","Hemolytic uremic syndrome"],["Entamoeba histolytica","Cysts in contaminated water","Flask-shaped ulcers; trophozoites with ingested red cells","Amebic liver abscess"]]},{"title":"Watery gastroenteritis by setting","cols":["Organism","Setting","Mechanism or clue"],"rows":[["Rotavirus","Unvaccinated infants and young children","Destroys villus tip cells; winter outbreaks"],["Norovirus","Cruise ships, nursing homes, schools","Very low infectious dose; vomiting prominent"],["ETEC","Traveler's diarrhea","Heat-labile toxin raises cAMP; heat-stable toxin raises cGMP"],["Vibrio cholerae","Contaminated water","Toxin keeps Gs active; rice-water stool"],["Bacillus cereus","Reheated fried rice","Preformed emetic toxin; vomiting within 1 to 6 hours"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Read HBV serology in order","text":"First ask about HBsAg (virus present now). Then anti-HBc (ever infected; IgM recent, IgG old). Then anti-HBs (immune). Anti-HBs alone means vaccine; IgM anti-HBc alone means window period."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Scrape test","text":"White plaque that wipes off is thrush. White plaque that stays is leukoplakia or hairy leukoplakia."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Which way the chronic odds run","text":"HBV becomes chronic in most newborns and few adults because the newborn immune system tolerates the virus. HCV becomes chronic in most adults because its envelope proteins keep changing and escape antibodies."}],"highYield":["Clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, and cephalosporins predispose to C. difficile colitis; diagnose by stool toxin or toxin gene testing.","EHEC O157:H7 leads to hemolytic uremic syndrome; Campylobacter leads to Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Rice-water stool from contaminated water means Vibrio cholerae.","IgM anti-HBc alone means the hepatitis B window period; anti-HBs alone means vaccination.","HCV becomes chronic in most adults and causes mixed cryoglobulinemia.","CMV esophagitis has linear ulcers and inclusions in endothelial and stromal cells; HSV has punched-out ulcers with intranuclear inclusions in squamous cells.","Herpangina (coxsackie A) affects the posterior mouth; primary HSV-1 gingivostomatitis affects the gums and lips.","Amebic liver abscess fluid looks like anchovy paste."],"pitfalls":["Alcohol hand gel does not kill C. difficile spores; soap and water does.","Anti-HBs alone means vaccine immunity; anti-HBs with IgG anti-HBc means past infection that cleared.","Gingivitis is reversible; periodontitis means bone and ligament are already lost.","Aphthous ulcers are not preceded by vesicles; vesicles that break into ulcers point to HSV or coxsackie virus.","A single gram-negative rod in a liver abscess with biliary disease suggests pyogenic abscess; a young traveler with a single right-lobe abscess suggests amebic abscess."],"dumps":[{"t":"HBV serology timeline","p":"Draw a timeline of acute hepatitis B that resolves. Plot HBsAg, IgM anti-HBc, IgG anti-HBc, and anti-HBs, and mark the window period.","l":["HBsAg rises first and falls as the infection clears","IgM anti-HBc appears during acute illness","Window period: HBsAg gone, anti-HBs not yet present, IgM anti-HBc positive","Anti-HBs appears after HBsAg is gone and gives immunity","IgG anti-HBc stays for life after infection","Chronic infection: HBsAg persists over 6 months with IgG anti-HBc","Vaccination gives anti-HBs only"]},{"t":"Diarrhea organism table","p":"Make a table of diarrhea organisms with columns for organism, source, bloody or watery, mechanism, and known complication. Include C. difficile, Shigella, Campylobacter, EHEC, cholera, ETEC, rotavirus, and norovirus.","l":["C. difficile: antibiotics; watery; toxins A and B; toxic megacolon","Shigella: low infectious dose; bloody; invasion; reactive arthritis","Campylobacter: poultry; bloody; invasion; Guillain-Barre syndrome","EHEC: undercooked beef; bloody; Shiga toxin; hemolytic uremic syndrome","Cholera: contaminated water; watery; cAMP rise; severe dehydration","ETEC: travelers; watery; heat-labile and heat-stable toxins","Rotavirus: infants; watery; villus injury","Norovirus: outbreaks; watery with vomiting"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Hepatitis Viruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric Gram-Negative Rods (Enterobacteriaceae)"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract, section on infectious enterocolitis"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Diarrhea | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7ylgYbLCEA0"}],"related":["GI-12","GI-11","GI-13-K4","GI-13-G9","GI-13-C2"]},"HE-1":{"id":"HE-1","summary":"How blood cells look on a smear, where they come from, and how they mature. Exam questions ask you to name a cell from its nucleus and granules, trace a lineage back to its progenitor, read a left shift or a high reticulocyte count, and explain why marrow failure or ineffective erythropoiesis enlarges the spleen.","notes":[{"h":"Reading a Wright-stained smear","points":["Red cell: about 7 to 8 µm, no nucleus, pink biconcave disc with central pallor about one third of its width.","Segmented neutrophil: 3 to 5 lobes joined by thin strands, fine pale pink to lilac granules.","Band: curved, horseshoe-shaped nucleus with no true segments; an immature neutrophil.","Eosinophil: usually two lobes, large bright orange-red granules.","Basophil: dark blue-purple granules that hide the nucleus; the rarest white cell.","Small lymphocyte: about the size of a red cell, round dark nucleus, thin rim of pale blue cytoplasm.","Monocyte: the largest white cell, folded or kidney-shaped nucleus, gray-blue cytoplasm, sometimes vacuoles.","Platelets: small (about 2 to 3 µm) purple-granular fragments with no nucleus."]},{"h":"Lineages from the stem cell","points":["The hematopoietic stem cell (CD34 positive) self-renews and gives two progenitors: common myeloid and common lymphoid.","Common myeloid progenitor: red cells, megakaryocytes (platelets), neutrophils, monocytes (which become tissue macrophages), eosinophils, basophils, and mast cells.","Common lymphoid progenitor: B cells, T cells (finish maturing in the thymus), and NK cells."]},{"h":"Where blood is made","points":["Yolk sac blood islands from about week 3, then the liver (main fetal site from about week 6), with a smaller contribution from the spleen.","Bone marrow takes over in the second half of gestation and is the main site at birth.","In adults, active red marrow sits mainly in the axial skeleton (vertebrae, sternum, ribs, pelvis, skull) and proximal femur and humerus.","Extramedullary hematopoiesis returns to the liver and spleen when the marrow fails or cannot keep up, causing hepatosplenomegaly.","Myelofibrosis: fibrotic marrow, massive splenomegaly, teardrop cells, and a leukoerythroblastic smear (nucleated red cells plus immature granulocytes).","Beta thalassemia major: ineffective erythropoiesis expands the marrow (crew-cut skull, prominent cheekbones) and drives splenic and hepatic blood formation."]},{"h":"Red cell maturation","points":["Proerythroblast, basophilic erythroblast, polychromatophilic erythroblast, orthochromatic erythroblast, reticulocyte, mature red cell.","Along the way the cell shrinks, the nucleus condenses and is extruded, and hemoglobin builds up, so cytoplasm turns from blue (ribosomes) to pink (hemoglobin).","The reticulocyte has no nucleus but keeps ribosomal RNA, which shows as a blue-gray tint on Wright stain and a reticular network with supravital stains.","Reticulocytes lose their RNA in about one day in the blood; the normal count is about 0.5 to 1.5%.","A high reticulocyte count means the marrow is answering anemia (hemolysis or blood loss); a low count with anemia means underproduction."]},{"h":"Granulocyte maturation and left shift","points":["Myeloblast, promyelocyte, myelocyte, metamyelocyte, band, segmented neutrophil.","Promyelocytes make the primary (azurophilic) granules with myeloperoxidase; specific granules appear at the myelocyte stage.","The myelocyte is the last stage that can divide; from the metamyelocyte on, cells only mature.","Left shift: more bands and earlier forms (metamyelocytes, myelocytes) in the blood, as in acute bacterial infection."]},{"h":"Platelets","points":["Megakaryocytes are huge polyploid marrow cells (endomitosis) that push cytoplasmic extensions into sinusoids and shed platelets.","Alpha granules: von Willebrand factor, fibrinogen, fibronectin, platelet factor 4, and PDGF.","Dense (delta) granules: ADP, calcium, and serotonin."]},{"h":"B and T zones in lymph node and spleen","points":["Lymph node cortex: follicles of B cells; germinal centers form in secondary follicles after antigen exposure.","Lymph node paracortex: T cells and high endothelial venules where lymphocytes enter from blood.","Lymph node medulla: medullary cords (plasma cells, macrophages) and medullary sinuses that drain to the efferent lymphatic.","Spleen white pulp: periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (T cells) around central arterioles and follicles (B cells).","Spleen marginal zone: between white and red pulp; macrophages and marginal zone B cells trap blood-borne antigens.","Spleen red pulp: cords of Billroth and sinusoids; red cells must squeeze through slits in sinusoid walls, and old or rigid cells are removed."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Cells on a normal peripheral smear","cols":["Cell","Nucleus","Cytoplasm or granules","Approximate share of white cells"],"rows":[["Neutrophil","3 to 5 lobes","Fine pale pink to lilac granules","About 40 to 70%"],["Band","Unsegmented horseshoe","Same as neutrophil","Up to about 5%"],["Lymphocyte","Round, dense, fills most of cell","Thin rim of pale blue","About 20 to 40%"],["Monocyte","Folded or kidney-shaped","Abundant gray-blue, may have vacuoles","About 2 to 8%"],["Eosinophil","Usually bilobed","Large orange-red granules","About 1 to 4%"],["Basophil","Hidden by granules","Coarse dark blue-purple granules","Under 1%"],["Red cell","None","Pink with central pallor","Not a white cell"],["Platelet","None","Small purple granular fragment","Not a white cell"]]},{"title":"Maturation sequences","cols":["Lineage","Stages in order","Key change to watch"],"rows":[["Erythroid","Proerythroblast, basophilic, polychromatophilic, orthochromatic erythroblast, reticulocyte, red cell","Nucleus condenses and is extruded; RNA lost; hemoglobin gained"],["Granulocytic","Myeloblast, promyelocyte, myelocyte, metamyelocyte, band, segmented neutrophil","Primary granules at promyelocyte, specific granules at myelocyte; nucleus indents then segments"],["Megakaryocytic","Megakaryoblast, megakaryocyte, platelets","Endomitosis makes a polyploid cell whose cytoplasm fragments"]]},{"title":"Lymphoid zones","cols":["Organ","Zone","Main cells"],"rows":[["Lymph node","Cortical follicles","B cells"],["Lymph node","Paracortex","T cells"],["Lymph node","Medullary cords and sinuses","Plasma cells and macrophages"],["Spleen","Periarteriolar lymphoid sheath","T cells"],["Spleen","White pulp follicles","B cells"],["Spleen","Marginal zone","Macrophages and marginal zone B cells"],["Spleen","Red pulp cords and sinusoids","Macrophages filtering red cells"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas","text":"White cells from most to least common in blood: Neutrophils, Lymphocytes, Monocytes, Eosinophils, Basophils."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Young Liver Synthesizes Blood","text":"Order of blood-forming sites in development: Yolk sac, Liver, Spleen, Bone marrow."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Blue to pink","text":"Young red cell precursors are blue from ribosomes and turn pink as hemoglobin fills them. A faint blue-gray red cell on a smear is a reticulocyte that still has RNA."}],"highYield":["A rising reticulocyte count after anemia means the marrow is responding, as in hemolysis or acute bleeding.","Bands and metamyelocytes in blood (left shift) point to acute bacterial infection.","Teardrop cells, nucleated red cells, and massive splenomegaly suggest myelofibrosis with extramedullary hematopoiesis.","Hepatosplenomegaly and frontal bossing in a child with severe microcytic anemia fit beta thalassemia major.","Dense granules hold ADP, calcium, and serotonin; alpha granules hold von Willebrand factor and fibrinogen.","T cells live in the lymph node paracortex and the splenic periarteriolar lymphoid sheath; B cells live in follicles."],"pitfalls":["Lymphocytes and monocytes are both mononuclear: the lymphocyte is small with a round nucleus and scant cytoplasm, while the monocyte is larger with a folded nucleus and gray-blue cytoplasm.","Eosinophil granules are orange-red and do not hide the nucleus; basophil granules are dark purple and cover it.","A band has a curved but unsegmented nucleus; a metamyelocyte has a shallow kidney-shaped indent.","Paracortex (T cells) and medullary cords (plasma cells) are easy to swap; the paracortex sits between cortex and medulla."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hematopoiesis tree","p":"Draw the hematopoietic stem cell at the top and branch it into common myeloid and common lymphoid progenitors. Draw arrows to every mature blood cell and write the stages of red cell and neutrophil maturation along their branches.","l":["Stem cell gives common myeloid and common lymphoid progenitors","Myeloid side: red cells, platelets, neutrophils, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils, mast cells","Lymphoid side: B cells, T cells, NK cells","Red cell stages: proerythroblast, erythroblast stages, reticulocyte, red cell","Nucleus extruded before the reticulocyte stage; reticulocyte keeps RNA","Neutrophil stages: myeloblast, promyelocyte, myelocyte, metamyelocyte, band, segmented","Megakaryocyte fragments into platelets","Monocytes become tissue macrophages"]},{"t":"Lymph node and spleen map","p":"Draw a lymph node and a cross-section of splenic tissue. Label where B cells, T cells, plasma cells, and macrophages sit, and mark where blood or lymph enters and leaves.","l":["Lymph node cortex follicles with B cells and germinal centers","Paracortex with T cells and high endothelial venules","Medullary cords with plasma cells; medullary sinuses to efferent lymphatic","Afferent lymphatics enter the capsule and drain into the subcapsular sinus","Splenic periarteriolar lymphoid sheath with T cells around central arteriole","Splenic follicles with B cells","Marginal zone with macrophages trapping blood-borne antigens","Red pulp cords and sinusoids filter old red cells"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Blood"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Hemopoiesis"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Section on blood and blood vessel formation"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-2","HE-3","HE-4","HE-9-A","HE-9-B1"]},"HE-2":{"id":"HE-2","summary":"What blood is made of and what each part does: plasma proteins, red cell design for gas transport, erythropoietin control, white cell jobs, and ABO and Rh typing. Exam questions ask you to read a CBC to classify an anemia, predict erythropoietin in different settings, explain edema from low albumin, and pick a safe donor.","notes":[{"h":"Plasma and serum","points":["Plasma is about 55% of blood volume and is about 92% water, with proteins, electrolytes, nutrients, hormones, gases, and wastes.","Plasma proteins: albumin (about 55 to 60%), globulins (about 35%, including immunoglobulins and transport proteins), and fibrinogen (about 4%).","Serum is the fluid left after blood clots, so it lacks fibrinogen and the clotting factors used up in clotting.","Plasma comes from blood drawn into an anticoagulant tube (citrate, EDTA, or heparin)."]},{"h":"Albumin","points":["Made by the liver; provides most (about 75 to 80%) of plasma oncotic pressure.","Carries unconjugated bilirubin, free fatty acids, calcium (most of the protein-bound calcium, which is about 40% of the total), hormones, and many drugs.","Starling forces: capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out; plasma oncotic pressure pulls it back.","Low albumin (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, protein malnutrition) lowers capillary oncotic pressure, so net filtration rises and edema forms.","Low albumin lowers total calcium while ionized calcium stays normal."]},{"h":"Red cell design for gas transport","points":["Biconcave shape gives a large surface area for diffusion and lets the cell fold through capillaries and splenic slits.","No nucleus or organelles leaves more room for hemoglobin; no mitochondria means the cell does not use the oxygen it carries and makes ATP by glycolysis.","Carbonic anhydrase in red cells turns CO2 and water into carbonic acid, which splits into H+ and bicarbonate.","Most CO2 (about 70%) travels in plasma as bicarbonate; H+ is buffered by deoxyhemoglobin.","Chloride shift: bicarbonate leaves the red cell through band 3 in exchange for chloride, and the process reverses in the lungs."]},{"h":"Erythropoietin","points":["Made mainly by peritubular interstitial (fibroblast-like) cells of the renal cortex, with a small amount from the liver.","Low tissue oxygen stabilizes hypoxia-inducible factor, which turns on the erythropoietin gene.","Erythropoietin keeps erythroid progenitors alive and speeds their maturation, so reticulocytes rise within days.","Erythropoietin tracks oxygen delivery to the kidney, so it rises with hypoxemia or anemia and falls when red cell mass is high."]},{"h":"Reading the CBC","points":["Reference values: hemoglobin about 13.5 to 17.5 g/dL in men and 12 to 16 g/dL in women; hematocrit about 41 to 53% in men and 36 to 46% in women; MCV 80 to 100 fL; MCHC 32 to 36 g/dL; RDW about 11.5 to 14.5%.","MCV sorts the anemia: under 80 fL microcytic, 80 to 100 fL normocytic, over 100 fL macrocytic.","Microcytic: iron deficiency, thalassemia, anemia of chronic disease (some cases), sideroblastic anemia including lead poisoning.","Macrocytic: megaloblastic (B12 or folate deficiency) or nonmegaloblastic (alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, marked reticulocytosis).","Reticulocyte count separates hemolysis or blood loss (high) from underproduction (low or normal).","High RDW means mixed cell sizes, as in iron deficiency; RDW is usually normal in thalassemia trait.","High MCHC is a clue to hereditary spherocytosis."]},{"h":"White cell jobs","points":["Neutrophils: phagocytose and kill bacteria with the respiratory burst; neutrophilia means bacterial infection (also steroids and stress).","Eosinophils: attack helminths with major basic protein and modulate allergic reactions; eosinophilia means parasites or allergy.","Basophils: release histamine and heparin; basophilia is a clue to myeloproliferative disease such as CML.","Monocytes: become tissue macrophages and present antigen; monocytosis appears in chronic infection such as tuberculosis.","Lymphocytes: B cells make antibody, T cells direct and kill, NK cells kill infected cells; lymphocytosis means viral infection (also pertussis and CLL)."]},{"h":"ABO and Rh","points":["A and B antigens are sugars added to the H antigen on red cells; type O has unmodified H antigen.","People make IgM anti-A and anti-B against the antigens they lack, starting in the first months of life after exposure to similar gut bacterial sugars.","Red cell transfusion: donor cells must lack antigens the recipient has antibodies against. O cells go to anyone; AB recipients take any ABO type.","Plasma transfusion runs the other way: AB plasma has no anti-A or anti-B and is the universal plasma donor.","Rh (D antigen): Rh-negative people make anti-D only after exposure (transfusion or pregnancy), and it is IgG that crosses the placenta."]}],"charts":[{"title":"ABO compatibility","cols":["Recipient type","Red cell antigens","Plasma antibodies","Can receive red cells from","Can receive plasma from"],"rows":[["O","None (H only)","Anti-A and anti-B","O","O, A, B, AB"],["A","A","Anti-B","A, O","A, AB"],["B","B","Anti-A","B, O","B, AB"],["AB","A and B","None","AB, A, B, O","AB"]]},{"title":"Predicting erythropoietin","cols":["Setting","Erythropoietin","Red cell mass","Why"],"rows":[["Chronic kidney disease","Low for the degree of anemia","Low (normocytic anemia)","Peritubular interstitial cells are lost"],["High altitude or chronic lung disease","High","High (secondary polycythemia)","Low arterial oxygen drives renal hypoxia sensing"],["Polycythemia vera","Low","High","JAK2 mutant precursors grow without erythropoietin, and high red cell mass suppresses it"],["Erythropoietin-secreting tumor (renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma)","High","High","Tumor makes erythropoietin with normal oxygen saturation"]]},{"title":"Anemia by MCV and reticulocyte count","cols":["Pattern","Reticulocytes","Main causes"],"rows":[["Microcytic","Low or normal","Iron deficiency, thalassemia, anemia of chronic disease, sideroblastic (lead)"],["Normocytic, underproduction","Low","Chronic kidney disease, aplastic anemia, early iron deficiency, anemia of chronic disease"],["Normocytic, hemolytic or blood loss","High","Spherocytosis, G6PD deficiency, sickle cell disease, autoimmune hemolysis, acute bleeding"],["Macrocytic, megaloblastic","Low","Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency"],["Macrocytic, nonmegaloblastic","Variable","Alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, brisk reticulocytosis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cells and plasma run in opposite directions","text":"O is the universal red cell donor because its cells carry no A or B antigen. AB is the universal plasma donor because its plasma carries no anti-A or anti-B."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PACCMAN","text":"Causes of eosinophilia: Parasites, Asthma, Chronic adrenal insufficiency, Churg-Strauss syndrome (eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis), Myeloproliferative disorders, Allergic processes, Neoplasia (for example Hodgkin lymphoma)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ask two questions of every anemia","text":"First, what is the MCV (small, normal, large)? Second, is the reticulocyte count high (hemolysis or bleeding) or low (production problem)? Most anemias fall out of those two answers."}],"highYield":["Edema with albumin of 2.0 g/dL (reference 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL) comes from low plasma oncotic pressure.","Polycythemia vera has low erythropoietin; secondary polycythemia from hypoxia or an erythropoietin-secreting tumor has high erythropoietin.","Normocytic anemia with a low reticulocyte count in chronic kidney disease reflects loss of erythropoietin production.","Microcytosis with a high RDW suggests iron deficiency; microcytosis with a normal RDW and a normal or high red cell count suggests thalassemia trait.","Venous red cells contain more chloride than arterial red cells because of the chloride shift.","Type O, Rh-negative red cells are given when there is no time to type the patient.","Lymphocytosis with atypical lymphocytes suggests a viral infection such as infectious mononucleosis."],"pitfalls":["Serum and plasma are swapped often: serum has no fibrinogen because it has clotted.","Universal donor rules flip between red cells (O) and plasma (AB).","Anti-A and anti-B are naturally occurring IgM, while anti-D appears only after exposure and is IgG.","A low total calcium with low albumin does not mean true hypocalcemia if ionized calcium is normal.","Erythropoietin is made by renal interstitial cells, and juxtaglomerular cells make renin."],"dumps":[{"t":"CO2 transport in the red cell","p":"Draw a red cell in a systemic capillary next to a tissue cell. Show CO2 entering, the carbonic anhydrase reaction, what happens to H+ and bicarbonate, and the chloride shift. Then show what reverses in the lung.","l":["CO2 diffuses from tissue into the red cell","Carbonic anhydrase makes carbonic acid, which splits into H+ and bicarbonate","H+ is buffered by deoxyhemoglobin","Bicarbonate leaves through band 3 in exchange for chloride (chloride shift)","Most CO2 travels in plasma as bicarbonate","Some CO2 binds hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin and some dissolves","In the lung the steps reverse and CO2 is exhaled"]},{"t":"Anemia sorting table","p":"Make a table with rows for microcytic, normocytic with low reticulocytes, normocytic with high reticulocytes, and macrocytic anemia. For each, list the MCV range, the reticulocyte pattern, and three causes.","l":["Microcytic: MCV under 80 fL","Microcytic causes: iron deficiency, thalassemia, anemia of chronic disease or sideroblastic anemia","Normocytic: MCV 80 to 100 fL","Low reticulocytes: chronic kidney disease, aplastic anemia, early chronic disease","High reticulocytes: hemolysis or acute blood loss","Macrocytic: MCV over 100 fL","Megaloblastic causes: B12 or folate deficiency; nonmegaloblastic: alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Blood Types; Transfusion; Tissue and Organ Transplantation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide in Blood and Tissue Fluids"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Section on red cell disorders and anemias"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-1","HE-3","HE-6","HE-7","HE-9-A1","HE-9-A2"]},"HE-3":{"id":"HE-3","summary":"How blood cells are removed and their parts recycled: red cell life span, heme breakdown to bilirubin, iron handling, and the two patterns of hemolysis. Exam questions ask you to trace bilirubin, read haptoglobin and urine findings to place hemolysis inside or outside vessels, interpret hepcidin and iron studies, and predict which cell count falls first when the marrow stops.","notes":[{"h":"Red cell life span and removal","points":["Red cells live about 120 days; with no nucleus they cannot repair membrane or enzyme damage.","Aging cells grow stiff and are removed mainly by macrophages of the splenic red pulp, with help from liver and marrow macrophages.","Globin is broken into amino acids, iron is recycled, and heme is turned into bilirubin."]},{"h":"Heme to bilirubin","points":["Heme oxygenase in macrophages opens the heme ring, releasing iron and carbon monoxide and forming green biliverdin.","Biliverdin reductase makes unconjugated bilirubin, which is fat soluble, water insoluble, and toxic to the brain in newborns.","Unconjugated bilirubin travels bound to albumin, so it does not pass into urine.","Hepatocytes take it up and conjugate it with glucuronic acid using UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1A1).","Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and is secreted into bile.","Gut bacteria turn it into urobilinogen; most becomes stercobilin (brown stool color), some is reabsorbed, and a little leaves in urine as yellow urobilin.","Hemolysis raises unconjugated bilirubin and urine urobilinogen with no bilirubin in urine, and chronic hemolysis causes black pigment gallstones."]},{"h":"Intravascular versus extravascular hemolysis","points":["Extravascular: macrophages in the spleen and liver eat damaged or antibody-coated cells; spherocytes and splenomegaly are typical.","Intravascular: cells burst inside vessels, releasing free hemoglobin into plasma.","Free hemoglobin binds haptoglobin, so haptoglobin becomes very low or undetectable.","Once haptoglobin is saturated, hemoglobin passes into urine (hemoglobinuria, dark urine).","Kidney tubular cells take up the filtered hemoglobin and later shed iron-laden cells, giving hemosiderinuria days later.","Both types raise LDH, unconjugated bilirubin, and the reticulocyte count."]},{"h":"Iron absorption and recycling","points":["Duodenal cytochrome b reduces dietary ferric iron (Fe3+) to ferrous iron (Fe2+), and DMT1 carries it into the duodenal enterocyte.","Heme iron from meat is absorbed by a separate route and more efficiently.","Iron leaves the enterocyte (and macrophages) through ferroportin; hephaestin reoxidizes it to Fe3+ for transport.","Transferrin carries iron in plasma to marrow, where cells take it in through transferrin receptors.","Ferritin stores iron inside cells (liver, macrophages); serum ferritin reflects stores but also rises with inflammation.","Hepcidin from the liver binds ferroportin and causes its breakdown, blocking both gut absorption and macrophage release.","Hepcidin rises with inflammation (IL-6) and iron excess, and falls with iron deficiency, hypoxia, and active erythropoiesis.","The body has no regulated route to excrete iron, so absorption is the control point."]},{"h":"Life span and marrow failure","points":["Neutrophils circulate only about 6 to 8 hours, platelets about 7 to 10 days, and red cells about 120 days.","After sudden marrow suppression (chemotherapy, radiation, drug-induced aplasia), neutrophils fall first, platelets next, and anemia appears last.","Neutropenia brings infection risk within about 1 to 2 weeks, while hemoglobin drops slowly over weeks."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Intravascular versus extravascular hemolysis","cols":["Finding","Intravascular","Extravascular"],"rows":[["Site of destruction","Inside blood vessels","Splenic and hepatic macrophages"],["Haptoglobin","Very low or absent","Normal or low (less marked)"],["LDH","High","High"],["Unconjugated bilirubin","High","High"],["Hemoglobinuria","Present","Absent"],["Hemosiderinuria","Present (days later)","Absent"],["Smear clue","Schistocytes in mechanical or microangiopathic hemolysis","Spherocytes"],["Examples","ABO transfusion reaction, mechanical heart valve, microangiopathic hemolysis, paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Hereditary spherocytosis, warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, sickle cell disease"]]},{"title":"Iron studies","cols":["Condition","Serum iron","TIBC (transferrin)","Ferritin","Transferrin saturation","Hepcidin"],"rows":[["Iron deficiency","Low","High","Low","Low","Low"],["Anemia of chronic disease","Low","Low or normal","Normal or high","Low or normal","High"],["Hereditary hemochromatosis","High","Low or normal","High","High","Inappropriately low"]]},{"title":"Circulating life span","cols":["Cell","Time in blood","Order of fall after marrow shutdown"],"rows":[["Neutrophil","About 6 to 8 hours","First (days)"],["Platelet","About 7 to 10 days","Second (about 1 to 2 weeks)"],["Red cell","About 120 days","Last (weeks)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Haptoglobin is the mop","text":"Haptoglobin mops up free hemoglobin in plasma. When cells burst inside vessels the mop is used up, so very low haptoglobin plus hemoglobinuria points to intravascular hemolysis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Hepcidin locks the door","text":"Ferroportin is the only exit door for iron from gut cells and macrophages. Hepcidin locks it, so high hepcidin traps iron in macrophages (anemia of chronic disease) and low hepcidin leaves the door open (hemochromatosis)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Short life, first to fall","text":"Order the counts by life span: neutrophils (hours), platelets (days), red cells (months). The shorter the life, the sooner the count falls when production stops."}],"highYield":["Very low haptoglobin with hemoglobinuria and hemosiderinuria means intravascular hemolysis.","Spherocytes with splenomegaly and normal urine point to extravascular hemolysis.","Hemolytic jaundice raises unconjugated bilirubin and urine urobilinogen but gives no bilirubin in urine.","Chronic hemolysis causes black pigment gallstones, even in children.","Inflammation raises hepcidin, trapping iron in macrophages and giving low serum iron with normal or high ferritin.","After chemotherapy, the neutrophil count falls first and anemia appears last.","Heme oxygenase releases carbon monoxide, which is why carboxyhemoglobin rises slightly in brisk hemolysis."],"pitfalls":["LDH and unconjugated bilirubin rise in both kinds of hemolysis; haptoglobin, hemoglobinuria, and hemosiderinuria separate them.","Ferritin is an acute phase reactant, so a normal ferritin in an inflamed patient does not rule out low iron stores.","DMT1 brings iron into the enterocyte on the luminal side; ferroportin exports it on the basolateral side.","Unconjugated bilirubin causes jaundice without dark urine because albumin keeps it out of the urine."],"dumps":[{"t":"Bilirubin pathway","p":"Trace heme from an old red cell in the spleen to the stool and urine. Name each enzyme, say where each step happens, and mark which forms are water soluble.","l":["Splenic macrophage breaks down the old red cell","Heme oxygenase: heme to biliverdin, releasing iron and carbon monoxide","Biliverdin reductase: biliverdin to unconjugated bilirubin","Unconjugated bilirubin travels on albumin and is water insoluble","UGT1A1 in the liver conjugates it with glucuronic acid (water soluble)","Conjugated bilirubin enters bile and the gut","Gut bacteria form urobilinogen; stercobilin colors stool and urobilin colors urine"]},{"t":"Iron absorption diagram","p":"Draw a duodenal enterocyte with the gut lumen on one side and a capillary on the other. Show how dietary iron gets in, how it is stored, how it leaves, and where hepcidin acts.","l":["Ferric iron reduced to ferrous iron at the brush border","DMT1 carries ferrous iron into the cell","Iron stored as ferritin inside the enterocyte","Ferroportin exports iron on the basolateral side","Hephaestin oxidizes iron for binding to transferrin","Transferrin carries iron to marrow transferrin receptors","Hepcidin from the liver causes ferroportin breakdown","Inflammation raises hepcidin; iron deficiency lowers it"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on heme degradation and bilirubin"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on iron metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders (section on hemolytic anemias)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-1","HE-2","HE-5","HE-7","HE-9-A1","HE-9-B1"]},"HE-4":{"id":"HE-4","summary":"How blood cell production is driven by growth factors and how bleeding is stopped and then limited. Exam questions ask you to walk through platelet plug formation and the clotting cascade, match PT and PTT to their pathways, use a mixing study to find an inhibitor, and apply Virchow triad to a patient with a clot.","notes":[{"h":"Growth factors by lineage","points":["Erythropoietin (kidney): red cells.","Thrombopoietin (made mostly in the liver): megakaryocytes and platelets.","G-CSF: neutrophils; GM-CSF: granulocytes and monocytes.","IL-5 (from Th2 cells): eosinophils.","IL-3 and stem cell factor (kit ligand) act on early multipotent progenitors."]},{"h":"Primary hemostasis: the platelet plug","points":["Injury causes brief reflex vasoconstriction, helped by endothelin.","Adhesion: von Willebrand factor binds exposed subendothelial collagen and the platelet receptor GpIb.","Activation: platelets change shape and degranulate, releasing ADP from dense granules and making thromboxane A2 through COX-1.","ADP (via P2Y12) and thromboxane A2 recruit more platelets and switch on GpIIb/IIIa.","Aggregation: fibrinogen links GpIIb/IIIa receptors on neighboring platelets into a plug.","Platelet defects show as mucosal bleeding and petechiae with a prolonged bleeding time."]},{"h":"Secondary hemostasis: the clotting cascade","points":["Extrinsic (tissue factor) pathway: tissue factor exposed at injury binds factor VIIa and activates factors X and IX; this starts clotting in the body.","Intrinsic (contact) pathway: XII, XI, IX, and VIII; IXa with VIIIa activates X.","Common pathway: Xa with Va, on phospholipid with calcium, converts prothrombin (II) to thrombin.","Thrombin cleaves fibrinogen to fibrin and also activates V, VIII, XI, XIII, and platelets, amplifying itself.","Factor XIIIa cross-links fibrin, making the clot stable.","PT measures extrinsic and common pathways (VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen); INR standardizes PT for warfarin monitoring.","PTT measures intrinsic and common pathways (XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, fibrinogen); it monitors unfractionated heparin."]},{"h":"Natural anticoagulants and fibrinolysis","points":["Antithrombin inactivates thrombin and factor Xa (and IXa, XIa, XIIa); heparin speeds this greatly.","Thrombomodulin on endothelium binds thrombin, which then activates protein C.","Activated protein C, with protein S as cofactor, cleaves and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa.","Tissue factor pathway inhibitor blocks the tissue factor and VIIa complex once Xa forms.","tPA from endothelium converts plasminogen to plasmin on the fibrin surface.","Plasmin breaks fibrin and fibrinogen into fibrin degradation products; D-dimer comes only from cross-linked fibrin, so it shows a clot formed and is being broken down."]},{"h":"Mixing study","points":["Patient plasma is mixed 1:1 with normal plasma and the prolonged test is repeated.","If the time corrects, a factor is missing (normal plasma supplies enough).","If it does not correct, an inhibitor is present, such as an acquired factor VIII antibody, lupus anticoagulant, or heparin."]},{"h":"Virchow triad and venous thrombosis","points":["Stasis: immobility, long travel, heart failure, atrial fibrillation, pregnant uterus compressing pelvic veins.","Endothelial injury: surgery (especially hip and knee), trauma, central venous catheters.","Hypercoagulability: pregnancy and estrogen (more clotting factors, less protein S), malignancy (tumor tissue factor and mucin), inherited thrombophilia.","Surgery adds all three: vessel injury, bed rest, and tissue factor release."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Localizing a bleeding disorder","cols":["Disorder","Platelet count","Bleeding time","PT","PTT","Other clue"],"rows":[["Immune thrombocytopenia","Low","Prolonged","Normal","Normal","Large platelets, otherwise normal smear"],["Platelet function defect (aspirin, uremia)","Normal","Prolonged","Normal","Normal","Abnormal aggregation"],["Von Willebrand disease","Normal","Prolonged","Normal","Normal or prolonged","Low von Willebrand factor activity (ristocetin cofactor); factor VIII may be low"],["Hemophilia A or B","Normal","Normal","Normal","Prolonged","Corrects with mixing"],["Acquired factor VIII inhibitor","Normal","Normal","Normal","Prolonged","Does not correct with mixing"],["Vitamin K deficiency or warfarin","Normal","Normal","Prolonged (first)","Prolonged or normal","Factor VII has the shortest half-life"],["Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Low","Prolonged","Prolonged","Prolonged","Low fibrinogen, high D-dimer, schistocytes"]]},{"title":"Brakes on clotting","cols":["Regulator","Source","Target"],"rows":[["Antithrombin","Liver","Thrombin, Xa, and other serine protease factors"],["Protein C and protein S","Liver (vitamin K dependent)","Factors Va and VIIIa"],["Thrombomodulin","Endothelium","Turns thrombin into a protein C activator"],["Tissue factor pathway inhibitor","Endothelium","Tissue factor and VIIa with Xa"],["Plasmin (from tPA)","Plasminogen activated on fibrin","Fibrin and fibrinogen"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Play Tennis outside, Play Table Tennis inside","text":"PT tests the extrinsic pathway (outside), and PTT tests the intrinsic pathway (inside)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Platelet versus factor bleeding","text":"Platelet problems bleed right away from skin and mucosa (petechiae, gums, nosebleeds). Factor problems bleed late and deep (joints, muscles, delayed bleeding after surgery)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Mixing fixes a shortage, not a saboteur","text":"Adding normal plasma fixes a missing factor. It cannot fix an antibody that keeps destroying or blocking the factor."}],"highYield":["Isolated prolonged PTT that corrects with mixing, with joint bleeding in a boy, fits hemophilia.","Isolated prolonged PTT that does not correct with mixing and a history of clots fits lupus anticoagulant.","Low platelets, prolonged PT and PTT, low fibrinogen, and high D-dimer mean consumption (DIC).","Aspirin blocks COX-1 and thromboxane A2 for the life of the platelet.","GpIb binds von Willebrand factor (adhesion); GpIIb/IIIa binds fibrinogen (aggregation).","Factor XII deficiency prolongs the PTT but does not cause bleeding.","A normal D-dimer makes acute venous thrombosis unlikely in a low-risk patient.","Pregnancy raises venous clot risk through both hypercoagulability and venous stasis."],"pitfalls":["Bleeding time tests platelets; PT and PTT test clotting factors.","D-dimer comes from cross-linked fibrin; fibrin degradation products in general can also come from fibrinogen.","Thrombin is a procoagulant until it binds thrombomodulin; then it starts the protein C brake.","Von Willebrand disease can prolong the PTT because von Willebrand factor carries and protects factor VIII.","G-CSF drives neutrophils, while GM-CSF also drives monocytes; IL-5 is the eosinophil factor."],"dumps":[{"t":"Clotting cascade","p":"Draw the extrinsic, intrinsic, and common pathways down to a cross-linked fibrin clot. Box the factors measured by PT and circle those measured by PTT, then add the natural brakes.","l":["Tissue factor with VIIa activates X (extrinsic)","XII to XI to IX; IXa with VIIIa activates X (intrinsic)","Xa with Va converts prothrombin to thrombin","Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin and activates V, VIII, XI, XIII","XIIIa cross-links fibrin","PT covers VII and common pathway; PTT covers XII, XI, IX, VIII and common pathway","Antithrombin blocks thrombin and Xa","Activated protein C with protein S inactivates Va and VIIIa","Plasmin breaks fibrin into D-dimer"]},{"t":"Platelet plug steps","p":"Draw a torn vessel wall with exposed collagen and show, step by step, how platelets adhere, activate, and aggregate. Label every receptor and the molecule that binds it.","l":["Reflex vasoconstriction and endothelin","Von Willebrand factor binds collagen","Platelet GpIb binds von Willebrand factor (adhesion)","Dense granules release ADP and calcium","COX-1 makes thromboxane A2","ADP acts on P2Y12 to recruit platelets","GpIIb/IIIa activated and bound by fibrinogen (aggregation)"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock (sections on hemostasis and thrombosis)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Blood Disorders (section on hemostasis)"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-1","HE-5","HE-6","HE-7","HE-9-C","HE-9-C1","HE-9-C2","HE-9-C4"]},"HE-5":{"id":"HE-5","summary":"The biochemistry behind red cells and clotting: heme synthesis and the porphyrias, hemoglobin oxygen binding, methemoglobin and carbon monoxide, red cell energy and antioxidant pathways, B12 and folate, and vitamin K. Exam questions ask you to place a block in a pathway, predict which way the oxygen curve shifts, and use lab markers to name the missing vitamin or enzyme.","notes":[{"h":"Heme synthesis","points":["Starts in the mitochondrion: glycine plus succinyl-CoA form delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) by ALA synthase, the rate-limiting enzyme, which needs pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6).","Heme represses ALA synthase (feedback), and drugs that increase cytochrome P450 demand for heme induce it.","Cytosol: ALA dehydratase joins two ALA into porphobilinogen; porphobilinogen deaminase builds hydroxymethylbilane, which becomes uroporphyrinogen III; uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase converts that to coproporphyrinogen III.","Back in the mitochondrion: protoporphyrin IX is formed and ferrochelatase inserts ferrous iron to make heme.","Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, causing microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling and raised protoporphyrin.","B6 deficiency (for example with isoniazid) lowers ALA synthase activity and causes sideroblastic anemia."]},{"h":"Porphyrias","points":["Acute intermittent porphyria: porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency (autosomal dominant); ALA and porphobilinogen build up.","Acute attacks: severe abdominal pain, neuropathy, psychiatric symptoms, and urine that darkens on standing; no skin photosensitivity.","Attacks are triggered by anything that induces ALA synthase: P450-inducing drugs, alcohol, fasting, and hormonal changes.","Porphyria cutanea tarda: uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency, the most common porphyria; uroporphyrin builds up.","PCT findings: blistering and fragile skin on sun-exposed areas (backs of hands), hypertrichosis, and tea-colored urine; linked to hepatitis C, alcohol, and iron overload."]},{"h":"Hemoglobin and oxygen binding","points":["HbA is two alpha and two beta chains (about 95 to 97% of adult hemoglobin); HbA2 is two alpha and two delta (about 2 to 3%); HbF is two alpha and two gamma (main fetal form, under 1% in adults).","Binding is cooperative: each O2 bound shifts hemoglobin from the low-affinity taut state toward the high-affinity relaxed state, giving a sigmoid curve.","Right shift (lower affinity, more O2 unloaded): higher 2,3-BPG, H+ (lower pH), CO2, and temperature, as in exercising muscle.","Left shift (higher affinity, less unloaded): HbF (binds 2,3-BPG poorly), carbon monoxide, methemoglobin, alkalosis, cold, and low 2,3-BPG."]},{"h":"Methemoglobin and carbon monoxide","points":["Methemoglobin has ferric iron (Fe3+), which cannot carry O2 and left-shifts the remaining sites, so delivery falls while PaO2 stays normal.","Clues: cyanosis that does not improve with oxygen, chocolate-brown blood, and pulse oximetry stuck around 85%.","NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase normally keeps iron ferrous; congenital deficiency of this enzyme causes lifelong methemoglobinemia.","Acquired causes: nitrites, dapsone, benzocaine and other topical anesthetics.","Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin about 200 times more tightly than O2, lowering O2 content and left-shifting the curve with a normal PaO2."]},{"h":"Red cell energy and antioxidant defense","points":["With no mitochondria, red cells get all ATP from glycolysis; ATP runs Na+/K+-ATPase and keeps the membrane flexible.","Pyruvate kinase deficiency: low ATP, rigid cells, extravascular hemolysis; 2,3-BPG builds up upstream and right-shifts the curve.","The pentose phosphate pathway (G6PD step) is the red cell's only NADPH source.","NADPH keeps glutathione reduced, and reduced glutathione clears hydrogen peroxide.","G6PD deficiency: oxidant stress (infection, fava beans, sulfonamides, primaquine, dapsone) denatures hemoglobin into Heinz bodies; splenic macrophages bite them out, leaving bite cells."]},{"h":"Vitamin B12 and folate","points":["Folate (as methylene-THF) donates the carbon for thymidylate synthase to turn dUMP into dTMP, so deficiency stalls DNA synthesis and gives megaloblasts.","Methionine synthase uses B12 to move a methyl from methyl-THF to homocysteine, making methionine and freeing THF.","In B12 deficiency folate gets trapped as methyl-THF, so DNA synthesis stalls the same way.","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase needs B12 to turn methylmalonyl-CoA into succinyl-CoA.","B12 deficiency: high homocysteine and high methylmalonic acid, with possible subacute combined degeneration of dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts.","Folate deficiency: high homocysteine with normal methylmalonic acid and no neurologic signs."]},{"h":"Vitamin K","points":["Vitamin K is the cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver, which adds a carboxyl to glutamate residues on factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S.","The resulting gamma-carboxyglutamate residues bind calcium, which bridges them to phosphatidylserine exposed on activated platelets, placing clotting complexes on the platelet surface.","Vitamin K epoxide reductase recycles vitamin K and is the enzyme warfarin blocks.","Deficiency (newborns, fat malabsorption, long antibiotic courses) prolongs the PT first because factor VII has the shortest half-life."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Heme synthesis checkpoints","cols":["Step","Enzyme","Location","Disorder when blocked"],"rows":[["Glycine plus succinyl-CoA to ALA","ALA synthase (B6, rate limiting)","Mitochondrion","Sideroblastic anemia (X-linked ALAS2 defect or B6 deficiency)"],["ALA to porphobilinogen","ALA dehydratase","Cytosol","Lead poisoning"],["Porphobilinogen to hydroxymethylbilane","Porphobilinogen deaminase","Cytosol","Acute intermittent porphyria"],["Uroporphyrinogen III to coproporphyrinogen III","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Cytosol","Porphyria cutanea tarda"],["Protoporphyrin IX plus Fe2+ to heme","Ferrochelatase","Mitochondrion","Lead poisoning"]]},{"title":"Acute intermittent porphyria versus porphyria cutanea tarda","cols":["Feature","Acute intermittent porphyria","Porphyria cutanea tarda"],"rows":[["Enzyme","Porphobilinogen deaminase","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase"],["What builds up","ALA and porphobilinogen","Uroporphyrin"],["Skin","No photosensitivity","Blistering on sun-exposed skin, hypertrichosis"],["Nervous system","Abdominal pain, neuropathy, psychiatric symptoms","Not affected"],["Triggers or links","P450-inducing drugs, alcohol, fasting","Hepatitis C, alcohol, iron overload, estrogen"]]},{"title":"Oxygen dissociation curve shifts","cols":["Right shift (unloads more O2)","Left shift (holds O2 tighter)"],"rows":[["Higher 2,3-BPG (altitude, chronic anemia, pyruvate kinase deficiency)","Lower 2,3-BPG (stored blood)"],["Higher H+ (acidosis)","Alkalosis"],["Higher CO2","Lower CO2"],["Higher temperature (fever, exercise)","Hypothermia"],["Exercise","HbF, carbon monoxide, methemoglobin"]]},{"title":"B12 versus folate deficiency","cols":["Marker","B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency"],"rows":[["Megaloblastic anemia and hypersegmented neutrophils","Yes","Yes"],["Homocysteine","High","High"],["Methylmalonic acid","High","Normal"],["Neurologic damage","Yes (dorsal columns, corticospinal tracts)","No"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CADET, face Right","text":"Right shift of the oxygen curve: CO2, Acid or Altitude, DPG (2,3-BPG), Exercise, Temperature."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"5 Ps of acute intermittent porphyria","text":"Painful abdomen, Port-wine colored urine, Polyneuropathy, Psychological disturbances, Precipitated by drugs, alcohol, or starvation."},{"kind":"trick","label":"1972","text":"Vitamin K dependent factors are X, IX, VII, and II, plus proteins C and S."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Two enzymes, two markers","text":"B12 serves two enzymes and folate serves one of them. B12 deficiency raises both homocysteine (methionine synthase) and methylmalonic acid (mutase); folate deficiency raises only homocysteine."}],"highYield":["Abdominal pain, neuropathy, and dark urine after starting a P450-inducing drug suggest acute intermittent porphyria.","Blistering on the backs of the hands in a patient with hepatitis C suggests porphyria cutanea tarda.","Cyanosis with a normal PaO2 that does not respond to oxygen after benzocaine spray means methemoglobinemia.","HbF has higher O2 affinity because it binds 2,3-BPG poorly, so oxygen moves from mother to fetus.","Bite cells and Heinz bodies after sulfonamide use fit G6PD deficiency.","High methylmalonic acid separates B12 deficiency from folate deficiency.","A newborn without prophylactic vitamin K can bleed from low factors II, VII, IX, and X.","Lead blocks ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, causing microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling."],"pitfalls":["The first and last steps of heme synthesis are mitochondrial; the middle steps are cytosolic.","Acute intermittent porphyria has no photosensitivity, while porphyria cutanea tarda has no neurovisceral attacks.","Carbon monoxide poisoning and methemoglobinemia both leave PaO2 normal; carbon monoxide gives cherry red skin, methemoglobin gives chocolate-brown blood.","Pyruvate kinase deficiency right-shifts the curve (more 2,3-BPG), while HbF and carbon monoxide left-shift it.","Folate replacement can correct the anemia of B12 deficiency while nerve damage keeps progressing."],"dumps":[{"t":"Heme synthesis map","p":"Draw a mitochondrion and the surrounding cytosol. Trace heme synthesis from glycine and succinyl-CoA to heme, naming each enzyme you know, and mark where lead, B6 deficiency, acute intermittent porphyria, and porphyria cutanea tarda act.","l":["Glycine plus succinyl-CoA form ALA in the mitochondrion","ALA synthase is rate limiting and needs B6","Heme feeds back to repress ALA synthase","ALA dehydratase in cytosol, inhibited by lead","Porphobilinogen deaminase deficient in acute intermittent porphyria","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficient in porphyria cutanea tarda","Ferrochelatase in the mitochondrion adds iron, inhibited by lead"]},{"t":"Oxygen curve sketch","p":"Draw the normal hemoglobin oxygen dissociation curve with PO2 on the x axis and saturation on the y axis. Add a right-shifted and a left-shifted curve and list what causes each shift.","l":["Sigmoid shape from cooperative binding","P50 about 26 to 27 mmHg on the normal curve","Right shift causes: 2,3-BPG, acid, CO2, temperature, exercise","Left shift causes: HbF, carbon monoxide, methemoglobin, alkalosis, cold","Right shift unloads more O2 to tissue","Myoglobin curve is hyperbolic and far to the left"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Globular Proteins (section on hemoglobin)"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on porphyrin metabolism and heme synthesis"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Sections on folic acid, vitamin B12, and vitamin K"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide in Blood and Tissue Fluids"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-2","HE-3","HE-4","HE-6","HE-9-A1","HE-9-B1","HE-9-C5"]},"HE-6":{"id":"HE-6","summary":"The genetics behind inherited blood diseases and blood cancers: the sickle mutation, thalassemia gene counts, inheritance patterns, driver mutations and translocations, Fanconi anemia, and inherited clotting risk. Exam questions ask you to predict how sickling changes, how many alpha genes are missing, what a child's risk is, and which gene a translocation turns on or off.","notes":[{"h":"Sickle hemoglobin","points":["A single base change in the beta globin gene (chromosome 11) swaps glutamate for valine at position 6.","Valine is hydrophobic; in deoxygenated HbS it sticks into a pocket on a neighboring hemoglobin, and the molecules polymerize into long fibers.","Polymers bend the cell into a rigid sickle, which blocks small vessels and is destroyed early (hemolysis).","More sickling: hypoxia, acidosis (right shift increases deoxyhemoglobin), dehydration (higher hemoglobin concentration), infection, and cold.","Less sickling: high HbF, which does not join the polymer; this is why infants are protected for the first months of life.","Sickle trait (HbAS) is usually silent but can cause hematuria and loss of urine concentrating ability from sickling in the hypoxic, hypertonic renal medulla."]},{"h":"Thalassemias","points":["Alpha globin: four genes, two on each chromosome 16; alpha thalassemia is usually caused by gene deletions.","One gene deleted: silent carrier. Two: alpha thalassemia trait with mild microcytosis. Three: HbH disease (beta4 tetramers) with moderate hemolytic anemia. Four: Hb Barts (gamma4) with hydrops fetalis.","Hb Barts and HbH bind O2 so tightly that they deliver almost none.","Two deletions on the same chromosome (cis) can pass a zero-gene chromosome to a child; this pattern is more common in people of Southeast Asian ancestry.","Beta thalassemia is usually caused by point mutations (splice site, promoter, nonsense), giving reduced (beta+) or absent (beta0) beta chains.","Beta thalassemia minor: mild microcytosis with HbA2 above about 3.5%. Major: severe anemia after about 6 months as HbF falls, with excess alpha chains precipitating in precursors (ineffective erythropoiesis)."]},{"h":"Inheritance patterns","points":["X-linked recessive: hemophilia A (factor VIII), hemophilia B (factor IX), G6PD deficiency. Carrier mother: each son 50% affected, each daughter 50% carrier. Affected father: all daughters carriers, no sons affected.","Autosomal recessive: sickle cell disease and the thalassemias. Two carrier parents: 25% affected, 50% carriers, 25% unaffected noncarriers.","Autosomal dominant: most hereditary spherocytosis (ankyrin, spectrin, band 3) and most von Willebrand disease. One affected parent: 50% of children affected."]},{"h":"Driver mutations in myeloid neoplasms","points":["CML: t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome joins BCR on chromosome 22 to ABL1 from chromosome 9.","BCR-ABL1 is a tyrosine kinase that is always on, driving proliferation and blocking apoptosis; imatinib blocks its ATP-binding site.","Polycythemia vera: JAK2 V617F (valine to phenylalanine) disables the inhibitory pseudokinase region of JAK2.","JAK2 then signals through STAT without erythropoietin, so red cells (and often granulocytes and platelets) expand while erythropoietin is low."]},{"h":"Translocations in lymphoma and leukemia","points":["Many lymphoma translocations involve chromosome 14 because it carries the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus, whose strong enhancer drives the partner gene in B cells.","t(8;14) Burkitt lymphoma: MYC overexpressed, driving very rapid proliferation (starry sky pattern).","t(14;18) follicular lymphoma: BCL2 overexpressed, blocking apoptosis, so cells accumulate slowly.","t(11;14) mantle cell lymphoma: cyclin D1 overexpressed, pushing cells from G1 into S phase.","t(15;17) acute promyelocytic leukemia: PML-RARA fusion blocks differentiation at the promyelocyte stage; all-trans retinoic acid releases the block."]},{"h":"Fanconi anemia","points":["Mostly autosomal recessive defects in Fanconi pathway genes that repair DNA interstrand crosslinks.","Cells break chromosomes when exposed to crosslinking agents (diepoxybutane or mitomycin C test).","Progressive marrow failure in childhood, short stature, absent or small thumbs and radial anomalies, and cafe-au-lait spots.","High risk of myelodysplastic syndrome, AML, and squamous cell carcinomas."]},{"h":"Inherited thrombophilia","points":["Factor V Leiden: a point mutation at the activated protein C cleavage site makes factor Va resistant to inactivation, so thrombin generation runs longer.","It is the most common inherited thrombophilia in people of European ancestry.","Prothrombin G20210A: a change in the untranslated region raises prothrombin levels, so more thrombin can form.","Both raise venous thrombosis risk several-fold, and risk multiplies with estrogen, pregnancy, or surgery; PT and PTT are normal."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Alpha thalassemia by number of deleted genes","cols":["Genes deleted","Name","Hemoglobin pattern","Clinical picture"],"rows":[["1","Silent carrier","Normal","No anemia"],["2","Alpha thalassemia trait","Normal pattern in adults","Mild microcytic anemia"],["3","HbH disease","HbH (beta4)","Moderate to severe hemolytic anemia, splenomegaly"],["4","Hb Barts hydrops fetalis","Hb Barts (gamma4)","Hydrops fetalis, usually fatal before or soon after birth"]]},{"title":"Inheritance of hematologic disorders","cols":["Pattern","Disorders","Offspring risk example"],"rows":[["X-linked recessive","Hemophilia A, hemophilia B, G6PD deficiency","Carrier mother and unaffected father: 50% of sons affected"],["Autosomal recessive","Sickle cell disease, alpha and beta thalassemia","Two carriers: 25% of children affected"],["Autosomal dominant","Most hereditary spherocytosis, most von Willebrand disease","One affected parent: 50% of children affected"]]},{"title":"Signature genetic changes","cols":["Change","Gene effect","Disease"],"rows":[["t(9;22)","BCR-ABL1 tyrosine kinase always on","Chronic myeloid leukemia"],["JAK2 V617F","Erythropoietin-independent JAK-STAT signaling","Polycythemia vera"],["t(8;14)","MYC overexpression","Burkitt lymphoma"],["t(14;18)","BCL2 overexpression (no apoptosis)","Follicular lymphoma"],["t(11;14)","Cyclin D1 overexpression","Mantle cell lymphoma"],["t(15;17)","PML-RARA (blocked differentiation)","Acute promyelocytic leukemia"],["Factor V Leiden","Factor Va resists activated protein C","Venous thrombosis"],["Prothrombin G20210A","More prothrombin made","Venous thrombosis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the alpha genes","text":"Count down from four working genes: lose one and nobody notices, lose two and the MCV drops, lose three and beta chains pair up as HbH, lose four and the fetus has only gamma4 Barts."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fourteen is the antibody chromosome","text":"When a B cell lymphoma translocation includes chromosome 14, the immunoglobulin heavy chain enhancer is turning on the partner gene: MYC on 8, cyclin D1 on 11, BCL2 on 18."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sickling follows anything that adds deoxyhemoglobin or concentrates it","text":"Hypoxia and acidosis increase deoxyhemoglobin; dehydration packs hemoglobin closer together. HbF dilutes HbS and cannot polymerize with it."}],"highYield":["Glutamate to valine at beta globin position 6 makes deoxygenated HbS polymerize.","Sickle cell crises tend to begin after about 6 months of age as HbF falls.","HbA2 above about 3.5% supports beta thalassemia minor.","Hydrops fetalis in a fetus of two parents with alpha thalassemia trait means all four alpha genes are deleted.","BCR-ABL1 from t(9;22) is the target of tyrosine kinase inhibitors in CML.","Low erythropoietin with high red cell mass points to JAK2-driven polycythemia vera.","Short stature, absent thumbs, and pancytopenia in a child suggest Fanconi anemia.","Venous thrombosis on oral contraceptives with resistance to activated protein C suggests factor V Leiden."],"pitfalls":["Alpha thalassemia usually comes from deletions; beta thalassemia usually comes from point mutations.","BCL2 blocks apoptosis (follicular lymphoma), while MYC drives proliferation (Burkitt lymphoma).","Factor V Leiden is a defect in factor V itself; protein C deficiency is a shortage of the brake.","All daughters of a father with hemophilia are carriers, and none of his sons are affected because sons get his Y chromosome.","Fanconi anemia (DNA crosslink repair, pancytopenia) is distinct from Diamond-Blackfan anemia (ribosomal protein genes, pure red cell aplasia), though both can involve the thumbs."],"dumps":[{"t":"Sickling triggers","p":"Draw an HbS molecule switching from oxygenated to deoxygenated and forming a polymer. Around it, list what makes polymerization worse and what makes it better, with one sentence of mechanism each.","l":["Glutamate to valine at position 6 of beta globin","Polymerization happens in the deoxygenated state","Hypoxia increases deoxyhemoglobin","Acidosis right-shifts the curve and increases deoxyhemoglobin","Dehydration raises hemoglobin concentration","HbF does not enter the polymer and protects","Sickled cells cause vaso-occlusion and hemolysis"]},{"t":"Translocation table","p":"Make a table with columns for translocation, gene turned on or created, what that gene does, and disease. Fill in t(9;22), t(8;14), t(14;18), t(11;14), and t(15;17), then add JAK2 V617F.","l":["t(9;22): BCR-ABL1 tyrosine kinase, CML","t(8;14): MYC, transcription factor for growth, Burkitt lymphoma","t(14;18): BCL2, blocks apoptosis, follicular lymphoma","t(11;14): cyclin D1, G1 to S progression, mantle cell lymphoma","t(15;17): PML-RARA, blocks differentiation, acute promyelocytic leukemia","Chromosome 14 carries the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus","JAK2 V617F: growth factor independent signaling, polycythemia vera"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders (sections on sickle cell disease and thalassemia)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Globular Proteins (section on hemoglobinopathies)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Blood Disorders"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-5","HE-4","HE-9-B1","HE-9-A2","HE-9-C2","HE-9-C4","HE-9-D1","HE-9-D2"]},"HE-7":{"id":"HE-7","summary":"Immune reactions that target blood: autoimmune hemolysis, transfusion reactions, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, antiphospholipid syndrome, Epstein-Barr virus in B cells, and infection risk after loss of the spleen. Exam questions ask you to classify a reaction by hypersensitivity type, read a Coombs test, sort transfusion reactions by timing, and explain why some antibodies cause clots instead of bleeding.","notes":[{"h":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","points":["Warm type: IgG binds red cells best at body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F).","Splenic macrophages with Fc receptors nibble IgG-coated membrane, leaving spherocytes, and hemolysis is extravascular.","Warm causes: idiopathic, SLE, CLL, and some drugs.","Cold agglutinin type: IgM binds red cells at temperatures below about 30 °C (86 °F), as in cool fingers, toes, ears, and nose, and fixes complement.","Cold findings: red cell clumping on the smear, acrocyanosis in the cold, and hemolysis driven by complement (C3b removal in the liver and some intravascular lysis).","Cold causes: Mycoplasma pneumoniae, EBV infectious mononucleosis, and lymphoproliferative disorders.","Direct Coombs test: anti-human globulin added to the patient's red cells clumps them if IgG or complement is already on their surface; warm is usually IgG positive, cold is usually C3 positive only.","Indirect Coombs test looks for free antibodies in serum, as in prenatal antibody screening and crossmatching."]},{"h":"Hypersensitivity types in blood disease","points":["Type II (antibody against a cell surface antigen): acute hemolytic transfusion reaction, autoimmune hemolytic anemia, hemolytic disease of the newborn, immune thrombocytopenia.","In hemolytic disease of the newborn, maternal IgG (classically anti-D) crosses the placenta and coats fetal red cells.","Type III (immune complexes deposit and fix complement): cryoglobulinemia, where immunoglobulins that precipitate in the cold form complexes that inflame small vessels.","Mixed cryoglobulinemia is strongly linked to hepatitis C and causes palpable purpura, joint pain, glomerulonephritis, and low complement.","Immediate (type I-like) reactions: allergic and anaphylactic transfusion reactions."]},{"h":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","points":["Heparin binds platelet factor 4 (released from platelet alpha granules) and forms a new antigen.","IgG binds the heparin and PF4 complex, and its Fc portion engages Fc receptors on platelets, activating them.","Activated platelets aggregate, release more PF4, and shed procoagulant particles, while antibody-coated platelets are cleared.","The platelet count falls by more than half about 5 to 10 days after starting heparin (sooner with recent heparin exposure).","The danger is clotting: venous and arterial thrombosis, with bleeding uncommon; risk is higher with unfractionated heparin."]},{"h":"Antiphospholipid syndrome","points":["Antibodies target phospholipid-binding proteins: lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, and anti-beta2 glycoprotein I.","In the test tube, lupus anticoagulant interferes with the phospholipid-dependent steps of the PTT, so the PTT is prolonged and does not correct with mixing.","In the body, the antibodies activate endothelium, platelets, monocytes, and complement, causing venous and arterial thrombosis.","Placental thrombosis leads to recurrent pregnancy loss and preeclampsia.","Anticardiolipin can give a false-positive syphilis screen; the syndrome can be primary or occur with SLE."]},{"h":"Epstein-Barr virus and B cells","points":["EBV glycoprotein gp350 binds CD21 (complement receptor 2) on B cells, and the virus stays latent in memory B cells.","CD8 cytotoxic T cells expand to kill infected B cells; these are the atypical lymphocytes of mononucleosis.","Mononucleosis: fever, pharyngitis, posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, and positive heterophile antibody.","Latent EBV proteins drive B cell growth; when T cell control fails or cofactors act, lymphoma can follow.","EBV-linked cancers: endemic Burkitt lymphoma (with malaria as cofactor), Hodgkin lymphoma, lymphomas after transplant or in HIV, and nasopharyngeal carcinoma."]},{"h":"Asplenia and encapsulated bacteria","points":["Polysaccharide capsules resist phagocytosis unless the bacteria are coated with antibody or complement.","The spleen filters blood, its marginal zone B cells make fast IgM against capsules, and its macrophages clear opsonized bacteria.","Without a spleen (surgical removal or sickle cell autosplenectomy from repeated infarcts), sepsis from Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, and Neisseria meningitidis can be rapid and fatal.","Smear clues of asplenia: Howell-Jolly bodies, target cells, and thrombocytosis.","Conjugate vaccines link the capsule sugar to a protein, recruit T cell help, and give class-switched IgG and memory, even in infants."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Transfusion reactions","cols":["Reaction","Mechanism","Timing","Key findings"],"rows":[["Acute hemolytic","Preformed IgM anti-A or anti-B fix complement on donor cells (type II), usually from ABO mismatch","Minutes to an hour","Fever, flank pain, hypotension, hemoglobinuria, positive direct Coombs, risk of DIC and kidney injury"],["Delayed hemolytic","Anamnestic IgG against minor red cell antigens (such as Kidd or Rh) from prior exposure","About 3 to 10 days","Falling hemoglobin, mild jaundice, new positive direct Coombs, extravascular hemolysis"],["Febrile nonhemolytic","Cytokines in the stored unit or recipient antibodies to donor white cell HLA","Within hours","Fever and chills with no hemolysis; reduced by leukoreduction"],["Anaphylactic","Anti-IgA antibodies in an IgA-deficient recipient react with donor IgA","Seconds to minutes","Hypotension, bronchospasm, urticaria, no fever"],["Septic","Bacterial contamination, most often of platelets stored at room temperature","Minutes to hours","High fever, rigors, hypotension, shock"],["TRALI","Donor anti-HLA or anti-neutrophil antibodies activate recipient neutrophils in lung capillaries","Within 6 hours","Hypoxemia, bilateral infiltrates, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema"]]},{"title":"Warm versus cold autoimmune hemolysis","cols":["Feature","Warm","Cold agglutinin"],"rows":[["Antibody","IgG","IgM (fixes complement)"],["Binding temperature","Body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F)","Below about 30 °C (86 °F)"],["Hemolysis site","Extravascular (spleen)","Mainly liver via C3b, some intravascular"],["Smear","Spherocytes","Red cell agglutinates"],["Direct Coombs","IgG positive, sometimes C3","C3 positive"],["Associations","SLE, CLL, drugs","Mycoplasma pneumoniae, EBV, lymphoproliferative disease"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Warm is G, cold is M","text":"Warm autoimmune hemolysis uses IgG and makes spherocytes in the spleen. Cold agglutinin disease uses IgM, which clumps cells in cold fingers and brings complement."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SHiNE SKiS","text":"Encapsulated organisms: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis, Escherichia coli, Salmonella, Klebsiella pneumoniae, group B Streptococcus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Some antibodies clot instead of bleed","text":"HIT and antiphospholipid syndrome both lower platelets or prolong a lab test, yet both cause thrombosis because the antibodies activate platelets or endothelium."}],"highYield":["Spherocytes with a positive direct Coombs test for IgG in a patient with SLE or CLL means warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia.","Hemolysis after Mycoplasma pneumonia with a C3-positive Coombs test fits cold agglutinin disease.","Fever, flank pain, and red urine minutes into a transfusion means an acute hemolytic reaction from ABO mismatch.","Anaphylaxis without fever during a first transfusion suggests IgA deficiency with anti-IgA antibodies.","Respiratory distress with bilateral infiltrates within 6 hours of transfusion, without volume overload, means TRALI.","A platelet count falling by half a week into heparin therapy with a new clot means HIT.","Recurrent miscarriage plus a prolonged PTT that does not correct with mixing suggests antiphospholipid syndrome.","Howell-Jolly bodies on a smear mean absent or nonfunctioning spleen."],"pitfalls":["HIT causes clots; bleeding is uncommon even though platelets are low.","Lupus anticoagulant prolongs the PTT in the lab and causes thrombosis in the patient.","Atypical lymphocytes in mononucleosis are reactive CD8 T cells; EBV itself infects B cells.","Febrile nonhemolytic reactions have fever without hemolysis; anaphylactic reactions have hypotension and wheeze without fever.","Cryoglobulinemia is type III (immune complex), while autoimmune hemolysis is type II."],"dumps":[{"t":"Transfusion reaction sort","p":"Make a table of the six transfusion reaction types with columns for mechanism, timing after the transfusion starts, and the one finding that sets it apart.","l":["Acute hemolytic: ABO IgM and complement, minutes, hemoglobinuria","Delayed hemolytic: anamnestic IgG to minor antigen, days, new positive Coombs","Febrile nonhemolytic: cytokines or anti-HLA, hours, fever without hemolysis","Anaphylactic: anti-IgA in IgA deficiency, minutes, hypotension without fever","Septic: contaminated unit, often platelets, high fever and shock","TRALI: donor antibodies activate recipient neutrophils, within 6 hours, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema"]},{"t":"HIT mechanism","p":"Draw a platelet, heparin, PF4, and an IgG antibody. Show step by step how the antibody activates the platelet and why the patient ends up with low platelets and new clots.","l":["PF4 released from platelet alpha granules","Heparin binds PF4 to form a new antigen","IgG binds the heparin and PF4 complex","IgG Fc engages platelet Fc receptors","Platelets activate, aggregate, and release more PF4","Antibody-coated platelets are cleared, so the count falls","Thrombosis in veins and arteries about 5 to 10 days after heparin starts"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity (section on antibody-mediated and immune complex diseases)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders (section on immunohemolytic anemias)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Blood Types; Transfusion; Tissue and Organ Transplantation"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses (section on Epstein-Barr virus)"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-2","HE-3","HE-4","HE-9-B1","HE-9-B2","HE-9-C3","HE-9-D2"]},"HE-8":{"id":"HE-8","summary":"The organisms that live in or attack blood cells: Plasmodium, Babesia, Schistosoma, and parvovirus B19. Exam questions ask you to trace a life cycle, name the vector or host, explain what makes a pathogen dangerous, and explain why some red cell traits protect against malaria.","notes":[{"h":"Plasmodium life cycle","points":["A female Anopheles mosquito injects sporozoites from its salivary glands into the blood.","Sporozoites reach the liver within an hour and enter hepatocytes, where liver schizogony makes thousands of merozoites. This stage causes no symptoms.","Merozoites leave the liver and invade red cells: ring form, then trophozoite, then schizont, which ruptures and releases new merozoites into the blood.","Each round of red cell rupture releases parasite products (including hemozoin) that trigger cytokines such as TNF, which cause the fever paroxysm.","Some merozoites become male and female gametocytes. A feeding mosquito takes these up, and sexual reproduction happens in the mosquito gut, ending with new sporozoites in its salivary glands.","P. vivax and P. ovale leave dormant hypnozoites in the liver. These wake weeks to months later and cause relapse even after the blood stage is cleared, so a drug that kills liver stages (primaquine) is needed."]},{"h":"Why P. falciparum is the most dangerous","points":["It invades red cells of all ages, so parasitemia can climb very high. P. vivax and P. ovale prefer reticulocytes and P. malariae prefers older cells, which caps their parasitemia.","Infected cells display PfEMP1 on surface knobs. PfEMP1 binds endothelial receptors (such as CD36 and ICAM-1), so mature parasites stick to small vessel walls (sequestration).","Sequestration blocks microvessels in the brain, kidney, lung, and placenta and keeps mature parasites out of the spleen, where they would be cleared.","Because mature stages are stuck in vessels, the peripheral smear shows mostly ring forms and gametocytes.","PfEMP1 is encoded by a family of about 60 var genes. The parasite expresses one at a time and switches, so antibodies made against one version stop working (antigenic variation)."]},{"h":"Red cell traits that protect against malaria","points":["Sickle cell trait: infected HbAS cells sickle more readily at low oxygen and are removed early by the spleen, and parasite growth is impaired. Protection is mainly against severe falciparum malaria.","G6PD deficiency: infected cells are under extra oxidant stress, so they are damaged and cleared earlier, limiting parasite growth.","Thalassemia trait (alpha or beta): lowers the risk of severe malaria; the proposed mechanisms include reduced parasite growth and stronger immune clearance of infected microcytic cells.","These traits stay common in malaria regions because carriers survive childhood malaria better (balanced selection).","Duffy antigen: P. vivax uses the Duffy antigen (DARC) as its main receptor to enter reticulocytes. Duffy-negative red cells, common in people of West African ancestry, largely resist P. vivax."]},{"h":"Babesia microti","points":["An intraerythrocytic protozoan spread by the Ixodes scapularis (deer or blacklegged) tick, mostly in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States.","Also spread by blood transfusion, since the parasite survives in stored red cells.","Smear: small ring forms inside red cells and, less often, tetrads shaped like a Maltese cross, which are pathognomonic.","On a blood smear, Babesia shows no hemozoin pigment, no schizonts, and no recognizable gametocytes, which helps separate it from Plasmodium.","The same tick carries Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme disease) and Anaplasma, so coinfection is common."]},{"h":"Schistosoma life cycle","points":["Eggs passed in stool or urine hatch in fresh water into miracidia, which infect a freshwater snail (the intermediate host).","The snail releases free-swimming cercariae, which penetrate intact human skin during wading or swimming.","They become schistosomula, travel through the lungs to the portal circulation, mature, and pair as male and female adults.","Adults live in venules: S. mansoni and S. japonicum in the mesenteric venules, S. haematobium in the vesical (bladder) venous plexus.","Females lay eggs that pass through the bowel or bladder wall. Eggs that get trapped in tissue cause the disease, and adults evade immunity by coating themselves with host antigens."]},{"h":"Parvovirus B19","points":["A small, nonenveloped, single-stranded DNA virus that replicates in the nucleus of dividing cells. Spread by respiratory droplets, transfusion, and across the placenta.","It enters erythroid progenitors through the P antigen (globoside) and kills them, stopping red cell production for about a week. Giant pronormoblasts appear in the marrow.","In a healthy person red cells live about 120 days, so a one week pause barely changes hemoglobin. The child gets fifth disease (slapped cheek rash) and adults may get symmetric arthritis.","In chronic hemolytic anemia (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis) red cells live only days to weeks, so the pause causes transient aplastic crisis with a sharp fall in hemoglobin and near-zero reticulocytes.","In the fetus, red cells turn over quickly, so infection causes severe anemia, high-output heart failure, and hydrops fetalis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Plasmodium species compared","cols":["Species","Red cells infected","Fever cycle","Liver hypnozoites","Smear clues"],"rows":[["P. falciparum","All ages","Irregular, not well synchronized","No","Many rings, often several per cell; banana-shaped gametocytes"],["P. vivax","Young cells (reticulocytes)","About 48 hours","Yes","Enlarged red cells with Schuffner dots"],["P. ovale","Young cells (reticulocytes)","About 48 hours","Yes","Enlarged oval red cells with Schuffner dots"],["P. malariae","Older cells","About 72 hours","No","Band-form trophozoites"]]},{"title":"Blood-borne pathogens at a glance","cols":["Pathogen","Type","Transmission","Target and key virulence feature"],"rows":[["Plasmodium","Apicomplexan protozoan","Female Anopheles mosquito; also transfusion and congenital","Hepatocytes then red cells; PfEMP1 adherence in P. falciparum"],["Babesia microti","Apicomplexan protozoan","Ixodes scapularis tick; transfusion","Red cells; rings and Maltese cross tetrads"],["Schistosoma species","Trematode (blood fluke), separate sexes","Cercariae from freshwater snails penetrate skin","Adults in mesenteric or vesical venules; eggs cause granulomas"],["Parvovirus B19","Nonenveloped single-stranded DNA virus","Respiratory droplets, transfusion, transplacental","Erythroid progenitors via P antigen"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Vivax and ovale come back","text":"Only the two species with hypnozoites relapse from the liver: P. vivax and P. ovale. Think V and O for liver sleepers."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sticky falciparum","text":"PfEMP1 makes infected cells sticky, stickiness causes sequestration, and sequestration explains both cerebral malaria and why you see only rings and gametocytes on the smear."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One tick, three bugs","text":"Ixodes scapularis can give Lyme disease, babesiosis, and anaplasmosis at once. A patient with Lyme disease plus hemolysis should make you look for Babesia."}],"highYield":["Malaria that returns months after travel, with no new exposure, means relapse from P. vivax or P. ovale hypnozoites.","P. falciparum invades red cells of all ages and causes the highest parasitemia.","PfEMP1, encoded by switchable var genes, drives both sequestration and immune evasion.","Duffy-negative red cells resist P. vivax because the parasite needs the Duffy antigen to enter.","Maltese cross tetrads in red cells, with no pigment, point to Babesia.","S. haematobium lives in the bladder venous plexus; S. mansoni and S. japonicum live in the mesenteric veins.","Parvovirus B19 uses the P antigen and causes aplastic crisis in sickle cell disease and hereditary spherocytosis.","Parvovirus B19 in pregnancy can cause fetal anemia and hydrops fetalis."],"pitfalls":["Babesia rings can look like P. falciparum rings. Babesia has no hemozoin pigment, no recognizable gametocytes, and may form tetrads; travel history and tick exposure help.","The mosquito is the definitive host of Plasmodium (sexual reproduction happens there); humans are the intermediate host.","The snail is the intermediate host of Schistosoma; the human is the definitive host where adults mate.","Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 shows very low reticulocytes, which separates it from a hemolytic crisis, where reticulocytes rise."],"dumps":[{"t":"Malaria life cycle","p":"Draw the Plasmodium life cycle as a loop between the mosquito and the human. Label each stage, where it happens, and where hypnozoites form.","l":["Female Anopheles injects sporozoites","Sporozoites infect hepatocytes and form liver schizonts","Merozoites released into blood invade red cells","Ring, trophozoite, schizont, then rupture with fever","Some merozoites form gametocytes","Mosquito takes up gametocytes; sexual stage in mosquito gut","Hypnozoites in liver for P. vivax and P. ovale cause relapse"]},{"t":"Pathogen comparison table","p":"Make a table on blank paper for Plasmodium falciparum, Babesia microti, Schistosoma, and parvovirus B19. Columns: type of organism, how it spreads, the cell or site it targets, and its key virulence feature.","l":["P. falciparum: protozoan, Anopheles mosquito, red cells of all ages","P. falciparum: PfEMP1 sequestration and var gene switching","Babesia: protozoan, Ixodes tick or transfusion, red cells","Babesia: rings and Maltese cross tetrads, coinfection with Borrelia","Schistosoma: fluke, skin penetration by cercariae from snails","Schistosoma: adults in mesenteric or vesical venules, eggs cause disease","Parvovirus B19: nonenveloped ssDNA virus, P antigen on erythroid progenitors","Parvovirus B19: aplastic crisis in hemolytic anemia, hydrops fetalis"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology (sections on Plasmodium, Babesia, and Schistosoma)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Parvoviruses"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases (section on malaria)"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-9-E","HE-9-E1","HE-9-E2","HE-9-E3","HE-9-B1","HE-6"]},"HE-9-A":{"id":"HE-9-A","summary":"Anemias and other problems that come from making too few or too many blood cells: iron deficiency, anemia of chronic disease, sideroblastic anemia, B12 and folate deficiency, aplastic anemia, polycythemia, neutropenia, and leukemoid reactions. Exam questions give you iron studies, a smear, or a cell count and ask for the cause or the next consequence.","notes":[{"h":"Microcytic anemias","points":["Iron deficiency is the most common anemia worldwide. Causes: chronic blood loss (menstrual or gastrointestinal, such as colon cancer in an older adult), poor intake, poor absorption (celiac disease, gastrectomy), and higher need in pregnancy and growth.","Iron deficiency profile: low ferritin (the earliest change), high TIBC, low serum iron, low transferrin saturation, high RDW, microcytic hypochromic cells, and pencil cells.","Anemia of chronic disease: IL-6 from inflammation makes the liver release hepcidin, which degrades ferroportin. Iron gets trapped in macrophages and gut cells, so ferritin is normal or high and TIBC is low.","Thalassemia trait: iron studies are normal, microcytosis is out of proportion to a mild anemia, the red cell count is normal or high, and target cells appear.","Sideroblastic anemia: heme synthesis fails, so iron builds up in mitochondria. Serum iron and ferritin are high, TIBC is low, and ringed sideroblasts show on Prussian blue stain of marrow."]},{"h":"Causes of sideroblastic anemia and lead poisoning","points":["Heme synthesis starts with ALA synthase, which needs pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6).","Hereditary form: X-linked ALAS2 mutation, so most patients are male.","Acquired forms: alcohol (a mitochondrial toxin), B6 deficiency (for example from isoniazid), copper deficiency, lead, and myelodysplastic syndrome.","Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase. ALA builds up, and zinc is put into protoporphyrin in place of iron, so zinc protoporphyrin rises.","Lead also inhibits pyrimidine 5' nucleotidase, so ribosomal RNA clumps show as basophilic stippling of red cells.","Other lead clues: abdominal colic, wrist or foot drop in adults, lead lines on gums and long bone metaphyses, and learning problems in children."]},{"h":"Macrocytic anemias","points":["Megaloblastic anemia comes from impaired DNA synthesis: macro-ovalocytes, MCV usually above 100 fL, and hypersegmented neutrophils (6 or more lobes). Ineffective erythropoiesis raises LDH and indirect bilirubin.","B12 deficiency: pernicious anemia (autoantibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor, atrophic gastritis of the body and fundus), ileal resection or Crohn disease, strict vegan diet, and the fish tapeworm Dibothriocephalus latus, which competes for B12.","B12 deficiency raises both methylmalonic acid and homocysteine and can cause subacute combined degeneration: loss of dorsal columns (vibration and position sense) and lateral corticospinal tracts (weakness, spasticity).","Folate deficiency: poor intake (alcohol use, poor diet), pregnancy, chronic hemolysis (high use), and drugs that block dihydrofolate reductase (methotrexate). Homocysteine rises, methylmalonic acid stays normal, and there are no neurologic signs.","Body folate stores last months, while B12 stores last years, so folate deficiency appears much sooner.","Nonmegaloblastic macrocytosis has round macrocytes and no hypersegmented neutrophils: alcohol, liver disease (extra membrane lipid), hypothyroidism, and reticulocytosis (reticulocytes are larger cells)."]},{"h":"Aplastic anemia","points":["Failure of hematopoietic stem cells, giving pancytopenia with low reticulocytes.","Most cases are autoimmune: cytotoxic T cells attack stem cells. Other causes: drugs (chloramphenicol, alkylating agents), benzene, radiation, viral hepatitis (a seronegative hepatitis), and Fanconi anemia (inherited DNA repair defect with short stature and thumb or radial defects).","Marrow biopsy shows a hypocellular marrow replaced by fat.","No splenomegaly. An enlarged spleen points to another cause such as hypersplenism, leukemia, or myelofibrosis."]},{"h":"Polycythemia","points":["Polycythemia vera is a myeloproliferative neoplasm with JAK2 V617F in nearly all cases. Red cells grow without erythropoietin, so erythropoietin is low.","PV clues: plethora, aquagenic pruritus (itching after a warm bath, from basophil and mast cell histamine), splenomegaly, and often high white cells and platelets.","PV complications: hyperviscosity and thrombosis (stroke, myocardial infarction, Budd-Chiari syndrome), erythromelalgia (burning, red hands and feet from platelet plugs), gout from high cell turnover, and late progression to myelofibrosis (spent phase) or AML.","Secondary polycythemia with appropriate high erythropoietin: tissue hypoxia from lung disease, high altitude, cyanotic heart disease, sleep apnea, or smoking (carbon monoxide).","Secondary polycythemia with inappropriate erythropoietin: tumors that make erythropoietin, mainly renal cell carcinoma and hepatocellular carcinoma. Oxygen saturation is normal.","Relative polycythemia: red cell mass is normal and plasma volume is low (dehydration, diuretics, burns)."]},{"h":"Blood loss and white cell changes","points":["Acute blood loss: hemoglobin is normal at first because whole blood is lost. It falls over 1 to 3 days as fluid moves from tissues into vessels or IV fluid is given.","Reticulocytes begin rising after a few days and peak around 7 to 10 days; iron stores are normal unless loss continues.","Chronic blood loss slowly drains iron and leads to iron deficiency anemia.","Neutropenia is an absolute neutrophil count below 1500/µL. Below 500/µL the risk of severe bacterial and fungal infection (Candida, Aspergillus) is high.","Neutropenia mechanisms: marrow failure or infiltration; drug-induced agranulocytosis (propylthiouracil, methimazole, clozapine, chemotherapy); immune destruction (autoimmune disease, Felty syndrome); and cyclic neutropenia from ELANE (neutrophil elastase) mutations, with drops about every 21 days.","Leukemoid reaction: a marked neutrophilia from infection or inflammation with left shift, toxic granulation, Dohle bodies, and high leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP).","Chronic myeloid leukemia: neutrophilia at all stages plus basophilia, low LAP, and the BCR-ABL1 fusion from t(9;22)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Iron studies in microcytic anemia","cols":["Disorder","Serum iron","Ferritin","TIBC","Transferrin saturation","Key clue"],"rows":[["Iron deficiency","Low","Low","High","Low","High RDW, pencil cells"],["Anemia of chronic disease","Low","Normal or high","Low","Low or normal","Hepcidin traps iron in macrophages"],["Thalassemia trait","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal","Target cells, high red cell count"],["Sideroblastic anemia","High","High","Low","High","Ringed sideroblasts in marrow"]]},{"title":"B12 versus folate deficiency","cols":["Feature","B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency"],"rows":[["Megaloblastic anemia and hypersegmented neutrophils","Yes","Yes"],["Homocysteine","High","High"],["Methylmalonic acid","High","Normal"],["Neurologic damage","Yes, subacute combined degeneration","No"],["Time for stores to run out","Years","Months"],["Classic causes","Pernicious anemia, ileal disease, vegan diet, fish tapeworm","Poor diet, alcohol, pregnancy, hemolysis, methotrexate"]]},{"title":"Types of polycythemia","cols":["Type","Red cell mass","Erythropoietin","Oxygen saturation","Examples"],"rows":[["Polycythemia vera","High","Low","Normal","JAK2 V617F myeloproliferative neoplasm"],["Secondary, appropriate","High","High","Low (pulse oximetry may read normal with carbon monoxide)","Lung disease, altitude, cyanotic heart disease, smoking"],["Secondary, inappropriate","High","High","Normal","Renal cell or hepatocellular carcinoma"],["Relative","Normal","Normal","Normal","Dehydration, diuretics, burns"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Hepcidin is the jailer","text":"Inflammation raises hepcidin, hepcidin locks iron inside macrophages, so the body has iron (high ferritin) that the marrow cannot use (low serum iron)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Folate is fast, B12 is slow and nervy","text":"Folate runs out in months and spares the nerves. B12 runs out over years, raises methylmalonic acid, and damages the spinal cord."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Leukemoid or CML","text":"High LAP and toxic granules mean the marrow is answering an infection. Low LAP, basophils, and a big spleen mean CML."}],"highYield":["Low ferritin is the most specific single test for iron deficiency.","Iron deficiency anemia in an older man or a postmenopausal woman needs a search for gastrointestinal blood loss such as colon cancer.","Basophilic stippling with high zinc protoporphyrin points to lead poisoning.","Methylmalonic acid is high in B12 deficiency and normal in folate deficiency.","Pancytopenia with a fatty, hypocellular marrow and no splenomegaly is aplastic anemia.","Polycythemia with low erythropoietin is polycythemia vera; with high erythropoietin and normal oxygen saturation, look for renal cell or hepatocellular carcinoma.","Hemoglobin right after acute hemorrhage underestimates the blood lost.","An absolute neutrophil count below 500/µL means high risk of severe bacterial and fungal infection."],"pitfalls":["Ferritin is an acute phase reactant, so it can be normal in iron deficiency when inflammation is also present.","Thalassemia trait and iron deficiency are both microcytic, but only iron deficiency has low ferritin and high RDW.","Folate given alone can correct the anemia of B12 deficiency while the nerve damage keeps getting worse.","Macrocytosis from alcohol or liver disease has round macrocytes and no hypersegmented neutrophils, unlike megaloblastic anemia.","Relative polycythemia has a high hematocrit with normal red cell mass, so the cause is lost plasma."],"dumps":[{"t":"Anemia by MCV","p":"Draw a flowchart that sorts anemia by MCV into microcytic, normocytic, and macrocytic. Under each group, list each cause from this unit and the one lab or smear finding that identifies it.","l":["Microcytic: iron deficiency with low ferritin and high TIBC","Microcytic: anemia of chronic disease with high ferritin and low TIBC","Microcytic: thalassemia trait with normal iron and target cells","Microcytic: sideroblastic with high iron and ringed sideroblasts","Macrocytic megaloblastic: B12 with high methylmalonic acid","Macrocytic megaloblastic: folate with normal methylmalonic acid","Macrocytic nonmegaloblastic: alcohol, liver disease, reticulocytosis","Aplastic anemia: pancytopenia, low reticulocytes, fatty marrow"]},{"t":"Polycythemia comparison","p":"Make a table comparing polycythemia vera, secondary polycythemia from hypoxia, secondary polycythemia from a tumor, and relative polycythemia. Include red cell mass, erythropoietin, oxygen saturation, and two complications or clues for polycythemia vera.","l":["PV: JAK2 V617F, low erythropoietin","PV: aquagenic pruritus and splenomegaly","PV complications: thrombosis such as Budd-Chiari, erythromelalgia, gout","PV can progress to myelofibrosis or AML","Hypoxic: high erythropoietin, low oxygen saturation","Tumor: high erythropoietin, normal saturation, renal cell or hepatocellular carcinoma","Relative: normal red cell mass, low plasma volume"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders (sections on anemias of diminished erythropoiesis)"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Micronutrients: Vitamins (sections on folic acid and cobalamin)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Blood Disorders"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-9-A1","HE-9-A2","HE-3","HE-5","HE-9-B","HE-9-D"]},"HE-9-B":{"id":"HE-9-B","summary":"Anemias from red cells being destroyed too early: sickle cell disease, thalassemia, G6PD deficiency, hereditary spherocytosis, PNH, other membrane and enzyme defects, mechanical hemolysis, and hemolytic disease of the newborn. Spleen disorders are here too. Exam questions link the defect to the smear finding, the site of hemolysis, and the expected complication.","notes":[{"h":"Hemolysis basics","points":["All hemolysis: high reticulocytes, high LDH, high indirect bilirubin, and low haptoglobin.","Extravascular hemolysis happens in splenic macrophages: splenomegaly, jaundice, and pigment gallstones.","Intravascular hemolysis happens in vessels: free hemoglobin, very low haptoglobin, hemoglobinuria, and hemosiderinuria."]},{"h":"Sickle cell disease","points":["A point mutation in the beta globin gene (glutamate to valine at position 6) makes HbS, which polymerizes when deoxygenated. Low oxygen, acidosis, and dehydration make it worse; HbF protects infants for the first months.","Vaso-occlusion causes dactylitis (painful swollen hands and feet in infants), acute chest syndrome (the top cause of death in adults), stroke in children, priapism, and avascular necrosis.","Repeated splenic infarcts cause autosplenectomy, with risk of encapsulated bacteria and Howell-Jolly bodies on smear.","Salmonella osteomyelitis is classic in sickle cell disease, though Staphylococcus aureus is still common.","Renal medulla hypoxia causes renal papillary necrosis and inability to concentrate urine; this can happen in sickle cell trait too.","Chronic hemolysis causes pigment gallstones, and parvovirus B19 causes aplastic crisis."]},{"h":"Thalassemia","points":["Beta thalassemia major: little or no beta chain, so free alpha chains precipitate in red cell precursors and kill them in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis). Symptoms start around 6 months, when HbF production falls.","Marrow expansion gives a crew-cut skull on X-ray and maxillary overgrowth; extramedullary hematopoiesis gives hepatosplenomegaly.","Iron overload comes from transfusions and from more gut absorption (hepcidin is suppressed). Heart failure from iron is a major cause of death.","Beta thalassemia minor: mild microcytic anemia, target cells, normal iron studies, and HbA2 above about 3.5% on electrophoresis.","Alpha thalassemia depends on how many of the 4 alpha genes are lost: 3 lost gives HbH disease (beta tetramers) and 4 lost gives Hb Barts and hydrops fetalis."]},{"h":"Enzyme and membrane defects","points":["G6PD deficiency (X-linked): low NADPH means glutathione cannot be kept reduced, so oxidants denature hemoglobin. Triggers: infection, fava beans, sulfonamides, primaquine, dapsone.","G6PD smear: Heinz bodies (denatured hemoglobin, seen with supravital stain) and bite cells (splenic macrophages remove the Heinz bodies). Hemolysis is episodic and mainly intravascular.","Hereditary spherocytosis (mostly autosomal dominant): defects in ankyrin, spectrin, band 3, or protein 4.2 cause membrane loss, so cells become spheres that are trapped and destroyed in the spleen.","Spherocytosis findings: spherocytes, high MCHC, splenomegaly, pigment gallstones, increased osmotic fragility, reduced eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) binding, and a negative Coombs test.","Pyruvate kinase deficiency (autosomal recessive): red cells cannot make enough ATP by glycolysis, become rigid, and are removed in the spleen.","Hereditary elliptocytosis: spectrin defects make elliptical cells; most patients have mild or no hemolysis."]},{"h":"PNH, microangiopathy, and mechanical hemolysis","points":["PNH: an acquired PIGA mutation in a stem cell blocks GPI anchors, so CD55 and CD59 are missing and complement lyses red cells in vessels.","PNH causes hemoglobinuria, venous thrombosis at unusual sites (hepatic, portal, cerebral veins), and cytopenias. Flow cytometry shows cells lacking CD55 and CD59.","TTP: ADAMTS13 deficiency (usually an autoantibody) leaves ultralarge von Willebrand factor multimers that build platelet microthrombi. Neurologic signs are prominent.","HUS: Shiga toxin from E. coli O157:H7, usually after bloody diarrhea in a child, injures glomerular endothelium. Acute kidney injury is prominent.","Both TTP and HUS show schistocytes, thrombocytopenia, and normal PT and PTT, which separates them from DIC.","Prosthetic heart valves and severe aortic stenosis shear red cells: schistocytes and intravascular hemolysis. Iron lost in the urine over time causes iron deficiency."]},{"h":"Hemolytic disease of the newborn","points":["An Rh-negative mother exposed to Rh-positive fetal blood (prior delivery, miscarriage, trauma) makes anti-D IgG. The first pregnancy is usually spared.","In a later Rh-positive pregnancy, anti-D IgG crosses the placenta and coats fetal red cells, causing hemolysis, anemia, extramedullary hematopoiesis, and hydrops fetalis in severe cases.","Before birth the placenta clears fetal bilirubin. After birth the immature newborn liver cannot keep up, so unconjugated bilirubin rises and can cause kernicterus.","Newborn: positive direct Coombs test (IgG on the baby's red cells). Mother: positive indirect Coombs test (anti-D in her serum).","ABO disease: group O mothers make IgG anti-A and anti-B. It can affect a first pregnancy but is milder, because fetal A and B antigens are weakly expressed."]},{"h":"Spleen disorders","points":["Rupture: blunt trauma, or minor trauma to a spleen enlarged by infectious mononucleosis. Pain may spread to the left shoulder.","Infarct: sickle cell disease, emboli (endocarditis, atrial fibrillation), and massive splenomegaly. Abscess: septic emboli from infective endocarditis.","After splenectomy: Howell-Jolly bodies, target cells, and thrombocytosis, plus lifelong risk of sepsis from encapsulated bacteria.","Hypersplenism: an enlarged spleen from portal hypertension or infiltrative disease traps and destroys cells, causing cytopenias with a cellular or hypercellular marrow. Aplastic anemia has a fatty marrow and no splenomegaly."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hemolytic anemias compared","cols":["Disorder","Inheritance or cause","Defect","Main site of hemolysis","Smear or test"],"rows":[["Sickle cell disease","Autosomal recessive","HbS polymerizes when deoxygenated","Both, mainly extravascular","Sickle cells; Howell-Jolly bodies after autosplenectomy"],["Beta thalassemia major","Autosomal recessive","Absent beta chains, excess alpha chains","Marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis) and spleen","Target cells, nucleated red cells; HbF high"],["G6PD deficiency","X-linked recessive","Low NADPH, oxidant injury","Mainly intravascular, episodic","Heinz bodies, bite cells"],["Hereditary spherocytosis","Mostly autosomal dominant","Ankyrin, spectrin, band 3, or protein 4.2","Extravascular (spleen)","Spherocytes, high MCHC, low EMA binding, Coombs negative"],["Pyruvate kinase deficiency","Autosomal recessive","Low ATP, rigid cells","Extravascular (spleen)","Enzyme assay"],["Hereditary elliptocytosis","Mostly autosomal dominant","Spectrin defects","Usually mild or none","Elliptocytes"],["PNH","Acquired PIGA mutation","No CD55 or CD59, complement lysis","Intravascular","Flow cytometry for CD55 and CD59"],["TTP or HUS","ADAMTS13 deficiency or Shiga toxin","Microthrombi shear red cells","Intravascular","Schistocytes, low platelets, normal PT and PTT"],["Valve hemolysis","Prosthetic valve, aortic stenosis","Mechanical shear","Intravascular","Schistocytes, hemosiderinuria"]]},{"title":"Rh versus ABO hemolytic disease","cols":["Feature","Rh disease","ABO disease"],"rows":[["Mother","Rh negative, sensitized","Group O"],["Antibody","Anti-D IgG made after exposure","IgG anti-A or anti-B present without exposure"],["First pregnancy affected","Usually no","Can be"],["Severity","Can be severe, with hydrops fetalis","Usually mild jaundice"],["Direct Coombs in newborn","Strongly positive","Weakly positive or negative"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Bite and Heinz go together","text":"Heinz bodies are clumps of oxidant-damaged hemoglobin inside the cell; bite cells are what is left after the spleen plucks them out. Both point to G6PD deficiency."},{"kind":"trick","label":"PNH is missing its shields","text":"CD55 and CD59 are shields against complement. PIGA loss removes the GPI anchor that holds them on, so complement punches holes in red cells."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Direct on the baby, indirect in the mother","text":"The direct Coombs test finds antibody already stuck to red cells (the baby). The indirect Coombs test finds free antibody in serum (the mother)."}],"highYield":["Salmonella osteomyelitis, autosplenectomy, and aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 are classic sickle cell complications.","HbA2 above about 3.5% with microcytosis and normal iron studies means beta thalassemia minor.","Hemolysis after sulfonamides, primaquine, dapsone, or fava beans with bite cells means G6PD deficiency.","Spherocytes with a negative Coombs test and a family history point to hereditary spherocytosis; with a positive Coombs test, think warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia.","Hemolysis plus thrombosis in hepatic or cerebral veins suggests PNH.","Schistocytes and low platelets with normal PT and PTT point to TTP or HUS; prolonged PT and PTT point to DIC.","Howell-Jolly bodies and target cells mean the spleen is absent or not working.","Unconjugated bilirubin in Rh disease rises after birth because the placenta no longer clears it."],"pitfalls":["Sickle cell trait is usually silent, but renal papillary necrosis and hematuria can still occur.","The G6PD activity test can look normal during an acute episode because young cells with more enzyme survive; retest weeks later.","Hereditary spherocytosis has a high MCHC; most other anemias have a normal or low MCHC.","Hypersplenism and aplastic anemia both cause pancytopenia, but hypersplenism has a big spleen and a cellular marrow.","ABO disease can occur in a first pregnancy; Rh disease usually needs prior sensitization."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hemolytic anemia table","p":"Make a table on blank paper for sickle cell disease, beta thalassemia major, G6PD deficiency, hereditary spherocytosis, PNH, and TTP. Columns: defect, where hemolysis happens, smear or test finding, and one complication.","l":["Sickle cell: HbS polymerizes; acute chest syndrome, stroke, autosplenectomy","Beta thalassemia major: ineffective erythropoiesis, crew-cut skull, iron overload","G6PD: low NADPH, Heinz bodies and bite cells after oxidant stress","Spherocytosis: ankyrin or spectrin defect, splenic trapping, gallstones","Spherocytosis: high MCHC, low EMA binding, Coombs negative","PNH: PIGA mutation, no CD55 or CD59, venous thrombosis","TTP: ADAMTS13 deficiency, schistocytes, normal PT and PTT"]},{"t":"Rh disease timeline","p":"Draw a timeline of two pregnancies in an Rh-negative mother with Rh-positive babies. Show when she is sensitized, what crosses the placenta, what happens to the second fetus, and what happens after birth.","l":["First birth or bleed exposes mother to Rh-positive fetal cells","Mother makes anti-D, first IgM then IgG","First baby usually unaffected","Anti-D IgG crosses placenta in later pregnancy","Fetal hemolysis, anemia, extramedullary hematopoiesis, hydrops fetalis","Placenta clears bilirubin before birth","After birth unconjugated bilirubin rises, kernicterus risk","Direct Coombs positive in baby, indirect Coombs positive in mother"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders (section on hemolytic anemias)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus (section on the spleen)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Blood Disorders"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Effector Mechanisms of Humoral Immunity"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-9-B1","HE-9-B2","HE-5","HE-6","HE-7","HE-9-A","HE-8"]},"HE-9-C":{"id":"HE-9-C","summary":"Bleeding and clotting disorders: DIC, hemophilia, ITP, von Willebrand disease, vitamin K deficiency, liver failure, inherited platelet defects, thrombophilias, and paraneoplastic blood syndromes. Exam questions hand you a platelet count, PT, PTT, and bleeding pattern and ask you to name the defect.","notes":[{"h":"Read the bleeding pattern first","points":["Platelet and von Willebrand factor problems cause mucocutaneous bleeding: petechiae, nosebleeds, gum bleeding, heavy menses, and a prolonged bleeding time.","Clotting factor problems cause deep bleeding: hemarthroses, muscle hematomas, and bleeding that starts hours after surgery.","PT tests the extrinsic and common pathways (factor VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen). PTT tests the intrinsic and common pathways (XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, fibrinogen).","A mixing study adds normal plasma: correction means a factor is missing; no correction means an inhibitor is present."]},{"h":"DIC","points":["Triggers: sepsis (most common), major trauma and burns, obstetric events (placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism), acute promyelocytic leukemia, and mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas (pancreas, lung, stomach).","Tissue factor or endothelial injury sets off widespread clotting that uses up platelets and factors and turns on fibrinolysis.","Labs: low platelets, prolonged PT and PTT, low fibrinogen, high D-dimer, and schistocytes.","Microthrombi injure the kidneys (acute kidney injury), lungs (ARDS), brain (confusion), and adrenals (hemorrhagic adrenal necrosis, as in meningococcemia).","Consumption of platelets and factors causes bleeding at the same time: oozing from IV and venipuncture sites, mucosa, and wounds."]},{"h":"Hemophilia and von Willebrand disease","points":["Hemophilia A (factor VIII) and B (factor IX) are X-linked recessive, so affected patients are almost all male. About a third have no family history (new mutation).","Hemophilia labs: isolated prolonged PTT, normal PT, normal platelet count, normal bleeding time, and correction on mixing.","An acquired factor VIII inhibitor (autoantibody in older adults, postpartum women, or autoimmune disease) also prolongs the PTT, but it does not correct on mixing.","Von Willebrand disease is the most common inherited bleeding disorder, usually autosomal dominant.","Von Willebrand factor links platelet GpIb to exposed collagen (adhesion) and carries factor VIII, protecting it from breakdown.","VWD labs: normal platelet count, prolonged bleeding time, normal or prolonged PTT (from low factor VIII), normal PT, and low ristocetin cofactor activity."]},{"h":"ITP and inherited platelet defects","points":["ITP: IgG autoantibodies to platelet GpIIb/IIIa coat platelets, which splenic macrophages destroy. The spleen also makes much of the antibody.","Children: acute ITP a few weeks after a viral infection, usually resolves on its own. Adults: chronic ITP, most often in women of reproductive age, sometimes with SLE, HIV, or hepatitis C.","ITP labs: isolated thrombocytopenia, normal PT and PTT, large platelets, and more megakaryocytes in the marrow. The spleen is not enlarged.","Bernard-Soulier syndrome: GpIb deficiency, giant platelets, mild thrombocytopenia, and defective adhesion. Ristocetin does not aggregate platelets.","Glanzmann thrombasthenia: GpIIb/IIIa deficiency, normal platelet count and size, and defective aggregation. Platelets do not clump with ADP or collagen.","Uremia: retained toxins impair platelet adhesion and aggregation, so bleeding time is prolonged with a normal count."]},{"h":"Vitamin K deficiency and liver failure","points":["Vitamin K is needed to gamma-carboxylate factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S.","Causes: newborns not given vitamin K at birth (sterile gut, little in breast milk), fat malabsorption (cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, biliary obstruction), long courses of broad-spectrum antibiotics, and poor intake.","Factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 6 hours), so the PT prolongs first. PTT prolongs later as the deficiency deepens.","Vitamin K deficiency: prolonged PT, normal fibrinogen, normal platelets, normal factor VIII.","Liver failure: prolonged PT and PTT, low fibrinogen, and thrombocytopenia (splenic sequestration and low thrombopoietin). Factor VIII stays normal or high because endothelial cells make it."]},{"h":"Thrombophilia and paraneoplastic syndromes","points":["Antithrombin III deficiency: thrombin and factor Xa are not inactivated; heparin has a weak effect because it works through antithrombin.","Protein C or S deficiency: factors Va and VIIIa are not inactivated. Starting warfarin can cause skin necrosis because protein C falls before the clotting factors.","Hyperhomocysteinemia (from B12, folate, or B6 deficiency, or cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency) injures endothelium and raises venous and arterial clot risk.","Suspect an inherited thrombophilia with a clot at a young age, recurrent clots, clots at unusual sites (cerebral sinus, mesenteric, hepatic veins), or a strong family history.","Trousseau syndrome: migratory superficial thrombophlebitis with pancreatic and other mucin-producing adenocarcinomas.","Erythrocytosis from ectopic erythropoietin: renal cell carcinoma and hepatocellular carcinoma. Pure red cell aplasia: thymoma."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Bleeding disorder lab patterns","cols":["Disorder","Platelets","Bleeding time","PT","PTT","Other key finding"],"rows":[["DIC","Low","Prolonged","Prolonged","Prolonged","Low fibrinogen, high D-dimer, schistocytes"],["Hemophilia A or B","Normal","Normal","Normal","Prolonged","Corrects on mixing; low factor VIII or IX"],["Acquired factor VIII inhibitor","Normal","Normal","Normal","Prolonged","Does not correct on mixing"],["ITP","Low","Prolonged","Normal","Normal","More megakaryocytes in marrow"],["Von Willebrand disease","Normal","Prolonged","Normal","Normal or prolonged","Low ristocetin cofactor activity"],["Vitamin K deficiency","Normal","Normal","Prolonged first","Normal or prolonged","Normal fibrinogen and factor VIII"],["Liver failure","Low","Normal or prolonged","Prolonged","Prolonged","Low fibrinogen, normal or high factor VIII"],["Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Mildly low, giant platelets","Prolonged","Normal","Normal","GpIb missing; no aggregation with ristocetin"],["Glanzmann thrombasthenia","Normal","Prolonged","Normal","Normal","GpIIb/IIIa missing; no aggregation with ADP or collagen"]]},{"title":"Causes of thrombophilia","cols":["Condition","Mechanism","Clue"],"rows":[["Antithrombin III deficiency","Thrombin and Xa not inactivated","Weak response to heparin; acquired form in nephrotic syndrome"],["Protein C deficiency","Va and VIIIa not inactivated","Warfarin-induced skin necrosis"],["Protein S deficiency","Loss of protein C cofactor","Young venous clots, family history"],["Factor V Leiden","Factor Va resists activated protein C","Most common inherited thrombophilia in people of European ancestry"],["Hyperhomocysteinemia","Endothelial injury","Low B12, folate, or B6; homocystinuria"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Platelet bleeds are shallow, factor bleeds are deep","text":"Skin and mucosal bleeding means a platelet or von Willebrand factor problem. Joint and muscle bleeding means a clotting factor problem."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Seven goes first","text":"Factor VII has the shortest half-life, so vitamin K deficiency, early liver disease, and new warfarin use all prolong the PT before the PTT."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Factor VIII separates liver from DIC","text":"Liver failure and DIC both prolong PT and PTT and lower fibrinogen. Factor VIII is normal or high in liver failure and low in DIC."}],"highYield":["Bleeding from IV sites plus low fibrinogen and high D-dimer means DIC; acute promyelocytic leukemia and amniotic fluid embolism are classic triggers.","A boy with hemarthroses and an isolated prolonged PTT that corrects on mixing has hemophilia A or B.","A prolonged PTT that does not correct on mixing points to an inhibitor such as an acquired factor VIII antibody.","Isolated thrombocytopenia after a viral illness in a child, with a normal spleen, is acute ITP.","Heavy menses and nosebleeds with a normal platelet count and low ristocetin cofactor activity is von Willebrand disease.","A newborn who bleeds on day 2 to 7 without vitamin K prophylaxis has vitamin K deficiency bleeding.","Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis can be the first sign of pancreatic adenocarcinoma."],"pitfalls":["Von Willebrand disease can prolong the PTT because factor VIII loses its carrier; it can be mistaken for hemophilia.","Bernard-Soulier syndrome and von Willebrand disease both fail with ristocetin; adding normal plasma corrects VWD but not Bernard-Soulier.","ITP and TTP both have low platelets; TTP also has schistocytes, hemolysis, and often neurologic and renal signs.","Bleeding time and PFA tests reflect platelet function; they stay normal in hemophilia."],"dumps":[{"t":"Coagulation cascade and lab tests","p":"Draw the clotting cascade with the extrinsic, intrinsic, and common pathways. Circle the factors the PT measures and the factors the PTT measures, and mark the vitamin K dependent factors.","l":["Extrinsic: tissue factor with factor VII, measured by PT","Intrinsic: XII, XI, IX, VIII, measured by PTT","Common: X, V, II, fibrinogen, measured by both","Vitamin K dependent: II, VII, IX, X, proteins C and S","Factor VII has the shortest half-life","Hemophilia A is factor VIII and hemophilia B is factor IX","Antithrombin inactivates thrombin and Xa; protein C with S inactivates Va and VIIIa"]},{"t":"Bleeding disorder lab table","p":"Make a table with columns for platelet count, bleeding time, PT, and PTT. Fill in rows for DIC, hemophilia, ITP, von Willebrand disease, vitamin K deficiency, and liver failure, and add one extra clue for each.","l":["DIC: everything abnormal, low fibrinogen, high D-dimer","Hemophilia: only PTT prolonged, corrects on mixing","ITP: only platelets low and bleeding time prolonged","VWD: bleeding time prolonged, PTT normal or prolonged, low ristocetin cofactor","Vitamin K deficiency: PT prolonged first, fibrinogen normal","Liver failure: PT and PTT prolonged, low platelets, factor VIII normal or high"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders (section on bleeding disorders)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Blood Disorders"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-9-C1","HE-9-C2","HE-9-C3","HE-9-C4","HE-9-C5","HE-4","HE-9-B"]},"HE-9-D":{"id":"HE-9-D","summary":"Cancers of blood-forming cells and lymphocytes: acute and chronic leukemias, Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphomas, plasma cell disorders, myelodysplasia, myelofibrosis, and Langerhans cell histiocytosis, plus tumor lysis syndrome. Exam questions match a patient's age, smear, marker, or translocation to the right neoplasm and predict its complications.","notes":[{"h":"Acute leukemias","points":["Blasts fill the marrow and crowd out normal cells: anemia, bleeding from low platelets, and infections from low neutrophils.","ALL: the most common cancer of children. Lymphoblasts are TdT positive; B-ALL is usually CD10 and CD19 positive. Bone pain, hepatosplenomegaly, and lymphadenopathy are common.","ALL spreads to the CNS and testes, sites shielded from chemotherapy. Down syndrome raises the risk.","AML: mainly older adults, usually 20% or more marrow blasts. Blasts are myeloperoxidase positive and may contain Auer rods.","AML risks: prior chemotherapy or radiation, benzene, myelodysplastic syndrome, and Down syndrome.","Acute promyelocytic leukemia, t(15;17) PML-RARA: promyelocyte granules set off DIC, which is an emergency."]},{"h":"Chronic leukemias and myeloid neoplasms","points":["CLL: older adults, mature B cells that are CD5 and CD23 positive, smudge cells on smear, often found by chance as lymphocytosis.","CLL complications: hypogammaglobulinemia with infections, warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, and Richter transformation to diffuse large B cell lymphoma.","CML: t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome makes the BCR-ABL1 tyrosine kinase. Neutrophils at every stage of maturation, basophilia, and splenomegaly; confirm by finding BCR-ABL1.","CML moves from chronic phase to accelerated phase to blast crisis, which behaves like acute leukemia.","Hairy cell leukemia: middle-aged men, B cells with hairlike projections, TRAP and CD11c positive, BRAF V600E, marrow fibrosis giving a dry tap, and massive splenomegaly without lymphadenopathy.","Myelodysplastic syndromes: clonal, ineffective hematopoiesis in older adults or after alkylating agents or radiation. Cytopenias, pseudo-Pelger-Huet neutrophils, ringed sideroblasts, and risk of AML.","Primary myelofibrosis: JAK2, CALR, or MPL mutations; megakaryocytes release growth factors that drive marrow fibrosis. Dry tap, teardrop cells, leukoerythroblastic smear, and massive splenomegaly from extramedullary hematopoiesis."]},{"h":"Hodgkin lymphoma","points":["Reed-Sternberg cells: large cells with two mirror-image nuclei, CD15 and CD30 positive, in a background of reactive cells.","Spreads in order to neighboring node groups, so it is often localized. Bimodal age (young adults and older adults). Some cases are linked to EBV.","Nodular sclerosis is the most common type and often shows a mediastinal mass in a young adult.","Fever, night sweats, and weight loss (B symptoms) signal worse disease."]},{"h":"Non-Hodgkin and T cell lymphomas","points":["Non-Hodgkin lymphoma spreads to noncontiguous nodes and often involves extranodal sites; most are B cell tumors.","Diffuse large B cell lymphoma is the most common and aggressive. Follicular lymphoma, t(14;18) BCL2, is indolent with waxing and waning nodes and can transform to DLBCL.","Burkitt lymphoma, t(8;14) MYC: starry sky pattern, very fast growth, EBV link; jaw mass in the endemic African form, abdominal mass in the sporadic form.","Mantle cell lymphoma, t(11;14) cyclin D1: CD5 positive, CD23 negative, older men. MALT lymphoma of the stomach is driven by Helicobacter pylori and can regress after eradication.","Adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma: HTLV-1, CD4 T cells, skin lesions, hypercalcemia, and lytic bone lesions.","Mycosis fungoides: skin-homing CD4 T cells in patches and plaques, Pautrier microabscesses in the epidermis. Spread to the blood as Sezary cells (cerebriform nuclei) is Sezary syndrome."]},{"h":"Plasma cell neoplasms","points":["Multiple myeloma: clonal plasma cells in the marrow of older adults, making an M spike (most often IgG) on serum protein electrophoresis.","Myeloma cells increase RANKL signaling, so osteoclasts make punched-out lytic lesions, fractures, and hypercalcemia.","Light chains filtered by the kidney form tubular casts (myeloma kidney) and appear in urine as Bence Jones protein. High serum protein makes red cells stack as rouleaux.","Normal antibodies fall, so bacterial infections are common. Anemia comes from marrow crowding.","MGUS: M protein below 3 g/dL, under 10% marrow plasma cells, and no end-organ damage. About 1% per year progress.","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia: IgM M spike, hyperviscosity (blurred vision, headache, bleeding), lymphadenopathy, and no lytic lesions.","AL amyloidosis: light chains fold into amyloid in kidney (nephrotic syndrome), heart (restrictive cardiomyopathy), tongue, and nerves."]},{"h":"Langerhans cell histiocytosis and tumor lysis syndrome","points":["LCH: clonal Langerhans cells that are CD1a and S100 positive with Birbeck granules (tennis racket shape on electron microscopy).","Children present with a lytic bone lesion, often in the skull (eosinophilic granuloma), or with multisystem disease; pituitary stalk involvement causes diabetes insipidus.","Tumor lysis syndrome: rapid killing of fast-growing tumors (Burkitt lymphoma, acute leukemias), usually soon after chemotherapy starts.","Dying cells release potassium, phosphate, and nucleic acids (broken down to uric acid). Phosphate binds calcium, so calcium falls.","Result: hyperkalemia (arrhythmias), hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia (tetany), hyperuricemia, and acute kidney injury from urate and calcium phosphate crystals."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Leukemias compared","cols":["Leukemia","Typical age","Cell and markers","Classic findings"],"rows":[["ALL","Children","Lymphoblasts, TdT positive","Bone pain, CNS and testis spread, Down syndrome risk"],["AML","Older adults","Myeloblasts, myeloperoxidase positive, Auer rods","20% or more blasts; APL with t(15;17) causes DIC"],["CLL","Older adults","Mature B cells, CD5 and CD23 positive","Smudge cells, hypogammaglobulinemia, autoimmune hemolysis, Richter transformation"],["CML","Middle-aged adults","Myeloid cells at all stages, BCR-ABL1","Basophilia, splenomegaly, low LAP, blast crisis"],["Hairy cell leukemia","Middle-aged men","Mature B cells, TRAP and CD11c positive, BRAF V600E","Dry tap, massive splenomegaly, no lymphadenopathy"]]},{"title":"Lymphomas compared","cols":["Lymphoma","Genetic or infectious link","Key feature","Behavior"],"rows":[["Hodgkin, nodular sclerosis","EBV in some cases","Reed-Sternberg cells CD15 and CD30 positive; mediastinal mass","Contiguous spread, often localized"],["Diffuse large B cell","Can arise from follicular lymphoma or CLL","Sheets of large B cells","Aggressive, most common NHL"],["Follicular","t(14;18) BCL2","Back-to-back follicles","Indolent, may transform"],["Burkitt","t(8;14) MYC; EBV","Starry sky pattern","Very fast growing"],["Mantle cell","t(11;14) cyclin D1","CD5 positive, CD23 negative","Aggressive, older men"],["MALT (marginal zone)","Helicobacter pylori","Gastric mucosal lymphoma","Indolent, may regress with H. pylori cure"],["Adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma","HTLV-1","CD4 T cells, hypercalcemia, lytic bone lesions","Aggressive"],["Mycosis fungoides","None known","Skin CD4 T cells, Pautrier microabscesses, Sezary cells","Indolent skin disease that can spread to blood"]]},{"title":"Plasma cell disorders","cols":["Disorder","M protein","Marrow plasma cells","End-organ damage or key feature"],"rows":[["MGUS","Below 3 g/dL","Under 10%","None"],["Multiple myeloma","Usually IgG or IgA, often 3 g/dL or more","10% or more","Hypercalcemia, renal injury, anemia, lytic bone lesions"],["Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia","IgM","Lymphoplasmacytic cells","Hyperviscosity, no lytic lesions"],["AL amyloidosis","Free light chains","Small clone","Amyloid in kidney, heart, tongue, nerves"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CRAB","text":"Calcium high, Renal injury, Anemia, Bone lytic lesions: the end-organ damage that defines multiple myeloma and is absent in MGUS."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Translocation pairs","text":"Burkitt 8;14 (MYC), follicular 14;18 (BCL2), mantle cell 11;14 (cyclin D1), CML 9;22 (BCR-ABL1), APL 15;17 (PML-RARA). Chromosome 14 carries the immunoglobulin heavy chain gene in the B cell ones."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Who has CD5","text":"The two classic CD5 positive B cell neoplasms are CLL and mantle cell lymphoma. CD23 separates them (positive in CLL, negative in mantle cell)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Dry tap pair","text":"A dry tap means a fibrotic marrow. In a patient with a huge spleen, think primary myelofibrosis (teardrop cells) or hairy cell leukemia (TRAP positive)."}],"highYield":["Auer rods are diagnostic of a myeloid blast; DIC in a new leukemia points to acute promyelocytic leukemia.","TdT marks lymphoblasts of ALL and is absent in mature B cell neoplasms such as CLL.","Smudge cells in an older adult with lymphocytosis mean CLL.","Basophilia with neutrophils at every stage and low LAP means CML; BCR-ABL1 confirms it.","Reed-Sternberg cells are CD15 and CD30 positive; nodular sclerosis is the most common Hodgkin type.","Punched-out skull lesions, rouleaux, and an M spike mean multiple myeloma.","Teardrop cells with a leukoerythroblastic smear mean marrow fibrosis.","After starting chemotherapy for Burkitt lymphoma, watch for high potassium, phosphate, and uric acid with low calcium."],"pitfalls":["AML and ALL both cause pancytopenia and bone pain; myeloperoxidase and Auer rods mark AML, TdT marks ALL.","A leukemoid reaction can look like CML, but it has high LAP and no basophilia or BCR-ABL1.","Myeloma lesions are purely lytic, so a bone scan (which shows bone formation) can miss them.","MGUS has an M protein but no CRAB features; do not call it myeloma.","Hodgkin lymphoma spreads to neighboring nodes in order; non-Hodgkin lymphoma skips around and goes extranodal."],"dumps":[{"t":"Leukemia comparison table","p":"Make a table for ALL, AML, CLL, CML, and hairy cell leukemia. Columns: typical age, cell type, the marker or genetic change that identifies it, and one complication.","l":["ALL: children, TdT positive lymphoblasts, CNS and testis spread","AML: older adults, myeloperoxidase positive, Auer rods","APL: t(15;17) PML-RARA, DIC","CLL: older adults, CD5 and CD23 positive, smudge cells","CLL: autoimmune hemolysis and Richter transformation","CML: t(9;22) BCR-ABL1, basophilia, blast crisis","Hairy cell: TRAP and CD11c positive, BRAF V600E, dry tap, big spleen"]},{"t":"Myeloma mechanisms","p":"Draw a plasma cell in the marrow and draw arrows from it to each problem it causes in bone, blood, kidney, and immune function. Label the mechanism on each arrow.","l":["RANKL increase activates osteoclasts: lytic lesions and fractures","Bone resorption releases calcium: hypercalcemia","Light chains form tubular casts: myeloma kidney","Bence Jones protein in urine","Marrow crowding: anemia","High serum protein: rouleaux","Low normal antibodies: infections","M spike on electrophoresis; light chains can form AL amyloid"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Blood Disorders"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Neoplasia"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-9-D1","HE-9-D2","HE-9-D3","HE-6","HE-9-A","HE-9-C1"]},"HE-9-E":{"id":"HE-9-E","summary":"Infections that show up in the blood and lymphatic system: babesiosis, malaria, schistosomiasis, mosquito-borne viruses, filoviruses, plague, cat scratch disease, and lymphangitis. Exam questions tie the exposure and smear or biopsy finding to the organism and predict complications.","notes":[{"h":"Babesiosis","points":["Follows an Ixodes tick bite in the northeastern or upper midwestern United States, usually in summer, or a blood transfusion.","Findings: fever, chills, fatigue, hemolytic anemia (high LDH, high indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin), and thrombocytopenia.","Severe disease risk: asplenia, older age, and immunosuppression (HIV, cancer, transplant, rituximab).","Severe cases can bring ARDS, DIC, kidney injury, and heart failure."]},{"h":"Malaria","points":["Fever paroxysms (chills, then high fever, then sweating) come when a synchronized group of infected red cells ruptures.","The cycle is about 48 hours for P. vivax and P. ovale and about 72 hours for P. malariae. P. falciparum fevers are often irregular.","Red cell rupture and splenic removal of infected and uninfected cells cause hemolytic anemia; the spleen enlarges and thrombocytopenia is common.","P. falciparum complications come from high parasitemia and sequestration: cerebral malaria (confusion, seizures, coma), severe anemia, hypoglycemia, acute kidney injury, hemoglobinuria, and acute respiratory distress.","Falciparum smear: many ring forms, often more than one per cell, and banana-shaped (crescent) gametocytes."]},{"h":"Schistosomiasis","points":["Disease comes from eggs trapped in tissue. A CD4 T helper 2 response builds granulomas with eosinophils, which later scar.","S. mansoni and S. japonicum eggs lodge in portal venules: periportal pipestem fibrosis and presinusoidal portal hypertension with splenomegaly and varices. Liver synthetic function is usually preserved.","S. haematobium eggs lodge in the bladder wall: hematuria, bladder fibrosis and calcification, obstruction, and squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder.","Peripheral eosinophilia is a key clue. Acute infection can cause fever, cough, and eosinophilia a few weeks after exposure (Katayama fever)."]},{"h":"Aedes-borne and filovirus infections","points":["Dengue (flavivirus, 4 serotypes): fever, severe headache behind the eyes, muscle and joint pain, rash, leukopenia, and thrombocytopenia.","Severe dengue is more likely after reinfection with a different serotype. Non-neutralizing antibodies help the virus enter Fc receptor-bearing cells (antibody-dependent enhancement), leading to plasma leakage, shock, and bleeding.","Chikungunya (alphavirus): high fever and severe symmetric joint pain that can persist for months.","Zika (flavivirus): usually mild rash, conjunctivitis, and joint pain; infection in pregnancy causes congenital microcephaly. Zika can also spread sexually and has been linked to Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Ebola and Marburg viruses: filamentous, enveloped, negative-sense RNA filoviruses. Fruit bats are the likely reservoir; people catch them through contact with blood or body fluids of an infected person or animal.","Filovirus illness: fever and weakness, then vomiting and diarrhea, then in many patients hemorrhage, DIC, shock, and multiorgan failure."]},{"h":"Lymphadenitis and lymphangitis","points":["Yersinia pestis: gram-negative coccobacillus with bipolar (safety pin) staining. Rodent fleas transmit it; in the United States most cases are in the Southwest.","Plague forms: bubonic (painful, swollen, suppurative node near the bite), septicemic (DIC, gangrene of fingers and toes), and pneumonic (spreads person to person by droplets).","Bartonella henselae: from a cat scratch or bite, often a kitten. A papule at the site is followed by tender regional lymphadenopathy; biopsy shows stellate necrotizing granulomas.","Acute lymphangitis: Streptococcus pyogenes spreads along lymphatics from a skin wound, causing red streaks toward tender regional nodes, with fever.","Reactive node patterns: follicular hyperplasia (B cell areas; rheumatoid arthritis, early HIV, toxoplasmosis), paracortical hyperplasia (T cell areas; viral infections such as mononucleosis, some drugs), and sinus histiocytosis (macrophages in sinuses; nodes draining a cancer).","Reactive follicles vary in size, have tingible body macrophages, and lack BCL2 in germinal centers. Follicular lymphoma has crowded uniform follicles, effaced architecture, and BCL2 positive germinal centers."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infections of the blood compared","cols":["Infection","Exposure","Blood or tissue finding","Major complications"],"rows":[["Babesiosis","Ixodes tick in northeastern or upper midwestern US; transfusion","Ring forms, Maltese cross tetrads, no pigment","Severe hemolysis, ARDS, DIC in asplenic, older, or immunosuppressed patients"],["Malaria (P. falciparum)","Anopheles mosquito in endemic area","Many rings, banana-shaped gametocytes","Cerebral malaria, severe anemia, hypoglycemia, kidney injury, hemoglobinuria"],["Malaria (P. vivax or P. ovale)","Anopheles mosquito in endemic area","Enlarged red cells with Schuffner dots","Relapse from hypnozoites, splenomegaly"],["Schistosomiasis (S. mansoni, S. japonicum)","Freshwater skin contact","Eggs in stool, eosinophilia","Pipestem fibrosis, presinusoidal portal hypertension"],["Schistosomiasis (S. haematobium)","Freshwater skin contact","Eggs in urine, eosinophilia","Hematuria, bladder fibrosis, squamous cell carcinoma"]]},{"title":"Aedes-borne viruses","cols":["Virus","Family","Hallmark","Severe outcome"],"rows":[["Dengue","Flavivirus","Fever, retro-orbital headache, myalgia, thrombocytopenia","Plasma leakage and shock after reinfection with another serotype"],["Chikungunya","Alphavirus","Severe symmetric arthralgia","Joint pain lasting months"],["Zika","Flavivirus","Mild rash, conjunctivitis","Congenital microcephaly"]]},{"title":"Reactive lymph node patterns","cols":["Pattern","Zone expanded","Common causes"],"rows":[["Follicular hyperplasia","B cell follicles","Rheumatoid arthritis, early HIV, toxoplasmosis"],["Paracortical hyperplasia","T cell paracortex","Viral infections such as mononucleosis, some drug reactions"],["Sinus histiocytosis","Sinuses filled with macrophages","Nodes draining a cancer"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Tertian and quartan","text":"Fever every third day (48 hours) is P. vivax or P. ovale; every fourth day (72 hours) is P. malariae. Count the fever day as day 1."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Eggs do the damage","text":"In schistosomiasis the adult worms hide quietly; the trapped eggs make granulomas. Mesenteric species scar the liver, the bladder species scars the bladder."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Second dengue is the dangerous one","text":"Antibodies from the first serotype help the second serotype get into macrophages, so the second infection is the one that leaks and shocks."}],"highYield":["Fever and hemolytic anemia after a summer in New England in a patient without a spleen points to babesiosis.","Banana-shaped gametocytes and multiple rings per red cell mean P. falciparum.","Cerebral malaria and hypoglycemia are P. falciparum complications.","S. haematobium is linked to squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder.","S. mansoni causes presinusoidal portal hypertension with preserved liver function and eosinophilia.","Severe dengue follows a second infection with a different serotype.","A painful suppurative bubo after a flea bite in the US Southwest is bubonic plague.","Tender axillary nodes with stellate necrotizing granulomas after a kitten scratch is cat scratch disease."],"pitfalls":["Babesia and P. falciparum both show small rings; only Plasmodium shows pigment and recognizable gametocytes on smear.","Schistosomal portal hypertension is presinusoidal, so liver function stays normal, unlike cirrhosis.","Chikungunya and dengue overlap early; prolonged severe arthralgia favors chikungunya, while bleeding and plasma leakage favor dengue.","A plague bubo is painful; the nodes of lymphoma are usually painless."],"dumps":[{"t":"Blood parasite comparison","p":"Make a table comparing babesiosis, P. falciparum malaria, P. vivax malaria, and schistosomiasis. Columns: how it is caught, what you see on smear or in samples, who gets severe disease, and the major complication.","l":["Babesiosis: Ixodes tick or transfusion, rings and Maltese cross tetrads","Babesiosis: severe in asplenia, older age, immunosuppression","P. falciparum: many rings, banana gametocytes","P. falciparum: cerebral malaria, severe anemia, hypoglycemia, kidney injury","P. vivax: 48 hour fever, relapse from hypnozoites","Schistosomiasis: freshwater exposure, eggs in stool or urine, eosinophilia","S. mansoni: pipestem fibrosis and portal hypertension","S. haematobium: hematuria and bladder squamous cell carcinoma"]},{"t":"Lymphadenitis causes","p":"List four infections that cause enlarged or painful lymph nodes or red streaks on the skin: plague, cat scratch disease, strep lymphangitis, and a viral cause of paracortical hyperplasia. For each, write the exposure, the organism, and the node or skin finding.","l":["Plague: rodent flea, Yersinia pestis, bipolar staining","Plague: painful suppurative bubo, septicemic and pneumonic spread","Cat scratch: Bartonella henselae from a kitten scratch","Cat scratch: regional nodes with stellate necrotizing granulomas","Lymphangitis: Streptococcus pyogenes from a skin wound, red streaks to nodes","Mononucleosis: paracortical T cell hyperplasia","Reactive follicles lack BCL2 and keep tingible body macrophages, unlike follicular lymphoma"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on arthropod-borne viral diseases"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus (section on reactive lymphadenitis)"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-9-E1","HE-9-E2","HE-9-E3","HE-8","HE-9-C1","HE-9-D2"]},"IM-1":{"id":"IM-1","summary":"How the thymus forms from the third pharyngeal pouch, fills with T cell precursors, sinks into the anterior mediastinum, and shrinks after puberty. Exam questions link the pouch origin to DiGeorge syndrome and ask you to sort out anterior mediastinal masses.","notes":[{"h":"Origin and descent","points":["The thymic epithelium comes from endoderm of the third pharyngeal pouch (its ventral part).","The dorsal part of the same pouch forms the inferior parathyroid glands, so thymus and inferior parathyroids share an origin.","The fourth pouch forms the superior parathyroids.","Neural crest derived mesenchyme forms the thymic capsule, the septa that divide it into lobules, and the connective tissue around its vessels.","The thymic primordia leave the pharynx, fuse in the midline, and descend into the anterior mediastinum, pulling the inferior parathyroids down below the superior ones."]},{"h":"Filling the thymus with T cells","points":["The endoderm forms a meshwork of epithelial reticular cells; it does not make lymphocytes itself.","T cell precursors made in fetal liver and bone marrow travel in the blood and colonize this framework during fetal life.","The lobules then split into a dense outer cortex of immature thymocytes and a paler inner medulla.","Hassall corpuscles, whorls of keratinizing epithelial reticular cells, appear in the medulla."]},{"h":"DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion)","points":["The third and fourth pouches fail to develop, so the thymus and parathyroids are small or absent.","No thymus gives an absent thymic shadow on chest X-ray and a low T cell count, with viral, fungal, and Pneumocystis infections.","No parathyroids gives low PTH and hypocalcemia, which shows up as tetany or seizures in the newborn.","Neural crest cells also fail to build the outflow tract, so conotruncal defects occur: truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot, interrupted aortic arch.","Other features: cleft palate and a typical facial appearance."]},{"h":"Involution and the adult thymus","points":["The thymus is largest relative to body size at birth and reaches its largest absolute size around puberty.","After puberty the cortex shrinks and adipose tissue replaces most of the lymphoid tissue (involution).","Long-lived naive and memory T cells already in the blood and nodes keep dividing on their own, so immunity persists after involution or adult thymectomy.","The adult thymus sits in the anterior and superior mediastinum, behind the manubrium and in front of the great vessels and pericardium."]},{"h":"Anterior mediastinal masses","points":["Thymoma: an epithelial tumor of the thymus in adults, linked to myasthenia gravis, pure red cell aplasia, and hypogammaglobulinemia.","Teratoma: a germ cell tumor that may contain fat, calcification, or teeth.","Lymphoma: often Hodgkin (nodular sclerosis) in young adults, with fevers, night sweats, or weight loss.","Retrosternal thyroid: a goiter that extends down from the neck behind the sternum."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pharyngeal pouch derivatives","cols":["Pouch","Main adult structures"],"rows":[["First","Middle ear cavity, auditory (eustachian) tube, mastoid air cells"],["Second","Lining of the palatine tonsil crypts"],["Third","Thymus (ventral part) and inferior parathyroids (dorsal part)"],["Fourth","Superior parathyroids and the ultimobranchial body (parafollicular C cells)"]]},{"title":"Anterior mediastinal masses","cols":["Mass","Typical patient","Clues"],"rows":[["Thymoma","Adult, often over 40","Myasthenia gravis, pure red cell aplasia, hypogammaglobulinemia"],["Teratoma","Young adult","Fat, calcification, or teeth on imaging"],["Lymphoma","Young adult (Hodgkin) or any age","B symptoms, other enlarged nodes"],["Retrosternal thyroid","Older adult","Mass continuous with a neck goiter"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CATCH-22","text":"Cardiac (conotruncal) defects, Abnormal facies, Thymic hypoplasia, Cleft palate, Hypocalcemia, chromosome 22q11 deletion."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Three goes lower","text":"The third pouch makes the inferior parathyroids because the thymus drags them down as it descends. The fourth pouch parathyroids stay higher, so they become the superior pair."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 4 Ts","text":"Anterior mediastinal masses: Thymoma, Teratoma, Terrible lymphoma, Thyroid (retrosternal)."}],"highYield":["Absent thymic shadow plus neonatal hypocalcemia plus a conotruncal heart defect means DiGeorge syndrome.","DiGeorge syndrome causes a T cell deficiency; B cells are present, though antibody responses to protein antigens can be weak without T cell help.","The thymus is endoderm (epithelium) plus neural crest (capsule and septa) plus bone marrow derived T cells.","Thymoma is the anterior mediastinal mass tied to myasthenia gravis.","Adult thymectomy for myasthenia gravis does not cause an overt T cell immunodeficiency like DiGeorge syndrome, because peripheral T cells persist.","The fat-replaced thymus of an adult is normal involution, not disease."],"pitfalls":["The inferior parathyroids come from the third pouch and the superior ones from the fourth; the numbers run opposite to their final height.","Thymic epithelial cells are endoderm, while the T cells inside the thymus come from bone marrow precursors.","DiGeorge hypocalcemia comes from missing parathyroids, not from vitamin D deficiency; PTH is low.","Thymoma is a tumor of thymic epithelial cells; thymic lymphoma is a tumor of lymphocytes."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pharyngeal pouches to adult glands","p":"Draw the four pharyngeal pouches and draw arrows showing where the thymus and each pair of parathyroids end up in the neck and chest.","l":["Third pouch ventral part forms the thymus","Third pouch dorsal part forms the inferior parathyroids","Fourth pouch forms the superior parathyroids","Thymus descends to the anterior mediastinum behind the manubrium","Inferior parathyroids pulled below the superior pair","Capsule and septa labeled as neural crest","T cell precursors shown arriving from bone marrow by the blood"]},{"t":"DiGeorge syndrome from defect to signs","p":"Write 22q11.2 deletion at the top of a page and draw arrows from it to each structure that fails to form and then to the finding each failure causes.","l":["Third and fourth pharyngeal pouches fail to develop","Thymic hypoplasia leads to low T cells and an absent thymic shadow","Recurrent viral, fungal, and Pneumocystis infections","Parathyroid hypoplasia leads to low PTH and hypocalcemia","Hypocalcemia causes tetany or seizures in the newborn","Neural crest failure leads to conotruncal defects such as truncus arteriosus or tetralogy of Fallot","Cleft palate and abnormal facies"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck (section on pharyngeal pouches)"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Immune System and Lymphoid Organs (section on the thymus)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax (section on the superior mediastinum and thymus)"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Congenital and Acquired Immunodeficiencies"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-2","IM-15-A2","IM-15-A","CV-18-G4","EN-12-B3","HE-9-D2"]},"IM-2":{"id":"IM-2","summary":"The microscopic layout of lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, and mucosal lymphoid tissue, and which zone holds B cells or T cells. Exam questions ask which zone is empty in an immunodeficiency, which zone grows in a reactive node, and how blood moves through the spleen.","notes":[{"h":"Lymph node","points":["Lymph enters through afferent vessels into the subcapsular sinus, then flows through cortical and medullary sinuses to the efferent vessel at the hilum.","Outer cortex: B cell follicles. Primary follicles are resting; secondary follicles have a pale germinal center surrounded by a mantle zone.","Paracortex (deep cortex): T cells and dendritic cells, plus high endothelial venules (HEVs) where naive lymphocytes leave the blood.","Medulla: medullary cords packed with plasma cells, macrophages, and lymphocytes, alternating with medullary sinuses lined by macrophages."]},{"h":"Zones that empty or grow","points":["DiGeorge syndrome (no T cells): the paracortex is thin or empty, while follicles can still form.","X-linked agammaglobulinemia (no mature B cells): follicles and germinal centers are absent, and tonsils are tiny.","Reactive follicular hyperplasia: large germinal centers from strong B cell stimulation, as in rheumatoid arthritis and early HIV infection.","Reactive paracortical hyperplasia: expanded T cell zone in viral infections such as infectious mononucleosis, after some vaccines, and with drugs such as phenytoin."]},{"h":"Spleen","points":["White pulp: the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS) of T cells wraps the central arteriole; B cell follicles sit beside it.","The marginal zone, between white and red pulp, has macrophages and marginal zone B cells that trap blood-borne antigens and respond to encapsulated bacteria.","Red pulp: splenic cords (cords of Billroth) of macrophages and reticular fibers, and venous sinusoids.","Blood path: splenic artery, trabecular arteries, central arterioles in white pulp, penicillar arterioles, then into cords or sinusoids, then pulp veins, trabecular veins, splenic vein.","Red cells in the cords must squeeze through slits between sinusoid endothelial cells; old or rigid cells get stuck and are eaten by macrophages."]},{"h":"Thymus","points":["Cortex: dark-staining, densely packed immature thymocytes on a network of epithelial reticular cells; positive selection happens here.","The blood thymus barrier in the cortex (continuous endothelium, thick basal lamina, epithelial reticular cell sheath) keeps blood antigens away from developing cells.","Medulla: paler, fewer and more mature T cells, and Hassall corpuscles; negative selection happens here.","The thymus has no lymphoid follicles and no afferent lymphatics, which helps tell it apart from a lymph node."]},{"h":"Mucosa associated lymphoid tissue (MALT)","points":["Includes the palatine, pharyngeal, and lingual tonsils, Peyer patches, and scattered lymphoid nodules along the gut and airways.","Peyer patches are clusters of follicles in the ileal mucosa and submucosa, under a dome of follicle-associated epithelium.","M cells in that epithelium take up antigens from the lumen and pass them to dendritic cells and lymphocytes below.","B cells activated here switch to IgA (TGF-beta drives the switch) and home as plasma cells to the lamina propria.","Plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA joined by a J chain; the polymeric Ig receptor carries it across the epithelium and leaves secretory component attached."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Where B cells and T cells live","cols":["Organ","B cell zone","T cell zone","Special features"],"rows":[["Lymph node","Cortical follicles and germinal centers","Paracortex","HEVs in paracortex; plasma cells in medullary cords"],["Spleen","Follicles and marginal zone of white pulp","PALS around central arteriole","Red pulp cords and sinusoids filter blood"],["Thymus","None normally","Cortex (immature) and medulla (mature)","Hassall corpuscles; blood thymus barrier"],["Peyer patch","Follicles with germinal centers","Interfollicular areas","M cells in dome epithelium; IgA production"]]},{"title":"Lymph node patterns","cols":["Condition","Zone affected","Change"],"rows":[["DiGeorge syndrome","Paracortex","Depleted"],["X-linked agammaglobulinemia","Follicles and germinal centers","Absent"],["Reactive follicular hyperplasia","Follicles","Enlarged germinal centers"],["Reactive paracortical hyperplasia","Paracortex","Expanded T cell zone"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"B on the outside","text":"In a lymph node, follicles (B cells) sit in the outer cortex just under the capsule, and the T cells sit deeper in the paracortex."},{"kind":"trick","label":"PALS hug the artery","text":"In the spleen the T cells form a sleeve around the central arteriole. Find the arteriole and you have found the T cell zone."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Cortex dark, medulla light","text":"Thymic cortex is crowded with small dark thymocytes; the medulla looks paler and has Hassall corpuscles. Positive selection is in the cortex, negative selection in the medulla."}],"highYield":["Naive lymphocytes enter lymph nodes through high endothelial venules in the paracortex.","The splenic marginal zone is key for responses to encapsulated bacteria, so asplenic patients are at risk from S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, and N meningitidis.","After splenectomy, Howell-Jolly bodies appear because the red pulp no longer removes nuclear remnants.","M cells are the entry point for some pathogens, such as Salmonella and Shigella, in Peyer patches.","Secretory IgA is the most produced antibody in the body.","Hassall corpuscles are found only in the thymic medulla."],"pitfalls":["The paracortex holds T cells; the cortex proper holds B cell follicles. Both lie outside the medulla.","Splenic PALS are T cells, while splenic follicles and the marginal zone are B cells.","A germinal center means an active B cell response; a primary follicle has none.","Thymic tissue lacks follicles, so a section with germinal centers is not normal thymus."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lymph node map","p":"Draw a lymph node in cross section. Label each zone, say which cells live there, and draw the path lymph takes from entry to exit.","l":["Afferent lymphatics entering the convex surface","Subcapsular sinus under the capsule","Cortical follicles of B cells, one with a germinal center","Paracortex of T cells with high endothelial venules","Medullary cords with plasma cells and medullary sinuses with macrophages","Efferent lymphatic leaving at the hilum","Note which zone is empty in DiGeorge syndrome and which in X-linked agammaglobulinemia"]},{"t":"Blood flow through the spleen","p":"Draw the path of a red blood cell from the splenic artery to the splenic vein, labeling white pulp and red pulp structures along the way.","l":["Splenic artery to trabecular arteries","Central arteriole surrounded by the PALS (T cells)","B cell follicle beside the PALS","Marginal zone between white and red pulp","Penicillar arterioles opening into splenic cords","Cells squeezing through endothelial slits into venous sinusoids","Old or rigid red cells trapped and eaten by cord macrophages","Pulp veins to trabecular veins to splenic vein"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Introduction to the Immune System (section on anatomy of lymphoid tissues)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus (section on reactive lymphadenitis)"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-1","IM-8","IM-15-A2","IM-15-A5","IM-15-A3","IM-15-F5"]},"IM-3":{"id":"IM-3","summary":"The structure of MHC (HLA) class I and class II molecules, how each one loads peptides, how HLA genes are inherited, and which HLA types are tied to which diseases. Exams also use MHC to explain NK cell killing and why transplant patients need crossmatching.","notes":[{"h":"Class I versus class II","points":["Class I (HLA-A, -B, -C): one heavy (alpha) chain paired with beta2-microglobulin, on all nucleated cells and platelets, not on mature red cells.","Class I shows peptides from inside the cell (8 to 10 amino acids) to CD8 T cells; CD8 binds the alpha3 domain.","Class II (HLA-DP, -DQ, -DR): an alpha and a beta chain, on antigen-presenting cells (dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells) and thymic epithelium.","Class II shows peptides from outside the cell (about 13 to 25 amino acids) to CD4 T cells; CD4 binds the beta2 domain.","IFN-gamma increases expression of both classes."]},{"h":"Loading peptides","points":["Class I path: cytosolic proteins (including viral proteins) are tagged with ubiquitin and cut in the proteasome.","TAP (transporter associated with antigen processing) pumps the peptides into the endoplasmic reticulum, where they load onto class I and go to the surface.","Class II path: class II is made in the ER with the invariant chain blocking its groove and steering it to endosomes.","Engulfed proteins are degraded in acidic endosomes; the invariant chain is cut down to a small piece called CLIP.","HLA-DM swaps CLIP out for a foreign peptide, and the loaded class II goes to the surface.","Dendritic cells can also load engulfed antigens onto class I (cross-presentation) to prime CD8 T cells against viruses and tumors."]},{"h":"HLA genetics","points":["The HLA genes sit together on the short arm of chromosome 6.","They are highly polymorphic and codominant, so each cell shows the alleles from both parents.","The linked set of HLA genes on one chromosome is a haplotype, and it is usually inherited as a block.","Each child gets one haplotype from each parent, so a sibling has a 25% chance of a full match, 50% chance of a half match, and 25% chance of no match.","A parent and child always share at least one haplotype (at least a half match), barring recombination."]},{"h":"NK cells and missing self","points":["NK cells carry inhibitory receptors (KIRs, CD94/NKG2A) that recognize self class I and turn the NK cell off.","Viruses and tumors often lower class I to escape CD8 T cells, which removes that brake.","If activating signals such as stress ligands are present and class I is missing, the NK cell kills the target.","NK cells also kill antibody-coated cells through CD16 (antibody dependent cellular cytotoxicity)."]},{"h":"Transplant matching","points":["Prior transfusion, pregnancy, or transplant can make a person produce antibodies against foreign HLA (sensitization).","Preformed antibodies against donor HLA or ABO antigens bind graft endothelium and fix complement within minutes to hours (hyperacute rejection).","The graft turns mottled and cyanotic from thrombosis and must be removed; this is type II hypersensitivity.","A crossmatch mixes recipient serum with donor lymphocytes to find these antibodies before surgery.","Closer HLA matching lowers T cell recognition of the graft and improves long-term survival."]}],"charts":[{"title":"MHC class I versus class II","cols":["Feature","Class I","Class II"],"rows":[["Loci","HLA-A, -B, -C","HLA-DP, -DQ, -DR"],["Chains","Heavy chain plus beta2-microglobulin","Alpha plus beta chain"],["Found on","All nucleated cells and platelets","Antigen-presenting cells and thymic epithelium"],["Peptide source","Cytosol (endogenous)","Endosome (exogenous)"],["Loading","Proteasome, TAP, ER","Invariant chain, CLIP, HLA-DM in endosome"],["Presents to","CD8 T cells","CD4 T cells"]]},{"title":"HLA and disease","cols":["HLA type","Disease"],"rows":[["B27","Ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, arthritis of inflammatory bowel disease"],["DQ2 and DQ8","Celiac disease"],["DR3 and DR4","Type 1 diabetes"],["DR4","Rheumatoid arthritis"],["DR2 (DR15)","Multiple sclerosis, Goodpasture syndrome"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Rule of 8","text":"MHC class times CD number equals 8: class I goes with CD8 (1 x 8) and class II goes with CD4 (2 x 4)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PAIR","text":"HLA-B27 diseases: Psoriatic arthritis, Ankylosing spondylitis, Inflammatory bowel disease arthritis, Reactive arthritis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One from mom, one from dad","text":"Draw two haplotypes for each parent (a, b and c, d). Children can be ac, ad, bc, or bd, so any sibling matches you fully one time in four."}],"highYield":["Mature red cells lack MHC class I, yet NK cells leave them alone because red cells also lack the stress ligands that activate NK cells.","TAP deficiency lowers surface class I and leaves CD8 T cells low.","Bare lymphocyte syndrome (class II deficiency) leaves patients with very few CD4 T cells.","HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 is present in nearly all celiac patients, so a negative test makes celiac disease very unlikely.","Hyperacute rejection happens within minutes to hours and is caused by preformed antibodies, not by T cells.","An identical twin is the only donor certain to be a full HLA match."],"pitfalls":["Class I gets peptides from the cytosol and loads them in the ER; class II gets peptides from endosomes. Viral proteins made inside the cell go to class I.","Beta2-microglobulin belongs to class I and is not encoded on chromosome 6.","NK cells kill cells that lack class I; CD8 T cells kill cells that show foreign peptide on class I.","Hyperacute rejection is antibody mediated; acute rejection is mostly T cell mediated over weeks to months."],"dumps":[{"t":"Two roads to the cell surface","p":"Draw a cell with two pathways side by side: a viral protein reaching MHC class I and an engulfed bacterial protein reaching MHC class II. Label every step.","l":["Viral protein in the cytosol tagged with ubiquitin","Proteasome cuts it into peptides","TAP moves peptides into the ER to load onto class I","Class I with peptide shown to a CD8 T cell","Class II made in the ER with the invariant chain in its groove","Engulfed protein degraded in an acidic endosome","Invariant chain trimmed to CLIP and swapped for peptide by HLA-DM","Class II with peptide shown to a CD4 T cell"]},{"t":"HLA inheritance and matching","p":"Draw a family tree with two parents and four children. Give each parent two HLA haplotypes and work out how often siblings match, then explain why sensitized recipients need a crossmatch.","l":["HLA genes on chromosome 6 inherited as haplotypes","Codominant expression of both parents' alleles","Four possible haplotype combinations in children","25% full match, 50% half match, 25% no match between siblings","Parent and child always share at least one haplotype","Transfusion, pregnancy, or transplant can create anti-HLA antibodies","Crossmatch tests recipient serum against donor lymphocytes","Positive crossmatch predicts hyperacute rejection"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Antigen Capture and Presentation to Lymphocytes"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Immune Responses Against Tumors and Transplants"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System (sections on HLA and disease and on transplant rejection)"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-5","IM-12","IM-13","IM-15-C4","MS-13-B1","MS-13-B7","GI-13-E2","EN-12-D1","NE-20-B5","IM-15-C2"]},"IM-4":{"id":"IM-4","summary":"Where lymph from each part of the body travels, which palpable node group enlarges with an infection or tumor at a given site, and what happens when the thoracic duct is injured. Exams test this with first-node spread of cancers, the Virchow node, and chylothorax.","notes":[{"h":"The two main ducts","points":["Lymph from the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdomen collects in the cisterna chyli, a sac in front of the L1 to L2 vertebrae just right of the aorta.","The thoracic duct runs up from the cisterna chyli through the aortic hiatus, between the aorta and azygos vein, and crosses to the left at about T4 to T5.","It ends at the left venous angle, where the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins join.","The thoracic duct drains everything except the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb, and the right side of the thorax.","That right upper quarter drains through the right lymphatic duct into the right venous angle.","Intestinal lymph (chyle) carries long-chain fat as chylomicrons from the lacteals into the cisterna chyli."]},{"h":"Palpable node groups","points":["Cervical nodes: scalp, face, mouth, pharynx, and neck.","Supraclavicular nodes: right side drains the mediastinum, lungs, and esophagus; left side receives the thoracic duct and drains the abdomen and thorax.","Axillary nodes: upper limb, most of the breast, and the chest and abdominal wall above the umbilicus.","Epitrochlear nodes: the ulnar (medial) side of the hand and forearm.","Superficial inguinal nodes: skin of the lower limb, abdominal wall below the umbilicus, buttock, perineum, scrotum, vulva, and anal canal below the pectinate line.","Popliteal nodes: the lateral foot and back of the leg, along the small saphenous vein."]},{"h":"First nodes for tumors","points":["Breast: lateral quadrants drain to axillary nodes (most breast lymph); medial quadrants drain to parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes.","Testis: para-aortic (lumbar) nodes near L1 to L2, following the gonadal vessels back to where the testis formed.","Scrotum: superficial inguinal nodes, because scrotal skin is body wall.","Anal canal above the pectinate line: internal iliac nodes. Below the line: superficial inguinal nodes.","Ovary: para-aortic nodes, the same logic as the testis."]},{"h":"Virchow node","points":["An enlarged, hard left supraclavicular node is the Virchow node.","Tumor cells from the stomach, pancreas, or other abdominal and thoracic organs travel up the thoracic duct and lodge there.","Gastric adenocarcinoma is the classic source; a palpable node there is called the Troisier sign."]},{"h":"Chylothorax","points":["Injury to the thoracic duct (surgery such as esophagectomy, trauma, or tumor such as lymphoma) leaks chyle into the pleural space.","Pleural fluid is milky white, rich in triglycerides (usually above 110 mg/dL), and contains chylomicrons and many lymphocytes.","Injury below about T5 tends to cause a right chylothorax; injury above that level, after the duct crosses, tends to cause a left one.","Ongoing loss drains fat, protein, fat-soluble vitamins, and lymphocytes.","Medium-chain triglycerides are absorbed straight into portal blood without chylomicrons, so an MCT diet lowers chyle flow."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Node group by drainage site","cols":["Node group","Drains","Enlarges with"],"rows":[["Cervical","Scalp, face, mouth, pharynx, neck","Pharyngitis, dental or scalp infection"],["Left supraclavicular (Virchow)","Abdomen and thorax by the thoracic duct","Gastric or other abdominal cancer"],["Right supraclavicular","Mediastinum, lungs, esophagus","Lung or esophageal cancer"],["Axillary","Upper limb, lateral breast, upper trunk wall","Hand infection, breast cancer"],["Epitrochlear","Ulnar side of hand and forearm","Infection of the little or ring finger"],["Superficial inguinal","Lower limb skin, lower trunk wall, perineum, scrotum, vulva, lower anal canal","Foot infection, scrotal or anal squamous cancer"],["Popliteal","Lateral foot and back of the leg","Infected heel or lateral foot"]]},{"title":"First nodal spread of tumors","cols":["Site","First nodes"],"rows":[["Lateral breast","Axillary"],["Medial breast","Parasternal (internal thoracic)"],["Testis","Para-aortic (lumbar)"],["Scrotum","Superficial inguinal"],["Anal canal above pectinate line","Internal iliac"],["Anal canal below pectinate line","Superficial inguinal"],["Ovary","Para-aortic (lumbar)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Organs drain home","text":"An organ's lymph follows its arteries back to where it formed. The testis and ovary formed near the kidneys, so they drain to para-aortic nodes even though they end up low."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Skin goes to the groin","text":"Below the umbilicus, skin and anything lined by skin (scrotum, vulva, lower anal canal) drains to the superficial inguinal nodes."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Draw the right upper quarter","text":"Shade the right head, neck, arm, and chest. That shaded quarter goes to the right lymphatic duct; everything else goes to the thoracic duct."}],"highYield":["A hard left supraclavicular node in an older adult with weight loss suggests gastric cancer.","Testicular cancer spreads first to para-aortic nodes; an inguinal node points to the scrotum or another skin source.","Squamous cancer of the anal canal below the pectinate line spreads to superficial inguinal nodes.","Milky pleural fluid with chylomicrons and a high triglyceride level after chest surgery means thoracic duct injury.","The thoracic duct empties at the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins.","Lateral breast tumors spread first to axillary nodes; medial ones can go to parasternal nodes."],"pitfalls":["Testis drains to para-aortic nodes, while scrotum drains to superficial inguinal nodes.","Above the pectinate line the anal canal drains to internal iliac nodes; below it, to superficial inguinal nodes.","Chylothorax is milky from chylomicrons; a pseudochylous effusion is milky from cholesterol crystals in a long-standing effusion and lacks chylomicrons.","Epitrochlear nodes enlarge with infection of the medial (ulnar) fingers and hand; thumb-side infections go straight to axillary nodes."],"dumps":[{"t":"Whole-body lymph map","p":"Draw an outline of the body and trace lymph from the left foot and from the right hand to the bloodstream. Label each duct, the cisterna chyli, and where each duct empties.","l":["Left foot lymph through superficial inguinal nodes to iliac and lumbar nodes","Cisterna chyli near L1 to L2","Thoracic duct through the aortic hiatus and up the posterior mediastinum","Duct crossing to the left at about T4 to T5","Thoracic duct ending at the left venous angle","Right hand lymph through axillary nodes to the right lymphatic duct","Right lymphatic duct ending at the right venous angle","Shaded right upper quarter drained by the right duct"]},{"t":"Where does this cancer go first","p":"Make a table with three columns (tumor site, first nodes, reason) for lateral breast, medial breast, testis, scrotum, upper anal canal, and lower anal canal. Add a row explaining the Virchow node.","l":["Lateral breast to axillary nodes","Medial breast to parasternal nodes","Testis to para-aortic nodes because it formed near the kidney","Scrotum to superficial inguinal nodes because it is skin","Upper anal canal to internal iliac nodes","Lower anal canal to superficial inguinal nodes","Virchow node: abdominal tumor cells travel up the thoracic duct to the left supraclavicular node"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax (section on the thoracic duct)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum (section on lymphatic drainage)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on the lymphatic system"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-8","IM-9","RE-13-E6","RE-13-J9","HE-9-D2"]},"IM-5":{"id":"IM-5","summary":"How antibodies are built and what each isotype does, how B cells get T cell help to switch class, how the T helper subsets differ, and how cytotoxic cells kill. Exams test these with immunodeficiencies, vaccine responses, and antibody titer curves.","notes":[{"h":"Antibody structure","points":["Two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains (kappa or lambda) held together by disulfide bonds.","Fab: the two arms. Variable domains of heavy and light chains each have three hypervariable regions (CDRs) that form the antigen-binding site.","Fc: the stem made of heavy chain constant domains. It sets the isotype and does the work: complement binding (C1q) and Fc receptor binding.","Papain cuts an antibody into two Fab pieces and one Fc piece; pepsin leaves one F(ab')2 piece."]},{"h":"The five isotypes","points":["IgM: pentamer with a J chain, made first in a primary response, best at activating complement; monomer IgM sits on naive B cells.","IgG: monomer, most abundant in serum, crosses the placenta through FcRn, opsonizes, neutralizes, and fixes complement.","IgA: monomer in serum; dimer with J chain and secretory component in tears, saliva, breast milk, and gut secretions.","IgE: binds FcεRI on mast cells and basophils; drives allergy and, with eosinophils, defense against helminths.","IgD: sits with IgM on naive B cells as a receptor; its serum role is unclear."]},{"h":"B cell help and class switching","points":["T dependent response: a B cell presents protein peptide on class II to a CD4 helper T cell.","CD40L on the activated T cell binds CD40 on the B cell; cytokines then pick the new isotype (IL-4 to IgE, TGF-beta to IgA, IFN-gamma to opsonizing IgG).","In germinal centers, activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) drives class switching and somatic hypermutation.","B cells with better-fitting receptors survive, so affinity rises over time (affinity maturation), and memory B cells and plasma cells form.","CD40L defect (X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome): normal or high IgM, very low IgG, IgA, and IgE, with pyogenic and opportunistic infections such as Pneumocystis.","T independent response: polysaccharides with repeating units crosslink B cell receptors and give mostly IgM, little memory, and weak responses under age 2.","Conjugate vaccines link polysaccharide to a protein carrier so T cells can help, giving IgG and memory in infants."]},{"h":"T helper subsets","points":["Th1: induced by IL-12, makes IFN-gamma, activates macrophages to kill intracellular microbes.","Th2: induced by IL-4, makes IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13, drives IgE switching, eosinophils, and mucus.","Th17: induced by IL-6 with TGF-beta (kept up by IL-23), makes IL-17, recruits neutrophils against extracellular bacteria and fungi such as Candida.","Regulatory T cells: CD4, CD25, and FOXP3 positive; secrete IL-10 and TGF-beta to suppress responses. FOXP3 loss causes IPEX syndrome."]},{"h":"How CD8 T cells and NK cells kill","points":["Perforin forms pores in the target membrane so granzymes can enter; granzymes activate caspases and trigger apoptosis.","Fas ligand on the killer binds Fas (CD95) on the target and starts the extrinsic apoptosis pathway through caspase-8.","Apoptosis kills without spilling cell contents, so there is little inflammation.","CD8 T cells need foreign peptide on class I; NK cells need no prior exposure and act on cells missing class I."]},{"h":"Primary and secondary responses","points":["Primary response: a lag of about a week, IgM first then IgG, low peak titer, low affinity.","Secondary response: shorter lag (a few days), mostly IgG, higher and longer-lasting titer, higher affinity, from memory B cells.","Active immunity: the person's own response after infection or vaccination; slow to start, long-lasting, with memory.","Passive immunity: ready-made antibody such as maternal IgG across the placenta, IgA in breast milk, or immune globulin; immediate but gone in weeks to months.","Maternal IgG fades by about 6 months, which is when infant infections from encapsulated bacteria rise."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Antibody isotypes","cols":["Isotype","Form","Key job","Crosses placenta","Fixes complement"],"rows":[["IgM","Pentamer (monomer on B cells)","First response; agglutination","No","Yes, strongest"],["IgG","Monomer","Opsonization, neutralization, secondary response","Yes","Yes"],["IgA","Dimer in secretions","Mucosal defense; in breast milk","No","No (classical)"],["IgE","Monomer","Mast cell degranulation; helminths","No","No"],["IgD","Monomer on naive B cells","B cell receptor","No","No"]]},{"title":"T helper subsets","cols":["Subset","Induced by","Main products","Main effect"],"rows":[["Th1","IL-12","IFN-gamma","Macrophage activation; intracellular microbes"],["Th2","IL-4","IL-4, IL-5, IL-13","IgE, eosinophils; helminths and allergy"],["Th17","IL-6 with TGF-beta; IL-23","IL-17, IL-22","Neutrophils; extracellular bacteria and fungi"],["Treg","TGF-beta, IL-2","IL-10, TGF-beta","Suppresses immune responses"]]},{"title":"Primary versus secondary antibody response","cols":["Feature","Primary","Secondary"],"rows":[["Lag phase","About a week","A few days"],["Main isotype","IgM then IgG","IgG"],["Peak titer","Lower","Higher"],["Affinity","Lower","Higher"],["Cells responding","Naive B cells","Memory B cells"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"IgM is big and first","text":"A pentamer is too big to cross the placenta, so IgM in a newborn's blood was made by the baby and points to congenital infection."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Th1 inside, Th2 worms","text":"Th1 cells handle microbes living inside macrophages by sending IFN-gamma. Th2 cells handle worms, which are too big to eat, with IgE and eosinophils."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Active is earned, passive is borrowed","text":"Earned immunity (infection, vaccine) leaves memory. Borrowed antibody (maternal IgG, immune globulin) works right away but leaves no memory."}],"highYield":["A normal or high IgM with low IgG, IgA, and IgE in a boy with Pneumocystis pneumonia points to CD40L deficiency.","IgM antibody to a pathogen means current or recent infection; IgG alone means past infection or vaccination.","Children under 2 respond poorly to plain polysaccharide vaccines, so they get conjugate vaccines.","IgG3 and IgG1 fix complement best; IgG crosses the placenta through FcRn.","Th17 defects (for example STAT3 loss in hyper-IgE syndrome) cause recurrent Candida and staphylococcal infections.","Perforin and granzyme killing and Fas ligand killing both end in caspase-driven apoptosis.","Hepatitis B exposure in a newborn gets both passive (immune globulin) and active (vaccine) immunity."],"pitfalls":["Fab binds antigen; Fc sets the isotype and binds complement and Fc receptors.","IgA is the most produced antibody overall, but IgG is the most abundant in serum.","Isotype switching changes the heavy chain constant region only; the antigen-binding site stays the same.","Th1 and Th17 both fight bacteria: Th1 helps macrophages with intracellular microbes, Th17 brings neutrophils for extracellular ones."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw an antibody","p":"Draw an IgG molecule. Label the heavy and light chains, variable and constant domains, Fab and Fc, hinge, and where complement and Fc receptors bind. Then sketch IgM and secretory IgA beside it.","l":["Two heavy and two light chains joined by disulfide bonds","Variable domains at the tips with hypervariable regions forming the binding site","Fab arms and Fc stem labeled","Hinge region between Fab and Fc","C1q and Fc receptor binding on the Fc","IgM drawn as a pentamer with a J chain","Secretory IgA drawn as a dimer with J chain and secretory component"]},{"t":"From naive B cell to high-affinity IgG","p":"Trace a B cell from first meeting a protein antigen to secreting high-affinity IgG on a second exposure. Then draw the primary and secondary antibody curves on one graph.","l":["B cell receptor binds antigen and presents peptide on class II","CD4 helper T cell CD40L binds B cell CD40","Cytokines pick the new isotype","Germinal center class switching and somatic hypermutation by AID","Affinity maturation and memory B cell formation","Primary curve: about a week of lag, IgM then IgG, low peak","Secondary curve: short lag, mostly IgG, higher peak and affinity"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Humoral Immune Responses"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Effector Mechanisms of T Cell-Mediated Immunity"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Resistance of the Body to Infection: II. Immunity and Allergy"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-3","IM-7","IM-13","IM-15-A5","IM-15-A3","IM-15-A1","IM-15-C1"]},"IM-6":{"id":"IM-6","summary":"How innate cells sense microbes and damage, call in leukocytes, start fever and the acute phase response, and eat and kill invaders. Exam questions link each step to a disease: leukocyte adhesion deficiency, Chediak-Higashi syndrome, granulomas, septic shock, and toxic shock syndrome.","notes":[{"h":"Pattern recognition","points":["TLR4 with CD14 (and MD-2) binds LPS (lipid A) from gram-negative bacteria on the cell surface.","TLR5 binds flagellin; TLR2 binds lipoproteins and lipoteichoic acid of gram-positive bacteria.","Endosomal TLRs sense nucleic acids: TLR3 double-stranded RNA, TLR7 and TLR8 single-stranded RNA, TLR9 unmethylated CpG DNA.","Most TLRs signal through MyD88 to NF-kappaB, which turns on TNF, IL-1, IL-6, and chemokines; TLR3 and TLR4 also trigger type I interferons.","The NLRP3 inflammasome senses damage signals (DAMPs) such as ATP and urate crystals and activates caspase-1.","Caspase-1 cuts pro-IL-1beta and pro-IL-18 into their active forms; gout flares are driven this way."]},{"h":"Leukocyte extravasation","points":["Rolling: endothelial E-selectin and P-selectin bind sialyl Lewis X on leukocytes. P-selectin comes from Weibel-Palade bodies; TNF and IL-1 induce E-selectin.","Tight adhesion: leukocyte integrins (LFA-1 and Mac-1, which share the CD18 chain) bind ICAM-1, which TNF and IL-1 induce on endothelium.","Diapedesis: PECAM-1 (CD31) lets cells squeeze between endothelial cells, mostly in postcapillary venules.","Chemotaxis: C5a, LTB4, IL-8, and bacterial formyl peptides pull neutrophils toward the site.","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1 (CD18 loss): delayed umbilical cord separation, skin infections without pus, poor wound healing, and a very high blood neutrophil count."]},{"h":"Fever and acute phase response","points":["IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha act on the hypothalamus, where COX-2 makes PGE2 that raises the temperature set point; NSAIDs block this step.","IL-6 drives the liver to make acute phase proteins.","Rise: CRP (opsonin), fibrinogen (raises ESR), serum amyloid A, ferritin, and hepcidin.","Fall: albumin and transferrin.","Hepcidin blocks ferroportin and traps iron in macrophages, which causes anemia of chronic disease.","Long-standing high serum amyloid A can deposit as AA amyloid."]},{"h":"Phagocytosis and Chediak-Higashi syndrome","points":["Opsonins coat microbes so phagocytes grab them: IgG binds Fc gamma receptors and C3b binds complement receptor 1.","The phagosome fuses with lysosomes (phagolysosome), and microbes are killed by the oxidative burst and lysosomal enzymes.","Chediak-Higashi syndrome: autosomal recessive LYST mutation disrupts lysosome trafficking and phagolysosome fusion.","Findings: giant granules in neutrophils, partial albinism, peripheral neuropathy, recurrent pyogenic infections with S aureus and streptococci, neutropenia, and easy bleeding."]},{"h":"Two kinds of granuloma","points":["Foreign body granuloma: material too large to engulf (suture, talc, splinter) collects macrophages, epithelioid cells, and foreign body giant cells.","The foreign material is often visible, and talc and many sutures shine under polarized light.","Immune granuloma: macrophages present antigen and make IL-12, Th1 cells make IFN-gamma, and macrophages turn epithelioid and fuse into Langhans giant cells.","TNF keeps immune granulomas intact, so anti-TNF drugs can reactivate latent tuberculosis.","Immune granulomas have a cuff of lymphocytes and may have caseous necrosis (tuberculosis); foreign body granulomas have few T cells."]},{"h":"Sepsis and toxic shock","points":["In gram-negative sepsis, LPS through TLR4 floods the blood with TNF, IL-1, and IL-6.","Nitric oxide causes vasodilation and hypotension; activated endothelium leaks fluid; tissue factor on monocytes and endothelium starts DIC.","Result: low blood pressure, edema, lactic acidosis, low platelets and fibrinogen, and failing lungs, kidneys, and liver.","SIRS needs two or more: temperature above 38 °C (100.4 °F) or below 36 °C (96.8 °F), pulse above 90/min, respirations above 20/min (or PaCO2 below 32 mm Hg), WBC above 12,000/mm3 or below 4,000/mm3 (or more than 10% bands).","Superantigens (S aureus TSST-1, S pyogenes pyrogenic exotoxins) bind MHC class II and the TCR V-beta region outside the peptide groove.","This activates up to about 20% of all T cells at once, releasing IL-2, IFN-gamma, and TNF, which causes fever, diffuse rash with later peeling, and shock."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Leukocyte extravasation steps","cols":["Step","Endothelial molecule","Leukocyte molecule","Defect"],"rows":[["Rolling","E-selectin, P-selectin","Sialyl Lewis X","LAD type 2 (no sialyl Lewis X)"],["Tight adhesion","ICAM-1","CD18 integrins (LFA-1, Mac-1)","LAD type 1 (CD18)"],["Diapedesis","PECAM-1","PECAM-1","None classic"],["Migration","Chemoattractant gradient","Receptors for C5a, LTB4, IL-8, formyl peptides","None classic"]]},{"title":"Acute phase reactants","cols":["Protein","Direction","Why it matters"],"rows":[["CRP","Up","Opsonin; activates classical complement"],["Fibrinogen","Up","Promotes rouleaux, so ESR rises"],["Serum amyloid A","Up","Source of AA amyloid"],["Ferritin","Up","Stores iron away from microbes"],["Hepcidin","Up","Blocks ferroportin; anemia of chronic disease"],["Albumin","Down","Liver shifts protein synthesis"],["Transferrin","Down","Less iron moved to tissues"]]},{"title":"Foreign body versus immune granuloma","cols":["Feature","Foreign body granuloma","Immune granuloma"],"rows":[["Trigger","Inert material (suture, talc, splinter)","Persistent antigen (tuberculosis, fungi, sarcoidosis)"],["Driver","Macrophages alone","Th1 cells and IFN-gamma"],["Giant cells","Foreign body type, scattered nuclei","Langhans type, nuclei in a horseshoe"],["Lymphocytes","Few","Prominent cuff"],["Necrosis","Usually none","Caseous in tuberculosis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Roll, stick, squeeze, follow","text":"Selectins roll, integrins stick, PECAM-1 squeezes through, chemoattractants lead the way. Name the molecule at each step and the defect falls out."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Clean up on aisle 8","text":"IL-8 is the main chemokine that calls neutrophils to clean up a site. The other neutrophil chemoattractants are C5a, LTB4, and bacterial formyl peptides."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Hot T-bone stEAK","text":"Interleukin jobs in order: IL-1 fever (hot), IL-2 T cell growth, IL-3 bone marrow, IL-4 IgE, IL-5 IgA and eosinophils, IL-6 acute phase proteins (aKute)."}],"highYield":["Delayed umbilical cord separation with high neutrophils and no pus means CD18 deficiency.","Giant cytoplasmic granules, light hair and skin, and recurrent pyogenic infections mean Chediak-Higashi syndrome.","IL-6 is the main driver of hepatic acute phase proteins, including hepcidin.","Lipid A of LPS binds TLR4 and CD14 to start gram-negative septic shock.","Superantigens skip antigen processing and bind outside the peptide groove, so they activate T cells regardless of antigen specificity.","Anti-TNF therapy can break down granulomas and reactivate latent tuberculosis.","Current sepsis definitions (Sepsis-3) use organ dysfunction scores, but SIRS criteria still appear in exam questions."],"pitfalls":["Selectins handle rolling and integrins handle firm adhesion; CD18 is an integrin chain, so LAD type 1 blocks firm adhesion.","Chediak-Higashi syndrome is a lysosome fusion defect; chronic granulomatous disease is an NADPH oxidase defect with catalase-positive infections.","Foreign body giant cells have scattered nuclei; Langhans giant cells have nuclei around the edge in a horseshoe.","Albumin and transferrin fall in inflammation, so low albumin in a septic patient does not by itself mean malnutrition.","Superantigens bind class II and TCR outside the groove; ordinary antigens sit inside the groove."],"dumps":[{"t":"Neutrophil from blood to bacteria","p":"Draw a postcapillary venule with a neutrophil leaving it to reach bacteria in the tissue. Label each step, the molecules on both cells, and the cytokines that turn the endothelium on.","l":["TNF and IL-1 from tissue macrophages activate the endothelium","Rolling on E-selectin and P-selectin through sialyl Lewis X","Firm adhesion of CD18 integrins to ICAM-1","Diapedesis through PECAM-1","Chemotaxis toward C5a, LTB4, IL-8, and formyl peptides","Opsonized bacterium bound by Fc and C3b receptors","Phagolysosome fusion, the step that fails in Chediak-Higashi syndrome","CD18 loss marked as leukocyte adhesion deficiency"]},{"t":"LPS to septic shock","p":"Starting with a gram-negative bacterium in the blood, trace the path to hypotension, DIC, and organ failure. Write the SIRS criteria in a box beside the path.","l":["LPS lipid A binds CD14 and TLR4 on macrophages","NF-kappaB turns on TNF, IL-1, and IL-6","Fever through hypothalamic PGE2","Nitric oxide causes vasodilation and hypotension","Endothelial activation causes capillary leak","Tissue factor triggers DIC with low platelets and fibrinogen","Organ dysfunction: lungs, kidneys, liver, lactic acidosis","SIRS: temperature above 38 °C (100.4 °F) or below 36 °C (96.8 °F), pulse above 90/min, respirations above 20/min, WBC above 12,000 or below 4,000/mm3"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Inflammation and Repair"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Innate Immunity"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on Staphylococcus and Streptococcus toxins"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on sepsis and septic shock"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-7","IM-13","RE-13-K9","CV-18-H5","PU-14-H9","IM-15-C4","IM-15-E2"]},"IM-7":{"id":"IM-7","summary":"How the three complement pathways start, what complement pieces do, how the body keeps complement in check, and what goes wrong when a part is missing. Exams lean on deficiency patterns and on reading C3 and C4 levels.","notes":[{"h":"Three ways to start","points":["Classical: C1q binds the Fc of IgM (one bound pentamer is enough) or of two or more bound IgG molecules; C1r and C1s then cut C4 and C2.","Lectin: mannose-binding lectin (MBL) binds mannose-rich sugars on microbes; its MASPs cut C4 and C2.","Classical and lectin pathways both make the C3 convertase C4b2a.","Alternative: C3 breaks down slowly on its own (tickover); C3b that lands on a microbe binds factor B, which factor D cuts, making C3bBb, stabilized by properdin.","All three converge on C3 convertase, then C5 convertase, then C5b to C9."]},{"h":"What complement does","points":["C3b: opsonin that coats microbes for phagocytes; also tags immune complexes.","C3a and C5a (and weaker C4a): anaphylatoxins that make mast cells release histamine.","C5a: strong neutrophil chemoattractant.","C5b to C9: membrane attack complex (MAC) that punches holes in thin-walled cells such as Neisseria.","Immune complex clearance: C3b-coated complexes bind CR1 on red cells and are carried to the liver and spleen for removal."]},{"h":"Regulators and their diseases","points":["C1 esterase inhibitor blocks C1r, C1s, and MASPs, and also kallikrein and factor XIIa.","DAF (CD55) breaks apart C3 convertases on host cells; CD59 blocks MAC assembly.","Hereditary angioedema: C1 inhibitor deficiency (autosomal dominant) lets kallikrein make excess bradykinin.","It causes nonpitting swelling of face, lips, larynx, or gut, without hives or itching; C4 is low, C3 is normal, and ACE inhibitors are avoided.","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria: an acquired PIGA mutation in a stem cell blocks GPI anchors, so CD55 and CD59 are missing.","PNH causes complement-driven intravascular hemolysis, dark morning urine, pancytopenia, and venous thrombosis; flow cytometry shows missing CD55 and CD59."]},{"h":"Deficiency patterns","points":["C1q, C2, or C4 (early classical) deficiency: immune complexes are cleared poorly, causing SLE-like disease.","C3 deficiency: severe recurrent pyogenic infections (encapsulated bacteria) and immune complex glomerulonephritis.","C5 to C9 (terminal) deficiency: recurrent Neisseria infections, especially meningococcal disease.","MBL deficiency: more infections in infancy and early childhood, usually mild.","Eculizumab (anti-C5) used for PNH creates the same Neisseria risk, so patients receive meningococcal vaccine."]},{"h":"Reading C3 and C4","points":["Low C3 and low C4: classical pathway consumption by immune complexes, as in active SLE.","Low C3 with normal C4: alternative pathway consumption, as in poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis; C3 usually recovers within about 8 weeks.","Low C4 with normal C3: C1 inhibitor deficiency in hereditary angioedema.","Falling C3 and C4 in a lupus patient suggest a flare."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Complement pathways","cols":["Pathway","Trigger","Starter molecule","C3 convertase"],"rows":[["Classical","IgM or IgG bound to antigen","C1q (with C1r, C1s)","C4b2a"],["Lectin","Mannose on microbial surfaces","MBL (with MASPs)","C4b2a"],["Alternative","Spontaneous C3 hydrolysis on microbial surfaces","C3b, factor B, factor D","C3bBb (stabilized by properdin)"]]},{"title":"Complement deficiencies","cols":["Missing piece","Main result"],"rows":[["C1q, C2, C4","SLE-like immune complex disease"],["C3","Severe pyogenic infections; immune complex glomerulonephritis"],["C5 to C9","Recurrent Neisseria infections"],["MBL","Infections in infancy and childhood"],["C1 esterase inhibitor","Hereditary angioedema"],["CD55 and CD59 (PIGA mutation)","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria"]]},{"title":"Serum complement patterns","cols":["Pattern","Pathway used up","Example"],"rows":[["Low C3, low C4","Classical","Active SLE"],["Low C3, normal C4","Alternative","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"],["Low C4, normal C3","Early classical (C1 inhibitor missing)","Hereditary angioedema"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Need two IgG, one IgM","text":"C1q must grab two Fc regions side by side. One IgM pentamer offers several at once; IgG needs at least two molecules close together."},{"kind":"trick","label":"C5 to C9 means Neisseria","text":"The MAC is how the body kills Neisseria. Anyone without a working MAC, by gene defect or by eculizumab, is at risk of meningococcal disease."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Swelling with no itch","text":"Hives and itching point to histamine. Deep swelling without hives that does not respond to antihistamines points to bradykinin and C1 inhibitor deficiency."}],"highYield":["Recurrent meningococcal infection means a terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency until proven otherwise.","Early classical component deficiency (C1q, C2, C4) predisposes to SLE.","Hereditary angioedema: low C4, normal C3, no hives, and ACE inhibitors are contraindicated.","PNH: missing CD55 and CD59 on flow cytometry, intravascular hemolysis, and thrombosis in unusual sites such as the hepatic veins.","C5a is the complement fragment that recruits neutrophils.","Low C3 with normal C4 after a strep throat or skin infection points to poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis."],"pitfalls":["C3b is the main opsonin; C3a is an anaphylatoxin. For C3 and C5, the b fragment stays on the surface and the a fragment floats away.","Angioedema from C1 inhibitor deficiency is bradykinin driven; allergic angioedema is histamine driven and comes with hives.","Early component deficiency causes autoimmunity; terminal component deficiency causes Neisseria infections.","PNH is an acquired stem cell mutation, not an inherited complement deficiency."],"dumps":[{"t":"Complement pathway map","p":"Draw the classical, lectin, and alternative pathways converging on C3. Continue to the MAC and mark where each effector fragment and each regulator acts.","l":["C1q binding IgM or IgG starts the classical pathway","MBL binding microbial mannose starts the lectin pathway","Classical and lectin pathways form C4b2a","Spontaneous C3 hydrolysis, factor B, factor D, and properdin form C3bBb","C3b opsonin and C3a anaphylatoxin from C3 cleavage","C5a anaphylatoxin and neutrophil chemoattractant","C5b to C9 forming the MAC","C1 inhibitor, DAF (CD55), and CD59 placed at their steps"]},{"t":"Complement disease table","p":"Make a table with four columns (defect, C3 level, C4 level, main clinical picture) for hereditary angioedema, active SLE, poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, terminal complement deficiency, and PNH.","l":["Hereditary angioedema: normal C3, low C4, bradykinin swelling without hives","Active SLE: low C3 and low C4 from immune complexes","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis: low C3, normal C4","Terminal (C5 to C9) deficiency: recurrent Neisseria","PNH: PIGA mutation, missing CD55 and CD59, intravascular hemolysis","Early classical (C1q, C2, C4) deficiency linked to SLE-like disease","C3 deficiency causes severe pyogenic infections"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Effector Mechanisms of Humoral Immunity"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Innate Immunity (section on the complement system)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Inflammation and Repair (section on the complement system)"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-5","IM-6","IM-15-C3","UR-12-A1","NE-20-G8"]},"IM-8":{"id":"IM-8","summary":"How the thymus, spleen, and lymph nodes do their jobs: the thymus builds a safe T cell repertoire, the spleen filters blood, and lymph nodes bring naive lymphocytes together with antigen. The exam asks you to predict what happens when one of these organs is removed, fails, or is suppressed by cortisol.","notes":[{"h":"Thymus: building T cells","points":["Bone marrow T cell precursors enter the thymus near the corticomedullary junction and move to the outer cortex as double negative (CD4- CD8-) thymocytes.","They rearrange the TCR beta chain, then the alpha chain, and become double positive (CD4+ CD8+).","Positive selection (cortex): thymocytes that bind self MHC on cortical epithelial cells with modest affinity survive; the rest die by neglect.","Binding MHC class I makes a CD8 T cell; binding MHC class II makes a CD4 T cell.","Negative selection (medulla): thymocytes that bind self peptide-MHC too strongly die by apoptosis or become FOXP3+ regulatory T cells.","AIRE lets medullary epithelial cells express tissue antigens (insulin, thyroid, parathyroid, adrenal proteins) so self-reactive cells can be deleted.","Most thymocytes never leave the thymus; only a small fraction exit as mature single positive naive T cells."]},{"h":"Spleen: the blood filter","points":["Red pulp (cords and sinusoids): rigid or aged red cells cannot squeeze through slits in the sinus wall, and macrophages eat them.","Pitting: splenic macrophages remove inclusions such as Howell-Jolly bodies (nuclear remnants) and Heinz bodies while the red cell survives.","White pulp: T cells in the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath, B cells in follicles.","Marginal zone B cells and macrophages trap blood-borne antigen and make fast IgM against bacterial polysaccharide capsules.","Splenic macrophages clear bacteria coated with IgG or C3b, which matters most for encapsulated bacteria.","About one third of the body's platelets are held in the spleen."]},{"h":"No spleen: what you see","points":["Blood smear: Howell-Jolly bodies, target cells, and sometimes Heinz bodies and acanthocytes.","Thrombocytosis after splenectomy because the splenic platelet pool is gone.","Overwhelming sepsis from encapsulated bacteria: S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, N meningitidis.","Sickle cell disease: repeated splenic infarcts cause functional asplenia in early childhood (autosplenectomy)."]},{"h":"Lymph nodes: where naive cells meet antigen","points":["Naive T and B cells leave the blood through high endothelial venules (HEVs) in the paracortex.","L-selectin (CD62L) on the lymphocyte binds addressins on HEVs and slows it down.","CCR7 on the lymphocyte binds the chemokines CCL19 and CCL21, which activates integrins for firm arrest.","Dendritic cells pick up antigen in tissue, switch on CCR7, and travel in afferent lymph to the node.","Lymphocytes that find no antigen leave through efferent lymph, drawn out by sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P).","Activated effector T cells lose L-selectin and CCR7, so they home to inflamed tissue instead of nodes."]},{"h":"Cortisol and the blood count","points":["Lymphopenia: lymphocytes redistribute out of the blood and lymphocyte apoptosis increases.","Eosinopenia and fewer monocytes and basophils.","Neutrophilia: neutrophils demarginate from vessel walls (less adhesion), and marrow release rises.","Thymic atrophy and shrinkage of lymphoid tissue (stress involution of the thymus).","Seen with severe stress, Cushing syndrome, and glucocorticoid treatment."]}],"charts":[{"title":"T cell maturation in the thymus","cols":["Stage","Where","What happens","Fails how"],"rows":[["Double negative","Outer cortex","TCR beta rearranges; pre-TCR signals","No RAG: no TCR, cells die (SCID)"],["Double positive","Cortex","TCR alpha rearranges; CD4 and CD8 both expressed","Most die by neglect"],["Positive selection","Cortex, cortical epithelial cells","Modest binding to self MHC lets cells live; MHC I to CD8, MHC II to CD4","No MHC II: no CD4 T cells (bare lymphocyte syndrome)"],["Negative selection","Medulla, medullary epithelial and dendritic cells","Strong binding to self antigen: apoptosis or Treg fate","AIRE loss: autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1"],["Single positive naive T cell","Exits to blood","Homes to lymph nodes and spleen",""]]},{"title":"Spleen jobs and what loss causes","cols":["Job","Cells or region","Finding after splenectomy"],"rows":[["Removes aged and rigid RBCs","Red pulp macrophages","Target cells, acanthocytes"],["Pits out inclusions","Red pulp macrophages","Howell-Jolly bodies, Heinz bodies"],["Clears opsonized encapsulated bacteria","Marginal zone macrophages, splenic macrophages","Sepsis from S pneumoniae, Hib, N meningitidis"],["Makes IgM to polysaccharide","Marginal zone B cells","Poor response to capsules"],["Stores platelets","Red pulp","Thrombocytosis"]]},{"title":"High cortisol and leukocytes","cols":["Cell","Change","Main reason"],"rows":[["Neutrophils","Up","Demargination and marrow release"],["Lymphocytes","Down","Redistribution and apoptosis"],["Eosinophils","Down","Redistribution and apoptosis"],["Thymus and lymphoid tissue","Shrink","Lymphocyte apoptosis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Please SHiNE my SKiS","text":"Encapsulated bacteria: Pseudomonas aeruginosa, S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, N meningitidis, E coli, Salmonella, Klebsiella, group B Streptococcus. The big three after splenectomy are S pneumoniae, Hib, and N meningitidis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Cortex first, medulla second","text":"Thymocytes move from cortex to medulla, and selection follows the same order: first prove you can see self MHC (positive, cortex), then prove you do not attack self (negative, medulla)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Steroids lift neutrophils off the wall","text":"Cortisol raises every count that depends on letting go of the vessel wall (neutrophils) and lowers the ones it pushes out of the blood or kills (lymphocytes, eosinophils)."}],"highYield":["Positive selection happens in the cortex on cortical epithelial MHC; negative selection happens in the medulla with AIRE.","Howell-Jolly bodies on a smear mean the spleen is absent or not working.","Asplenia raises the risk of fatal sepsis from S pneumoniae, Hib, and N meningitidis.","Sickle cell disease causes functional asplenia in early childhood through repeated splenic infarcts.","Naive lymphocytes enter nodes through HEVs using L-selectin and CCR7.","Cortisol causes neutrophilia, lymphopenia, eosinopenia, and thymic atrophy."],"pitfalls":["Positive selection is about MHC restriction (cortex); negative selection is about self tolerance (medulla). Do not swap the locations.","Neutrophilia from cortisol comes from demargination of cells already in vessels and from marrow release; it does not signal infection.","Thrombocytosis after splenectomy comes from losing the platelet pool; platelet production is not the main change.","Effector T cells lose L-selectin and CCR7; only naive and central memory cells home through HEVs."],"dumps":[{"t":"Thymic selection map","p":"Draw a thymic lobule with cortex and medulla. Trace a T cell precursor from entry to exit and label each stage and the cells that select it.","l":["Precursor enters from blood near the corticomedullary junction and moves to the outer cortex","Double negative stage with TCR beta rearrangement","Double positive stage in the cortex","Positive selection on cortical epithelial cell MHC; death by neglect if no binding","MHC I binding gives CD8, MHC II binding gives CD4","Negative selection in the medulla on medullary epithelial and dendritic cells","AIRE drives tissue antigen expression in the medulla","Strong binders die or become FOXP3+ Tregs; mature naive T cells exit"]},{"t":"Splenectomy flowchart","p":"A 20-year-old has his spleen removed after trauma. Make a flowchart from each lost splenic job to the blood smear finding or clinical risk it causes.","l":["Lost red pulp filtering leads to target cells and acanthocytes","Lost pitting leads to Howell-Jolly bodies and Heinz bodies","Lost platelet pool leads to thrombocytosis","Lost marginal zone B cells leads to weak IgM to capsules","Lost macrophage clearance of opsonized bacteria","Risk of overwhelming sepsis from S pneumoniae, Hib, N meningitidis","Same picture in functional asplenia from sickle cell disease"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on lymphocyte maturation in the thymus"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on the spleen"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on glucocorticoid effects on immunity and inflammation"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-1","IM-2","IM-12","IM-14"]},"IM-9":{"id":"IM-9","summary":"Lymph is the fluid and protein that leak out of capillaries and are carried back to the blood through lymphatic vessels. The exam asks you to explain where lymph comes from, why it is the only way to return filtered protein, what moves it, and what happens when the vessels are damaged or missing.","notes":[{"h":"How lymph forms","points":["Starling forces: capillary hydrostatic pressure and interstitial oncotic pressure push fluid out; plasma oncotic pressure pulls it back, and interstitial hydrostatic pressure opposes filtration only when it is positive.","Filtration out of capillaries is slightly greater than reabsorption into them.","The leftover fluid, about 2 to 3 L per day, enters lymphatic capillaries and becomes lymph.","Interstitial pressure is normally slightly negative in loose tissue (about -3 mmHg in subcutaneous tissue)."]},{"h":"What lymph contains","points":["Interstitial fluid with electrolytes and glucose at about plasma levels.","Plasma protein that leaked out of capillaries; lymph protein ranges from about 2 g/dL (limbs) to about 6 g/dL (liver).","Lymphocytes, especially in efferent lymph leaving nodes.","Chyle: milky intestinal lymph full of chylomicrons after a fatty meal.","Lymph is the only route back to the blood for filtered protein, because protein cannot be reabsorbed into capillaries against its gradient.","Without lymph flow, interstitial protein builds up, interstitial oncotic pressure rises, and edema follows."]},{"h":"What moves lymph","points":["Lymphatic capillaries have overlapping endothelial flaps and anchoring filaments that open when tissue swells.","Collecting lymphatics are divided by one-way valves into segments (lymphangions).","Each lymphangion contracts on its own when stretched by filling (intrinsic smooth muscle pump).","External squeezing helps: skeletal muscle contraction, arterial pulsation, and body movement.","Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure and draws thoracic duct lymph toward the left venous angle.","Rising interstitial pressure (from about -3 up to about 0 to +2 mmHg) can raise lymph flow about 20 fold before it levels off."]},{"h":"Lymphedema","points":["Edema from failed lymph drainage, so the fluid is protein rich.","Early lymphedema may pit; chronic protein-rich fluid causes fibrosis and fat deposition, so it becomes nonpitting.","Skin thickens, with peau d'orange and recurrent cellulitis.","Secondary causes: axillary node dissection or radiation for breast cancer, and Wuchereria bancrofti filariasis.","W bancrofti: mosquito-borne nematode whose adult worms live in lymphatics, causing elephantiasis and hydrocele.","Milroy disease: autosomal dominant FLT4 (VEGFR3) mutation, congenital lower limb lymphedema.","Turner syndrome (45,X): cystic hygroma of the neck in the fetus, webbed neck, and hand and foot lymphedema at birth."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Starling forces at the capillary","cols":["Force","Normal direction","Change that raises lymph load"],"rows":[["Capillary hydrostatic pressure","Out of capillary","Venous obstruction, heart failure, volume overload"],["Plasma oncotic pressure","Into capillary","Low albumin (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition)"],["Interstitial oncotic pressure","Out of capillary","Protein leak (inflammation) or protein not removed (lymph block)"],["Interstitial hydrostatic pressure","Into capillary when positive","Normally slightly negative, so it favors filtration"],["Capillary permeability","Sets leak rate","Inflammation, burns, sepsis"]]},{"title":"Kinds of edema","cols":["Mechanism","Example","Edema fluid protein","Pitting"],"rows":[["High capillary hydrostatic pressure","Heart failure","Low (transudate)","Yes"],["Low plasma oncotic pressure","Nephrotic syndrome","Low (transudate)","Yes"],["High permeability","Inflammation, burns","High (exudate)","Variable"],["Lymphatic obstruction","Axillary dissection, filariasis","High","Early yes, late no"]]},{"title":"Causes of lymphedema","cols":["Cause","Type","Key feature"],"rows":[["Axillary node dissection or radiation","Secondary","Arm swelling on the treated side"],["Wuchereria bancrofti","Secondary (parasitic)","Mosquito vector, elephantiasis, hydrocele"],["Milroy disease","Primary, autosomal dominant","FLT4 (VEGFR3) mutation, congenital leg swelling"],["Turner syndrome","Primary, chromosomal","Cystic hygroma, webbed neck, puffy hands and feet"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Lymph is the protein return line","text":"Capillaries can take water back but not protein. Anything that blocks lymph leaves protein behind, so lymphedema fluid is protein rich and the tissue scars."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Milroy, VEGF receptor 3","text":"VEGF-C acting on VEGFR3 (the FLT4 gene product) builds lymphatic vessels. One mutant FLT4 copy (autosomal dominant) is enough to make the legs swell from birth."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Webbed neck is an old hygroma","text":"In Turner syndrome the fetal jugular lymph sacs fail to drain, the neck balloons (cystic hygroma), and the stretched skin later leaves a webbed neck."}],"highYield":["About 2 to 3 L of lymph returns to the blood each day.","Lymph is the only way filtered plasma protein gets back to the circulation.","Lymph flow rises steeply as interstitial pressure rises, which is a safety factor against edema.","Lymphedema is protein rich and turns nonpitting as fibrosis develops.","Milroy disease is an FLT4 (VEGFR3) mutation; Turner syndrome gives cystic hygroma.","W bancrofti adults block lymphatics and cause elephantiasis of legs and scrotum."],"pitfalls":["Heart failure and nephrotic edema are low-protein transudates; lymphedema is high protein. Protein content tells you the mechanism.","Interstitial pressure is normally slightly negative; it becomes positive only as edema builds.","Chyle is intestinal lymph with chylomicrons; a milky pleural effusion with high triglyceride is a chylothorax from a torn or blocked thoracic duct (trauma, surgery, lymphoma).","Cystic hygroma is linked to Turner syndrome (and other aneuploidies); Milroy disease is a single gene disorder with a normal karyotype."],"dumps":[{"t":"Starling diagram","p":"Draw a capillary from arterial to venous end with the surrounding tissue and a lymphatic capillary. Label all four Starling forces and show where the extra fluid goes.","l":["Capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out, higher at the arterial end","Plasma oncotic pressure pulls fluid in","Interstitial oncotic pressure pulls fluid out","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure, slightly negative normally","Net filtration slightly exceeds reabsorption","Excess fluid and leaked protein enter the lymphatic capillary","About 2 to 3 L of lymph per day returns to the veins"]},{"t":"Lymph flow and lymphedema","p":"Make a table of the forces that move lymph, then list four causes of lymphedema with the mechanism of each.","l":["Intrinsic lymphangion contraction when stretched","One-way valves between lymphangions","Skeletal muscle pump and arterial pulsation","Inspiration lowers thoracic pressure and helps thoracic duct flow","Axillary dissection or radiation removes or scars drainage","W bancrofti adult worms block lymphatics","Milroy disease: FLT4 (VEGFR3) mutation, poor lymphatic development","Turner syndrome: cystic hygroma and congenital lymphedema"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on edema"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on filarial nematodes"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-4","IM-11","CV-10","CV-11"]},"IM-10":{"id":"IM-10","summary":"The chemistry behind immune defense: how phagocytes make bleach, why one purine enzyme decides whether lymphocytes live, which arachidonic acid products drive inflammation, and how vitamins, minerals, and protein supply keep the immune system working. The exam turns each of these into a deficiency you have to predict.","notes":[{"h":"Respiratory burst","points":["Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase in the pentose phosphate pathway makes NADPH.","NADPH oxidase in the phagosome membrane uses NADPH to turn O2 into superoxide.","Superoxide dismutase converts superoxide to hydrogen peroxide (H2O2).","Myeloperoxidase (in neutrophil granules) combines H2O2 with chloride to make hypochlorous acid (HOCl, bleach).","Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD): NADPH oxidase defect, most often X-linked (CYBB, gp91phox), some autosomal recessive."]},{"h":"CGD infections and tests","points":["CGD phagocytes can still make HOCl if the microbe supplies its own H2O2.","Catalase-positive microbes destroy their own H2O2, so they survive: S aureus, Aspergillus, Burkholderia cepacia, Serratia, Nocardia.","Uncleared organisms are walled off in granulomas (lymph nodes, liver, lung, skin).","Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) flow test: normal cells fluoresce after stimulation; CGD cells show little or no fluorescence.","Nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT): normal cells reduce the yellow dye to blue; CGD cells stay yellow."]},{"h":"ADA deficiency SCID","points":["Adenosine deaminase converts adenosine and deoxyadenosine to inosine and deoxyinosine in purine salvage.","Without ADA, deoxyadenosine builds up and lymphocytes phosphorylate it to dATP.","dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, so no deoxynucleotides are made for DNA.","Dividing lymphocytes die: T, B, and NK cells are all low (autosomal recessive SCID)."]},{"h":"Arachidonic acid products","points":["Phospholipase A2 frees arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids; glucocorticoids inhibit this step.","Cyclooxygenase (COX-1, COX-2) makes PGE2 (fever, pain, vasodilation), PGI2, and thromboxane A2.","Thromboxane A2 (platelets) causes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction; PGI2 (endothelium) does the opposite.","5-lipoxygenase makes LTB4, a strong neutrophil chemoattractant.","LTC4, LTD4, LTE4 (cysteinyl leukotrienes) cause bronchoconstriction and raise vascular permeability."]},{"h":"Vitamin D and granulomas","points":["Granuloma macrophages in sarcoidosis express 1-alpha-hydroxylase that PTH does not control.","They convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol, which raises gut calcium absorption: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, low PTH.","Toll-like receptor signals in macrophages induce the vitamin D receptor and 1-alpha-hydroxylase.","Calcitriol inside macrophages induces cathelicidin (LL-37), an antimicrobial peptide active against M tuberculosis."]},{"h":"Nutrition and immunity","points":["Zinc deficiency: thymic atrophy, lymphopenia, poor T cell function, poor wound healing.","Acrodermatitis enteropathica: autosomal recessive defect of the intestinal zinc transporter ZIP4 (SLC39A4); rash around mouth, anus, and limbs, alopecia, diarrhea.","Vitamin A keeps mucosal epithelium differentiated; deficiency causes squamous metaplasia (keratinization) of airway, urinary, and conjunctival epithelium.","Vitamin A deficiency makes measles more severe and more often fatal.","Protein-energy malnutrition is the most common cause of acquired immunodeficiency worldwide.","Malnutrition shrinks the thymus and weakens cell-mediated immunity (poor delayed skin test responses, low CD4 counts).","Marasmus: calorie deficit with severe wasting and no edema. Kwashiorkor: protein deficit with edema, fatty liver, and skin changes."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Arachidonic acid products","cols":["Product","Enzyme","Main actions"],"rows":[["PGE2","COX","Fever, pain sensitization, vasodilation"],["PGI2 (prostacyclin)","COX (endothelium)","Vasodilation, blocks platelet aggregation"],["Thromboxane A2","COX (platelets)","Platelet aggregation, vasoconstriction"],["LTB4","5-lipoxygenase","Neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion"],["LTC4, LTD4, LTE4","5-lipoxygenase","Bronchoconstriction, vascular permeability, vasoconstriction"]]},{"title":"Phagocyte killing defects","cols":["Defect","Inheritance","DHR or NBT","Typical problem"],"rows":[["NADPH oxidase (CGD)","Mostly X-linked","Abnormal","Catalase-positive bacteria and fungi, granulomas"],["Myeloperoxidase deficiency","Autosomal recessive","NBT normal","Often no symptoms; some Candida infections"],["Severe G6PD deficiency","X-linked","Can be abnormal","Hemolysis; rarely CGD-like infections"]]},{"title":"Nutrients and immune effects","cols":["Deficiency","Immune effect","Other findings"],"rows":[["Zinc","Thymic atrophy, poor T cell function","Acral and periorificial rash, alopecia, diarrhea, poor wound healing"],["Vitamin A","Mucosal squamous metaplasia, worse measles","Night blindness, Bitot spots, xerophthalmia"],["Vitamin D","Less macrophage cathelicidin","Rickets, osteomalacia"],["Protein-energy (marasmus, kwashiorkor)","Thymic atrophy, weak cell-mediated immunity","Wasting; edema and fatty liver in kwashiorkor"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Neutrophils arrive B4 others","text":"LTB4 is the leukotriene that calls in neutrophils."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Kwashiorkor: a protein-deficient MEAL","text":"Malnutrition, Edema, Anemia, Liver (fatty)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Borrowed peroxide","text":"A CGD phagocyte can only make bleach with H2O2 the microbe hands it. Catalase-positive microbes destroy their own H2O2, so they are the ones that cause infections."},{"kind":"trick","label":"ADA: too much of one building block","text":"Excess dATP shuts off ribonucleotide reductase, so the cell starves for all the other deoxynucleotides. Lymphocytes, which divide fast and trap dATP, die first."}],"highYield":["CGD: NADPH oxidase defect, abnormal DHR test, infections with S aureus, Aspergillus, B cepacia, Serratia, Nocardia.","ADA deficiency: dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, giving SCID with T, B, and NK cells all low.","LTB4 calls neutrophils; LTC4, LTD4, LTE4 constrict bronchi and raise permeability; PGE2 causes fever.","Sarcoidosis hypercalcemia comes from unregulated macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase making calcitriol, with low PTH.","Acrodermatitis enteropathica is inherited zinc malabsorption with rash, alopecia, diarrhea, and infections.","Vitamin A deficiency increases measles severity and mortality.","Protein-energy malnutrition is the leading cause of immunodeficiency worldwide."],"pitfalls":["CGD has normal phagocytosis and normal myeloperoxidase; only the oxidative burst fails.","MPO deficiency leaves the NBT test normal, because superoxide is still made.","Sarcoidosis hypercalcemia has low PTH and high calcitriol; primary hyperparathyroidism has high PTH.","Kwashiorkor has edema from low albumin; marasmus has wasting without edema.","Glucocorticoids block phospholipase A2 (both COX and LOX products fall); NSAIDs block only COX."],"dumps":[{"t":"Respiratory burst pathway","p":"Draw a neutrophil phagosome containing a bacterium. Trace the respiratory burst from glucose to HOCl and mark where CGD breaks the chain.","l":["Pentose phosphate pathway (G6PD) makes NADPH","NADPH oxidase on the phagosome membrane makes superoxide","Superoxide dismutase makes H2O2","Myeloperoxidase plus chloride makes HOCl","CGD: NADPH oxidase missing, usually X-linked","Catalase-positive organisms remove their own H2O2 and survive","DHR fluorescence low and NBT stays yellow in CGD"]},{"t":"Arachidonic acid tree","p":"Starting from a membrane phospholipid, draw the arachidonic acid pathways and write one main action next to each product.","l":["Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid; glucocorticoids inhibit it","COX branch to PGH2","PGE2: fever, pain, vasodilation","PGI2: vasodilation, platelet inhibition","Thromboxane A2: platelet aggregation, vasoconstriction","5-lipoxygenase branch","LTB4: neutrophil chemotaxis","LTC4, LTD4, LTE4: bronchoconstriction and vascular permeability"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Pentose Phosphate Pathway and Nicotinamide Adenine Dinucleotide Phosphate"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Nucleotide Metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on mediators of inflammation"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-6","IM-12","IM-15-A4","IM-15-C4"]},"IM-11":{"id":"IM-11","summary":"The biochemistry of what lymph carries: dietary fat packaged as chylomicrons that travel through the lymph before reaching the blood, and plasma protein that leaks out of capillaries at different rates in different organs. The exam asks you to follow a chylomicron from the gut to the liver and to predict what happens when one step is missing.","notes":[{"h":"Building a chylomicron","points":["Enterocytes take up 2-monoglyceride and free fatty acids from micelles.","Smooth ER enzymes re-esterify them into triglyceride.","ApoB-48 comes from the apoB gene; intestinal mRNA editing creates an early stop codon, so the protein is about 48% of apoB-100.","Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) loads triglyceride onto apoB-48 in the ER.","The chylomicron passes through the Golgi and is released from the basolateral side."]},{"h":"Into the lymph","points":["Chylomicrons are too large to enter blood capillaries, so they enter lacteals (lymphatic capillaries in the villi).","They travel through the cisterna chyli and thoracic duct and enter the blood at the left venous angle.","This route bypasses the liver, so dietary fat reaches muscle and adipose tissue first.","Short and medium chain fatty acids skip chylomicrons and go straight to portal blood.","Intestinal lymph full of chylomicrons is milky (chyle)."]},{"h":"Breaking down a chylomicron","points":["In the blood, HDL hands the chylomicron apoC-II and apoE.","ApoC-II activates lipoprotein lipase (LPL) on capillary endothelium of adipose tissue, muscle, and heart.","LPL frees fatty acids: muscle oxidizes them, adipose tissue stores them as triglyceride. Glycerol goes to the liver.","Insulin raises adipose LPL after a meal.","The smaller remnant keeps apoE and is taken up by the liver through apoE binding to hepatic receptors (LDL receptor and LRP1).","LPL or apoC-II deficiency: chylomicrons stay in plasma (very high triglyceride, pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas)."]},{"h":"Abetalipoproteinemia","points":["Autosomal recessive loss of MTP.","No apoB-48 or apoB-100 lipoproteins: chylomicrons, VLDL, and LDL are absent; plasma triglyceride and cholesterol are very low.","Fat stays trapped in enterocytes (lipid-laden, foamy cells on biopsy) and steatorrhea starts in infancy.","Altered red cell membrane lipids produce acanthocytes.","Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, mostly vitamin E: spinocerebellar ataxia and retinitis pigmentosa."]},{"h":"Why lymph protein differs by organ","points":["Liver sinusoids are discontinuous with gaps and little basement membrane, so plasma protein leaks freely; hepatic lymph protein is about 6 g/dL.","Intestinal capillaries are fenestrated; intestinal lymph protein is about 3 to 4 g/dL.","Skeletal muscle capillaries are continuous with tight junctions; lymph protein is about 2 g/dL.","Liver and gut supply most of the protein in thoracic duct lymph."]}],"charts":[{"title":"A chylomicron from start to finish","cols":["Step","Where","Key protein","If it fails"],"rows":[["Triglyceride resynthesis","Enterocyte smooth ER","Acyltransferases","Fat malabsorption"],["Lipid loading","Enterocyte ER","MTP and apoB-48","Abetalipoproteinemia"],["Exit to lymph","Lacteal, thoracic duct","","Chylothorax if the thoracic duct is cut"],["Hydrolysis of triglyceride","Capillary endothelium of muscle and fat","LPL activated by apoC-II","Type I hyperchylomicronemia"],["Remnant uptake","Liver","ApoE","Remnant buildup (apoE2 homozygotes)"]]},{"title":"Capillary type and lymph protein","cols":["Tissue","Capillary type","Lymph protein (about)"],"rows":[["Liver","Sinusoidal (discontinuous)","6 g/dL"],["Intestine","Fenestrated","3 to 4 g/dL"],["Skeletal muscle, skin","Continuous","2 g/dL"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"48 means gut, 100 means liver","text":"ApoB-48 is the short, edited intestinal form on chylomicrons. ApoB-100 is the full liver form on VLDL and LDL. MTP is needed for both, which is why abetalipoproteinemia removes all of them."},{"kind":"trick","label":"C-II cuts, E eats","text":"ApoC-II turns on lipoprotein lipase to cut triglyceride; apoE is the tag the liver uses to eat the remnant."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Leaky wall, rich lymph","text":"The leakier the capillary wall, the more protein in the lymph: sinusoid beats fenestrated beats continuous."}],"highYield":["Chylomicrons enter lacteals and the thoracic duct, reaching the blood without passing through the liver.","MTP loads triglyceride onto apoB-48; apoB-48 comes from mRNA editing of the apoB transcript.","ApoC-II activates lipoprotein lipase; apoE directs remnant uptake by the liver.","Abetalipoproteinemia: steatorrhea, acanthocytes, very low lipids, and vitamin E deficiency with ataxia.","Hepatic lymph has the most protein because liver sinusoids are discontinuous.","Medium chain fatty acids bypass the lymph and go to portal blood."],"pitfalls":["Abetalipoproteinemia removes chylomicrons and VLDL and LDL; LPL or apoC-II deficiency raises chylomicrons. Opposite plasma pictures.","LPL works on the capillary surface; hormone-sensitive lipase works inside fat cells. Do not swap them.","ApoB-48 and apoB-100 come from the same gene; the difference is RNA editing, not alternative genes.","Muscle lymph protein is low because muscle capillaries are continuous; low lymph protein there is normal."],"dumps":[{"t":"Chylomicron journey","p":"Draw an enterocyte, a lacteal, the thoracic duct, a muscle capillary, and the liver. Trace a dietary triglyceride from the gut lumen to the liver and label each protein that acts on it.","l":["Micelles deliver 2-monoglyceride and fatty acids to the enterocyte","Smooth ER re-esterifies triglyceride","MTP loads it onto apoB-48","Chylomicron enters the lacteal, not the portal blood","Thoracic duct carries it to the left venous angle","HDL donates apoC-II and apoE","ApoC-II activates LPL on muscle and adipose capillaries","ApoE-tagged remnant taken up by the liver"]},{"t":"Abetalipoproteinemia work-up","p":"A 9-month-old has fatty stools and poor growth. Make a flowchart from the missing protein to every lab and clinical finding you would expect.","l":["MTP missing (autosomal recessive)","No chylomicrons, VLDL, or LDL","Very low plasma triglyceride and cholesterol","Lipid-laden enterocytes on biopsy","Steatorrhea and failure to thrive","Acanthocytes on the smear","Vitamin E deficiency with ataxia and retinal degeneration"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Dietary Lipid Metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-9","IM-4","GI-7","CV-13"]},"IM-12":{"id":"IM-12","summary":"The genes that build and control lymphocytes: the recombination machinery that makes antigen receptors, the cytokine and signaling genes behind each type of SCID, and the genes that enforce tolerance. The exam gives you a lymphocyte count, an immunoglobulin pattern, or a pedigree and asks which gene is broken.","notes":[{"h":"V(D)J recombination","points":["RAG1 and RAG2 cut DNA at recombination signal sequences next to V, D, and J segments.","Heavy chain and TCR beta use V, D, and J; light chains and TCR alpha use V and J.","Nonhomologous end joining repairs the cuts (Artemis opens the hairpin ends).","TdT adds random N nucleotides at the joints, giving junctional diversity.","RAG deficiency: no receptors, so no T or B cells; NK cells need no rearranged receptor and are preserved."]},{"h":"SCID types by lymphocyte profile","points":["X-linked SCID: IL2RG mutation in the common gamma chain shared by IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, and IL-21 receptors.","No IL-7 signal means no T cells; no IL-15 signal means no NK cells; B cells are present but cannot work without T cell help.","JAK3 deficiency (autosomal recessive) copies the X-linked SCID profile because JAK3 signals for the common gamma chain.","ADA deficiency: T, B, and NK cells all absent.","T cell receptor excision circles (TRECs) are DNA circles cut out during TCR rearrangement; low TRECs on newborn screening flag SCID.","All SCID: absent thymic shadow, thrush, chronic diarrhea, Pneumocystis pneumonia, and danger from live vaccines."]},{"h":"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","points":["X-linked recessive mutation in WAS, which encodes WASp, an actin cytoskeleton regulator in blood cells.","Thrombocytopenia with small platelets, eczema, and recurrent infections.","Immunoglobulins: low IgM, high IgA and IgE, IgG often normal.","Higher risk of autoimmunity and B cell lymphoma.","Carrier mother and unaffected father: each son has a 50% chance of being affected; each daughter a 50% chance of being a carrier."]},{"h":"Hyper-IgM syndrome","points":["Most common form: X-linked CD40 ligand (CD154) deficiency on activated CD4 T cells.","CD40L binding CD40 on B cells drives class switching and germinal center formation.","Result: normal or high IgM with low IgG, IgA, and IgE.","CD40L also activates macrophages and dendritic cells, so cell-mediated defense is weak too.","Infections: Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, Cryptosporidium diarrhea, and recurrent sinopulmonary bacterial infections."]},{"h":"Tolerance genes","points":["AIRE (autosomal recessive): failed thymic negative selection causes autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1 (APS-1).","APS-1 classic triad: chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and primary adrenal insufficiency.","FOXP3 (X-linked) is the master transcription factor of regulatory T cells.","FOXP3 loss causes IPEX: immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy (neonatal type 1 diabetes, thyroiditis), enteropathy with severe diarrhea, X-linked."]},{"h":"Fas and lymphocyte death","points":["Fas (CD95) on activated lymphocytes binds Fas ligand and triggers caspase 8, then executioner caspases (extrinsic apoptosis).","This ends clonal expansion after an infection and deletes self-reactive lymphocytes in the periphery.","Autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome (ALPS), usually from FAS mutation: chronic lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, autoimmune cytopenias.","ALPS labs: more CD4- CD8- (double negative) alpha-beta T cells in blood; higher lymphoma risk."]}],"charts":[{"title":"SCID by gene","cols":["Gene","Inheritance","T","B","NK","Why"],"rows":[["IL2RG (common gamma chain)","X-linked","Absent","Present","Absent","No IL-7 and IL-15 signals"],["JAK3","Autosomal recessive","Absent","Present","Absent","No signal from common gamma chain"],["ADA","Autosomal recessive","Absent","Absent","Absent","dATP blocks ribonucleotide reductase"],["RAG1 or RAG2","Autosomal recessive","Absent","Absent","Present","No V(D)J recombination"]]},{"title":"Inherited immune disorders and their immunoglobulins","cols":["Disorder","Gene","Inheritance","Immunoglobulin pattern","Key infections or features"],"rows":[["Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","WAS","X-linked recessive","Low IgM, high IgA and IgE","Small platelets, eczema, encapsulated bacteria"],["Hyper-IgM syndrome","CD40LG","X-linked recessive","High or normal IgM; low IgG, IgA, IgE","Pneumocystis, Cryptosporidium"],["APS-1","AIRE","Autosomal recessive","Usually normal; organ-specific autoantibodies","Candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, adrenal failure"],["IPEX","FOXP3","X-linked recessive","Often high IgE","Neonatal diabetes, enteropathy, eczema"],["ALPS","FAS","Usually autosomal dominant","Often high IgG","Lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, cytopenias"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"WATER","text":"Wiskott-Aldrich: Thrombocytopenia, Eczema, Recurrent infections."},{"kind":"trick","label":"NK cells tell you the gene","text":"In SCID, NK cells present means RAG (they need no rearranged receptor). NK cells absent with B cells present means the common gamma chain or JAK3. Everything absent means ADA."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Stuck on M","text":"Hyper-IgM: B cells make IgM first and need CD40L from T cells to switch. No CD40L, no switch, so IgM is the only antibody left."},{"kind":"trick","label":"AIRE in the medulla, FOXP3 in the periphery","text":"AIRE failure lets self-reactive T cells escape the thymus (central tolerance). FOXP3 failure means there are no Tregs to hold them back afterward (peripheral tolerance)."}],"highYield":["RAG deficiency: T and B cells absent, NK cells present.","X-linked SCID (IL2RG): T and NK absent, B cells present but nonfunctional.","Low TRECs on newborn screening point to SCID.","Wiskott-Aldrich: small platelets, eczema, infections, low IgM, high IgA and IgE, X-linked.","CD40L deficiency: high IgM, low other isotypes, Pneumocystis and Cryptosporidium infections.","AIRE mutation: chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, Addison disease.","FAS mutation (ALPS): lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, autoimmune cytopenias, double negative T cells."],"pitfalls":["Wiskott-Aldrich platelets are small; immune thrombocytopenia and Bernard-Soulier platelets are normal sized or large.","Hyper-IgM patients have normal or high IgM but almost no IgG, so they lack the IgG that opsonizes and neutralizes.","X-linked SCID boys have B cells on flow cytometry; the B cells are present but cannot make useful antibody without T cell help.","In an X-linked pedigree, an affected father passes the gene to all daughters (carriers) and to none of his sons.","ALPS is lymphoproliferative but not malignant at first; the big nodes come from failed apoptosis."],"dumps":[{"t":"SCID classification table","p":"Build a table on blank paper for four SCID genes. For each, list the inheritance, which lymphocytes are present or absent, and the mechanism.","l":["IL2RG: X-linked, T and NK absent, B present","Common gamma chain is shared by IL-2, 4, 7, 9, 15, 21 receptors","JAK3: autosomal recessive, same profile as IL2RG","ADA: autosomal recessive, all three absent, dATP toxicity","RAG1 or RAG2: autosomal recessive, T and B absent, NK present","RAG needed for V(D)J recombination","Low TRECs on newborn screening in all"]},{"t":"Wiskott-Aldrich pedigree","p":"Draw a three-generation pedigree for a woman who carries a WAS mutation and her unaffected husband. Mark expected outcomes for their sons and daughters and list the clinical features of an affected son.","l":["X-linked recessive inheritance","Each son: 50% affected","Each daughter: 50% carrier, usually unaffected","Thrombocytopenia with small platelets and bleeding","Eczema","Recurrent infections, especially encapsulated bacteria","Low IgM, high IgA and IgE","Higher risk of lymphoma and autoimmunity"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Congenital and Acquired Immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Immunological Tolerance and Autoimmunity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-8","IM-10","IM-15-A","IM-15-A4"]},"IM-13":{"id":"IM-13","summary":"How the immune system gets switched on by microbes, tumors, and vaccines, and how it turns against the body in autoimmunity and hypersensitivity. These are the core rules the exam uses to explain almost every immune disease, so expect to classify reactions and predict what blocking a signal will do.","notes":[{"h":"Innate versus adaptive","points":["Innate: germline-encoded pattern recognition receptors (Toll-like receptors, NOD-like receptors) detect microbial patterns.","Examples: TLR4 senses LPS, TLR5 senses flagellin, TLR3 senses dsRNA, TLR9 senses unmethylated CpG DNA.","Innate cells and proteins: neutrophils, macrophages, NK cells, dendritic cells, complement. Response in minutes to hours, no memory.","Adaptive: T and B cells with rearranged receptors, clonal expansion over days, and memory.","Innate signals (cytokines, B7 on dendritic cells) are needed to start an adaptive response."]},{"h":"Activating a T cell","points":["Signal 1: the TCR binds a peptide on MHC (CD4 with class II, CD8 with class I).","Signal 2: CD28 on the T cell binds B7 (CD80 or CD86) on the antigen-presenting cell.","Signal 1 without signal 2 leads to anergy, a key form of peripheral tolerance.","CTLA-4 binds B7 more tightly than CD28 does and shuts activation down; Tregs express it constantly.","PD-1 on activated T cells binds PD-L1 or PD-L2 and dampens T cell signaling.","Antibodies that block CTLA-4 or PD-1 release this brake, boosting antitumor T cells and causing autoimmune side effects."]},{"h":"Immunity to tumors","points":["Tumor antigens include mutated proteins (neoantigens), viral oncoproteins (HPV E6 and E7), and overexpressed self proteins.","Dendritic cells cross-present tumor antigen to prime CD8 T cells; CD8 T cells kill tumor cells showing it on MHC class I.","NK cells kill cells that lose MHC class I (missing self) or express stress ligands.","Escape: loss of MHC class I or beta-2 microglobulin hides tumors from CD8 T cells.","Escape: PD-L1 on tumor cells switches off PD-1+ T cells; tumors also recruit Tregs and secrete TGF-beta."]},{"h":"Vaccines","points":["Live attenuated (MMR, varicella, yellow fever): replicate, give strong cellular and humoral immunity, often lifelong.","Live vaccines are avoided in severe immunodeficiency and in pregnancy.","Inactivated (rabies, hepatitis A, injected polio, injected influenza): mostly antibody, need boosters.","Subunit or recombinant (hepatitis B surface antigen, HPV): a single protein, safe in immunodeficiency.","Toxoid (tetanus, diphtheria): inactivated toxin that raises neutralizing antibody.","Conjugate (Hib, pneumococcal, meningococcal): capsule linked to a protein carrier turns a T-independent response into a T-dependent one, so infants under 2 years respond and form memory."]},{"h":"Why autoimmunity happens","points":["Failed central tolerance: self-reactive cells escape thymic negative selection (AIRE defects).","Failed peripheral tolerance: lost anergy, Treg failure (FOXP3), or lost Fas-mediated deletion.","HLA susceptibility: HLA-B27 (ankylosing spondylitis), HLA-DR3 and DR4 (type 1 diabetes), HLA-DR4 (rheumatoid arthritis), HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 (celiac disease).","Molecular mimicry: group A strep M protein cross-reacts with cardiac myosin and valve tissue, causing rheumatic fever.","Molecular mimicry: Campylobacter jejuni lipooligosaccharide resembles nerve gangliosides, causing Guillain-Barre syndrome."]},{"h":"Hypersensitivity","points":["Type I: IgE on mast cells cross-linked by antigen; histamine and leukotrienes within minutes.","Type II: IgG or IgM bind antigens on cells or matrix; complement, phagocytosis, or receptor blocking or stimulation.","Type III: antigen-antibody complexes deposit in vessels, glomeruli, and joints; complement and neutrophils damage tissue; low C3.","Type IV: T cells, no antibody; CD4 Th1 cells activate macrophages or CD8 cells kill directly; peaks at 48 to 72 hours."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Innate and adaptive immunity","cols":["Feature","Innate","Adaptive"],"rows":[["Receptors","Germline pattern recognition receptors","Rearranged TCR and BCR"],["Specificity","Shared microbial patterns","Single epitope"],["Speed","Minutes to hours","Days (first exposure)"],["Memory","None","Yes, faster and stronger on re-exposure"],["Main players","Neutrophils, macrophages, NK cells, complement","T cells, B cells, antibodies"]]},{"title":"Vaccine types","cols":["Type","Examples","Immunity","Notes"],"rows":[["Live attenuated","MMR, varicella, yellow fever","Strong cellular and humoral","Avoid in severe immunodeficiency and pregnancy"],["Inactivated","Rabies, hepatitis A, injected polio","Mostly humoral","Boosters needed"],["Subunit or recombinant","Hepatitis B, HPV","Humoral","Safe in immunodeficiency"],["Toxoid","Tetanus, diphtheria","Antitoxin antibody","Does not stop colonization"],["Conjugate polysaccharide","Hib, pneumococcal, meningococcal","T-dependent humoral with memory","Works in infants under 2 years"]]},{"title":"Hypersensitivity types","cols":["Type","Effector","Timing","Examples"],"rows":[["I","IgE, mast cells, basophils","Minutes","Anaphylaxis, allergic asthma, hives"],["II","IgG or IgM against cell or matrix antigen","Hours to days","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia, Goodpasture syndrome, myasthenia gravis, rheumatic fever"],["III","Immune complexes, complement, neutrophils","About 1 to 2 weeks after antigen (Arthus: hours)","SLE, poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, polyarteritis nodosa, serum sickness"],["IV","CD4 Th1 or CD8 T cells","48 to 72 hours","PPD test, contact dermatitis, granulomas, acute cellular rejection"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ACID","text":"Hypersensitivity types I to IV: Anaphylactic and atopic, Cytotoxic (antibody mediated), Immune complex, Delayed (cell mediated)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"RIP Always","text":"Inactivated vaccines: Rabies, Influenza (injected), Polio (injected Salk), hepatitis A."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Is there antibody, and where is the antigen?","text":"No antibody means type IV. IgE means type I. Antibody against something fixed on a cell or membrane means type II. Antibody bound to free antigen that lands somewhere else means type III."}],"highYield":["T cell activation needs TCR plus MHC (signal 1) and CD28 plus B7 (signal 2); signal 1 alone gives anergy.","CTLA-4 and PD-1 are inhibitory checkpoints that tumors exploit.","NK cells kill tumor cells that lose MHC class I.","Live attenuated vaccines give the strongest immunity but are avoided in immunodeficiency and pregnancy.","Conjugate vaccines make polysaccharide responses T-dependent, so infants respond.","Rheumatic fever: M protein mimicry; Guillain-Barre: C jejuni ganglioside mimicry.","Type III disease lowers complement (C3); type IV involves no antibody."],"pitfalls":["Rheumatic fever and Goodpasture syndrome are type II; poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is type III. Same organism can cause different types.","Contact dermatitis (poison ivy, nickel) is type IV; latex hives or anaphylaxis are type I.","CTLA-4 and CD28 bind the same ligand (B7); CTLA-4 inhibits, CD28 activates.","Losing MHC class I protects a tumor from CD8 T cells but makes it a target for NK cells.","Pure polysaccharide vaccines work poorly before age 2; the conjugate form fixes this."],"dumps":[{"t":"Two-signal T cell activation","p":"Draw a dendritic cell meeting a naive CD4 T cell. Label every molecule needed to activate the T cell and every molecule that can stop it.","l":["Peptide on MHC class II binds the TCR (signal 1)","CD4 binds MHC class II","B7 (CD80 or CD86) binds CD28 (signal 2)","Cytokines direct the type of T cell (signal 3)","Signal 1 without signal 2 causes anergy","CTLA-4 binds B7 more strongly than CD28 and inhibits","PD-1 binds PD-L1 or PD-L2 and inhibits"]},{"t":"Hypersensitivity comparison table","p":"Build a table of the four hypersensitivity types with the effector, the timing, the mechanism of damage, and two diseases for each.","l":["Type I: IgE and mast cells, minutes, anaphylaxis and allergic asthma","Type II: IgG or IgM against fixed antigen, complement and phagocytosis or receptor effects","Type II examples: autoimmune hemolytic anemia, myasthenia gravis, Goodpasture syndrome","Type III: immune complexes, complement, neutrophils, low C3","Type III examples: SLE, poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, serum sickness","Type IV: T cells, 48 to 72 hours, no antibody","Type IV examples: PPD test, contact dermatitis, granulomas"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Innate Immunity: The Early Defense Against Infections"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on immune responses against tumors"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-5","IM-6","IM-15-C","IM-15-D2"]},"IM-14":{"id":"IM-14","summary":"The microbiology of the systemic infections in this system: parvovirus B19, Haemophilus influenzae type b, influenza, measles, mumps, rubella, EBV, HHV-6 and HHV-7, group A strep, and Toxoplasma. The exam expects you to link each organism's structure, receptor, spread, and virulence factors to the disease it causes.","notes":[{"h":"Parvovirus B19","points":["Small, nonenveloped, single-stranded DNA virus; needs dividing cells to replicate.","Binds P antigen (globoside) on erythroid progenitors, which it infects and kills.","Red cell production stops for about a week (reticulocytopenia); healthy people barely notice because red cells live about 120 days.","Chronic hemolysis (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis): transient aplastic crisis.","Spread by respiratory droplets, blood products, and across the placenta; fetal infection can cause severe anemia and hydrops fetalis.","The slapped cheek rash of fifth disease appears with the antibody response, after the infectious phase."]},{"h":"Haemophilus influenzae type b","points":["Small gram-negative coccobacillus spread by respiratory droplets.","Polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP) capsule is the main virulence factor; it resists phagocytosis and complement.","IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA and helps colonization.","Grows on chocolate agar, which supplies factor X (hemin) and factor V (NAD+).","Causes meningitis, epiglottitis, pneumonia, and septic arthritis in unvaccinated children; the vaccine is PRP conjugated to a protein."]},{"h":"Influenza","points":["Orthomyxovirus: enveloped, segmented, negative-sense single-stranded RNA (8 segments in influenza A).","Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid on respiratory epithelium and drives membrane fusion.","Neuraminidase cleaves sialic acid so new virions can leave the cell.","Antigenic drift: point mutations in HA and NA cause yearly epidemics.","Antigenic shift: two strains swap gene segments (reassortment) to make a new HA or NA subtype; pandemics, influenza A only."]},{"h":"Measles, mumps, rubella","points":["Measles and mumps are paramyxoviruses: enveloped, nonsegmented, negative-sense single-stranded RNA.","Their F (fusion) protein fuses cells into syncytia; measles makes Warthin-Finkeldey giant cells in lymphoid tissue.","Measles: cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, Koplik spots, rash spreading head to toe; pneumonia and encephalitis are the dangerous complications.","Mumps: parotitis, orchitis, and aseptic meningitis.","Rubella: enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus (formerly classed as a togavirus); mild rash and postauricular lymphadenopathy.","All three spread by respiratory droplets; measles is also airborne and extremely contagious."]},{"h":"EBV, HHV-6, and HHV-7","points":["Herpesviruses: enveloped, double-stranded DNA, lifelong latency; spread by saliva.","EBV enters B cells through CD21 (complement receptor 2) and stays latent in memory B cells.","Atypical lymphocytes in mononucleosis are reactive CD8 T cells; heterophile antibodies come from polyclonal B cell activation.","HHV-6 and HHV-7 infect CD4 T cells and cause roseola infantum: high fever for days, then rash as the fever breaks."]},{"h":"Streptococcus pyogenes (group A)","points":["Gram-positive cocci in chains, catalase-negative, beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-sensitive.","M protein blocks phagocytosis and complement deposition; it is also the mimicry antigen in rheumatic fever.","Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens: they link MHC class II to the TCR beta chain outside the peptide groove and activate huge numbers of T cells.","Scarlet fever: sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue, circumoral pallor, later desquamation; superantigens can also cause streptococcal toxic shock."]},{"h":"Toxoplasma gondii","points":["Obligate intracellular protozoan; the cat is the definitive host where the sexual stage occurs in the gut.","Cats shed oocysts in feces; they become infectious after 1 to 5 days in the environment.","Humans are infected by sporulated oocysts (soil, litter, produce) or by tissue cysts with bradyzoites in undercooked meat.","Tachyzoites multiply fast and spread; immunity forces them into tissue cysts (bradyzoites) in brain, muscle, and retina.","Primary infection in pregnancy: tachyzoites cross the placenta, causing chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and intracranial calcifications."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Viruses in this unit","cols":["Virus","Genome","Envelope","Key receptor or protein","Spread"],"rows":[["Parvovirus B19","ssDNA","No","P antigen on erythroid progenitors","Respiratory, transplacental, blood"],["Influenza","Segmented negative-sense ssRNA","Yes","Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid; neuraminidase releases virions","Respiratory droplets"],["Measles","Nonsegmented negative-sense ssRNA","Yes","F protein forms syncytia","Airborne and droplets"],["Mumps","Nonsegmented negative-sense ssRNA","Yes","F protein; HN attachment protein","Respiratory droplets, saliva"],["Rubella","Positive-sense ssRNA","Yes","E1 and E2 envelope proteins","Respiratory droplets, transplacental"],["EBV","dsDNA","Yes","gp350 binds CD21 on B cells","Saliva"],["HHV-6 and HHV-7","dsDNA","Yes","Tropism for CD4 T cells","Saliva"]]},{"title":"Bacteria and parasite in this unit","cols":["Organism","Morphology and lab","Main virulence factors","Disease link"],"rows":[["H influenzae type b","Gram-negative coccobacillus; chocolate agar, needs factors X and V","PRP capsule, IgA protease","Meningitis, epiglottitis"],["S pyogenes","Gram-positive cocci in chains; catalase-negative, beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-sensitive","M protein, pyrogenic exotoxins (superantigens)","Scarlet fever, rheumatic fever"],["Toxoplasma gondii","Obligate intracellular protozoan","Tachyzoites invade any nucleated cell; tissue cysts persist","Congenital toxoplasmosis, encephalitis in AIDS"]]},{"title":"Antigenic drift versus shift","cols":["Feature","Drift","Shift"],"rows":[["Genetic change","Point mutations in HA and NA genes","Reassortment of whole segments"],["Which influenza","A and B","A only"],["Result","Seasonal epidemics","Pandemics"],["Population immunity","Partly escaped","Mostly absent"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Five and dime for chocolate","text":"When a child has flu, mom goes to the five (factor V) and dime (factor X) store to buy chocolate: H influenzae needs factors V and X and grows on chocolate agar."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"B-BRAS","text":"Group B strep is Bacitracin Resistant, group A strep is Sensitive."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Measles: 3 Cs","text":"Cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, followed by Koplik spots and the descending rash."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Shift needs a swap","text":"Only a segmented genome can trade whole genes. Influenza A has animal hosts that carry other subtypes, so it alone can shift."}],"highYield":["Parvovirus B19 binds P antigen on erythroid progenitors: aplastic crisis in hemolytic anemia, hydrops fetalis in the fetus.","Hib PRP capsule is the main virulence factor and the target of the conjugate vaccine.","Influenza hemagglutinin binds sialic acid; neuraminidase releases virions; shift by reassortment causes pandemics.","Measles F protein forms Warthin-Finkeldey giant cells.","EBV enters B cells through CD21; the atypical lymphocytes are CD8 T cells.","Group A strep pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens that cause scarlet fever.","Toxoplasma: cat is the definitive host; humans get oocysts from cat feces or tissue cysts from undercooked meat."],"pitfalls":["Parvovirus B19 is the only DNA virus here that is single-stranded; herpesviruses are double-stranded.","Measles and mumps are negative-sense; rubella is positive-sense. Do not group rubella with the paramyxoviruses.","Atypical lymphocytes in mono are T cells reacting to infected B cells; EBV itself infects the B cells.","Group A strep is bacitracin-sensitive and group B is resistant; both are beta-hemolytic.","Fresh cat feces are not yet infectious; oocysts need 1 to 5 days to sporulate."],"dumps":[{"t":"Toxoplasma life cycle","p":"Draw the Toxoplasma gondii life cycle with the cat, the environment, an intermediate host animal, a person, and a pregnant woman. Label each stage and every way a human can be infected.","l":["Sexual stage in the cat intestine (definitive host)","Oocysts shed in cat feces","Oocysts sporulate in the environment over 1 to 5 days","Intermediate hosts form tissue cysts with bradyzoites","Human infection from undercooked meat with tissue cysts","Human infection from sporulated oocysts in soil, litter, or produce","Tachyzoites spread and cross the placenta in primary maternal infection","Congenital findings: chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcifications"]},{"t":"Influenza drift and shift","p":"Draw an influenza virion and label its genome and surface proteins. Then draw two strains infecting one pig cell and show how a new pandemic strain forms.","l":["Enveloped orthomyxovirus with 8 segments of negative-sense RNA","Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid and drives fusion","Neuraminidase cleaves sialic acid to release virions","Drift: point mutations in HA and NA, yearly epidemics","Shift: two strains coinfect one cell","Segments reassort into a virus with a new HA or NA","Shift occurs only in influenza A and can cause pandemics"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Orthomyxoviruses (Influenza Viruses)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on herpesviruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on Haemophilus species"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-15-F","IM-13","IM-8","IM-15-F10"]},"IM-15-A":{"id":"IM-15-A","summary":"This unit covers the inborn immune defects: X-linked agammaglobulinemia, common variable immunodeficiency, selective IgA deficiency, DiGeorge syndrome, and SCID. The exam gives you an infection pattern and an age of onset, and asks you to name the broken arm of the immune system, the gene, and the first test to order.","notes":[{"h":"Reading the infection pattern","points":["B cell or antibody defect: encapsulated bacteria (S pneumoniae, H influenzae), enteroviruses, Giardia; sinus and lung infections.","T cell defect: viruses, fungi (Candida), Pneumocystis, intracellular organisms; failure to thrive.","Combined T and B defect (SCID): bacteria, viruses, fungi, and Pneumocystis, starting in the first months of life.","Phagocyte defect (chronic granulomatous disease): catalase-positive organisms such as S aureus, Aspergillus, Serratia, Nocardia.","Terminal complement (C5 to C9) defect: recurrent Neisseria infections.","Antibody defects show up after about 6 months, when maternal IgG fades."]},{"h":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Bruton)","points":["Mutation in BTK (Bruton tyrosine kinase) on the X chromosome; almost always boys.","Pre-B cells cannot mature into B cells, so blood B cells (CD19 or CD20) are absent.","All immunoglobulin classes are very low.","Tonsils and lymph nodes are small because they lack germinal centers.","Infections: encapsulated bacteria, enteroviral meningoencephalitis, vaccine polio, Giardia.","Avoid live vaccines."]},{"h":"Common variable immunodeficiency (CVID)","points":["B cells are usually present in normal numbers but fail to become plasma cells.","Low IgG plus low IgA and/or IgM; poor response to vaccines.","Onset is often in the 20s to 40s, so a new adult with recurrent pneumonia fits.","Recurrent sinusitis, pneumonia, bronchiectasis, chronic Giardia diarrhea.","Higher risk of autoimmune disease (cytopenias) and lymphoma."]},{"h":"Selective IgA deficiency","points":["Most common primary immunodeficiency; very low IgA with normal IgG and IgM.","Most people have no symptoms; some get sinus, lung, and Giardia infections.","Linked to celiac disease and other autoimmune diseases.","Anti-IgA antibodies can cause anaphylaxis after transfusion of blood products that contain IgA.","IgA-based tests (such as IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase) are falsely negative; use IgG-based celiac tests."]},{"h":"DiGeorge syndrome","points":["22q11.2 deletion (TBX1 gene); third and fourth pharyngeal pouches fail to develop.","No thymus means few T cells: viral and fungal infections.","Absent or small parathyroids mean hypocalcemia: tetany or seizures in the newborn.","Conotruncal heart defects: truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot, interrupted aortic arch.","Abnormal facies and cleft palate."]},{"h":"Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)","points":["No working T cells, so B cells also get no help; most common cause is X-linked IL2RG (common gamma chain) mutation.","Other causes: adenosine deaminase deficiency (toxic metabolites kill lymphocytes), RAG1 or RAG2 mutation.","Infant with failure to thrive, chronic diarrhea, thrush, Pneumocystis pneumonia, severe viral infections.","Absent thymic shadow on chest x-ray; low TRECs on newborn screening.","Live vaccines (rotavirus, BCG, MMR, varicella) can cause fatal infection.","Nonirradiated blood carries donor T cells that cause graft-versus-host disease; give irradiated products."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital immunodeficiencies at a glance","cols":["Disease","Defect","Key labs","Typical infections and clues"],"rows":[["X-linked agammaglobulinemia","BTK mutation blocks pre-B to B cell maturation","Absent B cells, all immunoglobulins very low","Boy over 6 months; encapsulated bacteria, enteroviruses, Giardia; tiny tonsils"],["Common variable immunodeficiency","B cells fail to become plasma cells","Normal or low B cells; low IgG plus low IgA and/or IgM","Adult onset; sinopulmonary infections, Giardia, autoimmunity, lymphoma"],["Selective IgA deficiency","Failure of IgA-producing plasma cells","Very low IgA, normal IgG and IgM","Often silent; celiac link; anaphylaxis to IgA in blood products"],["DiGeorge syndrome","22q11.2 deletion; 3rd and 4th pouches fail","Low T cells, low calcium, low PTH","Viral and fungal infections, tetany, conotruncal heart defect, cleft palate"],["SCID","IL2RG, ADA, or RAG defect; no functional T cells","Very low T cells, low immunoglobulins, low TRECs","Infant with thrush, Pneumocystis, diarrhea, no thymic shadow"]]},{"title":"Match the infection to the defect and the first test","cols":["Infection pattern","Defective arm","First test"],"rows":[["Encapsulated bacteria, enteroviruses, Giardia","B cells or antibody","Quantitative serum immunoglobulins (IgG, IgA, IgM)"],["Viruses, fungi, Pneumocystis","T cells","Flow cytometry for lymphocyte subsets (CD3, CD4, CD8, CD19)"],["S aureus, Aspergillus, other catalase-positive organisms","Phagocyte oxidative burst","Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) flow cytometry"],["Recurrent Neisseria","Terminal complement C5 to C9","CH50 (total hemolytic complement)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CATCH-22","text":"Cardiac defects, Abnormal facies, Thymic hypoplasia, Cleft palate, Hypocalcemia, from the 22q11.2 deletion in DiGeorge syndrome."},{"kind":"trick","label":"B for Bacteria, T for the rest","text":"Antibody defects mainly let in encapsulated bacteria and enteroviruses. T cell defects let in viruses, fungi, and Pneumocystis. If both groups show up in a baby, think SCID."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Bruton is a baby boy, CVID is a grown-up","text":"XLA shows up in boys after 6 months with no B cells. CVID shows up in adults of either sex with B cells present but no plasma cells."}],"highYield":["Boy over 6 months with recurrent otitis, pneumonia, absent B cells, and no tonsils: X-linked agammaglobulinemia (BTK).","Adult with new recurrent sinusitis, pneumonia, Giardia, low IgG and normal B cell count: CVID.","Anaphylaxis during a first blood transfusion: selective IgA deficiency with anti-IgA antibodies.","Newborn with tetany, truncus arteriosus, and cleft palate: DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2).","Infant with thrush, Pneumocystis pneumonia, chronic diarrhea, and no thymic shadow: SCID.","Recurrent Neisseria meningitis: terminal complement deficiency; screen with CH50.","S aureus and Aspergillus abscesses: phagocyte defect; order a DHR test."],"pitfalls":["XLA has absent B cells; CVID usually has normal B cell numbers. Both have low IgG.","Selective IgA deficiency gives false-negative IgA celiac serology; check total IgA or use IgG-based tests.","DiGeorge and SCID both lack a thymic shadow; DiGeorge adds hypocalcemia, heart defects, and facial changes.","Antibody defects rarely show up before 6 months because maternal IgG still protects the infant."],"dumps":[{"t":"Defect to infection table","p":"Make a table with five rows (B cell, T cell, combined, phagocyte, terminal complement). For each, list the typical organisms and the first test to order.","l":["B cell: encapsulated bacteria, enteroviruses, Giardia","B cell test: quantitative IgG, IgA, IgM","T cell: viruses, fungi, Pneumocystis","T cell test: flow cytometry for lymphocyte subsets","Combined (SCID): bacterial, viral, fungal, and Pneumocystis infections in infancy","Phagocyte: S aureus, Aspergillus; DHR test","Terminal complement: Neisseria; CH50"]},{"t":"B cell development map","p":"Draw a line from stem cell to pre-B cell to B cell to plasma cell. Mark where XLA, CVID, and selective IgA deficiency block it, and write the immunoglobulin pattern for each.","l":["XLA blocks pre-B to mature B cell (BTK)","XLA: no B cells, all immunoglobulins very low","CVID blocks B cell to plasma cell","CVID: B cells present, low IgG with low IgA and/or IgM","IgA deficiency: only IgA plasma cells fail","IgA deficiency: low IgA, normal IgG and IgM","Antibody defects appear after about 6 months"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Congenital and Acquired Immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Section on the pharyngeal pouches"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-1","IM-5","IM-7","IM-12","IM-15-B"]},"IM-15-B":{"id":"IM-15-B","summary":"This unit covers immune deficiency acquired after birth, from medications and from HIV. The exam asks you to link a drug to the infection it unmasks, to trace HIV from entry to AIDS, to read HIV tests, and to predict which opportunistic infection or cancer appears at a given CD4 count.","notes":[{"h":"Drug-induced immunodeficiency","points":["Glucocorticoids: block cytokine gene transcription and cause lymphopenia; risk of Pneumocystis and fungal infection.","Calcineurin inhibitors (cyclosporine, tacrolimus): block calcineurin, so NFAT cannot switch on the IL-2 gene and T cells make no IL-2; viral, fungal, and Pneumocystis infections.","TNF-alpha inhibitors (infliximab, adalimumab, etanercept): granulomas break down; latent tuberculosis reactivates. Screen for latent TB first.","Rituximab (anti-CD20): depletes B cells; hepatitis B reactivation (and PML from JC virus). Screen for hepatitis B first.","Chemotherapy: neutropenia (ANC below 500 cells per mm3 is severe) leads to gram-negative bacteremia (including Pseudomonas) and Candida or Aspergillus."]},{"h":"HIV structure and entry","points":["Enveloped retrovirus (lentivirus) with two copies of positive-sense single-stranded RNA.","gag: p24 capsid (and p17 matrix).","pol: reverse transcriptase (RNA to DNA), integrase (inserts DNA into host genome), protease (cleaves precursor proteins to mature virions).","env: gp160 cut into gp120 (binds CD4 and a coreceptor) and gp41 (fusion).","Coreceptor: CCR5 on macrophages and T cells early; CXCR4 on T cells often later.","CCR5 delta32 homozygotes lack surface CCR5 and resist infection by CCR5-using strains."]},{"h":"Natural history","points":["Spread by sex, blood (shared needles, transfusion), and mother to child (birth, breastfeeding).","Acute retroviral syndrome about 2 to 4 weeks after exposure: fever, sore throat, lymphadenopathy, rash; very high viral load.","Clinical latency: few symptoms for years, but the virus keeps replicating in lymphoid tissue and CD4 counts fall.","AIDS: CD4 below 200 cells per mm3 or any AIDS-defining illness."]},{"h":"Testing","points":["Screen: fourth generation immunoassay detects p24 antigen plus HIV-1 and HIV-2 antibodies, so it turns positive earlier than antibody-only tests.","If positive: HIV-1 and HIV-2 antibody differentiation assay; if that is negative or unclear, HIV-1 RNA (nucleic acid) test.","CD4 count stages immune damage; viral load measures replication and response to therapy.","Infants under 18 months: maternal IgG crosses the placenta, so antibody tests are positive whether or not the baby is infected; use HIV nucleic acid testing."]},{"h":"Noninfectious AIDS complications","points":["Kaposi sarcoma: HHV-8; purple skin, mouth, gut, or lung lesions of spindle cells and slit-like vessels.","Primary CNS lymphoma: EBV-driven B cell lymphoma; usually one ring-enhancing lesion at CD4 below about 50.","HIV-associated nephropathy: collapsing FSGS with heavy proteinuria, mostly in patients of African ancestry.","HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder and distal symmetric sensory neuropathy.","Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome: weeks after starting antiretroviral therapy, recovering T cells attack existing infections, so the patient gets worse."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Opportunistic infections by CD4 count (cells per mm3)","cols":["CD4 count","Infection","Classic clue"],"rows":[["Below 500","Oral thrush (Candida)","White plaques that scrape off"],["Below 500","Oral hairy leukoplakia (EBV)","White lateral tongue plaques that do not scrape off"],["Below 200","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","Dry cough, hypoxia, bilateral interstitial infiltrates"],["Below 100","Toxoplasma encephalitis","Multiple ring-enhancing brain lesions"],["Below 100","Candida esophagitis","Painful swallowing"],["Below 50","CMV retinitis","Retinal hemorrhages and exudates, vision loss"],["Below 50","Mycobacterium avium complex","Fever, weight loss, diarrhea, anemia"]]},{"title":"Immunosuppressive drugs and the infections they unmask","cols":["Drug","Immune effect","Infection to expect"],"rows":[["Glucocorticoids","Blunt cytokine production, lymphopenia","Pneumocystis, fungi"],["Cyclosporine, tacrolimus","Block calcineurin, no IL-2, T cells stay quiet","Pneumocystis, viral (CMV), fungal"],["TNF-alpha inhibitors","Granulomas lose TNF support","Reactivation of latent tuberculosis"],["Rituximab","Depletes CD20 B cells","Hepatitis B reactivation, PML"],["Cytotoxic chemotherapy","Neutropenia","Gram-negative bacteremia, Candida, Aspergillus"]]},{"title":"HIV genes and products","cols":["Gene","Product","Job"],"rows":[["gag","p24 capsid, p17 matrix","Structural core; p24 is detected by fourth generation tests"],["pol","Reverse transcriptase","Copies viral RNA into DNA"],["pol","Integrase","Inserts viral DNA into host DNA"],["pol","Protease","Cleaves precursor proteins so virions mature"],["env","gp120","Binds CD4 and CCR5 or CXCR4"],["env","gp41","Fuses viral envelope with the cell membrane"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"200, 100, 50 ladder","text":"Pneumocystis below 200. Toxoplasma below 100. CMV retinitis and MAC below 50. Each step down the ladder adds rarer, deeper infections."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One lesion or many","text":"Multiple ring-enhancing lesions in AIDS point to Toxoplasma. A single ring-enhancing lesion with EBV DNA in the CSF points to primary CNS lymphoma."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Screen before you suppress","text":"Before a TNF inhibitor, check for latent TB. Before rituximab, check for hepatitis B. The drug wakes up whatever is sleeping."}],"highYield":["Positive HIV test in a 4-month-old of an infected mother proves nothing; order nucleic acid testing.","Fever, rash, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy 3 weeks after exposure with negative antibody test: acute HIV; check RNA or p24.","CCR5 delta32 homozygote resists CCR5-using HIV strains.","Patient on infliximab with fever, cough, and upper lobe cavity: reactivated tuberculosis.","Rising liver enzymes after rituximab: hepatitis B reactivation.","Purple skin plaques in AIDS: Kaposi sarcoma from HHV-8.","Patient worsens weeks after starting antiretroviral therapy: immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome.","Nephrotic proteinuria in HIV: collapsing FSGS."],"pitfalls":["Viral load tracks replication; CD4 count tracks immune damage. Do not use one for the job of the other.","Thrush scrapes off; oral hairy leukoplakia does not.","Toxoplasma lesions are usually multiple; CNS lymphoma is usually solitary.","AIDS can be diagnosed with a CD4 above 200 if an AIDS-defining illness is present."],"dumps":[{"t":"HIV life cycle","p":"Draw an HIV virion and a CD4 T cell. Trace the virus from binding to budding and label the step each viral protein performs.","l":["gp120 binds CD4 and CCR5 or CXCR4","gp41 mediates fusion","Reverse transcriptase makes DNA from RNA","Integrase inserts DNA into host genome","Host makes viral RNA and proteins","Protease cleaves precursors so virions mature","p24 forms the capsid","CCR5 delta32 homozygotes block entry of R5 strains"]},{"t":"CD4 timeline","p":"Draw a graph of CD4 count and viral load over 10 years of untreated HIV. Mark acute infection, latency, and AIDS, and write which infections appear at 500, 200, 100, and 50.","l":["Acute phase: viral load spikes, CD4 dips, flu-like illness","Latency: steady CD4 decline, ongoing replication in lymph nodes","AIDS: CD4 below 200 or AIDS-defining illness","Below 500: thrush, oral hairy leukoplakia","Below 200: Pneumocystis","Below 100: Toxoplasma","Below 50: CMV retinitis, MAC, CNS lymphoma"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Congenital and Acquired Immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on AIDS and lentiviruses"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-5","IM-13","IM-15-A","IM-15-B2","CV-18-J2","PU-14-H9"]},"IM-15-C":{"id":"IM-15-C","summary":"This unit covers the four types of hypersensitivity and the diseases built on each: anaphylaxis, antibody attacks on cells and tissues, immune complex diseases such as lupus, and T cell injury such as granulomas and transplant rejection. The exam asks you to classify a disease by mechanism, then predict its labs, biopsy, and complications.","notes":[{"h":"Type I: immediate (IgE)","points":["First exposure: Th2 cells release IL-4, B cells switch to IgE, IgE coats mast cells through FcepsilonRI.","Re-exposure: allergen cross-links bound IgE and mast cells degranulate within minutes (histamine, tryptase).","Late phase hours later: leukotrienes, prostaglandins, and eosinophil influx.","Anaphylaxis triggers: peanut and other foods, insect stings, penicillin, latex.","Signs: urticaria, angioedema, wheeze from bronchospasm, hypotension from vasodilation and leaky vessels.","Serum tryptase peaks about 1 to 2 hours after onset and confirms mast cell activation; a normal value does not rule it out."]},{"h":"Type II: antibody against cells or matrix","points":["Warm AIHA: IgG binds red cells at body temperature; splenic macrophages remove membrane, making spherocytes. Causes: idiopathic, SLE, CLL, drugs.","Cold agglutinin disease: IgM binds red cells in cool extremities and fixes complement; acrocyanosis; Mycoplasma pneumoniae, EBV.","Both have a positive direct Coombs test (IgG in warm, C3 in cold).","Goodpasture: antibodies to the alpha3 chain of type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes; hemoptysis, rapidly progressive crescentic GN, linear IgG.","Myasthenia gravis: antibodies to the acetylcholine receptor (or MuSK) block and remove receptors; the muscle cell lives but cannot respond.","Myasthenia: fatigable ptosis, diplopia, proximal weakness; thymic hyperplasia common, thymoma in some."]},{"h":"Type III: immune complexes","points":["Antigen and antibody form complexes that lodge in vessels, glomeruli, and joints, fix complement, and draw in neutrophils.","SLE: apoptotic nuclear debris is cleared poorly, so nuclear antigens drive autoantibodies and immune complexes.","SLE labs: ANA (sensitive), anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith (specific), anti-histone (drug-induced), low C3 and C4 in flares.","SLE features: young woman with malar rash, photosensitivity, nonerosive arthritis, serositis, cytopenias, nephritis with wire loops, Libman-Sacks endocarditis.","Antiphospholipid syndrome: venous and arterial clots and recurrent pregnancy loss.","Polyarteritis nodosa: medium vessels, hepatitis B link, ANCA-negative, spares the lung; fibrinoid necrosis at different stages, renal and mesenteric microaneurysms.","PSGN: 1 to 4 weeks after group A strep throat or skin infection; cola urine, edema, hypertension, low C3, high ASO or anti-DNase B; subepithelial humps, granular immunofluorescence.","RA: HLA-DR4, antibodies to citrullinated peptides; synovial pannus erodes cartilage and bone.","RA: symmetric MCP, PIP, and wrist joints, DIP spared; rheumatoid factor, anti-CCP, nodules; Felty syndrome is RA plus splenomegaly plus neutropenia.","Serum sickness: about 1 to 2 weeks after foreign protein; fever, urticaria, arthralgia, proteinuria, low complement. Arthus reaction is the local version at an injection site."]},{"h":"Type IV: T cell mediated (delayed)","points":["Th1 cells make IFN-gamma that activates macrophages into epithelioid cells and giant cells; TNF keeps the granuloma intact.","Caseating granulomas with central necrosis: tuberculosis. Noncaseating: sarcoidosis.","Tuberculin skin test: read induration at 48 to 72 hours; a positive test means prior sensitization to mycobacterial antigens.","Sarcoidosis: bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, high ACE, hypercalcemia (macrophages make calcitriol), uveitis, erythema nodosum.","Lofgren syndrome: erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar adenopathy, and arthritis; usually resolves."]},{"h":"Transplant rejection and GVHD","points":["Hyperacute: preformed antibodies (ABO, HLA) in minutes; graft clots and turns dusky.","Acute: weeks to months; host T cells against donor MHC, or donor-specific antibodies.","Chronic: months to years; graft arteriosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis, bronchiolitis obliterans in lung grafts.","Graft-versus-host disease: donor T cells attack host skin (rash), liver (jaundice), and gut (diarrhea)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"The four hypersensitivity types","cols":["Type","Mediator","Timing","Examples"],"rows":[["I immediate","IgE on mast cells, histamine, leukotrienes","Minutes, late phase in hours","Anaphylaxis, allergic asthma, penicillin urticaria"],["II antibody to cell or matrix","IgG or IgM, complement, phagocytes, or receptor blockade","Hours to days","AIHA, Goodpasture, myasthenia gravis, acute rheumatic fever, hyperacute rejection"],["III immune complex","Antigen-antibody complexes, complement, neutrophils","Days to weeks","SLE, PAN, PSGN, RA, serum sickness, Arthus"],["IV T cell","Th1 macrophage activation or CD8 killing","48 to 72 hours or longer","TB granulomas, TB skin test, contact dermatitis, acute cellular rejection"]]},{"title":"Warm vs cold autoimmune hemolytic anemia","cols":["Feature","Warm AIHA","Cold agglutinin disease"],"rows":[["Antibody","IgG","IgM"],["Temperature of binding","Body temperature","Cool extremities"],["Hemolysis","Extravascular in spleen; spherocytes","Complement-mediated; red cell clumps on smear"],["Coombs test","Positive for IgG","Positive for C3"],["Causes","Idiopathic, SLE, CLL, methyldopa, penicillin","Mycoplasma pneumoniae, EBV, lymphoma"],["Extra clue","Splenomegaly","Acrocyanosis in the cold"]]},{"title":"SLE antibodies","cols":["Test","What it tells you"],"rows":[["ANA","Positive in nearly all SLE; good screen, poor specificity"],["Anti-dsDNA","Specific; level tracks lupus nephritis activity"],["Anti-Smith","Most specific; does not track activity"],["Anti-histone","Drug-induced lupus (hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid)"],["C3 and C4","Fall during flares as complexes consume complement"],["Antiphospholipid antibodies","Clots, pregnancy loss, prolonged PTT, false-positive syphilis test"]]},{"title":"Transplant rejection and GVHD","cols":["Type","Timing","Mechanism","Findings"],"rows":[["Hyperacute","Minutes","Preformed anti-donor antibody, complement","Graft thrombosis and ischemia"],["Acute","Weeks to months","Host T cells against donor MHC or new donor-specific antibody","Lymphocyte infiltrate, vasculitis; responds to immunosuppression"],["Chronic","Months to years","Ongoing T cell and antibody injury","Graft arteriosclerosis, fibrosis, bronchiolitis obliterans"],["GVHD","Weeks after graft","Donor T cells attack host tissue","Rash, jaundice, diarrhea"]]},{"title":"Drug reactions by hypersensitivity type","cols":["Drug example","Reaction","Type"],"rows":[["Penicillin","Anaphylaxis, urticaria","I"],["Penicillin, methyldopa","Coombs-positive hemolytic anemia","II"],["Chimeric monoclonal antibodies, antivenom","Serum sickness","III"],["Topical neomycin","Contact dermatitis","IV"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ACID","text":"Anaphylactic and Atopic (type I), Cytotoxic antibody (type II), Immune complex (type III), Delayed T cell (type IV)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Linear vs lumpy","text":"Linear IgG along the basement membrane means antibody bound to the membrane itself (Goodpasture). Granular, lumpy deposits mean immune complexes that landed there (PSGN, lupus)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the joints","text":"RA hits MCP, PIP, and wrists and spares the DIP joints. Osteoarthritis loves the DIP joints. Lupus arthritis looks like RA but does not erode bone."}],"highYield":["Hives, wheeze, and hypotension minutes after a bee sting with high tryptase: type I anaphylaxis.","Young man with hemoptysis, crescentic GN, linear IgG: Goodpasture (alpha3 chain of type IV collagen).","Ptosis that worsens by evening with an anterior mediastinal mass: myasthenia gravis with thymoma.","Child with cola urine 2 weeks after strep throat, low C3: PSGN.","Anti-dsDNA rising with low C3 and C4: active lupus nephritis.","Hepatitis B, ANCA-negative, renal and mesenteric microaneurysms, normal lungs: polyarteritis nodosa.","Hilar adenopathy, erythema nodosum, high calcium, noncaseating granulomas: sarcoidosis.","Fever, rash, arthralgia, and low complement 10 days after antithymocyte globulin: serum sickness."],"pitfalls":["Myasthenia gravis is type II even though cells are not killed; antibody blocks receptor function.","Hyperacute rejection is antibody-mediated (type II); acute cellular rejection is T cell mediated (type IV).","PAN spares the lungs; granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis do not.","TB skin test induration is measured; redness alone is not a positive result.","Rheumatoid factor is common in other diseases; anti-CCP is the more specific RA test."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypersensitivity table","p":"Make a four-row table for types I to IV. For each write the mediator, the timing, two diseases, and one drug example.","l":["Type I: IgE, mast cells, minutes; anaphylaxis; penicillin","Type II: antibody to cell or matrix; AIHA, Goodpasture, MG","Type II drug: penicillin or methyldopa hemolysis","Type III: immune complexes, complement; SLE, PSGN, PAN, RA","Type III drug: serum sickness from chimeric antibodies","Type IV: T cells, 48 to 72 hours; TB granuloma, rejection","Type IV drug: neomycin contact dermatitis"]},{"t":"Lupus from cause to organ","p":"Draw the path from poor clearance of apoptotic cells to organ damage in SLE. Label the antibodies, the complement change, and four organs hit.","l":["Apoptotic nuclear material cleared poorly","Autoantibodies: ANA, anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith","Immune complexes deposit and fix complement","Low C3 and C4 in flares","Kidney: lupus nephritis with wire loops","Skin: malar rash, photosensitivity","Heart: Libman-Sacks endocarditis, serositis","Blood: cytopenias; antiphospholipid clots"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on inflammatory rheumatic diseases"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-5","IM-6","IM-7","IM-13","UR-12-A1","PU-14-A4","PU-14-H9","IM-15-D"]},"IM-15-D":{"id":"IM-15-D","summary":"This unit covers systemic sclerosis, acute rheumatic fever, Sjogren syndrome, neonatal lupus from anti-Ro antibodies, and mixed connective tissue disease. The exam pairs each with its autoantibody or trigger and asks you to predict the organs it damages and its long-term complications.","notes":[{"h":"Systemic sclerosis: mechanism","points":["Starts with injury to small vessel endothelium and autoimmune activation.","T cells and macrophages release TGF-beta, which drives fibroblasts to lay down excess collagen.","Result: thick, tight skin, narrowed small arteries, and fibrosis of internal organs.","Raynaud phenomenon is often the first sign.","Esophageal smooth muscle is replaced by fibrosis: dysmotility, reflux, Barrett risk.","Interstitial lung disease is a leading cause of death; pulmonary hypertension also occurs."]},{"h":"Diffuse vs limited (CREST)","points":["Diffuse: skin thickening reaches the trunk and upper arms; early organ disease; anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I).","Diffuse disease carries the risk of scleroderma renal crisis (sudden severe hypertension and kidney failure).","Limited: skin of fingers, forearms, and face; slower course; anticentromere antibody.","Limited disease shows CREST and late pulmonary arterial hypertension."]},{"h":"Acute rheumatic fever","points":["Follows untreated group A strep pharyngitis by about 2 to 4 weeks; skin infection does not cause it.","Molecular mimicry: antibodies against streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac proteins (type II hypersensitivity).","Pancarditis: endocardium, myocardium, and pericardium are all inflamed.","Aschoff bodies: foci of inflammation with fibrinoid change, lymphocytes, and Anitschkow cells (caterpillar-shaped nuclei).","Diagnosis: evidence of recent strep (ASO, anti-DNase B, culture) plus two major Jones criteria, or one major and two minor.","Years of repeated attacks scar the mitral valve, causing mitral stenosis with fused commissures."]},{"h":"Sjogren syndrome","points":["Lymphocytes infiltrate and destroy salivary and lacrimal glands.","Dry mouth (xerostomia) with dental caries and dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca); parotid swelling.","Antibodies: anti-Ro (SSA) and anti-La (SSB); rheumatoid factor is often positive.","Often occurs with RA (secondary Sjogren).","Higher risk of marginal zone (MALT) B cell lymphoma; new or persistent parotid enlargement is a warning sign."]},{"h":"Neonatal lupus and MCTD","points":["Maternal anti-Ro (SSA) IgG crosses the placenta and injures the fetal AV node.","Result: congenital complete heart block with fetal bradycardia; it is permanent.","Neonatal rash and cytopenias fade over months as maternal IgG is cleared.","The mother may have Sjogren syndrome, SLE, or no symptoms at all.","Mixed connective tissue disease: overlap of SLE, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis features.","MCTD marker: high-titer anti-U1 RNP; Raynaud and puffy hands are common."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Autoimmune diseases in this unit","cols":["Disease","Antibody or trigger","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Diffuse systemic sclerosis","Anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I)","Endothelial injury, TGF-beta fibrosis","Truncal skin thickening, early organ disease","Interstitial lung disease, renal crisis"],["Limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)","Anticentromere","Same, slower and confined to distal skin","Calcinosis, Raynaud, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, telangiectasia","Pulmonary arterial hypertension"],["Acute rheumatic fever","Group A strep pharyngitis; ASO, anti-DNase B","M protein mimicry (type II)","Jones criteria; Aschoff bodies","Mitral stenosis"],["Sjogren syndrome","Anti-Ro (SSA), anti-La (SSB)","Lymphocytic gland destruction","Dry eyes, dry mouth, parotid swelling","Marginal zone lymphoma; neonatal heart block in offspring"],["Mixed connective tissue disease","High-titer anti-U1 RNP","Overlap autoimmunity","Raynaud, puffy hands, arthritis, myositis","Pulmonary hypertension, lung disease"]]},{"title":"Jones major criteria","cols":["Criterion","What it looks like"],"rows":[["Migratory polyarthritis","Large joints, one after another"],["Carditis","New murmur, pericardial rub, heart failure"],["Subcutaneous nodules","Painless nodules over bony surfaces and tendons"],["Erythema marginatum","Pink rings with clear centers on the trunk"],["Sydenham chorea","Rapid, purposeless involuntary movements"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"J (heart) NES","text":"Joints (migratory polyarthritis), heart (carditis), Nodules, Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea. These are the Jones major criteria."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CREST","text":"Calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasia: limited systemic sclerosis with anticentromere antibody."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ro crosses to the heart","text":"Anti-Ro is IgG, so it crosses the placenta. Rash and low counts fade as the IgG is cleared, but the scarred AV node stays blocked."}],"highYield":["Anti-Scl-70 with truncal skin thickening and lung fibrosis: diffuse systemic sclerosis.","Anticentromere with telangiectasia and later pulmonary hypertension: CREST.","Sudden severe hypertension and rising creatinine in diffuse scleroderma: renal crisis.","Child with migratory arthritis and new murmur 3 weeks after strep throat: acute rheumatic fever.","Anitschkow cells in an Aschoff body: rheumatic carditis.","Mitral stenosis decades after childhood rheumatic fever.","Newborn with complete heart block and a mother with dry eyes: anti-Ro (SSA).","Raynaud, puffy hands, myositis, and high-titer anti-U1 RNP: MCTD."],"pitfalls":["Strep skin infection can cause PSGN but not rheumatic fever; rheumatic fever needs pharyngitis.","Rheumatic fever is type II (antibody mimicry); PSGN is type III (immune complexes).","Anti-Scl-70 means diffuse disease; anticentromere means limited disease.","In neonatal lupus, heart block is permanent while rash and cytopenias resolve."],"dumps":[{"t":"Rheumatic fever timeline","p":"Draw a timeline from group A strep pharyngitis to chronic rheumatic heart disease. Label the delay, the mechanism, the Jones criteria, and the valve lesion.","l":["Untreated group A strep pharyngitis","About 2 to 4 weeks delay","M protein mimicry, type II hypersensitivity","Pancarditis with Aschoff bodies and Anitschkow cells","Jones criteria plus evidence of strep","Repeated attacks scar the mitral valve","Mitral stenosis years later"]},{"t":"Connective tissue antibody table","p":"Make a table of diffuse scleroderma, CREST, Sjogren syndrome, neonatal lupus, and MCTD. Write the antibody and the main organ risk for each.","l":["Diffuse: anti-Scl-70; lung fibrosis, renal crisis","CREST: anticentromere; pulmonary hypertension","Sjogren: anti-Ro and anti-La; dry glands, lymphoma","Neonatal lupus: maternal anti-Ro; congenital heart block","MCTD: anti-U1 RNP; overlap features","Systemic sclerosis fibrosis is driven by TGF-beta"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on inflammatory rheumatic diseases"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-15-C","IM-15-D1","IM-15-D2","CV-18-D5","CV-18-I8","IM-15-F"]},"IM-15-E":{"id":"IM-15-E","summary":"This unit covers amyloidosis, where misfolded proteins pile up outside cells and stiffen organs. The exam asks you to recognize amyloid on a stain, to tell primary (AL) from secondary (AA) amyloid by the protein and the setting, and to match the other amyloid proteins to their diseases.","notes":[{"h":"What amyloid is","points":["Proteins misfold into beta-pleated sheets that stack into rigid fibrils.","Fibrils are deposited outside cells, crowding tissue and stiffening organs.","H&E: pink, glassy, featureless material.","Congo red stain: apple-green birefringence under polarized light.","Biopsy: abdominal fat pad aspirate is the easy first site; an affected organ (kidney, heart) gives the highest yield."]},{"h":"Primary (AL) amyloidosis","points":["A monoclonal plasma cell clone makes excess immunoglobulin light chains (lambda more often than kappa).","A plasma cell dyscrasia; some patients also have multiple myeloma.","Find the clone with serum and urine protein electrophoresis, immunofixation, and serum free light chains.","Heart: restrictive cardiomyopathy with thick walls but low-voltage ECG.","Kidney: nephrotic syndrome.","Tongue and skin: macroglossia, periorbital purpura from fragile vessels.","Nerves: carpal tunnel syndrome and peripheral or autonomic neuropathy."]},{"h":"Secondary (AA) amyloidosis","points":["Serum amyloid A is an acute-phase protein the liver makes under IL-1, IL-6, and TNF.","Years of high SAA lead to AA deposits.","Settings: rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, chronic infections (tuberculosis, osteomyelitis, bronchiectasis).","Familial Mediterranean fever: MEFV (pyrin) mutation, recurrent fever and serositis, AA amyloid if untreated.","Kidney is hit most: proteinuria, nephrotic syndrome, kidney failure."]},{"h":"Other amyloid proteins","points":["Beta2-microglobulin: long-term dialysis; carpal tunnel and joint deposits.","Transthyretin, wild type: older adults, mainly the heart (age-related cardiac amyloid).","Transthyretin, mutant: hereditary cardiomyopathy and neuropathy.","A-beta (from amyloid precursor protein): Alzheimer plaques and vessel walls.","Calcitonin: amyloid in the stroma of medullary thyroid carcinoma.","Amylin (islet amyloid polypeptide): pancreatic islets in type 2 diabetes."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Amyloid proteins and their settings","cols":["Amyloid type","Precursor protein","Setting","Main target"],"rows":[["AL (primary)","Immunoglobulin light chain","Plasma cell dyscrasia, multiple myeloma","Heart, kidney, tongue, nerves, skin"],["AA (secondary)","Serum amyloid A","RA, IBD, chronic infection, familial Mediterranean fever","Kidney, liver, spleen"],["Dialysis-related","Beta2-microglobulin","Long-term dialysis","Joints, carpal tunnel"],["ATTR wild type","Normal transthyretin","Older adults","Heart"],["ATTR hereditary","Mutant transthyretin","Inherited","Heart, peripheral nerves"],["A-beta","Amyloid precursor protein","Alzheimer disease","Brain plaques, cerebral vessels"],["Calcitonin-derived","Calcitonin","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Tumor stroma"],["Islet amyloid","Amylin","Type 2 diabetes","Pancreatic islets"]]},{"title":"AL vs AA amyloidosis","cols":["Feature","AL (primary)","AA (secondary)"],"rows":[["Protein","Light chain","Serum amyloid A"],["Cause","Monoclonal plasma cells","Chronic inflammation"],["Key test","Protein electrophoresis, free light chains","Look for the inflammatory disease"],["Heart","Common: restrictive cardiomyopathy","Uncommon"],["Kidney","Nephrotic syndrome","Nephrotic syndrome, the main organ"],["Special clues","Macroglossia, periorbital purpura, carpal tunnel","RA, IBD, TB, familial Mediterranean fever"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"AL is Light chain, AA is Acute phase","text":"The second letter tells you the protein: AL comes from Light chains of a plasma cell clone. AA comes from serum Amyloid A, an acute-phase protein of chronic inflammation."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Thick heart, small voltage","text":"A thick ventricular wall on echo with low-voltage QRS on ECG means protein deposits are thickening the wall. Think amyloid."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Green under the lens","text":"Congo red alone looks red. The apple-green color appears only under polarized light, and that is the finding that proves amyloid."}],"highYield":["Apple-green birefringence with Congo red under polarized light: amyloid.","Macroglossia, periorbital purpura, and nephrotic syndrome with a monoclonal light chain: AL amyloidosis.","Long-standing RA or Crohn disease with new nephrotic syndrome: AA amyloidosis.","Recurrent fever and serositis in a patient of Mediterranean ancestry, then proteinuria: familial Mediterranean fever with AA amyloid.","Carpal tunnel syndrome after years of dialysis: beta2-microglobulin amyloid.","Older man with heart failure, thick walls, low voltage, and no light chain: wild-type transthyretin.","Amyloid in the stroma of a thyroid tumor: medullary carcinoma (calcitonin)."],"pitfalls":["Amyloid is extracellular; intracellular inclusions (such as tau tangles) are a different finding.","AL amyloid can occur without overt myeloma; a monoclonal light chain is enough.","AA amyloid rarely hits the heart; AL and transthyretin amyloid are the cardiac ones.","Fat pad biopsy can miss amyloid; a negative result does not rule it out."],"dumps":[{"t":"Amyloid protein match","p":"List the eight amyloid types in this unit. Beside each, write the precursor protein and the disease or setting.","l":["AL: light chain, plasma cell dyscrasia","AA: serum amyloid A, chronic inflammation","Beta2-microglobulin: long-term dialysis","Transthyretin wild type: older adult heart","Transthyretin mutant: hereditary heart and nerve disease","A-beta: Alzheimer disease","Calcitonin: medullary thyroid carcinoma","Amylin: type 2 diabetes islets"]},{"t":"AL amyloid body map","p":"Draw a body outline and mark every organ AL amyloid damages. Write the clinical finding at each site and how you would confirm the diagnosis.","l":["Heart: restrictive cardiomyopathy, low-voltage ECG","Kidney: nephrotic syndrome","Tongue: macroglossia","Eyes: periorbital purpura","Wrist: carpal tunnel syndrome","Nerves: peripheral and autonomic neuropathy","Confirm with Congo red stain of fat pad or organ biopsy","Find the clone with electrophoresis and free light chains"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart"}],"videos":[],"related":["HE-9-D3","UR-12-A3","NE-20-B1","NE-20-I2","IM-15-E1","IM-15-E2"]},"IM-15-F":{"id":"IM-15-F","summary":"This unit covers the classic childhood and systemic infections: the rash illnesses, Hib, influenza, mumps, EBV and CMV mononucleosis, and toxoplasmosis. The exam gives you a rash pattern, an age, and a timeline, and asks you to name the cause and predict the complication, especially in pregnancy and in weak immune systems.","notes":[{"h":"Rash illnesses","points":["Measles: fever with cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, then Koplik spots on the buccal mucosa, then a rash spreading from the head down.","Measles complications: pneumonia (main cause of death), encephalitis, subacute sclerosing panencephalitis years later; vitamin A deficiency worsens outcomes.","Rubella (enveloped positive-sense RNA virus): mild fever, pink rash spreading from face to trunk, postauricular and occipital lymph nodes, arthralgia in adult women.","Congenital rubella after infection in the first trimester: cataracts, sensorineural deafness, PDA, blueberry muffin rash.","Erythema infectiosum (parvovirus B19): slapped cheek, then lacy reticular rash on the limbs; adults get symmetric hand and wrist arthropathy.","Parvovirus B19 infects red cell precursors: aplastic crisis in sickle cell disease or spherocytosis, hydrops fetalis in pregnancy.","Roseola (HHV-6, less often HHV-7): child under 2 with 3 to 5 days of high fever, often 39 to 40 °C (102.2 to 104 °F), then a truncal rash as the fever breaks; febrile seizures.","Scarlet fever (group A strep toxin): sandpaper rash, circumoral pallor, strawberry tongue, Pastia lines, peeling of hands and feet about a week later."]},{"h":"Haemophilus influenzae","points":["Type b has a polyribosylribitol phosphate capsule; disease is now mostly in unvaccinated young children.","Epiglottitis: high fever, drooling, muffled voice, inspiratory stridor, child sitting forward.","Other invasive Hib disease: meningitis, bacteremia, pneumonia, septic arthritis.","Nontypeable strains (no capsule) cause mucosal infections: otitis media, sinusitis, conjunctivitis."]},{"h":"Influenza","points":["Abrupt fever, myalgia, headache, dry cough, and malaise.","Primary viral pneumonia: rapid, diffuse infiltrates and hypoxia.","Secondary bacterial pneumonia: patient improves, then worsens; S aureus or S pneumoniae.","Reye syndrome: aspirin given to a child with influenza or varicella causes liver failure (microvesicular fat) and encephalopathy."]},{"h":"Mononucleosis and EBV","points":["EBV infects B cells through CD21; reactive CD8 T cells appear in blood as atypical lymphocytes.","Adolescent with fever, exudative pharyngitis, posterior cervical nodes, and splenomegaly; heterophile (Monospot) positive.","Amoxicillin or ampicillin given for presumed strep throat causes a diffuse rash.","Complications: splenic rupture (avoid contact sports), hepatitis, cold autoimmune hemolytic anemia.","Cancers: Burkitt lymphoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, lymphomas in transplant and AIDS patients.","CMV: heterophile-negative mono, owl's eye intranuclear inclusions in enlarged cells.","Congenital CMV: periventricular calcifications and sensorineural hearing loss.","CMV in transplant and AIDS patients: retinitis, colitis, pneumonitis."]},{"h":"Mumps and toxoplasmosis","points":["Mumps: painful parotid swelling; orchitis in postpubertal males, aseptic meningitis, pancreatitis.","Serum amylase rises with parotitis alone; lipase points to pancreatitis.","Toxoplasma gondii: cat feces (oocysts) or undercooked meat (tissue cysts).","Healthy host: often silent or mono-like lymphadenopathy.","Congenital: chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, diffuse intracranial calcifications.","AIDS with CD4 below 100: multiple ring-enhancing brain lesions."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Childhood rash illnesses by pattern and timing","cols":["Disease","Cause","Fever and rash timing","Rash pattern","Key extra clue"],"rows":[["Measles","Measles virus (paramyxovirus)","Rash appears while still febrile, after 3 to 4 days of prodrome","Starts at head and hairline, spreads down, becomes confluent","Cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, Koplik spots"],["Rubella","Rubella virus","Mild or no fever with rash","Pink, face to trunk, fades in about 3 days","Postauricular and occipital nodes"],["Erythema infectiosum","Parvovirus B19","Mild prodrome; child well when rash appears","Slapped cheek, then lacy rash on limbs","Adult arthropathy, aplastic crisis"],["Roseola","HHV-6 (HHV-7)","High fever for 3 to 5 days, rash after fever ends","Pink macules on trunk, then neck and limbs","Under 2 years; febrile seizures"],["Scarlet fever","Group A strep exotoxin","Fever and sore throat, rash within 1 to 2 days","Sandpaper rash from neck and trunk outward, Pastia lines","Strawberry tongue, circumoral pallor, later peeling"]]},{"title":"Systemic infections and their complications","cols":["Condition","Cause","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Erythema infectiosum","Parvovirus B19","Slapped cheek, lacy rash","Aplastic crisis, hydrops fetalis"],["Hib disease","H influenzae type b","Epiglottitis, meningitis in unvaccinated child","Airway obstruction, hearing loss after meningitis"],["Influenza","Influenza A or B","Abrupt fever, myalgia, cough","Viral pneumonia, S aureus or pneumococcal pneumonia, Reye syndrome"],["Measles","Measles virus","3 Cs, Koplik spots, head-to-toe rash","Pneumonia, encephalitis, SSPE"],["Mononucleosis","EBV (CMV if heterophile negative)","Pharyngitis, posterior nodes, splenomegaly, atypical lymphocytes","Splenic rupture, hepatitis, AIHA, lymphomas"],["Mumps","Mumps virus (paramyxovirus)","Parotitis","Orchitis, aseptic meningitis, pancreatitis"],["Roseola","HHV-6","High fever then rash","Febrile seizures"],["Rubella","Rubella virus","Mild rash, postauricular nodes","Congenital cataracts, deafness, PDA"],["Scarlet fever","Group A strep","Sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue","Rheumatic fever, PSGN"],["Toxoplasmosis","Toxoplasma gondii","Mono-like nodes in healthy hosts","Congenital triad, ring-enhancing lesions in AIDS"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 3 Cs of measles","text":"Cough, Coryza, Conjunctivitis come first. Koplik spots follow, then the rash moves from head to toe."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Roseola rash follows the fever","text":"In roseola the rash shows up only after the fever breaks. In measles and scarlet fever the child still has a fever when the rash appears."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Where the calcium lands","text":"Congenital CMV leaves calcium around the ventricles (periventricular). Congenital toxoplasmosis scatters it through the brain (diffuse)."}],"highYield":["Child with sickle cell disease, sudden pallor, and very low reticulocyte count: parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis.","Unvaccinated toddler with drooling, stridor, and high fever: Hib epiglottitis.","Child given aspirin for flu develops vomiting, confusion, and liver failure: Reye syndrome.","Teen with SSPE years after measles: persistent defective measles virus in the brain.","Teen with sore throat who gets a diffuse rash after amoxicillin: EBV mononucleosis.","Newborn with cataracts, deafness, and PDA: congenital rubella.","Postpubertal male with parotid swelling then testicular pain: mumps orchitis.","Transplant patient with owl's eye inclusions in colon biopsy: CMV colitis."],"pitfalls":["Heterophile-negative mono is often CMV; young children with EBV can also be heterophile-negative.","Scarlet fever can lead to rheumatic fever and PSGN; strep skin infection leads only to PSGN.","Measles spreads head to toe and the child looks sick; rubella spreads the same way but the child looks well.","In fifth disease, the child is no longer contagious once the rash appears.","High amylase in mumps does not prove pancreatitis; parotitis raises amylase too."],"dumps":[{"t":"Exanthem timeline chart","p":"Draw a fever curve for measles, rubella, erythema infectiosum, roseola, and scarlet fever. Mark when the rash appears on each and where it starts.","l":["Measles: prodrome of 3 Cs and Koplik spots, rash while febrile, head to toe","Rubella: low fever, face to trunk, postauricular nodes","Fifth disease: slapped cheek, then lacy rash, child well","Roseola: 3 to 5 days high fever, rash after fever breaks","Scarlet fever: sore throat and fever, sandpaper rash within 1 to 2 days","Scarlet fever: strawberry tongue and later peeling"]},{"t":"Infections in pregnancy and immunosuppression","p":"Make a table of parvovirus B19, rubella, CMV, and Toxoplasma. For each, write the fetal outcome and the problem it causes in AIDS or transplant patients.","l":["Parvovirus B19: hydrops fetalis; chronic anemia in immunosuppression","Rubella: cataracts, deafness, PDA after infection in the first trimester","CMV fetal: periventricular calcifications, hearing loss","CMV immunosuppressed: retinitis, colitis, pneumonitis","Toxoplasma fetal: chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, diffuse calcifications","Toxoplasma in AIDS: multiple ring-enhancing lesions at CD4 below 100"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on herpesviruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on paramyxoviruses and rubella virus"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on parvoviruses"}],"videos":[],"related":["IM-14","IM-15-B2","IM-15-D2","NE-20-G13","NE-20-G8","PU-14-C2","HE-9-D2","UR-12-A1"]},"IN-1":{"id":"IN-1","summary":"The microscopic build of skin: the epidermal layers and their cells, the junctions that hold keratinocytes together and to the basement membrane, the dermis, and the glands, hair, and nails. Exam questions ask you to name the layer or protein that fails and predict where a blister splits or which disease results.","notes":[{"h":"Epidermal layers, deep to surface","points":["Stratum basale: one row of dividing stem cells on the basement membrane; they make keratins 5 and 14.","Stratum spinosum: cells joined by many desmosomes (the 'spines'); keratins switch to 1 and 10.","Stratum granulosum: keratohyalin granules (profilaggrin) and lamellar bodies that release barrier lipids.","Stratum lucidum: thin clear layer found only in thick skin of the palms and soles.","Stratum corneum: flat dead corneocytes with no nuclei, wrapped in a tough cornified envelope."]},{"h":"Non-keratinocyte cells of the epidermis","points":["Melanocytes: neural crest origin, sit in the stratum basale, pass melanin in melanosomes to nearby keratinocytes through their dendrites.","Skin color depends on melanosome size, number, and activity; melanocyte numbers are about the same across skin colors.","Langerhans cells: bone marrow origin, mostly in the stratum spinosum, contain tennis-racket Birbeck granules, stain for CD1a and S-100, and carry antigen to lymph nodes.","Merkel cells: in the stratum basale next to nerve endings, act as slowly adapting light touch receptors, plentiful in fingertips."]},{"h":"Cell junctions and the dermal-epidermal junction","points":["Desmosome: cell to cell; desmogleins 1 and 3 (cadherins) link to keratin through desmoplakin.","Adherens junction: cell to cell; E-cadherin links to actin through catenins.","Hemidesmosome: cell to basement membrane; BP180 (collagen XVII), BP230, and integrin alpha6 beta4.","Lamina lucida: laminin 332 and collagen XVII anchoring filaments; failure gives junctional epidermolysis bullosa.","Lamina densa: mainly type IV collagen.","Below the lamina densa: type VII collagen anchoring fibrils tie it to the dermis; failure gives dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa or epidermolysis bullosa acquisita (autoantibodies).","Keratin 5 or 14 mutation weakens basal cells themselves, so epidermolysis bullosa simplex splits inside the basal layer."]},{"h":"Dermis and hypodermis","points":["Papillary dermis: loose connective tissue in the dermal papillae, capillary loops, and Meissner corpuscles (light touch) in hairless skin.","Reticular dermis: dense irregular connective tissue (mostly type I collagen plus elastic fibers), larger vessels, follicles, and glands.","Hypodermis (subcutis): fat lobules and larger vessels; Pacinian corpuscles (vibration, deep pressure) lie in the deep dermis and subcutis.","Free nerve endings (pain, temperature, itch) run in the dermis and reach into the epidermis."]},{"h":"Glands, hair, and nails","points":["Eccrine glands: all over the body, most dense on palms, soles, and forehead; watery sweat; sympathetic cholinergic fibers on muscarinic receptors; overactivity is hyperhidrosis.","Apocrine glands: axilla, anogenital skin, and areola; open into hair follicles; active after puberty; odor comes from bacterial breakdown of the secretion.","Sebaceous glands: holocrine (whole cells break down into sebum), open into follicles, driven by androgens, absent on palms and soles; central to acne.","Hair follicle: bulb with matrix cells around the dermal papilla; bulge stem cells sit near the arrector pili insertion; arrector pili is smooth muscle under sympathetic control.","Hair cycle: anagen (growth, years), catagen (regression, weeks), telogen (rest, about 3 months); in telogen effluvium a stressor pushes many hairs into telogen and diffuse shedding starts about 2 to 3 months later.","Nail: the matrix makes the plate, the bed lies under it, and proximal and lateral folds frame it; an ingrown nail is the plate edge piercing the lateral nail fold, usually on the great toe."]},{"h":"Histology terms","points":["Hyperkeratosis: thick stratum corneum (callus, ichthyosis, psoriasis).","Parakeratosis: nuclei kept in the stratum corneum (psoriasis).","Acanthosis: thick stratum spinosum (psoriasis, seborrheic keratosis).","Spongiosis: fluid between keratinocytes in the epidermis (eczema and acute contact dermatitis).","Acantholysis: keratinocytes lose their connections and separate (pemphigus).","Dyskeratosis: single keratinocytes keratinize early and abnormally (squamous cell carcinoma, Darier disease)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Where the blister splits","cols":["Target","Location","Disease","Split level"],"rows":[["Desmoglein 1","Desmosomes, upper epidermis","Pemphigus foliaceus (autoantibody)","Subcorneal, just below the stratum corneum"],["Desmoglein 3 (with or without 1)","Desmosomes, lower epidermis","Pemphigus vulgaris (autoantibody)","Suprabasal; basal cells stay on the basement membrane"],["Keratin 5 or 14","Basal keratinocyte cytoskeleton","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex (mutation)","Within the basal cell layer"],["BP180 (collagen XVII), BP230","Hemidesmosomes","Bullous pemphigoid (autoantibody)","Subepidermal, through the lamina lucida"],["Laminin 332, collagen XVII","Lamina lucida","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa (mutation)","Within the lamina lucida"],["Type VII collagen","Anchoring fibrils below lamina densa","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (mutation), epidermolysis bullosa acquisita (autoantibody)","Below the lamina densa, in the upper dermis"]]},{"title":"Skin glands","cols":["Gland","Location and opening","Secretion","Control","Linked disorder"],"rows":[["Eccrine","Whole body skin; opens directly on the surface","Watery sweat (merocrine)","Sympathetic cholinergic (muscarinic)","Hyperhidrosis"],["Apocrine","Axilla, anogenital, areola; opens into hair follicle","Viscous, odorless until bacteria act on it","Mainly adrenergic; active after puberty","Body odor"],["Sebaceous","All hair-bearing skin; opens into hair follicle","Sebum (holocrine)","Androgens","Acne"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Californians Like Girls in String Bikinis","text":"Epidermal layers from surface to deep: Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Desmo versus hemidesmo","text":"A desmosome welds one keratinocyte to the next (pemphigus, split inside the epidermis). A hemidesmosome is half of one, anchoring the basal cell to the basement membrane (pemphigoid, split under the epidermis)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Roof thickness predicts the blister","text":"A split inside the epidermis leaves a thin roof, so blisters are flaccid and break easily. A split under the epidermis leaves the whole epidermis as the roof, so blisters are tense."}],"highYield":["Stratum lucidum is present only in thick skin of the palms and soles.","Birbeck granules and CD1a identify Langerhans cells.","Melanocytes come from neural crest; Langerhans cells come from bone marrow.","Anti-desmoglein 3 gives suprabasal acantholysis with a row of basal cells left on the floor (tombstone pattern) in pemphigus vulgaris.","Anti-BP180 and BP230 give a subepidermal blister with linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane in bullous pemphigoid.","Type VII collagen anchoring fibrils fail in dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa, so blisters heal with scarring and milia.","Eccrine sweat glands are sympathetic but use acetylcholine on muscarinic receptors.","Telogen effluvium appears about 2 to 3 months after a stressor such as childbirth, high fever, or surgery."],"pitfalls":["Pemphigus targets desmosomes (inside the epidermis); pemphigoid targets hemidesmosomes (below the epidermis).","Spongiosis is fluid between keratinocytes that stay attached; acantholysis is keratinocytes losing their attachments.","Hyperkeratosis is a thick stratum corneum; acanthosis is a thick stratum spinosum.","Apocrine and sebaceous glands open into hair follicles; eccrine glands open straight onto the skin surface."],"dumps":[{"t":"Epidermis and junction drawing","p":"Draw a cross section of thick skin from the stratum corneum down to the upper dermis. Label each epidermal layer, the cells found in it, and the junction proteins between cells and at the basement membrane.","l":["Five layers in order, with stratum lucidum only in thick skin","Keratins 5 and 14 in basal cells and 1 and 10 in suprabasal cells","Keratohyalin granules and lamellar bodies in the stratum granulosum","Melanocytes and Merkel cells in the basal layer; Langerhans cells in the spinous layer","Desmosomes with desmogleins between keratinocytes","Hemidesmosomes with BP180, BP230, and integrin alpha6 beta4","Lamina lucida (laminin 332), lamina densa (type IV collagen), anchoring fibrils (type VII collagen)","Meissner corpuscles in the dermal papillae"]},{"t":"Blistering disease table","p":"Make a table with columns for target protein, structure, disease, mutation or autoantibody, and split level. Fill in pemphigus vulgaris, pemphigus foliaceus, bullous pemphigoid, and the three main types of epidermolysis bullosa.","l":["Pemphigus vulgaris: anti-desmoglein 3, suprabasal split","Pemphigus foliaceus: anti-desmoglein 1, subcorneal split","Bullous pemphigoid: anti-BP180 and BP230, subepidermal split","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex: keratin 5 or 14 mutation, split in basal cells","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa: laminin 332 or collagen XVII mutation, split in lamina lucida","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa: type VII collagen mutation, split below the lamina densa","Intraepidermal splits give flaccid blisters; subepidermal splits give tense blisters"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin (sections on blistering disorders and definitions of terms)"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-2","IN-13-D","IN-13-D1","IN-13-D2","IN-13-D3","IN-13-C3","IN-13-B1","IN-13-H1"]},"IN-2":{"id":"IN-2","summary":"How the ectoderm splits into surface ectoderm, neuroectoderm, and neural crest, and how the skin and its pigment cells form before birth. Exam questions link a structure to its germ layer and explain newborn skin findings and pigment disorders by where melanoblasts did or did not go.","notes":[{"h":"Three ectoderm lineages","points":["Surface ectoderm: epidermis, hair, nails, sweat, sebaceous, and mammary glands, lens, corneal epithelium, anterior pituitary (Rathke pouch), tooth enamel, inner ear epithelium.","Neuroectoderm (neural tube): brain and spinal cord neurons and glia (except microglia), retina and optic nerve, posterior pituitary, pineal gland.","Neural crest: cells that leave the edges of the neural folds as the tube closes and migrate widely through the embryo."]},{"h":"Neural crest derivatives tied to skin and nerves","points":["Melanocytes of skin and hair.","Schwann cells, dorsal root ganglia, and sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia.","Chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla.","Odontoblasts (dentin), while enamel comes from surface ectoderm.","Pia and arachnoid, parafollicular C cells, and most bone and connective tissue of the face."]},{"h":"Where the dermis comes from","points":["Epidermis everywhere comes from surface ectoderm; the dermis has different sources by region.","Face and front of the neck: neural crest, because cranial neural crest forms most of the facial mesenchyme where there are no somites.","Back: dermatomes of the somites (paraxial mesoderm), which spread under the ectoderm of the back.","Limbs and body wall: lateral plate mesoderm."]},{"h":"Fetal skin timeline","points":["Early on, a single layer of surface ectoderm covers the embryo.","In the second month it divides into a basal layer and a surface periderm, a temporary layer that is later shed.","An intermediate layer then forms, and by about the end of the fourth month the epidermis has its adult layers.","Melanoblasts from neural crest move into the epidermis during the first trimester and settle among basal cells.","Hair buds grow down into the dermis from about the third month; mesoderm pushes into the bud end to form the dermal papilla.","Lanugo (fine first hairs) appears around the fifth month and is mostly shed around birth.","Vernix caseosa: whitish coat of sebum, shed periderm and epidermal cells, and hairs that protects fetal skin from amniotic fluid."]},{"h":"Newborn skin findings","points":["Dermal melanocytosis (Mongolian spot): melanocytes stopped in the dermis on their way to the epidermis; deep pigment looks blue-gray; usually lumbosacral; common in darker-skinned and East Asian infants; usually fades in childhood.","Congenital melanocytic nevus: brown nevus cell lesion present at birth, may grow hair; large lesions carry melanoma risk.","Infantile hemangioma: endothelial tumor that is absent or faint at birth, grows fast in the first months, then slowly regresses; GLUT1 positive.","Port-wine stain: capillary malformation present at birth that grows with the child and never regresses; in the V1 face area it can signal Sturge-Weber syndrome.","Dermal melanocytosis can be mistaken for bruising, so it should be documented at birth."]},{"h":"Piebaldism versus albinism","points":["Piebaldism: autosomal dominant KIT mutation; melanoblasts fail to migrate or survive, so patches have no melanocytes at all.","Piebaldism pattern: white forelock and white patches on the forehead, ventral trunk, and limbs, present at birth and stable.","Oculocutaneous albinism: autosomal recessive; melanocyte number is normal but melanin synthesis is reduced (tyrosinase deficiency in type 1).","Albinism affects all skin, hair, and eyes, with nystagmus, poor vision, and high skin cancer risk."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Ectoderm derivatives","cols":["Lineage","Skin and appendages","Nervous system","Other"],"rows":[["Surface ectoderm","Epidermis, hair, nails, sweat, sebaceous, and mammary glands","Placodes: inner ear and olfactory epithelium, some cranial sensory ganglion neurons","Lens, corneal epithelium, anterior pituitary, enamel"],["Neuroectoderm","None","CNS neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependyma, retina, optic nerve","Posterior pituitary, pineal gland"],["Neural crest","Melanocytes, facial dermis","Schwann cells, dorsal root and autonomic ganglia, pia and arachnoid","Adrenal medulla, odontoblasts, C cells, facial bones"]]},{"title":"Pigment and vascular lesions in newborns","cols":["Lesion","Cause","At birth","Course"],"rows":[["Dermal melanocytosis","Melanocytes arrested in the dermis","Blue-gray patch, often lumbosacral","Usually fades in early childhood"],["Congenital melanocytic nevus","Nevus cells present in skin from fetal life","Brown, sometimes hairy","Grows with child; large ones carry melanoma risk"],["Infantile hemangioma","Benign endothelial proliferation","Absent or a faint mark","Fast growth for months, then slow regression"],["Port-wine stain","Capillary malformation","Flat pink to red patch","Persists and darkens; may thicken"],["Piebaldism","KIT mutation, no melanocytes in patches","White forelock and white patches","Stable for life"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the melanocytes","text":"No melanocytes in a white patch means they never arrived (piebaldism) or were destroyed (vitiligo). Normal melanocytes with little pigment means a melanin synthesis defect (albinism)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Deep pigment looks blue","text":"Melanin high in the epidermis looks brown. Melanin deep in the dermis scatters light and looks blue-gray, as in dermal melanocytosis and blue nevi."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Face from crest, back from somites","text":"The face has no somites, so cranial neural crest builds its connective tissue, including the dermis. The back sits over the somites, so its dermis comes from the dermatomes."}],"highYield":["Melanocytes and Schwann cells are neural crest; microglia are mesoderm.","Enamel is surface ectoderm and dentin (odontoblasts) is neural crest.","Anterior pituitary is surface ectoderm; posterior pituitary is neuroectoderm.","Dermal melanocytosis is benign and fades, but it is often confused with bruising in suspected child abuse.","A port-wine stain over the V1 area should prompt evaluation for Sturge-Weber syndrome.","Piebaldism (KIT) shows a white forelock with absent melanocytes from birth; vitiligo is acquired loss of melanocytes."],"pitfalls":["Infantile hemangioma appears after birth and regresses; port-wine stain is present at birth and persists.","Albinism has normal melanocyte numbers; piebaldism and vitiligo have none in the white areas.","Facial dermis is neural crest, while back dermis is somite dermatome and limb dermis is lateral plate mesoderm.","Vernix caseosa is made of sebum and shed cells; lanugo is the fine fetal hair."],"dumps":[{"t":"Ectoderm family tree","p":"Draw the ectoderm splitting into surface ectoderm, neural tube, and neural crest. Under each branch, list the skin and nervous system structures it forms.","l":["Surface ectoderm: epidermis, hair, nails, skin glands, mammary glands","Surface ectoderm: lens, anterior pituitary, enamel","Neural tube: CNS neurons and glia, retina, posterior pituitary","Neural crest: melanocytes and Schwann cells","Neural crest: dorsal root and autonomic ganglia, adrenal medulla","Neural crest: odontoblasts and facial dermis","Back dermis from somite dermatomes, limb dermis from lateral plate mesoderm"]},{"t":"Fetal skin and newborn lesions","p":"Draw a timeline of fetal skin development from month 1 to birth, then list five newborn skin lesions with their cause and expected course.","l":["Periderm and basal layer form in the second month","Adult layering of the epidermis by about the end of the fourth month","Melanoblasts enter the epidermis in the first trimester","Hair buds with dermal papillae from about the third month; lanugo around the fifth month","Vernix caseosa as a protective coat of sebum and shed cells","Dermal melanocytosis: arrested dermal melanocytes, fades","Infantile hemangioma grows then regresses; port-wine stain persists","Piebaldism: absent melanocytes from failed migration, stable white patches"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Integumentary System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Section on neural crest cells and their derivatives"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin (section on disorders of pigmentation and melanocytes)"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-1","IN-7","IN-13-A","IN-13-A1","IN-13-A2","IN-13-E1"]},"IN-3":{"id":"IN-3","summary":"How the plasma membrane is built, how substances cross it, how water follows solutes, and how ion gradients set the resting membrane potential. Exam questions ask you to predict cell volume, membrane potential, and excitability when a pump, channel, or ion concentration changes, including the CFTR defect in cystic fibrosis.","notes":[{"h":"Membrane structure","points":["Phospholipid bilayer: polar heads face water, fatty acid tails face each other in the middle.","Integral proteins span the bilayer (channels, carriers, pumps, receptors); peripheral proteins attach to one surface.","Glycocalyx: sugar chains on outer glycoproteins and glycolipids; gives a negative surface charge and helps cell recognition.","Cholesterol buffers fluidity: it stiffens the membrane when warm and keeps tails from packing tightly when cold.","Unsaturated fatty acids have cis double bonds that kink the tails, so they increase fluidity; short chains also increase it."]},{"h":"Transport types","points":["Simple diffusion: down the gradient, no carrier, not saturable; rate rises in a straight line with the gradient (O2, CO2, lipid-soluble drugs).","Facilitated diffusion: carrier (GLUT) moves solute down its gradient without ATP; saturable with a maximum rate.","Primary active transport: ATP is used directly to move solutes against a gradient (Na+/K+-ATPase, Ca2+-ATPase, H+/K+-ATPase).","Secondary active transport: one solute rides down the Na+ gradient made by the Na+/K+-ATPase to carry another uphill (SGLT1 moves glucose with Na+ in the intestine).","Endocytosis: membrane folds in to bring in fluid or ligands in vesicles, needs energy (receptor-mediated uptake of LDL by clathrin-coated pits)."]},{"h":"Na+/K+-ATPase and cell swelling","points":["Each cycle moves 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in, so the pump is electrogenic and adds a few mV of negativity.","The pump keeps intracellular Na+ low, which keeps water out of the cell.","If ATP falls (hypoxia) or the pump is blocked (digoxin), Na+ builds up inside and water follows, so the cell swells.","K+ leaks out and the gradients run down, so the membrane depolarizes.","Cellular swelling (hydropic change) is the earliest sign of reversible hypoxic cell injury."]},{"h":"Osmosis and tonicity","points":["Osmosis: water moves across a membrane toward the side with more solute particles.","Osmolarity counts all solute particles; normal plasma osmolality is about 285 to 295 mOsm/kg.","Tonicity counts only solutes that cannot cross the cell membrane (effective osmoles), so tonicity decides cell volume.","Isotonic (0.9% NaCl): no change. Hypotonic: water enters and cells swell. Hypertonic: water leaves and cells shrink.","Urea crosses cell membranes freely, so it is an ineffective osmole; red cells in an isosmotic urea solution swell and lyse."]},{"h":"Membrane potential","points":["Nernst equation at body temperature: E = (61 / z) x log([ion outside] / [ion inside]) in mV.","K+ example: outside 4 mmol/L, inside 140 mmol/L gives E(K) of about -94 mV; Na+ (142 outside, 14 inside) gives about +61 mV.","At rest, K+ leak channels make the membrane far more permeable to K+ than to Na+, so the resting potential sits close to E(K).","Goldman equation: the resting potential is a weighted average of each ion's equilibrium potential, weighted by its permeability.","Typical resting values: about -70 mV in neurons and about -90 mV in skeletal and ventricular muscle.","Gibbs-Donnan effect: impermeant negative proteins and phosphates trapped inside pull in cations and push out diffusible anions; the Na+/K+-ATPase keeps the extra osmotic pull from swelling the cell."]},{"h":"Potassium and excitability","points":["Hyperkalemia makes E(K) less negative, so the resting membrane depolarizes.","Mild depolarization first brings the cell closer to threshold, but sustained depolarization closes the inactivation gates of voltage-gated Na+ channels.","With fewer Na+ channels available, nerve and muscle excitability falls: weakness, paralysis, peaked T waves, wide QRS, and arrhythmias.","Hypokalemia makes E(K) more negative and hyperpolarizes the membrane, moving it away from threshold: weakness, flat T waves, and U waves."]},{"h":"CFTR in sweat ducts and airways","points":["CFTR is a cAMP-regulated, ATP-gated Cl- channel; the common F508del mutation misfolds and is degraded before reaching the membrane.","Sweat gland: the coil makes isotonic sweat, and the duct reabsorbs Cl- through CFTR and Na+ through ENaC to make sweat hypotonic.","In cystic fibrosis the duct cannot reabsorb Cl- (and Na+ follows less), so sweat chloride is 60 mmol/L or higher (under 30 mmol/L makes CF unlikely).","Airway: CFTR secretes Cl- into the lumen; loss of secretion plus more Na+ absorption through ENaC pulls water out of the mucus, so secretions thicken and clearance fails."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Membrane transport compared","cols":["Type","Energy","Direction","Saturable","Example"],"rows":[["Simple diffusion","None","Down gradient","No","O2, CO2"],["Facilitated diffusion","None","Down gradient","Yes","GLUT4 glucose uptake in muscle and fat"],["Primary active","ATP directly","Against gradient","Yes","Na+/K+-ATPase"],["Secondary active","Na+ gradient made by ATP","Against gradient for the carried solute","Yes","SGLT1 (Na+ with glucose)"],["Endocytosis","ATP","Into the cell in vesicles","Yes (receptor-mediated)","LDL uptake by LDL receptor"]]},{"title":"Cell volume in different solutions","cols":["Solution","Effective osmolality vs cell","Water movement","Cell volume"],"rows":[["0.9% NaCl","Equal","None","No change"],["0.45% NaCl","Lower","Into cell","Swells"],["3% NaCl","Higher","Out of cell","Shrinks"],["Isosmotic urea","Lower (urea enters the cell)","Into cell","Swells and may lyse"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Pump out three, pump in two","text":"The Na+/K+-ATPase sends 3 Na+ out for every 2 K+ in. Stop it and Na+ (with water) piles up inside, so the cell swells and depolarizes."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Same channel, opposite jobs","text":"In the sweat duct CFTR brings Cl- back in, so CF gives salty sweat. In the airway CFTR sends Cl- out, so CF gives dry, thick mucus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tonicity asks what stays out","text":"To predict cell volume, ignore any solute that crosses the membrane (urea, ethanol) and count only the solutes that stay outside."}],"highYield":["Cholesterol keeps membrane fluidity steady over a range of temperatures.","Facilitated diffusion and active transport are saturable; simple diffusion is not.","SGLT1 is secondary active transport powered by the Na+ gradient from the Na+/K+-ATPase.","ATP loss in hypoxia stops the Na+/K+-ATPase, causing cell swelling, the first sign of reversible injury.","Resting membrane potential sits near E(K) because resting K+ permeability is highest.","Hyperkalemia depolarizes and, if sustained, inactivates Na+ channels and lowers excitability.","Sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or higher supports a diagnosis of cystic fibrosis.","Urea raises osmolarity but not tonicity."],"pitfalls":["Osmolarity is all particles; tonicity is only particles that cannot cross the membrane.","Hyperkalemia does not hyperpolarize; it depolarizes, and the drop in excitability comes from Na+ channel inactivation.","In CF, sweat Cl- is high because reabsorption fails in the duct, while airway problems come from failed secretion.","Secondary active transport uses no ATP at the carrier itself; ATP is spent by the pump that builds the Na+ gradient."],"dumps":[{"t":"Membrane and transporters","p":"Draw a section of plasma membrane. Label its parts, then draw one example of each transport type with arrows for the direction of movement and where energy is used.","l":["Phospholipid bilayer with polar heads facing out","Cholesterol and its role in fluidity","Integral and peripheral proteins and the glycocalyx","Simple diffusion of O2 or CO2","GLUT carrier for facilitated diffusion","Na+/K+-ATPase moving 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in","SGLT1 carrying glucose in with Na+","Receptor-mediated endocytosis in a clathrin-coated pit"]},{"t":"Resting potential and potassium","p":"Write the Nernst equation and calculate E(K) for outside 4 mmol/L and inside 140 mmol/L. Then draw the resting potential on a voltage line and show where it moves in hyperkalemia and hypokalemia.","l":["E = (61 / z) x log(out / in)","E(K) about -94 mV with these values","Resting potential lies near E(K) because of K+ leak channels","Goldman equation weights ions by permeability","Gibbs-Donnan effect from trapped intracellular anions","Hyperkalemia moves the resting potential toward zero","Sustained depolarization inactivates voltage-gated Na+ channels","Hypokalemia moves the resting potential further from threshold"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Substances Through Cell Membranes"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases (section on cystic fibrosis)"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-4","IN-8","IN-1"]},"IN-4":{"id":"IN-4","summary":"How the hypothalamus holds core temperature near a set point by balancing heat loss and heat production, and what happens when that balance fails. Exam questions separate fever from hyperthermia, heat exhaustion from heat stroke, and explain malignant hyperthermia, hypothermia, and cold injury by mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"Hypothalamic control","points":["Core and skin thermoreceptors send input to the hypothalamus, which compares it with a set point near 37 °C (98.6 °F).","Anterior hypothalamus (preoptic area): drives heat loss through skin vasodilation and sweating.","Posterior hypothalamus: drives heat conservation and production through vasoconstriction and shivering.","A lesion of the anterior hypothalamus causes hyperthermia; a lesion of the posterior hypothalamus leaves the person unable to stay warm in cold surroundings."]},{"h":"Routes of heat loss","points":["Radiation: infrared heat to cooler objects; the largest route at rest in a cool room.","Conduction: direct heat transfer to touching objects or air.","Convection: moving air or water carries away the warmed layer next to the skin; wind and water speed it up.","Evaporation: each gram of sweat that evaporates removes about 0.58 kcal.","When air temperature is above skin temperature, the body gains heat by radiation and conduction, so evaporation is the only way left to lose heat.","High humidity lowers the vapor pressure gradient, so sweat drips off without evaporating and cooling fails."]},{"h":"Heat conservation and production","points":["Sympathetic vasoconstriction of skin vessels (alpha-1) keeps warm blood in the core.","Piloerection by arrector pili muscles traps air; a minor effect in humans.","Shivering: involuntary muscle contraction that can raise heat production several fold.","Thyroid hormone raises basal metabolic rate over days to weeks of cold exposure.","Nonshivering thermogenesis: norepinephrine on beta-3 receptors in brown fat releases fatty acids that open UCP1 (thermogenin), letting protons leak back across the inner mitochondrial membrane so energy is released as heat instead of ATP.","Neonates depend on brown fat because they shiver poorly, have a large surface area for their mass, and have little subcutaneous fat."]},{"h":"Fever and hyperthermia","points":["Fever: microbial products trigger macrophages to release IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha (endogenous pyrogens).","These cytokines induce COX-2 in hypothalamic endothelium, and PGE2 raises the set point.","The body now feels cold: vasoconstriction and shivering (chills) raise temperature to the new set point; when the set point falls, sweating and flushing follow.","NSAIDs and acetaminophen block PGE2 synthesis, so they lower fever.","Hyperthermia: set point unchanged, heat gain or production exceeds heat loss (heat stroke, malignant hyperthermia, some drugs); antipyretics do not help."]},{"h":"Heat illness","points":["Heat exhaustion: core temperature usually below 40 °C (104 °F), normal mental status, heavy sweating, weakness, nausea, headache, and volume depletion.","Heat stroke: core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) with CNS dysfunction (confusion, seizures, coma).","Classic heat stroke: older adults or the chronically ill during heat waves, worse with anticholinergics or diuretics; develops over days; skin often dry.","Exertional heat stroke: young healthy people during hard exercise in heat; develops over hours; sweating often still present.","Complications: rhabdomyolysis (high CK, myoglobinuria), acute kidney injury, DIC, liver injury, and hyperkalemia."]},{"h":"Malignant hyperthermia","points":["Usually an autosomal dominant mutation in RYR1, the calcium release channel of skeletal muscle sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Triggers: volatile anesthetics (halothane, sevoflurane, isoflurane, desflurane) and succinylcholine.","Uncontrolled Ca2+ release causes sustained contraction (rigidity, masseter spasm) and huge ATP use.","Rising end-tidal CO2 (hypercarbia) is an early sign, followed by tachycardia, acidosis, hyperkalemia, rhabdomyolysis, and rising temperature."]},{"h":"Cold injury","points":["Hypothermia: core temperature below 35 °C (95 °F).","Mild (about 32 to 35 °C (89.6 to 95 °F)): shivering, tachycardia, confusion. Below about 32 °C (89.6 °F) shivering stops, and bradycardia and falling consciousness follow.","ECG: Osborn (J) waves, a positive hump where the QRS meets the ST segment; risk of ventricular fibrillation and asystole as temperature falls further.","Frostbite: tissue freezes; ice crystals and fluid shifts injure cells, and on rewarming endothelial injury and microvascular thrombosis cause ischemic necrosis.","Perniosis (chilblains): nonfreezing damp cold causes vasospasm and inflammation of small vessels, giving tender red-purple papules on fingers and toes."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Fever, hyperthermia, and heat illness","cols":["Condition","Set point","Core temperature","Mental status","Key mechanism"],"rows":[["Fever","Raised","Usually below 41 °C (105.8 °F)","Usually normal","IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha induce hypothalamic PGE2"],["Heat exhaustion","Normal","Usually below 40 °C (104 °F)","Normal","Volume and salt loss from sweating"],["Classic heat stroke","Normal","Above 40 °C (104 °F)","Altered","Heat load overwhelms impaired cooling in older or ill people"],["Exertional heat stroke","Normal","Above 40 °C (104 °F)","Altered","Muscle heat production exceeds heat loss during exercise"],["Malignant hyperthermia","Normal","Rising quickly","Under anesthesia","RYR1 calcium leak triggered by volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine"]]},{"title":"Cold injuries","cols":["Injury","Exposure","Mechanism","Findings"],"rows":[["Hypothermia","Cold environment or immersion","Core temperature below 35 °C (95 °F) slows metabolism and conduction","Shivering stops when severe, bradycardia, Osborn waves, arrhythmias"],["Frostbite","Below freezing","Ice crystals, endothelial injury, thrombosis","Numb, white or gray skin; blisters and necrosis after rewarming"],["Perniosis","Damp cold above freezing","Vasospasm and inflammation of small vessels","Tender red-purple papules on fingers and toes"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"A/C = Anterior Cooling; zap the Posterior and become a Poikilotherm","text":"The anterior hypothalamus cools (lesion gives hyperthermia). The posterior hypothalamus heats (lesion leaves you unable to stay warm, like a cold-blooded animal)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fever moves the thermostat","text":"In fever the thermostat is turned up, so the patient shivers and feels cold while temperature rises. In hyperthermia the thermostat is unchanged, so the patient feels hot and antipyretics do nothing."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Hot day, sweat is the only exit","text":"When the air is hotter than the skin, radiation and conduction bring heat in. Only evaporation removes it, and humidity blocks evaporation."}],"highYield":["PGE2 in the hypothalamus raises the set point in fever; COX inhibitors lower it.","Heat stroke is core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) plus CNS dysfunction.","Exertional heat stroke brings more rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury, and DIC.","Malignant hyperthermia: RYR1 mutation, volatile anesthetic or succinylcholine, rigidity, rising end-tidal CO2, then rising temperature.","UCP1 in brown fat uncouples oxidative phosphorylation to make heat in neonates.","Osborn (J) waves on ECG point to hypothermia.","Frostbite is a freezing injury; perniosis is a nonfreezing injury from damp cold."],"pitfalls":["Fever and hyperthermia both raise temperature, but only fever raises the set point.","Heat exhaustion keeps mental status normal; heat stroke does not.","Dry skin is typical of classic heat stroke, while people with exertional heat stroke often still sweat.","In malignant hyperthermia, rising CO2 and rigidity usually come before the temperature climbs."],"dumps":[{"t":"Thermoregulation loop","p":"Draw the hypothalamus with its inputs and outputs. Show the heat loss responses from the anterior hypothalamus and the heat conservation and production responses from the posterior hypothalamus, then show how pyrogens change the set point.","l":["Core and skin thermoreceptors feed the hypothalamus","Set point near 37 °C (98.6 °F)","Anterior hypothalamus: vasodilation and sweating","Posterior hypothalamus: vasoconstriction and shivering","Brown fat UCP1 and thyroid hormone for heat production","IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha lead to hypothalamic PGE2","PGE2 raises the set point in fever","Hyperthermia leaves the set point unchanged"]},{"t":"Heat and cold illness table","p":"Make a table comparing heat exhaustion, classic heat stroke, exertional heat stroke, malignant hyperthermia, hypothermia, frostbite, and perniosis by cause, temperature, key mechanism, and complications.","l":["Heat exhaustion: below 40 °C (104 °F) with normal mental status","Heat stroke: above 40 °C (104 °F) with CNS dysfunction","Classic in older adults during heat waves; exertional in young people during exercise","Rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury, and DIC as complications","Malignant hyperthermia: RYR1 and anesthetic triggers, hypercarbia and rigidity","Hypothermia: below 35 °C (95 °F), bradycardia, Osborn waves","Frostbite: freezing, ice crystals, thrombosis","Perniosis: nonfreezing damp cold, inflammatory papules"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Body Temperature Regulation and Fever"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases (sections on hyperthermia and hypothermia)"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-3","IN-1","IN-11"]},"IN-5":{"id":"IN-5","summary":"The proteins, lipids, vitamins, and minerals that build and protect skin, and the skin signs that appear when one is missing or in excess. Exam questions trace collagen synthesis and vitamin D synthesis, match a rash to a deficiency, read xanthomas as clues to a lipid disorder, and explain porphyria cutanea tarda.","notes":[{"h":"Collagen synthesis","points":["Inside the fibroblast: alpha chains with Gly-X-Y repeats are made on the rough ER.","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases add OH groups to proline and lysine; they need vitamin C and Fe2+.","Hydroxylysine is glycosylated, and three chains wind into a triple helix (procollagen).","Outside the cell: procollagen peptidases cut off the end propeptides to make tropocollagen.","Lysyl oxidase, a copper enzyme, cross-links tropocollagen into strong fibrils.","Failed hydroxylation gives scurvy; glycine substitutions in type I collagen block helix formation in osteogenesis imperfecta; failed propeptide cleavage or cross-linking gives some Ehlers-Danlos types and Menkes disease."]},{"h":"Vitamin C and copper","points":["Scurvy: perifollicular hemorrhages, corkscrew hairs, swollen bleeding gums, easy bruising, and poor wound healing.","Menkes disease: X-linked ATP7A mutation blocks copper transport out of intestinal cells, so lysyl oxidase activity falls.","Menkes findings: brittle, kinky, light-colored hair, low serum copper and ceruloplasmin, weak blood vessels, and neurodegeneration in infant boys."]},{"h":"Niacin and pellagra","points":["Pellagra: photosensitive dermatitis (sun-exposed skin, including a necklace on the neck), diarrhea, and dementia.","Niacin can be made from tryptophan, a pathway that needs vitamin B6 and B2.","Corn-based diets: niacin in corn is bound and poorly absorbed, and corn is low in tryptophan.","Hartnup disease: autosomal recessive defect of the neutral amino acid transporter in gut and kidney, so tryptophan is lost.","Carcinoid syndrome: tumor cells divert tryptophan into serotonin.","Isoniazid: depletes vitamin B6, which the tryptophan to niacin pathway needs."]},{"h":"Other deficiencies with skin signs","points":["Vitamin A: follicular hyperkeratosis (rough bumps around follicles), dry skin, plus night blindness.","Zinc: acrodermatitis enteropathica, with dermatitis around the mouth, anus, and on hands and feet, alopecia, diarrhea, and poor wound healing.","Biotin (raw egg white avidin, long-term antibiotics): dermatitis around the eyes, nose, and mouth, and alopecia.","Riboflavin (B2): angular cheilitis, cheilosis, glossitis.","Essential fatty acids (fat-free parenteral nutrition, fat malabsorption): dry scaly dermatitis and poor wound healing."]},{"h":"Vitamin D synthesis in skin","points":["UVB converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in the epidermis to previtamin D3, which heat converts to cholecalciferol (D3).","Liver 25-hydroxylase makes 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the storage form measured in blood.","Kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylase (stimulated by PTH and low phosphate) makes active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D.","Less skin synthesis with dark skin (melanin absorbs UVB), high latitude in winter, sunscreen, covering clothing, and older age.","Long sun exposure does not cause toxicity, because extra previtamin D3 is broken down by UV into inactive products."]},{"h":"Skin lipids and dermal matrix","points":["Lamellar bodies in the stratum granulosum release ceramides, cholesterol, and free fatty acids, which form the lipid sheets between corneocytes.","Sebum: mostly triglycerides, wax esters, and squalene; bacterial lipases release free fatty acids from it.","Dermal ground substance: hyaluronic acid (not sulfated, binds lots of water) and dermatan sulfate (the main sulfated GAG of the dermis)."]},{"h":"Porphyria cutanea tarda","points":["Most common porphyria; low activity of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase in the liver, mostly acquired, sometimes inherited.","Uroporphyrins build up, reach the skin, absorb visible light (around 400 nm), and make reactive oxygen species that injure the upper dermis.","Findings: blisters, erosions, fragility, and milia on the backs of the hands, hypertrichosis of the face, and tea-colored urine that fluoresces under a Wood lamp.","Triggers: alcohol, hepatitis C, iron overload (HFE mutations), estrogens, smoking, and HIV.","No acute abdominal or neurologic attacks."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Collagen synthesis steps and failures","cols":["Step","Site","Requirement","Disorder when it fails"],"rows":[["Alpha chain synthesis (Gly-X-Y)","Rough ER","Normal COL genes","Osteogenesis imperfecta (glycine substitutions in type I collagen)"],["Hydroxylation of proline and lysine","Rough ER","Vitamin C, Fe2+","Scurvy; lysyl hydroxylase deficiency in kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos"],["Glycosylation and triple helix","Rough ER","Hydroxylysine residues","Weak helix with any defect in the steps above"],["Propeptide cleavage","Outside the cell","Procollagen peptidases","Dermatosparaxis type of Ehlers-Danlos"],["Cross-linking","Outside the cell","Lysyl oxidase with copper","Menkes disease"]]},{"title":"Skin signs of nutrient deficiency","cols":["Nutrient","Skin and hair signs","Other clues","Common causes"],"rows":[["Vitamin C","Perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, bruising","Bleeding gums, poor healing","Very poor diet"],["Niacin (B3)","Photosensitive dermatitis","Diarrhea, dementia","Corn diet, Hartnup, carcinoid, isoniazid"],["Vitamin A","Follicular hyperkeratosis, dry skin","Night blindness, Bitot spots","Fat malabsorption, poor diet"],["Zinc","Periorificial and acral dermatitis, alopecia","Diarrhea, poor healing, taste loss","Acrodermatitis enteropathica, malabsorption"],["Biotin","Periorificial dermatitis, alopecia","Conjunctivitis, enteritis","Raw egg whites, long antibiotic use"],["Riboflavin (B2)","Angular cheilitis, cheilosis","Glossitis, corneal vascularization","Poor diet, alcohol use"],["Essential fatty acids","Dry scaly dermatitis","Poor healing, alopecia","Fat-free parenteral nutrition"]]},{"title":"Xanthomas and lipid disorders","cols":["Xanthoma","Look and site","Lipid disorder","Defect"],"rows":[["Eruptive","Crops of small yellow papules with red rims on buttocks and extensor surfaces","Severe hypertriglyceridemia (type I, also type V)","Lipoprotein lipase or apoC-II deficiency in type I"],["Tendon","Firm nodules on the Achilles and finger extensor tendons","Familial hypercholesterolemia (type IIa)","LDL receptor"],["Xanthelasma","Yellow plaques on the eyelids","Familial hypercholesterolemia, also seen with normal lipids","LDL receptor when familial"],["Palmar crease","Yellow streaks in palm creases","Dysbetalipoproteinemia (type III)","ApoE2 homozygosity"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 3 Ds of pellagra","text":"Dermatitis, Diarrhea, Dementia (a fourth D, Death, if untreated)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Vitamin C inside, copper outside","text":"Vitamin C works inside the fibroblast at hydroxylation. Copper works outside the cell at cross-linking by lysyl oxidase. Both defects give weak connective tissue."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Where the xanthoma sits","text":"Tendons and eyelids point to LDL (familial hypercholesterolemia). Sudden crops on the buttocks point to triglycerides. Palm creases point to apoE2."}],"highYield":["Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases need vitamin C and Fe2+; lysyl oxidase needs copper.","Corkscrew hairs and perifollicular hemorrhages mean vitamin C deficiency.","Kinky brittle hair in an infant boy with low copper and ceruloplasmin means Menkes disease (ATP7A).","Pellagra can come from Hartnup disease, carcinoid syndrome, or isoniazid as well as diet.","Acrodermatitis enteropathica (zinc) gives dermatitis around the orifices and on the hands and feet with alopecia.","Skin makes vitamin D3 from 7-dehydrocholesterol with UVB; the kidney makes the active form.","Eruptive xanthomas with triglycerides over about 1,000 mg/dL carry a risk of acute pancreatitis.","Porphyria cutanea tarda: blisters on the backs of the hands plus facial hypertrichosis, triggered by alcohol, hepatitis C, iron, or estrogen."],"pitfalls":["Menkes disease is low copper with a cross-linking defect; Wilson disease is copper overload in the liver and brain.","Biotin and zinc deficiency both cause periorificial dermatitis with alopecia; ask about raw egg whites versus malabsorption or an inherited zinc uptake defect.","25-hydroxyvitamin D reflects stores; 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is the active hormone and is a poor marker of stores.","Porphyria cutanea tarda causes skin blisters without the abdominal and neurologic attacks of acute intermittent porphyria."],"dumps":[{"t":"Collagen synthesis pathway","p":"Draw a fibroblast and trace collagen synthesis from gene to cross-linked fibril. At each step, label what it needs and the disorder that results when it fails.","l":["Gly-X-Y alpha chains made on rough ER","Proline and lysine hydroxylation needs vitamin C and Fe2+","Glycosylation of hydroxylysine","Triple helix of procollagen","Propeptide cleavage outside the cell","Lysyl oxidase cross-linking needs copper","Scurvy, osteogenesis imperfecta, Ehlers-Danlos types, and Menkes disease placed at the right steps"]},{"t":"Vitamin D and deficiency rashes","p":"Trace vitamin D synthesis from skin to kidney. Then list the skin signs of deficiency of vitamin C, niacin, vitamin A, zinc, biotin, riboflavin, and essential fatty acids.","l":["7-dehydrocholesterol converted by UVB in the epidermis","Liver 25-hydroxylation, then kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylation","Dark skin, high latitude, and sunscreen reduce synthesis","Vitamin C: perifollicular hemorrhage and corkscrew hairs","Niacin: photosensitive dermatitis with diarrhea and dementia","Vitamin A: follicular hyperkeratosis; zinc: acral and periorificial dermatitis","Biotin: periorificial dermatitis and alopecia; riboflavin: angular cheilitis","Essential fatty acids: scaly dermatitis"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on vitamins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on heme synthesis and the porphyrias"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-6","IN-10","IN-13-E2","IN-13-D"]},"IN-6":{"id":"IN-6","summary":"How reactive oxygen species form, what they damage, and how enzymes and non-vitamin antioxidants such as glutathione, ubiquinol, carotenoids, and melanin protect skin. Exam questions connect UV light to DNA damage and photoaging and explain why chronic granulomatous disease brings infections with catalase-positive organisms.","notes":[{"h":"How reactive oxygen species form","points":["Superoxide (O2 radical anion): one-electron reduction of O2, from electron leak in mitochondrial complexes I and III and from NADPH oxidase in phagocytes.","Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2): made when superoxide dismutase converts superoxide; a weak oxidant that crosses membranes easily.","Hydroxyl radical: the most reactive; made by the Fenton reaction (Fe2+ plus H2O2) and by ionizing radiation splitting water.","UV light, especially UVA, excites photosensitizers in skin that pass energy to oxygen and make reactive species."]},{"h":"What they damage","points":["Membrane lipids: lipid peroxidation of polyunsaturated fatty acids runs as a chain reaction and makes membranes leaky.","Proteins: oxidized side chains, cross-links, and fragmentation lead to misfolding and breakdown.","DNA: strand breaks and oxidized bases such as 8-oxoguanine, which pairs with adenine and causes G to T mutations."]},{"h":"Antioxidant enzymes","points":["Superoxide dismutase turns superoxide into H2O2 and O2: Cu/Zn form in the cytosol, Mn form in mitochondria.","Catalase (peroxisomes) turns H2O2 into water and O2.","Glutathione peroxidase contains selenium (as selenocysteine) and uses reduced glutathione (GSH) to turn H2O2 and lipid peroxides into water and alcohols.","Glutathione reductase regenerates GSH from oxidized glutathione (GSSG) using NADPH.","NADPH comes from the pentose phosphate pathway, whose rate-limiting enzyme is G6PD.","Red cells rely only on this pathway for NADPH, so G6PD deficiency causes hemolysis under oxidant stress (Heinz bodies, bite cells)."]},{"h":"Non-vitamin antioxidants in skin","points":["Reduced glutathione: a Glu-Cys-Gly tripeptide whose cysteine thiol gives electrons to neutralize oxidants and serves glutathione peroxidase.","Ubiquinol (reduced coenzyme Q10): fat-soluble, sits in membranes and mitochondria, stops lipid peroxidation, and helps regenerate vitamin E.","Carotenoids such as lycopene: long chains of conjugated double bonds quench singlet oxygen and trap peroxyl radicals; lycopene is not a vitamin A precursor.","Melanin: absorbs and scatters UV light, scavenges radicals, and forms caps over keratinocyte nuclei.","Eumelanin (brown-black) absorbs UV well and quenches radicals; pheomelanin (red-yellow, made with cysteine) protects poorly and can itself generate reactive oxygen species after UV exposure."]},{"h":"UVB, UVA, and photoaging","points":["UVB (about 280 to 315 nm): mostly absorbed in the epidermis and directly by DNA, forming cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers; causes sunburn.","UVA (about 315 to 400 nm): most of the UV reaching the ground, goes through window glass, reaches the dermis, and damages DNA mainly through reactive oxygen species.","Photoaging: UV-driven reactive oxygen species activate AP-1, which raises matrix metalloproteinases (such as MMP-1) that break down dermal collagen and lowers new collagen synthesis.","Solar elastosis: tangled, thickened abnormal elastic material collects in the upper dermis and looks blue-gray (basophilic) on H&E; clinically yellow, leathery, wrinkled skin."]},{"h":"Respiratory burst and chronic granulomatous disease","points":["Neutrophil NADPH oxidase makes superoxide in the phagolysosome; superoxide dismutase converts it to H2O2.","Myeloperoxidase (blue-green heme enzyme) combines H2O2 with Cl- to make hypochlorous acid (bleach).","Chronic granulomatous disease: defective NADPH oxidase, most often X-linked (gp91phox, CYBB), so no respiratory burst.","Catalase-negative bacteria leave their own H2O2 for the neutrophil's myeloperoxidase to use; catalase-positive organisms destroy it and survive.","Result: recurrent skin and lymph node abscesses and granulomas from S. aureus, Burkholderia cepacia, Serratia, Nocardia, and Aspergillus.","Tests: abnormal dihydrorhodamine flow cytometry; nitroblue tetrazolium stays yellow instead of turning blue."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Reactive oxygen species and their defenses","cols":["Species","Main sources","Removed by"],"rows":[["Superoxide","Mitochondrial electron leak, NADPH oxidase, xanthine oxidase","Superoxide dismutase (Cu/Zn cytosolic, Mn mitochondrial)"],["Hydrogen peroxide","Superoxide dismutase, peroxisomal oxidases","Catalase; glutathione peroxidase with GSH"],["Hydroxyl radical","Fenton reaction with Fe2+, ionizing radiation","No enzyme; prevented by removing H2O2 and binding free iron"],["Lipid peroxides","Radical attack on membrane fatty acids","Glutathione peroxidase; ubiquinol and vitamin E stop the chain"]]},{"title":"UVB and UVA compared","cols":["Feature","UVB","UVA"],"rows":[["Wavelength","About 280 to 315 nm","About 315 to 400 nm"],["Depth","Mostly epidermis","Reaches the dermis"],["Main DNA damage","Direct: cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers","Indirect: oxidative damage such as 8-oxoguanine"],["Acute effect","Sunburn","Immediate tanning"],["Chronic effect","Squamous and basal cell carcinoma","Photoaging, also adds to skin cancer"],["Window glass","Blocked","Passes through"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"UVA is Aging, UVB is Burning","text":"UVA penetrates deeper and drives photoaging; UVB is absorbed higher up and causes sunburn and direct DNA damage."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Catalase steals the weapon","text":"In chronic granulomatous disease the neutrophil cannot make its own H2O2. Catalase-negative bacteria hand over theirs, so they are killed; catalase-positive organisms break theirs down and survive."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the electrons back to G6PD","text":"H2O2 is cleared by glutathione peroxidase, which uses GSH. GSH is restored by glutathione reductase, which uses NADPH. NADPH comes from G6PD."}],"highYield":["The Fenton reaction (Fe2+ plus H2O2) makes hydroxyl radical, which is why free iron worsens oxidative injury.","Glutathione peroxidase needs selenium; glutathione reductase needs NADPH from G6PD.","Eumelanin protects better than pheomelanin, which can add oxidative stress after UV.","Cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers are the signature DNA lesion of UVB.","MMP-mediated collagen breakdown and solar elastosis are the hallmarks of photoaging.","Chronic granulomatous disease brings abscesses with catalase-positive organisms such as S. aureus and Aspergillus.","Myeloperoxidase deficiency is usually mild and leaves the nitroblue tetrazolium test normal."],"pitfalls":["Superoxide dismutase makes H2O2; catalase and glutathione peroxidase remove it.","Chronic granulomatous disease is an NADPH oxidase defect; myeloperoxidase deficiency is a different, milder disorder.","UVB damages DNA directly; UVA works mostly through reactive oxygen species.","Solar elastosis is a dermal change: blue-gray elastic material in the upper dermis below the epidermis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Oxidant and antioxidant map","p":"Draw O2 becoming superoxide, then H2O2, then hydroxyl radical. Label each enzyme that removes them and trace the NADPH supply for glutathione back to G6PD.","l":["Superoxide from mitochondria and NADPH oxidase","Superoxide dismutase forms (Cu/Zn cytosolic, Mn mitochondrial)","Catalase turns H2O2 into water and O2","Glutathione peroxidase with selenium and GSH","Glutathione reductase uses NADPH","NADPH from G6PD in the pentose phosphate pathway","Fenton reaction with Fe2+ makes hydroxyl radical","Damage to lipids, proteins, and DNA"]},{"t":"Neutrophil killing and CGD","p":"Draw a neutrophil phagolysosome with a bacterium inside. Label each step of the respiratory burst, then show which step fails in chronic granulomatous disease and why catalase-positive organisms survive.","l":["NADPH oxidase makes superoxide from O2","Superoxide dismutase makes H2O2","Myeloperoxidase makes hypochlorous acid from H2O2 and Cl-","CGD: NADPH oxidase defect, most often X-linked","Catalase-negative bacteria supply H2O2 and are killed","Catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Aspergillus, Burkholderia, Serratia, Nocardia) survive","Abnormal dihydrorhodamine and nitroblue tetrazolium tests"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Pentose Phosphate Pathway and Nicotinamide Adenine Dinucleotide Phosphate"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell Injury, Cell Death, and Adaptations (section on oxidative stress)"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Congenital and Acquired Immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin (section on actinic changes)"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-5","IN-7","IN-11","IN-12","IN-13-G"]},"IN-7":{"id":"IN-7","summary":"How cell cycle control, DNA repair, signaling genes, and structural genes explain inherited and sun-induced skin disease. Exam questions link a gene to a tumor or skin finding, read pedigrees for recurrence risk, and explain filaggrin, steroid sulfatase, and MC1R defects by mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"G1 to S checkpoint","points":["Cyclin D with CDK4/6 phosphorylates Rb; phosphorylated Rb releases E2F, which turns on genes needed for S phase.","Unphosphorylated Rb holds E2F and keeps the cell in G1.","p16 (CDKN2A) blocks CDK4/6 and keeps Rb active.","DNA damage stabilizes p53, which induces p21 to block CDKs (G1 arrest) and, if damage is too great, triggers apoptosis through BAX.","UV signature mutations are C to T (and CC to TT) changes at sites of two neighboring pyrimidines.","TP53 UV-signature mutations appear early in sun-damaged keratinocytes and drive actinic keratosis and cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma."]},{"h":"Xeroderma pigmentosum","points":["Autosomal recessive defect in nucleotide excision repair, which removes bulky helix-distorting lesions such as pyrimidine dimers.","Repair steps: recognize the lesion, unwind DNA (TFIIH helicases), cut on both sides, remove a short strand, fill the gap with DNA polymerase, and seal it with ligase.","Findings: severe sunburn or extreme sun sensitivity, freckling in early childhood, dry skin, and eye damage.","Basal cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and melanoma appear in childhood, often before age 10.","Some groups also develop progressive neurologic degeneration."]},{"h":"Driver mutations in skin tumors","points":["Basal cell carcinoma: loss of PTCH1 frees Smoothened, so hedgehog signaling (GLI) stays on.","Gorlin syndrome (nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome): autosomal dominant germline PTCH1; many basal cell carcinomas at a young age, jaw keratocysts, palmar pits, falx calcification.","BRAF V600E activates MAPK signaling; it is found in most acquired nevi and about half of cutaneous melanomas, so it starts growth but needs more hits for cancer.","NRAS mutations occur in a smaller share of melanomas and in congenital nevi.","Familial melanoma: germline CDKN2A (p16) loss, with many dysplastic nevi and a higher pancreatic cancer risk."]},{"h":"Inheritance patterns and recurrence risk","points":["Autosomal dominant (NF1, tuberous sclerosis, epidermolysis bullosa simplex): every generation, both sexes, male-to-male transmission possible; affected parent with unaffected partner gives 50% risk per child.","Many NF1 and tuberous sclerosis cases are new mutations with unaffected parents.","Autosomal recessive (oculocutaneous albinism, xeroderma pigmentosum): skips generations, consanguinity raises risk; two carriers give 25% affected, 50% carriers.","An unaffected sibling of a child with an autosomal recessive disease has a 2/3 chance of being a carrier.","X-linked recessive (X-linked ichthyosis): mostly males, no male-to-male transmission; a carrier mother gives 50% of sons affected and 50% of daughters carriers; an affected father gives all daughters carrier status and no affected sons."]},{"h":"Neurocutaneous syndromes","points":["NF1: chromosome 17; neurofibromin is a Ras GTPase-activating protein, so its loss leaves Ras active.","NF1 skin: six or more café-au-lait macules, axillary and inguinal freckling, cutaneous and plexiform neurofibromas; also Lisch nodules and optic glioma.","Tuberous sclerosis: TSC1 (hamartin) and TSC2 (tuberin) form a complex that keeps mTOR off; loss leaves mTOR active and hamartomas grow.","Tuberous sclerosis skin: ash-leaf (hypomelanotic) macules, often the earliest sign and easiest to see with a Wood lamp, facial angiofibromas, shagreen patch, periungual fibromas.","Tuberous sclerosis other organs: cortical tubers and seizures, cardiac rhabdomyomas, renal angiomyolipomas."]},{"h":"Barrier genes and ichthyosis","points":["Profilaggrin in keratohyalin granules is cut into filaggrin, which bundles keratin filaments and later breaks down into natural moisturizing factor.","Loss-of-function FLG mutations cause ichthyosis vulgaris: fine white scale on extensor legs, flexures spared, palmar hyperlinearity, keratosis pilaris, and a thin or absent granular layer.","FLG mutations are a major risk factor for atopic dermatitis because a leaky barrier lets allergens in and water out.","X-linked ichthyosis: steroid sulfatase deficiency lets cholesterol sulfate build up in the stratum corneum, so corneocytes do not shed.","X-linked ichthyosis shows large dark brown scales on the trunk and limbs in boys, corneal opacities, and, during the pregnancy, low maternal estriol and prolonged labor."]},{"h":"MC1R and pigment type","points":["Alpha-MSH binds MC1R, a Gs-coupled receptor on melanocytes, raising cAMP.","cAMP activates MITF, which turns on tyrosinase and related enzymes to favor eumelanin.","Weak MC1R signaling shifts production to pheomelanin.","MC1R loss-of-function variants give red hair, fair skin, freckles, and poor tanning, with higher melanoma risk."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Skin genes and their diseases","cols":["Gene or protein","Normal role","Disease","Inheritance or origin"],"rows":[["TP53","G1 arrest and apoptosis after DNA damage","Actinic keratosis, squamous cell carcinoma","Somatic UV-signature mutation"],["XP genes (nucleotide excision repair)","Remove pyrimidine dimers","Xeroderma pigmentosum","Autosomal recessive"],["PTCH1","Holds hedgehog signaling off","Basal cell carcinoma, Gorlin syndrome","Somatic; germline autosomal dominant in Gorlin"],["BRAF V600E, NRAS","MAPK signaling","Nevi, melanoma","Somatic"],["CDKN2A (p16)","Blocks CDK4/6","Familial melanoma","Germline autosomal dominant"],["NF1 (neurofibromin)","Turns Ras off","Neurofibromatosis type 1","Autosomal dominant"],["TSC1, TSC2 (hamartin, tuberin)","Keep mTOR off","Tuberous sclerosis","Autosomal dominant"],["FLG (filaggrin)","Bundles keratin, makes natural moisturizing factor","Ichthyosis vulgaris, atopic dermatitis risk","Semidominant"],["STS (steroid sulfatase)","Breaks down cholesterol sulfate for shedding","X-linked ichthyosis","X-linked recessive"],["MC1R","Drives eumelanin through cAMP and MITF","Red hair, fair skin, melanoma risk","Common variants"]]},{"title":"Recurrence risk by pattern","cols":["Pattern","Skin examples","Parents","Risk per child"],"rows":[["Autosomal dominant","NF1, tuberous sclerosis, epidermolysis bullosa simplex","One affected, one unaffected","50% affected"],["Autosomal recessive","Oculocutaneous albinism, xeroderma pigmentosum","Both carriers","25% affected, 50% carriers"],["X-linked recessive","X-linked ichthyosis","Carrier mother, unaffected father","Sons 50% affected; daughters 50% carriers"],["X-linked recessive","X-linked ichthyosis","Affected father, noncarrier mother","No affected sons; all daughters carriers"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Gas and brakes","text":"Oncogenes such as BRAF and NRAS are a stuck gas pedal (one hit is enough). Tumor suppressors such as TP53, PTCH1, CDKN2A, NF1, and TSC1/2 are brakes (both copies must fail)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ras off and mTOR off","text":"Neurofibromin turns Ras off and hamartin-tuberin turns mTOR off. Lose either and the growth signal stays on, so benign tumors appear in skin and nerves."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fine scale or dirty scale","text":"Fine white scale that spares the flexures in either sex points to filaggrin (ichthyosis vulgaris). Large dark brown scale in a boy points to steroid sulfatase (X-linked ichthyosis)."}],"highYield":["UV-signature C to T mutations in TP53 are the earliest step toward actinic keratosis and squamous cell carcinoma.","Xeroderma pigmentosum is a nucleotide excision repair defect with skin cancers in childhood.","PTCH1 loss and hedgehog activation underlie basal cell carcinoma and Gorlin syndrome.","BRAF V600E is common in both benign nevi and melanoma.","Germline CDKN2A (p16) mutation causes familial melanoma with dysplastic nevi and pancreatic cancer risk.","NF1 is on chromosome 17 and neurofibromin is a Ras GTPase-activating protein.","Ash-leaf macules are often the first sign of tuberous sclerosis.","Filaggrin loss raises atopic dermatitis risk; steroid sulfatase loss causes X-linked ichthyosis in boys."],"pitfalls":["BRAF V600E alone does not mean melanoma; it is also in most acquired nevi.","NF1 lesions are café-au-lait macules (extra pigment); tuberous sclerosis gives ash-leaf macules (less pigment).","In X-linked recessive inheritance an affected father cannot pass the disease to his sons.","Albinism reduces melanin synthesis in normal numbers of melanocytes; MC1R variants keep melanin synthesis going but shift it toward pheomelanin."],"dumps":[{"t":"Checkpoint and UV damage","p":"Draw the G1 to S checkpoint with cyclin D, CDK4/6, Rb, E2F, p16, p53, and p21. Then draw a UV photon forming a pyrimidine dimer and show how nucleotide excision repair removes it and what happens when it cannot.","l":["Cyclin D with CDK4/6 phosphorylates Rb to release E2F","p16 blocks CDK4/6","p53 induces p21 for G1 arrest or triggers apoptosis","UVB forms cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers","C to T signature mutations in TP53 in actinic keratosis and squamous cell carcinoma","Nucleotide excision repair: recognize, unwind, cut both sides, fill, seal","Xeroderma pigmentosum: autosomal recessive repair defect with childhood skin cancers"]},{"t":"Skin gene table with pedigrees","p":"Make a table of skin disorders with gene, protein role, and inheritance. Then draw three small pedigrees (autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, X-linked recessive) and write the recurrence risk under each.","l":["NF1: neurofibromin, Ras GAP, chromosome 17, autosomal dominant","Tuberous sclerosis: hamartin and tuberin, mTOR, autosomal dominant","PTCH1 and Gorlin syndrome; CDKN2A and familial melanoma","Oculocutaneous albinism and xeroderma pigmentosum: autosomal recessive","X-linked ichthyosis: steroid sulfatase, X-linked recessive","Autosomal dominant risk 50%; autosomal recessive carrier cross 25% affected","X-linked recessive: carrier mother gives 50% of sons affected; no male-to-male transmission","FLG and MC1R as common variants that raise disease risk"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia (sections on cell cycle regulation and tumor suppressor genes)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin (sections on melanocytic, epidermal, and adnexal tumors)"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on DNA repair"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-6","IN-2","IN-13-G1","IN-13-G2","IN-13-G3","IN-13-F1","IN-13-F2","IN-13-E1","IN-13-B1"]},"IN-8":{"id":"IN-8","summary":"How cells respond to stress: they adapt, get injured, die by necrosis or apoptosis, collect pigments or deposits, or turn neoplastic. The skin is a handy place to see all of these, and the exam asks you to match a change to its mechanism and its setting.","notes":[{"h":"Adaptations","points":["Hypertrophy: bigger cells, more structural protein; seen in cells that cannot divide (cardiac and skeletal muscle).","Hyperplasia: more cells, from stem cell or mature cell division; callus is epidermal hyperplasia from friction.","Atrophy: smaller cells from less protein synthesis plus ubiquitin-proteasome breakdown and autophagy.","Metaplasia: one mature cell type replaced by another better able to handle stress, by reprogramming stem cells.","Squamous metaplasia of bronchi in smokers; intestinal columnar metaplasia of the esophagus in Barrett esophagus.","Metaplasia and pathologic hyperplasia (endometrial hyperplasia from unopposed estrogen) can progress to dysplasia and carcinoma; benign prostatic hyperplasia does not."]},{"h":"How cells are injured","points":["ATP depletion stops the Na+/K+ ATPase, so sodium and water enter and the cell swells.","Anaerobic glycolysis builds lactic acid; low pH clumps chromatin.","Ribosomes detach from rough ER, so protein synthesis falls.","Calcium influx activates phospholipases, proteases, endonucleases, and ATPases, and opens the mitochondrial permeability transition pore.","Reactive oxygen species cause lipid peroxidation of membranes, protein oxidation, and DNA breaks.","Reversible: swelling, fatty change, membrane blebbing, ER dilation, ribosome detachment.","Irreversible: plasma membrane rupture (enzymes such as troponin leak into blood), lysosomal rupture, amorphous mitochondrial densities, pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis."]},{"h":"Apoptosis versus necrosis","points":["Intrinsic path: DNA damage or loss of survival signals lets BAX and BAK open the outer mitochondrial membrane; BCL-2 normally blocks this.","Cytochrome c leaves the mitochondrion, binds APAF-1, and activates caspase-9.","Extrinsic path: Fas ligand binds Fas (CD95) or TNF binds TNF receptor 1, recruiting adaptor proteins that activate caspase-8.","Cytotoxic T cells use perforin to let granzyme B into the target, where it activates caspases directly.","Executioner caspases 3 and 7 finish the job in all three routes.","Apoptosis: cell shrinks, chromatin condenses, apoptotic bodies form and are eaten, membrane stays intact, no inflammation.","Necrosis: cell swells, membrane ruptures, contents leak, inflammation follows."]},{"h":"Pigments and deposits","points":["Lipofuscin: yellow-brown wear and tear pigment from lipid peroxidation, in aging heart, liver, and neurons.","Hemosiderin: golden-brown iron storage pigment that stains blue with Prussian blue; seen in bruises and iron overload.","Melanin (made from tyrosine in melanocytes) and bilirubin (from heme breakdown, seen as jaundice) are the other key endogenous pigments.","Exogenous pigments: inhaled carbon in lung macrophages and hilar nodes (anthracosis); tattoo ink held for life in dermal macrophages.","Dystrophic calcification: in dead or damaged tissue with normal serum calcium (atheromas, damaged valves, caseous necrosis, psammoma bodies).","Metastatic calcification: in normal tissue when serum calcium or phosphate is high (hyperparathyroidism, chronic kidney disease, vitamin D excess); favors lung, kidney, gastric mucosa, and vessels.","Infiltrates: fat in hepatocytes (steatosis, from alcohol or obesity); cholesterol in macrophages (foam cells in atheromas, skin and tendon xanthomas).","Amyloid: misfolded protein in beta-pleated sheets; Congo red stain shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light."]},{"h":"Neoplasia","points":["Benign: well differentiated, slow, often encapsulated, no invasion, no metastasis.","Anaplasia: pleomorphism, large dark nuclei, high nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio, prominent nucleoli, atypical mitoses, tumor giant cells.","Metastasis is the one feature that proves a tumor is malignant.","Dysplasia is disordered growth with loss of orientation; carcinoma in situ is full-thickness dysplasia with an intact basement membrane.","Grade describes how differentiated the cells look; stage (TNM) describes size and spread and predicts outcome better.","Hallmarks include oncogene signaling (BRAF V600E in melanoma), two-hit loss of RB and TP53, BCL-2 overexpression, telomerase, VEGF angiogenesis, invasion, Warburg aerobic glycolysis, and immune evasion (PD-L1)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Types of necrosis","cols":["Type","Classic setting","What it looks like"],"rows":[["Coagulative","Myocardial infarct, ischemia of solid organs (not brain)","Cell outlines preserved, eosinophilic cells without nuclei"],["Liquefactive","Brain infarct, bacterial abscess","Tissue digested by enzymes into fluid"],["Caseous","Tuberculosis, some fungal infections","Cheese-like, granular debris inside a granuloma"],["Fat","Acute pancreatitis (enzymatic), breast trauma","Lipase frees fatty acids that bind calcium (saponification), chalky white"],["Fibrinoid","Vasculitis, malignant hypertension","Bright pink immune complexes and fibrin in vessel walls"],["Gangrenous","Limb ischemia","Dry: coagulative; wet: infection adds liquefactive change"]]},{"title":"Tumor naming","cols":["Tissue","Benign","Malignant"],"rows":[["Squamous epithelium","Squamous papilloma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],["Glandular epithelium","Adenoma","Adenocarcinoma"],["Fat","Lipoma","Liposarcoma"],["Smooth muscle","Leiomyoma","Leiomyosarcoma"],["Melanocytes","Nevus","Melanoma (malignant despite -oma)"],["Lymphocytes","None","Lymphoma (malignant despite -oma)"]]},{"title":"Tumor suppressors and oncogenes to know","cols":["Gene","Normal job","In cancer"],"rows":[["RB","Holds E2F, blocking G1 to S until CDK4/6 and cyclin D phosphorylate it","Both copies lost (two hits), cell cycle runs free"],["TP53","Senses DNA damage; p21 arrests G1, BAX triggers apoptosis","Both copies lost; damaged cells keep dividing"],["BRAF","Kinase in the MAP kinase growth pathway","V600E keeps it switched on (melanoma, nevi)"],["BCL2","Blocks mitochondrial apoptosis","Overexpressed, cells refuse to die (follicular lymphoma)"],["Telomerase (TERT)","Maintains telomeres in stem cells","Reactivated, cells become immortal"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Shrink versus swell","text":"Apoptosis shrinks the cell and stays quiet; necrosis swells the cell, bursts it, and calls in inflammation."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Dystrophic means dead","text":"Dystrophic calcification lands on dead or damaged tissue with normal blood calcium. Metastatic calcification lands on normal tissue because blood calcium or phosphate is high."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Grade is the look, stage is the spread","text":"Grade comes from the microscope (differentiation). Stage comes from the whole patient (size, nodes, metastasis) and matters more for prognosis."}],"highYield":["Brain infarcts undergo liquefactive necrosis; infarcts of the heart, kidney, and spleen undergo coagulative necrosis.","Irreversible injury is marked by membrane damage and the nuclear changes pyknosis, karyorrhexis, and karyolysis.","Cytochrome c plus APAF-1 activates caspase-9 (intrinsic); Fas or TNF activates caspase-8 (extrinsic).","Prussian blue stains hemosiderin; Congo red with apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid.","Tattoo pigment persists because dermal macrophages hold it and do not carry it away.","Carcinoma in situ has not crossed the basement membrane, so it cannot metastasize.","BRAF V600E is the classic activating mutation in melanoma and common nevi."],"pitfalls":["Fatty change and cell swelling are reversible; do not call a cell dead until you see membrane rupture or nuclear breakdown.","Apoptosis also shows pyknosis, but without swelling or inflammation; the setting and the inflammation tell them apart.","Metaplasia is reversible and is not dysplasia; it is the soil in which dysplasia can grow.","Melanoma, lymphoma, mesothelioma, and seminoma end in -oma yet are malignant."],"dumps":[{"t":"Cell injury flowchart","p":"Draw a flowchart starting from low oxygen in a cell. Show how ATP loss leads to reversible changes and then to irreversible injury.","l":["Low oxygen stops oxidative phosphorylation, ATP falls","Na+/K+ ATPase fails, sodium and water enter, cell and ER swell","Anaerobic glycolysis makes lactic acid, pH falls, chromatin clumps","Ribosomes detach, protein synthesis drops, fat accumulates","Calcium enters and activates phospholipases, proteases, and endonucleases","Mitochondrial permeability transition, more ATP loss and cytochrome c release","Membrane rupture and lysosomal enzyme leak mark irreversible injury","Nucleus shows pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis"]},{"t":"Apoptosis pathways","p":"Draw the intrinsic, extrinsic, and cytotoxic T cell routes to apoptosis side by side and label the key proteins in each.","l":["Intrinsic trigger: DNA damage via p53 or loss of growth factor signals","BAX and BAK open the outer mitochondrial membrane; BCL-2 blocks them","Cytochrome c binds APAF-1, activates caspase-9","Extrinsic: Fas ligand to Fas, or TNF to TNF receptor 1, activates caspase-8","Cytotoxic T cell: perforin pore lets granzyme B in, granzyme activates caspases","All routes converge on caspases 3 and 7","Result: shrinkage, chromatin condensation, apoptotic bodies, no inflammation"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell Injury, Cell Death, and Adaptations"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on intracellular accumulations and pathologic calcification"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-6","IN-10","IN-11","IN-13-A","IN-13-G3","IM-15-E"]},"IN-9":{"id":"IN-9","summary":"Healthy skin keeps microbes out with a dry, acidic, shedding surface and a crowd of harmless resident organisms. Infections start when that barrier breaks, and the depth the organism reaches names the disease. The exam also tests toxin-driven skin disease and which host problems invite which organism.","notes":[{"h":"Normal skin flora","points":["Staphylococcus epidermidis: the most common skin resident; coagulase negative.","Cutibacterium acnes: anaerobic gram-positive rod living deep in pilosebaceous units.","Corynebacterium (diphtheroids) in moist areas; Malassezia yeasts in oily, sebaceous areas.","Staphylococcus aureus is carried in the anterior nares by roughly a third of people, a source of later skin infection."]},{"h":"How skin resists colonization","points":["Stratum corneum is a dry, tight physical barrier.","Acidic surface (pH about 4.5 to 5.5) slows growth of many pathogens.","Sebum triglycerides are broken down to free fatty acids that are toxic to many bacteria.","Constant desquamation sheds organisms stuck to surface cells.","Keratinocytes and sweat make antimicrobial peptides: beta-defensins, cathelicidin (LL-37), and dermcidin.","Resident flora compete for nutrients and attachment sites (colonization resistance)."]},{"h":"Stages of a skin infection","points":["Entry through a breach: cut, bite, burn, fissure, insect bite, catheter, or injection.","Adherence: bacterial adhesins bind fibronectin, collagen, or other matrix proteins.","Colonization and invasion: spreading factors (hyaluronidase, streptokinase, DNases) loosen tissue.","Spread through tissue planes or lymphatics; damage comes from toxins and from the host's own inflammation."]},{"h":"Toxin-driven skin disease","points":["Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome: exfoliative toxins A and B are proteases that cleave desmoglein 1.","The split is high in the epidermis (stratum granulosum); Nikolsky sign is positive and mucosa is spared because desmoglein 3 holds it together.","Toxin is made at a distant site (nose, umbilicus), so the blisters are sterile; infants and people with kidney failure clear toxin poorly.","Superantigens (TSST-1, streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins) bind MHC class II and the T cell receptor V-beta region outside the peptide groove.","This activates up to about a fifth of all T cells, releasing a cytokine storm (IL-2, TNF, IFN-gamma) with fever, diffuse red rash, shock, and later desquamation of palms and soles."]},{"h":"Host factors","points":["Diabetes: neuropathy hides wounds, vascular disease limits blood flow, and hyperglycemia weakens neutrophils; foot infections are often polymicrobial.","Venous insufficiency and lymphedema leave protein-rich fluid that clears bacteria poorly, causing recurrent streptococcal cellulitis.","Tinea pedis fissures between toes are a common entry point for leg cellulitis.","Injection drug use: S aureus, including MRSA, abscesses at injection sites.","Neutropenia: Pseudomonas (ecthyma gangrenosum) and molds; pus may be scant.","T cell deficiency (HIV, transplant): severe HSV and VZV, extensive molluscum, mucocutaneous Candida."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Depth of infection","cols":["Disease","Level","Usual organism","Key look"],"rows":[["Impetigo","Superficial epidermis","S aureus, S pyogenes","Honey-colored crusts or bullae, children"],["Folliculitis","Hair follicle","S aureus (Pseudomonas after hot tubs)","Pustules centered on hairs"],["Erysipelas","Upper dermis and superficial lymphatics","S pyogenes","Raised, bright red, sharply demarcated border"],["Cellulitis","Deep dermis and subcutaneous fat","S pyogenes, S aureus","Warm, red, swollen, poorly demarcated"],["Abscess, furuncle","Dermis and subcutis, walled off","S aureus","Fluctuant, tender collection of pus"],["Necrotizing fasciitis","Fascia","S pyogenes or mixed aerobes and anaerobes","Pain out of proportion, rapid spread, crepitus, numb skin"]]},{"title":"Host context and likely organism","cols":["Host context","Likely organism"],"rows":[["Leg cellulitis with lymphedema or venous stasis","Group A or other beta-hemolytic streptococci"],["Injection drug use with abscess","S aureus, often MRSA"],["Diabetic foot ulcer","Mixed: S aureus, streptococci, gram-negative rods, anaerobes"],["Neutropenia with necrotic skin ulcer","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],["T cell deficiency with chronic oral and skin plaques","Candida albicans"],["T cell deficiency with widespread vesicles","HSV or varicella zoster virus"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Depth ladder","text":"Climb down the skin: impetigo (epidermis), erysipelas (upper dermis), cellulitis (deep dermis and fat), necrotizing fasciitis (fascia). The deeper it goes, the less defined the border and the sicker the patient."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Desmoglein 1 sits high","text":"Desmoglein 1 is the main desmoglein of the upper epidermis, so cutting it splits the skin high. Mucosa also has plenty of desmoglein 3, so it stays intact."}],"highYield":["S epidermidis is the most common normal skin organism; C acnes lives in hair follicles.","Erysipelas has a sharp raised border because it sits in the upper dermis and lymphatics.","Exfoliative toxin cleaves desmoglein 1, splitting the stratum granulosum in scalded skin syndrome.","Superantigens link MHC class II to T cell receptor V-beta without antigen processing.","Recurrent leg cellulitis follows lymphedema, venous insufficiency, and tinea pedis fissures.","Neutropenic patients get Pseudomonas skin infection (ecthyma gangrenosum)."],"pitfalls":["Scalded skin syndrome blisters are sterile because toxin, not bacteria, reaches the skin; culture the source, not the blister.","Scalded skin syndrome spares mucosa and splits in the granular layer; toxic epidermal necrolysis involves mucosa and causes full-thickness necrosis.","Erysipelas versus cellulitis: sharp edge and raised border point to erysipelas; a vague edge points to deeper cellulitis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Skin depth and infection","p":"Draw a cross section of skin from stratum corneum to fascia. Label the infection that occurs at each level and its usual organism.","l":["Epidermis: impetigo, S aureus or S pyogenes","Hair follicle: folliculitis, S aureus","Upper dermis and lymphatics: erysipelas, S pyogenes","Deep dermis and subcutaneous fat: cellulitis, streptococci and S aureus","Walled-off pus in dermis or subcutis: abscess, S aureus","Fascia: necrotizing fasciitis, S pyogenes or mixed flora","Note the border gets less distinct as depth increases"]},{"t":"Barrier defenses","p":"List every way normal skin keeps pathogens out, and for each one, name a condition that breaks it.","l":["Stratum corneum barrier: broken by cuts, burns, eczema","Acidic pH: raised by soaps or occlusion","Sebum free fatty acids: low before puberty, one reason tinea capitis is a childhood infection","Desquamation sheds attached microbes","Antimicrobial peptides: defensins, cathelicidin, dermcidin","Resident flora compete; broad-spectrum antibiotics remove them","Immune cells: neutropenia and T cell defects remove the second line"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on normal human microbiota"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on staphylococcal toxins and superantigens"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-1","IN-12","IN-11","IN-13-H3","IN-13-H4","IN-13-H7","IN-13-H8"]},"IN-10":{"id":"IN-10","summary":"Skin wounds heal in a fixed order: clot, inflammation, granulation tissue, then remodeling into a scar. This unit covers what speeds or stalls that process, abnormal scars, and the special injuries from burns, electricity, radiation, pressure, and brown recluse venom.","notes":[{"h":"Phases of wound healing","points":["Hemostasis (minutes): vasoconstriction, platelet plug, and fibrin clot; platelets release PDGF and TGF-beta.","Inflammation (days 1 to 3): neutrophils arrive first, then macrophages dominate by about day 3 and clear debris and release growth factors.","Proliferation (about day 3 to week 3): granulation tissue of fibroblasts, new capillaries (VEGF), and type III collagen; keratinocytes migrate to cover the gap.","Remodeling (weeks to a year): type III collagen is replaced by type I; zinc-dependent matrix metalloproteinases reshape the matrix.","Strength is about 10 percent of normal at 1 week and plateaus at about 70 to 80 percent by 3 months."]},{"h":"Primary versus secondary intention","points":["Primary intention: clean wound with edges brought together; little granulation tissue and a thin scar.","Secondary intention: large gap; more inflammation, much more granulation tissue, and contraction by myofibroblasts.","Delayed healing: infection (most common local cause), diabetes, ischemia, glucocorticoids, malnutrition, foreign bodies, and movement or pressure.","Vitamin C is a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase; without it collagen cannot form stable triple helices.","Zinc is a cofactor for matrix metalloproteinases and for cell proliferation.","Glucocorticoids blunt inflammation and collagen synthesis, giving weak scars."]},{"h":"Burn complications","points":["Only partial-thickness and full-thickness burns count toward burned surface area.","Hypovolemic burn shock: capillary leak moves plasma out of vessels in the first 24 to 48 hours.","Infection: Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the classic cause of burn wound sepsis.","Hypermetabolism: catecholamines, cortisol, and glucagon raise energy use and break down muscle.","Inhalation injury: facial burns, singed nasal hair, soot, hoarseness; carbon monoxide and cyanide poisoning.","Late: contractures over joints and squamous cell carcinoma in a chronic burn scar (Marjolin ulcer)."]},{"h":"Electrical and radiation injury","points":["Skin resists current, so entry and exit wounds look small, but current runs along nerves, vessels, and muscle and heats deep tissue.","Deep muscle necrosis causes compartment syndrome and rhabdomyolysis; myoglobin in urine injures the kidney.","Alternating current tends to cause ventricular fibrillation; lightning tends to cause asystole and cardiac arrest.","Lightning can leave Lichtenberg figures: branching, fern-like red skin marks that fade within about a day.","Ionizing radiation breaks DNA directly and, more often, through hydroxyl radicals made from water.","Acute radiation dermatitis: erythema, then dry and moist desquamation; chronic: atrophy, telangiectasia, fibrosis, and obliterative endarteritis.","Years later: basal cell and squamous cell carcinoma, and angiosarcoma in the radiation field."]},{"h":"Pressure, ear trauma, and spider bites","points":["Pressure over bony prominences (sacrum, ischial tuberosities, greater trochanters, heels) above capillary pressure causes ischemia; shear tears deep vessels.","Muscle is more sensitive to ischemia than skin, so damage under the surface is often worse than it looks.","Cauliflower ear: blunt trauma causes a hematoma that lifts the perichondrium off cartilage; avascular cartilage loses its blood supply, dies, and is replaced by fibrous tissue and irregular new cartilage.","Brown recluse venom contains sphingomyelinase D, which damages cell membranes and triggers complement, neutrophils, and small-vessel thrombosis.","The bite becomes a red, white, and blue lesion with a central necrotic ulcer; systemic hemolysis is rare."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Burn depth","cols":["Depth","Layers involved","Look and blanching","Sensation","Healing"],"rows":[["Superficial","Epidermis","Red, dry, blanches, no blisters","Painful","About a week, no scar"],["Superficial partial-thickness","Epidermis and papillary dermis","Blisters, moist, pink, blanches","Very painful","About 2 to 3 weeks, little scar"],["Deep partial-thickness","Into reticular dermis","Blisters, white or mottled, blanches poorly","Reduced, pressure felt","Over 3 weeks, scars, often grafted"],["Full-thickness","Entire dermis","White, leathery, or charred; no blanching","Painless","Only from edges; needs grafting"]]},{"title":"Rule of nines (adult)","cols":["Region","Percent of body surface"],"rows":[["Head and neck","9"],["Each arm","9"],["Front of trunk","18"],["Back of trunk","18"],["Each leg","18"],["Perineum","1"]]},{"title":"Abnormal scars and wound problems","cols":["Problem","Collagen or tissue","Key features"],"rows":[["Hypertrophic scar","Excess type III collagen in parallel bundles","Stays inside the wound edges, may regress"],["Keloid","Excess disorganized type I and III collagen","Grows beyond the wound, earlobes, chest, shoulders, darker skin, recurs after excision"],["Exuberant granulation tissue","Excess granulation tissue","Bulges above the skin and blocks re-epithelialization"],["Dehiscence","Weak early scar","Wound splits open, often abdominal, from coughing, vomiting, or ileus"],["Contracture","Excess myofibroblast contraction","Deforming scar, common after burns over joints, palms, and soles"]]},{"title":"Pressure injury stages","cols":["Stage","Findings"],"rows":[["1","Intact skin with nonblanching erythema"],["2","Partial-thickness loss with exposed dermis or an intact or ruptured serum blister"],["3","Full-thickness skin loss with visible fat; no bone, tendon, or muscle"],["4","Full-thickness loss with exposed fascia, muscle, tendon, cartilage, or bone"],["Unstageable","Base hidden by slough or eschar"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Three goes to one","text":"Granulation tissue lays down type III collagen; remodeling swaps it for strong type I. A scar never gets back past about 80 percent of normal strength."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Keloid keeps going","text":"A hypertrophic scar respects the wound border; a keloid crosses it. If the scar is bigger than the cut, think keloid."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Small hole, big damage","text":"With electrical injury, judge the patient by the muscle and the urine, not by the size of the skin wound. Dark urine with a positive blood dipstick and few red cells suggests myoglobin."}],"highYield":["Macrophages are the key cell of wound healing; neutrophils come first but macrophages drive repair from about day 3.","Vitamin C deficiency stops collagen hydroxylation, so wounds fail and old scars can reopen.","Full-thickness burns are painless because dermal nerve endings are destroyed.","Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the classic organism of burn wound sepsis.","Electrical injury causes rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuric acute kidney injury.","Chronic radiation damage includes obliterative endarteritis, which starves tissue and stalls healing.","Cauliflower ear results from a hematoma separating perichondrium from avascular cartilage.","Brown recluse venom is sphingomyelinase D, causing a necrotic ulcer."],"pitfalls":["Superficial burns are left out of the rule of nines total; count only partial-thickness and full-thickness areas.","Painless burns are not mild; loss of pain means full-thickness destruction of nerves.","A stage 2 pressure injury is partial thickness; once fat shows it is at least stage 3.","Black widow venom (alpha-latrotoxin) is neurotoxic with muscle cramps; brown recluse venom causes skin necrosis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Wound healing timeline","p":"Draw a timeline from the moment of a skin cut to one year later. Mark each phase, its main cells, and the main molecules.","l":["Hemostasis: platelets, fibrin, PDGF and TGF-beta release","Neutrophils in the first 1 to 2 days","Macrophages dominate by about day 3","Granulation tissue: fibroblasts, VEGF-driven capillaries, type III collagen","Re-epithelialization from wound edges","Myofibroblast contraction, most in secondary intention","Remodeling: type III to type I collagen, zinc-dependent metalloproteinases","Strength reaches only about 70 to 80 percent of normal"]},{"t":"Burn depth comparison","p":"Build a table comparing the four burn depths by layers involved, blisters, blanching, sensation, and healing. Then list the main complications of a large burn.","l":["Superficial: epidermis, no blisters, painful, heals without scar","Superficial partial-thickness: blisters, blanches, very painful","Deep partial-thickness: blisters, poor blanching, reduced sensation, scars","Full-thickness: no blanching, painless, needs grafting","Hypovolemic burn shock from capillary leak","Pseudomonas sepsis","Hypermetabolism with muscle wasting","Inhalation injury, carbon monoxide, contractures"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on tissue repair and wound healing"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on collagen synthesis and vitamin C"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-1","IN-5","IN-8","IN-9","IN-13-G2","IN-13-H12"]},"IN-11":{"id":"IN-11","summary":"The skin is an immune organ: keratinocytes, mast cells, and Langerhans cells sense danger and start both innate and adaptive responses. This unit links those responses to inflammation, granulomas, the four hypersensitivity types, autoimmune skin signs, tumor immunity, and vaccines.","notes":[{"h":"Innate defense in the skin","points":["Keratinocytes carry Toll-like receptors (TLR2 for gram-positive cell wall parts, TLR4 for LPS) and release IL-1 and TNF.","Keratinocytes secrete antimicrobial peptides: beta-defensins and cathelicidin (LL-37).","Mast cells sit beside dermal vessels and release histamine, TNF, and lipid mediators when triggered.","Langerhans cells (CD1a, langerin, Birbeck granules) catch antigen in the epidermis.","After capture they express CCR7, travel in afferent lymphatics to the regional node, and present peptide on MHC II (and MHC I) with B7 costimulation to prime naive T cells."]},{"h":"Acute inflammation steps","points":["Arteriolar dilation (histamine, prostaglandins, nitric oxide) brings redness and heat.","Postcapillary venules leak (histamine, bradykinin, leukotrienes C4, D4, E4) causing edema.","Rolling: E-selectin and P-selectin on endothelium bind sialyl Lewis X on leukocytes.","Firm adhesion: leukocyte beta-2 integrins (LFA-1, Mac-1) bind ICAM-1 on endothelium.","Transmigration through the venule wall uses PECAM-1 (CD31).","Chemotaxis toward C5a, LTB4, IL-8, and bacterial peptides.","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1: CD18 defect, very high blood neutrophils, infections without pus, poor wound healing, delayed umbilical cord separation."]},{"h":"Outcomes and granulomas","points":["Resolution when the cause is removed; macrophages clear dead neutrophils.","Abscess when pyogenic bacteria (S aureus) cause liquefactive necrosis walled off by granulation tissue.","Chronic inflammation when the cause persists: macrophages, lymphocytes, plasma cells, and fibrosis.","Granuloma: macrophages make IL-12, which drives Th1 cells; Th1 cells make IFN-gamma, which turns macrophages into epithelioid cells and giant cells; TNF keeps it intact.","Caseating granulomas: tuberculosis and some fungi. Noncaseating: sarcoidosis, Crohn disease, foreign bodies."]},{"h":"Autoimmune skin signs","points":["Lupus: photosensitive malar rash sparing nasolabial folds; discoid lesions scar and plug follicles.","Lupus skin biopsy: interface dermatitis with granular IgG, IgM, and C3 along the dermal-epidermal junction.","Dermatomyositis: Gottron papules over knuckles, heliotrope periorbital rash, proximal muscle weakness, raised cancer risk.","Systemic sclerosis: sclerodactyly and Raynaud; anticentromere in limited (CREST) disease, anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I) in diffuse disease with lung fibrosis."]},{"h":"Tumor immunity and vaccines","points":["CD8 T cells kill tumor cells showing mutant or viral peptides on MHC I; NK cells kill cells that have lost MHC I.","Tumors escape by dropping MHC I and by expressing PD-L1, which binds PD-1 and switches T cells off.","Transplant recipients on lifelong immunosuppression get many cutaneous squamous cell carcinomas, often outnumbering basal cell carcinomas.","HPV vaccine: recombinant L1 capsid protein self-assembles into virus-like particles with no DNA, inducing neutralizing IgG.","Live attenuated varicella vaccine replicates, so it induces antibody plus CD8 and CD4 T cell immunity.","Toxoid booster given to someone with high antitoxin IgG can cause an Arthus reaction: local immune complexes, complement, neutrophils, painful swelling within hours."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypersensitivity in the skin","cols":["Type","Mechanism","Skin example","Timing"],"rows":[["I","Allergen cross-links IgE on mast cells; histamine, leukotrienes","Urticaria, angioedema","Minutes, late phase hours"],["II","IgG against fixed skin antigens","Pemphigus (desmoglein), bullous pemphigoid (BP180, BP230)","Variable, depends on antibody level"],["III","Immune complexes in vessel walls activate complement, neutrophils","Arthus reaction, leukocytoclastic vasculitis (palpable purpura)","Arthus 4 to 12 hours; serum sickness 1 to 2 weeks"],["IV","Sensitized T cells release cytokines or kill","Allergic contact dermatitis, tuberculin skin test","48 to 72 hours"]]},{"title":"Leukocyte recruitment","cols":["Step","Endothelial molecule","Leukocyte molecule"],"rows":[["Rolling","E-selectin, P-selectin","Sialyl Lewis X"],["Rolling (naive lymphocytes in nodes)","Addressins on high endothelial venules","L-selectin"],["Firm adhesion","ICAM-1","LFA-1 and Mac-1 (CD18 integrins)"],["Transmigration","PECAM-1","PECAM-1"],["Chemotaxis","Bound chemokines","Receptors for C5a, LTB4, IL-8"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ACID","text":"Hypersensitivity types I to IV: Anaphylactic and Atopic (I), Cytotoxic antibody (II), Immune complex (III), Delayed cell-mediated (IV)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CREST","text":"Limited systemic sclerosis: Calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasia. Anticentromere antibody."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Roll, stick, squeeze, follow","text":"Selectins roll, integrins stick to ICAM-1, PECAM-1 squeezes through, chemokines and C5a lead the way."}],"highYield":["Langerhans cells carry skin antigen to regional lymph nodes and prime naive T cells.","LAD type 1 is a CD18 defect: neutrophilia, no pus, delayed umbilical cord separation.","IFN-gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages to form granulomas; TNF maintains them.","Allergic contact dermatitis and the tuberculin test are type IV reactions peaking at 48 to 72 hours.","Lupus skin shows granular immune deposits at the dermal-epidermal junction.","Anti-Scl-70 marks diffuse systemic sclerosis; anticentromere marks limited disease.","Loss of MHC I hides tumors from CD8 T cells but exposes them to NK cells.","The HPV vaccine contains no viral DNA; it is L1 virus-like particles."],"pitfalls":["Pemphigus and bullous pemphigoid are antibody-mediated (type II); delayed T cell reactions are type IV.","LAD type 1 raises the blood neutrophil count; patients have many neutrophils that cannot leave the vessels.","An Arthus reaction is antibody and complement driven (type III), unlike a delayed T cell reaction (type IV), and it peaks within hours.","Dermatomyositis skin findings can appear before muscle weakness; screen for an underlying cancer."],"dumps":[{"t":"Neutrophil leaving a vessel","p":"Draw a postcapillary venule and a neutrophil leaving it. Label each step and the molecules on both cells.","l":["Vasodilation and venular leak from histamine, bradykinin, leukotrienes","Margination of leukocytes to the wall","Rolling on E-selectin and P-selectin via sialyl Lewis X","Chemokines activate integrins","Firm adhesion of LFA-1 and Mac-1 (CD18) to ICAM-1","Transmigration with PECAM-1","Chemotaxis to C5a, LTB4, IL-8","LAD type 1: CD18 defect blocks firm adhesion"]},{"t":"Hypersensitivity table","p":"Build a table of the four hypersensitivity types with mechanism, key mediators, timing, and one skin example each.","l":["Type I: IgE, mast cells, histamine, minutes, urticaria","Type II: IgG to fixed antigen, pemphigus and bullous pemphigoid","Type III: immune complexes, complement, neutrophils","Type III examples: Arthus reaction in hours, leukocytoclastic vasculitis","Type IV: T cells, cytokines, 48 to 72 hours","Type IV examples: contact dermatitis, tuberculin test","Only type IV is antibody independent"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on innate immunity"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on immune responses against tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Inflammation and Repair"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-1","IN-13-B","IN-13-D","IM-5","IM-13","IM-15-C","IM-15-D1"]},"IN-12":{"id":"IN-12","summary":"The organisms behind common skin disease: staph and strep, dermatophytes and yeasts, the warts and pox viruses, the herpesviruses, and the vector-borne infections that show up first on the skin. The exam asks you to identify each from lab clues and to explain how its virulence factors make the disease.","notes":[{"h":"Staphylococcus aureus","points":["Gram-positive cocci in clusters; catalase positive, coagulase positive.","Protein A binds the Fc end of IgG, blocking opsonization and complement.","Panton-Valentine leukocidin forms pores in leukocytes and is linked to necrotic community MRSA abscesses.","Exfoliative toxins cleave desmoglein 1; TSST-1 is a superantigen (toxic shock syndrome).","MRSA carries mecA, which encodes PBP2a, a penicillin-binding protein with low affinity for beta-lactams."]},{"h":"Streptococcus pyogenes (group A)","points":["Gram-positive cocci in chains; beta-hemolytic, catalase negative, bacitracin sensitive, PYR positive.","M protein blocks complement deposition and phagocytosis; the hyaluronic acid capsule looks like host tissue and resists phagocytosis.","Streptolysin O lyses cells and is immunogenic (ASO titer); streptokinase turns plasminogen to plasmin and dissolves fibrin.","Pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens: scarlet fever and streptococcal toxic shock.","Skin infection by nephritogenic strains can cause glomerulonephritis; rheumatic fever follows only pharyngitis, because the rheumatogenic M types are throat strains."]},{"h":"Fungi in skin","points":["Dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton) make keratinases and live only in dead keratin: stratum corneum, hair, and nails.","KOH prep of scale shows branching septate hyphae.","Malassezia: lipophilic yeast; tinea versicolor gives hypopigmented or hyperpigmented scaly patches on the upper trunk.","Malassezia KOH prep: short hyphae with round yeasts (spaghetti and meatballs); its azelaic acid inhibits tyrosinase, causing pale spots.","Candida albicans: budding yeast and pseudohyphae; germ tube positive at 37 °C (98.6 °F).","Candida risks: moisture, diabetes, antibiotics that remove competing flora, glucocorticoids; T cell (Th17) defects cause mucocutaneous disease, neutropenia allows invasive disease."]},{"h":"Warts, molluscum, and herpes","points":["HPV: nonenveloped, circular double-stranded DNA; infects basal keratinocytes through tiny breaks and replicates as cells mature upward.","Koilocytes: keratinocytes with a clear halo around a wrinkled nucleus.","High-risk HPV (16, 18): E6 sends p53 for degradation and E7 binds Rb, releasing E2F.","Molluscum contagiosum: poxvirus, enveloped double-stranded DNA that replicates in the cytoplasm; umbilicated papules with molluscum bodies (large eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions).","HSV-1, HSV-2, and VZV: enveloped double-stranded DNA; infect epithelium, travel up sensory axons, and stay latent in ganglia; reactivation gives herpes labialis, genital herpes, or dermatomal zoster.","Tzanck smear of a vesicle base shows multinucleated giant cells in both HSV and VZV."]},{"h":"Acne and hot tub organisms","points":["Cutibacterium acnes: gram-positive anaerobic rod; lipases break sebum into irritating free fatty acids.","C acnes also activates TLR2 on monocytes and keratinocytes, releasing IL-1, IL-8, and IL-12 and drawing in neutrophils.","Pseudomonas aeruginosa: gram-negative rod, oxidase positive, non-lactose fermenter; grows in poorly chlorinated warm water.","Hot tub folliculitis: itchy follicular pustules in areas covered by a swimsuit, a day or two after exposure."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Dermatophyte genera","cols":["Genus","Skin","Hair","Nails"],"rows":[["Trichophyton","Yes","Yes","Yes"],["Microsporum","Yes","Yes","No"],["Epidermophyton","Yes","No","Yes"]]},{"title":"Skin viruses compared","cols":["Virus","Genome and envelope","Where it replicates","Hallmark finding","Spread"],"rows":[["HPV","Circular dsDNA, no envelope","Nucleus of maturing keratinocytes","Koilocytes","Skin contact, autoinoculation, sexual"],["Molluscum contagiosum","Linear dsDNA, enveloped poxvirus","Cytoplasm","Molluscum bodies, umbilicated papules","Skin contact, fomites, sexual in adults"],["HSV-1 and HSV-2","Linear dsDNA, enveloped","Nucleus","Multinucleated giant cells; latency in trigeminal or sacral ganglia","Contact with lesions or shedding"],["Varicella zoster virus","Linear dsDNA, enveloped","Nucleus","Multinucleated giant cells; latency in dorsal root and cranial ganglia","Respiratory droplets and lesion contact"]]},{"title":"Vector-borne infections with skin signs","cols":["Organism","Vector or source","Skin finding"],"rows":[["Borrelia burgdorferi","Ixodes tick","Erythema migrans: expanding red ring at the bite"],["Rickettsia rickettsii","Dermacentor tick","Rash starts at wrists and ankles, spreads to trunk, involves palms and soles"],["Leishmania","Sandfly","Painless papule that becomes an ulcer with a raised border"],["Dog and cat hookworm larvae (Ancylostoma)","Larvae in sand or soil contaminated by animal feces, enter bare skin","Itchy, serpiginous, slowly moving track (cutaneous larva migrans)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Coagulase sorts the staph","text":"Coagulase positive means S aureus. Coagulase negative usually means S epidermidis, a skin resident that causes trouble mostly on catheters and implants."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Epidermophyton skips hair, Microsporum skips nails","text":"Trichophyton hits skin, hair, and nails. Each of the other two skips one: Epidermophyton never hair, Microsporum never nails."},{"kind":"trick","label":"E6 for p53, E7 for Rb","text":"Pair the numbers in order: 6 comes before 7, and p53 is named before Rb in the phrase 'p53 and Rb'."}],"highYield":["mecA encodes PBP2a, the basis of MRSA beta-lactam resistance.","Protein A binds IgG Fc; M protein blocks complement; both defeat opsonization.","Group A strep skin infection can cause glomerulonephritis but not rheumatic fever.","Dermatophytes stay in dead keratin; KOH shows septate hyphae.","Tinea versicolor KOH: spaghetti and meatballs.","Candida forms pseudohyphae and is germ tube positive.","HSV-1 hides in the trigeminal ganglion, HSV-2 in sacral ganglia, VZV in dorsal root ganglia.","Rocky Mountain spotted fever rash moves from wrists and ankles inward to the trunk and involves palms and soles."],"pitfalls":["Tzanck smear cannot tell HSV from VZV; both show multinucleated giant cells.","Molluscum replicates in the cytoplasm, unlike most DNA viruses, because poxviruses carry their own RNA polymerase.","Tinea versicolor is caused by Malassezia, a yeast. Dermatophytes play no part despite the name.","Cutaneous larva migrans has no insect vector; larvae burrow directly into bare skin."],"dumps":[{"t":"Gram-positive cocci sorting","p":"Draw a flowchart that sorts gram-positive cocci from a skin infection down to S aureus, S epidermidis, and S pyogenes. Add each organism's main virulence factors.","l":["Catalase positive (clusters) versus catalase negative (chains)","Coagulase positive: S aureus; coagulase negative: S epidermidis","S aureus factors: protein A, PVL, exfoliative toxins, TSST-1","MRSA: mecA encodes PBP2a","Catalase negative, beta-hemolytic, bacitracin sensitive, PYR positive: S pyogenes","S pyogenes factors: M protein, hyaluronic acid capsule, streptolysin O, streptokinase, pyrogenic exotoxins","Skin strains can cause glomerulonephritis; rheumatic fever follows only pharyngitis"]},{"t":"Herpes latency cycle","p":"Draw the path of HSV from a lip lesion to the ganglion and back. Label where latency happens and what triggers reactivation.","l":["Primary infection of mucosal epithelium, vesicles on a red base","Virus enters sensory nerve endings","Retrograde axonal transport to the trigeminal ganglion (HSV-1)","Latency in the neuron nucleus with no virus production","Triggers: stress, fever, UV light, immunosuppression","Anterograde transport back to the same skin area","Tzanck smear shows multinucleated giant cells","Parallel: HSV-2 in sacral ganglia, VZV in dorsal root ganglia with dermatomal zoster"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on streptococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on cutaneous and superficial mycoses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on herpesviruses"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-9","IN-11","IN-13-H","IN-13-G2","IM-15-F9","HE-9-E1"]},"IN-13-A":{"id":"IN-13-A","summary":"Pigment problems come from too many melanocytes, too much melanin, or missing melanocytes. This unit covers the common mole (nevocellular nevus) and vitiligo, and the look-alikes the exam uses as distractors: albinism, tinea versicolor, freckles, lentigines, melasma, and the bronze skin of Addison disease.","notes":[{"h":"Nevocellular nevus: how it forms","points":["A benign tumor of melanocytes that grow in nests (nevus cells).","Starts with an activating BRAF (often V600E) or NRAS mutation that drives growth.","Growth then stops through oncogene-induced senescence, with p16 build-up holding the cells in arrest.","Nevi evolve with age: junctional in children, compound in young adults, intradermal in older adults.","Maturation with depth: superficial cells are larger, nested, and pigmented; deeper cells are smaller, less pigmented, and spindle shaped.","Loss of maturation with depth is a warning sign for melanoma."]},{"h":"Benign nevus or melanoma","points":["Benign nevus: small, symmetric, sharp border, one even color, stable over time.","Melanoma warning signs follow ABCDE: asymmetry, irregular border, color variation, diameter over 6 mm, evolution.","A mole that looks different from the patient's other moles (ugly duckling) deserves a biopsy.","Large congenital melanocytic nevi carry a higher lifetime melanoma risk; small congenital nevi carry little added risk."]},{"h":"Vitiligo","points":["Autoimmune: CD8 T cells destroy melanocytes, driven by IFN-gamma signaling.","Sharply demarcated, milk-white depigmented macules, often symmetric around the mouth, eyes, hands, genitals, and bony points.","Wood lamp makes patches bright blue-white and easier to see.","Koebner phenomenon: new patches appear at sites of skin injury.","Biopsy: melanocytes absent from the basal layer.","Linked to autoimmune thyroid disease (most common), type 1 diabetes, pernicious anemia, and Addison disease."]},{"h":"Freckles, lentigines, melasma","points":["Ephelides (freckles): normal number of melanocytes making more melanin; darken in summer and fade in winter.","Lentigo simplex: more melanocytes along the basal layer; does not darken with sun.","Solar lentigines: more basal melanocytes and melanin in chronically sun-exposed skin of older adults; they do not fade.","Melasma: symmetric brown patches on cheeks, forehead, and upper lip with pregnancy or estrogen; sun makes it worse."]},{"h":"Addison disease and pigment","points":["Primary adrenal insufficiency lowers cortisol, removing feedback, so the pituitary makes much more POMC and ACTH.","ACTH and the MSH sequences cut from POMC stimulate melanocortin 1 receptors on melanocytes.","Result: diffuse hyperpigmentation, strongest in palmar creases, gums and buccal mucosa, scars, and sun-exposed skin.","Secondary adrenal insufficiency has low ACTH, so the skin does not darken."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Nevus types by nest location","cols":["Type","Where the nests are","Look","Typical age"],"rows":[["Junctional","Dermal-epidermal junction","Flat, uniformly dark brown macule","Children"],["Compound","Junction and dermis","Slightly raised brown papule","Adolescents and young adults"],["Intradermal","Dermis only","Dome-shaped, often skin-colored or light tan","Older adults"]]},{"title":"Hypopigmented skin: telling them apart","cols":["Condition","Melanocytes","Distribution","Wood lamp","KOH"],"rows":[["Vitiligo","Absent","Patchy, sharp borders, often symmetric","Bright blue-white accentuation","Negative"],["Oculocutaneous albinism","Normal number, little melanin (often tyrosinase defect)","Whole body from birth, with eye findings","Diffusely pale","Negative"],["Tinea versicolor","Present, melanin production inhibited","Upper trunk, fine scale, may be pale or dark","May show yellow-green or copper-orange fluorescence","Short hyphae and yeast"],["Postinflammatory hypopigmentation","Present, melanin reduced","Sites of earlier rash or injury","Less accentuation than vitiligo","Negative"]]},{"title":"Brown spots compared","cols":["Lesion","Melanocyte number","Sun effect","Setting"],"rows":[["Ephelis (freckle)","Normal","Darkens in sun, fades in winter","Fair-skinned children"],["Lentigo simplex","Increased","No change with sun","Any age, any site"],["Solar lentigo","Increased","Caused by chronic sun, does not fade","Older adults, face and backs of hands"],["Melasma","Not much increased; more melanin","Worsens with sun","Pregnancy, estrogen, oral contraceptives"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ABCDE","text":"Melanoma warning signs: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variation, Diameter over 6 mm, Evolution (change in size, shape, color, or symptoms)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Junction to dermis with age","text":"Nevus cells drop from the junction into the dermis as a person ages: junctional (child), compound (young adult), intradermal (older adult)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Absent versus quiet","text":"In vitiligo the melanocytes are gone. In albinism, tinea versicolor, and postinflammatory hypopigmentation they are present but make less melanin."}],"highYield":["BRAF V600E is the most common driver mutation in common nevi.","Intradermal nevi have nests only in the dermis and are common in older adults.","Large congenital nevi raise melanoma risk.","Vitiligo: CD8 T cells destroy melanocytes; biopsy shows none.","Vitiligo is often seen with autoimmune thyroid disease.","Freckles have a normal number of melanocytes; lentigines have more.","Primary adrenal insufficiency darkens skin through high ACTH and POMC-derived MSH; secondary does not."],"pitfalls":["Vitiligo and albinism both look white; albinism is generalized from birth with eye findings and normal melanocyte numbers.","Tinea versicolor can be darker or lighter than surrounding skin; the fine scale and positive KOH settle it.","A uniform, small, flat dark mole in a child is most likely a junctional nevus; darkness alone does not mean melanoma.","Melasma follows pregnancy or estrogen; freckles follow sun exposure and fade."],"dumps":[{"t":"Three kinds of nevi","p":"Draw three skin cross sections side by side showing a junctional, a compound, and an intradermal nevus. Label where the nests sit and how the cells change with depth.","l":["Junctional: nests at the dermal-epidermal junction, flat lesion","Compound: nests at the junction and in the dermis, raised lesion","Intradermal: nests in the dermis only, dome-shaped and often pale","Age progression from junctional to intradermal","Superficial cells larger, nested, pigmented","Deep cells smaller, less pigment, spindle shaped (maturation)","Start: BRAF or NRAS mutation; stop: oncogene-induced senescence"]},{"t":"White patch workup","p":"Make a table comparing vitiligo, albinism, tinea versicolor, and postinflammatory hypopigmentation by melanocytes, distribution, Wood lamp, KOH, and associations.","l":["Vitiligo: melanocytes absent, CD8 T cell destruction","Vitiligo: bright Wood lamp accentuation, Koebner phenomenon","Vitiligo associations: autoimmune thyroid disease, type 1 diabetes","Albinism: normal melanocytes, defective melanin synthesis, generalized from birth","Tinea versicolor: Malassezia, upper trunk, fine scale, positive KOH","Postinflammatory: at sites of earlier inflammation, melanocytes present","Only vitiligo lacks melanocytes on biopsy"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-1","IN-8","IN-12","IN-13-E1","IN-13-F2","IN-13-G3"]},"IN-13-B":{"id":"IN-13-B","summary":"Acute inflammatory rashes: atopic dermatitis, contact dermatitis, erythema multiforme, and urticaria, plus Stevens-Johnson syndrome, pityriasis rosea, seborrheic dermatitis, and aphthous ulcers. The exam wants the immune mechanism behind each rash and the clue in its distribution, timing, or histology that names it.","notes":[{"h":"Atopic dermatitis","points":["Filaggrin loss-of-function mutations weaken the stratum corneum, so water escapes and allergens and microbes get in.","Th2 inflammation follows: IL-4 and IL-13 drive IgE and further weaken the barrier; IL-31 drives itch.","Infants: face, scalp, and extensor surfaces. Older children and adults: flexures (elbow and knee creases, neck, wrists).","Chronic scratching thickens skin into lichenification.","Part of the atopic triad with allergic rhinitis and asthma.","Complications: S aureus colonization and infection, and eczema herpeticum (widespread HSV with punched-out erosions and fever)."]},{"h":"Contact dermatitis","points":["Allergic: type IV. Small haptens (nickel, urushiol from poison ivy and oak) bind skin proteins and Langerhans cells sensitize T cells.","On re-exposure, memory T cells cause an itchy, vesicular eruption 48 to 72 hours later; patch test is positive.","Irritant: direct chemical or physical damage to keratinocytes (soaps, solvents, wet work); no sensitization needed and patch test is negative.","Shape follows exposure: earlobes, wrist, or belly button area from nickel; linear vesicles from plants; hands from wet work.","Histology of both, and of acute eczema: spongiosis (fluid between keratinocytes that stretches their junctions)."]},{"h":"Erythema multiforme and SJS/TEN","points":["Erythema multiforme: cytotoxic T cells attack keratinocytes, most often after HSV, sometimes after Mycoplasma pneumoniae.","Target lesions with a dusky or blistered center on hands, feet, and extensor limbs; mucosa limited or spared.","Histology: interface dermatitis with lymphocytes at the junction and scattered necrotic keratinocytes.","Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis: drug-triggered CD8 T cell killing causes full-thickness epidermal necrosis.","SJS involves under 10 percent of body surface; TEN over 30 percent; 10 to 30 percent is overlap.","Painful dusky skin, mucosal involvement (usually two or more sites), positive Nikolsky sign; triggers include sulfonamides, anticonvulsants, and allopurinol."]},{"h":"Urticaria and angioedema","points":["Mast cells release histamine, causing edema in the superficial dermis: itchy wheals that each last under 24 hours.","Triggers: IgE (foods, drugs, stings), direct mast cell release (opioids, radiocontrast), physical (pressure, cold, heat, exercise).","Chronic urticaria lasts over 6 weeks; many cases involve IgG autoantibodies to IgE or its receptor.","Angioedema is the same leak in deeper dermis and subcutis: swelling of lips, eyelids, tongue, or airway.","Bradykinin angioedema has no hives and does not respond to antihistamines: hereditary C1 inhibitor deficiency (low C4) or ACE inhibitors."]},{"h":"Other common rashes","points":["Pityriasis rosea: a single herald patch, then oval scaly patches along skin cleavage lines (Christmas tree on the back); clears on its own in about 6 to 8 weeks.","Seborrheic dermatitis: inflammatory response to Malassezia in oily areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds) with greasy yellow scale; cradle cap in infants; severe in HIV and Parkinson disease.","Aphthous ulcers: painful, shallow oral ulcers with a yellow-gray base and red halo on nonkeratinized mucosa; heal in 1 to 2 weeks.","Recurrent aphthous ulcers point toward Behçet disease, inflammatory bowel disease, or celiac disease."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Core acute inflammatory skin conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or trigger","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Atopic dermatitis","Filaggrin mutation, atopic family history","Barrier failure plus Th2 (IL-4, IL-13, IL-31)","Itchy rash: infant face and extensors, older flexures; lichenification","S aureus infection, eczema herpeticum"],["Allergic contact dermatitis","Nickel, urushiol, fragrances","Type IV hapten-specific T cells","Vesicles at contact sites 48 to 72 hours after re-exposure","Chronic hand dermatitis, secondary infection"],["Irritant contact dermatitis","Soaps, solvents, wet work","Direct keratinocyte damage","Burning, dry, cracked skin at exposure site","Fissures that admit bacteria"],["Erythema multiforme","HSV, Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Cytotoxic T cells against keratinocytes","Acral target lesions, mild mucosal disease","Recurrence with each herpes outbreak"],["Urticaria","Allergens, drugs, physical stimuli, autoimmunity","Mast cell degranulation, histamine","Wheals lasting under 24 hours","Angioedema, anaphylaxis"]]},{"title":"Blistering drug reactions and look-alikes","cols":["Feature","Erythema multiforme","SJS","TEN"],"rows":[["Usual trigger","HSV","Drugs","Drugs"],["Body surface detached","Minimal","Under 10 percent","Over 30 percent"],["Lesions","Typical raised targets, acral","Dusky macules, flat atypical targets, trunk","Sheets of detaching skin"],["Mucosa","Mild or none","Usually two or more sites","Usually two or more sites"],["Nikolsky sign","Negative","Positive","Positive"]]},{"title":"Hives versus bradykinin swelling","cols":["Feature","Histamine urticaria or angioedema","Bradykinin angioedema"],"rows":[["Hives","Present","Absent"],["Itch","Yes","Little or none"],["Causes","IgE allergy, physical, direct mast cell release","C1 inhibitor deficiency, ACE inhibitors"],["Antihistamine response","Good","None"],["Lab clue","None specific","Low C4 in hereditary disease"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Infants out, adults in","text":"Atopic dermatitis starts on the outside of the limbs and the face in infants and moves into the creases (flexures) in older children and adults."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Draw the exposure","text":"In contact dermatitis, the rash is a picture of what touched the skin: a watchband, an earring, a jeans snap, or a plant leaf dragged across the leg."},{"kind":"trick","label":"10 and 30","text":"Under 10 percent detached is SJS, over 30 percent is TEN, and between is overlap."}],"highYield":["Filaggrin mutations cause the barrier defect in atopic dermatitis.","Eczema herpeticum is disseminated HSV in atopic skin.","Allergic contact dermatitis is type IV and appears 48 to 72 hours after re-exposure.","Spongiosis is the histologic hallmark of eczematous dermatitis.","HSV is the most common trigger of erythema multiforme.","Each urticarial wheal lasts under 24 hours; chronic urticaria lasts over 6 weeks.","Hereditary angioedema: low C1 inhibitor, low C4, no hives.","Sulfonamides, anticonvulsants, and allopurinol are classic SJS/TEN triggers."],"pitfalls":["Erythema multiforme is usually triggered by infection (HSV); SJS and TEN are usually triggered by drugs.","Irritant contact dermatitis needs no prior sensitization and has a negative patch test.","Angioedema without hives in a patient on an ACE inhibitor is bradykinin mediated; antihistamines will not help.","Pityriasis rosea can mimic secondary syphilis; palms and soles involvement points to syphilis.","Aphthous ulcers sit on movable, nonkeratinized mucosa; recurrent HSV inside the mouth favors keratinized hard palate and gums."],"dumps":[{"t":"Allergic contact dermatitis","p":"Draw the sensitization and elicitation phases of poison ivy dermatitis, from the first touch to the rash on a second exposure.","l":["Urushiol is a hapten that binds skin proteins","Langerhans cells take up the hapten-protein complex","Langerhans cells migrate to the regional lymph node","Naive T cells are primed; first exposure gives no rash or a late mild one","Re-exposure: memory T cells home to the skin","Cytokines and cytotoxic T cells damage keratinocytes","Itchy linear vesicles 48 to 72 hours later","Spongiosis on biopsy; positive patch test"]},{"t":"Acute rash comparison","p":"Build a table comparing atopic dermatitis, allergic contact dermatitis, erythema multiforme, urticaria, and SJS/TEN by trigger, immune mechanism, timing, and key skin finding.","l":["Atopic dermatitis: filaggrin barrier defect plus Th2, chronic relapsing, flexural in adults","Allergic contact: type IV, 48 to 72 hours, distribution matches exposure","Erythema multiforme: HSV, cytotoxic T cells, acral target lesions","Urticaria: mast cells and histamine, wheals under 24 hours","SJS/TEN: drugs, CD8 T cells, full-thickness necrosis, mucosa involved","SJS under 10 percent, TEN over 30 percent","Bradykinin angioedema has no hives"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on inflammatory skin diseases"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-1","IN-11","IN-12","IN-13-C","IN-13-D","IM-15-C1","IM-15-C4"]},"IN-13-C":{"id":"IN-13-C","summary":"This unit covers chronic inflammatory skin disease: rosacea, lichen planus, and psoriasis, plus pyoderma gangrenosum, hidradenitis suppurativa, and the alopecias. The exam gives you a lesion and where it sits, and asks for the immune mechanism, the histology, or the disease it travels with.","notes":[{"h":"Rosacea","points":["Adults (often 30 to 50) with central facial flushing, persistent erythema, and telangiectasias on cheeks, nose, chin, and forehead.","Papules and pustules can appear, but there are no comedones.","Triggers cause flushing: heat, sunlight, alcohol, spicy food, hot drinks, and emotional stress.","Complications: rhinophyma (bulbous nose from sebaceous and fibrous overgrowth) and ocular rosacea (blepharitis, conjunctivitis, gritty eyes).","Look-alikes: acne vulgaris has comedones in teens; seborrheic dermatitis has greasy yellow scale in nasolabial folds, brows, and scalp; the lupus malar rash spares the nasolabial folds and comes with systemic findings."]},{"h":"Lichen planus","points":["The 6 Ps: pruritic, purple, polygonal, planar papules and plaques, often on flexor wrists, shins, and genitals.","Wickham striae: fine white lines on the surface of the papules.","Oral lesions: white lacy (reticular) network on the buccal mucosa.","Histology: band-like T cell infiltrate along the dermal-epidermal junction, sawtooth rete ridges, hypergranulosis, and Civatte (colloid) bodies, which are apoptotic basal keratinocytes.","Mechanism: cytotoxic CD8 T cells attack basal keratinocytes.","Linked to hepatitis C infection."]},{"h":"Psoriasis","points":["Dendritic cells make IL-23, which expands Th17 cells; IL-17 and TNF drive keratinocyte hyperproliferation and neutrophil recruitment.","Strong genetic risk; HLA-Cw6 is the classic allele.","Well-demarcated red plaques with silvery scale on elbows, knees, scalp, and lumbosacral skin.","Koebner phenomenon: new plaques form at sites of skin trauma.","Nails: pitting, onycholysis, and oil-drop discoloration; nail disease raises the odds of psoriatic arthritis (DIP joints, dactylitis, pencil-in-cup deformity).","Histology: acanthosis with regular elongation of rete ridges, parakeratosis, thin or absent granular layer, thin suprapapillary plates over dilated capillaries, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).","Auspitz sign: pinpoint bleeding when scale is lifted, because only a thin plate of epidermis covers the dilated papillary capillaries."]},{"h":"Pyoderma gangrenosum and hidradenitis suppurativa","points":["Pyoderma gangrenosum: neutrophilic dermatosis, not an infection; painful ulcer with a violaceous, undermined border, often on the legs.","Pathergy: trauma or surgical debridement makes it worse.","Associations: inflammatory bowel disease (especially ulcerative colitis), rheumatoid arthritis, and hematologic malignancy.","Hidradenitis suppurativa: chronic follicular occlusion in the axillae, groin, and perineum, with painful nodules, abscesses, sinus tracts, and rope-like scars.","Hidradenitis risk factors: obesity and smoking; onset after puberty."]},{"h":"Alopecia","points":["Nonscarring alopecia keeps the follicle openings, so hair can regrow; scarring alopecia destroys follicles and leaves smooth skin with no openings.","Alopecia areata: autoimmune T cell attack on anagen follicles; round smooth patches with exclamation point hairs (tapered toward the scalp).","Androgenetic alopecia: DHT (from 5-alpha reductase) miniaturizes follicles; vertex and frontotemporal thinning in men, widened central part in women.","Telogen effluvium: a stressor (childbirth, surgery, high fever, crash diet) shifts many follicles into telogen; diffuse shedding of club hairs about 2 to 4 months later.","Scarring alopecia: lichen planopilaris, discoid lupus, and other inflammation that destroys the follicle stem cells."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Chronic inflammatory skin conditions","cols":["Condition","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Histology","Associations or complications"],"rows":[["Rosacea","Vascular hyperreactivity and innate immune inflammation of facial skin","Central facial flushing, erythema, telangiectasias, papules and pustules, no comedones","Dilated vessels and perifollicular inflammation","Rhinophyma, ocular rosacea"],["Lichen planus","CD8 T cells kill basal keratinocytes","Pruritic purple polygonal planar papules, Wickham striae, lacy oral lesions","Band-like lymphocytes at the junction, sawtooth rete ridges, Civatte bodies","Hepatitis C"],["Psoriasis","IL-23 and Th17 (IL-17), TNF; HLA-Cw6","Silvery plaques on extensors and scalp, Koebner, nail pitting","Acanthosis, parakeratosis, thin granular layer, Munro microabscesses","Psoriatic arthritis"],["Pyoderma gangrenosum","Neutrophilic dermatosis","Painful ulcer, violaceous undermined border, pathergy","Dense neutrophils without infection","IBD, rheumatoid arthritis, hematologic malignancy"],["Hidradenitis suppurativa","Follicular occlusion and rupture","Nodules, abscesses, sinus tracts in axillae, groin, perineum","Follicular plugging, abscess, sinus tracts","Scarring; risk rises with obesity and smoking"]]},{"title":"Hair loss patterns","cols":["Type","Cause","Look","Scarring"],"rows":[["Alopecia areata","Autoimmune attack on anagen follicles","Round smooth patches, exclamation point hairs","No"],["Androgenetic alopecia","DHT-driven follicle miniaturization","Vertex and frontotemporal thinning in men, central thinning in women","No"],["Telogen effluvium","Stressor pushes follicles into telogen","Diffuse shedding about 2 to 4 months after the event","No"],["Scarring alopecia","Inflammation destroys follicle stem cells","Smooth shiny patches with no follicle openings","Yes"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 6 Ps of lichen planus","text":"Pruritic, Purple, Polygonal, Planar, Papules, Plaques."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Psoriasis sits outside, eczema sits inside","text":"Adult psoriasis favors extensor surfaces (elbows, knees). Adult atopic dermatitis favors flexures (antecubital and popliteal fossae)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Comedones decide acne versus rosacea","text":"Pustules on the face with blackheads or whiteheads point to acne vulgaris. Pustules with flushing and telangiectasias but no comedones point to rosacea."}],"highYield":["Facial flushing after wine and hot coffee, telangiectasias, pustules, no comedones: rosacea; may progress to rhinophyma.","Itchy purple flat-topped papules on the wrists with lacy white buccal lesions: lichen planus; screen for hepatitis C.","Psoriasis is driven by IL-23 and IL-17 from Th17 cells, with TNF as a key cytokine.","Munro microabscesses and the Auspitz sign are psoriasis clues; nail pitting predicts psoriatic arthritis.","Painful ulcer with a purple undermined edge that worsens after debridement in a patient with ulcerative colitis: pyoderma gangrenosum.","Recurrent painful axillary and groin nodules with sinus tracts in a smoker with obesity: hidradenitis suppurativa.","Exclamation point hairs in a smooth round patch: alopecia areata."],"pitfalls":["Lupus malar rash spares the nasolabial folds; seborrheic dermatitis involves them with greasy scale; rosacea has telangiectasias and pustules.","Koebner phenomenon is seen in both psoriasis and lichen planus, so use the lesion color and scale to separate them.","Pyoderma gangrenosum looks infected, but cultures are negative and debridement makes it worse.","Telogen effluvium starts months after the trigger, so ask about events 2 to 4 months back."],"dumps":[{"t":"Psoriasis from cytokine to biopsy","p":"Trace the psoriasis pathway from dendritic cell to plaque, then draw a psoriatic epidermis next to normal skin and label each change.","l":["Dendritic cells release IL-23","IL-23 expands Th17 cells that release IL-17 (with TNF)","Keratinocytes overproliferate and recruit neutrophils","Acanthosis with regularly elongated rete ridges","Parakeratosis and thin or absent granular layer","Thin suprapapillary plates over dilated capillaries (Auspitz sign)","Munro microabscesses in the stratum corneum"]},{"t":"Rosacea look-alike table","p":"Build a table comparing rosacea, acne vulgaris, seborrheic dermatitis, and lupus malar rash by age, comedones, scale, nasolabial folds, and complications.","l":["Rosacea: adults, flushing, telangiectasias, no comedones","Rosacea triggers: heat, sun, alcohol, spicy food","Rosacea complications: rhinophyma and ocular rosacea","Acne vulgaris: adolescents, comedones present","Seborrheic dermatitis: greasy yellow scale, involves nasolabial folds","Lupus malar rash: spares nasolabial folds, systemic features"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on Th17 cells and autoimmune inflammation"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on inflammatory skin diseases"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-1","IN-11","IN-13-B","IN-13-H"]},"IN-13-D":{"id":"IN-13-D","summary":"Blistering diseases come from a broken adhesion protein, either from a gene defect (epidermolysis bullosa) or from autoantibodies (pemphigus, pemphigoid, dermatitis herpetiformis). The exam asks you to match the target protein to the split level, the Nikolsky sign, the mouth findings, and the immunofluorescence pattern.","notes":[{"h":"Where skin holds together","points":["Desmosomes join keratinocytes to each other; their cadherins are desmoglein 1 and desmoglein 3.","Desmoglein 3 is concentrated in the lower epidermis and in mucosa; desmoglein 1 is concentrated in the upper epidermis.","Hemidesmosomes anchor basal cells to the basement membrane through BP180 (collagen XVII) and BP230.","Below the basal cell: lamina lucida (laminin 332), lamina densa (type IV collagen), then anchoring fibrils (type VII collagen) into the dermis.","The deeper the split, the more tense the blister and the more likely scarring."]},{"h":"Epidermolysis bullosa","points":["Inherited defects in adhesion proteins; blisters from minor friction starting at birth or infancy; mouth and nails are often involved.","Simplex: keratins 5 and 14; basal keratinocytes break; usually heals without scarring.","Junctional: laminin 332 or collagen XVII; split in the lamina lucida; severe forms can be fatal in infancy.","Dystrophic: type VII collagen (anchoring fibrils); split below the lamina densa.","Recessive dystrophic: heavy scarring, fused digits (mitten hands), nail loss, esophageal strictures, and aggressive squamous cell carcinoma in young adults."]},{"h":"Pemphigus","points":["Type II hypersensitivity: IgG against desmogleins causes acantholysis (keratinocytes fall apart).","Pemphigus vulgaris: anti-desmoglein 3 (often also 1); suprabasal split; basal cells stay attached like a row of tombstones.","Painful oral erosions usually come first, then flaccid bullae that rupture easily.","Nikolsky sign positive: gentle pressure shears off the epidermis.","Direct immunofluorescence: net-like (fishnet) IgG around keratinocytes.","Pemphigus foliaceus: anti-desmoglein 1 only; subcorneal split near the granular layer; scaly crusted erosions; no mucosal lesions."]},{"h":"Bullous pemphigoid","points":["IgG against BP180 and BP230 in hemidesmosomes; complement activation draws in eosinophils.","Older adults (usually over 60) with tense subepidermal bullae, often on flexures, trunk, and thighs, with itching.","Oral lesions are infrequent; Nikolsky sign negative.","Direct immunofluorescence: linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane."]},{"h":"Dermatitis herpetiformis","points":["IgA against epidermal transglutaminase, tied to celiac disease (gluten sensitivity).","Grouped, very itchy vesicles on extensor surfaces: elbows, knees, buttocks, upper back.","Histology: neutrophilic microabscesses at the tips of dermal papillae.","Direct immunofluorescence: granular IgA in the dermal papillae."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Autoimmune blistering diseases","cols":["Disease","Target antigen","Split level","Nikolsky","Mucosa","Immunofluorescence"],"rows":[["Pemphigus vulgaris","Desmoglein 3, often also 1 (IgG)","Suprabasal, tombstone basal layer","Positive","Painful oral erosions, often first","Net-like intercellular IgG"],["Pemphigus foliaceus","Desmoglein 1 (IgG)","Subcorneal, near granular layer","Positive","Spared","Intercellular IgG, strongest in upper epidermis"],["Bullous pemphigoid","BP180 and BP230 in hemidesmosomes (IgG)","Subepidermal","Negative","Infrequent","Linear IgG and C3 along basement membrane"],["Dermatitis herpetiformis","Epidermal transglutaminase (IgA)","Subepidermal, papillary tips","Negative","Rare","Granular IgA in dermal papillae"]]},{"title":"Epidermolysis bullosa types","cols":["Type","Defective protein","Split level","Course"],"rows":[["Simplex","Keratins 5 and 14","Within basal keratinocytes","Friction blisters, usually heal without scars"],["Junctional","Laminin 332 or collagen XVII","Lamina lucida","Can be severe and fatal in infancy"],["Dystrophic","Type VII collagen","Below the lamina densa","Scarring, fused digits, strictures, SCC in recessive form"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Vulgaris starts in the mouth","text":"Desmoglein 3 dominates in mucosa, so pemphigus vulgaris hits the mouth first. Foliaceus targets only desmoglein 1, which mucosa does not depend on, so the mouth is spared."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tense means deep","text":"A blister with a full epidermal roof (pemphigoid) stays tense. A blister with only part of the epidermis as its roof (pemphigus) is flaccid and ruptures."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fishnet versus single line","text":"Antibody between every keratinocyte draws a net: pemphigus. Antibody along one line at the base draws a single line: pemphigoid."}],"highYield":["Middle-aged adult with painful oral erosions, then flaccid bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign: pemphigus vulgaris (anti-desmoglein 3).","Older adult with itchy tense bullae and negative Nikolsky sign: bullous pemphigoid (anti-BP180 and BP230).","Eosinophils in a subepidermal blister point to bullous pemphigoid.","Grouped itchy vesicles on the elbows with chronic diarrhea: dermatitis herpetiformis and celiac disease.","Recessive dystrophic EB: type VII collagen defect, mitten hands, and early squamous cell carcinoma.","EB simplex: keratin 5 and 14 mutation; split within basal cells."],"pitfalls":["Pemphigus is intraepidermal and Nikolsky positive; pemphigoid is subepidermal and Nikolsky negative.","Pemphigus foliaceus and staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome both cut desmoglein 1, one by IgG and one by bacterial toxin.","Linear IgG at the basement membrane means pemphigoid; granular IgA at papillary tips means dermatitis herpetiformis.","EB is inherited and starts at birth; the autoimmune blistering diseases start later in life."],"dumps":[{"t":"Adhesion map with split levels","p":"Draw two keratinocytes resting on the basement membrane above the dermis. Label the adhesion proteins, then mark where each blistering disease splits the skin.","l":["Desmosomes with desmoglein 1 (upper) and desmoglein 3 (lower and mucosa)","Hemidesmosomes with BP180 (collagen XVII) and BP230","Lamina lucida with laminin 332, lamina densa, anchoring fibrils of type VII collagen","Pemphigus foliaceus: subcorneal split","Pemphigus vulgaris: suprabasal split","Bullous pemphigoid: subepidermal split","EB simplex in basal cells, junctional in lamina lucida, dystrophic below lamina densa"]},{"t":"Blistering disease comparison table","p":"Make a table for pemphigus vulgaris, pemphigus foliaceus, bullous pemphigoid, and dermatitis herpetiformis with columns for antigen, antibody class, split, Nikolsky sign, mucosa, and immunofluorescence.","l":["Vulgaris: desmoglein 3 with or without 1, IgG, suprabasal","Foliaceus: desmoglein 1, IgG, subcorneal, no mucosa","Pemphigoid: BP180 and BP230, IgG, subepidermal","Dermatitis herpetiformis: epidermal transglutaminase, IgA","Nikolsky positive only in pemphigus","Fishnet IgG in pemphigus, linear IgG and C3 in pemphigoid","Granular IgA at papillary tips in dermatitis herpetiformis"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-1","IN-11","IN-13-H"]},"IN-13-E":{"id":"IN-13-E","summary":"This unit links two skin findings to their molecular defects: albinism (melanin cannot be made) and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (collagen is built or processed wrongly). The exam asks you to name the enzyme or collagen type, predict the complications, and separate these from Chediak-Higashi, Marfan, and osteogenesis imperfecta.","notes":[{"h":"Oculocutaneous albinism","points":["Autosomal recessive; melanocyte numbers are normal, but they cannot make melanin.","Classic form (OCA1): loss of tyrosinase, the enzyme that turns tyrosine into DOPA and on toward melanin; other forms lose melanosome proteins such as the OCA2 (P) protein.","Very pale skin, white or light hair, and light irises from birth.","No melanin shield, so sunburn, actinic damage, and squamous and basal cell carcinoma occur early.","Eyes: nystagmus, reduced visual acuity, photophobia, and strabismus from abnormal retinal and optic pathway development."]},{"h":"Other forms of reduced pigment","points":["Ocular albinism: X-linked (affects boys); eyes are involved, skin is near normal.","Chediak-Higashi syndrome: autosomal recessive LYST defect in lysosomal trafficking.","Chediak-Higashi findings: partial albinism, giant granules in neutrophils, recurrent pyogenic (staphylococcal and streptococcal) infections, peripheral neuropathy, and bleeding.","Vitiligo differs: melanocytes are destroyed, so patches are depigmented in an acquired pattern."]},{"h":"Collagen building blocks","points":["Collagen is a triple helix with glycine at every third position.","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases (vitamin C dependent) hydroxylate residues inside the cell.","Procollagen peptidases trim the ends outside the cell; lysyl oxidase (copper dependent) then cross-links fibrils.","Type I: skin, bone, tendon. Type III: blood vessels, bowel, uterus, granulation tissue. Type V: works with type I in skin."]},{"h":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","points":["Classic type: type V collagen (COL5A1); hyperextensible soft skin and joint hypermobility.","Vascular type: type III collagen (COL3A1), autosomal dominant; thin translucent skin with visible veins.","Vascular type risks: arterial rupture, bowel perforation, uterine rupture in pregnancy, and berry aneurysms.","Kyphoscoliotic type: lysyl hydroxylase deficiency, autosomal recessive; hypotonia, kyphoscoliosis, fragile sclerae.","Skin: fragile, bruises easily, heals with wide atrophic cigarette-paper scars.","Joints: hypermobility with recurrent dislocations."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pigment disorders","cols":["Disorder","Inheritance","Defect","Melanocytes","Key findings"],"rows":[["Oculocutaneous albinism","Autosomal recessive","Tyrosinase or melanosome proteins (such as OCA2)","Normal number","Pale skin, hair, and eyes; skin cancer; nystagmus, low acuity"],["Ocular albinism","X-linked","Melanosome protein in the eye","Normal number","Eye findings in boys, skin near normal"],["Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Autosomal recessive","LYST lysosomal trafficking","Normal number, giant melanosomes","Partial albinism, giant granules, pyogenic infections, neuropathy"],["Vitiligo","Acquired, autoimmune","T cell destruction of melanocytes","Absent in patches","Well-defined depigmented patches"]]},{"title":"Connective tissue disorders compared","cols":["Disorder","Defective protein","Skin and joints","Other findings"],"rows":[["Ehlers-Danlos, classic","Type V collagen (COL5A1)","Hyperextensible fragile skin, cigarette-paper scars, hypermobility","Easy bruising"],["Ehlers-Danlos, vascular","Type III collagen (COL3A1)","Thin translucent skin, easy bruising","Arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture; berry aneurysms"],["Ehlers-Danlos, kyphoscoliotic","Lysyl hydroxylase","Hypermobility, fragile skin","Kyphoscoliosis, hypotonia, scleral fragility"],["Marfan syndrome","Fibrillin-1 (FBN1)","Long limbs, arachnodactyly, hypermobility","Lens subluxation, aortic root dilation and dissection"],["Osteogenesis imperfecta","Type I collagen (COL1A1, COL1A2)","Joint laxity","Repeated fractures, blue sclerae, hearing loss, dental defects"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Three for the vessels","text":"Type III collagen is the vascular collagen, and loss of it gives the vascular type of Ehlers-Danlos with arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count versus color","text":"Albinism has a normal melanocyte count with no pigment made. Vitiligo has no melanocytes at all in the patch. A biopsy shows melanocytes present in albinism and missing in vitiligo."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Bone, eye, or skin first","text":"Fractures with blue sclerae point to type I collagen (osteogenesis imperfecta). Tall with a displaced lens points to fibrillin (Marfan). Stretchy skin and bad scars point to Ehlers-Danlos."}],"highYield":["Albinism: normal melanocyte number, little or no melanin, classically tyrosinase loss; high risk of squamous and basal cell carcinoma.","Nystagmus and low visual acuity are common in oculocutaneous albinism.","Partial albinism, giant neutrophil granules, recurrent staphylococcal infections, and neuropathy: Chediak-Higashi (LYST).","Classic Ehlers-Danlos: type V collagen; vascular type: type III collagen; kyphoscoliotic type: lysyl hydroxylase.","Young adult with sudden colon perforation or arterial rupture and thin, bruised skin: vascular Ehlers-Danlos.","Lens subluxation and aortic root dilation point to Marfan, not Ehlers-Danlos."],"pitfalls":["Both scurvy and kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos impair collagen hydroxylation, but scurvy is acquired and adds perifollicular hemorrhage and gum disease.","Ocular albinism is X-linked; oculocutaneous albinism is autosomal recessive.","Joint hypermobility occurs in Ehlers-Danlos, Marfan, and osteogenesis imperfecta, so use skin, lens, and fractures to separate them.","Albinism raises nonmelanoma skin cancer risk; do not assume the main risk is pigmented melanoma."],"dumps":[{"t":"Collagen synthesis pathway","p":"Draw collagen synthesis from gene to cross-linked fibril. Mark the step that fails in each type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome and in osteogenesis imperfecta.","l":["Translation of alpha chains with glycine every third residue","Hydroxylation of proline and lysine (vitamin C dependent)","Kyphoscoliotic EDS: lysyl hydroxylase deficiency","Triple helix forms and procollagen is secreted","Procollagen peptidases cleave the ends outside the cell","Lysyl oxidase (copper dependent) cross-links fibrils","Classic EDS: type V collagen; vascular EDS: type III collagen","Osteogenesis imperfecta: type I collagen"]},{"t":"Melanin pathway and albinism","p":"Draw a melanocyte passing melanosomes to keratinocytes. Write the step tyrosinase controls and label where oculocutaneous albinism, ocular albinism, and Chediak-Higashi syndrome go wrong.","l":["Tyrosine to DOPA to melanin, catalyzed by tyrosinase","Melanin made in melanosomes and transferred to keratinocytes","Oculocutaneous albinism: autosomal recessive loss of tyrosinase or melanosome protein","Melanocyte number is normal in albinism","Ocular albinism: X-linked, eyes mainly","Chediak-Higashi: LYST trafficking defect, giant granules, infections","Consequences: sunburn, skin cancer, nystagmus, low acuity"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on melanin synthesis from tyrosine"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-5","IN-7","IN-8","IN-13-A"]},"IN-13-F":{"id":"IN-13-F","summary":"This unit covers common growths on the skin and mouth, from harmless (seborrheic keratosis, cysts, lipoma, hemangiomas) to premalignant (actinic keratosis, dysplastic nevi, leukoplakia). The exam asks you to recognize the lesion, explain what it can become, and spot skin signs that announce a hidden cancer.","notes":[{"h":"Actinic keratosis","points":["UV-induced keratinocyte dysplasia on sun-exposed skin (face, scalp, ears, dorsal hands) in older fair-skinned adults.","Rough, sandpaper-like scaly papules, often easier to feel than to see.","Histology: atypia in the lower epidermis, parakeratosis, and solar elastosis (sun-damaged blue-gray dermal elastic tissue).","A small fraction progress to squamous cell carcinoma; many lesions mean high cumulative UV."]},{"h":"Dysplastic nevi","points":["Larger than common nevi (often over 5 mm), with irregular borders and variable pigment.","Biopsy: cytologic atypia plus architectural atypia (bridging nests, lentiginous growth).","A single dysplastic nevus rarely becomes melanoma; many of them mark a person at high melanoma risk.","Familial cases (familial atypical multiple mole melanoma) carry CDKN2A (p16) mutations and need close skin surveillance."]},{"h":"Seborrheic keratosis and paraneoplastic signs","points":["Seborrheic keratosis: benign epidermal growth of older adults; tan to brown, waxy, stuck-on plaque.","Histology: basaloid cells with horn cysts; many carry FGFR3 activating mutations.","Leser-Trélat sign: sudden eruption of many seborrheic keratoses, a marker of internal cancer, classically gastric adenocarcinoma.","Acanthosis nigricans: velvety dark plaques in the neck and axillae; usually insulin resistance (obesity, type 2 diabetes, polycystic ovary syndrome), rarely gastric adenocarcinoma.","Dermatomyositis in an adult: look for ovarian, lung, and GI cancers.","Paraneoplastic pemphigus: severe oral erosions with lymphoma or other lymphoid neoplasm."]},{"h":"Cysts and lipoma","points":["Epidermal inclusion cyst: lined by stratified squamous epithelium with a granular layer, filled with laminated keratin; often has a central punctum.","A ruptured inclusion cyst triggers a foreign body giant cell reaction and becomes red and painful.","Trichilemmal (pilar) cyst: on the scalp, no granular layer, filled with dense compact keratin.","Lipoma: soft, mobile, painless mass of mature adipocytes; the most common soft tissue tumor of adults."]},{"h":"Vascular lesions","points":["Infantile hemangioma: appears in the first weeks of life, grows for months, then slowly involutes over years.","Cherry hemangioma: small bright red papules on the trunk of older adults; benign.","Pyogenic granuloma: rapidly growing, friable red nodule that bleeds easily; common on gums in pregnancy and after minor trauma.","Port-wine stain: capillary malformation present at birth that persists and darkens; in the V1 facial distribution it suggests Sturge-Weber syndrome (leptomeningeal angioma, seizures, glaucoma)."]},{"h":"White oral lesions","points":["Leukoplakia: white plaque that cannot be scraped off; tobacco and alcohol; may show dysplasia and can progress to squamous cell carcinoma, so biopsy it.","Oral hairy leukoplakia: corrugated white plaque on the lateral tongue, EBV, seen in HIV; cannot be scraped off; not premalignant.","Thrush (Candida): white curd-like plaques that scrape off and leave a red base."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Benign and premalignant skin lesions","cols":["Lesion","Cause","Look","Histology","Risk or significance"],"rows":[["Actinic keratosis","Cumulative UV damage","Rough sandpaper scaly papules on sun-exposed skin","Lower epidermal atypia, parakeratosis, solar elastosis","Some progress to squamous cell carcinoma"],["Dysplastic nevus","Melanocytic proliferation; CDKN2A in familial cases","Large, irregular border, variable color","Cytologic and architectural atypia","Marker of melanoma risk"],["Seborrheic keratosis","FGFR3 activating mutation","Waxy stuck-on tan to brown plaque","Basaloid cells, horn cysts","Benign; sudden eruption is Leser-Trélat sign"]]},{"title":"Vascular lesions","cols":["Lesion","Age","Course","Clue"],"rows":[["Infantile hemangioma","Appears in first weeks of life","Grows, then involutes over years","Bright red raised lesion in a baby"],["Cherry hemangioma","Older adults","Persists, benign","Small red papules on trunk"],["Pyogenic granuloma","Any age; pregnancy","Grows over weeks","Friable, bleeds, often on gums"],["Port-wine stain","Present at birth","Persists and darkens","V1 face: Sturge-Weber"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Scrape test for white mouth lesions","text":"Scrapes off: thrush. Does not scrape off on the side of the tongue in a patient with HIV: hairy leukoplakia. Does not scrape off in a smoker: leukoplakia, biopsy for dysplasia."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Babies grow and shrink, port-wine stays","text":"An infantile hemangioma shows up after birth, grows, and then fades. A port-wine stain is there at birth and never goes away."}],"highYield":["Rough scaly papules on the scalp of an older farmer: actinic keratosis, a precursor of squamous cell carcinoma.","Many large irregular moles plus family members with melanoma: dysplastic nevus syndrome with CDKN2A mutation.","Sudden crop of seborrheic keratoses with weight loss: Leser-Trélat sign of gastric adenocarcinoma.","Acanthosis nigricans is usually insulin resistance; think cancer when it appears suddenly in a thin older adult.","Rupture of an epidermal inclusion cyst produces foreign body giant cell inflammation.","Bleeding red gum nodule in a pregnant woman: pyogenic granuloma.","Port-wine stain in the V1 distribution with seizures: Sturge-Weber syndrome."],"pitfalls":["Seborrheic keratosis is benign even when dark; it looks stuck on rather than growing into the skin.","Most melanomas arise without a prior nevus; dysplastic nevi mark risk rather than serve as the usual precursor.","Epidermal inclusion cyst keeps a granular layer; trichilemmal cyst lacks one.","Oral hairy leukoplakia is EBV-related and not premalignant; leukoplakia can progress to cancer."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lesion to risk table","p":"Make a table of actinic keratosis, dysplastic nevus, seborrheic keratosis, leukoplakia, and oral hairy leukoplakia. For each, write the cause, what it looks like, and whether it can turn into cancer.","l":["Actinic keratosis: UV, scaly sun-exposed papules, can become squamous cell carcinoma","Dysplastic nevus: large irregular mole, marks melanoma risk, CDKN2A in families","Seborrheic keratosis: stuck-on waxy plaque, FGFR3, benign","Leser-Trélat: sudden many seborrheic keratoses with GI cancer","Leukoplakia: tobacco and alcohol, cannot scrape off, can become squamous cell carcinoma","Oral hairy leukoplakia: EBV, lateral tongue, HIV, not premalignant"]},{"t":"Skin signs of internal cancer","p":"List each paraneoplastic skin sign in this unit and draw an arrow to the cancer it points to. Add the more common noncancer cause where one exists.","l":["Leser-Trélat sign to gastric adenocarcinoma","Acanthosis nigricans to gastric adenocarcinoma","Acanthosis nigricans more often from insulin resistance","Dermatomyositis to ovarian, lung, and GI cancers","Paraneoplastic pemphigus to lymphoma","Sudden onset in an older adult raises concern for cancer"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on oral cavity lesions"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-8","IN-13-A","IN-13-G"]},"IN-13-G":{"id":"IN-13-G","summary":"This unit covers the three main skin cancers (basal cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, melanoma) and cutaneous T cell lymphoma. The exam asks you to match the lesion and biopsy to the cancer, name its risk factors and molecular driver, and predict how likely it is to spread.","notes":[{"h":"Basal cell carcinoma","points":["Most common skin cancer; UV-driven, on sun-exposed skin, especially the face and upper lip.","Driven by hedgehog pathway activation, usually loss of PTCH1 or activation of SMO; germline PTCH1 loss causes many early BCCs (Gorlin syndrome).","Pearly papule with telangiectasias and rolled borders; can ulcerate in the center (rodent ulcer).","Histology: nests of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading and retraction clefts from the stroma.","Invades and destroys locally; metastasis is rare."]},{"h":"Squamous cell carcinoma","points":["Risk factors: cumulative UV, actinic keratosis, transplant immunosuppression, arsenic, HPV, xeroderma pigmentosum, and chronic scars or ulcers.","Squamous cell carcinoma arising in a burn scar, chronic ulcer, or draining sinus is a Marjolin ulcer.","Favors the lower lip and sun-exposed skin, because those sites get the most UV.","Scaly, crusted, or ulcerated nodule; histology shows keratin pearls and intercellular bridges.","Bowen disease is squamous cell carcinoma in situ: a red scaly plaque with full-thickness atypia and an intact basement membrane.","Keratoacanthoma: dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin plug that grows over weeks and can regress; it looks like well-differentiated SCC.","Metastasizes more often than BCC, especially from the lip, ear, scars, and in transplant recipients."]},{"h":"Melanoma risk and subtypes","points":["Risk: intense intermittent UV (blistering sunburns), fair skin, many or dysplastic nevi, family history, CDKN2A, and xeroderma pigmentosum.","Superficial spreading: most common; trunk and legs; long horizontal (radial) phase.","Nodular: grows down from the start, so it is often thick when found.","Lentigo maligna: slow-growing on chronically sun-damaged faces of older adults.","Acral lentiginous: palms, soles, and nail beds; not tied to UV; the most common type in people with dark skin.","Mucosal: oral, nasal, anorectal, and genital (vulvar or penile) mucosa; not UV-related; often found late."]},{"h":"Melanoma growth and prognosis","points":["Radial growth phase: cells spread within the epidermis and superficial dermis and cannot metastasize.","Vertical growth phase: a clone grows down into the dermis as an expanding nodule, reaching vessels and lymphatics, so metastasis becomes possible.","Breslow thickness (mm from the granular layer to the deepest tumor cell) is the key prognostic factor in localized melanoma.","Ulceration and high mitotic rate also worsen prognosis.","Immunostains: S-100 (sensitive), HMB-45 and Melan-A (more specific melanocytic markers)."]},{"h":"Mycosis fungoides","points":["Cutaneous lymphoma of CD4 T cells in older adults.","Moves through patch, plaque, and tumor stages over years.","Biopsy: atypical lymphocytes with cerebriform (folded) nuclei in the epidermis, forming Pautrier microabscesses.","Sézary syndrome: the leukemic form with generalized erythroderma and circulating Sézary cells."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Skin cancers compared","cols":["Cancer","Key driver or risk","Classic look","Histology","Spread"],"rows":[["Basal cell carcinoma","UV; hedgehog activation (PTCH1)","Pearly papule, telangiectasias, rolled border","Basaloid nests with peripheral palisading","Local invasion, rare metastasis"],["Squamous cell carcinoma","Cumulative UV, actinic keratosis, immunosuppression, arsenic, HPV, scars","Scaly, crusted, or ulcerated nodule; lower lip","Keratin pearls, intercellular bridges","Metastasis more likely than BCC"],["Melanoma","Intermittent intense UV, many nevi, CDKN2A","Asymmetric, irregular, multicolored, enlarging pigmented lesion","Atypical melanocytes; S-100, HMB-45, Melan-A","Metastasizes once in vertical growth phase; Breslow depth predicts"],["Mycosis fungoides","Clonal CD4 T cells","Patches, then plaques, then tumors","Cerebriform cells, Pautrier microabscesses","Sézary syndrome is the leukemic form"]]},{"title":"Melanoma subtypes","cols":["Subtype","Typical site","Behavior"],"rows":[["Superficial spreading","Trunk, legs","Most common; long radial growth phase"],["Nodular","Anywhere","Early vertical growth; thick at diagnosis"],["Lentigo maligna","Sun-damaged face of older adults","Slow, long radial phase"],["Acral lentiginous","Palms, soles, nail beds","Not UV-related; most common in dark skin"],["Mucosal","Oral, nasal, anorectal, genital mucosa","Not UV-related; found late, poor prognosis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ABCDE of melanoma","text":"Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variation, Diameter over 6 mm, Evolution (change over time)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Upper lip basal, lower lip squamous","text":"BCC classically sits on the upper lip and central face. SCC classically sits on the lower lip, which takes more direct sun."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Depth decides melanoma","text":"A melanoma that stays flat (radial phase) cannot spread. Once it grows down (vertical phase), Breslow thickness tells you how dangerous it is."}],"highYield":["Pearly papule with telangiectasias on the nose: basal cell carcinoma; palisading basaloid nests; rarely metastasizes.","Gorlin syndrome: germline PTCH1 loss with many early basal cell carcinomas.","Kidney transplant recipient with multiple scaly nodules: squamous cell carcinoma.","Ulcer that turns into a growing mass in an old burn scar: Marjolin ulcer (squamous cell carcinoma).","Breslow thickness is the single most important prognostic factor for localized melanoma.","Dark streak under a nail or a lesion on the sole: acral lentiginous melanoma.","Itchy scaly patches for years, then plaques, with Pautrier microabscesses: mycosis fungoides; erythroderma with circulating cerebriform cells is Sézary syndrome."],"pitfalls":["Keratoacanthoma and well-differentiated SCC look alike; rapid growth over weeks and spontaneous regression favor keratoacanthoma.","Bowen disease is in situ SCC, so it has not yet invaded the dermis.","S-100 is sensitive but not specific; HMB-45 and Melan-A are more specific for melanocytes.","Acral and mucosal melanoma are not UV-driven, so they occur on palms, soles, nail beds, and mucosa that get little or no sun.","Melanoma is the most deadly skin cancer, but BCC is the most common."],"dumps":[{"t":"Three skin cancers side by side","p":"Draw a cross-section of skin and sketch BCC, SCC, and melanoma in their typical spots. Under each, write its driver, its look, its histology, and how often it spreads.","l":["BCC: UV and hedgehog (PTCH1), pearly papule, telangiectasias","BCC histology: basaloid nests with peripheral palisading; rare metastasis","SCC: cumulative UV, actinic keratosis, immunosuppression, scars","SCC histology: keratin pearls and intercellular bridges; more metastasis than BCC","Melanoma: intermittent UV, nevi, CDKN2A","Melanoma stains: S-100, HMB-45, Melan-A","Melanoma metastasis once in vertical growth phase"]},{"t":"Melanoma growth phases","p":"Draw a melanoma in the radial growth phase and again in the vertical growth phase. Mark where you measure Breslow thickness and label which subtype starts vertical.","l":["Radial phase: spread within epidermis and superficial dermis","Radial phase does not metastasize","Vertical phase: expanding nodule in the dermis reaches vessels","Breslow thickness from granular layer to deepest tumor cell, in mm","Thicker tumor means worse prognosis","Nodular melanoma grows vertically from the start","Lentigo maligna and superficial spreading have long radial phases"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on skin neoplasms"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-6","IN-8","IN-13-A","IN-13-F"]},"IN-13-H":{"id":"IN-13-H","summary":"This unit covers skin infections and infection-linked skin diseases: bacterial (impetigo, cellulitis, MRSA, wound and bite infections), fungal (candidiasis, tinea), viral (warts, molluscum, herpes, childhood rashes), mycobacterial, toxin-mediated, and parasitic. The exam gives you the setting and the lesion and asks for the organism, its virulence factor, or the complication to expect.","notes":[{"h":"Acne vulgaris","points":["Four steps: follicular hyperkeratinization, androgen-driven sebum, Cutibacterium acnes growth, and inflammation.","Comedones are noninflamed plugs (open blackheads, closed whiteheads); papules, pustules, and nodules are inflamed and can scar.","Worse with hyperandrogenism (polycystic ovary syndrome) and glucocorticoids."]},{"h":"Superficial bacterial infections","points":["Folliculitis (inside the follicle), furuncle (deep follicular abscess), carbuncle (several furuncles joined, with draining sinuses); mostly Staphylococcus aureus. Related pus collections: skin abscess (usually S aureus) and pilonidal abscess in the sacrococcygeal cleft around trapped hair.","Hot tub folliculitis: Pseudomonas aeruginosa from poorly chlorinated water; itchy pustules where the swimsuit sat.","Nonbullous impetigo: honey-colored crusts around the nose and mouth in children; S aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes.","Bullous impetigo: S aureus exfoliative toxin cuts desmoglein 1 and splits the granular layer.","MRSA: mecA on SCCmec encodes PBP2a, which does not bind beta-lactams; anterior nares are the main reservoir.","Community MRSA: Panton-Valentine leukocidin, recurrent abscesses in athletes, prisons, military, and injection drug use; hospital MRSA: devices, surgery, dialysis, more drug resistance."]},{"h":"Erysipelas, cellulitis, and erythema nodosum","points":["Erysipelas: upper dermis and superficial lymphatics; raised, sharply demarcated, bright red; usually S pyogenes.","Cellulitis: deep dermis and subcutaneous fat; ill-defined warm red borders; S pyogenes or S aureus.","Portals of entry: tinea pedis fissures, wounds, ulcers, insect bites, lymphedema, and venous stasis.","Complications: abscess, lymphangitis (red streaks), bacteremia, and necrotizing fasciitis.","Necrotizing fasciitis: pain out of proportion to the exam, crepitus, rapid spread along fascial planes; surgical emergency.","Erythema nodosum: septal panniculitis without vasculitis; tender red nodules on the shins that heal without scarring.","Erythema nodosum triggers: strep infection, sarcoidosis, TB, coccidioidomycosis, histoplasmosis, IBD, and pregnancy."]},{"h":"Fungal and viral skin infections","points":["Candidiasis: beefy red intertriginous plaques with satellite pustules; candidal diaper rash involves the skin folds, irritant diaper rash spares them.","Candida risk: diabetes, antibiotics, glucocorticoids (including inhaled), HIV, infancy, dentures; chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis points to a T cell (Th17) defect.","Tinea (dermatophytes: Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton) is named by site; corporis has a scaly annular border with central clearing.","Tinea versicolor is Malassezia, a yeast, not a dermatophyte: hypo or hyperpigmented trunk macules in hot humid weather.","Warts (HPV): common (hands), plantar (soles), flat (face, shins), and anogenital condyloma acuminatum (HPV 6 and 11); histology shows papillomatosis, hyperkeratosis, and koilocytes; extensive warts suggest T cell immunodeficiency.","Molluscum contagiosum (poxvirus): firm, flesh-colored, umbilicated dome papules spread by contact, autoinoculation, and sex; self-limited in children, extensive in HIV.","Herpes simplex: herpes labialis, genital herpes, herpetic whitlow (finger), and eczema herpeticum on atopic skin.","Zoster: painful dermatomal vesicles, often followed by postherpetic neuralgia in older adults; Ramsay Hunt syndrome (geniculate ganglion) adds facial palsy and ear vesicles."]},{"h":"Wounds, needle sticks, and toxins","points":["Needle stick risk: hepatitis B highest (about 30% if the source is HBeAg positive), then hepatitis C (about 2%), then HIV (about 0.3%); risk rises with a hollow-bore blood-filled needle, deep injury, and high source viral load.","Clostridium perfringens alpha toxin (lecithinase) breaks cell membranes, causing gas gangrene with myonecrosis and crepitus.","Clostridium tetani in a puncture wound makes tetanospasmin, which travels to the CNS and blocks GABA and glycine release from inhibitory interneurons, causing spastic paralysis and trismus.","Bacillus anthracis: protective antigen carries edema factor (adenylate cyclase, raises cAMP) and lethal factor (a protease that kills macrophages) into cells; a polypeptide (D-glutamate) capsule resists phagocytosis; cutaneous anthrax is a painless papule that becomes a black eschar with marked edema.","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome: young children; exfoliative toxin cleaves desmoglein 1 in the granular layer; mucosa spared.","Toxic epidermal necrolysis: drug-triggered full-thickness epidermal necrosis with mucosal involvement."]},{"h":"Leprosy and ectoparasites","points":["Mycobacterium leprae grows best below core body temperature, so it favors skin, peripheral nerves, nose, and earlobes; it depends on host cells and cannot be grown in culture (armadillos and mouse footpads are used).","Tuberculoid leprosy: strong Th1 response, few bacilli, few hypopigmented anesthetic plaques.","Lepromatous leprosy: Th2 response, many bacilli, diffuse nodules, leonine facies, symmetric nerve damage.","Scabies: Sarcoptes scabiei burrows in finger webs, wrists, and genitals; itch worse at night; crusted scabies with immunosuppression.","Head lice (nits on hair, children) and pubic lice (sexual contact); body lice live in clothing and spread Rickettsia prowazekii and Bartonella quintana; bedbug bites appear in linear clusters."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infectious skin conditions (one row per listed condition)","cols":["Condition","Usual cause","Classic findings","Key point or complication"],"rows":[["Acne vulgaris","Keratin plug, androgens, Cutibacterium acnes, inflammation","Comedones, papules, pustules, nodules in teens","Worse with PCOS and glucocorticoids; scarring"],["Candidiasis","Candida albicans","Beefy red folds with satellite pustules; thrush","Diabetes, antibiotics, steroids, HIV"],["Cellulitis","S pyogenes or S aureus","Warm red skin with ill-defined border","Abscess, bacteremia, necrotizing fasciitis"],["Erysipelas","S pyogenes","Raised, sharply demarcated bright red plaque","Lymphatic involvement"],["Erythema nodosum","Reaction to infection, sarcoidosis, IBD, pregnancy","Tender red shin nodules","Septal panniculitis, heals without scars"],["Folliculitis","S aureus; Pseudomonas in hot tubs","Pustules centered on hair follicles","Can deepen to furuncle and carbuncle; skin and pilonidal abscesses are related"],["Impetigo","S aureus or S pyogenes","Honey-colored crusts; bullous form from S aureus toxin","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"],["MRSA","S aureus with mecA (PBP2a)","Recurrent abscesses and furuncles","PVL in community strains; nasal reservoir"],["Molluscum contagiosum","Poxvirus","Umbilicated flesh-colored dome papules","Extensive in HIV"],["Tinea","Dermatophytes","Scaly annular plaques with central clearing","Versicolor is Malassezia, not a dermatophyte"],["Verrucae","HPV","Rough papules; condyloma acuminatum from HPV 6 and 11","Koilocytes; extensive with T cell defects"],["Wound infection and needle stick","Depends on exposure","See wound and bite chart","Needle stick risk HBV over HCV over HIV"]]},{"title":"Wound and bite organisms","cols":["Exposure","Organism","Clue"],"rows":[["Cat or dog bite","Pasteurella multocida","Cellulitis within about a day"],["Dog bite in a patient without a spleen","Capnocytophaga canimorsus","Overwhelming sepsis"],["Human bite or clenched fist injury","Eikenella corrodens","Hand infection after a punch"],["Seawater wound or raw oysters","Vibrio vulnificus","Hemorrhagic bullae, worst in liver disease"],["Fish tank or swimming pool","Mycobacterium marinum","Granulomatous nodules on the hand"],["Rose thorn or gardening","Sporothrix schenckii","Nodules spreading up the lymphatics of the arm"],["Deep traumatic wound with soil","Clostridium perfringens","Gas gangrene with crepitus"],["Puncture wound, not immunized","Clostridium tetani","Trismus and spastic paralysis"]]},{"title":"Tinea by site","cols":["Type","Site","Clue"],"rows":[["Capitis","Scalp","Children; scaly alopecia, kerion (boggy inflamed mass)"],["Corporis","Body","Annular scaly border with central clearing"],["Cruris","Groin","Spares the scrotum"],["Pedis","Feet, toe webs","Fissures can let in cellulitis"],["Barbae","Beard area","Pustules and inflamed plaques"],["Unguium (onychomycosis)","Nails","Thick, yellow, crumbly nails"],["Versicolor (not a dermatophyte)","Trunk","Malassezia; hypo or hyperpigmented macules"]]},{"title":"Rashes of childhood and toxin-mediated rashes","cols":["Disease","Cause","Key findings"],"rows":[["Measles","Measles virus (paramyxovirus)","Cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, Koplik spots, rash spreading down from the head"],["Rubella","Rubella virus","Postauricular lymph nodes, milder rash spreading down from the face"],["Roseola","HHV-6","Several days of high fever, then rash as fever breaks"],["Erythema infectiosum","Parvovirus B19","Slapped cheek, then lacy rash on limbs"],["Varicella","Varicella-zoster virus","Crops of vesicles in different stages, starting on the trunk"],["Hand-foot-and-mouth disease","Coxsackievirus A","Oral ulcers with vesicles on palms and soles"],["Herpangina","Coxsackievirus A","Fever with vesicles and ulcers on the posterior palate"],["Scarlet fever","S pyogenes pyrogenic exotoxin","Sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue, circumoral pallor, later desquamation"],["Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","S aureus exfoliative toxin","Young child, superficial split in granular layer, mucosa spared"],["Toxic epidermal necrolysis","Drug reaction","Full-thickness epidermal necrosis with mucosal involvement"]]},{"title":"Leprosy forms","cols":["Feature","Tuberculoid","Lepromatous"],"rows":[["T cell response","Th1, strong cell-mediated immunity","Th2, weak cell-mediated immunity"],["Bacilli","Few","Many"],["Skin","Few hypopigmented anesthetic plaques","Diffuse nodules, leonine facies"],["Nerves","Localized nerve damage","Widespread symmetric nerve damage"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Rule of threes for needle sticks","text":"Hepatitis B about 30% (HBeAg-positive source), hepatitis C about 2%, HIV about 0.3%. The order and the roughly tenfold steps are what to know."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Erysipelas is on the surface","text":"Erysipelas sits high in the dermis, so its edge is raised and sharp. Cellulitis sits deep in the fat, so its edge fades out."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Folds tell you Candida","text":"Candida loves warm moist folds, so a diaper rash in the creases with satellite spots is candidal. Irritant diaper rash hits skin touching the diaper and spares the creases."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tetanus versus botulism","text":"Tetanospasmin blocks inhibitory transmitters, so muscles go stiff (spastic). Botulinum toxin blocks acetylcholine release, so muscles go limp (flaccid)."}],"highYield":["Honey-colored crusts around a child's mouth followed weeks later by cola-colored urine: impetigo then poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis.","Recurrent abscesses in a wrestling team: community MRSA with Panton-Valentine leukocidin.","Red, swollen leg with pain far worse than it looks and crepitus: necrotizing fasciitis.","Tender shin nodules with bilateral hilar adenopathy: erythema nodosum with sarcoidosis.","Umbilicated papules on the face of an adult with HIV: molluscum contagiosum.","Cat bite with cellulitis by the next day: Pasteurella multocida; dog bite in an asplenic patient: Capnocytophaga.","Painless black eschar with large edema in a wool or hide worker: cutaneous anthrax.","Itchy burrows in the finger webs worse at night, with itchy family members: scabies."],"pitfalls":["Rheumatic fever follows strep pharyngitis only; glomerulonephritis can follow strep throat or skin infection.","Tinea versicolor is a yeast (Malassezia), not a dermatophyte, even though it is called tinea.","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome spares mucosa and splits high; toxic epidermal necrolysis involves mucosa and the full epidermis.","Varicella shows lesions in different stages at once; smallpox lesions are all at the same stage.","Erythema nodosum heals without ulcer or scar; a nodule that ulcerates on the calf suggests something else."],"dumps":[{"t":"Depth of skin infections","p":"Draw a cross-section of skin from epidermis to muscle. Place impetigo, bullous impetigo, folliculitis, furuncle, erysipelas, cellulitis, and necrotizing fasciitis at the right depth and write the usual organism next to each.","l":["Impetigo: superficial epidermis, S aureus or S pyogenes","Bullous impetigo: granular layer split by S aureus exfoliative toxin","Folliculitis in the follicle; furuncle deeper; carbuncle is several joined furuncles","Erysipelas: upper dermis and lymphatics, S pyogenes, sharp raised edge","Cellulitis: deep dermis and fat, S pyogenes or S aureus, ill-defined edge","Necrotizing fasciitis: along fascia, pain out of proportion, crepitus","Portal of entry such as tinea pedis fissures or wounds"]},{"t":"Exposure to organism table","p":"Make a table with exposures down the left (cat or dog bite, dog bite without spleen, human bite, seawater, fish tank, rose thorn, soil-contaminated deep wound, puncture wound, needle stick) and write the organism and its key clue for each.","l":["Cat or dog bite: Pasteurella multocida","Dog bite with asplenia: Capnocytophaga canimorsus","Human bite: Eikenella corrodens","Seawater: Vibrio vulnificus; fish tank: Mycobacterium marinum","Rose thorn: Sporothrix schenckii with lymphatic spread","Deep soil wound: C perfringens alpha toxin and gas gangrene","Puncture wound: C tetani, tetanospasmin blocks GABA and glycine release","Needle stick: hepatitis B over hepatitis C over HIV"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on the staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on the streptococci"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on cutaneous and subcutaneous mycoses"}],"videos":[],"related":["IN-9","IN-10","IN-11","IN-12","IN-13-C","IN-13-D"]},"MS-1":{"id":"MS-1","summary":"How somites, lateral plate mesoderm, and the pharyngeal arches build the bones, muscles, and joints, and how limb buds grow, rotate, and ossify. Exam questions link an embryonic step to its adult nerve supply, curve, or birth defect.","notes":[{"h":"Somites","points":["Paraxial mesoderm segments into somites from about day 20 (week 3 to 5).","Sclerotome (ventromedial) surrounds the notochord and neural tube to form vertebrae and ribs.","Dermomyotome splits into dermatome (dermis of the back) and myotome (skeletal muscle).","Epaxial myotome forms the deep back muscles (erector spinae), supplied by dorsal rami.","Hypaxial myotome forms limb and body wall muscles, supplied by ventral rami.","Each segment keeps its own spinal nerve, which is the basis of dermatomes and myotomes."]},{"h":"Vertebrae and discs","points":["Resegmentation: each vertebral body forms from the caudal half of one sclerotome fused to the cranial half of the next.","Because of this shift, segmental arteries end up running across the middle of each vertebral body.","The spinal nerve exits at the level of the disc, and each myotome bridges two vertebrae so muscles can move the spine.","Notochord persists between bodies as the nucleus pulposus; the annulus fibrosus forms from sclerotome cells.","A notochord remnant can give rise to a chordoma (often sacral or clival)."]},{"h":"Limb buds and patterning","points":["Limb buds appear in week 4 (upper about day 24, lower about day 28): lateral plate mesoderm core covered by ectoderm.","Limb muscle comes from hypaxial myotome cells that migrate into the bud; limb bone, cartilage, and tendon come from lateral plate mesoderm.","Apical ectodermal ridge (AER) secretes FGFs that drive proximal to distal outgrowth; loss of AER gives limb truncation.","Zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) at the posterior bud secretes sonic hedgehog for anterior to posterior (thumb to little finger) pattern.","Extra Shh activity on the anterior side gives mirror digits and preaxial polydactyly.","HOX genes set the identity of each segment; HOXD13 mutation causes synpolydactyly.","Thalidomide during limb bud formation (about days 24 to 36 after fertilization) causes phocomelia, with hands or feet attached close to the trunk."]},{"h":"Limb rotation","points":["In week 7 to 8 the upper limb rotates 90 degrees laterally and the lower limb rotates about 90 degrees medially.","Upper limb: extensors end up posterior and the thumb lateral.","Lower limb: extensors end up anterior, flexors posterior, and the big toe medial.","Medial rotation twists lower limb dermatomes into a spiral (L4 medial leg, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot)."]},{"h":"Bone and joint formation","points":["Intramembranous: mesenchyme turns straight into bone (skull vault, facial bones, most of the clavicle).","Endochondral: a hyaline cartilage model is replaced by bone (long bones, vertebrae, skull base).","Primary ossification centers appear in the diaphysis of most long bones by about week 12; secondary centers form in the epiphyses, mostly after birth.","Clavicle is the first bone to start ossifying (about week 5 to 6).","Joints form from interzone mesenchyme between bone models; synovial joints form when the interzone cavitates into a joint cavity."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Somite parts and their fates","cols":["Part","Adult derivative","Nerve supply"],"rows":[["Sclerotome","Vertebrae, ribs, annulus fibrosus","Not applicable"],["Dermatome","Dermis of the back","Segmental cutaneous branches"],["Epaxial myotome","Deep (intrinsic) back muscles","Dorsal rami"],["Hypaxial myotome","Limb, intercostal, and abdominal wall muscles","Ventral rami"],["Notochord (not a somite)","Nucleus pulposus","Not applicable"]]},{"title":"Limb patterning signals","cols":["Signal center","Molecule","Axis or job","Defect when disrupted"],"rows":[["Apical ectodermal ridge","FGFs (FGF8)","Proximal to distal outgrowth","Truncated limb (amelia or meromelia)"],["Zone of polarizing activity","Sonic hedgehog","Anterior to posterior (digit identity)","Ectopic anterior Shh gives polydactyly"],["Dorsal ectoderm","WNT7","Dorsal to ventral","Ventralized limb (dorsal side takes on ventral features)"],["Mesenchyme","HOX genes","Segment identity","Synpolydactyly with HOXD13 mutation"]]},{"title":"Pharyngeal arch muscles and nerves","cols":["Arch","Muscles","Nerve"],"rows":[["1st","Masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids, mylohyoid, anterior belly of digastric, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini","CN V3"],["2nd","Muscles of facial expression, stapedius, stylohyoid, posterior belly of digastric","CN VII"],["3rd","Stylopharyngeus","CN IX"],["4th","Most pharyngeal constrictors, cricothyroid, levator veli palatini","CN X (pharyngeal branches; superior laryngeal nerve to cricothyroid)"],["6th","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","CN X (recurrent laryngeal)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Epaxial goes to the back door","text":"Epaxial muscles sit behind the vertebral axis and are reached by the dorsal ramus. Everything in front (limbs, body wall) is hypaxial and comes off the ventral ramus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the arches by nerve","text":"Arches 1, 2, 3 go with cranial nerves 5, 7, 9 (skip one each time). Arches 4 and 6 both use CN X: superior laryngeal for 4, recurrent laryngeal for 6."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Thumbs out, big toes in","text":"Upper limb turns out (lateral) and lower limb turns in (medial). That is why the thumb is lateral, the big toe is medial, and the knee faces forward while the elbow faces back."}],"highYield":["Erector spinae are epaxial and supplied by dorsal rami; limb muscles are hypaxial and supplied by ventral rami.","Nucleus pulposus is the notochord remnant; chordoma arises from notochord rests.","AER makes FGF for length; ZPA makes Shh for thumb to pinky order.","Ectopic anterior Shh expression causes polydactyly.","Thalidomide exposure during limb bud formation causes phocomelia.","Skull vault and most of the clavicle form by intramembranous ossification; long bones form by endochondral ossification.","First arch muscles chew (CN V3); second arch muscles make facial expression (CN VII).","Medial rotation of the lower limb puts the knee extensors in front and twists the dermatomes into a spiral."],"pitfalls":["Dermatome in a somite means the embryonic block that forms dermis; in the clinic it means a skin area supplied by one spinal nerve.","Limb muscles come from somites (myotome), while limb bones and tendons come from lateral plate mesoderm.","The vertebral body is built from two neighboring sclerotome halves, so it does not match a single somite.","Stylopharyngeus is the only third arch muscle (CN IX) and is not a constrictor; the pharyngeal constrictors are fourth arch muscles (CN X)."],"dumps":[{"t":"Somite to adult","p":"Draw a cross section of a week 4 embryo showing the neural tube, notochord, and one somite. Label sclerotome, dermatome, and myotome, then draw arrows to what each becomes and which ramus supplies the muscles.","l":["Somite comes from paraxial mesoderm","Sclerotome moves around notochord and neural tube to form vertebrae and ribs","Dermatome forms dermis of the back","Epaxial myotome forms deep back muscles supplied by dorsal rami","Hypaxial myotome forms limb and body wall muscles supplied by ventral rami","Notochord becomes the nucleus pulposus","Vertebral body forms from caudal half of one sclerotome plus cranial half of the next"]},{"t":"Limb bud signals","p":"Draw a limb bud and label the AER, the ZPA, and the dorsal ectoderm. Beside each, write its signal molecule, the axis it controls, and the defect you see when it goes wrong.","l":["AER at the distal tip secretes FGF","AER drives proximal to distal growth; loss gives truncation","ZPA at the posterior margin secretes sonic hedgehog","Shh sets anterior to posterior digit order; ectopic anterior Shh gives polydactyly","Dorsal ectoderm WNT7 sets dorsal to ventral pattern","HOX genes set segment identity","Thalidomide during limb bud formation causes phocomelia","Upper limb rotates laterally, lower limb medially in weeks 7 to 8"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Axial Skeleton"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Muscular System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Limbs"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck (section on pharyngeal arches)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Embryology | Development of Muscular System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tuhv_JrmaXg"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Embryology | Development of Skeletal System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WmlbqVyhMts"}],"related":["MS-2","MS-6","MS-9","MS-3"]},"MS-2":{"id":"MS-2","summary":"The microscopic structure of the three muscle types, compact and spongy bone, the bone cells, the growth plate, and the three joint classes. Exam questions ask you to predict what changes in a sarcomere, a growth plate, or a joint when something goes wrong.","notes":[{"h":"Sarcomere bands","points":["Sarcomere runs from one Z line to the next; thin (actin) filaments anchor at the Z line.","A band is the full length of the thick (myosin) filaments; it stays the same length during contraction.","I band holds thin filaments only and shortens.","H zone holds thick filaments only and shortens; the M line sits in its center and links thick filaments.","Z lines move closer together because filaments slide past each other; no filament changes length."]},{"h":"Three muscle types","points":["Skeletal: long multinucleate fibers with peripheral nuclei, striated, triads (T tubule plus two terminal cisternae) at the A to I junction.","Skeletal regeneration comes from satellite cells.","Cardiac: branched cells with one or two central nuclei, striated, dyads at the Z line.","Intercalated discs join cardiac cells: gap junctions for electrical spread, desmosomes and fascia adherens for mechanical strength.","Smooth: fusiform cells with one central nucleus, no striations, dense bodies anchor actin (Z line equivalent).","Smooth muscle uses caveolae instead of a T tubule system and can divide (hyperplasia)."]},{"h":"Bone structure","points":["Compact bone is built of osteons: concentric lamellae around a central (Haversian) canal with vessels and nerves.","Volkmann canals run across the bone and connect Haversian canals to each other and to the periosteum.","Osteocytes sit in lacunae and touch each other through canaliculi joined by gap junctions.","Spongy (cancellous) bone is a lattice of trabeculae with marrow in the spaces; no osteons.","Woven bone has random collagen and is weak (fetal bone, fracture callus, Paget disease); lamellar bone has organized layers and replaces it."]},{"h":"Bone cells","points":["Osteoblasts come from mesenchymal stem cells (RUNX2) and secrete type I collagen osteoid and alkaline phosphatase.","Osteoblasts express RANKL and OPG, so they control osteoclast activity; PTH receptors are on osteoblasts.","Osteocytes are osteoblasts trapped in matrix; they sense load and secrete FGF23 (phosphate loss in urine) and sclerostin.","Osteoclasts come from the monocyte and macrophage line, fuse into multinucleated cells, and need M-CSF and RANKL.","Osteoclast ruffled border pumps acid (H+ made by carbonic anhydrase II) and releases cathepsin K into Howship lacunae."]},{"h":"Growth plate and joints","points":["Zones from epiphysis to diaphysis: reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, ossification.","Achondroplasia: activating FGFR3 mutation slows chondrocyte proliferation, giving a thin proliferative zone and short long bones.","Rickets: poor mineralization lets the hypertrophic zone pile up, giving a wide, irregular plate with frayed, cupped metaphyses.","Fibrous joints: sutures, syndesmoses (distal tibiofibular), gomphoses (tooth in socket).","Cartilaginous joints: synchondroses use hyaline cartilage (growth plate, first sternocostal joint); symphyses use fibrocartilage (pubic symphysis, intervertebral disc).","Synovial joints: avascular articular hyaline cartilage with no perichondrium; synoviocytes make hyaluronan and lubricin for synovial fluid."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle","cols":["Feature","Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth"],"rows":[["Cell shape","Long cylinder","Branched","Fusiform (spindle)"],["Nuclei","Many, peripheral","One or two, central","One, central"],["Striations","Yes","Yes","No"],["Cell junctions","None between fibers","Intercalated discs (gap junctions, desmosomes)","Gap junctions in single unit muscle"],["T tubule pattern","Triads at A to I junction","Dyads at Z line","No T tubules (caveolae)"],["Actin anchor","Z line","Z line","Dense bodies"],["Repair","Satellite cells","Very limited (scar)","Cell division"]]},{"title":"Growth plate zones","cols":["Zone (epiphysis to diaphysis)","What happens","Disease effect"],"rows":[["Reserve (resting)","Small scattered chondrocytes","Little change"],["Proliferative","Chondrocytes divide in stacked columns","Thin in achondroplasia (FGFR3 activation)"],["Hypertrophic","Chondrocytes enlarge and release VEGF","Expanded and disorganized in rickets"],["Calcification","Cartilage matrix mineralizes, chondrocytes die","Fails in rickets"],["Ossification","Osteoblasts lay bone on calcified cartilage","Fails in rickets, leaving excess osteoid"]]},{"title":"Joint classes","cols":["Class","Subtype","Tissue","Example"],"rows":[["Fibrous","Suture","Dense connective tissue","Sagittal suture"],["Fibrous","Syndesmosis","Ligament or interosseous membrane","Distal tibiofibular joint"],["Fibrous","Gomphosis","Periodontal ligament","Tooth in its socket"],["Cartilaginous","Synchondrosis (primary)","Hyaline cartilage","Epiphyseal plate, first sternocostal joint"],["Cartilaginous","Symphysis (secondary)","Fibrocartilage","Pubic symphysis, intervertebral disc"],["Synovial","Diarthrosis","Articular hyaline cartilage, capsule, synovium","Knee, shoulder"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HIZ shrinkage","text":"During contraction the H zone, the I band, and the Z to Z distance (sarcomere) all shorten. The A band is the one that stays the same."},{"kind":"trick","label":"A is for Always","text":"The A band is set by thick filament length, and myosin never changes length, so the A band always stays constant."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Blasts build, clasts cut","text":"Osteoblasts build bone and come from mesenchyme. Osteoclasts cut bone and come from blood (monocytes), which is why they are multinucleated like other fused macrophages."}],"highYield":["I band and H zone shorten during contraction; the A band stays constant.","Intercalated discs with gap junctions let cardiac muscle contract as a functional syncytium.","Osteoclasts are monocyte lineage cells that use cathepsin K and acid in Howship lacunae.","Osteocytes secrete FGF23, which increases urinary phosphate loss.","Achondroplasia (FGFR3 gain of function) shortens the proliferative zone, so endochondral bones are short while intramembranous bones grow normally.","Rickets widens the growth plate because hypertrophic cartilage is not mineralized and removed.","Articular cartilage has no blood supply and no perichondrium; it is fed by synovial fluid."],"pitfalls":["Triads belong to skeletal muscle; dyads belong to cardiac muscle.","Haversian canals run along the bone; Volkmann canals cross it.","Synchondroses are hyaline; symphyses are fibrocartilage.","Woven bone in an adult is abnormal (fracture callus, Paget disease, tumors); lamellar bone is the normal mature form."],"dumps":[{"t":"Sarcomere before and after","p":"Draw one sarcomere relaxed and the same sarcomere contracted. Label the Z lines, M line, A band, I band, and H zone in both, and mark which ones got shorter.","l":["Sarcomere spans Z line to Z line","Thin actin filaments attach at Z lines","Thick myosin filaments are centered on the M line","A band equals thick filament length and stays constant","I band (thin only) shortens","H zone (thick only) shortens","Filaments slide; they do not change length"]},{"t":"Growth plate column","p":"Draw a column of the epiphyseal growth plate from epiphysis to diaphysis and label the five zones. Next to it, show how the plate looks in achondroplasia and in rickets.","l":["Reserve zone sits nearest the epiphysis","Proliferative zone has stacked dividing chondrocytes","Hypertrophic zone has large chondrocytes","Calcification zone mineralizes matrix","Ossification zone has osteoblasts laying bone on calcified cartilage","Achondroplasia: thin proliferative zone from FGFR3 activation","Rickets: wide, disorganized hypertrophic zone from failed mineralization"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Cartilage"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Sarcomere Structure: Actin & Myosin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nrf_g5m8fVM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Smooth Muscle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2EeUy7xopdo"}],"related":["MS-7","MS-13-A2","MS-9","MS-1"]},"MS-3":{"id":"MS-3","summary":"The bones of the skull, spine, and limbs, the skull foramina and what passes through them, and the nerves that run against bone where fractures happen. Exam questions give a fracture or a curve and ask what structure or function is lost.","notes":[{"h":"Vertebrae by region","points":["Cervical: transverse foramina carry the vertebral artery (usually C6 to C1); C3 to C6 have bifid spines; C7 is the vertebra prominens.","Atlas (C1) has no body and no spinous process; it holds the skull and allows nodding.","Axis (C2) has the dens, the pivot for head rotation at the atlantoaxial joint.","Thoracic: costal facets on bodies and transverse processes for ribs; long downward spines; ribs limit motion.","Lumbar: large kidney-shaped bodies for weight bearing; sagittal facets favor flexion and extension."]},{"h":"Spinal curves","points":["Thoracic and sacral kyphoses are primary curves present before birth.","Cervical lordosis appears with head control and lumbar lordosis with standing and walking (secondary curves).","Scoliosis is lateral curvature plus vertebral rotation; adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is most common in girls.","Excess kyphosis can follow osteoporotic compression fractures.","Severe thoracic scoliosis stiffens the chest wall, causing restrictive lung disease (low TLC and vital capacity, normal or high FEV1/FVC)."]},{"h":"Skull foramina and pterion","points":["Superior orbital fissure: CN III, IV, V1, VI and the superior ophthalmic vein.","Foramen rotundum: V2. Foramen ovale: V3. Foramen spinosum: middle meningeal artery.","Internal acoustic meatus: CN VII and VIII. Jugular foramen: CN IX, X, XI and the internal jugular vein.","Foramen magnum: medulla, vertebral arteries, spinal roots of CN XI.","Pterion is where frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet; it is thin.","The anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery runs deep to the pterion, so a fracture there causes an epidural hematoma."]},{"h":"Upper limb bones","points":["Clavicle fractures most often in the middle third.","Scapula: glenoid fossa for the humeral head, acromion and coracoid process for muscle and ligament attachment.","Radius is lateral (thumb side) and widest at the wrist; ulna is medial and widest at the elbow (olecranon).","Humeral surgical neck: axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery.","Midshaft humerus (radial groove): radial nerve and deep brachial artery.","Medial epicondyle: ulnar nerve. Supracondylar fracture: median nerve and brachial artery.","Scaphoid is the most fractured carpal (snuffbox tenderness); its blood enters distally, so the proximal pole can undergo avascular necrosis.","Lunate is the most dislocated carpal and can compress the median nerve."]},{"h":"Lower limb bones","points":["Hip bone: ilium, ischium, and pubis meet and fuse at the acetabulum.","Femur carries body weight to the tibia; the fibula bears little weight but anchors muscles and forms the lateral malleolus.","Femoral neck fracture can tear the medial circumflex femoral artery and cause avascular necrosis of the femoral head.","Fibular neck fracture injures the common fibular nerve: foot drop, weak eversion, numb dorsum of foot.","Foot has medial longitudinal, lateral longitudinal, and transverse arches.","Medial longitudinal arch is held by the spring ligament, tibialis posterior tendon, and plantar aponeurosis; collapse gives flat foot (pes planus)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Regional vertebral features","cols":["Region","Key features","Functional result"],"rows":[["Cervical","Small bodies, transverse foramina, bifid spines (C3 to C6)","Wide range of motion, protects vertebral artery"],["C1 atlas","No body, no spinous process, lateral masses","Nodding at atlanto-occipital joint"],["C2 axis","Dens","Rotation at atlantoaxial joint"],["Thoracic","Costal facets, long downward spines","Rib attachment, limited flexion"],["Lumbar","Large bodies, sagittal facets","Weight bearing, flexion and extension, little rotation"]]},{"title":"Skull openings and contents","cols":["Opening","Contents"],"rows":[["Optic canal","CN II, ophthalmic artery"],["Superior orbital fissure","CN III, IV, V1, VI, superior ophthalmic vein"],["Foramen rotundum","CN V2"],["Foramen ovale","CN V3"],["Foramen spinosum","Middle meningeal artery"],["Internal acoustic meatus","CN VII, CN VIII"],["Jugular foramen","CN IX, X, XI, internal jugular vein"],["Hypoglossal canal","CN XII"],["Foramen magnum","Medulla, vertebral arteries, spinal roots of CN XI"]]},{"title":"Fracture site and structure at risk","cols":["Fracture site","Nerve at risk","Main deficit"],"rows":[["Humeral surgical neck","Axillary","Weak abduction (deltoid), numb lateral shoulder"],["Midshaft humerus (radial groove)","Radial","Wrist drop"],["Supracondylar humerus","Median","Weak thumb opposition and finger flexion"],["Medial epicondyle","Ulnar","Weak finger abduction, clawing of 4th and 5th digits"],["Fibular neck","Common fibular","Foot drop, weak eversion"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Standing Room Only","text":"V1 passes through the Superior orbital fissure, V2 through foramen Rotundum, V3 through foramen Ovale."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"So Long To Pinky, Here Comes The Thumb","text":"Proximal row lateral to medial: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform. Distal row medial to lateral: hamate, capitate, trapezoid, trapezium."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Bone touches nerve","text":"Picture where a nerve lies directly on bone: axillary around the surgical neck, radial in the spiral groove, ulnar behind the medial epicondyle, common fibular around the fibular neck. The fracture site predicts the nerve."}],"highYield":["Surgical neck fracture of the humerus injures the axillary nerve; midshaft fracture injures the radial nerve.","Pterion fracture tears the middle meningeal artery, causing an epidural hematoma with a lucid interval.","Severe thoracic scoliosis causes restrictive lung disease from reduced chest wall expansion.","Fibular neck fracture causes foot drop from common fibular nerve injury.","Scaphoid fracture risks avascular necrosis of the proximal pole because blood enters distally.","Femoral neck fracture risks avascular necrosis from damage to the medial circumflex femoral artery.","The dens of C2 is the pivot for head rotation."],"pitfalls":["Anatomical neck fractures of the humerus are rare; the surgical neck is the classic site for axillary nerve injury.","Foramen spinosum carries the middle meningeal artery; foramen ovale carries V3.","Kyphosis is a front to back curve; scoliosis is a side to side curve with rotation.","CN VII enters the internal acoustic meatus but exits the skull through the stylomastoid foramen."],"dumps":[{"t":"Cranial base map","p":"Draw the floor of the skull from above and label the openings from the optic canal back to the foramen magnum. Write what passes through each one.","l":["Optic canal: CN II and ophthalmic artery","Superior orbital fissure: CN III, IV, V1, VI","Foramen rotundum: V2","Foramen ovale: V3","Foramen spinosum: middle meningeal artery","Internal acoustic meatus: CN VII and VIII","Jugular foramen: CN IX, X, XI and internal jugular vein","Foramen magnum: medulla and vertebral arteries"]},{"t":"Limb fracture table","p":"Make a table with three columns: fracture site, nerve or vessel at risk, and the deficit you would test. Fill it for the upper and lower limb.","l":["Humeral surgical neck: axillary nerve, weak abduction","Midshaft humerus: radial nerve, wrist drop","Supracondylar humerus: median nerve and brachial artery","Medial epicondyle: ulnar nerve, claw hand","Scaphoid: avascular necrosis of proximal pole","Femoral neck: medial circumflex femoral artery, avascular necrosis","Fibular neck: common fibular nerve, foot drop"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the cranium)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Scapula Anatomy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=70j06vbmclM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Coxa & Pelvis Anatomy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tdlmHi0n0Ss"}],"related":["MS-5","MS-4","MS-13-H1","MS-13-E4"]},"MS-4":{"id":"MS-4","summary":"How joints are classified, how they are innervated, and how the shoulder, knee, and jaw stay stable. Exam questions give a dislocation, a positive exam test, or referred pain and ask which structure is involved.","notes":[{"h":"Joint classification","points":["By movement: synarthrosis (immovable), amphiarthrosis (slightly movable), diarthrosis (freely movable, all synovial).","Hinge: humeroulnar joint, interphalangeal joints (flexion and extension).","Pivot: median atlantoaxial joint, proximal radioulnar joint (rotation).","Ball and socket: shoulder, hip (movement in all axes).","Saddle: first carpometacarpal joint (thumb opposition). Condyloid: wrist, metacarpophalangeal joints.","Plane: acromioclavicular and intercarpal joints (gliding)."]},{"h":"Joint innervation","points":["Hilton law: nerves to the muscles that move a joint also supply that joint and the skin over it.","Joint nerves carry proprioception (position sense) and nociception (pain).","The hip is supplied by the femoral, obturator, and sciatic nerves; the obturator and femoral nerves also supply the knee region.","Hip disease (for example slipped capital femoral epiphysis) can therefore present as knee pain."]},{"h":"Shoulder","points":["Glenoid fossa is shallow, and the labrum only deepens it slightly, so bone gives little stability.","Rotator cuff tendons hold the humeral head in the glenoid (dynamic stability).","Anterior dislocation is most common, usually with the arm abducted and externally rotated.","The axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck and is stretched in anterior dislocation.","Axillary nerve injury: weak abduction past about 15 degrees (deltoid), weak lateral rotation (teres minor), numb skin over the lower deltoid."]},{"h":"Knee","points":["ACL runs from the anterior tibia to the lateral femoral condyle and stops forward slide of the tibia.","PCL runs from the posterior tibia to the medial femoral condyle and stops backward slide of the tibia.","MCL resists valgus stress (blow to the outside of the knee); LCL resists varus stress.","Medial meniscus is attached to the MCL and capsule, so it moves less and tears more often.","Classic unhappy triad from a lateral blow: ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus."]},{"h":"Temporomandibular joint","points":["Synovial joint between the mandibular condyle and the temporal bone, divided by an articular disc.","Lower compartment allows hinge rotation; upper compartment allows gliding (translation) onto the articular eminence.","Lateral pterygoid pulls the condyle and disc forward to open and protrude the jaw.","Innervated by the auriculotemporal and masseteric branches of CN V3.","Anterior disc displacement: on opening, the condyle snaps forward under the displaced disc and clicks; jaw pain and limited opening can follow."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Synovial joint types","cols":["Type","Movement","Example"],"rows":[["Hinge","Flexion and extension in one plane","Humeroulnar, interphalangeal"],["Pivot","Rotation around one axis","Median atlantoaxial, proximal radioulnar"],["Ball and socket","Movement in all axes","Shoulder, hip"],["Saddle","Two axes plus opposition","First carpometacarpal"],["Condyloid","Two axes, no rotation","Radiocarpal (wrist), metacarpophalangeal"],["Plane","Gliding","Acromioclavicular, intercarpal"]]},{"title":"Knee structures and their tests","cols":["Structure","Job","Test","Positive finding"],"rows":[["ACL","Stops anterior tibial slide","Anterior drawer, Lachman","Tibia pulls forward excessively"],["PCL","Stops posterior tibial slide","Posterior drawer","Tibia pushes back excessively"],["MCL","Resists valgus force","Valgus stress","Medial joint line opens"],["LCL","Resists varus force","Varus stress","Lateral joint line opens"],["Menisci","Cushion and spread load","McMurray","Painful click on rotation"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Ligament letters","text":"ACL attaches to the Anterior tibia and runs to the Lateral femur. PCL is the opposite: posterior tibia to medial femur. The name tells you where it sits on the tibia."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Valgus pushes the knee in","text":"A valgus force pushes the knee toward the midline and stretches the medial side, so it tests the MCL. Varus stretches the lateral side and tests the LCL."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Knee pain, check the hip","text":"Because of Hilton law, the obturator and femoral nerves carry pain from the hip and the knee region. Always examine the hip in a child or adolescent with knee pain."}],"highYield":["Anterior shoulder dislocation stretches the axillary nerve, causing deltoid weakness and numbness over the lower deltoid.","The rotator cuff provides most glenohumeral stability because the glenoid is shallow.","Lachman test is the most sensitive exam test for an ACL tear.","Medial meniscus tears more often because it is fixed to the MCL.","Hip disease can present as knee pain through the obturator and femoral nerves.","TMJ clicking points to articular disc displacement.","The first carpometacarpal joint is a saddle joint; the median atlantoaxial joint is a pivot."],"pitfalls":["A positive anterior drawer points to the ACL; a positive posterior drawer points to the PCL.","Valgus stress tests the MCL, even though the force is applied to the lateral knee.","Posterior shoulder dislocation is rare and classically follows seizures or electric shock.","The TMJ disc sits in a synovial joint; TMJ articular surfaces are covered with fibrocartilage."],"dumps":[{"t":"Knee from above","p":"Draw the top of the tibia viewed from above. Place the medial and lateral menisci, the ACL, the PCL, and the collateral ligaments, then write the clinical test for each.","l":["ACL attaches anteriorly on the tibia","PCL attaches posteriorly on the tibia","Medial meniscus is C-shaped and attached to the MCL","Lateral meniscus is more circular and more mobile","Anterior drawer and Lachman test the ACL","Posterior drawer tests the PCL","Valgus stress tests the MCL; varus stress tests the LCL","Unhappy triad: ACL, MCL, medial meniscus"]},{"t":"Shoulder dislocation","p":"Draw the glenohumeral joint from the front, then draw an anterior dislocation. Trace the axillary nerve and list what the patient loses.","l":["Glenoid is shallow; labrum deepens it","Rotator cuff provides dynamic stability","Anterior dislocation is most common","Mechanism is abduction plus external rotation","Axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck of the humerus","Deltoid weakness gives weak abduction","Teres minor weakness gives weak lateral rotation","Numb skin over the lower deltoid"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb (section on joints of the upper limb)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb (section on the knee joint)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the temporomandibular joint)"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-2","MS-5","MS-13-H1","MS-13-E1"]},"MS-5":{"id":"MS-5","summary":"The action and nerve supply of the rotator cuff, the limb muscles, the head and neck muscles, and the trunk muscles. Exam questions give a weakness or a deformity and ask which nerve or muscle is injured.","notes":[{"h":"Rotator cuff","points":["Supraspinatus: starts abduction (first about 15 degrees); suprascapular nerve.","Infraspinatus: lateral rotation; suprascapular nerve.","Teres minor: lateral rotation; axillary nerve.","Subscapularis: medial rotation; upper and lower subscapular nerves.","Supraspinatus is injured most often, from impingement under the acromion; test with the empty can test."]},{"h":"Upper limb nerve injuries","points":["Musculocutaneous: weak elbow flexion and supination (biceps, brachialis), numb lateral forearm.","Radial: wrist drop from paralysis of wrist and finger extensors; high lesions also weaken the triceps.","Median at the wrist: thenar atrophy and loss of thumb opposition (ape hand), numb lateral three and a half fingers (palm spared in carpal tunnel syndrome).","Median at the elbow: also loses lateral finger flexors, so a fist shows the hand of benediction.","Ulnar: claw hand (4th and 5th digits) and weak finger abduction and adduction (interossei).","Long thoracic: serratus anterior paralysis gives a winged scapula; injured in axillary surgery."]},{"h":"Lower limb nerve injuries","points":["Femoral: weak knee extension (quadriceps), lost patellar reflex, numb anterior thigh.","Obturator: weak thigh adduction, numb medial thigh.","Superior gluteal: weak gluteus medius and minimus; standing on the injured leg, the pelvis drops on the opposite side (Trendelenburg sign).","Inferior gluteal: weak gluteus maximus, trouble rising from a chair or climbing stairs.","Common fibular: foot drop, steppage gait, weak eversion, numb dorsum of foot.","Tibial: weak plantarflexion, inversion, and toe flexion; numb sole."]},{"h":"Head and neck","points":["Muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids): CN V3.","Masseter, temporalis, and medial pterygoid close the jaw; lateral pterygoid opens and protrudes it.","Muscles of facial expression: CN VII.","All tongue muscles are CN XII except palatoglossus (CN X); a CN XII lesion makes the tongue deviate toward the injured side.","Sternocleidomastoid (turns the head to the opposite side) and trapezius (shrugs the shoulder): CN XI."]},{"h":"Trunk","points":["Erector spinae extend the back and are supplied by dorsal rami.","Diaphragm is the main muscle of inspiration; phrenic nerve C3 to C5.","Abdominal wall (external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, rectus abdominis): thoracoabdominal nerves (T7 to T11), subcostal nerve (T12), and L1 branches.","Levator ani (puborectalis, pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus) forms the pelvic floor and supports pelvic organs; puborectalis helps fecal continence."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Rotator cuff","cols":["Muscle","Main action","Nerve"],"rows":[["Supraspinatus","Initial abduction (about 0 to 15 degrees)","Suprascapular"],["Infraspinatus","Lateral rotation","Suprascapular"],["Teres minor","Lateral rotation","Axillary"],["Subscapularis","Medial rotation","Upper and lower subscapular"]]},{"title":"Limb nerve injuries","cols":["Nerve","Key muscles","Classic sign"],"rows":[["Musculocutaneous","Biceps, brachialis","Weak elbow flexion and supination"],["Radial","Wrist and finger extensors","Wrist drop"],["Median","Thenar muscles, lateral finger flexors","Ape hand; hand of benediction if lesion is proximal"],["Ulnar","Interossei, medial lumbricals, hypothenar","Claw hand of 4th and 5th digits"],["Long thoracic","Serratus anterior","Winged scapula"],["Femoral","Quadriceps","Weak knee extension, lost patellar reflex"],["Obturator","Thigh adductors","Weak adduction"],["Superior gluteal","Gluteus medius and minimus","Trendelenburg sign (opposite hip drops)"],["Common fibular","Dorsiflexors and evertors","Foot drop"],["Tibial","Plantarflexors, tibialis posterior","Weak plantarflexion and inversion"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SItS","text":"Rotator cuff: Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, teres minor, Subscapularis. The small t reminds you teres minor is the one with a different nerve (axillary)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive","text":"The phrenic nerve comes from C3 to C5. A spinal cord injury above C3 stops spontaneous breathing."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"M's munch, lateral lowers","text":"Masseter, teMporalis, and Medial pterygoid close the jaw (they munch). The Lateral pterygoid Lowers the jaw (opens it)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Trendelenburg: the good side sags","text":"The gluteus medius on the stance leg holds the pelvis level. If it is weak, the pelvis drops on the opposite, lifted side. The lesion is on the side of the leg the patient stands on."}],"highYield":["Supraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator cuff muscle.","Teres minor is the only rotator cuff muscle supplied by the axillary nerve.","Midshaft humerus fracture or crutch pressure in the axilla gives wrist drop (radial nerve).","Long thoracic nerve injury after mastectomy causes a winged scapula.","Superior gluteal nerve injury gives a positive Trendelenburg sign with drop of the opposite hip.","Common fibular nerve injury gives foot drop and loss of eversion.","Tongue deviates toward the side of a CN XII lesion.","Diaphragm irritation refers pain to the shoulder through C3 to C5."],"pitfalls":["Supraspinatus starts abduction; the deltoid takes over after about 15 degrees.","Infraspinatus and teres minor both laterally rotate but have different nerves (suprascapular versus axillary).","Ape hand comes from median nerve injury; claw hand comes mainly from ulnar nerve injury.","In Trendelenburg sign the pelvis drops on the side opposite the injured nerve.","Palatoglossus is the one tongue muscle supplied by CN X."],"dumps":[{"t":"Upper limb nerve table","p":"Make a table of the musculocutaneous, radial, median, ulnar, axillary, and long thoracic nerves. For each, list the key muscles, the classic sign of injury, and a common injury site.","l":["Musculocutaneous: biceps and brachialis, weak elbow flexion","Radial: extensors, wrist drop, midshaft humerus","Median: thenar muscles, ape hand, carpal tunnel or supracondylar fracture","Ulnar: interossei, claw hand, medial epicondyle","Axillary: deltoid and teres minor, weak abduction, surgical neck or dislocation","Long thoracic: serratus anterior, winged scapula, axillary surgery"]},{"t":"Lower limb nerve table","p":"Make a table of the femoral, obturator, superior gluteal, inferior gluteal, common fibular, and tibial nerves. For each, list the key muscles, the movement lost, and the sign you would see.","l":["Femoral: quadriceps, knee extension, lost patellar reflex","Obturator: adductors, thigh adduction","Superior gluteal: gluteus medius and minimus, Trendelenburg sign","Inferior gluteal: gluteus maximus, trouble climbing stairs","Common fibular: dorsiflexors and evertors, foot drop","Tibial: plantarflexors and tibialis posterior, weak plantarflexion and inversion"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Neck"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (section on the anterolateral abdominal wall)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Muscles of the Upper Limb","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ToNL6jMEjuE"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Muscles of the Lower Limb | Anatomy Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q0WDD704P1M"}],"related":["MS-3","MS-4","MS-10","MS-13-H1"]},"MS-6":{"id":"MS-6","summary":"Where bone, cartilage, and tendon come from, which collagen types sit in which tissues, how collagen and elastic fibers are built, and why cartilage and tendons heal slowly. Exam questions name a vitamin, mineral, or gene defect and ask which collagen step fails.","notes":[{"h":"Embryonic origins","points":["Limb bones, cartilage, and tendons come from lateral plate (somatic) mesoderm.","Vertebrae and ribs come from sclerotome (paraxial mesoderm).","Most of the face and the pharyngeal arch cartilages come from neural crest mesenchyme.","Skeletal muscle itself comes from somite myotomes, so a limb's muscles and its tendons have different origins."]},{"h":"Collagen types","points":["Type I: bone, tendon, ligament, skin, fibrocartilage (annulus fibrosus); about 90 percent of body collagen.","Type II: hyaline cartilage, nucleus pulposus, vitreous humor.","Type III: reticular fibers, blood vessels, skin, early granulation tissue.","Type IV: basement membrane (network, not fibrils)."]},{"h":"Collagen synthesis","points":["Inside the cell (RER): alpha chains are made with repeating Gly-X-Y, where X and Y are often proline and lysine.","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases add OH groups; they need vitamin C (ascorbate) and iron.","Some hydroxylysines are glycosylated.","Three chains wind into a triple helix (procollagen), lined up by disulfide bonds between their C-terminal propeptides, and are secreted.","Outside the cell: procollagen peptidases cut off the end propeptides to make tropocollagen.","Lysyl oxidase (needs copper) forms covalent cross-links between tropocollagen molecules in the fibril."]},{"h":"Defects by step","points":["Scurvy: no vitamin C, poor hydroxylation, weak triple helix; bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, poor wound healing.","Osteogenesis imperfecta: COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutation (often glycine substitution) gives too little or faulty type I collagen; fragile bones, blue sclerae, hearing loss.","Menkes disease: X-linked ATP7A mutation blocks copper transport, so lysyl oxidase cannot cross-link; kinky hair, hypotonia, neurodegeneration."]},{"h":"Cartilage, tendon, and elastic fibers","points":["Aggrecan carries chondroitin and keratan sulfate chains and binds hyaluronan through link protein.","These negative GAGs pull in cations and water, which pushes back against compression; type II collagen resists stretch.","Articular cartilage has no blood vessels, so chondrocytes get nutrients by diffusion and repair is poor.","Tendons have few cells and a poor blood supply, so they heal slowly.","Elastic fibers have an elastin core laid on fibrillin-1 microfibrils; elastin is cross-linked by desmosine.","Elastic fibers fill the ligamentum flavum (yellow ligament), which recoils when the spine straightens.","Fibrillin-1 binds latent TGF-beta; in Marfan syndrome (FBN1 mutation) TGF-beta is released and overactive."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Collagen types","cols":["Type","Main tissues","Linked disorder"],"rows":[["I","Bone, tendon, ligament, skin, fibrocartilage","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],["II","Hyaline cartilage, nucleus pulposus, vitreous","Some chondrodysplasias"],["III","Reticular fibers, vessels, granulation tissue","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"],["IV","Basement membranes","Alport syndrome, Goodpasture syndrome"]]},{"title":"Collagen synthesis steps and defects","cols":["Step","Where","Requirement","Disease when it fails"],"rows":[["Gly-X-Y chain translation","RER","Normal COL genes","Osteogenesis imperfecta (glycine substitution)"],["Proline and lysine hydroxylation","RER","Vitamin C, iron","Scurvy"],["Glycosylation of hydroxylysine","RER","Glycosyltransferases","No classic exam disease"],["Triple helix formation","RER","Intact chains, disulfide bonds","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],["Propeptide cleavage","Outside cell","Procollagen peptidases","Some Ehlers-Danlos types"],["Cross-linking","Outside cell","Lysyl oxidase, copper","Menkes disease"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Be So Totally Cool, Read Books","text":"Type I: Bone, Skin, Tendon. Type II: Cartilage. Type III: Reticulin. Type IV: Basement membrane."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Type II: cartwolage","text":"Type II collagen is in cartilage, including the nucleus pulposus and vitreous body."},{"kind":"trick","label":"C for collagen, Cu for cross-links","text":"Vitamin C works inside the cell (hydroxylation). Copper works outside the cell (lysyl oxidase cross-links). Scurvy breaks the inside step; Menkes breaks the outside step."}],"highYield":["Vitamin C is a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase; deficiency weakens the triple helix.","Lysyl oxidase needs copper; Menkes disease (ATP7A) blocks cross-linking.","Osteogenesis imperfecta is most often a type I collagen defect (COL1A1 or COL1A2).","Hyaline cartilage and nucleus pulposus are type II collagen; annulus fibrosus is type I.","Aggrecan GAGs hold water, which lets articular cartilage resist compression.","Fibrillin-1 sequesters TGF-beta; FBN1 mutation in Marfan syndrome raises TGF-beta signaling.","Ligamentum flavum is rich in elastic fibers."],"pitfalls":["Hydroxylation happens inside the cell; cross-linking happens outside the cell.","Type IV collagen forms sheets in basement membranes, not fibrils.","Limb tendons come from lateral plate mesoderm even though limb muscles come from somites.","Fibrillin-1 is the scaffold that is defective in Marfan syndrome; elastin is the stretchy core laid on it. Lysyl oxidase cross-links both collagen and elastin (desmosine)."],"dumps":[{"t":"Collagen assembly line","p":"Draw a fibroblast and trace collagen synthesis from the RER to a cross-linked fibril outside the cell. At each step, write what it needs and the disease when it fails.","l":["Alpha chains are made in the RER with Gly-X-Y repeats","Proline and lysine hydroxylation needs vitamin C and iron (scurvy)","Hydroxylysine glycosylation","Triple helix forms as procollagen","Procollagen is secreted","Peptidases cleave propeptides to form tropocollagen","Lysyl oxidase with copper cross-links fibrils (Menkes)","Faulty or reduced type I chains cause osteogenesis imperfecta"]},{"t":"Why cartilage resists squeezing","p":"Draw a piece of articular cartilage matrix showing hyaluronan, aggrecan, GAG chains, and type II collagen. Explain how it resists compression and why it heals poorly.","l":["Hyaluronan backbone","Aggrecan core proteins attached through link protein","Chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate chains are negatively charged","Negative charges draw in cations and water","Bound water resists compression","Type II collagen network resists tension","No blood vessels, so nutrients arrive by diffusion and repair is slow"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycosaminoglycans, Proteoglycans, and Glycoproteins"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Connective Tissue"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Cartilage"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-8","MS-9","MS-13-A3","MS-13-D1","MS-13-D3"]},"MS-7":{"id":"MS-7","summary":"How skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle turn an electrical or chemical signal into force, and how force is graded. Exam questions give a channel, drug, or training change and ask how contraction or relaxation will change.","notes":[{"h":"Skeletal excitation-contraction coupling","points":["An action potential spreads along the sarcolemma and down the T tubules.","The dihydropyridine receptor (L-type voltage sensor) in the T tubule changes shape.","It is mechanically linked to ryanodine receptor 1 (RyR1) on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which opens and releases calcium.","Skeletal muscle does not need calcium entry from outside the cell to contract.","Calcium binds troponin C, which moves tropomyosin off the myosin binding sites on actin.","SERCA pumps calcium back into the SR (uses ATP), and the muscle relaxes."]},{"h":"Cross-bridge cycle","points":["Starting point: myosin head tightly bound to actin with no nucleotide (rigor state).","ATP binds myosin and releases it from actin.","ATP hydrolysis cocks the head; ADP and phosphate stay bound.","Head binds actin; phosphate release drives the power stroke; then ADP leaves.","Rigor mortis: without ATP, myosin cannot detach from actin, so muscles lock a few hours after death."]},{"h":"Grading skeletal force","points":["A motor unit is one motor neuron plus all the fibers it supplies.","Size principle: small motor units (slow, type I) are recruited first and large ones (fast, type II) last.","Summation: a second action potential before relaxation adds force, because the twitch lasts longer than the action potential.","At high firing rates twitches fuse into tetanus, the maximal sustained force.","Length-tension: active force is greatest at the resting sarcomere length with optimal actin-myosin overlap; too stretched or too short lowers force.","Force-velocity: the heavier the load, the slower the shortening; velocity is maximal at zero load and zero at maximal (isometric) force."]},{"h":"Cardiac muscle","points":["During the plateau, calcium enters through L-type calcium channels.","This calcium triggers RyR2 to release more calcium from the SR (calcium-induced calcium release).","The long refractory period lasts nearly as long as contraction, so cardiac muscle cannot summate or go into tetany.","Sympathetic beta-1 stimulation raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A.","PKA phosphorylates L-type channels (more calcium entry) and phospholamban.","Phosphorylated phospholamban stops inhibiting SERCA2, so the SR refills faster: faster relaxation and stronger next beat."]},{"h":"Smooth muscle","points":["Calcium enters through L-type channels or is released from the SR through IP3 receptors.","Calcium binds calmodulin; calcium-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase (MLCK).","MLCK phosphorylates the myosin light chain, which turns on cross-bridge cycling; smooth muscle has no troponin.","Myosin light chain phosphatase removes the phosphate and causes relaxation.","Latch state: dephosphorylated myosin stays attached and cycles slowly, holding tone with little ATP.","Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase; cGMP activates protein kinase G, lowers calcium, and activates the phosphatase, causing relaxation."]},{"h":"Fiber types and training","points":["Type I: slow, oxidative, red (myoglobin), many mitochondria and capillaries, fatigue resistant; posture muscles such as soleus.","Type II: fast, glycolytic, white, large glycogen stores, high force, fatigable.","Endurance training raises mitochondria, capillaries, and oxidative enzymes.","Resistance training enlarges type II fibers by adding myofibrils (hypertrophy)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Contraction in three muscle types","cols":["Feature","Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth"],"rows":[["Trigger","ACh at the neuromuscular junction","Pacemaker action potential","Nerves, hormones, stretch"],["Calcium source","SR only (RyR1)","Extracellular calcium triggers SR release (RyR2)","Extracellular and SR (IP3 receptor)"],["DHPR to RyR link","Mechanical","Calcium-induced calcium release","No T tubules"],["Calcium sensor","Troponin C","Troponin C","Calmodulin"],["Switch for cross-bridges","Tropomyosin moves off actin","Tropomyosin moves off actin","MLCK phosphorylates myosin light chain"],["Tetany possible","Yes","No (long refractory period)","Sustained tone (latch state)"]]},{"title":"Cross-bridge cycle","cols":["Step","Event","Result"],"rows":[["1","ATP binds myosin","Myosin releases actin"],["2","ATP is hydrolyzed","Head cocks with ADP and phosphate bound"],["3","Head binds exposed actin","Cross-bridge forms"],["4","Phosphate is released","Power stroke pulls actin toward the M line"],["5","ADP is released","Rigor state until new ATP binds"]]},{"title":"Muscle fiber types","cols":["Feature","Type I","Type II"],"rows":[["Speed","Slow twitch","Fast twitch"],["Energy","Oxidative phosphorylation","Glycolysis"],["Color","Red (myoglobin)","White"],["Mitochondria and capillaries","Many","Few"],["Fatigue","Resistant","Fatigable"],["Training response","Endurance raises oxidative capacity","Resistance causes hypertrophy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"One slow red ox","text":"Type 1 fibers are slow twitch, red, and use oxidative phosphorylation."},{"kind":"trick","label":"ATP lets go","text":"ATP is needed to release myosin from actin and to pump calcium back into the SR. With no ATP, muscle cannot relax, which is rigor mortis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Smooth muscle switch is on myosin","text":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle regulate the thin filament (troponin and tropomyosin). Smooth muscle regulates the thick filament (phosphorylating the myosin light chain)."}],"highYield":["Skeletal DHPR is mechanically coupled to RyR1; cardiac contraction depends on calcium entry triggering RyR2.","RyR1 mutation causes malignant hyperthermia with volatile anesthetics; dantrolene blocks RyR1.","ATP binding detaches myosin from actin; loss of ATP causes rigor mortis.","Small motor units are recruited first (size principle).","Phospholamban phosphorylation by PKA speeds SERCA2, improving relaxation and contractility.","The long cardiac refractory period prevents tetany.","Nitric oxide relaxes smooth muscle through cGMP; PDE5 inhibitors prolong this effect.","Endurance training raises oxidative capacity; resistance training enlarges type II fibers."],"pitfalls":["The power stroke happens with phosphate release; ATP binding causes detachment.","Smooth muscle uses calmodulin; skeletal and cardiac muscle use troponin C.","Phosphorylating the myosin light chain contracts smooth muscle; the phosphatase relaxes it.","Tetanus in muscle physiology means fused maximal contraction from rapid firing; the disease tetanus is caused by Clostridium tetani toxin.","Phospholamban inhibits SERCA when unphosphorylated; PKA phosphorylation removes that brake."],"dumps":[{"t":"Skeletal versus cardiac coupling","p":"Draw a T tubule next to the SR for a skeletal fiber and for a cardiac cell. Show how calcium is released in each, how it is removed, and why only one can go into tetany.","l":["Action potential travels down the T tubule","Skeletal: DHPR mechanically opens RyR1","Cardiac: L-type calcium entry triggers RyR2 (calcium-induced calcium release)","Calcium binds troponin C and moves tropomyosin","SERCA returns calcium to the SR","Phospholamban regulates SERCA2 in the heart; PKA phosphorylation speeds it","Cardiac long refractory period prevents tetany","Skeletal summation and tetanus come from rapid firing"]},{"t":"Smooth muscle on and off","p":"Draw a smooth muscle cell and trace the pathway from a rise in calcium to contraction, then from nitric oxide to relaxation.","l":["Calcium enters through L-type channels or SR IP3 receptors","Calcium binds calmodulin","Calcium-calmodulin activates MLCK","MLCK phosphorylates the myosin light chain and starts cross-bridge cycling","Myosin light chain phosphatase dephosphorylates and relaxes","Latch state holds tone with little ATP","Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase and raises cGMP","Protein kinase G lowers calcium and activates the phosphatase"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contraction of Skeletal Muscle"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation of Skeletal Muscle: Neuromuscular Transmission and Excitation-Contraction Coupling"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation and Contraction of Smooth Muscle"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Neuromuscular Junction | Excitation Contraction Coupling: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A96kAMPZXv8"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Neuromuscular Junction | Sliding Filament Theory: Part 3","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UW47uSLcM2g"}],"related":["MS-2","MS-10","MS-13-D2","MS-9"]},"MS-8":{"id":"MS-8","summary":"How vitamin D, PTH, calcitonin, and FGF23 keep calcium and phosphate in range, how muscle switches fuels as exercise goes on, and which minerals and cofactors bone and muscle depend on. Exam questions pair a lab pattern or an exercise history with the enzyme or hormone step that explains it, such as McArdle disease versus CPT II deficiency.","notes":[{"h":"Vitamin D activation","points":["Skin: UVB converts 7-dehydrocholesterol to cholecalciferol (vitamin D3); diet adds D2 and D3","Liver: 25-hydroxylase makes 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol), the main circulating and storage form","Kidney proximal tubule: 1-alpha-hydroxylase makes 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), the active hormone","Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D measures status: long half-life (weeks), loosely regulated, reflects skin plus diet supply","Calcitriol has a half-life of hours and is tightly controlled, so it can look normal even in deficiency","1-alpha-hydroxylase is turned up by PTH and low phosphate, and turned down by FGF23 and calcitriol itself"]},{"h":"Calcium and phosphate hormones","points":["Low ionized calcium is sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid chief cells, which then release PTH","PTH acts on osteoblasts (they carry the PTH receptor), which raise RANKL and lower OPG, so osteoclasts resorb bone","PTH increases calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule and blocks phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule","Calcitriol raises gut absorption of both calcium and phosphate","Calcitonin from thyroid C cells inhibits osteoclasts; minor role in adult calcium balance","FGF23 from osteocytes rises with high phosphate or calcitriol, causes phosphaturia, and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase","Osteoprotegerin (OPG) from osteoblasts is a decoy receptor that binds RANKL and limits osteoclast activation"]},{"h":"Muscle fuel and exercise","points":["First seconds: stored ATP plus phosphocreatine; creatine kinase moves phosphate from phosphocreatine to ADP","Up to about 2 minutes of hard effort: anaerobic glycolysis of muscle glycogen to lactate","Longer effort: oxidative metabolism of glucose, then a rising share from fatty acid beta-oxidation","Cori cycle: muscle lactate goes to the liver, becomes glucose by gluconeogenesis, and returns to muscle (net cost 4 ATP)","Creatinine forms nonenzymatically from creatine and phosphocreatine at a steady daily rate, so it tracks muscle mass"]},{"h":"Metabolic myopathies","points":["McArdle disease (glycogen storage disease type V): autosomal recessive loss of muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase)","McArdle: cramps and fatigue in brief intense exercise, myoglobinuria, high CK, blood lactate fails to rise with exercise","McArdle second wind: after about 10 minutes, blood flow brings glucose and fatty acids and the patient can continue","Carnitine shuttle: CPT I on the outer membrane, translocase, CPT II on the inner membrane move long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria","CPT II deficiency: weakness, muscle pain, and rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria after prolonged exercise, fasting, cold, or illness"]},{"h":"Minerals and cofactors","points":["Bone mineral is calcium hydroxyapatite, Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2, laid down on type I collagen","Magnesium is needed for PTH secretion and action; severe hypomagnesemia causes hypocalcemia that persists until magnesium is replaced","Copper is the cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen and elastin","Vitamin K drives gamma-carboxylation of glutamate on osteocalcin so it can bind calcium in bone matrix","Vitamin C drives prolyl and lysyl hydroxylation of collagen (see scurvy)"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hormones that set serum calcium and phosphate","cols":["Hormone","Source and trigger","Bone","Kidney","Gut","Net serum effect"],"rows":[["PTH","Parathyroid chief cells; low ionized calcium","Resorption via osteoblast RANKL","Calcium reabsorption up (DCT), phosphate excretion up (PCT), 1-alpha-hydroxylase up","Indirect, through calcitriol","Calcium up, phosphate down"],["Calcitriol","Kidney proximal tubule; PTH and low phosphate","Supplies mineral; resorption at high levels","Modest calcium and phosphate reabsorption","Calcium and phosphate absorption up","Calcium up, phosphate up"],["Calcitonin","Thyroid parafollicular C cells; high calcium","Inhibits osteoclasts","Minor increase in calcium excretion","None","Calcium down (minor)"],["FGF23","Osteocytes; high phosphate or calcitriol","No major direct effect","Phosphate excretion up, 1-alpha-hydroxylase down","Less absorption through lower calcitriol","Phosphate down"]]},{"title":"Muscle fuel across exercise duration","cols":["Time frame","Main ATP source","Key enzyme or pathway","Example"],"rows":[["0 to about 10 seconds","Stored ATP and phosphocreatine","Creatine kinase","100 m sprint, heavy lift"],["About 10 seconds to 2 minutes","Anaerobic glycolysis of muscle glycogen","Glycogen phosphorylase, glycolysis, lactate dehydrogenase","400 m run"],["Minutes to about an hour","Oxidative metabolism of glucose and glycogen, rising fat use","TCA cycle and oxidative phosphorylation","10 km run"],["Hours, or fasting","Fatty acid beta-oxidation","Carnitine shuttle (CPT I, CPT II), beta-oxidation","Marathon, long hike without food"]]},{"title":"McArdle disease versus CPT II deficiency","cols":["Feature","McArdle disease","CPT II deficiency"],"rows":[["Defect","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II (inner mitochondrial membrane)"],["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal recessive"],["Trigger","Brief, intense, or isometric exercise","Prolonged exercise, fasting, cold, infection"],["Fuel that fails","Muscle glycogen","Long-chain fatty acids"],["Second wind","Yes","No"],["Lactate rise with exercise","Absent","Normal"],["Myoglobinuria","Yes","Yes, often the presenting sign"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PTH = Phosphate Trashing Hormone","text":"PTH raises serum calcium but makes the proximal tubule dump phosphate, so serum phosphate falls."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Very Poor Carbohydrate Metabolism","text":"Glycogen storage diseases I, II, III, V: Von Gierke, Pompe, Cori, McArdle. McArdle (type V) is the Muscle one."},{"kind":"trick","label":"25 for status, 1,25 for action","text":"Order the 25-hydroxyvitamin D level to judge vitamin D stores. Calcitriol is the active hormone but is held steady by PTH, so it misleads."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sprint versus marathon","text":"Cramps in a short sprint point to glycogen (McArdle). Dark urine after a long run or a missed meal points to fat (CPT II)."}],"highYield":["Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the best test of vitamin D status","PTH acts on osteoclasts indirectly, through RANKL from osteoblasts","FGF23 lowers phosphate and calcitriol; excess FGF23 causes X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets","McArdle: flat lactate curve with exercise, myoglobinuria, and a second wind","CPT II deficiency: rhabdomyolysis after prolonged exercise or fasting, normal lactate rise","Hypomagnesemia causes hypocalcemia by impairing PTH release and action","Copper deficiency weakens collagen cross-links through lysyl oxidase","Serum creatinine depends on muscle mass as well as GFR"],"pitfalls":["Calcitriol level is a poor marker of vitamin D stores; high PTH can keep it normal while 25-hydroxyvitamin D is low.","Osteoclasts lack the PTH receptor; PTH signals through osteoblasts and RANKL.","McArdle and CPT II both cause myoglobinuria; the trigger (short burst versus prolonged effort or fasting) and the lactate response separate them.","A low serum creatinine in a frail older adult may reflect low muscle mass rather than excellent kidney function."],"dumps":[{"t":"Vitamin D and PTH map","p":"Draw skin, liver, kidney, gut, bone, and parathyroid. Trace vitamin D from 7-dehydrocholesterol to calcitriol, then add arrows for PTH, calcitonin, FGF23, and OPG with their effects on calcium and phosphate.","l":["UVB converts 7-dehydrocholesterol to cholecalciferol in skin","Liver 25-hydroxylation makes 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the status marker","Kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylation makes calcitriol","PTH raises RANKL on osteoblasts to activate osteoclasts","PTH increases distal tubule calcium reabsorption and causes phosphaturia","PTH and low phosphate turn on 1-alpha-hydroxylase; FGF23 turns it off","Calcitonin from C cells inhibits osteoclasts","OPG binds RANKL and blocks osteoclast activation"]},{"t":"Exercise fuel timeline","p":"Draw a timeline of exercise from the first second to several hours. Under each phase write the main fuel and enzyme, then mark where McArdle disease and CPT II deficiency each break down.","l":["Phosphocreatine and creatine kinase cover the first seconds","Anaerobic glycolysis of glycogen to lactate covers about 10 seconds to 2 minutes","Oxidative metabolism of glucose, then fatty acids, covers longer effort","Cori cycle returns lactate to the liver for gluconeogenesis","McArdle fails early: no glycogen breakdown, no lactate rise, second wind later","CPT II fails late: no long-chain fatty acid entry into mitochondria","Both can cause rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuria"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Sports Physiology"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycogen Metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fatty Acid, Triacylglycerol, and Ketone Body Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Endocrinology | Parathyroid Gland | Calcitonin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y64aXKCkHk4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Endocrinology - Calcium and Phosphate Regulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EEM0iRJNhU8"}],"related":["MS-7","MS-13-A","MS-13-A1","MS-13-A2","MS-13-A3","MS-13-E2"]},"MS-9":{"id":"MS-9","summary":"The genes that build the skeleton and muscle, and what happens when they fail: transcription factor defects, collagen and dystrophin mutations, repeat expansions, mitochondrial inheritance, and the tumor suppressor and fusion gene changes behind bone sarcomas. Exam questions ask you to predict severity from the type of mutation and to name the inheritance pattern from a pedigree or family history.","notes":[{"h":"Skeletal development genes","points":["RUNX2 is the master switch for osteoblast differentiation","RUNX2 haploinsufficiency (autosomal dominant) causes cleidocranial dysplasia: absent or small clavicles, open fontanelles, delayed suture closure, extra teeth","SOX9 drives chondrocyte differentiation from mesenchyme","HOX genes give each body segment its identity along the head to tail axis; mutations put structures in the wrong place (for example HOXD13 synpolydactyly)"]},{"h":"Achondroplasia and osteogenesis imperfecta","points":["Achondroplasia: FGFR3 gain-of-function mutation keeps the receptor active and slows chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate","Endochondral bone is short (limbs) while membranous bone is normal, so the head is large and the trunk near normal","Most achondroplasia cases are new autosomal dominant mutations linked to advanced paternal age","Type I collagen is two alpha-1 chains (COL1A1) and one alpha-2 chain (COL1A2), with glycine at every third residue","Glycine substitution: the bad chain enters many triple helices (3 of 4 when COL1A1 is hit) and spoils them (dominant negative), giving severe OI types II to IV","Null allele: no bad chain is made, only half the normal amount of collagen, giving mild OI type I (blue sclerae, fractures, hearing loss)"]},{"h":"Dystrophin and the reading frame","points":["DMD gene (Xp21) is the largest human gene, a big target, so about one third of cases are new mutations","Frameshift deletion leads to an early stop codon and no dystrophin: Duchenne, onset by about age 5, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy","In-frame deletion leaves a shorter, partly working dystrophin: Becker, later onset, slower course","Severity follows the reading frame, regardless of deletion size","Dystrophin anchors actin to the dystroglycan complex and extracellular matrix; without it the sarcolemma tears with contraction"]},{"h":"Repeat expansion and mitochondrial disease","points":["Myotonic dystrophy type 1: autosomal dominant CTG expansion in the DMPK gene, toxic RNA disrupts splicing","Anticipation: the repeat grows each generation; maternal transmission can cause a severe congenital form","Features: myotonia (slow grip release), distal weakness, cataracts, frontal balding, testicular atrophy, cardiac conduction block","Mitochondrial DNA comes only from the mother: all children of an affected mother can be affected, none from an affected father","Heteroplasmy: cells carry a mix of normal and mutant mtDNA, so severity varies widely within a family","Ragged red fibers on modified Gomori trichrome are clumps of abnormal mitochondria under the sarcolemma (MERRF, MELAS)"]},{"h":"Sarcoma genetics","points":["RB1 (13q14) controls the G1 to S checkpoint; a germline RB1 mutation (hereditary retinoblastoma) raises osteosarcoma risk","Germline TP53 loss (Li-Fraumeni syndrome) causes sarcomas, breast cancer, brain tumors, leukemia, and adrenocortical carcinoma at young ages","Ewing sarcoma: t(11;22) fuses EWSR1 to FLI1, making an abnormal transcription factor"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Musculoskeletal genetic disorders","cols":["Disorder","Gene","Inheritance","Mechanism","Key features"],"rows":[["Cleidocranial dysplasia","RUNX2","Autosomal dominant","Haploinsufficiency","Absent clavicles, open fontanelles, extra teeth"],["Achondroplasia","FGFR3","Autosomal dominant, mostly new mutations","Gain of function","Short limbs, large head, normal intelligence"],["Osteogenesis imperfecta type I","COL1A1 null allele","Autosomal dominant","Half the normal collagen (quantitative)","Blue sclerae, fractures, hearing loss"],["Osteogenesis imperfecta types II to IV","COL1A1 or COL1A2 glycine substitution","Mostly autosomal dominant","Dominant negative (qualitative)","Severe fractures and deformity, perinatal death in type II"],["Duchenne muscular dystrophy","DMD","X-linked recessive","Frameshift, no dystrophin","Gowers sign, pseudohypertrophy, dilated cardiomyopathy"],["Becker muscular dystrophy","DMD","X-linked recessive","In-frame, shortened dystrophin","Later onset, milder weakness"],["Myotonic dystrophy type 1","DMPK (CTG repeat)","Autosomal dominant with anticipation","Toxic RNA gain of function","Myotonia, cataracts, balding, testicular atrophy, conduction block"],["MERRF and MELAS","Mitochondrial tRNA genes","Maternal","Heteroplasmy, failed oxidative phosphorylation","Ragged red fibers, lactic acidosis, seizures or stroke-like episodes"],["Spinal muscular atrophy","SMN1 deletion","Autosomal recessive","Anterior horn cell loss","Floppy infant, tongue fasciculations"]]},{"title":"Bone and soft tissue sarcoma genetics","cols":["Tumor or syndrome","Genetic change","Normal role of the gene","Clinical clue"],"rows":[["Osteosarcoma after hereditary retinoblastoma","Germline RB1 loss plus second hit","Holds cells at the G1 to S checkpoint","Retinoblastoma in infancy, bone tumor in adolescence"],["Li-Fraumeni syndrome","Germline TP53 loss","Arrests the cell cycle or triggers apoptosis after DNA damage","Several early cancers in one family"],["Ewing sarcoma","t(11;22), EWSR1-FLI1 fusion","Fusion protein acts as an abnormal transcription factor","Boy with diaphyseal tumor, onion-skin periosteal reaction"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"My Tonia, My Testicles, My Toupee, My Cataracts","text":"Myotonic dystrophy: myotonia, testicular atrophy, frontal balding, cataracts. Add the heart: conduction block."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"11 + 22 = 33, Patrick Ewing's jersey number","text":"Ewing sarcoma carries t(11;22), the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count in threes","text":"Codons are read in threes. A deletion that is a multiple of 3 keeps the frame (Becker). Any other size shifts the frame and ends the protein early (Duchenne)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Three out of four","text":"Each collagen I molecule has two alpha-1 chains. If half the alpha-1 chains are faulty, only 1 in 4 molecules is fully normal, so a bad chain hurts more than a missing one."}],"highYield":["RUNX2 loss: cleidocranial dysplasia with absent clavicles","FGFR3 gain of function: achondroplasia, new mutations with advanced paternal age","OI: a bad collagen chain (dominant negative) is worse than no chain (null allele)","Duchenne versus Becker depends on the reading frame; deletion size does not decide it","Myotonic dystrophy: CTG repeat in DMPK with anticipation","Mitochondrial disease: maternal inheritance, heteroplasmy, ragged red fibers","Germline RB1 mutation raises osteosarcoma risk","Ewing sarcoma: EWSR1-FLI1 fusion from t(11;22)"],"pitfalls":["A large in-frame deletion causes Becker while a single-base deletion can cause Duchenne; judge by the frame.","Mild OI type I comes from too little normal collagen; the severe types come from abnormal collagen that poisons the helix.","Mitochondrial disorders skip transmission through fathers; an affected man's children are unaffected.","Achondroplasia spares membranous bone; the skull vault and face grow, so the head looks large."],"dumps":[{"t":"Collagen helix and OI","p":"Draw a type I collagen triple helix with two alpha-1 chains and one alpha-2 chain. Then draw what happens with a COL1A1 null allele and with a COL1A1 glycine substitution, and label which gives mild and which gives severe OI.","l":["Two alpha-1 chains (COL1A1) and one alpha-2 chain (COL1A2)","Glycine every third residue fits the tight center of the helix","Null allele: half as much normal collagen (quantitative defect)","Null allele gives mild type I OI with blue sclerae and hearing loss","Glycine substitution chain enters and distorts most helices","Dominant negative effect leaves only about 1 in 4 molecules normal","Glycine substitutions cause severe types II to IV"]},{"t":"Inheritance pattern table","p":"Make a table with columns for inheritance pattern, how it passes in a pedigree, and one musculoskeletal example. Fill rows for autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, X-linked recessive, repeat expansion with anticipation, and mitochondrial.","l":["Autosomal dominant: every generation, both sexes (achondroplasia, cleidocranial dysplasia)","Autosomal recessive: carrier parents, siblings at 1 in 4 risk (SMA, McArdle)","X-linked recessive: affected males, carrier mothers, no father to son spread (Duchenne, Becker)","Anticipation: earlier, more severe disease in later generations (myotonic dystrophy)","Mitochondrial: from mother to all children, never through fathers (MERRF, MELAS)","Heteroplasmy explains variable severity in mitochondrial disease","New mutations are common in achondroplasia and Duchenne"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Muscular Dystrophy - Duchenne, Becker and Mytonic","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o1uhhpjmzkw"}],"related":["MS-1","MS-6","MS-13-D","MS-13-D2","MS-13-D3","MS-13-F2","MS-13-F4"]},"MS-10":{"id":"MS-10","summary":"How nerves drive muscle: transmission at the neuromuscular junction, the stretch and tendon reflexes, and how electrolytes change excitability. Exam questions ask you to place a weakness at the nerve, the junction, or the muscle, and to separate myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, and botulism by their mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"Neuromuscular transmission","points":["Action potential reaches the terminal and opens presynaptic voltage-gated (P/Q-type) calcium channels","Calcium entry triggers SNARE proteins (synaptobrevin, syntaxin, SNAP-25) to fuse vesicles and release acetylcholine","Acetylcholine binds postsynaptic nicotinic receptors, ligand-gated cation channels, producing the end plate potential","The end plate potential normally exceeds threshold by a wide safety margin and fires a muscle action potential","Acetylcholinesterase in the cleft splits acetylcholine into acetate and choline, ending the signal"]},{"h":"Junction disorders","points":["Botulism: toxin cleaves SNARE proteins, so no acetylcholine release; descending flaccid paralysis with dilated pupils","Lambert-Eaton syndrome: antibodies against presynaptic calcium channels reduce release; often paraneoplastic with small cell lung cancer","Lambert-Eaton: proximal limb weakness that improves with repeated effort, low reflexes, dry mouth and other autonomic signs","Myasthenia gravis: antibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors block them and cause complement damage","Myasthenia gravis: weakness worsens with use and late in the day, ptosis and diplopia, normal reflexes and pupils, thymus abnormalities"]},{"h":"Spindles, tendon organs, and reflexes","points":["Muscle spindles lie in parallel with muscle fibers and sense length and rate of stretch","Ia afferents from spindles excite alpha motor neurons to the same muscle (monosynaptic stretch reflex) and inhibit the antagonist","Gamma motor neurons contract the spindle ends to keep it taut and set its sensitivity","Golgi tendon organs lie in series at the muscle-tendon junction and sense tension","Ib afferents act through inhibitory interneurons to relax the same muscle under high tension","Reflex roots: biceps C5 to C6, triceps C7, patellar L4, Achilles S1"]},{"h":"Neurogenic versus myopathic weakness","points":["Neurogenic (lower motor neuron): fasciculations, atrophy, low reflexes, often distal; CK normal or mildly high","Biopsy in denervation: grouped atrophy; after reinnervation, fiber type grouping","Spinal muscular atrophy: SMN1 deletion, anterior horn cell loss, floppy infant with tongue fasciculations","Myopathic: proximal weakness, high CK, reflexes kept until late","Type II fiber atrophy with normal CK points to disuse or corticosteroids"]},{"h":"Electrolytes and excitability","points":["Hypocalcemia lowers the threshold for sodium channel opening, so nerves fire on their own: tetany, perioral tingling, carpopedal spasm","Chvostek sign: tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear twitches the face","Trousseau sign: a blood pressure cuff inflated above systolic for 3 minutes causes carpal spasm","Hypokalemia hyperpolarizes the membrane, so threshold is harder to reach: weakness and cramps","Hyperkalemia depolarizes the membrane and inactivates sodium channels: weakness and cardiac arrhythmias"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Junction disorders side by side","cols":["Feature","Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton syndrome","Botulism"],"rows":[["Site","Postsynaptic","Presynaptic","Presynaptic"],["Target","Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (antibody)","Voltage-gated calcium channel (antibody)","SNARE proteins (toxin protease)"],["Repeated effort","Weakness worsens","Strength improves","Not a useful bedside clue"],["Muscles first affected","Eyelids and eye muscles, then bulbar","Proximal legs","Cranial nerves, then descending"],["Reflexes","Normal","Reduced","Normal or reduced"],["Autonomic signs and pupils","Absent; pupils spared","Dry mouth, constipation","Dilated pupils, dry mouth"],["Association","Thymoma or thymic hyperplasia","Small cell lung cancer","Home canned food, wounds, honey in infants"],["Repetitive nerve stimulation","Decrement","Increment at high rates","Increment at high rates"]]},{"title":"Muscle spindle versus Golgi tendon organ","cols":["Feature","Muscle spindle","Golgi tendon organ"],"rows":[["Location","In parallel with extrafusal fibers","In series at the muscle-tendon junction"],["Senses","Length and rate of stretch","Tension"],["Afferent","Ia (and II)","Ib"],["Reflex effect","Contracts the stretched muscle","Relaxes the tensed muscle"],["Efferent tuning","Gamma motor neurons","None"]]},{"title":"Deep tendon reflexes","cols":["Reflex","Main root","Muscle","Nerve"],"rows":[["Biceps","C5 to C6","Biceps brachii","Musculocutaneous"],["Triceps","C7","Triceps brachii","Radial"],["Patellar","L4","Quadriceps femoris","Femoral"],["Achilles","S1","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Tibial"]]},{"title":"Neurogenic versus myopathic weakness","cols":["Feature","Neurogenic","Myopathic"],"rows":[["Pattern","Often distal, asymmetric","Proximal, symmetric"],["Fasciculations","Present","Absent"],["Reflexes","Reduced","Normal until late"],["CK","Normal or mildly high","High"],["Biopsy","Grouped atrophy, fiber type grouping","Necrosis, regeneration, varied fiber size"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"S1, 2 buckle my shoe; L3, 4 kick the door; C5, 6 pick up sticks; C7, 8 lay them straight","text":"Achilles S1 to S2, patellar L3 to L4, biceps C5 to C6, triceps C7 to C8. The main roots to name are S1, L4, C5 to C6, and C7."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lambert-Eaton gets Easier with effort","text":"Repeated firing lets calcium build up in the terminal, so more acetylcholine is released and strength rises. In myasthenia the receptors are few, so strength falls."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Calcium guards the gate","text":"Calcium on the outside of the membrane stabilizes sodium channels. Take calcium away and the gate opens too easily, giving tetany."}],"highYield":["Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and blocks acetylcholine release","Lambert-Eaton: anti-calcium channel antibodies, small cell lung cancer, improves with use","Myasthenia gravis: anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies, ptosis and diplopia, worsens with use","Ia afferents carry spindle stretch; Ib afferents carry tendon organ tension","Grouped atrophy and fiber type grouping mean denervation and reinnervation","Type II fiber atrophy: disuse or corticosteroids","Hypocalcemia: Chvostek and Trousseau signs from increased excitability","Both low and high potassium cause weakness"],"pitfalls":["Ptosis with dilated pupils and descending paralysis is botulism; myasthenia spares the pupils.","Lambert-Eaton has low reflexes that can briefly return after exercise; myasthenia keeps normal reflexes.","Hyperkalemia causes weakness even though it depolarizes the membrane, because inactivated sodium channels cannot fire.","Steroid myopathy shows normal CK and type II atrophy, unlike inflammatory myositis with high CK."],"dumps":[{"t":"Neuromuscular junction","p":"Draw a neuromuscular junction from the motor nerve terminal to the muscle fiber. Label each step of transmission, then mark where botulinum toxin, Lambert-Eaton antibodies, and myasthenia antibodies act.","l":["Action potential opens presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels","Calcium triggers SNARE-mediated vesicle fusion","Acetylcholine binds postsynaptic nicotinic receptors","End plate potential triggers the muscle action potential","Acetylcholinesterase ends the signal","Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins","Lambert-Eaton antibodies block presynaptic calcium channels","Myasthenia antibodies block or destroy acetylcholine receptors"]},{"t":"Stretch reflex arc","p":"Draw the knee jerk reflex arc. Label the muscle spindle, afferent fiber type, spinal level, motor neurons, and the antagonist pathway. Add a Golgi tendon organ and its pathway in a second color.","l":["Tap stretches the quadriceps and its spindles","Ia afferent enters the cord at L4","Monosynaptic excitation of the quadriceps alpha motor neuron","Inhibitory interneuron relaxes the hamstrings","Gamma motor neurons keep the spindle taut","Golgi tendon organ sits in series at the tendon and senses tension","Ib afferent inhibits the same muscle through an interneuron"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation of Skeletal Muscle: Neuromuscular Transmission and Excitation-Contraction Coupling"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Motor Functions of the Spinal Cord; the Cord Reflexes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Musculoskeletal System | Neuromuscular Junction Pathologies: Part 4","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=31ri-1Nv61M"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Neurology | Spinal Cord: Stretch Reflex | Muscle Spindle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXV4fRC6nPc"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Neuromuscular Junction | Dr Najeeb | Part 1/2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RKhr_YXUVLE"}],"related":["MS-5","MS-7","MS-8","MS-11","MS-13-D2"]},"MS-11":{"id":"MS-11","summary":"How the immune system attacks joints, muscle, and connective tissue: autoimmunity in rheumatoid arthritis and the connective tissue diseases, hypersensitivity types, crystal-driven innate inflammation, and arthritis after infection or vaccination. Exam questions match an autoantibody to its disease and a joint picture to its immune mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","points":["Genetic risk: HLA-DR4 (shared epitope); environmental risk: smoking","Peptidyl arginine deiminase converts arginine to citrulline; citrullinated peptides break tolerance","CD4 T cells (Th1, Th17) and B cells drive synovitis; anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor (IgM against the Fc of IgG) appear","TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 inflame the synovium and cause systemic symptoms","Pannus (proliferating synovium with inflammatory cells and granulation tissue) erodes cartilage","RANKL from T cells and synovial fibroblasts activates osteoclasts, giving marginal erosions and periarticular osteopenia"]},{"h":"Rheumatoid arthritis clinical picture","points":["Symmetric MCP, PIP, and wrist synovitis; DIP joints spared","Morning stiffness over 1 hour that eases with use; fatigue and low-grade fever","Ulnar deviation, swan neck (PIP hyperextension, DIP flexion), boutonniere (PIP flexion, DIP hyperextension)","Rheumatoid nodules: central fibrinoid necrosis ringed by palisading histiocytes","Felty syndrome: rheumatoid arthritis, splenomegaly, and neutropenia with recurrent infections"]},{"h":"Other connective tissue diseases","points":["SLE arthritis is nonerosive; hand deformities can be correctable (Jaccoud arthropathy)","Systemic sclerosis: sclerodactyly, calcinosis, flexion contractures, Raynaud phenomenon","Mixed connective tissue disease: overlap of SLE, scleroderma, and myositis features with high anti-U1 RNP"]},{"h":"Hypersensitivity in joints and muscle","points":["Type II (antibody against tissue): rheumatic fever polyarthritis from anti-M protein cross-reaction (molecular mimicry)","Type II: Lambert-Eaton, where antibodies made against tumor calcium channels cross-react with nerve terminals","Type III (immune complex): SLE arthritis and nephritis","Type IV (T cell): polymyositis, with CD8 T cells killing muscle fibers"]},{"h":"Innate inflammation and infection-related arthritis","points":["Gout: macrophages take up urate crystals, NLRP3 inflammasome activates caspase-1, which releases IL-1 beta and recruits neutrophils","Septic arthritis: neutrophil proteases and bacterial enzymes destroy articular cartilage within days","Reactive arthritis: HLA-B27 linked sterile arthritis 1 to 4 weeks after enteric or Chlamydia infection","Parvovirus B19 and rubella infection: symmetric small joint arthritis in adults, mostly women, that mimics rheumatoid arthritis","Rubella vaccination: transient arthralgia or arthritis, most often in adult women"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Autoantibodies in rheumatic disease","cols":["Antibody","Disease","Clue"],"rows":[["Anti-CCP","Rheumatoid arthritis","Most specific for RA; can precede symptoms"],["Rheumatoid factor","Rheumatoid arthritis","IgM against IgG Fc; also seen in Sjogren syndrome and chronic infection"],["ANA","SLE and many others","Sensitive screening test, low specificity"],["Anti-dsDNA","SLE","Tracks disease activity and lupus nephritis"],["Anti-Smith","SLE","Most specific for SLE"],["Anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I)","Diffuse systemic sclerosis","Interstitial lung disease risk"],["Anticentromere","Limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)","Pulmonary hypertension risk"],["Anti-U1 RNP","Mixed connective tissue disease","Overlap features, Raynaud, puffy hands"],["Anti-Jo-1","Polymyositis and dermatomyositis","Interstitial lung disease, mechanic's hands"],["Anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La)","Sjogren syndrome","Anti-SSA in pregnancy risks neonatal heart block"]]},{"title":"Rheumatoid arthritis versus osteoarthritis","cols":["Feature","Rheumatoid arthritis","Osteoarthritis"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Autoimmune synovitis with pannus","Cartilage wear and repair failure"],["Joints","Symmetric MCP, PIP, wrist; DIP spared","DIP, PIP, hips, knees, first CMC"],["Morning stiffness","Over 1 hour","Under 30 minutes"],["Effect of use","Improves","Worsens"],["Hand findings","Ulnar deviation, swan neck, boutonniere","Heberden (DIP) and Bouchard (PIP) nodes"],["Radiograph","Marginal erosions, periarticular osteopenia, even joint space loss","Osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, uneven joint space loss"],["Systemic signs","Fatigue, nodules, anemia, high ESR","None"]]},{"title":"Hypersensitivity types in musculoskeletal disease","cols":["Type","Effector","Example"],"rows":[["II","IgG or IgM against tissue antigens","Rheumatic fever arthritis, Lambert-Eaton, myasthenia gravis"],["III","Immune complexes and complement","SLE arthritis, serum sickness arthralgia"],["IV","T cells","Polymyositis (CD8 T cells)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CREST","text":"Limited systemic sclerosis: Calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasia. Anticentromere antibody."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HLA-B27: PAIR","text":"Psoriatic arthritis, Ankylosing spondylitis, IBD-associated arthritis, Reactive arthritis."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ACID","text":"Hypersensitivity types I to IV: Anaphylactic, Cytotoxic (antibody), Immune complex, Delayed (T cell)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Swan neck versus boutonniere","text":"Look at the PIP. A swan's neck arches up (PIP hyperextended). A buttonhole lets the PIP poke through bent (PIP flexed)."}],"highYield":["Anti-CCP is the most specific test for rheumatoid arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis spares the DIP; osteoarthritis and psoriatic arthritis involve it","RANKL-driven osteoclasts cause RA bone erosions","Felty syndrome: RA, splenomegaly, neutropenia","Anti-U1 RNP: mixed connective tissue disease","Gout inflammation runs through the NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1 beta","Polymyositis is type IV (CD8 T cell) injury","Parvovirus B19 in adults can mimic early rheumatoid arthritis"],"pitfalls":["Rheumatoid factor alone is not specific for RA; anti-CCP is the better confirmatory antibody.","SLE arthritis does not erode bone; erosions point toward RA.","Lambert-Eaton is type II hypersensitivity even though it starts from a tumor, because antibodies attack a tissue antigen.","Reactive arthritis follows infection by weeks and the joint fluid is sterile; septic arthritis has organisms in the joint."],"dumps":[{"t":"RA joint drawing","p":"Draw a normal synovial joint and an RA joint side by side. Label the cells, cytokines, and structures that lead from citrullinated peptides to bone erosion.","l":["HLA-DR4 presents citrullinated peptides","CD4 T cells activate B cells and macrophages","Anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor are produced","TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 drive synovitis","Pannus grows over and erodes cartilage","RANKL activates osteoclasts at the joint margins","Periarticular osteopenia and marginal erosions on radiograph"]},{"t":"Autoantibody table","p":"From memory, make a two-column table matching each autoantibody to its disease: anti-CCP, anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith, anti-Scl-70, anticentromere, anti-U1 RNP, anti-Jo-1, anti-SSA, anti-SSB. Add one clinical clue for each.","l":["Anti-CCP: rheumatoid arthritis","Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith: SLE","Anti-Scl-70: diffuse systemic sclerosis","Anticentromere: limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)","Anti-U1 RNP: mixed connective tissue disease","Anti-Jo-1: myositis with interstitial lung disease","Anti-SSA and anti-SSB: Sjogren syndrome"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (section on joints)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Rheumatoid Arthritis Pathophysiology (signs and symptoms)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ld8PhyAHov8"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypersensitivity Type III (Immune complex hypersensitivity) - causes,  pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NKnAXcM5Ly0"}],"related":["MS-10","MS-13-B","MS-13-B4","MS-13-B5","MS-13-B7","MS-13-E1","MS-13-G1"]},"MS-12":{"id":"MS-12","summary":"The organisms that infect bone, joints, fascia, and muscle, and the virulence factors that let them do it. Exam questions give a setting (sickle cell disease, a nail through a sneaker, a deep dirty wound, a tick bite) and ask for the organism, its lab features, or the toxin behind the damage.","notes":[{"h":"Staphylococcus aureus","points":["Gram-positive cocci in clusters; catalase positive, coagulase positive","Protein A binds the Fc of IgG, blocking opsonization and phagocytosis","Surface adhesins bind collagen, fibronectin, and fibrinogen in bone and joint tissue","Biofilm on hardware and prostheses shields bacteria from antibodies and antibiotics","Most common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis; in children it seeds the metaphysis of long bones"]},{"h":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae","points":["Gram-negative diplococci, often inside neutrophils; ferments glucose only","Pili attach to mucosa and change antigens, so immunity does not last and no vaccine exists","IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA","Disseminated infection in sexually active young adults: tenosynovitis, pustular skin lesions, migratory polyarthralgia","Or a purulent monoarthritis, often the knee; terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency raises risk"]},{"h":"Clostridium perfringens and Streptococcus pyogenes","points":["C. perfringens: gram-positive, anaerobic, spore-forming rod in deep, dirty, low-oxygen wounds","Alpha toxin is a phospholipase C (lecithinase) that destroys cell membranes, causing myonecrosis and hemolysis","Gas from bacterial fermentation produces crepitus in gas gangrene","S. pyogenes: beta-hemolytic gram-positive cocci in chains, catalase negative, bacitracin sensitive","M protein resists phagocytosis and complement; hyaluronidase and streptokinase help spread along fascial planes","Pyrogenic exotoxins act as superantigens, linking MHC II to T cell receptors outside the peptide groove and causing toxic shock"]},{"h":"Borrelia burgdorferi","points":["Spirochete carried by Ixodes ticks in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States","Larvae pick up Borrelia from white-footed mice (the reservoir); nymphs transmit it to people","Transmission usually needs about 36 hours or more of tick attachment","Deer feed adult ticks and keep the tick population going but are not the bacterial reservoir","Late Lyme disease: intermittent or persistent arthritis of large joints, most often the knee"]},{"h":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis in the spine","points":["Spreads through blood from the lung to vertebral bodies, most often lower thoracic and upper lumbar (Pott disease)","Caseating granulomas destroy the anterior vertebral body, spread to the disc and next vertebra, and collapse them","Collapse makes an angular kyphosis (gibbus deformity)","Pus can track along the psoas sheath to form a psoas abscess with groin or hip pain"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Bone and joint infection by setting","cols":["Setting","Classic organism","Clue"],"rows":[["Most cases at any age","Staphylococcus aureus","Hematogenous spread to metaphysis in children"],["Sickle cell disease","Salmonella","Bone infarcts and functional asplenia"],["Injection drug use","Pseudomonas aeruginosa (also S. aureus)","Vertebrae or sternoclavicular joint"],["Nail puncture through a sneaker sole","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Foam in the shoe holds the organism"],["Cat or dog bite","Pasteurella multocida","Cellulitis within a day of the bite"],["Prosthetic joint or hardware","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Biofilm on foreign material"],["Vertebrae with slow course","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Pott disease, gibbus, psoas abscess"],["Sexually active young adult with septic joint","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Tenosynovitis and pustules"]]},{"title":"Organisms and virulence factors","cols":["Organism","Morphology and tests","Key virulence factors","Musculoskeletal disease"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Gram-positive cocci in clusters; catalase and coagulase positive","Protein A, collagen and fibronectin adhesins, biofilm","Osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, septic bursitis"],["Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Gram-negative diplococci; oxidase positive, glucose fermenter","Pili with antigenic variation, IgA protease","Disseminated gonococcal infection"],["Clostridium perfringens","Gram-positive anaerobic spore-forming rod","Alpha toxin (phospholipase C, lecithinase)","Gas gangrene (myonecrosis)"],["Streptococcus pyogenes","Gram-positive cocci in chains; beta-hemolytic, bacitracin sensitive","M protein, hyaluronidase, streptokinase, pyrogenic exotoxins","Necrotizing fasciitis, toxic shock"],["Borrelia burgdorferi","Spirochete","Outer surface protein switching","Lyme arthritis of the knee"],["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Acid-fast rod","Cord factor, mycolic acid cell wall","Pott disease of the spine"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"FAKE a Key Lyme pie","text":"Lyme disease: Facial nerve palsy, Arthritis, Kardiac block, Erythema migrans."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Mice hold it, deer feed it","text":"White-footed mice are the Borrelia reservoir for tick larvae. Deer feed adult ticks. Nymphs bite people."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lecithinase eats lecithin","text":"Cell membranes are rich in lecithin (phosphatidylcholine). An enzyme that cuts it destroys muscle and red cell membranes, the core of gas gangrene."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the most common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis","Protein A binds IgG Fc and blocks opsonization","Salmonella osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease","Pseudomonas after a puncture through a sneaker sole","Pasteurella multocida after cat or dog bites","C. perfringens alpha toxin is a lecithinase (phospholipase C)","Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens","Pott disease: vertebral collapse, gibbus deformity, psoas abscess"],"pitfalls":["Salmonella is the classic association in sickle cell disease, but S. aureus still causes many of those infections.","Gonococcal joint fluid cultures are often negative; culture or test the urethra, cervix, pharynx, and rectum too.","Deer are the host for adult ticks; mice are the Borrelia reservoir.","Gas gangrene toxin destroys membranes; streptococcal superantigens act by overactivating T cells."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lyme transmission cycle","p":"Draw the two-year Ixodes tick life cycle. Label each stage, the animal it feeds on, when it picks up or passes on Borrelia, and the stage of Lyme disease that causes knee arthritis.","l":["Eggs hatch into uninfected larvae","Larvae feed on white-footed mice and acquire Borrelia","Larvae molt into nymphs that keep the infection","Nymphs bite humans and transmit Borrelia","Transmission usually needs about 36 hours or more of attachment","Adults feed on deer","Late disease months later causes large joint arthritis, often the knee"]},{"t":"Setting to organism table","p":"Make a table listing each setting for bone or joint infection and the organism it points to. Add the Gram stain or morphology for each organism.","l":["S. aureus: gram-positive cocci in clusters, most common overall","Salmonella: gram-negative rod, sickle cell disease","Pseudomonas aeruginosa: gram-negative rod, sneaker puncture or injection drug use","Pasteurella multocida: gram-negative coccobacillus, animal bites","S. epidermidis: gram-positive cocci, coagulase negative, prosthetic material","M. tuberculosis: acid-fast rod, vertebrae","N. gonorrhoeae: gram-negative diplococci, sexually active young adults"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"The Neisseriae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Mycobacteria"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (section on osteomyelitis)"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-11","MS-13-G","MS-13-G1","MS-13-G2","MS-13-G3","MS-13-G4"]},"MS-13-A":{"id":"MS-13-A","summary":"Rickets, osteomalacia, and scurvy: two problems of mineralizing bone and one of building collagen. Exam questions give a child's skeletal findings or an adult's bone pain with lab values and ask for the deficiency, its mechanism, or the risk factor behind it.","notes":[{"h":"Rickets and osteomalacia: one mechanism","points":["Osteoid is made normally but does not mineralize, leaving wide seams of soft osteoid","Cause is usually vitamin D deficiency; phosphate loss (renal wasting) does the same","Before the growth plates close it is rickets (growth plate and bone); after they close it is osteomalacia (bone only)","Low vitamin D lowers gut calcium uptake, PTH rises, PTH wastes phosphate, and low phosphate stalls mineralization"]},{"h":"Risk factors for vitamin D deficiency","points":["Limited sun exposure, high latitude, covering clothing, and darker skin (melanin blocks UVB)","Exclusive breastfeeding without vitamin D supplements (breast milk is low in vitamin D)","Fat malabsorption: celiac disease, Crohn disease, cystic fibrosis, cholestasis, bariatric surgery","Liver disease (less 25-hydroxylation) and chronic kidney disease (less 1-alpha-hydroxylation)","Anticonvulsants such as phenytoin, phenobarbital, and carbamazepine speed vitamin D breakdown"]},{"h":"Osteomalacia","points":["Diffuse bone pain and tenderness, proximal muscle weakness, waddling gait, fractures","Looser zones (pseudofractures): thin lucent bands across the cortex at the femoral neck, pubic rami, ribs, and scapula","Labs: calcium low or normal, phosphate low, alkaline phosphatase high, PTH high, 25-hydroxyvitamin D low"]},{"h":"Rickets","points":["Craniotabes (soft skull), frontal bossing, delayed fontanelle closure","Rachitic rosary: swelling at the costochondral junctions","Bowed legs (genu varum) once walking, widened wrists, delayed teeth","Radiograph: widened, cupped, frayed metaphyses with a wide growth plate","X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets: excess FGF23 wastes phosphate; vitamin D level normal, calcitriol inappropriately low"]},{"h":"Scurvy","points":["Vitamin C is the cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase in collagen synthesis","Underhydroxylated chains cannot form stable triple helices and are degraded, leaving weak vessels and scars","Perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, swollen bleeding gums, easy bruising, hemarthrosis, poor wound healing","Children: painful subperiosteal hemorrhage, refusal to walk, and leg tenderness","Risks: diets without fruit and vegetables, alcohol use disorder, older adults living alone, very restricted diets"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Nutritional bone and collagen disorders","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Osteomalacia","Vitamin D deficiency after growth plate closure; renal phosphate loss","Failed mineralization of osteoid","Bone pain, proximal weakness, Looser zones","Fractures, falls, hypocalcemia"],["Rickets","Vitamin D deficiency in a growing child; X-linked hypophosphatemia","Failed mineralization of osteoid and growth plate cartilage","Rachitic rosary, craniotabes, frontal bossing, genu varum, cupped metaphyses","Short stature, deformity, hypocalcemic seizures"],["Scurvy","Vitamin C deficiency from poor diet or alcohol use disorder","Failed prolyl and lysyl hydroxylation of collagen","Perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, bleeding gums, poor healing","Hemarthrosis, subperiosteal hemorrhage, anemia"]]},{"title":"Lab patterns in mineralization disorders","cols":["Disorder","Calcium","Phosphate","Alkaline phosphatase","PTH","25-hydroxyvitamin D"],"rows":[["Vitamin D deficiency (rickets or osteomalacia)","Low or normal","Low","High","High","Low"],["X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets","Normal","Low","High","Normal or mildly high","Normal"],["Chronic kidney disease","Low","High","Normal or high","High","Variable"],["Osteoporosis (for contrast)","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Same disease, different age","text":"If the growth plates are open, failed mineralization is rickets. If they are closed, it is osteomalacia."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the PTH","text":"Low vitamin D, low calcium absorption, high PTH, phosphate dumped in urine. That chain explains why phosphate is low and alkaline phosphatase is high."},{"kind":"trick","label":"C makes the collagen hold","text":"Vitamin C adds the hydroxyl groups that let collagen chains bond into a firm triple helix. Without it, vessels leak and wounds reopen."}],"highYield":["Rickets and osteomalacia: defective mineralization with excess osteoid","Osteomalacia labs: low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH, low 25-hydroxyvitamin D","Looser zones are pseudofractures of osteomalacia","Rachitic rosary and craniotabes are signs of rickets","X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets: FGF23 excess with normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D","Anticonvulsants, fat malabsorption, and darker skin raise vitamin D deficiency risk","Scurvy: failed prolyl and lysyl hydroxylation of collagen","Perifollicular hemorrhage with corkscrew hairs points to scurvy"],"pitfalls":["Osteoporosis has normal labs and normally mineralized bone; osteomalacia has abnormal labs and soft, undermineralized bone.","Calcium can be normal in vitamin D deficiency because high PTH pulls calcium from bone.","X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets does not respond to ordinary vitamin D because the problem is phosphate wasting.","Scurvy bleeding comes from weak vessel walls; platelet counts and clotting tests are usually normal."],"dumps":[{"t":"Vitamin D deficiency cascade","p":"Draw a flowchart from low vitamin D to soft bone. At each step write which lab rises or falls, then add the bedside findings in a child and in an adult.","l":["Low vitamin D lowers gut calcium and phosphate absorption","Low calcium raises PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism)","PTH keeps calcium low-normal by resorbing bone","PTH wastes phosphate in urine, so phosphate falls","Alkaline phosphatase rises with osteoblast activity","Osteoid stays unmineralized","Child: rachitic rosary, craniotabes, genu varum, cupped metaphyses","Adult: bone pain, proximal weakness, Looser zones"]},{"t":"Collagen synthesis and scurvy","p":"Draw collagen synthesis from the ribosome to the fibril. Mark the step that needs vitamin C and list the scurvy findings that follow when it fails.","l":["Pro-alpha chains made on rough ER","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase need vitamin C","Hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix","Hydroxylysine allows glycosylation and later cross-links","Unstable helices are degraded, so collagen is weak","Fragile vessels: perifollicular hemorrhage, bleeding gums, hemarthrosis","Poor wound healing and subperiosteal hemorrhage in children"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Calcium Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_gD5XWvONik"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Endocrinology - Calcium and Phosphate Regulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EEM0iRJNhU8"}],"related":["MS-6","MS-8","MS-13-E","MS-13-E2"]},"MS-13-B":{"id":"MS-13-B","summary":"The inflammatory and overuse disorders of joints, tendons, bursae, and muscle: spondyloarthropathies, crystal arthritis, myositis, polymyalgia rheumatica, fibromyalgia, bursitis, and tendinopathy. Exam questions turn on a few sharp clues, such as crystal shape, which joints are involved, whether CK is high, and which HLA type or infection came first.","notes":[{"h":"Seronegative spondyloarthropathies","points":["Ankylosing spondylitis: HLA-B27, young men, enthesitis and bilateral sacroiliitis","AS back pain is worse in the morning and with rest, better with exercise; chest expansion and lumbar flexion fall","Syndesmophytes bridge vertebrae into a bamboo spine","AS complications: anterior uveitis, aortitis with aortic regurgitation, restrictive lung disease, fractures of the fused spine","Reactive arthritis: HLA-B27, 1 to 4 weeks after Chlamydia trachomatis or Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, Campylobacter; joint fluid sterile","Reactive arthritis: asymmetric leg arthritis, conjunctivitis, urethritis, enthesitis, keratoderma blennorrhagicum","Psoriatic arthritis: DIP involvement, dactylitis (sausage digits), nail pitting, pencil-in-cup deformity; axial disease linked to HLA-B27"]},{"h":"Gout and pseudogout","points":["Purine breakdown: hypoxanthine to xanthine to uric acid, both steps by xanthine oxidase","Underexcretion causes most gout: kidney disease, thiazide and loop diuretics, alcohol, low-dose aspirin","Overproduction: Lesch-Nyhan (HGPRT deficiency), tumor lysis, glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency","Acute gout: hot, red first MTP (podagra) after alcohol or a purine-rich meal","Chronic gout: tophi, punched-out erosions with overhanging edges, uric acid kidney stones","Pseudogout: calcium pyrophosphate crystals in the knee or wrist, chondrocalcinosis; linked to hyperparathyroidism and hemochromatosis"]},{"h":"Myositis and polymyalgia rheumatica","points":["Dermatomyositis: complement-mediated capillary injury, perifascicular atrophy, heliotrope rash, Gottron papules; screen for internal cancer","Polymyositis: endomysial CD8 T cells invading muscle fibers, no rash","Both: symmetric proximal weakness, high CK, myofiber necrosis; anti-Jo-1 marks interstitial lung disease risk","Polymyalgia rheumatica: adults over 50 with shoulder and hip girdle pain and stiffness, high ESR, normal CK and strength","PMR travels with giant cell arteritis; new headache, jaw claudication, or visual change signals risk of vision loss","Steroid myopathy: proximal weakness with normal CK and type II fiber atrophy without inflammation"]},{"h":"Bursitis and tendinopathy","points":["Prepatellar bursitis from kneeling; olecranon bursitis from leaning or trauma; subacromial and trochanteric from overuse","Septic bursitis (most often Staphylococcus aureus) follows a skin break, most often prepatellar or olecranon","Tendinopathy (tendinosis): overuse causes collagen disorganization and neovascularization with few inflammatory cells","Lateral epicondylitis: extensor carpi radialis brevis origin; medial epicondylitis: common flexor-pronator origin","Supraspinatus impingement: painful arc from about 60 to 120 degrees of abduction","De Quervain tenosynovitis: abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis, positive Finkelstein test","Plantar fasciitis: heel pain with first morning steps; fluoroquinolones raise Achilles tendinopathy and rupture risk"]},{"h":"Fibromyalgia, JIA, and other soft tissue","points":["Fibromyalgia: central pain sensitization; widespread pain for at least 3 months, fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, mostly women","Fibromyalgia labs are normal: ESR, CRP, and CK","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis: arthritis before age 16 lasting at least 6 weeks","Oligoarticular JIA: young girls, ANA positive, silent anterior uveitis; systemic JIA: daily spiking fever and salmon-colored rash","Adhesive capsulitis: capsular inflammation and fibrosis, loss of both active and passive shoulder motion, linked to diabetes","Ganglion cyst: myxoid degeneration near a joint capsule or tendon sheath, often dorsal wrist, no true lining"]}],"charts":[{"title":"Inflammatory musculoskeletal conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Ankylosing spondylitis","HLA-B27, young men","Enthesitis and sacroiliitis with new bone formation","Inflammatory back pain better with exercise, low chest expansion, bamboo spine","Anterior uveitis, aortic regurgitation, restrictive lung disease, spinal fracture"],["Bursitis","Kneeling, leaning on elbow, overuse, skin break","Inflammation of a synovial-lined bursa; S. aureus if septic","Local swelling and tenderness over the bursa","Septic bursitis, chronic bursitis"],["Fibromyalgia","Women, sleep disturbance, depression, anxiety","Central pain sensitization","Widespread pain at least 3 months, fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, normal labs","Chronic pain and disability without tissue damage"],["Gout","Men, alcohol, purine-rich food, diuretics, kidney disease","Urate crystals activate NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1 beta","Podagra, needle-shaped negatively birefringent crystals, tophi","Erosions, uric acid stones, urate nephropathy"],["Myositis (dermatomyositis, polymyositis)","Adults; dermatomyositis also in children and with internal cancer","DM: complement injury to capillaries; PM: CD8 T cells","Proximal weakness, high CK; DM rash (heliotrope, Gottron papules)","Interstitial lung disease (anti-Jo-1), dysphagia, myocarditis"],["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Adults over 50, women more often","Inflammation of shoulder and hip girdle bursae and synovium","Girdle pain and stiffness, high ESR, normal CK and strength","Giant cell arteritis with vision loss"],["Reactive arthritis","HLA-B27; Chlamydia or Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, Campylobacter","Sterile immune arthritis triggered by bacterial antigens","Arthritis, conjunctivitis, urethritis, enthesitis, keratoderma blennorrhagicum","Chronic or recurrent arthritis, uveitis"],["Tendinopathy","Repetitive overuse, age, fluoroquinolones","Collagen disorganization with little inflammation (tendinosis)","Epicondylitis, painful arc, Finkelstein test, heel pain","Tendon rupture (Achilles), rotator cuff tear"]]},{"title":"Gout versus pseudogout","cols":["Feature","Gout","Pseudogout (CPPD)"],"rows":[["Crystal","Monosodium urate","Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate"],["Shape","Needle-shaped","Rhomboid"],["Birefringence","Strongly negative (yellow when parallel)","Weakly positive (blue when parallel)"],["Typical joint","First MTP, ankle, knee","Knee, wrist"],["Imaging","Punched-out erosions, tophi","Chondrocalcinosis"],["Associations","Alcohol, diuretics, kidney disease, high cell turnover","Older age, hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia"]]},{"title":"Separating the arthritides","cols":["Feature","Ankylosing spondylitis","Reactive arthritis","Psoriatic arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"rows":[["HLA link","HLA-B27","HLA-B27","HLA-B27 with axial disease","HLA-DR4"],["Joints","Sacroiliac joints and spine","Asymmetric large leg joints","DIP joints, asymmetric; dactylitis","Symmetric MCP, PIP, wrist; DIP spared"],["Distinctive signs","Bamboo spine, low chest expansion","Conjunctivitis, urethritis, keratoderma","Nail pitting, pencil-in-cup","Ulnar deviation, swan neck, nodules"],["RF and anti-CCP","Negative","Negative","Negative","Positive"]]},{"title":"Weakness or girdle pain: telling them apart","cols":["Feature","Dermatomyositis","Polymyositis","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Steroid myopathy"],"rows":[["Strength","Proximal weakness","Proximal weakness","Normal (pain limits movement)","Proximal weakness, legs more"],["CK","High","High","Normal","Normal"],["ESR","Normal or high","Normal or high","High","Not raised by the myopathy itself"],["Biopsy","Perifascicular atrophy, perivascular inflammation, capillary loss","Endomysial CD8 T cells, fiber necrosis","Normal muscle","Type II fiber atrophy, no inflammation"],["Skin","Heliotrope rash, Gottron papules","Normal","Normal","Cushingoid features"]]},{"title":"Tendon and soft tissue disorders","cols":["Disorder","Structure","Key finding","Risk"],"rows":[["Lateral epicondylitis","Extensor carpi radialis brevis origin","Pain with resisted wrist extension","Repetitive gripping and wrist extension"],["Medial epicondylitis","Common flexor-pronator origin","Pain with resisted wrist flexion and pronation","Golf, throwing"],["Supraspinatus impingement","Supraspinatus tendon under the acromion","Painful arc about 60 to 120 degrees","Overhead work and sports"],["De Quervain tenosynovitis","Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis","Positive Finkelstein test","Repetitive thumb use, new parents lifting infants"],["Plantar fasciitis","Plantar fascia at the medial calcaneal tubercle","Heel pain worst with first morning steps","Running, prolonged standing, obesity"],["Achilles tendinopathy","Achilles tendon","Pain and thickening above the heel","Running, fluoroquinolones"],["Adhesive capsulitis","Glenohumeral joint capsule","Loss of active and passive motion","Diabetes"],["Ganglion cyst","Joint capsule or tendon sheath","Firm cyst, often dorsal wrist, no true lining","Young adults, women"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Can't see, can't pee, can't climb a tree","text":"Reactive arthritis: conjunctivitis, urethritis, arthritis."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HLA-B27: PAIR","text":"Psoriatic arthritis, Ankylosing spondylitis, IBD-associated arthritis, Reactive arthritis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"P for Pseudogout","text":"Pseudogout crystals are calcium Pyrophosphate, Positively birefringent, and rhomboid (Parallelogram-like). Gout crystals are the needles."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Weak or just sore?","text":"Weakness with high CK means myositis. Stiffness and pain with normal strength, normal CK, and high ESR means PMR. Pain everywhere with all labs normal means fibromyalgia."}],"highYield":["Ankylosing spondylitis: HLA-B27, sacroiliitis, bamboo spine, uveitis, aortic regurgitation","Gout: needle-shaped, negatively birefringent urate crystals; podagra","Pseudogout: rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals; chondrocalcinosis","Dermatomyositis: perifascicular atrophy and association with internal cancer","Polymyositis: endomysial CD8 T cells","PMR: over 50, high ESR, normal CK; watch for giant cell arteritis","Fluoroquinolones raise Achilles tendon rupture risk","Oligoarticular JIA with positive ANA needs eye screening for silent uveitis"],"pitfalls":["AS back pain improves with exercise; mechanical back pain gets worse with activity.","Gout crystals are negatively birefringent; pseudogout crystals are weakly positive. Shape (needle versus rhomboid) helps settle it.","PMR patients feel weak because of pain, but their strength and CK are normal; myositis has true weakness and high CK.","Tendinopathy is mostly degenerative (tendinosis), so inflammatory cells are scarce on histology.","Psoriatic arthritis can appear before the skin rash; DIP disease with nail pitting points to it over RA."],"dumps":[{"t":"Purine breakdown and gout","p":"Draw the purine degradation pathway from AMP and GMP to uric acid. Mark the enzyme xanthine oxidase, the HGPRT salvage step, and write the causes of overproduction and underexcretion beside the pathway.","l":["AMP to IMP to inosine to hypoxanthine","GMP to guanosine to guanine to xanthine","Xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid","HGPRT salvages hypoxanthine and guanine; loss (Lesch-Nyhan) raises uric acid","Overproduction: tumor lysis, glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency","Underexcretion causes most gout: kidney disease, thiazides, alcohol","Urate crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1 beta"]},{"t":"Weakness and pain comparison","p":"Build a table comparing dermatomyositis, polymyositis, polymyalgia rheumatica, steroid myopathy, and fibromyalgia. Use rows for strength, CK, ESR, biopsy findings, and one extra clue for each.","l":["Dermatomyositis: weakness, high CK, perifascicular atrophy, heliotrope rash and Gottron papules","Polymyositis: weakness, high CK, endomysial CD8 T cells","PMR: normal strength and CK, high ESR, giant cell arteritis link","Steroid myopathy: weakness, normal CK, type II fiber atrophy","Fibromyalgia: normal strength and labs, widespread pain, poor sleep","Anti-Jo-1 points to interstitial lung disease in myositis","Dermatomyositis calls for a search for internal cancer"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (section on joints)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles (section on inflammatory myopathies)"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Nucleotide Metabolism"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb (sections on the rotator cuff and elbow region)"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-4","MS-5","MS-11","MS-12","MS-13-E1","MS-13-G1"]},"MS-13-C":{"id":"MS-13-C","summary":"Metabolic bone disease covers conditions where bone building or bone breakdown goes wrong at the cell or hormone level. This unit centers on osteopetrosis (osteoclasts cannot resorb bone) and renal osteodystrophy, and teaches the lab pattern for each major bone disease. Exams love the calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and PTH table.","notes":[{"h":"Osteopetrosis: the mechanism","points":["Osteoclasts fail to resorb bone, so old bone is never removed and remodeled.","Osteoclasts need an acid pump and a chloride channel to dissolve mineral; loss of either stops resorption.","Causes: carbonic anhydrase II deficiency (no H+ supply), CLCN7 chloride channel mutations, TCIRG1 proton pump subunit mutations.","Carbonic anhydrase II deficiency also causes renal tubular acidosis and cerebral calcification.","Result: thick, dense, marble-like bone that is brittle and fractures easily."]},{"h":"Osteopetrosis: forms and complications","points":["Infantile autosomal recessive form: severe, presents in infancy, often fatal in childhood without treatment.","Adult autosomal dominant form (Albers-Schonberg disease): milder, often found by fractures or on x-ray.","Bone fills the marrow space, causing anemia, thrombocytopenia, and infections (pancytopenia).","Extramedullary hematopoiesis enlarges the liver and spleen.","Narrowed skull foramina compress cranial nerves II, VII, and VIII: vision loss, facial palsy, hearing loss.","Osteomyelitis of the jaw is common because dense bone has poor blood supply.","X-ray: diffusely dense bones, bone-in-bone, Erlenmeyer flask flaring of the distal femur.","Osteoclasts come from the monocyte-macrophage line, so stem cell transplant can cure the infantile form."]},{"h":"Reading bone labs","points":["Osteoporosis: calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and PTH all normal.","Paget disease: only alkaline phosphatase is high.","Osteomalacia and rickets (vitamin D deficiency): low calcium and phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase and PTH.","Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium and PTH, low phosphate.","Renal osteodystrophy: low calcium, high phosphate, high PTH, high alkaline phosphatase.","Alkaline phosphatase rises when osteoblasts are busy; it stays normal when bone is only lost."]},{"h":"Renal osteodystrophy","points":["Chronic kidney disease cannot excrete phosphate, so phosphate rises and binds calcium.","Damaged kidneys make less 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so calcitriol falls and gut calcium absorption drops; high FGF23 lowers calcitriol further.","Low calcium, high phosphate, and low calcitriol drive parathyroid hyperplasia (secondary hyperparathyroidism).","High PTH causes osteitis fibrosa cystica: subperiosteal resorption (radial side of the middle phalanges), salt and pepper skull, bone cysts.","Brown tumors are masses of osteoclast-like giant cells, fibrous tissue, and hemorrhage; hemosiderin makes them brown.","Low calcitriol also causes defective mineralization (osteomalacia), and chronic acidosis leaches bone buffer.","Long-standing secondary disease can become autonomous (tertiary hyperparathyroidism) with high calcium."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Lab patterns in bone disease","cols":["Condition","Serum calcium","Serum phosphate","Alkaline phosphatase","PTH"],"rows":[["Osteoporosis","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal"],["Osteopetrosis","Usually normal (can be low in the infantile form)","Normal","Normal","Usually normal (can rise if calcium is low)"],["Osteomalacia or rickets (vitamin D deficiency)","Low or low-normal","Low","High","High"],["Paget disease of bone","Normal","Normal","High (isolated)","Normal"],["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High","Low","Normal or high","High"],["Renal osteodystrophy (secondary hyperparathyroidism)","Low or normal","High","High","High"]]},{"title":"Osteopetrosis forms","cols":["Feature","Infantile (malignant) form","Adult form"],"rows":[["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant (Albers-Schonberg disease)"],["Example genes","TCIRG1, CLCN7; carbonic anhydrase II in a milder recessive variant with renal tubular acidosis","CLCN7"],["Onset","Infancy","Adolescence or adulthood"],["Marrow","Pancytopenia, hepatosplenomegaly from extramedullary hematopoiesis","Usually normal or mildly affected"],["Cranial nerves","Blindness, deafness, facial palsy common","Less common"],["Course","Often fatal in childhood without stem cell transplant","Fractures, osteoarthritis, jaw osteomyelitis; normal lifespan"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Marble bone","text":"Marble is hard, heavy, and shatters. Osteopetrosis bone is dense, fills the marrow cavity, and breaks easily."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Who uses the ALP?","text":"Alkaline phosphatase tracks osteoblast work. Bone that is only being lost (osteoporosis) has normal ALP; bone being rebuilt or poorly mineralized (Paget, osteomalacia, hyperparathyroid bone) has high ALP."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Kidney failure chain","text":"Phosphate up, calcitriol down, calcium down, PTH up, bone eaten. Trace these five steps in order and the lab pattern writes itself."}],"highYield":["Osteopetrosis is a failure of osteoclast resorption, giving dense but brittle bone.","Carbonic anhydrase II deficiency combines osteopetrosis with renal tubular acidosis.","Marrow obliteration in osteopetrosis causes pancytopenia with hepatosplenomegaly from extramedullary hematopoiesis.","Narrow foramina in osteopetrosis cause blindness and deafness.","Osteoporosis has all-normal bone labs; Paget disease has an isolated high alkaline phosphatase.","Renal osteodystrophy shows high phosphate, low calcium, and high PTH.","Brown tumors and subperiosteal resorption of the phalanges mark osteitis fibrosa cystica from high PTH."],"pitfalls":["Osteopetrosis and osteoporosis sound alike but are opposites: too much bone versus too little bone.","Primary hyperparathyroidism has low phosphate; secondary hyperparathyroidism from kidney failure has high phosphate.","Dense bone on x-ray does not mean strong bone; osteopetrotic bone fractures easily.","A brown tumor is a reactive lesion of hyperparathyroidism and is easy to mistake for a true giant cell tumor of bone."],"dumps":[{"t":"Bone lab table","p":"Build a table from memory with rows for osteoporosis, osteopetrosis, osteomalacia, Paget disease, primary hyperparathyroidism, and renal osteodystrophy. Columns: calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, PTH.","l":["Osteoporosis: all four normal","Osteopetrosis: usually all normal","Osteomalacia: low calcium and phosphate, high ALP and PTH","Paget: isolated high ALP","Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium, low phosphate, high PTH","Renal osteodystrophy: low calcium, high phosphate, high ALP and PTH","Explains that ALP reflects osteoblast activity"]},{"t":"CKD to bone","p":"Draw the kidney, gut, parathyroid gland, and bone. Use arrows to trace how chronic kidney disease leads to osteitis fibrosa cystica.","l":["Low GFR causes phosphate retention","Phosphate binds calcium and lowers ionized calcium","Less 1-alpha-hydroxylase means low calcitriol","Low calcitriol cuts gut calcium absorption","Low calcium and high phosphate stimulate PTH secretion and parathyroid hyperplasia","High PTH drives osteoclast resorption: subperiosteal erosions, cysts, brown tumors","Low calcitriol also causes poor mineralization"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"section on chronic kidney disease and its effects on bone"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Calcium Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_gD5XWvONik"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Parathyroid Disorders | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DQk1LfxcB1U"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ywe5jjiJJJo"}],"related":["MS-8","MS-13-A1","MS-13-E2","MS-13-E3","EN-12-A2","UR-12-A4"]},"MS-13-D":{"id":"MS-13-D","summary":"Congenital musculoskeletal diseases come from single-gene defects in structural proteins: fibrillin (Marfan), dystrophin (muscular dystrophy), collagen (osteogenesis imperfecta, Ehlers-Danlos), and a growth plate receptor (achondroplasia). Exams link each gene to its protein, its inheritance, and its classic physical findings.","notes":[{"h":"Marfan syndrome","points":["Autosomal dominant FBN1 mutation on chromosome 15; fibrillin-1 forms microfibrils that scaffold elastic fibers.","Fibrillin normally holds latent TGF-beta; defective fibrillin releases it, so excess TGF-beta signaling weakens tissue.","Tall and thin, long limbs, arm span greater than height, arachnodactyly, pectus excavatum or carinatum, joint hypermobility, scoliosis.","Lens dislocates upward (superotemporal ectopia lentis).","Aortic root dilation from medial degeneration (cystic medial necrosis) leads to aortic regurgitation and dissection, the main cause of death.","Mitral valve prolapse and spontaneous pneumothorax are also common."]},{"h":"Homocystinuria versus Marfan","points":["Homocystinuria: autosomal recessive, usually cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency, with high homocysteine.","Shares a tall, Marfan-like body build.","Lens dislocates downward (inferonasal); intellectual disability and arterial and venous thrombosis are present.","Marfan has normal intelligence and no clotting tendency."]},{"h":"Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy","points":["X-linked recessive DMD gene; dystrophin links the actin cytoskeleton to the dystroglycan complex and extracellular matrix.","Duchenne: frameshift deletions or nonsense mutations give absent dystrophin, so the membrane tears with each contraction and fibers die.","Boys under 5: proximal weakness, waddling gait, Gowers sign (uses hands to climb up the legs to stand).","Calf pseudohypertrophy: muscle replaced by fat and fibrous tissue.","CK very high (often 10 to 100 times normal) from birth.","Death in early adulthood from dilated cardiomyopathy or respiratory failure; scoliosis worsens breathing.","Becker: in-frame deletions give a shorter, partly working dystrophin; onset later, slower course, walking into adulthood.","Immunostaining: dystrophin absent in Duchenne, reduced or patchy in Becker; genetic deletion and duplication testing confirms the type."]},{"h":"Osteogenesis imperfecta","points":["Mostly autosomal dominant COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations: defective or too little type I collagen.","Glycine substitutions break the Gly-X-Y triple helix and act as dominant negatives; type I (mildest) makes too little normal collagen; type II is lethal around birth.","Multiple fractures with minimal trauma, bowed bones, short stature, wormian bones.","Blue sclerae (thin collagen lets the choroid show), conductive hearing loss (abnormal ossicles), dentinogenesis imperfecta (opalescent, fragile teeth), joint laxity."]},{"h":"Osteogenesis imperfecta versus child abuse","points":["Favors abuse: posterior rib fractures, metaphyseal corner fractures, fractures of different ages, bruises in a non-mobile infant, retinal hemorrhage, a story that does not fit.","Favors osteogenesis imperfecta: blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, wormian bones, low bone density, bowing, family history of fractures or hearing loss."]},{"h":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome and achondroplasia","points":["Ehlers-Danlos: a group of collagen disorders with stretchy skin, hypermobile and dislocating joints, easy bruising, poor wound healing.","Classic type: type V collagen (COL5A1, COL5A2). Vascular type: type III collagen (COL3A1), with arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture.","Achondroplasia: autosomal dominant FGFR3 gain of function that slows growth plate chondrocytes (endochondral bone).","Rhizomelic shortening (upper arms and thighs) with normal trunk length, macrocephaly, frontal bossing, normal intelligence.","Most cases are new mutations linked to older paternal age."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital musculoskeletal diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Gene and protein","Inheritance","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Marfan syndrome","FBN1, fibrillin-1","Autosomal dominant","Weak microfibrils plus excess TGF-beta signaling","Tall, arachnodactyly, arm span over height, pectus, hypermobility, upward lens dislocation","Aortic root dilation, aortic dissection, mitral valve prolapse, pneumothorax"],["Duchenne muscular dystrophy","DMD, dystrophin absent (frameshift or nonsense)","X-linked recessive","Unstable sarcolemma tears during contraction; fibers die and are replaced by fat and fibrous tissue","Onset under age 5, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy, very high CK","Dilated cardiomyopathy, scoliosis, respiratory failure"],["Becker muscular dystrophy","DMD, truncated dystrophin (in-frame)","X-linked recessive","Partly working dystrophin","Later onset, milder proximal weakness, high CK","Cardiomyopathy, sometimes out of proportion to weakness"],["Osteogenesis imperfecta","COL1A1 or COL1A2, type I collagen","Mostly autosomal dominant","Too little or structurally abnormal type I collagen","Fractures with minimal trauma, blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, joint laxity","Conductive hearing loss, deformity, short stature"],["Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Classic: COL5A1 or COL5A2 (type V). Vascular: COL3A1 (type III)","Mostly autosomal dominant","Faulty collagen in skin, joints, and vessels","Hyperextensible skin, hypermobile joints, easy bruising, wide scars","Joint dislocation; vascular type: arterial, bowel, or uterine rupture"],["Achondroplasia","FGFR3, gain of function","Autosomal dominant (most cases new mutations)","Reduced chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates","Rhizomelic limbs, normal trunk, macrocephaly, frontal bossing, normal intelligence","Foramen magnum and spinal stenosis"]]},{"title":"Lens dislocation","cols":["Condition","Direction","Other clues"],"rows":[["Marfan syndrome","Upward (superotemporal)","Normal intelligence, aortic root disease"],["Homocystinuria","Downward (inferonasal)","Intellectual disability, thrombosis, high homocysteine"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Patients can't BITE","text":"Osteogenesis imperfecta: Bones (multiple fractures), I (eye: blue sclerae), Teeth (dentinogenesis imperfecta), Ear (hearing loss)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Marfan looks up, homocystinuria looks down","text":"Marfan lens goes up and out; homocystinuria lens goes down and in. The patient with clots and intellectual disability is the one looking down."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Reading frame rule","text":"Frameshift deletion scrambles every codon after it, so no dystrophin (Duchenne). In-frame deletion skips a chunk but keeps reading, so a short dystrophin (Becker)."}],"highYield":["Marfan syndrome is an autosomal dominant FBN1 (fibrillin-1) defect with excess TGF-beta signaling.","Aortic root dilation and dissection are the main threats to life in Marfan syndrome.","Upward lens dislocation suggests Marfan; downward lens dislocation suggests homocystinuria.","Duchenne: absent dystrophin, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy, very high CK, death from cardiomyopathy or respiratory failure.","Becker: truncated dystrophin from an in-frame deletion, later onset, milder course.","Osteogenesis imperfecta is a type I collagen defect with fractures, blue sclerae, hearing loss, and dentinogenesis imperfecta.","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos (type III collagen) causes arterial and bowel rupture.","Achondroplasia is an FGFR3 gain of function with rhizomelic shortening and normal intelligence."],"pitfalls":["Calf pseudohypertrophy is fat and fibrous tissue, so big calves do not mean strong muscle.","Becker is not just mild Duchenne in time; check dystrophin staining (reduced versus absent) and the type of deletion.","Multiple fractures in a child are not automatically abuse; look for blue sclerae, abnormal teeth, and family history before deciding.","Osteogenesis imperfecta is type I collagen (bone); classic Ehlers-Danlos is type V and vascular Ehlers-Danlos is type III.","A Marfan-like build with thrombosis and learning problems points to homocystinuria."],"dumps":[{"t":"Structural protein table","p":"Make a table of Marfan syndrome, Duchenne, Becker, osteogenesis imperfecta, vascular Ehlers-Danlos, and achondroplasia. Columns: gene, protein, inheritance, two classic findings.","l":["Marfan: FBN1, fibrillin-1, autosomal dominant","Duchenne: DMD, absent dystrophin, X-linked recessive","Becker: DMD, truncated dystrophin, X-linked recessive","Osteogenesis imperfecta: COL1A1 or COL1A2, type I collagen, mostly autosomal dominant","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos: COL3A1, type III collagen","Achondroplasia: FGFR3 gain of function, autosomal dominant","Two correct findings listed for each"]},{"t":"Dystrophin and the muscle membrane","p":"Draw a muscle fiber membrane. Label actin, dystrophin, the dystroglycan complex, and the extracellular matrix. Then show what happens in Duchenne.","l":["Dystrophin links actin inside the cell to the dystroglycan complex in the membrane","Dystroglycan links to laminin in the extracellular matrix","Without dystrophin the membrane tears during contraction","Calcium enters and the fiber dies; CK leaks into blood","Dead fibers are replaced by fat and fibrous tissue (pseudohypertrophy)","Heart muscle and diaphragm are affected, causing cardiomyopathy and respiratory failure"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases, section on Marfan syndrome and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles, section on muscular dystrophies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors, section on osteogenesis imperfecta and achondroplasia"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Muscular Dystrophy - Duchenne, Becker and Mytonic","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o1uhhpjmzkw"}],"related":["MS-6","MS-7","MS-9","CV-18-I2","IN-13-E2"]},"MS-13-E":{"id":"MS-13-E","summary":"Degenerative musculoskeletal diseases come from wear, aging, hormone loss, or lost blood supply: osteoarthritis, osteoporosis, Paget disease, and avascular necrosis. This unit also covers pediatric hip and knee disorders, disc herniation, and Dupuytren contracture. Exams test the mechanism, the typical patient, and the findings that separate look-alikes.","notes":[{"h":"Osteoarthritis","points":["Chondrocyte injury and mechanical stress cause loss of proteoglycan and type II collagen.","Cartilage fibrillates and cracks; exposed bone polishes smooth (eburnation).","Subchondral sclerosis and cysts form; osteophytes grow at the joint margins.","Risk: age, obesity, prior joint injury, heavy mechanical load, joint malalignment.","Pain worse with use and better with rest; morning stiffness under 30 minutes.","Asymmetric weight-bearing joints (knees, hips, spine), first CMC, Heberden (DIP) and Bouchard (PIP) nodes; MCPs and wrists usually spared.","X-ray: joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis and cysts; synovial fluid noninflammatory."]},{"h":"Osteoporosis","points":["Reduced bone mass with normal mineralization; trabecular bone is hit first.","Low peak bone mass plus age-related loss; estrogen loss raises RANKL and lowers osteoprotegerin, so osteoclasts resorb more.","Secondary causes: glucocorticoids, hyperthyroidism, immobilization, alcohol, smoking.","Fractures: vertebral compression (height loss, kyphosis), hip, distal radius.","Serum calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase normal.","DEXA T-score minus 2.5 or lower is osteoporosis; minus 1 to minus 2.5 is osteopenia."]},{"h":"Paget disease of bone","points":["Overactive, huge osteoclasts start the disease, then osteoblasts lay down bone in a disorganized way.","Phases: lytic, mixed, then sclerotic (burned out); end result is thick but weak mosaic lamellar bone.","Older adults; skull, pelvis, spine, femur, tibia.","Isolated high alkaline phosphatase with normal calcium, phosphate, and PTH.","Complications: bone pain, enlarging skull (hat size), hearing loss, bowed tibia, fractures, high-output heart failure from arteriovenous shunting, osteosarcoma."]},{"h":"Avascular necrosis","points":["Bone infarction when blood supply is cut off; femoral head is the most common site.","Causes: glucocorticoids, alcohol, sickle cell disease, SLE, decompression sickness, femoral neck and scaphoid fractures.","Femoral head depends on retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery.","MRI is the most sensitive test; early x-rays can be normal."]},{"h":"Pediatric hip and adolescent knee","points":["Developmental dysplasia of the hip: newborn, often girl, breech; Ortolani test reduces a dislocated hip, Barlow test dislocates an unstable one.","Legg-Calve-Perthes: idiopathic femoral head osteonecrosis in young children (often boys about 4 to 8), painless or mildly painful limp.","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis: obese adolescent; the epiphysis slips off the femoral neck; hip or referred knee pain.","Osgood-Schlatter: traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle from patellar tendon pull in active teens.","Chondromalacia patellae: softened patellar cartilage, anterior knee pain worse with stairs or long sitting.","Osteochondritis dissecans: a fragment of subchondral bone and cartilage separates, causing pain, effusion, or locking."]},{"h":"Spine and hand","points":["Degenerated disc lets the nucleus pulposus herniate posterolaterally, where the posterior longitudinal ligament is thin.","Lumbar herniation compresses the lower root: L4 to L5 disc hits L5 (foot dorsiflexion, dorsum of foot).","Spondylosis is degenerative disc and facet change; spondylolisthesis is forward slip of one vertebra on the one below.","Dupuytren contracture: myofibroblast proliferation in the palmar fascia pulls the ring and little fingers into flexion."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Degenerative bone and joint diseases","cols":["Condition","Risk factors or cause","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Osteoarthritis","Age, obesity, prior injury, mechanical load","Chondrocyte injury, proteoglycan loss, cartilage fibrillation, reactive bone","Use-related pain, stiffness under 30 minutes, Heberden and Bouchard nodes, asymmetric weight-bearing joints","Deformity, loss of function, loose bodies"],["Osteoporosis","Menopause, age, low peak bone mass, glucocorticoids, hyperthyroidism, immobility, alcohol, smoking","Resorption exceeds formation (RANKL up with estrogen loss); mineralization normal","Normal labs, T-score minus 2.5 or lower, height loss, kyphosis","Vertebral compression, hip, and distal radius fractures"],["Paget disease of bone","Older adults; genetic factors (for example SQSTM1)","Osteoclast overactivity then chaotic osteoblast repair; mosaic lamellar bone","Bone pain, enlarging skull, bowed tibia, isolated high ALP","Hearing loss, fractures, high-output heart failure, osteosarcoma"],["Avascular necrosis","Glucocorticoids, alcohol, sickle cell, SLE, decompression sickness, femoral neck or scaphoid fracture","Interrupted blood supply causes bone infarction","Deep groin or joint pain; MRI changes before x-ray","Subchondral collapse, secondary osteoarthritis"]]},{"title":"Pediatric hip disorders","cols":["Disorder","Typical patient","What happens","Key clue"],"rows":[["Developmental dysplasia of the hip","Newborn, girl, breech, family history","Shallow acetabulum lets the femoral head slip out","Positive Ortolani or Barlow test"],["Legg-Calve-Perthes disease","Young child, usually boy, about 4 to 8 years","Idiopathic osteonecrosis of the femoral head","Painless or mild limp, reduced hip motion"],["Slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Obese adolescent","Epiphysis slips off the femoral neck through the growth plate","Hip or knee pain, limp, externally rotated leg"]]},{"title":"Adolescent knee disorders","cols":["Disorder","Site","Mechanism","Clue"],"rows":[["Osgood-Schlatter disease","Tibial tubercle","Traction apophysitis from patellar tendon pull","Tender bump below the kneecap in an active teen"],["Chondromalacia patellae","Patellar articular cartilage","Softening of cartilage","Anterior knee pain with stairs or long sitting"],["Osteochondritis dissecans","Subchondral bone, often the medial femoral condyle","Bone and cartilage fragment separates","Pain, effusion, locking or catching"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"LOSS (osteoarthritis x-ray)","text":"Loss of joint space, Osteophytes, Subchondral sclerosis, Subchondral cysts."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CASTS Bend LEGS (avascular necrosis)","text":"Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, the Bends (decompression), LEgg-Calve-Perthes, Gaucher disease, Slipped capital femoral epiphysis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Heberden at the fingertip","text":"Heberden nodes sit on the DIP joints, out at the fingertip. Bouchard nodes sit on the PIP joints, closer to the body. Both are osteophytes."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lumbar disc names the lower root","text":"In the lumbar spine the herniated disc usually hits the root that exits one level down: L4 to L5 disc, L5 root; L5 to S1 disc, S1 root."}],"highYield":["Osteoarthritis is noninflammatory cartilage failure with eburnation, subchondral cysts, and osteophytes.","Osteoarthritis stiffness lasts under 30 minutes; rheumatoid arthritis stiffness lasts longer than an hour.","Osteoporosis has normal calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase; DEXA T-score of minus 2.5 or lower confirms it.","Estrogen loss raises RANKL-driven osteoclast resorption.","Paget disease: isolated high alkaline phosphatase, mosaic bone, high-output heart failure, osteosarcoma risk.","The femoral head is the most common site of avascular necrosis; MRI detects it first.","Obese adolescent with hip or knee pain suggests slipped capital femoral epiphysis.","L4 to L5 disc herniation compresses the L5 root."],"pitfalls":["Osteoporosis is less bone with normal mineral; osteomalacia is soft bone with poor mineral and abnormal labs.","Paget bone looks thick on x-ray but breaks easily.","Legg-Calve-Perthes affects younger children and is not linked to obesity; slipped capital femoral epiphysis affects obese teens.","Hip disease in children can show up as knee pain, so examine the hip in a limping child.","Spondylosis is wear and tear; spondylolysis is a pars defect; spondylolisthesis is the slip."],"dumps":[{"t":"Osteoarthritic joint","p":"Draw a normal synovial joint next to an osteoarthritic joint. Label the changes in the cartilage and bone.","l":["Normal smooth hyaline cartilage and even joint space","Fibrillated and thinned cartilage","Joint space narrowing","Eburnation of exposed bone","Subchondral sclerosis","Subchondral cysts","Osteophytes at the joint margins","Loose cartilage or bone fragments (joint mice)"]},{"t":"Bone loss versus bone chaos","p":"Build a comparison table for osteoporosis, Paget disease, and avascular necrosis. Columns: cell or blood supply problem, typical patient, lab findings, imaging, two complications.","l":["Osteoporosis: resorption exceeds formation, postmenopausal or older adult","Osteoporosis: normal labs, low T-score, compression and hip fractures","Paget: osteoclast overactivity then disorganized formation, older adult","Paget: isolated high ALP, thick mosaic bone","Paget: hearing loss, heart failure, osteosarcoma","Avascular necrosis: loss of blood supply, femoral head most common","Avascular necrosis: MRI most sensitive, collapse and arthritis later"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb, section on the hip joint and blood supply of the femoral head"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back, section on intervertebral discs and herniation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Osteoporosis (Part I)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2cz2rkBy2rg"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Paget's Disease of the bone","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NVcYG3_B8HU"}],"related":["MS-4","MS-13-C","MS-13-B4","MS-13-H1","HE-9-B1"]},"MS-13-F":{"id":"MS-13-F","summary":"Musculoskeletal neoplasms include primary bone tumors, soft tissue sarcomas, and metastases. Exams test the patient's age, the location in the bone, the x-ray pattern, the tissue the tumor makes, and the gene or marker that confirms it.","notes":[{"h":"Bone-forming tumors","points":["Osteosarcoma: malignant cells make osteoid; most common primary malignant bone tumor apart from myeloma.","Peaks in adolescents and again in older adults with Paget disease or prior radiation.","Metaphysis around the knee (distal femur, proximal tibia).","X-ray: Codman triangle (lifted periosteum) and sunburst pattern.","Risk with germline RB1 (hereditary retinoblastoma) and TP53 (Li-Fraumeni) loss.","Spreads through blood, most often to the lungs.","Osteoid osteoma: under 2 cm cortical nidus with a sclerotic rim, young males, night pain relieved by NSAIDs because the nidus makes prostaglandin E2.","Osteoblastoma: larger, often in the vertebrae, pain not relieved by NSAIDs."]},{"h":"Cartilage tumors","points":["Osteochondroma: most common benign bone tumor; metaphyseal bony stalk with a cartilage cap, continuous with the marrow; EXT1 or EXT2 (multiple in hereditary exostoses).","Enchondroma: hyaline cartilage inside the medullary cavity, often in hand bones; multiple in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome (with hemangiomas).","Chondrosarcoma: malignant cartilage in older adults; pelvis, proximal femur, shoulder girdle.","Chondrosarcoma can arise from an enchondroma or osteochondroma and often carries IDH1 or IDH2 mutations."]},{"h":"Ewing sarcoma and giant cell tumors","points":["Ewing sarcoma: children and adolescents; diaphysis of long bones or pelvis.","Small round blue cells, CD99 positive, EWSR1-FLI1 fusion from t(11;22).","Onion-skin periosteal reaction; fever, high ESR, and leukocytosis can mimic osteomyelitis.","Giant cell tumor of bone: young adults, epiphysis near the knee, soap-bubble lytic lesion.","Neoplastic stromal cells express RANKL and recruit osteoclast-like giant cells.","Tenosynovial giant cell tumor: tumor of synovium or tendon sheath driven by CSF1 overexpression."]},{"h":"Soft tissue sarcomas","points":["Rhabdomyosarcoma: most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood; head and neck (orbit) or genitourinary tract.","Sarcoma botryoides: grape-like embryonal variant in the vagina or bladder of young children.","Rhabdomyoblasts stain for desmin and myogenin; alveolar type has PAX3 or PAX7 to FOXO1 fusions.","Leiomyosarcoma: adults; uterus and retroperitoneum; smooth muscle actin and desmin positive.","Sarcomas spread mainly through blood to the lungs; rhabdomyosarcoma can also reach lymph nodes."]},{"h":"Metastases and paraneoplastic bone disease","points":["Metastases are the most common malignant bone tumors; they seed red marrow in the spine, pelvis, ribs, and proximal long bones.","Osteoblastic: prostate. Osteolytic: kidney, thyroid, lung. Mixed: breast.","Complications: pathologic fracture, hypercalcemia, pain, spinal cord compression.","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy: clubbing, periosteal new bone on long bones, and arthritis as a paraneoplastic sign of lung cancer."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Named musculoskeletal neoplasms","cols":["Tumor","Age and site","Tissue or cell","Imaging or gross","Genetics or marker","Behavior"],"rows":[["Osteosarcoma","Teens and older adults (Paget, radiation); metaphysis around the knee","Malignant osteoid","Codman triangle, sunburst","RB1, TP53","Malignant; blood spread to lung"],["Ewing sarcoma","Children and teens; diaphysis, pelvis","Small round blue cells","Onion-skin periosteal reaction, lytic","CD99, t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1","Malignant; early metastasis; can mimic infection"],["Chondrosarcoma","Older adults; pelvis, proximal femur, shoulder girdle","Malignant hyaline cartilage","Lytic with ring and arc calcification","IDH1 or IDH2","Malignant; may arise from enchondroma or osteochondroma"],["Osteoid osteoma","Young males; cortex of long bones","Osteoid nidus under 2 cm","Lucent nidus with sclerotic rim","Nidus makes prostaglandin E2","Benign; night pain relieved by NSAIDs"],["Rhabdomyosarcoma","Children; head and neck, genitourinary tract","Rhabdomyoblasts (skeletal muscle lineage)","Botryoid grape-like mass in hollow organs","Desmin, myogenin; alveolar PAX3 or PAX7 to FOXO1","Malignant; blood and lymph spread"]]},{"title":"Other bone and soft tissue tumors","cols":["Tumor","Typical patient and site","Key feature","Note"],"rows":[["Osteochondroma","Adolescent; metaphysis near the knee","Bony stalk with cartilage cap, grows away from the joint","EXT1 or EXT2; rare chondrosarcoma"],["Enchondroma","Hand and foot bones","Intramedullary hyaline cartilage","Multiple in Ollier and Maffucci; chondrosarcoma risk"],["Osteoblastoma","Young adults; vertebrae","Larger than 2 cm","Pain not relieved by NSAIDs"],["Giant cell tumor of bone","Young adults; epiphysis near the knee","Soap-bubble lytic lesion","RANKL-expressing stromal cells recruit giant cells"],["Tenosynovial giant cell tumor","Synovium or tendon sheath","Hemosiderin-laden synovial mass","CSF1 overexpression"],["Leiomyosarcoma","Adults; uterus, retroperitoneum","Malignant spindle smooth muscle cells","Smooth muscle actin and desmin; lung metastasis"],["Bone metastases","Older adults; spine, pelvis, ribs","Most common malignant bone tumor","Blastic prostate, lytic kidney, thyroid, lung, mixed breast"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Where in the bone","text":"Epiphysis: giant cell tumor. Metaphysis: osteosarcoma and osteochondroma. Diaphysis: Ewing sarcoma. Location alone narrows most bone tumor questions."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Aspirin at night","text":"A young man woken by bone pain that aspirin or ibuprofen relieves has osteoid osteoma; the nidus makes prostaglandin E2, which NSAIDs block."}],"highYield":["Metastases are the most common malignant tumors in bone; prostate makes blastic lesions.","Osteosarcoma: teen with a metaphyseal knee tumor, Codman triangle, sunburst, lung metastasis.","Hereditary retinoblastoma (RB1) and Li-Fraumeni (TP53) raise osteosarcoma risk; Paget disease does in older adults.","Ewing sarcoma: diaphysis, onion skin, CD99, t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1, may mimic osteomyelitis.","Chondrosarcoma: older adult, pelvis or shoulder, IDH1 or IDH2.","Rhabdomyosarcoma: most common childhood soft tissue sarcoma; desmin and myogenin.","Giant cell tumor of bone: epiphyseal soap bubble; stromal cells make RANKL.","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy is a paraneoplastic sign of lung cancer."],"pitfalls":["Fever and high ESR with a child's bone lesion can be Ewing sarcoma, not just osteomyelitis.","Osteoid osteoma pain responds to NSAIDs; osteoblastoma pain does not.","Codman triangle shows aggressive growth but is not specific for osteosarcoma.","Multiple myeloma is the most common primary malignancy in bone, but it arises from plasma cells; that is why osteosarcoma is called the most common primary bone tumor apart from myeloma.","Myogenin marks skeletal muscle (rhabdomyosarcoma); smooth muscle actin marks smooth muscle (leiomyosarcoma)."],"dumps":[{"t":"Long bone tumor map","p":"Draw a long bone and label the epiphysis, metaphysis, diaphysis, and medullary cavity. Place each tumor where it usually arises.","l":["Giant cell tumor in the epiphysis","Osteosarcoma in the metaphysis","Osteochondroma as a metaphyseal stalk with a cartilage cap","Ewing sarcoma in the diaphysis","Enchondroma inside the medullary cavity","Osteoid osteoma in the cortex with a sclerotic rim","Labels Codman triangle at a lifted periosteum"]},{"t":"Sarcoma marker table","p":"Build a table for osteosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma, chondrosarcoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, and leiomyosarcoma. Columns: age, site, marker or gene, and where it spreads.","l":["Osteosarcoma: teens or older adults, knee metaphysis, RB1 or TP53, lung","Ewing: children and teens, diaphysis or pelvis, CD99 and EWSR1-FLI1, lung and bone","Chondrosarcoma: older adults, pelvis and shoulder, IDH1 or IDH2, lung","Rhabdomyosarcoma: children, head and neck or genitourinary, desmin and myogenin","Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma: PAX3 or PAX7 to FOXO1","Leiomyosarcoma: adults, uterus or retroperitoneum, smooth muscle actin and desmin","Sarcomas spread mainly through blood to the lungs"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, section on paraneoplastic syndromes"}],"videos":[],"related":["MS-13-E3","MS-13-G3","RE-13-J6","HE-9-D3","MS-9"]},"MS-13-G":{"id":"MS-13-G","summary":"Infectious musculoskeletal diseases include septic arthritis, osteomyelitis, necrotizing fasciitis, gangrene, and infective myositis. Exams test the likely organism for each patient, how the infection reaches the tissue, and the findings in joint fluid, bone, and skin.","notes":[{"h":"Septic arthritis","points":["Bacteria reach the joint through blood (most cases) or by direct inoculation (injection, surgery, bite).","The synovium is richly vascular with no basement membrane, so bacteria seed it easily.","Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause; Neisseria gonorrhoeae in sexually active young adults.","Risk: rheumatoid arthritis, prosthetic joints, injection drug use, diabetes, older age.","Neutrophil enzymes destroy cartilage within days, so a hot swollen joint is an emergency.","Gonococcal arthritis can be migratory with tenosynovitis and pustular skin lesions; joint cultures are often negative."]},{"h":"Synovial fluid","points":["Noninflammatory (osteoarthritis, trauma): clear, under 2,000 WBC/mm3, few neutrophils.","Inflammatory (rheumatoid arthritis, gout, pseudogout, reactive arthritis): cloudy, about 2,000 to 50,000 WBC/mm3, mostly neutrophils.","Septic: purulent, usually over 50,000 WBC/mm3, over 75 percent neutrophils, positive Gram stain or culture.","Gout: needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals. Pseudogout: rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals.","Crystals do not rule out infection; both can be present together."]},{"h":"Osteomyelitis","points":["Children: blood-borne seeding of the long bone metaphysis, where slow flow in looping vessels and few phagocytes let bacteria settle.","Adults: vertebral osteomyelitis with discitis from blood spread, and contiguous spread from diabetic foot ulcers.","S. aureus is most common; Salmonella in sickle cell disease; Pseudomonas after a nail through a sneaker or in injection drug use; Mycobacterium tuberculosis in the spine (Pott disease).","Sequestrum is dead bone; involucrum is new bone around it; Brodie abscess is a walled-off bone abscess.","ESR and CRP are high; MRI is the most sensitive imaging; x-ray changes lag about 2 weeks.","Chronic osteomyelitis can drain through a sinus tract, which may develop squamous cell carcinoma."]},{"h":"Necrotizing fasciitis","points":["Infection spreads rapidly along fascial planes and clots the vessels that feed the overlying skin.","Type I: polymicrobial (anaerobes plus aerobes), in diabetes or after surgery; Fournier gangrene of the perineum.","Type II: Streptococcus pyogenes (group A), alone or with S. aureus, can strike healthy people after minor injury.","Pain out of proportion to exam, swelling past the redness, crepitus, bullae, dusky skin, and systemic toxicity."]},{"h":"Gangrene","points":["Dry gangrene: arterial occlusion causes coagulative necrosis; black, shriveled, sharply demarcated.","Wet gangrene: bacteria infect the dead tissue, giving liquefactive necrosis; swollen, foul, spreading, risk of sepsis.","Diabetes and peripheral artery disease raise risk through poor perfusion, neuropathy, and weak neutrophil function.","Gas gangrene: Clostridium perfringens infects deep contaminated wounds; alpha toxin (lecithinase) destroys cell membranes.","Gas gangrene signs: rapid spread, severe pain, crepitus, bronze skin, hemolysis, and shock."]},{"h":"Infective myositis","points":["Pyomyositis: S. aureus abscess in a large muscle such as the thigh or buttock.","Trichinellosis: Trichinella spiralis larvae from undercooked pork or wild game encyst in skeletal muscle.","Trichinellosis findings: myalgia, fever, periorbital edema, and eosinophilia."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infectious musculoskeletal diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Organisms","Route and risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Septic arthritis","S. aureus; N. gonorrhoeae in sexually active adults; streptococci; gram-negatives in older adults and drug users","Blood spread or direct inoculation; rheumatoid arthritis, prosthetic joint, injection drug use, diabetes","Neutrophil enzymes and cytokines destroy cartilage","Acute hot swollen joint, fever, synovial WBC over 50,000/mm3","Cartilage loss, osteomyelitis, sepsis"],["Osteomyelitis","S. aureus; Salmonella (sickle cell); Pseudomonas (puncture, drug use); M. tuberculosis (spine)","Blood spread (child metaphysis, adult spine) or contiguous (diabetic foot, open fracture)","Pus raises pressure in bone, cuts blood supply, and kills bone","Bone pain, fever, high ESR and CRP, MRI changes","Sequestrum, chronic draining sinus, squamous cell carcinoma, pathologic fracture"],["Necrotizing fasciitis","Type I polymicrobial; type II Streptococcus pyogenes","Diabetes, surgery, perineal wounds (type I); minor skin injury (type II)","Spread along fascia with vessel thrombosis and toxins","Pain out of proportion, crepitus, bullae, systemic toxicity","Septic shock, toxic shock syndrome, limb loss, death"],["Wet gangrene","Mixed skin and gut bacteria","Ischemic tissue in diabetes or peripheral artery disease","Bacteria convert coagulative necrosis to liquefactive necrosis","Swollen, moist, foul, poorly demarcated tissue","Sepsis, amputation"],["Gas gangrene (clostridial myonecrosis)","Clostridium perfringens","Deep contaminated wounds, crush injury","Alpha toxin (lecithinase) destroys membranes of muscle and red cells","Severe pain, crepitus, bronze skin, thin brown discharge","Hemolysis, shock, renal failure, death"]]},{"title":"Synovial fluid categories","cols":["Category","Appearance","WBC per mm3","Neutrophils","Examples"],"rows":[["Normal","Clear","Under 200","Under 25 percent","Healthy joint"],["Noninflammatory","Clear, yellow","200 to 2,000","Under 25 percent","Osteoarthritis, trauma"],["Inflammatory","Cloudy","About 2,000 to 50,000","Over 50 percent","Rheumatoid arthritis, gout, pseudogout, reactive arthritis"],["Septic","Purulent","Usually over 50,000","Over 75 percent","Bacterial arthritis; Gram stain or culture often positive"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Dead bone, new bone","text":"Sequestrum is sequestered (dead and isolated). Involucrum is the envelope of new bone that wraps around it."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Dry is clean, wet is infected","text":"Dry gangrene stays coagulative and sharply edged. Add bacteria and enzymes and it turns wet, liquefied, and spreading."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pain out of proportion","text":"Severe pain with skin that looks only mildly red points to a deep process: necrotizing fasciitis, gas gangrene, or compartment syndrome."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the top cause of septic arthritis and osteomyelitis.","Septic joint fluid usually has more than 50,000 WBC/mm3 with mostly neutrophils.","Childhood hematogenous osteomyelitis settles in the metaphysis because blood flow there is slow.","Salmonella osteomyelitis is classic in sickle cell disease; Pseudomonas follows a nail through a sneaker.","MRI is the most sensitive imaging for osteomyelitis.","Necrotizing fasciitis: pain out of proportion, crepitus, bullae; type II is group A streptococcus.","Clostridium perfringens alpha toxin (lecithinase) causes gas gangrene with hemolysis and shock.","Trichinella from undercooked pork or game causes myalgia, periorbital edema, and eosinophilia."],"pitfalls":["Finding crystals in a joint does not exclude infection; a gouty joint can also be septic.","Crepitus can occur in type I necrotizing fasciitis and in gas gangrene; Gram stain and the deep tissue involved (fascia versus muscle) help separate them.","Early osteomyelitis can have a normal x-ray; order MRI if suspicion is high.","Gonococcal arthritis often has negative joint cultures; test the urethra, cervix, rectum, and throat."],"dumps":[{"t":"Osteomyelitis in a child's bone","p":"Draw a child's long bone and trace how blood-borne bacteria cause osteomyelitis. Label the metaphysis, growth plate, sequestrum, and involucrum.","l":["Bacteria arrive through the nutrient artery","Slow flow in metaphyseal vessel loops lets bacteria settle","Neutrophils and pus raise pressure inside the bone","Blood supply is cut off and bone dies (sequestrum)","Pus lifts the periosteum, which lays down new bone (involucrum)","Sinus tract can drain to the skin","S. aureus is the most common organism"]},{"t":"Hot joint workup","p":"Build a table of synovial fluid findings for noninflammatory, inflammatory, septic, gout, and pseudogout joints. Include appearance, WBC count, neutrophil percent, and crystals.","l":["Noninflammatory: clear, under 2,000 WBC/mm3","Inflammatory: cloudy, about 2,000 to 50,000 WBC/mm3, mostly neutrophils","Septic: purulent, usually over 50,000 WBC/mm3, over 75 percent neutrophils","Septic: Gram stain or culture positive","Gout: needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals","Pseudogout: rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals","Crystals and infection can coexist"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors, sections on osteomyelitis and infectious arthritis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on Clostridium perfringens and gas gangrene"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell Injury, Cell Death, and Adaptations, section on gangrenous necrosis"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Septic Arthritis - Overview (causes, pathophysiology, treatment)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tz07Uqx7_VY"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Bone Fracture - Types, Fracture Repair and Osteomyelitis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MNkI6Of2PRs"}],"related":["MS-12","MS-13-B4","IN-13-H3","CV-18-I6","HE-9-B1"]},"MS-13-H":{"id":"MS-13-H","summary":"Musculoskeletal trauma covers fractures and how they heal, soft tissue injuries, and the dangerous complications of injury: avascular necrosis, nerve and artery damage, fat embolism, compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis, and myositis ossificans. Exams link each injury to the structure at risk and the mechanism of harm.","notes":[{"h":"Fracture healing","points":["Hematoma and inflammation (first days): clot forms a fibrin scaffold; platelets and macrophages release growth factors.","Soft callus (about 1 to 3 weeks): granulation tissue and fibrocartilage bridge the gap.","Hard callus (weeks to months): woven bone replaces the cartilage by endochondral ossification.","Remodeling (months to years): woven bone becomes lamellar bone shaped along lines of stress.","Delayed union or nonunion: poor blood supply, infection, motion at the fracture, smoking, diabetes, malnutrition."]},{"h":"Fracture complications","points":["Scaphoid: blood enters distally and flows backward, so the proximal pole can die (avascular necrosis, nonunion).","Femoral neck: displaced fractures tear the retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery, causing femoral head necrosis.","Supracondylar humerus (child): brachial artery and median nerve injury; can lead to forearm compartment syndrome.","Long bone and pelvic fractures: fat embolism about 1 to 3 days later.","Fat embolism triad: hypoxemia, confusion, and petechiae (chest, axillae, conjunctivae)."]},{"h":"Soft tissue injury","points":["Sprain: ligament stretch or tear. Strain: muscle or tendon stretch or tear.","Contusion: bleeding into soft tissue from a blow. Dislocation: joint surfaces lose contact.","Ligaments heal slowly: dense regular tissue with poor blood supply and few fibroblasts; repair forms weaker scar."]},{"h":"Compartment syndrome","points":["Swelling in a closed fascial compartment raises pressure until capillary and venous flow stop.","Causes: tibial and forearm fractures, crush injury, reperfusion after ischemia, tight casts, circumferential burns.","Earliest signs: pain out of proportion and pain with passive stretch of the compartment's muscles.","Pulses are usually preserved early because arterial pressure is higher than compartment pressure.","Untreated forearm compartment syndrome leaves Volkmann ischemic contracture (fibrosed forearm flexors, flexed wrist, and clawed fingers)."]},{"h":"Rhabdomyolysis","points":["Muscle breakdown from crush injury, extreme exertion, heat, seizures, statins, or drugs and alcohol.","Dying muscle releases CK, myoglobin, potassium, and phosphate.","CK very high (often over 5 times normal, frequently many thousands of U/L).","Urine dipstick positive for blood with few or no red cells on microscopy (myoglobinuria); tea-colored urine.","Hyperkalemia can cause arrhythmia; calcium is often low early as it deposits in injured muscle.","Acute kidney injury from myoglobin casts, heme toxicity to tubules, and renal vasoconstriction."]},{"h":"Myositis ossificans","points":["Heterotopic bone forms in muscle after trauma, often in the thigh or upper arm of young athletes.","Zonal pattern: mature bone at the periphery, immature cells in the center.","Osteosarcoma shows the opposite: the most mature, mineralized tissue centrally with an infiltrative, atypical edge."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Fracture healing stages","cols":["Stage","Approximate timing","What happens"],"rows":[["Hematoma and inflammation","Hours to about 1 week","Clot forms; platelets and macrophages release growth factors; dead bone ends removed"],["Soft callus","About 1 to 3 weeks","Granulation tissue and fibrocartilage bridge the gap"],["Hard callus","Weeks to about 3 months","Woven bone replaces cartilage by endochondral ossification"],["Remodeling","Months to years","Woven bone replaced by lamellar bone along stress lines"]]},{"title":"Injuries and what they put at risk","cols":["Injury","Structure at risk","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Scaphoid fracture","Proximal pole blood supply","Retrograde flow from distal vessels is cut","Avascular necrosis, nonunion"],["Displaced femoral neck fracture","Retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery","Vessels along the neck tear","Femoral head avascular necrosis"],["Supracondylar humerus fracture","Brachial artery, median nerve","Displaced fragment injures the neurovascular bundle","Ischemia, compartment syndrome, Volkmann contracture"],["Long bone or pelvic fracture","Lungs, brain, skin","Marrow fat enters the veins","Fat embolism: hypoxemia, confusion, petechiae"],["Crush injury","Muscle and kidneys","Muscle necrosis releases myoglobin and potassium","Compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury"]]},{"title":"Soft tissue injuries","cols":["Injury","Tissue","Description"],"rows":[["Sprain","Ligament","Stretch or tear of a ligament"],["Strain","Muscle or tendon","Stretch or tear of muscle fibers or tendon"],["Contusion","Soft tissue","Bleeding into tissue from a blunt blow"],["Dislocation","Joint","Complete loss of contact between joint surfaces"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Sprain versus strain","text":"Strain has a T for tendon (and the muscle it belongs to). Sprain is the ligament."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 6 Ps","text":"Pain, Pallor, Paresthesia, Paralysis, Pulselessness, Poikilothermia. In compartment syndrome pain comes first; pulselessness comes late."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Blood but no red cells","text":"A dipstick positive for blood with no red cells on microscopy means heme pigment: myoglobin (rhabdomyolysis) or free hemoglobin (hemolysis)."}],"highYield":["Fracture healing order: hematoma, soft callus, hard callus, remodeling.","Scaphoid fractures risk proximal pole avascular necrosis because blood flows in from the distal end.","Displaced femoral neck fractures risk femoral head necrosis.","Supracondylar humerus fractures injure the brachial artery and median nerve.","Fat embolism after long bone fracture: hypoxemia, confusion, petechiae.","Compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch, with pulses often still present.","Rhabdomyolysis: very high CK, heme-positive urine without red cells, hyperkalemia, acute kidney injury.","Myositis ossificans matures from the outside in; osteosarcoma does the reverse."],"pitfalls":["A palpable pulse does not rule out compartment syndrome.","A normal first x-ray does not rule out a scaphoid fracture when the anatomic snuffbox is tender.","Fat embolism is delayed by a day or more, which helps separate it from immediate hemorrhage or pneumothorax.","Myositis ossificans can look worrying on biopsy; the orderly zonal pattern points away from osteosarcoma."],"dumps":[{"t":"Fracture healing timeline","p":"Draw a fractured long bone at four stages of healing. Label the cells and tissue present at each stage.","l":["Stage 1: hematoma with fibrin, platelets, and inflammatory cells","Growth factors from platelets and macrophages recruit repair cells","Stage 2: soft callus of granulation tissue and fibrocartilage","Stage 3: hard callus of woven bone by endochondral ossification","Stage 4: remodeling to lamellar bone by osteoclasts and osteoblasts","Lists two causes of delayed union (for example poor blood supply, smoking, infection, motion)"]},{"t":"Crush injury cascade","p":"Trace what happens after a leg is crushed under a heavy object for several hours. Draw arrows from the muscle to the heart and kidney.","l":["Swollen muscle raises compartment pressure","Capillary and venous flow stop, causing muscle and nerve ischemia","Muscle cells break down and release CK, myoglobin, potassium, and phosphate","Hyperkalemia threatens cardiac arrhythmia","Myoglobin forms tubular casts and injures tubules","Acute kidney injury follows","Urine is heme positive with few red cells"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors, section on fractures and fracture healing"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb, section on the scaphoid and supracondylar fractures"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb, section on fractures of the femoral neck"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone, section on fracture repair"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Bone Fracture - Types, Fracture Repair and Osteomyelitis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MNkI6Of2PRs"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Acute Compartment Syndrome","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=txAugSNF4Jk"}],"related":["MS-2","MS-13-E4","MS-13-F4","UR-12-A4"]},"NE-1":{"id":"NE-1","summary":"How the neural tube forms and closes in week 4, how its vesicles become the adult brain and ventricles, and which cells come from neuroectoderm, neural crest, or mesoderm. Exam questions link each step to the defect that follows when it fails: neural tube defects, holoprosencephaly, and the posterior fossa malformations.","notes":[{"h":"Neurulation","points":["The notochord (mesoderm) signals the overlying ectoderm to thicken into the neural plate in week 3 (BMP4 signaling is blocked by noggin and chordin).","The edges of the plate rise as neural folds, with the neural groove between them.","The folds meet and fuse in the midline starting near the future neck in week 4, then zip toward both ends.","The open ends are the neuropores: the anterior (cranial) neuropore closes about day 25, the posterior (caudal) neuropore about day 28.","Neural crest cells peel off the crests of the folds as they fuse and migrate widely.","The notochord later persists only as the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs."]},{"h":"Brain vesicles","points":["Three primary vesicles form by week 4: prosencephalon, mesencephalon, rhombencephalon.","By week 5 the prosencephalon splits into telencephalon and diencephalon, and the rhombencephalon into metencephalon and myelencephalon (five secondary vesicles).","The lumen of the tube becomes the ventricles, and the caudal tube becomes the spinal cord with its central canal.","The retina grows out of the diencephalon as the optic vesicle, so it is CNS tissue."]},{"h":"Who makes which cells","points":["Neuroectoderm (the tube itself): CNS neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependymal cells, retina, posterior pituitary, pineal gland.","Neural crest: dorsal root and cranial sensory ganglia, autonomic ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla chromaffin cells, melanocytes.","Mesoderm: microglia (from the monocyte and macrophage line)."]},{"h":"Neural tube defects","points":["Spina bifida occulta: the vertebral arches fail to fuse but the cord and meninges stay inside; often a tuft of hair or dimple over the low back.","Meningocele: meninges bulge through the bony defect in a sac; the cord stays in place.","Myelomeningocele: meninges plus spinal cord or roots sit in the sac; causes leg weakness and bladder and bowel dysfunction.","Anencephaly: the anterior neuropore fails to close, so the forebrain and skull vault do not form; polyhydramnios because the fetus cannot swallow.","Open defects leak alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), raising maternal serum and amniotic fluid AFP; amniotic fluid acetylcholinesterase is also high.","Skin-covered defects such as spina bifida occulta leave AFP normal.","Risk factors: low folate around conception, valproate, maternal diabetes. Folic acid 0.4 mg daily for all, 4 mg daily after a prior affected pregnancy."]},{"h":"Holoprosencephaly","points":["The prosencephalon fails to divide into two hemispheres in weeks 5 to 6.","Linked to sonic hedgehog (SHH) pathway defects, trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome), and fetal alcohol exposure.","Face and forebrain share midline signals, so the face shows midline defects: cyclopia, closely set eyes, cleft lip and palate, single central incisor."]},{"h":"Posterior fossa malformations","points":["Chiari I: cerebellar tonsils sit below the foramen magnum (usually more than 5 mm); often found in teens or adults with occipital headache worse on coughing.","Chiari I is strongly linked to a cervical syrinx (syringomyelia).","Chiari II: the cerebellar vermis and medulla herniate through the foramen magnum, with a lumbosacral myelomeningocele and obstructive hydrocephalus.","Dandy-Walker: the vermis fails to form or stays very small, and the fourth ventricle balloons into a cyst filling an enlarged posterior fossa, often with hydrocephalus."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Brain vesicles and what they become","cols":["Primary vesicle","Secondary vesicle","Adult structures","Ventricle"],"rows":[["Prosencephalon","Telencephalon","Cerebral hemispheres, basal ganglia","Lateral ventricles"],["Prosencephalon","Diencephalon","Thalamus, hypothalamus, retina, posterior pituitary, pineal","Third ventricle"],["Mesencephalon","Mesencephalon","Midbrain","Cerebral aqueduct"],["Rhombencephalon","Metencephalon","Pons, cerebellum","Upper fourth ventricle"],["Rhombencephalon","Myelencephalon","Medulla","Lower fourth ventricle"]]},{"title":"Neural tube defects","cols":["Defect","What is in the defect","Skin covered","Maternal and amniotic AFP","Typical result"],"rows":[["Spina bifida occulta","Bony arch gap only","Yes","Normal","Usually no symptoms; hair tuft or dimple"],["Meningocele","Meninges and CSF","Often","Normal if skin covered, high if open","Often normal nerve function"],["Myelomeningocele","Meninges, cord, roots","No","High","Leg paralysis, bladder and bowel dysfunction, Chiari II"],["Anencephaly","No forebrain or skull vault","No","High","Polyhydramnios; not compatible with life"]]},{"title":"Posterior fossa malformations","cols":["Malformation","What herniates or is missing","Associated with","Usual age at diagnosis"],"rows":[["Chiari I","Cerebellar tonsils below the foramen magnum","Syringomyelia, occipital headache","Adolescence or adulthood"],["Chiari II","Vermis and medulla through the foramen magnum","Lumbosacral myelomeningocele, hydrocephalus","Birth"],["Dandy-Walker","Vermis absent, cystic fourth ventricle","Hydrocephalus, enlarged posterior fossa","Infancy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the structures","text":"Chiari I moves one structure down (the tonsils). Chiari II moves two (vermis and medulla) and comes with the open spine."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Which end failed","text":"Anterior neuropore fails, the head end is open: anencephaly. Posterior neuropore fails, the tail end is open: myelomeningocele."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Inside or outside the CNS","text":"Cells that stay inside the brain and cord come from the tube. Cells out in the periphery (ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, melanocytes) come from crest. Microglia are the outsiders from mesoderm."}],"highYield":["High maternal AFP plus high amniotic acetylcholinesterase means an open neural tube defect.","Anencephaly causes polyhydramnios because the fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid.","Folic acid before conception lowers neural tube defect risk; valproate in pregnancy raises it.","Holoprosencephaly with cyclopia or cleft lip points to trisomy 13 or an SHH defect.","Chiari II almost always comes with a lumbosacral myelomeningocele and hydrocephalus.","A cape-shaped loss of pain and temperature in an adult with Chiari I means a syrinx.","Schwann cells and adrenal medulla are neural crest; oligodendrocytes and astrocytes are neuroectoderm."],"pitfalls":["Meningocele has no neural tissue in the sac; myelomeningocele does. Function is usually spared only in meningocele.","Spina bifida occulta does not raise AFP because the defect is covered by skin.","Microglia do not come from neuroectoderm like the other glia; they come from mesoderm.","Dandy-Walker is a missing vermis with a cyst; Chiari II is a vermis that is present but pushed down."],"dumps":[{"t":"Neural tube to adult brain","p":"Draw the three primary and five secondary brain vesicles. Beside each, write the adult structures it forms and the ventricle that comes from its lumen.","l":["Prosencephalon splits into telencephalon and diencephalon","Rhombencephalon splits into metencephalon and myelencephalon","Telencephalon: cerebral hemispheres and lateral ventricles","Diencephalon: thalamus, hypothalamus, retina, third ventricle","Mesencephalon: midbrain and cerebral aqueduct","Metencephalon: pons and cerebellum","Myelencephalon: medulla and lower fourth ventricle"]},{"t":"Neural tube defect table","p":"Make a table comparing spina bifida occulta, meningocele, myelomeningocele, and anencephaly. Include what is in the defect, AFP level, and the main clinical result. Add a row of risk factors underneath.","l":["Occulta: bony gap only, normal AFP, hair tuft or dimple","Meningocele: meninges and CSF only","Myelomeningocele: cord or roots in the sac, paralysis below the level","Anencephaly: failed anterior neuropore, no skull vault, polyhydramnios","Open defects raise maternal and amniotic AFP","Amniotic acetylcholinesterase high in open defects","Risk: low folate, valproate, maternal diabetes"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Central Nervous System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System (section on malformations and developmental diseases)"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-2","NE-10","NE-16","NE-20-C2"]},"NE-2":{"id":"NE-2","summary":"The microscopic parts of neurons, glia, and peripheral nerves, and how each responds to injury. Exam questions tie a cell type to the disease that targets it (Schwann cells in Guillain-Barré, oligodendrocytes in MS and PML) and ask what happens to an axon after it is cut or a neuron after it loses oxygen.","notes":[{"h":"Neuron parts and shapes","points":["Soma: holds the nucleus and Nissl substance (rough ER and ribosomes) for protein synthesis.","Nissl substance fills the soma and dendrites but is absent from the axon hillock and axon.","Dendrites receive input; the axon hillock and initial segment start the action potential; synaptic terminals release transmitter.","Multipolar neurons (one axon, many dendrites): motor neurons and most CNS neurons.","Pseudounipolar neurons: one process that splits into a peripheral and a central branch; found in dorsal root ganglia and most cranial sensory ganglia.","Bipolar neurons: one dendrite and one axon; found in the retina, olfactory epithelium, and the spiral and vestibular ganglia."]},{"h":"Peripheral nerve wrappings","points":["Endoneurium: loose connective tissue around each axon and its Schwann cell.","Perineurium: layers of flat epithelioid cells joined by tight junctions around each fascicle; this is the blood-nerve barrier.","Epineurium: dense connective tissue around the whole nerve, carrying its blood vessels.","Surgeons rejoin the perineurium when repairing a cut nerve so fascicles line up."]},{"h":"Myelin makers","points":["Schwann cell (PNS, neural crest): myelinates one internode of one axon.","A single Schwann cell can also wrap several unmyelinated axons without making myelin.","Oligodendrocyte (CNS, neuroectoderm): sends processes that myelinate internodes on many axons.","Nodes of Ranvier between internodes hold dense sodium channels, allowing fast saltatory conduction.","Guillain-Barré syndrome: immune attack on Schwann cell myelin, often after Campylobacter jejuni infection.","Multiple sclerosis: autoimmune loss of oligodendrocyte myelin. PML: JC virus kills oligodendrocytes in immunosuppressed patients."]},{"h":"Other glia","points":["Astrocytes (GFAP positive): foot processes on capillaries support the blood-brain barrier, buffer extracellular potassium, take up glutamate, and form the glial scar after injury.","Microglia: resident CNS macrophages; in HIV encephalitis they form microglial nodules and multinucleated giant cells.","Ependymal cells: ciliated cuboidal to columnar cells lining the ventricles and central canal; modified ependyma of the choroid plexus makes CSF."]},{"h":"Axon injury and repair","points":["Wallerian degeneration: the axon and myelin distal to a cut break down, and macrophages clear the debris.","Central chromatolysis in the cell body: the soma swells, the nucleus moves to the edge, and Nissl substance disperses as the cell ramps up protein synthesis.","PNS regrowth: Schwann cells line up in tubes (bands of Büngner) that guide the regrowing axon at about 1 mm per day.","CNS axons rarely regrow: oligodendrocytes make no guide tubes, CNS myelin contains growth inhibitors, and astrocytes form a glial scar."]},{"h":"Red neurons","points":["Marker of irreversible hypoxic or ischemic neuronal injury, seen about 12 to 24 hours after the insult.","Look: shrunken cell body, intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm, pyknotic nucleus, loss of Nissl substance.","Most vulnerable neurons: hippocampal CA1 pyramidal cells, cerebellar Purkinje cells, and neocortical pyramidal neurons.","Later stages after an infarct: neutrophils, then macrophages over several days, then reactive gliosis and a cystic space over weeks."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Neuron shapes","cols":["Type","Processes","Where found"],"rows":[["Multipolar","One axon, many dendrites","Motor neurons, most CNS neurons"],["Pseudounipolar","One process splitting into peripheral and central branches","Dorsal root ganglia, most cranial sensory ganglia"],["Bipolar","One dendrite, one axon","Retina, olfactory epithelium, spiral and vestibular ganglia"]]},{"title":"Glial cells","cols":["Cell","Origin","Main jobs","Disease link"],"rows":[["Astrocyte","Neuroectoderm","Barrier support, potassium buffering, glutamate uptake, scar","Reactive gliosis; astrocytoma"],["Oligodendrocyte","Neuroectoderm","CNS myelin on many axons","Multiple sclerosis, PML"],["Schwann cell","Neural crest","PNS myelin on one internode of one axon; guides regrowth","Guillain-Barré syndrome, schwannoma"],["Microglia","Mesoderm","Resident macrophages, phagocytosis","Multinucleated giant cells in HIV encephalitis"],["Ependymal cell","Neuroectoderm","Lines ventricles; choroid plexus makes CSF","Ependymoma"]]},{"title":"After an axon is cut","cols":["Site","Change","What it means"],"rows":[["Distal axon","Wallerian degeneration","Axon and myelin break down and are cleared"],["Cell body","Central chromatolysis","Neuron gearing up protein synthesis for repair"],["PNS tube","Schwann cell bands","Path for regrowth at about 1 mm per day"],["CNS","Glial scar, inhibitory myelin","Little regrowth"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Endo, peri, epi","text":"Read the layers from inside out: endo around the axon, peri around the fascicle, epi around the whole nerve. Peri is the barrier."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One versus many","text":"Schwann cells are one-to-one (one cell, one internode). Oligodendrocytes are one-to-many (one cell, many axons). So one oligodendrocyte loss strips several axons at once."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Red means dead","text":"A red neuron has bright red cytoplasm and a dark shrunken nucleus. It means irreversible injury from about half a day to a day earlier."}],"highYield":["Nissl substance is missing from the axon hillock, a classic identification point.","Dorsal root ganglion neurons are pseudounipolar; retinal and olfactory neurons are bipolar.","The perineurium, with its tight junctions, forms the blood-nerve barrier.","JC virus in PML destroys oligodendrocytes, so lesions are in white matter.","Microglial nodules with multinucleated giant cells mark HIV encephalitis.","Red neurons appear about 12 to 24 hours after ischemia; CA1 and Purkinje cells die first.","PNS axons regrow along Schwann cell tubes; CNS axons are blocked by the glial scar."],"pitfalls":["Schwann cells come from neural crest; oligodendrocytes come from the neural tube. Both make myelin.","Wallerian degeneration happens distal to the cut; chromatolysis happens in the cell body proximal to it.","Guillain-Barré targets PNS myelin and spares the CNS; MS targets CNS myelin and spares peripheral nerves.","Choroid plexus epithelium is modified ependyma, so the blood-CSF barrier sits in epithelial tight junctions."],"dumps":[{"t":"Peripheral nerve cross section","p":"Draw a cross section of a peripheral nerve with two fascicles. Label the three connective tissue layers, a myelinated axon with its Schwann cell, and the blood vessels. Mark which layer is the blood-nerve barrier.","l":["Endoneurium around each axon","Perineurium around each fascicle","Epineurium around the whole nerve","Perineurium labeled as the blood-nerve barrier with tight junctions","Schwann cell wrapping one internode of one axon","Vessels running in the epineurium"]},{"t":"Cut axon timeline","p":"Draw a motor neuron whose axon has been cut. Show what happens in the cell body, the distal axon, and the regrowing tip, and add a short note on why a CNS axon would not regrow.","l":["Swollen cell body with nucleus pushed to the edge","Dispersed Nissl substance (central chromatolysis)","Distal axon and myelin breaking down (Wallerian degeneration)","Macrophages clearing debris","Schwann cell tube guiding the new axon","Growth about 1 mm per day","CNS: glial scar, inhibitory myelin, no guide tubes"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Nerve Tissue & the Nervous System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System (section on cellular pathology of the CNS)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-1","NE-13","NE-20-A3","NE-20-B4","NE-20-B5","NE-20-G12"]},"NE-3":{"id":"NE-3","summary":"Where the main cortical areas, basal ganglia, cerebellum, thalamus, brainstem nuclei, and spinal cord regions sit, and what goes wrong when each is damaged. Exam questions give a deficit and ask for the lesion site, or give a lesion and ask for the deficit.","notes":[{"h":"Cortical areas","points":["Primary motor cortex: precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe; lesion gives contralateral weakness.","Primary somatosensory cortex: postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe; lesion gives contralateral sensory loss.","On both strips the leg sits medially (anterior cerebral artery territory) and the face and arm laterally (middle cerebral artery territory).","Primary visual cortex: banks of the calcarine sulcus in the occipital lobe; lesion gives a contralateral homonymous hemianopia.","Primary auditory cortex: transverse gyri of the superior temporal gyrus; one-sided lesion causes little hearing loss because each ear projects to both sides.","Broca area (dominant inferior frontal gyrus): lesion gives nonfluent speech with good comprehension. Wernicke area (dominant posterior superior temporal gyrus): fluent speech that makes no sense, with poor comprehension."]},{"h":"Basal ganglia","points":["Direct pathway: striatum inhibits the internal globus pallidus (GPi), releasing the thalamus, so movement increases.","Indirect pathway: striatum inhibits the external globus pallidus, releasing the subthalamic nucleus, which drives GPi to inhibit the thalamus, so movement decreases.","Dopamine from the substantia nigra pars compacta excites the direct pathway through D1 and inhibits the indirect pathway through D2; both effects increase movement.","Parkinson disease: loss of nigral dopamine neurons (Lewy bodies with alpha-synuclein) leaves the indirect pathway dominant, giving too little movement.","Huntington disease: early loss of GABA neurons in the caudate (indirect pathway) leaves the thalamus released, giving chorea; CAG repeat in HTT on chromosome 4.","Subthalamic nucleus lesion (often a small stroke): contralateral hemiballismus."]},{"h":"Cerebellum","points":["Compares the intended movement with what actually happens and corrects timing and force.","Purkinje cells are the only output of the cerebellar cortex and are inhibitory (GABA) onto the deep nuclei.","Vermis (midline) lesion: truncal ataxia and a wide-based gait; alcohol damages the anterior vermis.","Hemisphere lesion: ipsilateral limb ataxia, intention tremor, dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia, and falling toward the lesion side.","Signs are ipsilateral because cerebellar output crosses to the opposite cortex, and the corticospinal tract crosses back."]},{"h":"Thalamus","points":["Relay station: almost all sensation except smell passes through it to the cortex.","Thalamic infarct: contralateral loss of all body sensation.","Weeks to months later some patients develop thalamic pain syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy): burning contralateral pain set off by light touch."]},{"h":"Brainstem and spinal cord","points":["Cranial nerve nuclei: midbrain III and IV; pons V, VI, VII, VIII; medulla IX, X, XII; the XI nucleus sits in the upper cervical cord.","Gray horns: dorsal horn sensory, ventral horn motor (alpha and gamma motor neurons), lateral horn T1 to L2 sympathetic preganglionic neurons.","White columns: dorsal (fasciculus gracilis for legs, cuneatus for arms), lateral (lateral corticospinal tract), and anterolateral (spinothalamic tract).","Enlargements: cervical (about C4 to T1) for the arm plexus and lumbosacral (about L2 to S3) for the leg plexus.","The cord ends as the conus medullaris near L1 to L2 in adults; roots below form the cauda equina."]},{"h":"Consciousness","points":["Arousal needs the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) of the upper pons and midbrain, projecting through the thalamus to both hemispheres.","Coma comes from ARAS damage or from dysfunction of both hemispheres; one damaged hemisphere alone does not cause coma.","Delirium: acute, fluctuating loss of attention, often from infection, drugs, or metabolic upset, and usually reversible.","Brain death: irreversible loss of all brain and brainstem function: coma, no brainstem reflexes, and apnea with a high PaCO2; spinal reflexes may remain."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Cortical areas and lesions","cols":["Area","Location","Lesion result"],"rows":[["Primary motor (area 4)","Precentral gyrus, frontal lobe","Contralateral weakness with upper motor neuron signs"],["Primary somatosensory (areas 3, 1, 2)","Postcentral gyrus, parietal lobe","Contralateral loss of touch and position sense"],["Primary visual (area 17)","Calcarine sulcus, occipital lobe","Contralateral homonymous hemianopia, often macular sparing"],["Primary auditory (areas 41, 42)","Transverse temporal gyri","Trouble localizing sound; deafness only if both sides are lost"],["Broca (areas 44, 45)","Dominant inferior frontal gyrus","Nonfluent aphasia, comprehension intact, poor repetition"],["Wernicke (area 22)","Dominant posterior superior temporal gyrus","Fluent aphasia, poor comprehension, poor repetition"]]},{"title":"Thalamic relay nuclei","cols":["Nucleus","Input","Sends to"],"rows":[["VPL","Body touch, position, pain, temperature","Primary somatosensory cortex"],["VPM","Face sensation (trigeminal) and taste","Primary somatosensory and gustatory cortex"],["LGN","Vision (optic tract)","Primary visual cortex"],["MGN","Hearing (inferior colliculus)","Primary auditory cortex"],["VL","Cerebellum and basal ganglia","Motor cortex"]]},{"title":"States of consciousness","cols":["State","Arousal","Awareness","Brainstem reflexes"],"rows":[["Delirium","Fluctuates","Impaired attention","Present"],["Coma","Unarousable","Absent","Present or absent"],["Persistent vegetative state","Sleep-wake cycles, eyes open","Absent","Present"],["Brain death","None","Absent","Absent, with apnea"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Medial for Music, Lateral for Light","text":"MGN relays hearing; LGN relays vision."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"TRAP","text":"Parkinson disease: Tremor at rest, Rigidity (cogwheel), Akinesia or bradykinesia, Postural instability."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Don't Eat Greasy Foods","text":"Deep cerebellar nuclei from lateral to medial: Dentate, Emboliform, Globose, Fastigial."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Dopamine always says go","text":"Through D1 it pushes the go pathway; through D2 it blocks the stop pathway. Lose dopamine and you lose movement."}],"highYield":["Leg weakness greater than arm weakness points to the medial motor strip (anterior cerebral artery).","Huntington disease: caudate atrophy with enlarged lateral ventricles, autosomal dominant, anticipation.","Cerebellar hemisphere lesions cause ipsilateral signs; most other cerebral lesions cause contralateral signs.","The conus ends near L1 to L2, so a lumbar puncture lower down misses the cord.","A thalamic stroke can cause contralateral burning pain weeks later (thalamic pain syndrome).","Coma needs ARAS damage or bilateral hemisphere dysfunction.","Brain death keeps spinal reflexes but loses every brainstem reflex and the drive to breathe."],"pitfalls":["Broca and Wernicke aphasia both impair repetition; tell them apart by fluency and comprehension.","Parkinson disease is too little movement from a stronger indirect pathway; Huntington disease is too much movement from a weaker indirect pathway.","A vermis lesion affects the trunk on both sides; a hemisphere lesion affects the limbs on one side.","VPL carries the body and VPM carries the face; M for mouth helps place taste in VPM.","Delirium fluctuates and the patient can be roused; coma does not fluctuate and the patient cannot be roused."],"dumps":[{"t":"Basal ganglia wiring","p":"Draw the direct and indirect basal ganglia pathways from cortex to thalamus. Mark each connection as excitatory or inhibitory, add the substantia nigra with D1 and D2, and show what changes in Parkinson and Huntington disease.","l":["Cortex excites the striatum (glutamate)","Direct: striatum inhibits GPi, thalamus released","Indirect: striatum inhibits GPe, subthalamic nucleus released, GPi driven","GPi inhibits the thalamus","Dopamine excites direct via D1, inhibits indirect via D2","Parkinson: nigral dopamine loss, less movement","Huntington: caudate indirect neuron loss, chorea","Subthalamic lesion: hemiballismus"]},{"t":"Lesion to deficit table","p":"Make a table with these lesion sites: precentral gyrus, postcentral gyrus, calcarine cortex, Broca area, Wernicke area, cerebellar vermis, cerebellar hemisphere, VPL thalamus. Write the deficit and its side for each.","l":["Precentral: contralateral weakness","Postcentral: contralateral sensory loss","Calcarine: contralateral homonymous hemianopia","Broca: nonfluent aphasia, comprehension intact","Wernicke: fluent aphasia, poor comprehension","Vermis: truncal ataxia, wide-based gait","Hemisphere: ipsilateral limb ataxia and intention tremor","VPL: contralateral body sensory loss, later thalamic pain"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contributions of the Cerebellum and Basal Ganglia to Overall Motor Control"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Cortex, Intellectual Functions of the Brain, Learning, and Memory"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System (section on degenerative diseases)"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-7","NE-8","NE-11","NE-20-A2","NE-20-B3"]},"NE-4":{"id":"NE-4","summary":"The meninges and the spaces between them, the dural folds that set up herniation, the CSF spaces used for lumbar puncture, the blood-brain barrier, and CSF findings in meningitis. Exam questions place a bleed in the right space, predict what a herniation compresses, and read a CSF panel.","notes":[{"h":"Meninges and spaces","points":["Dura mater: tough outer layer; in the skull its two layers split to hold the dural venous sinuses.","Arachnoid: thin, avascular, pressed against the dura; pia: clings to the brain surface and follows every sulcus.","Epidural space: potential space in the skull, real space in the spinal canal (fat and veins).","Subdural space: potential space between dura and arachnoid. Subarachnoid space: real space with CSF and the large cerebral arteries."]},{"h":"Bleeds by space","points":["Epidural hematoma: a skull fracture at the pterion tears the middle meningeal artery; lens-shaped clot that stops at sutures; lucid interval, then rapid decline.","Subdural hematoma: torn bridging veins between cortex and dural sinuses; crescent clot that crosses sutures; elderly, alcohol use, infant shaking.","Spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage: usually a ruptured berry aneurysm (most often anterior communicating artery); sudden worst headache of life; xanthochromic CSF; vasospasm about 3 to 10 days later."]},{"h":"Dural folds and herniation","points":["Falx cerebri divides the hemispheres; tentorium cerebelli separates the cerebrum from the cerebellum, with the midbrain in its notch.","Subfalcine herniation: cingulate gyrus slides under the falx and compresses the anterior cerebral artery, causing contralateral leg weakness.","Uncal herniation: medial temporal lobe slides through the tentorial notch and presses on CN III (dilated, fixed pupil first, then down and out eye), the posterior cerebral artery (contralateral hemianopia), and the cerebral peduncle.","Compressing the ipsilateral peduncle gives contralateral weakness; pushing the opposite peduncle against the tentorium (Kernohan notch) gives ipsilateral weakness.","Tonsillar herniation: cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum compress the medulla, stopping breathing and circulation."]},{"h":"Cisterns, CSF flow, and lumbar puncture","points":["Cisterns are wide parts of the subarachnoid space, such as the cisterna magna behind the medulla.","Choroid plexus makes about 500 mL of CSF a day; about 150 mL is present at any time.","Flow: lateral ventricles, foramina of Monro, third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle, foramina of Luschka and Magendie, subarachnoid space.","Arachnoid granulations return CSF to the superior sagittal sinus.","The lumbar cistern runs from the conus (L1 to L2) to S2 and holds the cauda equina; puncture at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5, using the iliac crest line (about L4).","Needle path: skin, supraspinous and interspinous ligaments, ligamentum flavum, epidural space, dura, arachnoid, subarachnoid space."]},{"h":"Blood-brain and blood-CSF barriers","points":["Blood-brain barrier: endothelial tight junctions with no fenestrations, a basement membrane, and astrocyte foot processes.","Lipid-soluble molecules cross freely; glucose (GLUT1) and amino acids need carriers. L-dopa crosses; dopamine does not.","Blood-CSF barrier: choroid plexus capillaries are leaky, so the barrier is the tight junctions of the choroid epithelium.","Circumventricular organs have no barrier so they can sample or secrete into blood: area postrema (vomiting), posterior pituitary, OVLT, subfornical organ, median eminence."]},{"h":"Reading the CSF","points":["Normal: opening pressure about 6 to 20 cm H2O, 0 to 5 lymphocytes per µL, protein 15 to 45 mg/dL, glucose about 60% of blood glucose (40 to 70 mg/dL).","Low glucose means organisms or inflammatory cells are using it: bacterial, fungal, or tuberculous meningitis.","Neutrophils point to bacteria; lymphocytes point to viral, fungal, or tuberculous causes.","Guillain-Barré: high protein with a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Intracranial bleeds","cols":["Bleed","Space","Vessel","CT shape","Classic story"],"rows":[["Epidural","Between skull and dura","Middle meningeal artery","Lens shaped, stops at sutures","Temporal blow, lucid interval, rapid decline"],["Subdural","Between dura and arachnoid","Bridging veins","Crescent, crosses sutures","Older adult or heavy drinker, slow confusion"],["Subarachnoid","Between arachnoid and pia","Berry aneurysm or AVM","Blood in cisterns and sulci","Sudden worst headache, neck stiffness"]]},{"title":"Herniation syndromes","cols":["Type","What moves","What gets compressed","Result"],"rows":[["Subfalcine","Cingulate gyrus under the falx","Anterior cerebral artery","Contralateral leg weakness"],["Uncal","Uncus through the tentorial notch","CN III, posterior cerebral artery, cerebral peduncle","Blown pupil, contralateral hemianopia, hemiparesis"],["Tonsillar","Cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum","Medulla","Respiratory arrest, death"]]},{"title":"CSF patterns","cols":["Condition","Opening pressure","Main cells","Protein","Glucose"],"rows":[["Bacterial meningitis","High","Neutrophils, often in the thousands","High","Low"],["Viral meningitis","Normal or mildly high","Lymphocytes, tens to hundreds","Normal or mildly high","Normal"],["Fungal (cryptococcal) meningitis","Often very high","Lymphocytes","High","Low"],["Tuberculous meningitis","High","Lymphocytes","Very high","Low"],["Guillain-Barré syndrome","Normal","Normal count","High","Normal"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Artery is fast, vein is slow","text":"Epidural bleeds are arterial and expand in hours. Subdural bleeds are venous and can build over days to weeks."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Viral leaves the sugar alone","text":"Of the four meningitis types, only viral meningitis keeps CSF glucose normal."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Barrier-free zones do a job","text":"Each circumventricular organ needs blood contact for a reason: the area postrema tastes toxins, the OVLT reads osmolality, the posterior pituitary releases hormones."}],"highYield":["Pterion fracture plus lucid interval means an epidural hematoma from the middle meningeal artery.","A blown pupil on one side is the earliest sign of uncal herniation (CN III compression).","Rising intracranial pressure can produce the Cushing triad: hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing.","Lumbar puncture is done below the conus at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5.","Arachnoid granulations drain CSF into the superior sagittal sinus.","The area postrema lacks a barrier, so blood-borne toxins can trigger vomiting.","High protein with a normal cell count in CSF means Guillain-Barré syndrome."],"pitfalls":["Epidural clots stop at sutures; subdural clots cross sutures but stop at the falx and tentorium.","Uncal herniation usually gives contralateral weakness, but a Kernohan notch gives ipsilateral weakness and can mislead about the side.","Very early viral meningitis can show neutrophils; glucose stays normal, which keeps it apart from bacterial meningitis.","Choroid plexus capillaries are leaky; the blood-CSF barrier is in the epithelium, not the endothelium."],"dumps":[{"t":"Meninges and bleeds","p":"Draw a coronal slice through the skull, meninges, and brain at the vertex. Label the three meninges, the three spaces, the superior sagittal sinus, a bridging vein, and an arachnoid granulation. Then mark where an epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid bleed would sit.","l":["Dura, arachnoid, pia in order from skull to brain","Superior sagittal sinus between dural layers","Bridging vein crossing the subdural space","Arachnoid granulation projecting into the sinus","Epidural bleed between skull and dura (artery)","Subdural bleed between dura and arachnoid (vein)","Subarachnoid bleed in the CSF space (aneurysm)"]},{"t":"CSF panel table","p":"Build a table comparing CSF in bacterial, viral, fungal, and tuberculous meningitis and Guillain-Barré syndrome. Include opening pressure, cell type, protein, and glucose, and add normal values in the top row.","l":["Normal values with units: pressure, cells, protein, glucose","Bacterial: neutrophils, high protein, low glucose","Viral: lymphocytes, normal glucose","Fungal: lymphocytes, low glucose, very high pressure","TB: lymphocytes, very high protein, low glucose","Guillain-Barré: high protein, normal cells"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on cranial meninges)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System (sections on herniation, CNS trauma, and meningitis)"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-10","NE-20-A4","NE-20-C1","NE-20-G8","NE-20-H1"]},"NE-5":{"id":"NE-5","summary":"The receptors that pick up touch, pain, and stretch, the two main sensory tracts and where they cross, spinal reflexes, referred pain, how the body turns pain down, and the three common headache types. Exam questions ask you to predict sensory loss from a cord lesion and to name the root of a reflex.","notes":[{"h":"Receptors and fibers","points":["Fast-adapting receptors signal change (Meissner, Pacinian); slow-adapting receptors signal steady pressure or stretch (Merkel, Ruffini).","Free nerve endings carry pain and temperature over thin Aδ fibers (sharp pain, cold) and unmyelinated C fibers (burning pain, warmth).","Muscle spindles lie parallel to muscle fibers and sense length and its rate of change (Ia and II afferents); gamma motor neurons keep them taut.","Golgi tendon organs lie in series at the muscle-tendon junction and sense tension (Ib afferents)."]},{"h":"The two big sensory tracts","points":["Dorsal column medial lemniscus (fine touch, vibration, position): first neuron ascends on the same side in the dorsal column.","It synapses in nucleus gracilis or cuneatus in the lower medulla, crosses as internal arcuate fibers, rises as the medial lemniscus to VPL, then to the postcentral gyrus.","Spinothalamic tract (pain, temperature, crude touch): first neuron synapses in the dorsal horn, and the second neuron crosses within one or two segments in the anterior white commissure.","It rises in the contralateral anterolateral cord to VPL, then to the postcentral gyrus.","Cord lesion on one side: same-side loss of vibration and position below, opposite-side loss of pain and temperature starting one or two segments below.","Lesion above the lower medulla: all sensory loss is contralateral because both tracts have crossed."]},{"h":"Hemisection and syrinx","points":["Brown-Séquard hemisection: ipsilateral weakness (corticospinal), ipsilateral loss of vibration and position (dorsal column), contralateral loss of pain and temperature (spinothalamic).","At the lesion level: ipsilateral loss of all sensation and lower motor neuron signs; a lesion above T1 can add an ipsilateral Horner syndrome.","Syringomyelia: a fluid cavity in the central cervical cord cuts the crossing spinothalamic fibers on both sides.","Result: bilateral cape-like loss of pain and temperature over the shoulders and arms, with touch preserved; a growing syrinx adds hand muscle wasting."]},{"h":"Spinal reflexes","points":["Stretch reflex: tendon tap stretches the spindle, Ia afferent synapses directly on the alpha motor neuron, the same muscle contracts (monosynaptic).","An Ia inhibitory interneuron relaxes the antagonist at the same time (reciprocal inhibition).","Golgi tendon organ reflex: high tension fires Ib afferents, which act through an inhibitory interneuron to relax the same muscle.","Withdrawal reflex: a painful stimulus flexes the injured limb through several interneurons; the crossed extensor reflex stiffens the opposite limb to bear weight.","Reflex roots: biceps C5, triceps C7, patellar L4, Achilles S1."]},{"h":"Referred pain","points":["Visceral and skin afferents from the same spinal segment converge on the same dorsal horn neurons, so the brain blames the skin.","Heart (T1 to T4): pain felt in the chest, left arm, and jaw.","Diaphragm (phrenic nerve C3 to C5): pain felt at the shoulder tip."]},{"h":"Pain control and neuropathic pain","points":["Gate control: touch fibers (Aβ) activate inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn that damp pain transmission; rubbing an injury helps.","Descending control: the periaqueductal gray drives the rostral medulla (serotonin) and locus coeruleus (norepinephrine) to inhibit dorsal horn pain neurons.","Endogenous opioids (enkephalins, endorphins, dynorphins) cut transmitter release presynaptically and hyperpolarize the postsynaptic neuron.","Neuropathic pain comes from damage to the nervous system itself: postherpetic neuralgia, phantom limb pain, thalamic pain syndrome."]},{"h":"Headaches","points":["Migraine: often unilateral, pulsating, 4 to 72 hours, with nausea and light sensitivity; trigeminovascular activation releases CGRP; aura comes from cortical spreading depression.","Cluster headache: severe periorbital pain for 15 to 180 minutes with same-side tearing, runny nose, and sometimes ptosis and miosis; more common in men.","Tension headache: bilateral band-like pressure, no nausea, not worse with activity."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Sensory receptors","cols":["Receptor","Location","Senses","Adaptation","Fiber"],"rows":[["Meissner corpuscle","Dermal papillae of hairless skin","Light touch, low-frequency vibration","Fast","Aβ"],["Pacinian corpuscle","Deep dermis, joints","High-frequency vibration, deep pressure","Fast","Aβ"],["Merkel disc","Base of epidermis","Steady pressure, texture","Slow","Aβ"],["Ruffini ending","Dermis, joint capsules","Skin stretch, joint position","Slow","Aβ"],["Free nerve ending","Skin and most tissues","Pain, temperature, itch","Slow","Aδ and C"],["Muscle spindle","Within muscle, parallel to fibers","Muscle length and rate of stretch","Ia fast to change, II slow","Ia and II"],["Golgi tendon organ","Muscle-tendon junction","Muscle tension","Slow","Ib"]]},{"title":"Ascending pathways","cols":["Feature","Dorsal column medial lemniscus","Spinothalamic"],"rows":[["Carries","Fine touch, vibration, position","Pain, temperature, crude touch"],["First synapse","Nucleus gracilis or cuneatus, medulla","Dorsal horn of the cord"],["Crosses","Lower medulla (internal arcuate fibers)","Within 1 to 2 cord segments (anterior white commissure)"],["Thalamus","VPL","VPL"],["Hemisection loss below lesion","Same side","Opposite side"]]},{"title":"Headache types","cols":["Feature","Migraine","Cluster","Tension"],"rows":[["Location","Usually one side","One side, around the eye","Both sides, band-like"],["Quality","Pulsating","Severe, boring","Pressing"],["Duration","4 to 72 hours","15 to 180 minutes, daily in bouts","30 minutes to 7 days"],["Extras","Nausea, light and sound sensitivity, aura","Same-side tearing, runny nose, ptosis, miosis","No nausea; at most mild light or sound sensitivity"],["Mechanism","Trigeminovascular activation, CGRP","Hypothalamic, trigeminal autonomic","Not well defined"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"S1, 2 buckle my shoe; L3, 4 kick the door; C5, 6 pick up sticks; C7, 8 lay them straight","text":"Achilles S1 to S2, patellar L3 to L4, biceps C5 to C6, triceps C7 to C8."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive","text":"The phrenic nerve comes from C3 to C5, so diaphragm irritation is felt at the shoulder tip (C3 to C5 dermatomes)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pain crosses at once, touch crosses late","text":"Spinothalamic fibers cross within a segment or two of entering the cord; dorsal column fibers wait until the medulla. That is why a hemisection splits the losses to opposite sides."}],"highYield":["Brown-Séquard: same-side weakness and vibration loss, opposite-side pain and temperature loss.","Syringomyelia: cape-like pain and temperature loss with touch spared, often with Chiari I.","The stretch reflex is the only monosynaptic reflex; the Golgi tendon organ reflex relaxes the muscle.","Pacinian corpuscles sense vibration and adapt fast; Merkel discs sense steady pressure and adapt slowly.","Shoulder tip pain can come from the diaphragm (C3 to C5).","The periaqueductal gray drives descending opioid pain control.","Cluster headache gives same-side tearing and runny nose; migraine gives nausea and light sensitivity."],"pitfalls":["Muscle spindles are in parallel and sense length; Golgi tendon organs are in series and sense tension.","A dorsal column lesion causes loss on the same side; a spinothalamic lesion causes loss on the opposite side.","Syringomyelia loses pain and temperature on both sides; Brown-Séquard loses them on one side.","Postherpetic neuralgia is pain that lasts months after the shingles rash heals; it is neuropathic pain from nerve damage the virus left behind, not ongoing infection.","Migraine is worse with activity and makes patients lie still; cluster headache makes patients pace."],"dumps":[{"t":"Trace the two tracts","p":"Draw a spinal cord cross section, the medulla, the thalamus, and the cortex stacked top to bottom. Trace the dorsal column medial lemniscus pathway and the spinothalamic pathway from a receptor in the left foot to the cortex, marking each synapse and where each one crosses.","l":["First neuron cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglion","Dorsal column rises on the same side to nucleus gracilis","Crosses in the lower medulla as internal arcuate fibers","Medial lemniscus to VPL","Spinothalamic first synapse in the dorsal horn","Crosses within 1 to 2 segments in the anterior white commissure","Both end in the postcentral gyrus, leg area medially"]},{"t":"Stretch and tendon reflexes","p":"Draw the patellar reflex arc and the Golgi tendon organ reflex arc side by side. Label the receptor, afferent fiber, synapses, and effect on the muscle, and write the root level for the biceps, triceps, patellar, and Achilles reflexes.","l":["Spindle with Ia afferent","Direct synapse on the alpha motor neuron (monosynaptic)","Ia inhibitory interneuron relaxing the antagonist","Golgi tendon organ with Ib afferent","Inhibitory interneuron relaxing the same muscle","Biceps C5, triceps C7, patellar L4, Achilles S1"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Somatic Sensations: I. General Organization, the Tactile and Position Senses"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Somatic Sensations: II. Pain, Headache, and Thermal Sensations"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Motor Functions of the Spinal Cord; the Cord Reflexes"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-3","NE-7","NE-8","NE-20-H2","NE-20-G15"]},"NE-6":{"id":"NE-6","summary":"How vision, hearing, balance, taste, and smell are sensed and carried to the brain, plus the glands of the eye. Exam questions ask you to predict a visual field defect, read a pupil exam, interpret Weber and Rinne tests, and explain phototransduction and night blindness.","notes":[{"h":"Visual pathway","points":["Each retina's nasal half sees the temporal field; nasal fibers cross at the optic chiasm.","So each optic tract carries the opposite visual field to the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN).","Optic radiations: the Meyer loop runs through the temporal lobe carrying the upper field; the parietal radiations carry the lower field.","Both end on the banks of the calcarine sulcus; the macula is at the occipital pole, which has a dual blood supply."]},{"h":"Pupils","points":["Light reflex: CN II to the pretectal nucleus, then both Edinger-Westphal nuclei, CN III, ciliary ganglion, and the pupillary sphincter; both pupils constrict.","Near response: convergence, pupil constriction, and a rounder lens (ciliary muscle contracts, zonules relax).","Relative afferent pupillary defect: light in the damaged eye constricts both pupils less, so both dilate when the light swings to it; pupils are equal at rest.","Anisocoria from disease is an efferent problem: a large pupil from CN III palsy, a small one from Horner syndrome. A small difference can also be a harmless normal variant.","Compressive CN III lesions (aneurysm, uncal herniation) hit the outer pupillary fibers first; diabetic CN III palsy usually spares the pupil.","Argyll Robertson pupil (neurosyphilis): small, irregular pupils that constrict to near focus but not to light."]},{"h":"Phototransduction","points":["In the dark, high cGMP holds cGMP-gated sodium channels open, so the rod stays depolarized and releases glutamate steadily.","Light converts 11-cis retinal to all-trans retinal in rhodopsin, which activates transducin.","Transducin activates phosphodiesterase, cGMP falls, the channels close, and the cell hyperpolarizes, cutting glutamate release.","Retinal comes from vitamin A, so deficiency hits rods first: night blindness, then dry eye, Bitot spots, and corneal damage.","Rods: periphery, dim light, one pigment. Cones: fovea, color (three opsins), sharp detail."]},{"h":"Aqueous humor and tears","points":["Ciliary body epithelium secretes aqueous humor into the posterior chamber; it flows through the pupil to the anterior chamber.","It drains at the iridocorneal angle through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm; blocked outflow raises pressure (glaucoma).","Tear film: outer lipid layer (meibomian glands), middle watery layer (lacrimal gland), inner mucin layer (conjunctival goblet cells).","Lacrimal gland parasympathetics: CN VII, greater petrosal nerve, pterygopalatine ganglion, then to the gland."]},{"h":"Hearing","points":["Sound moves the tympanic membrane, malleus, incus, and stapes, which pushes the oval window; the size difference and lever action raise pressure about 20 times.","Endolymph in the scala media is potassium rich; when stereocilia bend, potassium flows in and depolarizes the hair cell.","Tonotopy: the narrow, stiff base hears high pitch; the wide, floppy apex hears low pitch. Noise and age damage the base first.","Weber and Rinne separate conductive loss (outer or middle ear) from sensorineural loss (cochlea or CN VIII)."]},{"h":"Balance","points":["Semicircular canals sense angular acceleration as endolymph lags and bends the cupula.","Utricle (horizontal) and saccule (vertical) sense linear acceleration and gravity through otoliths.","Vestibulo-ocular reflex: turning the head moves the eyes the same amount the other way to keep the image steady.","One-sided vestibular loss unbalances the input, causing vertigo and nystagmus with the fast phase away from the bad side.","Motion sickness comes from conflict between vestibular, visual, and body position signals."]},{"h":"Taste and smell","points":["Taste: anterior two thirds via CN VII (chorda tympani), posterior third via CN IX, to the nucleus solitarius, then VPM, then the gustatory cortex.","Smell: olfactory receptor neurons send axons through the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb, then to olfactory cortex with no thalamic relay.","Cribriform plate fracture tears these axons, causing anosmia and sometimes CSF leaking from the nose."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Visual field defects (right-sided lesions)","cols":["Lesion site","Common cause","Field defect"],"rows":[["Right optic nerve","Optic neuritis, trauma","Blind right eye"],["Optic chiasm, middle","Pituitary adenoma, craniopharyngioma","Bitemporal hemianopia"],["Right optic tract","Stroke, tumor","Left homonymous hemianopia"],["Right Meyer loop (temporal lobe)","Middle cerebral artery stroke","Left upper quadrantanopia"],["Right parietal radiations","Middle cerebral artery stroke","Left lower quadrantanopia"],["Right calcarine cortex","Posterior cerebral artery stroke","Left homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing"]]},{"title":"Pupil findings","cols":["Finding","Lesion","What you see"],"rows":[["Relative afferent pupillary defect","One optic nerve or retina","Equal pupils; both dilate when light swings to the bad eye"],["CN III palsy, compressive","Aneurysm or uncal herniation","Large fixed pupil, then ptosis and down-and-out eye"],["Horner syndrome","Sympathetic chain","Small pupil, ptosis, anhidrosis on one side"],["Argyll Robertson pupil","Dorsal midbrain (neurosyphilis)","Small irregular pupils, near response kept, light response lost"]]},{"title":"Weber and Rinne","cols":["Hearing","Weber","Rinne in the affected ear"],"rows":[["Normal","Midline","Air better than bone"],["Conductive loss","Louder in the affected ear","Bone better than air"],["Sensorineural loss","Louder in the normal ear","Air better than bone"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"COWS","text":"Cold Opposite, Warm Same: caloric testing, the fast phase of nystagmus beats away from a cold ear and toward a warm ear."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pie in the sky, pie on the floor","text":"Temporal (Meyer loop) lesions take the upper quarter of the field; parietal lesions take the lower quarter."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Weber picks a side","text":"In conductive loss the sound goes to the bad ear. In sensorineural loss it goes to the good ear."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Light turns rods off","text":"Rods and cones are most active in the dark and hyperpolarize when light hits them."}],"highYield":["Bitemporal hemianopia means the chiasm, most often a pituitary adenoma.","PCA stroke spares the macula; an optic tract lesion does not.","Argyll Robertson pupils accommodate but do not react to light, classic for neurosyphilis.","Vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness because rods need 11-cis retinal.","Endolymph is potassium rich; potassium entry depolarizes hair cells.","High-frequency hearing loss with age or noise reflects damage at the cochlear base.","Smell is the only sense that reaches cortex without a thalamic relay; cribriform fracture causes anosmia.","CN VII damage proximal to the greater petrosal branch causes a dry eye."],"pitfalls":["A relative afferent pupillary defect does not cause anisocoria; unequal pupils point to an efferent lesion.","In a negative Rinne (bone better than air), the loss is conductive.","Semicircular canals sense turning; utricle and saccule sense straight-line motion and tilt.","Taste from the anterior tongue travels with CN VII, while touch from the same area travels with CN V3.","Light hyperpolarizes rods by closing sodium channels; it does not open them."],"dumps":[{"t":"Visual pathway and lesions","p":"Draw both eyes, optic nerves, the chiasm, optic tracts, LGN, Meyer loop, parietal radiations, and calcarine cortex from above. Number six lesion sites and draw the visual field defect for each beside it.","l":["Nasal fibers crossing at the chiasm","Optic nerve lesion: one blind eye","Chiasm lesion: bitemporal hemianopia","Optic tract lesion: contralateral homonymous hemianopia","Meyer loop lesion: contralateral upper quadrantanopia","Parietal radiation lesion: contralateral lower quadrantanopia","Calcarine lesion: hemianopia with macular sparing"]},{"t":"Phototransduction steps","p":"Draw a rod in the dark and in the light. For each, show the state of rhodopsin, cGMP level, sodium channels, membrane potential, and glutamate release. Add where vitamin A fits.","l":["Dark: high cGMP, channels open, sodium in","Dark: rod depolarized, steady glutamate release","Light: 11-cis retinal becomes all-trans retinal","Transducin activates phosphodiesterase","cGMP falls, channels close, rod hyperpolarizes","Less glutamate released","Vitamin A supplies retinal; deficiency causes night blindness"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Eye: II. Receptor and Neural Function of the Retina"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Eye: III. Central Neurophysiology of Vision"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Sense of Hearing"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (sections on the orbit and the ear)"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-8","NE-14","NE-20-G9","NE-20-J4","NE-20-J8","NE-20-J10"]},"NE-7":{"id":"NE-7","summary":"How motor commands leave the brain for skeletal muscle and viscera, and how sensory signals from the body wall and organs get back in. Exam questions ask you to place a lesion by sorting upper from lower motor neuron signs and by matching a pattern of lost tracts to a named spinal cord syndrome.","notes":[{"h":"Lateral corticospinal tract","points":["Starts in the precentral gyrus (primary motor cortex), with help from premotor cortex; Betz cells sit in layer V.","Path: corona radiata, posterior limb of the internal capsule, middle part of the cerebral peduncle, basilar pons, medullary pyramid.","About 85 to 90 percent of fibers cross at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla and run in the lateral funiculus as the lateral corticospinal tract.","Fibers end on anterior horn alpha motor neurons (directly or through interneurons), whose axons leave in the ventral root to skeletal muscle.","The uncrossed remainder forms the anterior corticospinal tract for axial muscles.","Lesion above the decussation gives contralateral weakness; lesion in the cord gives ipsilateral weakness below the level."]},{"h":"Upper versus lower motor neuron","points":["UMN lesion (cortex, capsule, brainstem, cord tract): spastic tone, brisk reflexes, Babinski sign, little atrophy.","LMN lesion (anterior horn, root, nerve): flaccid weakness, reduced reflexes, fasciculations, marked atrophy.","ALS: loss of both UMNs and anterior horn cells, so both sets of signs appear together; sensation, eye movements, and sphincters are usually spared.","Poliomyelitis: poliovirus destroys anterior horn cells, giving asymmetric flaccid paralysis with pure LMN signs.","Spinal muscular atrophy: autosomal recessive SMN1 loss causes anterior horn degeneration; infants are floppy with tongue fasciculations and absent reflexes."]},{"h":"Somatic versus visceral motor","points":["Somatic motor is one neuron from the CNS to skeletal muscle; ACh acts on nicotinic (NM) receptors and is always excitatory.","Visceral motor is a two-neuron chain: a myelinated preganglionic neuron in the CNS synapses in an autonomic ganglion on an unmyelinated postganglionic neuron.","Visceral targets are smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands, and the effect can excite or inhibit.","The adrenal medulla is the exception: preganglionic sympathetic fibers end directly on chromaffin cells, which act as modified postganglionic neurons."]},{"h":"Spinal cord lesion patterns","points":["Subacute combined degeneration (vitamin B12 deficiency): dorsal columns plus lateral corticospinal tracts, with spinocerebellar tracts often involved.","Tabes dorsalis (tertiary syphilis): dorsal columns and dorsal roots, so sensory ataxia, positive Romberg sign, lightning pains, and lost reflexes.","Anterior spinal artery infarct: anterior two thirds of the cord; bilateral spastic weakness and pain and temperature loss below the level, with vibration and position sense spared.","Complete transection: loss of all motor and sensory function below the level, with spinal shock (flaccid, areflexic) first and UMN signs below the level later.","At the level of any cord lesion, damage to anterior horn cells adds LMN signs in that segment."]},{"h":"Visceral sensory pathways","points":["Visceral pain fibers travel back with sympathetic nerves to the T1 to L2 dorsal root ganglia, which is why organ pain is referred to the matching dermatomes.","Examples: heart pain to T1 to T4 (chest and medial left arm); early appendicitis to T10 (periumbilical).","Pain from pelvic organs below the pelvic pain line travels with the pelvic splanchnic (parasympathetic) nerves to S2 to S4.","Reflex afferents travel with parasympathetic nerves: carotid sinus baroreceptors by CN IX and aortic arch baroreceptors by CN X.","Both end in the nucleus solitarius (nucleus tractus solitarius) in the medulla, which adjusts vagal and sympathetic output."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Upper versus lower motor neuron signs","cols":["Sign","UMN lesion","LMN lesion"],"rows":[["Tone","Increased (spastic)","Decreased (flaccid)"],["Deep tendon reflexes","Increased","Decreased or absent"],["Babinski sign","Present","Absent"],["Fasciculations","Absent","Present"],["Atrophy","Mild, from disuse","Marked"],["Disease examples","Stroke in internal capsule; ALS (UMN part)","Poliomyelitis; spinal muscular atrophy; ALS (LMN part)"]]},{"title":"Spinal cord syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Tracts or parts lost","Key findings","Usual cause"],"rows":[["Subacute combined degeneration","Dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal, spinocerebellar","Lost vibration and position sense, ataxia, spastic weakness, Babinski sign","Vitamin B12 deficiency"],["Tabes dorsalis","Dorsal columns and dorsal roots","Sensory ataxia, Romberg sign, lightning pains, absent reflexes, Argyll Robertson pupils","Tertiary syphilis"],["Anterior spinal artery infarct","Anterior two thirds (corticospinal, spinothalamic, anterior horns)","Bilateral weakness and pain and temperature loss below level; dorsal columns spared","Aortic surgery or dissection, atherosclerosis"],["ALS","Lateral corticospinal tracts and anterior horns","Mixed UMN and LMN signs, no sensory loss","Degeneration; some familial cases with SOD1 mutation"],["Complete transection","All tracts at the level","Loss of all sensation and movement below level; spinal shock, then spasticity","Trauma"]]},{"title":"Somatic versus visceral motor pathway","cols":["Feature","Somatic motor","Visceral (autonomic) motor"],"rows":[["Neurons from CNS to target","One","Two (preganglionic and postganglionic)"],["Ganglion","None","Autonomic ganglion"],["Transmitter at target","ACh on nicotinic (NM) receptors","NE or ACh (on muscarinic receptors)"],["Targets","Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, glands"],["Effect","Always excitatory","Excitatory or inhibitory"],["Control","Voluntary","Involuntary"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cross point decides the side","text":"Find where the lesion sits relative to the pyramidal decussation. Above it (cortex, capsule, brainstem), the weakness is on the opposite side. Below it (spinal cord), the weakness is on the same side."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Back of the cord is the B12 and syphilis zone","text":"Both B12 deficiency and syphilis hit the dorsal columns. Add spastic signs (corticospinal) and you have B12. Add lost reflexes and lightning pains (dorsal roots) and you have tabes."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pain goes with sympathetics, reflexes go with parasympathetics","text":"Visceral pain fibers ride sympathetic nerves to T1 to L2 and refer to those dermatomes. Baroreceptor and chemoreceptor fibers ride CN IX and CN X to the nucleus solitarius."}],"highYield":["The lateral corticospinal tract runs in the posterior limb of the internal capsule and crosses at the caudal medulla.","ALS is the classic disease with UMN and LMN signs in the same patient and no sensory loss.","Polio and spinal muscular atrophy give pure LMN signs from anterior horn cell loss.","Anterior spinal artery infarct spares vibration and position sense because the posterior spinal arteries supply the dorsal columns.","Vitamin B12 deficiency damages dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts (subacute combined degeneration).","Carotid sinus afferents travel in CN IX and aortic arch afferents in CN X; both reach the nucleus solitarius.","Somatic motor uses one neuron and nicotinic receptors; visceral motor uses two neurons and a ganglion."],"pitfalls":["Babinski sign and brisk reflexes point to UMN damage; fasciculations and atrophy point to LMN damage. ALS has both, so mixed signs are the clue.","Right after a cord injury, spinal shock looks like an LMN lesion (flaccid, areflexic). UMN signs appear below the level over days to weeks.","Tabes dorsalis gives lost reflexes because the dorsal roots are damaged; subacute combined degeneration usually gives Babinski signs because the corticospinal tracts are damaged.","Visceral pain from most organs uses sympathetic pathways, but pain from pelvic organs below the pelvic pain line uses the pelvic splanchnic nerves."],"dumps":[{"t":"Corticospinal tract","p":"Draw the lateral corticospinal tract from cortex to muscle on a sketch that shows the brain, brainstem, and one spinal cord cross section. Label each structure it passes through and mark where it crosses.","l":["Starts in the precentral gyrus (primary motor cortex)","Passes through the posterior limb of the internal capsule","Runs in the cerebral peduncle, basilar pons, and medullary pyramid","Most fibers cross at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla","Descends in the lateral funiculus of the cord","Synapses on anterior horn alpha motor neurons","Ventral root and spinal nerve carry the LMN axon to skeletal muscle","Lesion above the decussation gives contralateral weakness, below gives ipsilateral"]},{"t":"Cord syndromes table","p":"Draw four spinal cord cross sections and shade the damaged area for subacute combined degeneration, tabes dorsalis, anterior spinal artery infarct, and complete transection. Under each, list the findings and the cause.","l":["Subacute combined degeneration shades dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts","B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration","Tabes dorsalis shades dorsal columns and dorsal roots, from tertiary syphilis","Tabes gives sensory ataxia, Romberg sign, and lost reflexes","Anterior spinal artery infarct shades the anterior two thirds and spares the dorsal columns","Anterior spinal artery infarct gives bilateral weakness and pain and temperature loss","Complete transection shades the whole cord and removes all function below the level","Transection shows spinal shock first, then UMN signs below the level"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cortical and Brain Stem Control of Motor Function"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Motor Functions of the Spinal Cord; the Cord Reflexes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on degenerative diseases affecting motor neurons"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on visceral afferent innervation and referred pain"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-3","NE-5","NE-9","NE-20-B","NE-20-H","NE-20-D"]},"NE-8":{"id":"NE-8","summary":"The twelve cranial nerves, the brachial plexus, the major limb nerves, and the dermatomes. Exam questions give a deficit, such as a down-and-out eye, a wrist drop, or a numb lateral foot, and ask which nerve, root, or site is injured.","notes":[{"h":"Cranial nerve deficits","points":["CN III palsy: eye down and out, ptosis, and a dilated pupil; a compressing posterior communicating artery aneurysm affects the pupil early.","CN IV palsy: vertical diplopia worse looking down and in (stairs, reading); the patient tilts the head away from the lesion.","CN VI palsy: the eye cannot abduct and drifts medially, giving horizontal diplopia.","CN V3 lesion: jaw deviates toward the weak side; CN X lesion: uvula deviates away from the lesion.","CN XII lesion: tongue deviates toward the lesion because the intact genioglossus pushes it over.","CN XI lesion: weak shoulder shrug on the same side and weak head turn to the opposite side."]},{"h":"Central versus peripheral facial palsy","points":["Motor neurons for the upper face get input from both hemispheres; neurons for the lower face get input mainly from the opposite hemisphere.","UMN lesion (stroke in cortex or internal capsule): weakness of the contralateral lower face only, forehead spared.","CN VII lesion (Bell palsy): weakness of the whole ipsilateral face, including the forehead and eye closure.","Depending on the site, a CN VII lesion can also cause hyperacusis (stapedius), lost taste on the anterior two thirds of the tongue, and less tearing."]},{"h":"Brachial plexus","points":["Roots C5 to T1, three trunks (upper C5 to C6, middle C7, lower C8 to T1), anterior and posterior divisions, three cords, five terminal branches.","Cords are named by their relation to the axillary artery: lateral, posterior, medial.","Erb palsy (upper trunk, C5 to C6): arm adducted, internally rotated, elbow extended, forearm pronated (waiter's tip).","Klumpke palsy (lower trunk, C8 to T1): claw hand from loss of intrinsic hand muscles, sometimes with Horner syndrome from T1 damage.","Axillary nerve (surgical neck fracture, anterior shoulder dislocation): weak deltoid abduction and numb lateral shoulder.","Radial nerve (midshaft humerus fracture): wrist drop and numb dorsum of the hand near the thumb.","Median nerve (carpal tunnel or supracondylar fracture): thenar weakness and numb lateral three and a half fingers on the palm.","Ulnar nerve (medial epicondyle fracture, hook of hamate): weak interossei, clawing of fingers 4 and 5, numb medial one and a half fingers.","Long thoracic nerve (C5 to C7, axillary surgery): serratus anterior paralysis with a winged scapula."]},{"h":"Lower limb nerves","points":["Common fibular nerve wraps the fibular neck; injury there gives foot drop, steppage gait, and numb dorsum of foot and lateral leg.","Tibial nerve: loss of plantar flexion and inversion and a numb sole.","Femoral nerve (L2 to L4): weak knee extension, reduced patellar reflex, numb anterior thigh and medial leg.","Obturator nerve (L2 to L4): weak thigh adduction and numb medial thigh, as after pelvic surgery or a difficult vaginal delivery.","Superior gluteal nerve: weak gluteus medius and minimus; standing on the affected leg makes the pelvis drop on the opposite side (Trendelenburg sign)."]},{"h":"Dermatomes and root lesions","points":["Landmarks: C6 thumb, T4 nipple, T10 umbilicus, L4 knee and medial leg, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot.","Matching reflexes: biceps C5 to C6, triceps C7, patellar L4, Achilles S1.","A posterolateral L4 to L5 disc herniation usually compresses the L5 root; L5 to S1 compresses S1."]},{"h":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia","points":["The MLF links the abducens nucleus on one side to the medial rectus subnucleus of the opposite oculomotor nucleus for conjugate lateral gaze.","MLF lesion: the eye on the lesion side fails to adduct on lateral gaze, and the opposite abducting eye shows nystagmus.","Convergence is preserved because it does not use the MLF.","Bilateral INO in a young adult strongly suggests multiple sclerosis; in an older adult, a brainstem stroke is more likely."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Cranial nerves at a glance","cols":["Nerve","Skull exit","Main functions","Sign of injury"],"rows":[["I Olfactory","Cribriform plate","Smell","Anosmia"],["II Optic","Optic canal","Vision; afferent limb of pupillary light reflex","Visual loss, afferent pupillary defect"],["III Oculomotor","Superior orbital fissure","Most eye muscles, levator palpebrae, pupillary constriction, accommodation","Eye down and out, ptosis, dilated pupil"],["IV Trochlear","Superior orbital fissure","Superior oblique","Vertical diplopia looking down and in"],["V Trigeminal","V1 superior orbital fissure, V2 foramen rotundum, V3 foramen ovale","Face sensation, muscles of mastication, afferent corneal reflex","Facial numbness; jaw deviates toward lesion"],["VI Abducens","Superior orbital fissure","Lateral rectus","Eye cannot abduct"],["VII Facial","Internal acoustic meatus, then stylomastoid foramen","Facial expression, taste anterior two thirds, lacrimal and salivary glands, stapedius","Whole half of face weak, hyperacusis"],["VIII Vestibulocochlear","Internal acoustic meatus","Hearing and balance","Sensorineural hearing loss, vertigo"],["IX Glossopharyngeal","Jugular foramen","Taste and sensation posterior third of tongue, stylopharyngeus, parotid, carotid sinus","Lost gag reflex (afferent limb)"],["X Vagus","Jugular foramen","Palate, pharynx, larynx, thoracic and abdominal viscera, aortic arch afferents","Uvula deviates away from lesion, hoarseness"],["XI Accessory","Jugular foramen","Sternocleidomastoid and trapezius","Weak shrug, shoulder droop"],["XII Hypoglossal","Hypoglossal canal","Tongue muscles","Tongue deviates toward lesion"]]},{"title":"Upper limb nerve injuries","cols":["Injury","Typical cause","Motor deficit","Sensory loss"],"rows":[["Erb palsy (C5 to C6)","Lateral traction on the neck at birth","Waiter's tip posture","Lateral arm and forearm"],["Klumpke palsy (C8 to T1)","Upward traction on the arm","Claw hand, weak intrinsic hand muscles","Medial forearm and hand"],["Axillary nerve","Surgical neck fracture, anterior dislocation","Weak abduction (deltoid)","Lateral shoulder"],["Radial nerve","Midshaft humerus fracture","Wrist drop","Dorsum of hand near thumb"],["Median nerve","Carpal tunnel, supracondylar fracture","Thenar weakness, weak thumb opposition","Palmar lateral three and a half fingers"],["Ulnar nerve","Medial epicondyle fracture, hook of hamate","Weak interossei, claw of fingers 4 and 5","Medial one and a half fingers"],["Long thoracic nerve","Axillary surgery, stab wound","Winged scapula","None"]]},{"title":"Lower limb nerve injuries","cols":["Nerve","Typical cause","Motor deficit","Sensory loss"],"rows":[["Common fibular","Fibular neck fracture or compression","Foot drop, weak eversion","Dorsum of foot and lateral leg"],["Tibial","Popliteal trauma, tarsal tunnel","Weak plantar flexion and inversion","Sole of foot"],["Femoral","Pelvic fracture","Weak knee extension, reduced patellar reflex","Anterior thigh, medial leg"],["Obturator","Pelvic surgery, difficult vaginal delivery","Weak thigh adduction","Medial thigh"],["Superior gluteal","Misplaced gluteal injection, hip surgery","Weak hip abduction, Trendelenburg sign","None"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Randy Travis Drinks Cold Beer","text":"Order of the brachial plexus from proximal to distal: Roots, Trunks, Divisions, Cords, Branches."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PED and TIP","text":"Peroneal (common fibular) Everts and Dorsiflexes, so injury gives foot dropPED. Tibial Inverts and Plantarflexes, so injury means you cannot stand on your TIPtoes."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Forehead test","text":"Ask the patient to raise the eyebrows. Forehead works: think central lesion (stroke). Forehead weak too: think CN VII itself (Bell palsy)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lick your wounds","text":"With a CN XII lesion the tongue points toward the bad side, as if licking a wound there. The uvula does the opposite and points away from a CN X lesion."}],"highYield":["A dilated pupil with a down-and-out eye and ptosis means CN III compression, classically by a posterior communicating artery aneurysm.","Forehead sparing means an upper motor neuron facial lesion; forehead involvement means a CN VII lesion.","Erb palsy is upper trunk (C5 to C6) with waiter's tip; Klumpke palsy is lower trunk (C8 to T1) with claw hand.","Midshaft humerus fracture injures the radial nerve and causes wrist drop.","Fibular neck fracture injures the common fibular nerve and causes foot drop.","In a positive Trendelenburg sign, the pelvis drops on the side opposite the injured superior gluteal nerve.","C6 thumb, T4 nipple, T10 umbilicus, L4 knee, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot.","INO: the eye on the side of the MLF lesion cannot adduct, convergence is intact, and bilateral INO suggests MS."],"pitfalls":["Tongue deviates toward a CN XII lesion, but the uvula deviates away from a CN X lesion.","Trendelenburg sign: the dropped hip is on the healthy side, and the weak gluteus medius is on the stance side.","Ptosis in CN III palsy is marked (levator palpebrae); ptosis in Horner syndrome is mild (superior tarsal muscle).","INO is named for the side of the MLF lesion, which is the side of the eye that fails to adduct; the nystagmus appears in the opposite, abducting eye."],"dumps":[{"t":"Brachial plexus","p":"Draw the brachial plexus from roots to terminal branches. Then mark where Erb palsy, Klumpke palsy, and the long thoracic nerve fit, and write the deficit for each terminal branch.","l":["Roots C5 to T1","Upper trunk C5 to C6, middle C7, lower C8 to T1","Lateral, posterior, and medial cords named by position around the axillary artery","Terminal branches: musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, ulnar","Long thoracic nerve from roots C5 to C7 to serratus anterior","Erb palsy at the upper trunk gives waiter's tip","Klumpke palsy at the lower trunk gives claw hand","Axillary: deltoid; radial: wrist drop; median: thenar; ulnar: interossei and claw of fingers 4 and 5"]},{"t":"Cranial nerve table","p":"Make a table on blank paper with all twelve cranial nerves. For each, write the skull exit, main function, and the sign you would see after injury.","l":["CN I cribriform plate; II optic canal","III, IV, VI, and V1 pass through the superior orbital fissure","V2 foramen rotundum; V3 foramen ovale","VII and VIII enter the internal acoustic meatus; VII exits the stylomastoid foramen","IX, X, and XI pass through the jugular foramen; XII through the hypoglossal canal","CN III injury: down and out eye, ptosis, dilated pupil","CN VI injury: failed abduction; CN IV injury: diplopia looking down and in","Tongue deviates toward a CN XII lesion; uvula deviates away from a CN X lesion"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on cranial nerves"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-3","NE-9","NE-20-I","NE-20-B","NE-6"]},"NE-9":{"id":"NE-9","summary":"The layout of sympathetic and parasympathetic outflow, the transmitters and receptors at each synapse, and two classic clinical pictures: Horner syndrome and autonomic dysreflexia. Exam questions ask you to trace a pathway to find a lesion or to predict a response from a receptor and its G protein.","notes":[{"h":"Sympathetic outflow","points":["Preganglionic cell bodies sit in the intermediolateral cell column (lateral horn) from T1 to L2, so the system is called thoracolumbar.","Axons leave in the ventral root, enter the white ramus, and synapse in a paravertebral chain ganglion or pass through as splanchnic nerves.","Splanchnic nerves synapse in prevertebral ganglia (celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric).","Preganglionic fibers are short and postganglionic fibers are long; gray rami return postganglionic fibers to every spinal nerve."]},{"h":"Parasympathetic outflow","points":["Craniosacral: brainstem nuclei of CN III, VII, IX, and X, plus the S2 to S4 lateral gray matter.","Ganglia lie near or in the target organ, so preganglionic fibers are long and postganglionic fibers are short.","The vagus supplies the thoracic organs and gut up to about the distal transverse colon; pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4) supply the rest of the colon, bladder, and erectile tissue."]},{"h":"Transmitters and receptors","points":["All preganglionic neurons (sympathetic and parasympathetic) release ACh onto nicotinic (NN) receptors in the ganglia.","Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic receptors.","Most sympathetic postganglionic neurons release norepinephrine onto adrenergic receptors.","Sweat glands are the exception: sympathetic postganglionic fibers release ACh onto muscarinic receptors.","Adrenal medulla chromaffin cells, driven by preganglionic ACh, release mostly epinephrine into the blood.","G proteins: alpha1 Gq, alpha2 Gi, beta1 and beta2 Gs, M1 Gq, M2 Gi, M3 Gq."]},{"h":"Horner syndrome","points":["First-order neuron: hypothalamus down the brainstem and cord to the ciliospinal center at C8 to T2.","Second-order (preganglionic) neuron: exits at T1, passes over the lung apex, and ascends the chain to the superior cervical ganglion.","Third-order (postganglionic) neuron: runs on the internal carotid artery (carotid plexus) to the dilator pupillae and superior tarsal muscle.","Loss gives ptosis (mild), miosis, and anhidrosis on the same side.","Pancoast tumor at the lung apex compresses the second-order neuron, often with arm pain and hand weakness from the lower brachial plexus.","Internal carotid dissection injures the third-order neuron, giving painful Horner syndrome; facial sweating is largely spared because those fibers follow the external carotid."]},{"h":"Autonomic dysreflexia","points":["Occurs with spinal cord injury at or above T6, above the major splanchnic outflow.","A stimulus below the lesion (full bladder, constipation, pressure sore) triggers a spinal sympathetic reflex.","Descending inhibition cannot cross the lesion, so splanchnic vasoconstriction goes unopposed and blood pressure rises sharply.","Baroreceptors sense the rise and drive vagal reflex bradycardia, which cannot lower the pressure.","Above the lesion: pounding headache, flushing, sweating; below the lesion: pale, cool skin. Removing the trigger (emptying the bladder) ends the episode."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Sympathetic versus parasympathetic","cols":["Feature","Sympathetic","Parasympathetic"],"rows":[["Origin","T1 to L2 lateral horn","CN III, VII, IX, X nuclei and S2 to S4"],["Ganglia","Paravertebral chain and prevertebral ganglia","Near or in the target organ"],["Preganglionic fiber","Short","Long"],["Postganglionic fiber","Long","Short"],["Ganglionic transmitter","ACh on nicotinic receptors","ACh on nicotinic receptors"],["Target transmitter","NE (ACh at sweat glands)","ACh on muscarinic receptors"]]},{"title":"Autonomic receptors","cols":["Receptor","G protein","Second messenger","Main effects"],"rows":[["Alpha1","Gq","Increased IP3, DAG, and Ca2+","Vascular smooth muscle contraction, pupil dilation (dilator pupillae), internal urethral sphincter contraction"],["Alpha2","Gi","Decreased cAMP","Reduced presynaptic NE release, reduced insulin release"],["Beta1","Gs","Increased cAMP","Increased heart rate and contractility, renin release"],["Beta2","Gs","Increased cAMP","Bronchodilation, skeletal muscle vasodilation, glycogenolysis, uterine relaxation"],["M1","Gq","Increased IP3, DAG, and Ca2+","CNS and enteric nervous system"],["M2","Gi","Decreased cAMP","Slower heart rate and AV conduction"],["M3","Gq","Increased IP3, DAG, and Ca2+","Gland secretion, gut motility, bronchoconstriction, detrusor contraction, miosis, accommodation"]]},{"title":"Parasympathetic ganglia of the head","cols":["Ganglion","Cranial nerve","Target","Effect"],"rows":[["Ciliary","CN III","Pupillary sphincter and ciliary muscle","Miosis and accommodation"],["Pterygopalatine","CN VII (greater petrosal nerve)","Lacrimal gland, nasal and palatine glands","Tearing and nasal secretion"],["Submandibular","CN VII (chorda tympani)","Submandibular and sublingual glands","Salivation"],["Otic","CN IX (lesser petrosal nerve)","Parotid gland","Salivation"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"QISS and QIQ till you're SIQ of SQS","text":"G proteins in order: alpha1 q, alpha2 i, beta1 s, beta2 s (QISS); M1 q, M2 i, M3 q (QIQ); D1 s, D2 i, H1 q (SIQ); H2 s, V1 q, V2 s (SQS)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Three neurons, three places","text":"For Horner syndrome, sort the cause by location: brainstem or cord (first order, such as lateral medullary stroke), lung apex (second order, Pancoast tumor), and carotid wall (third order, dissection)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Head ganglia by nerve number","text":"Count up the nerves: III goes to the ciliary ganglion, VII serves two ganglia (pterygopalatine for tears, submandibular for spit), and IX goes to the otic ganglion for the parotid."}],"highYield":["Sympathetic is thoracolumbar (T1 to L2); parasympathetic is craniosacral (CN III, VII, IX, X and S2 to S4).","Every autonomic ganglion uses ACh on nicotinic receptors.","Sympathetic fibers to sweat glands release ACh onto muscarinic receptors.","Alpha1 and M3 use Gq; alpha2 and M2 use Gi; beta receptors use Gs.","Horner syndrome: ipsilateral ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis from a break anywhere in the three-neuron oculosympathetic chain.","Pancoast tumor causes Horner syndrome by compressing the preganglionic neuron at the lung apex.","Parotid secretion comes from CN IX through the otic ganglion; tears come from CN VII through the pterygopalatine ganglion.","Autonomic dysreflexia needs a cord lesion at or above T6 and shows severe hypertension with reflex bradycardia."],"pitfalls":["The parotid is the one major salivary gland driven by CN IX; the submandibular and sublingual glands are driven by CN VII.","Pupil dilation is alpha1 on the dilator muscle; pupil constriction is M3 on the sphincter muscle.","In autonomic dysreflexia the bradycardia is a normal baroreceptor response; the problem is that the brain cannot send inhibition down past the lesion to the blood vessels.","Anhidrosis of the face is prominent with first- and second-order Horner lesions and largely absent with lesions on the internal carotid."],"dumps":[{"t":"Oculosympathetic pathway","p":"Draw the three-neuron pathway from the hypothalamus to the eye. Mark where a lateral medullary stroke, a Pancoast tumor, and a carotid dissection would interrupt it, and list the signs.","l":["First-order neuron from hypothalamus to ciliospinal center at C8 to T2","Second-order neuron exits at T1 and passes over the lung apex","Second-order neuron synapses in the superior cervical ganglion","Third-order neuron runs with the internal carotid artery","Targets: dilator pupillae, superior tarsal muscle, facial sweat glands","Lateral medullary stroke interrupts the first-order neuron","Pancoast tumor compresses the second-order neuron","Carotid dissection hits the third-order neuron and largely spares facial sweating","Signs: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis on the same side"]},{"t":"Autonomic synapse map","p":"Draw one sympathetic pathway to a blood vessel, one to a sweat gland, one to the adrenal medulla, and one parasympathetic pathway to the heart. Label each neuron, transmitter, and receptor.","l":["Sympathetic preganglionic neuron starts in the T1 to L2 lateral horn","Ganglion synapses use ACh on nicotinic receptors","Postganglionic fiber to blood vessel releases NE onto alpha1 receptors","Postganglionic fiber to sweat gland releases ACh onto muscarinic receptors","Preganglionic fiber ends directly on adrenal chromaffin cells, which release mostly epinephrine","Vagal preganglionic fiber synapses in a ganglion near the heart","Parasympathetic postganglionic fiber releases ACh onto M2 receptors to slow the heart"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Autonomic Nervous System and the Adrenal Medulla"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the autonomic nervous system"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the autonomic innervation of the head"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-7","NE-8","NE-13","NE-20-H","NE-17"]},"NE-10":{"id":"NE-10","summary":"The arteries that feed the brain and spinal cord, the path of cerebrospinal fluid, venous drainage, and how cerebral blood flow is kept steady. Exam questions give a stroke picture and ask for the vessel, show a ventricle pattern and ask where CSF is blocked, or ask what raised intracranial pressure does to perfusion.","notes":[{"h":"Circle of Willis","points":["Anterior supply: each internal carotid artery gives the anterior cerebral artery (ACA) and middle cerebral artery (MCA).","Posterior supply: the two vertebral arteries join to form the basilar artery, which ends by dividing into the posterior cerebral arteries (PCA).","The anterior communicating artery joins the two ACAs; each posterior communicating artery joins an internal carotid to a PCA.","Vertebral branches: PICA and the anterior spinal artery. Basilar branches: AICA, pontine arteries, superior cerebellar artery.","Lenticulostriate arteries come off the proximal MCA and supply the basal ganglia and internal capsule."]},{"h":"Cerebral territories and strokes","points":["ACA: medial frontal and parietal lobes, including the leg and foot area of motor and sensory cortex.","MCA: most of the lateral hemisphere, including face and arm cortex, Broca area, and Wernicke area.","PCA: occipital lobe and inferior temporal lobe; occlusion gives contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing.","Macular sparing happens because the occipital pole often gets collateral flow from the MCA.","Lenticulostriate occlusion causes a lacunar infarct; in the posterior limb of the internal capsule it gives pure motor hemiparesis.","Lacunar strokes are linked to chronic hypertension and hyaline arteriolosclerosis."]},{"h":"Brainstem strokes","points":["PICA (or vertebral) occlusion: lateral medullary syndrome with dysphagia and hoarseness (nucleus ambiguus), vertigo, ipsilateral Horner syndrome, ipsilateral face and contralateral body pain and temperature loss.","Anterior spinal artery occlusion in the medulla: medial medullary syndrome with contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral loss of vibration and position sense, and ipsilateral tongue deviation.","AICA occlusion: lateral pontine syndrome with ipsilateral facial paralysis and ipsilateral hearing loss on top of the lateral brainstem signs.","Basilar occlusion of the ventral pons: locked-in syndrome with quadriplegia and loss of speech, while consciousness, blinking, and vertical eye movements remain."]},{"h":"CSF production and flow","points":["Choroid plexus ependymal cells make about 500 mL of CSF a day; about 150 mL is present at any time.","Path: lateral ventricles, interventricular foramina (of Monro), third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle.","CSF leaves the fourth ventricle through the median aperture (Magendie) and two lateral apertures (Luschka) into the subarachnoid space.","Arachnoid granulations return CSF to the dural venous sinuses, mainly the superior sagittal sinus.","Ventricles upstream of a block dilate, and ventricles downstream stay normal (noncommunicating hydrocephalus).","Scarred arachnoid granulations (after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage) dilate all four ventricles (communicating hydrocephalus)."]},{"h":"Venous drainage","points":["Superficial cerebral veins drain to the superior sagittal sinus through bridging veins that cross the subdural space.","Deep veins join the great cerebral vein, which joins the inferior sagittal sinus to form the straight sinus.","Sinuses meet at the confluence, then flow through the transverse and sigmoid sinuses to the internal jugular vein at the jugular foramen.","Torn bridging veins cause a subdural hematoma: crescent-shaped, crosses suture lines, common in older adults with brain atrophy.","Dural sinus thrombosis blocks venous outflow and CSF absorption, raising ICP; causes include pregnancy, oral contraceptives, dehydration, and thrombophilia."]},{"h":"Cerebral blood flow and ICP","points":["Autoregulation keeps cerebral blood flow steady over a mean arterial pressure of about 60 to 150 mm Hg.","PaCO2 is the strongest local control: hypercapnia dilates cerebral arterioles and raises flow; hypocapnia from hyperventilation constricts them and lowers flow and ICP.","Low PaO2 dilates cerebral vessels only when it falls below about 50 mm Hg.","Cerebral perfusion pressure equals MAP minus ICP; normal ICP is about 5 to 15 mm Hg.","Cushing reflex: rising ICP lowers perfusion, brainstem ischemia triggers a sympathetic surge that raises MAP, baroreceptors then slow the heart, and brainstem compression makes breathing irregular."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Cerebral artery strokes","cols":["Artery","Territory","Classic deficit"],"rows":[["ACA","Medial frontal and parietal lobes","Contralateral leg and foot weakness and sensory loss"],["MCA","Lateral frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes","Contralateral face and arm weakness and sensory loss; aphasia if dominant side; hemineglect if nondominant side"],["PCA","Occipital lobe, inferior temporal lobe, thalamus","Contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing"],["Lenticulostriate","Basal ganglia and internal capsule","Pure motor hemiparesis (lacunar infarct)"]]},{"title":"Brainstem stroke syndromes","cols":["Artery","Syndrome","Unique clue","Other findings"],"rows":[["PICA","Lateral medullary (Wallenberg)","Dysphagia and hoarseness (nucleus ambiguus)","Vertigo, ipsilateral Horner, ipsilateral ataxia, ipsilateral face and contralateral body pain and temperature loss"],["Anterior spinal","Medial medullary","Tongue deviates toward the lesion (CN XII)","Contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral loss of vibration and position sense"],["AICA","Lateral pontine","Ipsilateral facial paralysis (facial nucleus)","Ipsilateral hearing loss, vertigo, ataxia, crossed pain and temperature loss"],["Basilar","Locked-in","Only blinking and vertical gaze remain","Quadriplegia, cannot speak, consciousness preserved"]]},{"title":"Where CSF is blocked and what dilates","cols":["Site of block","Example","Ventricles dilated","Ventricles normal"],"rows":[["Interventricular foramina","Colloid cyst of the third ventricle","Both lateral ventricles","Third and fourth"],["Cerebral aqueduct","Aqueductal stenosis","Lateral and third","Fourth"],["Fourth ventricle outlets","Dandy-Walker malformation, posterior fossa tumor","Lateral, third, and fourth","None"],["Arachnoid granulations","Scarring after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage","All four (communicating)","None"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Leg is medial, arm and face are lateral","text":"Picture the homunculus draped over the hemisphere. The leg hangs down the medial surface (ACA), while the face and arm sit on the lateral surface (MCA)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One unique sign per brainstem artery","text":"PICA means swallowing and voice (nucleus ambiguus). AICA means the face and ear (facial nucleus and labyrinthine artery). Medial medulla means the tongue (CN XII). Basilar means only the eyes are left."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Upstream swells","text":"Walk the CSF path from the lateral ventricles to the subarachnoid space. Every ventricle before the block dilates and every ventricle after it stays normal."}],"highYield":["ACA stroke hits the contralateral leg; MCA stroke hits the contralateral face and arm and causes aphasia or neglect.","PCA stroke gives contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing.","Lenticulostriate lacunar infarct in the internal capsule gives pure motor hemiparesis and is linked to hypertension.","Dysphagia and hoarseness point to PICA; ipsilateral facial paralysis points to AICA.","Aqueductal stenosis dilates the lateral and third ventricles but leaves the fourth ventricle normal.","Subdural hematoma comes from torn bridging veins; it is crescent-shaped and crosses suture lines.","Hyperventilation lowers PaCO2, which constricts cerebral vessels and lowers ICP.","Cushing triad: hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular breathing from raised ICP."],"pitfalls":["Lateral medullary syndrome causes crossed findings: pain and temperature loss on the same side of the face and the opposite side of the body.","In the medial medullary syndrome the tongue deviates toward the lesion while the body weakness is on the opposite side.","Bradycardia in the Cushing reflex is a baroreceptor response to the high blood pressure, so a slow pulse in head injury is a warning sign.","Communicating hydrocephalus dilates all four ventricles, while a block at the fourth ventricle outlets also dilates all four; the cause differs (absorption versus outflow)."],"dumps":[{"t":"Circle of Willis","p":"Draw the circle of Willis from the internal carotid and vertebral arteries. Label each vessel and its branches, then write the stroke deficit next to the ACA, MCA, PCA, lenticulostriate arteries, PICA, AICA, and basilar artery.","l":["Internal carotid gives the ACA and MCA","Vertebral arteries join to form the basilar, which splits into the PCAs","Anterior communicating artery joins the ACAs; posterior communicating joins internal carotid to PCA","ACA: contralateral leg; MCA: contralateral face and arm, aphasia or neglect","PCA: homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing","Lenticulostriate: pure motor lacunar stroke","PICA: lateral medullary syndrome with dysphagia and hoarseness","AICA: lateral pontine syndrome with facial paralysis","Basilar: locked-in syndrome"]},{"t":"CSF flow","p":"Draw the four ventricles and the subarachnoid space and trace CSF from where it is made to where it is absorbed. Mark four sites of obstruction and shade the ventricles that would dilate at each.","l":["Choroid plexus makes about 500 mL a day","Lateral ventricles to third ventricle through the interventricular foramina","Third ventricle to fourth ventricle through the cerebral aqueduct","Fourth ventricle to subarachnoid space through the foramina of Magendie and Luschka","Arachnoid granulations return CSF to the superior sagittal sinus","Aqueductal stenosis dilates the lateral and third ventricles only","Scarred arachnoid granulations dilate all four ventricles (communicating)"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the blood supply of the brain and the dural venous sinuses"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on cerebrovascular diseases"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-4","NE-3","NE-20-A","NE-20-C","NE-20-H","NE-11"]},"NE-11":{"id":"NE-11","summary":"The association cortex is the part of the cortex that plans behavior, directs attention, handles language, and recognizes what we see. Exam questions describe a behavior or speech pattern after a stroke or degeneration and ask you to name the damaged area, or match an EEG pattern to a sleep stage.","notes":[{"h":"Prefrontal cortex","points":["Handles executive function: planning, working memory, judgment, impulse control, and personality.","Dorsolateral damage causes poor planning, apathy, and perseveration.","Orbitofrontal damage causes disinhibition, impulsive and socially inappropriate behavior.","Frontotemporal dementia begins with personality change, disinhibition, apathy, or language decline while memory is relatively spared early.","FTD shows frontal and temporal atrophy; some cases have tau inclusions called Pick bodies."]},{"h":"Parietal association cortex","points":["Posterior parietal cortex builds a map of space and directs attention within it.","The right parietal lobe attends to both sides of space, while the left attends mostly to the right side.","A right (nondominant) parietal lesion therefore causes left hemispatial neglect: the patient ignores the left side of the body and the world.","A dominant (usually left) angular gyrus lesion causes Gerstmann syndrome: agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, and left-right confusion."]},{"h":"Language areas and aphasia","points":["Broca area (dominant inferior frontal gyrus) plans speech output.","Wernicke area (dominant posterior superior temporal gyrus) handles comprehension.","The arcuate fasciculus connects Wernicke area to Broca area and is needed for repetition.","Broca aphasia: nonfluent, effortful speech with intact comprehension; patients know their errors.","Wernicke aphasia: fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension; patients are often unaware.","Conduction aphasia: fluent speech and good comprehension but poor repetition.","Global aphasia: large dominant MCA stroke damaging all three, so speech, comprehension, and repetition all fail."]},{"h":"Visual association streams","points":["Dorsal stream (occipitoparietal) answers where: spatial location, motion, and visually guided reaching.","Ventral stream (occipitotemporal) answers what: object, color, and face recognition.","Fusiform gyrus damage on the ventral surface causes prosopagnosia, the inability to recognize familiar faces.","Dorsal stream damage impairs motion perception and accurate reaching toward seen objects."]},{"h":"EEG and sleep stages","points":["Awake and alert: beta waves (fast, low voltage). Awake, relaxed, eyes closed: alpha waves.","N1: theta waves, light sleep. N2: sleep spindles and K complexes; the largest share of the night.","N3: delta waves (slow, high voltage), deep slow-wave sleep, most common in the first third of the night.","REM: low-voltage mixed activity that looks like the awake EEG, with rapid eye movements, muscle atonia, and dreaming.","REM periods lengthen toward morning."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Aphasias","cols":["Type","Lesion site","Fluency","Comprehension","Repetition"],"rows":[["Broca","Dominant inferior frontal gyrus","Nonfluent","Intact","Impaired"],["Wernicke","Dominant posterior superior temporal gyrus","Fluent","Impaired","Impaired"],["Conduction","Arcuate fasciculus","Fluent","Intact","Impaired"],["Global","Broca area, Wernicke area, and arcuate fasciculus","Nonfluent","Impaired","Impaired"]]},{"title":"Association cortex lesions","cols":["Area","Normal role","Result of damage"],"rows":[["Prefrontal cortex","Planning, judgment, impulse control, personality","Disinhibition, apathy, poor judgment (as in frontotemporal dementia)"],["Nondominant (right) parietal","Attention to both sides of space","Left hemispatial neglect"],["Dominant angular gyrus","Linking written symbols, numbers, and body map","Gerstmann syndrome: agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, left-right confusion"],["Dorsal visual stream","Location and motion","Poor motion perception and visually guided reaching"],["Fusiform gyrus (ventral stream)","Face recognition","Prosopagnosia"]]},{"title":"EEG by state","cols":["State","EEG pattern","Notes"],"rows":[["Awake, alert","Beta","Eyes open, concentrating"],["Awake, relaxed","Alpha","Eyes closed"],["N1","Theta","Light sleep"],["N2","Sleep spindles and K complexes","Largest share of total sleep"],["N3","Delta","Deepest sleep; sleepwalking and sleep terrors arise here"],["REM","Low voltage, beta-like","Dreams, rapid eye movements, muscle atonia"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"At night, BATS Drink Blood","text":"EEG from awake to REM: Beta (awake), Alpha (relaxed), Theta (N1), Sleep spindles (N2), Delta (N3), Beta-like (REM)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Three questions sort the aphasias","text":"Ask: Is speech fluent? Is comprehension intact? Can they repeat? Broca fails fluency, Wernicke fails comprehension, conduction fails only repetition, and global fails all three."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Where is up, what is down","text":"The where stream runs up into the parietal lobe; the what stream runs down into the temporal lobe, where the fusiform face area sits."}],"highYield":["Frontal lobe damage and frontotemporal dementia cause disinhibition, apathy, and poor judgment with early memory relatively spared.","Right parietal lesion causes left hemispatial neglect.","Dominant angular gyrus lesion causes Gerstmann syndrome.","Broca aphasia is nonfluent with intact comprehension; Wernicke aphasia is fluent with impaired comprehension.","Conduction aphasia (arcuate fasciculus) impairs repetition with fluent speech and good comprehension.","Fusiform gyrus damage causes prosopagnosia.","N2 has sleep spindles and K complexes; N3 has delta waves.","REM shows a low-voltage, awake-like EEG with muscle atonia."],"pitfalls":["Broca patients understand and are frustrated by their speech; Wernicke patients speak easily but make little sense and often do not notice.","Neglect comes from the nondominant (right) parietal lobe, while Gerstmann syndrome comes from the dominant (left) angular gyrus.","Alpha waves mean awake and relaxed with eyes closed; they are not a sleep stage.","Frontotemporal dementia starts with behavior or language change, while Alzheimer disease starts with memory loss."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lateral brain map","p":"Draw a lateral view of the left hemisphere. Label Broca area, Wernicke area, the arcuate fasciculus, the angular gyrus, and the prefrontal cortex, and write the deficit from damage to each.","l":["Broca area in the inferior frontal gyrus gives nonfluent aphasia with intact comprehension","Wernicke area in the posterior superior temporal gyrus gives fluent aphasia with poor comprehension","Arcuate fasciculus links the two; damage gives conduction aphasia with poor repetition","Large MCA stroke across all three gives global aphasia","Dominant angular gyrus damage gives Gerstmann syndrome","Prefrontal damage gives disinhibition, apathy, and poor judgment","Right parietal damage (other hemisphere) gives left hemineglect"]},{"t":"Sleep stage table","p":"Make a table with columns for state, EEG wave, and key features. Fill in rows for awake alert, awake relaxed, N1, N2, N3, and REM.","l":["Awake alert: beta","Awake relaxed with eyes closed: alpha","N1: theta","N2: sleep spindles and K complexes, largest share of sleep","N3: delta, deepest sleep, more in the first third of the night","REM: low-voltage beta-like EEG","REM features: rapid eye movements, dreams, muscle atonia","REM periods lengthen toward morning"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Cortex, Intellectual Functions of the Brain, Learning, and Memory"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"States of Brain Activity: Sleep, Brain Waves, Epilepsy, Psychoses, and Dementia"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Section on frontotemporal lobar degeneration"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-3","NE-10","NE-12","NE-6","NE-20-B","NE-20-A"]},"NE-12":{"id":"NE-12","summary":"The hypothalamus controls temperature, appetite, water balance, and daily rhythms, and the limbic system handles memory and emotion. Exam questions link a lesion to a change in eating, temperature, memory, or behavior, and test the mechanism of fever and the common sleep disorders.","notes":[{"h":"Hypothalamic nuclei","points":["Supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei make ADH and oxytocin, which travel down axons to the posterior pituitary.","Arcuate nucleus releases dopamine (inhibits prolactin) and GHRH into the portal system.","Lateral nucleus drives hunger; lesions cause anorexia and weight loss.","Ventromedial nucleus signals satiety; lesions cause hyperphagia and obesity, as with a craniopharyngioma.","Leptin from fat cells stimulates satiety pathways and inhibits hunger pathways.","Anterior hypothalamus drives heat loss (sweating, vasodilation); lesions cause hyperthermia.","Posterior hypothalamus drives heat conservation (shivering, vasoconstriction); lesions cause hypothermia."]},{"h":"Fever","points":["Pyrogens such as bacterial LPS cause macrophages to release IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha.","These cytokines act near the preoptic anterior hypothalamus, where the blood-brain barrier is leaky, and induce COX-2.","COX-2 makes prostaglandin E2, which raises the hypothalamic set point.","Body temperature now sits below the new set point, so the person feels cold, shivers, and vasoconstricts until temperature rises, for example from 37 °C (98.6 °F) to 39 °C (102.2 °F).","When the set point falls back, the person sweats and vasodilates.","Hyperthermia (heat stroke) differs: the set point is normal and heat gain exceeds heat loss."]},{"h":"Limbic system","points":["Parts: hippocampus, amygdala, cingulate gyrus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamic nucleus.","Papez circuit: hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, mammillothalamic tract, anterior thalamus, cingulate gyrus, back to the hippocampus.","Bilateral hippocampal damage causes anterograde amnesia: new facts cannot be stored, old memories remain.","Thiamine deficiency (often alcohol use disorder) damages the mammillary bodies and medial thalamus.","Wernicke encephalopathy is confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia; Korsakoff syndrome adds lasting amnesia with confabulation.","Bilateral amygdala damage (for example HSV-1 encephalitis) causes Kluver-Bucy syndrome: hyperorality, hypersexuality, docility, and visual agnosia."]},{"h":"Circadian rhythm and narcolepsy","points":["Light hits melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells, which signal the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) through the retinohypothalamic tract.","The SCN signals the pineal gland through a sympathetic relay (superior cervical ganglion); darkness raises melatonin and light suppresses it.","Melatonin is made from serotonin, which is made from tryptophan.","Narcolepsy type 1: loss of orexin (hypocretin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, with low CSF hypocretin.","Orexin stabilizes wakefulness and keeps REM features out of waking, so its loss gives daytime sleepiness, sleep-onset REM, cataplexy, sleep paralysis, and hypnagogic hallucinations.","Cataplexy is sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion such as laughter, with consciousness preserved."]},{"h":"Sleep disorders","points":["REM sleep behavior disorder: normal REM atonia is lost, so patients act out dreams, sometimes violently.","REM sleep behavior disorder often precedes Parkinson disease or dementia with Lewy bodies by years.","Sleepwalking and sleep terrors arise from N3, usually in children, in the first third of the night, with no memory of the event.","Nightmares arise from REM, later in the night, and the child remembers the dream.","Restless legs syndrome: an urge to move the legs at rest, worse in the evening and relieved by movement; linked to iron deficiency (low ferritin), kidney disease, and pregnancy."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypothalamic nuclei","cols":["Nucleus","Main function","Result of a lesion"],"rows":[["Supraoptic","ADH (mainly)","Central diabetes insipidus"],["Paraventricular","Oxytocin (mainly), also ADH, CRH, TRH","Loss of oxytocin and ADH release"],["Arcuate","Dopamine and GHRH","Raised prolactin, reduced GH"],["Lateral","Hunger","Anorexia, weight loss"],["Ventromedial","Satiety","Hyperphagia, obesity"],["Anterior","Heat loss, cooling","Hyperthermia"],["Posterior","Heat conservation, warming","Hypothermia"],["Suprachiasmatic","Circadian rhythm","Disrupted sleep-wake cycle"]]},{"title":"Limbic lesions","cols":["Structure","Normal role","Lesion or disease","Result"],"rows":[["Hippocampus","Forming new declarative memories","Bilateral damage (hypoxia, HSV encephalitis)","Anterograde amnesia"],["Mammillary bodies","Memory relay in the Papez circuit","Thiamine deficiency (Wernicke-Korsakoff)","Confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia; then amnesia with confabulation"],["Amygdala","Fear and emotional memory","Bilateral damage (HSV-1 encephalitis)","Kluver-Bucy: hyperorality, hypersexuality, docility"],["Cingulate gyrus","Emotion, motivation, and the emotional part of pain","Damage","Apathy and reduced motivation"]]},{"title":"Sleep disorders","cols":["Disorder","Stage or cause","Key features","Linked to"],"rows":[["Narcolepsy type 1","Loss of lateral hypothalamic orexin neurons","Daytime sleepiness, cataplexy, sleep-onset REM, sleep paralysis","Low CSF hypocretin"],["REM sleep behavior disorder","Loss of REM atonia","Acting out dreams","Later Parkinson disease or Lewy body dementia"],["Sleepwalking and sleep terrors","N3 arousal","Child, first third of night, no memory","Usually outgrown"],["Restless legs syndrome","Brain iron and dopamine dysfunction","Urge to move legs at rest, eased by movement","Iron deficiency, kidney disease, pregnancy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"A/C and posterior heater","text":"Anterior hypothalamus is the A/C (cools); posterior hypothalamus is the heater (warms)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Zap the lateral, shrink laterally; zap the ventromedial, grow ventrally and medially","text":"Lateral nucleus lesion causes weight loss (anorexia). Ventromedial nucleus lesion causes overeating and weight gain."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fever is a thermostat change","text":"In fever, PGE2 turns the thermostat up and the body works to reach it (chills). In heat stroke the thermostat is normal and the body simply cannot shed heat."}],"highYield":["Supraoptic nucleus makes mostly ADH; paraventricular nucleus makes mostly oxytocin.","Ventromedial lesion causes hyperphagia; lateral lesion causes anorexia.","IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha raise the set point through COX-2 and prostaglandin E2.","Bilateral hippocampal damage causes anterograde amnesia.","Thiamine deficiency damages the mammillary bodies and causes Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.","Bilateral amygdala damage causes Kluver-Bucy syndrome.","Narcolepsy with cataplexy comes from loss of lateral hypothalamic orexin (hypocretin) neurons.","REM sleep behavior disorder often predicts a later synucleinopathy such as Parkinson disease."],"pitfalls":["Anterior hypothalamic lesions cause hyperthermia (heat loss center lost); posterior lesions cause hypothermia (heat conservation center lost).","Chills at fever onset mean the set point has risen above body temperature; the hypothalamus is working normally.","Sleep terrors arise from N3 and are not remembered; nightmares arise from REM and are remembered.","Cataplexy keeps consciousness intact, which separates it from a seizure or syncope."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypothalamus map","p":"Draw a sagittal view of the hypothalamus and pituitary. Label the supraoptic, paraventricular, arcuate, lateral, ventromedial, anterior, posterior, and suprachiasmatic nuclei and write what each does and what a lesion causes.","l":["Supraoptic and paraventricular make ADH and oxytocin for the posterior pituitary","Arcuate releases dopamine and GHRH","Lateral nucleus: hunger; lesion gives anorexia","Ventromedial nucleus: satiety; lesion gives hyperphagia and obesity","Anterior: heat loss; lesion gives hyperthermia","Posterior: heat conservation; lesion gives hypothermia","Suprachiasmatic: circadian rhythm, driven by retinal light input","Lateral hypothalamic orexin neurons lost in narcolepsy"]},{"t":"Fever pathway","p":"Trace how a bacterial infection raises body temperature. Start with the pathogen and end with shivering, and then show what happens when the fever breaks.","l":["Pyrogen such as LPS stimulates macrophages","Macrophages release IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha","Cytokines induce COX-2 near the preoptic anterior hypothalamus","COX-2 makes prostaglandin E2","PGE2 raises the hypothalamic set point","Body is below the new set point, so shivering and vasoconstriction raise temperature","When the set point falls, sweating and vasodilation bring temperature down","Heat stroke differs because the set point stays normal"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Behavioral and Motivational Mechanisms of the Brain: The Limbic System and the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Body Temperature Regulation and Fever"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"States of Brain Activity: Sleep, Brain Waves, Epilepsy, Psychoses, and Dementia"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on thiamine"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-11","NE-17","NE-18","NE-20-D","NE-20-G","NE-20-B"]},"NE-13":{"id":"NE-13","summary":"This unit covers how neurons and muscle cells hold a resting voltage, fire action potentials, pass signals across synapses, and conduct them along axons. Exam questions ask you to predict what happens when potassium shifts, when myelin is lost, when the neuromuscular junction is blocked, and when firing becomes abnormally synchronized in a seizure.","notes":[{"h":"Resting membrane potential","points":["The Na+/K+ ATPase pumps 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in per ATP, which keeps the gradients and adds a few millivolts of negativity.","At rest the membrane is most permeable to K+ through leak channels, so the resting potential sits close to the K+ equilibrium potential.","Nernst equation at body temperature: E = (61.5 / z) x log([ion outside] / [ion inside]) in mV. E for K+ is about -90 mV; E for Na+ is about +60 mV.","Resting potential is about -70 mV in neurons and about -90 mV in skeletal muscle.","Hyperkalemia shrinks the K+ gradient and depolarizes the cell; a mild rise makes cells easier to fire, but sustained depolarization inactivates Na+ channels and causes weakness and arrhythmias.","Hypokalemia hyperpolarizes the cell, moving it farther from threshold, which causes weakness or flaccid paralysis."]},{"h":"Action potential and refractory periods","points":["Threshold (about -55 mV in neurons) opens voltage-gated Na+ channels; Na+ rushes in and the upstroke heads toward E for Na+.","Within about 1 ms the Na+ channel inactivation gate closes, and slower voltage-gated K+ channels open, so K+ leaves and the cell repolarizes.","K+ channels close slowly, so the membrane briefly dips below the resting potential toward E for K+ (afterhyperpolarization).","Absolute refractory period: Na+ channels are inactivated, so no stimulus of any size can fire another spike.","Relative refractory period: some Na+ channels have recovered but K+ conductance is still high, so only a stronger than normal stimulus fires a spike.","The action potential is all or none and travels without losing size."]},{"h":"Graded potentials and summation","points":["EPSPs are small depolarizations from ligand-gated cation channels (for example AMPA receptors letting Na+ in).","IPSPs hold the cell below threshold by letting Cl- in (GABA-A, glycine receptors) or K+ out (GABA-B).","Temporal summation: one synapse fires repeatedly, and each potential adds to the last before it fades.","Spatial summation: many synapses fire at the same time, and their potentials add together.","The axon hillock (initial segment) has the densest voltage-gated Na+ channels, so it has the lowest threshold and is where the net sum triggers the spike."]},{"h":"Conduction velocity and myelin","points":["Myelin raises membrane resistance and lowers capacitance, so current spreads farther and faster along the internode.","Voltage-gated Na+ channels cluster at the nodes of Ranvier, so the spike regenerates only at nodes (saltatory conduction).","A wider axon has less internal resistance, so it conducts faster. Large myelinated A-alpha fibers are fastest; thin unmyelinated C fibers are slowest.","Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons (one cell, many axons); Schwann cells myelinate PNS axons (one cell per internode).","Demyelination lets current leak out across the bare segment, so conduction slows; if too little current reaches the next node, conduction blocks.","Multiple sclerosis strips CNS myelin; Guillain-Barré syndrome strips peripheral nerve myelin. Heat worsens conduction in demyelinated axons (Uhthoff phenomenon)."]},{"h":"Neuromuscular junction","points":["The nerve action potential opens presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels (P/Q type), and Ca2+ enters the terminal.","SNARE proteins (synaptobrevin on the vesicle, syntaxin and SNAP-25 on the membrane) fuse vesicles and release acetylcholine.","Acetylcholine binds nicotinic receptors (ligand-gated cation channels) on the end plate, producing an end-plate potential that fires the muscle.","Acetylcholinesterase in the cleft splits acetylcholine into acetate and choline, ending the signal; choline is taken back up."]},{"h":"Seizures and periodic paralysis","points":["A seizure is excessive, synchronous firing of cortical neurons when excitation (glutamate) outweighs inhibition (GABA).","Focal seizures start in one hemisphere and occur with or without impaired awareness; they can spread to both sides (focal to bilateral tonic-clonic).","Generalized seizures involve both hemispheres from the start: tonic-clonic (stiffening, then jerking, then postictal confusion) or absence.","Absence seizures: a child has brief staring spells with no postictal confusion, often triggered by hyperventilation; EEG shows 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharges.","Febrile seizures occur in children about 6 months to 5 years old with fever of 38 °C (100.4 °F) or higher and no CNS infection; simple ones are brief and generalized and carry a good outlook.","Hypokalemic periodic paralysis is an inherited (usually autosomal dominant) skeletal muscle Ca2+ or Na+ channel defect causing attacks of flaccid weakness with low serum K+.","Attacks follow carbohydrate-rich meals (insulin drives K+ into cells) or rest after exercise; potassium is normal between attacks."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Graded potentials vs action potentials","cols":["Feature","Graded potential (EPSP or IPSP)","Action potential"],"rows":[["Where","Dendrites and cell body","Starts at the axon hillock, travels down the axon"],["Channels","Ligand-gated (or sensory) channels","Voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels"],["Size","Varies with stimulus strength","All or none, fixed size"],["Direction","Depolarizing or hyperpolarizing","Always depolarizing upstroke"],["Over distance","Fades as it spreads","Regenerates, no loss of size"],["Summation","Yes, temporal and spatial","No"],["Refractory period","None","Absolute, then relative"]]},{"title":"Disorders of neuromuscular transmission","cols":["Disorder","Target","Effect on transmission","Key clinical clues"],"rows":[["Botulism","Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins","No acetylcholine release","Descending flaccid paralysis, ptosis, dilated pupils, dry mouth"],["Myasthenia gravis","Antibodies to postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors","Fewer working receptors, smaller end-plate potentials","Ptosis and diplopia; weakness worsens with repeated use; improves with acetylcholinesterase inhibitor"],["Lambert-Eaton syndrome","Antibodies to presynaptic P/Q voltage-gated Ca2+ channels","Less Ca2+ entry, less acetylcholine release","Proximal weakness that improves with repeated effort, low reflexes, dry mouth; small cell lung cancer"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Resting potential follows potassium","text":"Whatever ion the membrane is most permeable to pulls the voltage toward its own equilibrium potential. At rest that is K+, so any change in serum K+ moves the resting potential."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Use it and lose it, or use it and gain it","text":"Myasthenia gravis weakens with repeated effort as acetylcholine stores run down. Lambert-Eaton improves with repeated effort because Ca2+ builds up in the terminal."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Thick and wrapped is fast","text":"Two things speed conduction: a wider axon (less internal resistance) and myelin (current jumps node to node). Remove either and velocity drops."}],"highYield":["Resting potential sits near E for K+ because K+ leak channels dominate at rest.","Hyperkalemia depolarizes the resting membrane and, if sustained, inactivates Na+ channels and lowers excitability.","Absolute refractory period comes from Na+ channel inactivation; relative refractory period from lingering K+ efflux.","The axon hillock fires first because it has the highest density of voltage-gated Na+ channels.","Demyelination slows conduction or blocks it outright; heat makes demyelinated conduction worse.","Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and blocks acetylcholine release.","Child with brief staring spells and 3 Hz spike-and-wave on EEG: absence seizures.","Young man with flaccid weakness after a carbohydrate-heavy meal and low K+: hypokalemic periodic paralysis."],"pitfalls":["A mild K+ rise makes cells easier to fire, but a sustained rise keeps Na+ channels inactivated and causes weakness.","Absence seizures have no postictal confusion; a staring spell followed by confusion suggests a focal seizure with impaired awareness.","Myasthenia gravis is a postsynaptic problem; Lambert-Eaton and botulism are presynaptic problems.","Hypokalemic periodic paralysis shows low K+ only during attacks; a normal K+ between attacks does not rule it out."],"dumps":[{"t":"Action potential","p":"Draw a neuron action potential with voltage on the y-axis and time on the x-axis. Label resting potential, threshold, upstroke, peak, repolarization, afterhyperpolarization, and the absolute and relative refractory periods. Write which channel is responsible for each phase.","l":["Resting potential about -70 mV, set mainly by K+ leak channels","Threshold about -55 mV","Upstroke from voltage-gated Na+ channel opening and Na+ influx","Peak approaches E for Na+ (about +60 mV) but does not reach it","Repolarization from Na+ channel inactivation plus voltage-gated K+ efflux","Afterhyperpolarization from K+ channels closing slowly, toward E for K+","Absolute refractory period while Na+ channels are inactivated","Relative refractory period needs a stronger stimulus"]},{"t":"The neuromuscular junction","p":"Draw a motor nerve terminal, the synaptic cleft, and the muscle end plate. Label each step of transmission, then mark where botulinum toxin, myasthenia gravis antibodies, and Lambert-Eaton antibodies act.","l":["Action potential opens presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels","Ca2+ entry triggers SNARE-mediated vesicle fusion","Acetylcholine binds postsynaptic nicotinic receptors","End-plate potential triggers the muscle action potential","Acetylcholinesterase splits acetylcholine into acetate and choline","Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins (presynaptic)","Lambert-Eaton antibodies block presynaptic Ca2+ channels","Myasthenia gravis antibodies block postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation of Skeletal Muscle: Neuromuscular Transmission and Excitation-Contraction Coupling"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles, section on neuromuscular junction disorders"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-2","NE-15","NE-18","NE-20-B4","NE-20-B5","NE-20-G2"]},"NE-14":{"id":"NE-14","summary":"The brain runs almost entirely on glucose and needs several vitamins and cofactors to make energy, neurotransmitters, and myelin. This unit links each nutrient or toxin to the enzyme it affects and to the neurologic picture you will see on the exam: Wernicke encephalopathy, subacute combined degeneration, pellagra, hepatic encephalopathy, the sphingolipidoses, and lead poisoning.","notes":[{"h":"Brain fuel","points":["Glucose crosses the blood-brain barrier through GLUT1 on endothelial cells and enters neurons through GLUT3; neither needs insulin.","The brain stores almost no glycogen and fatty acids are not a meaningful brain fuel, so it needs a steady glucose supply.","After several days of fasting the liver makes ketone bodies (acetoacetate, beta-hydroxybutyrate), and the brain shifts to using them, sparing glucose and muscle protein.","Hypoglycemia first causes adrenergic symptoms (sweating, tremor, palpitations, hunger), then neuroglycopenic symptoms (confusion, behavior change, blurred vision, seizures, coma) as glucose falls further."]},{"h":"Thiamine (vitamin B1)","points":["Thiamine pyrophosphate is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase, and transketolase.","Without it, glucose cannot be fully oxidized, so ATP falls in the brain and heart.","Wernicke encephalopathy: confusion, ophthalmoplegia or nystagmus, and ataxia; lesions in the mammillary bodies, medial thalamus, and periaqueductal gray.","Korsakoff syndrome adds lasting memory loss with confabulation.","Dry beriberi is a symmetric peripheral neuropathy with muscle wasting; wet beriberi is high-output heart failure.","Giving glucose before thiamine pushes more flux through thiamine-dependent enzymes, uses up the last thiamine, and can trigger or worsen Wernicke encephalopathy."]},{"h":"Vitamin B12 vs folate","points":["Methionine synthase (needs B12, uses methyl-THF from folate) converts homocysteine to methionine, so both deficiencies raise homocysteine.","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (needs B12 only) converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, so only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid.","Both cause megaloblastic anemia from impaired DNA synthesis.","B12 deficiency damages myelin: subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns (lost vibration and position sense) and lateral corticospinal tracts (spastic weakness, Babinski sign), plus peripheral neuropathy and cognitive decline.","Folate deficiency spares the nervous system; giving folate alone can fix the anemia of B12 deficiency while nerve damage keeps progressing."]},{"h":"Ammonia and the brain","points":["The brain has no complete urea cycle; astrocytes clear ammonia with glutamine synthetase (glutamate + NH3 to glutamine).","Glutamine builds up inside astrocytes and pulls in water, so astrocytes swell and cerebral edema follows.","Using up glutamate and alpha-ketoglutarate disturbs neurotransmission and slows the TCA cycle.","Alzheimer type II astrocytes (enlarged, pale nuclei in gray matter) are the hallmark of hyperammonemia, seen in liver failure and Wilson disease.","Causes: liver failure, portosystemic shunting, and urea cycle defects such as ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency. Signs: asterixis, confusion, somnolence, coma."]},{"h":"Myelin lipids and lysosomal storage","points":["Myelin is about 70 to 80 percent lipid by dry weight, rich in cholesterol, galactocerebroside, sulfatide, and sphingomyelin.","Sphingolipids are broken down step by step in lysosomes; a missing enzyme lets its substrate build up in neurons or myelin.","Sphingolipidoses with neuronal storage (Tay-Sachs, Niemann-Pick type A) cause progressive loss of milestones in infancy.","Leukodystrophies destroy myelin: Krabbe disease and metachromatic leukodystrophy are lysosomal; X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy is a peroxisomal defect with very long chain fatty acid buildup."]},{"h":"Lead poisoning","points":["Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase (ALA builds up) and ferrochelatase (protoporphyrin builds up, measured as zinc protoporphyrin).","Heme synthesis fails, giving a microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling of red cells.","Children (old paint chips and dust) get encephalopathy with cerebral edema, learning problems, and developmental delay.","Adults (batteries, smelting, ammunition) get a peripheral motor neuropathy with wrist drop and foot drop, plus abdominal colic and nephropathy."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Vitamins and the nervous system","cols":["Vitamin","Active form and key enzymes","Neurologic findings when deficient","Clues"],"rows":[["B1 (thiamine)","Thiamine pyrophosphate: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase","Wernicke encephalopathy, Korsakoff syndrome, dry beriberi","Alcohol use disorder, malnutrition; worse if glucose given first"],["B3 (niacin)","NAD+ and NADP+ for redox reactions","Pellagra: dementia, with dermatitis and diarrhea","Corn-based diet, Hartnup disease, carcinoid syndrome, isoniazid"],["B6 (pyridoxine)","Pyridoxal phosphate: glutamate decarboxylase, aromatic amino acid decarboxylase, transaminases, ALA synthase","Seizures (low GABA), peripheral neuropathy","Isoniazid; sideroblastic anemia"],["B12 (cobalamin)","Methionine synthase, methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","Subacute combined degeneration, neuropathy, dementia","High methylmalonic acid and homocysteine, megaloblastic anemia"],["Folate","Tetrahydrofolate carries one-carbon units for methionine and DNA synthesis","No myelin damage in adults","High homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid"],["E (tocopherol)","Antioxidant protecting membrane lipids from peroxidation","Spinocerebellar ataxia, dorsal column loss, neuropathy","Fat malabsorption; hemolytic anemia; normal methylmalonic acid"]]},{"title":"Lysosomal and peroxisomal disorders that damage the nervous system","cols":["Disease","Deficient enzyme or protein","Substance that builds up","Inheritance","Key findings"],"rows":[["Tay-Sachs disease","Hexosaminidase A","GM2 ganglioside","Autosomal recessive","Neurodegeneration, cherry-red macula, no hepatosplenomegaly"],["Niemann-Pick disease type A","Sphingomyelinase","Sphingomyelin","Autosomal recessive","Neurodegeneration, cherry-red macula, hepatosplenomegaly, foam cells"],["Krabbe disease","Galactocerebrosidase","Galactocerebroside and psychosine","Autosomal recessive","Leukodystrophy, peripheral neuropathy, optic atrophy, globoid cells"],["Metachromatic leukodystrophy","Arylsulfatase A","Sulfatide (cerebroside sulfate)","Autosomal recessive","Central and peripheral demyelination, ataxia, dementia"],["Fabry disease","Alpha-galactosidase A","Ceramide trihexoside","X-linked recessive","Painful peripheral neuropathy, angiokeratomas, kidney and heart disease"],["X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy","ABCD1 peroxisomal transporter","Very long chain fatty acids","X-linked recessive","Demyelination in boys, adrenal insufficiency"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Think ATP","text":"Thiamine pyrophosphate enzymes: Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, Transketolase, Pyruvate dehydrogenase."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 3 Ds of pellagra","text":"Dermatitis, Diarrhea, Dementia (and death if untreated) from niacin deficiency."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"LEAD","text":"Lead lines on gums and bones, Encephalopathy and Erythrocyte basophilic stippling, Abdominal colic and sideroblastic Anemia, Drops (wrist and foot)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"MMA decides B12 vs folate","text":"Both raise homocysteine. Only B12 runs methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, so a high methylmalonic acid means B12 and means the nerves are at risk."}],"highYield":["GLUT1 at the blood-brain barrier and GLUT3 on neurons are insulin independent.","In prolonged fasting the brain switches to ketone bodies.","Malnourished patient with confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia: give thiamine before glucose.","High methylmalonic acid plus dorsal column and corticospinal signs: B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration).","Isoniazid causes B6 deficiency, which can cause seizures and neuropathy.","Hyperammonemia swells astrocytes through glutamine buildup; look for Alzheimer type II astrocytes.","Cherry-red macula with no hepatosplenomegaly: Tay-Sachs; with hepatosplenomegaly: Niemann-Pick.","Adult battery worker with wrist drop, colic, and basophilic stippling: lead poisoning."],"pitfalls":["Vitamin E deficiency mimics B12 deficiency (dorsal columns, ataxia) but has normal methylmalonic acid and no megaloblastic anemia.","Folate fixes the anemia of B12 deficiency but not the nerve damage, so check B12 before giving folate.","Alzheimer type II astrocytes are a sign of hyperammonemia and have nothing to do with Alzheimer disease.","Adrenoleukodystrophy is peroxisomal; Krabbe and metachromatic leukodystrophy are lysosomal."],"dumps":[{"t":"B12 and folate pathways","p":"Draw the methionine synthase reaction and the methylmalonyl-CoA mutase reaction. Mark which vitamins each needs, then write which lab values rise in B12 deficiency and in folate deficiency and which one damages myelin.","l":["Methionine synthase converts homocysteine to methionine","It needs B12 and takes a methyl group from methyl-THF (folate)","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA","Mutase needs B12 only","B12 deficiency: high homocysteine and high methylmalonic acid","Folate deficiency: high homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid","Both cause megaloblastic anemia","Only B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration"]},{"t":"Nutrient and toxin table","p":"Make a table with four columns (nutrient or toxin, enzyme affected, neurologic result, typical patient) for thiamine, B6, niacin, vitamin E, ammonia, and lead.","l":["Thiamine: PDH, alpha-KGDH, transketolase; Wernicke-Korsakoff and dry beriberi; alcohol use disorder","B6: glutamate decarboxylase; seizures and neuropathy; isoniazid","Niacin: NAD+; pellagra with dementia; corn diet, Hartnup, carcinoid","Vitamin E: antioxidant; spinocerebellar ataxia, dorsal column loss; fat malabsorption","Ammonia: astrocyte glutamine synthetase; astrocyte swelling, asterixis; liver failure, urea cycle defects","Lead: ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase; child encephalopathy, adult wrist and foot drop"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on vitamins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on sphingolipid metabolism and the sphingolipidoses"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, section on metabolic and toxic disorders"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-15","NE-20-D1","NE-20-D3","NE-20-D4","NE-20-E2","NE-20-E4"]},"NE-15":{"id":"NE-15","summary":"Neurotransmitters are built from amino acids and simple precursors by a few key enzymes, then broken down into products you can measure in urine. Exam questions ask you to trace each pathway, name the rate-limiting enzyme or cofactor, link breakdown products to tumors, and match each transmitter to its source nucleus and the diseases that change it.","notes":[{"h":"Catecholamine synthesis","points":["Phenylalanine to tyrosine by phenylalanine hydroxylase (needs BH4).","Tyrosine to L-DOPA by tyrosine hydroxylase (needs BH4); this is the rate-limiting step.","L-DOPA to dopamine by aromatic L-amino acid (DOPA) decarboxylase, which needs pyridoxal phosphate (B6).","Dopamine to norepinephrine by dopamine beta-hydroxylase inside vesicles; it needs vitamin C (and copper).","Norepinephrine to epinephrine by PNMT, which uses SAM as the methyl donor; cortisol from the adrenal cortex induces PNMT in the adrenal medulla."]},{"h":"Catecholamine breakdown","points":["Most catecholamine action ends by reuptake into the nerve terminal; the rest is broken down by MAO (mitochondria) and COMT (cytosol, mostly liver).","Dopamine breaks down to homovanillic acid (HVA).","Norepinephrine breaks down to normetanephrine and vanillylmandelic acid (VMA); epinephrine to metanephrine and VMA.","Neuroblastoma raises urinary HVA and VMA; pheochromocytoma raises plasma and urine metanephrines."]},{"h":"Serotonin and melatonin","points":["Tryptophan to 5-hydroxytryptophan by tryptophan hydroxylase (needs BH4), the rate-limiting step.","5-hydroxytryptophan to serotonin by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (B6).","Brain serotonin comes from the raphe nuclei of the brainstem.","In the pineal gland serotonin is converted to melatonin; darkness increases melatonin release through a pathway from the suprachiasmatic nucleus.","MAO breaks serotonin down to 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA), which rises in urine in carcinoid syndrome."]},{"h":"Glutamate, GABA, and glycine","points":["Glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter: AMPA receptors pass Na+ for fast EPSPs; NMDA receptors pass Ca2+ once depolarization removes their Mg2+ block.","GABA is made from glutamate by glutamate decarboxylase, which needs pyridoxal phosphate (B6).","GABA is the main inhibitory transmitter in the brain (GABA-A is a Cl- channel; GABA-B is G protein coupled and opens K+ channels).","Glycine is the main inhibitory transmitter in the spinal cord and brainstem and opens Cl- channels.","Excitotoxicity in ischemia: ATP fails, neurons depolarize, and glutamate reuptake stops, so glutamate floods the synapse.","Excess NMDA activation lets Ca2+ pour in, which activates proteases, phospholipases, and free radical production and kills the neuron."]},{"h":"Acetylcholine","points":["Choline acetyltransferase joins acetyl-CoA and choline to make acetylcholine in the nerve terminal.","Acetylcholinesterase in the synaptic cleft splits it into acetate and choline; choline is recycled into the terminal.","Cholinergic neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert project to the cortex and are lost early in Alzheimer disease, lowering cortical acetylcholine."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Source nuclei and disease changes","cols":["Transmitter","Main source nucleus","Location","Disease changes"],"rows":[["Dopamine","Substantia nigra pars compacta; ventral tegmental area","Midbrain","Low in Parkinson disease; high in Huntington disease and schizophrenia"],["Norepinephrine","Locus coeruleus","Pons","High in anxiety; low in depression"],["Serotonin","Raphe nuclei","Brainstem midline","Low in depression and anxiety"],["Acetylcholine","Nucleus basalis of Meynert","Basal forebrain","Low in Alzheimer disease and Huntington disease; high relative to dopamine in Parkinson disease"],["GABA","Nucleus accumbens; striatal medium spiny neurons","Basal ganglia","Low in anxiety and Huntington disease"]]},{"title":"Synthesis and breakdown at a glance","cols":["Transmitter","Precursor","Rate-limiting or key enzyme","Cofactor","Main breakdown product"],"rows":[["Dopamine","Tyrosine","Tyrosine hydroxylase","BH4, then B6","HVA"],["Norepinephrine","Dopamine","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Vitamin C, copper","VMA, normetanephrine"],["Epinephrine","Norepinephrine","PNMT (induced by cortisol)","SAM","VMA, metanephrine"],["Serotonin","Tryptophan","Tryptophan hydroxylase","BH4, then B6","5-HIAA"],["GABA","Glutamate","Glutamate decarboxylase","B6","Succinate (via GABA transaminase)"],["Acetylcholine","Acetyl-CoA and choline","Choline acetyltransferase","None special","Acetate and choline"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Walk the catecholamine ladder","text":"Phenylalanine, tyrosine, DOPA, dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine. Each rung adds one change: a hydroxyl, a hydroxyl, remove CO2, a hydroxyl, then a methyl."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Name the breakdown by the parent","text":"HVA comes from dopamine. VMA and metanephrines come from norepinephrine and epinephrine. 5-HIAA comes from serotonin."},{"kind":"trick","label":"B6 makes the signals","text":"Pyridoxal phosphate runs the decarboxylases that make GABA, dopamine, and serotonin. Low B6 means low GABA, which is why deficiency causes seizures."}],"highYield":["Tyrosine hydroxylase is rate limiting for catecholamines; tryptophan hydroxylase for serotonin.","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase needs vitamin C.","Cortisol induces PNMT, so the adrenal medulla makes mostly epinephrine.","Child with an abdominal mass and high urinary HVA and VMA: neuroblastoma.","High urinary 5-HIAA: carcinoid syndrome.","Glutamate excitotoxicity through NMDA receptors and Ca2+ influx kills neurons after ischemia.","Parkinson disease: low dopamine from substantia nigra loss. Huntington disease: low GABA and acetylcholine, high dopamine.","Alzheimer disease: loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert."],"pitfalls":["Tyrosine hydroxylase (catecholamines) and tryptophan hydroxylase (serotonin) are both BH4 dependent; do not mix up their substrates.","GABA is made from glutamate, so the brain's main inhibitory transmitter comes from its main excitatory one.","Glycine is the main inhibitory transmitter in the spinal cord; GABA dominates in the brain.","Neuroblastoma is screened with HVA and VMA; pheochromocytoma is screened with metanephrines."],"dumps":[{"t":"Catecholamine pathway","p":"Starting from phenylalanine, draw the pathway to epinephrine. Label each enzyme and cofactor, mark the rate-limiting step, then draw the breakdown arrows to HVA and VMA.","l":["Phenylalanine hydroxylase makes tyrosine (BH4)","Tyrosine hydroxylase makes L-DOPA, rate limiting (BH4)","DOPA decarboxylase makes dopamine (B6)","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase makes norepinephrine (vitamin C)","PNMT makes epinephrine (SAM, induced by cortisol)","MAO and COMT break down catecholamines","Dopamine to HVA; norepinephrine and epinephrine to VMA and metanephrines","High urinary HVA and VMA in neuroblastoma"]},{"t":"Transmitters and their nuclei","p":"Draw a sagittal sketch of the brainstem and basal forebrain. Place the substantia nigra, ventral tegmental area, locus coeruleus, raphe nuclei, and nucleus basalis of Meynert, label each with its transmitter, and write one disease linked to each.","l":["Substantia nigra (midbrain): dopamine; lost in Parkinson disease","Ventral tegmental area (midbrain): dopamine to limbic and frontal areas","Locus coeruleus (pons): norepinephrine","Raphe nuclei (brainstem midline): serotonin; low in depression","Nucleus basalis of Meynert (basal forebrain): acetylcholine; lost in Alzheimer disease","Huntington disease: loss of striatal GABA and acetylcholine neurons"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on amino acids as precursors of specialized products (catecholamines, serotonin)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Organization of the Nervous System, Basic Functions of Synapses, and Neurotransmitters"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, section on degenerative diseases"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-13","NE-14","NE-20-B1","NE-20-B3","NE-20-F3"]},"NE-16":{"id":"NE-16","summary":"Many neurologic diseases come from a specific kind of genetic error: repeat expansions, extra chromosomes, lost tumor suppressor genes, or amplified oncogenes. Exam questions give you a family history or a set of features and ask for the gene, the inheritance pattern, or the molecular mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"Trinucleotide repeat expansions","points":["A run of three bases grows past a threshold and disrupts the gene or its product.","Anticipation: repeats grow from one generation to the next, so onset comes earlier and disease is more severe.","Huntington repeats expand mainly through the father; fragile X expands through the mother, and congenital myotonic dystrophy comes from the mother.","Fragile X full mutation (more than about 200 CGG repeats) hypermethylates FMR1 and silences it.","Friedreich ataxia is autosomal recessive, so anticipation is not a feature; the GAA expansion lowers frataxin, a mitochondrial iron protein."]},{"h":"Down syndrome","points":["About 95 percent: meiotic nondisjunction, usually in maternal meiosis I; risk rises with maternal age.","About 4 percent: Robertsonian translocation (often 14;21); can be inherited from a parent with a balanced translocation and is not tied to maternal age.","About 1 percent: mosaicism from mitotic nondisjunction after fertilization; features are often milder.","The APP gene sits on chromosome 21, so an extra copy makes more amyloid-beta; Alzheimer plaques are present by about age 40 and dementia often follows."]},{"h":"Neurocutaneous syndromes","points":["NF1, NF2, tuberous sclerosis, and von Hippel-Lindau are autosomal dominant tumor suppressor disorders: one bad copy is inherited, and a tumor forms when the second copy is lost.","Neurofibromin is a Ras GTPase-activating protein; losing it leaves Ras switched on.","Hamartin (TSC1) and tuberin (TSC2) form a complex that normally restrains mTOR; losing either lets mTOR drive cell growth.","VHL protein normally targets HIF for breakdown; losing it raises HIF, which drives VEGF and erythropoietin.","Sturge-Weber syndrome is sporadic: a somatic activating GNAQ mutation causes a port-wine stain in the CN V1 area, ipsilateral leptomeningeal angioma, seizures, and glaucoma."]},{"h":"Spinal muscular atrophy","points":["Autosomal recessive loss (usually deletion) of SMN1 on chromosome 5q; SMN2 copy number changes severity.","Anterior horn motor neurons degenerate, giving pure lower motor neuron signs.","Severe infantile form (type 1, Werdnig-Hoffmann): floppy infant, tongue fasciculations, areflexia, and respiratory failure; sensation and intellect are normal."]},{"h":"Driver genes in nervous system tumors","points":["Retinoblastoma: both copies of RB1 (chromosome 13q14) are lost (two-hit); Rb normally holds cells in G1 by binding E2F.","Hereditary retinoblastoma (germline first hit) is often bilateral and early, with later risk of osteosarcoma; sporadic cases are usually unilateral.","Oligodendroglioma is defined by an IDH1 or IDH2 mutation plus 1p/19q codeletion, which predicts better response to treatment.","Neuroblastoma with MYCN (N-myc) amplification behaves aggressively and has a poor prognosis.","Oncogenes need only one activated copy (gain of function); tumor suppressors need both copies lost."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Trinucleotide repeat disorders","cols":["Disease","Repeat","Gene","Inheritance","Anticipation","Key features"],"rows":[["Huntington disease","CAG","HTT (chromosome 4)","Autosomal dominant","Yes, mainly through the father","Chorea, dementia, psychiatric change, caudate atrophy"],["Fragile X syndrome","CGG","FMR1 (X chromosome), silenced by hypermethylation","X-linked dominant","Yes, expands through the mother","Intellectual disability, long face, large jaw and ears, macroorchidism"],["Myotonic dystrophy type 1","CTG","DMPK (chromosome 19)","Autosomal dominant","Yes, congenital form through the mother","Myotonia, distal weakness, cataracts, frontal balding, testicular atrophy, heart block"],["Friedreich ataxia","GAA","FXN, frataxin (chromosome 9)","Autosomal recessive","No","Progressive ataxia, lost reflexes and position sense, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, diabetes, scoliosis"]]},{"title":"Neurocutaneous syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Gene and protein","Chromosome","Mechanism","Key nervous system and other findings"],"rows":[["NF1","NF1, neurofibromin","17","Tumor suppressor loss; Ras stays active","Neurofibromas, cafe-au-lait spots, Lisch nodules, axillary freckling, optic glioma, pheochromocytoma"],["NF2","NF2, merlin","22","Tumor suppressor loss","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, ependymomas, juvenile cataracts"],["Tuberous sclerosis","TSC1 hamartin; TSC2 tuberin","9 (TSC1); 16 (TSC2)","Tumor suppressor loss; mTOR active","Cortical tubers, subependymal nodules, seizures, ash-leaf spots, facial angiofibromas, cardiac rhabdomyoma, renal angiomyolipoma"],["von Hippel-Lindau","VHL","3","Tumor suppressor loss; HIF stays high","Hemangioblastomas of cerebellum, retina, and spinal cord; bilateral renal cell carcinoma; pheochromocytoma"],["Sturge-Weber","GNAQ (activating)","9 (somatic mutation, not inherited)","Somatic mosaic gain of function in developing vessels","Port-wine stain in CN V1 area, leptomeningeal angioma, seizures, glaucoma"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"X-Girlfriend's First Aid Helped Ace My Test","text":"Fragile X: CGG. Friedreich ataxia: GAA. Huntington disease: CAG. Myotonic dystrophy: CTG."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Huntington CAG: Caudate has less ACh and GABA","text":"The CAG repeat in Huntington disease destroys striatal neurons that make acetylcholine and GABA."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the chromosome letters","text":"NF1 has 17 letters in neurofibromatosis (count them); NF2 is on 22, the number with two 2s; VHL has 3 letters and sits on chromosome 3."}],"highYield":["Anticipation is seen in Huntington disease, fragile X, and myotonic dystrophy, but not in autosomal recessive Friedreich ataxia.","Fragile X silences FMR1 by hypermethylation of an expanded CGG repeat.","Down syndrome by Robertsonian translocation can recur in a family regardless of maternal age.","Extra APP on chromosome 21 leads to early-onset Alzheimer disease in Down syndrome.","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas: NF2 (merlin, chromosome 22).","Tuberous sclerosis: loss of hamartin or tuberin releases mTOR.","Floppy infant with tongue fasciculations and absent reflexes: spinal muscular atrophy (SMN1).","MYCN amplification in neuroblastoma predicts aggressive disease."],"pitfalls":["Sturge-Weber is sporadic and caused by an activating mutation, unlike the inherited tumor suppressor syndromes.","NF1, NF2, tuberous sclerosis, and von Hippel-Lindau are autosomal dominant (many cases are new mutations), yet each tumor still needs a second hit at the cellular level.","Spinal muscular atrophy causes lower motor neuron signs only; infant botulism also causes a floppy baby but adds constipation, ptosis, and sluggish pupils.","Huntington disease anticipation comes mainly from the father; myotonic dystrophy congenital disease comes from the mother."],"dumps":[{"t":"Repeat disorders table","p":"Build a table for Huntington disease, fragile X syndrome, myotonic dystrophy, and Friedreich ataxia with columns for repeat, gene, inheritance, anticipation, and key features.","l":["Huntington: CAG, HTT, autosomal dominant, anticipation","Fragile X: CGG, FMR1 hypermethylation, X-linked, anticipation","Myotonic dystrophy: CTG, DMPK, autosomal dominant, anticipation","Friedreich ataxia: GAA, frataxin, autosomal recessive, no anticipation","Huntington: chorea, dementia, caudate atrophy","Fragile X: intellectual disability, macroorchidism, long face","Myotonic dystrophy: myotonia, cataracts, balding, heart block","Friedreich: ataxia plus hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"]},{"t":"Two-hit and growth signals","p":"Draw a cell with the Ras, mTOR, HIF, and Rb pathways as simple boxes. Mark where neurofibromin, hamartin and tuberin, VHL protein, and Rb act, and write the disease caused when each is lost.","l":["Neurofibromin turns off Ras; loss causes NF1","Hamartin and tuberin restrain mTOR; loss causes tuberous sclerosis","VHL protein targets HIF for breakdown; loss causes von Hippel-Lindau","Rb binds E2F and holds cells in G1; loss causes retinoblastoma","Tumor suppressors need two hits; the first may be inherited","MYCN is an oncogene; amplification of one gene is enough","Oligodendroglioma: IDH mutation plus 1p/19q codeletion"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, sections on tumors and familial tumor syndromes"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Section on chromosomal abnormalities"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-15","NE-20-B3","NE-20-E1","NE-20-F3","NE-20-F4","NE-20-F6"]},"NE-17":{"id":"NE-17","summary":"The hypothalamus controls the pituitary in two ways: releasing hormones carried by portal blood to the anterior lobe, and nerve axons that end in the posterior lobe. Exam questions ask you to predict what happens when a tumor, head injury, or surgery damages the chiasm, stalk, or posterior pituitary, and how stress and thyroid hormone change nervous system function.","notes":[{"h":"Hypothalamus to anterior pituitary","points":["Hypothalamic neurons release hormones into capillaries in the median eminence, which lies outside the blood-brain barrier.","Hypophyseal portal veins carry these hormones down the stalk to a second capillary bed in the anterior pituitary.","Releasing hormones: TRH, CRH, GnRH (must be pulsatile), and GHRH. Inhibiting hormones: somatostatin (GH, TSH) and dopamine (prolactin).","Dopamine from the arcuate nucleus tonically inhibits prolactin through D2 receptors, so prolactin is the one hormone that rises when the stalk is cut."]},{"h":"Posterior pituitary","points":["ADH is made mainly in the supraoptic nucleus and oxytocin mainly in the paraventricular nucleus.","Both travel down axons of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract with carrier proteins (neurophysins) and are released into blood in the posterior lobe.","ADH acts on V2 receptors in the collecting duct to insert aquaporin-2 and reabsorb water; release rises with high plasma osmolality or low blood volume.","Central diabetes insipidus (head trauma, pituitary surgery, craniopharyngioma): too little ADH, so large volumes of dilute urine with high serum sodium; urine concentrates after desmopressin.","SIADH (head trauma, meningitis, subarachnoid hemorrhage, CNS tumors, small cell lung cancer): too much ADH, so euvolemic hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine.","Correcting chronic hyponatremia too fast can cause osmotic demyelination syndrome in the pons."]},{"h":"Sellar masses","points":["The optic chiasm sits just above the pituitary; crossing nasal retinal fibers carry the temporal visual fields.","Pressing on the middle of the chiasm causes bitemporal hemianopia; a pituitary adenoma pushing up from below affects the upper temporal fields first.","Pituitary adenoma is the most common sellar mass in adults; prolactinoma is the most common hormone-making type.","Craniopharyngioma arises from Rathke pouch remnants, is often calcified and cystic, and is the most common sellar tumor in children (a second peak occurs in middle age); it causes visual loss, growth failure, and diabetes insipidus.","Stalk effect: a nonfunctioning mass blocks dopamine flow, so prolactin rises mildly while other pituitary hormones fall.","Lateral spread into the cavernous sinus can affect CN III, IV, V1, V2, and VI."]},{"h":"Stress response","points":["Fast arm: the hypothalamus drives sympathetic outflow; preganglionic fibers go straight to adrenal medulla chromaffin cells, which release mostly epinephrine within seconds.","Slow arm (HPA axis): paraventricular nucleus releases CRH, the anterior pituitary releases ACTH, and the adrenal cortex releases cortisol over minutes to hours.","Cortisol raises blood glucose, supports vascular tone by keeping catecholamine responses working, suppresses inflammation, and feeds back to inhibit CRH and ACTH.","Cortisol flows from the cortex into the medulla and induces PNMT, linking the two arms."]},{"h":"Thyroid hormone and the nervous system","points":["Thyroid hormone is needed for neuron growth, synapse formation, and myelination, most critically from late fetal life through the first years.","Congenital hypothyroidism causes intellectual disability and short stature if untreated; newborn screening allows early treatment that prevents this.","Hypothyroidism slows the relaxation phase of deep tendon reflexes and causes cognitive slowing and depression.","Hyperthyroidism causes fine tremor, hyperreflexia, anxiety, and insomnia, partly from increased beta-adrenergic sensitivity."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypothalamic control of the anterior pituitary","cols":["Hypothalamic hormone","Effect on anterior pituitary","Notes"],"rows":[["TRH","Increases TSH and prolactin","Primary hypothyroidism can raise prolactin through high TRH"],["CRH","Increases ACTH","Drives the HPA stress response"],["GnRH","Increases FSH and LH","Must be pulsatile; continuous GnRH suppresses FSH and LH"],["GHRH","Increases GH","GH then drives IGF-1 from the liver"],["Somatostatin","Decreases GH and TSH","Also made in pancreatic delta cells"],["Dopamine","Decreases prolactin","Main control of prolactin; dopamine blockers raise prolactin"]]},{"title":"Central diabetes insipidus vs SIADH","cols":["Feature","Central diabetes insipidus","SIADH"],"rows":[["ADH","Too little","Too much"],["Urine","Large volume, dilute","Small volume, inappropriately concentrated"],["Serum sodium (reference 135 to 145 mEq/L)","High or high-normal","Low"],["Volume status","Can become dehydrated","Euvolemic"],["CNS causes","Head trauma, pituitary surgery, craniopharyngioma","Head trauma, meningitis, subarachnoid hemorrhage, CNS tumors"],["Response to desmopressin","Urine concentrates","Not used; restrict water"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cut the stalk, prolactin goes up","text":"Every anterior pituitary hormone needs a hypothalamic push except prolactin, which needs a dopamine brake. Remove the hypothalamus and only prolactin rises."},{"kind":"trick","label":"SON makes water stay","text":"Supraoptic nucleus makes ADH, which keeps water in. Paraventricular nucleus makes oxytocin (and CRH)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Below pushes up, above pushes down","text":"A pituitary adenoma presses the chiasm from below and hits upper temporal fields first. A craniopharyngioma often presses from above and can hit lower temporal fields first."}],"highYield":["Dopamine is the main inhibitor of prolactin; stalk compression causes mild hyperprolactinemia.","Pituitary adenoma compressing the chiasm causes bitemporal hemianopia.","Child with a calcified suprasellar mass, visual loss, and growth failure: craniopharyngioma.","Polyuria with dilute urine and high sodium after head trauma or pituitary surgery: central diabetes insipidus.","Hyponatremia with concentrated urine after subarachnoid hemorrhage or meningitis: SIADH.","ADH comes mainly from the supraoptic nucleus; oxytocin mainly from the paraventricular nucleus.","Cortisol induces PNMT, so the adrenal medulla makes mostly epinephrine.","Delayed relaxation of the ankle reflex is a classic sign of hypothyroidism."],"pitfalls":["A mildly raised prolactin with a large pituitary mass usually means stalk compression, while a very high prolactin points to a prolactinoma.","ADH and oxytocin are made in the hypothalamus; the posterior pituitary only stores and releases them.","Central diabetes insipidus responds to desmopressin; nephrogenic diabetes insipidus does not.","SIADH patients look euvolemic with a high urine sodium, which separates them from dehydrated patients with low sodium."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypothalamus and pituitary","p":"Draw the hypothalamus, pituitary stalk, anterior and posterior pituitary, and the optic chiasm above them. Show the portal system and the axon tract, label the hormones each lobe receives, and mark where a tumor causes bitemporal hemianopia.","l":["Hypothalamic hormones enter capillaries in the median eminence","Portal veins carry them down the stalk to the anterior pituitary","TRH, CRH, GnRH, GHRH stimulate; somatostatin and dopamine inhibit","Supraoptic and paraventricular axons run down the stalk to the posterior pituitary","ADH and oxytocin are released into blood from the posterior lobe","The optic chiasm sits above the sella","Compression of crossing nasal fibers causes bitemporal hemianopia","Stalk compression blocks dopamine and raises prolactin"]},{"t":"The stress response","p":"Draw the two arms of the stress response starting at the hypothalamus: the sympathetic adrenal medulla arm and the HPA axis. Label each hormone, its target, how fast it acts, and the feedback loop.","l":["Hypothalamus activates sympathetic preganglionic neurons","Preganglionic fibers synapse directly on adrenal medulla chromaffin cells","Adrenal medulla releases mostly epinephrine within seconds","Paraventricular nucleus releases CRH","Anterior pituitary releases ACTH","Adrenal cortex releases cortisol over minutes to hours","Cortisol feeds back to inhibit CRH and ACTH","Cortisol induces PNMT in the adrenal medulla"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System, section on the pituitary gland"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-9","NE-12","NE-15","NE-20-H1"]},"NE-18":{"id":"NE-18","summary":"The nervous system is partly shielded from immune attack by the blood-brain barrier, yet many neurologic diseases are immune driven. This unit covers how meningitis inflames the brain, how autoimmunity strips myelin or blocks the neuromuscular junction, how tumors trigger paraneoplastic syndromes, and how vaccines against meningitis and polio build protection.","notes":[{"h":"Blood-brain barrier and microglia","points":["The barrier is built from endothelial tight junctions, a basement membrane, pericytes, and astrocyte foot processes.","It limits entry of immune cells, antibodies, complement, and many drugs, so normal CSF has very few cells and little complement.","Microglia are the resident macrophages of the CNS; they come from yolk sac (mesodermal) precursors, phagocytose debris, present antigen, and release cytokines.","In bacterial meningitis, microglia and macrophages sense bacterial products through toll-like receptors and release IL-1 and TNF-alpha.","These cytokines activate endothelium, recruit neutrophils, and open the barrier, causing vasogenic edema, raised intracranial pressure, and risk of herniation.","Typical CSF: many neutrophils, high protein, and low glucose."]},{"h":"Multiple sclerosis","points":["Autoreactive CD4 T cells (Th1 making IFN-gamma, Th17 making IL-17) cross the barrier and attack CNS myelin; macrophages and microglia strip the myelin.","Strongest genetic link: HLA-DR2. It is most common in women aged 20 to 40.","CSF shows oligoclonal IgG bands (not present in serum) and a raised IgG index.","MRI shows white matter plaques around the lateral ventricles (periventricular).","Common findings: optic neuritis, internuclear ophthalmoplegia, sensory loss, weakness, symptoms that worsen with heat."]},{"h":"Guillain-Barré syndrome","points":["Weeks after Campylobacter jejuni diarrhea, antibodies made against bacterial lipooligosaccharide cross-react with peripheral nerve gangliosides (molecular mimicry).","Antibodies, complement, and macrophages damage peripheral myelin (or axons in the axonal variant).","Ascending symmetric weakness, areflexia, autonomic instability, and possible respiratory failure.","CSF shows high protein with a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation).","Miller Fisher variant: anti-GQ1b antibodies (GQ1b is rich in the nerves that move the eyes) cause ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia."]},{"h":"Neuromuscular junction autoimmunity","points":["Myasthenia gravis is type II hypersensitivity: antibodies to postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors block them, cause their internalization, and fix complement.","Myasthenia gravis is linked to thymic hyperplasia and thymoma.","Lambert-Eaton syndrome is type II hypersensitivity against presynaptic P/Q voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, often paraneoplastic with small cell lung cancer.","MS and acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM) are T cell-mediated (type IV); ADEM is a single episode of widespread demyelination, usually in a child, after a viral infection or vaccination."]},{"h":"Paraneoplastic syndromes","points":["A tumor expresses a protein also found in neurons; the immune response against the tumor attacks the nervous system too.","Neurologic symptoms often appear before the cancer is found.","Anti-Hu: small cell lung cancer, sensory neuronopathy, limbic encephalitis.","Anti-Yo (Purkinje cell antibody): ovarian or breast cancer, subacute cerebellar degeneration.","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis: young woman with ovarian teratoma, psychiatric symptoms, seizures, abnormal movements, and autonomic instability."]},{"h":"Vaccines against CNS infection","points":["Plain polysaccharide capsules trigger T cell-independent responses: mostly IgM, little memory, and poor responses in children under 2.","Conjugate vaccines (Hib, pneumococcal, meningococcal) link the polysaccharide to a protein carrier; B cells present carrier peptides to helper T cells.","T cell help brings class switching to IgG, affinity maturation, and memory, so the vaccine works in infants.","Inactivated polio vaccine (Salk) is injected and makes serum IgG that stops virus from reaching the CNS, with weak gut IgA.","Live oral polio vaccine (Sabin) makes both serum IgG and intestinal secretory IgA, blocking gut infection and spread; it rarely reverts and causes paralytic polio."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Immune mechanisms in neurologic disease","cols":["Disease","Target","Main mechanism","Key clues"],"rows":[["Myasthenia gravis","Postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor","Type II hypersensitivity (antibody)","Fatigable ptosis and diplopia; thymic hyperplasia or thymoma"],["Lambert-Eaton syndrome","Presynaptic P/Q voltage-gated Ca2+ channel","Type II hypersensitivity (antibody)","Weakness improves with use; small cell lung cancer"],["Guillain-Barré syndrome","Peripheral nerve gangliosides","Molecular mimicry; antibodies, complement, macrophages","After Campylobacter jejuni; ascending weakness; high CSF protein, normal cells"],["Miller Fisher variant","GQ1b ganglioside","Molecular mimicry; antibody","Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia"],["Multiple sclerosis","CNS myelin","Type IV (Th1 and Th17 T cells)","HLA-DR2, oligoclonal bands, periventricular plaques"],["ADEM","CNS myelin","Type IV (T cells)","Single episode after infection or vaccination, often in children"]]},{"title":"Paraneoplastic neurologic syndromes","cols":["Antibody","Usual tumor","Neurologic syndrome"],"rows":[["Anti-Hu","Small cell lung cancer","Sensory neuronopathy, limbic encephalitis, encephalomyelitis"],["Anti-Yo","Ovarian or breast cancer","Subacute cerebellar degeneration"],["Anti-NMDA receptor","Ovarian teratoma","Psychiatric symptoms, seizures, dyskinesias, autonomic instability"],["Anti-P/Q Ca2+ channel","Small cell lung cancer","Lambert-Eaton syndrome"]]},{"title":"Salk vs Sabin polio vaccine","cols":["Feature","Salk (inactivated, IPV)","Sabin (live attenuated, OPV)"],"rows":[["Route","Injection","Oral"],["Serum IgG","Yes","Yes"],["Intestinal secretory IgA","Weak","Strong"],["Stops gut infection and spread","Less well","Yes"],["Can revert to cause paralysis","No","Rarely"],["Safe in immunocompromised people","Yes","No"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Hu is for the lung, Yo is for the ovary","text":"Anti-Hu goes with small cell lung cancer and sensory loss. Anti-Yo goes with ovarian or breast cancer and cerebellar degeneration."},{"kind":"trick","label":"GQ1b moves the eyes","text":"The GQ1b ganglioside is concentrated in the nerves to the eye muscles, so anti-GQ1b gives the eye findings of Miller Fisher syndrome."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Conjugate means T cell help","text":"Sugar alone gets IgM and no memory. Add a protein carrier and helper T cells join in, giving IgG, memory, and protection in infants."}],"highYield":["IL-1 and TNF-alpha open the blood-brain barrier in bacterial meningitis and cause cerebral edema.","Microglia are the resident macrophages of the CNS.","Multiple sclerosis: Th1 and Th17 T cells, HLA-DR2, oligoclonal IgG bands, periventricular plaques.","Guillain-Barré after Campylobacter jejuni: molecular mimicry with nerve gangliosides; high CSF protein with normal cells.","Anti-GQ1b: Miller Fisher variant with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia.","Myasthenia gravis and Lambert-Eaton are type II hypersensitivity; MS and ADEM are T cell-mediated.","Young woman with psychosis, seizures, and an ovarian teratoma: anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis.","Conjugate vaccines turn a T cell-independent polysaccharide response into a T cell-dependent one."],"pitfalls":["Oligoclonal bands must be in the CSF and absent from serum to point to MS.","Guillain-Barré has a high CSF protein with a normal cell count; many white cells suggest infection instead.","Myasthenia gravis weakness worsens with use; Lambert-Eaton weakness improves with use.","The Sabin vaccine gives gut IgA and stops spread, but it can rarely revert, so only the Salk vaccine is safe for immunocompromised people."],"dumps":[{"t":"Meningitis and the barrier","p":"Draw a brain capillary with its tight junctions, basement membrane, pericyte, and astrocyte foot processes, and a microglial cell nearby. Then show step by step how bacteria in the CSF lead to cerebral edema.","l":["Endothelial tight junctions form the main barrier","Basement membrane, pericytes, and astrocyte foot processes support it","Microglia are resident CNS macrophages","Bacterial products bind toll-like receptors on microglia and macrophages","IL-1 and TNF-alpha are released","Endothelium is activated and neutrophils enter","Barrier permeability rises, causing vasogenic edema","Raised intracranial pressure risks herniation"]},{"t":"Autoimmune and paraneoplastic table","p":"Make a table for myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, Guillain-Barré syndrome, multiple sclerosis, and anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis with columns for target, mechanism, trigger or tumor, and key finding.","l":["Myasthenia gravis: postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor, type II, thymus","Lambert-Eaton: presynaptic Ca2+ channel, type II, small cell lung cancer","Guillain-Barré: gangliosides, molecular mimicry, Campylobacter jejuni","Miller Fisher: anti-GQ1b, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia","MS: CNS myelin, Th1 and Th17, HLA-DR2, oligoclonal bands","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis: ovarian teratoma, psychiatric symptoms and seizures"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on hypersensitivity diseases"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on vaccines and T cell-dependent antibody responses"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, sections on infections and demyelinating diseases"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on picornaviruses (poliovirus and polio vaccines)"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-13","NE-19","NE-20-B4","NE-20-B5","NE-20-B6","NE-20-G8","NE-20-G10"]},"NE-19":{"id":"NE-19","summary":"This unit covers the microbes that infect the brain, meninges, and nerves: the meningitis bacteria, the two clostridial neurotoxins, the neurotropic viruses, the CNS fungi, and the spirochetes and leprosy bacillus. Exam questions give a patient and a clue (a stain, an exposure, a vector, a toxin effect) and ask which organism or virulence factor explains it.","notes":[{"h":"Meningitis bacteria in children and adults","points":["Streptococcus pneumoniae: gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci, alpha-hemolytic, optochin sensitive; the most common cause of bacterial meningitis in adults.","Its polysaccharide capsule blocks phagocytosis; pneumolysin and IgA protease add to damage.","Neisseria meningitidis: gram-negative diplococci, spread by respiratory droplets in crowded groups such as dorms and military barracks.","Its capsule resists complement, and lipooligosaccharide (LOS) endotoxin drives septic shock, petechiae, DIC, and adrenal hemorrhage (Waterhouse-Friderichsen).","Deficiency of terminal complement (C5 to C9) raises the risk of repeated Neisseria infection.","Haemophilus influenzae type b: gram-negative coccobacillus with a polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP) capsule; needs factors X (hematin) and V (NAD) to grow.","Hib conjugate vaccine links PRP to a protein so infants make a T-cell-dependent antibody response; Hib meningitis is now mainly seen in unvaccinated children."]},{"h":"Neonatal meningitis","points":["Streptococcus agalactiae (group B): gram-positive cocci in chains, beta-hemolytic, bacitracin resistant; colonizes the maternal vagina and rectum and passes to the baby at birth.","Escherichia coli with the K1 capsule: the K1 polysaccharide resists phagocytosis and complement and helps the bacteria reach the meninges.","Listeria monocytogenes: gram-positive rod, tumbling motility, grows in the refrigerator; found in unpasteurized dairy and deli meats.","Listeria lives inside cells: listeriolysin O lets it escape the phagosome, and ActA builds actin tails that push it into the next cell without meeting antibody.","Listeria crosses the placenta and targets neonates, pregnant women, older adults, and people with weak cell-mediated immunity."]},{"h":"Clostridial neurotoxins","points":["Both toxins are zinc proteases that cleave SNARE proteins, so synaptic vesicles cannot fuse and release transmitter.","Botulinum toxin acts at peripheral cholinergic terminals and blocks acetylcholine release, causing descending flaccid paralysis.","Botulism signs: diplopia, ptosis, dysphagia, dilated pupils, then descending weakness. Adults eat preformed toxin in canned food; infants swallow spores (honey) that make toxin in the gut.","Tetanospasmin from Clostridium tetani spores in a wound enters motor nerve terminals and travels retrograde to the spinal cord.","There it blocks GABA and glycine release from inhibitory interneurons (Renshaw cells), so motor neurons fire unchecked: trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonos, spastic paralysis."]},{"h":"Neurotropic viruses","points":["Herpesviruses: enveloped dsDNA, envelope from the nuclear membrane; they ride axons back to sensory ganglia, stay latent, and travel out again on reactivation.","HSV-1 is latent in the trigeminal ganglion and causes temporal lobe encephalitis; VZV is latent in dorsal root and cranial nerve ganglia and reactivates as dermatomal zoster.","JC polyomavirus: nonenveloped circular dsDNA, latent in the kidney; with AIDS or other immunosuppression it infects oligodendrocytes and causes PML.","Enteroviruses (coxsackievirus, echovirus, poliovirus): nonenveloped positive-sense picornaviruses spread fecal-oral, mostly summer and fall; the top cause of viral (aseptic) meningitis.","West Nile virus: enveloped positive-sense flavivirus; Culex mosquitoes carry it from bird reservoirs; humans and horses are dead-end hosts.","Rabies: bullet-shaped, enveloped, negative-sense rhabdovirus; binds the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor at the bite site and climbs to the CNS by retrograde (dynein) axonal transport. Negri bodies sit in Purkinje and hippocampal neurons."]},{"h":"Fungi, spirochetes, and leprosy","points":["Cryptococcus neoformans: yeast with a thick polysaccharide capsule, in pigeon droppings; inhaled, then spreads to the meninges in AIDS. India ink shows a clear halo; latex agglutination detects capsular antigen.","Mucor and Rhizopus: broad, ribbon-like nonseptate hyphae branching at wide angles; ketoacidosis (acid pH, high glucose, free iron) lets them invade vessels from the sinuses into the orbit and brain.","Coccidioides: dimorphic; mold with arthroconidia in southwestern US desert soil, spherules full of endospores in tissue; can cause chronic basilar meningitis.","Treponema pallidum: thin spirochete too narrow to see on Gram stain; darkfield shows it in a chancre; VDRL and RPR detect nonspecific anticardiolipin antibody; FTA-ABS detects specific antibody and stays positive for life.","Borrelia burgdorferi: spirochete spread by Ixodes ticks (white-footed mouse reservoir, deer host); causes facial palsy and lymphocytic meningitis.","Mycobacterium leprae: acid-fast, cannot be grown in culture, prefers cooler sites (skin, nose, peripheral nerves) and invades Schwann cells; armadillos are a US reservoir."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Bacterial meningitis organisms","cols":["Organism","Stain and shape","Main virulence factor","Typical patient or source"],"rows":[["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci","Polysaccharide capsule, pneumolysin, IgA protease","Adults and older adults; asplenia"],["Neisseria meningitidis","Gram-negative diplococci","Capsule, LOS endotoxin, IgA protease","Teens and young adults in crowded living; terminal complement deficiency"],["Haemophilus influenzae type b","Gram-negative coccobacillus","PRP capsule","Unvaccinated young children"],["Streptococcus agalactiae (group B)","Gram-positive cocci in chains","Polysaccharide capsule","Neonates; mother colonized in the vagina and rectum"],["Escherichia coli K1","Gram-negative rod","K1 capsule","Neonates, especially preterm"],["Listeria monocytogenes","Gram-positive rod, tumbling motility","Listeriolysin O, ActA actin tails","Neonates, pregnancy, older adults, weak cell-mediated immunity; deli meat, soft cheese"]]},{"title":"Botulinum toxin versus tetanospasmin","cols":["Feature","Botulinum toxin","Tetanospasmin"],"rows":[["Source","Preformed toxin in food, or spores in infant gut or wounds","Spores in a contaminated wound"],["Site of action","Peripheral cholinergic nerve terminal","Inhibitory interneurons in the spinal cord, reached by retrograde transport"],["Transmitter blocked","Acetylcholine","GABA and glycine"],["Molecular action","Zinc protease cleaves SNARE proteins","Zinc protease cleaves SNARE protein (synaptobrevin)"],["Paralysis","Flaccid, descending, starts with cranial nerves","Spastic: trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonos"]]},{"title":"Viruses of the nervous system","cols":["Virus","Genome and envelope","Spread","Nervous system disease"],"rows":[["HSV-1, HSV-2, VZV","dsDNA, enveloped","Direct contact or respiratory (VZV); latent in sensory ganglia","Temporal lobe encephalitis, neonatal and aseptic meningitis, zoster"],["JC polyomavirus","Circular dsDNA, nonenveloped","Childhood infection, latent in kidney","PML in immunosuppression"],["Enteroviruses","Positive-sense ssRNA, nonenveloped","Fecal-oral","Aseptic meningitis; polio destroys anterior horn cells"],["West Nile virus","Positive-sense ssRNA, enveloped","Culex mosquito from birds","Encephalitis in older adults, flaccid paralysis"],["Rabies virus","Negative-sense ssRNA, enveloped, bullet-shaped","Bite of bat, raccoon, skunk, fox, or dog","Encephalitis with agitation, hydrophobia, paralysis; Negri bodies"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Please SHiNE my SKiS","text":"Encapsulated bacteria: Pseudomonas, S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae type b, N. meningitidis, E. coli, Salmonella, Klebsiella, group B Strep. Asplenia raises the risk of severe infection with encapsulated bacteria, especially S. pneumoniae, Hib, and N. meningitidis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Botulism blocks, tetanus tightens","text":"Botulinum stops acetylcholine at the muscle, so muscles go limp. Tetanus toxin stops the brakes (GABA and glycine) in the cord, so muscles lock up."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Both directions on the axon","text":"Rabies and tetanus toxin go retrograde toward the CNS. Herpesviruses go retrograde to reach the ganglion, then anterograde back to the skin when they reactivate."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ketoacidosis plus black eschar","text":"A patient in DKA with sinus pain and a black necrotic patch on the palate or nasal turbinate has Mucor or Rhizopus until proven otherwise."}],"highYield":["Lancet-shaped gram-positive diplococci in CSF from an adult point to Streptococcus pneumoniae.","Meningococcal LOS endotoxin causes petechial rash, shock, and adrenal hemorrhage.","Hib conjugate vaccine turns the PRP capsule into a T-cell-dependent antigen that infants can respond to.","Listeria spreads cell to cell by actin tails and is linked to deli meats and unpasteurized cheese.","Botulinum gives descending flaccid paralysis; tetanus gives spastic paralysis with trismus.","Rabies travels retrograde along axons; a longer distance from bite to brain means a longer incubation.","Cryptococcus: India ink halo and capsular antigen test in CSF of a patient with AIDS.","Mycobacterium leprae invades Schwann cells and favors cool body sites."],"pitfalls":["Both meningococcus and gonococcus are gram-negative diplococci; only meningococcus has a polysaccharide capsule.","Listeria is a gram-positive rod; a gram-positive rod in neonatal CSF is Listeria, while cocci in chains point to group B Streptococcus.","Mucor has broad nonseptate hyphae with wide-angle branching; Aspergillus has narrow septate hyphae branching at acute angles.","VDRL and RPR fall after treatment and can be falsely positive (pregnancy, lupus, some infections); FTA-ABS stays positive for life.","JC virus is latent in the kidney and damages oligodendrocytes; herpesviruses are latent in sensory ganglia."],"dumps":[{"t":"Meningitis organisms table","p":"On blank paper, make a table of six meningitis bacteria (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, Hib, group B Strep, E. coli K1, Listeria). Columns: Gram stain and shape, key virulence factor, who gets it.","l":["S. pneumoniae: gram-positive lancet diplococci, capsule, adults","N. meningitidis: gram-negative diplococci, capsule and LOS endotoxin, young adults in crowded living","Hib: gram-negative coccobacillus, PRP capsule, unvaccinated children","Group B Strep: gram-positive cocci in chains, maternal colonization, neonates","E. coli K1: gram-negative rod, K1 capsule, neonates","Listeria: gram-positive rod, tumbling motility, actin tails, deli meats and soft cheese","Capsules block phagocytosis and complement"]},{"t":"Two toxins at the synapse","p":"Draw a cholinergic neuromuscular junction and a spinal inhibitory interneuron synapsing on a motor neuron. Mark where botulinum toxin and tetanospasmin act, and label the transmitter each blocks and the type of paralysis.","l":["SNARE proteins needed for vesicle fusion","Both toxins are zinc proteases that cleave SNAREs","Botulinum acts at the neuromuscular junction and blocks acetylcholine","Botulinum gives descending flaccid paralysis starting with cranial nerves","Tetanospasmin moves retrograde up the motor axon to the spinal cord","Tetanospasmin blocks GABA and glycine from inhibitory interneurons","Tetanus gives spastic paralysis: trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonos"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Neisseriae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Mycology"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Section on spore-forming gram-positive bacilli (Clostridium)"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-20-G8","NE-20-G2","NE-20-G14","NE-20-G12","NE-20-G9","NE-20-G7","NE-18"]},"NE-20-A":{"id":"NE-20-A","summary":"Neurological vascular disease covers stroke from blocked or bursting vessels, brain injury from global loss of blood flow, bleeding inside the skull, and loss of blood supply to the spinal cord. Exams test the mechanism behind each lesion, where it sits, how it looks under the microscope over time, and what complication follows.","notes":[{"h":"Ischemic stroke types","points":["Thrombotic infarct: a clot forms on an atherosclerotic plaque, most often at the carotid bifurcation, the middle cerebral artery, or the basilar artery.","Embolic infarct: a clot travels from the heart (atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus after MI, valve disease) or a carotid plaque, usually into the MCA; reperfusion often turns it hemorrhagic.","Lacunar infarct: hypertensive arteriolosclerosis narrows small penetrating (lenticulostriate) arteries, leaving small cavities in the basal ganglia, internal capsule, thalamus, or pons.","TIA: brief focal deficit, usually gone within an hour, with no infarct on imaging; a warning sign for stroke.","Shared risk factors: hypertension (the strongest), atrial fibrillation, diabetes, smoking, dyslipidemia.","Noncontrast CT comes first because fresh blood is bright on CT right away, while early ischemia may not show for hours; hemorrhage must be excluded before clot-dissolving therapy."]},{"h":"What an infarct looks like over time","points":["About 12 to 24 hours: red neurons (shrunken, eosinophilic cytoplasm, dark pyknotic nuclei).","About 1 to 3 days: neutrophils enter.","From about day 3 to weeks: macrophages (microglia) eat the debris.","Weeks to months: reactive astrocytes form gliosis around a fluid-filled cyst, the end result of liquefactive necrosis."]},{"h":"Global hypoxic-ischemic injury","points":["Cardiac arrest, shock, drowning, or carbon monoxide cut oxygen to the whole brain; glutamate excitotoxicity and calcium influx kill neurons.","Most vulnerable neurons: hippocampal CA1 pyramidal cells, cerebellar Purkinje cells, and neocortical pyramidal cells (laminar necrosis in layers 3, 5, and 6).","Severe hypotension causes watershed infarcts in border zones between arterial territories, such as between the ACA and MCA (proximal arm and shoulder weakness)."]},{"h":"Intracranial hemorrhage","points":["Epidural hematoma: temporal bone fracture tears the middle meningeal artery; brief loss of consciousness, a lucid interval, then rapid decline; biconvex and stopped by sutures.","Subdural hematoma: tearing of bridging veins with brain atrophy (older adults, alcohol use) or shaking (infants); crescent-shaped and crosses sutures.","Subarachnoid hemorrhage: a saccular (berry) aneurysm ruptures, most often at the anterior communicating artery; sudden worst headache of life, neck stiffness, xanthochromic CSF.","Aneurysm risk: ADPKD, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, smoking, hypertension. After SAH: rebleeding, vasospasm with delayed infarcts (about days 3 to 14), and hydrocephalus.","Hypertensive intraparenchymal hemorrhage: Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms burst in the basal ganglia (putamen), thalamus, pons, and cerebellum.","Cerebral amyloid angiopathy: beta-amyloid in walls of cortical vessels causes recurrent lobar hemorrhage in older adults.","Arteriovenous malformation: tangled arteries drain straight into veins with no capillary bed; causes hemorrhage and seizures in young adults."]},{"h":"Other vessel lesions","points":["Carotid or vertebral dissection: an intimal tear after neck trauma lets blood into the wall, narrowing the lumen and seeding emboli; stroke in a young adult with neck pain (Horner syndrome with carotid dissection).","Subclavian steal: stenosis of the subclavian artery before the vertebral branch; arm exercise pulls blood backward down the vertebral artery, causing arm claudication, dizziness, and lower blood pressure in that arm.","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis: hypercoagulable states (pregnancy, postpartum, estrogen) clot the dural sinuses; headache, papilledema, seizures, hemorrhagic venous infarcts.","Hypertensive encephalopathy and PRES: severe hypertension overwhelms autoregulation, the blood-brain barrier leaks, and vasogenic edema forms, mainly in posterior (parieto-occipital) white matter; headache, confusion, vision loss, seizures; usually reversible.","Spinal cord supply: one anterior spinal artery (front two-thirds of the cord) and two posterior spinal arteries (dorsal columns); the artery of Adamkiewicz feeds the lower cord.","Anterior spinal artery syndrome after aortic surgery or dissection: paralysis and loss of pain and temperature below the lesion, bladder loss, with vibration and position sense spared."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Neurological vascular disease by condition","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Cerebral infarction","Atherosclerosis, atrial fibrillation, hypertension, diabetes, smoking","Thrombus, embolus, or small vessel occlusion causes liquefactive necrosis","Sudden focal deficit matching an arterial territory","Hemorrhagic conversion of embolic infarcts, edema, herniation, cystic scar"],["Cerebrovascular accident (ischemic or hemorrhagic)","Hypertension (strongest), atrial fibrillation, diabetes, dyslipidemia, smoking","About 85 percent ischemic, about 15 percent hemorrhagic","Focal deficit; headache and vomiting more with hemorrhage","Disability, aspiration, raised intracranial pressure, recurrent stroke"],["Ischemic-hypoxic encephalopathy","Cardiac arrest, shock, drowning, carbon monoxide","Global hypoperfusion, glutamate excitotoxicity, selective neuronal vulnerability","Coma, memory loss, proximal arm weakness from watershed infarcts","Persistent vegetative state, brain death"],["Intracranial hemorrhage","Head trauma, aneurysm, hypertension, amyloid angiopathy, AVM","Vessel rupture into the epidural, subdural, subarachnoid, or parenchymal space","Headache, lucid interval (epidural), thunderclap headache (SAH)","Herniation, vasospasm, hydrocephalus, seizures"],["Vascular lesions of spinal cord","Aortic surgery, dissection, atherosclerosis","Anterior spinal artery or artery of Adamkiewicz ischemia","Paralysis, lost pain and temperature, bladder loss; dorsal columns spared","Permanent paraplegia"]]},{"title":"Intracranial bleeds compared","cols":["Bleed","Vessel","Typical patient","Imaging and clues"],"rows":[["Epidural","Middle meningeal artery","Temporal bone fracture","Biconvex, does not cross sutures, lucid interval"],["Subdural","Bridging veins","Older adults, alcohol use, shaken infants","Crescent, crosses sutures, may be chronic"],["Subarachnoid","Saccular aneurysm, often anterior communicating artery","ADPKD, Ehlers-Danlos, smokers","Blood in basal cisterns, xanthochromic CSF"],["Hypertensive intraparenchymal","Lenticulostriate and other penetrating arteries (Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms)","Long-standing hypertension","Deep: putamen, thalamus, pons, cerebellum"],["Amyloid angiopathy","Cortical and leptomeningeal arteries with beta-amyloid","Older adults","Lobar, often recurrent"],["Arteriovenous malformation","Artery joined directly to vein","Young adults","Hemorrhage plus seizures"]]},{"title":"Time course of a cerebral infarct","cols":["Time after onset (about)","Main finding"],"rows":[["12 to 24 hours","Red neurons"],["1 to 3 days","Neutrophils"],["3 days to weeks","Macrophages (microglia) clear debris"],["Weeks to months","Reactive gliosis around a fluid-filled cyst (liquefactive necrosis)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Epidural artery, subdural vein","text":"Arterial bleeding is fast, so epidural patients crash after a lucid interval. Venous bleeding is slow, so subdural bleeds can build over days to weeks in older adults."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Shape follows the dura","text":"Epidural blood must peel the dura off the skull, and sutures hold the dura down, so it bulges into a lens. Subdural blood spreads freely under the dura and crosses sutures as a crescent."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Deep bleed or lobar bleed","text":"Deep bleeds (putamen, thalamus, pons, cerebellum) point to hypertension. Lobar bleeds near the cortex in an older adult point to amyloid angiopathy."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Front of the cord","text":"The anterior spinal artery feeds the front two-thirds of the cord, which is everything except the dorsal columns and the back of the dorsal horns. So a patient who can still feel vibration in the toes but cannot move or feel pinprick has lost the anterior spinal artery."}],"highYield":["Embolic infarcts often turn hemorrhagic when the clot breaks up and blood reenters damaged vessels.","Lacunar infarcts come from hypertensive arteriolosclerosis of small penetrating arteries.","Red neurons appear about 12 to 24 hours after an infarct; the end stage is a cyst lined by gliosis.","Hippocampal CA1 neurons and cerebellar Purkinje cells die first in global hypoxia.","Subarachnoid hemorrhage: worst headache of life, xanthochromia, then vasospasm days later.","ADPKD and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome raise the risk of berry aneurysm.","Anterior spinal artery syndrome spares vibration and position sense.","PRES is reversible vasogenic edema of posterior white matter in severe hypertension or eclampsia."],"pitfalls":["A TIA leaves no infarct on imaging; a deficit with an infarct on MRI is a stroke even if it resolves.","Cytotoxic edema follows ischemia; PRES and hypertensive encephalopathy cause vasogenic edema.","The lucid interval belongs to epidural hematoma; a slow decline over weeks in an older adult points to chronic subdural hematoma.","Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms cause deep hypertensive bleeds; saccular berry aneurysms cause subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Watershed infarcts come from low perfusion pressure across the whole brain, while embolic infarcts sit inside one arterial territory."],"dumps":[{"t":"Four intracranial bleeds","p":"Draw four skull cross-sections side by side showing an epidural, subdural, subarachnoid, and hypertensive intraparenchymal bleed. Label the vessel that bleeds, the shape on CT, and the classic patient for each.","l":["Epidural: middle meningeal artery, temporal bone fracture","Epidural: biconvex, limited by sutures, lucid interval","Subdural: bridging veins, older adults, alcohol use, shaken infants","Subdural: crescent that crosses sutures","Subarachnoid: ruptured berry aneurysm, often anterior communicating artery","Subarachnoid: blood in cisterns, xanthochromia, vasospasm, hydrocephalus","Intraparenchymal: Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms in putamen, thalamus, pons, cerebellum"]},{"t":"Spinal cord blood supply","p":"Draw a cross-section of the thoracic spinal cord. Label the anterior spinal artery, the two posterior spinal arteries, and the tracts each one supplies. Then shade the area lost in anterior spinal artery syndrome and list what the patient can and cannot do.","l":["One anterior spinal artery supplies the front two-thirds of the cord","Two posterior spinal arteries supply the dorsal columns","Artery of Adamkiewicz reinforces supply to the lower cord","Anterior territory includes corticospinal tracts, spinothalamic tracts, and anterior horns","Loss of motor function and pain and temperature below the lesion","Bladder dysfunction","Vibration and position sense spared","Causes: aortic surgery, aortic dissection"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Nervous System Disorders"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on vasculature of the spinal cord"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-10","NE-20-H1","NE-20-C1","NE-20-C2","NE-20-B1"]},"NE-20-B":{"id":"NE-20-B","summary":"This unit covers diseases where neurons slowly die (Alzheimer, ALS, Parkinson, Huntington, Friedreich ataxia, other dementias) and diseases where myelin is attacked (multiple sclerosis, ADEM, transverse myelitis, Guillain-Barre). Exams test the protein or gene behind each disease, the cells and tracts that are lost, and the signs that separate look-alikes.","notes":[{"h":"Alzheimer disease","points":["Beta then gamma secretase cut amyloid precursor protein into amyloid beta, which clumps into extracellular plaques.","Hyperphosphorylated tau forms intracellular neurofibrillary tangles; tangle burden tracks with the degree of dementia.","Risks: age, APOE e4, Down syndrome (APP gene on chromosome 21), and early-onset familial APP, PSEN1, or PSEN2 mutations.","Gross: narrow gyri, wide sulci, and enlarged ventricles (hydrocephalus ex vacuo), worst in the hippocampus and temporal lobes.","Micro: neuritic plaques, neurofibrillary tangles, amyloid angiopathy; loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert.","Course: short-term memory loss first, then language and visuospatial problems, then full dependence."]},{"h":"Other dementias and cognitive decline","points":["Dementia with Lewy bodies: cortical alpha-synuclein Lewy bodies; visual hallucinations, fluctuating attention, and parkinsonism early.","Frontotemporal dementia: early personality change, disinhibition, or language loss, with memory spared early; tau Pick bodies or TDP-43 inclusions.","Progressive supranuclear palsy: tau disease with vertical gaze palsy, axial rigidity, and early backward falls.","Vascular dementia: stepwise decline after multiple infarcts or diffuse small vessel disease, with hypertension and diabetes as risks.","Mild cognitive impairment: measurable decline beyond normal aging, with daily function still intact."]},{"h":"Motor neuron and movement disorders","points":["ALS: upper and lower motor neurons die together; weakness, atrophy, and fasciculations plus spasticity, hyperreflexia, and Babinski sign.","ALS spares sensation, bladder control, and eye movements; familial cases involve SOD1 or C9orf72; death from respiratory failure.","Parkinson disease: loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta with alpha-synuclein Lewy bodies; resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, postural instability.","Secondary parkinsonism: MPTP (a street-drug contaminant converted by MAO-B into a nigral toxin) and dopamine-blocking drugs such as antipsychotics.","Huntington disease: autosomal dominant CAG expansion in HTT on chromosome 4; caudate atrophy with loss of GABAergic neurons; chorea, dementia, depression in midlife.","Huntington shows anticipation: repeats expand further during spermatogenesis, so paternal transmission brings earlier onset.","Friedreich ataxia: autosomal recessive GAA expansion in frataxin; mitochondrial iron builds up and damages dorsal columns, spinocerebellar and corticospinal tracts.","Friedreich extras: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (main cause of death), diabetes, kyphoscoliosis, pes cavus.","Essential tremor: often familial, postural and action tremor of hands, head, or voice that may ease with alcohol. Cervical dystonia: sustained neck muscle contraction twisting the head (torticollis)."]},{"h":"Demyelinating disease","points":["Multiple sclerosis: autoimmune T cells and antibodies attack CNS myelin; most common in women aged 20 to 40 and at higher latitude; usually relapsing-remitting.","MS findings: optic neuritis, internuclear ophthalmoplegia (MLF lesion), sensory loss, weakness, bladder problems, and symptoms worse with heat (Uhthoff phenomenon).","MS tests: periventricular white matter plaques on MRI and oligoclonal IgG bands in CSF.","ADEM: one episode of widespread perivenular demyelination, mostly in children, after a viral infection or rarely a vaccine; encephalopathy is common.","Transverse myelitis: inflammation across one cord segment, after infection or with MS; bilateral weakness, a sensory level, and bladder dysfunction.","Guillain-Barre: after infection (Campylobacter jejuni, viruses), immune attack on peripheral myelin and roots; ascending symmetric flaccid weakness with lost reflexes.","Guillain-Barre complications: respiratory failure from diaphragm weakness and autonomic instability (arrhythmias, blood pressure swings); CSF has high protein with normal cell count."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Degenerative and demyelinating diseases by condition","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Alzheimer disease","Age, APOE e4, Down syndrome, APP, PSEN1, PSEN2","Amyloid beta plaques and tau tangles; cholinergic neuron loss","Short-term memory loss first, cortical atrophy","Full dependence, aspiration pneumonia"],["Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Mostly sporadic; SOD1 or C9orf72 in familial cases","Degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons","Mixed UMN and LMN signs; sensation, bladder, eye movements spared","Respiratory failure"],["Extrapyramidal diseases (Parkinson, Huntington, spinocerebellar degeneration)","Parkinson: age, MPTP, D2 blockers. Huntington: HTT CAG repeat. Friedreich: FXN GAA repeat","Nigral dopamine loss; caudate GABA neuron loss; frataxin loss with mitochondrial iron buildup","Resting tremor and rigidity; chorea and dementia; ataxia, lost position sense, pes cavus","Falls and dementia; suicide in Huntington; cardiomyopathy in Friedreich"],["Guillain-Barre syndrome","Campylobacter jejuni, CMV, EBV, other infections","Molecular mimicry; immune demyelination of peripheral nerves and roots","Ascending symmetric flaccid weakness, areflexia, high CSF protein with normal cells","Respiratory failure, autonomic instability"],["Multiple sclerosis","Women 20 to 40, higher latitude, low vitamin D","Autoimmune demyelination of CNS white matter","Optic neuritis, INO, sensory loss, heat-worsened symptoms, periventricular plaques","Disability, neurogenic bladder, spasticity"],["Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Children after viral infection, rarely vaccination","Monophasic perivenular inflammatory demyelination","Sudden multifocal deficits with encephalopathy","Usually recovers; can be severe or fatal"]]},{"title":"Dementias compared","cols":["Disease","Protein or lesion","Early clue"],"rows":[["Alzheimer","Amyloid beta plaques, tau tangles","Short-term memory loss"],["Lewy body dementia","Alpha-synuclein in cortex","Visual hallucinations, fluctuating attention, parkinsonism"],["Frontotemporal dementia","Tau Pick bodies or TDP-43","Personality and behavior change, language loss"],["Progressive supranuclear palsy","Tau","Vertical gaze palsy, early falls"],["Vascular dementia","Multiple infarcts, small vessel disease","Stepwise decline, focal signs"],["Mild cognitive impairment","Varies; may precede Alzheimer","Measurable decline with daily function intact"]]},{"title":"Tremors compared","cols":["Tremor","When it appears","Lesion or cause"],"rows":[["Resting tremor","At rest, eases with movement","Parkinson disease (substantia nigra)"],["Intention tremor","Worse as the hand nears a target","Cerebellar lesion (same side)"],["Essential tremor","Holding a posture or moving; may ease with alcohol","Often familial, no other neurologic signs"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"TRAP (Parkinson disease)","text":"Tremor at rest, Rigidity (cogwheel), Akinesia or bradykinesia, Postural instability."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SIN (Charcot triad of MS)","text":"Scanning speech, Intention tremor, Nystagmus. It points to MS plaques in the cerebellar pathways."},{"kind":"trick","label":"ALS spares the eyes and bladder","text":"ALS mixes UMN and LMN signs in the same limb while sensation, bladder control, and eye movements stay normal. A sensory level or bladder loss points elsewhere, such as cord compression."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One time versus many times","text":"ADEM is one attack in a child after an infection. MS is many attacks over years in a young adult, with lesions of different ages."}],"highYield":["APOE e4 raises late-onset Alzheimer risk; APP, PSEN1, and PSEN2 mutations cause early-onset familial disease.","Down syndrome patients develop Alzheimer changes early because the APP gene is on chromosome 21.","ALS is the classic disease with both UMN and LMN signs and no sensory loss.","Parkinson disease: Lewy bodies made of alpha-synuclein in the substantia nigra.","Huntington disease: CAG repeat on chromosome 4, caudate atrophy, anticipation through the father.","Friedreich ataxia patients most often die of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.","Guillain-Barre: high CSF protein with a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation).","Internuclear ophthalmoplegia in a young adult points to MS."],"pitfalls":["Lewy bodies in the substantia nigra mean Parkinson disease; Lewy bodies spread through the cortex with early hallucinations mean dementia with Lewy bodies.","Pick bodies are tau inclusions of frontotemporal dementia; Lewy bodies are alpha-synuclein.","Guillain-Barre attacks peripheral myelin (Schwann cells); MS and ADEM attack CNS myelin (oligodendrocytes).","Friedreich ataxia is autosomal recessive (GAA); Huntington disease is autosomal dominant (CAG).","Mild cognitive impairment leaves daily function intact; dementia means the person can no longer manage daily life alone."],"dumps":[{"t":"Neurodegeneration table","p":"Make a table for Alzheimer disease, Parkinson disease, Huntington disease, ALS, and Friedreich ataxia. Columns: gene or protein, cells or region lost, inheritance or main risk, key signs.","l":["Alzheimer: amyloid beta and tau, hippocampus and cortex, APOE e4 and APP or PSEN mutations, short-term memory loss","Parkinson: alpha-synuclein Lewy bodies, substantia nigra dopamine neurons, TRAP signs","Huntington: HTT CAG repeat on chromosome 4, caudate GABA neurons, autosomal dominant, chorea and dementia","ALS: upper and lower motor neurons, SOD1 or C9orf72 in familial cases, mixed UMN and LMN signs","Friedreich: frataxin GAA repeat, dorsal columns and spinocerebellar tracts, autosomal recessive","Friedreich extras: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, diabetes, kyphoscoliosis, pes cavus","Huntington anticipation through paternal transmission"]},{"t":"CNS versus PNS demyelination","p":"Draw a CNS axon wrapped by an oligodendrocyte and a peripheral axon wrapped by Schwann cells. Under each, list the demyelinating diseases that strike it and the key findings of each disease.","l":["Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons; Schwann cells myelinate peripheral axons","MS: relapsing-remitting, women 20 to 40, higher latitude","MS: optic neuritis, INO, heat-worsened symptoms, periventricular plaques, oligoclonal bands","ADEM: monophasic, children, after viral infection, perivenular demyelination","Transverse myelitis: sensory level, bilateral weakness, bladder dysfunction","Guillain-Barre: post-infection, ascending symmetric flaccid weakness, areflexia","Guillain-Barre: high CSF protein with normal cells; respiratory failure and autonomic instability"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Nervous System Disorders"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Immunologic Tolerance and Autoimmunity"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-16","NE-18","NE-15","NE-20-D2","NE-20-E1"]},"NE-20-C":{"id":"NE-20-C","summary":"The skull cannot expand, so extra water in the brain (edema) or extra CSF (hydrocephalus) raises intracranial pressure. Exams test which type of edema goes with which cause, where CSF flow is blocked, and the signs of raised pressure and herniation.","notes":[{"h":"Cerebral edema","points":["Vasogenic edema: the blood-brain barrier leaks around a tumor, abscess, or injury; fluid collects mostly in white matter.","Cytotoxic edema: ischemia or hypoxia stops the Na+/K+ ATPase, sodium and water enter cells, and the cells swell.","Interstitial edema: in obstructive hydrocephalus, CSF is pushed across the ependyma into periventricular white matter.","Because the skull is a fixed box, added volume of brain, blood, or CSF quickly raises intracranial pressure."]},{"h":"Raised pressure and herniation","points":["Signs: headache worse lying down, vomiting, papilledema, and falling consciousness.","Cushing triad: hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular breathing; a late sign of brainstem compression.","Subfalcine herniation: cingulate gyrus slides under the falx and can compress the anterior cerebral artery.","Uncal herniation: medial temporal lobe compresses CN III (dilated pupil on the same side), the posterior cerebral artery, and the cerebral peduncle; brainstem stretching causes Duret hemorrhages.","Tonsillar herniation: cerebellar tonsils push through the foramen magnum and compress the medulla, stopping breathing."]},{"h":"Hydrocephalus types","points":["Noncommunicating (obstructive): flow is blocked inside the ventricles, for example aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II, Dandy-Walker malformation, or a tumor.","Communicating: CSF moves through the ventricles but is not absorbed, usually because arachnoid granulations are scarred after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Hydrocephalus ex vacuo: ventricles look large only because brain tissue has been lost (Alzheimer, Huntington, other atrophy); pressure is normal.","In infants the skull sutures are open, so raised pressure enlarges the head; a bulging fontanelle and downward gaze (sunset eyes) may appear.","Chiari II: cerebellar vermis and tonsils herniate through the foramen magnum with a lumbosacral myelomeningocele. Dandy-Walker: absent or small vermis with a cystic fourth ventricle."]},{"h":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus","points":["Older adults; enlarged ventricles with opening pressure that is not consistently high.","Stretched periventricular fibers cause urinary incontinence, a broad shuffling magnetic gait, and dementia.","Symptoms, especially gait, may improve after removing CSF by lumbar puncture, which predicts benefit from a shunt."]},{"h":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","points":["Classic patient: obese woman of childbearing age.","Headache, papilledema, transient vision loss, pulsatile tinnitus, and diplopia from CN VI palsy.","Imaging is normal (no mass, no hydrocephalus); opening pressure is high, above about 25 cm H2O (normal about 10 to 20), with normal CSF content.","Associations: excess vitamin A and tetracyclines.","The main complication is permanent vision loss from optic nerve damage."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Increased intracranial fluid by condition","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Cerebral edema","Tumor, abscess, trauma, stroke, hypoxia, severe hypertension","Leaky blood-brain barrier (vasogenic), cell swelling (cytotoxic), or CSF forced into tissue (interstitial)","Headache, vomiting, papilledema, reduced consciousness","Herniation, Cushing triad, death"],["Hydrocephalus","Aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II, Dandy-Walker, tumor, scarring after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage","Blocked CSF flow or failed absorption enlarges the ventricles","Large head in infants; headache, vomiting, papilledema in adults; triad in NPH","Herniation, vision loss, developmental delay"]]},{"title":"Types of cerebral edema","cols":["Type","Mechanism","Typical causes"],"rows":[["Vasogenic","Blood-brain barrier breakdown lets plasma fluid into white matter","Tumor, abscess, trauma, hypertensive encephalopathy and PRES"],["Cytotoxic","Failed Na+/K+ ATPase lets water into cells","Ischemia, hypoxia"],["Interstitial","CSF forced across ependyma into periventricular white matter","Obstructive hydrocephalus"]]},{"title":"Hydrocephalus and look-alikes","cols":["Type","Problem","Examples","Pressure"],"rows":[["Noncommunicating","Block inside the ventricular system","Aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II, Dandy-Walker, tumor","High"],["Communicating","Poor absorption at arachnoid granulations","After meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage","High"],["Normal pressure","Large ventricles, communicating type","Older adults, often idiopathic","Not consistently high"],["Ex vacuo","Ventricles fill space left by lost brain","Alzheimer, Huntington, other atrophy","Normal"],["Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Raised pressure with normal ventricles","Obese women of childbearing age, vitamin A, tetracyclines","High"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Wet, wobbly, and wacky (NPH)","text":"Wet: urinary incontinence. Wobbly: magnetic gait. Wacky: dementia."},{"kind":"trick","label":"CN VI is a false localizer","text":"The abducens nerve runs a long path along the skull base, so any rise in intracranial pressure can stretch it. A CN VI palsy in IIH does not mean a lesion sits near the nerve."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Blown pupil first","text":"Pupillary fibers run on the outside of CN III, so the first sign of uncal compression is a dilated, sluggish pupil on the side of the mass."}],"highYield":["Vasogenic edema surrounds tumors and abscesses; cytotoxic edema follows ischemia.","Cushing triad: hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing.","Uncal herniation compresses CN III and gives a dilated pupil on the same side.","Aqueductal stenosis is a classic cause of congenital noncommunicating hydrocephalus.","Scarred arachnoid granulations after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage cause communicating hydrocephalus.","NPH: incontinence, magnetic gait, dementia, improving after CSF removal.","IIH: obese young woman, papilledema, CN VI palsy, normal imaging, high opening pressure."],"pitfalls":["Hydrocephalus ex vacuo has large ventricles from brain loss and normal pressure; true hydrocephalus is a CSF flow or absorption problem.","Communicating means CSF can pass between ventricles and subarachnoid space; the problem is absorption.","IIH has normal imaging, so large ventricles on CT point away from IIH.","Papilledema means raised intracranial pressure and is usually bilateral; one swollen disc with pain on eye movement points to optic neuritis."],"dumps":[{"t":"CSF flow and blockage","p":"Draw the ventricular system from the lateral ventricles to the arachnoid granulations. Label each step of CSF flow and mark where noncommunicating and communicating hydrocephalus block it.","l":["Choroid plexus makes CSF in the ventricles","Lateral ventricles to third ventricle through the foramina of Monro","Third to fourth ventricle through the cerebral aqueduct","Fourth ventricle to subarachnoid space through the foramina of Luschka and Magendie","Absorption into venous sinuses through arachnoid granulations","Noncommunicating: aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II, Dandy-Walker, tumor","Communicating: granulation scarring after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage"]},{"t":"Raised pressure table","p":"Build a table comparing normal pressure hydrocephalus, idiopathic intracranial hypertension, and hydrocephalus ex vacuo. Rows: typical patient, ventricle size, opening pressure, key symptoms.","l":["NPH: older adult, large ventricles, pressure not consistently high","NPH: incontinence, magnetic gait, dementia; may improve after CSF removal","IIH: obese woman of childbearing age, normal ventricles, high opening pressure","IIH: headache, papilledema, vision loss, CN VI palsy","IIH associations: vitamin A excess, tetracyclines","Ex vacuo: large ventricles from brain atrophy, normal pressure","Ex vacuo seen in Alzheimer and Huntington disease"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-4","NE-10","NE-20-A4","NE-20-G8","NE-20-F1"]},"NE-20-D":{"id":"NE-20-D","summary":"Metabolic and nutritional problems injure the brain and nerves: ammonia in liver failure, high glucose in diabetes, missing B12 or thiamine, and too-fast sodium correction. Inherited neuropathy (Charcot-Marie-Tooth) is included. Exams test the biochemical cause, which tracts or nerves are hit, and the pattern of findings.","notes":[{"h":"Hepatic encephalopathy","points":["Gut bacteria make ammonia; in cirrhosis or a portosystemic shunt it bypasses the liver and reaches the brain.","Astrocytes clear ammonia by making glutamine, which builds up and swells them.","Precipitants: GI bleeding (a protein load), infection, high protein intake, constipation, hypokalemia, and sedatives.","Hypokalemia raises kidney ammonia production.","Findings: confusion, day-night reversal, asterixis (flapping hands with wrists extended), and coma in severe cases."]},{"h":"Peripheral neuropathy patterns","points":["Distal symmetric (stocking-glove): longest nerves fail first; diabetes, alcohol, B12 and thiamine deficiency, HIV, and chemotherapy (vincristine, taxanes, cisplatin).","Mononeuropathy: one nerve, from compression (carpal tunnel) or ischemia (diabetic CN III palsy).","Mononeuritis multiplex: several named nerves in a patchy pattern, from vasculitis (polyarteritis nodosa) or leprosy.","Axonal injury lowers signal amplitude on nerve conduction and recovers slowly; demyelinating injury slows conduction velocity."]},{"h":"Diabetic neuropathy","points":["Aldose reductase turns excess glucose into sorbitol in Schwann cells, drawing in water and using up NADPH.","Advanced glycation end products and microvascular disease of the vasa nervorum add to nerve injury.","Findings: stocking-glove loss of pain, temperature, and vibration, with lost ankle reflexes.","Autonomic loss: gastroparesis, orthostatic hypotension, erectile dysfunction, and bladder retention.","Painless pressure injuries cause neuropathic foot ulcers under the metatarsal heads.","Pupil-sparing CN III palsy: ischemia hits the core of the nerve, while the outer pupillary fibers keep their blood supply."]},{"h":"Vitamin B12 deficiency","points":["Causes: pernicious anemia (antibodies to parietal cells or intrinsic factor), strict vegan diet, gastrectomy, ileal disease or resection, Diphyllobothrium latum.","B12 is needed for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase; methylmalonic acid and homocysteine rise.","Subacute combined degeneration hits the dorsal columns (lost vibration and position sense, positive Romberg sign) and lateral corticospinal tracts (spasticity, Babinski sign).","Peripheral neuropathy and dementia can occur, even without anemia."]},{"h":"Thiamine, myelin, and inherited neuropathy","points":["Wernicke encephalopathy: thiamine deficiency in alcohol use disorder, malnutrition, or hyperemesis; confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia.","Thiamine is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and transketolase, so energy-hungry brain areas fail; glucose given before thiamine can trigger an attack.","Korsakoff syndrome: lasting amnesia with confabulation; damage in the mammillary bodies and medial thalamus.","Osmotic demyelination: chronic hyponatremia corrected too fast (more than about 8 to 10 mEq/L in 24 hours) shrinks brain cells and destroys myelin in the central pons.","Osmotic demyelination signs appear days later: quadriparesis, dysarthria, dysphagia, and possible locked-in syndrome.","Charcot-Marie-Tooth: inherited motor and sensory neuropathy, most often autosomal dominant PMP22 duplication; distal weakness, foot drop, pes cavus, hammer toes."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Metabolic and nutritional neurological diseases by condition","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Hepatic encephalopathy","Cirrhosis, portosystemic shunts; triggered by GI bleed, infection, protein load, constipation, hypokalemia","Ammonia reaches the brain; astrocytes swell with glutamine","Confusion, sleep reversal, asterixis","Coma, cerebral edema in acute liver failure"],["Peripheral neuropathy","Diabetes, alcohol, B12 and thiamine deficiency, vasculitis, leprosy, HIV, chemotherapy, CMT","Axonal degeneration or demyelination","Stocking-glove sensory loss, weakness, lost ankle reflexes","Foot ulcers, falls, Charcot joints"],["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Pernicious anemia, vegan diet, gastrectomy, ileal disease, D. latum","Failed methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase damage myelin","Lost vibration and position sense, positive Romberg, spasticity, dementia","Permanent cord damage, megaloblastic anemia"],["Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome","Alcohol use disorder, malnutrition, hyperemesis","Thiamine-dependent enzymes fail; mammillary body and medial thalamic damage","Confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia; then amnesia with confabulation","Permanent memory loss, death"]]},{"title":"Neuropathy patterns","cols":["Pattern","What it looks like","Main causes"],"rows":[["Distal symmetric polyneuropathy","Stocking-glove loss, longest nerves first","Diabetes, alcohol, B12 or thiamine deficiency, HIV, chemotherapy"],["Mononeuropathy","One nerve","Compression, diabetic nerve ischemia"],["Mononeuritis multiplex","Several separate named nerves","Vasculitis, diabetes, leprosy"],["Demyelinating","Slow conduction velocity","Guillain-Barre, Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 1"],["Axonal","Low amplitude, slow recovery","Diabetes, alcohol, toxins, chemotherapy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"From low to high, your pons will die","text":"Raising sodium too fast in chronic hyponatremia causes osmotic demyelination of the pons. The reverse, from high to low, your brain will blow: lowering sodium too fast in hypernatremia causes cerebral edema."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pupil tells the cause","text":"Diabetic CN III palsy spares the pupil because ischemia hits the core of the nerve. A posterior communicating artery aneurysm presses on the outside first, so the pupil dilates."},{"kind":"trick","label":"B12 or folate","text":"Both raise homocysteine and cause megaloblastic anemia. Only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid and damages the dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Thiamine before glucose","text":"Glucose pushes more fuel through thiamine-dependent enzymes. Giving it first to a thiamine-deficient patient can set off Wernicke encephalopathy."}],"highYield":["Hypokalemia, GI bleeding, infection, and constipation all raise ammonia and can trigger hepatic encephalopathy.","Asterixis is the classic sign of hepatic encephalopathy.","Diabetic neuropathy: sorbitol buildup, advanced glycation end products, and microvascular injury.","Subacute combined degeneration: dorsal columns plus lateral corticospinal tracts, with a positive Romberg sign.","Wernicke triad: confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia; mammillary bodies are damaged.","Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 1A comes from PMP22 duplication and gives foot drop and pes cavus.","Osmotic demyelination follows overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia."],"pitfalls":["B12 deficiency can damage the nervous system without anemia, especially when folate intake is high.","Wernicke encephalopathy is acute and can reverse with thiamine; Korsakoff amnesia is chronic and mostly permanent.","Mononeuritis multiplex is several separate named nerves; distal symmetric neuropathy is a stocking-glove pattern.","Asterixis also appears in uremia and CO2 retention, so it supports hepatic encephalopathy only alongside liver disease."],"dumps":[{"t":"Spinal cord in B12 deficiency","p":"Draw a spinal cord cross-section. Shade the tracts damaged in subacute combined degeneration and write the sign that comes from each tract. Then list the causes of B12 deficiency beside the drawing.","l":["Dorsal columns: lost vibration and position sense","Positive Romberg sign from lost position sense","Lateral corticospinal tracts: spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign","Peripheral neuropathy and dementia can also occur","Methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are high","Causes: pernicious anemia, vegan diet, gastrectomy","Causes: ileal disease or resection, Diphyllobothrium latum"]},{"t":"Ammonia to brain","p":"Trace ammonia from the gut to the brain in a patient with cirrhosis. Mark where the liver is bypassed, what astrocytes do with ammonia, and list the triggers that push a stable patient into encephalopathy.","l":["Gut bacteria make ammonia from protein and urea","Portosystemic shunts and a failing liver let ammonia skip the urea cycle","Astrocytes combine ammonia with glutamate to make glutamine","Glutamine buildup swells astrocytes","Triggers: GI bleeding, infection, high protein intake, constipation","Hypokalemia raises kidney ammonia production","Findings: confusion, sleep reversal, asterixis"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on thiamine and vitamin B12"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-14","NE-20-B4","NE-20-G7","NE-20-I2"]},"NE-20-E":{"id":"NE-20-E","summary":"Many inherited and congenital diseases injure the developing brain: chromosomal disorders, enzyme defects that let toxic substances build up, storage diseases, and infections or toxins before birth. Exams test the missing enzyme or gene, what accumulates, how it is inherited, and the clue that tells similar diseases apart.","notes":[{"h":"Down syndrome and fragile X","points":["Down syndrome (trisomy 21): intellectual disability, hypotonia, flat face, upslanting eyes, single palmar crease.","Early-onset Alzheimer disease, because the APP gene is on chromosome 21.","Atlantoaxial instability can compress the spinal cord.","Associated: duodenal atresia, atrioventricular septal defect, and higher leukemia risk.","Fragile X: CGG expansion in FMR1 leads to hypermethylation and silencing; the most common inherited cause of intellectual disability.","Fragile X findings: autism features, long face, large jaw and ears, macroorchidism after puberty."]},{"h":"Leukodystrophies","points":["X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy: ABCD1 defect blocks very long chain fatty acid entry into peroxisomes for oxidation; they build up in white matter and the adrenal cortex; boys with behavior change, vision loss, and adrenal insufficiency.","Metachromatic leukodystrophy: autosomal recessive arylsulfatase A deficiency; sulfatides build up and destroy central and peripheral myelin.","Krabbe disease: autosomal recessive galactocerebrosidase deficiency; psychosine kills myelin-forming cells; multinucleated globoid cells; infants with irritability, spasticity, and neuropathy."]},{"h":"Amino acid and urea cycle disorders","points":["PKU: autosomal recessive phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency (or loss of its cofactor BH4); tyrosine becomes essential.","PKU signs: intellectual disability, seizures, musty odor, fair skin and hair (less melanin), eczema.","Maternal PKU: high phenylalanine in an untreated mother causes microcephaly, intellectual disability, and heart defects in the baby.","Maple syrup urine disease: branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase deficiency; leucine, isoleucine, and valine build up; sweet urine odor, poor feeding, seizures in the newborn.","OTC deficiency: X-linked; carbamoyl phosphate spills into pyrimidine synthesis, so orotic acid rises; hyperammonemia causes vomiting, lethargy, and cerebral edema in newborn boys."]},{"h":"Lysosomal storage and porphyria","points":["Tay-Sachs: hexosaminidase A deficiency, GM2 ganglioside; progressive neurodegeneration, exaggerated startle, cherry-red macula, no hepatosplenomegaly.","Niemann-Pick: sphingomyelinase deficiency; neurodegeneration, cherry-red macula, hepatosplenomegaly, foamy macrophages.","Fabry: X-linked alpha-galactosidase A deficiency; burning pain in hands and feet, angiokeratomas, decreased sweating, early stroke, kidney and heart disease.","Gaucher: glucocerebrosidase deficiency; type 1 spares the brain, types 2 and 3 are neuronopathic.","Hurler: alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency with corneal clouding; Hunter: X-linked iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency, milder, no corneal clouding.","Acute intermittent porphyria: autosomal dominant porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency; abdominal pain, neuropathy, psychiatric symptoms, high urine porphobilinogen; triggered by alcohol, fasting, and CYP450 inducers."]},{"h":"Wilson disease and congenital microcephaly","points":["Wilson disease: autosomal recessive ATP7B mutation; copper is not excreted into bile or loaded onto ceruloplasmin, so serum ceruloplasmin is low.","Copper in the basal ganglia causes tremor, dystonia, and psychiatric change; copper in Descemet membrane makes Kayser-Fleischer rings; liver disease from hepatitis to cirrhosis.","Congenital microcephaly: Zika, CMV, rubella, and toxoplasma infection in pregnancy, fetal alcohol syndrome, and maternal PKU."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital and genetic neurological diseases by condition","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Down syndrome","Trisomy 21, mostly maternal meiotic nondisjunction; rises with maternal age","Extra chromosome 21, including the APP gene","Intellectual disability, hypotonia, flat face, single palmar crease","Early Alzheimer disease, atlantoaxial instability, AV septal defect, duodenal atresia, leukemia"],["Leukodystrophies","Inherited enzyme or transporter defects (ABCD1, arylsulfatase A, galactocerebrosidase)","Toxic lipids build up and destroy myelin","Developmental regression, spasticity, vision loss, neuropathy","Adrenal insufficiency (X-ALD), death in childhood"],["Phenylketonuria","Autosomal recessive phenylalanine hydroxylase or BH4 deficiency","Phenylalanine builds up; tyrosine becomes essential","Intellectual disability, seizures, musty odor, fair skin","Maternal PKU harms the fetus (microcephaly, heart defects)"],["Storage diseases","Mostly autosomal recessive lysosomal enzyme defects; Fabry and Hunter are X-linked","Undegraded substrate fills lysosomes","Neurodegeneration, cherry-red macula, organ enlargement by type","Early death in infantile forms, stroke in Fabry"],["Wilson disease","Autosomal recessive ATP7B mutation","Copper not excreted into bile; low ceruloplasmin","Tremor, dystonia, psychiatric change, Kayser-Fleischer rings, liver disease","Cirrhosis, acute liver failure, hemolysis"]]},{"title":"Storage diseases and leukodystrophies","cols":["Disease","Missing enzyme or protein","What builds up","Inheritance","Clue"],"rows":[["Tay-Sachs","Hexosaminidase A","GM2 ganglioside","Autosomal recessive","Cherry-red macula, no hepatosplenomegaly"],["Niemann-Pick","Sphingomyelinase","Sphingomyelin","Autosomal recessive","Cherry-red macula with hepatosplenomegaly, foam cells"],["Fabry","Alpha-galactosidase A","Ceramide trihexoside (globotriaosylceramide)","X-linked recessive","Painful neuropathy, angiokeratomas, early stroke"],["Gaucher","Glucocerebrosidase","Glucocerebroside","Autosomal recessive","Types 2 and 3 involve the brain"],["Hurler","Alpha-L-iduronidase","Heparan and dermatan sulfate","Autosomal recessive","Corneal clouding"],["Hunter","Iduronate-2-sulfatase","Heparan and dermatan sulfate","X-linked recessive","No corneal clouding, milder"],["Metachromatic leukodystrophy","Arylsulfatase A","Sulfatides","Autosomal recessive","Central and peripheral demyelination"],["Krabbe","Galactocerebrosidase","Galactocerebroside, psychosine","Autosomal recessive","Globoid cells"],["X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy","ABCD1 peroxisomal transporter","Very long chain fatty acids","X-linked recessive","Adrenal insufficiency in a boy with white matter loss"]]},{"title":"Inborn errors that injure the brain","cols":["Disorder","Defect","Inheritance","Key clue"],"rows":[["Phenylketonuria","Phenylalanine hydroxylase or BH4","Autosomal recessive","Musty odor, fair skin"],["Maple syrup urine disease","Branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase","Autosomal recessive","Sweet urine odor, neonatal encephalopathy"],["OTC deficiency","Ornithine transcarbamylase","X-linked","Hyperammonemia with orotic aciduria"],["Acute intermittent porphyria","Porphobilinogen deaminase","Autosomal dominant","Abdominal pain, neuropathy, psychiatric symptoms, high urine porphobilinogen"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Hunters see clearly and aim for the X","text":"Hunter syndrome has no corneal clouding (see clearly) and is X-linked. Hurler has corneal clouding and is autosomal recessive."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"I Love Vermont maple syrup","text":"Isoleucine, Leucine, Valine build up in maple syrup urine disease."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CGG: Chin, Giant Gonads","text":"Fragile X is a CGG repeat; long face with a large jaw and macroorchidism."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Cherry-red spot plus organs","text":"Tay-Sachs and Niemann-Pick both give a cherry-red macula. A big liver and spleen point to Niemann-Pick; a normal-sized liver and spleen point to Tay-Sachs."}],"highYield":["Down syndrome: early Alzheimer disease from the extra APP gene, atlantoaxial instability, AV septal defect, duodenal atresia.","Fragile X is the most common inherited cause of intellectual disability.","X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy: very long chain fatty acids, white matter loss, and adrenal insufficiency.","PKU makes tyrosine essential; less tyrosine means less melanin and fair skin.","OTC deficiency raises orotic acid and ammonia without megaloblastic anemia.","Acute intermittent porphyria attacks are triggered by alcohol, fasting, and CYP450 inducers.","Wilson disease: low ceruloplasmin, Kayser-Fleischer rings, basal ganglia and liver damage.","Fabry disease causes painful neuropathy and early stroke in young men."],"pitfalls":["Down syndrome is the most common genetic cause of intellectual disability, but it is rarely inherited; fragile X is the most common inherited cause.","X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy is a peroxisomal disorder; metachromatic leukodystrophy and Krabbe disease are lysosomal.","OTC deficiency gives orotic aciduria with hyperammonemia; UMP synthase deficiency gives orotic aciduria with megaloblastic anemia and normal ammonia.","Acute intermittent porphyria has no photosensitivity, unlike porphyria cutanea tarda.","Wilson disease has low ceruloplasmin; serum copper can be low or normal even though tissue copper is high."],"dumps":[{"t":"Storage disease table","p":"On blank paper, build a table for Tay-Sachs, Niemann-Pick, Fabry, Gaucher, Hurler, Hunter, metachromatic leukodystrophy, and Krabbe. Columns: missing enzyme, what builds up, inheritance, one clue.","l":["Tay-Sachs: hexosaminidase A, GM2, cherry-red macula, no hepatosplenomegaly","Niemann-Pick: sphingomyelinase, sphingomyelin, cherry-red macula with hepatosplenomegaly","Fabry: alpha-galactosidase A, X-linked, painful neuropathy, angiokeratomas, early stroke","Gaucher: glucocerebrosidase, types 2 and 3 involve the brain","Hurler: alpha-L-iduronidase, corneal clouding","Hunter: iduronate-2-sulfatase, X-linked, no corneal clouding","Metachromatic leukodystrophy: arylsulfatase A, sulfatides","Krabbe: galactocerebrosidase, globoid cells","All autosomal recessive except Fabry and Hunter"]},{"t":"Phenylalanine pathway","p":"Draw the pathway from phenylalanine to tyrosine and on to melanin and dopamine. Mark the enzyme and cofactor missing in PKU, and list what goes wrong in the infant and in the baby of an untreated mother.","l":["Phenylalanine hydroxylase converts phenylalanine to tyrosine","BH4 is the cofactor; BH4 deficiency also causes PKU","Tyrosine becomes essential in PKU","Low tyrosine and melanin: fair skin and hair","Phenylalanine buildup: intellectual disability, seizures, musty odor","Autosomal recessive","Maternal PKU: microcephaly, intellectual disability, heart defects in the baby"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on phenylketonuria and other amino acid disorders"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on sphingolipidoses"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-16","NE-14","NE-20-B1","NE-1"]},"NE-20-F":{"id":"NE-20-F","summary":"Nervous system tumors are sorted on the exam by age, location, and histology. Children get mostly posterior fossa tumors (medulloblastoma, pilocytic astrocytoma) and neuroblastoma outside the brain; adults get metastases, glioblastoma, and meningioma. Know the classic slide for each tumor and the genetic syndromes (NF1, NF2, tuberous sclerosis, von Hippel-Lindau) that cause them.","notes":[{"h":"Sorting by age and site","points":["Adults: most intracranial tumors are metastases; the most common primary tumor is meningioma; the most common primary malignant tumor is glioblastoma.","Meningioma: dural-based, extra-axial tumor of arachnoid cap cells, most often in adult women; whorls and psammoma bodies; linked to NF2 and prior radiation; causes seizures or focal deficits by compression.","Adult primary tumors are mostly supratentorial; childhood brain tumors are mostly infratentorial (posterior fossa).","Brain metastases come mainly from lung, breast, melanoma, kidney, and colon; they are often multiple and sit at the gray-white junction.","Primary CNS lymphoma is a diffuse large B cell lymphoma; in AIDS it is EBV-driven and can look like a ring-enhancing mass, mimicking toxoplasmosis."]},{"h":"Gliomas","points":["Glioblastoma: IDH-wildtype astrocytic tumor of older adults, grade 4, GFAP positive.","Glioblastoma spreads along white matter and can cross the corpus callosum (butterfly glioma).","Glioblastoma slide: pseudopalisading tumor cells around necrosis plus microvascular proliferation; median survival is only about 15 months with treatment.","Diffuse astrocytoma in younger adults is usually IDH-mutant and grows slowly but can progress to a higher grade.","Oligodendroglioma: IDH-mutant with 1p/19q codeletion, frontal lobe, calcified, presents with seizures; fried-egg cells and chicken-wire capillaries.","Pilocytic astrocytoma: child, cerebellum, cyst with a mural nodule, Rosenthal fibers, BRAF alteration, grade 1 with a good prognosis after resection."]},{"h":"Embryonal and neuronal tumors","points":["Medulloblastoma: most common malignant brain tumor of childhood, arises in the cerebellum (usually the vermis).","Small round blue cells with Homer Wright rosettes; it is highly malignant but radiosensitive.","It can compress the fourth ventricle (obstructive hydrocephalus, truncal ataxia) and seed the CSF as drop metastases to the spinal cord.","Ganglioglioma: mixed neoplastic ganglion cells and glial cells, temporal lobe, children and young adults, chronic seizures, slow growth.","Central neurocytoma: uniform round neuronal cells inside the lateral ventricle near the foramen of Monro in young adults; can block CSF flow."]},{"h":"Neuroblastoma","points":["Neural crest tumor of young children (most under age 5); the adrenal medulla is the most common site, but it can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain.","Presents as an abdominal mass that can cross the midline; urinary HVA and VMA (catecholamine breakdown products) are high.","Homer Wright rosettes; stains for neuron-specific markers such as synaptophysin.","Opsoclonus-myoclonus (dancing eyes, dancing feet) is a paraneoplastic sign.","MYCN amplification predicts aggressive disease; some infant tumors regress on their own."]},{"h":"Peripheral nerve tumors and neurofibromatosis","points":["Schwannoma: S-100 positive Schwann cells; cellular Antoni A areas with Verocay bodies (palisaded nuclei) and loose Antoni B areas.","Vestibular schwannoma at the cerebellopontine angle: unilateral sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, imbalance; large tumors compress CN V and VII.","NF2 (merlin gene, chromosome 22): bilateral vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, ependymomas.","NF1 (neurofibromin gene, chromosome 17): neurofibromin normally turns off Ras, so its loss drives Ras signaling.","NF1 findings: café-au-lait macules, axillary freckling, cutaneous neurofibromas, Lisch nodules (iris hamartomas), optic pathway glioma, pheochromocytoma.","Plexiform neurofibromas in NF1 can turn into malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor."]},{"h":"Eye tumors and other tumor syndromes","points":["Retinoblastoma: leukocoria (white pupillary reflex) in a young child; RB1 on chromosome 13 needs two hits.","Hereditary retinoblastoma (germline first hit) is often bilateral and carries a later risk of osteosarcoma.","Uveal melanoma is the most common primary intraocular malignancy in adults and spreads by blood, often to the liver.","Tuberous sclerosis (TSC1 or TSC2, overactive mTOR): cortical tubers, subependymal nodules, subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, seizures, ash-leaf spots, cardiac rhabdomyoma, renal angiomyolipoma.","von Hippel-Lindau (VHL on chromosome 3, HIF not degraded): cerebellar and retinal hemangioblastomas, clear cell renal cell carcinoma, pheochromocytoma."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Nervous system tumors at a glance","cols":["Tumor","Typical patient and site","Key histology or marker","Classic clinical clue"],"rows":[["Glioblastoma","Older adult, cerebral hemisphere, can cross corpus callosum","Pseudopalisading necrosis, microvascular proliferation, GFAP positive, IDH-wildtype","Rapid headache, deficits, seizures; poor survival"],["IDH-mutant diffuse astrocytoma","Young to middle-aged adult, cerebral hemisphere","Infiltrating astrocytes, GFAP positive, IDH mutation","Seizures, slow growth, can progress"],["Oligodendroglioma","Adult, frontal lobe","Fried-egg cells, chicken-wire capillaries, calcification, IDH mutation with 1p/19q codeletion","Long history of seizures"],["Pilocytic astrocytoma","Child, cerebellum","Rosenthal fibers, BRAF alteration, cyst with mural nodule","Ataxia, hydrocephalus; good prognosis"],["Medulloblastoma","Child, cerebellar vermis","Small round blue cells, Homer Wright rosettes","Truncal ataxia, hydrocephalus, drop metastases"],["Meningioma","Adult woman, dura (convexity, parasagittal)","Arachnoid cap cells, whorls, psammoma bodies","Seizures or focal deficit; dural tail on MRI"],["Ganglioglioma","Child or young adult, temporal lobe","Neoplastic ganglion cells plus glial cells","Chronic seizures"],["Central neurocytoma","Young adult, lateral ventricle","Uniform round neuronal cells, synaptophysin positive","Hydrocephalus"],["Schwannoma","Adult, CN VIII at cerebellopontine angle","S-100 positive, Antoni A with Verocay bodies, Antoni B","Hearing loss, tinnitus, imbalance; bilateral in NF2"],["Neuroblastoma","Child under 5, adrenal medulla","Small round blue cells, Homer Wright rosettes, MYCN amplification in aggressive cases","Abdominal mass, high urine HVA and VMA, opsoclonus-myoclonus"],["Metastasis","Adult, gray-white junction, often multiple","Looks like the primary tumor","Known lung, breast, melanoma, or kidney cancer"],["Primary CNS lymphoma","AIDS (EBV) or older adult, periventricular","Diffuse large B cells","Ring-enhancing lesion in AIDS that mimics toxoplasmosis"]]},{"title":"Genetic tumor syndromes of the nervous system","cols":["Syndrome","Gene and chromosome","Nervous system tumors","Other findings"],"rows":[["Neurofibromatosis type 1","NF1 (neurofibromin), chromosome 17, autosomal dominant","Neurofibromas, plexiform neurofibroma to MPNST, optic pathway glioma","Café-au-lait macules, axillary freckling, Lisch nodules, pheochromocytoma"],["Neurofibromatosis type 2","NF2 (merlin), chromosome 22, autosomal dominant","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, ependymomas","Juvenile cataracts"],["Tuberous sclerosis","TSC1 (hamartin) or TSC2 (tuberin), autosomal dominant","Cortical tubers, subependymal nodules, subependymal giant cell astrocytoma","Seizures, ash-leaf spots, facial angiofibromas, cardiac rhabdomyoma, renal angiomyolipoma"],["von Hippel-Lindau","VHL, chromosome 3, autosomal dominant","Hemangioblastomas of cerebellum, spinal cord, and retina","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, pancreatic cysts"],["Hereditary retinoblastoma","RB1, chromosome 13, germline first hit","Retinoblastoma, often bilateral","Later osteosarcoma"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Two tumors share Homer Wright rosettes","text":"Medulloblastoma (in the cerebellum) and neuroblastoma (in the adrenal) are both small round blue cell tumors of childhood with Homer Wright rosettes. Location and urine HVA and VMA tell them apart."},{"kind":"trick","label":"NF1 and NF2 by chromosome","text":"NF1 is on chromosome 17 (17 letters in von Recklinghausen), and NF2 is on chromosome 22 (two 2s, two ears, so bilateral acoustic schwannomas)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Child versus adult tumor site","text":"Children's brain tumors sit low (posterior fossa); adults' sit high (cerebral hemispheres). Check the age and the site before you look at the histology."}],"highYield":["Metastases outnumber primary brain tumors in adults; lung is the most common source.","Glioblastoma: pseudopalisading necrosis, can cross the corpus callosum, GFAP positive.","Meningioma: dural-based, whorls and psammoma bodies, women, NF2, prior radiation.","Pilocytic astrocytoma: child, cerebellar cyst with mural nodule, Rosenthal fibers.","Medulloblastoma can spread through CSF and cause drop metastases in the spinal cord.","Neuroblastoma with MYCN amplification has a poor prognosis; watch for opsoclonus-myoclonus.","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas mean NF2.","Hemangioblastoma plus renal cell carcinoma means von Hippel-Lindau."],"pitfalls":["Meningioma is extra-axial (outside the brain tissue, attached to dura); gliomas are intra-axial and infiltrate the brain.","Psammoma bodies point to meningioma in the CNS; Rosenthal fibers point to pilocytic astrocytoma. Do not swap them.","Neuroblastoma and Wilms tumor both present as abdominal masses in children; neuroblastoma is adrenal, can cross the midline, and raises HVA and VMA.","A ring-enhancing lesion in AIDS can be toxoplasmosis or primary CNS lymphoma; a single lesion with EBV DNA in CSF favors lymphoma.","Oligodendroglioma (fried-egg cells) and central neurocytoma both have round uniform cells; neurocytoma sits inside the lateral ventricle."],"dumps":[{"t":"Brain tumor table","p":"Build a table from memory with rows for glioblastoma, oligodendroglioma, pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, meningioma, schwannoma, and neuroblastoma. Columns: age, site, histology, key clue.","l":["Glioblastoma: older adult, hemisphere, pseudopalisading necrosis, IDH-wildtype","Oligodendroglioma: adult frontal lobe, fried-egg cells, calcification, seizures","Pilocytic astrocytoma: child cerebellum, cyst with mural nodule, Rosenthal fibers","Medulloblastoma: child vermis, small round blue cells, drop metastases","Meningioma: adult woman, dura, whorls and psammoma bodies","Schwannoma: CN VIII, S-100, Antoni A and B, Verocay bodies","Neuroblastoma: child adrenal, high HVA and VMA, MYCN"]},{"t":"Tumor syndromes","p":"Draw four columns for NF1, NF2, tuberous sclerosis, and von Hippel-Lindau. Under each, write the gene and chromosome, the nervous system tumors, and the findings outside the nervous system.","l":["NF1: chromosome 17, neurofibromin, loss increases Ras signaling","NF1: café-au-lait, Lisch nodules, neurofibromas, optic glioma, pheochromocytoma","NF2: chromosome 22, bilateral vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, ependymomas","Tuberous sclerosis: TSC1 or TSC2, mTOR overactivity","Tuberous sclerosis: tubers, subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, seizures, ash-leaf spots, cardiac rhabdomyoma","von Hippel-Lindau: chromosome 3, HIF not degraded","von Hippel-Lindau: hemangioblastomas, renal cell carcinoma, pheochromocytoma","All four are autosomal dominant"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System (section on tumors)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of Infancy and Childhood (section on neuroblastic tumors)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Eye (section on retinoblastoma and uveal melanoma)"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-16","NE-20-C2","EN-12-G6","MS-13-F4","IM-15-B2","IN-13-G3"]},"NE-20-G":{"id":"NE-20-G","summary":"CNS and PNS infections are tested by matching the patient (age, immune status, exposure) to the organism and then to the lesion. Know the bacterial meningitis organisms by age, the CSF patterns, the toxin diseases (botulism and tetanus), the slow and opportunistic infections (prions, PML, SSPE, cryptococcus), and how each organism reaches the nervous system.","notes":[{"h":"Meningitis","points":["Neonates: group B streptococcus, E. coli, and Listeria.","Children and adults: S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis; older adults add Listeria.","Findings: fever, headache, neck stiffness, photophobia, altered mental status; a petechial or purpuric rash suggests meningococcemia.","Complications: sensorineural hearing loss, hydrocephalus, seizures, cerebral infarction, and adrenal hemorrhage with meningococcemia (Waterhouse-Friderichsen).","Tuberculous meningitis forms a thick basilar exudate that traps cranial nerves (palsies) and blocks CSF flow (hydrocephalus).","Spirochetes: Lyme disease gives lymphocytic meningitis, facial palsy (often bilateral), and radiculopathy; leptospirosis comes from water contaminated by animal urine."]},{"h":"Encephalitis and abscess","points":["Viral encephalitis histology: perivascular lymphocytic cuffs, microglial nodules, and neuronophagia.","HSV-1: the most common cause of sporadic fatal viral encephalitis; hemorrhagic necrosis of the temporal lobes, with confusion and personality change.","Arboviruses (West Nile, St. Louis, eastern and western equine) cycle between mosquitoes and birds; humans are dead-end hosts. West Nile can cause acute flaccid paralysis in older adults.","Rabies travels retrogradely up peripheral nerves after a bat, raccoon, or dog bite; Negri bodies in neurons, agitation, and hydrophobia from painful pharyngeal spasm.","Brain abscess spreads from a nearby sinus, ear, mastoid, or tooth, or seeds by blood from endocarditis, lung infection, or a right-to-left shunt that bypasses the lung filter.","Abscess organisms: streptococci, staphylococci, and anaerobes; a ring-enhancing lesion with headache, fever, and focal deficits. Lumbar puncture risks herniation."]},{"h":"Fungi and parasites","points":["Cryptococcus in AIDS (low CD4 count): subacute headache, raised opening pressure, positive cryptococcal capsular antigen, India ink shows the capsule.","Mucormycosis in diabetic ketoacidosis: hyphae invade vessels, causing a black necrotic eschar of the nose or palate that spreads to the orbit and brain.","Toxoplasma gondii in AIDS: multiple ring-enhancing lesions; from cat feces or undercooked meat.","Neurocysticercosis: swallowed Taenia solium eggs (fecal-oral) become brain cysts that cause seizures.","Naegleria fowleri: warm freshwater swimming; the amoeba enters through the cribriform plate and causes rapidly fatal meningoencephalitis."]},{"h":"Herpesviruses in the nervous system","points":["HSV-2: aseptic meningitis in adults and neonatal encephalitis after birth canal exposure.","CMV: encephalitis and retinitis in advanced AIDS; congenital CMV gives periventricular calcifications and hearing loss.","EBV: primary CNS lymphoma in AIDS.","VZV stays latent in dorsal root and cranial nerve ganglia and reactivates as painful dermatomal shingles.","Zoster ophthalmicus involves V1 and can damage the eye; Ramsay Hunt syndrome (geniculate ganglion) gives facial palsy with vesicles in the ear.","Postherpetic neuralgia is pain that persists after the rash heals, more common in older adults."]},{"h":"Toxins, slow viruses, and prions","points":["Botulinum toxin blocks acetylcholine release: descending symmetric flaccid paralysis starting with cranial nerves, with no fever and no sensory loss.","Foodborne botulism comes from preformed toxin; infant botulism from swallowed spores (honey) that make toxin in the gut; wound botulism from spores growing in a wound.","Tetanus toxin travels to the spinal cord and blocks GABA and glycine release from inhibitory interneurons: trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonus, spastic paralysis.","Polio: fecal-oral enterovirus that destroys anterior horn cells, causing asymmetric flaccid paralysis with areflexia and normal sensation.","Prion disease: normal alpha-helical PrP refolds into protease-resistant beta-pleated PrP; rapid dementia, myoclonus, spongiform change with no inflammation.","PML: JC virus reactivates in severe immunosuppression and destroys oligodendrocytes, causing multifocal white matter demyelination.","SSPE: defective measles virus persists after early childhood measles and appears years later as cognitive decline and myoclonus."]},{"h":"Leprosy and neurosyphilis","points":["M. leprae prefers cool body sites and invades Schwann cells of peripheral nerves.","Tuberculoid leprosy: strong Th1 response, few bacilli, a few anesthetic hypopigmented plaques, thickened nerves.","Lepromatous leprosy: Th2 response, many bacilli, leonine facies, symmetric glove-and-stocking neuropathy.","Tertiary neurosyphilis: meningovascular disease (strokes), general paresis (dementia, personality change), and tabes dorsalis.","Tabes dorsalis destroys dorsal roots and dorsal columns: sensory ataxia, positive Romberg sign, Argyll Robertson pupils, Charcot joints. A positive CSF VDRL confirms neurosyphilis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"CSF patterns in meningitis","cols":["Cause","Opening pressure","Main cell type","Glucose","Protein"],"rows":[["Bacterial","High","Neutrophils","Low","High"],["Viral (aseptic)","Normal or mildly high","Lymphocytes","Normal","Normal or mildly high"],["Fungal (cryptococcal)","High, often very high","Lymphocytes","Low or normal","High"],["Tuberculous","High","Lymphocytes","Low","High, often very high"]]},{"title":"Nervous system infections by condition","cols":["Condition","Organism and route","Key mechanism or lesion","Classic findings"],"rows":[["Arboviral encephalitis","West Nile, St. Louis, eastern and western equine viruses; mosquito from bird reservoir","Neuronal infection; anterior horn involvement in West Nile","Fever, encephalitis; flaccid paralysis in older adults"],["Botulism","C. botulinum toxin: preformed (food), spores in infant gut, or wound","Toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and blocks acetylcholine release","Descending flaccid paralysis, diplopia, ptosis; infant constipation and hypotonia"],["Brain abscess","Streptococci, staphylococci, anaerobes; contiguous or hematogenous","Cerebritis then encapsulated pus with edema","Headache, fever, focal deficit, ring-enhancing lesion"],["Encephalitis (including rabies)","Many viruses; rabies by animal bite","Perivascular cuffs, microglial nodules; Negri bodies in rabies","Fever, confusion, seizures; agitation and hydrophobia in rabies"],["Fungal infection","Cryptococcus (inhaled) in AIDS; Mucorales in diabetic ketoacidosis","Capsule; vascular invasion with necrosis","Raised opening pressure; black nasal eschar"],["Herpesviruses","HSV-1, HSV-2, CMV, EBV","Temporal lobe necrosis (HSV-1); periventricular disease (CMV)","Confusion, aphasia; neonatal disease; lymphoma (EBV)"],["Leprosy","M. leprae; prolonged close contact","Schwann cell infection; Th1 or Th2 response","Anesthetic skin patches, thickened nerves"],["Bacterial meningitis","Age-based organisms; nasopharynx then blood","Neutrophilic subarachnoid exudate","Fever, neck stiffness, photophobia; hearing loss later"],["Neurosyphilis","T. pallidum, years after primary infection","Endarteritis, cortical atrophy, dorsal column loss","Strokes, dementia, sensory ataxia, Argyll Robertson pupils"],["Poliomyelitis","Poliovirus, fecal-oral","Anterior horn cell destruction","Asymmetric flaccid paralysis, no sensory loss"],["Prion disease","Sporadic, inherited PRNP, or acquired (iatrogenic, variant CJD)","Misfolded beta-pleated PrP, spongiform change","Rapid dementia, startle myoclonus"],["PML","JC virus reactivation (AIDS, natalizumab, rituximab)","Lytic infection of oligodendrocytes","Multifocal white matter lesions, rapid deficits"],["SSPE","Persistent defective measles virus","Slow spread through neurons and glia","Cognitive decline, myoclonus, high CSF anti-measles antibody"],["Tetanus","C. tetani spores in a wound or umbilical stump","Toxin blocks GABA and glycine release in the cord","Trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonus"],["Varicella zoster virus","Reactivation from sensory ganglia","Ganglionitis and neuritis","Dermatomal vesicles, facial palsy in Ramsay Hunt, postherpetic neuralgia"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Botulism goes down, tetanus locks up","text":"Botulism blocks acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, so muscles go limp from the head downward. Tetanus blocks the inhibitory neurons in the cord, so muscles stay contracted."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tuberculoid tight, lepromatous loose","text":"A strong Th1 response holds the bacilli tightly in a few granulomas (tuberculoid). A weak Th2 response lets bacilli spread loosely through skin and nerves (lepromatous)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Read the host first","text":"Neonate, older adult, AIDS patient, diabetic in ketoacidosis, or patient on natalizumab: each host narrows the organism list before you read the rest of the stem."}],"highYield":["Neonatal meningitis: group B streptococcus, E. coli, Listeria.","Bacterial CSF: neutrophils, low glucose, high protein.","HSV-1 encephalitis targets the temporal lobes and is hemorrhagic.","Infant botulism comes from spores (honey); adult foodborne botulism comes from preformed toxin.","Tetanus toxin blocks GABA and glycine release from inhibitory interneurons.","PML: JC virus kills oligodendrocytes in AIDS or on natalizumab.","Prions cause spongiform change without inflammation.","Argyll Robertson pupils accommodate but do not react to light (tabes dorsalis)."],"pitfalls":["Eating Taenia solium cysts in undercooked pork gives an intestinal tapeworm; eating eggs from a human carrier gives neurocysticercosis.","Polio and Guillain-Barré both cause flaccid weakness; polio is asymmetric with CSF lymphocytes, while Guillain-Barré is symmetric and ascending with high CSF protein and few cells.","Botulism descends and starts with cranial nerves; Guillain-Barré usually ascends from the legs and causes sensory symptoms.","Toxoplasmosis and CNS lymphoma both ring-enhance in AIDS; PML lesions are white matter lesions that usually do not enhance.","Prion disease and SSPE both cause dementia with myoclonus; SSPE follows childhood measles and has high CSF measles antibody."],"dumps":[{"t":"Meningitis by age and CSF","p":"Draw a timeline from birth to old age and write the common bacterial meningitis organisms under each age group. Then build a CSF table for bacterial, viral, fungal, and tuberculous meningitis.","l":["Neonates: group B streptococcus, E. coli, Listeria","Children and adults: S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis","Older adults: S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, Listeria","Bacterial: neutrophils, low glucose, high protein","Viral: lymphocytes, normal glucose","Fungal and tuberculous: lymphocytes, low glucose, high protein","Complications: hearing loss, hydrocephalus, seizures, Waterhouse-Friderichsen"]},{"t":"How toxins and viruses reach nerves","p":"Draw a motor neuron running from the spinal cord to a muscle. Mark where botulinum toxin, tetanus toxin, poliovirus, rabies, and VZV act, and write the result of each.","l":["Botulinum toxin at the neuromuscular junction blocks acetylcholine release, causing flaccid paralysis","Tetanus toxin travels retrogradely to inhibitory interneurons and blocks GABA and glycine, causing spastic paralysis","Poliovirus destroys anterior horn cells, causing asymmetric flaccid paralysis","Rabies travels retrogradely along axons to the CNS","VZV lies latent in the dorsal root ganglion and reactivates as dermatomal shingles","Sensation stays normal in botulism, tetanus, and polio","Toxoid vaccine prevents tetanus"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System (section on infections)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on viral infections of the central nervous system"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on clostridia (botulism and tetanus)"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"section on Th1 and Th2 responses"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-19","NE-18","NE-4","IM-15-B2","IM-15-F4","EN-12-H2","NE-20-B4"]},"NE-20-H":{"id":"NE-20-H","summary":"Trauma to the brain and spinal cord is tested as a sequence: what the force did, what bled or tore, and how swelling or herniation leads to new signs. Know concussion versus contusion versus diffuse axonal injury, the epidural and subdural hematoma pictures, uncal herniation, and the phases and levels of spinal cord injury.","notes":[{"h":"Concussion and repeated injury","points":["Concussion: trauma-induced, transient loss of normal brain function (confusion, amnesia, sometimes brief loss of consciousness) with normal imaging.","Postconcussion syndrome: headache, dizziness, poor concentration, irritability, and sleep problems that last weeks to months.","Chronic traumatic encephalopathy (dementia pugilistica) follows repeated head injury: memory loss, mood and behavior change, parkinsonism.","CTE pathology: brain atrophy and tau deposits around vessels at the depths of cortical sulci."]},{"h":"Contusion and diffuse axonal injury","points":["Contusion is a bruise of brain tissue: hemorrhage and necrosis at the crests of gyri.","Coup injury is at the site of impact; contrecoup injury is on the opposite side as the brain rebounds.","The frontal and temporal poles are bruised most because they strike the rough skull base.","Diffuse axonal injury comes from rotational acceleration-deceleration that shears axons, especially in the corpus callosum and brainstem.","Axonal swellings form within hours; the patient is in a coma even though CT shows no mass lesion."]},{"h":"Hematomas","points":["Epidural hematoma: temporal bone fracture tears the middle meningeal artery; arterial blood strips dura off bone.","Epidural: lens-shaped (biconvex), stopped by suture lines, often a lucid interval then rapid decline.","Subdural hematoma: torn bridging veins between the cortex and dural sinuses; crescent-shaped and crosses suture lines.","Subdural risk: brain atrophy in older adults and people with alcohol use disorder, anticoagulants, and shaken infants.","Chronic subdural hematoma develops over weeks with fluctuating confusion and can rebleed.","Trauma can also cause subarachnoid and intraparenchymal hemorrhage."]},{"h":"Rising pressure and herniation","points":["The skull is a fixed box, so an expanding hematoma plus edema raises intracranial pressure and lowers cerebral perfusion pressure (MAP minus ICP).","Uncal herniation: the medial temporal lobe slides under the tentorium and compresses the ipsilateral CN III (dilated, fixed pupil, then down and out).","It also compresses the ipsilateral cerebral peduncle, giving contralateral hemiparesis.","Downward brainstem shift tears small pontine and midbrain branches of the basilar artery: Duret hemorrhages, usually fatal.","Cushing reflex (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) is a late sign; tonsillar herniation compresses the medulla and stops breathing."]},{"h":"Spinal cord injury","points":["Spinal shock comes first: flaccid paralysis, areflexia, sensory loss below the level, and urinary retention.","Neurogenic shock (hypotension with bradycardia) follows high injury because sympathetic outflow (T1 to L2) loses its brain input.","Over days to weeks, reflexes return as upper motor neuron signs: spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign.","The phrenic nerve comes from C3 to C5, so complete injury at or above C3 leaves a ventilator-dependent quadriplegic patient.","Cervical injury below C5 gives quadriplegia with spontaneous breathing; thoracic injury gives paraplegia.","Cord compression (metastasis, epidural abscess, disc): back pain, a sensory level, and upper motor neuron signs.","Cauda equina syndrome (below the cord, which ends about L1 to L2): saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, lost anal reflex, and lower motor neuron leg weakness."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Traumatic brain injury types","cols":["Injury","What tears or fails","Imaging","Classic course"],"rows":[["Concussion","Temporary functional disturbance, no structural lesion","Normal","Confusion or amnesia that resolves; possible postconcussion syndrome"],["Contusion","Small vessels and tissue at gyral crests (coup and contrecoup)","Hemorrhagic foci in frontal and temporal poles","Focal deficits, seizures"],["Diffuse axonal injury","Axons sheared by rotational force","Often normal or small hemorrhages in corpus callosum and brainstem","Immediate coma without a mass lesion"],["Epidural hematoma","Middle meningeal artery after temporal bone fracture","Biconvex, does not cross sutures","Lucid interval then rapid decline"],["Subdural hematoma","Bridging veins","Crescent, crosses sutures","Gradual or fluctuating decline; acute or chronic"],["Chronic traumatic encephalopathy","Neurons with tau deposits after repeated injury","Atrophy","Progressive dementia, mood change, parkinsonism"]]},{"title":"Spinal cord compression versus cauda equina syndrome","cols":["Feature","Spinal cord compression","Cauda equina syndrome"],"rows":[["Level","Above the conus (cord ends about L1 to L2)","Lumbar and sacral nerve roots below the cord"],["Motor signs","Upper motor neuron: spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski (after spinal shock)","Lower motor neuron: flaccid, often asymmetric leg weakness, reduced reflexes"],["Sensory","Sensory level on the trunk","Saddle anesthesia, radicular leg pain"],["Bladder and bowel","Dysfunction, often later","Urinary retention early, lost anal reflex and sphincter tone"],["Common causes","Vertebral metastasis (lung, breast, prostate), epidural abscess, trauma","Large central disc herniation, tumor, epidural abscess"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Epidural is arterial and fast","text":"Epidural blood is arterial (middle meningeal), so it expands fast after a lucid interval. Subdural blood is venous (bridging veins), so it often builds slowly, especially in an atrophic brain."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive","text":"The phrenic nerve comes from C3, C4, and C5. A complete cord injury at or above C3 stops diaphragm breathing."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Blown pupil side","text":"In uncal herniation the dilated pupil is on the same side as the mass, and the weakness is on the opposite side from the compressed peduncle."}],"highYield":["Concussion has normal imaging; diffuse axonal injury causes coma with little on CT.","Contusions favor the frontal and temporal poles.","Epidural hematoma: middle meningeal artery, temporal bone fracture, lucid interval, biconvex.","Subdural hematoma: bridging veins, crescent shape, older adults and shaken infants.","Uncal herniation: ipsilateral fixed dilated pupil, contralateral hemiparesis, then Duret hemorrhages.","Spinal shock gives flaccid areflexia first; spasticity and hyperreflexia come later.","Cauda equina syndrome: saddle anesthesia and urinary retention."],"pitfalls":["A lucid interval is classic for epidural hematoma but is not always present, and it can occur with subdural hematoma.","Spinal shock looks like a lower motor neuron lesion at first; the upper motor neuron picture appears only as reflexes return.","Neurogenic shock (bradycardia, warm skin) differs from hemorrhagic shock (tachycardia, cold skin).","Kernohan notch: compression of the opposite cerebral peduncle against the tentorium can cause weakness on the same side as the mass, a false localizing sign."],"dumps":[{"t":"Expanding hematoma to death","p":"Draw a coronal section of the brain with a temporal epidural hematoma. Use arrows to trace how it leads to herniation and death, and label each sign along the way.","l":["Temporal bone fracture tears the middle meningeal artery","Biconvex arterial clot between dura and skull","Rising intracranial pressure and falling perfusion pressure","Uncus herniates under the tentorium","Ipsilateral CN III compression: fixed dilated pupil","Ipsilateral cerebral peduncle compression: contralateral hemiparesis","Brainstem shifts down and Duret hemorrhages form","Cushing reflex and respiratory arrest"]},{"t":"Spinal cord injury levels","p":"Draw the spinal cord from C1 to the cauda equina. Mark C3 to C5, the sympathetic outflow, and where the cord ends, then write what a complete injury at C2, C6, T10, and below L2 would cause.","l":["C2: quadriplegia and ventilator dependence (above phrenic roots)","C6: quadriplegia with diaphragm breathing","T10: paraplegia","Below L2: cauda equina syndrome with lower motor neuron signs","Sympathetic outflow T1 to L2; high injury causes neurogenic shock","Spinal shock first, then spasticity and hyperreflexia","Cord ends about L1 to L2"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System (section on trauma)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back (section on the spinal cord and spinal nerve roots)"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-20-A4","NE-20-C1","NE-10","NE-3","NE-7","NE-8"]},"NE-20-I":{"id":"NE-20-I","summary":"Peripheral nerve and root problems are anatomy questions in disguise. Each one asks which nerve or root is pinched, where it runs, and what it supplies, so the exam can predict the exact numb area, weak muscle, and lost reflex. Learn the cranial nerve palsies, the wrist and elbow tunnels, and the cervical and lumbar root levels.","notes":[{"h":"Cranial nerve disorders","points":["Bell palsy: idiopathic lower motor neuron CN VII palsy, linked to HSV reactivation, diabetes, pregnancy, and Lyme disease.","Bell palsy weakens the whole half of the face, forehead included; a stroke (upper motor neuron) spares the forehead because the upper face has input from both hemispheres.","Branches inside the facial canal explain the extras: less tearing (greater petrosal), hyperacusis (stapedius), lost taste on the front two-thirds of the tongue (chorda tympani).","Trigeminal neuralgia: seconds-long electric-shock pain in V2 or V3, triggered by touch, chewing, or brushing teeth.","Usual cause is a blood vessel loop pressing on the trigeminal root; in a young patient, think of a multiple sclerosis plaque."]},{"h":"Upper limb entrapments","points":["Carpal tunnel syndrome: median nerve squeezed under the flexor retinaculum.","Risks: pregnancy, hypothyroidism, rheumatoid arthritis, diabetes, acromegaly, dialysis-related amyloid, repetitive wrist use.","Findings: night paresthesia of the thumb, index, middle, and half the ring finger; thenar atrophy; positive Tinel and Phalen signs.","Palm sensation is spared because the palmar cutaneous branch leaves before the tunnel and runs over the retinaculum.","Cubital tunnel: ulnar nerve at the medial epicondyle; numb ring and little fingers, weak intrinsic hand muscles.","Thoracic outlet syndrome: cervical rib or scalene muscles compress the lower trunk (C8 to T1), subclavian artery, or vein.","TOS gives ulnar-side hand paresthesia and intrinsic hand weakness, with arm pallor, coolness, or swelling when the vessels are involved, often worse with the arm overhead."]},{"h":"Discs and roots","points":["Disc herniation: the nucleus pulposus pushes through a torn annulus fibrosus, usually posterolaterally at L4 to L5 or L5 to S1.","A posterolateral lumbar herniation hits the lower root: an L4 to L5 disc compresses L5, and an L5 to S1 disc compresses S1.","Cervical roots exit above their vertebra, so a C5 to C6 disc compresses C6 and a C6 to C7 disc compresses C7.","Radiculopathy (root entrapment by disc, osteophytes, or foraminal narrowing) gives dermatomal pain, myotomal weakness, and a lost reflex.","Sciatica: pain from the buttock down the back of the thigh and below the knee, usually from L5 or S1; a straight leg raise reproduces it."]},{"h":"Lower limb entrapments","points":["Common fibular nerve at the fibular neck (fracture, leg crossing, tight cast): foot drop, weak eversion, numb lateral leg and dorsum of foot.","Inversion is spared in fibular nerve palsy (tibialis posterior is tibial) but weak in L5 radiculopathy, which tells them apart.","Tarsal tunnel syndrome: tibial nerve behind the medial malleolus; burning and numbness of the sole."]},{"h":"Stenosis and pain syndromes","points":["Lumbar spinal stenosis: neurogenic claudication, with leg pain on standing and walking that eases with sitting or bending forward; pulses are normal.","Cervical stenosis compresses the cord: myelopathy with clumsy hands, unsteady gait, and upper motor neuron signs in the legs.","Complex regional pain syndrome: burning pain out of proportion, allodynia, swelling, and skin color, temperature, and sweating changes after a limb injury.","CRPS type I has no identifiable nerve injury; type II follows a nerve injury.","Phantom limb pain is pain felt in an amputated limb, linked to cortical remapping and nerve-end neuromas."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Root lesions","cols":["Root","Sensory loss or pain","Weakness","Reflex lost"],"rows":[["C6","Lateral forearm, thumb, index finger","Elbow flexion, wrist extension","Biceps, brachioradialis"],["C7","Middle finger","Elbow extension (triceps), wrist flexion, finger extension","Triceps"],["L4","Anterior knee and medial leg","Knee extension (quadriceps), ankle dorsiflexion","Patellar"],["L5","Lateral leg, dorsum of foot, big toe","Big toe extension, ankle dorsiflexion and inversion, hip abduction","No reliable reflex"],["S1","Lateral foot, sole, little toe","Plantar flexion (gastrocnemius)","Achilles"]]},{"title":"Peripheral compression syndromes","cols":["Condition","Structure compressed and site","Main risks or causes","Classic findings"],"rows":[["Bell palsy","CN VII in the facial canal","HSV reactivation, diabetes, pregnancy, Lyme disease","Whole half-face weakness, hyperacusis, lost anterior taste, dry eye"],["Carpal tunnel syndrome","Median nerve under flexor retinaculum","Pregnancy, hypothyroidism, RA, diabetes, dialysis amyloid","Night paresthesia of lateral 3.5 fingers, thenar atrophy, palm spared"],["Disc herniation","Nucleus pulposus through annulus fibrosus","Age-related disc degeneration, lifting","Lower root compressed (L4 to L5 disc hits L5)"],["Nerve root entrapment","Root in the foramen","Disc, osteophytes, foraminal narrowing","Dermatomal pain, weakness, lost reflex"],["Sciatica","L5 or S1 root","Lumbar disc herniation","Pain below the knee, positive straight leg raise"],["Thoracic outlet syndrome","Lower trunk C8 to T1, subclavian artery or vein","Cervical rib, scalene muscles","Ulnar-side paresthesia, intrinsic weakness, arm pallor or swelling"],["Trigeminal neuralgia","CN V root entry zone","Vascular loop; MS in young patients","Brief lancinating V2 or V3 pain with trigger zones"],["Cubital tunnel syndrome","Ulnar nerve at the elbow","Leaning on elbows, elbow flexion","Fourth and fifth finger paresthesia, weak interossei"],["Tarsal tunnel syndrome","Tibial nerve behind medial malleolus","Ankle injury, swelling","Burning numb sole"],["Common fibular palsy","Common fibular nerve at fibular neck","Fibular neck fracture, leg crossing, cast","Foot drop, weak eversion, numb dorsum of foot"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Forehead test","text":"Can the patient wrinkle the forehead? If yes, the lesion is upper motor neuron (stroke). If no, the facial nerve itself is damaged (Bell palsy)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lumbar disc hits the next root down","text":"Name the lower vertebra in the disc level and that is the root hit by a typical posterolateral herniation: L4 to L5 gives L5, L5 to S1 gives S1."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"S1, S2 buckle my shoe; L3, L4 kick the door; C5, C6 pick up sticks; C7, C8 lay them straight","text":"Reflex levels: Achilles S1 to S2, patellar L3 to L4, biceps C5 to C6, triceps C7 to C8."}],"highYield":["Bell palsy paralyzes the forehead; a stroke spares it.","Carpal tunnel spares palm sensation because the palmar cutaneous branch runs above the retinaculum.","An L5 to S1 disc herniation compresses S1: lost Achilles reflex, weak plantar flexion.","L5 root lesion: weak big toe extension and foot dorsiflexion, numb dorsum of foot.","Thoracic outlet syndrome affects C8 to T1, so symptoms are on the ulnar side of the hand.","Trigeminal neuralgia in a young adult suggests multiple sclerosis.","Neurogenic claudication eases with bending forward; vascular claudication eases with stopping, regardless of posture."],"pitfalls":["Foot drop from common fibular palsy spares inversion; foot drop from L5 radiculopathy weakens inversion.","Carpal tunnel numbness spares the palm; a more proximal median nerve lesion numbs the palm too.","Cubital tunnel syndrome numbs the dorsal ulnar hand; ulnar compression at the wrist (Guyon canal) spares it because the dorsal branch leaves above the wrist.","Cervical stenosis produces upper motor neuron signs in the legs; lumbar stenosis produces only root symptoms.","Thoracic outlet syndrome and cubital tunnel syndrome both numb the ulnar fingers; TOS also numbs the medial forearm and can add arm pallor or swelling."],"dumps":[{"t":"Root map","p":"Draw the outline of an arm and a leg. Shade the dermatomes for C6, C7, L4, L5, and S1, and next to each write the weak muscle and the reflex that is lost.","l":["C6: thumb and lateral forearm; elbow flexion and wrist extension; biceps and brachioradialis reflexes","C7: middle finger; triceps; triceps reflex","L4: medial leg; quadriceps; patellar reflex","L5: dorsum of foot and big toe; big toe extension and dorsiflexion; no reliable reflex","S1: lateral foot and sole; plantar flexion; Achilles reflex","Lumbar posterolateral disc hits the lower root","Cervical C5 to C6 disc hits C6"]},{"t":"Facial nerve lesion","p":"Trace the facial nerve from the pons through the facial canal to the face. Label each branch and write what a patient loses with Bell palsy and why a stroke looks different.","l":["Greater petrosal nerve: lacrimal gland, reduced tearing","Nerve to stapedius: hyperacusis","Chorda tympani: taste on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue","Motor branches: whole half-face weakness including the forehead","Corneal exposure because the eye cannot close","Upper motor neuron lesion spares the forehead (bilateral cortical input)","Associations: HSV, diabetes, pregnancy, Lyme disease"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb (section on the carpal tunnel and nerve compression)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back (section on intervertebral discs)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the facial and trigeminal nerves)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-8","NE-7","NE-5","NE-20-B5","NE-20-G15","NE-20-H2","MS-13-E1"]},"NE-20-J":{"id":"NE-20-J","summary":"Eye and ear diseases are tested by matching the symptom pattern (pain or no pain, sudden or gradual, central or peripheral vision, hearing loss or not) to the structure that failed. Learn the red eye differential, the causes of vision loss, the retinal findings of systemic disease, and the vertigo and hearing loss patterns.","notes":[{"h":"Lids, conjunctiva, and cornea","points":["Blepharitis: anterior from staphylococcal or seborrheic disease at the lash base; posterior from meibomian gland dysfunction.","Hordeolum (stye) is an acute, painful gland infection, usually S. aureus; a chalazion is a painless meibomian lipogranuloma.","Keratitis: HSV gives a branching dendritic ulcer; contact lens wearers get Pseudomonas or Acanthamoeba ulcers.","Pterygium is a UV-related fibrovascular growth of conjunctiva onto the cornea, usually on the nasal side.","Corneal abrasion and corneal foreign body take up fluorescein; subconjunctival hemorrhage is a painless red patch with normal vision.","Alkali burns cause liquefactive necrosis and penetrate deeper than acid burns, which cause coagulative necrosis."]},{"h":"Lens, glaucoma, and uvea","points":["Cataract is opacified lens protein: painless gradual blur, glare, loss of the red reflex.","Cataract risks: age, diabetes (sorbitol), galactosemia (galactitol), corticosteroids, UV light, smoking, congenital rubella.","Glaucoma is an optic neuropathy with cupping of the optic disc and visual field loss.","Open-angle glaucoma: poor outflow through the trabecular meshwork, painless loss of peripheral vision.","Acute angle-closure glaucoma: the iris blocks the angle; painful red eye, halos, nausea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil. Pupil dilation can trigger it.","Anterior uveitis (iritis): ciliary flush, photophobia, small irregular pupil; linked to HLA-B27 disease, sarcoidosis, and juvenile idiopathic arthritis.","Posterior uveitis and chorioretinitis: toxoplasmosis, and CMV retinitis in AIDS.","Lens dislocation: upward in Marfan syndrome, downward in homocystinuria."]},{"h":"Retina","points":["Dry macular degeneration: drusen and slow central vision loss. Wet: VEGF-driven choroidal neovessels that leak and bleed, causing rapid central loss and distortion.","Age and smoking are the main risks for macular degeneration.","Central retinal artery occlusion: sudden painless monocular loss, pale retina, cherry-red spot. Amaurosis fugax is a brief version from carotid emboli.","Central retinal vein occlusion: diffuse retinal hemorrhages and swollen, tortuous veins.","Retinal detachment: flashes, floaters, then a curtain over the vision; myopia raises the risk.","Retinitis pigmentosa: inherited photoreceptor loss with night blindness, tunnel vision, and bone spicule pigment."]},{"h":"Ear and balance","points":["Acute otitis media: eustachian tube dysfunction in children lets middle ear fluid get infected (S. pneumoniae, nontypeable H. influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis).","Otitis media complications: perforation, mastoiditis, cholesteatoma, and conductive hearing loss; otitis media with effusion is fluid without acute infection.","Otitis externa: Pseudomonas or S. aureus, pain on pulling the pinna or pressing the tragus.","Malignant otitis externa: Pseudomonas skull base osteomyelitis in older diabetics, with cranial nerve palsies (often CN VII).","Auricular perichondritis: infection of the ear cartilage, often Pseudomonas, after a high (cartilage) ear piercing; red, painful, swollen pinna that spares the lobule.","Ménière disease: endolymphatic hydrops with vertigo lasting hours, fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss, tinnitus, and ear fullness.","Vestibular neuritis: continuous vertigo for days with normal hearing; labyrinthitis adds hearing loss.","BPPV: loose otoconia in the posterior canal give brief vertigo with head movement and a positive Dix-Hallpike test."]},{"h":"Vision and hearing basics","points":["Myopia focuses in front of the retina (long eye); hyperopia behind it (short eye); astigmatism comes from uneven corneal curvature.","Presbyopia is age-related loss of lens elasticity that makes near vision hard.","Childhood strabismus causes amblyopia because the brain suppresses the deviating eye's input.","Conductive hearing loss blocks sound before the cochlea; sensorineural loss damages hair cells or CN VIII. Both can cause tinnitus."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Eye conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk","Key mechanism","Classic findings"],"rows":[["Blepharitis","Staphylococci, seborrhea, meibomian gland dysfunction","Chronic lid margin inflammation","Crusted, red lid margins, burning"],["Cataract","Age, diabetes, galactosemia, steroids, UV, smoking, congenital rubella","Lens protein opacification; sorbitol or galactitol draw water into the lens","Painless blur, glare, lost red reflex"],["Conjunctivitis","Adenovirus, bacteria, Chlamydia, allergens","Conjunctival inflammation","Red eye with discharge, normal vision"],["Glaucoma","Open-angle: age, family history; angle-closure: narrow angle, pupil dilation","Optic nerve damage, often from high intraocular pressure","Disc cupping and field loss; acute closure is painful with a mid-dilated pupil"],["Iritis and keratitis","HLA-B27 disease (iritis); HSV, contact lenses (keratitis)","Anterior uveal or corneal inflammation","Ciliary flush, photophobia; dendritic or corneal ulcer"],["Macular degeneration","Age, smoking","Drusen (dry) or VEGF-driven choroidal neovascularization (wet)","Central vision loss, distortion in wet form"],["Uveitis","Anterior: HLA-B27, sarcoidosis, JIA; posterior: toxoplasmosis, CMV","Inflammation of iris, ciliary body, or choroid","Pain and photophobia (anterior); floaters and blurred vision (posterior)"]]},{"title":"Conjunctivitis types","cols":["Type","Cause","Discharge and clues"],"rows":[["Viral","Adenovirus","Watery, preauricular lymph node, very contagious"],["Bacterial","S. aureus, S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae","Purulent, lids stuck together in the morning"],["Hyperacute bacterial","N. gonorrhoeae","Copious pus, rapid onset, risk of corneal perforation"],["Chlamydial","C. trachomatis (serotypes D to K inclusion conjunctivitis; A to C trachoma)","Chronic follicular conjunctivitis; trachoma scars the lid and cornea and causes blindness"],["Allergic","Type I hypersensitivity (IgE, mast cells)","Itching, watery, both eyes, seasonal"]]},{"title":"Retinal findings","cols":["Condition","Fundus findings","Symptoms"],"rows":[["Hypertensive retinopathy","Arteriolar narrowing, AV nicking, flame hemorrhages, cotton wool spots","Often none until severe"],["Diabetic retinopathy","Microaneurysms, dot-blot hemorrhages, hard exudates; neovascularization when proliferative","Gradual loss; vitreous hemorrhage or detachment when proliferative"],["Papilledema","Bilateral swollen discs with blurred margins","Headache from raised intracranial pressure, vision usually normal early"],["Central retinal artery occlusion","Pale retina, cherry-red spot","Sudden painless monocular loss"],["Central retinal vein occlusion","Diffuse hemorrhages, dilated tortuous veins","Sudden or subacute painless blur"],["Retinal detachment","Gray, elevated retina","Flashes, floaters, curtain"],["Retinitis pigmentosa","Bone spicule pigment, thin vessels","Night blindness, tunnel vision"]]},{"title":"Ear conditions and vertigo","cols":["Condition","Cause","Vertigo pattern","Hearing"],"rows":[["Acute otitis media","S. pneumoniae, nontypeable H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis","None usually","Conductive loss, bulging red eardrum"],["Otitis externa","Pseudomonas, S. aureus","None","Mild conductive loss from canal swelling"],["Malignant otitis externa","Pseudomonas in older diabetics","None","Conductive loss from canal swelling; deep pain and cranial nerve palsies"],["Ménière disease","Endolymphatic hydrops","Episodes lasting hours","Fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss, tinnitus, fullness"],["Vestibular neuritis","Inflamed vestibular nerve, often after a viral illness","Continuous for days","Normal"],["Labyrinthitis","Inflamed inner ear","Continuous for days","Sensorineural loss"],["BPPV","Otoconia in the posterior semicircular canal","Seconds, triggered by head position","Normal"]]},{"title":"Hearing loss and other eye problems","cols":["Category","Examples","Key point"],"rows":[["Conductive hearing loss","Cerumen, perforation, barotrauma, eustachian tube dysfunction, otosclerosis (stapes fixation), cholesteatoma","Weber lateralizes to the bad ear; bone conduction beats air conduction"],["Sensorineural hearing loss","Presbycusis (high frequencies), noise-induced hair cell damage","Weber lateralizes to the good ear; air conduction beats bone conduction"],["Orbital infection","Preseptal cellulitis versus orbital cellulitis (often from ethmoid sinusitis)","Pain with eye movement, proptosis, and ophthalmoplegia mean orbital"],["Other eye infection","Dacryocystitis (blocked nasolacrimal duct); endophthalmitis (after surgery or trauma)","Endophthalmitis threatens vision"],["Eye trauma","Hyphema (blood in the anterior chamber); orbital floor fracture","Floor fracture traps inferior rectus and limits upward gaze"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Pupil in a painful red eye","text":"Small and irregular means iritis. Fixed and mid-dilated means acute angle closure. A normal pupil with discharge points to conjunctivitis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Time the vertigo","text":"Seconds with head movement is BPPV. Hours with hearing changes is Ménière disease. Days of constant vertigo is vestibular neuritis, or labyrinthitis if hearing is also lost."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sugar alcohols cloud the lens","text":"Aldose reductase turns glucose into sorbitol and galactose into galactitol. These pull water into the lens, so diabetes and galactosemia both cause cataracts."}],"highYield":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma: painful red eye, halos, fixed mid-dilated pupil; dilation triggers it.","Wet macular degeneration is VEGF-driven choroidal neovascularization.","Cherry-red spot plus sudden painless monocular loss means central retinal artery occlusion.","Anterior uveitis links to HLA-B27, sarcoidosis, and JIA.","HSV keratitis gives a dendritic ulcer on fluorescein.","Ménière disease: episodic vertigo, low-frequency hearing loss, tinnitus, fullness.","Malignant otitis externa: Pseudomonas, older diabetic, cranial nerve palsies.","Orbital floor fracture traps the inferior rectus and limits upward gaze."],"pitfalls":["Hordeolum hurts and is infected; chalazion is painless and granulomatous.","Vestibular neuritis and labyrinthitis feel the same, but only labyrinthitis causes hearing loss.","Preseptal cellulitis can look dramatic, but eye movement and vision stay normal; orbital cellulitis limits eye movement.","Open-angle glaucoma is painless and steals peripheral vision first; macular degeneration steals central vision first.","Marfan lens goes up, homocystinuria lens goes down."],"dumps":[{"t":"Red eye sorting","p":"Build a table with rows for viral, bacterial, gonococcal, and allergic conjunctivitis, keratitis, anterior uveitis, and acute angle-closure glaucoma. Columns: pain, vision, pupil, discharge, key clue.","l":["Viral: watery, preauricular node, normal vision","Bacterial: purulent; gonococcal is hyperacute with heavy pus","Allergic: itching, both eyes","Keratitis: pain, fluorescein uptake, dendrite in HSV, contact lens link","Anterior uveitis: ciliary flush, photophobia, small irregular pupil","Angle closure: severe pain, halos, nausea, fixed mid-dilated pupil","Vision is normal in conjunctivitis and reduced in the others"]},{"t":"Ear anatomy and disease","p":"Draw the outer, middle, and inner ear. Label where otitis externa, otitis media, otosclerosis, cholesteatoma, Ménière disease, BPPV, and vestibular neuritis occur, and mark each as conductive or sensorineural if it causes hearing loss.","l":["Outer canal: otitis externa (Pseudomonas, tragal pain)","Middle ear: otitis media from eustachian tube dysfunction; conductive loss","Stapes footplate fixed at the oval window in otosclerosis; conductive","Cholesteatoma erodes ossicles; conductive","Cochlea and endolymph: Ménière disease; sensorineural","Posterior semicircular canal: BPPV otoconia","Vestibular nerve: vestibular neuritis with normal hearing","Weber and Rinne patterns for each type of loss"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Eye"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Eye: I. Optics of Vision"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Sense of Hearing"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck (section on the ear)"}],"videos":[],"related":["NE-6","NE-8","MS-13-B1","MS-13-D1","IM-15-C1","EN-12-D1","NE-20-C2"]},"PU-1":{"id":"PU-1","summary":"How the lungs and airways grow out of the foregut, which tissues come from endoderm and which from mesoderm, and the five stages of lung development. Exam questions tie each step to what goes wrong: tracheoesophageal fistula, surfactant deficiency in preterm infants, and pulmonary hypoplasia.","notes":[{"h":"Lung bud","points":["In week 4 the respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) grows out of the ventral wall of the foregut.","Endoderm forms the lining epithelium of the larynx, trachea, bronchi, and alveoli, plus the airway glands.","Splanchnic mesoderm around the bud forms the cartilage, smooth muscle, connective tissue, and visceral pleura.","Laryngeal cartilages and muscles come from the mesenchyme of pharyngeal arches 4 and 6.","The bud splits into two primary bronchial buds in week 5; the right gives 3 secondary bronchi and the left gives 2, which sets up 3 lobes on the right and 2 on the left."]},{"h":"Tracheoesophageal septum","points":["Tracheoesophageal ridges grow in from each side and fuse into the tracheoesophageal septum.","The septum divides the foregut into the trachea in front and the esophagus behind.","Faulty or incomplete septation gives tracheoesophageal fistula, often with esophageal atresia.","Most common form (about 85 percent): the upper esophagus ends blindly and the lower esophagus connects to the trachea.","Clues: polyhydramnios, drooling, choking or cyanosis with the first feed, a feeding tube that coils in the upper pouch, and a gas-filled stomach.","H-type fistula (no atresia) shows up later as recurrent aspiration pneumonia.","Often part of VACTERL association."]},{"h":"Stages of lung development","points":["Embryonic (about weeks 4 to 7): lung bud, bronchial buds, lobes and segments.","Pseudoglandular (about weeks 5 to 16): branching down to terminal bronchioles; no gas exchange possible.","Canalicular (about weeks 16 to 26): respiratory bronchioles form and capillaries grow close to the airways.","Survival with intensive support first becomes possible near the end of the canalicular stage (about weeks 24 to 26), once these thin gas exchange units exist.","Saccular (about week 26 to birth): terminal sacs with type I and type II pneumocytes; the blood-air barrier thins.","Alveolar (late fetal life to about 8 years): true alveoli form; most alveoli are added after birth."]},{"h":"Surfactant and fetal lung maturity","points":["Type II pneumocytes begin making surfactant in the second trimester; levels become adequate at about week 35.","Main surfactant lipid: dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (lecithin).","An amniotic lecithin to sphingomyelin (L/S) ratio above 2 indicates mature fetal lungs.","Fetal cortisol speeds surfactant production; fetal hyperinsulinemia from maternal diabetes delays it.","Too little surfactant at birth causes neonatal respiratory distress syndrome."]},{"h":"Pulmonary hypoplasia","points":["Normal lung growth needs amniotic fluid in the airways, fetal breathing movements, and room in the chest.","Oligohydramnios (bilateral renal agenesis, urinary obstruction, early membrane rupture) leaves the lungs small: Potter sequence with flat face, limb deformities, and hypoplastic lungs.","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia: a pleuroperitoneal membrane fails to close, usually the left posterolateral (Bochdalek) defect.","Gut herniates into the chest and compresses the developing lung; the same side is worst, but the other lung can also be small.","Either cause gives respiratory failure at birth with pulmonary hypertension; diaphragmatic hernia also shows a scaphoid abdomen and bowel sounds in the chest."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Stages of lung development","cols":["Stage","Approximate timing","What forms","Gas exchange possible"],"rows":[["Embryonic","Weeks 4 to 7","Lung bud, main and lobar bronchi, segments","No"],["Pseudoglandular","Weeks 5 to 16","Branching to terminal bronchioles","No"],["Canalicular","Weeks 16 to 26","Respiratory bronchioles, capillaries close to airways","Limited, near the end (about weeks 24 to 26)"],["Saccular","Week 26 to birth","Terminal sacs, type I and II pneumocytes, thin barrier","Yes"],["Alveolar","Late fetal life to about 8 years","Mature alveoli in large numbers","Yes"]]},{"title":"Germ layer origins of the respiratory tract","cols":["Germ layer source","Structures formed"],"rows":[["Foregut endoderm","Epithelium of larynx, trachea, bronchi, alveoli; airway glands"],["Splanchnic mesoderm","Cartilage, smooth muscle, connective tissue, visceral pleura"],["Somatic mesoderm","Parietal pleura"],["Pharyngeal arches 4 and 6","Laryngeal cartilages and muscles"]]},{"title":"Causes of pulmonary hypoplasia","cols":["Cause","How it limits lung growth","Other clues"],"rows":[["Bilateral renal agenesis","No fetal urine, so oligohydramnios; no fluid to expand airways","Potter facies, limb deformities"],["Posterior urethral valves","Outflow obstruction, so oligohydramnios","Male infant, distended bladder, hydronephrosis"],["Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","Abdominal organs fill the chest and compress the lung","Scaphoid abdomen, bowel sounds in chest, shifted heart sounds"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Every Pulmonologist Can See Alveoli","text":"Embryonic, Pseudoglandular, Canalicular, Saccular, Alveolar: the five stages in order."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lining versus wall","text":"Anything that lines the airway or secretes (epithelium, glands) is endoderm. Anything that holds it up or squeezes it (cartilage, muscle, connective tissue) is splanchnic mesoderm."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Canals before sacs","text":"Canalicular means the canals for blood vessels arrive. Without capillaries next to the airways there is nothing to exchange gas with, which is why viability starts at the end of this stage."}],"highYield":["The lung bud comes off the ventral foregut in week 4.","Most common tracheoesophageal fistula: blind upper esophageal pouch with a fistula from trachea to lower esophagus.","Polyhydramnios plus choking with the first feed and a coiled feeding tube means esophageal atresia.","L/S ratio above 2 means mature fetal lungs.","Viability begins near the end of the canalicular stage, about weeks 24 to 26.","Oligohydramnios from renal agenesis causes pulmonary hypoplasia (Potter sequence).","Bochdalek hernia is usually left-sided and causes hypoplasia of the lung on that side.","Maternal diabetes delays surfactant production by raising fetal insulin."],"pitfalls":["Polyhydramnios goes with esophageal atresia (fetus cannot swallow); oligohydramnios goes with renal agenesis and pulmonary hypoplasia.","Surfactant production begins in the second trimester, yet adequate levels arrive only around week 35; a 28-week infant has some type II cells but not enough surfactant.","An H-type fistula has no atresia, so the infant feeds at first and presents later with repeated aspiration pneumonia.","Most alveoli form after birth; the alveolar stage continues into childhood."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lung bud to alveoli","p":"Draw a timeline from week 4 of development to age 8. Mark each stage of lung development, what forms in it, and the point where a preterm infant can first survive with support.","l":["Lung bud from ventral foregut in week 4","Pseudoglandular: branching to terminal bronchioles, no gas exchange","Canalicular: respiratory bronchioles and capillaries","Viability near weeks 24 to 26","Saccular: terminal sacs, type I and II pneumocytes","Surfactant adequate about week 35, L/S above 2","Alveolar stage continues to about age 8"]},{"t":"Tracheoesophageal fistula","p":"Draw the foregut being divided by the tracheoesophageal septum. Then draw the most common type of tracheoesophageal fistula and list the findings in the newborn.","l":["Tracheoesophageal ridges fuse to form the septum","Trachea ends up anterior, esophagus posterior","Most common: blind upper esophageal pouch","Lower esophagus connects to the trachea","Polyhydramnios before birth","Drooling, choking, cyanosis with feeds","Feeding tube coils in the upper pouch; air in the stomach","Can be part of VACTERL"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Respiratory System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Body Cavities (section on the diaphragm)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung (section on congenital anomalies)"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-2","PU-14-G2","PU-14-A1","PU-4"]},"PU-2":{"id":"PU-2","summary":"The microscopic structure of the airway from nose to alveolus: how the lining epithelium changes along the way, what each cell type does, and what makes up the thin barrier where gas crosses. Exam questions link these cells to smoking damage, ciliary defects, impaired diffusion, and laryngeal cancer.","notes":[{"h":"Epithelium along the airway","points":["Trachea and bronchi: pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells.","Larger bronchioles: ciliated simple columnar, with goblet cells thinning out.","Terminal bronchioles: simple cuboidal, ciliated, with many club cells and no goblet cells.","Respiratory bronchioles: cuboidal epithelium interrupted by alveoli opening off the wall.","Alveoli: type I and type II pneumocytes.","True vocal folds and oropharynx: nonkeratinized stratified squamous, which stands up to friction."]},{"h":"Bronchi versus bronchioles","points":["Trachea has C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings with the trachealis muscle behind; bronchi have irregular cartilage plates.","Bronchi have submucosal seromucous glands and goblet cells; bronchioles have neither cartilage nor glands.","Bronchioles have relatively more smooth muscle, so they narrow strongly in asthma.","Without cartilage, bronchioles stay open through smooth muscle tone and radial traction from the elastic alveolar walls attached around them.","Loss of that alveolar tethering (emphysema) lets bronchioles collapse on expiration and trap air."]},{"h":"Cells and their jobs","points":["Type I pneumocytes: thin squamous cells covering about 95 percent of the alveolar surface; they form the gas exchange surface.","Type II pneumocytes: cuboidal cells with lamellar bodies; they secrete surfactant and divide to replace type I cells after injury.","Club cells: nonciliated dome-shaped cells in bronchioles; they secrete protective proteins, detoxify inhaled toxins with cytochrome P450, and act as progenitor cells.","Alveolar macrophages (dust cells): come from blood monocytes and clear particles and microbes; hemosiderin-filled ones are called heart failure cells.","Goblet cells and submucosal glands make the mucus layer."]},{"h":"Mucociliary clearance","points":["Cilia beat toward the pharynx and carry the mucus blanket and trapped particles up and out.","Each cilium has a 9 plus 2 axoneme: nine outer microtubule doublets around two central microtubules.","Dynein arms on the doublets use ATP to slide the doublets past each other, which bends the cilium.","Primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome): defective dynein arms give sinusitis, bronchiectasis, infertility, and situs inversus in about half.","Smoking replaces ciliated columnar epithelium with stratified squamous epithelium (squamous metaplasia), which has no cilia.","Smoking also enlarges submucosal glands and adds goblet cells, so more mucus is made while less is cleared.","Retained mucus lets bacteria grow, leading to recurrent bronchitis and pneumonia."]},{"h":"Blood-air barrier","points":["Layers gas crosses: type I pneumocyte cytoplasm, fused basement membranes of alveolus and capillary, capillary endothelium.","It is under 1 micrometer thick over most of the surface.","Interstitial fibrosis or edema fluid thickens the barrier and slows diffusion.","Oxygen transfer suffers more than CO2 transfer, which shows as a low DLCO and desaturation with exercise."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Changes along the airway","cols":["Region","Epithelium","Cartilage","Glands and goblet cells","Smooth muscle"],"rows":[["Trachea","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","C-shaped rings","Many glands and goblet cells","Trachealis posteriorly"],["Bronchi","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Irregular plates","Glands and goblet cells","Present, spiral bundles"],["Bronchioles","Ciliated simple columnar to cuboidal","None","No glands; few goblet cells","Prominent"],["Terminal bronchioles","Simple cuboidal with club cells","None","None","Present"],["Respiratory bronchioles","Cuboidal, interrupted by alveoli","None","None","Thin bands"],["Alveoli","Type I and type II pneumocytes","None","None","None"]]},{"title":"Cells of the lower airway","cols":["Cell","Location","Main functions"],"rows":[["Type I pneumocyte","Alveolar wall","Thin gas exchange surface"],["Type II pneumocyte","Alveolar wall, often at corners","Surfactant; replaces type I cells"],["Club cell","Bronchioles, mostly terminal","Secretory proteins, P450 detoxification, progenitor"],["Alveolar macrophage","Alveolar space and wall","Phagocytosis of particles and microbes"],["Goblet cell","Trachea, bronchi, larger bronchioles","Mucus"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cartilage and glands go together","text":"Where you see cartilage, you see glands: trachea and bronchi. Lose the cartilage and the glands go too: bronchioles."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Type II does two things","text":"Type II pneumocytes do two jobs: make surfactant and replace type I cells. Type I does one: get out of the way so gas can cross."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Gets thinner as you go down","text":"Tall pseudostratified columnar, then simple columnar, then cuboidal, then flat type I cells. The lining flattens as the airway shifts from cleaning air to exchanging gas."}],"highYield":["Terminal bronchioles have club cells and no goblet cells, cartilage, or glands.","Type II pneumocytes are the stem cells of the alveolus after injury.","Cilia: 9 plus 2 microtubules moved by dynein arms.","Kartagener syndrome: sinusitis, bronchiectasis, infertility, situs inversus.","Smoking causes squamous metaplasia of bronchial epithelium, a reversible change and a step toward squamous cell carcinoma.","Thickened blood-air barrier (fibrosis, edema) lowers DLCO.","True vocal folds have nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, so laryngeal cancer is mostly squamous cell carcinoma."],"pitfalls":["Type I cells cover most of the surface, yet type II cells are more numerous; do not confuse coverage with cell count.","False vocal folds (vestibular folds) have respiratory epithelium; true vocal folds have stratified squamous epithelium.","Cystic fibrosis makes mucus too thick to clear; primary ciliary dyskinesia leaves normal mucus with cilia that cannot move it.","Bronchioles have no cartilage; their patency depends on alveolar tethering and muscle tone, which is why emphysema causes expiratory collapse."],"dumps":[{"t":"Trachea to alveolus","p":"Make a table with rows for trachea, bronchus, bronchiole, terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, and alveolus. Add columns for epithelium, cartilage, glands, goblet cells, and the main cell types.","l":["Trachea and bronchi: pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Cartilage and glands stop at the bronchus","Bronchioles: no cartilage, no glands","Terminal bronchiole: simple cuboidal with club cells","Respiratory bronchiole: alveoli open off its wall","Alveolus: type I for exchange, type II for surfactant and repair","Smooth muscle is prominent in bronchioles"]},{"t":"Clearing the airway","p":"Draw a cross section of a cilium and label its parts. Then draw normal bronchial epithelium beside the epithelium of a long-term smoker, and explain why the smoker keeps getting infections.","l":["Nine outer microtubule doublets around two central microtubules","Dynein arms use ATP to bend the cilium","Cilia move mucus up toward the pharynx","Smoker: squamous metaplasia with loss of cilia","Smoker: more goblet cells and larger submucosal glands","More mucus, less clearance, so bacteria stay and multiply","Dynein defects cause Kartagener syndrome"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Respiratory System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck (section on the larynx)"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-1","PU-7","PU-14-B2","PU-14-F4","PU-14-A2"]},"PU-3":{"id":"PU-3","summary":"The anatomy of the nose, paranasal sinuses, pharynx, and larynx: where the sinuses drain, where nosebleeds start, how the pharynx is divided, and which nerves move and feel the larynx. Exam questions use this anatomy to explain sinusitis, epistaxis, otitis media from big adenoids, hoarseness, and aspiration.","notes":[{"h":"Nasal cavity and sinus drainage","points":["Each lateral wall has superior, middle, and inferior conchae; the space under each is its meatus.","Middle meatus (semilunar hiatus area): frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoid sinuses.","Superior meatus: posterior ethmoid sinuses.","Sphenoethmoidal recess (above the superior concha): sphenoid sinus.","Inferior meatus: nasolacrimal duct, which is why crying gives a runny nose.","Ethmoid cells sit behind a paper-thin orbital wall (lamina papyracea), so ethmoid sinusitis can spread to the orbit."]},{"h":"Maxillary sinus","points":["Largest sinus and the one most often infected.","Its ostium sits high on the medial wall, so the sinus cannot drain by gravity when upright.","Roots of the maxillary molars (and often premolars) lie just under its floor.","A tooth infection or extraction can open into the sinus and cause odontogenic sinusitis."]},{"h":"Epistaxis","points":["Kiesselbach area sits on the anterior nasal septum, where four arteries anastomose.","Arteries there: sphenopalatine, anterior ethmoidal, greater palatine, and the septal branch of the superior labial.","About 90 percent of nosebleeds are anterior, from this area; usually mild, from nose picking, dry air, or trauma.","Posterior epistaxis comes from sphenopalatine branches; it bleeds more, runs down the throat, is harder to control, and is more common in older adults with hypertension."]},{"h":"Pharynx and Waldeyer ring","points":["Nasopharynx: behind the nasal cavity, above the soft palate; holds the adenoids and the pharyngotympanic (eustachian) tube openings.","Oropharynx: from the soft palate to the tip of the epiglottis; holds the palatine and lingual tonsils.","Laryngopharynx: from the epiglottis to the lower border of the cricoid (about C6), where it becomes the esophagus; holds the piriform recesses.","Waldeyer ring: pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids), tubal tonsils, palatine tonsils, and lingual tonsil.","Large adenoids block the choanae, causing mouth breathing and snoring, and block the eustachian tube, causing otitis media with effusion."]},{"h":"Larynx: cartilages and muscles","points":["Unpaired cartilages: thyroid, cricoid (the only complete ring), epiglottis. Paired: arytenoid, corniculate, cuneiform.","Posterior cricoarytenoid is the only muscle that abducts (opens) the vocal folds.","Lateral cricoarytenoid and the arytenoid muscles adduct the folds; thyroarytenoid and vocalis relax and fine-tune them.","Cricothyroid tilts the thyroid cartilage forward to tense and lengthen the folds, raising pitch."]},{"h":"Larynx: nerves","points":["Superior laryngeal nerve (from the vagus) splits into the internal and external laryngeal nerves.","Internal laryngeal nerve: sensation above the vocal folds; it carries the afferent limb of the cough reflex.","External laryngeal nerve: motor to cricothyroid; injury (for example during thyroid surgery near the superior thyroid artery) gives a weak voice that cannot reach high pitch.","Recurrent laryngeal nerve: all other intrinsic muscles, and sensation below the folds.","The right recurrent nerve loops under the right subclavian artery; the left loops under the aortic arch, so it has a longer path and more places to be injured.","Unilateral recurrent nerve injury gives hoarseness; bilateral injury leaves both folds near the midline and causes stridor.","Loss of supraglottic sensation means food and secretions enter the larynx without a cough, so the patient aspirates."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Where the sinuses and duct drain","cols":["Structure","Drains into"],"rows":[["Frontal sinus","Middle meatus"],["Maxillary sinus","Middle meatus"],["Anterior ethmoid cells","Middle meatus"],["Posterior ethmoid cells","Superior meatus"],["Sphenoid sinus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"],["Nasolacrimal duct","Inferior meatus"]]},{"title":"Laryngeal nerves","cols":["Nerve","Motor","Sensory","Result of injury"],"rows":[["Internal laryngeal","None","Mucosa above the vocal folds","Loss of cough reflex above the folds; aspiration"],["External laryngeal","Cricothyroid","None","Weak voice, cannot reach high pitch, tires easily"],["Recurrent laryngeal","All other intrinsic muscles, including posterior cricoarytenoid","Mucosa below the vocal folds","Unilateral: hoarseness. Bilateral: stridor and airway obstruction"]]},{"title":"Anterior versus posterior epistaxis","cols":["Feature","Anterior","Posterior"],"rows":[["Source","Kiesselbach area on the anterior septum","Sphenopalatine artery branches"],["Share of cases","About 90 percent","About 10 percent"],["Typical patient","Child or young adult; nose picking, dry air","Older adult, often hypertensive"],["Severity","Usually mild, stops with pressure","Heavier, drains into the throat, harder to control"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"LEGS","text":"Kiesselbach area arteries: superior Labial (septal branch), anterior Ethmoidal, Greater palatine, Sphenopalatine."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Posterior opens the door","text":"Posterior cricoarytenoid pulls the arytenoids apart to open the airway. It is the only abductor, so a paralyzed one leaves the fold sitting near the midline."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Most sinuses use the middle door","text":"Frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoid all drain into the middle meatus. Only the posterior ethmoid (superior meatus), the sphenoid (recess), and the tear duct (inferior meatus) use other doors."}],"highYield":["Maxillary sinus is most often infected because its ostium is high on the medial wall.","An upper molar abscess can spread into the maxillary sinus.","Nasolacrimal duct drains into the inferior meatus.","Most nosebleeds come from Kiesselbach area on the anterior septum.","Enlarged adenoids cause mouth breathing, snoring, and otitis media.","Posterior cricoarytenoid is the only vocal fold abductor.","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch; an aortic aneurysm, enlarged left atrium, or left hilar lung tumor can cause hoarseness.","Internal laryngeal nerve damage removes the cough reflex above the folds and leads to aspiration."],"pitfalls":["Cricothyroid is the one intrinsic laryngeal muscle supplied by the external laryngeal nerve; the recurrent nerve supplies all the others.","Unilateral recurrent nerve palsy gives hoarseness; bilateral palsy gives stridor, since both folds sit near the midline.","Posterior ethmoid cells drain to the superior meatus, while anterior ethmoid cells drain to the middle meatus.","The right recurrent nerve hooks under the subclavian artery, the left under the aortic arch; chest disease affects the left side."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lateral nasal wall","p":"Draw the lateral wall of the nasal cavity with the three conchae. Draw an arrow from each paranasal sinus and the nasolacrimal duct to the place it drains. Then mark Kiesselbach area on a sketch of the septum and name its arteries.","l":["Superior, middle, and inferior conchae with meatuses below them","Frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoid to the middle meatus","Posterior ethmoid to the superior meatus","Sphenoid to the sphenoethmoidal recess","Nasolacrimal duct to the inferior meatus","Kiesselbach area on the anterior septum","Sphenopalatine, anterior ethmoidal, greater palatine, superior labial"]},{"t":"Nerves of the larynx","p":"Trace the vagus nerve on both sides from the neck to the larynx. Draw the superior laryngeal nerve and its two branches, and the right and left recurrent laryngeal nerves with the vessels they hook under. Label what each nerve does and what happens when it is cut.","l":["Superior laryngeal splits into internal and external branches","Internal: sensation above the folds; loss causes aspiration","External: cricothyroid; loss causes weak high-pitched voice","Right recurrent hooks under the right subclavian artery","Left recurrent hooks under the aortic arch","Recurrent: all other intrinsic muscles and sensation below the folds","Unilateral recurrent injury: hoarseness; bilateral: stridor","Posterior cricoarytenoid is the only abductor"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head (section on the nose and paranasal sinuses)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Neck (sections on the pharynx and larynx)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-2","PU-14-C5","PU-14-C3","PU-14-C7","PU-14-F4"]},"PU-4":{"id":"PU-4","summary":"The anatomy of the chest: where the lungs and pleura sit against the ribs, how the two lungs differ, what lies at each hilum, what fills each part of the mediastinum, and what passes through the diaphragm. Exam questions use this to place a needle, predict where aspirated material goes, localize a mass, explain shoulder pain, and recognize chylothorax.","notes":[{"h":"Surface anatomy and thoracentesis","points":["Lower lung border: rib 6 at the midclavicular line, rib 8 at the midaxillary line, rib 10 at the paravertebral line.","Lower pleural border: rib 8, rib 10, rib 12 at the same lines, two ribs below the lung.","The gap between them is the costodiaphragmatic recess, where pleural fluid collects first.","Intercostal vein, artery, and nerve run in the costal groove along the lower border of each rib.","So a thoracentesis needle goes just above the upper border of the rib below the chosen space."]},{"h":"Right versus left lung and aspiration","points":["Right lung: three lobes, separated by the horizontal and oblique fissures.","Left lung: two lobes, one oblique fissure, a lingula, and a cardiac notch for the heart.","The right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical, so aspirated material usually goes right.","Upright patient: aspirate settles in the right lower lobe (basal segments).","Supine patient: aspirate settles in the superior segment of the right lower lobe (and the posterior segment of the right upper lobe)."]},{"h":"Hilum","points":["Each hilum holds a main bronchus, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, lymph nodes, and nerves.","Right pulmonary artery lies anterior to the right main bronchus.","Left pulmonary artery lies superior to the left main bronchus.","Pulmonary veins are the most anterior and inferior structures at both hila."]},{"h":"Mediastinum","points":["A plane through the sternal angle and the T4 to T5 disc separates the superior from the inferior mediastinum.","Superior: thymus, great vessels, superior vena cava, trachea, esophagus, thoracic duct, vagus and phrenic nerves.","Anterior (sternum to pericardium): thymus remnant, fat, lymph nodes. Masses: thymoma, teratoma and other germ cell tumors, lymphoma, retrosternal thyroid.","Middle: pericardium, heart, roots of great vessels, phrenic nerves. Masses: enlarged lymph nodes, bronchogenic and pericardial cysts.","Posterior: esophagus, descending aorta, azygos veins, thoracic duct, vagus nerves, sympathetic trunks. Masses: neurogenic tumors (schwannoma, neurofibroma, neuroblastoma in children)."]},{"h":"Pleura and pain","points":["Parietal pleura is pain-sensitive: costal pleura and the peripheral diaphragm by intercostal nerves, mediastinal and central diaphragmatic pleura by the phrenic nerve.","Visceral pleura has autonomic supply only and does not feel sharp pain.","Pleuritis gives sharp pain on breathing only once the parietal pleura is involved.","Irritation of the central diaphragmatic pleura refers pain to the shoulder because the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5) shares those cord segments with shoulder skin.","Irritation of the peripheral diaphragm refers pain to the lower chest wall and upper abdomen."]},{"h":"Diaphragm and thoracic duct","points":["T8: inferior vena cava. T10: esophagus and both vagal trunks. T12: aorta, thoracic duct, and azygos vein.","Phrenic nerves (C3 to C5) give all motor supply and sensation to the central diaphragm.","Thoracic duct starts at the cisterna chyli in the upper abdomen and enters the chest through the aortic hiatus at T12.","It runs up the posterior mediastinum between the aorta and azygos vein, crosses to the left at about T5, and empties into the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins.","Injury (surgery, trauma, tumor such as lymphoma) leaks lymph into the pleura: chylothorax, a milky fluid rich in triglycerides and chylomicrons."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Lung and pleural borders","cols":["Line","Lung border","Pleural border"],"rows":[["Midclavicular","Rib 6","Rib 8"],["Midaxillary","Rib 8","Rib 10"],["Paravertebral","Rib 10","Rib 12"]]},{"title":"Mediastinal compartments","cols":["Compartment","Main contents","Typical masses"],"rows":[["Superior","Thymus, great vessels, SVC, trachea, esophagus, thoracic duct","Retrosternal thyroid, thymoma, aortic arch aneurysm"],["Anterior","Thymus remnant, fat, lymph nodes","Thymoma, teratoma, lymphoma, retrosternal thyroid"],["Middle","Heart, pericardium, roots of great vessels, phrenic nerves","Lymph node enlargement, bronchogenic cyst, pericardial cyst"],["Posterior","Esophagus, descending aorta, azygos, thoracic duct, sympathetic trunks","Neurogenic tumors, esophageal tumors, aneurysm"]]},{"title":"Openings in the diaphragm","cols":["Level","Opening","Structures"],"rows":[["T8","Caval opening","Inferior vena cava"],["T10","Esophageal hiatus","Esophagus, anterior and posterior vagal trunks"],["T12","Aortic hiatus","Aorta, thoracic duct, azygos vein"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"I 8 10 Eggs At 12","text":"\"I ate ten eggs at twelve\": IVC at T8, esophagus (with the vagal trunks) at T10, aorta (with the thoracic duct and azygos vein) at T12."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"RALS","text":"Right pulmonary artery is Anterior, Left pulmonary artery is Superior to its main bronchus."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 4 Ts","text":"Anterior mediastinal masses: Thymoma, Teratoma, Thyroid (retrosternal), and Terrible lymphoma."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive","text":"The phrenic nerve comes from C3 to C5, which also explains referred pain to the shoulder."}],"highYield":["Pleura extends two ribs below the lung at every line: 8, 10, 12 versus 6, 8, 10.","Insert the thoracentesis needle just above the rib to avoid the neurovascular bundle under the rib above.","Upright aspiration goes to the right lower lobe; supine aspiration goes to the superior segment of the right lower lobe.","Shoulder pain from diaphragmatic irritation travels by the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5).","Visceral pleura does not feel sharp pain; parietal pleura does.","Anterior mediastinal mass: think thymoma (with myasthenia gravis), teratoma, lymphoma, or thyroid.","Posterior mediastinal mass: think neurogenic tumor.","Milky pleural fluid high in triglycerides means a thoracic duct leak (chylothorax)."],"pitfalls":["The neurovascular bundle runs under the lower border of each rib, so go over the top of the lower rib and never under the upper one.","Aspiration while lying down goes to the superior segment of the right lower lobe, which points backward, rather than the basal segments.","The thoracic duct passes through the aortic hiatus at T12, not the esophageal hiatus.","Pleuritic pain means the parietal pleura is involved; disease confined to the lung and visceral pleura can be painless.","The thoracic duct ends on the left side, at the left venous angle, even though it starts on the right of the aorta."],"dumps":[{"t":"Chest wall map","p":"Draw the front, side, and back of the chest. Mark the lower borders of the lung and pleura at the midclavicular, midaxillary, and paravertebral lines. Then draw one intercostal space in cross section with the vessels and nerve, and show where a thoracentesis needle should go.","l":["Lung: ribs 6, 8, 10","Pleura: ribs 8, 10, 12","Costodiaphragmatic recess between the two borders","Vein, artery, nerve in order from top to bottom in the costal groove","Bundle runs along the lower border of the rib","Needle goes just above the upper border of the rib below","Fluid collects first in the costodiaphragmatic recess"]},{"t":"Mediastinum and diaphragm","p":"Draw a side view of the mediastinum and divide it into superior, anterior, middle, and posterior parts. Write the main contents and typical masses in each. Underneath, draw the diaphragm with its three openings, their vertebral levels, and what passes through each.","l":["Sternal angle plane at T4 to T5 divides superior from inferior","Anterior: thymoma, teratoma, lymphoma, thyroid","Middle: heart and pericardium; lymph nodes and cysts","Posterior: esophagus, aorta, azygos, sympathetic trunks; neurogenic tumors","T8: inferior vena cava","T10: esophagus and vagal trunks","T12: aorta, thoracic duct, azygos vein","Phrenic nerve C3 to C5"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax (sections on pleurae, lungs, and mediastinum)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung (section on lesions of the pleura)"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-5","PU-14-D1","PU-14-H6","PU-14-F5","PU-14-D5"]},"PU-5":{"id":"PU-5","summary":"How blood and air move through the lungs: the low-pressure pulmonary circulation and the separate bronchial supply, how hypoxia constricts lung vessels, how gravity sets up West zones, and what controls airway and vascular resistance. Exam questions apply these ideas to pulmonary embolism, cor pulmonale, apical tuberculosis, and bronchoconstriction.","notes":[{"h":"Two blood supplies","points":["Pulmonary circulation carries the whole cardiac output at low pressure: about 25/8 mmHg, mean about 15 mmHg.","Pulmonary vascular resistance is about one tenth of systemic resistance.","Rising cardiac output recruits closed capillaries and widens open ones, so resistance falls as flow rises.","Bronchial arteries come from the thoracic aorta, directly or through an intercostal artery (systemic pressure), and feed the bronchi, connective tissue, and visceral pleura.","Some bronchial venous blood drains into the pulmonary veins, a small normal shunt.","Because lung tissue gets blood from both systems, most pulmonary emboli do not cause infarction.","Infarction becomes likely when bronchial flow is also poor, as in left heart failure; the infarct is wedge-shaped, hemorrhagic, and based on the pleura."]},{"h":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","points":["Low alveolar PO2 closes potassium channels in pulmonary arteriolar smooth muscle, which depolarizes it and lets calcium in, causing constriction.","This sends blood away from poorly ventilated alveoli toward well-ventilated ones and improves V/Q matching.","Systemic arterioles do the opposite: hypoxia dilates them to bring more blood to hungry tissue.","Generalized chronic hypoxia (COPD, sleep apnea, high altitude) constricts the whole pulmonary bed.","The result is pulmonary hypertension and right ventricular hypertrophy from lung disease: cor pulmonale."]},{"h":"West zones and V/Q","points":["Zone 1 (apex, only when alveolar pressure is high or arterial pressure low): alveolar > arterial > venous; no flow, so alveolar dead space.","Zone 2 (middle): arterial > alveolar > venous; flow depends on arterial minus alveolar pressure.","Zone 3 (base): arterial > venous > alveolar; flow depends on the usual arterial minus venous difference.","Both ventilation and perfusion are greater at the base, but perfusion rises more steeply.","So V/Q is highest at the apex (about 3) and lowest at the base (about 0.6); the whole lung averages about 0.8.","The apex has the highest alveolar PO2, which favors aerobic Mycobacterium tuberculosis in reactivation disease."]},{"h":"Airflow and airway resistance","points":["Conducting zone: trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles; no gas exchange; anatomic dead space about 150 mL.","Respiratory zone: respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs; gas exchange occurs here.","Medium-sized bronchi have the highest total resistance.","Small airways have tiny individual radii but are so many in parallel that their total cross-section is huge and their combined resistance is low.","Poiseuille law: resistance = 8 times viscosity times length divided by (pi times radius to the fourth power).","Halving the radius raises resistance 16-fold, so small changes in bronchial tone matter a lot."]},{"h":"What changes airway resistance","points":["Parasympathetic (vagal) acetylcholine on M3 receptors contracts airway smooth muscle and raises resistance.","Beta-2 stimulation by circulating epinephrine relaxes it and lowers resistance.","High lung volume pulls airways open by radial traction, lowering resistance; low lung volume narrows them.","In the large airways flow is turbulent, so resistance depends on gas density.","Denser gas (deep sea diving) raises resistance; a helium-oxygen mix lowers it."]},{"h":"Pulmonary vascular resistance and lung volume","points":["Pulmonary vascular resistance is lowest at functional residual capacity.","Low lung volume: extra-alveolar vessels lose radial traction and narrow, so resistance rises.","High lung volume: alveolar capillaries are stretched flat in the alveolar walls, so resistance rises."]}],"charts":[{"title":"West zones of the upright lung","cols":["Zone","Location","Pressure order","Blood flow"],"rows":[["Zone 1","Apex, abnormal states only (hemorrhage, positive pressure ventilation)","Alveolar > arterial > venous","None; alveolar dead space"],["Zone 2","Middle","Arterial > alveolar > venous","Set by arterial minus alveolar pressure"],["Zone 3","Base","Arterial > venous > alveolar","Set by arterial minus venous pressure; highest flow"]]},{"title":"Apex versus base (upright)","cols":["Feature","Apex","Base"],"rows":[["Ventilation","Lower","Higher"],["Perfusion","Much lower","Much higher"],["V/Q","About 3","About 0.6"],["Alveolar PO2","Highest","Lowest"],["Alveolar PCO2","Lowest","Highest"]]},{"title":"Pulmonary versus bronchial circulation","cols":["Feature","Pulmonary","Bronchial"],"rows":[["Source","Right ventricle","Thoracic aorta, directly or through an intercostal artery"],["Pressure","Low, mean about 15 mmHg","Systemic"],["Supplies","Alveolar capillaries for gas exchange","Airway walls, connective tissue, visceral pleura"],["Venous drainage","Pulmonary veins to left atrium","Bronchial veins to azygos system; some into pulmonary veins"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Read the zones top to bottom","text":"Put A for alveolar, a for arterial, v for venous. Zone 1 is A a v, zone 2 is a A v, zone 3 is a v A. Alveolar pressure slides down the list as you move down the lung."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lungs think backwards about hypoxia","text":"Systemic tissue that is short of oxygen calls for more blood. Lung tissue that is short of oxygen sends blood elsewhere, because that alveolus cannot load it."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Radius to the fourth","text":"Halve the radius and resistance goes up 2 x 2 x 2 x 2 = 16 times. Double it and resistance drops to one sixteenth."}],"highYield":["Dual blood supply means most pulmonary emboli do not infarct the lung.","Hypoxic vasoconstriction in the lung is the opposite of the systemic response.","Chronic hypoxia leads to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.","V/Q is highest at the apex; reactivation TB likes the apex for its high PO2.","Both ventilation and perfusion are highest at the base.","Medium-sized bronchi give the most airway resistance.","Resistance varies with 1 over radius to the fourth power.","Pulmonary vascular resistance is lowest at FRC."],"pitfalls":["The base has a low V/Q even though it gets the most ventilation, because perfusion rises even more there.","Small airways look like they should resist most, yet their parallel arrangement keeps total resistance low; disease there is silent until advanced.","Zone 1 does not exist in a healthy upright lung; it appears with low arterial pressure or high alveolar pressure.","Pulmonary vascular resistance rises at both low and high lung volumes, by different mechanisms."],"dumps":[{"t":"West zones","p":"Draw an upright lung divided into zones 1, 2, and 3. In each zone, write the order of alveolar, arterial, and venous pressure. Beside the lung, draw how ventilation, perfusion, and V/Q change from apex to base.","l":["Zone 1: alveolar > arterial > venous, no flow","Zone 2: arterial > alveolar > venous","Zone 3: arterial > venous > alveolar","Ventilation and perfusion both rise toward the base","Perfusion rises more steeply","V/Q about 3 at the apex, about 0.6 at the base","High apical PO2 favors reactivation TB"]},{"t":"Hypoxic vasoconstriction to cor pulmonale","p":"Trace what happens from low alveolar oxygen in one region, then in the whole lung, ending at the right ventricle. Contrast it with what hypoxia does to a systemic arteriole.","l":["Low alveolar PO2 closes K channels in pulmonary arteriolar muscle","Depolarization and calcium entry cause constriction","Local effect: blood diverted to better-ventilated alveoli","Global chronic hypoxia constricts the whole pulmonary bed","Pulmonary hypertension develops","Right ventricular hypertrophy, cor pulmonale","Systemic arterioles dilate in hypoxia"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation (section on airway resistance)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-6","PU-7","PU-14-E2","PU-14-E3","PU-14-H9"]},"PU-6":{"id":"PU-6","summary":"How air gets in and out of the lungs: lung volumes, minute and alveolar ventilation, the elastic forces of lung and chest wall, spirometry, and the brainstem and chemoreceptor control of breathing. Exam questions ask students to calculate ventilation, read flow-volume loops, predict the response to high CO2 or low O2, and sort out the sleep-related breathing disorders.","notes":[{"h":"Lung volumes and capacities","points":["Tidal volume (TV) about 500 mL; inspiratory reserve (IRV) about 3000 mL; expiratory reserve (ERV) about 1100 mL; residual volume (RV) about 1200 mL.","Functional residual capacity (FRC) = ERV + RV, about 2300 mL: the volume left after a normal breath out.","Vital capacity (VC) = IRV + TV + ERV, about 4600 mL; total lung capacity (TLC) = VC + RV, about 5800 mL.","Spirometry cannot measure RV, so it misses FRC and TLC too. Helium dilution measures gas that communicates with the airways; body plethysmography (Boyle law) also counts trapped gas."]},{"h":"Minute ventilation, alveolar ventilation, dead space","points":["Minute ventilation = TV x respiratory rate; 500 mL x 12 = 6 L/min.","Alveolar ventilation = (TV minus dead space) x rate; (500 minus 150) x 12 = 4.2 L/min.","Bohr equation: VD/VT = (PaCO2 minus mixed expired PCO2) / PaCO2; normal is about 0.3.","Physiologic dead space = anatomic dead space + alveolar dead space (ventilated but unperfused alveoli).","Rapid shallow breathing (250 mL x 24) keeps minute ventilation at 6 L/min but drops alveolar ventilation to 2.4 L/min.","PaCO2 varies inversely with alveolar ventilation, so hypoventilation raises PaCO2."]},{"h":"Compliance, recoil, and surfactant","points":["Compliance is change in volume per change in pressure. The lung recoils inward and the chest wall springs outward; at FRC these forces balance.","That balance makes intrapleural pressure about minus 5 cmH2O at FRC.","Law of Laplace: collapsing pressure = 2 x surface tension / radius, so small alveoli would empty into large ones.","Surfactant lowers surface tension, and more so in small alveoli where it is concentrated, which stabilizes them, raises compliance, and keeps fluid out of alveoli."]},{"h":"Spirometry and flow-volume loops","points":["Obstructive: FEV1 falls more than FVC, FEV1/FVC below 0.7, TLC normal or high, RV high from air trapping; expiratory limb scooped.","Restrictive: FEV1 and FVC both fall, FEV1/FVC normal or high, TLC low; loop is small and shifted to lower volumes.","DLCO separates restrictive causes: low in interstitial lung disease, normal in chest wall or neuromuscular causes.","Fixed upper airway obstruction (tracheal stenosis): both inspiratory and expiratory limbs flatten into plateaus.","Variable extrathoracic obstruction (vocal fold paralysis) flattens the inspiratory limb; variable intrathoracic obstruction flattens the expiratory limb."]},{"h":"Brainstem control","points":["Dorsal respiratory group (nucleus tractus solitarius region of the medulla): drives inspiration and receives input from CN IX and X.","Ventral respiratory group (medulla): quiet in normal breathing; fires both inspiratory and expiratory neurons during forced breathing.","Pneumotaxic center (upper pons): limits inspiration, which shortens each breath and raises the rate.","Apneustic center (lower pons): prolongs inspiration; unchecked, it causes long inspiratory gasps (apneusis).","Hering-Breuer inflation reflex: stretch receptors in the airways send signals by the vagus to stop inspiration when the lung is overinflated; it matters most in infants and at large tidal volumes."]},{"h":"Chemoreceptors","points":["Central chemoreceptors (ventral medulla) respond to hydrogen ions in CSF.","CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier easily and forms H+ in poorly buffered CSF; blood H+ crosses poorly.","Peripheral chemoreceptors: carotid bodies (CN IX) and aortic bodies (CN X); they respond mainly to PaO2 below about 60 mmHg, and also to PaCO2 and H+.","The carotid bodies are the main sensors of arterial H+, so they drive the hyperventilation of metabolic acidosis.","Ventilation rises steeply and steadily with PaCO2, but changes little with falling PaO2 until it drops below about 60 mmHg.","In chronic CO2 retention, CSF bicarbonate rises and central drive blunts; high-flow oxygen can then raise PaCO2, mostly by worsening V/Q matching and the Haldane effect."]},{"h":"Sleep apnea and obesity hypoventilation","points":["Obstructive sleep apnea: the pharynx collapses during sleep while chest and abdominal effort continue; risks are obesity, large tonsils, and a small jaw; snoring, daytime sleepiness, and morning headache are typical.","Central sleep apnea: no respiratory effort during the pauses; seen with heart failure (Cheyne-Stokes breathing), opioids, and brainstem injury.","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome: BMI 30 or more with daytime PaCO2 above 45 mmHg and no other cause.","Repeated nighttime hypoxia causes hypoxic vasoconstriction, so pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale follow.","Hypoxia also raises erythropoietin, causing secondary polycythemia."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Obstructive versus restrictive patterns","cols":["Measure","Obstructive","Restrictive (lung tissue)","Restrictive (chest wall or muscle)"],"rows":[["FEV1","Low","Low","Low"],["FVC","Normal or low","Low","Low"],["FEV1/FVC","Below 0.7","Normal or high","Normal or high"],["TLC","Normal or high","Low","Low"],["RV","High","Low","Normal or high (weak expiration)"],["DLCO","Low in emphysema, normal in asthma","Low","Normal"],["Examples","COPD, asthma, bronchiectasis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis, pneumoconiosis","Obesity, kyphoscoliosis, myasthenia gravis"]]},{"title":"Central versus peripheral chemoreceptors","cols":["Feature","Central","Peripheral"],"rows":[["Location","Ventral surface of the medulla","Carotid bodies (CN IX), aortic bodies (CN X)"],["Main stimulus","H+ in CSF made from arterial CO2","PaO2 below about 60 mmHg"],["Other stimuli","None of importance","PaCO2; arterial H+ (carotid)"],["Respond to anemia or CO poisoning","No","Little, because PaO2 is normal"],["Adapt in chronic CO2 retention","Yes, as CSF bicarbonate rises","No, become the main drive"]]},{"title":"Sleep-related breathing disorders","cols":["Disorder","Respiratory effort","Mechanism","Key findings"],"rows":[["Obstructive sleep apnea","Present","Pharyngeal collapse during sleep","Snoring, obesity, daytime sleepiness, hypertension"],["Central sleep apnea","Absent during pauses","Brainstem drive stops","Heart failure, opioids, brainstem lesion"],["Obesity hypoventilation","Reduced","Chest wall load and blunted drive","BMI 30 or more, daytime PaCO2 above 45 mmHg"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Capacities are sums","text":"Any capacity is two or more volumes added together. Any capacity that includes RV (FRC, TLC) needs helium dilution or plethysmography."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Shallow wastes air","text":"Dead space is a fixed tax of about 150 mL on every breath. Small breaths pay the same tax, so more of each breath is wasted."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ratio tells the family","text":"FEV1/FVC low means obstructive. FEV1/FVC normal or high with small volumes means restrictive. Then DLCO tells you whether the problem is in the lung or outside it."}],"highYield":["Alveolar ventilation = (TV minus dead space) x rate.","Rapid shallow breathing lowers alveolar ventilation and raises PaCO2 at the same minute ventilation.","Spirometry cannot measure RV, FRC, or TLC.","Obstructive: FEV1/FVC below 0.7 and high TLC; restrictive: normal ratio and low TLC.","Fixed upper airway obstruction flattens both limbs of the flow-volume loop.","Central chemoreceptors respond to CSF H+ from CO2; peripheral ones respond to PaO2 below 60 mmHg.","Surfactant prevents small alveoli from collapsing, as Laplace law predicts they otherwise would.","Sleep apnea and obesity hypoventilation lead to pulmonary hypertension and secondary polycythemia."],"pitfalls":["FEV1 and FVC both fall in restrictive disease, so the ratio stays normal or rises; do not call a low FEV1 obstruction without checking the ratio.","Peripheral chemoreceptors sense PaO2, not oxygen content, so they do not fire much in anemia or CO poisoning.","Obstructive sleep apnea has continued effort against a closed airway; central sleep apnea has no effort at all.","Emphysema increases compliance; fibrosis decreases it.","Hering-Breuer stops inspiration when the lung is overinflated; it is weak at normal adult tidal volumes."],"dumps":[{"t":"Spirogram and flow-volume loops","p":"Draw a spirogram and label all four volumes and four capacities with normal values. Then draw a normal flow-volume loop, an obstructive loop, a restrictive loop, and a fixed upper airway obstruction loop on the same axes.","l":["TV 500, IRV 3000, ERV 1100, RV 1200 mL","FRC = ERV + RV; VC = IRV + TV + ERV; TLC = VC + RV","RV, FRC, and TLC cannot be measured by spirometry","Obstructive loop: scooped expiratory limb, shifted to higher volumes","Restrictive loop: small, shifted to lower volumes, ratio preserved","Fixed upper airway obstruction: flat inspiratory and expiratory limbs","FEV1/FVC below 0.7 means obstruction"]},{"t":"Control of breathing","p":"Draw the brainstem and label the dorsal and ventral respiratory groups and the pontine centers. Add the central and peripheral chemoreceptors with their nerves and the stimulus each senses. Then explain what happens to breathing when PaCO2 rises and when PaO2 falls to 50 mmHg.","l":["Dorsal group: inspiration, input from CN IX and X","Ventral group: forced breathing, both inspiration and expiration","Pneumotaxic center limits inspiration; apneustic center prolongs it","Central chemoreceptors: CSF H+ formed from CO2","Carotid bodies by CN IX, aortic bodies by CN X","Peripheral chemoreceptors fire strongly when PaO2 is below about 60 mmHg","Rising PaCO2 increases ventilation steeply and steadily","Hering-Breuer reflex stops inspiration by vagal stretch receptors"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Regulation of Respiration"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-5","PU-7","PU-14-A","PU-14-B","PU-14-A1"]},"PU-7":{"id":"PU-7","summary":"How oxygen gets from the alveolus into blood and out to tissues: the alveolar gas equation and A-a gradient, the five causes of hypoxemia, dead space versus shunt, diffusion, the four types of hypoxia, and adaptation to altitude. Exam questions give an arterial blood gas and ask students to find the cause, predict the response to oxygen, or explain altitude illness.","notes":[{"h":"Alveolar gas equation and A-a gradient","points":["PAO2 = FiO2 x (Patm minus 47) minus PaCO2 / 0.8.","At sea level on room air: 0.21 x (760 minus 47) = about 150; 150 minus 40/0.8 = about 100 mmHg.","A-a gradient = PAO2 minus PaO2; normal is about 5 to 15 mmHg in young adults and rises with age.","A normal gradient points outside the lung (low inspired O2 or low ventilation); a widened gradient points to diffusion, V/Q mismatch, or shunt."]},{"h":"Five causes of hypoxemia","points":["Low inspired O2 (altitude): normal A-a gradient, corrects with O2.","Hypoventilation (opioids, neuromuscular weakness): high PaCO2, normal A-a gradient, corrects with O2.","Diffusion limitation (fibrosis): widened gradient, corrects with O2.","V/Q mismatch (COPD, asthma, pneumonia, embolism): widened gradient, corrects with O2; the most common cause.","Right-to-left shunt: widened gradient and poor correction with 100 percent O2, because shunted blood never meets the added oxygen."]},{"h":"Dead space versus shunt","points":["Dead space: ventilation without perfusion, V/Q approaches infinity; alveolar gas looks like inspired air. Example: pulmonary embolism.","Shunt: perfusion without ventilation, V/Q = 0; blood leaves with mixed venous gas (PO2 about 40, PCO2 about 46 mmHg). Examples: airway obstruction, alveoli filled with pus in pneumonia or with fluid in edema, atelectasis.","Shunt lowers PaO2; PaCO2 usually stays normal or low because the remaining units can blow off extra CO2 but cannot add extra O2 to blood that is already saturated.","Dead space alone would raise PaCO2, but the patient usually hyperventilates, so PaCO2 is often low in embolism.","Embolism also lowers PaO2 because blood diverted to the remaining vessels overperfuses those alveoli, creating low V/Q units."]},{"h":"Diffusion and DLCO","points":["Fick law: gas transfer = (surface area / thickness) x diffusion constant x partial pressure difference.","Thicker barrier (fibrosis, edema) or smaller area (emphysema, lung resection) means slower transfer.","Perfusion-limited gas (N2O, O2 at rest): equilibrates early in the capillary, so only more blood flow raises uptake.","Diffusion-limited gas (CO; O2 in fibrosis or during strenuous exercise at altitude): does not equilibrate before blood leaves the capillary.","Resting red cells spend about 0.75 s in the capillary and O2 equilibrates in about 0.25 s, leaving a safety margin that disease and exercise use up.","DLCO is low in emphysema and pulmonary fibrosis, and normal in chronic bronchitis and asthma."]},{"h":"Hypoxemia versus hypoxia","points":["Hypoxemia means low PaO2 in arterial blood; hypoxia means too little O2 reaching or used by tissue.","O2 content = (1.34 x Hb x SaO2) + (0.003 x PaO2); normal about 20 mL O2/dL.","Hypoxemic hypoxia: low PaO2, low SaO2, low content.","Anemic hypoxia: normal PaO2 and SaO2, low content from low hemoglobin.","Carbon monoxide: normal PaO2, low true SaO2 by co-oximetry (pulse oximetry reads falsely normal), low content, and a left-shifted curve.","Circulatory hypoxia (shock, heart failure, arterial blockage): normal PaO2, SaO2, and content, but low flow delivers too little.","Histotoxic hypoxia (cyanide blocks cytochrome c oxidase): normal PaO2, SaO2, and content; tissues cannot use O2, so venous O2 stays high and lactate rises."]},{"h":"High altitude","points":["Lower barometric pressure lowers inspired and alveolar PO2, giving hypoxemia with a normal A-a gradient.","Peripheral chemoreceptors drive hyperventilation, which lowers PaCO2 and causes respiratory alkalosis.","Over days the kidneys excrete bicarbonate, correcting pH and allowing ventilation to rise further.","Erythropoietin rises, raising hemoglobin over weeks; 2,3-BPG rises, shifting the curve right to help unloading.","Generalized hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction causes pulmonary hypertension and, over time, right ventricular hypertrophy.","Acute mountain sickness: headache, nausea, fatigue, poor sleep within hours of a fast ascent, usually above about 2500 m.","High altitude pulmonary edema: uneven vasoconstriction overloads the less constricted capillaries, which leak; dyspnea at rest and cough.","High altitude cerebral edema: confusion and ataxia; it can progress to coma. Descent and oxygen are the key responses to both edemas."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Causes of hypoxemia","cols":["Cause","PaCO2","A-a gradient","Corrects with supplemental O2","Example"],"rows":[["Low inspired O2","Normal or low","Normal","Yes","High altitude"],["Hypoventilation","High","Normal","Yes","Opioid overdose, neuromuscular disease"],["Diffusion limitation","Normal or low","Widened","Yes","Pulmonary fibrosis"],["V/Q mismatch","Normal, low, or high","Widened","Yes","COPD, asthma, embolism"],["Right-to-left shunt","Normal or low","Widened","Poorly","Pneumonia with filled alveoli, ARDS, atelectasis"]]},{"title":"Types of hypoxia","cols":["Type","PaO2","SaO2","O2 content","Example"],"rows":[["Hypoxemic","Low","Low","Low","Altitude, lung disease"],["Anemic","Normal","Normal","Low","Iron deficiency anemia"],["Carbon monoxide","Normal","Low by co-oximetry","Low","Smoke inhalation, faulty heater"],["Circulatory","Normal","Normal","Normal","Shock, heart failure"],["Histotoxic","Normal","Normal","Normal (venous O2 high)","Cyanide poisoning"]]},{"title":"Dead space versus shunt","cols":["Feature","Dead space","Shunt"],"rows":[["V/Q","Approaches infinity","Zero"],["Problem","Ventilated, not perfused","Perfused, not ventilated"],["Classic example","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumonia, pulmonary edema, airway obstruction"],["Effect on PaO2","Falls (from overperfused remaining units)","Falls"],["Response to 100 percent O2","Improves","Poor"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Gradient sorts the causes","text":"Normal A-a gradient: the problem is outside the lung (thin air or too little breathing). Then PaCO2 splits them: high means hypoventilation, normal or low means altitude."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Shunt ignores oxygen","text":"Blood that never passes a ventilated alveolus never sees the extra oxygen. If 100 percent O2 barely moves the PaO2, think shunt."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Which number is low","text":"Ask which of PaO2, SaO2, and content is low. All three low: hypoxemic. PaO2 normal but content low: anemia (SaO2 normal) or CO (true SaO2 low). None low: circulatory or histotoxic, then look at venous O2."}],"highYield":["PAO2 = FiO2 x (Patm minus 47) minus PaCO2/0.8, about 100 mmHg at sea level.","Normal A-a gradient with hypoxemia means hypoventilation or low inspired O2.","Shunt is the hypoxemia that does not correct with 100 percent oxygen.","Pulmonary embolism is the classic dead space problem; pneumonia filling alveoli is the classic shunt.","CO is diffusion-limited; N2O and resting O2 are perfusion-limited.","DLCO is low in emphysema and fibrosis, normal in asthma and chronic bronchitis.","Cyanide: normal PaO2, high venous O2, lactic acidosis.","Altitude: hyperventilation, respiratory alkalosis, renal bicarbonate loss, more EPO and 2,3-BPG."],"pitfalls":["Pulse oximetry reads near normal in CO poisoning because it cannot tell carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin; co-oximetry shows the true low SaO2.","Anemia lowers O2 content but leaves PaO2 and SaO2 normal.","Shunt rarely raises PaCO2 because healthy units can blow off extra CO2; it is oxygen that cannot be made up.","Pure hypoventilation gives a high PaCO2 with a normal gradient; a high PaCO2 with a widened gradient means lung disease is present, such as severe COPD with V/Q mismatch, alone or on top of hypoventilation.","Altitude hypoxemia has a normal A-a gradient; HAPE adds a widened gradient once edema develops."],"dumps":[{"t":"Working up hypoxemia","p":"Write the alveolar gas equation and calculate PAO2 at sea level on room air with a PaCO2 of 40 mmHg. Then build a flow chart that sorts the five causes of hypoxemia using the A-a gradient, PaCO2, and the response to 100 percent oxygen.","l":["PAO2 = FiO2 x (Patm minus 47) minus PaCO2/0.8","0.21 x 713 = about 150; minus 50 = about 100 mmHg","Normal gradient with high PaCO2: hypoventilation","Normal gradient with normal or low PaCO2: low inspired O2","Widened gradient that corrects with O2: V/Q mismatch or diffusion limitation","Widened gradient that does not correct: shunt","V/Q mismatch is the most common cause"]},{"t":"Four kinds of hypoxia","p":"Make a table of hypoxemic, anemic, circulatory, and histotoxic hypoxia, plus carbon monoxide poisoning. For each, write PaO2, SaO2, O2 content, venous O2, and one cause.","l":["Hypoxemic: all three low","Anemic: PaO2 and SaO2 normal, content low","CO: PaO2 normal, true SaO2 low, content low, pulse oximetry misleading","Circulatory: all normal, delivery low from poor flow","Histotoxic: all normal, venous O2 high","Cyanide blocks cytochrome c oxidase and causes lactic acidosis","O2 content = 1.34 x Hb x SaO2 + 0.003 x PaO2"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Principles of Gas Exchange; Diffusion of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide Through the Respiratory Membrane"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Aviation, High Altitude, and Space Physiology"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-5","PU-6","PU-8","PU-14-E2","PU-14-A2"]},"PU-8":{"id":"PU-8","summary":"How the blood carries oxygen and carbon dioxide, what shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve, and how to read a blood gas when breathing is the problem. Exam cases turn on O2 content math, carbon monoxide and methemoglobin traps, and judging whether renal compensation is the expected amount.","notes":[{"h":"Oxygen content","points":["CaO2 = (1.34 x Hb x SaO2) + (0.003 x PaO2), in mL O2 per dL of blood.","Normal: Hb 15 g/dL, SaO2 0.98, PaO2 95 mmHg gives about 20 mL O2/dL; dissolved O2 adds only about 0.3 of that.","PaO2 is the pressure of dissolved O2; it sets SaO2 but says nothing about how much hemoglobin there is.","Anemia: PaO2 and SaO2 normal, content low (Hb 7.5 g/dL halves content to about 10 mL/dL).","Polycythemia: PaO2 and SaO2 normal, content high.","O2 delivery = cardiac output x CaO2, so low content can be partly offset by a higher cardiac output."]},{"h":"The dissociation curve","points":["Sigmoid shape comes from cooperative binding: each O2 that binds makes the next bind more easily.","T (tense) state is deoxyhemoglobin with low O2 affinity; R (relaxed) state is oxyhemoglobin with high affinity.","P50 (PO2 at 50 percent saturation) is about 27 mmHg; a right shift raises P50, a left shift lowers it.","Right shift (easier unloading): more H+ (lower pH), more CO2, higher temperature, more 2,3-BPG, exercise.","Left shift (tighter binding): fetal hemoglobin, carbon monoxide, methemoglobin, alkalosis, low temperature, low 2,3-BPG.","The flat top keeps saturation near 90 percent until PaO2 drops below about 60 mmHg; the steep part sits at tissue PO2 levels."]},{"h":"Carbon dioxide transport","points":["About 70 percent travels as bicarbonate, about 20 to 25 percent as carbaminohemoglobin, and about 5 to 10 percent dissolved.","In red cells, carbonic anhydrase makes CO2 + H2O into H2CO3, which splits into H+ and HCO3-.","HCO3- leaves the red cell in exchange for Cl- through band 3 (the chloride shift); the reverse happens in the lungs.","H+ is buffered by deoxyhemoglobin, which is a better buffer than oxyhemoglobin."]},{"h":"Bohr and Haldane effects","points":["Bohr effect (tissues): high CO2 and H+ lower hemoglobin O2 affinity, so O2 unloads where metabolism is high.","Haldane effect (lungs): oxygenation of hemoglobin lowers its affinity for CO2 and H+, so CO2 is released into the alveoli.","In tissues the two work together: unloading O2 makes deoxyhemoglobin, which picks up more CO2 and H+."]},{"h":"Carbon monoxide and methemoglobin","points":["CO binds hemoglobin with about 200 times the affinity of O2, takes up binding sites, and shifts the curve left.","In CO poisoning PaO2 is normal (dissolved O2 is unaffected), true SaO2 and content are low, and standard pulse oximetry reads falsely normal.","Methemoglobin has ferric (Fe3+) iron that cannot carry O2, and it left-shifts the remaining ferrous hemes.","Methemoglobinemia gives chocolate-colored blood and cyanosis that does not improve with O2; pulse oximetry tends to read about 85 percent.","Common causes of methemoglobinemia: nitrites, dapsone, benzocaine and other topical anesthetics.","Co-oximetry measures carboxyhemoglobin and methemoglobin directly and is the test that settles both."]},{"h":"Respiratory acid-base","points":["Normal: pH 7.35 to 7.45, PaCO2 35 to 45 mmHg, HCO3- 22 to 26 mEq/L.","Respiratory acidosis (hypoventilation): HCO3- rises about 1 mEq/L per 10 mmHg PaCO2 acutely and 3 to 4 mEq/L chronically.","Respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation): HCO3- falls about 2 mEq/L per 10 mmHg acutely and 4 to 5 mEq/L chronically.","Renal compensation takes 3 to 5 days, which is why acute and chronic values differ.","If the measured HCO3- is clearly off the expected value, a second (metabolic) disorder is present."]}],"charts":[{"title":"What happens to the oxygen numbers","cols":["Condition","PaO2","True SaO2","Pulse oximeter reading","O2 content"],"rows":[["Anemia","Normal","Normal","Normal","Low"],["Polycythemia","Normal","Normal","Normal","High"],["Carbon monoxide poisoning","Normal","Low","Falsely normal","Low"],["Methemoglobinemia","Normal","Low","About 85 percent","Low"],["Hypoventilation or high altitude","Low","Low","Low","Low"]]},{"title":"Expected compensation in respiratory disorders","cols":["Disorder","Change in HCO3- per 10 mmHg change in PaCO2","Worked example","pH in the example"],"rows":[["Acute respiratory acidosis","Up about 1 mEq/L","PaCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3- 26 mEq/L","About 7.26"],["Chronic respiratory acidosis","Up 3 to 4 mEq/L","PaCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3- 31 mEq/L","About 7.34"],["Acute respiratory alkalosis","Down about 2 mEq/L","PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 22 mEq/L","About 7.49"],["Chronic respiratory alkalosis","Down 4 to 5 mEq/L","PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 19 to 20 mEq/L","About 7.44"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CADET, face Right","text":"Right shift factors: CO2, Acid (H+), 2,3-DPG (2,3-BPG), Exercise, Temperature."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pressure, saturation, content","text":"Ask three separate questions: Is PaO2 low (lungs)? Is SaO2 low (lungs or abnormal hemoglobin)? Is content low (any of these, or too little hemoglobin)? Anemia only fails the third."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Which gas pushes which","text":"Bohr: CO2 and H+ push O2 off hemoglobin in the tissues. Haldane: O2 pushes CO2 and H+ off hemoglobin in the lungs."}],"highYield":["Anemia lowers O2 content with a normal PaO2 and normal SaO2.","CO poisoning: normal PaO2, falsely normal pulse oximetry, low true saturation, left-shifted curve.","Chocolate-colored blood with cyanosis that does not respond to O2 means methemoglobinemia.","Fetal hemoglobin binds 2,3-BPG poorly, so its curve sits to the left of adult hemoglobin.","About 70 percent of CO2 is carried as bicarbonate made by red cell carbonic anhydrase.","The chloride shift swaps HCO3- for Cl- through band 3, so venous red cells hold more chloride.","Chronic respiratory acidosis raises HCO3- 3 to 4 mEq/L per 10 mmHg PaCO2; acute raises it only about 1."],"pitfalls":["PaO2 measures dissolved O2, so it stays normal in anemia, CO poisoning, and methemoglobinemia.","CO and methemoglobin both shift the curve left; they do not make the remaining hemoglobin release O2 more easily.","Bohr and Haldane are easy to swap: Bohr changes O2 binding, Haldane changes CO2 binding.","A PaCO2 of 60 mmHg with HCO3- of 20 mEq/L is not compensation; HCO3- should be up, so a metabolic acidosis is also present."],"dumps":[{"t":"Oxyhemoglobin curve","p":"Draw the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve with PO2 on the x axis and saturation on the y axis. Mark P50, then draw a right-shifted and a left-shifted curve and list what causes each.","l":["Sigmoid shape from cooperative binding","P50 at about 27 mmHg","Arterial point near PO2 100 mmHg, about 97 to 98 percent saturated","Mixed venous point near PO2 40 mmHg, about 75 percent saturated","Right shift: CO2, H+, 2,3-BPG, exercise, temperature","Left shift: fetal hemoglobin, CO, methemoglobin, alkalosis, cold","T state low affinity, R state high affinity"]},{"t":"CO2 from tissue to lung","p":"Draw a red cell in a tissue capillary and a red cell in a pulmonary capillary. Trace what happens to CO2 in each, including carbonic anhydrase, the chloride shift, and the Bohr and Haldane effects.","l":["CO2 enters the red cell and carbonic anhydrase makes H2CO3","H2CO3 splits into H+ and HCO3-","HCO3- exits in exchange for Cl- through band 3","H+ buffered by deoxyhemoglobin; Bohr effect unloads O2","Some CO2 binds hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin","In the lung, oxygenation releases H+ and CO2 (Haldane effect)","HCO3- re-enters, Cl- leaves, CO2 is re-formed and exhaled","Rough shares: about 70 percent bicarbonate, 20 to 25 percent carbamino, 5 to 10 percent dissolved"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide in Blood and Tissue Fluids"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Acid-Base Regulation"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Globular Proteins"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-7","PU-11","PU-6"]},"PU-9":{"id":"PU-9","summary":"The molecules that keep the lung working: surfactant, elastin, mucus, the antiprotease shield, and the antioxidant enzymes, plus the vitamins that support them. Questions link a biochemical failure to a lung disease, such as elastase winning over alpha-1 antitrypsin in emphysema or delayed surfactant in infants of diabetic mothers.","notes":[{"h":"Surfactant","points":["Made by type II pneumocytes and stored in lamellar bodies.","Main lipid is dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC, a lecithin); phosphatidylglycerol is a smaller but important part.","Surfactant proteins: SP-B and SP-C (hydrophobic) spread the film; SP-A and SP-D are collectins that also act as opsonins.","Lowers surface tension most in small alveoli, keeping them open at end expiration.","Fetal cortisol and thyroid hormone increase synthesis, which is why antenatal corticosteroids speed lung maturity.","Fetal hyperinsulinemia (maternal diabetes) slows synthesis, so these infants get respiratory distress at a later gestational age.","Maturity markers in amniotic fluid: lecithin to sphingomyelin ratio above 2.0 and phosphatidylglycerol present."]},{"h":"Proteases and antiproteases","points":["Neutrophil elastase can digest alveolar wall elastin; alpha-1 antitrypsin (a serpin made in the liver) inhibits it.","Cigarette smoke recruits neutrophils and macrophages, which release elastase and matrix metalloproteinases.","Oxidants in smoke oxidize a methionine in the reactive site of alpha-1 antitrypsin and inactivate it.","More protease plus less antiprotease tips the balance toward alveolar wall destruction (emphysema)."]},{"h":"Elastin and collagen","points":["Elastin is rich in glycine, proline, and lysine; desmosine cross-links let it stretch and recoil.","Lysyl oxidase, a copper-dependent enzyme, makes these cross-links (and collagen cross-links).","Elastin loss in emphysema raises compliance and lowers recoil, so air is trapped.","Collagen deposition in fibrosis lowers compliance and raises recoil, so lung volumes shrink."]},{"h":"Vitamins","points":["Retinoic acid keeps respiratory epithelium differentiated as ciliated and mucus-secreting cells.","Vitamin A deficiency causes squamous (keratinizing) metaplasia, loss of cilia and goblet cells, and more respiratory infections.","Cystic fibrosis blocks pancreatic enzyme release, fats are poorly absorbed, and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K run low.","Results: night blindness (A), rickets or osteomalacia (D), hemolysis and neuropathy (E), bleeding (K)."]},{"h":"Oxidant injury","points":["High inspired O2 and cigarette smoke generate superoxide, hydrogen peroxide, and hydroxyl radicals.","These damage membrane lipids, proteins, and DNA in alveolar epithelium and capillary endothelium.","Superoxide dismutase turns superoxide into hydrogen peroxide; catalase turns hydrogen peroxide into water and O2.","Glutathione peroxidase uses selenium and reduced glutathione to clear peroxides; NADPH regenerates glutathione.","Prolonged high FiO2 causes diffuse alveolar damage in adults and adds to bronchopulmonary dysplasia in neonates."]},{"h":"Mucus","points":["Mucins are large glycoproteins with heavy O-linked glycosylation on serine and threonine, added in the Golgi.","Secreted by goblet cells in the epithelium and by submucosal glands.","Chronic bronchitis: submucosal gland hyperplasia (Reid index above 0.4) and goblet cell increase make excess mucus.","Cystic fibrosis: too little water in the airway surface liquid makes mucus thick and hard for cilia to move."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Lung molecules and the diseases that follow when they fail","cols":["Molecule","Made by","Job","Disease when it fails"],"rows":[["Surfactant (DPPC, SP-A to SP-D)","Type II pneumocytes","Lowers surface tension","Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome"],["Alpha-1 antitrypsin","Hepatocytes","Inhibits neutrophil elastase","Panacinar emphysema"],["Elastin","Fibroblasts, cross-linked by lysyl oxidase","Elastic recoil","Emphysema (loss), poor recoil"],["Collagen","Fibroblasts and myofibroblasts","Tensile strength","Fibrosis when excess, low compliance"],["Mucins","Goblet cells, submucosal glands","Trap particles for clearance","Chronic bronchitis, cystic fibrosis plugging"],["Glutathione peroxidase","Most cells, needs selenium","Clears peroxides","Oxidant lung injury"]]},{"title":"Antioxidant enzymes","cols":["Enzyme","Reaction","Cofactor"],"rows":[["Superoxide dismutase","Superoxide to hydrogen peroxide","Copper and zinc (cytosol), manganese (mitochondria)"],["Catalase","Hydrogen peroxide to water and O2","Heme iron"],["Glutathione peroxidase","Hydrogen peroxide and lipid peroxides to water and alcohols","Selenium"],["Glutathione reductase","Regenerates reduced glutathione","FAD (riboflavin) and NADPH"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Steroids speed, insulin slows","text":"Cortisol and thyroid hormone push surfactant production forward. Insulin from the infant of a diabetic mother holds it back."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Floppy versus stiff","text":"Lose elastin and the lung is floppy (high compliance, emphysema). Add collagen and it is stiff (low compliance, fibrosis)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ADEK","text":"The fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K, which run low whenever fat absorption fails, as in cystic fibrosis."}],"highYield":["DPPC (lecithin) is the main surfactant lipid; an L/S ratio above 2.0 signals lung maturity.","Antenatal corticosteroids are given before preterm birth to increase surfactant.","Smoke both recruits elastase-releasing cells and oxidizes alpha-1 antitrypsin.","Lysyl oxidase needs copper to cross-link elastin and collagen.","Vitamin A deficiency causes squamous metaplasia of airway epithelium and more respiratory infections.","Selenium is the cofactor for glutathione peroxidase.","Reid index above 0.4 means submucosal gland hyperplasia of chronic bronchitis."],"pitfalls":["Maternal diabetes delays surfactant through fetal insulin, even in a term-sized infant.","SP-A and SP-D are immune opsonins; SP-B and SP-C do most of the surface tension work.","Emphysema raises compliance; fibrosis lowers it. Both lower DLCO.","Catalase and glutathione peroxidase both remove hydrogen peroxide; only glutathione peroxidase needs selenium."],"dumps":[{"t":"Protease and antiprotease balance","p":"Draw an alveolar wall with a neutrophil releasing elastase and alpha-1 antitrypsin blocking it. Add what cigarette smoke does to each side.","l":["Neutrophil elastase digests elastin in alveolar walls","Alpha-1 antitrypsin from the liver inhibits elastase","Smoke recruits neutrophils and macrophages","Macrophages release matrix metalloproteinases","Smoke oxidants inactivate alpha-1 antitrypsin","Net result: alveolar wall loss and emphysema","Loss of elastin raises compliance and lowers recoil"]},{"t":"Surfactant from cell to alveolus","p":"Draw a type II pneumocyte releasing surfactant into an alveolus. Label the components, the hormones that raise and lower production, and the tests of fetal lung maturity.","l":["Lamellar bodies store surfactant","DPPC is the main lipid; phosphatidylglycerol also present","SP-B and SP-C spread the film; SP-A and SP-D opsonize","Lowers surface tension, keeps small alveoli open","Cortisol and thyroid hormone increase production","Fetal insulin decreases production","L/S ratio above 2.0 and phosphatidylglycerol indicate maturity"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Phospholipid, Glycosphingolipid, and Eicosanoid Metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycoproteins"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung (section on obstructive lung diseases)"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-10","PU-14-B4","PU-14-A1","PU-14-G1"]},"PU-10":{"id":"PU-10","summary":"The single-gene lung diseases (cystic fibrosis, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, primary ciliary dyskinesia) and the cell cycle genes that tobacco damages in lung cancer. Exam questions ask you to connect the mutation to the protein defect and then to the organ findings.","notes":[{"h":"Cystic fibrosis","points":["Autosomal recessive; CFTR gene on chromosome 7; carrier frequency about 1 in 25 in people of Northern European descent.","CFTR is an ATP-gated chloride channel in the ABC transporter family; it also restrains sodium uptake through ENaC.","F508 deletion (the most common allele) makes a misfolded protein that is held in the ER, ubiquitinated, and destroyed by the proteasome.","Airways: less chloride secretion and more sodium absorption pull water out, leaving thick, dehydrated mucus.","Sweat glands: chloride cannot be reabsorbed, so sweat chloride is high (60 mmol/L or more is diagnostic).","Other findings: meconium ileus, pancreatic insufficiency, male infertility from absent vas deferens, recurrent Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas infections."]},{"h":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","points":["SERPINA1 gene on chromosome 14; alleles are codominant (PiMM normal, PiMZ intermediate, PiZZ severe).","The Z protein misfolds and polymerizes in the hepatocyte ER, so little reaches the blood.","Lung: unopposed elastase causes panacinar emphysema, worst in the lower lobes, often before age 45 in smokers.","Liver: retained protein injures hepatocytes and causes hepatitis and cirrhosis.","Liver biopsy shows PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules in hepatocytes."]},{"h":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia","points":["Autosomal recessive defects of dynein arms make cilia immotile or poorly beating.","Mucociliary clearance fails: chronic sinusitis, otitis media, recurrent pneumonia, bronchiectasis.","Sperm flagella share the same axoneme, so men are infertile; women have reduced fertility and more ectopic pregnancy.","Embryonic nodal cilia set left-right orientation; without them sidedness is random, so about half have situs inversus.","Kartagener syndrome is the triad of situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis."]},{"h":"Cell cycle brakes and tobacco","points":["Rb binds E2F and blocks the G1 to S transition; cyclin D with CDK4 and CDK6 phosphorylates Rb to release E2F.","p53 senses DNA damage and induces p21, which inhibits CDKs and holds the cell in G1 for repair or apoptosis.","Benzo[a]pyrene is activated by cytochrome P450 to a diol epoxide that binds guanine and forms DNA adducts.","Unrepaired adducts cause G to T transversions, including in TP53 hot spots, which removes the G1 brake."]},{"h":"Driver mutations in lung cancer","points":["Adenocarcinoma: activating EGFR mutations, ALK rearrangements (EML4-ALK), and KRAS mutations.","EGFR mutations are most common in nonsmokers, women, and people of East Asian ancestry.","KRAS mutations are more common in smokers; KRAS and EGFR mutations rarely occur in the same tumor.","Small cell carcinoma: loss of TP53 and RB1 in nearly all tumors.","EGFR and ALK changes matter because targeted kinase inhibitors exist for them."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Genetic lung diseases","cols":["Disease","Gene and inheritance","Protein defect","Lung finding","Other organs"],"rows":[["Cystic fibrosis","CFTR, chromosome 7, autosomal recessive","Misfolded chloride channel degraded by proteasome (F508 deletion)","Thick mucus, bronchiectasis, Pseudomonas","Pancreatic insufficiency, meconium ileus, high sweat chloride, male infertility"],["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","SERPINA1, chromosome 14, codominant","Misfolded protein trapped in hepatocyte ER","Panacinar emphysema, lower lobes","Cirrhosis with PAS-positive globules"],["Primary ciliary dyskinesia","Dynein arm genes, autosomal recessive","Immotile cilia","Bronchiectasis, recurrent pneumonia","Sinusitis, otitis, infertility, situs inversus in about half"]]},{"title":"Lung cancer drivers","cols":["Tumor","Common alteration","Who gets it"],"rows":[["Adenocarcinoma","EGFR activating mutation","Nonsmokers, women, East Asian ancestry"],["Adenocarcinoma","ALK rearrangement (EML4-ALK)","Younger nonsmokers"],["Adenocarcinoma","KRAS mutation","Smokers"],["Small cell carcinoma","TP53 and RB1 loss","Heavy smokers"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Same misfolding story, two endings","text":"Both CF and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency start with a protein that misfolds in the ER. CFTR is destroyed, so the channel is missing. The Z protein piles up, so the liver is injured and the lung is unprotected."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Cilia do three jobs","text":"Clear mucus, move sperm, and set left from right in the embryo. Lose dynein and all three fail: bronchiectasis, infertility, and situs inversus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Small cell loses both brakes","text":"Small cell carcinoma knocks out both G1 brakes, TP53 and RB1, which fits its fast growth."}],"highYield":["F508 deletion in CFTR causes a folding defect; the protein never reaches the membrane.","Sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or more confirms cystic fibrosis.","PiZZ alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency gives lower lobe panacinar emphysema plus cirrhosis.","PAS-positive, diastase-resistant hepatocyte globules point to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency.","Situs inversus with bronchiectasis and sinusitis is Kartagener syndrome.","Benzo[a]pyrene adducts cause G to T transversions in TP53.","EGFR-mutant adenocarcinoma is the classic lung cancer in a woman who never smoked."],"pitfalls":["Smoking-related emphysema is centriacinar and upper lobe; alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is panacinar and lower lobe.","Alpha-1 antitrypsin alleles are codominant, so PiMZ carriers have intermediate levels.","In CF the sweat chloride is high because reabsorption fails; in the airway the problem is too little secretion.","Only about half of patients with primary ciliary dyskinesia have situs inversus, so normal anatomy does not exclude it."],"dumps":[{"t":"CFTR from gene to symptoms","p":"Trace cystic fibrosis from the gene to the bedside. Start with the CFTR gene, show what F508 deletion does to the protein in the cell, then branch to airway, pancreas, sweat gland, and reproductive tract findings.","l":["Autosomal recessive, CFTR on chromosome 7","F508 deletion causes misfolding in the ER","Misfolded protein ubiquitinated and degraded by the proteasome","Airway: low chloride secretion, high sodium uptake, dehydrated mucus","Bronchiectasis with Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas","Pancreas: duct plugging, fat malabsorption, ADEK deficiency","Sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or more","Male infertility from absent vas deferens"]},{"t":"G1 checkpoint and tobacco","p":"Draw the G1 to S checkpoint with Rb, E2F, cyclin D with CDK4/6, p53, and p21. Then show where benzo[a]pyrene damage enters and what happens when TP53 is mutated.","l":["Rb bound to E2F blocks S phase entry","Cyclin D with CDK4/6 phosphorylates Rb and frees E2F","DNA damage activates p53","p53 induces p21, which inhibits CDKs","Arrest allows repair or leads to apoptosis","Benzo[a]pyrene diol epoxide forms guanine adducts","G to T transversions inactivate TP53, removing the brake"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases (section on cystic fibrosis)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-14-G1","PU-14-B4","PU-14-B2","PU-14-F","PU-9"]},"PU-11":{"id":"PU-11","summary":"Why the body needs oxygen at all: O2 is the last electron acceptor that lets mitochondria make most ATP. This unit covers what happens when O2 delivery or use fails (lactic acidosis, cyanide and CO), the respiratory quotient, 2,3-BPG, and the energy cost of breathing in emphysema.","notes":[{"h":"Oxygen and ATP","points":["Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) passes electrons to O2, making water.","Electron flow pumps protons out of the matrix; ATP synthase uses that gradient to make ATP.","Full oxidation of glucose yields about 30 to 32 ATP; glycolysis alone yields 2.","Without O2, NADH builds up and the electron transport chain stops.","Lactate dehydrogenase turns pyruvate into lactate to regenerate NAD+ so glycolysis can continue.","Result: lactic acidosis, an anion gap metabolic acidosis (lactate normally below about 2 mmol/L)."]},{"h":"Cyanide and carbon monoxide","points":["Smoke from burning plastics, foam, wool, and silk releases hydrogen cyanide; any fire releases CO.","Cyanide binds the ferric iron of cytochrome a3 in complex IV and stops O2 use.","CO also binds complex IV in addition to its effect on hemoglobin.","PaO2 stays normal because O2 still reaches the blood; cells cannot use it, so lactate rises.","In cyanide poisoning tissues extract little O2, so venous O2 saturation is high and venous blood looks bright red.","Hydroxocobalamin binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin; sodium thiosulfate helps rhodanese convert cyanide to thiocyanate."]},{"h":"Respiratory quotient","points":["RQ = CO2 produced divided by O2 consumed.","Carbohydrate 1.0 (glucose + 6 O2 makes 6 CO2), fat about 0.7, protein about 0.8; a mixed diet is about 0.8.","Example: VCO2 200 mL/min and VO2 250 mL/min gives RQ 0.8.","Heavy carbohydrate feeding raises CO2 production, and RQ above 1.0 means net fat synthesis.","More CO2 means more ventilation needed, a problem for patients with COPD or those weaning from a ventilator."]},{"h":"2,3-BPG","points":["Made in red cells by a side path of glycolysis: bisphosphoglycerate mutase converts 1,3-BPG to 2,3-BPG.","The shunt skips the phosphoglycerate kinase step, so the red cell gives up one ATP for each molecule routed through it.","2,3-BPG binds the central cavity of deoxyhemoglobin, stabilizes the T state, and shifts the curve right.","High altitude: hypoxia and respiratory alkalosis stimulate glycolysis, raising 2,3-BPG within hours to days.","Pyruvate kinase deficiency backs up glycolytic intermediates, raising 2,3-BPG and improving O2 unloading despite anemia.","Hexokinase deficiency and stored blood lower 2,3-BPG and shift the curve left."]},{"h":"Work of breathing in emphysema","points":["Normally breathing uses only a few percent of total O2 consumption.","In severe emphysema, air trapping and a flattened diaphragm make each breath cost far more energy.","Resting energy expenditure rises while dyspnea and early fullness cut food intake.","Result: weight loss and muscle wasting, and a low body mass index predicts worse survival."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Respiratory quotient by fuel","cols":["Fuel","RQ","Effect on CO2 load"],"rows":[["Fat","About 0.7","Lowest CO2 per O2 used"],["Protein","About 0.8","Intermediate"],["Carbohydrate","1.0","Highest of the three"],["Net fat synthesis from excess carbohydrate","Above 1.0","CO2 production rises further"]]},{"title":"When cells run short of usable oxygen","cols":["Cause","PaO2","Arterial O2 content","Venous O2 saturation","Lactate"],"rows":[["Hypoxemia (lung disease)","Low","Low","Low","High if severe"],["Severe anemia","Normal","Low","Low","High if severe"],["Carbon monoxide","Normal","Low","Low","High"],["Cyanide","Normal","Normal","High","High"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cyanide leaves the oxygen in the veins","text":"If the tissues cannot use O2, the blood comes back almost as full as it left. High venous saturation plus lactic acidosis points to cyanide."},{"kind":"trick","label":"RQ counts carbon versus oxygen","text":"Carbohydrate already carries a lot of oxygen in its structure, so it needs less inhaled O2 per CO2 made (RQ 1.0). Fat is mostly carbon and hydrogen, so it needs more O2 (RQ 0.7)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Blocks below raise BPG","text":"A glycolytic block below the 2,3-BPG shunt (pyruvate kinase) raises it; a block above the shunt (hexokinase) lowers it."}],"highYield":["O2 is the final electron acceptor at complex IV; without it, cells switch to lactate production.","Cyanide: normal PaO2, high venous O2 saturation, lactic acidosis.","Smoke inhalation can cause both CO and cyanide poisoning; hydroxocobalamin is the cyanide antidote of choice.","RQ is 1.0 for carbohydrate, about 0.7 for fat, and about 0.8 for protein.","High carbohydrate feeding raises CO2 production and ventilatory demand.","Pyruvate kinase deficiency and high altitude raise 2,3-BPG and shift the curve right.","Weight loss in severe emphysema reflects the high energy cost of breathing."],"pitfalls":["Cyanide and CO both leave PaO2 normal; CO lowers O2 content while cyanide does not.","Lactic acidosis is a metabolic acidosis with a high anion gap, even though the root problem is O2 delivery or use.","Fetal red cells contain 2,3-BPG; the left shift comes from gamma chains that bind it poorly.","An RQ above 1.0 means net lipogenesis; it cannot come from burning carbohydrate alone."],"dumps":[{"t":"Electron transport and its poisons","p":"Draw the electron transport chain from NADH to O2 with complexes I to IV and ATP synthase. Mark where cyanide and CO act, and show what happens to pyruvate when the chain stops.","l":["NADH donates electrons at complex I; FADH2 at complex II","Electrons pass through coenzyme Q and cytochrome c","Complex IV passes electrons to O2, making water","Proton gradient drives ATP synthase","Cyanide and CO bind cytochrome a3 in complex IV","NADH builds up when the chain stops","Pyruvate becomes lactate to regenerate NAD+, causing lactic acidosis"]},{"t":"The 2,3-BPG shunt","p":"Draw red cell glycolysis from glucose to pyruvate, including the side path that makes 2,3-BPG. Mark where pyruvate kinase deficiency and hexokinase deficiency act and what each does to the oxyhemoglobin curve.","l":["Glucose to glucose-6-phosphate by hexokinase","1,3-BPG to 2,3-BPG by bisphosphoglycerate mutase","2,3-BPG returns to the pathway as 3-phosphoglycerate","Shunt costs one ATP per molecule routed through it","2,3-BPG stabilizes deoxyhemoglobin (T state)","Pyruvate kinase deficiency raises 2,3-BPG, right shift","Hexokinase deficiency lowers 2,3-BPG, left shift"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Bioenergetics and Oxidative Phosphorylation"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycolysis"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Aviation, High Altitude, and Space Physiology"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-8","PU-7","PU-14-B4"]},"PU-12":{"id":"PU-12","summary":"How the lung defends itself and how immune responses cause lung disease. Exam questions link a disease to its hypersensitivity type and key cytokine: IgE and Th2 in asthma, Th1 and TNF-alpha in granulomas, anti-GBM antibodies in Goodpasture syndrome, and conjugate vaccines that recruit T cell help.","notes":[{"h":"Innate lung defenses","points":["Nose traps large particles (above about 10 micrometers); smaller ones stick to airway mucus and ride the mucociliary escalator up to be coughed or swallowed.","Secretory IgA (a dimer with J chain and secretory component) blocks microbe attachment on mucosa.","Alveolar macrophages clear microbes and particles small enough to reach the alveoli (about 1 to 5 micrometers).","Surfactant proteins A and D bind microbes and act as opsonins for macrophages.","Smoking paralyzes cilia and impairs macrophages; alcohol blunts cough and gag reflexes and raises aspiration risk; ciliary defects stop clearance."]},{"h":"Allergic asthma (type I)","points":["Dendritic cells present allergen and drive Th2 cells.","IL-4 drives B cell class switching to IgE; IL-5 recruits and activates eosinophils; IL-13 drives mucus production and airway hyperreactivity.","IgE coats mast cells through FcεRI; re-exposure cross-links IgE and triggers degranulation.","Early phase (minutes): histamine, prostaglandin D2, and leukotrienes C4, D4, and E4 cause bronchoconstriction, mucus, and leak.","Late phase (about 4 to 8 hours): eosinophils and Th2 cells arrive; major basic protein injures epithelium; sputum may show Charcot-Leyden crystals."]},{"h":"Granulomas (type IV)","points":["Macrophages make IL-12, which drives Th1 cells.","Th1 cells make IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages into epithelioid cells and giant cells.","TNF-alpha holds the granuloma together.","TNF-alpha blockers can let latent tuberculosis reactivate, so patients are screened first.","Tuberculosis granulomas caseate; sarcoidosis granulomas do not."]},{"h":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","points":["Repeated inhalation of organic antigens: thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay (farmer lung), bird proteins.","Type III (precipitating IgG immune complexes) and type IV (T cell) reactions in the interstitium and small airways.","Histology: lymphocytic interstitial infiltrate with poorly formed noncaseating granulomas.","Acute episodes bring fever, cough, and dyspnea 4 to 6 hours after exposure; chronic exposure causes fibrosis.","Unlike asthma: no IgE, no eosinophilia, restrictive pattern, reduced DLCO."]},{"h":"Lung and kidney together","points":["Goodpasture syndrome: type II hypersensitivity; IgG against the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen.","Goodpasture causes pulmonary hemorrhage (hemoptysis) and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with linear IgG on immunofluorescence.","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis: PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA), necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis.","It hits the upper airway (sinusitis, nasal ulcers, saddle nose), lungs (cavitating nodules), and kidneys (pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis)."]},{"h":"Vaccines for respiratory pathogens","points":["Toxoids (diphtheria, tetanus) are inactivated toxins that induce neutralizing IgG antitoxin.","Acellular pertussis vaccine contains pertussis toxoid plus adhesins such as filamentous hemagglutinin and pertactin.","Plain polysaccharides are T cell independent: mostly IgM, little memory, weak response under age 2.","Conjugate vaccines (Hib, pneumococcal) link polysaccharide to a protein so B cells present carrier peptides to helper T cells.","T cell help gives class switching to IgG, affinity maturation, memory, and protection in infants."]},{"h":"Lung cancer and immunity","points":["Tumors lower MHC class I to hide from CD8 T cells.","Tumors express PD-L1, which binds PD-1 on T cells and switches them off; anti-PD-1 and anti-PD-L1 drugs block this.","Small cell carcinoma shares antigens with neurons, so antibodies against the tumor also hit nerves.","Anti-Hu: paraneoplastic encephalomyelitis and sensory neuronopathy.","Anti-voltage-gated calcium channel: Lambert-Eaton syndrome, proximal weakness that improves with repeated effort."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypersensitivity in lung disease","cols":["Type","Mechanism","Lung example","Key clue"],"rows":[["I (immediate)","IgE on mast cells, Th2 cytokines","Allergic asthma, allergic rhinitis","Wheeze within minutes, eosinophils, high IgE"],["II (antibody to fixed antigen)","IgG against tissue antigen","Goodpasture syndrome","Linear IgG on basement membrane, hemoptysis, hematuria"],["III (immune complex)","Antigen and IgG complexes, complement","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (acute)","Precipitating antibodies, fever hours after exposure"],["IV (delayed, T cell)","Th1 cells, IFN-gamma, TNF-alpha","Tuberculosis, sarcoidosis, berylliosis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Granulomas"]]},{"title":"Polysaccharide versus conjugate vaccine","cols":["Feature","Plain polysaccharide","Polysaccharide-protein conjugate"],"rows":[["T cell help","No (T cell independent)","Yes (T cell dependent)"],["Main antibody","IgM","IgG after class switching"],["Memory","Little","Yes"],["Works under age 2","Poorly","Yes"],["Examples","Older pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine","Hib, pneumococcal conjugate"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ACID","text":"Hypersensitivity types I to IV: Anaphylactic and Atopic (I), antibody-mediated Cytotoxic (II), Immune complex (III), Delayed cell-mediated (IV)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"4, 5, 13 in order","text":"IL-4 makes IgE, IL-5 brings eosinophils, IL-13 makes mucus. The Th2 story of asthma in three numbers."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Conjugate means a T cell is involved","text":"Sugar alone gets a B cell response only. Hook it to a protein and helper T cells join, which brings IgG and memory."}],"highYield":["Secretory IgA and surfactant proteins A and D are first-line lung defenses.","IL-4 drives IgE class switching; IL-5 drives eosinophils.","TNF-alpha blockade can reactivate latent tuberculosis.","Farmer lung is hypersensitivity pneumonitis from thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay.","Goodpasture: anti-type IV collagen, linear IgG, hemoptysis plus glomerulonephritis.","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis: PR3-ANCA with sinus, lung, and kidney disease.","Conjugate vaccines protect infants because they recruit helper T cells.","Lambert-Eaton syndrome with small cell carcinoma comes from antibodies to presynaptic calcium channels."],"pitfalls":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is restrictive and lymphocytic; asthma is obstructive and eosinophilic.","Goodpasture shows linear IgG; granulomatosis with polyangiitis is pauci-immune with little immunoglobulin deposit.","Sarcoidosis granulomas do not caseate; tuberculosis granulomas usually do.","Toxoid vaccines neutralize the toxin; a vaccinated person can still carry the bacterium."],"dumps":[{"t":"Allergic asthma, start to finish","p":"Draw the steps of allergic asthma from first exposure to the late phase. Include the dendritic cell, Th2 cell, B cell, mast cell, and eosinophil, and label the cytokines and mediators.","l":["Dendritic cell presents allergen to naive CD4 T cell","Th2 differentiation","IL-4 class switches B cells to IgE","IgE binds FcεRI on mast cells","Re-exposure cross-links IgE and causes degranulation","Early phase: histamine, leukotrienes, prostaglandin D2, bronchoconstriction","IL-5 recruits eosinophils in the late phase","IL-13 increases mucus and airway hyperreactivity"]},{"t":"Building a granuloma","p":"Draw a granuloma forming around Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Label the macrophage, Th1 cell, epithelioid cells, giant cell, and the cytokines, then show what happens when TNF-alpha is blocked.","l":["Macrophage ingests the organism and makes IL-12","IL-12 drives Th1 differentiation","Th1 cells make IFN-gamma","IFN-gamma activates macrophages into epithelioid cells","Epithelioid cells fuse into multinucleated giant cells","TNF-alpha maintains the granuloma","TNF-alpha blockade breaks the granuloma and reactivates TB"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Immunity to Tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-13","PU-14-B1","PU-14-A4","PU-14-H9","PU-14-F8"]},"PU-13":{"id":"PU-13","summary":"The bacteria, viruses, and fungi behind lung infections, with the lab features and virulence factors that identify each. Exam questions pair a host or exposure clue with an organism, then ask about its toxin, capsule, culture needs, or how it evades immunity.","notes":[{"h":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","points":["Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci; alpha-hemolytic (partial, green).","Optochin sensitive and bile soluble, which separates it from viridans streptococci.","Polysaccharide capsule is the main virulence factor; IgA protease and pneumolysin add to it.","Most common cause of community-acquired pneumonia and of lobar pneumonia; rust-colored sputum.","Asplenia raises risk of severe infection with encapsulated organisms like this one."]},{"h":"Gram-negative lung pathogens","points":["Haemophilus influenzae: small coccobacillus, needs factor X (hemin) and factor V (NAD+), grows on chocolate agar.","H. influenzae type b capsule causes invasive disease; nontypeable strains cause otitis, sinusitis, and COPD flares.","Klebsiella pneumoniae: lactose-fermenting rod with a thick mucoid capsule; alcoholics and aspiration; currant jelly sputum, abscesses.","Pseudomonas aeruginosa: oxidase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting aerobic rod; blue-green pigment; cystic fibrosis and ventilated patients.","Legionella pneumophila: stains poorly with Gram stain; grows on buffered charcoal yeast extract agar with iron and cysteine.","Legionella spreads from contaminated water (cooling towers, plumbing), not person to person; hyponatremia and diarrhea are clues; urine antigen test."]},{"h":"Toxin-driven infections","points":["Corynebacterium diphtheriae: gram-positive club-shaped rods; the toxin gene comes from a prophage (lysogenic conversion).","Diphtheria toxin ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 and stops protein synthesis, killing cells.","Clinical result: gray pseudomembrane in the pharynx, bull neck, myocarditis, and cranial nerve palsies.","Bordetella pertussis: gram-negative coccobacillus; pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates Gi, so Gi cannot inhibit adenylyl cyclase and cAMP rises.","Clinical result: catarrhal stage, then paroxysmal cough with inspiratory whoop and post-tussive vomiting, plus marked lymphocytosis."]},{"h":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis","points":["Acid-fast because of mycolic acids in a waxy cell wall; obligate aerobe; slow growing.","Cord factor (trehalose dimycolate) activates macrophages and drives TNF-alpha release and granulomas.","Sulfatides help block phagosome and lysosome fusion, so the organism survives inside macrophages.","Spread by airborne droplet nuclei small enough to reach the alveoli.","Primary infection: Ghon focus in the lower part of the upper lobe or upper part of the lower lobe; reactivation favors the apices."]},{"h":"Respiratory viruses","points":["Influenza: orthomyxovirus with 8 segments of negative-sense RNA; hemagglutinin binds sialic acid, neuraminidase releases new virions.","Antigenic drift (point mutations) causes yearly epidemics; antigenic shift (segment reassortment, influenza A) causes pandemics.","Influenza is often followed by bacterial pneumonia from Staphylococcus aureus, S. pneumoniae, or H. influenzae.","Respiratory syncytial virus: nonsegmented negative-sense RNA pneumovirus (formerly grouped with paramyxoviruses).","RSV F protein fuses cells into syncytia; RSV is the top cause of bronchiolitis in infants.","Coronaviruses: enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA; SARS-CoV-2 spike protein binds ACE2."]},{"h":"Fungi","points":["Dimorphic fungi grow as molds at room temperature and as yeasts (or spherules) at body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F).","Histoplasma: Ohio and Mississippi river valleys, bird and bat droppings; small yeasts inside macrophages.","Blastomyces: Great Lakes and Ohio and Mississippi basins; large yeast with a thick wall and broad-based bud; skin and bone spread.","Coccidioides: southwestern United States desert dust; spherules filled with endospores; erythema nodosum and arthralgias.","Aspergillus: septate hyphae branching at acute angles (about 45 degrees); invasive disease with neutropenia.","Pneumocystis jirovecii: cysts on methenamine silver stain; diffuse interstitial pneumonia when CD4 count is below 200 cells/mm3."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Bacterial lung pathogens","cols":["Organism","Gram stain and shape","Key lab feature","Classic host or setting","Main virulence factor"],"rows":[["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Positive diplococci","Optochin sensitive, bile soluble","Community-acquired lobar pneumonia","Polysaccharide capsule"],["Haemophilus influenzae","Negative coccobacillus","Needs factors X and V","Unvaccinated children, COPD","Type b capsule"],["Klebsiella pneumoniae","Negative rod","Lactose fermenter, mucoid","Alcoholics, aspiration","Thick capsule"],["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Negative rod","Oxidase positive, blue-green pigment","Cystic fibrosis, ventilators","Exotoxin A, biofilm"],["Legionella pneumophila","Negative rod, stains poorly","Charcoal yeast extract agar","Contaminated water, older smokers","Survives in macrophages"],["Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Positive club-shaped rod","Black colonies on tellurite agar","Unvaccinated people","Diphtheria toxin (EF-2)"],["Bordetella pertussis","Negative coccobacillus","Bordet-Gengou or Regan-Lowe agar","Infants, waning immunity","Pertussis toxin (Gi)"],["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Acid-fast rod","Lowenstein-Jensen agar, slow growth","Close contacts, HIV, crowding","Cord factor, sulfatides"]]},{"title":"Lung fungi","cols":["Fungus","Where or who","Tissue form","Clue"],"rows":[["Histoplasma capsulatum","Ohio and Mississippi valleys, bird and bat droppings, caves","Small yeasts inside macrophages","Can mimic tuberculosis"],["Blastomyces dermatitidis","Great Lakes, Ohio and Mississippi basins","Large yeast, broad-based bud, thick wall","Skin and bone lesions"],["Coccidioides immitis","Southwestern United States deserts","Spherules with endospores","Erythema nodosum, dust exposure"],["Aspergillus fumigatus","Neutropenia, steroids, old lung cavities","Septate hyphae, acute-angle branching","Fungus ball, angioinvasion"],["Pneumocystis jirovecii","HIV with CD4 below 200 cells/mm3","Cysts on methenamine silver stain","Bilateral interstitial infiltrates"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Mold in the cold, yeast in the heat","text":"Dimorphic fungi grow as molds in the environment and as yeasts (Coccidioides as spherules) at body temperature."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Two toxins, two targets","text":"Diphtheria hits EF-2 and stops protein synthesis. Pertussis hits Gi and raises cAMP. Both work by ADP-ribosylation."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Drift versus shift","text":"Drift: small point mutations in hemagglutinin and neuraminidase, so yearly epidemics. Shift: a whole gene segment swapped between strains, so pandemics."}],"highYield":["Optochin sensitive, bile soluble, alpha-hemolytic diplococci: Streptococcus pneumoniae.","H. influenzae needs factors X and V; Legionella needs charcoal yeast extract agar.","Diphtheria toxin is prophage-encoded and inactivates EF-2.","Pertussis toxin inactivates Gi, raises cAMP, and causes lymphocytosis.","Cord factor and sulfatides let M. tuberculosis survive inside macrophages.","Influenza antigenic shift needs segment reassortment and occurs only in influenza A.","SARS-CoV-2 spike protein binds ACE2.","Pneumocystis appears when CD4 count falls below 200 cells/mm3; Aspergillus invades with neutropenia."],"pitfalls":["Viridans streptococci are also alpha-hemolytic but are optochin resistant and bile insoluble.","Aspergillus has septate hyphae with acute angles; Mucor has broad nonseptate hyphae with wide angles.","Blastomyces yeast has a broad-based bud; Cryptococcus has a narrow-based bud and a thick capsule.","RSV is negative-sense RNA; coronaviruses are positive-sense RNA."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pneumonia organism table","p":"Make a table of six bacterial causes of pneumonia: S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, Legionella, and M. tuberculosis. For each, write the Gram stain, one lab test, the classic patient, and one virulence factor.","l":["S. pneumoniae: gram-positive diplococci, optochin sensitive, capsule","H. influenzae: needs X and V factors, type b capsule","Klebsiella: mucoid capsule, alcoholics, currant jelly sputum","Pseudomonas: oxidase positive, cystic fibrosis and ventilators","Legionella: charcoal yeast extract agar, contaminated water, hyponatremia","M. tuberculosis: acid-fast, cord factor, droplet nuclei","Lobar pneumonia most often S. pneumoniae"]},{"t":"Two ADP-ribosylating toxins","p":"Draw a host cell and show where diphtheria toxin and pertussis toxin act. Trace each from its target to the clinical picture.","l":["Both are A-B toxins that ADP-ribosylate a host protein","Diphtheria toxin gene carried by a prophage","Diphtheria: EF-2 inactivated, protein synthesis stops, cells die","Diphtheria picture: pseudomembrane, bull neck, myocarditis, nerve palsies","Pertussis: Gi inactivated, adenylyl cyclase unchecked, cAMP rises","Pertussis picture: paroxysmal cough with whoop, post-tussive vomiting","Pertussis causes marked lymphocytosis"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on the streptococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on mycobacteria"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on medical mycology"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung (section on pulmonary infections)"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-14-H","PU-12","PU-14-H9","PU-14-H4","PU-14-H7"]},"PU-14-A":{"id":"PU-14-A","summary":"Restrictive lung diseases keep the lungs from filling: surfactant failure in newborns, diffuse alveolar damage in ARDS, scarring in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and the pneumoconioses, and granulomas in sarcoidosis. Exam questions ask you to match an exposure, histology, or lab pattern to the disease and to separate lung-tissue causes from chest wall and muscle causes.","notes":[{"h":"Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","points":["Surfactant deficiency; risk rises with prematurity, maternal diabetes, cesarean delivery without labor, and male sex.","High surface tension collapses alveoli at end expiration (atelectasis), causing shunt, hypoxemia, and hyaline membranes (fibrin and dead cells).","Signs within hours of birth: tachypnea, grunting, nasal flaring, retractions; ground-glass X-ray with air bronchograms.","Hypoxemia keeps the ductus arteriosus open (patent ductus arteriosus).","O2 and ventilation can cause bronchopulmonary dysplasia and retinopathy of prematurity."]},{"h":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome","points":["Diffuse alveolar damage after sepsis (most common), pneumonia, aspiration, trauma, or pancreatitis.","Macrophage cytokines recruit neutrophils, which release proteases and oxidants and break the capillary and epithelial barrier.","Protein-rich fluid floods alveoli and hyaline membranes form.","Criteria: onset within 1 week, bilateral infiltrates, edema not explained by heart failure, PaO2/FiO2 of 300 or less.","Example: PaO2 75 mmHg on FiO2 0.5 gives a ratio of 150 (moderate ARDS); hypoxemia responds poorly to O2 because of shunt."]},{"h":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","points":["Older adults, more often men and smokers; repeated alveolar epithelial injury with abnormal repair.","TGF-beta drives fibroblasts and myofibroblasts to lay down collagen.","Usual interstitial pneumonia pattern: patchy subpleural and basal scar of different ages, fibroblastic foci, and honeycomb cysts at end stage.","Dry cough, slowly worsening dyspnea, fine bibasilar inspiratory crackles, clubbing.","Spirometry: low FVC and TLC, FEV1/FVC normal or high; DLCO reduced."]},{"h":"Pneumoconioses","points":["Coal workers: upper lobes; coal macules and nodules, sometimes progressive massive fibrosis.","Silicosis (sandblasting, quarrying, foundries): upper lobe nodules, eggshell calcified hilar nodes, and higher tuberculosis risk because silica injures macrophages.","Asbestosis (shipyards, insulation, roofing, plumbing): lower lobes, ferruginous bodies (iron-coated fibers).","Asbestos also causes calcified pleural plaques, bronchogenic carcinoma (the most common cancer), and mesothelioma.","Berylliosis (aerospace, electronics): type IV reaction with noncaseating granulomas that mimic sarcoidosis."]},{"h":"Sarcoidosis","points":["Noncaseating granulomas in many organs from a Th1 response to an unknown antigen.","Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy is the classic X-ray finding, with or without interstitial lung disease.","Serum ACE is often elevated (made by granuloma cells).","Granuloma macrophages have 1-alpha-hydroxylase, make calcitriol, and cause hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria.","More common in Black women; usual onset age 20 to 40; eyes (uveitis), skin, and heart can be involved.","Lofgren syndrome: erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, arthritis (often ankles), and fever; usually resolves."]},{"h":"Intrinsic versus extrapulmonary restriction","points":["Intrinsic (lung tissue scar or fluid): low TLC, low DLCO, wide A-a gradient.","Extrapulmonary: obesity, kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular weakness (ALS, myasthenia gravis, Guillain-Barre syndrome).","The alveolar membrane is normal, so DLCO is usually normal; hypoventilation raises PaCO2 with a normal A-a gradient."]},{"h":"Other interstitial and occupational diseases","points":["Desquamative interstitial pneumonia: smokers; alveoli filled with pigmented macrophages; improves when smoking stops.","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia: uniform interstitial inflammation and fibrosis (all the same age); linked to connective tissue disease.","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia: polypoid plugs of loose fibroblast tissue (Masson bodies) in small airways and alveoli; responds to steroids.","Byssinosis: cotton dust, chest tightness on the first workday of the week; bagassosis: moldy sugarcane, a hypersensitivity pneumonitis.","Silo-filler disease: nitrogen dioxide from fresh silage causes pulmonary edema and later bronchiolitis obliterans.","Drug-induced fibrosis: bleomycin, amiodarone, busulfan, methotrexate (also nitrofurantoin); the drug history separates it from IPF."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Restrictive conditions in this unit","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","Prematurity, maternal diabetes, cesarean without labor","Surfactant deficiency, alveolar collapse","Grunting, retractions, ground-glass X-ray, hyaline membranes","Patent ductus arteriosus, bronchopulmonary dysplasia, retinopathy of prematurity"],["Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Sepsis, pneumonia, aspiration, trauma, pancreatitis","Neutrophil-mediated capillary leak, diffuse alveolar damage","Bilateral infiltrates, PaO2/FiO2 of 300 or less, hyaline membranes","Refractory hypoxemia, fibrosis"],["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Older age, smoking, male sex","Repeated epithelial injury, TGF-beta driven fibrosis","Clubbing, bibasilar crackles, UIP pattern, honeycombing","Pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale, lung cancer"],["Coal workers pneumoconiosis","Coal dust","Macrophage-laden coal dust","Upper lobe macules and nodules","Progressive massive fibrosis"],["Silicosis","Sandblasting, quarrying, foundries","Silica injures macrophages, fibrosis","Upper lobe nodules, eggshell calcified hilar nodes","Tuberculosis, lung cancer"],["Asbestosis","Shipyards, insulation, roofing, plumbing","Fiber-driven interstitial fibrosis","Lower lobe fibrosis, ferruginous bodies, pleural plaques","Bronchogenic carcinoma, mesothelioma"],["Berylliosis","Aerospace and electronics work","Type IV hypersensitivity","Noncaseating granulomas, hilar nodes","Fibrosis"],["Sarcoidosis","Black women, age 20 to 40","Th1 response, noncaseating granulomas","Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, high ACE, hypercalcemia","Uveitis, heart block, fibrosis"]]},{"title":"Pulmonary function in restriction","cols":["Measure","Intrinsic (fibrosis)","Extrapulmonary (obesity, kyphoscoliosis, weakness)"],"rows":[["TLC","Low","Low"],["FEV1/FVC","Normal or high","Normal or high"],["DLCO","Low","Usually normal"],["A-a gradient","Wide","Normal"],["PaCO2","Normal or low","High if hypoventilating"]]},{"title":"Interstitial pneumonia patterns","cols":["Pattern","Who","Histology"],"rows":[["Usual interstitial pneumonia (IPF)","Older adults, smokers","Patchy subpleural fibrosis of different ages, fibroblastic foci, honeycombing"],["Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","Connective tissue disease","Uniform inflammation and fibrosis of the same age"],["Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Smokers","Pigmented macrophages filling alveoli"],["Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia","After infection, drugs, or idiopathic","Polypoid fibroblast plugs (Masson bodies)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Asbestos sits low","text":"Asbestosis hits the lower lobes; coal and silica hit the upper lobes. Think of asbestos fibers sinking to the bottom."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Check DLCO to find the problem","text":"Restriction with a low DLCO means the lung tissue is sick. Restriction with a normal DLCO means the chest wall or muscles are the problem."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Same age or mixed ages","text":"UIP looks like scars of many ages side by side. NSIP looks like everything happened at once."}],"highYield":["Infants of diabetic mothers get respiratory distress because fetal insulin delays surfactant.","ARDS: PaO2/FiO2 of 300 or less with bilateral infiltrates not explained by heart failure.","IPF: TGF-beta driven fibrosis, UIP pattern, clubbing, restrictive spirometry, low DLCO.","Silicosis raises tuberculosis risk and shows eggshell calcification of hilar nodes.","Ferruginous bodies and pleural plaques mean asbestos exposure.","Sarcoidosis hypercalcemia comes from macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase.","Lofgren syndrome: erythema nodosum, hilar nodes, arthritis, fever, good prognosis.","Bleomycin and amiodarone are classic causes of drug-induced pulmonary fibrosis."],"pitfalls":["Neonatal RDS is surfactant deficiency; ARDS is diffuse alveolar damage with leak. Both show hyaline membranes.","Berylliosis granulomas look like sarcoidosis; ask about aerospace or electronics work.","Mesothelioma is the cancer most specific to asbestos, yet bronchogenic carcinoma is more common in asbestos workers.","Obesity and neuromuscular weakness cause restriction with a normal DLCO and a normal A-a gradient.","Byssinosis behaves like asthma (obstructive); bagassosis is a hypersensitivity pneumonitis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pneumoconiosis table","p":"Make a table of coal workers pneumoconiosis, silicosis, asbestosis, and berylliosis. For each, write the job, the lobe affected, the key finding, and the main complication.","l":["Coal: miners, upper lobes, coal macules","Coal complication: progressive massive fibrosis","Silica: sandblasting and quarrying, upper lobes, eggshell calcified hilar nodes","Silicosis raises tuberculosis risk","Asbestos: shipyards and insulation, lower lobes, ferruginous bodies, pleural plaques","Asbestos complications: bronchogenic carcinoma and mesothelioma","Beryllium: aerospace, noncaseating granulomas like sarcoidosis"]},{"t":"Neonatal RDS versus ARDS","p":"Draw two alveoli side by side, one from a premature newborn with RDS and one from an adult with ARDS. Label what goes wrong in each, then list causes and complications under each drawing.","l":["Newborn: low surfactant, collapsed alveolus","Newborn causes: prematurity, maternal diabetes, cesarean without labor","Adult: neutrophils damage capillary and epithelium, protein-rich fluid floods alveolus","Adult causes: sepsis, pneumonia, aspiration, trauma, pancreatitis","Both show hyaline membranes","ARDS criterion: PaO2/FiO2 of 300 or less, not explained by heart failure","Newborn complications: patent ductus, bronchopulmonary dysplasia, retinopathy of prematurity","Adult complication: later interstitial fibrosis"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung (sections on acute lung injury and chronic diffuse interstitial diseases)"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases (section on neonatal respiratory distress syndrome)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Pulmonary Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Respiratory Insufficiency: Pathophysiology, Diagnosis, Oxygen Therapy"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-9","PU-12","PU-6","PU-14-D4","PU-14-F5"]},"PU-14-B":{"id":"PU-14-B","summary":"Obstructive diseases make it hard to get air out: asthma, bronchiectasis, chronic bronchitis, and emphysema. Boards ask you to match each one to its mechanism and histology, read spirometry and DLCO, and use the blood gas to judge how sick an asthmatic really is.","notes":[{"h":"Asthma: mechanism","points":["Atopic asthma is a type I hypersensitivity driven by Th2 cells.","IL-4 switches B cells to IgE, IL-5 recruits and activates eosinophils, IL-13 drives mucus production.","Allergen cross-links IgE on mast cells, releasing histamine and leukotrienes within minutes (early phase); eosinophils and Th2 cells sustain a late phase hours later.","Nonatopic triggers: viral infection, cold air, exercise, irritants, and aspirin.","Aspirin-exacerbated disease: blocking COX shunts arachidonic acid toward leukotrienes; often comes with nasal polyps.","Airway remodeling: goblet cell hyperplasia, thickened subepithelial basement membrane, submucosal gland enlargement, and smooth muscle hypertrophy.","Sputum: Curschmann spirals (coiled mucus casts with shed epithelium) and Charcot-Leyden crystals (from eosinophil protein)."]},{"h":"Acute asthma attack","points":["Wheezing, prolonged expiration, tachypnea, use of accessory muscles.","Pulsus paradoxus: systolic pressure falls more than 10 mmHg on inspiration in a severe attack.","Obstruction is reversible: FEV1 rises after a bronchodilator.","Early gas: low PaCO2 and respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, with hypoxemia from V/Q mismatch.","A normal or rising PaCO2 during an attack means the patient is tiring and nearing respiratory failure."]},{"h":"Bronchiectasis","points":["Permanent dilation of bronchi from obstruction plus necrotizing infection that destroys muscle and elastic tissue.","Causes: cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, tumor or foreign body, severe past pneumonia.","Primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener when with situs inversus): dynein arm defect, sinusitis, infertility.","Daily cough with large amounts of foul purulent sputum, hemoptysis, clubbing.","Chronic infection can cause secondary (AA) amyloidosis."]},{"h":"Chronic bronchitis","points":["Clinical definition: productive cough for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years.","Smoking causes mucous gland hyperplasia in the large bronchi and goblet cell metaplasia in small airways.","Reid index (gland layer thickness divided by wall thickness from epithelium to cartilage) above 0.5; normal is below about 0.4.","Blue bloater: early hypoxemia and hypercapnia, cyanosis, hypoxic vasoconstriction, then pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale."]},{"h":"Emphysema","points":["Destruction of alveolar walls distal to the terminal bronchiole without significant fibrosis.","Protease and antiprotease imbalance: smoke recruits neutrophils and macrophages that release elastase, and oxidants inactivate alpha-1 antitrypsin.","Centriacinar: respiratory bronchioles destroyed, distal alveoli spared, upper lobes, smokers.","Panacinar: whole acinus, lower lobes, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (may also cause liver cirrhosis).","Paraseptal: distal acinus next to pleura and septa; subpleural bullae can rupture, causing spontaneous pneumothorax in young adults.","Lost elastic recoil lets small airways collapse on expiration, causing air trapping and a barrel chest.","Pink puffer: thin, dyspneic, breathes through pursed lips to keep airways open."]},{"h":"COPD testing","points":["Obstruction: FEV1/FVC below 0.7 after a bronchodilator.","Air trapping raises TLC, residual volume, and FRC.","Emphysema: low DLCO because alveolar surface and capillaries are lost; gases near normal until late.","Chronic bronchitis: normal DLCO; early hypoxemia and hypercapnia from V/Q mismatch."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Obstructive diseases at a glance","cols":["Disease","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Asthma","Th2 inflammation, reversible bronchoconstriction","Wheeze, Curschmann spirals, Charcot-Leyden crystals, eosinophils","Status asthmaticus, respiratory failure"],["Bronchiectasis","Obstruction plus necrotizing infection, permanent dilation","Copious purulent sputum, hemoptysis, clubbing","Recurrent infection, AA amyloidosis, cor pulmonale"],["Chronic bronchitis","Mucous gland hyperplasia, Reid index above 0.5","Productive cough, cyanosis, edema (blue bloater)","Hypoxemia, hypercapnia, cor pulmonale"],["Emphysema","Elastase destroys alveolar walls","Barrel chest, pursed-lip breathing, hyperresonance","Bullae, pneumothorax, low DLCO"]]},{"title":"Emphysema patterns","cols":["Type","Part of acinus","Lobe","Main cause"],"rows":[["Centriacinar","Respiratory bronchioles","Upper","Smoking"],["Panacinar","Entire acinus","Lower","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],["Paraseptal","Distal, next to pleura","Upper, subpleural","Cause often unknown; bullae in young adults"]]},{"title":"Lung function in obstructive disease","cols":["Test","Asthma (attack)","Emphysema","Chronic bronchitis"],"rows":[["FEV1/FVC","Low, improves with bronchodilator","Low, little reversal","Low, little reversal"],["TLC and RV","Increased","Increased","Increased"],["DLCO","Normal or high","Low","Normal"],["Blood gases","Low PaCO2 early; rising PaCO2 is danger","Near normal until late","Early hypoxemia and hypercapnia"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"PaCO2 in the asthmatic","text":"A breathless asthmatic should be blowing off CO2. If the PaCO2 is normal or climbing, the breathing muscles are failing, which is an emergency."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Bronchitis is blue, emphysema is pink","text":"Chronic bronchitis wrecks V/Q matching early, so the patient is cyanotic and retains CO2. Emphysema loses alveoli and capillaries together, so matching is kept and the patient works hard to stay pink."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Alpha-1 hits lungs low and liver too","text":"Panacinar, lower lobe emphysema in a young person, especially a nonsmoker, points to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Check the liver for cirrhosis from the misfolded protein."}],"highYield":["Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals in sputum mean asthma.","Reid index above 0.5 defines chronic bronchitis histologically.","Centriacinar emphysema is upper lobe and smoking; panacinar is lower lobe and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency.","Paraseptal emphysema bullae cause spontaneous pneumothorax in young adults.","COPD: FEV1/FVC below 0.7 with increased TLC and residual volume.","DLCO is low in emphysema and normal in chronic bronchitis and asthma.","Bronchiectasis plus situs inversus and infertility suggests Kartagener syndrome.","Aspirin-exacerbated asthma comes with nasal polyps and leukotriene excess."],"pitfalls":["Asthma and COPD both lower FEV1/FVC; reversibility with a bronchodilator favors asthma.","A normal PaCO2 in an acute asthma attack is a warning sign and should not be read as reassurance.","Bronchiectasis is permanent airway dilation; chronic bronchitis has normal sized airways with excess mucus glands.","Air trapping raises TLC in obstructive disease; restrictive disease lowers TLC."],"dumps":[{"t":"Asthma from allergen to airway","p":"Trace an atopic asthma attack from inhaled allergen to narrowed airway. Then list the airway changes seen after years of disease.","l":["Th2 cells release IL-4 (IgE switch), IL-5 (eosinophils), and IL-13 (mucus)","Allergen cross-links IgE on mast cells","Early phase: histamine and leukotrienes cause bronchoconstriction within minutes","Late phase: eosinophils and Th2 cells cause inflammation hours later","Remodeling: goblet cell hyperplasia, thick basement membrane, smooth muscle hypertrophy","Sputum: Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals","Nonatopic triggers: viral infection, cold air, exercise, aspirin"]},{"t":"COPD comparison table","p":"Make a table comparing chronic bronchitis and emphysema. Include definition, histology, cause, body type, blood gases, DLCO, TLC, and end-stage complication.","l":["Chronic bronchitis: productive cough 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years","Chronic bronchitis histology: mucous gland hyperplasia, Reid index above 0.5","Emphysema: alveolar wall destruction from protease and antiprotease imbalance","Centriacinar upper lobes in smokers; panacinar lower lobes in alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Blue bloater: early hypoxemia and hypercapnia; pink puffer: gases near normal until late","DLCO low in emphysema, normal in chronic bronchitis","Both: FEV1/FVC below 0.7, increased TLC and residual volume","Chronic bronchitis leads to cor pulmonale"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, section on obstructive lung diseases"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Pulmonary Disease, sections on asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on respiratory insufficiency (pathophysiology of emphysema and asthma)"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-6","PU-7","PU-12","PU-14-G","PU-14-D","IM-15-C"]},"PU-14-C":{"id":"PU-14-C","summary":"Upper respiratory infections and inflammation run from the nose to the large bronchi. Boards ask you to tell the croup child from the epiglottitis child, strep throat from mono, and allergic from infectious rhinitis, and to predict the dangerous complications of sinusitis and strep pharyngitis.","notes":[{"h":"Acute bronchitis and laryngitis","points":["Acute bronchitis: mostly viral, self-limited inflammation of the trachea and large bronchi.","Cough lasts up to about 3 weeks; chest radiograph shows no consolidation, which separates it from pneumonia.","Chronic bronchitis needs a productive cough for 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years.","Acute laryngitis: usually viral or from voice overuse; hoarseness without stridor or a toxic child."]},{"h":"Stridor in a child","points":["Croup: parainfluenza virus laryngotracheobronchitis, 6 months to 3 years old, barking cough, inspiratory stridor.","Croup film: steeple sign (subglottic narrowing) on a frontal neck radiograph.","Epiglottitis: classically Haemophilus influenzae type b in unvaccinated children; sudden high fever, drooling, dysphagia, muffled voice, tripod posture.","Epiglottitis film: thumb sign (swollen epiglottis) on lateral neck radiograph; the airway can close suddenly, so do not agitate the child or probe the throat.","Bacterial tracheitis: toxic child with high fever and thick purulent tracheal secretions, often Staphylococcus aureus, often after a viral illness."]},{"h":"Rhinitis","points":["Allergic rhinitis: type I hypersensitivity; IgE on mast cells, eosinophils in nasal secretions.","Pale, boggy, bluish turbinates, sneezing, itching, clear rhinorrhea; seasonal (pollens) or perennial (dust mites, animal dander).","Common cold: rhinovirus, a nonenveloped positive-sense RNA picornavirus.","Rhinovirus is acid labile, so it does not survive the stomach, and grows best near 33 °C (91.4 °F) in the cool nose; the major group binds ICAM-1.","Vasomotor rhinitis: nonallergic, triggered by temperature change, odors, or spicy food; no IgE or eosinophils."]},{"h":"Sinusitis","points":["Mucosal swelling blocks the sinus ostium, trapping secretions; most cases follow a viral cold.","Bacterial causes: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae (nontypeable), Moraxella catarrhalis.","Orbital cellulitis: ethmoid infection spreads through the thin lamina papyracea; proptosis, painful eye movement.","Cavernous sinus thrombosis: ophthalmoplegia (CN III, IV, VI), V1 and V2 numbness, headache, fever.","Mucormycosis in diabetic ketoacidosis: Rhizopus or Mucor invade vessels, black necrotic tissue on palate or turbinates."]},{"h":"Sore throat: strep, viral, mono","points":["Streptococcus pyogenes: beta-hemolytic, bacitracin sensitive, group A; M protein resists phagocytosis.","Strep throat: fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior cervical nodes, no cough.","Viral pharyngitis: cough, runny nose, hoarseness, conjunctivitis.","Rheumatic fever about 2 to 4 weeks after strep pharyngitis only: antibodies to M protein cross-react with heart proteins (molecular mimicry).","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis follows throat or skin infection: immune complex deposition, hematuria, edema, hypertension.","Mono (EBV infects B cells via CD21): exudative tonsillitis, posterior cervical nodes, splenomegaly, fatigue.","Mono blood: atypical lymphocytes (reactive CD8 T cells) and heterophile antibodies (Monospot).","Peritonsillar abscess: severe one-sided throat pain, trismus, muffled hot potato voice, uvula pushed to the opposite side."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Upper airway conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause","Classic findings","Complication to predict"],"rows":[["Acute bronchitis","Viruses","Cough up to about 3 weeks, clear chest film","Usually none"],["Epiglottitis","Haemophilus influenzae type b","Drooling, muffled voice, tripod posture, thumb sign","Sudden airway obstruction"],["Croup","Parainfluenza virus","Barking cough, inspiratory stridor, steeple sign","Airway narrowing"],["Bacterial tracheitis","Staphylococcus aureus","Toxic child, purulent tracheal secretions","Airway obstruction"],["Acute laryngitis","Viruses, voice overuse","Hoarseness","Usually none"],["Allergic rhinitis","IgE to allergens","Pale boggy mucosa, eosinophils","Sinusitis, nasal polyps"],["Common cold","Rhinovirus","Rhinorrhea, sneezing, sore throat","Sinusitis, otitis media"],["Sinusitis","Viruses; S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis","Facial pain, purulent discharge","Orbital cellulitis, cavernous sinus thrombosis"],["Strep pharyngitis","Streptococcus pyogenes","Fever, exudate, tender anterior nodes, no cough","Rheumatic fever, glomerulonephritis"],["Mononucleosis","EBV","Exudative tonsils, posterior nodes, splenomegaly","Splenic rupture"],["Peritonsillar abscess","Strep plus anaerobes","Uvular deviation, trismus, muffled voice","Airway compromise, deep neck spread"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Epiglottitis: the 4 Ds","text":"Drooling, dysphagia, distress, and dysphonia (muffled voice) in a febrile child."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Steeple below, thumb above","text":"Croup narrows the airway below the cords, so the frontal film looks like a steeple. Epiglottitis swells the structure above the cords, so the lateral film shows a thumb."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Rheumatic fever needs the throat","text":"Rheumatic fever follows strep pharyngitis only. Glomerulonephritis can follow either throat or skin infection."}],"highYield":["Thumb sign on lateral neck film means epiglottitis; steeple sign means croup.","Parainfluenza virus is the main cause of croup.","Rhinovirus is acid labile and binds ICAM-1; it is the main cause of the common cold.","Mucormycosis of the sinuses is a classic complication of diabetic ketoacidosis.","Strep throat: fever, exudate, tender anterior nodes, and no cough.","Rheumatic fever arises from molecular mimicry between M protein and heart proteins.","Atypical lymphocytes in mono are reactive CD8 T cells; EBV itself infects B cells through CD21.","Peritonsillar abscess pushes the uvula away from the abscess and causes trismus."],"pitfalls":["Acute bronchitis has no consolidation; crackles and a new infiltrate mean pneumonia.","Both strep and mono cause exudative tonsillitis; posterior nodes, splenomegaly, and atypical lymphocytes point to mono.","Bacterial tracheitis can look like severe croup, but the child is toxic and has thick pus in the trachea.","Pale boggy mucosa with eosinophils is allergic; a red swollen mucosa with neutrophils fits infection."],"dumps":[{"t":"Stridor and sore throat chart","p":"Make a table comparing croup, epiglottitis, bacterial tracheitis, and peritonsillar abscess. Include cause, age, key signs, and radiograph or exam clue.","l":["Croup: parainfluenza, 6 months to 3 years, barking cough, steeple sign","Epiglottitis: Haemophilus influenzae type b in unvaccinated children","Epiglottitis: drooling, muffled voice, tripod posture, thumb sign","Epiglottitis risk is sudden airway obstruction","Bacterial tracheitis: toxic child, purulent secretions, Staphylococcus aureus","Peritonsillar abscess: uvula deviated away, trismus, hot potato voice"]},{"t":"Strep throat to its sequelae","p":"Draw a timeline that starts with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis and ends with its two nonsuppurative complications. Label the mechanism of each.","l":["Group A, beta-hemolytic, bacitracin sensitive","Fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior nodes, no cough","M protein is the key virulence factor","Rheumatic fever about 2 to 4 weeks later, after pharyngitis only","Rheumatic fever mechanism: molecular mimicry with heart proteins","Glomerulonephritis after throat or skin infection","Glomerulonephritis mechanism: immune complex deposition"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, sections on upper respiratory tract and larynx"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Sections on streptococci, Haemophilus, picornaviruses, and paramyxoviruses"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on immediate hypersensitivity (allergy)"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-3","PU-13","PU-12","PU-14-H","IM-15-C","CV-18-D"]},"PU-14-D":{"id":"PU-14-D","summary":"The pleural space can fill with air, blood, lymph, or fluid, and the lung can collapse from blockage or compression. Boards ask you to name the fluid from its lab values, predict which way the trachea moves, and recognize asbestos and chest trauma patterns.","notes":[{"h":"Fluid in the pleural space","points":["Light criteria: exudate if pleural to serum protein above 0.5, pleural to serum LDH above 0.6, or pleural LDH above two thirds of the serum upper limit.","Transudate (low protein, from pressure changes): heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome.","Exudate (leaky inflamed pleura): pneumonia, malignancy, tuberculosis, pulmonary embolism, pancreatitis, autoimmune disease.","Chylothorax: thoracic duct disruption by trauma, surgery, or lymphoma; milky fluid, triglycerides above 110 mg/dL, chylomicrons.","Hemothorax: pleural fluid hematocrit at least half the blood hematocrit; from trauma, vessel rupture, or tumor.","Large hemothorax can cause hypovolemic shock; undrained clot organizes into fibrothorax that traps the lung.","Empyema: pus in the pleural space, usually after pneumonia."]},{"h":"Atelectasis","points":["Obstructive (resorption): a mucus plug, tumor, or foreign body blocks a bronchus and trapped air is absorbed into blood.","Resorption collapse pulls the mediastinum and trachea toward the collapsed side.","Compression atelectasis: fluid, air, or tumor pushes on the lung; mediastinum shifts away.","Contraction atelectasis: fibrosis prevents full expansion and is not reversible."]},{"h":"Pneumothorax","points":["Exam: hyperresonance, absent or reduced breath sounds, reduced fremitus on the affected side.","Primary spontaneous: tall thin young men (often smokers) with ruptured apical subpleural blebs.","Secondary: underlying lung disease such as COPD or cystic fibrosis.","Tension: a one-way valve lets air in but not out; trachea shifts away, venous return falls, neck veins distend, blood pressure drops."]},{"h":"Asbestos and the pleura","points":["Pleural plaques: well-circumscribed, often calcified plaques on the parietal pleura and diaphragm; the most common asbestos lesion.","Also benign pleural effusion and diffuse pleural fibrosis; asbestosis itself is lower lobe interstitial fibrosis.","Ferruginous bodies: golden brown beaded fibers coated with iron-containing protein (Prussian blue positive).","Bronchogenic carcinoma is the most common cancer after asbestos exposure, and risk multiplies with smoking.","Mesothelioma risk rises with asbestos but is not linked to smoking."]},{"h":"Mediastinum and chest wall","points":["Mediastinitis: esophageal perforation, infection after sternotomy, or inhalational anthrax (hemorrhagic, widened mediastinum).","Pneumomediastinum: air dissects into the mediastinum; subcutaneous crepitus and a Hamman crunch heard with each heartbeat.","Costochondritis: chest wall pain with reproducible tenderness over the costochondral junctions."]},{"h":"Chest trauma","points":["Flail chest: several adjacent ribs broken in two places; the segment moves inward on inspiration (paradoxical motion).","Pulmonary contusion: hemorrhage into alveoli with hypoxemia that worsens over the following hours.","Open pneumothorax: a penetrating wound lets air enter the pleural space with each breath.","Diaphragmatic rupture: more often on the left, so stomach or bowel appears in the left hemithorax."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pleural fluid types","cols":["Type","Appearance","Key lab clue","Causes"],"rows":[["Transudate","Clear, pale yellow","Fails all Light criteria","Heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome"],["Exudate","Often cloudy, may be bloody","Meets any one Light criterion","Pneumonia, cancer, tuberculosis, pulmonary embolism"],["Empyema","Pus","Many neutrophils, organisms, low glucose and pH","Bacterial pneumonia"],["Hemothorax","Blood","Fluid hematocrit at least half of blood hematocrit","Trauma, vessel rupture, malignancy"],["Chylothorax","Milky","Triglycerides above 110 mg/dL, chylomicrons","Thoracic duct injury, lymphoma"]]},{"title":"Which way does the trachea move?","cols":["Condition","Trachea and mediastinum","Percussion","Breath sounds"],"rows":[["Obstructive atelectasis","Toward the lesion","Dull","Reduced"],["Large pleural effusion","Away from the lesion","Dull","Reduced"],["Tension pneumothorax","Away from the lesion","Hyperresonant","Absent"],["Simple spontaneous pneumothorax","Usually midline","Hyperresonant","Reduced"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Collapse pulls, mass pushes","text":"When air leaves the lung (resorption atelectasis), the chest loses volume and the trachea is pulled toward that side. When something fills the chest (fluid, tension air), the trachea is pushed away."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Half the hematocrit means blood","text":"Bloody looking fluid needs only a little blood. Call it a hemothorax only when the fluid hematocrit is at least half the blood hematocrit."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Asbestos plus smoking multiplies","text":"Lung carcinoma risk from asbestos and smoking together is far higher than either alone. Mesothelioma risk does not change with smoking."}],"highYield":["Exudate if any Light criterion is met: protein ratio above 0.5, LDH ratio above 0.6, or LDH above two thirds of the serum upper limit.","Chylothorax: milky fluid with triglycerides above 110 mg/dL from thoracic duct injury or lymphoma.","Tension pneumothorax: trachea away, hypotension, distended neck veins, absent breath sounds.","Resorption atelectasis shifts the mediastinum toward the collapse.","Calcified parietal pleural plaques are the most common sign of asbestos exposure.","Ferruginous bodies are iron-coated asbestos fibers.","Hamman crunch with subcutaneous crepitus means pneumomediastinum.","Flail chest moves paradoxically inward on inspiration."],"pitfalls":["Effusion and atelectasis are both dull to percussion; effusion pushes the trachea away and atelectasis pulls it toward.","Pulmonary embolism effusions are usually exudates, so do not assume all non-infectious effusions are transudates.","Pleural plaques are benign; they mark exposure and do not turn into mesothelioma.","Hyperresonance with absent breath sounds is pneumothorax; dullness with absent breath sounds is fluid or collapse."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pleural fluid sort","p":"Write Light criteria from memory. Then sort these into transudate or exudate and give one lab clue each: heart failure, pneumonia, cirrhosis, lung cancer, nephrotic syndrome, chylothorax, hemothorax.","l":["Protein ratio above 0.5 means exudate","LDH ratio above 0.6 means exudate","Pleural LDH above two thirds of serum upper limit means exudate","Heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome are transudates","Pneumonia and cancer cause exudates","Chylothorax: triglycerides above 110 mg/dL, chylomicrons","Hemothorax: fluid hematocrit at least half the blood hematocrit"]},{"t":"Draw the shifted chest","p":"Draw three chest outlines: obstructive atelectasis of the left lower lobe, a large left effusion, and a left tension pneumothorax. Show trachea position and label percussion and breath sounds.","l":["Atelectasis: trachea pulled toward the left","Effusion: trachea pushed to the right, dull percussion","Tension pneumothorax: trachea pushed to the right, hyperresonant","Absent breath sounds on the affected side in tension pneumothorax","Tension pneumothorax lowers venous return: hypotension and distended neck veins","Resorption atelectasis cause: plug, tumor, or foreign body"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, sections on atelectasis, pneumoconioses, and pleural lesions"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, sections on pleurae and the thoracic duct"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-4","PU-6","PU-14-A","PU-14-F","PU-14-H"]},"PU-14-E":{"id":"PU-14-E","summary":"Pulmonary vascular disease covers fluid leaking into alveoli, clots and other material lodging in the pulmonary arteries, and high pressure in the pulmonary circuit. Boards ask you to use Starling forces, predict the blood gas in pulmonary embolism, tell fat, air, and amniotic emboli apart, and classify pulmonary hypertension.","notes":[{"h":"Pulmonary edema and Starling forces","points":["Fluid leaves capillaries when hydrostatic pressure pushing out beats oncotic pressure pulling in, or when the capillary wall leaks.","Cardiogenic: left heart failure raises pulmonary capillary pressure; wedge pressure above 18 mmHg.","Chronic congestion leaves hemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells) in alveoli.","Noncardiogenic causes: capillary injury (ARDS), low plasma oncotic pressure (nephrotic syndrome, liver failure), lymphatic obstruction (tumor in lymphatics).","Noncardiogenic edema from capillary injury has a normal wedge pressure and protein-rich fluid."]},{"h":"Pulmonary embolism","points":["Most emboli come from deep vein thrombosis of the leg veins above the knee.","Virchow triad: stasis (immobility, surgery, long travel), endothelial injury, hypercoagulability (cancer, pregnancy, estrogen, factor V Leiden).","Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, tachypnea.","Blood gas: hypoxemia with low PaCO2 and respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation.","Widened A-a gradient from V/Q mismatch and dead space.","D-dimer is sensitive but not specific; a normal value helps rule out embolism when suspicion is low.","A saddle embolus at the pulmonary artery bifurcation causes acute right heart strain (acute cor pulmonale) and can cause sudden death."]},{"h":"Pulmonary infarction","points":["Most emboli do not infarct the lung because the bronchial arteries keep the tissue alive.","Infarction happens when a medium or small artery is blocked and bronchial flow is poor, as in left heart failure.","Wedge shaped and hemorrhagic (red), with the base on the pleura and the apex toward the hilum.","Causes hemoptysis and pleuritic pain from fibrinous pleuritis over the infarct."]},{"h":"Nonthrombotic emboli","points":["Fat embolism: 24 to 72 hours after a long bone or pelvic fracture; hypoxemia, confusion, petechiae on chest, axillae, and conjunctivae.","Air embolism: air entering through a central venous catheter, or nitrogen bubbles forming in tissues and blood when a diver rises too fast (decompression sickness).","Amniotic fluid embolism: during labor or just after delivery; sudden dyspnea, hypotension, seizures, and DIC; fetal squamous cells found in maternal pulmonary vessels."]},{"h":"Pulmonary hypertension","points":["Defined by raised mean pulmonary artery pressure at rest (above about 20 mmHg by current criteria, 25 mmHg in older sources).","Group 1, pulmonary arterial hypertension: idiopathic or heritable (BMPR2 loss of function lets smooth muscle proliferate), drugs, connective tissue disease, HIV, shunts.","Group 2: left heart disease (most common cause overall).","Group 3: lung disease or hypoxia (COPD, interstitial disease, sleep apnea, altitude) through hypoxic vasoconstriction.","Group 4: chronic thromboembolic disease.","Vessels show medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis, and plexiform lesions in severe arterial disease; atherosclerosis appears in large pulmonary arteries.","Right ventricle hypertrophies, then fails (cor pulmonale): raised neck veins, hepatomegaly, peripheral edema."]},{"h":"Pulmonary AV malformations","points":["Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Osler-Weber-Rendu): autosomal dominant; telangiectasias of skin and mucosa, nosebleeds, GI bleeding.","Pulmonary AVMs shunt blood right to left past the alveoli, causing hypoxemia that oxygen corrects poorly.","Clots and bacteria bypass the lung filter: paradoxical embolic stroke and brain abscess; AVMs can also cause hemoptysis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Emboli to the lung","cols":["Type","Setting","Key findings","Major risk"],"rows":[["Thromboembolism","Deep vein thrombosis, Virchow triad","Pleuritic pain, hypoxemia, respiratory alkalosis, high D-dimer","Saddle embolus with acute right heart failure"],["Fat","Long bone or pelvic fracture, 24 to 72 hours later","Hypoxemia, confusion, petechial rash","Respiratory failure"],["Air","Central venous line, diving decompression","Sudden dyspnea, chest pain, joint pain in divers","Obstruction of flow, stroke"],["Amniotic fluid","Labor or just after delivery","Sudden collapse, hypotension, seizures","DIC, death"]]},{"title":"Pulmonary edema types","cols":["Feature","Cardiogenic","Noncardiogenic (capillary injury)"],"rows":[["Main force","Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure","Leaky capillary wall"],["Wedge pressure","Above 18 mmHg","18 mmHg or less"],["Edema fluid","Low protein","High protein"],["Examples","Left ventricular failure, mitral stenosis","ARDS from sepsis, aspiration, pancreatitis"]]},{"title":"Pulmonary hypertension groups","cols":["Group","Cause","Example"],"rows":[["1","Pulmonary arterial hypertension","Idiopathic, BMPR2 mutation, connective tissue disease"],["2","Left heart disease","Left ventricular failure, mitral valve disease"],["3","Lung disease or hypoxia","COPD, interstitial lung disease, sleep apnea"],["4","Chronic thromboembolism","Repeated or unresolved emboli"],["5","Unclear or multiple mechanisms","Sarcoidosis, chronic hemolytic anemia such as sickle cell disease, myeloproliferative disorders"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Two blood supplies protect the lung","text":"The lung has pulmonary and bronchial arteries. An embolus usually causes hemorrhage without infarction unless the bronchial supply is also weak, as in heart failure."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Shunts ignore oxygen","text":"Blood flowing through an AVM never meets alveolar air, so extra oxygen barely raises the PaO2. That clue separates a shunt from V/Q mismatch."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fat embolism triad","text":"Lungs, brain, skin: hypoxemia, confusion, and petechiae a day or more after a femur fracture."}],"highYield":["Cardiogenic edema: wedge pressure above 18 mmHg and heart failure cells.","Pulmonary embolism: hypoxemia, respiratory alkalosis, widened A-a gradient.","Saddle embolus causes acute cor pulmonale and sudden death.","Pulmonary infarcts are wedge-shaped, hemorrhagic, and pleural-based, usually with poor bronchial flow.","Fat embolism appears 24 to 72 hours after long bone fracture with a petechial rash.","Amniotic fluid embolism causes collapse and DIC during delivery.","BMPR2 mutation causes heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension; plexiform lesions are typical.","HHT pulmonary AVMs cause paradoxical stroke and brain abscess."],"pitfalls":["Most pulmonary emboli do not cause infarction, so a normal chest film does not rule out embolism.","Hypoxemia from a shunt improves little with oxygen; hypoxemia from V/Q mismatch improves well.","ARDS edema has a normal wedge pressure; a high wedge pressure points to the heart.","Cor pulmonale is right heart failure caused by lung or pulmonary vascular disease; right failure that follows left heart failure is a different label."],"dumps":[{"t":"From leg clot to right heart failure","p":"Trace a pulmonary embolism from its origin to its effects on the blood gas and the heart. Add the factors that raise risk.","l":["Virchow triad: stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability","Source is usually deep veins of the leg above the knee","Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, tachycardia","Hypoxemia with respiratory alkalosis","Widened A-a gradient from V/Q mismatch","D-dimer raised but not specific","Saddle embolus causes acute right heart strain","Infarction only when bronchial flow is poor"]},{"t":"Starling forces in the alveolar wall","p":"Draw a pulmonary capillary next to an alveolus. Label the forces that move fluid out and in, then show what changes in heart failure, ARDS, nephrotic syndrome, and lymphatic blockage.","l":["Capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out","Plasma oncotic pressure pulls fluid in","Lymphatics remove filtered fluid","Heart failure raises hydrostatic pressure, wedge above 18 mmHg","ARDS damages the capillary wall, protein-rich fluid","Nephrotic syndrome lowers oncotic pressure","Lymphatic obstruction blocks fluid removal"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, sections on pulmonary edema, pulmonary embolism, and pulmonary hypertension"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Pulmonary Disease, sections on pulmonary edema and pulmonary embolism"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-5","PU-7","PU-14-A","CV-18-B","CV-18-H"]},"PU-14-F":{"id":"PU-14-F","summary":"This unit covers tumors from the nose to the pleura, plus esophageal cancer. Boards ask you to match each lung cancer to its location, histology, smoking link, and paraneoplastic syndrome, predict the local effects of tumor spread, and recognize the virus-linked head and neck cancers.","notes":[{"h":"Lung cancer types","points":["Adenocarcinoma: most common lung cancer overall and the most common in nonsmokers and women; peripheral, gland forming, mucin producing.","Adenocarcinoma in situ: lepidic growth along intact alveolar walls with no invasion (formerly bronchioloalveolar carcinoma).","Squamous cell: central, strongly tied to smoking; keratin pearls, intercellular bridges; may cavitate.","Large cell: peripheral, poorly differentiated with no glandular or squamous features; poor prognosis.","Small cell: central, almost always in smokers; small cells, scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding; chromogranin and synaptophysin positive.","Small cell spreads early, so it is usually beyond surgery at diagnosis.","Bronchial carcinoid: low-grade neuroendocrine tumor, nests of uniform cells, chromogranin positive, often a central polypoid mass in a younger patient, not tied to smoking.","Carcinoid can block a bronchus (atelectasis, recurrent pneumonia) and rarely causes carcinoid syndrome."]},{"h":"Paraneoplastic syndromes","points":["Squamous cell: PTHrP causes hypercalcemia.","Small cell: ACTH (Cushing syndrome) and ADH (SIADH with hyponatremia).","Small cell: Lambert-Eaton syndrome, antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels; weakness improves with repeated effort.","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy (clubbing and periosteal new bone): especially adenocarcinoma."]},{"h":"Local spread","points":["Pancoast (superior sulcus) tumor: Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) from sympathetic chain invasion, and C8 to T1 brachial plexus deficits.","Superior vena cava syndrome: facial swelling and plethora, distended neck and chest veins, arm edema; usually a central tumor.","Recurrent laryngeal nerve invasion: hoarseness (the left nerve loops under the aortic arch).","Phrenic nerve invasion: paralyzed, elevated hemidiaphragm."]},{"h":"Nodules and the pleura","points":["Metastases are more common than primary lung cancer; they appear as multiple well-circumscribed nodules.","Hamartoma: most common benign lung tumor; cartilage and fat, popcorn calcification on imaging.","Mesothelioma: asbestos exposure decades earlier, no link to smoking; recurrent hemorrhagic effusion, tumor encasing the lung.","Mesothelioma markers: calretinin and cytokeratin positive; long slender microvilli on electron microscopy.","Metastatic adenocarcinoma in the pleura: calretinin negative, often CEA positive, short stubby microvilli."]},{"h":"Head and neck tumors","points":["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma: EBV linked, more common in southern China and parts of Africa; nonkeratinizing or undifferentiated.","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma presents with neck node metastasis or one-sided serous otitis media from eustachian tube blockage.","Laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma: smoking and alcohol; glottic tumors cause early hoarseness.","Vocal fold nodules: benign, bilateral, from voice overuse.","Juvenile laryngeal papillomatosis: HPV types 6 and 11, recurrent papillomas, hoarseness in children.","Nasal polyps: chronic rhinosinusitis, allergy, aspirin-exacerbated disease with asthma, or cystic fibrosis in a child; cause obstruction and anosmia.","Vocal fold polyps: usually one-sided, from voice strain or smoking; cause hoarseness.","HPV 16 causes oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma of the tonsil and base of tongue, often in nonsmokers.","Tobacco and alcohol cause squamous carcinoma of the oral cavity and pharynx."]},{"h":"Esophageal cancer","points":["Squamous cell: upper and middle third; smoking, alcohol, very hot drinks, achalasia, nitrosamines.","Adenocarcinoma: distal third; GERD leading to Barrett esophagus, obesity.","Both cause progressive dysphagia (solids then liquids) and weight loss; invasion forward can create a tracheoesophageal fistula with cough on swallowing."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Lung tumors","cols":["Tumor","Location","Smoking link","Histology or marker","Classic association"],"rows":[["Adenocarcinoma","Peripheral","Weaker; most common in nonsmokers","Glands, mucin; in situ form shows lepidic growth","Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy, clubbing"],["Squamous cell","Central","Strong","Keratin pearls, intercellular bridges","PTHrP hypercalcemia, cavitation"],["Large cell","Peripheral","Yes","Undifferentiated large cells","Poor prognosis"],["Small cell","Central","Strongest","Nuclear molding; chromogranin, synaptophysin","ACTH, ADH, Lambert-Eaton, early metastasis"],["Bronchial carcinoid","Central, polypoid","None","Nests of uniform cells; chromogranin","Bronchial obstruction; rare carcinoid syndrome"],["Mesothelioma","Pleura","None","Calretinin, cytokeratin; long microvilli","Asbestos, hemorrhagic effusion"],["Hamartoma","Peripheral nodule","None","Cartilage and fat","Popcorn calcification"]]},{"title":"Upper airway and esophageal tumors","cols":["Lesion","Cause or risk","Key feature"],"rows":[["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","EBV; southern China, parts of Africa","Neck nodes, one-sided serous otitis media"],["Laryngeal carcinoma","Smoking, alcohol","Early hoarseness if glottic"],["Vocal fold nodules","Voice overuse","Bilateral, benign"],["Juvenile papillomatosis","HPV 6 and 11","Recurrent papillomas in children"],["Nasal polyps","Rhinosinusitis, allergy, aspirin-exacerbated disease, cystic fibrosis","Obstruction, anosmia"],["Oropharyngeal carcinoma","HPV 16","Tonsil and base of tongue"],["Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma","Smoking, alcohol, hot drinks, achalasia, nitrosamines","Upper and middle third"],["Esophageal adenocarcinoma","GERD, Barrett esophagus, obesity","Distal third"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Popcorn is reassuring","text":"A single round nodule with popcorn calcification and fat points to a hamartoma. Several round nodules of different sizes point to metastases from another organ."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Central tumors start with S","text":"Squamous and small cell are the central lung cancers. Adenocarcinoma and large cell sit in the periphery."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Small cell makes hormones like a gland","text":"Small cell tumors are neuroendocrine, so they can secrete ACTH and ADH and trigger antibodies that cross-react with nerve terminals (Lambert-Eaton)."}],"highYield":["Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer overall and in nonsmokers.","Squamous cell carcinoma: central, keratin pearls, PTHrP hypercalcemia.","Small cell carcinoma: central, chromogranin positive, ACTH, ADH, Lambert-Eaton.","Pancoast tumor causes Horner syndrome and C8 to T1 deficits.","Mesothelioma: asbestos without smoking synergy, calretinin positive, long microvilli.","Metastases outnumber primary lung cancers; hamartoma shows popcorn calcification.","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is EBV linked; HPV 16 causes tonsil and base of tongue cancer.","Esophageal squamous cell is upper and middle; adenocarcinoma is distal with Barrett esophagus."],"pitfalls":["Carcinoid and small cell are both neuroendocrine and chromogranin positive; carcinoid is low grade with uniform cells and no smoking link.","Mesothelioma and pleural adenocarcinoma both cause bloody effusions; calretinin and long microvilli mark mesothelioma.","Vocal fold nodules are bilateral and from overuse; polyps are usually one-sided and tied to voice strain or smoking.","Adenocarcinoma is common in nonsmokers, but most adenocarcinomas still occur in smokers."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lung cancer master table","p":"Build a table of adenocarcinoma, squamous cell, large cell, small cell, and carcinoid. Include location, smoking link, histology or markers, and paraneoplastic syndrome.","l":["Adenocarcinoma: peripheral, most common, nonsmokers and women, mucin","Adenocarcinoma in situ: lepidic growth without invasion","Squamous: central, keratin pearls, intercellular bridges, PTHrP","Large cell: peripheral, undifferentiated, poor prognosis","Small cell: central, nuclear molding, chromogranin and synaptophysin","Small cell: ACTH, ADH, Lambert-Eaton, early metastasis","Carcinoid: low grade, central polypoid, no smoking link","Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy with adenocarcinoma"]},{"t":"Map local spread","p":"Draw the right and left lung apex and hilum with nearby structures. Label what a tumor damages at each spot and the sign it produces.","l":["Sympathetic chain at the apex: Horner syndrome","Brachial plexus C8 to T1: hand weakness and arm pain","Superior vena cava: facial plethora, distended neck veins, arm edema","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve under the aortic arch: hoarseness","Phrenic nerve: elevated hemidiaphragm","Esophagus: dysphagia; tracheoesophageal fistula from esophageal cancer"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, section on lung tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, sections on nasopharynx and larynx"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal Tract, section on esophageal tumors"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, sections on the recurrent laryngeal and phrenic nerves"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-4","PU-14-D","PU-14-C","PU-14-G","GI-13-K"]},"PU-14-G":{"id":"PU-14-G","summary":"Congenital lung disease ranges from a single gene defect (cystic fibrosis) to errors in how the foregut, diaphragm, and lung buds form. Boards ask you to explain the CFTR chloride defect in sweat and airway, predict its many organ effects, and recognize tracheoesophageal fistula, diaphragmatic hernia, sequestration, and bronchogenic cyst in a newborn or child.","notes":[{"h":"Cystic fibrosis: the defect","points":["Autosomal recessive mutation in CFTR on chromosome 7; most common is deletion of phenylalanine 508 (F508del).","F508del misfolds the protein, which is degraded before it reaches the cell surface.","CFTR is an ATP-gated chloride channel; cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates CFTR so ATP can open it.","Sweat duct: CFTR normally reabsorbs chloride, and sodium follows; without it both stay in the sweat, so sweat is salty.","Airway: CFTR normally secretes chloride and restrains ENaC; without it chloride secretion falls and sodium absorption through ENaC rises.","Water follows the sodium out of the airway lumen, so airway surface liquid is depleted and mucus becomes thick and hard to clear."]},{"h":"Cystic fibrosis: the clinical picture","points":["Newborn: meconium ileus (thick meconium blocks the ileum).","Pancreatic insufficiency: steatorrhea, poor growth, deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K.","Lungs: early Staphylococcus aureus, later mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa; bronchiectasis and clubbing.","Sinuses: chronic sinusitis and nasal polyps; nasal polyps in a child should prompt CF testing.","Most men are infertile from congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens.","Diagnosis: sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or higher."]},{"h":"Tracheoesophageal fistula","points":["The foregut fails to separate fully into trachea and esophagus.","Most common form: esophageal atresia (blind upper pouch) with a distal fistula from trachea to the lower esophagus.","Fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid, causing polyhydramnios.","Newborn drools and chokes, coughs, and turns cyanotic with the first feeds.","A nasogastric tube will not pass and coils in the upper pouch; air in the stomach shows the distal fistula.","Look for VACTERL: vertebral, anal atresia, cardiac, tracheoesophageal fistula, esophageal atresia, renal, and limb (radial) defects."]},{"h":"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","points":["Pleuroperitoneal fold (membrane) fails to close, usually on the left posterolateral side (Bochdalek).","Abdominal contents enter the chest and compress the developing lung, causing pulmonary hypoplasia.","Newborn: severe respiratory distress, scaphoid abdomen, bowel sounds in the chest, heart sounds shifted to the right."]},{"h":"Sequestration and bronchogenic cyst","points":["Pulmonary sequestration: nonfunctioning lung tissue with no bronchial connection, supplied by a systemic artery from the aorta.","Sequestration is usually in a lower lobe and becomes infected again and again.","Bronchogenic cyst: abnormal budding of the foregut; fluid-filled cyst lined by respiratory epithelium, usually in the mediastinum near the carina.","Bronchogenic cysts can compress an airway or become infected."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital lung and airway disorders","cols":["Condition","Embryologic or genetic cause","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Cystic fibrosis","CFTR mutation, autosomal recessive","Salty sweat, meconium ileus, steatorrhea","Bronchiectasis, Pseudomonas infection, infertility"],["Tracheoesophageal fistula","Incomplete foregut separation","Polyhydramnios, choking with feeds, tube coils in pouch","Aspiration pneumonia"],["Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","Failed closure of pleuroperitoneal fold","Scaphoid abdomen, bowel sounds in chest","Pulmonary hypoplasia"],["Pulmonary sequestration","Extra lung bud with systemic artery from aorta","Lower lobe mass, no airway connection","Recurrent infection"],["Bronchogenic cyst","Abnormal foregut budding","Fluid-filled mediastinal cyst","Airway compression, infection"]]},{"title":"CFTR in sweat versus airway","cols":["Site","Normal CFTR job","In cystic fibrosis","Result"],"rows":[["Sweat duct","Reabsorbs chloride from sweat","Chloride and sodium stay in sweat","Salty sweat, chloride 60 mmol/L or higher"],["Airway","Secretes chloride, limits ENaC","Less chloride out, more sodium in","Dry airway surface, thick mucus"],["Pancreatic duct","Secretes chloride and bicarbonate","Thick secretions block ducts","Pancreatic insufficiency"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Sweat goes the other way","text":"In most tissues CFTR pushes chloride out, so CF makes secretions dry. In the sweat duct CFTR pulls chloride back in, so CF makes sweat salty. Same broken channel, opposite direction."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"VACTERL","text":"Vertebral defects, Anal atresia, Cardiac defects, Tracheo-Esophageal fistula with esophageal atresia, Renal anomalies, Limb (radial) defects."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sequestration is fed by the aorta","text":"If a lung mass has its own artery from the aorta and no airway connection, it is a sequestration. A bronchogenic cyst is a fluid sac from the foregut, usually in the mediastinum."}],"highYield":["Cystic fibrosis: autosomal recessive CFTR mutation, F508del misfolding.","Sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or higher confirms cystic fibrosis.","Increased ENaC sodium absorption dries the airway surface liquid in cystic fibrosis.","Men with cystic fibrosis are usually infertile from absent vas deferens.","Esophageal atresia with distal fistula: polyhydramnios, choking with feeds, gastric air bubble.","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia: left posterolateral, scaphoid abdomen, pulmonary hypoplasia.","Pulmonary sequestration has a systemic arterial supply from the aorta."],"pitfalls":["Salty sweat comes from failed chloride reabsorption in the sweat duct, so sweat chloride rises even though the airway is dry.","A gasless abdomen suggests esophageal atresia without a distal fistula; air in the stomach means the distal fistula is present.","Sequestration has no bronchial connection; a bronchogenic cyst arises from the foregut and is usually mediastinal.","The danger in diaphragmatic hernia is the small, underdeveloped lung (pulmonary hypoplasia); moving the bowel back does not fix it."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw the CF epithelium","p":"Draw an airway cell and a sweat duct cell side by side. Show CFTR and ENaC with arrows for chloride, sodium, and water, first in health and then in cystic fibrosis.","l":["CFTR is an ATP-gated chloride channel regulated by cAMP and protein kinase A","Airway: CFTR secretes chloride into the lumen","Airway in CF: less chloride secretion, more sodium absorbed through ENaC","Water leaves the airway lumen, so mucus thickens","Sweat duct: CFTR reabsorbs chloride and sodium follows","Sweat duct in CF: chloride and sodium stay in sweat","Sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or higher"]},{"t":"Newborn with breathing trouble","p":"Make a table comparing tracheoesophageal fistula, congenital diaphragmatic hernia, pulmonary sequestration, and bronchogenic cyst. Include embryologic error, key sign, and main complication.","l":["TE fistula: incomplete foregut separation","TE fistula: polyhydramnios, choking with feeds, tube will not pass","TE fistula: VACTERL association","Diaphragmatic hernia: failed pleuroperitoneal fold closure, left posterolateral","Diaphragmatic hernia: scaphoid abdomen, pulmonary hypoplasia","Sequestration: systemic artery from aorta, no airway connection, recurrent infection","Bronchogenic cyst: foregut budding, fluid-filled, mediastinal"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Respiratory System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Body Cavities, section on the diaphragm"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of Infancy and Childhood, section on cystic fibrosis"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on cystic fibrosis and the CFTR mutation"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-1","PU-10","PU-14-B","GI-13-B","GI-13-H"]},"PU-14-H":{"id":"PU-14-H","summary":"This unit covers infections of the lungs and airways, from common pneumonias to tuberculosis, fungi, and childhood infections like pertussis and RSV. Boards ask you to match the organism to the patient and the pattern on imaging, follow tuberculosis from primary to reactivation, and predict toxin effects and complications.","notes":[{"h":"Pneumonia patterns","points":["Lobar: consolidation of a whole lobe; Streptococcus pneumoniae most often, also Klebsiella (alcoholism, currant jelly sputum).","Lobar stages: congestion, red hepatization (red cells, neutrophils, fibrin), gray hepatization (red cells broken down), resolution.","Consolidation exam: dullness, increased tactile fremitus, egophony, bronchial breath sounds.","Bronchopneumonia: patchy, often multilobar, centered on bronchioles; Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, Pseudomonas.","Atypical (interstitial): patchy interstitial infiltrates that look worse than the patient; dry cough, headache, myalgia.","Mycoplasma pneumoniae: no cell wall, cold agglutinins (IgM), young adults in close quarters such as dorms and barracks.","Others: Chlamydia pneumoniae, viruses, and Legionella (water aerosols, high fever, diarrhea, confusion, hyponatremia)."]},{"h":"Hospital, aspiration, abscess","points":["Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia: Pseudomonas, MRSA, and enteric gram-negative rods.","Chemical pneumonitis is sterile acid injury within hours of aspirating gastric contents; aspiration pneumonia is infection by oral bacteria over days in dependent segments.","Lung abscess: aspiration of oral anaerobes (Bacteroides, Fusobacterium, Peptostreptococcus) in alcoholism, seizures, or dysphagia.","Abscess shows an air-fluid level, often in the right lung (posterior upper lobe or superior lower lobe if supine), with foul sputum; Staphylococcus aureus and Klebsiella also cause abscess."]},{"h":"Tuberculosis","points":["Primary: Ghon focus in the lower upper lobe or upper lower lobe plus a hilar node (Ghon complex).","Th1 cells release IFN-gamma that activates macrophages; caseating granulomas wall off the bacilli, leaving latent infection.","Tuberculin skin test: type IV reaction read at 48 to 72 hours; can be positive after BCG vaccine.","IFN-gamma release assay: T cell response to antigens not in BCG, so BCG does not cause a false positive.","Reactivation: apical cavitation, fever, night sweats, weight loss, hemoptysis; risk rises with HIV and immunosuppression.","Miliary spread: tiny seed-like nodules in kidneys, meninges, adrenals, and vertebrae (Pott disease)."]},{"h":"Fungi","points":["Endemic dimorphic fungi grow as molds in soil and yeasts (or spherules) in tissue; they infect healthy hosts.","Histoplasma: Ohio and Mississippi valleys, bird and bat droppings, tiny yeasts inside macrophages.","Blastomyces: similar region, broad-based budding yeast; spreads to skin and bone.","Coccidioides: southwestern US deserts, spherules with endospores; spreads to skin, bone, and meninges.","All three form granulomas that can mimic tuberculosis.","Pneumocystis: CD4 below 200 per microliter, bilateral ground-glass infiltrates, hypoxemia on exertion, high LDH.","Aspergillus: aspergilloma in an old cavity, angioinvasive disease in neutropenia, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis in asthma and cystic fibrosis."]},{"h":"Childhood and toxin diseases","points":["Pertussis stages: catarrhal (cold-like, most contagious), paroxysmal (coughing fits, inspiratory whoop, posttussive vomiting), convalescent.","Pertussis toxin causes marked lymphocytosis; young infants may have apnea instead of a whoop, and pneumonia.","RSV: most common cause of infant bronchiolitis and pneumonia; winter epidemics with wheezing and tachypnea; severe in prematurity, congenital heart disease, and chronic lung disease.","Diphtheria: gray adherent pseudomembrane that bleeds when scraped, bull neck nodes; grows on tellurite agar, Elek test shows toxin.","Diphtheria toxin inactivates EF-2; it causes myocarditis, heart block, and cranial and peripheral neuropathy."]},{"h":"Viruses and zoonoses","points":["Influenza destroys ciliated epithelium, opening the door to secondary Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pneumoniae pneumonia.","Avian and human influenza strains can swap gene segments (reassortment) and create novel pandemic strains.","Coronaviruses: some cause common colds; SARS, MERS, and COVID-19 cause severe pneumonia.","SARS-CoV-2 enters cells through ACE2 on airway and alveolar cells; loss of smell, diffuse alveolar damage, and thrombosis; risk rises with age and comorbidity.","Inhalational anthrax: hemorrhagic mediastinitis with a widened mediastinum.","Tularemia: rabbits and ticks. Q fever: Coxiella from farm animal birth products. Melioidosis: Burkholderia pseudomallei in Southeast Asia."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pneumonia and airway infections","cols":["Condition","Organism","Classic setting or finding","Complication"],"rows":[["Lobar pneumonia","Streptococcus pneumoniae, Klebsiella","Whole lobe consolidation, rust or currant jelly sputum","Empyema, abscess, bacteremia"],["Bronchopneumonia","S. aureus, H. influenzae, Pseudomonas","Patchy, multilobar, around bronchioles","Abscess, empyema"],["Atypical pneumonia","Mycoplasma, Chlamydia pneumoniae, Legionella, viruses","Interstitial infiltrates worse than patient looks","Cold agglutinin hemolysis (Mycoplasma)"],["Lung abscess","Oral anaerobes; S. aureus, Klebsiella","Aspiration, air-fluid level, foul sputum","Empyema"],["Tuberculosis","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Ghon complex, apical cavities","Miliary spread, Pott disease"],["Fungal pneumonia","Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Coccidioides, Pneumocystis, Aspergillus","Endemic region or immunosuppression","Dissemination, cavities"],["Pertussis","Bordetella pertussis","Whoop, posttussive vomiting, lymphocytosis","Apnea and pneumonia in infants"],["RSV","Respiratory syncytial virus","Infant bronchiolitis in winter","Respiratory failure in premature infants"],["Diphtheria","Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Gray pseudomembrane, bull neck","Myocarditis, neuropathy"],["Coronavirus","SARS-CoV-2 and others","ACE2 entry, loss of smell","Diffuse alveolar damage, thrombosis"]]},{"title":"Reading tuberculosis tests","cols":["Feature","Tuberculin skin test","IFN-gamma release assay"],"rows":[["What it measures","Delayed-type (type IV) skin reaction","IFN-gamma from T cells exposed to TB antigens"],["Effect of BCG vaccine","Can be falsely positive","Not affected"],["Visits needed","Two (placement and reading at 48 to 72 hours)","One blood draw"],["Latent versus active","Cannot tell","Cannot tell"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Ghon is primary, apex is reactivation","text":"Primary TB lands in the middle of the lung near the fissure with a hilar node. Reactivation favors the apex, where oxygen is high, and cavitates."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Legionella hints","text":"Pneumonia plus diarrhea, confusion, and low sodium in an older patient exposed to water aerosols such as cooling towers points to Legionella."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Where aspiration goes","text":"The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical. Lying down, aspirate falls into the posterior right upper lobe and superior right lower lobe; standing, into the right lower lobe bases."}],"highYield":["Lobar stages: congestion, red hepatization, gray hepatization, resolution.","Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall and causes cold agglutinins in young adults.","Ghon complex: subpleural Ghon focus plus hilar node.","IFN-gamma release assay is not affected by BCG vaccination.","Pneumocystis: CD4 below 200, ground-glass infiltrates, high LDH.","Diphtheria toxin inactivates EF-2 and causes myocarditis and neuropathy.","Influenza is followed by Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pneumoniae pneumonia.","Inhalational anthrax causes a widened, hemorrhagic mediastinum."],"pitfalls":["Atypical pneumonia often looks worse on the film than the patient looks at the bedside; in lobar pneumonia the patient is acutely ill with dense consolidation.","A positive skin test after BCG may be false; use the IFN-gamma release assay.","Chemical pneumonitis starts within hours and is sterile; bacterial aspiration pneumonia develops over days.","Aspergilloma lives in an old cavity in a normal host; angioinvasive aspergillosis is in neutropenic patients."],"dumps":[{"t":"Tuberculosis timeline","p":"Draw the path of Mycobacterium tuberculosis from inhalation to latent infection, then to reactivation and miliary spread. Add how the skin test and blood test work.","l":["Ghon focus plus hilar node equals Ghon complex","Th1 cells release IFN-gamma that activates macrophages","Caseating granulomas contain the bacilli (latent infection)","Reactivation in the apex: cavitation, night sweats, weight loss, hemoptysis","Miliary spread to kidneys, meninges, adrenals, vertebrae (Pott disease)","HIV and immunosuppression raise reactivation risk","Skin test: type IV reaction at 48 to 72 hours, BCG can cause false positive","IFN-gamma release assay is not affected by BCG"]},{"t":"Organism by patient","p":"Make a table matching each patient to the most likely lung organism: college student in a dorm, alcoholic after a seizure, AIDS with CD4 of 90, hospital ventilator patient, infant in winter with wheezing, Arizona hiker, cave explorer in Ohio.","l":["Dorm student: Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Alcoholic after a seizure: oral anaerobes causing lung abscess","AIDS with low CD4: Pneumocystis","Ventilator patient: Pseudomonas, MRSA, enteric gram-negative rods","Infant in winter: RSV bronchiolitis","Arizona hiker: Coccidioides","Cave explorer in Ohio: Histoplasma"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, section on pulmonary infections"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Mycobacteria"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Medical Mycology"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on immunity to intracellular bacteria"}],"videos":[],"related":["PU-12","PU-13","PU-14-C","PU-14-D","IM-15-B","IM-15-F"]},"RE-1":{"id":"RE-1","summary":"How one bipotential gonad and two pairs of ducts become male or female organs, and how the placenta and breast form. Exam questions give a karyotype, a hormone defect, or a birth defect and ask you to predict the anatomy that results.","notes":[{"h":"Gonad: SRY decides","points":["Until about week 7 the gonad is bipotential (indifferent) and both duct pairs are present.","SRY on the short arm of the Y chromosome codes for testis-determining factor, which turns supporting cells into Sertoli cells and the gonad into a testis.","Without SRY the gonad becomes an ovary by default.","46,XY with an SRY deletion: no testis forms, the gonads become streaks, and the person has a uterus, tubes, and female external genitalia (Swyer syndrome).","46,XX with SRY translocated onto an X: testes form and the phenotype is male, but the testes are small and sperm are not made because other Y genes for spermatogenesis are missing."]},{"h":"Three male signals","points":["Sertoli cells make anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH), which makes the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts regress.","Leydig cells make testosterone, first driven by placental hCG and later by fetal LH.","Testosterone keeps the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts, which form the epididymis, ductus deferens, seminal vesicle, and ejaculatory duct.","5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT), which builds the penis, scrotum, and prostate.","With no AMH and no testosterone (female), the Mullerian ducts persist and the Wolffian ducts regress, leaving at most a Gartner duct cyst."]},{"h":"Mullerian ducts and uterine defects","points":["The unfused cranial parts of the paramesonephric ducts become the uterine tubes.","The caudal parts fuse in the midline to form the uterus, cervix, and upper vagina; the lower vagina comes from the urogenital sinus (sinovaginal bulbs).","After fusion the wall between the two ducts must be resorbed to leave one cavity.","No fusion gives uterus didelphys (two uteri, two cervices, often a double vagina); partial fusion gives a bicornuate uterus; failed septum resorption gives a septate uterus.","Ovaries come from the gonadal ridge, so ovarian function is normal in Mullerian defects; renal anomalies often travel with them."]},{"h":"External genitalia","points":["Genital tubercle: glans penis or glans clitoris.","Urogenital folds: ventral shaft of the penis around the spongy (penile) urethra, or labia minora.","Labioscrotal swellings: scrotum or labia majora.","Hypospadias (ventral urethral opening) means the urogenital folds did not fuse; epispadias (dorsal opening) comes from a misplaced genital tubercle and is linked to bladder exstrophy."]},{"h":"Testicular descent","points":["The testis starts high on the posterior abdominal wall, so its artery comes off the aorta and its lymph drains to para-aortic nodes.","The gubernaculum anchors the testis and guides it through the inguinal canal into the scrotum, usually by birth.","A pouch of peritoneum, the processus vaginalis, goes ahead of the testis and normally closes, leaving only the tunica vaginalis around the testis.","A wide patent processus vaginalis lets bowel enter the cord: indirect inguinal hernia through the deep ring, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels.","A narrow patent processus lets only peritoneal fluid through: communicating hydrocele that changes size and transilluminates.","In the female the gubernaculum becomes the ovarian ligament and the round ligament of the uterus."]},{"h":"Placenta and cord","points":["Fetal part: chorion frondosum (villous chorion). Maternal part: decidua basalis.","Villi have an inner cytotrophoblast layer and an outer syncytiotrophoblast layer, which makes hCG and faces maternal blood.","The umbilical cord holds two umbilical arteries (deoxygenated blood to the placenta) and one umbilical vein (oxygenated blood to the fetus) in Wharton jelly.","A single umbilical artery is linked to congenital anomalies, especially renal and cardiac."]},{"h":"Breast","points":["The breast starts as an ectodermal thickening, the mammary ridge (milk line), running from the axilla to the groin on each side.","Only the pectoral part of the ridge normally survives and sends cords into the mesenchyme that become the lactiferous ducts.","Parts of the ridge that fail to regress give supernumerary nipples (polythelia) or extra breast tissue (polymastia) anywhere along the line, most often in the axilla or on the lower chest or abdomen."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Indifferent structure and its adult derivatives","cols":["Embryonic structure","Male","Female","Driven by"],"rows":[["Gonad","Testis","Ovary","SRY present or absent"],["Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct","Epididymis, ductus deferens, seminal vesicle, ejaculatory duct","Regresses (Gartner duct remnant)","Testosterone"],["Paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct","Regresses (appendix testis remnant)","Uterine tubes, uterus, cervix, upper vagina","AMH absent in the female"],["Urogenital sinus","Prostate, bulbourethral glands, prostatic and membranous urethra","Lower vagina, urethral and paraurethral glands, greater vestibular glands","DHT for the prostate"],["Genital tubercle","Glans penis","Glans clitoris","DHT"],["Urogenital folds","Ventral penile shaft and spongy urethra","Labia minora","DHT"],["Labioscrotal swellings","Scrotum","Labia majora","DHT"],["Gubernaculum","Anchors testis in the scrotum","Ovarian ligament and round ligament","Leydig cell hormones in the male"]]},{"title":"Mullerian fusion defects","cols":["Defect","Step that failed","Uterine shape","Cervix"],"rows":[["Uterus didelphys","Ducts never fuse","Two separate uteri","Two cervices"],["Bicornuate uterus","Upper ducts fuse only partly","Heart-shaped, notched outer contour, two horns","One cervix"],["Septate uterus","Ducts fuse but the septum is not resorbed","Smooth outer contour, wall inside the cavity","One cervix"],["Unicornuate uterus","One duct fails to develop","Single banana-shaped horn","One cervix"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SEED","text":"Wolffian duct derivatives: Seminal vesicles, Epididymis, Ejaculatory duct, Ductus deferens."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Outside or inside","text":"Look at the outer contour of the uterus. A notch on the outside means a fusion problem (bicornuate); a smooth outside with a wall inside means a resorption problem (septate)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Who makes what","text":"Sertoli makes AMH, Leydig makes testosterone, 5-alpha-reductase in genital tissue makes DHT. Lose AMH and a uterus stays; lose testosterone action and the Wolffian ducts fade; lose DHT and the outside looks female."}],"highYield":["SRY absent or deleted means ovary or streak gonad; SRY present, even on an X, means testis.","Androgen insensitivity (46,XY): AMH still works, so there is no uterus and a short blind vagina, yet external genitalia are female and testes sit in the abdomen, inguinal canal, or labia.","5-alpha-reductase deficiency (46,XY): normal Wolffian structures, ambiguous or female external genitalia at birth, and virilization at puberty.","Septate uterus carries the highest risk of recurrent pregnancy loss among the fusion defects.","Indirect inguinal hernia and communicating hydrocele are the same defect, a patent processus vaginalis, differing only in its width.","Two arteries and one vein in the cord; a single umbilical artery prompts a search for renal and heart anomalies.","Extra nipples lie along the milk line from axilla to groin."],"pitfalls":["Hypospadias is ventral and from unfused urogenital folds; epispadias is dorsal and from a misplaced genital tubercle.","Testosterone builds the internal male ducts; DHT builds the external genitalia and prostate.","The upper vagina is Mullerian; the lower vagina comes from the urogenital sinus.","A communicating hydrocele changes size during the day; a noncommunicating hydrocele has a closed processus and a fixed size."],"dumps":[{"t":"Sex differentiation flowchart","p":"Draw a flowchart starting from a bipotential gonad with both duct pairs. Show what happens with SRY present and with SRY absent, and write in each hormone, the cell that makes it, and the structure it builds or removes.","l":["SRY on the Y chromosome makes the gonad a testis","No SRY means an ovary by default","Sertoli cells make AMH, which removes the Mullerian ducts","Leydig cells make testosterone, which keeps the Wolffian ducts","Wolffian ducts form epididymis, ductus deferens, seminal vesicle, ejaculatory duct","DHT from 5-alpha-reductase builds penis, scrotum, and prostate","Without AMH the Mullerian ducts form tubes, uterus, cervix, upper vagina","46,XY SRY deletion gives streak gonads and female anatomy; 46,XX SRY translocation gives testes"]},{"t":"Testicular descent and its defects","p":"Draw the testis in three positions: on the posterior abdominal wall, in the inguinal canal, and in the scrotum. Label the gubernaculum and processus vaginalis, then draw a hernia and a communicating hydrocele beside the normal result.","l":["Testis starts on the posterior abdominal wall","Gubernaculum guides descent into the scrotum","Processus vaginalis leads the way through the inguinal canal","Normal closure leaves only the tunica vaginalis","Wide patency lets bowel in: indirect inguinal hernia","Narrow patency lets fluid in: communicating hydrocele","Hernia enters the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","Testicular artery from the aorta and para-aortic lymph drainage reflect the starting point"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System (sections on the genital system)"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Third Month to Birth: The Fetus and Placenta"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Integumentary System (section on mammary glands)"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-3","RE-10","RE-13-C","RE-13-C1","RE-13-C4","RE-13-C9","RE-13-C10","RE-13-D3"]},"RE-2":{"id":"RE-2","summary":"The cells and tissues of the testis, ovary, female tract, breast, and prostate. Exam questions show a cell or a zone and ask what it makes or which disease starts there.","notes":[{"h":"Testis: two key cells","points":["Sertoli cells sit inside the seminiferous tubules and stretch from the basement membrane to the lumen.","Tight junctions between neighboring Sertoli cells form the blood-testis barrier, which shields developing haploid germ cells from the immune system.","Sertoli cells respond to FSH and make androgen-binding protein (keeps testosterone high in the tubule), inhibin B (feeds back to lower FSH), and AMH in the fetus.","Leydig cells sit in the interstitium between tubules near capillaries; they respond to LH and make testosterone.","Leydig cells look like pink polygonal cells with lipid droplets and sometimes Reinke crystals.","Sertoli cells and spermatogenesis are damaged by heat; Leydig cells are not, so testosterone stays normal in an undescended testis."]},{"h":"Sperm pathway","points":["Seminiferous tubules, straight tubules, rete testis, efferent ductules, epididymis (head, body, tail).","Ductus deferens, then the ejaculatory duct after it joins the seminal vesicle duct.","Prostatic urethra, membranous urethra, spongy (penile) urethra.","The epididymis is lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium with stereocilia; sperm gain motility and are stored there."]},{"h":"Female tract epithelium","points":["Uterine tube: simple columnar with ciliated cells and secretory (peg) cells; cilia move the egg toward the uterus.","Endometrium: simple columnar with tubular glands; the functional layer sheds and the basal layer regrows it.","Endocervix: simple columnar mucus-secreting epithelium.","Ectocervix and vagina: nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","The transformation zone is the area of squamous metaplasia at the squamocolumnar junction; HPV infection and cervical dysplasia arise here, so the Pap smear samples it."]},{"h":"Ovarian follicles","points":["Every follicle holds a primary oocyte arrested in prophase I until the LH surge.","Granulosa cells surround the oocyte; theca cells form outside the basement membrane once the follicle grows.","Theca interna makes androgens under LH, and granulosa cells convert them to estradiol with aromatase under FSH.","After ovulation the follicle becomes the corpus luteum, made of large granulosa lutein cells and smaller theca lutein cells that secrete progesterone and estrogen."]},{"h":"Breast unit","points":["The terminal duct lobular unit (TDLU) is a terminal duct with its cluster of small ductules (acini).","Each duct and acinus has two cell layers: inner luminal epithelial cells and outer myoepithelial cells on a basement membrane.","Most breast carcinomas, both ductal and lobular, arise in the TDLU.","Fibroadenoma arises from the specialized stroma of the TDLU and contains both stroma and ducts.","Loss of the myoepithelial layer around tumor nests marks invasive carcinoma."]},{"h":"Prostate zones","points":["Peripheral zone: posterior and lateral, largest share of glandular tissue, felt on digital rectal exam; most carcinomas arise here.","Transition zone: surrounds the proximal prostatic urethra; benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) arises here and compresses the urethra.","Central zone: surrounds the ejaculatory ducts and is seldom diseased.","Prostate glands have luminal secretory cells over basal cells; carcinoma glands lack the basal cell layer."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Follicle stages","cols":["Stage","Oocyte","Granulosa cells","Theca and other features"],"rows":[["Primordial","Primary oocyte in prophase I","One layer of flat cells","No theca"],["Primary","Primary oocyte; zona pellucida forms","One, then several layers of cuboidal cells","Theca begins to form around it"],["Secondary (antral)","Primary oocyte","Many layers with fluid spaces joining into an antrum","Theca interna (steroid-making) and theca externa (fibrous)"],["Mature (Graafian)","Finishes meiosis I at the LH surge, becomes a secondary oocyte","Oocyte sits on the cumulus oophorus, ringed by the corona radiata","Large antrum; bulges from the ovarian surface"],["Corpus luteum","Released at ovulation","Granulosa lutein cells (large, pale)","Theca lutein cells; secretes progesterone and estrogen, then becomes the corpus albicans"]]},{"title":"Where disease starts","cols":["Site","Normal tissue","Disease that arises there"],"rows":[["Cervical transformation zone","Squamocolumnar junction with squamous metaplasia","HPV infection, CIN, cervical squamous carcinoma"],["Breast TDLU epithelium","Luminal cells over myoepithelial cells","Ductal and lobular carcinoma"],["Breast TDLU stroma","Intralobular stroma","Fibroadenoma"],["Prostate transition zone","Glands around the proximal urethra","Benign prostatic hyperplasia"],["Prostate peripheral zone","Posterior and lateral glands","Prostate adenocarcinoma"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SEVEN UP","text":"Sperm pathway: Seminiferous tubules, Epididymis, Vas deferens, Ejaculatory duct, (Nothing), Urethra, Penis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sertoli supports, Leydig lives on LH","text":"Sertoli cells Support sperm and answer to FSH; Leydig cells answer to LH and make testosterone."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Peripheral is palpable","text":"Cancer starts in the peripheral zone, which lies against the rectum, so a hard nodule is felt on rectal exam. BPH grows in the middle around the urethra, so it blocks urine flow early."}],"highYield":["Inhibin B from Sertoli cells selectively lowers FSH; testosterone lowers LH.","The blood-testis barrier explains antisperm antibodies after trauma, vasectomy, or mumps orchitis.","Cryptorchidism lowers sperm count but leaves testosterone normal because heat harms Sertoli cells, not Leydig cells.","The Pap smear and HPV testing target the transformation zone.","Ciliary damage in the uterine tube (as after salpingitis) slows the egg and raises the risk of ectopic pregnancy.","BPH arises in the transition zone and usually spares the peripheral zone, so it does not predispose to prostate cancer."],"pitfalls":["Theca interna makes androgens under LH; granulosa cells aromatize them to estradiol under FSH.","Endocervix is columnar and ectocervix is squamous; the vagina is squamous throughout and has no glands.","Fibroadenoma comes from TDLU stroma; carcinoma comes from TDLU epithelium.","The oocyte in a Graafian follicle is still primary until the LH surge triggers completion of meiosis I."],"dumps":[{"t":"Seminiferous tubule cross section","p":"Draw a cross section of a seminiferous tubule with the interstitium around it. Label the Sertoli cells, Leydig cells, germ cells in order from base to lumen, the tight junctions, and write what each cell secretes and which pituitary hormone drives it.","l":["Spermatogonia at the basement membrane","Spermatocytes and spermatids moving toward the lumen","Sertoli cells spanning base to lumen","Tight junctions between Sertoli cells form the blood-testis barrier","Sertoli: androgen-binding protein and inhibin B under FSH","Leydig cells in the interstitium near capillaries","Leydig: testosterone under LH","Heat damages spermatogenesis but spares Leydig cells"]},{"t":"Female tract and follicle comparison","p":"Make a table of the female tract from uterine tube to vagina with the epithelium of each segment, then mark the transformation zone. Below it draw a primordial, primary, antral, and Graafian follicle side by side.","l":["Uterine tube: ciliated simple columnar with peg cells","Endometrium: simple columnar with glands; functional and basal layers","Endocervix: simple columnar mucus-secreting","Ectocervix and vagina: nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Transformation zone at the squamocolumnar junction is where dysplasia starts","Primordial: one flat layer; primary: cuboidal granulosa and zona pellucida","Antral: fluid antrum with theca interna and externa","Graafian: cumulus oophorus and corona radiata"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Male Reproductive System"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Female Reproductive System"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Female Genital System and Breast (sections on the cervix and the breast)"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-3","RE-7","RE-13-E6","RE-13-F1","RE-13-J1","RE-13-J6","RE-13-C1"]},"RE-3":{"id":"RE-3","summary":"How sperm and eggs are made, how they meet, and what happens from the zygote through week 8. Exam questions test meiotic arrest points, the steps of fertilization, where implantation goes wrong, and when the embryo is most vulnerable.","notes":[{"h":"Making gametes","points":["Spermatogenesis starts at puberty and runs continuously in the seminiferous tubules; one cycle takes about 64 to 74 days.","Each primary spermatocyte gives four functional spermatids after two meiotic divisions.","Oogonia multiply only before birth; those that survive become primary oocytes that arrest in prophase I and stay arrested until the LH surge before ovulation.","At the LH surge the oocyte finishes meiosis I, sheds the first polar body, and arrests again in metaphase II.","Meiosis II finishes only if a sperm enters, giving the ovum and the second polar body.","Each primary oocyte yields one functional ovum; the polar bodies degenerate.","The years-long arrest in prophase I is linked to more nondisjunction with older maternal age."]},{"h":"Spermiogenesis and sperm activation","points":["Spermiogenesis turns a round spermatid into a sperm without any cell division.","The Golgi apparatus forms the acrosome, a cap of enzymes over the nucleus.","A centriole forms the flagellum; mitochondria pack into the midpiece to power it.","The nucleus condenses and extra cytoplasm is shed as a residual body that Sertoli cells engulf.","Capacitation happens in the female tract over several hours: proteins and cholesterol are stripped from the sperm membrane and motility becomes hyperactive.","Only a capacitated sperm can undergo the acrosome reaction after binding the zona pellucida, releasing enzymes that digest a path through it."]},{"h":"Fertilization","points":["Fertilization usually happens in the ampulla of the uterine tube within about 24 hours of ovulation.","When the sperm and oocyte membranes fuse, calcium rises in the oocyte and cortical granules release enzymes that change the zona pellucida (cortical reaction), blocking other sperm.","The oocyte then completes meiosis II; male and female pronuclei join to form a diploid zygote.","The sperm sets genetic sex by carrying an X or a Y chromosome."]},{"h":"Week 1: travel and implantation","points":["The zygote cleaves as it moves down the tube, reaching the uterus as a morula around day 3 to 4.","The blastocyst has an inner cell mass (becomes the embryo) and an outer trophoblast (becomes placenta), and it hatches from the zona pellucida before implanting.","Implantation begins about day 6 in the secretory-phase endometrium, usually on the posterior wall of the uterine body.","Syncytiotrophoblast invades the endometrium and secretes hCG, which keeps the corpus luteum making progesterone so the endometrium is not shed.","hCG is detectable in maternal blood about 1 week after fertilization."]},{"h":"When implantation goes wrong","points":["Tubal ectopic pregnancy: most often in the ampulla; prior salpingitis (PID from chlamydia or gonorrhea) is a main risk because scarring and cilia loss slow the embryo.","Other ectopic risks: prior ectopic pregnancy, tubal surgery, and pregnancy with an IUD in place.","Ectopic presentation: missed period, pelvic pain, and spotting with a lower hCG than expected; rupture causes hemorrhage that pools in the rectouterine pouch.","Placenta previa: implantation in the lower uterine segment so the placenta covers or reaches the internal cervical os, causing painless bright red bleeding late in pregnancy.","Prior cesarean scar, multiparity, and older maternal age raise previa risk."]},{"h":"Weeks 2 to 8: the embryo forms","points":["Week 2: bilaminar disc of epiblast and hypoblast, with the amniotic cavity above and the yolk sac below.","Week 3: gastrulation; epiblast cells move through the primitive streak to form all three germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm), and the notochord forms.","Weeks 3 to 8 (embryonic period) are when organs form, so teratogens cause major structural defects then.","Before week 3 an exposure is all or none: the embryo dies or recovers fully."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Spermatogenesis and oogenesis","cols":["Feature","Spermatogenesis","Oogenesis"],"rows":[["When it starts","Puberty","Before birth"],["Germ cell supply","Spermatogonia renew throughout life","Fixed pool of oocytes made before birth"],["Meiosis I","Finished without pause","Arrested in prophase I until the LH surge"],["Meiosis II","Finished without pause","Arrested in metaphase II until fertilization"],["Gametes per primary cell","Four sperm","One ovum plus polar bodies"],["Cytoplasm","Shed as a residual body","Kept by the ovum; polar bodies get very little"]]},{"title":"From ovulation to week 8","cols":["Time after fertilization","Event","Key structure"],"rows":[["Day 0","Fertilization in the ampulla","Zygote"],["Days 3 to 4","Enters the uterus","Morula"],["Days 4 to 5","Cavity forms; zona pellucida lost","Blastocyst"],["About day 6","Implantation begins; hCG secretion starts","Syncytiotrophoblast"],["Week 2","Bilaminar disc","Epiblast and hypoblast"],["Week 3","Gastrulation; neural plate forms","Trilaminar disc"],["Weeks 3 to 8","Organogenesis; peak teratogen sensitivity","Embryo"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"An egg MET a sperm","text":"The secondary oocyte waits in Metaphase II until it meets a sperm."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Rule of twos and threes","text":"Week 2 has twos: two layers (epiblast, hypoblast), two cavities (amniotic cavity, yolk sac), two trophoblast layers. Week 3 has threes: three germ layers."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Golgi caps, centriole swims","text":"The Golgi builds the acrosome cap on the head; the centriole builds the tail."}],"highYield":["Primary oocytes wait in prophase I from fetal life until ovulation; secondary oocytes wait in metaphase II until fertilization.","The cortical reaction blocks polyspermy; two sperm fertilizing one egg gives a triploid conceptus, as in a partial mole.","hCG from the syncytiotrophoblast rescues the corpus luteum; this is the basis of pregnancy tests.","Prior PID is the classic risk factor for tubal ectopic pregnancy, and the ampulla is the most common site.","Painless third-trimester bleeding suggests placenta previa; painful bleeding with a firm uterus suggests placental abruption.","Weeks 3 to 8 after fertilization are the window for major structural birth defects.","All three germ layers come from the epiblast."],"pitfalls":["Spermiogenesis is the shape change from spermatid to sperm; spermatogenesis is the whole process from spermatogonium.","Capacitation happens in the female tract; the acrosome reaction happens at the zona pellucida.","Embryology weeks count from fertilization; obstetric gestational age counts from the last menstrual period and runs about 2 weeks ahead.","The hypoblast does not form endoderm; epiblast cells replace it during gastrulation."],"dumps":[{"t":"Meiosis side by side","p":"Draw spermatogenesis and oogenesis as two parallel columns from the diploid stem cell to the final gametes. Mark each arrest point and the event that releases it, and count the gametes at the bottom.","l":["Spermatogonium to primary spermatocyte to secondary spermatocyte to spermatid","Spermiogenesis turns spermatids into sperm","Four sperm per primary spermatocyte","Primary oocyte arrested in prophase I before birth","LH surge completes meiosis I and makes the first polar body","Secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase II","Fertilization completes meiosis II with the second polar body","One ovum per primary oocyte"]},{"t":"Ovulation to implantation map","p":"Draw the ovary, uterine tube, and uterus. Trace the egg and embryo from ovulation to implantation, writing the day and the event at each stop, and mark where tubal ectopic pregnancy and placenta previa occur.","l":["Ovulation releases a secondary oocyte","Fertilization in the ampulla with the cortical reaction","Morula enters the uterus about day 3 to 4","Blastocyst with inner cell mass and trophoblast hatches from the zona","Implantation about day 6 on the posterior uterine wall","Syncytiotrophoblast makes hCG to keep the corpus luteum","Ectopic site most often the ampulla, with prior salpingitis as a risk","Placenta previa sits over the internal os in the lower segment"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Gametogenesis: Conversion of Germ Cells Into Male and Female Gametes"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"First Week of Development: Ovulation to Implantation"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Second Week of Development: Bilaminar Germ Disc"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Third Week of Development: Trilaminar Germ Disc"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-1","RE-2","RE-6","RE-10","RE-13-H","RE-13-H2","RE-13-B6","RE-13-B7"]},"RE-4":{"id":"RE-4","summary":"Where the reproductive organs sit and what holds them in place: pelvic ligaments and pouches, the pelvic floor, the scrotum and spermatic cord, the breast, the penis, and the perineum. Exam questions link a structure to its contents, its support, or what happens when it tears or weakens.","notes":[{"h":"Broad ligament and pouches","points":["The broad ligament is a double fold of peritoneum draped from the sides of the uterus to the lateral pelvic walls.","Mesometrium: the largest part, beside the uterus. Mesosalpinx: around the uterine tube. Mesovarium: attaches the ovary to the back of the broad ligament.","The vesicouterine pouch lies between bladder and uterus; the rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas) lies between uterus and rectum.","The rectouterine pouch is the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity in a standing woman, so blood or pus collects there; it can be reached through the posterior vaginal fornix.","In the male the lowest recess is the rectovesical pouch."]},{"h":"Named pelvic ligaments","points":["Suspensory (infundibulopelvic) ligament: ovary to lateral pelvic wall; carries the ovarian artery and vein.","Ovarian ligament: ovary to the side of the uterus; no major vessels.","Round ligament: uterine horn through the deep ring and inguinal canal to the labium majus.","Cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament: cervix to lateral pelvic wall; carries the uterine artery and vein.","Uterosacral ligaments: cervix back to the sacrum; they hold the cervix posteriorly."]},{"h":"Pelvic floor support","points":["The levator ani (puborectalis, pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus) forms most of the pelvic floor and holds up the pelvic organs.","The perineal body is a fibromuscular knot between the vagina and anus where perineal muscles and the external anal sphincter attach.","The cardinal and uterosacral ligaments suspend the cervix and upper vagina from above.","Vaginal childbirth, aging and low estrogen after menopause, obesity, and chronic cough or straining weaken these supports.","Weak support lets organs drop: uterine prolapse (uterus into the vagina), cystocele (bladder into the anterior wall), rectocele (rectum into the posterior wall)."]},{"h":"Scrotum and spermatic cord","points":["Scrotal layers from outside in: skin, dartos fascia and muscle, external spermatic fascia, cremaster muscle and fascia, internal spermatic fascia, tunica vaginalis.","The spermatic fasciae come from the abdominal wall layers the testis passed through: external oblique, internal oblique, and transversalis fascia.","Cord contents: ductus deferens, testicular artery, artery to the ductus, cremasteric artery, pampiniform venous plexus, genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, autonomic nerves, and lymphatics.","The pampiniform plexus cools arterial blood going to the testis; its dilation is a varicocele."]},{"h":"Breast position","points":["The breast lies on the pectoralis major and serratus anterior from about rib 2 to rib 6, and from the sternal edge to the midaxillary line.","A loose retromammary space lets the breast slide over the pectoral fascia.","The axillary tail extends toward the axilla; the upper outer quadrant holds the most glandular tissue and the most cancers.","Suspensory (Cooper) ligaments run from the dermis to the deep fascia; carcinoma that pulls on them causes skin dimpling."]},{"h":"Penis and perineum","points":["Two corpora cavernosa lie dorsally; one corpus spongiosum lies ventrally around the urethra and expands into the glans.","Each corpus is wrapped in a tough tunica albuginea, thickest around the corpora cavernosa.","Penile fracture is a tear of the tunica albuginea of a corpus cavernosum when the erect penis is forcefully bent: a pop, sudden loss of erection, swelling, and bruising.","The perineum is diamond-shaped: pubic symphysis in front, ischiopubic rami and ischial tuberosities at the sides, sacrotuberous ligaments and coccyx behind.","A line between the ischial tuberosities splits it into the anterior urogenital triangle and the posterior anal triangle.","The prostate lies below the bladder and in front of the rectum, so its posterior surface is felt through the anterior rectal wall on digital rectal exam."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Female pelvic ligaments","cols":["Ligament","Runs from and to","Contents","Clinical link"],"rows":[["Suspensory (infundibulopelvic)","Ovary to lateral pelvic wall","Ovarian artery and vein","Clamped in oophorectomy; ureter lies close by at the pelvic brim"],["Ovarian","Ovary to side of uterus","No major vessels","Remnant of the gubernaculum"],["Round","Uterine horn through the inguinal canal to the labium majus","Small artery and lymphatics","Remnant of the gubernaculum; stretch pain in pregnancy"],["Cardinal (transverse cervical)","Cervix to lateral pelvic wall","Uterine artery and vein","Ureter passes beneath the uterine artery here"],["Uterosacral","Cervix to sacrum","Fibrous tissue and nerves","Supports the cervix; weak support allows prolapse"],["Broad","Uterus to lateral pelvic wall","Uterine tube, ovary, round and ovarian ligaments, vessels","Mesometrium, mesosalpinx, mesovarium"]]},{"title":"Scrotal layers and their origin","cols":["Scrotal or cord layer","Comes from"],"rows":[["Skin","Abdominal skin"],["Dartos fascia and muscle","Superficial fascia of the abdominal wall"],["External spermatic fascia","External oblique aponeurosis"],["Cremaster muscle and fascia","Internal oblique muscle"],["Internal spermatic fascia","Transversalis fascia"],["Tunica vaginalis","Peritoneum (processus vaginalis)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Each layer donates a coat","text":"As the testis passed through the abdominal wall, each layer gave it a covering in the same order: external oblique outside, internal oblique in the middle, transversalis fascia inside, peritoneum innermost."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ovarian vessels go high, uterine vessels go low","text":"Ovarian vessels ride in the suspensory ligament at the top of the broad ligament; uterine vessels ride in the cardinal ligament at its base."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lowest point collects","text":"Fluid runs downhill, so in a standing woman blood from a ruptured ectopic or cyst pools in the rectouterine pouch."}],"highYield":["The rectouterine pouch is where free fluid collects and is sampled through the posterior fornix.","The suspensory ligament of the ovary carries the ovarian vessels; the cardinal ligament carries the uterine vessels.","The round ligament travels through the inguinal canal, filling the place the spermatic cord takes in the male.","Skin dimpling over a breast mass means tumor is tethering the Cooper ligaments.","Penile fracture tears the tunica albuginea of the corpus cavernosum.","Cystocele bulges from the anterior vaginal wall; rectocele bulges from the posterior wall.","The prostate lies directly in front of the rectum, so the peripheral zone is reachable by digital rectal exam."],"pitfalls":["The ovarian ligament connects ovary to uterus and carries no major vessels; the suspensory ligament carries the ovarian vessels.","Cremaster comes from the internal oblique; the external spermatic fascia comes from the external oblique.","Dimpling comes from Cooper ligament traction; peau d'orange comes from blocked dermal lymphatics.","The corpus spongiosum surrounds the urethra; the corpora cavernosa do not."],"dumps":[{"t":"Female pelvis in two views","p":"Draw a midsagittal view of the female pelvis and a posterior view of the uterus with the broad ligament. Label the pouches, the three parts of the broad ligament, and each named ligament with what it carries.","l":["Vesicouterine pouch in front, rectouterine pouch behind the uterus","Rectouterine pouch as the lowest peritoneal point","Mesometrium, mesosalpinx, and mesovarium","Suspensory ligament with the ovarian vessels","Ovarian ligament from ovary to uterus","Round ligament heading to the inguinal canal","Cardinal ligament with the uterine vessels","Uterosacral ligaments to the sacrum"]},{"t":"Scrotum and perineum","p":"Draw the layers of the scrotum from skin to testis, labeling where each layer comes from, then list the spermatic cord contents. Next to it draw the perineum as a diamond and label its boundaries and two triangles.","l":["Skin and dartos outermost","External spermatic fascia from the external oblique","Cremaster from the internal oblique","Internal spermatic fascia from the transversalis fascia","Tunica vaginalis from the processus vaginalis","Cord holds ductus deferens, testicular artery, and pampiniform plexus","Perineal boundaries: pubic symphysis, ischiopubic rami, ischial tuberosities, sacrotuberous ligaments, coccyx","Urogenital triangle in front, anal triangle behind"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, section on the inguinal region, spermatic cord, and scrotum"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the breasts"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-1","RE-5","RE-13-I","RE-13-I2","RE-13-I3","RE-13-D5","RE-13-E6"]},"RE-5":{"id":"RE-5","summary":"The arteries, veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of the reproductive organs and breast. Exam questions ask why a varicocele is left-sided, which structure is injured in a pelvic operation, where a tumor spreads first, and which nerve controls each step of the male sexual response.","notes":[{"h":"Gonadal vessels","points":["Both gonadal arteries come off the abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries, because the gonads started high on the posterior wall.","The right gonadal vein drains straight into the inferior vena cava at an angle.","The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein at a right angle after a longer course.","The longer column of blood and right-angle entry raise pressure on the left, so varicocele is far more common on the left.","A varicocele feels like a bag of worms, grows with standing or straining, and can lower fertility by warming the testis.","A new varicocele on the right, or one that does not empty when lying down, suggests a mass blocking venous drainage."]},{"h":"Uterine artery and the ureter","points":["The uterine artery comes from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery.","It runs medially in the base of the broad ligament (cardinal ligament) toward the cervix, then climbs up the side of the uterus.","About 1 to 2 cm lateral to the cervix the ureter passes beneath the uterine artery.","Clamping the uterine vessels during hysterectomy can catch or cut the ureter, causing hydronephrosis or a urine leak.","The uterine artery joins with the ovarian artery near the uterine horn."]},{"h":"Lymph drainage","points":["Testis and ovary drain to para-aortic (lumbar) nodes, following the gonadal vessels back to their origin.","Uterine body, cervix, and upper vagina drain to internal and external iliac nodes.","Scrotum, vulva, and the lower vagina below the hymen drain to superficial inguinal nodes.","A testicular tumor spreads first to para-aortic nodes; a scrotal skin cancer spreads first to inguinal nodes."]},{"h":"Nerves of the male sexual response","points":["Erection: parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4) through the cavernous nerves.","Emission: sympathetic hypogastric nerves move semen into the prostatic urethra and close the internal urethral sphincter.","Ejaculation: the somatic pudendal nerve drives rhythmic contraction of the bulbospongiosus muscle.","Cavernous nerve injury in pelvic surgery causes erectile dysfunction; loss of sympathetic tone at the bladder neck causes retrograde ejaculation."]},{"h":"Pudendal nerve","points":["Roots S2 to S4. It leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis.","It hooks around the ischial spine and sacrospinous ligament and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen.","It runs in the pudendal canal to the perineum and gives the inferior rectal, perineal, and dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris.","The ischial spine is the landmark for a pudendal nerve block, felt through the vaginal wall during labor.","Injury from childbirth or prolonged cycling causes perineal numbness and can weaken the external urethral and anal sphincters."]},{"h":"Breast vessels and lymph","points":["Arteries: perforating branches of the internal thoracic artery (medial breast), lateral thoracic artery, and pectoral branches of the thoracoacromial artery, with help from intercostal arteries.","Most lymph (about three quarters) drains to axillary nodes, so the axilla is the first site checked for spread.","Medial quadrants also drain to parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels.","Axillary node surgery can cause arm lymphedema and can injure the long thoracic nerve, giving a winged scapula."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Reproductive lymph drainage","cols":["Organ","First nodes","Why"],"rows":[["Testis","Para-aortic","Follows the testicular vessels to their origin near the renal vessels"],["Ovary","Para-aortic","Follows the ovarian vessels in the suspensory ligament"],["Uterine body and cervix","Internal and external iliac","Follows the uterine vessels to the pelvic wall"],["Upper vagina","Internal and external iliac","Pelvic organ above the hymen"],["Lower vagina below the hymen and vulva","Superficial inguinal","Perineal skin and mucosa"],["Scrotum","Superficial inguinal","Perineal skin"],["Breast","Axillary, plus parasternal for medial quadrants","Follows lateral thoracic and internal thoracic vessels"]]},{"title":"Male sexual response","cols":["Step","Division","Nerve and level","What it does"],"rows":[["Erection","Parasympathetic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2 to S4","NO release relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle"],["Emission","Sympathetic","Hypogastric nerves, about T11 to L2","Moves semen into the urethra and closes the internal sphincter"],["Ejaculation","Somatic","Pudendal nerve, S2 to S4","Bulbospongiosus contracts to expel semen"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Point, Squeeze, and Shoot","text":"Point: Parasympathetic erection. Squeeze: Sympathetic emission. Shoot: Somatic (pudendal) ejaculation."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"S2, 3, 4 keeps the penis off the floor","text":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves for erection and the pudendal nerve both come from S2 to S4."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Water under the bridge","text":"The ureter (water) runs under the uterine artery (bridge) beside the cervix, so it is at risk when that artery is clamped."}],"highYield":["Left varicocele: the left gonadal vein enters the left renal vein at a right angle.","Testicular cancer spreads first to para-aortic nodes; scrotal skin cancer spreads first to superficial inguinal nodes.","The ureter passes beneath the uterine artery near the cervix and is the classic structure injured at hysterectomy.","Parasympathetic erection, sympathetic emission, somatic ejaculation.","The pudendal nerve block uses the ischial spine as its landmark.","Medial breast tumors can spread to parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic artery."],"pitfalls":["The scrotum and testis drain to different nodes: inguinal for the scrotum, para-aortic for the testis.","Erection is parasympathetic; emission is sympathetic.","The pudendal nerve leaves through the greater sciatic foramen and enters the perineum through the lesser sciatic foramen.","The uterine artery comes from the internal iliac; the ovarian artery comes from the aorta."],"dumps":[{"t":"Gonadal vessels and lymph","p":"Draw the abdominal aorta, IVC, and both kidneys. Add both gonadal arteries and veins, mark where each vein drains, and draw the para-aortic and superficial inguinal nodes with the organs that drain to each.","l":["Gonadal arteries from the aorta below the renal arteries","Right gonadal vein to the IVC","Left gonadal vein to the left renal vein at a right angle","Higher left-sided pressure explains left varicocele","Testis and ovary drain to para-aortic nodes","Scrotum, vulva, and lower vagina drain to superficial inguinal nodes","Uterus, cervix, and upper vagina drain to iliac nodes"]},{"t":"Pelvic nerves and the ureter","p":"Draw the posterior pelvis with the sacrum, ischial spine, and both sciatic foramina. Trace the pudendal nerve, then draw the uterine artery crossing over the ureter beside the cervix, and list the nerve for each step of the male sexual response.","l":["Pudendal nerve from S2 to S4","Exits through the greater sciatic foramen","Wraps around the ischial spine and sacrospinous ligament","Re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen into the pudendal canal","Ischial spine marked as the block landmark","Uterine artery from the internal iliac crossing above the ureter near the cervix","Erection: pelvic splanchnic; emission: hypogastric; ejaculation: pudendal"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum (sections on the pelvic vessels, lymph drainage, and nerves)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, section on the posterior abdominal wall vessels"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the breasts"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-4","RE-6","RE-13-D5","RE-13-D1","RE-13-J9","RE-13-E6"]},"RE-6":{"id":"RE-6","summary":"How the menstrual cycle, erection, pregnancy, lactation, and labor work. Exam questions ask you to predict a hormone level, a lab value in pregnancy, or the next step in a feedback loop.","notes":[{"h":"Menstrual cycle","points":["Follicular phase: FSH grows a dominant follicle whose estradiol drives the proliferative phase of the endometrium.","High sustained estradiol switches to positive feedback and triggers the LH surge; ovulation follows about 36 hours after the surge begins.","Luteal phase: the corpus luteum makes progesterone, which turns the endometrium secretory (coiled glands, glycogen, edematous stroma) and raises basal body temperature about 0.3 to 0.5 °C (0.5 to 0.9 °F).","Without hCG the corpus luteum lasts about 14 days, then regresses; falling progesterone causes spiral artery spasm and shedding of the functional layer (menses).","The luteal phase is fixed near 14 days, so differences in cycle length come from the follicular phase.","Ovulation therefore happens about 14 days before the next period, whatever the cycle length."]},{"h":"Erection","points":["Parasympathetic nerves and endothelium release nitric oxide (NO).","NO activates guanylyl cyclase, raising cGMP and lowering calcium in cavernosal smooth muscle, which relaxes.","Arterial inflow fills the sinusoids, which swell and press the small veins against the tunica albuginea, trapping blood.","Phosphodiesterase 5 breaks down cGMP to end the erection; PDE5 inhibitors prolong it."]},{"h":"Hormones of pregnancy","points":["hCG from the syncytiotrophoblast acts on the LH receptor to keep the corpus luteum making progesterone.","By about weeks 7 to 10 the placenta makes enough progesterone itself (luteal-placental shift), near the end of the first trimester.","The placenta lacks the enzyme (17-alpha-hydroxylase) to make androgens, so it needs fetal adrenal DHEA-S to make estrogen.","Fetal adrenal DHEA-S is 16-hydroxylated in the fetal liver, then turned into estriol by the placenta; low estriol can mean a problem in the fetal adrenal, liver, or placenta.","Human placental lactogen (hPL) rises with placental mass, causes maternal insulin resistance and lipolysis, and spares glucose for the fetus.","If the maternal pancreas cannot raise insulin output enough to overcome this resistance, gestational diabetes develops, usually in the second half of pregnancy."]},{"h":"Maternal adaptations","points":["Plasma volume rises about 40 to 50 percent and red cell mass only about 20 to 30 percent, giving a dilutional anemia.","Cardiac output rises about 30 to 50 percent while systemic vascular resistance falls, so blood pressure dips in the second trimester.","Renal plasma flow and GFR rise about 50 percent, so serum creatinine and BUN fall.","Progesterone drives the respiratory center, raising minute ventilation; PaCO2 falls to about 30 mm Hg (nonpregnant 35 to 45) with a compensated respiratory alkalosis.","Clotting factors and fibrinogen rise and protein S falls; with venous stasis from the uterus this makes pregnancy hypercoagulable."]},{"h":"Lactation","points":["In pregnancy estrogen grows the ducts and progesterone grows the alveoli, while prolactin, hPL, cortisol, and insulin ready the alveolar cells.","High placental progesterone blocks milk secretion until delivery; its fall after the placenta is delivered starts copious milk in about 2 to 3 days.","Suckling lowers hypothalamic dopamine, so prolactin rises and drives milk synthesis.","Suckling also releases oxytocin from the posterior pituitary, which contracts myoepithelial cells to eject milk (let-down) and contracts the uterus.","Suckling suppresses GnRH pulses, lowering LH and FSH and delaying ovulation (lactational amenorrhea)."]},{"h":"Labor","points":["Late in pregnancy estrogen adds oxytocin receptors and gap junctions to the myometrium.","The fetal head stretches the cervix, signaling the hypothalamus to release more oxytocin (Ferguson reflex).","Oxytocin and prostaglandins raise myometrial calcium and contraction, which pushes the head harder on the cervix: positive feedback until delivery.","Prostaglandins also soften (ripen) the cervix; prostaglandin inhibitors can delay labor."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Cycle phases in a 28-day cycle","cols":["Days","Ovarian phase","Main hormone","Endometrial phase"],"rows":[["1 to 5","Early follicular","Estrogen and progesterone low; FSH rising","Menstrual"],["6 to 14","Follicular","Estradiol rising","Proliferative"],["About 14","Ovulation","LH surge","Late proliferative"],["15 to 28","Luteal (fixed about 14 days)","Progesterone","Secretory"]]},{"title":"Maternal changes in pregnancy","cols":["System","Change","Result"],"rows":[["Blood","Plasma volume up more than red cell mass","Dilutional anemia"],["Heart","Cardiac output up","More flow to uterus, kidneys, and skin"],["Vessels","Systemic vascular resistance down","Lower blood pressure in the second trimester"],["Kidney","GFR up about 50 percent","Lower serum creatinine and BUN"],["Lungs","Minute ventilation up","PaCO2 about 30 mm Hg; respiratory alkalosis"],["Clotting","More fibrinogen and factors, less protein S","Higher risk of DVT and PE"],["Metabolism","hPL and other placental hormones raise insulin resistance","Gestational diabetes when insulin output cannot keep up"]]},{"title":"Lactation hormones","cols":["Hormone","Source","Role"],"rows":[["Estrogen","Placenta","Duct growth"],["Progesterone","Placenta","Alveolar growth; blocks milk secretion until delivery"],["Prolactin","Anterior pituitary","Milk synthesis; rises with suckling as dopamine falls"],["Oxytocin","Posterior pituitary","Myoepithelial contraction and milk ejection; uterine contraction"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Count back 14","text":"To find ovulation, count back 14 days from the next period. A 35-day cycle ovulates near day 21; a 28-day cycle near day 14."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Prolactin makes, oxytocin moves","text":"Prolactin makes the milk; oxytocin moves it out by squeezing the myoepithelial cells."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Five up, four down","text":"Volume, cardiac output, GFR, ventilation, and clotting go up; vascular resistance, creatinine, PaCO2, and hemoglobin concentration go down."}],"highYield":["A secretory endometrium proves ovulation occurred; anovulatory cycles stay proliferative.","hCG shares its alpha subunit with LH, FSH, and TSH and acts on the LH receptor.","Low maternal estriol can reflect fetal adrenal failure (as in anencephaly) or placental sulfatase deficiency.","A serum creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL can be abnormal in pregnancy because GFR normally rises.","Gestational diabetes causes fetal hyperinsulinemia, macrosomia, and neonatal hypoglycemia, since glucose crosses the placenta and insulin does not.","Failure to lactate after a postpartum hemorrhage suggests pituitary infarction (Sheehan syndrome).","PDE5 inhibitors with nitrates can cause dangerous hypotension because both raise cGMP."],"pitfalls":["The follicular phase varies in length; the luteal phase does not.","Prolactin is high throughout pregnancy, yet milk is not made until progesterone falls after delivery.","Pregnancy causes respiratory alkalosis; a normal PaCO2 of 40 mm Hg in late pregnancy suggests CO2 retention.","The anemia of pregnancy is dilutional; red cell mass actually rises."],"dumps":[{"t":"Menstrual cycle graph","p":"Draw a 28-day cycle with four stacked panels: pituitary hormones (FSH, LH), ovarian hormones (estradiol, progesterone), the ovary, and the endometrium. Mark ovulation and the length of each phase.","l":["FSH rises early to recruit follicles","Estradiol peaks just before the LH surge","LH surge triggers ovulation about day 14","Progesterone peaks in the mid-luteal phase from the corpus luteum","Proliferative endometrium under estrogen; secretory under progesterone","Basal body temperature rises after ovulation","Luteal phase fixed at about 14 days","Falling progesterone causes menses"]},{"t":"Pregnancy and lactation loop","p":"Build a table of the maternal changes of pregnancy for blood, heart, kidney, lungs, clotting, and glucose. Then draw the suckling reflex showing both prolactin and oxytocin arms, and draw the Ferguson reflex as a loop.","l":["Dilutional anemia from plasma volume outpacing red cell mass","Higher cardiac output with lower systemic vascular resistance","Higher GFR with lower creatinine","Respiratory alkalosis from progesterone-driven ventilation","Hypercoagulable state","hPL-driven insulin resistance leading to gestational diabetes when compensation fails","Suckling lowers dopamine, raising prolactin for milk synthesis","Suckling releases oxytocin for milk ejection and suppresses GnRH","Cervical stretch raises oxytocin, which strengthens contractions in a positive loop"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pregnancy and Lactation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male (section on the male sexual act)"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-3","RE-5","RE-7","RE-8","RE-13-A","RE-13-A2","RE-13-D1","RE-13-H4"]},"RE-7":{"id":"RE-7","summary":"How the hypothalamus, pituitary, and gonads talk to each other through GnRH, LH, FSH, sex steroids, inhibin, and prolactin. The exam asks you to predict hormone levels when one link in the axis is changed, from a GnRH analog to a prolactinoma to a shift in SHBG.","notes":[{"h":"GnRH must pulse","points":["Arcuate nucleus neurons release GnRH in pulses about every 1 to 2 hours into the hypophyseal portal blood.","Each pulse makes gonadotrophs release LH and FSH; faster pulses favor LH, slower pulses favor FSH.","Continuous GnRH (or a long-acting analog such as leuprolide) gives a brief flare, then receptor downregulation, so LH, FSH, and sex steroids fall.","This is why GnRH analogs given continuously treat prostate cancer, endometriosis, and central precocious puberty, while pulsatile pump dosing induces ovulation."]},{"h":"Male axis","points":["LH acts on Leydig cells to make testosterone.","FSH acts on Sertoli cells to make androgen-binding protein (keeps intratesticular testosterone high), inhibin B, and aromatase.","Testosterone feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary mainly to lower LH.","Inhibin B feeds back on the pituitary to lower FSH selectively.","Exogenous testosterone suppresses LH and FSH, so intratesticular testosterone falls and the testes shrink with low sperm counts."]},{"h":"Ovary: two cells, two gonadotropins","points":["LH acts on theca cells, which turn cholesterol into androstenedione.","Androstenedione diffuses to granulosa cells, where FSH-induced aromatase turns it into estradiol.","Low to moderate estradiol gives negative feedback on LH and FSH.","Estradiol held above about 200 pg/mL for about 2 days flips to positive feedback and triggers the midcycle LH surge.","Ovulation follows about 34 to 36 hours after the surge begins; the corpus luteum then secretes progesterone, estradiol, and inhibin A."]},{"h":"Transport in blood","points":["Testosterone circulates about 1 to 2% free, about 30 to 50% weakly bound to albumin, and the rest tightly bound to SHBG.","Free plus albumin-bound hormone is the bioavailable fraction that reaches tissues.","SHBG rises with estrogen (pregnancy, oral contraceptives), hyperthyroidism, and cirrhosis.","SHBG falls with androgens, insulin resistance (high insulin), obesity, and hypothyroidism.","Low SHBG raises free testosterone even when total testosterone is normal, as in PCOS with hirsutism."]},{"h":"What each steroid does","points":["DHT: external genitalia in the fetus, prostate growth, and male-pattern hair loss.","Testosterone: Wolffian duct development, spermatogenesis, muscle mass, libido, and voice deepening.","Estrogen: proliferative endometrium, breast duct growth, thin stretchy cervical mucus, epiphyseal closure, bone preservation, and higher hepatic binding proteins.","Progesterone: secretory endometrium, thick cervical mucus, quiet myometrium, breast lobule development, and a basal body temperature rise of about 0.3 to 0.5 °C (0.5 to 0.9 °F)."]},{"h":"Puberty","points":["Puberty starts when the GnRH pulse generator reactivates after childhood quiescence (kisspeptin signaling helps drive it).","The first sign in the lab is LH pulses during sleep, which later spread across the whole day.","Usual female sequence: thelarche, pubarche, growth spurt, menarche (menarche comes about 2 to 3 years after breast budding).","In boys, testicular enlargement comes first, and the growth spurt comes later than in girls."]},{"h":"Prolactin","points":["Dopamine from the hypothalamus tonically inhibits lactotrophs through D2 receptors; TRH and estrogen stimulate them.","Anything that cuts dopamine (stalk compression, D2 blocking drugs) raises prolactin.","High prolactin suppresses GnRH pulses, so LH, FSH, and sex steroids fall.","Result in women: amenorrhea, galactorrhea, infertility; in men: low libido, erectile dysfunction, infertility.","Primary hypothyroidism raises TRH, which can raise prolactin."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hormones of the reproductive axis","cols":["Hormone","Source","Target and action","Feedback"],"rows":[["GnRH","Hypothalamic arcuate neurons","Gonadotrophs; releases LH and FSH when pulsatile","Inhibited by sex steroids and prolactin"],["LH","Anterior pituitary","Leydig and theca cells make androgen; triggers ovulation; supports corpus luteum","Lowered by testosterone; surge triggered by sustained high estradiol"],["FSH","Anterior pituitary","Sertoli cells (ABP, inhibin B); granulosa cells (aromatase, follicle growth)","Lowered by inhibin and sex steroids"],["Inhibin B","Sertoli and granulosa cells","Pituitary gonadotrophs","Selectively lowers FSH"],["Prolactin","Anterior pituitary lactotrophs","Breast milk production; suppresses GnRH","Tonically inhibited by dopamine"]]},{"title":"Predict the labs","cols":["Situation","LH and FSH","Sex steroid"],"rows":[["Continuous GnRH analog","Low after initial flare","Low"],["Exogenous testosterone","Low","Serum testosterone high, intratesticular low"],["Primary gonadal failure","High","Low"],["Prolactinoma","Low or inappropriately normal","Low"],["Hypothalamic amenorrhea","Low or normal","Low estradiol"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"L for Leydig, L for LH","text":"LH goes to Leydig cells; FSH goes to Sertoli cells, which nurse sperm and make inhibin."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pulse or pause","text":"Pulsatile GnRH turns the axis on; a steady GnRH signal turns it off. If a drug gives GnRH continuously, expect low LH, FSH, and sex steroids."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Breasts, hair, height, period","text":"The usual order of female puberty: thelarche, pubarche, growth spurt, then menarche."}],"highYield":["Continuous leuprolide suppresses LH and FSH after a short flare; pulsatile GnRH stimulates them.","Inhibin B selectively suppresses FSH; testosterone mainly suppresses LH.","Theca cells (LH) make androgens; granulosa cells (FSH, aromatase) convert them to estradiol.","Sustained high estradiol switches feedback from negative to positive and triggers the LH surge.","Insulin resistance and obesity lower SHBG and raise free testosterone; estrogen raises SHBG.","Hyperprolactinemia causes amenorrhea and galactorrhea by suppressing GnRH pulses.","Puberty begins with sleep-associated LH pulses."],"pitfalls":["Total testosterone can look normal while free testosterone is high; check SHBG in suspected androgen excess.","Exogenous testosterone raises serum testosterone but lowers intratesticular testosterone and sperm production.","DHT builds the external genitalia and prostate; testosterone builds the Wolffian ducts. Do not swap them.","A GnRH analog first raises LH and FSH (flare) before suppressing them; timing in the stem matters."],"dumps":[{"t":"Two-cell model","p":"Draw one theca cell and one granulosa cell side by side. Show which gonadotropin acts on each, the steroid each makes, and how the cells cooperate to make estradiol.","l":["LH receptor on theca cell","Theca cell converts cholesterol to androstenedione","Androstenedione diffuses to granulosa cell","FSH receptor on granulosa cell induces aromatase","Aromatase converts androgen to estradiol","Sustained high estradiol switches to positive feedback and LH surge","Granulosa cells also make inhibin B"]},{"t":"Male axis feedback loops","p":"Draw the hypothalamus, pituitary, Leydig cell, and Sertoli cell. Add arrows for GnRH, LH, FSH, testosterone, ABP, and inhibin B, and mark each feedback loop.","l":["Pulsatile GnRH to anterior pituitary","LH to Leydig cell, which makes testosterone","FSH to Sertoli cell, which makes ABP and inhibin B","ABP keeps testosterone high in the seminiferous tubule","Testosterone inhibits GnRH and LH","Inhibin B inhibits FSH","Exogenous testosterone shuts down LH and FSH and shrinks the testes"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male (and Function of the Pineal Gland)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on disorders of the female reproductive tract (amenorrhea)"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-6","RE-8","RE-9","RE-13-A","RE-13-A1","RE-13-G3"]},"RE-8":{"id":"RE-8","summary":"The enzyme steps that turn cholesterol into progesterone, androgens, and estrogens, and what happens when one step is missing, overactive in fat, or not cleared by the liver. It also covers the glycoprotein hormones and the chemistry of semen, which the exam links to molar pregnancy and PSA.","notes":[{"h":"Cholesterol to sex steroids","points":["StAR moves cholesterol into the inner mitochondrial membrane (the rate-limiting step).","Cholesterol side-chain cleavage enzyme (CYP11A1, desmolase) makes pregnenolone; LH stimulates this step through cAMP.","3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase turns pregnenolone into progesterone.","17-alpha-hydroxylase then 17,20-lyase (both on CYP17) turn pregnenolone into DHEA, which 3-beta-HSD turns into androstenedione.","17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase turns androstenedione into testosterone.","Aromatase turns testosterone into estradiol and androstenedione into estrone."]},{"h":"Testosterone to DHT","points":["5-alpha-reductase (type 2) converts testosterone to the more potent DHT in genital skin and prostate.","5-alpha-reductase deficiency is autosomal recessive in 46,XY individuals with normal testes.","At birth: ambiguous or female-appearing external genitalia, while Wolffian ducts are normal (testosterone-driven) and Mullerian ducts are absent (AMH present).","At puberty: the testosterone surge virilizes the body (phallic growth, muscle, deep voice), but the prostate stays small and facial hair is sparse.","Labs: normal or high testosterone, low DHT, high testosterone to DHT ratio."]},{"h":"Aromatase in fat","points":["Adipose tissue aromatase converts androstenedione (from adrenal and ovary) to estrone.","After menopause, this peripheral estrone is the main estrogen.","In obesity, more fat means more estrone without matching progesterone (unopposed estrogen).","Unopposed estrogen drives endometrial hyperplasia and endometrioid endometrial carcinoma.","In men, obesity raises estrogen and causes gynecomastia, and the extra estrogen lowers LH and testosterone."]},{"h":"Glycoprotein hormones","points":["hCG, LH, FSH, and TSH share an identical alpha subunit; the beta subunit gives each its specific action.","Pregnancy tests and tumor markers measure beta-hCG for that reason.","hCG beta closely resembles LH beta, so hCG acts on LH receptors and keeps the corpus luteum alive.","Very high hCG in a hydatidiform mole or choriocarcinoma weakly stimulates TSH receptors, giving hyperthyroidism with low TSH.","Very high hCG also overstimulates the ovaries and causes theca lutein cysts."]},{"h":"Liver clearance","points":["The liver inactivates sex steroids, then conjugates them to glucuronides and sulfates for excretion in urine and bile.","Cirrhosis slows estrogen clearance and diverts adrenal androgens around the liver to fat, where they are aromatized.","Cirrhosis also raises SHBG, which binds testosterone more tightly than estradiol, so free estrogen rises relative to free testosterone.","Result: gynecomastia, spider angiomas, palmar erythema, testicular atrophy, and loss of male hair pattern."]},{"h":"Semen chemistry","points":["Seminal vesicles make most of the volume: fructose (sperm fuel), prostaglandins, and semenogelin that forms the coagulum.","Prostate adds a thin fluid with PSA, citrate, zinc, and acid phosphatase.","PSA is a serine protease (kallikrein) that liquefies the coagulum within about 15 to 30 minutes.","PSA marks prostate tissue of any kind: it rises in BPH (more tissue), prostatitis (leaky inflamed epithelium), and carcinoma.","Absent fructose with low volume points to absent vas deferens or seminal vesicles, or ejaculatory duct obstruction."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Steroid enzymes and what loss of each does","cols":["Enzyme","Reaction","Effect of deficiency"],"rows":[["StAR","Cholesterol into inner mitochondria","Almost no steroids made (lipoid adrenal hyperplasia)"],["Side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1)","Cholesterol to pregnenolone","Almost no steroids made"],["3-beta-HSD","Pregnenolone to progesterone; DHEA to androstenedione","Low cortisol, aldosterone, and potent sex steroids"],["17-alpha-hydroxylase / 17,20-lyase (CYP17)","Pregnenolone to DHEA","No sex steroids or cortisol; excess mineralocorticoid with hypertension and low potassium; 46,XY undervirilized"],["17-beta-HSD","Androstenedione to testosterone","Undervirilized 46,XY"],["Aromatase","Androgens to estrogens","Maternal and fetal virilization; tall stature from open epiphyses"],["5-alpha-reductase","Testosterone to DHT","Ambiguous genitalia at birth, virilization at puberty"]]},{"title":"Semen sources","cols":["Gland","Share of volume","Key products"],"rows":[["Seminal vesicles","About 60 to 70%","Fructose, prostaglandins, semenogelin"],["Prostate","About 20 to 30%","PSA, citrate, zinc, acid phosphatase"],["Testes and epididymis","About 5%","Sperm"],["Bulbourethral glands","Small","Mucus that lubricates the urethra"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Read the enzyme name","text":"Each enzyme name tells you where it acts on the steroid ring: 17-alpha-hydroxylase adds an OH at carbon 17, 17,20-lyase cuts between carbons 17 and 20, and 17-beta-HSD changes the group at carbon 17."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Shared alpha, special beta","text":"Four glycoprotein hormones share one alpha chain. When hCG is huge, it starts acting like its relatives LH and TSH."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Virilized at puberty","text":"In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, a child raised as a girl virilizes at puberty because the large pubertal rise in testosterone (helped by some type 1 5-alpha-reductase in skin) is enough to drive most of the change."}],"highYield":["StAR-mediated cholesterol transfer into mitochondria is the rate-limiting step in steroidogenesis.","Aromatase converts testosterone to estradiol and androstenedione to estrone.","5-alpha-reductase deficiency: 46,XY, ambiguous genitalia at birth, virilization at puberty, high testosterone to DHT ratio.","Obesity and anovulation give unopposed estrogen and endometrial hyperplasia.","Molar pregnancy with very high hCG can cause hyperthyroidism through the TSH receptor.","Cirrhosis causes hyperestrogenism: gynecomastia, spider angiomas, palmar erythema, testicular atrophy.","PSA liquefies semen and rises in BPH and prostatitis as well as cancer; fructose comes from the seminal vesicles."],"pitfalls":["5-alpha-reductase deficiency keeps normal Wolffian structures; androgen insensitivity has absent Wolffian structures and female external genitalia with breast development.","After menopause, the main estrogen is estrone made in fat; ovarian estradiol output has largely stopped.","A high PSA alone does not mean cancer; prostatitis and BPH raise it too.","The alpha subunit is shared; lab tests for pregnancy and trophoblastic tumors use beta-hCG."],"dumps":[{"t":"Steroid pathway map","p":"Starting from cholesterol, draw the pathway to progesterone, DHEA, androstenedione, testosterone, DHT, estrone, and estradiol. Label every enzyme on its arrow.","l":["StAR delivers cholesterol into mitochondria (rate-limiting)","Side-chain cleavage makes pregnenolone","3-beta-HSD makes progesterone and androstenedione","17-alpha-hydroxylase and 17,20-lyase lead to DHEA","17-beta-HSD makes testosterone (and estradiol from estrone)","Aromatase makes estradiol from testosterone and estrone from androstenedione","5-alpha-reductase makes DHT from testosterone"]},{"t":"Why cirrhosis feminizes","p":"A man with alcoholic cirrhosis has gynecomastia and small testes. Make a flowchart from liver failure to each of his findings.","l":["Less hepatic inactivation and conjugation of estrogen","Portosystemic shunting sends adrenal androstenedione to fat","More peripheral aromatization to estrone","Higher SHBG binds testosterone more than estradiol","Higher free estrogen to free testosterone ratio","Gynecomastia, spider angiomas, palmar erythema","Estrogen lowers LH, so testicular atrophy follows"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on steroid hormone synthesis"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male (and Function of the Pineal Gland)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract, section on gestational trophoblastic disease"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-7","RE-9","RE-13-C9","RE-13-H2","RE-13-J2","RE-13-F1"]},"RE-9":{"id":"RE-9","summary":"The nutrients and metabolic fuels that reproduction depends on: folate and iron in pregnancy, glucose across the placenta, lactose and antibodies in milk, zinc, vitamin A, energy availability, and the cholesterol supply for steroid hormones. Questions ask you to predict what a deficiency or excess does to the mother, fetus, or gonads.","notes":[{"h":"Folate and the neural tube","points":["Tetrahydrofolate carries one-carbon units for DNA building blocks.","5,10-methylene-THF gives a methyl group so thymidylate synthase can turn dUMP into dTMP.","N10-formyl-THF supplies two carbons of the purine ring.","Low folate slows DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing neuroepithelium.","The neural tube closes by about day 28 after conception, often before pregnancy is recognized, so supplements must start before conception.","Standard dose is 0.4 mg daily; higher doses are used after a prior affected pregnancy. Folate antagonists and valproate raise risk."]},{"h":"Iron in pregnancy","points":["Pregnancy needs about 1 g of extra iron: red cell mass expansion, fetus and placenta, and blood loss at delivery.","Plasma volume rises more (about 40 to 50%) than red cell mass (about 20 to 30%), so hemoglobin falls by dilution.","Dilutional anemia: normal MCV and normal ferritin.","Iron deficiency: low ferritin (below about 30 ng/mL), then low MCV and high RDW.","TIBC rises in normal pregnancy because estrogen raises transferrin, so TIBC alone cannot diagnose iron deficiency here."]},{"h":"Milk","points":["Lactose synthase is galactosyltransferase plus alpha-lactalbumin; alpha-lactalbumin switches the acceptor to glucose.","UDP-galactose plus glucose makes lactose, the main osmole that draws water into milk and sets milk volume.","Human milk has more lactose and less total protein than cow milk, with whey proteins making up most of the protein.","It carries secretory IgA, lactoferrin, and lysozyme for gut defense.","It is low in vitamin D (breastfed infants get supplements) and vitamin K (all newborns get vitamin K at birth)."]},{"h":"Glucose across the placenta","points":["Glucose crosses by facilitated diffusion on GLUT1, down the gradient from mother to fetus.","Maternal insulin does not cross the placenta.","Maternal hyperglycemia means fetal hyperglycemia, so fetal beta cells make more insulin.","Fetal insulin is anabolic: macrosomia with fat over the shoulders and trunk (shoulder dystocia risk).","After the cord is cut, glucose supply stops but insulin stays high, so neonatal hypoglycemia follows.","Fetal hyperinsulinemia also delays surfactant production (respiratory distress)."]},{"h":"Zinc and vitamin A","points":["Zinc is a cofactor for many enzymes and for zinc finger transcription factors, including steroid receptors.","Zinc deficiency: hypogonadism, delayed sexual maturation, growth failure, poor wound healing, loss of taste, and periorificial dermatitis.","Excess preformed vitamin A and retinoids such as isotretinoin act on retinoic acid receptors and disrupt neural crest and Hox patterning.","Retinoid embryopathy: craniofacial defects (small or absent ears, cleft palate), conotruncal heart defects, and CNS defects."]},{"h":"Energy availability and leptin","points":["Leptin from fat signals to the hypothalamus that energy stores are adequate.","Low energy availability (athletes, eating disorders, very low body fat) lowers leptin and GnRH pulsatility.","LH, FSH, and estradiol fall, causing hypothalamic amenorrhea.","Low estrogen speeds bone resorption, so bone density falls and stress fractures rise."]},{"h":"Cholesterol supply for steroids","points":["Gonads and placenta get most cholesterol from circulating LDL by LDL receptor-mediated endocytosis.","LH raises LDL receptor and StAR activity in Leydig and theca cells.","StAR moving cholesterol to the inner mitochondrial membrane is the rate-limiting step in gonadal steroidogenesis.","The placenta makes progesterone from maternal LDL cholesterol but lacks 17-alpha-hydroxylase, so it needs fetal adrenal DHEA-S (16-hydroxylated in fetal liver) to make estriol."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Anemia in pregnancy","cols":["Finding","Dilutional (physiologic)","Iron deficiency"],"rows":[["Hemoglobin","Mildly low","Low"],["MCV","Normal","Low"],["Ferritin","Normal","Low"],["RDW","Normal","High"],["Cause","Plasma volume rises more than red cell mass","Iron demand exceeds intake and stores"]]},{"title":"Nutrient problems in reproduction","cols":["Nutrient","Problem","Main consequence"],"rows":[["Folate","Deficiency before day 28","Neural tube defects"],["Iron","Deficiency","Microcytic anemia, low ferritin"],["Glucose","Maternal hyperglycemia","Fetal hyperinsulinemia, macrosomia, neonatal hypoglycemia"],["Zinc","Deficiency","Hypogonadism, delayed puberty"],["Vitamin A","Excess or retinoid drugs","Craniofacial, heart, and CNS defects"],["Energy (leptin)","Low availability","Hypothalamic amenorrhea, low bone density"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Sugar crosses, insulin stays","text":"Glucose goes through the placenta, insulin does not. So the fetus answers the mother's sugar with its own insulin, grows big, and crashes after birth."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Ferritin decides","text":"In a pregnant patient with low hemoglobin, look at ferritin and MCV. Normal means dilution; low means iron deficiency."},{"kind":"trick","label":"No fuel, no pulse","text":"When the brain senses low fuel (low leptin), it slows GnRH pulses and periods stop."}],"highYield":["Folate is needed for thymidylate and purine synthesis; neural tube closes by about day 28.","Physiologic anemia of pregnancy has normal MCV and ferritin; iron deficiency has low ferritin.","Lactose synthase = galactosyltransferase plus alpha-lactalbumin.","Breast milk is low in vitamin D and vitamin K.","Maternal hyperglycemia leads to fetal hyperinsulinemia, macrosomia, and neonatal hypoglycemia.","Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen; zinc deficiency causes hypogonadism.","Low leptin from low energy availability suppresses GnRH and causes amenorrhea with bone loss.","StAR is the rate-limiting step; gonads use LDL cholesterol."],"pitfalls":["Insulin does not cross the placenta; macrosomia comes from fetal insulin.","A raised TIBC is normal in pregnancy; use ferritin to diagnose iron deficiency.","Beta-carotene is not teratogenic; preformed vitamin A in large doses and retinoid drugs are.","HMG-CoA reductase makes cholesterol, but the gonads rely mainly on LDL uptake; the rate-limiting step for steroids is StAR."],"dumps":[{"t":"Diabetic pregnancy flowchart","p":"Make a flowchart from maternal hyperglycemia to every fetal and newborn consequence. Show what crosses the placenta and what does not.","l":["Glucose crosses by facilitated diffusion (GLUT1)","Maternal insulin does not cross","Fetal hyperglycemia stimulates fetal beta cells","Fetal hyperinsulinemia drives macrosomia","Shoulder dystocia risk from shoulder and trunk fat","Delayed surfactant production","Neonatal hypoglycemia after the cord is clamped"]},{"t":"Pregnancy anemia comparison","p":"Build a table comparing physiologic anemia of pregnancy with iron deficiency anemia. Include the cause and the hemoglobin, MCV, ferritin, RDW, and TIBC findings.","l":["Plasma volume rises about 40 to 50%, red cell mass about 20 to 30%","Dilutional: normal MCV","Dilutional: normal ferritin","Iron deficiency: low ferritin first","Iron deficiency: low MCV and high RDW","TIBC rises in normal pregnancy, so it does not separate the two","Total iron need is about 1 g"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on folic acid and one-carbon metabolism"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pregnancy and Lactation"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on disorders of pregnancy (gestational diabetes)"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-6","RE-8","RE-1","RE-13-A1","RE-13-A"]},"RE-10":{"id":"RE-10","summary":"How chromosome errors and gene mutations cause reproductive disease: sex chromosome aneuploidy, X inactivation, HPV oncoproteins, and the inherited breast, ovarian, and endometrial cancer syndromes. The exam asks you to connect a karyotype or mutation to its mechanism and phenotype.","notes":[{"h":"Nondisjunction","points":["Meiosis I nondisjunction: homologous chromosomes fail to separate.","Meiosis II nondisjunction: sister chromatids fail to separate.","47,XXY (Klinefelter): extra X from either parent; about half of cases come from the father (an XY sperm).","45,X (Turner): usually the lost sex chromosome is paternal, and risk does not rise with maternal age.","Nondisjunction or chromosome loss after fertilization gives mosaicism (for example 45,X/46,XX) with milder features.","45,X/46,XY mosaics carry gonadoblastoma risk in their dysgenetic gonads."]},{"h":"X inactivation","points":["XIST, a long noncoding RNA, coats one X in cis and silences it, forming the Barr body.","Inactivation is random in each cell early in the embryo and then fixed in its descendants.","Barr bodies = number of X chromosomes minus one: 46,XX has 1, 47,XXY has 1, 45,X has 0, 47,XXX has 2.","Some genes (including pseudoautosomal SHOX) escape inactivation, which is why 45,X and 47,XXY still have phenotypes."]},{"h":"Sex chromosome syndromes","points":["Klinefelter: tall, small firm testes, azoospermia, gynecomastia, low testosterone with high LH and FSH.","Klinefelter testes show hyalinized seminiferous tubules and low inhibin B; male breast cancer risk rises.","Turner: short stature (SHOX loss), streak ovaries, primary amenorrhea, high FSH and LH, low estrogen.","Turner also: webbed neck and lymphedema (from cystic hygroma), bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation, and horseshoe kidney."]},{"h":"HPV oncoproteins","points":["High-risk HPV (16, 18) DNA often integrates into host DNA, disrupting E2, the gene that normally restrains E6 and E7.","E6 binds p53 and recruits a ubiquitin ligase, so p53 is degraded and damaged cells neither arrest nor die.","E7 binds Rb and releases E2F, which pushes the cell from G1 into S phase.","Loss of both checkpoints lets mutations pile up in the cervical transformation zone; p16 staining rises as a marker."]},{"h":"BRCA and Lynch","points":["BRCA1 and BRCA2 are tumor suppressors that repair double-strand breaks by homologous recombination.","Inheritance is autosomal dominant; a tumor forms after the second allele is lost (two-hit).","BRCA1: breast and high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma; breast tumors are often triple-negative basal-like.","BRCA2: breast, ovarian, male breast, prostate, and pancreatic cancer.","Lynch syndrome: autosomal dominant germline mismatch repair mutations (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) with microsatellite instability.","In women with Lynch, endometrial carcinoma risk is as high as or higher than colorectal risk; ovarian risk also rises."]},{"h":"Breast cancer receptor groups","points":["Luminal A: ER and PR positive, HER2 negative, low proliferation, best prognosis, responds to hormone therapy.","Luminal B: ER positive with higher proliferation, with or without HER2.","HER2-enriched: ERBB2 gene amplification, ER negative, responds to HER2-targeted antibody therapy.","Basal-like: ER, PR, and HER2 negative (triple-negative), high grade, younger patients, linked to BRCA1."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Barr bodies by karyotype","cols":["Karyotype","Syndrome","Barr bodies"],"rows":[["46,XY","Typical male","0"],["46,XX","Typical female","1"],["47,XXY","Klinefelter","1"],["45,X","Turner","0"],["47,XXX","Triple X","2"]]},{"title":"Inherited reproductive cancer genes","cols":["Gene","Normal job","Inheritance","Main cancers"],"rows":[["BRCA1","Homologous recombination repair","Autosomal dominant","Breast (triple-negative), ovarian"],["BRCA2","Homologous recombination repair","Autosomal dominant","Breast (female and male), ovarian, prostate, pancreatic"],["MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 (Lynch)","DNA mismatch repair","Autosomal dominant","Colorectal, endometrial, ovarian"],["TP53 (target of HPV E6)","G1 arrest and apoptosis","Not inherited here; E6 causes p53 protein degradation","Cervical carcinoma (with E7 action on Rb)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Count Xs, subtract one","text":"Barr bodies always equal the number of X chromosomes minus one, no matter how many Y chromosomes there are."},{"kind":"trick","label":"E6 for p53, E7 for Rb","text":"Pair them in order: E6 with p53 and E7 with Rb. The smaller E number goes with the protein that comes first in the alphabet (p before R)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"BRCA2 reaches men","text":"Male breast cancer and prostate cancer in a family point to BRCA2 more than BRCA1."}],"highYield":["Postzygotic nondisjunction gives mosaicism with a milder phenotype.","XIST silences one X; Barr bodies = X count minus one.","HPV E6 degrades p53; E7 inactivates Rb and frees E2F.","BRCA1 and BRCA2 repair double-strand breaks by homologous recombination; both are autosomal dominant.","BRCA1 tumors are often triple-negative basal-like; BRCA2 adds male breast and prostate cancer.","Lynch syndrome: mismatch repair loss, microsatellite instability, endometrial and colorectal cancer.","HER2-positive breast cancer comes from ERBB2 gene amplification."],"pitfalls":["45,X usually results from loss of the paternal sex chromosome and is not tied to maternal age.","Klinefelter (47,XXY) has a Barr body; Turner (45,X) does not.","Triple-negative means ER, PR, and HER2 negative; it does not respond to hormone or HER2-targeted therapy.","Microsatellite instability with loss of MLH1 can be sporadic (promoter hypermethylation) as well as inherited; germline testing separates them."],"dumps":[{"t":"Nondisjunction diagram","p":"Draw meiosis I and meiosis II for the sex chromosomes in a father. Show how nondisjunction in each division can produce 47,XXY or 45,X offspring.","l":["Meiosis I separates homologs (X from Y)","Meiosis II separates sister chromatids","Meiosis I error in the father gives an XY sperm","XY sperm plus X egg gives 47,XXY","A sperm with no sex chromosome plus an X egg gives 45,X","Error after fertilization gives mosaicism","Mosaics have milder features"]},{"t":"HPV and the cell cycle","p":"Draw the G1 to S checkpoint with p53, p21, cyclin-CDK, Rb, and E2F. Then show where HPV E6 and E7 act.","l":["DNA damage activates p53","p53 induces p21, which inhibits cyclin-CDK","Unphosphorylated Rb holds E2F","Phosphorylated Rb releases E2F to start S phase","E6 targets p53 for ubiquitin-mediated degradation","E7 binds Rb and releases E2F","High-risk types 16 and 18 integrate and overexpress E6 and E7"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases, section on cytogenetic disorders involving sex chromosomes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, section on oncogenic DNA viruses"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Breast, section on carcinoma"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on DNA repair"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-13-C6","RE-13-C11","RE-13-J1","RE-13-J4","RE-13-E6","RE-13-J7"]},"RE-11":{"id":"RE-11","summary":"How the immune system behaves in the reproductive tract: protecting sperm and the fetus from attack, passing antibodies to the newborn, guarding the vagina, and causing damage in Rh disease and tubal infection. The exam asks you to link an immune mechanism to a clinical outcome such as infertility, hydrops, or ectopic pregnancy.","notes":[{"h":"Testis immune privilege","points":["Sperm antigens appear at puberty, long after self-tolerance forms, so they look foreign.","Sertoli cell tight junctions form the blood-testis barrier and hide meiotic and postmeiotic germ cells.","Local immunosuppression adds protection: TGF-beta, Fas ligand, anti-inflammatory macrophages, and regulatory T cells.","Barrier breach (trauma, torsion, vasectomy, mumps orchitis) exposes sperm and leads to antisperm antibodies.","Antisperm antibodies agglutinate sperm and reduce motility, causing infertility.","Autoimmune orchitis (alone or in autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes) destroys the testis, causing primary hypogonadism with high LH and FSH."]},{"h":"Tolerance of the fetus","points":["The fetus carries paternal antigens, so it is a semiallogeneic graft.","Trophoblast in contact with maternal blood lacks classical HLA-A and HLA-B, so maternal cytotoxic T cells have little to recognize.","Extravillous trophoblast expresses nonclassical HLA-G, which binds inhibitory receptors on uterine NK cells and T cells.","HLA-G prevents NK cells from attacking the cells for missing class I (the missing-self signal).","Complement regulators on trophoblast and expanded maternal regulatory T cells add more protection."]},{"h":"Passive immunity of the newborn","points":["Maternal IgG crosses the placenta by FcRn-mediated transcytosis, mostly in the third trimester.","IgM is a large pentamer that does not bind FcRn, so it does not cross.","IgM in cord blood was made by the fetus and signals congenital infection.","Maternal IgG wanes over about 6 months, so preterm infants and infants at 3 to 6 months are most vulnerable.","Breast milk secretory IgA coats the infant gut and blocks pathogen attachment; colostrum has the most."]},{"h":"Rh alloimmunization","points":["An RhD-negative mother is exposed to RhD-positive fetal red cells by fetomaternal hemorrhage, usually at delivery.","Sensitization usually happens at the first delivery and the first response is mostly IgM, so the first baby is usually spared.","In a later RhD-positive pregnancy, memory B cells make anti-D IgG that crosses the placenta.","IgG-coated fetal red cells are destroyed by splenic macrophages (type II hypersensitivity).","Fetal anemia leads to extramedullary hematopoiesis, hepatosplenomegaly, high-output heart failure, and hydrops fetalis.","After birth, unconjugated bilirubin rises fast and can cause kernicterus.","Anti-D immune globulin at about 28 weeks and after delivery or any bleeding event clears fetal cells before the mother is sensitized."]},{"h":"Vaginal defenses","points":["Estrogen loads vaginal epithelium with glycogen, which lactobacilli ferment to lactic acid, keeping the pH at or below 4.5; some also make hydrogen peroxide.","Secretory IgA and antimicrobial peptides add mucosal protection.","When lactobacilli are lost (douching, antibiotics, new partners), Gardnerella and anaerobes overgrow: bacterial vaginosis with pH above 4.5 and fishy amines."]},{"h":"HPV immunity and the vaccine","points":["The vaccine is L1 capsid protein that self-assembles into virus-like particles with no viral DNA.","It induces neutralizing IgG that blocks infection; it does not treat existing infection or lesions.","Most natural HPV infections and low-grade lesions are cleared by cell-mediated immunity within about 2 years.","Low CD4 counts (HIV, transplant immunosuppression) let HPV persist, so CIN and cervical cancer rise."]},{"h":"Tubal scarring after infection","points":["Chlamydia and gonorrhea ascend from the cervix to the tubes (salpingitis, PID).","Chlamydia is often silent, so infections go untreated and recur; repeat exposure drives stronger T cell-mediated inflammation.","Gonococcal lipooligosaccharide and peptidoglycan trigger TNF release that kills ciliated cells.","Healing causes fibrosis, loss of cilia, adhesions, and blocked tubes (hydrosalpinx).","Partial damage traps the embryo, causing ectopic pregnancy; complete blockage causes tubal factor infertility."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Immune protection of the fetus and newborn","cols":["Mechanism","What it does","Clinical link"],"rows":[["Trophoblast lacks HLA-A and HLA-B","Avoids maternal cytotoxic T cells","Fetus survives as a semiallogeneic graft"],["Trophoblast HLA-G","Inhibits uterine NK cells and T cells","Prevents missing-self NK attack"],["FcRn transfer of IgG","Passive systemic immunity","Also carries harmful IgG (anti-D, maternal autoantibodies)"],["IgM excluded from placenta","Fetal IgM is the fetus's own","Cord IgM suggests congenital infection"],["Secretory IgA in milk","Mucosal protection of infant gut","Fewer infant GI and respiratory infections"]]},{"title":"Immune damage in the reproductive tract","cols":["Condition","Trigger","Mechanism","Outcome"],"rows":[["Antisperm antibodies","Trauma, vasectomy, mumps orchitis","Breached blood-testis barrier","Sperm agglutination, infertility"],["Autoimmune orchitis","Autoimmunity, alone or with polyendocrine syndrome","Lymphocytic destruction of testis","Primary hypogonadism"],["Rh disease","RhD-negative mother sensitized earlier","Anti-D IgG, type II hypersensitivity","Fetal hemolysis, hydrops"],["Tubal scarring","Repeated chlamydial or gonococcal salpingitis","Inflammation then fibrosis","Ectopic pregnancy, infertility"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"G for Gestation","text":"HLA-G is the gestation HLA: it sits on trophoblast and tells maternal NK cells to stand down."},{"kind":"trick","label":"IgG goes, IgM stays","text":"G for gets across. IgM is too big to cross, so any IgM found in the newborn was made by the newborn."},{"kind":"trick","label":"First baby fine, second baby sick","text":"Rh disease needs prior sensitization and a class switch to IgG, so problems show up in a later Rh-positive pregnancy."}],"highYield":["The blood-testis barrier is formed by Sertoli cell tight junctions; breach leads to antisperm antibodies.","Trophoblast expresses HLA-G and lacks HLA-A and HLA-B.","IgG crosses the placenta by FcRn; IgM does not; secretory IgA comes in breast milk.","Rh disease is type II hypersensitivity by maternal anti-D IgG; anti-D immune globulin prevents sensitization.","Lactobacilli keep vaginal pH at or below 4.5; their loss allows bacterial vaginosis.","HPV vaccine is L1 virus-like particles that induce neutralizing antibodies.","HIV raises cervical cancer risk because T cell clearance of HPV fails.","Repeated chlamydial and gonococcal salpingitis scar the tubes, causing ectopic pregnancy and infertility."],"pitfalls":["Rh disease is type II hypersensitivity (IgG against a red cell surface antigen); there are no immune complexes.","The HPV vaccine prevents infection; it does not clear an established lesion.","ABO incompatibility can affect a first pregnancy and is usually milder than Rh disease.","Mumps orchitis is usually unilateral, so complete sterility is uncommon."],"dumps":[{"t":"Rh disease timeline","p":"Draw two pregnancies in an RhD-negative mother with RhD-positive babies. Show sensitization in the first, the attack in the second, and where anti-D immune globulin would prevent it.","l":["Fetomaternal hemorrhage at first delivery","Primary response is mainly IgM, which does not cross","Memory B cells form","Second pregnancy: anti-D IgG crosses by FcRn","Type II hypersensitivity: splenic destruction of fetal red cells","Fetal anemia leads to hydrops fetalis","Anti-D immune globulin at about 28 weeks and after delivery"]},{"t":"From chlamydia to ectopic pregnancy","p":"Make a flowchart from a silent chlamydial cervicitis to an ectopic pregnancy years later. Name the immune steps.","l":["Asymptomatic infection goes untreated","Ascent to the fallopian tube (salpingitis)","Repeated infection drives stronger T cell-mediated inflammation","Loss of ciliated cells","Fibrosis and adhesions narrow the tube","Embryo transport is slowed and implants in the tube","Complete blockage instead causes tubal factor infertility"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on hypersensitivity (antibody-mediated diseases)"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on immunologic tolerance"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract, section on pelvic inflammatory disease"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-12","RE-13-B5","RE-13-B6","RE-13-B7","RE-13-K2","RE-13-K6"]},"RE-12":{"id":"RE-12","summary":"The organisms behind sexually transmitted and pregnancy-related infections: their structure, how they grow, how they spread, and the lab features that identify them. The exam gives a smear, culture, or virulence factor and asks you to name the organism or explain how it causes disease.","notes":[{"h":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae","points":["Gram-negative diplococcus, oxidase positive, ferments glucose but not maltose (meningococcus ferments both).","Grown on Thayer-Martin agar, a selective chocolate agar with antibiotics that suppress normal flora.","Pili attach to epithelium and undergo antigenic variation, so reinfection is common and no vaccine exists.","IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA; lipooligosaccharide acts as endotoxin; there is no polysaccharide capsule.","Men show intracellular diplococci in neutrophils on Gram stain of urethral discharge; nucleic acid amplification is the usual test."]},{"h":"Chlamydia trachomatis","points":["Obligate intracellular bacterium that depends on host cell ATP.","Elementary body: small, infectious, enters cells by endocytosis.","Reticulate body: replicates by binary fission inside an inclusion, then turns back into elementary bodies that are released.","Cell wall has little peptidoglycan, so beta-lactams do not work well.","Serovars A to C: trachoma; D to K: urethritis, cervicitis, PID, neonatal conjunctivitis and pneumonia; L1 to L3: lymphogranuloma venereum."]},{"h":"Treponema pallidum","points":["Thin, tightly coiled spirochete with periplasmic flagella; too thin to see on Gram stain.","Seen by dark-field microscopy of chancre fluid; cannot be cultured on standard media.","Spread by sexual contact with lesions and across the placenta.","Diagnosis is mainly serologic: nontreponemal tests (RPR, VDRL) track activity; treponemal tests usually stay positive for life."]},{"h":"HSV-2 and HPV","points":["HSV-2: enveloped linear dsDNA herpesvirus; painful vesicles that ulcerate.","HSV-2 travels up sensory nerves and stays latent in sacral sensory ganglia, then reactivates as recurrent outbreaks.","HPV: nonenveloped circular dsDNA papillomavirus that infects basal keratinocytes through small breaks in the epithelium.","HPV-infected cells show koilocytes (perinuclear halo, wrinkled nucleus).","Low-risk types 6 and 11: genital warts (condyloma acuminatum). High-risk types 16 and 18: CIN and squamous carcinoma."]},{"h":"Vaginitis on the slide","points":["Trichomonas vaginalis: motile, pear-shaped flagellated protozoan on saline wet mount; frothy discharge, pH above 4.5.","Candida albicans: budding yeast with pseudohyphae on KOH prep; thick white discharge, pH normal.","Gardnerella vaginalis: clue cells (epithelial cells coated with bacteria) on wet mount; fishy odor with KOH, pH above 4.5, few white cells."]},{"h":"Chancroid and toxic shock","points":["Haemophilus ducreyi: small gram-negative coccobacillus that grows in chains on Gram stain ('school of fish').","Chancroid ulcers are painful with a ragged purulent base and painful suppurative inguinal nodes.","Staphylococcus aureus TSST-1 is a superantigen that links MHC class II to the T cell receptor beta chain outside the antigen groove.","This activates a huge fraction of T cells at once, causing a cytokine storm (IL-2, IFN-gamma, TNF).","Toxic shock: fever, hypotension, diffuse rash with later desquamation, and multiorgan injury, classically with retained tampons or nasal packing."]},{"h":"Group B strep and TORCH","points":["Streptococcus agalactiae: gram-positive cocci in chains, beta-hemolytic, bacitracin resistant, CAMP positive.","Colonizes the vagina and rectum of many pregnant women; screened by culture at 36 to 37 weeks.","Causes neonatal sepsis, pneumonia, and meningitis acquired during delivery.","TORCH: Toxoplasma, Other (syphilis, varicella-zoster, parvovirus B19, Listeria, HIV, Zika), Rubella, CMV, HSV.","Most TORCH organisms cross the placenta; HSV is mainly acquired during delivery."]}],"charts":[{"title":"STI organisms","cols":["Organism","Structure","Lab identification","Key virulence or feature"],"rows":[["Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Gram-negative diplococcus","Oxidase positive, glucose only, Thayer-Martin","Pili with antigenic variation, IgA protease, LOS"],["Chlamydia trachomatis","Obligate intracellular bacterium","Nucleic acid amplification; inclusions in cells","Elementary and reticulate bodies"],["Treponema pallidum","Thin spirochete","Dark-field microscopy, serology; no routine culture","Crosses placenta"],["Haemophilus ducreyi","Gram-negative coccobacillus","'School of fish' chains on Gram stain","Painful ulcer and nodes"],["HSV-2","Enveloped dsDNA virus","PCR of vesicle fluid","Latency in sacral sensory ganglia"],["HPV","Nonenveloped dsDNA virus","Koilocytes; HPV DNA testing","E6 and E7 in high-risk types"],["Trichomonas vaginalis","Flagellated protozoan","Motile trophozoites on wet mount","No cyst form; sexual spread"]]},{"title":"Vaginitis comparison","cols":["Feature","Bacterial vaginosis","Candida","Trichomonas"],"rows":[["Discharge","Thin gray, fishy","Thick white, curdlike","Frothy yellow-green"],["pH","Above 4.5","4.5 or below","Above 4.5"],["Microscopy","Clue cells, few white cells","Pseudohyphae and yeast on KOH","Motile flagellated organisms"],["Inflammation","Little","Itching, redness","Marked; punctate cervical hemorrhages"]]},{"title":"TORCH organisms","cols":["Organism","Main route to baby","Classic newborn findings"],"rows":[["Toxoplasma gondii","Transplacental","Chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, scattered intracranial calcifications"],["Treponema pallidum","Transplacental","Rhinorrhea, rash on palms and soles, later notched teeth and saddle nose"],["Rubella","Transplacental","Cataracts, sensorineural deafness, PDA"],["CMV","Transplacental","Sensorineural hearing loss, periventricular calcifications, microcephaly"],["HSV-2","Mostly during delivery","Skin, eye, and mouth vesicles; encephalitis; disseminated disease"],["Parvovirus B19","Transplacental","Fetal anemia and hydrops"],["Streptococcus agalactiae (not TORCH)","During delivery","Sepsis, pneumonia, meningitis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ABC, D to K, L","text":"Chlamydia serovars: A, B, C = Africa, Blindness, Chronic infection (trachoma). D to K = everything else genital and neonatal. L1 to L3 = Lymphogranuloma venereum."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"You do cry with ducreyi","text":"Chancroid from Haemophilus ducreyi is painful; the syphilis chancre is painless."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"MeninGococci ferment Maltose and Glucose","text":"Meningococcus ferments maltose and glucose; Gonococcus ferments glucose only."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"B for Bacitracin resistant, B for Babies","text":"Group B strep is bacitracin resistant and causes disease in babies."}],"highYield":["Gonococcus: oxidase positive, glucose only, Thayer-Martin, pilus antigenic variation means no vaccine.","Chlamydia: elementary body infects, reticulate body replicates; serovars A to C, D to K, L1 to L3.","Treponema pallidum: dark-field microscopy, no routine culture, crosses the placenta.","HSV-2 is enveloped and latent in sacral ganglia; HPV is nonenveloped; types 16 and 18 are high risk.","Clue cells mean Gardnerella; pseudohyphae on KOH mean Candida; motile flagellates mean Trichomonas.","TSST-1 is a superantigen linking MHC II and TCR outside the peptide groove.","Group B strep: beta-hemolytic, bacitracin resistant, CAMP positive; top cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis."],"pitfalls":["Group A strep is bacitracin sensitive; group B is bacitracin resistant.","Candida vaginitis has a normal pH; bacterial vaginosis and trichomoniasis raise it above 4.5.","Syphilis chancre is painless and indurated; chancroid is painful and soft.","Toxoplasma calcifications are scattered through the brain; CMV calcifications are periventricular.","Superantigens skip antigen processing; they do not need to fit in the MHC peptide groove."],"dumps":[{"t":"Chlamydia life cycle","p":"Draw the chlamydial life cycle in a host cell, from entry of the infectious form to release of new infectious particles. Label both forms.","l":["Elementary body attaches and enters by endocytosis","Elementary body is small, dense, and infectious","Inside an inclusion, it becomes a reticulate body","Reticulate body divides by binary fission","Reticulate bodies turn back into elementary bodies","Cell ruptures and releases elementary bodies","Obligate intracellular because it depends on host ATP"]},{"t":"Genital ulcer comparison table","p":"Build a comparison table of syphilis, chancroid, genital herpes, and lymphogranuloma venereum. Include organism, structure, pain, ulcer appearance, lymph nodes, and lab test.","l":["Syphilis: Treponema pallidum spirochete, painless indurated chancre, dark-field or serology","Chancroid: Haemophilus ducreyi coccobacillus, painful ragged ulcer","Chancroid nodes are painful and can suppurate","Herpes: HSV-2, painful grouped vesicles that ulcerate, PCR","Herpes recurs from latency in sacral ganglia","LGV: Chlamydia L1 to L3, small painless ulcer then painful inguinal nodes","Syphilis nodes are painless and rubbery"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on Neisseriae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on Chlamydiae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on spirochetes"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on staphylococci (toxic shock syndrome toxin)"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-11","RE-13-K","RE-13-K1","RE-13-K3","RE-13-K4","RE-13-K8","RE-13-K9","RE-13-K10"]},"RE-13-A":{"id":"RE-13-A","summary":"This unit covers the menstrual problems that come from hormones: amenorrhea, anovulation, abnormal bleeding without ovulation, menopause, and early ovarian failure. Exam questions give you FSH, LH, prolactin, TSH, and estradiol and ask you to find where the problem sits, or ask what unopposed estrogen and estrogen loss do to the body.","notes":[{"h":"Primary vs secondary amenorrhea","points":["Primary: no menarche by age 15, or by age 13 with no breast development.","Secondary: no menses for 3 months in someone who had regular cycles (6 months if cycles were irregular).","Pregnancy is the most common cause of secondary amenorrhea, so check hCG first.","Breast development shows estrogen has been present; pubic and axillary hair show androgen action."]},{"h":"Find the level with hormones","points":["Hypothalamus: low or normal FSH and LH, low estradiol (low energy availability, heavy exercise, stress, Kallmann syndrome).","Pituitary: low FSH and LH (Sheehan syndrome, pituitary mass); high prolactin suppresses GnRH pulses.","Hypothyroidism raises TRH, which raises prolactin, so check TSH with prolactin.","Ovary: high FSH (and LH) with low estradiol, because estradiol and inhibin no longer give negative feedback.","Outflow tract: normal FSH, LH, and estradiol; no bleed after an estrogen plus progestin challenge.","Asherman syndrome: intrauterine adhesions after dilation and curettage or infection destroy the basal endometrium."]},{"h":"Anovulation and abnormal uterine bleeding","points":["No LH surge or poor follicle development means no corpus luteum and no progesterone.","Causes: PCOS, hyperprolactinemia, thyroid disease, low energy availability; normal near menarche and in perimenopause.","Unopposed estrogen keeps the endometrium proliferating with no secretory change.","The thick, unstable lining outgrows its support and sheds at random: irregular, often heavy bleeding.","Long-term unopposed estrogen causes endometrial hyperplasia and raises endometrial carcinoma risk.","Dysfunctional uterine bleeding is now called AUB-O (ovulatory dysfunction) in the PALM-COEIN system.","Rule out structural causes first: polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy or hyperplasia."]},{"h":"Menopause and primary ovarian insufficiency","points":["Menopause: follicles run out, average age about 51; diagnosed after 12 months without menses.","Loss of estradiol and inhibin removes feedback, so FSH rises most and LH rises too.","Remaining estrogen is mostly estrone made by aromatase in adipose tissue.","Estrogen loss: hot flashes, vaginal and urethral atrophy, bone loss (more osteoclast activity), worse lipid profile and higher cardiovascular risk.","Perimenopause: falling inhibin B lets FSH rise first; cycles become irregular and often anovulatory.","Primary ovarian insufficiency: ovarian failure before age 40 with high FSH and low estradiol.","POI causes: Turner syndrome, FMR1 premutation, autoimmune oophoritis, chemotherapy (alkylating agents), pelvic radiation."]},{"h":"Androgen excess, pain, and infertility","points":["Hirsutism: male-pattern terminal hair only. Virilization adds clitoromegaly, deep voice, temporal balding, and more muscle.","Rapid virilization suggests an androgen-secreting tumor.","Very high testosterone with normal DHEA-S points to the ovary (Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor; PCOS gives milder rises).","Very high DHEA-S points to the adrenal, since DHEA-S comes almost only from the adrenal (adrenal tumor; CAH raises 17-hydroxyprogesterone).","Primary dysmenorrhea: endometrial prostaglandins (PGF2-alpha) at menses cause strong myometrial contractions and ischemia.","Mittelschmerz: one-sided midcycle pain when the follicle ruptures at ovulation.","Infertility types: ovulatory (PCOS, hyperprolactinemia, POI), tubal (scarring after PID), uterine (Asherman syndrome, submucosal leiomyoma)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Localizing amenorrhea with hormone levels","cols":["Level","FSH and LH","Estradiol","Other clue","Examples"],"rows":[["Hypothalamus","Low or normal","Low","Low weight, exercise, stress; anosmia in Kallmann","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, Kallmann syndrome"],["Pituitary","Low","Low","High prolactin or a sellar mass","Prolactinoma, Sheehan syndrome"],["Ovary","High","Low","Hot flashes, may have short stature or family history","Turner syndrome, POI, menopause"],["Outflow tract","Normal","Normal","Normal breasts; no bleed after estrogen plus progestin","Asherman syndrome, imperforate hymen, Mullerian agenesis"]]},{"title":"Reproductive hormone conditions","cols":["Condition","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Amenorrhea","Failure at hypothalamus, pituitary, ovary, or outflow tract (or pregnancy)","No menses; pattern of FSH, LH, prolactin, estradiol locates the cause","Low estrogen causes bone loss; depends on cause"],["Anovulation","No LH surge or poor follicle growth, so no corpus luteum or progesterone","Irregular cycles, no luteal rise in basal body temperature, infertility","Endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma from unopposed estrogen"],["AUB-O (dysfunctional uterine bleeding)","Estrogen-driven proliferation without progesterone-induced secretory change","Irregular, unpredictable, often heavy bleeding with no structural lesion","Iron deficiency anemia, endometrial hyperplasia"],["Menopause and perimenopause","Follicle depletion; low estradiol and inhibin; high FSH and LH","Hot flashes, irregular then absent menses, vaginal dryness","Osteoporosis, atrophic vaginitis, higher cardiovascular risk"],["Primary ovarian insufficiency","Ovarian failure before age 40","Amenorrhea with high FSH and low estradiol, menopausal symptoms","Infertility, osteoporosis, cardiovascular risk"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PALM-COEIN","text":"Structural causes of abnormal uterine bleeding: Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma, Malignancy and hyperplasia. Nonstructural: Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, Not yet classified."},{"kind":"trick","label":"FSH follows the ovary","text":"If the ovary has failed, FSH is high. If the brain has failed, FSH is low. If FSH and estradiol are both normal, look at the uterus and vagina."},{"kind":"trick","label":"DHEA-S means adrenal","text":"DHEA-S is made almost only by the adrenal. A very high DHEA-S says adrenal; a very high testosterone with a normal DHEA-S says ovary."}],"highYield":["Secondary amenorrhea: rule out pregnancy first.","High FSH with low estradiol means the ovary is the problem (menopause, POI, Turner syndrome).","Amenorrhea with galactorrhea: check prolactin and TSH.","Amenorrhea after a dilation and curettage with normal hormones: Asherman syndrome.","Anovulation leads to unopposed estrogen, endometrial hyperplasia, and higher endometrial cancer risk.","FSH is the best single lab marker of menopause.","POI is ovarian failure before 40; think Turner syndrome, FMR1 premutation, autoimmunity, chemotherapy, radiation.","Virilization with very high DHEA-S suggests an adrenal tumor; with very high testosterone and normal DHEA-S, an ovarian tumor."],"pitfalls":["Primary amenorrhea is defined by age 15, or age 13 without breast development; do not wait for 15 when there are no breasts.","Low FSH and LH can come from the hypothalamus or the pituitary; prolactin, imaging, and history separate them.","AUB-O is a diagnosis after structural causes (PALM) are ruled out.","Hirsutism alone is usually PCOS or idiopathic; virilization means much higher androgen levels and needs a search for a tumor.","Primary dysmenorrhea has a normal pelvis; pain from endometriosis or adenomyosis is secondary dysmenorrhea."],"dumps":[{"t":"Amenorrhea workup table","p":"Make a table with four rows (hypothalamus, pituitary, ovary, outflow tract). For each, write FSH and LH, estradiol, prolactin if relevant, and two example causes.","l":["Pregnancy test comes first for secondary amenorrhea","Hypothalamus: low or normal FSH and LH, low estradiol","Pituitary: low FSH and LH; prolactinoma raises prolactin and suppresses GnRH","Hypothyroidism can raise prolactin through TRH","Ovary: high FSH and LH with low estradiol","Outflow tract: normal hormones","Asherman syndrome is an outflow cause after dilation and curettage","Examples placed correctly (Kallmann, Sheehan, Turner, POI)"]},{"t":"Ovulatory cycle vs anovulatory cycle","p":"Draw two menstrual cycles side by side, one ovulatory and one anovulatory. Show estradiol, LH, and progesterone, and what happens to the endometrium in each.","l":["Ovulatory cycle has an LH surge at midcycle","Corpus luteum makes progesterone in the luteal phase","Progesterone turns proliferative endometrium secretory","Falling progesterone causes orderly withdrawal bleeding","Anovulatory cycle has no LH surge and no progesterone","Unopposed estrogen keeps the endometrium proliferating","Lining sheds irregularly and heavily","Long-term risk is endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Female Genital Tract, section on the endometrium and abnormal uterine bleeding"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on disorders of the female reproductive tract (amenorrhea and menopause)"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-7","RE-6","RE-13-G","RE-13-C11"]},"RE-13-B":{"id":"RE-13-B","summary":"This unit covers inflammation of the male and female reproductive tracts, from local infections like balanitis and candidal vaginitis to ascending pelvic infection and its scars. It also covers endometriosis and adenomyosis, where misplaced endometrium causes cyclic pain and infertility. Exam questions ask for the organism, the tissue finding, or the complication.","notes":[{"h":"Balanitis and orchitis","points":["Balanitis: inflammation of the glans, most often from Candida or bacteria.","Risk: uncircumcised foreskin, poor hygiene, diabetes mellitus.","A tight foreskin traps secretions and favors balanitis; repeated balanitis scars the foreskin and causes acquired phimosis.","Mumps orchitis: after puberty, painful swollen testis days after parotitis; can cause testicular atrophy and reduced fertility.","Epididymo-orchitis in sexually active young men: C. trachomatis or N. gonorrhoeae.","Epididymo-orchitis in older men: E. coli and other gram-negative rods from urinary infection.","Cremasteric reflex is kept in epididymitis, which helps separate it from torsion."]},{"h":"Cervicitis, PID, and salpingitis","points":["Mucopurulent cervicitis: C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae; friable cervix that bleeds on contact.","Trichomoniasis: punctate hemorrhages (strawberry cervix) with frothy yellow-green discharge.","HSV: ulcerative, necrotic cervicitis, often with painful vulvar vesicles and ulcers.","PID: ascending infection from cervix to endometrium, tubes, and pelvis by C. trachomatis, N. gonorrhoeae, and vaginal flora.","PID findings: cervical motion tenderness, adnexal tenderness, fever, purulent cervical discharge.","Salpingitis complications: hydrosalpinx, tubo-ovarian abscess, tubal infertility, ectopic pregnancy, chronic pelvic pain.","Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome: perihepatitis with right upper quadrant pain and violin-string adhesions from liver capsule to abdominal wall."]},{"h":"Endometritis","points":["Acute: polymicrobial infection after delivery (especially cesarean), miscarriage, or retained products.","Acute findings: fever, uterine tenderness, foul discharge.","Chronic: plasma cells in the endometrial stroma make the diagnosis; normal endometrium has none.","Chronic causes: retained products, intrauterine device, PID; presents with abnormal bleeding, pain, or infertility."]},{"h":"Endometriosis and adenomyosis","points":["Endometriosis: endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus.","Theories: retrograde menstruation, coelomic metaplasia, vascular or lymphatic spread (explains distant sites).","Ovary is the most common site; bleeding forms endometriomas (chocolate cysts) full of old blood.","Cyclic bleeding into tissue causes inflammation, fibrosis, and adhesions.","Symptoms: dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, pain with defecation, infertility; uterus is normal in size.","Adenomyosis: endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium.","Adenomyosis gives a uniformly enlarged, soft, tender uterus with dysmenorrhea and heavy bleeding.","Leiomyoma gives an irregular, firm, nodular uterus from smooth muscle tumors."]},{"h":"Vulvovaginal inflammation","points":["Candidal vaginitis risk: diabetes, antibiotics, pregnancy, immunosuppression.","Findings: thick white curd-like discharge, vulvar itching and redness, normal vaginal pH (4.5 or less).","KOH preparation shows pseudohyphae and budding yeast.","Lichen sclerosus: thin epidermis with dermal fibrosis forming white, parchment-like vulvar plaques, mostly after menopause.","Lichen sclerosus carries a small increase in vulvar squamous cell carcinoma risk."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Inflammatory diseases of the reproductive tract","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Balanitis","Candida, bacteria; uncircumcised, poor hygiene, diabetes","Trapped secretions under the foreskin","Red, sore, itchy glans","Scarring and acquired phimosis"],["Cervicitis","C. trachomatis, N. gonorrhoeae (also Trichomonas, HSV)","Infection of endocervical columnar epithelium","Mucopurulent discharge, friable cervix","Ascent to PID"],["Endometriosis","Retrograde menstruation and other theories","Ectopic endometrium bleeds with each cycle","Cyclic pain, dyspareunia, chocolate cysts","Adhesions, infertility"],["Endometritis","Delivery, miscarriage, retained products, IUD","Polymicrobial ascending infection","Fever, tender uterus; chronic form has plasma cells","Sepsis, infertility"],["Orchitis","Mumps after puberty; C. trachomatis or N. gonorrhoeae (young), E. coli (older)","Viral infection or spread from epididymis","Painful swollen testis","Atrophy, reduced fertility, abscess"],["PID","C. trachomatis, N. gonorrhoeae, vaginal flora","Ascending infection of upper tract","Cervical motion and adnexal tenderness, fever","Tubo-ovarian abscess, infertility, ectopic pregnancy"],["Salpingitis","Same organisms as PID","Tubal inflammation and scarring","Adnexal pain and tenderness","Hydrosalpinx, ectopic pregnancy, Fitz-Hugh-Curtis"],["Candidal vaginitis","Diabetes, antibiotics, pregnancy, immunosuppression","Overgrowth of Candida albicans","Thick white discharge, itching, normal pH","Recurrence when risk persists"]]},{"title":"Endometriosis vs adenomyosis vs leiomyoma","cols":["Feature","Endometriosis","Adenomyosis","Leiomyoma"],"rows":[["Tissue","Endometrium outside the uterus","Endometrium inside the myometrium","Benign smooth muscle tumor"],["Uterus on exam","Normal size, may be fixed by adhesions","Uniformly enlarged, soft, tender","Irregular, firm, nodular"],["Main symptoms","Dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, infertility","Dysmenorrhea, heavy bleeding","Heavy bleeding, pressure; often none"],["Classic lesion","Chocolate cyst of ovary","Glands deep in myometrium","Whorled smooth muscle bundles"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Adeno-MYO-sis is in the MYOmetrium","text":"The name tells you the site: adenomyosis puts endometrial glands in the muscle wall, so the whole uterus swells and hurts."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Chandelier sign","text":"Cervical motion tenderness in PID is so painful the patient seems to reach for the ceiling light. It tells you infection has spread past the cervix."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Plasma cells mean chronic","text":"Normal endometrium has no plasma cells. Seeing them in the stroma is the marker of chronic endometritis."}],"highYield":["Mucopurulent cervicitis: C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae; strawberry cervix: Trichomonas.","Cervical motion tenderness and adnexal tenderness with fever: PID.","PID followed by right upper quadrant pain: Fitz-Hugh-Curtis perihepatitis.","Prior salpingitis raises the risk of ectopic pregnancy and tubal infertility.","Chocolate cyst of the ovary: endometriosis.","Uniformly enlarged, tender uterus with heavy painful periods: adenomyosis.","Curd-like discharge, normal pH, pseudohyphae on KOH: Candida.","White parchment-like vulvar skin in an older woman: lichen sclerosus, with slight vulvar SCC risk."],"pitfalls":["Endometriosis keeps the uterus normal in size; adenomyosis enlarges it evenly; leiomyoma enlarges it irregularly.","Candidal vaginitis has a normal pH; bacterial vaginosis and trichomoniasis raise pH above 4.5.","Epididymitis in a young man is usually an STI; in an older man, think E. coli from the urinary tract.","Lichen sclerosus thins the epidermis; lichen simplex chronicus thickens it and does not raise cancer risk."],"dumps":[{"t":"Path of PID","p":"Draw the female reproductive tract from the vagina to the ovaries. Trace how infection climbs from the cervix to the pelvis and label each complication where it happens.","l":["Cervicitis from C. trachomatis or N. gonorrhoeae starts the process","Spread to endometrium (endometritis)","Spread to fallopian tubes (salpingitis)","Hydrosalpinx or pyosalpinx in the tube","Tubo-ovarian abscess at the tube and ovary","Tubal scarring causes infertility and ectopic pregnancy","Spread along the right paracolic gutter to the liver capsule (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis)","Exam findings: cervical motion and adnexal tenderness, fever"]},{"t":"Endometriosis vs adenomyosis","p":"Draw a uterus with tubes and ovaries. Mark where endometriosis lesions are found and where adenomyosis sits. Beside each, list the theory of cause, exam findings, and symptoms.","l":["Endometriosis: endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus","Common sites: ovary, uterosacral ligaments, pelvic peritoneum","Theories: retrograde menstruation, coelomic metaplasia, vascular or lymphatic spread","Chocolate cysts and adhesions","Symptoms: dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, infertility","Adenomyosis: glands and stroma within the myometrium","Adenomyosis: uniformly enlarged, tender uterus with heavy bleeding"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Female Genital Tract, sections on pelvic inflammatory disease, endometriosis, and adenomyosis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, sections on the penis and testis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Sections on the neisseriae and chlamydiae"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-12","RE-11","RE-13-K","RE-13-A"]},"RE-13-C":{"id":"RE-13-C","summary":"This unit covers reproductive problems present from birth: chromosome disorders (Turner, Klinefelter, fragile X), disorders of sex development, and malformations of the penis, foreskin, uterus, vagina, and hymen. Exam questions test the embryology behind each defect and the hormone pattern that goes with it.","notes":[{"h":"Turner and Klinefelter","points":["Turner syndrome: 45,X or mosaic; ovarian dysgenesis with streak ovaries.","Turner labs: low estradiol with high FSH and LH; presents as primary amenorrhea with no breast development.","Turner findings: short stature (SHOX loss), webbed neck from fetal cystic hygroma, lymphedema of hands and feet.","Turner organs: bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation of the aorta, horseshoe kidney.","Klinefelter syndrome: 47,XXY from meiotic nondisjunction.","Seminiferous tubule dysgenesis: low inhibin B, high FSH, azoospermia.","Leydig cell dysfunction: low testosterone, high LH; relatively more estrogen causes gynecomastia.","Klinefelter findings: small firm testes, tall stature with long limbs, gynecomastia, infertility."]},{"h":"Fragile X syndrome","points":["X-linked; CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in FMR1.","Full mutation (over 200 repeats) is hypermethylated, which silences FMR1.","Anticipation: repeats expand when passed through the mother.","Findings: intellectual disability, long face, large jaw, large ears, macroorchidism after puberty.","Premutation carriers (about 55 to 200 repeats): women risk primary ovarian insufficiency."]},{"h":"Disorders of sex development","points":["46,XX DSD (old term female pseudohermaphroditism): ovaries with virilized external genitalia.","46,XX DSD causes: 21-hydroxylase deficiency (most common) or excess maternal androgen.","46,XY DSD (old term male pseudohermaphroditism): testes with undervirilized external genitalia.","46,XY DSD causes: androgen insensitivity, 5-alpha-reductase deficiency.","Complete androgen insensitivity: X-linked androgen receptor defect.","CAIS: female external genitalia, blind vaginal pouch, no uterus or tubes (AMH works), undescended testes, sparse pubic hair.","CAIS labs: raised testosterone, estrogen, and LH; breasts develop from aromatized testosterone.","Mullerian agenesis: 46,XX, normal ovaries and pubic hair, absent uterus and upper vagina."]},{"h":"Penis and foreskin","points":["Hypospadias: ventral urethral opening from failed fusion of the urethral folds; linked to cryptorchidism and inguinal hernia.","Epispadias: dorsal opening from abnormal position of the genital tubercle; linked to bladder exstrophy.","Phimosis: foreskin cannot retract over the glans; normal in infancy, or acquired from scarring.","Paraphimosis: retracted foreskin trapped behind the glans, causing edema and ischemia; an emergency."]},{"h":"Testis, uterus, vagina, hymen","points":["Cryptorchidism: undescended testis, most often in the inguinal canal; more common in preterm infants.","Higher temperature impairs spermatogenesis; damaged Sertoli cells make less inhibin B, so FSH rises.","Cryptorchidism raises germ cell tumor risk.","Septate uterus: midline septum fails to resorb after the Mullerian ducts fuse; causes recurrent pregnancy loss.","Longitudinal vaginal septum: abnormal fusion or resorption; transverse septum: failed canalization.","Imperforate hymen: hymen fails to perforate; at menarche gives primary amenorrhea, cyclic pelvic pain, bulging bluish membrane, hematocolpos."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital and genetic reproductive diseases","cols":["Condition","Cause","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Cryptorchidism","Failed testicular descent; prematurity","Higher temperature damages germ cells","Empty scrotum, testis usually inguinal","Infertility, germ cell tumor"],["Epispadias","Abnormal genital tubercle position","Urethra opens on dorsal penis","Dorsal meatus","Bladder exstrophy, incontinence"],["Fragile X syndrome","FMR1 CGG expansion","Hypermethylation silences FMR1","Intellectual disability, long face, large ears and jaw, macroorchidism","Anticipation via mother; premutation causes POI"],["Hypospadias","Failed fusion of urethral folds","Urethra opens on ventral penis","Ventral meatus","Cryptorchidism, inguinal hernia, UTI"],["Imperforate hymen","Hymen fails to perforate","Menstrual blood trapped","Bluish bulging membrane, cyclic pain","Hematocolpos, primary amenorrhea"],["Klinefelter syndrome","47,XXY","Tubule and Leydig cell dysfunction","Small firm testes, tall, gynecomastia","Infertility, male breast cancer"],["Paraphimosis","Foreskin left retracted","Constricting ring blocks venous return","Swollen glans with tight band","Ischemia, necrosis"],["Phimosis","Physiologic in infancy or scarring","Narrow foreskin opening","Cannot retract foreskin","Balanitis, urinary obstruction"],["Pseudohermaphroditism (DSD)","21-hydroxylase deficiency or maternal androgen (46,XX); androgen insensitivity or 5-alpha-reductase deficiency (46,XY)","Gonads and external genitalia do not match","Ambiguous or opposite-appearing genitalia","Salt wasting in CAH; gonadal tumor in undescended testes"],["Septate vagina and uterus","Failed septum resorption or abnormal fusion or canalization","Persistent midline or transverse septum","Recurrent miscarriage, dyspareunia","Obstructed flow with transverse septum"],["Turner syndrome","45,X or mosaic","Ovarian dysgenesis, SHOX loss","Short stature, webbed neck, no breast development","Coarctation, bicuspid aortic valve, horseshoe kidney"]]},{"title":"Primary amenorrhea with a blind vaginal pouch","cols":["Feature","Complete androgen insensitivity","Mullerian agenesis"],"rows":[["Karyotype","46,XY","46,XX"],["Gonads","Testes (undescended)","Normal ovaries"],["Uterus and tubes","Absent (AMH present)","Absent (Mullerian ducts failed)"],["Breasts","Present","Present"],["Pubic and axillary hair","Sparse or absent","Normal"],["Testosterone","Male range or higher","Normal female range"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Hypo is below","text":"Hypospadias opens on the underside (ventral) of the penis. Epispadias opens on top and goes with exstrophy of the bladder (E with E)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Klinefelter labs follow two cell types","text":"Sick Sertoli cells: low inhibin B, high FSH. Sick Leydig cells: low testosterone, high LH. Both gonadotropins rise."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Hair tells the karyotype","text":"Blind vaginal pouch with no pubic hair: androgen insensitivity (46,XY). Blind pouch with normal pubic hair: Mullerian agenesis (46,XX)."}],"highYield":["Turner syndrome is the most common chromosomal cause of primary amenorrhea: streak ovaries, high FSH, low estradiol.","Turner: bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation, horseshoe kidney, webbed neck, lymphedema.","Klinefelter: small firm testes, gynecomastia, high FSH and LH, low testosterone.","Fragile X: CGG repeat, hypermethylation, macroorchidism, anticipation through the mother.","Hypospadias goes with cryptorchidism and inguinal hernia; epispadias goes with bladder exstrophy.","Cryptorchidism raises germ cell tumor risk and lowers fertility.","Primary amenorrhea with cyclic pain and a bluish bulging membrane: imperforate hymen.","Septate uterus is a classic cause of recurrent pregnancy loss."],"pitfalls":["Phimosis is a foreskin that will not go back over the glans; paraphimosis is a foreskin stuck behind it.","In CAIS testosterone is high, so the problem is the receptor; in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency testosterone is normal and DHT is low.","Both CAIS and Mullerian agenesis have breasts and a blind pouch; pubic hair and karyotype separate them.","Imperforate hymen has normal hormones and normal breasts; the block is mechanical."],"dumps":[{"t":"Turner vs Klinefelter","p":"Make a two-column table comparing Turner syndrome and Klinefelter syndrome. Include karyotype, gonad problem, FSH, LH, sex steroid level, body findings, and associated organ problems.","l":["Turner is 45,X or mosaic; Klinefelter is 47,XXY","Turner has streak ovaries; Klinefelter has small firm testes","Both have high FSH and LH","Turner has low estradiol; Klinefelter has low testosterone","Turner: short stature from SHOX loss; Klinefelter: tall with long limbs","Turner: webbed neck, lymphedema, bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation, horseshoe kidney","Klinefelter: gynecomastia and infertility"]},{"t":"Mullerian and genital development","p":"Draw the Mullerian ducts fusing to form the uterus and upper vagina. Mark where a septate uterus, a vaginal septum, and an imperforate hymen come from. Then draw the male urethral folds and genital tubercle and mark hypospadias and epispadias.","l":["Paired Mullerian ducts fuse in the midline","Midline septum normally resorbs; failure gives a septate uterus","Septate uterus causes recurrent pregnancy loss","Transverse vaginal septum comes from failed canalization","Imperforate hymen traps menstrual blood (hematocolpos)","Failed urethral fold fusion gives ventral hypospadias","Abnormal genital tubercle position gives dorsal epispadias","Epispadias goes with bladder exstrophy"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases, sections on cytogenetic disorders and fragile X syndrome"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on trinucleotide repeat expansion"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-1","RE-10","RE-13-A","RE-13-D"]},"RE-13-D":{"id":"RE-13-D","summary":"This unit covers benign problems of the penis and scrotum: erectile dysfunction, scrotal fluid and blood collections, varicocele, torsion, priapism, and Peyronie disease, plus the causes of male infertility. Exam questions turn on a few bedside clues: transillumination, position relative to the testis, the cremasteric reflex, and change with standing.","notes":[{"h":"Erectile dysfunction","points":["Erection needs parasympathetic input (pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2 to S4), nitric oxide, cGMP, and smooth muscle relaxation.","Vascular: atherosclerosis limits arterial inflow; often an early sign of coronary disease.","Neurogenic: diabetes, spinal cord injury, pelvic surgery such as prostatectomy.","Endocrine: hypogonadism, hyperprolactinemia.","Drug-induced (for example some antihypertensives and antidepressants) or psychogenic.","Preserved nocturnal or morning erections mean the nerves and vessels work, which points to a psychogenic cause."]},{"h":"Scrotal masses","points":["Hydrocele: fluid within the tunica vaginalis around the testis; smooth and transilluminates.","Communicating hydrocele: patent processus vaginalis lets peritoneal fluid in; size changes during the day.","Noncommunicating hydrocele: from infection, trauma, or tumor (also idiopathic in adults).","Hematocele: blood in the tunica vaginalis after trauma, torsion, or tumor; does not transilluminate.","Spermatocele: cystic dilation of the epididymis holding sperm; transilluminates and sits separate from the testis, usually above it.","Simple epididymal cyst feels the same but holds clear fluid without sperm.","Varicocele: dilated pampiniform plexus, a bag of worms that grows on standing and does not transilluminate.","Left varicocele is more common because the left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein.","A new left varicocele in an older man suggests renal cell carcinoma blocking the left renal vein."]},{"h":"Torsion, priapism, Peyronie disease","points":["Testicular torsion: spermatic cord twists, usually from a bell-clapper deformity.","Venous outflow is blocked first, then arterial inflow, leading to hemorrhagic infarction.","Torsion: sudden severe pain, high-riding testis, absent cremasteric reflex; an emergency.","Priapism: painful erection lasting over 4 hours.","Ischemic priapism: sickle cell disease and drugs; trapped hypoxic blood causes cavernosal fibrosis and later erectile dysfunction.","Peyronie disease: fibrous plaque of the tunica albuginea causing penile curvature and painful erection."]},{"h":"Male infertility","points":["Pretesticular: hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (low FSH and LH), such as Kallmann syndrome, pituitary disease, or anabolic steroid use.","Testicular: Klinefelter syndrome, cryptorchidism, varicocele, mumps orchitis; FSH is usually high.","Post-testicular: obstruction of the ducts; hormones are normal.","Cystic fibrosis: congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens gives obstructive azoospermia with low semen volume.","Varicocele impairs sperm production by raising scrotal temperature."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Scrotal swellings at the bedside","cols":["Condition","Contents and site","Transilluminates","Key clue","Complications or links"],"rows":[["Hydrocele","Clear fluid in tunica vaginalis around testis","Yes","Smooth, testis hard to feel through fluid","Communicating type links to inguinal hernia"],["Hematocele","Blood in tunica vaginalis","No","Follows trauma, torsion, or tumor","Look for an underlying testicular injury or tumor"],["Spermatocele","Sperm-filled cyst of the epididymis","Yes","Separate from testis, usually above it","Usually harmless"],["Varicocele","Dilated pampiniform plexus","No","Bag of worms, larger on standing","Infertility; new left one suggests renal cell carcinoma"],["Erectile dysfunction (not a mass)","Vascular, neurogenic, endocrine, drug, psychogenic","Not applicable","Nocturnal erections kept in psychogenic cause","Marker of vascular disease"]]},{"title":"Male infertility by level","cols":["Level","FSH and LH","Testosterone","Examples"],"rows":[["Pretesticular","Low","Low (high if taking anabolic steroids)","Kallmann syndrome, pituitary disease, anabolic steroids"],["Testicular","High (FSH especially)","Normal or low","Klinefelter syndrome, cryptorchidism, varicocele, mumps orchitis"],["Post-testicular","Normal","Normal","Duct obstruction, absent vas deferens in cystic fibrosis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Point, Squeeze, and Shoot","text":"Parasympathetic nerves cause erection (point), sympathetic nerves cause emission (squeeze), and somatic pudendal nerves cause ejaculation (shoot)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Light goes through water","text":"Fluid that is clear (hydrocele, spermatocele) lets light through. Blood (hematocele), veins (varicocele), and solid tumors do not."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Left vein, long route","text":"The left testicular vein empties into the left renal vein at a right angle, so pressure backs up easily. A renal tumor in that vein makes a new left varicocele."}],"highYield":["Bag of worms that enlarges on standing and does not transilluminate: varicocele.","New left varicocele in an older man: think renal cell carcinoma in the left renal vein.","Transilluminating mass separate from and above the testis: spermatocele.","Transilluminating fluid around the testis in an infant: communicating hydrocele from a patent processus vaginalis.","Sudden severe testicular pain, high-riding testis, absent cremasteric reflex: torsion.","Ischemic priapism in a patient with sickle cell disease; untreated it causes fibrosis and erectile dysfunction.","Normal nocturnal erections suggest psychogenic erectile dysfunction.","Cystic fibrosis causes infertility through absent vas deferens with normal testosterone."],"pitfalls":["Hydrocele surrounds the testis; spermatocele sits beside or above it. Both transilluminate.","Hematocele and hydrocele are both in the tunica vaginalis; only blood blocks the light.","Torsion loses the cremasteric reflex; epididymitis keeps it.","Peyronie disease is a plaque in the tunica albuginea; priapism is a prolonged erection without a plaque."],"dumps":[{"t":"Scrotal mass map","p":"Draw a testis with its epididymis, tunica vaginalis, and spermatic cord. Draw and label where a hydrocele, hematocele, spermatocele, and varicocele form, and write beside each whether it transilluminates.","l":["Hydrocele: fluid in tunica vaginalis around the testis, transilluminates","Hematocele: blood in tunica vaginalis, does not transilluminate","Spermatocele: cyst of the epididymis, separate from testis, transilluminates","Varicocele: pampiniform plexus in the cord, does not transilluminate","Varicocele enlarges on standing","Left testicular vein drains to left renal vein","Communicating hydrocele comes from a patent processus vaginalis"]},{"t":"Erectile dysfunction causes","p":"Make a table of the five types of erectile dysfunction. For each, write how it causes the problem and give one example. Add the test that points to a psychogenic cause.","l":["Erection requires parasympathetic input, nitric oxide, and cGMP","Vascular: atherosclerosis limits inflow","Neurogenic: diabetes, spinal cord injury, pelvic surgery","Endocrine: hypogonadism or hyperprolactinemia","Drug-induced: an example drug class","Psychogenic: nocturnal erections are preserved","ED can be an early sign of coronary artery disease"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, sections on the penis and the testis and epididymis"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the scrotum, testes, and spermatic cord"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male, section on the male sexual act"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-5","RE-4","RE-13-C","RE-13-B5"]},"RE-13-E":{"id":"RE-13-E","summary":"This unit covers breast disease, from inflammation and fibrocystic change to benign tumors and carcinoma. Exam questions ask which lumps raise cancer risk, which benign lesions mimic cancer, and how the main carcinoma types look under the microscope.","notes":[{"h":"Fibrocystic change and cancer risk","points":["Nonproliferative change: cysts, apocrine metaplasia, fibrosis; no added cancer risk.","Proliferative change without atypia (usual hyperplasia, sclerosing adenosis, papilloma): slightly raised risk, about 1.5 to 2 times.","Atypical ductal or lobular hyperplasia: moderately raised risk, about 4 to 5 times."]},{"h":"Inflammatory and reactive lesions","points":["Acute mastitis: Staphylococcus aureus enters through cracked nipples while breastfeeding; red, warm, painful breast, fever, possible abscess.","Periductal mastitis: in smokers; squamous metaplasia of lactiferous ducts and keratin plugs give a painful subareolar mass or abscess.","Mammary duct ectasia: dilated subareolar ducts with thick secretions and plasma cell-rich inflammation in older multiparous women.","Duct ectasia scarring pulls the nipple in, so it can mimic carcinoma.","Galactocele: milk-filled duct cyst during lactation or weaning; rupture causes local inflammation.","Traumatic fat necrosis: nonenzymatic fat necrosis after trauma or surgery with foamy macrophages, giant cells, and calcification.","Fat necrosis forms a firm mass that can look like carcinoma on exam and mammography."]},{"h":"Benign tumors","points":["Fibroadenoma: most common benign tumor in young women; small, firm, mobile; stroma and ducts both proliferate.","Fibroadenoma is estrogen sensitive and can grow in pregnancy.","Phyllodes tumor: older women; leaf-like projections of overgrown, hypercellular stroma; can recur or metastasize.","Intraductal papilloma: benign papillary growth in a large subareolar duct; common cause of bloody nipple discharge."]},{"h":"Carcinoma","points":["Risk: age, family history, atypical hyperplasia, and long estrogen exposure.","Estrogen exposure: early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, late first pregnancy, postmenopausal obesity (fat aromatase).","Most common site is the upper outer quadrant.","DCIS: malignant cells confined by basement membrane; microcalcifications on mammography; comedo type has central necrosis.","Invasive ductal carcinoma: most common type; firm stellate mass with desmoplastic stroma.","Lobular carcinoma: E-cadherin (CDH1) loss makes cells discohesive; they invade in single-file lines; often bilateral and multifocal.","Paget disease: eczematous nipple from large Paget cells with clear cytoplasm in the epidermis; nearly always an underlying ductal carcinoma.","Inflammatory carcinoma: tumor emboli in dermal lymphatics cause erythema, edema, and peau d'orange."]},{"h":"Male breast","points":["Gynecomastia: benign ductal and stromal growth from a raised estrogen to androgen ratio; often bilateral and centered under the areola.","Causes: puberty, older age, cirrhosis, Klinefelter syndrome, some drugs.","Male breast carcinoma: linked to BRCA2 mutation and Klinefelter syndrome; firm, often one-sided mass."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Conditions of the breast","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism or histology","Classic findings","Cancer link or complication"],"rows":[["Fibrocystic change","Hormonal; reproductive years","Cysts, apocrine metaplasia, fibrosis; may be proliferative","Lumpy, sometimes tender breasts","None if nonproliferative; higher with atypia"],["Galactocele","Lactation or weaning","Milk-filled dilated duct","Cystic mass while nursing","Rupture and local inflammation"],["Mammary duct ectasia","Older multiparous women","Dilated ducts, plasma cell-rich inflammation","Thick nipple discharge, nipple retraction","Mimics carcinoma; no added risk"],["Acute mastitis","Breastfeeding, cracked nipples","Staphylococcus aureus infection","Red, warm, painful breast with fever","Abscess"],["Traumatic fat necrosis","Trauma or surgery","Foamy macrophages, giant cells, calcification","Firm mass, may be calcified","Mimics carcinoma; no added risk"],["Fibroadenoma","Young women, estrogen","Proliferating stroma and ducts","Small, firm, mobile mass","Little or no added risk"],["Ductal carcinoma","Age, estrogen exposure, family history","DCIS confined to ducts; invasive type has desmoplasia","Microcalcifications or hard stellate mass","Metastasis to axillary nodes"],["Lobular carcinoma","Same general risks","E-cadherin loss, single-file invasion","Ill-defined thickening or mass","Bilateral and multifocal disease"],["Paget disease of the breast","Underlying ductal carcinoma","Paget cells within nipple epidermis","Eczematous, crusted nipple","Signals underlying carcinoma"]]},{"title":"Look-alikes to separate","cols":["Pair","First lesion","Second lesion"],"rows":[["Fibroadenoma vs phyllodes tumor","Young woman, small, mobile, balanced stroma and ducts","Older woman, larger, leaf-like hypercellular stroma, can recur"],["Acute mastitis vs inflammatory carcinoma","Breastfeeding, fever, Staphylococcus aureus","Not nursing, peau d'orange, tumor in dermal lymphatics"],["DCIS vs invasive ductal carcinoma","Basement membrane intact, microcalcifications","Breaches basement membrane, stellate mass with desmoplasia"],["Gynecomastia vs male breast carcinoma","Rubbery, central, often both sides","Hard, often off-center, one side; BRCA2, Klinefelter"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Lobular loses its glue","text":"E-cadherin holds cells together. Lobular carcinoma loses it, so cells drift apart and march in single file."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fibroadenoma is the breast mouse","text":"A small, smooth, very mobile lump in a young woman slips away under the fingers. That mobility is the classic fibroadenoma sign."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Count the estrogen years","text":"Early menarche, late menopause, no pregnancies, late first pregnancy, and postmenopausal fat all add estrogen exposure, and each raises breast cancer risk."}],"highYield":["Nonproliferative fibrocystic change carries no added cancer risk; atypical hyperplasia raises it about 4 to 5 times.","Breastfeeding woman with a red, painful breast and fever: Staphylococcus aureus mastitis.","Smoker with a painful subareolar mass: periductal mastitis from squamous metaplasia.","Bloody nipple discharge with a small subareolar lesion: intraductal papilloma.","Microcalcifications on mammography: DCIS; comedo type has central necrosis.","E-cadherin loss and single-file cells: invasive lobular carcinoma.","Eczematous nipple: Paget disease, which signals an underlying carcinoma.","Peau d'orange: inflammatory carcinoma with tumor in dermal lymphatics."],"pitfalls":["Fat necrosis and duct ectasia can look like cancer (mass, calcification, nipple retraction) but carry no added risk.","Phyllodes tumor is a stromal tumor of older women; do not call a large, fast-growing lump in a 50-year-old a fibroadenoma.","Inflammatory carcinoma looks infected but usually has no fever and occurs outside lactation.","Paget disease of the breast nearly always has an underlying carcinoma; vulvar Paget disease usually does not."],"dumps":[{"t":"Breast lesion risk ladder","p":"Draw a ladder from no added risk at the bottom to carcinoma at the top. Place nonproliferative change, proliferative change without atypia, atypical hyperplasia, carcinoma in situ, and invasive carcinoma on it, with examples and the size of risk.","l":["Nonproliferative: cysts, apocrine metaplasia, fibrosis; no added risk","Proliferative without atypia: usual hyperplasia, sclerosing adenosis, papilloma","Proliferative without atypia raises risk about 1.5 to 2 times","Atypical ductal or lobular hyperplasia raises risk about 4 to 5 times","DCIS is confined by the basement membrane","Invasive carcinoma has crossed the basement membrane","Fat necrosis and duct ectasia sit at the bottom despite mimicking cancer"]},{"t":"Ductal vs lobular vs Paget","p":"Make a table comparing ductal carcinoma in situ, invasive ductal carcinoma, invasive lobular carcinoma, and Paget disease of the breast. Include histology, how it is found, and one special feature of each.","l":["DCIS: confined to ducts, found by microcalcifications","Comedo DCIS has central necrosis","Invasive ductal: most common, firm stellate mass, desmoplasia","Lobular: E-cadherin (CDH1) loss","Lobular cells invade in single file","Lobular is often bilateral and multifocal","Paget: large clear cells in nipple epidermis","Paget almost always has underlying carcinoma"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Breast"},{"book":"Robbins Basic Pathology","where":"Female Genital System and Breast, section on the breast"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on breast cancer"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-6","RE-2","RE-13-J","RE-13-C6"]},"RE-13-F":{"id":"RE-13-F","summary":"This unit covers the two common benign prostate problems: benign prostatic hyperplasia and prostatitis. Exam questions test where BPH grows and why that causes urinary symptoms, and which organism and time course fit each type of prostatitis.","notes":[{"h":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia: cause","points":["Nodular hyperplasia of both glands and stroma in older men.","Grows in the transition (periurethral) zone, which wraps around the urethra.","Driven by DHT, made from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase type 2 in stromal cells.","BPH is not a precursor of prostate carcinoma; carcinoma arises mostly in the peripheral zone.","PSA can be mildly raised in BPH."]},{"h":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia: effects","points":["Obstruction: hesitancy, weak stream, intermittent flow, incomplete emptying, dribbling.","Irritation from the straining bladder: frequency, urgency, nocturia.","Bladder wall hypertrophy and trabeculation, sometimes diverticula.","Urinary retention; stagnant urine favors infection and bladder stones.","Back pressure causes bilateral hydronephrosis and can cause postrenal kidney injury."]},{"h":"Acute bacterial prostatitis","points":["Older men: E. coli and other gram-negative rods, usually from infected urine refluxing into prostatic ducts.","Younger sexually active men: C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae.","Can follow catheterization or prostate procedures.","Fever, chills, dysuria, frequency, perineal or low back pain.","Rectal exam: tender, warm, swollen, boggy prostate."]},{"h":"Chronic prostatitis","points":["Chronic bacterial: recurrent urinary tract infections with the same organism, because the prostate acts as a reservoir.","Often few systemic symptoms; low back pain, dysuria, perineal discomfort between episodes.","Chronic nonbacterial (chronic pelvic pain syndrome): months of pelvic pain with no bacteria on culture.","Chronic nonbacterial is the most common form of prostatitis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Prostate conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Older age, DHT","Nodular hyperplasia of glands and stroma in the transition zone","Hesitancy, weak stream, nocturia; enlarged smooth rubbery prostate","Retention, bladder hypertrophy, infection, hydronephrosis"],["Acute bacterial prostatitis","E. coli in older men; C. trachomatis, N. gonorrhoeae in younger men","Reflux of infected urine into prostatic ducts","Fever, chills, dysuria, tender boggy prostate","Abscess, bacteremia, chronic bacterial prostatitis"],["Chronic bacterial prostatitis","Incompletely cleared bacterial infection","Prostate harbors the same organism","Recurrent UTIs with one organism","Repeated urinary infection"],["Chronic nonbacterial prostatitis","Unknown; most common type","Inflammation or pain without bacteria","Pelvic pain lasting months, negative cultures","Long-term pain and sexual dysfunction"]]},{"title":"BPH vs prostate carcinoma","cols":["Feature","BPH","Prostate carcinoma"],"rows":[["Zone","Transition (periurethral)","Mostly peripheral"],["Tissue","Glands and stroma, nodular","Malignant glands"],["Early urinary symptoms","Common, because it squeezes the urethra","Uncommon, because it grows away from the urethra"],["Rectal exam","Smooth, rubbery, enlarged","Hard, irregular nodule"],["Relationship","Not a precursor","Separate disease"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Transition zone squeezes the tube","text":"BPH grows in the zone wrapped around the urethra, so it causes obstruction early. Cancer grows in the outer peripheral zone, so it is felt on rectal exam before it causes symptoms."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Age picks the bug","text":"Under about 35 and sexually active: think chlamydia and gonorrhea. Older man: think E. coli from the urine. The same rule works for epididymitis."}],"highYield":["BPH is DHT-driven hyperplasia of the transition zone and is not premalignant.","Older man with hesitancy, weak stream, and nocturia: BPH.","BPH complications: retention, bladder hypertrophy, UTI, stones, bilateral hydronephrosis.","Fever, dysuria, and a tender boggy prostate in an older man: acute prostatitis from E. coli.","Recurrent UTIs with the same organism in a man: chronic bacterial prostatitis.","Chronic pelvic pain with negative cultures: chronic nonbacterial prostatitis."],"pitfalls":["BPH and carcinoma can coexist, but BPH does not turn into carcinoma.","A raised PSA does not by itself separate BPH from cancer; prostatitis can raise it too.","Chronic bacterial prostatitis is defined by the same organism coming back; chronic nonbacterial has no organism at all."],"dumps":[{"t":"BPH from prostate to kidney","p":"Draw the bladder, prostate, urethra, ureters, and kidneys. Shade the zone where BPH grows and trace what obstruction does to each structure above it.","l":["BPH grows in the transition zone around the urethra","DHT from 5-alpha-reductase drives the growth","Urethral narrowing gives hesitancy and a weak stream","Bladder wall hypertrophy and trabeculation","Residual urine leads to infection and stones","Back pressure gives bilateral hydronephrosis","Carcinoma arises mostly in the peripheral zone"]},{"t":"Three types of prostatitis","p":"Make a table comparing acute bacterial, chronic bacterial, and chronic nonbacterial prostatitis. Include cause, typical patient, symptoms, exam, and culture result.","l":["Acute: E. coli and gram-negative rods in older men","Acute: C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae in younger men","Acute: fever, chills, dysuria, tender boggy prostate","Chronic bacterial: recurrent UTIs with the same organism","Chronic bacterial: prostate is the reservoir","Chronic nonbacterial: pelvic pain with negative cultures","Chronic nonbacterial is the most common type"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, section on the prostate"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the prostate"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on enteric gram-negative rods"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-8","RE-2","RE-12","RE-13-J"]},"RE-13-G":{"id":"RE-13-G","summary":"Ovarian and adnexal masses range from harmless functional cysts to PCOS, a whole-body endocrine and metabolic disorder. Exams test which cyst fits the clinical setting, why an enlarged ovary twists, and how insulin resistance and LH drive androgen excess in PCOS.","notes":[{"h":"Functional ovarian cysts","points":["Follicular cyst: a follicle that fails to rupture keeps growing; the most common functional cyst, usually resolves in a few cycles.","Follicular cysts can keep making estrogen, which may cause irregular bleeding and, rarely, endometrial hyperplasia or precocious puberty in girls.","Corpus luteum cyst: forms after ovulation; bleeding into it can rupture into the peritoneum, giving sudden pain and hemoperitoneum that can mimic ectopic pregnancy.","Theca lutein cysts: usually bilateral and multiple; very high hCG overstimulates theca cells (hydatidiform mole, choriocarcinoma, multiple gestation, ovarian hyperstimulation).","Theca lutein cysts regress once hCG falls, such as after a mole is evacuated."]},{"h":"Ovarian torsion","points":["The ovary twists on the infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament and the utero-ovarian ligament, which carry its blood supply.","Usually needs an enlarged ovary as a lever: a cyst, teratoma, or other mass, often larger than about 5 cm.","Thin-walled veins and lymphatics close first, so the ovary swells with edema and congestion; rising pressure then blocks arterial inflow and the ovary infarcts.","Presents with sudden severe unilateral pelvic pain, nausea, and vomiting; Doppler may show absent flow, though normal flow does not rule torsion out."]},{"h":"Paraovarian cysts","points":["Thin-walled cysts in the broad ligament (mesosalpinx) between the tube and ovary, arising from Wolffian (mesonephric) or Mullerian (paramesonephric) remnants or mesothelium.","Imaging shows a separate, normal ovary beside the cyst, which helps set it apart from an ovarian cyst.","Mostly asymptomatic and benign; large ones can twist, bleed, or rupture."]},{"h":"PCOS pathogenesis","points":["Faster GnRH pulses favor LH over FSH, so the LH to FSH ratio rises (often above 2 to 1).","LH drives theca cells to make androgens; relatively low FSH means granulosa aromatase cannot convert them all to estradiol.","Insulin resistance gives high insulin, which acts with LH on theca cells to raise androgen output and lowers hepatic SHBG, so free testosterone rises.","Excess androgen arrests follicle development, leaving many small follicles along the cortex (string of pearls) and no ovulation.","Adipose tissue aromatizes androgens to estrone, giving steady estrogen with no progesterone (unopposed estrogen)."]},{"h":"PCOS findings and complications","points":["Rotterdam criteria (2 of 3): oligo or anovulation, clinical or lab hyperandrogenism, polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound.","Findings: oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea, hirsutism, acne, male-pattern hair thinning, obesity, acanthosis nigricans.","Complications: infertility, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidemia, fatty liver, obstructive sleep apnea, and endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma from unopposed estrogen."]},{"h":"Tubo-ovarian cysts and abscess","points":["A complication of PID: ascending infection (often begun by N. gonorrhoeae or C. trachomatis, then mixed with anaerobes) inflames the tube, which adheres to the ovary.","The result is a fluid-filled or pus-filled tubo-ovarian complex: fever, pelvic pain, adnexal mass, and cervical motion tenderness.","Rupture spills pus into the peritoneum, causing peritonitis and sepsis.","Scarring later causes infertility, ectopic pregnancy, chronic pelvic pain, and hydrosalpinx."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Ovarian and adnexal cystic conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Follicular cyst","Anovulatory cycle, common in reproductive years","Unruptured follicle keeps enlarging; may secrete estrogen","Unilateral simple cyst, irregular bleeding or none","Torsion, rupture; rare estrogen effects"],["Corpus luteum cyst","After ovulation, early pregnancy","Corpus luteum fails to regress and fills with fluid or blood","Unilateral cyst, delayed menses possible","Rupture with hemoperitoneum, torsion"],["Theca lutein cysts","Very high hCG: mole, choriocarcinoma, multiple gestation, ovarian hyperstimulation","hCG overstimulates theca cells","Bilateral, multiple, enlarged ovaries","Torsion, rupture; regress when hCG falls"],["Paraovarian cyst","Embryonic remnants in the broad ligament","Wolffian, Mullerian, or mesothelial inclusion cyst","Thin-walled cyst with a separate normal ovary","Torsion, rupture, hemorrhage"],["Polycystic ovary syndrome","Insulin resistance, obesity, family history","High LH to FSH ratio, theca androgen excess, low SHBG, follicle arrest","Oligomenorrhea, hirsutism, acne, acanthosis nigricans, many small peripheral follicles","Infertility, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidemia, endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma"],["Tubo-ovarian cyst or abscess","PID (gonorrhea, chlamydia, anaerobes), multiple partners","Inflamed tube adheres to ovary, forming a fluid- or pus-filled complex","Fever, pelvic pain, adnexal mass, cervical motion tenderness","Rupture with peritonitis and sepsis, infertility, ectopic pregnancy"]]},{"title":"PCOS: from hormones to complications","cols":["Driver","Effect on the ovary or body","Clinical result"],"rows":[["High LH (fast GnRH pulses)","Theca cells make more androgen","Hirsutism, acne"],["Relatively low FSH","Less granulosa aromatase activity; follicles arrest","Anovulation, many small follicles, infertility"],["Hyperinsulinemia","Boosts theca androgen output; lowers hepatic SHBG","More free testosterone, acanthosis nigricans, type 2 diabetes"],["Peripheral aromatization","Androgens become estrone in fat","Unopposed estrogen, endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Name the cyst by the hormone around it","text":"Before ovulation, the follicular cyst can make estrogen. After ovulation, the corpus luteum cyst bleeds. Very high hCG gives theca lutein cysts on both sides."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Torsion follows the veins first","text":"Veins collapse before arteries because their walls are thin and their pressure is low. So the twisted ovary swells first, then infarcts."},{"kind":"trick","label":"PCOS in one loop","text":"Insulin and LH push theca cells to make androgen, androgen stops follicles, no ovulation means no progesterone, and estrogen goes unopposed on the endometrium."}],"highYield":["Bilateral theca lutein cysts point to very high hCG, especially a complete hydatidiform mole.","A ruptured corpus luteum cyst can cause hemoperitoneum and mimic ruptured ectopic pregnancy.","Ovarian torsion needs an enlarged ovary; mature cystic teratoma is a classic lead point.","PCOS: LH to FSH ratio high, insulin resistance lowers SHBG, free testosterone rises.","PCOS raises the risk of endometrial carcinoma through chronic unopposed estrogen.","Acanthosis nigricans in a woman with irregular cycles signals insulin resistance and PCOS.","Tubo-ovarian abscess is a complication of PID and can rupture into the peritoneum."],"pitfalls":["Paraovarian versus ovarian cyst: a paraovarian cyst sits in the broad ligament with a normal ovary seen separately.","Polycystic ovaries on ultrasound alone do not make PCOS; Rotterdam needs two of three criteria.","Theca lutein cysts are bilateral and hCG-driven; follicular and corpus luteum cysts are usually single.","Normal Doppler flow does not exclude torsion, because the arterial supply can be partly preserved early.","Tubo-ovarian abscess is an infection with fever and leukocytosis; a hemorrhagic corpus luteum cyst gives sudden pain without fever."],"dumps":[{"t":"PCOS pathway","p":"Draw a theca cell and a granulosa cell side by side. Show how LH, FSH, and insulin act on them, then trace the effects to the liver, fat, and endometrium.","l":["Fast GnRH pulses raise LH relative to FSH","LH stimulates theca cells to make androgens","Low FSH limits granulosa aromatase, so less estradiol","Insulin adds to theca androgen output","Insulin lowers hepatic SHBG, raising free testosterone","Androgen excess arrests follicles, giving anovulation and many small follicles","Fat aromatizes androgen to estrone, giving unopposed estrogen","Unopposed estrogen leads to endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma"]},{"t":"Adnexal cyst table","p":"On blank paper, build a table with rows for follicular cyst, corpus luteum cyst, theca lutein cysts, paraovarian cyst, and tubo-ovarian abscess. Columns: origin, one side or both, hormone link, main complication.","l":["Follicular: unruptured follicle, usually one side, may make estrogen","Corpus luteum: after ovulation, one side, bleeds and may cause hemoperitoneum","Theca lutein: both sides, driven by high hCG (mole, multiple gestation)","Paraovarian: broad ligament remnant, separate normal ovary","Tubo-ovarian abscess: PID complication with fever and adnexal mass","Any enlarged adnexal mass can twist (torsion)","Tubo-ovarian abscess can rupture and cause peritonitis"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Female Reproductive Tract"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-7","RE-13-A2","RE-13-B6","RE-13-H2","RE-13-J2"]},"RE-13-H":{"id":"RE-13-H","summary":"Placental disease covers gestational trophoblastic disease (moles and choriocarcinoma), preeclampsia, and problems of placental position, attachment, and amniotic fluid. Exams test the genetics of moles, the vascular story of preeclampsia, and how to tell painful from painless third-trimester bleeding.","notes":[{"h":"Complete versus partial mole","points":["Complete mole: an empty ovum (no maternal DNA) is fertilized by one sperm that duplicates, giving 46,XX; less often two sperm give 46,XY.","All DNA is paternal, so p57 (a maternally expressed gene) is negative; no fetal parts; all villi swell; trophoblast proliferates diffusely.","Partial mole: one normal ovum plus two sperm gives a triploid karyotype (69,XXY most often); fetal parts present, focal villous swelling, p57 positive.","Complete moles carry the higher risk of invasive mole and choriocarcinoma (choriocarcinoma about 2 to 3 percent); partial moles rarely progress."]},{"h":"Findings of complete mole","points":["Vaginal bleeding in the first half of pregnancy, uterus large for dates, no fetal heart tones.","Very high hCG causes hyperemesis gravidarum and bilateral theca lutein cysts.","hCG shares its alpha subunit with TSH and weakly stimulates the TSH receptor, so hyperthyroidism can occur.","Preeclampsia before 20 weeks of gestation strongly suggests a mole.","Ultrasound shows a snowstorm or cluster-of-grapes pattern; after evacuation, serial hCG must fall to zero."]},{"h":"Invasive mole and choriocarcinoma","points":["Invasive mole: hydropic villi grow into the myometrium; it can perforate the uterus and bleed; hCG stays high after evacuation.","Gestational choriocarcinoma: malignant cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast with no villi, forming a hemorrhagic, necrotic mass.","About half arise after a complete mole; the rest follow abortion, normal pregnancy, or ectopic pregnancy.","hCG is very high; spread is early and through the blood, to lung (cough, hemoptysis), vagina, brain, and liver.","Gestational choriocarcinoma responds very well to chemotherapy."]},{"h":"Preeclampsia","points":["Cytotrophoblast fails to remodel the spiral arteries, so they stay narrow, muscular, and high-resistance; the placenta becomes ischemic.","The ischemic placenta releases sFlt-1 (binds VEGF and PlGF) and soluble endoglin (binds TGF-beta), causing maternal endothelial dysfunction.","Less nitric oxide and prostacyclin, more thromboxane: vasospasm, hypertension, leaky vessels, and glomerular endotheliosis with proteinuria.","Definition: new blood pressure of 140/90 mm Hg or higher after 20 weeks, with proteinuria or end-organ damage (low platelets, high creatinine, high transaminases, pulmonary edema, headache, visual change).","Risk factors: nulliparity, prior preeclampsia, chronic hypertension, diabetes, kidney disease, antiphospholipid syndrome, obesity, multiple gestation, molar pregnancy.","Complications: eclampsia (seizures), HELLP syndrome, placental abruption, fetal growth restriction, stroke, DIC, liver rupture."]},{"h":"Placental position and attachment","points":["Placental abruption: the placenta separates early; painful bleeding, tender firm uterus, fetal distress; the released tissue factor can trigger DIC.","Abruption risks: hypertension and preeclampsia, cocaine, smoking, abdominal trauma, prior abruption.","Placenta previa: placenta covers or lies near the internal cervical os; painless bright red bleeding in the third trimester.","Placenta accreta spectrum: villi attach to (accreta), invade into (increta), or go through (percreta) the myometrium because the decidua basalis is deficient.","Biggest accreta risks are prior cesarean delivery and placenta previa together; the placenta does not separate, causing severe postpartum hemorrhage."]},{"h":"Amniotic fluid volume","points":["After about 16 weeks, fetal urine makes most of the amniotic fluid, and fetal swallowing removes it.","Oligohydramnios: too little urine (bilateral renal agenesis, posterior urethral valves, placental insufficiency) or ruptured membranes.","Potter sequence: low fluid compresses the fetus, giving pulmonary hypoplasia, flat face, limb deformities such as clubfeet.","Polyhydramnios: poor swallowing (esophageal or duodenal atresia, anencephaly) or maternal diabetes (fetal hyperglycemia causes osmotic diuresis)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Gestational trophoblastic disease and preeclampsia","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Complete hydatidiform mole","Empty ovum fertilized by one sperm (duplicates) or two sperm; extremes of maternal age","46,XX all paternal, p57 negative, diffuse villous swelling and trophoblast proliferation","Bleeding, uterus large for dates, very high hCG, no fetal parts, snowstorm ultrasound","Theca lutein cysts, hyperthyroidism, preeclampsia before 20 weeks, invasive mole, choriocarcinoma"],["Partial hydatidiform mole","Normal ovum fertilized by two sperm","Triploid (69,XXY most often), p57 positive, focal villous swelling","Fetal parts, hCG raised less, often looks like missed abortion","Persistent disease uncommon, choriocarcinoma rare"],["Invasive mole","Usually follows a complete mole","Hydropic villi penetrate the myometrium","hCG stays high after evacuation, bleeding","Uterine perforation, hemorrhage, villi carried to lung"],["Choriocarcinoma (gestational)","Complete mole (about half), abortion, normal or ectopic pregnancy","Malignant cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast with no villi","Very high hCG, bleeding, hemorrhagic necrotic mass","Early blood-borne spread to lung, vagina, brain, liver"],["Preeclampsia","Nulliparity, chronic hypertension, diabetes, multiple gestation, obesity, prior preeclampsia","Failed spiral artery remodeling, placental ischemia, sFlt-1 and soluble endoglin, endothelial injury","BP 140/90 mm Hg or higher after 20 weeks with proteinuria or end-organ damage","Eclampsia, HELLP, abruption, fetal growth restriction, DIC, stroke"]]},{"title":"Complete versus partial mole","cols":["Feature","Complete mole","Partial mole"],"rows":[["Karyotype","46,XX (sometimes 46,XY)","Triploid, 69,XXY most often"],["Origin of DNA","All paternal (empty ovum)","One maternal set, two paternal sets"],["Fetal parts","Absent","Present"],["Villous swelling","Diffuse","Focal"],["p57 staining","Negative","Positive"],["hCG","Very high","Mildly raised"],["Choriocarcinoma risk","About 2 to 3 percent","Rare"]]},{"title":"Third-trimester bleeding and fluid problems","cols":["Condition","Key feature","Main risk factors or causes","Main danger"],"rows":[["Placental abruption","Painful bleeding, tender firm uterus","Hypertension, cocaine, smoking, trauma","DIC, fetal hypoxia, maternal shock"],["Placenta previa","Painless bright red bleeding","Prior cesarean, multiparity, advanced maternal age","Maternal hemorrhage"],["Placenta accreta spectrum","Placenta does not separate after delivery","Prior cesarean plus placenta previa, uterine surgery","Severe postpartum hemorrhage"],["Oligohydramnios","Low amniotic fluid","Renal agenesis, urinary obstruction, ruptured membranes","Potter sequence with pulmonary hypoplasia"],["Polyhydramnios","Excess amniotic fluid","Esophageal or duodenal atresia, anencephaly, maternal diabetes","Preterm labor, malpresentation"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Complete is all dad","text":"A complete mole has only paternal DNA, so the maternally expressed p57 is missing and no fetus forms. A partial mole has mom's set too, so p57 is present and fetal parts appear."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Preeclampsia clock","text":"Hypertension with proteinuria after 20 weeks is preeclampsia. The same picture before 20 weeks should make you think of a hydatidiform mole."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pain sorts the bleed","text":"Abruption hurts because blood dissects behind the placenta and irritates the uterus. Previa bleeds painlessly because the placenta simply tears from a stretching lower segment."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fluid in, fluid out","text":"The fetus makes amniotic fluid as urine and removes it by swallowing. Kidney or urinary problems give too little fluid; swallowing problems give too much."}],"highYield":["Complete mole is 46,XX with all-paternal DNA, no fetal parts, and negative p57.","Very high hCG explains hyperemesis, theca lutein cysts, and hyperthyroidism in a molar pregnancy.","Persistent or rising hCG after mole evacuation means invasive mole or choriocarcinoma.","Choriocarcinoma has no villi and spreads early through the blood, most often to the lung.","Preeclampsia starts with failed spiral artery remodeling and antiangiogenic sFlt-1 release.","HELLP: hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets.","Painful bleeding is abruption; painless bleeding is previa; prior cesarean plus previa predicts accreta.","Bilateral renal agenesis gives oligohydramnios and Potter sequence with fatal pulmonary hypoplasia."],"pitfalls":["Partial mole is triploid and has fetal parts; complete mole is diploid and has none.","Invasive mole keeps its villi; choriocarcinoma has no villi at all.","Choriocarcinoma can follow a normal pregnancy, so a high hCG with lung nodules months after delivery should raise it.","Eclampsia means preeclampsia plus seizures; seizures from another cause are a different diagnosis.","Accreta is about depth of attachment; previa is about location over the os, though the two often occur together."],"dumps":[{"t":"Mole genetics","p":"Draw the fertilization events that make a complete mole and a partial mole. Label the chromosome count, where each set comes from, p57 status, and what the villi look like.","l":["Complete: empty ovum with no maternal DNA","One sperm duplicates (46,XX) or two sperm enter (46,XY)","Complete: all paternal, p57 negative, no fetal parts","Complete: diffuse villous swelling and trophoblast proliferation","Partial: normal ovum plus two sperm, triploid (69,XXY)","Partial: p57 positive, fetal parts, focal swelling","Complete moles carry the higher risk of invasive mole and choriocarcinoma"]},{"t":"Preeclampsia pathway","p":"Trace preeclampsia from the spiral arteries to the mother's organs. Draw a normal remodeled spiral artery beside a failed one, then list the maternal effects and complications.","l":["Normal: cytotrophoblast replaces artery smooth muscle, giving wide low-resistance vessels","Preeclampsia: remodeling fails, vessels stay narrow, placenta becomes ischemic","Ischemic placenta releases sFlt-1 and soluble endoglin","These bind VEGF, PlGF, and TGF-beta, causing endothelial dysfunction","Less nitric oxide and prostacyclin, more thromboxane, vasospasm","New BP 140/90 mm Hg or higher after 20 weeks with proteinuria or end-organ damage","Complications: eclampsia, HELLP, abruption, fetal growth restriction"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract (section on diseases of pregnancy)"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Third Month to Birth: The Fetus and Placenta"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Female Reproductive Tract (section on preeclampsia)"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-3","RE-7","RE-10","RE-13-G1","RE-13-J7"]},"RE-13-I":{"id":"RE-13-I","summary":"This unit covers the Bartholin cyst and pelvic organ prolapse (cystocele, urethrocele, rectocele). Exams test where each lesion sits, what weakens the pelvic floor, and which symptom points to which organ.","notes":[{"h":"Bartholin cyst and abscess","points":["Bartholin (greater vestibular) glands lie posterolateral to the vaginal opening, at about the 4 and 8 o'clock positions; they lubricate the vestibule.","Blockage of the duct (from inflammation, trauma, or thick mucus) traps secretions and forms a cyst in the posterolateral vestibule.","Infection turns it into an abscess: a hot, tender, fluctuant swelling that makes sitting and walking painful.","Mostly in reproductive-age women; a new Bartholin mass after about age 40 needs biopsy to exclude carcinoma.","The male homolog is the bulbourethral (Cowper) gland."]},{"h":"Why the pelvic floor fails","points":["Support comes from the levator ani, the endopelvic fascia (pubocervical anteriorly, rectovaginal posteriorly), and the cardinal and uterosacral ligaments.","Vaginal childbirth stretches and tears the levator ani and fascia and can injure the pudendal nerve.","Other risks: aging, estrogen loss after menopause, obesity, chronic cough, chronic constipation and straining, heavy lifting, connective tissue disorders.","Prolapse feels like pelvic pressure or a bulge at the vaginal opening that worsens with standing or straining."]},{"h":"Which organ is bulging","points":["Cystocele: the bladder bulges into the upper anterior vaginal wall through a weak pubocervical fascia.","Cystocele symptoms: incomplete emptying, frequency, urinary retention, recurrent UTIs; some women press the bulge to void.","Urethrocele: the urethra bulges into the distal anterior wall; a mobile urethra loses support and leaks with cough or laughter (stress incontinence).","Rectocele: the rectum bulges into the posterior vaginal wall through a weak rectovaginal septum.","Rectocele symptoms: hard-to-pass stools, incomplete evacuation, pressing on the posterior vaginal wall or perineum to defecate."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Benign vaginal and vulvar conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Bartholin cyst","Reproductive age, duct inflammation or trauma","Blocked Bartholin duct traps secretions","Painless or tender mass in the posterolateral vestibule","Abscess; carcinoma must be excluded in older women"],["Cystocele","Vaginal childbirth, aging, menopause, obesity, chronic cough or straining","Weak pubocervical fascia lets the bladder herniate","Bulge in the upper anterior vaginal wall","Incomplete emptying, retention, recurrent UTI"],["Urethrocele","Same pelvic floor risks","Urethra herniates into the distal anterior vaginal wall","Distal anterior wall bulge, leakage with cough or laughter","Stress urinary incontinence"],["Rectocele","Same pelvic floor risks, chronic constipation","Weak rectovaginal septum lets the rectum herniate","Bulge in the posterior vaginal wall","Difficult or incomplete defecation, need to splint"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Front is urine, back is stool","text":"Anterior wall bulges (cystocele, urethrocele) cause urinary symptoms. Posterior wall bulges (rectocele) cause trouble with bowel movements."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Upper versus lower front wall","text":"The bladder sits higher, so a cystocele bulges the upper anterior wall and causes retention. The urethra sits lower, so a urethrocele bulges the distal anterior wall and causes stress leakage."}],"highYield":["Bartholin cyst sits in the posterolateral vestibule at about 4 or 8 o'clock.","A Bartholin mass in a woman over about 40 is biopsied to rule out carcinoma.","Vaginal childbirth is the main risk factor for pelvic organ prolapse.","Estrogen loss after menopause weakens pelvic connective tissue and adds to prolapse.","Cystocele causes incomplete bladder emptying and recurrent UTIs.","Urethrocele causes stress incontinence; rectocele causes difficult defecation."],"pitfalls":["Bartholin cyst sits posterolateral at the vestibule; a Skene gland cyst sits beside the urethral opening.","The vaginal wall that bulges tells cystocele from rectocele: anterior is bladder, posterior is rectum.","Stress incontinence (leak with cough) points to urethral hypermobility; urge incontinence comes from detrusor overactivity.","Enterocele (small bowel in the upper posterior vagina) can mimic a high rectocele."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pelvic floor sagittal view","p":"Draw a midline sagittal view of the female pelvis. Label bladder, urethra, vagina, uterus, rectum, and levator ani, then draw where a cystocele, urethrocele, and rectocele bulge and write one symptom for each.","l":["Bladder anterior to the vagina, rectum posterior","Levator ani forms the floor; endopelvic fascia supports the walls","Cystocele: bladder into upper anterior wall","Cystocele symptom: incomplete emptying or recurrent UTI","Urethrocele: urethra into distal anterior wall, stress incontinence","Rectocele: rectum into posterior wall, difficult defecation","Main risks: vaginal childbirth, aging, menopause, obesity, chronic straining"]},{"t":"Bartholin gland","p":"Draw the vulvar vestibule from the front. Mark the Bartholin glands and ducts, show how a cyst forms, and list how a cyst differs from an abscess.","l":["Glands sit posterolateral to the vaginal opening at about 4 and 8 o'clock","Ducts open into the vestibule","Duct blockage traps mucus, forming a cyst","Cyst: often painless, soft mass","Abscess: infected, hot, tender, fluctuant","Most common in reproductive-age women","New mass after about 40 needs biopsy"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Female Reproductive Tract"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-2","RE-4","RE-13-A4","RE-13-B8"]},"RE-13-J":{"id":"RE-13-J","summary":"This unit gathers the precancers and tumors of the cervix, uterus, ovary, vagina, vulva, prostate, penis, and testis. Exams test the driver of each tumor (HPV, estrogen, germline mutations), the cell of origin, the tumor marker, and the typical spread.","notes":[{"h":"Cervix: CIN and invasive carcinoma","points":["High-risk HPV (16, 18) infects the transformation zone; E6 degrades p53, E7 inactivates RB, and koilocytes (raisin-like nucleus, clear perinuclear halo) mark infection.","CIN grade = how much epithelium atypical immature cells replace: CIN 1 lower third (LSIL), CIN 2 lower two-thirds, CIN 3 more than two-thirds to full thickness (CIN 2 and 3 are HSIL).","Risks: persistent HPV 16 or 18, early first intercourse, many partners, smoking, HIV and other immunodeficiency; most CIN 1 regresses.","Invasive cervical carcinoma is mostly squamous, with some adenocarcinoma; it presents with postcoital or irregular bleeding.","Lateral spread into the parametrium blocks the ureters, causing hydronephrosis and renal failure, a common cause of death."]},{"h":"Endometrium","points":["Unopposed estrogen causes hyperplasia: obesity, anovulation, PCOS, estrogen-only therapy, granulosa cell tumor; PTEN loss is common.","Atypical hyperplasia is the direct precursor of endometrioid carcinoma.","Endometrioid carcinoma: perimenopausal, estrogen-related, arises from hyperplasia; PTEN mutations and mismatch repair defects (Lynch syndrome).","Serous carcinoma: older women, atrophic endometrium, TP53 mutation, spreads early through the peritoneum, poor prognosis; both types present with postmenopausal bleeding.","Endometrial and endocervical polyps: benign overgrowths of glands and stroma causing intermenstrual or abnormal bleeding."]},{"h":"Myometrium","points":["Leiomyoma (fibroid): most common benign tumor in women; estrogen-sensitive smooth muscle, well circumscribed, whorled; often multiple; more common in Black women; grows in pregnancy, shrinks after menopause.","Fibroids cause heavy bleeding with iron deficiency anemia, pelvic pressure, and infertility (submucosal type).","Leiomyosarcoma: arises on its own in older women as a single large soft mass with necrosis, atypia, and many mitoses; leiomyomas do not transform into it."]},{"h":"Ovary","points":["Surface epithelial tumors (serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, Brenner) make up most ovarian tumors and most ovarian cancers.","High-grade serous carcinoma: TP53 in nearly all, BRCA1 or BRCA2 in many; often begins in the fallopian tube fimbria.","Serous carcinoma shows psammoma bodies, raised CA-125, ascites, and peritoneal and omental spread.","Germ cell: mature cystic teratoma (most common), struma ovarii (thyroid tissue, can cause hyperthyroidism), dysgerminoma (clear cells, LDH), yolk sac tumor (Schiller-Duval bodies, AFP).","Sex cord-stromal: granulosa cell tumor (estrogen, Call-Exner bodies), Sertoli-Leydig (androgen, virilization), fibroma with ascites and pleural effusion (Meigs syndrome).","Krukenberg tumor: bilateral ovarian metastasis of mucin-filled signet ring cells, usually from the stomach."]},{"h":"Prostate","points":["Adenocarcinoma risks: age, Black race, family history, BRCA2.","Arises in the peripheral (posterior) zone, so it can be felt on rectal exam and rarely causes early urinary obstruction.","Gleason score adds the two most common gland patterns (each graded 1 to 5); PSA rises, though BPH and prostatitis raise it too.","Spreads through the vertebral venous plexus to the lumbar spine and pelvis, making osteoblastic metastases with bone pain and raised alkaline phosphatase."]},{"h":"Penis and testis","points":["Penile squamous cell carcinoma: HPV 16, uncircumcised penis, phimosis, smoking; in situ forms are Bowen disease, erythroplasia of Queyrat, and bowenoid papulosis.","Testicular tumors: most are germ cell, with isochromosome 12p and germ cell neoplasia in situ as precursor; painless firm mass that does not transilluminate; risks are cryptorchidism, Klinefelter syndrome, gonadal dysgenesis, and a prior testicular tumor.","Seminoma: most common, uniform clear cells with lymphocytes, placental alkaline phosphatase, very radiosensitive, good prognosis; AFP stays normal.","Yolk sac tumor raises AFP; choriocarcinoma raises hCG; embryonal carcinoma is aggressive and hemorrhagic.","Leydig cell tumor: Reinke crystals; androgen causes precocious puberty in boys, estrogen causes gynecomastia in men.","Lymphoma is the most common testicular tumor in men over 60."]},{"h":"Vagina and vulva","points":["Vaginal squamous cell carcinoma: older women, HPV, arises from vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia.","Clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina: young women exposed to DES in utero.","Sarcoma botryoides (embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma): grape-like vaginal mass in girls under about 5, desmin positive.","HPV-related vulvar carcinoma: younger women, arises from usual (HPV-related) VIN.","HPV-independent vulvar carcinoma: older women with lichen sclerosus, arises from differentiated VIN with TP53 mutation.","Extramammary Paget disease of the vulva: intraepithelial adenocarcinoma cells in the epidermis, usually with no underlying carcinoma, unlike Paget disease of the breast."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Reproductive dysplasia and neoplasms compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia","Persistent HPV 16 or 18, many partners, early intercourse, smoking, HIV","E6 degrades p53, E7 inactivates RB in transformation zone cells","Koilocytes; abnormal Pap; CIN 1 to 3 by thickness","Progression to invasive carcinoma; most CIN 1 regresses"],["Invasive cervical carcinoma","Same as CIN, no screening","Squamous cell (most) or adenocarcinoma invades stroma","Postcoital or irregular bleeding","Ureteral obstruction, hydronephrosis, renal failure"],["Endometrial hyperplasia","Obesity, anovulation, PCOS, estrogen-only therapy, granulosa cell tumor","Unopposed estrogen, PTEN loss","Abnormal or postmenopausal bleeding","Atypical hyperplasia leads to endometrioid carcinoma"],["Leiomyoma (fibroid)","Reproductive age, Black women, estrogen","Benign estrogen-sensitive smooth muscle proliferation","Whorled circumscribed masses, heavy bleeding, pressure","Anemia, infertility, pregnancy complications"],["Leiomyosarcoma","Older women, arises de novo","Malignant smooth muscle tumor","Single large soft mass with necrosis, atypia, high mitoses","Hematogenous spread, often to lung"],["Prostate carcinoma","Age, Black race, family history, BRCA2","Adenocarcinoma of the peripheral zone","Hard nodule on rectal exam, raised PSA","Osteoblastic spine and pelvis metastases, high alkaline phosphatase"],["Ovarian tumors","BRCA1 and BRCA2, Lynch syndrome, nulliparity, family history","Surface epithelial, germ cell, or sex cord-stromal origin","Adnexal mass, bloating, ascites, CA-125 in serous carcinoma","Peritoneal and omental spread, torsion, hormone effects"],["Squamous cell carcinoma of penis","HPV 16, uncircumcised, phimosis, smoking","HPV-driven or chronic inflammation-driven squamous neoplasia","Ulcer or mass on glans or foreskin","Inguinal node spread"],["Testicular tumors","Cryptorchidism, Klinefelter syndrome, gonadal dysgenesis","Germ cell tumors with isochromosome 12p from germ cell neoplasia in situ","Painless firm mass, does not transilluminate","Para-aortic node spread; hematogenous spread in choriocarcinoma"],["Vaginal carcinoma","HPV (squamous), in utero DES (clear cell)","Squamous cell from VAIN; clear cell from vaginal adenosis","Bleeding, vaginal mass","Local spread to bladder and rectum"],["Vulvar carcinoma","HPV in younger women; lichen sclerosus in older women","Usual VIN (HPV) or differentiated VIN (TP53)","Itchy plaque, ulcer, or mass on the vulva","Inguinal node spread"],["Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia","HPV, smoking, immunodeficiency; lichen sclerosus for differentiated type","Usual VIN is HPV-driven; differentiated VIN has TP53 mutation","White, red, or pigmented vulvar plaques, itching","Progression to vulvar squamous cell carcinoma"]]},{"title":"Ovarian tumors by cell of origin","cols":["Group","Tumor","Key feature","Marker or hormone"],"rows":[["Surface epithelial","Serous carcinoma","Psammoma bodies; high-grade type with TP53, BRCA1 or BRCA2, tubal origin","CA-125"],["Surface epithelial","Mucinous tumor","Large multiloculated cyst of mucin-secreting cells","None classic"],["Surface epithelial","Endometrioid and clear cell carcinoma","Linked to endometriosis","None classic"],["Surface epithelial","Brenner tumor","Nests of urothelium-like cells with coffee-bean nuclei","None classic"],["Germ cell","Mature cystic teratoma","Hair, sebum, teeth; struma ovarii is thyroid tissue","Thyroid hormone if struma ovarii"],["Germ cell","Dysgerminoma","Sheets of uniform clear cells with lymphocytes","LDH"],["Germ cell","Yolk sac tumor","Schiller-Duval bodies, young females","AFP"],["Germ cell","Choriocarcinoma (ovarian)","Trophoblast without villi","hCG"],["Sex cord-stromal","Granulosa cell tumor","Call-Exner bodies; precocious puberty or endometrial hyperplasia","Estrogen, inhibin"],["Sex cord-stromal","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor","Virilization, hirsutism","Androgen"],["Sex cord-stromal","Fibroma and thecoma","Fibroma with ascites and pleural effusion, usually right-sided (Meigs); thecoma may make estrogen","Estrogen (thecoma)"],["Metastatic","Krukenberg tumor","Bilateral, mucin-filled signet ring cells, often gastric primary","None classic"]]},{"title":"Testicular tumors","cols":["Tumor","Typical patient","Histology","Marker"],"rows":[["Seminoma","Men about 30 to 40","Uniform large clear cells, fibrous septa with lymphocytes","Placental alkaline phosphatase; AFP normal"],["Embryonal carcinoma","Men about 20 to 30","Primitive sheets and glands, hemorrhage and necrosis","May raise hCG or AFP"],["Yolk sac tumor","Boys under about 3, also in mixed adult tumors","Schiller-Duval bodies","AFP"],["Choriocarcinoma","Young men","Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast, hemorrhage","Very high hCG (gynecomastia, hyperthyroidism)"],["Teratoma","Children (benign) and men (malignant potential)","Tissues from more than one germ layer","Usually none when pure"],["Leydig cell tumor","Boys and men","Golden-brown cells with Reinke crystals","Androgen or estrogen"],["Lymphoma","Men over 60","Diffuse large B cell lymphoma, often bilateral","None"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"E6 with p53, E7 with RB","text":"Pair them in order: the lower number, E6, takes the suppressor with a number in its name (p53); E7 takes RB. Losing both brakes lets infected cells divide and pile up mutations."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Two endometrial cancers, two backgrounds","text":"Endometrioid grows on a background of too much estrogen (hyperplasia, younger, obese). Serous grows on a thin, atrophic endometrium in an older woman, carries TP53, and spreads fast."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Markers by tissue","text":"Yolk sac makes AFP the way fetal yolk sac does. Trophoblast makes hCG the way placenta does. Seminoma and dysgerminoma are twins: clear cells, lymphocytes, normal AFP."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Testis drains to the abdomen","text":"The testis descends from the posterior abdominal wall, so its lymph goes to para-aortic nodes. The scrotal skin drains to superficial inguinal nodes."}],"highYield":["Cervical carcinoma kills through ureteral obstruction and renal failure.","Atypical endometrial hyperplasia is the precursor of endometrioid carcinoma; serous carcinoma carries TP53 and arises in atrophic endometrium.","Leiomyomas do not become leiomyosarcomas.","Prostate cancer arises in the peripheral zone and sends osteoblastic metastases to the lumbar spine through the vertebral venous plexus.","High-grade serous ovarian carcinoma: TP53, BRCA1 or BRCA2, tubal origin, CA-125, psammoma bodies.","Granulosa cell tumor makes estrogen (Call-Exner bodies); Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor virilizes.","Seminoma has placental alkaline phosphatase and normal AFP; yolk sac tumor raises AFP; choriocarcinoma raises hCG.","In utero DES exposure leads to clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina."],"pitfalls":["Extramammary Paget disease of the vulva usually has no underlying carcinoma; Paget disease of the breast nearly always does.","Benign prostatic hyperplasia arises in the transition zone; prostate carcinoma arises in the peripheral zone.","Krukenberg tumor is bilateral metastatic signet ring carcinoma; a primary mucinous ovarian tumor is usually one-sided.","Testicular masses that transilluminate are fluid (hydrocele, spermatocele); a solid tumor does not.","Struma ovarii causes hyperthyroidism from ovarian thyroid tissue, while a mole causes it through hCG."],"dumps":[{"t":"Ovarian tumor family tree","p":"On blank paper, draw a tree with three branches: surface epithelial, germ cell, and sex cord-stromal. Put each ovarian tumor on its branch with one key feature and its marker or hormone, then add Krukenberg tumor off to the side.","l":["Surface epithelial: serous (psammoma bodies, CA-125), mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, Brenner","High-grade serous: TP53, BRCA1 or BRCA2, begins in the tubal fimbria","Germ cell: mature cystic teratoma (most common), struma ovarii (hyperthyroidism)","Dysgerminoma: clear cells, LDH; yolk sac tumor: Schiller-Duval bodies, AFP","Granulosa cell: estrogen, Call-Exner bodies, endometrial hyperplasia","Sertoli-Leydig: androgen, virilization","Fibroma with ascites and pleural effusion is Meigs syndrome","Krukenberg: bilateral signet ring metastasis, usually gastric"]},{"t":"HPV to cervical cancer","p":"Draw the cervix with its transformation zone. Trace how high-risk HPV leads from infection to CIN 1, CIN 2, CIN 3, and invasive carcinoma, and show how lateral spread harms the kidneys.","l":["Transformation zone is where columnar cells undergo squamous metaplasia","High-risk HPV 16 and 18 infect immature metaplastic cells","E6 degrades p53; E7 inactivates RB","Koilocytes mark HPV infection","CIN 1 lower third (LSIL); CIN 2 and 3 thicker (HSIL)","Most CIN 1 regresses; persistent high-risk HPV drives progression","Invasive carcinoma is mostly squamous; presents with postcoital bleeding","Parametrial spread blocks ureters: hydronephrosis and renal failure"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Disorders of the Female Reproductive Tract"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"section on papillomaviruses"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-10","RE-12","RE-13-F1","RE-13-G3","RE-13-C1","RE-13-E6","RE-13-K6"]},"RE-13-K":{"id":"RE-13-K","summary":"Genitourinary infections include the classic sexually transmitted infections, bacterial vaginosis, and toxic shock syndrome. Exams test the organism behind each ulcer or discharge, the bedside tests that separate the vaginitides, syphilis serology, and the late or neonatal complications.","notes":[{"h":"Genital ulcers","points":["Primary syphilis (Treponema pallidum): single painless indurated chancre with a clean base and painless rubbery nodes.","Chancroid (Haemophilus ducreyi, gram-negative coccobacillus): painful soft ulcer with ragged undermined edges and gray-yellow exudate; painful inguinal nodes that may suppurate.","Genital herpes (HSV-2 mostly, HSV-1 rising): painful grouped vesicles on a red base that break into shallow ulcers.","Primary herpes adds fever, malaise, tender nodes, and dysuria; the virus then hides in sacral sensory ganglia and reactivates as milder outbreaks.","Tzanck smear shows multinucleated giant cells; neonatal herpes is mostly acquired during vaginal delivery.","Lymphogranuloma venereum (C. trachomatis L1 to L3): small painless ulcer, then painful inguinal buboes; can cause proctocolitis and strictures."]},{"h":"Vaginal discharge","points":["Normal vaginal pH is about 3.8 to 4.5, kept low by lactic acid from lactobacilli.","Bacterial vaginosis: lactobacilli are replaced by Gardnerella vaginalis and anaerobes; thin gray fishy discharge, pH above 4.5, positive whiff test, clue cells, few neutrophils.","Trichomoniasis: flagellated protozoan; frothy yellow-green malodorous discharge, strawberry cervix, pH above 4.5, motile trophozoites and many neutrophils on wet mount.","Candidal vaginitis: thick white curdy discharge, itching, pH normal, negative whiff test, pseudohyphae on KOH prep."]},{"h":"Chlamydia and gonorrhea","points":["C. trachomatis (serovars D to K) is obligate intracellular: infectious elementary body outside the cell, replicating reticulate body inside.","Chlamydia is often silent; it causes urethritis, cervicitis, epididymitis, PID, and reactive arthritis.","Neonatal chlamydia: conjunctivitis at about 1 to 2 weeks, then afebrile staccato-cough pneumonia at 1 to 3 months.","N. gonorrhoeae: purulent urethral discharge with intracellular gram-negative diplococci in neutrophils; cervicitis, PID, and neonatal conjunctivitis in the first few days.","Disseminated gonococcal infection: septic arthritis (often the knee), or tenosynovitis with pustular skin lesions and polyarthralgia.","Pili undergo antigenic variation, so reinfection is common; C5 to C9 (membrane attack complex) deficiency causes recurrent Neisseria infection."]},{"h":"HPV and nongonococcal urethritis","points":["HPV 6 and 11 cause condylomata acuminata: soft cauliflower-like warts with koilocytes.","Condylomata lata of secondary syphilis are flat, moist, gray plaques full of spirochetes.","High-risk HPV (16, 18) causes cervical, vulvar, vaginal, penile, anal, and oropharyngeal carcinoma.","Nongonococcal urethritis: C. trachomatis most often, then Mycoplasma genitalium, Ureaplasma urealyticum, and Trichomonas vaginalis.","Reactive arthritis after urethritis (or enteric infection): arthritis, conjunctivitis, and urethritis, mostly in HLA-B27-positive men."]},{"h":"Syphilis stages and tests","points":["Primary: painless chancre about 3 weeks after exposure; heals on its own.","Secondary: rash on trunk, palms, and soles, condylomata lata, generalized lymphadenopathy, fever, patchy hair loss.","Latent: no signs, positive serology. Tertiary (years later): gummas, aortitis of the ascending aorta (vasa vasorum endarteritis, aneurysm, aortic regurgitation), tabes dorsalis.","Tabes dorsalis damages dorsal columns and roots: lost proprioception, positive Romberg sign, shooting pains, Argyll Robertson pupils.","Nontreponemal tests (VDRL, RPR) detect anticardiolipin antibody; titers track activity and fall with treatment; false positives in pregnancy, SLE, viral infections.","Treponemal tests (FTA-ABS) detect antibody to the organism and stay positive for life, so they confirm infection without showing activity.","Congenital syphilis: snuffles, rash, saddle nose, Hutchinson teeth, mulberry molars, saber shins, sensorineural deafness."]},{"h":"Toxic shock syndrome","points":["S. aureus makes TSST-1, a superantigen that links MHC class II to the T cell receptor Vbeta region outside the peptide groove.","Up to about 20 percent of T cells fire at once, releasing IL-2, IFN-gamma, IL-1, and TNF in a cytokine storm.","Settings: retained or superabsorbent tampons, nasal packing, surgical wounds, postpartum infection.","Findings: fever of 38.9 °C (102 °F) or higher, hypotension, diffuse sunburn-like rash, desquamation of palms and soles 1 to 2 weeks later, and injury to three or more organ systems."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Genitourinary infections compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Chancroid","Haemophilus ducreyi, sexual contact","Gram-negative coccobacillus invades genital skin","Painful ragged ulcer with gray exudate, painful suppurative nodes","Bubo rupture, easier HIV transmission"],["Bacterial vaginosis","Gardnerella vaginalis and anaerobes, new partners, douching","Loss of lactobacilli raises pH; anaerobes make amines","Thin gray fishy discharge, pH above 4.5, clue cells, few neutrophils","Preterm birth, PID, higher STI acquisition"],["Chlamydia","C. trachomatis D to K (L1 to L3 for LGV), young age, multiple partners","Obligate intracellular; elementary body infects, reticulate body replicates","Often silent; urethritis, cervicitis","PID, infertility, ectopic pregnancy, reactive arthritis, neonatal conjunctivitis and pneumonia"],["Gonorrhea","N. gonorrhoeae, multiple partners","Pili attach and vary antigens; IgA protease","Purulent discharge, intracellular gram-negative diplococci","PID, septic arthritis, neonatal conjunctivitis, disseminated infection"],["Herpes simplex virus","HSV-2 (and HSV-1), sexual contact","Lytic infection of epithelium, latency in sacral ganglia","Painful grouped vesicles and ulcers, multinucleated giant cells","Recurrence, neonatal herpes, aseptic meningitis"],["Human papillomavirus","HPV 6, 11 (warts); 16, 18 (cancer)","E6 and E7 block p53 and RB in high-risk types","Cauliflower warts with koilocytes","Cervical, vulvar, vaginal, penile, anal, oropharyngeal carcinoma"],["Nongonococcal urethritis","C. trachomatis, Mycoplasma genitalium, Ureaplasma, Trichomonas","Urethral infection without gonococci","Dysuria, scant discharge","Epididymitis, reactive arthritis in HLA-B27-positive men"],["Syphilis","Treponema pallidum, sexual or transplacental spread","Spirochete spreads in blood; endarteritis of small vessels","Painless chancre, then palm and sole rash, condylomata lata","Gummas, aortitis, tabes dorsalis, congenital syphilis"],["Toxic shock syndrome","S. aureus with tampons, nasal packing, wounds","TSST-1 superantigen causes polyclonal T cell cytokine release","High fever, hypotension, diffuse rash, later desquamation","Shock, multiorgan failure"],["Trichomoniasis","Trichomonas vaginalis, sexual contact","Flagellated protozoan damages squamous epithelium","Frothy yellow-green discharge, strawberry cervix, motile trophozoites","Preterm birth, higher HIV acquisition"]]},{"title":"Vaginitis at the bedside","cols":["Test","Bacterial vaginosis","Trichomoniasis","Candidal vaginitis"],"rows":[["Discharge","Thin, gray-white, fishy","Frothy, yellow-green, foul","Thick, white, curdy"],["pH (normal 3.8 to 4.5)","Above 4.5","Above 4.5","Normal"],["Whiff test (KOH)","Positive fishy amine odor","Often positive","Negative"],["Microscopy","Clue cells, few neutrophils","Motile flagellated trophozoites, many neutrophils","Pseudohyphae and budding yeast on KOH"],["Other clue","No inflammation of vulva","Strawberry cervix","Itching, vulvar redness"]]},{"title":"Syphilis serology","cols":["Test type","Examples","What it detects","Behavior"],"rows":[["Nontreponemal","VDRL, RPR","Anticardiolipin antibody","Titer tracks activity, falls with treatment; false positives in pregnancy, SLE, viral infection"],["Treponemal","FTA-ABS","Antibody to T. pallidum antigens","Confirms infection, stays positive for life"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"You do cry with ducreyi","text":"Chancroid from Haemophilus ducreyi is the painful ulcer; the syphilis chancre is painless."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"VDRL false positives: VDRL","text":"Viral infection (mononucleosis, hepatitis), Drugs (injection drug use), Rheumatic fever and rheumatoid arthritis, Lupus and leprosy. Pregnancy also gives false positives."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Can't see, can't pee, can't climb a tree","text":"Reactive arthritis: conjunctivitis, urethritis, and arthritis, often after chlamydia in HLA-B27-positive men."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Acuminata versus lata","text":"Acuminata means pointed: raised cauliflower HPV warts. Lata means broad: flat, moist plaques of secondary syphilis that are full of spirochetes."}],"highYield":["Painful ulcer with painful suppurative nodes is chancroid; painless indurated ulcer is syphilis.","Clue cells and a positive whiff test with few neutrophils mean bacterial vaginosis.","Recurrent Neisseria infections point to terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency.","Chlamydia is the most common cause of nongonococcal urethritis and is often silent in women.","Tertiary syphilis aortitis hits the ascending aorta through endarteritis of the vasa vasorum.","FTA-ABS stays positive for life; RPR or VDRL titer follows treatment response.","TSST-1 is a superantigen that bypasses normal antigen processing and activates many T cells at once.","Hutchinson teeth, saddle nose, and saber shins mark late congenital syphilis."],"pitfalls":["Trichomoniasis and bacterial vaginosis both raise pH above 4.5; neutrophils and motile organisms point to trichomonas, clue cells to BV.","LGV ulcer is painless and small; the painful part is the later inguinal buboes.","Gonococcal neonatal conjunctivitis starts in the first few days; chlamydial conjunctivitis starts at about 1 to 2 weeks.","A positive RPR with negative FTA-ABS suggests a false positive, as in pregnancy or SLE.","Menstrual toxic shock from S. aureus usually has negative blood cultures, because the disease comes from toxin."],"dumps":[{"t":"Syphilis timeline","p":"Draw a timeline from exposure to tertiary syphilis. Mark each stage with its findings, then add a box comparing nontreponemal and treponemal tests and a list of congenital syphilis findings.","l":["Primary: painless indurated chancre about 3 weeks after exposure","Secondary: palm and sole rash, condylomata lata, lymphadenopathy","Latent: no signs, positive serology","Tertiary: gummas, ascending aortitis, tabes dorsalis, Argyll Robertson pupils","VDRL and RPR: anticardiolipin, titer tracks activity, false positives","FTA-ABS: specific, stays positive for life","Congenital: snuffles, Hutchinson teeth, mulberry molars, saddle nose, saber shins, deafness"]},{"t":"Vaginitis and ulcer table","p":"On blank paper, build two tables. First, compare bacterial vaginosis, trichomoniasis, and candidal vaginitis by discharge, pH, whiff test, and microscopy. Second, compare syphilis, chancroid, herpes, and LGV by organism, ulcer pain, and nodes.","l":["BV: thin gray fishy, pH above 4.5, whiff positive, clue cells","Trichomonas: frothy green, pH above 4.5, motile trophozoites, strawberry cervix","Candida: thick white, normal pH, whiff negative, pseudohyphae","Syphilis: T. pallidum, painless indurated chancre, painless nodes","Chancroid: H. ducreyi, painful ragged ulcer, painful suppurative nodes","Herpes: HSV-2, painful grouped vesicles, tender nodes","LGV: C. trachomatis L1 to L3, painless small ulcer, then painful buboes"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Spirochetes and Other Spiral Microorganisms"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"The Neisseriae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"section on sexually transmitted infections"}],"videos":[],"related":["RE-11","RE-12","RE-13-B2","RE-13-B6","RE-13-B8","RE-13-J1"]},"UR-1":{"id":"UR-1","summary":"How the kidneys, ureters, and bladder develop from intermediate mesoderm and the cloaca. Exam questions take one developmental step and ask what goes wrong when it fails: renal agenesis, horseshoe kidney, duplex ureter, urachal remnants, and posterior urethral valves.","notes":[{"h":"Three kidneys in sequence","points":["All three come from intermediate mesoderm and appear in order from head to tail.","Pronephros: week 4, in the neck region, never works, and regresses.","Mesonephros: appears in week 4 as the pronephros regresses, works briefly as an interim kidney in the first trimester; its duct (mesonephric or Wolffian duct) persists in males as genital ducts.","Metanephros: appears in week 5 and becomes the permanent kidney; it starts making urine late in the first trimester, and that urine becomes most of the amniotic fluid later in pregnancy."]},{"h":"Reciprocal induction","points":["The ureteric bud grows out of the mesonephric duct and pushes into the metanephric mesenchyme (metanephric blastema).","The bud tells the mesenchyme to form nephrons; the mesenchyme tells the bud to branch (GDNF from mesenchyme acts on the RET receptor of the bud).","Ureteric bud forms the collecting system: collecting ducts, minor and major calyces, renal pelvis, ureter.","Metanephric mesenchyme forms the nephron: Bowman capsule, proximal tubule, loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule.","If the bud never reaches the mesenchyme, no kidney forms (renal agenesis). Bilateral agenesis causes oligohydramnios and Potter sequence (pulmonary hypoplasia, flat face, limb deformities).","The ureteropelvic junction is the last part to canalize, so it is the most common site of congenital obstruction and hydronephrosis."]},{"h":"Ascent and fusion","points":["The kidneys form in the pelvis and ascend to the lumbar region, picking up new arteries from the aorta as they rise; lower ones normally regress.","Persistent lower vessels become accessory renal arteries; failed ascent leaves a pelvic kidney.","Horseshoe kidney: the lower poles fuse, and the fused isthmus catches under the inferior mesenteric artery, so the kidneys stay low.","Horseshoe kidney is often silent but raises the risk of ureteropelvic obstruction, hydronephrosis, stones, and infection; it is linked to Turner syndrome."]},{"h":"Ureteric bud splitting","points":["Early splitting of one bud gives a bifid (partial) ureter that joins before the bladder.","Two separate buds give a complete duplex ureter with two orifices.","In complete duplication the upper pole ureter opens lower and more medial and tends to obstruct (often with a ureterocele); the lower pole ureter opens higher and lateral and tends to reflux.","In a girl, an ectopic ureter that opens below the urethral sphincter (urethra or vagina) causes constant dribbling despite normal voiding."]},{"h":"Cloaca and allantois","points":["The urorectal septum divides the cloaca into the urogenital sinus in front and the anorectal canal behind (done by about week 7).","The urogenital sinus forms the bladder and urethra. The trigone comes from the incorporated mesonephric ducts.","The allantois connects the bladder apex to the umbilicus and normally closes into the urachus, then the median umbilical ligament.","Patent urachus: urine leaks from the umbilicus. Urachal cyst: a closed pocket that can become infected. Urachal remnants carry a risk of adenocarcinoma."]},{"h":"Posterior urethral valves","points":["Membrane-like folds in the posterior (prostatic) urethra of male infants block outflow.","Backpressure gives a thick-walled, distended bladder and bilateral hydronephrosis.","Low fetal urine output causes oligohydramnios and can lead to pulmonary hypoplasia."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Who forms what","cols":["Embryonic source","Adult structures"],"rows":[["Ureteric bud (from mesonephric duct)","Ureter, renal pelvis, major and minor calyces, collecting ducts"],["Metanephric mesenchyme","Bowman capsule, proximal tubule, loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule"],["Mesonephric ducts incorporated into the bladder","Trigone"],["Urogenital sinus (endoderm)","Rest of the bladder lining, urethra"],["Allantois","Urachus, then median umbilical ligament"]]},{"title":"Developmental defects","cols":["Defect","What failed","Key finding"],"rows":[["Renal agenesis","Ureteric bud does not reach or induce mesenchyme","Bilateral: oligohydramnios, Potter sequence; unilateral: other kidney enlarges"],["Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","Late or incomplete canalization","Hydronephrosis with a normal-caliber ureter"],["Horseshoe kidney","Lower poles fuse; ascent stopped by inferior mesenteric artery","Low kidneys; obstruction, stones, infection; Turner syndrome"],["Bifid or duplex ureter","Bud splits early, or two buds form","Upper pole ureter obstructs, lower pole ureter refluxes"],["Patent urachus","Allantois does not close","Urine from the umbilicus"],["Posterior urethral valves","Abnormal folds in the male posterior urethra","Distended bladder, bilateral hydronephrosis, oligohydramnios"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Bud builds the plumbing","text":"The ureteric bud makes everything that carries finished urine (collecting ducts to ureter). The mesenchyme makes everything that makes urine (capsule to distal tubule)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pro, meso, meta","text":"The Greek prefixes mean first, middle, and after. Only the after kidney (metanephros, week 5) stays."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Upper obstructs, lower refluxes","text":"In a duplex system the upper pole ureter enters the bladder lower and obstructs; the lower pole ureter enters higher and refluxes. The two cross."}],"highYield":["Metanephros appears in week 5 and becomes the permanent kidney.","Bilateral renal agenesis means no fetal urine, oligohydramnios, and Potter sequence with pulmonary hypoplasia.","The ureteropelvic junction is the most common site of congenital urinary obstruction.","Horseshoe kidney is trapped below the inferior mesenteric artery and is linked to Turner syndrome.","Fluid leaking from a newborn's umbilicus that is urine means a patent urachus.","Posterior urethral valves are the classic cause of bladder outlet obstruction in a male newborn.","Constant dribbling with normal voiding in a girl suggests an ectopic ureter below the sphincter."],"pitfalls":["Collecting ducts come from the ureteric bud, while the distal convoluted tubule comes from mesenchyme. The dividing line is between the distal tubule and the collecting duct.","The artery that traps a horseshoe kidney is the inferior mesenteric artery; the superior mesenteric artery arises higher and is out of the way.","When a fetal anomaly is the cause, oligohydramnios points to a fetal urine problem (agenesis, valves) and polyhydramnios points to a swallowing or gut problem. Ruptured membranes are the other common cause of oligohydramnios.","A patent urachus drains urine; a patent vitelline duct drains meconium or bowel contents from the umbilicus."],"dumps":[{"t":"Ureteric bud and mesenchyme","p":"Draw the ureteric bud growing into the metanephric mesenchyme. Label the adult structures each one forms, and write what happens if the bud never arrives.","l":["Ureteric bud grows from the mesonephric duct","Bud forms ureter, pelvis, calyces, collecting ducts","Mesenchyme forms Bowman capsule through distal convoluted tubule","Induction is reciprocal (each tells the other to develop)","No bud contact means renal agenesis","Bilateral agenesis gives oligohydramnios and Potter sequence","Metanephros appears in week 5"]},{"t":"Congenital defect table","p":"Make a table of horseshoe kidney, duplex ureter, patent urachus, and posterior urethral valves. For each, write the step that failed and the finding you would see.","l":["Horseshoe: fused lower poles caught under the inferior mesenteric artery","Horseshoe: risk of obstruction, stones, infection; Turner syndrome","Duplex: early bud split or two buds","Duplex: upper pole ureter obstructs, lower pole ureter refluxes","Patent urachus: allantois fails to close, urine at the umbilicus","Valves: male posterior urethra, distended bladder","Valves: bilateral hydronephrosis and oligohydramnios"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney (section on congenital and developmental anomalies)"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-3","UR-12-E3","UR-12-E4","UR-12-C1"]},"UR-2":{"id":"UR-2","summary":"The microscopic structure of the nephron, the juxtaglomerular apparatus, and the bladder wall. Exam questions link structure to function: the filtration barrier to proteinuria, tubule cell type to job and to ischemic injury, loop length to concentrating ability, and urothelium to a stretchy, watertight lining.","notes":[{"h":"Glomerular filtration barrier","points":["Layer 1, fenestrated endothelium: pores hold back blood cells.","Layer 2, glomerular basement membrane: type IV collagen and laminin, with heparan sulfate giving a strong negative charge.","Layer 3, podocyte foot processes: the slit diaphragm between them (nephrin) is the final size filter.","The barrier filters by size and by charge; albumin is negatively charged and is repelled.","Loss of the negative charge or effacement of foot processes lets albumin through (selective albuminuria, as in minimal change disease)."]},{"h":"Juxtaglomerular apparatus and mesangium","points":["Juxtaglomerular cells: modified smooth muscle in the afferent arteriole wall that store and secrete renin.","Macula densa: tall, crowded cells at the end of the thick ascending limb where it touches its own glomerulus; they sense tubular NaCl.","Extraglomerular mesangial (lacis) cells sit between the afferent and efferent arterioles and the macula densa.","Intraglomerular mesangial cells support the capillary loops, contract, make matrix, and phagocytose trapped proteins and immune complexes."]},{"h":"Tubule epithelium","points":["Proximal tubule: simple cuboidal with a tall brush border (microvilli), many mitochondria, basal infoldings, and pink cytoplasm.","Thin limbs: simple squamous; the descending thin limb is permeable to water.","Thick ascending limb and distal tubule: cuboidal with no brush border, so the lumen looks wider and cleaner.","Collecting duct: cuboidal to columnar with clear cell borders; principal cells handle Na+, K+, and water, intercalated cells handle acid and bicarbonate.","The proximal tubule straight part and the medullary thick ascending limb use the most ATP and get the least oxygen, so they die first in ischemic acute tubular necrosis."]},{"h":"Cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons","points":["Cortical nephrons (most nephrons): glomeruli in the outer cortex, short loops that barely enter the medulla, peritubular capillaries.","Juxtamedullary nephrons: glomeruli near the corticomedullary junction, long loops deep into the inner medulla, efferent arterioles that form the vasa recta.","Long loops build the steep medullary osmotic gradient, so juxtamedullary nephrons drive urine concentration."]},{"h":"Bladder wall","points":["Urothelium (transitional epithelium) lines the calyces, pelvis, ureters, bladder, and upper urethra.","Surface umbrella cells are large, sometimes binucleate, and carry stiff uroplakin plaques on their apical membrane.","Tight junctions and uroplakin plaques make the lining impermeable to urine.","On filling, the cells flatten and stored membrane vesicles fuse with the surface, so the lining stretches without tearing.","Under the urothelium: lamina propria, then the detrusor muscle in interwoven smooth muscle layers, then adventitia (serosa over the dome)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Nephron segments under the microscope","cols":["Segment","Epithelium","Key features","Main job"],"rows":[["Proximal tubule","Simple cuboidal","Brush border, many mitochondria","Bulk reabsorption of Na+, water, glucose, amino acids, bicarbonate"],["Descending thin limb","Simple squamous","Water permeable","Concentrates tubular fluid"],["Thick ascending limb","Cuboidal","No brush border, many mitochondria","NaCl reabsorption without water; macula densa at its end"],["Distal convoluted tubule","Cuboidal","No brush border, wider lumen","NaCl and Ca2+ reabsorption"],["Collecting duct","Cuboidal to columnar","Principal and intercalated cells","Fine control of Na+, K+, water, acid"]]},{"title":"Two nephron types","cols":["Feature","Cortical","Juxtamedullary"],"rows":[["Glomerulus position","Outer cortex","Near the corticomedullary junction"],["Loop of Henle","Short","Long, into the inner medulla"],["Capillaries after efferent arteriole","Peritubular capillaries","Vasa recta"],["Share of nephrons","Most","Minority"],["Role in concentrating urine","Small","Large"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Three layers, three filters","text":"Endothelium stops cells, the basement membrane is the main charge barrier, and the slit diaphragm is the final size filter. Lose the charge and albumin slips through."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Brush means busy","text":"A brush border plus packed mitochondria means a hard-working absorber (proximal tubule), and hard workers are the first to die when oxygen runs out."}],"highYield":["Heparan sulfate gives the basement membrane its negative charge; losing it causes albuminuria.","Podocyte foot process effacement is the lesion of minimal change disease.","Renin comes from juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole; NaCl is sensed by the macula densa.","IgA nephropathy deposits sit in the mesangium, where mesangial cells take up trapped immune complexes.","Proximal straight tubule and medullary thick ascending limb are most vulnerable to ischemia.","Juxtamedullary nephrons with long loops make concentrated urine possible.","Umbrella cells with uroplakin plaques keep urine from leaking back into tissue."],"pitfalls":["The macula densa is part of the distal thick ascending limb (a tubule). The renin-secreting cells are in the afferent arteriole wall (a vessel).","Brush border means proximal tubule; a cuboidal tubule without one is distal tubule or thick ascending limb.","Urothelium is not stratified squamous; its surface cells are dome-shaped umbrella cells that flatten when stretched.","Vasa recta come from efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons; cortical nephrons feed peritubular capillaries."],"dumps":[{"t":"Filtration barrier and JGA","p":"Draw a glomerular capillary loop with its three barrier layers, then add the afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole, and macula densa. Label each part and write its job.","l":["Fenestrated endothelium holds back cells","Basement membrane: type IV collagen, negative heparan sulfate","Podocyte foot processes with slit diaphragm","Loss of charge or foot processes gives albuminuria","Juxtaglomerular cells in afferent arteriole secrete renin","Macula densa in the distal thick ascending limb senses NaCl","Mesangial cells support loops and clear immune complexes"]},{"t":"Nephron segment table","p":"Make a table of the proximal tubule, thin limb, thick ascending limb, distal tubule, and collecting duct. Write the epithelium, one feature you would see on a slide, and the main job of each.","l":["Proximal: cuboidal, brush border, many mitochondria","Thin limb: simple squamous","Thick ascending limb: cuboidal, no brush border, water impermeable","Distal tubule: cuboidal, no brush border","Collecting duct: principal and intercalated cells","Proximal tubule and medullary thick limb are most vulnerable to ischemia","Juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops and feed the vasa recta"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Urinary System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney (section on glomerular diseases)"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-5","UR-6","UR-12-A3","UR-12-B1"]},"UR-3":{"id":"UR-3","summary":"Where the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra sit, what touches them, and how they are wrapped. Exam questions use these relations to predict where stones lodge, which organ is hurt in trauma, and why women get more bladder infections.","notes":[{"h":"Kidney position","points":["The kidneys are retroperitoneal, lying beside the vertebral column from about T12 to L3.","The right kidney sits a little lower than the left because the liver sits above it.","The hilum faces medially at about L1; the suprarenal glands cap the upper poles.","Posterior relations: diaphragm and 12th rib above, psoas major medially, quadratus lumborum behind, transversus abdominis laterally.","The subcostal, iliohypogastric, and ilioinguinal nerves run behind the kidney."]},{"h":"Anterior relations","points":["Right kidney: liver, descending (second part) duodenum, right colic flexure.","Left kidney: stomach, spleen, tail of the pancreas, jejunum, left colic flexure.","The descending duodenum lies directly on the right hilum, so right renal procedures can injure it."]},{"h":"Coverings and hilum","points":["From inside out: fibrous capsule, perirenal fat, renal fascia, pararenal fat.","Renal fascia encloses the kidney, perirenal fat, and suprarenal gland.","At the hilum, front to back: renal vein, renal artery, renal pelvis."]},{"h":"Ureter course and narrow points","points":["Each ureter (about 25 cm) runs down on the psoas major, behind the gonadal vessels.","It crosses the common iliac bifurcation (or start of the external iliac) at the pelvic brim.","It enters the bladder wall obliquely, which acts as a flap valve against reflux.","Stones lodge at the three narrow points: ureteropelvic junction, pelvic brim, ureterovesical junction (the narrowest)."]},{"h":"Bladder and urethra","points":["Trigone: smooth triangle between the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice.","Male urethra (about 18 to 22 cm): prostatic, membranous (through the external sphincter, narrowest), spongy (bulbous part then penile part).","Female urethra is about 4 cm and opens just in front of the vagina, so bacteria from the perineum reach the bladder easily."]},{"h":"Male urethral trauma","points":["Pelvic fracture tears the posterior urethra at the membranous segment: blood at the meatus, high-riding prostate, urine into the retropubic space.","Straddle injury crushes the bulbous spongy urethra against the pubis below the perineal membrane.","If Buck fascia tears, urine fills the superficial perineal space and spreads into the scrotum, penis, and lower anterior abdominal wall, but not the thighs."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Kidney relations","cols":["Side","Anterior relations","Posterior relations"],"rows":[["Right","Liver, descending duodenum, right colic flexure","Diaphragm, 12th rib, psoas major, quadratus lumborum"],["Left","Stomach, spleen, pancreatic tail, jejunum, left colic flexure","Diaphragm, 11th and 12th ribs, psoas major, quadratus lumborum"]]},{"title":"Male urethral injury","cols":["Mechanism","Segment torn","Where urine collects"],"rows":[["Pelvic fracture","Membranous (posterior urethra)","Retropubic and perivesical space, deep pelvis"],["Straddle injury","Bulbous part of spongy (anterior) urethra","Superficial perineal space, scrotum, penis, lower abdominal wall"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"VAP at the hilum","text":"Front to back the hilum reads Vein, Artery, Pelvis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Three pinch points","text":"Follow a stone down: it leaves the pelvis (ureteropelvic junction), steps over the iliac vessels (pelvic brim), then squeezes into the bladder (ureterovesical junction)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fracture goes deep, straddle stays shallow","text":"A pelvic fracture tears the urethra above the perineal membrane (deep leak). A fall astride a beam tears it below (superficial leak into the scrotum)."}],"highYield":["Kidneys lie from about T12 to L3, and the right kidney is lower because of the liver.","Renal hilum order front to back is vein, artery, pelvis.","The ureterovesical junction is the narrowest point and the most common place a stone sticks.","Short female urethra explains the higher rate of cystitis in women.","Blood at the meatus with a high-riding prostate after pelvic fracture means a membranous urethra tear.","Straddle injury tears the bulbous urethra; urine spreads into the scrotum but stays out of the thighs."],"pitfalls":["The left kidney sits higher; the liver pushes the right one down.","Gonadal vessels cross in front of the ureter; the ureter crosses in front of the iliac vessels.","Superficial perineal urine does not enter the thighs because the membranous layer of superficial fascia attaches to the fascia lata.","The membranous urethra is the narrowest segment, while the prostatic urethra is the widest."],"dumps":[{"t":"Kidney in place","p":"Draw both kidneys from the front with the organs that sit on them. Then draw a cross-section of one kidney showing its coverings and the order of hilar structures.","l":["Retroperitoneal, about T12 to L3; right lower than left","Right anterior: liver, duodenum, right colic flexure","Left anterior: stomach, spleen, pancreatic tail, jejunum, left colic flexure","Posterior: diaphragm, 12th rib, psoas major, quadratus lumborum","Coverings: fibrous capsule, perirenal fat, renal fascia, pararenal fat","Hilum front to back: vein, artery, pelvis"]},{"t":"Ureter to urethra","p":"Trace the path of urine from the renal pelvis to the outside in a man. Mark the three ureter narrow points and the urethral segment hurt in a pelvic fracture and in a straddle injury.","l":["Ureter runs on psoas, behind gonadal vessels","Crosses the iliac vessels at the pelvic brim","Narrow points: ureteropelvic junction, pelvic brim, ureterovesical junction","Trigone between ureteric orifices and internal urethral orifice","Urethra: prostatic, membranous, spongy","Pelvic fracture: membranous segment","Straddle: bulbous spongy segment"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (section on the kidneys, ureters, and suprarenal glands)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum (sections on the urinary bladder and urethra)"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-4","UR-1","UR-12-C2","UR-12-H2"]},"UR-4":{"id":"UR-4","summary":"How the urinary organs relate to the peritoneum and the pelvis. Exam questions use these relations to explain bladder rupture patterns, where pain is felt, why the ureter is hit during hysterectomy, and what kind of incontinence a patient has.","notes":[{"h":"Peritoneal position","points":["Kidneys and ureters are retroperitoneal on the posterior abdominal wall.","The bladder is extraperitoneal in the pelvis; only its superior surface (dome) is covered by peritoneum.","A full bladder rises above the pubis and lifts peritoneum off the anterior abdominal wall."]},{"h":"Bladder rupture","points":["Blunt blow to a full bladder (seat belt, a blow while intoxicated) bursts the dome, the weakest part.","Dome rupture is intraperitoneal: urine fills the peritoneal cavity and causes ascites and peritonitis.","Pelvic fracture fragments tear the anterolateral wall low down, so urine leaks extraperitoneally into the retropubic space."]},{"h":"Peritoneal pouches","points":["Male: peritoneum runs from the bladder dome straight onto the rectum, forming the rectovesical pouch.","Female: peritoneum runs from the bladder onto the uterus (vesicouterine pouch), then from the uterus onto the rectum (rectouterine pouch).","The rectovesical pouch in men and the rectouterine pouch in women are the lowest points of the peritoneal cavity, where fluid pools when upright."]},{"h":"Pain patterns","points":["Visceral pain fibers from the kidney and ureter travel with sympathetic nerves into roughly the T11 to L2 cord segments.","The brain reads the pain in the skin served by those segments: flank, groin, scrotum or labia, and upper anterior thigh.","As a stone moves down, the pain moves from flank toward groin.","Costovertebral angle (12th rib meets the erector spinae): the kidney lies right under it, so tapping there hurts in pyelonephritis.","Perinephric pus stays inside the renal fascia, but the fascia is open below, so pus can track down toward the pelvis."]},{"h":"Ureter at surgery","points":["Near the cervix the ureter passes under the uterine artery, about 1 to 2 cm lateral to the cervix.","Clamping the uterine artery at hysterectomy can catch the ureter.","Ligation blocks drainage and causes hydronephrosis with flank pain; transection leaks urine (urinoma or a fistula with constant vaginal leakage).","The ureter is also at risk at the pelvic brim, where it lies next to the ovarian vessels."]},{"h":"Incontinence","points":["Stress: weak pelvic floor or urethral support lets urine leak when abdominal pressure rises (cough, laugh, lift); risk rises after vaginal delivery and menopause.","Cystocele: the bladder bulges into the anterior vaginal wall when its support weakens.","Urge: involuntary detrusor contractions (detrusor overactivity) give a sudden need to void and leakage on the way.","Overflow: the bladder cannot empty (weak detrusor or outlet blockage), so it overfills and dribbles; post-void residual is high."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Bladder rupture","cols":["Type","Usual cause","Site of tear","Where urine goes"],"rows":[["Intraperitoneal","Blunt blow to a full bladder","Dome","Peritoneal cavity (ascites, peritonitis)"],["Extraperitoneal","Pelvic fracture","Anterolateral wall near the base","Retropubic space and perivesical tissue"]]},{"title":"Types of incontinence","cols":["Type","Mechanism","Typical patient","Clue"],"rows":[["Stress","Poor urethral support or weak sphincter","Woman after vaginal deliveries","Leaks with cough or lifting"],["Urge","Detrusor overactivity","Older adult, bladder irritation, or neurologic disease","Sudden strong urge, cannot reach the toilet"],["Overflow","Incomplete emptying from weak detrusor or obstruction","Diabetic neuropathy, prostate enlargement","Constant dribbling, high post-void residual"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Water under the bridge","text":"The ureter (water) runs under the uterine artery (bridge). The same phrase works for the ureter passing under the ductus deferens in men."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Dome versus base","text":"Top of the bladder has peritoneum, so a dome tear spills into the belly. The base has none, so a low tear spills into the pelvis."}],"highYield":["A blow to a full bladder tears the dome and spills urine into the peritoneal cavity.","Pelvic fracture causes extraperitoneal bladder rupture.","The rectouterine pouch is the lowest point of the female peritoneal cavity.","Ureteral colic radiates from flank to groin because of the T11 to L2 visceral afferents.","Costovertebral angle tenderness points to the kidney sitting under the 12th rib.","Hydronephrosis after hysterectomy suggests a ligated ureter under the uterine artery.","Leak with cough is stress; sudden urge is detrusor overactivity; constant dribbling with a full bladder is overflow."],"pitfalls":["Kidneys are retroperitoneal, while the bladder is extraperitoneal in the pelvis with only its dome covered by peritoneum.","Pain felt in the groin with a stone does not mean a groin problem; it is referred visceral pain.","Overflow incontinence can look like urge incontinence; a high post-void residual separates them.","Men have one pouch (rectovesical); women have two (vesicouterine and rectouterine)."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pelvis in side view","p":"Draw a side view of the male and female pelvis. Draw the peritoneum over the bladder and label each pouch. Then mark where a dome rupture and an extraperitoneal rupture would leak urine.","l":["Bladder is extraperitoneal; peritoneum covers only the dome","Male: rectovesical pouch","Female: vesicouterine and rectouterine pouches","Lowest peritoneal point collects fluid","Dome rupture leaks into the peritoneal cavity","Pelvic fracture rupture leaks into the retropubic space"]},{"t":"Incontinence table","p":"Make a table of stress, urge, and overflow incontinence. Include the mechanism, a typical patient, and the symptom that gives each away.","l":["Stress: weak pelvic floor or urethral support","Stress: leaks with cough, laugh, or lifting","Cystocele: bladder bulges into anterior vaginal wall","Urge: detrusor overactivity, sudden urge","Overflow: incomplete emptying from weak detrusor or obstruction","Overflow: constant dribbling, high post-void residual"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum (sections on the peritoneum of the pelvis, the urinary bladder, and the ureters)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (section on referred visceral pain from the kidneys and ureters)"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-3","UR-6","UR-12-C2","UR-12-H1"]},"UR-5":{"id":"UR-5","summary":"How blood flows through the kidney and how arteriole tone sets filtration. Exam questions ask you to predict GFR and filtration fraction after drugs or disease change the arterioles, and to link vascular anatomy to infarcts, papillary necrosis, and a left varicocele.","notes":[{"h":"The path of blood","points":["Renal artery, segmental arteries, interlobar arteries (between pyramids), arcuate arteries (at the corticomedullary junction), cortical radiate arteries.","Then afferent arteriole, glomerular capillaries, efferent arteriole.","Efferent arterioles of cortical nephrons form peritubular capillaries; those of juxtamedullary nephrons form the vasa recta.","Veins run back the same way to the renal vein.","The kidneys get about 20 to 25 percent of cardiac output."]},{"h":"End arteries and infarcts","points":["Segmental and interlobar arteries do not connect with each other, so each one is the only supply to its territory.","Blocking one (usually an embolus from the left heart, as in atrial fibrillation) kills a wedge of tissue.","Renal infarcts are pale (ischemic), wedge-shaped, with the base at the capsule and the tip pointing to the medulla."]},{"h":"Arterioles control filtration","points":["Afferent constriction lowers both renal plasma flow (RPF) and GFR; filtration fraction (GFR/RPF) stays about the same.","Efferent constriction raises glomerular pressure, so GFR rises while RPF falls; filtration fraction rises.","Prostaglandins dilate the afferent arteriole; NSAIDs remove that and drop GFR in a volume-depleted patient.","Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole more; ACE inhibitors remove that and drop GFR in bilateral renal artery stenosis."]},{"h":"Autoregulation","points":["Renal blood flow and GFR stay nearly constant over a mean arterial pressure range of roughly 80 to 180 mm Hg.","Myogenic response: higher pressure stretches the afferent arteriole wall, which contracts back.","Tubuloglomerular feedback: higher GFR sends more NaCl to the macula densa, which releases adenosine and constricts the afferent arteriole.","Low NaCl at the macula densa does the reverse: afferent dilation plus renin release."]},{"h":"Vasa recta and the medulla","points":["Vasa recta run in hairpin loops beside the loops of Henle and carry only about 1 to 2 percent of renal blood flow.","Slow flow and countercurrent exchange carry away reabsorbed water without washing out the medullary salt and urea.","Oxygen shunts across from descending to ascending vessels, so the inner medulla is hypoxic as well as hypertonic.","Low oxygen and high osmolality make HbS red cells sickle in the vasa recta, causing papillary necrosis, hematuria, and loss of concentrating ability, even in sickle cell trait."]},{"h":"Renal veins","points":["The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta, just below the superior mesenteric artery, to reach the IVC.","The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein; the right gonadal vein drains straight into the IVC.","Renal cell carcinoma growing into the left renal vein blocks left gonadal drainage and causes a left varicocele that stays when the patient lies down.","Compression of the left renal vein between the aorta and superior mesenteric artery (nutcracker) can also cause hematuria and a left varicocele."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Arteriole changes","cols":["Change","RPF","GFR","Filtration fraction"],"rows":[["Afferent constriction","Down","Down","No change"],["Afferent dilation","Up","Up","No change"],["Efferent constriction","Down","Up","Up"],["Efferent dilation","Up","Down","Down"]]},{"title":"Renal vessels in order","cols":["Vessel","Where it runs"],"rows":[["Segmental artery","Hilum into the kidney; end artery"],["Interlobar artery","Between pyramids in the renal columns"],["Arcuate artery","Along the corticomedullary junction"],["Cortical radiate artery","Up through the cortex"],["Afferent arteriole","Into the glomerulus; renin cells in its wall"],["Efferent arteriole","Out of the glomerulus"],["Peritubular capillaries or vasa recta","Around cortical tubules, or down into the medulla"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Efferent is the odd one","text":"Every afferent change moves RPF and GFR the same way. Only efferent changes split them, so only efferent changes move filtration fraction."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SAAD papa","text":"Causes of papillary necrosis: Sickle cell disease or trait, Acute pyelonephritis, Analgesics (NSAIDs), Diabetes mellitus."}],"highYield":["Afferent changes move RPF and GFR together; efferent constriction raises GFR and filtration fraction while RPF falls.","NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors drop GFR in patients who depend on prostaglandins or angiotensin II.","Tubuloglomerular feedback works through the macula densa sensing NaCl and releasing adenosine.","Renal infarcts are pale and wedge-shaped because segmental arteries are end arteries.","Sickling in the hypoxic, hypertonic vasa recta causes papillary necrosis and isosthenuria.","A new left varicocele that does not empty on lying down suggests left renal vein obstruction by renal cell carcinoma."],"pitfalls":["Very strong efferent constriction eventually lowers GFR because flow drops so much; the rise in GFR applies to mild to moderate constriction.","Filtration fraction is GFR divided by RPF; it rises when GFR falls less than RPF.","Arcuate arteries do not form complete arches; they behave as end arteries too.","A right varicocele is less common and suggests IVC or right-sided compression, since the right gonadal vein drains into the IVC."],"dumps":[{"t":"Renal blood flow","p":"Draw the vessels from the renal artery to the renal vein through one juxtamedullary nephron. Label each vessel in order and mark where flow is slow and oxygen is low.","l":["Renal, segmental, interlobar, arcuate, cortical radiate arteries","Afferent arteriole, glomerulus, efferent arteriole","Efferent of juxtamedullary nephron forms vasa recta","Segmental and interlobar are end arteries","Vasa recta flow is slow, about 1 to 2 percent of renal flow","Inner medulla is hypoxic and hypertonic","Left renal vein receives the left gonadal vein"]},{"t":"Arteriole prediction table","p":"Make a table showing what happens to RPF, GFR, and filtration fraction with afferent constriction, afferent dilation, efferent constriction, and efferent dilation. Add one drug or hormone that causes each.","l":["Afferent constriction: RPF down, GFR down, FF same","Afferent dilation (prostaglandins): RPF up, GFR up, FF same","Efferent constriction (angiotensin II): RPF down, GFR up, FF up","Efferent dilation (ACE inhibitor): RPF up, GFR down, FF down","NSAIDs block afferent dilation","Myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback keep GFR steady"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Glomerular Filtration, Renal Blood Flow, and Their Control"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen (section on renal vessels)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney (sections on renal infarcts and papillary necrosis)"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-6","UR-12-F4","UR-12-F5","UR-12-G1"]},"UR-6":{"id":"UR-6","summary":"How the kidney filters plasma, reclaims what the body needs, secretes waste, concentrates urine, and empties the bladder. This is the most calculation-heavy unit: expect to work clearance numbers, predict GFR changes, sort out hyponatremia and polyuria, and place a neurologic lesion from the bladder pattern.","notes":[{"h":"Starling forces and GFR","points":["Net filtration pressure = glomerular capillary pressure (about 60 mm Hg) minus Bowman space pressure (about 18) minus glomerular oncotic pressure (about 32).","GFR = Kf times net filtration pressure; normal GFR is about 125 mL/min.","Ureteral obstruction raises Bowman space pressure and lowers GFR.","Hypoalbuminemia lowers plasma oncotic pressure and tends to raise GFR.","Dehydration concentrates plasma proteins (higher oncotic pressure) and lowers renal flow, so GFR falls."]},{"h":"Clearance","points":["Clearance = (urine concentration times urine flow) / plasma concentration.","Inulin is filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals GFR. Creatinine slightly overestimates GFR because a little is secreted.","PAH is filtered and secreted almost completely, so its clearance is effective renal plasma flow (about 650 mL/min).","Filtration fraction = GFR / RPF, normally about 20 percent.","Clearance below GFR means net reabsorption (glucose, urea); above GFR means net secretion (PAH)."]},{"h":"Proximal tubule and loop","points":["Proximal tubule reabsorbs about two thirds of filtered Na+ and water, nearly all glucose and amino acids, and most bicarbonate (Na+/H+ exchange).","Glucose rides SGLT2 with Na+; above a plasma level of about 200 mg/dL the carriers begin to saturate and glucose appears in urine.","Descending thin limb lets water out; ascending limbs are water impermeable.","Thick ascending limb: Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2) reabsorbs about 25 percent of filtered Na+; K+ leaks back into the lumen.","That K+ back-leak makes the lumen positive, which pushes Mg2+ and Ca2+ between the cells into blood."]},{"h":"Distal hormone control","points":["Aldosterone (principal cells): more ENaC channels and Na+/K+-ATPase, so more Na+ reabsorbed and more K+ secreted.","ADH (V2 receptor, cAMP) inserts aquaporin-2 into the collecting duct luminal membrane so water follows the medullary gradient.","PTH increases Ca2+ reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule (and reduces phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule)."]},{"h":"Concentrating urine","points":["Thick ascending limb pumps NaCl out without water; the descending limb loses water; flow repeats this step along the loop (countercurrent multiplication).","The result is an interstitial gradient from about 300 mOsm/kg at the cortex to about 1200 mOsm/kg at the papilla.","Urea trapped in the inner medulla adds a large share of that gradient.","With ADH, collecting duct water leaves into the hypertonic medulla and urine reaches about 1200 mOsm/kg; without ADH, urine can be as dilute as about 50 mOsm/kg."]},{"h":"Fluid shifts","points":["Water moves between ECF and ICF only when ECF osmolality changes.","Isotonic gain or loss changes only ECF volume.","Losing water (dehydration) or gaining salt raises osmolality and pulls water out of cells.","Gaining water or losing more salt than water lowers osmolality and swells cells."]},{"h":"Micturition","points":["Filling: sympathetic hypogastric nerve (about T11 to L2) relaxes the detrusor and contracts the internal sphincter; pudendal nerve (S2 to S4) keeps the external sphincter closed.","Voiding: stretch signals reach the pontine micturition center, which, when the cortex allows, fires pelvic parasympathetics (S2 to S4) to contract the detrusor and relaxes both sphincters.","Cord lesion above the sacral segments: reflex spastic bladder, small capacity, sudden emptying, and sphincter dyssynergia.","Lesion at the sacral cord or its roots: flaccid, overfilled bladder with overflow dribbling."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Polyuria and hyponatremia","cols":["Condition","Problem","Serum Na+","Urine osmolality","Water deprivation","Desmopressin"],"rows":[["Central diabetes insipidus","No ADH made","High or high-normal","Low","Urine stays dilute","Urine concentrates"],["Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Kidney ignores ADH (lithium, hypercalcemia, V2 mutation)","High or high-normal","Low","Urine stays dilute","Little or no change"],["Primary polydipsia","Drinks too much water","Low or low-normal","Low","Urine concentrates","Not needed; already concentrates"],["SIADH","Too much ADH (small cell carcinoma, CNS disease, drugs)","Low","Inappropriately high","Not used","Not used"]]},{"title":"Fluid gain and loss","cols":["Change","Example","ECF volume","ICF volume","Osmolality"],"rows":[["Isotonic gain","Normal saline infusion","Up","No change","No change"],["Isotonic loss","Diarrhea, hemorrhage","Down","No change","No change"],["Hypertonic gain","Hypertonic saline, high salt intake","Up","Down","Up"],["Water loss","Sweating, diabetes insipidus","Down","Down","Up"],["Water gain","SIADH, water intoxication","Up","Up","Down"],["Salt loss greater than water loss","Adrenal insufficiency","Down","Up","Down"]]},{"title":"Bladder by lesion level","cols":["Lesion","Bladder","Symptoms"],"rows":[["Above the pons (cortex)","Uninhibited, coordinated","Urge incontinence"],["Spinal cord above the sacral segments","Spastic, small","Reflex emptying, detrusor sphincter dyssynergia"],["Sacral cord, cauda equina, or pelvic nerves","Flaccid, large","Retention with overflow dribbling"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"UV over P","text":"Write clearance as UV/P and plug in. Example: inulin urine 30 mg/mL, urine flow 1 mL/min, plasma 0.25 mg/mL gives 120 mL/min, which is GFR."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Concentrate on the test","text":"Water deprivation separates people who can concentrate (primary polydipsia) from those who cannot (diabetes insipidus). Desmopressin then separates no hormone (central, responds) from no response (nephrogenic)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Only osmolality moves water","text":"Ask one question: did ECF osmolality change? If no, the ICF stays put. If up, cells shrink. If down, cells swell."}],"highYield":["Ureteral obstruction lowers GFR by raising Bowman space pressure.","Inulin clearance equals GFR; PAH clearance equals effective renal plasma flow.","Clearance less than GFR means net reabsorption; more than GFR means net secretion.","Loop diuretics block NKCC2 and cause urinary loss of Ca2+ and Mg2+.","Aldosterone increases Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion through principal cells.","ADH acts on V2 receptors to insert aquaporin-2; lithium causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.","SIADH: low serum Na+ with inappropriately concentrated urine in a euvolemic patient.","Spinal cord injury above the sacral cord gives a spastic bladder; sacral injury gives a flaccid overflow bladder."],"pitfalls":["Creatinine clearance slightly overestimates true GFR because some creatinine is secreted.","Low albumin raises GFR only through the Starling equation; in nephrotic syndrome GFR is often normal or low because the glomerular disease itself lowers Kf.","Primary polydipsia and diabetes insipidus both give dilute urine; serum Na+ is low-normal in polydipsia and high-normal in diabetes insipidus.","Isotonic fluid loss does not change osmolality, so it does not trigger thirst through osmoreceptors or move water out of cells.","Sympathetic nerves fill the bladder and parasympathetic nerves empty it."],"dumps":[{"t":"Nephron transport map","p":"Draw a nephron from glomerulus to collecting duct. At each segment write what is reabsorbed, the main transporter, and the hormone or drug that acts there.","l":["Glomerulus: GFR set by Starling forces","Proximal tubule: two thirds of Na+ and water, nearly all glucose and amino acids, Na+/H+ exchange","Descending limb: water out","Thick ascending limb: NKCC2, no water, paracellular Mg2+ and Ca2+; loop diuretics","Distal tubule: NaCl reabsorption, PTH raises Ca2+ reabsorption","Collecting duct principal cell: aldosterone on ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase, K+ secretion","Collecting duct: ADH inserts aquaporin-2","Medullary gradient about 300 to 1200 mOsm/kg"]},{"t":"Water problems table","p":"Make a table of central diabetes insipidus, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, primary polydipsia, and SIADH. Fill in serum Na+, urine osmolality, the water deprivation result, and the desmopressin result.","l":["Central DI: high or high-normal Na+, dilute urine, concentrates with desmopressin","Nephrogenic DI: high or high-normal Na+, dilute urine, little or no response to desmopressin","Nephrogenic DI causes: lithium, hypercalcemia, V2 mutation","Primary polydipsia: low or low-normal Na+, urine concentrates with water deprivation","SIADH: low Na+, urine inappropriately concentrated, euvolemic","SIADH causes: small cell lung carcinoma, CNS disease, drugs"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Glomerular Filtration, Renal Blood Flow, and Their Control"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Renal Tubular Reabsorption and Secretion"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on urine concentration and dilution"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on micturition"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-5","UR-7","UR-2","UR-12-C1"]},"UR-7":{"id":"UR-7","summary":"How the kidney keeps blood pH and blood pressure in range. Exam questions hand you a blood gas or electrolyte panel and ask you to name the disorder, judge compensation, find the cause from the anion gap, sort out the renal tubular acidoses, or explain hypertension with low potassium.","notes":[{"h":"Reclaiming filtered bicarbonate","points":["Proximal tubule secretes H+ through Na+/H+ exchange; H+ meets filtered HCO3- in the lumen.","Brush border carbonic anhydrase turns H2CO3 into CO2 and water, which enter the cell.","Inside, carbonic anhydrase makes H+ and HCO3- again; HCO3- goes to blood and H+ is recycled.","This reclaims about 80 percent of filtered HCO3- but adds no new base. Acetazolamide blocks it."]},{"h":"Making new bicarbonate","points":["Each H+ the kidney excretes with a buffer adds one new HCO3- to blood.","Titratable acid: secreted H+ binds filtered phosphate (HPO4 2- to H2PO4-).","Ammonium: proximal tubule breaks down glutamine into NH4+ and new HCO3-; NH4+ is excreted in urine.","Ammonium excretion rises a great deal in chronic acidosis and is the main adaptive response; hyperkalemia suppresses it.","Alpha-intercalated cells secrete H+ with an H+-ATPase; urine pH can fall to about 4.5."]},{"h":"Reading a blood gas","points":["Normal: pH 7.35 to 7.45, PaCO2 35 to 45 mm Hg, HCO3- 22 to 28 mEq/L.","Step 1: acidemia or alkalemia. Step 2: if HCO3- moved in the same direction as pH, the primary problem is metabolic; if PaCO2 moved in the opposite direction, it is respiratory.","Metabolic acidosis: expected PaCO2 = 1.5 times HCO3- plus 8, plus or minus 2 (Winters formula).","Respiratory acidosis: HCO3- rises about 1 per 10 mm Hg PaCO2 acutely and about 3.5 per 10 after 3 to 5 days of renal compensation.","PaCO2 outside the expected range means a second, respiratory disorder is also present; compensation never brings pH all the way back."]},{"h":"Anion gap","points":["Anion gap = Na+ minus (Cl- plus HCO3-); normal about 8 to 12 mEq/L.","High gap: an unmeasured acid has been added (lactate, ketoacids, uremic acids, formate from methanol, glycolate and oxalate from ethylene glycol, salicylate).","Normal gap (hyperchloremic): HCO3- was lost and replaced by Cl- (diarrhea, renal tubular acidosis, acetazolamide).","Low albumin lowers the gap and can hide a high gap acidosis."]},{"h":"Vomiting alkalosis","points":["Vomiting removes HCl, so HCO3- made by parietal cells stays in blood (hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis).","Volume loss raises angiotensin II and aldosterone; proximal tubules reclaim HCO3- avidly, and principal and intercalated cells trade Na+ for K+ and H+.","Result: hypokalemia from renal K+ loss, and acidic urine despite alkalemia (paradoxical aciduria).","Low Cl- delivery also blocks distal HCO3- excretion; urine Cl- is low, and saline corrects it."]},{"h":"Renin, angiotensin, aldosterone","points":["Renin release: low perfusion pressure at juxtaglomerular cells, low NaCl at the macula densa, beta-1 sympathetic stimulation.","Renin turns angiotensinogen into angiotensin I; ACE (mostly in lung endothelium) makes angiotensin II and breaks down bradykinin.","Angiotensin II: arteriolar constriction (efferent more than afferent), more proximal Na+ reabsorption, aldosterone release, ADH release, thirst.","Atrial natriuretic peptide from stretched atria does the opposite: natriuresis, raised GFR, vasodilation, less renin and aldosterone."]},{"h":"Long-term pressure control","points":["Pressure natriuresis: higher arterial pressure makes the kidney excrete more Na+ and water, shrinking ECF volume until pressure falls back.","Over days to weeks this kidney mechanism sets the long-term arterial pressure.","Primary hyperaldosteronism (adrenal adenoma or hyperplasia): hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, high aldosterone, low renin.","Edema is absent in primary hyperaldosteronism because pressure natriuresis and ANP limit volume gain (aldosterone escape)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Primary disorders and expected compensation","cols":["Disorder","pH","Primary change","Compensation"],"rows":[["Metabolic acidosis","Low","HCO3- down","PaCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3- + 8 (plus or minus 2)"],["Metabolic alkalosis","High","HCO3- up","PaCO2 rises about 0.7 per 1 mEq/L HCO3- rise"],["Respiratory acidosis","Low","PaCO2 up","HCO3- up 1 per 10 mm Hg acute, about 3.5 per 10 chronic"],["Respiratory alkalosis","High","PaCO2 down","HCO3- down 2 per 10 mm Hg acute, about 4 to 5 per 10 chronic"]]},{"title":"Renal tubular acidosis","cols":["Type","Defect","Urine pH","Serum K+","Associations"],"rows":[["Type 1 (distal)","Alpha-intercalated cells cannot secrete H+","Above 5.5","Low","Calcium phosphate stones; amphotericin B, autoimmune disease"],["Type 2 (proximal)","Proximal tubule cannot reclaim HCO3-","Below 5.5 once serum HCO3- is low","Low","Fanconi syndrome, multiple myeloma, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],["Type 4","Low aldosterone or aldosterone resistance; hyperkalemia cuts ammonium production","Below 5.5","High","Diabetic hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism, ACE inhibitors, potassium-sparing diuretics"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"MUDPILES","text":"High anion gap acidosis: Methanol, Uremia, Diabetic ketoacidosis, Propylene glycol, Iron tablets or isoniazid, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HARDASS","text":"Normal anion gap acidosis: Hyperalimentation, Addison disease, Renal tubular acidosis, Diarrhea, Acetazolamide, Spironolactone, Saline infusion."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Volume beats pH","text":"When the body is short on volume, the kidney saves Na+ even if it must dump H+ and K+ to do it. That is why vomiting makes acid urine."}],"highYield":["Ammonium excretion from glutamine is the kidney's main adaptable way to excrete acid.","Use Winters formula to check whether a metabolic acidosis has the right respiratory compensation.","Diarrhea and RTA give a normal anion gap; ketoacidosis, lactate, and toxic alcohols give a high gap.","Distal RTA: urine pH above 5.5, hypokalemia, calcium phosphate stones.","Type 4 RTA is the classic RTA with hyperkalemia; types 1 and 2 cause hypokalemia.","Vomiting gives hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with low urine Cl- and paradoxical aciduria.","Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole more and stimulates aldosterone, ADH, and thirst.","Hypertension, hypokalemia, and low renin point to primary hyperaldosteronism."],"pitfalls":["Reclaiming filtered HCO3- adds no new base; only excreting H+ as titratable acid or ammonium makes new HCO3-.","A normal pH with abnormal PaCO2 and HCO3- usually means a mixed disorder, since compensation does not fully normalize pH.","Proximal RTA can make an acid urine once serum HCO3- falls below the lowered threshold; urine pH alone does not rule it out.","Renovascular hypertension and primary hyperaldosteronism both raise aldosterone; renin is high in renovascular disease and low in primary disease.","Hypokalemia after vomiting comes mostly from renal K+ loss, since gastric fluid holds little K+."],"dumps":[{"t":"Kidney acid handling","p":"Draw a proximal tubule cell and an alpha-intercalated cell. Show how filtered HCO3- is reclaimed, how new HCO3- is made, and where ammonium comes from.","l":["Na+/H+ exchanger secretes H+ in the proximal tubule","Luminal carbonic anhydrase converts H2CO3 to CO2 and water","HCO3- returns to blood; this only reclaims base","Glutamine metabolism makes NH4+ and new HCO3-","H+-ATPase in alpha-intercalated cells secretes H+","H+ buffered by phosphate is titratable acid","Each excreted H+ with a buffer adds one new HCO3- to blood","Ammonium excretion rises in chronic acidosis"]},{"t":"RAAS and blood pressure","p":"Trace the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system from the three triggers for renin release to every action of angiotensin II and aldosterone. Then add ANP and what it opposes.","l":["Triggers: low perfusion at JG cells, low NaCl at macula densa, beta-1 input","Renin cleaves angiotensinogen to angiotensin I","ACE makes angiotensin II and breaks down bradykinin","Angiotensin II: vasoconstriction, efferent arteriole, proximal Na+ reabsorption","Angiotensin II: aldosterone, ADH, thirst","Aldosterone: Na+ reabsorption, K+ and H+ secretion","ANP: natriuresis, vasodilation, inhibits renin and aldosterone","Pressure natriuresis sets long-term arterial pressure"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Acid-Base Regulation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on the role of the kidneys in long-term control of arterial pressure"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-6","UR-5","UR-12-F3","UR-12-A4"]},"UR-8":{"id":"UR-8","summary":"How the kidney handles glucose, protein, calcium, phosphate, potassium, and toxic alcohols, and how it makes erythropoietin and active vitamin D. Exam cases hand you a lab panel and ask you to predict the next value or name the step that went wrong.","notes":[{"h":"Glucose reabsorption and the renal threshold","points":["All filtered glucose is normally reabsorbed in the proximal tubule by sodium-coupled transport.","SGLT2 in the early proximal tubule takes up about 90 percent; SGLT1 further along takes the rest. GLUT2 moves glucose out the basolateral side.","The carriers saturate. Glucose starts to spill into urine at a plasma level of about 200 mg/dL (renal threshold); all nephrons are saturated at the transport maximum (about 375 mg/min).","Glucose left in the tubule holds water with it (osmotic diuresis): polyuria, thirst, and volume depletion in uncontrolled diabetes.","Glucosuria with normal plasma glucose points to a proximal tubule defect (Fanconi syndrome) or a lower threshold in pregnancy."]},{"h":"Three kinds of proteinuria","points":["Glomerular: a damaged filter leaks albumin; can exceed 3.5 g/day (nephrotic). The dipstick detects albumin well.","Overflow: a normal filter is overwhelmed by small proteins made in excess, such as Bence Jones light chains in multiple myeloma.","Tubular: small proteins (beta-2 microglobulin, light chains) are filtered normally but the proximal tubule fails to reclaim them; usually under 2 g/day.","The dipstick misses most light chains and tubular proteins; urine protein electrophoresis or immunofixation finds them."]},{"h":"Calcium, phosphate, vitamin D, and magnesium","points":["Liver adds the 25-hydroxyl; the proximal tubule adds the 1-alpha-hydroxyl to make calcitriol, the active form.","1-alpha-hydroxylase is turned up by PTH and low phosphate and turned down by FGF23 and high phosphate.","Calcitriol raises gut absorption of calcium and phosphate.","Low magnesium blocks PTH release and causes PTH resistance, so hypocalcemia will not correct until magnesium is replaced."]},{"h":"Potassium shifts and the ECG","points":["Into cells (lowers serum K): insulin and beta-2 stimulation (both turn up Na/K-ATPase) and alkalosis.","Out of cells (raises serum K): acidosis, cell lysis (rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, hemolysis), insulin deficiency, and hyperosmolality (water leaves cells and drags K with it).","Hyperkalemia ECG: peaked T waves, then PR prolongation and loss of P waves, a wide QRS, a sine wave, and ventricular fibrillation.","Hypokalemia ECG: flat T waves, ST depression, and U waves; muscle weakness and arrhythmias."]},{"h":"Erythropoietin","points":["Made by peritubular interstitial (fibroblast-like) cells of the renal cortex and outer medulla when they sense low oxygen delivery.","Hypoxia stabilizes HIF, which switches on the EPO gene; EPO keeps red cell precursors alive in the marrow.","Chronic kidney disease: lost EPO-producing tissue gives a normocytic, normochromic anemia.","Renal cell carcinoma can make EPO on its own, giving secondary polycythemia with a high EPO level."]},{"h":"Ethylene glycol and nephrotic lipids","points":["Alcohol dehydrogenase (using NAD+) starts the breakdown: ethylene glycol to glycolaldehyde, then glycolic acid, glyoxylic acid, and oxalic acid.","Early: the intact alcohol raises the osmolal gap. Later: the acids give a high anion gap metabolic acidosis as the osmolal gap falls.","Oxalate binds calcium: hypocalcemia, calcium oxalate crystals in urine, and acute tubular injury.","Blocking alcohol dehydrogenase (fomepizole, or ethanol as a competing substrate) stops toxic acids from forming.","Nephrotic syndrome: the liver makes more lipoproteins in response to low oncotic pressure, and lipoprotein breakdown slows, so cholesterol and LDL rise.","Lipiduria shows as oval fat bodies and fatty casts; their cholesterol esters give a Maltese cross under polarized light."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Calcium, phosphate, and PTH patterns","cols":["Condition","Serum calcium","Serum phosphate","PTH","Vitamin D finding"],"rows":[["Chronic kidney disease","Low or normal","High","High (secondary)","Low calcitriol (lost 1-alpha-hydroxylase)"],["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High","Low","High","High calcitriol (PTH drive)"],["Vitamin D deficiency","Low or normal","Low","High (secondary)","Low 25-hydroxyvitamin D"]]},{"title":"Proteinuria types","cols":["Type","Problem","Main protein","Example"],"rows":[["Glomerular","Leaky filter","Albumin","Diabetic nephropathy, minimal change disease"],["Overflow","Too much small protein made","Free light chains (Bence Jones)","Multiple myeloma"],["Tubular","Proximal tubule fails to reabsorb","Beta-2 microglobulin and other small proteins","Fanconi syndrome, tubulointerstitial injury"]]},{"title":"Toxic alcohols","cols":["Alcohol","Toxic product","Anion gap acidosis","Key finding"],"rows":[["Ethylene glycol","Glycolic and oxalic acids","Yes","Calcium oxalate crystals, acute kidney injury"],["Methanol","Formic acid","Yes","Vision loss (optic nerve injury)"],["Isopropanol","Acetone","No","Osmolal gap with ketosis but no acidosis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Gap swap","text":"In ethylene glycol poisoning the osmolal gap comes first and the anion gap comes later, because the parent alcohol is turned into acids over hours."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Insulin and beta push K in","text":"Both turn up the Na/K-ATPase. Anything that breaks cells or adds acid pushes K out."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fix magnesium first","text":"Calcium that will not come up despite replacement means check magnesium; without it the parathyroid cannot release PTH and tissues ignore what PTH there is."}],"highYield":["Glucosuria begins at a plasma glucose of about 200 mg/dL.","CKD: high phosphate, low calcitriol, low or normal calcium, high PTH.","Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium, low phosphate.","Peaked T waves are the first ECG sign of hyperkalemia; U waves mark hypokalemia.","Anemia of CKD is normocytic from low EPO; renal cell carcinoma can cause polycythemia from excess EPO.","Ethylene glycol: high anion gap acidosis, osmolal gap, calcium oxalate crystals, acute kidney injury.","Maltese crosses in urine mean lipiduria from nephrotic syndrome."],"pitfalls":["A negative dipstick does not rule out light chain proteinuria; the dipstick reacts mainly with albumin.","Hemolyzed blood samples give false hyperkalemia; repeat the draw before acting on an isolated high K.","Vitamin D deficiency and CKD both raise PTH, but phosphate is low in vitamin D deficiency and high in CKD.","Isopropanol raises the osmolal gap without an anion gap acidosis; ethylene glycol and methanol give both."],"dumps":[{"t":"Vitamin D and PTH loop","p":"Draw the path from skin or diet to active vitamin D. Show where PTH, phosphate, and FGF23 act, then write the calcium, phosphate, and PTH pattern for CKD, primary hyperparathyroidism, and vitamin D deficiency.","l":["Liver 25-hydroxylation, kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylation","1-alpha-hydroxylase sits in the proximal tubule","PTH and low phosphate turn it up; FGF23 and high phosphate turn it down","Calcitriol raises gut calcium and phosphate absorption","CKD: high phosphate, low calcitriol, high PTH","Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium, low phosphate, high PTH","Vitamin D deficiency: low phosphate, low or normal calcium, high PTH","Low magnesium blocks PTH release and action"]},{"t":"Ethylene glycol pathway","p":"Write out the breakdown of ethylene glycol step by step, name the first enzyme and its cofactor, and list the lab and urine findings that follow.","l":["Alcohol dehydrogenase with NAD+ starts the pathway","Glycolaldehyde, glycolic acid, glyoxylic acid, oxalic acid","Glycolic acid drives most of the acidosis","Early osmolal gap from the parent alcohol","Later high anion gap metabolic acidosis","Oxalate binds calcium: hypocalcemia","Calcium oxalate crystals in urine","Acute tubular injury and kidney failure","Fomepizole or ethanol blocks alcohol dehydrogenase"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Renal Tubular Reabsorption and Secretion"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on vitamin D"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-6","UR-7","UR-12-A","UR-12-B","UR-12-G","EN-12-D"]},"UR-9":{"id":"UR-9","summary":"The single-gene and tumor suppressor disorders that show up in the kidney: polycystic kidney disease, type IV collagen disease, VHL, WT1, tuberous sclerosis, cystinuria, and Fabry disease. Questions ask you to name the gene or protein, read the inheritance in a pedigree, and link the defect to the kidney finding.","notes":[{"h":"Polycystic kidney disease","points":["Autosomal dominant (ADPKD): PKD1 on chromosome 16 (polycystin-1, about 85 percent, more severe) or PKD2 on chromosome 4 (polycystin-2, milder).","Polycystins sit on the primary cilium and control calcium signaling; loss raises cAMP and drives cell growth and fluid secretion into cysts.","ADPKD: both kidneys large and full of cysts in adults, flank pain, hematuria, hypertension, liver cysts, berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse.","Autosomal recessive (ARPKD): PKHD1 on chromosome 6 (fibrocystin) causes cysts in infants, oligohydramnios with Potter sequence, and congenital hepatic fibrosis.","Dominant pedigree: every generation, both sexes, father to son passing, each child of an affected parent has a 50 percent risk. Recessive: unaffected carrier parents, each child has a 1 in 4 risk."]},{"h":"Type IV collagen disease","points":["The glomerular basement membrane uses a network of alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 chains of type IV collagen.","Alport syndrome: most often X-linked COL4A5 mutation; less often autosomal recessive COL4A3 or COL4A4.","Alport triad: hematuria progressing to kidney failure, sensorineural hearing loss, and eye defects (anterior lenticonus).","Electron microscopy in Alport: a split, layered basket-weave basement membrane with areas of thinning and thickening.","Thin basement membrane disease: heterozygous COL4A3 or COL4A4 mutation, a uniformly thin membrane, persistent microscopic hematuria, usually normal kidney function."]},{"h":"VHL and clear cell carcinoma","points":["VHL is a tumor suppressor on chromosome 3p. Its protein tags HIF for destruction when oxygen is normal.","Loss of both copies stabilizes HIF, which drives VEGF (new vessels), PDGF, and EPO, so clear cell tumors are very vascular.","Sporadic clear cell carcinoma: both hits are somatic (often 3p deletion plus mutation of the other copy), one tumor in an older adult.","VHL syndrome (autosomal dominant): one germline hit, so tumors are early, bilateral, and multiple, with cerebellar and retinal hemangioblastomas and pheochromocytoma."]},{"h":"Wilms tumor genes","points":["WT1 on 11p13 codes a transcription factor needed for kidney and gonad development.","WAGR (11p13 deletion): Wilms tumor, aniridia (PAX6), genitourinary anomalies, and intellectual disability.","Denys-Drash: WT1 point mutation with early diffuse mesangial sclerosis, nephrotic syndrome, and 46,XY disorder of sex development.","Beckwith-Wiedemann: 11p15 imprinting defect (extra IGF2 expression) with macroglossia, organomegaly, hemihyperplasia, omphalocele, and neonatal hypoglycemia."]},{"h":"Tuberous sclerosis, cystinuria, Fabry","points":["Tuberous sclerosis (autosomal dominant): TSC1 (hamartin, chromosome 9) or TSC2 (tuberin, chromosome 16) normally brakes mTOR; loss lets mTOR drive cell growth.","Kidney in tuberous sclerosis: angiomyolipomas (blood vessels, smooth muscle, fat), often multiple and bilateral and prone to bleeding, plus renal cysts.","Cystinuria (autosomal recessive): faulty proximal tubule and gut transporter for cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine (COLA).","Cystine dissolves poorly in acidic urine, so it forms hexagonal crystals and stones; the cyanide-nitroprusside test turns red-purple.","Fabry disease (X-linked recessive): alpha-galactosidase A deficiency builds up globotriaosylceramide in vessels, nerves, sweat glands, and kidney.","Fabry signs: angiokeratomas, burning pain in hands and feet, little sweating, corneal whorls, proteinuria leading to kidney failure, and heart and stroke disease."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Inherited kidney disorders","cols":["Disorder","Gene and product","Inheritance","Kidney finding","Other findings"],"rows":[["ADPKD","PKD1 (chr 16, polycystin-1), PKD2 (chr 4, polycystin-2)","Autosomal dominant","Large cystic kidneys in adults","Liver cysts, berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse"],["ARPKD","PKHD1 (chr 6, fibrocystin)","Autosomal recessive","Large cystic kidneys in infants","Congenital hepatic fibrosis, Potter sequence"],["Alport syndrome","COL4A5 most often; COL4A3 or COL4A4","X-linked most often","Hematuria to kidney failure; split GBM","Sensorineural hearing loss, lens defects"],["Thin basement membrane disease","Heterozygous COL4A3 or COL4A4","Autosomal dominant pattern","Thin GBM, microscopic hematuria","None"],["Von Hippel-Lindau","VHL (chr 3p)","Autosomal dominant","Bilateral clear cell carcinomas, cysts","Hemangioblastomas, pheochromocytoma"],["WAGR and Denys-Drash","WT1 (chr 11p13)","Usually new deletion or mutation","Wilms tumor; mesangial sclerosis in Denys-Drash","Aniridia (WAGR); disorder of sex development (Denys-Drash)"],["Beckwith-Wiedemann","11p15 imprinting region (IGF2)","Mostly sporadic","Wilms tumor, large kidneys","Macroglossia, hemihyperplasia, omphalocele"],["Tuberous sclerosis","TSC1 (hamartin), TSC2 (tuberin)","Autosomal dominant","Angiomyolipomas, cysts","Cortical tubers, seizures, ash-leaf spots, cardiac rhabdomyoma"],["Cystinuria","Dibasic amino acid transporter","Autosomal recessive","Cystine stones, hexagonal crystals","None outside the urinary tract"],["Fabry disease","GLA (alpha-galactosidase A)","X-linked recessive","Proteinuria to kidney failure","Angiokeratomas, painful neuropathy, hypohidrosis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"COLA","text":"Cystine, Ornithine, Lysine, Arginine: the four amino acids lost in cystinuria."},{"kind":"trick","label":"VHL is 3 letters, chromosome 3","text":"Von Hippel-Lindau has three words and sits on chromosome 3p."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Alport cannot see, hear, or pee","text":"A memory hook for the eye, ear, and kidney triad of type IV collagen disease."}],"highYield":["PKD1 on chromosome 16 is the common and more severe ADPKD gene.","ADPKD brings hypertension, kidney failure, and subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured berry aneurysm.","ARPKD pairs infant cystic kidneys with congenital hepatic fibrosis and portal hypertension.","Goodpasture antibodies target the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen; Alport patients lack normal alpha-3, 4, 5 networks.","VHL loss keeps HIF active, which drives VEGF and EPO in clear cell carcinoma.","TSC2 lies next to PKD1 on chromosome 16, so a deletion of both gives severe early polycystic disease.","Fabry disease and Hunter syndrome are the classic X-linked lysosomal storage diseases."],"pitfalls":["Cystinuria (transporter defect, stones) differs from cystinosis (lysosomal cystine buildup, Fanconi syndrome).","Alport and thin basement membrane disease both cause hematuria; only Alport brings hearing loss and progressive kidney failure.","WAGR and Beckwith-Wiedemann both raise Wilms risk but at different 11p sites: 11p13 (WT1) versus 11p15 (imprinting).","Angiomyolipoma contains fat; renal cell carcinoma rarely does, which helps on imaging."],"dumps":[{"t":"Polycystic kidney pedigrees","p":"Draw a three-generation pedigree for ADPKD and a separate one for ARPKD. Under each, write the gene, chromosome, protein, age of onset, and extrarenal findings.","l":["ADPKD shows in every generation with male-to-male passing","ADPKD risk to each child of an affected parent is 50 percent","PKD1, chromosome 16, polycystin-1; PKD2, chromosome 4, polycystin-2","ADPKD extras: liver cysts, berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse","ARPKD parents are unaffected carriers; each child has a 25 percent risk","PKHD1, chromosome 6, fibrocystin","ARPKD extras: congenital hepatic fibrosis, Potter sequence from oligohydramnios"]},{"t":"Two hits in VHL","p":"Draw a normal kidney cell and a VHL-deficient cell side by side. Show what happens to HIF in each, and explain why the syndrome gives bilateral tumors while sporadic cancer gives one.","l":["VHL protein tags HIF for destruction when oxygen is normal","Loss of both VHL copies stabilizes HIF","HIF turns on VEGF, PDGF, and EPO","VEGF makes clear cell tumors very vascular","Syndrome: one germline hit, so every cell needs only one more hit","Sporadic: two somatic hits needed in one cell, usually a 3p deletion plus a mutation","Syndrome extras: hemangioblastomas of cerebellum and retina, pheochromocytoma"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney (sections on cystic diseases and tumors)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on sphingolipid degradation and sphingolipidoses"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-12-E","UR-12-G","UR-12-C","UR-10"]},"UR-10":{"id":"UR-10","summary":"How antibodies, immune complexes, ANCA, and T cells injure the kidney, how complement levels sort glomerular disease, and how the urinary tract keeps bacteria out. Exams also ask about immune attack on renal cell carcinoma and hepatitis B vaccine serology.","notes":[{"h":"Three immune patterns in the glomerulus","points":["Type II (anti-GBM): IgG against the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen lays down in a smooth linear line along the GBM.","Goodpasture syndrome: the same antibody hits alveolar basement membrane, giving lung hemorrhage plus glomerulonephritis.","Type III (immune complex): complexes deposit or form in place, giving a granular, lumpy pattern. Examples: poststreptococcal GN, lupus nephritis, IgA nephropathy, membranous nephropathy.","Pauci-immune: little or no immunoglobulin on immunofluorescence; ANCA activate neutrophils that damage vessel walls. Examples: granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis."]},{"h":"Complement as a sorting test","points":["Low C3 with usually normal C4: poststreptococcal GN (alternative pathway) and membranoproliferative GN (C4 can also fall in the immune complex type).","Low C3 and C4: lupus nephritis (classical pathway); C4 is also low in cryoglobulinemia.","Normal complement: IgA nephropathy, anti-GBM disease, ANCA vasculitis.","Poststreptococcal C3 returns to normal in about 8 weeks; a C3 that stays low suggests membranoproliferative GN."]},{"h":"Small and medium vessel vasculitis","points":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis: PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA), necrotizing granulomas in sinuses, nose, and lungs, plus crescentic GN.","Microscopic polyangiitis: MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA), lung capillaritis and GN, no granulomas, spares the upper airway.","Polyarteritis nodosa: medium arteries, ANCA negative, spares glomeruli and lungs; renal artery microaneurysms cause infarcts and renin-driven hypertension; linked to hepatitis B.","IgA vasculitis in children: IgA complexes after an upper respiratory infection cause palpable purpura on buttocks and legs, arthralgia, abdominal pain, and intussusception.","IgA vasculitis kidney lesion: mesangial IgA deposits, the same as IgA nephropathy; platelets are normal."]},{"h":"Urinary tract defenses","points":["One-way urine flow and complete bladder emptying wash bacteria out.","The ureter runs obliquely through the bladder wall, so a filling bladder squeezes it shut (flap valve) and stops reflux.","Infected urothelial cells shed into urine; uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) binds E. coli type 1 fimbriae.","Obstruction (stones, prostate enlargement, valves) causes stasis; vesicoureteral reflux carries bladder urine to the kidney.","Catheters bypass the urethra and carry biofilm; pregnancy relaxes and compresses the ureters, so bacteriuria often becomes pyelonephritis."]},{"h":"Tumor immunity and the hepatitis B vaccine","points":["CD8 T cells recognize renal cell carcinoma peptides on MHC class I and kill with perforin and granzymes.","NK cells kill cells that lose MHC class I (missing self) and carry stress ligands.","Tumors escape by losing MHC class I and by expressing PD-L1, which binds PD-1 on T cells and shuts them down.","The recombinant hepatitis B surface antigen vaccine induces T cell-dependent IgG anti-HBs.","Anti-HBs alone (anti-HBc negative) means vaccine immunity; anti-HBs with anti-HBc means a past infection that resolved.","Preventing hepatitis B prevents its membranous nephropathy, which comes from viral antigen immune complexes."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Immune mechanism, immunofluorescence, and complement","cols":["Disease","Mechanism","Immunofluorescence","Complement"],"rows":[["Anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture)","Type II, anti-alpha-3 type IV collagen IgG","Linear IgG","Normal"],["Poststreptococcal GN","Type III immune complex","Granular IgG and C3","Low C3"],["Lupus nephritis","Type III immune complex","Granular, full house","Low C3 and C4"],["Membranoproliferative GN","Immune complex or C3 nephritic factor","Granular","Low C3"],["IgA nephropathy and IgA vasculitis","Type III, IgA complexes","Mesangial IgA","Normal"],["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Pauci-immune, PR3-ANCA","Little or none","Normal"],["Microscopic polyangiitis","Pauci-immune, MPO-ANCA","Little or none","Normal"]]},{"title":"Hepatitis B serology","cols":["Pattern","HBsAg","Anti-HBs","Anti-HBc"],"rows":[["Vaccinated","Negative","Positive","Negative"],["Past infection, resolved","Negative","Positive","Positive"],["Chronic infection","Positive","Negative","Positive"],["Never exposed or vaccinated","Negative","Negative","Negative"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Linear, lumpy, or little","text":"Linear IgG is anti-GBM, lumpy granular deposits are immune complexes, and little or no staining is pauci-immune ANCA disease."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Vaccine shows S only","text":"The vaccine contains surface antigen only, so a vaccinated person makes anti-HBs and never anti-HBc."},{"kind":"trick","label":"PR3 is c, MPO is p","text":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis is PR3 and cytoplasmic staining; microscopic polyangiitis is MPO and perinuclear staining."}],"highYield":["Hemoptysis plus hematuria with linear IgG on biopsy is Goodpasture syndrome.","Low C3 and C4 together point to lupus nephritis.","IgA nephropathy, anti-GBM disease, and ANCA vasculitis keep normal complement.","Saddle nose, sinusitis, lung nodules, and crescentic GN with PR3-ANCA mean granulomatosis with polyangiitis.","Polyarteritis nodosa spares glomeruli and causes renal artery microaneurysms.","Palpable purpura on the buttocks and legs of a child after a cold is IgA vasculitis.","PD-L1 on tumor cells turns off PD-1 bearing T cells."],"pitfalls":["IgA nephropathy and IgA vasculitis share the same kidney lesion; the vasculitis adds skin, joint, and gut involvement.","Microscopic polyangiitis looks like granulomatosis with polyangiitis in the lung and kidney but has no granulomas and no upper airway disease.","A cell that loses MHC class I escapes CD8 T cells but becomes a target for NK cells.","Anti-HBs is protective, while anti-HBc shows real exposure to the virus."],"dumps":[{"t":"Glomerular immune patterns","p":"Draw three glomerular capillary loops side by side: linear, granular, and pauci-immune immunofluorescence. Under each, write the hypersensitivity type, an example disease, and the complement level.","l":["Linear: type II, anti-GBM IgG","Anti-GBM target is the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","Granular: type III immune complexes","Granular examples: poststreptococcal GN, lupus, IgA nephropathy","Pauci-immune: ANCA-mediated neutrophil injury","Pauci-immune examples: granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis","Low C3 in poststreptococcal and membranoproliferative GN, low C3 and C4 in lupus","Normal complement in anti-GBM, ANCA, and IgA disease"]},{"t":"Keeping bacteria out","p":"Draw the bladder, ureterovesical junction, and urethra. Label each defense against infection, then list four conditions that break those defenses and how.","l":["One-way flow and complete emptying flush bacteria","Oblique intramural ureter acts as a flap valve","Urothelial cells shed with attached bacteria","Uromodulin binds type 1 fimbriae","Obstruction causes stasis","Vesicoureteral reflux carries bacteria to the kidney","Catheters bypass the urethra and carry biofilm","Pregnancy dilates and compresses the ureters"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity: Disorders Caused by Immune Responses"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on immune responses against tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney (section on glomerular diseases)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (section on vasculitis)"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-12-A","UR-12-H","UR-12-G","UR-11"]},"UR-11":{"id":"UR-11","summary":"The bacteria, parasites, and viruses behind urinary tract infection, urethritis, stones, and infection-linked glomerular disease. Questions test virulence factors, lab identification, how to read a urinalysis, and which infection leads to which kidney lesion.","notes":[{"h":"Escherichia coli and other uropathogens","points":["Uropathogenic E. coli: gram-negative, lactose-fermenting rod from the patient's own fecal flora; causes most community cystitis (roughly 75 to 95 percent).","Type 1 fimbriae bind mannose on bladder urothelium and cause cystitis; P fimbriae bind P blood group antigens on upper tract cells and cause pyelonephritis.","Staphylococcus saprophyticus: gram-positive coagulase-negative coccus, novobiocin resistant, second most common cause in young sexually active women.","Klebsiella: gram-negative lactose fermenter with a thick polysaccharide capsule that makes mucoid colonies.","Enterococcus: gram-positive cocci in pairs and chains, hardy in bile and salt, common after instrumentation.","Pseudomonas aeruginosa: oxidase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod that forms biofilm on catheters."]},{"h":"Proteus and struvite","points":["Proteus mirabilis urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide.","Ammonia raises urine pH, and magnesium ammonium phosphate (struvite) precipitates.","Struvite grows into staghorn calculi that fill the renal pelvis and calyces and shelter bacteria.","Proteus swarms across blood agar in waves, a clue to its identity in the lab."]},{"h":"Reading the urinalysis","points":["Leukocyte esterase comes from neutrophils and marks pyuria.","Nitrite forms when Enterobacterales reduce dietary nitrate; gram-positive organisms such as Enterococcus and S. saprophyticus leave it negative.","WBC casts form inside tubules, so they place the infection in the kidney (pyelonephritis).","Sterile pyuria (white cells but a negative routine culture): think urethritis from chlamydia or gonorrhea, or renal tuberculosis."]},{"h":"Urethritis","points":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae: gram-negative diplococci, often inside neutrophils; pili attach to mucosa and change antigens, so immunity does not last.","Gonococcal IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA; infection gives purulent discharge a few days after exposure.","Chlamydia trachomatis serovars D to K: obligate intracellular; the infectious elementary body enters the cell and becomes a reticulate body that divides inside an inclusion.","Chlamydia often causes scant discharge or none, and it is invisible on Gram stain.","Nucleic acid amplification testing of first-catch urine or a swab diagnoses both organisms."]},{"h":"Infection-linked glomerular disease","points":["Nephritogenic group A streptococci: poststreptococcal GN about 1 to 3 weeks after pharyngitis or 3 to 6 weeks after impetigo.","Hepatitis B: membranous nephropathy (also polyarteritis nodosa).","Hepatitis C: membranoproliferative GN with mixed cryoglobulinemia and low complement.","HIV: collapsing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (HIV-associated nephropathy) with heavy proteinuria."]},{"h":"Tuberculosis and schistosomiasis","points":["Renal tuberculosis spreads through the blood from the lung and forms caseating granulomas; urine shows sterile pyuria on routine culture.","Schistosoma haematobium: freshwater snails release cercariae that penetrate skin.","Adult worms live in the venules around the bladder; eggs with a terminal spine cross the bladder wall into urine.","Egg granulomas cause hematuria and fibrosis and raise the risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Urinary pathogens","cols":["Organism","Gram stain and lab clue","Key virulence or feature","Typical setting"],"rows":[["Escherichia coli","Gram-negative rod, lactose fermenter","Type 1 fimbriae (cystitis), P fimbriae (pyelonephritis)","Most community UTIs"],["Staphylococcus saprophyticus","Gram-positive cocci, coagulase negative, novobiocin resistant","Adheres to urothelium","Young sexually active women"],["Klebsiella","Gram-negative rod, lactose fermenter, mucoid","Polysaccharide capsule","Hospital, diabetes"],["Proteus mirabilis","Gram-negative rod, non-lactose fermenter, swarming","Urease, struvite stones","Recurrent UTI with stones"],["Enterococcus","Gram-positive cocci in chains, nitrite negative","Tolerates bile and salt","Instrumentation, catheters, hospital"],["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Gram-negative rod, oxidase positive, non-lactose fermenter","Biofilm","Catheters"]]},{"title":"Gonorrhea and chlamydia","cols":["Feature","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Chlamydia trachomatis D to K"],"rows":[["Organism","Gram-negative diplococcus","Obligate intracellular bacterium"],["Key factors","Pili with antigenic variation, IgA protease","Elementary body (infects) and reticulate body (divides)"],["Discharge","Purulent","Scant or none"],["Gram stain","Diplococci in neutrophils","No organisms seen"],["Diagnosis","Nucleic acid amplification test","Nucleic acid amplification test"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"P for pyelo","text":"P fimbriae take E. coli up to the kidney (pyelonephritis); type 1 fimbriae keep it in the bladder."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Elementary is entry, reticulate replicates","text":"The small elementary body enters the cell; the larger reticulate body reproduces inside it."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sterile pyuria list","text":"White cells with no growth on routine culture: think chlamydia, gonorrhea, and tuberculosis first."}],"highYield":["E. coli causes most UTIs; P fimbriae mark pyelonephritis strains.","Novobiocin resistance separates S. saprophyticus from S. epidermidis.","Urease makes urine alkaline and builds struvite staghorn stones.","Nitrite needs gram-negative nitrate reductase; Enterococcus and S. saprophyticus test negative.","WBC casts mean the kidney is involved.","Hepatitis C causes membranoproliferative GN with cryoglobulins; HIV causes collapsing FSGS.","Schistosoma haematobium links to squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder."],"pitfalls":["A negative nitrite does not rule out infection with a gram-positive organism.","WBCs alone in urine can come from the bladder; WBC casts place them in the kidney.","Reinfection with gonorrhea is common because pilin antigenic variation keeps antibodies from protecting.","Schistosoma mansoni and japonicum affect the liver and gut; haematobium affects the bladder."],"dumps":[{"t":"Schistosoma haematobium life cycle","p":"Draw the life cycle of Schistosoma haematobium from egg to adult. Label the hosts, the infective stage, where adults live, and how the eggs injure the bladder.","l":["Eggs passed in urine hatch in fresh water","Freshwater snail is the intermediate host","Cercariae penetrate intact skin","Larvae travel by blood through lungs and liver","Adults live in the venous plexus around the bladder","Eggs have a terminal spine","Egg granulomas cause hematuria and bladder fibrosis","Chronic injury raises squamous cell carcinoma risk"]},{"t":"Urinalysis in infection","p":"Make a table with columns for leukocyte esterase, nitrite, WBC casts, and culture. Fill in the expected result for E. coli cystitis, E. coli pyelonephritis, Enterococcus cystitis, chlamydial urethritis, and renal tuberculosis.","l":["Leukocyte esterase positive in all five (pyuria)","Nitrite positive with E. coli, negative with Enterococcus","Nitrite negative in chlamydial urethritis","WBC casts only in pyelonephritis","Routine culture grows E. coli and Enterococcus","Routine culture negative in chlamydia and tuberculosis (sterile pyuria)","Chlamydia found by nucleic acid amplification test"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on enteric gram-negative rods"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Sections on the neisseriae and chlamydiae"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney (sections on pyelonephritis and glomerular disease)"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-12-H","UR-12-C","UR-12-A","UR-10","HE-9-E3"]},"UR-12-A":{"id":"UR-12-A","summary":"Glomerulonephritis, glomerulosclerosis, nephrotic syndromes, and acute and chronic renal failure. Cases describe the urine, the biopsy, and the labs; you sort nephritic from nephrotic, name the lesion, and predict the complications.","notes":[{"h":"Nephritic versus nephrotic","points":["Nephritic: inflammation tears capillary walls, giving hematuria with dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts, oliguria, azotemia, hypertension, and proteinuria under 3.5 g/day.","Nephrotic: podocyte or membrane damage lets protein through, giving proteinuria over 3.5 g/day, low albumin, edema, hyperlipidemia, and fatty casts.","Nephrotic complications: edema from low oncotic pressure, high lipids from more hepatic lipoprotein synthesis, clots from urinary antithrombin III loss (renal vein thrombosis), infections from immunoglobulin loss."]},{"h":"Nephritic diseases","points":["Poststreptococcal GN: weeks after strep throat or impetigo; cola urine, periorbital edema, hypertension, low C3, granular IgG and C3, subepithelial humps on EM.","IgA nephropathy: gross hematuria within a day or two of a mucosal infection, mesangial IgA, normal C3; the most common GN worldwide.","Rapidly progressive GN: crescents of fibrin, parietal epithelial cells, and macrophages in most glomeruli, with kidney failure over weeks.","Crescentic types by immunofluorescence: linear (anti-GBM), granular (immune complex), little or none (pauci-immune, ANCA).","Lupus nephritis: diffuse proliferative class is most common and most severe, with subendothelial wire-loop deposits and a nephritic picture; membranous class gives nephrotic syndrome.","Lupus labs: low C3 and C4 and anti-dsDNA antibodies, which track nephritis activity.","Membranoproliferative GN: mesangial cells grow into the capillary wall and split the GBM (tram-track); immune complex type links to hepatitis B and C; dense deposit disease links to C3 nephritic factor."]},{"h":"Nephrotic diseases","points":["Minimal change disease: children; selective albuminuria; normal light microscopy and immunofluorescence; foot process effacement on EM; excellent steroid response; linked to Hodgkin lymphoma.","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis: scarring in parts of some glomeruli from podocyte injury, poor steroid response; secondary to HIV, heroin, sickle cell disease, and obesity.","Collapsing FSGS (HIV-associated nephropathy) collapses the capillary tuft and progresses fastest.","Membranous nephropathy: adults; subepithelial deposits with spike and dome GBM; anti-PLA2R antibodies in primary disease.","Secondary membranous causes: lupus, hepatitis B, solid tumors, and drugs such as NSAIDs; it carries the highest renal vein thrombosis risk.","Amyloidosis: AL (light chains) or AA (chronic inflammation) deposits in the glomerulus; Congo red shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light."]},{"h":"Diabetic glomerulosclerosis","points":["Early hyperfiltration raises GFR and strains the glomerulus.","Nonenzymatic glycation stiffens and thickens the basement membrane; hyaline arteriolosclerosis hits both afferent and efferent arterioles.","Mesangial matrix expands, first diffusely, then as nodules (Kimmelstiel-Wilson).","Course: microalbuminuria (30 to 300 mg/day), then overt proteinuria, nephrotic range, and kidney failure."]},{"h":"Acute kidney injury","points":["Prerenal (low perfusion): BUN to creatinine over 20, FENa under 1 percent, urine osmolality over 500 mOsm/kg; tubules work and hold sodium.","Intrinsic (acute tubular necrosis): BUN to creatinine under 15, FENa over 2 percent, urine osmolality under 350 mOsm/kg; tubules cannot hold sodium or concentrate.","Postrenal (obstruction): early values can look prerenal; with time they look like tubular injury."]},{"h":"Chronic kidney disease","points":["Diabetes and hypertension are the two leading causes.","Uremia: fibrinous pericarditis, encephalopathy with asterixis, nausea, and platelet dysfunction with bleeding.","Also hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, volume overload with hypertension, normocytic anemia from low EPO, and renal osteodystrophy.","Waxy casts mark advanced chronic disease; kidneys are usually small and scarred."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Listed conditions","cols":["Condition","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Glomerulonephritis (nephritic)","Immune injury to capillary walls","Hematuria, RBC casts, hypertension, oliguria","Rapidly progressive GN, chronic kidney disease"],["Glomerulosclerosis (FSGS, diabetic)","Podocyte loss or matrix expansion scars the glomerulus","Proteinuria, often nephrotic range","Progressive kidney failure"],["Nephrotic syndromes","Podocyte or GBM damage leaks protein","Proteinuria over 3.5 g/day, edema, high lipids, fatty casts","Thrombosis, infection, hyperlipidemia"],["Renal failure (acute and chronic)","Falling GFR from prerenal, intrinsic, or postrenal causes; nephron loss over time","Rising BUN and creatinine, oliguria, waxy casts in chronic disease","Hyperkalemia, acidosis, uremia, anemia, osteodystrophy"]]},{"title":"Glomerular diseases at a glance","cols":["Disease","Light microscopy","Immunofluorescence","Electron microscopy","Complement"],"rows":[["Poststreptococcal GN","Hypercellular glomeruli with neutrophils","Granular IgG, C3","Subepithelial humps","Low C3"],["IgA nephropathy","Mesangial proliferation","Mesangial IgA","Mesangial deposits","Normal"],["Anti-GBM disease","Crescents","Linear IgG","No deposits","Normal"],["Diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis","Wire loops","Granular, full house","Subendothelial deposits","Low C3 and C4"],["Membranoproliferative GN","Tram-track GBM","Granular","Subendothelial or dense intramembranous deposits","Low C3"],["Minimal change disease","Normal","Negative","Foot process effacement","Normal"],["FSGS","Segmental sclerosis","Negative, or IgM and C3 in scars","Foot process effacement","Normal"],["Membranous nephropathy","Thick GBM, spikes on silver stain","Granular IgG, C3","Subepithelial deposits","Normal"],["Diabetic nephropathy","Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules","Nonspecific linear IgG and albumin","Thick GBM, mesangial expansion","Normal"],["Amyloidosis","Pink acellular deposits, Congo red positive","Light chain staining in AL","Randomly arranged fibrils","Normal"]]},{"title":"Urine casts","cols":["Cast","Where it forms and why","Points to"],"rows":[["RBC cast","RBCs leak through a damaged glomerulus","Glomerulonephritis"],["WBC cast","Neutrophils in the tubules or interstitium","Pyelonephritis, interstitial nephritis"],["Muddy brown granular cast","Dead tubular cells","Acute tubular necrosis"],["Fatty cast","Lipid-laden tubular cells","Nephrotic syndrome"],["Waxy cast","Old casts in dilated, slow tubules","Advanced chronic kidney disease"]]},{"title":"Acute kidney injury indices","cols":["Index","Prerenal","Intrinsic (tubular necrosis)","Postrenal"],"rows":[["BUN to creatinine","Over 20","Under 15","Variable, often over 15"],["FENa","Under 1 percent","Over 2 percent","Under 1 percent early, over 2 percent later"],["Urine osmolality (mOsm/kg)","Over 500","Under 350","Under 350 later"],["Urine sodium (mEq/L)","Under 20","Over 40","Over 40 later"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"MAD HUNGER","text":"Consequences of kidney failure: Metabolic Acidosis, Dyslipidemia, Hyperkalemia, Uremia, Na and water retention, Growth delay, Erythropoietin failure, Renal osteodystrophy."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Days versus weeks","text":"Hematuria within days of a sore throat is IgA nephropathy; hematuria weeks later with low C3 is poststreptococcal GN."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Where the deposit sits","text":"Subepithelial (outside the GBM): poststreptococcal humps and membranous spikes. Subendothelial (inside): lupus wire loops and immune complex MPGN."}],"highYield":["Over 3.5 g/day of protein defines nephrotic range.","Minimal change disease is the top cause of nephrotic syndrome in children; it responds to steroids.","Membranous nephropathy with anti-PLA2R is primary; look for lupus, hepatitis B, tumors, and NSAIDs if PLA2R is negative.","Renal vein thrombosis follows antithrombin III loss, most often in membranous nephropathy.","Crescents mean rapidly progressive GN; immunofluorescence tells which type.","Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules and efferent arteriole hyalinosis are signs of diabetic nephropathy.","FENa under 1 percent means the tubules still hold sodium (prerenal).","Uremic bleeding comes from platelet dysfunction with a normal platelet count."],"pitfalls":["IgA nephropathy and poststreptococcal GN both follow infections; timing and C3 tell them apart.","Membranous nephropathy and lupus class V share subepithelial deposits; check ANA and anti-dsDNA.","FSGS and minimal change both show effacement on EM; FSGS has scars on light microscopy and resists steroids.","Diuretics raise FENa, so a prerenal patient on diuretics can look intrinsic.","Diabetic nephropathy and amyloidosis keep kidneys normal size or large, unlike most chronic kidney disease."],"dumps":[{"t":"Nephritic versus nephrotic table","p":"Build a two-column table on blank paper: nephritic and nephrotic. Fill in the urine findings, protein amount, blood pressure, edema pattern, and three example diseases for each.","l":["Nephritic: hematuria, dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts","Nephritic: protein under 3.5 g/day, oliguria, azotemia, hypertension","Nephritic examples: poststreptococcal, IgA, rapidly progressive GN","Nephrotic: protein over 3.5 g/day, low albumin","Nephrotic: edema, hyperlipidemia, fatty casts with Maltese crosses","Nephrotic examples: minimal change, FSGS, membranous","Nephrotic risks: thrombosis and infection"]},{"t":"Glomerular capillary deposits","p":"Draw one glomerular capillary loop with endothelium, GBM, and podocytes. Mark where deposits sit in poststreptococcal GN, membranous nephropathy, lupus wire loops, IgA nephropathy, and anti-GBM disease.","l":["Endothelium faces the lumen, podocytes face Bowman space","Subepithelial humps: poststreptococcal GN","Subepithelial spikes and domes: membranous nephropathy","Subendothelial deposits: diffuse proliferative lupus (wire loops)","Mesangial deposits: IgA nephropathy","Linear antibody along the whole GBM: anti-GBM disease","Effaced foot processes without deposits: minimal change disease"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Renal Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on acute kidney injury and chronic kidney disease"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-10","UR-11","UR-8","UR-12-B","EN-12-D"]},"UR-12-B":{"id":"UR-12-B","summary":"Acute tubular necrosis, the most common cause of intrinsic acute kidney injury, and Fanconi syndrome, a global failure of the proximal tubule. Questions ask for the cause, the cast, the phase of injury, and the lab pattern.","notes":[{"h":"How tubular necrosis happens","points":["Ischemic: shock, sepsis, or long prerenal states starve tubular cells of oxygen.","Nephrotoxic: drugs, pigments, and poisons directly injure tubular cells.","Most vulnerable: the straight proximal tubule and the medullary thick ascending limb, which use a lot of ATP in low-oxygen areas.","Dead cells slough into the lumen and form casts that block flow and raise tubular pressure, lowering GFR.","Filtrate leaks back through the damaged wall, and afferent arteriole constriction lowers filtration further.","Urine: muddy brown granular casts and renal tubular epithelial cells; FENa over 2 percent."]},{"h":"Three phases","points":["Initiation (about 36 hours): the insult; urine output starts to fall.","Maintenance (1 to 3 weeks): oliguria, rising BUN and creatinine, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and volume overload; hyperkalemia is the main danger.","Recovery: tubular cells regrow before they can concentrate urine, so polyuria washes out water, sodium, and potassium (risk of hypokalemia and dehydration)."]},{"h":"Nephrotoxins","points":["Myoglobin from rhabdomyolysis (crush injury, extreme exertion, seizures); hemoglobin from intravascular hemolysis.","Aminoglycosides build up in proximal tubule cells; injury usually appears after about a week of therapy and is often nonoliguric.","Iodinated contrast constricts renal vessels and injures tubules, worst in diabetes and existing kidney disease.","Cisplatin injures the proximal tubule and wastes magnesium; ethylene glycol leaves calcium oxalate crystals.","Rhabdomyolysis: dipstick positive for blood (heme) but few or no RBCs on microscopy, very high CK, high potassium and phosphate, low calcium early."]},{"h":"Fanconi syndrome","points":["Generalized proximal tubule failure: every proximal transporter leaks.","Glucosuria with normal plasma glucose, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and bicarbonaturia.","Bicarbonate loss gives proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis, a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis.","Phosphate loss causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults; potassium and uric acid are also lost.","Causes: cystinosis (most common inherited cause in children), Wilson disease, multiple myeloma light chains, heavy metals such as lead, and drugs such as tenofovir and ifosfamide."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Ischemic versus nephrotoxic tubular necrosis","cols":["Feature","Ischemic","Nephrotoxic"],"rows":[["Cause","Shock, sepsis, hemorrhage, long prerenal state","Drugs, pigments, contrast, poisons"],["Pattern of necrosis","Patchy, along many segments","Continuous along the proximal tubule"],["Basement membrane","Often ruptured","Usually intact"],["Urine","Muddy brown granular casts, FENa over 2 percent","Muddy brown granular casts, FENa over 2 percent"]]},{"title":"Nephrotoxins and their clues","cols":["Toxin","Source or setting","Clue"],"rows":[["Myoglobin","Rhabdomyolysis","Blood on dipstick, no RBCs, very high CK"],["Hemoglobin","Intravascular hemolysis, transfusion reaction","Blood on dipstick, no RBCs, pink plasma, low haptoglobin"],["Aminoglycosides","Antibiotic therapy","Injury after about a week, often nonoliguric"],["Iodinated contrast","Imaging studies","Creatinine rises within 1 to 3 days"],["Cisplatin","Chemotherapy","Low magnesium"],["Ethylene glycol","Antifreeze ingestion","Calcium oxalate crystals, anion and osmolal gaps"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Blood without blood","text":"A dipstick that reads heme while the microscope shows no red cells means myoglobin or free hemoglobin."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Recovery drains","text":"In the recovery phase the filter returns before the tubules can concentrate, so expect polyuria with low potassium."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fanconi leaks everything small","text":"Glucose, amino acids, phosphate, bicarbonate, uric acid, and potassium all spill because the whole proximal tubule fails."}],"highYield":["Muddy brown granular casts mean acute tubular necrosis.","The straight proximal tubule and thick ascending limb are the most ischemia-sensitive segments.","Death in the oliguric phase usually comes from hyperkalemia.","Rhabdomyolysis: dipstick positive for blood, no RBCs, very high CK.","Aminoglycoside injury is often nonoliguric.","Fanconi syndrome: glucosuria with normal plasma glucose plus a normal anion gap acidosis.","Cystinosis is the classic inherited cause of Fanconi syndrome in children."],"pitfalls":["Prerenal injury has FENa under 1 percent and bland urine; tubular necrosis has FENa over 2 percent and granular casts.","Myoglobinuria leaves plasma clear; hemoglobinuria turns plasma pink.","Fanconi glucosuria occurs with normal plasma glucose; diabetic glucosuria needs plasma glucose above about 200 mg/dL.","Hyperkalemia belongs to the maintenance phase; hypokalemia belongs to recovery."],"dumps":[{"t":"Course of tubular necrosis","p":"Draw a timeline of acute tubular necrosis with urine output on the vertical axis. Label each phase and write the main electrolyte and fluid problems under it.","l":["Initiation: insult, output starts to fall (about 36 hours)","Maintenance: oliguria for about 1 to 3 weeks","Maintenance: hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, volume overload","Maintenance: rising BUN and creatinine","Recovery: polyuria as tubules regrow","Recovery: loss of potassium, sodium, and water","Muddy brown granular casts during injury"]},{"t":"Nephron injury map","p":"Draw a nephron. Mark the segments most hurt by ischemia, show how sloughed cells block the tubule, and list what the proximal tubule loses in Fanconi syndrome.","l":["Straight proximal tubule is highly vulnerable","Medullary thick ascending limb is highly vulnerable","Sloughed cells form casts that block flow","Back-leak of filtrate into the interstitium","Fanconi: glucose, amino acids, phosphate, bicarbonate lost","Bicarbonate loss gives proximal renal tubular acidosis","Fanconi causes include cystinosis, Wilson disease, myeloma"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney (section on acute tubular injury)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Renal Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on acute kidney injury and chronic kidney disease"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-12-A","UR-8","UR-9","UR-6"]},"UR-12-C":{"id":"UR-12-C","summary":"Urinary obstruction and kidney stones: what blocks the flow at each age, what back pressure does to the kidney, and how stone type follows urine chemistry. Questions pair a patient with a likely cause, stone composition, or complication.","notes":[{"h":"Causes of hydronephrosis","points":["Newborn boys: posterior urethral valves block the bladder outlet and back up both kidneys.","Infants and children: ureteropelvic junction obstruction, the most common cause of congenital hydronephrosis.","Young adults: stones.","Pregnancy: progesterone relaxes ureteral muscle and the uterus compresses the ureters, more on the right.","Older men: prostatic enlargement (benign hyperplasia or cancer).","Women: pelvic tumors such as cervical cancer; any age: retroperitoneal fibrosis or neurogenic bladder."]},{"h":"How back pressure injures the kidney","points":["Pressure in the pelvis and tubules rises and opposes filtration, so GFR falls.","Filtration continues at a lower rate and filtrate leaks back into the interstitium.","High pressure compresses medullary vessels; the papillae lose blood flow and atrophy first, so concentrating ability fails early.","With time the cortex thins and scars, leaving a dilated pelvis surrounded by a thin rim of tissue."]},{"h":"One side or both","points":["Unilateral obstruction can stay silent for a long time because the other kidney keeps creatinine normal.","Bilateral obstruction (or bladder outlet block) causes postrenal acute kidney injury with oliguria or anuria.","After relief, postobstructive diuresis washes out retained urea, sodium, and water, and damaged tubules cannot concentrate; watch for dehydration and low potassium."]},{"h":"Stone types","points":["Calcium oxalate: the most common stone; hypercalciuria is the usual cause; also hyperoxaluria after ileal disease and ethylene glycol.","Calcium phosphate: forms in alkaline urine, as in distal renal tubular acidosis and primary hyperparathyroidism.","Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate): urease-producing bacteria such as Proteus alkalinize urine; forms staghorn calculi.","Uric acid: forms in acidic urine; radiolucent on plain X-ray; seen in gout and tumor lysis.","Cystine: hexagonal crystals in cystinuria, often in children, can form staghorns."]},{"h":"Renal colic and complications","points":["A stone in the ureter causes waves of severe flank pain radiating to the groin, with hematuria and nausea.","Stones lodge at three narrow points: the ureteropelvic junction, where the ureter crosses the iliac vessels, and the ureterovesical junction (narrowest).","Complications: obstruction, hydronephrosis, and infection above the stone.","An infected obstructed kidney can turn into pyonephrosis and sepsis and needs urgent drainage."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Listed conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Hydronephrosis","Posterior urethral valves, UPJ obstruction, stones, pregnancy, prostate enlargement, pelvic tumors","Back pressure lowers GFR and compresses medullary vessels","Dilated pelvis and calyces, thin cortex","Postrenal AKI if bilateral, infection, chronic kidney disease"],["Renal calculi","Hypercalciuria, urine pH extremes, urease-producing bacteria, gout, cystinuria, low urine volume","Supersaturated urine crystallizes","Renal colic, hematuria","Obstruction, hydronephrosis, infection above the stone"]]},{"title":"Kidney stones","cols":["Stone","Urine pH","Crystal shape","X-ray","Key causes"],"rows":[["Calcium oxalate","Mostly independent of pH","Envelope or dumbbell","Radiopaque","Hypercalciuria, ileal disease, ethylene glycol"],["Calcium phosphate","Alkaline","Wedge-shaped prism","Radiopaque","Distal renal tubular acidosis, primary hyperparathyroidism"],["Struvite","Alkaline","Coffin lid","Radiopaque","Urease-producing bacteria (Proteus)"],["Uric acid","Acidic","Rhomboid or rosette","Radiolucent","Gout, tumor lysis"],["Cystine","Acidic","Hexagonal","Faintly radiopaque","Cystinuria"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Three tight spots","text":"Picture the stone's trip: it sticks where the pelvis meets the ureter, where the ureter crosses the iliac vessels, and where it enters the bladder."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Alkaline builds phosphate","text":"Phosphate stones (calcium phosphate and struvite) need alkaline urine; uric acid and cystine stones need acidic urine."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Papillae first","text":"Obstruction hurts the inner medulla before the cortex, so the first lost function is the ability to concentrate urine."}],"highYield":["Posterior urethral valves are the most common cause of bladder outlet obstruction in newborn boys.","Ureteropelvic junction obstruction is the most common cause of congenital hydronephrosis.","Unilateral obstruction may not raise creatinine; bilateral obstruction causes anuria.","Postobstructive diuresis follows relief of a long obstruction.","Calcium oxalate is the most common stone; hypercalciuria is the most common cause.","Uric acid stones do not show on plain X-ray.","Proteus urease makes struvite staghorn stones."],"pitfalls":["Calcium phosphate and calcium oxalate are both radiopaque; urine pH and crystal shape separate them.","Struvite and cystine can both form staghorns; struvite needs alkaline urine and infection, cystine shows hexagonal crystals.","A normal creatinine does not rule out obstruction of one kidney.","Hydronephrosis in pregnancy is usually physiologic and right-sided."],"dumps":[{"t":"Stone table","p":"Build a table on blank paper for the five main kidney stones. Fill in urine pH, crystal shape, X-ray appearance, and two causes for each.","l":["Calcium oxalate: most common, envelope crystals, radiopaque","Calcium oxalate causes: hypercalciuria, ileal disease, ethylene glycol","Calcium phosphate: alkaline urine, distal RTA, hyperparathyroidism","Struvite: alkaline urine, urease bacteria, coffin-lid crystals, staghorn","Uric acid: acidic urine, radiolucent, gout and tumor lysis","Cystine: hexagonal crystals, cystinuria","Low urine volume raises risk for every type"]},{"t":"Obstruction by age","p":"Draw the urinary tract from kidney to urethra. Mark where obstruction happens in a newborn boy, a child, a young adult, a pregnant woman, an older man, and an older woman, then write what happens to GFR and the papillae.","l":["Newborn boy: posterior urethral valves","Child: ureteropelvic junction obstruction","Young adult: stone at a narrow point of the ureter","Pregnancy: ureters dilated and compressed, right more than left","Older man: prostatic enlargement","Older woman: pelvic tumor such as cervical cancer","Back pressure lowers GFR","Papillae atrophy first, so concentrating ability fails early"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney (sections on urinary tract obstruction and urolithiasis)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Renal Disease"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Section on the ureters"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-11","UR-9","UR-12-H","UR-12-E","UR-3"]},"UR-12-D":{"id":"UR-12-D","summary":"This unit covers inflammation of the kidney interstitium and bladder that is not a primary glomerular disease: drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis, analgesic nephropathy with papillary necrosis, chronic and xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, and interstitial cystitis. Exam items give a drug history or a scarred kidney and ask you to name the lesion, its cause, and what it will do to renal function.","notes":[{"h":"Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis (AIN)","points":["A hypersensitivity reaction (mostly T cell mediated, type IV) in the renal interstitium, starting days to weeks after a new drug, often about 2 weeks.","Common drugs: penicillins (classically methicillin), cephalosporins, NSAIDs, proton pump inhibitors, diuretics (thiazides, furosemide), sulfonamides, rifampin, allopurinol.","Findings: fever, rash, eosinophilia, rising creatinine, pyuria with WBC casts, and sometimes eosinophils in the urine (an insensitive test).","The full triad of fever, rash, and eosinophilia appears in only a minority of patients.","Histology: interstitial edema with lymphocytes, macrophages, and eosinophils; tubulitis; some drugs give small non-necrotizing granulomas; glomeruli are spared.","NSAIDs can cause AIN together with nephrotic-range proteinuria (minimal change pattern) and often without fever or rash.","Usually recovers over weeks once the drug is stopped; delay allows interstitial fibrosis."]},{"h":"Analgesic nephropathy and papillary necrosis","points":["Years of heavy use of combination analgesics (classically phenacetin with aspirin and caffeine) cause chronic interstitial nephritis with papillary necrosis.","The papillary tip is the most poorly perfused part of the kidney; aspirin blocks vasodilating prostaglandins and drug metabolites cause oxidative injury, so the papillae die first.","Cortex over a necrotic papilla then atrophies and scars, giving a chronic tubulointerstitial pattern.","Clinical: polyuria with poor concentrating ability, sterile pyuria, hematuria, slowly rising creatinine.","A sloughed papilla can pass down the ureter, causing colic and gross hematuria, or obstruct it.","Long-term phenacetin use raises the risk of urothelial carcinoma of the renal pelvis.","Other causes of papillary necrosis: diabetes mellitus, sickle cell disease or trait, severe acute pyelonephritis, and urinary obstruction."]},{"h":"Chronic pyelonephritis","points":["Two main routes: reflux nephropathy from vesicoureteral reflux beginning in childhood, and chronic obstruction (stones, prostatic enlargement, strictures) with repeated infection.","Gross: coarse, irregular, often asymmetric corticomedullary scars sitting over blunted, dilated, deformed calyces.","Reflux scars favor the upper and lower poles, where compound papillae allow urine to reflux into the kidney.","Histology: interstitial fibrosis, chronic inflammation (lymphocytes, plasma cells), and atrophic dilated tubules filled with pink hyaline casts that look like thyroid follicles (thyroidization).","Clinical: hypertension, polyuria and nocturia from lost concentrating ability, slowly progressive chronic kidney disease; WBC casts during flares.","Scarred kidneys can develop secondary focal segmental glomerulosclerosis with proteinuria."]},{"h":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","points":["A rare chronic destructive form of pyelonephritis, usually one kidney.","Typical setting: an obstructing stone (often staghorn) with Proteus or E. coli infection.","The kidney fills with yellow nodules of lipid-laden foamy macrophages, plus plasma cells, neutrophils, and giant cells.","Presents as an enlarged nonfunctioning kidney with a mass on imaging, so it can mimic renal cell carcinoma; the foamy cells can resemble clear tumor cells."]},{"h":"Interstitial cystitis (bladder pain syndrome)","points":["Chronic bladder or pelvic pain with urgency and frequency, with repeatedly negative urine cultures.","Most often in women; pain typically worsens as the bladder fills and eases after voiding.","A diagnosis of exclusion: rule out infection, stones, and bladder cancer first.","Cystoscopy shows Hunner lesions (red ulcerated patches) in some patients."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Inflammatory kidney and bladder diseases at a glance","cols":["Condition","Cause or setting","Key pathology","Classic findings"],"rows":[["Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis","Penicillins, NSAIDs, PPIs, diuretics, sulfonamides","Interstitial edema with lymphocytes and eosinophils; glomeruli spared","Fever, rash, eosinophilia, WBC casts, rising creatinine days to weeks after the drug"],["Analgesic nephropathy","Years of combination analgesics","Papillary necrosis, then cortical atrophy and interstitial fibrosis","Poor concentrating ability, sterile pyuria, hematuria, sloughed papilla"],["Chronic pyelonephritis","Vesicoureteral reflux or obstruction with repeated infection","Coarse asymmetric scars over blunted calyces; thyroidization","Hypertension, polyuria, nocturia, slowly progressive CKD"],["Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","Obstructing stone with Proteus","Foamy lipid-laden macrophages in yellow nodules","Enlarged nonfunctioning kidney that mimics renal cell carcinoma"],["Interstitial cystitis","Unknown cause; mostly women","Hunner lesions in some patients","Bladder pain with urgency and frequency, negative cultures"]]},{"title":"Causes of renal papillary necrosis","cols":["Cause","Why the papilla dies"],"rows":[["Sickle cell disease or trait","Red cells sickle in the hypoxic, hypertonic medulla and block the vasa recta"],["Acute pyelonephritis (severe)","Intense inflammation and swelling compress papillary blood flow"],["Analgesics","Lost prostaglandin vasodilation plus toxic metabolites in the papilla"],["Diabetes mellitus","Small vessel disease plus frequent infection"],["Urinary obstruction","Back pressure lowers medullary blood flow, often with infection"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SAAD papa with papillary necrosis","text":"Sickle cell disease or trait, Acute pyelonephritis, Analgesics, Diabetes mellitus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"New drug, then kidney rash","text":"If creatinine rises 1 to 3 weeks after starting an antibiotic, PPI, NSAID, or diuretic, and the urine has WBCs and WBC casts without bacteria, think AIN before infection."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Coarse and lopsided versus fine and even","text":"Chronic pyelonephritis gives coarse, asymmetric scars over deformed calyces. Benign nephrosclerosis gives two evenly shrunken kidneys with a fine granular surface."}],"highYield":["Fever, rash, eosinophilia, and WBC casts about 2 weeks after starting a penicillin: drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis.","WBC casts prove the inflammation is in the kidney, as in AIN or pyelonephritis, and not only in the bladder.","Long-term combination analgesic use: papillary necrosis and higher risk of urothelial carcinoma of the renal pelvis.","Thyroidization of tubules with coarse scars over blunted calyces: chronic pyelonephritis.","Reflux nephropathy scars cluster at the upper and lower poles.","Staghorn stone with Proteus and a kidney full of foamy macrophages: xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, a renal cell carcinoma mimic.","Bladder pain with urgency and frequency and repeatedly negative cultures in a woman: interstitial cystitis."],"pitfalls":["AIN and acute pyelonephritis both give WBC casts; AIN has a drug history, eosinophils, and a sterile culture, while pyelonephritis has bacteria and flank pain.","AIN spares the glomeruli, so red cell casts point instead to glomerulonephritis.","Thyroidization is not unique to chronic pyelonephritis but, with coarse scars over deformed calyces, it is the classic picture.","A mass in a kidney with a staghorn stone may be xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, not renal cell carcinoma; the foamy cells are macrophages, not tumor cells."],"dumps":[{"t":"Drug-induced AIN","p":"Write out the story of drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis from the drug to the urine findings. List the drugs, the timing, the immune mechanism, the histology, and the lab results.","l":["Drugs: penicillins, cephalosporins, NSAIDs, PPIs, diuretics, sulfonamides, rifampin, allopurinol","Onset days to weeks after the drug, often about 2 weeks","Mostly T cell mediated hypersensitivity in the interstitium","Interstitial edema with lymphocytes and eosinophils; glomeruli spared","Fever, rash, eosinophilia (full triad in a minority)","Pyuria, WBC casts, eosinophils in urine, negative culture","Rising creatinine that usually improves after stopping the drug","NSAIDs can add nephrotic-range proteinuria"]},{"t":"Chronic pyelonephritis kidney","p":"Draw a kidney with chronic pyelonephritis from reflux, cut in half lengthwise. Label the scars, the calyces, and where the scars are worst, then draw and label a small box of the histology.","l":["Coarse, irregular corticomedullary scars","Scars sit over blunted, dilated, deformed calyces","Upper and lower poles most affected (compound papillae)","Asymmetric involvement of the two kidneys","Interstitial fibrosis with lymphocytes and plasma cells","Atrophic tubules filled with pink hyaline casts (thyroidization)","Causes: vesicoureteral reflux or chronic obstruction","Results: hypertension, nocturia, progressive CKD"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, section on tubulointerstitial nephritis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, section on cystitis"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Section on renal disease"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-12-B","UR-12-E4","UR-12-H1","UR-12-C2","UR-12-F5"]},"UR-12-E":{"id":"UR-12-E","summary":"This unit covers inherited and developmental kidney disease: Alport syndrome, the cystic kidney diseases, renal agenesis, and vesicoureteral reflux. Exam items link a gene or developmental error to the kidney lesion and to findings outside the kidney, such as deafness, liver cysts, brain aneurysms, or Potter sequence.","notes":[{"h":"Alport syndrome","points":["Defect in type IV collagen (alpha 3, 4, or 5 chains) of the glomerular basement membrane, cochlea, and lens.","Most cases are X-linked (COL4A5), so males have more severe disease; autosomal forms also exist.","Hematuria in childhood, then proteinuria and progression to end-stage renal disease in young adulthood.","Extrarenal: sensorineural hearing loss and eye lesions, especially anterior lenticonus.","Electron microscopy: GBM thinned in places and thickened and split in others, giving a basket-weave look."]},{"h":"Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD)","points":["Mutations in PKD1 (polycystin-1, chromosome 16, most cases) or PKD2 (polycystin-2, chromosome 4).","Cysts grow from all parts of the nephron and slowly replace the kidney; symptoms usually start in adulthood.","Bilaterally enlarged lumpy kidneys, flank pain, hematuria, stones, infected cysts, hypertension, progressive renal failure.","Hypertension comes from cysts compressing vessels, causing local ischemia and renin release.","Extrarenal: liver cysts (most common), berry aneurysms with risk of subarachnoid hemorrhage, mitral valve prolapse, colonic diverticula."]},{"h":"Other cystic kidney diseases","points":["Autosomal recessive PKD (PKHD1, fibrocystin): presents in infancy with bilaterally enlarged kidneys made of elongated cysts (dilated collecting ducts) at right angles to the surface.","Severe ARPKD makes little fetal urine, so oligohydramnios and Potter sequence can occur; survivors develop congenital hepatic fibrosis with portal hypertension.","Multicystic dysplastic kidney: abnormal metanephric development, often with ureteral atresia; usually one side, sporadic, and not inherited.","Dysplastic kidney contains cysts plus immature tubules, undifferentiated mesenchyme, and sometimes cartilage; the other kidney compensates.","Acquired cystic disease: many cysts in kidneys of patients on long-term dialysis, with a higher risk of renal cell carcinoma."]},{"h":"Renal agenesis","points":["Failure of the ureteric bud to form or to induce the metanephric mesenchyme.","From about the second trimester, amniotic fluid is mostly fetal urine, so bilateral agenesis causes oligohydramnios.","Too little fluid compresses the fetus and stops normal lung growth: Potter sequence.","Potter sequence: pulmonary hypoplasia (the cause of death), flat face with low-set ears and small chin, limb deformities such as clubfeet.","Unilateral agenesis is usually silent; the single kidney undergoes compensatory hypertrophy.","Unilateral agenesis can come with genital tract anomalies on the same side, because the ureteric bud grows out of the mesonephric duct, next to the paramesonephric duct."]},{"h":"Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR)","points":["Primary VUR: the ureter runs too short a course through the bladder wall, so bladder contraction does not squeeze it shut.","Secondary VUR: high bladder pressure, as with posterior urethral valves or neurogenic bladder.","Urine flows back up to the kidney during voiding, carrying bacteria: recurrent febrile UTIs and pyelonephritis in children.","Repeated infection plus intrarenal reflux causes reflux nephropathy: polar scars, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease.","Voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG) is the diagnostic study and grades the reflux.","Often familial; mild primary reflux often resolves as the child grows and the intravesical ureter lengthens."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Cystic kidney diseases compared","cols":["Disease","Inheritance or cause","Age and sides","Kidney findings","Associated findings"],"rows":[["ADPKD","Autosomal dominant; PKD1 or PKD2","Adults; both kidneys","Huge kidneys with cysts from all nephron segments","Liver cysts, berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse, hypertension"],["ARPKD","Autosomal recessive; PKHD1","Infants; both kidneys","Enlarged kidneys with elongated collecting duct cysts at right angles to the surface","Oligohydramnios, Potter sequence, congenital hepatic fibrosis"],["Multicystic dysplastic kidney","Developmental, not inherited","Infants; usually one kidney","Cysts with immature tubules, mesenchyme, sometimes cartilage","Ureteral atresia; other kidney compensates"],["Acquired cystic disease","Long-term dialysis","Adults with end-stage disease; both kidneys","Many cortical and medullary cysts in scarred kidneys","Higher risk of renal cell carcinoma; cysts may bleed"]]},{"title":"Congenital urinary disorders: lesion and result","cols":["Condition","Basic defect","Main result"],"rows":[["Alport syndrome","Abnormal type IV collagen in GBM, ear, and eye","Hematuria to renal failure, deafness, anterior lenticonus"],["Bilateral renal agenesis","No ureteric bud induction on either side","No fetal urine, oligohydramnios, Potter sequence"],["Unilateral renal agenesis","No ureteric bud induction on one side","Compensatory hypertrophy of the other kidney"],["Primary vesicoureteral reflux","Short intravesical ureter, failed flap valve","Recurrent pyelonephritis and reflux nephropathy scars"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Alport: can't see, can't pee, can't hear high C","text":"Eye lesions (anterior lenticonus), renal disease with hematuria, and sensorineural hearing loss, all from abnormal type IV collagen."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"POTTER","text":"Pulmonary hypoplasia, Oligohydramnios (the trigger), Twisted face, Twisted skin, Extremity defects, Renal failure in utero."},{"kind":"trick","label":"ADPKD in adults, ARPKD in babies","text":"ADPKD shows up in adults. ARPKD shows up in newborns and brings the liver along as congenital hepatic fibrosis."}],"highYield":["Young man with hematuria, hearing loss, and anterior lenticonus: Alport syndrome; split, basket-weave GBM on electron microscopy.","Adult with bilateral flank masses, hypertension, and a family history: ADPKD; sudden worst headache means a ruptured berry aneurysm.","Liver cysts are the most common extrarenal finding in ADPKD.","Newborn with huge kidneys, Potter facies, and later portal hypertension: ARPKD with congenital hepatic fibrosis.","Infant with one cystic nonfunctioning kidney and a normal other kidney: multicystic dysplastic kidney.","Bilateral renal agenesis: oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, death shortly after birth.","Child with recurrent febrile UTIs: test for vesicoureteral reflux with a voiding cystourethrogram.","Long-term dialysis patient with new cysts and a solid mass: renal cell carcinoma in acquired cystic disease."],"pitfalls":["ADPKD and ARPKD both give big cystic kidneys; age, liver fibrosis versus liver cysts, and inheritance separate them.","Multicystic dysplastic kidney is a developmental error, not an inherited disease, and is usually one-sided.","Acquired cystic disease kidneys are usually not enlarged the way ADPKD kidneys are.","Alport syndrome and reactive arthritis both have 'can't see, can't pee' mnemonics; Alport adds deafness, reactive arthritis adds joints."],"dumps":[{"t":"Cystic kidney comparison","p":"Make a table comparing ADPKD, ARPKD, multicystic dysplastic kidney, and acquired cystic disease. Include inheritance, age, one or both kidneys, and findings outside the kidney.","l":["ADPKD: PKD1 or PKD2, adults, both kidneys","ADPKD extras: liver cysts, berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse, hypertension","ARPKD: PKHD1, infants, both kidneys","ARPKD extras: oligohydramnios, Potter sequence, congenital hepatic fibrosis","Multicystic dysplastic: not inherited, usually one kidney","Dysplasia shows immature tubules and sometimes cartilage","Acquired cystic disease: long-term dialysis","Acquired cystic disease raises renal cell carcinoma risk"]},{"t":"Bilateral renal agenesis to Potter sequence","p":"Draw a flow chart from failed ureteric bud development to the newborn findings of Potter sequence. Label each step.","l":["Ureteric bud fails to form or to induce metanephric mesenchyme","No kidneys means no fetal urine","Fetal urine is the main source of amniotic fluid later in pregnancy","Oligohydramnios","Fetal compression: flat face, low-set ears, small chin, limb deformities","Pulmonary hypoplasia","Pulmonary hypoplasia is the usual cause of death","Unilateral agenesis instead gives compensatory hypertrophy"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, sections on cystic diseases of the kidney and on Alport syndrome"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System, section on the urinary system"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on collagen"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-1","UR-9","UR-12-D2","UR-12-H1","UR-12-A","CV-18-I1"]},"UR-12-F":{"id":"UR-12-F","summary":"This unit covers diseases of the renal blood vessels: hemolytic uremic syndrome, hypertensive nephrosclerosis, renal artery stenosis, renal infarction, sickle cell nephropathy, and renal vein thrombosis. Exam items describe a vessel lesion or a blood smear and ask you to predict what happens to the kidney, the blood pressure, or the urine.","notes":[{"h":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) and TTP","points":["Typical HUS follows bloody diarrhea from Shiga toxin-producing E. coli O157:H7, mostly in young children, about a week after the diarrhea starts.","Shiga toxin binds Gb3 receptors on glomerular endothelium and stops protein synthesis, injuring the endothelial cells.","Injured endothelium triggers platelet-fibrin microthrombi in glomerular capillaries and arterioles (thrombotic microangiopathy).","Triad: microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with schistocytes, thrombocytopenia from platelet consumption, and acute kidney injury.","Hemolysis labs: high LDH, high indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin, negative direct Coombs test; PT and aPTT are normal, unlike DIC.","TTP: deficient ADAMTS13 (usually an autoantibody) leaves ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers that clump platelets; mostly adults, more neurologic signs, milder renal injury."]},{"h":"Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","points":["Benign nephrosclerosis: long-standing hypertension causes hyaline arteriolosclerosis, a pink glassy thickening from plasma proteins leaking into the arteriolar wall.","Narrowed arterioles cause patchy ischemic loss of nephrons, so both kidneys shrink evenly with a finely granular surface.","Benign nephrosclerosis usually causes mild proteinuria and slowly progressive CKD; diabetes and aging also cause hyaline arteriolosclerosis.","Malignant hypertension (very high pressure, often above 180/120 mm Hg, with organ damage) causes hyperplastic onion-skin arteriolosclerosis.","Malignant hypertension also causes fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles and pinpoint cortical hemorrhages, the flea-bitten kidney.","Malignant hypertension presents with acute kidney injury, hematuria, proteinuria, headache, and papilledema; ischemia drives more renin, which raises pressure further."]},{"h":"Renal artery stenosis","points":["Atherosclerotic stenosis is most common: older adults with vascular risk factors, lesion at the ostium or proximal artery.","Fibromuscular dysplasia: young women, a noninflammatory thickening of the arterial wall in the mid to distal artery, string of beads on angiography.","Low perfusion pressure at the juxtaglomerular cells raises renin, angiotensin II, and aldosterone, so hypertension follows (sometimes with hypokalemia).","Clues: an abdominal or flank bruit, resistant hypertension, a rise in creatinine after starting an ACE inhibitor.","The poorly perfused kidney shrinks from ischemic atrophy, while the other kidney faces high pressure and develops nephrosclerosis."]},{"h":"Renal infarction","points":["Branches of the renal artery are end arteries without useful collaterals, so a blocked branch causes infarction.","Most come from emboli: left atrial thrombus in atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus after myocardial infarction, endocarditis vegetations; less often local thrombosis.","Pale, wedge-shaped coagulative infarct with its base at the capsule and its tip toward the hilum; it heals as a V-shaped depressed scar.","Clinical: sudden flank pain, hematuria, and a raised serum LDH."]},{"h":"Sickle cell nephropathy","points":["The renal medulla is low in oxygen, very hypertonic, and acidic; all three push hemoglobin S to polymerize.","Hypertonicity pulls water out of red cells and raises the HbS concentration; acidity lowers oxygen affinity (Bohr effect), releasing more oxygen.","Sickled cells block the vasa recta, causing medullary microinfarcts and papillary necrosis.","Loss of the medullary concentration gradient leaves urine fixed near plasma osmolality (isosthenuria), causing polyuria, nocturia, and dehydration risk.","Hematuria is common, in sickle cell disease and in sickle cell trait.","Sickle cell trait is also linked to renal medullary carcinoma, a rare aggressive tumor of young people."]},{"h":"Renal vein thrombosis","points":["Causes: nephrotic syndrome (especially membranous nephropathy), renal cell carcinoma growing into the renal vein, and dehydration in infants.","Nephrotic syndrome is hypercoagulable because antithrombin is lost in the urine and the liver makes more clotting factors.","Findings: flank pain, hematuria, an enlarged swollen kidney, worsening proteinuria or renal function.","Left renal vein thrombosis can cause a left varicocele because the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Renal vascular diseases","cols":["Condition","Cause or setting","Vessel or kidney lesion","Classic findings"],"rows":[["Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Shiga toxin from E. coli O157:H7 after bloody diarrhea in a child","Glomerular endothelial injury with platelet-fibrin microthrombi","Schistocytes, thrombocytopenia, acute kidney injury"],["Benign nephrosclerosis","Long-standing hypertension","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis; evenly shrunken, finely granular kidneys","Mild proteinuria, slow CKD"],["Malignant hypertension","Very high blood pressure with organ damage","Onion-skin arteriolosclerosis, fibrinoid necrosis, flea-bitten kidney","Acute kidney injury, hematuria, papilledema"],["Atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis","Older adult with vascular risk factors","Ostial or proximal plaque; affected kidney atrophies","Renin-driven hypertension, bruit"],["Fibromuscular dysplasia","Young woman","Mid to distal artery, string of beads","Renin-driven hypertension, bruit"],["Renal infarction","Emboli from atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus, endocarditis","Pale wedge-shaped coagulative infarct","Sudden flank pain, hematuria"],["Sickle cell nephropathy","Sickle cell disease or trait","Sickling in vasa recta, medullary infarcts, papillary necrosis","Isosthenuria, hematuria"],["Renal vein thrombosis","Membranous nephropathy, renal cell carcinoma, infant dehydration","Thrombus in renal vein; swollen congested kidney","Flank pain, hematuria, enlarged kidney"]]},{"title":"HUS versus TTP","cols":["Feature","Typical HUS","TTP"],"rows":[["Usual patient","Young child after bloody diarrhea","Adult, more often a woman"],["Cause","Shiga toxin injures endothelium","Low ADAMTS13 leaves ultra-large vWF multimers"],["Shared findings","Schistocytes, thrombocytopenia","Schistocytes, thrombocytopenia"],["Main organ","Kidney (acute kidney injury)","Brain (confusion, seizures, focal deficits)"],["Coagulation tests","PT and aPTT normal","PT and aPTT normal"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Medulla makes cells sickle","text":"Low oxygen, high salt, and acid are all found in the renal medulla, and each one favors sickling. That is why even sickle trait can cause hematuria and isosthenuria."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Hyaline is slow, onion skin is fast","text":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis goes with years of mild hypertension and slow kidney decline. Onion-skin hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis goes with malignant hypertension and acute renal failure."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Who gets which stenosis","text":"Older smoker with a bruit points to atherosclerosis at the ostium. Young woman with hard-to-control blood pressure points to fibromuscular dysplasia with a string of beads."}],"highYield":["Child with bloody diarrhea, then pallor, petechiae, and oliguria: HUS from E. coli O157:H7 Shiga toxin.","Schistocytes with thrombocytopenia and normal PT and aPTT: thrombotic microangiopathy (HUS or TTP), not DIC.","Low ADAMTS13 activity: TTP.","Evenly shrunken kidneys with a fine granular surface and hyaline arterioles: benign nephrosclerosis.","Flea-bitten kidney with onion-skin arterioles and fibrinoid necrosis: malignant hypertension.","Young woman with resistant hypertension and an abdominal bruit: fibromuscular dysplasia.","Atrial fibrillation with sudden flank pain and hematuria: renal infarction from an embolus.","Sickle cell trait in a young adult with painless hematuria or inability to concentrate urine: medullary sickling."],"pitfalls":["HUS and TTP share schistocytes and low platelets; the toxin story and kidney focus point to HUS, low ADAMTS13 and brain signs point to TTP.","DIC also has schistocytes, but PT and aPTT are prolonged and fibrinogen falls.","In renal artery stenosis, the stenotic kidney becomes small and atrophic; the other kidney shows the hypertensive damage.","Renal infarcts are pale because the kidney is a solid organ with end arteries; red infarcts are seen in organs with dual blood supply or venous occlusion.","Isosthenuria means urine fixed near plasma osmolality; it is a loss of concentrating ability, not a loss of filtration."],"dumps":[{"t":"HUS pathway","p":"Draw a flow chart from eating undercooked ground beef to the lab results of hemolytic uremic syndrome. Add a side column showing how TTP differs.","l":["E. coli O157:H7 causes bloody diarrhea","Shiga toxin enters the blood and binds Gb3 on glomerular endothelium","Toxin stops protein synthesis and injures endothelial cells","Platelet-fibrin microthrombi form in small renal vessels","Red cells are sheared into schistocytes: high LDH, low haptoglobin","Platelets are consumed: thrombocytopenia","Glomerular thrombi cause acute kidney injury","TTP: low ADAMTS13, ultra-large vWF, more neurologic signs, mostly adults"]},{"t":"Two kinds of hypertensive kidney","p":"Draw two small kidneys side by side, one with benign nephrosclerosis and one with malignant hypertension. Under each, draw an arteriole cross section and label it.","l":["Benign: both kidneys evenly shrunken, finely granular surface","Benign arteriole: hyaline arteriolosclerosis, pink glassy wall","Benign course: mild proteinuria, slow CKD","Malignant: flea-bitten surface from pinpoint hemorrhages","Malignant arteriole: concentric onion-skin layers (hyperplastic)","Malignant arteriole: fibrinoid necrosis","Malignant course: acute kidney injury, hematuria, papilledema","Ischemia raises renin and makes pressure climb further"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, section on diseases involving blood vessels"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, section on arteriolosclerosis"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on the renin-angiotensin system and hypertension"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on Escherichia coli (enteric gram-negative rods)"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-5","UR-7","UR-12-A4","UR-12-D","HE-9-B1","UR-12-G1"]},"UR-12-G":{"id":"UR-12-G","summary":"This unit covers tumors of the kidney and urinary tract: renal cell carcinoma and its subtypes, oncocytoma, Wilms tumor in children, and the carcinomas of the bladder and collecting system. Exam items lean on the classic links: the gene or syndrome behind the tumor, its look under the microscope, its paraneoplastic hormones, and its risk factors.","notes":[{"h":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","points":["Most common renal cell carcinoma; arises from proximal tubule cells; peaks in the 60s and 70s, more often in men.","Loss of the VHL gene on chromosome 3p (sporadic and in von Hippel-Lindau disease) stabilizes HIF, which drives VEGF and other growth signals.","Gross: golden-yellow cut surface from lipid, with hemorrhage, necrosis, and cystic areas.","Micro: cells with clear cytoplasm filled with lipid and glycogen, in nests separated by a rich network of thin-walled vessels.","Risk factors: smoking (the strongest), obesity, hypertension, acquired cystic disease of long-term dialysis, von Hippel-Lindau disease.","Tends to grow into the renal vein and up the inferior vena cava, sometimes to the right heart."]},{"h":"How renal cell carcinoma presents","points":["Hematuria is the most common sign; the full triad of hematuria, flank pain, and flank mass is uncommon.","Often found by chance on imaging done for something else.","Paraneoplastic hormones: erythropoietin (polycythemia), PTHrP (hypercalcemia), renin (hypertension), sometimes ACTH (Cushing syndrome).","Spreads through the blood to lung (round cannonball nodules) and bone (lytic lesions), often before local symptoms appear.","A new left varicocele that does not empty when the man lies down suggests tumor blocking the left renal vein, where the left gonadal vein drains."]},{"h":"Other renal cell tumors","points":["Papillary carcinoma: second most common, often multifocal; trisomy 7 and 17; papillae with fibrovascular cores holding foamy macrophages.","Chromophobe carcinoma: from intercalated cells of the collecting duct; pale cells with distinct cell borders and a halo around the nucleus; better prognosis.","Oncocytoma: benign tumor from intercalated cells; large pink cells packed with mitochondria; brown tumor with a central stellate scar."]},{"h":"Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma)","points":["Most common primary kidney cancer of children, usually ages 2 to 5 years.","Large, smooth, unilateral flank mass, sometimes with hematuria, abdominal pain, or hypertension.","Triphasic histology: blastema (small round blue cells), stroma, and epithelium forming primitive tubules and glomeruli.","Tumor suppressor WT1 on chromosome 11p13; WT2 region at 11p15.5 is linked to Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome.","WAGR: deletion at 11p13 causing Wilms tumor, aniridia, genitourinary anomalies, and developmental delay.","Denys-Drash: WT1 mutation with early nephrotic syndrome (diffuse mesangial sclerosis), gonadal dysgenesis, and Wilms tumor.","Beckwith-Wiedemann: macroglossia, organomegaly, hemihyperplasia, omphalocele, neonatal hypoglycemia, and risk of Wilms tumor and hepatoblastoma."]},{"h":"Bladder and collecting system tumors","points":["Urothelial carcinoma is the most common bladder cancer; it also arises in the renal pelvis and ureter.","Classic sign: painless gross hematuria in an older adult.","Two paths: papillary tumors (most are low grade, recur often, and rarely invade) and flat carcinoma in situ (high grade, likely to invade).","The whole urothelium is exposed to the same urine carcinogens, so tumors are often multifocal and recur in new places.","Risk factors: smoking (most important), aromatic amines from dye, rubber, and leather industries, cyclophosphamide, phenacetin.","Squamous cell carcinoma: chronic irritation from Schistosoma haematobium eggs, long-term catheters, or stones causes squamous metaplasia that turns malignant.","Adenocarcinoma: arises from urachal remnants at the bladder dome or in bladder exstrophy."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Renal epithelial tumors","cols":["Tumor","Cell of origin","Genetics","Key pathology","Behavior"],"rows":[["Clear cell RCC","Proximal tubule","VHL loss on chromosome 3p","Golden-yellow mass; clear cells with lipid and glycogen; thin-walled vessels","Malignant; renal vein and IVC invasion, lung and bone spread"],["Papillary RCC","Proximal tubule","Trisomy 7 and 17","Papillae with foamy macrophages in the cores","Malignant; often multifocal"],["Chromophobe RCC","Intercalated cell","Loss of whole chromosomes","Pale cells, distinct borders, perinuclear halo","Malignant with better prognosis"],["Oncocytoma","Intercalated cell","No VHL loss","Pink cells packed with mitochondria; central stellate scar","Benign"]]},{"title":"Wilms tumor syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Genetic change","Main features besides Wilms tumor"],"rows":[["WAGR","Deletion at 11p13 (WT1 and PAX6)","Aniridia, genitourinary anomalies, developmental delay"],["Denys-Drash","WT1 mutation","Early nephrotic syndrome (diffuse mesangial sclerosis), gonadal dysgenesis"],["Beckwith-Wiedemann","Imprinting changes at 11p15.5 (WT2 region)","Macroglossia, organomegaly, hemihyperplasia, omphalocele, neonatal hypoglycemia"]]},{"title":"Bladder cancers and their causes","cols":["Type","Main risk factors","Notes"],"rows":[["Urothelial carcinoma","Smoking, aromatic amines (dyes), cyclophosphamide, phenacetin","Painless hematuria; multifocal; papillary or flat CIS"],["Squamous cell carcinoma","Schistosoma haematobium, long-term catheters, stones","Chronic irritation leads to squamous metaplasia"],["Adenocarcinoma","Urachal remnant, bladder exstrophy","Urachal tumors sit at the bladder dome"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"WAGR","text":"Wilms tumor, Aniridia, Genitourinary anomalies, Range of developmental delays, from a deletion at 11p13."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Problems in your Pee SAC","text":"Urothelial carcinoma risk factors: Phenacetin, Smoking, Aromatic amines (aniline dyes), Cyclophosphamide."},{"kind":"trick","label":"RCC makes hormones","text":"Think of the kidney's own products plus a few extras: erythropoietin gives polycythemia, renin gives hypertension, and PTHrP gives hypercalcemia."}],"highYield":["Older smoker with hematuria, a flank mass, and polycythemia: renal cell carcinoma making erythropoietin.","Golden-yellow kidney tumor with clear cells and VHL loss on 3p: clear cell renal cell carcinoma.","Bilateral renal cell carcinomas with cerebellar hemangioblastoma: von Hippel-Lindau disease.","Renal cell carcinoma likes to invade the renal vein and IVC and spread to lung and bone.","Brown kidney mass with a central stellate scar and mitochondria-rich cells: oncocytoma, which is benign.","A 3-year-old with a large unilateral flank mass and triphasic histology: Wilms tumor.","Child with aniridia should be screened for Wilms tumor (WAGR).","Painless gross hematuria in an older smoker: urothelial carcinoma until proven otherwise."],"pitfalls":["Clear cell and papillary RCC come from the proximal tubule; chromophobe RCC and oncocytoma come from intercalated cells.","Wilms tumor and neuroblastoma both give abdominal masses in young children; Wilms arises in the kidney, while neuroblastoma arises from adrenal or sympathetic tissue, can cross the midline, and raises urine catecholamine metabolites.","Flat carcinoma in situ looks less dramatic than a papillary tumor but is high grade and more likely to invade.","Schistosoma haematobium is linked to squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder, not urothelial carcinoma."],"dumps":[{"t":"Renal cell carcinoma","p":"Draw a kidney with a renal cell carcinoma at one pole. Show where it spreads, then list the hormones it can make and what each one causes.","l":["Clear cell type arises from proximal tubule cells","Golden-yellow cut surface with hemorrhage and necrosis","VHL loss on chromosome 3p","Grows into renal vein and inferior vena cava","Left renal vein blockage can cause a left varicocele","Spreads to lung and bone","Erythropoietin causes polycythemia","PTHrP causes hypercalcemia; renin causes hypertension","Risk: smoking, obesity, hypertension, dialysis cysts, VHL"]},{"t":"Bladder tumors","p":"Make a table of urothelial carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and adenocarcinoma of the bladder. For each, list risk factors, how it starts, and how it presents.","l":["Urothelial carcinoma is the most common type","Urothelial risk: smoking, aromatic amine dyes, cyclophosphamide, phenacetin","Papillary low-grade tumors recur often; flat CIS is high grade","Multifocal because all the urothelium sees the same carcinogens","Classic sign: painless gross hematuria","Squamous: Schistosoma haematobium, catheters, stones, squamous metaplasia","Adenocarcinoma: urachal remnant at the dome, bladder exstrophy"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, section on tumors of the kidney"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, section on bladder neoplasms"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases, section on Wilms tumor"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-9","UR-12-E2","UR-12-D1","UR-12-F","NE-20-F3"]},"UR-12-H":{"id":"UR-12-H","summary":"This unit covers urinary tract infections from the urethra to the kidney: urethritis, cystitis, and acute pyelonephritis. Exam items ask you to tell the levels apart by symptoms and urine findings, name the likely organism, explain who is at risk, and predict complications.","notes":[{"h":"How UTIs start","points":["Most UTIs are ascending: gut bacteria colonize the periurethral area, climb the urethra, and reach the bladder.","E. coli causes most UTIs; P fimbriae help uropathogenic strains bind urothelium and reach the kidney.","Other organisms: Staphylococcus saprophyticus (young sexually active women), Proteus and Klebsiella, Enterococcus, and hospital gram-negative rods.","Proteus makes urease, which splits urea to ammonia, makes urine alkaline, and favors struvite stones.","Hematogenous spread to the kidney is less common and is seen with bacteremia such as Staphylococcus aureus."]},{"h":"Cystitis","points":["Risk factors: female sex (short urethra close to the anus), sexual intercourse, pregnancy, urinary catheters, diabetes, and obstruction.","Symptoms: dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain, sometimes hematuria.","No fever, no flank pain, and no WBC casts, because the kidney is not involved.","Urine: pyuria, positive leukocyte esterase, and a positive nitrite test with most gram-negative rods.","Enterococcus and S. saprophyticus usually do not convert nitrate to nitrite, so the nitrite test can be negative."]},{"h":"Acute pyelonephritis","points":["Usually ascending infection; risk rises with vesicoureteral reflux, obstruction, pregnancy, diabetes, and catheters.","Fever, chills, flank pain, costovertebral angle tenderness, nausea, often with cystitis symptoms first.","WBC casts form in the tubules and prove the infection is in the kidney.","Histology: patchy neutrophilic inflammation of the interstitium with neutrophils filling tubules; glomeruli are spared early.","Complications: papillary necrosis (diabetes, obstruction), pyonephrosis (pus filling a blocked collecting system), perinephric abscess, and sepsis.","Repeated episodes with reflux or obstruction lead to chronic pyelonephritis."]},{"h":"Urethritis","points":["Gonococcal urethritis: Neisseria gonorrhoeae, a gram-negative diplococcus seen inside neutrophils on Gram stain.","Gonococcal discharge is heavy and purulent and starts a few days after exposure.","Nongonococcal urethritis: Chlamydia trachomatis (serovars D to K) most often; also Mycoplasma genitalium and Trichomonas.","Chlamydia is an obligate intracellular organism not seen on Gram stain; discharge is scant and watery, and many infections cause no symptoms.","Nucleic acid amplification testing detects both; coinfection is common.","Gonococcal pili change their antigens, so infection does not leave lasting immunity and reinfection is common."]},{"h":"Complications of urethritis","points":["Men: epididymitis (the usual cause in younger sexually active men) and prostatitis.","Women: cervicitis and pelvic inflammatory disease, leading to infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome).","Chlamydia can trigger reactive arthritis: arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis, often in HLA-B27 positive people.","Disseminated gonococcal infection causes septic arthritis, tenosynovitis, and pustular skin lesions."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Telling the level of a urinary infection","cols":["Feature","Urethritis","Cystitis","Acute pyelonephritis"],"rows":[["Main symptoms","Dysuria with urethral discharge","Dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain","Fever, chills, flank pain, CVA tenderness"],["Fever","Usually absent","Absent","Present"],["Usual organisms","N. gonorrhoeae, C. trachomatis","E. coli, S. saprophyticus, Proteus, Klebsiella","E. coli and other enteric gram-negative rods"],["Urine findings","Pyuria with negative routine culture","Pyuria, bacteriuria, often nitrite positive","Pyuria, bacteriuria, WBC casts"],["Main complications","Epididymitis, PID, reactive arthritis","Spread to the kidney","Papillary necrosis, abscess, pyonephrosis, sepsis"]]},{"title":"Gonococcal versus nongonococcal urethritis","cols":["Feature","Gonococcal","Nongonococcal (mostly Chlamydia)"],"rows":[["Organism","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Chlamydia trachomatis serovars D to K"],["Gram stain","Gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils","Neutrophils but no organisms seen"],["Discharge","Heavy, purulent","Scant, watery or mucoid, or none"],["Onset after exposure","A few days","Usually 1 to 3 weeks"],["Special complication","Disseminated gonococcal infection","Reactive arthritis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Reactive arthritis: can't see, can't pee, can't climb a tree","text":"Conjunctivitis, urethritis, and arthritis after Chlamydia (or a GI infection), often in HLA-B27 positive people."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Fever and casts climb to the kidney","text":"Bladder infection gives burning and urgency only. Add fever, flank pain, and WBC casts and the infection has reached the kidney."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Sterile pyuria","text":"WBCs in the urine with a negative routine culture: think Chlamydia or gonococcal urethritis, interstitial nephritis, or renal tuberculosis."}],"highYield":["E. coli is the most common cause of cystitis and pyelonephritis; P fimbriae aid ascent to the kidney.","Young sexually active woman with cystitis and a nitrite-negative urine: think Staphylococcus saprophyticus.","Alkaline urine and struvite stones: urease-positive Proteus.","Fever, flank pain, CVA tenderness, and WBC casts: acute pyelonephritis.","Diabetic with pyelonephritis who passes tissue in the urine: papillary necrosis.","Gram-negative intracellular diplococci in urethral pus: gonococcal urethritis.","Scant discharge with neutrophils but no organisms on Gram stain: Chlamydia trachomatis.","Urethritis followed by arthritis and conjunctivitis: reactive arthritis."],"pitfalls":["Cystitis and pyelonephritis can both cause dysuria; fever, flank pain, and WBC casts mean the kidney is involved.","A negative nitrite test does not rule out UTI; Enterococcus and S. saprophyticus often do not make nitrite.","Chlamydia does not appear on Gram stain because it lives inside cells and stains poorly; a Gram stain showing only neutrophils fits Chlamydia.","Pyonephrosis is pus in an obstructed collecting system; a perinephric abscess is pus that has broken through the capsule into the fat around the kidney."],"dumps":[{"t":"Three levels of urinary infection","p":"Draw the urinary tract from urethra to kidney. Mark urethritis, cystitis, and pyelonephritis, and beside each write its symptoms, organisms, and urine findings.","l":["Urethritis: dysuria with discharge; gonorrhea or chlamydia","Cystitis: dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain, no fever","Cystitis organisms: E. coli, S. saprophyticus, Proteus, Klebsiella","Pyelonephritis: fever, flank pain, CVA tenderness","WBC casts only with kidney involvement","Most infections ascend from the urethra","Risk factors: female sex, intercourse, pregnancy, catheters, diabetes, obstruction, reflux"]},{"t":"Gonorrhea versus chlamydia","p":"Make a two-column table comparing gonococcal and chlamydial urethritis. Include the organism, Gram stain, discharge, timing, and complications in men and women.","l":["Gonorrhea: gram-negative intracellular diplococci","Chlamydia: obligate intracellular, not seen on Gram stain","Gonorrhea: heavy purulent discharge after a few days","Chlamydia: scant or no discharge, often silent","Both diagnosed by nucleic acid amplification testing","Men: epididymitis and prostatitis","Women: PID, infertility, ectopic pregnancy, Fitz-Hugh-Curtis","Chlamydia: reactive arthritis; gonorrhea: disseminated infection with septic arthritis"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, section on pyelonephritis and urinary tract infection"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on the Neisseriae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on chlamydiae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology (a Lange text)","where":"Section on enteric gram-negative rods"}],"videos":[],"related":["UR-11","UR-12-D2","UR-12-E4","UR-12-C2","RE-13-K3","RE-13-K4","MS-13-B7"]}};
window.NPLEX_CASES = [{"id":"CV-C01","system":"CV","dx":"Heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension","codes":["CV-18-A1"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 14-month history of progressive shortness of breath on exertion and one episode of near-syncope while climbing stairs. Her father died of an undiagnosed cardiopulmonary illness at the age of 45 years. Oxygen saturation is 94% on room air. Physical examination shows a loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound, a lift at the left lower sternal border, and mild ankle edema. Echocardiography shows a dilated, hypertrophied right ventricle and a normal left ventricle. Right heart catheterization shows a mean pulmonary artery pressure of 46 mm Hg (normal about 14; pulmonary hypertension is above 20), a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure of 9 mm Hg (normal 6 to 12), and increased pulmonary vascular resistance. CT angiography and a ventilation-perfusion scan show no emboli or lung disease. Diagnosis: heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C01-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing shows a heterozygous loss-of-function mutation in BMPR2, the gene most often mutated in heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension. Which of the following best describes the normal function of the protein encoded by this gene?","options":["A serine/threonine kinase receptor that restrains vascular smooth muscle growth","A G protein-coupled receptor for endothelin-1 that drives vasoconstriction","An extracellular microfibril protein that holds TGF-beta inactive in the vessel wall","A receptor tyrosine kinase for PDGF that promotes smooth muscle proliferation"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-A1","obj":"CV-18-A.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize BMPR2 as bone morphogenetic protein receptor type 2, a member of the TGF-beta receptor family.","Recall that TGF-beta family receptors are serine/threonine kinases that signal through SMAD proteins.","Link loss of this growth-restraining signal to cell proliferation in small pulmonary arteries."],"why":"BMPR2 encodes bone morphogenetic protein receptor type 2, a serine/threonine kinase receptor in the TGF-beta superfamily. Its SMAD signaling normally inhibits proliferation of pulmonary vascular smooth muscle and supports endothelial survival. With one working copy lost, endothelial and smooth muscle cells proliferate, thickening the media and intima of small pulmonary arteries and raising resistance. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with incomplete penetrance, so many carriers never develop disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Endothelin-1 constricts and remodels pulmonary vessels and is high in this disease, but its receptors are G protein-coupled receptors and are not encoded by BMPR2.","This describes fibrillin-1. FBN1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome, with aortic root dilation and dissection.","PDGF does drive smooth muscle growth in pulmonary hypertension, but it acts through a receptor tyrosine kinase. BMPR2 signaling normally opposes this growth."],"study":{"topics":["BMPR2 and heritable PAH","TGF-beta receptor signaling"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-A1"]}},{"id":"CV-C01-Q2","stem":"During a commercial airline flight, the reduced cabin pressure lowers her alveolar oxygen tension, and her shortness of breath worsens. Which of the following events in pulmonary arteriolar smooth muscle initiates the vascular response to alveolar hypoxia?","options":["Adenosine binding A2A receptors, raising cyclic AMP in the cell","Inhibition of oxygen-sensitive K+ channels, depolarizing the cell","More nitric oxide release, raising cGMP and lowering cytosolic calcium","Opening of ATP-sensitive K+ channels, hyperpolarizing the cell"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that alveolar hypoxia constricts pulmonary arterioles, the opposite of the dilation seen in most systemic arterioles.","Constriction needs a rise in cytosolic calcium, which follows membrane depolarization.","Pick the option that depolarizes the cell: closing potassium channels."],"why":"Pulmonary arterioles constrict when alveolar oxygen falls, which normally diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. Hypoxia inhibits oxygen-sensitive potassium channels in the smooth muscle, the membrane depolarizes, voltage-gated L-type calcium channels open, and calcium entry triggers contraction. At altitude the whole lung is hypoxic, so the constriction is widespread and adds to a pulmonary vascular resistance that is already high in her disease.","whyNot":["Adenosine is a key metabolic vasodilator in the coronary circulation during hypoxia. Rising cyclic AMP relaxes vascular smooth muscle.","Correct.","Nitric oxide and cGMP relax smooth muscle. Hypoxia does not raise this pathway, and reduced nitric oxide signaling is part of the problem in pulmonary arterial hypertension.","This is how hypoxia dilates many systemic arterioles, such as those in skeletal muscle and the heart. Hyperpolarization closes calcium channels and relaxes the cell."],"study":{"topics":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Pulmonary and systemic responses to hypoxia"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C01-Q3","stem":"The lift on her anterior chest wall is palpated best at the left lower sternal border. Which of the following anatomic features best explains why enlargement of the affected chamber is palpable at this site?","options":["The right ventricle forms most of the sternocostal surface","The left atrium forms most of the base on the posterior surface","The right atrium forms the right border of the heart shadow","The left ventricle forms the apex and most of the left border"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the chamber under strain: pulmonary hypertension loads the right ventricle.","Recall which chamber faces the anterior chest wall.","Match the right ventricle to the left parasternal location of the heave."],"why":"Pulmonary hypertension raises right ventricular afterload, so the right ventricle hypertrophies and dilates. The right ventricle lies just behind the sternum and left parasternal region and forms most of the anterior (sternocostal) surface of the heart. A forceful, enlarged right ventricle therefore lifts the chest wall at the left lower sternal border, called a parasternal heave.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left atrium forms the base and lies against the esophagus. When it enlarges, as in mitral stenosis, it can push the esophagus back, but it cannot be felt on the front of the chest.","The right atrium forms the right heart border on a frontal chest film. It does not lie under the left lower sternum.","Left ventricular enlargement moves the apex beat down and to the left, near or beyond the left midclavicular line. Her left ventricle is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Surfaces and borders of the heart","Parasternal heave"],"comps":["CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C01-Q4","stem":"Two years later, she dies of progressive right-sided heart failure. Which of the following findings in the lungs at autopsy is most characteristic of her disease?","options":["Hemosiderin-laden macrophages filling the alveolar spaces","Hyaline membranes lining the alveolar ducts","Noncaseating granulomas along bronchovascular bundles","Plexiform lesions in small muscular pulmonary arteries"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A1","obj":"CV-18-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Classify her disease as precapillary: high pulmonary artery pressure with a normal wedge pressure.","Recall the vascular changes of severe pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Rule out lesions that point to left heart failure, acute alveolar injury, or granulomatous lung disease."],"why":"Severe pulmonary arterial hypertension remodels small muscular pulmonary arteries and arterioles with medial hypertrophy and intimal fibrosis, and in advanced idiopathic and heritable disease it produces plexiform lesions. A plexiform lesion is a tuft of small, slit-like capillary channels inside a dilated arterial segment, formed by proliferating endothelial cells. It reflects the uncontrolled endothelial and smooth muscle growth that follows loss of BMPR2 signaling.","whyNot":["These heart failure cells form when high left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure leaks red cells into the alveoli, as in chronic left heart failure or mitral stenosis. Her normal wedge pressure rules out that cause.","Hyaline membranes mark diffuse alveolar damage, as in acute respiratory distress syndrome. They reflect acute injury to the alveolar wall.","Noncaseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis, which can cause pulmonary hypertension through lung fibrosis. Her imaging showed no lung disease, and granulomas are not a feature of heritable disease.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary vascular remodeling","Plexiform lesions"],"comps":["CV-18-A1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C02","system":"CV","dx":"Systemic hypertension with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy","codes":["CV-18-A2"],"case":"A 58-year-old man who works as a delivery truck driver comes to the physician for a required work physical examination. He was diagnosed with hypertension 12 years ago but stopped treatment many years ago. He reports mild shortness of breath when climbing two flights of stairs. Blood pressure is 174/104 mm Hg on repeated measurements. Physical examination shows a sustained, nondisplaced apical impulse and a fourth heart sound at the apex. An ECG shows increased QRS voltage with a left ventricular strain pattern. Echocardiography shows concentric hypertrophy with an interventricular septal thickness of 15 mm (normal 6 to 10), normal chamber size, an ejection fraction of 62% (normal 52 to 72%), and impaired relaxation. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.1 mg/dL (normal 0.7 to 1.3). Diagnosis: hypertensive heart disease with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C02-Q1","stem":"One year later, he develops atrial fibrillation. With medication, his ventricular rate is maintained at about 75/min, but his exercise tolerance decreases further and the fourth heart sound is no longer heard. Loss of which of the following phases of the cardiac cycle best explains this decline?","options":["Isovolumetric relaxation before the mitral valve opens","Reduced ejection late in ventricular systole","Atrial systole at the end of ventricular filling","Rapid ventricular filling just after mitral opening"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note what changed: atrial fibrillation with a controlled rate, so a short diastole is not the explanation.","Recall that the fourth heart sound comes from atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle.","Conclude that a stiff ventricle relies on atrial systole for filling, which atrial fibrillation removes."],"why":"A stiff, concentrically hypertrophied left ventricle fills poorly in early diastole, so it depends more on atrial contraction at the end of diastole. In a normal heart the atrial kick adds about 20 percent of filling, and in a stiff ventricle its share is larger. Atrial fibrillation removes organized atrial contraction, so end-diastolic volume and stroke volume fall. The fourth heart sound is made by atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle, which is why it disappears.","whyNot":["Isovolumetric relaxation still occurs with every beat, and no blood enters the ventricle during it. Slow relaxation in this phase is part of his baseline disease.","Ejection depends on ventricular contraction, which continues in atrial fibrillation. This phase is not lost.","Correct.","Rapid filling still happens in atrial fibrillation because it is driven by the pressure gradient when the mitral valve opens. It was already reduced by his impaired relaxation and does not explain the new decline."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of the cardiac cycle","Atrial kick and diastolic dysfunction"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-A2"]}},{"id":"CV-C02-Q2","stem":"Echocardiography shows slowed early diastolic relaxation. Myocardial relaxation requires ATP and is delayed when myocardial ATP is depleted. Which of the following steps in the cross-bridge cycle directly detaches the myosin head from actin?","options":["Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and phosphate on the myosin head","Binding of an ATP molecule to the myosin head","Release of ADP at the end of the power stroke","Release of phosphate, which triggers the power stroke"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Picture the head after the power stroke: attached to actin with no nucleotide (the rigor state).","Ask what must happen for it to let go: a new ATP must bind.","Keep the roles apart: binding detaches, hydrolysis recocks, phosphate release powers the stroke."],"why":"After the power stroke, a myosin head bound to actin with no nucleotide is in the rigor state. Binding of a new ATP molecule lowers myosin's affinity for actin, and the head detaches. Hydrolysis of that ATP then recocks the head for the next cycle. When ATP is scarce, as in energy-starved hypertrophied muscle, heads stay attached longer and relaxation slows. Rigor mortis is the extreme case, with no ATP left to release the heads.","whyNot":["Hydrolysis happens after detachment and recocks the head into its high-energy position. The head is already free of actin at this step.","Correct.","ADP release ends the power stroke and leaves the head tightly bound to actin in the rigor state. A new ATP must bind before the head can let go.","Phosphate release strengthens binding to actin and starts the power stroke. It moves the filaments and does not separate them."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle","Energy use in diastolic relaxation"],"comps":["CV-5"]}},{"id":"CV-C02-Q3","stem":"He later reports pressure-like chest discomfort that occurs with heavy exertion and resolves within minutes of rest. Coronary angiography shows no significant stenosis of the epicardial arteries. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of his exertional ischemia?","options":["Rupture of a small plaque with a nonocclusive thrombus","Spasm of a large epicardial coronary artery","Vasculitis narrowing small coronary arteries","Myocyte mass outgrowing its capillary supply"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the clues: exertional pain, thick ventricle, clean epicardial arteries.","Compare oxygen supply and demand in a hypertrophied wall.","Choose the mechanism tied to hypertrophy itself."],"why":"In concentric hypertrophy, myocytes enlarge but capillary density does not keep pace, so the distance oxygen must diffuse increases. Oxygen demand rises with the larger muscle mass and high systolic wall stress. A thick, stiff wall with high diastolic pressure also compresses subendocardial vessels, which fill mainly in diastole. During exertion, demand outruns supply and the subendocardium becomes ischemic even with normal epicardial arteries.","whyNot":["Plaque rupture with thrombus causes unstable angina or infarction, and angiography would show a culprit lesion. His arteries are clear.","Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina typically occurs at rest, often in the early morning, and is not reliably brought on by exertion.","Vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa or Kawasaki disease can damage coronary arteries, but he has no systemic features, and his symptoms track with the work of a thickened ventricle.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of left ventricular hypertrophy","Subendocardial ischemia"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-8"]}},{"id":"CV-C02-Q4","stem":"Eight years later, still untreated, he has the sudden onset of right-sided weakness and slurred speech. CT of the head shows a hemorrhage in the left putamen and internal capsule. Rupture of which of the following vessels most likely caused this hemorrhage?","options":["Bridging veins crossing the subdural space","A saccular aneurysm at the anterior communicating artery","Lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery","The middle meningeal artery beneath the pterion"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.2","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the bleed: putamen and internal capsule, deep in the brain.","Recall which arteries supply that region: lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery.","Link chronic hypertension to damage in small penetrating arteries."],"why":"Long-standing hypertension damages small penetrating arteries and can form tiny microaneurysms in their walls. The lenticulostriate arteries arise from the first part of the middle cerebral artery and enter the brain to supply the basal ganglia and internal capsule. Their rupture causes intraparenchymal hemorrhage, and the putamen is the most common site of hypertensive hemorrhage. Damage to the internal capsule produces weakness on the opposite side of the body.","whyNot":["Torn bridging veins cause a subdural hematoma, usually after head trauma in older adults or people with brain atrophy. Blood collects over the brain surface.","A ruptured berry aneurysm causes subarachnoid hemorrhage with a sudden, severe headache. Blood fills the subarachnoid space around the circle of Willis.","Correct.","A torn middle meningeal artery follows a skull fracture at the pterion and causes an epidural hematoma, often with a lucid interval."],"study":{"topics":["Blood supply of the basal ganglia and internal capsule","Hypertensive intracerebral hemorrhage"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-A2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C03","system":"CV","dx":"Renovascular hypertension from atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis","codes":["CV-18-A2"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman is referred to the physician because of a blood pressure of 182/98 mm Hg despite treatment with three antihypertensive medications. Her blood pressure was normal until the age of 62 years. She has peripheral artery disease and has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years. Physical examination shows an epigastric bruit. Funduscopic examination shows arteriolar narrowing without papilledema. Serum potassium concentration is 3.2 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0), serum creatinine concentration is 1.3 mg/dL (normal 0.6 to 1.1), and plasma renin activity is increased. Ultrasonography shows a right kidney 8.6 cm long and a left kidney 11.2 cm long (normal 10 to 12). CT angiography shows an 80% stenosis at the origin of the right renal artery caused by an atherosclerotic plaque. Diagnosis: renovascular hypertension due to right renal artery stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C03-Q1","stem":"Assume that flow through the stenosis is laminar and follows Poiseuille's law. If the luminal radius at the stenosis is half its normal value, with length, viscosity, and all other factors unchanged, resistance in that segment increases by approximately which of the following factors?","options":["4-fold","8-fold","16-fold","32-fold"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Write Poiseuille's law: resistance = 8 x viscosity x length / (pi x radius to the fourth power).","Halve the radius: radius to the fourth power falls to 1/16.","Resistance therefore rises 16-fold."],"why":"Poiseuille's law states that resistance is proportional to vessel length and blood viscosity and inversely proportional to the fourth power of the radius. Halving the radius raises resistance by 2 to the fourth power, which is 16. This is why a moderate narrowing can sharply cut flow and pressure beyond it, and why the kidney beyond a tight stenosis senses low perfusion pressure.","whyNot":["A 4-fold rise would follow if resistance depended on cross-sectional area (radius squared). Poiseuille's law uses the fourth power.","An 8-fold rise would follow from the third power of the radius, which does not appear in Poiseuille's law.","Correct.","A 32-fold rise would need a fifth-power relationship. Resistance varies with the fourth power of the radius."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille's law","Resistance and vessel radius"],"comps":["CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C03-Q2","stem":"Decreased perfusion pressure distal to the stenosis is sensed within the right kidney and stimulates renin release. Which of the following cells secretes the renin?","options":["Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole","Podocytes on the outer surface of glomerular capillaries","Macula densa cells in the wall of the distal tubule","Interstitial fibroblasts near the peritubular capillaries"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Connect the clue (low pressure in the renal arterial tree) to the renin-angiotensin system.","Recall the juxtaglomerular apparatus and which of its parts stores renin.","Place the juxtaglomerular cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole."],"why":"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole near the glomerular vascular pole. They store renin in granules and release it when stretch in the arteriole falls, when the macula densa senses low sodium chloride delivery, and with sympathetic beta-1 stimulation. In renal artery stenosis, low afferent arteriolar pressure in the affected kidney increases renin, which raises angiotensin II and aldosterone. Excess aldosterone explains her low potassium.","whyNot":["Correct.","Podocytes form the filtration slits of the glomerular barrier. They do not make renin.","Macula densa cells sense sodium chloride in the tubular fluid and signal the juxtaglomerular cells, but they do not secrete renin themselves.","Peritubular interstitial fibroblasts in the cortex make erythropoietin in response to low oxygen delivery."],"study":{"topics":["Juxtaglomerular apparatus","Renin release"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-A2"]}},{"id":"CV-C03-Q3","stem":"The plaque at the origin of the renal artery contains many lipid-laden macrophages (foam cells). Which of the following features of macrophage lipid handling best explains why these cells continue to take up cholesterol until they are engorged?","options":["Scavenger receptors for oxidized LDL escape feedback by cell cholesterol","LDL receptors increase as cell cholesterol rises, through SREBP activation","ABCA1 transporters move cholesterol from HDL into the macrophage","ACAT is inhibited, so free cholesterol cannot be stored as ester"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how native LDL uptake is limited by feedback on the LDL receptor.","Note that LDL trapped in the intima is oxidized.","Identify the uptake route that lacks feedback control: scavenger receptors."],"why":"Native LDL enters cells through the LDL receptor, and rising cell cholesterol shuts off SREBP and lowers LDL receptor numbers, which limits uptake. LDL trapped in the intima becomes oxidized. Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36, and these receptors are not reduced as cholesterol builds up. ACAT stores the excess as cholesteryl ester droplets, which give the cells their foamy look.","whyNot":["Correct.","SREBP is activated when cell cholesterol is low. High cell cholesterol suppresses LDL receptor synthesis, which limits native LDL uptake.","ABCA1 does the reverse: it exports cholesterol from the macrophage to apolipoprotein A-I to form HDL. Loss of ABCA1 (Tangier disease) causes cholesterol buildup in macrophages.","ACAT is active in foam cells. It esterifies cholesterol, and the stored cholesteryl ester droplets give the cells their foamy appearance."],"study":{"topics":["Foam cell formation","LDL receptor regulation"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C03-Q4","stem":"Her hypertension has been present for about 4 years. Histologic examination of the left (unaffected) kidney would most likely show which of the following findings?","options":["Ischemic atrophy with shrunken tubules and crowded glomeruli","Hyperplastic onion-skin thickening with fibrinoid necrosis","Concentric ring-like calcification of the arteriolar media","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis from chronic high pressure"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note that the stenosis shields the right kidney from high pressure while the left kidney receives it in full.","Recall the arteriolar change of chronic, non-malignant hypertension.","Rule out malignant hypertension: no papilledema and pressures below that range."],"why":"The stenosis protects the right kidney's small vessels from high pressure, while the left kidney receives the full systemic pressure. Over years, plasma proteins leak into the walls of its arterioles and smooth muscle cells lay down extra matrix, producing hyaline arteriolosclerosis. The arterioles narrow, and the kidney develops benign nephrosclerosis. The right kidney instead shrinks from ischemia, which matches its small size on ultrasound.","whyNot":["Ischemic atrophy is the expected change in the right kidney, downstream of the stenosis, and it explains that kidney's small size.","Onion-skin hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis marks malignant hypertension, usually with pressures above about 200/120 mm Hg and papilledema. She has neither.","Ring-like medial calcification describes Mönckeberg medial sclerosis, which affects medium-sized muscular arteries in older adults and does not narrow the lumen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hyaline and hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","Renal artery stenosis pathology"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-18-I3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C04","system":"CV","dx":"Acute left-sided heart failure with pulmonary edema due to viral myocarditis","codes":["CV-18-B1"],"case":"A 29-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 3-day history of progressive shortness of breath. He cannot lie flat and is coughing up pink, frothy sputum. Two weeks ago, he had fever, sore throat, and diarrhea. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 98/64 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 86% on room air. Physical examination shows crackles over both lung fields and a third heart sound. A chest x-ray shows bilateral perihilar alveolar opacities and Kerley B lines. Serum troponin I concentration is increased. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25% (normal 52 to 72%). Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure is 28 mm Hg (normal 6 to 12). Diagnosis: acute left-sided heart failure with pulmonary edema due to viral myocarditis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C04-Q1","stem":"Which of the following changes in the forces across the pulmonary capillary wall is the primary cause of the fluid in his alveoli?","options":["Fall in plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Rise in pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure","Rise in capillary permeability to plasma proteins","Blockage of lymph drainage from the lung interstitium"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Read the wedge pressure: 28 mm Hg means high left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure.","Apply the Starling forces: high capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out.","Separate cardiogenic edema (high wedge pressure) from permeability edema (normal wedge pressure)."],"why":"Pulmonary capillary pressure is normally about 7 mm Hg, well below the plasma colloid osmotic pressure of about 28 mm Hg, so the lungs stay dry. A failing left ventricle raises left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure, shown by his wedge pressure of 28 mm Hg. When capillary pressure reaches the colloid osmotic pressure and lymph flow can no longer keep up, fluid fills the interstitium (Kerley B lines) and then the alveoli.","whyNot":["Low oncotic pressure from low albumin, as in nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis, favors edema, but nothing suggests low albumin here, and his wedge pressure explains the edema.","Correct.","Increased permeability causes noncardiogenic edema, as in acute respiratory distress syndrome or sepsis. In that setting the wedge pressure is not elevated.","Lymphatic blockage, as from tumor spread, causes interstitial fluid buildup. His lymphatics are working and are simply overwhelmed."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces in the lung","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C04-Q2","stem":"PCR of an endomyocardial biopsy specimen detects coxsackievirus B3 RNA. Which of the following best describes this virus and its most likely route of transmission to this patient?","options":["Enveloped, negative-sense single-stranded RNA, spread by respiratory droplets","Nonenveloped, double-stranded DNA, spread by droplets and fomites","Enveloped, double-stranded DNA, spread by close contact with saliva","Nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA, spread fecal-orally"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.6","dok":2,"approach":["Place coxsackievirus in its family: an enterovirus within the picornaviruses.","Recall picornavirus structure: small, nonenveloped, positive-sense RNA.","Match the route to his diarrheal prodrome: fecal-oral."],"why":"Coxsackievirus B is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family. It is small and nonenveloped, with a positive-sense, single-stranded RNA genome that can be translated directly as mRNA. Without an envelope it survives stomach acid and spreads mainly by the fecal-oral route, which fits his diarrheal illness two weeks earlier. It enters cardiac myocytes through the coxsackievirus and adenovirus receptor (CAR).","whyNot":["This describes influenza, which spreads by droplets and can occasionally cause myocarditis. It is not an enterovirus.","This describes adenovirus, another cause of viral myocarditis, especially in children. It is a DNA virus.","This describes herpesviruses such as Epstein-Barr virus and cytomegalovirus, which spread through saliva and other close contact.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Picornaviruses and enteroviruses","Viral causes of myocarditis"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K6"]}},{"id":"CV-C04-Q3","stem":"His wedge pressure was measured with a balloon-tipped catheter advanced into a small branch of the pulmonary artery and inflated until flow stopped. Which of the following best explains why this measurement approximates his left ventricular filling pressure?","options":["The catheter tip senses pressure in the bronchial arteries","A still column of blood links the tip to the left atrium","The balloon transmits right ventricular pressure forward","The tip lies in a pulmonary vein beside the left atrium"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.4","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the path from a pulmonary artery branch through capillaries and pulmonary veins to the left atrium.","Apply the rule that with no flow there is no pressure drop along a vessel.","Link left atrial pressure to left ventricular pressure in diastole through the open mitral valve."],"why":"Inflating the balloon blocks flow in that branch. With no flow there is no pressure drop along the vessels downstream, so the blood in the capillaries and pulmonary veins forms a static column connected to the left atrium. The tip therefore reads left atrial pressure. In diastole the mitral valve is open, so left atrial pressure approximates left ventricular end-diastolic pressure, unless a problem such as mitral stenosis separates them.","whyNot":["Bronchial arteries come from the systemic circulation (the aorta or intercostal arteries) and supply the airways. The catheter lies in a pulmonary artery branch.","Correct.","The inflated balloon blocks the pressure coming from the right ventricle. Before inflation the catheter reads pulmonary artery pressure.","The catheter stays in the pulmonary artery tree and cannot pass through the capillaries. It reflects venous pressure only through the static column of blood."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure","Pulmonary circulation pathway"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C04-Q4","stem":"The biopsy specimen also shows lymphocytes infiltrating the myocardium adjacent to necrotic myocytes. The infected myocytes display viral peptides on their surface. Which of the following immune mechanisms most directly kills these infected myocytes?","options":["Immune complexes depositing in small vessel walls","CD4 T cells recognizing viral peptide on MHC class I","IgE on mast cells triggering histamine release","CD8 T cells binding viral peptide on MHC class I"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note that viral proteins made inside the myocyte are presented on MHC class I.","Recall which T cells recognize MHC class I: CD8 cytotoxic T cells.","Link the lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis to cell-mediated killing."],"why":"Cardiac myocytes, like nearly all nucleated cells, present peptides from viral proteins made in their cytoplasm on MHC class I. Cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognize these complexes and kill the cell with perforin and granzymes or Fas ligand. This clears the virus but also destroys myocytes, and a lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis is the hallmark of viral myocarditis. Loss of myocytes weakens contraction and leads to acute left heart failure.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition (type III hypersensitivity) causes vasculitis, as in serum sickness or lupus. It inflames vessel walls and does not target infected myocytes.","CD4 T cells recognize peptide on MHC class II, found mainly on antigen-presenting cells. They help the response but do not directly kill infected myocytes.","IgE on mast cells drives type I hypersensitivity, such as anaphylaxis and allergic asthma. It plays no direct part in killing virus-infected myocytes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["MHC class I and cytotoxic T cells","Viral myocarditis immunopathology"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-K6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C05","system":"CV","dx":"Right-sided heart failure (cor pulmonale) due to cystic fibrosis lung disease","codes":["CV-18-B2"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis (homozygous for F508del) comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of ankle swelling, abdominal fullness, and fatigue. She has advanced lung disease with chronic airway infection by a mucoid gram-negative rod, and her FEV1 is 28% of predicted. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention to 7 cm above the sternal angle (normal 3 cm or less), a loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound, a tender, enlarged liver, and pitting edema to the mid-shins. On room air, arterial PO2 is 54 mm Hg (normal 80 to 100). Hematocrit is 52% (normal 36 to 46), and serum ALT activity is 88 U/L (normal 7 to 35). Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle, a flattened septum, and normal left ventricular function. Diagnosis: right-sided heart failure (cor pulmonale) due to cystic fibrosis lung disease.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C05-Q1","stem":"Her F508del mutation deletes one phenylalanine residue from the CFTR protein. Which of the following best explains the loss of CFTR function in her airway epithelial cells?","options":["A premature stop codon that produces a short, truncated protein","A protein that reaches the cell membrane but rarely opens its channel","A misfolded protein held in the endoplasmic reticulum and degraded","A promoter mutation that sharply lowers transcription of the gene"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-10","obj":"PU-10.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the mutation type: an in-frame deletion of one amino acid.","Recall what happens to misfolded membrane proteins: endoplasmic reticulum quality control and proteasomal degradation.","Connect the missing chloride channel to thick mucus, chronic lung disease, and pulmonary hypertension."],"why":"F508del is an in-frame deletion of three nucleotides that removes phenylalanine at position 508. The protein misfolds, is caught by quality control in the endoplasmic reticulum, and is degraded by the proteasome, so little CFTR reaches the apical membrane (a class II processing defect). Without this chloride channel, airway surface liquid becomes dehydrated and mucus thickens, leading to chronic infection, bronchiectasis, hypoxemia, and eventually pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.","whyNot":["Nonsense mutations such as G542X create a premature stop codon (class I). F508del removes one amino acid and keeps the reading frame.","Gating mutations such as G551D (class III) produce a protein that reaches the membrane but opens poorly.","Correct.","Promoter or splicing mutations reduce the amount of normal protein made. F508del leaves transcription intact and changes how the protein folds."],"study":{"topics":["CFTR mutation classes","Protein folding and ER quality control"],"comps":["CV-14"]}},{"id":"CV-C05-Q2","stem":"Her mucoid gram-negative rod is oxidase positive, produces a blue-green pigment, and has persisted for years despite a dense neutrophilic infiltrate in her airways. Which of the following bacterial products best explains this persistence?","options":["Alginate, which forms a protective biofilm matrix","Lipid A, which binds Toll-like receptor 4","Flagellin, which drives motility through airway mucus","Exotoxin A, which ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the organism: an oxidase-positive, pigment-making, mucoid gram-negative rod in cystic fibrosis is Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","Ask which product shields bacteria from phagocytes over the long term.","Choose the exopolysaccharide that defines the mucoid form: alginate."],"why":"The organism is Pseudomonas aeruginosa. In the cystic fibrosis lung it switches to a mucoid form that overproduces alginate, an exopolysaccharide. Alginate builds a biofilm that blocks phagocytosis by neutrophils and macrophages, scavenges oxidants, and limits antibody and antibiotic penetration. Neutrophils keep arriving but cannot clear the bacteria, and their proteases and oxidants damage the airways, driving bronchiectasis, hypoxemia, and pulmonary hypertension.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lipid A is the toxic part of endotoxin. It activates TLR4 and drives inflammation and septic shock, which helps the host detect the bacteria.","Flagellin is detected by TLR5, and Pseudomonas from chronic cystic fibrosis infection often loses its flagella, which helps it avoid detection.","Exotoxin A blocks host protein synthesis and kills tissue cells, much like diphtheria toxin. It damages tissue but does not shield the bacteria from clearance."],"study":{"topics":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa virulence factors","Biofilms and immune evasion"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C05-Q3","stem":"Her jugular venous pressure is assessed using the right internal jugular vein with the head of the bed elevated to 45 degrees. Which of the following best explains why this vein is preferred for estimating right atrial pressure?","options":["It lies superficial to sternocleidomastoid, where it is easy to see","It drains straight into the right atrium through its own opening","It lines up almost straight with the SVC and right atrium","It reaches the heart by crossing the midline in front of the aorta"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the venous path from the right internal jugular vein to the right atrium.","Note that a short, straight column transmits right atrial pressure best.","Rule out options that describe the external jugular or the left-sided veins."],"why":"The right internal jugular vein joins the right subclavian vein to form the right brachiocephalic vein, which runs almost straight down into the superior vena cava (SVC) and right atrium. This short, direct path lets the height of the pulsating column reflect right atrial pressure. In right heart failure, rising right atrial pressure shows as distended neck veins. The external jugular vein is easier to see but takes a more angled course through the deep fascia, so it is less reliable.","whyNot":["This describes the external jugular vein. The internal jugular lies deep to sternocleidomastoid, so its pulsation is seen as movement in the neck rather than as a visible vein.","No neck vein opens directly into the right atrium. Blood reaches it through the brachiocephalic veins and the SVC.","Correct.","This describes the left brachiocephalic vein, which carries left internal jugular blood across the midline. That longer route makes the left side less reliable."],"study":{"topics":["Veins of the neck and thorax","Jugular venous pressure"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-B2"]}},{"id":"CV-C05-Q4","stem":"A liver biopsy specimen would most likely show which of the following patterns?","options":["Fatty change with large lipid droplets in hepatocytes","Congestion and hepatocyte loss around central veins","Bile duct destruction by granulomas in portal tracts","Portal tract lymphocytes with interface hepatitis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B2","obj":"CV-18-B.2","dok":2,"approach":["Trace back-pressure from the right atrium through the inferior vena cava and hepatic veins to the central veins.","Recall that the central (zone 3) hepatocytes are also the least oxygenated.","Choose congestion and injury around central veins."],"why":"Right heart failure raises right atrial pressure, which passes back through the inferior vena cava and hepatic veins into the central veins of the liver lobules. Blood pools around the central veins (zone 3), the zone farthest from the oxygen-rich portal inflow. Hepatocytes there atrophy and may die, while better-oxygenated periportal cells survive. The dark, congested centers against paler surrounding tissue give the cut liver a nutmeg look.","whyNot":["Fatty change (steatosis) comes from alcohol, obesity, insulin resistance, or other metabolic causes. It does not reflect venous congestion and would not explain a tender, swollen liver with distended neck veins.","Correct.","Granulomatous bile duct destruction is the classic lesion of primary biliary cholangitis.","Interface hepatitis with portal lymphocytes is the pattern of chronic viral or autoimmune hepatitis."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic passive congestion of the liver","Hepatic lobule zones"],"comps":["CV-18-B2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C06","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction from Chagas cardiomyopathy, with neurohormonal activation","codes":["CV-18-B1"],"case":"A 54-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of progressive fatigue, shortness of breath after walking one block, and leg swelling. He moved to the United States from rural Bolivia 25 years ago. Pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 102/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows bibasilar crackles, a third heart sound, and pitting edema of the legs. An ECG shows right bundle branch block and left anterior fascicular block. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle, an ejection fraction of 27% (normal 52 to 72%), and a small apical aneurysm. Serum BNP is 1,240 pg/mL (normal less than 100), sodium 130 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), and creatinine 1.4 mg/dL (normal 0.7 to 1.3). IgG antibodies to Trypanosoma cruzi are positive on two assays. Diagnosis: Chagas cardiomyopathy with chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C06-Q1","stem":"He most likely acquired the infection during childhood. Which of the following is the usual route of transmission of this organism to humans in endemic areas?","options":["Bite of an infected female sandfly","Feces of an infected triatomine bug","Bite of an infected Anopheles mosquito","Bite of an infected tsetse fly"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism from the serology: Trypanosoma cruzi, the cause of Chagas disease.","Recall its vector: the triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bug.","Note that the parasite passes in the bug's feces during or after the blood meal."],"why":"Trypanosoma cruzi is carried by triatomine (reduviid, or kissing) bugs that feed at night, often on the face. The bug defecates while feeding, and trypomastigotes in its feces enter through the bite wound or the conjunctiva when the person rubs the area. Inside host cells, including cardiac myocytes, the parasite multiplies as amastigotes. Decades later, about 20 to 30 percent of infected people develop chronic heart disease. Blood transfusion, organ transplant, mother-to-child spread, and contaminated food are less common routes.","whyNot":["Sandflies transmit Leishmania, which causes cutaneous or visceral leishmaniasis.","Correct.","Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium species, the cause of malaria.","Tsetse flies transmit Trypanosoma brucei, the cause of African sleeping sickness. That parasite passes in the fly's saliva during the bite."],"study":{"topics":["Trypanosoma cruzi life cycle","Vector-borne protozoa"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K2"]}},{"id":"CV-C06-Q2","stem":"Myocytes from failing hearts such as his show smaller calcium transients with each beat and slower removal of calcium from the cytosol. Which of the following changes most directly reduces the calcium available for each contraction?","options":["More phospholamban phosphorylation by protein kinase A","Less sodium-calcium exchange, trapping calcium in the cytosol","Lower SERCA2a activity refilling the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Tighter closure of ryanodine receptors during diastole"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that each beat releases calcium stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Ask which pump refills that store: SERCA2a.","Link less refilling to smaller release, weaker contraction, and slower relaxation."],"why":"With each beat, calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum through ryanodine receptors binds troponin C. Relaxation and the next beat depend on SERCA2a pumping that calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. In heart failure, SERCA2a expression and activity fall, and its inhibitor phospholamban is less phosphorylated, so it inhibits SERCA2a more. Less calcium is stored, less is released with the next beat, and contraction weakens. Slower reuptake also slows relaxation.","whyNot":["Protein kinase A phosphorylation of phospholamban relieves its inhibition of SERCA2a and raises stored calcium, as during sympathetic stimulation. In failing hearts phospholamban phosphorylation is reduced.","The sodium-calcium exchanger is increased in failing myocytes and removes more calcium from the cell. If exchange were reduced, more calcium would stay in the cell.","Correct.","In heart failure, ryanodine receptors tend to leak calcium during diastole, which further drains the stores. Tighter closure would preserve them."],"study":{"topics":["Excitation-contraction coupling","Calcium handling in heart failure"],"comps":["CV-5","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C06-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best describes the change in his left ventricular pressure-volume loop compared with that of a healthy adult?","options":["Flatter end-systolic line, loop at larger volumes","Narrower loop shifted to a smaller end-diastolic volume","Steeper end-diastolic curve, normal end-systolic volume","No isovolumetric relaxation, with a much wider loop"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the primary defect: reduced contractility (ejection fraction 27%).","Recall that contractility is shown by the slope of the end-systolic pressure-volume line.","Predict the result: larger end-systolic and end-diastolic volumes, with a small stroke volume."],"why":"Contractility is shown by the slope of the end-systolic pressure-volume relationship. In systolic heart failure that slope falls, so at any afterload the ventricle empties less and end-systolic volume rises. The ventricle dilates and end-diastolic volume rises as the body retains fluid to raise preload. The loop moves to the right and becomes narrow, because stroke volume is small compared with the large end-diastolic volume, giving his ejection fraction of 27%.","whyNot":["Correct.","A narrow loop at small volumes reflects low preload, as in hemorrhage or dehydration. His ventricle is dilated.","A steep end-diastolic curve with normal systolic function describes a stiff ventricle, as in heart failure with preserved ejection fraction from hypertensive hypertrophy.","Loss of a true isovolumetric relaxation phase occurs in aortic regurgitation, because blood leaks back into the ventricle as soon as ejection ends. That loop widens from a large stroke volume."],"study":{"topics":["Pressure-volume loops","Contractility and the ESPVR"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C06-Q4","stem":"His serum sodium concentration is 130 mEq/L although his total body sodium is increased. Which of the following hormones most directly causes the low serum sodium concentration?","options":["Norepinephrine acting on renal tubular alpha-1 receptors","Aldosterone acting on collecting duct principal cells","Cortisol acting on renal mineralocorticoid receptors","Vasopressin acting on collecting duct V2 receptors"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B1","obj":"CV-18-B.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize hyponatremia with volume overload as a sign of excess free water.","Ask which hormone adds free water: vasopressin (ADH).","Explain its release despite low osmolality: baroreceptors sense low effective arterial volume."],"why":"In heart failure, low cardiac output reduces effective arterial blood volume. Arterial baroreceptors sense this and trigger nonosmotic release of vasopressin (ADH), which overrides the normal suppression of ADH by low plasma osmolality. Vasopressin binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells and inserts aquaporin-2 channels, so free water is reabsorbed. Water retained in excess of sodium dilutes plasma sodium. Low serum sodium in heart failure signals strong neurohormonal activation and a worse prognosis.","whyNot":["Norepinephrine raises heart rate, contractility, renin release, and tubular sodium reabsorption, which adds to fluid retention. It does not directly add free water.","Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption, and water follows, which expands volume and causes edema. On its own it does not dilute plasma sodium, and it also increases potassium secretion.","At high levels cortisol can act on mineralocorticoid receptors and retain sodium, which tends to keep sodium up. Low sodium is a feature of cortisol deficiency, through increased vasopressin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neurohormonal activation in heart failure","Hyponatremia in heart failure"],"comps":["CV-18-B1","CV-11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C55","system":"CV","dx":"Acute pulmonary embolism","codes":["CV-18-I7"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of sudden shortness of breath and sharp right-sided chest pain that worsens with inspiration. He returned from a 14-hour flight 3 days ago, and his right calf has been sore and swollen since the trip. Pulse is 116/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 104/68 mmHg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 89%. Arterial blood gas analysis on room air at sea level shows pH 7.49 (7.35 to 7.45), PaCO2 30 mmHg (35 to 45), and PaO2 62 mmHg (80 to 100). D-dimer is 2,400 ng/mL FEU (below 500). CT pulmonary angiography shows filling defects in both lower lobe pulmonary arteries. Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle. The diagnosis is acute pulmonary embolism.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C55-Q1","stem":"Using the alveolar gas equation (PAO2 = 150 mmHg minus PaCO2/0.8), which of the following best describes this patient's alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient and the primary mechanism of his hypoxemia?","options":["About 10 mmHg, caused by alveolar hypoventilation","About 10 mmHg, caused by low inspired oxygen pressure","About 50 mmHg, caused by ventilation-perfusion mismatch","About 50 mmHg, caused by thickening of the alveolar membrane"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.5","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate PAO2: 150 minus (30 divided by 0.8) is about 112 mmHg.","Subtract PaO2: 112 minus 62 gives an A-a gradient of about 50 mmHg, well above the normal 5 to 15 mmHg.","A wide gradient with a low PaCO2 rules out hypoventilation and low inspired oxygen; in PE the cause is ventilation-perfusion mismatch."],"why":"The clots block flow to ventilated lung, so blood is diverted to the remaining vessels, which are now overperfused relative to their ventilation. Blood leaving those low V/Q units is poorly oxygenated, and the A-a gradient widens to about 50 mmHg. Hypoxemia and pain drive hyperventilation, which explains the low PaCO2 and respiratory alkalosis.","whyNot":["Hypoventilation raises PaCO2 and keeps the A-a gradient normal. His PaCO2 is low. This fits opioid overdose or neuromuscular weakness.","Low inspired oxygen (high altitude) lowers PaO2 with a normal gradient. He was tested at sea level, and his gradient is wide.","Correct.","Membrane thickening (pulmonary fibrosis) does widen the gradient, but nothing in this case thickens the alveolar wall. The problem here is blocked blood flow."],"study":{"topics":["Alveolar gas equation and A-a gradient","Causes of hypoxemia","V/Q mismatch in pulmonary embolism"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-I7"]}},{"id":"CV-C55-Q2","stem":"CT pulmonary angiography shows no infarction of the lung parenchyma distal to the occluded arteries. Which of the following vessels most likely maintains the viability of this lung tissue?","options":["Bronchial veins draining lung tissue into the azygos system","Pericardiophrenic arteries supplying the lower lobes","Internal thoracic arteries entering through the visceral pleura","Bronchial arteries arising from the systemic circulation"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-I7","obj":"CV-18-I.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that the lung has a dual blood supply.","Identify the second, systemic source: the bronchial arteries, mostly from the descending thoracic aorta.","Link this to the fact that most emboli in healthy people do not cause infarction."],"why":"The bronchial arteries arise from the systemic circulation, mainly the descending thoracic aorta, and supply the bronchi and supporting lung tissue. When a pulmonary artery branch is blocked, bronchial flow keeps the tissue alive. Infarction becomes likely when this backup is weak, as in left heart failure or existing lung disease.","whyNot":["Veins drain blood away from tissue and cannot deliver oxygen. Bronchial veins do drain into the azygos system, but that is irrelevant to survival.","The pericardiophrenic arteries run with the phrenic nerves to the pericardium and diaphragm. They do not supply lung parenchyma.","The internal thoracic arteries supply the anterior chest wall and breast. They do not enter the lung through the visceral pleura.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dual blood supply of the lung","Bronchial arteries","Pulmonary infarction"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"CV-C55-Q3","stem":"One hour later, his blood pressure decreases to 86/54 mmHg. Repeat echocardiography shows flattening of the interventricular septum with bowing toward the left ventricle during diastole. Which of the following mechanisms most likely explains the decrease in his cardiac output?","options":["Pericardial fluid compressing all four chambers of the heart","Systemic vasoconstriction raising left ventricular afterload","Septal bowing into the left ventricle that limits its filling","Coronary artery embolism reducing left ventricular contraction"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.4","dok":3,"approach":["Clots suddenly raise pulmonary vascular resistance, so right ventricular afterload rises.","The thin-walled right ventricle dilates, and because both ventricles share the septum and pericardium, the septum shifts left.","A smaller left ventricle fills less, so stroke volume and cardiac output fall (ventricular interdependence)."],"why":"Acute right ventricular pressure overload dilates the right ventricle. Inside the fixed pericardial space, the septum bows into the left ventricle and reduces its preload. Left ventricular stroke volume falls, causing hypotension. Low aortic pressure and high right ventricular wall stress also reduce right coronary perfusion, which explains the troponin rise often seen in PE with right ventricular strain.","whyNot":["Tamponade would show a pericardial effusion on echocardiography. His echo shows a dilated right ventricle and septal shift instead.","Higher afterload would raise blood pressure. His problem is low left ventricular preload, and his pressure is falling.","Correct.","Venous clots travel to the lungs and cannot reach the coronary arteries unless they cross a right-to-left shunt. Nothing here suggests that."],"study":{"topics":["Acute right heart strain","Ventricular interdependence","Obstructive shock"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-B2"]}},{"id":"CV-C55-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sequences of enzymatic steps produces the fragment measured by this patient's D-dimer assay?","options":["Factor XIIIa cross-links fibrin, then plasmin cleaves the clot","Plasmin cleaves circulating fibrinogen that was never cross-linked","Tissue plasminogen activator directly digests cross-linked fibrin","Thrombin cleaves fibrinogen, then antithrombin breaks down fibrin"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["D-dimer is two D domains held together by a covalent cross-link.","That cross-link is made only by factor XIIIa in a formed clot.","Plasmin must then break the cross-linked fibrin to release the fragment."],"why":"Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin and activates factor XIII. Factor XIIIa, a transglutaminase, covalently links neighboring D domains of fibrin. When plasmin degrades this stabilized clot, the linked D domains are released as D-dimer. A high value shows that a clot formed and is being broken down, so it is sensitive but not specific.","whyNot":["Correct.","Plasmin acting on fibrinogen alone makes fibrinogen degradation products without D-dimer, because no cross-link exists. This pattern occurs in primary fibrinogenolysis.","Tissue plasminogen activator converts plasminogen to plasmin. Plasmin then does the work of digesting the fibrin.","Antithrombin inhibits thrombin and factor Xa. It does not degrade fibrin."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrin stabilization by factor XIII","Fibrinolysis","D-dimer"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C56","system":"CV","dx":"Deep vein thrombosis after surgery and immobility","codes":["CV-18-I10"],"case":"A 71-year-old woman is evaluated because of aching and swelling of the left leg 5 days after surgical repair of a left hip fracture. Because of pain, she has been mostly confined to bed since the operation. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F). Physical examination shows a warm, tender left calf with pitting edema; its circumference is 4 cm greater than that of the right calf. Compression duplex ultrasonography shows noncompressible left popliteal and femoral veins containing echogenic thrombus. Platelet count is 260,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000). The diagnosis is proximal deep vein thrombosis after surgery and immobility. Treatment with unfractionated heparin is started.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C56-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the composition of this patient's venous thrombus?","options":["Many trapped red cells in a fibrin mesh, formed where flow is slow","Mostly platelets with little fibrin, formed where shear stress is high","Platelets and fibrin attached to a damaged heart valve leaflet","Separated layers of red cells and plasma, formed after flow stops"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: a vein with stasis.","Low flow lets coagulation factors build up, so fibrin forms and traps red cells.","Choose the red (stasis) thrombus."],"why":"Venous thrombi form under low flow, where activated clotting factors are not washed away. Fibrin forms a mesh that traps many red cells, so the thrombus is red and gelatinous and often builds a long tail in the direction of flow. This fibrin-rich makeup is why venous thrombosis is treated with anticoagulants.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is an arterial (pale) thrombus, which forms on a ruptured plaque under high shear and is platelet rich. That is why arterial disease is treated with antiplatelet drugs.","This describes a valve vegetation, as in endocarditis. Her clot is in a leg vein.","This describes a postmortem clot, which settles into layers after circulation stops and is not attached to the vessel wall."],"study":{"topics":["Venous versus arterial thrombi","Virchow triad"],"comps":["CV-18-H6","CV-18-I10"]}},{"id":"CV-C56-Q2","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms that normally promotes venous return from the deep veins of the leg was most reduced by her bed rest?","options":["Sympathetic venoconstriction that keeps the deep veins narrowed","Plasma oncotic pressure that pulls tissue fluid into the leg veins","Arteriolar constriction that limits inflow of blood into the leg","Calf muscle contraction that squeezes blood through one-way valves"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-I10","obj":"CV-18-I.8","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the factor in Virchow triad that immobility causes: stasis.","Ask what normally moves venous blood upward from the legs against gravity.","The skeletal muscle pump, with venous valves, drives venous return from the legs."],"why":"The deep leg veins run within muscle compartments. Each calf contraction squeezes them, and the one-way valves let blood move only toward the heart. Without movement this pump stops, blood pools in the valve pockets and soleal sinuses, and stasis allows clotting factors to accumulate.","whyNot":["Sympathetic venous tone is still present during bed rest. Venoconstriction actually pushes blood toward the heart, so it is not the force she lost.","Plasma oncotic pressure is unchanged by immobility. It governs fluid shifts across capillaries, not forward flow in large veins.","Arteriolar tone controls inflow to capillaries. Bed rest does not remove it, and less inflow would not explain pooling in the veins.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Skeletal muscle pump","Venous return","Venous valves"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-12"]}},{"id":"CV-C56-Q3","stem":"Iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis occurs more often in the left leg than in the right leg. Which of the following anatomic relationships best explains this pattern?","options":["The left renal vein crossing between the aorta and superior mesenteric artery","The right common iliac artery crossing anterior to the left common iliac vein","The left common iliac artery crossing anterior to the right common iliac vein","The inguinal ligament pressing on the left femoral vein during hip flexion"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["Picture the aortic bifurcation at about L4 and the IVC formed to the right of midline at about L5.","The left common iliac vein must cross to the right to reach the IVC.","On its way it passes behind the right common iliac artery, which presses it against the lower lumbar spine."],"why":"The IVC lies to the right of the aorta, so the left common iliac vein takes a longer course across the midline. The right common iliac artery crosses in front of it and can compress it against the fifth lumbar vertebra. Chronic pulsatile compression narrows the vein and causes stasis. When this compression causes symptoms, it is called May-Thurner syndrome.","whyNot":["Compression of the left renal vein between the aorta and SMA (nutcracker) causes left flank pain, hematuria, or a left varicocele, not leg DVT.","Correct.","The right common iliac vein runs a short, nearly straight course to the IVC and is not crossed by the left common iliac artery.","The inguinal ligament is symmetric on both sides and does not explain a left-sided pattern."],"study":{"topics":["Iliac vessel relationships","May-Thurner anatomy","Formation of the IVC"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I10"]}},{"id":"CV-C56-Q4","stem":"On day 7 of heparin therapy, her platelet count is 98,000/µL, and duplex ultrasonography shows a new thrombus in the right femoral vein. Which of the following mechanisms best explains both findings?","options":["IgM cold agglutinins fixing complement on platelet surfaces","IgE on mast cells binding heparin and releasing histamine","IgG against platelet factor 4 and heparin activating platelets","Cytotoxic T cells destroying megakaryocytes in the bone marrow"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Platelets fell by more than half about 5 to 10 days after heparin started, and a new clot formed.","Low platelets plus new thrombosis points to heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT).","Name the mechanism: IgG antibodies to platelet factor 4 bound to heparin."],"why":"Heparin binds platelet factor 4 (PF4), creating a new epitope. IgG antibodies form against the PF4-heparin complex, and the immune complexes bind platelet Fc receptors. This activates platelets, which releases more PF4 and procoagulant microparticles, so platelets are consumed and cleared while thrombin generation rises. The result is a low count with new clots, so heparin must be stopped and replaced with a non-heparin anticoagulant.","whyNot":["Cold agglutinins are IgM antibodies against red cells, as in Mycoplasma infection. They cause hemolysis, not falling platelets with clots.","An IgE-mediated reaction causes hives, wheezing, or anaphylaxis within minutes. It does not lower platelets or cause clots.","Correct.","T cells attacking megakaryocytes would reduce platelet production but would not cause new thrombosis."],"study":{"topics":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Antibody-mediated platelet activation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C57","system":"CV","dx":"Deep vein thrombosis in a patient with factor V Leiden","codes":["CV-18-I10"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-day history of pain and swelling of the right leg. She has had no recent surgery, trauma, or prolonged travel. She began taking a combined estrogen-progestin oral contraceptive 4 months ago. Her mother had a deep vein thrombosis at age 40 years. Duplex ultrasonography shows thrombus in the right femoral vein. Prothrombin time is 12 seconds (11 to 13.5), and activated partial thromboplastin time is 30 seconds (25 to 35). An activated protein C resistance assay is abnormal, and genetic testing shows heterozygosity for factor V Leiden (F5 R506Q). The diagnosis is deep vein thrombosis in a patient with factor V Leiden.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C57-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the molecular mechanism by which this patient's variant promotes thrombosis?","options":["Activated factor V resists cleavage by activated protein C","Thrombomodulin fails to bind thrombin on the endothelial surface","Antithrombin cannot bind heparan sulfate on endothelial cells","A 3' untranslated region change raises plasma prothrombin levels"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.2","dok":2,"approach":["R506Q is a single amino acid change: arginine 506 becomes glutamine.","Arginine 506 is a cleavage site where activated protein C inactivates factor Va.","Factor Va lasts longer, so more thrombin is made."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is a missense point mutation (G to A) that replaces arginine 506 with glutamine. Activated protein C, with protein S as cofactor, normally shuts off factor Va by cleaving it, and arginine 506 is the first and fastest cleavage site. The mutant factor Va resists that cleavage, so prothrombinase stays active longer and thrombin generation rises. Routine PT and aPTT are normal because the clotting factors are present in normal amounts and work normally.","whyNot":["Correct.","Thrombomodulin defects are rare and would reduce protein C activation. Her abnormal test is resistance of factor Va to protein C that is already activated.","Antithrombin deficiency or dysfunction is a separate inherited thrombophilia. It does not explain APC resistance.","This describes the prothrombin G20210A variant, the second most common inherited thrombophilia. Her test shows a factor V variant."],"study":{"topics":["Factor V Leiden","Protein C pathway","Inherited thrombophilias"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C57-Q2","stem":"Her husband is tested and is also heterozygous for factor V Leiden. Which of the following is the probability that their first child will be homozygous for the variant?","options":["12.5%","25%","50%","75%"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.1","dok":2,"approach":["Each heterozygous parent passes the variant allele with a probability of 1/2.","A homozygous child must receive the variant from both parents.","Multiply: 1/2 times 1/2 equals 1/4, or 25%."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern for thrombosis risk, with incomplete penetrance. Two heterozygous parents give a 25% chance of a homozygous child, a 50% chance of a heterozygous child, and a 25% chance of an unaffected child. Homozygotes have a much higher lifetime risk of clotting than heterozygotes, which is why this calculation matters for counseling.","whyNot":["12.5% would apply if one more independent 1/2 event were required. With two heterozygous parents there are only two transmissions.","Correct.","50% is the chance of a heterozygous child, or of any affected child if only one parent carried the variant.","75% is the chance that a child carries at least one copy (heterozygous or homozygous), not the chance of being homozygous."],"study":{"topics":["Punnett square for two heterozygotes","Autosomal dominant inheritance with incomplete penetrance"],"comps":["CV-14"]}},{"id":"CV-C57-Q3","stem":"Two days later, she develops sudden weakness of the left arm. MRI of the brain shows a small acute infarct in the territory of the right middle cerebral artery. Echocardiography with agitated saline shows bubbles passing from the right atrium to the left atrium during a Valsalva maneuver through a flap-like opening in the atrial septum; no septal tissue is missing. Failure of which of the following normal processes most likely allowed a leg thrombus to reach her brain?","options":["Obliteration of the ductus venosus to form the ligamentum venosum","Sealing of the opening between septum primum and septum secundum","Division of the truncus arteriosus by the aorticopulmonary septum","Closure of the ductus arteriosus soon after the first breaths"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.2","dok":3,"approach":["A venous clot causing a stroke means it crossed from the right side of the heart to the left (paradoxical embolism).","A flap-like atrial opening that opens with straining is a patent foramen ovale.","The foramen ovale closes when the septum primum seals against the septum secundum after birth."],"why":"Before birth, the foramen ovale lets blood pass from the right to the left atrium through the gap between the septum secundum and the septum primum. After birth, left atrial pressure rises above right, pressing the septum primum against the septum secundum, and the two usually fuse. In about a quarter of adults they never fuse, leaving a patent foramen ovale. Straining raises right atrial pressure, opens the flap, and lets a venous clot reach the systemic arteries.","whyNot":["The ductus venosus bypasses the fetal liver. Its persistence would not create an atrial-level shunt.","Correct.","Failed truncal division causes persistent truncus arteriosus, a cyanotic defect present from infancy, not an adult atrial flap.","A patent ductus arteriosus connects the aorta and pulmonary artery, not the atria, and usually shunts left to right after birth."],"study":{"topics":["Patent foramen ovale","Paradoxical embolism","Atrial septation"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C57-Q4","stem":"Her thrombosis risk increased sharply after she began taking the combined oral contraceptive. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this added risk?","options":["Progestin destroys the valves in the deep veins of the lower leg","Estrogen causes thrombocytosis by raising thrombopoietin output","Estrogen stimulates erythropoietin release and thickens the blood","Estrogen raises hepatic clotting factors and lowers protein S"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I10","obj":"CV-18-I.8","dok":3,"approach":["Estrogen acts on the liver, where most clotting proteins are made.","It increases procoagulant factors and decreases natural anticoagulants such as protein S and antithrombin.","This acquired hypercoagulable state adds to the inherited APC resistance."],"why":"Oral estrogen passes through the liver and raises synthesis of fibrinogen and factors II, VII, VIII, and X while lowering protein S and antithrombin. Lower free protein S also produces an acquired resistance to activated protein C. Combined with factor V Leiden, these two hits on the protein C pathway multiply her risk well beyond either one alone.","whyNot":["No hormone destroys venous valves. Valve damage follows a prior DVT and leads to post-thrombotic syndrome.","Estrogen does not cause thrombocytosis. High platelet counts arise from inflammation, iron deficiency, or myeloproliferative disease.","Estrogen does not raise erythropoietin. Androgens and hypoxia do, and the higher hematocrit raises viscosity.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Estrogen and coagulation","Acquired hypercoagulable states"],"comps":["CV-18-I10","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C58","system":"CV","dx":"Varicose veins with chronic venous insufficiency","codes":["CV-18-I11"],"case":"A 56-year-old man comes to the physician because of heaviness and aching of the right leg that worsen through the day and improve with elevation. He has worked as a line cook for 30 years, standing about 10 hours a day. Physical examination shows dilated, tortuous superficial veins along the medial thigh and calf of the right leg, ankle edema, and brown, thickened skin above the medial malleolus. The ankle-brachial index is 1.05 (0.9 to 1.3). Duplex ultrasonography shows reflux in the main superficial vein of the medial leg and at its junction with the deep system, and incompetent valves in the deep veins. The diagnosis is varicose veins with chronic venous insufficiency.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C58-Q1","stem":"His varicosities follow the medial thigh and calf. Which of the following best describes the incompetent superficial vein and its normal site of termination in the deep system?","options":["Small saphenous vein into the femoral vein in the groin","Great saphenous vein into the popliteal vein behind the knee","Great saphenous vein into the femoral vein in the groin","Small saphenous vein into the popliteal vein behind the knee"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.4","dok":2,"approach":["Medial leg and thigh varicosities follow the great saphenous vein.","Trace its course: anterior to the medial malleolus, up the medial leg, behind the medial femoral condyle, and up the medial thigh.","It passes through the saphenous opening in the fascia lata to join the femoral vein."],"why":"The great saphenous vein begins at the medial end of the dorsal venous arch, passes anterior to the medial malleolus, and ascends the medial leg and thigh. It ends by passing through the saphenous opening in the fascia lata, just below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, to join the femoral vein. Valve failure at this saphenofemoral junction is the most common source of reflux in varicose veins.","whyNot":["The small saphenous vein ends in the popliteal fossa, not the groin.","The great saphenous vein passes behind the knee on its medial side but continues up to the groin. It does not end in the popliteal vein.","Correct.","This is the correct course of the small saphenous vein, which runs behind the lateral malleolus and up the back of the calf. Reflux there gives posterior calf varicosities."],"study":{"topics":["Great and small saphenous veins","Saphenous opening"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I11"]}},{"id":"CV-C58-Q2","stem":"In a healthy adult, foot venous pressure is about 90 mmHg during quiet standing and falls sharply within a few steps of walking. Which of the following best describes this patient's foot venous pressure during walking?","options":["It rises above arterial pressure because contracting muscles block outflow","It falls as far as in a healthy leg because his calf muscles still contract","It falls below normal because his dilated veins act as a large reservoir","It falls only a little because blood refluxes through incompetent valves"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-I11","obj":"CV-18-I.9","dok":3,"approach":["Standing still, foot venous pressure equals the weight of the blood column up to the heart, about 90 mmHg.","During walking, each calf contraction empties the veins, and competent valves break the column into short segments, so pressure falls.","With incompetent valves, blood refluxes back between contractions and the column stays intact."],"why":"During exercise, the muscle pump plus competent valves lowers foot venous pressure, which reduces capillary filtration and edema. When valves fail, the veins refill from above almost as fast as they are emptied, so pressure falls only slightly and stays high during walking (ambulatory venous hypertension). This sustained pressure drives the edema, skin changes, and ulcers of chronic venous insufficiency.","whyNot":["Venous pressure cannot rise above arterial pressure in a leg with intact arterial inflow, and muscle contraction pushes blood toward the heart.","Muscle contraction alone does not lower pressure. Without working valves, blood flows back as soon as the muscle relaxes.","Dilated veins hold more blood, which keeps pressure high. They do not lower it below normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ambulatory venous pressure","Muscle pump during exercise"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C58-Q3","stem":"Two years later, a shallow, irregular, weeping ulcer develops on the same leg. Which of the following best describes the most likely location and mechanism of this ulcer?","options":["Above the medial ankle, from sustained venous hypertension","Back of the heel, from prolonged pressure while lying in bed","Tips of the toes, from reduced arterial inflow and ischemia","Under the metatarsal heads, from repeated pressure on numb skin"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I11","obj":"CV-18-I.9","dok":2,"approach":["Link the disease to its pressure problem: high venous pressure in the lower leg.","That pressure is highest and the skin most affected in the gaiter area above the medial malleolus.","Choose the location and mechanism that match venous disease."],"why":"Sustained venous hypertension distends skin capillaries and lets fluid, fibrinogen, and red cells leak into the tissue. Trapped leukocytes, fibrosis (lipodermatosclerosis), and poor nutrient exchange make the skin fragile, and it breaks down into a shallow, irregular ulcer. These ulcers most often occur above the medial malleolus, where the brown, thickened skin already appears.","whyNot":["Correct.","Heel pressure injuries occur in bedbound patients. He is ambulatory, and his skin changes point to venous disease.","Painful, punched-out ulcers on the toe tips come from peripheral arterial disease. His ankle-brachial index is normal.","Ulcers under the metatarsal heads are neuropathic, as in diabetic neuropathy. Nothing here suggests sensory loss."],"study":{"topics":["Venous stasis ulcers","Arterial versus venous versus neuropathic ulcers"],"comps":["CV-18-I11","CV-18-I6"]}},{"id":"CV-C58-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best describes the brown pigment in the skin above his medial malleolus and its source?","options":["Lipofuscin from lipid peroxidation in aging dermal fibroblasts","Homogentisic acid polymer bound to collagen fibers of the dermis","Iron from leaked red cells, stored by macrophages as hemosiderin","Bilirubin carried in the blood and deposited in the dermis"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-I11","obj":"CV-18-I.9","dok":3,"approach":["High capillary pressure lets red cells leak into the dermis.","Macrophages engulf the red cells and break down hemoglobin.","The released iron is stored as ferritin, which aggregates into brown hemosiderin."],"why":"Macrophages break down the hemoglobin of leaked red cells. Heme oxygenase opens the heme ring, releasing iron and producing biliverdin, which becomes bilirubin and is carried away. The iron stays in the macrophage, bound to ferritin, and excess ferritin aggregates into golden-brown hemosiderin granules that stain blue with Prussian blue. This local iron deposit is what gives stasis skin its brown color.","whyNot":["Lipofuscin is a yellow-brown wear-and-tear pigment inside aging cells such as neurons and cardiac myocytes. It does not explain a localized patch above the ankle.","Homogentisic acid deposits occur in alkaptonuria (ochronosis) and darken cartilage, ears, and sclerae.","Correct.","Bilirubin is cleared from tissue and colors skin diffusely yellow in jaundice. It does not form a fixed brown patch over the ankle."],"study":{"topics":["Heme degradation","Ferritin and hemosiderin","Stasis dermatitis"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C59","system":"CV","dx":"Kaposi sarcoma in advanced HIV infection","codes":["CV-18-J2"],"case":"A 38-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of painless purple plaques and nodules on both lower legs. He has HIV infection and stopped taking antiretroviral therapy 3 years ago. Physical examination also shows a flat violaceous lesion on the hard palate and mild edema of both legs. CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 42/µL (500 to 1,500), and HIV RNA is 210,000 copies/mL. Biopsy of a leg nodule is positive on immunostaining for a latency protein of a virus known to cause this tumor. The diagnosis is Kaposi sarcoma in advanced HIV infection (AIDS).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C59-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the virus that causes this patient's tumor?","options":["Nonenveloped double-stranded DNA virus in the papillomavirus family","Enveloped double-stranded DNA virus in the gammaherpesvirus subfamily","Enveloped single-stranded RNA retrovirus that uses reverse transcriptase","Enveloped double-stranded DNA virus in the betaherpesvirus subfamily"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-J2","obj":"CV-18-J.3","dok":2,"approach":["Kaposi sarcoma is caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8, also called KSHV).","All herpesviruses are enveloped double-stranded DNA viruses that establish latency.","HHV-8 belongs with Epstein-Barr virus in the gammaherpesvirus subfamily, which infects lymphocytes and drives cell growth."],"why":"HHV-8 is an enveloped, linear double-stranded DNA gammaherpesvirus. It spreads mainly through saliva and also through sexual contact and transplanted organs. It stays latent in B cells and endothelial cells, where latency proteins such as LANA keep the viral genome and push cell growth. HHV-8 also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease.","whyNot":["Papillomaviruses are nonenveloped dsDNA viruses that cause warts and cervical, anal, and oropharyngeal cancers, not Kaposi sarcoma.","Correct.","This describes HIV itself. HIV causes the immune deficiency that allows Kaposi sarcoma but is not the tumor virus.","Cytomegalovirus (HHV-5) is a betaherpesvirus. In AIDS it causes retinitis and colitis, not Kaposi sarcoma."],"study":{"topics":["Herpesvirus subfamilies","HHV-8 transmission and latency"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-J2"]}},{"id":"CV-C59-Q2","stem":"After antiretroviral therapy is restarted, his CD4+ T-lymphocyte count increases over several months and his lesions regress. Restoration of which of the following immune responses best explains the regression?","options":["Virus-specific CD8 T-cell killing aided by CD4 T cells","IgE-triggered mast cell degranulation in the dermis","Complement lysis of infected cells by the attack complex","Neutrophil phagocytosis of the infected spindle cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.7","dok":3,"approach":["Latent virus sits inside cells, out of reach of antibodies and complement.","Cells infected with virus show viral peptides on MHC class I, which CD8 cytotoxic T cells recognize.","CD8 responses need CD4 helper T cells, so restoring CD4 cells restores control."],"why":"Intracellular viral infection is controlled by cell-mediated immunity. CD8 cytotoxic T cells recognize HHV-8 peptides on MHC class I and kill infected cells with perforin and granzymes. CD4 helper T cells make IL-2 and license dendritic cells, which sustains effective CD8 responses. When HIV depletes CD4 cells, HHV-8 escapes this control, and when ART restores them, lesions often regress.","whyNot":["Correct.","IgE and mast cells defend against parasites and cause allergy. They play no role in controlling herpesvirus-infected cells.","The membrane attack complex is most important against Neisseria and other extracellular bacteria. Latent virus inside cells escapes it.","Neutrophils mainly kill extracellular bacteria and fungi. They do not target virus-infected tumor cells."],"study":{"topics":["Cell-mediated immunity to viruses","CD4 help for CD8 T cells","Immune reconstitution"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C59-Q3","stem":"Which of the following histologic findings is most likely in the biopsy specimen of the leg nodule?","options":["Atypical melanocytes spreading upward through the epidermis","Spindle cells forming slitlike spaces with extravasated red cells","Thin-walled capillaries lined by a single layer of flat endothelium","Lobular capillaries with neutrophils and silver-staining bacilli"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-J2","obj":"CV-18-J.3","dok":2,"approach":["Kaposi sarcoma is a tumor of endothelial-type spindle cells.","The spindle cells form jagged, slitlike vascular channels.","Red cells leak out of these channels, leaving hemosiderin, which gives the lesion its purple color."],"why":"Nodular Kaposi sarcoma shows sheets of plump spindle cells forming slitlike vascular spaces packed with red cells, along with extravasated red cells and hemosiderin-laden macrophages. The spindle cells carry HHV-8, which is shown by immunostaining for LANA. The leaked blood and slow flow give the lesions their purple to brown color.","whyNot":["Upward (pagetoid) spread of atypical melanocytes describes melanoma, a pigmented tumor of melanocytes with no vascular channels.","Correct.","Well-formed capillaries lined by normal flat endothelium describe a benign capillary hemangioma, which lacks spindle cells and slitlike spaces.","This describes bacillary angiomatosis, caused by Bartonella henselae or quintana in AIDS. It can look like Kaposi sarcoma on the skin but responds to antibiotics."],"study":{"topics":["Kaposi sarcoma histology","Bacillary angiomatosis","Vascular tumors"],"comps":["CV-18-J2","CV-18-J1"]}},{"id":"CV-C59-Q4","stem":"The causative virus encodes a homolog of cellular cyclin D. Which of the following best describes how this viral cyclin promotes proliferation of infected cells?","options":["It activates p53, which induces p21 and arrests the cycle","It pairs with CDK6 to phosphorylate Rb and free E2F","It inhibits CDK1 and blocks the cell from entering mitosis","It stabilizes the Rb and E2F complex to hold cells in G1"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-J2","obj":"CV-18-J.3","dok":3,"approach":["Cyclin D normally binds CDK4 or CDK6 in G1.","The cyclin-CDK complex phosphorylates Rb, which releases E2F.","Free E2F turns on S-phase genes, so the cell passes the G1 to S checkpoint."],"why":"In G1, cyclin D partners with CDK4 or CDK6 to phosphorylate the retinoblastoma protein (Rb). Phosphorylated Rb releases E2F transcription factors, which turn on genes needed for DNA synthesis. The HHV-8 viral cyclin binds CDK6 and resists normal CDK inhibitors, so it keeps Rb phosphorylated and drives continuous cell cycle entry. Other HHV-8 latency proteins, such as LANA, also block p53 and Rb.","whyNot":["p53 activation and p21 production stop the cell cycle. Viruses that cause cancer block p53 instead.","Correct.","Blocking CDK1 would arrest cells at G2. Cyclin D partners act earlier, at the G1 to S transition.","Hypophosphorylated Rb bound to E2F holds cells in G1. A cyclin that promoted this would stop growth, the opposite of an oncogenic effect."],"study":{"topics":["Cyclins, CDKs, and Rb","Viral oncogenesis"],"comps":["CV-18-J2","CV-14"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C60","system":"CV","dx":"Secondary lymphedema after axillary lymph node dissection","codes":["CV-10","CV-18-H4"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of gradually increasing swelling and heaviness of the right arm and hand; her rings no longer fit. Ten months ago, she underwent right modified radical mastectomy with axillary lymph node dissection, followed by axillary radiation, for invasive ductal carcinoma. The right forearm circumference is 5 cm greater than the left. The swelling was pitting at first but is now firm and nonpitting, with thickened skin. Venous duplex ultrasonography shows no thrombosis. Serum albumin is 4.1 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0). Lymphoscintigraphy shows delayed lymph transport, dermal backflow, and no axillary node uptake. The diagnosis is secondary lymphedema of the right arm.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C60-Q1","stem":"Before her surgery, lymph from her right hand entered the venous circulation at which of the following sites?","options":["Cisterna chyli, anterior to the first and second lumbar vertebrae","Azygos vein, through lymphatics that follow the intercostal spaces","Junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins","Junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-10","obj":"CV-10.2","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the path: hand lymphatics, then axillary nodes, then apical axillary nodes.","The right subclavian lymph trunk carries this lymph to the right lymphatic duct or directly to the vein.","It enters the blood at the right venous angle, where the right subclavian and internal jugular veins meet."],"why":"Lymph from the upper limb drains through the axillary nodes to the apical group, then into the subclavian lymph trunk. On the right, this trunk joins the right lymphatic duct or opens on its own at the right venous angle. The right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax. All other lymph reaches the left venous angle through the thoracic duct.","whyNot":["The cisterna chyli collects lymph from the abdomen and lower limbs and is the start of the thoracic duct. It is not a point of entry into the blood.","Intercostal lymphatics drain the chest wall to parasternal and intercostal nodes. The azygos vein does not receive upper limb lymph.","The left venous angle receives the thoracic duct, which drains everything below the diaphragm plus the left side of the head, neck, thorax, and left upper limb. It would be correct for her left arm.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic drainage of the upper limb","Right lymphatic duct and thoracic duct"],"comps":["CV-10"]}},{"id":"CV-C60-Q2","stem":"Her serum albumin concentration is normal, and her veins are patent. Which of the following changes in Starling forces most likely sustains the fluid accumulation in her arm?","options":["Higher interstitial oncotic pressure from retained tissue protein","Lower plasma oncotic pressure from loss of albumin in the urine","Higher capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous outflow blockage","Higher capillary permeability from widened endothelial cell gaps"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["Capillaries normally filter a small net amount of fluid and protein that only lymphatics can return.","With lymphatics removed, the protein stays in the interstitium.","Interstitial oncotic pressure rises, which increases net filtration and traps more water."],"why":"Even with normal Starling forces, capillaries filter a little more fluid than they reabsorb, along with small amounts of plasma protein. Lymphatics normally return this fluid and protein to the blood, about 2 to 3 L per day for the whole body. Without lymph drainage, protein accumulates in the tissue, raising interstitial oncotic pressure and drawing still more fluid out of the capillaries. This is why lymphedema fluid is high in protein, unlike most other edemas.","whyNot":["Correct.","Low plasma oncotic pressure occurs in nephrotic syndrome and liver failure. Her albumin is normal.","Venous obstruction raises capillary hydrostatic pressure, as in DVT. Her duplex scan shows open veins.","Increased permeability occurs in inflammation, burns, and sepsis. Her edema came on gradually after node removal, with no sign of inflammation."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Lymph flow","Mechanisms of edema"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C60-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains the change in her swelling from soft and pitting to firm and nonpitting?","options":["Hemosiderin deposited from repeated bleeding into soft tissue","Glycosaminoglycans deposited in the dermis from thyroid disease","Protein-rich fluid driving fibrosis and fat buildup in tissue","Clot organizing in the deep veins and stiffening the arm tissue"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H4","obj":"CV-18-H.6","dok":2,"approach":["Early lymphedema is free fluid that can be pushed aside, so it pits.","Long-standing protein-rich fluid causes chronic inflammation that stimulates fibroblasts and fat cells.","Fibrosis and fat do not move under pressure, so the swelling becomes firm and nonpitting."],"why":"Stagnant, protein-rich interstitial fluid triggers chronic low-grade inflammation. Fibroblasts lay down collagen, adipose tissue expands, and the skin thickens. Once fibrosis and fat replace free fluid, pressing on the arm no longer leaves a pit, and elevation alone no longer reduces the swelling. Skin may take on a thick, dimpled texture (peau d'orange), and long-standing cases carry a small risk of angiosarcoma.","whyNot":["Hemosiderin causes brown discoloration, as in venous stasis. It does not make tissue firm, and she has no history of bleeding.","Glycosaminoglycan deposition causes nonpitting myxedema in hypothyroidism or pretibial myxedema in Graves disease. Nothing suggests thyroid disease here.","Correct.","Her duplex scan shows no venous thrombosis."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic lymphedema","Pitting versus nonpitting edema"],"comps":["CV-18-H4","CV-10"]}},{"id":"CV-C60-Q4","stem":"Eight months later, she has a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F) and a warm, spreading area of erythema without pus on the swollen forearm. Blood cultures grow beta-hemolytic, gram-positive cocci in chains that are sensitive to bacitracin. Which of the following surface proteins of this organism resists phagocytosis and is the main target of type-specific antibodies?","options":["Protein A, which binds the Fc portion of IgG and blocks opsonization","Internalin, which binds E-cadherin to enter epithelial cells","Coagulase, which clots plasma and wraps the organism in fibrin","M protein, which recruits factor H and limits C3b deposition"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":3,"approach":["Beta-hemolytic cocci in chains that are bacitracin sensitive are Streptococcus pyogenes (group A).","Lymphedema impairs local immune surveillance and is a strong risk factor for recurrent streptococcal cellulitis.","The major group A antiphagocytic surface protein, and the basis for typing, is M protein."],"why":"Streptococcus pyogenes is a common cause of nonpurulent cellulitis, and a lymphedematous limb is especially prone to repeated attacks. M protein extends from the cell wall and binds host factor H, which speeds breakdown of C3b, so less complement opsonin coats the bacterium and phagocytes cannot grab it. Antibodies against a given M type protect against that type, and M protein also shows molecular mimicry with cardiac proteins, the basis of rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["Protein A is the antiphagocytic surface protein of Staphylococcus aureus, which grows in clusters and is catalase positive.","Internalin lets Listeria monocytogenes enter gut epithelial cells. Listeria is a gram-positive rod.","Coagulase identifies Staphylococcus aureus. It is an enzyme that converts fibrinogen to fibrin, not a streptococcal surface protein.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Streptococcus pyogenes virulence factors","Cellulitis risk in lymphedema"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C07","system":"CV","dx":"Stable angina pectoris from a fixed atherosclerotic coronary narrowing","codes":["CV-18-C1"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman who works as a letter carrier comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of pressure-like substernal chest discomfort. The discomfort occurs when she walks uphill carrying her mail bag and resolves within 5 minutes of rest. It has never occurred at rest. She has type 2 diabetes mellitus and smokes cigarettes. Pulse is 76/min and blood pressure is 146/88 mm Hg. An ECG at rest shows no abnormalities. Serum LDL cholesterol is 168 mg/dL (reference below 100 mg/dL), and HDL cholesterol is 38 mg/dL (reference 50 mg/dL or higher for women). During treadmill exercise testing, at a heart rate of 142/min, she develops her usual discomfort and 2-mm horizontal ST-segment depression in leads V4 to V6 that resolves during recovery. Diagnosis: stable angina pectoris from a fixed atherosclerotic coronary narrowing.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C07-Q1","stem":"During exercise, rising shear stress on healthy coronary endothelium triggers release of nitric oxide, which dilates the artery. In atherosclerotic segments like hers, nitric oxide output is reduced and the artery dilates poorly. Which of the following reactions produces nitric oxide in endothelial cells?","options":["Guanylyl cyclase converting GTP into nitric oxide and cGMP","Nitric oxide synthase oxidizing L-arginine to L-citrulline","Arginase splitting L-arginine into ornithine and nitric oxide","Heme oxygenase breaking down heme into biliverdin and nitric oxide"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["The clue is a gas made by endothelium that relaxes smooth muscle.","Recall the enzyme that makes it and its amino acid substrate.","Separate the enzyme that makes nitric oxide from the enzyme nitric oxide activates."],"why":"Endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) oxidizes L-arginine, using oxygen and NADPH with tetrahydrobiopterin as a cofactor, to form nitric oxide and L-citrulline. Shear stress and calcium-calmodulin activate eNOS. Nitric oxide diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, and raises cGMP, which lowers cytosolic calcium and relaxes the vessel. Atherosclerosis and its risk factors reduce nitric oxide availability (endothelial dysfunction), so the narrowed artery cannot dilate well when demand rises.","whyNot":["Guanylyl cyclase is the target of nitric oxide in smooth muscle. It converts GTP to cGMP and does not make nitric oxide.","Correct.","Arginase converts L-arginine to ornithine and urea in the urea cycle. It competes with nitric oxide synthase for arginine but makes no nitric oxide.","Heme oxygenase breaks heme into biliverdin, iron, and carbon monoxide. Carbon monoxide is a different gas signal."],"study":{"topics":["Endothelial nitric oxide synthesis","Endothelial dysfunction in atherosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-11","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C07-Q2","stem":"Her high LDL enters the intima of her coronary arteries, where some of it is oxidized. Macrophages take up the oxidized LDL and become lipid-filled foam cells. Which of the following best explains why macrophages continue to take up cholesterol after their cholesterol content is high?","options":["Rising cell cholesterol activates SREBP and raises LDL receptor numbers","Oxidized LDL crosses the cell membrane only by passive diffusion","Macrophages lack the enzyme that esterifies cholesterol for storage","Scavenger receptors for oxidized LDL are not suppressed by cholesterol"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how most cells stop LDL uptake when cholesterol is high.","Identify which receptor macrophages use for oxidized LDL.","Ask whether that receptor is under the same sterol feedback."],"why":"Most cells take up native LDL through the LDL receptor. When cell cholesterol rises, SREBP stays in the endoplasmic reticulum, so transcription of the LDL receptor and HMG-CoA reductase falls and uptake stops. Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36, which lack this feedback. Uptake therefore continues, the cholesterol is esterified by ACAT and stored in droplets, and the cell becomes a foam cell.","whyNot":["High cell cholesterol does the opposite: it keeps SREBP inactive and lowers LDL receptor numbers. This feedback is why the LDL receptor pathway cannot make foam cells.","Oxidized LDL is a large particle taken up by receptor-mediated endocytosis. It does not diffuse across the membrane.","Macrophages do esterify cholesterol with ACAT. The stored cholesteryl ester droplets are what give foam cells their foamy look.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Scavenger receptors and foam cell formation","SREBP regulation of the LDL receptor"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C07-Q3","stem":"Eight months later she has chest discomfort at rest that lasts about 20 minutes and recurs several times over 2 days. During pain her ECG shows ST depression in V3 to V6. Serial troponin levels stay within the reference range. Which of the following changes in her coronary plaque most likely caused this new pattern?","options":["Dystrophic calcification of the core with an intact plaque surface","Slow plaque growth that thickens its collagen-rich fibrous cap","A fissured plaque cap with a thrombus partly blocking the lumen","Complete thrombotic occlusion causing full-thickness necrosis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C1","obj":"CV-18-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["Rest pain that is new and recurring with normal troponin defines unstable angina.","Unstable angina comes from an acute change in the plaque, not gradual growth.","Normal troponin rules out myocardial necrosis."],"why":"Pain at rest that is new or increasing, with ST depression and no troponin rise, is unstable angina. In most cases a plaque fissures or ruptures, platelets aggregate, and a thrombus forms that narrows the lumen without completely blocking it. Flow can fall at rest, and fragments may embolize downstream. Because troponin stays normal, the ischemia has not caused myocyte necrosis.","whyNot":["Calcification makes a plaque stiffer but does not by itself cause a sudden change in symptoms. An intact surface does not trigger thrombosis.","Slow growth with a thick fibrous cap describes a stable plaque. It causes symptoms only when demand rises, which is her original pattern.","Correct.","Complete occlusion with full-thickness necrosis is an ST-elevation MI. Troponin would rise and the ECG would show ST elevation."],"study":{"topics":["Unstable angina versus stable angina","Acute plaque change"],"comps":["CV-18-C1","CV-18-C3","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C07-Q4","stem":"Her exercise ECG showed ST depression, which reflects ischemia limited to the inner layer of the left ventricular wall. Her heart rate rose to 142/min during the test. Which of the following best explains why the subendocardium is to become ischemic when demand rises past what a narrowed artery can supply?","options":["It is supplied by end arteries that arise from the coronary sinus","Its myocytes depend on anaerobic glycolysis even when oxygen is plentiful","It is squeezed in systole and perfused mainly during diastole","It is fed by blood from the ventricular cavity that is low in oxygen"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-8","obj":"CV-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note where the ischemia is: the inner layer of the left ventricle.","Recall when left ventricular myocardium receives most of its flow.","Link a faster heart rate to a shorter diastole."],"why":"Coronary arteries run on the epicardial surface and send branches inward, so the subendocardium is farthest from the supply. During systole, pressure inside the left ventricular wall is highest in the subendocardium and compresses its vessels, so most of its flow occurs during diastole. Tachycardia shortens diastole more than systole, which cuts subendocardial perfusion just as oxygen demand rises. With a fixed stenosis, this layer becomes ischemic first, producing ST depression on the surface ECG.","whyNot":["The coronary sinus is a vein that drains the heart into the right atrium. It does not supply any myocardium.","Cardiac myocytes rely on aerobic metabolism, mostly fatty acid oxidation, when oxygen is available. Anaerobic glycolysis takes over only during ischemia.","Correct.","Small veins (Thebesian veins) drain into the chambers, but the ventricular cavity does not supply the myocardium in a normal adult heart. The subendocardium is fed by coronary branches."],"study":{"topics":["Subendocardial perfusion","Effect of heart rate on diastolic time"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-11","CV-18-C1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C08","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic ischemic heart disease (ischemic cardiomyopathy) with heart failure","codes":["CV-18-C2"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of increasing shortness of breath when climbing one flight of stairs, orthopnea, and ankle swelling. He has had no recent chest pain. He had myocardial infarctions at ages 60 and 66 years. Pulse is 88/min and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows crackles at both lung bases, an S3 gallop, and edema of both ankles. Serum BNP is 820 pg/mL (reference below 100 pg/mL). Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30% (reference 55% to 70%), a thin, akinetic scar in the inferior wall, and a hypokinetic anterior wall. Coronary angiography shows severe stenosis of all three major coronary arteries. Diagnosis: chronic ischemic heart disease (ischemic cardiomyopathy) with heart failure.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C08-Q1","stem":"Further imaging shows that his hypokinetic anterior wall has normal thickness and takes up a metabolic tracer, so its myocytes are alive. After bypass surgery restores blood flow to it, the anterior wall contracts normally over the following weeks. Which of the following best describes the anterior wall before surgery?","options":["Hibernating myocardium","Stunned myocardium","Transmural collagen scar","Ventricular aneurysm"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C2","obj":"CV-18-C.7","dok":2,"approach":["The muscle is alive but weak while flow is chronically low.","Function returns only after flow is restored.","Match that pattern to the term, and separate it from stunning, which follows a brief ischemic episode."],"why":"Hibernating myocardium is living muscle that lowers its contraction to match a chronically reduced blood supply. It stays weak as long as flow stays low and recovers after revascularization. It is common in chronic ischemic heart disease and is one reason bypass or stenting can improve ejection fraction. Detecting viable tissue with metabolic or perfusion imaging helps predict which segments will recover.","whyNot":["Correct.","Stunned myocardium follows a brief episode of severe ischemia after flow has already returned. It recovers on its own over hours to days without revascularization.","A scar is dead tissue replaced by collagen. It is thin, does not take up a metabolic tracer, and does not regain contraction, like his inferior wall.","A ventricular aneurysm is a thin scar that bulges outward during systole. It has no living muscle to recover."],"study":{"topics":["Hibernating versus stunned myocardium","Chronic ischemic heart disease"],"comps":["CV-18-C2","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C08-Q2","stem":"His healthy myocardium gets most of its ATP from oxidizing fatty acids. In his poorly perfused but living segments, a shift toward oxidizing glucose instead would help those cells keep working. Which of the following best explains why this shift helps when oxygen supply is limited?","options":["Glucose oxidation avoids the citric acid cycle and the NADH it forms","Glucose oxidation yields more ATP per gram of fuel than fatty acids","Glucose oxidation makes all of its ATP without using mitochondria","Glucose oxidation yields more ATP for each molecule of oxygen used"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.4","dok":3,"approach":["The limiting factor in ischemia is oxygen, not fuel.","Compare fuels by ATP made per oxygen consumed.","Fatty acids are more reduced and use more oxygen for each ATP they produce."],"why":"Fatty acids are more reduced than glucose, so their oxidation passes a larger share of electrons through FADH2 and uses more oxygen per carbon. As a result, complete oxidation of glucose produces more ATP for each molecule of oxygen consumed. When oxygen delivery is the limit, as in chronically ischemic myocardium, shifting fuel use toward glucose gets more work from the same oxygen. This is the rationale behind metabolic approaches that partly inhibit fatty acid oxidation in angina.","whyNot":["Pyruvate from glucose enters the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA and generates NADH, just as fatty acids do. Both fuels depend on the cycle.","Fatty acids yield more ATP per gram than glucose, which is why fat is the body's main energy store. The advantage of glucose is per oxygen, not per gram.","Only the glycolysis portion of glucose metabolism runs outside the mitochondria and yields 2 ATP. Complete oxidation needs the citric acid cycle and electron transport chain in mitochondria.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Myocardial fuel selection","ATP yield per oxygen for glucose and fatty acids"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-C2"]}},{"id":"CV-C08-Q3","stem":"His left ventricle has dilated over several years. At the same systolic pressure, which of the following best describes the effect of the larger chamber on the oxygen demand of the remaining viable myocardium?","options":["Demand rises, because a larger radius raises the tension in the wall","Demand falls, because stretched fibers contract with greater mechanical efficiency","Demand stays the same, because myocardial oxygen use depends only on heart rate","Demand falls, because each fiber shortens less to eject the same stroke volume"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Wall tension is a major driver of myocardial oxygen use.","Apply the law of Laplace: wall stress rises with pressure and radius and falls with wall thickness.","A dilated, thinned ventricle has a larger radius and a thinner wall."],"why":"By the law of Laplace, wall stress is about pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. A dilated ventricle has a larger radius and often a thinner wall, so each fiber must generate more tension to produce the same pressure. Wall tension, heart rate, and contractility are the main determinants of myocardial oxygen demand. Dilation therefore raises demand in a heart whose supply is already limited, which feeds the decline in chronic ischemic heart disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Moderate stretch improves force through the Frank-Starling mechanism, but in a chronically dilated heart this benefit is exhausted and wall stress dominates.","Heart rate is one determinant of oxygen demand, but wall tension and contractility matter as well.","A larger chamber does need less fiber shortening per stroke volume, but each fiber must produce more tension. The rise in tension raises oxygen use."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace and wall stress","Determinants of myocardial oxygen demand"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-B1","CV-18-C2"]}},{"id":"CV-C08-Q4","stem":"A plaque removed from one of his coronary arteries contains many activated CD4+ T cells that secrete interferon-gamma. Which of the following best explains how this cytokine makes a plaque more likely to rupture?","options":["It drives B cells to switch to IgE production against plaque lipids","It blocks collagen synthesis by smooth muscle cells in the cap","It shifts macrophages toward a repair state that lays down matrix","It stimulates fibroblasts to cross-link type I collagen in the cap"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":2,"approach":["Interferon-gamma is the main Th1 cytokine.","Plaque strength depends on collagen in the fibrous cap, made by smooth muscle cells.","Ask whether the cytokine adds or removes cap collagen."],"why":"Th1 cells in plaques secrete interferon-gamma. It inhibits collagen synthesis by intimal smooth muscle cells and activates macrophages, which release matrix metalloproteinases that break down collagen. Less collagen is made and more is destroyed, so the fibrous cap thins and becomes vulnerable to rupture. Plaque rupture with thrombosis is the usual trigger for acute coronary syndromes in patients like him.","whyNot":["Class switching to IgE is driven by IL-4 from Th2 cells, and interferon-gamma inhibits it. This is not how interferon-gamma weakens the cap.","Correct.","Interferon-gamma promotes classically activated (M1) macrophages that are pro-inflammatory. Repair-type (M2) macrophages are driven by IL-4 and IL-13.","Interferon-gamma reduces collagen production in the cap. Collagen cross-linking would strengthen the cap, the opposite effect."],"study":{"topics":["Inflammation and plaque stability","Th1 cytokines and macrophage activation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I3","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C09","system":"CV","dx":"Acute anterior wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction from proximal LAD occlusion","codes":["CV-18-C3"],"case":"A 46-year-old man who is a high school teacher is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after the onset of crushing chest pain and diaphoresis that began while he was shoveling snow. He does not smoke. His father had a myocardial infarction at age 50 years. Pulse is 98/min and blood pressure is 132/84 mm Hg. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V4. Serum troponin I is 4.8 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). LDL cholesterol is 118 mg/dL (reference below 100 mg/dL), and lipoprotein(a) is 210 nmol/L (reference below 75 nmol/L). Coronary angiography shows a thrombus occluding the proximal left anterior descending (LAD) artery, which is opened with a stent 90 minutes after the onset of pain. Diagnosis: acute anterior wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C09-Q1","stem":"On day 2, his ECG shows a new right bundle branch block. Which of the following vessels most likely supplied the injured conduction tissue?","options":["AV nodal branch of the right coronary artery","Sinoatrial nodal branch of the right coronary artery","Septal perforating branches of the LAD artery","Obtuse marginal branches of the circumflex artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-8","obj":"CV-8.1","dok":3,"approach":["The right bundle branch runs down the interventricular septum.","The anterior two-thirds of the septum is supplied by septal branches of the LAD.","His occluded artery is the LAD, which fits."],"why":"The LAD runs in the anterior interventricular groove and gives off septal perforating branches that supply the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. The right bundle branch and the anterior fascicle of the left bundle branch run through this territory and depend mainly on these branches. A new bundle branch block in anterior MI signals a large septal infarct and a risk of progression to complete heart block below the AV node.","whyNot":["The AV nodal artery supplies the AV node and the start of the bundle of His, not the length of the right bundle branch. It is usually involved in inferior MI from right coronary occlusion.","The sinoatrial nodal artery supplies the SA node in the right atrium. Its loss causes sinus node dysfunction, not bundle branch block.","Correct.","Obtuse marginal branches supply the lateral wall of the left ventricle. They do not reach the septum."],"study":{"topics":["Septal blood supply and the bundle branches","Conduction blocks in anterior MI"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C09-Q2","stem":"Flow was restored about 90 minutes after occlusion. Which of the following microscopic findings is most characteristic of irreversibly injured myocytes that are reperfused?","options":["Dense eosinophilic cross bands of hypercontracted sarcomeres","Granulation tissue with new capillaries and loose collagen","Wavy fibers at the infarct border stretched by nearby beating cells","Myocyte nuclei dividing to replace the cells that were lost"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["The key clue is reperfusion of tissue that is already dying.","Restored flow brings a surge of calcium into cells with damaged membranes.","Calcium overload causes the myofibrils to hypercontract."],"why":"When blood flow returns to myocytes that are already irreversibly injured, calcium floods in through damaged membranes. The sarcomeres contract intensely and lock, forming dense, eosinophilic transverse bands called contraction bands. This contraction band necrosis is typical of reperfused infarcts, which may also be hemorrhagic because injured vessels leak when flow returns. Reperfusion still salvages myocytes that were only reversibly injured, so it limits infarct size overall.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulation tissue appears at the infarct margins at about 7 to 14 days. It is far too late for the first hours after reperfusion.","Wavy fibers appear at the border of an early infarct as noncontracting dead fibers are stretched. They can appear within hours with or without reperfusion, so they do not mark reperfusion.","Adult cardiac myocytes are permanent cells and do not divide to replace lost tissue in any meaningful amount. Infarcts heal by scar."],"study":{"topics":["Contraction band necrosis and reperfusion injury","Infarct histology"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-2"]}},{"id":"CV-C09-Q3","stem":"His lipoprotein(a) is high. Lp(a) is an LDL-like particle in which apolipoprotein B-100 is linked to apolipoprotein(a). In addition to carrying cholesterol into the artery wall, which of the following best explains how apolipoprotein(a) promotes coronary thrombosis?","options":["It binds von Willebrand factor and increases platelet adhesion","It activates factor X directly and speeds thrombin formation","It inhibits antithrombin, leaving thrombin unopposed","It resembles plasminogen and interferes with fibrinolysis"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the structure of apolipoprotein(a).","Its repeated kringle domains are similar to those in plasminogen.","A plasminogen look-alike competes with plasminogen and slows clot breakdown."],"why":"Apolipoprotein(a) contains many kringle domains that closely resemble those of plasminogen. It competes with plasminogen for binding sites on fibrin and cells, so less plasmin is formed and clots are broken down more slowly. Lp(a) level is mostly set by inheritance at the LPA gene and changes little with diet. A high level is an independent risk factor for premature coronary disease, as in this patient with only modestly raised LDL.","whyNot":["Von Willebrand factor links platelets to exposed collagen through glycoprotein Ib. That is not the mechanism of Lp(a).","Factor X is activated by the tissue factor and intrinsic tenase complexes. Apolipoprotein(a) is not a known activator.","Lp(a) does not inhibit antithrombin. Low antithrombin activity comes from inherited deficiency, liver disease, or protein loss.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lipoprotein(a) structure and function","Fibrinolysis and plasminogen"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-14","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C09-Q4","stem":"On day 4 he develops new chest pain with fresh ST elevation in V1 to V4. His troponin I is still elevated from the first infarct. Which of the following serum markers is most useful for detecting a new infarct at this point?","options":["Creatine kinase MB","Lactate dehydrogenase","Aspartate aminotransferase","B-type natriuretic peptide"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Troponin stays high for about 7 to 10 days, so it cannot easily show a second event on day 4.","Look for a cardiac marker that returns to normal within a few days.","A fresh rise in that marker points to new necrosis."],"why":"Troponin I and T begin to rise about 2 to 4 hours after infarction and stay elevated for about 7 to 10 days. CK-MB rises over a similar early period, peaks at about 24 to 48 hours, and returns to normal within about 72 hours. By day 4 his CK-MB should be back near baseline, so a new rise strongly suggests reinfarction. This timing difference is the classic reason CK-MB is used to detect early reinfarction. Current practice can also detect reinfarction by a further rise in troponin of about 20% or more, but among these markers CK-MB is the one that has already returned to baseline.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lactate dehydrogenase rises later than CK-MB and stays high for a week or more. It is also nonspecific, so it cannot separate a new infarct from the first.","AST is found in liver and muscle as well as heart and is no longer used to diagnose MI. It is too nonspecific to confirm reinfarction.","BNP rises with ventricular wall stress and heart failure. It does not measure myocyte necrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac biomarker timing","Diagnosis of reinfarction"],"comps":["CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C10","system":"CV","dx":"Inferior wall MI with right ventricular involvement from right coronary artery occlusion, with bradycardia and second-degree AV block","codes":["CV-18-C3"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 2 hours after the onset of heavy pressure in her lower chest and upper abdomen with nausea and diaphoresis. Pulse is 44/min and blood pressure is 84/56 mm Hg. Jugular venous pressure is increased, and the lungs are clear to auscultation. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF and in the right-sided lead V4R, with ST-segment depression in leads I and aVL. A rhythm strip shows progressive lengthening of the PR interval until a P wave is not followed by a QRS complex. Serum troponin I is 2.6 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). Coronary angiography shows occlusion of a dominant right coronary artery (RCA) proximal to its right ventricular branches. Diagnosis: inferior wall MI with right ventricular involvement from RCA occlusion, with bradycardia and second-degree AV block.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following anatomic features best explains why her coronary occlusion has disturbed AV conduction?","options":["The AV nodal artery comes from the circumflex in right-dominant hearts","The AV nodal artery usually arises from the RCA near the crux","The AV node lies in the free wall of the right ventricle","The AV node is supplied by the first diagonal branch of the LAD"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.3","dok":2,"approach":["She has an RCA occlusion and a conduction block at the AV node.","Recall where the AV nodal artery arises.","Link the artery to the node it supplies."],"why":"The AV node lies in the lower part of the interatrial septum, in the triangle of Koch near the opening of the coronary sinus. Its artery usually arises from the RCA near the crux of the heart, the point where the posterior interventricular groove meets the atrioventricular groove. In most people the RCA is dominant, giving rise to the posterior descending artery and the AV nodal artery. RCA occlusion can therefore cause AV block along with inferior wall infarction.","whyNot":["In right-dominant hearts, the RCA gives the AV nodal artery. The circumflex supplies it mainly in left-dominant hearts.","Correct.","The AV node lies in the lower interatrial septum, in the triangle of Koch near the coronary sinus opening. It is not in the ventricular wall.","Diagonal branches of the LAD supply the anterolateral left ventricle. They do not supply the AV node."],"study":{"topics":["AV nodal blood supply","Coronary dominance"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C10-Q2","stem":"Her rhythm strip shows Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) second-degree AV block. This block often improves with atropine after inferior myocardial infarction. Which of the following best describes the site of the conduction delay and the reason for this response?","options":["In the His-Purkinje system, which has dense vagal innervation","In the bundle branches, which are supplied by the LAD","In the SA node, where vagal tone delays each atrial impulse","In the AV node, where vagal tone and ischemia slow conduction"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["A PR interval that lengthens before a dropped beat places the block in the AV node.","The AV node is strongly influenced by the vagus nerve.","Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors and removes vagal slowing."],"why":"In Mobitz type I block, conduction through the AV node slows a little more with each beat until one impulse fails to pass, and the cycle restarts. The AV node receives dense vagal innervation, and inferior MI often increases vagal tone through reflexes from the inferior wall, along with ischemia of the node itself. Acetylcholine acting on M2 receptors slows nodal conduction, so atropine often improves the block. Block at this level is usually transient after inferior MI.","whyNot":["The His-Purkinje system has little vagal innervation. Block there causes Mobitz type II or complete block with a fixed PR interval and responds poorly to atropine.","Bundle branch block widens the QRS but does not cause a progressively lengthening PR interval. The bundle branches are supplied mainly by the LAD and are usually affected in anterior MI.","SA node slowing causes sinus bradycardia or sinus pauses in which the P wave itself is missing. Here P waves appear on time but fail to conduct.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mobitz type I versus type II block","Autonomic control of the AV node"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C10-Q3","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains her low blood pressure with raised jugular venous pressure and clear lungs?","options":["Rupture of a papillary muscle with acute mitral regurgitation","Loss of over 40% of left ventricular mass with pulmonary congestion","Right ventricular failure that underfills the left ventricle","Cytokine-driven vasodilation with a low systemic vascular resistance"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B2","obj":"CV-18-B.2","dok":3,"approach":["Combine the findings: hypotension, high venous pressure, and clear lungs.","High venous pressure with clear lungs points to a problem on the right side.","ST elevation in V4R confirms right ventricular infarction."],"why":"Proximal RCA occlusion can infarct the right ventricle as well as the inferior left ventricle. The weak right ventricle cannot pump venous return forward, so blood backs up in the systemic veins (raised jugular venous pressure) while the left ventricle receives too little preload. Left ventricular output and blood pressure fall, yet the lungs stay clear because the left side is not congested. These patients depend on preload, so lowering it with venodilators can worsen the hypotension.","whyNot":["Acute mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture causes sudden pulmonary edema, so the lungs would not be clear. It also usually occurs a few days after MI, not in the first hours.","Massive left ventricular loss causes cardiogenic shock with pulmonary congestion and crackles. Her lungs are clear.","Correct.","Vasodilatory shock, as in sepsis, has a low or normal venous pressure. Her raised jugular venous pressure points to pump failure on the right."],"study":{"topics":["Right ventricular infarction","Preload dependence"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-B2","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C10-Q4","stem":"Within the first day after infarction, neutrophils begin to enter her necrotic myocardium. Which of the following most likely initiates this inflammatory response?","options":["Necrotic myocyte contents activating pattern recognition receptors","Cytotoxic T cells recognizing myocyte antigens shown on MHC class I","Antibodies to cardiac myosin forming immune complexes in the wall","IgE on mast cells binding cardiac proteins released from the infarct"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Necrosis releases cell contents that are normally hidden inside cells.","These molecules are danger signals recognized by innate immune receptors.","Innate immunity acts within hours, faster than any antibody or T-cell response."],"why":"Necrotic myocytes release damage-associated molecular patterns such as HMGB1, ATP, heat shock proteins, and mitochondrial DNA. These bind pattern recognition receptors, including Toll-like receptors and the NLRP3 inflammasome, on resident macrophages and endothelial cells. The cells release IL-1, TNF, and chemokines and display adhesion molecules, and complement is also activated. Neutrophils are recruited within hours and peak at about 1 to 3 days.","whyNot":["Correct.","A CD8 T-cell response needs priming and takes days. It does not explain neutrophil entry within hours.","Antibodies against heart antigens take weeks to form. They are linked to later immune complications such as post-MI (Dressler) pericarditis, not to early neutrophil entry.","IgE and mast cells drive immediate (type I) hypersensitivity to allergens. They do not start the response to necrotic tissue."],"study":{"topics":["Damage-associated molecular patterns","Acute inflammation after infarction"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-2","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C11","system":"CV","dx":"Post-myocardial infarction (Dressler) syndrome","codes":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-F3"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-day history of sharp chest pain that worsens with inspiration and when lying supine and improves when she sits up and leans forward. Three weeks ago, she had an anterior wall myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. A three-component friction rub is heard at the left sternal border. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 68 mm/h (reference below 30 mm/h), and leukocyte count is 11,800/µL (reference 4,500 to 11,000/µL). Echocardiography shows a small pericardial effusion and no new wall-motion abnormality. Diagnosis: post-myocardial infarction (Dressler) syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C11-Q1","stem":"Some patients develop pericarditis 1 to 3 days after a transmural infarct, but hers began about 3 weeks later. Which mechanism best explains her pericarditis?","options":["Fibrinous inflammation spreading from the necrotic wall to the overlying pericardium","An immune response to myocardial antigens exposed by the infarct","Coxsackievirus B infection picked up during her hospital stay","Bacterial seeding of the pericardium during the stent procedure"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.4","dok":3,"approach":["Use the timing: weeks after MI, not days.","A delay of weeks fits an adaptive immune response.","Necrosis exposes heart proteins the immune system does not normally see."],"why":"Dressler syndrome usually appears a few weeks after MI or heart surgery. Necrosis releases myocardial antigens, the immune system forms antibodies against them, and immune complexes are thought to deposit on the pericardium and pleura. The result is fever, pericarditis, sometimes pleuritis, and high inflammatory markers. The delay reflects the time needed for an adaptive immune response.","whyNot":["Early post-MI pericarditis comes from inflammation over a transmural infarct and appears in the first few days. Her onset at 3 weeks does not fit.","Correct.","Coxsackievirus B is a common cause of viral pericarditis and myocarditis. Nothing here links a new viral infection to her infarct, and the timing fits Dressler syndrome.","Purulent bacterial pericarditis is rare after a stent and causes a much sicker patient with a purulent effusion. Her small effusion and recent MI fit an immune cause."],"study":{"topics":["Dressler syndrome","Early versus late pericarditis after MI"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-F3","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C11-Q2","stem":"If the infarcted region of her anterior wall were examined now, 3 weeks after the infarct, which of the following microscopic findings would most likely be seen?","options":["Dense collagen scar that contains very few cells","Macrophages engulfing dead myocytes at the infarct edge","Maturing granulation tissue with increasing collagen","Coagulation necrosis with a dense band of neutrophils"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.5","dok":2,"approach":["Place 3 weeks on the healing timeline.","Neutrophils peak at about 1 to 3 days and macrophages at about 3 to 7 days.","Granulation tissue forms at about 7 to 14 days, and a dense scar forms by about 2 months."],"why":"Infarcts heal in a predictable order. Neutrophils peak at about 1 to 3 days, macrophages clear dead cells at about 3 to 7 days, and granulation tissue with new capillaries forms at about 7 to 14 days. From about 2 to 8 weeks, collagen steadily increases and cellularity falls. By about 2 months the area is a dense collagen scar, so at 3 weeks she has maturing granulation tissue.","whyNot":["A dense, cell-poor collagen scar is expected by about 2 months. At 3 weeks healing is still in progress.","Macrophage removal of dead myocytes peaks at about 3 to 7 days. This is also when the wall is softest and most likely to rupture.","Correct.","Coagulation necrosis with a neutrophil band is the picture at about 1 to 3 days."],"study":{"topics":["Histologic timeline of myocardial infarction","Repair by connective tissue"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-2"]}},{"id":"CV-C11-Q3","stem":"She also has pain along the ridge of her left trapezius muscle. Which of the following nerves carries pain from the parietal pericardium that is referred to this area?","options":["Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","Second intercostal nerve","Cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Trapezius ridge pain is referred to the C3 to C5 dermatomes.","Find the nerve with C3 to C5 roots that supplies the pericardium.","Separate pericardial pain from pain arising in the heart muscle itself."],"why":"The fibrous pericardium and the parietal layer of serous pericardium receive sensory fibers from the phrenic nerves (C3 to C5). Pain from inflamed parietal pericardium is referred to skin supplied by the same spinal segments, including the trapezius ridge and shoulder. Trapezius ridge pain is a classic clue to pericarditis. Myocardial ischemic pain travels with sympathetic fibers instead.","whyNot":["The vagus nerve carries some visceral sensation from the heart and lungs, mainly for reflexes, and does not cause referred pain to the trapezius.","Correct.","Intercostal nerves supply the chest wall and parietal pleura of the ribs, not the parietal pericardium. Their pain stays over the chest wall.","The cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers and the visceral pain fibers that run with them from the heart to spinal segments about T1 to T4. That ischemic pain is referred to the chest and inner arm, not the trapezius."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of the pericardium","Referred pain from the thorax"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F3"]}},{"id":"CV-C11-Q4","stem":"A new myocardial infarction must be excluded. Which of the following ECG findings most strongly supports pericarditis rather than a new ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","options":["Horizontal ST depression in V4 to V6 that resolves with rest","ST elevation in V1 to V4 with reciprocal depression in II, III, aVF","ST elevation across many territories with PR-segment depression","Convex ST elevation in II, III, and aVF with new Q waves there"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F3","obj":"CV-18-F.1","dok":3,"approach":["Pericarditis inflames the whole epicardial surface, not one artery's territory.","An MI produces ST elevation in one territory, often with reciprocal depression.","Inflamed atrial epicardium shifts the PR segment."],"why":"Pericarditis injures the epicardium over most of the heart, so concave ST elevation appears in leads from several coronary territories, usually without reciprocal ST depression except in aVR. Inflammation of the atrial epicardium creates an atrial injury current that depresses the PR segment. An ST-elevation MI follows the territory of one artery, often shows reciprocal depression, and may develop new Q waves. These differences let clinicians separate the two at the bedside.","whyNot":["Horizontal ST depression that resolves with rest fits exercise-induced subendocardial ischemia, as in stable angina.","ST elevation in V1 to V4 with reciprocal inferior depression follows the LAD territory. That pattern points to a new anterior infarct.","Correct.","Convex ST elevation limited to the inferior leads with new Q waves points to an RCA or circumflex infarct."],"study":{"topics":["ECG in pericarditis versus STEMI","Atrial injury current and PR depression"],"comps":["CV-18-F3","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C12","system":"CV","dx":"Cardiogenic shock from left ventricular pump failure after a large anterior myocardial infarction","codes":["CV-18-H5","CV-18-C3"],"case":"A 74-year-old man in the coronary care unit is evaluated 14 hours after a large anterior wall myocardial infarction. He is confused, and his skin is cool, pale, and clammy. Pulse is 122/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 78/52 mm Hg. Crackles are heard over both lung fields. Urine output has been 10 mL/h for 4 hours. Serum lactate is 6.1 mmol/L (reference 0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L), and creatinine has increased from 1.0 to 2.3 mg/dL (reference 0.7 to 1.3 mg/dL). A pulmonary artery catheter shows a cardiac index of 1.6 L/min/m² (reference 2.5 to 4.0 L/min/m²), a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure of 28 mm Hg (reference 6 to 12 mm Hg), and an increased systemic vascular resistance. Diagnosis: cardiogenic shock from left ventricular pump failure.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C12-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his cool, pale skin and high systemic vascular resistance?","options":["Nitric oxide released from activated endothelium in the skin","More carotid baroreceptor firing, which stimulates the vagus","Direct constriction of skin vessels by high lactate levels","Less baroreceptor firing, which raises sympathetic tone"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Low arterial pressure reduces stretch on the carotid sinus and aortic arch.","Less baroreceptor firing removes inhibition of the sympathetic outflow.","Sympathetic alpha-1 stimulation constricts skin arterioles."],"why":"When blood pressure falls, the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors fire less. The medullary centers respond by raising sympathetic output and lowering vagal output. Norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors constricts arterioles in the skin, kidneys, and gut, which raises systemic vascular resistance and makes the skin cool and pale, while sympathetic stimulation of sweat glands makes it clammy. The response protects brain and heart flow but adds afterload to a failing ventricle.","whyNot":["Nitric oxide dilates vessels. Widespread nitric oxide release causes the warm skin and low resistance of septic shock.","Increased baroreceptor firing happens when pressure rises, and it slows the heart and dilates vessels. His pressure is low.","Lactate and acidosis tend to dilate local vessels. They cannot explain the high systemic resistance.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Baroreceptor reflex","Hemodynamic profiles of shock"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C12-Q2","stem":"His lactate is 6.1 mmol/L. In poorly perfused tissues, which of the following best explains why converting pyruvate to lactate allow ATP production to continue?","options":["It makes ATP directly by substrate-level phosphorylation","It restores the NAD+ that glycolysis needs to continue","It feeds acetyl-CoA into the citric acid cycle without oxygen","It reoxidizes FADH2 so the electron transport chain can work"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.4","dok":2,"approach":["Without oxygen, the electron transport chain cannot reoxidize NADH.","Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step.","Lactate dehydrogenase turns NADH back into NAD+ as it reduces pyruvate."],"why":"Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase needs NAD+ to keep glycolysis running. Normally the NADH it forms is reoxidized by the mitochondria, but that requires oxygen. In hypoperfused tissue, lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and regenerates NAD+, so glycolysis can keep making its 2 ATP per glucose. Lactate builds up in the blood, and a high level is a marker of tissue hypoperfusion and a poor prognosis in shock.","whyNot":["The lactate dehydrogenase reaction makes no ATP. The ATP comes from earlier glycolytic steps (phosphoglycerate kinase and pyruvate kinase).","Correct.","Pyruvate enters the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA through pyruvate dehydrogenase. Making lactate diverts pyruvate away from that pathway.","FADH2 is reoxidized only by the electron transport chain, which needs oxygen. Lactate formation does not involve FAD."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis and lactate","NAD+ regeneration"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C12-Q3","stem":"His creatinine continues to rise. Urine dipstick is negative for blood, and urine microscopy shows many muddy brown granular casts. Which process is most likely occurring in his kidneys?","options":["Prerenal azotemia with intact tubules that reabsorb sodium avidly","Tubules blocked by myoglobin released from injured skeletal muscle","Immune complex deposition in the glomerular basement membrane","Ischemic injury of tubular epithelium from low renal blood flow"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H5","obj":"CV-18-H.8","dok":3,"approach":["Shock lowers renal perfusion.","Muddy brown granular casts are made of shed tubular epithelial cells.","A dipstick negative for blood argues against myoglobin."],"why":"In shock, low cardiac output and intense renal vasoconstriction reduce blood flow to the kidneys. Tubular epithelial cells, especially in the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb, have high energy needs and are injured first. Injured cells detach and form muddy brown granular casts, tubules become blocked, and glomerular filtration falls. This acute tubular necrosis is one of the main forms of organ damage in progressive shock.","whyNot":["Prerenal azotemia shows bland urine or hyaline casts. Muddy brown granular casts mean the tubular cells themselves are injured.","Myoglobin causes a dipstick that is positive for blood without red cells on microscopy. His dipstick is negative.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis causes blood and red cell casts in the urine. His dipstick is negative for blood.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acute tubular necrosis in shock","Stages of shock"],"comps":["CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C12-Q4","stem":"On day 2, his C-reactive protein is 160 mg/L (reference below 10 mg/L). Which of the following cytokines, released by macrophages in the infarct, is the main stimulus for hepatocytes to make this protein?","options":["Interleukin-6","Interleukin-10","Interleukin-4","Interleukin-2"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["C-reactive protein is an acute-phase protein made by the liver.","Identify the cytokine that most strongly drives acute-phase protein synthesis.","Rule out anti-inflammatory and lymphocyte growth cytokines."],"why":"C-reactive protein is an acute-phase reactant made by hepatocytes. Tissue injury, such as a large infarct, activates macrophages to release IL-1, TNF, and especially IL-6. IL-6 is the main stimulus for hepatocytes to make C-reactive protein and other acute-phase proteins such as fibrinogen and serum amyloid A. C-reactive protein binds damaged cells and microbes and activates complement.","whyNot":["Correct.","IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that suppresses macrophages. It does not drive acute-phase protein production.","IL-4 drives Th2 responses and IgE class switching. It does not stimulate C-reactive protein production.","IL-2 is a T-cell growth factor made mainly by activated T cells. It does not drive the acute-phase response."],"study":{"topics":["Acute-phase response","IL-6 and C-reactive protein"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C13","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic aortic regurgitation from aortic root dilation","codes":["CV-18-D1"],"case":"A 34-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue and a pounding sensation in his chest when he lies on his left side. He is 196 cm tall, and his arm span exceeds his height. Ophthalmologic examination 1 year ago showed bilateral upward subluxation of the lenses. His father died suddenly of an aortic dissection at age 41 years. Blood pressure is 152/48 mm Hg, and peripheral pulses are bounding. A high-pitched, early diastolic decrescendo murmur is heard best with the patient sitting up and leaning forward at end-expiration. Echocardiography shows an aortic root diameter of 5.1 cm (reference under about 4.0 cm), moderate to severe aortic regurgitation, and a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 58% (reference 55% to 70%). Diagnosis: chronic aortic regurgitation due to aortic root dilation.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C13-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing in this patient is most likely to show a mutation in the gene encoding which of the following proteins?","options":["Fibrillin-1, a glycoprotein of elastic fiber microfibrils","Cystathionine beta-synthase, an enzyme of homocysteine use","Elastin, the core protein of arterial elastic lamellae","Type III collagen, a fibrillar collagen of vessel walls"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.3","dok":2,"approach":["Group the clues: tall build, long arm span, upward lens displacement, aortic root dilation, father with dissection.","Recognize Marfan syndrome, autosomal dominant.","Recall the gene: FBN1 on chromosome 15, which encodes fibrillin-1."],"why":"This is Marfan syndrome. FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, which forms the microfibril scaffold that elastic fibers are built on. Defective fibrillin-1 weakens the aortic media (cystic medial degeneration) and the suspensory ligaments of the lens. Fibrillin-1 also normally holds TGF-beta in an inactive form, so loss of it increases TGF-beta signaling, which adds to aortic root enlargement.","whyNot":["Correct.","Homocystinuria (CBS deficiency) can give a tall build and lens dislocation, but the lenses usually shift downward, inheritance is autosomal recessive, and the main vascular problem is thrombosis.","Elastin (ELN) loss in Williams syndrome causes supravalvular aortic stenosis, not root dilation with lens displacement.","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (COL3A1) causes arterial and bowel rupture with thin, translucent skin. It does not cause tall stature or lens dislocation."],"study":{"topics":["Marfan syndrome genetics","Elastic fiber structure"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-I2"]}},{"id":"CV-C13-Q2","stem":"Which of the following changes in his left ventricular pressure-volume loop, compared with a normal loop, is most likely in this chronic, compensated state?","options":["Normal end-diastolic volume and smaller stroke volume, with a much higher peak systolic pressure","Larger end-diastolic and end-systolic volumes, with a smaller stroke volume","Larger end-diastolic volume and stroke volume, with a higher peak systolic pressure","Smaller end-diastolic volume and smaller stroke volume, with a normal peak pressure"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion: wide pulse pressure and early diastolic decrescendo murmur mean aortic regurgitation.","Ask which phase is lost: the leaky aortic valve lets blood enter the ventricle from the aorta as soon as ejection ends.","Add the volume load: regurgitant blood plus normal atrial filling raises end-diastolic volume and total stroke volume."],"why":"In aortic regurgitation, blood flows back into the left ventricle from the aorta during diastole, so the ventricle starts filling the moment ejection stops. Because the ventricle is never a closed chamber at that point, there is no true isovolumetric relaxation. The regurgitant volume adds to normal filling from the atrium, so end-diastolic volume rises, and the Frank-Starling mechanism produces a large total stroke volume. That large stroke volume raises systolic pressure, and the back-leak lowers diastolic pressure, which explains the 152/48 reading.","whyNot":["This is aortic stenosis: the ventricle must generate a much higher systolic pressure to push blood through a narrow valve, while stroke volume does not rise.","This is systolic heart failure, as in dilated cardiomyopathy. His ejection fraction of 58% shows that contraction is still preserved.","Correct.","This is mitral stenosis: a narrow mitral valve limits filling, so end-diastolic volume and stroke volume both fall."],"study":{"topics":["Pressure-volume loops in valve disease","Frank-Starling mechanism"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-4"]}},{"id":"CV-C13-Q3","stem":"His murmur is produced by blood flowing across one valve during part of the cardiac cycle. Which of the following best describes when this flow begins?","options":["At S1, when left ventricular pressure rises above left atrial pressure","At A2, when left ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure","After an opening snap, when left atrial pressure exceeds ventricular pressure","After an ejection click, when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Place the murmur in the cycle: early diastolic and decrescendo.","Name the valve: the aortic valve should be closed during diastole.","Link it to pressures: once ejection ends, aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure, and a leaky aortic valve lets blood flow back."],"why":"At the end of ejection, left ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure and the aortic valve closes, producing A2. In aortic regurgitation the cusps do not seal, so blood flows back from the high-pressure aorta into the relaxing ventricle starting at that moment. The gradient is largest early in diastole and narrows as the ventricle fills and aortic pressure falls, which is why the murmur is decrescendo.","whyNot":["This describes mitral regurgitation, a holosystolic murmur that starts with S1 and is heard best at the apex.","Correct.","This describes mitral stenosis, a low-pitched diastolic rumble that starts after the opening snap of a stiff mitral valve.","This describes forward flow through a stenotic aortic valve (often a bicuspid valve with an ejection click), which produces a systolic crescendo-decrescendo murmur."],"study":{"topics":["Timing of murmurs in the cardiac cycle","Wiggers diagram"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D1"]}},{"id":"CV-C13-Q4","stem":"His ventricle has handled the extra volume for years with a near-normal ejection fraction. Which of the following structural adaptations of the left ventricle best explains this?","options":["Concentric hypertrophy, with new sarcomeres added in parallel","Interstitial amyloid deposition with a stiff, thick wall","Eccentric hypertrophy, with new sarcomeres added in series","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with myocyte disarray"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D1","obj":"CV-18-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the load: aortic regurgitation causes volume overload.","Match load to remodeling: volume overload leads to eccentric hypertrophy, pressure overload leads to concentric hypertrophy.","Link structure to function: a larger cavity can eject a larger stroke volume at the same ejection fraction."],"why":"Chronic aortic regurgitation is a volume overload. Myocytes respond by adding sarcomeres in series, so the cells lengthen and the chamber enlarges, with a modest increase in wall thickness. The larger chamber holds the extra volume at a tolerable filling pressure and ejects a large stroke volume, so forward output is maintained for years. Over time, rising wall stress and fibrosis lead to systolic dysfunction and heart failure.","whyNot":["Concentric hypertrophy is the response to pressure overload, as in aortic stenosis or systemic hypertension. Wall thickness rises while the cavity stays normal or small.","Amyloid infiltration causes restrictive cardiomyopathy with a stiff, non-dilated ventricle.","Correct.","This is hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a genetic sarcomere disease, not an adaptation to valve leakage."],"study":{"topics":["Ventricular remodeling: volume versus pressure overload"],"comps":["CV-18-D1","CV-18-B1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C14","system":"CV","dx":"Rheumatic mitral stenosis","codes":["CV-18-D2"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman, gravida 1, para 0, at 26 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of worsening shortness of breath, two-pillow orthopnea, and cough with blood-streaked sputum. She grew up in a rural area with limited access to medical care. At age 10 years, she had an illness with fever and painful, swollen knees and ankles. Pulse is 108/min and regular. Cardiac examination shows a loud S1, an opening snap after S2, and a low-pitched diastolic rumble at the apex. Echocardiography shows thickened mitral leaflets with commissural fusion, a mitral valve area of 1.1 cm2 (reference 4 to 6 cm2), and an enlarged left atrium. Diagnosis: rheumatic mitral stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C14-Q1","stem":"The valve damage began years ago after a streptococcal pharyngitis. Which immune mechanism best explains how that infection injured her heart?","options":["Type III hypersensitivity, with immune complexes trapped in valve capillaries","Persistent bacterial infection of the valve leaflets with local complement activation","Type I hypersensitivity, with IgE-armed mast cells degranulating in the valve","Type II hypersensitivity, with antibodies that cross-react with heart proteins"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":2,"approach":["Link the history: fever and migrating large-joint arthritis in childhood suggest acute rheumatic fever.","Recall the cause: group A streptococcal pharyngitis followed by an immune reaction, not direct infection.","Classify it: antibodies against streptococcal antigens (M protein) bind similar host proteins, which is molecular mimicry and type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"Acute rheumatic fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 4 weeks. Antibodies made against streptococcal antigens, especially M protein, cross-react with host proteins in the heart such as cardiac myosin and valve components. This is molecular mimicry, classified as type II hypersensitivity, and T cells reacting to the same shared antigens add to the damage. Repeated inflammation heals by fibrosis, producing thickened leaflets with commissural fusion years later.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition is the mechanism of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, which follows the same organism but affects the kidney.","Rheumatic fever is a sterile, immune-mediated disease. Live bacteria growing on a valve describes infective endocarditis.","Type I reactions cause allergy and anaphylaxis within minutes of antigen exposure. They do not cause delayed valve scarring.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular mimicry","Hypersensitivity types"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D5"]}},{"id":"CV-C14-Q2","stem":"Before pregnancy, her resting pulse was 72/min and she had no symptoms. It is now 108/min. Which of the following best explains how this change in heart rate produced her symptoms?","options":["A faster rate increases backflow from the ventricle into the left atrium in systole","A faster rate weakens ventricular contraction and lowers forward stroke volume","A shorter systole raises aortic pressure and the afterload on the left ventricle","A shorter diastole raises the left atrial pressure needed to fill the ventricle"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-D2","obj":"CV-18-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion: a narrow mitral valve limits flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle.","Recall that tachycardia shortens diastole much more than systole.","Apply it: the same filling volume must cross the valve in less time, so the pressure gradient and left atrial pressure rise and back up into the lungs."],"why":"Flow across a stenotic mitral valve happens only in diastole and depends on the pressure gradient and the time available. As heart rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. To fill the ventricle in less time, the gradient across the valve must rise, so left atrial pressure climbs. That pressure passes back to the pulmonary veins and capillaries, causing dyspnea, orthopnea, and blood-streaked sputum. This is why pregnancy, fever, exercise, and atrial fibrillation with a fast rate often unmask mitral stenosis.","whyNot":["Systolic backflow is mitral regurgitation. In pure mitral stenosis the valve closes, so there is no systolic leak to increase.","Healthy myocardium contracts more strongly at faster rates (the force-frequency effect), and in mitral stenosis the left ventricle itself is normal. The problem is filling.","Heart rate does not raise afterload in this way, and afterload is not the limiting step in mitral stenosis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Diastolic filling time and heart rate","Hemodynamics of mitral stenosis"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C14-Q3","stem":"Months later she develops atrial fibrillation, and transesophageal echocardiography shows a thrombus in the left atrial appendage. This part of the left atrium develops from which embryonic structure?","options":["Septum primum","Primitive atrium","Right horn of the sinus venosus","Common pulmonary vein"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the typical site of thrombus in atrial fibrillation: the left atrial appendage.","Recall the two embryonic sources of the adult left atrium.","Match them: trabeculated appendage from the primitive atrium, smooth-walled part from the pulmonary veins."],"why":"The adult left atrium has two embryonic sources. The smooth-walled main portion forms when the common pulmonary vein and its branches are absorbed into the atrial wall. The original primitive atrium is left behind as the trabeculated, pouch-like appendage. Blood stagnates in this narrow, ridged pouch when the atrium no longer contracts, so it is the usual site of thrombus in atrial fibrillation and mitral stenosis.","whyNot":["The septum primum forms part of the interatrial septum (including the flap of the foramen ovale), not the appendage.","Correct.","The right sinus horn forms the smooth-walled part of the right atrium (sinus venarum), not any part of the left atrium.","The common pulmonary vein is absorbed into the left atrial wall to form its smooth-walled main part, not the trabeculated appendage."],"study":{"topics":["Development of the atria","Atrial fibrillation and thrombus"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C14-Q4","stem":"Her sputum contains macrophages filled with golden-brown granules that stain blue with Prussian blue. Which process best explains these cells?","options":["Peroxidation of organelle membranes inside the macrophages, leaving lipofuscin granules","Small bleeds from congested alveolar capillaries, with hemoglobin stored as hemosiderin","Bilirubin crystals formed in macrophages from hemolysis within the circulation","Inhaled carbon particles from air pollution taken up by alveolar macrophages"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B1","obj":"CV-18-B.1","dok":3,"approach":["Read the stain: Prussian blue positive means iron, which in tissue is hemosiderin.","Ask where the iron came from: red cells that leaked into alveoli.","Link to the lesion: mitral stenosis raises left atrial and pulmonary capillary pressure, causing chronic passive congestion and small bleeds."],"why":"In mitral stenosis, high left atrial pressure passes back to the pulmonary veins and capillaries, causing chronic passive congestion. Engorged capillaries leak red cells into the alveoli. Alveolar macrophages engulf these red cells and break hemoglobin down, storing the iron as hemosiderin, which is golden-brown and Prussian blue positive. These hemosiderin-laden macrophages are often called heart failure cells, and the same small bleeds explain her blood-streaked sputum.","whyNot":["Lipofuscin is a wear-and-tear pigment of aging cells. It is yellow-brown but does not stain with Prussian blue because it holds no iron.","Correct.","Bilirubin does not contain iron, so it is Prussian blue negative, and intravascular hemolysis does not load alveolar macrophages with pigment.","Anthracotic pigment is black and does not contain iron, so it does not stain with Prussian blue."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic passive congestion of the lung","Hemosiderin and other pigments"],"comps":["CV-18-D2","CV-18-B1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C15","system":"CV","dx":"Mitral valve prolapse","codes":["CV-18-D4"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of brief episodes of palpitations and occasional sharp chest pain unrelated to exertion. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Auscultation at the apex shows a midsystolic click followed by a short, late systolic murmur. She does not have tall stature, lens subluxation, or joint hypermobility. Echocardiography shows thickened, redundant mitral leaflets, with the posterior leaflet billowing more than 2 mm above the annulus into the left atrium during systole, and mild mitral regurgitation. Ejection fraction is 63% (reference 55% to 70%). Diagnosis: mitral valve prolapse.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C15-Q1","stem":"She is examined while squatting and again immediately after she stands up. Which of the following changes in her auscultatory findings is most likely on standing?","options":["Earlier click and longer murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume falls","Later click and shorter murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume rises","Earlier click and longer murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume rises","Later click and shorter murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume falls"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-D4","obj":"CV-18-D.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what standing does: blood pools in the legs, venous return and preload fall.","Link volume to prolapse: a smaller ventricle leaves the redundant leaflet and chordae relatively longer, so the leaflet prolapses earlier in systole.","Predict: earlier click, longer murmur."],"why":"Standing suddenly reduces venous return, so left ventricular end-diastolic volume falls. In a smaller ventricle the redundant leaflet and its chordae are relatively too long for the chamber, so the leaflet reaches its prolapse point earlier in systole. The click moves closer to S1 and the late systolic murmur lengthens. Squatting, which raises venous return and afterload, does the reverse. Valsalva strain acts like standing.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is the effect of squatting: more venous return and higher afterload enlarge the ventricle, so the click moves later. Standing does the opposite.","Standing lowers venous return, so ventricular volume falls rather than rises.","Smaller volume makes the prolapse happen sooner, so the click moves earlier, not later."],"study":{"topics":["Bedside maneuvers and murmurs","Preload and ventricular volume"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-4"]}},{"id":"CV-C15-Q2","stem":"The midsystolic click is produced when the prolapsing leaflet is suddenly checked during ventricular contraction. Sudden tensing of which structure produces the click?","options":["Pectinate muscles","Crista terminalis","Septomarginal trabecula","Chordae tendineae"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how the mitral valve is held shut in systole: papillary muscles contract and pull on chordae tendineae.","Apply it to prolapse: the redundant leaflet balloons back until its chordae snap taut.","Choose the structure that tenses at that moment."],"why":"During ventricular systole the papillary muscles contract and their chordae tendineae hold the mitral leaflets so they meet without everting into the left atrium. In mitral valve prolapse the leaflets and chordae are redundant, so the leaflet billows backward until the slack chordae suddenly tighten. That sudden tensing produces the midsystolic click. The murmur that follows is the mitral regurgitation that starts once the leaflets no longer meet.","whyNot":["Pectinate muscles are ridges in the atrial walls and appendages. They do not connect to the valve leaflets.","The crista terminalis is a ridge in the right atrium between its smooth and trabeculated parts, unrelated to the mitral valve.","The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is in the right ventricle and carries the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle. It is not attached to the mitral valve.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mitral valve apparatus","Heart sounds"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C15-Q3","stem":"A resected leaflet from a patient with the same disease shows a markedly expanded middle (spongiosa) layer. The material that expands this layer and draws water into it is best described as which of the following?","options":["Proteoglycans with glycosaminoglycan side chains","Hydroxyapatite crystals of calcium phosphate","Amyloid fibrils with a beta-pleated sheet structure","Cholesterol esters stored within foam cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-D4","obj":"CV-18-D.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the histology of mitral valve prolapse: myxomatous degeneration of the spongiosa.","Ask which matrix molecules hold water: glycosaminoglycans are highly negatively charged and attract water.","Pick proteoglycans, the core-protein molecules that carry glycosaminoglycan chains."],"why":"Mitral valve prolapse is caused by myxomatous degeneration. The spongiosa layer fills with proteoglycans, whose glycosaminoglycan side chains (such as chondroitin sulfate and dermatan sulfate) carry many negative charges and bind water. At the same time collagen and elastic fibers in the fibrosa become fragmented. The leaflets become thick, soft, and redundant, so they balloon into the atrium under systolic pressure.","whyNot":["Correct.","Calcium phosphate deposits make valve cusps rigid, as in calcific aortic stenosis.","Amyloid is a misfolded protein deposit that stiffens tissue. It is seen in restrictive cardiomyopathy, not floppy leaflets.","Lipid-laden foam cells are a feature of atherosclerotic plaques."],"study":{"topics":["Myxomatous degeneration","Proteoglycans and glycosaminoglycans"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-D4"]}},{"id":"CV-C15-Q4","stem":"Six years later she has had fever up to 38.4 °C (101.1 °F) and fatigue for three weeks. The fever began about two weeks after a tooth extraction. Blood cultures grow alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant, gram-positive cocci in chains. Which property of this organism best explains how it settles on her mitral valve?","options":["IgA protease that cleaves secretory antibody at mucosal surfaces","M protein that blocks complement deposition and neutrophil phagocytosis","Dextran that binds fibrin-platelet deposits on the damaged leaflet","Coagulase that converts fibrinogen to fibrin around the bacteria"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the organism: alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant streptococci after dental work are viridans group streptococci.","Recall the setting: subacute endocarditis on a previously abnormal valve.","Link virulence to site: dextran lets the bacteria stick to fibrin-platelet deposits on the damaged leaflet."],"why":"Viridans group streptococci live in the mouth and enter the blood during dental procedures. An abnormal, regurgitant valve develops small sterile fibrin-platelet deposits where flow is turbulent. Strains such as Streptococcus sanguinis make dextran, an extracellular polysaccharide that binds these fibrin-platelet deposits, so the bacteria adhere and grow into vegetations. The course is subacute, with weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue.","whyNot":["IgA protease helps Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria, and Haemophilus colonize mucosa. Pneumococci are optochin sensitive.","M protein is the main virulence factor of group A streptococci, which are beta-hemolytic and rarely cause endocarditis.","Correct.","Coagulase is the marker of Staphylococcus aureus, a gram-positive coccus in clusters that causes acute endocarditis, often on normal valves."],"study":{"topics":["Viridans streptococci","Subacute infective endocarditis"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C16","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease with mitral stenosis and aortic regurgitation","codes":["CV-18-D5"],"case":"A 52-year-old man is referred to a cardiac surgeon because of worsening exertional dyspnea and ankle swelling. He had acute rheumatic fever at age 9 years and a recurrence at age 14 years after another episode of pharyngitis. Pulse is 84/min and irregularly irregular. Cardiac examination shows a low-pitched diastolic rumble at the apex, a soft early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border, a loud P2, and a left parasternal heave. Echocardiography shows thickened, restricted mitral leaflets with a valve area of 1.3 cm2 (reference 4 to 6 cm2), mild aortic regurgitation, right ventricular hypertrophy, and an estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 58 mm Hg (reference 15 to 30 mm Hg). Diagnosis: chronic rheumatic heart disease.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C16-Q1","stem":"He undergoes mitral valve replacement. Which of the following gross findings is most likely in the excised mitral valve?","options":["Thick leaflets, fused commissures, and short, thick, fused chordae","Bulky, friable vegetations that have eroded through the leaflets","Thin, ballooning leaflets with long, thin, stretched chordae","Calcium nodules at the leaflet bases with free, unfused commissures"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D5","obj":"CV-18-D.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that chronic rheumatic disease follows repeated inflammation that heals by fibrosis.","Picture where scarring occurs: leaflet edges and commissures fuse, and chordae shorten and fuse.","Match the gross picture: a narrow, fish-mouth orifice."],"why":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease is the scarred end stage of repeated bouts of valvulitis. Fibrosis thickens the leaflets, fuses them at the commissures, and shortens, thickens, and fuses the chordae tendineae. The result is a rigid, funnel-shaped mitral orifice often called a fish-mouth or buttonhole deformity. The mitral valve is involved most often, followed by the aortic valve, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Correct.","Destructive vegetations are typical of acute infective endocarditis, often caused by Staphylococcus aureus.","Ballooning leaflets with elongated chordae describe myxomatous mitral valve prolapse.","Calcium at the base with free commissures describes degenerative calcification, such as mitral annular calcification or calcific aortic stenosis."],"study":{"topics":["Gross pathology of rheumatic valve disease"],"comps":["CV-18-D5","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C16-Q2","stem":"His second attack at 14 followed a new streptococcal pharyngitis and added to the damage from the first. Which feature of adaptive immunity best explains why a later infection can quickly re-trigger the cross-reactive response?","options":["Plasma cells switch from IgG back to IgM production to respond faster on re-exposure","Memory B and T cells formed in the first attack respond rapidly on re-exposure","Mast cells coated with IgE in the first attack degranulate on re-exposure","Natural killer cells recognize streptococcal peptides presented on MHC class I"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that rheumatic fever is an adaptive, antigen-specific response that cross-reacts with heart proteins.","Apply the principle of immunologic memory: the first response leaves memory B and T cells.","On a new infection those cells respond faster and more strongly, so recurrences add cumulative damage."],"why":"The first episode of rheumatic fever creates long-lived memory B and T cells specific for streptococcal antigens that also cross-react with heart proteins. A later group A streptococcal pharyngitis reactivates these cells, so the secondary response is faster and larger. Each recurrence adds new valve inflammation, and the scarring is cumulative. This is why patients with a history of rheumatic fever are given long-term antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent new streptococcal infections.","whyNot":["Class switching goes from IgM to IgG, IgA, or IgE and cannot be reversed. A secondary response is mainly IgG.","Correct.","IgE-coated mast cells mediate type I hypersensitivity, which is not the mechanism of rheumatic fever.","Natural killer cells do not recognize specific peptides on MHC class I. They kill cells that have lost MHC class I or show stress signals."],"study":{"topics":["Primary versus secondary immune response","Recurrent rheumatic fever"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D5"]}},{"id":"CV-C16-Q3","stem":"A throat culture from his original pharyngitis would most likely have shown which laboratory profile?","options":["Alpha-hemolytic, optochin-sensitive, bile-soluble gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci","Beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-resistant, CAMP-positive gram-positive cocci in chains","Catalase-positive, coagulase-positive gram-positive cocci in grape-like clusters","Beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-sensitive, PYR-positive gram-positive cocci in chains"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.6","dok":2,"approach":["Name the trigger of rheumatic fever: Streptococcus pyogenes (group A) pharyngitis.","Recall its identifying tests: beta-hemolysis, bacitracin sensitivity, and PYR positivity.","Rule out the other streptococci and staphylococci by their own tests."],"why":"Rheumatic fever follows pharyngitis caused by Streptococcus pyogenes, the group A streptococcus. In the lab it grows as gram-positive cocci in chains that are catalase negative, produce complete (beta) hemolysis on blood agar, are inhibited by bacitracin, and are PYR positive. Skin infections with group A strains can cause poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis but are not the classic trigger of rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["This is Streptococcus pneumoniae, which causes pneumonia, otitis media, and meningitis.","This is Streptococcus agalactiae (group B), a cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis, not rheumatic fever.","This is Staphylococcus aureus. It does not trigger rheumatic fever.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Streptococcus identification","Group A streptococcus"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C16-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sequences best explains his increased pulmonary artery pressure and right ventricular hypertrophy?","options":["Recurrent thromboemboli from leg veins lodge in and gradually obstruct the pulmonary arteries","A left-to-right shunt delivers excess blood flow into the pulmonary circulation","High left atrial pressure backs up into the lungs, then pulmonary arterioles constrict and remodel","Chronic alveolar hypoxia from obstructive lung disease constricts and then remodels pulmonary arterioles"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-A1","obj":"CV-18-A.5","dok":3,"approach":["Start upstream at the obstruction: mitral stenosis raises left atrial pressure.","Follow the pressure back: pulmonary veins, capillaries, and then pulmonary arteries.","Add the arterial response and its result: reactive constriction and remodeling raise the right ventricle's afterload, causing hypertrophy, a loud P2, and a parasternal heave."],"why":"Mitral stenosis keeps left atrial pressure high. With no valves in the pulmonary veins, that pressure passes straight back to the pulmonary capillaries. The pulmonary arteries must then run at a higher pressure to keep blood moving forward, and over time the arterioles constrict and their walls thicken, raising pulmonary artery pressure further. The right ventricle faces this higher afterload and develops concentric hypertrophy, which produces the parasternal heave, while the loud P2 reflects forceful closure of the pulmonic valve.","whyNot":["Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension needs a history of clots. His valve disease already explains the finding.","A shunt such as an atrial or ventricular septal defect causes pulmonary hypertension from excess flow. He has no shunt.","Correct.","Hypoxic vasoconstriction from lung disease is a cause of pulmonary hypertension, but nothing in his case suggests chronic lung disease."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease","Right ventricular response to afterload"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-A1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C17","system":"CV","dx":"Carcinoid heart disease","codes":["CV-18-D6"],"case":"A 61-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital because of a 1-year history of episodic facial flushing, watery diarrhea, and wheezing, and more recent leg swelling and abdominal fullness. Imaging shows a small tumor in the terminal ileum and multiple liver metastases. Her 24-hour urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA) is 180 mg/24 h (reference about 2 to 9 mg/24 h). Physical examination shows jugular venous distention and a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Echocardiography shows thickened, retracted, poorly mobile tricuspid leaflets with severe tricuspid regurgitation, a thickened pulmonic valve, and normal mitral and aortic valves. Diagnosis: carcinoid heart disease from a metastatic ileal neuroendocrine tumor.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C17-Q1","stem":"She also has a scaly rash on sun-exposed skin of her hands and neck and has become forgetful. Reduced synthesis of which cofactor best explains these new findings?","options":["Tetrahydrobiopterin, made from guanosine triphosphate","FAD and FMN, made from riboflavin","Thiamine pyrophosphate, made from thiamine","NAD+ and NADP+, made in part from tryptophan"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-5","obj":"IN-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the new findings as pellagra: dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, diarrhea, and dementia.","Link to the tumor: carcinoid cells convert a large share of dietary tryptophan into serotonin.","Recall that tryptophan is also a precursor for niacin, which forms NAD+ and NADP+."],"why":"Tryptophan normally supplies part of the body's niacin, which is built into NAD+ and NADP+. A carcinoid tumor diverts a much larger share of tryptophan than normal into serotonin, so less is left for niacin synthesis. The result can be pellagra: dermatitis in sun-exposed areas, diarrhea, and dementia. Her diarrhea may come from both serotonin excess and pellagra.","whyNot":["Tetrahydrobiopterin is a cofactor for tryptophan hydroxylase, but it is made from GTP, and its deficiency causes a form of hyperphenylalaninemia with neurologic signs rather than a photosensitive rash.","Riboflavin deficiency causes cheilosis, angular stomatitis, and glossitis. Riboflavin is not made from tryptophan.","Thiamine deficiency causes beriberi and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, not a photosensitive dermatitis, and thiamine is not made from tryptophan.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tryptophan metabolism","Niacin and pellagra"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-D6"]}},{"id":"CV-C17-Q2","stem":"An ileal carcinoid often causes no systemic symptoms until it has spread to the liver. Which venous route best explains why her symptoms appeared only after liver metastases formed?","options":["Left gastric vein draining into the portal vein","Superior mesenteric vein draining into the portal vein","Hepatic veins draining into the inferior vena cava","Inferior mesenteric vein draining into the splenic vein"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["Trace venous drainage of the ileum: superior mesenteric vein to portal vein to liver.","Recall that the liver inactivates serotonin (by monoamine oxidase) on this first pass.","Ask how liver metastases differ: they release products into the hepatic veins, which drain straight into the inferior vena cava and right heart."],"why":"Blood from the ileum travels through the superior mesenteric and portal veins to the liver, which breaks down serotonin and other vasoactive products before they reach the systemic circulation. Liver metastases secrete these products downstream of that filter, into the hepatic veins, which empty directly into the inferior vena cava. The products reach the right heart in high concentration, causing flushing, diarrhea, bronchospasm, and right-sided valve damage. Primary tumors outside the portal drainage, such as bronchial or ovarian carcinoids, can cause the syndrome without liver spread.","whyNot":["The left gastric vein drains the stomach into the portal vein, so its blood is filtered by the liver as well.","The ileum drains through the superior mesenteric vein into the portal vein, so serotonin from the primary tumor passes through the liver and is inactivated there.","Correct.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains the distal colon and reaches the portal system through the splenic vein, so its blood also passes through the liver."],"study":{"topics":["Portal and systemic venous drainage","First-pass hepatic metabolism"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-D6"]}},{"id":"CV-C17-Q3","stem":"A biopsy of the thickened tricuspid tissue would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Surface plaques of smooth muscle cells in a mucopolysaccharide-rich matrix","Small, sterile fibrin-platelet vegetations along the lines of leaflet closure","Granulomas with plump macrophages, giant cells, and foci of fibrinoid necrosis","Ballooning leaflets with an expanded spongiosa and fragmented collagen fibers"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D6","obj":"CV-18-D.9","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion: right-sided valve thickening and retraction in a patient with carcinoid syndrome.","Recall that carcinoid plaques are fibrous deposits laid on top of the valve and endocardium.","Match the histology: smooth muscle cells and myofibroblasts in a matrix rich in mucopolysaccharides."],"why":"Carcinoid heart disease produces plaque-like fibrous thickenings on the endocardium of the right heart chambers and on the tricuspid and pulmonic valves. They are made of smooth muscle cells and myofibroblasts in a matrix rich in acid mucopolysaccharides, laid on top of the existing valve. Serotonin and related products are thought to drive this fibrosis. The plaques stiffen and retract the leaflets, which typically causes tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonic stenosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Small sterile vegetations describe nonbacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis, seen with hypercoagulable states such as advanced cancer, mainly on left-sided valves.","Granulomas with activated macrophages (Anitschkow cells) and giant cells are Aschoff bodies of acute rheumatic carditis.","An expanded spongiosa in floppy leaflets describes myxomatous degeneration in mitral valve prolapse."],"study":{"topics":["Carcinoid heart disease pathology"],"comps":["CV-18-D6","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C17-Q4","stem":"Her mitral and aortic valves are normal even though the tumor products are reaching her right heart in high amounts. Which enzyme and location best explain why the left-sided valves are spared?","options":["Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase in the lung","Catechol-O-methyltransferase in the left atrial wall","Tryptophan hydroxylase in the alveolar epithelium","Monoamine oxidase in the pulmonary vascular bed"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: right-sided lesions with left-sided sparing.","Ask what lies between the right and left heart: the pulmonary circulation.","Recall that monoamine oxidase in the lung vascular bed breaks serotonin down to 5-HIAA before blood reaches the left heart."],"why":"Blood from the right heart passes through the lungs before reaching the left heart. The pulmonary vascular bed takes up serotonin and breaks it down with monoamine oxidase to 5-HIAA, so much less reaches the mitral and aortic valves. Left-sided lesions do occur when this filter is bypassed, such as with a patent foramen ovale and right-to-left shunting, a primary bronchial carcinoid, or very high hormone levels.","whyNot":["Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase makes serotonin from 5-hydroxytryptophan, so it would add serotonin rather than remove it.","COMT mainly breaks down catecholamines such as epinephrine and dopamine, not serotonin, and the left atrial wall is not a major site of serotonin clearance.","Tryptophan hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme for serotonin synthesis, so it would also add serotonin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Serotonin metabolism","Lung as a metabolic filter"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-15"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C18","system":"CV","dx":"Calcific aortic stenosis","codes":["CV-18-D1"],"case":"A 79-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after he lost consciousness while climbing two flights of stairs. For 6 months, he has had chest pressure and shortness of breath with exertion. Blood pressure is 118/84 mm Hg. A harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur is heard at the right upper sternal border and radiates to both carotid arteries. S2 is soft and single, and the carotid upstroke is weak and delayed. Echocardiography shows a three-cusp aortic valve with heavily calcified, poorly mobile cusps, an aortic valve area of 0.7 cm2 (reference about 3 to 4 cm2), a mean gradient of 48 mm Hg, concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, and an ejection fraction of 60% (reference 55% to 70%). Serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference 8.5 to 10.2 mg/dL). Diagnosis: severe calcific aortic stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C18-Q1","stem":"Which process best explains the calcium deposits in his aortic valve?","options":["Metastatic calcification of normal tissue due to raised serum calcium and phosphate","Dystrophic calcification of damaged valve tissue despite normal serum calcium","Fibrous fusion of the commissures from earlier rheumatic valve inflammation","Myxomatous expansion of the spongiosa with proteoglycan accumulation"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D1","obj":"CV-18-D.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the normal serum calcium.","Recall the two types of pathologic calcification: dystrophic (damaged tissue, normal calcium) and metastatic (normal tissue, high calcium).","Link to age-related wear on a three-cusp aortic valve: dystrophic."],"why":"Calcific aortic stenosis of older adults results from years of mechanical stress and low-grade injury to the cusps, with lipid deposition, inflammation, and calcification that resembles bone formation. Because the calcium is laid down in damaged tissue while serum calcium is normal, it is dystrophic calcification. The calcium masses form in the cusps from the aortic side and stiffen them, typically without fusing the commissures.","whyNot":["Metastatic calcification needs high serum calcium or phosphate, as in hyperparathyroidism or kidney failure. His calcium is normal.","Correct.","Rheumatic aortic stenosis shows commissural fusion and almost always involves the mitral valve too. His cusps are calcified with no mention of fusion or mitral disease.","Myxomatous change makes leaflets soft and floppy, causing prolapse and regurgitation rather than stenosis."],"study":{"topics":["Dystrophic versus metastatic calcification","Calcific aortic stenosis"],"comps":["CV-18-D1","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C18-Q2","stem":"The cusps of the diseased valve developed from which of the following embryonic structures?","options":["Endocardial cushion swellings of the truncus arteriosus","Atrioventricular endocardial cushions surrounding the atrioventricular canal","Septum primum tissue at the upper border of the primitive atrium","Mesenchyme of the right and left horns of the sinus venosus"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the valve from the murmur: systolic ejection murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids means the aortic valve.","Recall that the aortic and pulmonic (semilunar) valves form in the outflow tract.","Pick the outflow tract cushions, which also receive neural crest cells."],"why":"The aortic and pulmonic valves develop from endocardial cushion tissue in the outflow tract (truncal swellings), with a contribution from cardiac neural crest cells. As the outflow tract divides into the aorta and pulmonary trunk, small swellings on the cushions are hollowed out to form three cusps for each valve. Abnormal formation or fusion of these swellings produces a bicuspid aortic valve, which calcifies and becomes stenotic at a younger age than a three-cusp valve.","whyNot":["Correct.","The atrioventricular cushions give rise to tissue of the mitral and tricuspid valves and the atrioventricular septum, not the semilunar valves.","The septum primum forms part of the interatrial septum and the flap of the foramen ovale.","The sinus venosus forms the smooth part of the right atrium, the coronary sinus, and part of the sinoatrial region."],"study":{"topics":["Development of the semilunar valves","Bicuspid aortic valve"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G1"]}},{"id":"CV-C18-Q3","stem":"Two days later, he develops atrial fibrillation. His shortness of breath worsens and his blood pressure falls abruptly. Which of the following best explains why loss of atrial contraction harms him more than it would harm a person with a normal heart?","options":["Losing the atrial contraction raises the gradient across his narrow aortic valve directly","His stiff, thick ventricle relies on atrial contraction for a larger share of filling","Atrial fibrillation removes the main source of coronary blood flow to his thick ventricle","Atrial fibrillation lengthens diastole and lets too much blood fill his thick ventricle"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the remodeling: aortic stenosis causes pressure overload and concentric hypertrophy.","Recall the cost: a thick, stiff ventricle relaxes and fills poorly in early diastole.","Apply it: atrial contraction supplies a larger share of filling, so its loss drops preload and stroke volume sharply."],"why":"In a normal heart, most ventricular filling happens passively in early diastole, and atrial contraction adds a smaller share at the end. A concentrically hypertrophied ventricle from aortic stenosis is stiff and relaxes slowly, so passive filling falls and atrial contraction supplies a much larger part of the end-diastolic volume. When atrial fibrillation removes that contraction, and the fast rate also shortens diastole, preload and stroke volume drop. Pressure backs up into the lungs and cardiac output falls.","whyNot":["The atrium sits upstream of the mitral valve. Losing its contraction lowers ventricular filling and stroke volume, so the aortic gradient tends to fall rather than rise.","Correct.","Coronary flow is driven by aortic pressure during diastole. Atrial contraction does not supply coronary blood flow.","Atrial fibrillation usually brings a fast ventricular rate, which shortens diastole and reduces filling, the opposite of this option."],"study":{"topics":["Atrial contribution to ventricular filling","Diastolic dysfunction"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C18-Q4","stem":"Over the past year he has had recurrent melena from bleeding gastrointestinal angiodysplasia. He had no unusual bleeding earlier in life, including after a tooth extraction at age 60. Testing now shows loss of the largest von Willebrand factor (vWF) multimers. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this change?","options":["Autoantibodies against ADAMTS13 block normal cleavage of the largest vWF multimers","Congestive liver injury reduces hepatic synthesis and secretion of vWF","High shear stress across the narrow valve unfolds vWF so ADAMTS13 cleaves it","A germline VWF mutation prevents normal assembly of multimers in endothelial cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-9-B","obj":"HE-9-B.9","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: no bleeding problems until recently, so the defect is acquired.","Link it to the valve: blood jets through a narrow aortic valve at high shear.","Recall that shear unfolds large vWF multimers and exposes the site cleaved by the protease ADAMTS13."],"why":"Von Willebrand factor circulates as multimers, and the largest ones are the most effective at binding platelets to injured vessel walls. High shear stress across a stenotic aortic valve stretches these large multimers open and exposes the site that the plasma protease ADAMTS13 cleaves. The largest multimers are lost, which resembles type 2A von Willebrand disease. Together with bleeding from angiodysplasia this is called Heyde syndrome, and it often improves after the valve is replaced.","whyNot":["Antibodies that block ADAMTS13 cause acquired thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, in which very large vWF multimers build up, the opposite of this finding.","vWF is made by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes, not the liver.","Correct.","An inherited defect usually causes bleeding from early life. His normal bleeding history until recently points to an acquired cause."],"study":{"topics":["Von Willebrand factor structure and function","Acquired von Willebrand syndrome"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-D1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C19","system":"CV","dx":"Subacute infective endocarditis (viridans group streptococci) on a rheumatic mitral valve","codes":["CV-18-D3","CV-18-K1"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman with mitral regurgitation from childhood rheumatic fever comes to the physician because of low-grade fevers, night sweats, fatigue, and a 4-kg weight loss over 5 weeks. Two molars were extracted 7 weeks ago. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F). Physical examination shows splinter hemorrhages, tender nodules on the pads of two fingers, and a palpable spleen tip. A holosystolic murmur that radiates to the left axilla is louder than it was last year. Hemoglobin is 10.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), and MCV is 84 fL (80 to 100). Three blood cultures grow alpha-hemolytic, gram-positive cocci in chains that are optochin resistant and bile insoluble. Echocardiography shows a 9-mm vegetation on the atrial surface of the anterior mitral leaflet. Diagnosis: subacute infective endocarditis caused by viridans group streptococci.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C19-Q1","stem":"The organism adheres to fibrin-platelet deposits on the scarred leaflet by means of an extracellular polysaccharide. Which of the following dietary sugars is the substrate for synthesis of this polysaccharide?","options":["Sucrose","Galactose","Lactose","Maltose"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant, bile-insoluble streptococci are viridans streptococci.","Recall their adhesin: dextran (a glucose polymer) that binds fibrin-platelet deposits on damaged valves.","Name the substrate: glucosyltransferase builds dextran from sucrose."],"why":"Viridans streptococci such as S. mutans and S. sanguinis carry glucosyltransferase (dextransucrase). This enzyme splits sucrose, links the glucose units into dextran, and releases fructose. Dextran helps the bacteria stick to tooth surfaces and to the sterile fibrin-platelet deposits that form on valves scarred by rheumatic disease. Bacteremia after dental work lets them seed those deposits.","whyNot":["Correct.","Galactose is a monosaccharide from lactose. It is not used to build dextran, which is a glucose polymer.","Lactose is a glucose-galactose disaccharide from milk. Many bacteria ferment it, but it is not the substrate for dextran synthesis.","Maltose is a glucose-glucose disaccharide from starch breakdown. Glucosyltransferase does not use it to build dextran."],"study":{"topics":["Viridans streptococci virulence","Dextran and glucosyltransferase"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C19-Q2","stem":"The mitral valve lies posterior to the left half of the sternum at the level of the fourth costal cartilage. At which of the following sites is her murmur most likely to be heard best?","options":["Second right intercostal space at the sternal border","Fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line","Fourth left intercostal space at the lower sternal border","Second left intercostal space at the sternal border"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Link the murmur to the valve: a holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla in this case is mitral regurgitation.","Separate the valve's position from its listening area: sound travels with blood flow toward the left ventricle.","Pick the mitral area: the cardiac apex, fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line."],"why":"Each valve is heard best downstream of it, in the direction its blood flows. Mitral sounds travel toward the left ventricular apex, so the mitral area is the fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line. Mitral regurgitation is holosystolic, loudest at the apex, and radiates to the left axilla. A vegetation that erodes a leaflet or chordae makes the regurgitation and murmur worse.","whyNot":["This is the aortic area. Aortic stenosis would be heard best here, with radiation to the carotids.","Correct.","This is the tricuspid area. Tricuspid regurgitation, common in endocarditis in people who inject drugs, is heard best here.","This is the pulmonic area. Pulmonic stenosis or a split S2 is heard best here."],"study":{"topics":["Auscultation areas of the heart valves","Mitral regurgitation murmur"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C19-Q3","stem":"Urinalysis shows red blood cells, red blood cell casts, and 1+ protein. Serum C3 concentration is 58 mg/dL (90 to 180). Which of the following mechanisms best explains her renal findings?","options":["Septic emboli causing wedge-shaped infarcts in the renal cortex","Antibodies binding type IV collagen in the glomerular basement membrane","Immune complexes deposited in glomeruli, activating complement","Bacteria invading the renal tubules and interstitium from the blood"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.2","dok":3,"approach":["Read red cell casts as glomerular bleeding, which means glomerulonephritis.","Read the low C3 as complement being consumed.","Match a long-standing infection with low complement and glomerulonephritis to immune complex deposition (type III hypersensitivity)."],"why":"Weeks of bacteremia produce antibodies that bind circulating bacterial antigens. These immune complexes lodge in the glomeruli and activate complement, which lowers serum C3 and recruits inflammatory cells. The result is a glomerulonephritis with red cell casts, a type III hypersensitivity reaction.","whyNot":["Renal infarcts from emboli cause flank pain and hematuria, but they do not produce red cell casts or low C3.","Anti-GBM disease is type II hypersensitivity. It often affects the lungs as well, and serum complement is usually normal.","Correct.","Bacterial invasion of the tubules and interstitium is pyelonephritis, which produces white cell casts."],"study":{"topics":["Type III hypersensitivity","Glomerulonephritis in endocarditis"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C19-Q4","stem":"Additional laboratory studies show serum iron 35 µg/dL (60 to 170), total iron-binding capacity 210 µg/dL (250 to 450), and ferritin 380 ng/mL (12 to 150). Which of the following mechanisms best explains her anemia?","options":["Chronic blood loss that has depleted total body iron stores","Hepcidin release driven by IL-6 that causes ferroportin breakdown","Antibody-mediated destruction of circulating red blood cells","Vitamin B12 deficiency slowing DNA synthesis in red cell precursors"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-9-A1","obj":"HE-9-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Read the pattern: low iron, low TIBC, and high ferritin with a normal MCV.","Recognize anemia of chronic disease (anemia of inflammation) from a long infection.","Name the mechanism: IL-6 raises hepcidin, which makes cells internalize and degrade ferroportin, trapping iron in macrophages and enterocytes."],"why":"Ongoing infection raises IL-6, which makes the liver release hepcidin. Hepcidin binds ferroportin, the iron exporter on enterocytes and macrophages, and causes it to be taken in and degraded. Iron stays locked in storage, so ferritin is high while serum iron falls. The liver also makes less transferrin, so TIBC is low. Red cell production is limited by the iron supply.","whyNot":["Iron deficiency from blood loss gives low ferritin and high TIBC, the opposite of this pattern.","Correct.","Immune hemolysis would raise reticulocytes, LDH, and indirect bilirubin. It does not trap iron in stores.","B12 deficiency gives a macrocytic anemia with a high MCV. Her MCV is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Anemia of chronic disease","Hepcidin and ferroportin"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C20","system":"CV","dx":"Acute bacterial endocarditis of the aortic valve (Staphylococcus aureus)","codes":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-D3"],"case":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus and an infected foot ulcer is brought to the emergency department because of 4 days of fever, rigors, and shortness of breath. An echocardiogram 1 year ago showed normal valves. Temperature is 39.7 °C (103.5 °F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 104/52 mm Hg. Physical examination shows painless erythematous macules on both soles and a new early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border. Serum lactate is 4.6 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2). Blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase positive and coagulase positive. Echocardiography shows a 14-mm vegetation on the aortic valve with a perforated leaflet and moderate to severe regurgitation. Diagnosis: acute bacterial endocarditis caused by Staphylococcus aureus.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C20-Q1","stem":"His new murmur begins immediately after S2 and decreases in intensity through diastole. Which of the following pressure relationships best explains the timing and shape of this murmur?","options":["Atrium-to-ventricle gradient is highest at the end of diastole","Ventricle-to-atrium gradient stays high throughout all of systole","Ventricle-to-aorta gradient is highest in mid systole","Aorta-to-ventricle gradient is highest just after S2, then falls"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.7","dok":3,"approach":["Connect the early diastolic decrescendo murmur and the perforated aortic leaflet to aortic regurgitation.","Ask when the leak is driven hardest: right after the aortic valve closes, when aortic pressure is far above left ventricular pressure.","Follow the gradient through diastole: aortic pressure falls and ventricular pressure rises as the ventricle fills, so flow and loudness fade."],"why":"A murmur is loudest when the pressure gradient driving the abnormal flow is largest. In aortic regurgitation, backflow begins as soon as the aortic valve closes (S2), when aortic pressure is far above left ventricular pressure. Through diastole the aorta drains backward and the ventricle fills, so the gradient narrows and the murmur fades. This produces a high-pitched, blowing, early diastolic decrescendo murmur.","whyNot":["A gradient that peaks at the end of diastole fits mitral stenosis, where atrial contraction causes presystolic accentuation.","A gradient that stays high through all of systole fits mitral regurgitation, a holosystolic murmur.","A gradient that peaks in mid systole fits aortic stenosis, a crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Aortic regurgitation murmur","Pressure gradients in the cardiac cycle"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D1"]}},{"id":"CV-C20-Q2","stem":"Susceptibility testing shows that the isolate grows in the presence of oxacillin and cefoxitin. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this resistance?","options":["D-Ala-D-Lac replacing D-Ala-D-Ala in cell wall precursors","Methylation of the binding site on 23S ribosomal RNA","A mecA-encoded penicillin-binding protein with low drug affinity","Hydrolysis of the beta-lactam ring by a plasmid-encoded penicillinase"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-12","obj":"IN-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize oxacillin and cefoxitin resistance in S. aureus as methicillin resistance (MRSA).","Recall that oxacillin resists staphylococcal penicillinase, so penicillinase cannot explain it.","Name the mechanism: mecA codes for PBP2a, which keeps building cell wall when beta-lactams are present."],"why":"Methicillin-resistant S. aureus carries the mecA gene on the SCCmec element. mecA encodes PBP2a, a transpeptidase that binds beta-lactams poorly, so cell wall cross-linking continues. Laboratories use cefoxitin or oxacillin to detect it. The plain penicillinase most S. aureus strains make cannot break down oxacillin.","whyNot":["This is the vancomycin resistance mechanism of enterococci (vanA). It does not affect beta-lactams.","This (erm) methylation causes resistance to macrolides and clindamycin.","Correct.","Most S. aureus make penicillinase, which explains resistance to penicillin G. Oxacillin and nafcillin are built to resist it."],"study":{"topics":["MRSA and mecA","Beta-lactam resistance mechanisms"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K1"]}},{"id":"CV-C20-Q3","stem":"His elevated serum lactate reflects poor tissue perfusion. Which of the following best describes the reaction that produces lactate in hypoxic cells and its purpose?","options":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA for the citric acid cycle","Alanine aminotransferase converting pyruvate to alanine for transport to liver","Pyruvate carboxylase converting pyruvate to oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","Lactate dehydrogenase reducing pyruvate to regenerate NAD+ for glycolysis"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.4","dok":3,"approach":["Without enough oxygen, the electron transport chain slows and NADH builds up.","Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step.","Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and oxidizes NADH back to NAD+, so glycolysis can keep making ATP."],"why":"When oxygen delivery falls, mitochondria cannot reoxidize NADH. Glycolysis would stop at glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase without NAD+. Lactate dehydrogenase solves this by reducing pyruvate to lactate while turning NADH into NAD+. The cell keeps a small ATP supply, and lactate spills into the blood. A rising lactate is a marker of tissue hypoperfusion in sepsis.","whyNot":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase sends pyruvate into aerobic metabolism. Its flux falls when oxygen is short, which is why pyruvate is diverted to lactate.","Alanine aminotransferase makes alanine, used in the glucose-alanine cycle. It does not make lactate.","Pyruvate carboxylase is a mitochondrial gluconeogenic enzyme. It does not make lactate.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis","Lactic acidosis in shock"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C20-Q4","stem":"On hospital day 3, he has no chest pain, and ECG shows no ST-segment changes. The PR interval has lengthened to 0.30 s (0.12 to 0.20), and intermittent second-degree AV block develops later. Which of the following complications most likely explains these findings?","options":["Septic embolus occluding the left anterior descending artery","Immune complex deposition damaging the sinoatrial node","Rupture of mitral chordae tendineae causing acute regurgitation","Aortic annular abscess invading nearby conduction tissue"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.2","dok":3,"approach":["Notice new and progressing AV conduction delay in a patient with aortic valve endocarditis.","Recall the anatomy: the aortic annulus sits beside the membranous septum, where the AV bundle (bundle of His) passes.","Conclude that infection has spread beyond the leaflet into a perivalvular (ring) abscess, a typical S. aureus complication."],"why":"S. aureus is destructive and often spreads from the leaflets into the valve ring. The aortic annulus lies next to the membranous interventricular septum and the AV node and bundle of His. An abscess there causes new PR prolongation or higher-grade AV block. In aortic valve endocarditis, a new conduction block suggests a perivalvular abscess until proven otherwise.","whyNot":["An embolus to a coronary artery would cause an infarct, usually with chest pain or ST-segment changes. Neither is present.","Immune complex disease in endocarditis affects the kidneys and skin. SA node damage would cause sinus pauses or sinus bradycardia. The PR interval reflects AV conduction.","Chordal rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation and pulmonary edema. It does not damage conduction tissue.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Perivalvular abscess","AV node and bundle of His location"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-7"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C21","system":"CV","dx":"Tricuspid valve endocarditis in a person who injects drugs (Staphylococcus aureus)","codes":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-D3"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman who injects heroin comes to the emergency department because of 9 days of fever, chills, pleuritic chest pain, and cough with blood-streaked sputum. Temperature is 39.2 °C (102.6 °F). Physical examination shows injection marks over both antecubital fossae. A holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border, and the jugular venous pulse shows large v waves. Blood cultures grow catalase-positive, coagulase-positive, gram-positive cocci in clusters that are oxacillin susceptible. Echocardiography shows a 15-mm vegetation on the tricuspid valve with severe regurgitation. CT scan of the chest shows multiple peripheral nodules in both lungs, several with central cavitation. Diagnosis: tricuspid valve endocarditis caused by methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C21-Q1","stem":"Which of the following processes best explains the multiple peripheral, cavitating lung nodules on her CT scan?","options":["Reactivated tuberculosis spreading through the bronchial tree","Vegetation fragments crossing a patent foramen ovale to systemic arteries","Vegetation fragments carried by the pulmonary arteries to the lungs","Aspirated oral anaerobes forming a single dependent abscess"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.2","dok":3,"approach":["Locate the source: a right-sided (tricuspid) vegetation.","Trace where its fragments go: right ventricle, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary arteries.","Match the result: many peripheral nodules that cavitate as the infected emboli form small abscesses (septic pulmonary emboli)."],"why":"Fragments that break off a tricuspid vegetation travel with venous blood through the right ventricle into the pulmonary arteries. They lodge in small peripheral branches of both lungs, cause infarcts, and form abscesses that cavitate. This explains her pleuritic pain and blood-streaked sputum. Left-sided vegetations embolize instead to the brain, kidneys, spleen, and limbs.","whyNot":["Reactivation tuberculosis usually causes upper lobe cavitation with weeks to months of symptoms. It does not explain a tricuspid vegetation with many bilateral peripheral nodules.","Crossing a patent foramen ovale sends emboli into the systemic circulation (paradoxical embolism), so they would bypass the lungs.","Correct.","Aspiration causes a lung abscess in a dependent segment, usually a single lesion. Her lesions are multiple and peripheral."],"study":{"topics":["Septic pulmonary emboli","Right-sided versus left-sided embolization"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-H1"]}},{"id":"CV-C21-Q2","stem":"Her murmur at the left lower sternal border increases in intensity during inspiration. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this change?","options":["Decreased intrathoracic pressure that augments right heart preload","Lung expansion that displaces the left ventricle toward the chest wall","Increased right ventricular contractility during inspiration","Increased intrathoracic pressure that augments left ventricular filling"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the murmur: holosystolic at the left lower sternal border with large v waves means tricuspid regurgitation.","Recall that inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure.","Apply it: lower intrathoracic pressure draws more venous blood into the right heart, so the right ventricle ejects and regurgitates more, and the murmur gets louder."],"why":"During inspiration the diaphragm descends and intrathoracic pressure falls. More blood returns from the venae cavae to the right atrium and right ventricle. A larger right ventricular volume means more blood flows back through the leaky tricuspid valve in systole, so the murmur grows louder (Carvallo sign). Right-sided murmurs tend to increase with inspiration, while left-sided murmurs do not.","whyNot":["Correct.","Inflating lungs move between the heart and the chest wall, which tends to soften heart sounds.","Inspiration does not raise right ventricular contractility. The louder murmur comes from greater right ventricular filling (preload).","Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, and it briefly reduces left heart filling."],"study":{"topics":["Heart sounds and respiration","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-4"]}},{"id":"CV-C21-Q3","stem":"Which of the following factors best explains why the infection involves the tricuspid valve rather than a left-sided valve?","options":["Injected particles injure the first valve that venous blood crosses","The tricuspid valve closes against the highest pressure in the heart","Rheumatic damage, which favors the tricuspid valve, was already present","Low oxygen tension in venous blood selects for anaerobic organisms"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.1","dok":3,"approach":["Trace injected material: from an arm vein to the superior vena cava, right atrium, and then the tricuspid valve.","Recall that particles and drug additives in injected material are thought to damage the endothelium of the tricuspid valve.","Add skin flora (S. aureus) introduced with each injection, which can then attach to the injured valve."],"why":"In people who inject drugs, every injection delivers particles, drug additives, and skin bacteria into venous blood. The tricuspid valve is the first valve this material crosses, and repeated endothelial injury there is the accepted explanation for the right-sided preference. S. aureus from the skin is the most common organism. It is virulent enough to infect even a previously normal valve.","whyNot":["Correct.","The tricuspid valve closes against right ventricular pressure, which is far lower than left ventricular pressure. Higher pressure stress on left-sided valves would favor the mitral and aortic valves.","Rheumatic heart disease mostly affects the mitral valve, then the aortic valve.","S. aureus is a facultative anaerobe that grows well in oxygenated blood, and anaerobes are rare causes of endocarditis."],"study":{"topics":["Endocarditis in people who inject drugs","Valve involvement patterns in endocarditis"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C21-Q4","stem":"She injects into her right median cubital vein. Which of the following sequences of veins most directly carries the injected bacteria to the right atrium?","options":["Basilic, axillary, subclavian, right brachiocephalic, inferior vena cava","Basilic, axillary, subclavian, right brachiocephalic, superior vena cava","Cephalic, external jugular, azygos, right brachiocephalic, superior vena cava","Basilic, brachial, internal jugular, right brachiocephalic, superior vena cava"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.4","dok":2,"approach":["The median cubital vein links the cephalic and basilic veins in the cubital fossa.","The basilic vein joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein, which becomes the subclavian vein at the first rib.","The subclavian joins the internal jugular to form the brachiocephalic vein, and the two brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava."],"why":"Blood from the median cubital vein drains into the basilic vein, which joins the brachial veins at the lower border of teres major to form the axillary vein. The axillary vein becomes the subclavian vein at the outer border of the first rib. The subclavian and internal jugular veins join to form the brachiocephalic vein. Both brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium.","whyNot":["The inferior vena cava drains the lower body. Upper limb blood returns through the superior vena cava.","Correct.","The cephalic vein drains into the axillary vein, and the azygos vein drains the posterior thoracic wall into the superior vena cava. This route is wrong.","The internal jugular vein drains the head and neck. Arm blood reaches the brachiocephalic vein through the subclavian vein."],"study":{"topics":["Venous drainage of the upper limb","Formation of the superior vena cava"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C22","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy (Trypanosoma cruzi)","codes":["CV-18-K2","CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 54-year-old man comes to the physician because of 6 months of palpitations, exertional dyspnea, and ankle swelling. He also has dysphagia to both solids and liquids. He grew up in a rural adobe home in Bolivia and moved to California 20 years ago. ECG shows right bundle branch block with left anterior fascicular block and frequent premature ventricular contractions. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30% (55 to 70) and a thin-walled apical aneurysm. Two different serologic tests detect IgG antibodies against Trypanosoma cruzi. Diagnosis: chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C22-Q1","stem":"He most likely acquired the infection as a child in his home. Which of the following was the most likely mode of transmission?","options":["Triatomine bug feces rubbed into the bite wound or eye","Bite of a female Anopheles mosquito injecting sporozoites","Bite of a female sandfly that injects promastigotes","Bite of a tsetse fly that injects parasites in its saliva"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism from the diagnosis: Trypanosoma cruzi.","Recall its vector: triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bugs that live in cracks of adobe walls and thatched roofs in Latin America.","Recall the route: the bug defecates while feeding, and parasites in the feces enter through the bite wound or the conjunctiva."],"why":"T. cruzi is spread by triatomine bugs that hide in the walls of poorly built rural homes and feed at night. The bug passes infective trypomastigotes in its feces while feeding. Scratching rubs the feces into the bite wound, or the parasites enter through the conjunctiva, which can cause one-sided eyelid swelling (Romaña sign). Blood transfusion, organ transplant, mother-to-baby spread, and contaminated food are less common routes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium species, which cause malaria.","Sandflies transmit Leishmania species.","Tsetse flies transmit Trypanosoma brucei, the cause of African sleeping sickness, through their saliva."],"study":{"topics":["Trypanosoma cruzi life cycle","Protozoan vectors"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K2"]}},{"id":"CV-C22-Q2","stem":"Right bundle branch block is the most common conduction abnormality in chronic Chagas heart disease. In the right ventricle, the right bundle branch normally reaches the base of the anterior papillary muscle by way of which of the following structures?","options":["Chordae tendineae of the tricuspid leaflets","Crista terminalis of the right atrium","Pectinate muscles of the right auricle","Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the right bundle branch: it runs down the right side of the interventricular septum.","Recall the muscular bridge from the septum to the anterior papillary muscle in the right ventricle.","Name it: the septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) carries the right bundle branch, so the anterior papillary muscle contracts in time."],"why":"The right bundle branch descends on the right side of the interventricular septum. Much of it crosses the right ventricular cavity in the septomarginal trabecula, also called the moderator band, to reach the base of the anterior papillary muscle. This shortcut lets the papillary muscle tense the tricuspid chordae before the ventricle ejects. Chagas inflammation and fibrosis commonly involve the right bundle branch, so right bundle branch block is a classic finding.","whyNot":["Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords from papillary muscles to valve leaflets. They contain no conduction tissue.","The crista terminalis is a ridge in the right atrium that separates its smooth and rough parts. The SA node lies near its upper end.","Pectinate muscles are ridges in the atrial walls and auricles. They carry no bundle branch.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Right bundle branch and moderator band","Right ventricle internal anatomy"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C22-Q3","stem":"His dilated, thin-walled left ventricle generates about the same systolic pressure as a normal ventricle, yet its wall stress and oxygen demand per beat are higher. Which of the following relationships best explains this?","options":["Force of contraction rises as end-diastolic fiber length increases","Fluid movement depends on hydrostatic and oncotic pressure gradients","Wall stress rises with chamber radius and falls with wall thickness","Flow through a vessel rises with the fourth power of its radius"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note what changed: the chamber radius is larger and the wall is thinner, while pressure is unchanged.","Recall the law of Laplace: wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius, divided by wall thickness.","Apply it: a larger radius and thinner wall raise wall stress, and higher wall stress raises myocardial oxygen demand."],"why":"The law of Laplace says that wall stress equals pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. A dilated ventricle has a larger radius and, in this case, a thinner wall, so each fiber must carry more tension to make the same pressure. Wall stress is a major driver of myocardial oxygen use, which is one reason a dilated heart works less efficiently.","whyNot":["This is the Frank-Starling relationship. It describes how filling changes stroke volume. It does not explain the rise in wall stress.","These are Starling forces across capillaries. They explain capillary fluid exchange and edema.","Correct.","This is Poiseuille's law for flow through a tube. It explains how vessel radius controls flow and resistance."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace","Myocardial oxygen demand"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"CV-C22-Q4","stem":"A barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus that tapers smoothly to a narrow point at a lower esophageal sphincter that fails to relax. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this finding?","options":["Loss of ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus","Fibrous replacement of smooth muscle from systemic sclerosis","Peptic stricture from long-standing acid reflux","Circumferential growth of squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K2","obj":"CV-18-K.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the pattern of achalasia: dilated esophagus, smooth taper, and a lower esophageal sphincter that does not relax.","Recall that sphincter relaxation depends on inhibitory neurons of the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus.","Link it to the diagnosis: chronic Chagas disease destroys these enteric neurons, causing megaesophagus and megacolon."],"why":"Chronic T. cruzi infection damages neurons of the enteric nervous system, especially the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus. Loss of inhibitory neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP means the lower esophageal sphincter cannot relax, and peristalsis fails. The esophagus slowly dilates above the closed sphincter (megaesophagus). The same process in the colon produces megacolon.","whyNot":["Correct.","Systemic sclerosis replaces esophageal smooth muscle with fibrosis, which leaves the lower sphincter weak and open and causes reflux.","A peptic stricture narrows the distal esophagus, but the sphincter still relaxes and dysphagia is usually to solids first.","Carcinoma usually causes progressive dysphagia to solids first, with weight loss and an irregular narrowing on barium study."],"study":{"topics":["Chagas megaesophagus","Enteric nervous system"],"comps":["CV-18-K2","CV-17"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C23","system":"CV","dx":"Lyme carditis with third-degree AV block (Borrelia burgdorferi)","codes":["CV-18-K3","CV-18-E2"],"case":"A 26-year-old man from Connecticut comes to the physician because of lightheadedness and one episode of syncope. He went camping in wooded areas in June. Five weeks ago, he had an expanding erythematous rash with central clearing on his thigh that resolved without treatment. Pulse is 42/min. ECG shows P waves at 86/min with no relationship to narrow QRS complexes at 42/min. Two-tier serologic testing (enzyme immunoassay followed by Western blot) is positive for IgM and IgG antibodies against Borrelia burgdorferi. Diagnosis: Lyme carditis with third-degree AV block.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C23-Q1","stem":"His escape QRS complexes are narrow, which places the block above the bundle branches. In Lyme carditis, the block most often occurs in the structure that normally delays conduction between the atria and ventricles. In which of the following locations is this structure found?","options":["Subepicardium where the superior vena cava meets the right atrium","Interatrial septum near the opening of the coronary sinus","Posterior wall of the left atrium between the pulmonary veins","Muscular interventricular septum near the cardiac apex"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.1","dok":3,"approach":["Use the narrow escape QRS: the block is at or above the bundle of His.","Identify the structure that delays the impulse between atria and ventricles: the AV node.","Locate it: the lower interatrial septum, in the triangle of Koch near the coronary sinus opening, just above the septal tricuspid leaflet."],"why":"The AV node sits in the lower part of the interatrial septum, within the triangle of Koch, close to the opening of the coronary sinus and the septal leaflet of the tricuspid valve. It slows conduction so the ventricles fill before they contract. Lyme carditis most often blocks conduction at this level. A junctional escape rhythm then takes over, giving narrow QRS complexes at about 40 to 60/min.","whyNot":["This is the location of the SA node, the normal pacemaker. His P waves at 86/min show the SA node is working.","Correct.","The area between the pulmonary veins is where many atrial fibrillation triggers arise. It contains no AV conduction tissue.","The septum near the apex holds bundle branch and Purkinje fibers. Block there would give a wide, slow ventricular escape rhythm."],"study":{"topics":["AV node and triangle of Koch","Escape rhythms"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-18-K3"]}},{"id":"CV-C23-Q2","stem":"He recalls removing a small tick from his thigh about 2 days after the trip began. An infected Ixodes tick removed within about 24 hours of attachment rarely transmits B. burgdorferi. Which of the following best explains why transmission usually requires 36 to 48 hours or more of feeding?","options":["Spirochetes must first multiply inside the host's red blood cells","Spirochetes must move from the tick midgut to its salivary glands","The tick must molt into its adult stage while it is still attached","The tick must ingest the human's blood before it becomes infected"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall where B. burgdorferi lives in an unfed tick: the midgut.","Recall the trigger: the incoming blood meal activates the spirochetes, which multiply, change surface proteins, and migrate to the salivary glands.","Conclude that this migration takes time, so prompt tick removal lowers the risk."],"why":"In an unfed Ixodes tick, B. burgdorferi sits in the midgut. Once the tick starts feeding, the spirochetes multiply, switch surface proteins (OspA down, OspC up), and travel to the salivary glands. Only then are they injected with saliva. This process usually needs 36 to 48 hours or more of attachment, so checking for ticks daily and removing them promptly prevents most infections.","whyNot":["Babesia, which the same tick can carry, infects red blood cells. B. burgdorferi is an extracellular spirochete.","Correct.","Ticks molt between stages only after they drop off the host. Nymphs, which are small and easy to miss, transmit most human cases.","The tick acquired the spirochete earlier, as a larva or nymph feeding on infected mice. It does not need human blood to become infected."],"study":{"topics":["Lyme disease transmission","Ixodes tick life cycle"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K3"]}},{"id":"CV-C23-Q3","stem":"His Western blot shows IgG bands as well as IgM bands. Switching from IgM to IgG against the spirochete's protein antigens requires T-cell help. Which of the following interactions is required for this class switching?","options":["Bacterial lipopolysaccharide binding TLR4 on macrophages","Peptide on MHC class I binding CD8 on cytotoxic T cells","CD40 ligand on helper T cells binding CD40 on B cells","Complement C5a binding its receptor on neutrophils"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the shift from IgM to IgG over the weeks after infection.","Recall that class switching to protein antigens is T-cell dependent.","Name the contact signal: CD40 ligand (CD154) on activated helper T cells binding CD40 on B cells, together with cytokines."],"why":"B cells first make IgM. To switch to IgG, a B cell presents protein peptides on MHC class II to a helper T cell. The T cell then binds CD40 on the B cell through CD40 ligand and releases cytokines. These signals turn on activation-induced cytidine deaminase and class switch recombination. Without CD40 ligand, as in X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome, patients make IgM but little IgG.","whyNot":["B. burgdorferi lacks classic lipopolysaccharide, and TLR4 on macrophages drives innate inflammation. Its lipoproteins signal mainly through TLR2.","MHC class I with CD8 activates cytotoxic T cells, which kill infected cells. It does not drive B cell class switching.","Correct.","C5a attracts and activates neutrophils. It plays no direct role in class switching."],"study":{"topics":["Class switch recombination","T-dependent antibody responses"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-K3"]}},{"id":"CV-C23-Q4","stem":"If his infection had not been treated, which of the following manifestations would most likely have developed months after the rash?","options":["Migratory polyarthritis with subcutaneous nodules and chorea","Sudden monoarthritis of the big toe with urate crystals in fluid","Episodic swelling of one or a few large joints, often the knee","Symmetric small-joint arthritis with morning stiffness of the hands"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K3","obj":"CV-18-K.4","dok":2,"approach":["Place the patient on the Lyme timeline: early localized (erythema migrans), early disseminated (carditis, facial palsy), late (arthritis).","Recall the late form: attacks of arthritis in one or a few large joints, most often the knee.","Rule out look-alikes from other diseases by their distinct features."],"why":"Untreated Lyme disease can progress over months to late disease. The most common late finding in the United States is Lyme arthritis, with intermittent attacks of swelling in one or a few large joints, especially the knee. The swelling is often large and less painful than it looks. Carditis and facial nerve palsy belong to the earlier disseminated stage, which this patient is in now.","whyNot":["This combination is acute rheumatic fever after group A streptococcal pharyngitis.","This is acute gout from monosodium urate crystals.","Correct.","Symmetric small-joint arthritis with morning stiffness describes rheumatoid arthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Stages of Lyme disease","Lyme arthritis"],"comps":["CV-18-K3","CV-17"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C24","system":"CV","dx":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Rickettsia rickettsii)","codes":["CV-18-K4","CV-18-I12"],"case":"A 9-year-old girl in rural North Carolina is brought to the emergency department in July because of 5 days of fever, severe headache, and myalgias. Her parents found a tick on her scalp 8 days ago after she played with the family dog. Temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), pulse is 132/min, and blood pressure is 86/50 mm Hg. A rash that began as pink macules on the wrists and ankles has spread to the trunk and become petechial, including the palms and soles. There is edema of the hands and feet. Platelet count is 64,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), and serum sodium is 129 mEq/L (135 to 145). Diagnosis: Rocky Mountain spotted fever caused by Rickettsia rickettsii. Doxycycline is started.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C24-Q1","stem":"Her petechial rash, edema of the hands and feet, and thrombocytopenia all result from infection of one principal host cell type. Which of the following cells does R. rickettsii primarily infect?","options":["Monocytes in blood and tissues","Red blood cells in the circulation","Neutrophils in blood and tissues","Endothelial cells of small vessels"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K4","obj":"CV-18-K.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the findings: petechiae, edema, low blood pressure, and low platelets.","Find the common cause: damage to small blood vessels (a vasculitis).","Name the target: R. rickettsii infects vascular endothelial cells (and vascular smooth muscle) of small vessels."],"why":"R. rickettsii enters and multiplies in the endothelial cells of small vessels throughout the body. Infected endothelium is injured, which produces a small-vessel vasculitis. Leaky vessels cause edema and low blood volume, bleeding into the skin causes petechiae, and platelets are used up at sites of endothelial damage. The same vessel damage in the brain, lungs, and kidneys explains the severe forms of the disease.","whyNot":["Ehrlichia chaffeensis infects monocytes, forming morulae in their cytoplasm.","Babesia infects red blood cells, causing hemolytic anemia.","Anaplasma phagocytophilum infects neutrophils (granulocytes).","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Rickettsial vasculitis","Tick-borne intracellular bacteria"],"comps":["CV-18-K4","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C24-Q2","stem":"Two sets of routine blood cultures show no growth after 5 days. Which of the following properties of the organism best explains this finding?","options":["It is a spirochete too thin to see on Gram stain","It has a mycolic acid cell wall that needs weeks to grow","It is a virus that replicates only in host cell nuclei","It is a bacterium that grows only inside host cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: R. rickettsii.","Recall its key property: an obligate intracellular gram-negative bacterium.","Apply it: it cannot grow on the cell-free media used for routine blood cultures, so diagnosis relies on serology, PCR, or skin biopsy staining."],"why":"Rickettsiae are small gram-negative bacteria that stain poorly and grow only inside living host cells. They take nutrients and energy molecules from the host cell, so they do not grow in routine blood culture bottles. Diagnosis uses indirect immunofluorescent antibody testing, PCR, or immunohistochemistry on a skin biopsy. Treatment must start on clinical suspicion because early tests are often negative.","whyNot":["Thin spirochetes such as Treponema and Borrelia are hard to see on Gram stain. R. rickettsii is a gram-negative coccobacillus.","Mycobacteria have mycolic acid walls and grow slowly. That would not fit this acute tick-borne illness.","R. rickettsii is a gram-negative bacterium that multiplies mainly in the host cell cytoplasm.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Obligate intracellular bacteria","Diagnosis of Rocky Mountain spotted fever"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K4"]}},{"id":"CV-C24-Q3","stem":"Within endothelial cells, R. rickettsii escapes the phagosome and spreads directly into adjacent cells without re-entering the bloodstream. Which of the following host processes does it use for this movement?","options":["Clathrin-coated pits taking it up from the cell surface","Polymerization of host actin into a tail that pushes it","Dynein motors carrying it along microtubules","SNARE-mediated fusion of vesicles with the plasma membrane"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize cell-to-cell spread in the cytoplasm, like Listeria.","Recall which cytoskeletal protein builds a moving tail: actin.","Apply it: the bacterium recruits host actin to its surface and is pushed by actin polymerization into a neighboring cell."],"why":"Spotted fever group rickettsiae, including R. rickettsii, recruit host actin to one end of the bacterium. Continuous actin polymerization forms a tail that pushes the bacterium through the cytoplasm and into protrusions that enter the next cell. This lets infection spread along the endothelium while the bacteria stay hidden from antibodies. Listeria monocytogenes and Shigella use the same host system.","whyNot":["Clathrin-coated pits bring cargo into the cell. They do not move bacteria from one cell to the next.","Correct.","Dynein moves cargo toward the minus ends of microtubules. Some viruses use it to reach the nucleus, but rickettsiae do not use it to spread between cells.","SNARE proteins fuse vesicles with membranes during secretion. They do not move cytoplasmic bacteria."],"study":{"topics":["Actin-based motility","Cell-to-cell spread of intracellular bacteria"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K4"]}},{"id":"CV-C24-Q4","stem":"Her serum albumin concentration is 3.9 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), and her jugular veins are flat. Which of the following changes in capillary fluid exchange best explains the edema of her hands and feet?","options":["Increased capillary permeability to fluid and protein","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous congestion","Impaired lymphatic drainage from lymph node obstruction","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the Starling factors: capillary hydrostatic pressure, plasma oncotic pressure, capillary permeability, and lymph drainage.","Use the clues: flat neck veins rule out venous congestion, and normal albumin rules out low oncotic pressure.","Connect the remaining factor to endothelial infection: damaged endothelium makes capillaries leaky."],"why":"Injured endothelium in rickettsial vasculitis lets fluid and protein leak into the interstitium. In Starling terms, the filtration coefficient rises and the reflection coefficient for protein falls. Fluid leaves the vessels even though hydrostatic and oncotic pressures are not abnormal. This causes edema and, at the same time, low blood volume and hypotension.","whyNot":["Correct.","Venous congestion would raise jugular venous pressure. Her neck veins are flat.","Lymphatic blockage causes lymphedema over weeks to months, usually from surgery, cancer, or filarial infection.","Low albumin would lower oncotic pressure, but her albumin is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces and edema","Capillary permeability"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C25","system":"CV","dx":"Dilated cardiomyopathy from chronic alcohol use (alcoholic cardiomyopathy)","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 52-year-old right-handed man comes to the physician because of 4 months of progressive dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, and ankle swelling. He has consumed 10 to 12 beers daily for 15 years. His pulse is 104/min and blood pressure is 112/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention, bibasilar crackles, an S3 gallop, and a laterally displaced apical impulse. Coronary angiography shows no significant stenosis. Echocardiography shows dilation of all four chambers with relatively thin walls, diffuse hypokinesis, and a left ventricular ejection fraction of 25% (reference 55% to 70%). Diagnosis: dilated cardiomyopathy caused by chronic alcohol use (alcoholic cardiomyopathy).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C25-Q1","stem":"Ethanol and its metabolite acetaldehyde are directly toxic to myocytes and weaken their contraction. In a healthy ventricular myocyte, which of the following directly triggers release of most of the calcium that activates contraction?","options":["Calcium binding calmodulin and activating myosin light-chain kinase","IP3 binding to receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum after Gq signaling","Calcium entering through L-type channels and opening ryanodine receptors","T-tubule voltage sensors mechanically pulling ryanodine receptors open"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["The question asks what starts calcium release in cardiac muscle specifically.","Cardiac muscle uses calcium-induced calcium release: a small trigger influx releases a much larger store.","Rule out the skeletal muscle (mechanical coupling) and smooth muscle (IP3, calmodulin) mechanisms."],"why":"During the plateau of the ventricular action potential, L-type (dihydropyridine) calcium channels in the T tubules open. This small calcium influx opens ryanodine receptors (RyR2) on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which release most of the calcium that binds troponin C. This is calcium-induced calcium release. Because the trigger influx and the sarcoplasmic reticulum store set how much calcium is released, they help set the force of each beat.","whyNot":["Calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light-chain kinase drives smooth muscle contraction. Cardiac muscle is regulated by calcium binding to troponin C on the thin filament.","IP3-mediated calcium release is a main pathway in smooth muscle after Gq-coupled receptor activation. It plays only a minor role in ventricular myocytes.","Correct.","Direct mechanical coupling of the T-tubule voltage sensor (DHP receptor) to RyR1 is how skeletal muscle releases calcium. Cardiac muscle needs calcium entry to open RyR2."],"study":{"topics":["Excitation-contraction coupling in cardiac muscle","Cardiac versus skeletal versus smooth muscle calcium handling"],"comps":["CV-5","CV-6"]}},{"id":"CV-C25-Q2","stem":"In his liver, alcohol dehydrogenase converts ethanol to acetaldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase converts acetaldehyde to acetate. Both reactions reduce the same cofactor. Which of the following ratios is most likely increased in his hepatocytes?","options":["NADPH to NADP+","NADH to NAD+","ATP to ADP","FADH2 to FAD"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-E1","obj":"CV-18-E.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the two dehydrogenase steps of ethanol metabolism.","Both use NAD+ as the electron acceptor, producing NADH.","Large alcohol loads therefore raise the NADH to NAD+ ratio."],"why":"Alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase both transfer electrons to NAD+, so heavy drinking raises the NADH to NAD+ ratio. The high ratio pushes pyruvate toward lactate and oxaloacetate toward malate, which slows gluconeogenesis. It also slows fatty acid oxidation and favors glycerol-3-phosphate formation, which promotes fatty liver.","whyNot":["NADPH is used, not produced, by the microsomal ethanol-oxidizing system (CYP2E1), which oxidizes NADPH to NADP+. NADPH is mainly made by the pentose phosphate pathway.","Correct.","ATP and ADP describe energy charge. They are not the electron carriers reduced by these dehydrogenase reactions.","FAD is the cofactor for enzymes such as succinate dehydrogenase. Neither alcohol dehydrogenase nor aldehyde dehydrogenase uses FAD."],"study":{"topics":["Ethanol metabolism","Effects of a high NADH to NAD+ ratio"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C25-Q3","stem":"A new grade 2/6 holosystolic murmur is heard best at the apex and radiates to the left axilla. Which of the following best explains this murmur?","options":["Mitral valve, leaflets pulled apart by a dilated ventricle","Aortic valve, cusps unable to close in a dilated aortic root","Tricuspid valve, leaflets pulled apart by a dilated right ventricle","Pulmonary valve, cusps unable to close in a dilated pulmonary trunk"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-D2","obj":"CV-18-D.4","dok":3,"approach":["A holosystolic murmur means blood leaks backward through an atrioventricular valve during systole.","Apex location with radiation to the axilla points to the mitral valve.","In dilated cardiomyopathy, annular dilation and outward displacement of the papillary muscles stop the leaflets from meeting (functional regurgitation)."],"why":"Mitral regurgitation produces a holosystolic murmur best heard at the apex that radiates to the left axilla. In dilated cardiomyopathy the leaflets are structurally normal, but the mitral annulus stretches and the papillary muscles are pulled outward and apically as the ventricle enlarges. The leaflets can no longer meet in systole, so blood leaks back into the left atrium. This is called functional or secondary mitral regurgitation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Aortic regurgitation from a dilated root causes an early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border, not a holosystolic apical murmur.","Tricuspid regurgitation is also holosystolic and common in dilated cardiomyopathy, but it is heard best at the left lower sternal border and gets louder with inspiration. It does not radiate to the axilla.","Pulmonary regurgitation causes a diastolic murmur at the left upper sternal border, usually with pulmonary hypertension."],"study":{"topics":["Valve auscultation areas","Functional mitral regurgitation"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C25-Q4","stem":"Eight months later, he develops sudden weakness of the right face and right arm and nonfluent speech with intact comprehension. Echocardiography shows a thrombus at the left ventricular apex. An embolus from this thrombus has most likely occluded which of the following arteries?","options":["Left middle cerebral artery","Basilar artery","Left anterior cerebral artery","Right middle cerebral artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Localize the deficit: right face and arm weakness with nonfluent speech and intact comprehension (expressive aphasia) means a left (dominant) frontal lesion in a right-handed person.","Face and arm motor cortex and Broca area are supplied by the middle cerebral artery.","Trace the embolus: left ventricle, aorta, left common carotid, left internal carotid, then straight into the left middle cerebral artery."],"why":"A poorly contracting, dilated ventricle allows blood to pool (stasis), so mural thrombi form, often at the apex. An embolus leaving the left ventricle enters the aorta and can reach either carotid. The middle cerebral artery is the direct continuation of the internal carotid and is the most common site for cardiac emboli. The left middle cerebral artery supplies the lateral motor cortex for the right face and arm and, in most right-handed people, the Broca speech area.","whyNot":["Correct.","Basilar artery occlusion affects the brainstem and occipital lobes, causing cranial nerve deficits, bilateral signs, or reduced consciousness.","The left anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial motor cortex, so it causes right leg more than arm weakness and spares the face.","A right middle cerebral artery embolus would cause left-sided face and arm weakness and usually neglect, not aphasia."],"study":{"topics":["Cardioembolic stroke","Cerebral arterial territories"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C26","system":"CV","dx":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with left ventricular outflow tract obstruction","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 19-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after he collapsed during a sprint drill at college soccer practice and regained consciousness within 1 minute. He has had chest tightness with strenuous exercise for several months. His paternal uncle died suddenly at age 34 years. Physical examination shows a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases in intensity when he stands from a squatting position and decreases with sustained handgrip. ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy with deep, narrow Q waves in the lateral leads. Echocardiography shows asymmetric septal thickening of 24 mm (reference up to about 11 mm), a small left ventricular cavity, systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve, and a resting outflow gradient of 50 mm Hg. Diagnosis: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with left ventricular outflow tract obstruction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C26-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing finds a pathogenic variant in MYH7, the gene for beta-myosin heavy chain. His father carries the same variant. If the patient later has children with a partner who does not carry the variant, which of the following is the probability that each child inherits it?","options":["25%","75%","100%","50%"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the inheritance pattern: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy from sarcomere gene variants is autosomal dominant.","A heterozygous parent passes the variant allele to each child with a probability of one half.","Penetrance affects whether a carrier gets disease, not whether the allele is inherited."],"why":"Most familial hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is autosomal dominant and is caused by variants in sarcomere genes, most often MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) and MYBPC3 (cardiac myosin-binding protein C). The patient is heterozygous, so each child has a 50% chance of inheriting the variant. Penetrance is incomplete and age-dependent, and severity varies even within one family, so relatives who carry the same variant may have mild disease or none. First-degree relatives are offered genetic testing and echocardiographic screening.","whyNot":["25% is the chance that a child of two carrier parents is affected in an autosomal recessive disorder.","75% is the chance of inheriting at least one variant allele when both parents are heterozygous for a dominant trait.","100% would apply only if the patient were homozygous for the variant, which is rare.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Autosomal dominant inheritance and penetrance","Sarcomere genes in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"comps":["CV-14"]}},{"id":"CV-C26-Q2","stem":"A teammate's screening echocardiogram shows a left ventricular wall thickness of 13 mm. Which of the following additional findings would most strongly favor physiologic athlete's heart over hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","options":["Thickening confined to the septum, sparing the free wall","A small left ventricular cavity, under 45 mm at end-diastole","Regression of wall thickness after 3 months of detraining","Impaired early diastolic relaxation on Doppler imaging"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-12","obj":"CV-12.5","dok":3,"approach":["A wall thickness of 13 to 15 mm is a gray zone where athlete's heart and mild hypertrophic cardiomyopathy overlap.","Athlete's heart is a reversible adaptation to training with normal relaxation and an enlarged cavity.","Pick the finding that only a physiologic adaptation would show."],"why":"Training causes a physiologic, reversible increase in cardiac mass, usually with an enlarged left ventricular cavity and normal diastolic function. When training stops for about 3 months, the wall thickness falls in athlete's heart but not in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, whose hypertrophy comes from a sarcomere defect. Regression with detraining therefore points strongly toward a physiologic adaptation.","whyNot":["Asymmetric septal thickening is the classic pattern of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Athletic hypertrophy is usually symmetric.","A small cavity under about 45 mm favors hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Trained athletes usually have a normal or enlarged cavity, often over 55 mm.","Correct.","Impaired relaxation favors hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Athlete's heart typically has normal or enhanced diastolic filling."],"study":{"topics":["Athlete's heart versus hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Cardiac adaptation to training"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"CV-C26-Q3","stem":"His syncope occurred during an intense sprint. Which of the following pairs of changes during exercise most increases his outflow tract obstruction?","options":["Decreased heart rate and increased end-diastolic volume","Decreased contractility and increased afterload","Increased afterload and increased end-diastolic volume","Increased contractility and decreased afterload"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-E1","obj":"CV-18-E.2","dok":3,"approach":["In this disease the obstruction is dynamic: it worsens whenever the left ventricular cavity gets smaller or ejects faster.","During exercise, sympathetic drive raises contractility and heart rate, and dilation of muscle arterioles lowers systemic vascular resistance.","Stronger, faster ejection into a lower resistance pulls the anterior mitral leaflet toward the septum and narrows the outflow tract."],"why":"Exercise raises sympathetic tone, which increases contractility and heart rate, and metabolic vasodilation in working muscle lowers systemic vascular resistance. Stronger contraction empties the small ventricle further, and lower afterload speeds ejection. Both bring the thickened septum and the anterior mitral leaflet (systolic anterior motion) closer together, raising the outflow gradient. Cardiac output then cannot rise to meet demand, and syncope or a ventricular arrhythmia can follow.","whyNot":["A slower heart rate and a larger end-diastolic volume widen the outflow tract and lessen the obstruction, as with squatting or leg raising.","Decreased contractility and increased afterload both reduce the obstruction. This is why handgrip, which raises afterload, softens his murmur.","Higher afterload and a larger ventricular volume both reduce the obstruction. Squatting produces both and softens the murmur.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dynamic outflow obstruction","Cardiovascular response to exercise"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-6"]}},{"id":"CV-C26-Q4","stem":"If his collapse had been fatal, which of the following findings on microscopic examination of the interventricular septum would most likely be seen at autopsy?","options":["Lymphocytic infiltrate surrounding necrotic myocytes","Hypertrophied myocytes arranged in a chaotic, swirling pattern","Amorphous pink extracellular deposits with apple-green birefringence","Coagulation necrosis with neutrophils and loss of nuclei"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-E1","obj":"CV-18-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Link the diagnosis to its histologic hallmark.","Sarcomere protein defects produce hypertrophied myocytes that lose their parallel arrangement (myofiber disarray).","Rule out the patterns of myocarditis, amyloidosis, and acute infarction."],"why":"The characteristic histology of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is myocyte disarray: large, hypertrophied myocytes that branch and run in a disorganized, swirling pattern, most marked in the septum, with interstitial and replacement fibrosis. Disarray and fibrosis create an unstable electrical substrate. Ventricular arrhythmias from this substrate are the main cause of sudden death in young athletes with this disease.","whyNot":["A lymphocytic infiltrate with adjacent myocyte necrosis is the picture of viral or other lymphocytic myocarditis.","Correct.","Amorphous pink deposits with apple-green birefringence on Congo red staining define amyloidosis, which causes restrictive cardiomyopathy.","Coagulation necrosis with neutrophils is seen about 1 to 3 days after a myocardial infarction."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of the cardiomyopathies","Sudden cardiac death in young athletes"],"comps":["CV-18-E1","CV-2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C27","system":"CV","dx":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy from hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of 6 months of progressive dyspnea on exertion, leg swelling, and early satiety. He underwent surgery for bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome 5 years ago. Blood pressure is 102/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention that increases with inspiration and hepatomegaly. ECG shows low QRS voltage in the limb leads. Echocardiography shows a left ventricular wall thickness of 17 mm (reference up to about 11 mm), a normal-sized ventricle, an ejection fraction of 55%, markedly enlarged atria, and no pericardial effusion. Serum and urine studies show no monoclonal protein. A technetium pyrophosphate scan shows intense cardiac uptake, and genetic testing finds a pathogenic transthyretin (TTR) variant. Diagnosis: restrictive cardiomyopathy from hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C27-Q1","stem":"Transthyretin is made mainly in the liver and normally circulates as a tetramer that carries thyroxine and retinol-binding protein. Which of the following mechanisms best explains how his TTR variant causes disease?","options":["Excess intestinal iron uptake deposits iron within myocytes","Low alpha-galactosidase A activity lets glycosphingolipids build up in lysosomes","Unstable tetramers release monomers that misfold into beta-sheet fibrils","Clonal plasma cells secrete free light chains that form fibrils"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-15-E","obj":"IM-15-E.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the protein: transthyretin is a transport tetramer made by the liver.","Pathogenic variants make the tetramer less stable, so it falls apart into monomers.","The monomers misfold into insoluble beta-pleated sheet fibrils that deposit in the heart and nerves."],"why":"Hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis is autosomal dominant. A single amino acid substitution destabilizes the transthyretin tetramer, which dissociates into monomers that misfold and assemble into beta-pleated sheet amyloid fibrils. Fibrils deposit in the myocardial interstitium, the carpal tunnel, and peripheral nerves. Wild-type transthyretin can do the same with age, mostly in older men, without any variant.","whyNot":["Increased iron absorption is hereditary hemochromatosis (usually HFE variants), which can cause a dilated or restrictive cardiomyopathy through iron overload, not amyloid.","Alpha-galactosidase A deficiency is Fabry disease, an X-linked lysosomal storage disorder that can thicken the ventricle. It does not form amyloid fibrils.","Correct.","Free light chains from a plasma cell clone cause AL amyloidosis. His negative serum and urine studies for monoclonal protein and the TTR variant point away from it."],"study":{"topics":["Protein misfolding and amyloid","Hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"CV-C27-Q2","stem":"Two months later, he reports dysphagia to solid foods. A barium swallow shows smooth extrinsic compression of the anterior wall of the esophagus at the level of the heart. Enlargement of which of the following chambers most likely explains this finding?","options":["Left atrium","Left ventricle","Right atrium","Right ventricle"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy enlarges the atria because they must push blood into stiff ventricles.","Recall which surface of the heart each chamber forms.","The left atrium forms most of the base (posterior surface) and sits directly in front of the esophagus."],"why":"The left atrium forms most of the posterior surface (base) of the heart and lies just anterior to the esophagus, separated from it by the pericardium. When the left atrium enlarges, it can indent the esophagus and cause trouble swallowing solids. The same relationship is why transesophageal echocardiography gives such clear views of the left atrium.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left ventricle forms the apex, the left border, and much of the inferior (diaphragmatic) surface.","The right atrium forms the right border of the heart and receives the venae cavae. It does not lie against the esophagus.","The right ventricle forms most of the anterior (sternocostal) surface, the side farthest from the esophagus."],"study":{"topics":["Surfaces and borders of the heart","Relations of the left atrium"],"comps":["CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C27-Q3","stem":"His echocardiogram shows thick ventricular walls, yet his ECG shows low QRS voltage. Which of the following best explains this combination of findings?","options":["Concentric myocyte hypertrophy from long-standing pressure overload","Extracellular deposits thickening the wall without adding myocytes","Fluid in the pericardial sac that damps the cardiac electrical signal","Thinning and scarring of the wall after a large transmural infarction"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-E1","obj":"CV-18-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["QRS voltage reflects the mass of electrically active myocytes.","Wall thickness on echo reflects everything in the wall, including extracellular material.","Thick walls with low voltage mean the thickening is from something electrically silent."],"why":"Amyloid fibrils accumulate between myocytes, so the wall thickens while the amount of working, electrically active muscle stays the same or falls. The ECG therefore shows low voltage despite a thick wall on echo, a classic clue to infiltrative cardiomyopathy. The deposits also make the ventricle stiff, which causes the restrictive filling, enlarged atria, and rising neck veins on inspiration.","whyNot":["True myocyte hypertrophy from pressure overload adds electrically active muscle and raises QRS voltage.","Correct.","A pericardial effusion can lower QRS voltage, but his echocardiogram shows no effusion, and an effusion would not thicken the walls.","A large infarction thins and scars the wall. His walls are thick, and nothing in the case suggests a prior infarction."],"study":{"topics":["Infiltrative cardiomyopathy","Restrictive physiology"],"comps":["CV-18-E1","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C27-Q4","stem":"His earlier carpal tunnel syndrome resulted from amyloid deposits in the carpal tunnel compressing a nerve. Which of the following deficits would this compression most likely cause?","options":["Weak finger abduction with sensory loss in the little finger","Weak thumb adduction with a positive Froment sign","Weak thumb opposition with index fingertip sensory loss","Weak wrist extension with wrist drop"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-I2","obj":"NE-20-I.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the nerve that passes through the carpal tunnel: the median nerve.","Recall what the median nerve supplies distal to the wrist: the thenar muscles and palmar skin of the thumb, index, middle, and half of the ring finger.","Match the deficit to that distribution."],"why":"The median nerve runs through the carpal tunnel under the flexor retinaculum. Amyloid in the retinaculum and tendon sheaths narrows the tunnel and compresses it. Distal to the wrist, the median nerve supplies the thenar muscles, including opponens pollicis, and the palmar skin of the lateral three and a half digits. Bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome often appears years before the heart disease in transthyretin amyloidosis and is a useful early clue.","whyNot":["Weak finger abduction (dorsal interossei) and a numb little finger point to the ulnar nerve, which passes through the Guyon canal, not the carpal tunnel.","Weak thumb adduction (adductor pollicis) with a positive Froment sign is an ulnar nerve deficit.","Correct.","Wrist drop comes from radial nerve injury, usually in the arm, and the radial nerve does not pass through the carpal tunnel."],"study":{"topics":["Median nerve at the wrist","Extracardiac clues to amyloidosis"],"comps":["CV-18-E1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C28","system":"CV","dx":"Acute viral myocarditis from coxsackievirus B","codes":["CV-18-K6"],"case":"A 23-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 2 days of sharp chest pain, fatigue, and dyspnea. Ten days earlier she had fever, sore throat, and diarrhea that resolved spontaneously. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 98/64 mm Hg. Serum troponin I concentration is 3.2 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). ECG shows sinus tachycardia with diffuse nonspecific ST-segment and T-wave changes. Echocardiography shows global hypokinesis with an ejection fraction of 35% (reference 55% to 70%). Coronary angiography is normal. Endomyocardial biopsy shows a lymphocytic infiltrate with adjacent myocyte necrosis, and PCR of the tissue detects coxsackievirus B3. Diagnosis: acute viral myocarditis caused by coxsackievirus B.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C28-Q1","stem":"Coxsackievirus B is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family. After it enters a cell, its genome is translated directly by host ribosomes into one large polyprotein that viral proteases then cleave. Which of the following genome types allows this?","options":["Negative-sense single-stranded RNA","Positive-sense single-stranded RNA","Linear double-stranded DNA","Segmented double-stranded RNA"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.6","dok":2,"approach":["Ask what a genome must be for ribosomes to read it right away.","Only positive-sense RNA can act directly as messenger RNA.","Picornaviruses are small, nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA viruses."],"why":"Picornaviruses, including coxsackieviruses, have a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome that works as messenger RNA as soon as it enters the cytoplasm. Host ribosomes translate it into one polyprotein, which viral proteases cleave into structural proteins and the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. The virus is nonenveloped, spreads mainly by the fecal-oral route, and enters cardiomyocytes through the coxsackievirus and adenovirus receptor (CAR).","whyNot":["Negative-sense RNA cannot be translated directly. These viruses (for example influenza and rabies) must carry their own RNA polymerase to make a positive strand first.","Correct.","Double-stranded DNA must be transcribed into messenger RNA before translation, usually in the nucleus, as in adenoviruses and herpesviruses.","Segmented double-stranded RNA is the genome of reoviruses such as rotavirus, which must transcribe messenger RNA with a packaged polymerase."],"study":{"topics":["Picornavirus structure and replication","Viral genome types"],"comps":["CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C28-Q2","stem":"Infected cardiomyocytes display viral peptides on MHC class I molecules. Which of the following cells recognizes these complexes and directly kills the myocytes, contributing to the injury seen on her biopsy?","options":["Antibody-secreting plasma cell","CD4+ helper T cell","Natural killer (NK) cell","CD8+ cytotoxic T cell"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.6","dok":2,"approach":["Peptide on MHC class I is read by a T-cell receptor plus the CD8 coreceptor.","CD8+ T cells kill their targets with perforin and granzymes or Fas ligand.","Match the effector cell to both the antigen presentation and the killing."],"why":"Every nucleated cell, including cardiomyocytes, presents peptides from intracellular proteins on MHC class I. CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognize viral peptide on MHC class I with their T-cell receptor and kill the infected cell through perforin and granzymes or Fas ligand. This clears the virus but also destroys myocytes, so much of the damage in viral myocarditis comes from the immune response itself.","whyNot":["Plasma cells secrete antibody against free virus and surface antigens. They do not recognize peptide-MHC complexes or kill cells themselves.","CD4+ helper T cells recognize peptide on MHC class II, which is found on antigen-presenting cells, and they mainly direct other cells rather than kill directly.","NK cells kill cells that have lost MHC class I (missing self) or are coated with antibody. They do not recognize specific peptide-MHC complexes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["MHC class I and CD8+ T cells","Immune injury in viral myocarditis"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C28-Q3","stem":"Myocarditis often causes arrhythmias. Inflammation that damages the intercalated discs can slow the spread of action potentials from one myocyte to the next. Which of the following junctions in the disc directly carries this current?","options":["Tight junctions","Fasciae adherentes","Gap junctions","Desmosomes"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the parts of the intercalated disc: fasciae adherentes, desmosomes, and gap junctions.","Separate mechanical junctions from the electrical junction.","Gap junctions (connexin 43 channels) let ions flow between neighboring cells."],"why":"Gap junctions are channels built from connexins (mainly connexin 43 in ventricular muscle) that let ions pass directly between neighboring myocytes. This electrical coupling lets the myocardium act as a functional syncytium. Inflammation and scarring disturb gap junctions, which slows and fragments conduction and helps create reentrant arrhythmias.","whyNot":["Tight junctions seal epithelial and endothelial cells. They are not part of the intercalated disc.","Fasciae adherentes anchor actin filaments of the sarcomeres to the cell membrane and transmit force, not current.","Correct.","Desmosomes anchor intermediate filaments (desmin) and hold cells together under strain. They do not pass current."],"study":{"topics":["Intercalated disc structure","Cardiac muscle histology"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C28-Q4","stem":"One year later, her ejection fraction is 25% and all four chambers are dilated. Which of the following processes best explains this outcome?","options":["Interstitial amyloid deposits stiffening an otherwise normal ventricle","Atherosclerotic narrowing of the coronaries causing repeated infarcts","Inherited sarcomere protein defect causing myocyte disarray","Ongoing immune injury causing myocyte loss and replacement fibrosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the new picture as dilated cardiomyopathy following myocarditis.","Most myocarditis resolves, but in some patients viral persistence or autoimmune injury continues.","Ongoing myocyte loss is replaced by fibrous tissue, and the weakened ventricle dilates."],"why":"Viral myocarditis is a recognized cause of dilated cardiomyopathy. In a minority of patients, persistent viral genome or an autoimmune response against cardiac proteins such as myosin keeps injuring myocytes after the acute infection. Lost myocytes are replaced by fibrosis, the remaining muscle cannot keep up, and the ventricle remodels into a dilated, poorly contracting chamber.","whyNot":["Amyloid causes a stiff, thick-walled restrictive cardiomyopathy with a normal-sized ventricle, not four-chamber dilation, and is not a result of myocarditis.","Her angiography was normal and she is young, so ischemic cardiomyopathy from coronary disease is very unlikely.","Sarcomere gene defects with myocyte disarray cause hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, which is inherited and would not follow an infection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Outcomes of myocarditis","Causes of dilated cardiomyopathy"],"comps":["CV-18-K6","CV-18-E1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C29","system":"CV","dx":"Dengue hemorrhagic fever (dengue with warning signs) in a second dengue infection","codes":["CV-18-K5"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the emergency department on day 5 of a febrile illness because of abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, and bleeding gums. The illness began 4 days after she returned from a 2-week trip to Southeast Asia, with sudden fever to 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), severe headache, retro-orbital pain, and intense myalgias and arthralgias. Her fever resolved today. She had confirmed dengue while living in the Caribbean 4 years ago. Laboratory studies show a platelet count of 32,000/µL (reference 150,000 to 400,000/µL), leukocyte count of 2,900/µL (reference 4,500 to 11,000/µL), and hematocrit of 47% (reference 36% to 46%), up from 38% on day 2. Ultrasonography shows ascites and a right pleural effusion. Dengue NS1 antigen is positive. Diagnosis: dengue hemorrhagic fever (dengue with warning signs) during a second dengue infection.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C29-Q1","stem":"Her travel companion asks how to avoid this infection on a future trip. Which of the following characteristics of the vector should guide prevention?","options":["Aedes mosquitoes biting mainly during the daytime","Triatomine bugs biting the face during sleep at night","Anopheles mosquitoes biting mainly from dusk to dawn","Ixodes ticks needing to stay attached for more than a day"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.5","dok":2,"approach":["Name the vector of dengue virus: Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti.","Recall its behavior: it bites during the day and breeds in small containers of standing water near homes.","Prevention therefore means daytime repellent and covering skin, not only bed nets at night."],"why":"Dengue virus is a flavivirus spread by Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti and also Aedes albopictus. Aedes aegypti bites mostly during the day, with peaks in the early morning and late afternoon, and breeds in water-holding containers around homes. Daytime repellent, long sleeves, and removing standing water matter more than bed nets alone.","whyNot":["Correct.","Triatomine (kissing) bugs transmit Trypanosoma cruzi, the cause of Chagas disease, through feces rubbed into the bite or mucosa.","Night-biting Anopheles mosquitoes transmit malaria, where bed nets are a mainstay of prevention.","Ixodes ticks transmit Lyme disease, and transmission usually requires attachment for more than a day."],"study":{"topics":["Arthropod vectors of cardiovascular infections","Dengue virus transmission"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K5"]}},{"id":"CV-C29-Q2","stem":"Her first dengue infection was with a different serotype. Which of the following mechanisms best explains why this second infection is more severe?","options":["Antigenic drift produces a new viral hemagglutinin that her antibodies miss","Non-neutralizing IgG boosts viral entry into Fc receptor-bearing monocytes","Virus-induced loss of CD4+ T cells leaves the new infection unchecked","Cross-reactive IgE from the first infection triggers mast cell degranulation"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the key history: a prior infection with a different dengue serotype.","Antibodies from the first serotype bind the new serotype but do not neutralize it.","The antibody-coated virus is taken up through Fc gamma receptors on monocytes and macrophages, where it replicates (antibody-dependent enhancement)."],"why":"There are four dengue serotypes. Infection gives lasting immunity to that serotype only. In a later infection with a different serotype, cross-reactive IgG binds the virus without neutralizing it, and the Fc portion directs it into Fc gamma receptor-bearing monocytes and macrophages. This antibody-dependent enhancement raises viral load and, with cross-reactive T cells and cytokine release, drives the capillary leak of dengue hemorrhagic fever.","whyNot":["Antigenic drift of hemagglutinin describes influenza. Her two dengue infections were caused by different, established serotypes, not a drifted strain.","Correct.","Progressive loss of CD4+ T cells is the hallmark of HIV infection, not dengue.","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation is type I hypersensitivity (allergy and anaphylaxis). It is not the basis of severe dengue."],"study":{"topics":["Antibody-dependent enhancement","Dengue serotypes and immunity"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-K5"]}},{"id":"CV-C29-Q3","stem":"Between day 2 and day 5, her hematocrit rose while ascites and a pleural effusion appeared. She has no history of heart, liver, or kidney disease, and her neck veins are flat. Which of the following changes in capillary fluid exchange best explains these findings?","options":["Obstructed lymphatic drainage of the pleura and peritoneum","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from impaired albumin synthesis","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous congestion","Increased capillary permeability to plasma and protein"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["A rising hematocrit with new effusions means plasma is leaving the vessels while red cells stay behind.","Check each Starling force against the case: flat neck veins argue against high venous pressure, and she has no liver or kidney disease.","Dengue injures the endothelium, so permeability rises and the reflection coefficient for protein falls."],"why":"Fluid movement across capillaries depends on hydrostatic and oncotic pressures and on how permeable the wall is to water and protein. In dengue, viral and immune effects on the endothelium make capillaries leak plasma and protein into serous cavities. Plasma volume falls, red cells are concentrated, and the hematocrit rises. This leak usually peaks around the time the fever breaks and can lead to shock.","whyNot":["Lymphatic obstruction causes protein-rich edema over weeks, usually from tumor, surgery, or filariasis. Nothing in the case suggests it.","Failing albumin synthesis occurs with chronic liver disease and develops over weeks because albumin has a half-life of about 3 weeks, which does not fit a 3-day change.","Venous congestion raises capillary hydrostatic pressure in heart failure, but her neck veins are flat and she has no heart disease.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Capillary leak in dengue"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C29-Q4","stem":"Which of the following complications is she at greatest risk of developing over the next 24 to 48 hours?","options":["Hypovolemic shock from continued plasma leakage","Rupture of an enlarged spleen","Acute kidney failure from immune complex glomerulonephritis","Ischemic stroke from a cardioembolic clot"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K5","obj":"CV-18-K.6","dok":2,"approach":["Place her in the course of dengue: the fever has just broken and warning signs have appeared, which marks the critical phase.","The critical phase usually lasts about 24 to 48 hours and is driven by capillary leak.","Ongoing leak shrinks plasma volume and can progress to shock."],"why":"Dengue has a febrile phase, a critical phase, and a recovery phase. The critical phase begins around defervescence, usually about days 3 to 7 of illness, and lasts about 24 to 48 hours. Plasma leakage during this window can reduce circulating volume enough to cause hypovolemic shock (dengue shock syndrome), which is the main cause of death. Her rising hematocrit, effusions, and warning signs put her at high risk.","whyNot":["Correct.","Splenic rupture is a rare event and is classically linked to infectious mononucleosis.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis is not a typical early complication of dengue. Kidney injury in dengue usually follows shock.","Cardioembolic stroke requires a cardiac source of clot, such as atrial fibrillation, which she does not have."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of dengue","Dengue shock syndrome"],"comps":["CV-18-K5","CV-18-H5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C30","system":"CV","dx":"Wet beriberi: thiamine deficiency with high-output heart failure","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 36-year-old man comes to the physician 4 months after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery because of dyspnea, leg swelling, and burning paresthesias in both feet. He has vomited after most meals for 6 weeks. His pulse is 116/min and blood pressure is 112/46 mm Hg. Physical examination shows warm extremities, bounding pulses, jugular venous distention, and pitting edema to the knees. Echocardiography shows dilated ventricles, an ejection fraction of 65%, and a cardiac output of 10 L/min (reference 4 to 8 L/min). Serum lactate concentration is 4.6 mmol/L (reference 0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). Whole-blood thiamine concentration is low. Diagnosis: wet beriberi, thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency causing high-output heart failure.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C30-Q1","stem":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of thiamine and a cofactor for several enzymes. Decreased activity of which of the following enzymes best explains his increased serum lactate concentration?","options":["Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate carboxylase","Pyruvate dehydrogenase","Lactate dehydrogenase"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify which listed enzyme needs thiamine pyrophosphate.","Pyruvate dehydrogenase links glycolysis to the citric acid cycle by converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA.","When it slows, pyruvate builds up and is converted to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase."],"why":"The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex needs thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, and NAD+. Without enough thiamine, pyruvate cannot enter the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA. Pyruvate accumulates and is reduced to lactate, causing lactic acidosis, and aerobic ATP production falls in high-demand tissues such as heart, nerve, and brain.","whyNot":["Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase is a glycolytic enzyme that uses NAD+, not thiamine. Reduced activity would slow glycolysis and lower pyruvate and lactate production.","Pyruvate carboxylase uses biotin, not thiamine. Its deficiency also raises lactate, but his deficiency is thiamine.","Correct.","Lactate dehydrogenase makes lactate from pyruvate. Reduced activity would lower lactate, and it does not need thiamine."],"study":{"topics":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex cofactors","Causes of lactic acidosis"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C30-Q2","stem":"To confirm a functional deficiency, a laboratory measures an enzyme's activity in his erythrocytes before and after adding thiamine pyrophosphate. A large increase with the added cofactor confirms deficiency. Which of the following enzymes and pathways does this test measure?","options":["Transketolase, pentose phosphate pathway","Glutathione reductase, glutathione recycling","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, pentose phosphate pathway","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, citric acid cycle"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the thiamine-dependent enzymes: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase, and transketolase.","Red blood cells have no mitochondria, so the three mitochondrial dehydrogenases are absent.","That leaves transketolase in the nonoxidative pentose phosphate pathway."],"why":"Transketolase is a thiamine pyrophosphate-dependent enzyme in the nonoxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway, which red blood cells use. The erythrocyte transketolase activity test measures activity with and without added thiamine pyrophosphate. A large increase with added cofactor shows that the enzyme was short of thiamine, which confirms functional deficiency.","whyNot":["Correct.","Glutathione reductase uses FAD, so a similar activation test with added FAD assesses riboflavin (vitamin B2) status, not thiamine.","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is in the pentose phosphate pathway but uses NADP+, not thiamine pyrophosphate.","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase does need thiamine pyrophosphate, but it is mitochondrial and red blood cells have no mitochondria."],"study":{"topics":["Thiamine-dependent enzymes","Pentose phosphate pathway"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C30-Q3","stem":"His deficiency also reduces mitochondrial ATP production in his cardiomyocytes. In the cross-bridge cycle, which of the following steps is directly caused by ATP binding to the myosin head?","options":["Release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Shift of tropomyosin off the myosin-binding sites on actin","Power stroke that slides actin toward the sarcomere center","Release of the myosin head from actin"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Walk through the cross-bridge cycle step by step.","ATP binding detaches myosin from actin, ATP hydrolysis cocks the head, and phosphate release triggers the power stroke.","Calcium release and the tropomyosin shift come from excitation and calcium binding to troponin, not from ATP binding to myosin."],"why":"In the cross-bridge cycle, myosin stays tightly bound to actin until ATP binds the myosin head, which lowers its affinity for actin and releases it. Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and phosphate then re-cocks the head, and release of phosphate after rebinding triggers the power stroke. Without ATP, myosin stays locked to actin (rigor). ATP is also needed for SERCA to pump calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum so the muscle can relax, so an energy-starved heart struggles with both contraction and relaxation.","whyNot":["Calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum is triggered by calcium entry through L-type channels opening ryanodine receptors.","Tropomyosin moves off the myosin-binding sites when calcium binds troponin C.","The power stroke is triggered by release of inorganic phosphate after the cocked head binds actin, not by ATP binding.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle","Myocardial energy use"],"comps":["CV-5"]}},{"id":"CV-C30-Q4","stem":"His cardiac output is high, yet he has heart failure with edema. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the high-output state?","options":["Sympathetic vasoconstriction raising afterload and filling pressure","Peripheral vasodilation lowering systemic vascular resistance","A left-to-right shunt through an acquired septal defect","Increased blood viscosity from a rising hematocrit"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B","obj":"CV-18-B.6","dok":3,"approach":["Read the clues: warm extremities, bounding pulses, a low diastolic pressure, and a wide pulse pressure.","These point to low systemic vascular resistance.","Low resistance raises venous return and cardiac output, while the energy-poor heart and kidney sodium retention lead to congestion."],"why":"In wet beriberi, impaired oxidative metabolism in tissues is thought to cause local metabolic vasodilation, so peripheral arterioles dilate. Systemic vascular resistance falls, venous return and cardiac output rise, and the pulse pressure widens. The kidneys sense low effective arterial filling and retain sodium and water through the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. The heart, short of energy, cannot keep pace, so filling pressures rise and edema forms. Thiamine replacement usually reverses the high-output state quickly.","whyNot":["Sympathetic vasoconstriction raises resistance and lowers output. It describes low-output heart failure with cool, pale extremities.","Correct.","A left-to-right shunt raises pulmonary flow, not systemic output, and nothing in the case suggests a septal defect.","Higher blood viscosity raises resistance and would tend to lower cardiac output, and his hematocrit is not reported as high."],"study":{"topics":["High-output heart failure","Wet versus dry beriberi"],"comps":["CV-18-E1","CV-13","CV-11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C31","system":"CV","dx":"Malignant pericardial effusion from metastatic lung adenocarcinoma","codes":["CV-18-F1","CV-18-F2"],"case":"A 61-year-old woman who has never smoked comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of worsening shortness of breath, fatigue, and a dull sensation of fullness in her chest. Six months ago, biopsy of a right upper lobe lung mass showed adenocarcinoma. Pulse is 102/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows distant heart sounds. Systolic pressure falls by 6 mm Hg with inspiration (normal fall is under 10 mm Hg). An ECG shows low QRS voltage, and a chest x-ray shows an enlarged, water-bottle-shaped cardiac silhouette. Echocardiography shows a large circumferential pericardial effusion with no collapse of the right atrium or right ventricle. Pericardiocentesis drains 800 mL of bloody fluid. Diagnosis: malignant pericardial effusion from metastatic lung adenocarcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C31-Q1","stem":"Pericardiocentesis through the left fifth intercostal space immediately adjacent to the sternum is considered. Which of the following anatomic features allows a needle at this site to enter the pericardial sac without passing through the left lung?","options":["The oblique fissure, which separates the left upper and lower lobes here","The cardiac notch of the left lung, with a matching notch in the pleura","The costodiaphragmatic recess, which holds no lung during quiet breathing","The lingula, which curls behind the heart and out of the needle path"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Picture what lies under the left fifth intercostal space beside the sternum.","Recall that the anterior border of the left lung and its pleura curve away from the midline at the level of the fourth and fifth costal cartilages.","That gap leaves part of the pericardium covered only by the chest wall, so a needle can enter the sac without crossing lung."],"why":"The anterior border of the left lung bends laterally to form the cardiac notch, and the left pleural sac has a smaller notch in the same place. This leaves a small area of pericardium with no lung or pleura in front of it, sometimes called the bare area. A needle placed in the left fifth or sixth intercostal space close to the sternum can enter the pericardial sac here. Staying close to the sternum also matters because the internal thoracic artery runs just lateral to the sternal border.","whyNot":["The oblique fissure is a cleft between two lobes. A needle placed into a fissure still passes through pleura and lung tissue on either side.","Correct.","The costodiaphragmatic recess lies much lower, near the eighth to tenth ribs at the lateral chest wall, and it is still lined by parietal pleura. It is the target for draining a pleural effusion.","The lingula is the tongue-shaped lower part of the left upper lobe. It lies in front of the heart, below the cardiac notch, so it would be in the way of a needle aimed lower."],"study":{"topics":["Surface anatomy of the lungs and pleura","Pericardiocentesis approaches"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F2"]}},{"id":"CV-C31-Q2","stem":"Her 800-mL effusion developed without hypotension. In contrast, about 200 mL of blood accumulating within minutes after a penetrating cardiac wound can cause fatal tamponade. Which of the following properties of the pericardium best explains this difference?","options":["The fibrous pericardium stretches when filled slowly, keeping sac pressure low","The serous pericardium absorbs slowly forming fluid as fast as it is produced","The right ventricle thickens over weeks and resists compression from outside","The pericardium stiffens over several weeks and shields the chambers from fluid"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-F2","obj":"CV-18-F.4","dok":3,"approach":["Compare the two cases: a large volume tolerated versus a small volume that kills. The difference is the rate of filling.","Recall the pericardial pressure and volume curve: the tough fibrous pericardium is stiff over minutes but stretches over weeks.","Tamponade depends on the pressure inside the sac exceeding the filling pressures of the heart, and pressure is what slow filling keeps low."],"why":"The fibrous pericardium is dense collagen that barely stretches over minutes. When fluid enters quickly, pressure in the sac rises steeply, exceeds right heart filling pressure, and the chambers cannot fill. When fluid builds over weeks, the sac gradually enlarges, so pressure rises little even with a large volume. This is why a slowly growing malignant effusion can reach 1 L or more before tamponade, while acute hemopericardium of about 200 mL can be fatal.","whyNot":["Correct.","If the serous layer removed fluid as fast as it formed, no effusion would build up. Her 800 mL effusion shows that formation outpaced removal.","The right ventricle does not hypertrophy as a defense against outside pressure. Right ventricular hypertrophy follows pressure overload from inside, as in pulmonary hypertension.","A stiffer pericardium would raise sac pressure faster, making tamponade more likely. Stiffening and scarring describe constrictive pericarditis, which limits filling."],"study":{"topics":["Pericardial pressure and volume relationship","Rate of fluid accumulation and tamponade"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F2"]}},{"id":"CV-C31-Q3","stem":"Molecular testing of her tumor shows a small deletion in one allele of the EGFR gene. The deletion keeps the receptor tyrosine kinase active in the absence of bound growth factor. Which of the following best describes this mutation?","options":["Loss of function in a tumor suppressor gene, so both copies must be lost","Loss of function in a DNA mismatch repair gene, causing unstable repeats","Gain of function from a translocation that fuses two separate genes","Gain of function in a proto-oncogene, so one altered copy is enough"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the effect: the receptor is active without its ligand, so the change adds function.","Classify the gene: a normal gene whose overactivity drives cell growth is a proto-oncogene.","Recall that activating changes in proto-oncogenes act dominantly at the cell level, so one altered allele is enough."],"why":"EGFR is a receptor tyrosine kinase. Normally, binding of a growth factor causes the receptors to pair up and phosphorylate each other, which turns on growth signals such as the RAS and MAPK pathway. Small in-frame deletions in the kinase region keep the receptor active without ligand. Because the altered protein is overactive, one mutated copy drives growth even with a normal second copy. EGFR mutations are more common in lung adenocarcinomas of people who have never smoked.","whyNot":["Tumor suppressors such as TP53 and RB1 usually need both copies lost (the two-hit model). Her mutation adds activity to a growth receptor, which is the opposite effect.","Mismatch repair defects, as in Lynch syndrome, cause microsatellite instability. They do not switch on a single receptor directly.","A fusion gene from a chromosomal rearrangement, such as EML4 and ALK in some lung adenocarcinomas, also adds function. Her change is a small deletion within one gene.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Proto-oncogenes and tumor suppressor genes","Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-F1"]}},{"id":"CV-C31-Q4","stem":"Cytologic examination of the pericardial fluid shows clusters of large atypical cells, some forming small glands. Reactive mesothelial cells can have a similar appearance. Which of the following immunohistochemical staining patterns best supports metastatic lung adenocarcinoma?","options":["S-100 and HMB-45 positive, pan-cytokeratin negative","CK20 and CDX2 positive, CK7 negative","TTF-1 and napsin A positive, calretinin negative","Calretinin positive and TTF-1 negative"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F1","obj":"CV-18-F.6","dok":3,"approach":["Name the two cell types to separate: reactive mesothelium versus metastatic lung adenocarcinoma.","Recall the markers: TTF-1 and napsin A mark lung adenocarcinoma, while calretinin marks mesothelial cells.","Pick the pattern that is positive for the lung markers and negative for the mesothelial marker."],"why":"Lung and breast cancers are the most common sources of metastasis to the pericardium, and malignant effusions are often bloody. TTF-1 (thyroid transcription factor 1) and napsin A are expressed by most lung adenocarcinomas. Calretinin is a calcium-binding protein expressed by mesothelial cells and mesothelioma. A tumor cell population that is TTF-1 and napsin A positive and calretinin negative supports metastatic lung adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["S-100 and HMB-45 positivity with no cytokeratin points to melanoma, which can also spread to the pericardium.","CK20 and CDX2 positive with CK7 negative is the typical colorectal adenocarcinoma pattern. Lung adenocarcinoma is usually CK7 positive and CK20 negative.","Correct.","This pattern marks mesothelial cells. It fits reactive mesothelium or mesothelioma."],"study":{"topics":["Metastatic tumors of the pericardium","Immunohistochemistry of carcinoma versus mesothelium"],"comps":["CV-18-F1","CV-18-F2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C32","system":"CV","dx":"Tuberculous pericardial effusion progressing to cardiac tamponade","codes":["CV-18-F2","CV-18-F3"],"case":"A 38-year-old man with untreated HIV infection (CD4 count 140 cells/µL, reference 500 to 1500) is brought to the emergency department because of lightheadedness and shortness of breath for 2 days. He has had night sweats, weight loss, and cough for 6 weeks. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse is 124/min, and blood pressure is 86/64 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention to the angle of the jaw and muffled heart sounds. Systolic pressure falls by 18 mm Hg with each inspiration (normal fall is under 10 mm Hg). Echocardiography shows a large pericardial effusion with diastolic collapse of the right atrium and right ventricle. The drained fluid is an exudate with a high adenosine deaminase level, and culture later grows Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Diagnosis: tuberculous pericardial effusion progressing to cardiac tamponade.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C32-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains the large fall in his systolic pressure during each inspiration?","options":["Inspiration raises intrathoracic pressure and squeezes the venae cavae shut","Inspiration triggers vagal slowing that lowers cardiac output with each breath","Inspiration lowers right ventricular filling, so less blood reaches the lungs","Inspiration adds right ventricular filling that bows the septum leftward"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the sign: a fall in systolic pressure of more than 10 mm Hg on inspiration is pulsus paradoxus.","Recall normal breathing effects: inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure and increases venous return to the right heart.","Apply the fixed space: with the sac full, the right ventricle can only expand by pushing the septum into the left ventricle, which then fills less and ejects less."],"why":"Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, which draws more blood into the right atrium and right ventricle. Normally the free walls of the heart expand to take this volume. In tamponade the fluid-filled sac fixes the total volume of the heart, so the extra right ventricular filling bows the interventricular septum toward the left ventricle. Left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall, so systolic pressure drops more than 10 mm Hg. This ventricular interdependence is the main cause of pulsus paradoxus.","whyNot":["Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure. Raised intrathoracic pressure that limits venous return happens during a Valsalva maneuver or positive pressure ventilation.","Inspiration speeds the heart rate slightly by reducing vagal tone (respiratory sinus arrhythmia). Vagal slowing comes with expiration.","Inspiration increases right ventricular filling. The problem is that the extra right-sided volume crowds the left ventricle.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulsus paradoxus","Ventricular interdependence"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-F2"]}},{"id":"CV-C32-Q2","stem":"Before the fluid is drained, a right heart catheter is placed. Normal values are right atrial (RA) pressure 2 to 6 mm Hg and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure 6 to 12 mm Hg. Which of the following sets of pressures (mm Hg) is most likely to be found?","options":["RA 5, RV 42/6, wedge 26","RA 17, RV 64/17, wedge 10","RA 19, RV 34/19, wedge 19","RA 2, RV 18/2, wedge 3"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F2","obj":"CV-18-F.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that in tamponade the pressure in the sac squeezes every chamber equally during diastole.","Predict that right atrial pressure, right ventricular diastolic pressure, and wedge pressure all rise to about the same value as the pericardial pressure.","Pick the set with high, equal diastolic pressures on both sides of the heart."],"why":"In tamponade the chambers can only fill until their pressure matches the pressure in the pericardial sac. Because the fluid surrounds all four chambers, the diastolic pressures in the right atrium, right ventricle, and left atrium (measured as the wedge pressure) all rise and become nearly equal, often around 15 to 20 mm Hg. Equal, raised diastolic pressures across the heart are the classic catheter finding. Constrictive pericarditis also equalizes pressures, but the clinical picture of a large effusion and diastolic chamber collapse points to tamponade.","whyNot":["A high wedge pressure with a normal right atrial pressure points to left heart failure or mitral valve disease, with the left side congested and the right side spared.","A high right ventricular systolic pressure with a normal wedge pressure fits pulmonary hypertension or a pulmonary embolus with right heart strain. The left-sided filling pressure is not raised to match.","Correct.","Low pressures everywhere fit hypovolemia. His distended neck veins show that his right-sided pressure is high."],"study":{"topics":["Hemodynamics of cardiac tamponade","Right heart catheter pressures"],"comps":["CV-18-F2","CV-6"]}},{"id":"CV-C32-Q3","stem":"A pericardial biopsy specimen shows caseating granulomas. Which of the following cytokines, secreted by CD4+ T lymphocytes, activates his macrophages to kill this organism and form granulomas?","options":["Transforming growth factor beta","Interferon gamma","Interleukin 4","Interleukin 12"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the response: granulomas are a cell-mediated (type IV) response to an intracellular organism.","Identify the helper cell: Th1 CD4 T cells drive this response.","Name the Th1 cytokine that activates macrophages: interferon gamma."],"why":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis survives inside macrophages. Macrophages present its antigens and release interleukin 12, which pushes CD4 T cells toward the Th1 type. Th1 cells release interferon gamma, which activates macrophages to kill ingested bacilli and to transform into epithelioid cells and giant cells that form granulomas. With few CD4 cells, people with HIV form poor granulomas and are prone to spread of tuberculosis outside the lungs, including to the pericardium.","whyNot":["Transforming growth factor beta comes from regulatory T cells and other cells. It dampens immune responses and promotes fibrosis.","Correct.","Interleukin 4 comes from Th2 cells. It drives IgE class switching and allergic responses and does not activate macrophages to kill mycobacteria.","Interleukin 12 is made by macrophages and dendritic cells. It steers T cells toward Th1, but it comes from the macrophage side of the loop."],"study":{"topics":["Th1 response and granuloma formation","Tuberculosis in HIV infection"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C32-Q4","stem":"The organism stains poorly with Gram stain but retains carbol fuchsin after decolorization with acid-alcohol. Which of the following components of its cell envelope is responsible for this staining property?","options":["Polysaccharide capsule","Lipoteichoic acid","Lipopolysaccharide","Mycolic acid"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.7","dok":2,"approach":["Name the stain: holding carbol fuchsin after an acid-alcohol wash means the organism is acid-fast (Ziehl-Neelsen stain).","Recall what makes mycobacteria acid-fast: a waxy, lipid-rich cell wall.","Pick the long-chain fatty acid that forms that waxy layer."],"why":"Mycobacteria have a cell wall rich in mycolic acids, very long-chain fatty acids linked to arabinogalactan and peptidoglycan. This waxy layer keeps out the crystal violet and iodine of the Gram stain, so the organism stains poorly. Once carbol fuchsin is driven in with heat, the same layer holds the dye against acid-alcohol, which is what acid-fast means. Mycolic acids also help the organism resist drying and survive inside macrophages.","whyNot":["A polysaccharide capsule, as in Streptococcus pneumoniae, blocks phagocytosis. Capsules are shown with special stains and do not cause acid-fastness.","Lipoteichoic acid is found in Gram-positive cell walls. It anchors the wall to the membrane and triggers inflammation, and it does not make cells acid-fast.","Lipopolysaccharide is the endotoxin of the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria. Mycobacteria lack it.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acid-fast stain","Mycobacterial cell wall"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C33","system":"CV","dx":"Acute viral pericarditis (coxsackievirus B)","codes":["CV-18-F3"],"case":"A 24-year-old man comes to the physician because of sharp central chest pain for 2 days. The pain worsens when he lies supine or inspires deeply, improves when he sits up and leans forward, and radiates to the ridge of the left trapezius muscle. One week ago he had a sore throat, fever, and diarrhea, and his roommate had a similar illness. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F). Auscultation with the patient leaning forward shows a scratchy, three-component sound at the left sternal border. An ECG shows diffuse ST-segment elevation with PR depression. Serum troponin is within the reference range. Echocardiography shows a small pericardial effusion with normal wall motion. Stool PCR detects coxsackievirus B. Diagnosis: acute viral pericarditis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C33-Q1","stem":"Pain from his inflamed parietal pericardium is referred to the skin over the trapezius ridge. Which of the following nerves carries this pain?","options":["Phrenic nerve, from spinal segments C3 to C5","Upper thoracic sympathetic afferents, from T1 to T4","Vagus nerve, through its cardiac branches","Intercostobrachial nerve, from spinal segment T2"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the pain source: the parietal pericardium, which has sensory fibers, unlike the visceral layer.","Recall its nerve supply: the phrenic nerve, which runs between the fibrous pericardium and the mediastinal pleura.","Link segments to skin: C3 to C5 also supply the skin over the trapezius ridge and shoulder, so the brain maps the pain there."],"why":"The fibrous pericardium and the parietal layer of serous pericardium receive sensory fibers from the phrenic nerves, which arise from C3 to C5. The skin over the trapezius ridge is supplied by the same cervical segments through the supraclavicular nerves. Pain from the parietal pericardium is therefore referred to that area. Trapezius ridge pain is a useful clue that separates pericarditis from myocardial ischemia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Sympathetic afferents from T1 to T4 carry pain from the heart muscle itself. That explains ischemic pain referred to the chest wall and medial left arm.","The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers to the heart and visceral sensations such as stretch, which take part in reflexes. It does not carry the pain of pericarditis.","The intercostobrachial nerve supplies skin of the axilla and medial upper arm. It carries no fibers from the pericardium."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of the pericardium","Referred pain"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F3"]}},{"id":"CV-C33-Q2","stem":"His ECG shows ST-segment elevation in most leads, reflecting an injury current in the outer myocardium. Which of the following features of the heart wall best explains how pericardial inflammation affects the myocardium?","options":["The epicardium is the visceral serous layer and lies on the myocardium","The fibrous pericardium is fused to the myocardium with no space between them","The parietal serous layer is attached directly to the outer myocardium","The endocardium continues around the valve rings onto the outer heart surface"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.1","dok":3,"approach":["List the layers from outside in: fibrous pericardium, parietal serous layer, pericardial cavity, visceral serous layer (epicardium), myocardium, endocardium.","Notice which inflamed layer touches heart muscle: the visceral serous layer is the epicardium itself.","Conclude that inflammation of the epicardium spreads to the subepicardial myocardium, producing diffuse ST elevation."],"why":"The serous pericardium has two layers. The parietal layer lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium, and the visceral layer covers the heart as the epicardium. The epicardium is a layer of mesothelium over loose connective tissue and fat that carries the coronary vessels, and it sits directly on the myocardium. Inflammation of the epicardium irritates the outer myocardium beneath it. Because the whole surface is involved, ST elevation appears in most leads, not in one coronary territory.","whyNot":["Correct.","The fibrous pericardium is the outer sac. It is separated from the heart by the two serous layers and the pericardial cavity.","The parietal serous layer lines the fibrous pericardium. It faces the heart across the pericardial cavity and does not touch the myocardium.","The endocardium lines the inside of the chambers and covers the valves. It does not wrap onto the outer surface of the heart."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the heart wall","ECG changes in pericarditis"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C33-Q3","stem":"Purified RNA from this virus, without any viral proteins, can initiate infection when introduced into a cell. Which of the following features of the virus best explains this finding?","options":["Negative-sense single-stranded RNA genome packaged with its own polymerase","Double-stranded RNA genome split into segments within a nonenveloped capsid","Positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome that serves directly as mRNA","RNA genome that is copied into DNA by a reverse transcriptase it carries"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the virus: coxsackievirus B is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family.","Recall its genome: nonenveloped, positive-sense, single-stranded RNA.","Reason it out: host ribosomes can translate a positive-sense genome right away, making the viral polymerase, so naked RNA is infectious."],"why":"Picornaviruses such as coxsackievirus have a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome. It reads like mRNA, so host ribosomes translate it into one large polyprotein that is cut into viral proteins, including the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. Because the virus needs nothing from the virion to start, the purified RNA alone is infectious. Coxsackievirus B spreads by the fecal-oral route and is a leading cause of viral pericarditis and myocarditis.","whyNot":["Negative-sense RNA must first be copied into positive-sense mRNA by the polymerase carried in the virion, so the naked RNA alone cannot start infection. Influenza and measles viruses work this way.","Double-stranded RNA viruses such as rotavirus must bring their own polymerase to make mRNA, so their naked genome is not infectious.","Correct.","Retroviruses such as HIV need the reverse transcriptase packaged in the virion to make DNA, so their purified RNA alone does not start infection."],"study":{"topics":["Picornavirus genome and replication","Positive-sense and negative-sense RNA viruses"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-F3"]}},{"id":"CV-C33-Q4","stem":"The scratchy, three-component sound at his left sternal border is a pericardial friction rub. Which of the following types of exudate on the pericardial surfaces is most likely responsible for this sound?","options":["Purulent exudate packed with neutrophils and bacteria","Serous fluid with few cells that pools between the layers","Fibrinous exudate that roughens the serous surfaces","Caseous material from necrotizing granulomas"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F3","obj":"CV-18-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the usual pattern of viral pericarditis: fibrinous or serofibrinous inflammation.","Link the pattern to the sound: deposits of fibrin make the smooth serous surfaces rough.","Rough layers rubbing together with each heartbeat produce the scratchy rub."],"why":"Acute viral pericarditis usually produces a fibrinous or serofibrinous exudate. Fibrin deposits make the normally smooth, moist serous layers dry and shaggy, sometimes called bread and butter pericarditis. As the heart moves, these rough surfaces rub, producing a scratchy sound that can have three parts linked to atrial systole, ventricular systole, and early ventricular filling. The same fibrinous pattern is seen in pericarditis after a myocardial infarction and in uremia.","whyNot":["Purulent pericarditis follows bacterial spread, for example from pneumonia or a chest wound. It fits a very ill patient with high fever, and his course and virus test point to a viral cause.","Thin serous fluid separates the layers and keeps them smooth. A large serous effusion tends to muffle heart sounds and can make a rub fade.","Correct.","Caseous pericarditis comes from tuberculosis or fungal infection and develops over weeks. His acute illness after a viral prodrome does not fit."],"study":{"topics":["Morphologic types of pericarditis","Pericardial friction rub"],"comps":["CV-18-F3","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C34","system":"CV","dx":"Bicuspid aortic valve with coarctation of the aorta in Turner syndrome","codes":["CV-18-G1"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of short stature and absent breast development. Her height is 142 cm. Physical examination shows a broad chest with widely spaced nipples and a low posterior hairline. Blood pressure is 152/88 mm Hg in the right arm and 104/70 mm Hg in the right leg (normally the leg pressure equals or exceeds the arm pressure). Femoral pulses are weak and delayed. Auscultation shows an ejection click and a soft midsystolic murmur at the right upper sternal border and a separate systolic murmur over the left upper back. Karyotype is 45,X. Echocardiography shows a bicuspid aortic valve with mild stenosis, a mildly dilated ascending aorta, and coarctation of the aorta just distal to the left subclavian artery. Diagnosis: bicuspid aortic valve with coarctation of the aorta in Turner syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C34-Q1","stem":"In a 46,XX female, most genes on one X chromosome are inactivated in each cell. Which of the following best explains why loss of the second X chromosome causes this patient's short stature?","options":["Her single X is always paternal, and imprinting silences its growth genes","Some X genes, such as SHOX, escape inactivation and need two working copies","Her cells inactivate the single remaining X, so no X genes are expressed","Her remaining X carries an expanded trinucleotide repeat that silences growth genes"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the puzzle: if one X is normally silenced, a female with one X should match a 46,XX female.","Resolve it: some X genes escape inactivation, including those in the pseudoautosomal region shared with Y, so normal cells use two active copies.","Apply it: with one X, these genes are expressed at half dose. Half a dose of SHOX causes short stature."],"why":"X inactivation silences most of one X chromosome in each female cell, but a set of genes escapes it. These include genes in the pseudoautosomal regions, which are shared with the Y chromosome so that males also carry two copies. SHOX lies in pseudoautosomal region 1 and is needed in two copies for normal long bone growth. A 45,X female has only one copy, and this haploinsufficiency explains much of the short stature in Turner syndrome. Loss of other escaping genes is thought to contribute to the other features.","whyNot":["In most cases of Turner syndrome the single X comes from the mother, because the paternal sex chromosome is the one lost. Imprinting does not explain the short stature.","Correct.","A cell with one X keeps it active. X inactivation leaves one active X in every cell, and a cell with no active X could not survive.","Trinucleotide repeat expansion causes disorders such as fragile X syndrome and Huntington disease. Turner syndrome is a loss of a whole chromosome."],"study":{"topics":["X inactivation and genes that escape it","Turner syndrome genetics"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-G1"]}},{"id":"CV-C34-Q2","stem":"A chest x-ray shows notching of the inferior borders of several posterior ribs, caused by enlarged collateral arteries that bypass the narrowed segment. Which of the following collateral pathways is responsible for the rib notching?","options":["Vertebral arteries joining to form the basilar artery","Internal thoracic arteries to the intercostal arteries","Celiac trunk to the superior mesenteric artery through pancreatic arcades","Superior epigastric arteries to the inferior epigastric arteries"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the block: the coarctation lies just distal to the left subclavian artery, so the subclavian arteries are proximal to it and the descending aorta is distal.","Find a bridge: the internal thoracic artery (a subclavian branch) feeds anterior intercostal arteries, which connect to posterior intercostal arteries from the descending aorta.","Link to the x-ray: the enlarged, tortuous posterior intercostal arteries run in the costal grooves and erode the lower rib borders."],"why":"With coarctation distal to the left subclavian artery, blood reaches the lower body by detouring around the narrowing. The internal thoracic arteries arise from the subclavian arteries and give off anterior intercostal arteries. These connect with the posterior intercostal arteries, which carry blood backward into the descending aorta beyond the coarctation. The posterior intercostal arteries enlarge and press on the costal grooves on the lower edge of the ribs, producing notching. The first two ribs are usually spared because their intercostal arteries come from the costocervical trunk.","whyNot":["The vertebral arteries arise from the subclavian arteries proximal to the coarctation and supply the brain. Their junction as the basilar artery is normal anatomy and does not bypass the aorta.","Correct.","Pancreaticoduodenal arcades connect the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery. They enlarge with narrowing of one of those vessels and lie in the abdomen.","The internal thoracic artery also continues as the superior epigastric artery, which joins the inferior epigastric artery in the rectus sheath. This collateral carries blood to the lower body but runs in the abdominal wall, so it does not notch ribs."],"study":{"topics":["Collateral circulation in coarctation of the aorta","Intercostal arteries"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-G1"]}},{"id":"CV-C34-Q3","stem":"If her aortic stenosis worsens over the years, her left ventricle will face a higher systolic pressure, and its wall will thicken while the chamber size stays normal. Which of the following relationships best explains why this thickening benefits the ventricle?","options":["Fick principle: flow equals oxygen uptake divided by arteriovenous O2 difference","Poiseuille law: resistance to flow rises steeply as the radius of a tube falls","Frank-Starling law: more stretch of the fibers raises the force of contraction","Law of Laplace: wall stress rises with pressure and radius, falls with thickness"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the load: aortic stenosis causes pressure overload of the left ventricle.","Recall the law that links pressure, chamber size, and wall thickness: Laplace, wall stress equals pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness.","Apply it: a thicker wall brings stress back toward normal, which is why pressure overload produces concentric hypertrophy."],"why":"By the law of Laplace, wall stress is about pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. When systolic pressure rises, as with aortic stenosis or coarctation, stress on the wall rises. Adding sarcomeres in parallel thickens the wall and brings stress back toward normal without enlarging the chamber, a pattern called concentric hypertrophy. The cost is a stiffer ventricle with impaired filling and a higher oxygen demand.","whyNot":["The Fick principle is used to measure cardiac output or organ blood flow from oxygen uptake. It says nothing about wall stress.","Poiseuille law explains why a narrow valve or vessel greatly increases resistance to flow. It explains why the stenosis raises pressure, but it does not explain the response of the ventricular wall.","The Frank-Starling law links end-diastolic volume to stroke volume. It explains beat-to-beat matching of output to filling and does not explain why a thicker wall lowers stress.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace","Concentric and eccentric hypertrophy"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-D1"]}},{"id":"CV-C34-Q4","stem":"A dilated ascending aorta is common with a bicuspid aortic valve and increases the later risk of aneurysm and dissection. The main abnormality lies in the layer that normally gives the aorta its elastic recoil. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on histologic examination of this layer?","options":["Fragmented elastic lamellae and loss of smooth muscle in the media","Granulomas with giant cells surrounding a fragmented elastic lamina","Lipid-filled foam cells under a fibrous cap in the intima","Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum in the adventitia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-G1","obj":"CV-18-G.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the structure of an elastic artery: the media of the aorta holds many layers of elastic lamellae with smooth muscle between them.","Identify the lesion of bicuspid valve aortopathy: noninflammatory medial degeneration.","Pick the option that describes loss of elastic tissue and smooth muscle in the media with no inflammation."],"why":"The aorta is an elastic artery. Its thick media contains dozens of concentric elastic lamellae with smooth muscle cells and matrix between them, which let it stretch in systole and recoil in diastole. In aortas linked to a bicuspid valve, as in Marfan syndrome, the media shows medial degeneration: elastic lamellae fragment, smooth muscle cells are lost, and pools of proteoglycan-rich matrix collect. The weakened wall dilates and can tear, causing dissection.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomatous inflammation with giant cells near the internal elastic lamina describes giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis. Her aortic dilation has no inflammation.","Foam cells and a fibrous cap in the intima describe an atherosclerotic plaque. Atherosclerosis mainly affects the abdominal aorta and is the main cause of abdominal aortic aneurysm.","Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum is the lesion of tertiary syphilis. It starves the media of blood and gives a tree-bark appearance to the aortic root."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of elastic arteries","Medial degeneration of the aorta"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-I2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C35","system":"CV","dx":"Patent ductus arteriosus with congenital rubella syndrome","codes":["CV-18-G2"],"case":"A 3-month-old girl is brought to the physician because of poor feeding, sweating during feeds, and poor weight gain. Her mother was never vaccinated and had a fever and a fine rash during the first trimester. The infant has bilateral cataracts and failed her newborn hearing screen. Pulse is 168/min (normal about 100 to 160), and blood pressure is 82/34 mm Hg, a wide pulse pressure for her age. Peripheral pulses are bounding. A continuous, machine-like murmur is heard best below the left clavicle. Echocardiography shows a large patent ductus arteriosus with left-to-right flow and an enlarged left atrium and left ventricle. Diagnosis: patent ductus arteriosus with congenital rubella syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C35-Q1","stem":"Serologic testing for rubella is performed. Which of the following results best indicates that the infant was infected in utero rather than carrying antibody passed from her mother?","options":["Rubella IgG in her serum at the same level as her mother's","Rubella IgA in her mother's breast milk","Rubella-specific IgM in her own serum","Rubella IgG in her serum that falls steadily over her first 6 months"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-G2","obj":"CV-18-G.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall which antibody class crosses the placenta: only IgG, through neonatal Fc receptors.","Reason that any IgM in the infant must have been made by the infant herself.","Conclude that rubella-specific IgM in her serum shows her own immune response to infection."],"why":"IgG is the only antibody class carried across the placenta, so every newborn whose mother is immune has maternal rubella IgG. IgM is a large pentamer that does not cross. Rubella-specific IgM in an infant's serum therefore means the infant's own B cells responded to the virus, which confirms congenital infection. Rubella IgG that persists beyond the age when maternal IgG should have disappeared, roughly 6 to 12 months, also supports the diagnosis.","whyNot":["IgG equal to the mother's level is what passive placental transfer produces. It cannot separate infection from maternal antibody.","IgA in breast milk is made by the mother and protects the infant's gut. It says nothing about whether the infant was infected.","Correct.","A steady fall in IgG over the first months is the expected decay of maternal antibody (half-life about 3 to 4 weeks). It points away from the infant making her own antibody."],"study":{"topics":["Placental transfer of IgG","Diagnosing congenital infection"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C35-Q2","stem":"During surgical ligation of the ductus, the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which curves under the aortic arch beside the ductus, is at risk. The ductus and the low course of this nerve on the left are both explained by the same pharyngeal arch artery. Which of the following arch arteries is it?","options":["Left second arch artery","Left sixth arch artery","Left fourth arch artery","Left third arch artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the derivatives: the sixth arch arteries form the proximal pulmonary arteries, and the distal part of the left sixth forms the ductus arteriosus.","Recall that the recurrent laryngeal nerves are sixth arch nerves that hook under the sixth arch arteries as the heart descends.","On the left the distal sixth artery persists as the ductus, so the nerve stays hooked under it beside the aortic arch."],"why":"The recurrent laryngeal nerves supply the muscles of the sixth pharyngeal arch and loop beneath the sixth arch arteries. On the right, the distal sixth arch artery and the fifth disappear, so the right nerve moves up and hooks around the right subclavian artery, a fourth arch derivative. On the left, the distal sixth arch artery persists as the ductus arteriosus, which becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. The left nerve therefore stays hooked under the aortic arch next to the ductus, where it can be injured in ductus surgery.","whyNot":["The second arch artery mostly regresses and leaves small remnants such as the stapedial artery. It does not form the ductus.","Correct.","The left fourth arch artery forms part of the aortic arch. The left nerve passes under the arch, but its low course is set by the sixth arch artery, the ductus.","The third arch arteries form the common carotid arteries and the start of the internal carotid arteries. They are unrelated to the ductus."],"study":{"topics":["Pharyngeal arch artery derivatives","Course of the recurrent laryngeal nerves"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C35-Q3","stem":"Before birth, patency of the ductus is maintained by prostaglandin E2, produced by both the placenta and the ductus wall. Which of the following precursors and enzymes produce prostaglandin E2?","options":["Arachidonic acid, by 5-lipoxygenase","Sphingosine, by sphingosine kinase","Cholesterol, by side-chain cleavage enzyme","Arachidonic acid, by cyclooxygenase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the molecule: prostaglandins are eicosanoids, 20-carbon signals made from a membrane fatty acid.","Name the precursor: arachidonic acid, released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2.","Split the pathways: cyclooxygenase makes prostaglandins and thromboxanes, while 5-lipoxygenase makes leukotrienes."],"why":"Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid, a 20-carbon omega-6 fatty acid, from membrane phospholipids. Cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) turns it into prostaglandin H2, which other enzymes convert to prostaglandin E2, prostacyclin, and thromboxane A2. PGE2 relaxes the smooth muscle of the ductus. After birth the placental source is gone and the lungs break down circulating PGE2, so the level falls and the ductus constricts. This is why COX inhibitors can close a patent ductus and why PGE1 is given to keep it open in duct-dependent heart defects.","whyNot":["5-lipoxygenase turns arachidonic acid into leukotrienes, which cause bronchoconstriction and attract neutrophils. It does not make prostaglandins.","Sphingosine kinase makes sphingosine 1-phosphate, a lipid signal that guides lymphocyte movement. It is unrelated to prostaglandin synthesis.","Side-chain cleavage of cholesterol is the first step in steroid hormone synthesis. Prostaglandins are made from a fatty acid.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Eicosanoid synthesis","Prostaglandins and the ductus arteriosus"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-G2"]}},{"id":"CV-C35-Q4","stem":"Suppose her ductus is never closed. Years later, pulmonary vascular resistance rises until flow through the ductus reverses (Eisenmenger syndrome). Her toes become cyanotic and clubbed while her fingers remain pink. Which of the following anatomic facts best explains this pattern?","options":["The ductus joins the aorta at its root, before the coronary arteries arise","The ductus joins the aorta just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery","The ductus joins the arch between the brachiocephalic trunk and the left common carotid","The ductus joins the right subclavian artery close to where that vessel begins"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-G2","obj":"CV-18-G.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the shunt: with reversed flow, deoxygenated blood moves from the pulmonary artery into the aorta through the ductus.","Locate where it enters: the ductus joins the aorta just beyond the origin of the left subclavian artery.","Trace the distribution: branches that leave the aorta before that point (to the head and arms) carry oxygenated blood, while the descending aorta carries mixed blood to the lower body."],"why":"The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk near its bifurcation to the aorta just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery. When pulmonary pressure exceeds aortic pressure, deoxygenated blood enters the aorta at this point. The brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, and left subclavian arteries have already branched off and still carry well-oxygenated blood to the head and arms. The descending aorta carries the mixed blood to the legs, so cyanosis and clubbing appear in the toes but not the fingers (differential cyanosis).","whyNot":["Entry at the aortic root would send deoxygenated blood to every branch, including the coronary arteries, causing cyanosis of both hands and feet.","Correct.","Entry between the brachiocephalic trunk and the left common carotid would also darken the left head and arm. The ductus enters further along, past all three arch branches.","The ductus does not connect to the right subclavian artery. If it did, the right arm would turn blue."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the aortic arch","Eisenmenger syndrome and differential cyanosis"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-G2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C36","system":"CV","dx":"Ostium secundum atrial septal defect in Holt-Oram syndrome","codes":["CV-18-G3"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of shortness of breath when climbing stairs and recent episodes of palpitations. Her father and brother have similar thumb abnormalities, and her father had a congenital heart defect repaired as a child. Physical examination shows bilateral hypoplastic thumbs. The second heart sound is widely split and does not vary with respiration. A soft midsystolic murmur is heard at the left upper sternal border. An ECG shows incomplete right bundle branch block. Echocardiography shows a 14-mm defect in the center of the atrial septum at the fossa ovalis with left-to-right flow, an enlarged right atrium and right ventricle, and a pulmonary-to-systemic flow ratio (Qp:Qs) of 2.1 (normal 1.0). Genetic testing shows a TBX5 mutation. Diagnosis: ostium secundum atrial septal defect in Holt-Oram syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C36-Q1","stem":"She asks about the risk to her future children. Based on her family history, which of the following is the inheritance pattern of her condition and the risk to each child?","options":["Autosomal dominant, 50% for each child","X-linked dominant, 50% for each child","Mitochondrial, nearly 100% for each child","Autosomal recessive, 25% for each child"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.5","dok":3,"approach":["Read the pedigree: affected people appear in two generations, and her father passed it to a son (her brother).","Rule out patterns: father-to-son transmission excludes X-linked inheritance, and a father cannot pass on mitochondrial DNA.","Conclude autosomal dominant: an affected parent passes the mutant allele to each child with a 50% chance."],"why":"Holt-Oram syndrome is autosomal dominant and is caused by mutations in TBX5, a transcription factor needed for development of the upper limb and the heart. Her family shows affected people in each generation and transmission from father to son, which fits autosomal dominant inheritance. Each child of an affected parent has a 50% chance of inheriting the mutant allele. Severity can vary within a family, so an affected child may have different heart or limb findings from hers.","whyNot":["Correct.","A father passes his X chromosome only to daughters, so father-to-son transmission rules out any X-linked pattern.","Mitochondria are passed down through the egg, so an affected father cannot pass on a mitochondrial disorder.","Autosomal recessive traits usually appear in siblings of unaffected carrier parents, not in parent and child across generations. Her child would be at risk only if her partner were also a carrier."],"study":{"topics":["Pedigree analysis","Holt-Oram syndrome"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C36-Q2","stem":"A secundum defect forms when the flap that should cover the foramen ovale is too short, often from excessive resorption. Which of the following embryonic structures normally forms this flap, the floor of the fossa ovalis?","options":["Septum secundum","Sinus venosus","Endocardial cushions","Septum primum"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the two atrial septa: the thin septum primum grows first, and the thick septum secundum grows to its right.","Recall the opening: the foramen ovale is a gap in the septum secundum that the septum primum covers from the left like a flap valve.","Link to the adult heart: the septum primum becomes the floor of the fossa ovalis, and the septum secundum forms its rim (limbus)."],"why":"The septum primum grows down toward the endocardial cushions. Before it fuses, holes appear in its upper part and merge into the ostium secundum. The thicker septum secundum then grows on its right side but leaves an opening, the foramen ovale. The septum primum acts as a flap valve over this opening, letting blood pass from right to left in the fetus. After birth, rising left atrial pressure presses the flap against the septum secundum, and it becomes the floor of the fossa ovalis. If the septum primum is resorbed too much, the flap is too short and a secundum defect remains.","whyNot":["The septum secundum forms the thick upper rim (limbus) of the fossa ovalis. A small septum secundum can also cause a secundum defect, but it is not the flap.","The sinus venosus forms the smooth part of the right atrium. A defect near the opening of the superior vena cava is a sinus venosus defect, which lies outside the fossa ovalis.","The endocardial cushions close the ostium primum and form part of the atrioventricular valves. Failure here causes an ostium primum defect or an atrioventricular septal defect, often in Down syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Atrial septation","Types of atrial septal defect"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C36-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her second heart sound is widely split and does not vary with respiration?","options":["Shunt flow keeps right ventricular volume about equal in both breathing phases","Delayed left ventricular activation makes the aortic valve close after the pulmonic valve","High pulmonary artery pressure makes the pulmonic valve close early with every beat","Right bundle branch block delays right ventricular contraction only on inspiration"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall normal splitting: inspiration increases venous return to the right heart, delaying pulmonic valve closure, so the split widens.","Add the defect: the large left-to-right shunt keeps the right ventricle loaded, so pulmonic closure is late in every beat.","Explain the fixed part: when inspiration brings more systemic venous blood to the right atrium, less blood crosses from the left atrium, so right ventricular volume barely changes."],"why":"In an atrial septal defect the two atria act as one shared chamber. Shunted blood adds to right ventricular volume, prolonging right ventricular ejection and delaying pulmonic valve closure, so S2 is widely split. During inspiration, more systemic venous blood returns to the right atrium, but this raises right atrial pressure slightly and reduces the left-to-right flow across the defect. During expiration the shunt increases again. Right ventricular filling stays about the same through the breathing cycle, so the split is fixed.","whyNot":["Correct.","Delayed left ventricular activation, as in left bundle branch block, delays aortic closure and causes paradoxical splitting, which narrows on inspiration.","High pulmonary artery pressure makes P2 loud. It does not explain a split that stays wide and fixed, and her shunt flow is still left to right.","Right bundle branch block widens the split, but the split still increases on inspiration because the breathing effect on venous return remains. Block affects every beat, not only inspiration."],"study":{"topics":["Splitting of S2","Physiology of atrial septal defect"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C36-Q4","stem":"Before her defect is closed, she develops a deep vein thrombosis of the calf. Immediately after straining to lift a heavy box, she has sudden weakness of the right face and arm from an embolic stroke. Which of the following events best explains how a venous thrombus reached her brain?","options":["Left atrial pressure briefly rose above right atrial pressure","Pulmonary capillaries widened enough for the clot to pass through","Right atrial pressure briefly rose above left atrial pressure","The clot crossed from the pulmonary artery to the aorta by a ductus"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-G3","obj":"CV-18-G.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the event: a venous clot causing a stroke is a paradoxical embolism, which needs a path from the right heart to the left heart.","Recall the usual direction: left atrial pressure is normally higher, so her shunt runs left to right.","Apply the trigger: straining, then releasing it, sends a surge of venous blood to the right atrium, so right atrial pressure briefly exceeds left atrial pressure and the clot crosses the defect."],"why":"Normally left atrial pressure is a few mm Hg higher than right atrial pressure, so flow across an atrial septal defect is left to right. Straining against a closed glottis (a Valsalva maneuver) holds back venous return, and when the strain is released, pooled venous blood rushes into the right atrium. For a few beats right atrial pressure exceeds left atrial pressure, and blood, along with any clot it carries, crosses into the left atrium and the systemic arteries. Coughing can have the same effect. This is a paradoxical embolism.","whyNot":["Left atrial pressure higher than right atrial pressure is the usual state and drives left-to-right flow. It would carry blood away from the systemic side, not bring a venous clot to it.","Pulmonary capillaries are about 5 to 10 µm wide and trap venous clots, which is how pulmonary emboli form. They do not widen enough to let a clot through.","Correct.","Her echocardiogram shows an atrial septal defect only. A ductus is a different shunt, and she has no evidence of one."],"study":{"topics":["Paradoxical embolism","Atrial pressures and shunt direction"],"comps":["CV-18-G3","CV-18-H1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C37","system":"CV","dx":"Tetralogy of Fallot","codes":["CV-18-G4"],"case":"A 4-month-old boy is brought to a pediatric clinic because his lips turn blue when he feeds or cries. During one episode he became limp and deeply cyanotic, then improved when his mother held his knees to his chest. Physical examination shows a harsh systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border and a single second heart sound. Oxygen saturation is 82% (95 to 100%) on room air. Chest x-ray shows a boot-shaped heart with decreased pulmonary vascular markings. Echocardiography shows a large ventricular septal defect, an aorta that overrides the septum, narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract, and right ventricular hypertrophy. The diagnosis is tetralogy of Fallot.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C37-Q1","stem":"The four structural findings on this infant's echocardiogram trace back to one error during septation of the outflow tract. Which of the following developmental errors best explains all four findings?","options":["Failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse","Failure of the conotruncal ridges to spiral","Excess resorption of the septum primum","Anterosuperior shift of the outflow septum"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["List the four findings: VSD, overriding aorta, right ventricular outflow narrowing, right ventricular hypertrophy.","Ask which single shift could narrow the right outflow and leave the aorta sitting over a septal gap.","Match that to displacement of the infundibular (outflow) septum toward the right ventricular side."],"why":"In tetralogy of Fallot the infundibular (outflow) septum, built largely from neural crest derived conotruncal tissue, is displaced anteriorly and superiorly. This narrows the right ventricular outflow tract and leaves a malaligned ventricular septal defect with the aorta riding over it. Right ventricular hypertrophy follows because the ventricle pumps against the outflow obstruction. One displacement therefore explains all four findings.","whyNot":["Endocardial cushion fusion failure produces atrioventricular septal defects (ostium primum ASD, inlet VSD, a common AV valve), classically in trisomy 21. It does not narrow the outflow tract.","Failure of the conotruncal ridges to spiral produces transposition of the great arteries, where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk from the left.","Excess resorption of the septum primum leaves a secundum atrial septal defect, which causes a left-to-right shunt at the atrial level and no outflow obstruction.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Outflow tract septation","Conotruncal defects"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G4"]}},{"id":"CV-C37-Q2","stem":"A different newborn has tetralogy of Fallot with complete pulmonary atresia. On day 2 of life his cyanosis suddenly worsens because a fetal channel is closing. That channel develops from which embryonic aortic arch?","options":["Left fourth aortic arch","Right fourth aortic arch","Left sixth aortic arch","Third aortic arch"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.7","dok":3,"approach":["With pulmonary atresia, no blood leaves the right ventricle for the lungs, so lung flow must come from the aorta.","The fetal channel that carries aortic blood to the pulmonary artery is the ductus arteriosus, which closes in the first days of life.","Recall that the ductus arteriosus is the distal part of the left sixth aortic arch."],"why":"When the pulmonary valve is atretic, all pulmonary blood flow in the newborn depends on the ductus arteriosus carrying blood from the aorta into the pulmonary artery. The ductus begins to constrict within hours of birth and usually closes functionally over the first few days as arterial oxygen rises and prostaglandin E2 falls, so lung flow drops and cyanosis worsens. The ductus arteriosus forms from the distal part of the left sixth aortic arch, while the proximal sixth arches form the proximal pulmonary arteries.","whyNot":["The left fourth arch forms part of the adult arch of the aorta. It does not form a channel that closes after birth.","The right fourth arch forms the proximal right subclavian artery, a permanent vessel that does not close after birth.","Correct.","The third arches form the common carotid arteries and the first part of the internal carotid arteries. They have no role in neonatal pulmonary flow."],"study":{"topics":["Aortic arch derivatives","Ductus-dependent pulmonary flow"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G2"]}},{"id":"CV-C37-Q3","stem":"Further testing of the 4-month-old shows a serum calcium of 6.9 mg/dL (8.8 to 10.8 mg/dL), a low parathyroid hormone level, and no thymic shadow on chest x-ray. Which genetic finding is most likely?","options":["Trisomy 21 on karyotype","Microdeletion at 22q11.2","Microdeletion at 7q11.23","Monosomy X on karyotype"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.5","dok":2,"approach":["Group the findings: a conotruncal heart defect, low calcium from low parathyroid hormone, and an absent thymus.","Link parathyroid and thymus loss to failed development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches.","Name the deletion syndrome that combines pouch defects with conotruncal defects."],"why":"A 22q11.2 microdeletion (DiGeorge syndrome) disrupts neural crest migration and development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches. The pouches form the parathyroid glands and thymus, so infants have hypocalcemia from hypoparathyroidism and T-cell deficiency from thymic hypoplasia. Neural crest cells also build the conotruncal septum, so tetralogy of Fallot and truncus arteriosus are common. The deletion is found by FISH or chromosomal microarray, since it is too small to see on a routine karyotype.","whyNot":["Trisomy 21 is most strongly linked to atrioventricular septal defects. It does not cause hypoparathyroidism or an absent thymus.","Correct.","A 7q11.23 deletion causes Williams syndrome, with supravalvular aortic stenosis and high, not low, calcium.","Monosomy X (Turner syndrome) is linked to coarctation of the aorta and bicuspid aortic valve, with normal parathyroid and thymic development."],"study":{"topics":["22q11.2 deletion syndrome","Pharyngeal pouch derivatives"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-1"]}},{"id":"CV-C37-Q4","stem":"During the spell described in the case, his color improved when his knees were held to his chest. Which of the following best explains this improvement?","options":["It lowered outflow resistance in the narrowed right ventricle","It raised systemic resistance and cut the right-to-left shunt","It reduced venous return and lengthened ventricular filling","It lowered pulmonary resistance and drew blood to the lungs"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-G4","obj":"CV-18-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["Shunt direction across the VSD depends on the balance between right ventricular outflow resistance and systemic vascular resistance.","Flexing the legs compresses the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance.","Higher systemic resistance pushes less blood right to left and sends more right ventricular output through the pulmonary valve."],"why":"In tetralogy of Fallot, blood leaving the right ventricle chooses between the narrowed outflow tract and the VSD into the aorta. When systemic vascular resistance falls or outflow spasm worsens, more deoxygenated blood shunts right to left and a hypercyanotic spell follows. Pressing the knees to the chest (the infant version of squatting) kinks the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance, which reduces the right-to-left shunt and sends more blood to the lungs. It also increases venous return to the right heart.","whyNot":["Knee-chest positioning does not relax the infundibular obstruction. Drugs such as beta blockers are used to reduce infundibular spasm.","Correct.","Squatting and knee-chest positioning increase venous return from the legs. Reduced venous return would lower right ventricular output to the lungs and would not help.","Knee-chest positioning acts on the systemic side. Pulmonary vascular resistance is already low here, and the fixed outflow narrowing, not pulmonary vascular tone, limits lung flow."],"study":{"topics":["Tet spells and squatting","Shunt direction and vascular resistance"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-G4"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C38","system":"CV","dx":"Systemic embolism to the superior mesenteric artery from a left atrial thrombus","codes":["CV-18-H1"],"case":"A 76-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department with sudden, severe periumbilical pain that began 3 hours ago. She has atrial fibrillation and stopped taking her anticoagulant 2 months ago. Pulse is 118/min and irregularly irregular, and blood pressure is 142/86 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a soft, mildly tender abdomen, and the pain is out of proportion to these findings. Serum lactate is 5.8 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). CT angiography shows an embolus blocking the superior mesenteric artery a few centimeters beyond its origin from the aorta. Echocardiography shows remaining thrombus in the left atrial appendage. The diagnosis is acute mesenteric ischemia from a cardiac embolus.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C38-Q1","stem":"Given the site where the embolus has lodged, which of the following segments of bowel is most likely to keep its normal arterial supply?","options":["Sigmoid colon","Terminal ileum","Ascending colon","Cecum"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.1","dok":3,"approach":["An embolus in the proximal superior mesenteric artery (SMA) cuts off the branches that arise beyond it.","List the SMA territory: jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and about the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon.","Pick the segment supplied by a different trunk, the inferior mesenteric artery."],"why":"The SMA supplies midgut structures: the small bowel beyond the duodenum, the cecum and appendix, the ascending colon, and about the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. The sigmoid colon is hindgut and receives blood from the sigmoid branches of the inferior mesenteric artery, so an SMA embolus leaves it perfused. Knowing the boundary between midgut and hindgut supply predicts which bowel will be ischemic.","whyNot":["Correct.","The terminal ileum is supplied by the ileocolic artery, a branch of the SMA, so it is at high risk here.","The ascending colon is supplied by the right colic and ileocolic branches of the SMA, so it is at risk. It would stay perfused if the inferior mesenteric artery were the vessel blocked.","The cecum is supplied by the ileocolic artery from the SMA and is at risk with this embolus."],"study":{"topics":["Mesenteric arterial territories","Midgut versus hindgut supply"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H1"]}},{"id":"CV-C38-Q2","stem":"Which of the following factors is mainly responsible for formation of the thrombus that later broke off and became her embolus?","options":["Endothelial injury from a jet of regurgitant blood","Hyperviscosity from an increased red cell mass","Antibodies against phospholipid-binding proteins","Stasis of blood in the poorly emptying appendage"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H1","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the source: a left atrial appendage thrombus in a patient with atrial fibrillation.","Apply Virchow triad and ask which arm atrial fibrillation disturbs.","Loss of organized atrial contraction leaves blood pooling in the appendage, which is stasis."],"why":"In atrial fibrillation the atria quiver and no longer contract in an organized way. Blood pools in the left atrial appendage, a blind pouch, and stasis lets clotting factors accumulate and keeps platelets in contact with the wall. A thrombus forms there and pieces can break off into the systemic circulation, where they lodge in the brain, gut, kidneys, spleen, or limbs. This is why anticoagulation is used in atrial fibrillation to prevent stroke and other systemic emboli.","whyNot":["Jet lesions from regurgitant valves injure endothelium, but nothing in the case points to valve disease. Endothelial injury is the main factor in arterial thrombosis on a plaque.","Hyperviscosity from polycythemia can promote thrombosis, but nothing in her case suggests a raised red cell mass. Her atrial fibrillation explains the thrombus.","Antiphospholipid syndrome causes arterial and venous thrombi, but nothing in the case suggests it, such as lupus, pregnancy losses, or a prolonged PTT. Atrial fibrillation fully explains her thrombus.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Virchow triad","Atrial fibrillation and systemic emboli"],"comps":["CV-18-H1","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C38-Q3","stem":"Her increased lactate comes from ischemic intestinal cells that keep making ATP through glycolysis without oxygen. Which of the following reactions allows glycolysis to continue in these cells?","options":["Oxidation of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA in mitochondria","Conversion of lactate back to glucose in hepatocytes","Reduction of pyruvate to lactate, regenerating NAD+","Transamination of pyruvate to alanine by alanine aminotransferase"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.4","dok":3,"approach":["Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step.","Without oxygen, the electron transport chain cannot reoxidize NADH.","Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and returns NAD+ so glycolysis can keep running."],"why":"Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase uses NAD+ and produces NADH. Normally mitochondria reoxidize NADH through the electron transport chain, but that requires oxygen. In ischemic tissue, lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and converts NADH back to NAD+, so glycolysis continues and yields a net 2 ATP per glucose. The lactate spills into the blood, which is why a raised serum lactate is a clue to bowel ischemia.","whyNot":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase feeds pyruvate into the citric acid cycle. This path depends on oxygen being available to reoxidize NADH and is what fails in ischemia.","Gluconeogenesis from lactate (the Cori cycle) happens in the liver and uses ATP. It clears lactate and does not keep glycolysis running in the ischemic gut.","Correct.","Alanine aminotransferase transfers an amino group from glutamate to pyruvate to make alanine. It does not regenerate NAD+, so it cannot sustain anaerobic glycolysis."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis","Lactate dehydrogenase and NAD+"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C38-Q4","stem":"On day 2 she develops fever, hypotension, and leukocytosis after gram-negative bacteria cross the necrotic bowel wall into the blood. Which of the following host recognition events most directly initiates this inflammatory response?","options":["Toll-like receptor 2 binding lipoteichoic acid","Toll-like receptor 3 binding double-stranded RNA","Toll-like receptor 9 binding unmethylated CpG DNA","Toll-like receptor 4 binding lipid A"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-H5","obj":"CV-18-H.8","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-negative bacteria carry lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) in their outer membrane.","Lipid A is the toxic part of lipopolysaccharide.","Lipid A is recognized by toll-like receptor 4 (with CD14 and MD-2) on macrophages, which triggers TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 release."],"why":"Once the bowel wall dies, gut gram-negative bacteria and their lipopolysaccharide reach the circulation. Lipid A binds the TLR4 complex (with CD14 and MD-2) on macrophages and other innate cells. Signaling through NF-kappa B releases TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6, which cause fever, vasodilation, and leukocytosis and can progress to septic shock.","whyNot":["TLR2 recognizes lipoteichoic acid and lipoproteins, typical of gram-positive bacteria. It would be the main receptor in a gram-positive infection.","TLR3 recognizes double-stranded RNA inside endosomes and responds mainly to viral infection.","TLR9 recognizes unmethylated CpG DNA inside endosomes. It contributes to responses against many microbes, but lipid A is the main trigger of gram-negative septic shock.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Toll-like receptors","Endotoxin and septic shock"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C39","system":"CV","dx":"Hemorrhage from a ruptured spleen with hypovolemia","codes":["CV-18-H2"],"case":"A 19-year-old college student is brought to the emergency department 40 minutes after he was tackled during an intramural rugby game. Two weeks ago he had sore throat, fever, fatigue, and swollen posterior cervical lymph nodes, and a heterophile antibody test was positive. He reports left upper quadrant pain and pain at the tip of his left shoulder. Pulse is 124/min, and blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg. Physical examination shows cool, pale skin and left upper quadrant tenderness. Hemoglobin is 13.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5 g/dL). Bedside ultrasound shows a large amount of free fluid in the abdomen. The diagnosis is hemorrhage from a ruptured spleen with hypovolemia.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C39-Q1","stem":"His heart rate rose within minutes of the bleed. Which of the following changes in the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptor reflex best explains this finding?","options":["Increased stretch receptor firing with decreased sympathetic outflow","Decreased stretch receptor firing with increased sympathetic outflow","Increased stretch receptor firing with increased vagal outflow","Decreased stretch receptor firing with increased vagal outflow"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Blood loss lowers arterial pressure, so the arterial wall stretches less.","Less stretch means fewer impulses from the carotid sinus (via cranial nerve IX) and aortic arch (via cranial nerve X) to the medulla.","The medulla responds by reducing vagal tone and raising sympathetic outflow, which speeds the heart and constricts vessels."],"why":"Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch fire in proportion to arterial wall stretch. A fall in pressure after hemorrhage reduces their firing, which removes their normal inhibition of the vasomotor center. Sympathetic outflow rises and vagal outflow falls, so heart rate, contractility, arteriolar tone, and venous tone all increase. This is why tachycardia with cool, pale skin is an early sign of significant blood loss.","whyNot":["Increased firing with less sympathetic outflow is also the response to a rise in arterial pressure, the opposite of his situation.","Correct.","Increased stretch receptor firing happens when pressure rises. The extra vagal outflow would slow the heart, which is the response to hypertension.","Firing does fall after hemorrhage, but the reflex lowers vagal outflow. Raised vagal outflow would slow his heart."],"study":{"topics":["Baroreceptor reflex","Compensation for hemorrhage"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C39-Q2","stem":"His hemoglobin concentration is near normal even though he has lost a large volume of blood. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Whole blood was lost, so its concentration has not changed yet","Aldosterone has expanded his plasma volume by retaining sodium","Red cells lost into the abdomen have already been reabsorbed","Erythropoietin has already increased red cell production"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H2","obj":"CV-18-H.5","dok":3,"approach":["Hemoglobin is a concentration (grams per deciliter), not a total amount.","In acute hemorrhage, cells and plasma are lost together in the same proportion.","Hemoglobin falls only after interstitial fluid shifts into vessels or IV fluid is given, which takes hours."],"why":"In acute bleeding, red cells and plasma leave together, so the volume falls but the concentration of hemoglobin stays close to baseline at first. Over the next hours, lower capillary hydrostatic pressure pulls interstitial fluid into the capillaries, and IV fluids add more volume, which dilutes the remaining red cells. The full drop in hemoglobin often appears over about 24 to 72 hours. A near normal early hemoglobin therefore does not rule out major hemorrhage, and pulse and blood pressure are better early guides.","whyNot":["Correct.","Aldosterone-driven sodium retention takes hours and adds plasma volume. Any added plasma would dilute red cells and lower hemoglobin, not keep it normal.","Blood in the peritoneal cavity is absorbed slowly over days to weeks, too late to affect a hemoglobin drawn 40 minutes after injury.","Erythropoietin rises within hours, but new red cells take several days to reach the blood, seen as a reticulocyte rise at about 3 to 5 days."],"study":{"topics":["Hemoglobin in acute blood loss","Transcapillary refill"],"comps":["CV-18-H2","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C39-Q3","stem":"The virus that caused his illness two weeks ago enters B cells through CD21. A blood smear at that time showed many large atypical lymphocytes. Which of the following cell types are these atypical lymphocytes most likely to be?","options":["CD4+ helper T cells infected through the CD4 molecule","Infected B cells maturing into antibody-secreting plasma cells","Cytotoxic CD8+ T cells reacting to infected B cells","Monocytes that carry viral inclusion bodies"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.6","dok":2,"approach":["Heterophile-positive infectious mononucleosis is caused by Epstein-Barr virus, which infects B cells through CD21.","The body controls infected B cells with a large cytotoxic T-cell response.","The atypical lymphocytes on the smear are these activated CD8+ T cells."],"why":"Epstein-Barr virus binds CD21 (complement receptor 2) on B cells and drives them to proliferate. The host responds with a strong expansion of cytotoxic CD8+ T cells that kill infected B cells. These activated T cells are large with abundant cytoplasm that scallops around neighboring red cells, and they are the atypical lymphocytes seen in mononucleosis. The same lymphoid expansion enlarges the spleen and makes it fragile, which is why contact sports are avoided for several weeks after the illness.","whyNot":["Infection of CD4+ T cells through the CD4 molecule describes HIV. Epstein-Barr virus targets B cells through CD21.","Infected B cells are present, but they make up a small fraction of circulating lymphocytes. The atypical cells are mostly the T cells responding to them.","Correct.","Monocytes are not the atypical cells in mononucleosis. Large cells with owl-eye inclusions suggest cytomegalovirus infection of tissue cells."],"study":{"topics":["Epstein-Barr virus","Atypical lymphocytes"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C39-Q4","stem":"He undergoes splenectomy. A blood smear several weeks later shows erythrocytes containing small, round, dark purple nuclear remnants. Loss of which of the following splenic functions best explains this finding?","options":["Removal of red cell inclusions by red pulp macrophages","Production of IgM by marginal zone B cells","Synthesis of erythropoietin by splenic interstitial cells","Storage of about a third of platelets in the spleen"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-10","obj":"CV-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the inclusions as Howell-Jolly bodies, which are DNA remnants in red cells.","Recall that red cells normally squeeze through the cords and sinusoids of the red pulp.","Red pulp macrophages remove inclusions during this passage, so the inclusions persist after splenectomy."],"why":"Blood entering the red pulp passes through the splenic cords and must squeeze through narrow slits in the sinusoid walls. Macrophages there remove old or damaged red cells and pluck out inclusions such as nuclear remnants, a process called pitting. Without a spleen, red cells keep their Howell-Jolly bodies, so these bodies on a smear are a marker of absent or poorly functioning splenic tissue.","whyNot":["Correct.","Marginal zone B cells make IgM against encapsulated bacteria. Losing them explains the infection risk after splenectomy, not nuclear remnants in red cells.","Erythropoietin is made mainly by peritubular interstitial cells in the kidney, with a small share from the liver. The spleen is not a source.","The spleen holds about a third of the platelet mass. Losing this pool causes a rise in platelet count after splenectomy, not red cell inclusions."],"study":{"topics":["Red pulp and white pulp function","Howell-Jolly bodies"],"comps":["CV-10"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C40","system":"CV","dx":"Splenic infarction in sickle cell trait at high altitude","codes":["CV-18-H3"],"case":"A 38-year-old man with sickle cell trait develops sudden, sharp left upper quadrant pain on the second day of a ski trip at about 3,000 m (about 9,800 ft) elevation. He has never had a similar episode. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 104/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows left upper quadrant tenderness. Lactate dehydrogenase is 410 U/L (140 to 280 U/L). Hemoglobin electrophoresis shows about 38% hemoglobin S and 60% hemoglobin A. Contrast CT shows a wedge-shaped area of the spleen that does not enhance, with its base at the capsule. The diagnosis is splenic infarction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C40-Q1","stem":"This splenic infarct is pale. A lung infarct after a pulmonary embolus is usually dark red and hemorrhagic. Which of the following features of the lung best explains this difference?","options":["Its cells consume more oxygen than splenic cells do","Its arteries are end arteries with no collateral flow","The embolus also blocks the pulmonary venous outflow","Bronchial arteries keep perfusing the dead tissue"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-H3","obj":"CV-18-H.4","dok":3,"approach":["Pale infarcts form in solid organs with end-arterial supply, such as the spleen, kidney, and heart.","Red infarcts form where blood keeps entering the dead tissue.","The lung has a dual supply: pulmonary arteries and bronchial arteries from the systemic circulation."],"why":"The spleen and kidney are supplied by end arteries, so when one branch is blocked the territory loses its blood and dies pale, in a wedge with its base at the capsule. The lung receives blood from both the pulmonary arteries and the bronchial arteries. After a pulmonary embolus, bronchial blood keeps flowing into the dying, loose lung tissue and leaks out, giving a hemorrhagic red infarct. The same dual supply explains why most pulmonary emboli in healthy lungs do not cause infarction at all.","whyNot":["Oxygen demand affects how fast cells die, but it does not decide whether blood fills the infarct.","End arteries without collaterals describe the spleen and kidney and lead to pale infarcts. The lung has a dual supply.","A pulmonary embolus lodges in a pulmonary artery and does not block venous outflow, so it cannot explain the blood-filled infarct this way.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dual blood supply of the lung","End arteries"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"CV-C40-Q2","stem":"If tissue from the infarcted area were examined 2 days after the onset of pain, which of the following microscopic patterns would most likely be seen?","options":["Liquefactive necrosis with a cyst forming","Fat necrosis with chalky deposits of calcium soaps","Coagulative necrosis with preserved cell outlines","Caseous necrosis with surrounding granulomas"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H3","obj":"CV-18-H.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cause of cell death: ischemia in a solid organ.","Recall that ischemic death in solid organs other than the brain gives coagulative necrosis.","In coagulative necrosis, proteins denature, so cell outlines and tissue architecture remain while nuclei fade."],"why":"Ischemia in solid organs such as the spleen, kidney, and heart causes coagulative necrosis. Denaturation of structural proteins and enzymes slows breakdown of the dead cells, so ghost outlines of cells and the basic architecture persist for days while nuclei disappear. Neutrophils gather at the edge of the infarct within 1 to 2 days, and the area is slowly replaced by a fibrous scar.","whyNot":["Liquefactive necrosis with cyst formation is the pattern of ischemic infarcts in the brain and of abscesses.","Fat necrosis with calcium soaps is typical of acute pancreatitis or trauma to fatty tissue such as the breast.","Correct.","Caseous necrosis with granulomas suggests tuberculosis or some fungal infections, not an ischemic infarct."],"study":{"topics":["Patterns of necrosis","Morphology of infarcts"],"comps":["CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"CV-C40-Q3","stem":"Low oxygen tension at altitude triggered sickling in his spleen. Which of the following molecular events most directly drives the sickling?","options":["Excess alpha chains that form insoluble aggregates","Valine contacts linking deoxygenated HbS molecules","Spectrin deficiency that makes the membrane rigid","Unstable globin that precipitates as Heinz bodies"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H3","obj":"CV-18-H.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the sickle mutation: a point mutation in the beta-globin gene that replaces glutamate with valine at position 6.","Valine is hydrophobic and, in deoxygenated hemoglobin S, fits into a pocket on a neighboring hemoglobin molecule.","These contacts build long polymers that deform the red cell."],"why":"Hemoglobin S results from a missense point mutation in the beta-globin gene that places valine instead of glutamate at position 6. When hemoglobin S gives up its oxygen, the hydrophobic valine binds a complementary site on an adjacent molecule, and the molecules stack into long polymers that distort and stiffen the cell. In sickle cell trait, about 40% of hemoglobin is hemoglobin S, so sickling needs severe stress such as high altitude, dehydration, or acidosis. The spleen is vulnerable because blood moves slowly through its red pulp, where oxygen and pH are low.","whyNot":["Excess alpha chains precipitate in beta-thalassemia because too little beta globin is made. They damage red cell precursors and do not form sickle polymers.","Correct.","Spectrin deficiency causes hereditary spherocytosis, where red cells lose membrane and become spheres.","Heinz bodies are precipitated hemoglobin. They form when oxidant stress damages hemoglobin in G6PD deficiency, or with unstable hemoglobin variants. They do not cause sickling."],"study":{"topics":["Hemoglobin S polymerization","Sickle cell trait"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C40-Q4","stem":"Which of the following would most likely produce a red (hemorrhagic) infarct instead of a pale one like this patient's?","options":["Embolic occlusion of a renal artery branch","Atherosclerotic occlusion of a splenic artery branch","Torsion of the testis on its spermatic cord","Thrombosis of a coronary artery branch"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H3","obj":"CV-18-H.4","dok":3,"approach":["Pale infarcts follow arterial occlusion in solid organs with end-arterial supply.","Red infarcts form when blood keeps entering dead tissue, as with venous occlusion.","Torsion twists the cord and compresses the thin-walled veins before the thicker arteries, so blood enters but cannot leave."],"why":"In testicular torsion the spermatic cord twists. Veins have thin walls and low pressure, so they collapse first while the arteries keep pushing some blood in. The testis becomes congested, then infarcts with blood trapped inside, producing a red infarct. Ovarian torsion and volvulus of the bowel behave the same way, while arterial occlusion in the kidney, heart, and spleen gives pale infarcts.","whyNot":["The kidney has end arteries, so a branch embolus produces a pale wedge-shaped infarct.","An occluded splenic artery branch gives a pale infarct like this patient's, since the spleen is supplied by end arteries.","Correct.","The heart muscle is solid tissue supplied by end arteries, so a coronary thrombosis typically produces a pale infarct."],"study":{"topics":["Red versus pale infarcts","Venous occlusion and infarction"],"comps":["CV-18-H3","CV-9"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C41","system":"CV","dx":"Generalized edema from nephrotic syndrome (minimal change disease)","codes":["CV-18-H4"],"case":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to the clinic because his eyes have been puffy every morning for a week, and now his legs and scrotum are swollen. He had a mild cold 2 weeks ago. Blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. Physical examination shows periorbital edema, scrotal edema, and pitting edema of both legs. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no red cells. The urine protein-to-creatinine ratio is 6.2 mg/mg (under 0.2 mg/mg). Serum albumin is 1.6 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0 g/dL), total cholesterol is 380 mg/dL (under 170 mg/dL), and serum creatinine is 0.3 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7 mg/dL). The diagnosis is nephrotic syndrome, most likely minimal change disease, with generalized edema.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C41-Q1","stem":"Which of the following changes in Starling forces at his systemic capillaries is the main cause of fluid accumulation in his tissues?","options":["Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","Increased capillary permeability to protein","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Decreased interstitial hydrostatic pressure"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Find the key lab: serum albumin of 1.6 g/dL from heavy urinary protein loss.","Albumin provides most of the plasma oncotic pressure that holds fluid inside capillaries.","With less oncotic pull, net filtration rises and fluid collects in loose tissues such as the eyelids and scrotum."],"why":"Net filtration across a capillary depends on capillary hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid out and plasma oncotic pressure pulling it back. Albumin supplies most of the plasma oncotic pressure, so heavy proteinuria with a low serum albumin lowers this pull and more fluid stays in the interstitium. Edema shows first where tissue is loose, such as around the eyes in the morning. Renal sodium retention also adds to the edema in nephrotic syndrome, but the low oncotic pressure is the classic Starling explanation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Increased permeability to protein drives edema in inflammation, burns, and sepsis. The leak in his disease is at the glomerulus, while his systemic capillaries are normal.","Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure drives edema in heart failure and venous obstruction. Nothing in his case points to high venous pressure.","A fall in interstitial hydrostatic pressure would favor filtration slightly, but it is not the main problem here. Interstitial pressure actually rises as edema fluid collects."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Hypoalbuminemia and edema"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C41-Q2","stem":"A woman develops chronic arm swelling after removal of her axillary lymph nodes. Compared with this boy's edema fluid, her edema fluid is most likely to have which of the following features?","options":["A higher protein concentration","A lower protein concentration","More neutrophils and fibrin strands","Similar protein but more red blood cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-10","obj":"CV-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that lymphatics return filtered fluid and the protein that leaks out of capillaries back to the blood.","When lymphatics are blocked, the protein stays behind in the interstitium.","His edema comes from low plasma protein with normal capillaries, so his fluid is a low-protein transudate."],"why":"Each day about 2 to 3 L of filtered fluid, along with plasma proteins that slowly leak from capillaries, returns to the blood through lymphatics. Lymphatic obstruction, as after axillary node dissection or in filariasis, traps that protein in the tissue, raising interstitial oncotic pressure and producing protein-rich edema that can lead to fibrosis over time. In nephrotic syndrome, plasma albumin is low and capillary walls are intact, so the edema fluid is a protein-poor transudate.","whyNot":["Correct.","A lower protein concentration describes his transudate. Lymphatic obstruction does the opposite because protein can no longer be cleared from the tissue.","Neutrophils and fibrin are features of an inflammatory exudate, such as in infection or acute inflammation. Simple lymphatic obstruction is not inflammatory at the start.","Red cells in edema fluid suggest hemorrhage or trauma. Lymphatic obstruction changes protein content, not red cell content."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic function","Lymphedema versus transudate"],"comps":["CV-10","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C41-Q3","stem":"Three weeks later he develops fever and diffuse abdominal tenderness. Paracentesis yields cloudy ascitic fluid with a neutrophil count of 1,500/µL (under 250/µL). Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause?","options":["Candida albicans","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Clostridioides difficile"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-H4","obj":"CV-18-H.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the infection as spontaneous bacterial peritonitis in a child with nephrotic ascites.","Recall that nephrotic children lose IgG and complement factors in the urine, which weakens opsonization.","Choose the encapsulated organism classically linked to peritonitis in nephrotic children."],"why":"Children with nephrotic syndrome lose IgG and some complement factors in the urine. Encapsulated bacteria resist phagocytosis unless they are coated by antibody and complement, so weak opsonization raises the risk of invasive infection by them. Streptococcus pneumoniae is the classic cause of spontaneous peritonitis in nephrotic children, and gram-negative rods such as Escherichia coli are the other main cause.","whyNot":["Candida peritonitis is mainly seen with bowel perforation, peritoneal dialysis, or long antibiotic courses, not as a spontaneous infection in nephrotic syndrome.","Correct.","Staphylococcus aureus causes skin, line, and bone infections. It is an unusual cause of spontaneous peritonitis and is not the organism linked to loss of opsonins here.","Clostridioides difficile causes colitis after antibiotic use. It does not cause spontaneous peritonitis."],"study":{"topics":["Opsonization and encapsulated bacteria","Infections in nephrotic syndrome"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C41-Q4","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his total cholesterol concentration of 380 mg/dL?","options":["Increased intestinal uptake of dietary cholesterol","Reduced use of cholesterol for bile acid synthesis","Loss-of-function mutation in the LDL receptor gene","Increased hepatic synthesis of apoB lipoproteins"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.1","dok":2,"approach":["Link the hyperlipidemia to the other nephrotic findings: low albumin and low plasma oncotic pressure.","Recall that the liver responds by increasing protein synthesis, including albumin and lipoproteins.","More apoB-containing lipoproteins (VLDL and LDL) leave the liver, so cholesterol rises."],"why":"In nephrotic syndrome the liver responds to protein loss and low oncotic pressure by making more proteins, including apoB-containing lipoproteins such as VLDL, which becomes LDL. Clearance of lipoproteins is also reduced, partly because lipoprotein lipase activity falls. The combined result is high cholesterol and often high triglycerides, which usually settle once the proteinuria resolves.","whyNot":["Intestinal absorption of cholesterol is not the driver in nephrotic syndrome. The extra lipoproteins come from the liver.","Reduced conversion of cholesterol to bile acids can raise cholesterol, but it is not the mechanism linked to protein loss in nephrotic syndrome.","An LDL receptor mutation causes familial hypercholesterolemia, which is present from birth and would not appear suddenly with proteinuria and edema.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hyperlipidemia in nephrotic syndrome","Lipoprotein metabolism"],"comps":["CV-13"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C42","system":"CV","dx":"Acute arterial thrombosis of the superficial femoral artery","codes":["CV-18-H6"],"case":"A 64-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because his right leg suddenly became painful, pale, and cool this morning. For the past year he has had calf pain after walking two blocks. He has type 2 diabetes and a 45-pack-year smoking history. Physical examination shows that the right femoral pulse is present, but the right popliteal and pedal pulses are absent. His heart rhythm is regular, and echocardiography shows no cardiac thrombus. Platelet count is 260,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000/µL). Angiography shows a thrombus on an ulcerated atherosclerotic plaque that blocks the right superficial femoral artery. The diagnosis is acute arterial thrombosis of the superficial femoral artery.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C42-Q1","stem":"The first step in forming his thrombus was adhesion of platelets to the subendothelium exposed by the ulcerated plaque. Which of the following interactions mediates this adhesion under the high shear stress of arterial flow?","options":["P2Y12 receptor binding adenosine diphosphate","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptor binding fibrinogen","Tissue factor binding factor VIIa","Glycoprotein Ib binding von Willebrand factor"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the steps of platelet plug formation: adhesion, activation, then aggregation.","Adhesion is the step where a resting platelet first sticks to exposed collagen.","At high arterial shear, von Willebrand factor bound to collagen captures platelets through glycoprotein Ib."],"why":"When a plaque ulcerates, subendothelial collagen is exposed. Von Willebrand factor from plasma and endothelium binds the collagen and unfolds under the high shear of arterial flow. Platelets then stick to it through their glycoprotein Ib receptor, which is the adhesion step. The adherent platelets activate, release ADP and thromboxane A2, and then aggregate, building a platelet-rich arterial thrombus.","whyNot":["ADP binding the P2Y12 receptor amplifies platelet activation and recruits more platelets. It acts after the platelets have already adhered.","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa binding fibrinogen links platelets to one another. This is aggregation, the step that follows adhesion and activation.","Tissue factor binding factor VIIa starts the coagulation cascade that makes thrombin and fibrin. It is not the receptor interaction that makes platelets stick to the vessel wall.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Platelet adhesion and von Willebrand factor","Arterial thrombus formation"],"comps":["CV-18-H6","CV-18-I"]}},{"id":"CV-C42-Q2","stem":"Before today, his leg tolerated a year of gradual narrowing of the superficial femoral artery. Collateral branches of which of the following arteries most likely carried blood around the narrowing to the region of the knee?","options":["Superficial circumflex iliac artery","Deep femoral artery","Inferior epigastric artery","Obturator artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["The superficial femoral artery continues as the popliteal artery behind the knee.","Look for an artery that arises above the narrowing and has branches that reach the knee.","The deep femoral (profunda femoris) artery sends perforating branches and the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery down to join the genicular arteries."],"why":"The femoral artery divides into the superficial femoral artery and the deep femoral (profunda femoris) artery. Branches of the deep femoral artery, including its perforating arteries and the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery, connect with genicular branches of the popliteal artery around the knee. When the superficial femoral artery narrows slowly, these channels enlarge and carry blood around the blockage. This is why chronic narrowing caused only calf pain on walking, while sudden thrombosis today overwhelmed the collaterals.","whyNot":["The superficial circumflex iliac artery supplies skin near the anterior superior iliac spine and does not reach the knee.","Correct.","The inferior epigastric artery runs up the anterior abdominal wall and joins the superior epigastric artery. It is a collateral around aortic or iliac blockage, not around the thigh.","The obturator artery supplies the medial thigh muscles and the hip. Its branches do not reach the knee anastomosis."],"study":{"topics":["Arteries of the thigh","Genicular anastomosis"],"comps":["CV-9"]}},{"id":"CV-C42-Q3","stem":"He is started on daily aspirin. Which of the following best explains why one dose of aspirin impairs a platelet for the rest of its life span of about 7 to 10 days?","options":["Platelets have no nucleus to make new cyclooxygenase","Aspirin is stored inside platelet dense granules","Platelets lack the enzymes that clear salicylate","Aspirin binds glycoprotein IIb/IIIa for about 10 days"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the target: aspirin irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1).","Without COX-1, platelets cannot turn arachidonic acid into thromboxane A2, a key signal for aggregation.","A platelet has no nucleus, so it cannot replace the blocked enzyme, and the effect lasts until new platelets are made."],"why":"Aspirin acetylates a serine in the active site of COX-1, which blocks the enzyme permanently. Platelets use COX-1 to convert arachidonic acid into thromboxane A2, which promotes vasoconstriction and platelet aggregation. Platelets have no nucleus and cannot make meaningful amounts of new enzyme, so each exposed platelet stays inhibited for its life span. Normal function returns only as the marrow releases new platelets, about 10% of the total each day.","whyNot":["Correct.","Dense granules store ADP, calcium, and serotonin. Aspirin is not stored there.","Salicylate is cleared by the liver and kidneys within hours. The lasting effect comes from the permanently acetylated enzyme, not from drug left in the platelet.","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa is blocked by drugs such as abciximab and eptifibatide. Aspirin does not bind this receptor."],"study":{"topics":["Thromboxane A2 synthesis","Aspirin and COX-1"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C42-Q4","stem":"He undergoes bypass surgery and receives unfractionated heparin. Six days later his platelet count is 95,000/µL, and a new thrombus forms in a vein of his left arm. Which of the following mechanisms best explains these events?","options":["Heparin directly clumps platelets without any antibody","IgE against heparin causes mast cell degranulation","IgG to heparin-platelet factor 4 activates platelets","Heparin depletes antithrombin, leaving thrombin unchecked"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing and pattern: a platelet fall of more than 50% about 5 to 10 days after starting heparin, plus new thrombosis.","This is heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), an antibody-mediated reaction.","IgG binds heparin-platelet factor 4 complexes, and the immune complexes activate platelets through Fc gamma receptors."],"why":"In heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, heparin binds platelet factor 4 (PF4) and creates a new antigen. The patient makes IgG against heparin-PF4 complexes, and the antibody-coated complexes bind Fc gamma IIa receptors on platelets and activate them. Activated platelets release procoagulant particles and are cleared, so the count falls while clots form in veins and arteries. The usual onset is about 5 to 10 days after first exposure, the time needed to make IgG.","whyNot":["A mild, early fall in platelets from direct heparin effect can occur within the first 1 to 2 days, but it is harmless and does not cause thrombosis.","IgE-mediated reactions cause hives, wheezing, or anaphylaxis, not a falling platelet count with clots.","Correct.","Heparin works by boosting antithrombin. Any small drop in antithrombin does not explain a large fall in platelets."],"study":{"topics":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Fc receptor-mediated platelet activation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C43","system":"CV","dx":"Abdominal aortic aneurysm (infrarenal)","codes":["CV-18-I1"],"case":"A 72-year-old man comes to the physician for a routine health maintenance examination. He has hypertension and has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 50 years. He has no abdominal or back pain. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 152/88 mm Hg (normal below 120/80 mm Hg). Physical examination shows a nontender, pulsatile mass just above the umbilicus. Ultrasonography shows fusiform dilation of the abdominal aorta below the renal arteries, with an outer diameter of 5.8 cm (normal about 2 cm). The renal arteries arise from normal-caliber aorta above the dilation. The diagnosis is infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C43-Q1","stem":"He undergoes open surgical repair. During the operation, an unpaired artery arising from the anterior wall of the aneurysm at about the level of the third lumbar vertebra is ligated. If collateral flow to this artery's territory is poor, which of the following segments of bowel is at greatest risk for ischemia after surgery?","options":["Ascending colon","Jejunum","Sigmoid colon","Second part of the duodenum"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.1","dok":3,"approach":["Name the vessel: an unpaired anterior branch of the aorta at L3, below the renal arteries, is the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).","Recall the IMA territory: distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum.","Pick the segment in that territory that is classically injured after aortic repair: the sigmoid colon."],"why":"The inferior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at about L3, so it arises from the segment that an infrarenal AAA usually involves. It supplies the left colon, including the sigmoid colon and upper rectum. When it is tied off during repair, the left colon depends on collaterals from the superior mesenteric artery (through the marginal artery) and from the internal iliac arteries. If these are poor, ischemic colitis develops, most often in the sigmoid colon.","whyNot":["The ascending colon is supplied by the ileocolic and right colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery, which are not tied off in an infrarenal repair.","The jejunum is supplied by jejunal branches of the superior mesenteric artery, which arises at about L1, above the renal arteries and above this aneurysm.","Correct.","The second part of the duodenum is supplied by the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries, from the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery."],"study":{"topics":["Inferior mesenteric artery territory","Branches of the abdominal aorta by vertebral level"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"CV-C43-Q2","stem":"Which of the following processes best explains the progressive weakening of the aortic wall in this patient's aneurysm?","options":["Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum","Granulomatous inflammation with giant cells in the media","An intimal tear that lets blood split the media","Protease breakdown of medial elastin and collagen"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I1","obj":"CV-18-I.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the setting: an older male smoker with hypertension and an infrarenal aneurysm has the typical atherosclerosis-associated AAA.","Recall what thins the media in AAA: inflammatory cells release matrix metalloproteinases that digest elastin and collagen.","Rule out mechanisms that belong to other aortic diseases (syphilis, dissection, arteritis)."],"why":"Typical AAAs arise in older smokers with atherosclerosis. Macrophages and other inflammatory cells in the aortic wall release matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) that break down elastin and collagen in the media, and their inhibitors are reduced. Thick intimal plaque also slows diffusion of nutrients to the inner media. The thinned, weakened wall slowly dilates under arterial pressure.","whyNot":["This describes tertiary syphilis, which narrows the vasa vasorum and causes aneurysms of the ascending aorta and arch, not the infrarenal aorta.","Granulomas with giant cells describe giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis, which mainly involve the thoracic aorta and its branches.","An intimal tear with blood tracking through the media describes aortic dissection, which is a different disease from a fusiform aneurysm.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of abdominal aortic aneurysm","Matrix metalloproteinases in vessel wall remodeling"],"comps":["CV-18-I1","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C43-Q3","stem":"For a cylindrical vessel, Laplace's law states that wall tension equals transmural pressure times radius. If the pressure inside this patient's aneurysm equals that in a normal 2-cm aorta, which of the following is closest to the ratio of wall tension in the aneurysm to normal wall tension?","options":["About 1.7 times","About 3 times","About 8 times","About 70 times"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Write Laplace's law for a cylinder: tension = pressure x radius.","Compare radii: 5.8 cm divided by 2 cm is 2.9, so the radius is about 3 times normal.","With pressure unchanged, tension rises in direct proportion to radius: about 3 times."],"why":"Laplace's law for a cylinder says wall tension equals pressure times radius. The aneurysm's radius is about 2.9 times normal, so at the same pressure the wall tension is about 3 times normal. Because the aneurysm wall is also thinner, the stress on each unit of wall (tension divided by thickness) rises even more. This growing load on a weakening wall is why rupture risk climbs steeply as an aneurysm enlarges, and why elective repair is advised at about 5.5 cm in men.","whyNot":["About 1.7 times would come from taking the square root of the radius ratio, which has no basis in Laplace's law.","Correct.","About 8 times comes from squaring the radius ratio, which would apply to cross-sectional area, not wall tension.","About 70 times comes from raising the radius ratio to the fourth power, which belongs to Poiseuille's law for resistance and flow."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace","Aneurysm rupture risk and size"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"CV-C43-Q4","stem":"Before the repair is scheduled, he develops fever and new back pain. One week earlier he had diarrhea after eating undercooked chicken. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F). Blood cultures grow a motile, oxidase-negative, gram-negative rod that does not ferment lactose and produces hydrogen sulfide. CT scan shows rapid enlargement of the aneurysm with surrounding soft-tissue thickening. Which of the following is the most likely causal organism?","options":["Klebsiella pneumoniae","Salmonella enterica","Escherichia coli","Shigella sonnei"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-I1","obj":"CV-18-I.4","dok":2,"approach":["List the lab clues: gram-negative rod, oxidase negative, motile, lactose nonfermenter, hydrogen sulfide producer.","Add the history: poultry-borne diarrhea followed by bacteremia in an older adult with a diseased aorta.","Match to the organism known for seeding atherosclerotic or aneurysmal arteries: nontyphoidal Salmonella."],"why":"Nontyphoidal Salmonella is a motile, oxidase-negative, gram-negative rod that does not ferment lactose and makes hydrogen sulfide. It is commonly acquired from poultry and eggs. In adults over about 50, Salmonella bacteremia can seed atherosclerotic or aneurysmal arterial walls and cause an infected (mycotic) aneurysm, which tends to enlarge quickly and rupture.","whyNot":["Klebsiella ferments lactose and is nonmotile, so it does not fit these culture results.","Correct.","Escherichia coli usually ferments lactose and does not produce hydrogen sulfide.","Shigella does not ferment lactose but is nonmotile and does not produce hydrogen sulfide. It has no animal reservoir and rarely enters the bloodstream."],"study":{"topics":["Nontyphoidal Salmonella","Infected (mycotic) aneurysm","Enteric gram-negative rod identification"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-I1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C44","system":"CV","dx":"Acute Stanford type A aortic dissection in Marfan syndrome","codes":["CV-18-I2"],"case":"A 34-year-old man with Marfan syndrome is brought to the emergency department because of sudden, severe, tearing chest pain that radiates to the area between his shoulder blades. He had bilateral ectopia lentis as a teenager. He is 196 cm tall. Blood pressure is 158/92 mm Hg in the right arm and 124/76 mm Hg in the left arm. Physical examination shows arachnodactyly. A soft early diastolic murmur is heard along the left sternal border. CT angiography shows an intimal flap that begins in the ascending aorta just above the aortic valve and extends beyond the origin of the left subclavian artery. The diagnosis is acute Stanford type A aortic dissection.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C44-Q1","stem":"His Marfan syndrome is caused by a mutation in FBN1. Normal fibrillin-1 microfibrils sequester a growth factor in a latent form in the extracellular matrix. Increased signaling by which of the following factors contributes to degeneration of this patient's aortic media?","options":["Platelet-derived growth factor","Tumor necrosis factor alpha","Transforming growth factor beta","Vascular endothelial growth factor"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the gene product: FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, a scaffold for elastic fibers.","Recall the second role of fibrillin-1: it binds and sequesters latent TGF-beta in the matrix.","Connect the defect to disease: abnormal fibrillin releases too much active TGF-beta, which damages the aortic wall."],"why":"Marfan syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in FBN1 on chromosome 15. Fibrillin-1 forms microfibrils that support elastic fibers and also hold transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta) in a latent complex. Mutant fibrillin fails to sequester TGF-beta, and the excess signaling contributes to loss of smooth muscle and elastic tissue in the aortic media. This medial weakness leads to aortic root dilation and dissection.","whyNot":["Platelet-derived growth factor drives smooth muscle migration in atherosclerosis but is not stored by fibrillin-1.","Tumor necrosis factor alpha is an inflammatory cytokine, and Marfan aortic disease is characteristically noninflammatory.","Correct.","Vascular endothelial growth factor drives new vessel growth and is not held latent by fibrillin microfibrils."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrillin-1 and TGF-beta signaling","Marfan syndrome genetics"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-I2"]}},{"id":"CV-C44-Q2","stem":"The false lumen of an aortic dissection forms within the aortic wall, usually between the middle and outer thirds of one layer. In a normal adult aorta, which of the following best describes the main components of that layer?","options":["Fenestrated elastic laminae with smooth muscle between them","Endothelium resting on a thin layer of loose connective tissue","Loose collagen containing vasa vasorum, nerves, and fibroblasts","Longitudinal smooth muscle bundles with few elastic fibers"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the layer: a dissection plane runs within the tunica media.","Recall that the aorta is an elastic artery whose media is its thickest layer.","Describe that media: many concentric fenestrated elastic laminae with smooth muscle cells, collagen, and ground substance between them."],"why":"In a dissection, blood enters through an intimal tear and splits the tunica media, most often between its middle and outer thirds. The aorta is an elastic artery. Its media is the thickest layer and contains dozens of concentric, fenestrated elastic laminae with smooth muscle cells, collagen, and proteoglycan ground substance between them. These laminae store energy during systole and recoil in diastole, and their loss is what makes the media vulnerable to splitting.","whyNot":["Correct.","This describes the tunica intima. The intimal tear is the entry point, but the false lumen runs in the media.","This describes the tunica adventitia, the outer layer that carries vasa vasorum and nerves to the outer wall.","Longitudinal smooth muscle bundles are typical of the adventitia of large veins such as the inferior vena cava, not the aortic wall."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of elastic arteries","Layers of the aortic wall"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-I2"]}},{"id":"CV-C44-Q3","stem":"Two hours later, he becomes confused. Pulse is 128/min, and blood pressure is 84/60 mm Hg. Jugular venous distention is present, and heart sounds are muffled. Systolic pressure decreases by 18 mm Hg with each inspiration. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the decrease in systolic pressure during inspiration?","options":["Inspiration lowers venous return to the right heart, reducing output","Lung expansion compresses the false lumen and narrows the true lumen","Inspiration raises vagal tone, slowing the heart and lowering output","Right ventricular filling rises and bows the septum to the left"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","obj":"CV-15.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize cardiac tamponade: hypotension, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds after a type A dissection ruptures into the pericardium.","Recall the effect of inspiration: lower intrathoracic pressure increases venous return to the right ventricle.","Apply the fixed pericardial volume: the right ventricle can only expand by bowing the septum left, so left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall."],"why":"A type A dissection can rupture back into the pericardial sac, causing hemopericardium and tamponade. The tight pericardium fixes the total volume of the heart. During inspiration, falling intrathoracic pressure draws more blood into the right ventricle, which can only enlarge by pushing the interventricular septum into the left ventricle. Left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall, so systolic pressure drops by more than 10 mm Hg with inspiration (pulsus paradoxus). Pooling of blood in the expanding pulmonary veins adds to the fall.","whyNot":["Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart. It does not reduce it.","There is no recognized mechanism by which lung expansion compresses the false lumen to cause a cyclic fall in pressure.","Inspiration lowers vagal tone and slightly speeds the heart (sinus arrhythmia), the opposite of this option.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulsus paradoxus","Cardiac tamponade physiology","Effects of respiration on venous return"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-F"]}},{"id":"CV-C44-Q4","stem":"A resected segment of his ascending aorta is examined microscopically. Which of the following findings in the media is most characteristic of the process that predisposed him to dissection?","options":["Fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils across the vessel wall","Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells throughout the media","Plasma cells around narrowed, thickened vasa vasorum","Elastic fiber breakup, smooth muscle loss, and mucoid pools"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I2","obj":"CV-18-I.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the predisposing condition: Marfan syndrome weakens the aortic media.","Name the lesion: cystic medial degeneration.","Describe it: elastic fiber fragmentation, smooth muscle loss, and pools of proteoglycan-rich ground substance, with little inflammation."],"why":"Marfan syndrome and long-standing hypertension both predispose to dissection through cystic medial degeneration. The media shows fragmented and separated elastic fibers, loss of smooth muscle cells, and small cleftlike or cystic spaces filled with proteoglycan-rich (mucoid) ground substance. Inflammation is characteristically absent. The weakened media splits easily once blood enters through an intimal tear.","whyNot":["Fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils describes necrotizing vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa, which affects medium-sized arteries.","Granulomas with giant cells describe giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis.","Plasma cells around narrowed vasa vasorum describe syphilitic aortitis, which causes aneurysms of the ascending aorta but rarely dissection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cystic medial degeneration","Risk factors for aortic dissection"],"comps":["CV-18-I2","CV-14"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C45","system":"CV","dx":"Atherosclerotic carotid artery disease with amaurosis fugax","codes":["CV-18-I3"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman comes to the physician because of an episode of painless vision loss in her right eye yesterday. She describes a shade coming down over the eye that lasted about 5 minutes and then lifted. She has type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension and smoked for 30 years. Blood pressure is 148/90 mm Hg (normal below 120/80 mm Hg). A bruit is heard over the right side of the neck near the angle of the mandible. Serum LDL cholesterol is 168 mg/dL (optimal below 100 mg/dL). Duplex ultrasonography shows an ulcerated plaque at the origin of the right internal carotid artery that narrows the lumen diameter by about 50%. The diagnosis is atherosclerotic carotid artery disease with amaurosis fugax, a warning sign of high stroke risk.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C45-Q1","stem":"Her transient vision loss was most likely caused by a small embolus from the plaque that lodged in a branch of which of the following arteries?","options":["Right posterior cerebral artery","Right superficial temporal artery","Right ophthalmic artery","Left middle cerebral artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note that vision was lost in one eye only, which points to the retina rather than the visual cortex or optic radiations.","Trace the path: plaque at the right internal carotid origin, up the internal carotid, into its first major intracranial branch.","That branch is the ophthalmic artery, which gives off the central retinal artery."],"why":"Monocular vision loss means the retina or optic nerve of that eye lost blood flow. The internal carotid artery has no branches in the neck. Its first major branch inside the skull is the ophthalmic artery, which gives off the central retinal artery. An embolus from an ulcerated plaque at the internal carotid origin can travel into this branch and cause transient monocular blindness (amaurosis fugax).","whyNot":["The posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital cortex. Its ischemia causes a homonymous hemianopia that affects the visual field of both eyes, and it usually arises from the basilar artery, not the carotid.","The superficial temporal artery is a branch of the external carotid artery and does not supply the retina. It is the classic site of giant cell arteritis.","Correct.","The left middle cerebral artery cannot receive an embolus from a right carotid plaque, and middle cerebral territory loss affects both eyes' visual fields, not one eye."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the internal carotid artery","Amaurosis fugax"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C45-Q2","stem":"At the plaque, the lumen radius is about half of normal. If flow is laminar and blood viscosity and segment length are unchanged, the resistance of the narrowed segment has increased by approximately which of the following factors?","options":["About 16 times","About 256 times","About 4 times","About 2 times"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall Poiseuille's law: resistance is proportional to viscosity times length divided by radius to the fourth power.","Halving the radius divides the denominator by 2 to the fourth power, which is 16.","So resistance in that segment rises about 16 times."],"why":"Poiseuille's law states that resistance equals 8 times viscosity times length, divided by pi times the radius to the fourth power. Because radius is raised to the fourth power, halving the radius raises resistance 16 times. A 50% diameter narrowing is the same as halving the radius. This steep relationship explains why modest further plaque growth or a thrombus on an ulcerated plaque can sharply cut flow.","whyNot":["Correct.","About 256 times is the increase when the radius falls to one quarter of normal (4 to the fourth power).","About 4 times would be true if resistance depended on radius squared (cross-sectional area).","About 2 times would be true only if resistance were inversely proportional to radius itself."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille's law","Vascular resistance and radius"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C45-Q3","stem":"Macrophages in her plaque become foam cells by taking up oxidized LDL. Which of the following best explains why these cells continue to take up cholesterol after they are heavily loaded?","options":["LDL receptor numbers rise as the cell's cholesterol increases","Scavenger receptors are not downregulated by cholesterol","SREBP-2 is activated by high levels of cell cholesterol","HDL delivers extra stored cholesterol into macrophages"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the receptor: oxidized LDL enters macrophages through scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36.","Contrast with the LDL receptor, which is shut down by high intracellular cholesterol through SREBP-2.","Conclude that scavenger receptor uptake has no feedback brake, so lipid keeps accumulating."],"why":"Oxidized LDL in the intima is recognized by macrophage scavenger receptors, such as SR-A and CD36, which are pattern recognition receptors of innate immunity. Unlike the LDL receptor, these receptors are not downregulated when the cell fills with cholesterol. Uptake continues, cholesteryl ester droplets build up, and the cell becomes a foam cell. Loaded macrophages also release cytokines that recruit more inflammatory cells.","whyNot":["LDL receptor expression falls when cell cholesterol rises, because SREBP-2 is held inactive. This feedback is the reason native LDL does not overload cells.","Correct.","SREBP-2 is activated when cell cholesterol is low and suppressed when it is high.","HDL removes cholesterol from macrophages (reverse cholesterol transport). It does not load them."],"study":{"topics":["Scavenger receptors and foam cells","Macrophages in atherosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C45-Q4","stem":"Her plaque formed at the carotid bifurcation, a common site of atherosclerosis. Which of the following local conditions best explains why arterial branch points are prone to plaque formation?","options":["Absent vasa vasorum causing ischemia of the inner wall","Low and oscillating shear stress on endothelial cells","Lower pressure at branches that draws plasma lipids inward","High, steady laminar shear stress on endothelial cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I3","obj":"CV-18-I.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall where plaques form: branch points, ostia, and the outer wall of the carotid bulb.","Link flow pattern to endothelial behavior: steady laminar flow is protective, disturbed flow is not.","Choose low, oscillating shear stress, which turns on proinflammatory, adhesive endothelial genes."],"why":"Steady laminar flow with high shear stress induces protective endothelial genes, including endothelial nitric oxide synthase, which keep the surface anti-inflammatory and less permeable. At branch points such as the carotid bulb, flow separates and recirculates, so shear stress is low and changes direction. Endothelium there makes less nitric oxide, expresses adhesion molecules such as VCAM-1, and lets more LDL into the intima. This is why plaques cluster at bifurcations and ostia.","whyNot":["The inner wall of all arteries is nourished by diffusion from the lumen, so lack of vasa vasorum there is not specific to branch points.","Correct.","Pressure differences between branches do not draw lipids into the wall. LDL entry depends on endothelial permeability.","High, steady laminar shear stress protects the endothelium and is found in straight segments where plaques are less common."],"study":{"topics":["Shear stress and endothelial function","Sites of atherosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-I3","CV-11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C46","system":"CV","dx":"Atherosclerosis risk in metabolic syndrome with atherogenic dyslipidemia","codes":["CV-18-I3"],"case":"A 48-year-old man is evaluated at a workplace health screening. He has a sedentary lifestyle and a diet high in fast food. Waist circumference is 108 cm (risk threshold above 102 cm in men). Blood pressure is 142/90 mm Hg (normal below 120/80 mm Hg). Fasting laboratory studies show glucose 118 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99 mg/dL), triglycerides 260 mg/dL (normal below 150 mg/dL), HDL cholesterol 34 mg/dL (desired above 40 mg/dL in men), and LDL cholesterol 138 mg/dL (optimal below 100 mg/dL). High-sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) is 4.2 mg/L (low risk below 1.0 mg/L). Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 45 mg/g (normal below 30 mg/g). The diagnosis is metabolic syndrome with atherogenic dyslipidemia, which raises his risk of atherosclerosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C46-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains this patient's elevated fasting triglyceride concentration?","options":["Increased hepatic VLDL secretion from excess fatty acids","Loss of hepatic LDL receptors slowing the clearance of LDL","Reduced hepatic secretion of apoB-100 particles","Increased intestinal absorption of dietary cholesterol"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the driver: central obesity with high glucose points to insulin resistance.","Follow the fatty acids: insulin normally suppresses adipocyte lipolysis, so resistant fat cells release more free fatty acids to the liver.","The liver packages the extra fatty acids as triglyceride in VLDL, so fasting triglycerides rise."],"why":"In insulin resistance, insulin fails to suppress hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes, so more free fatty acids flow to the liver. The liver re-esterifies them into triglyceride and secretes more VLDL with apoB-100. Reduced lipoprotein lipase activity slows VLDL clearance and adds to the rise. Through cholesteryl ester transfer protein, triglyceride then moves from VLDL into HDL and LDL, which explains his low HDL and the small, dense LDL typical of this pattern.","whyNot":["Correct.","Loss of LDL receptors raises LDL cholesterol, as in familial hypercholesterolemia, but does not by itself raise fasting triglycerides.","Reduced apoB-100 secretion would lower VLDL and triglycerides. Insulin resistance does the opposite.","Dietary cholesterol absorption mainly affects LDL cholesterol. Fasting triglycerides come from hepatic VLDL, not from chylomicrons."],"study":{"topics":["Insulin resistance and VLDL","Atherogenic dyslipidemia"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C46-Q2","stem":"In a research study, infusion of acetylcholine into his brachial artery causes much less forearm vasodilation than in healthy controls. Infusion of sodium nitroprusside, which releases nitric oxide directly, causes a normal response. Which of the following steps is most likely impaired?","options":["Alpha-1 receptor signaling in forearm arterioles","Smooth muscle guanylyl cyclase response to nitric oxide","Release of nitric oxide by the endothelium","Smooth muscle beta-2 receptor signaling through cAMP"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the acetylcholine pathway: acetylcholine acts on endothelial muscarinic receptors to make nitric oxide.","Use the control: nitroprusside bypasses the endothelium and relaxes smooth muscle normally, so the smooth muscle response is intact.","Locate the defect in the endothelium: reduced release and availability of nitric oxide (endothelial dysfunction)."],"why":"Acetylcholine binds endothelial M3 receptors, raises endothelial calcium, and activates endothelial nitric oxide synthase. Nitric oxide diffuses into smooth muscle, activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raises cGMP, and relaxes the vessel. Nitroprusside supplies nitric oxide without the endothelium, so a normal response to it proves the smooth muscle pathway works. A blunted acetylcholine response with a normal nitroprusside response is the standard test result for endothelial dysfunction, an early step in atherosclerosis that is common in insulin resistance.","whyNot":["Alpha-1 receptors cause vasoconstriction. Acetylcholine-induced dilation does not depend on them.","A smooth muscle guanylyl cyclase defect would also blunt the response to nitroprusside, which was normal.","Correct.","Beta-2 receptors respond to epinephrine, not acetylcholine, and a beta-2 defect would not explain this test result."],"study":{"topics":["Endothelial nitric oxide","Endothelial dysfunction"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C46-Q3","stem":"Visceral adipose tissue in central obesity contains many activated macrophages. Which of the following cytokines released from this tissue most directly stimulates hepatic synthesis of C-reactive protein in this patient?","options":["Interleukin 4","Interleukin 10","Interleukin 2","Interleukin 6"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize CRP as an acute phase protein made by hepatocytes.","Recall the main cytokine that induces acute phase protein synthesis: interleukin 6.","Link visceral fat macrophages and adipocytes as an ongoing source of interleukin 6 in obesity."],"why":"C-reactive protein is an acute phase protein made by hepatocytes. Interleukin 6 is the main signal that drives its synthesis, with help from interleukin 1 and tumor necrosis factor. In central obesity, activated macrophages and adipocytes in visceral fat release interleukin 6 steadily, producing low-grade chronic inflammation. An hs-CRP above about 3 mg/L marks higher cardiovascular risk.","whyNot":["Interleukin 4 is a Th2 cytokine that drives IgE class switching and does not induce CRP.","Interleukin 10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that dampens acute phase responses.","Interleukin 2 is a T-cell growth factor and does not drive hepatic acute phase protein synthesis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acute phase response","Inflammation in obesity"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C46-Q4","stem":"Two years later, a kidney biopsy is performed because of increasing albuminuria. It shows small arterioles with walls thickened by homogeneous, glassy, eosinophilic material and narrowed lumens. Which of the following processes most likely produced this arteriolar change?","options":["Lipid-laden macrophages collecting beneath the endothelium","Concentric smooth muscle growth with duplicated basement membrane","Calcium deposition in the media of muscular arteries","Plasma proteins leaking into the wall, plus excess matrix"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I3","obj":"CV-18-I.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the lesion: glassy pink arteriolar wall thickening is hyaline arteriolosclerosis.","Link it to his conditions: chronic hypertension and diabetes or prediabetes.","Recall the mechanism: plasma protein leakage through injured endothelium plus extra matrix from smooth muscle cells."],"why":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis is a form of arteriosclerosis seen with long-standing hypertension, diabetes, and aging. Chronic hemodynamic and metabolic stress injures the endothelium, so plasma proteins leak into the arteriolar wall. Smooth muscle cells also lay down extra extracellular matrix. The result is homogeneous pink wall thickening that narrows the lumen and causes ischemic injury to the kidney (nephrosclerosis).","whyNot":["Lipid-laden macrophages beneath the endothelium form fatty streaks and atheromas in large and medium arteries, not arterioles.","Concentric, onion-skin smooth muscle growth with duplicated basement membrane is hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, seen in severe (malignant) hypertension.","Medial calcification is Monckeberg medial sclerosis, which affects muscular arteries rather than arterioles and does not narrow the lumen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Types of arteriosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-I3","CV-18-A"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C47","system":"CV","dx":"Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","codes":["CV-18-I4"],"case":"A 31-year-old man comes to the physician for evaluation after his father died of a myocardial infarction at age 46. He does not smoke and is not obese. Physical examination shows nodular thickening of both Achilles tendons and bilateral corneal arcus. Fasting serum LDL cholesterol is 292 mg/dL (optimal below 100 mg/dL), triglycerides are 120 mg/dL (normal below 150 mg/dL), and HDL cholesterol is 48 mg/dL (desired above 40 mg/dL in men). Thyroid and kidney function test results are normal. Genetic testing shows one pathogenic variant in the LDL receptor gene (LDLR). The diagnosis is heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C47-Q1","stem":"His wife also has heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia caused by a different pathogenic LDLR variant. Which of the following is the probability that their first child inherits a pathogenic variant from each parent and has the severe form of the disease?","options":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the inheritance: familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant, and having two pathogenic alleles causes a more severe disease.","Each heterozygous parent passes the variant to a child with a probability of 1/2.","Multiply: 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4, or 25%."],"why":"Familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant with a gene dosage effect. Each heterozygous parent passes the pathogenic allele half the time, so the chance that a child inherits both is 1/2 x 1/2, or 25%. A child with two pathogenic alleles (homozygous or compound heterozygous) has very little LDL receptor function, LDL cholesterol often above 500 mg/dL, and coronary disease that can begin in childhood. Each child also has a 50% chance of being heterozygous and a 25% chance of being unaffected.","whyNot":["Correct.","50% is the chance that a child is heterozygous, carrying exactly one pathogenic allele.","75% is the chance that a child has at least one pathogenic allele (affected in either form).","100% would apply only if a parent had two pathogenic alleles and could pass nothing else."],"study":{"topics":["Autosomal dominant inheritance with gene dosage","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-I4"]}},{"id":"CV-C47-Q2","stem":"In his hepatocytes, fewer functional LDL receptors means less LDL-derived cholesterol reaches the cell interior. Which of the following best describes the hepatocyte response to the decreased intracellular cholesterol?","options":["SREBP-2 is activated, increasing HMG-CoA reductase","HMG-CoA reductase is ubiquitinated and degraded faster","SREBP-2 stays in the ER, lowering HMG-CoA reductase","ACAT activity rises, storing more cholesteryl ester"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the sensor: SCAP in the endoplasmic reticulum senses membrane cholesterol.","Low cholesterol lets SCAP carry SREBP-2 to the Golgi, where it is cleaved and released.","The released SREBP-2 enters the nucleus and raises transcription of HMG-CoA reductase and the LDL receptor."],"why":"When cholesterol in the endoplasmic reticulum membrane is low, SCAP escorts SREBP-2 to the Golgi. Two proteases (S1P and S2P) cleave it, and the freed transcription factor enters the nucleus and binds sterol regulatory elements. This raises synthesis of HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis, and of the LDL receptor. In heterozygotes, the normal allele is upregulated this way, which is why they respond to statins.","whyNot":["Correct.","Faster ubiquitination and degradation of HMG-CoA reductase is triggered by high sterol levels, not low ones.","SREBP-2 stays in the endoplasmic reticulum when cell cholesterol is high, not low.","ACAT esterifies cholesterol for storage when the cell has excess cholesterol. Low cholesterol reduces its activity."],"study":{"topics":["SREBP-2 regulation","HMG-CoA reductase control","LDL receptor pathway"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I4"]}},{"id":"CV-C47-Q3","stem":"An elastic-stained section of his father's left anterior descending artery shows a plaque with foam cells and a lipid core. The plaque lies between the endothelium and a dark, wavy band, and beneath the band are layers of smooth muscle cells. Which of the following correctly identifies the layer containing the plaque and the band?","options":["Media; the band is the external elastic lamina","Intima; the band is the internal elastic lamina","Adventitia; the band is the external elastic lamina","Media; the band is the internal elastic lamina"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the layers of a muscular artery: intima, internal elastic lamina, media, external elastic lamina, adventitia.","Place the plaque: it sits between the endothelium and the first elastic band, so it is in the intima.","Identify the band: the elastic layer just under the intima, above the smooth muscle media, is the internal elastic lamina."],"why":"Coronary arteries are muscular arteries. From the lumen outward they have an intima (endothelium and subendothelial connective tissue), a wavy internal elastic lamina, a media of circular smooth muscle, an external elastic lamina, and an adventitia. Atherosclerotic plaques form in the intima, above the internal elastic lamina. As a plaque grows, the internal elastic lamina can fragment and the underlying media thins.","whyNot":["The external elastic lamina lies outside the media, so it cannot be the band directly beneath the plaque with smooth muscle below it.","Correct.","The adventitia is the outermost layer, and atheromas do not form there.","The media is the smooth muscle layer beneath the band. The plaque is above the band, so it is in the intima."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of a muscular artery","Location of atherosclerotic plaque"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C47-Q4","stem":"His father's fatal infarction most likely followed rupture of a coronary plaque with a thin fibrous cap. T-helper 1 cells in such plaques release a cytokine that decreases collagen synthesis by intimal smooth muscle cells and activates macrophages. Which of the following cytokines is most likely responsible?","options":["Interleukin 5","Transforming growth factor beta","Interferon gamma","Interleukin 10"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the cell: T-helper 1 cells.","Recall their signature cytokine: interferon gamma, which activates macrophages.","Link it to cap thinning: interferon gamma lowers smooth muscle collagen production while activated macrophages release matrix metalloproteinases."],"why":"The fibrous cap is made of collagen laid down by intimal smooth muscle cells. Th1 cells in the plaque release interferon gamma, which reduces smooth muscle collagen synthesis and activates macrophages. Activated macrophages release matrix metalloproteinases that digest the cap. A thin cap over a large lipid core is the typical vulnerable plaque that ruptures and triggers thrombosis and myocardial infarction.","whyNot":["Interleukin 5 is a Th2 cytokine that drives eosinophil growth and does not reduce cap collagen.","Transforming growth factor beta stimulates collagen synthesis and tends to stabilize plaques.","Correct.","Interleukin 10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that dampens macrophage activation."],"study":{"topics":["Vulnerable plaque","Th1 cells and interferon gamma"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-C"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C48","system":"CV","dx":"Peripheral arterial disease with intermittent claudication","codes":["CV-18-I6"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of cramping pain in her right calf that begins after she walks about two blocks and resolves within a few minutes of standing still. She has smoked for 40 years. Physical examination shows normal femoral pulses bilaterally. The right popliteal, dorsalis pedis, and posterior tibial pulses are diminished. The skin of the right lower leg is shiny with sparse hair. Resting ankle-brachial index (ABI) is 0.62 on the right (normal 1.0 to 1.4) and 1.05 on the left. The diagnosis is peripheral arterial disease of the right leg with intermittent claudication.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C48-Q1","stem":"Based on the pulse findings and the location of her pain, stenosis of which of the following arteries is most likely?","options":["Anterior tibial artery","Superficial femoral artery","Deep artery of the thigh (profunda femoris)","Common iliac artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Use the pulses: a normal femoral pulse places the lesion below the common femoral artery, and a weak popliteal pulse places it above the popliteal artery.","The artery between those two points that continues to the popliteal is the superficial femoral artery in the adductor canal.","Confirm with symptoms: calf claudication fits a femoropopliteal lesion, one level above the painful muscles."],"why":"The femoral artery continues down the thigh as the superficial femoral artery, passes through the adductor canal, and becomes the popliteal artery at the adductor hiatus. A normal femoral pulse with a weak popliteal pulse localizes the narrowing to this segment. The superficial femoral artery in the adductor canal is the most common site of symptomatic atherosclerosis in the leg, and it typically causes calf claudication.","whyNot":["The anterior tibial artery arises below the popliteal artery, so narrowing there would not weaken the popliteal pulse.","Correct.","The deep artery of the thigh supplies the thigh muscles and does not continue to the popliteal artery, so narrowing there would not weaken the popliteal pulse.","A common iliac lesion would weaken the femoral pulse and usually causes buttock and thigh claudication."],"study":{"topics":["Arteries of the lower limb","Localizing PAD by pulses and symptoms"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I6"]}},{"id":"CV-C48-Q2","stem":"Her calf blood flow at rest is normal, yet pain develops with walking. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Arterioles offset the stenosis at rest but not during exercise","Walking triggers sympathetic constriction of the calf arterioles","Exercise lowers arterial pressure, so less blood reaches the calf","Calf contraction collapses the stenosis and stops flow completely"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-12","obj":"CV-12.3","dok":3,"approach":["Think of the circuit as two resistances in series: the fixed stenosis and the adjustable arterioles downstream.","At rest, the arterioles dilate enough to keep total resistance and flow normal.","During exercise the arterioles are already dilated, so total resistance cannot fall enough, and flow cannot rise to meet demand."],"why":"The stenosis adds a fixed resistance in series with the calf arterioles. At rest, the arterioles dilate to compensate, so resting flow stays normal. During walking, muscle metabolites call for flow several times higher, but the arterioles have little dilation left and the stenosis caps the flow. The pressure drop across the stenosis also grows as flow increases. Oxygen supply falls short of demand, anaerobic metabolites build up, and pain begins. It eases when demand drops with rest.","whyNot":["Correct.","During exercise, local metabolic vasodilation in working muscle overrides sympathetic constriction.","Arterial systolic pressure rises during walking. It does not fall.","Muscle contraction does not close a proximal arterial stenosis. The problem is limited flow reserve, not complete collapse."],"study":{"topics":["Flow reserve in claudication","Exercise hyperemia"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C48-Q3","stem":"She drinks alcohol heavily and eats few vegetables. Fasting plasma homocysteine is 24 µmol/L (normal 5 to 15 µmol/L), mean corpuscular volume (MCV) is 106 fL (normal 80 to 100 fL), and serum methylmalonic acid is within the reference range. Deficiency of which of the following vitamins most likely explains her elevated homocysteine concentration?","options":["Cobalamin (vitamin B12)","Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Folate (vitamin B9)","Thiamine (vitamin B1)"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that homocysteine is remethylated to methionine by methionine synthase, which needs N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate and vitamin B12.","Use methylmalonic acid to separate the two: it rises in B12 deficiency and stays normal in folate deficiency.","Combine with the large red cells and poor diet with heavy drinking: folate deficiency."],"why":"Methionine synthase moves a methyl group from N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate, through methylcobalamin, to homocysteine to make methionine. A lack of folate or B12 therefore raises homocysteine and causes a macrocytic anemia. Only B12 deficiency also raises methylmalonic acid, because B12 is a cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. High homocysteine injures endothelium and promotes thrombosis, and it is a recognized risk factor for atherosclerosis.","whyNot":["B12 deficiency would raise both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid. Her methylmalonic acid is normal.","B6 deficiency can raise homocysteine through cystathionine beta-synthase, but it causes a microcytic (sideroblastic) anemia, not a high MCV.","Correct.","Thiamine is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase and does not affect homocysteine metabolism."],"study":{"topics":["Homocysteine metabolism","Folate versus B12 deficiency"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C48-Q4","stem":"Which of the following serious outcomes is she most likely to have over the next 5 years?","options":["Pulmonary embolism","Major amputation of the right leg","Rupture of an abdominal aortic aneurysm","Myocardial infarction or stroke"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I6","obj":"CV-18-I.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that PAD is a marker of widespread atherosclerosis.","Recall that most patients with claudication stay stable in the leg, and few need amputation.","Conclude that coronary and cerebrovascular events are the most common serious outcomes and the leading cause of death."],"why":"Peripheral arterial disease reflects atherosclerosis throughout the arterial tree, so patients with it usually also have coronary and carotid disease. In people with claudication, myocardial infarction and stroke are far more common than limb loss and are the main causes of death. Only a small minority progress to critical limb ischemia or major amputation over five years. This is why PAD is treated as a coronary risk equivalent.","whyNot":["Pulmonary embolism arises from venous thrombosis and is not a typical consequence of arterial atherosclerosis.","Major amputation occurs in only a small minority of patients with claudication over five years, and the risk is lower in those who stop smoking.","PAD raises the chance of having an AAA, but rupture is much less common than myocardial infarction or stroke in this group.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Prognosis of peripheral arterial disease","Atherosclerosis as a systemic disease"],"comps":["CV-18-I6","CV-18-C"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C49","system":"CV","dx":"Giant cell (temporal) arteritis with large-vessel involvement","codes":["CV-18-I5"],"case":"A 74-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of new right-sided headache, jaw claudication, and scalp tenderness. Twice this week she has had transient blurred vision in the right eye. She also has aching and morning stiffness of both shoulders. Physical examination shows a thickened, tender right superficial temporal artery with a diminished pulse. Blood pressure is 142/80 mm Hg in the right arm and 118/74 mm Hg in the left arm. Laboratory studies show an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 88 mm/h (less than 42 for her age and sex) and a C-reactive protein concentration of 64 mg/L (less than 10). Temporal artery biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation of the media with multinucleated giant cells and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina. Diagnosis: giant cell (temporal) arteritis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C49-Q1","stem":"Permanent vision loss in this disease most often results from occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries, which supply the optic nerve head. These arteries are branches of an artery that arises from which of the following vessels?","options":["Superficial temporal artery","Basilar artery","External carotid artery","Internal carotid artery"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: one-eye vision loss from ischemia of the optic nerve head.","The posterior ciliary arteries are branches of the ophthalmic artery.","The ophthalmic artery is a branch of the internal carotid artery, so the eye is fed by a different carotid system than the scalp and jaw."],"why":"Arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is the usual cause of blindness in giant cell arteritis. It results from inflammatory occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries, which come off the ophthalmic artery. The ophthalmic artery is the first major branch of the internal carotid artery after it leaves the cavernous sinus. The biopsied superficial temporal artery belongs to the external carotid system, so this disease can threaten both carotid territories at once.","whyNot":["The superficial temporal artery is the inflamed artery that was biopsied. It is a terminal branch of the external carotid and does not give rise to the vessels that feed the eye.","The basilar artery supplies the brainstem and, through the posterior cerebral arteries, the occipital cortex. Vision loss from that system affects the visual fields of both eyes, while her symptoms are in one eye.","The external carotid artery supplies the scalp, face, and chewing muscles through branches such as the superficial temporal and maxillary arteries. It explains her scalp tenderness and jaw claudication, while the optic nerve head is supplied from the internal carotid system.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ophthalmic artery and its branches","Arteritic ischemic optic neuropathy"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I5"]}},{"id":"CV-C49-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate?","options":["Hemoglobin polymerizes and makes the red cells rigid and sickle shaped","Red cells gain water and swell into spheres that resist stacking","Fibrinogen masks red cell surface charge, letting cells form rouleaux","Albumin binds red cells and adds to their mutual surface repulsion"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-I5","obj":"CV-18-I.12","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: ESR rises in inflammation, so the change must come from acute-phase proteins.","IL-6 drives the liver to make fibrinogen, the main plasma protein that speeds sedimentation.","Fibrinogen lowers the negative-charge repulsion between red cells, so they stack and fall faster."],"why":"IL-6 from the inflamed arteries signals hepatocytes to make acute-phase proteins, chiefly fibrinogen. Red cells normally carry a negative surface charge (the zeta potential) that keeps them apart. Large, asymmetric plasma proteins such as fibrinogen reduce this repulsion, so red cells stack into rouleaux. A stack has more mass for its surface area than a single cell, so it falls faster and the ESR rises.","whyNot":["Sickled cells are rigid and cannot form rouleaux, so sickle cell anemia tends to give a low ESR. Polymerization of hemoglobin S also has no link to inflammation.","Spherocytes stack poorly, so swollen spherical cells lower the ESR, as in hereditary spherocytosis.","Correct.","Albumin opposes rouleaux formation, so a high albumin tends to slow the ESR and a low albumin speeds it. It cannot explain a high ESR in active inflammation."],"study":{"topics":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Acute-phase proteins and IL-6"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C49-Q3","stem":"She also reports aching and fatigue of the left arm when she carries groceries. Imaging shows narrowing of the artery that continues as the left axillary artery. This narrowed artery arises from which of the following?","options":["Brachiocephalic trunk","Arch of the aorta","Left common carotid artery","Thyrocervical trunk"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: lower left arm pressure plus arm claudication means a stenosis upstream of the left axillary artery.","The artery that becomes the axillary artery is the subclavian artery.","The left subclavian is the third branch of the aortic arch; the right subclavian comes from the brachiocephalic trunk."],"why":"The subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery at the outer border of the first rib. On the left, the subclavian artery arises directly from the arch of the aorta as its third branch, after the brachiocephalic trunk and the left common carotid artery. Giant cell arteritis involves the aorta and its main branches in a sizable share of patients, and subclavian narrowing gives arm claudication and unequal arm pressures, as in this woman.","whyNot":["The brachiocephalic trunk gives rise to the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries. It would be the answer for a narrowing that lowered pressure in the right arm.","Correct.","The left common carotid artery is the second arch branch and supplies the head and neck. It does not give rise to the artery of the arm.","The thyrocervical trunk is a branch of the subclavian artery, so it lies downstream of the narrowed vessel."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the aortic arch","Large-vessel giant cell arteritis"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-I5"]}},{"id":"CV-C49-Q4","stem":"In this disease, adventitial dendritic cells present an unidentified antigen to CD4+ T lymphocytes, which then drive inflammation in the media. Which of the following T-lymphocyte responses is mainly responsible for the granulomatous inflammation in her artery wall?","options":["CD8 T cells releasing perforin and granzymes that kill endothelial cells","Th2 cells releasing IL-4 and IL-5 that recruit eosinophils","Th1 cells releasing interferon gamma that activates macrophages","Regulatory T cells releasing IL-10 that quiets macrophages"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.3","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: the biopsy shows granulomas with multinucleated giant cells and no mention of eosinophils or immune complexes.","Granulomas are a delayed (type IV) hypersensitivity pattern driven by CD4 T cells.","Th1 cells release interferon gamma, which activates macrophages that gather into granulomas and fuse into giant cells."],"why":"Giant cell arteritis is a T cell-mediated (type IV) hypersensitivity reaction in the artery wall. Activated dendritic cells in the adventitia prime CD4 T cells, which polarize mainly to Th1 and Th17 cells. Interferon gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages, which form granulomas, fuse into multinucleated giant cells, and release enzymes and growth factors. These damage the elastic laminae and cause intimal thickening that narrows the lumen and causes ischemia, as in her jaw and eye.","whyNot":["CD8 T cells kill cells that display foreign peptide on MHC class I, as in viral infection and transplant rejection. Cytotoxic killing does not build granulomas.","Th2 cells and eosinophils drive allergic inflammation and the eosinophil-rich vasculitis of eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which occurs with asthma. Her biopsy does not describe eosinophils.","Correct.","Regulatory T cells and IL-10 suppress inflammation. Lack of them can allow autoimmunity, but they do not produce granulomas."],"study":{"topics":["Type IV hypersensitivity","Th1 cells and granuloma formation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C50","system":"CV","dx":"Secondary Raynaud phenomenon caused by systemic sclerosis","codes":["CV-18-I8"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 10-year history of episodes in which her fingers turn white, then blue, then red on exposure to cold or emotional stress. Over the past 3 years, the skin of her fingers and face has become thick and tight. She also has heartburn and a sensation of solid food sticking behind the sternum. Physical examination shows tight, shiny skin over the fingers distal to the metacarpophalangeal joints, with normal skin over the upper arms and trunk. There is a healing pitted ulcer on the right index fingertip, firm subcutaneous calcium deposits in two finger pads, and telangiectasias on the face and lips. Nailfold capillaroscopy shows enlarged capillary loops with areas of capillary dropout. Antinuclear antibody assay is positive. Diagnosis: secondary Raynaud phenomenon caused by systemic sclerosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C50-Q1","stem":"During an attack, her fingers turn red as they rewarm after the blue phase. Which of the following best explains the red phase?","options":["Reactive hyperemia from local vasodilator metabolites","Polycythemia that raises the hemoglobin content of skin capillaries","Increased sympathetic alpha-adrenergic tone in the digital arteries","Rising venous pressure that traps deoxygenated blood in the skin"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.6","dok":2,"approach":["Match each color to blood flow: white is no flow, blue is slow flow with deoxygenated blood, red is flow returning.","During ischemia, metabolites build up in the tissue.","When the spasm ends, these metabolites dilate arterioles and flow overshoots: reactive hyperemia."],"why":"The white phase is vasospasm of the digital arteries that stops flow. The blue phase follows as a small amount of slowly moving blood gives up its oxygen. While flow is low, vasodilator metabolites such as adenosine, carbon dioxide, hydrogen ions, and potassium build up in the tissue. When the spasm relaxes, these metabolites hold the arterioles open, and flow rises above its resting level. This overshoot, called reactive hyperemia, makes the fingers red and often throbbing.","whyNot":["Correct.","Polycythemia can make skin look ruddy all the time, but it would not appear in a pattern that follows each cold attack. Her color change comes from a change in flow.","Sympathetic alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction triggered by cold or stress causes the white phase, not the red one.","Slow flow with deoxygenated blood in the skin explains the blue phase. The red phase means fresh arterial blood is flowing in again."],"study":{"topics":["Raynaud color sequence","Reactive hyperemia and local metabolic control"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I8"]}},{"id":"CV-C50-Q2","stem":"Which of the following autoantibodies is most likely to be found in her serum?","options":["Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody","Anti-Jo-1 antibody","Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) antibody","Anticentromere antibody"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-I8","obj":"CV-18-I.10","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: skin thickening only on the fingers and face, with calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, and telangiectasias.","This pattern is the limited cutaneous form of systemic sclerosis (CREST).","Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis is most strongly linked to anticentromere antibody."],"why":"Her skin changes stop at the fingers and face, with calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasias. These five features make up CREST syndrome, the limited cutaneous form of systemic sclerosis. Raynaud phenomenon often comes years before skin changes in this form, as it did for her. Anticentromere antibody is the autoantibody most closely tied to this form, which carries a later risk of pulmonary arterial hypertension.","whyNot":["Anti-double-stranded DNA is specific for systemic lupus erythematosus and tracks with lupus nephritis. It is not typical of systemic sclerosis.","Anti-Jo-1 is a marker of inflammatory myositis (polymyositis or dermatomyositis), often with interstitial lung disease. She has no muscle weakness.","Anti-Scl-70 is linked to diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis, in which skin thickening spreads to the upper arms and trunk and interstitial lung disease is common. Her trunk and upper arms are spared.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Limited vs diffuse systemic sclerosis","Autoantibodies in connective tissue disease"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I8"]}},{"id":"CV-C50-Q3","stem":"The fibrosis in her skin and esophagus is driven largely by transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta), which stimulates fibroblasts to deposit excess collagen. Which of the following best describes how the TGF-beta receptor transmits this signal into the cell?","options":["Serine/threonine phosphorylation of SMAD proteins","Tyrosine autophosphorylation that recruits RAS","Activation of Gs, which raises cyclic AMP","Associated JAK kinases that phosphorylate STAT proteins"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","obj":"CV-14.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the mediator: TGF-beta is the main profibrotic cytokine in systemic sclerosis.","Recall its receptor class: TGF-beta receptors are serine/threonine kinases.","The activated receptor phosphorylates SMAD proteins, which carry the signal to the nucleus."],"why":"TGF-beta binds the type II receptor, a serine/threonine kinase, which then phosphorylates and activates the type I receptor. The type I receptor phosphorylates SMAD2 and SMAD3. These join SMAD4, move into the nucleus, and switch on genes for collagen and other matrix proteins. In systemic sclerosis, persistent TGF-beta signaling keeps fibroblasts producing matrix, which thickens the skin and stiffens the esophageal wall.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tyrosine autophosphorylation and RAS recruitment describe receptor tyrosine kinases such as the receptors for insulin, EGF, and PDGF.","Gs coupling and cyclic AMP are used by receptors such as the beta-adrenergic receptors and the glucagon receptor, not by TGF-beta receptors.","JAK-STAT signaling is used by many cytokine receptors, such as those for IL-6, interferons, and erythropoietin. TGF-beta uses SMAD proteins instead."],"study":{"topics":["TGF-beta and SMAD signaling","Receptor classes and second messengers"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I8"]}},{"id":"CV-C50-Q4","stem":"Fingertip ulceration is rare in primary Raynaud phenomenon. Which of the following changes in her digital arteries best explains why her attacks cause tissue loss?","options":["Immune complex leukocytoclastic vasculitis of postcapillary venules","Intimal proliferation and fibrosis that keep the lumen narrow","Cholesterol-rich atheroma with a thin fibrous cap","Cold-reacting IgM antibodies that agglutinate red cells in the fingers"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I8","obj":"CV-18-I.10","dok":3,"approach":["Compare primary and secondary Raynaud: primary is pure vasospasm in a normal vessel; secondary adds structural vessel disease.","In systemic sclerosis, endothelial injury leads to intimal proliferation and fibrosis of small arteries, and capillaries are lost (seen on nailfold capillaroscopy).","A vessel that is already narrow closes completely during spasm, so ischemia is deeper and lasts longer, causing ulcers."],"why":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon the digital arteries are structurally normal, so blood flow returns fully after each spasm and ulcers are rare. In systemic sclerosis, endothelial injury triggers intimal proliferation and fibrosis of small arteries, and capillaries drop out, as her nailfold capillaroscopy shows. The lumen stays narrow between attacks, so each spasm shuts the vessel completely and ischemia lasts longer. The result is pitting scars, ulcers, and sometimes gangrene of the fingertips.","whyNot":["Leukocytoclastic vasculitis from immune complexes affects postcapillary venules and gives palpable purpura, as in IgA vasculitis or cryoglobulinemia. It does not explain cold-triggered digital ischemia.","Correct.","Atheroma forms mainly in large and medium arteries, not in small digital arteries, and it is not the lesion of systemic sclerosis.","Cold agglutinins cause acrocyanosis and hemolysis in cold exposure. They are not part of systemic sclerosis, and her antibody findings point to an autoimmune connective tissue disease."],"study":{"topics":["Primary vs secondary Raynaud phenomenon","Vasculopathy of systemic sclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-I8","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C51","system":"CV","dx":"Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease)","codes":["CV-18-I9"],"case":"A 36-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of cramping pain in the arch of his right foot when he walks. For the past 2 weeks he has had pain in his right toes at rest that is worse at night. He has smoked about 30 cigarettes daily since the age of 15 years. Last year he had tender, erythematous cords along superficial veins of both forearms that resolved spontaneously. He does not have diabetes mellitus. Physical examination shows a small dry ulcer at the tip of the right great toe. Femoral and popliteal pulses are normal; the right dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are absent. Serum LDL cholesterol concentration is 96 mg/dL (less than 100). Angiography shows segmental occlusions of the tibial arteries with corkscrew-shaped collateral vessels. Diagnosis: thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C51-Q1","stem":"A biopsy specimen of an involved segment of his tibial artery obtained during the acute phase is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Transmural fibrinoid necrosis with lesions at different stages of healing","Lipid-rich atheroma with cholesterol clefts beneath a fibrous cap","Inflamed thrombus with microabscesses that spreads to the nearby vein and nerve","Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells centered on the internal elastic lamina"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I9","obj":"CV-18-I.11","dok":2,"approach":["Key clues: young heavy smoker, distal small and medium arteries, migratory superficial thrombophlebitis, and normal lipids.","Buerger disease is a segmental inflammation of arteries, veins, and nerves of the limbs.","Its acute lesion is a cellular, inflamed thrombus with microabscesses, with inflammation that extends to the neighboring vein and nerve."],"why":"Thromboangiitis obliterans is a segmental, thrombosing inflammation of small and medium arteries of the hands and feet that nearly always occurs in heavy tobacco users. In the acute phase, the lumen holds a highly cellular thrombus with small microabscesses, sometimes with giant cells, and the inflammation spreads to the adjacent vein and nerve. The internal elastic lamina is usually preserved. Later the thrombus organizes and the artery, vein, and nerve are bound together by fibrosis. Nerve involvement helps explain his severe rest pain.","whyNot":["Transmural fibrinoid necrosis with lesions of different ages is the pattern of polyarteritis nodosa. That disease does not involve veins and is not tied to smoking.","Atheroma is the lesion of atherosclerotic peripheral arterial disease, usually in older patients with risk factors such as diabetes and high LDL. His age, normal lipids, and phlebitis point elsewhere.","Correct.","Granulomas with giant cells centered on a fragmented internal elastic lamina describe giant cell arteritis, which affects older adults and the carotid branches and aorta."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of thromboangiitis obliterans","Comparing vasculitis lesions"],"comps":["CV-18-I9","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C51-Q2","stem":"Angiography shows that the longest occlusion is in the artery that descends in the anterior compartment of the leg with the deep fibular nerve. Flow in which of the following arteries is most directly reduced as a result?","options":["Dorsalis pedis artery","Medial plantar artery","Posterior tibial artery","Fibular artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the occluded vessel: the artery of the anterior compartment, running with the deep fibular nerve, is the anterior tibial artery.","Follow it distally: it crosses the front of the ankle and continues on the dorsum of the foot.","Its continuation is the dorsalis pedis artery."],"why":"The popliteal artery divides into the anterior tibial artery and the tibioperoneal trunk. The anterior tibial artery passes into the anterior compartment, runs down with the deep fibular nerve, and crosses the front of the ankle midway between the malleoli. On the dorsum of the foot it continues as the dorsalis pedis artery, whose pulse is felt just lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon. An occlusion of the anterior tibial artery therefore removes direct flow to the dorsalis pedis.","whyNot":["Correct.","The medial plantar artery is a terminal branch of the posterior tibial artery in the sole, so it lies in a different arterial line.","The posterior tibial artery is a branch of the tibioperoneal trunk in the posterior compartment. It runs with the tibial nerve and is felt behind the medial malleolus.","The fibular artery also comes from the tibioperoneal trunk and runs in the posterior compartment near the fibula. It is not downstream of the anterior tibial artery."],"study":{"topics":["Arteries of the leg and foot","Peripheral pulse sites"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I9"]}},{"id":"CV-C51-Q3","stem":"One segment of a tibial artery has its radius reduced by half by thrombus and wall inflammation. If the pressure difference across that segment and blood viscosity remain constant, which of the following best approximates flow through the segment compared with baseline?","options":["About 25 percent of baseline","About 50 percent of baseline","About 12 percent of baseline","About 6 percent of baseline"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["Name the law: Poiseuille's law says resistance is inversely proportional to the radius to the fourth power.","Halving the radius gives (1/2) to the fourth power, which is 1/16.","Resistance rises 16-fold, so flow at the same pressure difference falls to 1/16, about 6 percent."],"why":"By Poiseuille's law, flow equals the pressure difference times pi times the radius to the fourth power, divided by 8 times viscosity times length. Because radius enters to the fourth power, halving it raises resistance 16-fold. With the same pressure difference, flow falls to 1/16 of baseline, which is about 6 percent. This steep relationship is why modest narrowing of small distal arteries can leave toes without enough blood at rest.","whyNot":["About 25 percent (1/4) would follow if flow depended on cross-sectional area (radius squared). That underestimates the effect of narrowing on resistance.","About 50 percent would follow if flow changed in direct proportion to radius. Resistance depends far more steeply on radius than that.","About 12 percent (1/8) would follow if flow depended on the radius cubed. Poiseuille's law uses the fourth power.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille's law","Resistance and vessel radius"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I6"]}},{"id":"CV-C51-Q4","stem":"His carboxyhemoglobin concentration is 8 percent (less than 2 percent in nonsmokers). Which of the following best describes how carbon monoxide further reduces oxygen delivery to his ischemic toes?","options":["It takes up heme sites and shifts the curve left, so less oxygen unloads","It oxidizes heme iron to the ferric form, producing methemoglobin","It shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve to the right, so oxygen unloads too early","It lowers the dissolved oxygen in plasma, reducing the arterial PO2"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-I9","obj":"CV-18-I.11","dok":3,"approach":["Key fact: carbon monoxide binds the iron of heme with an affinity about 200 times or more that of oxygen.","Bound CO lowers oxygen content by taking heme sites.","It also makes the remaining heme groups hold oxygen more tightly (a left shift), so less oxygen is released in tissues."],"why":"Carbon monoxide binds ferrous heme far more tightly than oxygen does, so each CO molecule takes a site that oxygen could have used, lowering oxygen content. CO also changes the shape of the hemoglobin molecule so the remaining heme groups hold their oxygen more tightly, shifting the dissociation curve to the left. Tissues with already poor flow, such as his toes, then get even less oxygen. The arterial PO2 stays normal, because it reflects only dissolved oxygen.","whyNot":["Correct.","Oxidizing heme iron to the ferric (Fe3+) form produces methemoglobin, as with nitrites or dapsone. Carbon monoxide binds ferrous (Fe2+) heme without oxidizing it.","A right shift helps oxygen unload in tissues. Carbon monoxide shifts the curve in the opposite direction.","The arterial PO2 depends on alveolar oxygen and lung function, which carbon monoxide does not change. Oxygen content falls because hemoglobin sites are occupied."],"study":{"topics":["Carbon monoxide and hemoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I9"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C52","system":"CV","dx":"Polyarteritis nodosa associated with hepatitis B","codes":["CV-18-I12"],"case":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of fever, myalgias, and a 6-kg weight loss. For 3 weeks he has had crampy abdominal pain that begins about 30 minutes after meals. One week ago he developed numbness of the left ring and little fingers, and yesterday he awoke with a right foot drop. Blood pressure is 168/98 mm Hg; 1 year ago it was 124/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tender erythematous nodules and livedo reticularis over both legs. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.9 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Urinalysis shows mild proteinuria with no red blood cell casts. Hepatitis B surface antigen is positive, and ANCA is negative. A chest x-ray shows no abnormalities. Angiography shows multiple small aneurysms and segmental narrowing of the renal and mesenteric arteries. Diagnosis: polyarteritis nodosa associated with hepatitis B.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C52-Q1","stem":"His arterial injury is attributed to a viral protein circulating in his blood. Which of the following immune mechanisms best explains the damage to his arteries?","options":["Sensitized T cells form granulomas around viral antigen in the arterial media","Antigen-antibody complexes deposit in vessel walls and activate complement","Antibodies bind antigens fixed on the surface of normal endothelial cells","IgE bound to mast cells triggers histamine release within the vessel wall"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.3","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: hepatitis B surface antigen circulates in large amounts in his blood.","Soluble antigen plus antibody forms circulating immune complexes.","Complexes that lodge in vessel walls fix complement and recruit neutrophils: type III hypersensitivity."],"why":"In hepatitis B-associated polyarteritis nodosa, circulating hepatitis B surface antigen combines with antibody to form immune complexes. These deposit in the walls of medium-sized arteries, activate complement, and draw in neutrophils, whose enzymes and oxidants cause fibrinoid necrosis. This is type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity. The weakened wall then forms the small aneurysms and narrowings seen on his angiogram.","whyNot":["T cell granulomas are type IV hypersensitivity, the mechanism of giant cell arteritis. Polyarteritis nodosa shows neutrophils and fibrinoid necrosis without granulomas.","Correct.","Antibody against an antigen fixed on cells or matrix is type II hypersensitivity, as in anti-glomerular basement membrane disease. Here the antigen circulates in the blood.","IgE on mast cells is type I hypersensitivity, which causes allergy and anaphylaxis. It does not cause necrotizing arteritis."],"study":{"topics":["Type III hypersensitivity","Hepatitis B and vasculitis"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C52-Q2","stem":"Three years ago, after donating blood, he was told that he tested positive for hepatitis B surface antigen. Today, tests for IgM antibody to hepatitis B core antigen and for antibody to hepatitis B surface antigen are negative. Which of the following best describes the status of his hepatitis B infection?","options":["Acute infection in its early phase","Past infection with lasting immunity","Chronic infection","Window period of a resolving infection"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-I12","obj":"CV-18-I.14","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: surface antigen has been present for years, far longer than 6 months.","IgM anti-core is a marker of recent infection, and it is negative.","Persistent surface antigen without anti-surface antibody means chronic infection."],"why":"Hepatitis B surface antigen that persists for more than 6 months defines chronic infection. IgM antibody to core antigen appears in acute infection and fades over months, so a negative result fits long-standing infection. Antibody to surface antigen appears when the infection clears or after vaccination, and he has none. Ongoing antigen production in chronic infection supplies the antigen for the immune complexes in his arteries.","whyNot":["Acute infection would show IgM antibody to core antigen, and surface antigen would not have been present 3 years ago.","Past infection with immunity shows antibody to surface antigen and IgG anti-core, with no surface antigen in the blood.","Correct.","In the window period surface antigen has already disappeared and antibody to surface antigen has not yet appeared, with IgM anti-core as the main marker. His surface antigen is positive."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis B serology","Acute vs chronic hepatitis B"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C52-Q3","stem":"His postprandial abdominal pain is caused by narrowing of the artery that supplies the jejunum and ileum. This artery arises from which of the following locations?","options":["Anterior aorta at about the T12 vertebra","Anterior aorta at about the L1 vertebra","Lateral aorta at about the L1 to L2 vertebrae","Anterior aorta at about the L3 vertebra"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","obj":"CV-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the artery: the jejunum and ileum are midgut, supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.","Recall the unpaired anterior branches of the abdominal aorta and their levels: celiac trunk T12, superior mesenteric L1, inferior mesenteric L3.","The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at about L1."],"why":"The jejunum and ileum are midgut structures supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. It arises from the anterior surface of the abdominal aorta at about the L1 vertebral level, just below the celiac trunk, and passes behind the neck of the pancreas. Narrowing of its branches limits the increase in intestinal blood flow that normally follows a meal, which causes postprandial pain and food avoidance with weight loss, a pattern called intestinal angina.","whyNot":["About T12 is the level of the celiac trunk, which supplies the foregut (stomach, liver, spleen, and proximal duodenum).","Correct.","Paired lateral branches at about L1 to L2 are the renal arteries. They are also often involved in polyarteritis nodosa, but they do not supply the small bowel.","About L3 is the level of the inferior mesenteric artery, which supplies the hindgut from the distal transverse colon to the upper rectum."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the abdominal aorta","Midgut blood supply"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C52-Q4","stem":"His blood pressure has increased as aneurysms and stenoses have developed in his renal arteries. Which of the following is the initiating event in this increase in blood pressure?","options":["Macula densa cells sense a rise in tubular sodium chloride and trigger renin release","Atrial myocytes release natriuretic peptide in response to a larger blood volume","Carotid baroreceptors increase their firing and withdraw sympathetic tone","Juxtaglomerular cells sense a lower afferent arteriolar pressure and release renin"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: narrowed renal arteries lower the pressure reaching the glomeruli.","Juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole act as pressure sensors and release renin when pressure falls.","Renin starts the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which raises vascular resistance and sodium retention."],"why":"Narrowing of renal arteries lowers the pressure in the afferent arterioles downstream. The juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole wall sense this lower stretch and release renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which angiotensin-converting enzyme turns into angiotensin II. Angiotensin II constricts arterioles and stimulates aldosterone release, so sodium retention and vasoconstriction raise systemic blood pressure. This renovascular hypertension is common in polyarteritis nodosa.","whyNot":["A fall in sodium chloride delivery to the macula densa stimulates renin release. A rise in sodium chloride delivery suppresses renin.","Atrial natriuretic peptide promotes sodium excretion and vasodilation, so it lowers blood pressure.","Increased baroreceptor firing withdraws sympathetic tone, which lowers blood pressure. It is a response to high pressure, not its cause.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Renovascular hypertension"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-A2","CV-18-I12"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C53","system":"CV","dx":"Kawasaki disease with coronary artery dilation","codes":["CV-18-I12"],"case":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of fever for 6 days and marked irritability. His temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F). Physical examination shows bilateral nonexudative conjunctival injection with limbal sparing, red cracked lips, and a strawberry tongue. There is a polymorphous erythematous rash over the trunk, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and a single firm 2-cm left cervical lymph node. Laboratory studies show a C-reactive protein concentration of 110 mg/L (less than 10) and a leukocyte count of 19,600/µL (about 6,000 to 17,000 at his age). A rapid streptococcal antigen test and throat culture are negative. Echocardiography shows dilation of the proximal left anterior descending coronary artery. Diagnosis: Kawasaki disease.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C53-Q1","stem":"Kawasaki disease targets medium-sized muscular arteries such as the coronary arteries. Histologic examination of a normal segment of his left anterior descending artery is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Endothelium with fenestrations and a basal lamina, with no media at all","A media built mostly of many fenestrated elastic lamellae","A smooth muscle media bounded by a distinct internal elastic lamina","A thin media, wide lumen, and valves formed by folds of intima"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the vessel: coronary arteries are medium-sized muscular (distributing) arteries.","Recall the muscular artery wall: a thick media of smooth muscle between a prominent internal elastic lamina and an external elastic lamina.","Rule out the elastic artery, vein, and capillary patterns."],"why":"Muscular arteries, such as the coronary, renal, and mesenteric arteries, have a media made mostly of layered smooth muscle. A distinct, wavy internal elastic lamina separates the intima from the media, and an external elastic lamina often marks the outer edge of the media. In Kawasaki disease, inflammation damages this smooth muscle and elastic tissue, and the weakened segment balloons into an aneurysm.","whyNot":["Endothelium with pores (fenestrations) on a basal lamina describes a fenestrated capillary, found in places such as the kidney and endocrine glands.","Many fenestrated elastic lamellae in the media mark an elastic (conducting) artery such as the aorta or pulmonary trunk. Kawasaki disease usually spares these.","Correct.","A thin media, a wide lumen, and valves describe a medium-sized vein, especially in the limbs."],"study":{"topics":["Muscular vs elastic artery histology","Vessel wall layers"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-8"]}},{"id":"CV-C53-Q2","stem":"Scarlet fever was considered in the differential diagnosis because it can also cause a red tongue, rash, and later desquamation. The rash of scarlet fever is caused by a streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin that acts as a superantigen. Which of the following best describes how a superantigen activates T lymphocytes?","options":["It cross-links IgE on mast cells and basophils, releasing histamine","It links MHC class II to the T cell receptor beta chain outside the groove","It is processed and presented in the MHC class II groove like a normal peptide","It binds CD14 and TLR4 on macrophages in the same way as endotoxin"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the toxin: streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin A of Streptococcus pyogenes is a superantigen.","A superantigen skips antigen processing and binds the outside of MHC class II and the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain.","Because many T cells share beta chain variable regions, a huge number are activated at once, releasing large amounts of cytokines."],"why":"A normal peptide antigen is processed, loaded into the groove of an MHC molecule, and recognized only by the rare T cell clones whose receptors fit it. A superantigen is not processed. It binds intact to the outer surface of MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells and to the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain. This links any T cell with a matching beta chain, activating a large fraction of all T cells. The surge of cytokines such as IL-2, interferon gamma, and TNF causes fever, rash, and, with toxic shock toxins, shock.","whyNot":["Cross-linking IgE on mast cells and basophils is type I hypersensitivity, triggered by allergens, not streptococcal toxins.","Correct.","Processing and presentation in the MHC groove is how conventional peptide antigens work. Only a tiny fraction of T cells respond to any one peptide.","CD14 and TLR4 recognize lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) from gram-negative bacteria and activate innate immunity. Streptococci are gram-positive and lack lipopolysaccharide."],"study":{"topics":["Superantigens","Streptococcus pyogenes exotoxins"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C53-Q3","stem":"Over the next 2 weeks, the dilated segment enlarges into an aneurysm with an internal diameter 3 times that of the normal segment just upstream. Total blood flow through the artery is unchanged. Which of the following best describes the mean blood velocity in the aneurysm compared with the velocity upstream?","options":["About the same as upstream","About one third as fast","About three times as fast","About one ninth as fast"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","obj":"CV-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Apply the continuity principle: flow equals velocity times cross-sectional area, and flow is the same in both segments.","Area depends on the diameter squared, so tripling the diameter makes the area 9 times larger.","Velocity falls to 1/9, and slow flow in the aneurysm favors thrombus formation."],"why":"When the same volume of blood per minute passes through segments in series, flow equals velocity times cross-sectional area in each segment. Cross-sectional area depends on the radius (or diameter) squared, so a 3-fold wider lumen has 9 times the area. Average velocity there drops to about one ninth of the upstream value. Slow, swirling flow in the aneurysm, together with injured endothelium and the high platelet counts of later Kawasaki disease, promotes thrombus that can block the artery and cause myocardial infarction.","whyNot":["Velocity stays the same only if the cross-sectional area is unchanged. Here the area is much larger.","One third as fast would be true if velocity depended on diameter directly. Area, and so velocity, depends on diameter squared.","Velocity rises, not falls, where a vessel narrows. Three times faster would fit a segment whose area was one third of normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Continuity of flow and velocity","Thrombosis in coronary aneurysms"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C53-Q4","stem":"In the third week of illness, the skin of his fingertips and toes begins to desquamate. Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely at this stage?","options":["Positive antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (ANCA)","Rising antistreptolysin O titer","Platelet count well above the reference range","Low complement C3 and C4 levels"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I12","obj":"CV-18-I.15","dok":2,"approach":["Place him on the timeline: peeling of the fingers and toes marks the subacute phase, about weeks 2 to 4.","Recall the hallmark laboratory change of this phase: reactive thrombocytosis.","Rule out markers of other diseases: ANCA vasculitis, streptococcal infection, and immune complex disease."],"why":"Kawasaki disease runs in phases. In the acute febrile phase, platelet counts are usually normal. In the subacute phase, about weeks 2 to 4, the fingers and toes peel and the platelet count rises, often to 500,000 to more than 1,000,000/µL (reference about 150,000 to 450,000). Platelet counts then fall back toward normal during convalescence. The high platelet count adds to the risk of thrombosis inside coronary aneurysms in this phase.","whyNot":["ANCA is a marker of small-vessel vasculitides such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis. It is negative in Kawasaki disease.","A rising antistreptolysin O titer points to a recent Streptococcus pyogenes infection, as in rheumatic fever. His streptococcal tests were negative.","Correct.","Low C3 and C4 suggest complement consumption by immune complexes, as in lupus or cryoglobulinemia. Complement is not low in Kawasaki disease."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of Kawasaki disease","Laboratory findings in vasculitis"],"comps":["CV-18-I12","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C54","system":"CV","dx":"Multifocal infantile hemangiomas with liver involvement","codes":["CV-18-J1"],"case":"A 10-week-old girl is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. She was born at 31 weeks' gestation. Two weeks after birth, her parents noticed small red marks on her scalp, back, and right thigh, which have since grown rapidly. She is feeding well and gaining weight. Physical examination shows seven bright red, raised, lobulated lesions 0.5 to 2 cm in diameter that partially blanch with pressure. Because she has five or more cutaneous lesions, abdominal ultrasonography is performed and shows several small, well-defined, highly vascular lesions scattered throughout the liver. Diagnosis: multifocal infantile hemangiomas of the skin with liver involvement.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C54-Q1","stem":"The skin lesions consist of tightly packed, capillary-sized channels lined by plump endothelial cells. In a normal continuous capillary, which of the following cells surrounds the endothelium and lies within the same basal lamina?","options":["Myoepithelial cell","Fibroblast","Smooth muscle cell","Pericyte"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","obj":"CV-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: the channels are capillary-sized, so compare them with the normal capillary wall.","A capillary wall has endothelium, a basal lamina, and scattered contractile cells with long processes.","These cells, enclosed by the endothelial basal lamina, are pericytes."],"why":"A continuous capillary is a tube of endothelial cells joined by tight junctions and resting on a basal lamina. Pericytes wrap their long processes around the endothelium and sit inside a split in that basal lamina. They have contractile proteins, help stabilize the vessel, and can give rise to smooth muscle cells and fibroblasts after injury. Proliferating infantile hemangiomas contain both endothelial cells and pericytes arranged in capillary-sized lobules.","whyNot":["Myoepithelial cells wrap glandular secretory units, such as sweat and breast glands, not blood vessels.","Fibroblasts lie in the surrounding connective tissue outside the basal lamina and do not wrap the endothelium.","Smooth muscle cells form the media of arteries, arterioles, and veins. Capillaries have no muscular media.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Capillary structure","Pericytes"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-J1"]}},{"id":"CV-C54-Q2","stem":"At 14 weeks of age, the liver lesions have increased in size and number. Her serum TSH concentration is now increased and her free T4 concentration decreased for her age, although her newborn thyroid screen was normal. An enzyme produced by the hemangioma tissue is inactivating thyroid hormone faster than her thyroid gland can replace it. Which of the following reactions does this enzyme catalyze?","options":["Removing an inner-ring iodine, converting T4 to reverse T3","Removing an outer-ring iodine, converting T4 into active T3","Blocking iodide uptake by the sodium-iodide symporter","Blocking thyroid peroxidase coupling of iodotyrosines"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-J1","obj":"CV-18-J.1","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: normal newborn screen, then low free T4 with high TSH once large liver hemangiomas appear, so the hormone is being destroyed outside the thyroid.","Hemangioma tissue can express type 3 iodothyronine deiodinase.","Type 3 deiodinase removes an inner-ring iodine, turning T4 into inactive reverse T3 (and T3 into inactive T2)."],"why":"Infantile hemangiomas, especially large or numerous liver lesions, can express high levels of type 3 iodothyronine deiodinase. This enzyme removes an iodine from the inner ring of thyroid hormone, converting T4 into inactive reverse T3 and T3 into inactive T2. When the lesions break down hormone faster than the thyroid can make it, free T4 falls and TSH rises. This is called consumptive hypothyroidism, and it matters in infancy because thyroid hormone is needed for brain development.","whyNot":["Correct.","Removing an outer-ring iodine is done by type 1 and type 2 deiodinases and activates the hormone by making T3. It would not lower her thyroid hormone activity.","Blocking the sodium-iodide symporter reduces hormone synthesis within the gland, as perchlorate or thiocyanate can. The problem here is breakdown of hormone outside the thyroid.","Blocking thyroid peroxidase reduces hormone synthesis within the gland, as thionamide drugs do. Her gland works normally, as shown by her normal newborn screen."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid hormone deiodinases","Consumptive hypothyroidism"],"comps":["CV-18-J1","CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C54-Q3","stem":"Over the next month the liver lesions enlarge. Her pulse is 180/min (about 100 to 160 at her age). Physical examination shows bounding pulses, a wide pulse pressure, and hepatomegaly. Which of the following is the initiating change in this high-output state?","options":["Reduced venous return as the enlarged liver presses on the vena cava","Shunting through the lesions that lowers systemic vascular resistance","Lower contractility that reduces stroke volume and causes reflex tachycardia","Higher systemic vascular resistance from a surge of catecholamine release"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-J1","obj":"CV-18-J.1","dok":3,"approach":["Key clues: bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure, and tachycardia point to high cardiac output with low resistance.","Large vascular liver lesions act as low-resistance shunts between arteries and veins.","Lower systemic vascular resistance lowers diastolic pressure and increases venous return, so the heart must pump more blood each minute."],"why":"Large or numerous liver hemangiomas can contain arteriovenous and portovenous shunts. Blood takes this low-resistance path, so systemic vascular resistance falls. Diastolic pressure drops, which widens the pulse pressure and makes pulses bounding. More blood returns quickly to the heart, and cardiac output rises with tachycardia to keep arterial pressure up. If the demand outstrips the heart, she develops high-output heart failure with an enlarged liver.","whyNot":["Reduced venous return lowers cardiac output by the Frank-Starling mechanism. A high-output state needs more venous return, not less.","Correct.","Lower contractility reduces stroke volume and cardiac output. It would give weak pulses and a narrow pulse pressure, not bounding pulses with a wide pulse pressure.","Higher systemic vascular resistance would narrow the pulse pressure and lower cardiac output. Her findings show the opposite."],"study":{"topics":["High-output heart failure","Systemic vascular resistance and pulse pressure"],"comps":["CV-18-J1","CV-11","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C54-Q4","stem":"Her parents ask what the skin lesions will do over time if they cause no complications. Which of the following is the most likely course?","options":["More growth for a few months, then slow regression over several years","Slow conversion into angiosarcoma during early childhood","Steady shrinking that is complete within a few more weeks","Growth in proportion to her body throughout life, without any regression"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-J1","obj":"CV-18-J.1","dok":2,"approach":["Key clues: lesions absent at birth, appeared in the first weeks, and are growing fast.","This is the natural history of infantile hemangioma: rapid growth in early infancy, then slow involution.","Rule out the patterns of vascular malformations and malignant vascular tumors."],"why":"Infantile hemangiomas are absent or faint at birth and appear in the first weeks of life. Most of their growth happens in the first 3 to 5 months. Growth then slows and stops, and the lesions involute slowly over several years, with most regression finished by about age 4. Some leave behind thin, wrinkled skin, fibrofatty tissue, or telangiectasias. Female sex and preterm birth, both present here, are known risk factors.","whyNot":["Correct.","Infantile hemangiomas are benign and do not turn into angiosarcoma. Angiosarcoma is a rare malignant tumor, usually of older adults.","Infantile hemangiomas do not disappear within weeks. Involution takes years.","Growth in proportion to the child with no regression describes a vascular malformation, such as a capillary malformation (port-wine stain), which is present at birth."],"study":{"topics":["Natural history of infantile hemangioma","Hemangioma vs vascular malformation"],"comps":["CV-18-J1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C01","system":"EN","dx":"Graves disease in pregnancy","codes":["EN-12-A4"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman, gravida 2, para 1, at 20 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of palpitations, heat intolerance, and a 4-kg (9-lb) weight loss despite a good appetite. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 128/64 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a diffusely enlarged, symmetric, nontender thyroid gland with a bruit, bilateral proptosis with lid retraction, and a fine tremor of the outstretched hands. Serum studies show a TSH concentration of less than 0.01 μU/mL (0.4 to 4.0) and a free T4 concentration of 3.4 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8). Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin is present at a high titer. Diagnosis: Graves disease in pregnancy.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C01-Q1","stem":"Her 66-year-old aunt also has palpitations and weight loss. The aunt's serum TSH concentration is low and her free T4 concentration is high. Physical examination shows an irregular, nodular thyroid gland and no eye or skin abnormalities. A radioactive iodine uptake scan of the aunt's thyroid gland is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Diffusely increased uptake throughout both lobes","One cold nodule with normal uptake in the remaining gland tissue","Diffusely decreased uptake throughout both lobes","Several hot nodules with suppressed uptake between them"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A4","obj":"EN-12-A.9","dok":3,"approach":["Both women have thyrotoxicosis: low TSH with high free T4.","The aunt is older, has a nodular gland, and has no eye or skin signs. This points to toxic multinodular goiter.","In toxic multinodular goiter, autonomous nodules make hormone, the high hormone level suppresses TSH, and the rest of the gland goes quiet. The scan shows patchy uptake."],"why":"Toxic multinodular goiter develops when some nodules in a long-standing goiter become autonomous and make thyroid hormone without TSH control. The extra hormone suppresses TSH, so the normal tissue between the nodules takes up little iodine. The scan shows several hot nodules on a quiet background. In Graves disease, antibodies stimulate every follicular cell, so uptake is diffusely increased, and only Graves disease causes ophthalmopathy and pretibial myxedema.","whyNot":["Diffusely increased uptake is the Graves disease pattern, because the stimulating antibody acts on the whole gland. Radioiodine is not given in pregnancy, so the pregnant patient would not be scanned until after delivery.","A single cold nodule takes up little iodine and does not make the patient thyrotoxic. Cold nodules raise concern for cancer.","Diffusely low uptake with thyrotoxicosis means hormone leaking from a damaged gland (subacute or painless thyroiditis) or taken by mouth. It does not fit a nodular gland making excess hormone.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Graves disease versus toxic multinodular goiter","Radioactive iodine uptake patterns"],"comps":["EN-12-A4","EN-12-C2"]}},{"id":"EN-C01-Q2","stem":"Her thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin is of the IgG class. Which of the following is the most likely effect of this antibody on her infant after delivery?","options":["Transient hypothyroidism from maternal IgM blocking the TSH receptor","Permanent hypothyroidism from antibody-mediated follicular destruction","Transient thyrotoxicosis that resolves as maternal IgG is cleared","Congenital goiter from fetal T cells sensitized to thyroglobulin"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-10","obj":"EN-10.1","dok":3,"approach":["Name the mechanism: Graves disease is a type II hypersensitivity in which IgG binds the TSH receptor and turns it on.","IgG is the only antibody class that crosses the placenta, carried by the neonatal Fc receptor.","Maternal IgG stimulates the fetal and newborn thyroid until it is broken down over weeks to a few months, so the effect is temporary."],"why":"In Graves disease, thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin binds the TSH receptor and acts like TSH, which is a stimulatory form of type II hypersensitivity. Because it is IgG, it crosses the placenta. When the mother's titer is high, the antibody stimulates the infant's thyroid and causes neonatal thyrotoxicosis, with tachycardia, poor weight gain, and irritability. The infant makes none of this antibody, so the condition fades as maternal IgG is cleared over about 1 to 3 months.","whyNot":["Some mothers make TSH receptor blocking antibodies that cause transient neonatal hypothyroidism, but those are IgG. IgM is too large to cross the placenta.","Antibody-mediated destruction is not the Graves mechanism, and passively acquired antibody cannot cause permanent disease because the infant does not keep making it.","Correct.","Fetal T cells are not primed against maternal thyroid antigens. A congenital goiter can occur in Graves pregnancies, but it comes from maternal stimulating IgG or antithyroid drugs."],"study":{"topics":["Neonatal Graves disease","Placental transfer of IgG"],"comps":["EN-10","EN-12-A4"]}},{"id":"EN-C01-Q3","stem":"Her obstetrician compares her results with those of a euthyroid patient at the same gestational age. The comparison patient has a total T4 concentration of 15 μg/dL (5 to 12), a free T4 concentration of 1.2 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8), and a TSH concentration of 1.5 μU/mL (0.4 to 4.0). Which of the following best explains the comparison patient's laboratory findings?","options":["Placental hCG stimulation of the thyrotropin receptor","More thyroxine-binding globulin made by the liver","Greater peripheral deiodination of T4 to T3","Reduced hepatic degradation of free thyroxine"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the split: total T4 is high, but free T4 and TSH are normal, so she is euthyroid.","Total T4 includes bound hormone. A rise in total with a normal free level means more binding protein.","Estrogen in pregnancy raises hepatic production of thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG)."],"why":"About 99.97% of T4 in plasma is bound to proteins, mostly TBG, and only the free fraction enters cells and acts. Estrogen in pregnancy increases TBG synthesis and slows its clearance. More TBG binds more T4, so free T4 falls briefly, TSH rises a little, and the gland makes more hormone until free T4 is normal again. The result is a high total T4 with normal free T4 and TSH. This is why free T4 and TSH are the tests used to judge thyroid status in pregnancy.","whyNot":["hCG shares an alpha subunit with TSH and can weakly stimulate the TSH receptor, mostly near the end of the first trimester. That raises free T4 and lowers TSH, which this patient does not show.","Correct.","More conversion of T4 to T3 would use up T4 and would not raise total T4.","Slower breakdown of free T4 would raise free T4 and suppress TSH. Her free T4 and TSH are normal."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroxine-binding globulin","Thyroid tests in pregnancy"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C01-Q4","stem":"A biopsy specimen of her thyroid gland taken before any treatment would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Flattened cuboidal cells lining follicles distended by colloid","Dense fibrosis replacing follicles and extending beyond the capsule","Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells around damaged follicles","Tall columnar cells lining follicles with scalloped, pale colloid"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall normal follicles: cuboidal cells around a lumen filled with colloid.","In Graves disease, antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor continuously, so follicular cells are overactive.","Overactive cells grow taller and take colloid back in faster, so colloid is scant and its edge looks scalloped."],"why":"Normal thyroid follicles are lined by cuboidal cells around a lumen of colloid, which stores thyroglobulin. Constant stimulation in Graves disease makes the follicular cells hypertrophic and hyperplastic, so they become tall and columnar and may fold into the lumen. The cells pull colloid back in for hormone release faster than it is stored, so the colloid is pale and scant, with scalloped edges where it is being resorbed.","whyNot":["Flat cells around large colloid-filled follicles mean an inactive gland storing colloid, as in a colloid goiter after TSH stimulation has eased.","Fibrosis that spreads beyond the capsule into the neck is Riedel thyroiditis, which gives a rock-hard gland.","Granulomas with giant cells around injured follicles are typical of subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis, a painful gland after a viral illness.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid follicle histology","Histology of Graves disease"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-12-A4"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C02","system":"EN","dx":"Thyroid storm in toxic multinodular goiter","codes":["EN-12-A4"],"case":"A 74-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by his daughter because of a 1-day history of confusion, agitation, vomiting, and diarrhea. He has had a large nodular goiter for 20 years. Three weeks ago, he underwent CT of the abdomen with intravenous iodinated contrast. Temperature is 40.1 °C (104.2 °F), pulse is 148/min and irregularly irregular, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 152/58 mm Hg. He is disoriented. The skin is warm and moist. Examination shows a large, nontender, multinodular thyroid gland, a tremor, and no proptosis. An ECG shows atrial fibrillation with a rapid ventricular response. Serum TSH concentration is less than 0.01 μU/mL (0.4 to 4.0), and free T4 concentration is 4.8 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8). Diagnosis: thyroid storm in toxic multinodular goiter.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C02-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his tachycardia and atrial fibrillation?","options":["Increased cardiac beta-1 adrenergic receptor expression","Raised plasma catecholamine concentrations","Increased vagal tone at the sinoatrial and AV nodes","Left ventricular pressure overload from high vascular resistance"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A4","obj":"EN-12-A.10","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize thyroid storm: severe thyrotoxicosis with fever, delirium, and a rapid arrhythmia.","Thyroid hormone makes the heart more sensitive to normal catecholamine levels.","The cause is more beta-1 receptors on cardiac cells, which is why beta blockers control the heart rate quickly."],"why":"Thyroid hormone increases the number of beta-1 adrenergic receptors in the heart and raises the rate of atrial depolarization. Ordinary amounts of catecholamine then produce a much larger effect, so patients have sinus tachycardia, palpitations, and, especially in older adults, atrial fibrillation. Thyroid storm is the life-threatening extreme of thyrotoxicosis, often set off by a trigger such as an iodine load, infection, or surgery. Beta blockers such as propranolol are a first step in treatment because they block this heightened sensitivity.","whyNot":["Correct.","Plasma catecholamine levels are normal or low in thyrotoxicosis. The sympathetic signs come from greater tissue sensitivity. High catecholamines would point to pheochromocytoma.","More vagal tone slows the heart. Thyroid hormone does the opposite.","Thyroid hormone relaxes arterioles and lowers systemic vascular resistance, which is why his diastolic pressure is low and his pulse pressure wide."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac effects of thyroid hormone","Thyroid storm"],"comps":["EN-12-A4","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C02-Q2","stem":"Treatment includes propranolol, an antithyroid drug, and intravenous hydrocortisone. In addition to covering possible adrenal insufficiency, hydrocortisone reduces the effects of thyroid hormone on his tissues by which of the following mechanisms?","options":["Reducing peripheral conversion of T4 to T3","Blocking thyroid hormone binding to nuclear receptors","Inhibiting iodide uptake by the sodium-iodide symporter","Blocking release of stored hormone from thyroglobulin"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["The gland releases mostly T4, which is a prohormone.","Most T3, the active form, is made in peripheral tissues by deiodinase enzymes that remove one iodine from T4.","Glucocorticoids, propylthiouracil, and propranolol all slow this conversion, which lowers the active hormone quickly."],"why":"The thyroid secretes far more T4 than T3, yet T3 binds the nuclear receptor with about 10 times the affinity. Most circulating T3 comes from 5'-deiodination of T4 in the liver, kidney, and muscle. High-dose glucocorticoids inhibit this conversion, so T3 levels fall within a day or so. This is useful in thyroid storm because the antithyroid drug stops new hormone synthesis but does nothing to hormone already in the blood.","whyNot":["Correct.","No standard drug blocks the thyroid hormone receptor. Glucocorticoids work before that step by lowering the amount of T3 made.","Perchlorate and thiocyanate compete with iodide at the symporter. Glucocorticoids do not block iodide uptake.","Large doses of iodide, given at least an hour after the antithyroid drug, block release of stored hormone. Glucocorticoids do not act this way."],"study":{"topics":["Deiodinases and T3 activation","Treatment of thyroid storm"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-8"]}},{"id":"EN-C02-Q3","stem":"The antithyroid drug he receives is propylthiouracil. This drug lowers the synthesis of new thyroid hormone by inhibiting which of the following steps in the follicular cell?","options":["Iodide transport into the cell by the sodium-iodide symporter","Oxidation and organification of iodide on thyroglobulin","Endocytosis and proteolysis of thyroglobulin to free T4 and T3","Secretion of iodide into the follicular lumen by pendrin"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-8","obj":"EN-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["List the steps: iodide trapping, transfer into the lumen, oxidation and organification, coupling, then storage and release.","Thionamides (propylthiouracil and methimazole) inhibit thyroid peroxidase.","Thyroid peroxidase oxidizes iodide, attaches it to tyrosines on thyroglobulin, and couples MIT and DIT."],"why":"Follicular cells take up iodide through the sodium-iodide symporter on the basolateral membrane. Pendrin moves it into the follicular lumen. At the apical membrane, thyroid peroxidase oxidizes iodide and attaches it to tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin to form MIT and DIT, then couples them into T3 and T4. Propylthiouracil and methimazole block thyroid peroxidase, so no new hormone is made. Hormone already stored in colloid can still be released, which is why the effect takes weeks to become complete.","whyNot":["Iodide trapping is blocked by perchlorate and thiocyanate. It is also the step that radioactive iodine depends on.","Correct.","Lysosomal proteolysis of thyroglobulin releases stored hormone. Large doses of iodide and lithium slow this step.","Pendrin moves iodide across the apical membrane into the lumen. Its genetic loss causes Pendred syndrome (goiter with deafness), and thionamides do not target it."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid hormone synthesis","Thionamide mechanism"],"comps":["EN-8","EN-7"]}},{"id":"EN-C02-Q4","stem":"On examination, his nodular neck mass rises when he swallows. Which of the following best explains this movement?","options":["Fixation to the prevertebral fascia over the vertebral bodies","Suspension from the hyoid bone by the infrahyoid muscles","Enclosure within the carotid sheath beside the larynx","Pretracheal fascia binding it to the laryngeal cartilages"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-3","obj":"EN-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the gland: the lobes lie beside the larynx and trachea, and the isthmus crosses the second and third tracheal rings.","The gland is wrapped in the pretracheal layer of deep cervical fascia, which attaches to the thyroid and cricoid cartilages.","The larynx rises during swallowing, so the thyroid and any mass in it rise too."],"why":"The thyroid sits in the pretracheal (visceral) fascia, which is attached above to the thyroid and cricoid cartilages. Dense ligaments also bind the gland to the cricoid cartilage and upper tracheal rings. When the larynx moves up during swallowing, the thyroid moves with it. A neck mass that rises on swallowing is therefore likely to be in the thyroid gland.","whyNot":["Prevertebral fascia covers the vertebral column and the muscles on it. A structure fixed there would stay still during swallowing.","The infrahyoid (strap) muscles lie in front of the thyroid but do not suspend it. Their own movement does not explain why a mass inside the gland rises.","The carotid sheath holds the common carotid artery, internal jugular vein, and vagus nerve lateral to the thyroid. It is not attached to the larynx and does not carry the gland upward.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid gland position and fascia","Examination of a neck mass"],"comps":["EN-3"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C03","system":"EN","dx":"Hashimoto thyroiditis with primary hypothyroidism","codes":["EN-12-C1","EN-12-B5"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of fatigue, cold intolerance, constipation, heavy menses, and a 5-kg (11-lb) weight gain. She has vitiligo. Temperature is 36.2 °C (97.2 °F), pulse is 56/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 132/86 mm Hg. Physical examination shows dry, coarse skin and a diffusely enlarged, firm, rubbery, nontender thyroid gland. The relaxation phase of the ankle reflexes is delayed. Serum studies show a TSH concentration of 24 μU/mL (0.4 to 4.0), a free T4 concentration of 0.5 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8), and an anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody titer of 640 IU/mL (less than 35). Diagnosis: Hashimoto thyroiditis with primary hypothyroidism.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C03-Q1","stem":"A core biopsy specimen of her thyroid gland is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Hyperplastic follicles lined by tall columnar cells","Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells around leaked colloid","Lymphoid follicles with germinal centers and Hürthle cells","Dense fibrosis extending into the adjacent strap muscles"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-C1","obj":"EN-12-C.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the disease: painless, rubbery goiter in a middle-aged woman with hypothyroidism and anti-TPO antibodies is Hashimoto thyroiditis.","The gland is the site of an ongoing autoimmune reaction, so it fills with lymphocytes and plasma cells, which form germinal centers.","Surviving follicular cells change into Hürthle cells, with pink granular cytoplasm packed with mitochondria."],"why":"In Hashimoto thyroiditis, tolerance to thyroid antigens is lost. T cells and B cells invade the gland and organize into lymphoid follicles with germinal centers, as in a lymph node. Follicles shrink and are destroyed. Remaining follicular cells become Hürthle (oxyphil) cells with abundant eosinophilic, granular cytoplasm. The infiltrate enlarges the gland, so it is firm and painless while its function slowly fails.","whyNot":["Tall columnar cells in hyperplastic follicles mean an overstimulated gland, as in Graves disease, the opposite of her hypothyroid state.","Granulomas with giant cells around colloid describe subacute granulomatous thyroiditis, which causes a painful, tender gland after a viral illness.","Correct.","Fibrosis that extends beyond the capsule into the strap muscles is Riedel thyroiditis. In Hashimoto thyroiditis any fibrosis stays inside the capsule."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of Hashimoto thyroiditis","Hürthle cells"],"comps":["EN-12-C1","EN-10"]}},{"id":"EN-C03-Q2","stem":"She is treated with levothyroxine. Nine years later, she notes painless enlargement of the thyroid gland over 6 weeks, with hoarseness and difficulty swallowing. Which of the following neoplasms is most likely?","options":["Medullary carcinoma from C cells","Primary B-cell lymphoma","Follicular carcinoma","Parathyroid carcinoma"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-C1","obj":"EN-12-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note the setting: long-standing Hashimoto thyroiditis, then a rapidly enlarging gland that compresses nearby structures.","Years of lymphoid stimulation in the gland raise the risk of a B-cell clone escaping control.","This makes primary thyroid B-cell lymphoma the classic tumor linked to Hashimoto thyroiditis."],"why":"Hashimoto thyroiditis usually moves from a possible brief thyrotoxic phase, as follicles break down, to permanent hypothyroidism. It also clusters with other autoimmune diseases, such as vitiligo, type 1 diabetes, and pernicious anemia. The gland contains lymphoid tissue that is chronically stimulated by antigen, and this raises the risk of primary thyroid B-cell lymphoma, often marginal zone (MALT) or diffuse large B-cell type. The usual presentation is rapid, painless growth in a middle-aged or older woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis.","whyNot":["Medullary carcinoma arises from calcitonin-secreting C cells. It is linked to RET mutations and MEN 2, not to autoimmune thyroiditis.","Correct.","Follicular carcinoma usually presents as a solitary nodule and spreads by blood to bone and lung. It is not associated with Hashimoto thyroiditis.","Parathyroid carcinoma is rare, causes marked hypercalcemia, and is unrelated to thyroid autoimmunity."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid lymphoma","Course of Hashimoto thyroiditis"],"comps":["EN-12-C1","EN-10"]}},{"id":"EN-C03-Q3","stem":"Which of the following immune mechanisms is chiefly responsible for the loss of her thyroid follicular cells?","options":["IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation in the gland","Granulomatous response to colloid after viral injury","CD8+ cytotoxic T-cell killing of follicular cells","Antibody stimulation of the thyrotropin receptor"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-10","obj":"EN-10.2","dok":2,"approach":["Her anti-TPO antibodies show autoimmunity against the thyroid, but they are mainly a diagnostic marker.","The main damage in Hashimoto thyroiditis comes from cell-mediated immunity.","Cytotoxic CD8+ T cells kill follicular cells, helped by cytokines such as interferon gamma from CD4+ T cells."],"why":"Hashimoto thyroiditis is driven mainly by T cells. CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognize thyroid antigens and kill follicular cells. CD4+ helper T cells release interferon gamma, which activates macrophages and adds to the injury. Antibodies against thyroid peroxidase and thyroglobulin are present in most patients and are the main laboratory marker, and they may add some antibody-dependent killing. A high anti-TPO titer supports the diagnosis and predicts progression to hypothyroidism.","whyNot":["IgE and mast cells mediate type I hypersensitivity, such as allergy and anaphylaxis. They play no role in autoimmune thyroiditis.","A granulomatous reaction to leaked colloid after a viral infection is the mechanism of subacute granulomatous thyroiditis, which is self-limited.","Correct.","Antibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor cause Graves disease and hyperthyroidism, not follicle loss."],"study":{"topics":["Immune mechanisms of Hashimoto thyroiditis","Anti-TPO antibodies"],"comps":["EN-10","EN-12-C1"]}},{"id":"EN-C03-Q4","stem":"After she starts levothyroxine, her fatigue improves only gradually over several weeks, whereas an injection of epinephrine raises heart rate within seconds. Which of the following best explains the slower onset of thyroid hormone action?","options":["It acts through a G protein-coupled receptor that generates cAMP","It changes gene transcription through a nuclear receptor","It must first be stored as colloid within the thyroid follicles","It opens ligand-gated ion channels in the target cell membrane"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-9","obj":"EN-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Compare receptor types: epinephrine binds membrane receptors that change enzyme activity within seconds.","T3 enters the cell and binds a receptor in the nucleus that is a transcription factor.","New mRNA and protein must be made before the effect appears, which takes hours to days. The long half-life of T4 (about 7 days) also means levels take weeks to reach a steady state."],"why":"The thyroid hormone receptor sits in the nucleus bound to thyroid hormone response elements in DNA, usually as a heterodimer with the retinoid X receptor. When T3 binds, the receptor releases corepressors, recruits coactivators, and changes transcription of target genes such as Na/K-ATPase and beta-1 adrenergic receptors. Effects that depend on making new proteins develop over hours to days. Peptide hormones and catecholamines act through membrane receptors and second messengers that modify existing proteins, so they act within seconds to minutes.","whyNot":["Gs-coupled receptors with cAMP are used by TSH, glucagon, and beta-adrenergic agonists. They act quickly.","Correct.","Levothyroxine is absorbed from the gut into the blood and acts directly on tissues. Colloid storage happens only in the thyroid, which is failing in this patient.","Ligand-gated ion channels, such as the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, respond within milliseconds."],"study":{"topics":["Nuclear hormone receptors","Levothyroxine pharmacology"],"comps":["EN-9","EN-6"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C04","system":"EN","dx":"Subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis","codes":["EN-12-C2"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 5-day history of pain in the front of her neck that radiates to both ears, with fever and palpitations. Two weeks ago, she had a sore throat, rhinorrhea, and cough that resolved without treatment. Temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. The thyroid gland is slightly enlarged, firm, and exquisitely tender. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 78 mm/h (0 to 20). Serum TSH concentration is 0.05 μU/mL (0.4 to 4.0), and free T4 concentration is 2.6 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8). The 24-hour radioactive iodine uptake is 2% (10 to 30). Diagnosis: subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C04-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her high free T4 concentration together with a low radioactive iodine uptake?","options":["Release of preformed hormone from injured follicles","Stimulation of the gland by circulating hCG","Autonomous hormone synthesis by a follicular adenoma","Antibodies that block the thyrotropin receptor"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-C2","obj":"EN-12-C.3","dok":3,"approach":["Uptake measures how actively the gland is making new hormone.","High hormone with low uptake means the hormone is not newly made. It is leaking from a damaged gland, or it is being taken by mouth.","A painful, tender gland with fever and a high ESR after a viral illness points to inflammatory destruction of follicles."],"why":"In subacute granulomatous thyroiditis, inflammation breaks open follicles, and stored T4 and T3 spill into the blood. The patient becomes thyrotoxic, and the high hormone level suppresses TSH. Without TSH, and with follicular cells injured, the gland traps very little iodine, so uptake is low. The tender gland, fever, and raised ESR separate this from painless forms. Histology shows granulomas with multinucleated giant cells around fragments of colloid.","whyNot":["Correct.","hCG stimulates the TSH receptor in early pregnancy or with molar pregnancy. It increases hormone synthesis, so uptake would be high.","An autonomous adenoma makes new hormone and shows a hot nodule with high uptake. It does not cause fever or a tender gland.","Blocking antibodies to the TSH receptor reduce thyroid function and would cause hypothyroidism, not thyrotoxicosis."],"study":{"topics":["Destructive thyrotoxicosis","Radioactive iodine uptake"],"comps":["EN-12-C2","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C04-Q2","stem":"Which of the following infectious agents has been implicated as a trigger of her condition?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pyogenes","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Coxsackievirus"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-11","obj":"EN-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing: a cold with rhinorrhea and cough 2 weeks before the thyroid pain.","Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis follows viral upper respiratory infections, and the gland itself is not infected by bacteria.","Viruses linked to it include coxsackievirus, mumps virus, and adenovirus."],"why":"Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis usually begins 2 to 8 weeks after a viral upper respiratory infection. Coxsackievirus, mumps virus, measles virus, and adenovirus have been implicated. It is thought that viral injury or a cross-reacting immune response damages follicular cells, and the granulomas form around leaked colloid. It is more common in women in their 30s to 50s and follows a seasonal pattern like that of viral infections.","whyNot":["Staphylococcus aureus causes acute suppurative thyroiditis, a bacterial infection with a tender, sometimes fluctuant gland and usually normal thyroid function.","Streptococcus pyogenes can infect the thyroid directly in acute suppurative thyroiditis. Her preceding illness, with rhinorrhea and cough, suggests a virus.","Tuberculosis of the thyroid is rare and forms caseating granulomas. The granulomas of de Quervain thyroiditis are noncaseating and form around colloid.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of subacute thyroiditis","Viral triggers of endocrine disease"],"comps":["EN-11","EN-12-C2"]}},{"id":"EN-C04-Q3","stem":"If she is followed without antithyroid drugs, which of the following sequences of thyroid function is most likely over the next 6 months?","options":["Thyrotoxicosis, then transient hypothyroidism, then normal function","Thyrotoxicosis that persists until the gland is ablated","Thyrotoxicosis, then permanent hypothyroidism needing lifelong treatment","Normal function for months, then recurrent thyrotoxicosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-12-C2","obj":"EN-12-C.4","dok":2,"approach":["The gland is releasing a fixed store of hormone from damaged follicles.","Once that store is used up, and while follicles are still injured, hormone levels fall below normal for a while.","As inflammation settles, follicles regenerate and function returns to normal in most patients."],"why":"The thyrotoxic phase lasts a few weeks, until stored colloid is exhausted. A hypothyroid phase often follows, lasting weeks to a few months, because the injured gland cannot yet make enough hormone. Over about 6 to 12 months most patients recover normal function, and only a small share stay hypothyroid. Antithyroid drugs do not help because they block synthesis, and the problem is leakage of hormone already made. Beta blockers and anti-inflammatory drugs treat symptoms.","whyNot":["Correct.","Ongoing thyrotoxicosis needing ablation fits Graves disease or a toxic nodule, where the gland keeps making new hormone.","Permanent hypothyroidism is the usual end of Hashimoto thyroiditis. It is uncommon after subacute thyroiditis.","Recurrence happens in a few patients, but the typical course is a single episode that resolves."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of subacute thyroiditis"],"comps":["EN-12-C2","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C04-Q4","stem":"A 50-year-old man has a slowly enlarging, painless, rock-hard thyroid gland that is fixed to the trachea and strap muscles. He has hoarseness and stridor, and CT of the abdomen shows retroperitoneal fibrosis. Thyroid function is normal. A biopsy specimen of his thyroid gland is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Hyperplastic follicles lined by tall columnar cells","Granulomas around fragments of colloid with giant cells","Dense fibrosis with many IgG4-positive plasma cells","Lymphoid germinal centers with oxyphilic Hürthle cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-C","obj":"EN-12-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the differences from the case: no pain, no thyrotoxicosis, and a hard gland fixed to nearby structures.","Fibrosis that invades outside the gland, together with retroperitoneal fibrosis, points to Riedel thyroiditis.","Riedel thyroiditis is often part of IgG4-related disease."],"why":"Riedel thyroiditis replaces the gland with dense fibrous tissue that extends through the capsule into the trachea, esophagus, strap muscles, and nerves. The hard, fixed gland can mimic cancer and cause stridor, dysphagia, or hoarseness. Many cases belong to IgG4-related disease, with IgG4-positive plasma cells and fibrosis at other sites, such as the retroperitoneum. Painless (silent) and postpartum thyroiditis are different again: they are lymphocytic, cause brief thyrotoxicosis with low uptake, and have no pain or fibrosis.","whyNot":["Tall columnar hyperplastic cells mean an overstimulated gland, as in Graves disease, which is not rock-hard or fixed.","Granulomas around colloid are the histology of subacute granulomatous thyroiditis, which is painful and causes transient thyrotoxicosis.","Correct.","Germinal centers and Hürthle cells are typical of Hashimoto thyroiditis. Its fibrosing form stays within the thyroid capsule."],"study":{"topics":["Riedel thyroiditis","Comparing the thyroiditis types"],"comps":["EN-12-C","EN-12-C1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C05","system":"EN","dx":"Iodine deficiency goiter with hypothyroidism","codes":["EN-12-B5"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman who recently moved from a remote mountain village in Nepal comes to the physician because of neck swelling for several years and a 1-year history of fatigue, cold intolerance, and weight gain. Her family cooked with uniodized rock salt. Temperature is 36.1 °C (97.0 °F), pulse is 54/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 128/84 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a large, nontender goiter with several palpable nodules and delayed relaxation of the ankle reflexes. Serum studies show a TSH concentration of 38 μU/mL (0.4 to 4.0), a free T4 concentration of 0.4 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8), negative anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies, and an LDL-cholesterol concentration of 182 mg/dL (less than 100). Urinary iodine concentration is 22 μg/L (100 or more). Diagnosis: iodine deficiency goiter with hypothyroidism.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C05-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her increased serum LDL-cholesterol concentration?","options":["Fewer LDL receptors on hepatocytes","Increased intestinal absorption of cholesterol","Increased hepatic secretion of VLDL","Absence of apolipoprotein C-II on chylomicrons"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B5","obj":"EN-12-B.10","dok":2,"approach":["Link the lipid change to her low thyroid hormone level.","Thyroid hormone increases transcription of the LDL receptor gene in the liver.","With less thyroid hormone, the liver clears less LDL from the blood, so LDL rises."],"why":"T3 increases the number of LDL receptors on hepatocytes. In hypothyroidism, fewer receptors means slower clearance of LDL, so total and LDL cholesterol rise, and the hyperlipidemia improves with thyroid hormone replacement. The same low metabolic state explains her other signs: less heat production causes cold intolerance, less beta-adrenergic sensitivity causes bradycardia, and slower muscle contraction and relaxation cause the delayed reflex relaxation phase. Her goiter comes from chronically high TSH stimulating the gland.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cholesterol absorption in the gut is not driven by thyroid hormone status.","More VLDL secretion raises triglycerides and is seen with insulin resistance and alcohol use. It is not the main lipid change in hypothyroidism.","Loss of apolipoprotein C-II prevents lipoprotein lipase from clearing chylomicrons and causes very high triglycerides (type I hyperlipoproteinemia)."],"study":{"topics":["Hypothyroidism and LDL cholesterol","Clinical features of hypothyroidism"],"comps":["EN-12-B5","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C05-Q2","stem":"Her village diet is also low in selenium. A deficiency of this trace element would most directly impair which of the following steps in thyroid hormone metabolism?","options":["Uptake of iodide by the sodium-iodide symporter","Coupling of iodotyrosines by thyroid peroxidase","Proteolysis of thyroglobulin in lysosomes","Conversion of T4 to T3 by deiodinases"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-7","obj":"EN-7.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall which enzymes need selenium: deiodinases and glutathione peroxidase are selenoproteins.","Deiodinases remove iodine from T4 to make active T3.","Selenium deficiency therefore reduces formation of T3 in tissues and adds to the effects of iodine deficiency."],"why":"Iodine is built into the hormone itself, so a shortage limits how much T4 and T3 the gland can make. Free T4 falls, TSH rises by negative feedback, and the gland enlarges. Selenium is needed for a different step. The iodothyronine deiodinases contain selenocysteine at the active site and convert T4 to T3 in peripheral tissues. Selenium also supports glutathione peroxidase, which protects the thyroid from the hydrogen peroxide used in hormone synthesis.","whyNot":["The sodium-iodide symporter is driven by the sodium gradient from Na/K-ATPase and needs no selenium. Its activity rises in iodine deficiency because TSH is high.","Thyroid peroxidase is a heme enzyme that uses hydrogen peroxide. It does not contain selenium.","Thyroglobulin is broken down by lysosomal proteases after endocytosis. This step does not depend on selenium.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Selenium and deiodinases","Iodine in thyroid hormone synthesis"],"comps":["EN-7","EN-8"]}},{"id":"EN-C05-Q3","stem":"Her 41-year-old sister has fatigue and cold intolerance. Two years ago, the sister had severe postpartum hemorrhage and was unable to breastfeed afterward. The sister's free T4 concentration is 0.5 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8), her TSH concentration is 0.6 μU/mL (0.4 to 4.0), and her 8 AM serum cortisol and plasma ACTH concentrations are both low. Which of the following is the most likely site of the sister's disorder?","options":["Thyroid follicular epithelium","Anterior pituitary gland","Posterior pituitary gland","Adrenal cortex"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Pair each target hormone with its trophic hormone: low free T4 with a TSH that is not raised, and low cortisol with low ACTH.","If the target gland were failing, the trophic hormones would rise by negative feedback. Low or normal trophic hormones point higher up the axis.","Two axes failing together after postpartum hemorrhage and failed lactation points to the anterior pituitary (Sheehan syndrome)."],"why":"Negative feedback lets you localize an endocrine problem. In primary hypothyroidism, as in the patient, low free T4 removes feedback, so TSH rises sharply. In the sister, TSH stays in the normal range even though free T4 is low, which means the pituitary cannot respond. Low cortisol with low ACTH shows the same pattern in the adrenal axis. The pituitary enlarges in pregnancy, and severe postpartum hemorrhage can cause ischemic necrosis of the anterior lobe. Loss of prolactin explains her failure to lactate.","whyNot":["Primary thyroid failure would raise TSH by loss of negative feedback, as in the woman with iodine deficiency goiter.","Correct.","The posterior pituitary releases ADH and oxytocin. Its loss causes diabetes insipidus and does not lower TSH or ACTH.","Primary adrenal failure would raise ACTH and would not explain low free T4 with a normal TSH."],"study":{"topics":["Localizing endocrine disease by feedback","Sheehan syndrome"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-12-B4"]}},{"id":"EN-C05-Q4","stem":"After treatment, she undergoes thyroidectomy because the goiter compresses her trachea. While mobilizing the lower part of the left lobe, the surgeon ligates an artery that enters the posterior surface of the gland after passing medially behind the carotid sheath. This artery most likely arises from which of the following?","options":["Brachiocephalic trunk","Thyrocervical trunk","Internal thoracic artery","External carotid artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-4","obj":"EN-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the vessel: an artery reaching the lower posterior gland from behind the carotid sheath is the inferior thyroid artery.","Recall its origin: the thyrocervical trunk, a branch of the first part of the subclavian artery.","Contrast it with the superior thyroid artery, which comes from the external carotid and enters the upper pole."],"why":"The thyroid has two main paired arteries. The superior thyroid artery is the first branch of the external carotid artery and reaches the upper pole. The inferior thyroid artery comes from the thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian artery, runs up and then medially behind the carotid sheath, and enters the posterior gland near the lower pole. In some people a small thyroid ima artery rises from the brachiocephalic trunk or aortic arch to the isthmus. Veins drain through the superior and middle thyroid veins to the internal jugular vein and through the inferior thyroid veins to the brachiocephalic veins.","whyNot":["The brachiocephalic trunk is the usual source of the inconstant thyroid ima artery, which reaches the isthmus from below in the midline.","Correct.","The internal thoracic artery runs down behind the anterior chest wall and does not supply the thyroid.","The external carotid artery gives the superior thyroid artery, which supplies the upper pole and travels with the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve."],"study":{"topics":["Arterial supply of the thyroid"],"comps":["EN-4","EN-3"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C06","system":"EN","dx":"Congenital hypothyroidism from thyroid dysgenesis (lingual thyroid)","codes":["EN-12-B5"],"case":"A 3-week-old girl born at term is brought to the physician after her newborn screening test showed a high TSH concentration. Her parents report that she is sleepy, feeds poorly, and has remained jaundiced. Temperature is 36.2 °C (97.2 °F), pulse is 104/min, and respirations are 34/min. Physical examination shows jaundice, a large protruding tongue, a wide posterior fontanelle, and an umbilical hernia. No thyroid tissue is palpable in the neck. Serum TSH concentration is 85 μU/mL (reference for age, less than 10), and free T4 concentration is 0.5 ng/dL (reference for age, 0.9 to 2.2). A technetium-99m thyroid scan shows no tissue in the normal position and a small focus of uptake at the base of the tongue. Diagnosis: congenital hypothyroidism from thyroid dysgenesis (lingual thyroid).","questions":[{"id":"EN-C06-Q1","stem":"If thyroid hormone replacement were delayed for several months, which of the following outcomes would be most likely?","options":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with heart failure","Recurrent hypoglycemic seizures from excess insulin","Permanent intellectual disability and short stature","Accelerated bone age with early closure of growth plates"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B5","obj":"EN-12-B.12","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the signs of congenital hypothyroidism: lethargy, poor feeding, prolonged jaundice, large tongue, wide fontanelle, umbilical hernia.","Thyroid hormone is required for brain maturation and skeletal growth in the first years of life.","Without early treatment, the brain damage is permanent and growth is stunted."],"why":"Thyroid hormone drives neuronal growth, synapse formation, and myelination in the first 2 to 3 years of life, and it is needed for normal bone growth and maturation. Untreated congenital hypothyroidism causes permanent intellectual disability and short stature with delayed bone age. Newborn screening exists because most affected infants look normal at birth, and treatment started within the first 2 weeks gives normal or near-normal development. Thyroid dysgenesis (absent, small, or ectopic gland) is the most common cause, followed by dyshormonogenesis. Maternal iodine deficiency is the leading cause worldwide.","whyNot":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is linked to sarcomere gene mutations and infants of diabetic mothers. Hypothyroidism causes bradycardia and sometimes pericardial effusion.","Hypoglycemic seizures from excess insulin fit congenital hyperinsulinism or an infant of a diabetic mother, not hypothyroidism.","Correct.","Hypothyroidism delays bone age. Accelerated bone age with early growth plate closure suggests excess sex steroids or thyroid hormone."],"study":{"topics":["Congenital hypothyroidism","Newborn screening"],"comps":["EN-12-B5","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C06-Q2","stem":"Which of the following embryologic events best explains the location of her thyroid tissue?","options":["Persistence of the second pharyngeal cleft","Migration with the third pharyngeal pouch","Abnormal fusion of the lateral lingual swellings","Failed descent from the foramen cecum"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-1","obj":"EN-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall where the thyroid begins: an endodermal outgrowth at the foramen cecum, at the junction of the anterior two thirds and posterior third of the tongue.","The gland descends in front of the hyoid bone and larynx along the thyroglossal duct to reach the lower neck by about week 7.","Tissue that never leaves its starting point remains at the base of the tongue as a lingual thyroid."],"why":"The thyroid starts in week 4 as a midline thickening of endoderm in the floor of the pharynx, marked later by the foramen cecum of the tongue. It grows down as a bilobed diverticulum, stays attached by the thyroglossal duct, passes in front of the hyoid bone, and reaches its final position in front of the trachea. If descent fails, the gland stays at the base of the tongue. A lingual thyroid is often the only thyroid tissue present and is often too small to make enough hormone, as in this infant.","whyNot":["A persistent second pharyngeal cleft forms a lateral branchial cleft cyst or sinus along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. It does not contain thyroid tissue.","The third pharyngeal pouch forms the thymus and inferior parathyroid glands, which may end up ectopic in the mediastinum. It is not the source of thyroid follicular cells.","The lateral lingual swellings form the anterior two thirds of the tongue. Their fusion does not determine where thyroid tissue lies.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid descent","Lingual thyroid"],"comps":["EN-1","EN-12-E1"]}},{"id":"EN-C06-Q3","stem":"TSH stimulates hormone synthesis and growth in her small amount of functioning thyroid tissue. Binding of TSH to its receptor on follicular cells signals mainly through which of the following pathways?","options":["Gq-coupled phospholipase C with IP3 and calcium","JAK2 kinase activation of STAT transcription factors","Gs-coupled adenylyl cyclase and cAMP","Intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity of the receptor"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["TSH is a glycoprotein hormone from the anterior pituitary, like LH and FSH.","These hormones bind G protein-coupled receptors linked to Gs.","Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, cAMP activates protein kinase A, and this raises iodide uptake, hormone synthesis, and gland growth."],"why":"The TSH receptor is a G protein-coupled receptor. Its main signal runs through Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, and protein kinase A, which increase iodide uptake, thyroglobulin and thyroid peroxidase expression, hormone release, and growth of follicular cells. At high TSH levels the receptor can also engage Gq, but cAMP is the main pathway. The same Gs/cAMP pathway is used by ACTH, LH, FSH, PTH, and glucagon. Chronic TSH stimulation is why iodine deficiency and dyshormonogenesis cause goiter.","whyNot":["Gq with IP3 and calcium is the main pathway for TRH, GnRH, and oxytocin. TSH uses it only at high concentrations.","JAK/STAT signaling is used by growth hormone and prolactin, whose receptors are cytokine-type receptors without their own kinase.","Correct.","Receptors with intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity bind insulin and IGF-1."],"study":{"topics":["Hormone signaling pathways","TSH receptor"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C06-Q4","stem":"After levothyroxine is started, T3 formed in her developing brain binds receptors that regulate genes needed for myelination. Which of the following best describes how this receptor controls its target genes?","options":["It moves from cytoplasm to nucleus after heat shock proteins are released","It pairs with the retinoid X receptor on response elements in DNA","It activates JAK kinases that phosphorylate STAT transcription factors","It binds cAMP response elements after phosphorylation by protein kinase A"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-9","obj":"EN-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["T3 enters cells through membrane transporters such as MCT8 and reaches the nucleus.","The thyroid hormone receptor belongs to the nuclear receptor family and is a DNA-binding transcription factor.","It binds DNA as a heterodimer with the retinoid X receptor (RXR) at thyroid hormone response elements."],"why":"Thyroid hormone receptors sit in the nucleus already bound to DNA. They usually pair with the retinoid X receptor, and the pair attaches to thyroid hormone response elements near target genes. Without hormone, the receptor holds corepressors that silence the gene. When T3 binds, the corepressors leave and coactivators are recruited, which switches transcription on. This is how T3 controls genes for myelin proteins, neuronal growth, and Na/K-ATPase.","whyNot":["Glucocorticoid receptors wait in the cytoplasm bound to heat shock proteins and enter the nucleus once hormone binds. Thyroid hormone receptors are already in the nucleus.","Correct.","JAK/STAT signaling is used by growth hormone, prolactin, and cytokines acting on membrane receptors.","CREB binds cAMP response elements after protein kinase A phosphorylates it. This is the pathway of Gs-coupled hormones such as TSH and glucagon."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid hormone receptor","Thyroid hormone and brain development"],"comps":["EN-9","EN-6"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C07","system":"EN","dx":"Infected thyroglossal duct cyst","codes":["EN-12-E1"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-day history of a painful swelling in the front of her neck that began after a cold. She has had a small painless lump in the same place since childhood. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a 2.5-cm tender, erythematous, fluctuant mass in the midline of the neck just below the hyoid bone. The mass moves upward when she swallows and when she protrudes her tongue. Leukocyte count is 14,200/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000). Ultrasonography shows a thick-walled midline cyst containing debris and a normally located thyroid gland. Serum TSH concentration is normal. Diagnosis: infected thyroglossal duct cyst.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C07-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why the mass moves upward when she protrudes her tongue?","options":["It lies within the pretracheal fascia around the thyroid","It arises from remnants of the second pharyngeal cleft","It is attached to the sternocleidomastoid by fascia","Its tract connects to the foramen cecum of the tongue"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-E1","obj":"EN-12-E.1","dok":2,"approach":["A midline cyst near the hyoid bone that has been present since childhood suggests a thyroglossal duct cyst.","The cyst is a leftover piece of the duct along which the thyroid descended from the foramen cecum of the tongue.","Its tract stays linked to the hyoid bone and the base of the tongue, so tongue protrusion pulls it upward."],"why":"The thyroglossal duct normally disappears after the thyroid reaches the neck. If a segment persists, its epithelium secretes fluid and forms a cyst anywhere from the base of the tongue to the thyroid, most often near or just below the hyoid bone. The tract runs close to, and often through, the hyoid and connects up to the foramen cecum. Tongue protrusion pulls on this tract, so the cyst rises. Because the tract can open into the oral cavity, mouth bacteria can infect the cyst, often after an upper respiratory infection, which is the most common complication.","whyNot":["Pretracheal fascia explains why thyroid masses rise with swallowing. It does not explain movement with tongue protrusion, which needs a link to the tongue.","Second pharyngeal cleft remnants form branchial cleft cysts in the lateral neck along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid.","A fascial attachment to the sternocleidomastoid would tie a lateral mass to that muscle. It would not move with the tongue.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroglossal duct cyst","Examination of midline neck masses"],"comps":["EN-12-E1","EN-1"]}},{"id":"EN-C07-Q2","stem":"After the infection resolves, excision of the cyst and its tract is planned. Before surgery, the surgeon confirms by imaging that a thyroid gland is present in its normal position. Which of the following is the main reason for this step?","options":["The cyst may be the only thyroid tissue present","The cyst may open into a pyriform sinus fistula","The cyst may be a lateral branchial cleft cyst","The cyst may compress the recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-12-E1","obj":"EN-12-E.2","dok":3,"approach":["Thyroid tissue can stop anywhere along the path of descent.","In a few patients, a midline mass along the thyroglossal tract is ectopic thyroid and the neck has no other thyroid.","Removing it would leave the patient permanently hypothyroid, so a normal gland must be confirmed first."],"why":"A midline mass along the thyroglossal tract is usually a simple cyst, but it can be ectopic thyroid tissue, and sometimes that tissue is the patient's only thyroid. Ultrasonography or a thyroid scan confirms a normal gland before surgery. Its location also tells it apart from a branchial cleft cyst. A thyroglossal duct cyst is in the midline and moves with swallowing and tongue protrusion. A second branchial cleft cyst lies laterally along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid and does not move with the tongue.","whyNot":["Correct.","A pyriform sinus fistula is a third or fourth pouch remnant that causes recurrent infection in the left thyroid lobe. It is not part of the thyroglossal tract.","Her mass is in the midline and moves with the tongue. A branchial cleft cyst is lateral, and examination already rules it out.","The recurrent laryngeal nerves run in the tracheoesophageal grooves, away from a midline cyst near the hyoid bone."],"study":{"topics":["Ectopic thyroid tissue","Midline versus lateral neck cysts"],"comps":["EN-12-E1","EN-1"]}},{"id":"EN-C07-Q3","stem":"A 30-year-old woman had a midline neck mass removed 6 months ago without preoperative imaging. Pathologic examination showed normal thyroid follicles, and she has no other thyroid tissue. She now has amenorrhea and a milky nipple discharge. Her serum TSH concentration is 62 μU/mL (0.4 to 4.0), free T4 concentration is 0.3 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8), and prolactin concentration is 48 ng/mL (less than 25). Which of the following best explains her increased prolactin concentration?","options":["A prolactin-secreting adenoma of the anterior pituitary","Reduced renal clearance of circulating prolactin","Excess TRH acting on pituitary lactotrophs","Estrogen-induced growth of pituitary lactotrophs"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-12-B5","obj":"EN-12-B.11","dok":3,"approach":["Read the thyroid tests: high TSH with low free T4 is primary hypothyroidism, here caused by removal of her only thyroid tissue.","Low thyroid hormone removes feedback on the hypothalamus, so TRH secretion rises.","TRH stimulates lactotrophs as well as thyrotrophs. Prolactin rises and suppresses GnRH, causing amenorrhea and galactorrhea."],"why":"In primary hypothyroidism, TSH is high and free T4 is low. In central hypothyroidism, free T4 is low but TSH is low or inappropriately normal. A TSH-secreting adenoma gives high TSH with high free T4. Here, the loss of thyroid hormone raises hypothalamic TRH, and TRH also stimulates prolactin release. Raised prolactin inhibits GnRH pulses, so LH and FSH fall, which causes menstrual disturbance and infertility. Thyroid hormone replacement lowers TRH and corrects the prolactin level, so thyroid function should be checked in anyone with hyperprolactinemia.","whyNot":["A prolactinoma can cause the same reproductive symptoms, but it does not explain her high TSH and low free T4. Her thyroid loss accounts for everything.","Kidney failure reduces prolactin clearance, but nothing suggests kidney disease here.","Correct.","High estrogen, as in pregnancy, enlarges lactotrophs. She has amenorrhea from suppressed gonadotropins, so her estrogen is likely low."],"study":{"topics":["Hypothyroidism and hyperprolactinemia","Thyroid test patterns"],"comps":["EN-12-B5","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C07-Q4","stem":"Needle aspiration of the infected cyst yields pus. Culture grows gram-positive cocci in chains that are catalase-negative, beta-hemolytic, and sensitive to bacitracin. Which of the following organisms is most likely?","options":["Haemophilus influenzae","Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Streptococcus pyogenes"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-11","obj":"EN-11.2","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-positive cocci in chains that are catalase-negative are streptococci.","Beta-hemolysis with bacitracin sensitivity identifies group A Streptococcus.","Group A Streptococcus, Staphylococcus aureus, and Streptococcus pneumoniae are the classic causes of bacterial neck and thyroid infections."],"why":"Bacterial infections of the thyroid region, including acute suppurative thyroiditis and infected developmental cysts, are most often caused by Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes, and Streptococcus pneumoniae, with oral anaerobes in some cases. Laboratory features tell them apart. Streptococcus pyogenes is a catalase-negative, beta-hemolytic coccus in chains that is sensitive to bacitracin. Infection often follows an upper respiratory illness, when organisms from the pharynx reach a tract that opens into the mouth or pharynx.","whyNot":["Haemophilus influenzae is a small gram-negative coccobacillus that needs factors X and V to grow.","Staphylococcus aureus forms clusters and is catalase-positive and coagulase-positive.","Streptococcus pneumoniae is a lancet-shaped diplococcus that is alpha-hemolytic, optochin-sensitive, and bile-soluble.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Identifying streptococci","Bacteria in thyroid and neck infections"],"comps":["EN-11","EN-12-H1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C08","system":"EN","dx":"Acute suppurative thyroiditis from a pyriform sinus fistula","codes":["EN-12-H1"],"case":"A 7-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of a 3-day history of fever, pain on swallowing, and a painful swelling on the left side of the anterior neck. She had a similar episode 1 year ago. Temperature is 39.0 °C (102.2 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a warm, red, tender, fluctuant swelling over the left lobe of the thyroid gland. Leukocyte count is 18,500/mm3 (4,500 to 13,500), and erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 62 mm/h (0 to 20). Serum TSH and free T4 concentrations are within the reference ranges. Ultrasonography shows an abscess in the left thyroid lobe. A later barium swallow shows a sinus tract from the left pyriform sinus. Diagnosis: acute suppurative thyroiditis from a pyriform sinus fistula.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C08-Q1","stem":"Needle aspiration of the left thyroid lobe is performed. Which of the following is most likely to be seen in the aspirate?","options":["Neutrophils and gram-positive cocci","Atypical follicular cells with nuclear grooves","Multinucleated giant cells around colloid","Lymphocytes, plasma cells, and Hürthle cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-12-H1","obj":"EN-12-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Put the findings together: high fever, leukocytosis, a red, fluctuant gland, an abscess on ultrasound, and normal thyroid function.","This is a bacterial infection of the thyroid, so the aspirate is pus.","Pus contains neutrophils and the causative bacteria, most often gram-positive cocci such as Staphylococcus aureus or streptococci."],"why":"Acute suppurative thyroiditis is a bacterial infection that forms an abscess in the gland. Patients have fever and a painful, tender, sometimes fluctuant swelling, often with pain on swallowing. Thyroid function is usually normal because the infection is localized and most of the gland keeps working. This helps separate it from subacute thyroiditis, which usually causes thyrotoxicosis. The thyroid normally resists infection because of its capsule, rich blood supply and lymphatic drainage, and high iodine content, so a recurrent left-sided infection in a child suggests a pyriform sinus fistula.","whyNot":["Correct.","Nuclear grooves in atypical follicular cells suggest papillary carcinoma, which does not cause fever or a fluctuant swelling.","Giant cells around colloid are found in subacute granulomatous thyroiditis, which causes pain and fever but no abscess and usually causes thyrotoxicosis.","Lymphocytes, plasma cells, and Hürthle cells are found in Hashimoto thyroiditis, which is painless and causes no fever."],"study":{"topics":["Acute suppurative thyroiditis","Thyroid aspiration findings"],"comps":["EN-12-H1","EN-11"]}},{"id":"EN-C08-Q2","stem":"Her sinus tract is a remnant of the fourth pharyngeal pouch. Which of the following structures normally develops from this same pouch?","options":["Superior parathyroid glands","Inferior parathyroid glands","Palatine tonsil crypt epithelium","Thymus"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-1","obj":"EN-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the pouch derivatives: second pouch, palatine tonsil; third pouch, thymus and inferior parathyroids; fourth pouch, superior parathyroids.","The structures from the third pouch travel farther down with the thymus, past the fourth pouch structures.","This is why third pouch glands end up as the inferior parathyroids, and why the superior glands come from the fourth pouch."],"why":"The third pharyngeal pouch forms the thymus and the inferior parathyroid glands. As the thymus migrates into the chest, it pulls these parathyroids down past the fourth pouch glands, so third pouch glands end up inferior. They may also be pulled too far, into the thymus or anterior mediastinum. The fourth pouch forms the superior parathyroid glands, which stay closer to their origin and are more constant in position. Parafollicular C cells come from neural crest cells that enter the ultimobranchial body, a derivative of the most caudal pouch. A pyriform sinus fistula is a remnant of the third or fourth pouch, and it is usually on the left.","whyNot":["Correct.","The inferior parathyroid glands come from the third pouch and descend with the thymus. This is why ectopic inferior parathyroids are found in the mediastinum.","The epithelium of the palatine tonsil develops from the second pouch.","The thymus develops from the third pouch."],"study":{"topics":["Pharyngeal pouch derivatives","Parathyroid embryology"],"comps":["EN-1","EN-3"]}},{"id":"EN-C08-Q3","stem":"A 13-year-old boy on the same ward has a craniopharyngioma and has fatigue and slowed growth. His free T4 concentration is 0.6 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8), TSH concentration is 1.1 μU/mL (0.7 to 5.7), and prolactin concentration is 42 ng/mL (less than 20). After an intravenous injection of TRH, his serum TSH concentration rises. Which of the following is the most likely site of the defect causing his low free T4 concentration?","options":["Thyroid gland","Hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary","Peripheral tissue deiodinases"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Low free T4 with a TSH that is not raised means the problem is above the thyroid (central hypothyroidism).","TSH rises after TRH injection, so the pituitary thyrotrophs can still respond. The missing signal is TRH from the hypothalamus.","Raised prolactin supports this, because loss of hypothalamic dopamine releases lactotrophs from inhibition."],"why":"A failing thyroid would remove feedback and raise TSH, so his normal TSH with low free T4 points to the pituitary or hypothalamus. The TRH injection separates the two. Pituitary thyrotrophs that respond to TRH are working, so the missing piece is TRH from the hypothalamus. Prolactin is the one pituitary hormone mainly held down by the hypothalamus, through dopamine. When a craniopharyngioma damages the hypothalamus or stalk, prolactin rises while the other pituitary hormones fall. TSH in hypothalamic hypothyroidism may be normal in the assay but has less biological activity.","whyNot":["Primary thyroid failure would raise TSH well above the reference range.","Correct.","Pituitary thyrotroph failure would give little or no TSH rise after TRH, and would not explain a raised prolactin.","Deiodinase defects change T3 production. They would not lower free T4 with an inappropriately normal TSH."],"study":{"topics":["Central hypothyroidism","Localizing endocrine disease by feedback"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-12-B4"]}},{"id":"EN-C08-Q4","stem":"She later undergoes excision of the sinus tract with partial left thyroidectomy. After surgery, her voice is weak and breathy, and laryngoscopy shows that the left vocal fold does not move. Injury to which of the following nerves most likely explains these findings?","options":["External branch of the left superior laryngeal nerve","Internal branch of the left superior laryngeal nerve","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","Left ansa cervicalis"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-3","obj":"EN-3.2","dok":3,"approach":["A vocal fold that does not move means loss of the muscles that open and close the glottis.","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.","The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs in the tracheoesophageal groove, right behind the thyroid lobe, close to the inferior thyroid artery, so it is at risk in thyroid surgery."],"why":"The recurrent laryngeal nerve climbs in the tracheoesophageal groove, passes close to the branches of the inferior thyroid artery behind the thyroid lobe, and enters the larynx. It supplies every intrinsic laryngeal muscle except the cricothyroid, including the posterior cricoarytenoid, the only muscle that opens the vocal folds. Injury on one side leaves that fold fixed and the voice weak and breathy. Injury on both sides can narrow the airway enough to cause stridor. The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve runs with the superior thyroid artery, and its injury only weakens high-pitched sounds.","whyNot":["The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid, which tenses the fold. Injury reduces vocal pitch and projection, but the fold still moves.","The internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve gives sensation above the vocal folds. Injury raises aspiration risk but does not paralyze the fold.","Correct.","The ansa cervicalis supplies the infrahyoid strap muscles. Injury has little effect and does not affect the vocal folds."],"study":{"topics":["Recurrent laryngeal nerve","Nerve injury in thyroid surgery"],"comps":["EN-3","EN-4"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C09","system":"EN","dx":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma with postoperative hypocalcemia","codes":["EN-12-G5","EN-12-B3"],"case":"A 36-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a painless lump in her neck. At age 8 years, she received radiation therapy to the neck for Hodgkin lymphoma. Physical examination shows a firm, 2-cm nodule in the right thyroid lobe and a firm, enlarged right cervical lymph node. Serum TSH concentration is normal. Ultrasonography shows a hypoechoic nodule with microcalcifications. She undergoes total thyroidectomy with central neck dissection, which confirms papillary thyroid carcinoma with lymph node metastases. On the second postoperative day, she has numbness around the mouth and tingling in her fingers. Tapping over the facial nerve causes twitching of the facial muscles. Serum calcium concentration is 7.1 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.2), albumin concentration is 4.0 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), and PTH concentration is 6 pg/mL (10 to 65). Diagnosis: papillary thyroid carcinoma with postoperative hypoparathyroidism.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C09-Q1","stem":"Microscopic examination of her thyroid tumor is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Sheets of pleomorphic spindle cells and bizarre giant cells","Clear, overlapping nuclei with grooves and psammoma bodies","Nests of polygonal cells in a stroma rich in amyloid","Uniform follicles with invasion through the tumor capsule"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G5","obj":"EN-12-G.11","dok":2,"approach":["Use the clues: a young woman, childhood neck radiation, microcalcifications, and spread to cervical lymph nodes point to papillary carcinoma.","Papillary carcinoma is diagnosed mainly by its nuclear features.","Look for clear (Orphan Annie eye) nuclei, nuclear grooves, and psammoma bodies, which show up as microcalcifications on ultrasound."],"why":"Papillary carcinoma is the most common thyroid cancer, and childhood radiation to the neck is its best-known risk factor. The diagnosis rests on the nuclei: they look empty and clear (Orphan Annie eye nuclei), overlap, and show grooves and pseudoinclusions. Laminated calcified psammoma bodies are common. The tumor spreads mainly through lymphatics to cervical nodes. Even with nodal spread, most patients do very well after surgery, with 10-year survival above 90%.","whyNot":["Bizarre spindle and giant cells describe anaplastic carcinoma, a rapidly fatal tumor of older adults.","Correct.","Polygonal or spindle cells in an amyloid stroma are medullary carcinoma, which arises from C cells and secretes calcitonin.","Capsular invasion by a follicular tumor with uniform nuclei defines follicular carcinoma, which spreads through blood rather than lymph."],"study":{"topics":["Papillary thyroid carcinoma","Radiation and thyroid cancer"],"comps":["EN-12-G5","EN-9"]}},{"id":"EN-C09-Q2","stem":"Molecular testing of her tumor is performed. Which of the following genetic alterations is most likely, given her history?","options":["PAX8-PPARG gene fusion","Germline RET point mutation","Inactivating TP53 mutation","RET/PTC gene rearrangement"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-9","obj":"EN-9.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the tumor: papillary carcinoma after childhood radiation.","Ionizing radiation causes double-strand DNA breaks, and faulty repair joins pieces of different genes.","In the thyroid, this most often fuses the RET kinase domain to a partner gene (RET/PTC), which activates MAP kinase signaling."],"why":"Papillary carcinomas are driven by activation of the MAP kinase pathway. In sporadic tumors, the most common change is the BRAF V600E point mutation. In tumors that follow radiation, as seen in children after the Chernobyl accident, RET/PTC rearrangements are much more common. These fusions place the RET tyrosine kinase domain under the promoter of a partner gene that is always expressed in follicular cells, so the kinase is switched on all the time. Follicular carcinomas tend to carry RAS mutations or PAX8-PPARG fusions, and medullary carcinomas carry RET point mutations.","whyNot":["PAX8-PPARG fusion is typical of follicular carcinoma.","Germline activating RET point mutations cause MEN 2A and 2B, with medullary carcinoma of the C cells.","TP53 loss is found in anaplastic carcinoma and marks progression to an aggressive tumor.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mutations in thyroid cancer"],"comps":["EN-9","EN-12-G5"]}},{"id":"EN-C09-Q3","stem":"During surgery, the surgeon finds both superior parathyroid glands easily on the back of the upper thyroid lobes but has to search for the inferior glands. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["Fixed location inside the carotid sheath","Location on the anterior surface of the isthmus","Variable location near the lower thyroid poles","Location above the thyroid beside the hyoid bone"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-3","obj":"EN-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["The parathyroids usually lie on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes, two on each side.","The superior glands are fairly constant, near the level of the cricoid cartilage.","The inferior glands are more variable. They may sit near the lower pole, in the thyrothymic ligament or thymus, or in the mediastinum."],"why":"The superior parathyroids lie in a fairly constant spot on the back of the upper part of each lobe. The inferior parathyroids have a longer embryonic path, so their position varies more. They are usually near the lower pole, but they can lie in fat around the lower pole, inside the thymus, or in the mediastinum. Surgeons must look for them carefully, because removing them by mistake or cutting off their blood supply causes hypoparathyroidism. Central neck dissection works in the same area, which raises the risk.","whyNot":["The carotid sheath holds the carotid artery, internal jugular vein, and vagus nerve. The parathyroids are not inside it.","Parathyroid glands lie on the posterior surface of the lobes.","Correct.","Glands that fail to descend can rarely lie high in the neck, but that is the exception. The inferior glands normally sit low."],"study":{"topics":["Parathyroid gland locations","Surgical anatomy of the thyroid"],"comps":["EN-3","EN-1"]}},{"id":"EN-C09-Q4","stem":"Her surgeon reports that all four parathyroid glands were identified and left in place. Interruption of which of the following vessels close to the thyroid capsule is the most likely cause of her hypocalcemia?","options":["Inferior thyroid artery branches","Superior thyroid artery at the upper pole","Thyroid ima artery at the isthmus","Middle thyroid vein at the lateral border"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-4","obj":"EN-4.4","dok":3,"approach":["Read the labs: low calcium with low PTH and normal albumin after thyroid surgery means hypoparathyroidism.","The glands were left in place, so their blood supply must have been cut.","Both the superior and inferior parathyroid glands depend mainly on the inferior thyroid artery, whose branches are divided during thyroidectomy."],"why":"The parathyroid glands are small and get blood from tiny end branches. Most of this blood, for both upper and lower glands, comes from the inferior thyroid artery. When the surgeon ligates its branches close to the thyroid capsule, glands left in place can lose their blood supply and stop making PTH. Without PTH, calcium falls within a day or two, causing perioral numbness, tingling, and the Chvostek sign. The problem is often temporary if the glands recover their blood supply. Accidental removal of parathyroid tissue causes the same picture.","whyNot":["Correct.","The superior thyroid artery can add a little blood to the superior glands, but it is not the main parathyroid supply.","The thyroid ima artery is present in only a few people and supplies the isthmus, away from the parathyroids.","The middle thyroid vein drains blood away from the gland. Ligating it does not cut off the arterial supply to the parathyroids."],"study":{"topics":["Parathyroid blood supply","Post-thyroidectomy hypocalcemia"],"comps":["EN-4","EN-12-B3"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C10","system":"EN","dx":"Follicular thyroid carcinoma with bone metastasis","codes":["EN-12-G5"],"case":"A 63-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of worsening pain in his right hip. He has had a slowly enlarging lump in his neck for 2 years. Physical examination shows a firm, 4-cm nodule in the left thyroid lobe and no cervical lymphadenopathy. Serum TSH and calcium concentrations are normal. X-ray of the pelvis shows a lytic lesion in the right ilium. Biopsy of the bone lesion shows follicles that contain colloid, and the tumor cells stain for thyroglobulin. He undergoes total thyroidectomy, and pathologic examination confirms the diagnosis. Diagnosis: follicular thyroid carcinoma with bone metastasis.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C10-Q1","stem":"Fine-needle aspiration of his thyroid nodule before surgery showed only follicular cells and could not separate adenoma from carcinoma. Which of the following findings in the resected tumor establishes that it is a carcinoma?","options":["Tumor cells inside capsular blood vessels","Crowded microfollicles with very little colloid","Nuclear grooves with intranuclear inclusions","Frequent mitotic figures among tumor cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G5","obj":"EN-12-G.10","dok":3,"approach":["Follicular adenoma and follicular carcinoma can look identical cell by cell.","The only reliable difference is invasion: through the full thickness of the capsule or into blood vessels.","That is why aspiration cytology cannot make the call, and the whole capsule must be examined after surgery."],"why":"Follicular adenomas and carcinomas are both encapsulated nodules of follicular cells with similar nuclei. Carcinoma is diagnosed when tumor cells invade through the capsule or into vessels in or beyond the capsule. Vascular invasion also explains how follicular carcinoma spreads: through the blood, to bone and lung, while lymph node spread is uncommon. Bone metastases are usually lytic, as in this patient. Because the metastases often keep follicular function, they may make thyroglobulin and take up iodine.","whyNot":["Correct.","Microfollicles with scant colloid occur in both adenoma and carcinoma, so they cannot separate the two.","Nuclear grooves and pseudoinclusions are features of papillary carcinoma, not follicular carcinoma.","Mitoses can occur in both benign and malignant follicular tumors and do not define carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Follicular adenoma versus carcinoma","Spread of thyroid cancers"],"comps":["EN-12-G5"]}},{"id":"EN-C10-Q2","stem":"After thyroidectomy, his bone metastasis is treated with radioactive iodine-131 while his TSH concentration is raised. The uptake of this isotope by the metastatic cells depends most directly on which of the following?","options":["Thyroid peroxidase at the apical membrane","Sodium-iodide symporter on the basal membrane","Pendrin chloride-iodide exchanger at the apical side","Megalin-mediated uptake of thyroglobulin"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-8","obj":"EN-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Radioactive iodine works only if tumor cells pull iodide in from the blood the way normal follicular cells do.","The first step of thyroid hormone synthesis is active iodide uptake by the sodium-iodide symporter on the basolateral membrane.","TSH increases symporter expression, which is why TSH is raised before treatment."],"why":"The sodium-iodide symporter on the basolateral membrane uses the sodium gradient to bring two sodium ions and one iodide ion into the cell, concentrating iodide many times above plasma levels. Well-differentiated follicular carcinoma cells often keep this transporter, so they take up iodine-131, and its beta radiation destroys them. TSH increases symporter expression, so TSH is raised by stopping thyroid hormone or by giving recombinant TSH before treatment. The later steps are pendrin moving iodide into the lumen, thyroid peroxidase oxidizing and attaching it, and proteolysis of thyroglobulin to release hormone.","whyNot":["Thyroid peroxidase oxidizes and organifies iodide after it enters the cell. It helps keep iodine in the cell but does not bring it in.","Correct.","Pendrin moves iodide out of the cell into the follicular lumen. It acts after uptake.","Megalin is a receptor that takes thyroglobulin back from the lumen. It does not carry free iodide in from the blood."],"study":{"topics":["Sodium-iodide symporter","Radioactive iodine therapy"],"comps":["EN-8","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C10-Q3","stem":"Tumor cells from the lower part of the left lobe entered the inferior thyroid veins before reaching the systemic circulation. These veins drain most directly into which of the following?","options":["Internal jugular veins","External jugular veins","Subclavian veins","Brachiocephalic veins"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-4","obj":"EN-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Follicular carcinoma spreads through the blood, so trace its venous route.","The superior and middle thyroid veins drain into the internal jugular vein.","The inferior thyroid veins run down in front of the trachea into the brachiocephalic veins, and from there to the superior vena cava, right heart, lungs, and systemic circulation."],"why":"The thyroid drains through three pairs of veins. The superior and middle thyroid veins empty into the internal jugular veins. The inferior thyroid veins come from the lower poles and isthmus, often form a plexus in front of the trachea, and drain into the left and right brachiocephalic veins. Tumor cells that invade these veins reach the lungs first and can then reach the bones. This explains the typical metastatic sites of follicular carcinoma.","whyNot":["The internal jugular veins receive the superior and middle thyroid veins.","The external jugular veins drain the scalp and face and do not receive thyroid veins.","The subclavian veins drain the upper limb and join the internal jugular veins to form the brachiocephalic veins.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Venous drainage of the thyroid","Hematogenous spread of follicular carcinoma"],"comps":["EN-4","EN-12-G5"]}},{"id":"EN-C10-Q4","stem":"After treatment, he takes levothyroxine once each morning. His serum T4 concentration stays nearly constant between doses. Which of the following best explains this stability?","options":["Continuous recycling of T4 through the bile","Slow absorption of T4 from the gut over several days","Binding of over 99% of T4 to plasma proteins","Rapid uptake of T4 into colloid stores"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Ask why a once-daily dose gives a steady level: the hormone must be cleared slowly.","More than 99% of T4 is bound to thyroxine-binding globulin, transthyretin, and albumin, and only free hormone is metabolized or enters cells.","The bound pool acts as a large reservoir, so T4 has a half-life of about 7 days."],"why":"Only the free fraction of thyroid hormone enters cells, binds receptors, and is broken down. For T4, this free fraction is very small, about 0.03%. The rest is bound mainly to thyroxine-binding globulin, with smaller amounts on transthyretin and albumin. The bound pool refills the free pool as hormone is used, so T4 has a half-life of about 7 days. A daily dose then adds only a small amount to a large, steady pool. T3 is less tightly bound and has a half-life of about 1 day.","whyNot":["Some T4 conjugates are excreted in bile and partly reabsorbed, but this is a small effect and does not explain the long half-life.","Levothyroxine is absorbed in the small intestine within a few hours.","Correct.","Colloid storage happens only in thyroid follicles, and his thyroid has been removed."],"study":{"topics":["Plasma binding of thyroid hormone","Half-life of T4 and T3"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-8"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C11","system":"EN","dx":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma in MEN2A","codes":["EN-12-G5","EN-12-G6"],"case":"A 36-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a painless lump in her neck that she noticed 2 months ago. Her father had a thyroidectomy for thyroid cancer at age 41 and later had an adrenal tumor removed. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 78/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a firm 2.5-cm nodule in the right thyroid lobe and an enlarged right cervical lymph node. Serum studies show a calcitonin concentration of 1,450 pg/mL (normal under 5), calcium 11.0 mg/dL (normal 8.5 to 10.5), and PTH 88 pg/mL (normal 10 to 65). Plasma free metanephrines are within the reference range. Fine-needle aspiration shows polygonal and spindle-shaped cells in an amorphous eosinophilic stroma. Diagnosis: medullary thyroid carcinoma in multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C11-Q1","stem":"Congo red staining of the stroma in the aspirate shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light. This material is most likely derived from which of the following proteins?","options":["Transthyretin","Immunoglobulin light chains","Serum amyloid A protein","Calcitonin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G5","obj":"EN-12-G.12","dok":2,"approach":["Apple-green birefringence with Congo red identifies amyloid.","Medullary carcinoma is a tumor of parafollicular C cells, which secrete calcitonin.","In this tumor the amyloid is formed locally from the hormone the tumor cells make."],"why":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from parafollicular C cells, which make calcitonin. The tumor secretes large amounts of calcitonin, and altered calcitonin molecules fold into amyloid fibrils that collect in the stroma. This is a localized endocrine amyloid, so it does not point to a systemic amyloid disease. Her very high serum calcitonin is the same product measured in the blood and is used as a tumor marker.","whyNot":["Transthyretin forms amyloid in senile cardiac amyloidosis and in familial amyloid polyneuropathy, not in an endocrine tumor.","Light chain (AL) amyloid comes from a plasma cell clone, as in multiple myeloma, and deposits in many organs. It is the wrong source for amyloid inside a C cell tumor.","Serum amyloid A is an acute-phase reactant that forms AA amyloid in long-standing inflammation, such as rheumatoid arthritis or chronic infection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Medullary thyroid carcinoma pathology","Types of amyloid"],"comps":["EN-12-G5","EN-2"]}},{"id":"EN-C11-Q2","stem":"Genetic testing shows a heterozygous germline mutation that she shares with her father. Which of the following best explains how a single inherited mutant allele drives C cell hyperplasia and carcinoma in this syndrome?","options":["Gain-of-function mutation in the Gs alpha subunit","Loss-of-function mutation in the VHL tumor suppressor","Loss-of-function mutation in menin with a second hit","Gain-of-function mutation in the RET tyrosine kinase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-9","obj":"EN-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Medullary thyroid carcinoma with an adrenal tumor and hyperparathyroidism in a parent fits MEN2A.","MEN2 is caused by mutations in the RET proto-oncogene, which encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase.","An activating mutation in one allele is enough to signal growth, so no second hit is needed."],"why":"MEN2A and MEN2B are caused by germline activating mutations of RET, a receptor tyrosine kinase. The mutant receptor signals without its normal ligand, so one mutant allele drives cell growth in C cells, chromaffin cells, and parathyroid cells. This is why the syndrome is autosomal dominant with very high penetrance for medullary carcinoma, and why carriers are offered prophylactic thyroidectomy. In contrast, a tumor suppressor needs loss of both alleles in the tumor cell.","whyNot":["Activating Gs alpha (GNAS) mutations occur in McCune-Albright syndrome and some GH-secreting adenomas. They do not cause C cell tumors.","Loss of VHL causes von Hippel-Lindau disease, with pheochromocytoma, renal cell carcinoma, and hemangioblastomas, but not medullary thyroid carcinoma.","Menin is the tumor suppressor lost in MEN1 (parathyroid, pituitary, and pancreatic tumors). MEN1 is also autosomal dominant, but a tumor forms only after a second hit removes the normal allele.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["RET proto-oncogene in MEN2","Oncogenes versus tumor suppressor genes"],"comps":["EN-9","EN-12-G6"]}},{"id":"EN-C11-Q3","stem":"Immunohistochemical staining of the tumor cells is positive for calcitonin, chromogranin A, and synaptophysin. The tumor cells share their embryologic origin with which of the following cell types?","options":["Adrenal medullary chromaffin cells","Adenohypophyseal corticotroph cells","Parathyroid chief cells","Thyroid follicular cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Chromogranin A and synaptophysin mark neuroendocrine cells.","Thyroid C cells are classically derived from neural crest cells that migrate into the thyroid through the ultimobranchial body.","Pick the other neural crest endocrine cell, which also forms the pheochromocytoma of MEN2A."],"why":"Parafollicular C cells are classically taught as neural crest derivatives, like the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla. Both make secretory granules, so their tumors stain for chromogranin A and synaptophysin. This shared origin helps explain why MEN2A combines medullary thyroid carcinoma with pheochromocytoma. Follicular cells, parathyroid cells, and islet cells come from endoderm, and the adenohypophysis comes from oral ectoderm.","whyNot":["Correct.","The adenohypophysis, including corticotrophs, develops from Rathke pouch, an outgrowth of oral ectoderm.","Parathyroid chief cells come from endoderm of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches.","Thyroid follicular cells develop from endoderm of the floor of the primitive pharynx at the foramen cecum. Their tumors are papillary and follicular carcinomas."],"study":{"topics":["Neural crest derivatives","Neuroendocrine markers"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-12-G6"]}},{"id":"EN-C11-Q4","stem":"The surgeon plans to examine all of her parathyroid glands during thyroidectomy. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["A single enlarged gland with three atrophic glands","Four small glands with fatty replacement of the parenchyma","Chief cell hyperplasia in multiple glands","A single gland with capsular and vascular invasion"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G3","obj":"EN-12-G.6","dok":3,"approach":["High calcium with high PTH means primary hyperparathyroidism.","In MEN syndromes every parathyroid cell carries the germline mutation, so several glands enlarge.","A single adenoma is typical of sporadic disease, and carcinoma is rare."],"why":"Her raised calcium with an inappropriately high PTH is primary hyperparathyroidism. In MEN2A, as in MEN1, all parathyroid tissue carries the inherited mutation, so the usual lesion is hyperplasia of several glands made of chief cells. A sporadic adenoma, by contrast, is one enlarged gland, and the high calcium suppresses the other glands so they look small. Surgeons therefore look at all four glands in MEN patients.","whyNot":["One enlarged gland with suppressed, small remaining glands is the picture of a sporadic parathyroid adenoma, the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism overall.","Small, suppressed glands are expected when hypercalcemia comes from a source other than the parathyroids, such as PTH-related peptide, because the high calcium shuts off PTH secretion. Her PTH is high.","Correct.","Capsular and vascular invasion define parathyroid carcinoma, which is rare and usually causes very high calcium levels."],"study":{"topics":["Parathyroid adenoma versus hyperplasia","MEN2A components"],"comps":["EN-12-G3","EN-12-A2"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C12","system":"EN","dx":"Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma arising in a longstanding multinodular goiter","codes":["EN-12-G5"],"case":"A 74-year-old man comes to the physician because of a rapidly enlarging neck mass over the past 6 weeks, with increasing difficulty swallowing and a change in his voice. He has had a goiter for more than 20 years with normal thyroid function and no symptoms other than mild neck fullness. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 142/84 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a hard, fixed, 8-cm mass in the left side of a nodular goiter and soft inspiratory stridor. Serum TSH is 1.8 µU/mL (normal 0.4 to 4.0), and free T4 is 1.1 ng/dL (normal 0.8 to 1.8). Core biopsy shows sheets of pleomorphic giant cells and spindle cells with many mitoses and necrosis, invading skeletal muscle. Diagnosis: anaplastic thyroid carcinoma arising in a longstanding multinodular goiter.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C12-Q1","stem":"Molecular analysis of the tumor is performed. Which of the following genetic changes is most characteristic of this tumor and best explains its loss of differentiation and rapid growth?","options":["RET/PTC rearrangement","PAX8-PPARG gene fusion","Inactivating TP53 mutation","Germline RET point mutation"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-12-G5","obj":"EN-12-G.14","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the tumor: an older adult with a rapidly growing, invasive, undifferentiated thyroid tumor has anaplastic carcinoma.","Anaplastic carcinoma often arises from a pre-existing differentiated tumor or goiter by gaining further mutations.","The mutation tied to this final step is loss of the guardian of the genome, p53."],"why":"Anaplastic carcinoma is the most aggressive thyroid cancer. It usually affects people over 60, grows within weeks, invades the trachea, esophagus, and nerves, and most patients die within a year. Many cases arise from a differentiated carcinoma or a longstanding goiter, and inactivating TP53 mutations are the characteristic change in this step to an undifferentiated tumor. Without p53, cells with DNA damage keep dividing, which fits the marked pleomorphism and many mitoses.","whyNot":["RET/PTC rearrangements are a hallmark of papillary carcinoma, especially after radiation. They are not what defines the anaplastic step.","PAX8-PPARG fusion is characteristic of follicular carcinoma, which spreads through blood to bone and lung.","Correct.","Germline RET point mutations cause hereditary medullary carcinoma in MEN2, a C cell tumor of younger patients."],"study":{"topics":["Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma","Mutations in thyroid cancers"],"comps":["EN-12-G5","EN-9"]}},{"id":"EN-C12-Q2","stem":"A radioiodine scan performed 3 years earlier showed several nodules within the goiter. Which of the following scan features of a nodule is associated with the highest risk of malignancy?","options":["Increased uptake with suppression of other tissue","Absent uptake compared with surrounding tissue","Uptake equal to that of surrounding tissue","Diffusely increased uptake in both lobes"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G5","obj":"EN-12-G.13","dok":2,"approach":["Radioiodine uptake shows whether nodule cells still trap iodide and make hormone.","Cancer cells are usually less differentiated and trap little iodide.","So a cold nodule carries more risk than a warm or hot nodule."],"why":"A euthyroid multinodular goiter has normal TSH and free T4 and usually causes only cosmetic or compressive symptoms. On a radioiodine scan, a nodule that takes up little or no tracer (a cold nodule) has lost the ability to trap iodide, and this group contains most of the cancers. Most cold nodules are still benign, so the next step is fine-needle aspiration. Hot nodules are autonomous and are almost never malignant.","whyNot":["A hot nodule that suppresses the rest of the gland is an autonomously functioning nodule. It may cause hyperthyroidism but is rarely malignant.","Correct.","A warm nodule behaves like normal tissue and carries a lower risk than a cold nodule.","Diffusely increased uptake in both lobes is the pattern of Graves disease, not a sign of a malignant nodule."],"study":{"topics":["Hot and cold thyroid nodules","Euthyroid multinodular goiter"],"comps":["EN-12-G5","EN-12-B5"]}},{"id":"EN-C12-Q3","stem":"Laryngoscopy shows that the left vocal fold is immobile in the paramedian position, and his voice is weak and breathy. Tumor invasion of which of the following nerves at which site best explains these findings?","options":["Recurrent laryngeal nerve in the tracheoesophageal groove","External laryngeal nerve beside the superior thyroid artery","Internal laryngeal nerve at the thyrohyoid membrane","Ansa cervicalis on the anterior carotid sheath"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-3","obj":"EN-3.2","dok":3,"approach":["An immobile vocal fold means paralysis of the intrinsic laryngeal muscles that move it.","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.","This nerve ascends in the groove between the trachea and esophagus, directly behind the thyroid lobe."],"why":"The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs up in the tracheoesophageal groove, close to the back of each thyroid lobe, and supplies every intrinsic laryngeal muscle except the cricothyroid. When it is invaded or cut, the vocal fold on that side cannot move and rests near the midline, so the voice becomes weak and breathy. Damage to both nerves can close the airway. The same nerve is at risk during thyroidectomy.","whyNot":["Correct.","The external laryngeal nerve runs with the superior thyroid artery and supplies the cricothyroid. Injury makes it hard to raise pitch and tires the voice, but the vocal fold still moves.","The internal laryngeal nerve pierces the thyrohyoid membrane and carries sensation from the larynx above the vocal folds. Injury impairs the cough reflex, not fold movement.","The ansa cervicalis supplies the infrahyoid strap muscles. It does not supply laryngeal muscles."],"study":{"topics":["Recurrent laryngeal nerve course","Nerve injury in thyroid surgery"],"comps":["EN-3","EN-4"]}},{"id":"EN-C12-Q4","stem":"He undergoes palliative total thyroidectomy. Because of a medication error at discharge, levothyroxine is not started. Which of the following patterns of serum TSH and free T4 is most likely 6 weeks later?","options":["High TSH and low free T4","High TSH and high free T4","Low TSH and low free T4","Normal TSH and low free T4"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Removing the thyroid removes the target gland, so free T4 falls over several weeks.","Low thyroid hormone removes negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary.","A healthy pituitary then raises TSH, giving the primary hypothyroid pattern."],"why":"T4 has a half-life of about 7 days, so 6 weeks after total thyroidectomy without replacement free T4 is low. The pituitary and hypothalamus are intact, so the loss of feedback drives TSH high. High TSH with low free T4 places the problem in the target gland. This is the same pattern as Hashimoto thyroiditis or iodine deficiency.","whyNot":["Correct.","High TSH with high free T4 suggests a TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma or thyroid hormone resistance. He has no thyroid tissue to make T4.","Low TSH with low free T4 means the pituitary or hypothalamus is failing (central hypothyroidism). His pituitary is normal.","A normal TSH with low free T4 can be seen in central hypothyroidism or severe nonthyroidal illness, but an intact pituitary responds to low T4 by raising TSH."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid feedback patterns","Primary versus central hypothyroidism"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-12-B5"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C13","system":"EN","dx":"Nonthyroidal illness (euthyroid sick) syndrome in septic shock","codes":["EN-12-B5"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman is admitted to the intensive care unit with septic shock from Escherichia coli pyelonephritis. She has no history of thyroid disease and took no medications before admission. On the fourth hospital day she still requires a norepinephrine infusion. Temperature is 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 92/54 mm Hg on norepinephrine. The thyroid gland is not enlarged. Serum studies show a TSH concentration of 1.2 µU/mL (normal 0.4 to 4.0), free T4 1.0 ng/dL (normal 0.8 to 1.8), total T3 42 ng/dL (normal 80 to 180), and reverse T3 56 ng/dL (normal 10 to 24). Diagnosis: nonthyroidal illness (euthyroid sick) syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C13-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her low T3 and raised reverse T3?","options":["Autoimmune destruction of thyroid follicular cells","Increased thyroxine-binding globulin synthesis in the liver","Reduced hypothalamic TRH with low pituitary TSH output","Reduced peripheral 5'-deiodinase activity"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B5","obj":"EN-12-B.13","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: low T3, high reverse T3, and normal TSH and free T4 in a critically ill patient.","Most T3 is made outside the thyroid by removing an outer-ring iodine from T4 (5'-deiodination).","The same enzyme activity also clears reverse T3, so less activity lowers T3 and raises reverse T3 together."],"why":"About 80 percent of circulating T3 is made in peripheral tissues by 5'-deiodinase removing an iodine from the outer ring of T4. Severe illness, cytokines, fasting, and some drugs reduce this activity. Less T4 becomes T3, and reverse T3, which is normally cleared by the same outer-ring deiodination, builds up. TSH usually stays normal, and the pattern reverses as the patient recovers, so it is not treated as thyroid disease.","whyNot":["Autoimmune destruction (Hashimoto thyroiditis) lowers free T4 and raises TSH. Her TSH and free T4 are normal.","A rise in thyroxine-binding globulin, as in pregnancy, raises total T4 and total T3. Severe illness tends to lower binding proteins instead.","Prolonged critical illness can blunt TRH and TSH, but that would not explain a high reverse T3, and her TSH and free T4 are normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Euthyroid sick syndrome","Deiodinase and reverse T3"],"comps":["EN-12-B5","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C13-Q2","stem":"Her morning serum cortisol is 34 µg/dL (normal 5 to 23). Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Angiotensin II stimulation of the zona glomerulosa","Autonomous cortisol secretion by an adrenal adenoma","Cytokine-driven release of CRH and ACTH","Increased hepatic synthesis of cortisol-binding globulin"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-10","obj":"EN-10.5","dok":2,"approach":["Sepsis releases large amounts of IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha from activated macrophages.","These cytokines act on the hypothalamus and pituitary to raise CRH and ACTH.","The adrenal responds with more cortisol, which then limits the inflammatory response."],"why":"During sepsis, IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha stimulate hypothalamic CRH and pituitary ACTH, and the adrenal cortex releases more cortisol. Cortisol then dampens the immune response and supports blood pressure. This loop keeps inflammation from running out of control. A high cortisol in severe illness is an appropriate stress response, not a sign of Cushing syndrome.","whyNot":["Angiotensin II stimulates aldosterone from the zona glomerulosa, not cortisol from the zona fasciculata.","An adrenal adenoma would make cortisol without regard to illness. Nothing in her history suggests one, and acute stress fully explains the finding.","Correct.","Cortisol-binding globulin usually falls during severe inflammation and sepsis. A rise would increase total cortisol but would not explain a true stress response."],"study":{"topics":["Cytokines and the HPA axis","Stress response to sepsis"],"comps":["EN-10","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C13-Q3","stem":"Deficiency of which of the following trace elements would most directly impair the enzymes whose reduced activity explains her thyroid hormone pattern?","options":["Iron","Selenium","Iodine","Manganese"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-7","obj":"EN-7.3","dok":2,"approach":["Her pattern comes from reduced deiodinase activity converting T4 to T3.","Deiodinases are selenoproteins with selenocysteine at the active site.","So selenium deficiency impairs T4 to T3 conversion, while iodine deficiency impairs hormone synthesis."],"why":"The deiodinases that convert T4 to T3 contain selenocysteine in their active site, so they depend on dietary selenium. Low selenium reduces conversion of T4 to active T3. Iodine, in contrast, is the raw material for making T4 and T3 inside the thyroid. Iodine deficiency lowers both hormones, raises TSH, and causes goiter.","whyNot":["Iron is needed by thyroid peroxidase, a heme enzyme that organifies iodide. Iron deficiency can impair hormone synthesis, not peripheral conversion.","Correct.","Iodine deficiency limits synthesis of T4 and T3 in the thyroid, so TSH rises and a goiter forms. It does not act on the deiodinases.","Manganese is a cofactor for enzymes such as mitochondrial superoxide dismutase and arginase. It has no role in deiodination."],"study":{"topics":["Selenium and deiodinases","Iodine and thyroid hormone synthesis"],"comps":["EN-7","EN-8"]}},{"id":"EN-C13-Q4","stem":"Her low T3 may blunt her cardiovascular response to catecholamines. Through which of the following actions does T3 normally increase the cardiac response to sympathetic stimulation?","options":["Increased adrenal epinephrine secretion","Upregulation of myocardial beta-1 receptors","Inhibition of myocardial Na/K-ATPase","Activation of a membrane Gs-coupled receptor"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["T3 enters cells and binds a nuclear receptor that changes gene transcription.","In the heart, one of the genes it increases is the beta-1 adrenergic receptor.","More receptors mean a larger response to the same level of catecholamines."],"why":"T3 binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors and increases transcription of target genes. In heart muscle it increases beta-1 adrenergic receptors and proteins that handle calcium, so heart rate and contractility rise for a given catecholamine level. This is why hyperthyroid patients have tachycardia even though their catecholamine levels are normal, and why beta blockers relieve many symptoms. T3 also raises Na/K-ATPase activity and basal metabolic rate.","whyNot":["Thyroid hormone increases tissue sensitivity to catecholamines. It does not raise adrenal epinephrine output, and catecholamine levels are normal or low in hyperthyroidism.","Correct.","T3 increases Na/K-ATPase synthesis, which raises oxygen use and heat production. Inhibiting the pump is the action of digoxin.","Thyroid hormone acts mainly through intracellular nuclear receptors, not through G protein-coupled receptors on the cell membrane."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac actions of thyroid hormone","Thyroid hormone and beta-1 receptors"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-9"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C14","system":"EN","dx":"Cushing disease from a pituitary corticotroph adenoma","codes":["EN-12-A1","EN-12-G4"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of weight gain, easy bruising, and difficulty climbing stairs. She takes an estrogen-containing oral contraceptive. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 154/96 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a rounded face, a dorsocervical fat pad, central obesity with thin extremities, wide purple abdominal striae, and weakness of the hip flexors. Fasting plasma glucose is 142 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99). Late-night salivary cortisol is raised on two occasions. Plasma ACTH is 94 pg/mL (normal 10 to 60). MRI of the brain shows a 6-mm pituitary adenoma. Diagnosis: Cushing disease from a pituitary corticotroph adenoma.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C14-Q1","stem":"Before the MRI was obtained, which of the following test results would have most strongly favored a pituitary source over ectopic ACTH secretion?","options":["Suppressed plasma ACTH concentration","No cortisol suppression with low-dose dexamethasone","Raised 24-hour urine free cortisol","Cortisol suppression with high-dose dexamethasone"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A1","obj":"EN-12-A.1","dok":3,"approach":["A high ACTH with hypercortisolism means ACTH-dependent Cushing syndrome: a pituitary adenoma or an ectopic source.","Pituitary corticotroph adenomas keep some sensitivity to glucocorticoid feedback; ectopic tumors usually do not.","So a high dose of dexamethasone lowers cortisol in pituitary disease but usually not with ectopic ACTH."],"why":"Once hypercortisolism is confirmed, plasma ACTH separates ACTH-dependent from ACTH-independent causes. Her ACTH is high, so the source is a pituitary adenoma or an ectopic tumor such as small cell lung carcinoma. Pituitary adenoma cells still respond partly to strong glucocorticoid feedback, so high-dose dexamethasone usually suppresses cortisol by more than half. Ectopic ACTH-secreting tumors usually do not suppress.","whyNot":["A suppressed ACTH points to an ACTH-independent cause, such as an adrenal adenoma or exogenous glucocorticoids. Her ACTH is high.","Failure to suppress with low-dose dexamethasone shows that cortisol excess is present, but it occurs with every cause of Cushing syndrome and does not locate the source.","A raised 24-hour urine free cortisol confirms hypercortisolism but does not tell a pituitary source from an ectopic one.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dexamethasone suppression tests","Localizing Cushing syndrome"],"comps":["EN-12-A1","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C14-Q2","stem":"Which of the following tests is most reliable for confirming hypercortisolism in this patient?","options":["24-hour urine free cortisol","Random plasma ACTH concentration","Overnight 1-mg dexamethasone suppression test","8 AM total serum cortisol"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A1","obj":"EN-12-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Estrogen raises cortisol-binding globulin, which raises total serum cortisol without raising free cortisol.","Any test that measures total serum cortisol can give a falsely high result.","Urine free cortisol reflects only unbound cortisol filtered by the kidney, so binding proteins do not affect it."],"why":"The three screening tests for Cushing syndrome are 24-hour urine free cortisol, late-night salivary cortisol, and the low-dose dexamethasone suppression test. Estrogen increases hepatic synthesis of cortisol-binding globulin, so total serum cortisol rises even when free cortisol is normal. This can make the dexamethasone test look like a failure to suppress. Urine free cortisol measures only free hormone, so it is reliable in a woman taking estrogen.","whyNot":["Correct.","Plasma ACTH is used after cortisol excess is confirmed, to find its source. It does not confirm hypercortisolism.","The overnight dexamethasone test measures total serum cortisol the next morning. Estrogen raises cortisol-binding globulin, so the result can be falsely positive.","A single morning total cortisol is affected by binding globulin, and the morning peak overlaps with normal values. It is a poor screening test."],"study":{"topics":["Screening tests for Cushing syndrome","Cortisol-binding globulin and estrogen"],"comps":["EN-12-A1","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C14-Q3","stem":"Which of the following actions of cortisol contributes most to her hyperglycemia?","options":["Increased hepatic glycogenolysis via cAMP","Increased GLUT4 translocation in muscle","Reduced renal glucose reabsorption by SGLT2","Increased gluconeogenesis from amino acids"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.6","dok":2,"approach":["Cortisol is a catabolic, counterregulatory hormone that raises blood glucose.","It breaks down muscle protein and induces hepatic gluconeogenic enzymes.","The amino acids released from muscle feed gluconeogenesis, which also explains her thin limbs and proximal weakness."],"why":"Cortisol induces hepatic gluconeogenic enzymes such as PEPCK and supplies the substrate by breaking down muscle protein. The liver turns the released amino acids into glucose, while cortisol also reduces glucose uptake in muscle and fat. The result is hyperglycemia, sometimes diabetes. The same muscle breakdown causes her proximal weakness.","whyNot":["cAMP-driven glycogenolysis is how glucagon and epinephrine raise glucose within minutes. Cortisol actually increases hepatic glycogen stores.","GLUT4 translocation is an action of insulin that lowers blood glucose. Cortisol opposes insulin and reduces glucose uptake.","Reduced SGLT2 reabsorption would increase glucose loss in urine and lower blood glucose. This is how SGLT2 inhibitor drugs work.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Metabolic actions of cortisol","Steroid-induced hyperglycemia"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-7"]}},{"id":"EN-C14-Q4","stem":"She has had two skin infections that healed slowly. Excess cortisol reduces synthesis of both prostaglandins and leukotrienes mainly through which of the following actions?","options":["Acetylation of cyclooxygenase","Inhibition of 5-lipoxygenase","Inhibition of phospholipase A2","Blockade of leukotriene receptors"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-10","obj":"EN-10.5","dok":2,"approach":["Both prostaglandins and leukotrienes are made from arachidonic acid.","Arachidonic acid is freed from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2.","Blocking that first step cuts off both branches, which is why glucocorticoids suppress both."],"why":"Glucocorticoids induce annexin A1 (lipocortin) and reduce the expression of inflammatory enzymes, which lowers phospholipase A2 activity. Less arachidonic acid is released from membranes, so both the cyclooxygenase products (prostaglandins) and the lipoxygenase products (leukotrienes) fall. Cortisol also blocks NF-kB, reducing cytokine production. Together these effects weaken inflammation, slow wound healing, and raise infection risk.","whyNot":["Acetylation of cyclooxygenase is the action of aspirin. It lowers prostaglandins and thromboxane but not leukotrienes.","Inhibition of 5-lipoxygenase (as by zileuton) lowers leukotrienes only, not prostaglandins.","Correct.","Leukotriene receptor blockers such as montelukast block leukotriene effects but do not reduce prostaglandin or leukotriene synthesis."],"study":{"topics":["Anti-inflammatory actions of cortisol","Arachidonic acid pathway"],"comps":["EN-10","EN-6"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C15","system":"EN","dx":"Adrenal crisis after abrupt withdrawal of long-term prednisone","codes":["EN-12-B2"],"case":"A 61-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of 2 days of vomiting, abdominal pain, weakness, and confusion. He has taken prednisone 15 mg daily for 11 months for polymyalgia rheumatica and stopped it 4 days ago when he ran out. Temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 76/42 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a rounded face and no hyperpigmentation of the skin or mucosa. Serum studies show sodium 132 mEq/L (normal 136 to 145), potassium 4.4 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0), glucose 56 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99), and cortisol 1.6 µg/dL (morning normal 5 to 23). Diagnosis: acute adrenal insufficiency (adrenal crisis) after abrupt withdrawal of long-term prednisone.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C15-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why stopping prednisone led to adrenal crisis?","options":["Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage from consumptive coagulopathy","Autoimmune destruction of all three cortical zones","Fasciculata atrophy from prolonged ACTH suppression","Granulomatous replacement of the adrenal cortex"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B2","obj":"EN-12-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Long-term prednisone suppresses CRH and ACTH by negative feedback.","Without ACTH, the zona fasciculata atrophies and cannot make cortisol on demand.","Stopping prednisone suddenly removes the only glucocorticoid source, and crisis follows."],"why":"Supraphysiologic glucocorticoids taken for more than a few weeks suppress hypothalamic CRH and pituitary ACTH. ACTH is the trophic hormone for the zona fasciculata, so the zone atrophies. When prednisone is stopped abruptly, the axis cannot recover fast enough, and the patient develops cortisol deficiency with hypotension, hypoglycemia, vomiting, and confusion. Recovery of the axis can take months, which is why long courses are tapered.","whyNot":["Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage causes crisis in sepsis with DIC or with anticoagulation. He has neither, and his history points to steroid withdrawal.","Autoimmune adrenalitis destroys all cortical zones and causes primary insufficiency with hyperpigmentation and hyperkalemia. He has neither.","Correct.","Granulomatous destruction from tuberculosis or histoplasmosis causes primary adrenal insufficiency, usually with hyperpigmentation and hyperkalemia."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of adrenal crisis","HPA axis suppression by glucocorticoids"],"comps":["EN-12-B2","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C15-Q2","stem":"Which of the following patterns of plasma hormone concentrations is most likely in this patient?","options":["Low ACTH and normal aldosterone","High ACTH and low aldosterone","High ACTH and normal aldosterone","Low ACTH and low aldosterone"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B2","obj":"EN-12-B.5","dok":3,"approach":["Prednisone suppresses the pituitary, so this is secondary adrenal insufficiency with low ACTH.","Aldosterone is controlled mainly by the renin-angiotensin system and potassium, not by ACTH.","So aldosterone stays near normal, which matches his normal potassium and lack of hyperpigmentation."],"why":"In secondary adrenal insufficiency the problem is low ACTH, here from suppression by exogenous glucocorticoid. The zona fasciculata atrophies, but the zona glomerulosa keeps working because angiotensin II and potassium drive aldosterone. That is why hyperkalemia is absent. Hyperpigmentation is also absent because ACTH and other POMC-derived peptides are low.","whyNot":["Correct.","High ACTH with low aldosterone is primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease). It causes hyperpigmentation and hyperkalemia, which he lacks.","High ACTH would cause hyperpigmentation and is seen when the adrenal itself fails. Exogenous steroid suppresses ACTH instead.","Low aldosterone would cause hyperkalemia. His aldosterone axis is intact because it does not depend on ACTH."],"study":{"topics":["Primary versus secondary adrenal insufficiency","Control of aldosterone"],"comps":["EN-12-B2","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C15-Q3","stem":"His serum potassium is normal because his aldosterone secretion is intact. Which of the following is the main mechanism by which aldosterone promotes potassium excretion?","options":["Proximal tubule Na+/H+ exchanger","Principal cell ENaC and potassium channels","Alpha intercalated cell H+/K+ ATPase","Thick ascending limb Na-K-2Cl cotransporter"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-5","obj":"EN-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["Aldosterone acts on mineralocorticoid receptors in the late distal tubule and collecting duct.","In principal cells it increases ENaC sodium channels, Na/K-ATPase, and apical potassium channels.","Sodium entry makes the lumen negative, which drives potassium secretion into the urine."],"why":"Aldosterone release is driven mainly by angiotensin II and by high plasma potassium. In collecting duct principal cells it increases apical ENaC channels and basolateral Na/K-ATPase. Sodium reabsorption creates a lumen-negative voltage that pushes potassium out through apical potassium channels. Aldosterone also stimulates hydrogen ion secretion by alpha intercalated cells, so its loss causes hyperkalemia with a mild metabolic acidosis.","whyNot":["The proximal tubule Na+/H+ exchanger reabsorbs sodium and bicarbonate. It is driven by angiotensin II, not by aldosterone acting on potassium.","Correct.","The H+/K+ ATPase of alpha intercalated cells secretes hydrogen ions and reabsorbs potassium. It conserves potassium rather than excreting it.","The Na-K-2Cl cotransporter of the thick ascending limb is the target of loop diuretics and is not the main site of aldosterone action."],"study":{"topics":["Aldosterone actions in the collecting duct","Regulation of aldosterone"],"comps":["EN-5","EN-12-B2"]}},{"id":"EN-C15-Q4","stem":"While he was taking prednisone, his muscle and joint inflammation was well controlled. Which of the following actions of glucocorticoids most directly reduced his production of IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha?","options":["Inhibition of calcineurin phosphatase activity","Inhibition of NF-kB-driven gene transcription","Neutralization of soluble TNF-alpha","Inhibition of mTOR kinase signaling"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-10","obj":"EN-10.5","dok":2,"approach":["Glucocorticoids bind a cytoplasmic receptor that moves to the nucleus.","The hormone-receptor complex blocks transcription factors that turn on inflammatory genes.","NF-kB is the main transcription factor for IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha."],"why":"The glucocorticoid-receptor complex enters the nucleus and interferes with NF-kB and AP-1, the transcription factors that switch on genes for IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha, and many other inflammatory proteins. Glucocorticoids also induce IkB, which keeps NF-kB in the cytoplasm. In the blood they lower lymphocyte and eosinophil counts and raise neutrophils. These broad effects explain both their benefit in polymyalgia rheumatica and their side effects.","whyNot":["Calcineurin inhibition is how cyclosporine and tacrolimus block IL-2 production in T cells. It is not how glucocorticoids act.","Correct.","Neutralizing TNF-alpha is the action of monoclonal antibodies such as infliximab. Glucocorticoids reduce cytokine production rather than binding the cytokine.","mTOR inhibition is how sirolimus blocks T cell proliferation in response to IL-2. It does not describe glucocorticoid action."],"study":{"topics":["Glucocorticoid mechanism of action","NF-kB and inflammatory cytokines"],"comps":["EN-10","EN-9"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C16","system":"EN","dx":"Conn syndrome from an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma","codes":["EN-12-A1","EN-12-G1"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the physician for follow-up of hypertension that remains uncontrolled despite treatment with amlodipine. He reports muscle cramps, weakness, and waking at night to urinate. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 168/102 mm Hg. There is no edema. Serum studies show sodium 144 mEq/L (normal 136 to 145), potassium 2.8 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0), and bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 28). The plasma aldosterone concentration is raised. CT of the abdomen shows a 1.5-cm nodule in the left adrenal gland, and adrenal vein sampling shows that the excess aldosterone comes from the left side. Diagnosis: Conn syndrome from an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C16-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional laboratory findings is most likely and best distinguishes his condition from hypertension caused by renal artery stenosis?","options":["Raised plasma renin activity","Low 24-hour urine potassium excretion","Raised serum cortisol after dexamethasone","Suppressed plasma renin activity"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A1","obj":"EN-12-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Both primary hyperaldosteronism and renal artery stenosis cause hypertension with high aldosterone and hypokalemia.","In primary disease the adrenal makes aldosterone on its own, so sodium retention and high pressure suppress renin.","In renal artery stenosis the kidney is underperfused, so renin is high and drives aldosterone."],"why":"In Conn syndrome the adenoma secretes aldosterone autonomously. Sodium retention expands volume and raises pressure at the juxtaglomerular cells, so renin is suppressed. A high aldosterone to renin ratio is the screening test. In secondary hyperaldosteronism, such as renal artery stenosis, low renal perfusion raises renin, which drives the aldosterone. Patients with primary disease rarely have edema because of aldosterone escape.","whyNot":["High renin is the hallmark of secondary hyperaldosteronism, as in renal artery stenosis, heart failure, or cirrhosis.","Aldosterone excess increases potassium secretion, so urine potassium is inappropriately high despite hypokalemia.","Failure of cortisol suppression with dexamethasone indicates Cushing syndrome. His tumor makes aldosterone, not cortisol.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Primary versus secondary hyperaldosteronism","Aldosterone to renin ratio"],"comps":["EN-12-A1","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C16-Q2","stem":"The left adrenal gland is removed laparoscopically. Which of the following gross findings best supports a cortical adenoma rather than adrenocortical carcinoma?","options":["Small, encapsulated, yellow cortical nodule","Mass larger than 6 cm with central necrosis","Tumor thrombus extending into the adrenal vein","Mass invading the upper pole of the kidney"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G1","obj":"EN-12-G.1","dok":2,"approach":["Cortical adenomas are small, well-circumscribed, and lipid-rich, so they look yellow.","Carcinomas are large and invasive, with hemorrhage and necrosis.","Choose the feature of a benign, small, lipid-rich tumor."],"why":"Aldosterone-producing adenomas are usually small, often under 2 cm, encapsulated, and bright yellow on cut section because the cells are full of lipid. Adrenocortical carcinomas are rare, large (often over 6 cm), and invasive, with hemorrhage, necrosis, and spread into veins and nearby organs. Carcinomas are often functional and frequently virilizing. Metastases to the adrenal are more common than primary adrenocortical carcinoma.","whyNot":["Correct.","Large size with necrosis suggests adrenocortical carcinoma, not a benign adenoma.","Growth into the adrenal vein or inferior vena cava is a sign of malignancy, seen with adrenocortical carcinoma.","Direct invasion of the kidney is a feature of a malignant tumor. Adenomas stay within their capsule."],"study":{"topics":["Adrenal adenoma versus carcinoma","Aldosterone-producing adenoma"],"comps":["EN-12-G1","EN-12-A1"]}},{"id":"EN-C16-Q3","stem":"Eating large amounts of black licorice can also cause hypertension with hypokalemia. Which of the following findings would best distinguish licorice-induced hypertension from this patient's condition?","options":["Low plasma ACTH concentration","Low serum potassium","Low plasma aldosterone","High serum bicarbonate"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-8","obj":"EN-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Cortisol binds the mineralocorticoid receptor as strongly as aldosterone does.","Renal 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 normally inactivates cortisol to cortisone, protecting the receptor.","Licorice blocks this enzyme, so cortisol acts like aldosterone, and the body lowers its own aldosterone and renin."],"why":"Mineralocorticoid receptors in the collecting duct bind cortisol and aldosterone equally well, and cortisol circulates at much higher levels. The enzyme 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 converts cortisol to cortisone in these cells, so only aldosterone normally activates the receptor. Glycyrrhetinic acid in licorice inhibits this enzyme. Cortisol then activates the receptor, causing sodium retention, hypertension, and hypokalemia with low renin and low aldosterone. In Conn syndrome aldosterone is high.","whyNot":["Licorice does not change pituitary ACTH, and an aldosteronoma does not either, so ACTH does not separate the two.","Both conditions cause hypokalemia through increased potassium secretion by principal cells.","Correct.","Both conditions over-activate the mineralocorticoid receptor, so both raise bicarbonate through hydrogen ion secretion."],"study":{"topics":["11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2","Apparent mineralocorticoid excess"],"comps":["EN-8","EN-12-A1"]}},{"id":"EN-C16-Q4","stem":"On the CT scan, the radiologist notes that the gland containing the nodule differs in shape and position from the gland on the opposite side. Which of the following best describes the normal gland that contains his nodule?","options":["Pyramidal gland capping the superior pole of the kidney","Crescent-shaped gland lying within the renal capsule at the hilum","Pyramidal gland lying in the lesser sac in front of the pancreas","Crescent-shaped gland on the medial border above the hilum"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-3","obj":"EN-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["The nodule is in the left adrenal gland.","The left gland is crescent shaped and lies along the medial border of the left kidney from the upper pole toward the hilum.","The right gland is pyramidal and sits on the upper pole of the right kidney."],"why":"Both adrenal glands are retroperitoneal and lie within the renal fascia, separated from the kidneys by perirenal fat. The left gland is crescent shaped and extends along the medial border of the left kidney down toward the hilum. The right gland is pyramidal, caps the upper pole of the right kidney, and lies behind the inferior vena cava. Knowing these shapes helps when reading CT scans and planning adrenal surgery.","whyNot":["A pyramidal gland capping the upper pole describes the right adrenal gland, not the left.","The adrenal glands lie outside the renal capsule, in their own compartment of renal fascia, separated from the kidney by fat.","The adrenal glands are retroperitoneal, not in the lesser sac. The left gland lies behind the lesser sac and pancreas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Adrenal gland anatomy","Retroperitoneal relations of the adrenals"],"comps":["EN-3","EN-4"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C17","system":"EN","dx":"Simple virilizing 21-hydroxylase deficiency with peripheral precocious puberty","codes":["EN-12-A1"],"case":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of pubic hair, adult body odor, and rapid growth over the past year. He has never had episodes of vomiting, dehydration, or hospitalization. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. His height is above the 97th percentile. Physical examination shows Tanner stage 3 pubic hair, an enlarged penis, and prepubertal testes with a volume of 2 mL each. Bone age is 9 years. Serum studies show sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 136 to 145), potassium 4.3 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0), testosterone 150 ng/dL (prepubertal under 10), and 17-hydroxyprogesterone 5,200 ng/dL (normal under 100). Diagnosis: simple virilizing 21-hydroxylase deficiency with peripheral precocious puberty.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C17-Q1","stem":"His physician also considered 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency, which also causes virilization. Which of the following steroids accumulates in 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency, but not in this patient's disorder, and causes hypertension?","options":["Androstenedione","11-Deoxycorticosterone","Corticosterone","17-Hydroxyprogesterone"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A1","obj":"EN-12-A.4","dok":3,"approach":["11-beta-hydroxylase acts after 21-hydroxylase, converting 11-deoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol.","Blocking it piles up 11-deoxycorticosterone, a mineralocorticoid.","In 21-hydroxylase deficiency this steroid cannot be formed, so blood pressure is normal or low."],"why":"In 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency, high ACTH drives the pathway, and 11-deoxycorticosterone builds up. This steroid activates mineralocorticoid receptors, so patients have hypertension, low renin, and sometimes hypokalemia, together with virilization from shunting to androgens. In 21-hydroxylase deficiency, 11-deoxycorticosterone is not made, so the risk is salt wasting rather than hypertension. The simple virilizing form, as in this boy, keeps enough aldosterone to avoid salt-wasting crises.","whyNot":["Androstenedione is raised in both disorders and causes virilization, not hypertension.","Correct.","Corticosterone is made by 11-beta-hydroxylase, so it is low in 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency. It is raised in 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency.","17-Hydroxyprogesterone is the substrate of 21-hydroxylase and is the key raised steroid in this boy's disorder. It has no mineralocorticoid effect."],"study":{"topics":["Forms of congenital adrenal hyperplasia","11-deoxycorticosterone and hypertension"],"comps":["EN-12-A1","EN-8"]}},{"id":"EN-C17-Q2","stem":"A GnRH stimulation test shows LH and FSH responses in the prepubertal range. Which of the following best explains these results together with his small testes?","options":["Early activation of the hypothalamic GnRH pulse generator","Activating mutation of the LH receptor in Leydig cells","Adrenal androgens suppressing GnRH secretion","Constitutive Gs-alpha activation in endocrine tissues"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-12-A1","obj":"EN-12-A.13","dok":3,"approach":["Virilization with prepubertal testes means the androgens are not coming from the testes.","Low LH and FSH show the hypothalamic GnRH pulse generator has not turned on, so this is peripheral precocious puberty.","In congenital adrenal hyperplasia the adrenal makes the androgens, and they suppress GnRH by negative feedback."],"why":"Central precocious puberty starts with early GnRH pulses, so LH and FSH rise and the testes enlarge. In peripheral precocious puberty, sex steroids come from outside the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis. Here the adrenal glands make androgens under high ACTH drive. These androgens feed back to suppress GnRH, LH, and FSH, so the testes stay small. Years of high sex steroids can later trigger central puberty as well.","whyNot":["Early GnRH pulses cause central precocious puberty with pubertal LH and FSH and enlarging testes.","Activating LH receptor mutations (familial male-limited precocious puberty) make the Leydig cells produce testosterone, so the testes usually enlarge, and 17-hydroxyprogesterone would not be high.","Correct.","Constitutive Gs-alpha activation is the cause of McCune-Albright syndrome, with cafe-au-lait spots and fibrous dysplasia. It does not raise 17-hydroxyprogesterone."],"study":{"topics":["Central versus peripheral precocious puberty","Feedback control of gonadotropins"],"comps":["EN-12-A1","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C17-Q3","stem":"His parents are healthy and not related to each other. Genetic testing confirms that he has two mutant CYP21A2 alleles. What is the probability that the parents' next child will be affected?","options":["1 in 8","1 in 4","1 in 2","2 in 3"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-9","obj":"EN-9.2","dok":2,"approach":["Congenital adrenal hyperplasia from CYP21A2 mutations is autosomal recessive.","Healthy parents of an affected child must both be carriers.","Two carriers have a one in four chance with each pregnancy of passing on two mutant alleles."],"why":"21-hydroxylase deficiency is autosomal recessive and is caused by mutations in CYP21A2, the most common cause of congenital adrenal hyperplasia. An affected child of unaffected parents shows that each parent carries one mutant allele. For each pregnancy, the chance of an affected child is 1 in 4, a carrier 1 in 2, and a noncarrier 1 in 4. Newborn screening measures 17-hydroxyprogesterone to catch affected infants early.","whyNot":["1 in 8 has no basis for two carrier parents; each pregnancy has a fixed 1 in 4 risk.","Correct.","1 in 2 is the risk for the child of a parent with an autosomal dominant disorder, or the chance that a child of two carriers is a carrier.","2 in 3 is the chance that an unaffected sibling of an affected child is a carrier, not the chance of being affected."],"study":{"topics":["Genetics of congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Autosomal recessive risk"],"comps":["EN-9","EN-12-A1"]}},{"id":"EN-C17-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains the high serum testosterone in this patient?","options":["Precursor shunting to androgens under high ACTH drive","Increased aromatase conversion of estrogens to androgens","Excess 11-deoxycortisol converted to androstenedione","Testicular Leydig cell stimulation by raised LH"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-8","obj":"EN-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["21-hydroxylase deficiency blocks cortisol synthesis, so ACTH rises from lost feedback.","ACTH drives the pathway, and 17-hydroxyprogesterone builds up behind the block.","The 17,20-lyase action of CYP17 turns the excess precursors into androgens, which are then converted to testosterone."],"why":"21-hydroxylase converts progesterone to 11-deoxycorticosterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol. When it is blocked, cortisol falls, ACTH rises, and the cortex becomes hyperplastic. Precursors such as 17-hydroxyprogesterone build up and are diverted by 17,20-lyase to DHEA and androstenedione, which are converted to testosterone. The raised 17-hydroxyprogesterone is the diagnostic marker.","whyNot":["Correct.","Aromatase converts androgens to estrogens, not the reverse.","11-deoxycortisol is made by 21-hydroxylase, so it is low in this disorder. It is raised in 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency.","His LH is in the prepubertal range and his testes are small, so the testes are not the source of the testosterone."],"study":{"topics":["Adrenal steroid synthesis pathway","21-hydroxylase deficiency"],"comps":["EN-8","EN-12-A1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C18","system":"EN","dx":"Autoimmune Addison disease in autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 2","codes":["EN-12-B2"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 4 months of fatigue, a 5-kg weight loss, salt craving, and light-headedness on standing. She has had type 1 diabetes mellitus for 3 years and takes levothyroxine for Hashimoto thyroiditis. Recently she has had frequent hypoglycemia. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 94/60 mm Hg supine and 78/50 mm Hg standing. Physical examination shows darkened palmar creases and buccal mucosa. Serum studies show sodium 128 mEq/L (normal 136 to 145), potassium 5.9 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0), glucose 62 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99), morning cortisol 2.0 µg/dL (normal 5 to 23), and ACTH 520 pg/mL (normal 10 to 60). 21-hydroxylase antibodies are present. Diagnosis: autoimmune Addison disease in autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 2.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C18-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her hyperkalemia?","options":["Loss of zona glomerulosa aldosterone secretion","Loss of medullary epinephrine-driven potassium uptake","Antibody blockade of renal mineralocorticoid receptors","Loss of zona reticularis androgen production"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B2","obj":"EN-12-B.3","dok":3,"approach":["Primary adrenal insufficiency destroys the whole cortex, including the zona glomerulosa.","Without aldosterone, principal cells secrete less potassium and reabsorb less sodium.","Hyperkalemia and hyponatremia with volume loss follow."],"why":"Autoimmune adrenalitis destroys all three zones of the adrenal cortex, so both cortisol and aldosterone fall. Loss of aldosterone reduces sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion in the collecting duct, causing hyperkalemia, hyponatremia, volume depletion, and salt craving. Loss of cortisol adds hypotension and hypoglycemia, and the high ACTH causes hyperpigmentation. In secondary insufficiency aldosterone is preserved, so hyperkalemia does not occur.","whyNot":["Correct.","The adrenal medulla is spared in autoimmune adrenalitis. Epinephrine shifts potassium into cells through beta-2 receptors, but loss of medullary output is not the cause here.","Antibodies against the mineralocorticoid receptor are not a recognized cause of hyperkalemia. Her 21-hydroxylase antibodies target steroid synthesis in the adrenal cortex.","Loss of adrenal androgens reduces axillary and pubic hair in women but does not affect potassium."],"study":{"topics":["Electrolytes in Addison disease","Primary adrenal insufficiency"],"comps":["EN-12-B2","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C18-Q2","stem":"Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 is caused by mutations in the AIRE gene. Which of the following additional findings would have pointed to type 1 rather than type 2 in this patient?","options":["Autoimmune primary hypothyroidism","Chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis","Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Onset of Addison disease in adulthood"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-10","obj":"EN-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["AIRE lets thymic medullary epithelial cells show tissue antigens to developing T cells so self-reactive cells are deleted.","AIRE loss causes autosomal recessive APS-1 in childhood: chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and Addison disease.","APS-2 combines Addison disease with autoimmune thyroid disease or type 1 diabetes, usually in adults."],"why":"AIRE drives expression of tissue-specific self antigens in the thymus so that self-reactive T cells are deleted. Loss of both AIRE copies causes APS-1, which begins in childhood with chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, then hypoparathyroidism and Addison disease. The candidiasis is linked to autoantibodies against IL-17 and IL-22, which weaken defense against Candida. APS-2 is more common, polygenic and HLA associated, and pairs Addison disease with autoimmune thyroid disease or type 1 diabetes in adults, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Autoimmune thyroid disease is a defining part of APS-2, as in this patient, so it does not point to APS-1.","Correct.","Type 1 diabetes is a common component of APS-2 and does not distinguish the syndromes.","Adult onset is typical of APS-2. APS-1 usually begins in childhood."],"study":{"topics":["Autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes","AIRE and central tolerance"],"comps":["EN-10","EN-12-B3"]}},{"id":"EN-C18-Q3","stem":"Loss of cortisol contributes to her hypoglycemia and to a fall in her insulin needs. Which of the following best explains why cortisol raises blood glucose more slowly than glucagon or epinephrine does?","options":["Inhibition of insulin release through alpha-2 receptors","Activation of hepatic glycogen phosphorylase by cAMP","Induction of gluconeogenic enzyme gene transcription","Stimulation of muscle glycogenolysis through beta-2 receptors"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-5","obj":"EN-5.6","dok":3,"approach":["Glucagon and epinephrine use membrane receptors and second messengers, so they act within minutes.","Cortisol uses an intracellular receptor that changes gene transcription, so its effect takes hours.","Cortisol deficiency removes this slow, sustained support of fasting glucose."],"why":"During hypoglycemia, glucagon and epinephrine respond first. They act through cell-surface receptors and cAMP to activate glycogen phosphorylase and gluconeogenesis within minutes. Cortisol and growth hormone act over hours. Cortisol binds an intracellular receptor and increases transcription of gluconeogenic enzymes such as PEPCK, and it supplies amino acids from muscle. In adrenal insufficiency this slow support is lost, so a person with type 1 diabetes needs less insulin and has more hypoglycemia.","whyNot":["Alpha-2 inhibition of insulin release is a rapid effect of epinephrine, not of cortisol.","cAMP activation of glycogen phosphorylase is how glucagon and epinephrine rapidly break down liver glycogen. Cortisol does not use this pathway.","Correct.","Beta-2 receptor stimulation of muscle glycogenolysis is an epinephrine effect. Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so it supplies lactate rather than free glucose."],"study":{"topics":["Glucose counterregulation","Fast and slow counterregulatory hormones"],"comps":["EN-5","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C18-Q4","stem":"Which of the following components of her adrenal glands is most likely to be preserved?","options":["Small cells in ovoid clusters beneath the capsule","Lipid-rich cells in radial cords of the middle zone","Basophilic chromaffin cells around a central vein","Eosinophilic cells in a network of anastomosing cords"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Her 21-hydroxylase antibodies target an enzyme of the adrenal cortex.","Autoimmune adrenalitis destroys all three cortical zones and spares the medulla.","Match each histologic description to its zone, and pick the medulla."],"why":"In autoimmune adrenalitis, lymphocytes infiltrate and destroy the cortex, which shrinks to a thin rim. The medulla, made of chromaffin cells that secrete catecholamines, is not a target and is preserved. From outside in, the cortex has the zona glomerulosa (ovoid clusters, aldosterone), the zona fasciculata (lipid-rich cells in long cords, cortisol), and the zona reticularis (eosinophilic cells in a network, androgens). The medulla sits in the center around the central vein.","whyNot":["Ovoid clusters under the capsule form the zona glomerulosa, which makes aldosterone. Its loss explains her hyperkalemia.","Lipid-rich cells in straight cords form the zona fasciculata, which makes cortisol and is destroyed in autoimmune adrenalitis.","Correct.","A network of eosinophilic cells forms the zona reticularis, which makes androgens and is also destroyed."],"study":{"topics":["Adrenal histology by zone","Pathology of autoimmune adrenalitis"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-12-B2"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C19","system":"EN","dx":"Tuberculous adrenalitis causing Addison disease","codes":["EN-12-B2"],"case":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of 6 months of fatigue, poor appetite, an 8-kg weight loss, and dizziness on standing. Fifteen years ago he was treated for pulmonary tuberculosis but stopped therapy after 2 months. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg. Physical examination shows darkened skin over the knuckles and darkened gingiva. Serum studies show sodium 127 mEq/L (normal 136 to 145), potassium 6.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0), morning cortisol 2.4 µg/dL (normal 5 to 23), and ACTH 610 pg/mL (normal 10 to 60). A chest x-ray shows fibrocalcific scarring at the right apex. CT of the abdomen shows enlargement of both adrenal glands with foci of calcification. Diagnosis: Addison disease from tuberculous adrenalitis.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C19-Q1","stem":"A CT-guided biopsy of an adrenal gland is performed. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Enlarged cells with intranuclear inclusions","Small intracellular yeasts within macrophages","Lymphocytic infiltrate with cortical atrophy","Acid-fast bacilli in caseating granulomas"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-11","obj":"EN-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Old, incompletely treated tuberculosis with enlarged, calcified adrenals points to tuberculous adrenalitis.","Mycobacterium tuberculosis has a mycolic acid-rich wall that retains carbol fuchsin, so it is acid fast.","The host response forms granulomas with central caseous necrosis."],"why":"Tuberculosis reaches the adrenals through the blood and destroys the glands with caseating granulomas. The mycolic acids in the cell wall make the bacilli acid fast on Ziehl-Neelsen staining. Infectious adrenalitis enlarges the glands early and leaves calcification later, while autoimmune adrenalitis shrinks them. Tuberculosis is the leading cause of Addison disease worldwide, and it can appear years after the lung infection.","whyNot":["Large cells with intranuclear inclusions describe cytomegalovirus, a cause of adrenalitis in patients with AIDS.","Small yeasts inside macrophages describe Histoplasma capsulatum, which can also cause adrenalitis. His history of untreated pulmonary tuberculosis makes M. tuberculosis the cause here.","Lymphocytic infiltration with a small, atrophic cortex is autoimmune adrenalitis. His glands are enlarged and calcified.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Infectious causes of adrenalitis","Mycobacterium tuberculosis features"],"comps":["EN-11","EN-12-B2"]}},{"id":"EN-C19-Q2","stem":"The biopsy needle is directed toward the right adrenal gland. Which of the following structures lies directly anterior to the medial part of this gland?","options":["Inferior vena cava","Abdominal aorta","Left renal vein","Body of the pancreas"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-3","obj":"EN-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["The right adrenal gland is pyramidal and sits on the upper pole of the right kidney.","Its medial part lies directly behind the inferior vena cava.","The left gland has different anterior relations, including the pancreas and splenic vessels."],"why":"The right adrenal gland lies in the retroperitoneum within the renal fascia, on the upper pole of the right kidney. Its medial part lies behind the inferior vena cava, and its lateral part touches the bare area of the liver. Its short vein drains straight into the inferior vena cava, which matters in surgery. The left gland lies behind the stomach, pancreas, and splenic vessels.","whyNot":["Correct.","The aorta lies to the left of the inferior vena cava and is medial to the left adrenal gland, not in front of the right one.","The left renal vein passes between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery and receives the left adrenal vein.","The body of the pancreas lies in front of the left adrenal gland and left kidney."],"study":{"topics":["Relations of the adrenal glands","Retroperitoneal anatomy"],"comps":["EN-3","EN-4"]}},{"id":"EN-C19-Q3","stem":"Two weeks later, during an episode of vomiting, his blood pressure falls to 72/40 mm Hg. It responds poorly to intravenous saline and norepinephrine until hydrocortisone is given. Which of the following actions of cortisol best explains the improved response to norepinephrine?","options":["Increased release of ADH from the posterior pituitary","Permissive enhancement of vascular alpha-1 responses","Increased synthesis of vasodilator prostaglandins","Increased parasympathetic tone to the heart"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["Norepinephrine raises blood pressure mainly through alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle.","Cortisol has a permissive effect: it does not constrict vessels itself but is needed for a full catecholamine response.","Without cortisol, vessels respond poorly to pressors, so shock is refractory until cortisol is replaced."],"why":"Cortisol is permissive for catecholamines. It supports adrenergic receptor expression and signaling in vascular smooth muscle, so norepinephrine and angiotensin II can keep up vascular tone. When cortisol is missing, vessels respond poorly to catecholamines and blood pressure stays low despite fluids and pressors. Giving hydrocortisone restores the response within hours. This is a key reason adrenal crisis presents as shock.","whyNot":["Cortisol deficiency increases ADH release, which contributes to hyponatremia. Replacing cortisol lowers ADH rather than raising it.","Correct.","Cortisol reduces prostaglandin synthesis by inhibiting phospholipase A2 and COX-2 expression. More vasodilator prostaglandins would lower pressure.","More parasympathetic tone would slow the heart and lower cardiac output, which would not improve his blood pressure."],"study":{"topics":["Permissive effect of cortisol","Shock in adrenal insufficiency"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-12-B2"]}},{"id":"EN-C19-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains the darkening of his skin and gingiva?","options":["Hemosiderin deposition from iron overload","Dermal deposition of carotenoid pigments","Melanocyte stimulation by adrenal androgens","Excess POMC-derived ACTH and MSH"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B2","obj":"EN-12-B.3","dok":2,"approach":["Low cortisol removes negative feedback, so the pituitary makes much more ACTH.","ACTH is cut from proopiomelanocortin (POMC), which also yields alpha-MSH.","ACTH and MSH stimulate melanocortin 1 receptors on melanocytes, darkening skin creases, scars, and mucosa."],"why":"In primary adrenal insufficiency, low cortisol releases the pituitary from feedback, and corticotrophs make large amounts of POMC. POMC is cleaved into ACTH and melanocyte-stimulating hormone. Both activate melanocortin 1 receptors on melanocytes and increase melanin. The darkening is most visible in palmar creases, knuckles, scars, and the gums and buccal mucosa. Secondary adrenal insufficiency has low ACTH and no hyperpigmentation.","whyNot":["Iron overload in hemochromatosis gives a bronze color, partly from iron and partly from melanin, but his findings are explained by adrenal failure with high ACTH.","Carotenoid deposition gives yellow-orange skin, most visible on the palms and soles, with normal sclerae. It is seen with high carrot intake or hypothyroidism and does not darken the gums.","Adrenal androgen production falls in Addison disease, and androgens are not the cause of the hyperpigmentation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["POMC processing","Hyperpigmentation in Addison disease"],"comps":["EN-12-B2","EN-6"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C20","system":"EN","dx":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome from meningococcemia","codes":["EN-12-H2","EN-12-B2"],"case":"An 18-year-old man who lives in a college dormitory is brought to the emergency department because of 12 hours of fever, headache, and a spreading rash. He has not received any meningococcal vaccine. Temperature is 40.0 °C (104.0 °F), pulse is 136/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 74/38 mm Hg. Physical examination shows neck stiffness and petechiae and purpura over the trunk and legs. Laboratory studies show a platelet count of 42,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000), prothrombin time 24 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5), fibrinogen 90 mg/dL (normal 200 to 400), and a markedly raised D-dimer. Gram stain of cerebrospinal fluid shows gram-negative diplococci. Despite antibiotics, fluids, and vasopressors, he dies 14 hours after arrival. Autopsy shows massive hemorrhage into both adrenal glands. Diagnosis: Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome from meningococcemia.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C20-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the adrenal findings at autopsy?","options":["DIC-related microthrombi with hemorrhagic necrosis","Granulomatous inflammation eroding adrenal arteries","Septic emboli forming bilateral adrenal abscesses","Autoantibody-mediated destruction of cortical cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-H2","obj":"EN-12-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Low platelets, long prothrombin time, low fibrinogen, and high D-dimer mean disseminated intravascular coagulation.","In DIC, small vessels fill with fibrin thrombi while clotting factors and platelets are used up.","In the adrenals, thrombosis plus bleeding causes hemorrhagic necrosis of both glands and acute adrenal insufficiency."],"why":"In meningococcemia, endotoxin triggers widespread activation of coagulation. Microthrombi block small vessels, and the consumption of platelets and factors causes bleeding. This is the same process that produces the petechial and purpuric rash. In the adrenals the result is bilateral hemorrhagic necrosis, which abruptly ends cortisol and aldosterone production. The resulting acute adrenal insufficiency adds to septic shock and makes it refractory to fluids and pressors.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomatous inflammation is caused by tuberculosis or fungi and destroys the adrenals over months, not hours.","Septic emboli can form abscesses, but the picture here is diffuse hemorrhage from DIC, not abscess formation.","Autoantibody-mediated destruction is autoimmune Addison disease, which develops slowly and shrinks the glands."],"study":{"topics":["Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome","Disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"comps":["EN-12-H2","EN-12-B2"]}},{"id":"EN-C20-Q2","stem":"Which of the following components of the organism is most responsible for his shock and disseminated intravascular coagulation?","options":["Mucosal IgA1 protease","Antiphagocytic polysaccharide capsule","Adhesive type IV pili","Lipooligosaccharide endotoxin"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-11","obj":"EN-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-negative diplococci in the cerebrospinal fluid of a dormitory resident point to Neisseria meningitidis.","Its outer membrane carries lipooligosaccharide, an endotoxin that activates macrophages and endothelium.","The resulting cytokine release and tissue factor expression cause shock and DIC."],"why":"Neisseria meningitidis is a gram-negative diplococcus spread by respiratory droplets, which is why close living quarters raise risk. Its outer membrane lipooligosaccharide acts like the lipopolysaccharide of other gram-negative bacteria. It binds TLR4 on macrophages and triggers release of TNF-alpha and IL-1 and expression of tissue factor. These cause vasodilation, capillary leak, and DIC. The organism also sheds outer membrane blebs, so endotoxin levels in the blood become very high.","whyNot":["IgA1 protease cleaves mucosal IgA and helps colonization of the nasopharynx. It does not cause shock.","The polysaccharide capsule lets the organism resist phagocytosis and survive in blood, and it is the vaccine target, but it does not directly trigger shock and DIC.","Type IV pili attach the organism to nasopharyngeal epithelium. They help colonization, not the systemic inflammatory response.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neisseria meningitidis virulence factors","Endotoxin and septic shock"],"comps":["EN-11","EN-12-H2"]}},{"id":"EN-C20-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why a polysaccharide-protein conjugate meningococcal vaccine produces class-switched IgG and memory B cells against the capsule?","options":["Polysaccharide presented on MHC class I to CD8 T cells","Carrier protein activating complement on the capsule","Carrier peptides recruiting CD4 T cell help","Repetitive epitopes cross-linking B cell receptors"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-10","obj":"EN-10.6","dok":3,"approach":["Pure polysaccharides cannot be presented on MHC molecules, so they cause a T cell independent response with mostly IgM and little memory.","In a conjugate, a capsule-specific B cell takes up the whole molecule and presents peptides from the carrier protein on MHC class II.","Helper T cells that recognize those peptides give CD40 ligand and cytokine signals for class switching and memory."],"why":"Polysaccharide antigens cross-link B cell receptors and activate B cells without T cell help. The response is mostly IgM, short lived, and weak in young children. Linking the polysaccharide to a protein carrier changes this. B cells specific for the capsule internalize the conjugate and present carrier peptides on MHC class II to CD4 T helper cells. The T cells then drive germinal center reactions, class switching to IgG, affinity maturation, and long-lived memory, which protect against invasive meningococcal disease and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome.","whyNot":["Polysaccharides are not presented on MHC class I, and CD8 T cells do not provide help for antibody class switching.","Activating complement is not the role of the carrier protein. Its job is to supply peptides that recruit T cell help.","Correct.","Cross-linking of B cell receptors by repeating sugar units is how a plain polysaccharide vaccine works. It gives a T cell independent IgM response with little memory."],"study":{"topics":["Conjugate vaccines","T cell dependent and independent antigens"],"comps":["EN-10","EN-11"]}},{"id":"EN-C20-Q4","stem":"In the normal adrenal gland, blood flows from capsular arteries through cortical sinusoids into the medulla before leaving through a single central vein on each side. Which of the following depends on this direction of flow?","options":["Medullary catecholamine stimulation of the zona glomerulosa","Cortisol induction of medullary PNMT for epinephrine","Direct drainage of cortisol into the hepatic portal vein","Delivery of medullary dopamine to the zona fasciculata"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-4","obj":"EN-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["Arteries enter at the capsule, so blood passes through the cortex before reaching the medulla.","Medullary chromaffin cells are therefore bathed in blood with very high cortisol.","Cortisol induces PNMT, the enzyme that methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine."],"why":"Each adrenal gland receives superior arteries from the inferior phrenic artery, middle arteries from the aorta, and inferior arteries from the renal artery. These form a capsular plexus that feeds sinusoids running inward through the cortex to the medulla. Blood leaves through a central vein: the right vein drains into the inferior vena cava, and the left into the left renal vein. Because medullary cells receive cortisol-rich blood, cortisol induces PNMT, which converts norepinephrine to epinephrine. This is why the adrenal medulla is the main source of circulating epinephrine.","whyNot":["Blood flows from cortex to medulla, so medullary products do not reach the zona glomerulosa in this way. Aldosterone is controlled mainly by angiotensin II and potassium.","Correct.","Adrenal veins drain to the inferior vena cava on the right and the left renal vein on the left, not into the hepatic portal vein.","Flow runs from cortex to medulla, so medullary dopamine does not reach the zona fasciculata. Cortisol synthesis is controlled by ACTH."],"study":{"topics":["Adrenal blood supply","PNMT and epinephrine synthesis"],"comps":["EN-4","EN-8"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C21","system":"EN","dx":"Pheochromocytoma","codes":["EN-12-G6","EN-12-G1"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman comes to the physician because of episodes of pounding headache, palpitations, and drenching sweats during the past 5 months. Each episode lasts 10 to 20 minutes. During an episode in the office, her temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 196/114 mm Hg. Between episodes, her blood pressure is 148/94 mm Hg. Fasting serum glucose is 132 mg/dL (reference, 70 to 99 mg/dL). Plasma free metanephrines are 6 times the upper reference limit. CT scan of the abdomen shows a 4-cm right adrenal mass. Diagnosis: pheochromocytoma.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C21-Q1","stem":"Her father underwent thyroidectomy at age 42 years for a calcitonin-secreting thyroid carcinoma, and her sister has hypercalcemia. Genetic testing of this patient is most likely to show which of the following germline mutations?","options":["Gain-of-function mutation in RET","Loss-of-function mutation in VHL","Loss-of-function mutation in MEN1","Loss-of-function mutation in NF1"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G6","obj":"EN-12-G.16","dok":3,"approach":["A calcitonin-secreting thyroid carcinoma is medullary thyroid carcinoma.","Pheochromocytoma plus medullary thyroid carcinoma plus hyperparathyroidism in one family is MEN2A.","MEN2A and MEN2B are caused by activating (gain-of-function) mutations of the RET proto-oncogene."],"why":"Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of chromaffin cells. About a quarter or more of cases are hereditary. The family history here (medullary thyroid carcinoma in her father, hypercalcemia in her sister) fits MEN2A: medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and parathyroid hyperplasia. MEN2 is caused by a gain-of-function mutation in RET, a receptor tyrosine kinase, inherited as autosomal dominant.","whyNot":["Correct.","Von Hippel-Lindau disease causes pheochromocytoma with hemangioblastomas and clear cell renal carcinoma, but not medullary thyroid carcinoma.","MEN1 (menin, a tumor suppressor) causes parathyroid, pituitary, and pancreatic endocrine tumors. It does not cause medullary thyroid carcinoma or pheochromocytoma.","Neurofibromatosis type 1 causes pheochromocytoma with neurofibromas and cafe-au-lait macules, but not medullary thyroid carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Hereditary syndromes with pheochromocytoma","MEN1 versus MEN2"],"comps":["EN-12-G6","EN-12-G5"]}},{"id":"EN-C21-Q2","stem":"Plasma free metanephrines were measured instead of plasma catecholamines. Which of the following best explains why metanephrines are the more sensitive test for this tumor?","options":["They are precursors that accumulate upstream of tyrosine hydroxylase","They are formed by monoamine oxidase in sympathetic nerve terminals","They are released into plasma only during hypertensive episodes","They are formed continuously by COMT inside the tumor cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-8","obj":"EN-8.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the pathway: tyrosine to DOPA (tyrosine hydroxylase), dopamine (DOPA decarboxylase), norepinephrine (dopamine beta-hydroxylase), epinephrine (PNMT).","Metanephrine and normetanephrine are the O-methylated products of COMT acting on epinephrine and norepinephrine.","Tumor cells contain COMT and make metanephrines all the time, while catecholamine release is episodic."],"why":"Catecholamines are released from the tumor in bursts and have a half-life of only minutes, so a single plasma catecholamine level can be normal between attacks. Pheochromocytoma cells also contain COMT, which converts the catecholamines stored in the cell to metanephrines. This leaks out steadily, independent of the episodes, so plasma free or urine fractionated metanephrines are raised almost all the time. Further breakdown by MAO and COMT yields vanillylmandelic acid.","whyNot":["Metanephrines are breakdown products, not precursors. Tyrosine hydroxylase is the first and rate-limiting step, converting tyrosine to DOPA.","MAO in nerve terminals deaminates norepinephrine to deaminated metabolites. Metanephrines require O-methylation by COMT.","Episodic release describes catecholamines. Metanephrines stay raised between episodes, which is why they are the better test.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Catecholamine synthesis and breakdown","Testing for pheochromocytoma"],"comps":["EN-8","EN-12-G6"]}},{"id":"EN-C21-Q3","stem":"Analysis of the resected tumor shows that it secretes mainly epinephrine. Which of the following features of normal adrenal blood flow allows chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla to synthesize epinephrine?","options":["Aldosterone from the zona glomerulosa induces dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Cortisol-rich blood flows from the cortex into medullary sinusoids","Medullary venous blood drains directly into the inferior vena cava","The medulla is supplied only by arterioles that bypass the cortex"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-4","obj":"EN-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["Converting norepinephrine to epinephrine needs PNMT.","PNMT is induced by high local cortisol concentrations.","Blood from the capsular plexus passes through cortical sinusoids into the medulla, delivering cortisol-rich blood to chromaffin cells."],"why":"The adrenal gland is supplied by superior adrenal arteries (from the inferior phrenic artery), middle adrenal arteries (from the aorta), and inferior adrenal arteries (from the renal artery). These form a capsular plexus. Most blood then runs through cortical sinusoids toward the medulla, picking up cortisol on the way. This high cortisol concentration induces PNMT in chromaffin cells, which methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine. Extra-adrenal paragangliomas lack this cortisol supply and usually secrete norepinephrine.","whyNot":["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase makes norepinephrine from dopamine. Aldosterone does not regulate medullary enzymes; cortisol induces PNMT.","Correct.","The right adrenal vein does drain into the inferior vena cava (the left drains into the left renal vein), but venous outflow does not control medullary enzyme expression.","The medulla has a dual supply: some medullary arterioles run straight through, but much of its blood arrives after passing through the cortex."],"study":{"topics":["Adrenal blood supply and drainage","Cortisol induction of PNMT"],"comps":["EN-4","EN-8"]}},{"id":"EN-C21-Q4","stem":"Epinephrine raises her blood glucose within minutes, mainly by stimulating hepatic glycogenolysis. Which of the following hormones raises blood glucose on the same rapid time scale, mainly by the same hepatic mechanism?","options":["Glucagon","Growth hormone","Triiodothyronine","Cortisol"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-5","obj":"EN-5.6","dok":2,"approach":["Sort counterregulatory hormones into fast (minutes) and slow (hours).","Glucagon and epinephrine act within minutes through cAMP signaling, starting with glycogenolysis.","Cortisol and growth hormone act over hours by changing gene expression and reducing insulin sensitivity."],"why":"When glucose falls, glucagon and epinephrine are the first line of defense. Both activate hepatic glycogen phosphorylase within minutes and later support gluconeogenesis. Cortisol and growth hormone act more slowly: cortisol induces gluconeogenic enzymes and growth hormone antagonizes insulin action, effects that take hours. That is why a patient lacking cortisol or GH can still correct acute hypoglycemia but becomes hypoglycemic during prolonged fasting.","whyNot":["Correct.","Growth hormone raises glucose slowly by antagonizing insulin and promoting lipolysis, not by rapid glycogenolysis.","Thyroid hormone increases metabolic rate and glucose turnover over days. It is not a rapid counterregulatory hormone.","Cortisol raises glucose over hours by inducing gluconeogenic enzymes such as PEPCK and supplying amino acids from muscle."],"study":{"topics":["Glucose counterregulation"],"comps":["EN-5","EN-7"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C22","system":"EN","dx":"Neuroblastoma of the adrenal medulla","codes":["EN-12-G1"],"case":"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a swollen abdomen, poor appetite, and a 1-kg weight loss over 2 months. His temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 128/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg. Examination shows a firm, nodular left upper quadrant mass that extends across the midline. CT scan shows a 9-cm left suprarenal mass with stippled calcifications. Urine homovanillic acid and vanillylmandelic acid concentrations are increased. Biopsy shows sheets of small round blue cells, some arranged around central fibrillary material in Homer Wright rosettes. Diagnosis: neuroblastoma of the left adrenal medulla.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C22-Q1","stem":"Molecular studies of the tumor are performed. Which of the following findings is most strongly associated with aggressive behavior and a poor prognosis in this tumor?","options":["Translocation t(11;22) forming EWSR1-FLI1","Amplification of the MYCN oncogene","Deletion of WT1 on chromosome 11p13","Biallelic loss of RB1 on chromosome 13q14"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G1","obj":"EN-12-G.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the tumor as neuroblastoma: small round blue cells, Homer Wright rosettes, raised HVA and VMA in a toddler.","Recall the key molecular marker of neuroblastoma: amplification of MYCN (N-MYC).","MYCN amplification predicts rapid growth and a poor outcome."],"why":"Neuroblastoma is the most common extracranial solid tumor of young children and most often arises in the adrenal medulla. Amplification of the MYCN oncogene, often seen as double minutes or homogeneously staining regions, is present in about a quarter of cases and is the strongest single marker of aggressive disease. Other prognostic factors include age over 18 months, high stage, and unfavorable histology. Some children present with opsoclonus-myoclonus as a paraneoplastic sign.","whyNot":["The EWSR1-FLI1 fusion from t(11;22) defines Ewing sarcoma, another small round blue cell tumor, usually in bone.","Correct.","WT1 deletion is linked to Wilms tumor, a kidney tumor of the same age group that rarely crosses the midline and does not raise HVA or VMA.","Biallelic RB1 loss causes retinoblastoma, which forms Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes in the eye."],"study":{"topics":["Neuroblastoma features and prognosis","Small round blue cell tumors"],"comps":["EN-12-G1"]}},{"id":"EN-C22-Q2","stem":"Many tumors of this type arise outside the adrenal gland, along the paravertebral sympathetic chain. Which of the following best explains why the tumor can arise at both sites?","options":["Both derive from intermediate mesoderm","Both derive from foregut endoderm","Both derive from surface ectoderm","Both derive from neural crest cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-1","obj":"EN-1.4","dok":3,"approach":["Neuroblastoma is a tumor of primitive sympathetic neuroblasts.","Chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla are modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons.","Both adrenal medulla and sympathetic ganglia come from neural crest, so the tumor can arise anywhere along that lineage."],"why":"Neural crest cells migrate ventrally to form sympathetic chain ganglia, and some invade the developing adrenal gland to form the medulla. The adrenal cortex has a different origin, from mesoderm (coelomic epithelium near the gonadal ridge). Because neuroblastoma and pheochromocytoma are tumors of neural crest-derived sympathoadrenal cells, they arise in the medulla or along the sympathetic chain, never from the cortex itself.","whyNot":["Mesoderm forms the adrenal cortex, which gives rise to adrenocortical adenomas and carcinomas, not neuroblastoma.","Foregut endoderm forms thyroid follicular cells, parathyroids, and pancreatic islets, not sympathetic tissue.","Surface ectoderm forms the epidermis, the lens, and (as oral ectoderm) Rathke pouch. Sympathetic ganglia and the adrenal medulla come from neural crest.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Adrenal embryology","Neural crest derivatives"],"comps":["EN-1","EN-2"]}},{"id":"EN-C22-Q3","stem":"His urine homovanillic acid concentration is increased. Homovanillic acid is formed by MAO and COMT acting on the product of which of the following enzymes?","options":["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase","DOPA decarboxylase","Tyrosine hydroxylase"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-8","obj":"EN-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Homovanillic acid (HVA) is the end product of dopamine breakdown; VMA comes from norepinephrine and epinephrine.","Dopamine is made from L-DOPA by DOPA decarboxylase (aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase).","So the enzyme whose product feeds HVA is DOPA decarboxylase."],"why":"In the catecholamine pathway, tyrosine hydroxylase makes L-DOPA, DOPA decarboxylase makes dopamine, dopamine beta-hydroxylase makes norepinephrine, and PNMT makes epinephrine. Dopamine is broken down by MAO and COMT to homovanillic acid, while norepinephrine and epinephrine are broken down to vanillylmandelic acid. Neuroblastoma cells release a lot of dopamine, so both HVA and VMA are typically raised in the urine.","whyNot":["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase makes norepinephrine, whose breakdown products are normetanephrine and VMA.","PNMT makes epinephrine, which is broken down to metanephrine and VMA.","Correct.","Tyrosine hydroxylase makes L-DOPA, the rate-limiting step. L-DOPA must first be decarboxylated to dopamine before it becomes HVA."],"study":{"topics":["Catecholamine metabolites HVA and VMA"],"comps":["EN-8","EN-12-G1"]}},{"id":"EN-C22-Q4","stem":"Small round blue cell tumors of childhood can look alike on routine stains. Which of the following immunohistochemical stains is most likely to be positive in this tumor?","options":["Leukocyte common antigen (CD45)","Myogenin","Pan-cytokeratin","Synaptophysin"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.5","dok":3,"approach":["Neuroblastoma comes from neural crest-derived neuroendocrine precursors.","Neuroendocrine cells contain synaptic-like vesicles and dense-core granules.","Synaptophysin (vesicle protein) and chromogranin (granule protein) mark neuroendocrine differentiation."],"why":"Endocrine cells that store hormones or amines in secretory granules, such as chromaffin cells, C cells, and islet cells, express neuroendocrine proteins. Synaptophysin is a membrane protein of small synaptic-like vesicles, and chromogranin A is found in dense-core granules. Tumors derived from these cells, including neuroblastoma, pheochromocytoma, carcinoid tumors, and small cell carcinoma, stain for these markers. This helps separate neuroblastoma from other small round blue cell tumors.","whyNot":["CD45 marks leukocytes and is positive in lymphoma and leukemia.","Myogenin marks skeletal muscle differentiation and is positive in rhabdomyosarcoma.","Cytokeratins mark epithelial cells and are positive in carcinomas, not in neuroblastoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neuroendocrine markers","Small round blue cell tumors"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-12-G1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C23","system":"EN","dx":"Prolactinoma (pituitary microadenoma)","codes":["EN-12-G4"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician because she has had no menstrual periods for 8 months and has noticed a milky discharge from both nipples. She and her husband have been trying to conceive for 1 year. She takes no medications. Her temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 72/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 114/72 mm Hg. White nipple discharge is expressible bilaterally. Visual fields are full. A urine pregnancy test is negative. Serum prolactin is 168 ng/mL (reference, under 25 ng/mL), and TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference, 0.5 to 4.5 μU/mL). MRI shows a 7-mm pituitary lesion. Diagnosis: prolactinoma (pituitary microadenoma).","questions":[{"id":"EN-C23-Q1","stem":"A 46-year-old man is found to have a 2.4-cm prolactin-secreting pituitary adenoma. Prolactinomas in men are more often macroadenomas at diagnosis than those in premenopausal women. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["Decreased libido is subtle, delaying diagnosis","Estrogen in women restrains lactotroph adenoma growth","Lactotroph adenomas in men are usually nonsecretory","Galactorrhea never occurs in men with hyperprolactinemia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G4","obj":"EN-12-G.7","dok":2,"approach":["Pituitary adenomas are classed by size (microadenoma under 1 cm, macroadenoma 1 cm or more) and by hormone.","In premenopausal women, amenorrhea and galactorrhea are obvious early, so tumors are found small.","In men, decreased libido and erectile dysfunction are easy to overlook, so the tumor grows until mass effects appear."],"why":"Prolactinoma is the most common functioning pituitary adenoma. In women of reproductive age it causes galactorrhea, amenorrhea, and infertility, which bring patients to care while the tumor is still a microadenoma, as in this patient. In men and postmenopausal women, the hormonal signs are subtle, so the tumor is often found later as a macroadenoma causing headache or visual field loss.","whyNot":["Correct.","Estrogen stimulates lactotrophs; that is why prolactinomas can enlarge during pregnancy.","Prolactinomas in men secrete prolactin, often in very high amounts.","Men with hyperprolactinemia can develop galactorrhea, though it is less common than in women."],"study":{"topics":["Pituitary adenoma classification","Prolactinoma presentation"],"comps":["EN-12-G4","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C23-Q2","stem":"Before the MRI, her physician measured TSH because primary hypothyroidism can also cause hyperprolactinemia. Which of the following best explains how primary hypothyroidism raises serum prolactin?","options":["GnRH stimulation of lactotroph secretion","TRH stimulation of lactotroph secretion","CRH stimulation of lactotroph secretion","TSH stimulation of lactotroph secretion"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.7","dok":2,"approach":["In primary hypothyroidism, low thyroid hormone removes negative feedback on the hypothalamus.","TRH secretion rises.","TRH stimulates lactotrophs as well as thyrotrophs, so prolactin rises."],"why":"Prolactin is the only anterior pituitary hormone under dominant inhibitory control: hypothalamic dopamine acting on D2 receptors keeps it low. TRH is a stimulator of prolactin release. When primary hypothyroidism raises TRH, both TSH and prolactin increase, and galactorrhea and menstrual changes can follow. Checking TSH avoids mistaking this for a prolactinoma.","whyNot":["GnRH acts on gonadotrophs to release LH and FSH. It does not raise prolactin.","Correct.","CRH drives ACTH release from corticotrophs and is not the link between hypothyroidism and prolactin.","TSH acts on thyroid follicular cells. Lactotrophs do not respond to TSH."],"study":{"topics":["Regulation of prolactin secretion"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C23-Q3","stem":"Which of the following sets of serum findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["Normal LH, normal FSH, high estradiol","High LH, low FSH, high estradiol","Low LH, low FSH, low estradiol","High LH, high FSH, low estradiol"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-9","obj":"EN-9.6","dok":3,"approach":["High prolactin suppresses pulsatile GnRH release from the hypothalamus.","Less GnRH means less LH and FSH: a central (secondary) cause of hypogonadism.","Without gonadotropin drive, the ovary makes little estradiol."],"why":"Hyperprolactinemia causes hypogonadotropic (secondary) hypogonadism. LH and FSH are low or inappropriately normal and estradiol is low, giving amenorrhea and infertility. Kallmann syndrome produces the same pattern through failed migration of GnRH neurons. Primary gonadal failure, such as Turner syndrome or Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY), gives the opposite pattern: high LH and FSH from loss of sex steroid and inhibin feedback.","whyNot":["High estradiol with normal gonadotropins would not cause amenorrhea through gonadotropin loss.","A raised LH with low FSH does not fit gonadotropin suppression; a high LH-to-FSH ratio suggests polycystic ovary syndrome.","Correct.","High gonadotropins with low sex steroids mean primary gonadal failure, such as premature ovarian insufficiency or Klinefelter syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Primary versus secondary hypogonadism","Prolactin and GnRH"],"comps":["EN-9","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C23-Q4","stem":"Prolactin causes her galactorrhea by binding receptors on mammary alveolar epithelial cells. The prolactin receptor signals through the same pathway as the receptor for which of the following hormones?","options":["Growth hormone","Oxytocin","Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Insulin"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Prolactin is a single-chain peptide hormone in the same family as growth hormone.","Its receptor is a cytokine-type receptor with no intrinsic kinase; it activates associated JAK2 kinase, which phosphorylates STAT proteins.","Growth hormone uses the same JAK/STAT pathway."],"why":"Hormones fall into groups by signaling pathway. Gs and cAMP: ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, PTH, glucagon. Gq and IP3-calcium: GnRH, TRH, oxytocin. Intrinsic tyrosine kinase: insulin and IGF-1. JAK/STAT (cytokine receptor family): growth hormone and prolactin. Intracellular receptors: steroids, thyroid hormone, and calcitriol. Prolactin binding activates JAK2 and STAT5, which drive milk protein genes in the breast.","whyNot":["Correct.","Oxytocin acts through a Gq-coupled receptor that raises IP3 and intracellular calcium.","TSH acts through a Gs-coupled receptor that raises cAMP.","The insulin receptor has intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity and signals through IRS proteins, not JAK/STAT."],"study":{"topics":["Hormone signaling pathways"],"comps":["EN-6"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C24","system":"EN","dx":"Acromegaly from a somatotroph pituitary adenoma","codes":["EN-12-A3","EN-12-G4"],"case":"A 47-year-old man comes to the physician because his rings no longer fit and his shoe size has increased over the past 3 years. He also reports headaches, excessive sweating, and snoring. His temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 154/96 mm Hg. Examination shows coarse facial features, frontal bossing, prognathism with widely spaced lower teeth, macroglossia, and large, thick hands. Fasting serum glucose is 121 mg/dL (reference, 70 to 99 mg/dL). MRI shows a 1.6-cm pituitary macroadenoma. Diagnosis: acromegaly from a growth hormone-secreting somatotroph adenoma.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C24-Q1","stem":"If the same adenoma had begun secreting excess growth hormone when he was 11 years old, which of the following would most likely have been the main additional feature?","options":["Isolated enlargement of the hands and jaw","Short stature from IGF-1 receptor downregulation","Excessive linear growth of long bones","Premature fusion of the epiphyseal plates"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A3","obj":"EN-12-A.7","dok":3,"approach":["At age 11, the epiphyseal growth plates are still open.","IGF-1 drives chondrocyte proliferation at open growth plates, lengthening long bones.","GH excess before epiphyseal closure causes gigantism (very tall stature); after closure, bones can only widen, causing acromegaly."],"why":"The effect of growth hormone excess depends on whether the epiphyses are open. Before closure, IGF-1 stimulates the growth plates and the child grows very tall (gigantism), often with some acral features as well. After closure, bones and soft tissue thicken instead: coarse face, prognathism, large hands and feet, and macroglossia. Acromegaly also causes insulin resistance and cardiovascular disease, including hypertension and cardiomyopathy, a leading cause of its excess mortality.","whyNot":["Isolated acral and jaw enlargement is the adult pattern, seen after the epiphyses have fused.","Excess GH raises IGF-1 and promotes growth. Short stature from GH resistance occurs in Laron syndrome, a GH receptor defect.","Correct.","GH and IGF-1 excess do not close the growth plates early. Early closure is caused by sex steroid excess, as in precocious puberty."],"study":{"topics":["Gigantism versus acromegaly"],"comps":["EN-12-A3","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C24-Q2","stem":"Serum IGF-1 and a 75-g oral glucose tolerance test are ordered. The age-adjusted IGF-1 reference range is 80 to 250 ng/mL, and growth hormone normally falls below 1 ng/mL after the glucose load. Which of the following sets of results would confirm the diagnosis?","options":["IGF-1 610 ng/mL; GH nadir 0.3 ng/mL","IGF-1 610 ng/mL; GH nadir 3.8 ng/mL","IGF-1 45 ng/mL; GH nadir 6.2 ng/mL","IGF-1 160 ng/mL; GH nadir 0.4 ng/mL"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A3","obj":"EN-12-A.8","dok":3,"approach":["GH is secreted in pulses, so a single random GH level is unreliable; IGF-1 reflects average GH secretion over the day.","Hyperglycemia normally suppresses GH. An adenoma secretes GH autonomously, so GH fails to fall.","Confirmation needs both a raised IGF-1 and failure of GH suppression after glucose."],"why":"IGF-1 is made by the liver in response to GH, circulates bound to binding proteins, and has a long half-life, so it is a stable screen for GH excess. The oral glucose tolerance test is the confirmatory test: in healthy people glucose suppresses GH below about 1 ng/mL, but a somatotroph adenoma keeps secreting. A raised IGF-1 with a GH nadir of 3.8 ng/mL confirms acromegaly, and MRI then locates the adenoma.","whyNot":["Raised IGF-1 with normal suppression does not confirm acromegaly; it can occur in puberty or pregnancy and needs repeat testing.","Correct.","Low IGF-1 with high GH suggests GH resistance, such as Laron syndrome, malnutrition, or liver failure.","Normal IGF-1 with normal GH suppression excludes GH excess."],"study":{"topics":["Laboratory confirmation of acromegaly"],"comps":["EN-12-A3","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C24-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains his fasting hyperglycemia?","options":["GH-induced autoimmune loss of beta cells","IGF-1 blockade of insulin receptors","IGF-1 stimulation of hepatic gluconeogenesis","Direct GH antagonism of insulin action"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Split GH effects into direct effects on tissues and indirect effects through IGF-1.","Direct GH effects are metabolic: lipolysis and opposing insulin (diabetogenic).","IGF-1 effects are growth effects and are insulin-like, so they do not explain hyperglycemia."],"why":"GH acts directly on liver, muscle, and fat to raise blood glucose. It increases hepatic glucose output, reduces glucose uptake in muscle, and promotes lipolysis, and the released free fatty acids worsen insulin resistance. This is why acromegaly often causes glucose intolerance or diabetes. Its growth effects, such as linear growth and protein synthesis, come mostly through hepatic IGF-1. GH release is stimulated by GHRH, sleep, exercise, and hypoglycemia, and inhibited by somatostatin and IGF-1.","whyNot":["GH does not cause autoimmune beta cell destruction; that is type 1 diabetes.","IGF-1 can weakly activate the insulin receptor. It does not block it.","IGF-1 has insulin-like effects and tends to lower blood glucose.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Direct and indirect actions of GH"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-12-A3"]}},{"id":"EN-C24-Q4","stem":"Histologic examination of the resected adenoma shows cells resembling normal somatotrophs. On hematoxylin and eosin staining, normal somatotrophs share their staining property with which of the following cell types?","options":["Basophilic thyrotrophs","Acidophilic lactotrophs","Basophilic corticotrophs","Pituicytes of the neurohypophysis"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Anterior pituitary cells are sorted by H and E staining into acidophils, basophils, and chromophobes.","Acidophils make the simple protein hormones GH and prolactin.","So somatotrophs group with lactotrophs."],"why":"Acidophils secrete growth hormone (somatotrophs) and prolactin (lactotrophs). Basophils secrete the glycoprotein hormones FSH, LH, and TSH, plus ACTH (a mnemonic is B-FLAT: Basophils make FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH). Chromophobes stain weakly. Some adenomas contain mammosomatotrophs that make both GH and prolactin, which reflects this shared lineage. The posterior pituitary contains pituicytes and the axon terminals of hypothalamic neurons (Herring bodies), not hormone-secreting epithelial cells.","whyNot":["Thyrotrophs are basophils that make the glycoprotein TSH.","Correct.","Corticotrophs are basophils that make ACTH from POMC.","Pituicytes are glial cells of the posterior pituitary and do not secrete GH or other anterior lobe hormones."],"study":{"topics":["Pituitary histology"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-3"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C25","system":"EN","dx":"Pituitary apoplexy in a nonfunctioning macroadenoma","codes":["EN-12-G4","EN-12-B4"],"case":"A 61-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 3 hours after the sudden onset of the worst headache of his life, with vomiting and double vision. For 1 year he has had trouble seeing cars approaching from either side while driving. His temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 86/54 mm Hg. Examination shows right ptosis, a dilated right pupil, and a right eye that rests down and out. Visual field testing shows bitemporal hemianopia. Serum sodium is 128 mEq/L (reference, 136 to 145 mEq/L), and serum glucose is 64 mg/dL (reference, 70 to 99 mg/dL). MRI shows a 2.8-cm sellar mass with suprasellar extension and acute hemorrhage. Diagnosis: pituitary apoplexy in a nonfunctioning macroadenoma.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C25-Q1","stem":"Which of the following acute hormone deficiencies most likely accounts for his hypotension, hypoglycemia, and hyponatremia?","options":["Adrenocorticotropic hormone","Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Growth hormone","Antidiuretic hormone"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-F","obj":"EN-12-F.3","dok":3,"approach":["Pituitary apoplexy is sudden hemorrhage or infarction of an adenoma that destroys the remaining gland.","Loss of ACTH means loss of cortisol within hours to days, and cortisol is needed for vascular tone and gluconeogenesis.","Cortisol deficiency also increases ADH release and impairs free water excretion, causing hyponatremia."],"why":"In apoplexy, the expanding hemorrhage raises pressure in the sella. It causes sudden severe headache, visual loss from chiasm compression, and ophthalmoplegia from compression of cranial nerves III, IV, and VI in the cavernous sinus. The most dangerous result is acute secondary adrenal insufficiency. Without ACTH, cortisol falls, causing hypotension, hypoglycemia, and hyponatremia, and treatment with glucocorticoids is urgent. Hyperkalemia is uncommon because aldosterone is controlled mainly by angiotensin II and potassium.","whyNot":["Correct.","Thyroxine has a half-life of about a week, so loss of TSH causes hypothyroidism slowly, not acute shock.","GH deficiency can contribute to fasting hypoglycemia in children, but it does not cause shock or hyponatremia.","ADH deficiency would cause polyuria and a tendency to hypernatremia, the opposite of his sodium."],"study":{"topics":["Pituitary apoplexy","Secondary adrenal insufficiency"],"comps":["EN-12-F","EN-12-B2"]}},{"id":"EN-C25-Q2","stem":"Six months before this event, his serum prolactin was 42 ng/mL (reference, 2 to 18 ng/mL). Which of the following best explains this earlier prolactin elevation?","options":["Increased TRH release from hypothalamic compression","Prolactin secretion by the adenoma cells","Stalk compression blocking dopamine delivery","Reduced renal clearance of prolactin"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G4","obj":"EN-12-G.8","dok":3,"approach":["The tumor is a nonfunctioning macroadenoma, so it does not make prolactin itself.","Prolactin is held down by dopamine carried from the hypothalamus through the stalk portal vessels.","A large mass that compresses the stalk cuts off dopamine, so normal lactotrophs release more prolactin (the stalk effect)."],"why":"A nonfunctioning macroadenoma causes problems by mass effect. It compresses the optic chiasm (bitemporal hemianopia), stretches the dura (headache), crushes normal pituitary tissue (hypopituitarism), and compresses the stalk. Stalk compression interrupts dopamine delivery to lactotrophs and produces mild hyperprolactinemia, usually under about 100 ng/mL. This must be distinguished from a macroprolactinoma, where prolactin is usually far higher.","whyNot":["Compression of the hypothalamus reduces releasing hormones; it does not cause a surge in TRH.","A prolactin-secreting macroadenoma usually produces prolactin well above 100 to 200 ng/mL; this tumor is nonfunctioning.","Correct.","Renal failure can raise prolactin modestly, but nothing suggests kidney disease, and the stalk effect explains the finding."],"study":{"topics":["Mass effects of pituitary macroadenoma","Stalk effect"],"comps":["EN-12-G4","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C25-Q3","stem":"His right ptosis, dilated right pupil, and down-and-out right eye are best explained by extension of the hemorrhage into which of the following structures?","options":["Optic chiasm above the sella","Floor of the third ventricle","Sphenoid sinus below the sella","Right cavernous sinus"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-3","obj":"EN-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Ptosis, a dilated pupil, and a down-and-out eye mean an oculomotor nerve (CN III) palsy.","The pituitary sits in the sella turcica, with the cavernous sinuses on each side.","CN III runs in the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus, so lateral expansion of the tumor compresses it."],"why":"The pituitary lies in the sella turcica, roofed by the diaphragma sellae. The optic chiasm lies just above, so upward growth causes bitemporal hemianopia by compressing crossing nasal retinal fibers. The cavernous sinuses lie on each side and contain the internal carotid artery and CN VI, with CN III, CN IV, V1, and V2 in the lateral wall. Sudden lateral expansion in apoplexy can therefore cause ophthalmoplegia, most often from CN III palsy.","whyNot":["Chiasm compression explains his bitemporal hemianopia but does not affect eye movement or pupil size.","Pressure on the third ventricle floor and hypothalamus can cause hydrocephalus or hypothalamic dysfunction, not CN III palsy.","Downward extension into the sphenoid sinus causes no cranial nerve palsy; the sinus is the surgical route to the pituitary.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Relations of the sella turcica","Cavernous sinus contents"],"comps":["EN-3","EN-12-F"]}},{"id":"EN-C25-Q4","stem":"Six weeks after surgical decompression, an intravenous CRH stimulation test is performed. Neither ACTH nor cortisol rises. Which of the following best explains this result?","options":["Loss of CRH neurons in the paraventricular nucleus","Loss of somatostatin tone on corticotrophs","Adrenal cortical resistance to ACTH","Destroyed corticotrophs in the anterior lobe"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-5","obj":"EN-5.1","dok":3,"approach":["Match the releasing hormone to its target: CRH stimulates corticotrophs to release ACTH.","Giving CRH bypasses the hypothalamus and tests the pituitary directly.","No ACTH response means the corticotrophs themselves are gone."],"why":"Each hypothalamic hormone has an anterior pituitary target: TRH to thyrotrophs (TSH, and prolactin), CRH to corticotrophs (ACTH), GnRH to gonadotrophs (LH and FSH), GHRH to somatotrophs (GH), somatostatin inhibiting GH and TSH, and dopamine inhibiting prolactin. A releasing hormone test separates a pituitary lesion (no response) from a hypothalamic one (response present). The hemorrhage destroyed his corticotrophs. If the pituitary stalk were cut instead, prolactin would rise while other anterior hormones fell, and diabetes insipidus could follow.","whyNot":["With a hypothalamic lesion, the corticotrophs are intact and exogenous CRH would still raise ACTH.","Somatostatin inhibits GH and TSH release. Losing it would not block an ACTH response to CRH.","Adrenal resistance to ACTH would leave ACTH high and rising after CRH, with only cortisol failing to rise.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypothalamic releasing hormones and targets","Stimulation testing of the pituitary"],"comps":["EN-5","EN-12-B4"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C26","system":"EN","dx":"Sheehan syndrome after postpartum hemorrhage","codes":["EN-12-F1","EN-12-B4"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician 4 months after the vaginal delivery of her second child. Delivery was complicated by uterine atony with blood loss of about 2.5 L, hypotension, and transfusion of 6 units of packed red blood cells. Since then she has had fatigue, lightheadedness on standing, cold intolerance, and no menstrual periods. Her temperature is 36.1 °C (97.0 °F), pulse is 56/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 94/58 mm Hg. Axillary and pubic hair are sparse. Serum sodium is 131 mEq/L (reference, 136 to 145 mEq/L), fasting glucose is 63 mg/dL (reference, 70 to 99 mg/dL), and 8 AM serum cortisol is 2.1 μg/dL (reference, 5 to 25 μg/dL). Diagnosis: Sheehan syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C26-Q1","stem":"Which of the following made her anterior pituitary especially vulnerable to ischemic injury during the hemorrhage?","options":["Autoimmune lymphocytic infiltration of the anterior lobe","Enlargement of the gland from lactotroph hyperplasia","Hemorrhage into a silent pituitary adenoma","Venous congestion from cavernous sinus thrombosis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-F1","obj":"EN-12-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["During pregnancy, estrogen causes lactotroph hyperplasia, and the anterior pituitary nearly doubles in size.","Its blood supply does not increase to match, so the enlarged gland sits at the edge of adequate perfusion.","Severe hemorrhage with hypotension then causes ischemic necrosis of the anterior lobe."],"why":"Sheehan syndrome is ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary after severe postpartum hemorrhage and shock. The pregnant gland is large and metabolically active because of lactotroph hyperplasia, but it still depends mainly on low-pressure portal blood, so a fall in blood pressure starves it. The posterior pituitary has a direct arterial supply from the inferior hypophyseal arteries and is usually spared, so diabetes insipidus is uncommon.","whyNot":["Lymphocytic hypophysitis does occur around pregnancy, but it is an autoimmune inflammation, unrelated to hemorrhage and ischemia.","Correct.","Bleeding into an adenoma is pituitary apoplexy. Nothing suggests a prior adenoma, and her injury followed systemic shock.","Cavernous sinus thrombosis causes ophthalmoplegia and proptosis and does not explain necrosis after hemorrhagic shock."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of Sheehan syndrome"],"comps":["EN-12-F1","EN-4"]}},{"id":"EN-C26-Q2","stem":"Which of the following additional findings is most likely to have been present soon after delivery?","options":["Inability to breastfeed","Persistent galactorrhea","Polyuria with dilute urine","Hyperpigmented palmar creases"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-12-F1","obj":"EN-12-F.2","dok":2,"approach":["Anterior pituitary necrosis removes prolactin, ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, and GH.","Lactotrophs are abundant and were working hardest after delivery, so prolactin loss shows first.","Without prolactin, milk production fails, often the earliest sign."],"why":"The presentation of Sheehan syndrome follows from the hormones lost. Low prolactin causes failure of lactation. Low LH and FSH cause amenorrhea and, with low adrenal androgens, loss of axillary and pubic hair. Low TSH causes hypothyroidism (fatigue, cold intolerance, bradycardia). Low ACTH causes secondary adrenal insufficiency (hypotension, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia), as in this patient.","whyNot":["Correct.","Galactorrhea requires prolactin; her lactotrophs have been destroyed.","Diabetes insipidus is uncommon because the posterior pituitary keeps its direct arterial supply.","Hyperpigmentation comes from high ACTH in primary adrenal failure. In Sheehan syndrome ACTH is low, so skin is pale."],"study":{"topics":["Clinical features of Sheehan syndrome"],"comps":["EN-12-F1","EN-12-B4"]}},{"id":"EN-C26-Q3","stem":"MRI 1 year later shows the sella turcica filled with cerebrospinal fluid and a thin rim of pituitary tissue along its floor. The same appearance can occur in people with no prior pituitary injury. In that primary form, which of the following is the cause?","options":["Autoimmune hypophysitis causing gland atrophy","Ischemic necrosis after a hypotensive episode","Arachnoid herniation through a defective diaphragma","Rathke cleft cyst expanding within the pars intermedia"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B4","obj":"EN-12-B.8","dok":2,"approach":["A CSF-filled sella with a flattened gland is an empty sella.","Secondary empty sella follows destruction or shrinkage of the gland, as after Sheehan necrosis, surgery, or radiation.","Primary empty sella occurs when arachnoid and CSF herniate through an incomplete diaphragma sellae and press the gland flat."],"why":"Hypopituitarism has several causes: nonfunctioning macroadenomas, Sheehan necrosis, pituitary apoplexy, surgery, and radiation. Any of these can leave an empty sella once the damaged tissue shrinks (secondary empty sella). Primary empty sella syndrome is different: a defect in the diaphragma sellae lets the arachnoid and CSF pulsations push into the sella and flatten the gland. It is common in obese women and in people with raised intracranial pressure, and pituitary function is often normal.","whyNot":["Hypophysitis can end in atrophy and a secondary empty sella; it is not the cause of the primary form.","Ischemic necrosis after hypotension is the secondary form seen in this patient, not the primary form.","Correct.","A Rathke cleft cyst is a fluid-filled mass within the sella; it expands the gland region rather than leaving an empty sella."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of hypopituitarism","Empty sella syndrome"],"comps":["EN-12-B4","EN-3"]}},{"id":"EN-C26-Q4","stem":"Which of the following features of the anterior lobe's blood supply best explains why it infarcted while her posterior lobe was spared?","options":["It receives direct arterial blood from superior hypophyseal arteries","It receives direct arterial blood from inferior hypophyseal arteries","It receives most of its blood through low-pressure portal veins","It drains its venous blood into the cavernous sinuses"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-4","obj":"EN-4.2","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the portal system: superior hypophyseal arteries form a capillary plexus in the median eminence, which drains into long portal veins down the stalk.","These veins feed a second capillary bed in the anterior lobe, so the anterior lobe gets mostly venous, low-pressure blood.","The posterior lobe gets arterial blood directly from the inferior hypophyseal arteries, so it tolerates hypotension better."],"why":"The hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system carries releasing hormones from the median eminence to the anterior lobe. The same design means the anterior lobe receives blood that has already passed through one capillary bed, at low pressure. When systemic pressure falls, flow through this portal circuit drops sharply and the anterior lobe infarcts. The posterior lobe, fed directly by inferior hypophyseal arteries, usually survives.","whyNot":["The superior hypophyseal arteries mainly supply the median eminence and upper stalk; the anterior lobe receives little direct arterial blood.","The inferior hypophyseal arteries supply the posterior lobe, which explains its sparing, not the anterior lobe's injury.","Correct.","Both lobes drain into the cavernous sinuses, so venous drainage does not explain why only one infarcted."],"study":{"topics":["Hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system"],"comps":["EN-4","EN-12-F1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C27","system":"EN","dx":"Craniopharyngioma with growth failure","codes":["EN-12-G4","EN-12-B4"],"case":"A 9-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of headaches for 1 year and frequently bumping into doorframes. Her height has fallen from the 50th percentile to below the 5th percentile over 2 years. Her temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Visual field testing shows bitemporal hemianopia. Funduscopic examination shows bilateral optic disc pallor. CT scan of the head shows a 3-cm suprasellar mass with cystic areas and calcifications. At surgery, the cyst contains thick, dark brown fluid resembling machine oil. Diagnosis: craniopharyngioma.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C27-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["Galactorrhea with very high prolactin","Polyuria with dilute urine","Hyperpigmentation of the gums and skin","Weight loss with heat intolerance"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G4","obj":"EN-12-G.9","dok":2,"approach":["Craniopharyngioma sits above the sella and presses on the hypothalamus, stalk, and optic chiasm.","ADH is made in the hypothalamus and carried down the stalk; damage there causes central diabetes insipidus.","The result is polyuria with dilute urine."],"why":"Craniopharyngioma is a benign but locally aggressive suprasellar tumor from Rathke pouch remnants. It has a peak in childhood and a second peak in older adults. It is often cystic and calcified, with oily fluid rich in cholesterol. It causes headache and hydrocephalus, bitemporal hemianopia from chiasm compression, and endocrine deficits: GH deficiency with growth failure, delayed puberty, hypothyroidism, and central diabetes insipidus from hypothalamic or stalk damage.","whyNot":["Stalk compression can mildly raise prolactin, but very high prolactin points to a prolactinoma, and galactorrhea is rare in a prepubertal girl.","Correct.","Hyperpigmentation requires high ACTH, as in primary adrenal failure. A suprasellar tumor lowers ACTH.","Heat intolerance and weight loss mean hyperthyroidism. A suprasellar mass tends to cause central hypothyroidism instead."],"study":{"topics":["Craniopharyngioma presentation"],"comps":["EN-12-G4","EN-12-B1"]}},{"id":"EN-C27-Q2","stem":"This tumor arises from remnants of an embryonic structure that also forms part of the normal pituitary. Which of the following describes that structure?","options":["Outpouching of oral ectoderm from the roof of the stomodeum","Downgrowth of the diencephalon forming the infundibulum","Neural crest cells migrating into the third pharyngeal pouch","Endodermal diverticulum at the foramen cecum of the tongue"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-1","obj":"EN-1.3","dok":2,"approach":["Craniopharyngioma comes from Rathke pouch remnants.","Rathke pouch is an upgrowth of oral ectoderm from the roof of the primitive mouth (stomodeum).","Rathke pouch forms the anterior pituitary; the infundibulum forms the posterior pituitary."],"why":"The pituitary has two embryonic sources. Rathke pouch, an outpouching of oral ectoderm, grows upward and forms the adenohypophysis (anterior lobe). The infundibulum, a downgrowth of the diencephalon (neuroectoderm), forms the stalk and posterior lobe. Rathke pouch normally loses its connection to the mouth, but epithelial remnants along its path can later give rise to craniopharyngioma, which is why the tumor contains squamous epithelium.","whyNot":["Correct.","The infundibulum forms the posterior pituitary and stalk, which are neural tissue and do not give rise to craniopharyngioma.","The third pharyngeal pouch is endoderm that forms the inferior parathyroids and thymus. Neural crest cells reaching the ultimobranchial body form thyroid C cells, not pituitary tissue.","The diverticulum at the foramen cecum forms the thyroid gland; its remnants cause thyroglossal duct cysts."],"study":{"topics":["Pituitary embryology"],"comps":["EN-1","EN-12-G4"]}},{"id":"EN-C27-Q3","stem":"Before surgery, which of the following sets of findings best fits the cause of her growth failure?","options":["Normal IGF-1, normal GH response, normal bone age","Low IGF-1, high GH response, delayed bone age","Normal IGF-1, normal GH response, delayed bone age","Low IGF-1, low GH response, delayed bone age"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B","obj":"EN-12-B.14","dok":3,"approach":["Her growth slowed and crossed percentiles after a tumor appeared near the pituitary, suggesting acquired GH deficiency.","In GH deficiency, both GH secretion and hepatic IGF-1 are low.","GH deficiency (and any accompanying central hypothyroidism) slows skeletal maturation, so bone age is delayed."],"why":"Short stature is sorted with IGF-1, stimulated GH, and bone age. GH deficiency: low GH response, low IGF-1, delayed bone age. GH receptor defects (Laron syndrome): high GH, low IGF-1. Constitutional growth delay: normal GH, delayed bone age, late puberty, and normal adult height. Familial short stature: normal GH and bone age that matches chronologic age. A child who falls off her growth curve with a sellar mass has acquired GH deficiency.","whyNot":["This is familial short stature: a short child growing at a normal rate with normal bone age, not one falling across percentiles.","High GH with low IGF-1 is GH resistance, as in Laron syndrome, a GH receptor defect.","This is constitutional growth delay, a normal variant without a fall across percentiles or a pituitary mass.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of short stature"],"comps":["EN-12-B","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C27-Q4","stem":"To test her GH reserve, she is given a stimulus that normally raises GH secretion. Which of the following normally stimulates GH release from the anterior pituitary?","options":["Hyperglycemia after an oral glucose load","Raised circulating IGF-1 levels","Insulin-induced hypoglycemia","Elevated plasma free fatty acids"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["GH is a counterregulatory hormone that helps defend blood glucose.","A fall in glucose increases GHRH and decreases somatostatin, so GH rises.","High glucose, IGF-1, and free fatty acids all feed back to suppress GH."],"why":"GH secretion is driven by GHRH and inhibited by somatostatin. It rises with deep sleep, exercise, stress, fasting, and hypoglycemia, and falls with hyperglycemia, raised free fatty acids, and IGF-1 feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary. Provocative tests for GH deficiency use stimuli such as insulin-induced hypoglycemia, arginine, or glucagon. A low peak GH response supports GH deficiency.","whyNot":["Hyperglycemia suppresses GH; that is the basis of the glucose suppression test for acromegaly.","IGF-1 inhibits GH release by negative feedback.","Correct.","Free fatty acids suppress GH release, part of the feedback loop that limits GH-driven lipolysis."],"study":{"topics":["Regulation of GH secretion"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-5"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C28","system":"EN","dx":"Central diabetes insipidus from hypothalamic Langerhans cell histiocytosis","codes":["EN-12-B1","EN-12-B4"],"case":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of excessive thirst and urination for 2 months. He has been waking at night to drink. He also has a tender swelling on his scalp. His temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 147 mEq/L (reference, 136 to 145 mEq/L), serum osmolality is 302 mOsm/kg H2O (reference, 275 to 295 mOsm/kg H2O), serum glucose is 88 mg/dL (reference, 70 to 99 mg/dL), and urine osmolality is 110 mOsm/kg H2O. A skull x-ray shows a punched-out lytic lesion. MRI shows a thickened pituitary stalk and loss of the normal posterior pituitary bright spot. Biopsy of the skull lesion shows Langerhans cells that are positive for CD1a. Diagnosis: central diabetes insipidus from Langerhans cell histiocytosis.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C28-Q1","stem":"His 14-year-old cousin has polyuria and dilute urine while taking lithium for bipolar disorder. Which of the following best distinguishes the cousin's disorder from this patient's disorder?","options":["Plasma ADH is high in the cousin and low in this patient","Plasma ADH is low in the cousin and high in this patient","Serum sodium is low in the cousin and high in this patient","Urine osmolality is high in the cousin and low in this patient"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B1","obj":"EN-12-B.1","dok":3,"approach":["Lithium causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus by impairing aquaporin-2 expression in collecting duct principal cells.","In nephrogenic DI, the hypothalamus senses high osmolality and releases plenty of ADH, but the kidney cannot respond.","In central DI, the defect is ADH deficiency, so plasma ADH is low."],"why":"Central and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus both cause polyuria with dilute urine and a tendency to hypernatremia when water intake is limited. Central DI is ADH deficiency from head trauma, surgery, tumors, or infiltrative disease such as Langerhans cell histiocytosis. Nephrogenic DI is resistance to ADH from V2 receptor or aquaporin-2 defects, lithium, hypercalcemia, or hypokalemia, and ADH (or its marker copeptin) is high. The ADH level separates them.","whyNot":["Correct.","This reverses the pattern. Low ADH is the defect in central DI.","Both forms of diabetes insipidus tend toward high-normal or high sodium; low sodium is not expected in lithium-induced DI.","Urine is dilute in both disorders."],"study":{"topics":["Central versus nephrogenic diabetes insipidus"],"comps":["EN-12-B1","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C28-Q2","stem":"Over the next year, the infiltrate destroys the TRH-secreting neurons of the hypothalamus. Which of the following sets of thyroid test results is then most likely?","options":["Low free T4 and markedly raised TSH","Low free T4 and low-normal TSH","High free T4 and suppressed TSH","Normal free T4 and mildly raised TSH"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B4","obj":"EN-12-B.9","dok":3,"approach":["Without TRH, thyrotrophs lose their main stimulus.","TSH falls or stays inappropriately normal, and the thyroid makes less T4.","Central hypothyroidism gives low free T4 without the high TSH of primary hypothyroidism."],"why":"Hypothalamic lesions such as craniopharyngioma, infiltrative disease (Langerhans cell histiocytosis, sarcoidosis), and radiation cause deficiency of releasing hormones. The anterior pituitary is intact but understimulated, so TSH, ACTH, LH, FSH, and GH output fall. Diabetes insipidus is common because ADH neurons live in the hypothalamus. Prolactin may rise mildly because dopamine no longer reaches lactotrophs. A low free T4 with a low or normal TSH is the thyroid signature of central disease.","whyNot":["Low T4 with high TSH is primary hypothyroidism, such as Hashimoto thyroiditis.","Correct.","High T4 with suppressed TSH is primary hyperthyroidism, such as Graves disease.","Normal T4 with mildly raised TSH is subclinical primary hypothyroidism."],"study":{"topics":["Hypothalamic lesions and hypopituitarism","Central hypothyroidism"],"comps":["EN-12-B4","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C28-Q3","stem":"A supervised water deprivation test is performed. After 6 hours, his urine osmolality is 125 mOsm/kg H2O and his serum sodium has risen to 149 mEq/L. Desmopressin is then given. Which of the following urine osmolalities 2 hours later is most likely?","options":["65 mOsm/kg H2O","135 mOsm/kg H2O","450 mOsm/kg H2O","1,150 mOsm/kg H2O"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-12-B1","obj":"EN-12-B.2","dok":3,"approach":["Water deprivation should concentrate the urine. His stays dilute, so he has diabetes insipidus.","In central DI, the kidney can still respond to ADH, so desmopressin raises urine osmolality by more than 50 percent.","Chronic polyuria washes out the medullary gradient, so the rise is large but falls short of maximal concentration."],"why":"The water deprivation test separates three causes of polyuria. In primary polydipsia, urine concentrates during deprivation and changes little after desmopressin. In central DI, urine stays dilute during deprivation and then concentrates sharply after desmopressin, often by more than 50 percent. In nephrogenic DI, urine stays dilute after both steps, rising less than about 10 percent. Because long-standing polyuria washes out medullary solute, even central DI rarely reaches near-maximal urine osmolality right away.","whyNot":["Desmopressin increases water reabsorption, so it cannot make urine more dilute than before.","A rise of less than 10 percent after desmopressin means nephrogenic DI.","Correct.","Near-maximal concentration is unlikely right away because chronic polyuria has washed out the medullary gradient."],"study":{"topics":["Water deprivation test"],"comps":["EN-12-B1","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C28-Q4","stem":"The posterior lobe itself is not infiltrated. Which of the following best explains why disease limited to the hypothalamus and pituitary stalk causes ADH deficiency?","options":["ADH is made in the anterior lobe and released into portal veins","ADH is made in hypothalamic nuclei and moves down axons","ADH is made in hypothalamic nuclei and carried in portal veins","ADH is made by pituicytes and stored in Herring bodies"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-8","obj":"EN-8.5","dok":3,"approach":["ADH and oxytocin are synthesized in neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei.","They are packaged with carrier proteins called neurophysins and transported down axons in the stalk.","The posterior lobe only stores and releases them, so cutting or infiltrating the stalk stops delivery."],"why":"ADH is made as a larger precursor (preprovasopressin) in magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei. The precursor is cleaved into ADH, neurophysin II, and copeptin inside granules that travel down the axons of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract. The hormone is stored in swollen axon terminals (Herring bodies) in the posterior lobe and released into blood when osmolality rises. The normal T1 bright spot on MRI reflects these stored granules; its loss here shows that ADH is no longer arriving.","whyNot":["The anterior lobe makes ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, GH, and prolactin, not ADH.","Correct.","Portal veins carry releasing hormones to the anterior lobe. ADH reaches the posterior lobe inside axons.","Pituicytes are supporting glial cells. Herring bodies are axon terminals of hypothalamic neurons, not pituicyte products."],"study":{"topics":["Synthesis and transport of ADH and oxytocin"],"comps":["EN-8","EN-2"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C29","system":"EN","dx":"SIADH from small cell lung carcinoma","codes":["EN-12-A","EN-12-G"],"case":"A 66-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of increasing confusion and nausea for 1 week. He has smoked 1.5 packs of cigarettes daily for 45 years and takes no medications. His temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 82/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg without orthostatic change. Mucous membranes are moist, jugular venous pressure is normal, and there is no edema. Serum sodium is 121 mEq/L (reference, 136 to 145 mEq/L), serum osmolality is 252 mOsm/kg H2O (reference, 275 to 295 mOsm/kg H2O), urine osmolality is 480 mOsm/kg H2O, and urine sodium is 52 mEq/L. TSH and morning cortisol are normal. CT scan of the chest shows a large central hilar mass. Biopsy shows small cell carcinoma. Diagnosis: SIADH from small cell lung carcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C29-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his urine sodium concentration is high despite his hyponatremia?","options":["Proximal tubule injury from tumor-derived toxins","Primary aldosterone deficiency causing renal salt wasting","ADH directly blocks sodium channels in collecting ducts","Water retention expands volume and induces natriuresis"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-12-A","obj":"EN-12-A.11","dok":3,"approach":["Euvolemic hyponatremia with urine more concentrated than plasma means ADH is acting inappropriately.","Retained water slightly expands the extracellular volume.","The kidney responds as it would to any volume expansion: renin and aldosterone fall, natriuretic peptides rise, and sodium is excreted."],"why":"In SIADH, ADH is released despite low plasma osmolality. Retained water dilutes sodium, and the mild volume expansion suppresses the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and raises atrial natriuretic peptide, so urine sodium stays above about 30 to 40 mEq/L. Because the extra water is spread through all body compartments, edema does not develop and the patient looks euvolemic. Causes include small cell lung carcinoma, CNS disease, lung disease, and drugs. Hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency must be excluded first.","whyNot":["Tumor products do not cause proximal tubule salt wasting in this setting, and that would not explain the concentrated urine.","Primary adrenal failure causes salt wasting with hypotension and hyperkalemia; his blood pressure and cortisol are normal.","ADH acts through V2 receptors to insert aquaporin-2 water channels. It does not block sodium channels; it modestly increases sodium reabsorption.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["SIADH laboratory pattern"],"comps":["EN-12-A","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C29-Q2","stem":"Three months later, he has proximal muscle weakness, leg edema, serum potassium of 2.8 mEq/L (reference, 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), and fasting glucose of 214 mg/dL (reference, 70 to 99 mg/dL). His blood pressure is 168/98 mm Hg. Which of the following is the most likely cause of these new findings?","options":["PTH-related peptide secretion by the tumor","Autoantibodies to presynaptic calcium channels","Metastatic destruction of both adrenal glands","Ectopic ACTH secretion by the tumor"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G","obj":"EN-12-G.17","dok":3,"approach":["Hypokalemia, hyperglycemia, hypertension, edema, and proximal weakness point to cortisol excess.","Small cell carcinoma is a neuroendocrine tumor that can secrete ACTH as well as ADH.","Ectopic ACTH levels are often very high, so mineralocorticoid effects of cortisol (hypokalemia, hypertension) are prominent and develop quickly."],"why":"Small cell lung carcinoma is the classic source of ectopic ACTH and ADH. Ectopic ACTH drives adrenal cortisol to levels that overwhelm 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 in the kidney, so cortisol acts on mineralocorticoid receptors and causes hypokalemia and hypertension. Hypercalcemia of malignancy is a different paraneoplastic syndrome, caused by PTH-related peptide from squamous cell lung carcinoma or renal cell carcinoma, with suppressed PTH.","whyNot":["PTH-related peptide causes hypercalcemia and is typical of squamous cell lung carcinoma and renal cell carcinoma, not this picture.","Lambert-Eaton syndrome causes proximal weakness with small cell carcinoma, but it does not cause hypokalemia, hyperglycemia, or hypertension.","Bilateral adrenal destruction would cause adrenal insufficiency with hypotension, hyperkalemia, and hypoglycemia.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Endocrine paraneoplastic syndromes"],"comps":["EN-12-G","EN-12-A1"]}},{"id":"EN-C29-Q3","stem":"In a healthy person, a fall in plasma osmolality to his level would stop ADH release almost completely. Which of the following sensors normally mediates this response?","options":["Atrial stretch receptors in the left atrium","Macula densa cells sensing low tubular sodium","Osmoreceptors in the anterior hypothalamus","Carotid sinus baroreceptors sensing low volume"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-5","obj":"EN-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["ADH is controlled by two inputs: plasma osmolality and effective blood volume.","Osmolality is the sensitive minute-to-minute controller; a change of about 1 to 2 percent alters ADH release.","Osmoreceptors in the anterior hypothalamus near the third ventricle sense this and signal the supraoptic and paraventricular neurons."],"why":"Hypothalamic osmoreceptors shrink when plasma osmolality rises and swell when it falls. Low osmolality silences ADH release so that dilute urine is excreted. Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus, aortic arch, and atria stimulate ADH when volume falls by about 10 percent or more. ADH binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells, raises cAMP, and inserts aquaporin-2 channels into the luminal membrane. In SIADH, the tumor releases ADH regardless of these controls.","whyNot":["Atrial stretch receptors respond to volume, and atrial stretch suppresses ADH; they do not sense osmolality.","The macula densa senses tubular sodium chloride and controls renin release and glomerular filtration, not ADH.","Correct.","Baroreceptors increase ADH when blood volume falls; his volume is normal and they respond only to larger changes."],"study":{"topics":["Control of ADH release"],"comps":["EN-5","EN-8"]}},{"id":"EN-C29-Q4","stem":"The tumor cells stain for chromogranin A and synaptophysin, markers of neuroendocrine cells. Normal neuroendocrine cells come from several germ layers. Which of the following normal endocrine cells is derived from neural crest?","options":["Thyroid C cells","Parathyroid chief cells","Thyroid follicular cells","Pancreatic beta cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Sort endocrine cells by origin: neural crest, endoderm, or ectoderm.","Neural crest gives chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla and the parafollicular C cells of the thyroid.","Endoderm gives thyroid follicular cells, parathyroid cells, and islet cells."],"why":"Endocrine cells come from different germ layers. Neural crest: adrenal chromaffin cells and thyroid C cells (via the ultimobranchial body). Endoderm: thyroid follicular cells, parathyroid chief cells, and pancreatic islet cells. Ectoderm: the adenohypophysis from Rathke pouch. Despite these different origins, cells that store hormones in secretory granules share neuroendocrine markers such as chromogranin and synaptophysin, and so do the tumors that arise from them.","whyNot":["Correct.","Parathyroid chief cells come from the endoderm of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches.","Thyroid follicular cells come from the endodermal thyroid diverticulum at the foramen cecum.","Islet cells, including beta cells, come from foregut endoderm of the pancreatic buds."],"study":{"topics":["Embryonic origin of endocrine cells"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C30","system":"EN","dx":"Primary hyperparathyroidism from a parathyroid adenoma","codes":["EN-12-A2","EN-12-G3"],"case":"A 57-year-old woman comes to the physician because of fatigue, constipation, and aching in her legs for 6 months. Two months ago she passed a calcium oxalate kidney stone. Her temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 78/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg. Serum calcium is 11.6 mg/dL (reference, 8.5 to 10.2 mg/dL), phosphate is 2.1 mg/dL (reference, 2.5 to 4.5 mg/dL), and intact PTH is 128 pg/mL (reference, 10 to 65 pg/mL). Twenty-four-hour urine calcium is 340 mg (reference, 100 to 300 mg). A sestamibi scan shows a single focus of uptake below the lower pole of the right thyroid lobe. Diagnosis: primary hyperparathyroidism from a parathyroid adenoma.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C30-Q1","stem":"A radiograph shows a lytic lesion of the mandible. Biopsy shows fibrous tissue with many osteoclast-type giant cells and hemosiderin-laden macrophages. Which of the following best explains this lesion?","options":["Metastatic carcinoma secreting PTH-related peptide","Giant cell tumor arising from neoplastic stromal cells","PTH-driven resorption with bleeding and fibrous repair","Defective mineralization of newly formed osteoid"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A2","obj":"EN-12-A.5","dok":3,"approach":["High PTH increases osteoclast activity (indirectly, through RANKL from osteoblasts).","Severe resorption causes microfractures and bleeding; macrophages take up the blood (hemosiderin) and fibrous tissue fills in.","The result is a brown tumor, part of osteitis fibrosa cystica. It is a reactive lesion."],"why":"Primary hyperparathyroidism, usually from a single adenoma, causes hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, and high PTH. Its effects are summarized as stones (kidney stones), bones (bone pain, osteitis fibrosa cystica), groans (constipation, abdominal pain), and psychiatric overtones. In bone, PTH drives osteoclastic resorption. Early radiographic signs are subperiosteal resorption on the radial side of the middle phalanges. In severe disease, hemorrhage and repair produce brown tumors, named for their hemosiderin color.","whyNot":["PTH-related peptide from a carcinoma would suppress PTH. Her PTH is high, and the lesion lacks tumor cells.","Giant cell tumor of bone is a true neoplasm, typically at the ends of long bones, and is not caused by PTH excess.","Correct.","Defective mineralization is osteomalacia, usually from vitamin D deficiency, with low or normal calcium."],"study":{"topics":["Osteitis fibrosa cystica and brown tumors"],"comps":["EN-12-A2","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C30-Q2","stem":"Her 34-year-old brother is found to have serum calcium of 10.9 mg/dL, intact PTH of 68 pg/mL, and 24-hour urine calcium of 45 mg (reference ranges as above). He has never had kidney stones. Which of the following best explains his findings?","options":["Gain-of-function calcium-sensing receptor mutation","Loss-of-function mutation in the MEN1 gene","PTH-related peptide from an occult tumor","Loss-of-function calcium-sensing receptor mutation"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-12-A2","obj":"EN-12-A.12","dok":3,"approach":["Mild hypercalcemia with mildly raised PTH could be primary hyperparathyroidism, but his urine calcium is low.","The calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid and kidney cells sets the calcium level that turns off PTH and controls renal calcium excretion.","An inactivating mutation raises that set point: PTH is not suppressed, and the kidney reabsorbs calcium avidly, giving familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia."],"why":"Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH) is usually autosomal dominant and caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the calcium-sensing receptor gene (CASR). The parathyroid needs a higher calcium level to suppress PTH, and the thick ascending limb reabsorbs more calcium, so urine calcium is low. Calcium is mildly raised, PTH is normal or mildly raised, and complications are rare. A calcium-to-creatinine clearance ratio under about 0.01 favors FHH, and parathyroid surgery does not help.","whyNot":["Activating calcium-sensing receptor mutations lower the set point, causing hypocalcemia with low PTH and relatively high urine calcium.","MEN1 causes multigland primary hyperparathyroidism with high urine calcium and stones, not low urine calcium.","PTH-related peptide would suppress his own PTH, and hypercalcemia of malignancy is usually more severe.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia","Calcium-sensing receptor"],"comps":["EN-12-A2","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C30-Q3","stem":"Which of the following actions of PTH best explains her serum phosphate concentration?","options":["Less phosphate reabsorbed in the proximal tubule","Increased phosphate secretion in the distal tubule","Increased phosphate deposition in bone matrix","Decreased intestinal phosphate absorption"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-5","obj":"EN-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Her phosphate is low while PTH is high.","PTH removes sodium-phosphate cotransporters (NaPi-IIa) from the proximal tubule luminal membrane.","Less phosphate is reabsorbed, so more is lost in urine and serum phosphate falls."],"why":"Low ionized calcium is sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor and increases PTH release. PTH raises calcium in three ways: it promotes bone resorption, increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule, and stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase to make calcitriol, which increases intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption. It also causes phosphaturia by reducing proximal tubule phosphate reabsorption, which is why PTH excess gives hypophosphatemia. Calcitonin from thyroid C cells opposes PTH by inhibiting osteoclasts, but its role in adults is minor.","whyNot":["Correct.","The kidney handles phosphate by filtration and proximal reabsorption; distal tubular secretion of phosphate is not the mechanism.","PTH increases bone resorption, releasing phosphate from bone; it does not drive phosphate into bone.","PTH increases calcitriol, which raises intestinal phosphate absorption."],"study":{"topics":["Renal actions of PTH","Calcium and phosphate homeostasis"],"comps":["EN-5","EN-12-A2"]}},{"id":"EN-C30-Q4","stem":"Which of the following cell types makes up most of the resected adenoma and secretes the hormone causing her hypercalcemia?","options":["Follicular cells lining colloid","Chief cells with pale cytoplasm","Oxyphil cells packed with mitochondria","Parafollicular cells with calcitonin granules"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Her hypercalcemia is driven by PTH.","Within the parathyroid, PTH is made by chief cells.","Chief cells are small polygonal cells with pale-staining cytoplasm; oxyphil cells are larger and pink because they are packed with mitochondria."],"why":"The parathyroid contains chief cells, which synthesize and secrete PTH, and oxyphil cells, which appear after puberty, increase with age, and have abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm from many mitochondria. Most parathyroid adenomas are composed mainly of chief cells. In the pancreas, by comparison, islet alpha cells make glucagon, beta cells make insulin, and delta cells make somatostatin.","whyNot":["Thyroid follicular cells make thyroid hormone and have no role in calcium regulation.","Correct.","Oxyphil cells are a minority population and are not the main PTH-secreting cell; oxyphil adenomas exist but are uncommon.","Parafollicular C cells are in the thyroid and secrete calcitonin, which lowers calcium."],"study":{"topics":["Parathyroid histology"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-12-G3"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C31","system":"EN","dx":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism in chronic kidney disease","codes":["EN-12-A2"],"case":"A 61-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease from hypertensive nephrosclerosis comes to the physician because of diffuse bone pain and itching for 6 months. Her estimated glomerular filtration rate is 19 mL/min/1.73 m2. Laboratory studies show serum calcium 8.3 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 6.4 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), albumin 3.9 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), alkaline phosphatase 210 U/L (40 to 120), intact PTH 420 pg/mL (10 to 65), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D 36 ng/mL (30 to 100). Serum 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is below the reference range. X-rays of the hands show subperiosteal bone resorption along the radial sides of the middle phalanges. The diagnosis is secondary hyperparathyroidism due to chronic kidney disease.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C31-Q1","stem":"Three years later, she receives a kidney transplant, and graft function is normal. Six months after transplantation, serum calcium is 11.3 mg/dL, phosphate is 2.3 mg/dL, and intact PTH is 365 pg/mL. Which of the following best explains these findings?","options":["Calcitriol overproduction by the transplanted kidney","Persistent phosphate retention by the transplanted kidney","Autonomous PTH secretion by hyperplastic glands","Ectopic PTH-related peptide secretion by a malignancy"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-A2","obj":"EN-12-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the new pattern: high calcium, low phosphate, and a PTH that is still very high after the kidney problem was fixed.","Normal parathyroid glands would lower PTH once calcium is high, so these glands no longer respond to feedback.","Years of stimulation in CKD made the glands hyperplastic, and they now secrete PTH on their own. This is tertiary hyperparathyroidism."],"why":"In CKD, phosphate retention, low calcitriol, and low calcium stimulate the parathyroid glands for years. The glands become hyperplastic, sometimes nodular, and they express fewer calcium-sensing and vitamin D receptors. After transplantation the original stimulus is gone, but the enlarged glands keep secreting PTH regardless of calcium. High PTH with high calcium after long-standing secondary hyperparathyroidism is tertiary hyperparathyroidism. The low phosphate reflects PTH-driven phosphate excretion by the healthy graft.","whyNot":["A working graft does make calcitriol, but calcitriol raises calcium and suppresses PTH. It cannot explain a PTH this high alongside hypercalcemia.","Her phosphate is now low. A healthy graft exposed to high PTH excretes phosphate, which is what the labs show.","Correct.","PTH-related peptide from a tumor raises calcium, and the high calcium then suppresses the patient's own PTH. Intact PTH would be low, as in humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy."],"study":{"topics":["Secondary versus tertiary hyperparathyroidism","Renal osteodystrophy"],"comps":["EN-12-A2","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C31-Q2","stem":"Her physician wants to correct her deficiency of active vitamin D hormone. Which of the following forms of vitamin D would be most effective, given her kidney disease?","options":["Calcifediol","Calcitriol","Ergocalciferol","Cholecalciferol"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-7","obj":"EN-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["Her 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal but her 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is low, so the block is at the kidney step.","The 1-alpha-hydroxylase that makes the active hormone sits in proximal tubule cells, which are lost in advanced CKD.","Only a form that already carries the 1-alpha hydroxyl group bypasses that step. Calcitriol is 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D itself."],"why":"Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) is made in skin when UVB light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol, and vitamin D2 (ergocalciferol) comes from plant and fungal sources. The liver adds a 25-hydroxyl group to make calcifediol, the main storage form. The kidney then adds the 1-alpha hydroxyl group, a step stimulated by PTH and low phosphate and suppressed by FGF23. In advanced CKD, proximal tubule mass is lost and phosphate retention raises FGF23, so this last step fails. Every precursor still needs the kidney, so calcitriol is the form that works.","whyNot":["Calcifediol is already 25-hydroxylated, but it still needs renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase to become the active hormone.","Correct.","Ergocalciferol is the plant and fungal form of vitamin D. It also needs 25- and 1-alpha hydroxylation, so it meets the same kidney block.","Cholecalciferol still needs both liver and kidney hydroxylation. It would raise her 25-hydroxyvitamin D, which is already normal, without fixing the kidney block. It is the right choice for simple vitamin D deficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation steps","Vitamin D in chronic kidney disease"],"comps":["EN-7","EN-12-A2"]}},{"id":"EN-C31-Q3","stem":"Her serum calcium is only slightly below the reference range despite her low calcitriol, partly because of the bone resorption seen on her x-rays. Which of the following best explains how her elevated PTH increases bone resorption?","options":["Increased RANKL expression by osteoblasts","Binding of PTH to receptors on osteoclasts","Increased osteoprotegerin release by osteoblasts","Increased calcitonin secretion acting on osteoclasts"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-5","obj":"EN-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Bone resorption is carried out by osteoclasts, so ask which cell PTH actually acts on.","PTH receptors (PTH1R) are on osteoblasts and osteocytes. Osteoclasts do not have them.","PTH makes these cells express more RANKL and less osteoprotegerin, so more osteoclasts form and become active."],"why":"PTH binds PTH1R, a Gs-coupled receptor on osteoblasts and osteocytes. Continuously high PTH makes these cells increase RANKL and decrease osteoprotegerin, the decoy receptor that normally blocks RANKL. RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their maturation, so resorption rises and calcium and phosphate are released. In the kidney, PTH also increases distal tubule calcium reabsorption, reduces proximal tubule phosphate reabsorption, and stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase. In CKD this bone resorption helps hold serum calcium near normal at the cost of the skeleton (osteitis fibrosa cystica, part of renal osteodystrophy).","whyNot":["Correct.","Osteoclasts lack PTH receptors. PTH acts on them only indirectly, through signals from osteoblasts.","Osteoprotegerin blocks RANKL and reduces resorption. PTH lowers osteoprotegerin release.","Calcitonin from thyroid C cells inhibits osteoclasts and lowers calcium. It opposes PTH and is not raised by it."],"study":{"topics":["PTH actions on bone and kidney","RANKL and osteoprotegerin"],"comps":["EN-5","EN-12-A2"]}},{"id":"EN-C31-Q4","stem":"She later undergoes subtotal parathyroidectomy. The surgeon dissects to find both inferior glands on the posterior surface of the lower thyroid poles, close to the tracheoesophageal groove. After surgery her voice is hoarse, and laryngoscopy shows the left vocal fold fixed near the midline. Which of the following structures was most likely injured?","options":["Left ansa cervicalis","Internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve","External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-3","obj":"EN-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Place the glands: four on the posterior thyroid lobes, with the inferior pair near the lower poles where the inferior thyroid artery enters.","The recurrent laryngeal nerve climbs in the tracheoesophageal groove right through this area.","Hoarseness with a fixed vocal fold means loss of the intrinsic laryngeal muscles, which the recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies."],"why":"The superior parathyroid glands sit in a fairly constant spot on the posterior upper thyroid lobes near the cricothyroid junction. The inferior glands are more variable. They usually lie near the lower poles, close to the inferior thyroid artery and the recurrent laryngeal nerve, but they can be found anywhere along their descent path. Because the surgeon must search and dissect in this area, the nerve is at risk. The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies every intrinsic laryngeal muscle except the cricothyroid, so injury leaves the vocal fold fixed and the voice hoarse.","whyNot":["The ansa cervicalis supplies the infrahyoid strap muscles. It has no role in moving the vocal folds.","The internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve carries sensation from the larynx above the vocal folds. Injury blunts the cough reflex but leaves the folds moving.","The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve runs with the superior thyroid artery near the upper pole and supplies the cricothyroid. Injury reduces pitch range but does not fix the vocal fold.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Location of the parathyroid glands","Recurrent laryngeal nerve relations"],"comps":["EN-3","EN-1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C32","system":"EN","dx":"DiGeorge syndrome with neonatal hypocalcemic tetany","codes":["EN-12-B3"],"case":"A 4-day-old boy in the neonatal intensive care unit has repeated episodes of rhythmic jerking of the arms and legs. He was born at term after an uncomplicated pregnancy. Examination shows low-set ears, a short philtrum, and a harsh systolic murmur. Echocardiography shows truncus arteriosus. A chest x-ray shows no thymic shadow. Laboratory studies show serum total calcium 6.2 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 10.1 mg/dL (4.5 to 9.0), magnesium 2.0 mg/dL (1.7 to 2.4), and intact PTH 3 pg/mL (10 to 65). Fluorescence in situ hybridization shows a deletion at chromosome 22q11.2. The diagnosis is DiGeorge syndrome with hypocalcemia from hypoparathyroidism.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C32-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in peripheral blood is most likely in this infant?","options":["Low T cell count with normal B cell count","Normal lymphocyte counts with low serum IgA","Low B cell count with normal T cell count","Low T and B cell counts with absent adenosine deaminase"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-1","obj":"EN-1.5","dok":3,"approach":["Link the findings: hypocalcemia from missing parathyroids, a conotruncal heart defect, facial changes, and no thymic shadow all point to the 22q11.2 deletion.","The thymus and inferior parathyroids come from the third pharyngeal pouch, and the superior parathyroids from the fourth.","With little or no thymus, T cells cannot mature, but B cells develop in the bone marrow and are unaffected."],"why":"In the 22q11.2 deletion (TBX1 is a key gene in the deleted region), the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches and the neural crest cells that migrate into the pharyngeal arches develop poorly. The third pouch forms the thymus and inferior parathyroids, and the fourth forms the superior parathyroids. Loss of parathyroid tissue causes low PTH and hypocalcemic tetany in the first days of life. Thymic hypoplasia leaves T cell precursors from the marrow with nowhere to mature, so T cell counts are low while B cell counts stay normal. Antibody responses that need T cell help may still be weak.","whyNot":["Correct.","Selective IgA deficiency has normal lymphocyte counts. It is not linked to pharyngeal pouch defects.","Low B cells with normal T cells fits X-linked (Bruton) agammaglobulinemia, a BTK defect in B cell maturation. The thymus is normal in that disorder.","Loss of both T and B cells with absent adenosine deaminase is a form of severe combined immunodeficiency. It does not cause hypoparathyroidism or heart defects."],"study":{"topics":["22q11.2 deletion syndrome","Thymus development and T cell maturation"],"comps":["EN-1","EN-12-B3"]}},{"id":"EN-C32-Q2","stem":"Which of the following structures normally develops from the same pharyngeal pouch as the thymus?","options":["Superior parathyroid glands","Inferior parathyroid glands","Parafollicular C cells","Palatine tonsils"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-1","obj":"EN-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the thymus comes from the third pharyngeal pouch.","The third pouch has a dorsal part that becomes the inferior parathyroids and a ventral part that becomes the thymus.","As the thymus descends into the chest, it pulls these glands down with it, so they end up below the glands from the fourth pouch."],"why":"Each pharyngeal pouch is lined by endoderm. The second pouch forms the lining of the palatine tonsil. The third pouch forms the thymus and the inferior parathyroid glands, and the fourth pouch forms the superior parathyroid glands. The ultimobranchial body, near the fourth pouch, is invaded by neural crest cells that become parafollicular C cells. Because third pouch derivatives migrate farther, the inferior parathyroids end up lower than the superior ones and may stop anywhere along the thymic path, including the anterior mediastinum within or near the thymus.","whyNot":["The superior parathyroid glands come from the fourth pouch. Their shorter migration explains their more constant position.","Correct.","Parafollicular C cells come from neural crest cells that enter the ultimobranchial body, near the fourth pouch. They make calcitonin, not PTH.","The palatine tonsils develop from the second pharyngeal pouch."],"study":{"topics":["Pharyngeal pouch derivatives","Ectopic parathyroid glands"],"comps":["EN-1","EN-3"]}},{"id":"EN-C32-Q3","stem":"An electrocardiogram is obtained. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Peaked T waves","Shortened QT interval","Prolonged QT interval","Prominent U waves"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B3","obj":"EN-12-B.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the electrolyte problem: very low calcium, high phosphate, and low PTH.","Low extracellular calcium lengthens the plateau (phase 2) of the ventricular action potential.","A longer plateau lengthens the ST segment, so the QT interval is prolonged."],"why":"Without PTH, there is less bone resorption, less distal tubule calcium reabsorption, less calcitriol, and less phosphate excretion, so calcium falls and phosphate rises. Low ionized calcium makes nerves and muscle more excitable, causing tetany, seizures, perioral numbness, and Chvostek and Trousseau signs. In the heart, low extracellular calcium lengthens the plateau of the ventricular action potential. On the ECG this shows as a long ST segment and a prolonged QT interval, which raises the risk of torsades de pointes.","whyNot":["Peaked T waves are an early sign of hyperkalemia.","A short QT interval is seen with hypercalcemia, the opposite problem.","Correct.","Prominent U waves point to hypokalemia. His abnormality is in calcium."],"study":{"topics":["ECG changes in calcium and potassium disorders","Signs of hypocalcemia"],"comps":["EN-12-B3","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C32-Q4","stem":"In a healthy newborn, a fall in serum ionized calcium increases PTH secretion within minutes. Which of the following mechanisms produces this response?","options":["Reduced calcitonin release from thyroid parafollicular cells","Reduced calcium-sensing receptor activation on chief cells","Increased renal production of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","Opening of voltage-gated calcium channels in the chief cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-5","obj":"EN-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["PTH secretion is controlled minute to minute by ionized calcium itself.","Chief cells carry the calcium-sensing receptor, a G protein-coupled receptor that suppresses PTH release when calcium binds it.","When calcium falls, the receptor is less active, so the brake on PTH secretion lifts. In chief cells, low calcium, not high calcium, triggers secretion."],"why":"The calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid chief cells is a G protein-coupled receptor. When ionized calcium is high, it activates the receptor, which suppresses PTH release and PTH gene expression. When calcium falls, receptor activity drops and stored PTH is released within minutes. PTH then raises calcium by increasing bone resorption, distal tubule calcium reabsorption, and renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity, and it increases phosphate excretion in the proximal tubule. Calcitonin from C cells has the opposite, weaker effect. This infant lacks parathyroid tissue, so the signal has no gland to act on.","whyNot":["Calcitonin from C cells lowers calcium by inhibiting osteoclasts. It does not control PTH release.","Correct.","Calcitriol is made in response to PTH, over hours, and it suppresses PTH gene expression. It is a downstream effect, not the trigger.","In most secretory cells, calcium entry triggers release, but chief cells work the other way: higher calcium suppresses PTH. Calcium entry cannot explain secretion that is triggered by falling calcium."],"study":{"topics":["Calcium-sensing receptor","Calcium homeostasis"],"comps":["EN-5","EN-6"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C33","system":"EN","dx":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a","codes":["EN-12-B3"],"case":"A 9-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of muscle cramps and tingling around her mouth for 2 months. She is at the 3rd percentile for height and above the 95th percentile for weight. Examination shows a round face and short fourth and fifth fingers; when she makes a fist, the fourth and fifth knuckles are not visible. Firm subcutaneous nodules on both forearms are ossified on x-ray. Tapping over the facial nerve causes twitching of the upper lip. Laboratory studies show serum calcium 7.1 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 7.0 mg/dL (4.0 to 5.6), albumin 4.2 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), magnesium 2.0 mg/dL (1.7 to 2.2), and creatinine 0.5 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7). Genetic testing shows an inactivating GNAS mutation on the maternally inherited allele. The diagnosis is pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C33-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional laboratory findings would best distinguish this patient's condition from hypoparathyroidism caused by parathyroid gland failure?","options":["Low serum ionized calcium concentration","Low urinary phosphate excretion","Low serum 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D concentration","Elevated serum PTH concentration"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B3","obj":"EN-12-B.7","dok":2,"approach":["List what both disorders share: low calcium, high phosphate, low calcitriol, and reduced phosphate excretion, because PTH action is missing in both.","The difference is where the problem lies. In gland failure, PTH is low. In pseudohypoparathyroidism, the glands are normal and the target tissues do not respond.","With end-organ resistance, low calcium keeps stimulating the glands, so PTH is high."],"why":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a is end-organ resistance to PTH caused by an inactivating mutation in GNAS, the gene for the Gs alpha subunit. Kidney cells cannot raise cAMP in response to PTH, so the chemistry looks like hypoparathyroidism. The parathyroid glands sense the low calcium and secrete more PTH, so serum PTH is high. In true hypoparathyroidism (surgical, autoimmune, DiGeorge syndrome, or severe hypomagnesemia), PTH is low or inappropriately normal. Albright hereditary osteodystrophy (short stature, round face, obesity, short fourth and fifth metacarpals, subcutaneous ossification) also points to type 1a.","whyNot":["Low ionized calcium is present in both disorders and cannot separate them.","Phosphate excretion is low in both, since PTH-driven phosphate excretion is missing in both.","Calcitriol is low in both, because PTH cannot stimulate renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase in either one.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pseudohypoparathyroidism","Albright hereditary osteodystrophy"],"comps":["EN-12-B3","EN-9"]}},{"id":"EN-C33-Q2","stem":"Testing of other endocrine axes is planned. This patient is most likely to show end-organ resistance to which of the following hormones?","options":["Growth hormone","Insulin","Thyrotropin","Aldosterone"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["The mutated gene, GNAS, encodes the Gs alpha subunit, so hormones that signal through Gs and cAMP are affected.","Sort the options by pathway: TSH uses Gs, insulin uses a receptor tyrosine kinase, growth hormone uses JAK/STAT, and aldosterone uses an intracellular receptor.","Only TSH depends on Gs, so it is the hormone most likely to show resistance."],"why":"Hormones that signal through Gs and cAMP include ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, PTH, and glucagon. In pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a, Gs alpha activity is reduced most in tissues that mainly express the maternal GNAS allele, such as the renal proximal tubule, thyroid, gonads, and pituitary. Children with type 1a therefore often have mild TSH resistance (high TSH with low or normal T4) and may also show resistance to gonadotropins and GHRH. Hormones that use other pathways, such as receptor tyrosine kinases, JAK/STAT, or intracellular receptors, are unaffected.","whyNot":["Growth hormone signals through JAK2 and STAT proteins, not Gs. Short stature in this condition comes from early growth plate closure and GHRH resistance.","Insulin acts through an intrinsic tyrosine kinase receptor and is unaffected by loss of Gs alpha. Her obesity is part of the GNAS phenotype.","Correct.","Aldosterone is a steroid that binds an intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor acting on DNA. It does not use cAMP."],"study":{"topics":["Hormone signaling pathways","Gs protein and cAMP"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-12-B3"]}},{"id":"EN-C33-Q3","stem":"Her serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D concentration is normal, but her 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D concentration is low. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Failed PTH stimulation of renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase","Reduced UVB conversion of 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin","Loss of functioning nephrons from chronic kidney disease","Deficient hepatic 25-hydroxylation of cholecalciferol"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-7","obj":"EN-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["A normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D shows that the skin and liver steps are working.","The block is at the last step, renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation in the proximal tubule.","That enzyme is induced by PTH through Gs and cAMP. With Gs alpha loss in the proximal tubule, the PTH signal fails, and her high phosphate suppresses the enzyme further."],"why":"Vitamin D3 forms in skin when UVB converts 7-dehydrocholesterol, the liver adds a 25-hydroxyl group, and proximal tubule cells add the 1-alpha hydroxyl group to make calcitriol. PTH and low phosphate stimulate 1-alpha-hydroxylase, while high phosphate, FGF23, and calcitriol itself suppress it. In this patient, PTH cannot raise cAMP in the proximal tubule, so the enzyme is not induced despite high PTH. Her normal creatinine shows that nephron mass is intact, so the problem is signaling rather than lost kidney tissue.","whyNot":["Correct.","Poor sun exposure lowers 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the storage form. Her 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal.","CKD lowers calcitriol by destroying proximal tubule mass, but her creatinine is normal for her age.","Liver failure would lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D. A normal level shows that hepatic 25-hydroxylation is working."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation","Regulation of 1-alpha-hydroxylase"],"comps":["EN-7","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C33-Q4","stem":"If a parathyroid gland from this patient were examined microscopically, which of the following findings would be most likely?","options":["Atrophy of chief cells with fatty replacement","Lymphocytic infiltration with loss of chief cells","Oxyphil cell adenoma with a rim of normal gland","Diffuse hyperplasia of chief cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Chief cells are the PTH-secreting cells of the parathyroid. Oxyphil cells are larger, mitochondria-rich cells with no clear secretory role.","Here the glands themselves are normal but are constantly stimulated by low calcium.","Chronic stimulation of an endocrine gland causes hyperplasia of the responding cells, so chief cells increase in number."],"why":"The parathyroid gland has two main cell types. Chief cells are small, numerous cells with central nuclei and pale to clear cytoplasm, and they secrete PTH. Oxyphil cells are larger, with granular eosinophilic cytoplasm packed with mitochondria; they become more common with age, and their role is unclear. In pseudohypoparathyroidism, low calcium stimulates the glands continuously, as in secondary hyperparathyroidism from vitamin D deficiency or CKD. The result is diffuse chief cell hyperplasia with high PTH output.","whyNot":["Atrophy follows long suppression of PTH, as with chronic high calcium. Her glands are being stimulated, not suppressed.","Lymphocytic destruction of chief cells is the picture of autoimmune hypoparathyroidism, which causes low PTH.","An adenoma is a clonal tumor that causes primary hyperparathyroidism with high calcium. Her calcium is low.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Parathyroid histology","Parathyroid hyperplasia"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-12-B3"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C34","system":"EN","dx":"Hypocalcemia from hypomagnesemia in alcohol use disorder","codes":["EN-12-B3"],"case":"A 52-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is brought to the emergency department because of muscle cramps and painful spasms of his hands for 2 days. He drinks about 12 beers daily and has had chronic diarrhea with greasy stools for 1 year. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 128/82 mm Hg. Tapping anterior to the ear causes twitching of the upper lip. Laboratory studies show serum calcium 6.9 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), albumin 3.8 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), magnesium 0.7 mg/dL (1.7 to 2.2), phosphate 2.9 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), and intact PTH 14 pg/mL (10 to 65). CT of the abdomen shows pancreatic calcifications. He receives intravenous calcium gluconate, but his calcium falls again within hours. The diagnosis is hypocalcemia due to hypomagnesemia.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C34-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why intravenous calcium alone did not correct this patient's hypocalcemia?","options":["Impaired PTH release caused by magnesium deficiency","Excess renal calcium loss driven by high PTH","Calcium deposition in the calcified pancreas","Calcitonin release triggered by the calcium infusion"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-7","obj":"EN-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the mismatch: severe hypocalcemia with a PTH in the low-normal range. Healthy glands would push PTH far above the range.","The other striking value is a very low magnesium, from poor intake, alcohol-induced urinary loss, and malabsorption.","Severe magnesium deficiency blunts PTH release and makes bone and kidney resistant to PTH, so calcium will not hold until magnesium is replaced."],"why":"Magnesium is needed both for normal PTH secretion and for PTH action on its targets. When magnesium is very low, chief cells release too little PTH despite hypocalcemia, and kidney and bone respond poorly to the PTH that is present. Intravenous calcium raises serum calcium briefly, but without an adequate PTH response the level falls again. Once magnesium is replaced, PTH secretion rises quickly and end-organ response recovers over several days, so calcium corrects. Alcohol use disorder causes hypomagnesemia through poor intake, increased urinary loss, diarrhea, and fat malabsorption.","whyNot":["Correct.","His PTH is low-normal, not high, and PTH actually reduces urinary calcium loss.","Chronic calcific pancreatitis does not take up large amounts of calcium from the blood. Calcium binding by fat necrosis lowers calcium mainly in acute pancreatitis.","Calcitonin has only a small, short-lived effect on serum calcium in adults and cannot explain persistent hypocalcemia."],"study":{"topics":["Magnesium and PTH","Causes of hypocalcemia"],"comps":["EN-7","EN-12-B3"]}},{"id":"EN-C34-Q2","stem":"When a blood pressure cuff on his arm is inflated above systolic pressure for 3 minutes, his wrist and fingers flex and his thumb adducts. Which of the following best explains this response?","options":["Decreased acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction","Increased calcium release from the muscle sarcoplasmic reticulum","Reduced sodium channel activity in motor neuron membranes","Lower depolarization threshold of peripheral nerve axons"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-B3","obj":"EN-12-B.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize Trousseau sign: carpal spasm when arm ischemia is added to low calcium.","Extracellular calcium normally stabilizes nerve membranes by making voltage-gated sodium channels harder to open.","When ionized calcium falls, sodium channels open closer to the resting potential, so axons fire on their own and the muscles contract."],"why":"Low ionized calcium raises nerve excitability. Calcium ions at the outer surface of the membrane normally make it harder for voltage-gated sodium channels to open. With less calcium, the firing threshold moves closer to the resting potential, so peripheral nerves fire spontaneously or with very little stimulus. This causes paresthesias, perioral numbness, Chvostek sign (facial twitching when the facial nerve is tapped), and Trousseau sign (carpal spasm with cuff ischemia). Hypoparathyroidism from any cause, including severe hypomagnesemia, produces these signs along with QT prolongation.","whyNot":["Low calcium does reduce acetylcholine release at the junction, but that would weaken contraction rather than cause spasm.","Sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium release is triggered by the muscle action potential. Low serum calcium does not increase it directly.","Low calcium makes sodium channels easier to open, which increases activity. Reduced sodium channel activity would lower excitability.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Trousseau and Chvostek signs","Calcium and nerve excitability"],"comps":["EN-12-B3","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C34-Q3","stem":"Severe magnesium deficiency also makes renal tubule cells resistant to PTH. Binding of PTH to its renal receptor normally triggers which of the following signaling steps?","options":["Phosphorylation of STAT proteins by JAK kinases","Gs activation of adenylyl cyclase to form cAMP","Binding of the hormone-receptor complex to DNA","Autophosphorylation of receptor tyrosine residues"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["PTH is a peptide hormone, so it acts at a cell-surface receptor.","Its receptor, PTH1R, is a G protein-coupled receptor that signals mainly through Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, and protein kinase A.","Adenylyl cyclase uses magnesium-bound ATP, and magnesium is also needed for G protein function, which helps explain why low magnesium blunts PTH action."],"why":"Hormones that work through Gs and cAMP include PTH, ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, and glucagon. When PTH binds PTH1R on renal tubule cells, Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, cAMP rises, and protein kinase A changes transporter activity. Phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule falls, calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule rises, and 1-alpha-hydroxylase is induced. Magnesium is a cofactor for adenylyl cyclase, so severe deficiency weakens this response.","whyNot":["JAK/STAT signaling is used by growth hormone and prolactin.","Correct.","Steroid and thyroid hormones bind intracellular receptors that act on DNA. PTH is a peptide and does not cross the membrane.","Receptor tyrosine kinase autophosphorylation is how insulin and IGF-1 signal."],"study":{"topics":["Hormone signaling pathways","PTH receptor signaling"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-5"]}},{"id":"EN-C34-Q4","stem":"Five years later, he develops diabetes mellitus from progressive chronic pancreatitis. After starting insulin, he has frequent episodes of severe hypoglycemia. Loss of which of the following islet cell types, along with beta cells, best explains this tendency?","options":["PP cells","Alpha cells","Delta cells","Epsilon cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Diabetes from chronic pancreatitis comes from destruction of whole islets, not only beta cells.","The islet hormone that protects against hypoglycemia is glucagon, which drives hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.","Glucagon comes from alpha cells, so losing them removes the main fast defense against insulin-induced hypoglycemia."],"why":"Islets contain beta cells (insulin, the majority), alpha cells (glucagon), delta cells (somatostatin), and small numbers of PP and epsilon cells. In type 1 diabetes, the immune attack targets beta cells and alpha cells survive. In chronic pancreatitis, fibrosis destroys islets as a whole, so alpha cells are lost as well. Without glucagon, the liver responds poorly when glucose falls, so insulin therapy more easily causes severe hypoglycemia. Ongoing alcohol use and poor food intake add to the risk.","whyNot":["PP cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide, which affects pancreatic exocrine secretion and appetite. It has no major role in raising glucose.","Correct.","Delta cells secrete somatostatin, which inhibits both insulin and glucagon release. Losing them would not weaken the defense against hypoglycemia.","Epsilon cells secrete ghrelin and are very few in adult islets. Their loss does not explain severe hypoglycemia."],"study":{"topics":["Islet cell types and hormones","Diabetes from pancreatic disease"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-12-D1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C35","system":"EN","dx":"Diabetic ketoacidosis in new-onset type 1 diabetes","codes":["EN-12-D1"],"case":"A 14-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of vomiting and abdominal pain for 1 day. Over the past 3 weeks, she has had increased thirst, frequent urination, and a 4-kg weight loss. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 122/min, respirations are 30/min and deep, and blood pressure is 98/62 mm Hg. Her mucous membranes are dry, and her breath has a fruity odor. Laboratory studies show glucose 512 mg/dL (70 to 99), Na+ 131 mEq/L (135 to 145), K+ 5.9 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), Cl- 95 mEq/L (98 to 106), HCO3- 8 mEq/L (22 to 28), and beta-hydroxybutyrate 6.4 mmol/L (less than 0.6). Arterial pH is 7.11 (7.35 to 7.45). The diagnosis is diabetic ketoacidosis from new-onset type 1 diabetes.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C35-Q1","stem":"Her total body potassium is most likely depleted. Which of the following best explains her elevated serum potassium concentration?","options":["Hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism from kidney damage","Potassium release from hemolysis of red blood cells","Shift of potassium out of cells from insulin deficiency","Reduced renal potassium excretion from volume depletion"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-D1","obj":"EN-12-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the serum level from body stores: vomiting and osmotic diuresis have drained her potassium, yet the serum value is high.","Insulin normally drives potassium into cells by stimulating the Na+/K+-ATPase. Without insulin, potassium stays outside cells.","High plasma osmolality also pulls water out of cells, and potassium follows. Once insulin is given, serum potassium will fall quickly."],"why":"In DKA, total body potassium is low because of urinary loss from osmotic diuresis and secondary hyperaldosteronism, plus vomiting. Serum potassium is often normal or high because insulin deficiency removes the main stimulus for cellular potassium uptake through the Na+/K+-ATPase, and hyperosmolality moves water and potassium out of cells. Acidosis may add to the shift. The anion gap here is 28 mEq/L (8 to 12) from keto acids. Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state differs: some insulin remains, ketosis is minimal, glucose and osmolality are usually much higher, and altered mental status dominates.","whyNot":["Hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism occurs with long-standing diabetic kidney disease in older adults. She has new-onset diabetes and is volume depleted, which raises renin and aldosterone.","Hemolysis in a damaged sample can falsely raise potassium, but nothing here suggests it, and the high value is expected in DKA.","Correct.","Osmotic diuresis increases urinary potassium loss in DKA, which is why total body potassium falls. The kidneys are not retaining potassium here."],"study":{"topics":["Potassium in diabetic ketoacidosis","DKA versus hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state"],"comps":["EN-12-D1","EN-7"]}},{"id":"EN-C35-Q2","stem":"Which of the following hepatic changes most directly allows the fatty acids released from her adipose tissue to be converted into the ketone bodies measured in her serum?","options":["Relief of carnitine palmitoyltransferase I inhibition","Increased oxaloacetate drawing acetyl-CoA into the citric acid cycle","Increased hepatic malonyl-CoA stimulating fatty acid synthesis","Inhibition of hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-7","obj":"EN-7.2","dok":3,"approach":["Without insulin and with high glucagon, adipose lipolysis floods the liver with fatty acids.","To be oxidized, long-chain fatty acids must enter mitochondria through carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT-1), which malonyl-CoA normally inhibits.","Glucagon inactivates acetyl-CoA carboxylase, so malonyl-CoA falls and CPT-1 opens. Beta-oxidation then makes more acetyl-CoA than the citric acid cycle can use, and the excess becomes ketone bodies."],"why":"Insulin deficiency with glucagon excess activates hormone-sensitive lipase and releases free fatty acids. In the liver, glucagon lowers malonyl-CoA by inhibiting acetyl-CoA carboxylase, which removes the brake on CPT-1 and speeds fatty acid entry into mitochondria. Beta-oxidation produces large amounts of acetyl-CoA, while oxaloacetate is diverted into gluconeogenesis. The excess acetyl-CoA is converted through HMG-CoA to acetoacetate, which is reduced to beta-hydroxybutyrate. These keto acids consume bicarbonate and raise the anion gap.","whyNot":["Correct.","Oxaloacetate is drawn off for gluconeogenesis in DKA. Low oxaloacetate is one reason acetyl-CoA is diverted into ketone bodies.","Malonyl-CoA rises in the fed, insulin-rich state and blocks fatty acid oxidation. In DKA it falls.","Hormone-sensitive lipase is activated, not inhibited, when insulin is absent. Its activity supplies the fatty acids for ketogenesis."],"study":{"topics":["Ketone body synthesis","Regulation of fatty acid oxidation"],"comps":["EN-7","EN-12-D1"]}},{"id":"EN-C35-Q3","stem":"Serum antibodies against glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD65) and insulin are present. Which of the following is mainly responsible for the loss of her beta cells?","options":["Complement lysis directed by GAD65 antibodies","Amyloid deposition derived from islet amylin","Cytotoxic CD8+ T cell killing of beta cells","Fibrosis of islets from recurrent pancreatitis"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"EN-10","obj":"EN-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["Antibodies to GAD65, insulin, IA-2, and ZnT8 confirm autoimmune diabetes, but they mark the disease rather than cause it.","GAD65 and the other target antigens are inside the cell, out of reach of antibodies in living cells.","The damage is done by T cells. Islets show insulitis, and CD8+ cytotoxic T cells kill beta cells with help from CD4+ T cells and macrophages."],"why":"Type 1 diabetes is a T cell-mediated autoimmune disease linked to HLA-DR3 and DR4. Autoreactive CD4+ T cells recognize beta cell antigens and help CD8+ T cells, which kill beta cells directly through perforin, granzymes, and Fas ligand. The islets show a lymphocytic infiltrate called insulitis. Autoantibodies to GAD65, insulin, IA-2, and ZnT8 appear months to years before symptoms and are used to confirm the diagnosis and estimate risk. Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults (LADA, sometimes called type 1.5) has the same antibodies but a slower course that can be mistaken for type 2 diabetes.","whyNot":["GAD65 is an intracellular enzyme, so antibodies to it are markers of the immune attack rather than its cause.","Islet amyloid derived from amylin is a feature of type 2 diabetes.","Correct.","Islet loss from fibrosis occurs in chronic pancreatitis, usually in adults with repeated pancreatic injury such as from alcohol."],"study":{"topics":["Immunopathogenesis of type 1 diabetes","Diabetes autoantibodies and LADA"],"comps":["EN-10","EN-12-D1"]}},{"id":"EN-C35-Q4","stem":"Compared with an adult who has type 2 diabetes and similar hyperglycemia, which of the following best explains why this patient developed ketoacidosis?","options":["Peripheral insulin resistance in skeletal muscle","Amyloid deposition within pancreatic islets","Excess hepatic extraction of portal insulin","Lack of residual insulin to restrain lipolysis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-D1","obj":"EN-12-D.1","dok":3,"approach":["Small amounts of insulin strongly suppress adipose lipolysis and hepatic ketogenesis, so ketoacidosis needs a very low insulin level.","In type 2 diabetes, beta cells still release insulin, often in large amounts, so ketosis is usually prevented even when glucose is high.","In type 1 diabetes, autoimmune destruction leaves almost no insulin, so lipolysis and ketogenesis run unchecked."],"why":"Type 1 diabetes is autoimmune beta cell loss that leads to absolute insulin deficiency. It usually starts in childhood or young adulthood and is prone to ketoacidosis. Type 2 diabetes combines insulin resistance with progressive beta cell dysfunction and islet amyloid, and it is linked to obesity and family history. Adipose lipolysis is suppressed by much lower insulin levels than are needed to drive glucose uptake into muscle, so the residual insulin in type 2 diabetes usually holds ketone production down. Severe decompensation in type 2 diabetes more often takes the form of hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state.","whyNot":["Insulin resistance in muscle raises glucose but does not drive ketosis, because the insulin that is present still restrains lipolysis. It is the main defect in type 2 diabetes.","Islet amyloid is a feature of type 2 diabetes, which is less prone to ketoacidosis.","The liver normally removes a large share of portal insulin on its first pass in everyone. This does not set type 1 diabetes apart.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Type 1 versus type 2 diabetes","Why ketoacidosis occurs in type 1 diabetes"],"comps":["EN-12-D1","EN-7"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C36","system":"EN","dx":"Type 2 diabetes with metabolic syndrome","codes":["EN-12-D1"],"case":"A 47-year-old man comes to the physician for a health maintenance examination. He has no symptoms. His father and his identical twin brother have type 2 diabetes. He is 178 cm tall and weighs 112 kg; BMI is 35 kg/m2, and waist circumference is 118 cm. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 78/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 142/90 mm Hg. Examination shows hyperpigmented, velvety plaques on the back of the neck and in both axillae. Fasting laboratory studies show glucose 141 mg/dL (70 to 99), hemoglobin A1c 7.2% (4.0 to 5.6), triglycerides 262 mg/dL (less than 150), and HDL cholesterol 33 mg/dL (40 or greater). A repeat fasting glucose 2 weeks later is 137 mg/dL. The diagnosis is type 2 diabetes mellitus with metabolic syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C36-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the skin lesions on his neck and axillae?","options":["Insulin acting on keratinocyte IGF-1 receptors","Superficial Malassezia infection of the stratum corneum","Deposition of advanced glycation end products in dermis","Excess ACTH stimulating melanocortin receptors on melanocytes"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-D1","obj":"EN-12-D.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion: velvety, hyperpigmented plaques in skin folds are acanthosis nigricans.","In this setting it signals insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia.","At high levels, insulin also binds IGF-1 receptors on keratinocytes and dermal fibroblasts and drives their growth, thickening the skin."],"why":"Acanthosis nigricans is thickened, papillomatous, hyperkeratotic skin in flexural areas such as the neck and axillae. The dark color comes mainly from the thickened keratin layer rather than extra melanin. Insulin resistance makes beta cells secrete more insulin, and at high levels insulin stimulates IGF-1 receptors in the skin. It is a skin sign of the same insulin resistance that drives metabolic syndrome. Any three of five criteria define the syndrome: a large waist (over 102 cm in men), triglycerides of 150 mg/dL or more, HDL below 40 mg/dL in men, blood pressure of 130/85 mm Hg or more, and fasting glucose of 100 mg/dL or more. This man meets all five.","whyNot":["Correct.","Malassezia causes tinea versicolor, with flat, finely scaling patches of altered color on the trunk. The skin is not thick or velvety.","Glycation end products stiffen collagen and vessel walls in long-standing diabetes. They do not cause thickened pigmented plaques in skin folds.","Excess ACTH darkens skin diffusely, especially palmar creases and mucosa, as in Addison disease. It does not cause thick velvety plaques."],"study":{"topics":["Acanthosis nigricans","Metabolic syndrome criteria"],"comps":["EN-12-D1","EN-7"]}},{"id":"EN-C36-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the pattern of appetite-regulating hormones most likely contributing to his obesity?","options":["Increased leptin signaling at arcuate neurons","High leptin with reduced hypothalamic response","Low leptin from deficient adipocyte secretion","High fasting ghrelin secreted by the gastric fundus"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-7","obj":"EN-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Adipocytes release leptin in proportion to fat mass, so a man with a BMI of 35 has a high leptin level.","If leptin worked normally, that level would suppress appetite through the arcuate nucleus.","Continued weight gain despite high leptin means the hypothalamus is resistant to it."],"why":"Leptin from adipocytes acts on the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus. It stimulates POMC neurons, which reduce appetite, and inhibits NPY/AgRP neurons, which increase appetite. Ghrelin from the gastric fundus does the reverse: it rises before meals and stimulates NPY/AgRP neurons. In common obesity, leptin levels are high but the hypothalamic response is blunted, which is called leptin resistance, and fasting ghrelin is usually lower than normal. Visceral fat releases free fatty acids and inflammatory cytokines that add to insulin resistance.","whyNot":["Increased leptin signaling would reduce appetite and favor weight loss. In common obesity, signaling is reduced despite high leptin.","Correct.","Low leptin from a leptin gene mutation causes severe early-onset obesity, but it is rare. Most obese adults have high leptin.","Fasting ghrelin is usually lower than normal in people with obesity."],"study":{"topics":["Leptin and ghrelin","Hypothalamic control of appetite"],"comps":["EN-7","EN-12-D1"]}},{"id":"EN-C36-Q3","stem":"Compared with type 1 diabetes, which of the following best describes the inheritance of this patient's form of diabetes?","options":["Lower monozygotic twin concordance, with HLA-DR3 and DR4 linkage","Autosomal dominant inheritance through a single glucokinase gene","Maternal mitochondrial inheritance of a single tRNA mutation","Higher monozygotic twin concordance, with polygenic inheritance"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-9","obj":"EN-9.3","dok":2,"approach":["Use the family history: his father and identical twin are both affected, which suggests a strong inherited component.","Type 2 diabetes is polygenic, and identical twins show much higher concordance than in type 1 diabetes.","Type 1 diabetes has the HLA-DR3 and DR4 links but lower twin concordance, because environmental triggers play a larger role."],"why":"Board review sources teach that concordance in identical twins is higher for type 2 diabetes than for type 1 diabetes, even though type 1 has the stronger single-region (HLA) association. Type 2 risk comes from many genes, each with a small effect on insulin secretion or action, combined with obesity and inactivity. Type 1 risk is concentrated in the HLA region (DR3 and DR4), but many people with these alleles never develop the disease. Maturity-onset diabetes of the young is autosomal dominant, caused by single-gene mutations such as glucokinase or HNF1A, and usually starts before age 25 in lean people.","whyNot":["Lower twin concordance with HLA-DR3 and DR4 linkage describes type 1 diabetes. Type 2 diabetes shows higher concordance and no strong HLA link.","Single-gene autosomal dominant inheritance through glucokinase or HNF1A is maturity-onset diabetes of the young. It usually appears in lean young people, not in an obese 47-year-old with metabolic syndrome.","Mitochondrial diabetes is rare, passes only through the mother, and is often paired with deafness. His father is affected, which does not fit maternal inheritance.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Genetics of diabetes types","Maturity-onset diabetes of the young"],"comps":["EN-9","EN-12-D1"]}},{"id":"EN-C36-Q4","stem":"In a healthy person, an oral glucose load releases more insulin than an intravenous glucose infusion that produces the same plasma glucose concentrations. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["Ghrelin release from the stomach stimulating beta cells","GLP-1 and GIP release from intestinal endocrine cells","Glucose entry into beta cells through SGLT1","Somatostatin release from gastric D cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-5","obj":"EN-5.5","dok":2,"approach":["The plasma glucose is matched, so the extra insulin must come from a signal created by glucose passing through the gut.","Glucose in the intestinal lumen stimulates K cells to release GIP and L cells to release GLP-1.","These incretins act on beta cell receptors that raise cAMP, which amplifies glucose-stimulated insulin release."],"why":"Beta cells take up glucose through GLUT2, and glucokinase phosphorylates it. Glucose metabolism raises the ATP to ADP ratio, which closes ATP-sensitive potassium channels, depolarizes the cell, opens voltage-gated calcium channels, and triggers insulin exocytosis. Incretins amplify this signal: GIP from duodenal and jejunal K cells and GLP-1 from ileal and colonic L cells act through Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells. This incretin effect accounts for a large share of insulin release after a meal, and it is reduced in type 2 diabetes.","whyNot":["Ghrelin rises before meals and tends to inhibit insulin secretion.","Correct.","Beta cells take up glucose through GLUT2, not SGLT1. SGLT1 absorbs glucose in the small intestine.","Somatostatin inhibits insulin release, so it would reduce the response."],"study":{"topics":["Insulin secretion in the beta cell","Incretin effect"],"comps":["EN-5","EN-12-D1"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C37","system":"EN","dx":"Diabetic nephropathy and peripheral neuropathy in longstanding type 2 diabetes","codes":["EN-12-D1"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman comes to the physician because of burning pain and numbness in both feet for 1 year and swelling of her ankles for 2 months. She has had type 2 diabetes for 18 years. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 74/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 148/88 mm Hg. Examination shows pitting edema of both ankles. Sensation to pinprick and vibration is decreased in both feet in a stocking distribution, and ankle reflexes are absent. Funduscopy shows microaneurysms and dot-blot hemorrhages. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin A1c 8.4% (4.0 to 5.6), creatinine 1.6 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1), and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 640 mg/g (less than 30). The diagnosis is diabetic nephropathy with distal symmetric polyneuropathy.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C37-Q1","stem":"A kidney biopsy is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Subepithelial immune deposits with basement membrane spikes","Crescents of proliferating parietal epithelial cells","Nodular mesangial sclerosis with basement membrane thickening","Isolated podocyte foot process effacement on electron microscopy"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-D1","obj":"EN-12-D.5","dok":2,"approach":["Link the findings: long-standing diabetes, retinopathy, and heavy albuminuria point to diabetic nephropathy.","The earliest change is thickening of the glomerular basement membrane, followed by expansion of mesangial matrix.","Advanced disease shows nodular glomerulosclerosis (Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules) and hyaline arteriolosclerosis of both afferent and efferent arterioles."],"why":"Chronic hyperglycemia glycates basement membrane and mesangial proteins and drives growth factors such as TGF-beta. The glomerular basement membrane thickens, the mesangial matrix expands, and in many patients round, PAS-positive Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules form. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis affects both afferent and efferent arterioles. Albuminuria appears early and can progress to nephrotic-range proteinuria with falling GFR. The same microvascular disease causes retinopathy, and diabetes also causes autonomic neuropathy with gastroparesis and peripheral vascular disease with foot ulcers.","whyNot":["Subepithelial deposits with spikes are the picture of membranous nephropathy, often from anti-PLA2R antibodies. Diabetes does not cause immune deposits.","Crescents mark rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, with kidney function falling over days to weeks.","Correct.","Foot process effacement with otherwise normal glomeruli is minimal change disease. Diabetic kidneys show obvious changes on light microscopy."],"study":{"topics":["Diabetic nephropathy pathology","Chronic complications of diabetes"],"comps":["EN-12-D1","EN-7"]}},{"id":"EN-C37-Q2","stem":"Her neuropathy is partly caused by accumulation of an osmotically active sugar alcohol in Schwann cells. Which of the following enzymes forms this metabolite?","options":["Aldose reductase","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase","Hexokinase","Sorbitol dehydrogenase"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-12-D1","obj":"EN-12-D.4","dok":2,"approach":["Schwann cells take up glucose without needing insulin, so their glucose rises with blood glucose.","Extra glucose enters the polyol pathway, where aldose reductase reduces glucose to sorbitol using NADPH.","Sorbitol crosses membranes poorly, and these cells have little sorbitol dehydrogenase, so sorbitol builds up and draws in water."],"why":"The polyol pathway has two steps. Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol using NADPH, and sorbitol dehydrogenase converts sorbitol to fructose. Schwann cells, the lens, the retina, and the kidney have aldose reductase but little sorbitol dehydrogenase, so sorbitol accumulates during hyperglycemia. Sorbitol causes osmotic injury, and NADPH use reduces regeneration of glutathione, which increases oxidative stress. Together with nonenzymatic glycation (advanced glycation end products), this helps explain neuropathy, cataracts, and vascular damage in diabetes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase makes NADPH in the pentose phosphate pathway. It does not make sorbitol.","Hexokinase phosphorylates glucose to start glycolysis. Its product is not the sugar alcohol of the polyol pathway.","Sorbitol dehydrogenase removes sorbitol by converting it to fructose. Low activity of this enzyme in nerve and lens is why sorbitol builds up."],"study":{"topics":["Polyol pathway","Mechanisms of diabetic neuropathy"],"comps":["EN-12-D1","EN-7"]}},{"id":"EN-C37-Q3","stem":"Six weeks after this visit, she receives 3 units of packed red blood cells for an acute gastrointestinal hemorrhage. Her daily glucose readings do not change. Two weeks later, hemoglobin A1c is measured again. Compared with its value without the transfusion, which of the following is most likely?","options":["Falsely high, since stored donor cells are heavily glycated","Unchanged, since HbA1c reflects only the most recent 2 weeks","Falsely high, since bleeding leaves mainly older red cells behind","Falsely low, since donor cells were exposed to less glucose"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-12-D1","obj":"EN-12-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["HbA1c forms when glucose attaches to hemoglobin without an enzyme, at a rate set by the glucose level the red cell is exposed to.","Red cells live about 120 days, so HbA1c reflects about the past 3 months, weighted toward recent weeks.","Donor cells from a person with normal glucose carry little HbA1c, and new red cells made after bleeding have had little time to glycate, so her HbA1c will read low."],"why":"Glucose binds the N-terminal valine of the hemoglobin beta chain nonenzymatically and rearranges into stable HbA1c. Because this depends on glucose level and time, HbA1c reflects average glucose over the red cell lifespan, about 3 months. Anything that adds young or donor red cells, such as transfusion, hemolysis, or recovery from blood loss, lowers HbA1c without any change in glycemia. Diabetes is diagnosed by HbA1c of 6.5% or more, fasting glucose of 126 mg/dL or more, 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test glucose of 200 mg/dL or more, or random glucose of 200 mg/dL or more with symptoms. When red cell lifespan is altered, glucose-based tests are more reliable.","whyNot":["Stored donor blood carries the donor's own HbA1c, which is usually normal. It does not raise her value.","HbA1c reflects about 3 months of glucose, not 2 weeks. Fructosamine is the marker that covers about 2 to 3 weeks.","Blood loss removes red cells of all ages, and the marrow then releases young cells. This lowers HbA1c.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["HbA1c and red cell lifespan","Diagnostic criteria for diabetes"],"comps":["EN-12-D1","EN-7"]}},{"id":"EN-C37-Q4","stem":"Islets are most densely concentrated in the body and tail of the pancreas. From which of the following embryonic structures does this part of the pancreas develop?","options":["Neural crest cells of the vagal region","Splanchnic mesoderm of the dorsal mesentery","Dorsal pancreatic bud of foregut endoderm","Ventral pancreatic bud of foregut endoderm"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-1","obj":"EN-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["The pancreas forms from two endodermal outgrowths of the foregut at the level of the duodenum: a dorsal bud and a ventral bud.","The ventral bud rotates with the bile duct and fuses below and behind the dorsal bud, forming the uncinate process and lower head.","The dorsal bud forms the upper head, neck, body, and tail, so the islet-rich tail comes from the dorsal bud."],"why":"Both the exocrine acini and the endocrine islets come from foregut endoderm. Endocrine progenitor cells arise from the developing duct epithelium and cluster into islets. The ventral bud gives rise to the uncinate process and lower part of the head, and the dorsal bud forms the rest of the gland. Islets are found throughout the pancreas but are most dense in the body and tail, which come from the dorsal bud.","whyNot":["Neural crest gives rise to the adrenal medulla, C cells, and enteric neurons. Islet cells are endodermal.","Splanchnic mesoderm forms the connective tissue and vessels of the pancreas, not its epithelial islet cells.","Correct.","The ventral bud forms the uncinate process and lower part of the head. It does not form the body or tail."],"study":{"topics":["Pancreas development","Origin of islet cells"],"comps":["EN-1","EN-2"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C38","system":"EN","dx":"Insulinoma in MEN1","codes":["EN-12-G2","EN-12-G6"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the physician because of episodes of sweating, palpitations, and confusion over the past 4 months. They occur before breakfast or after exercise and resolve when she eats. She has gained 6 kg during this time. Her brother had a parathyroid tumor removed at age 30. During a supervised fast, she becomes confused after 14 hours, and her plasma glucose is 38 mg/dL (70 to 99). At that time, serum insulin is 22 µU/mL (expected less than 3 during hypoglycemia), C-peptide is 2.9 ng/mL (expected less than 0.6 during hypoglycemia), and a sulfonylurea screen is negative. Her symptoms resolve after intravenous dextrose. CT shows a 1.5-cm mass in the pancreatic head. Genetic testing shows a germline MEN1 mutation. The diagnosis is insulinoma in multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C38-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional findings is most likely at the time of her hypoglycemia during the fast?","options":["High serum free fatty acids","Undetectable serum proinsulin","Low serum beta-hydroxybutyrate","Positive urine ketones on dipstick"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G2","obj":"EN-12-G.3","dok":3,"approach":["During a normal fast, insulin falls, which releases fatty acids from fat and lets the liver make ketones.","Here insulin stays high despite low glucose, so it keeps blocking lipolysis and ketogenesis.","The result is hypoglycemia with low beta-hydroxybutyrate and low free fatty acids, which points to insulin as the cause."],"why":"An insulinoma is a beta cell tumor that secretes insulin regardless of glucose. Whipple triad is symptoms of hypoglycemia, a documented low glucose, and relief when glucose is raised. During a supervised fast, the diagnostic pattern is low glucose with inappropriately high insulin, C-peptide, and proinsulin. Because insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase and hepatic fatty acid oxidation, beta-hydroxybutyrate stays low. In fasting hypoglycemia that is not driven by insulin, such as prolonged starvation, ketones are high.","whyNot":["Free fatty acids rise when insulin falls during a normal fast. High insulin keeps lipolysis switched off, so they stay low.","Insulinomas usually release extra proinsulin because tumor cells process it incompletely, so proinsulin is high.","Correct.","Urine ketones appear when insulin is low and fat is being burned. Her insulin is high, so ketogenesis is suppressed."],"study":{"topics":["Whipple triad","Supervised fast in hypoglycemia"],"comps":["EN-12-G2","EN-7"]}},{"id":"EN-C38-Q2","stem":"Before her diagnosis, several causes of fasting hypoglycemia were considered. Which of the following causes would have shown low serum insulin and low C-peptide concentrations at the time of hypoglycemia?","options":["Meglitinide ingestion","Acute liver failure","Sulfonylurea ingestion","Surreptitious insulin injection"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-12-G2","obj":"EN-12-G.4","dok":3,"approach":["Sort the causes by whether insulin drives the hypoglycemia.","Sulfonylureas and meglitinides make beta cells release insulin, so insulin and C-peptide are both high. Injected insulin raises insulin but suppresses C-peptide.","Liver failure causes hypoglycemia because the liver cannot make glucose, so the beta cells respond normally and shut off insulin."],"why":"During fasting, the liver keeps glucose normal first through glycogenolysis and then through gluconeogenesis. In acute liver failure both are impaired, so glucose falls even though insulin and C-peptide are appropriately suppressed. Sepsis also causes hypoglycemia without excess insulin, by increasing glucose use and impairing hepatic glucose output. Insulinoma and insulin secretagogues raise both insulin and C-peptide, while injected insulin raises insulin with low C-peptide.","whyNot":["Meglitinides also close K-ATP channels and give the same high insulin and C-peptide pattern as sulfonylureas.","Correct.","Sulfonylureas close beta cell K-ATP channels and release endogenous insulin, so insulin and C-peptide are both high. A drug screen separates this from insulinoma.","Injected insulin raises serum insulin but suppresses the patient's own beta cells, so C-peptide is low while insulin is high."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of hypoglycemia","Hepatic glucose production"],"comps":["EN-12-G2","EN-7"]}},{"id":"EN-C38-Q3","stem":"Screening for other tumors associated with her genetic syndrome is planned. Which of the following findings is most likely to be present?","options":["Elevated serum calcium concentration","Elevated serum alpha-fetoprotein concentration","Elevated plasma metanephrine concentration","Elevated serum calcitonin concentration"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G6","obj":"EN-12-G.15","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the syndrome: insulinoma, a brother with a parathyroid tumor, and a germline MEN1 mutation.","MEN1 tumors are the 3 Ps: parathyroid, pituitary, and pancreatic endocrine tumors.","Primary hyperparathyroidism is the most common and usually earliest feature, so hypercalcemia is the most likely finding."],"why":"MEN1 is autosomal dominant and caused by loss of the tumor suppressor menin. Parathyroid hyperplasia or adenomas develop in nearly all carriers and cause hypercalcemia with high PTH. Pancreatic and duodenal neuroendocrine tumors (gastrinoma, insulinoma, and others) and pituitary adenomas (often prolactinoma) are the other main tumors. MEN2A (RET mutation) combines medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and parathyroid hyperplasia. MEN2B has medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas, and a marfanoid habitus, without parathyroid disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Alpha-fetoprotein is a marker for hepatocellular carcinoma and yolk sac tumors. It is not part of MEN1 screening.","High metanephrines point to pheochromocytoma, which belongs to MEN2.","High calcitonin signals medullary thyroid carcinoma, a feature of MEN2A and MEN2B, which are caused by RET mutations."],"study":{"topics":["Multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes","Primary hyperparathyroidism"],"comps":["EN-12-G6","EN-12-A2"]}},{"id":"EN-C38-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her serum C-peptide concentration helps exclude injected insulin as the cause of her hypoglycemia?","options":["C-peptide is cleared mainly by the liver on its first pass","C-peptide stays bound to insulin by disulfide bonds","C-peptide is cut from proinsulin in the rough endoplasmic reticulum","C-peptide is released with insulin in equal molar amounts"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-8","obj":"EN-8.4","dok":2,"approach":["Follow insulin synthesis: preproinsulin loses its signal peptide in the rough ER to become proinsulin.","In secretory granules, prohormone convertases cut out C-peptide, leaving the A and B chains joined by disulfide bonds.","Insulin and C-peptide are released together in equal molar amounts. Injected insulin contains no C-peptide and suppresses her own beta cells, so a high C-peptide means the insulin is her own."],"why":"Preproinsulin is made on ribosomes of the rough ER, where its signal peptide is removed to form proinsulin. Proinsulin folds, forms disulfide bonds, and moves through the Golgi into secretory granules, where prohormone convertases remove the connecting C-peptide. Each insulin molecule released is matched by one C-peptide molecule. Pharmaceutical insulin has no C-peptide, so exogenous insulin gives high insulin with low C-peptide. Insulinoma and sulfonylurea use both give high insulin and high C-peptide, and a drug screen separates them. The liver removes much of secreted insulin on first pass, while C-peptide is cleared mainly by the kidney.","whyNot":["The liver clears much of secreted insulin on its first pass. C-peptide is cleared mainly by the kidney.","The disulfide bonds hold the A and B chains of insulin together. C-peptide is cut away and released as a separate peptide.","The rough ER removes the signal peptide to make proinsulin. C-peptide is cut out later, inside secretory granules.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Insulin synthesis and processing","C-peptide in the evaluation of hypoglycemia"],"comps":["EN-8","EN-12-G2"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C39","system":"EN","dx":"Glucagonoma","codes":["EN-12-G2"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of a painful, itchy rash for 3 months and a 9-kg weight loss. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 124/76 mm Hg. Examination shows red, scaling plaques with crusted, blistering edges that spread outward on the groin, buttocks, and legs. His tongue is smooth and red. Laboratory studies show fasting glucose 214 mg/dL (70 to 99), hemoglobin 10.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), and plasma glucagon 1,200 pg/mL (50 to 150). CT shows a 4.5-cm mass in the tail of the pancreas. The diagnosis is glucagonoma.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C39-Q1","stem":"Somatostatinoma can also cause diabetes and weight loss. Which of the following findings in this patient best distinguishes his tumor from a somatostatinoma?","options":["Elevated fasting plasma glucose","Necrolytic migratory erythema","Unintended loss of body weight","Large tumor size at diagnosis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"EN-12-G2","obj":"EN-12-G.5","dok":2,"approach":["List what both tumors share: diabetes (glucagon raises glucose output, somatostatin blocks insulin release), weight loss, and large size when found.","Somatostatinoma adds gallstones, steatorrhea, and achlorhydria, because somatostatin blocks CCK, pancreatic enzyme secretion, and gastrin.","Glucagonoma adds necrolytic migratory erythema, a spreading, blistering rash, so the rash is the distinguishing finding."],"why":"Glucagonoma is an alpha cell tumor. Its classic features are diabetes or glucose intolerance, weight loss, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and venous thrombosis. The rash is linked to the very low plasma amino acid levels that excess glucagon causes. Somatostatinoma, a delta cell tumor, inhibits many gut and pancreatic hormones and causes diabetes, gallstones, steatorrhea, and achlorhydria. Both share hyperglycemia and weight loss, so those findings cannot separate them.","whyNot":["Fasting hyperglycemia occurs with both tumors. Glucagon raises hepatic glucose output, and somatostatin suppresses insulin release.","Correct.","Weight loss occurs with both tumors, from catabolism in glucagonoma and malabsorption in somatostatinoma.","Both tumors are usually large when found because their early symptoms are vague. Size does not separate them."],"study":{"topics":["Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","Necrolytic migratory erythema"],"comps":["EN-12-G2","EN-2"]}},{"id":"EN-C39-Q2","stem":"His plasma amino acid concentrations are markedly decreased. Which of the following effects of the tumor's hormone best explains this finding?","options":["Increased GLUT4-mediated glucose uptake","Increased fatty acid synthesis in the liver","Increased hepatic gluconeogenesis","Increased hepatic glycogen synthesis"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-7","obj":"EN-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["The tumor makes glucagon, the main hormone of the fasting state.","Glucagon drives the liver to make new glucose, and amino acids such as alanine are a major carbon source for gluconeogenesis.","With very high glucagon, the liver pulls amino acids out of the blood continuously, so plasma levels fall and muscle protein is broken down to supply them."],"why":"Insulin and glucagon have opposite effects on fuel use. Insulin, the fed-state hormone, increases GLUT4 glucose uptake in muscle and fat, glycogen synthesis, glycolysis, fatty acid synthesis, and protein synthesis. Glucagon, the fasting hormone, acts mainly on the liver to increase glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, and ketogenesis. In glucagonoma, constant gluconeogenesis uses up amino acids, which lowers plasma amino acid levels, wastes muscle, and contributes to weight loss and the rash.","whyNot":["GLUT4 glucose uptake into muscle and fat is stimulated by insulin. Glucagon has no direct effect on GLUT4.","Fatty acid synthesis in the liver is an insulin effect. Glucagon inhibits it.","Correct.","Glycogen synthesis is an insulin effect. Glucagon breaks glycogen down."],"study":{"topics":["Insulin versus glucagon effects","Gluconeogenesis substrates"],"comps":["EN-7","EN-12-G2"]}},{"id":"EN-C39-Q3","stem":"His symptoms are treated with octreotide, a long-acting analog of an islet hormone that inhibits secretion of glucagon, insulin, and many gut hormones. In normal islets, this hormone is secreted by which of the following cell types?","options":["Alpha cells","Delta cells","PP cells","Beta cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Name the hormone: octreotide is a somatostatin analog.","Somatostatin inhibits glucagon, insulin, and many gut hormones, which is why it controls the rash and other effects of glucagon excess.","Within the islet, somatostatin is made by delta cells."],"why":"Islets contain beta cells (insulin, the most numerous), alpha cells (glucagon), delta cells (somatostatin), and a few PP cells (pancreatic polypeptide). Somatostatin from delta cells acts locally to inhibit release of both insulin and glucagon, and it also inhibits many gut hormones. Octreotide copies this effect and lasts much longer than natural somatostatin, so it lowers glucagon output from the tumor and often improves the rash. A tumor of delta cells themselves is a somatostatinoma.","whyNot":["Alpha cells make glucagon, the hormone this tumor overproduces. Octreotide suppresses their secretion rather than copying their hormone.","Correct.","PP cells make pancreatic polypeptide, which affects pancreatic exocrine secretion and appetite. It does not broadly inhibit islet and gut hormones.","Beta cells make insulin. Insulin does inhibit glucagon release inside the islet, but octreotide lowers insulin secretion too, so it does not mimic insulin."],"study":{"topics":["Islet cell types and hormones","Somatostatin actions"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-12-G2"]}},{"id":"EN-C39-Q4","stem":"The hormone secreted by this tumor in the pancreatic tail first reaches which of the following capillary beds at its highest concentration?","options":["Hepatic sinusoids","Renal glomerular capillaries","Splenic red pulp sinusoids","Pulmonary alveolar capillaries"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-4","obj":"EN-4.5","dok":2,"approach":["Veins from the pancreatic body and tail drain into the splenic vein.","The splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein.","Portal blood flows through the liver sinusoids before it reaches the systemic circulation, so the liver sees the highest hormone levels."],"why":"Pancreatic venous blood drains into the portal system: the body and tail into the splenic vein, and the head into pancreaticoduodenal veins that join the superior mesenteric and portal veins. All of it passes through hepatic sinusoids before entering the inferior vena cava. This suits the physiology, because the liver is the main target of both insulin and glucagon. The liver also removes about half of secreted insulin on its first pass, so portal insulin levels are much higher than peripheral levels. For the same reason, glucagon measured in a peripheral vein understates what the liver receives.","whyNot":["Correct.","The kidneys receive pancreatic hormones from systemic arterial blood, after hepatic passage. Pancreatic veins do not drain into the renal veins.","The splenic vein carries pancreatic venous blood away from the spleen toward the portal vein. Pancreatic hormones do not enter the splenic sinusoids directly.","Pancreatic hormones reach the lungs only after passing through the liver, the inferior vena cava, and the right heart."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous drainage of the pancreas","Hepatic first-pass extraction of insulin"],"comps":["EN-4","EN-3"]}}]},{"id":"EN-C40","system":"EN","dx":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome","codes":["EN-9"],"case":"A 16-year-old girl is brought to the physician because she has never had a menstrual period. Breast development began at age 11 and is now complete. She is 172 cm tall. Axillary and pubic hair are sparse. The external genitalia are female, and the vagina is short and ends blindly. Pelvic ultrasonography shows no uterus or ovaries, and bilateral inguinal masses are found to be testes. Karyotype is 46,XY. Serum testosterone is 640 ng/dL (adult male 300 to 1000; adult female 15 to 70), and serum LH is 26 mIU/mL (adult male 2 to 12). The diagnosis is complete androgen insensitivity syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"EN-C40-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient best distinguishes her condition from 5-alpha-reductase deficiency?","options":["46,XY karyotype with bilateral testes","Absent uterus and fallopian tubes","Serum testosterone in the adult male range","Female genitalia and breast development"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-9","obj":"EN-9.5","dok":3,"approach":["List what both conditions share: a 46,XY karyotype, testes, normal or high testosterone, and no uterus, because Sertoli cells still make anti-Mullerian hormone.","In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, testosterone still acts on a normal androgen receptor, so the body virilizes at puberty with little breast growth.","In androgen insensitivity, no tissue responds to androgens, so the external genitalia are female, and testosterone converted to estradiol produces breasts."],"why":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome is an X-linked defect in the androgen receptor. The testes make testosterone and anti-Mullerian hormone normally, so Mullerian structures regress, but neither testosterone nor DHT can act. The external genitalia develop as female, Wolffian structures regress, and body hair is sparse. At puberty, aromatase converts the high testosterone to estradiol, which drives breast development. In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, DHT is low, so the external genitalia are ambiguous at birth, but testosterone acts on the normal receptor and causes marked virilization at puberty without breast growth.","whyNot":["Both conditions have a 46,XY karyotype and testes, so this finding cannot separate them.","Both conditions lack a uterus, because Sertoli cells in both make anti-Mullerian hormone.","Testosterone is normal to high in both conditions. In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, the testosterone to DHT ratio is high.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Androgen insensitivity syndrome","5-alpha-reductase deficiency"],"comps":["EN-9","EN-6"]}},{"id":"EN-C40-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the combination of elevated serum testosterone and elevated LH in this patient?","options":["Deficient hypothalamic GnRH secretion","Estradiol suppression of pituitary gonadotroph secretion","Primary failure of testosterone synthesis by Leydig cells","Absent androgen feedback at hypothalamus and pituitary"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"EN-6","obj":"EN-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Use feedback logic: when the target hormone and its trophic hormone are both high, the problem is not too little target hormone.","Testosterone normally suppresses GnRH and LH through androgen receptors in the hypothalamus and pituitary.","If those receptors cannot respond, the brain reads testosterone as missing and keeps LH high, which drives the testes to make more testosterone."],"why":"Paired hormone levels localize endocrine disorders. Low target hormone with high trophic hormone means target gland failure, such as primary hypogonadism. Low target hormone with low or normal trophic hormone means pituitary or hypothalamic failure. High target hormone with low trophic hormone means the target gland is secreting on its own. High target hormone with high trophic hormone means either a tumor secreting the trophic hormone or resistance to feedback. Here the androgen receptor defect affects the hypothalamus and pituitary too, so LH and testosterone both rise.","whyNot":["Low GnRH would lower LH, and testosterone would then fall too.","Estradiol suppresses LH release. If estradiol were controlling the axis, LH would be low.","Primary testicular failure would cause low testosterone with high LH. Her testosterone is high.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Negative feedback in endocrine axes","Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis"],"comps":["EN-6","EN-9"]}},{"id":"EN-C40-Q3","stem":"In a hematoxylin and eosin section of her pituitary gland, the cells that secrete her elevated trophic hormone would most likely appear as which of the following?","options":["Basophilic cells in the anterior lobe","Acidophilic cells in the anterior lobe","Chromophobe cells in the anterior lobe","Pituicytes among axons of the posterior lobe"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"EN-2","obj":"EN-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the hormone: her LH is high, and LH is made by gonadotrophs.","Sort anterior pituitary cells by staining: acidophils make GH and prolactin, basophils make FSH, LH, ACTH, and TSH, and chromophobes stain poorly.","Gonadotrophs are therefore basophils in the anterior lobe."],"why":"The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) contains acidophils, basophils, and chromophobes. Acidophils make GH (somatotrophs) and prolactin (lactotrophs). Basophils make FSH and LH (gonadotrophs), TSH (thyrotrophs), and ACTH (corticotrophs); the mnemonic B-FLAT links basophils to FSH, LH, ACTH, and TSH. Chromophobes have few granules and include degranulated cells and stem cells. The posterior pituitary has no hormone-making cells. It holds axon terminals of hypothalamic neurons, their swollen storage endings (Herring bodies), and supporting pituicytes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Acidophils secrete growth hormone and prolactin, not LH.","Chromophobes contain few hormone granules and stain weakly. They are not the main LH-secreting cells.","Pituicytes are glial support cells of the posterior lobe. The posterior lobe releases ADH and oxytocin made in the hypothalamus."],"study":{"topics":["Pituitary histology","Anterior pituitary cell types"],"comps":["EN-2","EN-1"]}},{"id":"EN-C40-Q4","stem":"Androgen effects on target tissues develop over hours to days rather than seconds. Which of the following mechanisms of the receptor that is defective in this patient explains this slow onset?","options":["Phosphorylating tyrosine residues on intracellular substrates","Recruiting JAK kinases that phosphorylate STAT proteins","Binding hormone response elements to alter transcription","Activating adenylyl cyclase through a stimulatory G protein"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"EN-9","obj":"EN-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Testosterone and DHT are steroids, so they are lipid soluble and cross the plasma membrane.","The androgen receptor sits in the cytoplasm bound to heat shock proteins. Hormone binding releases them, and the receptor dimerizes and moves into the nucleus.","There it binds hormone response elements in DNA and changes transcription. Making new mRNA and protein takes hours, which explains the slow onset."],"why":"Steroid hormones, calcitriol, and thyroid hormone act through intracellular receptors that are ligand-activated transcription factors. Each receptor has a hormone-binding domain and a zinc finger DNA-binding domain. Steroid receptors usually bind DNA as homodimers, while the thyroid hormone receptor pairs with the retinoid X receptor. Because their effects need new mRNA and protein, they begin over hours and last longer than the seconds-to-minutes effects of peptide hormones that act through second messengers. In androgen insensitivity, mutations in the hormone-binding or DNA-binding domain block this process in every target tissue.","whyNot":["Tyrosine kinase activity is how the insulin and IGF-1 receptors signal.","JAK/STAT signaling is used by growth hormone and prolactin receptors.","Correct.","Gs activation of adenylyl cyclase is used by peptide hormones such as LH, TSH, and PTH, and it acts within seconds to minutes."],"study":{"topics":["Intracellular hormone receptors","Androgen receptor"],"comps":["EN-9","EN-6"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C01","system":"GI","dx":"Mumps parotitis in an unvaccinated college student","codes":["GI-13-A1"],"case":"A 19-year-old man comes to the student health clinic because of a 2-day history of painful swelling at both angles of the jaw and pain with chewing. Fever, malaise, and headache began 3 days earlier. He did not receive childhood vaccinations, and several students in his residence hall have had a similar illness. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tender, bilateral parotid swelling that obscures the angles of the mandible. The parotid duct openings are erythematous, and no pus is expressed with gland massage. The testes are normal. Serum amylase concentration is 310 U/L (normal 25 to 125), and serum lipase concentration is 48 U/L (normal 10 to 140). RT-PCR of a buccal swab detects mumps virus. Diagnosis: mumps parotitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C01-Q1","stem":"During the same week, a 76-year-old man who is dehydrated 4 days after a colectomy develops painful swelling of the right parotid gland and a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F). Which of the following findings would best distinguish his condition from that of the college student?","options":["Raised serum amylase with a normal lipase concentration","Pus expressed from the parotid duct opening","Swelling that obscures the angle of the mandible","Pain in the gland that worsens with chewing"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-A1","obj":"GI-13-A.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the older patient's setting: postoperative, dehydrated, one gland involved. This points to acute bacterial parotitis.","List what both conditions share: a swollen, tender parotid, pain with chewing, fever, and a raised salivary amylase.","Pick the finding seen only with bacterial infection: pus at the duct opening."],"why":"Acute bacterial parotitis develops when salivary flow falls, as with dehydration after surgery, and bacteria ascend the parotid (Stensen) duct. Staphylococcus aureus is the usual cause. The gland fills with neutrophils, so massaging it pushes pus out of the duct opening. Mumps is a viral infection of the gland tissue, so the duct openings may be red, but no pus comes out.","whyNot":["Any inflamed salivary gland leaks salivary-type amylase into the blood while lipase stays normal. This pattern points to the salivary glands but does not tell viral from bacterial disease.","Correct.","Marked swelling hides the angle of the mandible whenever the parotid enlarges, whatever the cause.","Chewing stimulates salivary secretion and stretches the inflamed gland capsule, so it hurts in both mumps and bacterial parotitis."],"study":{"topics":["Mumps versus bacterial parotitis","Risk factors for bacterial parotitis"],"comps":["GI-13-A1","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C01-Q2","stem":"His roommate, a 22-year-old man, has recurrent painful swelling below the left side of the mandible that begins within minutes of eating and subsides over the next hour. Radiography shows a radiopaque calculus in the floor of the mouth. Which of the following features of the affected gland best explains why calculi form most often in this gland?","options":["Many small ducts that open along the sublingual fold","Mainly serous, amylase-rich saliva drained through a short duct","A duct that opens opposite the upper second molar tooth","Thicker, mucin-rich saliva drained upward through a long duct"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-A","obj":"GI-13-A.2","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the swelling: below the mandible with a stone in the floor of the mouth means the submandibular gland and its duct.","Recall the two features that favor stone formation in this gland: viscous saliva and a long duct that runs upward against gravity.","Link slow flow of thick saliva to precipitation of calcium salts."],"why":"About 80 to 90% of salivary stones form in the submandibular gland. Its saliva is a mixed serous and mucous secretion that is more viscous and more alkaline, with more calcium, than parotid saliva. Its duct (Wharton duct) is long and runs upward and forward to open beside the frenulum of the tongue, so saliva drains against gravity and stagnates. A stone blocks outflow, so the gland swells and hurts when eating stimulates secretion, and stagnant saliva can become infected (sialadenitis).","whyNot":["The sublingual gland drains through many small ducts in the sublingual fold. Short ducts with little stasis make stones in this gland rare.","This describes the parotid gland. Thin serous saliva flows easily, which is one reason parotid stones are uncommon.","The parotid (Stensen) duct opens on the inner cheek opposite the upper second molar. A stone there would cause swelling in front of the ear.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Sialolithiasis","Salivary gland ducts"],"comps":["GI-13-A","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C01-Q3","stem":"His raised serum amylase comes from the inflamed glands. Which of the following best describes the secretory units that make up nearly all of these glands?","options":["Serous acini that secrete a watery, amylase-rich fluid","Mucous acini that secrete a viscous, mucin-rich fluid","Exocrine acini with scattered islets of endocrine cells","Mixed acini of mucous cells capped by serous demilunes"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the glands involved: the parotid glands.","Recall that the parotid is an almost purely serous gland.","Connect serous cells to their product, salivary amylase, which explains the raised serum amylase."],"why":"The parotid gland is made almost entirely of serous acini. Serous cells have basophilic cytoplasm, round central nuclei, and apical secretory granules, and they make a watery fluid rich in salivary amylase. When mumps virus inflames the parotid, this amylase leaks into the blood. The submandibular gland is mixed but mostly serous, and the sublingual gland is mostly mucous.","whyNot":["Correct.","Mucous acini, with pale, foamy cytoplasm and flattened basal nuclei, make up most of the sublingual gland and the minor salivary glands of the palate.","Acini mixed with islets of Langerhans describe the pancreas. Mumps can inflame the pancreas, but his normal lipase points away from it.","Mucous tubules capped by serous demilunes are typical of the submandibular and sublingual glands."],"study":{"topics":["Serous versus mucous salivary glands","Salivary amylase"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C01-Q4","stem":"Mumps virus first replicates in the upper respiratory mucosa. After recovery, his saliva contains the main antibody class that protects mucosal surfaces. Which of the following mediates the transport of this antibody into his saliva?","options":["Neonatal Fc receptor carrying IgG across the epithelium","Polymeric Ig receptor on the basolateral epithelial surface","M cell transcytosis of antigen to underlying lymphoid follicles","Passive leakage of plasma IgM between epithelial cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the antibody in saliva and other mucosal secretions: secretory IgA.","Recall how it crosses the epithelium: plasma cells under the epithelium release dimeric IgA joined by a J chain, and epithelial cells carry it across.","Match this to the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor."],"why":"Plasma cells in the gland stroma and mucosa secrete IgA as a dimer held together by a J chain. The polymeric immunoglobulin receptor on the basolateral surface of epithelial cells binds the dimer and carries it across the cell by transcytosis. At the apical surface the receptor is cleaved, and the piece that stays attached, called secretory component, protects IgA from digestion in the secretions. Secretory IgA blocks viruses and bacteria from attaching to mucosal cells.","whyNot":["The neonatal Fc receptor carries IgG across the placenta and protects IgG from breakdown, which gives IgG its long half-life. It is not the route for antibody in saliva.","Correct.","M cells over Peyer patches carry antigen from the gut lumen to lymphoid tissue to start mucosal immune responses. They move antigen inward, not antibody outward.","IgM can also be carried by the polymeric Ig receptor, but it is a minor secretory antibody, and it moves by active transcytosis, not leakage."],"study":{"topics":["Secretory IgA","Polymeric immunoglobulin receptor"],"comps":["GI-11"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C02","system":"GI","dx":"Pleomorphic adenoma of the parotid gland","codes":["GI-13-K"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a painless lump in front of her right ear that has grown slowly over 3 years. She has no facial weakness, numbness, or pain. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 72/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a 2.5-cm, firm, mobile, nontender mass in the superficial part of the right parotid gland. Facial nerve function is normal on both sides. There is no cervical lymphadenopathy. Fine-needle aspiration shows epithelial and myoepithelial cells in a myxoid and chondroid stroma. She undergoes superficial parotidectomy. Diagnosis: pleomorphic adenoma of the right parotid gland.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C02-Q1","stem":"Her surgeon removes the tumor with a margin of normal gland tissue and avoids simple enucleation. Which of the following features of this tumor best explains this choice?","options":["Perineural invasion along branches of the facial nerve","Cystic spaces lined by oncocytes over lymphoid stroma","Finger-like projections through an incomplete capsule","Infiltrating mixture of mucus-secreting and squamoid cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K","obj":"GI-13-K.12","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the most common salivary tumor: a slow-growing, mobile parotid mass with epithelial, myoepithelial, and chondromyxoid tissue.","Recall that this benign tumor is not fully encapsulated.","Connect the small extensions of tumor beyond the capsule to recurrence when the tumor is shelled out."],"why":"Pleomorphic adenoma is the most common salivary gland tumor, and most arise in the parotid. It is benign and mixes epithelial and myoepithelial cells with myxoid, hyaline, or cartilage-like stroma. The capsule is incomplete, and small tongues of tumor push into the surrounding gland. Shelling out the main mass leaves these projections behind, so recurrence is common unless a rim of normal tissue is removed. Long-standing tumors carry a small risk of malignant change.","whyNot":["Perineural invasion is the hallmark of adenoid cystic carcinoma, a malignant tumor that often causes pain or facial weakness. Her nerve function is normal and the aspirate shows a benign mixed tumor.","Oncocytic cysts over dense lymphoid stroma describe Warthin tumor, the second most common benign parotid tumor, which is strongly linked to smoking. Its cytology differs from this aspirate.","Correct.","Mucus-secreting, squamoid, and intermediate cells describe mucoepidermoid carcinoma, the most common malignant salivary tumor."],"study":{"topics":["Pleomorphic adenoma","Salivary gland tumors"],"comps":["GI-13-K","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C02-Q2","stem":"During the operation, the surgeon identifies and preserves a nerve that branches within the gland, between its superficial and deep parts. Injury to this nerve would most likely cause which of the following?","options":["Weak right eye closure and a drooping mouth corner","Deviation of the jaw to the right on opening the mouth","Numbness of the skin over the right angle of the mandible","Reduced secretion of saliva from the right parotid gland"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the nerve that divides into its branches inside the parotid gland: the facial nerve (CN VII).","Recall what the facial nerve does after it leaves the stylomastoid foramen: it supplies the muscles of facial expression.","Predict weakness of the orbicularis oculi and the muscles of the mouth on the same side."],"why":"After leaving the stylomastoid foramen, the facial nerve enters the parotid gland and divides into its temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical branches within it. The plane of the nerve separates the superficial part of the gland from the deep part. These branches carry only motor fibers to the muscles of facial expression, so injury weakens eye closure and lets the corner of the mouth droop on that side. This is why a parotid mass that causes facial weakness raises concern for a malignant tumor.","whyNot":["Correct.","The muscles of mastication, including the lateral pterygoid that opens the jaw, are supplied by CN V3. Weakness of one lateral pterygoid makes the jaw deviate toward the weak side.","Skin over the angle of the mandible is supplied by the great auricular nerve (C2 and C3), which can be cut during parotid surgery but runs on the surface of the gland, not through it.","Secretomotor fibers to the parotid come from CN IX through the otic ganglion and reach the gland with the auriculotemporal nerve. The facial nerve branches in the gland do not carry them."],"study":{"topics":["Facial nerve in the parotid gland","Parotid gland anatomy"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-K"]}},{"id":"GI-C02-Q3","stem":"Five years later, she receives radiation therapy for an unrelated oropharyngeal cancer, and both parotid glands are in the radiation field. Over the next year, her mouth stays dry, and she develops many new carious lesions along the gum line. Loss of which of the following salivary functions most directly explains the new dental caries?","options":["Haptocorrin binding of dietary vitamin B12","Amylase digestion of dietary starch into maltose","Lingual lipase hydrolysis of dietary triglycerides","Bicarbonate buffering of acid from plaque bacteria"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that radiation has destroyed the serous acini, so salivary flow has fallen.","Recall how caries form: plaque bacteria ferment sugars to acid, which dissolves enamel.","Choose the salivary function that neutralizes this acid."],"why":"Saliva protects the teeth in several ways. Its bicarbonate buffers the acid that plaque bacteria such as Streptococcus mutans make from dietary sugar, and its calcium and phosphate help remineralize enamel. It also contains IgA, lysozyme, and lactoferrin that limit bacterial growth, and its flow washes the teeth. When salivary flow falls, as after radiation or in Sjogren syndrome, acid stays on the enamel and caries develop quickly, often at the gum line.","whyNot":["Haptocorrin (R binder) protects vitamin B12 from stomach acid. It has no role in protecting enamel.","Salivary amylase starts starch digestion. Its loss would not promote caries, and breaking starch into sugars actually gives plaque bacteria more substrate.","Lingual lipase begins fat digestion, mainly in infants. It has no protective role for the teeth.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Protective functions of saliva","Xerostomia and dental caries"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C02-Q4","stem":"Before surgery, her swallowing is evaluated. When a swallowed bolus enters the pharynx, her lower esophageal sphincter relaxes before the peristaltic wave reaches it. Which of the following most directly mediates this relaxation?","options":["Vagal excitatory neurons releasing acetylcholine","Sympathetic fibers releasing norepinephrine onto beta-2 receptors","Vagal inhibitory neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP","Gastrin released from antral G cells after the meal"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the pharyngeal phase of swallowing is a reflex coordinated by the swallowing center in the medulla.","Recognize that the sphincter must open ahead of the bolus, which needs active inhibition of its tonic contraction.","Identify the inhibitory transmitters released by vagal pathways in the myenteric plexus: nitric oxide and VIP."],"why":"Swallowing starts voluntarily in the mouth, and the pharyngeal and esophageal phases then run as a reflex organized in the medulla. Through the vagus nerve, the swallowing center activates inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus of the esophagus and stomach. These neurons release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide, which relax the smooth muscle of the lower esophageal sphincter and the proximal stomach (receptive relaxation). Loss of these inhibitory neurons causes achalasia.","whyNot":["Acetylcholine from vagal excitatory neurons contracts esophageal smooth muscle and drives peristalsis. It does not relax the sphincter.","Sympathetic input has little role in swallowing and does not produce the rapid relaxation that precedes the bolus.","Correct.","Gastrin increases lower esophageal sphincter tone, and it is released only after food reaches the stomach."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of swallowing","Lower esophageal sphincter control"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C03","system":"GI","dx":"Acute alcohol-related pancreatitis complicated by a pseudocyst","codes":["GI-13-B1"],"case":"A 42-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of a 1-day history of severe epigastric pain that radiates to the back, with nausea and vomiting. He has drunk about 10 beers daily for 15 years and drank heavily for 2 days before the pain began. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Physical examination shows epigastric tenderness with guarding and decreased bowel sounds. Serum lipase concentration is 1850 U/L (normal 10 to 140), and serum calcium concentration is 8.6 mg/dL (normal 8.5 to 10.5). Ultrasonography shows no gallstones. CT shows an edematous pancreas with peripancreatic fluid. Diagnosis: acute alcohol-related pancreatitis. Five weeks later, he has early satiety and epigastric fullness, and CT shows a 7-cm, well-defined fluid collection in the lesser sac behind the stomach.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C03-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the key initiating event in the pathogenesis of his pancreatic injury?","options":["Activation of trypsinogen to trypsin within acinar cells","Release of insulin and glucagon from destroyed islet cells","Reflux of bile into the pancreatic duct through a common channel","IgG4-positive plasma cells infiltrating and destroying acini"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-B1","obj":"GI-13-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that pancreatic enzymes are normally made as inactive zymogens and become active only in the duodenum.","Recognize that any cause of acute pancreatitis, alcohol included, leads to activation of these enzymes inside the gland.","Identify trypsin as the key enzyme, because it activates the other zymogens."],"why":"Acute pancreatitis begins when trypsinogen is activated to trypsin inside acinar cells or ducts. Trypsin then activates the other zymogens, such as phospholipase A2 and elastase, and these enzymes digest the gland, its fat, and its blood vessels. Alcohol promotes this by thickening pancreatic secretions so they plug small ducts, by directly injuring acinar cells, and by causing abnormal enzyme release. Gallstones and alcohol cause most cases, with hypertriglyceridemia, hypercalcemia, ERCP, mumps, trauma, and some drugs such as thiazides as less common causes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Islet hormones do not digest tissue. Islet damage may follow severe necrosis, but it is a result of the injury, not its cause.","Bile reflux through a shared channel is one proposed way that a gallstone at the ampulla triggers pancreatitis. Even then, the injury runs through trypsin activation, and he has no gallstones.","IgG4-positive plasma cells mark autoimmune pancreatitis, which causes a painless, diffusely enlarged pancreas and responds to glucocorticoids."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of acute pancreatitis","Causes of acute pancreatitis"],"comps":["GI-13-B1","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C03-Q2","stem":"The fluid collection seen 5 weeks later is drained, and part of its wall is examined microscopically. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Papillary mucinous epithelium lining dilated main ducts","Columnar mucinous epithelium over ovarian-type stroma","Glycogen-rich cuboidal epithelium lining many small cysts","Fibrous granulation tissue with no epithelial lining"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-B1","obj":"GI-13-B.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the complication: a well-defined fluid collection in the lesser sac several weeks after acute pancreatitis is a pseudocyst.","Recall why it is called a pseudocyst: the wall is reactive tissue, not epithelium.","Exclude the cystic neoplasms, which all have an epithelial lining."],"why":"A pancreatic pseudocyst forms when enzyme-rich fluid and necrotic debris from pancreatitis are walled off, often in the lesser sac. Over about 4 weeks the body builds a wall of granulation tissue that matures into fibrous tissue. There is no epithelial lining, which is what makes it a pseudocyst. Large pseudocysts can press on the stomach and cause early satiety, and they can become infected, bleed, or rupture.","whyNot":["Papillary mucin-producing epithelium in dilated ducts describes an intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm, a precursor of pancreatic cancer.","Mucinous epithelium over ovarian-type stroma defines a mucinous cystic neoplasm, which occurs almost only in women, usually in the body or tail, and has malignant potential.","Glycogen-rich cuboidal cells lining many small cysts describe a serous cystadenoma, a benign tumor usually seen in older women.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pancreatic pseudocyst","Complications of acute pancreatitis"],"comps":["GI-13-B1","GI-13-K6"]}},{"id":"GI-C03-Q3","stem":"His initial CT shows peripancreatic fluid confined behind the posterior parietal peritoneum. Which of the following structures is most likely to be surrounded by this fluid?","options":["Proximal first part of the duodenum","Lower pole of the spleen","Second part of the duodenum","Proximal jejunum"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that fluid behind the posterior parietal peritoneum lies in the retroperitoneum, where most of the pancreas sits.","Sort the options into intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal structures.","Pick the only retroperitoneal one: the descending duodenum, which curves around the pancreatic head."],"why":"The pancreas, except for its tail, is a secondarily retroperitoneal organ. The duodenum from its second to fourth parts, the ascending and descending colon, and part of the rectum share this location, along with the kidneys, ureters, adrenal glands, aorta, and inferior vena cava. Inflammatory fluid from pancreatitis spreads through these retroperitoneal spaces and can surround the duodenum and reach the flanks.","whyNot":["The proximal first part of the duodenum (the bulb, about 2 cm) has a mesentery and is intraperitoneal. The rest of the duodenum is retroperitoneal.","The spleen is intraperitoneal, held by the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments. The pancreatic tail reaches it within the splenorenal ligament.","Correct.","The jejunum hangs on the mesentery and is intraperitoneal. Fluid would reach it only by spreading into the peritoneal cavity."],"study":{"topics":["Retroperitoneal organs","Peritoneal relations of the duodenum"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C03-Q4","stem":"When he resumes eating, a meal delivers fatty acids and amino acids to his duodenum. Which of the following hormones, with its source cell, most directly increases pancreatic enzyme secretion in response?","options":["Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide from K cells","Gastrin from G cells","Cholecystokinin from I cells","Secretin from S cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the stimulus: fat and protein products in the duodenum.","Recall which duodenal hormone responds to fat and amino acids and which responds to acid.","Match enzyme secretion from acinar cells to cholecystokinin, made by I cells."],"why":"Fatty acids, monoglycerides, and amino acids in the duodenum and jejunum stimulate I cells to release cholecystokinin. CCK acts on acinar cells, largely through vagal reflexes, to release digestive enzymes, and it contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi. It also slows gastric emptying. Acid entering the duodenum instead triggers secretin from S cells, which drives bicarbonate secretion from duct cells. Patients with acute pancreatitis are often kept from eating at first so that this stimulation is avoided.","whyNot":["GIP from K cells responds to glucose and fat and increases insulin release. It does not drive enzyme secretion.","Gastrin from antral G cells responds to peptides and amino acids in the stomach and stimulates gastric acid secretion.","Correct.","Secretin from duodenal S cells responds mainly to acid and stimulates duct cells to secrete bicarbonate-rich fluid, not enzymes."],"study":{"topics":["Cholecystokinin","GI hormones and their source cells"],"comps":["GI-6"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C04","system":"GI","dx":"Hypertriglyceridemia-induced acute pancreatitis with steatotic liver disease","codes":["GI-13-B1"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman with obesity and type 2 diabetes mellitus is brought to the emergency department because of an 8-hour history of severe epigastric pain radiating to the back and repeated vomiting. She rarely drinks alcohol. Her BMI is 36 kg/m2. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows epigastric tenderness. Her plasma is visibly milky. Laboratory studies show serum lipase 2400 U/L (normal 10 to 140), triglycerides 2150 mg/dL (normal below 150), glucose 248 mg/dL (normal fasting 70 to 99), ALT 68 U/L (normal 10 to 40), and AST 49 U/L (normal 10 to 40). Ultrasonography shows no gallstones and a diffusely echogenic liver. Diagnosis: hypertriglyceridemia-induced acute pancreatitis with metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C04-Q1","stem":"On hospital day 2, she has perioral tingling, and her serum calcium concentration is 7.4 mg/dL (normal 8.5 to 10.5). Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Reduced intestinal calcium absorption from vitamin D deficiency","Calcium binding to fatty acids released in peripancreatic fat","Increased urinary calcium loss from renal tubular injury","Calcium uptake into bone driven by excess calcitonin"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-B1","obj":"GI-13-B.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that pancreatic lipase leaks into the fat around the pancreas during acute pancreatitis.","Follow the chemistry: lipase splits triglyceride into free fatty acids, which bind calcium.","Link calcium soap formation to a falling serum calcium."],"why":"In acute pancreatitis, activated lipase escapes into peripancreatic and omental fat and splits triglycerides into free fatty acids. The fatty acids combine with calcium to form insoluble calcium soaps, seen as chalky white spots of fat necrosis. This takes calcium out of the blood, and a low calcium is a sign of more severe disease. Other classic findings are epigastric pain radiating to the back, lipase above 3 times normal, and in hemorrhagic disease, periumbilical (Cullen) or flank (Grey Turner) bruising.","whyNot":["Vitamin D deficiency lowers calcium over weeks to months. It does not explain a fall within 2 days of acute pancreatitis.","Correct.","Tubular injury is not a usual cause of hypocalcemia here. Calcium falls because it is deposited in necrotic fat.","Calcitonin has only a small effect on serum calcium in adults and is not raised in pancreatitis."],"study":{"topics":["Fat necrosis and hypocalcemia","Findings in acute pancreatitis"],"comps":["GI-13-B1"]}},{"id":"GI-C04-Q2","stem":"Dietary triglyceride reaches her blood in chylomicrons that enterocytes assemble with apolipoprotein B-48. A child with a mutation that prevents enterocytes from loading lipid onto apolipoprotein B would most likely have which of the following?","options":["Eruptive xanthomas, lipemia retinalis, and recurrent pancreatitis","Steatorrhea, acanthocytes, and ataxia from vitamin E deficiency","Tendon xanthomas and early coronary artery disease","Enlarged orange tonsils and very low HDL cholesterol"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["Trace fat absorption: micelles deliver fatty acids and monoglycerides to enterocytes, which re-form triglyceride and package it with apoB-48 into chylomicrons that enter lacteals.","Recognize the defect: without microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, chylomicrons cannot form (abetalipoproteinemia).","Predict the result: fat stays in enterocytes and is lost in the stool, and fat-soluble vitamins, especially vitamin E, are not absorbed."],"why":"After bile salt micelles deliver fatty acids and monoglycerides to the enterocyte, the cell re-forms triglyceride in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum. Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein loads this lipid onto apoB-48 to form chylomicrons, which leave through lacteals into the lymph. In abetalipoproteinemia, MTP is absent, so no chylomicrons or VLDL form. Fat builds up in enterocytes and is lost as steatorrhea, red cells develop spiky membranes (acanthocytes), and vitamin E deficiency damages the spinal cord and retina.","whyNot":["This triad comes from lipoprotein lipase or apoC-II deficiency. Chylomicrons form normally but are not cleared from the blood, much like her own hypertriglyceridemia. In the child described, chylomicrons cannot form at all.","Correct.","Tendon xanthomas and early coronary disease come from familial hypercholesterolemia, a defect in the LDL receptor or apoB-100 binding.","Orange tonsils with very low HDL describe Tangier disease, a defect in the ABCA1 cholesterol transporter."],"study":{"topics":["Chylomicron formation","Abetalipoproteinemia"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C04-Q3","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms most directly links her insulin resistance to the accumulation of triglyceride in her hepatocytes?","options":["Increased export of hepatocyte triglyceride as VLDL","Decreased hepatic fatty acid synthesis from glucose","Increased fatty acid release from adipose tissue lipolysis","Increased hepatic beta-oxidation of fatty acids in mitochondria"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C","obj":"GI-13-C.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that insulin normally suppresses lipolysis in adipose tissue.","Predict what happens when adipose tissue resists insulin: more free fatty acids flow to the liver.","Recognize that the liver re-forms these fatty acids into triglyceride faster than it can burn or export them."],"why":"In obesity with insulin resistance, insulin fails to restrain hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue, so fat cells release large amounts of free fatty acids. The liver takes them up and re-forms them into triglyceride, and high insulin levels also drive new fatty acid synthesis in hepatocytes. When intake exceeds what the liver can oxidize or export as VLDL, triglyceride collects as steatosis. Simple steatosis is fat alone. Steatohepatitis (MASH) adds hepatocyte ballooning and inflammation and can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma.","whyNot":["VLDL export removes triglyceride from the liver. More export would reduce fat, not cause it.","Hepatic fatty acid synthesis from glucose rises, not falls, in insulin-resistant states with high insulin levels.","Correct.","Beta-oxidation burns fatty acids. More of it would lower liver fat, so it cannot explain the accumulation."],"study":{"topics":["MASLD pathogenesis","Insulin and lipolysis"],"comps":["GI-13-C","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C04-Q4","stem":"In a healthy person, the pancreas secretes trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, and procarboxypeptidase as inactive precursors. Which of the following explains how all three become active in the duodenal lumen?","options":["Enteropeptidase activates trypsin, which activates the rest","Gastric acid entering the duodenum cleaves each zymogen","Bile salts unfold each zymogen to expose its active site","Brush-border aminopeptidases remove the inhibitory peptide from each"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the brush-border enzyme of the duodenum that starts protein digestion: enteropeptidase (enterokinase).","Recognize that it acts on trypsinogen only.","Follow the cascade: trypsin then activates the other zymogens and more trypsinogen."],"why":"Enteropeptidase on the duodenal brush border cleaves trypsinogen to trypsin. Trypsin then activates chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, proelastase, and more trypsinogen, so one brush-border enzyme switches on the whole cascade. Keeping these enzymes inactive until they reach the duodenum protects the pancreas, and a trypsin inhibitor made by acinar cells adds a second safeguard. Pancreatitis, as in this patient, starts when this protection fails and trypsin is activated inside the gland.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pepsinogen in the stomach is activated by acid. Pancreatic zymogens are not, and duodenal acid is neutralized by bicarbonate.","Bile salts emulsify fat and help pancreatic lipase work, with colipase. They do not activate protease zymogens.","Aminopeptidases on the brush border finish digesting small peptides into amino acids. They do not activate the pancreatic zymogens."],"study":{"topics":["Activation of pancreatic proteases","Protein digestion"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-B1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C05","system":"GI","dx":"Chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency in cystic fibrosis","codes":["GI-13-B1","GI-13-K6","GI-13-E3"],"case":"A 26-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of bulky, greasy, foul-smelling stools and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. She has had three episodes of acute pancreatitis since adolescence and has had intermittent epigastric pain since then. She does not drink alcohol. Her BMI is 17.8 kg/m2. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows digital clubbing, scattered crackles, and mild epigastric tenderness. Fecal elastase concentration is 62 µg/g of stool (normal above 200), and fecal fat excretion is 21 g/day (normal below 7). Fasting plasma glucose concentration is 132 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99). CT shows an atrophic pancreas with multiple calcifications. Diagnosis: chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency caused by cystic fibrosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C05-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her raised fasting plasma glucose concentration?","options":["Excess glucagon secretion from an alpha-cell tumor of the pancreas","Autoimmune T-cell destruction of pancreatic beta cells","Peripheral insulin resistance caused by obesity","Loss of islets as fibrosis replaces the pancreatic tissue"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-B1","obj":"GI-13-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize chronic pancreatitis from repeated attacks, steatorrhea, and an atrophic, calcified pancreas.","Recall that ongoing inflammation replaces acini and later islets with fibrous tissue.","Link islet loss to diabetes, which appears after exocrine failure."],"why":"Chronic pancreatitis is ongoing inflammation that replaces the pancreas with fibrous tissue, with duct strictures and calcifications. Causes include alcohol, cystic fibrosis, duct obstruction, and hereditary mutations in PRSS1 and SPINK1. Acini are lost first, causing steatorrhea and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency. As fibrosis advances, islets are also destroyed, so insulin and glucagon both fall, and diabetes develops. In cystic fibrosis this is called cystic fibrosis-related diabetes.","whyNot":["A glucagonoma raises glucose and causes weight loss and a migratory necrolytic rash. It would not explain her calcified, atrophic pancreas.","Autoimmune beta-cell destruction is type 1 diabetes. It spares the exocrine pancreas and does not explain her calcified, fibrotic gland.","Insulin resistance from obesity drives type 2 diabetes. She is underweight, with a BMI of 17.8.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic pancreatitis","Pancreatogenic diabetes"],"comps":["GI-13-B1","GI-10"]}},{"id":"GI-C05-Q2","stem":"Her CFTR defect has reduced the delivery of pancreatic enzymes to her duodenum. If her malabsorption goes untreated, which of the following is most likely to develop?","options":["Prolonged prothrombin time from vitamin K deficiency","Megaloblastic anemia from folate deficiency","Microcytic anemia from failed duodenal iron absorption","Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.6","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the defect: faulty CFTR reduces chloride and bicarbonate secretion by duct cells, so thick secretions plug the ducts and destroy acini.","Predict the result: too little lipase, so fat is not digested and is lost in the stool.","Recognize that vitamins A, D, E, and K travel with fat, so they are malabsorbed too."],"why":"CFTR is a chloride channel in the apical membrane of pancreatic duct cells that also drives bicarbonate and water secretion. When it fails, pancreatic secretions are scant and thick, ducts plug, and acini are destroyed. Without lipase, dietary fat is not digested, so fat and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K are lost in the stool. Vitamin K deficiency reduces carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, and X, which prolongs the prothrombin time. CFTR defects in the gut and liver also cause meconium ileus in newborns and focal biliary cirrhosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Folate is water soluble and absorbed in the jejunum without pancreatic enzymes, so folate deficiency is not expected from pancreatic insufficiency.","Iron is absorbed in the duodenum without needing pancreatic enzymes. Iron deficiency suggests blood loss or duodenal mucosal disease such as celiac disease.","Thiamine is water soluble. Its deficiency is typical of alcohol use disorder or malnutrition, not pancreatic insufficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Cystic fibrosis and the pancreas","Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C05-Q3","stem":"Her chronic pancreatitis increases her lifetime risk of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. In this cancer, the most frequently mutated oncogene encodes a protein whose mutation causes which of the following?","options":["Constitutive tyrosine kinase activity from a gene fusion","Impaired GTP hydrolysis that keeps it in the active state","Loss of a CDK4/6 inhibitor that restrains the G1 to S transition","Overexpression of an antiapoptotic mitochondrial membrane protein"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-K6","obj":"GI-13-K.9","dok":2,"approach":["Name the oncogene mutated in about 90% of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas: KRAS.","Recall that KRAS is a small G protein that is active when bound to GTP.","Predict the effect of the usual point mutation: GTP is not hydrolyzed, so growth signaling stays on."],"why":"KRAS encodes a small GTPase that relays signals from growth factor receptors to the MAP kinase pathway. Normally it switches itself off by hydrolyzing GTP to GDP. Point mutations, often at codon 12, block this GTPase activity, so KRAS stays bound to GTP and keeps signaling. This is usually the earliest mutation in pancreatic precursor lesions. Pancreatic cancer is linked to smoking and chronic pancreatitis, and a tumor in the head of the pancreas typically causes painless jaundice with a palpable gallbladder (Courvoisier sign), weight loss, migratory thrombophlebitis, and a raised CA 19-9.","whyNot":["A fusion that creates a constantly active tyrosine kinase describes BCR-ABL in chronic myeloid leukemia.","Correct.","This describes loss of CDKN2A (p16), which is also very common in pancreatic cancer. CDKN2A is a tumor suppressor gene, not an oncogene.","Overexpression of the antiapoptotic protein BCL2 follows the t(14;18) translocation in follicular lymphoma."],"study":{"topics":["KRAS in pancreatic cancer","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"],"comps":["GI-13-K6","GI-13-B1"]}},{"id":"GI-C05-Q4","stem":"A biopsy specimen of her pancreas shows dense fibrosis with scattered remaining islets of Langerhans and a few residual acini. On hematoxylin and eosin staining, which of the following features identifies the enzyme-producing cells?","options":["Pale cords of cells arranged around fenestrated capillaries","Tall columnar cells with apical droplets of mucin","Cells with flattened basal nuclei and pale, foamy cytoplasm","Basophilic basal cytoplasm and apical eosinophilic granules"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that acinar cells make large amounts of secreted protein.","Link protein synthesis to abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum, which stains blue at the base of the cell.","Link stored enzyme to zymogen granules, which stain pink at the apex."],"why":"Pancreatic acinar cells are pyramidal cells built for protein export. Their basal cytoplasm is packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum, whose ribosomal RNA stains strongly basophilic, and their apical cytoplasm holds eosinophilic zymogen granules full of digestive enzyme precursors. Islets of Langerhans are pale-staining clusters of endocrine cells arranged in cords around a dense network of fenestrated capillaries. In chronic pancreatitis the islets tend to survive longer than the acini, which is why exocrine failure comes before diabetes.","whyNot":["Pale cords of cells around fenestrated capillaries describe islets of Langerhans, which release insulin and glucagon into the blood rather than enzymes into ducts.","Tall columnar cells with apical mucin describe goblet cells or mucous duct epithelium, not enzyme-secreting acini.","Flattened basal nuclei and pale, foamy cytoplasm describe mucous acini, such as those in the sublingual gland.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of the exocrine pancreas","Pancreatic islets"],"comps":["GI-2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C06","system":"GI","dx":"Alcohol-related hepatitis with a Mallory-Weiss tear","codes":["GI-13-C4"],"case":"A 51-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of repeated hematemesis after a night of heavy drinking and forceful retching. He has drunk about a pint of vodka daily for 20 years and has had 3 weeks of increasing jaundice, anorexia, and right upper quadrant pain. Temperature is 38.0 °C (100.4 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus and tender hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show leukocyte count 14,800/mm3 (normal 4500 to 11,000), AST 186 U/L (normal 10 to 40), ALT 72 U/L (normal 10 to 40), total bilirubin 8.4 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), and prothrombin time 17.5 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5). Endoscopy shows a 1.5-cm mucosal laceration at the gastroesophageal junction and no varices. Diagnosis: alcohol-related hepatitis with a Mallory-Weiss tear.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C06-Q1","stem":"A liver biopsy specimen from this patient is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Ballooned hepatocytes with Mallory-Denk bodies and neutrophils","Granulomatous destruction of small interlobular bile ducts","Ground-glass hepatocytes containing hepatitis B surface antigen","Portal lymphoid follicles with mild macrovesicular steatosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C4","obj":"GI-13-C.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize alcohol-related hepatitis: heavy drinking, fever, tender hepatomegaly, jaundice, leukocytosis, and an AST more than twice the ALT.","Recall the three histologic features of alcoholic hepatitis.","Match ballooning, Mallory-Denk bodies, and neutrophils to the key."],"why":"Alcohol-related liver disease moves from steatosis to alcoholic hepatitis to cirrhosis. In alcoholic hepatitis, injured hepatocytes swell (ballooning degeneration), damaged keratin filaments clump into eosinophilic Mallory-Denk bodies, and neutrophils gather around dying cells. An AST to ALT ratio above 2 is typical because alcohol injures mitochondria, which hold much of the AST, and because alcohol-related pyridoxal phosphate deficiency lowers ALT more than AST.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomatous destruction of small bile ducts is the lesion of primary biliary cholangitis, which mainly affects middle-aged women and causes cholestasis with a high alkaline phosphatase.","Ground-glass hepatocytes packed with HBsAg in their endoplasmic reticulum mark chronic hepatitis B infection, not alcohol injury.","Lymphoid follicles in portal tracts with mild steatosis are typical of chronic hepatitis C infection."],"study":{"topics":["Alcohol-related hepatitis histology","AST to ALT ratio"],"comps":["GI-13-C4","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C06-Q2","stem":"On hospital day 2, he becomes confused and has horizontal nystagmus, bilateral lateral rectus palsies, and an unsteady, wide-based gait. A deficiency of the cofactor for which of the following enzymes is most likely responsible?","options":["Aminolevulinate synthase in heme synthesis","Pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenases","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase in propionate metabolism","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases in collagen synthesis"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize Wernicke encephalopathy: confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia in a person with heavy alcohol use.","Identify the missing vitamin: thiamine, whose active form is thiamine pyrophosphate.","Recall which enzymes need thiamine pyrophosphate: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase, and transketolase."],"why":"Heavy alcohol use reduces thiamine intake, absorption, and storage. Thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, which feed and run the citric acid cycle, and for transketolase. Without it, neurons in high-demand areas such as the mammillary bodies and the region around the third ventricle cannot make enough ATP. Giving glucose without thiamine can make this worse, so thiamine is given first or with glucose.","whyNot":["Aminolevulinate synthase needs pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6). B6 deficiency, also common with alcohol use, causes sideroblastic anemia and seizures.","Correct.","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase needs vitamin B12. B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, which develop slowly.","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases need vitamin C. Scurvy causes bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhages, and poor wound healing."],"study":{"topics":["Thiamine and Wernicke encephalopathy","Vitamin cofactors"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C06-Q3","stem":"His prothrombin time stays prolonged after he receives parenteral vitamin K. Which of the following best explains the prolonged prothrombin time?","options":["Platelet sequestration in an enlarged spleen","Interference with the clotting assay by antiphospholipid antibodies","Reduced hepatocyte synthesis of clotting factors","Decreased release of von Willebrand factor from endothelium"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the prothrombin time measures the extrinsic and common pathways, factors VII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen, all made in the liver.","Use the failed vitamin K response: if vitamin K deficiency were the cause, the prothrombin time would correct.","Conclude that the injured hepatocytes cannot make enough clotting factor protein."],"why":"Hepatocytes make most clotting factors, including fibrinogen and factors II, V, VII, IX, and X. When liver cell function fails, factor levels fall, and the prothrombin time rises early because factor VII has the shortest half-life, about 4 to 6 hours. A prolonged prothrombin time that does not correct with vitamin K shows that the liver cannot make the proteins, rather than lacking the vitamin to activate them. Low serum albumin reflects the same loss of synthetic function, and failure of the urea cycle can also raise blood ammonia.","whyNot":["Low platelets cause mucosal bleeding and a long bleeding time, but they do not prolong the prothrombin time, which is measured in plasma.","Antiphospholipid antibodies mainly prolong the aPTT in the laboratory while causing clotting in the patient. They do not explain liver-related coagulopathy.","Correct.","Von Willebrand factor helps platelets adhere and carries factor VIII. Its deficiency prolongs the bleeding time and sometimes the aPTT, not the prothrombin time."],"study":{"topics":["Synthetic functions of the liver","Prothrombin time in liver disease"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C06-Q4","stem":"Six months later, after another episode of forceful vomiting, he has severe retrosternal chest pain, and crepitus is palpated in the soft tissues of the neck. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Herniation of the gastric fundus through the hiatus","Transmural rupture of the distal esophagus","Rupture of submucosal esophageal varices","Recurrent mucosal laceration at the gastroesophageal junction"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G","obj":"GI-13-G.12","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize crepitus in the neck as subcutaneous emphysema, which means air has escaped from a hollow organ.","Recall that a mucosal tear does not let air out, but a full-thickness tear does.","Identify Boerhaave syndrome: transmural rupture of the distal esophagus after forceful vomiting."],"why":"Mallory-Weiss tears are lacerations of the mucosa and submucosa at the gastroesophageal junction caused by retching. They bleed but usually heal on their own. Boerhaave syndrome is a full-thickness rupture, usually of the left posterolateral wall of the distal esophagus. Air and gastric contents escape into the mediastinum, causing severe chest pain, pneumomediastinum, and subcutaneous emphysema that can be felt in the neck. It is an emergency with a high death rate from mediastinitis.","whyNot":["A hiatal hernia can cause reflux and chest discomfort but does not release air into the soft tissues of the neck.","Correct.","Variceal rupture causes massive hematemesis in patients with portal hypertension. It does not let air into the tissues, and his endoscopy showed no varices.","Another Mallory-Weiss tear would cause hematemesis but no air leak, because the muscular wall stays intact."],"study":{"topics":["Mallory-Weiss versus Boerhaave syndrome","Esophageal rupture"],"comps":["GI-13-G","GI-13-J1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C07","system":"GI","dx":"Hereditary hemochromatosis with hepatocellular carcinoma","codes":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-K4"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of 2 months of right upper quadrant discomfort and a 7-kg (15-lb) weight loss. He has had type 2 diabetes mellitus for 6 years and chronic hand pain. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 80/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows grayish-bronze skin, a firm, nodular liver edge 4 cm below the right costal margin, and enlarged second and third metacarpophalangeal joints. Serum ferritin is 2850 ng/mL (normal 24 to 336), transferrin saturation is 82% (normal 20 to 50), and alpha-fetoprotein is 960 ng/mL (normal below 10). Hepatitis B and C serologies are negative. CT shows a cirrhotic liver with a 6-cm arterially enhancing right lobe mass. He is homozygous for HFE C282Y. Diagnosis: hereditary hemochromatosis with cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C07-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the excess iron in his body?","options":["High hepcidin, trapping iron in enterocytes and macrophages","Increased iron delivery from repeated transfusions of red blood cells","Loss of transferrin, leaving iron unbound in the plasma","Low hepcidin, allowing high ferroportin activity in enterocytes"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the body has no regulated way to excrete iron, so iron balance is set by absorption.","Recall that hepcidin from the liver controls absorption by degrading ferroportin, the iron exporter on enterocytes and macrophages.","Recognize that the HFE mutation lowers hepcidin, so ferroportin stays active and iron absorption stays high."],"why":"Hereditary hemochromatosis is autosomal recessive, and most cases are homozygous for the HFE C282Y mutation. HFE normally helps hepatocytes sense iron and make hepcidin. With low hepcidin, ferroportin on duodenal enterocytes keeps exporting iron into the blood even when stores are full. Over decades, iron builds up in the liver, pancreas, heart, skin, and joints, causing cirrhosis, diabetes, bronze skin, cardiomyopathy, arthropathy, and a greatly raised risk of hepatocellular carcinoma.","whyNot":["High hepcidin blocks iron export and traps iron in cells. This is the pattern of anemia of chronic disease, with low serum iron and low transferrin saturation.","Transfusional iron overload causes secondary hemochromatosis, as in thalassemia major. He has had no transfusions, and his genotype explains his disease.","Transferrin is not lost in hemochromatosis. His transferrin saturation is high because there is too much iron for the transferrin present.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hepcidin and ferroportin","Hereditary hemochromatosis"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C07-Q2","stem":"A 45-year-old farmer from a region where stored peanuts and maize are often contaminated with Aspergillus flavus also develops hepatocellular carcinoma. A characteristic mutation caused by the mold toxin is most likely found in which of the following genes?","options":["TP53, which arrests the cell cycle after DNA damage","APC, which targets beta-catenin for degradation","BRCA2, which repairs double-strand DNA breaks","KRAS, which relays growth factor signals as a GTPase"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-K4","obj":"GI-13-K.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the toxin: Aspergillus flavus makes aflatoxin B1.","Recall how it becomes a carcinogen: liver cytochrome P450 converts it to an epoxide that binds guanine in DNA.","Recall the signature result: a G to T change at codon 249 of TP53."],"why":"Aflatoxin B1 is a mycotoxin in moldy grain and nuts. Liver enzymes activate it to a reactive epoxide that forms DNA adducts at guanine, producing a characteristic G to T transversion at codon 249 of the TP53 tumor suppressor gene. Loss of p53 removes the checkpoint that halts damaged cells. Other major risk factors for hepatocellular carcinoma are chronic hepatitis B, which can cause cancer even without cirrhosis, hepatitis C, alcohol, and hemochromatosis. Alpha-fetoprotein is often raised, and the tumor spreads mainly through the blood.","whyNot":["Correct.","APC loss starts most colorectal cancers. Beta-catenin pathway mutations occur in some liver tumors, but they are not the aflatoxin signature.","BRCA2 mutations raise the risk of breast, ovarian, prostate, and pancreatic cancer. They are inherited, not caused by aflatoxin.","KRAS mutations drive pancreatic and many colorectal cancers. They are not the hallmark of aflatoxin exposure."],"study":{"topics":["Aflatoxin and TP53","Hepatocellular carcinoma risk factors"],"comps":["GI-13-K4","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C07-Q3","stem":"His 50-year-old brother is also homozygous for HFE C282Y but has normal iron studies. Ten years ago, the brother had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, in which food passes from a small gastric pouch directly into the jejunum. Which of the following best explains his normal iron stores?","options":["Food no longer passes through the terminal ileum","Colonic transit of dietary iron is faster","Intrinsic factor from the gastric body is lost","Food no longer passes through the duodenum"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the main site of iron absorption: the duodenum and upper jejunum.","Recognize that a Roux-en-Y bypass sends food past the duodenum.","Predict that less iron is absorbed, which offsets the effect of his mutation."],"why":"Dietary iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum. There, ferric iron is reduced to ferrous iron, taken up by DMT1, and exported to the blood by ferroportin. A Roux-en-Y bypass routes food from the gastric pouch to the jejunum, so it skips the duodenum, and the loss of gastric acid also reduces conversion of ferric to ferrous iron. Iron deficiency is a common late effect of this surgery, and here it offsets his genetic tendency to absorb too much iron. Folate is absorbed in the jejunum, and vitamin B12 and bile acids in the terminal ileum.","whyNot":["The terminal ileum absorbs vitamin B12 and bile acids, not iron, and a Roux-en-Y bypass does not exclude it.","Very little iron is absorbed in the colon, so faster colonic transit would not change his iron balance.","Intrinsic factor is needed for vitamin B12 absorption, not iron absorption.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Sites of nutrient absorption","Iron absorption"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-10"]}},{"id":"GI-C07-Q4","stem":"Three years before this diagnosis, he had fever, diarrhea, and severe right lower quadrant pain 4 days after eating undercooked pork. At surgery, the appendix was normal, and the mesenteric lymph nodes near the terminal ileum were enlarged and inflamed. A gram-negative rod was isolated from stool cultured at 25 °C (77 °F). Which of the following organisms was most likely responsible?","options":["Shigella dysenteriae","Escherichia coli O157:H7","Yersinia enterocolitica","Listeria monocytogenes"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Link the clues: undercooked pork, right lower quadrant pain mimicking appendicitis, mesenteric lymphadenitis, and growth at cold temperature.","Recognize Yersinia enterocolitica, a gram-negative rod that invades through Peyer patches of the terminal ileum.","Note that iron overload favors this iron-hungry organism."],"why":"Yersinia enterocolitica is a gram-negative rod spread by undercooked pork, unpasteurized milk, and contaminated water. It invades through M cells over Peyer patches in the terminal ileum and spreads to nearby mesenteric lymph nodes, causing pain in the right lower quadrant that can mimic appendicitis. It grows at refrigerator temperatures, which is used for cold enrichment in the laboratory. It depends on outside sources of iron, so patients with iron overload, such as in hemochromatosis, are at higher risk of severe infection.","whyNot":["Shigella causes bloody diarrhea with colitis spread person to person. It does not cause mesenteric adenitis mimicking appendicitis.","E. coli O157:H7 spreads in undercooked beef and makes Shiga toxin, causing bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Correct.","Listeria is a gram-positive rod spread by deli meats and soft cheeses. It also grows in the cold, but it causes sepsis and meningitis in newborns, pregnant women, and older adults, not mesenteric adenitis."],"study":{"topics":["Yersinia enterocolitica","Iron overload and infection"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C08","system":"GI","dx":"Wilson disease presenting as acute hepatitis with hemolysis","codes":["GI-13-C4"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of a 1-week history of jaundice and fatigue. Her school performance has declined, and a hand tremor began 6 months ago. She takes no medications. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 106/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 110/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus, hepatomegaly, and golden-brown corneal limbal rings on slit-lamp examination. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.9 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0), reticulocytes 9% (normal 0.5 to 2.5), a negative direct antiglobulin test, total bilirubin 11.6 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin 3.1 mg/dL (normal 0 to 0.3), AST 182 U/L and ALT 96 U/L (normal 10 to 40), ceruloplasmin 8 mg/dL (normal 20 to 40), and 24-hour urine copper 410 µg (normal below 40). Diagnosis: Wilson disease with hepatitis and hemolysis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C08-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her low serum ceruloplasmin concentration?","options":["Ceruloplasmin is consumed by binding free hemoglobin","Apoceruloplasmin lacks copper and is rapidly degraded","Ceruloplasmin leaks into the urine through damaged glomeruli","Defective intestinal copper export limits ceruloplasmin synthesis"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the gene: ATP7B, a copper-transporting ATPase in hepatocytes, mutated in Wilson disease (autosomal recessive).","Recall its two jobs: loading copper onto apoceruloplasmin and excreting excess copper into bile.","Predict that ceruloplasmin without copper is unstable and degraded, so serum ceruloplasmin falls while free copper rises."],"why":"ATP7B moves copper into the Golgi of hepatocytes, where it is built into ceruloplasmin, and into bile for excretion. When ATP7B fails, ceruloplasmin is secreted without copper and is quickly broken down, so serum ceruloplasmin is low. Copper that cannot leave in bile builds up in the liver, causing hepatitis and later cirrhosis. It then spills into the blood as free copper, which deposits in the cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings) and basal ganglia, is excreted in large amounts in urine, and damages red cell membranes to cause Coombs-negative hemolysis.","whyNot":["Free hemoglobin is bound by haptoglobin, which falls in hemolysis. Ceruloplasmin does not bind hemoglobin.","Correct.","Urinary loss of ceruloplasmin is not the cause. Her high urine copper reflects free copper, not lost ceruloplasmin.","Defective intestinal copper export is caused by ATP7A mutations in Menkes disease, which leads to copper deficiency, kinky hair, and low ceruloplasmin. She has copper overload, with high urine copper and corneal rings."],"study":{"topics":["Wilson disease and ATP7B","Copper metabolism"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C08-Q2","stem":"Copper is normally removed from the body in bile. In a different patient, obstruction at which of the following sites would most likely block both biliary copper excretion and drainage of the main pancreatic duct?","options":["Cystic duct","Minor duodenal papilla","Common hepatic duct","Ampulla of Vater"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Trace bile: canaliculi, bile ductules, right and left hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, then the common bile duct after the cystic duct joins.","Recall where the common bile duct meets the main pancreatic duct: the hepatopancreatic ampulla (of Vater), surrounded by the sphincter of Oddi.","Choose the site shared by both ducts."],"why":"Bile leaves the liver through the right and left hepatic ducts, which form the common hepatic duct at the porta hepatis. The cystic duct joins it to form the common bile duct, which runs behind the duodenum and through the head of the pancreas. The common bile duct joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens at the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum, controlled by the sphincter of Oddi. A gallstone or tumor at the ampulla therefore blocks both bile and pancreatic juice.","whyNot":["A stone in the cystic duct blocks only the gallbladder, causing biliary colic or cholecystitis. Bile from the liver still reaches the duodenum.","The minor papilla drains the accessory pancreatic duct, a minor route in most people. Bile does not pass through it.","Blockage of the common hepatic duct stops bile flow but lies above the pancreas, so pancreatic drainage is unaffected.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Biliary tree anatomy","Hepatopancreatic ampulla"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-D2"]}},{"id":"GI-C08-Q3","stem":"Before the copper studies returned, the differential diagnosis included autoimmune hepatitis. Which of the following findings would have most supported autoimmune hepatitis?","options":["Hypergammaglobulinemia and interface hepatitis with plasma cells","Centrilobular necrosis after a large acetaminophen ingestion","Steatohepatitis in a patient with obesity and insulin resistance","Hepatitis that began 2 months after starting isoniazid therapy"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C4","obj":"GI-13-C.9","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the profile of autoimmune hepatitis: usually young women, with autoantibodies such as ANA and anti-smooth muscle antibody.","Recall its laboratory and biopsy hallmarks: raised IgG and interface hepatitis rich in plasma cells.","Separate it from drug and toxin injury, which follows an exposure."],"why":"Autoimmune hepatitis is a T-cell mediated attack on hepatocytes, most common in young women. Serum IgG is high, and ANA or anti-smooth muscle antibodies are often present. Biopsy shows interface hepatitis, in which lymphocytes and many plasma cells spill from the portal tracts into the nearby hepatocytes. It responds to glucocorticoids. Drug and toxin hepatitis follows an exposure and has its own patterns, such as zone 3 necrosis from acetaminophen and hepatitis from isoniazid.","whyNot":["Correct.","Acetaminophen overdose causes centrilobular (zone 3) necrosis through its toxic metabolite NAPQI. It would point to drug injury, and she takes no medications.","Steatohepatitis with obesity and insulin resistance describes MASH, a metabolic liver disease, not an autoimmune one.","Isoniazid can cause hepatitis weeks to months after it is started. This would point to a drug cause."],"study":{"topics":["Autoimmune hepatitis","Drug and toxin hepatitis"],"comps":["GI-13-C4","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C08-Q4","stem":"Most of her serum bilirubin is unconjugated. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Inherited reduction in UGT1A1 gene transcription","Heme breakdown outpaces hepatic glucuronidation capacity","Bacterial deconjugation of bilirubin in the colon with reabsorption","Defective canalicular export of bilirubin glucuronides by MRP2"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize hemolysis: anemia, high reticulocyte count, and a negative direct antiglobulin test with copper overload.","Trace bilirubin: heme oxygenase and biliverdin reductase make unconjugated bilirubin, which travels on albumin to the liver for conjugation by UGT1A1.","Conclude that heavy heme breakdown, with an injured liver, produces more bilirubin than the liver can conjugate."],"why":"Red cell breakdown releases heme, which macrophages convert to biliverdin with heme oxygenase and then to unconjugated bilirubin with biliverdin reductase. Unconjugated bilirubin is insoluble in water and travels to the liver bound to albumin. Hepatocytes conjugate it with glucuronic acid using UGT1A1 and export it into bile through MRP2. In the gut, bacteria convert it to urobilinogen and stercobilin. Her copper-driven hemolysis makes bilirubin faster than her injured liver can conjugate it, so unconjugated bilirubin dominates.","whyNot":["Reduced UGT1A1 transcription is Gilbert syndrome, a harmless cause of mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia with stress or fasting. It does not cause anemia or a bilirubin this high.","Correct.","Gut bacteria can deconjugate some bilirubin, and a little is reabsorbed, which matters mainly in newborns. In adults most is reduced to urobilinogen and stercobilin, so this cannot produce her high unconjugated bilirubin.","Defective MRP2 is Dubin-Johnson syndrome, which raises conjugated bilirubin and gives the liver a black color."],"study":{"topics":["Bilirubin metabolism","Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C09","system":"GI","dx":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (PiZZ) with cirrhosis","codes":["GI-13-C2"],"case":"A 44-year-old man comes to the physician because of 3 months of increasing abdominal girth, ankle swelling, and exertional dyspnea. He has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 20 years and rarely drinks alcohol. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus, spider angiomas, palmar erythema, shifting dullness, and splenomegaly. Laboratory studies show albumin 2.9 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.0), total bilirubin 2.6 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), prothrombin time 15.8 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5), platelet count 88,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000), and alpha-1 antitrypsin 22 mg/dL (normal 100 to 200). Viral hepatitis serologies are negative. Chest CT shows panacinar emphysema, worst in the lower lobes. His genotype is PiZZ. Diagnosis: alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency with cirrhosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C09-Q1","stem":"His liver biopsy specimen shows round, PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules in periportal hepatocytes. Which of the following best explains the formation of these globules?","options":["Glycogen stored because of a debranching enzyme defect","Hyaline aggregates of damaged keratin intermediate filaments","Misfolded protein retained in the endoplasmic reticulum","Lysosomes filled with oxidized lipid from membrane injury"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the PiZZ variant: a single amino acid change (Glu342Lys) that makes alpha-1 antitrypsin misfold.","Recall what happens to misfolded secretory proteins: they polymerize and stay in the endoplasmic reticulum.","Use the stain: PAS-positive and resistant to diastase means glycoprotein, not glycogen."],"why":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin is a glycoprotein made in hepatocytes and secreted into the blood, where it inhibits neutrophil elastase. The Z variant misfolds and forms polymers that cannot leave the endoplasmic reticulum. These polymers collect as PAS-positive globules that diastase cannot digest, because they are glycoprotein rather than glycogen. The retained protein injures hepatocytes and can lead to cirrhosis, while the low serum level leaves the lung alveoli open to elastase, causing panacinar emphysema that smoking makes much worse.","whyNot":["Glycogen in storage diseases such as Cori disease is PAS positive but is digested by diastase.","Hyaline clumps of damaged keratin filaments are Mallory-Denk bodies, typical of alcoholic hepatitis.","Correct.","Lysosomes filled with oxidized lipid are lipofuscin, a brown wear-and-tear pigment that builds up with age."],"study":{"topics":["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","PAS with diastase stain"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C09-Q2","stem":"Which of the following cells is most directly responsible for the fibrous septa that have converted his liver to cirrhosis?","options":["Kupffer cells within the sinusoidal lumen","Periportal hepatocytes containing the abnormal protein","Hepatic stellate cells in the space of Disse","Fenestrated sinusoidal endothelial cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C2","obj":"GI-13-C.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that cirrhosis is bridging fibrosis with regenerative nodules.","Identify the cell that stores vitamin A in the space of Disse and turns into a collagen-producing myofibroblast after injury.","Name the hepatic stellate (Ito) cell."],"why":"Hepatic stellate cells sit in the space of Disse between hepatocytes and sinusoidal endothelium, where they normally store vitamin A. Chronic injury releases cytokines such as TGF-beta from Kupffer cells and damaged hepatocytes, which activate stellate cells into contractile myofibroblasts. These cells lay down type I and III collagen, forming septa that bridge portal tracts and central veins. Surviving hepatocytes regrow as nodules. The main causes of cirrhosis are alcohol, chronic hepatitis B and C, fatty liver disease, and metabolic diseases such as this one.","whyNot":["Kupffer cells are resident macrophages. They release cytokines that activate stellate cells, but they do not make the collagen.","Hepatocytes holding misfolded protein are injured and die, which drives the process, but hepatocytes do not deposit the fibrous septa.","Correct.","Sinusoidal endothelial cells lose their fenestrations in cirrhosis (capillarization), but they are not the main source of collagen."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic stellate cells","Pathogenesis of cirrhosis"],"comps":["GI-13-C2","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C09-Q3","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient results mainly from portal hypertension rather than from loss of hepatocyte function?","options":["Spider angiomas on the chest","Scleral icterus","Prolonged prothrombin time","Congestive splenomegaly"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C2","obj":"GI-13-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Sort the findings of cirrhosis into two groups: those from failing hepatocytes and those from high portal pressure.","Hepatocyte failure: jaundice, low albumin, coagulopathy, hyperestrogenism, and encephalopathy.","Portal hypertension: splenomegaly, ascites, varices, and caput medusae."],"why":"Cirrhosis causes two kinds of problems. Loss of hepatocyte function leads to jaundice, low albumin, a prolonged prothrombin time, and hepatic encephalopathy, and poor breakdown of estrogen causes spider angiomas, palmar erythema, gynecomastia, and testicular atrophy. Scarring also blocks blood flow through the liver and raises portal pressure. Blood backs up through the splenic vein, so the spleen enlarges and traps platelets, and collateral veins open in the esophagus, rectum, and abdominal wall.","whyNot":["Spider angiomas come from excess estrogen because the failing liver cannot break it down.","Jaundice reflects failing uptake, conjugation, and excretion of bilirubin by hepatocytes.","A prolonged prothrombin time reflects poor hepatocyte synthesis of clotting factors.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Findings in cirrhosis","Portal hypertension"],"comps":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"GI-C09-Q4","stem":"Blood from his enlarged spleen normally joins blood from the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck of the pancreas. Which of the following sequences describes the normal path of this blood after that point?","options":["Portal vein, sinusoids, central veins, hepatic veins, inferior vena cava","Portal vein, sinusoids, central veins, portal triads, inferior vena cava","Portal vein, central veins, sinusoids, hepatic veins, inferior vena cava","Portal vein, sinusoids, hepatic veins, central veins, inferior vena cava"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the splenic and superior mesenteric veins join behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein.","Follow portal blood into the liver: branches in the portal triads open into sinusoids, which run toward the central vein of each lobule.","Central veins drain into hepatic veins, which empty into the inferior vena cava."],"why":"The portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas from the splenic and superior mesenteric veins, with the inferior mesenteric vein usually draining into the splenic vein. It enters the liver at the porta hepatis and branches with the hepatic artery and bile ducts in the portal triads. Blood from both vessels mixes in the sinusoids, flows past hepatocytes toward the central vein, and leaves through the hepatic veins into the inferior vena cava. In cirrhosis, resistance in the sinusoids raises pressure all the way back to the spleen.","whyNot":["Correct.","Portal triads are where blood enters the lobule. Blood does not return to them after the central veins.","Central veins lie downstream of the sinusoids. Blood reaches them only after passing hepatocytes.","Central veins drain into the hepatic veins, not the other way around."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous system","Blood flow through the hepatic lobule"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C10","system":"GI","dx":"Carbon tetrachloride hepatotoxicity in an industrial worker","codes":["GI-13-C4"],"case":"A 36-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of a 2-day history of nausea, vomiting, and right upper quadrant pain, followed by yellowing of the eyes. Three days ago, she spilled carbon tetrachloride while degreasing metal parts in a poorly ventilated workshop. She drinks heavily on weekends. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 102/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 114/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus and tender hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show ALT 3850 U/L (normal 10 to 40), AST 4620 U/L (normal 10 to 40), total bilirubin 4.2 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), prothrombin time 18.0 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5), and creatinine 1.9 mg/dL (normal 0.6 to 1.1). Serum acetaminophen is undetectable, and viral hepatitis serologies are negative. Liver biopsy shows centrilobular necrosis with fatty change. Diagnosis: carbon tetrachloride hepatotoxicity.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the initial step in this solvent's injury to her hepatocytes?","options":["Direct dissolution of hepatocyte membranes by unchanged solvent","Glucuronidation to a reactive acyl glucuronide metabolite","Cytochrome P450 conversion to a trichloromethyl free radical","Immune attack after the solvent binds hepatocyte proteins as a hapten"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that carbon tetrachloride itself is fairly inert and must be activated by the liver.","Identify the activating enzyme: cytochrome P450, mainly CYP2E1, which forms the trichloromethyl radical.","Follow the damage: the radical starts lipid peroxidation of membranes."],"why":"Carbon tetrachloride is the classic example of free radical liver injury. Cytochrome P450, mostly CYP2E1 in zone 3 hepatocytes, converts it to the trichloromethyl free radical. The radical reacts with fatty acids in membranes and starts a chain of lipid peroxidation that damages the endoplasmic reticulum, mitochondria, and plasma membrane. Damage to the endoplasmic reticulum halts apoprotein synthesis, so triglyceride cannot be exported as lipoprotein, and fat collects. Severe injury ends in centrilobular necrosis. Alcohol induces CYP2E1, so heavy drinkers are more vulnerable.","whyNot":["Carbon tetrachloride can depress the nervous system as a solvent, but liver injury needs metabolic activation. Damage centered in zone 3, where CYP2E1 is concentrated, shows this.","Glucuronidation is a phase II reaction that usually detoxifies compounds. Acyl glucuronides form from carboxylic acid drugs, not from carbon tetrachloride.","Correct.","Hapten-driven immune injury causes some drug hepatitis that appears after a delay and often with rash or eosinophilia. Carbon tetrachloride injury is direct and dose-dependent."],"study":{"topics":["Carbon tetrachloride and free radical injury","Lipid peroxidation"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C10-Q2","stem":"Her physician notes that chronic heavy alcohol use also raises the risk of liver injury from acetaminophen. Which of the following best explains this added risk?","options":["More glucuronide conjugates that precipitate within bile canaliculi","More NAPQI formed by CYP2E1 and less glutathione to conjugate it","Higher glutathione levels that convert NAPQI into a toxic adduct","Slower CYP2E1 activity that prolongs exposure to the parent drug"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the usual fate of acetaminophen: most is safely conjugated with glucuronide or sulfate (phase II).","Recall that a small fraction is oxidized by CYP2E1 (phase I) to the toxic metabolite NAPQI, which glutathione conjugates and neutralizes.","Apply chronic alcohol: CYP2E1 is induced and glutathione stores are low, so more NAPQI is made and less is neutralized."],"why":"Phase I reactions, mainly by cytochrome P450 enzymes, add or expose reactive groups. Phase II reactions join the drug to glucuronide, sulfate, or glutathione so it can be excreted. Acetaminophen is mostly handled by glucuronidation and sulfation, and a small amount is oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI. Glutathione normally conjugates NAPQI. When glutathione runs out, NAPQI binds cell proteins and kills zone 3 hepatocytes. Chronic alcohol use induces CYP2E1 and lowers glutathione stores, so injury occurs at lower doses. N-acetylcysteine treats overdose by restoring glutathione.","whyNot":["Glucuronide conjugates are water soluble and excreted in urine and bile. They are the safe pathway, not the source of injury.","Correct.","Glutathione neutralizes NAPQI. Higher glutathione would protect the liver, which is why N-acetylcysteine is the antidote.","Chronic alcohol induces CYP2E1 rather than slowing it. The parent drug itself is not the toxic agent."],"study":{"topics":["Phase I and phase II metabolism","Acetaminophen toxicity"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C10-Q3","stem":"She progresses to acute liver failure and receives a liver transplant. Two weeks later, Doppler ultrasonography shows thrombosis of the hepatic artery of the graft, with normal portal venous flow. Which of the following structures in the graft is at greatest risk of ischemic necrosis?","options":["Periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes","Centrilobular (zone 3) hepatocytes","Sinusoidal Kupffer cells","Intrahepatic bile duct epithelium"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the liver's dual supply: most blood flow comes from the portal vein, the rest from the hepatic artery, and both mix in the sinusoids.","Recognize that with portal flow intact, hepatocytes still receive blood through the sinusoids.","Identify the structure supplied only by the hepatic artery: the bile ducts, through the peribiliary plexus."],"why":"The liver gets about 75% of its blood flow from the portal vein and about 25% from the hepatic artery, and the two sources each supply roughly half of its oxygen. Both empty into the sinusoids, so hepatocytes can survive on portal blood alone, which is why liver infarction is uncommon. The bile ducts are different. They are supplied only by arterial branches that form a plexus around them. After hepatic artery thrombosis, especially in a transplanted liver that has no collateral arteries, the bile ducts become ischemic and can necrose, leak, or narrow.","whyNot":["Zone 1 hepatocytes sit nearest the portal triad and receive the most oxygenated sinusoidal blood. With portal flow intact they are well supplied.","Zone 3 hepatocytes are the most sensitive to a general drop in hepatic blood flow or oxygen, as in shock. Here portal blood still reaches the sinusoids, and the bile ducts depend entirely on the artery.","Kupffer cells line the sinusoids and are bathed in portal blood. They are not at special risk.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dual blood supply of the liver","Arterial supply of the bile ducts"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C10-Q4","stem":"Her biopsy shows necrosis centered on the central veins, with surviving hepatocytes around the portal triads. Which of the following best explains why hepatocytes in the injured zone are most affected?","options":["Lowest oxygen tension and most cytochrome P450 activity","Most Kupffer cells, which release the toxic free radical","Highest oxygen tension and greatest glutathione content","Closest contact with portal blood carrying the absorbed toxin"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Picture the hepatic acinus: zone 1 lies next to the portal triad, and zone 3 surrounds the central vein.","Recall that blood loses oxygen as it flows from zone 1 to zone 3.","Recall that zone 3 hepatocytes hold the most cytochrome P450, so toxins that need activation do their damage there."],"why":"In the hepatic acinus, blood enters at the portal triad (zone 1) and flows through the sinusoids to the central vein (zone 3). Hepatocytes extract oxygen along the way, so zone 3 cells live at the lowest oxygen tension and are first to die in ischemia. Zone 3 cells also have the most cytochrome P450, including CYP2E1, so toxins that are activated by P450, such as carbon tetrachloride and acetaminophen, injure them most. Zone 1 cells, rich in oxygen, are the first hit by toxins that arrive directly in portal blood and need no activation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Kupffer cells are found throughout the sinusoids and do not activate carbon tetrachloride. The radical forms inside hepatocytes.","High oxygen and plenty of glutathione describe zone 1 hepatocytes, which survived here.","Zone 1 cells meet portal blood first. Toxins that need no activation injure zone 1 most."],"study":{"topics":["Zones of the hepatic acinus","Zone 3 vulnerability"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C11","system":"GI","dx":"Autoimmune hepatitis type 1","codes":["GI-13-C4"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of fatigue, joint aches, and yellow discoloration of her eyes. She takes levothyroxine for Hashimoto thyroiditis and no other medications or supplements, and she does not drink alcohol. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 82/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scleral icterus and mild tender hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 13.2 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0), ALT 642 U/L (normal 7 to 56), AST 588 U/L (normal 10 to 40), alkaline phosphatase 148 U/L (normal 40 to 130), total bilirubin 4.1 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), and serum IgG 2,650 mg/dL (normal 700 to 1,600). Serologic tests for hepatitis A, B, and C are negative. A liver biopsy is performed. Diagnosis: autoimmune hepatitis type 1.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C11-Q1","stem":"Serum autoantibody testing is performed. Antibodies against which of the following antigens are most likely to be present?","options":["Cytochrome P450 2D6","Filamentous actin","Tissue transglutaminase","Gastric H+/K+ ATPase"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the young woman with another autoimmune disease, high IgG, and a hepatitis pattern with negative viral serology: autoimmune hepatitis.","Recall that type 1 is marked by antinuclear and anti-smooth muscle antibodies, and type 2 by anti-LKM1.","Name the antigen that anti-smooth muscle antibodies recognize."],"why":"Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis is defined by antinuclear antibodies and anti-smooth muscle antibodies. Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mainly recognize filamentous actin (F-actin), which is why they are also called anti-actin antibodies. The antibodies are markers of the disease; the liver damage itself is driven mainly by T cells. A high serum IgG is another typical finding.","whyNot":["Anti-liver-kidney microsomal type 1 antibodies target cytochrome P450 2D6. They define autoimmune hepatitis type 2, which is seen mostly in children and adolescents.","Correct.","Antibodies to tissue transglutaminase mark celiac disease, which injures the small bowel and causes malabsorption.","Antibodies to the H+/K+ ATPase of parietal cells mark autoimmune gastritis, which causes atrophic body gastritis and pernicious anemia."],"study":{"topics":["Autoantibodies in autoimmune hepatitis","Type 1 versus type 2 autoimmune hepatitis"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-C4"]}},{"id":"GI-C11-Q2","stem":"Her physician considers drug-induced liver injury, but she has taken no acetaminophen, isoniazid, or other hepatotoxic drugs. Which of the following findings on the liver biopsy is most likely and best distinguishes her disease from acetaminophen toxicity?","options":["Interface hepatitis with portal plasma cell infiltrates","Centrilobular coagulative necrosis with little inflammation","Granulomatous destruction of interlobular bile ducts","Macrovesicular steatosis with Mallory-Denk bodies"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C4","obj":"GI-13-C.9","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize autoimmune hepatitis from the high IgG, other autoimmunity, and negative viral tests.","Recall its lesion: inflammation spilling from the portal tract into the lobule (interface hepatitis) rich in plasma cells.","Contrast this with acetaminophen injury, which is zone 3 necrosis with sparse inflammation."],"why":"Autoimmune hepatitis shows interface hepatitis: lymphocytes and many plasma cells cross the limiting plate and destroy periportal hepatocytes. The plasma cells match the high serum IgG. Acetaminophen and other toxic injuries kill zone 3 hepatocytes around the central vein and cause little inflammation. Isoniazid hepatitis looks like viral hepatitis on biopsy, so history and serology matter as well.","whyNot":["Correct.","Coagulative necrosis around the central vein (zone 3) with little inflammation is the pattern of acetaminophen toxicity. Zone 3 hepatocytes have the most cytochrome P450 2E1 and the least oxygen.","Granulomatous destruction of small interlobular bile ducts is the lesion of primary biliary cholangitis, which causes a cholestatic pattern with a high alkaline phosphatase.","Large fat droplets with Mallory-Denk bodies point to alcohol-related or metabolic liver disease. She does not drink, and her pattern is a high-transaminase hepatitis."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of autoimmune hepatitis","Acetaminophen zone 3 necrosis"],"comps":["GI-13-C4","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C11-Q3","stem":"Urinalysis shows bilirubin and an increased concentration of urobilinogen. Which of the following best explains the increased urine urobilinogen in this patient?","options":["Accelerated heme breakdown from red cell hemolysis","Complete obstruction of bile flow into the duodenum","Renal conversion of filtered conjugated bilirubin","Impaired hepatic reuptake of absorbed urobilinogen"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gut bacteria make urobilinogen, some of which is absorbed into portal blood and normally taken back up by the liver.","Hepatocyte injury reduces that reuptake, so more urobilinogen passes into the systemic blood and urine.","The bilirubin in her urine shows a conjugated component, which fits liver injury and argues against pure hemolysis."],"why":"Bile still reaches the gut in hepatocellular injury, so bacteria keep making urobilinogen. Part of it is absorbed into portal blood, and a healthy liver takes most of it back up and re-excretes it in bile. Injured hepatocytes clear it poorly, so more spills into the urine. Conjugated bilirubin leaking from damaged cells is water soluble and also appears in the urine. Hemolysis raises urobilinogen without urine bilirubin, and complete obstruction makes urobilinogen disappear.","whyNot":["Hemolysis raises urine urobilinogen by increasing bilirubin production, but urine bilirubin stays negative because the extra bilirubin is unconjugated. Her hemoglobin is normal and her urine contains bilirubin.","Complete obstruction stops bilirubin from reaching the gut, so no urobilinogen is formed and urine urobilinogen falls to zero.","Urobilinogen is made by gut bacteria from bilirubin in the intestinal lumen. The kidney does not convert bilirubin to urobilinogen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Urine bilirubin and urobilinogen patterns","Enterohepatic circulation of urobilinogen"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C11-Q4","stem":"Her prothrombin time is 18 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5), and the platelet count is 240,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000). The prothrombin time does not change after an intravenous dose of vitamin K. Which of the following best explains the prolonged prothrombin time?","options":["Reduced hepatocyte synthesis of clotting factors","Consumption of clotting factors in disseminated coagulation","Impaired micellar absorption of vitamin K from the gut","Circulating autoantibody inhibitor directed at factor VIII"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.5","dok":3,"approach":["The prothrombin time depends mostly on factor VII, plus factors X, V, II, and fibrinogen, all made by hepatocytes.","Separate a vitamin K problem from a synthesis problem by giving vitamin K by vein: only a vitamin K deficit corrects.","No correction with a normal platelet count means the liver cannot make the factors."],"why":"Hepatocytes make almost all clotting factors. Factor VII has the shortest half-life, about 6 hours, so the prothrombin time rises within a day or so of severe liver injury. Giving vitamin K by vein corrects a prolonged prothrombin time caused by poor absorption, but not one caused by failing hepatocytes. A prothrombin time that does not correct marks severe hepatocyte dysfunction, along with a low albumin and a high ammonia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Disseminated intravascular coagulation consumes factors and platelets together. Her platelet count is normal, and nothing suggests a trigger.","Poor absorption of vitamin K would be corrected by giving vitamin K intravenously, which bypasses the gut. Her prothrombin time did not change.","Factor VIII is in the intrinsic pathway, so an inhibitor prolongs the PTT, not the prothrombin time."],"study":{"topics":["Synthetic function of the liver","Prothrombin time in liver failure"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C12","system":"GI","dx":"Primary biliary cholangitis","codes":["GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of an 8-month history of generalized itching that is worse at night, and fatigue. She also has dry eyes and a dry mouth. She does not drink alcohol and takes no medications. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows excoriations on the arms and back, yellow plaques on both upper eyelids, and mild hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show alkaline phosphatase 486 U/L (normal 40 to 130), GGT 390 U/L (normal 9 to 48), ALT 68 U/L (normal 7 to 56), total bilirubin 1.4 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), and total cholesterol 310 mg/dL (desirable below 200). Ultrasonography shows normal extrahepatic bile ducts without dilation. Serologic tests for autoantibodies are sent. Diagnosis: primary biliary cholangitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C12-Q1","stem":"A liver biopsy is performed. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Bile ductular proliferation with bile lakes and portal edema","Concentric onion-skin fibrosis around medium-sized bile ducts","Granulomatous destruction of small interlobular bile ducts","Periportal hepatocytes with PAS-positive cytoplasmic globules"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C1","obj":"GI-13-C.2","dok":2,"approach":["A middle-aged woman with pruritus, a high alkaline phosphatase, and normal extrahepatic ducts has intrahepatic cholestasis.","Primary biliary cholangitis targets the small interlobular ducts inside the portal tracts.","Its signature lesion is lymphocytic and granulomatous destruction of these ducts (the florid duct lesion)."],"why":"Primary biliary cholangitis is an autoimmune attack on small intrahepatic bile ducts. Lymphocytes and sometimes granulomas surround and destroy the interlobular ducts, and the ducts slowly disappear (ductopenia). Primary sclerosing cholangitis is different: it affects larger intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts with onion-skin fibrosis, is linked to ulcerative colitis, and raises the risk of cholangiocarcinoma. Her dry eyes and mouth fit the Sjogren syndrome that often accompanies primary biliary cholangitis.","whyNot":["Ductular proliferation, portal edema, and bile lakes are the picture of large duct (extrahepatic) obstruction. Her ultrasound shows no duct dilation.","Concentric onion-skin fibrosis around larger ducts is the lesion of primary sclerosing cholangitis, which usually affects men with ulcerative colitis and causes beaded ducts on cholangiography.","Correct.","PAS-positive globules in periportal hepatocytes are misfolded alpha-1 antitrypsin, seen in alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Primary biliary versus primary sclerosing cholangitis"],"comps":["GI-13-C1","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C12-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her serum cholesterol concentration and eyelid plaques?","options":["Defective LDL receptor-mediated uptake by hepatocytes","Increased intestinal absorption of dietary cholesterol","Retention of cholesterol normally excreted in bile","Loss of lipoprotein lipase activity in capillary beds"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C1","obj":"GI-13-C.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cholestatic pattern: high alkaline phosphatase and GGT, pruritus, normal extrahepatic ducts.","Recall that bile is the body's main route for removing cholesterol, as free cholesterol and as bile acids.","When bile flow fails, biliary lipids back up into blood and are deposited in skin as xanthomas."],"why":"The liver gets rid of cholesterol by secreting it into bile, both unchanged and after converting it to bile acids. In cholestasis this outflow is blocked, and biliary cholesterol and phospholipid reflux into the blood, partly as an abnormal particle called lipoprotein X. Serum cholesterol rises and is deposited in the skin as xanthelasma and xanthomas. A raised GGT along with the alkaline phosphatase confirms the alkaline phosphatase comes from the liver, not bone. Retained bile acids also contribute to the itching.","whyNot":["LDL receptor defects (familial hypercholesterolemia) cause high LDL from birth with tendon xanthomas. Her cholesterol rose with a cholestatic liver disease.","Cholesterol absorption needs bile salt micelles. Cholestasis lowers intestinal bile salts, so absorption falls rather than rises.","Correct.","Lipoprotein lipase deficiency raises chylomicrons and triglycerides and causes eruptive xanthomas and pancreatitis, not cholestatic hypercholesterolemia."],"study":{"topics":["Laboratory pattern of cholestasis","Xanthomas in cholestasis"],"comps":["GI-13-C1","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C12-Q3","stem":"Her disease progresses over several years with persistent cholestasis. Which of the following is she at greatest risk of developing as a result of reduced bile flow?","options":["Perifollicular hemorrhages","Impaired dark adaptation","Angular cheilitis and glossitis","Megaloblastic anemia"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Bile salts form micelles that carry dietary fat and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K to the enterocyte.","Chronic cholestasis therefore causes deficiency of these four vitamins, not of water-soluble vitamins.","Match the deficiency to its sign: vitamin A deficiency impairs rhodopsin regeneration, causing night blindness."],"why":"Vitamins A, D, E, and K dissolve in fat and are absorbed inside bile salt micelles. Low bile flow in long-standing cholestasis causes fat malabsorption and deficiency of all four. Vitamin A is needed to make rhodopsin, so deficiency first shows as poor vision in dim light. The other deficiencies are osteomalacia (D), hemolytic anemia with neuropathy (E), and a prolonged prothrombin time (K). Water-soluble vitamins are absorbed without micelles.","whyNot":["Perifollicular hemorrhages are a sign of scurvy (vitamin C deficiency). Vitamin C is water soluble and absorbed without bile.","Correct.","Angular cheilitis and glossitis point to riboflavin or other B vitamin deficiency, which does not depend on bile salts.","Megaloblastic anemia comes from folate or vitamin B12 deficiency. These water-soluble vitamins do not need bile for absorption; B12 needs intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum."],"study":{"topics":["Fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption","Vitamin A and night blindness"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C12-Q4","stem":"The autoantibody that is most characteristic of her disease is detected. Which of the following is the target antigen of this antibody?","options":["Thyroid peroxidase in follicular cells","Intrinsic factor secreted by gastric parietal cells","Double-stranded DNA in cell nuclei","E2 subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["The characteristic antibody in primary biliary cholangitis is antimitochondrial antibody, present in about 95% of patients.","Recall its target: the E2 component of pyruvate dehydrogenase, an enzyme of the inner mitochondrial membrane.","Rule out antibodies that belong to other autoimmune diseases."],"why":"Antimitochondrial antibodies are the serologic hallmark of primary biliary cholangitis. Their main target is the E2 subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDC-E2). Bile duct epithelial cells display this antigen in a form that attracts an immune attack, which is thought to explain why the damage centers on small bile ducts. A positive antimitochondrial antibody with a cholestatic pattern is usually enough for the diagnosis without biopsy.","whyNot":["Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies mark Hashimoto thyroiditis. They can occur with other autoimmune diseases but are not characteristic of this one.","Antibodies to intrinsic factor are found in autoimmune gastritis and cause vitamin B12 malabsorption and pernicious anemia.","Anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies are specific for systemic lupus erythematosus and track with lupus nephritis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Antimitochondrial antibody","Autoantibodies in GI disease"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-C1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C13","system":"GI","dx":"Gilbert syndrome unmasked by fasting","codes":["GI-13-C3"],"case":"A 19-year-old man comes to the physician because his roommate noticed that his eyes looked yellow. Over the past 3 days he had a febrile respiratory illness and ate very little. He has no abdominal pain, and his urine is normal in color. He takes no medications and does not drink alcohol. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows mild scleral icterus and no hepatosplenomegaly. Laboratory studies show total bilirubin 3.2 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin 0.2 mg/dL (normal 0 to 0.3), hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5), and a reticulocyte count of 1% (normal 0.5 to 2.5). Haptoglobin, LDH, ALT, AST, and alkaline phosphatase are within normal limits. Diagnosis: Gilbert syndrome unmasked by fasting.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C13-Q1","stem":"His newborn nephew develops severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia that persists despite phototherapy and progresses to kernicterus. The nephew is found to have Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I. Which of the following best explains the difference in severity between the two conditions?","options":["Normal versus accelerated red cell destruction","Normal versus defective canalicular MRP2 transport","Partial versus absent hepatic UGT1A1 activity","Normal versus deficient biliverdin reductase activity"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C3","obj":"GI-13-C.6","dok":3,"approach":["Both patients have isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia without hemolysis, so the problem is conjugation.","UGT1A1 attaches glucuronic acid to bilirubin. Gilbert syndrome has mildly reduced activity; Crigler-Najjar type I has none.","Relate the amount of residual enzyme to how high bilirubin rises and whether it reaches the brain."],"why":"Gilbert syndrome is common and benign. Reduced expression of UGT1A1, often from a promoter variant, leaves roughly a third of normal activity, so bilirubin rises only mildly and mainly during fasting, stress, or illness. Crigler-Najjar type I has no UGT1A1 activity, so unconjugated bilirubin climbs high in the newborn period, crosses the immature blood-brain barrier, and causes kernicterus. Crigler-Najjar type II has partial deficiency and a milder course.","whyNot":["Neither condition involves hemolysis. Both are defects in bilirubin conjugation; this patient's hemoglobin, reticulocyte count, haptoglobin, and LDH are normal.","MRP2 moves conjugated bilirubin into bile. Its defect causes Dubin-Johnson syndrome, a conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, which neither patient has.","Correct.","Biliverdin reductase converts biliverdin to bilirubin. A deficiency would lower bilirubin production, not cause unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia."],"study":{"topics":["Gilbert versus Crigler-Najjar syndromes","UGT1A1 function"],"comps":["GI-13-C3","GI-9"]}},{"id":"GI-C13-Q2","stem":"His urine remains normal in color despite the hyperbilirubinemia. Which of the following describes the form of his excess bilirubin in plasma that best explains this finding?","options":["Conjugated to glucuronate and loosely bound to albumin","Free in plasma and reabsorbed by proximal tubules","Bound to haptoglobin and cleared by macrophages","Bound to albumin and not filtered"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["His bilirubin is almost all indirect (unconjugated), since the direct fraction is normal.","Unconjugated bilirubin is water insoluble and travels in plasma tightly bound to albumin.","The albumin-bilirubin complex is too large to pass the glomerular filter, so no bilirubin enters the urine."],"why":"Macrophages break heme into biliverdin with heme oxygenase, and biliverdin reductase converts it to unconjugated bilirubin. Unconjugated bilirubin is not water soluble, so it is carried to the liver bound to albumin and cannot be filtered by the kidney. That is why jaundice from unconjugated bilirubin is called acholuric. After hepatocytes conjugate it with UGT1A1, MRP2 pumps it into bile, and gut bacteria turn it into urobilinogen and stercobilin.","whyNot":["Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and only loosely bound to albumin, so when it is high it is filtered and appears in urine. His excess bilirubin is unconjugated.","Unconjugated bilirubin in plasma is almost entirely bound to albumin. It is not filtered and then reabsorbed.","Haptoglobin binds free hemoglobin, not bilirubin. Hemoglobin-haptoglobin complexes are cleared by macrophages.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Bilirubin transport on albumin","Bilirubin metabolism pathway"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C3"]}},{"id":"GI-C13-Q3","stem":"Once his bilirubin is conjugated, hepatocytes secrete it into bile canaliculi. Within the hepatic lobule, which of the following best describes the flow of this bile?","options":["Into the sinusoid lumen, then to the central vein","Toward the portal triad, opposite to sinusoidal blood flow","Toward the central vein, in the same direction as sinusoidal blood flow","Into the space of Disse, then into hepatic lymphatic vessels"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Picture the classic lobule: portal triads at the corners and a central vein in the middle.","Blood from the portal vein and hepatic artery flows through sinusoids from the triad to the central vein.","Canaliculi between hepatocytes carry bile in the opposite direction, through the canals of Hering to the bile ductules of the portal triad."],"why":"In the hepatic lobule, blood enters at the portal triad and flows through sinusoids to the central vein. Bile canaliculi, formed by grooves between adjacent hepatocytes and sealed by tight junctions, carry bile the other way. They drain through the canals of Hering into the bile ductules of the portal triad. The two flows run in opposite directions and never mix in health.","whyNot":["Bile is kept apart from blood by tight junctions around the canaliculi, and it drains toward the portal triad rather than into the sinusoids.","Correct.","Blood moves from the portal triad toward the central vein. Bile moves the other way, so it does not travel with the blood toward the central vein.","The space of Disse lies between hepatocytes and sinusoidal endothelium and drains to lymphatics, but it carries plasma filtrate, not bile."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic lobule organization","Bile canaliculi and canals of Hering"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C13-Q4","stem":"During his 3 days of poor intake, his serum beta-hydroxybutyrate concentration rose. Which of the following best explains why his liver, which produced these ketone bodies, did not oxidize them for its own energy?","options":["Ketone bodies cannot cross the hepatocyte plasma membrane","Hepatocytes lack succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase","Hepatic mitochondria lack carnitine palmitoyltransferase I","Hepatocytes lack mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase activity"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["In fasting, the liver oxidizes fatty acids to acetyl-CoA and turns the excess into acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate.","To use acetoacetate, a tissue must first convert it to acetoacetyl-CoA with succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (thiophorase).","The liver lacks this enzyme, so it exports ketones to other tissues."],"why":"In fasting, liver glycogen runs low within about a day, and blood glucose then depends on gluconeogenesis from alanine, lactate, and glycerol. At the same time, the liver oxidizes fatty acids and converts the extra acetyl-CoA into ketone bodies. Muscle, heart, and brain use ketones after converting acetoacetate to acetoacetyl-CoA with thiophorase. The liver lacks thiophorase, so it can make ketones but not use them, and they are exported as fuel.","whyNot":["Ketone bodies move freely across cell membranes. That is how they leave the liver for muscle and brain.","Correct.","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I brings fatty acids into hepatic mitochondria. The liver needs it to make ketones in the first place.","Hepatocytes have mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase. It is the rate-limiting enzyme of ketone body synthesis, which is why the liver can make ketones."],"study":{"topics":["Ketogenesis and ketone use","Fed and fasting metabolism"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C14","system":"GI","dx":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome","codes":["GI-13-C"],"case":"A 26-year-old woman comes to the physician because of yellow eyes for 4 weeks. She has had brief episodes of mild jaundice since adolescence. Three months ago she started an oral contraceptive. She has no pruritus or abdominal pain and does not drink alcohol. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows mild scleral icterus; the liver and spleen are not enlarged. Laboratory studies show total bilirubin 3.8 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin 2.6 mg/dL (normal 0 to 0.3), and a hemoglobin concentration of 13.6 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0). ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase, and GGT are within normal limits. Ultrasonography shows a normal gallbladder and no bile duct dilation. A liver biopsy is performed. Diagnosis: Dubin-Johnson syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C14-Q1","stem":"Which of the following biopsy findings is most likely and distinguishes her condition from Rotor syndrome?","options":["Coarse dark pigment within hepatocyte lysosomes","Hemosiderin granules in periportal hepatocytes and bile ducts","Copper-associated protein granules in periportal hepatocytes","Normal hepatocyte morphology with no pigment accumulation"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C","obj":"GI-13-C.11","dok":2,"approach":["Isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal liver enzymes and ducts suggests an inherited excretion defect.","Dubin-Johnson syndrome (MRP2 defect) and Rotor syndrome look alike in the blood.","Only Dubin-Johnson syndrome leaves dark pigment in the liver."],"why":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome is an autosomal recessive defect in MRP2, the canalicular pump that moves conjugated bilirubin and other organic anions into bile. Conjugated bilirubin backs up into the blood. A dark, melanin-like pigment collects in hepatocyte lysosomes and makes the liver look black. Rotor syndrome causes a similar but milder conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with a normal, unpigmented liver. Estrogens and pregnancy reduce biliary excretion further, which is why her jaundice worsened on an oral contraceptive.","whyNot":["Correct.","Hemosiderin in periportal hepatocytes is the pattern of hereditary hemochromatosis, which raises transaminases and ferritin rather than causing isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.","Periportal copper and copper-associated protein are seen in Wilson disease and in long-standing cholestasis. Neither fits her isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal enzymes.","A normal, unpigmented liver is the finding in Rotor syndrome, the milder look-alike of Dubin-Johnson syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Dubin-Johnson versus Rotor syndrome","MRP2 and canalicular transport"],"comps":["GI-13-C","GI-9"]}},{"id":"GI-C14-Q2","stem":"Which of the following urinalysis results is most likely in this patient?","options":["Bilirubin absent; urobilinogen increased","Bilirubin absent; urobilinogen normal","Bilirubin present; urobilinogen absent","Bilirubin present; urobilinogen normal"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Her raised bilirubin is mostly direct (conjugated), and conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and filtered at the glomerulus, so urine bilirubin is present.","Urobilinogen depends on bilirubin reaching the gut. Her ducts are open, so bacteria still make urobilinogen.","With normal liver cells to reclaim it, urine urobilinogen stays about normal."],"why":"Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and only loosely bound to albumin, so it passes into the urine when its blood level rises. In Dubin-Johnson syndrome bile still reaches the intestine, and hepatocytes are otherwise healthy, so urobilinogen formation and reuptake are about normal. This combination separates it from hemolysis (no urine bilirubin, high urobilinogen) and complete obstruction (urine bilirubin with absent urobilinogen).","whyNot":["No urine bilirubin with high urobilinogen is the pattern of hemolysis, in which the extra bilirubin is unconjugated. Her hemoglobin is normal and her bilirubin is mostly direct.","Absent urine bilirubin fits normal people and unconjugated causes such as Gilbert syndrome. Her bilirubin is mostly conjugated.","Bilirubin with no urobilinogen means bile is not reaching the gut, as in complete obstruction. Her ducts are normal and bile still flows.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Urine findings in conjugated hyperbilirubinemia"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C"]}},{"id":"GI-C14-Q3","stem":"Her normal ultrasound argued against biliary obstruction. Progressive obstruction by a tumor at which of the following sites would cause jaundice together with a distended gallbladder?","options":["Common hepatic duct proximal to the cystic duct junction","Cystic duct at the neck of the gallbladder","Distal common bile duct within the pancreatic head","Right hepatic duct at the porta hepatis"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.3","dok":3,"approach":["Trace bile: right and left hepatic ducts to the common hepatic duct, which joins the cystic duct to form the common bile duct.","The common bile duct runs through the head of the pancreas to the ampulla of Vater, where it joins the pancreatic duct under the sphincter of Oddi.","Only a block below the cystic duct junction both stops all bile flow and lets bile back up into the gallbladder."],"why":"Bile leaves the liver through the right and left hepatic ducts at the porta hepatis, which join as the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct from the gallbladder joins it to form the common bile duct. This duct passes behind the duodenum and through the pancreatic head to the ampulla of Vater. Slow obstruction of the distal common bile duct, as by pancreatic head cancer, blocks all bile flow and lets the gallbladder fill and distend. This is the basis of Courvoisier sign.","whyNot":["Obstruction of the common hepatic duct above the cystic duct causes jaundice, but bile cannot back up into the gallbladder, so it stays small.","Cystic duct obstruction distends the gallbladder, but bile from the liver still flows down the common bile duct, so there is no jaundice.","Correct.","Blocking one hepatic duct leaves the other lobe draining normally, so jaundice usually does not occur, and the gallbladder is not distended."],"study":{"topics":["Biliary tree anatomy","Sites of bile duct obstruction"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C14-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["Normal albumin, normal PT, normal ammonia","Normal albumin, prolonged PT, high ammonia","Low albumin, normal PT, normal ammonia","Low albumin, prolonged PT, high ammonia"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.5","dok":2,"approach":["Dubin-Johnson syndrome affects one transport step: moving conjugated bilirubin into bile.","Synthetic and metabolic work of the liver (albumin, clotting factors, urea cycle) is untouched.","So albumin, prothrombin time, and ammonia stay normal, unlike in liver failure."],"why":"The liver makes albumin and most clotting factors and removes ammonia by turning it into urea. When hepatocytes fail, albumin falls, the prothrombin time lengthens, and ammonia rises, leading to edema, bleeding, and encephalopathy. Dubin-Johnson syndrome is a single transporter defect, so these functions are normal. This is why it is a benign condition that needs no treatment.","whyNot":["Correct.","A prolonged PT with high ammonia but normal albumin can occur early in acute liver failure because albumin has a long half-life. Her normal transaminases rule out such injury.","Low albumin with normal liver function suggests protein loss in the urine or gut, or malnutrition, not an inherited excretion defect.","Low albumin, a prolonged PT, and high ammonia together signal failing hepatocytes, as in cirrhosis or acute liver failure. Her hepatocytes are otherwise healthy."],"study":{"topics":["Liver synthetic and metabolic function","Benign inherited hyperbilirubinemias"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C15","system":"GI","dx":"Alcohol-related cirrhosis with spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and hepatorenal syndrome","codes":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-I1","GI-13-I2"],"case":"A 58-year-old man with a 30-year history of heavy alcohol use is brought to the emergency department because of a 1-week history of increasing abdominal girth, fever, mild diffuse abdominal pain, and decreased urine output. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jaundice, spider angiomas, gynecomastia, and tense ascites with mild diffuse tenderness; there is no rigidity or guarding. Serum albumin is 2.4 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5), and creatinine is 2.6 mg/dL (normal 0.7 to 1.3; 0.9 three months ago). Urine sodium is below 10 mEq/L, and urinalysis shows no protein, blood, or casts. Paracentesis shows ascitic albumin 0.8 g/dL and 620 neutrophils/mm3. Diagnosis: alcohol-related cirrhosis with spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and hepatorenal syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C15-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the serum-ascites albumin gradient in this patient?","options":["High sinusoidal pressure from portal hypertension","Exudation of protein from inflamed peritoneum","Peritoneal seeding by metastatic carcinoma","Low oncotic pressure from glomerular albumin loss"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-I1","obj":"GI-13-I.1","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate the gradient: serum albumin 2.4 minus ascitic albumin 0.8 equals 1.6 g/dL.","A gradient of 1.1 g/dL or more means the fluid formed from high hydrostatic pressure in the portal circulation.","In cirrhosis, fibrosis raises sinusoidal pressure; low albumin and sodium retention add to fluid build-up."],"why":"The serum-ascites albumin gradient reflects the hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid out of the sinusoids. A value of 1.1 g/dL or more indicates portal hypertension, as in cirrhosis, Budd-Chiari syndrome, or heart failure. Ascites in cirrhosis forms from high sinusoidal pressure plus low oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia. Splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial volume, which activates renin-angiotensin-aldosterone and makes the kidney retain sodium and water.","whyNot":["Correct.","Peritoneal inflammation, as in tuberculous peritonitis, adds protein to the fluid and gives a gradient below 1.1 g/dL. Infection of portal hypertensive ascites does not change the gradient much.","Peritoneal carcinomatosis makes a protein-rich exudate with a gradient below 1.1 g/dL.","Nephrotic syndrome causes ascites through low oncotic pressure, but the gradient is below 1.1 g/dL. His urinalysis shows no protein."],"study":{"topics":["Serum-ascites albumin gradient","Mechanism of ascites in cirrhosis"],"comps":["GI-13-I1","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"GI-C15-Q2","stem":"Culture of the ascitic fluid grows Escherichia coli only. Which of the following best explains how this organism most likely reached the ascitic fluid?","options":["Direct inoculation during an earlier paracentesis","Rupture of a pyogenic abscess through the liver capsule","Translocation of gut bacteria into lymph and blood","Leakage of colonic flora through a perforated diverticulum"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-I2","obj":"GI-13-I.2","dok":3,"approach":["Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is infection of ascites with no surgical source, diagnosed by 250 or more neutrophils/mm3.","It is usually one enteric gram-negative organism, most often E. coli or Klebsiella.","In cirrhosis, gut bacteria cross a leaky bowel wall into mesenteric lymph and blood and seed ascites that has poor opsonic activity."],"why":"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis develops in cirrhotic ascites without a perforation or abscess. Bacterial overgrowth, a leaky gut wall, and impaired immune defenses let gut bacteria move into mesenteric lymph nodes and the blood. Ascites with a low protein level has little complement and opsonic activity, so the bacteria grow. Secondary peritonitis comes from a perforated or inflamed viscus, is polymicrobial, and causes a rigid abdomen. SBP is a common trigger of hepatorenal syndrome.","whyNot":["Infection from a needle puncture is rare and would more likely involve skin flora such as staphylococci, not a single enteric gram-negative rod.","A ruptured liver abscess is a cause of secondary peritonitis and would usually show an abscess on imaging and often mixed organisms.","Correct.","A perforated viscus causes secondary peritonitis: polymicrobial flora including anaerobes, and a rigid abdomen with guarding. He has a single organism and no rigidity."],"study":{"topics":["Spontaneous versus secondary peritonitis","Bacterial translocation"],"comps":["GI-13-I2","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C15-Q3","stem":"His creatinine does not improve after 2 days of intravenous albumin, and kidney ultrasonography is normal. Which of the following best explains his decline in renal function?","options":["Immune complex glomerulonephritis from bacterial antigens","Renal vasoconstriction driven by splanchnic vasodilation","Allergic interstitial nephritis with eosinophiluria","Ischemic acute tubular necrosis with shed epithelial casts"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-C2","obj":"GI-13-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Kidney failure in advanced cirrhosis with low urine sodium, bland urine, and no response to volume expansion is hepatorenal syndrome.","Portal hypertension causes splanchnic arterial vasodilation (nitric oxide), which lowers effective arterial blood volume.","The body responds with renin-angiotensin, sympathetic, and vasopressin activation that intensely constricts renal arteries."],"why":"Hepatorenal syndrome is a functional kidney failure. Splanchnic vessels dilate in portal hypertension, so the effective arterial volume falls even though total body fluid is high. Strong renin-angiotensin, sympathetic, and vasopressin activity then constricts the renal arteries and lowers the glomerular filtration rate. The kidneys are normal on histology and recover after liver transplant. Low urine sodium shows the tubules still work.","whyNot":["Glomerulonephritis would show hematuria, red cell casts, or protein in the urine. His urinalysis is clean.","Correct.","Interstitial nephritis is usually a reaction to a drug and shows white cells and white cell casts in the urine. Nothing suggests a drug exposure, and his urinalysis is bland.","Acute tubular necrosis damages tubules, so urine sodium is high and the urine contains muddy brown casts. His urine sodium is below 10 mEq/L and the sediment is bland."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatorenal syndrome","Splanchnic vasodilation in cirrhosis"],"comps":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"GI-C15-Q4","stem":"Physical examination also shows dilated veins radiating from the umbilicus. The collateral veins that carry portal blood to these surface veins reach the anterior abdominal wall within which of the following peritoneal folds?","options":["Falciform ligament","Gastrosplenic ligament","Gastrohepatic ligament","Splenorenal ligament"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["Dilated periumbilical veins (caput medusae) form when portal blood flows out through paraumbilical veins.","The paraumbilical veins run with the ligamentum teres, the remnant of the umbilical vein.","The ligamentum teres lies in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament, which runs from the liver to the umbilicus."],"why":"The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and carries the ligamentum teres and the small paraumbilical veins in its free edge. In portal hypertension these veins open up and carry portal blood to the superficial veins around the umbilicus, producing caput medusae. Each of the other ligaments carries a different set of vessels: gastric arteries in the gastrohepatic, short gastrics in the gastrosplenic, and the splenic vessels with the pancreatic tail in the splenorenal.","whyNot":["Correct.","The gastrosplenic ligament carries the short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels.","The gastrohepatic ligament, part of the lesser omentum, carries the left and right gastric vessels along the lesser curvature.","The splenorenal ligament carries the splenic artery and vein and the tail of the pancreas."],"study":{"topics":["Peritoneal ligaments and their contents","Caput medusae"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-5"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C16","system":"GI","dx":"Hypervitaminosis A with noncirrhotic portal hypertension","codes":["GI-13-C5"],"case":"A 44-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of increasing abdominal girth, dry skin, hair loss, and bone pain. For 2 years he has taken about 100,000 IU of vitamin A daily to improve his skin. He does not drink alcohol. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows dry, scaly skin, splenomegaly, and shifting dullness. Platelet count is 92,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000). ALT is 74 U/L (normal 7 to 56), and albumin is 3.8 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5). Doppler ultrasonography shows ascites, splenomegaly, and patent portal and hepatic veins. A liver biopsy shows perisinusoidal fibrosis and hypertrophied fat-storing cells containing lipid droplets, without nodular cirrhosis. Diagnosis: hypervitaminosis A with noncirrhotic portal hypertension.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C16-Q1","stem":"He also reports daily headaches and episodes of blurred vision. Which of the following findings is most likely on further evaluation?","options":["Low serum ceruloplasmin concentration","Raised serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D concentration","Low serum vitamin B12 concentration","Raised cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.7","dok":2,"approach":["Link the chronic high vitamin A intake to his skin, bone, and liver findings.","Recall that vitamin A toxicity raises intracranial pressure (pseudotumor cerebri), causing headache, papilledema, and blurred vision.","Raised intracranial pressure shows up as a high cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure on lumbar puncture."],"why":"Chronic vitamin A excess causes dry skin, hair loss, bone pain, liver injury, and raised intracranial pressure. The headache and transient blurring of vision come from idiopathic intracranial hypertension, with papilledema and a high opening pressure. Vitamin A is also strongly teratogenic. Vitamin D toxicity looks different: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, kidney stones, and polyuria.","whyNot":["Low ceruloplasmin suggests Wilson disease, which causes liver and neuropsychiatric disease, but not this vitamin A picture or raised intracranial pressure.","Raised 25-hydroxyvitamin D would point to vitamin D excess, which causes hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, and kidney stones. He took vitamin A only.","Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and neuropathy, not headache with visual blurring from raised intracranial pressure.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin A toxicity","Vitamin A versus vitamin D toxicity"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"GI-C16-Q2","stem":"Which of the following cells is responsible for the fibrosis in this patient's liver, and where is it located?","options":["Endothelial cells lining hepatic sinusoids","Kupffer cells within the sinusoid lumen","Cholangiocytes lining the canals of Hering","Stellate cells in the space of Disse"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["The biopsy describes fat-storing cells next to the sinusoids. These are hepatic stellate (Ito) cells.","Stellate cells sit in the space of Disse and normally store vitamin A in lipid droplets.","When activated, they become myofibroblasts and deposit collagen, causing perisinusoidal fibrosis."],"why":"Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells live in the space of Disse, between hepatocytes and the sinusoidal endothelium. At rest they store vitamin A in lipid droplets. Excess vitamin A loads and activates them, and activated stellate cells turn into myofibroblasts that lay down collagen in the space of Disse. This perisinusoidal fibrosis raises resistance to sinusoidal flow and can cause portal hypertension before cirrhosis develops. The same cell drives fibrosis in alcohol-related and viral liver disease.","whyNot":["Sinusoidal endothelial cells are fenestrated and lack a basement membrane. They lose their fenestrae in fibrosis but do not make the scar.","Kupffer cells are resident macrophages in the sinusoids. They release cytokines that activate stellate cells but do not lay down most of the collagen.","Cholangiocytes line the bile ductules and canals of Hering. They proliferate in biliary disease but are not the main collagen-producing cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic stellate cells","Space of Disse"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-C2"]}},{"id":"GI-C16-Q3","stem":"Which of the following conditions causes portal hypertension at the same anatomic level as in this patient?","options":["Budd-Chiari syndrome","Portal vein thrombosis","Alcohol-related cirrhosis","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C5","obj":"GI-13-C.10","dok":3,"approach":["His portal and hepatic veins are patent, so the block is inside the liver.","The biopsy shows perisinusoidal fibrosis, an intrahepatic cause.","Choose the option that also raises resistance inside the liver: cirrhosis."],"why":"Portal hypertension is classified by where the resistance lies. Prehepatic causes block the portal vein, as in thrombosis. Intrahepatic causes raise resistance within the liver, as in cirrhosis, schistosomiasis, and perisinusoidal fibrosis from vitamin A. Posthepatic causes block outflow, as in Budd-Chiari syndrome, right heart failure, and constrictive pericarditis. All levels can cause splenomegaly and varices; ascites is typical of sinusoidal and posthepatic causes.","whyNot":["Budd-Chiari syndrome is thrombosis of the hepatic veins, which blocks outflow after the liver. This is posthepatic portal hypertension.","Portal vein thrombosis blocks flow before blood reaches the liver. This is prehepatic portal hypertension.","Correct.","Constrictive pericarditis raises right atrial and hepatic vein pressure. This is posthepatic portal hypertension."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of portal hypertension"],"comps":["GI-13-C5","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C16-Q4","stem":"His splenomegaly results from raised pressure transmitted back from the portal vein. Which of the following describes the normal course of the vein that carries this pressure to the spleen?","options":["Joins the inferior vena cava behind the second part of the duodenum","Joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the pancreatic neck","Joins the inferior mesenteric vein at the duodenojejunal flexure","Joins the left renal vein anterior to the abdominal aorta"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["The spleen drains through the splenic vein, which runs behind the pancreas.","The splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein.","High portal pressure is therefore transmitted directly back to the spleen, causing congestive splenomegaly."],"why":"The splenic vein runs along the back of the pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein. The portal vein carries this blood to the liver sinusoids, which drain through central veins to the hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava. When resistance rises in the liver, pressure backs up through the splenic vein into the spleen. The enlarged spleen traps platelets, explaining his thrombocytopenia.","whyNot":["The splenic vein drains to the portal system, not the inferior vena cava. Portal blood reaches the vena cava only after passing through the liver.","Correct.","The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein, not the reverse, and the junction is behind the pancreas.","The left renal vein crosses anterior to the aorta, but the splenic vein does not join it. A splenorenal connection exists only as a surgical shunt or a spontaneous collateral."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous system anatomy","Congestive splenomegaly"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-C5"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C17","system":"GI","dx":"Esophageal variceal hemorrhage in hepatitis C cirrhosis","codes":["GI-13-J1","GI-13-C5","GI-13-L7"],"case":"A 61-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after vomiting a large amount of bright red blood. He has no abdominal or chest pain. He has chronic hepatitis C infection, acquired through injection drug use 30 years ago and never treated, and cirrhosis diagnosed 2 years ago. Temperature is 36.6 °C (97.9 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 84/50 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jaundice, splenomegaly, and ascites. Hemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5), and platelet count is 68,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000). Upper endoscopy shows large actively bleeding varices in the distal esophagus. Diagnosis: esophageal variceal hemorrhage in hepatitis C cirrhosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C17-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why these veins dilated?","options":["Erosive esophagitis weakening the walls of submucosal veins","Azygos blood diverted via esophageal veins into the portal vein","Portal blood diverted via the left gastric vein to the azygos","Pressure transmitted upward from an obstructed superior vena cava"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J1","obj":"GI-13-J.1","dok":3,"approach":["Cirrhosis raises portal pressure, so portal blood seeks lower-pressure systemic routes.","The left gastric (coronary) vein connects with esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system.","Extra flow dilates the thin submucosal veins of the distal esophagus, which can rupture and bleed massively."],"why":"In portal hypertension, blood from the portal system flows backward through the left gastric vein into the esophageal venous plexus, which drains to the azygos system. The thin-walled submucosal veins of the distal esophagus dilate into varices. When they rupture, bleeding is painless and often massive. Variceal hemorrhage is one of the leading causes of death in cirrhosis, and low platelets and clotting factors make it worse.","whyNot":["Esophagitis damages mucosa and can bleed, but it does not cause the vein dilation of varices.","Flow goes from high to low pressure. In portal hypertension, blood moves from the portal side toward the systemic azygos side, not the reverse.","Correct.","Superior vena cava obstruction can cause downhill varices in the upper esophagus, but he has cirrhosis with portal hypertension and distal varices."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal varices","Portosystemic collaterals"],"comps":["GI-13-J1","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C17-Q2","stem":"Proctoscopy shows anorectal varices. These varices connect a portal tributary with the middle and inferior rectal veins. Which of the following is the portal tributary involved?","options":["Inferior vesical vein","Median sacral vein","Internal pudendal vein","Superior rectal vein"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["Portosystemic anastomoses form where portal and systemic veins meet.","The middle and inferior rectal veins drain to the internal iliac system (systemic).","The portal partner in the rectum is the superior rectal vein, a tributary of the inferior mesenteric vein."],"why":"The rectum has a portal and a systemic venous drainage. The superior rectal vein drains to the inferior mesenteric vein and then the portal system. The middle rectal vein drains to the internal iliac vein, and the inferior rectal vein drains through the internal pudendal vein to the internal iliac vein. In portal hypertension, blood flows from the superior rectal vein into these systemic veins, forming anorectal varices. The other main sites are the esophagus (left gastric to azygos) and the umbilicus (paraumbilical to abdominal wall veins).","whyNot":["The inferior vesical vein drains the bladder to the internal iliac vein. It is systemic and not part of this anastomosis.","The median sacral vein drains to the common iliac vein. It is systemic and not part of this anastomosis.","The internal pudendal vein receives the inferior rectal vein and drains to the internal iliac vein. It is on the systemic side.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Portosystemic anastomoses","Venous drainage of the rectum"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-J1"]}},{"id":"GI-C17-Q3","stem":"A year earlier he had palpable purpura on both legs, arthralgias, and hematuria with proteinuria. Serum C4 was low. Which of the following best explains these findings?","options":["Cold-precipitating IgM rheumatoid factor-IgG complexes","Antibodies to type IV collagen in basement membranes","Galactose-deficient IgA1 complexes in small vessel walls","Autoantibodies to neutrophil cytoplasmic myeloperoxidase"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L7","obj":"GI-13-L.9","dok":3,"approach":["Chronic hepatitis C stimulates B cells, which can make monoclonal IgM with rheumatoid factor activity.","This IgM binds IgG (often with HCV antigen) to form complexes that precipitate in the cold: mixed cryoglobulins.","The complexes deposit in small vessels and glomeruli and fix complement, causing purpura, arthralgia, glomerulonephritis, and low C4."],"why":"Most adults infected with HCV, about 75 to 85%, develop chronic infection, and a minority progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma over decades. Chronic infection also drives B cells to make IgM rheumatoid factor that binds IgG and viral antigen. These mixed cryoglobulins precipitate in cooler tissues and deposit in small vessels and glomeruli, consuming complement. The result is palpable purpura, arthralgias, and membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Anti-glomerular basement membrane disease affects the kidney and lung and does not cause purpura with low complement and rheumatoid factor.","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura) causes palpable purpura and nephritis, but usually in children, with normal complement and no rheumatoid factor.","Myeloperoxidase ANCA vasculitis (microscopic polyangiitis) is pauci-immune, so complement is not consumed."],"study":{"topics":["HCV mixed cryoglobulinemia","Outcomes of chronic HCV"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C17-Q4","stem":"No vaccine is available to prevent infection with this virus. Which of the following features of the virus best explains this?","options":["Dependence on a helper virus for envelope proteins","Rapid variation of envelope glycoprotein epitopes","Lack of a lipid envelope on the virion","Integration of viral DNA into the host genome"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.5","dok":2,"approach":["HCV is an enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA flavivirus.","Its RNA-dependent RNA polymerase has no proofreading, so mutations build up fast.","The envelope glycoproteins change constantly, so antibodies made against one variant miss the next, and vaccine design has failed."],"why":"Hepatitis C virus is a flavivirus with an RNA genome. Its RNA-dependent RNA polymerase makes many errors and cannot proofread, so each patient carries a swarm of related variants (quasispecies). The hypervariable regions of the envelope glycoproteins keep changing and escape neutralizing antibodies. This explains both the high rate of chronic infection and the lack of a vaccine. Hepatitis B, by contrast, is a partially double-stranded DNA hepadnavirus with a stable surface antigen used in its vaccine.","whyNot":["Dependence on a helper virus describes hepatitis D, which needs HBsAg from hepatitis B. Vaccinating against hepatitis B prevents it.","Correct.","Lack of an envelope describes hepatitis A and E, which spread by the fecal-oral route. Hepatitis A has an effective vaccine.","Integration of viral DNA describes hepatitis B, which nonetheless has an effective vaccine. HCV is an RNA virus with no DNA stage."],"study":{"topics":["Comparison of hepatitis viruses","HCV antigenic variation"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C18","system":"GI","dx":"Acute hepatitis B in an adult","codes":["GI-13-L7"],"case":"A 34-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 10-day history of fatigue, nausea, right upper quadrant discomfort, and dark urine. Three months ago he had unprotected sexual intercourse with a new partner. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 122/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jaundice and tender hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show ALT 1,840 U/L (normal 7 to 56), AST 1,210 U/L (normal 10 to 40), alkaline phosphatase 160 U/L (normal 40 to 130), total bilirubin 6.2 mg/dL (normal 0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin 4.5 mg/dL (normal 0 to 0.3), and prothrombin time 13 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5). HBsAg and IgM anti-HBc are positive; anti-HBs is negative. Tests for hepatitis A IgM and hepatitis C antibody are negative. Diagnosis: acute hepatitis B.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C18-Q1","stem":"Eight weeks later his symptoms have resolved. Serologic testing now shows HBsAg negative, anti-HBs negative, and IgM anti-HBc positive. Which of the following best describes his status?","options":["Chronic infection with low replication","Window period of acute infection","Resolved infection with immunity","Early acute infection before antibodies appear"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L7","obj":"GI-13-L.8","dok":3,"approach":["HBsAg has cleared, but anti-HBs has not yet appeared.","In this gap, IgM anti-HBc is the only positive marker.","This is the window period, which still means recent acute infection."],"why":"In acute hepatitis B, HBsAg appears first and IgM anti-HBc follows with symptoms. As the infection clears, HBsAg disappears, and there can be a gap of weeks before anti-HBs becomes detectable. During this window period, IgM anti-HBc is the only marker, so it must be tested to avoid missing the infection. Recovery is later marked by anti-HBs and IgG anti-HBc. HBsAg lasting more than 6 months defines chronic infection.","whyNot":["Chronic infection requires HBsAg persisting longer than 6 months, with IgG anti-HBc. His HBsAg has already cleared.","Correct.","Resolved infection shows anti-HBs with IgG anti-HBc. He has no anti-HBs yet, and his anti-HBc is still IgM.","Early acute infection shows HBsAg before any antibody. His HBsAg is gone and IgM anti-HBc is present."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis B serology","Window period"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C18-Q2","stem":"Which of the following is mainly responsible for the hepatocyte injury in this patient?","options":["Toxic accumulation of HBsAg in hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum","Complement-mediated lysis by antibody bound to infected cells","CD8 T cells recognizing viral peptides on MHC class I","Direct cytopathic lysis of hepatocytes by replicating virus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.6","dok":2,"approach":["HBV itself does little direct damage to the cells it infects.","Infected hepatocytes present viral peptides on MHC class I.","Cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognize these peptides and kill the cells, which raises ALT and also clears the virus."],"why":"The liver damage in hepatitis B comes mainly from the immune response. Cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognize HBV peptides on MHC class I and kill infected hepatocytes by perforin, granzymes, and Fas ligand. Adults with a strong T cell response have marked ALT elevation but usually clear the virus. Newborns mount a weak response, have little injury, and most become chronically infected. Circulating HBsAg immune complexes can also cause polyarteritis nodosa and membranous nephropathy.","whyNot":["HBsAg fills the endoplasmic reticulum in chronic carriers (ground-glass hepatocytes), but these cells are not killed by it.","Antibodies such as anti-HBs neutralize free virus, but antibody and complement are not the main cause of hepatocyte death.","Correct.","HBV is not directly cytopathic. Many people carry high viral loads with little liver damage when the immune response is weak, as in neonatal infection."],"study":{"topics":["Immunopathogenesis of hepatitis B"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-L7"]}},{"id":"GI-C18-Q3","stem":"His partner completed the hepatitis B vaccine series 5 years ago. Her serology shows HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, and total anti-HBc negative. Which of the following best explains the absence of anti-HBc?","options":["Anti-HBc disappears within months after any exposure","Vaccine-induced anti-HBs neutralizes the core protein","The vaccine is a live virus that lacks the core gene","The vaccine contains only recombinant HBsAg"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.7","dok":2,"approach":["Ask which viral proteins the vaccine contains.","The vaccine is recombinant HBsAg only, with no core protein.","So vaccination produces anti-HBs alone, while natural infection produces both anti-HBs and anti-HBc."],"why":"The hepatitis B vaccine is a subunit vaccine of recombinant HBsAg produced in yeast. It teaches the immune system to make anti-HBs, which neutralizes the virus. Because no core antigen is given, vaccinated people never make anti-HBc. Anti-HBs alone therefore means vaccine immunity, while anti-HBs together with IgG anti-HBc means recovery from natural infection.","whyNot":["IgG anti-HBc usually persists for life after natural infection. That is why it marks past infection.","Anti-HBs binds the surface antigen. It does not neutralize or remove core protein, which is inside the virion.","The hepatitis B vaccine is not a live virus. It is a subunit vaccine of surface antigen made in yeast.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis B vaccine serology"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-L7"]}},{"id":"GI-C18-Q4","stem":"His serum bilirubin is raised in both the conjugated and unconjugated fractions. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Inherited absence of hepatic UGT1A1 activity","Extrahepatic obstruction of the common bile duct by a stone","Accelerated heme breakdown from immune-mediated hemolysis","Hepatocyte injury impairing conjugation and excretion"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Indirect bilirubin is total minus direct: 6.2 minus 4.5 equals 1.7 mg/dL, so both fractions are high.","ALT above 1,000 U/L with only a small alkaline phosphatase rise is a hepatocellular pattern.","Injured hepatocytes take up and conjugate less bilirubin, and conjugated bilirubin leaks back into blood from damaged cells and canaliculi."],"why":"In hepatitis, injured hepatocytes cannot take up, conjugate, and excrete bilirubin normally. Some unconjugated bilirubin is not processed, and conjugated bilirubin leaks back into the blood because excretion into bile is the slowest step. The result is a mixed hyperbilirubinemia with bilirubin in the urine. A very high ALT with a modest alkaline phosphatase distinguishes this from obstructive jaundice, where conjugated bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase dominate.","whyNot":["Absent UGT1A1 (Crigler-Najjar type I) causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia from birth with no conjugated rise.","A common duct stone raises mainly conjugated bilirubin with a high alkaline phosphatase and pain. His transaminases are far higher than his alkaline phosphatase, which points to hepatocyte injury.","Hemolysis raises mainly unconjugated bilirubin, with no bilirubin in the urine. His dark urine and high direct fraction show conjugated bilirubin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mixed hyperbilirubinemia","Hepatocellular versus obstructive jaundice"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-L7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C19","system":"GI","dx":"Amebic liver abscess","codes":["GI-13-L"],"case":"A 36-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of fever and right upper quadrant pain that radiates to the right shoulder. He returned 4 months ago from a year of agricultural work in rural southern Mexico, where he had bloody diarrhea for about a week. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tender hepatomegaly and tenderness over the right lower intercostal spaces. Leukocyte count is 16,500/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000) with no eosinophilia. Alkaline phosphatase is 210 U/L (normal 40 to 130). CT scan of the abdomen shows a single 7-cm abscess in the right lobe of the liver. Serum antibodies to Entamoeba histolytica are positive. Diagnosis: amebic liver abscess.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C19-Q1","stem":"Aspiration of the abscess is performed. Which of the following findings in the aspirated fluid is most likely?","options":["Reddish brown odorless fluid with no bacteria on Gram stain","Foul-smelling pus containing gram-negative rods and anaerobes","Clear fluid containing daughter cysts and protoscolex hooklets","Green bile-stained fluid with cholesterol crystals"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L","obj":"GI-13-L.10","dok":3,"approach":["Travel to an endemic area, prior bloody diarrhea, a single right lobe abscess, and positive amebic serology point to amebic abscess.","Amebae dissolve liver tissue into a thick, odorless, reddish brown fluid often called anchovy paste.","The fluid is usually sterile on Gram stain and culture; trophozoites are found mainly in the abscess wall."],"why":"Amebic liver abscess is a cavity of liquefied liver tissue made by Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites. The contents are reddish brown, odorless, and usually free of bacteria and neutrophils, because amebae lyse neutrophils. Pyogenic abscesses are often multiple, follow biliary infection or portal spread from bowel disease, and contain foul pus with enteric gram-negative rods or anaerobes. Travel history and serology help separate the two.","whyNot":["Correct.","Foul pus with mixed bacteria is the content of a pyogenic abscess, which usually comes from biliary disease or portal spread and grows E. coli, Klebsiella, or anaerobes.","Clear fluid with daughter cysts and hooklets is a hydatid cyst of Echinococcus granulosus, usually found in people exposed to dogs and sheep.","Bile-stained fluid suggests a biloma from bile duct injury, not an abscess after amebic dysentery."],"study":{"topics":["Amebic versus pyogenic liver abscess"],"comps":["GI-13-L","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C19-Q2","stem":"Which of the following would most likely have been found in an intestinal biopsy specimen during his diarrheal illness?","options":["Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites containing red cells","Acid-fast oocysts on the brush border without ulceration","Rhabditiform larvae in crypts with eosinophilic infiltrates","Flagellated trophozoites attached to villi without invasion"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.7","dok":2,"approach":["The liver abscess and earlier bloody diarrhea point to invasive Entamoeba histolytica.","Trophozoites invade the colonic mucosa and spread sideways in the submucosa, making flask-shaped ulcers.","The trophozoites often contain ingested red blood cells, which separates them from harmless amebae."],"why":"Entamoeba histolytica is spread by cysts in fecally contaminated food and water. Trophozoites attack the colon, most often the cecum and ascending colon, and make narrow-necked, broad-based flask-shaped ulcers. They ingest red blood cells, and this is a key way to recognize them. From the ulcers, trophozoites can enter portal venous blood and reach the liver.","whyNot":["Correct.","Acid-fast oocysts on the brush border are Cryptosporidium, which causes watery diarrhea, severe in people with advanced HIV infection.","Larvae in the crypts are Strongyloides, which penetrates skin and can cause hyperinfection with corticosteroid use.","Flagellated trophozoites stuck to villi without invasion describe Giardia, which causes fatty, nonbloody diarrhea from contaminated water."],"study":{"topics":["Entamoeba histolytica","Comparison of intestinal protozoa"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L"]}},{"id":"GI-C19-Q3","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely route by which trophozoites traveled from his colon to his liver?","options":["Common bile duct ascending from the duodenum","Superior mesenteric vein to portal vein","Ileocolic lymphatics to the thoracic duct","Inferior vena cava to the hepatic veins"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Amebic ulcers are usually in the cecum and ascending colon, which drain to the superior mesenteric vein.","The superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein.","Portal blood enters the liver first, so amebae lodge in the hepatic sinusoids."],"why":"Venous blood from the cecum and ascending colon drains through the ileocolic and right colic veins to the superior mesenteric vein. It joins the splenic vein to form the portal vein, which delivers the blood to the liver sinusoids before it reaches the hepatic veins and inferior vena cava. Trophozoites in this blood are trapped in the liver. Flow from the superior mesenteric vein tends to stream into the right lobe, which helps explain why most amebic abscesses form there.","whyNot":["Spread up the common bile duct is the route of ascending cholangitis and many pyogenic abscesses, not amebic abscess.","Correct.","Lymph from the colon drains to the thoracic duct and then the systemic veins, which would take organisms to the lungs first, not directly to the liver.","Blood in the inferior vena cava flows toward the heart. Hepatic veins drain the liver into the vena cava, not the other way."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous drainage of the colon","Route of amebic liver abscess"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C19-Q4","stem":"During his diarrheal illness, stool studies would most likely have shown which of the following?","options":["Watery stool, high osmotic gap, stops with fasting","Watery stool, low osmotic gap, persists with fasting","Bloody stool, low osmotic gap, persists with fasting","Greasy stool, high fecal fat, stops with fasting"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Invasive amebic colitis damages the mucosa and causes bloody stool: an inflammatory diarrhea.","Inflammatory diarrhea does not come from unabsorbed solutes, so the stool osmotic gap is low and fasting does not stop it.","Compare with osmotic diarrhea (high gap, stops with fasting) and secretory diarrhea (low gap, watery, persists)."],"why":"Diarrhea is grouped by mechanism. Osmotic diarrhea comes from unabsorbed solutes, so the stool osmotic gap is high (above about 100 to 125 mOsm/kg) and the diarrhea stops with fasting. Secretory diarrhea comes from active chloride secretion, as with cholera toxin, so the gap is low (below about 50 mOsm/kg) and it persists with fasting. Inflammatory diarrhea comes from mucosal damage, with blood in the stool, a low gap, and persistence with fasting. Amebic colitis is a classic inflammatory cause.","whyNot":["A high osmotic gap that stops with fasting is osmotic diarrhea, such as from lactose that is not absorbed.","Watery diarrhea with a low gap that continues during fasting is secretory diarrhea, as from cholera toxin raising cAMP and opening CFTR chloride channels. It is not bloody.","Correct.","Greasy stool with high fecal fat is malabsorption, as in Giardia infection or pancreatic insufficiency, not invasive colitis."],"study":{"topics":["Types of diarrhea","Stool osmotic gap"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-12"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C20","system":"GI","dx":"Lynch syndrome right-sided colon adenocarcinoma with liver metastasis","codes":["GI-13-K7","GI-13-K4"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of fatigue and shortness of breath on exertion. Her mother had endometrial cancer at the age of 49 years, and a maternal uncle had colon cancer at the age of 44 years. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows pallor. Hemoglobin is 8.6 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0), mean corpuscular volume is 71 fL (normal 80 to 100), and serum ferritin is 6 ng/mL (normal 12 to 150). A test for fecal occult blood is positive. Colonoscopy shows an ulcerated mass in the ascending colon; biopsy shows poorly differentiated mucinous adenocarcinoma with many tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes. CT scan of the abdomen shows two solid masses in the liver. Diagnosis: Lynch syndrome with adenocarcinoma of the ascending colon.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C20-Q1","stem":"Which of the following molecular findings is most likely in her tumor?","options":["Microsatellite instability from mismatch repair loss","Germline STK11 mutation with hamartomatous polyps","Biallelic APC loss with chromosomal instability","Defective nucleotide excision repair of thymine dimers"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Early colon cancer and endometrial cancer in close relatives fit Lynch syndrome (hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer).","Lynch syndrome is a germline defect in a DNA mismatch repair gene such as MLH1 or MSH2.","Without mismatch repair, replication errors build up in short repeated sequences, causing microsatellite instability."],"why":"Lynch syndrome is autosomal dominant. Patients inherit one faulty mismatch repair gene (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, or PMS2), and the second copy is lost in the tumor. Errors made when DNA polymerase slips on repetitive sequences are not fixed, so microsatellites change length, and mutations pile up in growth control genes. These cancers tend to be right-sided, mucinous, and full of lymphocytes. Sporadic cancers usually follow the APC, KRAS, TP53 adenoma to carcinoma sequence instead.","whyNot":["Correct.","STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, with hamartomatous polyps and mucocutaneous pigmentation.","APC loss followed by KRAS activation and TP53 loss is the chromosomal instability pathway of most sporadic cancers and of familial adenomatous polyposis, which presents with hundreds of polyps.","Nucleotide excision repair defects cause xeroderma pigmentosum, with skin cancers after sunlight exposure, not hereditary colon cancer."],"study":{"topics":["Mismatch repair and Lynch syndrome","Pathways to colorectal cancer"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-K7"]}},{"id":"GI-C20-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her tumor presented with iron deficiency anemia rather than bowel obstruction?","options":["Annular napkin-ring growth typical of right colon tumors","Hemolysis induced by cytokines secreted by the tumor","Wide lumen and liquid stool in the right colon","Impaired duodenal iron absorption from liver metastases"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K7","obj":"GI-13-K.10","dok":3,"approach":["A microcytic anemia with low ferritin and occult blood points to slow bleeding from the colon.","The cecum and ascending colon have a wide lumen and liquid contents, so bulky tumors rarely block them.","These exophytic tumors ooze blood and present with iron deficiency anemia."],"why":"Right-sided colon cancers tend to be bulky, polypoid masses in a wide segment filled with liquid stool. They bleed slowly and rarely obstruct, so iron deficiency anemia is a common first sign. Left-sided cancers often grow around the wall in a napkin-ring pattern in a narrow segment with formed stool, causing a change in bowel habit and obstruction. Iron deficiency anemia in an older man or a postmenopausal woman should prompt a search for colon cancer. CEA is used to watch for recurrence after treatment, not for screening.","whyNot":["Annular napkin-ring growth is typical of left-sided cancers, which narrow the lumen and cause a change in bowel habit and obstruction.","Her anemia is microcytic with a very low ferritin, which shows iron loss from bleeding, not hemolysis.","Correct.","Iron is absorbed in the duodenum, and liver metastases do not block this. The low ferritin reflects chronic blood loss."],"study":{"topics":["Right versus left colon cancer","Colorectal cancer risk factors"],"comps":["GI-13-K7","GI-10"]}},{"id":"GI-C20-Q3","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely nature of the liver masses?","options":["Multiple cavernous hemangiomas","Primary hepatocellular carcinoma","Hepatocellular adenomas from estrogen exposure","Colorectal adenocarcinoma metastases"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K4","obj":"GI-13-K.7","dok":2,"approach":["Metastases are the most common malignant tumors of the liver, far outnumbering primary liver cancer.","Colon cancer drains through the portal vein, so the liver is the first capillary bed it reaches.","Multiple solid liver masses in a patient with colon cancer are metastases until proven otherwise."],"why":"Metastatic tumors are much more common in the liver than primary cancers. Cancers of the colon, stomach, and pancreas reach the liver through portal venous blood, and breast and lung cancers reach it through the hepatic artery. Metastases are usually multiple. Among benign lesions, cavernous hemangioma is the most common, and hepatocellular adenoma is linked to estrogen or anabolic steroid exposure and carries a risk of rupture.","whyNot":["Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor, but multiple solid masses in a patient with colon cancer are much more likely to be metastases.","Hepatocellular carcinoma usually arises in a liver with cirrhosis or chronic HBV or HCV infection. Nothing in her history suggests either, and she has a known primary tumor.","Hepatocellular adenomas are linked to oral contraceptives and anabolic steroids and can rupture. With a known colon cancer, metastasis is far more likely.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Liver metastases","Benign liver tumors"],"comps":["GI-13-K4","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C20-Q4","stem":"During her evaluation she develops septic shock from a urinary tract infection, with a blood pressure near 72/40 mm Hg for several hours. Two days later she has left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stools, and colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa limited to the splenic flexure. Which of the following best explains the vulnerability of this segment?","options":["It is supplied by end arteries arising directly from the aorta","It lies where superior and inferior mesenteric supply meet","It receives its blood only from the superior rectal artery","It drains to the systemic rather than the portal venous system"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.2","dok":3,"approach":["Ischemia after a period of hypotension affects watershed zones, where two arterial territories meet.","The superior mesenteric artery (middle colic) supplies the colon up to near the splenic flexure; the inferior mesenteric artery (left colic) supplies beyond it.","At the junction, the marginal artery is often thin, so this segment is the first to suffer when pressure falls."],"why":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, from the duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut beyond that. The marginal artery links the two along the colon, but at the splenic flexure the connection is often thin. When blood pressure falls, these border zones get the least flow. The splenic flexure and the rectosigmoid junction are the classic watershed sites for ischemic colitis.","whyNot":["The splenic flexure is supplied by colic branches of the mesenteric arteries through the marginal artery, not by end arteries straight from the aorta.","Correct.","The superior rectal artery, the end of the inferior mesenteric artery, supplies the upper rectum, not the splenic flexure.","The splenic flexure drains to the inferior mesenteric vein and the portal system. Venous drainage does not explain ischemia from low blood pressure."],"study":{"topics":["Colonic blood supply","Watershed ischemic colitis"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-J3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C21","system":"GI","dx":"Familial adenomatous polyposis","codes":["GI-13-K7"],"case":"A 19-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of intermittent bright red blood in his stools and loose bowel movements. His father died of colon cancer at the age of 41 years, and his paternal aunt had a colectomy at the age of 25 years. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Hemoglobin concentration is 11.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5). Colonoscopy shows more than 400 sessile and pedunculated polyps, most 2 to 8 mm in diameter, carpeting the colon and rectum. Biopsy specimens of several polyps show tubular adenomas with low-grade dysplasia. Diagnosis: familial adenomatous polyposis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C21-Q1","stem":"Five years after prophylactic colectomy, he develops a firm mass in the abdominal wall that is diagnosed as a desmoid tumor. Radiographs show osteomas of the mandible, and he has several epidermoid cysts on his back. Which of the following is the most likely genetic basis of his entire presentation?","options":["Biallelic germline mutations in MUTYH","Heterozygous germline mutation in STK11","Heterozygous germline mutation in APC","Heterozygous germline mutation in MLH1"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize osteomas, epidermoid cysts, and desmoid tumors with colonic polyposis as Gardner syndrome.","Recall that Gardner syndrome is a variant of familial adenomatous polyposis caused by the same gene.","Use the family history: an affected father and aunt fit autosomal dominant inheritance of one mutant allele."],"why":"Gardner syndrome is familial adenomatous polyposis plus extracolonic growths such as osteomas, epidermoid cysts, desmoid tumors, and extra teeth. Both come from a heterozygous germline mutation in APC, a tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. The extracolonic lesions mark a variant of the same disease and do not point to a different gene. Turcot syndrome is the other variant, with brain tumors such as medulloblastoma.","whyNot":["MUTYH-associated polyposis also produces many adenomas, but it is autosomal recessive, so an affected father and aunt would be unexpected. The classic Gardner lesions (osteomas, epidermoid cysts, desmoid tumors) point to APC.","STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, with hamartomatous polyps and dark macules on the lips and buccal mucosa. It does not produce hundreds of adenomas.","Correct.","MLH1 mutations cause Lynch syndrome, in which a few adenomas progress quickly to cancer, often in the right colon. Lynch syndrome does not carpet the colon with hundreds of polyps."],"study":{"topics":["Gardner and Turcot variants of FAP","Hereditary polyposis syndromes"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-K7"]}},{"id":"GI-C21-Q2","stem":"One polyp in the rectum measures 3.5 cm. Microscopic examination shows long, slender, finger-like projections of dysplastic columnar epithelium over thin fibrovascular cores, making up most of the lesion. Compared with his 5-mm polyps, which of the following best describes this lesion?","options":["Villous adenoma with a higher risk of invasive carcinoma","Tubular adenoma with a similar risk of invasive carcinoma","Hyperplastic polyp with no meaningful malignant risk","Hamartomatous polyp with a low risk of invasive carcinoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K7","obj":"GI-13-K.11","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the architecture: finger-like fronds of dysplastic epithelium define a villous adenoma.","Recall that malignant risk in adenomas rises with size, villous component, and grade of dysplasia.","Combine the two: a large villous adenoma carries more risk than a small tubular one."],"why":"Adenomas are classified by their architecture. Tubular adenomas are mostly branching glands, villous adenomas are mostly long fronds, and tubulovillous adenomas are a mix. The risk of invasive carcinoma rises with size (especially above 2 cm), with the villous fraction, and with high-grade dysplasia. This 3.5-cm villous lesion is the polyp most likely to already contain cancer.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tubular adenomas are made of branching tubular glands. A small tubular adenoma carries a lower risk than a large villous one.","Hyperplastic polyps show serrated crypts without dysplasia, are usually small and rectosigmoid, and have little malignant potential.","Hamartomatous polyps, such as juvenile and Peutz-Jeghers polyps, are disorganized normal tissue without dysplastic fronds."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of colonic polyps","Adenoma size and villous risk"],"comps":["GI-13-K7"]}},{"id":"GI-C21-Q3","stem":"Upper endoscopy for surveillance shows several adenomas surrounding the major duodenal papilla. A biopsy of normal-appearing mucosa next to the papilla is taken. Which of the following histologic features best identifies the segment of bowel sampled?","options":["Aggregated lymphoid follicles in the submucosa","Straight crypts rich in goblet cells without villi","Mucous glands in the submucosa","Tubular glands lined by parietal and chief cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum.","Recall the histologic marker of the duodenum: Brunner glands in the submucosa.","Match the other features to the ileum, colon, and stomach and exclude them."],"why":"The major papilla opens into the second part of the duodenum, so the biopsy is duodenal. The duodenum is the only segment with Brunner glands, mucous glands in the submucosa that secrete alkaline mucus to protect against gastric acid. Periampullary adenomas and carcinomas are a leading cause of death in FAP after colectomy, which is why the duodenum is surveyed.","whyNot":["Aggregated lymphoid follicles (Peyer patches) mark the ileum.","Straight crypts packed with goblet cells and no villi describe the colon.","Correct.","Parietal and chief cells line the fundic glands of the gastric body and fundus."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of the small intestine segments"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C21-Q4","stem":"Without colectomy, one of his adenomas would be expected to progress to carcinoma. Which of the following sequences of somatic events best describes that progression?","options":["TP53 loss, then KRAS activation, then loss of the second APC allele","KRAS activation, then loss of both APC alleles, then TP53 loss","MLH1 loss, then microsatellite instability, then TGFBR2 mutation","Second APC allele loss, then KRAS activation, then TP53 loss"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note that he already carries one mutant APC allele in every cell.","Recall that the adenoma to carcinoma sequence starts with APC loss, followed by KRAS activation and then TP53 loss.","Separate this from the mismatch repair pathway of Lynch syndrome."],"why":"In familial adenomatous polyposis every colonic cell starts with one mutant APC allele, so a single somatic hit to the second allele frees beta-catenin and starts an adenoma. KRAS activation then drives growth of the adenoma, and loss of TP53 late in the sequence allows transformation to carcinoma. This is the chromosomal instability pathway. Lynch syndrome tumors follow a separate route, in which loss of mismatch repair (MLH1, MSH2) causes microsatellite instability.","whyNot":["TP53 loss is a late event in the sequence. It permits the change from a large adenoma to invasive carcinoma.","APC loss is the gatekeeper event and comes first in this pathway, before KRAS activation.","This is the mismatch repair pathway of Lynch syndrome. Frameshift mutations build up in genes with repeat sequences such as TGFBR2.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Adenoma to carcinoma sequence","Lynch syndrome and microsatellite instability"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-K7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C22","system":"GI","dx":"Acute calculous cholecystitis from cholesterol stones","codes":["GI-13-D1","GI-13-D2"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of a 14-hour history of constant right upper quadrant pain that radiates to the right shoulder blade, with nausea and vomiting. She has had three shorter episodes of similar pain over the past year. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 106/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Deep palpation of the right upper quadrant causes inspiratory arrest. Leukocyte count is 15,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000). Serum total bilirubin is 1.0 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2), and lipase is normal. Ultrasonography shows multiple gallstones, one impacted in the gallbladder neck, a gallbladder wall 6 mm thick (normal under 3), and pericholecystic fluid. Diagnosis: acute calculous cholecystitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C22-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the initial injury to the gallbladder wall in this patient?","options":["Primary bacterial infection ascending from the duodenal lumen","Chemical irritation by bile trapped behind the stone","Wall ischemia and bile stasis during a critical illness","Reflux of activated pancreatic enzymes into the gallbladder"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-D1","obj":"GI-13-D.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the stone impacted in the gallbladder neck, which blocks outflow into the cystic duct.","Recall that acute calculous cholecystitis begins as chemical inflammation from trapped bile.","Treat bacterial infection as a later, secondary event."],"why":"In acute calculous cholecystitis a stone obstructs the gallbladder neck or cystic duct. Trapped bile becomes more concentrated, and phospholipase from the injured mucosa converts lecithin to lysolecithin, which, with bile acids, damages the mucosa and releases prostaglandins. The gallbladder distends, its wall becomes edematous, and inflammation spreads to the parietal peritoneum, which produces the Murphy sign. Bacteria infect the stagnant bile later in many patients.","whyNot":["Bacteria can be cultured from the bile in many cases, but infection follows the obstruction and chemical injury rather than starting it.","Correct.","This describes acalculous cholecystitis, which occurs in critically ill patients after trauma, burns, major surgery, or sepsis. She has an impacted stone and no critical illness.","Pancreatic enzyme reflux does not cause cholecystitis, and her normal lipase argues against pancreatic involvement."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of acute cholecystitis","Calculous versus acalculous cholecystitis"],"comps":["GI-13-D1"]}},{"id":"GI-C22-Q2","stem":"She has taken a combined estrogen-progestin oral contraceptive for 12 years. Analysis of the stones removed at cholecystectomy shows that they are composed mainly of cholesterol. Which of the following mechanisms most directly links her estrogen exposure to this type of stone?","options":["Increased hemolysis with excess unconjugated bilirubin in bile","Bacterial beta-glucuronidase activity in the bile ducts","Increased conversion of cholesterol to bile acids","Increased hepatic secretion of cholesterol into bile"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-D2","obj":"GI-13-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the stone type: cholesterol stones form when bile is supersaturated with cholesterol.","Recall that estrogen increases hepatic cholesterol uptake and secretion into bile.","Exclude the mechanisms that produce pigment stones."],"why":"Cholesterol stays dissolved in bile only when enough bile acids and lecithin are present to carry it in micelles and vesicles. Estrogen increases hepatic uptake of lipoprotein cholesterol and its secretion into bile, which supersaturates the bile. Progesterone adds gallbladder hypomotility and stasis. This is why female sex, pregnancy, and estrogen therapy are risk factors, along with obesity and rapid weight loss.","whyNot":["Chronic hemolysis, as in sickle cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis, causes black pigment stones made of calcium bilirubinate.","Bacterial beta-glucuronidase deconjugates bilirubin in infected ducts and forms brown pigment stones, which are common in East Asia and with liver flukes.","Converting cholesterol to bile acids lowers the cholesterol saturation of bile and protects against stones.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cholesterol versus pigment gallstones","Risk factors for cholesterol stones"],"comps":["GI-13-D2","GI-9"]}},{"id":"GI-C22-Q3","stem":"Each of her episodes began about 30 minutes after a meal of fried food. Release of which of the following best explains why such meals precipitate her pain?","options":["Gastrin from G cells of the gastric antrum","Cholecystokinin from duodenal I cells","Secretin from S cells of the duodenal mucosa","Motilin from enteroendocrine cells of the duodenum"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Link fat in the duodenum to the hormone that contracts the gallbladder.","Recall that cholecystokinin from I cells contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi.","Apply it: contraction against a stone in the neck raises pressure and causes pain."],"why":"Fatty acids and amino acids entering the duodenum stimulate I cells of the duodenum and jejunum to release cholecystokinin. CCK contracts gallbladder smooth muscle and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi to deliver bile for fat digestion. When a stone blocks the neck, contraction against the obstruction raises pressure in the gallbladder and causes pain. This is why biliary pain typically follows fatty meals.","whyNot":["Gastrin stimulates gastric acid secretion and mucosal growth and has no major role in gallbladder contraction.","Correct.","Secretin responds to acid in the duodenum and stimulates bicarbonate-rich fluid from pancreatic and bile duct cells. It does not contract the gallbladder.","Motilin drives the migrating motor complex during fasting, and its release is suppressed by eating."],"study":{"topics":["Cholecystokinin actions","Gallbladder filling and emptying"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C22-Q4","stem":"During laparoscopic cholecystectomy, the surgeon clips the artery that crosses the hepatocystic triangle to reach the gallbladder. This artery is most commonly a branch of which of the following?","options":["Right hepatic artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Left gastric artery","Splenic artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the artery in the hepatocystic (Calot) triangle as the cystic artery.","Recall that it usually arises from the right hepatic artery.","Trace it back: right hepatic, proper hepatic, common hepatic, celiac trunk."],"why":"The cystic artery usually arises from the right hepatic artery within the hepatocystic triangle, which is bounded by the cystic duct, the common hepatic duct, and the inferior surface of the liver. The right hepatic artery comes from the proper hepatic artery, the continuation of the common hepatic artery, which is one of the three branches of the celiac trunk. Because the right hepatic artery often runs close to the cystic duct, surgeons identify these structures before clipping.","whyNot":["Correct.","The gastroduodenal artery arises from the common hepatic artery and runs behind the first part of the duodenum. It is the vessel eroded by a posterior duodenal ulcer.","The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach and the distal esophagus.","The splenic artery runs along the upper border of the pancreas to the spleen and gives short gastric and left gastro-omental branches."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the celiac trunk","Cystic artery and hepatocystic triangle"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C23","system":"GI","dx":"Mirizzi syndrome","codes":["GI-13-D2","GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 67-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-week history of yellow skin, itching, dark urine, and pale stools. He has had episodes of right upper quadrant pain after meals for 2 years. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 136/82 mm Hg. He is jaundiced. Serum studies show total bilirubin of 6.4 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin of 5.0 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), alkaline phosphatase of 420 U/L (40 to 130), and ALT of 76 U/L (10 to 40). Ultrasonography shows a 3-cm stone impacted in the gallbladder neck, with dilated intrahepatic ducts and a dilated common hepatic duct. The common bile duct below the cystic duct junction is of normal caliber and contains no stones. Diagnosis: Mirizzi syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C23-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains this patient's jaundice?","options":["Stone obstruction of the distal common bile duct","Obstruction of the cystic duct alone","Extrinsic compression of the common hepatic duct","Malignant stricture at the hepatic duct confluence"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-D2","obj":"GI-13-D.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the dilated ducts above the stone and the normal common bile duct below it.","Place the obstruction at the common hepatic duct, beside the gallbladder neck.","Recognize Mirizzi syndrome: a stone in the gallbladder neck or cystic duct presses on the common hepatic duct from outside."],"why":"In Mirizzi syndrome a large stone lodges in the gallbladder neck or cystic duct, and the stone and surrounding inflammation press on the adjacent common hepatic duct. Bile cannot leave the liver, so conjugated bilirubin backs up into the blood and urine, and stools turn pale. The ducts above the compression dilate while the common bile duct below stays normal. Over time the stone can erode into the duct and form a cholecystobiliary fistula.","whyNot":["A stone in the distal common bile duct (choledocholithiasis) would dilate the common bile duct as well, and ultrasonography shows a normal, stone-free common bile duct.","Cystic duct obstruction alone causes biliary colic or cholecystitis without jaundice, because bile from the liver still drains through the common hepatic and common bile ducts.","Correct.","A tumor at the confluence of the right and left hepatic ducts would leave the common hepatic duct below it undilated. Here the common hepatic duct is dilated down to the impacted stone."],"study":{"topics":["Mirizzi syndrome","Levels of biliary obstruction"],"comps":["GI-13-D2","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C23-Q2","stem":"Three days later, while awaiting surgery, he has shaking chills and becomes confused. Temperature is 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 86/50 mm Hg. Which of the following best explains this change?","options":["Ascending cholangitis","Biliary colic","Gallstone pancreatitis","Gallstone ileus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-D2","obj":"GI-13-D.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant disease (Charcot triad) plus hypotension and confusion (Reynolds pentad).","Recall that bacteria multiply in obstructed, stagnant bile within the duct system.","Match the stone's location in the biliary tree to its complication."],"why":"Obstruction of the bile ducts lets enteric bacteria such as Escherichia coli, Klebsiella, and Enterococcus ascend and multiply in stagnant bile. Rising duct pressure pushes bacteria into the blood and causes sepsis. Fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain form the Charcot triad, and adding hypotension and confusion forms the Reynolds pentad, which calls for urgent biliary drainage. Cholangitis requires obstruction of the duct system, which his compressed common hepatic duct provides.","whyNot":["Correct.","Biliary colic is transient pain from a stone briefly blocking the cystic duct, without fever or shock.","Gallstone pancreatitis needs a stone at or near the ampulla, where it blocks the pancreatic duct. It presents with epigastric pain radiating to the back and a high lipase.","Gallstone ileus follows a cholecystoenteric fistula. A large stone passes into the bowel and obstructs it, usually near the ileocecal valve, causing vomiting and distension."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of gallstones by location","Charcot triad and Reynolds pentad"],"comps":["GI-13-D2","GI-13-B1"]}},{"id":"GI-C23-Q3","stem":"Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography is planned to place a stent above the compression. To cannulate the bile duct, the endoscope must be advanced into which of the following parts of the duodenum?","options":["Horizontal part","Ascending part","Superior part","Descending part"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the common bile duct joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla.","Locate the ampulla's opening, the major duodenal papilla, on the posteromedial wall of the descending (second) part.","Name the duodenal parts in order: superior, descending, horizontal, ascending."],"why":"The common bile duct passes behind the first part of the duodenum and through the head of the pancreas, then joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla (of Vater). The ampulla opens at the major duodenal papilla on the posteromedial wall of the descending part. The minor papilla, for the accessory pancreatic duct, lies a little above it in the same part.","whyNot":["The horizontal part crosses the inferior vena cava and aorta at about L3, passing under the superior mesenteric vessels. No ducts open into it.","The ascending part rises to the duodenojejunal flexure, held by the suspensory muscle of the duodenum (ligament of Treitz), and has no papilla.","The superior part, which includes the duodenal cap, has no papilla. The common bile duct and gastroduodenal artery pass behind it.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Parts of the duodenum","Major duodenal papilla"],"comps":["GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C23-Q4","stem":"His obstruction also keeps bile acids from reaching the intestine. In health, the liver secretes several times more bile acid into the intestine each day than it synthesizes, and only a small fraction appears in stool. Which of the following best accounts for this conservation?","options":["Bacterial conversion of bile acids to cholesterol in the colon","Sodium-coupled active uptake in the terminal ileum","Passive diffusion of conjugated bile acids in the duodenum","Reabsorption of bile acids from the gallbladder into blood"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize that a small bile acid pool must be recycled several times a day.","Recall that conjugated bile acids are reclaimed by an active sodium-dependent transporter in the terminal ileum.","Follow them through portal blood back to hepatocytes for resecretion."],"why":"About 95% of secreted bile acids are reabsorbed, mostly by the apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter in the terminal ileum. They return in portal blood bound to albumin, and hepatocytes take them up and secrete them again, so the pool cycles several times a day. The liver synthesizes only enough new bile acid to replace the small amount lost in stool. Ileal disease or resection breaks this circuit, shrinking the pool and causing steatorrhea and a higher risk of cholesterol gallstones.","whyNot":["Colonic bacteria deconjugate and dehydroxylate bile acids into secondary bile acids such as deoxycholate. They do not convert bile acids back into cholesterol.","Correct.","Conjugated bile acids are ionized at intestinal pH and cross the proximal intestine poorly. This keeps them in the lumen, where they form micelles for fat absorption.","The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile by absorbing sodium and water. It does not return bile acids to the blood."],"study":{"topics":["Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C24","system":"GI","dx":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma in a porcelain gallbladder","codes":["GI-13-D1","GI-13-K"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of yellow skin, generalized itching, a dull right upper quadrant ache, and a 7-kg (15-lb) weight loss. She was told she had gallstones 15 years ago and declined surgery. She is jaundiced, and a firm mass is palpable below the right costal margin. Serum total bilirubin is 9.1 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2), direct bilirubin is 7.6 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), and alkaline phosphatase is 610 U/L (40 to 130). CT scan shows gallstones, a continuous rim of calcification in the gallbladder wall, and an irregular mass in the gallbladder neck that invades the adjacent liver and narrows the common hepatic duct, with dilated intrahepatic ducts. Biopsy shows adenocarcinoma. Diagnosis: gallbladder adenocarcinoma in a porcelain gallbladder.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C24-Q1","stem":"Her serum calcium concentration is 9.3 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5). Which of the following best describes the calcification in her gallbladder wall?","options":["Calcification within a cluster of cholesterol polyps","Dystrophic calcification from chronic inflammation","Calcified wall of a hepatic echinococcal cyst","Metastatic calcification from a high serum calcium"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-D1","obj":"GI-13-D.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note the normal serum calcium, which excludes metastatic calcification.","Link calcium deposits in damaged tissue with a normal serum calcium to dystrophic calcification.","Connect long-standing gallstones to chronic cholecystitis, and the porcelain gallbladder to cancer risk."],"why":"Long-standing gallstones cause repeated injury and chronic cholecystitis, with fibrosis of the gallbladder wall. Calcium salts deposit in the damaged wall while serum calcium stays normal, which is dystrophic calcification. A calcified (porcelain) gallbladder marks long-standing chronic inflammation and is a classic risk factor for gallbladder adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["Cholesterol polyps are small, lipid-laden projections of the mucosa (cholesterolosis). They do not calcify the whole wall.","Correct.","Echinococcal (hydatid) cysts can calcify, but they form in the liver and would not outline the gallbladder wall.","Metastatic calcification occurs in normal tissue when serum calcium or phosphate is high, as in hyperparathyroidism. Her serum calcium is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Porcelain gallbladder","Dystrophic versus metastatic calcification"],"comps":["GI-13-D1","GI-13-K"]}},{"id":"GI-C24-Q2","stem":"Which of the following sets of findings in her urine and stool is most likely?","options":["Urine bilirubin absent, urine urobilinogen normal, brown stools","Urine bilirubin absent, urine urobilinogen high, dark stools","Urine bilirubin present, urine urobilinogen high, brown stools","Urine bilirubin present, urine urobilinogen low, pale stools"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K","obj":"GI-13-K.13","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize progressive jaundice with a high direct bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase as obstructive (posthepatic) jaundice.","Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble, so it spills into urine when it backs up into blood.","With bile blocked from the gut, bacteria make little urobilinogen and stercobilin, so urine urobilinogen falls and stools turn pale."],"why":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma near the neck, like cholangiocarcinoma, obstructs the bile ducts and causes progressive, often painless jaundice with weight loss. Conjugated bilirubin regurgitates into the blood and, being water soluble, appears in urine and darkens it. Little bilirubin reaches the intestine, so gut bacteria produce little urobilinogen (lowering urine urobilinogen) and little stercobilin (giving pale, clay-colored stools). Cholangiocarcinoma has different risk factors, including primary sclerosing cholangitis and liver flukes such as Clonorchis sinensis.","whyNot":["This pattern fits Gilbert syndrome or another mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. It does not fit a high direct bilirubin.","This pattern fits hemolysis. Unconjugated bilirubin is bound to albumin and does not enter urine, while increased bilirubin delivery to the gut raises urobilinogen.","Bilirubin in urine with increased urobilinogen fits hepatocellular disease such as hepatitis, where some bilirubin still reaches the gut. Duct obstruction lowers urobilinogen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Obstructive jaundice","Gallbladder cancer and cholangiocarcinoma"],"comps":["GI-13-K","GI-9","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C24-Q3","stem":"At surgery, the tumor has spread from the gallbladder neck along the free edge of the lesser omentum into the transverse fissure on the visceral surface of the liver. Which of the following groups of structures, passing through this fissure, is at greatest risk of encasement?","options":["Right, middle, and left hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava","Splenic artery, splenic vein, and the tail of the pancreas","Portal vein, hepatic arteries, and hepatic ducts","Left gastric artery, left gastric vein, and esophageal branches"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the transverse fissure on the visceral surface of the liver as the porta hepatis.","Recall that the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct run in the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) to the porta.","Exclude vessels that leave the liver posteriorly near the bare area."],"why":"The porta hepatis is the transverse fissure on the visceral surface of the liver, between the quadrate and caudate lobes. The portal vein and hepatic arteries enter the liver there, and the right and left hepatic ducts leave it and join to form the common hepatic duct. Bile then flows down the common hepatic duct, joins the cystic duct to form the common bile duct, and meets the pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater under the sphincter of Oddi.","whyNot":["The hepatic veins drain directly into the inferior vena cava on the posterior surface of the liver near the bare area, away from the porta hepatis.","The splenic vessels run along the pancreas to the splenic hilum, far from the liver.","Correct.","The left gastric vessels run along the lesser curvature of the stomach toward the esophagus, away from the free edge of the lesser omentum."],"study":{"topics":["Porta hepatis","Path of bile from liver to duodenum"],"comps":["GI-3"]}},{"id":"GI-C24-Q4","stem":"Her gallstones formed in part because bile is concentrated during storage in the gallbladder. Which of the following processes concentrates gallbladder bile?","options":["Active sodium absorption with water following osmotically","Active secretion of bile acids by the gallbladder epithelium","Cholecystokinin-stimulated secretion of chloride and water","Filtration of bile water through the gallbladder serosa"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that gallbladder epithelium absorbs sodium actively, with chloride and water following.","Note that bile acids, cholesterol, lecithin, and bilirubin stay behind and become more concentrated.","Link prolonged storage and concentration to stone formation."],"why":"The gallbladder epithelium actively absorbs sodium, and chloride and water follow. Bile acids, cholesterol, lecithin, and bilirubin are left behind, so gallbladder bile is often about five times more concentrated than hepatic bile and can be concentrated more than tenfold. When emptying is infrequent, as in fasting, pregnancy, or rapid weight loss, concentrated bile stays longer in the gallbladder, and cholesterol and calcium bilirubinate can crystallize.","whyNot":["Correct.","Bile acids are made and secreted by hepatocytes. The gallbladder stores them and does not secrete them.","Cholecystokinin contracts the gallbladder to empty it. Secretion of chloride and water would dilute bile, as secretin does in the bile ducts.","Bile water is not filtered across the serosa. It leaves bile through the mucosa, following absorbed sodium."],"study":{"topics":["Gallbladder concentration of bile","Stasis and gallstone formation"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-D2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C25","system":"GI","dx":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis with pernicious anemia","codes":["GI-13-E1","GI-13-G7"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis comes to the physician because of fatigue and tingling in both feet for 6 months. Vibration sense is decreased in the toes. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.2 g/dL (12 to 16), mean corpuscular volume is 114 fL (80 to 100), and a peripheral blood smear shows hypersegmented neutrophils. Serum vitamin B12 concentration is 105 pg/mL (200 to 900). Serum antibodies to intrinsic factor and to parietal cells are present. Upper endoscopy shows thin, flat mucosa with loss of rugal folds in the gastric body and fundus and a normal antrum. Biopsy specimens of the body show loss of oxyntic glands, a lymphocytic and plasma cell infiltrate, and intestinal metaplasia. Diagnosis: autoimmune atrophic gastritis with pernicious anemia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C25-Q1","stem":"Serum gastrin concentration is 850 pg/mL (under 100). Persistent elevation of this hormone places her at increased risk for tumors arising from which of the following cell types?","options":["Parietal cells of the fundic glands","Chief cells of the fundic glands","Enterochromaffin-like cells","Somatostatin-secreting antral D cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-E1","obj":"GI-13-E.1","dok":3,"approach":["Link loss of parietal cells to achlorhydria.","Without acid, antral D cells release less somatostatin, and G cells secrete gastrin unchecked.","Gastrin is trophic for enterochromaffin-like cells, which become hyperplastic and can form carcinoid tumors."],"why":"Destruction of parietal cells removes gastric acid. Acid normally stimulates antral D cells to release somatostatin, which restrains G cells, so achlorhydria lets gastrin rise steeply. Gastrin acts on CCK2 receptors of enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells in the body mucosa, stimulating histamine release and cell growth. Chronic stimulation causes ECL cell hyperplasia and, in some patients, multiple small gastric neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumors. Achlorhydria also impairs iron and B12 absorption and allows bacterial overgrowth.","whyNot":["Parietal cells have gastrin receptors, but they are the target of the autoimmune attack and are destroyed.","Chief cells, which secrete pepsinogen, are lost along with parietal cells when the oxyntic glands atrophy.","Correct.","Antral D cells release somatostatin in response to acid. With achlorhydria they are less active, which is one reason gastrin rises."],"study":{"topics":["Achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia","ECL cell hyperplasia and carcinoid tumors"],"comps":["GI-13-E1","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C25-Q2","stem":"Serum methylmalonic acid concentration is 1.1 µmol/L (0.07 to 0.27), and homocysteine is 31 µmol/L (5 to 15). Reduced activity of which of the following enzymes accounts for the result that distinguishes her anemia from folate deficiency?","options":["Methionine synthase","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase","Dihydrofolate reductase","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note that both methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are high.","Recall that B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, while folate is needed only in the methionine synthase reaction.","Conclude that the high methylmalonic acid points to B12 deficiency through loss of mutase activity."],"why":"Vitamin B12 is the cofactor for two enzymes. Methionine synthase uses methylcobalamin to move a methyl group from N5-methyltetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, so a lack of either B12 or folate raises homocysteine. Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase uses adenosylcobalamin to convert methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, and only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid. Abnormal fatty acid handling from this block is thought to contribute to the myelin damage behind her neuropathy.","whyNot":["Methionine synthase activity falls in both B12 and folate deficiency, so a high homocysteine does not separate the two.","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase uses biotin. Its deficiency causes propionic acidemia.","Dihydrofolate reductase regenerates tetrahydrofolate and is the target of methotrexate and trimethoprim. It does not need B12.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["B12 versus folate deficiency","B12-dependent enzymes"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-E1"]}},{"id":"GI-C25-Q3","stem":"Which of the following serum markers is most likely to be decreased as a direct result of the glandular loss in her gastric body?","options":["Chromogranin A","Pepsinogen I","Secretin","Cholecystokinin"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cells of the oxyntic glands of the body and fundus: parietal cells, chief cells, mucous neck cells, and ECL cells.","Recall that chief cells secrete pepsinogen, mainly pepsinogen I in the body and fundus.","Predict that atrophy of these glands lowers acid, intrinsic factor, and pepsinogen I, while antral G cells are spared."],"why":"Autoimmune gastritis destroys the oxyntic glands of the body and fundus. Parietal cells make hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, and chief cells make pepsinogen, so all three fall. Pepsinogen I comes almost entirely from chief and mucous neck cells of the body and fundus, so a low serum pepsinogen I (or a low pepsinogen I to II ratio) is a marker of body atrophy. Antral G cells survive and secrete more gastrin.","whyNot":["Chromogranin A is released by neuroendocrine cells. It rises in this disease because hypergastrinemia expands the ECL cell population.","Correct.","Secretin comes from duodenal S cells, which are not affected by gastric atrophy.","Cholecystokinin comes from I cells of the duodenum and jejunum, outside the stomach."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types and secretions"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"GI-C25-Q4","stem":"Her brother has chronic gastritis caused by Helicobacter pylori infection. Compared with her disease, his gastritis is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Antibodies against the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase","Hypergastrinemia with enterochromaffin-like cell hyperplasia","Body-predominant atrophy with achlorhydria","Antrum-predominant inflammation with normal acid output"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-G7","obj":"GI-13-G.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recall where each type starts: H. pylori in the antrum, autoimmune gastritis in the body and fundus.","Predict acid output: antral infection spares parietal cells, so acid is normal or high.","Assign autoantibodies, achlorhydria, and marked hypergastrinemia to the autoimmune type."],"why":"H. pylori colonizes the antrum and causes chronic active gastritis with neutrophils, lymphoid follicles, and later intestinal metaplasia. Parietal cells in the body are spared early, so acid output is normal or increased, which predisposes to duodenal ulcer. H. pylori gastritis raises the risk of intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma. Autoimmune gastritis targets the body and fundus, with antibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor, achlorhydria, and pernicious anemia.","whyNot":["Antibodies to the H+/K+-ATPase of parietal cells are the hallmark of autoimmune gastritis.","Marked hypergastrinemia with ECL cell hyperplasia follows achlorhydria, which is typical of autoimmune gastritis.","Body-predominant atrophy with achlorhydria is the pattern of autoimmune gastritis. Long-standing H. pylori pangastritis can reach this stage, but it is not the typical pattern.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["H. pylori versus autoimmune gastritis"],"comps":["GI-13-G7","GI-12"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C26","system":"GI","dx":"Dermatitis herpetiformis with gluten-sensitive enteropathy","codes":["GI-13-E2"],"case":"A 27-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of intensely itchy blisters on his elbows, knees, and buttocks, with bloating and loose stools. Examination shows grouped vesicles and excoriated papules on the extensor surfaces. Hemoglobin concentration is 10.8 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), mean corpuscular volume is 73 fL (80 to 100), and serum ferritin is 7 ng/mL (24 to 336). Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin shows granular IgA deposits in the dermal papillae. Serum IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase are markedly increased. Biopsy specimens from the second part of the duodenum show villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes. Diagnosis: dermatitis herpetiformis with gluten-sensitive enteropathy.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C26-Q1","stem":"Which of the following steps makes dietary gliadin peptides strongly immunogenic in this patient?","options":["Deamidation by tissue transglutaminase, increasing binding to HLA-DQ2","Crosslinking of T cell receptor V beta chains by gliadin as a superantigen","Binding of gliadin to IgE on intestinal mast cells, causing degranulation","Activation of the lectin complement pathway by gliadin on the epithelium"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gliadin is rich in glutamine and proline and resists complete digestion.","Tissue transglutaminase converts glutamine residues to negatively charged glutamate (deamidation).","Deamidated peptides bind HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 tightly and activate gluten-specific CD4 T cells."],"why":"Gliadin peptides resist digestion and cross the epithelium. In the lamina propria, tissue transglutaminase deamidates their glutamine residues to glutamate. The added negative charges let the peptides bind tightly in the groove of HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 on antigen-presenting cells, which present them to CD4 T cells. These T cells drive inflammation, largely through interferon-gamma, and help B cells make IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase, endomysium, and deamidated gliadin. In dermatitis herpetiformis, IgA targets epidermal transglutaminase and deposits in the dermal papillae.","whyNot":["Correct.","Superantigens such as toxic shock syndrome toxin bind outside the antigen groove and activate many T cells nonspecifically. Gliadin is processed and presented in the HLA groove.","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation describes a food allergy such as wheat allergy, a type I reaction. Celiac disease is T cell mediated.","Complement activation by gliadin is not the driving mechanism. The disease depends on antigen presentation by HLA-DQ2 or DQ8."],"study":{"topics":["Celiac disease immunopathogenesis","HLA-DQ2 and DQ8"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-E2"]}},{"id":"GI-C26-Q2","stem":"Most of the intraepithelial lymphocytes in his duodenal biopsy are CD8+ T cells. If he does not follow a gluten-free diet, chronic activation of this cell population most increases his risk of which of the following malignancies?","options":["Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","Gastric extranodal marginal zone B-cell lymphoma","Burkitt lymphoma of the terminal ileum","Adenocarcinoma of the sigmoid colon"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E2","obj":"GI-13-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note that celiac disease expands intraepithelial T lymphocytes in the small bowel.","Recall that long-standing, untreated celiac disease predisposes to a T-cell lymphoma arising from these cells.","Separate it from B-cell lymphomas and carcinomas with other causes."],"why":"In celiac disease, intraepithelial CD8+ T cells expand and are activated by IL-15 from the stressed epithelium. Persistent stimulation in untreated or refractory disease can lead to clonal expansion and enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma, usually in the jejunum. Other findings of celiac disease are villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia, worst in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum, iron deficiency anemia, and dermatitis herpetiformis. A strict gluten-free diet lowers the lymphoma risk.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gastric MALT lymphoma is a B-cell lymphoma driven by chronic Helicobacter pylori infection.","Burkitt lymphoma is a B-cell lymphoma with a MYC translocation, linked to Epstein-Barr virus, and often found in the ileocecal region in sporadic cases. It is not linked to celiac disease.","Colonic adenocarcinoma follows the adenoma to carcinoma sequence and is not a typical complication of celiac disease. Celiac disease does raise the risk of small-bowel adenocarcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of celiac disease","Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma"],"comps":["GI-13-E2","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C26-Q3","stem":"His biopsy is compared with a full-thickness section of normal bowel wall. The section shows tall, closely spaced circular mucosal folds covered by long villi, with no glands in the submucosa and no aggregated lymphoid follicles. From which of the following segments was this section most likely taken?","options":["Ascending colon","Jejunum","Ileum","Duodenum"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.4","dok":3,"approach":["Use the absence of submucosal glands to exclude the duodenum.","Use the absence of aggregated lymphoid follicles (Peyer patches) to exclude the ileum, and the villi to exclude the colon.","Recall that tall, closely spaced plicae circulares are most prominent in the jejunum."],"why":"Each region of the bowel has a histologic signature. The duodenum has Brunner glands in the submucosa, the ileum has Peyer patches, and the colon has crypts full of goblet cells and no villi. The jejunum has the tallest and most numerous plicae circulares and long villi, without those other markers. Together with the distal duodenum, it is where celiac disease is most severe.","whyNot":["The colon has no villi and no plicae circulares. Its mucosa is flat, with straight crypts rich in goblet cells.","Correct.","The ileum has Peyer patches, aggregated lymphoid follicles in the mucosa and submucosa, which are absent here.","The duodenum has Brunner glands in its submucosa, which are absent here."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of the small intestine segments"],"comps":["GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C26-Q4","stem":"His serum vitamin B12 concentration is normal. Which of the following pairs of absorption sites best explains why he is deficient in iron but not in vitamin B12?","options":["Iron in the jejunum; vitamin B12 in the duodenum","Iron in the duodenum; vitamin B12 in the terminal ileum","Iron in the stomach; vitamin B12 in the proximal ileum","Iron in the terminal ileum; vitamin B12 in the proximal jejunum"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note that celiac disease is worst in the proximal small bowel and often spares the ileum.","Recall that iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum and B12 in the terminal ileum.","Predict iron deficiency with preserved B12 absorption."],"why":"Iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum and upper jejunum through DMT1 after reduction to the ferrous form. Folate is absorbed mainly in the jejunum, and vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor and bile acids are absorbed in the terminal ileum. Celiac disease damages the proximal small bowel most, so iron deficiency is common, folate deficiency can follow, and B12 deficiency is less common. Ileal resection, as in Crohn disease, produces the opposite pattern, with B12 and bile acid loss.","whyNot":["The jejunum is the main site of folate absorption. B12 is not absorbed in the duodenum, where it is released from haptocorrin before binding intrinsic factor.","Correct.","The stomach releases iron and B12 from food with acid and pepsin but absorbs neither, and B12 uptake occurs in the terminal ileum.","The terminal ileum absorbs B12 and bile acids and absorbs little iron."],"study":{"topics":["Sites of nutrient absorption"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-13-E2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C27","system":"GI","dx":"Adult-type lactase deficiency","codes":["GI-13-E4"],"case":"A 31-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of bloating, cramping abdominal pain, flatulence, and watery diarrhea that begin 1 to 2 hours after he drinks milk or eats ice cream. Symptoms do not occur when he avoids dairy foods. His weight is stable. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Stool is negative for occult blood and leukocytes. Serum IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase are absent. After an oral load of 25 g of lactose, breath hydrogen concentration rises by 45 ppm (a rise of 20 ppm or more is abnormal). Duodenal biopsy specimens show normal villi. Diagnosis: adult-type (primary) lactase deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C27-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the rise in breath hydrogen after the lactose load?","options":["Hepatic metabolism of excess absorbed glucose","Hydrolysis of lactose by gastric acid in the stomach","Colonic bacterial fermentation of undigested lactose","Mucosal oxidation of absorbed galactose in the jejunum"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E4","obj":"GI-13-E.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that human cells do not produce hydrogen gas.","Undigested lactose passes to the colon, where bacteria ferment it to short-chain fatty acids, hydrogen, methane, and carbon dioxide.","Hydrogen is absorbed into blood and exhaled, so breath hydrogen rises."],"why":"Lactase on the brush border of the small intestine splits lactose into glucose and galactose. In adult-type lactase deficiency, lactase expression declines after weaning, so lactose stays in the lumen, holds water osmotically, and reaches the colon. Colonic bacteria ferment it, producing gas (hydrogen, carbon dioxide, methane) that causes bloating and flatulence, and the hydrogen is exhaled. Normal villi separate this primary form from secondary lactase deficiency after viral enteritis or celiac disease, in which the brush border is damaged.","whyNot":["Absorbed glucose is metabolized to carbon dioxide and water. Breath hydrogen comes from bacterial fermentation.","Gastric acid does not meaningfully hydrolyze lactose. Its digestion depends on brush-border lactase.","Correct.","Human cells oxidize galactose and glucose to carbon dioxide and water. They do not produce hydrogen gas."],"study":{"topics":["Primary and secondary lactase deficiency","Hydrogen breath test"],"comps":["GI-13-E4","GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C27-Q2","stem":"He begins drinking milk pretreated with lactase enzyme and has no symptoms. Which of the following apical transporters absorbs both monosaccharides released from this lactose?","options":["Insulin-responsive glucose transporter (GLUT4)","Proton-coupled peptide transporter (PepT1)","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 1 (SGLT1)","Facilitative fructose transporter (GLUT5)"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the products of lactose hydrolysis: glucose and galactose.","Recall that both enter the enterocyte with sodium through SGLT1 on the apical membrane.","Note that they then leave across the basolateral membrane through GLUT2."],"why":"Lactose yields glucose and galactose, and both are taken up at the brush border by SGLT1, which couples their entry to sodium moving down the gradient kept by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase. Fructose enters by facilitated diffusion through GLUT5. All three monosaccharides exit to the blood through GLUT2. Because SGLT1 also carries sodium and water follows, glucose plus sodium in oral rehydration solution increases water absorption even when cholera toxin is driving secretion.","whyNot":["GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive transporter of skeletal muscle and adipose tissue and has no role in intestinal absorption.","PepT1 absorbs dipeptides and tripeptides with hydrogen ions and does not carry sugars.","Correct.","GLUT5 carries fructose by facilitated diffusion and does not carry glucose or galactose well."],"study":{"topics":["Monosaccharide transporters","Oral rehydration solution"],"comps":["GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C27-Q3","stem":"Before diagnosis, a stool sample collected after a dairy meal shows sodium 32 mmol/L, potassium 28 mmol/L, and osmolality 290 mOsm/kg. Which of the following best describes his diarrhea?","options":["Osmotic diarrhea that stops with fasting","Secretory diarrhea from raised enterocyte cAMP","Inflammatory diarrhea with fecal leukocytes","Secretory diarrhea that persists with fasting"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate the stool osmotic gap: 290 minus 2 times (32 + 28) = 170 mOsm/kg.","A gap above about 125 means unmeasured solutes such as lactose hold water in the lumen. A gap below about 50 means electrolytes do, as in secretory diarrhea.","Osmotic diarrhea stops when the offending solute is no longer eaten, as with fasting."],"why":"The stool osmotic gap estimates solutes other than sodium and potassium salts. Here it is 170 mOsm/kg, so most stool osmolality comes from unmeasured solutes, the undigested lactose and its fermentation products. Osmotic diarrhea stops with fasting because the solute is no longer delivered. Secretory diarrhea, such as cholera toxin raising cAMP and opening CFTR chloride channels, has a small gap and continues during fasting. Inflammatory diarrhea shows blood and leukocytes in stool.","whyNot":["Correct.","Raised cAMP opening CFTR chloride channels is the mechanism of cholera toxin, which causes secretory diarrhea with a small gap.","Inflammatory diarrhea produces blood and leukocytes in stool, and his stool is negative for both.","Secretory diarrhea has a small osmotic gap (under about 50) because secreted sodium and chloride account for stool osmolality, and it continues during fasting."],"study":{"topics":["Stool osmotic gap","Osmotic versus secretory diarrhea"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-13-E4"]}},{"id":"GI-C27-Q4","stem":"Two years later, he has taken 150 mg of elemental zinc daily for 10 months to prevent colds. He now has an unsteady gait and decreased vibration and position sense in the legs. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.8 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), and absolute neutrophil count is 900/mm3 (1,500 to 8,000). Serum vitamin B12 concentration is normal. Deficiency of which of the following minerals is most likely?","options":["Iron","Magnesium","Copper","Selenium"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize anemia, neutropenia, and a posterior column myelopathy with a normal B12.","Link this combination to copper deficiency.","Identify excess zinc as the cause: zinc induces metallothionein in enterocytes, which traps dietary copper."],"why":"High zinc intake induces metallothionein in enterocytes. Metallothionein binds copper more tightly than zinc and holds it in the cell, and the copper is lost in stool when the enterocyte is shed. Copper is a cofactor for enzymes such as cytochrome c oxidase and for iron handling (ceruloplasmin and hephaestin oxidize iron for transport), so deficiency causes anemia, neutropenia, and a myelopathy that mimics B12 deficiency. Gastric bypass surgery and malabsorption are other causes.","whyNot":["Iron deficiency causes a microcytic anemia but not neutropenia or a myelopathy.","Magnesium deficiency causes muscle cramps, tetany, arrhythmias, and low calcium and potassium.","Correct.","Selenium deficiency causes cardiomyopathy (Keshan disease) and muscle weakness, without anemia and neutropenia."],"study":{"topics":["Copper deficiency from excess zinc","Trace mineral deficiencies"],"comps":["GI-8"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C28","system":"GI","dx":"Short bowel syndrome after ileal resection for Crohn disease","codes":["GI-13-E"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman with Crohn disease comes to the physician because of 6 to 8 loose, greasy, foul-smelling stools daily and a 9-kg (20-lb) weight loss. Over the past 3 years, repeated resections for strictures and fistulas removed most of her ileum, including the terminal ileum and ileocecal valve, leaving about 150 cm of small bowel joined to an intact colon. Hemoglobin concentration is 10.1 g/dL (12 to 16), and mean corpuscular volume is 108 fL (80 to 100). Serum vitamin B12 concentration is 120 pg/mL (200 to 900). Fecal fat excretion is 22 g/day (under 7). Two weeks ago she passed a kidney stone composed of calcium oxalate. Diagnosis: short bowel syndrome after ileal resection.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C28-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her calcium oxalate kidney stone?","options":["Increased hepatic synthesis of oxalate from glyoxylate","Uric acid supersaturation from loss of bicarbonate in stool","Increased intestinal calcium absorption causing hypercalciuria","Unabsorbed fatty acids binding calcium in the gut lumen"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E","obj":"GI-13-E.5","dok":3,"approach":["Link fat malabsorption to excess fatty acids in the lumen.","Fatty acids bind calcium, so less calcium is available to bind dietary oxalate.","Free, soluble oxalate is absorbed in the retained colon, and urinary oxalate rises."],"why":"Dietary calcium normally binds oxalate in the gut lumen, forming an insoluble salt that leaves in stool. With fat malabsorption, unabsorbed fatty acids bind calcium (saponification), leaving oxalate free and soluble. Unabsorbed bile acids and fatty acids also increase colonic permeability, so the colon absorbs much more oxalate. This enteric hyperoxaluria causes calcium oxalate stones and requires an intact colon. Her ileal resection also explains the steatorrhea (bile acid loss) and B12 deficiency (loss of the receptor for intrinsic factor and B12).","whyNot":["Increased hepatic oxalate production occurs in primary hyperoxaluria, an inherited enzyme defect. Her problem starts in the gut.","Loss of bicarbonate and volume in diarrhea does favor uric acid stones, but her stone is calcium oxalate.","Calcium absorption falls with fat malabsorption, because calcium is bound by fatty acids and vitamin D is poorly absorbed.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Short bowel syndrome","Enteric hyperoxaluria"],"comps":["GI-13-E","GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C28-Q2","stem":"Her resected ileum showed transmural inflammation, fissuring ulcers, and noncaseating granulomas. Which of the following immune mechanisms best explains the granulomas?","options":["IL-4 and IL-5 from Th2 cells recruiting eosinophils","Histamine release from mast cells triggered by IgE","Complement activation by immune complexes in vessel walls","Interferon-gamma from Th1 cells activating macrophages"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that granulomas are collections of activated macrophages (epithelioid cells) and giant cells.","Macrophage activation is driven by interferon-gamma from Th1 cells, supported by IL-12 from macrophages.","Place this in Crohn disease: NOD2 variants impair handling of commensal bacteria, and Th1 and Th17 responses drive transmural inflammation."],"why":"In Crohn disease, NOD2 variants impair intracellular sensing of bacterial peptidoglycan, so commensal bacteria that cross the epithelium are cleared poorly. Persistent antigen drives macrophages and dendritic cells to make IL-12 and IL-23, which push CD4 T cells toward Th1 and Th17 responses. Interferon-gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages, which become epithelioid cells and form granulomas, while IL-17 recruits neutrophils. The inflammation extends through the full thickness of the wall, causing strictures and fistulas.","whyNot":["A Th2 response with IL-4, IL-5, and eosinophils fits allergic disease and helminth infection and does not form these granulomas.","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation is an immediate (type I) hypersensitivity reaction and does not form granulomas.","Immune complex deposition (type III hypersensitivity) causes vasculitis and glomerulonephritis. It does not form granulomas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Crohn disease immunopathogenesis","NOD2 and Th1 and Th17 responses"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"GI-C28-Q3","stem":"Despite adequate total calories, she develops dry, scaly dermatitis, hair thinning, and poor wound healing. The plasma triene to tetraene ratio is 0.5 (under 0.2). Lack of which of the following dietary fatty acids is the primary cause?","options":["Palmitic acid","Linoleic acid","Oleic acid","Arachidonic acid"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.1","dok":3,"approach":["Link severe fat malabsorption to essential fatty acid deficiency.","Recall that humans cannot insert double bonds beyond carbon 9, so linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) must come from the diet.","Read the high triene to tetraene ratio: without linoleic acid, the body makes Mead acid (a triene) instead of arachidonic acid (a tetraene)."],"why":"Linoleic and alpha-linolenic acids are the essential fatty acids because humans lack the desaturases that add double bonds beyond carbon 9. Chronic steatorrhea can cause deficiency even when calories are adequate. Without linoleic acid, arachidonic acid falls and oleic acid is converted to Mead acid, raising the triene to tetraene ratio above about 0.2. Deficiency causes scaly dermatitis, hair loss, and poor wound healing.","whyNot":["Palmitic acid is the main product of fatty acid synthase and can be made from carbohydrate, so it is not essential.","Correct.","Oleic acid is made by desaturating stearic acid at carbon 9. In essential fatty acid deficiency, oleic acid is the precursor of Mead acid.","Arachidonic acid is made from linoleic acid, so it is needed in the diet only when linoleic acid is lacking. Its fall here follows the linoleic acid deficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Essential fatty acids","Essential fatty acid deficiency"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-E"]}},{"id":"GI-C28-Q4","stem":"A semi-elemental enteral formula containing mostly dipeptides and tripeptides is chosen to improve her protein absorption. Which of the following transport mechanisms moves these peptides into her enterocytes?","options":["Proton cotransport through PepT1","Endocytosis by microfold (M) cells","Sodium cotransport through SGLT1","Facilitated diffusion through GLUT5"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that protein digestion yields free amino acids and small peptides.","Free amino acids enter mainly with sodium, while dipeptides and tripeptides enter with hydrogen ions through PepT1.","Inside the enterocyte, peptidases split them into amino acids that exit to the blood."],"why":"Brush-border peptidases leave a mix of free amino acids and dipeptides and tripeptides. Free amino acids enter through several sodium-linked transporters, while di- and tripeptides enter through PepT1, which uses the hydrogen ion gradient kept by the apical Na+/H+ exchanger. Because peptides use a separate carrier, they are absorbed well even when single amino acid transporters are defective. In Hartnup disease, loss of a neutral amino acid transporter causes tryptophan deficiency and pellagra-like findings, yet tryptophan in peptides is still absorbed.","whyNot":["Correct.","M cells over Peyer patches sample particles and microbes for the immune system and do not absorb dietary protein in meaningful amounts.","SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose with sodium. It does not carry peptides.","GLUT5 carries fructose by facilitated diffusion."],"study":{"topics":["Amino acid and peptide absorption","Hartnup disease"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-8"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C29","system":"GI","dx":"Bacterial overgrowth in a blind loop after Billroth II gastrectomy","codes":["GI-13-E"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of bulky, foul-smelling, greasy stools, bloating, numbness in both feet, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. Twenty years ago he had a partial gastrectomy with Billroth II gastrojejunostomy for a bleeding peptic ulcer. Hemoglobin concentration is 10.2 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), and mean corpuscular volume is 110 fL (80 to 100). Serum vitamin B12 concentration is 140 pg/mL (200 to 900), and serum folate is 24 ng/mL (2.7 to 17). Fecal fat excretion is 18 g/day (under 7). Culture of jejunal aspirate grows more than 100,000 colony-forming units/mL of mixed anaerobic and aerobic bacteria. Diagnosis: bacterial overgrowth in the afferent (blind) loop.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C29-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the combination of a low serum vitamin B12 and a high serum folate concentration?","options":["Bacterial uptake of B12 and bacterial synthesis of folate","Ethanol-induced malabsorption of both B12 and folate","Ileal mucosal damage with preserved jejunal folate uptake","Autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor with a high-folate diet"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E","obj":"GI-13-E.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the stasis in the afferent loop and the heavy bacterial growth in the aspirate.","Recall that anaerobic bacteria take up B12 in the lumen before it reaches the ileum.","Recall that many intestinal bacteria synthesize folate, which is absorbed and raises serum folate."],"why":"Food does not pass through the afferent loop of a Billroth II, so stasis lets colonic-type bacteria multiply. These bacteria take up vitamin B12 in the lumen, so less reaches the ileum, causing B12 deficiency with macrocytic anemia and neuropathy. Many of the same bacteria make folate, which the jejunum absorbs, so serum folate is normal or high. Bacterial deconjugation of bile acids adds steatorrhea. This pattern helps separate bacterial overgrowth from other causes of malabsorption, which usually lower folate too.","whyNot":["Correct.","Alcohol use lowers folate, which is the opposite of what is seen here.","Ileal mucosal disease would lower B12, but it does not raise folate, and the culture points to overgrowth as the cause.","Pernicious anemia lowers B12 through loss of intrinsic factor, but it does not explain a high folate, and nothing here points to an autoimmune process."],"study":{"topics":["Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Blind loop syndrome"],"comps":["GI-13-E","GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C29-Q2","stem":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency is also considered. After a 25-g oral dose of D-xylose, urinary xylose excretion over 5 hours is 2.1 g (normal above 4), and fecal elastase is 380 µg/g (normal above 200). Which of the following is the best interpretation of these results?","options":["Pancreatic lipase output is too low for fat digestion","The defect lies in the small-bowel lumen or mucosa","Xylose uptake requires bile acid micelles in the lumen","Xylose absorption requires pancreatic amylase activity"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-E","obj":"GI-13-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that D-xylose is absorbed directly by the small-bowel mucosa without pancreatic enzymes or bile.","A low urinary xylose therefore points to mucosal disease or bacterial consumption of xylose in the lumen.","A normal fecal elastase confirms normal pancreatic enzyme output."],"why":"D-xylose is a pentose that needs no digestion, so its absorption tests the surface and lumen of the small bowel. In pancreatic insufficiency, xylose absorption is normal while fat is poorly digested, and fecal elastase is low. In mucosal disease such as celiac disease, or in bacterial overgrowth, urinary xylose is low. In overgrowth the bacteria consume xylose in the lumen, so the result typically improves after antibiotics. A related enzyme problem, congenital enteropeptidase deficiency, blocks activation of trypsinogen and therefore of the other pancreatic proteases.","whyNot":["Low pancreatic lipase output would come with a low fecal elastase, and his elastase is normal. Pancreatic insufficiency also leaves D-xylose absorption normal.","Correct.","D-xylose is water soluble and is absorbed without micelles. Bile acids are needed for fat absorption.","D-xylose is a monosaccharide and needs no amylase. That is why it separates mucosal from pancreatic causes."],"study":{"topics":["D-xylose test","Pancreatic versus mucosal malabsorption"],"comps":["GI-13-E","GI-13-E3"]}},{"id":"GI-C29-Q3","stem":"In normal vitamin B12 absorption, pancreatic juice mixes with gastric contents in the duodenum. Which of the following is the role of pancreatic proteases in this process?","options":["Cleaving intrinsic factor so B12 can enter ileal cells","Releasing B12 from the proteins in which it is ingested","Degrading haptocorrin so B12 can bind intrinsic factor","Converting cyanocobalamin to active coenzyme forms"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Trace B12 in order: release from food, binding to haptocorrin, release from haptocorrin, binding to intrinsic factor, ileal uptake.","Place the pancreatic proteases at the duodenal step.","Predict that pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 absorption by leaving B12 trapped on haptocorrin."],"why":"Gastric acid and pepsin release B12 from food proteins, and free B12 binds haptocorrin (R binder) from saliva and the stomach. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade haptocorrin, releasing B12 to bind intrinsic factor from parietal cells. The intrinsic factor and B12 complex is taken up in the terminal ileum by the cubilin receptor. In the blood, B12 travels on transcobalamin II to the tissues.","whyNot":["Intrinsic factor stays intact. The whole intrinsic factor and B12 complex binds the cubilin receptor in the terminal ileum.","Release of B12 from food proteins is done by gastric acid and pepsin, which is why low acid states can impair B12 absorption.","Correct.","Conversion to methylcobalamin and adenosylcobalamin happens inside cells after absorption."],"study":{"topics":["Steps of vitamin B12 absorption"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-13-E1"]}},{"id":"GI-C29-Q4","stem":"His steatorrhea results in part from bacterial deconjugation of bile acids in the afferent loop. Which of the following best explains why deconjugated bile acids leave the jejunal lumen too early?","options":["They are packaged into chylomicrons with dietary lipids","They are degraded by pancreatic lipase to free cholesterol","They are less ionized and diffuse passively across the mucosa","They bind the sodium-dependent bile acid transporter of the jejunum"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that conjugation with glycine or taurine lowers the pKa of bile acids, keeping them ionized at intestinal pH.","Ionized bile acids cannot cross the mucosa passively, so they stay in the lumen to form micelles until the terminal ileum reclaims them.","Deconjugated bile acids are less ionized, so they diffuse out early and the luminal concentration falls."],"why":"In the normal enterohepatic circulation, conjugated bile acids stay in the lumen through the jejunum, form micelles that carry fats to the mucosa, and are reabsorbed actively in the terminal ileum. Bacteria in a blind loop deconjugate them. Unconjugated bile acids have a higher pKa, so more are un-ionized, and they are absorbed passively in the proximal bowel or precipitate. The luminal bile acid concentration falls below what is needed for micelles, and fat is malabsorbed.","whyNot":["Chylomicrons carry triglycerides, cholesterol esters, and fat-soluble vitamins. Bile acids are not packaged into them.","Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides. It does not degrade bile acids, and bile acids are not turned back into cholesterol.","Correct.","The apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter is found in the terminal ileum. The jejunum lacks it."],"study":{"topics":["Bile acid conjugation and micelles","Enterohepatic circulation"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C30","system":"GI","dx":"Achalasia","codes":["GI-13-F1"],"case":"A 45-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of difficulty swallowing both solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food at night, and a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss. Barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus that tapers smoothly to a bird-beak narrowing at the gastroesophageal junction. Esophageal manometry shows absent peristalsis in the esophageal body and a high resting pressure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) with incomplete relaxation on swallowing. Upper endoscopy shows retained food and no mass at the gastroesophageal junction. Diagnosis: achalasia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C30-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the failure of her lower esophageal sphincter to relax?","options":["Loss of excitatory myenteric neurons releasing acetylcholine","Hypertrophy of LES muscle driven by excess gastrin","Smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis of the LES","Loss of myenteric neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-F1","obj":"GI-13-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note incomplete LES relaxation with aperistalsis on manometry.","Recall that LES relaxation during swallowing depends on inhibitory myenteric neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP.","Conclude that loss of these neurons leaves excitatory cholinergic tone unopposed."],"why":"LES tone at rest comes from the muscle itself and from excitatory cholinergic neurons. When a person swallows, inhibitory neurons of the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus release nitric oxide and VIP, which relax the sphincter. In achalasia these inhibitory neurons are selectively lost, probably through an immune attack, so the LES stays closed and its pressure rises. In Chagas disease, Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the same plexus and causes secondary achalasia.","whyNot":["Excitatory cholinergic neurons maintain LES tone and are relatively preserved. Losing them would lower LES pressure.","Gastrin can raise LES tone, but excess gastrin does not cause failed relaxation, and nothing here suggests hypergastrinemia.","Smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis occur in systemic sclerosis, which lowers LES pressure and causes reflux.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Achalasia pathophysiology","Enteric neurotransmitters"],"comps":["GI-13-F1","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C30-Q2","stem":"In a healthy person, manometry after a swallow shows a contraction that travels down the esophageal body in sequence. In this patient, it shows only weak contractions that occur at the same time along its length. Loss of which of the following normal mechanisms best explains this difference?","options":["Receptive relaxation of the proximal stomach","Slow-wave pacing generated by interstitial cells of Cajal","Motilin-driven migrating motor complexes","Relaxation below the bolus with contraction above it"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that peristalsis needs contraction above the bolus and relaxation below it, coordinated by the myenteric plexus.","Relaxation ahead of the bolus comes from inhibitory neurons, and longer inhibition in more distal segments sets the timing of the wave.","Without inhibitory neurons, all segments contract at once, so the bolus is not propelled."],"why":"Peristalsis is a reflex of the myenteric plexus. Excitatory neurons (acetylcholine, substance P) contract the muscle above a bolus, while inhibitory neurons (nitric oxide, VIP) relax the muscle below it. In the smooth muscle esophagus, inhibition that lasts longer in more distal segments delays their contraction and creates a sequential wave. Losing the inhibitory neurons in achalasia removes this delay, so contractions become simultaneous or absent. Elsewhere in the gut, slow waves from interstitial cells of Cajal set contraction frequency, and motilin drives the migrating motor complex during fasting.","whyNot":["Receptive relaxation is a vagal reflex that relaxes the proximal stomach as food arrives. It does not coordinate the esophageal wave.","Slow waves from interstitial cells of Cajal set the maximum contraction frequency in the stomach and intestine. The order of a swallow-induced esophageal wave depends on the myenteric plexus.","Motilin drives the migrating motor complex that sweeps the stomach and small intestine between meals. It does not produce the swallow-induced esophageal wave.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Peristalsis and the myenteric plexus","Slow waves and the migrating motor complex"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F1"]}},{"id":"GI-C30-Q3","stem":"She declines treatment. Fifteen years later, she has a 4-month history of worsening dysphagia, now with difficulty swallowing her own saliva, and a further 8-kg (18-lb) weight loss. Which of the following tumors is most likely?","options":["Squamous cell carcinoma of the mid esophagus","Adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus","Leiomyoma of the distal esophagus","Gastrointestinal stromal tumor of the cardia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K1","obj":"GI-13-K.1","dok":2,"approach":["Link long-standing untreated achalasia to stasis of food in a dilated esophagus.","Chronic stasis and irritation of the squamous lining raise the risk of squamous cell carcinoma, usually in the middle of the dilated esophagus.","Separate this from adenocarcinoma, which arises from Barrett esophagus after chronic reflux."],"why":"Food and fluid retained in the dilated esophagus cause chronic inflammation of the squamous lining. Over many years this raises the risk of squamous cell carcinoma, usually in the mid esophagus. Other risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma are alcohol, tobacco, caustic injury, and Plummer-Vinson syndrome. Adenocarcinoma arises in the distal third from Barrett esophagus, with GERD and obesity as risk factors. Both cancers present with progressive dysphagia, first to solids and then to liquids, with weight loss.","whyNot":["Correct.","Distal adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett esophagus after chronic reflux. An untreated achalasia sphincter tends to block reflux.","Leiomyomas are benign, slow-growing smooth muscle tumors and are not linked to achalasia.","Gastrointestinal stromal tumors arise from interstitial cells of Cajal, most often in the stomach, and are not linked to achalasia."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma versus adenocarcinoma"],"comps":["GI-13-K1","GI-13-G2"]}},{"id":"GI-C30-Q4","stem":"She later undergoes pneumatic balloon dilation, with the balloon centered on the most distal of the three normal narrowings of the esophagus. Which of the following forms this narrowing, and at what vertebral level?","options":["Median arcuate ligament at T12","Right crus of the diaphragm at T10","Aortic arch and left main bronchus at T4","Cricopharyngeus muscle at C6"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the three normal narrowings from top to bottom: cricopharyngeus, aortic arch and left main bronchus, diaphragmatic hiatus.","Recall that the most distal one is where the esophagus passes through the diaphragm at T10.","Identify the right crus as the muscle whose fibers form the esophageal hiatus."],"why":"The esophagus begins at the cricopharyngeus at about C6, descends through the superior and posterior mediastinum behind the trachea and left atrium, and passes through the esophageal hiatus at T10 to join the stomach. Fibers of the right crus of the diaphragm surround the hiatus and act as an external sphincter that adds to LES pressure. The three narrowings, at the cricopharyngeus, at the crossing of the aortic arch and left main bronchus, and at the hiatus, are common sites for swallowed objects to lodge and for caustic injury.","whyNot":["The median arcuate ligament forms the aortic hiatus at T12, which transmits the aorta and thoracic duct.","Correct.","The aortic arch and left main bronchus cross the esophagus in the thorax at about the T4 level, forming the middle narrowing.","The cricopharyngeus forms the upper esophageal sphincter at about C6, the most proximal narrowing."],"study":{"topics":["Course and narrowings of the esophagus"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-F1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C31","system":"GI","dx":"Adhesive small bowel obstruction after appendectomy","codes":["GI-13-I2","GI-13-F2"],"case":"A 38-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 2-day history of crampy periumbilical pain, repeated bilious vomiting, and abdominal distension. He has passed no stool or flatus for 24 hours. He underwent open appendectomy for perforated appendicitis 3 years ago. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mmHg. The abdomen is distended, with a healed right lower quadrant scar and high-pitched, tinkling bowel sounds. Serum studies show Na+ 136 mEq/L (135 to 145), K+ 3.1 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), Cl- 88 mEq/L (98 to 106), and HCO3- 34 mEq/L (22 to 28). CT scan shows dilated small bowel proximal to a transition point in the right lower quadrant, with collapsed bowel distally and no hernia or mass. The diagnosis is small bowel obstruction from postoperative adhesions.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C31-Q1","stem":"Which of the following processes most likely produced the fibrous band that is obstructing this patient's bowel?","options":["Implantation of ectopic endometrial tissue on the peritoneum at surgery","Organization of persistent serosal fibrin by fibroblasts into collagen","Granulomatous reaction to retained suture material forming a mass","Hypertrophic scarring of the skin incision tethering the underlying ileum"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-I2","obj":"GI-13-I.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: earlier surgery for perforated appendicitis, so the peritoneum was both cut and inflamed.","Injured mesothelium leaks a fibrin-rich exudate that glues nearby serosal surfaces together.","Fibrin that is not cleared becomes scaffolding for fibroblasts and new vessels, which lay down a collagen band that can kink or trap a loop of bowel."],"why":"Surgery and peritonitis injure the mesothelium and cause a fibrinous exudate between serosal surfaces. Normally, mesothelial cells release tissue plasminogen activator, and plasmin dissolves this fibrin within days. Injury and inflammation reduce this fibrinolysis, so the fibrin persists and is organized by fibroblasts into collagen bands. These adhesions are the most common cause of small bowel obstruction in adults who have had abdominal surgery.","whyNot":["Endometriosis can cause pelvic adhesions, but it comes from retrograde menstruation or metaplasia in women. It does not arise from a surgical procedure in a man.","Correct.","A suture granuloma forms a small nodule of macrophages around foreign material. It does not form the long fibrous bands that obstruct bowel.","Skin scars stay in the dermis. They do not reach through the abdominal wall muscles to bind the bowel."],"study":{"topics":["Formation of peritoneal adhesions","Causes of small bowel obstruction"],"comps":["GI-13-I2","GI-13-F2"]}},{"id":"GI-C31-Q2","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient best distinguishes his condition from adynamic ileus?","options":["High-pitched bowel sounds and a CT transition point","Abdominal distension with repeated bilious vomiting","Absence of stool and flatus for the past 24 hours","Serum potassium concentration below the reference range"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F2","obj":"GI-13-F.2","dok":3,"approach":["Both mechanical obstruction and adynamic ileus cause distension, vomiting, and no passage of stool or gas, so those findings cannot separate them.","In mechanical obstruction, the bowel above the block contracts hard, which makes high-pitched, tinkling sounds.","Imaging shows dilated loops only up to one point, with collapsed bowel beyond it. In ileus, the bowel is quiet and gas is spread diffusely through small bowel and colon."],"why":"Mechanical obstruction has a physical block. The bowel proximal to it works against the block, so bowel sounds are high pitched and rushing, and CT shows a transition point with dilated loops above and collapsed bowel below. Adynamic ileus is a failure of motility with no physical block. Bowel sounds are reduced or absent, and gas fills small bowel and colon diffusely, with no transition point.","whyNot":["Correct.","Distension and vomiting occur in both conditions because contents build up in either case. They do not tell you whether a physical block is present.","Obstipation occurs in both conditions. It tells you contents are not moving forward, without telling you why.","Low potassium is a known cause of adynamic ileus, so it would point away from mechanical obstruction. Here it is a result of vomiting."],"study":{"topics":["Mechanical obstruction versus adynamic ileus","Bowel sounds and imaging in obstruction"],"comps":["GI-13-F2","GI-13-I2"]}},{"id":"GI-C31-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why this patient's kidneys have not corrected his elevated serum bicarbonate concentration?","options":["Loss of pancreatic bicarbonate in the vomited intestinal fluid","Hypoaldosteronism that limits distal hydrogen ion secretion","Release of bicarbonate from bone as it buffers lost gastric acid","Chloride and volume depletion that preserve bicarbonate"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.7","dok":3,"approach":["Read the labs: high HCO3-, low Cl-, and low K+ after days of vomiting point to a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis.","Vomiting removes HCl, which generates the alkalosis. Something must also stop the kidney from excreting the extra bicarbonate.","Volume loss activates renin and aldosterone, and low chloride limits bicarbonate secretion in the collecting duct, so the kidney keeps reabsorbing bicarbonate."],"why":"Vomiting is triggered when the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema and visceral afferents from the distended gut activate the medullary vomiting center. Each liter of vomited gastric juice removes H+ and Cl-, which raises plasma bicarbonate. Volume depletion then activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which increases Na+ reabsorption with H+ and K+ secretion. Low chloride also limits the collecting duct exchanger that secretes bicarbonate in return for chloride. The kidney therefore holds on to bicarbonate until volume and chloride are replaced.","whyNot":["Losing bicarbonate-rich intestinal or pancreatic fluid lowers serum bicarbonate and causes metabolic acidosis. His bicarbonate is high.","Low aldosterone reduces H+ and K+ secretion and causes a hyperkalemic acidosis. Volume loss raises aldosterone in this patient.","Bone buffers acid in chronic metabolic acidosis, releasing carbonate and calcium. Losing acid does not draw bicarbonate out of bone.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Metabolic alkalosis from vomiting","Vomiting center and chemoreceptor trigger zone"],"comps":["GI-6","UR-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C31-Q4","stem":"At laparoscopy, the obstructing band attaches a loop of ileum to the parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall. Which of the following layers forms the outer surface of the ileal wall at the site of attachment?","options":["Muscularis externa with inner circular and outer longitudinal layers","Adventitia of dense connective tissue blending into nearby tissues","Serosa of mesothelium over a thin layer of loose connective tissue","Submucosa of dense irregular tissue containing the Meissner plexus"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Decide whether the ileum is intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal. It hangs on a mesentery, so it is intraperitoneal.","Intraperitoneal gut is covered by visceral peritoneum, which forms the serosa.","Adhesions join serosal (mesothelial) surfaces, so the band attaches to the serosa."],"why":"The gut wall has four layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and an outer serosa or adventitia. Intraperitoneal segments such as the jejunum and ileum are covered by serosa, which is a mesothelium on a thin layer of loose connective tissue. Adhesions form between injured mesothelial surfaces, which is why they link loops of bowel to one another and to the parietal peritoneum.","whyNot":["The muscularis externa lies deep to the serosa. It holds the myenteric plexus between its two layers but is not the outer surface.","Adventitia covers gut that has no peritoneal covering, such as most of the esophagus and the retroperitoneal parts of the colon and duodenum. The ileum is intraperitoneal.","Correct.","The submucosa lies between the mucosa and the muscularis externa. It is deep inside the wall and is not exposed to the peritoneal cavity."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the gut wall","Serosa versus adventitia"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C32","system":"GI","dx":"Strangulated indirect inguinal hernia","codes":["GI-13-F3"],"case":"A 67-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of a painful right groin swelling for 10 hours and three episodes of vomiting. For 2 years, he has had a right groin bulge that appeared with straining and disappeared when he lay down. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 128/80 mmHg. Physical examination shows a tense, tender, erythematous right inguinoscrotal mass that cannot be reduced. The abdomen is distended. Leukocyte count is 15,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000), and serum lactate concentration is 3.6 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2). At emergency surgery, the hernia sac contains a dark, edematous loop of ileum. The diagnosis is a strangulated indirect inguinal hernia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C32-Q1","stem":"Which of the following sequences of events most likely produced the dark, edematous loop of ileum?","options":["Arterial occlusion at the neck of the sac, then venous thrombosis","Mucosal invasion by bacteria, then ischemia of the serosal surface","Venous outflow blocked at the neck, then arterial inflow lost","Embolus to the superior mesenteric artery, then mucosal infarction"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F3","obj":"GI-13-F.3","dok":2,"approach":["An irreducible hernia is incarcerated. Fever, tenderness, and a high lactate mean the trapped bowel has lost its blood supply, so it is strangulated.","The narrow neck of the sac squeezes the mesentery. Thin-walled, low-pressure veins collapse before thick-walled arteries do.","Blood keeps flowing in but cannot leave, so the loop swells until tissue pressure exceeds arterial pressure and the bowel infarcts."],"why":"In an incarcerated hernia, the neck of the sac first compresses the mesenteric veins. Venous outflow stops while arterial inflow continues, so the loop becomes congested and edematous. The swelling tightens the loop in the neck even more until arterial inflow fails. The result is a hemorrhagic infarct, which is why strangulated bowel looks dark red to black. Gangrene then allows bacteria to cross the wall, causing peritonitis and sepsis.","whyNot":["Arteries have thick walls and high pressure, so they are compressed last. The narrow neck squeezes the low-pressure veins first, which is why the loop became congested and edematous before it infarcted.","Bacteria cross the wall after ischemia has damaged the mucosal barrier. They follow ischemia and are a late step in strangulation.","Correct.","An embolus to the superior mesenteric artery infarcts long segments of small bowel and right colon. It does not explain a single loop trapped in a groin sac."],"study":{"topics":["Incarceration versus strangulation","Hemorrhagic infarction of bowel"],"comps":["GI-13-F3","GI-13-J3"]}},{"id":"GI-C32-Q2","stem":"At surgery, the hernia sac lies within the internal spermatic fascia beside the ductus deferens and extends into the scrotum. Which of the following best describes the course of this sac?","options":["Through the linea semilunaris, at the level of the arcuate line","Through the deep ring, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","Through the inguinal triangle, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels","Through the femoral ring, below the inguinal ligament"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.5","dok":2,"approach":["A sac inside the spermatic cord coverings that reaches the scrotum followed the path of the testis through the deep ring.","The deep inguinal ring sits lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, so an indirect hernia begins lateral to them.","A direct hernia pushes through Hesselbach triangle medial to the vessels and lies outside the internal spermatic fascia."],"why":"An indirect inguinal hernia enters the deep inguinal ring, which lies lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, and follows the spermatic cord through the canal. It is covered by the same layers as the cord, including internal spermatic fascia, and can reach the scrotum. It often reflects a patent processus vaginalis. A direct hernia bulges through the weak posterior wall of the canal in Hesselbach triangle, bounded by the inferior epigastric vessels, the lateral border of the rectus abdominis, and the inguinal ligament.","whyNot":["This is a Spigelian hernia through the semilunar line on the anterior abdominal wall. It does not travel with the spermatic cord.","Correct.","This is the path of a direct hernia. It bulges through the posterior wall of the canal and usually stays outside the internal spermatic fascia, so it rarely reaches the scrotum.","This is a femoral hernia. It passes below the inguinal ligament, medial to the femoral vein, and presents in the upper thigh."],"study":{"topics":["Indirect versus direct inguinal hernia","Hesselbach triangle"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-F3"]}},{"id":"GI-C32-Q3","stem":"The loop of ileum could slide into the hernia sac because it is suspended from the posterior abdominal wall by a mesentery. Which of the following segments of the gastrointestinal tract has the same relationship to the peritoneum as the ileum?","options":["Ascending colon","Transverse colon","Second part of the duodenum","Descending colon"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["An organ with a mesentery is intraperitoneal and mobile.","Recall the retroperitoneal gut: duodenum parts 2 to 4, ascending and descending colon, and part of the rectum. These are fixed to the posterior wall.","The transverse colon hangs on the transverse mesocolon, so it is intraperitoneal like the ileum."],"why":"Intraperitoneal organs are wrapped in visceral peritoneum and attached by a mesentery, which lets them move. The jejunum, ileum, transverse colon, and sigmoid colon all have mesenteries, so they can enter hernia sacs or twist. Secondarily retroperitoneal structures, such as the ascending and descending colon and most of the duodenum, lost their mesentery during development and are fixed to the posterior abdominal wall.","whyNot":["The ascending colon is secondarily retroperitoneal and covered by peritoneum only on its front and sides.","Correct.","The second part of the duodenum is retroperitoneal and fixed beside the head of the pancreas. Only the first part of the duodenum is partly intraperitoneal.","The descending colon is secondarily retroperitoneal. Its mesentery fused with the posterior wall during development, so it is fixed in place."],"study":{"topics":["Intraperitoneal versus retroperitoneal organs"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C32-Q4","stem":"Before the loop became strangulated, contractions of the ileum proximal to the hernia were increased. Which of the following sets the maximum frequency of these contractions?","options":["Spike potentials fired by the submucosal (Meissner) plexus","Migrating motor complexes released by motilin during fasting","Vagal efferent bursts from the dorsal motor nucleus","Slow waves generated by the interstitial cells of Cajal"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Gut smooth muscle contracts only when spike potentials ride on top of a slow wave.","Slow waves are rhythmic membrane depolarizations made by the interstitial cells of Cajal, the pacemakers of the gut.","So the slow-wave rate is the ceiling for contraction frequency. Nerves and hormones change how many slow waves trigger a contraction and how strong it is."],"why":"Interstitial cells of Cajal produce slow waves at a set rate in each region, about 3 per minute in the stomach, about 12 in the duodenum, and about 8 to 9 in the terminal ileum. A contraction occurs only when a slow wave reaches threshold and triggers spike potentials, so the slow-wave rate is the maximum contraction rate. The myenteric plexus coordinates peristalsis by contracting the segment above a bolus and relaxing the segment below it. Proximal to an obstruction, this reflex is strongly driven, causing colicky pain.","whyNot":["The submucosal plexus mainly controls secretion and local blood flow. It does not set the rhythm of smooth muscle contraction.","Motilin triggers the migrating motor complex every 90 to 120 minutes during fasting to sweep debris along. It does not set the minute-to-minute contraction rate.","Vagal input changes the strength of contractions through the enteric nervous system, but the gut keeps its slow-wave rhythm even after vagotomy.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Slow waves and interstitial cells of Cajal","Peristaltic reflex"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C33","system":"GI","dx":"Ileocolic intussusception with a Meckel diverticulum lead point","codes":["GI-13-F4","GI-13-H3"],"case":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of 12 hours of episodes of severe abdominal pain during which he cries and draws his knees to his chest. Between episodes he is lethargic. He has vomited four times and has passed one stool of dark red blood mixed with mucus. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 130/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 98/60 mmHg. Physical examination shows a sausage-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant. Abdominal ultrasonography shows a target sign. Air enema reduction fails. At surgery, the terminal ileum has telescoped into the cecum and ascending colon, with an inverted Meckel diverticulum at its tip. The diagnosis is ileocolic intussusception with a Meckel diverticulum lead point.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C33-Q1","stem":"Histologic examination of the resected diverticulum shows only ileal-type mucosa. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the dark red, mucoid stool?","options":["Venous congestion of the intussusceptum from trapped mesentery","Shiga toxin destroying the epithelial cells of the colonic mucosa","Acid from ectopic gastric mucosa ulcerating the adjacent ileum","Rupture of dilated submucosal vessels in the wall of the cecum"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F4","obj":"GI-13-F.4","dok":3,"approach":["The biopsy rules out ectopic gastric mucosa as the bleeding source.","In intussusception, the inner segment (intussusceptum) pulls its mesentery in with it, and the outer segment squeezes that mesentery.","Veins are compressed first, so the inner bowel becomes congested, edematous, and ischemic. Its mucosa sloughs and releases blood and mucus, giving currant jelly stool."],"why":"Intussusception is telescoping of one bowel segment into the next, most often ileocolic in young children. A lead point, such as hypertrophied Peyer patches after a viral illness or a Meckel diverticulum in an older child, is pulled forward by peristalsis. The mesentery of the inner segment is trapped, so venous outflow stops, and the mucosa becomes congested and ischemic. Blood mixed with mucus from the sloughing mucosa produces currant jelly stool. Without reduction, the segment infarcts. Volvulus differs because the bowel twists on its mesentery, as in sigmoid volvulus in older adults or midgut volvulus with malrotation in infants.","whyNot":["Correct.","Shiga toxin from EHEC causes bloody diarrhea and can lead to hemolytic uremic syndrome. It does not cause a palpable mass or a target sign.","Ectopic gastric mucosa in a Meckel diverticulum causes painless bleeding from an ulcer in nearby ileum. This diverticulum had no gastric mucosa, and his bleeding came with colicky pain.","Dilated, fragile submucosal vessels (angiodysplasia) bleed painlessly in older adults. They are not a cause of bleeding in a child with intussusception."],"study":{"topics":["Intussusception and currant jelly stool","Intussusception versus volvulus"],"comps":["GI-13-F4","GI-13-H3"]}},{"id":"GI-C33-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the origin and wall structure of the lesion that served as this patient's lead point?","options":["Patent urachus joining the ileum to the bladder dome","False diverticulum of mucosa herniating where vasa recta enter","Duplication cyst from failed recanalization of the ileum","True diverticulum from a persistent vitelline duct"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H3","obj":"GI-13-H.3","dok":2,"approach":["A Meckel diverticulum is a remnant of the vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct, which joins the midgut to the yolk sac in the embryo.","The duct normally obliterates early in development. If its intestinal end persists, it forms an outpouching on the antimesenteric border of the ileum.","Because it is a remnant of the whole gut tube, it contains all layers of the wall, so it is a true diverticulum."],"why":"Meckel diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the gastrointestinal tract. It is a true diverticulum containing mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis, on the antimesenteric border of the ileum. The rule of twos describes it: about 2% of people, about 2 feet (60 cm) from the ileocecal valve, about 2 inches long, often with 2 types of ectopic tissue (gastric and pancreatic), and usually symptomatic by age 2. It presents as painless rectal bleeding from ectopic gastric mucosa, or as obstruction when it acts as a lead point for intussusception or a band for volvulus.","whyNot":["The urachus is the allantois remnant joining the bladder to the umbilicus. A patent urachus leaks urine at the umbilicus and has no link to the ileum.","False diverticula of mucosa and submucosa form where vasa recta pierce the muscle, as in sigmoid diverticulosis in older adults. They lack a muscle layer.","Duplication cysts are rare tubular or cystic structures attached to the mesenteric side of the bowel. They are not remnants of the vitelline duct.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Meckel diverticulum and the rule of twos","True versus false diverticula"],"comps":["GI-13-H3","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C33-Q3","stem":"At the onset of his illness, the pain was dull and poorly localized around the umbilicus. Which of the following best explains this location?","options":["Foregut structure supplied by the celiac trunk, with afferents to about T5 to T9","Hindgut structure supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery, with afferents to about L1 to L2","Midgut structure supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, with afferents to about T10","Parietal peritoneum supplied by somatic intercostal nerves, with afferents to about T12"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the involved bowel: terminal ileum, cecum, and ascending colon.","These are midgut derivatives, supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.","Visceral afferents from the midgut enter the cord at about T10, so the pain is felt in the T10 dermatome around the umbilicus."],"why":"Visceral pain fibers travel back with sympathetic nerves and enter the spinal cord at the levels that supplied the embryonic gut segment. Pain is then referred to the dermatomes of those levels. Foregut pain (to the second part of the duodenum, celiac trunk) is felt in the epigastrium. Midgut pain (to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon, superior mesenteric artery) is felt around the umbilicus. Hindgut pain (inferior mesenteric artery) is felt in the suprapubic region. Visceral pain is dull and poorly localized because the gut is innervated on both sides of the midline by few afferent fibers.","whyNot":["The foregut ends at the second part of the duodenum. Its pain is felt in the epigastrium, and none of this boy's involved bowel is foregut.","The hindgut begins at the distal third of the transverse colon. Its pain is felt in the suprapubic region.","Correct.","Parietal peritoneum gives sharp, well-localized somatic pain over the inflamed area. It explains sharp, localized pain later in an illness. His early pain was dull and central."],"study":{"topics":["Foregut, midgut, and hindgut derivatives","Referred visceral pain"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C33-Q4","stem":"During the same week, his 10-month-old sister develops vomiting and profuse watery diarrhea during a winter outbreak at her day care center. Stool testing identifies a nonenveloped virus with a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome. Which of the following mechanisms best explains her diarrhea?","options":["Loss of villus-tip enterocytes, with villus blunting and malabsorption","ADP-ribosylation of Gs protein, raising enterocyte cAMP and chloride loss","Invasion of colonic mucosa through M cells, with ulceration and bleeding","Inactivation of the 60S ribosomal subunit in colonic epithelial cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the virus: nonenveloped, segmented double-stranded RNA, infant, winter, day care. This is rotavirus.","Rotavirus infects the mature absorptive enterocytes at the tips of small intestinal villi.","Loss of these cells blunts villi and reduces absorption and disaccharidase activity, so fluid and unabsorbed sugar stay in the lumen. The viral protein NSP4 also increases secretion."],"why":"Rotavirus, a reovirus with a segmented double-stranded RNA genome, is the classic cause of severe watery diarrhea in infants and young children, especially in winter. It kills mature enterocytes at the villus tips, which blunts the villi and leaves immature crypt cells that absorb poorly. Its NSP4 protein acts as an enterotoxin that raises intracellular calcium and chloride secretion. Norovirus, a single-stranded RNA calicivirus, causes outbreaks in all ages, and the enteroviruses (coxsackievirus, echovirus) are single-stranded RNA picornaviruses spread by the fecal-oral route.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is the mechanism of cholera toxin and the heat-labile toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli, which are bacteria. It does not describe a double-stranded RNA virus.","M cell invasion with mucosal ulceration describes Shigella and invasive bacteria, which cause bloody diarrhea with fever.","Ribosome inactivation is the action of Shiga toxin and Shiga-like toxin. It causes bloody diarrhea. Her illness is watery and viral."],"study":{"topics":["Rotavirus diarrhea","Comparison of viral causes of enteritis"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C34","system":"GI","dx":"Chagas disease megacolon","codes":["GI-13-F5"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of worsening constipation and abdominal distension. She had normal bowel habits until 8 years ago and now has a bowel movement once every 2 to 3 weeks. She grew up in a rural area of Bolivia in a house with an adobe and thatch roof and moved to the United States 10 years ago. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 62/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mmHg. Physical examination shows a distended, tympanitic abdomen. An electrocardiogram shows right bundle branch block. Abdominal radiograph shows a massively dilated sigmoid colon and rectum. Serologic testing is positive for IgG antibodies against Trypanosoma cruzi. The diagnosis is megacolon caused by chronic Chagas disease.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C34-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms most likely underlies this patient's megacolon?","options":["Mechanical blockage by a stenosing rectal adenocarcinoma","Transmural colitis with cytokine-mediated loss of muscle tone","Congenital absence of ganglion cells from a RET mutation","Acquired inflammatory destruction of enteric ganglion cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F5","obj":"GI-13-F.5","dok":2,"approach":["Normal bowel function for decades, followed by slowly progressive constipation, means the problem was acquired.","Childhood in an area with triatomine bugs, positive T. cruzi serology, and a conduction block point to chronic Chagas disease.","In chronic Chagas disease, parasite-driven inflammation destroys enteric neurons, especially in the myenteric plexus, so the colon cannot propel stool and dilates."],"why":"Trypanosoma cruzi is spread by triatomine (reduviid) bugs in rural Latin America. Years after infection, parasite persistence and immune-mediated inflammation destroy neurons of the enteric plexuses and the cardiac conduction system. Loss of enteric neurons causes megaesophagus and megacolon, and cardiac involvement causes dilated cardiomyopathy and conduction blocks such as right bundle branch block. Hirschsprung disease differs because ganglion cells are absent from birth, so symptoms start in the newborn. Toxic megacolon differs because it is an acute, life-threatening dilation during severe colitis.","whyNot":["A rectal cancer can cause obstruction and proximal dilation, but the dilated segment here includes the rectum itself, and serology explains the cause.","This describes toxic megacolon, an acute complication of ulcerative colitis or C. difficile colitis with fever and systemic toxicity. Her course is slow and painless.","This is Hirschsprung disease. It presents in the newborn with delayed passage of meconium, which does not fit normal bowel habits until middle age.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of megacolon","Chronic Chagas disease"],"comps":["GI-13-F5","IN-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C34-Q2","stem":"Her physician contrasts her disease with Hirschsprung disease, in which the aganglionic segment always begins at the anus and extends a variable distance proximally. Which of the following best explains this distribution in Hirschsprung disease?","options":["Rectal neurons arise from sacral somites, which fail to form in the embryo","Rectal ganglion cells degenerate after birth from ischemia","Neural crest cells migrate craniocaudally and reach the rectum last","Endoderm of the cloaca fails to form the intrinsic enteric plexuses"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.5","dok":3,"approach":["Enteric neurons and glia come from neural crest cells, mainly vagal neural crest.","These cells enter the foregut and migrate down the gut in a craniocaudal direction, so the rectum is colonized last.","If migration stops early (most often from a loss-of-function RET mutation), the most distal bowel is left without ganglion cells, in one continuous segment from the anus upward."],"why":"Vagal neural crest cells enter the foregut and migrate toward the hindgut during the embryonic period, forming the myenteric and submucosal plexuses. RET, a receptor tyrosine kinase activated by GDNF, drives their survival and migration. When migration stops short, the distal colon and rectum lack ganglion cells. The aganglionic segment stays contracted and acts as a functional obstruction, so the normal bowel above it dilates. This is why the rectum is always involved and why the defect extends proximally in one continuous segment.","whyNot":["Somites form muscle, bone, and dermis of the body wall. Enteric neurons do not come from somites.","Ganglion cells in Hirschsprung disease never arrived, so they are absent at birth. There is no postnatal degeneration.","Correct.","Endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the gut. Enteric neurons come from neural crest, which is ectoderm."],"study":{"topics":["Neural crest origin of enteric neurons","Hirschsprung disease and RET"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"GI-C34-Q3","stem":"Anorectal manometry shows that balloon distension of her rectum fails to cause relaxation of the internal anal sphincter. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Injury to the pudendal nerve that supplies the external sphincter","Fibrosis of the levator ani muscle that limits pelvic floor descent","Loss of intramural neurons mediating internal sphincter relaxation","Increased sympathetic tone carried by the lumbar splanchnic nerves"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["Rectal distension normally triggers reflex relaxation of the internal anal sphincter (the rectoanal inhibitory reflex).","This reflex runs through intrinsic enteric neurons in the wall of the rectum and sphincter, which release nitric oxide and VIP.","Chagas disease destroys those neurons, so the reflex is lost, just as it is in Hirschsprung disease."],"why":"Defecation begins when stool stretches the rectal wall. Enteric neurons then relax the internal anal sphincter, which is smooth muscle, and the urge to defecate is felt. The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle under voluntary control through the pudendal nerve (S2 to S4), so a person can delay defecation. The gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility after a meal. Loss of the intrinsic neurons removes internal sphincter relaxation, so stool cannot pass easily and the colon above dilates.","whyNot":["Pudendal nerve injury weakens the external sphincter and causes incontinence. It does not affect reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter.","Levator ani dysfunction can cause dyssynergic defecation, but this muscle is skeletal and does not control the internal sphincter's reflex relaxation.","Correct.","Sympathetic input raises internal sphincter tone, but it does not abolish the intrinsic relaxation reflex. Chagas disease destroys the enteric neurons that run that reflex."],"study":{"topics":["Defecation reflex and rectoanal inhibitory reflex","Internal versus external anal sphincter"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"GI-C34-Q4","stem":"A biopsy is planned to examine the neural plexus that coordinates contraction of the muscular coat of her colon. Which of the following locations must the tissue sample include?","options":["Between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers","Lamina propria directly beneath the surface epithelium","Between the muscularis mucosae and the inner circular muscle","Between the outer longitudinal muscle and the serosa"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["The plexus that controls motility of the muscular wall is the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus.","It sits in the muscularis externa, between its inner circular and outer longitudinal layers.","A superficial suction biopsy reaches only mucosa and submucosa, so a full-thickness sample is needed to see this plexus."],"why":"The gut wall has mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia. The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa and mainly regulates secretion and blood flow. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies between the circular and longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa and controls motility. In Chagas disease the myenteric plexus is the main target, which is why motility fails.","whyNot":["Correct.","The lamina propria contains capillaries, lymphocytes, and lymphatics. It does not contain either enteric plexus.","This is the submucosa, which holds the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. That plexus mainly controls secretion and local blood flow.","The tissue between the longitudinal muscle and serosa is subserosal connective tissue. No major enteric plexus lies there."],"study":{"topics":["Location of the myenteric and submucosal plexuses","Layers of the gut wall"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-6"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C35","system":"GI","dx":"Zenker diverticulum","codes":["GI-13-F"],"case":"A 74-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of progressive difficulty swallowing. He often regurgitates undigested food that he ate several hours earlier, and his wife reports bad breath. He notices a gurgling sound in his neck when he drinks and has had nighttime coughing spells. He has lost 5 kg (11 lb) over this period. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 78/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 136/82 mmHg. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. A barium swallow shows a contrast-filled pouch extending posteriorly from the pharynx just above the cricopharyngeus muscle. The diagnosis is Zenker diverticulum.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C35-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains the formation of this patient's pouch?","options":["Persistent embryonic connection between the foregut and the trachea","Pulsion of mucosa through a weak area above a poorly relaxing sphincter","Failure of lower esophageal sphincter relaxation from loss of inhibitory neurons","Traction on the full esophageal wall by scarred mediastinal lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-F","obj":"GI-13-F.6","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the pouch: posterior hypopharynx, just above the cricopharyngeus, in Killian triangle.","The cricopharyngeus (upper esophageal sphincter) does not open fully during swallowing, so pressure in the hypopharynx rises.","Over years, that pressure pushes mucosa and submucosa out through the weak area. This is a false (pulsion) diverticulum."],"why":"Zenker diverticulum forms in Killian triangle, a weak area of the posterior pharyngeal wall between the thyropharyngeal part of the inferior constrictor and the cricopharyngeus. Poor relaxation of the cricopharyngeus raises pressure during each swallow, and mucosa and submucosa bulge out without a muscle layer. Food collects in the pouch, causing dysphagia, regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis, gurgling, and aspiration. It occurs mostly in older adults.","whyNot":["A persistent foregut to trachea connection is a tracheoesophageal fistula, a congenital defect that presents in newborns with choking and feeding problems.","Correct.","This is achalasia. It affects the lower esophageal sphincter and causes a dilated esophagus above a narrow distal segment, far below this pouch.","Traction diverticula form in the midesophagus when scarred lymph nodes (for example after tuberculosis or histoplasmosis) pull out all layers of the wall. They are true diverticula and usually cause no symptoms."],"study":{"topics":["Zenker diverticulum and Killian triangle","Pulsion versus traction diverticula"],"comps":["GI-13-F","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C35-Q2","stem":"In a normal swallow, the muscle at the lower edge of this patient's pouch relaxes to let the bolus pass. Which of the following best describes the phase of swallowing in which this occurs and how it is controlled?","options":["Reflex pharyngeal phase coordinated by the medulla","Esophageal phase controlled by vagal nitric oxide release","Esophageal phase controlled by the myenteric plexus alone","Voluntary oral phase controlled by the motor cortex"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["The muscle below the pouch is the cricopharyngeus, which forms the upper esophageal sphincter.","It relaxes when the bolus reaches the pharynx, during the involuntary pharyngeal phase.","That phase is a reflex triggered by touch receptors in the pharynx and run by the swallowing center in the medulla through cranial nerves IX and X."],"why":"Chewing uses the muscles of mastication, supplied by CN V3. Swallowing then begins with a voluntary oral phase in which the tongue pushes the bolus back. When the bolus touches the pharynx, the pharyngeal phase starts as a reflex coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. The soft palate closes the nasopharynx, the larynx rises and the epiglottis covers it, breathing stops, and the upper esophageal sphincter relaxes. In the esophageal phase, peristalsis carries the bolus down, and vagal fibers that release nitric oxide and VIP relax the lower esophageal sphincter and proximal stomach.","whyNot":["Correct.","Vagal nitric oxide relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter at the end of the esophagus. The pouch sits at the upper sphincter.","The myenteric plexus helps run peristalsis in the smooth muscle of the lower esophagus. The cricopharyngeus is skeletal muscle controlled from the brainstem.","The oral phase moves the bolus to the back of the mouth and is voluntary. Opening of the upper esophageal sphincter happens after it, as a reflex."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of swallowing","Upper versus lower esophageal sphincter"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C35-Q3","stem":"During surgery, a biopsy specimen is taken from the lining of the pouch. Which of the following types of epithelium is most likely to be seen?","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium with many goblet cells","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["The pouch is a false diverticulum, so its lining is the mucosa of the area it came from.","It arises from the hypopharynx just above the esophagus.","The hypopharynx and esophagus are lined by moist, nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium that resists abrasion from food."],"why":"The oropharynx, hypopharynx, and esophagus carry rough food boluses, so they are lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium kept moist by mucous glands. The stomach and intestines are lined by simple columnar epithelium for secretion and absorption. The anal canal is columnar above the pectinate line and stratified squamous below it. Because the pouch is mucosa pushed out of the hypopharynx, it keeps the same squamous lining.","whyNot":["Keratinized epithelium covers dry surfaces such as skin and the hard palate. The pharynx and esophagus are normally nonkeratinized.","Correct.","Columnar epithelium with goblet cells lines the intestine. In the esophagus it appears only as Barrett metaplasia near the gastroesophageal junction.","Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the nasopharynx and airways. The hypopharynx, where this pouch arises, is a food passage with squamous lining."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelium of the gastrointestinal tract"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-F"]}},{"id":"GI-C35-Q4","stem":"An endoscope passed into this patient's esophagus would meet a natural narrowing just below the opening of the pouch. Which of the following best describes this narrowing and its approximate vertebral level?","options":["Aortic arch and left main bronchus at about T4 to T5","Esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm at about T10","Thoracic inlet behind the manubrium at about T1","Cricopharyngeus muscle at about C6"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["The pouch arises just above the cricopharyngeus, so the narrowing below its opening is the start of the esophagus.","The esophagus begins at the cricopharyngeus at about the level of C6 and the cricoid cartilage.","The other two narrowings are where the aortic arch and left main bronchus cross it and where it passes through the diaphragm at T10."],"why":"The esophagus begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage, about C6, where the cricopharyngeus forms the upper esophageal sphincter. It descends through the superior and posterior mediastinum and has three natural narrowings: at the cricopharyngeus, where the aortic arch and then the left main bronchus cross it, and at the esophageal hiatus at T10. These are the sites where swallowed objects tend to lodge and where an endoscope meets resistance. The cricopharyngeal narrowing is the tightest, and a Zenker pouch sits just above it.","whyNot":["This is the middle (thoracic) narrowing, well below the neck. The pouch arises in the neck.","This is the lowest narrowing where the esophagus enters the abdomen. It is far below the pouch.","The thoracic inlet is the opening at the top of the thorax. It is not one of the three classic esophageal narrowings.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Course and narrowings of the esophagus"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-F"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C36","system":"GI","dx":"Collagenous colitis","codes":["GI-13-G4"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of 6 to 8 watery, nonbloody stools daily, including at night, with mild crampy abdominal pain and a 3-kg (6.6-lb) weight loss. She takes naproxen daily for osteoarthritis of the knees. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Hemoglobin concentration is 13.1 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), and C-reactive protein is 3 mg/L (below 10). Stool testing for Clostridioides difficile toxin is negative. Serum IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies are negative with a normal total IgA. Colonoscopy shows normal-appearing mucosa throughout. Biopsy specimens confirm collagenous colitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C36-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings is most likely to be seen on this patient's colonic biopsy specimens?","options":["Crypt abscesses with crypt distortion and basal plasmacytosis","Noncaseating granulomas with transmural lymphoid aggregates","Thickened collagen band beneath the surface epithelium","Hyalinized small vessels with atypical stromal fibroblasts"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G4","obj":"GI-13-G.4","dok":2,"approach":["Chronic watery diarrhea with a normal-looking colon at colonoscopy points to microscopic colitis.","Microscopic colitis has two forms. The collagenous form has a thick subepithelial collagen band (over about 10 µm), and the lymphocytic form has excess intraepithelial lymphocytes without the band.","Collagenous colitis also has more intraepithelial lymphocytes and lamina propria inflammation, but the collagen band defines it."],"why":"Microscopic colitis causes chronic, nonbloody watery diarrhea, often nocturnal, mostly in middle-aged and older women. The colon looks normal, so the diagnosis needs biopsies. Collagenous colitis shows a collagen band under the surface epithelium thicker than about 10 µm, with surface damage and inflammation in the lamina propria. It is linked to autoimmune disease, celiac disease, and drugs such as NSAIDs and proton pump inhibitors. Other noninfectious causes of colitis include radiation and NSAID injury.","whyNot":["Crypt abscesses with crypt architectural distortion and basal plasmacytosis are features of ulcerative colitis. That disease causes bloody diarrhea and visible mucosal inflammation.","Noncaseating granulomas and transmural inflammation define Crohn disease, which usually shows ulcers or strictures on colonoscopy.","Correct.","Hyalinized vessels and atypical fibroblasts are features of chronic radiation injury. Nothing in her history suggests radiation therapy."],"study":{"topics":["Microscopic colitis: collagenous and lymphocytic","Noninfectious colitis"],"comps":["GI-13-G4","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"GI-C36-Q2","stem":"Before colonoscopy, irritable bowel syndrome was included in the differential diagnosis. Which of the following features of this patient's presentation most strongly argued against that diagnosis?","options":["Nocturnal bowel movements and weight loss","Cramping abdominal pain that eases after defecation","Loose stools that have persisted for several months","Normal hemoglobin and C-reactive protein levels"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-F","obj":"GI-13-F.7","dok":3,"approach":["Irritable bowel syndrome is recurrent abdominal pain linked to defecation, with a change in stool frequency or form, and no structural disease.","Its symptoms come from altered motility and visceral hypersensitivity during waking hours, so they do not wake the patient from sleep and do not cause weight loss.","Nocturnal diarrhea and weight loss are alarm features that point to organic disease and call for further testing."],"why":"Irritable bowel syndrome is a disorder of gut and brain interaction. Altered motility, visceral hypersensitivity, and changes in central pain processing cause recurrent pain that is related to defecation, with altered stool form. There is no inflammation or structural lesion. Diarrhea that wakes a patient at night, weight loss, bleeding, anemia, and onset after age 50 are alarm features that suggest an organic cause such as microscopic colitis, inflammatory bowel disease, or cancer. Gastroparesis, a motility disorder with measurably delayed gastric emptying, causes early satiety and vomiting from delayed gastric emptying, usually from diabetic autonomic neuropathy.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pain that improves after defecation is one of the core features of irritable bowel syndrome, so it supports that diagnosis.","Irritable bowel syndrome is defined by symptoms lasting months, so chronic loose stools fit it.","Normal blood counts and inflammatory markers are expected in irritable bowel syndrome, so they support that diagnosis."],"study":{"topics":["Irritable bowel syndrome and alarm features","Irritable bowel syndrome versus gastroparesis"],"comps":["GI-13-F","GI-13-G4"]}},{"id":"GI-C36-Q3","stem":"Celiac disease was considered because it is associated with microscopic colitis. In a patient with celiac disease, which of the following steps allows dietary gliadin peptides to activate the T-cell response in the small intestine?","options":["Presentation of native gliadin on HLA-B27 to CD8 T cells","Tissue transglutaminase deamidation for HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 binding","Activation of the classical complement pathway by bound gliadin","Cross-linking of IgE on mucosal mast cells by gliadin peptides"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.2","dok":2,"approach":["Gliadin, from wheat gluten, is rich in proline and glutamine, so it resists digestion and crosses the epithelium as long peptides.","Tissue transglutaminase in the lamina propria converts some glutamines to glutamate (deamidation), adding negative charges.","These charged peptides bind tightly to HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 on antigen-presenting cells, which activate gluten-specific CD4 T cells."],"why":"Celiac disease needs gluten, the right HLA class II genes, and tissue transglutaminase. Deamidated gliadin binds HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 strongly and is presented to CD4 T cells, which release interferon-gamma and drive villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis. Gliadin bound to tissue transglutaminase also helps B cells make IgA antibodies against tissue transglutaminase and endomysium, which are the screening tests. Because selective IgA deficiency is more common in celiac disease and can cause a false negative IgA test, total IgA is checked at the same time.","whyNot":["HLA-B27 is a class I allele linked to the seronegative spondyloarthropathies. Celiac disease is linked to the class II alleles HLA-DQ2 and DQ8.","Correct.","Complement activation is not the driver of celiac disease. The injury comes from gluten-specific CD4 T cells and cytotoxic intraepithelial lymphocytes.","IgE cross-linking on mast cells causes wheat allergy, an immediate hypersensitivity with hives or anaphylaxis. Celiac disease is a T cell-mediated disorder."],"study":{"topics":["Celiac disease immunopathogenesis","Celiac serology and IgA deficiency"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-E2"]}},{"id":"GI-C36-Q4","stem":"Stool was tested for C. difficile toxin because she had received clindamycin for a dental infection 6 weeks before her symptoms began. If C. difficile had been the cause, which of the following mechanisms would have produced her diarrhea?","options":["Heat-stable toxin activating guanylate cyclase in enterocytes","Preformed superantigen toxin acting on the vagal afferents","Toxins A and B inactivating Rho GTPases of colonocytes","Spread between enterocytes by polymerizing host actin"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L1","obj":"GI-13-L.1","dok":2,"approach":["Clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, and cephalosporins suppress normal colonic flora, which lets C. difficile spores germinate and grow.","C. difficile makes toxin A (enterotoxin) and toxin B (cytotoxin).","Both toxins glucosylate Rho family GTPases, which breaks down the actin cytoskeleton, opens tight junctions, kills colonocytes, and triggers inflammation."],"why":"C. difficile is a spore-forming, anaerobic gram-positive rod. After antibiotics disrupt the normal flora, it grows in the colon and releases toxins A and B. These toxins inactivate Rho GTPases, so colonocytes lose their cytoskeleton and tight junctions and die. Neutrophils, fibrin, and mucus erupt from damaged crypts and form yellow-white pseudomembranes. Patients have watery diarrhea, cramps, and fever, and severe cases can progress to toxic megacolon. Diagnosis is by stool toxin testing or by PCR for the toxin genes.","whyNot":["Heat-stable toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli raises cGMP and causes traveler's diarrhea. It does not cause antibiotic-associated colitis.","Preformed Staphylococcus aureus enterotoxins cause vomiting within hours of eating contaminated food. They do not cause weeks of diarrhea after antibiotics.","Correct.","Actin-based spread between cells is used by Listeria and Shigella. C. difficile does not invade cells; it damages them with toxins from the lumen."],"study":{"topics":["Clostridioides difficile toxins and pseudomembranous colitis"],"comps":["GI-13-L1","GI-12"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C37","system":"GI","dx":"Acute appendicitis with pelvic abscess","codes":["GI-13-G1"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 3 days of abdominal pain. The pain began around the umbilicus and moved to the right lower quadrant within a day. She did not seek care until fever and nausea developed. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mmHg. Physical examination shows right lower quadrant tenderness with guarding. Rectal examination shows tenderness anteriorly. Leukocyte count is 18,200/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000) with 88% neutrophils (40 to 70). A urine pregnancy test is negative. CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis shows a dilated appendix with an appendicolith, periappendiceal fat stranding, and a 5-cm rim-enhancing fluid collection in the pelvis. The diagnosis is perforated acute appendicitis with a pelvic abscess.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C37-Q1","stem":"Which of the following sequences of events best explains how the appendicolith led to perforation in this patient?","options":["Arterial thrombosis, then mucosal necrosis and stone formation","Luminal obstruction, rising pressure, ischemia, then invasion","Viral ulceration of the mucosa, then fibrosis with a stricture","Bacterial invasion of the wall, then lymphoid hyperplasia"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G1","obj":"GI-13-G.1","dok":2,"approach":["Start with the appendicolith. It blocks the lumen of a narrow, blind-ended tube.","Mucus keeps being secreted, so pressure rises. Veins collapse first, then arterial flow falls, and the wall becomes ischemic.","Ischemic mucosa lets luminal bacteria invade the wall, causing suppurative inflammation, gangrene, and perforation."],"why":"Appendicitis usually starts with obstruction of the lumen by a fecalith or by lymphoid hyperplasia after an infection. Continued secretion raises pressure, which blocks venous outflow and then arterial inflow. Ischemia lets bacteria invade the wall, producing neutrophilic inflammation through the muscularis. Early pain is visceral and felt around the umbilicus. When inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum, pain localizes to McBurney point. Untreated, the gangrenous wall can perforate, causing an abscess or peritonitis.","whyNot":["Fecaliths form from hardened stool before the illness. Arterial thrombosis is not the first event in appendicitis.","Correct.","Viral illness can cause lymphoid hyperplasia that blocks the lumen, but mucosal ulceration followed by fibrosis does not describe acute appendicitis.","This reverses the order. Lymphoid hyperplasia can be a first step that blocks the lumen, and bacterial invasion comes after ischemia."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of acute appendicitis","Visceral to parietal pain shift"],"comps":["GI-13-G1","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C37-Q2","stem":"At laparoscopy, the inflamed appendix is hidden by adhesions behind the cecum. Which of the following landmarks most reliably leads the surgeon to the base of the appendix?","options":["Deepest haustral fold of the cecal wall","Line of omental appendices along the ascending colon","Last Peyer patch on the antimesenteric ileum","Convergence point of the three taeniae coli"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["The tip of the appendix can lie in many places (retrocecal, pelvic, subcecal), but its base is fixed on the cecum.","The three taeniae coli are bands of longitudinal muscle on the colon.","Followed down the cecum, they converge at the base of the appendix, where they become its continuous longitudinal muscle layer."],"why":"The colon differs from the small intestine by its taeniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices (fat tags). The taeniae are three bands of the outer longitudinal muscle. They meet at the base of the appendix, which on the skin surface lies near McBurney point, one third of the way from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus. Because the tip of the appendix varies in position, following the taeniae is the reliable way to find the base.","whyNot":["Haustra are sacculations of the colon wall between taeniae. They change with contraction and do not mark the appendix base.","Omental appendices are fat tags along the colon. They help identify colon but do not point to the appendix, and the appendix itself lacks them.","Peyer patches are lymphoid follicles in the ileal mucosa on the antimesenteric side. They are not visible from the outside and do not lead to the appendix.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Features of the colon versus small intestine","Appendix location and McBurney point"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-G1"]}},{"id":"GI-C37-Q3","stem":"The same month, her 15-year-old brother undergoes surgery for suspected appendicitis. At surgery, the appendix is normal, but the terminal ileum is thickened and the mesenteric lymph nodes are enlarged. Stool culture grows a gram-negative coccobacillus that grows well after cold enrichment at 4 °C (39.2 °F). Which of the following exposures most likely caused his infection?","options":["Eating fried rice left at room temperature","Eating raw oysters harvested in summer","Eating undercooked ground beef at a picnic","Preparing raw pork chitterlings at home"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.3","dok":3,"approach":["A gram-negative organism that grows in the cold and causes terminal ileitis with mesenteric adenitis is Yersinia enterocolitica.","It invades through M cells over Peyer patches and spreads to mesenteric lymph nodes, causing right lower quadrant pain that mimics appendicitis.","Its main reservoir is pigs, so pork products, especially chitterlings, and unpasteurized milk are typical sources."],"why":"Yersinia enterocolitica is a gram-negative coccobacillus that can grow at refrigerator temperatures. It crosses the ileal mucosa through M cells, multiplies in Peyer patches, and spreads to mesenteric lymph nodes. The result is fever, diarrhea, and right lower quadrant pain from mesenteric adenitis, a pseudoappendicitis that can lead to surgery. Pigs are the main reservoir, and handling raw chitterlings is a classic source, along with contaminated milk.","whyNot":["Fried rice left at room temperature is the classic source of Bacillus cereus, a gram-positive rod whose preformed toxin causes vomiting within hours. It does not cause terminal ileitis or mesenteric adenitis.","Raw oysters carry Vibrio species, which are curved gram-negative rods linked to warm seawater. They do not cause mesenteric adenitis.","Undercooked ground beef is the classic source of EHEC O157:H7, which makes Shiga toxin and causes bloody diarrhea that can progress to hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Yersinia enterocolitica and pseudoappendicitis","Foodborne bacterial sources"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C37-Q4","stem":"The abscess formed while she was mostly sitting and walking during the days before admission. In which of the following peritoneal spaces is this collection most likely located?","options":["Rectouterine pouch","Vesicouterine pouch","Hepatorenal recess","Right subphrenic space"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.6","dok":2,"approach":["Fluid and pus in the peritoneal cavity flow to the lowest space for the patient's position.","In an upright woman, the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity is the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) between the uterus and rectum.","This fits the CT finding of a pelvic collection and the anterior tenderness on rectal examination."],"why":"Gravity decides where infected fluid collects. When upright, the lowest peritoneal space is the rectouterine pouch in women and the rectovesical pouch in men. When supine, the lowest space in the upper abdomen is the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch). Fluid can also track up the right paracolic gutter to the subphrenic spaces. A collection in the rectouterine pouch lies just in front of the rectum, so it causes anterior rectal tenderness and can be drained through the rectum or vagina.","whyNot":["Correct.","The vesicouterine pouch between the bladder and uterus is shallow and higher than the rectouterine pouch, so fluid passes it by.","The hepatorenal recess is the most dependent upper abdominal space when a person lies supine. It lies in the upper abdomen, far above the pelvis.","The subphrenic spaces lie under the diaphragm. Pus reaches them by tracking up the paracolic gutters, and they are not pelvic."],"study":{"topics":["Dependent peritoneal spaces","Pelvic peritoneal pouches"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-G1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C38","system":"GI","dx":"Barrett esophagus","codes":["GI-13-G2"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of 15 years of heartburn and regurgitation that wake him at night. BMI is 33 kg/m2. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 74/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 134/84 mmHg. Physical examination shows central obesity and no other abnormalities. Upper endoscopy shows a small sliding hiatal hernia and salmon-colored mucosa extending 4 cm above the gastroesophageal junction into the distal esophagus. Multiple biopsy specimens are taken. Histologic examination confirms Barrett esophagus without dysplasia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C38-Q1","stem":"Surveillance endoscopy with biopsy is planned for this patient. Which of the following findings on a future biopsy would indicate the next step in the progression toward cancer?","options":["Low-grade dysplasia in the columnar epithelium","Gastric cardiac-type mucosa without goblet cells","At least 15 eosinophils per high-power field in the squamous mucosa","Basal cell hyperplasia of the remaining squamous mucosa"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G2","obj":"GI-13-G.2","dok":2,"approach":["Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia, which is the first step in the sequence toward esophageal adenocarcinoma.","The sequence runs from metaplasia to low-grade dysplasia, then high-grade dysplasia, then invasive adenocarcinoma.","Surveillance biopsies look for dysplasia, which is the next step and the main predictor of cancer risk."],"why":"Chronic acid and bile reflux injures the squamous epithelium of the distal esophagus. It heals with columnar epithelium containing goblet cells (intestinal metaplasia), which resists acid better. Over years, the metaplastic cells can gain genetic changes, such as TP53 mutation, that lead to low-grade and then high-grade dysplasia and finally adenocarcinoma. The risk of cancer rises with the grade of dysplasia, which is why patients with Barrett esophagus have endoscopic surveillance with biopsies.","whyNot":["Correct.","Columnar mucosa without goblet cells is cardiac-type mucosa. It is a less specific finding and is not a step beyond intestinal metaplasia.","Many eosinophils in squamous mucosa define eosinophilic esophagitis, an allergic disorder. It is not part of the Barrett sequence.","Basal cell hyperplasia and elongated papillae are signs of reflux injury to squamous mucosa. They precede metaplasia and do not mark progression toward cancer."],"study":{"topics":["Barrett metaplasia to adenocarcinoma sequence"],"comps":["GI-13-G2","GI-13-K1"]}},{"id":"GI-C38-Q2","stem":"Which of the following histologic findings in the salmon-colored mucosa established this patient's diagnosis?","options":["Keratinized squamous cells in place of nonkeratinized ones","Columnar epithelium with goblet cells in place of squamous","Parietal and chief cells forming gastric glands in the submucosa","Atypical squamous cells extending into the lamina propria"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Know the normal lining: the esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, and the stomach starts with simple columnar epithelium at the gastroesophageal junction.","Salmon-colored mucosa above the junction means columnar epithelium has replaced the squamous lining.","Goblet cells in that columnar lining show intestinal-type metaplasia, which defines Barrett esophagus."],"why":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which looks pale pink at endoscopy. The stomach and intestine are lined by simple columnar epithelium, which looks red and velvety. Normally the two meet at the gastroesophageal junction (the Z-line). In Barrett esophagus, chronic reflux causes the distal squamous lining to be replaced by columnar epithelium with goblet cells, an intestinal pattern that is not normally found in the esophagus or stomach.","whyNot":["Reflux does not make the esophageal lining keratinize. In Barrett esophagus the squamous lining is replaced by columnar epithelium.","Correct.","Gastric glands deep in the submucosa would be a heterotopia. Barrett esophagus is a change in the surface epithelium.","Atypical squamous cells invading the lamina propria describe squamous cell carcinoma, which is not the result of Barrett metaplasia."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelium of the esophagus and gastroesophageal junction","Intestinal metaplasia"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G2"]}},{"id":"GI-C38-Q3","stem":"This patient's reflux injury depends on acid secreted by gastric parietal cells. Blocking which of the following would most completely reduce acid output regardless of the stimulus?","options":["Histamine H2 receptors on parietal cells","Muscarinic M3 receptors on parietal cells","H+/K+ ATPase in the parietal cell apical membrane","Gastrin (CCK-B) receptors on enterochromaffin-like cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Parietal cells respond to three stimuli: acetylcholine (M3), gastrin (CCK-B), and histamine (H2).","Each receptor acts through its own second messenger (calcium for M3 and CCK-B, cAMP for H2), so blocking one leaves the others working.","All three pathways converge on the H+/K+ ATPase, the final step that pumps H+ into the lumen in exchange for K+."],"why":"Acid secretion has a cephalic phase (sight, smell, and taste of food acting through the vagus), a gastric phase (stretch and peptides in the stomach, which release gastrin), and an intestinal phase. Acetylcholine and gastrin raise intracellular calcium, and histamine from enterochromaffin-like cells raises cAMP. These signals move H+/K+ ATPase pumps to the apical membrane and activate them. Because every stimulus works through this pump, blocking it reduces acid output the most. Somatostatin from D cells inhibits acid secretion.","whyNot":["Blocking H2 receptors removes the histamine signal and also blunts gastrin's effect, which works largely through histamine. Acetylcholine can still stimulate parietal cells directly.","Blocking M3 receptors removes vagal stimulation, mainly during the cephalic phase. Gastrin and histamine can still drive secretion.","Correct.","Blocking gastrin receptors removes one stimulus, but acetylcholine and histamine released by other signals can still drive the parietal cell."],"study":{"topics":["Parietal cell stimulation and the proton pump","Phases of gastric acid secretion"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-G8"]}},{"id":"GI-C38-Q4","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely type and location of the cancer this patient is at increased risk of developing?","options":["Squamous cell carcinoma of the middle third","Adenocarcinoma of the distal third","Adenocarcinoma of the upper third","Squamous cell carcinoma of the upper third"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K1","obj":"GI-13-K.1","dok":2,"approach":["Barrett esophagus is glandular (columnar) metaplasia, so the cancer that follows is a glandular cancer, adenocarcinoma.","Barrett esophagus forms in the distal esophagus near the gastroesophageal junction, so the tumor arises there.","Obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure and reflux, which adds to the same risk."],"why":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett esophagus in the distal third. Its risk factors are chronic reflux, Barrett esophagus, obesity, and smoking. Squamous cell carcinoma arises in the upper and middle thirds and is linked to alcohol, tobacco, achalasia, caustic injury, and Plummer-Vinson syndrome. Both types present with progressive dysphagia, first to solids and then to liquids, with weight loss.","whyNot":["Squamous cell carcinoma most often arises in the middle third, linked to alcohol and tobacco. Barrett esophagus leads to a glandular tumor.","Correct.","Adenocarcinoma follows the columnar metaplasia, which forms in the distal esophagus. The upper third keeps its squamous lining.","Squamous cell carcinoma of the upper esophagus is linked to alcohol, tobacco, and Plummer-Vinson syndrome. It does not arise from Barrett metaplasia."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal adenocarcinoma versus squamous cell carcinoma"],"comps":["GI-13-K1","GI-13-G2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C39","system":"GI","dx":"Angiodysplasia of the cecum with sigmoid diverticulosis","codes":["GI-13-J4","GI-13-G3"],"case":"A 79-year-old woman comes to the physician because of three episodes of painless passage of maroon blood per rectum over the past 6 months and increasing fatigue. She also reports exertional lightheadedness. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mmHg. Physical examination shows conjunctival pallor and a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border that radiates to the carotid arteries. Hemoglobin concentration is 8.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), mean corpuscular volume is 72 fL (80 to 100), and serum ferritin is 8 ng/mL (15 to 200). Colonoscopy shows several small, flat, cherry-red lesions in the cecum and numerous diverticula in the sigmoid colon without inflammation. Echocardiography shows severe calcific aortic stenosis. The diagnosis is cecal angiodysplasia with sigmoid diverticulosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C39-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best links this patient's heart disease to the bleeding from her colonic lesions?","options":["Vitamin K deficiency from reduced colonic bacterial synthesis","Antibodies against platelet glycoprotein IIb/IIIa","Septic emboli from the valve eroding mucosal vessels","Shear-induced loss of large von Willebrand factor multimers"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J4","obj":"GI-13-J.4","dok":3,"approach":["Angiodysplasia with aortic stenosis is Heyde syndrome.","High-velocity, turbulent flow through the narrow valve stretches large von Willebrand factor multimers, exposing a site that ADAMTS13 cleaves.","Loss of the largest multimers reduces platelet adhesion, which matters most in fragile, thin-walled vessels such as angiodysplasias, so they bleed."],"why":"Angiodysplasias are dilated, thin-walled, tortuous submucosal vessels, mostly in the cecum and right colon of older adults. They are thought to form when repeated contraction of the colon wall partly blocks submucosal veins, and the high wall tension of the wide cecum adds to this. They cause painless, often recurrent lower GI bleeding and iron deficiency. In aortic stenosis, shear stress causes an acquired von Willebrand syndrome, which makes the lesions bleed more. Replacing the valve often reduces the bleeding. Angiodysplasia is also linked to end-stage kidney disease.","whyNot":["Vitamin K deficiency raises the PT and causes bleeding from many sites. Aortic stenosis does not cause it.","Antibodies against glycoprotein IIb/IIIa occur in immune thrombocytopenia, which lowers the platelet count and causes petechiae. Nothing links them to aortic stenosis.","Septic emboli come from infective endocarditis, which causes fever and vegetations. Her valve is calcified, and her lesions are vascular ectasias.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Angiodysplasia and Heyde syndrome","Acquired von Willebrand syndrome"],"comps":["GI-13-J4","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"GI-C39-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the outpouchings in this patient's sigmoid colon?","options":["Mucosa and submucosa herniating where vasa recta pierce the muscle","All wall layers bulging at a persistent embryonic duct remnant","Dilated submucosal veins draining into the inferior mesenteric vein","Mucosal folds raised over areas of submucosal lymphoid hyperplasia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G3","obj":"GI-13-G.3","dok":2,"approach":["Sigmoid diverticula in an older adult are acquired false diverticula.","Low-fiber diet and strong segmental contractions raise pressure in the narrow sigmoid colon.","That pressure pushes mucosa and submucosa out through weak points in the muscularis externa where the vasa recta enter the wall."],"why":"Diverticulosis comes from high intraluminal pressure acting on weak spots in the colon wall. The sigmoid colon is the narrowest part of the colon, so segmental contractions there produce the highest pressures. Mucosa and submucosa herniate through the muscularis where vasa recta penetrate it, which is why the vessel is stretched over the dome of the pouch. That vessel can rupture into the lumen and cause painless bleeding, often large. Diverticulitis is inflammation of a pouch after obstruction or microperforation and causes left lower quadrant pain, fever, abscess, or fistula.","whyNot":["Correct.","A full-thickness outpouching from an embryonic duct remnant is a Meckel diverticulum, which arises from the ileum.","Dilated submucosal veins in the anal canal are hemorrhoids, and dilated vessels in the right colon are angiodysplasia. Neither forms outpouchings.","Lymphoid hyperplasia raises small nodules on the mucosa. It does not create pouches that extend outside the colon wall."],"study":{"topics":["Diverticulosis versus diverticulitis","False diverticula and vasa recta"],"comps":["GI-13-G3","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C39-Q3","stem":"A month later, she has a brisk episode of bleeding. Angiography shows active extravasation of contrast from a vessel in the cecum. Which of the following arteries is the most likely source?","options":["Sigmoid branch of the inferior mesenteric artery","Left colic branch of the inferior mesenteric artery","Ileocolic branch of the superior mesenteric artery","Middle colic branch of the superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["The cecum is a midgut derivative, so it is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.","Among the branches of the superior mesenteric artery, the ileocolic artery supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix.","The middle colic artery supplies the transverse colon, and the branches of the inferior mesenteric artery supply the distal colon."],"why":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, from the second part of the duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. Its colic branches are the ileocolic (terminal ileum, cecum, appendix), right colic (ascending colon), and middle colic (transverse colon). The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum through its left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal branches. The marginal artery links the two systems. Its weak points, the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction, are the watershed zones for ischemic colitis.","whyNot":["The sigmoid arteries supply the sigmoid colon. They would be the source if one of her sigmoid diverticula bled.","The left colic artery supplies the distal transverse and descending colon. It does not reach the cecum.","Correct.","The middle colic artery supplies the transverse colon. It does not supply the cecum."],"study":{"topics":["Arterial supply of the colon","Watershed zones of the colon"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C39-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains the low mean corpuscular volume of this patient's erythrocytes?","options":["Delayed nuclear maturation from impaired DNA synthesis","Defective globin chain synthesis from an inherited gene mutation","Lead inhibition of ferrochelatase and ALA dehydratase","Too little iron for heme synthesis, so extra divisions occur"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Low hemoglobin, low MCV, and low ferritin together mean iron deficiency. Ferritin reflects body iron stores.","Repeated blood loss from the colon removes iron faster than the gut can absorb it, which drains her stores over months.","Without iron, the precursor cannot finish making heme and hemoglobin, so it divides extra times before maturing, and the red cells end up small and pale."],"why":"Iron is absorbed as ferrous iron in the duodenum and is needed for the final step of heme synthesis, when ferrochelatase inserts it into protoporphyrin IX. When iron runs out, hemoglobin builds up slowly in red cell precursors, so they go through extra divisions and give rise to small, pale (microcytic, hypochromic) cells. Low ferritin separates iron deficiency from other microcytic anemias. In an older adult, iron deficiency calls for a search for gastrointestinal blood loss. Other mineral deficiencies show different findings: zinc causes poor wound healing and dysgeusia, and copper causes anemia, neutropenia, and myelopathy.","whyNot":["Impaired DNA synthesis from folate or vitamin B12 deficiency delays nuclear maturation and makes large cells (macrocytic anemia). Her cells are small.","Thalassemia causes microcytosis from reduced globin synthesis, but ferritin is normal or high. Her low ferritin shows iron deficiency.","Lead poisoning can cause microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling, but ferritin is not low, and nothing suggests lead exposure.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Iron deficiency anemia from chronic GI blood loss","Mineral deficiency findings"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C40","system":"GI","dx":"Helicobacter pylori duodenal ulcer with bleeding","codes":["GI-13-G5"],"case":"A 45-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of black, tarry stools for 1 day and one episode of vomiting coffee-ground material. For 3 months, he has had burning epigastric pain that improves with meals and returns 2 to 3 hours later, often waking him at night. He does not take NSAIDs. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 98/62 mmHg. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.4 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), serum urea nitrogen is 38 mg/dL (7 to 20), and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.2). Upper endoscopy shows a 1.5-cm ulcer on the posterior wall of the duodenal bulb with a visible vessel. Antral biopsy specimens show curved bacilli in the surface mucus. The diagnosis is a bleeding Helicobacter pylori-associated duodenal ulcer.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C40-Q1","stem":"Which of the following arteries is most likely the source of this patient's bleeding?","options":["Gastroduodenal artery","Left gastric artery","Splenic artery","Superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G5","obj":"GI-13-G.5","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the ulcer: posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum.","The gastroduodenal artery runs directly behind the first part of the duodenum.","A posterior duodenal ulcer that erodes deeply can open this artery and cause major bleeding. An anterior ulcer is more likely to perforate and release free air."],"why":"Peptic ulcers can bleed, perforate, or obstruct. The vessel at risk depends on the ulcer's location. A posterior duodenal bulb ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery, a branch of the common hepatic artery, causing hematemesis and melena. An anterior duodenal ulcer is more likely to perforate into the peritoneal cavity and cause free air under the diaphragm. An ulcer on the lesser curvature of the stomach can erode the left gastric artery. Duodenal ulcers cause pain that improves with meals and are almost never malignant, while gastric ulcer pain worsens with meals, and gastric ulcers need biopsy to exclude cancer.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature of the stomach. It is the usual source of bleeding from lesser curvature gastric ulcers.","The splenic artery runs along the top of the pancreas behind the stomach. A posterior gastric ulcer could erode it, but it does not lie behind the duodenum.","The superior mesenteric artery passes behind the neck of the pancreas and in front of the third part of the duodenum. It is not related to the duodenal bulb."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of peptic ulcer disease","Gastric versus duodenal ulcers"],"comps":["GI-13-G5","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C40-Q2","stem":"A urea breath test is used to confirm eradication of the organism after treatment. Which of the following properties of the organism allows both this test and its survival in the stomach?","options":["Spore coat that resists gastric acid","Catalase that breaks down hydrogen peroxide","Polysaccharide capsule that blocks phagocytosis","Urease that converts urea to ammonia and CO2"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.6","dok":2,"approach":["In the breath test, the patient swallows urea labeled with carbon-13 or carbon-14.","If H. pylori is present, its urease splits the urea into ammonia and labeled CO2, which is absorbed and breathed out.","The same ammonia buffers acid around the organism, letting it survive in the mucus layer."],"why":"Helicobacter pylori is a curved, flagellated, microaerophilic gram-negative rod. Its flagella let it swim into the mucus layer, and its urease makes ammonia that neutralizes acid around it. Urease also explains the diagnostic tests: the urea breath test and the rapid urease test on biopsy tissue. Stool antigen testing and biopsy with histology or culture also detect it. The breath test and stool antigen detect active infection, so they are used to confirm eradication.","whyNot":["H. pylori does not form spores. Spore-forming bacteria include Bacillus and Clostridium species.","H. pylori is catalase positive, but catalase protects against oxidants. It does not neutralize acid or produce the labeled CO2 measured in the test.","A capsule helps bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae avoid phagocytosis. It does not protect against acid or explain the breath test.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Helicobacter pylori virulence factors","Tests for H. pylori"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"GI-C40-Q3","stem":"Infection of this patient's gastric antrum increases the acid load delivered to his duodenum. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this increase?","options":["Increased secretin release from duodenal S cells","Destruction of parietal cells in the gastric body and fundus","Reduced somatostatin from antral D cells, releasing gastrin","Autonomous gastrin secretion by a pancreatic tumor"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Normally, acid in the antrum stimulates D cells to release somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release from G cells.","Antral-predominant H. pylori gastritis damages D cells, so somatostatin falls.","Gastrin is no longer held back, so parietal cells secrete more acid, and more acid reaches the duodenum."],"why":"Somatostatin is the main brake on acid secretion. It acts locally on G cells, enterochromaffin-like cells, and parietal cells. When H. pylori infects the antrum, it reduces D cells and somatostatin, so gastrin and acid output rise. The excess acid entering the duodenum causes gastric metaplasia there, which H. pylori can then colonize, and the duodenal mucosa ulcerates. Infection centered in the gastric body has the opposite effect, causing atrophy and low acid.","whyNot":["Secretin is released by acid in the duodenum. It stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate and inhibits gastrin, so it lowers acid.","Destruction of parietal cells lowers acid output. This happens in autoimmune gastritis and in H. pylori gastritis centered in the gastric body.","Correct.","A gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome) causes very high gastrin and multiple ulcers. It is a tumor, and this patient's biopsy shows infection."],"study":{"topics":["Somatostatin and gastrin regulation","H. pylori and duodenal ulcers"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-G5"]}},{"id":"GI-C40-Q4","stem":"Endoscopy also shows a second ulcer on the posterior wall of the gastric body. If this ulcer perforated, into which of the following spaces would gastric contents first leak?","options":["Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch)","Omental bursa (lesser sac)","Right subphrenic space","Left paracolic gutter"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["The lesser sac (omental bursa) lies directly behind the stomach and lesser omentum, in front of the pancreas.","A perforation through the posterior wall of the stomach therefore opens into the lesser sac first.","Fluid can then pass through the omental (epiploic) foramen into the greater sac."],"why":"The peritoneal cavity has a large greater sac and a smaller lesser sac behind the stomach. The lesser sac is bounded in front by the stomach and lesser omentum and behind by the pancreas and posterior abdominal wall. It opens into the greater sac only through the omental (epiploic) foramen, which lies behind the free edge of the lesser omentum, the hepatoduodenal ligament. That ligament carries the portal vein, proper hepatic artery, and common bile duct. A posterior gastric perforation therefore fills the lesser sac first and may stay walled off there.","whyNot":["The hepatorenal recess is part of the greater sac. Lesser sac fluid can reach it only by passing through the omental foramen.","Correct.","The right subphrenic space lies between the liver and diaphragm in the greater sac. Contents from an anterior perforation can reach it.","The left paracolic gutter lies beside the descending colon in the greater sac. Fluid reaches it only after it spreads."],"study":{"topics":["Lesser sac and omental foramen","Hepatoduodenal ligament contents"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-G5"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C41","system":"GI","dx":"Curling ulcer after severe burns","codes":["GI-13-G7"],"case":"A 34-year-old man is admitted to the burn unit after a house fire with full-thickness and partial-thickness burns covering 45% of his total body surface area. He required large-volume fluid resuscitation for hypotension during the first 24 hours. On hospital day 4, he vomits blood and passes black stools. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Hemoglobin has fallen from 14.1 g/dL to 9.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5). Upper endoscopy shows multiple shallow erosions in the gastric body and a 1.5-cm ulcer on the posterior wall of the duodenal bulb. Diagnosis: Curling ulcer (acute stress ulceration) after severe burns.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C41-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the most important mechanism of mucosal injury in this patient?","options":["Vagally mediated increase in parietal cell acid output","Mucosal ischemia from hypovolemia and splanchnic constriction","Reduced prostaglandin synthesis from cyclooxygenase-1 inhibition","Urease-producing bacterial colonization of the antral mucosa"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G7","obj":"GI-13-G.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: a severe burn with hypotension, and ulcers that appear within days.","Recall that Curling ulcers follow burns, while Cushing ulcers follow brain injury.","Link burn shock to low splanchnic blood flow, which weakens the mucosal barrier."],"why":"After a major burn, plasma is lost from the circulation and the body shunts blood away from the gut. Low mucosal blood flow cuts the supply of oxygen and bicarbonate to the epithelium, slows cell renewal, and weakens the mucus and bicarbonate barrier. Even normal amounts of acid can then damage the mucosa and produce multiple acute erosions and ulcers within days. Curling ulcers are classically described in the proximal duodenum.","whyNot":["Vagal overstimulation that raises acid output is the mechanism of a Cushing ulcer, which follows head injury or raised intracranial pressure. This patient had burns and shock, with no brain injury.","Correct.","Loss of protective prostaglandins from cyclooxygenase-1 inhibition is how NSAIDs injure the mucosa. Nothing in his history points to NSAID use.","Helicobacter pylori causes chronic antral gastritis and most ordinary duodenal ulcers over months to years. It does not explain ulcers that appear within days of a burn."],"study":{"topics":["Stress ulcers: Curling and Cushing","Gastric mucosal defenses"],"comps":["GI-13-G5","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"GI-C41-Q2","stem":"On hospital day 6, he develops brisk hematemesis and his blood pressure falls to 78/40 mm Hg. Repeat endoscopy shows a visible vessel spurting from the base of the posterior duodenal bulb ulcer. Which of the following arteries is most likely bleeding?","options":["Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","Left gastric artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Splenic artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the ulcer: posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum.","Recall which artery runs directly behind the duodenal bulb.","The gastroduodenal artery, a branch of the common hepatic artery, lies there."],"why":"The celiac trunk gives off the common hepatic artery, which gives off the gastroduodenal artery. This vessel descends directly behind the first part of the duodenum before dividing into the right gastroepiploic and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. An ulcer that penetrates the posterior wall of the duodenal bulb can erode it and cause life-threatening bleeding. Anterior duodenal ulcers tend to perforate into the peritoneal cavity instead.","whyNot":["The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery comes from the superior mesenteric artery and supplies the lower duodenum and pancreatic head. It does not run behind the first part of the duodenum.","The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature of the stomach. It is the usual source of bleeding from a lesser curvature gastric ulcer.","Correct.","The splenic artery runs along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach. A posterior gastric ulcer can erode it, but it does not lie behind the duodenal bulb."],"study":{"topics":["Celiac trunk branches","Bleeding versus perforating duodenal ulcers"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-G5"]}},{"id":"GI-C41-Q3","stem":"After the bleeding is controlled, enteral feeding with a peptide-based formula is started through a nasogastric tube. Gastric acid output rises during the feeding. Which of the following best explains the effect of the peptides on acid secretion?","options":["Stimulation of antral G cells to release gastrin","Stimulation of antral D cells to release somatostatin","Stimulation of duodenal S cells to release secretin","Stimulation of duodenal I cells to release cholecystokinin"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the phase: food in the stomach means the gastric phase.","Recall that peptides and amino acids in the antrum are a direct stimulus for G cells.","Follow gastrin to histamine release and parietal cell activation."],"why":"In the gastric phase, which produces most of the acid in a meal, peptides and amino acids in the antrum stimulate G cells to release gastrin into the blood. Gastrin acts mainly on enterochromaffin-like cells, which release histamine onto nearby parietal cells, and it also stimulates parietal cells directly. Gastric distension adds vagovagal and local cholinergic reflexes. All of these signals converge on the H+/K+ ATPase, the final step that pumps acid into the lumen.","whyNot":["Correct.","Somatostatin from D cells inhibits gastrin release and parietal cell secretion. D cells are stimulated mainly by acid in the lumen, which acts as a brake on secretion.","Secretin is released when acid enters the duodenum. It stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate and inhibits gastrin release, so it lowers gastric acid output.","Cholecystokinin is released by fats and amino acids in the duodenum. It stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzymes and slows gastric emptying, and its overall effect on acid secretion is inhibitory."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of gastric acid secretion","Gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C41-Q4","stem":"Biopsies taken from the gastric body during endoscopy show intact oxyntic glands. If these glands were instead destroyed by autoimmune gastritis, which of the following sets of findings would be expected?","options":["Low acid, low intrinsic factor, low gastrin","Low acid, low intrinsic factor, high gastrin","Low acid, normal intrinsic factor, low gastrin","High acid, low intrinsic factor, high gastrin"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that oxyntic glands of the body and fundus hold parietal and chief cells.","Parietal cells make both HCl and intrinsic factor, so both fall when they are destroyed.","The antrum is spared, and loss of acid removes somatostatin braking, so gastrin rises."],"why":"Autoimmune gastritis targets parietal cells and intrinsic factor in the body and fundus. Losing parietal cells removes both hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, which leads to achlorhydria and, over years, vitamin B12 deficiency. Chief cells are also lost, so pepsinogen I falls. The antral G cells are spared, and without acid to trigger somatostatin release, gastrin climbs high and drives enterochromaffin-like cell hyperplasia.","whyNot":["The antral G cells are spared in autoimmune gastritis. Without acid to stimulate somatostatin release, gastrin rises.","Correct.","Intrinsic factor comes from the same parietal cells that make acid, so it falls along with acid. Gastrin rises, since the antral G cells are spared and lose acid feedback.","Acid comes from parietal cells, which are destroyed. High acid with high gastrin is the pattern of a gastrin-secreting tumor (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome)."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types","Autoimmune gastritis"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-E1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C42","system":"GI","dx":"Reflux esophagitis with a sliding hiatal hernia in pregnancy","codes":["GI-13-G8","GI-13-G6"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman at 30 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of a 6-week history of burning retrosternal pain after meals and when lying down, sour regurgitation at night, and pain with swallowing. Her symptoms began at about 20 weeks' gestation and have worsened. Her body mass index before pregnancy was 31 kg/m2. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the fetal heart rate is normal. Because of persistent odynophagia, upper endoscopy is performed. It shows a 3-cm sliding hiatal hernia and several linear erosions in the distal 5 cm of the esophagus. Diagnosis: reflux esophagitis with a sliding hiatal hernia during pregnancy.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C42-Q1","stem":"In addition to her hiatal hernia and higher intra-abdominal pressure, which of the following most directly contributes to her reflux?","options":["Motilin-mediated increase in migrating motor complexes","Increased vagal cholinergic tone to the LES muscle","Estrogen-mediated increase in parietal cell acid output","Progesterone-mediated decrease in LES resting tone"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-G8","obj":"GI-13-G.9","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the risk factors named: pregnancy, obesity, and hiatal hernia.","Recall that progesterone relaxes smooth muscle throughout the gut.","Apply it to the lower esophageal sphincter: lower resting tone lets acid reflux."],"why":"Reflux happens when the antireflux barrier fails through transient sphincter relaxations, a low resting sphincter pressure, or a hiatal hernia. In pregnancy, rising progesterone relaxes the smooth muscle of the lower esophageal sphincter and lowers its resting pressure. The enlarging uterus raises intra-abdominal pressure and pushes gastric contents upward. Her obesity and sliding hernia add to the same problem, which is why symptoms worsen as pregnancy advances.","whyNot":["Migrating motor complexes sweep the fasting gut clean and would speed clearance. Progesterone tends to slow gut motility.","Vagal cholinergic input contracts the lower esophageal sphincter and raises its tone. More of it would protect against reflux.","Pregnancy hormones do not meaningfully raise gastric acid output. Reflux in pregnancy comes from a weak barrier at the gastroesophageal junction.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["GERD mechanisms","Hormonal effects on gut smooth muscle"],"comps":["GI-13-G8","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C42-Q2","stem":"Biopsies are taken from the mid and distal esophagus. Which of the following findings would most strongly support reflux esophagitis over eosinophilic esophagitis?","options":["A sharply punched-out ulcer in the mid esophagus near the aortic arch","Dense eosinophils, 15 or more per high-power field, in mid and distal biopsies","Scattered eosinophils, under 15 per high-power field, in distal biopsies only","Concentric mucosal rings and linear furrows along the esophageal length"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G6","obj":"GI-13-G.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that both conditions can show eosinophils and basal cell hyperplasia.","Separate them by eosinophil count and by where they are found.","Reflux injures the distal esophagus most, with only a few eosinophils."],"why":"Acid reflux injures the squamous epithelium closest to the stomach. Biopsies show basal zone hyperplasia, elongated lamina propria papillae, and a few intraepithelial eosinophils and neutrophils, mostly in the distal esophagus. Eosinophilic esophagitis is an allergic, Th2-driven disease with dense eosinophils (15 or more per high-power field) along the whole esophagus. A normal mid-esophageal biopsy with mild distal changes points to reflux.","whyNot":["A discrete mid-esophageal ulcer where the aortic arch narrows the lumen suggests pill esophagitis, from a tablet such as doxycycline lodging there.","Fifteen or more eosinophils per high-power field in both the mid and distal esophagus is the histologic definition of eosinophilic esophagitis, often in a patient with atopy and food impaction.","Correct.","Stacked rings (trachealization) and linear furrows are the classic endoscopic look of eosinophilic esophagitis."],"study":{"topics":["Reflux versus eosinophilic esophagitis","Pill esophagitis"],"comps":["GI-13-G6","GI-13-G8"]}},{"id":"GI-C42-Q3","stem":"In her type of hiatal hernia, which part of the stomach is displaced into the thorax together with the gastroesophageal junction?","options":["Antrum","Pylorus","Fundus","Cardia"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Define a sliding hernia: the gastroesophageal junction moves up through the hiatus.","Name the stomach region that surrounds the gastroesophageal junction.","That region, the cardia, goes up with it."],"why":"The stomach has five regions: cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus. The cardia is the small zone that surrounds the opening of the esophagus. In a sliding hiatal hernia, the most common type, the gastroesophageal junction and cardia slide up through the esophageal hiatus into the thorax. This removes the help that the crura normally give the sphincter and favors reflux. In a paraesophageal hernia, the junction stays put and the fundus herniates beside the esophagus.","whyNot":["The antrum is the distal stomach near the pylorus. It is not displaced in a small sliding hernia.","The pylorus is the outlet to the duodenum, far from the hiatus. It stays in the abdomen.","The fundus rolls up beside an esophagus that stays in place in a paraesophageal (rolling) hernia. In a sliding hernia the junction itself moves up.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Regions of the stomach","Sliding versus paraesophageal hernia"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-F3"]}},{"id":"GI-C42-Q4","stem":"Refluxed acid is normally cleared from her esophagus by secondary peristalsis triggered by distension. Which of the following best describes how the enteric nervous system propels the bolus?","options":["Cholinergic contraction above and nitrergic relaxation below the bolus","Simultaneous contraction of the whole length of the esophageal body","Nitrergic relaxation above and cholinergic contraction below the bolus","Motilin-driven contraction that moves from the distal to the proximal end"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the law of the intestine: contract behind the bolus, relax in front of it.","Name the transmitters: acetylcholine and substance P contract, nitric oxide and VIP relax.","Match the option with the right transmitter on the right side."],"why":"Peristalsis is coordinated by the myenteric plexus. When a bolus stretches the wall, excitatory neurons release acetylcholine and substance P to contract the circular muscle on the oral side. Inhibitory neurons release nitric oxide and VIP to relax the muscle on the anal side. The ring of contraction moves down the tube and pushes the bolus ahead of it. Secondary peristalsis uses this local reflex to clear refluxed acid back into the stomach.","whyNot":["Correct.","Simultaneous contractions do not propel anything. This is the abnormal pattern of distal esophageal spasm.","This pattern would push the bolus backward toward the mouth. The contraction must be behind the bolus.","Motilin drives the fasting migrating motor complex in the stomach and small bowel, and it moves in the oral to anal direction."],"study":{"topics":["Peristalsis and the myenteric plexus","Esophageal acid clearance"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C43","system":"GI","dx":"Ulcerative colitis with toxic megacolon","codes":["GI-13-G9","GI-13-F5"],"case":"A 24-year-old man with ulcerative colitis diagnosed 3 years ago is brought to the emergency department because of 10 days of bloody diarrhea, 12 stools daily, and 2 days of increasing abdominal swelling and pain. He stopped his maintenance medication 2 months ago. Temperature is 39.0 °C (102.2 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. The abdomen is distended and diffusely tender with reduced bowel sounds. Leukocyte count is 16,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000). An abdominal x-ray shows the transverse colon dilated to 8 cm (normal under 6 cm). Diagnosis: ulcerative colitis complicated by toxic megacolon.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C43-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings was most likely seen on colonoscopic biopsy when his disease was first diagnosed?","options":["Continuous mucosal inflammation from the rectum with crypt abscesses","Caseating granulomas with ulcers clustered in the ileocecal region","Skip lesions with transmural inflammation and noncaseating granulomas","Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites that contain ingested red cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G9","obj":"GI-13-G.10","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the distribution of ulcerative colitis: rectum first, then continuous proximal spread.","Recall its depth: mucosa and submucosa only.","Match the histology: neutrophils in crypts form crypt abscesses."],"why":"Ulcerative colitis always involves the rectum and extends proximally in one continuous stretch. Inflammation is limited to the mucosa and submucosa. Neutrophils invade crypts to form crypt abscesses, and chronic disease distorts crypt architecture. Ulcers undermine the mucosa, and islands of regenerating mucosa between them form pseudopolyps. Granulomas and skip lesions argue for Crohn disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Caseating granulomas in the ileocecal region suggest intestinal tuberculosis.","Skip lesions, transmural inflammation, and noncaseating granulomas are the pattern of Crohn disease.","Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites that ingest red cells are typical of amebic colitis from Entamoeba histolytica."],"study":{"topics":["Ulcerative colitis versus Crohn disease"],"comps":["GI-13-G9","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C43-Q2","stem":"He recovers after medical treatment. Which of the following long-term complications is more characteristic of his disease than of Crohn disease?","options":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Enterocutaneous fistula","Calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis","Primary sclerosing cholangitis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G9","obj":"GI-13-G.11","dok":3,"approach":["Sort complications by mechanism: transmural or ileal disease points to Crohn disease.","Recall which extraintestinal problem is linked most strongly to ulcerative colitis.","Primary sclerosing cholangitis fits; it occurs in a large share of patients with ulcerative colitis."],"why":"Most patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis have inflammatory bowel disease, usually ulcerative colitis. The bile ducts become inflamed and fibrosed, giving a beaded pattern on cholangiography, a cholestatic rise in alkaline phosphatase, and p-ANCA positivity in many cases. It raises the risk of cholangiocarcinoma and of colorectal cancer. Fistulas, strictures, B12 deficiency, and oxalate stones all come from transmural or ileal disease in Crohn disease.","whyNot":["Vitamin B12 is absorbed in the terminal ileum, which is often diseased or resected in Crohn disease. Ulcerative colitis spares the small bowel, apart from mild backwash ileitis.","Fistulas need transmural inflammation, which is a feature of Crohn disease. Ulcerative colitis stays in the mucosa.","Oxalate stones follow fat malabsorption in ileal Crohn disease. Unabsorbed fat binds calcium, which frees oxalate to be absorbed in the colon.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of IBD","Primary sclerosing cholangitis"],"comps":["GI-13-G9","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C43-Q3","stem":"His younger brother has Crohn disease of the terminal ileum and carries a loss-of-function NOD2 variant. Which of the following best explains how this variant predisposes to Crohn disease?","options":["Excess binding of bacterial lipopolysaccharide by surface TLR4","Impaired cytosolic sensing of bacterial peptidoglycan","Deficient transport of secretory IgA across the epithelium","Loss of FOXP3 expression in regulatory T cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that NOD2 is an intracellular pattern recognition receptor.","Name its ligand: muramyl dipeptide from bacterial peptidoglycan.","Link weak sensing to poor clearance of commensal bacteria and a chronic Th1 and Th17 response."],"why":"NOD2 is a cytosolic receptor in Paneth cells, monocytes, and macrophages that recognizes muramyl dipeptide from bacterial peptidoglycan. Loss-of-function variants weaken antimicrobial defensin release and bacterial killing, so commensal bacteria cross the epithelium and persist. The immune system answers with an exaggerated Th1 response (interferon-gamma) and Th17 response (IL-17) that drives transmural inflammation and noncaseating granulomas. Crohn disease often affects the terminal ileum, where Paneth cells are most dense.","whyNot":["Toll-like receptor 4 senses lipopolysaccharide at the cell surface. NOD2 is a separate, cytosolic receptor, and the Crohn variants reduce its function.","Correct.","Secretory IgA is carried across the epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor. NOD2 is an innate sensor inside the cell and has no role in IgA transport.","FOXP3 mutations cause IPEX syndrome, with severe enteropathy, type 1 diabetes, and dermatitis in infant boys. They are not the NOD2 mechanism."],"study":{"topics":["NOD2 and innate immunity","Th1 and Th17 in Crohn disease"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"GI-C43-Q4","stem":"Two years later he has a total colectomy with an end ileostomy. Over the next several months, the ileostomy output averages 2 L/day. He then develops poor healing of the stoma site, reduced taste, and scaly, red plaques around the mouth and on the hands. Deficiency of which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Selenium","Iron","Zinc","Copper"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note the cause: large, ongoing intestinal fluid losses.","Group the findings: poor wound healing, dysgeusia, and periorificial and acral dermatitis.","Match the triad to the mineral lost in intestinal fluid."],"why":"Zinc is lost in large amounts in diarrhea and high-output stomas. It is a cofactor for many enzymes, including those needed for cell division, collagen formation, and taste. Deficiency causes poor wound healing, loss of taste and smell, hair loss, and a scaly, red dermatitis around the mouth, anus, and on the hands and feet, called acrodermatitis. The inherited form, acrodermatitis enteropathica, comes from a defect in intestinal zinc uptake.","whyNot":["Selenium deficiency causes cardiomyopathy (Keshan disease) and muscle weakness. Skin and taste changes are not its pattern.","Iron deficiency causes microcytic anemia, fatigue, glossitis, and spoon-shaped nails. It does not cause periorificial dermatitis.","Correct.","Copper deficiency causes anemia, neutropenia, and a myelopathy that resembles vitamin B12 deficiency. It does not cause this rash or taste loss."],"study":{"topics":["Zinc deficiency","Mineral deficiencies"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C44","system":"GI","dx":"Internal hemorrhoids with a coexisting anal fissure","codes":["GI-13-J2"],"case":"A 33-year-old woman comes to the physician 3 months after a vaginal delivery because of bright red blood on the toilet paper after bowel movements and a soft swelling that protrudes from the anus during defecation and reduces spontaneously. She has had constipation with hard stools since late pregnancy. For the past 3 weeks, she has also had sharp, tearing pain during defecation that lasts for an hour afterward. Vital signs are within normal limits. Inspection shows a small linear tear in the posterior midline of the anoderm. Anoscopy shows engorged vascular cushions above the pectinate line. Diagnosis: grade II internal hemorrhoids with an acute anal fissure.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C44-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the development of her hemorrhoids?","options":["Portal hypertension diverting blood through rectal portosystemic anastomoses","Straining that engorges and displaces the anal vascular cushions","Obstruction and infection of the anal glands at the pectinate line","Weakness of the pelvic floor allowing full-thickness rectal descent"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J2","obj":"GI-13-J.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note her risks: constipation, straining, and recent pregnancy.","Recall that hemorrhoids are normal vascular cushions that become enlarged and slide down.","Choose the mechanism that links straining to cushion engorgement."],"why":"Everyone has anal cushions, made of submucosal vessels and connective tissue that help seal the anal canal. Straining with hard stools and the pressure of a pregnant uterus on pelvic veins engorge these cushions, and their supporting tissue stretches so they slide downward. Internal hemorrhoids arise above the pectinate line. They cause painless bright red bleeding and may prolapse; grade II hemorrhoids return on their own. External hemorrhoids lie below the line and hurt when they thrombose.","whyNot":["Portal hypertension opens anorectal varices at the portosystemic anastomosis, which are a different lesion from hemorrhoids. She has no liver disease.","Correct.","Obstructed, infected anal glands produce an anorectal abscess, which causes constant throbbing pain and fever.","Full-thickness descent of the rectal wall through the anus is rectal prolapse, which shows concentric mucosal folds. Her swelling is engorged vascular cushions."],"study":{"topics":["Hemorrhoid pathogenesis","Internal versus external hemorrhoids"],"comps":["GI-13-J2","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C44-Q2","stem":"Her hemorrhoids are painless, but her fissure is very painful. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["Sympathetic afferents above the pectinate line and vagal afferents below","Absent sensory fibers above the pectinate line and pelvic splanchnic fibers below","Pudendal somatic fibers above the pectinate line and visceral afferents below","Visceral afferents above the pectinate line and pudendal somatic fibers below"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.5","dok":2,"approach":["Locate each lesion: internal hemorrhoids above the pectinate line, fissure below it.","Recall that the area above the line is endoderm-derived gut with visceral innervation.","The area below is ectoderm-derived skin supplied by the inferior rectal branches of the pudendal nerve."],"why":"The pectinate line marks where gut endoderm meets ectoderm. Above it, the mucosa has visceral innervation that senses stretch but not cutting or tearing, so internal hemorrhoids are painless. Below it, the anoderm is supplied by somatic fibers in the inferior rectal nerve, a branch of the pudendal nerve, so a fissure there is sharply painful. The same line divides the epithelium, the arterial supply, portal versus systemic venous drainage, and internal iliac versus superficial inguinal lymph drainage.","whyNot":["The vagus does not supply the anal canal. Visceral fibers above the line travel with the pelvic splanchnic and inferior hypogastric nerves, and the area below is somatic.","The mucosa above the line does have visceral afferents that sense stretch, and pelvic splanchnic nerves carry visceral fibers.","This reverses the anatomy. The pudendal nerve supplies the skin-lined anal canal below the line.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pectinate line anatomy","Anal canal innervation"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-2"]}},{"id":"GI-C44-Q3","stem":"Eight months later, she returns because of 2 days of constant, throbbing perianal pain that is worse when sitting. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F). There is a tender, red, fluctuant swelling beside the anal verge. Which of the following is the most likely origin of this new problem?","options":["Thrombosis within the inferior rectal venous plexus","Strangulation of a prolapsed, irreducible internal hemorrhoid","Infected, obstructed anal glands at the pectinate line","Extension of the posterior fissure into the rectal wall"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G","obj":"GI-13-G.13","dok":2,"approach":["Note the new features: fever, constant pain, and a fluctuant swelling.","Recognize an anorectal abscess.","Recall that most begin in anal glands that open into the crypts at the pectinate line."],"why":"Anal glands open into the anal crypts at the pectinate line. When a gland duct becomes blocked, bacteria multiply and form an abscess that spreads into the perianal or ischioanal tissues. The result is constant throbbing pain, fever, and a tender fluctuant swelling. After drainage, many abscesses leave a fistula between the crypt and the perianal skin. Recurrent abscesses and fistulas should raise concern for Crohn disease.","whyNot":["A thrombosed external hemorrhoid is a painful, bluish, firm lump at the anal verge. It does not cause fever or fluctuance.","A strangulated internal hemorrhoid is a painful, dark, irreducible mass protruding through the anus. It is not a fluctuant collection beside the anus.","Correct.","Fissures stay in the anoderm below the pectinate line. A fissure does not track into the rectum to form a fluctuant mass."],"study":{"topics":["Anorectal abscess and fistula","Anal fissure"],"comps":["GI-13-G","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"GI-C44-Q4","stem":"Her delivery was prolonged, with forceps assistance. Stretch injury to both pudendal nerves during such a delivery would most directly impair which of the following components of fecal continence?","options":["Reflex relaxation of the internal anal sphincter to rectal filling","Resting tone of the internal anal sphincter between defecations","Rectal stretch sensation carried by pelvic splanchnic afferents","Voluntary contraction of the external anal sphincter"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["List the parts of continence: rectal sensation, internal sphincter reflex, and external sphincter control.","Recall which nerve supplies each part.","The pudendal nerve supplies the voluntary external sphincter, so its loss causes urge incontinence."],"why":"When stool enters the rectum, stretch receptors signal fullness and the internal anal sphincter relaxes by a reflex through the myenteric plexus. Continence then depends on voluntary contraction of the external anal sphincter and puborectalis, which are skeletal muscles supplied by the pudendal nerve (S2 to S4). Pudendal injury from a prolonged or instrumented delivery weakens this voluntary squeeze. The person feels the urge but cannot hold stool until she reaches a toilet.","whyNot":["Reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter when the rectum fills is mediated by the myenteric plexus in the gut wall. It survives pudendal injury.","The internal sphincter is smooth muscle with tone maintained by its own properties and autonomic input. The pudendal nerve does not supply it.","Stretch of the rectum is sensed by visceral afferents traveling with the pelvic splanchnic nerves. These are separate from the pudendal nerve.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Defecation reflex","Fecal incontinence after childbirth"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-5"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C45","system":"GI","dx":"Esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula and duodenal atresia in trisomy 21","codes":["GI-13-H1"],"case":"A newborn girl born at 37 weeks' gestation is noted to have frothy oral secretions, and she chokes, coughs, and becomes cyanotic at her first feed. The pregnancy was complicated by polyhydramnios. She has upslanting palpebral fissures, a flat facial profile, and single transverse palmar creases. A nasogastric tube cannot be passed beyond 10 cm, and a chest x-ray shows it coiled in a blind upper esophageal pouch. An abdominal x-ray shows two large gas-filled bubbles in the upper abdomen with no gas beyond them. Karyotype is 47,XX,+21. Diagnosis: esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula and duodenal atresia in a newborn with trisomy 21.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C45-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the presence of gas in her stomach?","options":["A fistula joins the trachea to the distal esophagus","Bacteria in the stomach produce gas by fermenting milk","A fistula joins the trachea to the proximal esophageal pouch","Swallowed air passes through a narrowed but open esophagus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H1","obj":"GI-13-H.1","dok":3,"approach":["Note the blind upper pouch: swallowed air cannot reach the stomach that way.","Ask how air enters the stomach anyway: through the airway.","A fistula from the trachea to the lower esophagus carries inspired air into the stomach."],"why":"The most common type of esophageal atresia, about 85% of cases, has a blind upper pouch and a fistula from the trachea to the distal esophagus. Each breath and cry pushes air through the fistula into the stomach, so the abdomen contains gas. Saliva fills the blind pouch and spills into the larynx, causing drooling, choking, and cyanosis with feeds. The fetus could not swallow amniotic fluid, which caused polyhydramnios. Look for VACTERL anomalies: vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, and limb defects.","whyNot":["Correct.","The newborn gut has very few bacteria, and she has not been fed successfully. Fermentation does not explain gas within the first hours of life.","A fistula from the trachea to the upper pouch can send saliva into the lungs. Without a distal connection, air could not reach the stomach, and the abdomen would be gasless.","The case states the esophagus ends in a blind pouch, so no air can pass from the upper esophagus to the stomach."],"study":{"topics":["Types of esophageal atresia","VACTERL association"],"comps":["GI-13-H1","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C45-Q2","stem":"Abnormal development of which of the following structures most likely produced her esophageal defect?","options":["Pleuroperitoneal membrane","Aorticopulmonary septum","Tracheoesophageal septum","Urorectal septum"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion as a faulty separation of the foregut into trachea and esophagus.","Recall that the respiratory diverticulum grows from the ventral foregut in week 4.","The tracheoesophageal septum divides the two; when it deviates or fails, atresia and fistula result."],"why":"In week 4, the respiratory diverticulum buds from the ventral wall of the foregut. Tracheoesophageal ridges grow toward each other and fuse into the tracheoesophageal septum, which separates the ventral trachea from the dorsal esophagus. If the septum deviates posteriorly or the division is incomplete, the esophagus may end blindly and a channel may remain between the trachea and esophagus. These defects often occur with other VACTERL anomalies.","whyNot":["Failure of a pleuroperitoneal membrane to close leaves a congenital diaphragmatic hernia, usually on the left, with bowel in the chest.","The aorticopulmonary septum divides the truncus arteriosus. Its defects cause persistent truncus arteriosus or transposition of the great arteries.","Correct.","The urorectal septum divides the cloaca into the rectum and urogenital sinus. Defects cause anorectal malformations and fistulas to the urinary tract."],"study":{"topics":["Foregut division","Tracheoesophageal fistula embryology"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-H1"]}},{"id":"GI-C45-Q3","stem":"At surgery, the second part of the duodenum ends blindly just distal to the major duodenal papilla, with no pancreatic tissue around it. Failure of which of the following developmental processes most likely explains this finding?","options":["Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut around the SMA","Recanalization of the solid-stage duodenal lumen","Migration of the ventral pancreatic bud around the duodenum","Obliteration of the vitelline duct at the ileum"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note the findings: a blind-ending duodenum with a double bubble and no distal gas.","Recall that the duodenal lumen fills with cells in weeks 5 to 6 and opens again by about the end of week 8.","Failed reopening leaves atresia, which is strongly linked to Down syndrome."],"why":"Epithelial cells briefly fill the lumen of the duodenum during weeks 5 to 6. The lumen normally reopens by vacuolization by about the end of the second month. If this recanalization fails, the duodenum is blocked, usually near or just beyond the major papilla, so vomitus contains bile. The stomach and proximal duodenum fill with gas to make the double bubble sign, and the fetus cannot absorb swallowed fluid, adding to polyhydramnios. About a quarter to a third of infants with duodenal atresia have Down syndrome.","whyNot":["Malrotation leaves the cecum high and allows Ladd bands or volvulus to compress the duodenum. It does not create a blind-ending duodenum.","Correct.","Abnormal migration of the ventral pancreatic bud forms an annular pancreas, a ring of pancreas around the duodenum. The case states there is no pancreatic tissue around the obstruction.","A persistent vitelline duct remnant forms a Meckel diverticulum in the ileum. It does not cause duodenal obstruction at birth."],"study":{"topics":["Duodenal atresia","Gut recanalization"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-H"]}},{"id":"GI-C45-Q4","stem":"Saliva pools in her blind upper pouch. In a healthy newborn, once a bolus of milk reaches the pharynx, which of the following controls its passage into the esophagus?","options":["Reflex program of the medullary swallowing center","Local myenteric reflexes independent of the brainstem","Sympathetic outflow from the thoracic spinal cord","Voluntary commands from the primary motor cortex"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Divide swallowing into oral, pharyngeal, and esophageal phases.","Recall that only the oral phase is voluntary.","The pharyngeal phase is a reflex run by the medullary swallowing center."],"why":"Swallowing begins voluntarily when the tongue pushes the bolus back. Contact with the pharynx triggers a fixed reflex sequence controlled by the swallowing center in the medulla. The soft palate seals the nasopharynx, the larynx rises and the epiglottis covers it, breathing stops briefly, and the upper esophageal sphincter relaxes. Primary peristalsis then carries the bolus down, and vagal inhibitory nerves relax the lower esophageal sphincter ahead of it.","whyNot":["Correct.","The pharynx and upper esophagus are skeletal muscle controlled by brainstem motor nuclei. The myenteric plexus governs the smooth muscle of the lower esophagus.","Sympathetic outflow does not drive swallowing. The pharyngeal phase uses cranial nerves IX, X, and the nucleus ambiguus.","Only the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary. Once the bolus touches the pharynx, the sequence cannot be stopped by will."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of swallowing","Medullary swallowing center"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C46","system":"GI","dx":"Plummer-Vinson syndrome","codes":["GI-13-H2"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 5 months of progressive difficulty swallowing solid food, with a sensation that food sticks in her throat. She also reports fatigue. She had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass 8 years ago and stopped taking supplements 3 years ago. Physical examination shows pallor, a smooth red tongue, cracks at the corners of the mouth, and spoon-shaped fingernails. Hemoglobin is 8.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), mean corpuscular volume is 68 fL (80 to 100), and ferritin is 5 ng/mL (15 to 150). A barium swallow shows a thin shelf-like membrane in the upper esophagus just below the cricopharyngeus. Diagnosis: Plummer-Vinson syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C46-Q1","stem":"This patient has an increased long-term risk of which of the following?","options":["Gastrointestinal stromal tumor of the stomach","Extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of the stomach","Squamous cell carcinoma of the upper esophagus","Adenocarcinoma arising in Barrett esophagus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H2","obj":"GI-13-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion: an upper esophageal web with iron deficiency anemia.","Recall the cancer linked to Plummer-Vinson syndrome.","Contrast it with the lower esophageal Schatzki ring, a benign ring at the squamocolumnar junction."],"why":"Plummer-Vinson syndrome combines iron deficiency anemia, dysphagia, and webs in the upper esophagus. Webs are thin mucosal folds that cause dysphagia mainly for solids. The syndrome carries a higher risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the hypopharynx and upper esophagus, so patients need follow-up. A Schatzki ring is a different lesion in the lower esophagus at the squamocolumnar junction that causes intermittent solid food dysphagia, often with a hiatal hernia.","whyNot":["A gastrointestinal stromal tumor arises from interstitial cells of Cajal, usually with a KIT mutation. It has no link to iron deficiency or webs.","Gastric MALT lymphoma (extranodal marginal zone lymphoma) is driven by chronic Helicobacter pylori infection. It is not linked to esophageal webs.","Correct.","Distal esophageal adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett metaplasia after long-standing reflux. A Schatzki ring sits at that level but carries no comparable cancer risk."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal webs versus Schatzki rings","Plummer-Vinson syndrome"],"comps":["GI-13-H2","GI-13-K1"]}},{"id":"GI-C46-Q2","stem":"She is treated with intravenous iron. Six months later, her ferritin is normal, but hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL with an absolute neutrophil count of 900/mm3 (1,500 to 8,000). She has a new unsteady gait with loss of vibration and position sense in the feet. She has been taking a high-dose zinc supplement daily since her treatment began. Serum vitamin B12 and methylmalonic acid levels are normal. Deficiency of which of the following most likely explains these new findings?","options":["Folate","Copper","Vitamin E","Vitamin B12"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Group the findings: anemia, neutropenia, and a dorsal column myelopathy.","B12 deficiency looks similar, but her B12 and methylmalonic acid are normal.","Link high zinc intake and gastric bypass to copper deficiency."],"why":"Copper deficiency produces anemia, neutropenia, and a myelopathy that closely mimics subacute combined degeneration from B12 deficiency. Two of her risks point there. Gastric bypass bypasses the stomach and proximal small bowel, where copper is absorbed. High zinc intake induces metallothionein in enterocytes, which binds copper tightly so it is lost when the cells shed into the lumen. Stopping zinc and replacing copper corrects the blood counts, though nerve damage may not fully recover.","whyNot":["Folate deficiency causes a megaloblastic anemia but no myelopathy.","Correct.","Vitamin E deficiency causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia. It does not cause neutropenia, and it is not linked to zinc excess.","Vitamin B12 deficiency also causes anemia, neutropenia, and dorsal column myelopathy, but her normal B12 and methylmalonic acid levels rule it out."],"study":{"topics":["Copper deficiency","Zinc and copper interaction"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C46-Q3","stem":"Biopsy of the web would most likely show which of the following lining epithelia?","options":["Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Simple columnar with interspersed goblet cells","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Keratinized stratified squamous"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the web: the upper esophagus.","Recall that a web is a fold of mucosa, covered by the normal local epithelium.","The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium."],"why":"An esophageal web is a thin fold of mucosa that projects into the lumen, so it is covered by the normal esophageal lining: nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium over lamina propria. This lining resists abrasion from swallowed food. The stomach and intestine are lined by simple columnar epithelium. At the gastroesophageal junction, chronic acid reflux can replace squamous cells with columnar cells and goblet cells, which is Barrett metaplasia and the precursor of adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["Correct.","Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells is intestinal metaplasia, the change of Barrett esophagus in the distal esophagus.","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the trachea and bronchi.","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium covers the skin. The esophagus is normally nonkeratinized."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal epithelium","Barrett metaplasia"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G2"]}},{"id":"GI-C46-Q4","stem":"Her bypass routes food from a small gastric pouch directly into the jejunum. Diversion of food away from which of the following segments most directly explains her original anemia?","options":["Terminal ileum","Ascending colon","Duodenum","Distal jejunum"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the anemia: microcytic with very low ferritin, so iron deficiency.","Recall where iron is absorbed: mostly the duodenum and proximal jejunum.","Note that the Roux-en-Y bypass diverts food past the duodenum."],"why":"Iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum. There, acid helps keep it soluble, duodenal cytochrome b reduces ferric to ferrous iron, and DMT1 carries it into enterocytes. A Roux-en-Y gastric bypass sends food from a small gastric pouch straight into the jejunum, skipping the duodenum. The small pouch also makes little acid. Folate is absorbed in the jejunum, and B12 and bile acids in the terminal ileum.","whyNot":["The terminal ileum absorbs vitamin B12 and bile acids. Loss of it would cause a macrocytic anemia, and it still receives food after this operation.","The colon absorbs water and electrolytes and very little iron.","Correct.","The distal jejunum continues to receive food after a Roux-en-Y bypass. It is not the main site of iron uptake."],"study":{"topics":["Sites of nutrient absorption","Iron absorption"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-8"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C47","system":"GI","dx":"Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","codes":["GI-13-H4"],"case":"A 4-week-old boy, his parents' first child, is brought to the physician because of forceful vomiting after every feed for 5 days. The vomitus is milk without green or yellow staining, and he is eager to feed again right after vomiting. He was born at term and fed well until 3 weeks of age. At 2 weeks, he received a course of oral erythromycin after a pertussis exposure. He weighs less than he did at birth. His mucous membranes are dry. A firm, mobile, olive-sized mass is felt in the epigastrium. Ultrasonography shows pyloric muscle thickness of 5 mm (normal under 3 mm). Diagnosis: infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C47-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his vomiting began at 3 weeks of age rather than at birth?","options":["Gradual twisting of the midgut around the mesenteric root","Delayed failure of recanalization of the pyloric lumen","Progressive scarring after an ulcer in the pyloric channel","Postnatal hypertrophy of the pyloric circular muscle"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H4","obj":"GI-13-H.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing: normal feeding, then onset at 2 to 6 weeks.","Recall that the pylorus is open at birth and the muscle thickens over the first weeks.","Choose the option that develops after birth and makes a palpable mass."],"why":"In infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, the circular smooth muscle of the pylorus thickens after birth. The channel gradually narrows until milk cannot leave the stomach, which usually happens between 2 and 6 weeks of age. The thick muscle is the olive felt in the epigastrium, and visible peristaltic waves may cross the upper abdomen. Risk is higher in first-born boys and after macrolide exposure, especially erythromycin given in the first 2 weeks of life.","whyNot":["Midgut volvulus with malrotation causes sudden bilious vomiting because the twist is below the papilla. It does not produce an olive-shaped mass.","Failed recanalization causes atresia, which blocks the gut completely and causes vomiting from the first feeds.","Peptic scarring of the pylorus is a cause of gastric outlet obstruction in adults with chronic ulcer disease. It does not occur at this age.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pyloric stenosis","Neonatal causes of vomiting"],"comps":["GI-13-H4","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C47-Q2","stem":"Which of the following sets of serum findings is most likely in this infant?","options":["Chloride high, potassium low, bicarbonate low","Chloride normal, potassium high, bicarbonate high","Chloride low, potassium high, bicarbonate low","Chloride low, potassium low, bicarbonate high"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.7","dok":3,"approach":["Note that the vomitus is pure gastric juice: HCl.","Losing H+ leaves bicarbonate behind, and losing Cl- lowers serum chloride.","Volume loss raises aldosterone, and bicarbonate in the urine carries K+ out, so potassium falls."],"why":"Each parietal cell that secretes H+ into the lumen returns an equal amount of bicarbonate to the blood. Normally that bicarbonate is neutralized when acid enters the duodenum, but vomiting removes the acid so bicarbonate stays in the blood, causing metabolic alkalosis. Chloride is lost with the acid. Volume depletion activates aldosterone, which saves sodium at the cost of potassium and H+, so the kidney wastes potassium and keeps the alkalosis going. The result is hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, corrected with saline and potassium before surgery.","whyNot":["High chloride, low potassium, and low bicarbonate is a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, the pattern of diarrhea, which loses intestinal bicarbonate.","Vomiting gastric juice always lowers chloride, and potassium falls through renal losses. Neither stays normal or rises.","High potassium with metabolic acidosis suggests renal failure or adrenal insufficiency, such as congenital adrenal hyperplasia with salt wasting.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acid-base effects of vomiting","Contraction alkalosis"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-H4"]}},{"id":"GI-C47-Q3","stem":"His vomitus contains no bile because bile enters the gut distal to the obstruction. In which part of the duodenum does bile normally enter?","options":["Fourth (ascending) part","Second (descending) part","First (superior) part","Third (horizontal, inferior) part"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Connect nonbilious vomiting to an obstruction proximal to the bile entry point.","Recall where the common bile duct and pancreatic duct open.","The major duodenal papilla is on the posteromedial wall of the second part."],"why":"The duodenum has four parts: superior, descending, horizontal, and ascending. The common bile duct and main pancreatic duct join at the hepatopancreatic ampulla and open at the major duodenal papilla in the second, descending part. Any block above this point, such as a thick pylorus, leaves vomitus free of bile. A block below it, such as midgut volvulus or most duodenal atresias, causes bilious vomiting. This is a key bedside sign for sorting neonatal obstruction.","whyNot":["The fourth part rises to the duodenojejunal flexure at the ligament of Treitz. It also lies beyond the papilla.","Correct.","The first part, including the duodenal bulb, lies before the papilla. Bile does not enter here.","The third part crosses in front of the aorta and behind the superior mesenteric vessels. It lies beyond the papilla, and obstruction here causes bilious vomiting."],"study":{"topics":["Parts of the duodenum","Bilious versus nonbilious vomiting"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C47-Q4","stem":"The thickened muscle in this condition has reduced neuronal nitric oxide synthase in the enteric nerve plexus that controls this muscle. Which of the following identifies this plexus and its location?","options":["Myenteric plexus within the muscularis externa","Submucosal plexus within the submucosa","Submucosal plexus within the muscularis externa","Myenteric plexus within the submucosa"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the thickened layer: circular smooth muscle, part of the muscularis externa.","Recall which plexus sits between the circular and longitudinal muscle.","That is the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus, which controls motility."],"why":"The gut wall has four layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia. The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa and regulates secretion and blood flow. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa and controls motility. Its nitrergic neurons normally relax the pylorus, and reduced nitric oxide signaling there has been described in pyloric stenosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa and controls secretion and local blood flow. The thickened layer here is the muscle coat.","The submucosal plexus is not located in the muscularis externa.","The myenteric plexus does not sit in the submucosa. It lies between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the gut wall","Enteric nervous system"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-6"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C48","system":"GI","dx":"Biliary atresia","codes":["GI-13-H"],"case":"A 6-week-old girl is brought to the physician because of yellow discoloration of the skin and sclerae since 2 weeks of age and pale gray stools. She was born at term and is breastfed. She is gaining weight. The liver edge is firm and palpable 4 cm below the right costal margin. Serum total bilirubin is 8.4 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.0), with direct bilirubin of 6.9 mg/dL (under 0.3). A hepatobiliary scan shows uptake of tracer by the liver but no excretion into the intestine after 24 hours. An intraoperative cholangiogram shows obliterated extrahepatic bile ducts. Diagnosis: biliary atresia.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C48-Q1","stem":"Without surgical drainage of bile, which of the following is the most likely outcome for this infant?","options":["Resolution of jaundice once breastfeeding is stopped","Biliary cirrhosis within the first years of life","Spontaneous reopening of the bile ducts by age 1 year","Kernicterus from bilirubin deposition in basal ganglia"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H","obj":"GI-13-H.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize biliary atresia as progressive inflammatory obliteration of the extrahepatic ducts.","Bile backs up into the liver, injuring ducts and hepatocytes.","Ongoing injury causes fibrosis and biliary cirrhosis."],"why":"Biliary atresia is a progressive, inflammatory destruction of the extrahepatic bile ducts in the first weeks of life. Bile cannot leave the liver, so conjugated bilirubin rises, stools lose their color, and bile acids injure the liver. Portal fibrosis and bile duct proliferation progress to biliary cirrhosis and portal hypertension, often within the first 1 to 2 years without treatment. A Kasai procedure works best when done early, and biliary atresia is the most common reason for liver transplantation in children.","whyNot":["Breast milk jaundice is an unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia in a well infant with normal stools. It does not cause conjugated jaundice with acholic stools.","Correct.","The obliteration in biliary atresia is progressive and does not reverse. Without a hepatoportoenterostomy (Kasai procedure) or transplant, the damage continues.","Kernicterus is caused by unconjugated bilirubin, which is lipid soluble and crosses the blood-brain barrier. Her bilirubin is mostly conjugated."],"study":{"topics":["Biliary atresia","Neonatal conjugated hyperbilirubinemia"],"comps":["GI-13-H","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C48-Q2","stem":"The obliterated ducts develop from which of the following embryonic structures?","options":["Dorsal pancreatic bud of the foregut endoderm","Septum transversum splanchnic mesoderm","Hepatic diverticulum of foregut endoderm","Vitelline duct of the midgut endoderm"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the liver and biliary tree bud from the foregut in week 4.","The hepatic diverticulum gives liver cords and, through its stalk, the bile ducts.","Its cystic part forms the gallbladder and cystic duct."],"why":"In week 4, the hepatic diverticulum grows from the endoderm of the distal foregut into the septum transversum. Its cranial part forms hepatocytes and intrahepatic ducts, while its caudal cystic part forms the gallbladder and cystic duct. The stalk connecting it to the duodenum becomes the common bile duct. The ventral pancreatic bud also arises near this stalk and rotates behind the duodenum, which explains annular pancreas when it migrates abnormally.","whyNot":["The dorsal pancreatic bud forms most of the pancreas, including the accessory duct. The bile ducts arise from the hepatic diverticulum.","The septum transversum gives rise to the connective tissue of the liver and the central tendon of the diaphragm. It does not form duct epithelium.","Correct.","The vitelline duct connects the midgut to the yolk sac and normally disappears. A remnant forms a Meckel diverticulum."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatobiliary embryology","Pancreatic development"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-H"]}},{"id":"GI-C48-Q3","stem":"Which of the following urine findings is most likely in this infant?","options":["Bilirubin present, urobilinogen absent","Bilirubin absent, urobilinogen increased","Bilirubin present, urobilinogen increased","Bilirubin absent, urobilinogen absent"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note the conjugated hyperbilirubinemia: conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and passes into urine.","Note the acholic stools: no bile reaches the gut.","Without bilirubin in the gut, bacteria make no urobilinogen to reabsorb and excrete."],"why":"Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and loosely bound to albumin, so when it builds up in blood it is filtered into the urine, making it dark. Urobilinogen is made by gut bacteria from bilirubin that reaches the intestine, and some of it is reabsorbed and excreted in urine. With complete obstruction, no bilirubin reaches the gut, so urobilinogen disappears from urine and the stools turn pale. Hemolysis shows the opposite pattern: unconjugated bilirubin that cannot enter urine, and high urine urobilinogen.","whyNot":["Correct.","Absent urine bilirubin with high urobilinogen is the pattern of hemolysis. The excess bilirubin is unconjugated, which cannot pass into urine.","Bilirubin and urobilinogen can both be raised in some hepatocellular injury, but urobilinogen needs bile to reach the gut. Her ducts are blocked.","Her conjugated bilirubin is high and water soluble, so it spills into the urine. Urine bilirubin cannot be absent."],"study":{"topics":["Bilirubin metabolism","Types of jaundice"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"GI-C48-Q4","stem":"Surgery is delayed. At 9 weeks of age, she bleeds for a long time from a heel-stick site. Prothrombin time is 24 seconds (11 to 14) and corrects within 24 hours after a parenteral dose of vitamin K. Which of the following best explains the prolonged prothrombin time?","options":["Consumption of clotting factors in widespread microthrombi","An inhibitory antibody against coagulation factor VIII","Poor vitamin K absorption without bile acid micelles","Reduced hepatic synthesis of clotting factor proteins"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note the fix: parenteral vitamin K corrects the prothrombin time.","Recall that vitamin K is fat soluble and needs bile acid micelles to be absorbed.","Without bile in the gut, vitamin K deficiency develops, and factors II, VII, IX, and X are not carboxylated."],"why":"Vitamins A, D, E, and K are fat soluble and are absorbed within bile acid micelles. With no bile reaching the gut, they are poorly absorbed. Vitamin K is needed for gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S, so deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time and causes bleeding. Parenteral vitamin K bypasses the gut, which is why the clotting test corrects. Over time she is also at risk for night blindness, rickets, and vitamin E neuropathy and hemolysis.","whyNot":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation uses up factors and platelets, usually in a severely ill patient. Vitamin K would not correct it.","A factor VIII inhibitor prolongs the partial thromboplastin time and leaves the prothrombin time normal. Vitamin K does not affect it.","Correct.","Hepatocyte failure lowers clotting factor production, but that does not correct with vitamin K. The quick correction shows the liver can still make the factors."],"study":{"topics":["Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","Vitamin K and clotting factors"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C49","system":"GI","dx":"Omphalocele with cleft lip and palate in trisomy 13","codes":["GI-13-H"],"case":"A newborn boy is examined shortly after birth at 38 weeks' gestation. Prenatal ultrasonography showed an abdominal wall defect and a single fused forebrain consistent with holoprosencephaly. He has a midline abdominal wall defect about 5 cm wide at the base of the umbilical cord, through which loops of small bowel and part of the liver protrude. He also has a bilateral cleft lip with a cleft palate, small eyes, and an extra digit on each hand. Karyotype is 47,XY,+13. Diagnosis: omphalocele with cleft lip and palate in a newborn with trisomy 13.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C49-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes his abdominal wall defect, as compared with gastroschisis?","options":["Viscera covered by intact skin protruding through a weak umbilical ring","Uncovered viscera passing through a full-thickness defect right of the cord","Exposed bladder mucosa through a defect in the lower abdominal wall","Viscera in a sac of peritoneum and amnion, with the cord on the sac"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-13-H","obj":"GI-13-H.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the defect is midline at the base of the cord and contains liver.","Recall that an omphalocele keeps a membrane covering, while gastroschisis does not.","Link the trisomy to omphalocele, which is often associated with chromosomal anomalies."],"why":"An omphalocele is a midline defect at the umbilical ring through which viscera protrude inside a sac of peritoneum and amnion, with the umbilical cord inserting on the sac. It is often linked to trisomy 13 or 18, Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, and heart defects. Gastroschisis is a defect beside the cord, usually on the right, with uncovered bowel that floats in amniotic fluid and becomes thick and matted. Gastroschisis is rarely associated with other anomalies, but it carries a risk of intestinal atresia.","whyNot":["A skin-covered bulge at the umbilicus is an umbilical hernia, common in infants and usually closing by itself.","Uncovered bowel beside a normally inserted cord, usually to the right, is gastroschisis. It is seldom linked to trisomies.","Exposed bladder mucosa in the lower abdominal wall is bladder exstrophy, a defect of the infraumbilical body wall and cloacal membrane.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Omphalocele versus gastroschisis","Abdominal wall defects"],"comps":["GI-13-H","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C49-Q2","stem":"He has trouble feeding, and milk comes out of his nose when he tries to suck. Failure of fusion of which of the following structures most directly explains this problem?","options":["Two medial nasal prominences","Lateral palatine shelves","Maxillary and mandibular prominences","Maxillary and medial nasal prominences"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-H","obj":"GI-13-H.7","dok":3,"approach":["Note the symptom: milk passes from mouth to nose, so the oral and nasal cavities connect.","The roof of the mouth is the secondary palate.","The secondary palate forms from fusion of the two lateral palatine shelves."],"why":"The upper lip forms when each maxillary prominence fuses with the merged medial nasal prominences; failure gives cleft lip. The secondary palate forms when the lateral palatine shelves grow up above the tongue and fuse with each other and the nasal septum; failure gives cleft palate. With a cleft palate, the infant cannot seal the oral cavity to make suction, and milk passes into the nose. Cleft lip and palate are common in trisomy 13, often with holoprosencephaly.","whyNot":["The two medial nasal prominences fuse to form the intermaxillary segment, including the philtrum and primary palate. A failure here causes a median cleft lip, which is rare and does not explain nasal regurgitation.","Correct.","Failure of the maxillary and mandibular prominences to merge widens the mouth (macrostomia). It does not create an opening into the nose.","Failure of the maxillary and medial nasal prominences to fuse produces cleft lip. A cleft lip alone does not open the mouth into the nasal cavity behind the gums."],"study":{"topics":["Cleft lip versus cleft palate","Palate development"],"comps":["GI-13-H","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C49-Q3","stem":"Failure of which of the following normal developmental events most directly explains the bowel loops within the defect?","options":["Return of the herniated midgut loop","Recanalization of the intestinal lumen","Obliteration of the vitelline duct","Rotation of the midgut around the superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the midgut normally herniates into the umbilical cord in week 6.","It rotates and normally returns to the abdomen by week 10.","If it fails to return, the bowel stays in a sac at the umbilicus: an omphalocele."],"why":"In week 6, the midgut grows faster than the abdominal cavity and herniates into the umbilical cord. While outside, it rotates 90 degrees counterclockwise around the superior mesenteric artery, and it completes 270 degrees as it returns to the abdomen around week 10. If the bowel does not return, it remains outside within the cord covering, forming an omphalocele. A large omphalocele that contains liver also reflects failure of the lateral body folds to close the abdominal wall. Malrotation often coexists.","whyNot":["Correct.","Failed recanalization causes atresia or stenosis of the gut lumen. It does not move bowel outside the abdomen.","A persistent vitelline duct remnant causes a Meckel diverticulum or an umbilical fistula draining ileal contents. It does not leave bowel loops outside the abdominal wall.","Failed or incomplete rotation is malrotation, which leaves the bowel inside the abdomen in an abnormal position with Ladd bands and a risk of volvulus."],"study":{"topics":["Physiologic midgut herniation","Midgut rotation and malrotation"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-13-H"]}},{"id":"GI-C49-Q4","stem":"After repair of his defects, a swallow study is performed. As he swallows, the lower esophageal sphincter relaxes before the bolus arrives. Which of the following mediates this relaxation?","options":["Vagal neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP","Vagal neurons releasing acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors","Sympathetic neurons releasing norepinephrine","Circulating gastrin from antral G cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note that the sphincter opens before the bolus arrives, so the signal must be neural.","Recall that the vagus carries both excitatory and inhibitory fibers to the sphincter.","Inhibitory fibers act through myenteric neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP."],"why":"The lower esophageal sphincter is tonically contracted at rest to block reflux. When a swallow begins, the medullary swallowing center sends signals down the vagus to inhibitory myenteric neurons. These release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide, which relax the sphincter and the proximal stomach before the bolus arrives. In achalasia, these inhibitory neurons are lost, so the sphincter fails to relax.","whyNot":["Correct.","Acetylcholine acting on muscarinic receptors contracts the sphincter muscle and maintains its tone between swallows.","Sympathetic input has only a minor role in the lower esophageal sphincter and does not produce swallow-induced relaxation.","Gastrin raises lower esophageal sphincter tone if anything. It does not relax the sphincter during a swallow."],"study":{"topics":["Lower esophageal sphincter control","Receptive relaxation"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C50","system":"GI","dx":"Acute mesenteric ischemia from an atrial fibrillation embolus","codes":["GI-13-J3"],"case":"A 76-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 4 hours after the sudden onset of severe abdominal pain around the umbilicus, followed by one episode of vomiting and a loose stool. She has atrial fibrillation and stopped taking her anticoagulant 1 month ago. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 118/min and irregularly irregular, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 142/84 mm Hg. She is writhing in pain, but the abdomen is soft and only mildly tender without guarding. Serum lactate is 5.6 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2). CT angiography shows an embolus in the superior mesenteric artery distal to its origin. Diagnosis: acute mesenteric ischemia from an embolus.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C50-Q1","stem":"Which of the following histories, if present instead, would most suggest chronic mesenteric ischemia?","options":["Crampy lower abdominal pain relieved by defecation","Painful bloody diarrhea after an episode of shock","Sudden severe pain beginning shortly after cardioversion","Pain after meals, fear of eating, and weight loss"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J3","obj":"GI-13-J.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that chronic mesenteric ischemia comes from atherosclerosis of several mesenteric arteries.","Flow is enough at rest but not after meals, when gut demand rises.","Look for pain after eating, food fear, and weight loss: intestinal angina."],"why":"Chronic mesenteric ischemia is usually caused by atherosclerotic narrowing of two or more of the celiac trunk, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric arteries. After a meal, blood flow to the gut must rise, and when it cannot, the patient has pain 15 to 60 minutes after eating. Patients learn to avoid food and lose weight. Acute mesenteric ischemia, as in this patient, is most often an embolus from atrial fibrillation to the superior mesenteric artery, with severe pain out of proportion to the exam and a rising lactate.","whyNot":["Crampy pain relieved by defecation, with no alarm features, suggests irritable bowel syndrome, a functional disorder without ischemia.","Painful bloody diarrhea after low blood pressure suggests ischemic colitis at a watershed area of the colon, such as the splenic flexure.","Sudden pain soon after cardioversion suggests an embolus dislodged from the left atrium, which is acute mesenteric ischemia like hers.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acute versus chronic mesenteric ischemia","Ischemic colitis"],"comps":["GI-13-J3","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C50-Q2","stem":"At laparotomy, the segments supplied by the occluded artery are dusky. Which of the following segments is most likely to remain well perfused?","options":["Cecum and appendix","Terminal ileum","Descending colon","Ascending colon"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the occluded vessel: the superior mesenteric artery.","Recall its territory: distal duodenum to the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon.","Choose the segment in the inferior mesenteric territory."],"why":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, from the distal duodenum through the jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and about the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut, from the distal transverse colon to the upper rectum, including the descending colon. Emboli often lodge beyond the first jejunal or middle colic branches, so the proximal jejunum may be spared. The marginal artery links the two territories, but its weakest points at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction are prone to ischemic colitis after hypotension.","whyNot":["The cecum and appendix are supplied by the ileocolic artery, a branch of the superior mesenteric artery.","The terminal ileum is supplied by the ileocolic and ileal branches of the superior mesenteric artery. It is at high risk.","Correct.","The ascending colon is supplied by the right colic and ileocolic branches of the superior mesenteric artery."],"study":{"topics":["Mesenteric arterial territories","Watershed areas of the colon"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-1"]}},{"id":"GI-C50-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her pain was first felt around the umbilicus?","options":["Foregut visceral afferents enter the cord at T10, the segment of umbilical skin","Midgut visceral afferents enter the cord at T10, the segment of umbilical skin","Hindgut visceral afferents enter the cord at T10, the segment of umbilical skin","Intercostal nerves in the parietal peritoneum near the umbilicus are irritated"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the ischemic bowel as a midgut derivative.","Recall that visceral pain is poorly localized and referred by embryologic origin.","Midgut pain is referred to the periumbilical region, the T10 dermatome."],"why":"Visceral pain fibers from the gut travel with sympathetic nerves and enter the spinal cord at the segments that supplied the gut tube in the embryo. The brain cannot localize this input well and refers it to the skin served by the same segments. Foregut pain is felt in the epigastrium, midgut pain around the umbilicus at about T10, and hindgut pain in the suprapubic region. Pain becomes sharp and localized only when the parietal peritoneum is inflamed, which is why early mesenteric ischemia causes severe pain with a soft abdomen.","whyNot":["Foregut visceral pain, as from the stomach or duodenum, enters higher thoracic segments and is referred to the epigastrium.","Correct.","Hindgut visceral pain, as from the sigmoid colon, enters lower segments and is referred to the suprapubic region.","Irritation of the parietal peritoneum gives sharp, well-localized pain over the diseased organ with guarding. Her soft abdomen shows the peritoneum is not yet involved."],"study":{"topics":["Referred visceral pain","Foregut, midgut, and hindgut"],"comps":["GI-1","GI-5"]}},{"id":"GI-C50-Q4","stem":"She undergoes embolectomy and resection of a short ileal segment and has no caloric intake for the next 3 days. On the third day, which of the following is the main source of her blood glucose?","options":["Hepatic conversion of circulating ketone bodies into new glucose","Hepatic glycogenolysis releasing glucose from stored glycogen","Muscle glycogenolysis releasing free glucose into the blood","Hepatic gluconeogenesis from amino acids, lactate, and glycerol"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing: 3 days without food.","Liver glycogen lasts about a day, so after that the liver must make new glucose.","Gluconeogenesis uses alanine and other amino acids from muscle, lactate, and glycerol from fat."],"why":"In the first hours of fasting, the liver keeps blood glucose up by breaking down its glycogen. Those stores are largely gone after about 24 to 48 hours. The liver then relies on gluconeogenesis, using alanine and other amino acids from muscle protein, lactate from red cells and muscle (the Cori cycle), and glycerol released from fat. At the same time, fatty acid oxidation feeds hepatic ketogenesis. The liver exports ketones to the brain and muscle but cannot use them, because it lacks the enzyme succinyl-CoA:acetoacetate CoA transferase (thiophorase).","whyNot":["Ketone bodies come from acetyl-CoA and cannot be converted into glucose. The brain uses them in place of glucose as fasting continues.","Liver glycogen is the main source in the first 12 to 24 hours of fasting, but it is largely used up within about 1 to 2 days.","Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so its glycogen supplies glucose only to the muscle itself. It cannot release free glucose into the blood.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Fasting metabolism","Gluconeogenesis substrates"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C51","system":"GI","dx":"Necrotizing enterocolitis in a premature infant","codes":["GI-13-J3"],"case":"A 12-day-old boy born at 29 weeks' gestation is evaluated in the neonatal intensive care unit because of feeding intolerance, abdominal distension, and bloody stools. He has been fed a cow's milk-based preterm formula since day 4 of life. Temperature is 36.0 °C (96.8 °F), pulse is 182/min, respirations are 64/min, and blood pressure is 50/30 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a distended, tender abdomen with erythema of the abdominal wall and absent bowel sounds. Gastric aspirates are bilious. Laboratory studies show a platelet count of 82,000/mm3 (150,000 to 450,000) and an arterial pH of 7.24 (7.35 to 7.45). Diagnosis: necrotizing enterocolitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C51-Q1","stem":"An abdominal radiograph is obtained. Which of the following radiographic findings is most likely?","options":["Linear and bubbly gas within the bowel wall","Gas confined to a dilated stomach and duodenal bulb","Scattered calcifications along the peritoneal surfaces","Dilated loops with a bubbly mass in the right lower quadrant"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-J3","obj":"GI-13-J.5","dok":2,"approach":["Name the core process: ischemic injury of immature mucosa followed by bacterial invasion.","Ask what invading bacteria produce inside damaged bowel: gas from fermentation.","Pick the finding that shows gas inside the bowel wall, called pneumatosis intestinalis."],"why":"Necrotizing enterocolitis starts with mucosal injury in the immature bowel of a premature infant, aided by reduced blood flow, an immature barrier, and formula feeding. Bacteria cross the damaged mucosa and ferment luminal substrate, producing hydrogen gas that tracks into the submucosa and subserosa. On radiograph this gas appears as linear or bubbly lucencies in the bowel wall, called pneumatosis intestinalis, the hallmark finding. Gas can also enter the portal venous system, and free air means the bowel has perforated.","whyNot":["Correct.","This double bubble sign points to duodenal atresia, which presents within the first day of life with bilious vomiting and is linked to Down syndrome.","Peritoneal calcifications suggest meconium peritonitis from a bowel perforation that happened before birth.","A bubbly right lower quadrant mass of thick meconium with dilated proximal loops suggests meconium ileus, an early sign of cystic fibrosis seen in the first days of life."],"study":{"topics":["Necrotizing enterocolitis","Pneumatosis intestinalis"],"comps":["GI-13-J3","GI-13-J"]}},{"id":"GI-C51-Q2","stem":"His mother was unable to provide breast milk. Human milk lowers the risk of this disease, partly because it contains secretory IgA directed against microbes from the mother's own intestine. In which of the following sites were the B cells that produce this milk IgA most likely first activated?","options":["Germinal centers in the mother's splenic white pulp","Axillary lymph nodes that drain the mother's breast","Mesenteric lymph nodes of the infant","Peyer patches in the mother's ileum"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["Note that the milk IgA targets the mother's gut microbes, so the B cells met those antigens in her gut.","Recall how gut antigens are sampled: M cells over Peyer patches pass them to underlying lymphoid follicles.","Link this to homing of IgA-committed B cells from the gut to other mucosal sites, including the lactating breast."],"why":"M cells in the epithelium over Peyer patches take up luminal antigens and pass them to dendritic cells and B cells in the follicles below. Activated B cells switch to IgA and gain homing receptors that send them to mucosal tissues, including the lactating mammary gland, which is often called the enteromammary link. Plasma cells in the breast then secrete dimeric IgA that the polymeric Ig receptor carries into milk. The infant receives antibody aimed at exactly the microbes he is likely to meet from his mother, which helps protect the immature gut.","whyNot":["The spleen mounts systemic responses to blood-borne antigens, mainly IgM and IgG, and these B cells do not home to the breast.","Axillary nodes drain lymph from breast tissue, but the IgA plasma cells in milk-producing tissue are mostly gut-primed cells that homed there.","A premature infant makes very little IgA of his own, and milk antibodies come from the mother.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Gut mucosal immunity and M cells","Secretory IgA in breast milk"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-J3"]}},{"id":"GI-C51-Q3","stem":"He undergoes resection of a necrotic segment of ileum. Microscopy shows mucosal necrosis and gas-filled cysts in the layer between the muscularis mucosae and the muscularis externa. Which of the following neural structures normally lies within this layer?","options":["Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","Submucosal (Meissner) plexus","Prevertebral sympathetic ganglion cells","Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the layer: the tissue between the muscularis mucosae and the muscularis externa is the submucosa.","Recall which enteric plexus lies in the submucosa and which lies within the muscle coat.","Exclude neurons whose cell bodies lie outside the gut wall."],"why":"The gut wall has four layers: mucosa (with its thin muscularis mucosae), submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia. The submucosa holds large vessels, lymphatics, and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus, which mainly controls secretion and local blood flow. In necrotizing enterocolitis, gas cysts often collect in the submucosa and subserosa. If necrosis extends through the muscularis externa and serosa, the bowel perforates.","whyNot":["The myenteric plexus lies between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa and mainly controls motility.","Correct.","Prevertebral ganglia, such as the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia, sit outside the gut wall near the aorta. Only their postganglionic fibers reach the bowel.","Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies lie in the brainstem (vagal nuclei) and sacral spinal cord (S2 to S4). Their fibers synapse on enteric neurons in the wall."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the gut wall","Enteric nervous system plexuses"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C51-Q4","stem":"Feeds are stopped for 10 days. In healthy people between meals, a cyclic wave of strong contractions sweeps undigested debris and bacteria from the stomach through the small intestine about every 90 minutes. Which of the following pairs this motor pattern with the hormone that triggers it?","options":["Mass movement, triggered by gastrin","Receptive relaxation, triggered by vasoactive intestinal peptide","Segmentation, triggered by cholecystokinin","Migrating motor complex, triggered by motilin"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing (fasting) and the purpose (clearing debris and bacteria from the stomach and small bowel).","Name the pattern: the migrating motor complex.","Recall the hormone that starts its strongest phase: motilin from M cells of the upper small intestine."],"why":"During fasting, the stomach and small intestine cycle through the migrating motor complex about every 90 to 120 minutes. Its phase III is a burst of strong peristaltic contractions that sweeps residue and bacteria toward the colon, acting as a housekeeper that limits bacterial overgrowth. Motilin, released from M cells of the duodenum and jejunum, rises in step with these bursts and triggers the gastric phase III. Eating interrupts the cycle and replaces it with the fed pattern.","whyNot":["Mass movements push colonic contents toward the rectum a few times a day, often after a meal as part of the gastrocolic reflex.","Receptive relaxation lets the proximal stomach accept a meal and is driven by vagal release of VIP and nitric oxide during swallowing.","Segmentation mixes chyme with digestive juices in the fed state. Cholecystokinin is released by fat and protein after a meal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Migrating motor complex","Motilin"],"comps":["GI-6"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C52","system":"GI","dx":"Gastric MALT lymphoma","codes":["GI-13-K2"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of epigastric discomfort and early satiety. She has lost 3 kg (6.6 lb). Physical examination shows mild epigastric tenderness and no lymphadenopathy. Hemoglobin is 11.4 g/dL (12 to 16). Upper endoscopy shows thickened, nodular mucosa in the gastric antrum with a shallow ulcer. Biopsy shows chronic active gastritis with curved bacilli in the surface mucus and a dense infiltrate of small, atypical lymphocytes that invade and destroy gastric glands (lymphoepithelial lesions). The lymphoma cells are CD20 positive. CT shows no disease beyond the stomach. Diagnosis: gastric mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma with Helicobacter pylori infection.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C52-Q1","stem":"Flow cytometry of the biopsy shows that the lymphoma cells are CD5 negative and CD10 negative. Which of the following best describes the cell of origin of this tumor?","options":["Interstitial cells of Cajal with a KIT mutation","Germinal center B cells with BCL2 overexpression","Marginal zone B cells of gastric lymphoid tissue","Mantle zone B cells with cyclin D1 overexpression"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-K2","obj":"GI-13-K.3","dok":2,"approach":["Use the markers: CD20 positive means a B cell tumor; CD5 negative and CD10 negative exclude mantle cell and follicular lymphoma.","Recall that chronic H. pylori gastritis creates lymphoid follicles in a stomach that normally has none.","Match the tumor to the B cells in the marginal zone around these acquired follicles."],"why":"The normal stomach has no organized lymphoid tissue. Chronic H. pylori infection induces lymphoid follicles, and MALT lymphoma arises from the marginal zone B cells around them, so it is an extranodal marginal zone B cell lymphoma. The cells are CD20 positive and typically CD5 and CD10 negative. Lymphoepithelial lesions, where tumor cells invade and destroy glands, are characteristic.","whyNot":["Gastrointestinal stromal tumor arises from interstitial cells of Cajal, is CD117 (KIT) positive, and is a spindle cell tumor without CD20.","Follicular lymphoma comes from germinal center B cells, is CD10 positive, and carries t(14;18), which overexpresses BCL2.","Correct.","Mantle cell lymphoma is CD5 positive and carries t(11;14), which overexpresses cyclin D1."],"study":{"topics":["MALT lymphoma","B cell lymphoma markers"],"comps":["GI-13-K2","GI-11"]}},{"id":"GI-C52-Q2","stem":"She receives a proton pump inhibitor, clarithromycin, and amoxicillin. Six months later, a urea breath test is negative and repeat biopsies show regression of the lymphoma. Which of the following best explains this response?","options":["Antibodies against the organism lyse tumor cells by complement","Growth depends on help from H. pylori-specific T cells","Clarithromycin is directly cytotoxic to the lymphoma cells","Lower gastric acid removes a growth signal from the B cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that early MALT lymphoma is still driven by the chronic antigen that started it.","Identify the source of the growth signal: CD4 T cells that recognize H. pylori antigens and help the B cells.","Predict that removing the antigen removes T cell help, so the tumor shrinks."],"why":"In early gastric MALT lymphoma, the tumor B cells still depend on stimulation from H. pylori-specific CD4 T cells, which supply CD40 ligand and cytokines. When the infection is cured, the antigen is gone, T cell help stops, and many tumors regress. Tumors that carry t(11;18), which joins API2 and MALT1, activate NF-kB on their own and usually do not respond to eradication alone.","whyNot":["Antibodies to H. pylori bind the bacteria. The tumor B cells do not display H. pylori antigens for these antibodies to target.","Correct.","Clarithromycin is given to kill H. pylori. It has no meaningful direct effect on lymphoma cells.","Acid suppression helps the antibiotics work and heals the ulcer, but acid does not drive B cell growth."],"study":{"topics":["H. pylori and MALT lymphoma","T cell help for B cells"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-K2"]}},{"id":"GI-C52-Q3","stem":"H. pylori infection also raises her risk of gastric adenocarcinoma. Which of the following sequences most likely precedes the H. pylori-associated type of this cancer?","options":["Foveolar hyperplasia with giant rugal folds and protein loss","Reflux esophagitis, then columnar metaplasia at the cardia","Atrophic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia","Germline CDH1 loss, then signet ring cells infiltrating singly"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K2","obj":"GI-13-K.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the two Lauren types of gastric adenocarcinoma: intestinal and diffuse.","Link chronic H. pylori gastritis to the intestinal type.","Trace the stepwise path of mucosal injury that leads to gland-forming cancer."],"why":"Intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma follows years of chronic injury. H. pylori gastritis, sometimes with dietary nitrosamines, causes atrophy of the glands, then intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells, then dysplasia, and finally an invasive carcinoma that forms glands. The diffuse type has a weaker link to environmental injury and grows as single signet ring cells that thicken the wall.","whyNot":["Giant rugal folds with protein loss and foveolar hyperplasia describe Menetrier disease, a hypertrophic gastropathy that is not the usual path to H. pylori-related cancer.","Reflux injury and columnar metaplasia lead to Barrett esophagus and adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus and gastroesophageal junction. H. pylori is not a risk factor for these.","Correct.","Loss of E-cadherin (CDH1) causes diffuse-type carcinoma, with signet ring cells and a rigid linitis plastica stomach. Germline CDH1 mutations cause hereditary diffuse gastric cancer."],"study":{"topics":["Intestinal vs diffuse gastric adenocarcinoma","Atrophic gastritis and intestinal metaplasia"],"comps":["GI-13-K2","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"GI-C52-Q4","stem":"Her gastritis is most severe in the antrum. Another patient has autoimmune gastritis that destroys the mucosa of the gastric body and fundus but spares the antrum. Which of the following secretion changes is most likely in that patient?","options":["Low serum gastrin, high acid, normal intrinsic factor","Low pepsinogen, normal acid, low serum gastrin","Low acid and intrinsic factor, high serum gastrin","Low serum gastrin and acid, normal intrinsic factor"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":3,"approach":["Map the cells: body and fundus glands contain parietal cells (HCl and intrinsic factor) and chief cells (pepsinogen); G cells live in the antrum.","Predict loss of acid and intrinsic factor when body and fundus mucosa is destroyed.","Apply feedback: with no acid, antral D cells release less somatostatin, so the spared G cells oversecrete gastrin."],"why":"Autoimmune gastritis targets parietal cells and intrinsic factor in the body and fundus. Losing parietal cells removes HCl and intrinsic factor, which leads to achlorhydria and, over time, vitamin B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia). Chief cells are also lost, so pepsinogen I falls. The antrum is spared, and without acid to trigger somatostatin, G cells release large amounts of gastrin. In her antral-predominant H. pylori gastritis, by contrast, acid output is often normal or high.","whyNot":["High acid with low gastrin does not fit loss of parietal cells. With no parietal cells, acid falls.","Pepsinogen does fall, but parietal cells are destroyed along with chief cells, so acid cannot stay normal, and gastrin rises.","Correct.","Acid falls, but gastrin rises because acid feedback is lost, and intrinsic factor falls with the parietal cells."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types","Autoimmune gastritis"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G7"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C53","system":"GI","dx":"Small intestinal carcinoid tumor with carcinoid syndrome","codes":["GI-13-K3"],"case":"A 59-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of episodes of facial flushing and watery diarrhea several times daily. He also has intermittent wheezing. Physical examination shows a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration, and a firm, nodular, enlarged liver. A 24-hour urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA) level is 96 mg (2 to 8). CT shows a 2-cm mass in the distal ileum, enlarged mesenteric lymph nodes, and multiple liver masses. Biopsy of a liver mass shows nests of uniform cells that stain for chromogranin A and synaptophysin. Diagnosis: metastatic well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor of the ileum with carcinoid syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C53-Q1","stem":"Echocardiography shows thickened, fixed tricuspid and pulmonic valve leaflets, while the mitral and aortic valves are normal. Which of the following best explains why the left-sided valves are spared?","options":["Pulmonary monoamine oxidase inactivates serotonin","Higher left-sided pressures wash away fibrous plaques","Hepatic metastases release serotonin into the bile","Left-sided endocardium lacks serotonin receptors"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K3","obj":"GI-13-K.5","dok":3,"approach":["Trace tumor products from liver metastases: hepatic veins, inferior vena cava, right heart.","Ask what happens to serotonin as blood passes through the lungs.","Conclude that only right-sided valves see high serotonin levels."],"why":"Liver metastases release serotonin and other vasoactive products directly into the hepatic veins, so they reach the right heart without first-pass clearance. Serotonin stimulates fibroblasts to lay down plaque-like fibrous tissue on the tricuspid and pulmonic valves, causing regurgitation and stenosis. The lungs contain monoamine oxidase and clear much of the serotonin before blood reaches the left heart, so the mitral and aortic valves are usually spared. Left-sided lesions appear with a right-to-left shunt, such as a patent foramen ovale, or with a bronchial carcinoid.","whyNot":["Correct.","Valve plaques reflect exposure to tumor products. Pressure differences do not remove them.","Serotonin from liver metastases enters hepatic venous blood. Biliary release would not explain the valve pattern.","Both sides of the heart have similar tissue. The difference is how much serotonin each side receives."],"study":{"topics":["Carcinoid heart disease","Serotonin metabolism"],"comps":["GI-13-K3","CV-18-D6"]}},{"id":"GI-C53-Q2","stem":"Before the tumor spread to the liver, he had no flushing, although the ileal tumor was already secreting serotonin. Which of the following venous pathways carried this serotonin to the site where it was inactivated?","options":["Ileal veins, inferior mesenteric vein, left renal vein, inferior vena cava","Ileal veins, superior mesenteric vein, portal vein, sinusoids","Ileal veins, right gonadal vein, inferior vena cava","Ileal veins, splenic vein, hepatic veins, then portal vein"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify that ileal venous blood drains to the liver before reaching the systemic circulation.","Trace the vessels: superior mesenteric vein, joining the splenic vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein.","Recall that hepatocytes inactivate serotonin as portal blood passes through the sinusoids."],"why":"Veins of the ileum drain into the superior mesenteric vein. Behind the neck of the pancreas the superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein to form the hepatic portal vein, which enters the liver and branches into sinusoids. Hepatocytes break down serotonin with monoamine oxidase, so little reaches the central veins, hepatic veins, and inferior vena cava. Once tumor grows in the liver, its products enter the hepatic veins directly and bypass this first-pass clearance, which is why the syndrome usually appears after liver metastasis.","whyNot":["The inferior mesenteric vein drains the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum and usually joins the splenic vein. It does not drain to the left renal vein, and the ileum does not drain into it.","Correct.","The gonadal veins drain the testes or ovaries. The gut drains through the portal system.","The ileum does not drain to the splenic vein, and blood flows from the portal vein into the liver and out through the hepatic veins, in that order."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic portal system","First-pass hepatic clearance"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-K3"]}},{"id":"GI-C53-Q3","stem":"He also develops a scaly, hyperpigmented rash on the sun-exposed skin of his neck and forearms, a red, swollen tongue, and difficulty concentrating. Deficiency of which of the following cofactors best explains these new findings?","options":["NAD+ and NADP+","Ascorbate","Thiamine pyrophosphate","Biotin"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize pellagra: photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, glossitis, and mental changes.","Recall that tryptophan is a precursor of both serotonin and niacin.","Link niacin to its cofactor forms, NAD+ and NADP+."],"why":"Carcinoid tumors can divert a large share of dietary tryptophan into serotonin. Less tryptophan is left to make niacin, so niacin and its cofactors NAD+ and NADP+ fall. Many dehydrogenases in energy metabolism need these electron carriers, and tissues with high turnover, such as skin, gut, and brain, show the deficiency first. The result is pellagra: dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, diarrhea, glossitis, and confusion or dementia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Ascorbate deficiency causes scurvy, with bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhages, and poor wound healing from impaired collagen hydroxylation.","Thiamine pyrophosphate deficiency causes Wernicke encephalopathy and beriberi (neuropathy or heart failure), without a photosensitive rash.","Biotin deficiency, seen with heavy raw egg white intake, causes a scaly rash around the face, hair loss, and lethargy, without a sun-exposed pattern."],"study":{"topics":["Pellagra and niacin","Tryptophan metabolism"],"comps":["GI-8"]}},{"id":"GI-C53-Q4","stem":"He is kept fasting for 48 hours, and the large-volume watery diarrhea continues. Stool sodium is 95 mEq/L, stool potassium is 45 mEq/L, and measured stool osmolality is 290 mOsm/kg. Which of the following best classifies his diarrhea?","options":["Osmotic, with a stool osmotic gap of about 10 mOsm/kg","Osmotic, with a stool osmotic gap of about 150 mOsm/kg","Secretory, with a stool osmotic gap of about 150 mOsm/kg","Secretory, with a stool osmotic gap of about 10 mOsm/kg"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate the gap: 290 minus 2 times (95 plus 45) equals 290 minus 280, or about 10 mOsm/kg.","Interpret it: a gap below about 50 means electrolytes account for the stool water, which fits secretory diarrhea.","Confirm with fasting: secretory diarrhea continues without food, while osmotic diarrhea stops."],"why":"The stool osmotic gap is 290 minus 2 times the sum of stool sodium and potassium. Here it is about 10 mOsm/kg. A small gap, below about 50, means the water in stool is held by secreted electrolytes, which is secretory diarrhea. A large gap, above about 100, means an unmeasured solute such as unabsorbed lactose is holding water, which is osmotic diarrhea. Serotonin and other tumor products drive intestinal secretion and speed motility, so his diarrhea persists during fasting.","whyNot":["The gap is small, which points away from an unabsorbed solute. Osmotic diarrhea also stops with fasting.","A gap of 150 with diarrhea that stops on fasting would fit osmotic diarrhea, such as lactose intolerance. His numbers do not give this gap, and his diarrhea continued.","His values give a gap of about 10, and a gap of 150 would mean an unmeasured solute was present.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Stool osmotic gap","Secretory vs osmotic diarrhea"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-13-K3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C54","system":"GI","dx":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 with a pancreatic gastrinoma","codes":["GI-13-K3"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 5-month history of burning epigastric pain, watery diarrhea, and loose, greasy stools. She has had two kidney stones, and her brother had a parathyroid adenoma removed. Upper endoscopy shows prominent, thickened gastric folds and multiple ulcers, including ulcers in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum. Laboratory studies show a serum calcium of 11.4 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5) and a fasting serum gastrin of 1,250 pg/mL (less than 100), with a gastric pH of 1.5. CT shows a 2-cm mass in the head of the pancreas. Genetic testing shows a germline MEN1 mutation. Diagnosis: multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 with a pancreatic gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome).","questions":[{"id":"GI-C54-Q1","stem":"Before the CT, a secretin stimulation test was done to confirm the cause of her hypergastrinemia. Which of the following results is most likely?","options":["Serum gastrin falls by half","Serum gastrin falls below 100 pg/mL","Serum gastrin rises sharply","Serum gastrin does not change"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K3","obj":"GI-13-K.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that secretin normally inhibits gastrin release from antral G cells.","Recall that gastrinoma cells respond to secretin in the opposite way.","Predict a paradoxical rise in gastrin, which confirms a gastrinoma."],"why":"In healthy people, secretin suppresses gastrin release from G cells. Gastrinoma cells express secretin receptors that stimulate gastrin release, so intravenous secretin causes a sharp, paradoxical rise in serum gastrin, commonly defined as an increase of 120 pg/mL or more. Her low gastric pH with very high gastrin already argues against causes of high gastrin with low acid, such as atrophic gastritis or proton pump inhibitor use, and the secretin test confirms an autonomous tumor.","whyNot":["Secretin does not raise gastrin release from normal G cells, so a fall or no change is expected when high gastrin comes from normal G cells, as in atrophic gastritis or proton pump inhibitor use. A gastrinoma responds with a rise.","Normalization of gastrin would mean the gastrin source is suppressible normal tissue, which does not fit a tumor.","Correct.","An unchanged level would not support a gastrinoma. Tumor cells respond to secretin with a rise."],"study":{"topics":["Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","Secretin stimulation test"],"comps":["GI-13-K3","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C54-Q2","stem":"In a healthy person, a meal that delivers acid and fat into the duodenum triggers hormones that slow gastric emptying, neutralize the acid, and contract the gallbladder. Which of the following hormone and source cell pairs are released in response?","options":["Motilin from M cells and GIP from K cells","Gastrin from G cells and motilin from M cells","Secretin from I cells and CCK from S cells","Secretin from S cells and CCK from I cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Acid in the duodenum is the main stimulus for secretin, from S cells.","Fat and protein in the duodenum release cholecystokinin (CCK), from I cells.","Check the actions: secretin brings bicarbonate; CCK contracts the gallbladder, releases pancreatic enzymes, and slows gastric emptying."],"why":"Acid entering the duodenum releases secretin from S cells, which drives pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion to neutralize the acid. Fatty acids and amino acids release cholecystokinin from I cells, which contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion, and slows gastric emptying. Fat and glucose also release GIP from K cells, which boosts insulin release.","whyNot":["GIP does rise after fat, but motilin is a fasting hormone that drives the migrating motor complex and falls after a meal.","Gastrin rises during the gastric phase in response to peptides and vagal input, and acid in the gut suppresses it. Motilin acts in the fasting state.","These hormones are correct, but their source cells are reversed. S cells make secretin and I cells make CCK.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Gastrointestinal hormones","Duodenal phase of digestion"],"comps":["GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C54-Q3","stem":"The thickened gastric folds seen at endoscopy reflect the trophic effect of gastrin on the mucosa of the gastric body. Which of the following best describes the hyperplastic cell type responsible for her high acid output?","options":["Chief cells, which also secrete gastric lipase","Parietal cells, which also secrete intrinsic factor","Surface mucous cells, which also secrete bicarbonate","Antral G cells, which also secrete somatostatin"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the acid-secreting cell of the gastric body glands.","Recall the other product of that same cell.","Link gastrin's growth effect to an expanded parietal cell mass and thick rugal folds."],"why":"Gastrin acts on parietal cells directly and through histamine from enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells. Over time it also acts as a growth factor, expanding the parietal cell and ECL cell mass and thickening the folds of the body and fundus. Parietal cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor, which binds vitamin B12 for absorption in the ileum. A larger parietal cell mass explains her very high acid output and her low gastric pH.","whyNot":["Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase. They do not secrete acid.","Correct.","Surface mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate that protect the mucosa. They do not secrete acid.","Antral G cells secrete gastrin, while somatostatin comes from D cells. Her gastrin comes from the tumor, and G cells do not secrete acid."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types","Trophic effects of gastrin"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C54-Q4","stem":"Her stools contain excess fat even though her pancreas secretes normal amounts of digestive enzymes. Which of the following best explains her steatorrhea?","options":["Low duodenal pH inactivates pancreatic lipase","Gastrin directly inhibits pancreatic lipase release","Bile salt synthesis by hepatocytes is suppressed","Excess CCK release depletes bile in the gallbladder"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that pancreatic lipase, with colipase and bile salts, digests triglycerides in the duodenum at a near-neutral pH.","Note that a gastrinoma delivers far more acid than pancreatic bicarbonate can neutralize.","Predict that acid inactivates lipase and impairs micelle formation, so fat goes unabsorbed."],"why":"Fat digestion depends on pancreatic lipase and colipase acting at the surface of fat droplets that bile salts have emulsified. These steps work at a near-neutral pH, which pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate normally provide. In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome the acid load overwhelms this buffering, so lipase is inactivated and bile acids precipitate and cannot form micelles. Acid injury to the small bowel mucosa adds to the malabsorption, and the large fluid load adds to the diarrhea.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gastrin does not block lipase release. Her pancreas releases normal amounts of enzyme.","Bile salt synthesis is normal. The problem is that bile acids precipitate in an acid duodenum.","CCK is released normally by fat. Gallbladder contraction delivers bile, and bile is not depleted in this condition."],"study":{"topics":["Fat digestion and pancreatic lipase","Steatorrhea in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-K3"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C55","system":"GI","dx":"Oral leukoplakia in a smoker with chronic periodontitis","codes":["GI-13-K5","GI-13-L4"],"case":"A 61-year-old man comes to the dentist because of a painless white patch on his tongue that he noticed 3 months ago. He has smoked 1.5 packs of cigarettes daily for 40 years and drinks 4 to 5 beers daily. Examination shows a 1.5-cm white, well-demarcated plaque on the right lateral border of the anterior two thirds of the tongue that does not rub off with gauze. His gingiva is red and swollen and bleeds on probing, with deep periodontal pockets and several loose molars. Dental radiographs show loss of alveolar bone around the molars. Diagnosis: oral leukoplakia and chronic periodontitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C55-Q1","stem":"A biopsy of the tongue plaque is performed. Which of the following histologic findings would most strongly indicate a risk of progression to invasive squamous cell carcinoma?","options":["Budding yeast and pseudohyphae within keratin","Ballooned superficial cells with EBV inclusions","Thickened surface keratin with no atypia","Epithelial dysplasia with atypical mitoses"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K5","obj":"GI-13-K.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize leukoplakia as a clinical term for a white plaque that cannot be scraped off and has no other cause.","Recall that its cancer risk depends on what the biopsy shows.","Pick the finding that marks a precancerous change in the squamous epithelium."],"why":"Leukoplakia is a clinical description, and biopsy shows a range from simple hyperkeratosis to dysplasia to carcinoma. Dysplasia, with atypical nuclei, loss of normal maturation, and abnormal mitoses above the basal layer, is a precursor of invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Tobacco and alcohol act together as the main risks for oral squamous cell carcinoma, and lesions on the lateral tongue and floor of the mouth carry the highest risk.","whyNot":["Yeast and pseudohyphae mean candidiasis, whose white plaques scrape off. Candida can also colonize leukoplakia, but it is not the marker of cancer risk.","Ballooned cells with EBV point to oral hairy leukoplakia, a benign lesion of the lateral tongue in immunosuppressed patients such as those with HIV.","Hyperkeratosis without atypia is the most common biopsy result and carries a low risk of cancer.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Oral leukoplakia","Epithelial dysplasia"],"comps":["GI-13-K5","GI-13-K"]}},{"id":"GI-C55-Q2","stem":"Which of the following organisms and mechanisms best explains the destruction of tooth-supporting tissue in his gum disease?","options":["Candida albicans, by invading the epithelium with hyphae","Porphyromonas gingivalis, through inflammatory bone resorption","Streptococcus mutans, by fermenting sucrose to lactic acid","Actinomyces israelii, by forming sulfur granules in sinus tracts"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L4","obj":"GI-13-L.5","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two plaque diseases: caries destroys tooth mineral, and periodontitis destroys the ligament and bone that hold the tooth.","Recall the key anaerobe of subgingival plaque in periodontitis.","Link its products to host inflammation and osteoclast activation."],"why":"Plaque at the gum line first causes gingivitis, which is reversible with good oral hygiene. When the subgingival biofilm shifts toward gram-negative anaerobes such as Porphyromonas gingivalis, the inflammation deepens. Bacterial proteases (gingipains) and lipopolysaccharide drive a host response with cytokines and RANKL that activates osteoclasts. The periodontal ligament and alveolar bone are lost, pockets deepen, and teeth loosen.","whyNot":["Candida causes oral thrush with white plaques that scrape off. It is not the driver of periodontal bone loss.","Correct.","Streptococcus mutans makes acid from sucrose that dissolves enamel and causes dental caries, which can lead to a tooth abscess. It does not destroy the periodontal ligament and bone.","Actinomyces israelii causes cervicofacial actinomycosis, a slowly spreading infection with draining sinus tracts and sulfur granules, often after dental work."],"study":{"topics":["Periodontitis","Porphyromonas gingivalis"],"comps":["GI-13-L4","GI-9"]}},{"id":"GI-C55-Q3","stem":"The lesion is excised. Afterward, he has loss of both general sensation and taste over the right anterior two thirds of the tongue, and tongue protrusion is normal. Injury to which of the following nerves best explains these deficits?","options":["Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Lingual nerve","Chorda tympani in the middle ear"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.2","dok":3,"approach":["Map the anterior two thirds: general sensation by the lingual nerve (V3), taste by chorda tympani fibers (VII).","Recall that chorda tympani fibers join the lingual nerve in the infratemporal fossa and run with it to the tongue.","Pick the one nerve that carries both modalities at the site of surgery."],"why":"General sensation from the anterior two thirds of the tongue travels in the lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). Taste from the same area travels in the chorda tympani (facial nerve), whose fibers join the lingual nerve and run with it along the side of the tongue. Injury to the lingual nerve near the tongue therefore removes both touch and taste. Motor function is intact because the hypoglossal nerve supplies the tongue muscles.","whyNot":["The glossopharyngeal nerve carries both taste and general sensation from the posterior third of the tongue.","The hypoglossal nerve is motor. A lesion makes the tongue deviate toward the injured side on protrusion, without sensory loss.","Correct.","A chorda tympani lesion in the middle ear removes taste from the anterior two thirds and reduces submandibular and sublingual secretion, but general sensation is spared."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of the tongue","Lingual nerve and chorda tympani"],"comps":["GI-4"]}},{"id":"GI-C55-Q4","stem":"His tobacco and alcohol use also increase his risk of squamous cell carcinoma at another site in the digestive tract that is lined by the same type of epithelium as the lateral tongue. Which of the following sites has this epithelium?","options":["Gastric cardia","Anal canal above the pectinate line","Duodenal bulb","Middle third of the esophagus"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the lining of the lateral tongue: nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","Find the gut segment with the same lining.","Link shared epithelium and shared carcinogen exposure to shared squamous cell carcinoma risk."],"why":"The lateral tongue, floor of mouth, and esophagus are lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Tobacco smoke and alcohol bathe this whole upper aerodigestive lining, so a patient with oral squamous lesions is also at higher risk for esophageal squamous cell carcinoma, which most often arises in the middle third. Below the gastroesophageal junction the lining changes to simple columnar epithelium, where adenocarcinomas arise instead.","whyNot":["The gastric cardia is lined by simple columnar epithelium. Cancers here are adenocarcinomas.","Above the pectinate line the anal canal has columnar epithelium. Stratified squamous epithelium lies below the line.","The duodenum is lined by simple columnar epithelium with villi and goblet cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelium of the digestive tract","Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-K1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C56","system":"GI","dx":"Clostridioides difficile pseudomembranous colitis","codes":["GI-13-L1"],"case":"A 72-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 4 days of profuse watery diarrhea, about 10 stools daily, with lower abdominal cramps. Eight days ago she finished a course of clindamycin for a dental abscess. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. The abdomen is mildly distended and diffusely tender. Leukocyte count is 19,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000). A stool PCR assay is positive for the toxin B gene. Flexible sigmoidoscopy shows multiple raised yellow-white plaques on erythematous colonic mucosa. Diagnosis: Clostridioides difficile pseudomembranous colitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C56-Q1","stem":"A biopsy of one of the plaques is taken. Which of the following best describes the composition of the plaques?","options":["Fibrin, mucin, and neutrophils from damaged crypts","Necrotic squamous cells with budding yeast","Granulomas with central caseous necrosis","A thick collagen band under intact surface epithelium"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L1","obj":"GI-13-L.1","dok":2,"approach":["Connect the clinical setting (recent clindamycin, toxin B gene) to toxin-mediated colitis.","Recall how toxin damage to crypts produces the yellow-white plaques.","Pick the exudate that erupts from injured crypts."],"why":"Antibiotics such as clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, and cephalosporins disrupt the normal colonic flora and let C. difficile overgrow. Its toxins A and B injure the surface epithelium and crypts, which release a mushroom-shaped exudate of fibrin, mucin, neutrophils, and cell debris. These pseudomembranes form the yellow-white plaques seen at endoscopy. Severe disease can progress to toxic megacolon and perforation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Necrotic squamous cells with yeast describe Candida plaques, which form on squamous mucosa such as the mouth and esophagus.","Caseating granulomas suggest tuberculosis, which can involve the ileocecal region and does not form surface pseudomembranes.","A thick subepithelial collagen band describes collagenous colitis, a cause of chronic watery diarrhea with a normal-looking colon."],"study":{"topics":["Pseudomembranous colitis","C. difficile risk factors"],"comps":["GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C56-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the toxins of this organism injure colonic epithelial cells?","options":["Cross-linking MHC class II and T cell receptors as a superantigen","Glucosylation of Rho GTPases, disrupting the actin cytoskeleton","ADP-ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit, raising cyclic AMP in enterocytes","Cleavage of 28S ribosomal RNA, halting protein synthesis"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that C. difficile toxins A and B are glucosyltransferases.","Identify their targets, the Rho family GTPases that control the actin cytoskeleton.","Predict cell rounding, loss of tight junctions, cell death, and inflammation."],"why":"Toxins A and B enter colonocytes and add glucose to Rho, Rac, and Cdc42, the small GTPases that organize actin. The actin cytoskeleton collapses, tight junctions open, and cells round up and die. The damage triggers strong neutrophil recruitment, fluid loss, and pseudomembranes. Toxin B is the more potent cytotoxin, and stool PCR targets the toxin B gene because nearly all disease-causing strains carry it, including strains that lack toxin A.","whyNot":["Staphylococcus aureus enterotoxins act as superantigens. Preformed toxin in food causes vomiting within a few hours.","Correct.","Cholera toxin and the heat-labile toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli ADP-ribosylate Gs, raise cAMP, and open CFTR, causing large-volume watery diarrhea without cell death.","Shiga toxin and Shiga-like toxins of EHEC cleave 28S rRNA in the 60S subunit and stop protein synthesis."],"study":{"topics":["C. difficile toxins A and B","Mechanisms of bacterial enterotoxins"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C56-Q3","stem":"On the third day her abdominal distension worsens. An abdominal radiograph shows a markedly dilated transverse colon that has lost its normal haustral markings. Which of the following structures normally produces the haustra?","options":["Transverse rectal folds","Plicae circulares","Taeniae coli","Omental appendices"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","obj":"GI-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the radiograph: a dilated colon without haustra in severe colitis suggests toxic megacolon.","Recall what haustra are: sacculations of the colon wall seen only in the large intestine.","Pick the colon feature that gathers the wall into these sacs: the taeniae coli."],"why":"In the colon, the outer longitudinal muscle is gathered into three bands, the taeniae coli. The taeniae are shorter than the colon wall, so the wall bunches into sacs between them, called haustra. Haustra, taeniae coli, and omental appendices together mark large intestine on gross inspection and imaging. In toxic megacolon, inflammation spreads into the muscle layer, muscle tone is lost, and the dilated colon loses its haustral pattern, which raises the risk of perforation.","whyNot":["Transverse rectal folds are shelves of mucosa, submucosa, and circular muscle inside the rectum, which has no haustra.","Plicae circulares are permanent circular folds of mucosa and submucosa in the small intestine. On radiograph they cross the full width of the bowel, unlike haustral markings.","Correct.","Omental appendices are small fat-filled pouches of peritoneum on the outer colon surface. They help identify the colon but do not form the haustra."],"study":{"topics":["Gross features of the colon","Toxic megacolon"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C56-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sets of stool findings best fits the type of diarrhea she has?","options":["Leukocytes and lactoferrin present; continues with fasting","No leukocytes; gap above 100 mOsm/kg; stops with fasting","No leukocytes; high fecal fat; stops with fasting","No leukocytes; gap below 50 mOsm/kg; continues with fasting"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Classify her illness: toxins destroy the mucosa and recruit neutrophils, which is inflammatory diarrhea.","Recall the stool marker of inflammation: leukocytes, or lactoferrin and calprotectin released by neutrophils.","Recall that inflammatory diarrhea does not depend on eating, so it continues with fasting."],"why":"Diarrhea is grouped as secretory, osmotic, or inflammatory. In inflammatory diarrhea, damaged mucosa leaks fluid, protein, and cells, and neutrophils enter the lumen, so stool contains leukocytes and neutrophil proteins such as lactoferrin and calprotectin. Fever and leukocytosis are common. Because the problem is mucosal injury, the diarrhea continues during fasting.","whyNot":["Correct.","A large osmotic gap that resolves with fasting describes osmotic diarrhea, such as lactose intolerance or laxative use.","High fecal fat that stops with fasting suggests fat malabsorption, such as pancreatic insufficiency.","A small gap without leukocytes that continues with fasting describes pure secretory diarrhea, such as cholera."],"study":{"topics":["Inflammatory diarrhea","Fecal lactoferrin and calprotectin"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-13-L1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C57","system":"GI","dx":"Shigella dysentery","codes":["GI-13-L1"],"case":"A 27-year-old man who works at a child care center comes to the physician because of 2 days of fever, crampy lower abdominal pain, and frequent small-volume stools containing blood and mucus. He has painful straining to pass stool. Several children at the center have had a similar illness this week. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 102/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. The lower abdomen is tender. Stool microscopy shows many neutrophils and erythrocytes. Stool culture grows Shigella sonnei. Diagnosis: Shigella dysentery (shigellosis).","questions":[{"id":"GI-C57-Q1","stem":"Three weeks after the diarrhea resolves, he develops a painful, swollen right knee, bilateral conjunctivitis, and dysuria. Synovial fluid culture is sterile. Which of the following host factors most increases the risk of this complication?","options":["HLA-B27","HLA-DQ2","HLA-DR3","HLA-DR4"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L1","obj":"GI-13-L.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize reactive arthritis: sterile arthritis, conjunctivitis, and urethritis weeks after an enteric or genital infection.","Recall the enteric triggers: Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter, and Yersinia.","Link reactive arthritis to its MHC class I association."],"why":"Reactive arthritis is a sterile inflammatory arthritis that follows infection elsewhere, most often enteric infection with Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter, or Yersinia, or genital infection with Chlamydia. It typically affects large joints of the legs and may come with conjunctivitis and urethritis. Many patients carry HLA-B27, the same allele linked to ankylosing spondylitis and the other seronegative spondyloarthropathies.","whyNot":["Correct.","HLA-DQ2 (with DQ8) is linked to celiac disease.","HLA-DR3 is linked to type 1 diabetes, Graves disease, and systemic lupus erythematosus.","HLA-DR4 is linked to rheumatoid arthritis and type 1 diabetes."],"study":{"topics":["Reactive arthritis","Complications of enteric infection"],"comps":["GI-13-L1","MS-13-B7"]}},{"id":"GI-C57-Q2","stem":"The infection spread quickly among the children by person-to-person contact without a common food source. Which of the following features of the organism best explains this pattern?","options":["A reservoir in the intestines of poultry flocks","A reservoir in reptiles such as pet turtles","Flagellar motility toward the mucosal surface","Infection with as few as 10 to 100 organisms"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note the pattern: spread by direct contact among young children, with no food vehicle.","Recall which enteric pathogen needs only a tiny inoculum.","Link low infectious dose to easy fecal-oral spread on hands and surfaces."],"why":"Shigella resists gastric acid well, so as few as 10 to 100 organisms can cause disease. A tiny inoculum on hands or toys is enough, which makes person-to-person spread easy in child care centers and other crowded settings. Humans are its only reservoir. Shigella is nonmotile and does not produce H2S, which helps separate it from Salmonella in the laboratory.","whyNot":["A poultry reservoir fits Campylobacter jejuni and Salmonella, which usually spread through undercooked chicken.","Reptiles, such as pet turtles, are a reservoir for nontyphoidal Salmonella, which needs a much larger dose to cause disease.","Shigella is nonmotile. Salmonella and Campylobacter are motile.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Shigella vs Salmonella vs Campylobacter","Infectious dose"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L1"]}},{"id":"GI-C57-Q3","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains why his stools are frequent and small in volume and contain blood, mucus, and neutrophils, unlike the large-volume stools of cholera?","options":["Cyclic AMP-driven chloride secretion through CFTR","Invasion and destruction of the colonic mucosa","Unabsorbed solute holding water in the lumen","Serotonin-driven increase in intestinal motility"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the clues to inflammatory diarrhea: blood, mucus, neutrophils, fever, tenesmus.","Recall that Shigella enters through M cells and spreads cell to cell in the colonic epithelium.","Link an inflamed, irritable rectosigmoid colon to frequent small stools."],"why":"Shigella invades the colonic epithelium, spreads from cell to cell, and kills the cells, which produces ulcers and intense neutrophil infiltration. The damaged mucosa leaks blood, mucus, and inflammatory cells into the lumen. An inflamed rectum and sigmoid colon hold little stool and cause urgency and tenesmus, so stools are frequent and small. Cholera, by contrast, leaves the mucosa intact and makes the small intestine secrete liters of fluid.","whyNot":["Cholera toxin raises cAMP and opens CFTR, causing large-volume, watery stools without blood or neutrophils.","Correct.","Unabsorbed solute, such as lactose, causes osmotic diarrhea that stops with fasting and has no blood or neutrophils.","Serotonin drives secretion and speeds transit in carcinoid syndrome, causing watery diarrhea without mucosal destruction."],"study":{"topics":["Inflammatory vs secretory diarrhea","Shigella invasion"],"comps":["GI-7","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C57-Q4","stem":"His roommate returns from travel with prolonged fever and is found to have typhoid fever with ulcers in the terminal ileum. Which of the following histologic features would identify a biopsy as ileum rather than jejunum or duodenum?","options":["Mucous glands deep in the submucosa","Tall, closely spaced circular folds with villi","Large lymphoid aggregates in the submucosa","Crypts rich in goblet cells and no villi"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the identifying feature of each small bowel segment.","Match the ileum to Peyer patches, the large lymphoid aggregates that extend into the submucosa.","Link Salmonella Typhi to invasion through M cells over Peyer patches, which explains the ileal ulcers."],"why":"Peyer patches are clusters of lymphoid follicles in the mucosa and submucosa of the ileum, covered by M cells that sample luminal antigens. Salmonella Typhi enters through these M cells, multiplies in macrophages, and spreads through the blood. Inflammation and necrosis of the Peyer patches produce ulcers in the terminal ileum, which can bleed or perforate.","whyNot":["Submucosal mucous glands are Brunner glands, which identify the duodenum.","Tall, closely spaced plicae circulares are the hallmark of the jejunum.","Correct.","Goblet cell-rich crypts without villi identify the colon."],"study":{"topics":["Peyer patches","Histology of the small intestine"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-11"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C58","system":"GI","dx":"Candida esophagitis and oral thrush in advanced HIV infection","codes":["GI-13-L2","GI-13-L5"],"case":"A 38-year-old man with HIV infection who stopped antiretroviral therapy 2 years ago comes to the physician because of 3 weeks of painful swallowing and a sore mouth. He has lost 6 kg (13.2 lb). Temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows creamy white plaques on the buccal mucosa and palate that scrape off and leave a red, bleeding base. CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 62/mm3 (500 to 1,500). Upper endoscopy shows numerous adherent white plaques along the length of the esophagus. Diagnosis: Candida esophagitis and oral thrush in advanced HIV infection.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C58-Q1","stem":"Biopsy specimens are taken from the esophageal plaques. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Enlarged endothelial cells with inclusions at the base of linear ulcers","Multinucleated squamous cells with intranuclear inclusions at ulcer edges","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae in a fibrinous exudate","More than 15 eosinophils per high-power field in the epithelium"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L2","obj":"GI-13-L.3","dok":2,"approach":["Match the endoscopic pattern: white plaques point to Candida, punched-out ulcers to HSV, and long linear ulcers to CMV.","Recall the tissue form of Candida albicans.","Pick yeast with pseudohyphae in the plaque material."],"why":"Candida albicans grows in tissue as budding yeast with pseudohyphae, best shown with PAS or silver stains. In the esophagus it forms white plaques of fungus, fibrin, and debris that stick to the squamous lining. In patients with HIV and low CD4 counts, Candida is the most common cause of infectious esophagitis, and esophageal candidiasis is an AIDS-defining illness.","whyNot":["Inclusions in endothelial and stromal cells at the base of long, shallow linear ulcers describe CMV esophagitis.","Multinucleated squamous cells with ground-glass intranuclear inclusions at the edges of small, punched-out ulcers describe HSV esophagitis.","Correct.","Many intraepithelial eosinophils describe eosinophilic esophagitis, an allergic disorder with rings and furrows."],"study":{"topics":["Infectious esophagitis","Candida morphology"],"comps":["GI-13-L2","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C58-Q2","stem":"Which of the following conditions also predisposes to oral infection by this organism by impairing local mucosal defenses?","options":["Splenectomy after abdominal trauma","Inhaled corticosteroid use without rinsing","Deficiency of terminal complement components","Deficiency of C1 esterase inhibitor"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L5","obj":"GI-13-L.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that mucosal control of Candida depends on T cells (especially Th17 cells) and a balanced oral flora.","Look for a condition that suppresses local immunity in the mouth.","Exclude defects that mainly raise risk from encapsulated bacteria, Neisseria, or swelling."],"why":"Oral thrush is overgrowth of Candida albicans, which normally lives in the mouth in small numbers. It appears when local defenses or the competing flora fail: in young infants, with inhaled corticosteroids that deposit in the mouth, after broad-spectrum antibiotics, in diabetes, in denture wearers, and in HIV with falling CD4 counts. Steroid from an inhaler suppresses local immunity, which is why patients are told to rinse after each use.","whyNot":["Splenectomy raises the risk of sepsis from encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae.","Correct.","Terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency raises the risk of recurrent Neisseria infections.","C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency causes hereditary angioedema, with recurrent swelling and no infection risk."],"study":{"topics":["Oral thrush risk factors","Mucosal antifungal immunity"],"comps":["GI-13-L5","IM-6"]}},{"id":"GI-C58-Q3","stem":"At endoscopy, the paler esophageal lining meets the redder gastric lining at an irregular line near the gastroesophageal junction. Which of the following epithelial transitions normally occurs at this line?","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous to simple cuboidal","Pseudostratified columnar to simple columnar","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous to goblet cell-rich columnar","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous to simple columnar"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the normal esophageal lining: nonkeratinized stratified squamous.","Recall the normal gastric lining: simple columnar mucus-secreting cells.","Recognize that goblet cells above this line would be abnormal intestinal metaplasia."],"why":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which resists abrasion from swallowed food. At the squamocolumnar junction, or Z line, it changes abruptly to the simple columnar, mucus-secreting epithelium of the stomach. With chronic acid reflux, columnar epithelium with goblet cells can replace the distal esophageal squamous lining. This intestinal metaplasia is Barrett esophagus, the precursor of esophageal adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["The esophagus is nonkeratinized in humans, and the stomach is lined by columnar cells.","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the trachea and bronchi.","Columnar epithelium with goblet cells at or above the junction is Barrett metaplasia, an abnormal change caused by chronic reflux.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Gastroesophageal junction histology","Barrett esophagus"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-13-G2"]}},{"id":"GI-C58-Q4","stem":"During swallowing, the lower esophageal sphincter relaxes as the peristaltic wave approaches it. Which of the following mediates this relaxation?","options":["Norepinephrine released from postganglionic sympathetic fibers","Nitric oxide and VIP from vagal inhibitory pathways","Gastrin released from antral G cells","Acetylcholine acting on muscarinic receptors"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that swallowing is coordinated by the swallowing center in the medulla.","Recall that vagal fibers synapse on enteric inhibitory neurons at the sphincter.","Identify their transmitters: nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide."],"why":"Swallowing begins with a voluntary oral phase, then a reflex pharyngeal phase and esophageal phase coordinated by the medulla through the vagus nerves. As peristalsis starts, vagal fibers activate inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus at the lower esophageal sphincter. These neurons release nitric oxide and VIP, which relax the sphincter smooth muscle so the bolus can enter the stomach. Loss of these inhibitory neurons causes achalasia.","whyNot":["Sympathetic input does not drive the swallow-induced relaxation of the sphincter.","Correct.","Gastrin does not mediate the swallow-induced relaxation of the sphincter.","Acetylcholine from excitatory vagal pathways contracts esophageal smooth muscle and maintains sphincter tone."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of swallowing","Lower esophageal sphincter control"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F1"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C59","system":"GI","dx":"Norovirus gastroenteritis in a cruise ship outbreak","codes":["GI-13-L3"],"case":"A 67-year-old woman comes to the ship's physician during a cruise because of sudden nausea, repeated vomiting, and watery, nonbloody diarrhea that began 14 hours ago. More than 100 other passengers and crew members have had similar symptoms over the past 3 days. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Mucous membranes are dry. Serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (135 to 145), and serum creatinine is 1.4 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1). Reverse transcription PCR of stool is positive for norovirus. Diagnosis: norovirus gastroenteritis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C59-Q1","stem":"On the same voyage, a crew member eats fried rice that was cooked the day before, left at room temperature, and then reheated. Two hours later he develops nausea and repeated vomiting. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his illness?","options":["Bacillus cereus","Rotavirus","Vibrio cholerae","Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L3","obj":"GI-13-L.4","dok":2,"approach":["Use the time to onset: vomiting within a few hours means a toxin already formed in the food.","Use the food: rice left warm lets spores germinate and make toxin.","Match reheated rice to Bacillus cereus."],"why":"Bacillus cereus spores survive cooking. When cooked rice sits at room temperature, the spores germinate and the bacteria make a heat-stable emetic toxin. Reheating does not destroy this toxin, so vomiting begins within about 1 to 6 hours of eating. A separate diarrheal form appears later, after about 8 to 16 hours.","whyNot":["Correct.","Rotavirus causes watery diarrhea in unvaccinated infants and young children after an incubation of about 2 days.","Vibrio cholerae causes profuse rice-water stools after drinking contaminated water, with an incubation of about 1 to 3 days.","Enterotoxigenic E. coli is the classic cause of traveler's diarrhea, with watery stools 1 to 3 days after eating contaminated food or water."],"study":{"topics":["Watery gastroenteritis by setting","Bacillus cereus"],"comps":["GI-13-L3","GI-12"]}},{"id":"GI-C59-Q2","stem":"The outbreak continued despite wide use of alcohol-based hand sanitizer. Which of the following best describes the causative virus?","options":["Segmented double-stranded RNA reovirus","Enveloped single-stranded RNA coronavirus","Nonenveloped double-stranded DNA enteric adenovirus","Nonenveloped single-stranded RNA calicivirus"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.4","dok":3,"approach":["Classify norovirus: a calicivirus with a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome.","Recall that it lacks an envelope.","Link the missing envelope to resistance to alcohol and survival on surfaces."],"why":"Norovirus is a nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus in the calicivirus family. Without a lipid envelope, it resists alcohol-based hand sanitizers, survives on surfaces, and tolerates freezing and mild heat. A very small dose causes infection, and patients shed large amounts in vomit and stool. These traits make it the leading cause of outbreaks on cruise ships and in nursing homes, schools, and restaurants, where soap and water and bleach-based cleaning are needed.","whyNot":["Rotavirus is a segmented double-stranded RNA reovirus. It mainly affects unvaccinated infants and young children.","Enveloped viruses are inactivated readily by alcohol. Coronaviruses are mainly respiratory pathogens.","Enteric adenovirus types 40 and 41 cause diarrhea mostly in young children, not adult outbreaks.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Norovirus","Viral causes of gastroenteritis"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-L3"]}},{"id":"GI-C59-Q3","stem":"She is given an oral rehydration solution containing glucose and sodium chloride. Which of the following best explains why adding glucose increases intestinal water absorption?","options":["SGLT1 couples sodium uptake to glucose at the brush border","Glucose enters by GLUT5 and pulls water in by osmosis","Glucose closes CFTR chloride channels in crypt cells","Glucose raises insulin, which adds aquaporins to enterocytes"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how glucose crosses the apical membrane: SGLT1, with two sodium ions per glucose.","Note that SGLT1 keeps working during most infectious diarrheas.","Link solute absorption to water following it across the epithelium."],"why":"SGLT1 on the brush border carries two sodium ions with each glucose or galactose molecule into the enterocyte. Glucose leaves the cell through GLUT2 on the basolateral side, and sodium is pumped out by Na+/K+-ATPase. The absorbed solute creates an osmotic gradient, and water follows. This transporter remains largely functional in most acute diarrheal illnesses, so a solution with both glucose and sodium restores fluid even while diarrhea continues.","whyNot":["Correct.","GLUT5 transports fructose by facilitated diffusion, without sodium. Glucose enters by SGLT1.","Glucose does not close CFTR. Cholera toxin opens CFTR by raising cAMP.","Insulin does not control water uptake in the intestine, and enterocyte glucose uptake does not depend on insulin."],"study":{"topics":["Monosaccharide absorption","Oral rehydration solution"],"comps":["GI-7"]}},{"id":"GI-C59-Q4","stem":"Her symptoms persist for 3 weeks, and she undergoes upper endoscopy with biopsy of the proximal small intestine. Which of the following features identifies the specimen as duodenum rather than jejunum?","options":["Peyer patches in the mucosa and submucosa","Mucous glands in the submucosa","Villi lined by simple columnar epithelium","Goblet cells scattered among absorptive cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["List what all small bowel segments share: villi, simple columnar epithelium, and goblet cells.","Find the feature unique to the duodenum.","Link Brunner glands to their alkaline mucus, which protects against acid from the stomach."],"why":"Brunner glands are coiled mucous glands in the submucosa of the duodenum. They secrete alkaline, bicarbonate-rich mucus that helps neutralize acid from the stomach and protects the duodenal mucosa. The jejunum lacks these glands and has tall, closely spaced plicae circulares, and the ileum has Peyer patches.","whyNot":["Peyer patches identify the ileum.","Correct.","Villi with simple columnar epithelium line the whole small intestine.","Goblet cells occur throughout the small intestine and colon, so they do not identify a segment."],"study":{"topics":["Brunner glands","Histology of the small intestine"],"comps":["GI-2"]}}]},{"id":"GI-C60","system":"GI","dx":"Primary herpetic gingivostomatitis in a toddler","codes":["GI-13-L6"],"case":"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of 3 days of fever, irritability, drooling, and refusal to eat or drink. Temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F), pulse is 140/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 94/58 mm Hg. Physical examination shows swollen, erythematous gingiva that bleed easily, clusters of small vesicles and shallow ulcers on the gingiva, anterior tongue, and lips, and tender submandibular and anterior cervical lymph nodes. Mucous membranes are dry. A Tzanck smear of a vesicle shows multinucleated giant cells, and PCR is positive for herpes simplex virus type 1 (HSV-1). Diagnosis: primary herpetic gingivostomatitis.","questions":[{"id":"GI-C60-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient most reliably distinguishes his illness from herpangina?","options":["Occurrence in a child younger than 5 years","Vesicles that progress to shallow ulcers","Gingivitis with lip and anterior oral vesicles","High fever with irritability and refusal to drink"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L6","obj":"GI-13-L.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall herpangina: coxsackie A virus with vesicles on the soft palate, uvula, and tonsillar pillars.","Recall primary HSV-1: inflamed, bleeding gums and vesicles in the front of the mouth and on the lips.","Pick the feature found in only one of the two."],"why":"Both illnesses strike young children with fever, sore mouth, and vesicles that become ulcers. Primary HSV-1 gingivostomatitis involves the front of the mouth: swollen, bleeding gingiva and vesicles on the gums, tongue, buccal mucosa, and lips. Herpangina, caused by coxsackie A viruses, places vesicles at the back of the mouth on the soft palate, uvula, and tonsillar pillars, and the gums are spared. Hand-foot-and-mouth disease (coxsackie A16) adds vesicles on the palms and soles.","whyNot":["Both occur mostly in young children.","Vesicles become ulcers in both illnesses.","Correct.","Both illnesses can cause high fever, irritability, and poor intake."],"study":{"topics":["Herpetic gingivostomatitis vs herpangina","Oral vesicular lesions"],"comps":["GI-13-L6"]}},{"id":"GI-C60-Q2","stem":"After the illness resolves, the virus remains latent and can later reactivate as cold sores. Latent virus that infected the anterior tongue and lower gingiva most likely resides in neurons of which of the following ganglia?","options":["Trigeminal ganglion","Geniculate ganglion","Submandibular ganglion","Inferior ganglion of the glossopharyngeal nerve"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that HSV travels up sensory axons and stays latent in their cell bodies.","Map general sensation: anterior two thirds of the tongue by the lingual nerve and lower gingiva by branches of V3.","Find the ganglion that holds these sensory cell bodies."],"why":"HSV-1 enters the endings of sensory neurons in the mucosa and travels by retrograde axonal transport to their cell bodies, where it stays latent. General sensation from the anterior two thirds of the tongue travels in the lingual nerve, and the lower gums are supplied by other branches of the mandibular nerve (V3). The cell bodies of these neurons lie in the trigeminal ganglion. Reactivation sends virus back down the axons, usually causing cold sores on the lip.","whyNot":["Correct.","The geniculate ganglion holds the cell bodies of chorda tympani taste fibers (facial nerve). The gingiva has no taste fibers, so general sensory neurons are the better fit.","The submandibular ganglion is a parasympathetic ganglion for the submandibular and sublingual glands, with no sensory cell bodies.","The inferior glossopharyngeal ganglion holds sensory neurons for the posterior third of the tongue and the pharynx."],"study":{"topics":["Sensory innervation of the tongue and gums","HSV latency"],"comps":["GI-4","GI-13-L6"]}},{"id":"GI-C60-Q3","stem":"Because of his painful mouth and fever, he drinks little, and his salivary flow falls. If salivary flow stayed low for months, loss of which of the following salivary functions would most directly promote dental caries?","options":["Lubrication of food by mucins","Starch digestion by salivary amylase","Fat digestion by lingual lipase","Bicarbonate buffering of plaque acid"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that caries forms when plaque bacteria make acid that dissolves enamel.","Ask which salivary function opposes this acid.","Pick bicarbonate buffering, which also allows calcium and phosphate to remineralize enamel."],"why":"Dental caries forms when bacteria such as Streptococcus mutans ferment sugars into acid that dissolves enamel. Saliva protects teeth in several ways: bicarbonate buffers plaque acid, calcium and phosphate remineralize early lesions, flow washes away sugars, and IgA, lysozyme, and lactoferrin limit bacteria. When flow stays low, as in Sjogren syndrome, after head and neck radiation, or with anticholinergic drugs, caries develops rapidly, often at the gum line.","whyNot":["Mucins lubricate food for swallowing, and their loss makes chewing and swallowing harder, but buffering of acid is the key defense against caries.","Salivary amylase starts starch digestion in the mouth, but losing it does not promote caries, and pancreatic amylase completes starch digestion.","Lingual lipase begins fat digestion, mainly in infants, and has no role in protecting enamel.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Functions of saliva","Dental caries and dry mouth"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-L4"]}},{"id":"GI-C60-Q4","stem":"Secretory IgA in his saliva helps neutralize viruses on the oral mucosa. Which of the following steps delivers this antibody into saliva in a protease-resistant form?","options":["Synthesis of IgA by the salivary acinar cells","Leakage of monomeric serum IgA across tight junctions","Transcytosis by the polymeric Ig receptor","Transport by the neonatal Fc receptor on acinar cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that plasma cells beneath the epithelium make dimeric IgA joined by a J chain.","Identify the receptor that binds this dimer on the basolateral surface and carries it across the cell.","Note that cleavage at the apical surface leaves a piece of the receptor attached that shields IgA from proteases."],"why":"Plasma cells in the gland interstitium secrete dimeric IgA linked by a J chain. The polymeric Ig receptor on the basolateral surface of epithelial cells binds the dimer and carries it across the cell by transcytosis. At the apical surface the receptor is cleaved, and a part of it, the secretory component, stays bound to the IgA. Secretory component protects the antibody from proteases in saliva and the gut lumen. Selective IgA deficiency removes this barrier and predisposes to recurrent sinus, lung, and gut infections.","whyNot":["IgA is made by plasma cells, not by epithelial acinar cells. The epithelium only transports it.","Serum IgA is mostly monomeric and does not cross intact epithelium in meaningful amounts. Without secretory component it would be degraded.","Correct.","The neonatal Fc receptor transports IgG across the placenta and protects IgG from breakdown. It does not carry IgA into secretions."],"study":{"topics":["Secretory IgA and the polymeric Ig receptor","Mucosal immunity"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-9"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C01","system":"HE","dx":"Lead poisoning with microcytic anemia","codes":["HE-9-A1"],"case":"A 36-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of fatigue, intermittent crampy abdominal pain, and constipation. For the past year, he has worked at a plant that recycles lead-acid batteries. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows pallor and weakness of wrist extension on the right. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 10.4 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), MCV of 71 fL (80 to 100), and reticulocyte count of 0.8% (0.5 to 2.5). A peripheral blood smear shows hypochromic, microcytic erythrocytes with coarse basophilic stippling. Blood lead concentration is 68 µg/dL (less than 5), and erythrocyte zinc protoporphyrin concentration is markedly increased. Diagnosis: lead poisoning with microcytic anemia.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C01-Q1","stem":"A bone marrow aspirate stained with Prussian blue shows erythroid precursors with iron-laden mitochondria forming a ring around the nucleus. Inhibition of which of the following enzymes best explains both this finding and the increased zinc protoporphyrin concentration?","options":["Aminolevulinate synthase 2","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Aminolevulinate dehydratase","Ferrochelatase"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-A1","obj":"HE-9-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Ringed sideroblasts mean iron reaches the mitochondria of erythroid precursors but is not built into heme.","High zinc protoporphyrin means protoporphyrin IX is present but received zinc in place of iron.","The mitochondrial enzyme that inserts ferrous iron into protoporphyrin IX is ferrochelatase, and lead inhibits it."],"why":"Ferrochelatase is the last enzyme of heme synthesis. It works in the mitochondria and inserts ferrous iron into protoporphyrin IX. When lead blocks it, iron piles up in the mitochondria that sit around the nucleus, which gives ringed sideroblasts, and the unused protoporphyrin takes up zinc instead, so zinc protoporphyrin rises. Lead also inhibits ALA dehydratase, and it inhibits pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase, which leaves clumps of ribosomal RNA in the cell and causes basophilic stippling.","whyNot":["ALAS2 is the erythroid form of the first enzyme of heme synthesis. X-linked ALAS2 mutations and pyridoxine deficiency (for example from isoniazid) cause sideroblastic anemia, but protoporphyrin is low because little is made.","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency causes porphyria cutanea tarda, with blistering skin and high urine uroporphyrin. It does not cause ringed sideroblasts or high zinc protoporphyrin.","Lead does inhibit ALA dehydratase, which raises urinary ALA. This is an early cytosolic step, so blocking it alone would lower protoporphyrin, not raise it.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Sideroblastic anemia","Lead poisoning"],"comps":["HE-9-A1","HE-5"]}},{"id":"HE-C01-Q2","stem":"A 27-year-old coworker has a normal blood lead concentration. His hemoglobin concentration is 12.1 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), MCV is 63 fL (80 to 100), and erythrocyte count is 6.2 million/mm3 (4.3 to 5.9). Serum ferritin concentration is 140 ng/mL (20 to 250), serum iron concentration is 95 µg/dL (50 to 170), and total iron-binding capacity is 310 µg/dL (250 to 400). A peripheral blood smear shows target cells. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the coworker's anemia?","options":["Iron deficiency anemia","Sideroblastic anemia","Thalassemia trait","Anemia of chronic disease"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-9-A1","obj":"HE-9-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Microcytosis with normal ferritin, serum iron, and TIBC rules out iron deficiency, iron trapping, and iron overload.","A high red cell count with a very low MCV and only mild anemia points to reduced globin chain production, where the marrow makes many small cells.","Target cells complete the picture of thalassemia trait."],"why":"Thalassemia trait lowers globin chain output, so each red cell holds less hemoglobin and is small. Iron supply is normal, so ferritin, serum iron, and TIBC are normal. The marrow keeps making cells, so the erythrocyte count is normal or high and the anemia is mild for how low the MCV is. Target cells form because the membrane is large compared with the small amount of hemoglobin inside.","whyNot":["Iron deficiency gives low ferritin, high TIBC, and low transferrin saturation, and the erythrocyte count is usually low.","Sideroblastic anemia shows high serum iron, high ferritin, and ringed sideroblasts in the marrow.","Correct.","In anemia of chronic disease, IL-6 raises hepcidin, which traps iron in macrophages. Ferritin is high, TIBC is low, and serum iron is low. The MCV is usually normal or only mildly low."],"study":{"topics":["Iron studies in microcytic anemia","Thalassemia trait"],"comps":["HE-9-A1","HE-9-B1"]}},{"id":"HE-C01-Q3","stem":"Urine studies show a markedly increased concentration of delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA). In which of the following cell compartments, and from which substrates, is this compound normally made?","options":["Mitochondria, from two molecules of porphobilinogen","Mitochondria, from glycine and succinyl-CoA","Cytosol, from glycine and succinyl-CoA","Cytosol, from two molecules of porphobilinogen"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-5","obj":"HE-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Lead blocks ALA dehydratase, the step after ALA, so ALA builds up and spills into the urine.","ALA is made by the first and rate-limiting enzyme, ALA synthase.","ALA synthase works in the mitochondria, joins glycine to succinyl-CoA, and needs pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)."],"why":"Heme synthesis starts in the mitochondria, where ALA synthase joins glycine and succinyl-CoA to form ALA, using vitamin B6 as a cofactor. ALA moves to the cytosol, where ALA dehydratase joins two ALA molecules into porphobilinogen, and the middle steps continue there. The last steps return to the mitochondria, ending with ferrochelatase. Heme feeds back to repress ALA synthase, the rate-limiting enzyme, so heme levels control the whole pathway.","whyNot":["Porphobilinogen is a downstream product, and the step that forms it takes place in the cytosol.","Correct.","The substrates are right, but ALA synthase is a mitochondrial enzyme. Succinyl-CoA comes from the citric acid cycle inside the mitochondria.","Porphobilinogen is made from ALA, not the reverse. Two ALA molecules combine in the cytosol to form one porphobilinogen."],"study":{"topics":["Heme synthesis pathway","ALA synthase regulation"],"comps":["HE-5","HE-9-A1"]}},{"id":"HE-C01-Q4","stem":"His 31-year-old sister, who has no lead exposure, is brought to the emergency department because of severe diffuse abdominal pain, confusion, and dark urine 1 week after she began a very low-calorie diet. The abdomen is soft without guarding. Her skin is normal. Urine porphobilinogen concentration is markedly increased. Deficiency of which of the following enzymes most likely explains her findings?","options":["Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Porphobilinogen deaminase","Ferrochelatase","Aminolevulinate dehydratase"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-5","obj":"HE-5.2","dok":3,"approach":["Abdominal pain with a benign abdominal examination, mental status change, and normal skin point to an acute porphyria.","High urine porphobilinogen means the block is at the enzyme that uses porphobilinogen.","Fasting induces hepatic ALA synthase, which pushes more precursor into the block and sets off an attack."],"why":"Acute intermittent porphyria is an autosomal dominant partial deficiency of porphobilinogen deaminase (hydroxymethylbilane synthase). ALA and porphobilinogen build up and are neurotoxic, which causes abdominal pain, neuropathy, and psychiatric symptoms. The skin is spared because the block comes before the light-absorbing porphyrins form. Attacks follow anything that induces hepatic ALA synthase, such as fasting, alcohol, and drugs that induce cytochrome P450. Porphyria cutanea tarda is the contrast: uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency, blistering of sun-exposed skin, and a link to hepatitis C.","whyNot":["Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency causes porphyria cutanea tarda, with blistering of sun-exposed skin and high urine uroporphyrin, not acute neurovisceral attacks.","Correct.","Ferrochelatase deficiency causes erythropoietic protoporphyria, with painful skin soon after sun exposure. Urine porphobilinogen is normal.","ALA dehydratase is the enzyme lead inhibits. Blocking it raises ALA but not porphobilinogen, because porphobilinogen is the product of that step."],"study":{"topics":["Acute intermittent porphyria","Porphyria cutanea tarda"],"comps":["HE-5"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C02","system":"HE","dx":"Iron deficiency anemia from chronic intravascular hemolysis of a mechanical aortic valve","codes":["HE-9-A1","HE-9-B1"],"case":"A 68-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of fatigue and shortness of breath with exertion. Nine years ago, his aortic valve was replaced with a mechanical prosthesis. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 134/70 mm Hg. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 9.2 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), MCV of 74 fL (80 to 100), reticulocyte count of 4.2% (0.5 to 2.5), serum LDH of 640 U/L (140 to 280), haptoglobin of less than 10 mg/dL (30 to 200), and ferritin of 7 ng/mL (20 to 250). A peripheral blood smear shows schistocytes. Urine hemosiderin is present. Three stool tests for occult blood are negative. Diagnosis: iron deficiency anemia from chronic intravascular hemolysis caused by a mechanical aortic valve.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C02-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his low serum ferritin concentration?","options":["Urinary loss of hemoglobin and hemosiderin","Iron trapped in splenic macrophages after phagocytosis","Iron consumed by a hyperactive erythroid marrow","Hepcidin-mediated block of duodenal iron absorption"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B","obj":"HE-9-B.10","dok":3,"approach":["Schistocytes, high LDH, absent haptoglobin, and urine hemosiderin mean red cells are breaking open inside the vessels.","Free hemoglobin saturates haptoglobin, is filtered by the glomerulus, and is taken up by tubular cells, which later shed into the urine as hemosiderin.","Iron that leaves in the urine is lost from the body, so over months the stores run out."],"why":"Turbulent, high-velocity flow across a mechanical valve shears red cells, so they lyse in the circulation. Free hemoglobin first binds haptoglobin, and once haptoglobin is used up, hemoglobin dimers pass the glomerulus. Tubular cells take up the iron, store it as hemosiderin, and slough into the urine. These losses are small each day but never stop, so iron stores fall and the anemia becomes microcytic. In extravascular hemolysis, by contrast, the iron stays in macrophages and is reused.","whyNot":["Correct.","Iron from red cells eaten by splenic macrophages stays in the body and is recycled, so extravascular hemolysis does not empty iron stores.","An active marrow uses iron, but that iron returns to the pool when the cells die. Without a route of loss, increased production alone does not empty stores.","High hepcidin blocks absorption in anemia of chronic disease, but ferritin is then high. In iron deficiency, hepcidin is low."],"study":{"topics":["Intravascular hemolysis","Mechanical hemolytic anemia"],"comps":["HE-9-B","HE-3"]}},{"id":"HE-C02-Q2","stem":"Before attributing the anemia to the valve, his physician wants to confirm that iron stores are depleted. Which of the following additional serum findings are most likely?","options":["High TIBC and low transferrin saturation","Low TIBC and low transferrin saturation","High TIBC and high transferrin saturation","Low TIBC and high transferrin saturation"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-A1","obj":"HE-9-A.1","dok":2,"approach":["A ferritin concentration of 7 ng/mL means iron stores are empty.","When stores are low, the liver makes more transferrin, so TIBC rises.","Little iron is available to load the extra transferrin, so transferrin saturation falls."],"why":"Iron deficiency has a typical pattern: low ferritin, low serum iron, high TIBC, low transferrin saturation, high RDW, and small, pale cells. TIBC is an indirect measure of transferrin, which the liver makes more of when iron is scarce. In older men and postmenopausal women, the usual cause is chronic gastrointestinal blood loss, so that must be excluded first. Other causes are heavy menses, pregnancy, poor intake, and poor absorption, as in celiac disease. In this patient the loss is urinary, from valve hemolysis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Low TIBC with low saturation is the anemia of chronic disease pattern, where hepcidin traps iron and ferritin is high.","High saturation means plenty of circulating iron, which cannot happen when stores are empty.","Low TIBC with high saturation fits iron overload, as in hereditary hemochromatosis or sideroblastic anemia."],"study":{"topics":["Iron deficiency anemia","Interpreting iron studies"],"comps":["HE-9-A1","HE-3"]}},{"id":"HE-C02-Q3","stem":"He is treated with oral ferrous sulfate, and his hemoglobin concentration rises. A woman with anemia of chronic disease from rheumatoid arthritis takes the same dose but absorbs little iron. Which of the following best explains why he absorbs iron well?","options":["High hepcidin, so DMT1 imports more ferrous iron","High transferrin saturation pulling iron from enterocytes","Low hepcidin, so ferroportin keeps exporting iron","Low hepcidin, so DMT1 imports more ferric iron"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-3","obj":"HE-3.4","dok":3,"approach":["Duodenal enterocytes take up ferrous iron through DMT1 on the apical side and release it through ferroportin on the basolateral side.","Hepcidin from the liver binds ferroportin and causes it to be internalized and degraded.","Iron deficiency suppresses hepcidin, while IL-6 in inflammation raises it, so absorption is high in him and low in her."],"why":"Dietary ferric iron is reduced to ferrous iron at the brush border and enters the enterocyte through DMT1. Ferroportin exports it into plasma, where transferrin carries it to the marrow, and iron that is not exported stays in the cell as ferritin and is lost when the cell sheds. Hepcidin controls the whole system by degrading ferroportin on enterocytes and macrophages. In iron deficiency, hepcidin is low, so ferroportin stays active and absorption rises. In inflammation, IL-6 raises hepcidin, so both gut absorption and release from macrophages fall.","whyNot":["DMT1 does carry ferrous iron, but high hepcidin lowers absorption by degrading ferroportin. High hepcidin is the problem in the woman with arthritis, not in him.","His transferrin saturation is low, not high. Low saturation reflects the deficit and does not by itself move iron across the cell.","Correct.","Hepcidin is low in him, but DMT1 carries ferrous iron, not ferric iron. Ferric iron must first be reduced at the brush border by duodenal cytochrome b."],"study":{"topics":["Iron absorption and hepcidin","Anemia of chronic disease"],"comps":["HE-3","HE-9-A1"]}},{"id":"HE-C02-Q4","stem":"Laboratory studies 3 years ago, before his iron stores fell, showed a hemoglobin concentration of 11.4 g/dL, MCV of 93 fL, reticulocyte count of 6%, and undetectable haptoglobin. At that time, which of the following best classified his anemia?","options":["Macrocytic anemia from reduced marrow output","Normocytic anemia from reduced marrow output","Microcytic anemia from red cell destruction","Normocytic anemia from red cell destruction"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-2","obj":"HE-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Use MCV first: 80 to 100 fL is normocytic, so 93 fL is normocytic.","Then use the reticulocyte count: a high count with anemia means the marrow is responding and cells are being destroyed or lost.","Undetectable haptoglobin points to destruction (hemolysis) rather than bleeding."],"why":"Anemias are sorted two ways. MCV gives cell size: below 80 fL is microcytic, 80 to 100 fL is normocytic, and above 100 fL is macrocytic. The reticulocyte count shows the marrow response: it is low in underproduction and high in hemolysis or after bleeding. His early anemia was normocytic with a high reticulocyte count and absent haptoglobin, which is hemolysis with iron still available. As urinary iron loss emptied his stores, his cells became small and the picture changed to iron deficiency.","whyNot":["His MCV was not above 100 fL, and his marrow output was increased. A very high reticulocyte count can raise the MCV slightly because reticulocytes are larger than mature cells.","Reduced marrow output gives a low reticulocyte count. His was high.","His MCV was 93 fL, in the normal range. Microcytosis came later, after his iron stores were gone.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anemia classification by MCV","Reticulocyte count"],"comps":["HE-2","HE-9-B"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C03","system":"HE","dx":"Pernicious anemia with subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord","codes":["HE-9-A1"],"case":"A 61-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of fatigue, tingling in both feet, and unsteadiness when walking in the dark. She has Hashimoto thyroiditis treated with levothyroxine. She has never had abdominal surgery and eats a mixed diet. Vital signs are within normal limits. Examination shows pallor and a smooth, beefy-red tongue. Vibration and joint position sense are decreased in both lower extremities, and the Romberg sign is present. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 8.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), MCV of 117 fL (80 to 100), reticulocyte count of 0.4% (0.5 to 2.5), and serum vitamin B12 concentration of 105 pg/mL (200 to 900). A peripheral blood smear shows macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils. Antibodies to intrinsic factor are present. Diagnosis: pernicious anemia with subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C03-Q1","stem":"Endoscopic biopsy specimens show atrophy of the mucosa of the gastric body and fundus with loss of parietal cells and intestinal metaplasia. Which of the following most directly causes her vitamin B12 deficiency?","options":["Lack of pancreatic proteases to free B12 from haptocorrin","Loss of absorptive surface in the terminal ileum","Uptake of dietary B12 by an intestinal tapeworm","Lack of intrinsic factor for terminal ileal uptake"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-A1","obj":"HE-9-A.4","dok":2,"approach":["Antibodies to intrinsic factor and atrophy of the body and fundus point to autoimmune gastritis, which destroys parietal cells.","Parietal cells make intrinsic factor as well as acid.","B12 is absorbed only when bound to intrinsic factor at receptors in the terminal ileum."],"why":"In the stomach, B12 is released from food and binds haptocorrin. Pancreatic proteases free it in the duodenum, and it then binds intrinsic factor from parietal cells. The B12 and intrinsic factor complex is taken up by cubilin receptors in the terminal ileum. In pernicious anemia, autoimmune destruction of parietal cells and antibodies that block intrinsic factor prevent this uptake. Ileal resection or Crohn disease and the fish tapeworm Dibothriocephalus latus cause the same deficiency at other points in the pathway.","whyNot":["Pancreatic insufficiency can leave B12 bound to haptocorrin and lower absorption, but her lesion is in the stomach and her antibodies target intrinsic factor.","Ileal resection or Crohn disease removes the absorptive site. She has had no surgery, and her disease is in the stomach.","Dibothriocephalus (formerly Diphyllobothrium) latus, acquired from raw freshwater fish, competes for B12 in the gut. It does not explain gastric atrophy or intrinsic factor antibodies.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin B12 absorption","Pernicious anemia"],"comps":["HE-9-A1","HE-5"]}},{"id":"HE-C03-Q2","stem":"Which of the following combinations of serum concentrations is most likely in this patient?","options":["Normal methylmalonic acid, normal homocysteine","Normal methylmalonic acid, high homocysteine","High methylmalonic acid, high homocysteine","High methylmalonic acid, normal homocysteine"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-5","obj":"HE-5.6","dok":3,"approach":["B12 is a cofactor for two enzymes: methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.","Folate is needed only for the methionine synthase reaction, which turns homocysteine into methionine.","So both deficiencies raise homocysteine, but only B12 deficiency also raises methylmalonic acid."],"why":"Methionine synthase uses B12 to move a methyl group from 5-methyltetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, which makes methionine and frees tetrahydrofolate. Without B12 or folate, homocysteine rises and less folate is available to make thymidine, so DNA synthesis slows and cells become megaloblastic. Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase uses B12 alone to turn methylmalonyl-CoA into succinyl-CoA. In B12 deficiency, methylmalonic acid builds up, and abnormal fatty acids may be built into myelin, which helps explain nerve damage that folate deficiency does not cause.","whyNot":["Normal levels of both would argue against a true cellular deficiency of either vitamin.","High homocysteine with normal methylmalonic acid is the folate deficiency pattern.","Correct.","Methionine synthase needs B12, so B12 deficiency raises homocysteine as well as methylmalonic acid. A normal homocysteine would not be expected."],"study":{"topics":["B12 and folate enzymes","Methylmalonic acid and homocysteine"],"comps":["HE-5","HE-9-A1"]}},{"id":"HE-C03-Q3","stem":"A bone marrow aspirate shows a hypercellular marrow with large erythroid precursors. Late-stage precursors have pink, hemoglobin-filled cytoplasm but large nuclei with fine, open chromatin. Which of the following best explains the appearance of these precursors?","options":["Hemoglobin synthesis lagging behind nuclear maturation","Nuclear maturation lagging behind hemoglobin synthesis","Early nuclear extrusion before hemoglobin accumulates","Ribosomal RNA retained after nuclear extrusion"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.4","dok":3,"approach":["Normal erythroid maturation runs from proerythroblast to orthochromatic erythroblast: the nucleus shrinks and condenses while hemoglobin fills the cytoplasm, and then the nucleus is extruded.","Here the cytoplasm has matured (pink and full of hemoglobin) while the nucleus still looks young.","B12 deficiency slows DNA synthesis but not RNA and protein synthesis, so the nucleus falls behind."],"why":"As erythroid cells mature, they get smaller, the nucleus condenses and is pushed out, and hemoglobin builds up until the cytoplasm turns from blue to pink. The cell leaves the marrow as a reticulocyte that still has some ribosomal RNA, which it loses in about a day in the blood. In B12 or folate deficiency, DNA synthesis is slow, so nuclei stay large and immature while hemoglobin synthesis goes on. Many of these megaloblasts die in the marrow, which is why the marrow is full but the reticulocyte count is low. A high reticulocyte count would instead mean the marrow is responding well, as after bleeding or hemolysis.","whyNot":["A cytoplasm that lags behind the nucleus is the pattern of iron deficiency, where hemoglobin synthesis is limited and cells are small.","Correct.","Nuclear extrusion needs a small, condensed nucleus. These nuclei are large and open, so extrusion is delayed, not early.","Retained ribosomal RNA gives reticulocytes their blue-gray color, or causes basophilic stippling when it clumps. It does not explain large, immature nuclei in marrow precursors."],"study":{"topics":["Erythroid maturation","Megaloblastic change"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-9-A1"]}},{"id":"HE-C03-Q4","stem":"Her antibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor cause disease through the same type of hypersensitivity reaction as which of the following?","options":["Anaphylaxis to plasma in IgA deficiency","Mixed cryoglobulinemia with vasculitis","Serum sickness after antitoxin infusion","Hemolytic disease of the newborn"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-7","obj":"HE-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["Pernicious anemia is caused by antibodies against antigens on a cell or a specific tissue protein, which makes it a type II reaction.","Find the other choice in which antibody binds an antigen fixed on cells.","In hemolytic disease of the newborn, maternal IgG binds antigens on fetal red cells."],"why":"Type II hypersensitivity is antibody directed against antigens on cells or in tissue, which causes cell destruction, inflammation, or loss of function. In pernicious anemia, antibodies target the parietal cell proton pump and block intrinsic factor, along with T cell injury to the gastric glands. Hemolytic disease of the newborn, acute hemolytic transfusion reactions, and autoimmune hemolytic anemia are the hematologic type II reactions. Type III reactions, such as cryoglobulinemia and serum sickness, come from circulating immune complexes that deposit in vessel walls.","whyNot":["Anaphylaxis in IgA-deficient patients is classed as a type I reaction. The patient's anti-IgA antibodies react with IgA in donor plasma within minutes.","Cryoglobulins are immune complexes that precipitate in the cold and deposit in small vessels. This is a type III reaction, often linked to hepatitis C.","Serum sickness is a type III reaction caused by immune complexes against a foreign protein, with fever, rash, and joint pain 1 to 2 weeks after exposure.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types","Autoimmune gastritis"],"comps":["HE-7","HE-9-A1"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C04","system":"HE","dx":"Hereditary spherocytosis with parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis","codes":["HE-9-B1"],"case":"An 8-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a 3-day history of fatigue and pallor. He had fever and a runny nose 1 week ago, and his younger sister recently had a bright red rash on both cheeks. He has had jaundice with past infections, and his father had a splenectomy as a teenager. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 98/58 mm Hg. Examination shows pallor, scleral icterus, and splenomegaly. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 5.0 g/dL (11.5 to 15.5), MCHC of 37.8 g/dL (32 to 36), and reticulocyte count of 0.2% (0.5 to 2.5). A peripheral blood smear shows many small, round erythrocytes without central pallor. A direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is negative. Diagnosis: hereditary spherocytosis with parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C04-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the shape of his erythrocytes and his high MCHC?","options":["Membrane loss from weak cytoskeletal anchoring","Oxidative precipitation of hemoglobin as inclusions","Polymerization of hemoglobin when deoxygenated","IgG coating with partial phagocytosis by macrophages"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B1","obj":"HE-9-B.4","dok":3,"approach":["A negative Coombs test excludes antibody-coated cells, and a father who needed splenectomy points to an inherited membrane defect.","Defects in ankyrin, spectrin, band 3, or protein 4.2 weaken the links between the lipid bilayer and the cytoskeleton.","Unsupported membrane is lost as small vesicles, so the cell keeps its hemoglobin but loses surface and becomes a dense sphere."],"why":"In hereditary spherocytosis, usually autosomal dominant, a defect in ankyrin, spectrin, band 3, or protein 4.2 weakens the vertical links between the membrane and the spectrin skeleton. Bits of membrane bleb off, so surface area falls while hemoglobin content stays the same, and the cells become dense spheres with a high MCHC and no central pallor. Spheres cannot bend through the splenic cords, so they are trapped and eaten, which causes splenomegaly, jaundice, and pigment gallstones. Increased osmotic fragility or reduced eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) binding on flow cytometry confirms the diagnosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Oxidative precipitation of hemoglobin forms Heinz bodies and bite cells in G6PD deficiency.","Polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin produces sickle cells, not spherocytes.","Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia also makes spherocytes, through partial phagocytosis of IgG-coated cells, but the direct Coombs test would be positive."],"study":{"topics":["Hereditary spherocytosis","Red cell membrane skeleton"],"comps":["HE-9-B1","HE-2"]}},{"id":"HE-C04-Q2","stem":"Which of the following characteristics of the causal agent best explains the sudden fall in his reticulocyte count?","options":["Enveloped DNA virus binding CD21 on mature B cells","Nonenveloped DNA virus binding P antigen on erythroid cells","Enveloped RNA virus binding CD4 on helper T lymphocytes","Nonenveloped RNA virus binding ICAM-1 on respiratory epithelium"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-8","obj":"HE-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["A cold-like illness in him and a red rash on his sister's cheeks point to parvovirus B19 (erythema infectiosum).","Parvovirus B19 is a small, nonenveloped, single-stranded DNA virus that enters cells through the P antigen (globoside) on erythroid progenitors.","It kills these progenitors and stops red cell production for about a week, which matters greatly when red cells survive only days to weeks."],"why":"Parvovirus B19 replicates in dividing erythroid progenitors, which carry its receptor, the P blood group antigen. Infection halts red cell production for about a week. A healthy person barely notices, because red cells live about 120 days. In chronic hemolysis, such as spherocytosis or sickle cell disease, red cells live only days to weeks, so a short pause causes a sharp fall in hemoglobin with almost no reticulocytes, called transient aplastic crisis. In a fetus, the same block can cause severe anemia, heart failure, and hydrops fetalis.","whyNot":["Epstein-Barr virus is an enveloped DNA virus that binds CD21 on B cells and causes mononucleosis, not erythroid failure.","Correct.","HIV is an enveloped RNA virus that binds CD4. It can cause cytopenias over time, but not a sudden halt in red cell production after a cold.","Rhinovirus is a nonenveloped RNA virus that binds ICAM-1 and causes the common cold without marrow effects."],"study":{"topics":["Parvovirus B19","Transient aplastic crisis"],"comps":["HE-8","HE-9-B1"]}},{"id":"HE-C04-Q3","stem":"Most of the carbon dioxide that his red cells pick up in systemic capillaries is carried to the lungs by which of the following mechanisms?","options":["Carbonic anhydrase and the chloride shift","Simple diffusion of dissolved CO2 into the plasma","Carbamino binding of CO2 to plasma proteins","Binding of CO2 to the ferrous iron of heme groups"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-2","obj":"HE-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["About 70% of CO2 travels as bicarbonate, about 20% bound to hemoglobin as carbamino compounds, and about 10% dissolved.","Red cells contain carbonic anhydrase, which turns CO2 and water into carbonic acid that splits into H+ and bicarbonate.","Band 3 exchanges bicarbonate out of the cell for chloride (the chloride shift), so most CO2 rides in plasma as bicarbonate."],"why":"Red cells are built for gas transport. The biconcave shape gives a large surface for diffusion and lets the cell bend through capillaries, and having no nucleus or mitochondria leaves room for hemoglobin and means the cell uses none of the oxygen it carries. Carbonic anhydrase inside the cell converts CO2 to bicarbonate, and band 3 (anion exchanger 1) moves bicarbonate into plasma in exchange for chloride. Deoxyhemoglobin buffers the H+ that is released, which also helps unload oxygen in the tissues.","whyNot":["Correct.","Dissolved CO2 carries only about 10% of the total.","Carbamino compounds form mainly with hemoglobin inside red cells, not with plasma proteins, and they carry a minority of CO2.","CO2 does not bind heme iron. It binds the amino ends of globin chains as carbaminohemoglobin, which carries a smaller share. Carbon monoxide is the gas that binds heme iron."],"study":{"topics":["Carbon dioxide transport","Red cell structure and function"],"comps":["HE-2","HE-5"]}},{"id":"HE-C04-Q4","stem":"Over the next 2 years, his family does not fill his prescription for folic acid. At a follow-up visit, he takes no medications and eats fruit and vegetables daily. His hemoglobin concentration is 7.8 g/dL (11.5 to 15.5), MCV is 104 fL (80 to 100), and reticulocyte count is 1.2% (0.5 to 2.5). Neutrophils with six nuclear lobes are seen. Serum folate concentration is low, and serum vitamin B12 concentration is within the reference range. Which of the following best explains his folate deficiency?","options":["Inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase by a drug","Methyl-folate trapping from vitamin B12 deficiency","Folate demand of a hyperactive marrow","Poor dietary intake of folate-rich foods"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-A1","obj":"HE-9-A.5","dok":3,"approach":["A high MCV with hypersegmented neutrophils, low folate, and normal B12 means megaloblastic anemia from folate deficiency.","He eats a good diet and takes no drugs, so look at demand.","Chronic hemolysis keeps the marrow making several times the normal number of red cells, and every new cell needs folate for DNA synthesis."],"why":"Folate stores last only about 3 to 4 months, so deficiency develops quickly when demand rises. Chronic hemolytic anemias, pregnancy, and other states of rapid cell turnover increase folate use, which is why patients with spherocytosis or sickle cell disease take folic acid. Other causes are poor intake, alcohol use, and drugs such as methotrexate. Macrocytosis from reticulocytosis alone, liver disease, or alcohol is nonmegaloblastic: neutrophils are not hypersegmented, and folate and B12 are normal.","whyNot":["Methotrexate and trimethoprim inhibit dihydrofolate reductase, but he takes no medications.","Methyl-folate trapping happens when B12 is low, because methionine synthase cannot free tetrahydrofolate. His B12 is normal.","Correct.","Poor intake is a common cause, especially with alcohol use disorder, but he eats fruit and vegetables daily."],"study":{"topics":["Folate deficiency","Megaloblastic versus nonmegaloblastic macrocytosis"],"comps":["HE-9-A1","HE-5"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C05","system":"HE","dx":"Acquired aplastic anemia","codes":["HE-9-A1"],"case":"A 23-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 2-week history of easy bruising, bleeding gums, and fatigue, and 1 day of fever. Three months ago, he had acute hepatitis with negative tests for hepatitis A, B, and C viruses. He takes no medications. Temperature is 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 112/68 mm Hg. Examination shows pallor, petechiae on the legs, and scattered ecchymoses, with no lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 7.0 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), absolute neutrophil count of 250/mm3 (1,500 to 8,000), platelet count of 11,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000), and reticulocyte count of 0.1% (0.5 to 2.5). Bone marrow biopsy shows less than 10% cellularity, mostly fat, without fibrosis or abnormal cells. Diagnosis: acquired aplastic anemia.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C05-Q1","stem":"Treatment with antithymocyte globulin and cyclosporine is followed by a gradual rise in all of his blood counts. Which of the following mechanisms best explains his disorder?","options":["Cytotoxic T cell injury to hematopoietic stem cells","Marrow fibrosis driven by megakaryocyte growth factors","Clonal dysplastic hematopoiesis with ineffective maturation","Splenic sequestration of circulating blood cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-A1","obj":"HE-9-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Pancytopenia with an empty, fatty marrow and no splenomegaly means stem cell failure.","Seronegative hepatitis is a known trigger, along with drugs, benzene, radiation, and inherited Fanconi anemia.","Recovery with drugs that suppress T cells shows that activated cytotoxic T cells were destroying the stem cells."],"why":"Most acquired aplastic anemia is immune mediated. Activated cytotoxic T cells, often after a trigger such as hepatitis or a drug, release interferon gamma and TNF and destroy hematopoietic stem cells. All three lines fall, the reticulocyte count is very low, and the marrow is replaced by fat. The spleen is not enlarged, which helps separate aplastic anemia from cytopenias caused by sequestration or infiltration. Immunosuppression with antithymocyte globulin and cyclosporine works in many patients, and stem cell transplant can cure it.","whyNot":["Correct.","Fibrosis driven by megakaryocyte growth factors is primary myelofibrosis, with a dry tap, teardrop cells, and a large spleen.","Clonal, ineffective hematopoiesis is myelodysplastic syndrome, which usually shows a hypercellular marrow with dysplastic cells.","Sequestration (hypersplenism) needs an enlarged spleen and leaves the marrow cellular. His spleen is normal and his marrow is empty."],"study":{"topics":["Aplastic anemia","Causes of pancytopenia"],"comps":["HE-9-A1","HE-9-D"]}},{"id":"HE-C05-Q2","stem":"A 6-year-old girl seen in the same clinic has pancytopenia, short stature, café-au-lait spots, and absent thumbs. Her lymphocytes show many chromosome breaks after exposure to diepoxybutane. Which of the following defects most likely underlies her marrow failure?","options":["Defective mismatch repair of replication errors","Telomere shortening from telomerase mutations","Defective repair of DNA interstrand crosslinks","Defective nucleotide excision of pyrimidine dimers"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-6","obj":"HE-6.6","dok":2,"approach":["Congenital marrow failure with short stature and thumb and radial anomalies points to Fanconi anemia.","Increased breakage after a DNA crosslinking agent (diepoxybutane or mitomycin C) is the diagnostic test.","The Fanconi anemia proteins work together to repair interstrand crosslinks."],"why":"Fanconi anemia is usually autosomal recessive and is caused by mutations in any of the FANC genes, whose proteins repair DNA interstrand crosslinks. Without this repair, dividing cells accumulate DNA damage, and hematopoietic stem cells are lost over childhood, which leads to marrow failure. Patients often have short stature, thumb and radial anomalies, and skin pigment changes. Their genomic instability raises the risk of acute myeloid leukemia and of squamous cell cancers of the head and neck.","whyNot":["Defective mismatch repair causes Lynch syndrome, with colorectal and endometrial cancer, not congenital marrow failure.","Telomerase mutations cause dyskeratosis congenita, another inherited marrow failure, but with nail dystrophy, oral leukoplakia, and lacy skin pigment, and without crosslink-induced breakage.","Correct.","Defective nucleotide excision repair of pyrimidine dimers is xeroderma pigmentosum, with sun sensitivity and skin cancers."],"study":{"topics":["Fanconi anemia","DNA repair defects"],"comps":["HE-6","HE-9-A1"]}},{"id":"HE-C05-Q3","stem":"A 45-year-old woman receives high-dose chemotherapy that abruptly stops production in her bone marrow. Complete blood counts are obtained daily over the next 4 weeks, with no bleeding or transfusions. Which of the following sequences of decline is most likely?","options":["Erythrocytes first, then platelets, then neutrophils","Platelets first, then neutrophils, then erythrocytes","Neutrophils first, then platelets, then erythrocytes","Platelets first, then erythrocytes, then neutrophils"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-3","obj":"HE-3.5","dok":3,"approach":["When production stops, each cell type falls at a rate set by how long it lives in the blood.","Neutrophils circulate for only hours, platelets for about 7 to 10 days, and red cells for about 120 days.","So neutropenia appears first, thrombocytopenia next, and anemia last."],"why":"Blood counts reflect a balance between production and removal. Neutrophils stay in the blood for only several hours before moving into tissues, so a stop in production causes neutropenia within days. Platelets survive about 7 to 10 days, so they fall next. Red cells live about 120 days, so anemia develops slowly unless there is also bleeding or hemolysis. This is why fever from infection and bleeding from low platelets are often the first signs of sudden marrow failure, as after chemotherapy.","whyNot":["Red cells live the longest, so they fall last, not first.","Platelets live about 7 to 10 days, much longer than neutrophils, so they cannot fall before neutrophils.","Correct.","Neutrophils have the shortest life span in the blood, so they fall first, and red cells have the longest, so they fall last."],"study":{"topics":["Blood cell life spans","Marrow failure timeline"],"comps":["HE-3","HE-9-A1"]}},{"id":"HE-C05-Q4","stem":"A 71-year-old woman also has pancytopenia. She received cyclophosphamide and radiation therapy for breast cancer 6 years ago. A bone marrow biopsy shows a hypercellular marrow with ringed sideroblasts, neutrophils with bilobed nuclei and sparse granules, and blasts making up 6% of nucleated cells. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Acute myeloid leukemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Acquired aplastic anemia","Vitamin B12 deficiency anemia"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D","obj":"HE-9-D.11","dok":2,"approach":["Pancytopenia with a hypercellular marrow means ineffective production, not stem cell loss.","Dysplastic cells (pseudo-Pelger-Huet neutrophils and ringed sideroblasts) after an alkylating agent and radiation point to a clonal disorder.","Blasts are increased but below 20%, which fits myelodysplastic syndrome rather than acute myeloid leukemia."],"why":"Myelodysplastic syndromes are clonal stem cell disorders of older adults, or of people treated years earlier with alkylating agents or radiation. The marrow is usually full, but the cells mature abnormally and many die before release, so the blood shows cytopenias. Typical dysplastic changes are bilobed, hypogranular neutrophils (pseudo-Pelger-Huet cells), ringed sideroblasts, and abnormal megakaryocytes. Blasts are below 20%, and the disease can progress to acute myeloid leukemia.","whyNot":["Acute myeloid leukemia needs blasts of at least 20% in most cases. Her blasts are 6%, although she is at risk of progressing to it.","Correct.","Aplastic anemia has an empty, fatty marrow without dysplasia. Her marrow is hypercellular.","B12 deficiency gives a hypercellular marrow, but neutrophils are hypersegmented, not bilobed, and blasts and ringed sideroblasts are not increased."],"study":{"topics":["Myelodysplastic syndromes","Therapy-related myeloid neoplasms"],"comps":["HE-9-D","HE-9-D1"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C06","system":"HE","dx":"Polycythemia vera with Budd-Chiari syndrome","codes":["HE-9-A2"],"case":"A 54-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of a 3-day history of right upper quadrant abdominal pain and increasing abdominal girth. For 1 year, she has had headaches and itching after warm showers. She does not smoke. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 148/88 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 97% on room air. Examination shows facial plethora, tender hepatomegaly, shifting dullness, and splenomegaly. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 19.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), hematocrit of 59% (36 to 46), leukocyte count of 14,200/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000), platelet count of 640,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000), and serum erythropoietin concentration of 1.8 mU/mL (4 to 24). Doppler ultrasonography shows thrombosis of the hepatic veins. A JAK2 V617F mutation is detected. Diagnosis: polycythemia vera with Budd-Chiari syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C06-Q1","stem":"A 63-year-old man has a hematocrit of 57% (41 to 53). His oxygen saturation is 98% on room air, and a CT scan shows a 6-cm mass in the left kidney. Compared with the patient in the case, which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this man?","options":["High erythropoietin, low arterial oxygen saturation","Normal red cell mass with reduced plasma volume","Low erythropoietin and a JAK2 V617F mutation","High erythropoietin, normal oxygen saturation"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-A2","obj":"HE-9-A.7","dok":3,"approach":["A renal mass with polycythemia suggests renal cell carcinoma secreting erythropoietin.","Tumor erythropoietin is made without any hypoxic signal, so it is inappropriately high while oxygen saturation is normal.","In polycythemia vera, a JAK2-mutant clone grows on its own, so erythropoietin is low."],"why":"Polycythemia is sorted by erythropoietin and oxygen status. In polycythemia vera, a JAK2 V617F clone makes red cells without erythropoietin, and the high red cell mass suppresses erythropoietin to low levels. Secondary polycythemia has high erythropoietin. It is appropriate when tissue oxygen is low (high altitude, lung disease, smoking with carbon monoxide) and inappropriate when a tumor such as renal cell or hepatocellular carcinoma makes it. Relative polycythemia comes from a low plasma volume, as in dehydration, with a normal red cell mass.","whyNot":["High erythropoietin with low oxygen saturation is the appropriate response to hypoxia, as in chronic lung disease or high altitude. His saturation is normal.","A normal red cell mass with low plasma volume is relative polycythemia from dehydration, which does not fit a hormone-secreting renal mass.","Low erythropoietin with a JAK2 mutation is polycythemia vera, the patient in the case, not a tumor making erythropoietin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Polycythemia vera versus secondary polycythemia","Paraneoplastic erythropoietin"],"comps":["HE-9-A2","HE-2"]}},{"id":"HE-C06-Q2","stem":"Eight months later, she has sudden severe pain, redness, and swelling of the right first metatarsophalangeal joint. Joint fluid shows needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals. Which of the following best explains this complication?","options":["Platelet-mediated microvascular occlusion of the toe","Purine overproduction from rapid cell turnover","Septic embolism from an infected hepatic thrombus","Reduced urate excretion from hepatic congestion"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-A2","obj":"HE-9-A.8","dok":2,"approach":["Needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals are monosodium urate, so this is gout.","Polycythemia vera expands all three myeloid lines, and the large number of cells being made and destroyed breaks down a large amount of nucleic acid.","Purine breakdown produces uric acid, which raises serum urate."],"why":"The complications of polycythemia vera follow from three features. Hyperviscosity and clonal platelets cause thrombosis, such as stroke, myocardial infarction, Budd-Chiari syndrome, and erythromelalgia (burning, red hands or feet from platelet plugs in small vessels). High cell turnover raises uric acid and causes gout. Over years, the marrow can scar and progress to myelofibrosis (spent phase), and some patients develop acute myeloid leukemia.","whyNot":["Platelet plugs in small vessels cause erythromelalgia, a burning red extremity, but they do not produce urate crystals in joint fluid.","Correct.","A septic joint would show bacteria and many neutrophils, not urate crystals, and infected hepatic vein thrombi are not a feature of this disease.","The liver does not excrete urate, and her renal function is not described as impaired. The problem is overproduction."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of polycythemia vera","Secondary gout"],"comps":["HE-9-A2"]}},{"id":"HE-C06-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains her low serum erythropoietin concentration?","options":["Hepatocytes suppressed by hepatic venous outflow obstruction","Renal juxtaglomerular cells suppressed by high blood volume","Renal peritubular cells injured by microvascular thrombi","Renal peritubular cells sensing high oxygen delivery"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-2","obj":"HE-2.4","dok":3,"approach":["Erythropoietin is made mainly by peritubular interstitial cells of the renal cortex, with a small hepatic share.","These cells sense oxygen through HIF: when oxygen delivery falls, HIF escapes degradation and turns on the erythropoietin gene.","Her red cell mass is high, so oxygen delivery to the kidney is high and erythropoietin is switched off, while the JAK2-mutant clone keeps growing without it."],"why":"Erythropoietin production follows tissue oxygen, not the red cell count itself. Peritubular interstitial cells in the renal cortex sense oxygen through hypoxia-inducible factor, which is broken down when oxygen is plentiful. Erythropoietin is high at high altitude and in hypoxic lung disease. It is low in chronic kidney disease because the producing cells are lost, and low in polycythemia vera because the high red cell mass delivers plenty of oxygen while the clone grows without the hormone.","whyNot":["The liver makes only a small share of erythropoietin after birth, so hepatic vein thrombosis cannot explain a level this low. Her low level reflects renal suppression.","Juxtaglomerular cells make renin, not erythropoietin.","Nothing suggests renal infarction, and her low erythropoietin is explained by normal suppression of healthy cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Erythropoietin regulation","Hypoxia-inducible factor"],"comps":["HE-2","HE-9-A2"]}},{"id":"HE-C06-Q4","stem":"Her husband comes to the emergency department in winter because of headache, dizziness, and nausea that began after he ran a gas heater in a closed garage. His PaO2 is 96 mm Hg (75 to 100), and pulse oximetry reads 98%. Carboxyhemoglobin is 22% (less than 3). Which of the following best explains his reduced oxygen delivery to tissues despite a normal PaO2?","options":["Less oxygen content and a left-shifted curve","A right-shifted curve that impairs oxygen loading","Oxidation of heme iron to the ferric state","Low dissolved oxygen limiting hemoglobin loading"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-5","obj":"HE-5.4","dok":3,"approach":["PaO2 measures dissolved oxygen, which is normal because his lungs work normally.","Carbon monoxide binds heme with about 200 times the affinity of oxygen, so fewer sites carry oxygen and oxygen content falls.","CO on some sites makes the remaining sites hold oxygen more tightly, shifting the curve left, so less oxygen is released in tissues."],"why":"Oxygen content depends mostly on hemoglobin saturation, not PaO2. Carbon monoxide occupies heme sites, so content falls even though PaO2 stays normal, and standard pulse oximetry reads carboxyhemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin, so the saturation looks falsely normal. CO also shifts the dissociation curve to the left, which further reduces unloading. Methemoglobin acts in a similar way: ferric iron cannot carry oxygen and the curve shifts left. Methemoglobinemia can be congenital (cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency) or acquired from nitrites, dapsone, or benzocaine.","whyNot":["Correct.","Carbon monoxide shifts the curve to the left, not the right. A right shift would actually help unloading in tissues.","Oxidation of heme iron to the ferric state is methemoglobinemia, caused by nitrites, dapsone, or benzocaine. His history and carboxyhemoglobin level point to carbon monoxide.","His PaO2 is normal, so dissolved oxygen and loading in the lungs are not the problem."],"study":{"topics":["Carbon monoxide poisoning","Methemoglobinemia"],"comps":["HE-5"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C07","system":"HE","dx":"Splenic rupture after blunt trauma during infectious mononucleosis","codes":["HE-9-B","HE-7"],"case":"A 19-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 40 minutes after he was tackled during a football game. He has left upper quadrant pain that spreads to the left shoulder. Ten days ago, he was diagnosed with infectious mononucleosis after 1 week of fever, sore throat, and fatigue; a heterophile antibody test was positive. Temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F), pulse is 128/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 86/52 mm Hg. Examination shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and left upper quadrant tenderness with guarding. A peripheral blood smear shows many large lymphocytes with abundant cytoplasm that wraps around adjacent erythrocytes. A CT scan shows a splenic laceration with hemoperitoneum, and an emergency splenectomy is done. Diagnosis: traumatic splenic rupture during infectious mononucleosis.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C07-Q1","stem":"A peripheral blood smear obtained 3 weeks after surgery is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Erythrocytes with small round nuclear remnants","Fragmented erythrocytes with sharp angular edges","Teardrop erythrocytes with circulating nucleated cells","Erythrocytes with diffuse coarse blue granules"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B","obj":"HE-9-B.11","dok":2,"approach":["The spleen normally removes inclusions from red cells as they squeeze through the cords (pitting).","Without a spleen, small DNA remnants left after nuclear extrusion stay in circulating red cells.","These are Howell-Jolly bodies, and target cells and thrombocytosis are also common after splenectomy."],"why":"The red pulp filters the blood: red cells must squeeze from the cords into the sinusoids, and macrophages remove old cells and pluck out inclusions. After splenectomy, Howell-Jolly bodies (nuclear remnants), target cells, and sometimes Heinz bodies and acanthocytes appear, and the platelet count rises because the spleen normally holds about a third of platelets. The spleen can rupture after blunt trauma, especially when enlarged by mononucleosis. It can also infarct in sickle cell disease or from emboli, and septic emboli from endocarditis can cause a splenic abscess.","whyNot":["Correct.","Schistocytes come from mechanical damage, as in microangiopathy or prosthetic valves, not from loss of the spleen.","Teardrop cells with nucleated cells (a leukoerythroblastic picture) point to marrow fibrosis or infiltration.","Diffuse coarse blue granules are basophilic stippling, from retained ribosomal RNA in lead poisoning or thalassemia."],"study":{"topics":["Post-splenectomy blood smear","Splenic rupture"],"comps":["HE-9-B","HE-1"]}},{"id":"HE-C07-Q2","stem":"Flow cytometry of the large atypical lymphocytes on his smear would most likely identify which of the following cells?","options":["Plasma cells secreting heterophile antibody","B cells infected through the CD21 receptor","CD8 T cells reacting to infected B cells","CD56-positive natural killer cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-7","obj":"HE-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Epstein-Barr virus enters B cells through CD21, the receptor for complement C3d.","Infected B cells multiply and present viral antigens, which drives a large cytotoxic T cell response.","The atypical lymphocytes (Downey cells) are mostly these activated CD8 T cells."],"why":"Epstein-Barr virus infects pharyngeal epithelium and B cells, entering B cells through CD21. Infected B cells proliferate and make polyclonal antibodies, including the heterophile antibodies detected by the monospot test. Cytotoxic CD8 T cells expand to control the infection, and they appear in the blood as large atypical lymphocytes and enlarge the nodes, liver, and spleen. When T cell control is weak or the B cells acquire other changes, EBV contributes to Burkitt lymphoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, and lymphomas in people who are immunosuppressed.","whyNot":["Infected B cells make heterophile antibody, but plasma cells are rarely seen in the blood and do not look like these cells.","EBV does infect B cells through CD21, but infected B cells are a small fraction of blood lymphocytes. The atypical cells are the T cells reacting to them.","Correct.","Natural killer cells do respond to EBV, but they are not the main atypical lymphocyte population."],"study":{"topics":["Epstein-Barr virus","Atypical lymphocytes"],"comps":["HE-7","HE-9-D2"]}},{"id":"HE-C07-Q3","stem":"On arrival, before intravenous fluids are given, his hemoglobin concentration is 14.1 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5). Which of the following best explains this value?","options":["Red cells released from the contracting spleen","Loss of plasma and red cells in equal proportion","Hemoconcentration from vomiting and fever","Rapid erythropoietin-driven reticulocyte release"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-9-A","obj":"HE-9-A.9","dok":3,"approach":["In acute hemorrhage, whole blood is lost, so plasma and red cells leave together.","The concentration of hemoglobin in the blood that remains is unchanged at first.","Hemoglobin falls only as fluid moves from the interstitium into vessels, or as IV fluids are given, over hours to days."],"why":"Hemoglobin is a concentration, so it does not reflect acute blood loss until plasma volume is restored. Over the next hours to about 2 to 3 days, interstitial fluid shifts into the vessels and dilutes the remaining red cells. Erythropoietin rises, and the reticulocyte count increases after about 3 to 5 days, peaking at about 1 week. Iron stores are normal after a single bleed, but slow, chronic blood loss drains iron and leads to iron deficiency anemia.","whyNot":["The human spleen holds few red cells, and his spleen has ruptured.","Correct.","Nothing suggests vomiting, and a low-grade fever does not concentrate the blood enough to explain the finding.","Erythropoietin raises the reticulocyte count only after several days, not within the first hour."],"study":{"topics":["Acute blood loss","Reticulocyte response"],"comps":["HE-9-A","HE-2"]}},{"id":"HE-C07-Q4","stem":"An enlarged posterior cervical lymph node was biopsied before his injury. Expansion of which of the following regions of the node is most consistent with the predominant lymphocyte response seen on his smear?","options":["Subcapsular and medullary sinuses","Medullary cords with plasma cells","Paracortex deep to the follicles","Cortical follicles with germinal centers"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.7","dok":3,"approach":["The predominant response in mononucleosis is a large expansion of activated T cells.","In the lymph node, T cells live in the paracortex, between and below the B cell follicles, where high endothelial venules bring them in.","So the T cell response expands the paracortex."],"why":"Each lymph node has B cell follicles in the outer cortex, a T cell paracortex beneath them, and a medulla of cords (plasma cells and macrophages) and sinuses that drain lymph. In the spleen, T cells sit in the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath, B cells in the follicles and marginal zone, and the red pulp cords and sinusoids filter red cells. In mononucleosis, the T cell response expands the nodal paracortex with immunoblasts. The same reaction infiltrates the spleen and stretches its thin capsule, which is why it can rupture with minor trauma.","whyNot":["Sinuses carry lymph and contain macrophages, and expanded sinuses (sinus histiocytosis) are typical of nodes draining tumors.","Medullary cords hold plasma cells and macrophages, which are not the dominant reacting cells here.","Correct.","Follicles with germinal centers are B cell areas. Follicular hyperplasia can occur, but it does not reflect the T cell response that dominates his smear."],"study":{"topics":["Lymph node structure","Spleen structure"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-7"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C08","system":"HE","dx":"Chronic myeloid leukemia, chronic phase","codes":["HE-9-D1"],"case":"A 47-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of fatigue, night sweats, early satiety, and a dragging sensation in the left upper abdomen. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 124/76 mm Hg. Examination shows a firm, nontender spleen palpable 12 cm below the left costal margin. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 10.8 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), leukocyte count of 172,000/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000), and platelet count of 520,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000). A peripheral blood smear shows granulocytes at every stage of maturation, with many myelocytes and segmented neutrophils, 7% basophils (0 to 1), and 2% blasts. Fluorescence in situ hybridization detects a BCR-ABL1 fusion gene. Diagnosis: chronic myeloid leukemia, chronic phase.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C08-Q1","stem":"Four years later, despite treatment, she has fever, bone pain, and worsening anemia. Which of the following findings would best indicate that her disease has entered blast crisis?","options":["Basophils making up 7% of blood leukocytes","Granulocytes present at every stage of maturation","Platelet count above 450,000/mm3","Blasts making up 30% of marrow cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D1","obj":"HE-9-D.4","dok":2,"approach":["Chronic phase CML shows a full range of maturing granulocytes, basophilia, and few blasts.","Progression means the clone loses the ability to mature, so blasts increase.","Blasts of at least 20% in blood or marrow define blast crisis, which behaves like acute leukemia."],"why":"CML has a chronic phase that lasts years, in which the clone still matures, so the blood shows neutrophils at every stage, basophilia, and often thrombocytosis. Without effective treatment, more mutations build up and the disease can pass through an accelerated phase with rising blasts and basophils. It then reaches blast crisis, with at least 20% blasts in blood or marrow. Blast crisis is myeloid in about two thirds of patients and lymphoid in about one third, and it behaves like acute leukemia.","whyNot":["Basophilia of this degree is already present in chronic phase, as it was at her diagnosis.","A full range of maturing granulocytes is the chronic phase picture, not blast crisis.","Thrombocytosis is common in chronic phase and was present at diagnosis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of CML","Blast crisis"],"comps":["HE-9-D1","HE-6"]}},{"id":"HE-C08-Q2","stem":"The fusion gene detected in her cells drives granulocyte proliferation by which of the following mechanisms?","options":["Fusion transcription factor blocking differentiation","Constitutive cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase activity","Overexpression of an antiapoptotic mitochondrial protein","Loss of a cell cycle checkpoint tumor suppressor"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-6","obj":"HE-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["BCR-ABL1 forms from t(9;22), the Philadelphia chromosome, which joins BCR on chromosome 22 to ABL1 on chromosome 9.","ABL1 is a tyrosine kinase that is normally tightly regulated.","The fusion protein is always active, so growth signals stay on without growth factors, and maturation still proceeds."],"why":"The BCR-ABL1 fusion protein is a tyrosine kinase that is always switched on. It activates RAS, JAK-STAT, and PI3K pathways, so myeloid progenitors grow and survive without normal growth factor control, while their ability to mature is kept. That is why the blood fills with maturing granulocytes. Imatinib and related drugs block the kinase. A similar idea applies to polycythemia vera, where JAK2 V617F keeps JAK2 signaling on without erythropoietin.","whyNot":["A fusion transcription factor that blocks maturation is PML-RARA from t(15;17) in acute promyelocytic leukemia. CML cells still mature.","Correct.","Overexpression of the antiapoptotic protein BCL2 follows t(14;18) in follicular lymphoma.","Loss of a tumor suppressor such as TP53 or RB is a different mechanism and is not caused by t(9;22)."],"study":{"topics":["BCR-ABL1","Tyrosine kinase oncogenes"],"comps":["HE-6","HE-9-D1"]}},{"id":"HE-C08-Q3","stem":"Before the genetic result was available, a leukemoid reaction to severe infection was considered. Which of the following additional findings would most strongly favor chronic myeloid leukemia?","options":["Toxic granulation and Döhle bodies in neutrophils","High leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score","Neutrophilia with increased band forms","Low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-A","obj":"HE-9-A.11","dok":3,"approach":["Both conditions can cause a very high leukocyte count with immature granulocytes in the blood.","In a leukemoid reaction, normal neutrophils are activated and rich in leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP).","CML neutrophils come from the malignant clone and have low LAP activity."],"why":"A leukemoid reaction is a benign, very high neutrophil count, often above 50,000/mm3, in response to severe infection, inflammation, or tumors. The neutrophils are activated, so they show toxic granulation, Döhle bodies, and a high LAP score, and there is a left shift with many bands. CML shows a low LAP score, basophilia, and often eosinophilia and splenomegaly. The BCR-ABL1 fusion gene confirms CML.","whyNot":["Toxic granulation and Döhle bodies are signs of activated neutrophils in infection and favor a leukemoid reaction.","A high LAP score favors a leukemoid reaction. It can also be high in polycythemia vera.","A left shift with many bands occurs in both conditions and does not separate them.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Leukemoid reaction","Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase"],"comps":["HE-9-A","HE-9-D1"]}},{"id":"HE-C08-Q4","stem":"Her smear shows two peaks among the granulocytes: segmented neutrophils and a precursor that has a round nucleus, contains both primary (azurophilic) and secondary (specific) granules, and is the last stage still able to divide. Which of the following cells is this precursor?","options":["Myelocyte","Promyelocyte","Metamyelocyte","Band neutrophil"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.5","dok":2,"approach":["Granulocytes mature from myeloblast to promyelocyte, myelocyte, metamyelocyte, band, and segmented neutrophil.","Promyelocytes have only primary granules. Specific granules first appear in the myelocyte.","The myelocyte is the last stage that divides. From the metamyelocyte on, the cells only mature."],"why":"In granulocyte maturation, the myeloblast has fine chromatin and nucleoli and few or no granules. The promyelocyte makes primary (azurophilic) granules, and the myelocyte begins making specific granules and is the last stage that can divide. The metamyelocyte has an indented nucleus, the band has a curved, unsegmented nucleus, and the mature neutrophil has three to five lobes. A left shift means more bands and earlier forms in the blood, as in infection. CML often shows peaks of myelocytes and segmented neutrophils.","whyNot":["Correct.","Promyelocytes contain only primary granules. Specific granules have not yet formed.","Metamyelocytes have an indented, bean-shaped nucleus and can no longer divide.","Band neutrophils have a curved, unsegmented nucleus and do not divide."],"study":{"topics":["Granulocyte maturation","Left shift"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-9-D1"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C09","system":"HE","dx":"Sickle cell anemia with acute chest syndrome and functional asplenia","codes":["HE-9-B1"],"case":"A 14-year-old girl with sickle cell anemia (HbSS) is brought to the emergency department because of a 1-day history of fever, cough, and right-sided chest pain. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 30/min, and blood pressure is 108/64 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 89% on room air. Crackles are heard over the right lower lung field. No spleen is palpable. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin concentration of 6.4 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0; her baseline is 8.0) and reticulocyte count of 12% (0.5 to 2.5). A peripheral blood smear shows sickled erythrocytes, target cells, and Howell-Jolly bodies. A chest x-ray shows a new infiltrate in the right lower lobe. Diagnosis: sickle cell anemia with acute chest syndrome and functional asplenia.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C09-Q1","stem":"Her disease results from substitution of valine for glutamate at position 6 of the beta globin chain. Which of the following conditions during this illness most promotes polymerization of her hemoglobin?","options":["Hypoxemia from the consolidated lung","Dilution of red cell contents by hypotonic fluids","A higher proportion of fetal hemoglobin","Respiratory alkalosis from rapid breathing"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-6","obj":"HE-6.1","dok":3,"approach":["The valine at position 6 creates a hydrophobic patch that fits into a pocket on a neighboring HbS molecule, but only when the hemoglobin is deoxygenated.","Anything that raises the fraction or concentration of deoxygenated HbS speeds polymerization.","Her lung consolidation lowers oxygen saturation, which raises deoxy-HbS and drives more sickling in the lung, a vicious cycle."],"why":"HbS polymerizes into long fibers when it gives up oxygen, which distorts and stiffens red cells. Hypoxia, acidosis, fever, and high 2,3-BPG increase the deoxygenated fraction. Dehydration raises the concentration of HbS inside the cell. Fetal hemoglobin does not join the polymer and dilutes HbS, so a high HbF level protects. In acute chest syndrome, infection or fat emboli cause hypoxemia, which causes more sickling and vaso-occlusion in the lung.","whyNot":["Correct.","Hypotonic fluid swells the cells and lowers the HbS concentration, which slows polymerization.","Fetal hemoglobin does not take part in the polymer and dilutes HbS, so a higher proportion reduces sickling.","Alkalosis shifts the dissociation curve to the left, so more hemoglobin stays oxygenated and sickling falls."],"study":{"topics":["HbS polymerization","Factors that promote sickling"],"comps":["HE-6","HE-9-B1"]}},{"id":"HE-C09-Q2","stem":"Two years later, she has painless gross hematuria and passes a small piece of tissue in her urine. Which of the following best explains this complication?","options":["Immune complex deposition in glomeruli","Calcium oxalate stones from enteric hyperoxaluria","Medullary sickling with papillary ischemia","Urothelial carcinoma of the renal pelvis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B1","obj":"HE-9-B.1","dok":3,"approach":["The renal medulla has low oxygen tension, high osmolality, and low pH, which all favor sickling.","Sickled cells block the vasa recta that supply the papillae, causing ischemic necrosis.","A sloughed papilla passes in the urine with painless hematuria."],"why":"Sickle cell disease causes damage through vaso-occlusion and through hemolysis. Vaso-occlusion explains dactylitis, acute chest syndrome, stroke, splenic autoinfarction, priapism, and renal papillary necrosis. The renal medulla is the most sickling-prone site in the body, so even people with sickle cell trait can have hematuria and papillary necrosis. Chronic hemolysis leads to pigment gallstones and to aplastic crisis with parvovirus B19. Bone infarcts make Salmonella osteomyelitis more likely.","whyNot":["Immune complex glomerulonephritis gives hematuria with red cell casts and proteinuria, not sloughed tissue.","Calcium oxalate stones usually cause colicky pain, and enteric hyperoxaluria needs fat malabsorption.","Correct.","Urothelial carcinoma is rare at her age, and a tumor fragment would not be the expected cause in sickle cell disease."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of sickle cell disease","Renal papillary necrosis"],"comps":["HE-9-B1"]}},{"id":"HE-C09-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why she is at increased risk of sepsis from Streptococcus pneumoniae?","options":["Inherited deficiency of terminal complement components","Impaired neutrophil killing from chronic hemolysis","Defective T cell help from repeated transfusions","Loss of splenic IgM and macrophage clearance"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-7","obj":"HE-7.7","dok":3,"approach":["Howell-Jolly bodies and a nonpalpable spleen show she has lost splenic function from repeated infarcts.","Polysaccharide capsules resist phagocytosis unless the bacteria are coated with antibody and complement.","The spleen makes early IgM against capsules (marginal zone B cells) and its macrophages clear opsonized bacteria from the blood."],"why":"Encapsulated bacteria, such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, and Neisseria meningitidis, escape phagocytosis unless antibody and complement coat them. The spleen supplies both: marginal zone B cells make rapid IgM against capsular polysaccharides, and splenic macrophages remove opsonized bacteria from blood. In sickle cell anemia, repeated infarcts destroy splenic function in early childhood. Vaccines against these organisms create opsonizing antibodies to the capsule before exposure, and conjugate vaccines add T cell help so infants respond.","whyNot":["Terminal complement deficiency raises the risk of Neisseria infections, not pneumococcal sepsis in asplenia.","Chronic hemolysis does not impair neutrophil killing. Defective neutrophil killing is seen in chronic granulomatous disease.","Transfusions do not destroy T cell help. Her risk comes from loss of the spleen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Asplenia and encapsulated bacteria","Vaccines in asplenia"],"comps":["HE-7","HE-9-B1"]}},{"id":"HE-C09-Q4","stem":"She is started on hydroxyurea, which increases the proportion of her hemoglobin that is fetal hemoglobin. Compared with hemoglobin A, fetal hemoglobin has which of the following?","options":["Two alpha and two delta chains, and increased oxygen affinity","Two alpha and two gamma chains, and increased oxygen affinity","Four gamma chains, and increased oxygen affinity","Two alpha and two gamma chains, and decreased oxygen affinity"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-5","obj":"HE-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Adult hemoglobin A is two alpha and two beta chains. Hemoglobin A2 is two alpha and two delta chains. Fetal hemoglobin is two alpha and two gamma chains.","Gamma chains bind 2,3-BPG poorly, and 2,3-BPG normally lowers oxygen affinity.","So fetal hemoglobin has higher oxygen affinity, and its dissociation curve is shifted to the left."],"why":"Hemoglobin binds oxygen cooperatively: each oxygen that binds makes the next one bind more easily, which gives the sigmoid curve. 2,3-BPG, H+, CO2, and higher temperature lower affinity and shift the curve to the right, which helps unloading in active tissues. Fetal hemoglobin and carbon monoxide shift the curve to the left. The left shift lets the fetus draw oxygen from maternal blood. In sickle cell disease, gamma chains cannot join the HbS polymer, so more HbF means less sickling.","whyNot":["Two alpha and two delta chains form hemoglobin A2, a minor adult hemoglobin, not fetal hemoglobin.","Correct.","Four gamma chains form hemoglobin Barts, seen in alpha thalassemia when no alpha chains are made.","The chain makeup is right, but fetal hemoglobin binds oxygen more tightly than hemoglobin A, not less."],"study":{"topics":["Hemoglobin types","Oxygen dissociation curve"],"comps":["HE-5","HE-6"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C10","system":"HE","dx":"Transfusion-dependent beta thalassemia major","codes":["HE-9-B1"],"case":"A 7-year-old boy is brought to the physician for a scheduled visit. He was diagnosed with beta thalassemia major at 9 months of age and has received red cell transfusions every 3 to 4 weeks since then. He is short for his age. Vital signs are within normal limits. Examination shows frontal bossing, prominent maxillae, and hepatosplenomegaly. Laboratory studies before transfusion show a hemoglobin concentration of 8.1 g/dL (11.5 to 15.5) and a serum ferritin concentration of 3,400 ng/mL (7 to 140). A peripheral blood smear shows microcytic, hypochromic erythrocytes, target cells, and nucleated red cells. A skull x-ray shows a hair-on-end (crew-cut) pattern. Diagnosis: transfusion-dependent beta thalassemia major.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the ineffective erythropoiesis that drives his skeletal changes?","options":["Polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin","Antibody-mediated lysis of erythroid precursors","Precipitation of excess unpaired alpha chains","Deficiency of renal erythropoietin production"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B1","obj":"HE-9-B.2","dok":3,"approach":["In beta thalassemia major, little or no beta globin is made, but alpha chains are made normally.","Unpaired alpha chains are unstable and precipitate inside erythroid precursors, damaging their membranes.","Most precursors die in the marrow, so anemia drives erythropoietin up, and the marrow expands into the skull and facial bones."],"why":"Excess alpha chains precipitate in developing red cells, and most die in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis), while the few that escape are destroyed in the spleen. Severe anemia raises erythropoietin, so the marrow expands and thins the bones, which gives the crew-cut skull and maxillary overgrowth, and blood is made in the liver and spleen. Ineffective erythropoiesis lowers hepcidin, so gut iron absorption rises, and with transfusions this causes iron overload in the heart, liver, and endocrine glands. In beta thalassemia minor, the anemia is mild, and electrophoresis shows increased HbA2 (above about 3.5%).","whyNot":["Polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin is the mechanism of sickle cell disease.","Antibodies are not part of thalassemia. Antibody-mediated destruction of red cells is immune hemolysis.","Correct.","Erythropoietin is high, not low, in thalassemia major. The kidney responds normally to the anemia."],"study":{"topics":["Beta thalassemia major","Ineffective erythropoiesis"],"comps":["HE-9-B1","HE-3"]}},{"id":"HE-C10-Q2","stem":"A liver biopsy specimen shows clusters of erythroid precursors and megakaryocytes in the hepatic sinusoids. Which of the following developmental sequences of hematopoietic sites best explains why blood cells are being made in his liver and spleen?","options":["Bone marrow, then yolk sac, then liver and spleen","Yolk sac, then liver and spleen, then bone marrow","Yolk sac, then bone marrow, then liver and spleen","Liver and spleen, then yolk sac, then bone marrow"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.3","dok":2,"approach":["Blood formation begins in the yolk sac in the third week of development.","The liver, helped by the spleen, becomes the main site for most of fetal life, and the bone marrow takes over late in gestation.","When the marrow cannot meet demand after birth, the liver and spleen can resume their fetal role (extramedullary hematopoiesis)."],"why":"Hematopoiesis moves through three sites: the yolk sac in the first weeks, the liver (with the spleen) from about the second month, and the bone marrow from about the fifth to seventh month on. After birth the marrow normally makes all blood cells. When the marrow is overwhelmed by ineffective erythropoiesis, as in beta thalassemia major, or replaced by fibrosis, as in primary myelofibrosis, the liver and spleen make blood again. This extramedullary hematopoiesis enlarges both organs.","whyNot":["The bone marrow is the final site, not the first.","Correct.","The liver and spleen come before the marrow, not after it. The marrow is the last site to take over.","The yolk sac is the first site, not the second."],"study":{"topics":["Sites of hematopoiesis","Extramedullary hematopoiesis"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-9-B1"]}},{"id":"HE-C10-Q3","stem":"Forty minutes into a routine transfusion, he develops chills and a temperature of 38.7 °C (101.7 °F) from a baseline of 36.9 °C (98.4 °F). Pulse is 104/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, unchanged from before the transfusion. He has no dyspnea, rash, or back pain. Repeat typing confirms ABO compatibility, the direct antiglobulin test is negative, and his plasma and urine show no free hemoglobin. A Gram stain of the unit shows no organisms. Which of the following best explains this reaction?","options":["Recipient IgM binding donor ABO antigens","Donor antibodies against recipient leukocyte antigens","Recipient anti-IgA antibodies reacting with donor plasma","Cytokines from donor leukocytes in the stored unit"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-7","obj":"HE-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["Fever and chills with stable blood pressure and no signs of hemolysis, lung injury, or allergy point to a febrile nonhemolytic reaction.","These are common in patients who receive many transfusions.","They are caused by cytokines released from white cells in the stored unit, or by recipient antibodies to donor white cells, and leukoreduction prevents most of them."],"why":"Transfusion reactions are sorted by timing and findings. Acute hemolytic reactions occur within minutes from preformed recipient IgM against donor ABO antigens, with fever, back pain, hypotension, and hemoglobinuria. Febrile nonhemolytic reactions cause fever and chills within hours from cytokines made by donor leukocytes during storage. Anaphylaxis from anti-IgA in IgA-deficient patients causes hypotension and wheezing within minutes. TRALI causes respiratory distress within 6 hours, septic reactions cause fever with shock, and delayed hemolytic reactions appear days later.","whyNot":["ABO-incompatible transfusion causes intravascular hemolysis with free hemoglobin, back pain, and often hypotension. His tests show no hemolysis.","Donor antibodies against recipient leukocytes cause TRALI, with acute respiratory distress and pulmonary edema. He has no dyspnea.","Anti-IgA reactions cause anaphylaxis with hypotension, wheezing, or urticaria, not isolated fever.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Transfusion reactions","Febrile nonhemolytic reaction"],"comps":["HE-7"]}},{"id":"HE-C10-Q4","stem":"His mother's cousin has a moderate microcytic hemolytic anemia with splenomegaly. Supravital staining of her erythrocytes shows inclusions formed by tetramers of beta globin chains. Which of the following genotypes most likely explains her disorder?","options":["Deletion of three alpha globin genes","Deletion of two alpha globin genes","Deletion of four alpha globin genes","Point mutation at a beta globin splice site"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-6","obj":"HE-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Tetramers of beta chains are hemoglobin H, which forms only when alpha chains are very scarce.","There are four alpha globin genes, two on each chromosome 16.","Loss of three genes leaves too little alpha chain, so excess beta chains form HbH, which precipitates and causes hemolysis."],"why":"Alpha thalassemia is usually caused by gene deletions, and severity follows the number of genes lost. One deletion is a silent carrier, two cause alpha thalassemia trait with mild microcytosis, three cause HbH disease with moderate hemolytic anemia, and four cause Hb Barts (four gamma chains) with hydrops fetalis. Beta thalassemia is usually caused by point mutations, often at splice sites or in the promoter, which reduce or abolish beta chain output.","whyNot":["Correct.","Two deletions cause alpha thalassemia trait, with mild microcytosis and no HbH inclusions.","Loss of all four genes causes Hb Barts hydrops fetalis, which is usually fatal before or soon after birth.","A beta globin splice site mutation causes beta thalassemia, in which alpha chains are in excess, not beta chains."],"study":{"topics":["Alpha thalassemia genetics","Hemoglobin H disease"],"comps":["HE-6","HE-9-B1"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C11","system":"HE","dx":"G6PD deficiency with acute hemolysis after eating fava beans","codes":["HE-9-B1"],"case":"A 22-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of fatigue, low back pain, and dark urine that began 1 day after he ate a large meal of fresh fava beans. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows pallor and scleral icterus without splenomegaly. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.1 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), reticulocytes 7% (0.5 to 1.5), lactate dehydrogenase 760 U/L (140 to 280), haptoglobin under 10 mg/dL (30 to 200), and indirect bilirubin 3.6 mg/dL (0.2 to 0.8). The direct antiglobulin test is negative. Urine dipstick is positive for blood, but no red cells are seen on microscopy. Red cell G6PD activity measured 3 months later is low. Diagnosis: acute hemolysis from glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency triggered by fava beans.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C11-Q1","stem":"His peripheral blood smear shows many red cells with semicircular pieces missing from their edges. Which of the following best explains the formation of these cells?","options":["Weak spectrin anchoring lets membrane bleb off as microvesicles","Fibrin strands in small vessels slice red cells into fragments","Splenic macrophages remove IgG-coated patches of membrane","Splenic macrophages remove clumps of denatured hemoglobin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B1","obj":"HE-9-B.3","dok":3,"approach":["Missing semicircular pieces describe bite cells.","Oxidant stress in G6PD deficiency denatures hemoglobin, which precipitates and attaches to the membrane as Heinz bodies.","Splenic macrophages pluck out the Heinz bodies with a piece of membrane, leaving the bite."],"why":"Without enough G6PD, red cells cannot protect hemoglobin from oxidants such as the products of fava beans. Oxidized hemoglobin denatures and clumps into Heinz bodies, which bind the inner membrane. Many of these cells lyse in the circulation, which is why he has hemoglobinuria and a very low haptoglobin. Others pass through the spleen, where macrophages remove the Heinz body and the membrane over it, leaving bite cells. Heinz bodies are seen with a supravital stain such as crystal violet, while bite cells show on the routine Wright stain.","whyNot":["Weak links between spectrin, ankyrin, and band 3 cause membrane loss and spherocytes in hereditary spherocytosis, a chronic disorder that does not flare after fava beans.","Fibrin strands in small vessels shear red cells into schistocytes in microangiopathic hemolysis, as in TTP, HUS, or DIC. Schistocytes are irregular fragments, and nothing here suggests vessel injury.","Partial removal of IgG-coated membrane makes spherocytes in warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia. His direct antiglobulin test is negative.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Heinz bodies and bite cells","Red cell morphology in hemolysis"],"comps":["HE-9-B1","HE-3"]}},{"id":"HE-C11-Q2","stem":"Fava beans contain compounds that generate hydrogen peroxide inside red cells. Which of the following is the most direct biochemical consequence of his enzyme defect that leaves his red cells unable to handle this oxidant load?","options":["Less NADH to reduce methemoglobin to hemoglobin","Less NADPH to regenerate reduced glutathione","Less 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate to unload oxygen","Less ATP to power the sodium-potassium ATPase"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-5","obj":"HE-5.5","dok":3,"approach":["G6PD catalyzes the first step of the pentose phosphate pathway, which makes NADPH.","Mature red cells have no mitochondria, so this pathway is their only source of NADPH.","Glutathione reductase uses NADPH to keep glutathione reduced, and reduced glutathione detoxifies hydrogen peroxide."],"why":"G6PD converts glucose-6-phosphate to 6-phosphogluconolactone and reduces NADP+ to NADPH. Red cells lack mitochondria and other NADPH-producing routes, so they depend on this step. NADPH lets glutathione reductase regenerate reduced glutathione, which glutathione peroxidase uses to remove hydrogen peroxide. When NADPH runs short, peroxide oxidizes hemoglobin and membrane proteins, and the cells hemolyze. Between oxidant exposures, the small amount of NADPH is usually enough, so most patients have no anemia at baseline.","whyNot":["NADH from the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step of glycolysis feeds cytochrome b5 reductase, which reduces methemoglobin. A defect there causes methemoglobinemia with cyanosis, not hemolysis.","Correct.","2,3-BPG is made in the Rapoport-Luebering shunt of glycolysis. It is low in hexokinase deficiency and high in pyruvate kinase deficiency, and it affects oxygen unloading rather than oxidant defense.","ATP for the sodium-potassium pump comes from glycolysis. ATP loss is the problem in pyruvate kinase deficiency, which causes chronic hemolysis that does not depend on oxidant exposure."],"study":{"topics":["Pentose phosphate pathway in red cells","Glutathione and NADPH"],"comps":["HE-5","HE-9-B1"]}},{"id":"HE-C11-Q3","stem":"His grandparents lived in a region where Plasmodium falciparum malaria was once common. Which of the following best explains why G6PD deficiency alleles are frequent in such regions?","options":["Parasitized cells suffer oxidant damage and are cleared early","Red cells lack the Duffy antigen that merozoites need for entry","Parasitized cells sickle at low oxygen tension and are removed","Red cells lack the glycophorin receptor for merozoite binding"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-8","obj":"HE-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Malaria selects for red cell traits that limit parasite growth.","A growing parasite adds oxidant stress to the red cell it lives in.","A G6PD-deficient red cell cannot handle that stress, so it is damaged and removed before the parasite multiplies well."],"why":"Plasmodium falciparum digests hemoglobin and generates reactive oxygen species inside the red cell. In G6PD deficiency the infected cell has little NADPH to neutralize these oxidants, so it is damaged and recognized by phagocytes earlier in the parasite cycle. This lowers parasite burden and the risk of severe malaria, which gave carriers a survival advantage. Sickle cell trait and thalassemia trait were selected in malarial regions in the same way.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lack of the Duffy antigen protects against Plasmodium vivax, which uses it to enter red cells. It is common in people of West African descent and does not affect falciparum.","Sickling of parasitized cells at low oxygen tension, with early splenic removal, is how sickle cell trait protects against severe falciparum malaria.","Glycophorins are red cell surface proteins used by falciparum merozoites, but G6PD deficiency does not remove them."],"study":{"topics":["Red cell traits that protect against malaria","G6PD deficiency epidemiology"],"comps":["HE-8","HE-9-E2"]}},{"id":"HE-C11-Q4","stem":"A 7-year-old girl seen in the same clinic has lifelong mild jaundice, splenomegaly, and anemia that does not worsen after she eats fava beans or takes oxidant drugs. Her direct antiglobulin test is negative, the smear shows no spherocytes, elliptocytes, or bite cells, and her red cell 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate is increased. Both parents are healthy. Which of the following best explains her hemolysis?","options":["Low NADPH leaves hemoglobin open to oxidation","Low ATP leaves red cells rigid and trapped in the spleen","Complement lyses red cells lacking GPI-anchored proteins","Spectrin defects make red cells elliptical and fragile"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B1","obj":"HE-9-B.8","dok":2,"approach":["Chronic hemolysis without oxidant triggers, normal morphology, and healthy parents suggests an autosomal recessive glycolytic enzyme defect.","High 2,3-BPG points to a block below the 2,3-BPG shunt, at pyruvate kinase.","Without ATP, red cells lose cations and water, become rigid, and are removed in the spleen."],"why":"Pyruvate kinase catalyzes the last ATP-generating step of glycolysis, which red cells rely on for all their ATP. When it is deficient, intermediates above the block build up, including 2,3-BPG, and ATP falls. The sodium-potassium pump fails, the cells lose potassium and water, and they become dehydrated and rigid. The spleen traps these cells, so the hemolysis is chronic, extravascular, and associated with splenomegaly. The disease is autosomal recessive, which fits healthy parents. The high 2,3-BPG shifts the oxygen curve to the right, so patients often tolerate their anemia well.","whyNot":["Low NADPH is the problem in G6PD deficiency, which causes episodes of hemolysis after oxidant triggers and shows bite cells. Her anemia is steady and unrelated to triggers.","Correct.","Complement lysis of cells lacking CD55 and CD59 is paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, an acquired disorder with intravascular hemolysis, usually in adults.","Spectrin defects that make red cells elliptical cause hereditary elliptocytosis, usually autosomal dominant. Her smear shows no elliptocytes."],"study":{"topics":["Pyruvate kinase deficiency","Hereditary elliptocytosis"],"comps":["HE-9-B1","HE-5"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C12","system":"HE","dx":"Hemolytic disease of the newborn from maternal anti-D","codes":["HE-9-B2"],"case":"A male newborn is evaluated in the nursery 6 hours after a vaginal delivery at 38 weeks' gestation because of jaundice. His 29-year-old mother is blood group O, Rh(D)-negative. Her first child, who is Rh(D)-positive, was born 3 years ago in a setting where she did not receive anti-D immune globulin. An antibody screen at 28 weeks in this pregnancy detected anti-D. Physical examination of the infant shows pallor, jaundice, and hepatosplenomegaly. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.8 g/dL (14 to 22), reticulocytes 14% (3 to 7), and cord blood total bilirubin 4.2 mg/dL (under 2), almost all unconjugated. The infant is blood group O, Rh(D)-positive, and his direct antiglobulin test is positive. Diagnosis: hemolytic disease of the newborn due to maternal anti-D.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C12-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely source of the antibody destroying this infant's red cells?","options":["Antibodies the fetus made against maternal Rh-negative cells","Naturally occurring IgM anti-D that rises with each pregnancy","IgG anti-D made after an earlier Rh-positive delivery","IgG anti-A and anti-B that group O mothers naturally make"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B2","obj":"HE-9-B.5","dok":3,"approach":["Rh-negative people do not have natural anti-D, so she must have been sensitized.","Fetal Rh-positive cells entered her circulation at her first delivery, and without anti-D immune globulin she made anti-D.","In this pregnancy, memory B cells produced IgG anti-D, which crossed the placenta and coated fetal red cells."],"why":"Rh-negative women make anti-D only after exposure to Rh-positive red cells, usually from fetal-maternal hemorrhage at delivery. The first response is mostly IgM and too late to harm that baby. In the next Rh-positive pregnancy, a fast IgG response occurs, and IgG crosses the placenta through neonatal Fc receptors. Fetal red cells coated with IgG are destroyed in the fetal spleen, causing anemia and, in severe cases, hydrops fetalis. Anti-D immune globulin given after delivery clears fetal cells before she can be sensitized.","whyNot":["The fetal immune system does not mount an antibody response against maternal red cells, and that would not hemolyze the infant's own cells.","Anti-D is not naturally occurring. The clinically important naturally occurring antibodies are anti-A and anti-B, and IgM does not cross the placenta.","Correct.","Group O mothers do make IgG anti-A and anti-B that can cause ABO hemolytic disease, usually milder than Rh disease and possible even in a first pregnancy. This infant is group O, so he has no A or B antigen to target."],"study":{"topics":["Rh alloimmunization","ABO versus Rh hemolytic disease"],"comps":["HE-9-B2","HE-2"]}},{"id":"HE-C12-Q2","stem":"By 18 hours of age, his total serum bilirubin has risen to 14 mg/dL, almost all unconjugated. Which of the following best explains why the bilirubin is rising much faster now than before birth?","options":["Bilirubin no longer crosses the placenta to the mother","Breast milk inhibits conjugation of bilirubin in the liver","Hepatic glucuronosyltransferase activity falls after delivery","Biliary obstruction blocks excretion of conjugated bilirubin"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B2","obj":"HE-9-B.6","dok":3,"approach":["A positive direct antiglobulin test shows maternal IgG on his red cells, so hemolysis continues after birth.","Before birth, unconjugated bilirubin crossed the placenta and was cleared by the mother's liver.","After birth, his immature liver must clear it alone, so unconjugated bilirubin climbs and kernicterus becomes a risk."],"why":"Maternal IgG anti-D stays in the infant's blood for weeks and keeps destroying his red cells. In the fetus, unconjugated bilirubin crosses the placenta and the mother's liver conjugates and excretes it, so cord bilirubin is only modestly raised. After birth the newborn liver, which has low UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity, cannot keep up. Unconjugated bilirubin that exceeds albumin binding can enter the brain and deposit in the basal ganglia, causing kernicterus. That is why these infants need close bilirubin monitoring, phototherapy, and sometimes exchange transfusion.","whyNot":["Correct.","Breast milk jaundice appears after the first week of life and is mild. It does not explain a rapid rise on the first day.","Newborn glucuronosyltransferase activity is low at birth and rises over the following weeks. It does not fall after delivery.","Biliary obstruction raises conjugated bilirubin. His bilirubin is almost all unconjugated."],"study":{"topics":["Neonatal bilirubin handling","Coombs tests in hemolytic disease"],"comps":["HE-9-B2","HE-3"]}},{"id":"HE-C12-Q3","stem":"An exchange transfusion is planned. Which of the following red cell units is most appropriate for this infant?","options":["Group AB, Rh-negative red cells","Group A, Rh-negative red cells","Group O, Rh-positive red cells","Group O, Rh-negative red cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-2","obj":"HE-2.7","dok":2,"approach":["The infant's plasma contains maternal IgG anti-D and the mother's IgG anti-A and anti-B.","Donor cells must lack D antigen or the maternal anti-D will destroy them.","Donor cells must also lack A and B antigens, since the infant is group O and carries maternal anti-A and anti-B."],"why":"Red cells given to a patient must lack any antigen that the patient's plasma can attack. This infant's plasma holds maternal anti-D that crossed the placenta, plus maternal IgG anti-A and anti-B, since his mother is group O. Newborns do not make their own anti-A and anti-B until a few months of age. Group O red cells have no A or B antigen, and Rh-negative cells have no D antigen, so group O, Rh-negative cells will survive. The units are also crossmatched against the mother's plasma because it contains the offending antibody.","whyNot":["Group AB cells carry A and B antigens and would be attacked by the maternal anti-A and anti-B in this infant's plasma. Group AB plasma, on the other hand, is the universal plasma donor.","Group A cells would be attacked by the maternal anti-A in his plasma. Group A cells would be suitable only for a group A or AB recipient.","Rh-positive cells carry D antigen, so the maternal anti-D still in his blood would destroy them.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["ABO and Rh compatibility","Red cell versus plasma donors"],"comps":["HE-2","HE-9-B2"]}},{"id":"HE-C12-Q4","stem":"Ceftriaxone is avoided in this infant because it can raise the risk of bilirubin entering the brain. Which of the following best explains this effect?","options":["It inhibits heme oxygenase in splenic macrophages","It displaces unconjugated bilirubin from albumin","It blocks secretion of conjugated bilirubin into bile","It inhibits biliverdin reductase in splenic macrophages"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-3","obj":"HE-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Heme from destroyed red cells is turned into biliverdin and then unconjugated bilirubin in macrophages.","Unconjugated bilirubin is water-insoluble and travels to the liver bound to albumin.","Drugs that compete for albumin binding raise free bilirubin, the form that crosses the blood-brain barrier."],"why":"Macrophages break heme down with heme oxygenase to biliverdin, carbon monoxide, and iron, and biliverdin reductase converts biliverdin to unconjugated bilirubin. This lipid-soluble bilirubin is carried bound to albumin to the liver, where UDP-glucuronosyltransferase conjugates it for excretion into bile. In the gut, bacteria convert it to urobilinogen, which becomes stercobilin in stool and urobilin in urine. Only the small unbound fraction of unconjugated bilirubin can cross into the brain. Ceftriaxone and sulfonamides compete for albumin binding sites, raising free bilirubin and the risk of kernicterus in a jaundiced newborn.","whyNot":["Blocking heme oxygenase would reduce bilirubin production, which would lower the risk of brain injury.","Correct.","Blocking biliary secretion would raise conjugated bilirubin. Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and does not cross the blood-brain barrier.","Blocking biliverdin reductase would leave biliverdin, which is not neurotoxic like unconjugated bilirubin."],"study":{"topics":["Heme catabolism","Bilirubin transport and kernicterus"],"comps":["HE-3","HE-9-B2"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C13","system":"HE","dx":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria with hepatic vein thrombosis","codes":["HE-9-B1"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 2 weeks of right upper quadrant pain and increasing abdominal girth. For several months she has had fatigue and episodes of dark urine that are most noticeable in the morning. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tender hepatomegaly and shifting dullness. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.9 g/dL (12 to 16), reticulocytes 5% (0.5 to 1.5), leukocytes 3,100/µL (4,500 to 11,000), platelets 92,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), lactate dehydrogenase 1,450 U/L (140 to 280), and indirect bilirubin 2.4 mg/dL (0.2 to 0.8). Doppler ultrasonography shows occlusion of the hepatic veins. Flow cytometry shows red cells and granulocytes lacking CD55 and CD59. Diagnosis: paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria complicated by hepatic vein thrombosis (Budd-Chiari syndrome).","questions":[{"id":"HE-C13-Q1","stem":"Sequencing of her marrow cells shows an acquired mutation in PIGA. Which of the following best explains how this mutation leads to her hemolysis?","options":["Mutant factor H fails to bind red cell surface sialic acid","Loss of plasma C1 inhibitor allows unchecked classical pathway activity","Complement regulators cannot be anchored to the cell membrane","IgM autoantibodies fix complement on red cells in the cold"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-9-B","obj":"HE-9-B.7","dok":3,"approach":["PIGA encodes an enzyme needed to build the glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor.","CD55 (decay-accelerating factor) and CD59 (which blocks membrane attack complex formation) are GPI-anchored.","Without them, red cells cannot shut down complement that is always being activated at a low rate, so they lyse in the vessels."],"why":"PIGA is an X-linked gene needed for the first step of GPI anchor synthesis. A somatic mutation in a stem cell gives rise to clones of blood cells with no GPI-anchored proteins. CD55 normally speeds the breakdown of C3 and C5 convertases, and CD59 prevents the membrane attack complex from forming, so their loss leaves red cells exposed to the alternative pathway, which runs at a low level all the time. The result is intravascular hemolysis, hemoglobinuria, and release of free hemoglobin. Flow cytometry for CD55, CD59, or the FLAER reagent detects the abnormal clone.","whyNot":["Factor H defects allow complement attack on endothelium in atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome. Factor H is a plasma protein and does not depend on PIGA.","Deficiency of C1 inhibitor causes hereditary angioedema, with bradykinin-mediated swelling, not hemolysis.","Correct.","Cold IgM autoantibodies that fix complement cause cold agglutinin disease, often after Mycoplasma infection or with lymphoma. Their direct antiglobulin test is positive for C3, and PIGA is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Complement regulatory proteins"],"comps":["HE-9-B1","HE-7"]}},{"id":"HE-C13-Q2","stem":"Which of the following additional laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["Hemosiderin in urinary epithelial cells","Positive direct antiglobulin test","Many spherocytes on the peripheral smear","Bilirubin on urine dipstick testing"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-3","obj":"HE-3.3","dok":3,"approach":["Complement lysis in PNH happens inside blood vessels.","Free hemoglobin is filtered by the kidney, and tubular cells absorb it and store the iron as hemosiderin.","These iron-laden cells shed into the urine, so chronic intravascular hemolysis gives hemosiderinuria."],"why":"Intravascular hemolysis releases hemoglobin into plasma. Haptoglobin binds it and is quickly used up, LDH rises, and once haptoglobin is gone, free hemoglobin passes into the urine. Renal tubular cells take up some of this hemoglobin and convert its iron to hemosiderin, which appears in shed cells within several days. Chronic hemosiderinuria can cause iron deficiency in PNH. Extravascular hemolysis in the spleen, by contrast, gives spherocytes and splenomegaly, without hemoglobinuria or hemosiderinuria.","whyNot":["Correct.","The direct antiglobulin test is negative in PNH because no antibody is involved. A positive result indicates immune hemolysis.","Spherocytes come from partial removal of membrane in the spleen, as in hereditary spherocytosis or warm autoimmune hemolysis. They point to extravascular hemolysis.","Urine bilirubin is conjugated bilirubin, which appears in hepatitis or biliary obstruction. Unconjugated bilirubin from hemolysis is bound to albumin and is not filtered."],"study":{"topics":["Intravascular versus extravascular hemolysis","Hemosiderinuria"],"comps":["HE-3","HE-9-B1"]}},{"id":"HE-C13-Q3","stem":"Repeat flow cytometry shows that CD59 is also missing from her monocytes, platelets, and a subset of lymphocytes. The PIGA mutation most likely arose in which of the following cells?","options":["Common myeloid progenitor","Granulocyte-monocyte progenitor","Megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor","Hematopoietic stem cell"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Find the lowest cell that gives rise to every affected lineage.","Red cells, granulocytes, monocytes, and platelets come from the common myeloid progenitor, but lymphocytes come from the common lymphoid progenitor.","Only the hematopoietic stem cell produces both myeloid and lymphoid cells."],"why":"Hematopoiesis starts with the self-renewing hematopoietic stem cell, which gives rise to the common myeloid progenitor and the common lymphoid progenitor. The myeloid progenitor makes the megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor (platelets and red cells) and the granulocyte-monocyte progenitor (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, and monocytes). The lymphoid progenitor makes B cells, T cells, and NK cells. A defect found in both lymphoid and myeloid cells must start in the stem cell. This also explains the pancytopenia and the link between PNH and aplastic anemia, which both involve the stem cell compartment.","whyNot":["A mutation in the common myeloid progenitor would affect red cells, platelets, granulocytes, and monocytes but would spare lymphocytes.","A mutation in the granulocyte-monocyte progenitor would affect only granulocytes and monocytes.","A mutation in the megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor would affect only red cells and platelets.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hematopoietic lineages","Clonal stem cell disorders"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-9-A1"]}},{"id":"HE-C13-Q4","stem":"After recovery she asks about starting a combined estrogen-progestin oral contraceptive. Which of the following effects of estrogen would most add to her risk of another venous thrombosis?","options":["Decreased hepatic synthesis of fibrinogen and factor VII","Increased hepatic synthesis of antithrombin III","Decreased platelet surface expression of GpIIb/IIIa","Increased hepatic synthesis of clotting factors"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.6","dok":2,"approach":["Virchow triad lists stasis, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability.","PNH already makes her blood hypercoagulable.","Estrogen adds to this by raising liver production of clotting factors and lowering natural anticoagulants."],"why":"Virchow triad explains venous thrombosis as stasis, endothelial injury, or hypercoagulability. Estrogen acts on the liver to raise fibrinogen and factors II, VII, VIII, and X, and it lowers protein S and causes acquired resistance to activated protein C. This is the same hypercoagulable effect seen in pregnancy. Immobility and surgery add stasis, surgery adds endothelial injury, and malignancy adds procoagulant release. A patient with PNH and a prior thrombosis already carries a high risk, so estrogen-containing contraception is generally avoided.","whyNot":["Estrogen raises fibrinogen and factor VII. Lower levels would reduce thrombosis risk.","Estrogen tends to lower antithrombin, and more antithrombin would reduce, not raise, clotting risk.","Less GpIIb/IIIa would impair platelet aggregation and cause bleeding, as in Glanzmann thrombasthenia.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Virchow triad","Estrogen and venous thrombosis"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-9-B1"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C14","system":"HE","dx":"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura from acquired ADAMTS13 autoantibodies","codes":["HE-9-B","HE-9-C"],"case":"A 42-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of 3 days of fatigue, headache, and new confusion. He has no diarrhea. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg. Physical examination shows scattered petechiae on the legs and mild disorientation without focal deficits. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), platelets 14,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), lactate dehydrogenase 1,900 U/L (140 to 280), haptoglobin under 10 mg/dL (30 to 200), creatinine 1.4 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3), and normal prothrombin and partial thromboplastin times. The direct antiglobulin test is negative. The peripheral smear shows many fragmented red cells. ADAMTS13 activity is under 5% (60 to 130), and an inhibitory IgG antibody is detected. Diagnosis: immune thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C14-Q1","stem":"Which of the following features best distinguishes his disorder from hemolytic uremic syndrome caused by Escherichia coli O157:H7?","options":["Schistocytes from shearing in occluded small vessels","Consumption of clotting factors and fibrinogen","Persistence of ultralarge von Willebrand multimers","Normal PT and PTT despite severe thrombocytopenia"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B","obj":"HE-9-B.9","dok":3,"approach":["Both TTP and HUS are thrombotic microangiopathies with schistocytes, thrombocytopenia, and normal PT and PTT.","The difference is the trigger: ADAMTS13 deficiency in TTP, and Shiga toxin injury to renal endothelium in HUS.","Low ADAMTS13 means ultralarge von Willebrand factor multimers are never cut down."],"why":"ADAMTS13 is a plasma protease that cleaves ultralarge von Willebrand factor multimers as they are released from endothelium. When an autoantibody blocks it, these sticky multimers persist and bind platelets under high shear, forming platelet-rich microthrombi throughout the body, especially in the brain. In HUS, Shiga toxin from E. coli O157:H7 binds globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) on renal endothelial cells and injures them, so ADAMTS13 is normal and kidney failure dominates. Both disorders consume platelets and shear red cells, but neither activates the clotting cascade enough to prolong the PT and PTT.","whyNot":["Schistocytes form in both disorders as red cells are cut by microthrombi in small vessels.","Consumption of clotting factors and fibrinogen is typical of DIC. It is absent in both TTP and HUS.","Correct.","Normal PT and PTT with low platelets occurs in both TTP and HUS, so it does not separate them. It does separate both from DIC."],"study":{"topics":["TTP versus HUS","ADAMTS13 and von Willebrand factor"],"comps":["HE-9-B","HE-9-C"]}},{"id":"HE-C14-Q2","stem":"On his Wright-stained smear, some small fragments must be classified as schistocytes or as platelets. Which of the following features identifies a fragment as a schistocyte?","options":["Dark purple nucleus with scant pale blue cytoplasm","Pink-red color, no granules, and sharp angular edges","Bluish-gray color, no granules, and size larger than mature red cells","Purple granular center within a pale blue rim"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["A schistocyte is a piece of a red cell, so it keeps the color of red cells.","Red cells stain pink-red with eosin from hemoglobin and have no nucleus or granules.","Platelets are small with purple granules in the center and a pale rim."],"why":"On a Wright stain, normal red cells are 7 to 8 µm, pink-red, anucleate disks with central pallor. Schistocytes are fragments of these cells, so they keep the pink-red color but have sharp angles, points, or helmet shapes. Platelets are 2 to 3 µm, anucleate, with a purple granular center (granulomere) and a pale blue rim (hyalomere). Lymphocytes have a round dark nucleus with little cytoplasm, and neutrophils have 3 to 5 lobes with faint lilac granules. Separating schistocytes from platelets matters, because counting fragments as platelets can falsely raise the automated platelet count.","whyNot":["A dark nucleus with scant blue cytoplasm describes a small lymphocyte or a nucleated red cell. Schistocytes have no nucleus.","Correct.","Large bluish-gray cells without granules are polychromatophilic red cells (reticulocytes), which show active marrow response to hemolysis.","A purple granular center with a pale rim describes a platelet."],"study":{"topics":["Peripheral smear cell identification","Schistocytes"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-9-B"]}},{"id":"HE-C14-Q3","stem":"Endothelial Weibel-Palade bodies are one source of the von Willebrand factor that builds up in this disorder. Which of the following platelet structures also stores and releases von Willebrand factor?","options":["Open canalicular system","Alpha granules","Dense granules","Lysosomal granules"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Platelets get their stored proteins from megakaryocytes, which package them into granules before the cytoplasm fragments into platelets.","Platelet granules come in two main types, alpha and dense.","Large proteins such as von Willebrand factor and fibrinogen are stored in alpha granules, while dense granules hold small molecules."],"why":"Platelets form when megakaryocytes in the marrow extend cytoplasmic processes into sinusoids and shed fragments. Each platelet carries granules made by the megakaryocyte. Alpha granules are the most numerous and hold proteins, including von Willebrand factor, fibrinogen, factor V, platelet factor 4, and platelet-derived growth factor. Dense granules hold ADP, calcium, and serotonin. Both granule types release their contents when platelets are activated.","whyNot":["The open canalicular system is a network of membrane channels that connects the platelet interior to the surface. It is a route for release, not a storage site.","Correct.","Dense granules store small molecules, ADP, calcium, and serotonin, which recruit and activate more platelets.","Platelet lysosomes contain acid hydrolases and do not store von Willebrand factor."],"study":{"topics":["Platelet granule contents","Megakaryocytes and platelet formation"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C14-Q4","stem":"Under the high shear stress of arterioles, the ultralarge von Willebrand factor multimers in his plasma capture platelets. Which of the following platelet receptors mediates this initial binding?","options":["Glycoprotein Ib","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa","P2Y12 ADP receptor","Glycoprotein VI"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Primary hemostasis has two platelet steps, adhesion and aggregation.","Adhesion under shear depends on von Willebrand factor binding a specific platelet receptor.","That receptor is glycoprotein Ib, part of the GpIb-IX-V complex."],"why":"When a vessel is injured, von Willebrand factor binds exposed subendothelial collagen and unfolds under shear, exposing its A1 domain. Platelet glycoprotein Ib binds this site and tethers the platelet. The platelet then becomes activated, releases ADP and thromboxane A2, and changes its GpIIb/IIIa receptors so they bind fibrinogen and link platelets together. In TTP, ultralarge multimers are especially adhesive, so under high shear they bind GpIb without any vessel injury and build platelet-rich microthrombi.","whyNot":["Correct.","GpIIb/IIIa binds fibrinogen and links platelets during aggregation, after adhesion and activation. It is the receptor missing in Glanzmann thrombasthenia.","P2Y12 is the receptor through which released ADP activates more platelets. It is the target of clopidogrel.","Glycoprotein VI binds collagen directly and helps activate platelets. It does not bind von Willebrand factor."],"study":{"topics":["Platelet adhesion","Primary hemostasis"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-9-C4"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C15","system":"HE","dx":"Hepatosplenic schistosomiasis (Schistosoma mansoni) with hypersplenism","codes":["HE-9-E3"],"case":"A 33-year-old woman comes to the emergency department after vomiting bright red blood. She grew up in a rural area of northeastern Brazil, where she often bathed and washed clothes in local streams, and moved to the United States 4 years ago. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 98/62 mm Hg. Physical examination shows the spleen 8 cm below the left costal margin, a firm left hepatic lobe, and no jaundice or spider angiomata. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL (12 to 16), leukocytes 3,400/µL (4,500 to 11,000) with 16% eosinophils (1 to 4), platelets 62,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), albumin 4.0 g/dL (3.5 to 5.5), and INR 1.0. Endoscopy shows large esophageal varices. Ultrasonography shows thick periportal fibrosis. Stool examination shows eggs with a prominent lateral spine. Diagnosis: hepatosplenic schistosomiasis due to Schistosoma mansoni with portal hypertension and hypersplenism.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C15-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her portal hypertension with preserved hepatic synthetic function?","options":["Sinusoidal distortion by regenerative cirrhotic nodules","Postsinusoidal block from occlusion of the hepatic veins","Presinusoidal fibrosis from granulomas around trapped eggs","Prehepatic block from thrombosis of the splenic vein alone"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-E3","obj":"HE-9-E.4","dok":3,"approach":["Eggs laid in mesenteric veins are swept by portal flow into the liver, where they lodge in small portal vein branches.","Egg antigens trigger granulomas with eosinophils, which heal by fibrosis around portal tracts (pipestem fibrosis).","The block lies before the sinusoids, so hepatocytes are spared and albumin and INR stay normal."],"why":"Schistosoma mansoni adults live in mesenteric venules and release eggs, many of which travel to the liver and lodge in presinusoidal portal venules. A CD4 T helper 2 response forms granulomas rich in eosinophils and macrophages around each egg, and the healing scar thickens the portal tracts into pipestem fibrosis. Portal pressure rises, causing splenomegaly and varices, while the liver cells themselves keep working. Schistosoma haematobium lives in the vesical venous plexus instead, and its eggs cause hematuria, bladder fibrosis, and squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder.","whyNot":["Cirrhosis distorts the sinusoids and usually reduces synthetic function, causing low albumin and a high INR. Her albumin and INR are normal and she has no stigmata of chronic liver disease.","Hepatic vein occlusion (Budd-Chiari syndrome) causes tender hepatomegaly and ascites. Ultrasonography would show the obstructed hepatic veins, not periportal fibrosis.","Correct.","Isolated splenic vein thrombosis causes left-sided portal hypertension with gastric varices. It does not cause periportal fibrosis of the liver."],"study":{"topics":["Schistosomal liver disease","Presinusoidal portal hypertension"],"comps":["HE-9-E3","HE-8"]}},{"id":"HE-C15-Q2","stem":"In which of the following sites do the adult worms most likely live, allowing their eggs to reach both her liver and her stool?","options":["Intrahepatic bile ducts and gallbladder","Vesical venous plexus around the bladder","Lymphatic channels in the bowel wall","Mesenteric venules draining the bowel"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-8","obj":"HE-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["Her stream exposure fits cercariae from freshwater snails penetrating her skin.","Schistosomula migrate to the portal system and mature, and S. mansoni pairs settle in mesenteric venules.","Eggs pass through the bowel wall into stool or flow back with portal blood to the liver."],"why":"The Schistosoma life cycle needs a freshwater snail as intermediate host. Snails release free-swimming cercariae that penetrate human skin, lose their tails, and travel through the lungs to the portal venous system. Adult male and female worms pair and move to their final venules: mesenteric veins for S. mansoni and S. japonicum, and the vesical venous plexus for S. haematobium. Eggs either cross the bowel wall to leave in stool or are carried by portal blood to the liver, where they cause granulomas. Eggs that reach fresh water hatch into miracidia, which infect snails and complete the cycle.","whyNot":["Bile ducts are home to the liver flukes Clonorchis and Opisthorchis, which are acquired by eating raw freshwater fish.","The vesical venous plexus is home to S. haematobium, whose terminal-spined eggs appear in urine and cause bladder disease.","Schistosomes live in veins. Filarial worms such as Wuchereria bancrofti live in lymphatics.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Schistosoma life cycle","Species and their sites"],"comps":["HE-8","HE-9-E3"]}},{"id":"HE-C15-Q3","stem":"A bone marrow examination is done to evaluate her low blood counts. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Cellular marrow with all lineages present","Fibrotic marrow with a dry tap on aspiration","Hypocellular marrow replaced mainly by fat","Marrow replaced by sheets of immature blasts"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-B","obj":"HE-9-B.12","dok":3,"approach":["Pancytopenia with massive splenomegaly from portal hypertension suggests hypersplenism.","In hypersplenism, an enlarged spleen pools and removes blood cells that the marrow made normally.","The marrow responds to this loss, so it is cellular with all lineages present."],"why":"An enlarged, congested spleen holds a large share of the circulating platelets and also traps red and white cells. This lowers all three counts in the blood, although the marrow keeps making cells, and often more than usual. A marrow examination therefore shows normal or increased cellularity with normal maturation of all lineages. Marrow failure, by contrast, shows empty or replaced marrow and usually no splenomegaly. Treating the portal hypertension or removing the spleen corrects cytopenias from hypersplenism.","whyNot":["Correct.","A fibrotic marrow with a dry tap suggests primary myelofibrosis, which also gives a large spleen, but from extramedullary hematopoiesis, with teardrop cells on the smear.","A fatty, hypocellular marrow means marrow failure, as in aplastic anemia, which does not cause splenomegaly.","Sheets of blasts indicate acute leukemia, which would show blasts in the blood and does not fit her long exposure history and liver findings."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersplenism","Marrow failure versus peripheral destruction"],"comps":["HE-9-B","HE-9-A1"]}},{"id":"HE-C15-Q4","stem":"Her eosinophil count is increased. Which of the following best describes how these cells damage schistosome larvae?","options":["Engulfing larvae and killing them inside phagolysosomes","Degranulating onto antibody-coated larvae","Releasing histamine from granules armed with IgE","Presenting worm antigens to naive T cells in lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-2","obj":"HE-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Helminths are too large to be phagocytosed.","Eosinophils bind antibody (IgE or IgG) coating the larval surface.","They then release toxic granule proteins such as major basic protein onto the larva."],"why":"Eosinophils have bilobed nuclei and large orange-red granules containing major basic protein, eosinophil cationic protein, and eosinophil peroxidase. A T helper 2 response to helminths raises IL-5, which increases eosinophil production, and IgE and IgG coat the parasite surface. Eosinophils bind these antibodies through Fc receptors and release their granule proteins directly onto the larva, a form of antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity. This is why eosinophilia suggests parasitic or allergic disease, while neutrophilia suggests bacterial infection and lymphocytosis suggests viral infection.","whyNot":["Phagocytic killing in phagolysosomes is how neutrophils and macrophages kill bacteria. Larvae are far too large to engulf.","Correct.","Histamine release from IgE-armed granules is the role of basophils and mast cells, which drive allergic reactions.","Presenting antigen to naive T cells in lymph nodes is the role of dendritic cells."],"study":{"topics":["Eosinophil function","Leukocyte responses to infection"],"comps":["HE-2","HE-7"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C16","system":"HE","dx":"Acute promyelocytic leukemia with disseminated intravascular coagulation","codes":["HE-9-D1","HE-9-C1"],"case":"A 47-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of 1 week of bleeding gums, nosebleeds, and easy bruising. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 124/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows gingival oozing, scattered petechiae, and large ecchymoses on the arms. There is no lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.4 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), leukocytes 1,900/µL (4,500 to 11,000), and platelets 21,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000). The peripheral smear shows abnormal promyelocytes with dense azurophilic granules, and some cells contain bundles of Auer rods. A bone marrow aspirate shows 80% abnormal promyelocytes that are strongly myeloperoxidase-positive. Diagnosis: acute promyelocytic leukemia with disseminated intravascular coagulation.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C16-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why this subtype of acute myeloid leukemia so often presents with disseminated intravascular coagulation?","options":["Promyelocyte contents activate both clotting and fibrinolysis","Leukemic infiltration of the liver halts clotting factor synthesis","Leukemic cells secrete autoantibodies that neutralize factor VIII","Leukostasis from extreme blast counts causes vascular stasis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D1","obj":"HE-9-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["Acute promyelocytic leukemia is the AML subtype with heavily granulated promyelocytes and Auer rods.","These cells express tissue factor and other procoagulants, which drive widespread thrombin generation.","They also express annexin II, which increases plasmin generation, so fibrinolysis is excessive too."],"why":"AML is defined by at least 20% myeloid blasts or blast equivalents in the marrow, and Auer rods are myeloperoxidase-positive crystallized granules. Abnormal promyelocytes in APL carry tissue factor and other procoagulants that activate the coagulation cascade throughout the circulation, consuming platelets and clotting factors. They also express annexin II, which speeds plasminogen activation and adds hyperfibrinolysis. The result is severe bleeding, which is the main cause of early death in APL. Chemotherapy that lyses these cells can worsen DIC, which is one reason differentiation therapy is used.","whyNot":["Correct.","Liver infiltration severe enough to stop factor synthesis is rare in AML and would not explain the typical sudden, consumptive coagulopathy of APL.","Autoantibodies to factor VIII cause acquired hemophilia, with an isolated prolonged PTT. Leukemic myeloid cells do not secrete antibodies.","Leukostasis occurs with very high blast counts, often over 100,000/µL. APL often presents with a low white count, as here, and leukostasis causes ischemia rather than DIC."],"study":{"topics":["Acute promyelocytic leukemia","AML features and risk factors"],"comps":["HE-9-D1","HE-9-C1"]}},{"id":"HE-C16-Q2","stem":"Which of the following sets of coagulation results is most likely in this patient?","options":["Normal PT, prolonged PTT, normal fibrinogen, normal D-dimer","Prolonged PT, normal PTT, normal fibrinogen, normal D-dimer","Normal PT and PTT, normal fibrinogen, increased D-dimer","Prolonged PT and PTT, low fibrinogen, increased D-dimer"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C1","obj":"HE-9-C.1","dok":3,"approach":["DIC means unregulated thrombin generation throughout the circulation.","Clotting factors and fibrinogen are used up, so PT and PTT both prolong and fibrinogen falls.","Plasmin breaks down the cross-linked fibrin that forms, so D-dimer rises."],"why":"In DIC, a trigger such as sepsis, trauma, placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, APL, or mucin-secreting adenocarcinoma activates coagulation throughout the body. Platelets and factors are consumed faster than they are made, so the platelet count drops and both PT and PTT prolong. Fibrinogen is converted to fibrin, so fibrinogen falls. Fibrinolysis of the fibrin produces fibrin degradation products and D-dimer, which rise. Red cells sheared by fibrin strands in small vessels appear as schistocytes.","whyNot":["An isolated prolonged PTT with normal fibrinogen and D-dimer suggests a deficiency in the intrinsic pathway, such as hemophilia, or von Willebrand disease.","An isolated prolonged PT is the early pattern of vitamin K deficiency or warfarin use, because factor VII falls first.","A high D-dimer with normal clotting times fits a localized clot such as deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism, without factor consumption.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["DIC laboratory findings","Causes of DIC"],"comps":["HE-9-C1","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C16-Q3","stem":"Cytogenetic analysis shows t(15;17). He is started on all-trans retinoic acid. Which of the following best explains how this drug induces remission?","options":["It blocks BCL2 so tumor cells undergo apoptosis","It inhibits the constitutively active BCR-ABL kinase","It lifts the PML-RARA block so promyelocytes mature","It lowers MYC transcription so cells stop dividing"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-6","obj":"HE-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["t(15;17) fuses PML on chromosome 15 with the retinoic acid receptor alpha gene (RARA) on chromosome 17.","The PML-RARA fusion protein represses genes needed for myeloid maturation, so cells stay stuck at the promyelocyte stage.","High doses of retinoic acid bind the fusion receptor, release the repression, and let the cells differentiate into neutrophils."],"why":"In APL, PML-RARA acts as a transcriptional repressor that recruits corepressors and blocks myeloid differentiation at the promyelocyte stage. Pharmacologic doses of all-trans retinoic acid bind the fusion receptor, release the corepressors, and promote its degradation. The leukemic promyelocytes then mature into neutrophils and die normally, which also reduces the procoagulant burden. Arsenic trioxide also targets PML-RARA for degradation. This is the main example of treating a translocation by reversing a differentiation block.","whyNot":["BCL2 overexpression from t(14;18) blocks apoptosis in follicular lymphoma. Retinoic acid does not act on BCL2.","BCR-ABL comes from t(9;22) in chronic myeloid leukemia and is targeted by imatinib.","Correct.","MYC overexpression from t(8;14) drives proliferation in Burkitt lymphoma. Retinoic acid does not act on MYC."],"study":{"topics":["Translocations in leukemia and lymphoma","PML-RARA"],"comps":["HE-6","HE-9-D1"]}},{"id":"HE-C16-Q4","stem":"Tissue factor on his leukemic cells starts coagulation by binding a plasma factor. Which of the following names that factor and the screening test most affected by its deficiency?","options":["Factor VII, measured by the PT","Factor XIII, measured by the PT","Factor XI, measured by the PTT","Factor XII, measured by the PTT"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["Tissue factor starts the extrinsic pathway.","Tissue factor binds factor VII, and the complex activates factor X (and factor IX).","The PT starts clotting with tissue factor, so it reflects factor VII and the common pathway."],"why":"The extrinsic pathway begins when tissue factor binds factor VII to form the tissue factor-VIIa complex, which activates factor X and some factor IX. The intrinsic (contact) pathway begins with factor XII and runs through XI, IX, and VIII. Both pathways meet at factor X in the common pathway, where Xa with factor V turns prothrombin into thrombin, and thrombin turns fibrinogen into fibrin. Factor XIIIa then cross-links the fibrin. The PT tests the extrinsic and common pathways, the PTT tests the intrinsic and common pathways, and neither test detects factor XIII deficiency.","whyNot":["Correct.","Factor XIII cross-links fibrin after it forms. Its deficiency causes bleeding with a normal PT and PTT.","Factor XI belongs to the intrinsic pathway, which the PTT measures. Tissue factor does not bind it.","Factor XII starts the contact pathway, which the PTT measures. Factor XII deficiency prolongs the PTT but does not cause bleeding."],"study":{"topics":["Coagulation cascade","PT and PTT"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-9-C1"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C17","system":"HE","dx":"Hemophilia A with hemarthrosis","codes":["HE-9-C2"],"case":"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a swollen, warm right knee and refusal to walk since a minor fall the day before. He bled for 2 days after a lip laceration at 14 months of age. His mother's brother had repeated joint bleeds as a child. His mother, father, and 5-year-old sister are healthy. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a tense, tender right knee effusion and a large hematoma on the left thigh. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.6 g/dL (11 to 14), prothrombin time 12 seconds (11 to 13.5), partial thromboplastin time 72 seconds (25 to 35), and factor VIII activity 2% (50 to 150). Diagnosis: hemophilia A with hemarthrosis.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C17-Q1","stem":"Before the factor assay was resulted, a 1:1 mixing study with normal pooled plasma was performed. Which of the following results was most likely?","options":["PT and PTT both prolong only after 2 hours of incubation","PTT corrects at once and stays normal after incubation","PTT fails to correct either at once or after incubation","PTT corrects at once but prolongs again after incubation"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C2","obj":"HE-9-C.3","dok":3,"approach":["Normal plasma supplies about 50% of every factor, which is enough to normalize the PTT if a factor is simply missing.","An inherited deficiency has no antibody, so the correction holds after incubation.","An acquired factor VIII inhibitor corrects at first but slowly destroys the added factor VIII, so the PTT prolongs again after incubation."],"why":"Hemophilia A (factor VIII) and hemophilia B (factor IX) are X-linked deficiencies of the intrinsic pathway that cause hemarthroses and deep muscle bleeds, with a normal PT and an isolated prolonged PTT. Mixing the patient's plasma with normal plasma replaces the missing factor, so the PTT corrects and stays corrected. An acquired factor VIII inhibitor, seen in older adults, postpartum women, or autoimmune disease, is an IgG autoantibody that inactivates factor VIII over time, giving a time-dependent failure of correction. A factor assay then tells hemophilia A from B, since the clinical picture is the same.","whyNot":["A defect limited to the intrinsic pathway leaves the PT normal, so the PT would not change on mixing.","Correct.","Failure to correct at once is typical of a lupus anticoagulant, which binds the phospholipid in the test reagent.","Correction that is lost after incubation is the pattern of an acquired factor VIII inhibitor, a time-dependent antibody."],"study":{"topics":["Mixing studies","Hemophilia A versus acquired factor VIII inhibitor"],"comps":["HE-9-C2","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C17-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his factor VIII deficiency prolongs the PTT but leaves the PT normal?","options":["Factor VIII cross-links fibrin, a step the PTT alone measures","Factor VIII is consumed by tissue factor in the PT reagent","Factor VIIIa aids IXa in activating X, which the PT bypasses","Factor VIII activates factor VII, a step the PTT alone measures"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["The PTT starts clotting through contact activation and runs through factors XII, XI, IX, and VIII.","Factor VIIIa is the cofactor for factor IXa in the tenase complex that activates factor X.","The PT reagent supplies tissue factor, which with factor VIIa activates factor X directly, skipping factor VIII."],"why":"In the intrinsic pathway, factor IXa and its cofactor VIIIa assemble on a phospholipid surface with calcium to form the tenase complex, which activates factor X. The PTT test depends on this step, so low factor VIII prolongs it. The PT test adds tissue factor, which with factor VIIa activates factor X without needing factors IX or VIII. In the body, tissue factor-VIIa makes only a small amount of thrombin, and the tenase complex is needed to amplify it on platelets. This is why hemophilia causes serious bleeding even though the extrinsic pathway is intact.","whyNot":["Fibrin is cross-linked by factor XIIIa, and neither the PT nor the PTT detects that step.","Factor VIII is not consumed in the PT reagent. Its level is simply irrelevant to the PT.","Correct.","Factor VIII does not activate factor VII. Factor VII belongs to the extrinsic pathway, which the PT measures."],"study":{"topics":["Tenase complex","Intrinsic versus extrinsic pathway"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-9-C2"]}},{"id":"HE-C17-Q3","stem":"Which of the following additional laboratory results is most likely in this patient?","options":["Low platelet count and normal bleeding time","Normal platelet count and normal bleeding time","Low platelet count and prolonged bleeding time","Normal platelet count and prolonged bleeding time"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.5","dok":2,"approach":["Platelet count and bleeding time test primary hemostasis, the platelet plug.","PT, PTT, and the mixing study test secondary hemostasis, the clotting factors.","His defect is in a clotting factor, so his platelet plug forms normally."],"why":"Bleeding disorders can be sorted by which step fails. Platelet number problems lower the platelet count, and platelet function or von Willebrand factor problems prolong the bleeding time with a normal count; both cause mucosal and skin bleeding. Clotting factor deficiencies prolong the PT, the PTT, or both, and a mixing study separates a missing factor from an inhibitor. Consumption, as in DIC, lowers platelets and fibrinogen and prolongs both clotting times. In hemophilia, the platelet plug forms normally but is not stabilized by fibrin, so small cuts stop at first and then rebleed, and deep bleeds into joints and muscles are typical.","whyNot":["A low platelet count with a normal bleeding time is unlikely unless the drop is mild. Either way, hemophilia does not lower the platelet count.","Correct.","A low platelet count with a prolonged bleeding time fits thrombocytopenia, as in immune thrombocytopenic purpura, which causes petechiae and mucosal bleeding.","A prolonged bleeding time with a normal platelet count points to a platelet function defect or von Willebrand disease."],"study":{"topics":["Interpreting bleeding tests","Primary versus secondary hemostasis"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-9-C3"]}},{"id":"HE-C17-Q4","stem":"His sister plans to have children with a man who does not have hemophilia. What is the probability that her first son will have hemophilia A?","options":["0%","12.5%","25%","50%"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-6","obj":"HE-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Hemophilia A is X-linked recessive.","His mother has an affected son and an affected brother, so she is an obligate carrier.","His sister has a 1/2 chance of being a carrier, and a carrier's son has a 1/2 chance of being affected, so 1/2 times 1/2 is 1/4."],"why":"In X-linked recessive disorders, affected males pass the gene to all daughters and to no sons, and carrier females pass it to half of their children. The boy's mother must be a carrier because both her son and her brother are affected. Their father is unaffected, so the sister got a normal X from him and has a 50% chance of having received the mutant X from her mother. If she is a carrier, each son has a 50% chance of hemophilia. Multiplying these gives 25%. Hemophilia B and G6PD deficiency follow the same pattern, while sickle cell disease and thalassemias are autosomal recessive and most hereditary spherocytosis and von Willebrand disease are autosomal dominant.","whyNot":["A 0% risk would apply only if the sister were known not to be a carrier, which carrier testing could show.","12.5% (1/2 times 1/2 times 1/2) would be the risk for a son of the sister's daughter, not for the sister's own son.","Correct.","50% is the risk for a son of a known carrier. The sister is only a possible carrier."],"study":{"topics":["X-linked recessive inheritance","Carrier risk calculation"],"comps":["HE-6","HE-9-C2"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C18","system":"HE","dx":"Von Willebrand disease type 1 with heavy menstrual bleeding","codes":["HE-9-C4"],"case":"A 16-year-old girl comes to the physician because of heavy menstrual periods since menarche at age 12. She changes pads every hour on the heaviest days and also has frequent nosebleeds and gum bleeding after brushing. Her mother has a similar history. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg. Physical examination shows pallor and a few bruises on the shins. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL (12 to 16), mean corpuscular volume 74 fL (80 to 100), ferritin 6 ng/mL (15 to 150), platelets 255,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), prothrombin time 12 seconds (11 to 13.5), partial thromboplastin time 39 seconds (25 to 35), von Willebrand factor antigen 34% (50 to 150), ristocetin cofactor activity 31% (50 to 150), and factor VIII activity 44% (50 to 150). Diagnosis: von Willebrand disease type 1.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C18-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her mildly prolonged partial thromboplastin time?","options":["Low von Willebrand factor blocks contact activation of factor XII","Low von Willebrand factor impairs platelet binding to fibrinogen","Low von Willebrand factor reduces hepatic synthesis of factor IX","Low von Willebrand factor shortens the half-life of factor VIII"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C4","obj":"HE-9-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Von Willebrand factor has two jobs: it links platelets to collagen and it carries factor VIII in plasma.","Bound factor VIII is protected from breakdown, so low von Willebrand factor leaves less factor VIII.","Factor VIII is in the intrinsic pathway, so its fall can prolong the PTT."],"why":"Von Willebrand factor binds exposed collagen and platelet GpIb, so its deficiency weakens platelet adhesion and causes mucosal bleeding, a prolonged bleeding time, and a normal platelet count. It also binds and stabilizes factor VIII in plasma. When von Willebrand factor is low, factor VIII is cleared faster, and its level falls with it. If factor VIII drops enough, the PTT prolongs, while the PT stays normal. Ristocetin cofactor activity, which measures von Willebrand factor binding to GpIb, is low in most types.","whyNot":["Contact activation of factor XII does not involve von Willebrand factor.","Platelets bind fibrinogen through GpIIb/IIIa during aggregation. That step does not need von Willebrand factor and is not measured by the PTT.","Factor IX is made in the liver and does not depend on von Willebrand factor for synthesis or survival.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Von Willebrand factor functions","Laboratory findings in von Willebrand disease"],"comps":["HE-9-C4","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C18-Q2","stem":"Her ristocetin-induced platelet aggregation is decreased. The same abnormal result would also be expected in which of the following disorders?","options":["Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Glanzmann thrombasthenia","Hemophilia A","Aspirin-induced platelet dysfunction"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C","obj":"HE-9-C.8","dok":3,"approach":["Ristocetin makes von Willebrand factor bind platelet GpIb, causing platelets to clump.","The test is abnormal when either von Willebrand factor or GpIb is lacking.","GpIb deficiency is Bernard-Soulier syndrome, which also shows large platelets and a low platelet count."],"why":"Ristocetin-induced aggregation tests the link between von Willebrand factor and GpIb, the adhesion step. In Bernard-Soulier syndrome, an autosomal recessive lack of GpIb, the test stays abnormal even when normal plasma is added, and platelets are large and reduced in number. In Glanzmann thrombasthenia, GpIIb/IIIa is missing, so platelets do not aggregate with ADP, collagen, or epinephrine, but ristocetin agglutination is normal and platelets look normal. Uremia causes an acquired platelet dysfunction from retained toxins that impairs adhesion and aggregation, and it improves with dialysis or desmopressin.","whyNot":["Correct.","Glanzmann thrombasthenia lacks GpIIb/IIIa, so aggregation with ADP and collagen fails while ristocetin agglutination is normal.","Hemophilia A is a clotting factor deficiency, and platelet adhesion and aggregation are normal.","Aspirin blocks thromboxane A2 production and reduces aggregation with arachidonic acid and collagen, but ristocetin agglutination does not depend on thromboxane."],"study":{"topics":["Bernard-Soulier syndrome versus Glanzmann thrombasthenia","Platelet function tests"],"comps":["HE-9-C","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C18-Q3","stem":"After platelets adhere at a site of injury and release ADP and thromboxane A2, which of the following directly links neighboring platelets into an aggregate?","options":["Factor XIIIa cross-linking strands of fibrin","Thromboxane A2 binding platelet GpIa/IIa receptors","Von Willebrand factor bridging collagen to GpIb","Fibrinogen bridging activated GpIIb/IIIa receptors"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Primary hemostasis runs in order: vasoconstriction, adhesion, activation, and aggregation.","ADP and thromboxane A2 activate platelets, which changes GpIIb/IIIa into a form that binds fibrinogen.","Each fibrinogen molecule can bind GpIIb/IIIa on two platelets, linking them together."],"why":"After injury, reflex vasoconstriction slows blood flow. Von Willebrand factor then attaches platelets to exposed collagen through GpIb. The adherent platelets activate, change shape, and release ADP from dense granules and newly made thromboxane A2, which recruit and activate more platelets. Activation switches on GpIIb/IIIa, and fibrinogen bridges these receptors on adjacent platelets to build the platelet plug. Secondary hemostasis then forms fibrin around the plug, and factor XIIIa cross-links it.","whyNot":["Factor XIIIa cross-links fibrin in secondary hemostasis, which stabilizes the clot after the platelet plug has formed.","Thromboxane A2 acts on its own G protein-coupled receptor to activate platelets. GpIa/IIa is a collagen receptor.","Von Willebrand factor bridging collagen to GpIb is adhesion, the first platelet step. It anchors platelets to the wall rather than linking them to each other.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Platelet aggregation","Primary hemostasis steps"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-9-C4"]}},{"id":"HE-C18-Q4","stem":"Her cousin has similar mucosal bleeding but normal von Willebrand studies. Electron microscopy shows that his platelets lack dense granules. Release of which of the following would be reduced when his platelets are activated?","options":["Factor V and thrombospondin","Platelet factor 4 and platelet-derived growth factor","ADP and serotonin","Fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Platelets have two main granule types, alpha and dense.","Alpha granules hold large proteins, and dense granules hold small molecules.","Dense granules contain ADP, calcium, and serotonin, so their absence reduces release of these."],"why":"Platelets bud off from megakaryocyte cytoplasm and carry granules packaged by the megakaryocyte. Dense granules store ADP, calcium, and serotonin, which recruit more platelets and support clotting on the platelet surface. Alpha granules store proteins, including von Willebrand factor, fibrinogen, factor V, thrombospondin, platelet factor 4, and platelet-derived growth factor. Without dense granules, released ADP is low, so secondary aggregation is weak and mucosal bleeding results.","whyNot":["Factor V and thrombospondin are also alpha granule proteins.","Platelet factor 4 and platelet-derived growth factor are alpha granule proteins.","Correct.","Fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor are stored in alpha granules."],"study":{"topics":["Platelet dense granules","Platelet storage pool disease"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-4"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C19","system":"HE","dx":"Vitamin K deficiency bleeding in a newborn who did not receive prophylaxis","codes":["HE-9-C5"],"case":"A 4-day-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of oozing from the umbilical stump and two black, tarry stools. She was born at home at term after an uncomplicated pregnancy, and her parents declined all newborn injections. She is exclusively breastfed. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 156/min, respirations are 44/min, and blood pressure is 70/42 mm Hg. Physical examination shows oozing at the umbilical stump and a few bruises on the back. The liver is not enlarged, and she is not jaundiced. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 12.8 g/dL (14.5 to 22.5), prothrombin time 42 seconds (reference for age about 10 to 16), and partial thromboplastin time 88 seconds (reference for age about 31 to 55). Bleeding stops within hours of intravenous vitamin K. Diagnosis: vitamin K deficiency bleeding of the newborn.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C19-Q1","stem":"When vitamin K deficiency develops gradually, the PT prolongs before the PTT. Which of the following vitamin K-dependent factors has the shortest plasma half-life and therefore falls first?","options":["Factor VII","Factor IX","Factor II (prothrombin)","Factor X"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C5","obj":"HE-9-C.6","dok":2,"approach":["The PT is the first test to prolong, so the first factor to fall must be one the PT measures but the PTT does not.","Factor VII is the only vitamin K-dependent factor in the extrinsic pathway alone.","Its half-life is about 4 to 6 hours, the shortest of the vitamin K-dependent procoagulants."],"why":"Factors II, VII, IX, and X need vitamin K for activity. When new synthesis stops, each factor falls at a rate set by its half-life. Factor VII falls within hours, so the PT prolongs first. Factors IX, X, and prothrombin fall more slowly, and once they drop the PTT prolongs too, as in this infant. Newborns are at risk because little vitamin K crosses the placenta, breast milk has little vitamin K, and the gut is not yet colonized by bacteria that make it. In older patients, fat malabsorption, long courses of broad-spectrum antibiotics, and poor intake are the usual causes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Factor IX is measured by the PTT, and its half-life is longer than that of factor VII.","Prothrombin (factor II) has the longest half-life, about 2 to 3 days, so it falls last.","Factor X has a half-life of about 1.5 to 2 days and is measured by both the PT and the PTT."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin K deficiency","Half-lives of clotting factors"],"comps":["HE-9-C5","HE-5"]}},{"id":"HE-C19-Q2","stem":"Which of the following results would best distinguish her coagulopathy from that caused by severe neonatal liver failure?","options":["Prolonged prothrombin time","Normal platelet count","Normal factor VIII activity","Prolonged partial thromboplastin time"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C5","obj":"HE-9-C.7","dok":3,"approach":["Both vitamin K deficiency and liver failure lower factors II, VII, IX, and X, so both prolong the PT and the PTT.","Factor VIII is made largely by endothelial cells, so it stays normal in vitamin K deficiency and normal or high in liver failure.","Liver failure also lowers platelets and fibrinogen, while vitamin K deficiency leaves them normal."],"why":"Severe liver failure reduces synthesis of almost all clotting factors, including fibrinogen and factor V, which do not need vitamin K. Platelets are also low from splenic sequestration and reduced thrombopoietin made by the liver. Factor VIII is normal or even high because endothelial cells produce it. Vitamin K deficiency affects only factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S, so the platelet count and fibrinogen are normal and the PT corrects quickly after vitamin K, as it did here. A failing liver does not respond to vitamin K.","whyNot":["A prolonged PT occurs in both disorders because both lower factor VII.","Correct.","Normal factor VIII is expected in both, since factor VIII is made by endothelial cells.","A prolonged PTT occurs in both once factors IX, X, and II fall."],"study":{"topics":["Coagulopathy of liver disease","Vitamin K deficiency labs"],"comps":["HE-9-C","HE-9-C5"]}},{"id":"HE-C19-Q3","stem":"The vitamin K-dependent factors made by her liver were released into plasma but could not bind to activated platelet membranes. Which of the following best explains this defect?","options":["They lacked hydroxylysine residues needed for cross-linking","They lacked the GPI anchor that holds proteins on membranes","They lacked gamma-carboxyglutamate residues to bind calcium","They lacked sialic acid residues needed for receptor binding"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-5","obj":"HE-5.7","dok":2,"approach":["Vitamin K is the cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver.","This enzyme adds a second carboxyl group to glutamate residues on factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S.","These gamma-carboxyglutamate residues bind calcium, which bridges the factors to phosphatidylserine on activated platelets."],"why":"Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase uses reduced vitamin K (hydroquinone) to convert glutamate residues in the Gla domain to gamma-carboxyglutamate. In the process vitamin K becomes an epoxide, and vitamin K epoxide reductase, the target of warfarin, regenerates it. The paired carboxyl groups chelate calcium, which lets the factors bind phosphatidylserine exposed on activated platelets. Assembly on this surface speeds factor activation many thousandfold. Without vitamin K, the liver releases undercarboxylated factors that cannot bind the membrane and so are nearly inactive.","whyNot":["Hydroxylysine depends on vitamin C and is needed for collagen cross-linking. A lack causes scurvy with fragile vessels.","GPI anchors hold proteins such as CD55 and CD59 to cell membranes. Clotting factors are soluble plasma proteins without GPI anchors.","Correct.","Sialic acid is a sugar on many glycoproteins. It is not how clotting factors bind platelet phospholipid."],"study":{"topics":["Gamma-carboxylation","Vitamin K cycle"],"comps":["HE-5","HE-9-C5"]}},{"id":"HE-C19-Q4","stem":"Which of the following pairs of results would best support a factor deficiency rather than consumption of clotting factors as the cause of her bleeding?","options":["Normal fibrinogen and normal D-dimer","Normal fibrinogen and increased D-dimer","Low fibrinogen and normal D-dimer","Low fibrinogen and increased D-dimer"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.5","dok":3,"approach":["Both PT and PTT are prolonged, which can mean low factors from poor synthesis or from consumption.","Consumption, as in DIC, uses up fibrinogen and forms fibrin that plasmin breaks into D-dimer.","A synthesis problem limited to vitamin K-dependent factors leaves fibrinogen and D-dimer normal."],"why":"A prolonged PT and PTT can come from low factor production, a circulating inhibitor, or consumption. Fibrinogen and D-dimer help decide. In DIC, fibrinogen falls, D-dimer rises, and platelets drop. With vitamin K deficiency, fibrinogen is not vitamin K-dependent and no excess fibrin is formed, so both stay normal. A mixing study that corrects the PT and PTT would further confirm a factor deficiency rather than an inhibitor.","whyNot":["Correct.","Normal fibrinogen with high D-dimer suggests a localized clot, such as venous thrombosis, without systemic consumption.","Low fibrinogen with normal D-dimer suggests reduced fibrinogen production, as in liver failure or congenital hypofibrinogenemia, without consumption.","Low fibrinogen with high D-dimer is the pattern of consumption in DIC."],"study":{"topics":["Localizing a bleeding disorder","DIC versus factor deficiency"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-9-C1"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C20","system":"HE","dx":"Heterozygous factor V Leiden with postpartum deep vein thrombosis","codes":["HE-6","HE-9-C"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of pain and swelling of her left leg for 2 days. She had a cesarean delivery 3 weeks ago. Her mother had a pulmonary embolism at age 45. She does not smoke. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 122/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a warm, tender left calf that measures 4 cm more in circumference than the right. Platelets are 310,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), prothrombin time is 12 seconds (11 to 13.5), and partial thromboplastin time is 30 seconds (25 to 35). Duplex ultrasonography shows an occlusive thrombus in the left femoral and popliteal veins. Genetic testing shows that she is heterozygous for factor V Leiden (R506Q). Diagnosis: postpartum deep vein thrombosis in a woman heterozygous for factor V Leiden.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C20-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains how her mutation promotes venous thrombosis?","options":["Low protein S leaves activated protein C with little activity","Antithrombin fails to bind heparan sulfate on endothelium","Prothrombin levels rise from a 3' untranslated region variant","Factor Va resists cleavage by activated protein C"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-6","obj":"HE-6.7","dok":2,"approach":["Activated protein C normally shuts down clotting by cleaving factors Va and VIIIa.","Factor V Leiden replaces arginine 506, the cleavage site for activated protein C, with glutamine.","Factor Va then stays active longer and keeps making thrombin."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is a point mutation (R506Q) at the main site where activated protein C cuts factor Va. The mutant factor Va is inactivated about ten times more slowly, so the prothrombinase complex keeps generating thrombin. It is the most common inherited thrombophilia in people of European descent, and heterozygotes have several times the normal risk of venous thrombosis. The risk multiplies with other factors such as pregnancy, the postpartum period, surgery, or estrogen. The prothrombin G20210A mutation, a different variant, raises prothrombin levels.","whyNot":["Low protein S is protein S deficiency. Her mutation is in factor V itself, which leaves protein C and protein S levels normal.","Failure of antithrombin activity is antithrombin deficiency, which causes heparin resistance.","A variant in the 3' untranslated region that raises prothrombin levels is the prothrombin G20210A mutation, the second most common inherited thrombophilia.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Factor V Leiden","Prothrombin G20210A"],"comps":["HE-6","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C20-Q2","stem":"A different patient with a first deep vein thrombosis at age 24 needs unusually high doses of unfractionated heparin to prolong the PTT. Deficiency of which of the following is most likely?","options":["Protein S","Tissue plasminogen activator","Protein C","Antithrombin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C","obj":"HE-9-C.9","dok":3,"approach":["Heparin works by binding antithrombin and greatly speeding its inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa.","If antithrombin is low, heparin has little to act on, so the PTT does not prolong as expected.","A clot at a young age plus heparin resistance points to inherited antithrombin deficiency."],"why":"Antithrombin is a serine protease inhibitor that neutralizes thrombin, factor Xa, and other activated factors, and heparin speeds this about a thousandfold. Inherited antithrombin deficiency, usually autosomal dominant, causes venous thrombosis and resistance to heparin. Protein C and protein S deficiencies also cause venous clots and raise the risk of warfarin-induced skin necrosis, but heparin works normally. Hyperhomocysteinemia injures endothelium and raises both venous and arterial risk. A clot before about age 50, recurrent clots, clots in unusual sites such as cerebral or mesenteric veins, or a strong family history suggest an inherited thrombophilia.","whyNot":["Protein S is the cofactor for activated protein C. Its deficiency causes venous thrombosis without heparin resistance.","Low tissue plasminogen activator would slow fibrinolysis, but heparin would still work normally through antithrombin.","Protein C deficiency causes venous thrombosis and warfarin-induced skin necrosis, but heparin works normally.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Inherited thrombophilias","Antithrombin and heparin"],"comps":["HE-9-C","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C20-Q3","stem":"Her D-dimer is markedly increased. Which of the following best explains the presence of this fragment in her plasma?","options":["Thrombin cleaved fibrinopeptides from fibrinogen","Activated protein C degraded factors Va and VIIIa","Plasmin degraded cross-linked fibrin in the clot","Plasmin degraded circulating fibrinogen"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["D-dimer contains two D domains joined by a covalent cross-link.","Only factor XIIIa makes that cross-link, and only within fibrin.","When plasmin breaks down cross-linked fibrin, D-dimer is released, so its presence shows that a clot formed and is being lysed."],"why":"Tissue plasminogen activator from endothelium binds fibrin and converts plasminogen to plasmin on the clot. Plasmin cuts fibrin into fibrin degradation products, and pieces that keep the factor XIIIa cross-link between adjacent D domains are D-dimer. Plasmin acting on fibrinogen alone makes degradation products but no D-dimer, because fibrinogen is not cross-linked. Other natural brakes on clotting work earlier: antithrombin inhibits thrombin and Xa, thrombomodulin turns thrombin into an activator of protein C, and tissue factor pathway inhibitor blocks tissue factor-VIIa.","whyNot":["Thrombin releases fibrinopeptides A and B when it converts fibrinogen to fibrin. These are not D-dimer.","Activated protein C inactivates factors Va and VIIIa, which limits thrombin generation but does not produce fibrin fragments.","Correct.","Plasmin breakdown of fibrinogen makes fibrinogen degradation products but no D-dimer, because fibrinogen has no cross-links."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrinolysis and D-dimer","Natural anticoagulants"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-9-C1"]}},{"id":"HE-C20-Q4","stem":"Her thrombophilia workup also includes tests for antiphospholipid antibodies, which can prolong the PTT in the laboratory yet cause clots in patients. Which of the following best explains this paradox?","options":["They inhibit factor VIII in the tube but spare it in the body","They block reagent phospholipid but activate cells in vivo","They consume clotting factors in vivo, which prolongs the PTT","They cause thrombocytopenia that triggers excess factor synthesis"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-7","obj":"HE-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Antiphospholipid antibodies bind proteins attached to phospholipid, mainly beta2 glycoprotein I.","In the test tube they tie up the phospholipid that the PTT reagent needs, so clotting is slower.","In the body they activate endothelium, platelets, monocytes, and complement, which promotes clotting."],"why":"Antiphospholipid antibodies include lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, and anti-beta2 glycoprotein I antibodies. In vitro, they bind the phospholipid-protein complexes the PTT reagent supplies, so the PTT prolongs and does not correct on mixing. In vivo, binding of beta2 glycoprotein I on cell surfaces activates endothelial cells to express tissue factor and adhesion molecules, activates platelets, and triggers complement. This causes venous and arterial thrombosis and placental injury with pregnancy loss. The syndrome can be primary or occur with systemic lupus erythematosus.","whyNot":["An antibody that inhibits factor VIII causes acquired hemophilia, with bleeding rather than clotting.","Correct.","Consumption of clotting factors causes DIC-like bleeding, and it is not how these antibodies prolong the PTT.","Antiphospholipid syndrome can cause mild thrombocytopenia, but low platelets do not trigger excess factor synthesis or clotting."],"study":{"topics":["Antiphospholipid syndrome","Lupus anticoagulant paradox"],"comps":["HE-7","HE-9-C"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C21","system":"HE","dx":"Langerhans cell histiocytosis with lytic skull lesions and central diabetes insipidus","codes":["HE-9-D"],"case":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of increased thirst and urination for 2 months and a painless swelling on his head. He wakes at night to drink water. His temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a 2-cm soft, nontender mass over the left parietal bone and a scaly papular eruption on the scalp. Serum sodium is 147 mEq/L (reference, 135 to 145 mEq/L), and serum osmolality is 302 mOsm/kg H2O (reference, 275 to 295 mOsm/kg H2O). Urine osmolality is 110 mOsm/kg H2O and rises after desmopressin is given. X-rays show several punched-out lytic lesions of the skull. Biopsy of the mass shows sheets of histiocytes with grooved nuclei and many eosinophils. Diagnosis: Langerhans cell histiocytosis with central diabetes insipidus.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C21-Q1","stem":"Immunohistochemical staining shows that the lesional histiocytes are positive for CD1a and S100. Electron microscopy of these cells is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Birbeck granules","Russell bodies","Weibel-Palade bodies","Auer rods"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D","obj":"HE-9-D.13","dok":2,"approach":["CD1a and S100 positivity identify the lesional cells as Langerhans cells.","Langerhans cells contain a unique cytoplasmic organelle seen by electron microscopy.","That organelle is the rod-shaped Birbeck granule, which can have a terminal bulge like a tennis racket."],"why":"Langerhans cell histiocytosis is a clonal proliferation of Langerhans-type dendritic cells. These cells are CD1a positive and S100 positive (and langerin, CD207, positive). On electron microscopy they contain Birbeck granules, rod-shaped organelles that sometimes have a bulb at one end. Children present with lytic bone lesions (eosinophilic granuloma when single) or with multisystem disease, in which pituitary stalk involvement causes central diabetes insipidus.","whyNot":["Correct.","Russell bodies are cytoplasmic inclusions of stored immunoglobulin in plasma cells.","Weibel-Palade bodies store von Willebrand factor and P-selectin in endothelial cells.","Auer rods are fused azurophilic granules in myeloblasts of acute myeloid leukemia."],"study":{"topics":["Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Cell inclusions in hematopathology"],"comps":["HE-9-D","HE-1"]}},{"id":"HE-C21-Q2","stem":"The many eosinophils in the lesion are recruited from the blood. These eosinophils developed in the bone marrow from which of the following progenitor cells?","options":["Megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor","Early thymic T cell progenitor","Common lymphoid progenitor","Common myeloid progenitor"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Start at the hematopoietic stem cell, which splits into common myeloid and common lymphoid progenitors.","The lymphoid branch makes B cells, T cells, and NK cells.","The myeloid branch makes granulocytes (including eosinophils), monocytes, red cells, and megakaryocytes."],"why":"All blood cells come from the hematopoietic stem cell. It gives rise to a common myeloid progenitor and a common lymphoid progenitor. The common myeloid progenitor gives rise to the granulocyte and monocyte lines (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, monocytes) and to the megakaryocyte-erythroid line. Eosinophil production is driven mainly by IL-5. The common lymphoid progenitor gives rise to B cells, T cells, and NK cells.","whyNot":["The megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor branches off the myeloid line and makes only red cells and platelets.","Early thymic progenitors give rise to T cells in the thymus, not to granulocytes.","The common lymphoid progenitor makes B cells, T cells, and NK cells. It does not make granulocytes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hematopoiesis lineage tree"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C21-Q3","stem":"Before the biopsy, the differential diagnosis of the skull lesion included acute bacterial osteomyelitis. Which of the following findings on a complete blood count would most support a pyogenic bacterial infection?","options":["Lymphocytosis with atypical lymphocytes","Basophilia","Neutrophilia with increased band forms","Eosinophilia"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-2","obj":"HE-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Pyogenic bacteria are cleared mainly by neutrophils, which phagocytose and kill them.","Bacterial infection triggers release of neutrophils, including immature band forms, from the marrow reserve.","So neutrophilia with a left shift points to bacterial infection."],"why":"Each white cell type has a typical trigger. Neutrophils are the first-line phagocytes against bacteria, and acute bacterial infection releases mature and band neutrophils from the marrow (a left shift). Eosinophilia points to parasitic (especially tissue helminth) or allergic disease. Lymphocytosis, often with atypical reactive lymphocytes, points to viral infection. Basophilia is uncommon and suggests a myeloproliferative neoplasm such as CML.","whyNot":["Lymphocytosis with atypical lymphocytes is typical of viral infection such as infectious mononucleosis.","Basophilia is rare in infection and is a clue to chronic myeloid leukemia or another myeloproliferative neoplasm.","Correct.","Eosinophilia suggests helminth infection or allergic disease. It does not suggest a pyogenic bacterial infection."],"study":{"topics":["White cell functions","Interpreting the white cell differential"],"comps":["HE-2","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C21-Q4","stem":"During chemotherapy for multisystem disease, the boy receives a unit of packed red cells. Within 15 minutes he develops fever, flank pain, hypotension, and red urine. A clerical error is found; group A blood was given to a group O recipient. This reaction belongs to the same hypersensitivity type as which of the following?","options":["Anaphylaxis in IgA deficiency","Hemolytic disease of the newborn","Mixed cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Allergic contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-7","obj":"HE-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction from ABO-incompatible blood.","Preformed recipient anti-A antibodies bind donor red cell antigens and fix complement, which is type II hypersensitivity.","Find the other condition caused by antibodies against a cell-surface antigen."],"why":"In an ABO-incompatible transfusion, the recipient's preformed IgM anti-A antibodies bind the donor red cells and activate complement, causing intravascular hemolysis within minutes. Antibody directed against an antigen on a cell surface is type II hypersensitivity. Hemolytic disease of the newborn is also type II: maternal IgG crosses the placenta and coats fetal red cells, which are then destroyed. Autoimmune hemolytic anemia belongs here too.","whyNot":["Anaphylaxis to IgA in an IgA-deficient recipient is type I hypersensitivity, mediated by IgE on mast cells.","Correct.","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis is type III. Circulating immune complexes deposit in vessel walls and activate complement.","Allergic contact dermatitis is type IV, a delayed reaction mediated by sensitized T cells."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types in hematology","Transfusion reactions"],"comps":["HE-7","HE-5"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C22","system":"HE","dx":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia with thrombosis after pancreaticoduodenectomy","codes":["HE-7","HE-9-C"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman undergoes pancreaticoduodenectomy for adenocarcinoma of the head of the pancreas. Subcutaneous unfractionated heparin is started on the first postoperative day for thromboprophylaxis. Her platelet count is 260,000/mm3 (reference, 150,000 to 400,000/mm3) before surgery and 95,000/mm3 on postoperative day 7. On day 8 she develops swelling and pain of the left calf. Her temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. Duplex ultrasonography shows thrombus in the left femoral and popliteal veins. An immunoassay for antibodies against platelet factor 4 and heparin is positive, and a serotonin release assay is positive. Diagnosis: heparin-induced thrombocytopenia with thrombosis.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C22-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains both the fall in her platelet count and her venous thrombosis?","options":["Loss of ADAMTS13 activity leaving large vWF multimers","Direct nonimmune aggregation of platelets by heparin","IgG against platelet GpIIb/IIIa causing splenic clearance","IgG against PF4-heparin complexes activating platelets"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-7","obj":"HE-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["A platelet drop of more than half, 5 to 10 days after starting heparin, with new thrombosis is the classic pattern.","The positive PF4-heparin immunoassay and serotonin release assay show a functional, platelet-activating antibody.","Activated platelets aggregate and release procoagulant microparticles, so platelets fall while clots form."],"why":"Heparin binds platelet factor 4 (PF4), a positively charged protein released from platelet granules, and the complex exposes new epitopes. The patient makes IgG against PF4-heparin. These immune complexes bind platelet Fc gamma receptors (FcγRIIa) and activate the platelets, which aggregate, release more PF4, and generate thrombin. Activated platelets are cleared, so the count falls, and the same activation produces venous and arterial thrombosis. This is why HIT is a prothrombotic disorder even though platelets are low.","whyNot":["ADAMTS13 deficiency causes thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, with schistocytes and microvascular platelet thrombi rather than large-vein thrombosis.","Nonimmune heparin-associated thrombocytopenia occurs in the first 1 to 2 days, is mild, and does not cause thrombosis.","Antibodies against GpIIb/IIIa cause immune thrombocytopenia (ITP), which causes bleeding, not thrombosis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"],"comps":["HE-7","HE-9-C"]}},{"id":"HE-C22-Q2","stem":"Five months before her cancer was diagnosed, she had several episodes of tender, cordlike superficial veins that appeared in the right forearm, resolved, and then appeared in the left thigh. Which of the following most likely explains those episodes?","options":["Ectopic erythropoietin raising red cell mass","Inherited factor V resistant to activated protein C","Procoagulant mucins and tissue factor from tumor","Immune complex deposition in small-vessel walls"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C","obj":"HE-9-C.10","dok":2,"approach":["Recurrent superficial venous thrombosis that moves from site to site is migratory thrombophlebitis.","Appearing before a pancreatic adenocarcinoma is found, it is a paraneoplastic syndrome (Trousseau syndrome).","The tumor releases procoagulants such as mucin and tissue factor that activate coagulation."],"why":"Trousseau syndrome is migratory thrombophlebitis linked to an underlying cancer, classically mucin-secreting adenocarcinoma of the pancreas, stomach, or lung. Tumor cells release tissue factor, mucins, and other procoagulants that activate coagulation throughout the body. The thromboses can appear before the cancer is found, so unexplained migratory thrombophlebitis calls for a search for malignancy. Other hematologic paraneoplastic syndromes include erythrocytosis from ectopic erythropoietin (renal cell and hepatocellular carcinoma) and pure red cell aplasia with thymoma.","whyNot":["Ectopic erythropoietin from renal cell or hepatocellular carcinoma causes erythrocytosis, not migratory superficial thrombophlebitis.","Factor V Leiden raises lifelong risk of deep venous thrombosis but does not cause migratory superficial thrombophlebitis heralding a cancer.","Correct.","Immune complex deposition in small vessels causes leukocytoclastic vasculitis with palpable purpura, not tender cordlike veins that move from site to site."],"study":{"topics":["Trousseau syndrome","Hematologic paraneoplastic syndromes"],"comps":["HE-9-C","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C22-Q3","stem":"After heparin is stopped, her platelet count recovers. She then begins adjuvant chemotherapy. Assuming a single cycle abruptly halts marrow output and there is no bleeding or hemolysis, which of the following patterns of blood counts is expected over the next 3 weeks?","options":["Anemia first, then neutropenia, then thrombocytopenia","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia first, anemia much later","Anemia and thrombocytopenia first, neutropenia much later","Thrombocytopenia first, then anemia, then neutropenia"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-3","obj":"HE-3.5","dok":3,"approach":["When production stops, a cell count falls at a rate set by how long that cell survives in the blood.","Neutrophils circulate for only hours, and platelets live about 7 to 10 days.","Red cells live about 120 days, so hemoglobin falls only about 1% per day and anemia appears last."],"why":"After sudden marrow suppression, the cells with the shortest life span disappear first. Neutrophils circulate for less than a day, so neutropenia follows as soon as the marrow reserve is used up, usually within 1 to 2 weeks. Platelets survive about 7 to 10 days, so thrombocytopenia appears on a similar time scale. Red cells survive about 120 days, so loss of production lowers hemoglobin slowly, and anemia comes much later unless there is bleeding or hemolysis.","whyNot":["Anemia cannot come first after marrow suppression, because red cells have the longest life span (about 120 days).","Correct.","Red cells outlive neutrophils by months, so anemia does not appear with or before neutropenia.","Thrombocytopenia does appear early, but neutropenia appears at least as early and anemia comes last, not in the middle."],"study":{"topics":["Life spans of blood cells","Chemotherapy-induced cytopenias"],"comps":["HE-3","HE-9-A"]}},{"id":"HE-C22-Q4","stem":"Even without heparin, she was at high risk for deep venous thrombosis. Postoperative bed rest contributes to that risk mainly through which of the following?","options":["Injury of venous endothelium exposing tissue factor","Antibody-mediated activation of circulating platelets","Increased hepatic production of procoagulant proteins","Loss of calf muscle pump action, slowing venous flow"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.6","dok":2,"approach":["Virchow triad: stasis, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability.","Immobility removes the calf muscle pump, so blood pools in deep leg veins.","Stasis keeps activated clotting factors in place and lets platelets reach the vessel wall."],"why":"Virchow triad names three conditions that promote thrombosis: stasis of blood, injury to the endothelium, and hypercoagulability. Bed rest abolishes the calf muscle pump, so flow in deep leg veins slows. Slow flow prevents dilution and clearance of activated clotting factors and lets platelets contact the endothelium. In this patient, surgery adds endothelial injury and a hypercoagulable state, and her pancreatic adenocarcinoma adds hypercoagulability. Pregnancy and estrogen use act mainly by raising clotting factor levels.","whyNot":["Endothelial injury is the surgery's contribution, not the contribution of immobility.","Antibody-mediated platelet activation is the mechanism of HIT, which is separate from the risk created by bed rest.","Higher hepatic production of clotting factors is how estrogen and pregnancy cause hypercoagulability.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Virchow triad","Risk factors for venous thrombosis"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-9-C"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C23","system":"HE","dx":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia with warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia (Evans syndrome)","codes":["HE-9-D1","HE-9-C3"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue and easy bruising for 3 weeks. His temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 132/78 mm Hg. He has scleral icterus, petechiae on the legs, painless cervical and axillary lymphadenopathy, and a spleen tip palpable 3 cm below the left costal margin. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.8 g/dL (reference, 13.5 to 17.5 g/dL), reticulocytes 9% (reference, 0.5% to 2.5%), platelets 18,000/mm3 (reference, 150,000 to 400,000/mm3), and leukocytes 62,000/mm3 (reference, 4,500 to 11,000/mm3) with 85% small mature lymphocytes and many smudge cells. Serum LDH is 480 U/L (reference, 140 to 280 U/L), and haptoglobin is undetectable. Flow cytometry shows a clonal B cell population positive for CD5 and CD23. Diagnosis: chronic lymphocytic leukemia with autoimmune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C23-Q1","stem":"During the past 2 years he has had three episodes of pneumococcal pneumonia. His absolute neutrophil count is 2,800/mm3 (reference, 1,500 to 8,000/mm3). Which of the following best explains his susceptibility to these infections?","options":["Hypogammaglobulinemia","Defective neutrophil oxidative burst","Impaired cytotoxic T cell function","Deficiency of terminal complement components"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D1","obj":"HE-9-D.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recurrent infection with an encapsulated bacterium points to a lack of opsonizing antibody.","In CLL the clonal B cells do not make useful antibody and they suppress normal B cells.","The result is hypogammaglobulinemia, a common cause of infection in CLL."],"why":"CLL is a neoplasm of mature, CD5-positive, CD23-positive B cells that mostly affects older adults. The smear shows small mature lymphocytes and smudge cells, which are fragile cells crushed during smear preparation. Normal B cell function is suppressed, so most patients develop hypogammaglobulinemia. Without IgG to opsonize encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, recurrent pneumonia follows. Other features of CLL include warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia and Richter transformation to diffuse large B cell lymphoma.","whyNot":["Correct.","A defective oxidative burst (chronic granulomatous disease) causes infections with catalase-positive organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus and Aspergillus.","Impaired T cell function predisposes to viral, fungal, and intracellular infections more than to recurrent pneumococcal pneumonia.","Terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency predisposes mainly to recurrent Neisseria infections."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Infection patterns by immune defect"],"comps":["HE-9-D1","HE-7"]}},{"id":"HE-C23-Q2","stem":"A direct antiglobulin test is positive with anti-IgG reagent and negative with anti-C3 reagent. The peripheral blood smear shows many spherocytes. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of his hemolysis?","options":["Warm-reacting IgG, with splenic phagocytosis","Shearing of red cells on fibrin strands in small vessels","Complement lysis of cells lacking GPI-anchored proteins","IgM fixing complement in the cold, with agglutination"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-7","obj":"HE-7.1","dok":3,"approach":["A positive direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test means antibody or complement is on the red cell surface.","IgG without C3 that causes spherocytes is warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, a known complication of CLL.","Splenic macrophages take bites of IgG-coated membrane, leaving spherocytes, and then remove the cells (extravascular hemolysis)."],"why":"Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia is caused by IgG autoantibodies that bind red cells best at 37 °C (98.6 °F). Macrophages in the spleen bind the Fc portion of IgG and remove pieces of membrane, turning the cells into spherocytes, and then destroy them, so hemolysis is mainly extravascular. It is associated with CLL, SLE, and some drugs. Cold agglutinin disease is caused by IgM that binds in cooler body parts and fixes complement, so the direct antiglobulin test is positive for C3. It follows Mycoplasma pneumoniae or EBV infection.","whyNot":["Correct.","Shearing on fibrin strands is microangiopathic hemolysis. It produces schistocytes and a negative Coombs test.","Complement lysis of cells lacking GPI-anchored CD55 and CD59 is paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, which is Coombs negative.","IgM cold agglutinins fix complement, so the test would be positive for C3. This is cold agglutinin disease."],"study":{"topics":["Warm versus cold autoimmune hemolysis","Direct antiglobulin test"],"comps":["HE-7","HE-3"]}},{"id":"HE-C23-Q3","stem":"His coagulation studies are normal, and no schistocytes are seen. A bone marrow biopsy shows increased numbers of megakaryocytes. Which of the following best explains his thrombocytopenia?","options":["Marrow replacement of megakaryocytes by leukemic cells","Reduced hepatic synthesis of thrombopoietin","IgG against GpIIb/IIIa leading to splenic clearance","Platelet consumption in widespread microvascular thrombi"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C3","obj":"HE-9-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Increased marrow megakaryocytes mean platelets are being made, so the problem is peripheral destruction.","Normal coagulation tests and no schistocytes argue against consumptive processes such as DIC or TTP.","Autoantibodies against platelet glycoproteins, with clearance in the spleen, are immune thrombocytopenia."],"why":"In immune thrombocytopenia (ITP), IgG autoantibodies bind platelet membrane glycoproteins, most often GpIIb/IIIa. Splenic macrophages remove the coated platelets, and the spleen also makes much of the antibody. The marrow responds with more megakaryocytes. Platelets are low while the other lines and coagulation tests are normal, unless another process is present. Acute ITP in children follows a viral infection and usually resolves; chronic ITP in adults, mostly women, persists. ITP can also be secondary to CLL, SLE, or HIV, and ITP with autoimmune hemolysis is Evans syndrome.","whyNot":["Marrow replacement would reduce megakaryocytes. This biopsy shows them increased.","Low thrombopoietin, as in liver failure, would reduce megakaryocytes rather than increase them.","Correct.","Consumption in microthrombi (DIC, TTP) causes schistocytes, and DIC prolongs clotting times. Neither is present."],"study":{"topics":["Immune thrombocytopenia","Evans syndrome"],"comps":["HE-9-C3","HE-7"]}},{"id":"HE-C23-Q4","stem":"His physician also considered another B cell neoplasm of older men that causes pancytopenia, massive splenomegaly without lymphadenopathy, and a dry tap on marrow aspiration, with cells positive for tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase and CD11c. That neoplasm is most often driven by which of the following genetic changes?","options":["BCR-ABL1 fusion from t(9;22)","Cyclin D1 overexpression from t(11;14)","JAK2 V617F point mutation","BRAF V600E point mutation"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-9-D1","obj":"HE-9-D.5","dok":2,"approach":["TRAP positivity, CD11c, massive splenomegaly, and a dry tap identify hairy cell leukemia.","Nearly all cases carry an activating BRAF V600E mutation.","BRAF V600E constantly activates the MAP kinase pathway, driving cell growth and survival."],"why":"Hairy cell leukemia is a neoplasm of mature B cells with fine hairlike cytoplasmic projections. The cells are TRAP positive and CD11c positive. They infiltrate the splenic red pulp and the marrow, where they cause reticulin fibrosis, so aspiration often gives a dry tap. Patients have pancytopenia and massive splenomegaly, usually without lymphadenopathy, which helps separate it from CLL. Almost every case has a BRAF V600E mutation, which activates RAF-MEK-ERK signaling.","whyNot":["BCR-ABL1 from t(9;22) defines chronic myeloid leukemia and some cases of ALL.","Cyclin D1 overexpression from t(11;14) defines mantle cell lymphoma, a CD5-positive B cell neoplasm.","JAK2 V617F drives myeloproliferative neoplasms such as polycythemia vera, not B cell neoplasms.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hairy cell leukemia","Driver mutations in hematologic neoplasms"],"comps":["HE-9-D1","HE-6"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C24","system":"HE","dx":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia in a child with Down syndrome, complicated by tumor lysis syndrome","codes":["HE-9-D1"],"case":"A 5-year-old girl with Down syndrome is brought to the physician because of fatigue, leg pain, and limping for 3 weeks. Her temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), pulse is 132/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 98/58 mm Hg. She is pale and has petechiae, tenderness over both tibias, and hepatosplenomegaly. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 6.9 g/dL (reference, 11.5 to 13.5 g/dL), platelets 22,000/mm3 (reference, 150,000 to 400,000/mm3), and leukocytes 86,000/mm3 (reference, 5,000 to 14,500/mm3) with 80% blasts. Bone marrow aspirate is replaced by blasts expressing CD10 and CD19. Diagnosis: precursor B cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Induction chemotherapy is started.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C24-Q1","stem":"Children with Down syndrome also have a greatly increased risk of acute megakaryoblastic leukemia. Which of the following findings in her blasts best identifies them as lymphoblasts?","options":["Myeloperoxidase-positive cytoplasmic granules","Nuclear terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase","Tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase activity","Needle-shaped cytoplasmic Auer rods"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D1","obj":"HE-9-D.1","dok":2,"approach":["Both ALL and acute megakaryoblastic leukemia occur more often in Down syndrome, so the lineage must be proven.","TdT is a DNA polymerase used in V(D)J recombination by immature B and T cells.","Nuclear TdT marks lymphoblasts; myeloperoxidase and Auer rods mark myeloid blasts."],"why":"ALL is a neoplasm of immature lymphoid cells, mostly in children. The blasts express TdT, an enzyme that adds nucleotides during antigen receptor gene rearrangement, and B-ALL blasts also express CD10 and CD19. Children present with marrow failure (anemia, bleeding, fever) and bone pain from marrow expansion. ALL tends to spread to the CNS and testes, so CNS prophylaxis is part of treatment. Down syndrome raises the risk of both ALL and acute megakaryoblastic leukemia (AML M7), so lineage markers are needed.","whyNot":["Myeloperoxidase-positive granules identify myeloid blasts (AML). Lymphoblasts are myeloperoxidase negative.","Correct.","TRAP positivity marks hairy cell leukemia, a neoplasm of mature B cells in older adults.","Auer rods are fused azurophilic granules seen in myeloblasts and promyelocytes, not in lymphoblasts."],"study":{"topics":["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Markers of lymphoid versus myeloid blasts"],"comps":["HE-9-D1","HE-6"]}},{"id":"HE-C24-Q2","stem":"Forty-eight hours after induction begins, her urine output falls, she has muscle cramps and carpopedal spasm, and an ECG shows peaked T waves. Which of the following sets of serum findings is most likely?","options":["Potassium high, phosphate high, calcium low, uric acid high","Potassium high, phosphate high, calcium high, uric acid high","Potassium low, phosphate high, calcium low, uric acid high","Potassium high, phosphate low, calcium high, uric acid low"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-9-D","obj":"HE-9-D.14","dok":3,"approach":["Rapid killing of a large, fast-growing leukemia releases cell contents into the blood: tumor lysis syndrome.","Cells are rich in potassium and phosphate, and their nucleic acid purines are broken down by xanthine oxidase to uric acid.","Phosphate binds calcium and precipitates, lowering serum calcium; urate and calcium phosphate crystals injure the kidney."],"why":"Tumor lysis syndrome occurs when therapy kills a large, high-turnover tumor such as acute leukemia or Burkitt lymphoma. Released intracellular potassium causes hyperkalemia and peaked T waves. Released phosphate causes hyperphosphatemia and binds calcium, causing hypocalcemia with cramps and tetany. Purines from DNA and RNA are degraded through hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid by xanthine oxidase. Uric acid and calcium phosphate crystals deposit in renal tubules and cause acute kidney injury, which worsens the potassium and phosphate rise.","whyNot":["Correct.","Calcium falls in tumor lysis syndrome because released phosphate binds it. Hypercalcemia is not part of the pattern.","Potassium rises, not falls, because dying cells release their large intracellular potassium stores.","Phosphate and uric acid both rise from cell breakdown, and calcium falls."],"study":{"topics":["Tumor lysis syndrome","Purine degradation to uric acid"],"comps":["HE-9-D","HE-9-D1"]}},{"id":"HE-C24-Q3","stem":"Ten days into induction her absolute neutrophil count is 300/µL (reference, 1,500 to 8,000/µL), and she develops a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F). Which of the following best describes her neutropenia?","options":["Immune destruction, with mild infection risk","Splenic sequestration, with mainly viral infection risk","Marrow failure, with high bacterial and fungal risk","Inherited ELANE mutation, with infections every 21 days"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-A","obj":"HE-9-A.10","dok":3,"approach":["Classify by mechanism: her marrow is replaced by leukemia and suppressed by chemotherapy, so production has failed.","An absolute neutrophil count below 500/µL is severe neutropenia.","Severe neutropenia carries a high risk of serious bacterial and fungal infection, so this fever is treated as an emergency."],"why":"Neutropenia can come from marrow failure (aplastic anemia, leukemia, chemotherapy), from idiosyncratic drug-induced agranulocytosis (for example with propylthiouracil), from immune destruction, or from inherited causes such as cyclic neutropenia due to ELANE mutation. Here both the leukemia and the chemotherapy shut down production. The risk of infection rises steeply as the count falls, and below 500/µL patients are at high risk of bacterial sepsis (gram-negative rods, Staphylococcus) and invasive fungal infection (Candida, Aspergillus). Fever in this setting needs immediate empiric antibiotics.","whyNot":["Immune destruction is a different mechanism, and an absolute neutrophil count of 300/µL carries a high risk, not a mild one.","Splenic sequestration causes mild neutropenia, and neutropenia predisposes to bacterial and fungal infection rather than viral infection.","Correct.","Cyclic neutropenia from ELANE mutation causes recurring neutropenia about every 21 days from birth. Her neutropenia began with chemotherapy."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of neutropenia","Febrile neutropenia"],"comps":["HE-9-A","HE-3"]}},{"id":"HE-C24-Q4","stem":"She receives a recombinant cytokine that shortens her neutropenia. Her eosinophil and monocyte counts do not change during treatment. Which of the following cytokines did she most likely receive?","options":["Thrombopoietin","Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor","Interleukin-5","Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-4","obj":"HE-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Match each growth factor to the lineage it drives.","G-CSF acts mainly on the neutrophil line; GM-CSF also raises monocytes and eosinophils.","A rise in neutrophils without a change in monocytes or eosinophils fits G-CSF."],"why":"Each blood lineage has a main growth factor. Erythropoietin drives red cells, thrombopoietin drives megakaryocytes and platelets, IL-5 drives eosinophils, and G-CSF drives neutrophil production and release. GM-CSF acts earlier, on granulocyte-monocyte progenitors, so it raises neutrophils, monocytes, and eosinophils together. Recombinant G-CSF (filgrastim) is used to shorten chemotherapy-induced neutropenia.","whyNot":["Thrombopoietin drives megakaryocytes and platelet production, not neutrophils.","Correct.","IL-5 drives eosinophil production and would raise the eosinophil count without shortening neutropenia.","GM-CSF stimulates the granulocyte-monocyte progenitor broadly, so monocytes and eosinophils would also rise."],"study":{"topics":["Hematopoietic growth factors"],"comps":["HE-4","HE-1"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C25","system":"HE","dx":"Classical Hodgkin lymphoma, nodular sclerosis type","codes":["HE-9-D2"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a painless lump above her left clavicle for 2 months. She has had night sweats, generalized itching, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. Her temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a firm, rubbery, nontender 3-cm left supraclavicular lymph node and a 2-cm left lower cervical node. Chest x-ray shows an anterior mediastinal mass. Excisional biopsy of the supraclavicular node shows broad collagen bands dividing the node into nodules containing lacunar cells, eosinophils, and small lymphocytes. The large atypical cells are positive for CD15 and CD30. Diagnosis: classical Hodgkin lymphoma, nodular sclerosis type.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C25-Q1","stem":"Staging studies are performed. Which of the following features of this disease most distinguishes it from most non-Hodgkin lymphomas?","options":["Noncontiguous spread to distant nodal groups","Frequent extranodal involvement at diagnosis","Neoplastic cells forming most of the tumor mass","Orderly spread to adjacent nodal groups"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D2","obj":"HE-9-D.6","dok":3,"approach":["Hodgkin lymphoma usually begins in one nodal group, often cervical or mediastinal.","It spreads in an orderly way to the next contiguous group, which is why staging guides treatment so well.","Non-Hodgkin lymphomas more often skip nodal groups and involve extranodal sites early."],"why":"Classical Hodgkin lymphoma contains a small number of Reed-Sternberg cells and variants (CD15 and CD30 positive) in a large background of reactive cells: lymphocytes, eosinophils, histiocytes, and plasma cells. It has a bimodal age distribution, some cases are EBV associated, and nodular sclerosis is the most common type, typically in young women with a mediastinal mass. Spread is contiguous from one node group to the next, and extranodal disease is uncommon at diagnosis. Non-Hodgkin lymphomas more often spread noncontiguously and involve extranodal sites such as the gut, skin, and marrow.","whyNot":["Noncontiguous spread is typical of non-Hodgkin lymphoma.","Frequent extranodal involvement is a feature of non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Hodgkin lymphoma rarely starts outside lymph nodes.","In Hodgkin lymphoma the neoplastic Reed-Sternberg cells are a small minority, often 1% to 5% of cells. Non-Hodgkin lymphomas are mostly neoplastic cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hodgkin versus non-Hodgkin lymphoma"],"comps":["HE-9-D2"]}},{"id":"HE-C25-Q2","stem":"Before the biopsy, her physician considered reactive lymphadenopathy from infectious mononucleosis. A lymph node from a patient with acute Epstein-Barr virus mononucleosis would most likely show which of the following patterns?","options":["Paracortical hyperplasia","Follicular hyperplasia","Sinus histiocytosis","Effacement by sheets of monotonous cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-E","obj":"HE-9-E.8","dok":2,"approach":["Each zone of a lymph node expands when its own cells respond: follicles (B cells), paracortex (T cells), sinuses (histiocytes).","Acute viral infection such as EBV mononucleosis produces a strong T cell response.","The T cell zone, the paracortex, expands with activated immunoblasts while node architecture is preserved."],"why":"Reactive lymph nodes keep their normal architecture, which helps separate them from lymphoma. Follicular hyperplasia (enlarged B cell germinal centers) is seen in rheumatoid arthritis, toxoplasmosis, and early HIV infection. Paracortical hyperplasia (expanded T cell zones with immunoblasts) is seen in viral infections such as EBV, after some vaccinations, and with drugs such as phenytoin. Sinus histiocytosis is seen in nodes draining a cancer. Acute bacterial lymphangitis, as from Streptococcus pyogenes, shows red streaks tracking toward tender regional nodes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Follicular hyperplasia reflects a B cell response, as in rheumatoid arthritis, toxoplasmosis, or early HIV infection.","Sinus histiocytosis is the typical reaction in nodes draining a carcinoma.","Effacement of the architecture by monotonous cells suggests non-Hodgkin lymphoma, not a reactive process."],"study":{"topics":["Patterns of reactive lymphadenitis"],"comps":["HE-9-E","HE-9-D2"]}},{"id":"HE-C25-Q3","stem":"Her biopsy is reviewed alongside one from a 58-year-old man with waxing and waning painless generalized lymphadenopathy for 3 years. His node is filled with closely packed neoplastic follicles of small cleaved B cells. Which of the following molecular mechanisms most likely drives his lymphoma?","options":["BCR-ABL1 activation causing constant kinase signaling","Cyclin D1 overexpression promoting the G1 to S transition","BCL2 overexpression blocking apoptosis","MYC activation driving rapid cell proliferation"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-9-D2","obj":"HE-9-D.7","dok":3,"approach":["An indolent, waxing and waning lymphoma of older adults with back-to-back follicles of small cleaved cells is follicular lymphoma.","Follicular lymphoma carries t(14;18), which places BCL2 next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain promoter.","BCL2 is antiapoptotic, so the cells accumulate instead of proliferating rapidly."],"why":"Follicular lymphoma is an indolent B cell lymphoma with t(14;18). The translocation drives BCL2 overexpression, and BCL2 blocks the mitochondrial pathway of apoptosis, so neoplastic cells live too long. Other key non-Hodgkin lymphomas: diffuse large B cell lymphoma is the most common and is aggressive; Burkitt lymphoma has t(8;14) with MYC activation and a starry sky pattern; mantle cell lymphoma has t(11;14) with cyclin D1 overexpression and is CD5 positive; MALT lymphoma of the stomach is linked to Helicobacter pylori.","whyNot":["BCR-ABL1 from t(9;22) drives chronic myeloid leukemia, not a follicular B cell lymphoma.","Cyclin D1 overexpression from t(11;14) drives mantle cell lymphoma.","Correct.","MYC activation from t(8;14) drives Burkitt lymphoma, a highly aggressive tumor with a starry sky pattern."],"study":{"topics":["Translocations in non-Hodgkin lymphoma"],"comps":["HE-9-D2","HE-6"]}},{"id":"HE-C25-Q4","stem":"Before the biopsy, her physician also considered infectious lymphadenitis. A biopsy of a tender axillary lymph node from a different patient, a 16-year-old boy, shows stellate necrotizing granulomas with central neutrophils, and a Warthin-Starry silver stain shows clusters of small bacilli. Which of the following is the most likely organism and route of infection?","options":["Bartonella henselae, from a cat scratch","Toxoplasma gondii, from cat feces","Yersinia pestis, transmitted by rodent fleas","Streptococcus pyogenes, spread from a skin wound"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-9-E","obj":"HE-9-E.7","dok":2,"approach":["Stellate (star-shaped) necrotizing granulomas with central neutrophils are the classic node lesion of cat scratch disease.","Small bacilli on a Warthin-Starry silver stain fit Bartonella henselae, a gram-negative bacillus carried by cats.","Plague and streptococcal infection cause suppurative nodes without granulomas, and Toxoplasma is a protozoan, not a bacillus."],"why":"Cat scratch disease follows a scratch or bite from a cat, usually a kitten, and causes tender regional lymphadenopathy draining the inoculation site. The node shows stellate necrotizing granulomas with neutrophils in the center. Yersinia pestis, carried by rodent fleas, causes a painful, suppurative bubo and can spread to the blood (septicemic plague) and lungs (pneumonic plague); it shows bipolar safety pin staining. Streptococcus pyogenes causes acute lymphangitis with red streaks.","whyNot":["Correct.","Toxoplasma is a protozoan, not a bacillus, and Toxoplasma lymphadenitis shows follicular hyperplasia with small clusters of epithelioid histiocytes, not stellate necrotizing granulomas.","Yersinia pestis causes a painful suppurative bubo with necrosis, not stellate granulomas.","Streptococcus pyogenes causes acute lymphangitis and suppurative lymphadenitis, not granulomas."],"study":{"topics":["Infectious lymphadenitis","Cat scratch disease and plague"],"comps":["HE-9-E"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C26","system":"HE","dx":"Multiple myeloma with hypercalcemia and lytic bone lesions","codes":["HE-9-D3"],"case":"A 67-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of low back pain, fatigue, constipation, and confusion for 2 weeks. His temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL (reference, 13.5 to 17.5 g/dL), calcium 13.1 mg/dL (reference, 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL), creatinine 2.3 mg/dL (reference, 0.7 to 1.3 mg/dL), total protein 9.8 g/dL (reference, 6.0 to 8.3 g/dL), and albumin 3.4 g/dL (reference, 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). The blood smear shows rouleaux. Serum protein electrophoresis shows an IgG kappa M spike of 4.2 g/dL. Bone marrow biopsy shows 40% clonal plasma cells. X-rays show punched-out lytic lesions of the skull and lumbar spine. Diagnosis: multiple myeloma.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C26-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the main mechanism of his lytic bone lesions and hypercalcemia?","options":["Excess 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity in tumor cells","Osteoclast activation through increased RANKL","Autonomous secretion of parathyroid hormone","Tumor secretion of parathyroid hormone-related protein"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D3","obj":"HE-9-D.9","dok":3,"approach":["Punched-out lesions mean focal bone resorption without new bone formation.","Myeloma cells increase RANKL on marrow stromal cells and release other osteoclast-activating factors.","Osteoclasts resorb bone and release calcium, while osteoblast activity is suppressed."],"why":"Myeloma cells and the marrow stroma interact to raise RANKL and lower osteoprotegerin, its decoy receptor. RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, so osteoclasts multiply and resorb bone. Myeloma cells also release factors such as DKK1 that suppress osteoblasts, so the lesions are purely lytic and do not show on a bone scan. Calcium released from bone causes hypercalcemia with confusion and constipation. Other features include renal injury from light chain casts, anemia, rouleaux, infections, and an M spike.","whyNot":["Excess calcitriol from 1-alpha-hydroxylase causes hypercalcemia in granulomatous disease and some lymphomas, not in myeloma.","Correct.","Autonomous PTH secretion is primary hyperparathyroidism, which does not cause punched-out lesions with an M spike.","PTHrP causes humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy in squamous cell carcinoma and some other tumors. It is not the main mechanism in myeloma."],"study":{"topics":["Bone disease in multiple myeloma","Causes of hypercalcemia"],"comps":["HE-9-D3"]}},{"id":"HE-C26-Q2","stem":"Three years ago, routine testing showed an IgG kappa M protein of 1.2 g/dL with no symptoms. Which of the following findings now most clearly separates his current disease from the condition he had then?","options":["Rouleaux formation on the blood smear","End-organ damage such as lytic bone lesions","IgG class of the monoclonal protein","Kappa light chain restriction of the plasma cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D3","obj":"HE-9-D.10","dok":3,"approach":["A small M protein without symptoms is monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS).","Both MGUS and myeloma are clonal, so the protein class and light chain restriction do not separate them.","Myeloma is defined by end-organ damage: hypercalcemia, renal injury, anemia, and bone lesions."],"why":"MGUS is a clonal plasma cell disorder with an M protein below 3 g/dL, fewer than 10% marrow plasma cells, and no end-organ damage. It becomes myeloma at a rate of about 1% per year. Myeloma has the CRAB features: hypercalcemia, renal insufficiency, anemia, and bone lesions. Waldenström macroglobulinemia is a different disorder with an IgM M protein, hyperviscosity, and no lytic lesions. Free light chains made by plasma cell clones can also misfold and deposit as AL amyloid.","whyNot":["Rouleaux reflect a high protein level that makes red cells stack. They can be seen with any large M protein and do not define end-organ damage.","Correct.","MGUS and myeloma both most often produce IgG, so the class does not separate them.","Light chain restriction shows the cells are clonal, which is true in both MGUS and myeloma."],"study":{"topics":["MGUS versus multiple myeloma","Waldenström macroglobulinemia"],"comps":["HE-9-D3"]}},{"id":"HE-C26-Q3","stem":"His protein electrophoresis was performed on serum instead of plasma. Plasma electrophoresis could show an extra band in the beta-gamma region that might be mistaken for a small M protein. Which of the following plasma proteins causes that band?","options":["Albumin","Transferrin","Haptoglobin","Fibrinogen"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-2","obj":"HE-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Plasma is the fluid part of unclotted blood; serum is what remains after blood clots.","Clotting converts fibrinogen to fibrin, so serum lacks fibrinogen and consumed clotting factors.","A protein present in plasma but missing from serum must come from the clotting system."],"why":"Plasma is about 92% water, with albumin, globulins, fibrinogen, electrolytes, nutrients, and hormones. Serum is plasma minus fibrinogen and the clotting factors used up during clotting. On electrophoresis, fibrinogen runs as a band between the beta and gamma regions and can look like a small M protein. That is why protein electrophoresis is done on serum. Albumin is the largest plasma protein and the main source of plasma oncotic pressure.","whyNot":["Albumin is present in both plasma and serum and runs as the largest band at the anodal end.","Transferrin is present in both plasma and serum. It is a normal component of the beta region.","Haptoglobin is present in both plasma and serum and runs in the alpha-2 region.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Plasma versus serum","Plasma proteins"],"comps":["HE-2"]}},{"id":"HE-C26-Q4","stem":"A 49-year-old woman who grew up in southern Japan has hypercalcemia, lytic bone lesions, skin nodules, and generalized lymphadenopathy. Serum protein electrophoresis shows no M spike. Her blood smear shows atypical lymphocytes with multilobed, flower-shaped nuclei. Which of the following viruses most likely caused her disease?","options":["Human immunodeficiency virus type 1","Human herpesvirus 8","Human T-lymphotropic virus type 1","Epstein-Barr virus"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-9-D2","obj":"HE-9-D.8","dok":2,"approach":["Hypercalcemia and lytic lesions without an M spike point away from myeloma.","Flower cells, skin lesions, and lymphadenopathy in a person from southern Japan or the Caribbean fit adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma.","This neoplasm of CD4-positive T cells is caused by HTLV-1."],"why":"Adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma is a neoplasm of CD4-positive T cells caused by HTLV-1, a retrovirus endemic in southern Japan, the Caribbean, and parts of Africa and South America. Its viral Tax protein drives T cell proliferation. Patients have skin lesions, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, hypercalcemia, and lytic bone lesions, and the smear shows flower cells. Mycosis fungoides is a different T cell lymphoma of skin-homing CD4 T cells with Pautrier microabscesses; Sezary cells appear in blood when it spreads.","whyNot":["HIV raises the risk of B cell lymphomas such as diffuse large B cell lymphoma, but it does not directly transform T cells into flower cells.","HHV-8 causes Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, and multicentric Castleman disease.","Correct.","EBV is linked to Burkitt lymphoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, and nasopharyngeal carcinoma, not to flower cell T cell leukemia."],"study":{"topics":["Adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma","Viruses that cause lymphoma"],"comps":["HE-9-D2","HE-8"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C27","system":"HE","dx":"Plasmodium falciparum malaria in a returning traveler","codes":["HE-9-E2"],"case":"A 34-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of fever, chills, headache, and muscle aches for 3 days. He returned 12 days ago from a 3-week visit to family in Ghana and did not take malaria prophylaxis. His temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 104/64 mm Hg. He has mild scleral icterus and a palpable spleen tip. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL (reference, 13.5 to 17.5 g/dL), platelets 62,000/mm3 (reference, 150,000 to 400,000/mm3), total bilirubin 3.0 mg/dL (reference, 0.1 to 1.2 mg/dL), mostly unconjugated, and glucose 82 mg/dL (reference, 70 to 99 mg/dL). Thin blood smear shows numerous small ring forms, several red cells with two or more rings, and crescent-shaped gametocytes, with 8% parasitemia. Diagnosis: Plasmodium falciparum malaria.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C27-Q1","stem":"He asks whether the infection can come back months later, as happened to his brother after a trip to India, where Plasmodium vivax is common. Which of the following best explains why relapse is not expected with his infection?","options":["P. falciparum does not pass through hepatocytes","P. falciparum lacks a sexual gametocyte stage","P. falciparum forms no dormant hypnozoites","P. falciparum infects only reticulocytes"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-8","obj":"HE-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the life cycle: sporozoites from the female Anopheles bite go to the liver, multiply as schizonts, and release merozoites that start the red cell cycle.","In P. vivax and P. ovale some liver forms stay dormant as hypnozoites and wake up months later, causing relapse.","P. falciparum has no hypnozoites, so once the blood stage is cleared, relapse does not occur."],"why":"The female Anopheles mosquito injects sporozoites, which reach hepatocytes within an hour and multiply as liver schizonts (exoerythrocytic schizogony). Liver schizonts release merozoites that invade red cells and repeat asexual cycles. Some parasites become gametocytes, which a mosquito takes up to complete the sexual cycle. P. vivax and P. ovale also form hypnozoites, dormant liver stages that cause relapse and need primaquine (or tafenoquine) to eradicate. P. falciparum and P. malariae do not form hypnozoites.","whyNot":["All human Plasmodium species, including P. falciparum, go through a liver stage after the bite.","P. falciparum does form gametocytes, the crescent-shaped forms seen on his smear.","Correct.","P. vivax and P. ovale prefer reticulocytes. P. falciparum invades red cells of all ages."],"study":{"topics":["Plasmodium life cycle","Relapse and hypnozoites"],"comps":["HE-8","HE-9-E2"]}},{"id":"HE-C27-Q2","stem":"His smear shows only ring forms and gametocytes; mature trophozoites and schizonts are not seen in the peripheral blood. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Adherence of mature-stage cells to venular endothelium","Rupture of red cells before trophozoites can mature","Rapid clearance of mature stages by splenic macrophages","Development of mature stages only within hepatocytes"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-8","obj":"HE-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["As P. falciparum matures inside a red cell, it places PfEMP1 on knobs on the red cell surface.","PfEMP1 binds endothelial receptors, so cells with mature parasites stick in small venules of organs.","These sequestered cells are missing from the circulating blood, so the smear shows mostly rings."],"why":"P. falciparum is the most dangerous species for several reasons. It invades red cells of all ages, so parasitemia can be very high. Mature parasites export PfEMP1 to knobs on the red cell membrane, and PfEMP1 binds endothelial receptors such as ICAM-1 and CD36. The cells are sequestered in small vessels of the brain, kidney, lung, and placenta, causing microvascular obstruction and avoiding removal by the spleen. The parasite switches among var genes that encode different PfEMP1 variants, so antibody responses keep falling behind.","whyNot":["Correct.","Rings do mature into trophozoites and schizonts. They are simply hidden in the microvasculature.","Sequestration helps parasites avoid the spleen. Clearance does not explain the absence of mature forms.","The liver stage happens only once, before the blood stage. Red cell maturation takes place in the blood."],"study":{"topics":["PfEMP1 and sequestration","Virulence of P. falciparum"],"comps":["HE-8","HE-9-E2"]}},{"id":"HE-C27-Q3","stem":"On hospital day 2, his urine becomes very dark, almost black, his urine output falls to 300 mL/24 h, and his serum creatinine rises to 3.1 mg/dL (reference, 0.7 to 1.3 mg/dL). Urine dipstick is strongly positive for blood, but microscopy shows few red cells. Which of the following most likely explains these findings?","options":["Immune complex deposition in glomeruli","Obstruction of the ureters by blood clots","Ascending bacterial infection of the kidneys","Intravascular hemolysis with hemoglobinuria"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-E2","obj":"HE-9-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["A dipstick positive for blood with few red cells on microscopy means free hemoglobin (or myoglobin) in the urine.","Massive rupture of parasitized red cells releases hemoglobin into the plasma, which saturates haptoglobin and is filtered.","Hemoglobin, together with sequestration in renal vessels and hypotension, injures the tubules and causes acute kidney injury."],"why":"Severe P. falciparum malaria can cause brisk intravascular hemolysis. Free hemoglobin is filtered and makes the urine dark, a picture called blackwater fever, and acute kidney injury follows from tubular injury and microvascular sequestration. Other complications of falciparum malaria include cerebral malaria with confusion, seizures, and coma; severe anemia; hypoglycemia; and acute respiratory distress syndrome. The thin smear shows small rings, multiple rings per cell, and banana-shaped gametocytes.","whyNot":["Immune complex glomerulonephritis with nephrotic syndrome is associated with chronic P. malariae infection, and it causes proteinuria rather than dark urine within days.","Ureteral clots would show red cells on microscopy and are not a feature of malaria.","Ascending pyelonephritis would show white cells and bacteria in the urine and does not cause a dipstick positive for heme without red cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of falciparum malaria"],"comps":["HE-9-E2","HE-3"]}},{"id":"HE-C27-Q4","stem":"His fevers are irregular. A patient with untreated Plasmodium malariae infection, by contrast, may have fever spikes that recur every 72 hours. Each fever spike in such a patient coincides with which of the following events?","options":["Ingestion of gametocytes by a mosquito","Synchronized rupture of infected red cells","Reactivation of hypnozoites in hepatocytes","Release of merozoites from liver schizonts"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-9-E2","obj":"HE-9-E.2","dok":3,"approach":["Fever comes from host cytokines released when parasite material enters the blood.","When the erythrocytic cycles become synchronized, many schizonts rupture at the same time, releasing merozoites, pigment, and debris.","So fever spikes recur at the length of the red cell cycle: about 48 hours for P. vivax and P. ovale and 72 hours for P. malariae."],"why":"In the erythrocytic cycle, merozoites invade red cells, mature into trophozoites and schizonts, and burst the cell to release new merozoites. When the cycles synchronize, the bursts happen together and trigger TNF and other pyrogenic cytokines, giving periodic fever. The period matches the cycle: about 48 hours (tertian) for P. vivax and P. ovale and 72 hours (quartan) for P. malariae. P. falciparum fevers are often irregular. Repeated red cell rupture also causes hemolytic anemia, and splenic work causes splenomegaly; thrombocytopenia is common.","whyNot":["Gametocytes taken up by a mosquito continue the cycle in the mosquito and do not cause fever in the patient.","Correct.","P. malariae does not form hypnozoites, and hypnozoite reactivation causes relapse months later, not spikes every 72 hours.","Merozoite release from the liver occurs once, at the start of the blood stage, so it cannot explain repeated spikes."],"study":{"topics":["Periodic fevers in malaria"],"comps":["HE-9-E2","HE-8"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C28","system":"HE","dx":"Babesiosis in an asplenic adult","codes":["HE-9-E1"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman comes to the physician in July because of fever, chills, fatigue, and muscle aches for 1 week. She lives on Cape Cod, Massachusetts, and works in her garden daily. She had a splenectomy 10 years ago after a car crash. Her temperature is 39.1 °C (102.4 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 112/68 mm Hg. She has mild jaundice. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.0 g/dL (reference, 12.0 to 16.0 g/dL), reticulocytes 7% (reference, 0.5% to 2.5%), platelets 58,000/mm3 (reference, 150,000 to 400,000/mm3), LDH 820 U/L (reference, 140 to 280 U/L), haptoglobin below 10 mg/dL (reference, 30 to 200 mg/dL), and indirect bilirubin 2.8 mg/dL (reference, 0.2 to 0.8 mg/dL). The blood smear shows intraerythrocytic ring forms, some arranged in tetrads, with 9% parasitemia. Diagnosis: babesiosis.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C28-Q1","stem":"Most healthy adults infected with this parasite have mild or no symptoms. Which of the following best explains the high parasitemia and severe illness in this patient?","options":["Loss of complement synthesis by the spleen","Loss of splenic removal of infected red cells","Loss of splenic iron storage for erythropoiesis","Loss of splenic production of neutrophils"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-9-E1","obj":"HE-9-E.1","dok":3,"approach":["Babesia lives inside red cells, so control depends on removing infected cells.","The spleen filters red cells and its macrophages remove infected or abnormal ones.","Without a spleen, infected cells stay in circulation and parasitemia climbs, so asplenia is a key risk factor for severe disease."],"why":"Babesiosis is a tick-borne infection of red cells, seen mostly in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States. The spleen is the main defense: its red pulp macrophages remove parasitized red cells, and it supports antibody responses. Severe disease, with high parasitemia, hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and sometimes kidney failure or ARDS, occurs mainly in people without a spleen, older adults, and immunosuppressed patients such as those with HIV, cancer, or on rituximab.","whyNot":["Most complement proteins are made by the liver, not the spleen.","Correct.","Iron for erythropoiesis is stored mainly in liver and marrow macrophages, and its loss would not raise parasitemia.","Adult neutrophils are made in the bone marrow, not the spleen."],"study":{"topics":["Risk factors for severe babesiosis","Functions of the spleen"],"comps":["HE-9-E1","HE-3"]}},{"id":"HE-C28-Q2","stem":"Two weeks later she notices an expanding red patch with central clearing on her thigh. Which of the following organisms, transmitted by the same vector as her parasite, most likely caused this lesion?","options":["Borrelia burgdorferi","Rickettsia rickettsii","Ehrlichia chaffeensis","Francisella tularensis"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-8","obj":"HE-8.4","dok":2,"approach":["Babesia microti is spread by the Ixodes scapularis (deer or black-legged) tick and occasionally by blood transfusion.","The same tick carries Borrelia burgdorferi, so coinfection is common in the same regions.","An expanding annular lesion with central clearing is erythema migrans of Lyme disease."],"why":"Babesia microti is transmitted by Ixodes scapularis, the same tick that transmits Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme disease) and Anaplasma phagocytophilum. Coinfection should be considered in any patient with babesiosis. Babesia can also be transmitted by transfusion of blood from an asymptomatic donor. On smear it forms ring forms inside red cells, and the tetrad (Maltese cross) form is distinctive. Unlike malaria, there is no hemozoin pigment and no gametocytes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Rickettsia rickettsii causes Rocky Mountain spotted fever and is carried by Dermacentor ticks. Its rash starts on wrists and ankles.","Ehrlichia chaffeensis is carried by the lone star tick (Amblyomma) and does not cause erythema migrans.","Francisella tularensis is spread by ticks, deer flies, and contact with rabbits. It causes an ulcer with regional lymphadenopathy."],"study":{"topics":["Babesia transmission and smear","Ixodes-borne coinfections"],"comps":["HE-8","HE-9-E1"]}},{"id":"HE-C28-Q3","stem":"Her hemolysis is partly intravascular. One week after treatment, her hemoglobin is rising and a urine dipstick is negative for blood. Which of the following urine findings at this time would best confirm that she had intravascular hemolysis?","options":["Red cell casts seen on urine sediment microscopy","Hemosiderin in shed tubular epithelial cells","Increased urobilinogen in the urine","Conjugated bilirubin detected on the urine dipstick"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-3","obj":"HE-3.3","dok":3,"approach":["In intravascular hemolysis, free hemoglobin saturates haptoglobin, is filtered by the glomerulus, and is reabsorbed by proximal tubular cells.","Tubular cells break down the hemoglobin and store its iron as hemosiderin.","These cells are shed into the urine for weeks, so hemosiderinuria (seen with an iron stain) outlasts hemoglobinuria."],"why":"Red cells can be destroyed inside vessels (intravascular) or by macrophages in the spleen and liver (extravascular). Both raise LDH, unconjugated bilirubin, and reticulocytes, and both can lower haptoglobin. Only intravascular hemolysis puts enough free hemoglobin into the plasma for it to be filtered by the kidney. Proximal tubular cells take up the filtered hemoglobin and store its iron as hemosiderin, and these cells are shed into the urine. Hemosiderinuria appears a few days after the hemolysis and can persist for weeks, after hemoglobinuria has cleared. On the smear, schistocytes suggest mechanical intravascular injury, while spherocytes suggest extravascular removal.","whyNot":["Red cell casts come from bleeding into the tubules from inflamed glomeruli, as in glomerulonephritis. They are not a sign of hemolysis.","Correct.","Urobilinogen rises with any hemolysis because more bilirubin reaches the gut, and it falls back once hemolysis stops. It does not show where destruction occurred.","Hemolysis raises unconjugated bilirubin, which is bound to albumin and not filtered, so the urine dipstick stays negative for bilirubin."],"study":{"topics":["Intravascular versus extravascular hemolysis","Hemosiderinuria"],"comps":["HE-3","HE-7"]}},{"id":"HE-C28-Q4","stem":"In a person with a normal spleen, senescent red cells are removed after about 120 days. After her splenectomy, which of the following cells took over most of this removal?","options":["Lymph node dendritic cells","Circulating neutrophils","Hepatic Kupffer cells","Pulmonary alveolar macrophages"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-3","obj":"HE-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Red cells lose flexibility and surface markers as they age over about 120 days.","Macrophages of the mononuclear phagocyte system recognize and engulf them, mainly in the spleen.","Without a spleen, the liver's Kupffer cells (with marrow macrophages) take over this job."],"why":"Red cells have no nucleus and cannot repair themselves, so after about 120 days they become rigid and expose signals such as phosphatidylserine. Macrophages in the splenic red pulp remove most of them, and macrophages in the liver (Kupffer cells) and bone marrow remove the rest. After splenectomy, the liver handles most of the load, but it is less efficient at removing inclusions and parasites. That is why Howell-Jolly bodies appear on the smear and why intraerythrocytic infections such as babesiosis become severe.","whyNot":["Dendritic cells capture and present antigen to T cells. They do not remove aged red cells.","Neutrophils phagocytose bacteria. They do not clear senescent red cells.","Correct.","Alveolar macrophages clear particles and microbes in the lung and are not a major site of red cell removal."],"study":{"topics":["Red cell life span and removal"],"comps":["HE-3","HE-1"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C29","system":"HE","dx":"Primary myelofibrosis with massive splenomegaly","codes":["HE-9-D"],"case":"A 66-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue, early satiety, and left upper abdominal fullness for 6 months. He has lost 5 kg (11 lb). His temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 94/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 124/76 mm Hg. The spleen is palpable 14 cm below the left costal margin. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.6 g/dL (reference, 13.5 to 17.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 90 fL (reference, 80 to 100 fL), leukocytes 12,800/mm3 (reference, 4,500 to 11,000/mm3), and platelets 410,000/mm3 (reference, 150,000 to 400,000/mm3). The smear shows teardrop red cells, nucleated red cells, and immature granulocytes. Bone marrow aspiration yields a dry tap; biopsy shows dense reticulin and collagen fibrosis with clusters of atypical megakaryocytes. Testing shows a JAK2 V617F mutation. Diagnosis: primary myelofibrosis.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C29-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the marrow fibrosis in this patient?","options":["Growth factors released by clonal megakaryocytes","Deposition of amyloid light chains in the marrow","Granulomatous inflammation replacing the marrow","Clonal proliferation of fibroblasts within the marrow"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-D","obj":"HE-9-D.12","dok":2,"approach":["Primary myelofibrosis is a myeloproliferative neoplasm driven by JAK2, CALR, or MPL mutation in a stem cell.","The neoplastic megakaryocytes release PDGF and TGF-beta.","These factors stimulate normal, nonclonal fibroblasts to lay down reticulin and collagen."],"why":"In primary myelofibrosis the neoplastic clone includes atypical megakaryocytes that release fibrogenic growth factors such as PDGF and TGF-beta. These factors drive polyclonal (non-neoplastic) fibroblasts to fill the marrow with reticulin and collagen. The fibrotic marrow gives a dry tap. Red cells squeezed out through fibrotic marrow become teardrop cells, and immature granulocytes and nucleated red cells leak into the blood (a leukoerythroblastic picture). Hematopoiesis moves to the spleen and liver, causing massive splenomegaly.","whyNot":["Correct.","Light chain amyloid deposits occur in plasma cell disorders and do not cause reticulin fibrosis with a JAK2 mutation.","Granulomas replacing the marrow occur with tuberculosis or sarcoidosis, not with a JAK2-positive neoplasm.","The fibroblasts are reactive, not part of the neoplastic clone."],"study":{"topics":["Primary myelofibrosis"],"comps":["HE-9-D","HE-6"]}},{"id":"HE-C29-Q2","stem":"A biopsy of the spleen would show erythroid, myeloid, and megakaryocytic precursors. The spleen can support blood cell production in this patient because it was a hematopoietic organ before birth. Which of the following sequences of hematopoietic sites during development is correct?","options":["Liver and spleen, then yolk sac, then bone marrow","Yolk sac, then bone marrow, then liver and spleen","Bone marrow, then liver and spleen, then yolk sac","Yolk sac, then liver and spleen, then bone marrow"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"HE-1","obj":"HE-1.3","dok":2,"approach":["Blood formation starts in yolk sac blood islands in week 3.","From about the second month, the liver becomes the main site, with help from the spleen.","The bone marrow takes over in the last months of gestation; when marrow fails after birth, liver and spleen can resume this role."],"why":"Hematopoiesis begins in the yolk sac in the third week, moves to the liver (with a contribution from the spleen) from about the second month, and shifts to the bone marrow late in gestation. After birth, the marrow is the normal site. When the marrow is replaced by fibrosis, as in myelofibrosis, or overwhelmed by ineffective erythropoiesis, as in beta thalassemia major, the liver and spleen take up hematopoiesis again. This extramedullary hematopoiesis enlarges them, which explains his massive splenomegaly.","whyNot":["The yolk sac comes first, before the liver and spleen.","The liver and spleen take over before the bone marrow, not after it.","This reverses the true order. The marrow is the last site, not the first.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Sites of hematopoiesis in development","Extramedullary hematopoiesis"],"comps":["HE-1","HE-9-D"]}},{"id":"HE-C29-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best describes how his JAK2 mutation promotes the growth of the neoplastic clone?","options":["Fusion with BCR creates a constitutive tyrosine kinase","Loss of a tumor suppressor that blocks G1 to S transition","Overexpression of an antiapoptotic mitochondrial protein","Cytokine-independent activation of JAK-STAT signaling"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"HE-6","obj":"HE-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["JAK2 is a tyrosine kinase that sits on receptors for erythropoietin, thrombopoietin, and G-CSF.","Normally it is active only when the cytokine binds.","The V617F mutation turns on the kinase without cytokine, so STAT signals for growth and survival never stop."],"why":"JAK2 is a cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase bound to type I cytokine receptors. Normally erythropoietin or thrombopoietin binding activates JAK2, which phosphorylates STAT proteins that enter the nucleus and turn on growth genes. The V617F point mutation disrupts an inhibitory region, so JAK2 signals without cytokine. It is found in nearly all polycythemia vera and in about half of essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis. In CML, by contrast, t(9;22) fuses BCR to ABL1 to make a constitutively active tyrosine kinase.","whyNot":["BCR-ABL1 fusion from t(9;22) is the driver of chronic myeloid leukemia, not of JAK2-mutant neoplasms.","Loss of a G1 to S checkpoint describes tumor suppressors such as RB or p16, not JAK2.","Overexpression of the antiapoptotic protein BCL2 describes follicular lymphoma with t(14;18).","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["JAK2 V617F","BCR-ABL1 in CML"],"comps":["HE-6","HE-9-D"]}},{"id":"HE-C29-Q4","stem":"His reticulocyte count is 1.0% (reference, 0.5% to 2.5%), and his red cell distribution width is increased. Which of the following best classifies his anemia?","options":["Normocytic anemia from marrow underproduction","Normocytic anemia from peripheral hemolysis","Macrocytic anemia from impaired DNA synthesis","Microcytic anemia from impaired heme synthesis"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-2","obj":"HE-2.5","dok":3,"approach":["Classify by size: an MCV of 90 fL is normocytic.","Judge the marrow response: with hemoglobin 8.6 g/dL, a reticulocyte count of 1.0% is inappropriately low (the corrected count is well under 2%).","A low reticulocyte response means underproduction, here from marrow fibrosis."],"why":"Anemia is classified first by MCV: below 80 fL is microcytic, 80 to 100 fL is normocytic, and above 100 fL is macrocytic. Next comes the reticulocyte count, which shows whether the marrow is responding. Correcting for anemia (1.0% times 8.6 divided by a normal hemoglobin of about 15 g/dL) gives about 0.6%, far too low for this degree of anemia, so production is inadequate. In hemolysis the reticulocyte count would be high. The raised RDW reflects the mix of teardrop cells and other abnormal shapes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Hemolysis causes a high reticulocyte count. His count is inappropriately low.","His MCV is normal. Impaired DNA synthesis (B12 or folate deficiency) gives an MCV above 100 fL.","His MCV is 90 fL, which is normocytic, not microcytic. Impaired heme synthesis (iron deficiency) gives a low MCV."],"study":{"topics":["Classifying anemia with the CBC","Reticulocyte index"],"comps":["HE-2","HE-9-A"]}}]},{"id":"HE-C30","system":"HE","dx":"Severe dengue with plasma leakage and thrombocytopenia","codes":["HE-9-E"],"case":"A 27-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of abdominal pain, repeated vomiting, and bleeding gums. She returned from Thailand 6 days ago. She had high fever, headache, eye pain, and muscle aches for 4 days, and the fever resolved yesterday. She had dengue during a trip to Brazil 3 years ago. Her temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 122/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 92/76 mm Hg. She has petechiae, a tender enlarged liver, shifting dullness, and decreased breath sounds at the right lung base. Laboratory studies show hematocrit 50% (reference, 36% to 46%), up from 38% 2 days ago, platelets 24,000/mm3 (reference, 150,000 to 400,000/mm3), and albumin 2.6 g/dL (reference, 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). Dengue NS1 antigen is positive. Diagnosis: severe dengue with plasma leakage.","questions":[{"id":"HE-C30-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her current illness is more severe than her first dengue infection?","options":["Loss of immunity to the serotype of the first infection","Reactivation of latent virus from the first infection","Antibody-dependent enhancement by heterotypic antibodies","Coinfection with chikungunya virus from the same mosquito"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-9-E","obj":"HE-9-E.5","dok":3,"approach":["There are four dengue virus serotypes; infection gives lasting immunity only to the same serotype.","On reinfection with a different serotype, old antibodies bind the new virus without neutralizing it.","The antibody-coated virus enters Fc receptor-bearing monocytes and macrophages more easily, raising viral load and cytokine release that make capillaries leak."],"why":"Dengue is a flavivirus spread by Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus. A second infection with a different serotype is the main risk factor for severe dengue. Cross-reactive, non-neutralizing antibodies from the first infection help the new virus enter monocytes through Fc receptors (antibody-dependent enhancement). The result is higher viremia, strong cytokine release, plasma leakage, and severe thrombocytopenia, often starting as the fever ends. The same Aedes mosquitoes spread chikungunya, known for long-lasting arthralgia, and Zika, known for congenital microcephaly.","whyNot":["Immunity to the first serotype is long lasting. Severe disease follows infection with a different serotype.","Dengue virus does not establish latency, so it cannot reactivate.","Correct.","Chikungunya is spread by the same mosquitoes, but it causes fever with severe, persistent arthralgia, not plasma leakage. Her NS1 test confirms dengue."],"study":{"topics":["Severe dengue","Dengue, chikungunya, and Zika"],"comps":["HE-9-E","HE-7"]}},{"id":"HE-C30-Q2","stem":"Her serum albumin is 2.6 g/dL. Which change in the Starling forces across her capillaries results most directly from this finding?","options":["Increased interstitial hydrostatic pressure","Decreased lymphatic flow from the interstitium","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Decreased capillary oncotic pressure"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"HE-2","obj":"HE-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Albumin supplies most of the oncotic pressure of plasma.","Low albumin lowers capillary oncotic pressure, which normally pulls fluid back into vessels.","Net filtration rises and fluid collects in the interstitium and body cavities, adding to the leak from damaged capillaries."],"why":"Starling forces set fluid movement across capillaries: capillary hydrostatic pressure and interstitial oncotic pressure push fluid out, while capillary oncotic pressure and interstitial hydrostatic pressure pull it in. Albumin is the main contributor to plasma oncotic pressure, so hypoalbuminemia favors edema, ascites, and effusions. In severe dengue, leaky capillaries also let albumin escape, which lowers serum albumin further. Albumin also carries unconjugated bilirubin, fatty acids, and much of the plasma calcium, so a low albumin lowers the measured total calcium.","whyNot":["Interstitial hydrostatic pressure rises as fluid accumulates, which opposes filtration. It is a consequence, not the direct effect of low albumin.","Lymphatic obstruction causes protein-rich lymphedema and is unrelated to serum albumin.","Increased hydrostatic pressure causes edema in heart failure or venous obstruction, not from low albumin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Functions of albumin"],"comps":["HE-2"]}},{"id":"HE-C30-Q3","stem":"On hospital day 2 she oozes blood from venipuncture sites, and her serum creatinine rises to 2.4 mg/dL (reference, 0.6 to 1.1 mg/dL). Prothrombin time and activated partial thromboplastin time are prolonged, fibrinogen is 90 mg/dL (reference, 200 to 400 mg/dL), D-dimer is markedly increased, and the smear shows schistocytes. Which of the following best explains both her bleeding and her kidney injury?","options":["Autoantibodies against platelet glycoproteins","Inherited deficiency of factor VIII","Microthrombi consuming factors and platelets","Reduced hepatic synthesis of clotting factors"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"HE-9-C1","obj":"HE-9-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["Prolonged PT and aPTT, low fibrinogen, high D-dimer, low platelets, and schistocytes together mean disseminated intravascular coagulation.","Widespread activation of coagulation forms fibrin microthrombi in small vessels, which injure organs such as the kidney.","The same process uses up platelets and clotting factors, and fibrinolysis is activated, so the patient bleeds from puncture sites and mucosa."],"why":"DIC is always secondary, here to severe viral infection with endothelial injury and shock. Tissue factor and cytokines trigger coagulation throughout the circulation. Fibrin microthrombi block small vessels in the kidney, lungs, brain, and adrenals, causing ischemic injury, and red cells sheared on fibrin strands become schistocytes. At the same time, platelets, fibrinogen, and other factors are consumed, and plasmin breaks down fibrin (raising D-dimer), so the patient bleeds. Clotting and bleeding happen together.","whyNot":["Antiplatelet autoantibodies cause immune thrombocytopenia, with normal PT, aPTT, and fibrinogen and no schistocytes.","Hemophilia A prolongs only the aPTT, presents with joint and muscle bleeding from childhood, and does not cause microthrombi.","Correct.","Liver dysfunction prolongs PT and aPTT, but it does not explain the high D-dimer, schistocytes, and kidney injury from microthrombi."],"study":{"topics":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"comps":["HE-9-C1","HE-4"]}},{"id":"HE-C30-Q4","stem":"Her physician also considered other viral hemorrhagic fevers. A nurse who cared for patients during an outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo develops fever, vomiting, diarrhea, and bleeding, and a filamentous RNA virus is isolated. How was this nurse most likely infected?","options":["Bite of an infected Aedes mosquito","Direct contact with infected body fluids","Inhalation of aerosolized rodent excreta","Bite of an infected Ixodes tick"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"HE-9-E","obj":"HE-9-E.6","dok":2,"approach":["A filamentous RNA virus causing hemorrhagic fever in central Africa is a filovirus: Ebola or Marburg.","Filoviruses spread from person to person through direct contact with blood and other body fluids of sick patients.","Health care workers and family caregivers are at high risk; fruit bats are the likely natural reservoir."],"why":"Ebola and Marburg viruses are filoviruses, long filamentous RNA viruses. Fruit bats are the likely reservoir, and outbreaks begin when a person is infected by contact with an animal. The virus then spreads between people through direct contact with blood, vomit, stool, and other body fluids, so caregivers are at high risk. Illness starts with fever and progresses to vomiting, diarrhea, hemorrhage, DIC, and multiorgan failure. Dengue, by contrast, is spread by Aedes mosquitoes and not from person to person.","whyNot":["Aedes mosquitoes spread dengue, chikungunya, Zika, and yellow fever, not filoviruses.","Correct.","Inhaled rodent excreta spread hantavirus, a bunyavirus that causes pulmonary syndrome or hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome.","Ixodes ticks transmit Babesia, Borrelia, and Anaplasma, not filoviruses."],"study":{"topics":["Ebola and Marburg viruses","Viral hemorrhagic fevers"],"comps":["HE-9-E","HE-9-C1"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C01","system":"IM","dx":"DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion) with hypocalcemic tetany and truncus arteriosus","codes":["IM-15-A2"],"case":"A 4-day-old boy in the neonatal intensive care unit develops carpopedal spasm and brief generalized jerking movements. A murmur was heard at birth, and echocardiography showed truncus arteriosus. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 156/min, respirations are 52/min, and blood pressure is 68/40 mm Hg. Physical examination shows low-set ears, a short philtrum, micrognathia, and a cleft of the soft palate. Serum total calcium concentration is 6.1 mg/dL (N = 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL). A chest x-ray shows no thymic shadow. Diagnosis: DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion syndrome) with hypocalcemic tetany and truncus arteriosus.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C01-Q1","stem":"Additional serum studies are obtained. Which of the following sets of findings best explains his tetany?","options":["High parathyroid hormone and high phosphorus","Low parathyroid hormone and low phosphorus","Low parathyroid hormone and high phosphorus","High parathyroid hormone and low phosphorus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-1","obj":"IM-1.3","dok":3,"approach":["Link the missing thymus to failed development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches.","Those pouches also form the parathyroid glands, so parathyroid hormone (PTH) is low.","Without PTH, calcium falls and the kidney keeps phosphate, so phosphorus rises."],"why":"In 22q11.2 deletion syndrome the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches develop poorly. The third pouch forms the thymus and inferior parathyroids, and the fourth pouch forms the superior parathyroids. Parathyroid hypoplasia gives primary hypoparathyroidism. Low PTH lowers bone resorption, renal calcium reabsorption, and calcitriol synthesis, so calcium falls. PTH also normally makes the kidney excrete phosphate, so its absence raises serum phosphorus. The same pouch failure explains the absent thymic shadow, and abnormal neural crest migration explains the conotruncal heart defect.","whyNot":["High PTH with high phosphorus fits chronic kidney disease or PTH resistance (pseudohypoparathyroidism), where the glands are present but the response fails.","Low PTH removes the phosphaturic effect of the hormone, so phosphorus rises rather than falls.","Correct.","High PTH with low phosphorus is secondary hyperparathyroidism, as in vitamin D deficiency, where the parathyroids are intact and respond to low calcium."],"study":{"topics":["Pharyngeal pouch derivatives","Hypoparathyroidism and calcium balance"],"comps":["IM-1","IM-15-A2"]}},{"id":"IM-C01-Q2","stem":"The organ that is missing on his chest x-ray normally develops from the same pharyngeal pouch as which of the following structures?","options":["Superior parathyroid glands","Parafollicular C cells of the thyroid","Epithelium of the palatine tonsils","Inferior parathyroid glands"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-1","obj":"IM-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the missing organ as the thymus.","Recall that the thymus comes from the endoderm of the third pharyngeal pouch.","The other derivative of the third pouch is the pair of inferior parathyroid glands."],"why":"The thymic epithelium arises from the ventral part of the third pharyngeal pouch, and the dorsal part of the same pouch forms the inferior parathyroids. Neural crest cells form the thymic capsule and septa. As the thymus descends into the anterior mediastinum, it pulls the third pouch parathyroids down with it. That is why the third pouch glands end up below the fourth pouch glands.","whyNot":["The superior parathyroid glands come from the fourth pharyngeal pouch. They do not migrate with the thymus, so they end up higher.","Parafollicular C cells come from the ultimobranchial body of the fourth pouch region, with neural crest contribution.","The palatine tonsil epithelium comes from the second pharyngeal pouch.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Thymus embryology","Pharyngeal pouch derivatives"],"comps":["IM-1","IM-15-A2"]}},{"id":"IM-C01-Q3","stem":"Flow cytometry shows a markedly low CD3+ T cell count with normal B cell and natural killer cell counts. His bone marrow lymphoid progenitors are normal. Which of the following best explains the low T cell count?","options":["Absent thymic epithelium for precursor maturation","Failure of marrow stem cells to form lymphoid progenitors","Defective recombination of T cell receptor gene segments","Loss of interleukin-7 receptor signaling in thymocytes"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-1","obj":"IM-1.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note that the marrow makes normal lymphoid progenitors.","T cell precursors must leave the marrow and mature inside a thymic epithelial framework.","A hypoplastic thymus gives the precursors nowhere to mature, so T cells are low while B and NK cells are normal."],"why":"The thymus begins as an endodermal epithelial framework. During fetal life, T cell precursors from the bone marrow (and earlier from fetal liver) colonize it. The epithelial reticular cells then organize an outer cortex packed with developing thymocytes and an inner medulla with Hassall corpuscles. In DiGeorge syndrome this epithelial framework is small or absent. Normal precursors arrive but cannot mature, so T cells are low while B and NK cells are normal.","whyNot":["Correct.","A stem cell defect in lymphoid progenitor formation, as in reticular dysgenesis, would also remove B and NK cells.","Failed T cell receptor gene recombination, as in RAG deficiency, would also block B cell development because B cells use the same recombinase.","Loss of interleukin-7 receptor signaling causes a lymphocyte-intrinsic form of SCID with a normal thymic epithelium. It does not explain the absent thymus in this infant."],"study":{"topics":["Thymic epithelial framework","T cell maturation"],"comps":["IM-1","IM-3"]}},{"id":"IM-C01-Q4","stem":"Which of the following tests is most likely to confirm the underlying genetic abnormality?","options":["Standard G-banded karyotype","PCR sizing of a trinucleotide repeat","Chromosomal microarray analysis","Methylation analysis of an imprinted region"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-15-A2","obj":"IM-15-A.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the typical features of 22q11.2 deletion syndrome: conotruncal defect, facial changes, cleft palate, thymic hypoplasia, and hypocalcemia.","Recall that the deletion is usually about 3 Mb, too small to see on a routine karyotype.","Choose a test that detects small copy number losses, such as chromosomal microarray or FISH."],"why":"Most patients have a deletion of about 3 Mb on the long arm of chromosome 22. Loss of one copy of TBX1 in the deleted segment is thought to drive the pharyngeal arch, cardiac outflow, and palate defects. A standard karyotype cannot see deletions this small, so it is usually normal. Chromosomal microarray detects the copy number loss, and FISH with a 22q11.2 probe can also confirm it.","whyNot":["A G-banded karyotype resolves changes of about 5 Mb or more, so the typical 22q11.2 microdeletion is missed.","Trinucleotide repeat sizing diagnoses expansion disorders such as fragile X syndrome or Huntington disease.","Correct.","Methylation analysis diagnoses imprinting disorders such as Prader-Willi or Angelman syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["22q11.2 deletion syndrome","Genetic testing methods"],"comps":["IM-15-A2","IM-1"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C02","system":"IM","dx":"Severe combined immunodeficiency from adenosine deaminase deficiency","codes":["IM-15-A4"],"case":"A 7-month-old girl is brought to the physician because of cough and fast breathing for 4 days. She has had watery diarrhea and poor weight gain since age 3 months and three courses of treatment for oral thrush. Her parents are first cousins. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 152/min, respirations are 58/min, and blood pressure is 88/52 mm Hg. Weight is below the 3rd percentile. Physical examination shows white plaques on the buccal mucosa, no palpable lymph nodes, and no visible tonsils. Absolute lymphocyte count is 350/mm3 (N = 4,000 to 13,500/mm3). A chest x-ray shows bilateral interstitial infiltrates and no thymic shadow. Bronchoalveolar lavage shows Pneumocystis jirovecii. Erythrocyte adenosine deaminase activity is absent. Diagnosis: severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) from adenosine deaminase deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C02-Q1","stem":"A deoxynucleotide accumulates in her lymphocytes and leads to their death. This metabolite inhibits which of the following enzymes?","options":["Thymidylate synthase","Glutamine PRPP amidotransferase","Dihydrofolate reductase","Ribonucleotide reductase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-10","obj":"IM-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["Adenosine deaminase normally converts adenosine and deoxyadenosine to inosine and deoxyinosine.","Without it, deoxyadenosine is phosphorylated and dATP builds up, especially in lymphocytes.","dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, so the cell cannot make deoxynucleotides for DNA synthesis."],"why":"Adenosine deaminase is part of purine salvage. When it is missing, deoxyadenosine accumulates and lymphocytes phosphorylate it to dATP. High dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, the enzyme that makes all four deoxyribonucleotides. Dividing lymphocytes can no longer synthesize DNA and die. T, B, and NK cells are all lost, and inheritance is autosomal recessive, which fits her consanguineous parents.","whyNot":["Thymidylate synthase makes dTMP from dUMP. It is the target of 5-fluorouracil, not of dATP.","Glutamine PRPP amidotransferase is the committed step of de novo purine synthesis. It is feedback-inhibited by purine nucleotides, but that does not explain lymphocyte death in this disease.","Dihydrofolate reductase regenerates tetrahydrofolate. It is the target of methotrexate and trimethoprim.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Adenosine deaminase deficiency","Purine salvage pathway"],"comps":["IM-10","IM-15-A4"]}},{"id":"IM-C02-Q2","stem":"Flow cytometry of her peripheral blood is most likely to show which of the following patterns?","options":["Normal T cells, absent B cells, normal NK cells","Absent T cells, absent B cells, absent NK cells","Absent T cells, normal B cells, absent NK cells","Absent T cells, absent B cells, normal NK cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-12","obj":"IM-12.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that toxic dATP harms every developing lymphocyte line.","So ADA deficiency removes T, B, and NK cells together.","Compare the other SCID genotypes, which spare one line."],"why":"SCID genotypes are sorted by which lymphocytes remain. ADA deficiency poisons all lymphocyte lines, so T, B, and NK cells are all absent. X-linked SCID from a common gamma chain (IL2RG) mutation removes T and NK cells but leaves nonfunctional B cells. RAG deficiency removes T and B cells but spares NK cells, which do not rearrange receptor genes. All forms have very low T cell receptor excision circles on newborn screening because few T cells leave the thymus.","whyNot":["Absent B cells with normal T and NK cells is the pattern of X-linked agammaglobulinemia.","Correct.","Absent T and NK cells with B cells present is the pattern of X-linked SCID from a common gamma chain mutation.","Absent T and B cells with NK cells present is the pattern of RAG1 or RAG2 deficiency."],"study":{"topics":["SCID genotypes and lymphocyte profiles","T cell receptor excision circles"],"comps":["IM-12","IM-15-A4"]}},{"id":"IM-C02-Q3","stem":"Which of the following exposures would pose the greatest risk to this patient before her immune system is reconstituted?","options":["Live attenuated rotavirus vaccine","Intravenous immune globulin infusion","Irradiated red blood cell transfusion","Inactivated influenza vaccine"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-A4","obj":"IM-15-A.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize that she has no functional T or B cells.","A live vaccine organism can replicate unchecked and cause disseminated disease.","Inactivated vaccines, irradiated blood, and immune globulin carry no replicating agent or viable donor lymphocytes."],"why":"Infants with SCID cannot control even weakened organisms. Live vaccines such as rotavirus, BCG, and oral polio can cause severe or fatal vaccine-strain infection, so they are withheld. Blood products must be irradiated (and CMV-safe) because viable donor T cells in nonirradiated blood can engraft and attack the host as transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease. Immune globulin replacement and Pneumocystis prophylaxis are standard until stem cell transplantation or other definitive treatment.","whyNot":["Correct.","Immune globulin is part of her treatment and replaces the antibodies she cannot make.","Irradiation inactivates donor lymphocytes, so this is the safe way to transfuse a patient with SCID.","An inactivated vaccine cannot replicate. It is safe, though she may not mount an antibody response to it."],"study":{"topics":["Live vaccines in immunodeficiency","Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease"],"comps":["IM-15-A4","IM-15-A"]}},{"id":"IM-C02-Q4","stem":"At autopsy of a similar infant, the thymus is tiny, with scattered epithelial cells, almost no thymocytes, no Hassall corpuscles, and no distinction between cortex and medulla. In a normal infant thymus, which of the following features identifies the cortex?","options":["Dense immature thymocytes","Concentric Hassall corpuscles","Germinal centers with B cells","Loose mature T lymphocytes"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the layout of a thymic lobule: dark outer cortex and pale inner medulla.","The cortex is dark because it is packed with immature thymocytes on an epithelial reticular network.","The medulla holds fewer, more mature T cells and Hassall corpuscles."],"why":"Each thymic lobule has a darkly staining cortex and a paler medulla. The cortex is crowded with immature, dividing thymocytes held in a mesh of epithelial reticular cells. Its capillaries form the blood thymus barrier, which limits blood-borne antigen while T cells develop. The medulla contains fewer, more mature T cells and the concentric keratinized Hassall corpuscles. In SCID the thymus never fills with thymocytes, so this cortex and medulla pattern does not form.","whyNot":["Correct.","Hassall corpuscles are whorls of keratinized epithelial reticular cells found in the medulla.","Germinal centers are B cell structures in lymph node and splenic follicles. The normal thymus does not contain them.","Loosely packed mature T cells give the medulla its pale staining."],"study":{"topics":["Thymic histology","Blood thymus barrier"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-3"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C03","system":"IM","dx":"X-linked (Bruton) agammaglobulinemia","codes":["IM-15-A5"],"case":"A 14-month-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of fever and cough. Since age 8 months he has had three episodes of otitis media and one of pneumonia. His mother's brother died of infection in early childhood. Temperature is 39.0 °C (102.2 °F), pulse is 142/min, respirations are 44/min, and blood pressure is 90/56 mm Hg. He has barely visible tonsils and no palpable cervical lymph nodes. A chest x-ray shows right lower lobe consolidation. Blood cultures grow Streptococcus pneumoniae. Serum IgG is 80 mg/dL (N = 350 to 1,200 mg/dL), IgA is undetectable (N = 15 to 100 mg/dL), and IgM is 10 mg/dL (N = 40 to 150 mg/dL). CD19+ B cells are less than 1% of lymphocytes (N = 13% to 35%). T and NK cell counts are normal. Diagnosis: X-linked (Bruton) agammaglobulinemia.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C03-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the defect in this patient's B cell development?","options":["Heavy chain gene segment recombination failure","Class switch failure from absent CD40 ligand","Pre-B cell arrest from defective kinase signaling","Plasma cell differentiation failure in mature B cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-15-A5","obj":"IM-15-A.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note absent circulating B cells with normal T cells and very low levels of every immunoglobulin class.","X-linked inheritance through the maternal side points to the BTK gene.","Bruton tyrosine kinase carries the pre-B cell receptor signal, so B cells stop at the pre-B stage."],"why":"Bruton tyrosine kinase transmits signals from the pre-B cell receptor that tell the cell to proliferate and continue maturing. When BTK is mutated, B cell development stops at the pre-B stage in the bone marrow. Few mature B cells reach the blood, so follicles, tonsils, and all immunoglobulin classes are markedly reduced. Infections begin after about 6 months, when maternal IgG that crossed the placenta has been used up.","whyNot":["Failed gene segment recombination from RAG deficiency would also prevent T cell development and cause SCID. This boy's T cells are normal.","Absent CD40 ligand causes X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome. B cell numbers are normal and IgM is normal or high.","Correct.","Failed plasma cell differentiation with normal B cell numbers describes common variable immunodeficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Bruton tyrosine kinase","B cell development stages"],"comps":["IM-15-A5","IM-12"]}},{"id":"IM-C03-Q2","stem":"Another infant has absent T cells and B cells but normal natural killer cells. The defect lies in the first step of antigen receptor gene rearrangement, a step that this patient's pre-B cells complete normally. Which of the following activities is most likely defective in that infant?","options":["Signaling through the common gamma chain","Deamination of cytidine in switch regions","Addition of nontemplated nucleotides at junctions","Cleavage at recombination signal sequences"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-12","obj":"IM-12.1","dok":3,"approach":["Absent T and B cells with spared NK cells means a defect shared only by cells that rearrange receptor genes.","V(D)J recombination begins when RAG1 and RAG2 cut DNA at recombination signal sequences.","Without these cuts no T cell receptor or immunoglobulin can form, so both lines fail."],"why":"T and B cells both build their antigen receptors by V(D)J recombination. RAG1 and RAG2 make double-strand breaks at recombination signal sequences next to each gene segment, and DNA repair enzymes join the segments. TdT then adds random nucleotides at the joints, which increases junctional diversity. RAG deficiency blocks receptor formation, so T and B cells are absent, while NK cells, which do not rearrange receptor genes, remain. In XLA the RAG step is intact and the block comes later.","whyNot":["Loss of common gamma chain signaling causes X-linked SCID with absent T and NK cells and present B cells, which does not match.","Activation-induced cytidine deaminase works in class switching and somatic hypermutation. Its loss gives an autosomal hyper-IgM syndrome with normal B cell numbers.","TdT adds nontemplated nucleotides. Losing it reduces junctional diversity, but receptors still form, so it does not cause SCID.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["V(D)J recombination","RAG deficiency"],"comps":["IM-12","IM-15-A4"]}},{"id":"IM-C03-Q3","stem":"In addition to infections with encapsulated bacteria, this patient is at particular risk for which of the following?","options":["Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis","Chronic enteroviral meningoencephalitis","Recurrent Neisseria meningitidis bacteremia","Disseminated nontuberculous mycobacterial infection"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-A","obj":"IM-15-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the defective arm: antibody production (B cells).","Antibody defects allow encapsulated bacteria, Giardia, and enteroviruses, which are controlled mainly by neutralizing antibody.","Match each distractor to its own defective arm."],"why":"The infection pattern points to the defective arm of immunity. B cell defects give infections with encapsulated bacteria (which need antibody for opsonization), Giardia, and enteroviruses (which are neutralized by antibody). Boys with XLA can develop chronic enteroviral meningoencephalitis, and live oral polio vaccine can cause vaccine-associated paralysis. The first test for a suspected B cell defect is serum immunoglobulin levels, followed by flow cytometry for B cells.","whyNot":["Invasive aspergillosis points to a phagocyte defect such as chronic granulomatous disease or severe neutropenia.","Correct.","Recurrent Neisseria bacteremia points to a terminal complement (C5 to C9) deficiency. The screening test is CH50.","Disseminated mycobacterial infection points to a defect in the IL-12 and interferon-gamma pathway or a T cell defect."],"study":{"topics":["Infection patterns by immune defect","Enteroviral infection in agammaglobulinemia"],"comps":["IM-15-A","IM-15-A5"]}},{"id":"IM-C03-Q4","stem":"A lymph node biopsy from this patient would most likely show depletion of which of the following regions?","options":["Cortical follicles and germinal centers","Paracortex around high endothelial venules","Medullary sinuses with macrophages","Subcapsular sinus macrophage layer"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Match each lymph node zone to its main cell type.","B cells live in the cortical follicles and form germinal centers there.","With almost no mature B cells, follicles and germinal centers are absent or tiny."],"why":"Lymph node B cells gather in cortical follicles, which form germinal centers after antigen exposure. T cells occupy the paracortex. In XLA, B cells are nearly absent, so nodes and tonsils are small and lack follicles and germinal centers. The paracortex is preserved. The opposite pattern occurs in DiGeorge syndrome, where the paracortex is depleted and follicles are intact.","whyNot":["Correct.","The paracortex is the T cell zone. It is depleted in T cell defects such as DiGeorge syndrome.","Medullary sinuses carry lymph past macrophages. They do not depend on B cells and are not depleted here.","The subcapsular sinus receives afferent lymph and is lined by macrophages. It is not a B cell zone."],"study":{"topics":["Lymph node zones","Lymphoid histology in immunodeficiency"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-15-A5"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C04","system":"IM","dx":"Common variable immunodeficiency with chronic giardiasis","codes":["IM-15-A1"],"case":"A 32-year-old woman comes to the physician because of foul-smelling, greasy diarrhea, bloating, and a 4-kg (9-lb) weight loss over 2 months. She has had four episodes of sinusitis and two episodes of pneumonia during the past 3 years. She was healthy as a child. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. The abdomen is mildly distended and nontender. Stool antigen testing is positive for Giardia duodenalis. Serum IgG is 210 mg/dL (N = 700 to 1,600 mg/dL), IgA is 15 mg/dL (N = 70 to 400 mg/dL), and IgM is 25 mg/dL (N = 40 to 230 mg/dL). She has no antibody response to tetanus or pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccines. Diagnosis: common variable immunodeficiency with chronic giardiasis.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C04-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the defect underlying her immunodeficiency?","options":["Absent B cells from arrest at the pre-B cell stage","Absent T cells from failed thymic development","Normal B cells that fail to become plasma cells","Low NK cells from a common gamma chain defect"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-A1","obj":"IM-15-A.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note adult onset, low IgG and IgA, and absent vaccine responses.","This is common variable immunodeficiency, in which B cells are present but do not mature into antibody-secreting plasma cells.","So the B cell count is usually normal, which separates it from XLA."],"why":"In common variable immunodeficiency, most patients have normal numbers of circulating B cells. The defect is in their differentiation into plasma cells, so IgG and IgA (and often IgM) are low and vaccine responses fail. It often begins in the second to fourth decade. Patients get sinopulmonary infections and Giardia, and they have higher rates of autoimmune disease (such as autoimmune cytopenias), granulomatous disease, and B cell lymphoma.","whyNot":["Absent B cells from a pre-B cell block is X-linked agammaglobulinemia, which presents in infant boys.","Absent T cells from thymic failure is DiGeorge syndrome, which would bring viral and fungal infections and normal B cell function.","Correct.","Common gamma chain defects cause X-linked SCID in infancy, with absent T and NK cells."],"study":{"topics":["Common variable immunodeficiency","Antibody deficiency comparison"],"comps":["IM-15-A1","IM-15-A"]}},{"id":"IM-C04-Q2","stem":"Monthly intravenous immune globulin is started. The infused IgG promotes phagocytosis of bacteria by binding receptors on neutrophils and macrophages. Which portion of the IgG molecule binds these receptors?","options":["J chain linking two monomers","Constant domain of the light chains","Hypervariable regions of the Fab","Fc region of the heavy chains"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the two jobs of an antibody: the Fab binds antigen and the Fc carries out effector functions.","Phagocytes carry Fc gamma receptors.","So opsonization depends on the Fc region formed by the heavy chain constant domains."],"why":"IgG has two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains. The two Fab arms each contain hypervariable regions from both chains that form the antigen-binding site. The Fc region is made only of heavy chain constant domains. It sets the isotype's functions, including binding Fc receptors on phagocytes (opsonization) and binding C1q to start the classical complement pathway. Replacement IgG coats bacteria through its Fab and links them to phagocytes through its Fc.","whyNot":["The J chain joins IgA dimers and IgM pentamers. IgG is a monomer without a J chain.","Light chains help form the Fab. They do not take part in Fc receptor binding.","Hypervariable regions in the Fab bind the antigen itself. They determine specificity, not effector function.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Immunoglobulin structure","Opsonization"],"comps":["IM-5","IM-6"]}},{"id":"IM-C04-Q3","stem":"Secretory IgA helps clear Giardia from the small intestine. Which of the following cells normally take up luminal antigen to start the intestinal IgA response?","options":["Paneth cells in crypt bases","M cells overlying Peyer patches","Goblet cells of the villi","Enteroendocrine cells of the crypts"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["The IgA response begins in gut-associated lymphoid tissue, mainly the Peyer patches of the ileum.","Specialized epithelial cells over these patches transport antigen from the lumen to the immune cells beneath.","Those cells are M (microfold) cells."],"why":"Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue includes the tonsils and the Peyer patches of the ileum. M cells in the epithelium over Peyer patches take up luminal antigens and pass them to dendritic cells and lymphocytes in the dome below. B cells there switch to IgA under T cell and cytokine signals such as TGF-beta. They become plasma cells that home to the lamina propria and secrete IgA that is carried across the epithelium into the lumen.","whyNot":["Paneth cells secrete defensins and lysozyme. They do not transport antigen to lymphoid tissue.","Correct.","Goblet cells secrete mucus, which forms a barrier but does not start the IgA response.","Enteroendocrine cells secrete hormones such as CCK and secretin. They do not sample antigen."],"study":{"topics":["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","M cells and IgA"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-5"]}},{"id":"IM-C04-Q4","stem":"An enlarged cervical lymph node is removed to exclude lymphoma. In a normal lymph node, the antibody-secreting cells that she fails to produce in adequate numbers are located mainly in which of the following regions?","options":["Primary follicles","Paracortex","Medullary cords","Subcapsular sinus"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the missing cells as plasma cells.","Recall the lymph node layout from capsule inward: subcapsular sinus, cortex with follicles, paracortex, then medulla.","Plasma cells collect in the medullary cords, near the medullary sinuses that lead to efferent lymph."],"why":"Lymph enters a node through afferent vessels into the subcapsular sinus. The outer cortex holds B cell follicles, and primary follicles become secondary follicles with germinal centers after antigen exposure. The paracortex holds T cells and high endothelial venules. The medulla has medullary cords rich in plasma cells and macrophages, separated by medullary sinuses that drain to the efferent lymphatic. Antibody made in the cords enters the efferent lymph and reaches the blood.","whyNot":["Primary follicles hold resting naive B cells that have not yet met antigen.","The paracortex is the T cell zone, where naive T cells enter through high endothelial venules.","Correct.","The subcapsular sinus receives afferent lymph and is lined by macrophages."],"study":{"topics":["Lymph node microanatomy","Plasma cells"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-8"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C05","system":"IM","dx":"Selective IgA deficiency with anaphylactic transfusion reaction","codes":["IM-15-A3","IM-15-C1"],"case":"A 26-year-old woman receives a transfusion of packed red blood cells after blood loss during an emergency appendectomy. Within 5 minutes of starting the transfusion, she develops flushing, diffuse hives, wheezing, and light-headedness. She has had frequent sinus infections since childhood and has never received blood products. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 128/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 76/40 mm Hg. Physical examination shows diffuse urticaria and expiratory wheezes. The transfusion is stopped and she is treated. Serum IgA is undetectable (N = 70 to 400 mg/dL), and IgG and IgM are normal. Anti-IgA antibodies are present. Diagnosis: selective IgA deficiency with an anaphylactic transfusion reaction.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C05-Q1","stem":"Two years later, she develops chronic diarrhea, bloating, and iron deficiency anemia, and celiac disease is suspected. Which of the following serologic tests is most appropriate?","options":["IgG antibody to tissue transglutaminase","IgA antibody to tissue transglutaminase","IgE antibody to wheat gliadin","IgA antibody to endomysium"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-A3","obj":"IM-15-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that selective IgA deficiency is associated with celiac disease.","Standard celiac screening measures IgA autoantibodies, which she cannot make.","So IgA-based tests are falsely negative, and an IgG-based test is needed."],"why":"Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency, and most patients have no symptoms. Some have sinopulmonary or Giardia infections, and celiac and other autoimmune diseases are more common. Because the usual celiac screening tests detect IgA antibodies, they are falsely negative in these patients. IgG-based tests, such as IgG antibody to tissue transglutaminase or to deamidated gliadin peptides, are used instead.","whyNot":["Correct.","IgA tissue transglutaminase is the usual screening test, but she cannot make IgA, so the result would be falsely negative.","IgE to wheat detects IgE-mediated wheat allergy, a type I reaction. Celiac disease is a T cell-mediated response to gluten.","IgA endomysial antibody is specific for celiac disease, but it is also IgA-based and would be falsely negative."],"study":{"topics":["Selective IgA deficiency","Celiac serology"],"comps":["IM-15-A3","IM-15-A"]}},{"id":"IM-C05-Q2","stem":"Despite the absence of IgA, her mucosal infections have been mild. Which of the following immunoglobulins is most likely carried into her mucosal secretions by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor in increased amounts to compensate?","options":["IgD","IgM","IgE","IgG"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.2","dok":3,"approach":["Ask how an antibody reaches mucosal secretions: the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor carries it across the epithelium.","This receptor binds antibodies that contain a J chain.","Besides dimeric IgA, the only J chain antibody is pentameric IgM."],"why":"Secretory IgA is a dimer joined by a J chain. The polymeric immunoglobulin receptor on epithelial cells binds the J chain antibody and carries it across into the lumen, and part of the receptor stays on as secretory component. Pentameric IgM also has a J chain, so it uses the same transport route. In IgA deficiency, secretory IgM often increases and partly takes over the mucosal role. IgM is also the first isotype made in a primary response and the strongest activator of complement.","whyNot":["IgD is a monomer that serves mainly as a receptor on naive B cells. It is not transported into secretions.","Correct.","IgE is a monomer bound to mast cells and basophils. It is not carried into secretions by the polymeric receptor.","IgG is a monomer without a J chain, so the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor does not carry it. It crosses the placenta through the neonatal Fc receptor instead."],"study":{"topics":["Immunoglobulin isotypes","Secretory immunoglobulin transport"],"comps":["IM-5","IM-2"]}},{"id":"IM-C05-Q3","stem":"Her reaction had the clinical features of type I hypersensitivity. In type I hypersensitivity, the immediate reaction is often followed hours later by a second wave of swelling and airway narrowing without further exposure. Which of the following best explains this late-phase reaction?","options":["Recruited eosinophils and new leukotrienes","Delayed release of preformed histamine","Th1 cell activation of tissue macrophages","Immune complex deposition in small vessels"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-C1","obj":"IM-15-C.1","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two phases: the immediate phase comes from mediators released within minutes when mast cells degranulate.","Mast cells also make lipid mediators and cytokines that recruit eosinophils and other leukocytes.","These recruited cells and newly made leukotrienes cause the late phase hours later."],"why":"In type I hypersensitivity, a first exposure drives Th2 cells and IL-4 to switch B cells to IgE, which binds FcepsilonRI on mast cells. On re-exposure, antigen cross-links this IgE, and mast cells release stored histamine and tryptase within minutes. They also synthesize leukotrienes, prostaglandins, and cytokines that recruit eosinophils, neutrophils, and Th2 cells. About 2 to 24 hours later, these cells and leukotrienes such as LTC4 and LTD4 produce the late phase, with tissue swelling and sustained bronchoconstriction.","whyNot":["Correct.","Preformed histamine is released in the first minutes and is short-lived, so it explains the immediate phase.","Th1 activation of macrophages is type IV hypersensitivity, which peaks 48 to 72 hours after exposure.","Immune complex deposition is type III hypersensitivity, as in serum sickness, which appears 1 to 2 weeks after exposure."],"study":{"topics":["Type I hypersensitivity phases","Mast cell mediators"],"comps":["IM-15-C1","IM-15-C"]}},{"id":"IM-C05-Q4","stem":"In a healthy person, most of the secretory IgA in respiratory and intestinal secretions is produced by plasma cells located in which of the following sites?","options":["Lymph node medullary cords","Splenic red pulp","Bone marrow","Mucosal lamina propria"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Secretory IgA must cross the mucosal epithelium, so it is made close to that epithelium.","IgA plasma cells arise in mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue and home back to the mucosa.","They settle in the lamina propria just beneath the epithelium."],"why":"B cells activated in mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, such as tonsils and Peyer patches, switch to IgA and leave through the lymph. They return to mucosal surfaces throughout the body and settle in the lamina propria as plasma cells. There they secrete dimeric IgA, which the overlying epithelium carries into the lumen. This shared mucosal homing is why a response started in the gut can protect other mucosal surfaces.","whyNot":["Medullary cord plasma cells release antibody into efferent lymph and then the blood, not directly into mucosal secretions.","Splenic red pulp plasma cells add antibody to the blood. They do not supply mucosal secretions.","Bone marrow plasma cells make most serum IgG and the monomeric serum IgA, not the dimeric secretory IgA.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","IgA plasma cell homing"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-5"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C06","system":"IM","dx":"X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome (CD40 ligand deficiency) with Pneumocystis pneumonia","codes":["IM-12"],"case":"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of fever, dry cough, and increasing difficulty breathing for 5 days. Since age 9 months he has had recurrent otitis media, two episodes of pneumonia, and Cryptosporidium diarrhea. Temperature is 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), pulse is 148/min, respirations are 50/min, and blood pressure is 92/56 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 86% on room air. Diffuse fine crackles are heard. A chest x-ray shows bilateral interstitial infiltrates. Silver stain of bronchoalveolar lavage fluid shows cysts of Pneumocystis jirovecii. Serum IgM is 320 mg/dL (N = 40 to 150 mg/dL), IgG is 60 mg/dL (N = 450 to 1,200 mg/dL), and IgA is undetectable (N = 20 to 100 mg/dL). B cell and T cell counts are normal. Genetic testing confirms X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C06-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his immunoglobulin pattern?","options":["Absent Bruton tyrosine kinase in pre-B cells","Absent CD40 on B cells and macrophages","Absent activation-induced cytidine deaminase in B cells","Absent CD40 ligand on activated CD4+ T cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-12","obj":"IM-12.4","dok":2,"approach":["High IgM with low IgG, IgA, and IgE means B cells are present but cannot switch isotype.","Class switching needs CD40 ligand on activated helper T cells to bind CD40 on B cells.","X-linked inheritance and Pneumocystis infection point to the T cell side: the CD40 ligand gene (CD40LG) on the X chromosome."],"why":"Isotype switching needs two signals from a helper T cell: CD40 ligand binding CD40 on the B cell, and cytokines such as IL-4 and IFN-gamma that choose the new isotype. Without CD40 ligand, B cells keep making IgM, and IgG, IgA, and IgE stay low. CD40 ligand also activates macrophages and dendritic cells through CD40, so cell-mediated immunity is weak. That is why these boys get Pneumocystis and Cryptosporidium as well as pyogenic infections.","whyNot":["BTK deficiency causes X-linked agammaglobulinemia with absent B cells and low IgM.","CD40 deficiency gives a similar picture, but the CD40 gene is autosomal, so it would be autosomal recessive, not X-linked.","AID deficiency also blocks class switching, but it is autosomal recessive and leaves T cell help intact, so opportunistic infections are not typical.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hyper-IgM syndromes","Isotype switching"],"comps":["IM-12","IM-5"]}},{"id":"IM-C06-Q2","stem":"CD40 ligand normally stimulates dendritic cells to increase their expression of B7 (CD80 and CD86). A naive CD4+ T cell recognizes a Pneumocystis peptide on MHC class II on a dendritic cell that expresses no B7. Which of the following outcomes is most likely for this T cell?","options":["Th1 differentiation","Clonal expansion","Anergy","Memory cell formation"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-13","obj":"IM-13.2","dok":3,"approach":["Full activation of a naive T cell needs signal 1 (T cell receptor binding peptide on MHC) and signal 2 (CD28 binding B7).","Without B7 there is no costimulation through CD28.","Signal 1 alone makes the T cell unresponsive, which is anergy."],"why":"Naive T cells need two signals. Signal 1 is the T cell receptor binding its peptide on MHC. Signal 2 is CD28 on the T cell binding B7 on the antigen-presenting cell. With signal 1 alone, the T cell becomes anergic and does not respond to later exposure. CD40 ligand normally boosts B7 and IL-12 on dendritic cells, which helps prime Th1 responses. Later, CTLA-4 and PD-1 on activated T cells send inhibitory signals that turn the response down.","whyNot":["Th1 differentiation needs full activation plus IL-12 from the antigen-presenting cell.","Clonal expansion needs both signal 1 and CD28 costimulation, which drive IL-2 production.","Correct.","Memory cells form only after a full activation response with costimulation."],"study":{"topics":["Two-signal T cell activation","Anergy and costimulation"],"comps":["IM-13","IM-12"]}},{"id":"IM-C06-Q3","stem":"Dendritic cells carry Pneumocystis antigens from the lung to the hilar lymph nodes through afferent lymphatics. Naive T cells that recognize these antigens most likely entered the node through which of the following routes?","options":["Afferent lymphatics with dendritic cells","Paracortical high endothelial venules","Efferent lymphatics at the hilum","Medullary sinuses from the hilum"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-8","obj":"IM-8.4","dok":2,"approach":["Separate how antigen arrives (dendritic cells in afferent lymph) from how naive T cells arrive.","Naive lymphocytes circulate in blood and leave it in the node through specialized venules.","These high endothelial venules sit in the paracortex, the T cell zone."],"why":"Naive lymphocytes move from blood to lymph node through high endothelial venules in the paracortex. L-selectin on the lymphocyte lets it roll on the venule wall, and the chemokine receptor CCR7 responds to chemokines made in the T cell zone, which triggers integrin binding and exit from the vessel. In the paracortex the T cells meet dendritic cells that brought antigen in through afferent lymph. Lymphocytes that are not activated leave through efferent lymph and return to the blood.","whyNot":["Afferent lymph brings dendritic cells and antigen into the node. Most naive T cells enter from the blood.","Correct.","Efferent lymphatics are the exit route for lymphocytes leaving the node.","Medullary sinuses drain lymph toward the efferent vessel. They are not an entry route for naive T cells."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphocyte homing","High endothelial venules"],"comps":["IM-8","IM-2"]}},{"id":"IM-C06-Q4","stem":"A biopsy of an enlarged cervical lymph node is performed. Which of the following abnormalities is most likely?","options":["Absent B cells in the cortex","Distended subcapsular sinus macrophages","Absent paracortical T cells","Follicles lacking germinal centers"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that germinal centers form inside cortical follicles when activated B cells receive T cell help.","That help depends on CD40 ligand binding CD40.","Without it, B cells fill primary follicles, but germinal centers do not form."],"why":"The lymph node cortex holds B cell follicles. After antigen exposure, B cells that receive help from follicular helper T cells form germinal centers, where class switching, somatic hypermutation, and memory B cell formation take place. CD40 ligand is needed for germinal center formation. In X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome the follicles contain B cells but lack germinal centers, while the paracortex has normal numbers of T cells.","whyNot":["His B cell numbers are normal, so the cortex has B cells. Absent cortical B cells fits X-linked agammaglobulinemia.","Sinus macrophage prominence (sinus histiocytosis) is a nonspecific reactive change, not the defining lesion of this disease.","His T cell numbers are normal, so the paracortex is populated. Paracortical depletion fits DiGeorge syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lymph node microanatomy","Germinal center formation"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-12"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C07","system":"IM","dx":"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","codes":["IM-12"],"case":"A 10-month-old boy is brought to the physician because of ear pain and small red spots on his skin. He has had an itchy rash since age 2 months and two episodes of otitis media. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 132/min, respirations are 32/min, and blood pressure is 90/58 mm Hg. Examination shows eczematous plaques on the cheeks and flexures, scattered petechiae, and a bulging right tympanic membrane. Platelet count is 32,000/mm3 (N = 150,000 to 450,000/mm3), and mean platelet volume is 5.6 fL (N = 7.5 to 11.5 fL). Serum IgM is 18 mg/dL (N = 40 to 150 mg/dL), IgA is 190 mg/dL (N = 15 to 90 mg/dL), IgE is 850 IU/mL (N less than 30 IU/mL), and IgG is normal. Genetic testing shows a mutation in the WAS gene. Diagnosis: Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C07-Q1","stem":"His mother is found to be a heterozygous carrier of the mutation, and his father is unaffected. They plan another pregnancy, and the sex of the fetus is not yet known. Which of the following is the probability that their next child will be affected?","options":["12.5%","25%","50%","75%"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-12","obj":"IM-12.3","dok":3,"approach":["Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is X-linked recessive.","A carrier mother passes the mutant X to half her children, and only sons who receive it are affected.","Probability of a son (1/2) times probability he gets the mutant X (1/2) gives 1/4."],"why":"The WAS gene is on the X chromosome, and the disease is X-linked recessive. Each child of a carrier mother has a 1 in 2 chance of inheriting the mutant X. A son who inherits it is affected. A daughter who inherits it is usually an unaffected carrier, because she also receives a normal X from her father. With the sex unknown, the risk is 1/2 (son) times 1/2 (mutant X), or 25%. For a known son, the risk is 50%.","whyNot":["Calculating 12.5% halves the risk one time too many. Only two independent events matter here: being male and inheriting the mutant X.","Correct.","50% is the risk for a son, not for a child of unknown sex.","75% is the chance that a child of two carrier parents gets at least one mutant allele in an autosomal recessive disease. It does not fit an X-linked disease with an unaffected father."],"study":{"topics":["X-linked recessive inheritance","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome"],"comps":["IM-12"]}},{"id":"IM-C07-Q2","stem":"He also has poor antibody responses to polysaccharide antigens. In the spleen, which of the following cells normally produce the rapid IgM response that helps clear encapsulated bacteria from the blood?","options":["Germinal center B cells","Marginal zone B cells","Red pulp cord macrophages","Periarteriolar T cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-8","obj":"IM-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Capsular polysaccharides are T-independent antigens.","In the spleen, the B cells that answer T-independent antigens quickly with IgM sit in the marginal zone.","Their IgM, with marginal zone macrophages, clears encapsulated bacteria from blood."],"why":"Blood flows through the splenic marginal zone, where macrophages and marginal zone B cells sample it. Marginal zone B cells respond rapidly to polysaccharide antigens and make IgM without much T cell help. IgM activates complement, and C3b-coated bacteria are removed by splenic macrophages. The spleen also removes aged red cells and inclusions and stores about a third of the platelets. Asplenia, or weak polysaccharide responses as in Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, raises the risk of sepsis from encapsulated bacteria.","whyNot":["Germinal center B cells give T-dependent, class-switched, high-affinity responses, which take days and need protein antigens.","Correct.","Red pulp macrophages remove aged red cells and opsonized particles but do not produce antibody.","The periarteriolar lymphoid sheath holds T cells, which cannot recognize polysaccharides and do not make antibody."],"study":{"topics":["Splenic functions","Marginal zone B cells"],"comps":["IM-8","IM-2"]}},{"id":"IM-C07-Q3","stem":"Within hours after bacteria enter his middle ear, and before any antibody is made, neutrophils and macrophages recognize the bacteria. The receptors these cells use for this recognition have which of the following properties?","options":["Germline-encoded and pattern-specific","Generated by somatic gene rearrangement","Clonally distributed on individual cells","Improved affinity on repeated exposure"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-13","obj":"IM-13.1","dok":2,"approach":["Hours after exposure, before antibody, points to innate immunity.","Innate cells use pattern recognition receptors such as Toll-like receptors.","These receptors are inherited unchanged in the germline and recognize molecules shared by many microbes."],"why":"Innate immunity acts within minutes to hours. Its cells, such as neutrophils, macrophages, and NK cells, along with complement, use pattern recognition receptors coded in the germline. Every cell of a type carries the same receptors, which recognize conserved microbial molecules such as LPS or flagellin, and there is no memory. Adaptive immunity uses T and B cell receptors made by gene rearrangement. Each clone has its own specificity, responses take days, and repeat exposure brings faster, stronger responses through memory.","whyNot":["Correct.","Somatic gene rearrangement creates T cell and B cell receptors, which belong to adaptive immunity.","Clonal distribution, with each cell carrying a unique receptor, is a feature of lymphocytes.","Rising affinity on repeat exposure comes from somatic hypermutation in B cells, an adaptive feature."],"study":{"topics":["Innate versus adaptive immunity","Pattern recognition receptors"],"comps":["IM-13","IM-6"]}},{"id":"IM-C07-Q4","stem":"Many of his small platelets are destroyed in the spleen. In the open circulation of the red pulp, blood from arterial capillaries passes through which of the following before it enters the venous sinusoids?","options":["Periarteriolar lymphoid sheath","Germinal centers of follicles","Splenic cords of Billroth","Trabecular veins"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Trace splenic blood flow: trabecular artery, then central arteriole in the white pulp, then penicillar arterioles into the red pulp.","In the open circulation, blood empties into the cords of Billroth.","Cells must squeeze through slits in sinusoid walls to re-enter the circulation, and macrophages in the cords remove damaged cells and platelets."],"why":"The white pulp is lymphoid tissue: the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath of T cells around central arterioles, B cell follicles, and the marginal zone. The red pulp has splenic cords and venous sinusoids. In the open circulation, blood leaves arterial capillaries into the cords of Billroth, then squeezes through narrow slits between sinusoid lining cells. Macrophages in the cords remove old or abnormal cells and antibody-coated platelets. Sinusoids drain to pulp veins, trabecular veins, and the splenic vein.","whyNot":["The periarteriolar lymphoid sheath surrounds the central arteriole in the white pulp, upstream of the red pulp.","Germinal centers lie in white pulp follicles and are not part of the red pulp blood path.","Correct.","Trabecular veins receive blood after it has passed through the sinusoids and pulp veins."],"study":{"topics":["Splenic histology","Splenic blood flow"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-8"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C08","system":"IM","dx":"Chronic granulomatous disease with Aspergillus pneumonia","codes":["IM-10"],"case":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of fever and cough for 3 weeks. At age 3 years he had a Staphylococcus aureus liver abscess, and he has had two episodes of suppurative cervical lymphadenitis that required drainage. Temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 30/min, and blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. Crackles are heard over the left upper lung. Leukocyte count is 14,800/mm3 (N = 4,500 to 13,500/mm3). CT of the chest shows multiple nodules. Lung biopsy shows granulomas and septate hyphae that branch at acute angles, and culture grows Aspergillus fumigatus. A dihydrorhodamine flow cytometry test shows no increase in neutrophil fluorescence after stimulation. Diagnosis: X-linked chronic granulomatous disease with Aspergillus pneumonia.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C08-Q1","stem":"Which of the following reactions is defective in this patient's neutrophils?","options":["Glucose 6-phosphate to 6-phosphogluconolactone","NADPH and oxygen to superoxide","Superoxide to hydrogen peroxide","Hydrogen peroxide and chloride to hypochlorous acid"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-10","obj":"IM-10.1","dok":2,"approach":["The dihydrorhodamine test measures whether stimulated neutrophils make reactive oxygen species.","No change in fluorescence means the respiratory burst never starts.","The first enzyme of the burst is NADPH oxidase, which turns oxygen into superoxide using NADPH."],"why":"The respiratory burst begins when NADPH oxidase in the phagosome membrane transfers electrons from NADPH to oxygen, making superoxide. Superoxide dismutase converts superoxide to hydrogen peroxide, and myeloperoxidase combines hydrogen peroxide with chloride to make hypochlorous acid (bleach). The pentose phosphate pathway supplies the NADPH. In chronic granulomatous disease, a defective NADPH oxidase subunit (most often the X-linked gp91phox) stops the burst at its first step, so the dihydrorhodamine test shows no oxidation.","whyNot":["G6PD makes NADPH in the pentose phosphate pathway. Severe G6PD deficiency can weaken the burst, but it presents mainly with hemolysis. Chronic granulomatous disease is a defect of NADPH oxidase itself.","Correct.","Superoxide dismutase converts superoxide to hydrogen peroxide. It is downstream of the defect and has no substrate here.","Myeloperoxidase makes hypochlorous acid. Its deficiency leaves superoxide production intact and is usually mild, with Candida as the main risk."],"study":{"topics":["Respiratory burst","Chronic granulomatous disease"],"comps":["IM-10","IM-6"]}},{"id":"IM-C08-Q2","stem":"Which of the following properties of an organism best predicts that it will cause serious infection in this patient?","options":["Polysaccharide capsule","Obligate intracellular growth","Lipooligosaccharide outer membrane","Production of catalase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-10","obj":"IM-10.2","dok":3,"approach":["His neutrophils cannot make their own hydrogen peroxide.","Many microbes make hydrogen peroxide as a by-product, which neutrophil myeloperoxidase can still use to kill them.","Catalase-positive organisms break down their own hydrogen peroxide, so the neutrophil has nothing to work with."],"why":"CGD neutrophils can still use hydrogen peroxide made by the microbe inside the phagosome. Catalase destroys this hydrogen peroxide, so catalase-positive organisms survive. Typical CGD pathogens are catalase-positive: Staphylococcus aureus, Aspergillus, Burkholderia cepacia, Serratia marcescens, and Nocardia. The diagnosis is made with an abnormal dihydrorhodamine flow cytometry test or, in older practice, a nitroblue tetrazolium test that fails to turn blue.","whyNot":["A polysaccharide capsule predicts infection in antibody, complement, or splenic defects, where opsonization fails.","Obligate intracellular organisms are controlled mainly by T cells and NK cells, which are normal in CGD.","Lipooligosaccharide is the outer membrane endotoxin of Neisseria and Haemophilus. Recurrent Neisseria infection points to terminal complement deficiency, not to a phagocyte killing defect.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Catalase-positive organisms in CGD","Dihydrorhodamine and nitroblue tetrazolium tests"],"comps":["IM-10","IM-15-A"]}},{"id":"IM-C08-Q3","stem":"A 4-year-old girl has partial albinism, peripheral neuropathy, recurrent pyogenic infections, and giant cytoplasmic granules in her neutrophils. Which of the following processes is most likely defective in her phagocytes?","options":["Phagolysosome fusion","NADPH oxidase activity","Integrin-mediated adhesion","C3b opsonin generation"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Giant granules, partial albinism, and neuropathy describe Chediak-Higashi syndrome.","Its LYST mutation disrupts lysosomal trafficking.","Lysosomes fuse into giant granules and fail to fuse with phagosomes, so ingested bacteria are not killed."],"why":"Phagocytosis starts when IgG (binding Fc gamma receptors) and C3b (binding complement receptors) coat a microbe. The phagocyte engulfs it into a phagosome, which then fuses with lysosomes to form a phagolysosome. In Chediak-Higashi syndrome, an autosomal recessive LYST mutation impairs lysosomal trafficking. Giant granules form in neutrophils, melanosomes are not delivered normally (partial albinism), nerves are affected, and phagolysosome fusion fails, so pyogenic infections recur.","whyNot":["Correct.","NADPH oxidase is the defect in chronic granulomatous disease. Neutrophil granules and pigmentation are normal there.","Integrin-mediated adhesion fails in leukocyte adhesion deficiency, which brings delayed umbilical cord separation and infections without pus.","C3b generation fails in C3 deficiency, which causes pyogenic infections without giant granules or albinism."],"study":{"topics":["Opsonization and phagocytosis","Chediak-Higashi syndrome"],"comps":["IM-6","IM-10"]}},{"id":"IM-C08-Q4","stem":"Two years earlier, a skin abscess on his thigh was drained and the wound was sutured. A firm nodule now present at the scar is excised. Microscopy shows macrophages, epithelioid cells, and multinucleated giant cells surrounding birefringent suture material, with few lymphocytes. Which of the following best distinguishes this lesion from an immune granuloma?","options":["It contains multinucleated giant cells","It forms around caseous necrosis","It lacks an antigen-specific Th1 response","It is built from activated epithelioid macrophages"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.5","dok":3,"approach":["Birefringent suture material with giant cells and few lymphocytes describes a foreign body granuloma.","Ask what drives each granuloma type.","Foreign body granulomas form because macrophages cannot digest inert material. Immune granulomas need T cells that recognize antigen and release IFN-gamma."],"why":"Both granuloma types are collections of activated macrophages, epithelioid cells, and often multinucleated giant cells. A foreign body granuloma forms around inert material too large to ingest, such as suture, talc, or a splinter. It needs no specific T cell response, and the material is often visible, sometimes birefringent under polarized light. An immune granuloma, as in tuberculosis or sarcoidosis, is driven by Th1 cells that recognize antigen and release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages and draws a cuff of lymphocytes.","whyNot":["Multinucleated giant cells appear in both immune and foreign body granulomas, so they do not tell them apart.","Caseous necrosis marks some immune granulomas, especially tuberculosis. It is not found in this lesion, and many immune granulomas lack it.","Correct.","Epithelioid macrophages make up both types of granuloma."],"study":{"topics":["Foreign body versus immune granuloma","Granulomatous inflammation"],"comps":["IM-6","IM-15-C4"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C09","system":"IM","dx":"Hereditary angioedema (C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency)","codes":["IM-7"],"case":"A 19-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of swelling of her lips and left hand and crampy abdominal pain with vomiting for 1 day. She has had similar episodes since age 12 years, often after dental work or minor injury. The swelling does not itch, and antihistamines have not helped. Her father has had similar episodes. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows nonpitting swelling of the lips and the back of the left hand without urticaria. There is no stridor. CT of the abdomen shows thickened, edematous loops of small bowel. Serum C1 esterase inhibitor concentration is 5 mg/dL (N = 21 to 39 mg/dL). Diagnosis: hereditary angioedema from C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C09-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mediators is most directly responsible for her swelling?","options":["Histamine","C3a","Platelet-activating factor","Bradykinin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-7","obj":"IM-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["C1 esterase inhibitor controls C1 of the classical pathway and also kallikrein and factor XIIa of the contact system.","Without it, kallikrein cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen and releases bradykinin.","Bradykinin raises vascular permeability without mast cell activation, so there is no itch or urticaria and antihistamines fail."],"why":"C1 esterase inhibitor is a serine protease inhibitor. It inactivates C1r and C1s of the classical complement pathway and kallikrein and factor XIIa of the contact system. In hereditary angioedema, an autosomal dominant deficiency of this inhibitor lets kallikrein generate excess bradykinin, which increases venular permeability. Swelling of the skin, gut, and airway follows without hives or itch. Other complement regulators matter elsewhere: DAF (CD55) and CD59 protect red cells, and their loss in PIGA-mutant paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria causes intravascular hemolysis.","whyNot":["Histamine from mast cells causes allergic angioedema with urticaria and itch, which respond to antihistamines.","C3a is an anaphylatoxin that can trigger mast cell release, but uncontrolled C1 mainly consumes C4 and C2 and does not explain bradykinin-type swelling without urticaria.","Platelet-activating factor is a mast cell and leukocyte lipid mediator in anaphylaxis, which brings urticaria and hypotension.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["C1 esterase inhibitor","Bradykinin-mediated angioedema"],"comps":["IM-7","IM-15-C1"]}},{"id":"IM-C09-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the accumulation of fluid in her tissues during an attack?","options":["Filtration from leaky venules outpacing lymph flow","Reduced plasma oncotic pressure from protein loss","Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous block","Obstruction of lymphatic vessels draining the area"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-9","obj":"IM-9.1","dok":3,"approach":["Normally, Starling forces cause a small net filtration from capillaries, and lymphatics return it to the blood.","Bradykinin opens gaps between venular endothelial cells, so protein and fluid leak out much faster.","When filtration exceeds the maximum lymph flow, fluid collects in the interstitium."],"why":"Fluid movement across capillaries depends on hydrostatic and oncotic pressures in the capillary and the interstitium. Normally, filtration slightly exceeds reabsorption, and lymphatics return the excess, about 2 to 3 L each day, to the blood. Bradykinin increases venular permeability, so protein leaks out. This lowers the oncotic gradient and raises filtration. When filtration outruns the lymphatics' capacity, edema forms, here in the lips, hand, and bowel wall.","whyNot":["Correct.","Low plasma oncotic pressure, as in nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis, causes generalized pitting edema that develops slowly, not episodic local swelling.","Raised hydrostatic pressure from venous obstruction, as in deep vein thrombosis, swells the drained limb but does not explain recurrent swelling in many sites.","Lymphatic obstruction, as after node dissection or in filariasis, causes persistent lymphedema, not attacks that resolve in days."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces and lymph formation","Mechanisms of edema"],"comps":["IM-9","IM-7"]}},{"id":"IM-C09-Q3","stem":"Serum complement studies are obtained during the attack. Which of the following patterns is most likely?","options":["Normal C3 and normal C4","Low C4 with normal C3","Low C3 and low C4","Low C3 with normal C4"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-7","obj":"IM-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["Without C1 esterase inhibitor, activated C1s keeps cleaving its substrates, C4 and C2.","C4 is consumed, but C3 is not, because the activation does not proceed far down the cascade.","Low C4 with normal C3 is the typical screening pattern for hereditary angioedema."],"why":"Complement levels point to where activation is happening. Unregulated C1 in hereditary angioedema cleaves C4 and C2, so C4 is low, while C3 stays normal. In active SLE, immune complexes drive the classical pathway all the way, so both C3 and C4 fall. In poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, activation is mainly through the alternative pathway, so C3 is low and C4 is normal.","whyNot":["Normal C3 and C4 would be unusual in hereditary angioedema. C4 is low in most patients even between attacks.","Correct.","Low C3 and C4 together suggest classical pathway consumption by immune complexes, as in active SLE.","Low C3 with normal C4 suggests alternative pathway activation, as in poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis."],"study":{"topics":["Interpreting complement levels","Hereditary angioedema"],"comps":["IM-7","IM-15-C3"]}},{"id":"IM-C09-Q4","stem":"Before diagnosis, her episodes were treated as allergic angioedema. In IgE-mediated angioedema, mast cells synthesize lipid mediators from arachidonic acid. Which of the following mediators both increases venular permeability and constricts bronchial smooth muscle?","options":["Leukotriene B4","Leukotriene D4","Prostaglandin E2","Thromboxane A2"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-10","obj":"IM-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["Split arachidonic acid products by enzyme: cyclooxygenase makes prostaglandins and thromboxane, and 5-lipoxygenase makes leukotrienes.","Among leukotrienes, LTB4 attracts neutrophils, while LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4 constrict airways and increase permeability.","So the mediator with both actions is LTD4."],"why":"Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids. Cyclooxygenase converts it to prostaglandins and thromboxane. PGE2 causes fever, pain, and vasodilation, and thromboxane A2 causes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction. 5-Lipoxygenase makes LTB4, a strong neutrophil chemoattractant, and the cysteinyl leukotrienes LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4. These cysteinyl leukotrienes increase venular permeability and cause sustained bronchoconstriction in allergic reactions and asthma.","whyNot":["LTB4 is a 5-lipoxygenase product whose main action is neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion.","Correct.","PGE2 causes vasodilation, fever, and pain, and it relaxes rather than constricts airway smooth muscle.","Thromboxane A2 mainly aggregates platelets and constricts vessels. It does not increase venular permeability."],"study":{"topics":["Arachidonic acid metabolites","Leukotrienes"],"comps":["IM-10","IM-15-C1"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C10","system":"IM","dx":"Meningococcal sepsis with terminal complement (C7) deficiency","codes":["IM-7","IM-6"],"case":"A 20-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 8 hours after the onset of fever, headache, and a spreading rash. He had meningococcal meningitis at age 12 years. Temperature is 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), pulse is 132/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 78/40 mm Hg. He is lethargic and has neck stiffness. There are nonblanching purpuric lesions on the legs and trunk. Leukocyte count is 22,000/mm3 (N = 4,500 to 11,000/mm3), and platelet count is 60,000/mm3 (N = 150,000 to 450,000/mm3). Prothrombin time is prolonged. Blood cultures grow gram-negative diplococci identified as Neisseria meningitidis. After recovery, total hemolytic complement (CH50) is undetectable, C3 and C4 are normal, and C7 is absent. Diagnosis: meningococcal sepsis with terminal complement (C7) deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C10-Q1","stem":"Another patient, a 7-year-old girl, has had recurrent pneumococcal pneumonia and bacteremia since infancy, and a kidney biopsy shows immune complex glomerulonephritis. Total hemolytic complement (CH50) and alternative pathway hemolytic activity (AH50) are both undetectable. Deficiency of which of the following complement proteins most likely explains her findings?","options":["C1q","C7","C3","C1 esterase inhibitor"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-7","obj":"IM-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["CH50 and AH50 both absent means the missing protein is shared by the classical and alternative pathways: C3 or a terminal component (C5 to C9).","Severe pyogenic infections mean opsonization is failing, which a terminal component defect would not cause.","C3 is needed for both C3b opsonization and immune complex clearance, so its deficiency explains both findings."],"why":"Each complement deficiency has a typical pattern. C3 deficiency removes the main opsonin and the central step of all three pathways, giving severe infections with encapsulated bacteria and immune complex glomerulonephritis. Deficiency of C1q, C2, or C4 mainly causes SLE-like immune complex disease. Deficiency of C5 to C9 causes recurrent Neisseria infections, as in the boy in this case. Mannose-binding lectin deficiency increases infections in early childhood.","whyNot":["C1q deficiency mainly causes SLE-like disease. It blocks only the classical pathway, so AH50 would be normal, and C3b can still form through the alternative and lectin pathways.","C7 deficiency also makes CH50 and AH50 undetectable, but C3b opsonization is intact, so it causes recurrent Neisseria infection rather than pneumococcal disease and glomerulonephritis.","Correct.","C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency causes hereditary angioedema with low C4. It does not abolish CH50 and AH50 or cause pyogenic infection."],"study":{"topics":["Complement deficiency patterns","C3 deficiency"],"comps":["IM-7","IM-15-A"]}},{"id":"IM-C10-Q2","stem":"Which of the following complement functions is absent in this patient?","options":["C5a neutrophil chemotaxis","C3a mast cell activation","C5b-9 pore formation","C3b opsonization"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-7","obj":"IM-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["C7 is part of the terminal pathway, after C5 is cleaved.","C5a and C3b are made before C7 acts, so they are still available.","The step lost is assembly of the membrane attack complex (C5b-9), which lyses Neisseria."],"why":"Complement has several effector functions. C3b coats microbes for phagocytosis and helps clear immune complexes. C3a and C5a are anaphylatoxins that trigger mast cell release, and C5a also draws neutrophils. C5b binds C6, C7, C8, and many C9 molecules to form the membrane attack complex, a pore that lyses cells. Neisseria are especially vulnerable to this lysis. In C7 deficiency, cleavage up through C5 is intact, but the pore never forms, so Neisseria infections recur.","whyNot":["C5a is released when C5 is cleaved, before C7 is needed, so neutrophil chemotaxis is preserved.","C3a is produced early in the cascade and is unaffected by loss of C7.","Correct.","C3b is made upstream of C7, so opsonization still works. That is why he is not prone to all encapsulated bacteria."],"study":{"topics":["Complement effector functions","Membrane attack complex"],"comps":["IM-7"]}},{"id":"IM-C10-Q3","stem":"Which of the following interactions most directly starts the cytokine release responsible for his hypotension and purpura?","options":["Lipooligosaccharide binding TLR4","Peptidoglycan binding TLR2","Flagellin binding TLR5","Unmethylated CpG DNA binding TLR9"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["He meets SIRS criteria: temperature above 38 °C (100.4 °F), pulse above 90/min, respirations above 20/min, and leukocytes above 12,000/mm3.","The organism is gram-negative, and its endotoxin in Neisseria is lipooligosaccharide.","Endotoxin binds TLR4 (with CD14 and MD-2) on macrophages, which release TNF, IL-1, and IL-6."],"why":"Endotoxin is the lipid A-containing glycolipid of the gram-negative outer membrane. In Neisseria it takes the form of lipooligosaccharide. With CD14 and MD-2, it binds TLR4 on monocytes and macrophages. NF-kB signaling then drives release of TNF, IL-1, and IL-6. These cytokines cause fever, vasodilation, capillary leak, and tissue factor expression, leading to hypotension, DIC with purpura, and organ dysfunction. SIRS criteria include temperature above 38 °C (100.4 °F) or below 36 °C (96.8 °F), pulse above 90/min, respirations above 20/min, and leukocytes above 12,000/mm3 or below 4,000/mm3.","whyNot":["Correct.","Peptidoglycan and lipoteichoic acid signal through TLR2 and are the main triggers in gram-positive sepsis.","Flagellin activates TLR5, but Neisseria are not flagellated.","Unmethylated CpG DNA activates TLR9 inside endosomes. It is not the main driver of gram-negative septic shock."],"study":{"topics":["Endotoxin and TLR4 signaling","SIRS criteria and septic shock"],"comps":["IM-6","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C10-Q4","stem":"TNF and IL-1 released during his infection increase expression of ICAM-1 on endothelial cells. Neutrophils bind ICAM-1 for firm adhesion through which of the following molecules?","options":["L-selectin","PECAM-1","Sialyl Lewis X","LFA-1 integrin"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Order the steps of extravasation: rolling, firm adhesion, diapedesis, then chemotaxis.","Rolling uses selectins and sialyl Lewis X. Firm adhesion uses neutrophil beta-2 integrins binding endothelial ICAM-1.","The beta-2 integrin LFA-1 (CD11a/CD18) binds ICAM-1."],"why":"Neutrophils leave blood in a fixed order. They first roll along the endothelium as E-selectin and P-selectin bind sialyl Lewis X. TNF and IL-1 increase endothelial ICAM-1, and chemokines such as IL-8 activate neutrophil beta-2 integrins (LFA-1 and Mac-1, which share CD18). These integrins bind ICAM-1 and stop the cell. PECAM-1 then guides diapedesis between endothelial cells, and the neutrophil follows C5a, LTB4, and IL-8 to the infection. CD18 deficiency causes leukocyte adhesion deficiency.","whyNot":["L-selectin on leukocytes helps with rolling and lymphocyte homing, not firm adhesion to ICAM-1.","PECAM-1 (CD31) works in diapedesis, the step after firm adhesion.","Sialyl Lewis X is the ligand that binds endothelial selectins during rolling.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Leukocyte extravasation","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency"],"comps":["IM-6"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C11","system":"IM","dx":"AIDS presenting with Pneumocystis pneumonia and Kaposi sarcoma","codes":["IM-15-B2"],"case":"A 34-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 3-week history of progressive shortness of breath, dry cough, and fever. He has lost 7 kg (15 lb) over 4 months and has never been tested for HIV. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse 112/min, respirations 28/min, and blood pressure 108/66 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 87% on room air. Violaceous plaques are present on both shins and on the hard palate. Chest x-ray shows bilateral diffuse interstitial infiltrates. An HIV-1/2 antigen and antibody immunoassay is positive. CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 62/mm3 (normal 500 to 1500), and plasma HIV-1 RNA is 280,000 copies/mL. Methenamine silver stain of bronchoalveolar lavage fluid shows cup-shaped cysts. Diagnosis: AIDS with Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia and Kaposi sarcoma.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C11-Q1","stem":"Biopsy of a palatal plaque shows spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces that contain extravasated erythrocytes. Which of the following best explains the development of this lesion?","options":["EBV infection of proliferating B lymphocytes","HHV-8 infection of endothelial-lineage cells","Bartonella henselae stimulation of capillary growth","HPV infection of squamous epithelial cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-B2","obj":"IM-15-B.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize spindle cells with slit-like vascular spaces and red cell extravasation as Kaposi sarcoma.","Recall that Kaposi sarcoma is a vascular tumor driven by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8).","Link its appearance to loss of T cell control of the virus in AIDS."],"why":"Kaposi sarcoma is a tumor of endothelial-lineage (lymphatic-type) spindle cells infected with human herpesvirus 8, also called Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus. Viral proteins push the infected cells to divide and form leaky, slit-like vessels, which is why the lesions are purple and contain extravasated red cells. In a healthy host, cytotoxic T cells keep HHV-8 latent. When the CD4 count falls in AIDS, that control is lost and the tumor appears in skin, the mouth, the gut, and the lungs.","whyNot":["EBV drives B cell proliferation and is linked to primary CNS lymphoma and oral hairy leukoplakia in AIDS. It does not cause a spindle cell vascular tumor.","Correct.","Bartonella henselae causes bacillary angiomatosis, which also forms red-purple skin lesions in AIDS. Its biopsy shows plump endothelial cells with neutrophils and clumps of bacteria on silver stain, and it responds to antibiotics.","High-risk HPV transforms squamous epithelium and causes anal and cervical carcinoma in people with HIV. It does not form vascular spindle cell lesions."],"study":{"topics":["Kaposi sarcoma and HHV-8","Bacillary angiomatosis versus Kaposi sarcoma"],"comps":["IM-15-B2","IM-15-B"]}},{"id":"IM-C11-Q2","stem":"He recalls an illness 9 years ago, about 3 weeks after unprotected intercourse, with fever, sore throat, rash, and swollen lymph nodes that resolved within 2 weeks. He then felt well until this year. Which of the following best describes HIV activity during the years without symptoms?","options":["Ongoing replication in lymph nodes with CD4 loss","Silent integrated provirus with no virion production","Replication limited to genital mucosal epithelium","Viral clearance from blood with persistence in brain"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-B2","obj":"IM-15-B.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the remote illness as acute retroviral syndrome, the first stage of HIV infection.","Recall that the next stage, clinical latency, has no symptoms but is not virologically quiet.","Pick the option describing continuous replication in lymphoid tissue with slow CD4 decline."],"why":"After acute infection, HIV enters a period of clinical latency that lasts about 8 to 10 years on average without treatment. The patient feels well, but billions of virions are made each day, mainly in lymph nodes and gut-associated lymphoid tissue, and CD4+ T cells are destroyed and replaced. Over the years the losses outpace replacement, the CD4 count falls, and opportunistic infections appear once it drops below 200/mm3. That threshold, or any AIDS-defining illness, defines AIDS.","whyNot":["Correct.","A latent provirus does exist in some resting memory T cells, and it is the reason HIV cannot be cured. Most infected cells, however, are actively making virus throughout the asymptomatic years.","Genital mucosa is a common entry site. Infected dendritic cells carry the virus to lymph nodes within days, so replication is not confined to the mucosa.","Plasma viral load falls after acute infection to a set point, but the virus stays detectable in blood during latency. The brain is one reservoir, not the only site of virus."],"study":{"topics":["Natural history of HIV infection","Acute retroviral syndrome and clinical latency"],"comps":["IM-15-B2"]}},{"id":"IM-C11-Q3","stem":"His partner tests positive for HIV. She gave birth to their daughter 6 weeks ago. Which of the following is the most appropriate test to determine whether the infant is infected?","options":["CD4+ T-lymphocyte count and percentage","HIV-1/2 antigen and antibody combination immunoassay","HIV-1/2 antibody differentiation immunoassay","HIV-1 RNA or DNA nucleic acid amplification test"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-15-B2","obj":"IM-15-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note that the mother is infected, so the infant received maternal IgG across the placenta.","Recall that maternal antibody can persist up to about 18 months and makes antibody-based tests positive in uninfected infants.","Choose a test that detects the virus itself: an HIV nucleic acid test."],"why":"Maternal IgG crosses the placenta, so every infant born to a mother with HIV has anti-HIV antibodies at birth, infected or not. These antibodies can last up to about 18 months. Infants are therefore tested with a nucleic acid test that detects HIV RNA or proviral DNA, usually repeated at set ages in the first months. In adults, the antigen and antibody immunoassay is the screening test, a differentiation assay confirms HIV-1 versus HIV-2, the viral load measures replication, and the CD4 count stages disease.","whyNot":["The CD4 count stages immune damage in a person known to be infected. It cannot diagnose infection, and infant CD4 counts are normally much higher than adult values.","This is the standard first screening test in adults and older children. In a young infant, its antibody component detects maternal IgG and gives a positive result even when the infant is uninfected.","This test confirms a reactive screen in adults and tells HIV-1 from HIV-2. It also detects antibody and is misleading in infants who carry maternal IgG.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["HIV testing algorithm","HIV diagnosis in infants"],"comps":["IM-15-B2","IM-15-B"]}},{"id":"IM-C11-Q4","stem":"HIV proteins are synthesized in the cytosol of infected CD4+ T cells. Cytotoxic CD8+ T cells recognize peptides from these proteins presented on which of the following molecules?","options":["HLA-B heavy chain with beta2-microglobulin","HLA-DR alpha chain paired with a beta chain","HLA-DM alpha chain paired with a beta chain","HLA-DQ alpha chain paired with a beta chain"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-3","obj":"IM-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the antigen as endogenous: viral protein made in the cytosol of the infected cell.","Recall that cytosolic peptides are loaded onto MHC class I in the endoplasmic reticulum and presented to CD8+ T cells.","Pick the class I molecule: HLA-A, -B, or -C heavy chain with beta2-microglobulin."],"why":"Proteins made inside a cell, such as viral proteins, are cut by the proteasome, pumped into the endoplasmic reticulum by TAP, and loaded onto MHC class I. Class I is an HLA-A, -B, or -C heavy chain paired with beta2-microglobulin, and it is found on all nucleated cells. CD8 binds class I, so cytotoxic T cells can find and kill any infected cell. MHC class II (HLA-DP, -DQ, -DR) has an alpha and a beta chain, is found mainly on antigen-presenting cells, and presents peptides from engulfed proteins to CD4+ T cells.","whyNot":["Correct.","HLA-DR is a class II molecule. It presents peptides from endocytosed proteins to CD4+ helper T cells, which do not carry CD8.","HLA-DM is a class II-like molecule inside endosomes that removes the invariant chain fragment (CLIP) so class II can load peptide. It does not present peptide on the cell surface.","HLA-DQ is also class II and presents to CD4+ T cells. It is best known for its links to celiac disease (DQ2, DQ8)."],"study":{"topics":["MHC class I versus class II","Antigen processing pathways"],"comps":["IM-3","IM-13"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C12","system":"IM","dx":"Toxoplasma encephalitis in advanced AIDS","codes":["IM-15-B2","IM-15-F10"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by her brother because of a 10-day history of headache and increasing confusion. Two days ago she developed weakness of her right arm and leg. She was diagnosed with HIV infection 6 years ago and stopped antiretroviral therapy 2 years ago. Serum Toxoplasma gondii IgG was positive when HIV was diagnosed. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse 96/min, respirations 16/min, and blood pressure 124/78 mm Hg. She is oriented to person only, and there is right hemiparesis. CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 74/mm3 (normal 500 to 1500). Contrast-enhanced MRI of the brain shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions with surrounding edema in the basal ganglia and at the gray-white matter junction. Diagnosis: Toxoplasma encephalitis.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C12-Q1","stem":"Her CD4+ T-lymphocyte count was 340/mm3 two years ago, just before she stopped antiretroviral therapy. At that count, which of the following opportunistic conditions was she most at risk of developing?","options":["Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","Oral hairy leukoplakia","Cytomegalovirus retinitis","Disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-B2","obj":"IM-15-B.5","dok":2,"approach":["Place 340/mm3 on the CD4 scale: below normal but above 200.","Recall the thresholds: Pneumocystis below 200, Toxoplasma below 100, CMV retinitis and MAC below 50.","Choose the condition that appears early, while the count is still above 200."],"why":"Opportunistic infections in HIV follow the CD4 count closely. Conditions such as oral thrush, oral hairy leukoplakia (EBV), herpes zoster, and Kaposi sarcoma can appear while the count is still above 200/mm3. Pneumocystis pneumonia becomes common below 200, Toxoplasma encephalitis below 100, and CMV retinitis and disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex below 50. Her current count of 74/mm3 fits the toxoplasmosis she has now.","whyNot":["Pneumocystis pneumonia occurs mainly once the CD4 count falls below 200/mm3, which is why prophylaxis starts at that level.","Correct.","CMV retinitis is a late complication that appears when the CD4 count falls below 50/mm3.","Disseminated MAC occurs when the CD4 count is below 50/mm3, far lower than 340."],"study":{"topics":["Opportunistic infections by CD4 count"],"comps":["IM-15-B2","IM-15-B"]}},{"id":"IM-C12-Q2","stem":"Her 26-year-old roommate, who has a normal immune system, acquires a primary Toxoplasma gondii infection during the same month. If the roommate develops symptoms, which of the following is most likely?","options":["Fever with painless cervical lymphadenopathy","Multiple ring-enhancing brain lesions","Hydrocephalus with diffuse brain calcifications","Bilateral diffuse interstitial pneumonitis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-F10","obj":"IM-15-F.11","dok":2,"approach":["Note the host: an immunocompetent adult with a new infection.","Recall that T cell immunity quickly turns tachyzoites into dormant tissue cysts, so most primary infections are silent.","Pick the usual symptomatic form in healthy adults: a mononucleosis-like illness with lymphadenopathy."],"why":"Toxoplasma presentations depend on the host. Most healthy adults have no symptoms, and those who do usually have fever, fatigue, and painless cervical lymphadenopathy that looks like mononucleosis but is heterophile negative. Infection acquired in pregnancy can cross the placenta and cause chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and diffuse intracranial calcifications in the fetus. In AIDS with a CD4 count below 100/mm3, latent cysts reactivate and form multiple ring-enhancing brain lesions, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Correct.","Multiple ring-enhancing lesions come from reactivated infection in severe T cell deficiency, as in this patient. A healthy host contains the parasite.","Hydrocephalus with diffuse calcifications is a sign of congenital toxoplasmosis, acquired across the placenta during fetal life.","Toxoplasma pneumonitis is rare and occurs in severely immunocompromised hosts. It is not an expected form in a healthy adult."],"study":{"topics":["Toxoplasmosis by host type","Congenital TORCH infections"],"comps":["IM-15-F10","IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C12-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains the source of the parasites now causing her brain lesions?","options":["Sexual reproduction of the parasite within her brain","Recent ingestion of oocysts shed in cat feces","Conversion of dormant bradyzoites in tissue cysts","Spread to the brain from an intestinal sexual stage"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.7","dok":3,"approach":["Note that she was Toxoplasma IgG positive years ago, so she already carried the parasite.","Recall that after primary infection, the parasite persists in brain and muscle as tissue cysts full of slowly dividing bradyzoites.","With CD4 loss, bradyzoites convert to rapidly dividing tachyzoites that destroy brain tissue."],"why":"Toxoplasma gondii reproduces sexually only in the cat intestine, the definitive host, which sheds oocysts in its feces. Humans become infected by swallowing oocysts from soil or litter or tissue cysts in undercooked meat. In the human host the parasite spreads as tachyzoites and then, under T cell pressure, settles into tissue cysts that hold bradyzoites for life. In AIDS, loss of IFN-gamma-producing T cells lets bradyzoites convert back to tachyzoites, so most cases of Toxoplasma encephalitis are reactivations in people who were already seropositive.","whyNot":["The sexual stage of Toxoplasma occurs only in the intestinal epithelium of cats. Humans are intermediate hosts and carry only asexual stages.","Ingested oocysts cause a new infection. She was already seropositive years earlier, so reactivation of an old infection is far more likely.","Correct.","There is no intestinal sexual stage in humans. Only cats support the sexual cycle."],"study":{"topics":["Toxoplasma gondii life cycle","Reactivation of latent infection in AIDS"],"comps":["IM-14","IM-15-F10"]}},{"id":"IM-C12-Q4","stem":"Her 38-year-old brother has had repeated sexual exposure to partners with HIV but remains uninfected. Genetic testing shows he is homozygous for a 32-base-pair deletion in the CCR5 gene. Which of the following viral proteins normally binds the host molecule that he lacks?","options":["gp41 transmembrane protein","gp120 envelope protein","p24 capsid protein","p17 matrix protein"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-15-B2","obj":"IM-15-B.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that the CCR5 delta32 deletion prevents CCR5 from reaching the cell surface.","Recall the entry sequence: gp120 binds CD4, changes shape, and then binds a coreceptor (CCR5 or CXCR4).","Name the viral protein that binds the coreceptor: gp120."],"why":"HIV entry starts when the envelope glycoprotein gp120 binds CD4. This shifts gp120 so it can bind a chemokine coreceptor, CCR5 on macrophages and memory T cells or CXCR4 on T cells. Coreceptor binding lets gp41 insert into the host membrane and fuse the viral and cell membranes. Most transmitted strains use CCR5, so people homozygous for the delta32 deletion, who have no CCR5 on their cells, strongly resist infection. Rare CXCR4-using strains can still infect them.","whyNot":["gp41 carries out membrane fusion after gp120 has bound CD4 and the coreceptor. It does not bind CCR5 itself.","Correct.","p24 is the capsid protein encoded by gag. It is detected by fourth-generation screening tests and plays no part in receptor binding.","p17 is the gag-encoded matrix protein that lines the inside of the envelope. It does not bind host receptors."],"study":{"topics":["HIV structure and genes","HIV entry and coreceptors"],"comps":["IM-15-B2"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C13","system":"IM","dx":"Reactivated pulmonary tuberculosis during TNF-alpha inhibitor and prednisone therapy for plaque psoriasis","codes":["IM-15-B1","IM-15-C4"],"case":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-week history of cough, night sweats, and a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss. He has severe plaque psoriasis. Five months ago he began adalimumab, a monoclonal antibody against TNF-alpha, and he has taken prednisone 20 mg daily for the past 2 months for a flare. A tuberculin skin test done for employment 10 years ago showed 14 mm of induration, and he did not complete treatment for latent infection. Temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), pulse 98/min, respirations 20/min, and blood pressure 128/80 mm Hg. Chest x-ray shows a cavitary lesion in the right upper lobe. Sputum smear shows acid-fast bacilli, and culture grows Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Diagnosis: reactivation pulmonary tuberculosis during TNF-alpha inhibitor and glucocorticoid therapy.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C13-Q1","stem":"His tuberculin skin test 10 years ago was read 48 to 72 hours after intradermal injection of purified protein derivative. Which of the following best explains the induration?","options":["IgE-coated mast cells releasing histamine","Immune complexes fixing complement in venules","Antibody-dependent lysis of antigen-coated cells","Th1 cells recruiting and activating macrophages"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C4","obj":"IM-15-C.11","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing: induration that peaks 48 to 72 hours after injection.","A delay of days points to a type IV (delayed-type) reaction carried out by memory T cells.","Memory Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which recruits and activates macrophages, producing firm induration."],"why":"The tuberculin test injects mycobacterial proteins into the skin. In a person previously sensitized, memory CD4+ Th1 cells recognize the antigen, release IFN-gamma and TNF, and recruit monocytes and macrophages, which takes 2 to 3 days. The result is a firm, raised area measured as induration, not redness. The same Th1 and macrophage response walls off M tuberculosis in caseating granulomas, where central necrosis is surrounded by epithelioid macrophages and Langhans giant cells. Sarcoidosis, by contrast, forms noncaseating granulomas.","whyNot":["Histamine release from IgE-coated mast cells is type I hypersensitivity. It produces a wheal and flare within minutes and fades within hours.","Local immune complex deposition with complement is the Arthus reaction, a type III reaction. It peaks at about 4 to 10 hours and causes hemorrhagic necrosis, not delayed induration.","Antibody-dependent cell lysis is a type II mechanism directed at cell surface antigens. Tuberculin is a soluble protein injected into the dermis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tuberculin skin test","Caseating versus noncaseating granulomas"],"comps":["IM-15-C4","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C13-Q2","stem":"Which of the following effects of his therapy most directly allowed the latent infection to reactivate?","options":["Inhibition of calcineurin-dependent IL-2 transcription","Depletion of CD20-positive B cells","Loss of TNF-dependent granuloma integrity","Suppression of neutrophil production in the marrow"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-B1","obj":"IM-15-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note his drugs: a TNF-alpha inhibitor plus a glucocorticoid.","Recall that latent M tuberculosis is held inside granulomas, and TNF keeps those granulomas organized.","Blocking TNF lets the granuloma break down and the bacilli multiply."],"why":"In latent tuberculosis, bacilli survive inside granulomas built and maintained by Th1 cells and activated macrophages. TNF-alpha recruits cells, keeps macrophages activated, and holds the granuloma together. Blocking TNF with drugs such as adalimumab or infliximab lets granulomas loosen and the bacilli escape, which is why latent tuberculosis must be screened for and treated before these drugs are started. Prednisone adds to the risk by suppressing T cell and macrophage function.","whyNot":["Calcineurin inhibitors such as cyclosporine and tacrolimus block IL-2 transcription in T cells and are linked to Pneumocystis and viral infections. He is not taking one.","CD20 depletion is the action of rituximab. It removes B cells and is linked to hepatitis B reactivation, and B cells are not the main defense against M tuberculosis.","Correct.","Glucocorticoids raise the neutrophil count, and TNF inhibitors rarely cause neutropenia. Neutropenia also predisposes to bacterial and fungal sepsis more than to tuberculosis."],"study":{"topics":["Drug-induced immunodeficiency","TNF-alpha and granuloma maintenance"],"comps":["IM-15-B1","IM-15-C4"]}},{"id":"IM-C13-Q3","stem":"Infected macrophages and dendritic cells secrete a cytokine that drives naive CD4+ T cells toward the helper subset needed to control M tuberculosis. Which of the following is this cytokine?","options":["Interleukin-4","Interleukin-10","Transforming growth factor-beta","Interleukin-12"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the helper subset that controls intracellular mycobacteria: Th1, which activates macrophages with IFN-gamma.","Recall which cytokine from antigen-presenting cells induces Th1 differentiation.","Answer IL-12."],"why":"Each helper T cell subset is induced by a different cytokine signal. IL-12 from infected macrophages and dendritic cells drives Th1 cells, which make IFN-gamma to activate macrophages so they can kill intracellular bacteria such as M tuberculosis. IL-4 drives Th2 cells (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13; IgE and eosinophils), IL-6 with TGF-beta drives Th17 cells (IL-17; neutrophils), and TGF-beta with IL-2 drives FOXP3-positive regulatory T cells. Inherited defects in the IL-12 or IFN-gamma receptor cause severe mycobacterial infections.","whyNot":["IL-4 drives Th2 differentiation, which supports IgE class switching and eosinophils against helminths. It suppresses the Th1 response needed here.","IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine made by regulatory T cells and macrophages. It dampens macrophage activation rather than inducing Th1 cells.","TGF-beta induces regulatory T cells, or Th17 cells when IL-6 is also present. Neither subset is the main defense against M tuberculosis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["T helper subsets and their inducing cytokines"],"comps":["IM-5","IM-15-C4"]}},{"id":"IM-C13-Q4","stem":"A complete blood count is obtained. Which of the following effects of his daily prednisone on the leukocyte differential is most likely?","options":["Neutropenia from marrow suppression, with lymphocytosis","Eosinophilia from IL-5 release, with neutropenia","Neutrophilia from demargination, with lymphopenia","Lymphocytosis from thymic release, with eosinopenia"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-8","obj":"IM-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the glucocorticoid pattern on a blood count: neutrophils up; lymphocytes, eosinophils, and monocytes down.","Name the cause of the neutrophilia: cells that were stuck to vessel walls enter the circulating pool.","Choose the option that pairs demargination with lymphopenia."],"why":"High cortisol, from stress, Cushing syndrome, or drugs such as prednisone, changes the blood count within hours. Neutrophils rise mainly because they detach from vessel walls (demargination), with added release from the marrow and slower exit into tissues. Lymphocytes and eosinophils fall because they are redistributed to lymphoid tissue and marrow and undergo apoptosis. Over time, high cortisol also shrinks the thymus. These changes are one reason glucocorticoids weaken T cell defense against intracellular organisms.","whyNot":["Glucocorticoids raise the neutrophil count and lower the lymphocyte count, the reverse of this pattern.","Glucocorticoids cause eosinopenia. That is why they are used in eosinophilic disorders and why low eosinophils are a clue to cortisol excess.","Correct.","Glucocorticoids cause lymphopenia and thymic atrophy. They do not release T cells from the thymus."],"study":{"topics":["Glucocorticoid effects on the blood count"],"comps":["IM-8","IM-15-B1"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C14","system":"IM","dx":"Acute cellular rejection of a deceased-donor kidney transplant with CMV viremia","codes":["IM-15-C4"],"case":"A 47-year-old woman comes to the transplant clinic because of fatigue and low-grade fever for 5 days. Seven weeks ago she received a deceased-donor kidney transplant for end-stage kidney disease from autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. She takes tacrolimus, mycophenolate mofetil, and prednisone. The donor was cytomegalovirus (CMV) seropositive, and she was seronegative. Temperature is 38.0 °C (100.4 °F), pulse 92/min, respirations 16/min, and blood pressure 146/90 mm Hg. The graft in the right iliac fossa is mildly tender. Serum creatinine is 2.4 mg/dL (normal 0.6 to 1.1), up from 1.2 mg/dL 2 weeks ago. Plasma CMV DNA is 45,000 IU/mL. Allograft biopsy shows a dense interstitial lymphocytic infiltrate with lymphocytes invading tubular epithelium; C4d staining of peritubular capillaries is negative. Diagnosis: acute T cell-mediated rejection with CMV viremia.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C14-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism of the biopsy findings?","options":["Recipient T cells responding to donor HLA","Preformed recipient antibodies to donor HLA","Donor T cells responding to recipient HLA","Gradual intimal fibrosis of graft arteries"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C4","obj":"IM-15-C.13","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing (7 weeks after transplant) and the biopsy: lymphocytes in the interstitium and tubules, C4d negative.","Weeks to months after transplant with tubulitis means acute cellular rejection.","In acute cellular rejection, the recipient's CD8+ and CD4+ T cells attack donor HLA on graft cells."],"why":"Rejection is classified by timing and mechanism. Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes, from preformed antibodies against donor ABO or HLA antigens, and causes graft thrombosis. Acute rejection occurs over weeks to months: in the cellular form, recipient T cells that recognize donor HLA invade the interstitium and tubules, as here; in the antibody-mediated form, new donor-specific antibodies injure capillaries and leave C4d deposits. Chronic rejection develops over months to years as graft arteriosclerosis with intimal thickening and fibrosis. Graft-versus-host disease is the reverse direction, donor T cells attacking the host.","whyNot":["Correct.","Preformed antibodies to donor HLA cause hyperacute rejection within minutes to hours of reperfusion, with thrombosis of graft vessels. This graft worked well for weeks.","Donor T cells attacking recipient tissue is graft-versus-host disease, which targets skin, liver, and gut. It follows hematopoietic stem cell transplants and lymphocyte-rich solid organs such as liver or small bowel, and it is very rare after kidney transplant.","Intimal fibrosis of graft arteries is chronic rejection, which develops over months to years. Her biopsy shows active lymphocytic infiltration at 7 weeks."],"study":{"topics":["Types of transplant rejection","Graft-versus-host disease"],"comps":["IM-15-C4","IM-3"]}},{"id":"IM-C14-Q2","stem":"Before she was listed for a deceased-donor kidney, her two brothers were tested as possible living donors. Assuming no recombination within the HLA region, which of the following is the probability that a given brother inherited the same two HLA haplotypes that she did?","options":["6.25%","12.5%","25%","50%"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-3","obj":"IM-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the HLA genes on chromosome 6 are tightly linked and pass to a child as one haplotype from each parent.","Each sibling has a 1/2 chance of getting the same maternal haplotype and a 1/2 chance of getting the same paternal haplotype.","Multiply: 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4, or 25%."],"why":"The HLA genes sit close together on the short arm of chromosome 6, so each parent passes on a block of alleles called a haplotype. A child gets one haplotype from each parent, and both are expressed (codominant expression). For any sibling, the chance of sharing the same haplotype from the mother is 1/2, and from the father is 1/2, so the chance of a full match is 1/4. The chance of sharing exactly one haplotype is 1/2, and the chance of sharing none is 1/4. A fully matched sibling usually gives the best long-term graft survival.","whyNot":["6.25% is (1/4) squared. It would be the chance that two siblings are both full matches, not that one sibling is.","12.5% does not correspond to any standard haplotype outcome in two-parent inheritance.","Correct.","50% is the chance that a sibling shares exactly one haplotype (haploidentical), not both."],"study":{"topics":["HLA haplotype inheritance","Donor matching for transplantation"],"comps":["IM-3"]}},{"id":"IM-C14-Q3","stem":"She had three pregnancies and received several blood transfusions while on dialysis. Before surgery, her serum was tested against lymphocytes from the donor. Which of the following results would have most strongly predicted hyperacute rejection?","options":["Proliferation of her T cells with donor cells","Binding of her IgG to donor lymphocytes","One-allele mismatch at HLA-A with the donor","Anti-A antibodies in her serum with a group O donor"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-3","obj":"IM-3.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note her sensitizing events: pregnancies and transfusions expose a person to foreign HLA.","Recall that hyperacute rejection is caused by preformed antibodies that bind graft endothelium within minutes.","A positive crossmatch, her antibodies binding donor cells, detects those preformed antibodies."],"why":"Pregnancy exposes a woman to paternal HLA on fetal cells, and transfusions and prior transplants expose a person to donor HLA. Both can leave preformed anti-HLA antibodies. If a graft carrying those HLA antigens is connected to the circulation, the antibodies bind its endothelium, fix complement, and cause thrombosis within minutes, which is hyperacute rejection. The crossmatch tests the recipient's serum against donor lymphocytes; a positive result is a contraindication to that transplant. HLA matching reduces later acute and chronic rejection.","whyNot":["T cell proliferation in mixed culture (the mixed lymphocyte reaction) measures T cell recognition of donor HLA. T cells cause acute cellular rejection over weeks, not hyperacute rejection.","Correct.","Single HLA mismatches are common and are managed with immunosuppression. A mismatch raises the risk of later rejection but does not predict hyperacute rejection unless antibodies against that allele already exist.","A group O donor's cells carry neither A nor B antigen, so her anti-A antibodies have no target. ABO incompatibility would matter if the donor were group A."],"study":{"topics":["Crossmatching and sensitization","Hyperacute rejection"],"comps":["IM-3","IM-15-C4"]}},{"id":"IM-C14-Q4","stem":"Three weeks later she develops bloody diarrhea and crampy abdominal pain. Colonoscopy shows multiple shallow ulcers in the right colon. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on biopsy of an ulcer base?","options":["Enlarged cells with intranuclear inclusions","Trophozoites containing ingested erythrocytes","Multinucleated cells with ground-glass nuclei","Pseudomembranes of fibrin, mucus, and neutrophils"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-F","obj":"IM-15-F.13","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: a transplant recipient on strong immunosuppression with CMV viremia from a seropositive donor.","CMV commonly causes colitis with ulcers in transplant and AIDS patients.","The diagnostic biopsy finding is large cells with an owl's eye intranuclear inclusion."],"why":"Cytomegalovirus is a herpesvirus that remains latent after infection and causes disease when T cell immunity is weak. The highest risk after transplant is a seronegative recipient of an organ from a seropositive donor, as in this patient. CMV can cause fever, leukopenia, hepatitis, pneumonitis, retinitis, and colitis with ulcers. Infected cells, often endothelial or stromal cells at the ulcer base, become very large and show a dense intranuclear inclusion with a clear halo (owl's eye), sometimes with cytoplasmic inclusions too.","whyNot":["Correct.","Trophozoites with ingested red cells are the mark of Entamoeba histolytica, which forms flask-shaped ulcers after fecal-oral exposure.","Multinucleated cells with ground-glass nuclei (molding, margination, multinucleation) are typical of herpes simplex virus, which more often causes esophagitis or proctitis.","Pseudomembranes of fibrin, mucus, and neutrophils describe Clostridioides difficile colitis, which usually follows antibiotic use and forms yellow plaques more than ulcers."],"study":{"topics":["CMV disease in transplant recipients","Infectious colitis histology"],"comps":["IM-15-F","IM-15-B"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C15","system":"IM","dx":"Penicillin anaphylaxis","codes":["IM-15-C1"],"case":"A 23-year-old woman is treated at an urgent care clinic for streptococcal pharyngitis with an intramuscular injection of benzathine penicillin G. As a child she had a mild rash while taking amoxicillin. Within 10 minutes of the injection, she develops generalized itching, hives, swelling of the lips, wheezing, and lightheadedness. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse 128/min, respirations 26/min, and blood pressure 78/42 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 90% on room air. There is diffuse urticaria, angioedema of the lips, and bilateral expiratory wheezing. She receives intramuscular epinephrine and intravenous fluids and improves within 30 minutes. Serum tryptase drawn 1 hour after onset is 38 ng/mL (normal less than 11.4). Diagnosis: penicillin anaphylaxis.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C15-Q1","stem":"A vasovagal reaction was considered at first because of her hypotension. Which of the following best explains why the elevated serum tryptase supports anaphylaxis instead?","options":["Release from activated eosinophils","Secretion by activated neutrophils","Leakage from hypoperfused hepatocytes","Degranulation of tissue mast cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C1","obj":"IM-15-C.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that tryptase is a protease stored almost only in mast cell granules.","A rise in serum tryptase within 1 to 3 hours shows that mast cells degranulated.","Vasovagal syncope causes hypotension and bradycardia without mast cell activation, so tryptase stays normal."],"why":"Anaphylaxis is a severe systemic type I reaction. Allergen cross-links IgE on mast cells and basophils, which release histamine and other mediators, causing urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm, and hypotension. Tryptase is stored almost only in mast cells and peaks in serum about 1 to 2 hours after onset, so a high level confirms mast cell activation. A vasovagal reaction causes hypotension with a slow pulse, pallor, and no hives or wheezing, and tryptase stays normal.","whyNot":["Eosinophils release major basic protein and peroxidase, not tryptase. They arrive in the late phase and are not the source of an early tryptase rise.","Neutrophils release myeloperoxidase and elastase. They do not contain meaningful amounts of tryptase.","Hepatocytes do not store tryptase. Shock liver raises transaminases, not tryptase.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anaphylaxis diagnosis","Mast cell mediators"],"comps":["IM-15-C1","IM-10"]}},{"id":"IM-C15-Q2","stem":"Six hours later her wheezing returns despite an oral antihistamine. Mast cells activated earlier have synthesized lipid mediators from arachidonic acid. Which of the following mediators most likely causes this sustained bronchoconstriction?","options":["Leukotriene B4","Prostacyclin (PGI2)","Leukotriene D4","Prostaglandin E2"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-10","obj":"IM-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note that an antihistamine did not help, so a mediator other than histamine is responsible.","Recall the 5-lipoxygenase products: LTB4 attracts neutrophils, while LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4 constrict bronchi and increase vascular permeability.","Pick the cysteinyl leukotriene."],"why":"Mast cells release stored histamine within seconds and then make new lipid mediators from arachidonic acid over minutes to hours. The 5-lipoxygenase pathway produces LTB4, a neutrophil attractant, and the cysteinyl leukotrienes LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4, which are far more potent bronchoconstrictors than histamine and increase vascular permeability. The cyclooxygenase pathway produces prostaglandins and thromboxane. Antihistamines do not block leukotrienes, which is one reason bronchospasm can persist or return in a late phase.","whyNot":["LTB4 is a 5-lipoxygenase product, but its main action is neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion. It does not cause strong bronchoconstriction.","Prostacyclin, made by endothelium, causes vasodilation and inhibits platelet aggregation. It does not constrict bronchi.","Correct.","PGE2 is a cyclooxygenase product that causes fever, pain, and vasodilation. In the airways it tends to relax smooth muscle."],"study":{"topics":["Arachidonic acid pathways","Late-phase allergic response"],"comps":["IM-10","IM-15-C1"]}},{"id":"IM-C15-Q3","stem":"A different patient receives high-dose intravenous penicillin for 2 weeks for endocarditis and develops anemia with spherocytes and a direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test positive for IgG. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of this reaction?","options":["IgE cross-linking on mast cells by penicillin","Drug-antibody complexes deposited in vessel walls","Drug-specific T cells releasing cytokines in tissue","IgG binding penicillin attached to red cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-C","obj":"IM-15-C.15","dok":2,"approach":["Note the findings: hemolysis with spherocytes and IgG on the red cell surface.","At high doses, penicillin binds red cell membranes and acts as a hapten.","IgG binds the drug-coated red cells, and splenic macrophages remove them: a type II reaction."],"why":"The same drug can cause different types of hypersensitivity. Penicillin can cause anaphylaxis (type I, IgE on mast cells), hemolytic anemia (type II, IgG against drug bound to red cells, also seen with methyldopa through true autoantibodies), and serum sickness-like illness (type III, immune complexes). Topical drugs such as neomycin cause contact dermatitis (type IV, T cells). Here, high-dose penicillin coats the red cells, IgG binds the drug, and macrophages remove the coated cells, which explains the spherocytes and positive Coombs test.","whyNot":["IgE cross-linking on mast cells is type I hypersensitivity, which causes anaphylaxis and urticaria within minutes. It does not cause hemolysis.","Immune complex deposition is type III hypersensitivity, which causes fever, rash, arthralgia, and nephritis. It does not coat red cells with IgG.","Drug-specific T cells cause type IV reactions such as contact dermatitis and some drug rashes. They do not produce a positive direct Coombs test.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Drug hypersensitivity by type","Drug-induced immune hemolytic anemia"],"comps":["IM-15-C","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C15-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her reaction began within minutes of the injection?","options":["Preformed IgG forming complexes in vessels","IgE already bound to mast cells","Memory Th1 cells releasing interferon-gamma","Complement activation by IgM on endothelium"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-13","obj":"IM-13.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note her earlier exposure to amoxicillin, which can sensitize a person to penicillins.","Sensitization made penicillin-specific IgE, which sits on mast cell Fc epsilon receptors.","On re-exposure, the drug cross-links that IgE at once, so mediators are released within minutes."],"why":"Hypersensitivity types differ by effector and timing. Type I needs prior sensitization: the first exposure produces IgE that binds Fc epsilon RI on mast cells, so on re-exposure the antigen cross-links bound IgE and mast cells degranulate within minutes. Type II (IgG or IgM against cell or matrix antigens) acts over hours to days, type III (immune complexes) appears about 1 to 2 weeks after a new antigen or 4 to 10 hours in a local Arthus reaction, and type IV (T cells) takes 48 to 72 hours.","whyNot":["Immune complex reactions take hours locally and 1 to 2 weeks systemically. They cause vasculitis and complement consumption, not reactions within minutes.","Correct.","Th1 cells drive delayed (type IV) reactions that need 2 to 3 days to recruit macrophages.","Complement activation by IgM against endothelium occurs in hyperacute rejection of an ABO-incompatible graft. Penicillin anaphylaxis is driven by IgE, not IgM."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types and timing","Sensitization in type I reactions"],"comps":["IM-13","IM-15-C1"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C16","system":"IM","dx":"Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia in autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome","codes":["IM-15-C2","IM-12"],"case":"A 9-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a 1-week history of fatigue, pallor, and yellow eyes. He has had painless enlarged lymph nodes in the neck and axillae since age 3 years. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse 118/min, respirations 20/min, and blood pressure 100/60 mm Hg. He has scleral icterus, nontender lymphadenopathy, and a spleen palpable 6 cm below the left costal margin. Hemoglobin is 7.1 g/dL (normal 11.5 to 15.5), reticulocytes are 9% (normal 0.5 to 2.5), and total bilirubin is 3.4 mg/dL (normal 0.3 to 1.2), mostly unconjugated. Blood smear shows spherocytes. Direct antiglobulin test is positive for IgG and negative for C3. CD4-negative, CD8-negative alpha-beta T cells are 8% of T cells (normal less than 2.5). A heterozygous FAS mutation is found. Diagnosis: warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia in autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C16-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the main mechanism of red cell destruction in this patient?","options":["Oxidant precipitation of hemoglobin in red cells","Complement membrane attack complex lysis in vessels","Fragmentation of red cells by intravascular fibrin","Splenic macrophages removing IgG-coated red cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C2","obj":"IM-15-C.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the direct antiglobulin test: IgG on red cells, no C3, with spherocytes.","IgG warm antibodies bind red cells at body temperature and do not fix complement well.","Splenic macrophages with Fc receptors bite off membrane (making spherocytes) or engulf the cells: extravascular hemolysis."],"why":"Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia is caused by IgG autoantibodies that bind red cells at 37 °C (98.6 °F). Macrophages in the spleen bind the IgG through Fc receptors and remove pieces of membrane, turning the cells into spherocytes, or engulf them whole. It is linked to SLE, CLL, and other immune dysregulation such as ALPS. Cold agglutinin disease is different: IgM binds red cells in cool peripheral blood and fixes complement, giving a C3-positive Coombs test, acrocyanosis, and an association with Mycoplasma pneumoniae and EBV.","whyNot":["Oxidant precipitation of hemoglobin forms Heinz bodies and bite cells in G6PD deficiency. The Coombs test would be negative.","Membrane attack complex lysis in vessels is the main mechanism in ABO transfusion reactions and paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, and it contributes in cold agglutinin disease. His Coombs test is negative for C3.","Fragmentation by fibrin strands produces schistocytes in microangiopathic hemolytic anemia such as TTP or HUS. The Coombs test would be negative.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Warm versus cold autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Extravascular hemolysis"],"comps":["IM-15-C2","IM-8"]}},{"id":"IM-C16-Q2","stem":"The FAS mutation most likely causes his lymphadenopathy and autoimmunity by impairing which of the following?","options":["Release of perforin and granzymes by cytotoxic cells","Expression of tissue antigens by thymic medullary cells","Apoptosis of activated and self-reactive lymphocytes","Development of FOXP3-positive regulatory T cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-12","obj":"IM-12.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that Fas (CD95) is a death receptor; binding Fas ligand activates caspases and apoptosis.","After an immune response, repeatedly activated lymphocytes kill each other through Fas, ending clonal expansion.","Without Fas, lymphocytes accumulate and self-reactive cells survive: lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, and autoimmune cytopenias."],"why":"Fas is a death receptor on lymphocytes. When it binds Fas ligand on another activated lymphocyte, it triggers the extrinsic apoptosis pathway through caspase 8. This activation-induced cell death shrinks lymphocyte clones after an infection is cleared and removes peripheral lymphocytes that recognize self antigen. In autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome, FAS mutations block this pathway, so lymphocytes build up in nodes and spleen, unusual double-negative T cells appear, and autoantibodies cause hemolytic anemia and thrombocytopenia.","whyNot":["Perforin and granzyme release is a separate killing pathway. Defects in it cause familial hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis.","Thymic expression of tissue antigens depends on AIRE. AIRE mutation causes autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1, not lymphoproliferation.","Correct.","FOXP3 controls regulatory T cell development. FOXP3 mutation causes IPEX syndrome with enteropathy, diabetes, and eczema."],"study":{"topics":["Fas-mediated apoptosis","Peripheral tolerance and ALPS"],"comps":["IM-12","IM-15-C2"]}},{"id":"IM-C16-Q3","stem":"If his spleen were examined microscopically, IgG-coated red cells would be found trapped and ingested mainly in which of the following locations?","options":["Splenic cords of the red pulp","Periarteriolar lymphoid sheaths","Germinal centers of white pulp follicles","Lumen of the central arterioles"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Trace splenic blood flow: central arteriole in the white pulp, then penicillar arterioles that open into the cords of the red pulp.","Red cells must squeeze from the cords through slits into the venous sinusoids.","Macrophages lining the cords catch rigid or antibody-coated red cells at this point."],"why":"The spleen has white pulp and red pulp. White pulp is lymphoid tissue around central arterioles: the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath holds T cells, follicles hold B cells, and a marginal zone surrounds them. Branches of the central arteriole end in the red pulp, where blood flows openly into the splenic cords (of Billroth). Red cells must squeeze through narrow slits in the sinusoid walls to return to the veins, and cord macrophages remove cells that are old, rigid, or coated with IgG.","whyNot":["Correct.","The periarteriolar lymphoid sheath is white pulp made of T cells. Red cells pass through central arterioles there but are not filtered or ingested.","Germinal centers are sites of B cell proliferation and selection. They do not filter red cells.","Red cells flow freely through central arterioles. Trapping occurs after blood leaves the arteriole and enters the cords."],"study":{"topics":["Splenic microanatomy and blood flow"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-8"]}},{"id":"IM-C16-Q4","stem":"Splenectomy is generally avoided in this syndrome. Loss of which of the following splenic functions poses the greatest threat to his life after splenectomy?","options":["Removal of nuclear remnants from red cells","Storage of about one third of the platelet mass","Removal of aged red cells from the circulation","Clearance of encapsulated bacteria from blood"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-8","obj":"IM-8.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the spleen's jobs: filtering red cells, clearing bacteria, making IgM, and storing platelets.","Ask which loss can kill: overwhelming infection by encapsulated organisms.","Marginal zone B cells and macrophages clear opsonized encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae."],"why":"The spleen filters blood. Red pulp macrophages remove aged red cells and pit out inclusions such as Howell-Jolly bodies. The marginal zone holds macrophages and B cells that respond quickly to polysaccharide capsules with IgM and remove opsonized bacteria from blood. The spleen also stores about one third of the platelets. After splenectomy, people are at lifelong risk of overwhelming sepsis from encapsulated organisms such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, and Neisseria meningitidis, and this risk is higher in ALPS.","whyNot":["Removal of nuclear remnants (Howell-Jolly bodies) is lost after splenectomy, but these inclusions are harmless. They serve only as a sign of absent splenic function.","Loss of platelet storage causes a mild, usually harmless rise in the platelet count after splenectomy.","The liver and bone marrow macrophages take over removal of aged red cells, so this loss is not dangerous.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Functions of the spleen","Asplenia and encapsulated bacteria"],"comps":["IM-8","IM-2"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C17","system":"IM","dx":"Goodpasture syndrome (anti-GBM disease)","codes":["IM-15-C2"],"case":"A 26-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 1-week history of coughing up blood and passing dark urine. He smokes one pack of cigarettes daily. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse 104/min, respirations 22/min, and blood pressure 158/96 mm Hg. Crackles are heard over both lung bases. Serum creatinine is 3.8 mg/dL (normal 0.7 to 1.3); it was 0.9 mg/dL 1 month ago. Urinalysis shows 2+ protein and red blood cell casts. Chest x-ray shows bilateral patchy alveolar opacities. Serum anti-glomerular basement membrane antibodies are positive, and ANCA tests are negative. Kidney biopsy shows crescents in 80% of glomeruli. Diagnosis: Goodpasture syndrome (anti-GBM disease).","questions":[{"id":"IM-C17-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the target of the antibodies that injure both his lungs and his kidneys?","options":["Proteinase 3 in neutrophil granules","Alpha3 chain of type IV collagen","Myeloperoxidase in neutrophil granules","Phospholipase A2 receptor on podocytes"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C2","obj":"IM-15-C.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the combination: pulmonary hemorrhage plus rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, with anti-GBM antibodies and negative ANCA.","Ask what antigen the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes share.","Both contain the noncollagenous domain of the alpha3 chain of type IV collagen."],"why":"Goodpasture syndrome is a type II hypersensitivity in which IgG binds the noncollagenous (NC1) domain of the alpha3 chain of type IV collagen. This chain is found mainly in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, which explains hematuria with rapidly progressive crescentic glomerulonephritis and hemoptysis. The alveolar epitope is normally hidden, and smoking or other lung injury is thought to expose it, so pulmonary hemorrhage is more common in smokers. Immunofluorescence shows linear IgG along the basement membrane.","whyNot":["Anti-proteinase 3 antibodies (c-ANCA) occur in granulomatosis with polyangiitis, another pulmonary-renal syndrome. His ANCA tests are negative.","Correct.","Anti-myeloperoxidase antibodies (p-ANCA) occur in microscopic polyangiitis, which can also cause lung hemorrhage and crescentic nephritis. His ANCA tests are negative.","Antibodies to the phospholipase A2 receptor cause primary membranous nephropathy, which presents with nephrotic syndrome and does not affect the lungs."],"study":{"topics":["Anti-GBM disease","Pulmonary-renal syndromes"],"comps":["IM-15-C2","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C17-Q2","stem":"HLA typing shows the allele most strongly associated with his disease. The same allele also increases the risk of which of the following?","options":["Multiple sclerosis","Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Ankylosing spondylitis","Celiac disease"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-3","obj":"IM-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the HLA association of Goodpasture syndrome: HLA-DR2 (the DR15 split).","Recall which other disease is linked to DR2.","Multiple sclerosis shares the DR2 association."],"why":"Many autoimmune diseases are linked to particular HLA alleles, likely because those alleles present certain self peptides well to T cells. HLA-DR2 (DR15) is associated with Goodpasture syndrome and multiple sclerosis. HLA-B27 is linked to ankylosing spondylitis and reactive and psoriatic arthritis, DQ2 and DQ8 to celiac disease, DR3 and DR4 to type 1 diabetes, and DR4 to rheumatoid arthritis. An HLA association raises risk but does not cause disease by itself.","whyNot":["Correct.","Type 1 diabetes is linked to DR3 and DR4. DR2 (DR15) is actually associated with protection from type 1 diabetes.","Ankylosing spondylitis is linked to HLA-B27, a class I allele, not to DR2.","Celiac disease is linked to HLA-DQ2 and DQ8, which present gluten-derived peptides to T cells."],"study":{"topics":["HLA and disease associations"],"comps":["IM-3","IM-12"]}},{"id":"IM-C17-Q3","stem":"Which of the following immunofluorescence findings is most likely on his kidney biopsy, with its correct interpretation?","options":["Granular IgG and C3, from circulating immune complexes","Mesangial IgA, from deposited IgA-containing complexes","Linear IgG, from antibody bound to a fixed antigen","Scant immunoglobulin, from ANCA-driven neutrophil injury"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-13","obj":"IM-13.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the mechanism: antibody binding directly to a structural antigen in the basement membrane (type II).","An antigen spread evenly along the membrane gives an even, linear stain.","Immune complexes from the blood (type III) deposit in clumps and give a granular stain."],"why":"Immunofluorescence helps classify immune-mediated kidney disease. In type II hypersensitivity, antibody binds an antigen that is part of the tissue itself; in anti-GBM disease the antigen lines the whole basement membrane, so IgG staining is smooth and linear. In type III hypersensitivity, antigen-antibody complexes form in the blood or in place and lodge in clumps, giving a lumpy granular pattern, as in lupus nephritis and poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. ANCA vasculitis shows little or no immunoglobulin and is called pauci-immune.","whyNot":["Granular IgG and C3 is the pattern of type III immune complex disease, such as lupus nephritis or poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis.","Mesangial IgA deposits are seen in IgA nephropathy and IgA vasculitis, which do not cause lung hemorrhage with anti-GBM antibodies.","Correct.","Scant immunoglobulin (pauci-immune) staining is seen in ANCA-associated vasculitis. His ANCA tests are negative and anti-GBM antibodies are present."],"study":{"topics":["Immunofluorescence patterns in glomerulonephritis","Type II versus type III hypersensitivity"],"comps":["IM-13","IM-15-C2"]}},{"id":"IM-C17-Q4","stem":"Antibody bound to the glomerular basement membrane activates the classical complement pathway. Which of the following complement fragments most directly recruits neutrophils into the glomeruli?","options":["C4b","C5a","C3b","C5b-9"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-7","obj":"IM-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that bound IgG activates C1 and the classical pathway, generating C3 and C5 convertases.","Sort the fragments by job: opsonin, anaphylatoxin and chemoattractant, or membrane attack complex.","C5a is the strongest complement chemoattractant for neutrophils."],"why":"Complement fragments have distinct jobs. C3b coats targets so phagocytes can bind them (opsonization) and helps clear immune complexes. C3a and C5a are anaphylatoxins that make mast cells release histamine, and C5a is also a potent chemoattractant that brings neutrophils to the site. C5b through C9 form the membrane attack complex, which punches holes in cell membranes. In anti-GBM disease, neutrophils drawn in by C5a release proteases and oxidants that break the basement membrane and lead to crescents.","whyNot":["C4b is part of the classical C3 convertase (C4b2a). It has no chemotactic role.","Correct.","C3b is the main opsonin. It helps phagocytes bind targets but does not itself draw neutrophils to the site.","C5b-9 is the membrane attack complex, which lyses cells and can injure glomerular cells directly. It does not attract neutrophils."],"study":{"topics":["Complement effector functions"],"comps":["IM-7","IM-6"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C18","system":"IM","dx":"Myasthenia gravis with thymoma","codes":["IM-15-C2"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of drooping eyelids and double vision that worsen toward evening. She also has difficulty climbing stairs and tires while chewing meat. Vital signs are within normal limits. Sustained upward gaze for 60 seconds produces worsening bilateral ptosis, which improves after an ice pack is held over the eyelids for 2 minutes. Proximal limb strength is 4/5 and decreases with repeated testing. Deep tendon reflexes and sensation are normal. Repetitive nerve stimulation at 3 Hz shows a decremental response. Serum acetylcholine receptor-binding antibodies are positive. CT scan of the chest shows a 5-cm mass in the anterior mediastinum. Diagnosis: myasthenia gravis with thymoma.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C18-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her weakness increases with repeated muscle use?","options":["Loss of lower motor neurons in the anterior horn","Too few receptors to reach threshold as release falls","Excess acetylcholine from blocked acetylcholinesterase","Reduced calcium entry at presynaptic nerve terminals"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-15-C2","obj":"IM-15-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: weakness that worsens with use and a decremental response to repeated stimulation.","Recall that the amount of acetylcholine released falls with each impulse in a train, even in healthy people.","With fewer receptors, end-plate potentials drop below threshold as release declines, so more fibers fail."],"why":"Myasthenia gravis is a type II hypersensitivity in which antibodies against the acetylcholine receptor (or MuSK) reduce working receptors without killing the muscle cell. The antibodies cross-link receptors so they are internalized, activate complement that damages the end-plate folds, and can block acetylcholine binding. Normally the end-plate potential far exceeds threshold, so the normal fall in acetylcholine release during repeated firing does not matter. With fewer receptors, that safety margin is lost, more fibers fail with each impulse, and weakness and the decrement worsen with use. Rest restores release, and cooling slows acetylcholinesterase, which is why the ice pack helps.","whyNot":["Loss of lower motor neurons, as in ALS, causes fasciculations, atrophy, and weakness that does not fluctuate with use.","Correct.","Excess acetylcholine from cholinesterase blockade occurs in organophosphate poisoning, which causes cholinergic signs such as miosis, salivation, and bronchorrhea.","Reduced presynaptic calcium entry is the defect in Lambert-Eaton syndrome, caused by antibodies to voltage-gated calcium channels. Strength there improves briefly with repeated effort, and reflexes are reduced."],"study":{"topics":["Myasthenia gravis mechanism","Myasthenia gravis versus Lambert-Eaton syndrome"],"comps":["IM-15-C2","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C18-Q2","stem":"The mass lies behind the manubrium, anterior to the great vessels. Which of the following lesions also characteristically arises in this mediastinal compartment?","options":["Schwannoma of an intercostal nerve","Esophageal duplication cyst","Mature cystic teratoma","Bronchogenic cyst"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-1","obj":"IM-1.5","dok":2,"approach":["Place the thymus: anterior and superior mediastinum, behind the manubrium and in front of the great vessels and pericardium.","Recall the classic anterior mediastinal masses: thymoma, teratoma and other germ cell tumors, lymphoma, and retrosternal thyroid.","Choose the germ cell tumor."],"why":"The adult thymus lies in the anterior and superior mediastinum, behind the manubrium and in front of the left brachiocephalic vein, aortic arch, and pericardium. Masses in this compartment are classically thymoma, teratoma and other germ cell tumors, lymphoma, and retrosternal goiter. Thymoma is the most common anterior mediastinal mass in adults and is the one linked to myasthenia gravis and other paraneoplastic autoimmune disorders. Middle mediastinal masses include bronchogenic cysts and lymph nodes, and posterior masses include nerve sheath tumors.","whyNot":["Schwannomas and other neurogenic tumors arise from intercostal nerves or sympathetic chain in the posterior mediastinum, along the spine.","Esophageal duplication cysts arise along the esophagus in the middle or posterior mediastinum.","Correct.","Bronchogenic cysts arise from the foregut near the trachea and carina, in the middle mediastinum."],"study":{"topics":["Thymic location","Mediastinal masses by compartment"],"comps":["IM-1"]}},{"id":"IM-C18-Q3","stem":"Neoplastic epithelial cells in thymomas often fail to express AIRE, which may help explain the autoimmunity seen with these tumors. A germline loss-of-function mutation in AIRE causes a syndrome with which of the following combinations?","options":["Candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency","Lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, autoimmune cytopenias","Hypocalcemia, conotruncal defects, absent thymus","Enteropathy, type 1 diabetes, and eczematous dermatitis"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-12","obj":"IM-12.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what AIRE does: drives medullary thymic epithelial cells to express tissue-specific self antigens.","Without AIRE, T cells that recognize these antigens escape negative selection.","The result is autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1: chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and adrenal insufficiency."],"why":"AIRE is a transcription regulator in medullary thymic epithelial cells. It switches on genes for proteins normally made only in other organs, such as parathyroid and adrenal proteins, so developing T cells that react strongly to them are deleted. Loss of AIRE causes autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1 (APECED), with chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and adrenal insufficiency. The candidiasis comes from autoantibodies that neutralize IL-17 and IL-22, cytokines that defend against Candida. Thymomas that lack AIRE can produce a similar loss of tolerance.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, and autoimmune cytopenias describe autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome from FAS mutation.","Hypocalcemia, conotruncal heart defects, and an absent thymus describe 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge) syndrome, a developmental defect of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches.","Enteropathy, type 1 diabetes, and eczema make up IPEX syndrome, caused by FOXP3 mutation and loss of regulatory T cells."],"study":{"topics":["AIRE and central tolerance","Monogenic autoimmune syndromes"],"comps":["IM-12","IM-8"]}},{"id":"IM-C18-Q4","stem":"She asks whether removing her thymus will weaken her immune defenses. Which of the following best explains why thymectomy at her age causes little loss of T cell immunity?","options":["Lymph nodes take over production of new T cells","Bone marrow assumes thymic selection of T cells","B cells assume the functions of helper T cells","Peripheral T cells persist and divide"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-1","obj":"IM-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the thymus is most active before puberty and then involutes, with fat replacing much of the lymphoid tissue.","By adulthood, the T cell pool is already formed and is kept up mainly by division of existing peripheral T cells.","Removing the thymus therefore leaves established T cell immunity largely intact."],"why":"The thymus is largest relative to body size in early childhood and begins to involute after puberty, as adipose tissue replaces much of the cortex and medulla. Thymic output of new T cells falls steadily with age. The adult T cell pool is kept up mainly by long-lived naive and memory T cells that slowly divide in the periphery in response to cytokines such as IL-7 and IL-15. For this reason, thymectomy in an adult causes little measurable immune deficit, while absence of the thymus in infancy, as in DiGeorge syndrome, causes severe T cell deficiency.","whyNot":["Lymph nodes house and activate mature T cells but do not produce new T cells from precursors.","Positive and negative selection of T cells require thymic epithelium. Bone marrow makes T cell precursors but cannot select T cells.","B cells cannot perform helper T cell functions. They depend on T cell help for class switching and affinity maturation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Thymic involution","Maintenance of the peripheral T cell pool"],"comps":["IM-1","IM-8"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C19","system":"IM","dx":"Systemic lupus erythematosus with diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis","codes":["IM-15-C3"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of hand joint pain, a facial rash that worsens with sun exposure, and ankle swelling. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse 92/min, respirations 16/min, and blood pressure 152/94 mm Hg. Physical examination shows malar erythema sparing the nasolabial folds, tender wrists and finger joints without deformity, and ankle edema. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (normal 12 to 16), platelet count is 92,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000), and serum creatinine is 1.6 mg/dL (normal 0.6 to 1.1). Urinalysis shows 3+ protein and red blood cell casts. Antinuclear and anti-dsDNA antibodies are positive. Serum C3 is 48 mg/dL (normal 90 to 180), and C4 is 6 mg/dL (normal 10 to 40). Kidney biopsy shows diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis with wire-loop lesions. Diagnosis: systemic lupus erythematosus with diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C19-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains how her antinuclear autoantibodies arise?","options":["Cross-reactive antibodies to streptococcal antigens","Drug-induced antibodies directed against histones","Poor clearance of apoptotic nuclear debris","Inherited loss of FOXP3-positive regulatory T cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-C3","obj":"IM-15-C.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the antigens in SLE are nuclear: DNA, histones, and ribonucleoproteins.","These antigens become exposed when apoptotic cells are not cleared promptly.","Persistent nuclear debris activates self-reactive B and T cells, producing antinuclear antibodies and immune complexes."],"why":"In SLE, nuclear material from dying cells is cleared too slowly, for example because of inherited deficiency of early complement proteins (C1q, C2, C4) or defects in phagocyte uptake. Nucleic acids in this debris stimulate Toll-like receptors and type I interferon, and self-reactive B cells make antibodies to DNA and nuclear proteins. These antibodies form immune complexes that deposit in kidneys, skin, joints, and serosa (type III). ANA is very sensitive, anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are specific, anti-dsDNA tracks nephritis activity, and C3 and C4 fall during flares as complement is consumed.","whyNot":["Cross-reaction with streptococcal antigens (molecular mimicry) causes acute rheumatic fever. It does not produce antinuclear antibodies.","Drug-induced lupus, from drugs such as hydralazine or procainamide, produces anti-histone antibodies and rarely affects the kidneys. She takes no such drug, and anti-dsDNA points to idiopathic SLE.","Correct.","Loss of FOXP3-positive regulatory T cells causes IPEX syndrome in infant boys, with enteropathy, diabetes, and eczema."],"study":{"topics":["SLE pathogenesis","Autoantibodies in SLE"],"comps":["IM-15-C3","IM-12"]}},{"id":"IM-C19-Q2","stem":"Three years later she has a second-trimester pregnancy loss and then a deep venous thrombosis of the left leg. Which of the following coagulation findings is most likely?","options":["Prolonged PT that corrects after vitamin K is given","Prolonged aPTT that does not correct after mixing","Low fibrinogen with a markedly elevated D-dimer level","Prolonged aPTT that corrects after mixing with normal plasma"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C3","obj":"IM-15-C.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize pregnancy loss plus venous thrombosis in a woman with SLE as antiphospholipid syndrome.","Recall that the lupus anticoagulant binds phospholipid in the test tube and prolongs the aPTT.","Because it is an inhibitor, adding normal plasma does not correct the prolonged aPTT."],"why":"Antiphospholipid syndrome occurs alone or with SLE and causes venous and arterial thrombosis and recurrent pregnancy loss. The antibodies (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I) promote clotting in the body. In the laboratory, lupus anticoagulant binds the phospholipid used in clotting tests, so the aPTT is prolonged. A mixing study does not correct it, which shows an inhibitor and not a missing factor. Anticardiolipin antibodies can also cause a false-positive syphilis (VDRL) test.","whyNot":["A prolonged PT that corrects with vitamin K reflects deficiency of factors II, VII, IX, and X from malabsorption or warfarin. It causes bleeding, not thrombosis.","Correct.","Low fibrinogen with high D-dimer is the pattern of disseminated intravascular coagulation, which is consumptive and usually causes bleeding along with clotting.","An aPTT that corrects with mixing points to a factor deficiency, such as hemophilia A or B. Factor deficiency causes bleeding."],"study":{"topics":["Antiphospholipid syndrome","Mixing studies"],"comps":["IM-15-C3","IM-15-C"]}},{"id":"IM-C19-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains her serum complement levels?","options":["Alternative pathway activation that spares C4","Unchecked C1 activity from C1 inhibitor deficiency","Classical pathway consumption by immune complexes","Inherited deficiency of the terminal components"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-7","obj":"IM-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note that both C3 and C4 are low.","C4 is used only by the classical and lectin pathways; C3 is used by all three.","Immune complexes activate the classical pathway, consuming C4 and C3 together."],"why":"Complement levels show which pathway is being used up. Immune complexes in active SLE bind C1q and activate the classical pathway, which consumes C4 and C2 and then C3, so both C3 and C4 are low; levels rise toward normal when the flare is controlled. In poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, the alternative pathway is activated, so C3 is low while C4 stays normal. In hereditary angioedema, C1 inhibitor deficiency lets C1 cleave C4 unchecked, so C4 is low while C3 is normal.","whyNot":["Alternative pathway activation lowers C3 but leaves C4 normal, as in poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. Her C4 is low.","C1 inhibitor deficiency (hereditary angioedema) lowers C4 but leaves C3 normal, and it causes swelling without urticaria.","Correct.","Terminal component (C5 to C9) deficiency raises the risk of Neisseria infections. It does not lower C3 and C4."],"study":{"topics":["Interpreting complement levels"],"comps":["IM-7","IM-15-C3"]}},{"id":"IM-C19-Q4","stem":"Helper T cells that support her anti-dsDNA response escaped deletion during development. Which of the following best describes where and how T cells with high affinity for self antigen are normally deleted?","options":["Thymic medulla, by strong self-antigen binding","Germinal centers, by somatic hypermutation","Thymic cortex, for failing to bind self-MHC","Bone marrow, by receptor editing of light chains"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-8","obj":"IM-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the order of thymic maturation: double-negative, then double-positive thymocytes in the cortex, then single-positive cells in the medulla.","Positive selection in the cortex keeps cells that bind self-MHC weakly.","Negative selection in the medulla deletes cells that bind self-peptide on MHC strongly."],"why":"Thymocytes enter the cortex as CD4-negative, CD8-negative (double-negative) cells, rearrange their T cell receptor, and become double-positive. In the cortex, cortical epithelial cells test them for weak binding to self-MHC; cells that fail die by neglect, which is positive selection. Survivors become CD4 or CD8 single-positive and move to the medulla, where medullary epithelial cells (using AIRE) and dendritic cells display self antigens. Cells that bind these strongly undergo apoptosis, which is negative selection. Escapees are normally held in check by peripheral tolerance.","whyNot":["Correct.","Somatic hypermutation in germinal centers raises B cell antibody affinity. It is a process of B cells, not a T cell deletion mechanism.","Death in the cortex for failing to bind self-MHC is the failure of positive selection. It removes useless T cells, not self-reactive ones.","Receptor editing in the bone marrow rescues self-reactive B cells by changing their light chain. It does not apply to T cells."],"study":{"topics":["Thymic positive and negative selection","Central tolerance"],"comps":["IM-8","IM-12"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C20","system":"IM","dx":"Polyarteritis nodosa associated with chronic hepatitis B","codes":["IM-15-C3"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of fever, muscle aches, a 7-kg (15-lb) weight loss, and abdominal pain that begins about 30 minutes after meals. One week ago she developed weakness of her right foot. She has untreated chronic hepatitis B. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse 96/min, respirations 16/min, and blood pressure 168/100 mm Hg. Examination shows livedo reticularis, tender nodules on both calves, and right foot drop. The lungs are clear. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 88 mm/h (normal 0 to 20), and serum creatinine is 1.5 mg/dL (normal 0.6 to 1.1). HBsAg is positive, and ANCA tests are negative. Angiography shows multiple microaneurysms of the renal and mesenteric arteries. Biopsy of a calf nodule shows necrotizing inflammation of a medium-sized artery. Diagnosis: polyarteritis nodosa associated with chronic hepatitis B.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C20-Q1","stem":"Biopsies taken from several of her affected arteries at the same time would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Granulomas with giant cells along the internal elastic lamina","Necrotizing granulomas involving small vessels of the lung","Concentric onion-skin intimal thickening without inflammation","Segmental fibrinoid necrosis at varied stages of healing"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C3","obj":"IM-15-C.8","dok":3,"approach":["Identify polyarteritis nodosa: a necrotizing vasculitis of medium-sized muscular arteries, ANCA negative, sparing the lungs.","Recall its key histologic feature: segmental transmural fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils.","Because lesions start at different times, acute necrosis, healing, and fibrotic scars appear side by side."],"why":"Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic necrotizing vasculitis of small and medium-sized muscular arteries, often linked to hepatitis B through immune complexes of HBsAg and antibody. It affects the kidneys, gut, nerves, and skin and characteristically spares the lungs, and ANCA is negative. Lesions are segmental and often at branch points: acute ones show transmural fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils, while older ones show fibrous thickening of the wall. Different stages coexist in the same patient. Weakened walls form microaneurysms, and narrowed lumens cause infarcts such as her mesenteric ischemia, foot drop from mononeuritis multiplex, and renin-mediated hypertension.","whyNot":["Granulomas with giant cells along a fragmented internal elastic lamina describe giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis, which affect large arteries.","Necrotizing granulomas with small-vessel vasculitis in the lung describe granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which is c-ANCA positive. Her lungs are spared and ANCA is negative.","Onion-skin intimal thickening is hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis of malignant hypertension, a change in arterioles without vasculitis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Polyarteritis nodosa","Vasculitis by vessel size"],"comps":["IM-15-C3","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C20-Q2","stem":"Circulating complexes of hepatitis B surface antigen and antibody deposit in her arteries. A similar systemic immune complex reaction can follow treatment with a chimeric monoclonal antibody. Which of the following presentations is most typical of that reaction?","options":["Hives and wheezing within 30 minutes of the first infusion","Fever, rash, arthralgia, and low C3 about 10 days later","Hemolysis with a positive direct Coombs test after 3 days","Vesicular dermatitis at the infusion site after 48 hours"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-C","obj":"IM-15-C.14","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the systemic immune complex reaction to a foreign protein as serum sickness (type III).","Antibody to the foreign protein takes about 1 to 2 weeks to form, then complexes form while antigen is still present.","Complexes deposit in vessels, joints, and kidneys and consume complement: fever, rash, arthralgia, proteinuria, low C3."],"why":"Serum sickness is a systemic type III reaction. A foreign protein, such as the mouse portion of a chimeric monoclonal antibody or antivenom, triggers antibody production over about 1 to 2 weeks. Antibody then meets the antigen still in the blood, forming complexes that lodge in small vessels, synovium, and glomeruli and activate complement. This produces fever, urticarial rash, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, proteinuria, and low complement levels, and it subsides as the antigen is cleared. The Arthus reaction is the local version, a necrotizing vasculitis at an injection site that peaks within hours in a person who already has high antibody levels.","whyNot":["Hives and wheezing within minutes are a type I or infusion reaction, mediated by IgE or direct mast cell activation, not immune complexes.","Correct.","Hemolysis with a positive Coombs test is a type II reaction, from antibody binding drug-coated or self antigens on red cells.","Vesicular dermatitis after 48 hours at a contact site is a type IV (T cell) reaction, as in contact dermatitis from topical neomycin."],"study":{"topics":["Serum sickness and the Arthus reaction","Type III hypersensitivity"],"comps":["IM-15-C","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C20-Q3","stem":"Neutrophils are abundant in her acutely inflamed arteries. In a child with a CD18 mutation (leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1), neutrophils could not have entered these vessel walls. Which of the following interactions is defective in that child?","options":["LFA-1 binding to ICAM-1","Sialyl-Lewis X binding to E-selectin","C5a binding to its neutrophil receptor","PECAM-1 binding to PECAM-1"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the steps of extravasation: rolling (selectins), firm adhesion (integrins), diapedesis (PECAM-1), and chemotaxis.","CD18 is the beta2 subunit shared by the leukocyte integrins LFA-1 and Mac-1.","Without CD18, LFA-1 cannot bind ICAM-1, so neutrophils roll but cannot stop and exit."],"why":"Neutrophils leave the blood in steps. TNF and IL-1 from the inflamed site make endothelium express E-selectin and P-selectin, which bind sialyl-Lewis X on neutrophils and make them roll. Chemokines such as IL-8 then activate beta2 integrins (LFA-1 and Mac-1, which share CD18) to bind ICAM-1 tightly, stopping the cell. The neutrophil crawls between endothelial cells using PECAM-1 and follows gradients of C5a, LTB4, and IL-8 to the target. In leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1, CD18 is missing, so neutrophils cannot adhere firmly, causing neutrophilia, delayed umbilical cord separation, and infections without pus.","whyNot":["Correct.","Sialyl-Lewis X binding to selectins mediates rolling. Its loss causes leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 2, from a defect in fucose metabolism, not a CD18 mutation.","C5a binding its G protein-coupled receptor directs chemotaxis. The receptor is not CD18, so chemotactic signaling is intact in this disorder.","PECAM-1 binding mediates diapedesis between endothelial cells. It does not involve CD18."],"study":{"topics":["Leukocyte extravasation","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency"],"comps":["IM-6","IM-7"]}},{"id":"IM-C20-Q4","stem":"Her fever and weight loss reflect high levels of IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha. Which of the following iron study patterns is most likely?","options":["High serum iron and low hepcidin","High serum iron and high transferrin saturation","High transferrin and low ferritin","Low serum iron and high ferritin"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the acute phase response: IL-6 makes the liver produce more CRP, fibrinogen, serum amyloid A, ferritin, and hepcidin, and less albumin and transferrin.","Hepcidin degrades ferroportin, trapping iron in macrophages and enterocytes.","Serum iron falls while stored iron (ferritin) rises: the pattern of anemia of chronic disease."],"why":"IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha drive the acute phase response. They cause fever by inducing prostaglandin E2 in the hypothalamus, which raises the temperature set point. IL-6 makes hepatocytes produce more CRP, fibrinogen (which raises the ESR), serum amyloid A, ferritin, and hepcidin, and less albumin and transferrin. Hepcidin breaks down ferroportin, the iron exporter, so iron is trapped in macrophages and gut cells. Serum iron falls, ferritin rises, and transferrin is low to normal, the pattern of anemia of chronic disease.","whyNot":["Inflammation raises hepcidin, which lowers serum iron. Low hepcidin with high iron is the pattern of hereditary hemochromatosis.","High iron with high transferrin saturation indicates iron overload. Inflammation lowers serum iron and saturation.","High transferrin with low ferritin is the pattern of iron deficiency. Inflammation lowers transferrin and raises ferritin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acute phase response","Anemia of chronic disease"],"comps":["IM-6","IM-15-C3"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C21","system":"IM","dx":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis after impetigo","codes":["IM-15-C3"],"case":"A 7-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of dark brown urine and puffy eyelids for 2 days. Three weeks ago he had crusted, honey-colored sores on the anteromedial surfaces of both lower legs that were not treated. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 134/88 mm Hg (above the 95th percentile for age). Examination shows periorbital edema, 1+ pitting edema of the ankles, and healing crusted lesions on both shins. Urinalysis shows 3+ blood, 1+ protein, and red blood cell casts. Serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7). Serum C3 is 38 mg/dL (90 to 180), and C4 is 24 mg/dL (10 to 40). The antistreptolysin O titer is within the reference range, and the anti-DNase B titer is elevated. Diagnosis: poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis after impetigo.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C21-Q1","stem":"Over the next 3 days his serum creatinine rises to 1.6 mg/dL, and a renal biopsy is performed. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Subendothelial wire loops with full-house staining","Mesangial IgA deposits with mesangial proliferation","Linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane","Subepithelial humps with granular IgG and C3"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C3","obj":"IM-15-C.9","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize nephritic syndrome 1 to 4 weeks after a group A streptococcal skin infection with low C3.","Recall that this is a type III reaction in which immune complexes form in or deposit on the glomerulus.","Match the disease to its biopsy picture: lumpy subepithelial deposits on electron microscopy and granular staining on immunofluorescence."],"why":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is a type III hypersensitivity reaction. Streptococcal antigens lodge in the glomerulus, antibodies bind them, and complement is activated, drawing in neutrophils and causing a diffusely hypercellular glomerulus. Electron microscopy shows dense subepithelial humps on the outer side of the basement membrane. Immunofluorescence shows granular, lumpy-bumpy deposits of IgG and C3. Skin infections favor a rise in anti-DNase B, while the ASO titer often stays low after impetigo.","whyNot":["Wire loops with IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q (full-house) are typical of diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis, which lowers both C3 and C4.","Mesangial IgA is IgA nephropathy, which causes hematuria within 1 to 2 days of a mucosal infection with normal C3. His 3-week delay and low C3 point away from it.","Linear IgG outlining the basement membrane is the pattern of anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture syndrome), a type II reaction against collagen IV. It would fit a young adult with hemoptysis and nephritis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis pathology","Immunofluorescence patterns in glomerular disease"],"comps":["IM-15-C3","IM-7"]}},{"id":"IM-C21-Q2","stem":"Which of the following initiating events best explains why his C3 is low while his C4 remains normal?","options":["Mannose-binding lectin binding to bacterial sugars","C1 inhibitor deficiency with unchecked C1 activity","C3b binding factor B on bacterial antigens","C1q binding to IgG in circulating immune complexes"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-7","obj":"IM-7.1","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: C3 low, C4 normal.","Recall that the classical and lectin pathways both cleave C4 to build C4b2a, while the alternative pathway builds C3bBb without C4.","Conclude that the alternative pathway is doing most of the consuming."],"why":"All three complement pathways converge on a C3 convertase. The classical pathway (C1q binding IgG or IgM complexes) and the lectin pathway (mannose-binding lectin on microbial sugars) both cleave C4 and C2 to make C4b2a. The alternative pathway starts with slow spontaneous hydrolysis of C3 (tickover); on a surface that lacks regulators, C3b binds factor B, factor D cleaves it, and C3bBb amplifies itself. Streptococcal antigens in the glomerulus favor this loop, so C3 is used up and C4 is spared.","whyNot":["The lectin pathway uses MBL-associated serine proteases that cleave C4 and C2, just like C1s. It would also lower C4.","Without C1 inhibitor, C1 keeps cleaving C4, so C4 falls while C3 stays normal. That is hereditary angioedema, the opposite pattern.","Correct.","C1q binding starts the classical pathway, in which C1s cleaves C4 and C2. That route would consume C4 along with C3, as in active lupus."],"study":{"topics":["Three complement pathways","C3 and C4 patterns"],"comps":["IM-7","IM-15-C3"]}},{"id":"IM-C21-Q3","stem":"During the skin infection 3 weeks earlier, which group of lymph nodes was most likely to have been enlarged and tender?","options":["Vertical group of superficial inguinal nodes","Popliteal nodes in the fat of the popliteal fossa","Epitrochlear nodes above the medial elbow","Internal iliac nodes along the pelvic wall"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-4","obj":"IM-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Place the lesions: the anteromedial lower legs.","Recall that superficial lymph vessels from the medial foot and anteromedial leg follow the great saphenous vein.","Follow those vessels to the vertical group of superficial inguinal nodes near the saphenous opening."],"why":"Superficial lymph from most of the lower limb, including the anteromedial leg and medial foot, travels with the great saphenous vein to the vertical (inferior) group of superficial inguinal nodes. These nodes then drain to the deep inguinal and external iliac nodes. Only the lateral foot and posterolateral calf, which drain with the small saphenous vein, go first to the popliteal nodes. Tender inguinal nodes are a common finding with impetigo or cellulitis of the shin.","whyNot":["Correct.","Popliteal nodes receive superficial lymph that runs with the small saphenous vein from the lateral foot and the back of the calf. Lesions on the front and inner shin drain elsewhere.","Epitrochlear nodes drain the ulnar side of the hand and forearm. They would enlarge with an infection of the little finger, for example.","Internal iliac nodes drain pelvic organs, the deep perineum, and the gluteal region. They are not palpable and do not receive superficial lymph from the leg."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic drainage of the lower limb","Palpable lymph node groups"],"comps":["IM-4"]}},{"id":"IM-C21-Q4","stem":"Twelve weeks later his urine has cleared of red cell casts and his blood pressure is normal. Which of the following serum complement results is most likely at this visit?","options":["C3 decreased, C4 decreased","C3 normal, C4 normal","C3 decreased, C4 normal","C3 normal, C4 decreased"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-7","obj":"IM-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the expected course: C3 falls during the first weeks and returns to normal within about 6 to 8 weeks.","Note that his clinical findings have resolved by 12 weeks.","Use the rule that persistently low C3 suggests another diagnosis."],"why":"In poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, C3 is low in almost every child during the acute phase and returns to normal within about 6 to 8 weeks, along with clinical recovery. C4 stays normal throughout. A C3 that remains low past 8 weeks is a red flag for C3 glomerulopathy, membranoproliferative disease, or lupus. Reading complement this way helps sort nephritis: low C3 and C4 in active SLE, low C3 alone in poststreptococcal disease, and low C4 alone in hereditary angioedema.","whyNot":["Low C3 and low C4 together signal classical pathway consumption, as in active lupus nephritis. It would not fit a patient whose nephritis is resolving.","Correct.","This is the pattern during the acute illness. If C3 stayed low beyond about 8 weeks, the diagnosis should be reconsidered, for example C3 glomerulopathy or membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis.","Normal C3 with low C4 is the pattern of hereditary angioedema, in which C1 inhibitor deficiency lets C1 consume C4."],"study":{"topics":["Complement levels in glomerulonephritis","Course of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"],"comps":["IM-7","IM-15-C3"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C22","system":"IM","dx":"Rheumatoid arthritis with Felty syndrome","codes":["IM-15-C3"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman comes to the physician because of fatigue and three skin infections over the past 4 months. She has a 9-year history of rheumatoid arthritis treated irregularly. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Examination shows symmetric swelling of the metacarpophalangeal, proximal interphalangeal, and wrist joints with ulnar deviation of the fingers, firm nontender nodules over the extensor forearms, and a spleen palpable 5 cm below the left costal margin. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (12 to 16), leukocyte count is 2,600/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000) with an absolute neutrophil count of 700/mm3 (1,500 to 8,000), and platelet count is 210,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000). Rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies are positive. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 58 mm/h (0 to 30). Diagnosis: rheumatoid arthritis with Felty syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C22-Q1","stem":"Radiographs of her hands are most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Marginal MCP and wrist erosions sparing the DIP joints","DIP osteophytes with subchondral sclerosis and joint space loss","DIP erosions with pencil-in-cup deformity and periostitis","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges near tophi"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C3","obj":"IM-15-C.10","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize seropositive, symmetric small joint arthritis with nodules as rheumatoid arthritis.","Recall that synovial pannus erodes bone first at the bare areas at the joint margins.","Apply the joint distribution: MCP, PIP, and wrist involved, DIP spared."],"why":"In rheumatoid arthritis, inflamed synovium proliferates into a pannus of fibroblasts, macrophages, and lymphocytes. Pannus secretes enzymes and RANKL, which activates osteoclasts at the joint margins where bone is not covered by cartilage. Radiographs show periarticular osteopenia, symmetric joint space narrowing, and marginal erosions of the MCP, PIP, and wrist joints, with the DIP joints typically spared. Felty syndrome is the triad of rheumatoid arthritis, splenomegaly, and neutropenia, usually in long-standing seropositive disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Osteophytes and sclerosis at the DIP joints (Heberden nodes) are osteoarthritis, a wear-and-tear disease without synovial pannus or autoantibodies.","Pencil-in-cup DIP erosions with new bone formation are typical of psoriatic arthritis, which is seronegative and often asymmetric.","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges beside soft tissue tophi are chronic gout, caused by urate crystal deposits."],"study":{"topics":["Rheumatoid arthritis joint pattern","Radiographic signs of arthritis"],"comps":["IM-15-C3"]}},{"id":"IM-C22-Q2","stem":"Testing of her HLA genotype is most likely to show which of the following susceptibility alleles and functions?","options":["HLA-DR3, presenting pancreatic islet cell peptides to CD4 T cells","HLA-DR4, presenting citrullinated peptides to CD4 T cells","HLA-DQ2, presenting deamidated gliadin to CD4 T cells","HLA-DR2, presenting myelin peptides to CD4 T cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-3","obj":"IM-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the anti-CCP antibodies: her immune response targets citrullinated proteins.","Recall that HLA-DR4 alleles carrying the shared epitope bind citrullinated peptides well.","Connect class II presentation to CD4 T cell help for the B cells that make anti-CCP."],"why":"Seropositive rheumatoid arthritis is strongly associated with HLA-DR4 (HLA-DRB1 alleles with the shared epitope). Inflammation and smoking increase citrullination, the conversion of arginine to citrulline by peptidylarginine deiminase. The shared epitope pocket binds citrullinated peptides tightly, so CD4 T cells respond and help B cells make anti-CCP antibodies. Anti-CCP is the most specific serologic test for rheumatoid arthritis.","whyNot":["HLA-DR3 and DR4 together raise the risk of type 1 diabetes, where T cells attack pancreatic beta cells. DR3 is not the rheumatoid arthritis allele.","Correct.","HLA-DQ2 (and DQ8) present gluten peptides modified by tissue transglutaminase in celiac disease.","HLA-DR2 is linked to multiple sclerosis and Goodpasture syndrome. It is not the main risk allele for seropositive rheumatoid arthritis."],"study":{"topics":["HLA associations with disease","Citrullination and anti-CCP"],"comps":["IM-3","IM-15-C3"]}},{"id":"IM-C22-Q3","stem":"Additional studies show serum iron of 35 µg/dL (50 to 170), total iron-binding capacity of 210 µg/dL (250 to 400), and ferritin of 380 ng/mL (12 to 150). Which of the following best explains the low serum iron despite high ferritin?","options":["Transferrin induction by IL-1, binding free serum iron","Ferroportin induction by TNF-alpha, raising iron export","Hepcidin induction by IL-6, trapping iron in macrophages","Haptoglobin induction by IL-6, binding iron after hemolysis"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the pattern of anemia of chronic disease: low iron, low TIBC, high ferritin.","Recall that IL-6 makes the liver produce hepcidin as part of the acute phase response.","Apply hepcidin's action: it degrades ferroportin, trapping iron in macrophages and enterocytes."],"why":"Chronic inflammation drives the acute phase response. IL-6 is the main signal telling hepatocytes to make hepcidin, along with CRP, fibrinogen, serum amyloid A, and ferritin. Hepcidin binds ferroportin, the only iron exporter, and causes it to be internalized and degraded. Iron stays locked in macrophages and duodenal cells, so serum iron falls even though stores (ferritin) are high. This anemia of chronic disease likely adds to her low hemoglobin along with splenic sequestration.","whyNot":["Transferrin is a negative acute phase protein. Inflammation lowers it, which is why her iron-binding capacity is low.","More ferroportin would release iron into plasma and raise serum iron. In inflammation, ferroportin is removed from the cell surface.","Correct.","Haptoglobin is an acute phase protein, but it binds free hemoglobin. It does not control serum iron."],"study":{"topics":["Acute phase response","Anemia of chronic disease"],"comps":["IM-6"]}},{"id":"IM-C22-Q4","stem":"Her neutropenia results partly from removal of antibody-coated neutrophils in her enlarged spleen. In which splenic compartment are these coated cells mainly engulfed by macrophages?","options":["Periarteriolar lymphoid sheath","Germinal centers of the white pulp","Splenic cords of the red pulp","Trabecular veins"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the target: IgG-coated blood cells that must be filtered out of circulating blood.","Recall that in the open circulation, blood is released into the splenic cords, which are packed with macrophages.","Choose the red pulp cords as the site where opsonized cells are removed."],"why":"Splenic red pulp is made of the cords of Billroth and the venous sinusoids. Blood from penicillar arterioles empties into the cords, a loose meshwork full of macrophages, and must squeeze through slits in the sinusoid walls to return to the circulation. Macrophages in the cords use Fc receptors to grab IgG-coated cells, which is how the spleen clears antibody-coated neutrophils in Felty syndrome and antibody-coated red cells in warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia. The white pulp (T cell sheath, B cell follicles, marginal zone) handles immune responses to blood-borne antigens.","whyNot":["The periarteriolar lymphoid sheath is the T cell zone of the white pulp, wrapped around the central arteriole. It is not the main site for filtering blood cells.","Germinal centers are where activated B cells proliferate and undergo affinity maturation, within white pulp follicles.","Correct.","Trabecular veins carry blood that has already passed through the red pulp out toward the splenic vein. They are lined by endothelium with no filtering macrophages."],"study":{"topics":["Splenic red and white pulp","Fc receptor clearance in the spleen"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-8"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C23","system":"IM","dx":"Pulmonary sarcoidosis with hypercalcemia","codes":["IM-15-C4"],"case":"A 36-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of dry cough, shortness of breath on exertion, and painful red eyes. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Examination shows tender red nodules on both shins and ciliary flush with inflammatory cells in the anterior chamber of both eyes. A chest x-ray shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy with reticular opacities in both upper lobes. Serum calcium is 11.9 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), parathyroid hormone is 9 pg/mL (10 to 65), and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is 92 pg/mL (18 to 72). Transbronchial biopsy shows noncaseating granulomas, and stains for acid-fast bacilli and fungi are negative. Diagnosis: pulmonary sarcoidosis with hypercalcemia.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C23-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional laboratory findings is most likely?","options":["Elevated serum parathyroid hormone-related peptide","Positive cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies","Positive interferon-gamma release assay for tuberculosis","Elevated serum angiotensin-converting enzyme activity"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-C4","obj":"IM-15-C.12","dok":2,"approach":["Pull together the findings: hilar adenopathy, uveitis, erythema nodosum, noncaseating granulomas.","Name the disease: sarcoidosis.","Recall that epithelioid cells in sarcoid granulomas secrete angiotensin-converting enzyme."],"why":"Sarcoidosis is a multisystem disease of noncaseating granulomas, most often in the lungs and hilar nodes. Other common sites are the eyes (anterior uveitis), skin (erythema nodosum), and salivary glands. Epithelioid macrophages in the granulomas produce angiotensin-converting enzyme, so serum ACE is elevated in many patients and roughly tracks the total granuloma burden. Lofgren syndrome, an acute form with erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar adenopathy, and ankle arthritis, usually resolves on its own.","whyNot":["PTH-related peptide causes hypercalcemia with squamous cell carcinoma of the lung. It would not raise 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D or produce noncaseating granulomas.","c-ANCA points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which causes necrotizing granulomas and vasculitis of the airways and kidneys.","A positive assay would suggest tuberculosis, which forms caseating granulomas with acid-fast bacilli. Her stains were negative, and sarcoidosis often blunts T cell responses to tuberculin.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Clinical features of sarcoidosis","Granulomatous lung disease differential"],"comps":["IM-15-C4","IM-10"]}},{"id":"IM-C23-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her hypercalcemia?","options":["Hepatic 25-hydroxylase increased by inflammation","Macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase escaping PTH control","Renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase stimulated by PTH","Macrophage 24-hydroxylase deficiency slowing calcitriol breakdown"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-10","obj":"IM-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note high calcium with suppressed PTH and high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D.","Conclude that calcitriol is being made outside the kidney's normal feedback loop.","Recall that activated granuloma macrophages express 1-alpha-hydroxylase driven by IFN-gamma, without PTH control."],"why":"Normally the kidney's 1-alpha-hydroxylase is switched on by PTH and low phosphate and switched off by calcitriol and high calcium. In sarcoidosis, IFN-gamma activated macrophages in the granulomas express their own 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which ignores these signals. They convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol, which raises intestinal calcium absorption and causes hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria while PTH is suppressed. Inside macrophages, calcitriol also induces the antimicrobial peptide cathelicidin, part of normal innate defense.","whyNot":["Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is loosely regulated and only makes the storage form, 25-hydroxyvitamin D. It does not produce active calcitriol.","Correct.","Her PTH is suppressed, so the kidney's 1-alpha-hydroxylase is turned down. The calcitriol must come from another source.","A lack of 24-hydroxylase raises calcitriol in a rare inherited hypercalcemia of infancy. It is not the mechanism in granulomatous disease."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation and regulation","Hypercalcemia of granulomatous disease"],"comps":["IM-10","IM-15-C4"]}},{"id":"IM-C23-Q3","stem":"The granulomas consist of tight clusters of epithelioid macrophages and multinucleated giant cells. Which of the following signals most directly converts macrophages into these cells?","options":["Interferon-gamma from Th1 CD4 T cells","Interleukin-4 from Th2 CD4 T cells","Interleukin-17 from Th17 CD4 T cells","Interleukin-10 from regulatory T cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-C4","obj":"IM-15-C.11","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the granuloma as a type IV, T cell mediated reaction.","Recall the loop: macrophages make IL-12, which pushes CD4 T cells toward Th1.","Th1 cells secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages into epithelioid and giant cells."],"why":"Granulomatous inflammation is a delayed (type IV) hypersensitivity. Antigen-presenting macrophages release IL-12, which drives naive CD4 T cells to become Th1 cells. Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages into epithelioid cells that may fuse into multinucleated giant cells. TNF from macrophages keeps the granuloma organized, which is why TNF blockers can reactivate latent tuberculosis. Tuberculosis forms caseating granulomas, while sarcoidosis forms noncaseating ones. A tuberculin skin test uses the same pathway and is read as induration at 48 to 72 hours.","whyNot":["Correct.","IL-4 drives IgE class switching and alternatively activated macrophages involved in tissue repair. It does not produce the activated, epithelioid macrophages of a Th1 granuloma like those in sarcoidosis.","IL-17 recruits neutrophils and helps defend against extracellular bacteria and fungi. It is not the main driver of granulomas.","IL-10 suppresses macrophage activation and limits inflammation. It works against granuloma formation."],"study":{"topics":["Type IV granulomatous inflammation","Th1 cytokines"],"comps":["IM-15-C4","IM-10"]}},{"id":"IM-C23-Q4","stem":"An enlarged hilar lymph node is sampled by endobronchial ultrasound. In a normal lymph node, most lymphocytes entering from the blood cross the vessel wall at which site?","options":["Medullary sinuses, through the efferent lymphatic","Subcapsular sinus, through afferent lymphatics","Cortical follicles, through germinal center capillaries","Paracortex, through high endothelial venules"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the zones of a lymph node: cortex with follicles, paracortex, and medulla.","Identify the special vessel for lymphocyte entry: the high endothelial venule.","Locate high endothelial venules in the paracortex, the T cell zone."],"why":"A lymph node has an outer cortex with B cell follicles and germinal centers, a deeper paracortex rich in T cells, and a medulla of cords and sinuses. Lymph enters through afferent lymphatics into the subcapsular sinus and leaves through the efferent lymphatic at the hilum. Blood lymphocytes enter through high endothelial venules in the paracortex, using L-selectin to bind the tall endothelium. T cells stay in the paracortex, and B cells move on to the follicles.","whyNot":["The efferent lymphatic is the exit. Lymphocytes leave the node through the medullary sinuses and efferent vessel at the hilum.","Afferent lymphatics carry lymph, dendritic cells, and antigen from tissues into the subcapsular sinus. They are not where blood lymphocytes enter.","Follicles hold B cells and germinal centers, but blood lymphocytes do not enter through capillaries there. B cells also enter at the paracortical venules and then move to the follicles.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lymph node microanatomy","Lymphocyte homing"],"comps":["IM-2"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C24","system":"IM","dx":"Diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis with scleroderma renal crisis","codes":["IM-15-D1"],"case":"A 44-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of severe headache and blurred vision for 1 day. Over 8 months she has had progressive skin tightening of her fingers, arms, chest, and face, with Raynaud phenomenon, heartburn, and dysphagia. Two weeks ago she began taking prednisone for joint pain. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 204/122 mm Hg. Examination shows thick, bound-down skin from the fingers to the upper arms and over the chest. Serum creatinine is 2.6 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1); it was 0.8 mg/dL 1 month ago. Hemoglobin is 9.4 g/dL (12 to 16), platelet count is 96,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000), and the blood smear shows schistocytes. Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) antibodies are positive. Diagnosis: diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis with scleroderma renal crisis.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C24-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her difficulty swallowing both solids and liquids along with heartburn?","options":["Loss of myenteric inhibitory neurons with high sphincter pressure","Smooth muscle fibrosis with low sphincter pressure","Eosinophilic infiltration of the mucosa with esophageal rings","Inflammation of striated muscle in the upper esophagus"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-15-D1","obj":"IM-15-D.1","dok":3,"approach":["Connect skin fibrosis to the same process in the gut wall.","Recall that the lower two-thirds of the esophagus is smooth muscle and is the part affected.","Predict weak peristalsis (dysphagia for solids and liquids) and a weak lower esophageal sphincter (reflux)."],"why":"Systemic sclerosis starts with endothelial injury and autoimmunity. Activated T cells and macrophages release TGF-beta and other mediators that push fibroblasts to lay down excess collagen, while damaged small arteries cause ischemia (Raynaud phenomenon). In the esophagus, the smooth muscle of the lower two-thirds atrophies and is replaced by collagen. Peristalsis weakens and the lower sphincter loses tone, so food sticks and acid refluxes, sometimes leading to Barrett esophagus. The same fibrosis in the lungs causes interstitial lung disease.","whyNot":["Loss of inhibitory neurons is achalasia. The sphincter stays tight, which blocks reflux, so heartburn is not a feature.","Correct.","Eosinophilic esophagitis causes food impaction and rings in atopic patients. It does not replace the muscle layer with collagen.","Inflammatory myopathies such as polymyositis weaken the striated upper third and cause trouble starting a swallow, with nasal regurgitation."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis","Esophageal motility disorders"],"comps":["IM-15-D1"]}},{"id":"IM-C24-Q2","stem":"Which of the following features of her disease placed her at greatest risk of this renal complication?","options":["Rapid skin thickening of trunk and proximal limbs","Calcinosis cutis and telangiectasias on the fingers and face","Raynaud phenomenon present for many years before skin changes","Skin thickening limited to the fingers, hands, and face"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-D1","obj":"IM-15-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the renal crisis: abrupt severe hypertension, rising creatinine, and microangiopathic hemolysis.","Recall that renal crisis occurs mainly in diffuse disease, usually in the first few years.","Pick the feature that defines diffuse disease: rapidly spreading skin thickening proximal to the elbows and onto the trunk."],"why":"Systemic sclerosis is divided by how far skin thickening spreads. Diffuse cutaneous disease involves skin above the elbows or knees and the trunk, progresses quickly, and brings early visceral disease such as interstitial lung disease and scleroderma renal crisis. It is associated with anti-Scl-70 (and anti-RNA polymerase III) antibodies. Limited cutaneous disease (CREST: calcinosis, Raynaud, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, telangiectasia) stays distal, progresses slowly, carries anticentromere antibodies, and leads to pulmonary hypertension. Glucocorticoids, especially at doses of about 15 mg/day of prednisone or more, also raise the risk of renal crisis, so her recent prednisone matters too.","whyNot":["Correct.","Calcinosis and telangiectasia are part of CREST (limited) disease. Its main late visceral threat is pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Years of Raynaud before skin changes is the usual course of limited cutaneous disease, which carries a low risk of renal crisis.","Skin disease distal to the elbows and knees, with or without the face, defines limited cutaneous disease, which rarely causes renal crisis."],"study":{"topics":["Diffuse versus limited systemic sclerosis","Scleroderma renal crisis"],"comps":["IM-15-D1"]}},{"id":"IM-C24-Q3","stem":"At her first visit, before serologic results were available, mixed connective tissue disease was also considered. Which of the following findings would most have supported that diagnosis?","options":["Antihistone antibodies after starting hydralazine","Anti-dsDNA antibodies with low C3 and C4","High-titer anti-U1 RNP with features of myositis","Anti-RNA polymerase III antibodies with rapid skin spread"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-D","obj":"IM-15-D.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that mixed connective tissue disease blends features of SLE, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis.","Recall the defining serology: high-titer anti-U1 ribonucleoprotein antibodies.","Choose the option that combines this antibody with overlap features such as myositis."],"why":"Mixed connective tissue disease is an overlap syndrome. Patients typically have Raynaud phenomenon, puffy swollen hands, arthritis, and features borrowed from SLE, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis, such as proximal muscle weakness with a raised creatine kinase. The defining laboratory finding is a high titer of anti-U1 RNP antibodies, usually with a speckled ANA. Her anti-Scl-70 antibodies and widespread skin thickening point instead to diffuse systemic sclerosis.","whyNot":["Antihistone antibodies after hydralazine, procainamide, or isoniazid suggest drug-induced lupus.","Anti-dsDNA with low complement is specific for active systemic lupus erythematosus, often with nephritis.","Correct.","Anti-RNA polymerase III marks diffuse systemic sclerosis with a high risk of renal crisis. It would support her final diagnosis."],"study":{"topics":["Mixed connective tissue disease","Autoantibodies in connective tissue disease"],"comps":["IM-15-D","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C24-Q4","stem":"Her anti-topoisomerase I antibodies are high-affinity, class-switched IgG directed against a nuclear protein. Production of these antibodies most directly requires which of the following?","options":["T-independent B cell activation by repeating self epitopes","Cross-reaction of streptococcal M protein antibodies with self","Polyclonal B cell activation by bacterial lipopolysaccharide","Loss of tolerance in autoreactive CD4 helper T cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-13","obj":"IM-13.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note that the antibodies are high-affinity IgG against a protein antigen.","Recall that class switching and affinity maturation need CD4 T cell help through CD40L and cytokines.","Conclude that self-reactive helper T cells must have escaped central and peripheral tolerance."],"why":"Self-reactive lymphocytes are normally removed in the thymus and bone marrow (central tolerance) or silenced in the tissues by anergy, regulatory T cells, and deletion (peripheral tolerance). High-affinity IgG against a protein antigen can only form when a B cell gets help from a CD4 T cell that recognizes peptides from the same antigen. So autoreactive helper T cells must escape tolerance, a failure made more likely by HLA susceptibility genes and tissue damage that exposes hidden self antigens. Molecular mimicry, as with streptococcal M protein in rheumatic fever and Campylobacter jejuni in Guillain-Barre syndrome, is another way tolerance can break.","whyNot":["T-independent activation by repetitive antigens mainly gives low-affinity IgM. It does not drive class switching and affinity maturation.","Cross-reaction with M protein is the molecular mimicry of rheumatic fever. It does not target topoisomerase I.","Lipopolysaccharide can activate many B cells at once, but this gives mostly low-affinity IgM and no germinal center maturation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Central and peripheral tolerance","T cell help in autoantibody production"],"comps":["IM-13","IM-5"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C25","system":"IM","dx":"Acute rheumatic fever with carditis","codes":["IM-15-D2"],"case":"A 10-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of fever and painful joints for 6 days. Pain and swelling began in the right knee, moved to the left ankle, and now involve the right wrist. He had an untreated sore throat about 3 weeks ago. Temperature is 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 108/64 mm Hg. Examination shows a swollen, tender right wrist and a grade 3/6 holosystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. An ECG shows a PR interval of 0.22 seconds (0.10 to 0.18 for age). Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 92 mm/h (0 to 20), C-reactive protein is 8.6 mg/dL (less than 0.5), and the antistreptolysin O titer is 720 Todd units (less than 200). A throat culture is negative. Diagnosis: acute rheumatic fever with carditis.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C25-Q1","stem":"Endomyocardial tissue from this patient would show Aschoff bodies containing Anitschkow cells. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the injury to his heart?","options":["Antibodies to M protein binding cardiac myosin","Immune complexes of streptolysin O deposited in the valves","Superantigen-driven polyclonal T cell activation","Direct invasion of the endocardium by streptococci"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-D2","obj":"IM-15-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the 2 to 4 week delay after untreated streptococcal pharyngitis and the sterile inflammation.","Recall that M protein shares epitopes with cardiac myosin and other heart proteins.","Classify the injury as type II hypersensitivity caused by molecular mimicry."],"why":"Acute rheumatic fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 4 weeks. Antibodies and CD4 T cells made against streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin, valve tissue, and other self proteins, a type II hypersensitivity reaction driven by molecular mimicry. The result is pancarditis. Aschoff bodies are foci of fibrinoid change with lymphocytes and plasma cells, and Anitschkow cells are activated macrophages with caterpillar-shaped nuclei.","whyNot":["Correct.","Immune complex deposition is a type III mechanism, as in poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. Rheumatic carditis is driven by cross-reacting antibodies and T cells.","Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins act as superantigens in streptococcal toxic shock syndrome, which causes shock within hours, with no delay of weeks.","Direct bacterial invasion is infective endocarditis. His throat culture is negative and the injury came weeks after the infection cleared."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of rheumatic fever","Type II hypersensitivity"],"comps":["IM-15-D2","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C25-Q2","stem":"If he has recurrent episodes over the next 20 years, which of the following valve lesions is he most likely to develop?","options":["Mitral prolapse from myxomatous degeneration","Mitral stenosis from commissural fusion","Tricuspid regurgitation from septic vegetations","Aortic stenosis from calcification of a bicuspid valve"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-D2","obj":"IM-15-D.4","dok":3,"approach":["Confirm the diagnosis with the Jones criteria: two major criteria (migratory polyarthritis, carditis) plus evidence of streptococcal infection.","Recall that the acute lesion is mitral regurgitation from valvulitis.","Predict the chronic lesion: repeated attacks scar and fuse the mitral commissures, causing stenosis."],"why":"The diagnosis rests on evidence of recent streptococcal infection plus two major Jones criteria, or one major and two minor. The major criteria are migratory polyarthritis, carditis, subcutaneous nodules, erythema marginatum, and Sydenham chorea. Fever, a raised ESR or CRP, and a prolonged PR interval are minor. Acute carditis usually causes mitral regurgitation. Over years, especially with repeat infections, healing leaves thickened leaflets, fused commissures, and short, thick chordae, producing fish-mouth mitral stenosis.","whyNot":["Myxomatous degeneration causes floppy leaflets with a midsystolic click, often with connective tissue disorders. It is not a postinflammatory scar.","Correct.","Tricuspid vegetations suggest infective endocarditis in people who inject drugs. Rheumatic disease rarely affects the right-sided valves alone.","A calcified bicuspid aortic valve is a congenital problem that becomes stenotic by middle age. It is not a result of rheumatic fever."],"study":{"topics":["Jones criteria","Chronic rheumatic heart disease"],"comps":["IM-15-D2"]}},{"id":"IM-C25-Q3","stem":"The mechanism that links his sore throat to his heart disease also explains which of the following conditions?","options":["Toxic shock syndrome from a retained vaginal tampon","Glomerulonephritis 3 weeks after streptococcal impetigo","Guillain-Barre syndrome after Campylobacter enteritis","Polyarteritis nodosa in chronic hepatitis B infection"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-13","obj":"IM-13.5","dok":2,"approach":["Name the mechanism in rheumatic fever: molecular mimicry.","Define it: an immune response to a microbial antigen cross-reacts with a similar host antigen.","Find the option with the same mechanism: Campylobacter lipooligosaccharide mimics peripheral nerve gangliosides."],"why":"Molecular mimicry happens when a microbe carries an antigen that looks like a host molecule. The immune response that clears the infection then attacks the look-alike self tissue. In rheumatic fever, M protein resembles cardiac myosin. In Guillain-Barre syndrome after Campylobacter jejuni enteritis, antibodies to bacterial lipooligosaccharide cross-react with gangliosides on peripheral nerves, causing ascending paralysis. Other routes to autoimmunity include failed central or peripheral tolerance and HLA susceptibility.","whyNot":["Toxic shock syndrome is caused by TSST-1, a superantigen that activates many T cells at once without any cross-reaction with self.","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis follows the same organism, but it is caused by immune complexes deposited in glomeruli (type III). No cross-reacting antibody is involved.","Correct.","Hepatitis B associated polyarteritis nodosa is caused by deposits of viral antigen and antibody complexes in vessel walls."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular mimicry","Mechanisms of autoimmunity"],"comps":["IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C25-Q4","stem":"Which of the following mediators acts directly in the hypothalamus to raise his temperature set point?","options":["Bradykinin cleaved from kininogen","Prostaglandin E2 made by COX-2","Leukotriene B4 made by neutrophils","Histamine released by mast cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha are the pyrogenic cytokines of the acute phase response.","Recall that they induce COX-2 in hypothalamic vessels, producing PGE2.","PGE2 raises the set point, which explains why NSAIDs reduce fever."],"why":"IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha from activated macrophages drive the acute phase response. In the brain, they induce COX-2 in the vascular endothelium of the hypothalamus, which makes prostaglandin E2. PGE2 acts on neurons in the preoptic area to raise the temperature set point, so the body conserves and makes heat until the new set point is reached. The same cytokines make the liver produce CRP and fibrinogen, which raises his CRP and ESR.","whyNot":["Bradykinin increases vascular permeability and causes pain. It is not the final signal for fever.","Correct.","Leukotriene B4 is made by the lipoxygenase pathway and attracts neutrophils. Fever comes from the cyclooxygenase pathway.","Histamine causes vasodilation, itching, and edema in type I reactions. It does not reset the hypothalamic thermostat."],"study":{"topics":["Mechanism of fever","Acute phase cytokines"],"comps":["IM-6"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C26","system":"IM","dx":"Primary Sjogren syndrome in a mother whose newborn has congenital heart block","codes":["IM-15-D3"],"case":"A 32-year-old woman comes to the physician for a postpartum visit. For 2 years she has had a dry mouth, a gritty feeling in both eyes, and intermittent swelling of both parotid glands. A Schirmer test shows 3 mm of wetting in 5 minutes (10 mm or more). Antinuclear antibodies are positive, with anti-Ro (SSA) and anti-La (SSB) antibodies, and rheumatoid factor is positive. A labial salivary gland biopsy shows focal lymphocytic sialadenitis. Ten days ago she delivered a boy at 38 weeks' gestation whose heart rate was 54/min, and an ECG of the infant showed complete atrioventricular block. Diagnosis: primary Sjogren syndrome in the mother, with congenital heart block in her newborn.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C26-Q1","stem":"Six years later she develops persistent, firm enlargement of the right parotid gland. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Primary effusion lymphoma of the pleural cavity","Mantle cell lymphoma of the gastrointestinal tract","Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma of the mediastinum","Marginal zone B cell lymphoma of the parotid"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-D3","obj":"IM-15-D.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that Sjogren syndrome causes chronic lymphocytic infiltration of the salivary glands.","Persistent B cell stimulation in this mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue raises the risk of a clonal B cell tumor.","Name the tumor: extranodal marginal zone (MALT) lymphoma, often in the parotid."],"why":"In Sjogren syndrome, CD4 T cells and B cells infiltrate the salivary and lacrimal glands and destroy the acini, causing dry mouth and keratoconjunctivitis sicca. B cells are chronically activated, which shows up as anti-Ro, anti-La, rheumatoid factor, and hypergammaglobulinemia. This ongoing stimulation raises the risk of B cell lymphoma, most often extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of the parotid. Persistent unilateral gland enlargement warrants evaluation for lymphoma. Sjogren syndrome can be primary or occur with rheumatoid arthritis or SLE.","whyNot":["Primary effusion lymphoma is linked to HHV-8 in immunosuppressed patients and presents as a body cavity effusion.","Mantle cell lymphoma comes from naive B cells with a t(11;14) translocation. It is not linked to Sjogren syndrome and does not typically arise in the parotid.","Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma typically presents as a mediastinal mass in young adults and is not driven by chronic salivary inflammation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Sjogren syndrome complications","Marginal zone lymphoma"],"comps":["IM-15-D3"]}},{"id":"IM-C26-Q2","stem":"Which of the following additional findings is her son most likely to develop during the first weeks of life?","options":["Transient annular erythematous rash of the face","Cataracts with a patent ductus arteriosus","Hypocalcemic seizures with an absent thymic shadow","Chorioretinitis with periventricular calcifications"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-D3","obj":"IM-15-D.6","dok":3,"approach":["Link the mother's anti-Ro antibodies to neonatal lupus.","Recall that neonatal lupus includes permanent heart block and transient skin, liver, and blood findings.","Predict the annular, light-sensitive rash that fades as maternal antibody is cleared."],"why":"Anti-Ro (SSA) IgG from a mother with Sjogren syndrome or SLE crosses the placenta and binds fetal tissues. In the fetal heart, it causes inflammation and fibrosis of the conduction system between about 16 and 24 weeks, producing congenital heart block, which is permanent. The same antibodies cause neonatal lupus in the skin: an annular, erythematous rash, often around the eyes and on the face, that appears in the first weeks and fades by about 6 to 8 months as maternal IgG is cleared. Liver enzyme elevation and cytopenias can also occur and are transient.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cataracts, deafness, and patent ductus arteriosus are congenital rubella syndrome.","Hypocalcemia with an absent thymus and heart defects is DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion). It is a developmental defect of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches.","Chorioretinitis with periventricular calcifications is congenital cytomegalovirus infection."],"study":{"topics":["Neonatal lupus","Anti-Ro antibodies in pregnancy"],"comps":["IM-15-D3","IM-5"]}},{"id":"IM-C26-Q3","stem":"Her dry mouth also reduces the delivery of secretory IgA to the oral mucosa. In mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, IgA responses begin when which of the following cells carries luminal antigen to the underlying lymphoid follicles?","options":["Paneth cells at the base of the crypts","Goblet cells scattered along the villi","Enteroendocrine cells within the crypts","M cells over lymphoid follicles"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that secretory IgA is made by plasma cells primed in mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue.","Identify the cell in the follicle-associated epithelium that samples the lumen.","Name the M cell, which transcytoses antigen to dendritic cells and lymphocytes beneath it."],"why":"Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue includes the tonsils, Peyer patches in the ileum, and scattered follicles in other mucosae. The epithelium over these follicles contains M cells, which take up antigens and microbes from the lumen and pass them to dendritic cells and lymphocytes below. With TGF-beta and T cell help, B cells switch to IgA, then home to mucosae and glands, including the salivary glands. There, plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA joined by J chain, which the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor carries across the epithelium as secretory IgA.","whyNot":["Paneth cells sit at the base of small intestinal crypts and secrete defensins and lysozyme. They do not transport antigen.","Goblet cells secrete mucus that forms a barrier over the epithelium. They are not the main antigen-sampling cells of lymphoid follicles.","Enteroendocrine cells release hormones such as cholecystokinin and secretin. They have no role in delivering antigen to lymphoid tissue.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","Secretory IgA"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-5"]}},{"id":"IM-C26-Q4","stem":"The antibody that damaged her son's conduction system reached the fetus in which of the following ways?","options":["IgE, carried to the fetus bound to maternal mast cells","IgM, which as a pentamer diffuses across the placenta","IgG, carried across the placenta by FcRn","Secretory IgA, carried across by the poly-Ig receptor"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that only one isotype crosses the placenta.","Name the receptor: the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) on syncytiotrophoblast.","Apply it: maternal anti-Ro IgG enters fetal blood and reaches the fetal heart."],"why":"IgG is the only isotype that crosses the placenta. Syncytiotrophoblast cells take up maternal IgG and move it into fetal blood using the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), which gives newborns passive protection, and also transfers harmful autoantibodies such as anti-Ro. IgM is the pentameric first-response antibody and the strongest complement activator. Secretory IgA is a dimer in mucosal secretions and breast milk, IgE arms mast cells, and IgD is a receptor on naive B cells.","whyNot":["IgE sits on mast cells and basophils through FcepsilonRI and mediates allergy. It is not transferred to the fetus in any meaningful amount.","IgM is a large pentamer and cannot cross the placenta. IgM in a newborn means the fetus made it, as in congenital infection.","Correct.","The polymeric immunoglobulin receptor moves IgA across mucosal epithelium into secretions such as breast milk. It does not carry IgA across the placenta."],"study":{"topics":["Immunoglobulin isotypes","Placental transfer of IgG"],"comps":["IM-5"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C27","system":"IM","dx":"Primary (AL) amyloidosis with nephrotic syndrome and restrictive cardiomyopathy","codes":["IM-15-E1"],"case":"A 63-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue, leg swelling, shortness of breath on exertion, and foamy urine for 4 months. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 104/68 mm Hg. Examination shows macroglossia with tooth indentations, purpura around both eyes, jugular venous distention, and 3+ pitting edema of both legs. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (3.5 to 5.5), and 24-hour urine protein is 7.8 g (less than 0.15). An ECG shows low voltage. Echocardiography shows thickened ventricular walls, normal-sized ventricular cavities, and impaired diastolic filling. Serum free lambda light chain is 410 mg/L (5.7 to 26.3). A bone marrow biopsy shows 12% clonal lambda-restricted plasma cells. An abdominal fat pad aspirate shows extracellular, amorphous, pink material. Diagnosis: primary (AL) amyloidosis with nephrotic syndrome and restrictive cardiomyopathy.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C27-Q1","stem":"Which of the following stain findings would confirm the nature of the material in the fat pad aspirate?","options":["Magenta staining with periodic acid-Schiff that resists diastase","Blue granular staining with Prussian blue under bright light","Orange-red droplets with oil red O on frozen section","Apple-green birefringence with Congo red under polarized light"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-15-E1","obj":"IM-15-E.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the clinical picture of amyloidosis: nephrotic syndrome, restrictive heart, macroglossia, periorbital purpura.","Recall that amyloid fibrils are proteins folded into cross-beta-pleated sheets.","Apply the stain: Congo red binds the ordered fibrils and shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light."],"why":"Amyloid is not one protein. It is any of several misfolded proteins that stack into rigid fibrils with a cross-beta-pleated sheet structure. The fibrils are deposited outside cells, where they resist breakdown and gradually replace normal tissue. Under ordinary light, amyloid is amorphous, glassy, and pink on H&E. Congo red dye lines up along the regular fibrils, so under polarized light the deposits show apple-green birefringence. An abdominal fat pad aspirate is a simple first biopsy site; organ biopsy is used when the fat pad is negative.","whyNot":["Diastase-resistant PAS staining marks glycoproteins and mucins, as in alpha1-antitrypsin globules or Whipple disease. It is not specific for amyloid.","Prussian blue stains iron in hemosiderin, as in hemochromatosis or old hemorrhage.","Oil red O stains neutral lipid, as in fatty change. Amyloid is protein and does not take up lipid stains.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Structure of amyloid","Congo red staining"],"comps":["IM-15-E1","IM-15-E2"]}},{"id":"IM-C27-Q2","stem":"Which of the following additional findings is most likely to develop from the same disease process?","options":["Bronze skin hyperpigmentation with diabetes mellitus","Numbness of the thumb and index finger at night","Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy with anterior uveitis","Inflammatory back pain with sacroiliac joint fusion"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-E1","obj":"IM-15-E.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify AL amyloidosis: clonal lambda plasma cells and organ deposits.","List the typical sites of AL deposits: kidney, heart, tongue, skin vessels, peripheral nerves, and the carpal tunnel.","Predict median nerve compression at the wrist, which causes numbness of the thumb and index finger, worse at night."],"why":"In primary (AL) amyloidosis, a clone of plasma cells secretes excess light chains, more often lambda than kappa. It is a plasma cell dyscrasia and may occur with multiple myeloma. The light chains misfold and deposit in many organs. Glomerular deposits cause nephrotic syndrome. Myocardial deposits stiffen the ventricles and cause restrictive cardiomyopathy with low ECG voltage. Deposits in the tongue cause macroglossia, and fragile vessels in the skin bleed as periorbital purpura. Deposits in the flexor retinaculum compress the median nerve and cause carpal tunnel syndrome, and peripheral and autonomic neuropathy are common.","whyNot":["Bronze skin with diabetes is hereditary hemochromatosis, which can also cause a cardiomyopathy. Light chain deposits do not cause pigmentation.","Correct.","Hilar adenopathy with uveitis is sarcoidosis, a granulomatous disease without monoclonal light chains.","Sacroiliac fusion with inflammatory back pain is ankylosing spondylitis. Long-standing ankylosing spondylitis is a cause of AA amyloidosis and has no link to light chain deposits."],"study":{"topics":["Clinical features of AL amyloidosis","Plasma cell dyscrasias"],"comps":["IM-15-E1"]}},{"id":"IM-C27-Q3","stem":"The fibrils in his tissues are built mainly from N-terminal fragments of the clonal light chain. Which part of the immunoglobulin molecule do these fragments contain?","options":["The heavy chain constant domains that bind Fc receptors","The hinge region joining the Fab arms to the Fc","The variable domain, part of the antigen-binding site","The J chain that links monomers into polymers"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the layout of a light chain: one variable domain at the N-terminus and one constant domain at the C-terminus.","Connect the N-terminal fragment to the variable domain.","Recall that the variable domains of the light and heavy chains together form the antigen-binding site in the Fab region."],"why":"An antibody has two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains joined by disulfide bonds. Each light chain has an N-terminal variable domain and a C-terminal constant domain (kappa or lambda). The variable domains of one light and one heavy chain, with their hypervariable loops, form the antigen-binding site of each Fab arm. The Fc region, made only of heavy chain constant domains, decides isotype functions such as complement fixation and Fc receptor binding. In AL amyloidosis, the unique variable domain sequence of the clone makes the light chain prone to misfold, which is why fibrils are mostly N-terminal variable region fragments.","whyNot":["Fc receptor binding comes from the heavy chain constant region, which is not part of a light chain.","The hinge region is part of the heavy chain, between the first and second constant domains. Light chains have no hinge.","Correct.","The J chain is a separate polypeptide that joins IgM pentamers and IgA dimers. It is not part of a light chain."],"study":{"topics":["Immunoglobulin structure","AL amyloid protein"],"comps":["IM-5","IM-15-E1"]}},{"id":"IM-C27-Q4","stem":"In a normal lymph node, plasma cells that form after germinal center reactions collect in which region before many of them migrate to the bone marrow?","options":["Paracortex around high endothelial venules","Mantle zone surrounding germinal centers","Medullary cords near the hilum","Subcapsular sinus beneath the capsule"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that B cells are activated in the follicles and proliferate in germinal centers.","Follow their progeny inward: plasma cells settle in the medulla.","Choose the medullary cords, which lie between the medullary sinuses."],"why":"In the lymph node cortex, B cells in follicles meet antigen, and with T cell help they form germinal centers, where class switching and affinity maturation take place. Their progeny become plasma cells that move to the medullary cords, strands of tissue between the medullary sinuses near the hilum, where they secrete antibody into the outgoing lymph. Many long-lived plasma cells then travel to the bone marrow. A clone of such marrow plasma cells is the source of the light chains in AL amyloidosis.","whyNot":["The paracortex is the T cell zone where lymphocytes enter from the blood. Plasma cells do not gather there.","The mantle zone is a rim of small naive B cells around a germinal center. Its cells are not antibody-secreting plasma cells.","Correct.","The subcapsular sinus receives lymph from afferent vessels and is lined by macrophages. It is a channel for lymph and does not hold plasma cells."],"study":{"topics":["Lymph node microanatomy","Plasma cell differentiation"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-5"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C28","system":"IM","dx":"Secondary (AA) amyloidosis with nephrotic syndrome in long-standing ankylosing spondylitis","codes":["IM-15-E2"],"case":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of swelling of both legs and foamy urine for 3 months. He has a 25-year history of ankylosing spondylitis with persistent back pain despite NSAID treatment. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 80/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg. Examination shows reduced lumbar flexion, a fixed thoracic kyphosis, and 3+ pitting edema of both legs. Serum albumin is 2.3 g/dL (3.5 to 5.5), serum creatinine is 1.7 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3), C-reactive protein is 6.4 mg/dL (less than 0.5), and 24-hour urine protein is 6.8 g (less than 0.15). Serum and urine immunofixation show no monoclonal protein. A renal biopsy shows glomerular deposits with apple-green birefringence on Congo red stain that react with antibody to serum amyloid A. Diagnosis: secondary (AA) amyloidosis with nephrotic syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C28-Q1","stem":"Which of the following conditions also carries a risk of this same type of amyloidosis?","options":["Familial Mediterranean fever with frequent attacks","Medullary carcinoma of the thyroid gland","Multiple myeloma secreting a lambda light chain paraprotein","Alzheimer disease with cortical neuritic plaques"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-E2","obj":"IM-15-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the amyloid type from the biopsy: serum amyloid A, so AA amyloidosis.","Recall that serum amyloid A is an acute phase protein made by the liver during sustained inflammation.","Pick another condition with long-standing or recurrent systemic inflammation."],"why":"Secondary (AA) amyloidosis comes from serum amyloid A, an acute phase protein that the liver makes in large amounts under the influence of IL-1, IL-6, and TNF. When inflammation lasts for years, as in rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, inflammatory bowel disease, chronic infections such as osteomyelitis or tuberculosis, or familial Mediterranean fever, SAA levels stay high. Incomplete breakdown by macrophages leaves AA fragments that form fibrils. The kidney is the main target, and the usual presentation is nephrotic syndrome. Controlling the underlying inflammation lowers SAA and can slow the disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Medullary thyroid carcinoma deposits amyloid made from calcitonin locally within the tumor stroma.","Myeloma can cause amyloidosis, but its deposits are made of light chains (AL type).","Alzheimer plaques contain A-beta amyloid cleaved from amyloid precursor protein. They do not involve serum amyloid A."],"study":{"topics":["AA amyloidosis","Causes of chronic inflammation"],"comps":["IM-15-E2","IM-6"]}},{"id":"IM-C28-Q2","stem":"His kidney disease progresses, and he receives hemodialysis for 12 years. He then develops bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome, shoulder pain with amyloid in the synovium, and cystic lesions of the carpal bones. These new deposits do not react with antibody to serum amyloid A. Which of the following proteins most likely forms them?","options":["Beta2-microglobulin","Transthyretin","Islet amyloid polypeptide","Immunoglobulin lambda light chain"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-15-E","obj":"IM-15-E.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: long-term hemodialysis.","Recall that beta2-microglobulin, the light chain of MHC class I, is normally filtered by the kidney and is poorly removed by many dialysis membranes.","Match its typical sites: carpal tunnel, synovium, and bone cysts."],"why":"Different amyloid proteins appear in different settings. Beta2-microglobulin, part of every MHC class I molecule, builds up in patients on long-term dialysis and deposits in joints, tendons, and bone, causing carpal tunnel syndrome and cystic bone lesions. Transthyretin forms amyloid in the hearts of older adults (wild-type) and in hereditary forms. A-beta forms plaques in Alzheimer disease, calcitonin deposits in medullary thyroid carcinoma, and amylin deposits in the islets in type 2 diabetes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Wild-type transthyretin deposits in older adults, mainly in the heart, and can cause carpal tunnel syndrome. It does not explain dialysis-related joint and bone cysts.","Amylin (islet amyloid polypeptide) deposits in the pancreatic islets in type 2 diabetes.","Light chain (AL) amyloid needs a plasma cell clone, and his immunofixation found none."],"study":{"topics":["Types of amyloid proteins","Dialysis-related amyloidosis"],"comps":["IM-15-E","IM-3"]}},{"id":"IM-C28-Q3","stem":"Before his kidney disease began, during a flare of spondylitis with a high CRP, which of the following serum proteins would have been decreased because of the inflammation?","options":["Ceruloplasmin","Transferrin","Fibrinogen","Haptoglobin"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that IL-6, IL-1, and TNF shift the liver's protein production during inflammation.","Sort proteins into positive acute phase reactants (rise) and negative ones (fall).","Recall that albumin and transferrin are the classic negative acute phase reactants."],"why":"During the acute phase response, IL-6, IL-1, and TNF-alpha cause hepatocytes to increase production of CRP, serum amyloid A, fibrinogen, haptoglobin, ceruloplasmin, ferritin, and hepcidin. At the same time, the liver makes less albumin and transferrin, the negative acute phase reactants. Lower transferrin and more hepcidin both keep iron away from microbes. The same cytokines also cause fever by raising hypothalamic PGE2.","whyNot":["Ceruloplasmin is a positive acute phase protein. Its level rises during inflammation.","Correct.","Fibrinogen rises in inflammation. Its increase makes red cells stack and is the main reason the ESR goes up.","Haptoglobin is a positive acute phase protein and rises during inflammation unless hemolysis is using it up."],"study":{"topics":["Acute phase reactants","Negative acute phase proteins"],"comps":["IM-6"]}},{"id":"IM-C28-Q4","stem":"His 19-year-old son has 6 months of low back pain and morning stiffness that improve with exercise. The son's inherited risk is most strongly linked to which of the following?","options":["HLA-DR4, a class II molecule recognized by CD4 T cells","HLA-B27, a class II molecule recognized by CD4 T cells","HLA-DR2, a class II molecule recognized by CD4 T cells","HLA-B27, a class I molecule recognized by CD8 T cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-3","obj":"IM-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize inflammatory back pain in a young man whose father has ankylosing spondylitis.","Recall the HLA association of the seronegative spondyloarthropathies: HLA-B27.","Classify HLA-B as MHC class I, which presents peptides to CD8 T cells."],"why":"HLA-B27 is strongly linked to ankylosing spondylitis and the other seronegative spondyloarthropathies: reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, and arthritis with inflammatory bowel disease. HLA-B27 is an MHC class I molecule, made of a heavy chain paired with beta2-microglobulin, found on all nucleated cells, and recognized by CD8 T cells. Most HLA-B27 carriers never develop disease, so the allele raises risk without being enough on its own.","whyNot":["HLA-DR4 is the main susceptibility allele for rheumatoid arthritis, which affects small peripheral joints.","HLA-B is encoded in the class I region. Class II molecules are HLA-DP, -DQ, and -DR, which present to CD4 T cells.","HLA-DR2 is associated with multiple sclerosis and Goodpasture syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["HLA associations with disease","MHC class I and class II"],"comps":["IM-3"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C29","system":"IM","dx":"Parvovirus B19 transient aplastic crisis in a child with sickle cell disease","codes":["IM-15-F1"],"case":"A 6-year-old boy with homozygous sickle cell disease (HbSS) is brought to the emergency department because of increasing fatigue and pallor for 3 days. One week ago he had a low-grade fever and a runny nose. His 8-year-old sister currently has bright red cheeks and a lacy rash on her arms. Temperature is 37.3 °C (99.1 °F), pulse is 138/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 98/58 mm Hg. Examination shows marked pallor, a grade 2/6 systolic flow murmur, and no palpable spleen. Hemoglobin is 3.8 g/dL (his baseline 7.9; reference 11.5 to 14.5), reticulocyte count is 0.2% (his baseline 11%; reference 0.5 to 2.5), leukocyte count is 7,800/mm3 (4,500 to 13,500), and platelet count is 260,000/mm3 (150,000 to 450,000). Serum parvovirus B19 IgM is positive. Diagnosis: transient aplastic crisis caused by parvovirus B19.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C29-Q1","stem":"His mother is at 20 weeks' gestation and has never been infected with this virus. If she becomes infected, her fetus is at greatest risk of which of the following?","options":["Hydrops fetalis from severe fetal anemia","Congenital cataracts with patent ductus arteriosus","Periventricular calcifications with microcephaly","Hydrocephalus with diffuse intracranial calcifications"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-F1","obj":"IM-15-F.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that parvovirus B19 can cross the placenta.","Note that fetal red cells have short lifespans and the fetus is making red cells rapidly, so halting erythropoiesis causes severe anemia.","Predict high-output heart failure with fluid in body cavities: nonimmune hydrops fetalis."],"why":"Parvovirus B19 causes erythema infectiosum (fifth disease) in children: a slapped-cheek rash followed by a lacy, reticular rash on the limbs, as in his sister. Adults more often get a symmetric arthropathy. Because the virus stops red cell production, patients who rely on a high red cell turnover, such as those with sickle cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis, can have a sudden transient aplastic crisis. Infection in pregnancy, especially before about 20 weeks, can cause severe fetal anemia, heart failure, and nonimmune hydrops fetalis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cataracts, deafness, and patent ductus arteriosus are congenital rubella syndrome.","Periventricular calcifications with microcephaly and hearing loss are typical of congenital cytomegalovirus infection.","Hydrocephalus, diffuse calcifications, and chorioretinitis are typical of congenital toxoplasmosis."],"study":{"topics":["Parvovirus B19 syndromes","Congenital infections"],"comps":["IM-15-F1","IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C29-Q2","stem":"Which of the following properties of the causative virus best explains the sudden stop in his red cell production?","options":["Latent infection of B lymphocytes through CD21","Lytic infection of erythroid precursors bearing P antigen","Infection of CD4 helper T cells using the CCR5 coreceptor","Infection of hepatocytes through the bile acid transporter NTCP"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the selective drop in reticulocytes with normal white cells and platelets.","Recall that parvovirus B19 binds the P antigen (globoside) on erythroid progenitors.","Apply its lytic replication in these dividing cells: red cell production halts for about 1 to 2 weeks."],"why":"Parvovirus B19 is a small, nonenveloped, single-stranded DNA virus. It spreads by respiratory droplets, through blood products, and across the placenta. It enters cells through the P blood group antigen (globoside), which is plentiful on erythroid progenitors, and it needs dividing cells to replicate. Infected progenitors are destroyed, so red cell production stops for about 1 to 2 weeks. A healthy person barely notices, but a child with sickle cell disease, whose red cells survive only about 10 to 20 days, quickly becomes severely anemic.","whyNot":["Epstein-Barr virus enters B cells through CD21 and establishes latency, causing mononucleosis. It does not selectively stop red cell production.","Correct.","HIV uses CD4 with CCR5 or CXCR4 to infect helper T cells, causing immunodeficiency over years.","Hepatitis B and D viruses enter hepatocytes through the sodium taurocholate cotransporting polypeptide (NTCP)."],"study":{"topics":["Parvovirus B19 virology","Viral receptors and tropism"],"comps":["IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C29-Q3","stem":"His peripheral blood smear is most likely to show which of the following findings that reflects his loss of splenic function?","options":["Basophilic stippling","Schistocytes","Howell-Jolly bodies","Teardrop cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-8","obj":"IM-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the nonpalpable spleen in a 6-year-old with HbSS: repeated infarcts have caused autosplenectomy.","Recall that the spleen normally removes nuclear remnants from red cells as they pass through the red pulp.","Predict persistent nuclear remnants: Howell-Jolly bodies."],"why":"In sickle cell disease, repeated infarction shrinks the spleen until it no longer works, usually by early childhood. Without splenic function, red cells keep inclusions the spleen would normally pit out, such as Howell-Jolly bodies (small round DNA remnants). Other signs of asplenia are target cells and a raised platelet count. The bigger clinical threat is sepsis from encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, and Neisseria meningitidis, which is why these children receive vaccines and penicillin prophylaxis.","whyNot":["Basophilic stippling is clumped ribosomal RNA, seen in lead poisoning and thalassemia.","Schistocytes are red cell fragments sheared in small vessels, as in microangiopathic hemolytic anemia or a mechanical valve.","Correct.","Teardrop cells form as red cells squeeze out of a fibrotic marrow, as in myelofibrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Findings in asplenia","Sickle cell disease complications"],"comps":["IM-8","IM-2"]}},{"id":"IM-C29-Q4","stem":"Over years, sickled cells were trapped in his spleen as blood passed through the red pulp, leading to infarction and fibrosis. Which of the following sequences describes the normal path of blood through the spleen?","options":["Trabecular artery, cords, penicillar arterioles, venous sinusoids","Central artery, penicillar arterioles, cords, venous sinusoids","Central artery, venous sinusoids, cords, penicillar arterioles","Penicillar arterioles, central artery, venous sinusoids, cords"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-2","obj":"IM-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Start at the hilum: splenic artery to trabecular arteries to central arteries in the white pulp.","Follow the central artery as it branches into penicillar arterioles in the red pulp.","Recall the open circulation: blood empties into the cords, then squeezes through slits into the venous sinusoids and on to the pulp and trabecular veins."],"why":"Blood enters the spleen through the splenic artery, which branches into trabecular arteries and then central arteries. Each central artery is wrapped in the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath of the white pulp. In the red pulp it divides into penicillar arterioles, most of which empty into the open meshwork of the splenic cords. To return to the circulation, red cells must squeeze through narrow slits between the endothelial cells of the venous sinusoids, then drain into pulp veins, trabecular veins, and the splenic vein. Rigid sickled cells cannot pass these slits, so they pile up in the cords, and repeated congestion leads to infarction and autosplenectomy.","whyNot":["Trabecular arteries lead into central arteries in the white pulp. They do not open directly into the cords.","Correct.","This sequence puts the venous sinusoids before the cords. Blood leaves the arterioles into the cords first and only then enters the sinusoids.","The central artery runs through the white pulp before it branches into penicillar arterioles. This order reverses those steps."],"study":{"topics":["Splenic circulation","Autosplenectomy in sickle cell disease"],"comps":["IM-2","IM-8"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C30","system":"IM","dx":"Haemophilus influenzae type b epiglottitis in an unvaccinated child","codes":["IM-15-F2"],"case":"A 3-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department 6 hours after the sudden onset of high fever and sore throat. She has not received any childhood vaccinations. Temperature is 39.9 °C (103.8 °F), pulse is 160/min, respirations are 36/min, and blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg. She is sitting upright, leaning forward with her neck extended, and drooling. Her voice is muffled, and she has soft inspiratory stridor. A lateral neck radiograph shows a swollen epiglottis (thumb sign). In the operating room, after her airway is secured, the epiglottis appears cherry red and swollen. Blood cultures grow gram-negative coccobacilli. Diagnosis: epiglottitis caused by Haemophilus influenzae type b.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C30-Q1","stem":"Her 5-year-old cousin, who is fully vaccinated against this organism, develops acute otitis media, and culture of the middle ear fluid grows Haemophilus influenzae. Which of the following best explains the infection despite vaccination?","options":["Antigenic drift in the type b capsular polysaccharide","Cleavage of vaccine-induced serum IgG by IgA protease","Loss of maternal antibody after the first 6 months of life","Infection by a nontypeable strain that lacks a capsule"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-F2","obj":"IM-15-F.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the vaccine targets only the type b capsule (polyribosylribitol phosphate).","Recall that most otitis media, sinusitis, and COPD flares due to H influenzae come from nontypeable strains.","Conclude that a strain with no capsule is not covered by the vaccine."],"why":"Haemophilus influenzae type b, with its polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP) capsule, causes invasive disease in unvaccinated young children: epiglottitis with drooling and stridor, meningitis, bacteremia, and pneumonia. Since conjugate vaccines came into use, these infections have become rare. Nontypeable strains have no capsule, stay on mucosal surfaces, and cause otitis media, sinusitis, conjunctivitis, and exacerbations of chronic bronchitis. The vaccine raises antibody only to the type b capsule, so it does not prevent these infections.","whyNot":["Antigenic drift describes small mutations in influenza virus hemagglutinin and neuraminidase. The type b capsule does not drift.","IgA protease cleaves IgA1 at mucosal surfaces. It does not destroy the serum IgG made by the vaccine.","Maternal antibody fades by about 6 months, but a fully vaccinated 5-year-old has already made antibody against the type b capsule.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Invasive Hib disease","Nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae"],"comps":["IM-15-F2","IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C30-Q2","stem":"On sheep blood agar, the organism grows only as tiny colonies next to a streak of Staphylococcus aureus. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Need for hemin and NAD released near the staphylococci","Need for reduced oxygen tension created by staphylococcal growth","Need for carbon dioxide released by staphylococcal metabolism","Need for cysteine and iron supplied by staphylococcal enzymes"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize satellite growth: Haemophilus colonies cluster around a Staphylococcus aureus streak.","Recall that Haemophilus needs factor X (hemin) and factor V (NAD).","Apply it: S aureus lyses red cells to release hemin and secretes NAD, so both factors are available near the streak."],"why":"Haemophilus influenzae is a small gram-negative coccobacillus that needs two growth factors: factor X (hemin) and factor V (NAD). Intact sheep red cells hold the hemin, and the agar contains enzymes that break down NAD, so the organism barely grows on plain blood agar. Near a beta-hemolytic Staphylococcus aureus streak, lysed red cells release hemin and the staphylococci secrete NAD, producing satellite colonies. In the laboratory, the organism is grown on chocolate agar, in which heated, lysed red cells supply both factors. Its key virulence factor is the PRP capsule, and it also makes an IgA protease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Needing low oxygen describes microaerophiles such as Campylobacter and Helicobacter. It does not explain growth right next to a staphylococcal streak.","Extra carbon dioxide helps Neisseria grow, but it is provided by a CO2 incubator. Haemophilus satellite growth depends on specific growth factors.","Cysteine and iron are the growth requirements of Legionella, which is grown on buffered charcoal yeast extract agar."],"study":{"topics":["Haemophilus growth requirements","Culture media for fastidious bacteria"],"comps":["IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C30-Q3","stem":"The vaccine she did not receive links the type b capsular polysaccharide to a protein carrier. Which of the following best explains why this design protects children younger than 2 years?","options":["The carrier protein activates CD8 cytotoxic T cells against the bacteria","The carrier protein acts as a superantigen on many T cells","The polysaccharide cross-links B cell receptors without T cells","Carrier peptides recruit CD4 T cell help for the B cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that plain polysaccharide is a T-independent antigen and is poorly immunogenic before age 2.","Recall that a B cell specific for PRP takes up the whole conjugate and presents carrier peptides on MHC class II.","Apply T cell help: CD40L and cytokines from CD4 T cells drive class switching, affinity maturation, and memory against PRP."],"why":"Polysaccharide antigens such as the PRP capsule cannot be presented on MHC, so on their own they activate B cells only by cross-linking their receptors. This T-independent response is weak in children under 2 years, gives mostly IgM, and leaves little memory. In a conjugate vaccine, a B cell that recognizes PRP takes in the whole conjugate and presents peptides from the protein carrier on MHC class II. A carrier-specific CD4 T cell then provides help through CD40L to CD40 and cytokines. That help starts a germinal center reaction, producing high-affinity, class-switched IgG against PRP and long-lived memory.","whyNot":["Haemophilus is an extracellular bacterium, and protection comes from opsonizing antibody against the capsule. Cytotoxic T cells kill infected host cells.","Superantigens bind outside the peptide groove and activate many T cells at once, causing toxic shock. Vaccine carriers are chosen to avoid this.","Cross-linking without T cells is the T-independent response to plain polysaccharide. Children under 2 respond poorly to it, and it gives mostly IgM and no memory.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["T-dependent and T-independent B cell activation","Conjugate vaccines"],"comps":["IM-5"]}},{"id":"IM-C30-Q4","stem":"A 34-year-old man who had a splenectomy after a motor vehicle collision is also at increased risk of severe infection with this organism. Loss of which of the following functions most directly accounts for this risk?","options":["CD8 cytotoxic T cell killing of virus-infected host cells","Neutrophil oxidative burst killing of catalase-positive bacteria","Removal of opsonized encapsulated bacteria from the blood","Eosinophil major basic protein release against helminths"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-8","obj":"IM-8.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note that Hib, like S pneumoniae and N meningitidis, has a polysaccharide capsule that resists phagocytosis.","Recall that the spleen makes IgM against blood-borne polysaccharides in its marginal zone and clears opsonized bacteria in its red pulp.","Predict overwhelming sepsis from encapsulated bacteria after splenectomy."],"why":"Encapsulated bacteria resist phagocytosis until they are coated with antibody and complement. The spleen is the main place where this happens for organisms in the blood. Marginal zone B cells make rapid IgM against capsular polysaccharides, and macrophages in the red pulp remove the opsonized bacteria. After splenectomy or functional asplenia, these patients can develop overwhelming sepsis from S pneumoniae, Hib, and N meningitidis. Blood smears also show Howell-Jolly bodies, target cells, and thrombocytosis.","whyNot":["Cytotoxic T cells work in lymph nodes and tissues throughout the body. Splenectomy does not leave a person unusually vulnerable to viruses.","A defective oxidative burst is chronic granulomatous disease, which raises the risk of catalase-positive organisms such as S aureus and Aspergillus. Splenectomy does not affect it.","Correct.","Eosinophils defend against helminths in tissues, a role that does not depend on the spleen."],"study":{"topics":["Functions of the spleen","Infections after splenectomy"],"comps":["IM-8","IM-2"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C31","system":"IM","dx":"Influenza A with secondary Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia","codes":["IM-15-F3"],"case":"A 67-year-old woman comes to the emergency department in January because of a 2-day history of fever, productive cough, and shortness of breath. Nine days ago she had the abrupt onset of fever, headache, diffuse myalgias, and a dry cough, and these symptoms had begun to improve by day 5. Temperature is 39.3 °C (102.7 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 88% on room air. Crackles are heard over both lower lung fields. Leukocyte count is 18,600/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000) with 88% neutrophils. Chest x-ray shows bilateral patchy consolidation with several thin-walled cavities. Nasopharyngeal RT-PCR detects influenza A (H3N2). Sputum Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters, and culture grows Staphylococcus aureus. Diagnosis: influenza A with secondary S aureus pneumonia.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C31-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why the bacterial pneumonia developed as she was recovering from the viral illness?","options":["Loss of ciliated airway epithelium and mucociliary clearance","Antibody-mediated lysis of alveolar macrophages","Viral suppression of neutrophil production in the bone marrow","Aspiration of oropharyngeal anaerobes during sleep"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-F3","obj":"IM-15-F.3","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the two-phase course: she improved from influenza, then got worse again with high fever and a productive cough.","That pattern points to a secondary bacterial pneumonia, classically S aureus or S pneumoniae.","Ask what influenza does to the airway: it kills the ciliated columnar cells that clear bacteria."],"why":"Influenza virus infects and kills the ciliated columnar cells of the trachea and bronchi. The mucociliary escalator stops working, and the bare basement membrane gives S aureus and S pneumoniae a place to attach and grow. Viral neuraminidase can also expose bacterial binding sites on the airway surface. The classic story is a patient who starts to recover from influenza and then gets worse again, as she did. S aureus pneumonia after influenza is often necrotizing, which explains the cavities.","whyNot":["Correct.","Influenza does not cause antibodies that lyse macrophages. Macrophage function can be dampened after influenza, but not by antibody-mediated lysis.","Influenza does not suppress the marrow in this way. Her neutrophil count is high, which shows the marrow is responding normally.","Aspiration happens with reduced consciousness or poor swallowing and causes anaerobic, often foul-smelling infection in dependent lung segments. Her illness followed a typical influenza course and grew S aureus."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of influenza","Secondary bacterial pneumonia"],"comps":["IM-15-F3","IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C31-Q2","stem":"She received the seasonal influenza vaccine 14 months ago. Sequencing shows that the hemagglutinin of her H3N2 strain differs from that of the earlier vaccine strain by several single amino acid substitutions in its antibody-binding sites. Which of the following genetic mechanisms most likely produced this change?","options":["Reassortment of genome segments between two influenza strains","Phenotypic mixing of envelope proteins during coinfection","Recombination of viral DNA integrated into host chromosomes","Point mutations by the viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the clues: same subtype (H3N2), a few single amino acid changes.","Small, stepwise changes within one subtype are antigenic drift.","Link drift to its cause: copying errors by an RNA polymerase that lacks proofreading."],"why":"Influenza A has a segmented, negative-sense RNA genome that is copied by its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. This enzyme has no proofreading, so point mutations build up. Mutations that change the antibody-binding sites on hemagglutinin and neuraminidase are selected because they escape existing immunity. This gradual change is antigenic drift. The subtype stays the same, and drift is why the vaccine is updated every year and seasonal epidemics keep coming back.","whyNot":["Reassortment is antigenic shift. When two strains infect one cell, whole genome segments swap, which can give an entirely new hemagglutinin or neuraminidase subtype. Shift causes pandemics, and it changes the subtype instead of making a few amino acid substitutions.","Phenotypic mixing puts one virus's surface proteins around another virus's genome during coinfection. The genome is unchanged, so the change is lost in the next generation.","Influenza is an RNA virus that replicates without a DNA stage and never integrates into host chromosomes. Integration is a feature of retroviruses such as HIV.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Antigenic drift and shift","Influenza virus structure"],"comps":["IM-14","IM-15-F3"]}},{"id":"IM-C31-Q3","stem":"Early in her infection, plasmacytoid dendritic cells in the airway took up influenza virions into endosomes and released large amounts of interferon-alpha. Which of the following receptors most likely detected the viral genome in these cells?","options":["Toll-like receptor 4 with CD14","Toll-like receptor 5","Toll-like receptor 7","Toll-like receptor 9"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the viral genome: influenza has single-stranded RNA.","Recall which pattern recognition receptors sit in endosomes and sense nucleic acids: TLR3, 7, 8, and 9.","Match single-stranded RNA to TLR7, the main sensor in plasmacytoid dendritic cells."],"why":"Influenza has a single-stranded RNA genome. Inside endosomes, TLR7 (and TLR8 in humans) binds single-stranded RNA. Plasmacytoid dendritic cells carry high levels of TLR7 and respond by making large amounts of type I interferon, which puts nearby cells into an antiviral state. Keeping these nucleic acid sensors in endosomes lets them sample material taken up from outside the cell, which helps avoid reacting to the cell's own RNA.","whyNot":["TLR4 with CD14 binds lipopolysaccharide from gram-negative bacteria at the cell surface. It drives the cytokine storm of gram-negative sepsis.","TLR5 binds flagellin, the protein of bacterial flagella, at the cell surface. Viruses have no flagellin.","Correct.","TLR9 is also in endosomes, but it binds unmethylated CpG DNA from bacteria and DNA viruses such as herpesviruses. Influenza has an RNA genome."],"study":{"topics":["Toll-like receptors","Type I interferon"],"comps":["IM-6","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C31-Q4","stem":"Influenza nucleoprotein is made in the cytosol of her infected bronchial epithelial cells, and these same cells then display peptides from it to CD8 T cells. Which of the following steps is required for these peptides to reach the molecules that display them?","options":["Exchange for CLIP in an endosome catalyzed by HLA-DM","Transport into the endoplasmic reticulum by TAP","Uptake of the protein by phagocytosis","Degradation within acidified endolysosomes"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-3","obj":"IM-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the antigen: a viral protein made inside the cell is an endogenous antigen.","Endogenous antigens are presented on MHC class I to CD8 T cells.","Trace the class I path: proteasome, TAP into the endoplasmic reticulum, loading onto class I."],"why":"Viral proteins made inside a cell are presented on MHC class I. The proteasome cuts them into peptides in the cytosol. TAP (transporter associated with antigen processing) pumps the peptides into the endoplasmic reticulum, where they load onto newly made class I molecules (a heavy chain with beta2-microglobulin). The complex travels to the cell surface, and CD8 T cells that recognize it kill the infected cell. Without TAP, class I molecules stay empty and unstable, and few reach the surface.","whyNot":["HLA-DM removes CLIP so class II molecules can load peptides from proteins taken up from outside the cell. That path leads to CD4 T cells.","Correct.","Phagocytosis brings in outside antigen, mainly for class II presentation by antigen-presenting cells. Her epithelial cells made the nucleoprotein themselves.","Acidified endolysosomes digest proteins taken up from outside the cell for class II presentation. Proteins made in the cytosol are cut by the proteasome."],"study":{"topics":["MHC class I antigen processing","TAP transporter"],"comps":["IM-3","IM-5"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C32","system":"IM","dx":"Measles pneumonia in a child with kwashiorkor and vitamin A deficiency","codes":["IM-15-F4"],"case":"A 3-year-old girl is brought to a clinic in a displacement camp during a measles outbreak because of 6 days of fever, cough, runny nose, and red eyes, and 2 days of rapid breathing. A red rash began at the hairline 3 days ago and spread downward. Her diet has been mostly maize porridge for months, and she is unvaccinated. Temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F), pulse is 150/min, respirations are 54/min, and oxygen saturation is 88% on room air. Physical examination shows a confluent maculopapular rash, dry conjunctivae with foamy gray patches, sparse depigmented hair, pitting edema of the feet and legs, a protuberant abdomen with hepatomegaly, and diffuse crackles. Serum albumin is 1.9 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5), and serum retinol is 0.35 µmol/L (deficiency below 0.70). Diagnosis: measles pneumonia in a child with kwashiorkor and vitamin A deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C32-Q1","stem":"She recovers. At age 11 years, she develops declining school performance, personality change, and repetitive myoclonic jerks, followed by progressive dementia. EEG shows periodic high-voltage complexes, and cerebrospinal fluid shows a markedly increased titer of antimeasles antibodies. Which of the following best explains this condition?","options":["Accumulation of a misfolded host prion protein","Persistent infection by a defective measles virus in neurons","Reactivation of latent JC virus in oligodendrocytes","Autoimmune demyelination triggered by measles virus antigens"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-F4","obj":"IM-15-F.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: a slowly progressive neurologic decline years after measles.","High antimeasles antibody in the CSF means measles antigen is still present in the brain.","Name the late complication: subacute sclerosing panencephalitis, from persistent defective virus."],"why":"This is subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE). It appears about 7 to 10 years after measles, most often when infection happened early in childhood. Mutant measles virus persists in neurons and glia. Mutations in the matrix protein and other envelope genes block assembly of complete virions, so the virus spreads from cell to cell and escapes clearance. The brain keeps making antibody against it, which is why CSF antimeasles antibody is so high. Measles vaccination prevents SSPE.","whyNot":["Prion disease such as Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease also causes myoclonus and periodic EEG complexes, but it is not linked to measles and does not raise antimeasles antibody in the CSF.","Correct.","JC virus reactivation causes progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy in people with severe immunosuppression. It does not raise antimeasles antibody.","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis is an autoimmune demyelinating illness that can follow measles, but it begins days to weeks after the rash, not years later."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of measles","Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis"],"comps":["IM-15-F4","IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C32-Q2","stem":"A nasopharyngeal specimen from the child is inoculated into cell culture. Within days, many cells merge into large multinucleated cells containing dozens of nuclei. Which of the following viral proteins directly causes this effect?","options":["Fusion glycoprotein","Hemagglutinin glycoprotein","Neuraminidase glycoprotein","Matrix protein"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the effect: multinucleated cells formed by fusion are syncytia.","Recall that paramyxoviruses carry an F (fusion) protein active at neutral pH.","F protein on the infected cell surface fuses it with neighboring cells."],"why":"Measles virus is an enveloped, nonsegmented, negative-sense RNA paramyxovirus spread by respiratory droplets. Its hemagglutinin binds the cell receptor, and its fusion (F) protein then merges the viral envelope with the cell membrane at neutral pH. F protein displayed on the surface of infected cells also fuses them with neighboring cells, forming syncytia. In tissue these appear as multinucleated giant cells, such as Warthin-Finkeldey cells in lymphoid tissue and the giant cells of measles pneumonia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Hemagglutinin attaches the virus to its cell receptors (CD150 on immune cells and nectin-4 on epithelial cells) and triggers the fusion protein, but it does not merge membranes itself.","Measles virus has no neuraminidase. Mumps virus has a combined hemagglutinin-neuraminidase protein, and influenza has a separate neuraminidase that releases new virions.","Matrix protein lines the inside of the envelope and organizes assembly and budding. It does not fuse cells."],"study":{"topics":["Paramyxovirus structure","Syncytia and giant cells"],"comps":["IM-14","IM-15-F4"]}},{"id":"IM-C32-Q3","stem":"Which of the following changes in her respiratory tract best explains why vitamin A deficiency makes her measles more severe?","options":["Goblet cell hyperplasia with mucus hypersecretion","Impaired hydroxylation of collagen in the airway wall","Absent dynein arms in the cilia of airway cells","Keratinizing squamous metaplasia of airway lining"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-10","obj":"IM-10.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the role of retinoic acid: it controls differentiation of epithelial cells.","Without it, specialized mucus-secreting ciliated epithelium turns into keratinizing squamous epithelium.","Link the loss of mucociliary clearance to more severe pneumonia."],"why":"Retinoic acid, the active form of vitamin A, binds nuclear receptors that guide epithelial differentiation. Without it, the ciliated, mucus-secreting columnar epithelium of the airways is replaced by keratinizing squamous epithelium. Mucociliary clearance fails, so measles pneumonia and secondary bacterial infections are more severe. The same change in the conjunctiva causes xerophthalmia and Bitot spots. Measles also lowers vitamin A levels further, and the WHO recommends vitamin A for children with measles because it lowers mortality.","whyNot":["Goblet cell hyperplasia follows chronic irritation such as smoking and chronic bronchitis. Vitamin A deficiency reduces goblet cells.","Collagen hydroxylation needs vitamin C. Its deficiency causes scurvy, with bleeding gums and poor wound healing.","Absent dynein arms describe primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome), an inherited disorder, not a nutritional one.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin A and epithelial differentiation","Vitamin A in measles"],"comps":["IM-10","IM-15-F4"]}},{"id":"IM-C32-Q4","stem":"During her evaluation, a tuberculin skin test and a Candida skin test are placed. At 72 hours, neither shows any induration. Which of the following best explains these results?","options":["Loss of B cells from bone marrow failure","Thymic atrophy with impaired T cell function","Deficiency of terminal complement components","Absence of mast cells in the dermis"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-10","obj":"IM-10.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the test: recall antigen skin tests measure delayed-type (type IV) hypersensitivity.","No response to common antigens such as Candida means anergy, a failure of T cell immunity.","Link protein-energy malnutrition to thymic atrophy and weak cell-mediated immunity."],"why":"Skin tests with recall antigens measure delayed-type (type IV) hypersensitivity, which needs memory CD4 Th1 cells and macrophages. Loss of these reactions is called anergy. Protein-energy malnutrition is the most common cause of acquired immunodeficiency in the world. It shrinks the thymus, lowers T cell numbers and function, and weakens cell-mediated immunity, so infections such as measles and tuberculosis are more severe. Measles itself also suppresses cell-mediated immunity for weeks, which adds to her risk.","whyNot":["B cells make antibody. Skin test reactions read at 48 to 72 hours are driven by T cells and macrophages, not antibody.","Correct.","Deficiency of C5 to C9 raises the risk of recurrent Neisseria infections. Complement does not drive delayed skin test reactions.","Mast cells drive immediate (type I) reactions, a wheal and flare within minutes. Delayed reactions do not depend on mast cells."],"study":{"topics":["Malnutrition and immunity","Delayed-type hypersensitivity"],"comps":["IM-10","IM-13"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C33","system":"IM","dx":"Infectious mononucleosis complicated by splenic rupture","codes":["IM-15-F5"],"case":"A 17-year-old boy comes to the physician because of 8 days of sore throat, fever, and fatigue. Temperature is 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows enlarged tonsils with gray-white exudate, tender posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, and a spleen tip palpable 3 cm below the left costal margin. Leukocyte count is 15,200/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000) with 62% lymphocytes, many of them atypical. Serum ALT is 118 U/L (normal 10 to 40). A heterophile antibody test is positive. Four days later, he is brought to the emergency department after being struck in the abdomen during a basketball game. He has left upper quadrant pain that radiates to the left shoulder. Pulse is 128/min and blood pressure is 86/52 mm Hg. Ultrasonography shows free intraperitoneal fluid. Diagnosis: infectious mononucleosis complicated by splenic rupture.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C33-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the atypical lymphocytes in his blood?","options":["Plasma cells secreting heterophile antibody","Monocytes activated by viral infection","B cells transformed by the virus they carry","CD8 T cells responding to infected B cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-F5","obj":"IM-15-F.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the virus: EBV, which infects B cells through CD21.","Ask how the body controls infected B cells: a huge cytotoxic T cell response.","Those activated CD8 T cells are the atypical lymphocytes (Downey cells)."],"why":"EBV infects B cells through CD21 and drives them to proliferate. The host answers with a large expansion of cytotoxic CD8 T cells that recognize EBV antigens on the infected B cells. These activated T cells are the atypical lymphocytes: large cells with abundant pale blue cytoplasm that scallops around neighboring red cells. This T cell response causes most of the symptoms and enlarges the lymph nodes, tonsils, liver, and spleen. Giving amoxicillin during the illness often triggers a diffuse rash.","whyNot":["Heterophile antibodies are IgM made by polyclonally activated B cells, but plasma cells are rare in blood and are not the atypical lymphocytes.","Monocytes are not the atypical cells. The name mononucleosis refers to the many mononuclear cells in the blood, which are mostly activated T cells.","Infected B cells are present, but they are few in the blood. The large atypical cells are the T cells that attack them.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Atypical lymphocytes","EBV and B cells"],"comps":["IM-15-F5","IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C33-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his spleen ruptured after minor trauma?","options":["Infarction of the spleen from sickled red cells","Amyloid deposits stiffening the walls of splenic arterioles","Lymphocytic infiltration of a thin, stretched capsule","Abscess formation from septic emboli to the spleen"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-F5","obj":"IM-15-F.6","dok":3,"approach":["Connect the illness to the organ: mononucleosis causes rapid splenic enlargement.","Recall what happens to the capsule: it is stretched thin and infiltrated by lymphocytes.","An enlarged spleen below the rib margin is exposed, so mild trauma can tear it."],"why":"In mononucleosis the spleen enlarges quickly as activated lymphocytes fill the red and white pulp. Lymphocytes also infiltrate the capsule and trabeculae, which become thin and weak. The enlarged spleen extends below the protection of the ribs, so even mild trauma can rupture it, most often in the first 3 weeks of illness. Patients are told to avoid contact sports for at least 3 to 4 weeks. Other EBV complications include hepatitis, autoimmune hemolytic anemia, and EBV-linked cancers such as Burkitt lymphoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, and nasopharyngeal carcinoma.","whyNot":["Infarction from sickled red cells occurs in sickle cell disease and leaves a small, scarred spleen (autosplenectomy), not a swollen one.","Amyloidosis can enlarge the spleen, but nothing in his history suggests a chronic disease that produces amyloid, and his spleen enlarged over days.","Correct.","Septic emboli from endocarditis can cause a splenic abscess with fever and a cavity on imaging. His illness and laboratory findings point to EBV."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of EBV infection","Splenic rupture in mononucleosis"],"comps":["IM-15-F5"]}},{"id":"IM-C33-Q3","stem":"Biopsy of an enlarged tonsil shows scattered infected B cells with condensed chromatin, fragmented nuclei, and intact plasma membranes, each in contact with a CD8 T cell, and no surrounding neutrophils. Which of the following mechanisms most likely caused these changes in the B cells?","options":["Caspase activation by granzymes","Insertion of the C5b-9 membrane attack complex","Lysosomal enzyme release after ATP depletion","Hypochlorous acid formed by myeloperoxidase"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.5","dok":3,"approach":["Read the morphology: condensed chromatin, nuclear fragments, intact membrane, and no inflammation mean apoptosis.","Identify the killer: a CD8 T cell in contact with each target.","Recall how cytotoxic T cells trigger apoptosis: perforin lets granzymes in, and granzymes activate caspases."],"why":"The changes are apoptosis. Cytotoxic CD8 T cells, and NK cells, release perforin, which forms pores in the target cell membrane, and granzymes, which are serine proteases that pass through the pores. Granzyme B cleaves and activates caspases, which take the cell apart from the inside without spilling its contents, so no inflammation follows. CD8 T cells can also trigger apoptosis when their Fas ligand binds Fas on the target, which activates caspase-8.","whyNot":["Correct.","The membrane attack complex punches holes in membranes and causes lysis. It mainly kills Neisseria and other bacteria, and lysed cells do not keep an intact membrane.","ATP depletion with lysosomal enzyme release causes necrosis, with cell swelling, ruptured membranes, and inflammation.","Neutrophils make hypochlorous acid in the respiratory burst to kill ingested microbes. No neutrophils are present, and T cells do not kill this way."],"study":{"topics":["Perforin and granzymes","Apoptosis pathways"],"comps":["IM-5","IM-3"]}},{"id":"IM-C33-Q4","stem":"Before his CD8 T cell response developed, NK cells killed some B cells in which lytic viral replication had reduced surface MHC class I. Which of the following best explains how the NK cells identified these targets?","options":["TCR recognition of viral peptide bound to class I","CD4 binding to viral peptide on MHC class II","Absent inhibitory signal from self class I","Rearranged receptors specific for viral antigens"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-3","obj":"IM-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the key change in the target: less MHC class I on the surface.","Recall that NK cells carry inhibitory receptors that bind self class I.","Loss of class I removes the brake, so activating signals win and the NK cell kills."],"why":"NK cells carry inhibitory receptors, such as killer-cell immunoglobulin-like receptors, that bind self MHC class I. A normal cell sends this signal and is spared. Many viruses, including EBV during lytic replication, and many tumors lower class I to hide from CD8 T cells. These cells then fail to deliver the inhibitory signal, activating receptors on the NK cell take over, and the NK cell releases perforin and granzymes. This is missing self recognition. NK cells need no prior sensitization and act within days of infection.","whyNot":["This is how CD8 T cells recognize targets. It needs class I on the target, which these cells have lost.","CD4 is a coreceptor on helper T cells, which do not directly kill infected cells. NK cells do not recognize peptides on class II.","Correct.","Rearranged antigen-specific receptors belong to T and B cells. NK cells use germline-encoded receptors and are part of innate immunity."],"study":{"topics":["NK cells and missing self","MHC class I"],"comps":["IM-3","IM-13"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C34","system":"IM","dx":"Mumps parotitis with orchitis","codes":["IM-15-F6"],"case":"A 23-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 1-day history of severe pain and swelling of the right testis. Six days ago he developed fever and painful swelling on both sides of the face in front of the ears. He did not receive childhood vaccinations, and a mumps outbreak has been reported at his university. Temperature is 39.1 °C (102.4 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 124/76 mm Hg. Both parotid glands are enlarged and tender. The right testis is swollen and very tender, and the overlying scrotal skin is red. The cremasteric reflex is present. Doppler ultrasonography shows increased blood flow to the right testis. Serum amylase is 286 U/L (normal 25 to 125). Serum mumps IgM is positive. Diagnosis: mumps parotitis with orchitis.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C34-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why this complication can lead to atrophy of the affected testis?","options":["Edema confined by the inelastic tunica albuginea","Thrombosis of the testicular artery from viral vasculitis","Destruction of Leydig cells by antisperm antibodies","Twisting of the spermatic cord around the swollen testis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-F6","obj":"IM-15-F.7","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the complication: mumps orchitis in a postpubertal man.","Recall the anatomy: the testis is wrapped in a tough, inelastic capsule, the tunica albuginea.","Swelling inside a rigid capsule raises pressure, cuts tubular blood flow, and causes pressure necrosis."],"why":"Mumps virus reaches glandular tissue through the blood, and after puberty the testis is a common target. The inflamed seminiferous tubules swell, but the testis is enclosed by the tunica albuginea, a dense, inelastic fibrous capsule. Pressure inside the testis rises, blood flow to the tubules falls, and germ cells die from pressure necrosis. Up to about half of affected testes later shrink. Orchitis is usually on one side, so sterility is uncommon. Other mumps complications are aseptic meningitis and pancreatitis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Mumps does not cause a vasculitis that clots the testicular artery. His Doppler study shows increased blood flow.","Antisperm antibodies target sperm, not Leydig cells, and they do not explain shrinkage of the testis.","Torsion twists the cord and cuts off blood flow, so Doppler shows reduced flow and the cremasteric reflex is usually absent. He has increased flow and an intact reflex."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of mumps","Mumps orchitis"],"comps":["IM-15-F6"]}},{"id":"IM-C34-Q2","stem":"Public health officials offer vaccination to unvaccinated contacts on campus but advise against it for a contact who takes tacrolimus after a kidney transplant. Which of the following properties of this vaccine explains both its strong cytotoxic T cell response and this precaution?","options":["Conjugation of a polysaccharide to a protein carrier","Replication of the weakened virus in host cells","Formaldehyde inactivation that preserves surface antigens","Use of a toxin treated to remove its toxicity"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-13","obj":"IM-13.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the vaccine: MMR, a live attenuated vaccine.","Live virus makes its proteins inside host cells, so they reach MHC class I and CD8 T cells.","The same ability to replicate makes it unsafe when cell-mediated immunity is suppressed."],"why":"MMR is a live attenuated vaccine. The weakened virus replicates inside host cells, so its proteins are made in the cytosol and presented on MHC class I to CD8 T cells, while the virus particles also drive strong antibody production. One or two doses give long-lasting cellular and humoral immunity. Because the virus can still replicate, it can cause serious disease in people with weak cell-mediated immunity, so live vaccines are avoided in severe immunodeficiency, in transplant recipients taking immunosuppressive drugs, and in pregnancy.","whyNot":["Conjugate vaccines (Hib, pneumococcal, meningococcal) link a polysaccharide to a protein so T cells help B cells make IgG. They contain nothing alive and are safe in immunosuppression.","Correct.","Inactivated vaccines, such as injected polio and hepatitis A vaccines, cannot replicate. They mainly induce antibody and are safe in immunosuppressed patients.","Toxoid vaccines (tetanus, diphtheria) contain inactivated toxin and induce antitoxin antibody. They do not replicate."],"study":{"topics":["Live attenuated vaccines","Comparison of vaccine types"],"comps":["IM-13","IM-5"]}},{"id":"IM-C34-Q3","stem":"Ultrasonography 3 months later, after the swelling has resolved, shows a 1.5-cm solid hypoechoic mass in the left testis. Orchiectomy shows a seminoma. To which of the following lymph node groups does this tumor most likely spread first?","options":["Internal iliac nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes","Lumbar (para-aortic) nodes","External iliac nodes"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-4","obj":"IM-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall where the testis forms: on the posterior abdominal wall near the kidney.","As it descends, it brings its vessels and lymphatics with it in the spermatic cord.","So testicular lymph drains to the lumbar (para-aortic) nodes near the renal veins, not to the groin."],"why":"The testis develops on the posterior abdominal wall and descends into the scrotum, bringing its vessels and lymphatics with it. Its lymph vessels run with the testicular vessels in the spermatic cord to the lumbar (para-aortic) nodes at about the level of the renal veins. The scrotal skin forms locally and drains to the superficial inguinal nodes. So testicular cancer spreads first to retroperitoneal nodes, while cancer of the scrotal skin spreads first to the groin.","whyNot":["Internal iliac nodes drain pelvic organs such as the prostate, bladder base, cervix, and the anal canal above the pectinate line.","Superficial inguinal nodes drain the scrotal skin, perineum, anal canal below the pectinate line, and lower limb. Testicular cancer reaches them only if it invades the scrotum or the scrotum has had surgery.","Correct.","External iliac nodes receive lymph from the deep inguinal nodes and parts of the bladder and uterus. The testis bypasses them because its lymphatics run with the testicular vessels."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic drainage of the testis","Testis versus scrotum drainage"],"comps":["IM-4"]}},{"id":"IM-C34-Q4","stem":"Mumps virions carry their own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase into the cells they infect, and purified genomic RNA alone cannot start an infection. Which of the following features of the viral genome explains these findings?","options":["Negative-sense single-stranded RNA","Positive-sense single-stranded RNA","Single-stranded linear DNA","Double-stranded linear DNA"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.4","dok":2,"approach":["Ask why a virus must bring its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase: host cells have no enzyme that copies RNA from RNA.","Purified RNA that cannot start infection means the genome cannot be read directly by ribosomes.","Both point to a negative-sense RNA genome, as in all paramyxoviruses."],"why":"Mumps virus is an enveloped paramyxovirus with one nonsegmented, negative-sense RNA strand. A negative-sense genome is the complement of mRNA, so ribosomes cannot read it. The virus must bring its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase to copy the genome into mRNA, and naked genomic RNA cannot start infection. Mumps spreads by respiratory droplets and saliva, replicates in the upper airway and nearby lymph nodes, and then reaches the parotid glands, testes, pancreas, and meninges through the blood.","whyNot":["Correct.","Positive-sense RNA works directly as mRNA, so these viruses (such as polio and hepatitis C virus) do not need to carry a polymerase, and their purified RNA is infectious.","Single-stranded DNA viruses, such as parvovirus B19, are copied into double-stranded DNA by host enzymes. They do not use an RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.","DNA viruses do not need an RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. Their genes are transcribed from DNA, usually by host RNA polymerase II in the nucleus."],"study":{"topics":["Negative-sense RNA viruses","Paramyxoviruses"],"comps":["IM-14","IM-15-F6"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C35","system":"IM","dx":"Roseola infantum with a febrile seizure","codes":["IM-15-F7"],"case":"A 13-month-old boy is brought to the physician because of a rash that appeared this morning. For the past 3 days he has had fever up to 40.0 °C (104.0 °F) with irritability but no cough, runny nose, or diarrhea. Yesterday, during a rapid rise in temperature, he had a generalized tonic-clonic seizure that lasted 2 minutes, with full recovery within an hour. The fever resolved overnight. He attends day care, and his vaccinations are up to date. Temperature is now 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 118/min, and respirations are 28/min. He is alert and playful. Physical examination shows a pink, blanching maculopapular rash on the trunk and neck, mild cervical lymphadenopathy, and a supple neck. Neurologic examination shows no abnormalities. Diagnosis: roseola infantum with a simple febrile seizure.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C35-Q1","stem":"His 4-month-old cousin, who spends each day with him, remains well. This illness is rare in infants younger than 6 months. Which of the following best explains this age pattern?","options":["Antiviral IgM made by the infant's own B cells","Inability of infant cells to support viral latency","Absent viral entry receptors on infant T cells","Transplacental maternal IgG against the virus"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-F7","obj":"IM-15-F.8","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the illness: roseola, caused by HHV-6, spread by saliva from people who already carry it.","Nearly all mothers have been infected and carry IgG against the virus.","That IgG crosses the placenta and protects the infant until it wanes after about 6 months."],"why":"Roseola is caused by HHV-6, mostly HHV-6B, and less often HHV-7. Nearly all adults carry the virus and shed it in saliva, so infants are exposed early. In the first months of life, maternal IgG that crossed the placenta protects them. As that antibody fades after about 6 months, infection follows, so most children are infected between 6 months and 2 years. The illness shows 3 to 5 days of high fever, then a pink rash on the trunk as the fever breaks, and the rapid fever raises the risk of febrile seizures.","whyNot":["An infant makes IgM only after being infected. A 4-month-old who is protected without ever having been ill is relying on antibody from the mother.","HHV-6 becomes latent after infection at any age. Latency does not explain why young infants escape the illness.","Cells that HHV-6 can infect are present from birth, and congenital HHV-6 infection occurs, so lack of receptors does not explain the age pattern.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Roseola infantum","Passive maternal antibody"],"comps":["IM-15-F7","IM-5"]}},{"id":"IM-C35-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the virus that most likely caused his illness?","options":["Enveloped dsDNA virus that enters B cells through CD21","Nonenveloped ssDNA virus that infects erythroid precursors","Enveloped RNA virus that forms syncytia in respiratory epithelium","Enveloped dsDNA virus that infects CD4 T cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.5","dok":2,"approach":["Use the pattern: high fever for days, then a truncal rash as the fever breaks, in a child under 2 means roseola.","Name the cause: HHV-6, a herpesvirus.","Recall its features: enveloped dsDNA, lifelong latency, replication in CD4 T cells, spread by saliva."],"why":"Roseola is caused by human herpesvirus 6, mostly HHV-6B, and less often HHV-7. Like all herpesviruses, HHV-6 is an enveloped, double-stranded DNA virus that stays latent for life after the first infection. It replicates mainly in CD4 T cells and is shed in saliva, which is how it passes from older children and adults to infants. Reactivation can cause disease in transplant recipients.","whyNot":["This describes Epstein-Barr virus, which causes infectious mononucleosis in adolescents. EBV is also spread by saliva, but it does not cause roseola.","This describes parvovirus B19, which causes erythema infectiosum (slapped cheek rash) and can halt red cell production.","This describes measles virus, a paramyxovirus. Measles causes cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, and a rash that appears while the fever is still high.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Human herpesvirus 6","Herpesvirus family features"],"comps":["IM-14","IM-15-F7"]}},{"id":"IM-C35-Q3","stem":"During the first hour of each fever spike, his hands and feet were cool and pale and he shivered, even as his core temperature rose. Which of the following best explains these findings?","options":["Direct stimulation of skeletal muscle by viral toxins","Excess thyroid hormone increasing metabolic heat","Raised hypothalamic set point from prostaglandin E2","Failure of sweat gland function from viral injury"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["Notice that he acts as if he is cold while his temperature is rising.","That means the hypothalamic set point has been raised above his current temperature.","Link the raised set point to cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha) that induce prostaglandin E2."],"why":"During infection, immune cells release IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha. These cytokines act near the hypothalamus to induce COX-2, which makes prostaglandin E2. PGE2 raises the hypothalamic set point. The body now senses its normal temperature as too cold, so it conserves and makes heat: skin vessels constrict, giving cool, pale hands and feet, and muscles shiver until core temperature reaches the new set point. When the set point falls, he flushes and sweats. The same cytokines drive the acute phase response, raising CRP, fibrinogen, and ferritin.","whyNot":["Viruses do not make toxins that stimulate muscle. Shivering is a reflex controlled by the hypothalamus.","Fever from infection is not caused by thyroid hormone. Hyperthyroidism raises heat production but causes warm, sweaty skin, not cold hands and shivering.","Correct.","Roseola does not damage sweat glands. During the rising phase of fever, the hypothalamus turns sweating off because the body is trying to keep heat."],"study":{"topics":["Mechanism of fever","Acute phase response"],"comps":["IM-6"]}},{"id":"IM-C35-Q4","stem":"His parents give ibuprofen, which inhibits cyclooxygenase. Arachidonic acid released from his cell membranes can still be converted to which of the following mediators?","options":["Thromboxane A2, a platelet aggregator","Leukotriene B4, a neutrophil chemoattractant","Prostacyclin, a platelet aggregation inhibitor","Prostaglandin E2, a mediator of fever and pain"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-10","obj":"IM-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["Split arachidonic acid metabolism into its two main branches: cyclooxygenase and 5-lipoxygenase.","Prostaglandins, prostacyclin, and thromboxane all come from cyclooxygenase, so ibuprofen blocks them.","Leukotrienes come from 5-lipoxygenase, which ibuprofen does not block."],"why":"Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids. Cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) converts it to prostaglandins, prostacyclin, and thromboxane, so ibuprofen lowers all of these. 5-Lipoxygenase is a separate enzyme that converts arachidonic acid to leukotrienes. LTB4 draws neutrophils, and LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4 constrict bronchi and increase vascular permeability. Blocking COX leaves this pathway running and can shift more substrate into it, which is one reason NSAIDs can trigger bronchospasm in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease.","whyNot":["Thromboxane A2 is made by cyclooxygenase in platelets. Its loss is why NSAIDs impair platelet aggregation.","Correct.","Prostacyclin is made by cyclooxygenase in endothelial cells, so ibuprofen lowers it as well.","PGE2 is made by cyclooxygenase. Lowering it is how ibuprofen reduces his fever and pain."],"study":{"topics":["Arachidonic acid pathways","Leukotrienes"],"comps":["IM-10","IM-6"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C36","system":"IM","dx":"Congenital rubella syndrome","codes":["IM-15-F8"],"case":"A female newborn is evaluated shortly after birth at 38 weeks' gestation. Her 24-year-old mother received no prenatal care and was never vaccinated. At about 9 weeks' gestation, the mother had a mild illness with low-grade fever, a pink rash that began on the face and spread to the trunk, enlarged tender nodes behind the ears, and aching finger joints. Birth weight is 2,150 g (small for gestational age). Physical examination shows bilateral white pupillary reflexes, a continuous machinery-like murmur below the left clavicle, hepatosplenomegaly, and scattered blue-purple papules and macules on the skin. She fails the newborn hearing screen in both ears. Platelet count is 46,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 450,000). Echocardiography shows a patent ductus arteriosus. Serologic studies are pending. Diagnosis: congenital rubella syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C36-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why maternal infection at 9 weeks' gestation caused these malformations, whereas infection after 20 weeks usually does not?","options":["Maternal antibodies attacking fetal organ antigens","Viral inhibition of cell division during organogenesis","Teratogenic effect of the mother's mild fever","Placental impermeability to the virus in early pregnancy"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-F8","obj":"IM-15-F.9","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the timing of organogenesis: about weeks 3 to 8, with eyes, ears, and heart finishing soon after.","Rubella infects fetal cells, slows their division, and damages small vessels.","Damage during organ formation produces malformations, while later infection finds organs already built."],"why":"Rubella virus reaches the fetus through maternal viremia and infection of the placenta. In fetal cells it slows cell division, causes apoptosis, and damages small vessels in developing organs. The harm is greatest while organs are forming, so infection in the first 11 weeks carries a risk of defects of up to about 90%. After about 20 weeks, defects are rare. The classic findings are cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and heart defects such as patent ductus arteriosus. The blue-purple blueberry muffin lesions are spots of extramedullary hematopoiesis in the skin.","whyNot":["The defects come from the virus infecting fetal cells. Maternal IgG against rubella protects the fetus, which is why infants of immune mothers are rarely affected.","Correct.","High fever early in pregnancy is linked to neural tube defects, but her fever was low grade, and fever does not produce this pattern of eye, ear, and heart defects.","The placenta does not block rubella early in pregnancy. Early infection is the most dangerous, because organs are still forming."],"study":{"topics":["Congenital rubella syndrome","Timing of teratogenic infection"],"comps":["IM-15-F8"]}},{"id":"IM-C36-Q2","stem":"Which of the following serologic results at birth would best confirm that she was infected before birth?","options":["Rising rubella IgG titer in the mother's serum","Rubella-specific IgG in the infant's serum","Low-avidity rubella IgG in the mother's serum","Rubella-specific IgM in the infant's serum"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-5","obj":"IM-5.6","dok":3,"approach":["Ask which antibodies in a newborn could have come from the mother: only IgG crosses the placenta.","IgM is the first antibody of a primary response and cannot cross the placenta.","So rubella IgM in the newborn must have been made by the fetus in response to its own infection."],"why":"IgG is the only antibody that crosses the placenta, carried by the neonatal Fc receptor. Rubella IgG in a newborn may simply be the mother's antibody, which fades over 6 to 12 months. IgM is a large pentamer that cannot cross the placenta, and it is the first antibody made in a primary response. Rubella IgM in the infant must have been made by the fetus's own B cells, which proves fetal infection. Tests on the mother can date her infection but cannot prove that the fetus was infected.","whyNot":["A rising maternal titer shows active or recent infection in the mother, not in the fetus.","IgG in the newborn can come from the mother across the placenta, so a positive result at birth does not prove fetal infection.","Low-avidity IgG shows the mother's infection was recent, which helps date her illness, but it does not show whether the fetus was infected.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Primary antibody response","Placental transfer of IgG"],"comps":["IM-5","IM-15-F8"]}},{"id":"IM-C36-Q3","stem":"The mother's illness included enlarged nodes behind her ears. An isolated infection at which of the following sites would most likely also enlarge this node group?","options":["Scalp above and behind the ear","Skin of the lower lip and chin","Skin of the lateral chest wall","Ulnar side of the hand and forearm"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-4","obj":"IM-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Name the node group: nodes behind the ear are the mastoid (retroauricular) nodes.","Recall their territory: the scalp above and behind the ear and the back of the auricle.","Pick the site inside that territory."],"why":"The nodes behind the ear are the mastoid (retroauricular) nodes. They drain the scalp above and behind the ear, the back of the auricle, and part of the external acoustic meatus, and they empty into the deep cervical nodes. Scalp infections such as infected lice bites or tinea capitis often enlarge them. In rubella, the virus spreads through lymphoid tissue widely, but the postauricular, posterior cervical, and suboccipital nodes enlarge most noticeably.","whyNot":["Correct.","The lower lip and chin drain to the submental nodes under the chin.","The lateral chest wall drains to the axillary nodes.","The ulnar side of the hand and forearm drains to the epitrochlear nodes above the medial epicondyle."],"study":{"topics":["Head and neck lymph node groups","Lymphadenopathy by site"],"comps":["IM-4","IM-15-F8"]}},{"id":"IM-C36-Q4","stem":"The mother acquired rubella from a coworker. Which of the following sequences best describes how the virus reached the fetus?","options":["Respiratory droplets, then viremia that seeded the placenta","Fecal-oral spread, then ascending infection through the cervix","Mosquito bite, then viremia that seeded the placenta","Sexual contact, then infection during passage through the birth canal"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how rubella spreads between people: respiratory droplets.","It replicates in the nasopharynx and nearby nodes, then enters the blood.","During viremia it infects the placenta and crosses to the fetus."],"why":"Rubella virus is an enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, formerly classed as a togavirus. It spreads by respiratory droplets, replicates in the nasopharynx and nearby lymph nodes (which is why the nodes behind the ears enlarge), and then enters the blood. During this viremia it infects the placenta and crosses to the fetus. Infants with congenital rubella can shed virus in urine and throat secretions for months and can infect others.","whyNot":["Correct.","Fecal-oral spread fits hepatitis A virus and enteroviruses. Ascending infection through the cervix is how bacteria such as group B streptococci reach the amniotic fluid.","Many viruses once grouped with rubella as togaviruses, such as the alphaviruses, are spread by mosquitoes, but rubella spreads person to person by respiratory droplets.","Infection in the birth canal is how herpes simplex virus reaches newborns. It cannot explain malformations that formed in the first trimester."],"study":{"topics":["Rubella virus transmission","Congenital infections"],"comps":["IM-14","IM-15-F8"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C37","system":"IM","dx":"Scarlet fever after streptococcal pharyngitis","codes":["IM-15-F9"],"case":"A 7-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a 2-day history of sore throat, fever, headache, and vomiting, and a rash that appeared this morning. She has no cough or runny nose. Temperature is 39.2 °C (102.6 °F), pulse is 120/min, and respirations are 22/min. The tonsils are red and swollen with patches of exudate, and the anterior cervical nodes are enlarged and tender. The tongue is red with prominent papillae. The face is flushed except around the mouth. A fine, red, blanching rash with a rough texture covers the neck, chest, and trunk, with linear red streaks in the antecubital and axillary folds. A rapid antigen detection test of a throat swab is positive for group A streptococcus. Diagnosis: scarlet fever.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C37-Q1","stem":"She receives a 10-day course of amoxicillin started on the day of diagnosis. This treatment most reliably prevents which of the following complications?","options":["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","Guillain-Barre syndrome","Acute rheumatic fever","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-15-F9","obj":"IM-15-F.10","dok":3,"approach":["List the two classic nonsuppurative complications of group A strep: rheumatic fever and glomerulonephritis.","Recall their mechanisms: rheumatic fever from cross-reacting antibodies, PSGN from immune complexes.","Only rheumatic fever is prevented by prompt antibiotic treatment of the throat infection."],"why":"Acute rheumatic fever follows group A strep pharyngitis by about 2 to 4 weeks. Antibodies against M protein cross-react with heart, joint, and brain antigens (molecular mimicry). Treating the throat infection with penicillin or amoxicillin within about 9 days of symptom onset clears the organism before a strong cross-reacting response develops, and it prevents most cases. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, which can follow throat or skin infection, is caused by immune complexes and is not reliably prevented by antibiotics. After scarlet fever, the skin of the hands and feet often peels about a week later.","whyNot":["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura in children usually follows a viral infection and is caused by antiplatelet antibodies. It is not a complication of strep throat that antibiotics prevent.","Guillain-Barre syndrome is linked most often to Campylobacter jejuni enteritis through molecular mimicry with nerve gangliosides, not to strep pharyngitis.","Correct.","PSGN appears 1 to 3 weeks after strep throat or 3 to 6 weeks after impetigo, with dark urine, edema, hypertension, and low C3. Antibiotic treatment does not reliably prevent it."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of group A strep","Prevention of rheumatic fever"],"comps":["IM-15-F9","IM-13"]}},{"id":"IM-C37-Q2","stem":"A throat culture grows small colonies surrounded by complete clearing on sheep blood agar. Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in chains, and the catalase test is negative. Which of the following additional results best identifies the organism that caused her illness?","options":["Growth inhibited by an optochin disk","Hippurate hydrolysis with a positive CAMP test","Growth inhibited by a bacitracin disk","Growth in 6.5% sodium chloride and on bile esculin"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-14","obj":"IM-14.6","dok":2,"approach":["Read the culture: complete (beta) hemolysis, cocci in chains, catalase negative means a beta-hemolytic streptococcus.","The two main beta-hemolytic streptococci are group A and group B.","Group A is bacitracin sensitive, and group B is bacitracin resistant."],"why":"Complete (beta) hemolysis, gram-positive cocci in chains, and a negative catalase test identify a beta-hemolytic streptococcus. Group A (S pyogenes) is separated from group B (S agalactiae) by bacitracin: group A is sensitive and group B is resistant. Group A is also PYR positive. S pyogenes causes pharyngitis and scarlet fever through its M protein, which resists phagocytosis, and its pyrogenic exotoxins.","whyNot":["Optochin sensitivity identifies S pneumoniae, which is alpha-hemolytic and appears as lancet-shaped diplococci.","Hippurate hydrolysis and a positive CAMP test identify group B streptococcus, a cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis that is resistant to bacitracin.","Correct.","Growth in 6.5% salt and on bile esculin identifies Enterococcus, which is usually not beta-hemolytic and causes urinary tract infections and endocarditis."],"study":{"topics":["Identification of streptococci","Streptococcus pyogenes"],"comps":["IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C37-Q3","stem":"Her rash is caused by streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins. The same family of toxins can cause streptococcal toxic shock syndrome. Which of the following best describes how these toxins activate so many T cells?","options":["Being processed into peptides presented on class II","Binding CD28 on T cells in place of B7 molecules","Cross-linking IgE bound to mast cells in the dermis","Bridging class II to TCR V-beta outside the groove"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-6","obj":"IM-6.7","dok":3,"approach":["Name the toxin class: pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens.","Recall how superantigens bind: unprocessed, to the outside of MHC class II and the V-beta region of the TCR.","This links any T cell with that V-beta family, so a large fraction of all T cells fire at once."],"why":"Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins, like S aureus TSST-1, are superantigens. They are not processed. They clamp the outside of an MHC class II molecule on an antigen-presenting cell to the V-beta region of a T cell receptor, whatever peptide sits in the groove. Every T cell with a matching V-beta family is activated, up to about 20% of all T cells instead of the usual tiny fraction. The resulting flood of IL-2, IFN-gamma, and TNF causes fever and rash, and in toxic shock, capillary leak, shock, and organ failure.","whyNot":["Normal protein antigens are processed and presented as peptides in the class II groove. This activates only the rare T cells whose receptor fits that peptide.","CD28 binding B7 provides the second signal for T cell activation. Superantigens act at the T cell receptor, not by replacing B7.","Cross-linking IgE on mast cells causes immediate hypersensitivity such as hives and anaphylaxis, not activation of large numbers of T cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Superantigens","Toxic shock syndrome"],"comps":["IM-6","IM-14"]}},{"id":"IM-C37-Q4","stem":"Two weeks later, her 6-year-old classmate has a few days of low-grade fever and runny nose. After these symptoms resolve, bright red cheeks appear, followed by a lacelike pink rash on the arms that fades and returns with warm baths. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the classmate's rash?","options":["Human herpesvirus 6","Streptococcus pyogenes","Parvovirus B19","Rubella virus"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-15-F1","obj":"IM-15-F.12","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: the rash appears after the mild febrile illness has ended.","Note the pattern: slapped cheeks, then a lacy, reticular rash on the limbs that recurs with heat.","This is erythema infectiosum, caused by parvovirus B19."],"why":"Erythema infectiosum (fifth disease) from parvovirus B19 begins with a mild prodrome of fever and coryza. Several days later, when the child already feels well, bright red cheeks appear, followed by a lacy, reticular rash on the limbs that can fade and return for weeks with heat, sunlight, or exercise. The rash is immune mediated, so the child is no longer contagious once it appears. By contrast, scarlet fever and measles rashes appear while the child is febrile, and the roseola rash appears just as a high fever breaks.","whyNot":["Roseola from HHV-6 affects children under about 2 years, with 3 to 5 days of high fever and a truncal rash as the fever breaks. It does not cause red cheeks followed by a lacy limb rash.","Scarlet fever comes with fever and sore throat at the same time as a sandpaper rash with circumoral pallor and Pastia lines. The rash is not lacy and does not come and go.","Correct.","Rubella causes a mild pink rash that spreads from the face to the trunk over a day and fades in about 3 days, with enlarged nodes behind the ears."],"study":{"topics":["Childhood exanthems","Erythema infectiosum"],"comps":["IM-15-F1","IM-14"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C38","system":"IM","dx":"Abetalipoproteinemia","codes":["IM-11"],"case":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of an unsteady gait that has worsened over the past year. Since infancy he has had large, pale, foul-smelling stools, and his weight has stayed below the 3rd percentile. His parents are first cousins. Physical examination shows a wide-based gait, absent knee and ankle reflexes, and decreased vibration and position sense in the feet. Hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL (normal 11.5 to 14.5), total cholesterol is 42 mg/dL (normal 100 to 170), triglycerides are 6 mg/dL (normal 30 to 100), and INR is 1.6 (normal 0.9 to 1.1). A peripheral blood smear shows many red cells with irregular spiky projections. Duodenal biopsy shows normal villi with enterocytes filled with clear lipid droplets. Genetic testing shows biallelic pathogenic variants in MTTP. Diagnosis: abetalipoproteinemia.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C38-Q1","stem":"Deficiency of which of the following vitamins best explains his neurologic findings?","options":["Vitamin A","Vitamin E","Vitamin B12","Vitamin D"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-11","obj":"IM-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Link the defect to its result: no MTP means no chylomicrons or VLDL, so fat and fat-soluble vitamins are not absorbed or carried.","Match the neurologic picture: ataxia, areflexia, and loss of vibration and position sense mean damage to the spinocerebellar tracts, posterior columns, and peripheral nerves.","That pattern is the hallmark of vitamin E deficiency."],"why":"Without microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP), enterocytes cannot load triglyceride onto apoB-48, so no chylomicrons form, and the liver cannot make VLDL or LDL. Fat stays trapped in enterocytes, and fat-soluble vitamins are poorly absorbed and poorly carried in plasma. Vitamin E, the main antioxidant in cell membranes, falls very low. Its loss damages long axons in the posterior columns, spinocerebellar tracts, and peripheral nerves, causing ataxia, absent reflexes, and loss of vibration and position sense. The spiky red cells (acanthocytes) reflect abnormal membrane lipids, and the high INR reflects vitamin K deficiency.","whyNot":["Vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness, xerophthalmia, and dry skin. It does not cause ataxia or loss of vibration and position sense.","Correct.","B12 deficiency can damage the posterior columns, but B12 is water soluble and is absorbed with intrinsic factor in the ileum, so a failure to make chylomicrons does not cause it.","Vitamin D deficiency causes rickets with bone pain and deformity, and low calcium can cause tetany, but not this sensory ataxia."],"study":{"topics":["Abetalipoproteinemia","Vitamin E deficiency"],"comps":["IM-11"]}},{"id":"IM-C38-Q2","stem":"A 9-year-old girl at the same clinic has had recurrent acute pancreatitis and eruptive xanthomas. Her plasma is milky after an overnight fast, and fasting triglycerides are 2,400 mg/dL (normal 30 to 100). Lipoprotein lipase activity in her post-heparin plasma is low but becomes normal when a small amount of plasma from a healthy donor is added. Which of the following is the most likely cause of her disorder?","options":["Deficiency of microsomal triglyceride transfer protein","Deficiency of apolipoprotein C-II","Deficiency of the LDL receptor","Homozygosity for apolipoprotein E2"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-11","obj":"IM-11.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize fasting chylomicronemia: milky fasting plasma, very high triglycerides, eruptive xanthomas, and pancreatitis.","This means chylomicrons are not being broken down by lipoprotein lipase.","Donor plasma restores activity, so the enzyme is present but its plasma activator, apoC-II, is missing."],"why":"After chylomicrons enter the blood, they pick up apoC-II and apoE from HDL. ApoC-II activates lipoprotein lipase on the capillary endothelium of muscle and adipose tissue, which frees fatty acids from triglyceride for uptake. The smaller remnants are then cleared by the liver through apoE binding. Without apoC-II, the enzyme is present but inactive, so chylomicrons persist after fasting and triglycerides become very high (familial chylomicronemia). Adding normal plasma supplies apoC-II and restores activity, which points to the activator rather than the enzyme.","whyNot":["MTP deficiency is the boy's disorder. It prevents chylomicron and VLDL formation, so triglycerides are very low, not high.","Correct.","LDL receptor deficiency (familial hypercholesterolemia) raises LDL cholesterol and causes tendon xanthomas and early coronary disease. Chylomicrons clear normally.","ApoE2 homozygosity slows hepatic uptake of chylomicron and VLDL remnants (dysbetalipoproteinemia), with palmar xanthomas. Lipoprotein lipase activity is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Chylomicron metabolism","Familial chylomicronemia"],"comps":["IM-11"]}},{"id":"IM-C38-Q3","stem":"Although his lacteals carry no chylomicrons, lymph continues to flow from his intestine through the thoracic duct. Which of the following functions of this lymph cannot be performed by any other route?","options":["Return of filtered plasma protein to the blood","Delivery of absorbed amino acids to the liver","Transport of absorbed glucose to the liver","Removal of carbon dioxide from the intestinal wall"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-9","obj":"IM-9.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that capillaries leak a small amount of plasma protein into the tissues every day.","Blood capillaries cannot take that protein back against its gradient.","Lymph is the only route that returns it to the blood."],"why":"Each day, blood capillaries filter a small amount of plasma protein into the interstitium. They cannot reabsorb it, so the lymphatics are the only route back to the blood. Lymph is interstitial fluid that carries this protein and lymphocytes, plus chylomicrons in the intestine after a fatty meal. If lymph flow stopped, protein would build up in the tissues, raise interstitial oncotic pressure, and cause edema. His intestinal lymph lacks chylomicrons but still returns protein and lymphocytes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Amino acids enter the capillaries of the villi and reach the liver in the portal vein.","Glucose and other monosaccharides are absorbed into the villous capillaries and carried in portal blood.","Carbon dioxide diffuses freely into capillary blood and leaves in venous blood."],"study":{"topics":["Lymph composition","Lymph return of protein"],"comps":["IM-9"]}},{"id":"IM-C38-Q4","stem":"Lymph from the liver normally has a protein concentration of about 6 g/dL, compared with about 2 g/dL in lymph from most peripheral tissues such as skeletal muscle. Which of the following features of hepatic capillaries best explains this difference?","options":["Continuous endothelium with tight junctions","Thicker basement membrane around sinusoids","Higher hydrostatic pressure in the sinusoids","Discontinuous endothelium with large gaps"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-11","obj":"IM-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Lymph protein reflects how much protein leaks out of the capillaries in that organ.","Compare capillary types: continuous in muscle, fenestrated in the intestine, sinusoidal in the liver.","The sinusoidal wall has large gaps, so protein passes almost freely."],"why":"Hepatic sinusoids have a discontinuous endothelium with large openings and almost no basement membrane, so plasma proteins pass nearly freely into the space of Disse. Liver interstitial fluid, and the lymph made from it, therefore has a protein content close to that of plasma. Intestinal capillaries are fenestrated and leak more protein than muscle capillaries, giving intermediate values. Skeletal muscle has continuous capillaries with tight junctions that hold back most protein. The liver and intestines together make about two thirds of all lymph.","whyNot":["Continuous endothelium with tight junctions is the muscle pattern, which holds protein in the blood. It explains the low value in muscle lymph.","Hepatic sinusoids have little or no basement membrane. A thicker barrier would lower protein leak.","Pressure in the hepatic sinusoids is normally low, only a few mm Hg. Protein leaks because of the gaps in the wall, not high pressure.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Capillary types","Regional lymph protein content"],"comps":["IM-11","IM-9"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C39","system":"IM","dx":"Gastric adenocarcinoma with a left supraclavicular (Virchow) node and chylothorax from thoracic duct obstruction","codes":["IM-4"],"case":"A 63-year-old man comes to the physician because of 4 months of early satiety, epigastric pain, and a 9-kg (20-lb) weight loss, and 2 weeks of worsening shortness of breath. Pulse is 98/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a firm, nontender, fixed 2-cm lymph node in the left supraclavicular fossa. There is dullness to percussion and decreased breath sounds over the lower half of the left hemithorax. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5). Upper endoscopy shows an ulcerated mass on the lesser curvature of the stomach, and biopsy shows adenocarcinoma. CT shows enlarged celiac and posterior mediastinal nodes compressing the thoracic duct. Thoracentesis yields milky white fluid that stays opaque after centrifugation. Diagnosis: gastric adenocarcinoma with a left supraclavicular (Virchow) node and chylothorax from thoracic duct obstruction.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C39-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why metastasis from this tumor appears in the left supraclavicular region?","options":["Thoracic duct ending at the left venous angle","Tumor cells traveling upward along the left phrenic nerve","Gastric lymph draining directly into the right lymphatic duct","Portal venous blood carrying tumor cells to the left subclavian vein"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-4","obj":"IM-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["Trace gastric lymph: celiac nodes, intestinal trunk, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct.","Recall where the thoracic duct ends: the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins.","Tumor cells moving up the duct lodge in the left supraclavicular nodes beside that junction."],"why":"Lymph from the stomach drains to the celiac nodes, then through the intestinal trunk to the cisterna chyli and the thoracic duct. The thoracic duct rises through the posterior mediastinum and ends at the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins (the left venous angle). Tumor cells moving up the duct, or backing up when it is blocked, lodge in the left supraclavicular nodes next to its end. A hard, enlarged Virchow node therefore suggests cancer of the abdomen or thorax, most classically the stomach.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tumors do not spread along the phrenic nerve to the neck. Irritation of the diaphragm causes referred shoulder pain, not nodal metastasis.","The right lymphatic duct drains only the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb, and the right thorax. Abdominal lymph does not reach it.","Portal blood goes to the liver, so blood-borne spread of gastric cancer reaches the liver first. Neck nodes are reached by lymph, not venous blood."],"study":{"topics":["Virchow node","Thoracic duct anatomy"],"comps":["IM-4"]}},{"id":"IM-C39-Q2","stem":"If the tumor completely blocked flow through the thoracic duct, lymph from which of the following regions would continue to drain normally?","options":["Small intestine","Right upper limb","Left lower limb","Right lower limb"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-4","obj":"IM-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Divide the body by lymphatic duct, not by side alone.","The thoracic duct drains both lower limbs, the pelvis, the abdomen, and the left upper body.","The right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper limb, right head and neck, and right thorax."],"why":"The thoracic duct begins at the cisterna chyli and drains both lower limbs, the pelvis, the abdomen including the gut, the left side of the thorax, the left upper limb, and the left side of the head and neck, which is about three quarters of the body. The right lymphatic duct, or the separate right lymph trunks, drains only the right upper limb, the right side of the head and neck, and the right side of the thorax into the right venous angle. So the right upper limb keeps its normal drainage.","whyNot":["Intestinal lymph passes through the intestinal trunk to the cisterna chyli and the thoracic duct. Its blocked flow is the source of his chylothorax.","Correct.","The left lower limb drains through the left lumbar trunk into the cisterna chyli and the thoracic duct.","Both lower limbs drain through the lumbar trunks to the cisterna chyli and the thoracic duct. The right leg does not drain to the right lymphatic duct."],"study":{"topics":["Thoracic duct territory","Right lymphatic duct territory"],"comps":["IM-4"]}},{"id":"IM-C39-Q3","stem":"Which of the following additional pleural fluid findings would best confirm that this effusion is chyle?","options":["Cholesterol above 200 mg/dL with cholesterol crystals","Neutrophil predominance with a pH below 7.20","Triglyceride above 110 mg/dL with chylomicrons","Hematocrit more than half of the blood hematocrit"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-4","obj":"IM-4.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the clue: milky fluid that stays opaque after centrifugation, so the cloudiness is from lipid, not cells.","Separate the two milky lipid effusions: chylothorax (triglyceride and chylomicrons) and pseudochylothorax (cholesterol).","A blocked thoracic duct leaks chyle, so expect high triglyceride with chylomicrons."],"why":"Chyle is intestinal lymph rich in chylomicrons, which make it milky. A pleural triglyceride above 110 mg/dL, or chylomicrons found on lipoprotein analysis, confirms a chylothorax, and the fluid is usually lymphocyte predominant. Chylothorax follows injury to the thoracic duct from surgery or trauma, or obstruction by tumor, most often lymphoma. The duct crosses from right to left in the posterior mediastinum at about T4 to T6, so damage below that level tends to cause a right-sided effusion and damage above it a left-sided one.","whyNot":["High cholesterol with cholesterol crystals defines a pseudochylothorax, a milky effusion that forms in a long-standing pleural effusion, such as in rheumatoid arthritis or old tuberculosis. Triglyceride is low and chylomicrons are absent.","Neutrophils with a low pH suggest an empyema. Pus can look milky, but it clears when spun because the cloudiness comes from cells.","Correct.","A pleural hematocrit above half that of blood defines a hemothorax."],"study":{"topics":["Chylothorax","Pleural fluid analysis"],"comps":["IM-4","IM-9"]}},{"id":"IM-C39-Q4","stem":"To reduce chyle flow, he is given a diet in which most fat comes from medium-chain triglycerides. Which of the following best explains why this diet lowers thoracic duct flow?","options":["Their fatty acids pass into portal blood bound to albumin","They are packaged in enterocytes with apoB-100 instead of apoB-48","They cause relaxation of lacteal smooth muscle","They remain stored in enterocytes as cytoplasmic lipid droplets"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"IM-11","obj":"IM-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how long-chain fat is absorbed: re-esterified to triglyceride and packaged with apoB-48 into chylomicrons.","Chylomicrons are too large for blood capillaries, so they enter lacteals and the thoracic duct.","Medium-chain fatty acids skip packaging and go straight into portal blood, so less fat enters the lymph."],"why":"Long-chain fatty acids are rebuilt into triglyceride in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of the enterocyte. Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein then loads the triglyceride onto apoB-48 to form chylomicrons. These particles are too large to enter blood capillaries, so they enter the lacteals and travel through the thoracic duct, bypassing the liver until they reach the blood. Medium-chain fatty acids (about 6 to 12 carbons) are more water soluble. They cross the enterocyte without being packaged and enter portal blood bound to albumin. With fewer chylomicrons, chyle flow falls and the leak slows.","whyNot":["Correct.","ApoB-100 is made by the liver for VLDL. Enterocytes make apoB-48 by editing the same mRNA, and any chylomicrons they still make carry apoB-48.","Medium-chain triglycerides act by changing the route of absorption, not by acting on lymphatic smooth muscle.","Fat stays in enterocytes when chylomicrons cannot be made, as in abetalipoproteinemia. Medium-chain fatty acids leave the cell readily."],"study":{"topics":["Chylomicron assembly","Medium-chain triglycerides"],"comps":["IM-11","IM-9"]}}]},{"id":"IM-C40","system":"IM","dx":"Upper limb lymphedema after mastectomy with axillary node dissection for breast cancer","codes":["IM-9","IM-4"],"case":"A 57-year-old woman comes to the physician because of progressive swelling and heaviness of her left arm over the past year. Two years ago, she underwent a left modified radical mastectomy with axillary lymph node dissection, followed by radiation to the axilla, for invasive ductal carcinoma in the upper outer quadrant of the left breast. Four of 15 axillary nodes contained tumor. Vital signs are within normal limits. The circumferences of the left upper arm and forearm are 5 cm larger than on the right, and the hand and fingers are swollen. The skin of the forearm is thickened and firm, and finger pressure leaves no pit. There is no erythema, warmth, or venous distention. Duplex ultrasonography shows no venous thrombosis. Diagnosis: lymphedema of the left upper limb after mastectomy with axillary node dissection.","questions":[{"id":"IM-C40-Q1","stem":"Her tumor arose in the upper outer quadrant. A tumor of the same size in the medial quadrants of the breast would be more likely than hers to spread first to which of the following node groups?","options":["Apical axillary nodes","Pectoral axillary nodes","Supraclavicular nodes","Parasternal nodes"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IM-4","obj":"IM-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the two main drainage routes of the breast: axillary and parasternal.","Lateral quadrants drain mainly to the axilla, beginning with the pectoral group.","Medial quadrants send much of their lymph to the parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels."],"why":"More than three quarters of breast lymph, especially from the lateral quadrants, drains to the axillary nodes, first to the pectoral (anterior) group. Most of the rest, especially from the medial quadrants, drains to the parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels. Medial tumors are therefore more likely to involve parasternal nodes, which axillary surgery does not reach. Some lymph also crosses to the opposite breast or passes toward the abdominal wall.","whyNot":["Apical axillary nodes receive lymph only after it passes through the other axillary groups. They are a later stop, not the first.","The pectoral nodes are the first stop for lymph from the lateral breast, which is why her tumor was staged by axillary dissection.","Supraclavicular nodes receive breast lymph only after it has passed through the axillary nodes, later in the course of spread.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic drainage of the breast","Nodal spread of breast cancer"],"comps":["IM-4"]}},{"id":"IM-C40-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her swelling no longer pits with pressure?","options":["Glycosaminoglycan accumulation from thyroid hormone deficiency","High capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous obstruction","Low plasma oncotic pressure from reduced albumin synthesis","Trapped protein driving fibrosis and fat deposition"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IM-9","obj":"IM-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the cause: surgery and radiation destroyed the lymph channels that drain the arm.","Without lymph flow, filtered protein stays in the tissue and holds water there.","Over months the protein-rich fluid causes inflammation, fibrosis, and fat deposition, so the tissue becomes firm and no longer pits."],"why":"Axillary dissection and radiation remove and scar the lymph channels that drain the arm. Filtered plasma protein can no longer return to the blood, so it builds up in the interstitium, raises interstitial oncotic pressure, and holds water in the tissue. Early lymphedema pits. Over months, the protein-rich fluid triggers chronic inflammation, fibroblast activity, and fat deposition, so the skin and subcutaneous tissue become thick and firm and no longer pit. Other causes of lymphedema include Wuchereria bancrofti filariasis, Milroy disease (FLT4 mutation), and the cystic hygroma of Turner syndrome.","whyNot":["Hypothyroidism causes myxedema, a nonpitting swelling from glycosaminoglycan buildup, but it is generalized, not limited to the arm on the side of surgery.","Venous obstruction raises capillary pressure and causes pitting edema, often with distended veins. Her ultrasound shows no thrombosis.","Low albumin, as in cirrhosis or nephrotic syndrome, causes soft, pitting edema in both legs and around the eyes, not swelling of one arm.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphedema","Mechanisms of edema"],"comps":["IM-9"]}},{"id":"IM-C40-Q3","stem":"Three years later, a nodule recurs in her chest wall. Biopsy shows tumor cells with normal MHC class I expression and strong PD-L1 expression, surrounded by many CD8 T cells that are not killing them. Which of the following best explains the tumor's escape from these T cells?","options":["Tumor antigen presented only on MHC class II","Fas ligand on T cells binding Fas on tumor cells","PD-L1 delivering an inhibitory signal through PD-1","Loss of class I preventing recognition by CD8 T cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IM-13","obj":"IM-13.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how CD8 T cells kill tumors: they recognize tumor peptides on MHC class I.","Class I is normal here, so recognition is possible.","PD-L1 on the tumor binds PD-1 on the T cells and switches them off."],"why":"CD8 T cells recognize tumor peptides on MHC class I and kill the tumor cell, and NK cells kill tumor cells that lose class I. Tumors escape either by losing class I or by switching off the T cells. Here class I is normal, so the T cells can see the tumor. But the tumor expresses PD-L1, which binds PD-1 on activated T cells and sends an inhibitory signal that stops killing and cytokine release. Checkpoint inhibitor drugs that block PD-1 or PD-L1 remove this brake.","whyNot":["Antigen shown only on class II would be seen by CD4 helper T cells. This tumor expresses class I, so CD8 T cells can recognize it.","Fas ligand on T cells binding Fas on a target triggers apoptosis of the tumor cell. It is a way of killing, not a way of escape.","Correct.","Loss of class I does hide tumors from CD8 T cells, but this biopsy shows normal class I. Tumors that lose class I also become targets for NK cells."],"study":{"topics":["Antitumor immunity","Immune checkpoints"],"comps":["IM-13","IM-3"]}},{"id":"IM-C40-Q4","stem":"Her physical therapist prescribes repeated arm exercises performed while she wears a compression sleeve. Which of the following mechanisms most directly moves lymph toward the trunk during these exercises?","options":["Reabsorption of interstitial protein into venous capillaries","Muscle compression of lymphatic vessels with one-way valves","Relaxation of smooth muscle in collecting lymphatic walls","Backflow of lymph into dermal lymphatic capillaries"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IM-9","obj":"IM-9.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall what moves lymph: lymphatic smooth muscle contraction and external compression, with valves keeping flow one way.","Exercise adds external compression from contracting skeletal muscle.","Valves let the squeezed lymph move only toward the trunk."],"why":"Collecting lymphatics are divided by valves into short segments (lymphangions). Their smooth muscle contracts when the segment fills and stretches, pushing lymph forward. External compression from contracting skeletal muscle, pulsing arteries, and breathing squeezes the vessels too, and the valves let lymph move only toward the trunk. Exercise in a compression sleeve adds external pumping and limits fluid buildup. In a normal limb, a rise in interstitial fluid pressure also increases lymph flow, until the vessels reach their maximum capacity.","whyNot":["Venous capillaries reabsorb water but very little protein. Protein returns to the blood almost only through lymph.","Correct.","Lymphatic smooth muscle helps move lymph by contracting, not relaxing. Relaxation would slow flow.","Dermal backflow is a sign of blocked lymph channels seen on imaging of lymphedema. It reflects failure of forward flow."],"study":{"topics":["Lymph propulsion","Lymphedema management"],"comps":["IM-9"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C01","system":"IN","dx":"Atopic dermatitis complicated by molluscum contagiosum","codes":["IN-13-B1","IN-13-H9"],"case":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of an itchy rash that has been present since infancy and new bumps on his trunk for 2 months. As an infant, he had red, oozing patches on his cheeks and the extensor surfaces of his arms and legs. His father has asthma, and his mother has allergic rhinitis. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows dry skin and thickened, lichenified plaques with excoriations in the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Several dozen firm, flesh-colored, dome-shaped papules 2 to 4 mm in diameter with central umbilication are present on the trunk and in the antecubital fossae, and some are arranged in lines along scratch marks. Diagnosis: atopic dermatitis complicated by molluscum contagiosum.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C01-Q1","stem":"Two months later, he is brought to the emergency department because of fever and painful skin lesions for 2 days. His temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F). Physical examination shows clusters of uniform, punched-out erosions with hemorrhagic crusts over the eczematous plaques of his neck and arms. Which of the following is the most likely cause of these new lesions?","options":["Herpes simplex virus","Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pyogenes","Varicella zoster virus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-B1","obj":"IN-13-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: uniform, punched-out erosions clustered on top of eczema in a febrile child.","This pattern is eczema herpeticum, a herpes simplex infection that spreads across skin with a broken barrier.","Atopic skin is prone to it because the barrier is damaged and Th2 cytokines lower the skin's own antimicrobial peptides."],"why":"Eczema herpeticum is herpes simplex virus (usually type 1) spreading rapidly through eczematous skin. It shows up as many monomorphic vesicles that quickly become punched-out erosions with hemorrhagic crusts, often with fever. Atopic skin is vulnerable because filaggrin loss weakens the barrier, and IL-4 and IL-13 from Th2 cells reduce keratinocyte production of antimicrobial peptides such as cathelicidin and defensins. It is a dermatologic urgency because the virus can spread to the eye or disseminate.","whyNot":["Correct.","Staphylococcus aureus colonizes most atopic skin and causes weeping, honey-crusted impetiginization of eczema. It does not produce uniform punched-out erosions.","Streptococcus pyogenes causes impetigo with honey-colored crusts or cellulitis with spreading warm erythema, not clusters of uniform punched-out erosions.","Primary varicella appears in crops over the whole body, most densely on the trunk, with lesions in different stages at once. It does not concentrate on eczematous plaques."],"study":{"topics":["Eczema herpeticum","Infections that complicate atopic dermatitis"],"comps":["IN-13-B1","IN-12"]}},{"id":"IN-C01-Q2","stem":"Genetic testing shows that he is heterozygous for a loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin gene (FLG). His father carries the same mutation and has fine white scale on both shins and accentuated palmar creases. Which of the following best explains how this mutation weakens the epidermal barrier in both patients?","options":["Loss of E-cadherin adhesion between spinous layer cells","Defective keratin bundling and less natural moisturizing factor","Failure to remove sulfate from cholesterol sulfate in the cornified layer","Weak keratin 5 and 14 filaments in basal keratinocytes"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-7","obj":"IN-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["The father's fine scale on the shins with prominent palmar creases is ichthyosis vulgaris, which shares FLG mutations with atopic dermatitis.","Filaggrin comes from profilaggrin in keratohyalin granules of the stratum granulosum.","It aggregates keratin filaments into a tight cornified layer and is then broken down into natural moisturizing factor that keeps the stratum corneum hydrated."],"why":"Filaggrin is made as profilaggrin in the keratohyalin granules of the stratum granulosum. It bundles keratin filaments so corneocytes flatten into a tight, tough layer, and its breakdown products (amino acids and urocanic acid) form natural moisturizing factor, which holds water and keeps the surface slightly acidic. Loss-of-function FLG mutations leave a dry, leaky stratum corneum. That causes ichthyosis vulgaris, which is semidominant, and is the strongest genetic risk factor for atopic dermatitis because allergens and microbes can get through the barrier.","whyNot":["E-cadherin forms adherens junctions between keratinocytes. Its loss is not the mechanism of filaggrin-related barrier failure.","Correct.","This is steroid sulfatase deficiency, the cause of X-linked ichthyosis. Cholesterol sulfate builds up and corneocytes fail to shed, giving large dark scales in boys. It does not involve filaggrin.","Mutations in keratins 5 and 14 make basal keratinocytes fragile and cause epidermolysis bullosa simplex, with blisters from friction rather than dry scaling skin."],"study":{"topics":["Filaggrin and the stratum corneum barrier","Ichthyosis vulgaris versus X-linked ichthyosis"],"comps":["IN-7","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C01-Q3","stem":"His mother asks why some of the papules on his trunk are arranged in straight lines. Which of the following best explains this arrangement?","options":["Viral spread along a sensory dermatome","Lymphatic spread from a primary papule","Contact with a plant resin while playing","Autoinoculation of virus by scratching"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-H9","obj":"IN-13-H.9","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the papules: firm, flesh-colored, umbilicated domes in a young child are molluscum contagiosum.","Molluscum spreads by direct skin contact and by autoinoculation.","Scratching itchy eczema carries virus from a lesion along the scratch line, so new papules form in a row."],"why":"Molluscum contagiosum is a poxvirus infection of keratinocytes that produces firm, flesh-colored, dome-shaped papules with a central dimple. It spreads by skin-to-skin contact, shared objects, and autoinoculation, and in adults it can spread by sexual contact. Children with atopic dermatitis often have many lesions because they scratch and their barrier is weak, and scratching drags virus along the skin so papules line up. In healthy children the lesions resolve on their own over months. Very numerous or large lesions in an adult suggest HIV infection.","whyNot":["A dermatomal band of grouped vesicles on a red base is herpes zoster, from reactivation in a sensory ganglion. Molluscum does not live in nerves.","Nodules that climb up a limb along lymphatics are typical of sporotrichosis after a thorn injury. Molluscum stays within the epidermis.","Linear streaks after brushing against a plant are typical of allergic contact dermatitis to urushiol, which gives itchy vesicles, not umbilicated papules.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Molluscum contagiosum transmission","Molluscum in atopic dermatitis and HIV"],"comps":["IN-13-H9","IN-12"]}},{"id":"IN-C01-Q4","stem":"One papule is removed by curettage. Microscopy shows large, eosinophilic inclusion bodies within the cytoplasm of the infected keratinocytes. Which of the following features of the causative virus best explains the location of these inclusions?","options":["Replication of its DNA genome in the cytoplasm","Integration of the viral genome into host chromosomes","Nuclear transcription by host RNA polymerase II","Reverse transcription of an RNA genome into DNA"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-12","obj":"IN-12.5","dok":3,"approach":["The inclusions are molluscum bodies, the hallmark of molluscum contagiosum, a poxvirus.","Poxviruses are large, enveloped dsDNA viruses that carry their own DNA-dependent RNA polymerase.","Because they bring their own enzymes, they replicate entirely in the cytoplasm, so viral factories form cytoplasmic inclusions."],"why":"Poxviruses, including molluscum contagiosum virus, are the only DNA viruses that replicate in the cytoplasm. They carry their own DNA-dependent RNA polymerase and other enzymes in the virion, so they do not need the host nucleus. Viral assembly sites fill the keratinocyte cytoplasm and form large eosinophilic molluscum (Henderson-Patterson) bodies. Human papillomavirus, the cause of warts, is a nonenveloped circular dsDNA virus that replicates in the nucleus and produces koilocytes with wrinkled nuclei and a perinuclear halo.","whyNot":["Correct.","Integration into host DNA is a feature of retroviruses and of high-risk HPV in cancers. It does not create cytoplasmic inclusions.","Most DNA viruses, such as HPV and herpesviruses, transcribe their genes in the nucleus with host RNA polymerase II, giving nuclear changes. Poxviruses avoid the nucleus.","Reverse transcription is used by retroviruses and hepatitis B virus. Molluscum contagiosum virus has a DNA genome and no reverse transcriptase."],"study":{"topics":["Poxvirus replication","HPV versus molluscum contagiosum"],"comps":["IN-12","IN-13-H9"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C02","system":"IN","dx":"Allergic contact dermatitis from poison oak (urushiol)","codes":["IN-13-B2"],"case":"A 34-year-old man who works on a trail maintenance crew comes to urgent care because of an intensely itchy rash on his forearms and shins for 2 days. Four days ago, he spent a day cutting back brush that included poison oak while wearing a short-sleeved shirt. He had a similar rash after trail work last summer. He takes no medications. His temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 74/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 122/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows erythematous papules and vesicles arranged in linear streaks on the dorsal forearms, wrists, and anterior shins, with a few tense bullae. There are no mucosal lesions. Diagnosis: allergic contact dermatitis to urushiol from poison oak.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C02-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the 2-day delay between his exposure to poison oak and the onset of his rash?","options":["Degranulation of IgE-coated dermal mast cells","Recruitment of sensitized memory T cells","Deposition of immune complexes in dermal venules","Direct toxic injury to keratinocytes by the resin"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-13-B2","obj":"IN-13-B.2","dok":2,"approach":["He reacted after a prior exposure last summer, so he was already sensitized.","Allergic contact dermatitis is a type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity.","Memory T cells must be recruited to the skin, recognize hapten-modified proteins, and release cytokines, which takes about 48 to 72 hours."],"why":"Urushiol is a hapten. It binds skin proteins, and at first exposure Langerhans cells carry the modified proteins to lymph nodes to sensitize T cells. On re-exposure, memory T cells travel to the skin, recognize the antigen, and release cytokines such as IFN-gamma that recruit more cells and injure keratinocytes. Recruiting and activating these cells takes time, so the eruption appears about 48 to 72 hours after contact. Irritant contact dermatitis, in contrast, needs no sensitization and appears soon after direct damage.","whyNot":["IgE on mast cells mediates type I reactions such as hives and anaphylaxis, which start within minutes of exposure.","Correct.","Immune complex deposition (type III) causes vasculitis or serum sickness. It is not the mechanism of a plant contact rash.","Direct chemical damage is irritant contact dermatitis. It does not need prior sensitization, and his reaction does."],"study":{"topics":["Type IV hypersensitivity timing","Allergic versus irritant contact dermatitis"],"comps":["IN-13-B2","IN-11"]}},{"id":"IN-C02-Q2","stem":"A punch biopsy specimen is obtained from the edge of a vesicle on his forearm. Which of the following histologic findings is most likely?","options":["Regular acanthosis with confluent parakeratosis","Subepidermal cleft with many eosinophils","Intercellular edema within the spinous layer","Suprabasal acantholysis above an intact basal row"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-B2","obj":"IN-13-B.3","dok":2,"approach":["Allergic contact dermatitis is an eczematous (spongiotic) dermatitis.","Cytokines from T cells make fluid collect between keratinocytes, stretching the desmosomes apart so cells look spongy.","When the fluid pools, it forms intraepidermal vesicles, which match his clinical vesicles."],"why":"Spongiosis is edema between keratinocytes in the spinous layer. The fluid widens the spaces between cells until the desmosomal connections look like sponge fibers, and pockets of fluid form intraepidermal vesicles. It is the histologic hallmark of acute eczematous dermatitis, including allergic contact dermatitis, with a perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate below. Linear streaks of vesicles on exposed skin point to a plant brushing across the skin, while nickel causes rashes on the earlobes or wrist, and wet work gives hand dermatitis.","whyNot":["Regular acanthosis with parakeratosis is the chronic hyperproliferative pattern of psoriasis.","A subepidermal blister rich in eosinophils is bullous pemphigoid, an autoimmune disease of older adults.","Correct.","Suprabasal acantholysis with a row of basal cells left on the floor is pemphigus vulgaris, caused by antibodies against desmoglein 3."],"study":{"topics":["Spongiosis","Distribution clues in contact dermatitis"],"comps":["IN-13-B2","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C02-Q3","stem":"During his first exposure last summer, urushiol bound to skin proteins and was taken up by an antigen-presenting cell that resides in the epidermis. Electron microscopy of this cell type would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Tennis racket-shaped Birbeck granules","Melanosomes in various stages of maturation","Dense-core granules facing a nerve terminal","Metachromatic granules containing histamine"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-1","obj":"IN-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["The antigen-presenting cell of the epidermis is the Langerhans cell, found mostly in the stratum spinosum.","Langerhans cells come from the bone marrow, express CD1a and langerin, and capture antigens that enter the skin.","Their ultrastructural marker is the Birbeck granule, a rod-shaped organelle with a bulb at one end."],"why":"Langerhans cells are dendritic cells of bone marrow origin that sit in the spinous layer and extend processes between keratinocytes. They take up haptens and other antigens, then migrate to lymph nodes to present them to T cells, which is how sensitization to urushiol or nickel occurs. They stain for CD1a and S-100, and on electron microscopy they contain Birbeck granules, which are rod-shaped and often look like tennis rackets. The other dendritic-looking cells of the epidermis have different jobs and different granules.","whyNot":["Correct.","Melanosomes are made by melanocytes, which arise from neural crest, sit in the basal layer, and pass pigment to keratinocytes. Melanocytes do not present antigen.","Dense-core granules next to a nerve ending are typical of Merkel cells, the touch receptors of the basal layer.","Histamine-filled metachromatic granules belong to mast cells, which live in the dermis and drive type I reactions."],"study":{"topics":["Langerhans cells","Cells of the epidermis"],"comps":["IN-1","IN-11"]}},{"id":"IN-C02-Q4","stem":"After capturing the urushiol-modified proteins during his first exposure, the antigen-presenting cells left the epidermis. To which of the following sites did they most likely migrate to activate naive T cells?","options":["Periarteriolar sheath of the splenic white pulp","Medullary cords of a regional lymph node","Germinal center of a lymph node follicle","Paracortex of a regional lymph node"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-11","obj":"IN-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["Antigen from the skin is carried by Langerhans cells through afferent lymphatics, not the blood.","Dendritic cells deliver antigen to the zone where naive T cells gather.","In lymph nodes, naive T cells are concentrated in the paracortex, so that is where priming happens."],"why":"When keratinocytes are stressed, they release IL-1 and TNF and act as part of innate defense. These signals help Langerhans cells mature after they capture antigen. The cells then travel through afferent lymphatics to the draining lymph node, guided by the chemokine receptor CCR7, and settle in the paracortex, the T cell zone. There they present peptide on MHC molecules with costimulation to naive T cells. The primed effector and memory T cells later return to the skin, which is why a second exposure causes the rash.","whyNot":["The splenic periarteriolar sheath is a T cell zone, but the spleen filters blood-borne antigen. Skin antigens drain by lymphatics to regional nodes.","Medullary cords hold plasma cells and macrophages near the exit of the node. They are not the T cell priming zone.","Germinal centers in follicles are where activated B cells proliferate and undergo class switching. Naive T cell priming happens in the T cell zone.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Langerhans cell migration","Lymph node compartments"],"comps":["IN-11","IN-1"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C03","system":"IN","dx":"Erythema multiforme minor after recurrent herpes labialis","codes":["IN-13-B3"],"case":"A 22-year-old woman comes to student health because of a rash on her hands and forearms for 3 days. Ten days ago, she had a painful cold sore on her lower lip that has now crusted. She has had two similar rashes in the past 2 years, each after a cold sore. She takes no medications. Her temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 72/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows symmetric, fixed, round lesions 1 to 2 cm in diameter on the palms, dorsal hands, and extensor forearms. Each lesion has a dusky center, a pale edematous ring, and an outer erythematous ring. Two shallow erosions are present on the buccal mucosa. Nikolsky sign is negative. Diagnosis: erythema multiforme minor triggered by recurrent herpes labialis.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C03-Q1","stem":"A punch biopsy specimen is obtained from the center of a lesion on her hand. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Band-like lymphocytes with sawtooth rete ridges","Spongiosis forming intraepidermal vesicles","Interface dermatitis with necrotic keratinocytes","Neutrophils in venule walls with karyorrhectic debris"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-B3","obj":"IN-13-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Target lesions with a dusky center after herpes labialis are erythema multiforme.","The reaction is driven by cytotoxic T cells that attack keratinocytes expressing herpes simplex antigens.","T cells gather along the dermal-epidermal junction (interface) and kill keratinocytes, and the dead cells make the center dusky."],"why":"Erythema multiforme is a cell-mediated reaction, most often to herpes simplex virus and less often to Mycoplasma pneumoniae or drugs. Viral DNA fragments are carried to the skin, and cytotoxic T cells attack keratinocytes expressing viral antigen. Biopsy shows a lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermal-epidermal junction with vacuolar change of the basal layer and scattered necrotic (apoptotic) keratinocytes. Clinically, the lesions are fixed acral targets, and mucosal involvement is absent or limited to the mouth in the minor form.","whyNot":["A band of lymphocytes with sawtooth rete ridges is lichen planus, which presents as flat purple papules without targets.","Spongiosis with vesicles is the eczematous pattern of contact and atopic dermatitis, which presents as itchy, ill-defined patches.","Correct.","Neutrophils destroying venule walls with nuclear dust is leukocytoclastic vasculitis, which presents as palpable purpura."],"study":{"topics":["Erythema multiforme pathology","Interface dermatitis"],"comps":["IN-13-B3","IN-8"]}},{"id":"IN-C03-Q2","stem":"Her 21-year-old roommate began taking lamotrigine 2 weeks ago. She now has fever, painful hemorrhagic crusting of the lips, conjunctivitis, and dusky macules with flaccid blisters on her face and trunk. Light lateral pressure shears off the epidermis. The detached epidermis covers about 8% of her body surface area. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in the roommate?","options":["Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Toxic epidermal necrolysis","Erythema multiforme major","Stevens-Johnson syndrome"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-B","obj":"IN-13-B.6","dok":3,"approach":["A new anticonvulsant, fever, two or more mucosal sites, and sheets of detaching skin point to Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis.","These are one disease graded by the area of epidermal detachment.","Under 10% of body surface is Stevens-Johnson syndrome, 10 to 30% is overlap, and over 30% is toxic epidermal necrolysis. At 8% this is Stevens-Johnson syndrome."],"why":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis are severe drug reactions in which cytotoxic T cells cause widespread keratinocyte death and full-thickness epidermal necrosis. Common triggers include sulfonamides, anticonvulsants (lamotrigine, carbamazepine, phenytoin), and allopurinol, usually 1 to 4 weeks after starting the drug. Patients have fever, painful mucosal erosions at several sites, and a positive Nikolsky sign. The two names describe the same process, separated by the percentage of body surface that detaches.","whyNot":["Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome causes a superficial split from an exfoliative toxin in young children and spares the mucosa. This patient has prominent mucosal disease after a drug.","Toxic epidermal necrolysis requires detachment of more than 30% of body surface area. Her 8% is below that.","Erythema multiforme major has typical raised acral target lesions with mucosal involvement, usually after herpes simplex. It does not cause widespread sheets of detaching skin after a new drug.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome versus toxic epidermal necrolysis","Drugs that trigger severe skin reactions"],"comps":["IN-13-B","IN-13-B3"]}},{"id":"IN-C03-Q3","stem":"Between episodes of herpes labialis, the virus that triggers her rash persists in which of the following?","options":["Lower motor neurons of the facial nucleus","Sensory neurons of the trigeminal ganglion","Memory B cells in cervical lymph nodes","Basal keratinocytes at the vermilion border"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-12","obj":"IN-12.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recurrent cold sores are caused by herpes simplex virus type 1, an enveloped dsDNA virus.","After the first infection of the lip epithelium, the virus enters sensory nerve endings and travels by retrograde axonal transport to the cell body.","The lip is supplied by the trigeminal nerve, so the virus stays latent in the trigeminal ganglion and reactivates down the same nerve."],"why":"Herpes simplex virus and varicella zoster virus infect epithelium first, then enter sensory nerve endings and move to the neuron cell bodies in sensory ganglia. There the viral genome persists as an episome with very limited gene expression, hidden from the immune system. Stress, fever, sunlight, or menstruation can trigger reactivation, and the virus travels back down the axon to the skin. For herpes labialis, the ganglion is the trigeminal ganglion. Genital herpes reactivates from sacral dorsal root ganglia, and zoster appears in the dermatome of the affected ganglion. A Tzanck smear of a fresh vesicle shows multinucleated giant cells.","whyNot":["Herpes simplex virus enters through sensory nerve endings in the skin, not motor neurons. Facial motor neurons do not supply the lip epithelium.","Correct.","Memory B cells are the latency site of Epstein-Barr virus, not herpes simplex virus.","Keratinocytes are where the virus replicates during an outbreak, but they are killed or shed. They are not the site of latency."],"study":{"topics":["Herpes simplex latency and reactivation","Tzanck smear"],"comps":["IN-12","IN-13-B3"]}},{"id":"IN-C03-Q4","stem":"The dermis of her lips and cheeks develops from the same embryonic source as which of the following?","options":["Dermis of the lower back","Skeletal muscle of the tongue","Enamel-forming ameloblasts","Dentin-forming odontoblasts"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-2","obj":"IN-2.2","dok":3,"approach":["The dermis of the face and much of the head arises from neural crest cells of the head region.","Odontoblasts, which make dentin, are also neural crest derivatives.","The trunk dermis instead arises from somites (dermatomes), and tongue muscle arises from occipital somites."],"why":"Neural crest cells migrate widely and form melanocytes, Schwann cells, dorsal root and autonomic ganglia, the adrenal medulla, and odontoblasts. In the head they also form most of the facial connective tissue, including the facial dermis, bones of the face, and the dental papilla that becomes dentin and pulp. Below the head, dermis comes from the dermatome portion of the somites, with some contribution from lateral plate mesoderm in the limbs and ventral body wall. So the facial dermis and odontoblasts share an origin, while the dermis of the back does not.","whyNot":["The dermis of the back arises from the dermatomes of the somites, which is paraxial mesoderm, not neural crest.","Most tongue muscles come from occipital somite myoblasts that are innervated by the hypoglossal nerve. They are paraxial mesoderm.","Ameloblasts make enamel and come from the oral ectoderm that forms the enamel organ, not from neural crest.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neural crest derivatives","Origin of the dermis"],"comps":["IN-2","IN-1"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C04","system":"IN","dx":"Cholinergic urticaria","codes":["IN-13-B4"],"case":"A 19-year-old man who runs on his college cross-country team comes to the physician because of itchy, prickling bumps that appear within minutes of starting to run, during hot showers, and when he feels anxious. Each episode fades within an hour. He has no lip or tongue swelling, wheezing, or lightheadedness during episodes. He takes no medications. After 10 minutes of running on a treadmill in the office, his temperature is 37.8 °C (100.0 °F), pulse is 132/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 138/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows numerous 2- to 3-mm wheals surrounded by large erythematous flares on the trunk and upper arms. His skin is moist with sweat. Diagnosis: cholinergic urticaria.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C04-Q1","stem":"His 25-year-old cousin has had recurrent episodes of nonpitting swelling of the face, hands, and once the bowel wall, each lasting 2 to 3 days, without hives or itching. Serum C4 concentration is low. Which of the following mediators most likely causes the cousin's swelling?","options":["Histamine","Bradykinin","Leukotriene C4","Prostaglandin D2"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-B4","obj":"IN-13-B.5","dok":3,"approach":["Swelling that lasts days, involves deep tissue, and comes without hives or itch is angioedema that does not come from mast cells.","A low C4 points to C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency, because unchecked C1 keeps consuming C4.","C1 inhibitor also controls kallikrein, so its loss lets kallikrein cleave kininogen into bradykinin, which opens venules."],"why":"Urticaria comes from mast cell degranulation, which releases histamine and other mediators into the superficial dermis and produces itchy wheals that each last less than 24 hours. Angioedema is the same kind of leak in the deep dermis and subcutis. Hereditary angioedema from C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency is different: it causes swelling without hives or itch, lasts days, and can involve the larynx and gut. C1 inhibitor normally blocks kallikrein and factor XIIa, so when it is missing, bradykinin accumulates. Antihistamines do not help, and ACE inhibitors, which slow bradykinin breakdown, must be avoided.","whyNot":["Histamine from mast cells causes itchy wheals and allergic angioedema. The cousin has no hives and a low C4, which points away from mast cells.","Correct.","Leukotriene C4 is a mast cell product that contributes to allergic bronchoconstriction and vascular leak. It is not the mediator of complement-linked angioedema.","Prostaglandin D2 is released by mast cells and causes flushing and bronchoconstriction. It is not the mediator in C1 inhibitor deficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Urticaria versus angioedema","Hereditary angioedema"],"comps":["IN-13-B4","IN-11"]}},{"id":"IN-C04-Q2","stem":"The wheals on his trunk represent fluid that has leaked into the superficial dermis within minutes. Which of the following most directly explains this leak?","options":["Endothelial cell contraction in postcapillary venules","Neutrophil-mediated injury of capillary endothelial cells","Lymphatic obstruction trapping filtered protein in the dermis","Arteriolar constriction raising capillary hydrostatic pressure"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-11","obj":"IN-11.2","dok":3,"approach":["A wheal forms within minutes and fades within hours, so the leak must be fast and reversible.","Histamine from mast cells binds H1 receptors on endothelial cells.","The endothelial cells of postcapillary venules contract, opening gaps between them, so protein-rich fluid escapes into the dermis."],"why":"Increased vascular permeability is the first step of acute inflammation. Histamine, bradykinin, and leukotrienes make endothelial cells of postcapillary venules contract, which opens gaps between cells within seconds to minutes. The response is short-lived, which fits a wheal that fades within an hour. The surrounding flare is arteriolar dilation through an axon reflex. Later steps of inflammation bring leukocytes to the same venules: selectins mediate rolling, integrins binding ICAM-1 give firm adhesion, PECAM-1 allows transmigration, and C5a, LTB4, and IL-8 guide chemotaxis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Neutrophils can injure endothelium and cause a slower, longer-lasting leak, but they are not present in a wheal that forms within minutes.","Lymphatic obstruction causes slowly developing, persistent lymphedema, not swelling that comes and goes within an hour.","Arteriolar constriction would lower capillary pressure and reduce flow. The flare around the wheal shows that arterioles are dilated."],"study":{"topics":["Vascular permeability in acute inflammation","Wheal and flare"],"comps":["IN-11","IN-13-B4"]}},{"id":"IN-C04-Q3","stem":"His wheals appear as his core temperature rises and he begins to sweat. The sweat glands responsible for his thermoregulatory sweating are innervated by which of the following?","options":["Parasympathetic fibers releasing acetylcholine","Sympathetic fibers releasing norepinephrine","Sympathetic fibers releasing acetylcholine","Somatic motor fibers releasing acetylcholine"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-1","obj":"IN-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Thermoregulatory sweat comes from eccrine glands, which open directly onto the skin over most of the body.","Eccrine glands are innervated by the sympathetic nervous system, but the postganglionic fibers release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors.","In cholinergic urticaria, this acetylcholine-driven sweating response is linked to the hives, which explains the name."],"why":"Eccrine glands are coiled glands in the deep dermis that open directly onto the skin surface and make watery sweat for cooling. They are an exception in the autonomic system: they receive sympathetic postganglionic fibers that release acetylcholine onto M3 muscarinic receptors. That is why anticholinergic drugs cause dry skin and why botulinum toxin treats hyperhidrosis. Apocrine glands open into hair follicles in the axilla and anogenital area and respond mainly to adrenergic stimulation. Sebaceous glands are holocrine and controlled by androgens rather than nerves.","whyNot":["The skin has essentially no parasympathetic supply. Eccrine glands receive sympathetic fibers even though those fibers release acetylcholine.","Sympathetic adrenergic fibers drive most sympathetic targets and act on apocrine glands, but eccrine thermoregulatory sweating uses acetylcholine.","Correct.","Somatic motor fibers release acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors of skeletal muscle. They do not supply glands."],"study":{"topics":["Eccrine, apocrine, and sebaceous glands","Sympathetic cholinergic sweating"],"comps":["IN-1","IN-4"]}},{"id":"IN-C04-Q4","stem":"His teammate receives a tetanus toxoid booster 2 years after his previous dose. Six hours later, a painful, indurated, erythematous area develops at the injection site and becomes hemorrhagic over the next day. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of the teammate's reaction?","options":["IgE cross-linking on sensitized dermal mast cells","IgG binding to keratinocyte surface glycoproteins","Local immune complexes activating complement","Memory T cells releasing cytokines"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-11","obj":"IN-11.4","dok":3,"approach":["A frequent booster leaves high levels of circulating IgG against tetanus toxoid.","Injected antigen meets that IgG in the vessel walls of the dermis and forms immune complexes.","Complement activation and neutrophils damage the vessels within hours, which is a type III (Arthus) reaction."],"why":"The Arthus reaction is a localized type III hypersensitivity. When antigen is injected into a person with high levels of specific IgG, immune complexes form in and around small dermal vessels. They activate complement, releasing C5a, which draws neutrophils that damage vessel walls, causing edema, hemorrhage, and sometimes necrosis about 4 to 12 hours later. Type I reactions such as urticaria appear within minutes, type II reactions involve antibody against fixed tissue antigens such as pemphigus, and type IV reactions such as the tuberculin test peak at 48 to 72 hours.","whyNot":["IgE cross-linking on mast cells is type I hypersensitivity, which causes hives or anaphylaxis within minutes, not a hemorrhagic plaque after 6 hours.","IgG binding to fixed tissue antigens is type II hypersensitivity, as in pemphigus. Tetanus toxoid is not a keratinocyte antigen.","Correct.","Memory T cell reactions are type IV and take 48 to 72 hours to peak, as in the tuberculin skin test."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types in the skin","Arthus reaction"],"comps":["IN-11","IN-13-B4"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C05","system":"IN","dx":"Papulopustular rosacea","codes":["IN-13-C1"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of facial redness for 2 years. She flushes easily, and the redness worsens after hot drinks, red wine, spicy food, and time in the sun. Her eyes often feel gritty. She has never had acne. Her temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 70/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 124/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows persistent erythema of the central cheeks, nose, chin, and forehead with telangiectasias and scattered papules and pustules. No comedones are present. The eyelid margins are mildly erythematous. There is no joint swelling. Diagnosis: papulopustular rosacea.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C05-Q1","stem":"If her disease progresses over many years without treatment, which of the following changes of the nose is most likely to develop?","options":["Pearly papule with rolled borders on the ala","Thickened nasal skin with sebaceous hyperplasia","Perforation of the nasal septum from vasculitis","Collapse of the nasal bridge from cartilage loss"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-C1","obj":"IN-13-C.1","dok":2,"approach":["Rosacea is a chronic inflammatory disease of the central face with flushing, persistent erythema, telangiectasias, and papules and pustules.","Long-standing inflammation and vascular leak in the nose lead to fibrosis and enlargement of sebaceous glands.","The result is a bulbous, thickened nose called rhinophyma, which is more common in men but can occur in women."],"why":"Rosacea begins with flushing triggered by heat, sunlight, alcohol, spicy food, and emotion, and progresses to persistent central facial erythema with telangiectasias. Papules and pustules then appear, without the comedones of acne. In the phymatous form, years of inflammation cause fibrosis and sebaceous gland hyperplasia, most often in the nose (rhinophyma). Ocular rosacea is another complication, with gritty eyes, blepharitis, and conjunctival injection, as her eyelid margins suggest.","whyNot":["A pearly papule with rolled borders is basal cell carcinoma, a result of cumulative UV damage, not of rosacea.","Correct.","Septal perforation occurs with cocaine use or granulomatosis with polyangiitis, not rosacea.","Saddle-nose collapse comes from destruction of nasal cartilage, as in granulomatosis with polyangiitis, relapsing polychondritis, or congenital syphilis."],"study":{"topics":["Rosacea stages and complications","Rhinophyma"],"comps":["IN-13-C1","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C05-Q2","stem":"Which of the following additional findings in this patient would most strongly suggest systemic lupus erythematosus rather than rosacea?","options":["Erythema sparing the nasolabial folds","Greasy yellow scale in the nasolabial folds","Flushing within minutes of drinking red wine","Open and closed comedones on the forehead"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-C1","obj":"IN-13-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["Rosacea, lupus, acne, and seborrheic dermatitis can all cause a red face, so look for the feature that belongs to only one.","The malar rash of lupus covers the cheeks and nasal bridge but characteristically spares the nasolabial folds.","Greasy scale belongs to seborrheic dermatitis, comedones belong to acne, and trigger-linked flushing belongs to rosacea."],"why":"The butterfly (malar) rash of systemic lupus is a photosensitive erythema over the cheeks and nasal bridge that typically spares the nasolabial folds, and it lacks pustules. Rosacea has central facial erythema with flushing triggers, telangiectasias, papules, and pustules without comedones. Acne vulgaris begins in adolescence and is defined by comedones. Seborrheic dermatitis causes greasy yellow scale in the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and scalp. Each condition has a feature that sets it apart.","whyNot":["Correct.","Greasy yellow scale in the nasolabial folds and eyebrows is seborrheic dermatitis, which often coexists with rosacea but is not lupus.","Flushing after alcohol, heat, or spicy food is a classic rosacea trigger and supports her current diagnosis.","Comedones are the primary lesion of acne vulgaris. Their presence points to acne, not lupus."],"study":{"topics":["Differential diagnosis of facial erythema","Malar rash of lupus"],"comps":["IN-13-C1","IN-13-H1"]}},{"id":"IN-C05-Q3","stem":"On a hot day, as her core temperature rises, her skin vessels dilate and she begins to sweat. Which of the following brain regions integrates the rise in core temperature and drives these heat loss responses?","options":["Ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus","Supraoptic nucleus above the optic chiasm","Posterior hypothalamus near the mammillary bodies","Preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-4","obj":"IN-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Heat loss responses are cutaneous vasodilation and sweating.","The hypothalamus compares core and skin temperature input with a set point.","The preoptic anterior hypothalamus drives heat loss, and the posterior hypothalamus drives heat conservation and heat production."],"why":"Core temperature is sensed by warm-sensitive neurons in the preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus, which also receive input from skin thermoreceptors. When temperature rises above the set point, this region reduces sympathetic tone to skin arterioles, causing vasodilation, and activates sympathetic cholinergic fibers to eccrine glands, causing sweating. When temperature falls, the posterior hypothalamus drives vasoconstriction, piloerection, and shivering. A lesion of the anterior hypothalamus causes hyperthermia, and a lesion of the posterior hypothalamus causes poor tolerance of cold.","whyNot":["The ventromedial nucleus is a satiety center. Lesions cause overeating and obesity, not loss of heat responses.","The supraoptic nucleus makes antidiuretic hormone for water balance. It does not control skin blood flow or sweating.","The posterior hypothalamus drives heat conservation and production, such as vasoconstriction and shivering. It is not the heat loss center.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypothalamic thermoregulation","Heat loss and heat gain centers"],"comps":["IN-4","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C05-Q4","stem":"Ultraviolet light, one of her triggers, generates reactive oxygen species in the skin, including superoxide and hydrogen peroxide. Which of the following reactions produces the hydroxyl radical, the most damaging of these species?","options":["Breakdown of hydrogen peroxide by catalase","Reaction of hydrogen peroxide with ferrous iron","Dismutation of superoxide by superoxide dismutase","Reduction of hydrogen peroxide by glutathione peroxidase"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-6","obj":"IN-6.1","dok":3,"approach":["Superoxide is converted to hydrogen peroxide, which is not itself a radical.","Hydrogen peroxide becomes far more dangerous when it meets a transition metal such as Fe2+.","Fe2+ plus hydrogen peroxide gives Fe3+, hydroxide, and the hydroxyl radical. This is the Fenton reaction."],"why":"Reactive oxygen species form from electron leak in mitochondria, from NADPH oxidase in phagocytes, and from UV and ionizing radiation. Superoxide is converted to hydrogen peroxide by superoxide dismutase. In the presence of free ferrous iron, hydrogen peroxide undergoes the Fenton reaction to form the hydroxyl radical, the most reactive species. Hydroxyl radicals start lipid peroxidation of membranes, oxidize proteins, and break DNA strands. Oxidative stress from UV exposure is thought to contribute to the inflammation of rosacea.","whyNot":["Catalase breaks hydrogen peroxide down to water and oxygen, which removes it safely rather than making radicals.","Correct.","Superoxide dismutase converts superoxide to hydrogen peroxide and oxygen. It is a protective step and does not make hydroxyl radicals.","Glutathione peroxidase reduces hydrogen peroxide to water using glutathione. It is an antioxidant defense."],"study":{"topics":["Fenton reaction","Reactive oxygen species and antioxidant enzymes"],"comps":["IN-6","IN-10"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C06","system":"IN","dx":"Oral and cutaneous lichen planus with chronic hepatitis C","codes":["IN-13-C2"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman who works as a nurse comes to the physician because of an itchy rash on her wrists and shins for 3 months and a burning sensation in her mouth when she eats spicy food. Twenty-five years ago, she had a needle-stick injury from a hollow-bore needle used on a patient of unknown infection status and was not tested afterward. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows flat-topped, violaceous, polygonal papules on the flexor wrists and shins, with fine white lines on their surfaces. A lacy white network is present on both buccal mucosae and cannot be scraped off. Serum alanine aminotransferase activity is 68 U/L (7 to 35). Anti-hepatitis C virus antibody is positive, and hepatitis C virus RNA is detected. Diagnosis: oral and cutaneous lichen planus associated with chronic hepatitis C.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C06-Q1","stem":"A biopsy specimen of a papule on her wrist would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Subepidermal blister with many eosinophils","Regular acanthosis with neutrophils in the parakeratotic scale","Bandlike T cell infiltrate with sawtooth rete ridges","Spongiosis with small intraepidermal vesicles"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-C2","obj":"IN-13-C.3","dok":2,"approach":["Purple, polygonal, planar, pruritic papules with white lines (Wickham striae) are lichen planus.","Lichen planus is an interface dermatitis driven by T cells attacking basal keratinocytes.","A dense band of lymphocytes hugs the dermal-epidermal junction, and damage to the basal layer reshapes the rete ridges into a sawtooth pattern."],"why":"Lichen planus is a T cell-mediated attack on basal keratinocytes. Biopsy shows a dense, bandlike lymphocytic infiltrate along the dermal-epidermal junction, vacuolar damage to the basal layer, hypergranulosis, and pointed, sawtooth rete ridges. Dead basal keratinocytes appear as round eosinophilic Civatte (colloid) bodies. Clinically, the papules are pruritic, purple, polygonal, and planar, with Wickham striae on the surface. Oral lesions are a lacy white network, and lichen planus has a well-known association with hepatitis C infection.","whyNot":["A subepidermal blister with eosinophils is bullous pemphigoid, which forms tense bullae.","Regular acanthosis with neutrophils in parakeratotic scale (Munro microabscesses) is psoriasis, which forms silvery-scaled plaques.","Correct.","Spongiosis with vesicles is the eczematous pattern of contact or atopic dermatitis, not the purple flat papules of lichen planus."],"study":{"topics":["Lichen planus histology","The six Ps of lichen planus"],"comps":["IN-13-C2","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C06-Q2","stem":"Her 58-year-old brother has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years and drinks heavily. He has a painless, sharply demarcated, 2-cm white plaque on the floor of his mouth that cannot be scraped off. He has no other skin or mucosal lesions, and HIV testing is negative. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in the brother?","options":["Oral leukoplakia","Oral hairy leukoplakia","Pseudomembranous candidiasis","Reticular lichen planus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-F","obj":"IN-13-F.7","dok":3,"approach":["A white plaque that cannot be scraped off rules out thrush, which wipes away.","A single sharply demarcated plaque on the floor of the mouth in a heavy smoker and drinker, with negative HIV testing, fits leukoplakia.","Lichen planus forms a lacy, bilateral network, and hairy leukoplakia forms corrugated plaques on the lateral tongue in immunosuppressed patients."],"why":"Leukoplakia is a clinical term for a white oral plaque that cannot be scraped off and has no other specific cause. It is strongly linked to tobacco and alcohol use. Biopsy may show only hyperkeratosis or may show dysplasia, and a fraction of lesions progress to squamous cell carcinoma, especially on the floor of the mouth and ventral tongue. For that reason, every leukoplakia should be biopsied. Oral hairy leukoplakia is caused by Epstein-Barr virus in immunosuppressed patients and is not premalignant.","whyNot":["Correct.","Oral hairy leukoplakia causes corrugated white plaques on the lateral tongue in patients with HIV or other immunosuppression. It is caused by Epstein-Barr virus.","Thrush forms creamy white patches that scrape off and leave a red base. The brother's plaque cannot be scraped off.","Reticular lichen planus forms a bilateral, lacy white network on the buccal mucosa, like his sister's. A single solid plaque on the floor of the mouth does not fit."],"study":{"topics":["White lesions of the oral cavity","Leukoplakia and oral cancer risk"],"comps":["IN-13-F","IN-13-C2"]}},{"id":"IN-C06-Q3","stem":"Assume her needle-stick source patient had been infected with hepatitis B virus (HBeAg positive), hepatitis C virus, and HIV, and that she was not immune to hepatitis B at the time. Which of the following ranks her risk of acquiring each infection from that single injury, from highest to lowest?","options":["Hepatitis C virus, hepatitis B virus, HIV","Hepatitis B virus, HIV, hepatitis C virus","HIV, hepatitis C virus, hepatitis B virus","Hepatitis B virus, hepatitis C virus, HIV"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-13-H12","obj":"IN-13-H.12","dok":2,"approach":["Transmission risk after a needle stick depends mostly on how much virus is in the blood.","Hepatitis B, especially when the source is HBeAg positive, reaches very high blood levels, so the risk can be 30% or more.","Hepatitis C carries a risk of about 2%, and HIV about 0.3%."],"why":"Hepatitis B virus is the most transmissible bloodborne pathogen after a percutaneous injury. With an HBeAg-positive source and a nonimmune worker, the risk of clinical hepatitis is about 22 to 31%, and the risk of any infection is higher still, which is why hepatitis B vaccination is required for health care workers. Hepatitis C transmits in about 1 to 2% of exposures, and HIV in about 0.3%. Risk rises with a hollow-bore needle that carries blood inside it, a deep injury, visible blood on the device, and a high viral load in the source.","whyNot":["Hepatitis C transmission after a needle stick is about 2%, well below the risk from an HBeAg-positive hepatitis B source.","Hepatitis B is the highest, but HIV is lower than hepatitis C, not higher.","HIV has the lowest transmission risk of the three, about 0.3% after a percutaneous exposure.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Needle-stick transmission risk","Hepatitis B vaccination for health care workers"],"comps":["IN-13-H12","IN-12"]}},{"id":"IN-C06-Q4","stem":"Her biopsy specimen also shows round, eosinophilic, anucleate bodies near the dermal-epidermal junction with no surrounding neutrophils. Which of the following mechanisms most directly produced these bodies?","options":["Membrane attack complex lysis after antibody binding","Granzyme B delivered through perforin pores","Inflammasome activation of caspase 1","ATP depletion with cell swelling and membrane rupture"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-8","obj":"IN-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["These are Civatte bodies, which are basal keratinocytes that died by apoptosis.","Shrunken, eosinophilic cells with no inflammation around them mean apoptosis, not necrosis.","In lichen planus, CD8 cytotoxic T cells trigger apoptosis by releasing perforin and granzyme B, which activates caspases inside the target cell."],"why":"Cytotoxic T cells kill target cells in two main ways. They release perforin, which forms pores in the target membrane, and granzyme B, which enters through them and activates caspases. They can also use Fas ligand to engage Fas and activate caspase 8, which is the extrinsic pathway. The intrinsic pathway instead starts inside the cell, when BCL-2 family proteins allow cytochrome c to leave the mitochondria and activate caspase 9. Apoptotic cells shrink, condense their nuclei, and break into apoptotic bodies that are removed without inflammation. Necrosis causes swelling, rupture, and inflammation.","whyNot":["Complement lysis after antibody binding is a type II mechanism. It causes cells to swell and burst, and lichen planus is not antibody mediated.","Correct.","Inflammasome activation of caspase 1 causes pyroptosis, an inflammatory form of cell death that releases IL-1 beta.","ATP depletion with swelling and rupture is necrosis, which provokes inflammation. These bodies are shrunken and have no inflammation around them."],"study":{"topics":["Apoptosis by cytotoxic T cells","Apoptosis versus necrosis"],"comps":["IN-8","IN-13-C2"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C07","system":"IN","dx":"Erythrodermic psoriasis with psoriatic nail pitting","codes":["IN-13-C3"],"case":"A 60-year-old man is admitted to the hospital from the emergency department because of generalized redness and scaling of his skin for 1 week, with chills. He has had plaque psoriasis on his elbows, knees, and scalp for 20 years. A course of oral prednisone prescribed for a flare was stopped abruptly 10 days ago. His temperature is 36.0 °C (96.8 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Physical examination shows erythema with fine scale covering more than 90% of his body surface. The fingernails show many small pits, distal separation of the nail plate from the nail bed, and yellow-brown oil-drop discoloration. Diagnosis: erythrodermic psoriasis with psoriatic nail disease.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C07-Q1","stem":"Which of the following cytokine pathways is most central to the keratinocyte hyperproliferation in his disease?","options":["IL-5 release by Th2 cells recruiting eosinophils","IL-4 and IL-13 release by Th2 cells","IL-23 sustaining IL-17 release by Th17 cells","IL-10 and TGF-beta release by regulatory T cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-13-C3","obj":"IN-13-C.4","dok":2,"approach":["Psoriasis is a T cell-mediated disease with genetic risk (HLA-Cw6) and triggers such as trauma (Koebner phenomenon), infection, and steroid withdrawal.","Dendritic cells in the skin release IL-23, which expands and maintains Th17 cells.","IL-17 and IL-22 from Th17 cells drive keratinocyte hyperproliferation, and TNF amplifies the inflammation."],"why":"In psoriasis, activated dermal dendritic cells make IL-23, which maintains Th17 cells. These cells release IL-17 and IL-22, which make keratinocytes divide rapidly and produce chemokines and antimicrobial peptides that draw in more neutrophils and T cells. TNF amplifies the loop. This explains why biologic drugs that block TNF, IL-17, or IL-23 work so well. Clinically, psoriasis causes silvery-scaled plaques on extensor surfaces and scalp, nail pitting, and in some patients psoriatic arthritis. Abrupt withdrawal of systemic steroids can trigger erythrodermic or pustular flares.","whyNot":["IL-5 recruits and activates eosinophils, as in asthma and parasitic infection. Psoriatic plaques are rich in neutrophils and T cells, not eosinophils.","IL-4 and IL-13 from Th2 cells drive atopic dermatitis, the barrier-related itchy eczema of atopic patients.","Correct.","IL-10 and TGF-beta are suppressive cytokines that dampen inflammation. They do not drive keratinocyte proliferation."],"study":{"topics":["IL-23 and Th17 axis in psoriasis","Psoriasis triggers"],"comps":["IN-13-C3","IN-11"]}},{"id":"IN-C07-Q2","stem":"Before this episode, scraping the scale from a plaque on his elbow produced pinpoint bleeding. Which of the following histologic features best explains this finding?","options":["Thin suprapapillary plates over dilated capillaries","Reduced or absent stratum granulosum","Neutrophil collections within the parakeratotic scale","Uniformly elongated rete ridges with acanthosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-C3","obj":"IN-13-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Pinpoint bleeding after removing scale is the Auspitz sign.","For bleeding to occur, blood vessels must sit very close to the surface.","In psoriasis, the dermal papillae extend upward with dilated, tortuous capillaries, and the epidermis above each papilla (the suprapapillary plate) is thinned."],"why":"Psoriasis has a characteristic histology: acanthosis with regularly elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis (retained nuclei in the stratum corneum), a thin or absent stratum granulosum, and neutrophil collections in the stratum corneum (Munro microabscesses). The dermal papillae are tall, with dilated, tortuous capillaries, and the epidermis over the tips of the papillae is thin. When the scale is lifted off, only a few cell layers separate the capillaries from the surface, so they tear and bleed in tiny points. That is the Auspitz sign.","whyNot":["Correct.","Loss of the granular layer explains the defective keratinization and parakeratotic scale. It does not explain bleeding.","Munro microabscesses are a classic psoriasis finding, but neutrophils in the scale do not explain bleeding when the scale is removed.","Elongated rete ridges thicken the epidermis between papillae. They do not put vessels near the surface."],"study":{"topics":["Psoriasis histology","Auspitz sign"],"comps":["IN-13-C3","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C07-Q3","stem":"The pits in his fingernail plates most likely reflect psoriatic involvement of which of the following parts of the nail unit?","options":["Nail bed beneath the plate","Hyponychium under the free edge","Lateral nail fold","Proximal nail matrix"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-1","obj":"IN-1.7","dok":3,"approach":["Pits are small defects on the upper surface of the nail plate.","The nail plate is made by the matrix, and the proximal matrix forms the top (dorsal) layer of the plate.","Psoriatic foci in the proximal matrix shed clusters of abnormal cells that leave pits as the plate grows out."],"why":"The nail unit has a matrix under the proximal nail fold that makes the nail plate, a nail bed that the plate rests on, a hyponychium under the free edge, and proximal and lateral folds that frame the plate. The proximal part of the matrix makes the dorsal plate surface, so psoriasis there causes pits. Psoriasis in the nail bed causes oil-drop discoloration, subungual hyperkeratosis, and onycholysis, which he also has. An ingrown nail occurs when the plate grows into the lateral nail fold.","whyNot":["Nail bed disease causes oil-drop spots, splinter hemorrhages, and onycholysis. The nail bed does not form the plate surface, so it does not cause pits.","The hyponychium seals the space under the free edge. Disease there causes subungual hyperkeratosis and lifting, not pits.","The lateral nail fold borders the plate. It is the site of ingrown nails and paronychia, not pitting.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Nail unit anatomy","Nail findings in psoriasis"],"comps":["IN-1","IN-13-C3"]}},{"id":"IN-C07-Q4","stem":"During a heat wave, the air in his room reaches 38 °C (100.4 °F), which is higher than his skin temperature. Which of the following is the only route by which he can still lose heat to the environment?","options":["Evaporation of water from skin","Radiation to surrounding surfaces","Conduction to the bed surface","Convection to the moving room air"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-4","obj":"IN-4.2","dok":3,"approach":["Radiation, conduction, and convection all move heat from a warmer object to a cooler one.","When the environment is warmer than the skin, these routes reverse and the body gains heat.","Evaporation removes heat as water changes to vapor, so it still works when the air is hotter than the skin."],"why":"At rest in a cool room, most heat is lost by radiation, with smaller amounts by conduction, convection, and evaporation. The first three depend on a temperature gradient from skin to surroundings. When ambient temperature rises above skin temperature, the gradient reverses and the body absorbs heat by those routes. Evaporation of sweat and water from skin is then the only way to lose heat. High humidity slows evaporation, which is why humid heat is so dangerous, and patients with extensive skin disease such as erythroderma are especially vulnerable to temperature swings.","whyNot":["Correct.","Radiation sends heat to cooler surfaces. When surroundings are warmer than skin, he gains heat by radiation.","Conduction moves heat to a cooler object in contact. A bed at room temperature above skin temperature would add heat.","Convection carries heat away only if the moving air is cooler than the skin. Here the air is warmer, so convection adds heat."],"study":{"topics":["Routes of heat loss","Heat loss in hot and humid environments"],"comps":["IN-4","IN-1"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C08","system":"IN","dx":"Recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa","codes":["IN-13-D1"],"case":"A newborn girl in the neonatal intensive care unit is evaluated for blisters that were present at birth. She was born at 39 weeks' gestation to healthy parents who are first cousins. Her 4-year-old brother is healthy. Physical examination shows blisters and erosions on the hands, feet, knees, and elbows, and new blisters form at sites of handling and where the diaper rubs. Oral erosions are present, and several toenails are thickened and dystrophic. Older erosions are healing with atrophic scars and small white cysts. Immunofluorescence mapping of a skin biopsy specimen shows that the blister forms beneath the lamina densa. Diagnosis: recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C08-Q1","stem":"A mutation in the gene for which of the following proteins is most likely responsible for her disease?","options":["Type XVII collagen","Laminin 332","Type VII collagen","Keratins 5 and 14"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-13-D1","obj":"IN-13-D.1","dok":2,"approach":["The split is beneath the lamina densa, at the level of the anchoring fibrils, and the blisters heal with scars and milia.","Anchoring fibrils are made of type VII collagen, which ties the lamina densa to the papillary dermis.","Loss of type VII collagen (COL7A1 mutations) causes dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa."],"why":"Epidermolysis bullosa types are defined by where the skin splits, which depends on the defective protein. In the simplex type, mutations in keratins 5 and 14 make basal keratinocytes fragile, so the split is within the basal cells. In the junctional type, defects in laminin 332 or collagen XVII separate the skin through the lamina lucida. In the dystrophic type, defective type VII collagen weakens anchoring fibrils, so the split is below the lamina densa. Because this split is in the dermis, blisters heal with scarring and milia.","whyNot":["Collagen XVII (BP180) defects also cause junctional epidermolysis bullosa through the lamina lucida. This protein is the target of autoantibodies in bullous pemphigoid.","Laminin 332 defects cause junctional epidermolysis bullosa, with a split in the lamina lucida above the lamina densa.","Correct.","Keratin 5 and 14 mutations cause epidermolysis bullosa simplex, with a split within basal keratinocytes that usually heals without scarring. It is usually autosomal dominant."],"study":{"topics":["Types of epidermolysis bullosa","Anchoring fibrils"],"comps":["IN-13-D1","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C08-Q2","stem":"As she grows older, she is at greatest risk of developing which of the following malignancies?","options":["Cutaneous T cell lymphoma of the trunk","Squamous cell carcinoma in chronic wounds","Basal cell carcinoma on the sun-exposed face","Melanoma arising in a congenital nevus"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-D1","obj":"IN-13-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["In recessive dystrophic disease, blisters break down into chronic wounds that heal with scars, over and over.","Repeated wounding and scarring create an environment that promotes keratinocyte cancer.","These patients develop aggressive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, often by early adulthood, and it is a major cause of death."],"why":"Recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa begins at birth with blisters from minor friction. The skin, mouth, esophagus, and nails are involved. Cycles of blistering and scarring cause mitten deformities with fused fingers and toes, joint contractures, esophageal strictures, and loss of nails. The most serious long-term complication is squamous cell carcinoma arising in chronic wounds and scars. These cancers appear in adolescence or early adulthood, grow aggressively, and spread early.","whyNot":["Cutaneous T cell lymphoma (mycosis fungoides) arises from skin-homing T cells and is not linked to epidermolysis bullosa.","Correct.","Basal cell carcinoma is driven by cumulative UV exposure and is not the cancer linked to chronic wounds in epidermolysis bullosa.","Melanoma in a large congenital nevus is a separate risk that does not depend on blistering disease."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa","Squamous cell carcinoma in chronic wounds"],"comps":["IN-13-D1","IN-10"]}},{"id":"IN-C08-Q3","stem":"Her parents are both heterozygous carriers of the disease-causing mutation. Which of the following is the probability that her healthy 4-year-old brother is a carrier?","options":["1/4","1/2","2/3","3/4"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-7","obj":"IN-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Healthy parents with an affected child, plus consanguinity, point to autosomal recessive inheritance with both parents as carriers.","A carrier-by-carrier cross gives 1/4 unaffected noncarrier, 1/2 carrier, and 1/4 affected.","The brother is known to be unaffected, so remove the affected 1/4. Of the remaining 3/4, carriers make up 2/4, so his risk is 2/3."],"why":"When both parents are carriers of an autosomal recessive mutation, each child has a 1/4 chance of being affected, 1/2 of being a carrier, and 1/4 of being a noncarrier. Once a sibling is known to be unaffected, the affected outcome is no longer possible, so the denominator becomes 3/4. His chance of being a carrier is (1/2) divided by (3/4), which equals 2/3. Each new pregnancy for these parents still has a 1/4 chance of an affected child. Autosomal dominant skin disorders such as neurofibromatosis type 1 and epidermolysis bullosa simplex instead give a 1/2 risk to each child of an affected parent.","whyNot":["1/4 is the chance that any child of two carriers is affected. It is not his carrier risk given that he is healthy.","1/2 is the carrier risk for a child of two carriers before anything is known about the child. Knowing he is unaffected raises it.","Correct.","3/4 is the chance that a child of two carriers is unaffected, not the chance that a healthy child is a carrier."],"study":{"topics":["Autosomal recessive recurrence risk","Carrier probability in an unaffected sibling"],"comps":["IN-7","IN-13-D1"]}},{"id":"IN-C08-Q4","stem":"Another infant in the unit, born at term, is covered with a white, greasy coating at birth. This coating is composed mainly of which of the following?","options":["Apocrine secretions trapped in the lanugo hair","Maternal IgG deposited on the fetal skin surface","Eccrine sweat mixed with amniotic fluid proteins","Sebum mixed with shed periderm and keratinocytes"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-2","obj":"IN-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["The white, greasy coating on a newborn is vernix caseosa.","Fetal sebaceous glands become active late in gestation under the influence of maternal and fetal hormones.","Their sebum mixes with desquamated periderm and keratinocytes, and lanugo hair helps hold the coating on the skin."],"why":"The fetal epidermis starts as a single layer covered by the periderm, a temporary outer layer that is shed as the epidermis stratifies. Melanoblasts migrate in from the neural crest, and hair buds grow down into the dermis over dermal papillae to form hair follicles. Fine lanugo hair covers the fetus in the second half of pregnancy. Sebaceous glands bud from the hair follicles and secrete sebum, which combines with shed periderm and keratinocytes to form vernix caseosa. Vernix protects the skin from amniotic fluid and helps with barrier function and temperature control after birth.","whyNot":["Apocrine glands become active at puberty. They do not make vernix.","Maternal IgG crosses the placenta into the fetal blood. It does not form a coating on the skin.","Eccrine glands are not yet secreting much sweat at birth, and vernix is lipid rich, not watery.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Fetal skin development","Vernix caseosa"],"comps":["IN-2","IN-1"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C09","system":"IN","dx":"Bullous pemphigoid","codes":["IN-13-D2"],"case":"An 82-year-old man in a nursing home, bedbound since a stroke 1 year ago, is evaluated for an intensely itchy rash with blisters for 3 weeks. His temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 78/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 132/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tense bullae 1 to 3 cm in diameter filled with clear fluid, many arising on red, urticarial plaques of the inner thighs, flexor forearms, and lower abdomen. Nikolsky sign is negative. There are no oral lesions. Over the sacrum, there is a 3-cm ulcer with full-thickness skin loss and visible subcutaneous fat. No bone, tendon, or muscle is visible. Diagnosis: bullous pemphigoid.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C09-Q1","stem":"Which of the following most directly causes the epidermis to separate from the dermis in his blistering disease?","options":["IgG to desmosomes directly blocking cell adhesion","Cytotoxic T cells killing basal keratinocytes","IgG to hemidesmosomes with complement and eosinophils","IgA deposits in dermal papillae attracting neutrophils"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-13-D2","obj":"IN-13-D.3","dok":2,"approach":["Tense bullae on urticarial plaques in an older adult, with a negative Nikolsky sign and no oral lesions, are bullous pemphigoid.","The target antigens are the hemidesmosomal proteins BP180 (collagen XVII) and BP230, which anchor basal cells to the basement membrane.","Bound IgG activates complement, which recruits eosinophils and neutrophils whose proteases cut through the lamina lucida."],"why":"Bullous pemphigoid is a type II hypersensitivity in which IgG autoantibodies bind BP180 and BP230 in hemidesmosomes. The antibodies cause most of their damage through inflammation rather than by blocking adhesion directly. They activate complement, and the inflammatory cells that arrive, especially eosinophils, release proteases that split the skin in the lamina lucida. The full-thickness epidermis forms the blister roof, so bullae are tense and rarely rupture. Direct immunofluorescence shows linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane. It mainly affects people over 70, and oral lesions are uncommon.","whyNot":["Antibodies against desmosomal desmogleins cause pemphigus, which forms fragile intraepidermal blisters and a positive Nikolsky sign.","Cytotoxic T cells killing keratinocytes is the mechanism of erythema multiforme and Stevens-Johnson syndrome, not autoimmune bullae with eosinophils.","Correct.","IgA deposits in dermal papillae with neutrophils describe dermatitis herpetiformis, which forms tiny, intensely itchy vesicles on extensor surfaces with celiac disease."],"study":{"topics":["Bullous pemphigoid mechanism","Type II hypersensitivity in the skin"],"comps":["IN-13-D2","IN-11"]}},{"id":"IN-C09-Q2","stem":"A 40-year-old woman at another facility has crusted, scaly erosions on her scalp, face, and upper chest. Intact blisters are rarely seen because they are very superficial and break easily. Nikolsky sign is positive, and there are no oral lesions. Her autoantibodies target desmoglein 1. At which of the following levels does her skin most likely split?","options":["Just above the basal layer of keratinocytes","Subcorneal, within the granular layer","In the lamina lucida of the basement membrane","Below the lamina densa in the upper dermis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-D3","obj":"IN-13-D.5","dok":3,"approach":["Superficial crusted erosions on the seborrheic areas, no mucosal disease, and antibodies to desmoglein 1 are pemphigus foliaceus.","Desmoglein 1 is most strongly expressed in the upper epidermis, while desmoglein 3 is concentrated in the lower layers and mucosa.","Loss of desmoglein 1 adhesion splits the epidermis high, just below the stratum corneum."],"why":"Pemphigus vulgaris targets desmoglein 3 (often desmoglein 1 too), splits just above the basal layer, and starts with painful oral erosions. Pemphigus foliaceus targets desmoglein 1 only, splits in the granular layer, and spares the mucosa because desmoglein 3 holds mucosal cells together. Both have a positive Nikolsky sign and net-like intercellular IgG on immunofluorescence. Bullous pemphigoid targets hemidesmosomes, splits beneath the epidermis, gives tense bullae with a negative Nikolsky sign, and shows linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane.","whyNot":["A split just above the basal layer is pemphigus vulgaris, which targets desmoglein 3 and almost always involves the mouth.","Correct.","A lamina lucida split is bullous pemphigoid or junctional epidermolysis bullosa. It gives tense blisters and a negative Nikolsky sign.","A split below the lamina densa is dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa or epidermolysis bullosa acquisita, from loss of type VII collagen function."],"study":{"topics":["Pemphigus vulgaris versus pemphigus foliaceus versus bullous pemphigoid","Desmoglein distribution"],"comps":["IN-13-D3","IN-13-D2"]}},{"id":"IN-C09-Q3","stem":"In his blisters, the split occurs through the lamina lucida. Which of the following structures forms the uppermost layer of the blister floor?","options":["Basal keratinocytes joined by desmosomes","Papillary dermis rich in type III collagen","Anchoring fibrils of type VII collagen","Lamina densa rich in type IV collagen"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-1","obj":"IN-1.4","dok":3,"approach":["From top to bottom, the junction is basal keratinocyte with hemidesmosomes, then lamina lucida, then lamina densa, then anchoring fibrils and dermis.","A split through the lamina lucida leaves the basal cells and hemidesmosomes in the roof.","The first layer below the split is the lamina densa, made mostly of type IV collagen."],"why":"The dermal-epidermal junction has distinct layers. Basal keratinocytes attach through hemidesmosomes containing BP180 (collagen XVII), BP230, and integrin alpha6 beta4. Below them, the lamina lucida contains laminin 332 and the anchoring filaments of collagen XVII. The lamina densa is a sheet of type IV collagen. Anchoring fibrils of type VII collagen loop from the lamina densa into the papillary dermis. A lamina lucida split, as in bullous pemphigoid and junctional epidermolysis bullosa, places the lamina densa in the floor. That is why antibodies to BP180 stain the roof on salt-split skin. A split below the lamina densa occurs in dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa.","whyNot":["Basal keratinocytes sit above the lamina lucida, so they form the bottom of the blister roof, not the floor.","The papillary dermis lies beneath the lamina densa and anchoring fibrils, so it is deeper in the floor.","Anchoring fibrils lie below the lamina densa. They are in the floor, but deeper, not the uppermost layer.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dermal-epidermal junction layers","Split level in blistering diseases"],"comps":["IN-1","IN-13-D1"]}},{"id":"IN-C09-Q4","stem":"Which of the following findings would indicate that his sacral ulcer has progressed to the next stage?","options":["Partial-thickness loss with exposed dermis","Exposed bone, tendon, or muscle","Undermining with tunneling into the fat","Nonblanching erythema of intact skin"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-10","obj":"IN-10.7","dok":2,"approach":["Full-thickness skin loss with visible fat but no bone, tendon, or muscle is a stage 3 pressure injury.","Stage 4 adds exposure of deeper structures: bone, tendon, muscle, ligament, or cartilage.","Undermining and tunneling can occur in both stage 3 and stage 4, so they do not define the next stage."],"why":"Pressure injuries form when sustained pressure and shear over a bony prominence, such as the sacrum, close capillaries and cause ischemia. Muscle and fat are more sensitive to ischemia than skin, so damage may be deeper than it looks. Stage 1 is nonblanching erythema of intact skin. Stage 2 is partial-thickness loss with exposed dermis. Stage 3 is full-thickness skin loss with visible fat. Stage 4 is full-thickness loss with exposed bone, tendon, muscle, ligament, or cartilage. Immobility, poor nutrition, moisture, and loss of sensation all raise risk, as in this bedbound patient.","whyNot":["Partial-thickness loss with exposed dermis is stage 2. His ulcer already shows full-thickness loss.","Correct.","Undermining and tunneling can be present in stage 3 or stage 4. They do not by themselves mark the next stage.","Nonblanching erythema of intact skin is stage 1, the earliest stage. His ulcer is already past it."],"study":{"topics":["Pressure injury staging","Pathogenesis of pressure ulcers"],"comps":["IN-10","IN-1"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C10","system":"IN","dx":"Pemphigus vulgaris","codes":["IN-13-D3"],"case":"A 48-year-old woman is referred to a dermatologist because of painful mouth sores for 2 months and blisters on her skin for 3 weeks. The mouth sores began first and have made eating difficult. The skin blisters are soft, break easily, and leave raw, painful areas. She takes no medications. Her temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows extensive erosions of the buccal mucosa, gingiva, and palate. Flaccid bullae and large erosions are present on the scalp, chest, and back. Gentle lateral pressure on normal-appearing skin next to a blister shears off the epidermis. Diagnosis: pemphigus vulgaris.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C10-Q1","stem":"Direct immunofluorescence of a biopsy specimen of skin next to a blister would most likely show which of the following patterns?","options":["Linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane","Granular IgG and C3 in a band at the junction","Intercellular IgG in a net-like pattern","Granular IgA in the dermal papillae"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-D3","obj":"IN-13-D.4","dok":2,"approach":["Early painful oral erosions, flaccid bullae, and a positive Nikolsky sign are pemphigus vulgaris.","The autoantibodies are IgG against desmoglein 3 (often also desmoglein 1), which sit on the surfaces of keratinocytes at desmosomes.","Staining outlines every keratinocyte, giving a fishnet or chicken-wire pattern."],"why":"In pemphigus vulgaris, IgG binds desmogleins on the surface of keratinocytes throughout the epidermis. Direct immunofluorescence therefore shows IgG (often with C3) outlining each cell in a net-like intercellular pattern. Biopsy shows suprabasal acantholysis with a single row of basal cells still attached to the basement membrane, the tombstone pattern. The blisters are thin-roofed and flaccid, and the oral mucosa is usually involved first, often for months before skin lesions appear.","whyNot":["Linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane is bullous pemphigoid, in which antibodies bind hemidesmosomes.","A granular band of IgG and C3 at the junction is the lupus band seen in cutaneous lupus erythematosus.","Correct.","Granular IgA in the dermal papillae is dermatitis herpetiformis, linked to celiac disease."],"study":{"topics":["Immunofluorescence patterns in blistering disease","Pemphigus vulgaris"],"comps":["IN-13-D3","IN-13-D2"]}},{"id":"IN-C10-Q2","stem":"Early in her illness, her autoantibodies were directed only against desmoglein 3, and she had oral erosions without skin blisters. Which of the following best explains why her skin was spared at that stage?","options":["Desmoglein 1 in skin maintains adhesion when desmoglein 3 fails","Mucosal epithelium lacks the protective stratum corneum","Skin keratinocytes adhere by E-cadherin in place of desmogleins","IgG cannot pass the dermal basement membrane into skin"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-1","obj":"IN-1.3","dok":3,"approach":["Desmosomes link neighboring keratinocytes through the cadherins desmoglein and desmocollin.","Oral mucosa relies mainly on desmoglein 3, while skin also expresses abundant desmoglein 1.","When only desmoglein 3 is blocked, desmoglein 1 keeps skin cells attached, so blisters form only in the mouth until antibodies to desmoglein 1 appear."],"why":"Keratinocytes are joined to each other by desmosomes, built from desmogleins and desmocollins that link to keratin filaments. Adherens junctions use E-cadherin and link to actin. Hemidesmosomes attach basal cells to the basement membrane through BP180, BP230, and integrin alpha6 beta4. Desmoglein 3 dominates in mucosa, and desmoglein 1 is abundant in skin, especially the upper layers. Antibodies against desmoglein 3 alone therefore cause mucosal disease, and adding antibodies to desmoglein 1 brings skin blisters. Antibodies to desmoglein 1 alone cause pemphigus foliaceus with superficial skin blisters and no mucosal lesions. Antibodies to hemidesmosomes cause bullous pemphigoid.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lacking a stratum corneum does not change cell-to-cell adhesion. The antibodies act at desmosomes in the living layers of both tissues.","Skin keratinocytes do have adherens junctions with E-cadherin, but they also rely on desmosomes. E-cadherin does not replace desmogleins.","IgG crosses the basement membrane into the epidermis easily. Her later skin blisters show that the antibodies reach the skin."],"study":{"topics":["Desmosomes, hemidesmosomes, and adherens junctions","Desmoglein compensation"],"comps":["IN-1","IN-13-D3"]}},{"id":"IN-C10-Q3","stem":"A 2-year-old boy has fever and diffuse, tender erythema followed by superficial flaccid blisters that wrinkle and peel with light pressure. The mouth and eyes are spared. A culture of fluid from a blister is sterile, and a nasal swab grows Staphylococcus aureus. Which of the following best explains his blistering?","options":["Toxin cleavage of desmoglein 1","Autoantibody binding to desmoglein 3","Superantigen activation of many T cells","Bacterial invasion of the spinous layer"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-9","obj":"IN-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Diffuse tender erythema with superficial peeling in a young child, sterile blister fluid, and a distant staphylococcal focus point to staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome.","The skin damage comes from a circulating toxin, not from bacteria in the skin.","Exfoliative toxins A and B are serine proteases that cut desmoglein 1, splitting the epidermis in the granular layer."],"why":"In staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, Staphylococcus aureus grows at a distant site, such as the nose, conjunctiva, or umbilicus, and releases exfoliative toxins into the blood. The toxins cleave desmoglein 1, the same protein targeted by antibodies in pemphigus foliaceus, so the split is high in the epidermis and the mucosa is spared. Young children are affected because their kidneys clear the toxin poorly. This differs from superantigen diseases such as toxic shock syndrome, where TSST-1 or streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins activate large numbers of T cells and cause diffuse rash, fever, and shock.","whyNot":["Correct.","Autoantibodies to desmoglein 3 cause pemphigus vulgaris, which begins in the mouth. This child's mucosa is spared and his illness is linked to infection.","Superantigens activate many T cells and cause toxic shock syndrome with fever, hypotension, and a diffuse rash that desquamates later. They do not cleave desmosomes.","Direct invasion would give a positive blister culture, as in bullous impetigo. His blister fluid is sterile because the toxin acts from a distance."],"study":{"topics":["Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Exfoliative toxins versus superantigens"],"comps":["IN-9","IN-13-D3"]}},{"id":"IN-C10-Q4","stem":"Which of the following histologic terms describes the change that allows her epidermis to shear off with gentle lateral pressure?","options":["Dyskeratosis","Spongiosis","Parakeratosis","Acantholysis"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-1","obj":"IN-1.8","dok":2,"approach":["A positive Nikolsky sign means the keratinocytes are no longer held firmly to each other.","Loss of cohesion between keratinocytes because their connections fail is called acantholysis.","In pemphigus, antibodies against desmogleins cause acantholysis, so a sideways push separates the layers."],"why":"Acantholysis is loss of attachment between keratinocytes after desmosomes fail, leaving rounded, separated cells inside a blister. It defines pemphigus. Acanthosis is thickening of the spinous layer, as in psoriasis. Hyperkeratosis is a thickened stratum corneum, and parakeratosis is retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum, as in psoriasis. Spongiosis is edema between keratinocytes, as in eczema and contact dermatitis. Dyskeratosis is abnormal early keratinization of individual cells below the granular layer, as in squamous cell carcinoma in situ.","whyNot":["Dyskeratosis is premature keratinization of single cells. It does not cause the epidermis to shear off.","Spongiosis is edema between keratinocytes in eczematous dermatitis. The cells stay connected by stretched desmosomes.","Parakeratosis is retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum from rapid turnover, as in psoriasis. It does not weaken adhesion.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dermatopathology terms","Nikolsky sign"],"comps":["IN-1","IN-13-D3"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C11","system":"IN","dx":"Oculocutaneous albinism type 1 (tyrosinase-negative)","codes":["IN-13-E1"],"case":"An 8-year-old girl is brought to the pediatric genetics clinic for evaluation of lifelong pale skin and poor vision. She has had nystagmus since early infancy and burns after brief sun exposure. Her parents and her 12-year-old brother have normal pigmentation and vision. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 100/64 mm Hg. Physical examination shows white hair and eyebrows, pink-white skin without pigmented nevi, gray-blue irides that transilluminate, and horizontal pendular nystagmus. Best-corrected visual acuity is 20/200 in each eye. Molecular testing shows two different loss-of-function variants in the tyrosinase gene (TYR), one inherited from each parent. Diagnosis: oculocutaneous albinism type 1A (tyrosinase-negative).","questions":[{"id":"IN-C11-Q1","stem":"Her parents ask about her brother. What is the probability that her unaffected brother is a heterozygous carrier of a TYR variant?","options":["1/4","1/2","2/3","3/4"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-13-E1","obj":"IN-13-E.1","dok":3,"approach":["Unaffected parents with an affected child and two variants, one from each parent, point to autosomal recessive inheritance with both parents as carriers.","A carrier by carrier cross gives 1 affected, 2 carriers, and 1 noncarrier out of 4.","The brother is unaffected, so remove the affected outcome: 2 of the remaining 3 are carriers."],"why":"Oculocutaneous albinism type 1 is autosomal recessive, so each parent carries one nonworking TYR allele. Each child has a 1 in 4 chance of two mutant alleles, a 2 in 4 chance of one, and a 1 in 4 chance of none. Because the brother is known to be unaffected, only three outcomes remain, and two of them are carriers, so his risk is 2/3. This conditional step is a favorite exam trap.","whyNot":["One in four is the chance that any child of two carriers is affected. It is the wrong number for an unaffected sibling.","One in two is the chance that a child of two carriers is a carrier before you know the child's phenotype. Here you already know he is unaffected, which removes one outcome.","Correct.","Three in four is the chance that a child of two carriers is unaffected. It is not the chance of being a carrier."],"study":{"topics":["Autosomal recessive inheritance","Carrier risk in unaffected siblings"],"comps":["IN-13-E1","IN-7"]}},{"id":"IN-C11-Q2","stem":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to the same clinic because of recurrent staphylococcal skin abscesses. Physical examination shows silvery-gray hair, light skin, and decreased sensation in both feet. Which of the following is most likely to be found on his peripheral blood smear?","options":["Hypersegmented nuclei in many neutrophils","Giant cytoplasmic granules in neutrophils","Bilobed nuclei in nearly all neutrophils","Basophilic stippling of red blood cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-13-E","obj":"IN-13-E.2","dok":3,"approach":["Partial albinism plus recurrent pyogenic infections plus peripheral neuropathy is the triad of Chediak-Higashi syndrome.","The defect is in LYST, a lysosomal trafficking regulator, so lysosomes and related granules fuse abnormally into giant granules.","In neutrophils this shows as giant granules, and the cells kill bacteria poorly."],"why":"Chediak-Higashi syndrome is an autosomal recessive defect in the lysosomal trafficking regulator (LYST). Lysosome-related organelles fuse into giant granules, which are easy to see in neutrophils. Melanosomes clump too, so pigment is diluted and hair looks silvery, but this is partial albinism. Poor phagolysosome function causes recurrent pyogenic infections, and neuropathy develops over time. The girl in the case has complete loss of melanin with normal immune function. X-linked ocular albinism differs again: the eyes are affected while skin and hair are near normal.","whyNot":["Hypersegmented neutrophils (6 or more lobes) point to vitamin B12 or folate deficiency. They do not cause pigment dilution or pyogenic infections.","Correct.","Bilobed neutrophil nuclei are the Pelger-Huet anomaly, a benign inherited finding with normal neutrophil function.","Basophilic stippling of red cells is seen in lead poisoning and some thalassemias. Lead can cause neuropathy, but it does not lighten hair or cause recurrent abscesses."],"study":{"topics":["Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Forms of albinism"],"comps":["IN-13-E1","IN-2"]}},{"id":"IN-C11-Q3","stem":"Her mother asks whether her daughter's condition is the same as that of a nephew who was born with a white forelock and stable, sharply bordered white patches on the forehead and abdomen, with normal vision. The nephew's white patches are most likely explained by which of the following?","options":["Normal melanocytes with absent tyrosinase activity","Postnatal T cell destruction of mature melanocytes","Defective transport of melanosomes within melanocytes","Failed migration of neural crest melanoblasts"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-2","obj":"IN-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["A white forelock and white patches present at birth that never change suggest piebaldism.","Melanocytes come from neural crest melanoblasts that must migrate to the skin during embryonic life.","In piebaldism (often a KIT mutation) melanoblasts fail to migrate or survive in those areas, so the patches have no melanocytes at all."],"why":"Melanoblasts arise from the neural crest and travel through the dermis to settle in the epidermis and hair bulbs. In piebaldism, usually caused by a dominant KIT mutation, melanoblasts do not migrate into or survive in certain areas, typically the forehead, central trunk, and limbs. Those patches lack melanocytes from birth and stay stable. In albinism the melanocytes reach every site in normal numbers, but they cannot make melanin, so the whole body is affected.","whyNot":["Normal melanocyte numbers with no tyrosinase activity is the girl's condition. It affects all skin, hair, and eyes, not separate patches present at birth.","T cell destruction of melanocytes is vitiligo. Vitiligo begins after birth and the patches usually spread over time.","Defective melanosome transport inside melanocytes occurs in Griscelli syndrome, which causes diffuse silvery hair, not a white forelock with stable patches.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neural crest origin of melanocytes","Piebaldism versus albinism"],"comps":["IN-2","IN-13-E1"]}},{"id":"IN-C11-Q4","stem":"Her 36-year-old father develops sharply demarcated, milk-white macules on the backs of his hands and around his mouth over 6 months. The macules have no scale. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Chalk-white Wood lamp accentuation, no melanocytes on biopsy","Yellow-green Wood lamp fluorescence and yeast forms on KOH","Septate hyphae on a KOH preparation from the lesion border","Faint Wood lamp accentuation and normal melanocytes on biopsy"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-A2","obj":"IN-13-A.4","dok":3,"approach":["New, sharply demarcated, milk-white macules without scale on the hands and around the mouth in an adult suggest vitiligo.","Vitiligo removes the melanocytes completely, so the skin has no melanin at all.","Skin with no melanin glows bright chalk-white under Wood lamp, and a biopsy shows no melanocytes."],"why":"Vitiligo is an acquired loss of melanocytes. Because no melanin remains, the macules look milk-white and are sharply accentuated under Wood lamp, and a biopsy with melanocyte stains shows no melanocytes in the basal layer. Its typical sites are around the mouth and eyes and on the hands. This also separates it from his daughter's albinism, in which melanocytes are present in normal numbers. Hypopigmented conditions that keep some melanin, such as postinflammatory hypopigmentation, show only faint accentuation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Yellow-green fluorescence and yeasts with short hyphae on KOH describe tinea versicolor. Those macules are usually on the upper trunk and have a fine scale.","Septate hyphae on KOH mean a dermatophyte, as in tinea corporis, which forms scaly annular plaques with an active border.","Faint accentuation with melanocytes still present fits postinflammatory hypopigmentation, where melanin is reduced but the melanocytes remain."],"study":{"topics":["Wood lamp examination","Hypopigmented versus depigmented lesions"],"comps":["IN-13-A2","IN-12"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C12","system":"IN","dx":"Vitiligo with autoimmune primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease)","codes":["IN-13-A2"],"case":"A 30-year-old man comes to the endocrinology clinic because of 4 months of fatigue, a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss, salt craving, and lightheadedness on standing. He has had white patches on his hands and face for 3 years. Temperature is 36.6 °C (97.9 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 94/58 mm Hg supine and 80/50 mm Hg standing. Physical examination shows diffuse brown hyperpigmentation, darkest in the palmar creases, knuckles, and buccal mucosa, and sharply demarcated depigmented macules on the dorsal hands and around the eyes. Serum studies show sodium 128 mEq/L (135 to 145), potassium 5.8 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), glucose 66 mg/dL (70 to 100), 8 AM cortisol 2 µg/dL (5 to 25), and ACTH 620 pg/mL (10 to 60). Antibodies to 21-hydroxylase are present. Diagnosis: vitiligo with autoimmune primary adrenal insufficiency.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C12-Q1","stem":"A new depigmented macule has appeared along a healed scratch on his forearm. Which of the following best explains the loss of pigment in his macules?","options":["Inhibition of tyrosinase by a fungal dicarboxylic acid","Loss of melanin from keratinocytes after inflammation","Cytotoxic T cell destruction of epidermal melanocytes","Reduced tyrosinase activity in intact melanocytes"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-13-A2","obj":"IN-13-A.3","dok":2,"approach":["A new lesion at a site of trauma is the Koebner phenomenon, which vitiligo shows.","Vitiligo is autoimmune, and he already has one autoimmune endocrine disease.","The effector cells are CD8 cytotoxic T cells that recognize melanocyte antigens and kill the melanocytes."],"why":"In vitiligo, CD8 cytotoxic T cells recognize melanocyte proteins such as tyrosinase and kill the melanocytes, helped by interferon-gamma signaling. With no melanocytes left, the skin is depigmented, and trauma can trigger new lesions (Koebner phenomenon). Vitiligo clusters with other organ-specific autoimmune diseases, especially autoimmune thyroid disease, type 1 diabetes, and autoimmune adrenal insufficiency, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Azelaic acid from Malassezia inhibits tyrosinase in tinea versicolor, which causes scaly macules on the trunk, not a Koebner response on the hands.","Postinflammatory hypopigmentation follows an earlier rash in the same spot, keeps its melanocytes, and is only partly lightened.","Correct.","Intact melanocytes that cannot make melanin describe albinism, which is present from birth and affects the whole body."],"study":{"topics":["Vitiligo pathogenesis","Koebner phenomenon"],"comps":["IN-13-A2","IN-11"]}},{"id":"IN-C12-Q2","stem":"His hyperpigmentation would be absent if his adrenal insufficiency were caused by a pituitary adenoma. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["Low aldosterone increases melanosome transfer to keratinocytes","Low cortisol removes inhibition of melanocyte tyrosinase","High renin activates angiotensin receptors on melanocytes","High POMC-derived peptides stimulate melanocortin 1 receptors"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-13-A","obj":"IN-13-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Low cortisol removes negative feedback, so the pituitary raises production of POMC, the precursor of ACTH.","POMC is cleaved into ACTH and alpha-MSH, and both can bind the melanocortin 1 receptor on melanocytes.","In pituitary disease ACTH and POMC are low, so the stimulus for melanin is missing."],"why":"In primary adrenal insufficiency the adrenal gland fails, cortisol falls, and the pituitary responds by making much more POMC. POMC is cut into ACTH and melanocyte-stimulating hormone, and both activate the melanocortin 1 receptor on melanocytes, which increases melanin synthesis. The darkening shows first in creases, knuckles, scars, and mucosa. In secondary adrenal insufficiency the pituitary is the problem, so ACTH and POMC peptides are low and the skin does not darken. His vitiligo macules stay white because they have no melanocytes to respond.","whyNot":["Aldosterone does not control melanosome transfer. Low aldosterone explains his hyperkalemia, hyponatremia, and salt craving.","Cortisol does not directly regulate tyrosinase. Low cortisol is shared by primary and secondary disease, so it cannot explain the difference.","Renin is high in primary adrenal insufficiency, but angiotensin does not drive melanin synthesis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["POMC processing","Primary versus secondary adrenal insufficiency"],"comps":["IN-13-A","IN-7"]}},{"id":"IN-C12-Q3","stem":"His depigmented macules sunburn easily, whereas his normally pigmented skin tans. Fair-skinned, red-haired people, whose melanocytes make mainly pheomelanin rather than eumelanin, also burn easily. Which of the following best explains why pheomelanin protects poorly against ultraviolet light?","options":["It is deposited only in keratinocytes of the stratum corneum","It generates reactive oxygen species when exposed to UV light","It cannot be transferred from melanocytes to keratinocytes","It is synthesized by melanocytes that lack tyrosinase activity"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-6","obj":"IN-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Melanin protects by absorbing UV light and by quenching free radicals, so it acts as an antioxidant.","Eumelanin (brown-black) does both well and is chemically stable.","Pheomelanin (red-yellow) contains sulfur from cysteine and acts as a photosensitizer, producing reactive oxygen species under UV."],"why":"Eumelanin absorbs a broad band of UV and visible light and scavenges free radicals, so it protects DNA in basal keratinocytes. Pheomelanin is made when cysteine joins the melanin pathway, giving sulfur-containing units that absorb less UV and generate reactive oxygen species when irradiated. Making pheomelanin also uses up cysteine and glutathione, an important skin antioxidant. Other non-vitamin antioxidants in skin include ubiquinol (coenzyme Q10) and dietary carotenoids such as lycopene, but none of them replaces the shielding of eumelanin.","whyNot":["Both types of melanin are carried in melanosomes in basal and lower epidermal keratinocytes, where they shield the nuclei.","Correct.","Pheomelanin is packaged in melanosomes and transferred to keratinocytes in the same way as eumelanin.","Pheomelanin synthesis also starts with tyrosinase. Melanocytes that lack tyrosinase make no melanin of either type."],"study":{"topics":["Eumelanin versus pheomelanin","Skin antioxidants"],"comps":["IN-6","IN-7"]}},{"id":"IN-C12-Q4","stem":"A punch biopsy of hyperpigmented skin from a palmar crease is examined. The brown pigment is most likely to show which of the following features?","options":["Fontana-Masson-positive granules in basal keratinocytes","Black exogenous particles within dermal macrophages","Golden-brown perinuclear granules from lipid peroxidation","Prussian blue-positive granules in dermal macrophages"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-8","obj":"IN-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["His hyperpigmentation comes from ACTH and MSH stimulating melanocytes, so the pigment is melanin.","Melanin made by melanocytes is passed to basal keratinocytes, where it caps the nucleus.","Melanin reduces silver salts, so it stains black with Fontana-Masson, and it is Prussian blue negative."],"why":"Hormonal hyperpigmentation increases melanin, an endogenous pigment, in the basal layer. Melanin reduces silver, so Fontana-Masson stains it black, and it does not contain iron. Hemosiderin is the main brown pigment that must be told apart from melanin, and Prussian blue stains it blue. Lipofuscin is a yellow-brown residue of lipid peroxidation inside lysosomes. Carbon and tattoo ink are exogenous pigments held by macrophages.","whyNot":["Correct.","Black exogenous particles in dermal macrophages are tattoo pigment. Inhaled carbon causes the same picture in the lung (anthracosis), not in palmar skin.","Golden-brown perinuclear granules from lipid peroxidation are lipofuscin, the wear-and-tear pigment of aging, long-lived cells.","Prussian blue stains hemosiderin, the iron pigment left after bleeding, as in old bruises or stasis dermatitis."],"study":{"topics":["Endogenous and exogenous pigments","Special stains for pigment"],"comps":["IN-8","IN-13-A"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C13","system":"IN","dx":"Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","codes":["IN-13-E2"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl comes to the physician for a sports physical before joining a gymnastics team. She bruises easily, has had three shoulder dislocations, and says cuts on her knees healed as wide, thin scars. Her mother has similar skin. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 66/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 110/70 mm Hg. Height is 163 cm (5 ft 4 in). Physical examination shows soft, velvety skin that stretches 4 cm from the forearm and snaps back, wide atrophic scars over both knees and the forehead, and bruises on the shins. She can bend each thumb to touch the forearm and hyperextends both elbows. Sclerae are white, and the lenses are in normal position. Echocardiography shows a normal aortic root. Genetic testing shows a heterozygous COL5A1 variant. Diagnosis: classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C13-Q1","stem":"The protein encoded by the mutated gene has which of the following main roles in normal dermis?","options":["Forming the anchoring fibrils beneath the basal lamina","Forming the sheet network of the lamina densa","Hydroxylating lysine residues to allow cross-linking","Regulating the diameter of type I collagen fibrils"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-13-E2","obj":"IN-13-E.3","dok":2,"approach":["COL5A1 encodes a chain of type V collagen.","Type V collagen is a minor fibrillar collagen that co-assembles with type I collagen.","It sets the size and shape of type I fibrils, so its loss gives large, irregular fibrils and weak, stretchy skin."],"why":"Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is usually caused by dominant COL5A1 or COL5A2 variants. Type V collagen sits inside type I collagen fibrils and controls how they start and how thick they become. With too little working type V collagen, dermal fibrils are irregular and large, often looking like cauliflowers on electron microscopy. The skin stretches too far, tears easily, and heals with wide atrophic scars. Other types map to other proteins: vascular type to type III collagen and kyphoscoliotic type to lysyl hydroxylase.","whyNot":["Anchoring fibrils are type VII collagen. Its loss causes dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa, with blisters below the lamina densa.","The lamina densa network is type IV collagen, the target in Alport and Goodpasture syndromes.","Lysine hydroxylation is done by lysyl hydroxylase. Its deficiency causes the kyphoscoliotic type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types and their locations","Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"],"comps":["IN-13-E2","IN-5"]}},{"id":"IN-C13-Q2","stem":"Her 26-year-old cousin has thin, translucent skin with visible veins, extensive bruising, and only mild joint laxity, and is diagnosed with a different type of this disorder. This cousin is at greatest risk for which of the following?","options":["Recurrent long bone fractures after minor trauma","Spontaneous rupture of a medium-sized artery","Severe kyphoscoliosis present from infancy","Progressive conductive hearing loss"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-E2","obj":"IN-13-E.4","dok":3,"approach":["Thin, translucent skin with visible veins and easy bruising, with little joint laxity, points to the vascular type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","The vascular type is caused by COL3A1 variants that reduce type III collagen.","Type III collagen is abundant in arteries, bowel, and uterus, so these walls can rupture."],"why":"Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome comes from defects in type III collagen, which gives strength to the walls of arteries, hollow organs, and the pregnant uterus. Patients have thin, translucent skin and bruise easily, while joint laxity is often limited to small joints. The danger is spontaneous rupture or dissection of medium-sized arteries, perforation of the bowel (often the sigmoid colon), and uterine rupture in pregnancy. Berry aneurysms can also occur. It is the most life-threatening form of the disorder.","whyNot":["Fractures after minor trauma point to osteogenesis imperfecta, a type I collagen disorder, not to fragile vessels.","Correct.","Severe kyphoscoliosis from infancy, with low muscle tone, is the kyphoscoliotic type, caused by lysyl hydroxylase deficiency.","Conductive hearing loss from abnormal ossicles is a feature of osteogenesis imperfecta."],"study":{"topics":["Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Type III collagen"],"comps":["IN-13-E2","IN-5"]}},{"id":"IN-C13-Q3","stem":"A 16-year-old teammate is 188 cm (6 ft 2 in) tall, has an arm span greater than her height, hypermobile joints, and upward displacement of both lenses. Echocardiography shows aortic root dilation. A defect in which of the following is most likely?","options":["Type I collagen of bone and sclera","Type V collagen of dermal fibrils","Type III collagen of vessel walls","Fibrillin-1 of microfibrils"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-13-E","obj":"IN-13-E.5","dok":2,"approach":["Tall stature, long arms, lens displacement, and aortic root dilation together suggest Marfan syndrome.","Marfan syndrome is caused by FBN1 variants that affect fibrillin-1.","Fibrillin-1 forms microfibrils that support elastic fibers and hold TGF-beta in reserve, which explains the eye, skeletal, and aortic findings."],"why":"Marfan syndrome is an autosomal dominant defect in fibrillin-1, a glycoprotein that forms the microfibril scaffold of elastic fibers. The suspensory ligaments of the lens are made of these microfibrils, so the lenses slip, usually upward. Weak elastic fibers in the aortic media lead to aortic root dilation and dissection, and extra TGF-beta signaling adds to the problem. Joint hypermobility is shared with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, so the defective protein and the other findings separate them.","whyNot":["Type I collagen defects cause osteogenesis imperfecta, with fractures, blue sclerae, and hearing loss, not tall stature with lens displacement.","Type V collagen defects cause classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, as in the patient, with stretchy skin and atrophic scars but normal height and lenses.","Type III collagen defects cause vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, with arterial and bowel rupture but no lens displacement or tall stature.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Marfan syndrome","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],"comps":["IN-13-E","IN-5"]}},{"id":"IN-C13-Q4","stem":"Electron microscopy of her skin shows enlarged, irregular collagen fibrils. The defect most affects the dermal layer that gives skin most of its tensile strength. Which of the following best describes this layer?","options":["Dense irregular connective tissue with thick collagen bundles","Loose connective tissue with fine collagen and Meissner corpuscles","Adipose lobules divided by septa containing Pacinian corpuscles","Type IV collagen sheet just beneath the basal keratinocytes"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-1","obj":"IN-1.5","dok":2,"approach":["The layer that resists stretching is the one with the most and thickest collagen.","That is the reticular dermis, the thick layer below the papillary dermis.","Match it to its histology: dense irregular connective tissue with coarse collagen bundles running in many directions."],"why":"The dermis has two layers. The thin papillary dermis is loose connective tissue with fine collagen, capillary loops, and Meissner corpuscles for light touch. The much thicker reticular dermis is dense irregular connective tissue with large bundles of type I collagen and elastic fibers, and it gives skin its strength. Below it, the hypodermis is fat with fibrous septa, and Pacinian corpuscles for vibration sit in the deep dermis and subcutis. Free nerve endings reach into the epidermis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Loose connective tissue with fine collagen and Meissner corpuscles is the papillary dermis, the thin layer in the dermal papillae.","Fat lobules with fibrous septa and Pacinian corpuscles describe the hypodermis (subcutis), which cushions and insulates.","A type IV collagen sheet under the basal cells is the lamina densa of the basement membrane, not a dermal layer."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the dermis","Cutaneous sensory receptors"],"comps":["IN-1","IN-13-E2"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C14","system":"IN","dx":"Invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma arising in actinic keratosis in a kidney transplant recipient","codes":["IN-13-F1","IN-13-G2"],"case":"A 64-year-old man comes to the transplant clinic because of a growing, tender lump on the back of his left hand. He received a deceased-donor kidney transplant 12 years ago and takes tacrolimus, mycophenolate, and prednisone. He worked outdoors as a roofer for 30 years. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 74/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 136/82 mm Hg. Physical examination shows fair skin with many rough, scaly, pink papules that feel like sandpaper on the scalp, forehead, ears, and dorsal hands. On the left hand, one of these lesions has become a 1.5-cm indurated, hyperkeratotic nodule with central ulceration. Excisional biopsy shows atypical keratinocytes forming keratin pearls and invading the reticular dermis. Diagnosis: invasive squamous cell carcinoma arising in an actinic keratosis.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C14-Q1","stem":"A shave biopsy of one of the rough, scaly papules on his forehead shows no dermal invasion. Which of the following additional findings is most likely?","options":["Full-thickness keratinocyte atypia over an intact basement membrane","Uniform basaloid cells with keratin-filled horn cysts","Atypia of lower epidermal keratinocytes with parakeratosis","Nests of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-F1","obj":"IN-13-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Rough, sandpaper-like scaly papules on sun-exposed skin of an older fair-skinned man are actinic keratoses.","Actinic keratosis is UV-induced dysplasia that starts in the basal and lower epidermal keratinocytes.","Expect lower epidermal atypia, overlying parakeratosis, and solar elastosis in the dermis."],"why":"Actinic keratosis is a dysplasia of keratinocytes caused by years of UV exposure. The atypia begins in the basal layer and involves the lower part of the epidermis, while the stratum corneum keeps its nuclei (parakeratosis), which is why the lesion feels rough. The dermis below usually shows solar elastosis, a sign of chronic sun damage. Most actinic keratoses do not progress, but some become squamous cell carcinoma, and the risk is much higher in transplant recipients.","whyNot":["Atypia through the full thickness of the epidermis is squamous cell carcinoma in situ (Bowen disease), a step further along than a typical actinic keratosis.","Uniform basaloid cells with horn cysts and no atypia are seborrheic keratosis, a benign stuck-on lesion.","Correct.","Basaloid nests with peripheral palisading are basal cell carcinoma, which usually forms a pearly papule."],"study":{"topics":["Actinic keratosis histology","Dysplasia to carcinoma sequence"],"comps":["IN-13-F1","IN-8"]}},{"id":"IN-C14-Q2","stem":"His 68-year-old brother, who has never taken immunosuppressive drugs, also has several cutaneous squamous cell carcinomas, along with many hard keratotic papules on his palms and soles. Both brothers drank water from the same rural well throughout childhood. Which of the following exposures most likely contributed?","options":["Inorganic arsenic","Vinyl chloride","Aromatic amines","Airborne asbestos fibers"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-G2","obj":"IN-13-G.2","dok":2,"approach":["Squamous cell carcinomas plus keratoses of the palms and soles in someone without immunosuppression suggest a systemic carcinogen.","A shared rural well water source points to an ingested contaminant.","Chronic arsenic ingestion causes palmar and plantar keratoses, Bowen disease, and squamous cell carcinoma."],"why":"Long-term arsenic in drinking water is a known cause of skin cancer. It causes punctate keratoses on the palms and soles, squamous cell carcinoma in situ, invasive squamous cell carcinoma, and basal cell carcinoma, including on skin that sees little sun. Other risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma are cumulative UV exposure, actinic keratosis, transplant immunosuppression, HPV, xeroderma pigmentosum, and chronic scars or ulcers (Marjolin ulcer). Sun-exposed skin and the lower lip, which faces the sun more than the upper lip, are favored sites.","whyNot":["Correct.","Vinyl chloride exposure in plastics manufacturing causes angiosarcoma of the liver, not skin cancer with palmar keratoses.","Aromatic amines from dye and rubber work and from smoking cause urothelial carcinoma of the bladder.","Asbestos causes mesothelioma, lung cancer, and pleural plaques, not skin cancer."],"study":{"topics":["Risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma","Arsenic toxicity"],"comps":["IN-13-G2","IN-10"]}},{"id":"IN-C14-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his immunosuppressive regimen has increased his risk of this cancer?","options":["Reduced complement deposition on tumor cell membranes","Reduced neutrophil oxidative burst directed at tumor cells","Reduced IL-2-driven expansion of CD8 cytotoxic T cells","Reduced eosinophil killing of antibody-coated tumor cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-11","obj":"IN-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Tacrolimus is a calcineurin inhibitor that blocks NFAT activation and IL-2 production in T cells.","Without IL-2, CD8 T cells cannot expand to kill keratinocytes that carry UV-induced mutant proteins.","Loss of this surveillance lets many dysplastic clones grow, so transplant recipients get many squamous cell carcinomas."],"why":"Cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognize mutant peptides shown on MHC class I and kill tumor cells, and NK cells kill tumor cells that hide by dropping MHC class I. Tumors can also escape by expressing PD-L1, which turns T cells off. Tacrolimus and similar drugs block IL-2 production, so the T cell arm of this surveillance is weakened. Organ transplant recipients therefore develop squamous cell carcinoma at many times the usual rate, often in large numbers, and the tumors behave more aggressively. Oncogenic HPV types may add to the risk in these patients.","whyNot":["Complement plays little part in clearing solid tumors, and his drugs do not mainly target complement.","The neutrophil respiratory burst kills bacteria and fungi. Loss of it causes infections, as in chronic granulomatous disease, not skin cancer.","Correct.","Eosinophil antibody-dependent killing is aimed at helminths, not at UV-damaged keratinocytes."],"study":{"topics":["Tumor immunosurveillance","Calcineurin inhibitors"],"comps":["IN-11","IN-13-G2"]}},{"id":"IN-C14-Q4","stem":"Sequencing of the tumor shows many C-to-T substitutions, including tandem CC-to-TT changes, at adjacent pyrimidines in TP53. Which of the following most likely caused these mutations?","options":["Ionizing radiation causing double-strand breaks","UVB light forming cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers","UVA light forming 8-oxoguanine through oxidation","Benzo[a]pyrene forming bulky guanine adducts"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-6","obj":"IN-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["C-to-T and CC-to-TT changes at adjacent pyrimidines are the UV signature mutation.","UVB is absorbed directly by DNA and links neighboring pyrimidines into cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers.","If the dimers are not repaired by nucleotide excision repair, replication puts in the wrong base, giving the signature."],"why":"UVB is absorbed directly by DNA and joins neighboring pyrimidines into cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers and 6-4 photoproducts. Nucleotide excision repair normally removes them, but unrepaired lesions produce C-to-T and CC-to-TT changes, often in TP53. UVB is also the main cause of sunburn. UVA penetrates into the dermis and harms DNA mainly through reactive oxygen species, and it drives photoaging by inducing matrix metalloproteinases that break down collagen, leaving solar elastosis.","whyNot":["Ionizing radiation causes strand breaks, deletions, and rearrangements rather than tandem changes at pyrimidine pairs.","Correct.","UVA penetrates deeper and damages DNA mostly through oxidation. 8-oxoguanine leads to G-to-T transversions, not CC-to-TT changes.","Benzo[a]pyrene from tobacco smoke binds guanine and leads mainly to G-to-T transversions, as in lung cancer."],"study":{"topics":["UVB versus UVA injury","Nucleotide excision repair"],"comps":["IN-6","IN-7"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C15","system":"IN","dx":"Superficial spreading melanoma in a patient with multiple dysplastic nevi","codes":["IN-13-F2","IN-13-G3"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman comes to the dermatology clinic because a mole on her upper back has grown and darkened over 6 months. Her mother had melanoma diagnosed at age 42. She has fair skin, freckles, and brown hair. Physical examination shows more than 60 nevi; about 15 of them are 6 to 9 mm with irregular, fuzzy borders and mixed tan and brown color. The changing lesion is an 11-mm asymmetric plaque with a notched border, areas of brown, black, and blue-gray, and a 3-mm raised nodule. Excision shows atypical melanocytes spreading singly and in nests through all levels of the epidermis, and a dermal nodule of atypical melanocytes with mitoses. Breslow thickness is 1.6 mm. Diagnosis: superficial spreading melanoma in a patient with multiple dysplastic nevi.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C15-Q1","stem":"A biopsy of one of her 7-mm nevi with fuzzy borders is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Architectural disorder with random melanocyte atypia","Uniform nests of small melanocytes that mature with depth","Spindle and epithelioid melanocytes with Kamino bodies","Atypical melanocytes in sheets invading the reticular dermis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-F2","obj":"IN-13-F.2","dok":2,"approach":["Nevi larger than 6 mm with irregular borders and mixed color in a patient with many moles and a family history of melanoma are dysplastic nevi.","Dysplastic nevi are defined on biopsy by architectural disorder, such as bridging of nests between rete ridges, plus cytologic atypia.","The atypia is random and mild to moderate, which separates them from melanoma."],"why":"Dysplastic nevi are larger than common nevi (often over 5 to 6 mm), with irregular borders and uneven color. On biopsy they show disordered architecture, such as nests that bridge between rete ridges and dermal fibrosis, with scattered atypical melanocytes. One dysplastic nevus rarely becomes melanoma, but many of them mark a person at high risk, especially with a family history. Familial melanoma is often linked to germline CDKN2A mutations that remove p16, a brake on the cell cycle.","whyNot":["Correct.","Uniform small nests that become smaller and more mature with depth describe an ordinary acquired nevus, which is usually under 6 mm with a smooth border.","Large spindle and epithelioid cells with eosinophilic Kamino bodies describe a Spitz nevus, typically a pink papule in a child.","Sheets of atypical melanocytes invading the reticular dermis are invasive melanoma, not a stable nevus."],"study":{"topics":["Dysplastic nevus histology","Familial melanoma"],"comps":["IN-13-F2","IN-7"]}},{"id":"IN-C15-Q2","stem":"Which of the following features of the excised tumor best predicts her risk of metastasis?","options":["Number of colors seen within the lesion","Depth of invasion from the granular layer","Diameter of the lesion on the skin surface","Extent of pagetoid spread in the epidermis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-G3","obj":"IN-13-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["Her raised nodule means the tumor has entered the vertical growth phase.","Vertical growth carries tumor cells into the dermis, near lymphatics and blood vessels, so it allows metastasis.","The deeper the invasion, the higher the risk, which is measured as Breslow thickness."],"why":"Melanoma first grows outward within the epidermis and superficial dermis (radial growth phase), where metastasis is unlikely. Clones that gain the ability to grow downward form a dermal nodule (vertical growth phase) and can reach lymphatics and vessels. Breslow thickness, measured in millimeters from the granular layer to the deepest tumor cell, is the strongest predictor of outcome for localized melanoma. Melanocytic origin can be confirmed with immunostains such as S-100, HMB-45, and Melan-A.","whyNot":["Color variation helps raise suspicion of melanoma clinically, but it does not measure how deep the tumor reaches.","Correct.","Surface diameter reflects radial growth, which spreads along the epidermis and carries little risk of metastasis.","Pagetoid spread is part of the radial growth phase and helps diagnose melanoma, but it occurs within the epidermis."],"study":{"topics":["Radial and vertical growth phases","Breslow thickness"],"comps":["IN-13-G3","IN-8"]}},{"id":"IN-C15-Q3","stem":"Her 34-year-old sister has red hair, many freckles, and skin that burns and never tans. She carries loss-of-function variants in the gene for the melanocyte receptor that binds alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone. Which of the following best explains her pigmentation?","options":["High cAMP increases eumelanin but blocks melanosome transfer","Absent tyrosinase activity prevents synthesis of all melanin","Low cAMP lowers MITF and tyrosinase, favoring pheomelanin","Failed neural crest migration reduces melanocyte numbers"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-7","obj":"IN-7.7","dok":3,"approach":["The receptor that binds alpha-MSH on melanocytes is MC1R, a Gs-coupled receptor.","MC1R signaling raises cAMP, which increases MITF and tyrosinase expression and switches synthesis toward eumelanin.","With loss of function, cAMP stays low and melanocytes make mostly red-yellow pheomelanin, which protects poorly against UV."],"why":"MC1R is a Gs-coupled receptor on melanocytes. When alpha-MSH binds it, cAMP rises, protein kinase A activates the transcription factor MITF, and tyrosinase and related enzymes increase, favoring brown-black eumelanin. Loss-of-function MC1R variants keep cAMP low, so melanocytes make mostly pheomelanin. The result is red hair, freckles, and fair skin that burns, and these variants raise melanoma risk.","whyNot":["Loss of receptor function lowers cAMP. High cAMP would push melanocytes toward eumelanin and darker skin.","Absent tyrosinase activity is oculocutaneous albinism type 1, with white hair and no pigment of either type. Red hair needs pheomelanin, which still needs tyrosinase.","Correct.","Failed migration of melanoblasts causes piebaldism, with white patches lacking melanocytes, not red hair and freckles."],"study":{"topics":["MC1R signaling","Regulation of melanogenesis"],"comps":["IN-7","IN-6"]}},{"id":"IN-C15-Q4","stem":"The malignant cells in her tumor share their embryologic origin with which of the following cells?","options":["Cells of the retinal pigment epithelium","Keratinocytes of the epidermis","Schwann cells of peripheral nerves","Fiber cells of the lens"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-2","obj":"IN-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Melanoma is a tumor of melanocytes.","Melanocytes arise from the neural crest, which separates from the folding neural tube.","Choose the other neural crest derivative, the Schwann cell."],"why":"Ectoderm splits into three lines. Surface ectoderm forms the epidermis, hair, nails, skin and mammary glands, lens, anterior pituitary, and tooth enamel. Neuroectoderm from the neural tube forms the brain, spinal cord, retina and retinal pigment epithelium, and posterior pituitary. Neural crest forms melanocytes, Schwann cells, sensory and autonomic ganglia, adrenal medulla, and much of the face's connective tissue. Melanoma and schwannoma therefore share a neural crest origin, and both often stain for S-100.","whyNot":["The retinal pigment epithelium is pigmented but comes from neuroectoderm (the outer layer of the optic cup), like the neural retina.","Keratinocytes come from surface ectoderm, which also forms hair, nails, and skin glands. They give rise to basal and squamous cell carcinomas.","Correct.","The lens comes from surface ectoderm, which forms the lens placode."],"study":{"topics":["Ectoderm derivatives","Neural crest derivatives"],"comps":["IN-2","IN-13-G3"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C16","system":"IN","dx":"Eruptive seborrheic keratoses (Leser-Trélat sign) with gastric adenocarcinoma","codes":["IN-13-F3"],"case":"A 68-year-old man comes to the physician because many itchy brown spots have appeared on his chest and back over the past 3 months. He also reports early satiety, epigastric pain, and a 7-kg (15-lb) weight loss. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows more than 40 tan to dark brown, waxy, sharply bordered papules and plaques on the trunk that look stuck on to the skin. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5). Upper endoscopy shows a 4-cm ulcerated mass in the gastric antrum, and biopsy shows invasive adenocarcinoma. Diagnosis: eruptive seborrheic keratoses (sign of Leser-Trélat) with gastric adenocarcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C16-Q1","stem":"A shave biopsy of one skin lesion shows a sharply bordered proliferation of uniform basaloid cells with keratin-filled horn cysts and no atypia. Activating mutations in which of the following genes are most common in such lesions?","options":["CTNNB1, which encodes beta-catenin","KIT, a receptor tyrosine kinase","BRAF, a serine-threonine kinase","FGFR3, a receptor tyrosine kinase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-13-F3","obj":"IN-13-F.3","dok":2,"approach":["Uniform basaloid cells with horn cysts and no atypia in a waxy stuck-on lesion is a seborrheic keratosis.","Seborrheic keratosis is a benign clonal proliferation of epidermal keratinocytes.","Sporadic lesions most often carry activating FGFR3 mutations that drive keratinocyte growth."],"why":"Seborrheic keratoses are benign epidermal tumors of older adults. They look waxy and stuck on, and on biopsy show basaloid keratinocytes with keratin-filled horn cysts and no atypia. Many carry activating mutations in FGFR3, a receptor tyrosine kinase, showing that they are true clonal neoplasms. Single lesions are harmless, but a sudden eruption of many lesions is the sign of Leser-Trélat, which can signal an internal cancer, most often gastric adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["Activating CTNNB1 mutations that stabilize beta-catenin are found in pilomatricoma, a hair matrix tumor with shadow cells.","Activating KIT mutations occur in gastrointestinal stromal tumors, mastocytosis, and some acral and mucosal melanomas. Loss-of-function KIT variants cause piebaldism.","Activating BRAF mutations, especially V600E, are typical of melanocytic nevi and many melanomas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Seborrheic keratosis","Growth factor receptor mutations"],"comps":["IN-13-F3","IN-7"]}},{"id":"IN-C16-Q2","stem":"Which of the following additional skin findings is most likely to develop in association with his malignancy?","options":["Erosive stomatitis with polymorphous blistering","Necrolytic migratory erythema of the groin","Episodic facial flushing with telangiectasias","Velvety hyperpigmented plaques in the axillae"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-F3","obj":"IN-13-F.4","dok":3,"approach":["Sudden eruptive seborrheic keratoses with gastric cancer is a paraneoplastic pattern.","Gastric adenocarcinoma is thought to release growth factors, such as TGF-alpha, that stimulate epidermal growth.","The skin sign most tied to gastric adenocarcinoma, often with Leser-Trélat, is malignant acanthosis nigricans."],"why":"Acanthosis nigricans is thick, velvety, darkened skin in body folds such as the axillae and neck. Most cases come from insulin resistance and obesity, but sudden, widespread acanthosis nigricans in an older, thin adult suggests cancer, most often gastric adenocarcinoma, and it often appears with the Leser-Trélat sign. Tumor growth factors acting on epidermal receptors are thought to drive both. Other paraneoplastic skin signs include dermatomyositis with ovarian, lung, and gastrointestinal cancers and paraneoplastic pemphigus with lymphoma.","whyNot":["Painful erosive stomatitis with varied blisters is paraneoplastic pemphigus, which is linked mainly to non-Hodgkin lymphoma and chronic lymphocytic leukemia.","Necrolytic migratory erythema in the groin and perineum is the classic sign of a glucagonoma of the pancreas.","Episodic flushing with telangiectasias is carcinoid syndrome, usually from a small bowel neuroendocrine tumor with liver metastases.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Paraneoplastic skin signs","Acanthosis nigricans"],"comps":["IN-13-F3","IN-11"]}},{"id":"IN-C16-Q3","stem":"Five months later he develops red-purple scaly papules over the knuckles and has difficulty climbing stairs and rising from a chair. Serum creatine kinase is 3,400 U/L (40 to 300). Which of the following additional findings is most likely?","options":["Scarring plaques with follicular plugging on the ears","Lilac discoloration of the upper eyelids","Thick skin of the fingers with anticentromere antibodies","Erythema of the cheeks that spares the nasolabial folds"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-11","obj":"IN-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Scaly papules over the knuckles are Gottron papules.","Gottron papules with proximal muscle weakness and high creatine kinase indicate dermatomyositis, which can be paraneoplastic in older adults.","The other classic skin sign of dermatomyositis is the heliotrope rash on the upper eyelids."],"why":"Dermatomyositis is an autoimmune disease of skin and muscle. Its skin signs are Gottron papules over the knuckles and a lilac (heliotrope) rash of the upper eyelids, often with eyelid swelling. Proximal weakness and a high creatine kinase reflect muscle inflammation. In older adults it can be paraneoplastic, with ovarian, lung, and gastrointestinal cancers among the most common, so new dermatomyositis calls for a cancer search. Lupus causes interface dermatitis with immune deposits at the dermal-epidermal junction, and systemic sclerosis causes sclerodactyly.","whyNot":["Scarring plaques with follicular plugging on the ears and face are discoid lupus.","Correct.","Thickened finger skin (sclerodactyly) with anticentromere antibodies is limited systemic sclerosis (CREST), which does not cause Gottron papules or a high creatine kinase.","A malar rash sparing the nasolabial folds is the photosensitive rash of systemic lupus erythematosus."],"study":{"topics":["Dermatomyositis","Autoimmune skin findings"],"comps":["IN-11","IN-13-F3"]}},{"id":"IN-C16-Q4","stem":"The gastric biopsy shows cells with pleomorphic nuclei and many mitoses. Which of the following features is the most reliable evidence that a tumor is malignant?","options":["Spread to a distant site","Numerous mitotic figures","Marked nuclear pleomorphism","Rapid growth over weeks"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-8","obj":"IN-8.7","dok":3,"approach":["List the features that suggest malignancy: anaplasia, rapid growth, local invasion, and metastasis.","Most of these features can also show up in benign or reactive lesions.","Metastasis, spread to a site not connected to the primary tumor, occurs only with malignant tumors."],"why":"Benign and malignant tumors differ in differentiation, growth rate, local invasion, and metastasis. Anaplasia (pleomorphism, high nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio, atypical mitoses) and fast growth favor cancer but can be seen in benign or reactive lesions. Invasion is strong evidence, and metastasis is the one finding that proves a tumor is malignant. His gastric tumor is an adenocarcinoma (a malignant tumor of glandular epithelium), while his seborrheic keratoses are benign despite their sudden appearance. Grade describes how differentiated a tumor is, and stage describes how far it has spread.","whyNot":["Correct.","Many mitoses show rapid cell division, which can also occur in benign tumors, healing tissue, and hyperplasia.","Pleomorphism is common in cancers, but it also appears in dysplasia and in some benign tumors, so it does not prove malignancy alone.","Rapid growth is not specific. His eruptive seborrheic keratoses grew quickly and are benign."],"study":{"topics":["Benign versus malignant tumors","Grade and stage"],"comps":["IN-8","IN-13-F3"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C17","system":"IN","dx":"Basal cell carcinoma of the scalp after childhood irradiation for tinea capitis","codes":["IN-13-G1"],"case":"A 70-year-old woman comes to the dermatology clinic because of a sore on her scalp that bleeds when she combs her hair. At age 4 she received X-ray treatment of the scalp for ringworm. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 72/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 134/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a 1.4-cm pearly papule on the vertex with a rolled border, branching telangiectasias, and a central ulcer. The surrounding scalp skin is thin, shiny, and hairless, with fine telangiectasias. There is no cervical lymphadenopathy. Biopsy shows nests of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading and clefts between the nests and the surrounding stroma. Diagnosis: basal cell carcinoma of the scalp after childhood scalp irradiation for tinea capitis.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C17-Q1","stem":"If untreated, this tumor is most likely to follow which of the following courses?","options":["Slow local destruction with very rare metastasis","Hematogenous spread to the lungs within a few months","Spontaneous regression after a few weeks of rapid growth","Early spread to the occipital and cervical lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-G1","obj":"IN-13-G.1","dok":2,"approach":["A pearly papule with rolled border and telangiectasias, with basaloid nests and peripheral palisading, is basal cell carcinoma.","Basal cell carcinoma grows slowly and invades local tissue, and it can erode deeply if neglected.","Its cells depend on the surrounding stroma, which helps explain why metastasis is very rare."],"why":"Basal cell carcinoma is the most common skin cancer. It arises from basal keratinocytes, driven by UV and other DNA damage and by activated hedgehog signaling. It grows slowly and invades locally, and neglected tumors can destroy skin, cartilage, and even bone (rodent ulcer), but metastasis is very rare. Its look is a pearly papule with rolled borders and telangiectasias, sometimes with central ulceration.","whyNot":["Correct.","Early spread to the lungs through the blood is typical of aggressive tumors such as melanoma or angiosarcoma.","Rapid growth over weeks followed by regression is the pattern of keratoacanthoma, a squamous lesion with a central keratin plug.","Early lymph node spread is more typical of melanoma, Merkel cell carcinoma, and high-risk squamous cell carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Basal cell carcinoma behavior","Skin cancer comparison"],"comps":["IN-13-G1","IN-8"]}},{"id":"IN-C17-Q2","stem":"Sequencing of the tumor shows loss of both copies of a gene that encodes the receptor for a secreted developmental signaling protein. Which of the following is the most likely molecular consequence?","options":["Unchecked CDK4 activity with hyperphosphorylated Rb","Unrestrained smoothened signaling with GLI activation","Stabilized beta-catenin entering the nucleus","Constitutive BRAF kinase activity in the MAPK pathway"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-7","obj":"IN-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["The tumor suppressor most often lost in basal cell carcinoma is PTCH1, the receptor for sonic hedgehog.","Unbound PTCH1 normally holds back smoothened.","Losing both PTCH1 copies frees smoothened, which activates GLI transcription factors and drives growth."],"why":"PTCH1 encodes Patched, the receptor for sonic hedgehog. Without ligand, Patched blocks smoothened, keeping the pathway off. When both PTCH1 copies are lost, smoothened is always active and GLI transcription factors turn on growth genes. Most sporadic basal cell carcinomas carry this change, and germline PTCH1 mutations cause Gorlin syndrome (nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome), with many basal cell carcinomas starting young.","whyNot":["Unchecked CDK4 activity follows loss of CDKN2A (p16), the gene behind familial melanoma.","Correct.","Stabilized beta-catenin follows loss of APC or activating CTNNB1 mutations, as in colorectal cancer and pilomatricoma.","Constitutive BRAF activity comes from activating BRAF mutations, typical of nevi and melanoma, not from loss of a receptor gene."],"study":{"topics":["Hedgehog signaling","Gorlin syndrome"],"comps":["IN-7","IN-13-G1"]}},{"id":"IN-C17-Q3","stem":"Biopsy of the thin, hairless scalp skin beside the tumor shows dermal fibrosis, loss of hair follicles, scattered enlarged atypical fibroblasts, and small arteries with thickened walls and narrowed lumens. Which of the following best explains these changes?","options":["Hypertrophic scarring after a thermal burn","Acute radiation dermatitis from a recent exposure","Late radiation injury with obliterative endarteritis","Photoaging with metalloproteinase breakdown of collagen"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-10","obj":"IN-10.6","dok":2,"approach":["She had scalp irradiation in childhood, and the skin is thin, shiny, hairless, and telangiectatic.","Ionizing radiation damages DNA directly and through hydroxyl radicals from water, killing dividing cells and injuring vessels.","Late effects include fibrosis, atypical radiation fibroblasts, obliterative endarteritis, and later cancers in the field."],"why":"Ionizing radiation breaks DNA directly and through free radicals made from water. Early effects in skin, within days to weeks, are erythema and desquamation. Months to years later, damage to small arteries narrows their lumens (obliterative endarteritis), so the tissue becomes ischemic, atrophic, and fibrotic, with telangiectasias and atypical fibroblasts. Hair follicles are lost, causing permanent alopecia. Irradiated skin carries a higher risk of later basal cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and angiosarcoma.","whyNot":["A hypertrophic burn scar is raised and has thick collagen bundles, without radiation-type atypical fibroblasts or vascular narrowing, and she has no burn history.","Acute radiation dermatitis appears within weeks of exposure as erythema and desquamation. Her exposure was decades ago.","Correct.","Photoaging shows solar elastosis with thin, damaged collagen, but it does not cause atypical fibroblasts or narrowed arteries."],"study":{"topics":["Radiation injury to skin","Ionizing radiation and DNA damage"],"comps":["IN-10","IN-13-G1"]}},{"id":"IN-C17-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best describes the organism that most likely caused her childhood scalp infection?","options":["Yeast that forms pseudohyphae in moist skin folds","Mold with broad nonseptate hyphae that invades vessels","Mold with septate hyphae that digests keratin","Lipophilic yeast that forms short hyphae in sebum"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-12","obj":"IN-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Ringworm of the scalp is tinea capitis.","Tinea is caused by dermatophytes such as Trichophyton and Microsporum.","Dermatophytes are molds with septate hyphae that use keratinases to live in stratum corneum, hair, and nails."],"why":"Dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum, and Epidermophyton) are molds that live only in keratinized tissue. They make keratinases that let them digest stratum corneum, hair, and nails, and they rarely invade living tissue. In tinea capitis they invade the hair shaft, causing scaly patches with broken hairs and sometimes a boggy kerion. A KOH preparation shows branching septate hyphae. Before griseofulvin became available in the late 1950s, scalp X-ray treatment was used to remove infected hair, which is why some adults now have radiation-related tumors.","whyNot":["Pseudohyphae in moist skin folds describe Candida, which infects intertriginous skin and mucosa rather than hair shafts.","Broad nonseptate hyphae invading vessels describe Mucor and Rhizopus, which cause rhinocerebral infection in diabetic ketoacidosis.","Correct.","A lipophilic yeast with short hyphae is Malassezia, which causes tinea versicolor (spaghetti and meatballs on KOH), not scalp ringworm."],"study":{"topics":["Dermatophytes","KOH preparation findings"],"comps":["IN-12","IN-13-H10"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C18","system":"IN","dx":"Inflammatory acne vulgaris with polycystic ovary syndrome","codes":["IN-13-H1"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl comes to the adolescent clinic because of worsening acne over 2 years. She has 4 or 5 menstrual periods a year and dark hair growing on her chin and lower abdomen. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Body mass index is 31 kg/m2. Physical examination shows open and closed comedones, inflammatory papules, pustules, and three tender nodules on the face, chest, and upper back. Serum total testosterone is 82 ng/dL (15 to 70); 17-hydroxyprogesterone and prolactin are within the reference ranges. The LH to FSH ratio is 3:1. Pelvic ultrasonography shows enlarged ovaries with many peripheral follicles. Diagnosis: inflammatory acne vulgaris with polycystic ovary syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C18-Q1","stem":"Her high androgen levels worsen her acne mainly through which of the following effects in the pilosebaceous unit?","options":["Loss of cohesion between follicular keratinocytes","Direct stimulation of bacterial growth in the follicle","Increased apocrine sweat secretion into the follicle","Sebaceous gland enlargement with greater sebum output"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-13-H1","obj":"IN-13-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Polycystic ovary syndrome raises ovarian androgen production, as her testosterone shows.","Sebocytes have androgen receptors and convert testosterone to dihydrotestosterone.","Androgen signaling enlarges sebaceous glands and increases sebum, one of the four steps of acne."],"why":"Acne develops in four steps: abnormal keratinization plugs the follicle (microcomedo), androgens increase sebum, Cutibacterium acnes grows in the sebum-rich plug, and inflammation follows. Androgens act on sebocytes, which convert testosterone to dihydrotestosterone and respond by enlarging and making more sebum. Conditions with high androgens, such as polycystic ovary syndrome, and drugs such as glucocorticoids and anabolic steroids, worsen acne. Comedones are noninflammatory, while papules, pustules, and nodules show inflammation.","whyNot":["Acne involves the opposite: follicular keratinocytes stick together and build up, plugging the follicle as a microcomedo.","Androgens do not act on bacteria. Cutibacterium acnes grows more because there is more sebum for it to feed on.","Apocrine glands open into hair follicles in the axillae and groin, but their sweat does not drive acne on the face and trunk.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Steps in acne pathogenesis","Hyperandrogenism in polycystic ovary syndrome"],"comps":["IN-13-H1","IN-5"]}},{"id":"IN-C18-Q2","stem":"Gram stain of material from a pustule shows gram-positive rods, and the organism grows best under anaerobic conditions. Which of the following activities of this organism most directly promotes the inflammatory lesions?","options":["Exfoliative toxin cleavage of desmoglein 1","Coagulase conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin around the organism","Superantigen cross-linking of MHC class II and T cell receptors","Lipase release of free fatty acids from sebum triglycerides"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-12","obj":"IN-12.7","dok":3,"approach":["A gram-positive anaerobic rod in an acne pustule is Cutibacterium acnes.","C. acnes lives on sebum and makes lipases that split triglycerides into free fatty acids.","Free fatty acids irritate the follicle wall, and C. acnes also activates TLR2 on monocytes, releasing IL-1, IL-8, and TNF-alpha."],"why":"Cutibacterium acnes is a gram-positive anaerobic rod that grows in the oxygen-poor, sebum-filled follicle. Its lipases break sebum triglycerides into free fatty acids, which are irritating and help rupture the follicle wall. The bacteria also activate TLR2 on monocytes and neutrophils, which release IL-1, IL-8, and TNF-alpha and draw in more inflammatory cells. This turns comedones into papules, pustules, and nodules. Hot tub folliculitis is a different follicular infection, caused by the gram-negative, oxidase-positive Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","whyNot":["Exfoliative toxins that cleave desmoglein 1 come from Staphylococcus aureus and cause bullous impetigo and staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome.","Coagulase is a S. aureus enzyme that helps wall off abscesses. The organism here is an anaerobic gram-positive rod.","Superantigens that bridge MHC class II and T cell receptors come from S. aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes and cause toxic shock syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cutibacterium acnes","TLR2 in innate immunity"],"comps":["IN-12","IN-13-H1"]}},{"id":"IN-C18-Q3","stem":"Which of the following pairs of lipids is most characteristic of the secretion her enlarged glands produce in excess?","options":["Ceramides and free cholesterol","Glucosylceramides and phospholipids","Squalene and wax esters","Phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-5","obj":"IN-5.6","dok":2,"approach":["Androgens enlarge her sebaceous glands, so the secretion in excess is sebum.","Sebum is mostly triglycerides, with wax esters and squalene that are made almost only by sebaceous glands.","Separate sebum lipids from the barrier lipids of the stratum corneum, which come from lamellar bodies."],"why":"Skin makes two different lipid mixtures. Sebaceous glands secrete sebum, made of triglycerides (about half), wax esters, and squalene, with small amounts of cholesterol. Wax esters and squalene are typical of human sebum and are hardly made elsewhere in the body. The stratum corneum barrier is a different mixture of ceramides, cholesterol, and free fatty acids released from lamellar bodies of the granular layer. The dermis contains glycosaminoglycans such as hyaluronic acid and dermatan sulfate, which hold water rather than forming a lipid barrier.","whyNot":["Ceramides, cholesterol, and free fatty acids form the lipid barrier between corneocytes in the stratum corneum.","Glucosylceramides and phospholipids are packed into lamellar bodies in the granular layer and are processed into the stratum corneum barrier lipids.","Correct.","Phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin are major lipids of cell membranes, not of sebum."],"study":{"topics":["Sebum composition","Stratum corneum lipid barrier"],"comps":["IN-5","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C18-Q4","stem":"At age 22 years, she returns with recurrent painful deep nodules in both axillae and the groin that rupture, drain, and heal with sinus tracts and rope-like scars. She smokes 10 cigarettes daily. Which of the following is the primary process in this condition?","options":["Superficial staphylococcal infection of follicle openings","Occlusion and rupture of terminal hair follicles","Primary bacterial infection of apocrine sweat glands","Autoimmune destruction of eccrine sweat glands"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-C","obj":"IN-13-C.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recurrent deep painful nodules, sinus tracts, and rope-like scars in the axillae and groin are hidradenitis suppurativa.","The disease starts with follicular hyperkeratosis that plugs terminal hair follicles.","The plugged follicle dilates and ruptures, spilling keratin and bacteria into the dermis and setting off chronic inflammation."],"why":"Hidradenitis suppurativa is a chronic inflammatory disease of follicles in the axillary, inguinal, perineal, and inframammary skin. The follicle becomes plugged with keratin, dilates, and ruptures, and its contents trigger a strong immune reaction. Repeated cycles form painful nodules, abscesses, draining sinus tracts, and rope-like scars. Obesity and smoking are the main modifiable risk factors, and it shares a follicular occlusion mechanism with severe nodular acne.","whyNot":["Superficial staphylococcal infection of follicle openings is folliculitis, which forms small pustules and heals without sinus tracts.","Correct.","This condition was once thought to start as an apocrine gland infection, but apocrine involvement is secondary, and bacteria colonize lesions after the follicle ruptures.","Eccrine glands are not targeted by an autoimmune attack in this condition, and eccrine loss would cause reduced sweating, not nodules."],"study":{"topics":["Hidradenitis suppurativa","Follicular occlusion disorders"],"comps":["IN-13-C","IN-13-H1"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C19","system":"IN","dx":"Candidal diaper dermatitis","codes":["IN-13-H2"],"case":"A 7-month-old boy is brought to the pediatric clinic because of a red rash in the diaper area for 5 days that has not improved with a barrier cream. Ten days ago he completed a course of amoxicillin for acute otitis media. He is breastfed, eats pureed foods, and is growing normally. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 122/min, and respirations are 30/min. Physical examination shows bright, beefy red plaques on the lower abdomen, genitals, and inner thighs that extend deep into the inguinal folds, with scattered satellite papules and pustules beyond the border. White plaques on the buccal mucosa leave a red base when scraped. A KOH preparation from a satellite pustule shows budding yeast cells and pseudohyphae. Diagnosis: candidal diaper dermatitis.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C19-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings best distinguishes his rash from irritant contact diaper dermatitis?","options":["Burning discomfort during diaper changes","Involvement of the inguinal skin folds","Erythema of the convex buttock surfaces","Onset after long contact with wet diapers"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-13-H2","obj":"IN-13-H.2","dok":2,"approach":["Irritant diaper dermatitis comes from urine and stool touching the skin.","Skin folds are protected from contact, so irritant dermatitis spares the creases.","Candida thrives in warm, moist folds, so it involves the creases and adds satellite pustules."],"why":"Irritant diaper dermatitis affects the convex skin that touches the wet diaper, such as the buttocks, genitals, and lower abdomen, and spares the deep folds. Candidal diaper dermatitis involves the folds, where moisture is trapped, and shows beefy red plaques with satellite papules and pustules. Thrush in the mouth often comes with it. Persistent or severe mucocutaneous candidiasis should prompt a search for host problems such as diabetes, steroid use, or defects in T cell immunity.","whyNot":["Both types of diaper dermatitis can sting and burn, so discomfort does not tell them apart.","Correct.","Erythema of the convex surfaces is the typical pattern of irritant dermatitis, and Candida can involve these areas too, so it does not separate them.","Prolonged wet diaper contact sets the stage for both, since moisture irritates skin and also helps Candida grow."],"study":{"topics":["Diaper dermatitis types","Cutaneous candidiasis"],"comps":["IN-13-H2","IN-12"]}},{"id":"IN-C19-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains how his recent antibiotic course predisposed him to this infection?","options":["Loss of commensal bacteria that compete with Candida","Drug-induced neutropenia that reduced yeast killing","Suppression of Th17 responses to fungal antigens","Direct stimulation of germ tube formation by the drug"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-12","obj":"IN-12.4","dok":2,"approach":["Candida albicans is a normal commensal of the mouth, gut, and perineal skin.","Normal bacteria limit Candida by competing for nutrients and attachment sites.","A broad-spectrum antibiotic kills those bacteria, so Candida overgrows and causes thrush and diaper dermatitis."],"why":"Candida albicans is a dimorphic fungus that lives as a commensal on mucosa and skin. It appears as budding yeast and, when invading, as pseudohyphae and true hyphae, and it forms germ tubes in serum at 37 °C (98.6 °F). Overgrowth needs a change in the host, such as moisture, antibiotics that remove competing bacteria, diabetes, glucocorticoids, or defects in T cells (mucocutaneous disease) or neutrophils (invasive disease). In this infant the antibiotic course plus a moist diaper area allowed mucocutaneous infection.","whyNot":["Correct.","Amoxicillin rarely causes neutropenia, and his normal course with no fever or other infections gives no sign of it.","Defective Th17 responses (for example from STAT3 or AIRE defects) cause chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis. Amoxicillin does not suppress Th17 cells.","Germ tubes form when Candida switches to hyphae in response to body temperature and serum, not to antibiotics."],"study":{"topics":["Candida albicans","Host defenses against fungi"],"comps":["IN-12","IN-13-H2"]}},{"id":"IN-C19-Q3","stem":"An 8-month-old girl is brought to the same clinic because of diarrhea, patchy hair loss, and irritability that began after she was switched from breast milk to formula 4 weeks ago. Physical examination shows sharply bordered, crusted, red plaques around the mouth and anus and on the hands and feet. The plaques are colonized by Candida. Serum alkaline phosphatase is 70 U/L (150 to 420). Deficiency of which of the following is most likely?","options":["Riboflavin","Essential fatty acids","Biotin","Zinc"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-5","obj":"IN-5.4","dok":3,"approach":["Periorificial and acral dermatitis, alopecia, and diarrhea form the triad of acrodermatitis enteropathica.","Symptoms starting after weaning from breast milk fit the inherited zinc absorption defect, which breast milk partly masks.","Alkaline phosphatase is a zinc-dependent enzyme, so a low value supports zinc deficiency."],"why":"Zinc is needed for cell division, wound healing, and many enzymes, including alkaline phosphatase. In acrodermatitis enteropathica, inherited defects in the intestinal zinc transporter (SLC39A4) cause symptoms once the infant stops breastfeeding. The rash is crusted and sharply bordered around the mouth, anus, and on the hands and feet, and it is often colonized by Candida, so it can be mistaken for candidiasis. Diarrhea, hair loss, irritability, and poor healing complete the picture, and zinc supplements reverse it.","whyNot":["Riboflavin deficiency causes angular cheilitis, glossitis, and seborrheic-like dermatitis, without acral plaques or diarrhea.","Essential fatty acid deficiency causes a generalized dry, scaly dermatitis and poor growth, not crusted periorificial and acral plaques.","Biotin deficiency can cause periorificial dermatitis and hair loss, but it does not lower alkaline phosphatase, and it tends to bring hypotonia and seizures rather than acral plaques with diarrhea.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acrodermatitis enteropathica","Nutritional deficiency dermatoses"],"comps":["IN-5","IN-13-H2"]}},{"id":"IN-C19-Q4","stem":"The boy also has greasy, yellow scale on the scalp vertex and fine scaling in the eyebrows and nasolabial folds. These findings are most likely an inflammatory response to which of the following?","options":["A dimorphic yeast forming pseudohyphae in moist skin","A dermatophyte that invades the hair shaft","A gram-positive coccus releasing exfoliative toxin","A lipophilic yeast living on sebum-rich skin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-B","obj":"IN-13-B.8","dok":2,"approach":["Greasy yellow scale on the scalp in an infant is cradle cap, a form of seborrheic dermatitis.","Seborrheic dermatitis affects sebum-rich areas: scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, and behind the ears.","It is an inflammatory response to Malassezia, a lipophilic yeast that feeds on sebum."],"why":"Seborrheic dermatitis is inflammation in sebaceous areas, driven by the response to Malassezia, a yeast that needs lipid to grow. In infants, sebaceous glands are active for a few months because of maternal androgens, and cradle cap appears as greasy yellow scale on the scalp. In adults it involves the scalp (dandruff), eyebrows, and nasolabial folds. Severe or widespread disease in adults suggests HIV infection or Parkinson disease.","whyNot":["Candida is the dimorphic yeast causing his diaper rash, but it lives in moist folds and does not cause greasy scalp and eyebrow scale.","Dermatophytes that invade the hair shaft cause tinea capitis, with patchy hair loss and broken hairs, which is uncommon in infants and does not involve eyebrows and nasolabial folds.","A gram-positive coccus with exfoliative toxin is S. aureus, which causes bullous impetigo or scalded skin syndrome with blisters and erosions.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Seborrheic dermatitis","Malassezia"],"comps":["IN-13-B","IN-12"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C20","system":"IN","dx":"Streptococcal cellulitis of the leg entering through tinea pedis fissures","codes":["IN-13-H3","IN-13-H10"],"case":"A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the emergency department because of 3 days of spreading redness, warmth, and swelling of his left lower leg with chills. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 106/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 132/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tender, warm, swollen erythema over the anterior left shin with indistinct borders and no fluctuance or crepitus. The skin between the fourth and fifth left toes is macerated, scaly, and fissured. Leukocyte count is 14,200/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000) with 84% neutrophils, random serum glucose is 262 mg/dL (under 140), and hemoglobin A1c is 9.4% (under 5.7). A KOH preparation of the toe web scale shows branching septate hyphae. Diagnosis: nonpurulent cellulitis of the left leg, most likely streptococcal, entering through fissures of tinea pedis.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C20-Q1","stem":"The indistinct border of his erythema reflects infection centered at which of the following levels?","options":["Deep dermis and subcutaneous fat","Deep fascia overlying skeletal muscle","Upper dermis and superficial lymphatics","Subcorneal epidermis and follicle openings"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-13-H3","obj":"IN-13-H.3","dok":2,"approach":["The border of skin redness depends on how deep the infection lies.","Deep infection in the dermis and subcutaneous fat spreads in loose tissue and fades gradually into normal skin.","Superficial dermal infection with lymphatic involvement gives a raised, sharp edge, as in erysipelas."],"why":"Cellulitis involves the deep dermis and subcutaneous fat, so the redness is flat and fades into normal skin with no clear edge. It is usually caused by beta-hemolytic streptococci, with Staphylococcus aureus more likely when there is pus or an abscess. Erysipelas sits higher, in the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, and gives a raised, sharply bordered, bright red plaque, nearly always from Streptococcus pyogenes. Both usually enter through a break in the skin, such as toe web fissures, wounds, or ulcers.","whyNot":["Correct.","Infection spreading along the deep fascia is necrotizing fasciitis, with pain out of proportion to the findings, rapid spread, and sometimes crepitus.","Infection of the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics is erysipelas, which forms a raised, sharply demarcated, bright red plaque.","Infection just under the stratum corneum is impetigo, with honey-colored crusts or blisters, and infection of follicle openings is folliculitis."],"study":{"topics":["Cellulitis versus erysipelas","Depth of skin infections"],"comps":["IN-13-H3","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C20-Q2","stem":"The organism in his toe webs belongs to the same group as the organism that causes which of the following conditions?","options":["Pityriasis rosea","Erythrasma","Tinea cruris","Tinea versicolor"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-H10","obj":"IN-13-H.10","dok":2,"approach":["Septate branching hyphae in macerated toe webs indicate tinea pedis, a dermatophyte infection.","Dermatophyte infections are named by site: capitis, corporis, cruris, pedis, barbae, and unguium.","Tinea cruris, in the groin, is caused by the same dermatophytes and often spreads from the feet."],"why":"Dermatophytes such as Trichophyton rubrum cause tinea at different sites: scalp (capitis, sometimes with kerion and hair loss), body (corporis, annular plaques with a scaly border and central clearing), groin (cruris), feet (pedis), beard (barbae), and nails (unguium, or onychomycosis). The feet often seed the groin and nails in the same person. Tinea versicolor is the exception to the naming, because it is caused by Malassezia yeast, not a dermatophyte. Fissured tinea pedis is a common entry point for streptococcal cellulitis of the leg.","whyNot":["Pityriasis rosea is a self-limited rash probably linked to human herpesvirus 6 or 7 reactivation, with a herald patch, not a fungal infection.","Erythrasma is a bacterial infection of skin folds by Corynebacterium minutissimum, which glows coral-red under Wood lamp.","Correct.","Tinea versicolor carries the tinea name but is caused by Malassezia, a lipophilic yeast, not a dermatophyte."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of tinea","Tinea versicolor"],"comps":["IN-13-H10","IN-12"]}},{"id":"IN-C20-Q3","stem":"Which of the following effects of chronic hyperglycemia most directly increases his risk of bacterial skin infection?","options":["Loss of activity of terminal complement components","Impaired neutrophil chemotaxis and phagocytosis","Selective deficiency of secretory IgA","Depletion of circulating CD4 T cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-9","obj":"IN-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["His hemoglobin A1c of 9.4% shows long-standing poor glucose control.","High glucose impairs neutrophil adherence, chemotaxis, phagocytosis, and killing, and diabetes also causes neuropathy and poor blood flow.","Combined with broken skin from tinea pedis, this raises the risk of streptococcal and staphylococcal skin infection."],"why":"Poorly controlled diabetes weakens the innate defenses that clear bacteria from skin. High glucose impairs neutrophil movement, phagocytosis, and oxidative killing, while neuropathy hides small injuries and vascular disease slows healing. The host context predicts the organism. Fissured toe webs, venous insufficiency, and lymphedema favor beta-hemolytic streptococci. Injection drug use favors S. aureus, including MRSA. Neutropenia adds gram-negative rods such as Pseudomonas, and T cell deficiency adds viral and fungal infections.","whyNot":["Terminal complement deficiency (C5 to C9) causes recurrent Neisseria infections, and it is not caused by diabetes.","Correct.","Selective IgA deficiency is a congenital defect linked to sinopulmonary and gastrointestinal infections, not to diabetes.","CD4 T cell depletion is the hallmark of HIV infection and causes opportunistic infections, while poorly controlled diabetes mainly affects neutrophils."],"study":{"topics":["Diabetes and infection risk","Predicting organisms from host factors"],"comps":["IN-9","IN-13-H3"]}},{"id":"IN-C20-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains his elevated temperature?","options":["Cytokine-induced PGE2 raising the hypothalamic set point","Bacterial toxin acting directly on skeletal muscle","Sympathetic surge from pain raising metabolic rate","Impaired heat loss with an unchanged hypothalamic set point"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-4","obj":"IN-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["Bacterial infection activates macrophages to release IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha.","These cytokines induce COX-2 in the hypothalamus, which makes PGE2.","PGE2 raises the hypothalamic set point, so the body conserves and makes heat until core temperature reaches the new set point."],"why":"In fever, pyrogenic cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha) from macrophages reach the preoptic hypothalamus and induce COX-2, which makes PGE2. PGE2 raises the temperature set point, so the body constricts skin vessels and shivers, which explains his chills, until the core matches the new set point. Antipyretics such as NSAIDs and acetaminophen work by blocking PGE2 production. In hyperthermia the set point stays normal, and temperature rises because heat production or gain exceeds heat loss.","whyNot":["Correct.","Bacterial products act mainly by stimulating immune cells to release cytokines, not by heating muscle directly.","Pain can raise heart rate and metabolic rate slightly, but it does not reset the thermostat to produce a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F).","Impaired heat loss or excess heat production with a normal set point is hyperthermia, as in heat stroke or malignant hyperthermia, and antipyretics do not help."],"study":{"topics":["Fever mechanism","Fever versus hyperthermia"],"comps":["IN-4","IN-13-H3"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C21","system":"IN","dx":"Erythema nodosum with acute sarcoidosis (Löfgren syndrome)","codes":["IN-13-H5"],"case":"A 32-year-old woman comes to the rheumatology clinic because of a 2-week history of painful red lumps on both shins, swollen ankles, and fatigue. Her temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows multiple tender, warm, erythematous, poorly demarcated nodules 2 to 4 cm in diameter over the anterior shins and periarticular swelling of both ankles. There is no ulceration. A chest x-ray shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. Serum calcium concentration is 10.9 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), and serum angiotensin-converting enzyme activity is increased. Diagnosis: erythema nodosum with acute sarcoidosis (Löfgren syndrome).","questions":[{"id":"IN-C21-Q1","stem":"A deep incisional biopsy specimen of one shin nodule is obtained. In which of the following sites is the inflammatory infiltrate most likely to be centered?","options":["Fibrous septa between subcutaneous fat lobules","Papillary dermis just beneath the epidermis","Basal layer along the dermal-epidermal junction","Fat lobules of the subcutis around inflamed arteries"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-H5","obj":"IN-13-H.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize tender red nodules on the anterior shins that do not ulcerate as erythema nodosum.","Recall that erythema nodosum is a septal panniculitis without vasculitis.","Locate the septa: the fibrous bands that divide the subcutaneous fat into lobules."],"why":"Erythema nodosum is a panniculitis, an inflammation of the subcutaneous fat, and it is centered on the fibrous septa between fat lobules. Early lesions show edema and neutrophils in the septa, and older lesions show lymphocytes, histiocytes, and giant cells with septal fibrosis. There is no vasculitis and little fat necrosis, which is why the nodules heal without scarring or ulceration. It is a reactive pattern linked to streptococcal infection, sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, endemic fungi, inflammatory bowel disease, pregnancy, and drugs.","whyNot":["Correct.","The papillary dermis is the site of many superficial dermatoses, such as the neutrophilic microabscesses of dermatitis herpetiformis. Erythema nodosum sits much deeper, in the subcutis.","Damage along the basal layer is interface dermatitis, seen in lichen planus and lupus. It produces surface scale and pigment change, not deep tender nodules.","Lobular panniculitis with vasculitis describes erythema induratum (nodular vasculitis), which favors the calves and can ulcerate and scar."],"study":{"topics":["Septal versus lobular panniculitis","Causes of erythema nodosum"],"comps":["IN-13-H","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C21-Q2","stem":"A biopsy specimen of a hilar lymph node shows tightly packed epithelioid macrophages and multinucleated giant cells with no central necrosis. Which of the following signals most directly drives the formation of these lesions?","options":["Interleukin-4 from Th2 cells","Interferon-gamma from Th1 cells","Interleukin-17 from Th17 cells","Interleukin-10 from regulatory T cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-11","obj":"IN-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the lesion: noncaseating granulomas, the hallmark of sarcoidosis.","Recall that granulomas form when CD4+ Th1 cells activate macrophages.","Name the cytokine: interferon-gamma turns macrophages into epithelioid cells and giant cells."],"why":"A granuloma is a focus of chronic inflammation made of activated macrophages. Macrophages present antigen and release IL-12, which drives CD4+ T cells toward the Th1 subset. Th1 cells release interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages so they become epithelioid cells and fuse into multinucleated giant cells. TNF-alpha helps hold the granuloma together. Sarcoid granulomas lack central necrosis (noncaseating), while tuberculous granulomas usually have central caseous necrosis.","whyNot":["IL-4 from Th2 cells drives IgE class switching and allergic, eosinophil-rich inflammation, as in asthma and helminth infection. It does not build granulomas.","Correct.","IL-17 from Th17 cells recruits neutrophils and defends against extracellular bacteria and fungi at mucosal surfaces. It is central to psoriasis.","IL-10 from regulatory T cells suppresses macrophage activation and would dampen granuloma formation."],"study":{"topics":["Granuloma formation","Caseating versus noncaseating granulomas"],"comps":["IN-11","IN-13-H5"]}},{"id":"IN-C21-Q3","stem":"Her serum parathyroid hormone concentration is 8 pg/mL (10 to 65). Activated macrophages in her granulomas carry out a conversion that is not regulated by parathyroid hormone. Which of the following conversions most likely explains her hypercalcemia?","options":["7-dehydrocholesterol to cholecalciferol","Cholecalciferol to 25-hydroxyvitamin D","25-hydroxyvitamin D to 24,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","25-hydroxyvitamin D to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-5","obj":"IN-5.5","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the normal pathway: UVB in the epidermis, 25-hydroxylation in the liver, 1-alpha-hydroxylation in the kidney.","Note that low PTH with high calcium means the calcium source is independent of PTH.","Recall that granuloma macrophages express 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which makes active calcitriol without PTH control."],"why":"Vitamin D is made when UVB light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in the epidermis to cholecalciferol. The liver adds a 25-hydroxyl group, and the kidney adds the 1-alpha-hydroxyl group to make 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), the active hormone. Normally PTH controls the renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so calcitriol production falls when calcium is high. Macrophages in sarcoid granulomas express their own 1-alpha-hydroxylase that ignores this feedback, so calcitriol rises, gut calcium absorption rises, and PTH is suppressed.","whyNot":["This UVB-driven step happens in the epidermis. It depends on sun exposure and skin pigment and does not explain PTH-independent hypercalcemia.","The liver does this 25-hydroxylation, and it is loosely regulated. 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the storage form and has little activity of its own.","24-hydroxylation produces an inactive metabolite. It is the route the kidney uses to turn vitamin D off when calcium is high.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D synthesis and activation","Hypercalcemia in granulomatous disease"],"comps":["IN-5","IN-13-H5"]}},{"id":"IN-C21-Q4","stem":"Her hypercalcemia persists for a year without treatment. A renal biopsy specimen shows calcium deposits in the basement membranes of otherwise viable tubules and in the interstitium. Which of the following best describes this process?","options":["Dystrophic calcification of necrotic tubular cells","Metastatic calcification of normal tissue","Amyloid deposition with apple-green birefringence","Cholesterol deposition within foamy macrophages"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-8","obj":"IN-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the setting: calcium is deposited in living, previously normal tissue.","Link it to the serum: she has sustained hypercalcemia.","Name the process: calcification of normal tissue driven by high serum calcium is metastatic calcification."],"why":"Pathologic calcification comes in two forms. Dystrophic calcification occurs in dead or damaged tissue, such as old infarcts, atheromas, and damaged heart valves, while serum calcium stays normal. Metastatic calcification occurs in normal tissue when serum calcium or phosphate is high. It favors tissues that lose acid and so have an alkaline interior, such as the kidney tubules, lung alveoli, and gastric mucosa. In the kidney this nephrocalcinosis can impair function, which is one reason hypercalcemia from sarcoidosis is treated.","whyNot":["Dystrophic calcification needs prior necrosis and occurs with normal serum calcium. Here the tubules are viable and calcium is high.","Correct.","Amyloid is a proteinaceous deposit identified with Congo red and apple-green birefringence under polarized light. It is not calcium.","Cholesterol-laden foam cells form xanthomas and atheromas. They are lipid infiltrates, not mineral deposits."],"study":{"topics":["Dystrophic versus metastatic calcification","Tissue infiltrates: lipid and amyloid"],"comps":["IN-8","IN-13-H5"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C22","system":"IN","dx":"Recurrent furunculosis from community-acquired MRSA","codes":["IN-13-H6","IN-13-H8"],"case":"A 20-year-old man who is on his college wrestling team comes to the student health clinic because of a painful boil on his thigh, his third in 4 months. Two teammates have had similar lesions. His temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F). Physical examination shows a 2-cm tender, fluctuant, erythematous nodule on the posterior thigh centered on a hair follicle, with a central pustule. Two healed scars are present nearby. Incision and drainage yields thick yellow pus. Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters. Culture grows Staphylococcus aureus that is catalase positive, coagulase positive, and resistant to oxacillin. Diagnosis: recurrent furunculosis caused by community-acquired methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA).","questions":[{"id":"IN-C22-Q1","stem":"Three months later he develops fever and a 6-cm indurated, painful plaque on the back of his neck with several openings draining pus. Compared with his thigh lesion, which of the following best describes the extent of this new lesion?","options":["Superficial infection of the upper follicle only","One follicle with an abscess in the deep dermis","Several follicles joined by subcutaneous abscesses","Diffuse spread through dermis and subcutis without pus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-H6","obj":"IN-13-H.6","dok":3,"approach":["Place each follicular infection on a depth scale: folliculitis, furuncle, carbuncle.","Note the new features: a large plaque, several draining openings, and fever.","Match them to a carbuncle, which links several infected follicles through the subcutis."],"why":"Follicular infections are graded by depth and extent. Folliculitis is a superficial pustule at the follicle opening. A furuncle (boil), like his thigh lesion, is a deeper abscess around one follicle that extends into the dermis. A carbuncle is a group of furuncles connected by abscesses in the subcutaneous tissue, with several draining openings, often on the thick skin of the nape or back. Carbuncles more often cause fever and bacteremia. Staphylococcus aureus causes most of these lesions.","whyNot":["Infection limited to the upper follicle is folliculitis, which produces small pustules. Hot tub folliculitis from Pseudomonas aeruginosa is one example.","A single follicle with a deep dermal abscess is a furuncle, which describes his earlier thigh lesion.","Correct.","Diffuse spreading infection of the dermis and subcutis without a pus collection is cellulitis."],"study":{"topics":["Folliculitis, furuncle, and carbuncle","Depth of skin infections"],"comps":["IN-13-H6","IN-9"]}},{"id":"IN-C22-Q2","stem":"Molecular typing of his isolate is performed. Which of the following results is most likely?","options":["Small SCCmec type IV with PVL genes","mecA absent with PVL genes present","Plasmid beta-lactamase gene with TSST-1","Large SCCmec type II without PVL genes"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-13-H8","obj":"IN-13-H.8","dok":3,"approach":["Oxacillin resistance in S. aureus means the mecA gene is present.","Recall that mecA rides on a mobile element, the staphylococcal cassette chromosome mec (SCCmec).","Match the setting: a healthy athlete with recurrent abscesses fits the community strain, which carries small SCCmec types and Panton-Valentine leukocidin."],"why":"All MRSA strains carry mecA on a mobile genetic island called SCCmec. Hospital strains usually carry large SCCmec types (I, II, III) that also hold other resistance genes, and they rarely make Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL). Community strains usually carry the small type IV element, stay susceptible to more non-beta-lactam drugs, and often make PVL, a pore-forming toxin that kills neutrophils and causes necrotic abscesses and furuncles. They spread through skin contact and shared equipment in athletes, military recruits, prisons, and people who inject drugs. The anterior nares are the main reservoir, which is why recurrent cases may be decolonized.","whyNot":["Correct.","Without mecA the isolate would be susceptible to oxacillin. This pattern fits a PVL-producing methicillin-susceptible strain.","Plasmid penicillinase inactivates penicillin but not oxacillin. TSST-1 causes toxic shock syndrome, not recurrent abscesses.","Large SCCmec types without PVL describe hospital-acquired MRSA, seen with catheters, surgery, and long stays in health care settings."],"study":{"topics":["Community versus hospital MRSA","Panton-Valentine leukocidin"],"comps":["IN-13-H8","IN-12"]}},{"id":"IN-C22-Q3","stem":"He asks why the cephalexin he was first given did not work. Which of the following best explains the resistance of his isolate to both oxacillin and cephalexin?","options":["Secreted beta-lactamase that opens the beta-lactam ring","Altered penicillin-binding protein with low affinity","Peptidoglycan precursors ending in D-Ala-D-Lac","Membrane efflux pump that exports beta-lactams"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-12","obj":"IN-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note that oxacillin is built to resist staphylococcal beta-lactamase, so enzyme breakdown cannot explain resistance to it.","Recall the mecA product: PBP2a, a transpeptidase that beta-lactams bind poorly.","Apply it: PBP2a keeps building the cell wall, so nearly all beta-lactams fail."],"why":"Beta-lactam drugs kill bacteria by binding penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan. The mecA gene of MRSA encodes PBP2a, which has a low affinity for beta-lactams and keeps cross-linking the wall when the native PBPs are blocked. This makes MRSA resistant to penicillinase-resistant penicillins and to most cephalosporins, including cephalexin. S. aureus itself is a gram-positive coccus in clusters that is catalase and coagulase positive, and protein A on its surface binds the Fc portion of IgG.","whyNot":["Most S. aureus strains make penicillinase, which explains penicillin resistance. Oxacillin and related drugs were designed to withstand this enzyme.","Correct.","Changing the D-Ala-D-Ala terminus to D-Ala-D-Lac is the mechanism of vancomycin resistance in enterococci (vanA). It does not affect beta-lactams.","Efflux pumps cause resistance to tetracyclines and macrolides. They are not the basis of methicillin resistance."],"study":{"topics":["mecA and PBP2a","Beta-lactam mechanism of action"],"comps":["IN-12","IN-13-H8"]}},{"id":"IN-C22-Q4","stem":"Because of his recurrent abscesses, an inherited phagocyte defect is considered. Deficiency of which of the following enzymes would most strongly predispose a patient to recurrent abscesses with catalase-positive organisms such as this one?","options":["Myeloperoxidase","Adenosine deaminase","Superoxide dismutase","NADPH oxidase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-6","obj":"IN-6.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how neutrophils kill: NADPH oxidase makes superoxide, which becomes hydrogen peroxide, and myeloperoxidase turns it into hypochlorous acid.","Note that catalase-positive bacteria destroy their own hydrogen peroxide.","So a phagocyte that cannot make superoxide has no oxidant to use against them: chronic granulomatous disease from NADPH oxidase deficiency."],"why":"The respiratory burst begins when NADPH oxidase in the phagosome membrane transfers electrons from NADPH to oxygen, making superoxide. Superoxide dismutase converts it to hydrogen peroxide, and myeloperoxidase combines hydrogen peroxide with chloride to make bleach (hypochlorous acid). In chronic granulomatous disease NADPH oxidase is missing. Catalase-negative bacteria leak their own hydrogen peroxide into the phagosome, which the cell can still use, but catalase-positive organisms such as S. aureus, Serratia, Burkholderia, Nocardia, and Aspergillus break it down. The result is recurrent abscesses and granulomas. The dihydrorhodamine flow test or nitroblue tetrazolium test screens for it.","whyNot":["Myeloperoxidase deficiency is common and usually silent, with occasional Candida infections. Neutrophils still make hydrogen peroxide and kill most bacteria.","Adenosine deaminase deficiency causes severe combined immunodeficiency, with lymphocyte loss and infections of every type from infancy.","Superoxide dismutase detoxifies superoxide. Inherited loss of SOD1 is linked to familial ALS, not to phagocyte killing defects.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Respiratory burst","Chronic granulomatous disease"],"comps":["IN-6","IN-12"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C23","system":"IN","dx":"Nonbullous impetigo followed by poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","codes":["IN-13-H7"],"case":"A 5-year-old girl is brought to the pediatric clinic because of dark, cola-colored urine and puffy eyes for 2 days. Three weeks ago she had itchy sores around her nose and mouth that formed honey-colored crusts. A culture at that time grew beta-hemolytic, catalase-negative, bacitracin-sensitive gram-positive cocci in chains. Her temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 128/84 mm Hg (above the 95th percentile for her age). Physical examination shows periorbital edema and healed facial skin without scars. Urinalysis shows red blood cells, red blood cell casts, and 1+ protein. Serum C3 concentration is 42 mg/dL (90 to 180). Diagnosis: nonbullous impetigo caused by Streptococcus pyogenes, followed by poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C23-Q1","stem":"Her 3-month-old brother develops several large, flaccid bullae on the trunk that rupture and leave shiny erosions with thin brown crusts. A swab grows a coagulase-positive organism. Which of the following mechanisms most likely produces his blisters?","options":["Exfoliative toxin cleaving desmoglein 1","Streptococcal hyaluronidase digesting dermal matrix","Autoantibody binding to desmoglein 3","Autoantibody binding to collagen XVII"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-H7","obj":"IN-13-H.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize flaccid bullae in an infant with a coagulase-positive organism as bullous impetigo from S. aureus.","Recall that staphylococcal exfoliative toxins are proteases that cut desmoglein 1.","Desmoglein 1 holds keratinocytes together in the upper epidermis, so the split is in the granular layer."],"why":"Impetigo is a superficial infection of the epidermis in young children. The nonbullous form, with honey-colored crusts, is caused by S. aureus or S. pyogenes. The bullous form is caused by S. aureus strains that make exfoliative toxins. These serine proteases cleave desmoglein 1, so keratinocytes separate in the stratum granulosum and form fragile, flaccid bullae. When the toxin spreads through the blood, the same mechanism produces staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome. Pemphigus foliaceus targets the same protein with autoantibodies.","whyNot":["Correct.","Hyaluronidase helps S. pyogenes spread through the dermis in cellulitis. It does not split the epidermis to form bullae.","Autoantibodies to desmoglein 3 cause pemphigus vulgaris, an adult disease with a suprabasal split and mouth erosions.","Autoantibodies to collagen XVII (BP180) cause bullous pemphigoid, with tense subepidermal bullae in older adults."],"study":{"topics":["Bullous versus nonbullous impetigo","Desmogleins and blistering diseases"],"comps":["IN-13-H7","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C23-Q2","stem":"Her antistreptolysin O titer is within the reference range, while her anti-DNase B titer is markedly increased. Which of the following best explains the normal antistreptolysin O titer?","options":["Streptolysin O is poorly immunogenic in young children","Skin strains of the organism lack the streptolysin O gene","Skin lipid cholesterol inactivates streptolysin O","Antibody to DNase B neutralizes streptolysin O in serum"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-12","obj":"IN-12.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the organism from the culture: S. pyogenes (group A strep), beta-hemolytic, catalase negative, bacitracin sensitive.","Recall that streptolysin O is a cholesterol-binding, oxygen-labile hemolysin.","Apply it: skin lipids bind and inactivate the toxin in impetigo, so little antibody forms. Anti-DNase B is the better marker after skin infection."],"why":"Streptolysin O is a pore-forming toxin that binds cholesterol in host membranes. In skin infection, cholesterol in skin lipids binds and inactivates it, so the ASO response is weak. Anti-DNase B rises well after both skin and throat infection. Other group A strep factors are M protein (blocks complement and phagocytosis), the hyaluronic acid capsule, streptokinase, and pyrogenic exotoxins. Nephritogenic strains cause glomerulonephritis after either throat or skin infection. Rheumatic fever follows only pharyngitis, which is why it is not expected after impetigo.","whyNot":["Children mount a strong ASO response after streptococcal pharyngitis, so the toxin is clearly immunogenic at her age.","All group A streptococci make streptolysin O. Skin strains differ in their M protein types, not in this gene.","Correct.","Antibodies to DNase B and to streptolysin O target different proteins and do not cross-react."],"study":{"topics":["Group A streptococcal virulence factors","ASO versus anti-DNase B"],"comps":["IN-12","IN-13-H7"]}},{"id":"IN-C23-Q3","stem":"Her facial lesions healed without scarring. In which of the following tissue levels did the bacteria most likely proliferate?","options":["Hair follicle extending into the deep dermis","Subcutaneous tissue and deep fascia","Upper dermis and superficial lymphatics","Epidermis just below the stratum corneum"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-9","obj":"IN-9.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify honey-colored crusts in a child as nonbullous impetigo.","Recall that skin infections are named by the depth they reach.","Impetigo stays within the epidermis, which regenerates from the basal layer without a scar."],"why":"Skin infections follow a sequence: entry through a small break, adherence, colonization, invasion, and spread. The depth reached names the disease. Impetigo stays in the superficial epidermis, under the stratum corneum. Folliculitis involves the hair follicle. Erysipelas involves the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, with a raised, sharp border. Cellulitis reaches the deep dermis and subcutis, an abscess is a walled-off pus collection, and necrotizing fasciitis spreads along the fascia. Because impetigo spares the dermis, it heals without scarring.","whyNot":["Infection centered on a hair follicle and reaching the deep dermis is a furuncle.","Infection along the deep fascia is necrotizing fasciitis, with pain out of proportion to exam and rapid spread.","Infection of the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics is erysipelas, which has a raised, sharply demarcated border.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Depth of skin infections","Impetigo"],"comps":["IN-9","IN-13-H7"]}},{"id":"IN-C23-Q4","stem":"Healing of her facial erosions began as the deepest keratinocytes divided to restore the epidermis. Which of the following keratin pairs is characteristic of those dividing cells?","options":["Keratins 5 and 14","Keratins 4 and 13","Keratins 8 and 18","Keratins 1 and 10"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-1","obj":"IN-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the layer that renews the epidermis: the stratum basale.","Recall the keratin switch: basal cells make keratins 5 and 14, and cells switch to keratins 1 and 10 as they move into the stratum spinosum.","Match the other pairs to their tissues to exclude them."],"why":"The stratum basale holds the dividing stem cells of the epidermis, and they express keratins 5 and 14. As cells move up into the stratum spinosum they switch to keratins 1 and 10. In the stratum granulosum they gain keratohyalin granules and lamellar bodies, which release lipids that seal the skin. In the stratum corneum they lose their nuclei and become flat, dead squames. The stratum lucidum appears only in the thick skin of the palms and soles. Mutations in keratins 5 or 14 cause epidermolysis bullosa simplex, with blisters through the basal layer.","whyNot":["Correct.","Keratins 4 and 13 mark the suprabasal cells of nonkeratinized stratified epithelia such as the oral mucosa and esophagus.","Keratins 8 and 18 are found in simple epithelia such as hepatocytes and glandular cells, not in the epidermis.","Keratins 1 and 10 are made by suprabasal cells in the stratum spinosum and above, which no longer divide."],"study":{"topics":["Epidermal layers","Keratin pairs"],"comps":["IN-1"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C24","system":"IN","dx":"Anogenital warts (condyloma acuminatum)","codes":["IN-13-H11"],"case":"A 24-year-old man comes to the sexual health clinic because of painless bumps on his penis and around his anus for 3 months. He has had three male partners in the past year and uses condoms inconsistently. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows multiple soft, flesh-colored, cauliflower-like papules 2 to 6 mm in diameter on the penile shaft and perianal skin. A biopsy specimen shows papillomatosis, acanthosis, and hyperkeratosis, with keratinocytes in the upper layers that have wrinkled nuclei surrounded by clear halos. Polymerase chain reaction detects human papillomavirus (HPV) type 6. An HIV test is negative. Diagnosis: anogenital warts (condyloma acuminatum).","questions":[{"id":"IN-C24-Q1","stem":"His roommate received a kidney transplant 2 years ago and takes tacrolimus and mycophenolate. The roommate has dozens of common warts on both hands that keep recurring. Which of the following best explains the roommate's extensive warts?","options":["Suppressed T cell-mediated immunity","Reduced neutrophil oxidative burst","Impaired membrane attack complex formation","Low IgA levels at mucosal surfaces"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-H11","obj":"IN-13-H.11","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the drugs: tacrolimus blocks calcineurin and IL-2 production, and mycophenolate blocks lymphocyte proliferation.","Recall that clearance of HPV-infected keratinocytes depends on cell-mediated immunity.","Connect them: T cell suppression lets warts spread and persist."],"why":"Warts are benign HPV-driven proliferations of keratinocytes. Common warts are caused mainly by HPV 2, plantar warts by HPV 1, flat warts by HPV 3 and 10, and anogenital warts by HPV 6 and 11. Histology shows papillomatosis, hyperkeratosis, and koilocytes, which are keratinocytes with wrinkled nuclei and a clear halo. Most warts regress when T cells recognize infected cells. Patients with weak T cell immunity, such as transplant recipients and people with untreated HIV, develop numerous, persistent warts.","whyNot":["Correct.","A defective oxidative burst (chronic granulomatous disease) causes abscesses with catalase-positive bacteria and fungi.","Defects in C5 to C9 predispose to recurrent Neisseria infections.","IgA deficiency causes recurrent sinopulmonary and gastrointestinal infections such as Giardia. It does not cause widespread warts."],"study":{"topics":["Types of warts and HPV","Warts in T cell immunodeficiency"],"comps":["IN-13-H11","IN-11"]}},{"id":"IN-C24-Q2","stem":"He asks whether vaccination would have prevented his warts. The 9-valent HPV vaccine includes types 6 and 11. By which of the following mechanisms does this vaccine mainly protect?","options":["Cytotoxic T cells killing E6- and E7-expressing cells","Neutralizing antibody against L1 capsid protein","Replication of attenuated virus in basal keratinocytes","Interferon induction by viral DNA within the particle"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-11","obj":"IN-11.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recall what the vaccine contains: recombinant L1 protein that self-assembles into virus-like particles.","These particles carry no viral DNA, so they cannot infect or replicate.","A protein antigen given this way mainly induces neutralizing antibody that blocks infection of basal cells."],"why":"The HPV vaccine is a subunit vaccine. The L1 major capsid protein is made in yeast and folds into empty shells called virus-like particles. They look like the virus to B cells, so they induce high levels of neutralizing IgG that blocks the virus before it reaches basal keratinocytes. Because there is no viral DNA, there is no E6 or E7 antigen and no replication, so the vaccine prevents new infection but does not treat existing warts. Live attenuated vaccines such as varicella replicate and induce both antibody and cell-mediated immunity.","whyNot":["E6 and E7 are not in the vaccine, so it does not induce T cells against infected cells. This is why it does not clear existing infection.","Correct.","Replication of an attenuated virus describes live vaccines such as varicella and MMR. The HPV vaccine contains no live virus.","Virus-like particles contain no viral DNA. Protection comes from antibody, not interferon."],"study":{"topics":["HPV virus-like particle vaccine","Live versus subunit vaccines"],"comps":["IN-11","IN-13-H11"]}},{"id":"IN-C24-Q3","stem":"A flat lesion on his anal margin shows high-grade dysplasia, and polymerase chain reaction detects HPV 16. Which of the following best explains how this viral type drives infected cells past the G1 to S checkpoint?","options":["L1 protein activating TP53 transcription","E1 protein inducing p21 expression","E6 protein increasing p53 stability","E7 protein binding and inactivating Rb"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-7","obj":"IN-7.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the G1 to S checkpoint: Rb holds E2F until cyclin D and CDK4/6 phosphorylate it.","Recall the high-risk HPV oncoproteins: E7 binds Rb, and E6 targets p53 for degradation.","Apply it: E7 releases E2F and pushes the cell into S phase."],"why":"Cell entry into S phase is gated by Rb. Unphosphorylated Rb binds E2F and blocks transcription of S-phase genes. Cyclin D with CDK4/6, then cyclin E with CDK2, phosphorylate Rb and release E2F. p53 senses DNA damage and induces p21, a CDK inhibitor that stops this process, or triggers apoptosis. High-risk HPV types (16, 18) make an E7 protein that binds Rb tightly and frees E2F, and an E6 protein that sends p53 to the proteasome. Low-risk types 6 and 11 make weaker versions, so they cause warts but rarely cancer.","whyNot":["L1 is the capsid protein. It forms the virion shell and has no role in cell cycle control.","E1 is the viral helicase that copies the viral genome. It does not control the host G1 to S checkpoint.","E6 of high-risk HPV does the opposite. It recruits a ubiquitin ligase that degrades p53.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Rb, E2F, and the G1 to S checkpoint","HPV E6 and E7"],"comps":["IN-7","IN-13-H11"]}},{"id":"IN-C24-Q4","stem":"Morphometric analysis of his penile wart shows an epidermis about three times normal thickness, with more cell layers than normal, keratinocytes of normal size, and orderly maturation toward the surface. Which of the following best describes this change?","options":["Dysplasia with loss of orderly maturation","Hyperplasia from virus-driven keratinocyte division","Metaplasia from reprogramming of basal stem cells","Hypertrophy from enlargement of individual keratinocytes"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-8","obj":"IN-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Count, then size: more cell layers with normal-sized cells means more cells.","More cells is hyperplasia, which needs cells that can divide.","Orderly maturation excludes dysplasia, and an unchanged cell type excludes metaplasia."],"why":"Hyperplasia is an increase in cell number. Keratinocytes can divide, so the epidermis responds to growth signals by adding layers. Here the signal is HPV, whose proteins push basal and suprabasal cells to keep dividing. Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size, typical of cells that cannot divide, such as cardiac and skeletal muscle. A callus is physiologic epidermal hyperplasia from friction. Metaplasia replaces one adult cell type with another, such as squamous metaplasia of the bronchi in smokers. Pathologic hyperplasia and metaplasia can progress to dysplasia, as in the anal lesion driven by HPV 16.","whyNot":["Dysplasia shows disordered maturation and atypical nuclei, which this lesion lacks.","Correct.","Metaplasia changes the type of epithelium. The epidermis here is still keratinizing squamous epithelium.","Hypertrophy would show larger cells, not more layers of normal-sized cells."],"study":{"topics":["Cellular adaptations","Hyperplasia versus dysplasia"],"comps":["IN-8","IN-13-H11"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C25","system":"IN","dx":"Pasteurella multocida wound infection after a cat bite","codes":["IN-13-H12"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman comes to urgent care because of pain and swelling of her left hand that began 12 hours after her cat bit her. Her temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows two puncture wounds on the dorsum of the hand with 4 cm of surrounding erythema, warmth, tenderness, and serous drainage. Range of motion of the fingers is full. Culture of the drainage grows small gram-negative coccobacilli that are oxidase positive. Diagnosis: Pasteurella multocida wound infection after a cat bite.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C25-Q1","stem":"Her brother, who had a splenectomy after a car crash, is bitten on the forearm by his dog. Two days later he develops fever, hypotension, purpura, and disseminated intravascular coagulation. Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause?","options":["Eikenella corrodens","Vibrio vulnificus","Capnocytophaga canimorsus","Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-13-H12","obj":"IN-13-H.13","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the exposure: a dog bite.","Identify the host factor: asplenia, which removes the main defense against bloodstream bacteria.","Match both: Capnocytophaga canimorsus, a dog mouth organism, causes fulminant sepsis in asplenic patients."],"why":"The source of a wound points to the organism. Cat and dog bites carry Pasteurella multocida, which causes fast cellulitis within a day. Capnocytophaga canimorsus, also from dog saliva, causes overwhelming sepsis with purpura and DIC in people without a spleen or with alcohol use disorder. Human bites and clenched-fist injuries carry Eikenella corrodens. Salt water and raw shellfish carry Vibrio vulnificus, fish tanks carry Mycobacterium marinum, and rose thorns carry Sporothrix schenckii.","whyNot":["Eikenella corrodens comes from the human mouth and infects human bites and clenched-fist injuries.","Vibrio vulnificus causes bullous cellulitis and sepsis after salt water exposure or raw oysters, especially in liver disease.","Correct.","Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae causes a violet cellulitis of the hands in people who handle fish, shellfish, or raw meat."],"study":{"topics":["Organisms by wound exposure","Infections in asplenia"],"comps":["IN-13-H12","IN-9"]}},{"id":"IN-C25-Q2","stem":"A clean laceration on her forearm from the same incident was sutured. A biopsy specimen of that laceration taken on day 4 would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Platelets with no inflammatory cells","Neutrophils with a fibrin clot only","Myofibroblasts with mature type I collagen","Macrophages with early granulation tissue"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-10","obj":"IN-10.1","dok":2,"approach":["Lay out the timeline: hemostasis in minutes, neutrophils in the first 1 to 2 days, macrophages by about day 3.","Granulation tissue (new capillaries and fibroblasts) begins to form around days 3 to 5.","Place day 4 in that timeline."],"why":"Wound healing proceeds in overlapping phases. Hemostasis forms a platelet and fibrin plug within minutes. Neutrophils arrive within 24 hours, and macrophages replace them by about day 3. Macrophages clear debris and release growth factors such as TGF-beta and VEGF, which start the proliferative phase: granulation tissue with new capillaries, fibroblasts, and type III collagen. Over weeks to months, remodeling replaces type III collagen with type I through zinc-dependent metalloproteinases. A scar regains only about 70 to 80 percent of the strength of normal skin.","whyNot":["A platelet plug without inflammation describes the first minutes after injury.","A neutrophil-rich wound with only fibrin describes the first 1 to 2 days.","Mature type I collagen belongs to the remodeling phase, weeks to months later.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of wound healing"],"comps":["IN-10"]}},{"id":"IN-C25-Q3","stem":"Her wound heals slowly. She reports eating no fruits or vegetables for several months. Examination shows swollen, bleeding gums and perifollicular hemorrhages with corkscrew hairs. Which of the following steps of collagen synthesis is most directly impaired?","options":["Translation of pro-alpha chains on ribosomes","Hydroxylation of proline residues in the ER","Cleavage of procollagen peptides outside the cell","Lysyl oxidase cross-linking of fibrils outside the cell"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-5","obj":"IN-5.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhages, and corkscrew hairs as scurvy from vitamin C deficiency.","Recall that prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases need vitamin C to keep their iron in the ferrous (Fe2+) state.","Without hydroxyproline the triple helix is unstable, so collagen is weak and wounds heal poorly."],"why":"Collagen synthesis begins with translation of pro-alpha chains rich in glycine, proline, and lysine. In the rough ER, prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases add hydroxyl groups, which needs vitamin C and Fe2+. Hydroxylysines are then glycosylated, and three chains form a triple helix held by hydrogen bonds from hydroxyproline. Outside the cell, procollagen peptidases cut off the end propeptides, and copper-dependent lysyl oxidase cross-links the fibrils. In scurvy the helix is unstable, so vessels are fragile and wounds lack strong collagen.","whyNot":["Translation proceeds normally in scurvy. The defect is a modification made after translation.","Correct.","Failure of procollagen peptidase causes the dermatosparaxis type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","Lysyl oxidase needs copper. Its failure is seen in Menkes disease, with kinky hair and weak vessels."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen synthesis steps","Scurvy"],"comps":["IN-5","IN-10"]}},{"id":"IN-C25-Q4","stem":"Two days later an abscess forms at one puncture site. It is incised, drained, and left open to heal from its base. Compared with healing of her sutured laceration, which of the following is most characteristic of healing of this wound?","options":["Less granulation tissue and less inflammation","Faster re-epithelialization across a narrow gap","Wound contraction by myofibroblasts","Greater final tensile strength than normal skin"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-10","obj":"IN-10.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the healing mode: an open wound with separated edges heals by secondary intention.","Recall the features: more inflammation, more granulation tissue, and contraction.","Name the cell that pulls the edges together: the myofibroblast."],"why":"Healing by primary intention closes a clean incision whose edges are brought together, with little granulation tissue and a thin scar. Healing by secondary intention fills a larger, open defect. It needs more inflammation and granulation tissue, and myofibroblasts, fibroblasts with smooth muscle actin, contract the wound and can shrink it a great deal. Infection, diabetes, ischemia, glucocorticoids, malnutrition, vitamin C or zinc deficiency, and foreign bodies all delay healing, and several apply to this wound.","whyNot":["Less granulation tissue and inflammation describe primary intention healing of a sutured wound.","Fast re-epithelialization across a narrow gap describes a sutured incision, not an open wound.","Correct.","No scar is stronger than normal skin. Scars reach only about 70 to 80 percent of normal strength."],"study":{"topics":["Primary versus secondary intention","Factors that delay healing"],"comps":["IN-10"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C26","system":"IN","dx":"Compound nevocellular nevus","codes":["IN-13-A1"],"case":"A 26-year-old man comes to the physician for a skin examination. He has a mole on his upper back that he first noticed as a flat brown spot during adolescence and that has become slightly raised. He reports no itching, bleeding, or recent change. Physical examination shows a 4-mm, round, symmetric, uniformly tan-brown, slightly raised papule with a regular border. It is excised for cosmetic reasons. Microscopic examination shows nests of uniform nevus cells at the dermal-epidermal junction and within the dermis. Cells deeper in the dermis are smaller, contain less pigment, and are arranged in cords. Diagnosis: compound nevocellular nevus.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C26-Q1","stem":"When this lesion first appeared as a flat brown spot in adolescence, which of the following histologic patterns would it most likely have shown?","options":["Single atypical cells scattered through all epidermal layers","Nests within the dermis with no junctional component","Dendritic pigmented cells deep in the reticular dermis","Nests confined to the dermal-epidermal junction"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-A1","obj":"IN-13-A.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that nevi evolve with age as nests move from the junction into the dermis.","Link shape to location: a flat macule has nests only at the junction, and a raised papule has dermal nests.","So the early, flat lesion was a junctional nevus."],"why":"Nevocellular nevi begin when a melanocyte acquires an activating BRAF or NRAS mutation, proliferates, and then stops growing through oncogene-induced senescence. Early nevi are junctional: nests sit at the dermal-epidermal junction, and the lesion is flat. Over time nests migrate into the dermis, producing a raised compound nevus with both components. Later the junctional component disappears, leaving an intradermal nevus, often a flesh-colored papule in older adults. Cells that descend deeper become smaller and less pigmented, a benign feature called maturation.","whyNot":["Single atypical cells spreading upward through the epidermis (pagetoid spread) suggest melanoma in situ.","A purely dermal nevus is an intradermal nevus. It is the late stage, usually a raised, lightly pigmented papule in older adults.","Dendritic pigmented melanocytes deep in the dermis describe a blue nevus, which looks blue-black and does not start as a junctional lesion.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Junctional, compound, and intradermal nevi","Oncogene-induced senescence"],"comps":["IN-13-A1","IN-2"]}},{"id":"IN-C26-Q2","stem":"Several other pigmented lesions are present on his skin. Which of the following lesions is most concerning for melanoma?","options":["5-mm round, evenly tan papule, unchanged for 10 years","4-mm dome-shaped, flesh-colored papule that has softened","3-mm symmetric dark brown macule with a regular border","8-mm macule, notched border, black and pink areas"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-13-A1","obj":"IN-13-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Apply the ABCDE criteria: asymmetry, border irregularity, color variation, diameter above 6 mm, and evolution.","Check each lesion against them.","Only the 8-mm lesion has an irregular border, several colors, and a large diameter."],"why":"Benign nevi are usually small (under 6 mm), symmetric, evenly colored, sharply bordered, and stable over time. Melanoma is suggested by asymmetry, irregular or notched borders, multiple colors (black, blue, red, pink, or white areas), diameter above 6 mm, and change in size, shape, or color. Evolution is the most important single sign. Large congenital melanocytic nevi, especially those above 20 cm, carry an increased risk of melanoma.","whyNot":["A small, evenly colored lesion that has been stable for years has the features of a benign nevus.","A soft, flesh-colored, dome-shaped papule is typical of an older intradermal nevus that has lost its pigment.","A small, symmetric, evenly dark lesion with a regular border meets no ABCDE criteria.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["ABCDE criteria","Nevus versus melanoma"],"comps":["IN-13-A1"]}},{"id":"IN-C26-Q3","stem":"His newborn daughter has a 7-cm blue-gray, flat patch with ill-defined borders over the lumbosacral area. Which of the following best explains this lesion?","options":["Melanocytes arrested in the dermis during migration","Proliferating capillary endothelial cells in the dermis","Nests of melanocytes at the dermal-epidermal junction","Dilated mature capillaries from a GNAQ mutation"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-2","obj":"IN-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize a blue-gray, flat lumbosacral patch in a newborn as dermal melanocytosis (Mongolian spot).","Recall that melanocytes come from neural crest and migrate through the dermis to the epidermis.","Cells that stop in the dermis look blue because deep pigment scatters short-wavelength light (the Tyndall effect)."],"why":"Melanocytes arise from the neural crest and migrate through the dermis to reach the basal layer of the epidermis. When some stop in the deep dermis, their melanin appears blue-gray through the overlying skin. This is dermal melanocytosis, most common over the lumbosacral area and in infants with darker skin, and it usually fades in childhood. Congenital melanocytic nevi are brown and often raised, infantile hemangiomas are bright red and grow after birth, and port-wine stains are flat red-purple patches that persist.","whyNot":["Correct.","Proliferating endothelial cells form an infantile hemangioma, a bright red lesion that appears in the first weeks and then regresses.","Junctional nests make a brown lesion, as in a congenital or acquired nevus, not a blue-gray one.","A GNAQ mutation causes a port-wine stain, a red to purple patch of dilated capillaries that persists for life."],"study":{"topics":["Melanocyte migration from neural crest","Pigmented and vascular birthmarks"],"comps":["IN-2","IN-13-A1"]}},{"id":"IN-C26-Q4","stem":"Molecular testing of his excised nevus shows an activating BRAF V600E mutation, the same mutation found in many melanomas. Which of the following additional changes would most likely be needed for a cell in this nevus to become melanoma?","options":["Loss of p16 with telomerase reactivation","Loss of VEGF secretion by nevus cells","Overexpression of wild-type retinoblastoma protein","Increased expression of p16 (CDKN2A)"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-8","obj":"IN-8.8","dok":3,"approach":["Recall why nevi stop growing: oncogenic BRAF triggers senescence through p16 and Rb.","Ask what a cell needs to escape: loss of the senescence brake and a way to keep dividing without shortening telomeres.","Pick the change that removes p16 and restores telomerase."],"why":"Cancer requires several hallmarks: sustained growth signaling (here BRAF), loss of tumor suppressors such as RB and TP53 by two hits, evasion of apoptosis, limitless replication through telomerase, angiogenesis driven by VEGF, invasion, a shift to glycolysis (Warburg effect), and immune evasion. In a nevus, BRAF signaling alone induces p16, which keeps Rb active and halts the cell cycle. Melanoma commonly loses CDKN2A (p16) and reactivates telomerase, often through TERT promoter mutations, which lets the BRAF-driven cell keep dividing.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tumors need VEGF-driven angiogenesis to grow beyond a small size. Losing VEGF would slow progression.","More functional Rb would strengthen the G1 arrest that keeps the nevus benign.","p16 is the brake that enforces senescence. More of it would keep the nevus from growing."],"study":{"topics":["Hallmarks of cancer","BRAF and progression to melanoma"],"comps":["IN-8","IN-13-A1","IN-7"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C27","system":"IN","dx":"Exertional heat stroke","codes":["IN-4"],"case":"A 21-year-old military recruit is brought to the field medical station after collapsing during a 10-km march in hot, humid weather. He is confused and combative. His rectal temperature is 41.6 °C (106.9 °F), pulse is 148/min, respirations are 30/min, and blood pressure is 92/54 mm Hg. His skin is hot and wet with sweat. Laboratory studies show creatine kinase 48,000 U/L (about 25 to 200), potassium 5.9 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), creatinine 2.4 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.2), and platelet count 62,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000). Diagnosis: exertional heat stroke with rhabdomyolysis.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C27-Q1","stem":"A second recruit on the same march is dizzy and nauseated, with a rectal temperature of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F), heavy sweating, tachycardia, and normal mental status. Which of the following findings best distinguishes the first recruit's condition from that of the second recruit?","options":["Tachycardia from reduced effective circulating blood volume","Cutaneous vasodilation diverting cardiac output to the skin","CNS dysfunction with core temperature over 40 °C (104 °F)","Profuse sweating driven by sympathetic cholinergic fibers"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-4","obj":"IN-4.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the second recruit's picture as heat exhaustion: heat stress with intact mental status.","Recall the definition of heat stroke: core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) with central nervous system dysfunction.","Find the feature only the first recruit has."],"why":"Heat exhaustion and heat stroke both come from heat stress. In heat exhaustion, sweating and skin vasodilation are working, and volume depletion causes dizziness, nausea, and tachycardia, but the brain functions normally. Heat stroke is failure of thermoregulation, with core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) and central nervous system dysfunction such as confusion, seizures, or coma. Exertional heat stroke strikes healthy young people during heavy exercise, and they often still sweat. Classic heat stroke strikes older or chronically ill people during heat waves, often with dry skin. Exertional cases often bring rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury, and DIC, as here.","whyNot":["Both recruits are tachycardic from volume loss and heat. This finding is shared.","Skin vasodilation is the normal heat-loss response in both conditions.","Correct.","Both recruits sweat. Sweating continues in exertional heat stroke, so it does not separate the two."],"study":{"topics":["Heat exhaustion versus heat stroke","Classic versus exertional heat stroke"],"comps":["IN-4","IN-8"]}},{"id":"IN-C27-Q2","stem":"Because of the severe rhabdomyolysis, an inherited muscle disorder is considered. His father once developed masseter rigidity, rising end-tidal CO2, and a temperature of 40.5 °C (104.9 °F) shortly after receiving sevoflurane and succinylcholine. Which of the following best describes the defect in the father's condition?","options":["Absent dystrophin anchoring actin to the sarcolemma","Unregulated calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum","Chloride channel loss causing delayed muscle relaxation","Impaired long-chain fatty acid transport into mitochondria"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-4","obj":"IN-4.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize rigidity, rising CO2, and fever after a volatile anesthetic and succinylcholine as malignant hyperthermia.","Recall the gene: RYR1, the ryanodine receptor calcium release channel of skeletal muscle.","Connect it: uncontrolled calcium release drives sustained contraction, ATP use, CO2 production, and heat."],"why":"Malignant hyperthermia is an autosomal dominant disorder, most often from RYR1 mutations. The mutant ryanodine receptor opens too easily when exposed to volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine, so calcium floods out of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Muscles contract continuously, burn ATP, and generate CO2, lactic acid, and heat. Rigidity, hypercarbia, tachycardia, rising temperature, hyperkalemia, and rhabdomyolysis follow. Dantrolene treats it by blocking the ryanodine receptor. Some RYR1 variants are also linked to exertional heat illness and rhabdomyolysis.","whyNot":["Absent dystrophin causes Duchenne muscular dystrophy. These boys can have severe reactions to anesthetics, but the mechanism is membrane fragility, not calcium channel failure.","Correct.","Loss of the muscle chloride channel causes myotonia congenita, with stiffness and slow relaxation after voluntary contraction.","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency causes rhabdomyolysis with prolonged exercise or fasting, not a reaction to anesthesia."],"study":{"topics":["Malignant hyperthermia and RYR1","Excitation-contraction coupling"],"comps":["IN-4","MS-7"]}},{"id":"IN-C27-Q3","stem":"He dies of multiorgan failure 3 days later. Electron microscopy of renal tubular cells is performed at autopsy. Which of the following findings indicates irreversible injury?","options":["Blebbing of the plasma membrane","Swelling with dilated endoplasmic reticulum","Amorphous densities within mitochondria","Ribosomes detached from rough endoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-8","obj":"IN-8.2","dok":2,"approach":["Sort the findings into reversible and irreversible changes.","Reversible changes come from ATP loss: swelling, blebs, ribosome detachment, and fatty change.","Irreversible changes mark mitochondrial and membrane failure: amorphous mitochondrial densities, membrane rupture, and nuclear breakdown."],"why":"Cell injury starts with ATP depletion, calcium influx, oxidative stress, and membrane damage. Early on, failure of the Na+/K+-ATPase lets water in, so cells swell, the ER dilates, blebs form, and ribosomes detach. These changes reverse if the insult stops. Injury becomes irreversible when mitochondria cannot recover (large amorphous densities from calcium and protein), lysosomal and plasma membranes rupture, and the nucleus shrinks (pyknosis), fragments (karyorrhexis), or fades (karyolysis).","whyNot":["Plasma membrane blebs form early as the cytoskeleton detaches. They are reversible.","Swelling and ER dilation are the first and most common signs of reversible injury.","Correct.","Ribosome detachment reduces protein synthesis and is reversible."],"study":{"topics":["Reversible versus irreversible cell injury"],"comps":["IN-8","IN-3"]}},{"id":"IN-C27-Q4","stem":"In his overheated, poorly perfused skeletal muscle, ATP stores fall. Which of the following changes in the muscle cells most likely results from reduced Na+/K+-ATPase activity?","options":["Decreased intracellular calcium with relaxation","Increased intracellular potassium with hyperpolarization","Decreased intracellular sodium with cell shrinkage","Increased intracellular sodium with cell swelling"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-3","obj":"IN-3.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the Na+/K+-ATPase pumps 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in for each ATP.","When ATP falls, sodium accumulates inside and potassium leaks out.","Water follows the sodium, so cells swell and the membrane depolarizes."],"why":"The Na+/K+-ATPase keeps intracellular sodium low and potassium high. Because it moves three positive charges out for every two in, it also adds a little directly to the negative resting potential. When ATP is depleted, sodium builds up inside, water follows by osmosis, and the cell swells. This is the earliest change of hypoxic cell injury. Potassium leaks out of the cells, and with muscle breakdown this helps explain his serum potassium of 5.9 mEq/L. The membrane depolarizes rather than hyperpolarizes.","whyNot":["Intracellular calcium rises in ATP depletion because calcium pumps also fail. This activates damaging enzymes.","Potassium leaves the cell when the pump fails, and the membrane depolarizes.","Pump failure raises intracellular sodium. Cells swell rather than shrink.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Na+/K+-ATPase","Cell swelling in hypoxic injury"],"comps":["IN-3","IN-8"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C28","system":"IN","dx":"Partial- and full-thickness thermal burns from a house fire","codes":["IN-10"],"case":"A 45-year-old woman is admitted to the burn unit after being rescued from a house fire. She weighs 70 kg. Her temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg. The entire right upper limb is red, moist, and blistered; it blanches with pressure and is very painful to touch. The entire front of the trunk is dry, white, and leathery; it does not blanch and is insensate to pinprick. Her nasal hairs are singed, and she coughs up carbonaceous sputum. Diagnosis: partial-thickness and full-thickness thermal burns with inhalation injury.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C28-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the depth of her trunk burn and the total body surface area burned, estimated by the rule of nines?","options":["Full-thickness, 18 percent","Full-thickness, 27 percent","Superficial partial-thickness, 18 percent","Deep partial-thickness, 18 percent"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"IN-10","obj":"IN-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Classify depth: dry, white, leathery, insensate skin that does not blanch is full-thickness.","Apply the rule of nines: each upper limb is 9 percent, and the front of the trunk is 18 percent.","Add every partial- and full-thickness area: 9 plus 18 is 27 percent."],"why":"Burn depth is judged at the bedside. Superficial burns (epidermis only) are red and painful without blisters. Superficial partial-thickness burns reach the papillary dermis and are moist, blistered, blanching, and very painful, like her arm. Deep partial-thickness burns reach the reticular dermis and are less painful and blanch poorly. Full-thickness burns destroy the whole dermis with its nerves and vessels, so they are white or leathery, insensate, and nonblanching. In adults the rule of nines gives 9 percent to the head, 9 percent to each upper limb, 18 percent each to the front and back of the trunk, and 18 percent to each lower limb. Superficial burns are not counted. Large burns cause hypovolemia, a hypermetabolic state, infection with Pseudomonas, and later contracture.","whyNot":["The depth is right, but 18 percent counts only the front of the trunk and leaves out the arm.","Correct.","Moist, blistered, painful skin is a superficial partial-thickness burn, which describes her arm, not her trunk. The 18 percent also leaves out the arm.","Deep partial-thickness burns still have some sensation and dermal structures. The total also leaves out her arm."],"study":{"topics":["Burn depth classification","Rule of nines"],"comps":["IN-10","IN-1"]}},{"id":"IN-C28-Q2","stem":"Ten months later, the grafted areas on her trunk have raised, firm, red scars that remain within the original burn margins and have started to flatten. Which of the following best describes these scars?","options":["Hypertrophic scars, likely to regress over time","Keloids, likely to extend beyond the wound","Exuberant granulation tissue blocking re-epithelialization","Wound dehiscence from inadequate collagen"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-10","obj":"IN-10.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the two key features: the scars stay within the wound borders and are flattening.","Recall that hypertrophic scars stay inside the wound and often regress, while keloids grow beyond it and persist.","Choose the option that matches both features."],"why":"Excess scar tissue takes two forms. Hypertrophic scars are raised but stay within the original wound. They contain mostly type III collagen arranged in bundles parallel to the epidermis and often flatten over months to years. They are very common after burns. Keloids extend beyond the wound edges, contain thick, disorganized type I and III collagen, rarely regress, recur after excision, and favor the earlobes, chest, and shoulders, especially in people with darker skin. Other healing problems are exuberant granulation tissue, dehiscence, and contracture.","whyNot":["Correct.","Keloids grow past the wound margins and do not regress. These scars stay within the margins and are flattening.","Exuberant granulation tissue (proud flesh) is soft, red tissue that rises above the skin and blocks epithelial growth during healing, not a firm scar months later.","Dehiscence is reopening of a wound, usually in the first 1 to 2 weeks, from tension, infection, or poor collagen synthesis."],"study":{"topics":["Hypertrophic scar versus keloid","Complications of wound healing"],"comps":["IN-10"]}},{"id":"IN-C28-Q3","stem":"During the first hours she receives large volumes of intravenous fluid. Which of the following fluids, infused alone in large volume, would most likely cause her cells to swell?","options":["0.9% sodium chloride","3% sodium chloride","5% dextrose in water","5% albumin in saline"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-3","obj":"IN-3.4","dok":3,"approach":["Separate osmolarity from tonicity: tonicity depends only on solutes that cannot cross cell membranes.","5% dextrose is about isosmotic in the bag, but cells take up and metabolize the glucose.","What remains is free water, which is hypotonic, so water moves into cells and they swell."],"why":"Water moves by osmosis toward the side with more effective solute. Osmolarity counts all dissolved particles, while tonicity counts only those that stay outside cells and pull water. Normal saline is isotonic, so cell volume does not change. Hypertonic saline draws water out of cells. Dextrose 5% starts at about 250 to 280 mOsm/L, but glucose enters cells and is metabolized, leaving free water that acts as a hypotonic solution. Urea works the same way: it crosses membranes freely, so it is an ineffective osmole that adds osmolarity without tonicity.","whyNot":["Normal saline is isotonic. Sodium and chloride stay mostly outside cells, so cell volume does not change.","3% saline is hypertonic. It pulls water out of cells and shrinks them.","Correct.","Albumin in saline is isotonic and stays mostly in the vascular space, where its oncotic pressure holds fluid in vessels."],"study":{"topics":["Osmolarity versus tonicity","Intravenous fluids and cell volume"],"comps":["IN-3"]}},{"id":"IN-C28-Q4","stem":"On day 6 her full-thickness trunk wound develops blue-green exudate with a sweet, grapelike odor. Culture grows oxidase-positive, gram-negative rods. Which of the following best explains why intact skin normally resists colonization by this organism?","options":["Dry, acidic surface with antimicrobial peptides and flora","Moist, neutral surface that favors gram-negative organisms","Keratinocytes secrete IgE into the stratum corneum","Melanin absorbs ultraviolet light and kills surface bacteria"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-9","obj":"IN-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a gram-negative rod that thrives in moist environments.","List the defenses of intact skin: a dry keratinized barrier, acidic pH, sebum fatty acids, shedding of squames, antimicrobial peptides, and competing flora.","A full-thickness burn removes all of these and leaves moist, dead tissue."],"why":"Healthy skin resists colonization in several ways. The stratum corneum is dry and constantly shed. The surface pH is about 4.5 to 5.5, and fatty acids released from sebum are toxic to many bacteria. Keratinocytes make antimicrobial peptides such as defensins and cathelicidin. Resident flora, including Staphylococcus epidermidis, Cutibacterium acnes, Corynebacterium, and Malassezia, compete for space and nutrients. Some people also carry S. aureus in the nares. A burn removes this barrier and leaves moist, avascular eschar where Pseudomonas thrives.","whyNot":["Correct.","Intact skin is dry and acidic. A moist, neutral surface is what a burn wound offers and what favors Pseudomonas.","Keratinocytes do not secrete IgE. IgE is made by plasma cells and binds mast cells. The main secreted antibody at mucosal surfaces is IgA.","Melanin protects keratinocyte nuclei from UV damage. It does not sterilize the skin surface."],"study":{"topics":["Normal skin flora","Skin barrier defenses"],"comps":["IN-9","IN-10"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C29","system":"IN","dx":"Accidental hypothermia with frostbite of the toes","codes":["IN-4"],"case":"A 55-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after being found lying in the snow on a winter morning. He is drowsy and slurring his speech. His core temperature is 29.5 °C (85.1 °F), pulse is 38/min, respirations are 8/min, and blood pressure is 84/50 mm Hg. He is not shivering. An ECG shows sinus bradycardia, prolonged intervals, and a positive deflection at the junction of the QRS complex and ST segment (Osborn J wave). The toes of both feet are white, hard, and numb. After rewarming, they become swollen with hemorrhagic blisters. Diagnosis: accidental hypothermia with frostbite of the toes.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C29-Q1","stem":"His brother works on a fishing boat and develops itchy, tender, red-purple papules on several fingers after weeks of cold, damp exposure at temperatures above freezing. Which of the following best explains the brother's lesions?","options":["Microvascular thrombosis after tissue freezing","Inflammation from damp cold without freezing","Ice crystals forming in the extracellular fluid","Cryoglobulin immune complexes in small vessels"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-4","obj":"IN-4.7","dok":3,"approach":["Note the exposure: damp cold above freezing, repeated over weeks.","Recall that frostbite needs tissue freezing, with ice crystals, endothelial injury, and thrombosis.","Nonfreezing damp cold causes perniosis (chilblains), an inflammatory reaction in small vessels."],"why":"Frostbite occurs when tissue freezes. Ice crystals form outside cells and draw water out of them, and endothelial injury leads to thrombosis and ischemia when the tissue rewarms, as in this patient's blistered toes. Perniosis (chilblains) follows repeated exposure to damp cold above freezing. Vessels constrict and then react with inflammation, causing itchy or painful red-purple papules on the fingers and toes. In the patient, systemic hypothermia below 35 °C (95 °F) slows the heart, produces Osborn J waves, and raises the risk of arrhythmias. Shivering usually stops once core temperature falls below about 32 °C (89.6 °F).","whyNot":["Thrombosis after freezing and rewarming is the mechanism of frostbite, as in the patient's toes.","Correct.","Ice crystal formation requires tissue freezing, which did not occur in the brother.","Cryoglobulinemia causes purpura and vasculitis from immune complexes that precipitate in the cold, often with hepatitis C. It is a systemic disease that does not depend on damp exposure."],"study":{"topics":["Frostbite versus perniosis","Hypothermia"],"comps":["IN-4"]}},{"id":"IN-C29-Q2","stem":"Unlike this patient, a healthy newborn exposed to cold produces heat mainly without shivering. Which of the following is the main source of that heat?","options":["Myosin ATP hydrolysis in skeletal muscle","Thyroid hormone induction of Na+/K+-ATPase","Uncoupling protein 1 in brown adipose tissue","Cutaneous vasoconstriction reducing heat loss"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-4","obj":"IN-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the age: newborns shiver poorly.","Recall nonshivering thermogenesis: sympathetic stimulation of brown fat through beta-3 receptors.","Name the protein: UCP1 (thermogenin) lets protons leak back into the matrix, releasing energy as heat."],"why":"The body keeps heat by cutaneous vasoconstriction and piloerection, and makes heat by shivering, thyroid hormone, and nonshivering thermogenesis. Brown fat cells are packed with mitochondria. Norepinephrine acting on beta-3 receptors activates UCP1 (thermogenin), which lets protons flow back across the inner mitochondrial membrane without making ATP, so the energy is released as heat. Newborns have a large share of brown fat around the neck, between the shoulders, and near the kidneys, and they shiver poorly, so they rely on it. Thyroid hormone raises heat production too, but over days, not minutes.","whyNot":["Myosin ATP hydrolysis during shivering is the main source of extra heat in cold adults, but newborns shiver poorly.","Thyroid hormone increases Na+/K+-ATPase and metabolic rate, but this takes days and is not the newborn's main immediate source of heat.","Correct.","Vasoconstriction conserves heat. It does not produce heat."],"study":{"topics":["Brown fat and UCP1","Heat production and conservation"],"comps":["IN-4"]}},{"id":"IN-C29-Q3","stem":"Rewarming restores blood flow to his toes, and reperfusion generates reactive oxygen species. Glutathione peroxidase in the reperfused cells converts hydrogen peroxide to water. Which of the following combinations is required to keep this enzyme working?","options":["Selenium with NADPH from the pentose phosphate pathway","Heme iron with FADH2 from mitochondrial fatty acid oxidation","Manganese with NADH from the mitochondrial citric acid cycle","Copper and zinc with NADH from glycolysis"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"IN-6","obj":"IN-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the cofactor of glutathione peroxidase: selenium.","Glutathione peroxidase uses reduced glutathione and turns it into oxidized glutathione.","Glutathione reductase regenerates it using NADPH, which comes from G6PD in the pentose phosphate pathway."],"why":"Cells detoxify reactive oxygen species with a chain of enzymes. Superoxide dismutase (Cu/Zn in the cytosol, Mn in mitochondria) turns superoxide into hydrogen peroxide. Catalase and glutathione peroxidase turn hydrogen peroxide into water. Glutathione peroxidase contains selenium and uses reduced glutathione, which glutathione reductase must regenerate with NADPH. That NADPH comes from glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase in the pentose phosphate pathway. This is why G6PD deficiency leaves red cells exposed to oxidant damage.","whyNot":["Correct.","Heme iron is the cofactor of catalase, which does not need a reducing agent. FADH2 feeds the electron transport chain.","Manganese is the cofactor of mitochondrial superoxide dismutase. NADH from the citric acid cycle feeds the electron transport chain.","Copper and zinc are cofactors of cytosolic superoxide dismutase, not glutathione peroxidase, and NADH does not regenerate glutathione."],"study":{"topics":["Antioxidant enzymes","Glutathione and NADPH"],"comps":["IN-6","IN-8"]}},{"id":"IN-C29-Q4","stem":"Over the next 3 weeks, two toes become black, dry, shriveled, and sharply demarcated from viable tissue. Microscopic examination of an amputated toe would most likely show which of the following patterns?","options":["Caseous necrosis surrounded by granulomas","Fibrinoid necrosis of small arterial walls","Liquefactive necrosis from neutrophil enzyme digestion","Coagulative necrosis with preserved cell outlines"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-8","obj":"IN-8.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize black, dry, shriveled, demarcated toes as dry gangrene.","Recall that dry gangrene is ischemic coagulative necrosis of a limb.","Coagulative necrosis keeps the ghost outlines of cells because proteins denature before enzymes digest them."],"why":"Necrosis types match their settings. Coagulative necrosis follows ischemia in most solid organs, such as a myocardial infarct, and preserves tissue architecture with ghost cells. Gangrenous necrosis is ischemic necrosis of a limb. Dry gangrene is mainly coagulative, while wet gangrene adds bacterial infection and liquefaction. Liquefactive necrosis occurs in brain infarcts and abscesses, caseous necrosis in tuberculosis and some fungal infections, fat necrosis in acute pancreatitis and breast trauma, and fibrinoid necrosis in vasculitis and malignant hypertension.","whyNot":["Caseous necrosis with granulomas is typical of tuberculosis.","Fibrinoid necrosis of vessel walls is seen in vasculitis and malignant hypertension.","Liquefactive necrosis follows bacterial infection or brain infarction. It would fit wet gangrene, with swelling and pus.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Types of necrosis","Dry versus wet gangrene"],"comps":["IN-8","IN-4"]}}]},{"id":"IN-C30","system":"IN","dx":"High-voltage electrical injury with rhabdomyolysis","codes":["IN-10"],"case":"A 36-year-old utility line worker is brought to the trauma center after his right hand touched a 7,200-volt power line. He briefly lost consciousness. His temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 138/86 mm Hg. There is a 1-cm charred wound on the right palm and a 1-cm exit wound on the left heel. The right forearm is tense, swollen, and painful with passive finger extension. Serum creatine kinase is 32,000 U/L (about 25 to 200), and potassium is 7.0 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0). Urine is dark brown, and dipstick is strongly positive for blood, but microscopy shows no red blood cells. Diagnosis: high-voltage electrical injury with rhabdomyolysis.","questions":[{"id":"IN-C30-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his skin wounds are small while his forearm muscle damage is extensive?","options":["Muscle is injured mainly by the flash of an electrical arc","Deep tissues of low resistance carry most of the current","Current spreads mainly along the skin surface","Skin offers lower resistance than muscle to current flow"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"IN-10","obj":"IN-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that dry skin has high resistance and nerves, vessels, and muscle have low resistance.","Once current crosses the skin at the contact point, it travels through the deep, low-resistance tissues.","Heat produced along that path destroys muscle and nerve under nearly intact skin."],"why":"Electrical injury is like an iceberg: small entry and exit wounds hide extensive deep damage. Current crosses the skin at the contact points and then follows the low-resistance tissues, such as nerves, vessels, and muscle, generating heat and breaking cell membranes along the way. Bone has high resistance and heats up, which adds to injury of the muscle beside it. Muscle necrosis causes compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia, and myoglobinuric kidney injury. Current crossing the heart can cause arrhythmias. Lightning causes cardiac arrest and fern-like Lichtenberg figures on the skin.","whyNot":["An arc flash causes surface thermal burns. It does not explain deep muscle necrosis under intact skin.","Correct.","Current that stays on the surface would cause wide skin burns, not deep muscle necrosis.","Dry skin has a higher resistance than muscle, which is why the skin wounds stay small once current enters the deep tissues."],"study":{"topics":["Electrical injury pathology","Rhabdomyolysis"],"comps":["IN-10","IN-3"]}},{"id":"IN-C30-Q2","stem":"Over the next hour he develops generalized muscle weakness and decreased deep tendon reflexes. Which of the following best explains the reduced excitability of his nerves and muscles?","options":["Increased potassium efflux through leak channels","Hyperpolarization moving the membrane away from threshold","Sustained depolarization inactivating sodium channels","Opening of more voltage-gated sodium channels at rest"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-3","obj":"IN-3.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note his serum potassium of 7.0 mEq/L.","High extracellular potassium shrinks the potassium gradient, so the resting membrane potential depolarizes.","A sustained depolarization closes the inactivation gates of voltage-gated sodium channels, so fewer channels can open and cells fire poorly."],"why":"The resting potential depends mainly on the potassium gradient. Hyperkalemia raises extracellular potassium, makes the potassium equilibrium potential less negative, and depolarizes cells. A small depolarization first raises excitability, but sustained depolarization closes the inactivation gates of voltage-gated sodium channels. With fewer sodium channels available, action potentials weaken or fail, causing weakness, reduced reflexes, and dangerous cardiac conduction changes. Hypokalemia does the opposite: it hyperpolarizes the membrane and moves it away from threshold.","whyNot":["Potassium efflux falls when extracellular potassium is high, because the outward gradient is smaller.","Hyperpolarization is the effect of hypokalemia, which also causes weakness, but by moving the membrane farther from threshold.","Correct.","Sustained depolarization reduces the number of sodium channels available to open."],"study":{"topics":["Hyperkalemia and membrane excitability","Sodium channel inactivation"],"comps":["IN-3"]}},{"id":"IN-C30-Q3","stem":"On day 3 the necrotic forearm muscle becomes swollen with crepitus, bronze skin discoloration, and thin, foul-smelling brown drainage. Gram stain shows large gram-positive rods and few neutrophils. Which of the following toxin activities most directly causes the tissue destruction?","options":["Blocking glycine release from spinal interneurons","Cleavage of SNARE proteins at the neuromuscular junction","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2","Phospholipase hydrolysis of cell membrane lecithin"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"IN-13-H12","obj":"IN-13-H.14","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize crepitus, bronze skin, foul drainage, and large gram-positive rods in dead muscle as gas gangrene from Clostridium perfringens.","Recall its main toxin: alpha toxin, a lecithinase (phospholipase C).","Lecithinase breaks cell membranes, causing myonecrosis and hemolysis and killing neutrophils, which explains why so few are seen."],"why":"Clostridium perfringens is an anaerobic, gram-positive rod that grows in dead, poorly perfused muscle. Its alpha toxin is a phospholipase C (lecithinase) that splits lecithin in cell membranes, destroying muscle cells, red cells, and leukocytes. Gas from fermentation produces crepitus. Few neutrophils are seen because the toxin destroys them. Clostridium tetani in a puncture wound makes tetanospasmin, which travels to the spinal cord and blocks release of GABA and glycine from inhibitory interneurons, causing spastic paralysis.","whyNot":["Blocking glycine and GABA release from inhibitory interneurons is the action of tetanospasmin, which causes spastic paralysis without tissue necrosis.","Cleaving SNARE proteins at the neuromuscular junction is the action of botulinum toxin, which causes flaccid paralysis.","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 is the action of diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Clostridial toxins","Gas gangrene"],"comps":["IN-13-H12","IN-10"]}},{"id":"IN-C30-Q4","stem":"Assume an intracellular potassium concentration of 140 mEq/L in his skeletal muscle. At body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F), the value of 2.303RT/F is about 61 mV. Which of the following is closest to the potassium equilibrium potential of his muscle cells at his current serum potassium?","options":["-106 mV","-94 mV","-79 mV","-61 mV"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"IN-3","obj":"IN-3.5","dok":3,"approach":["Write the Nernst equation for a cation: E = 61 mV x log10 ([K+] outside / [K+] inside).","Plug in his values: 7.0 / 140 = 0.05, and log10 of 0.05 is about -1.3.","Multiply: 61 x -1.3 is about -79 mV, less negative than the normal value of about -94 mV."],"why":"The Nernst equation gives the membrane potential at which an ion's chemical and electrical gradients balance. For potassium at body temperature, E = 61 mV x log10 ([K+]o / [K+]i). With normal serum potassium of 4 mEq/L, the ratio is 4/140, and E is about -94 mV. At 7.0 mEq/L the ratio is 1/20, and E is about -79 mV. At rest, potassium leak channels make the membrane most permeable to potassium, so the resting potential sits near this value, as the Goldman equation predicts. Impermeant intracellular anions (the Gibbs-Donnan effect) help hold potassium inside the cell. A rise in serum potassium therefore depolarizes his cells.","whyNot":["About -106 mV would need a lower serum potassium than normal, as in hypokalemia.","About -94 mV is the normal value with serum potassium of 4 mEq/L, not his current value.","Correct.","About -61 mV would need a tenfold ratio, a serum potassium of 14 mEq/L."],"study":{"topics":["Nernst equation","Resting membrane potential"],"comps":["IN-3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C01","system":"MS","dx":"Osteomalacia after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass","codes":["MS-13-A1"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 9-month history of aching pain in her ribs, hips, and lower legs and increasing difficulty climbing stairs and rising from a chair. She had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass 7 years ago and stopped taking her prescribed supplements 3 years ago. Physical examination shows tenderness to pressure over the ribs and tibiae, weakness of the hip flexors, and a waddling gait. Laboratory studies show: calcium 8.3 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 2.1 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), alkaline phosphatase 312 U/L (40 to 120), parathyroid hormone 131 pg/mL (10 to 65), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D 7 ng/mL (30 to 100). Serum creatinine and aminotransferase activities are within the reference ranges. Diagnosis: osteomalacia due to vitamin D deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C01-Q1","stem":"Radiographs of the pelvis and femurs are obtained. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Bilateral transverse lucent bands in the femoral necks","Coarse trabeculae with cortical thickening and bone enlargement","Subchondral cysts with osteophytes and joint space narrowing","Multiple lytic lesions with sharply punched-out margins"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-A1","obj":"MS-13-A.3","dok":2,"approach":["Link the low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH, and low 25-hydroxyvitamin D to defective mineralization of osteoid in an adult.","Recall that unmineralized osteoid cannot carry normal loads, so it develops incomplete stress fractures that do not heal.","Pick the radiographic sign of these pseudofractures, called Looser zones."],"why":"Osteomalacia is defective mineralization of new osteoid after the growth plates have closed. Soft, poorly mineralized bone develops Looser zones, also called pseudofractures. These are narrow lucent bands that run across the cortex, often on both sides, at the femoral necks, pubic rami, ribs, and lateral border of the scapula. The rest of the skeleton often looks diffusely osteopenic.","whyNot":["Correct.","This describes Paget disease of bone, which also raises alkaline phosphatase. In Paget disease, calcium, phosphate, and PTH are usually normal, and the bone is enlarged and dense.","These are findings of osteoarthritis, a disease of articular cartilage. They do not explain diffuse bone pain or the mineral abnormalities here.","Punched-out lytic lesions suggest multiple myeloma. Myeloma causes bone pain, but it tends to raise calcium and does not lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D."],"study":{"topics":["Looser zones in osteomalacia","Laboratory pattern of vitamin D deficiency"],"comps":["MS-13-A1","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C01-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her vitamin D deficiency?","options":["Increased hepatic catabolism of vitamin D by induced cytochrome P450","Reduced 25-hydroxylation of cholecalciferol in the liver","Reduced 1-alpha-hydroxylation of 25-hydroxyvitamin D in the kidney","Poor absorption of fat-soluble vitamin D from the small bowel"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-A1","obj":"MS-13-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note that 25-hydroxyvitamin D is very low, so the problem is in the supply of vitamin D that reaches the liver.","Normal creatinine and aminotransferases argue against kidney or liver disease, and no enzyme-inducing drug is listed.","Roux-en-Y bypass sends food past the duodenum and proximal jejunum and delays mixing with bile, so fat-soluble vitamins are poorly absorbed."],"why":"Vitamin D is fat soluble. It is absorbed with dietary fat in bile salt micelles in the small intestine. After Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, food skips the duodenum and proximal jejunum and meets bile only further down in the common channel, so less vitamin D is absorbed. Without supplements, her stores ran down over years. A low 25-hydroxyvitamin D level shows that the body is short of substrate.","whyNot":["Enzyme-inducing anticonvulsants such as phenytoin speed vitamin D breakdown. She takes no such drug.","Severe liver disease can impair 25-hydroxylation, but her aminotransferases are normal, and the liver has a large reserve for this step.","Kidney failure lowers calcitriol, but 25-hydroxyvitamin D stays normal. Her creatinine is normal and her 25-hydroxyvitamin D is very low.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Risk factors for vitamin D deficiency","Fat-soluble vitamin absorption after bariatric surgery"],"comps":["MS-13-A1","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C01-Q3","stem":"She is started on oral cholecalciferol. Her physician plans to follow serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D rather than 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D to judge when her stores are restored. Which of the following best explains this choice?","options":["It is formed directly in the skin by ultraviolet B light","It is long-lived and its synthesis tracks supply","Its production in the kidney is stimulated by PTH","It binds the vitamin D receptor with the highest affinity"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the pathway: 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin to cholecalciferol, then hepatic 25-hydroxylation, then renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation.","Ask which step is tightly controlled by hormones and which simply follows the amount of substrate.","The best marker of stores is the long-lived form whose level follows supply."],"why":"UVB light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin to cholecalciferol, which also comes from the diet. The liver adds a 25-hydroxyl group with little feedback control, so 25-hydroxyvitamin D rises and falls with supply and circulates for about 2 to 3 weeks. The kidney then makes calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) under tight control by PTH, phosphate, and FGF23. Calcitriol lasts only hours, and in deficiency the high PTH can keep it normal or even high, so it is a poor measure of stores.","whyNot":["The skin makes cholecalciferol (vitamin D3) from 7-dehydrocholesterol. The 25-hydroxylation step happens in the liver.","Correct.","PTH stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which makes calcitriol. 25-hydroxyvitamin D is made in the liver.","Calcitriol is the form that binds the vitamin D receptor most strongly and acts as the hormone."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation pathway","Why 25-hydroxyvitamin D measures vitamin D status"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-A1"]}},{"id":"MS-C01-Q4","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms most directly accounts for her low serum phosphate concentration?","options":["Rapid uptake of phosphate into remineralizing bone","Insulin-driven shift of phosphate into cells","PTH excess reducing renal phosphate reabsorption","FGF23 excess reducing renal phosphate reabsorption"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Vitamin D deficiency lowers intestinal calcium absorption, and the falling calcium stimulates the parathyroid glands.","Secondary hyperparathyroidism follows, and PTH is high in this case.","PTH removes sodium-phosphate cotransporters from the proximal tubule, so phosphate is lost in the urine."],"why":"In vitamin D deficiency the gut absorbs less calcium, and PTH rises to defend serum calcium. PTH increases bone resorption by raising RANKL on osteoblasts, increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule, and stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase. It also lowers phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule. The result is a calcium that is low or low-normal and a clearly low phosphate. Reduced gut absorption of phosphate adds to this, but PTH-driven urinary loss is the main cause.","whyNot":["Hungry bone after parathyroidectomy or early in vitamin D treatment pulls phosphate into bone. Her labs were drawn before any treatment.","Insulin moves phosphate into cells during refeeding. Nothing in her history suggests refeeding.","Correct.","FGF23 excess causes phosphate wasting in X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets and tumor-induced osteomalacia. In those disorders PTH and 25-hydroxyvitamin D are usually normal."],"study":{"topics":["Secondary hyperparathyroidism","PTH actions on the kidney"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-A1"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C02","system":"MS","dx":"Nutritional rickets in an exclusively breastfed toddler","codes":["MS-13-A2"],"case":"An 18-month-old boy is brought to the physician because he is not yet walking steadily and his legs have become bowed. He has been exclusively breastfed and has received no vitamin supplements. He has darkly pigmented skin and lives in a northern city with long winters. Physical examination shows frontal bossing, beading of the costochondral junctions, widened wrists, and bowed legs. Laboratory studies show: calcium 8.1 mg/dL (8.8 to 10.8), phosphate 3.1 mg/dL (3.8 to 6.5), alkaline phosphatase 960 U/L (about 150 to 420), parathyroid hormone 148 pg/mL (10 to 65), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D 6 ng/mL (30 to 100). Diagnosis: nutritional rickets due to vitamin D deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C02-Q1","stem":"A 2-year-old girl seen in the same clinic also has bowed legs and widened wrists. Her mother and maternal grandmother are short and have bowed legs. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings would most strongly suggest X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets in the girl?","options":["Calcium low, phosphate low, 25-hydroxyvitamin D low, PTH high","Calcium normal, phosphate low, 25-hydroxyvitamin D normal, PTH normal","Calcium high, phosphate high, 25-hydroxyvitamin D high, PTH suppressed","Calcium low, phosphate high, 25-hydroxyvitamin D normal, PTH very high"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-A2","obj":"MS-13-A.4","dok":3,"approach":["X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets comes from loss of PHEX function, which raises FGF23 from osteocytes.","FGF23 causes renal phosphate wasting and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so phosphate is low while vitamin D stores are normal.","Calcium stays near normal, so PTH is not strongly stimulated. Pick the pattern with an isolated low phosphate."],"why":"In X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets, loss of PHEX function raises FGF23. FGF23 removes sodium-phosphate cotransporters from the proximal tubule and suppresses renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so phosphate is low and calcitriol is low or inappropriately normal. Calcium, 25-hydroxyvitamin D, and PTH are usually normal or near normal. The skeletal signs look like nutritional rickets, but the disease does not respond to ordinary vitamin D doses. Affected mothers pass it to half their children, which fits this family.","whyNot":["This is the pattern of nutritional vitamin D deficiency, as in the boy in this case.","Correct.","High calcium, suppressed PTH, and high 25-hydroxyvitamin D point to vitamin D toxicity, which does not cause rickets.","Low calcium with high phosphate and very high PTH is the pattern of chronic kidney disease, which causes renal osteodystrophy."],"study":{"topics":["X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets","FGF23 and renal phosphate handling"],"comps":["MS-13-A2","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C02-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why the long bones of his legs have bowed?","options":["Failure of newly formed osteoid to mineralize","Reduced synthesis of type I collagen by osteoblasts","Reduced chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate","Reduced bone mass with a normally mineralized matrix"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-A2","obj":"MS-13-A.1","dok":2,"approach":["Low calcium and low phosphate leave too little mineral for hydroxyapatite crystals to form.","Osteoblasts keep making osteoid, but it stays soft and unmineralized.","Soft bone bends under the weight of a child who is starting to walk."],"why":"Vitamin D deficiency lowers intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate, and secondary hyperparathyroidism adds renal phosphate loss. With too little mineral, the osteoid that osteoblasts lay down cannot form hydroxyapatite. Unmineralized osteoid is flexible, so weight-bearing bones bow. The same defect is called rickets when it occurs before the growth plates close and osteomalacia after they close.","whyNot":["Correct.","Reduced or abnormal type I collagen causes osteogenesis imperfecta, with brittle bones that fracture. The collagen matrix is made normally in rickets.","Reduced chondrocyte proliferation from FGFR3 gain of function causes achondroplasia, with short limbs and normal mineral levels.","This describes osteoporosis. The bone that remains is normally mineralized, and calcium, phosphate, and PTH are normal."],"study":{"topics":["Mineralization defect in rickets and osteomalacia"],"comps":["MS-13-A2","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C02-Q3","stem":"A radiograph of his wrist shows widened, cupped, and frayed distal radial and ulnar metaphyses. A biopsy of the growth plate would most likely show which of the following?","options":["A thin, disorganized zone of proliferating chondrocytes","Dense calcified cartilage cores persisting in the diaphysis","A thick, disorganized zone of hypertrophic chondrocytes","Early fusion of the plate with bony bridging"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the zones from the epiphysis toward the shaft: reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, and ossification.","Hypertrophic chondrocytes need enough phosphate to undergo apoptosis and let their matrix calcify.","Without mineralization the hypertrophic cells pile up, so the plate becomes thick and irregular, seen as a widened, cupped metaphysis."],"why":"In a normal growth plate, chondrocytes divide in the proliferative zone, enlarge in the hypertrophic zone, and then die as the matrix around them calcifies and is replaced by bone. Low phosphate blocks apoptosis of hypertrophic chondrocytes and mineralization of their matrix. The hypertrophic zone expands and becomes disorganized, and the metaphysis widens and cups on radiographs.","whyNot":["A thin proliferative zone is the defect in achondroplasia, where FGFR3 gain of function slows chondrocyte proliferation.","Persistent calcified cartilage cores in dense bone are seen in osteopetrosis, where osteoclasts fail to resorb.","Correct.","Premature closure follows growth plate fractures or early exposure to sex steroids. Rickets widens the plate."],"study":{"topics":["Growth plate zones","Growth plate in rickets compared with achondroplasia"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-A2"]}},{"id":"MS-C02-Q4","stem":"Two days after a febrile illness, he has spasms of the hands and feet, and tapping over the facial nerve makes the corner of his mouth twitch. Which of the following best explains his increased neuromuscular excitability?","options":["Increased acetylcholine release from motor nerve terminals","Lower threshold for opening of neuronal sodium channels","Increased calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Reduced acetylcholinesterase activity at the end plate"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Link carpopedal spasm and a Chvostek sign to hypocalcemia.","Extracellular calcium normally binds the outer membrane surface near sodium channels and raises the voltage needed to open them.","When calcium falls, sodium channels open closer to the resting potential, so nerves fire on their own."],"why":"Extracellular calcium stabilizes excitable membranes. Calcium ions bind negative charges on the outside of the membrane near sodium channel gates, which makes the channels harder to open. In hypocalcemia, a smaller depolarization opens sodium channels, so peripheral nerves fire repeatedly and cause tetany. The Chvostek sign (facial twitch when the facial nerve is tapped) and the Trousseau sign (carpal spasm with a blood pressure cuff) show this excitability.","whyNot":["Calcium entry into the nerve terminal triggers acetylcholine release, so low extracellular calcium reduces release.","Correct.","In skeletal muscle, sarcoplasmic reticulum release is triggered by the T-tubule voltage sensor and does not depend on extracellular calcium.","Acetylcholinesterase inhibition, as with organophosphates, causes fasciculations and weakness. Hypocalcemia does not affect the enzyme."],"study":{"topics":["Hypocalcemia and membrane excitability","Chvostek and Trousseau signs"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-13-A2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C03","system":"MS","dx":"Scurvy in an older man with alcohol use disorder","codes":["MS-13-A3"],"case":"A 67-year-old man is brought to the physician by his daughter because of 2 months of fatigue, bleeding gums, easy bruising, and a painful, swollen right knee. He has alcohol use disorder, lives alone, and for the past year has eaten mostly toast, tea, and canned meat with no fruit or vegetables. A shin laceration from 6 weeks ago has not healed. Physical examination shows swollen, purple, friable gums, large ecchymoses on the thighs, and a tense right knee effusion. Laboratory studies show: hemoglobin 10.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), platelet count 238,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000), prothrombin time 12.5 seconds (11 to 13.5), and partial thromboplastin time 31 seconds (25 to 35). Plasma ascorbic acid is 0.1 mg/dL (0.4 to 2.0). Diagnosis: scurvy.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C03-Q1","stem":"Examination of his skin is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Perifollicular hemorrhages with coiled hairs","Palpable purpura with IgA deposits in vessel walls","Rough follicular papules without bleeding","Spider angiomas with radiating small vessels"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-A3","obj":"MS-13-A.6","dok":2,"approach":["Bleeding with a normal platelet count, PT, and PTT points to weak vessel walls, not a clotting factor or platelet problem.","A diet without fruit or vegetables and a very low ascorbic acid level identify vitamin C deficiency.","Weak collagen around hair follicles leads to small bleeds there, and abnormal keratinization produces coiled, corkscrew hairs."],"why":"Scurvy weakens the collagen that supports small blood vessels. Early skin signs are follicular hyperkeratosis with perifollicular hemorrhages, often on the legs, and coiled corkscrew hairs. Larger bleeds cause ecchymoses, swollen bleeding gums, and hemarthrosis. Coagulation tests are normal because platelets and the clotting cascade are intact. Risk is highest in people who eat no fruit or vegetables, including many with alcohol use disorder.","whyNot":["Correct.","Palpable purpura with IgA deposits is IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura), seen mostly in children after an upper respiratory infection.","Follicular papules without bleeding fit vitamin A deficiency. Bleeding around the follicle is what points to vitamin C.","Spider angiomas come from high estrogen in chronic liver disease. They are common with heavy alcohol use but do not explain bleeding with normal coagulation tests."],"study":{"topics":["Clinical findings of scurvy"],"comps":["MS-13-A3","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C03-Q2","stem":"Which of the following steps in collagen synthesis is most directly impaired in this patient?","options":["Cleavage of terminal propeptides from procollagen","Cross-linking of lysine residues by lysyl oxidase","Glycosylation of hydroxylysine residues in procollagen","Hydroxylation of proline residues in procollagen"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-A3","obj":"MS-13-A.5","dok":2,"approach":["Vitamin C is the cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases in the rough endoplasmic reticulum.","Hydroxyproline forms hydrogen bonds that hold the three alpha chains together in a stable triple helix.","Without it the triple helix is unstable at body temperature and is degraded, so vessel walls and wounds are weak."],"why":"Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases need iron in the ferrous state, and ascorbate keeps the iron reduced. Without vitamin C, proline and lysine residues in procollagen are poorly hydroxylated. Underhydroxylated chains form unstable triple helices that are degraded inside the cell, so less collagen is secreted. Capillary walls and healing wounds depend on new collagen, so they bleed and fail to heal.","whyNot":["Procollagen peptidases remove propeptides outside the cell. Defects cause the dermatosparaxis type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","Lysyl oxidase acts outside the cell and needs copper. Its failure is seen in Menkes disease.","Glycosylation does not use vitamin C. It falls only as a downstream effect, because fewer hydroxylysine residues are available.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin C and prolyl hydroxylation","Steps of collagen synthesis"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-A3"]}},{"id":"MS-C03-Q3","stem":"Aspiration of his right knee yields grossly bloody fluid. Which of the following structures of this synovial joint is the most likely source of the bleeding?","options":["Inner portion of the medial meniscus","Articular hyaline cartilage of the femoral condyles","Calcified cartilage deep to the tidemark","Synovial membrane lining the joint capsule"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Bleeding needs a vascular tissue that faces the joint cavity.","Articular hyaline cartilage and the inner menisci have no blood vessels and are nourished by synovial fluid.","The synovial membrane has a rich capillary network under its lining cells, so fragile capillaries there bleed into the joint."],"why":"A synovial joint has articular hyaline cartilage on the bone ends, a cavity filled with synovial fluid, and a capsule lined by synovial membrane. The synovial membrane is a thin layer of synoviocytes that make hyaluronan and lubricin, lying on a loose, highly vascular subintima. In scurvy the walls of these capillaries are weak, so they bleed into the cavity and cause hemarthrosis. Articular cartilage has no vessels at all.","whyNot":["Only the outer third of each meniscus has a blood supply. The inner portion is avascular.","Articular hyaline cartilage is avascular and gets its nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid, so it cannot bleed.","The calcified cartilage under the tidemark is avascular and is covered by the articular cartilage, so it does not face the cavity.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Structure of synovial joints","Vascular supply of joint tissues"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C03-Q4","stem":"Additional studies show a serum calcium of 7.4 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5) with a normal albumin, magnesium 0.8 mg/dL (1.7 to 2.2), and parathyroid hormone 12 pg/mL (10 to 65). His calcium stays low despite intravenous calcium until magnesium is replaced. Which of the following best explains his hypocalcemia?","options":["Reduced osteocalcin carboxylation from vitamin K depletion","Reduced lysyl oxidase activity from copper depletion","Impaired PTH secretion and action from magnesium depletion","Reduced renal calcium reabsorption from vitamin C depletion"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Low calcium should drive PTH up, yet his PTH is low-normal, so the parathyroid response is blunted.","Severe hypomagnesemia, common in alcohol use disorder, impairs PTH release and makes bone and kidney resistant to PTH.","Calcium corrects only after magnesium is replaced, which confirms the link."],"why":"Magnesium is needed for the parathyroid glands to secrete PTH and for target tissues to respond to it. In severe magnesium depletion, PTH stays inappropriately low and calcium falls. Alcohol use disorder causes magnesium loss through poor intake, diarrhea, and renal wasting. Calcium given alone does not correct the problem until magnesium is restored. Mild hypomagnesemia can stimulate PTH, and the block appears with severe depletion.","whyNot":["Vitamin K carboxylates osteocalcin and clotting factors. Deficiency affects bone quality and clotting, and his PT is normal.","Copper is the cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen. Copper deficiency weakens connective tissue but does not lower calcium.","Correct.","Vitamin C has no direct role in renal calcium handling."],"study":{"topics":["Magnesium and PTH secretion","Mineral and cofactor roles in bone"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-A3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C04","system":"MS","dx":"Ankylosing spondylitis","codes":["MS-13-B1"],"case":"A 26-year-old man comes to the physician because of 2 years of low back and buttock pain with morning stiffness that lasts about 90 minutes. The pain wakes him in the second half of the night, improves with exercise, and does not improve with rest. He has also had heel pain. Physical examination shows tenderness over both sacroiliac joints and at the Achilles tendon insertions. Forward flexion of the lumbar spine is reduced, and chest expansion is 2.0 cm (normal 5 cm or more). Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 38 mm/h (0 to 15), and rheumatoid factor is negative. Radiographs show bilateral sacroiliitis with erosions and sclerosis. HLA-B27 testing is positive. Diagnosis: ankylosing spondylitis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C04-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the normal function of the HLA molecule linked to his disease?","options":["Presents endocytosed peptides to CD4 T cells","Presents lipid antigens to NKT cells","Presents cytosolic peptides to CD8 T cells","Binds immune complexes as a complement protein"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-B1","obj":"MS-13-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["HLA-B is one of the three classical MHC class I loci (A, B, and C).","Class I molecules are found on nearly all nucleated cells and display peptides made inside the cell.","Peptides on class I molecules are recognized by CD8 cytotoxic T cells."],"why":"HLA-B27 is an MHC class I molecule. Like other class I molecules, it carries peptides from cytosolic proteins, cut by the proteasome and loaded in the endoplasmic reticulum, to the cell surface for CD8 T cells. How it causes spondyloarthritis is not fully known. Leading ideas are presentation of cross-reactive microbial peptides, misfolding of HLA-B27 heavy chains that triggers cell stress, and IL-23 and IL-17 driven inflammation at entheses. The result is enthesitis and sacroiliitis that heal with new bone.","whyNot":["This is the function of MHC class II molecules (HLA-DR, DP, and DQ). HLA-DR4 is linked to rheumatoid arthritis.","CD1d, a nonclassical molecule, presents lipids to NKT cells. HLA-B27 presents peptides.","Correct.","Complement proteins C2, C4, and factor B are encoded in the MHC class III region. HLA-B27 is not a complement protein."],"study":{"topics":["HLA-B27 and seronegative spondyloarthropathies","MHC class I antigen presentation"],"comps":["MS-13-B1","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C04-Q2","stem":"Twelve years later, a high-pitched, blowing, early diastolic decrescendo murmur is heard along the sternal border while he leans forward. Which of the following hemodynamic findings is most likely?","options":["Wide pulse pressure with a low diastolic pressure","Large v waves in the jugular venous pulse","Pulsus paradoxus with equal diastolic chamber pressures","Narrow pulse pressure with a delayed carotid upstroke"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-B1","obj":"MS-13-B.2","dok":3,"approach":["An early diastolic decrescendo murmur along the sternal border is aortic regurgitation.","Aortitis in ankylosing spondylitis dilates the aortic root and pulls the cusps apart.","Blood flows back into the ventricle during diastole, so diastolic pressure falls, and the larger stroke volume raises systolic pressure."],"why":"Ankylosing spondylitis can inflame the aortic root and the base of the valve. The root dilates and the cusps fail to close, causing aortic regurgitation. Backflow during diastole drops aortic diastolic pressure, while the left ventricle ejects a larger stroke volume, raising systolic pressure. The result is a wide pulse pressure with bounding pulses. Other extra-articular features of the disease include anterior uveitis, restrictive lung disease, and fractures of the fused spine.","whyNot":["Correct.","Large v waves suggest tricuspid regurgitation, which causes a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border.","Pulsus paradoxus with equal diastolic pressures in all chambers is cardiac tamponade.","A narrow pulse pressure with a slow, delayed upstroke is aortic stenosis, which causes a systolic ejection murmur."],"study":{"topics":["Extra-articular features of ankylosing spondylitis","Hemodynamics of aortic regurgitation"],"comps":["MS-13-B1"]}},{"id":"MS-C04-Q3","stem":"As the disease advances, his spine fuses in the forward-flexed posture that eases his pain. Which of the following changes in spinal curvature is most likely?","options":["Increased lumbar lordosis and reduced thoracic kyphosis","Loss of lumbar lordosis and increased thoracic kyphosis","Lateral thoracic curvature with vertebral rotation","Increased cervical lordosis with normal thoracic curvature"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the normal curves: cervical and lumbar lordosis, and thoracic and sacral kyphosis.","Bony bridges called syndesmophytes join the vertebrae in whatever posture the patient holds.","A forward-flexed posture flattens the lumbar spine and bends the thoracic spine further forward."],"why":"The adult spine has two curves that are convex forward (cervical and lumbar lordosis) and two that are convex backward (thoracic and sacral kyphosis). In ankylosing spondylitis, inflammation at the vertebral corners and the annulus fibrosus heals with syndesmophytes that fuse the spine, the bamboo spine on radiographs. Patients lean forward for comfort, so the lumbar lordosis is lost and thoracic kyphosis increases. Severe thoracic deformity, like severe scoliosis, stiffens the chest wall and limits lung expansion.","whyNot":["Excess lumbar lordosis occurs with pregnancy, obesity, or weak abdominal muscles. Inflammation and fusion in this disease flatten the lumbar spine.","Correct.","Lateral curvature with vertebral rotation is scoliosis, which is usually idiopathic and starts in adolescence.","The cervical spine also stiffens and the head is pushed forward, so cervical lordosis is lost."],"study":{"topics":["Normal spinal curvatures","Spinal posture in ankylosing spondylitis"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-13-B1"]}},{"id":"MS-C04-Q4","stem":"His reduced chest expansion is partly due to inflammation and fusion of the posterior synovial joints that let the ribs rise during inspiration. These joints are formed with which of the following vertebral features?","options":["Transverse foramina of the cervical vertebrae","Bifid spinous processes of the cervical vertebrae","Mammillary processes of the lumbar vertebrae","Costal facets of the thoracic vertebrae"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["The ribs move at joints with the vertebrae during breathing, which lifts and widens the chest.","Only thoracic vertebrae carry costal facets on their bodies and transverse processes for the rib heads and tubercles.","Fusion of these synovial joints locks the ribs and limits chest expansion."],"why":"Each typical rib meets the thoracic spine at two synovial joints. The rib head joins costal facets on the vertebral bodies (costovertebral joint), and the rib tubercle joins the facet on the transverse process (costotransverse joint). Rib elevation at these joints expands the chest during inspiration. Ankylosing spondylitis inflames and fuses these joints, so chest expansion falls and breathing depends more on the diaphragm.","whyNot":["Transverse foramina carry the vertebral arteries in the cervical spine. Ribs do not attach there.","Bifid cervical spines anchor the ligamentum nuchae and neck muscles, not ribs.","Mammillary processes on lumbar vertebrae anchor deep back muscles. Lumbar vertebrae have no rib joints.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Regional features of vertebrae","Costovertebral joints and breathing"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-4"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C05","system":"MS","dx":"Septic prepatellar bursitis from Staphylococcus aureus","codes":["MS-13-B2"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman who installs floor tile comes to the emergency department because of 3 days of painful swelling over the front of her right knee. She works kneeling most of the day and had several small abrasions on that knee last week. Temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), pulse 98/min, respirations 16/min, and blood pressure 126/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a warm, red, fluctuant swelling directly over the patella. She can flex the knee to 120 degrees with pain only at full flexion, and there is no joint effusion. Aspiration of the swelling yields cloudy fluid with 52,000 leukocytes/mm3, 90% neutrophils. Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters, and culture grows a catalase-positive, coagulase-positive organism. Diagnosis: septic prepatellar bursitis caused by Staphylococcus aureus.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C05-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely route by which the organism reached the bursa?","options":["Direct entry through overlying skin abrasions","Contiguous spread from osteomyelitis of the patella","Spread from the adjacent knee joint cavity","Hematogenous seeding during transient bacteremia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B2","obj":"MS-13-B.3","dok":3,"approach":["The prepatellar bursa lies between the skin and the patella, just under the skin surface.","It does not communicate with the knee joint, which is why knee motion is preserved and there is no effusion.","Superficial bursae such as the prepatellar and olecranon bursae are usually infected by organisms entering through skin breaks over them."],"why":"Septic bursitis most often involves the superficial prepatellar and olecranon bursae, which take repeated pressure and minor skin injury. Kneeling work causes chronic friction, the cause of most prepatellar bursitis, and abrasions let skin flora such as Staphylococcus aureus enter the bursa directly. Septic arthritis, by contrast, usually follows bacteremia. Deeper bursae, such as the subacromial and trochanteric bursae, are more often inflamed by overuse than infected. Preserved knee motion without an effusion helps tell bursitis from septic arthritis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Patellar osteomyelitis is rare and nothing here suggests it. Her skin abrasions over the bursa explain the infection.","The prepatellar bursa does not communicate with the knee joint, and her knee has no effusion.","Hematogenous seeding is the usual route for septic arthritis and for childhood osteomyelitis. It is uncommon for superficial bursae."],"study":{"topics":["Bursitis by site and cause","Prepatellar bursa anatomy"],"comps":["MS-13-B2","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C05-Q2","stem":"The bursal fluid contains IgG antibodies against the organism, yet neutrophils ingest the bacteria poorly. Which of the following bacterial products best explains this finding?","options":["M protein","IgA protease","Exfoliative toxin","Protein A"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase and coagulase positive identify Staphylococcus aureus.","IgG opsonizes bacteria when its Fc region binds Fc receptors on neutrophils.","Staphylococcal protein A binds the Fc region of IgG, so the antibody is held the wrong way round and cannot opsonize."],"why":"Protein A is a cell wall protein of Staphylococcus aureus that binds the Fc portion of IgG. Antibody bound by its Fc end cannot link the bacterium to neutrophil Fc receptors, and complement activation is reduced, so phagocytosis fails. Other factors help S. aureus cause bone and joint infection: adhesins that bind collagen and fibronectin, and biofilm on prosthetic hardware. Together they make it the most common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis.","whyNot":["M protein is the main antiphagocytic protein of Streptococcus pyogenes, a catalase-negative organism that grows in chains.","IgA protease is made by Neisseria, Haemophilus influenzae, and Streptococcus pneumoniae. It cleaves IgA at mucosal surfaces and does not act on IgG.","Exfoliative toxin from S. aureus splits desmoglein 1 and causes scalded skin syndrome. It does not block opsonization.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Staphylococcus aureus virulence factors"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C05-Q3","stem":"If the infection had spread into her knee joint, the articular cartilage could have been destroyed within days. Which of the following most directly causes this rapid cartilage loss?","options":["Immune complexes activating complement in synovium","Proteases and oxidants released by neutrophils","Pannus with RANKL-activated osteoclasts","CD8 T cells lysing chondrocytes"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Septic arthritis fills the joint with neutrophils, much like the 52,000 cells/mm3 in her bursal fluid.","Activated neutrophils release elastase, collagenases, and reactive oxygen species.","These degrade cartilage matrix within days, much faster than any chronic autoimmune process."],"why":"In septic arthritis, bacteria in the joint cavity recruit large numbers of neutrophils. Neutrophil elastase, matrix metalloproteinases, and reactive oxygen species break down aggrecan and type II collagen. Cytokines such as IL-1 and TNF also stimulate chondrocytes to release their own proteases. Because cartilage has no blood supply and repairs poorly, damage can be permanent within days, which is why an infected joint is drained urgently.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition with complement activation is type III hypersensitivity, as in SLE arthritis, which is usually nonerosive.","Correct.","Pannus with RANKL-driven osteoclasts erodes cartilage and bone in rheumatoid arthritis over months to years.","CD8 T cells kill cells that show foreign peptides on class I molecules, as in viral infection. They are not the main effectors in acute bacterial joint infection."],"study":{"topics":["Cartilage destruction in septic arthritis"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C05-Q4","stem":"After drainage and antibiotics, the skin incision over the bursa heals. Which of the following best describes the main collagen in the granulation tissue at 1 week and in the mature scar months later?","options":["Type III at 1 week, replaced by type I in the scar","Type II at 1 week, replaced by type I in the scar","Type I at 1 week, replaced by type III in the scar","Type IV at 1 week, replaced by type III in the scar"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Granulation tissue forms first, with new capillaries and fibroblasts laying down a thin, flexible collagen.","That early collagen is type III, the type in reticular fibers and vessel walls.","During remodeling, fibroblasts replace it with stronger type I collagen, the type in tendon, ligament, and bone."],"why":"Early granulation tissue is rich in type III collagen, which forms thin fibers suited to a new, vascular scaffold. Over weeks to months, matrix metalloproteinases remove it and fibroblasts lay down type I collagen, which has high tensile strength. A mature scar reaches only about 70 to 80 percent of the strength of normal skin. Type II collagen belongs to hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus, and type IV to basement membranes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Type II collagen is found in hyaline cartilage, the vitreous, and the nucleus pulposus, not in healing skin.","This reverses the real sequence. Type I collagen is the strong, late collagen of a mature scar.","Type IV collagen forms basement membranes. It does not make up the bulk of granulation tissue or scar."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types and their tissues","Collagen in wound healing"],"comps":["MS-6"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C06","system":"MS","dx":"Fibromyalgia","codes":["MS-13-B3"],"case":"A 43-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 10 months of pain in her neck, shoulders, back, hips, and legs on both sides of the body. She also reports fatigue, waking unrefreshed despite 8 hours in bed, and trouble concentrating at work. Physical examination shows tenderness to light pressure at many soft tissue sites. The joints show no swelling, muscle strength is 5/5 throughout, and deep tendon reflexes are 2+ and symmetric. Laboratory studies show: erythrocyte sedimentation rate 8 mm/h (0 to 20), C-reactive protein 0.3 mg/dL (0 to 0.8), creatine kinase 88 U/L (30 to 170), thyroid-stimulating hormone 1.9 μU/mL (0.5 to 4.5), and a normal complete blood count. Diagnosis: fibromyalgia.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C06-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her widespread pain?","options":["Denervation of muscle fibers by motor neuron loss","CD8 T cell infiltration of skeletal muscle fibers","Increased central amplification of sensory input","Inflammation of bursae around the shoulders and hips"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B3","obj":"MS-13-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Widespread pain for more than 3 months with fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, and poor concentration fits fibromyalgia.","Normal ESR, CRP, CK, strength, and joints rule out inflammation and muscle injury.","The pain comes from increased sensitivity of central pain pathways, so ordinary pressure is felt as pain."],"why":"Fibromyalgia is a disorder of central pain sensitization. Pathways in the spinal cord and brain amplify incoming signals, and descending pain inhibition is weaker, so pressure that should feel normal is painful. There is no tissue inflammation or muscle damage, which is why inflammatory markers, CK, and the joint examination are normal. It is more common in women and is linked with poor sleep, fatigue, and mood disorders.","whyNot":["Motor neuron loss causes weakness, atrophy, fasciculations, and reflex changes. Her strength and reflexes are normal.","CD8 T cell invasion of muscle fibers is polymyositis, which causes proximal weakness and a high CK.","Correct.","Bursitis around the shoulder and hip girdles is typical of polymyalgia rheumatica, which occurs after age 50 with a high ESR."],"study":{"topics":["Central sensitization in fibromyalgia"],"comps":["MS-13-B3","MS-10"]}},{"id":"MS-C06-Q2","stem":"During the neurologic examination, tapping her patellar tendon produces brisk extension of the knee. Which of the following receptors and spinal cord segments mediate this reflex?","options":["Golgi tendon organ with group Ib afferents, mainly L4","Golgi tendon organ with group Ib afferents, mainly S1","Muscle spindle with group Ia afferents, mainly L4","Muscle spindle with group Ia afferents, mainly S1"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["Tapping the tendon briefly stretches the quadriceps.","Stretch is sensed by muscle spindles, whose group Ia afferents synapse directly on alpha motor neurons to the same muscle.","The patellar reflex runs through L2 to L4, mainly L4. The Achilles reflex tests S1."],"why":"The patellar reflex is a monosynaptic stretch reflex. A tendon tap stretches the quadriceps and its intrafusal fibers, Ia afferents fire, and they excite alpha motor neurons in the L2 to L4 segments, mainly L4, so the quadriceps contracts. Gamma motor neurons set spindle sensitivity. Golgi tendon organs sit at the muscle-tendon junction, sense tension through Ib afferents, and inhibit the same muscle. Normal reflexes in fibromyalgia fit the absence of nerve or muscle disease.","whyNot":["Golgi tendon organs sense tension and inhibit the same muscle through Ib afferents. They do not drive the knee jerk.","Both parts are wrong. Ib afferents from tendon organs mediate inhibition, and S1 serves the Achilles reflex.","Correct.","Muscle spindles and Ia fibers are right, but S1 is the level of the Achilles reflex."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs","Reflexes and their root levels"],"comps":["MS-10"]}},{"id":"MS-C06-Q3","stem":"She begins a graded aerobic walking and cycling program. After 3 months, which of the following changes in her leg muscles is most likely?","options":["Higher glycolytic enzyme activity in type II fibers","Greater mitochondrial and capillary density","Hypertrophy of type II fibers with more myofibrils","Conversion of type I fibers into type IIx fibers"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Endurance exercise is sustained, low-force work that depends on oxidative metabolism.","Type I fibers are slow, oxidative, and fatigue resistant, and trained muscle adapts by increasing oxidative capacity.","Resistance training, not aerobic training, causes type II fiber hypertrophy."],"why":"Repeated aerobic exercise increases mitochondrial number and size, oxidative enzymes, myoglobin, and capillary density in trained muscle, mainly in type I and type IIa fibers. These changes let the muscle burn more fatty acid and produce less lactate at a given workload. Heavy resistance training instead enlarges type II fibers by adding myofibrils. Type I fibers look red because of their myoglobin and dense capillary supply.","whyNot":["Endurance training raises oxidative capacity. Sprint and resistance training are the stimuli for higher glycolytic capacity.","Correct.","Type II fiber hypertrophy is the main response to heavy resistance training.","Endurance training shifts fibers toward more oxidative types, such as type IIx toward type IIa. It does not convert type I fibers into type IIx."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle fiber types","Training adaptations in muscle"],"comps":["MS-7","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C06-Q4","stem":"Her antinuclear antibody test is positive at a titer of 1:80, and she is worried that she has systemic lupus erythematosus. Which of the following antibody results would be most specific for that diagnosis?","options":["Anti-Scl-70","Anti-Jo-1","Anti-CCP","Anti-Smith"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["A low-titer ANA is common in healthy people, especially women, and is not specific.","Disease-specific antibodies are used to confirm a suspected connective tissue disease.","Anti-Smith and anti-dsDNA are highly specific for SLE. The other choices point to other diseases."],"why":"ANA is sensitive for SLE but not specific, and a titer of 1:80 is found in more than 1 in 10 healthy people. Anti-Smith antibodies, against small nuclear ribonucleoproteins, are highly specific for SLE, though only a minority of patients have them. Anti-dsDNA is also specific and tracks disease activity and lupus nephritis. Her normal inflammatory markers and examination already make SLE unlikely.","whyNot":["Anti-Scl-70 (anti-topoisomerase I) marks diffuse systemic sclerosis, with skin thickening and interstitial lung disease.","Anti-Jo-1 marks inflammatory myositis with interstitial lung disease.","Anti-CCP is the most specific antibody for rheumatoid arthritis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Autoantibodies in rheumatic diseases"],"comps":["MS-11"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C07","system":"MS","dx":"Acute gout of the first metatarsophalangeal joint","codes":["MS-13-B4"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of sudden, severe pain in his right great toe that woke him at 3 AM. The evening before, he drank several beers and ate a large steak dinner. He has hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse 92/min, respirations 14/min, and blood pressure 148/88 mm Hg. The first metatarsophalangeal joint is red, hot, swollen, and too tender to touch. Serum urate is 9.2 mg/dL (3.5 to 7.2). Aspiration of the joint yields cloudy fluid with 28,000 leukocytes/mm3, mostly neutrophils. Gram stain and culture are negative. Diagnosis: acute gout of the first metatarsophalangeal joint.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C07-Q1","stem":"Polarized light microscopy of the joint fluid is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Needle-shaped crystals with strong negative birefringence","Rhomboid crystals with weak positive birefringence","Needle-shaped crystals with strong positive birefringence","Flat rectangular plates with notched corners"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B4","obj":"MS-13-B.6","dok":2,"approach":["Sudden, severe first MTP pain after alcohol and a large meat meal, with high urate and a thiazide, points to gout.","Monosodium urate forms long, thin needles.","Urate crystals are strongly negatively birefringent: yellow when parallel to the compensator axis and blue when perpendicular."],"why":"Gout is confirmed by finding monosodium urate crystals in joint fluid, often inside neutrophils. They are needle shaped and strongly negatively birefringent, so they look yellow when their long axis lies parallel to the compensator and blue when perpendicular. Other features of gout include tophi (urate deposits in soft tissue), punched-out bone erosions with overhanging edges, and uric acid kidney stones. Serum urate can be normal during an attack, so crystals are the standard for diagnosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Weakly positively birefringent rhomboid crystals are calcium pyrophosphate, seen in pseudogout.","Urate crystals are needle shaped but negatively birefringent. Strong positive birefringence does not fit urate.","Flat plates with notched corners are cholesterol crystals, found in chronic effusions such as long-standing rheumatoid arthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Gout findings and urate crystals"],"comps":["MS-13-B4"]}},{"id":"MS-C07-Q2","stem":"Before this attack, his 24-hour urinary uric acid excretion on a regular diet was 410 mg/day (people who overproduce urate typically excrete more than 800 mg/day). Which of the following best explains his hyperuricemia?","options":["Increased cell turnover releasing nucleic acid purines","Reduced renal urate excretion from thiazide therapy","Excess urate production from glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency","Increased de novo purine synthesis from HGPRT deficiency"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-B4","obj":"MS-13-B.5","dok":3,"approach":["Compare urinary uric acid with serum urate. A high serum level with normal urinary output means the kidney is not clearing urate well.","About 90 percent of gout comes from underexcretion.","Thiazides cause volume contraction and compete with urate for tubular secretion, so net reabsorption rises."],"why":"Uric acid is the end product of purine breakdown: xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid. Serum urate rises from overproduction or, far more often, from reduced renal excretion. Thiazide and loop diuretics cause volume contraction and compete with urate at organic anion transporters in the proximal tubule. A normal 24-hour urinary uric acid with a high serum urate shows underexcretion here, and alcohol and purine-rich meat added to the load that night.","whyNot":["High cell turnover, as in tumor lysis or myeloproliferative disease, causes overproduction with high urinary uric acid.","Correct.","Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (von Gierke disease) raises urate and appears in childhood with hypoglycemia and hepatomegaly.","HGPRT deficiency (Lesch-Nyhan syndrome) blocks purine salvage, raises de novo synthesis, and causes overproduction with high urinary uric acid, usually in boys with neurologic disease."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of hyperuricemia","Purine breakdown and xanthine oxidase"],"comps":["MS-13-B4","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C07-Q3","stem":"Neutrophils fill the joint within hours after crystals are shed into it. Which of the following events in synovial macrophages most directly starts this inflammation?","options":["Sensitized CD4 T cells releasing interferon gamma","Immune complexes fixing complement in vessel walls","NLRP3 inflammasome activation releasing IL-1 beta","IgE cross-linking on mast cells releasing histamine"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Urate crystals are a danger signal recognized by innate immunity, with no need for prior sensitization.","Macrophages take up the crystals, which activates the NLRP3 inflammasome and caspase-1.","Caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1 beta to IL-1 beta, which recruits neutrophils and drives the rapid, intense attack."],"why":"Synovial macrophages take up monosodium urate crystals, which activate the NLRP3 inflammasome. This complex activates caspase-1, which cleaves pro-IL-1 beta into active IL-1 beta. IL-1 beta activates endothelium, triggers more cytokines, and brings in large numbers of neutrophils within hours. Because this is innate immunity, no prior exposure is needed, which explains the sudden onset of attacks.","whyNot":["Sensitized CD4 T cells releasing interferon gamma is type IV hypersensitivity, which develops over days.","Immune complex deposition with complement is type III hypersensitivity, as in SLE or serum sickness.","Correct.","IgE-mediated mast cell release is type I hypersensitivity, as in allergy and anaphylaxis."],"study":{"topics":["NLRP3 inflammasome in gout"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-B4"]}},{"id":"MS-C07-Q4","stem":"A 74-year-old woman seen the same day has an acutely swollen knee. Her joint fluid shows rhomboid crystals with weak positive birefringence, and a knee radiograph shows linear calcification of the menisci. Which of the following conditions is most strongly associated with her disorder?","options":["Tumor lysis syndrome","Thiazide diuretic use","Hemochromatosis","Lesch-Nyhan syndrome"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B","obj":"MS-13-B.15","dok":3,"approach":["Rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals with chondrocalcinosis identify calcium pyrophosphate deposition, or pseudogout.","Pseudogout usually affects older adults, most often the knee and wrist.","Its classic metabolic associations are hemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, and hypomagnesemia. The other choices raise urate and cause gout."],"why":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals form in cartilage and cause chondrocalcinosis, the linear calcification of menisci and articular cartilage seen on radiographs. Acute attacks mimic gout but usually strike the knee or wrist of older adults. Hemochromatosis and primary hyperparathyroidism are classic associations, as are hypomagnesemia and hypophosphatasia. Gout instead favors the first MTP joint and is linked to hyperuricemia.","whyNot":["Tumor lysis releases nucleic acids that raise urate and can cause gout and uric acid nephropathy.","Thiazides reduce urate excretion and promote gout. They are not a recognized cause of calcium pyrophosphate deposition.","Correct.","Lesch-Nyhan syndrome causes urate overproduction and gout."],"study":{"topics":["Pseudogout compared with gout"],"comps":["MS-13-B","MS-13-B4"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C08","system":"MS","dx":"Dermatomyositis with ovarian cancer","codes":["MS-13-B5"],"case":"A 54-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 3 months of increasing difficulty climbing stairs, rising from a chair, and lifting her arms to wash her hair. She has also had a rash on her face and hands, abdominal bloating, early satiety, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. Physical examination shows a violaceous rash on the upper eyelids with periorbital edema and raised, scaly, violet papules over the knuckles. Strength is 4-/5 in the hip flexors and deltoids and 5/5 distally. Serum creatine kinase is 2,850 U/L (30 to 170). Serum CA-125 is 410 U/mL (under 35), and imaging shows a left adnexal mass. Biopsy confirms high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma. Diagnosis: dermatomyositis associated with ovarian cancer.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C08-Q1","stem":"A deltoid muscle biopsy shows atrophy of fibers at the edges of the fascicles and inflammation around perimysial vessels. Which of the following immune mechanisms best explains this pattern?","options":["CD8 T cell lysis of fibers that express MHC class I","Granulomas formed by activated macrophages","Antibody blockade of acetylcholine receptors","Complement attack on intramuscular capillaries"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-B5","obj":"MS-13-B.7","dok":3,"approach":["Atrophy at the edges of fascicles, called perifascicular atrophy, is the signature of dermatomyositis.","In dermatomyositis, antibodies and the membrane attack complex injure endomysial capillaries, which drop out.","Fibers at the fascicle edges are farthest from the remaining blood supply, so they become ischemic and shrink."],"why":"Dermatomyositis is a complement-driven injury of small vessels. Membrane attack complex is deposited on endomysial capillaries, which are lost. The watershed fibers at the edge of each fascicle become ischemic and atrophic, giving perifascicular atrophy, with CD4 T cells and B cells around perimysial vessels. Polymyositis instead shows CD8 T cells invading fibers within the endomysium. Both cause proximal weakness and a high CK, and both can come with anti-Jo-1 antibodies and interstitial lung disease. Dermatomyositis in adults is linked to cancers such as ovarian, lung, and gastric carcinoma.","whyNot":["CD8 T cell invasion of fibers that express MHC class I is the mechanism of polymyositis, which lacks perifascicular atrophy.","Granulomas in muscle suggest sarcoidosis. They do not produce perifascicular atrophy.","Antibodies against acetylcholine receptors cause myasthenia gravis, with fatigable ptosis and a normal CK.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dermatomyositis compared with polymyositis"],"comps":["MS-13-B5","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C08-Q2","stem":"She develops difficulty starting a swallow, with coughing and nasal regurgitation of liquids. Which of the following histologic features describes the normal muscle tissue responsible for this symptom?","options":["Branched fibers with central nuclei and intercalated discs","Fusiform cells with central nuclei and no striations","Striated fibers with central nuclei and dyads","Long striated fibers with many peripheral nuclei"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.2","dok":3,"approach":["Trouble starting a swallow with nasal regurgitation points to weakness of the pharynx and upper esophagus.","These muscles are skeletal muscle, so inflammatory myositis can weaken them.","Skeletal muscle fibers are long, striated, and multinucleated, with nuclei at the periphery."],"why":"The pharyngeal constrictors and the upper third of the esophagus are skeletal muscle, the middle third is mixed, and the lower third is smooth muscle. Myositis weakens the striated muscles, so the result is difficulty starting a swallow, coughing, and nasal regurgitation. Skeletal muscle fibers are long, multinucleated cells with peripheral nuclei and triads of T tubules and sarcoplasmic reticulum. Cardiac muscle has branched cells with central nuclei and intercalated discs, and smooth muscle has fusiform cells with a central nucleus and no striations.","whyNot":["Branched fibers with central nuclei and intercalated discs are cardiac muscle.","Fusiform cells without striations are smooth muscle, found in the lower esophagus. Weakness there slows passage of food through the chest and does not impair starting a swallow.","Central nuclei with dyads describe cardiac muscle, where each T tubule pairs with one sarcoplasmic reticulum cisterna.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-B5"]}},{"id":"MS-C08-Q3","stem":"In polymyositis, by contrast, biopsy shows CD8 T cells surrounding and invading non-necrotic muscle fibers. Which of the following types of hypersensitivity best describes the injury in polymyositis?","options":["Type II, antibodies against cell surface antigens","Type IV, T cell-mediated cytotoxicity","Type III, immune complex deposition","Type I, IgE-mediated mast cell release"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Classify by effector: IgE (type I), antibody against fixed antigen (type II), immune complexes (type III), and T cells (type IV).","In polymyositis, muscle fibers display MHC class I, and CD8 T cells recognize and kill them.","Injury carried out by T cells without antibody is type IV hypersensitivity."],"why":"Type IV hypersensitivity is injury caused by T cells, either CD4 T cells that release cytokines or CD8 T cells that kill target cells directly. In polymyositis, muscle fibers up-regulate MHC class I, and CD8 T cells with perforin and granzymes invade and destroy them. Other musculoskeletal examples: rheumatic fever polyarthritis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome are type II, and SLE arthritis is type III.","whyNot":["Type II reactions use antibodies against fixed cell or matrix antigens, as in rheumatic fever and Lambert-Eaton syndrome.","Correct.","Type III reactions come from circulating immune complexes, as in SLE arthritis.","Type I reactions are immediate IgE-driven responses such as anaphylaxis."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types in musculoskeletal disease"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-B5"]}},{"id":"MS-C08-Q4","stem":"In her intact deltoid fibers, a motor nerve impulse still triggers contraction. Which of the following events directly links T-tubule depolarization to calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum in these fibers?","options":["Calcium entry through L-type channels triggering RyR2","IP3 binding to receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Dihydropyridine receptor shape change opening RyR1","Calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light chain kinase"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Skeletal muscle couples voltage to calcium release mechanically.","The dihydropyridine receptor in the T tubule acts as a voltage sensor and is physically linked to ryanodine receptor 1 in the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Calcium then binds troponin C, and SERCA later pumps it back to end the contraction."],"why":"In skeletal muscle, the action potential spreads down the T tubules and changes the shape of the dihydropyridine receptor, an L-type calcium channel that works mainly as a voltage sensor. It is mechanically coupled to ryanodine receptor 1 on the terminal cisternae, which opens and releases calcium. No calcium entry from outside the cell is required. Calcium binds troponin C, tropomyosin moves aside, and cross-bridges form. SERCA pumps calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum for relaxation.","whyNot":["Calcium-induced calcium release through L-type channels and RyR2 is how cardiac muscle couples excitation to contraction.","IP3-mediated release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum contributes to smooth muscle contraction.","Correct.","Calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light chain kinase drives smooth muscle contraction. Skeletal muscle uses troponin C."],"study":{"topics":["Excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle"],"comps":["MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C09","system":"MS","dx":"Polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis","codes":["MS-13-B6"],"case":"A 72-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 6 weeks of aching and stiffness in both shoulders, her neck, and both hips. Morning stiffness lasts about 2 hours, and she needs help getting out of bed. For 2 weeks she has also had a new right-sided headache, scalp tenderness when combing her hair, and jaw pain when chewing. Temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F). Physical examination shows painful, limited active shoulder abduction, with full strength when pain is controlled. The right temporal artery is tender and thickened with a reduced pulse. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 86 mm/h (0 to 30), C-reactive protein is 5.8 mg/dL (0 to 0.8), and serum creatine kinase is 62 U/L (30 to 170). Diagnosis: polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C09-Q1","stem":"Without prompt treatment, she is at greatest risk for permanent vision loss. Which of the following mechanisms most likely causes this complication?","options":["Occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries","Compression of the optic chiasm by a pituitary mass","Acute closure of the anterior chamber angle","Thrombosis of the central retinal vein"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B6","obj":"MS-13-B.9","dok":3,"approach":["Headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, and an abnormal temporal artery in a patient with polymyalgia rheumatica indicate giant cell arteritis.","The arteritis involves branches of the external carotid and ophthalmic arteries.","Narrowing of the short posterior ciliary arteries cuts off blood to the optic nerve head, causing ischemic optic neuropathy and sudden vision loss."],"why":"Polymyalgia rheumatica occurs in adults over 50, more often women, with shoulder and hip girdle pain and stiffness, a high ESR, and a normal CK. About 15 to 20 percent of patients also have giant cell arteritis, a granulomatous vasculitis of large and medium arteries. Narrowing of the posterior ciliary arteries, branches of the ophthalmic artery, starves the optic nerve head and causes arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy. Vision loss is sudden and often permanent.","whyNot":["Correct.","A pituitary mass compresses the chiasm and causes a slowly developing bitemporal hemianopia.","Acute angle-closure glaucoma causes a painful red eye with a fixed, mid-dilated pupil. It is not caused by arteritis.","Central retinal vein occlusion causes painless vision loss with widespread retinal hemorrhages and is linked to hypertension and diabetes."],"study":{"topics":["Polymyalgia rheumatica and giant cell arteritis","Blood supply of the optic nerve head"],"comps":["MS-13-B6"]}},{"id":"MS-C09-Q2","stem":"A colleague suggests polymyositis as an alternative diagnosis. Which of the following findings in this patient argues most strongly against inflammatory myositis?","options":["Elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Onset after the age of 50 years","Symmetric proximal girdle involvement","Normal serum creatine kinase"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B6","obj":"MS-13-B.8","dok":2,"approach":["Inflammatory myositis destroys muscle fibers, which releases creatine kinase into the blood.","Polymyalgia rheumatica inflames bursae, tendon sheaths, and joints around the girdles, and spares the muscle fibers.","A normal CK with full strength points away from myositis."],"why":"Polymyositis and dermatomyositis cause true proximal weakness from myofiber necrosis, so CK is high, often many times normal, and biopsy shows inflammatory infiltrates. In polymyalgia rheumatica, the apparent weakness comes from pain, and CK and muscle biopsy are normal. Steroid myopathy is another cause of proximal weakness with a normal CK, but it is painless and follows corticosteroid use.","whyNot":["ESR can be high in both conditions, so it does not separate them.","Polymyalgia rheumatica requires age over 50, but myositis also occurs at this age.","Both disorders affect the shoulder and hip girdles on both sides.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Polymyalgia rheumatica compared with inflammatory myositis"],"comps":["MS-13-B6","MS-13-B5"]}},{"id":"MS-C09-Q3","stem":"Ultrasound of her shoulder shows inflammation of the subacromial bursa around the tendon that passes under the acromion and starts abduction of the arm. Which of the following nerves innervates the muscle of this tendon?","options":["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Axillary nerve","Upper subscapular nerve"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["The tendon under the acromion that starts abduction belongs to supraspinatus.","Supraspinatus starts abduction for about the first 15 degrees, and deltoid takes over after that.","Supraspinatus and infraspinatus are both supplied by the suprascapular nerve."],"why":"Supraspinatus runs from the supraspinous fossa under the acromion to the greater tubercle. It starts abduction and is supplied by the suprascapular nerve (C5 to C6), as is infraspinatus, a lateral rotator. Teres minor, also a lateral rotator, is supplied by the axillary nerve, and subscapularis, a medial rotator, by the subscapular nerves. Its course under the acromion makes supraspinatus the rotator cuff tendon most often injured, and the subacromial bursa next to it is often inflamed in polymyalgia rheumatica.","whyNot":["Correct.","The long thoracic nerve supplies serratus anterior. Injury causes a winged scapula.","The axillary nerve supplies deltoid, which abducts the arm after the first 15 degrees, and teres minor.","The subscapular nerves supply subscapularis, a medial rotator, and teres major."],"study":{"topics":["Rotator cuff muscles and nerves"],"comps":["MS-5","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C09-Q4","stem":"She is treated with high-dose prednisone. Five months later her girdle pain has resolved, but she has painless, progressive difficulty rising from a chair. Serum creatine kinase is 48 U/L (30 to 170). A muscle biopsy would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Endomysial lymphocytes with necrotic fibers","Ragged red fibers on Gomori trichrome stain","Type II fiber atrophy without inflammation","Grouped atrophy with fiber type grouping"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Painless proximal weakness with a normal CK after months of high-dose corticosteroids suggests steroid myopathy.","Corticosteroids increase protein breakdown and reduce protein synthesis, mostly in fast glycolytic fibers.","The biopsy shows selective type II fiber atrophy with no necrosis or inflammation, the same pattern seen with disuse."],"why":"Long-term glucocorticoids cause catabolic muscle wasting that falls mostly on type II (fast, glycolytic) fibers. The result is painless proximal weakness, especially of the hip flexors, with a normal CK because fibers shrink without dying. Biopsy shows type II fiber atrophy without inflammation. Neurogenic disease instead shows grouped atrophy and fiber type grouping, and inflammatory myopathy shows necrosis and infiltrates with a high CK.","whyNot":["Endomysial lymphocytes with necrotic fibers indicate inflammatory myositis, which would raise CK.","Ragged red fibers reflect abnormal mitochondria in mitochondrial myopathies such as MERRF.","Correct.","Grouped atrophy and fiber type grouping mean denervation and reinnervation, as in spinal muscular atrophy, usually with fasciculations."],"study":{"topics":["Steroid myopathy","Neurogenic compared with myopathic weakness"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-13-B6"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C10","system":"MS","dx":"Reactive arthritis after Chlamydia trachomatis urethritis","codes":["MS-13-B7"],"case":"A 24-year-old man comes to the physician because of 5 days of pain and swelling in his right knee and left ankle. Three weeks ago he had dysuria and a urethral discharge, and a nucleic acid amplification test of urine was positive for Chlamydia trachomatis. He also reports red, irritated eyes and pain in both heels. Temperature is 37.8 °C (100.0 °F). Physical examination shows bilateral conjunctival injection, a warm knee effusion, a swollen left ankle, and tenderness at the Achilles tendon and plantar fascia insertions. Knee aspiration yields fluid with 16,000 leukocytes/mm3, mostly neutrophils, and no crystals. Gram stain and culture are negative. HLA-B27 testing is positive. Diagnosis: reactive arthritis after Chlamydia trachomatis urethritis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the arthritis in this patient?","options":["Replication of viable bacteria within the synovium","Immune reaction after a distant infection","Autoantibodies against citrullinated synovial proteins","Deposition of crystals within the joint cavity"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-B7","obj":"MS-13-B.10","dok":3,"approach":["Arthritis that starts weeks after urethritis, with conjunctivitis and enthesitis, fits reactive arthritis.","The synovial culture is negative and no crystals are present, so the joint is neither infected nor affected by crystal disease.","In HLA-B27 positive hosts, the immune response to bacterial antigens from a distant site drives a sterile arthritis."],"why":"Reactive arthritis is a sterile inflammatory arthritis that follows infection elsewhere, usually Chlamydia trachomatis urethritis or enteric infection with Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, or Campylobacter. Bacterial antigens, and sometimes nonviable bacterial fragments, reach the joint, and HLA-B27 carriers mount a persistent immune response. Typical features are asymmetric arthritis of the lower limbs, conjunctivitis, urethritis, enthesitis, dactylitis, and keratoderma blennorrhagicum on the soles.","whyNot":["Live bacteria in the joint is septic arthritis, which would give a positive Gram stain or culture. In sexually active young adults, Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a common cause.","Correct.","Antibodies to citrullinated peptides drive rheumatoid arthritis, a symmetric small joint arthritis linked to HLA-DR4.","Crystal arthritis (gout or pseudogout) is excluded because no crystals are present."],"study":{"topics":["Reactive arthritis pathogenesis","HLA-B27 associated arthritis"],"comps":["MS-13-B7","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C10-Q2","stem":"A 34-year-old kindergarten teacher develops symmetric pain and swelling of the MCP joints, PIP joints, and wrists. Two weeks earlier, several children in her class had bright red cheeks followed by a lacy rash on the arms. Which of the following is the most likely cause of her arthritis?","options":["Recent rubella vaccination","Parvovirus B19 infection","HLA-B27 associated reactive arthritis","Disseminated Neisseria gonorrhoeae"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.7","dok":2,"approach":["Bright red cheeks followed by a lacy rash in children is erythema infectiosum, or fifth disease.","In adults, especially women, parvovirus B19 often causes a symmetric small joint arthritis.","Symmetric hand arthritis after exposure to children with fifth disease points to parvovirus."],"why":"Parvovirus B19 causes fifth disease in children. Adults, especially women, often develop a symmetric arthritis of the hands, wrists, and knees that can mimic rheumatoid arthritis and usually resolves within weeks. Rubella infection, and less often rubella vaccination, causes a similar short-lived arthralgia in adult women. Reactive arthritis follows enteric or chlamydial infection in HLA-B27 carriers and is usually asymmetric and in the lower limbs.","whyNot":["Rubella vaccination can cause brief arthralgia in adult women, but no vaccine is mentioned and her exposure fits fifth disease.","Correct.","Reactive arthritis is usually asymmetric and affects large lower-limb joints after genitourinary or enteric infection.","Disseminated gonococcal infection causes tenosynovitis, pustular skin lesions, and migratory arthralgia or purulent monoarthritis in sexually active young adults."],"study":{"topics":["Postinfectious and postvaccine arthritis"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-B7"]}},{"id":"MS-C10-Q3","stem":"His 52-year-old father has scaly plaques on the elbows, pitted fingernails, and swelling of several distal interphalangeal joints. A radiograph of the father's hands would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Periarticular osteopenia with erosions at the MCP joints","Osteophytes with joint space narrowing at the DIP joints","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges","Pencil-in-cup erosions at the DIP joints"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B","obj":"MS-13-B.13","dok":3,"approach":["Skin plaques, nail pitting, and DIP arthritis identify psoriatic arthritis.","Psoriatic arthritis combines erosion with new bone formation, and severe erosion at the DIP joint gives a pencil-in-cup shape.","Rheumatoid arthritis spares the DIP joints and shows periarticular osteopenia with marginal erosions at the MCP and PIP joints."],"why":"Psoriatic arthritis is a seronegative spondyloarthropathy. It typically involves the DIP joints, causes dactylitis (sausage digits) and nail pitting, and can involve the spine, a pattern linked to HLA-B27. Radiographs show erosions with new bone formation nearby, and advanced disease produces pencil-in-cup deformities. Rheumatoid arthritis is a symmetric MCP and PIP arthritis that spares the DIP joints, with periarticular osteopenia, marginal erosions, and positive rheumatoid factor or anti-CCP.","whyNot":["Periarticular osteopenia with MCP erosions is the pattern of rheumatoid arthritis, which spares the DIP joints.","Osteophytes at the DIP joints (Heberden nodes) mark osteoarthritis, which does not cause nail pitting or skin plaques.","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges are typical of chronic tophaceous gout.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Psoriatic arthritis compared with rheumatoid arthritis"],"comps":["MS-13-B","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C10-Q4","stem":"His heel pain arises at the attachment of the plantar aponeurosis to the calcaneus. Long-term failure of this support, together with the spring ligament and tibialis posterior, would most likely produce which of the following deformities?","options":["Pes planus from medial longitudinal arch collapse","Pes cavus from an exaggerated longitudinal arch","Hallux valgus from lateral deviation of the great toe","Talipes equinovarus with plantarflexion and inversion"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["The plantar aponeurosis acts like a tie-beam between the calcaneus and the toes, holding up the longitudinal arches.","The medial longitudinal arch is also held by the spring (plantar calcaneonavicular) ligament under the head of the talus and by the tibialis posterior tendon.","When these supports fail, the medial arch collapses and the foot becomes flat."],"why":"The foot has medial and lateral longitudinal arches and a transverse arch. The medial longitudinal arch is the highest and depends on the plantar aponeurosis, the spring ligament under the head of the talus, and the tibialis posterior tendon. When these weaken, the arch collapses and the foot becomes flat (pes planus), often with the heel turned outward. Pes cavus is the opposite, an abnormally high arch.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pes cavus is an abnormally high arch, often from neuromuscular disease such as Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease. Loss of arch support does the opposite.","Hallux valgus is lateral deviation of the great toe at the first MTP joint, linked to narrow footwear. It does not come from loss of arch support.","Talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) is a congenital deformity present at birth."],"study":{"topics":["Arches of the foot","Pes planus"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-13-B7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C11","system":"MS","dx":"Lateral epicondylitis (tendinopathy of the extensor carpi radialis brevis origin)","codes":["MS-13-B8"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of pain over the outer aspect of his right elbow. He installs drywall and uses a power screwdriver for most of the workday. The pain is worse when he grips tools or lifts a cup with the palm facing down. He recalls no injury. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows tenderness 1 cm distal to the right lateral epicondyle. Resisted extension of the wrist with the elbow extended reproduces the pain. Elbow range of motion is full, and there is no swelling or warmth. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 8 mm/h (normal 0 to 15). Ultrasonography shows a thickened, hypoechoic common extensor tendon origin. Diagnosis: lateral epicondylitis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C11-Q1","stem":"Eight months later, his symptoms persist despite rest and physical therapy, and the diseased tendon origin is surgically debrided. Which of the following findings is most likely on microscopic examination of the excised tissue?","options":["Disorganized collagen, new vessels, and scant inflammation","Urate crystal deposits rimmed by foreign body giant cells","Sheets of neutrophils with liquefactive necrosis of the tendon","Noncaseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B8","obj":"MS-13-B.11","dok":3,"approach":["Note the clues: months of overuse pain, no warmth or swelling, and a normal sedimentation rate.","Recognize chronic tendinopathy as a degenerative process (tendinosis) more than an inflammatory one.","Pick the histology of failed repair: disorganized collagen, fibroblasts, and small vessels with few inflammatory cells."],"why":"Lateral epicondylitis is overuse tendinopathy at the origin of the extensor carpi radialis brevis. Repeated small tears outpace repair, so the tendon shows tendinosis: collagen fibers that are thin, loosely packed, and out of line, more ground substance, many fibroblasts, and ingrowth of small vessels. Inflammatory cells are scarce, which is why the term epicondylitis is a misnomer and why his sedimentation rate is normal.","whyNot":["Correct.","Urate crystals with giant cells form a tophus in chronic gout. Gout causes episodic, warm, inflamed joints, which he does not have.","Neutrophils with liquefactive necrosis describe an abscess, as in septic bursitis or a tendon sheath infection. He has no warmth, swelling, or systemic signs.","Noncaseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis or a foreign body reaction. Overuse tendinopathy does not form granulomas."],"study":{"topics":["Tendinosis histology","Lateral and medial epicondylitis"],"comps":["MS-13-B8","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C11-Q2","stem":"Analysis of the debrided tendon shows that much of its normal collagen has been replaced by thinner fibrils of the collagen type that predominates in early granulation tissue. This collagen type is also the main component of which of the following?","options":["Lamellar bone of the lateral epicondyle","Reticular fiber network of lymph nodes","Hyaline cartilage of the articular surface","Lamina densa of epithelial basement membranes"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the collagen of early granulation tissue as type III.","Recall where type III collagen is normally found: reticular fibers, blood vessel walls, and early scar.","Match type III collagen to the reticular framework of lymphoid organs."],"why":"Healthy tendon is built mostly of thick, parallel type I collagen fibrils, which give it high tensile strength. Repair tissue in tendinopathy contains more type III collagen, which forms thinner, weaker fibrils, the same collagen laid down first in granulation tissue. Type III collagen also forms reticular fibers, the fine supporting mesh of lymph nodes, spleen, bone marrow, and liver, and it is abundant in blood vessel walls.","whyNot":["Bone matrix is mainly type I collagen, the same collagen that normally makes up healthy tendon.","Correct.","Hyaline cartilage contains mainly type II collagen, which is also found in the nucleus pulposus and vitreous humor.","The lamina densa of basement membranes is made of type IV collagen, which forms a sheet-like network. Defects in type IV collagen cause Alport syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types and tissues","Granulation tissue and scar"],"comps":["MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C11-Q3","stem":"He asks why the tendon is still painful even though he took 6 weeks off work. Which of the following features of the tendon best explains its slow healing?","options":["Continuous bathing of the tendon in synovial fluid","A matrix built mainly of type II collagen","Sparse blood supply and low cell density","Absence of resident collagen-producing cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that repair needs cells to make new matrix and blood to deliver nutrients and more cells.","Tendons have few cells (tenocytes) and a poor blood supply, especially near the bony origin.","Choose the option that combines low vascularity with low cellularity."],"why":"Tendon is dense regular connective tissue with relatively few tenocytes (specialized fibroblasts) spread between thick collagen bundles. Its blood supply enters mainly at the muscle junction, the bone attachment, and the surrounding paratenon, and the region near the bony origin is poorly vascular. With few cells to make new matrix and little blood to bring nutrients and repair cells, tendon heals over months. Articular cartilage has no blood vessels at all and heals even more poorly.","whyNot":["Bathing in synovial fluid hampers healing of intra-articular structures such as the anterior cruciate ligament. The common extensor origin lies outside the elbow joint.","Type II collagen is the main collagen of hyaline cartilage. Tendon matrix is mainly type I collagen.","Correct.","Tendons do contain collagen-producing cells, the tenocytes. They are few in number, so repair is slow, but they are present."],"study":{"topics":["Tendon structure and blood supply","Healing of tendon and cartilage"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-H"]}},{"id":"MS-C11-Q4","stem":"To measure grip strength, the physician asks him to squeeze a dynamometer with the wrist held first in slight extension and then in full flexion. Grip force is much lower with the wrist fully flexed, on both the affected and the unaffected side. Which of the following best explains the lower force with the wrist flexed?","options":["Finger flexors contract at a higher velocity, which lowers force","Finger flexor sarcomeres are too short for optimal filament overlap","Finger flexor sarcomeres are stretched beyond optimal filament overlap","Fewer finger flexor motor units can be recruited in this position"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["The long finger flexors cross the front of the wrist, so wrist flexion shortens them.","Apply the length-tension relationship: force is greatest at a sarcomere length with optimal actin and myosin overlap.","At very short lengths, thin filaments overlap each other and thick filaments press on the Z discs, so fewer cross-bridges form."],"why":"The flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus pass in front of the wrist. Flexing the wrist shortens them before the grip starts, so their sarcomeres work below optimal length, where thin filaments overlap and fewer cross-bridges can form. This is called active insufficiency. During a normal grip, the wrist extensors, including the extensor carpi radialis brevis, hold the wrist slightly extended to keep the finger flexors near optimal length, which is why gripping loads his painful tendon.","whyNot":["Velocity lowers force only when a muscle is shortening. A dynamometer grip is nearly isometric, so velocity is close to zero in both wrist positions.","Correct.","Overstretching reduces overlap between thick and thin filaments and lowers force, but wrist flexion shortens the finger flexors. Wrist extension combined with finger extension would stretch them.","Motor unit recruitment depends on the drive from the motor neuron pool and does not fall because of wrist position. The loss of force appears on the healthy side too."],"study":{"topics":["Length-tension relationship","Active insufficiency of finger flexors"],"comps":["MS-7","MS-5"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C12","system":"MS","dx":"Autosomal recessive osteopetrosis from carbonic anhydrase II deficiency","codes":["MS-13-C1"],"case":"An 18-month-old boy is brought to the physician because of a fracture of the right femur after he fell from a sitting position. He has had poor weight gain since infancy. His parents are first cousins. His length and weight are below the 3rd percentile. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium concentration of 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), chloride 114 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106), and bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 28), with a urine pH of 6.8. Radiographs show diffusely increased bone density and a bone-within-bone appearance of the vertebrae. CT of the head shows calcifications in the basal ganglia. Diagnosis: autosomal recessive osteopetrosis caused by carbonic anhydrase II deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C12-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the increased bone density in this patient?","options":["Osteoclast precursors cannot form because RANKL is absent","Osteoblasts lay down excess osteoid that mineralizes too rapidly","Osteoclasts cannot acidify the space beneath the ruffled border","Excess calcitonin secretion suppresses osteoclast activity"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-C1","obj":"MS-13-C.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the reaction carbonic anhydrase II catalyzes: carbon dioxide and water to carbonic acid, a source of hydrogen ions.","Osteoclasts pump these hydrogen ions into the resorption pit to dissolve hydroxyapatite.","Without the enzyme, bone is not resorbed and accumulates, even though osteoclasts are present."],"why":"Osteoclasts dissolve bone mineral by secreting acid into the sealed space under the ruffled border (Howship lacuna). Carbonic anhydrase II in the osteoclast makes the hydrogen ions that the vacuolar proton pump moves into this space. Without it, mineral is not dissolved, old bone and calcified cartilage are not removed, and the skeleton becomes dense but brittle. The same enzyme is needed in the renal tubules, which explains his normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with an inappropriately alkaline urine.","whyNot":["RANKL deficiency is a real but rare cause of osteopetrosis in which osteoclasts are nearly absent. It does not cause renal tubular acidosis, which points to carbonic anhydrase II deficiency here.","Osteopetrosis is a disease of too little resorption. Osteoblast bone formation is not the primary problem.","Correct.","Calcitonin has only a minor role in human bone, and calcitonin excess, as in medullary thyroid carcinoma, does not cause osteopetrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Osteoclast acid secretion","Causes of osteopetrosis"],"comps":["MS-13-C1","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C12-Q2","stem":"At the age of 3 years, he has trouble finding toys across a room. His pupils react sluggishly to light, and funduscopic examination shows pale optic discs. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his visual loss?","options":["Compression of the optic nerves in narrowed bony canals","Calcification of the occipital cortex causing cortical blindness","Autoimmune demyelination of the optic nerves","Retinal ganglion cell loss from chronic metabolic acidosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-C1","obj":"MS-13-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["Pale optic discs and a sluggish pupillary light reflex localize the problem to the optic nerves.","In osteopetrosis, unresorbed bone narrows the skull foramina and canals.","Link narrowing of the optic canals to compression and atrophy of the optic nerves."],"why":"Bone that cannot be remodeled keeps thickening, so the canals and foramina of the skull base become narrow. Nerves passing through them are compressed, causing optic atrophy and vision loss, hearing loss, and facial nerve palsy. Encroachment of bone on the marrow space can also cause anemia or pancytopenia with extramedullary hematopoiesis and hepatosplenomegaly. These complications are most frequent and most severe in the infantile autosomal recessive forms.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cortical blindness spares the pupillary light reflex and leaves the optic discs normal, because the lesion lies behind the lateral geniculate body.","Optic neuritis from demyelination usually affects older children and adults and is often painful. It is unrelated to osteopetrosis.","Metabolic acidosis from renal tubular acidosis affects growth and bone, but it does not destroy retinal ganglion cells."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of osteopetrosis","Skull foramina and cranial nerves"],"comps":["MS-13-C1"]}},{"id":"MS-C12-Q3","stem":"A bone biopsy shows many large multinucleated cells lying in shallow pits on the bone surface. These cells arise from which of the following?","options":["Mesenchymal stromal cells of the bone marrow","Chondrocytes of the growth plate","Osteocytes released from their lacunae","Monocyte and macrophage precursors"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cells: large, multinucleated, and sitting in pits on the bone surface. These are osteoclasts.","Recall that osteoclasts form by fusion of hematopoietic precursors.","Separate this lineage from the mesenchymal lineage that gives osteoblasts and osteocytes."],"why":"Osteoclasts form when monocyte and macrophage precursors fuse under the influence of M-CSF and RANKL, which osteoblasts and stromal cells supply. The mature cell is multinucleated, seals onto bone, and resorbs it with acid and cathepsin K in a Howship lacuna. In carbonic anhydrase II deficiency the osteoclasts are present but cannot make enough acid. Because osteoclasts come from blood-forming stem cells, hematopoietic stem cell transplantation can correct some severe infantile forms of osteopetrosis.","whyNot":["Mesenchymal stromal cells give rise to osteoblasts, chondrocytes, and fat cells. Osteoblasts are mononuclear and secrete type I collagen and alkaline phosphatase.","Growth plate chondrocytes build the cartilage model for long bone growth. They do not resorb bone.","Osteocytes are osteoblasts trapped in mineralized matrix. They are small, single-nucleus cells that sense mechanical load and secrete FGF23.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Bone cell lineages","Osteoclast formation"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-C1"]}},{"id":"MS-C12-Q4","stem":"At the age of 2 years, he develops diffuse muscle weakness during an episode of gastroenteritis with diarrhea. Deep tendon reflexes are decreased. Serum potassium concentration is 2.4 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0). Which of the following best explains his weakness?","options":["Muscle cells are hyperpolarized farther from threshold","Less acetylcholine is released at each motor end plate","Nerve sodium channels open with less depolarization","Sustained depolarization inactivates muscle sodium channels"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the cause of the low potassium: urinary potassium loss from his renal tubular acidosis, made worse by diarrhea.","Apply the Nernst equation: lower extracellular potassium makes the resting membrane potential more negative.","A more negative resting potential sits farther from threshold, so muscle fibers are harder to fire and he is weak."],"why":"The resting membrane potential of nerve and muscle depends mostly on the potassium gradient across the membrane. When extracellular potassium falls, the gradient grows and the resting potential becomes more negative (hyperpolarized), so a larger stimulus is needed to reach threshold. Muscle fibers fire less easily, which causes weakness and low reflexes. Renal tubular acidosis from carbonic anhydrase II deficiency wastes potassium in the urine, and diarrhea adds more loss. High potassium also causes weakness, but by a different route: sustained depolarization keeps sodium channels inactivated.","whyNot":["Correct.","Reduced acetylcholine release causes weakness in Lambert-Eaton syndrome and hypermagnesemia. Low potassium acts on the muscle membrane itself.","A lower threshold for sodium channel opening is the effect of hypocalcemia, which causes tetany and spasms instead of weakness.","Sustained depolarization with sodium channel inactivation is how hyperkalemia causes weakness. His potassium is low."],"study":{"topics":["Potassium and resting membrane potential","Hypokalemia and hyperkalemia weakness"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-13-C1"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C13","system":"MS","dx":"Marfan syndrome","codes":["MS-13-D1"],"case":"A 17-year-old boy comes to the physician for a preparticipation sports examination before joining his high school basketball team. His father died suddenly at the age of 36 years. He is 198 cm (6 ft 6 in) tall, and his arm span is 208 cm. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows long, thin fingers, pectus excavatum, a high-arched palate, and mild thoracic scoliosis. A midsystolic click is heard at the apex. Slit-lamp examination shows upward and outward displacement of both lenses. Echocardiography shows a dilated aortic root and mitral valve prolapse. Diagnosis: Marfan syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C13-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing shows a heterozygous missense mutation in FBN1. Which of the following mechanisms contributes most to the progressive dilation of his aortic root?","options":["Haploinsufficiency of elastin from a 7q11.23 deletion","Excess TGF-beta activity from defective microfibrils","Reduced lysyl oxidase cross-linking from copper deficiency","Defective triple helix formation in type I collagen"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-D1","obj":"MS-13-D.1","dok":3,"approach":["FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, the main protein of extracellular microfibrils.","Microfibrils normally bind latent TGF-beta and keep it inactive.","Defective microfibrils release too much active TGF-beta, which weakens the aortic media."],"why":"Marfan syndrome is autosomal dominant, caused by mutations in FBN1 on chromosome 15. Fibrillin-1 microfibrils are the scaffold for elastic fibers and also hold latent TGF-beta in the matrix. When fibrillin-1 is abnormal, TGF-beta is activated in excess, which promotes matrix breakdown and loss of smooth muscle in the aortic media (cystic medial degeneration). The aortic root dilates, and the risk of aortic dissection rises. Excess TGF-beta activity also contributes to the skeletal overgrowth.","whyNot":["Deletion at 7q11.23 that includes the elastin gene causes Williams syndrome, which narrows the aorta above the valve (supravalvular aortic stenosis).","Correct.","Lysyl oxidase needs copper, and its failure in Menkes disease weakens collagen and elastin. Copper metabolism is normal in Marfan syndrome.","Defective type I collagen triple helices cause osteogenesis imperfecta, with fractures and blue sclerae."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrillin-1 and TGF-beta","Cystic medial degeneration"],"comps":["MS-13-D1","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C13-Q2","stem":"Homocystinuria can produce a similar tall, thin body habitus. Which of the following findings would most strongly favor homocystinuria in a tall patient with long fingers?","options":["Pectus excavatum with a high-arched palate and scoliosis","Arm span exceeding height with long, slender fingers and toes","Prolapse of the mitral valve with a midsystolic apical click","Downward lens displacement and a prior venous thrombosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-D1","obj":"MS-13-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["List what both disorders share: tall stature, long limbs and digits, and chest deformity.","Find the features that separate them: lens direction, thrombosis, and intellectual disability.","Pick the combination that only homocystinuria explains."],"why":"Both disorders cause a marfanoid habitus and lens dislocation. In Marfan syndrome the lens usually moves up and out, and the main threats are aortic root dilation, aortic dissection, and mitral valve prolapse. In homocystinuria, most often from cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency, the lens usually moves down and in, and high homocysteine damages endothelium, causing arterial and venous thrombosis. Intellectual disability is also common in homocystinuria and is not a feature of Marfan syndrome.","whyNot":["Pectus deformity, a high-arched palate, and scoliosis occur in both disorders as part of the skeletal overgrowth.","Long limbs and digits are part of the marfanoid habitus seen in both disorders, so they do not separate them.","Mitral valve prolapse is a typical cardiovascular finding in Marfan syndrome. It points away from homocystinuria.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Marfan syndrome versus homocystinuria","Marfan cardiovascular complications"],"comps":["MS-13-D1"]}},{"id":"MS-C13-Q3","stem":"The same gene defect affects the yellow ligaments that connect the laminae of adjacent vertebrae and recoil as the spine straightens after flexion. Which of the following best describes the normal fibers responsible for this recoil?","options":["Type II collagen fibrils embedded in aggrecan","Type III collagen fibers forming a reticular mesh","Type I collagen fibrils cross-linked by lysyl oxidase","Elastin cores built on fibrillin microfibrils"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the yellow ligaments as the ligamenta flava, which are rich in elastic fibers.","Recall how an elastic fiber is built: an amorphous elastin core around a sheath of microfibrils.","Link the microfibril protein to the FBN1 defect."],"why":"The ligamenta flava are yellow because they are rich in elastic fibers, which let them stretch during flexion and recoil during extension. An elastic fiber forms when tropoelastin is laid down on a scaffold of fibrillin-1 microfibrils and then cross-linked into an elastin core. Fibrillin microfibrils also bind latent TGF-beta and hold it inactive in the matrix. Elastic fibers are also prominent in the aortic media and the suspensory ligaments of the lens, which explains the aortic and lens findings in Marfan syndrome.","whyNot":["Type II collagen with aggrecan forms hyaline cartilage, which resists compression.","Type III collagen forms reticular fibers in lymphoid organs and vessel walls. They support cells but do not provide elastic recoil.","Type I collagen fibrils give tensile strength to tendon, ligament, and bone, but collagen does not stretch and recoil the way elastic fibers do.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Elastic fiber structure","Ligamenta flava"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-D1"]}},{"id":"MS-C13-Q4","stem":"When he folds his thumb across his palm and closes his fingers over it, the tip of the thumb extends well beyond the ulnar border of the hand. Movement at the first carpometacarpal joint contributes to this sign. Which of the following best describes this joint?","options":["Hinge joint between the trapezoid and first metacarpal","Condyloid joint between the scaphoid and first metacarpal","Saddle joint between the trapezium and first metacarpal","Plane joint between the capitate and first metacarpal"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the bones of the first carpometacarpal joint: the trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal.","Recall that both surfaces are concave in one direction and convex in the other.","Classify this as a saddle joint, which allows flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and opposition."],"why":"The first carpometacarpal joint lies between the trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal. Each surface is shaped like a saddle, so the joint moves in two planes and adds some rotation, which allows opposition of the thumb. In Marfan syndrome, long digits and joint laxity let the thumb tip pass beyond the ulnar border of the closed hand, the thumb sign. Other synovial joint types include the pivot (atlantoaxial), hinge (interphalangeal), condyloid (radiocarpal), ball and socket (shoulder), and plane (intercarpal).","whyNot":["The trapezoid articulates with the second metacarpal. Hinge joints, such as the elbow and interphalangeal joints, move in one plane.","The scaphoid does not articulate with the first metacarpal. Condyloid joints, such as the radiocarpal and metacarpophalangeal joints, have an oval head in an oval socket.","Correct.","The capitate articulates with the third metacarpal. Plane joints, such as the intercarpal joints, allow only gliding."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial joint types","Carpometacarpal joints"],"comps":["MS-4","MS-3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C14","system":"MS","dx":"Duchenne muscular dystrophy","codes":["MS-13-D2"],"case":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of frequent falls and difficulty climbing stairs over the past year. He walked at 18 months of age. His mother's brother used a wheelchair from the age of 11 years and died of respiratory failure at 20 years. Physical examination shows a waddling gait, firm, enlarged calves, and weakness of the hip and shoulder girdle muscles. To rise from the floor, he pushes on his knees and thighs with his hands. Serum creatine kinase activity is 16,800 U/L (normal 40 to 200). Diagnosis: Duchenne muscular dystrophy.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C14-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings is most likely on biopsy of his enlarged calf muscle?","options":["Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation","Uniformly enlarged fibers with normal architecture","Necrotic fibers replaced by fat and fibrous tissue","Endomysial CD8 T cells invading nonnecrotic fibers"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-D2","obj":"MS-13-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["Notice that the calves are large while he is weak.","Recall that absent dystrophin makes the sarcolemma tear with contraction, so fibers die repeatedly.","Dead fibers are replaced by fat and connective tissue, which enlarges the calf without adding strength (pseudohypertrophy)."],"why":"Dystrophin links the actin cytoskeleton through the dystroglycan complex to the extracellular matrix. Without it, the sarcolemma tears during contraction, calcium floods in, and fibers undergo necrosis. Regeneration cannot keep up, so the biopsy shows fibers of varied size, necrotic and regenerating fibers, and progressive replacement by fat and fibrous tissue. The calves enlarge from this fibrofatty tissue, which is pseudohypertrophy. Creatine kinase leaking from damaged fibers explains the very high serum level.","whyNot":["Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation is typical of dermatomyositis, an inflammatory myopathy with a heliotrope rash and Gottron papules.","Uniformly large, normal fibers describe true hypertrophy, as after resistance training. His large calves are weak, which points to pseudohypertrophy.","Correct.","CD8 T cells invading fibers are typical of polymyositis, which presents in adults. A family history in a maternal uncle points to an X-linked dystrophy."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle biopsy in Duchenne dystrophy","Pseudohypertrophy"],"comps":["MS-13-D2","MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C14-Q2","stem":"Genetic testing shows a deletion of several exons of the DMD gene whose combined length is not a multiple of three nucleotides. Which of the following best explains why this deletion causes his severe phenotype?","options":["It removes the promoter so that no mRNA is transcribed","It shifts the reading frame, creating an early stop codon","It produces a protein that blocks the normal allele's product","It yields a shortened protein that keeps both binding ends"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["Codons are read three nucleotides at a time.","Deleting a number of nucleotides that is not divisible by three shifts the reading frame downstream.","A frameshift soon creates a stop codon, so no functional dystrophin is made, which gives Duchenne dystrophy."],"why":"The reading frame rule explains most of the difference between Duchenne and Becker dystrophy. A frameshift deletion scrambles every codon after the break and soon reaches a premature stop codon, so the mRNA is degraded and no functional dystrophin is made. An in-frame deletion removes some codons but keeps the rest of the message in register, so a shorter, partly working dystrophin is made, which gives the milder Becker form. The DMD gene is one of the largest human genes, which is why new mutations are common.","whyNot":["The deletion described lies within the coding exons. Promoter deletions are uncommon, and the key here is that the deleted length is not divisible by three.","Correct.","A boy has only one X chromosome and therefore one DMD allele. Dominant-negative effects require a normal allele whose product can be disrupted.","A shortened protein that keeps the actin-binding and dystroglycan-binding ends results from an in-frame deletion and causes Becker dystrophy."],"study":{"topics":["Reading frame rule","Duchenne versus Becker genetics"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-D2"]}},{"id":"MS-C14-Q3","stem":"At the age of 17 years, he has morning headaches and daytime sleepiness. When he lies supine, his abdomen moves inward as he inhales. Weakness of which of the following muscles, with its nerve supply, best explains this finding?","options":["Diaphragm, phrenic nerves from C3 to C5","Sternocleidomastoid, spinal accessory nerve (CN XI)","External intercostals, intercostal nerves from T1 to T11","Rectus abdominis, thoracoabdominal nerves from T7 to T11"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.5","dok":2,"approach":["Normally the diaphragm descends during inspiration and pushes the abdominal wall outward.","Inward movement of the abdomen during inspiration (paradoxical breathing) means the diaphragm is weak and is being pulled up by negative chest pressure.","Name the diaphragm's nerve supply: the phrenic nerves from C3 to C5."],"why":"The diaphragm is the main muscle of inspiration. When it contracts, it descends and pushes the abdominal contents down and the abdominal wall out. When it is weak, the accessory muscles expand the chest, the negative pressure pulls the diaphragm upward, and the abdomen moves in, especially when lying supine. Diaphragm weakness causes hypoventilation during sleep, with morning headaches from carbon dioxide retention and daytime sleepiness. Respiratory failure and dilated cardiomyopathy are the main causes of death in Duchenne dystrophy.","whyNot":["Correct.","The sternocleidomastoid is an accessory muscle that raises the sternum in forced inspiration. Paradoxical abdominal motion points to the diaphragm.","The external intercostals help raise the ribs in inspiration, but their weakness does not make the abdomen move inward when he lies supine.","The rectus abdominis flexes the trunk and helps forced expiration. Its weakness does not cause inward abdominal motion during inspiration."],"study":{"topics":["Diaphragm innervation","Paradoxical breathing"],"comps":["MS-5","MS-13-D2"]}},{"id":"MS-C14-Q4","stem":"His findings are compared with those of an unrelated 16-year-old boy who first had exercise-induced calf cramps at the age of 11 years, still walks independently, and has a serum creatine kinase activity of 4,200 U/L. Which of the following results of dystrophin immunostaining is most likely in the older boy's muscle?","options":["No staining at the sarcolemma of nearly all fibers","Normal, continuous staining at the sarcolemma of all fibers","Faint, patchy staining at the sarcolemma of most fibers","Strong staining confined to the myofiber nuclei"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-D2","obj":"MS-13-D.4","dok":3,"approach":["Later onset, slower course, and walking in the teens point to Becker dystrophy.","Becker dystrophy makes a reduced amount of a shortened dystrophin.","Immunostaining therefore shows reduced, uneven staining along the fiber membrane, instead of the absent staining of Duchenne."],"why":"Becker muscular dystrophy is the milder form of the same X-linked disease. Symptoms start later, often in late childhood or adolescence, many patients walk into adulthood, and creatine kinase is high but usually lower than in Duchenne. Because some shortened dystrophin is made, immunostaining of the sarcolemma is faint and patchy, and Western blot shows protein of reduced size or amount. In Duchenne dystrophy, staining is absent from nearly all fibers. Genetic testing then shows whether the deletion keeps or shifts the reading frame.","whyNot":["Absent staining in nearly all fibers is the Duchenne pattern, as in the younger boy. The older boy's later onset and preserved walking point to Becker dystrophy.","Normal dystrophin staining would point to another myopathy, such as a limb-girdle dystrophy, which can look similar clinically.","Correct.","Dystrophin is a cytoskeletal protein that sits under the sarcolemma. It is not a nuclear protein."],"study":{"topics":["Becker muscular dystrophy","Dystrophin immunostaining"],"comps":["MS-13-D2","MS-9"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C15","system":"MS","dx":"Myotonic dystrophy type 1","codes":["MS-13-D2"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-year history of difficulty releasing her grip on door handles and tools and frequent tripping. She had cataract surgery in both eyes at the age of 29 years. Her mother developed cataracts in her 50s and has no weakness. Physical examination shows bilateral ptosis, a long, thin face with wasting of the temporal muscles, weakness of neck flexion, and bilateral foot drop. After a firm handshake, she opens her hand slowly. Percussion of the thenar eminence causes a sustained contraction. An ECG shows a PR interval of 240 msec (normal 120 to 200). Diagnosis: myotonic dystrophy type 1.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C15-Q1","stem":"Two years later, she gives birth to a boy after a pregnancy complicated by polyhydramnios. At birth he is severely hypotonic, has facial weakness, and needs ventilatory support. Which of the following best explains why his disease is more severe than his mother's?","options":["Inheritance of a second mutant DMPK allele from the father","Further expansion of the CTG repeat during transmission","Imprinting that silences the normal paternal DMPK allele","High mutant mitochondrial DNA load inherited from the oocyte"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.5","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the pattern across three generations: cataracts only in the grandmother, adult disease in the mother, and severe newborn disease in the son.","Earlier onset and greater severity in each generation is anticipation.","Anticipation in myotonic dystrophy comes from an unstable CTG repeat that grows when passed on, most dramatically through the mother."],"why":"Myotonic dystrophy type 1 is autosomal dominant and caused by an expanded CTG repeat in the untranslated end of the DMPK gene on chromosome 19. The repeat is unstable and tends to grow in each generation, so onset is earlier and disease is more severe (anticipation). The largest expansions, which cause congenital myotonic dystrophy with hypotonia and breathing failure, are passed on almost always by an affected mother. The expanded RNA traps splicing regulators, causing missplicing of the muscle chloride channel and other proteins, which explains myotonia, weakness, cataracts, and conduction block.","whyNot":["Nothing suggests the father is affected, and one expanded allele is enough to cause disease. A second mutant allele is not needed to explain the severity.","Correct.","Imprinting explains disorders such as Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes. One expanded DMPK allele is enough to cause disease, whatever the parent of origin.","Mitochondrial disorders are passed through the mother and vary with mutant load, but myotonic dystrophy is a nuclear gene disorder with autosomal dominant inheritance."],"study":{"topics":["Trinucleotide repeat expansion","Anticipation in myotonic dystrophy"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-D2"]}},{"id":"MS-C15-Q2","stem":"Wasting of her temporal and masseter muscles gives her face a narrow, hollow appearance. From which pharyngeal arch do these muscles develop, and which nerve supplies them?","options":["Fourth arch, superior laryngeal nerve (CN X)","Third arch, glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Second arch, facial nerve (CN VII)","First arch, mandibular nerve (CN V3)"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the temporalis and masseter as muscles of mastication.","Recall that the muscles of mastication come from the first pharyngeal arch.","Each arch keeps its own cranial nerve, and the first arch nerve is the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve."],"why":"Each pharyngeal arch forms muscles that keep the nerve of that arch. The first arch forms the muscles of mastication (temporalis, masseter, medial and lateral pterygoids), plus the mylohyoid, anterior belly of the digastric, tensor tympani, and tensor veli palatini, all supplied by the mandibular nerve (CN V3). Wasting of the temporalis and masseter is typical of myotonic dystrophy and produces the long, narrow face. Her ptosis comes from weakness of the levator palpebrae superioris, which is supplied by the oculomotor nerve.","whyNot":["The fourth arch forms the cricothyroid and most pharyngeal constrictors, supplied by branches of the vagus nerve.","The third arch forms only the stylopharyngeus, supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve.","The second arch forms the muscles of facial expression, the stapedius, the stylohyoid, and the posterior belly of the digastric, supplied by the facial nerve.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pharyngeal arch derivatives","Muscles of mastication"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C15-Q3","stem":"Her weakness of neck flexion is accompanied by thin sternocleidomastoid muscles. Which of the following correctly describes the nerve supply and an action of one of these muscles?","options":["Accessory nerve; turns the face toward the opposite side","Accessory nerve; turns the face toward the same side as the muscle","Ansa cervicalis; depresses the hyoid bone during swallowing","Facial nerve; tenses the skin of the anterior neck"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the sternocleidomastoid's attachments: mastoid process above, sternum and clavicle below.","Contracting one side pulls the mastoid toward the same-side clavicle, which tilts the head toward that side and turns the face to the other side.","Name its motor nerve: the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI)."],"why":"The sternocleidomastoid runs from the mastoid process to the manubrium and the medial clavicle. One muscle acting alone tilts the head toward its own side and rotates the face toward the opposite side. Both muscles together flex the neck. Motor supply is the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI), which also supplies the trapezius. Weakness and wasting of the sternocleidomastoid with neck flexion weakness is a typical early finding in myotonic dystrophy type 1.","whyNot":["Correct.","This reverses the rotation. Because the muscle pulls the mastoid process down and forward, the face turns away from the contracting side.","The ansa cervicalis supplies the infrahyoid muscles, such as the sternohyoid and omohyoid, which depress the hyoid.","The facial nerve supplies the platysma, which tenses the skin of the neck. The platysma is a muscle of facial expression."],"study":{"topics":["Sternocleidomastoid action","Accessory nerve"],"comps":["MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C15-Q4","stem":"Her skeletal muscles stay contracted after a voluntary grip, yet her ventricles relax fully between beats even during the tachycardia of exercise. Which of the following properties of cardiac muscle prevents its contractions from summating into tetany?","options":["A refractory period that lasts almost as long as contraction","Absence of ryanodine receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Gap junctions that make the myocardium contract as a syncytium","Contraction triggered only by calcium entering from outside the cell"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Tetany needs a new action potential to arrive before the muscle relaxes.","The cardiac action potential has a long plateau from calcium entry through L-type channels.","Its refractory period lasts through most of the contraction, so a second contraction cannot be added on top of the first."],"why":"In skeletal muscle the action potential lasts a few milliseconds, much shorter than the twitch, so repeated stimuli can summate into tetanus. In ventricular muscle the plateau of the action potential keeps sodium channels inactivated for most of systole, so the absolute refractory period lasts nearly as long as the contraction itself. A new beat cannot start until the muscle is relaxing, which protects filling. Her myotonia is different: missplicing of the skeletal muscle chloride channel leaves the membrane prone to repetitive firing, so the muscle keeps contracting after the voluntary effort ends.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cardiac muscle has ryanodine receptors (RyR2). Calcium entering through L-type channels opens them, releasing calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (calcium-induced calcium release).","Gap junctions do spread excitation so the ventricles contract as a unit, but they do not stop one contraction from summating with the next.","Most of the calcium for cardiac contraction comes from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium entering from outside mainly acts as a trigger for that release."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac refractory period","Calcium-induced calcium release"],"comps":["MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C16","system":"MS","dx":"Type I osteogenesis imperfecta in a toddler evaluated for abuse","codes":["MS-13-D3"],"case":"A 20-month-old girl is brought to the emergency department because she would not use her right arm after a fall from a low couch. Her father had several fractures in childhood and began to lose his hearing in his 30s. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows blue-gray sclerae, a swollen, tender right upper arm, and mildly lax joints. Radiographs show a transverse fracture of the middle third of the right humeral shaft. A skeletal survey shows generalized osteopenia, a healed fracture of the left tibia, and many small extra bones within the lambdoid suture. A child protection evaluation is started. Diagnosis: osteogenesis imperfecta type I.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C16-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional findings on the skeletal survey would most strongly suggest nonaccidental trauma instead of her bone disease?","options":["Compression and wedging of several vertebral bodies","Anterior bowing of both femurs and both tibias","Thin cortices and slender shafts of the long bones","Corner fractures of the distal femoral metaphyses"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-D3","obj":"MS-13-D.6","dok":3,"approach":["List the features already pointing to osteogenesis imperfecta: blue sclerae, wormian bones, osteopenia, and an affected father.","Recall the fracture patterns that are highly specific for abuse: classic metaphyseal lesions and posterior rib fractures.","Pick the finding produced by shaking or twisting forces that bone fragility alone rarely explains."],"why":"Osteogenesis imperfecta and child abuse both cause multiple fractures, and the two must be told apart carefully. Clues for osteogenesis imperfecta include blue or gray sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, wormian bones, osteopenia, bowing, and a family history of fractures or early hearing loss. Clues for abuse include classic metaphyseal lesions (corner or bucket-handle fractures), posterior rib fractures, fractures of different ages without a fitting history, and bruising in protected areas, usually in bone of normal density. Both can occur together, so the evaluation continues even when osteogenesis imperfecta is confirmed.","whyNot":["Vertebral compression fractures are common in osteogenesis imperfecta because of low bone mass.","Long bone bowing results from repeated microfractures in weak bone and is typical of osteogenesis imperfecta.","Thin cortices and slender shafts reflect too little type I collagen matrix. They support osteogenesis imperfecta.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Osteogenesis imperfecta versus abuse","Classic metaphyseal lesions"],"comps":["MS-13-D3","MS-13-H"]}},{"id":"MS-C16-Q2","stem":"Her primary teeth are gray-brown and translucent and wear down quickly. Which of the following best explains these dental findings?","options":["Defective amelogenin in the developing enamel","Abnormal type I collagen in the dentin matrix","Excess fluoride built into enamel crystals","Tetracycline bound to developing tooth mineral"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-D3","obj":"MS-13-D.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the organic matrix of dentin, like bone, is mainly type I collagen.","Osteogenesis imperfecta is a type I collagen disorder, so dentin is abnormal (dentinogenesis imperfecta).","Weak dentin looks translucent and gray-brown, and the enamel on top chips off, so teeth wear quickly."],"why":"Osteogenesis imperfecta is caused by mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2, the genes for type I collagen. Every tissue rich in type I collagen is affected: bone (fractures with little trauma), sclera (thin, so the dark choroid shows through as blue), dentin (dentinogenesis imperfecta), ligaments (joint laxity), and the middle ear ossicles (conductive hearing loss, often starting in early adulthood, as in her father). Enamel contains no collagen, though it chips away from the weak dentin beneath it.","whyNot":["Amelogenin defects cause amelogenesis imperfecta, a disorder of enamel with normal dentin and normal bones.","Correct.","Excess fluoride causes dental fluorosis, with white or brown mottling of enamel. It does not weaken bone in childhood.","Tetracycline taken while teeth are forming stains them yellow to brown in bands. It does not cause fractures or blue sclerae."],"study":{"topics":["Osteogenesis imperfecta features","Dentinogenesis imperfecta"],"comps":["MS-13-D3","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C16-Q3","stem":"Genetic testing shows a heterozygous COL1A1 mutation that creates a premature stop codon, and the mutant mRNA is destroyed by nonsense-mediated decay. Which of the following best explains why her disease is milder than osteogenesis imperfecta caused by a glycine substitution in the triple helix?","options":["Mutant chains enter triple helices and disrupt most of them","The mutation affects only collagen made by osteoblasts","Collagen is made in half the usual amount but is normal","The normal allele doubles its output and fully compensates"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note that her mutant mRNA is destroyed, so no mutant chains are made.","Only the normal allele produces chains, so collagen is reduced in amount but normal in structure (haploinsufficiency).","Compare glycine substitutions, where abnormal chains are built into helices and damage many molecules (dominant negative effect)."],"why":"Type I collagen has two pro-alpha1 chains (COL1A1) and one pro-alpha2 chain (COL1A2). Glycine must occupy every third position because only this smallest amino acid fits in the center of the triple helix. A glycine substitution produces a chain that still joins helices but kinks them, and because each molecule contains two alpha1 chains, about three quarters of the molecules are abnormal. This dominant negative effect causes the severe forms. A null allele removed by nonsense-mediated decay leaves only normal chains in half the amount, which gives the milder type I form.","whyNot":["This is the dominant negative mechanism of glycine substitutions, which cause the more severe forms. Her mutant mRNA is destroyed, so no mutant chains are made.","Type I collagen made by fibroblasts and odontoblasts is also affected, which explains her blue sclerae, lax joints, and abnormal teeth.","Correct.","The remaining normal allele does not fully make up the loss. If it did, she would have no fractures."],"study":{"topics":["Dominant negative versus null alleles","Collagen triple helix structure"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C16-Q4","stem":"Which of the following deficits should be looked for most carefully in her injured right limb?","options":["Inability to spread the fingers apart","Inability to abduct the arm at the shoulder","Inability to extend the wrist and fingers","Inability to flex the elbow with the forearm supinated"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.4","dok":3,"approach":["Locate the fracture: the middle third of the humeral shaft.","Recall that the radial nerve runs in the radial groove on the back of the humeral shaft at this level.","Predict radial nerve injury: weak wrist and finger extension (wrist drop)."],"why":"The radial nerve winds around the back of the middle of the humerus in the radial groove, with the deep brachial artery. A midshaft fracture can stretch or trap it, causing wrist drop and loss of finger extension at the metacarpophalangeal joints, with sensory loss over the back of the hand. Other classic pairings: surgical neck fracture with the axillary nerve, supracondylar fracture with the median nerve and brachial artery, and medial epicondyle fracture with the ulnar nerve.","whyNot":["Inability to spread the fingers reflects weak interossei from ulnar nerve injury, as with medial epicondyle fractures.","Loss of shoulder abduction suggests axillary nerve injury, which goes with fractures of the surgical neck of the humerus or shoulder dislocation.","Correct.","Weak elbow flexion with the forearm supinated suggests musculocutaneous nerve injury, which is rare with humeral shaft fractures."],"study":{"topics":["Nerves at risk with humeral fractures","Radial nerve course"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-11"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C17","system":"MS","dx":"Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome with recurrent shoulder dislocation","codes":["MS-13-D"],"case":"A 22-year-old woman comes to the emergency department 1 hour after her left shoulder dislocated while she reached overhead to place a box on a shelf. This is her fourth shoulder dislocation since the age of 16 years. She bruises easily, and her mother has similar skin and joint problems. Neither has had arterial or bowel rupture. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows a squared-off left shoulder with the humeral head palpable anteriorly. Her skin is soft and stretches easily, and there are wide, thin, shiny scars over both knees and the forehead. She can place her palms flat on the floor with her knees straight. Diagnosis: classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C17-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her skin and joint findings?","options":["Glycine substitutions in the type I collagen triple helix","Mutant type V collagen disrupting type I fibril assembly","Mutant type IV collagen weakening basement membranes","Mutant type III collagen weakening arteries and bowel wall"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-D","obj":"MS-13-D.7","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the classic type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome: stretchy skin, wide atrophic scars, lax joints, and no arterial or bowel rupture.","Recall that classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is most often caused by mutations in type V collagen (COL5A1 or COL5A2).","Type V collagen helps control the size of type I fibrils, so its loss makes dermal and ligament fibrils irregular and weak."],"why":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is a group of disorders of collagen structure or processing. In the classic type, mutant type V collagen fails to regulate the assembly of type I collagen fibrils, so dermis and ligaments contain irregular, weak fibrils. The result is soft, stretchy skin that splits easily and heals with wide, thin scars, joint hypermobility with dislocations, and easy bruising from fragile small vessels. The vascular type, caused by type III collagen mutations, carries the risk of arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture.","whyNot":["Glycine substitutions in type I collagen cause the severe forms of osteogenesis imperfecta, with fractures and blue sclerae.","Correct.","Type IV collagen mutations cause Alport syndrome, with kidney disease, hearing loss, and eye findings.","Type III collagen mutations cause vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, with thin, translucent skin and rupture of arteries, bowel, or uterus. Her family has none of these."],"study":{"topics":["Types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Collagen types and disease"],"comps":["MS-13-D","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C17-Q2","stem":"Because of her easy bruising, a nutritional cause of weak collagen is considered before genetic testing. In vitamin C deficiency, which of the following steps of collagen synthesis is impaired?","options":["Cross-linking of collagen fibrils by lysyl oxidase","Glycosylation of hydroxylysine residues in the rough ER","Cleavage of propeptides from procollagen outside the cell","Hydroxylation of proline and lysine in the rough ER"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the order of collagen synthesis: chain synthesis, hydroxylation, glycosylation, triple helix formation, secretion, propeptide cleavage, then cross-linking.","Identify the step that needs ascorbate: prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases.","Predict the result: unstable helices and fragile vessels, causing bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, and easy bruising."],"why":"Collagen chains are made in the rough endoplasmic reticulum as repeats of Gly-X-Y. Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases then add hydroxyl groups, and they need vitamin C (and iron) as cofactors. Hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix, and hydroxylysine is needed for glycosylation and later cross-linking. In scurvy, underhydroxylated collagen forms unstable helices, so vessel walls are fragile and wounds heal poorly. Other steps fail in other diseases: propeptide cleavage in some Ehlers-Danlos types and cross-linking in Menkes disease.","whyNot":["Lysyl oxidase needs copper. Its failure in Menkes disease, a disorder of copper transport, weakens collagen and elastin.","Glycosylation of hydroxylysine follows hydroxylation and does not itself require vitamin C.","Procollagen peptidases cleave the propeptides outside the cell. Defects in this step cause rare types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen synthesis steps","Scurvy"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C17-Q3","stem":"After reduction of her shoulder, which of the following findings would most likely indicate a nerve injury caused by the dislocation?","options":["Lateral shoulder numbness and weak arm abduction","Weak elbow flexion and lateral forearm numbness","Weak wrist extension and numbness of the dorsal hand","Scapular winging when pushing against a wall"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["In an anterior dislocation, the humeral head moves forward and down, below the coracoid process.","The axillary nerve wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus just below the joint capsule and is stretched.","Predict axillary nerve signs: weak deltoid abduction and numbness over the lateral shoulder."],"why":"The glenohumeral joint trades stability for range of motion. The glenoid fossa is shallow, so the joint depends on the rotator cuff muscles and the capsule and ligaments to hold the humeral head in place. With lax capsular collagen in Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, the head dislocates easily, most often anteriorly. The axillary nerve passes through the quadrangular space and around the surgical neck, so anterior dislocation can stretch it. Injury weakens the deltoid and teres minor and numbs the skin over the lower deltoid.","whyNot":["Correct.","Musculocutaneous nerve injury can occur with dislocation, but it is much less common than axillary nerve injury.","Weak wrist extension with dorsal hand numbness points to radial nerve injury, usually from a fracture of the humeral shaft.","Scapular winging comes from long thoracic nerve injury, which affects the serratus anterior. This nerve runs on the chest wall and is not stretched by anterior dislocation."],"study":{"topics":["Glenohumeral stability","Axillary nerve injury"],"comps":["MS-4","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C17-Q4","stem":"Six weeks later, she inverts her right ankle while stepping off a curb. There is swelling and tenderness just anterior and inferior to the lateral malleolus, with no tenderness over the bones. The anterior drawer test shows increased forward movement of the foot. Radiographs show no fracture. Which of the following best describes this injury?","options":["Sprain of the anterior talofibular ligament","Strain of the fibularis brevis musculotendinous unit","Contusion of the soft tissue over the lateral malleolus","Dislocation of the talocrural joint"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.3","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the tenderness: anterior and inferior to the lateral malleolus, the site of the anterior talofibular ligament.","A positive anterior drawer test means this ligament is torn or stretched.","An injury to a ligament is a sprain, while a strain affects muscle or tendon."],"why":"A sprain is a stretch or tear of a ligament. A strain is an injury to a muscle or tendon. A contusion is bleeding into soft tissue from a direct blow, and a dislocation is complete loss of contact between joint surfaces. Inversion with the foot pointed down most often injures the anterior talofibular ligament, and the anterior drawer test checks it. Ligaments heal slowly because they are dense collagen with few fibroblasts and limited blood supply, and the repair scar is weaker than the original ligament. In Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, the lax collagen raises the risk of sprains.","whyNot":["Correct.","A fibularis brevis strain would cause pain along the tendon behind the malleolus or at its insertion on the fifth metatarsal, with pain on resisted eversion. A positive anterior drawer test points to a ligament.","A contusion follows a direct blow and causes bruising without joint instability. Her anterior drawer test is abnormal.","A talocrural dislocation would cause obvious deformity and usually a fracture. Her joint surfaces remain in contact."],"study":{"topics":["Sprain, strain, contusion, dislocation","Lateral ankle ligaments"],"comps":["MS-13-H","MS-4"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C18","system":"MS","dx":"Achondroplasia","codes":["MS-13-D"],"case":"A 10-month-old boy is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. He was born at term to a 27-year-old mother and a 45-year-old father, both of average height, and there is no family history of short stature. His length is below the 1st percentile, and his head circumference is above the 97th percentile. Physical examination shows short arms and legs with redundant skin folds, a trunk of normal length, a prominent forehead, a flat nasal bridge, and short fingers with a gap between the middle and ring fingers. Radiographs show short tubular bones. Diagnosis: achondroplasia.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C18-Q1","stem":"Which of the following patterns of limb shortening is most characteristic of his disorder?","options":["Hands and feet shortened most","Forearms and lower legs shortened most","Trunk shortened more than the limbs","Upper arms and thighs shortened most"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-D","obj":"MS-13-D.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that achondroplasia impairs endochondral bone growth at the growth plates.","The proximal long bones (humerus and femur) normally grow the most in length, so they lose the most.","Name the pattern: rhizomelic shortening, with a trunk of near-normal length."],"why":"Achondroplasia is the most common skeletal dysplasia. All long bones are short, but the proximal segments, the humerus and femur, are shortened most (rhizomelia). The trunk is close to normal length, the head is large with frontal bossing and a flat nasal bridge, and the hands are short with a trident shape. Intelligence is normal. Motor milestones can be delayed in infancy because of low muscle tone and the large head, which is different from cognitive delay.","whyNot":["The hands are short in achondroplasia, but the most marked shortening is in the proximal segments.","Shortening that is worst in the forearms and lower legs is mesomelic. It is seen in other skeletal dysplasias.","A trunk shorter than the limbs points to a spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, which affects the spine. In achondroplasia the trunk is close to normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Achondroplasia features","Rhizomelic shortening"],"comps":["MS-13-D","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C18-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains how this child acquired his disorder?","options":["Both parents carry one copy of a recessive mutant allele","A new FGFR3 gain-of-function mutation in a sperm cell","An FGFR3 loss-of-function allele inherited with low penetrance","Maternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 4"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Achondroplasia is autosomal dominant with essentially full penetrance, so an affected parent would show it.","Both parents are of average height, so the mutation is new.","Most new achondroplasia mutations arise in the father's germ cells and become more frequent with advanced paternal age."],"why":"Achondroplasia is caused by gain-of-function mutations in FGFR3, most often the same glycine-to-arginine change at position 380. FGFR3 normally slows chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate, and the overactive receptor slows it too much. About 80% of cases are new mutations, and these arise almost always on the paternal chromosome, with risk rising with paternal age. Recurrence risk for these parents is low, but each child of an affected person has a 50% chance of inheriting it.","whyNot":["Achondroplasia is autosomal dominant, so carrier parents of average height do not explain it. Two affected parents can have a child with the severe homozygous form.","Correct.","The FGFR3 change in achondroplasia increases receptor activity, and penetrance is essentially complete, so a parent carrying it would be affected.","Uniparental disomy causes disease mainly through imprinting or by unmasking a recessive allele. It is not how achondroplasia arises."],"study":{"topics":["FGFR3 and achondroplasia","Advanced paternal age and new mutations"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-D"]}},{"id":"MS-C18-Q3","stem":"If a biopsy of his proximal tibial growth plate were examined, which of the following would most likely be seen?","options":["Retained calcified cartilage cores within dense trabeculae","Widened hypertrophic zone with unmineralized cartilage","Short, disorganized columns in the proliferative zone","Early closure of the plate with bony bridging across it"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the growth plate zones from the epiphysis: reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, and ossification.","Overactive FGFR3 inhibits chondrocyte proliferation.","Predict a short, poorly organized proliferative zone, so less cartilage is made for bone to replace."],"why":"Long bones grow in length when chondrocytes in the proliferative zone divide and stack into columns, then enlarge in the hypertrophic zone before the matrix calcifies and is replaced by bone. In achondroplasia, overactive FGFR3 slows proliferation and hypertrophy, so the columns are short and disorganized and bone length suffers. Intramembranous bone formation is spared, so the skull vault and the width of the long bones develop normally, which helps explain the large head. In rickets, the problem is mineralization, so the hypertrophic zone widens with uncalcified cartilage.","whyNot":["Retained calcified cartilage within dense bone is seen in osteopetrosis, where osteoclasts fail to remove it.","A widened hypertrophic zone with unmineralized cartilage is the growth plate picture of rickets from vitamin D deficiency.","Correct.","Early fusion of the growth plate follows trauma, infection, or excess sex steroids. It does not explain disproportionate shortening from birth."],"study":{"topics":["Growth plate zones","Achondroplasia versus rickets"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C18-Q4","stem":"His parents ask whether his limbs formed abnormally early in pregnancy. His limbs have every normal segment and differ only in length. In the embryo, loss of which of the following signals during limb bud outgrowth would produce limbs that end abruptly, with proximal segments present and distal segments missing?","options":["HOX transcription factors in limb mesenchyme","WNT7a from the dorsal ectoderm","Sonic hedgehog from the zone of polarizing activity","FGF from the apical ectodermal ridge"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.3","dok":3,"approach":["Link each signal center to its axis: the apical ectodermal ridge for proximal to distal, the zone of polarizing activity for anterior to posterior, and the dorsal ectoderm for dorsal to ventral.","Truncation of the distal limb means proximal to distal outgrowth stopped.","The apical ectodermal ridge drives that outgrowth through FGF signaling."],"why":"The apical ectodermal ridge is a thickened rim of ectoderm at the tip of the limb bud. It secretes FGFs that keep the underlying mesenchyme dividing, so the limb grows out from proximal to distal. Removing the ridge early truncates the limb near the body, and later removal truncates it more distally. Sonic hedgehog from the zone of polarizing activity sets the anterior to posterior pattern (extra expression causes polydactyly), HOX genes give each segment its identity, and thalidomide disrupts outgrowth to cause phocomelia. His limbs are complete but short because his defect lies in later growth plate function.","whyNot":["HOX genes give segments their identity. Their mutations change the shape or number of segments, as in synpolydactyly, instead of stopping outgrowth.","WNT7a from the dorsal ectoderm sets the dorsal to ventral pattern, such as nails on the back of the digits.","Sonic hedgehog from the zone of polarizing activity patterns the limb from thumb side to little finger side. Its ectopic expression causes extra digits.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Limb bud signaling centers","Limb defects"],"comps":["MS-1"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C19","system":"MS","dx":"Primary osteoarthritis of the knee","codes":["MS-13-E1"],"case":"A 67-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-year history of pain in her right knee. The pain worsens with walking and climbing stairs and eases with rest. She has about 15 minutes of stiffness after waking and after sitting for long periods. She worked as a mail carrier for 30 years. Her body mass index is 34 kg/m2. Vital signs are within normal limits. Examination of the right knee shows bony enlargement, crepitus with movement, medial joint line tenderness, a mild varus deformity, and a small, cool effusion. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 12 mm/h (normal 0 to 30). Synovial fluid analysis shows a leukocyte count of 700/mm3 (noninflammatory, below 2,000) and no crystals. Radiographs show medial joint space narrowing, osteophytes, and subchondral sclerosis. Diagnosis: primary osteoarthritis of the right knee.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C19-Q1","stem":"Three years later, she undergoes total knee arthroplasty. The medial femoral condyle has a smooth, polished, ivory-like surface. Which of the following best explains this appearance?","options":["Inflamed synovial pannus spreading across the cartilage","Exposed subchondral bone polished by friction","Chalky urate deposits coating the articular cartilage","Calcium pyrophosphate crystals deposited within cartilage"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-E1","obj":"MS-13-E.1","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the sequence of osteoarthritis: proteoglycan loss, fibrillation and cracking of cartilage, then full-thickness cartilage loss.","Once cartilage is gone, bone rubs on bone.","The rubbing polishes and thickens the subchondral bone, called eburnation."],"why":"Osteoarthritis starts with chondrocyte injury from age, load, and obesity. Chondrocytes release enzymes that break down proteoglycan and type II collagen, the cartilage softens, splits along its surface (fibrillation), and wears away. When subchondral bone is exposed, friction polishes it into a smooth, ivory-like surface (eburnation), and the bone beneath thickens (sclerosis) and may form cysts. Osteophytes grow at the joint margins. Her medial compartment carries the most load, especially with a varus knee, so it wears first.","whyNot":["Pannus is inflamed, proliferating synovium that erodes cartilage in rheumatoid arthritis. Her synovial fluid is noninflammatory.","Correct.","Chalky white urate deposits on joint surfaces are seen in chronic gout. Her synovial fluid contained no crystals.","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition (chondrocalcinosis) occurs in pseudogout. It does not produce a polished bone surface."],"study":{"topics":["Osteoarthritis pathogenesis","Eburnation and osteophytes"],"comps":["MS-13-E1","MS-6"]}},{"id":"MS-C19-Q2","stem":"Examination of her hands is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Ulnar deviation and swelling of the metacarpophalangeal joints","Sausage-shaped swelling of an entire digit with nail pitting","Hard bony enlargement of the distal interphalangeal joints","Soft, warm, symmetric swelling of both wrists"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E1","obj":"MS-13-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Osteoarthritis in older women often involves the hands as well as weight-bearing joints.","Bony osteophytes at the distal interphalangeal joints are Heberden nodes, and at the proximal interphalangeal joints Bouchard nodes.","Separate these hard, cool swellings from the soft, warm synovitis of inflammatory arthritis."],"why":"Osteoarthritis causes pain that worsens with use, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, and asymmetric involvement of weight-bearing joints such as the knee and hip. In the hands it targets the distal and proximal interphalangeal joints and the base of the thumb, producing hard bony swellings: Heberden nodes at the distal joints and Bouchard nodes at the proximal joints. The metacarpophalangeal joints and wrists are usually spared. Radiographs show joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and cysts.","whyNot":["Ulnar deviation with metacarpophalangeal swelling is typical of rheumatoid arthritis, which also causes prolonged morning stiffness.","A sausage digit with nail pitting points to psoriatic arthritis.","Correct.","Soft, warm, symmetric wrist swelling indicates synovitis from an inflammatory arthritis such as rheumatoid arthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Osteoarthritis findings","Heberden and Bouchard nodes"],"comps":["MS-13-E1","MS-13-A"]}},{"id":"MS-C19-Q3","stem":"Loss of proteoglycan is one of the earliest changes in her articular cartilage. Which of the following components of normal articular cartilage is mainly responsible for resisting compressive loads?","options":["Water bound by the sulfated chains of aggrecan","Elastic fibers that recoil after the load is removed","A dense capillary network supplying the chondrocytes","Type I collagen fibers aligned parallel to the surface"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the structure of aggrecan: a core protein with many chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate chains, attached in large numbers to hyaluronan.","The negatively charged sulfate groups attract cations and water, which swell the matrix.","Bound water resists compression like a cushion, and losing proteoglycan lets cartilage deform and wear."],"why":"Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage made of type II collagen and proteoglycan, mainly aggrecan. Each aggrecan molecule carries many chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate chains and attaches to hyaluronan to form huge aggregates. Their dense negative charge draws in water, which resists compression and is pushed out and drawn back with each load. Cartilage has no blood vessels, so chondrocytes rely on diffusion from synovial fluid, which helps explain why damaged cartilage repairs so poorly.","whyNot":["Correct.","Elastic fibers are found in elastic cartilage of the ear and epiglottis. Hyaline articular cartilage relies on bound water to resist compression.","Articular cartilage is avascular. Chondrocytes receive nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid.","Articular cartilage collagen is type II, and collagen fibrils mainly resist tension. Type I collagen is found in bone, tendon, and fibrocartilage."],"study":{"topics":["Cartilage matrix and aggrecan","Why cartilage heals poorly"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-E1"]}},{"id":"MS-C19-Q4","stem":"To reduce her knee pain, she begins a 12-week program of progressive resistance exercise for the quadriceps. Which of the following changes in the trained muscle is most likely?","options":["Higher mitochondrial density, mainly in type I fibers","Enlargement of existing fibers, mainly type II","Formation of many new fibers by fiber splitting","Conversion of most type II fibers to type I fibers"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the two training types: endurance training improves oxidative capacity, and resistance training builds strength.","Strength gains come mostly from adding myofibrils to existing fibers.","Type II fibers show the most hypertrophy with resistance training."],"why":"Type I fibers are slow, oxidative, red, and rich in mitochondria and myoglobin, and they resist fatigue. Type II fibers are fast and glycolytic, generate high force, and fatigue quickly. Heavy resistance training enlarges existing fibers by adding actin and myosin filaments, with the largest gains in type II fibers, so strength increases. Endurance training instead increases mitochondria, capillaries, and oxidative enzymes, mainly in type I fibers. Early strength gains in the first weeks also come from better neural recruitment.","whyNot":["More mitochondria in type I fibers is an adaptation to endurance training, such as long-distance running or cycling.","Correct.","In adult humans, strength training enlarges existing fibers. New fiber formation contributes little or nothing.","Training changes fiber subtypes only slightly, mostly within type II fibers. It does not convert most fast fibers into slow fibers."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle fiber types","Adaptations to training"],"comps":["MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C20","system":"MS","dx":"Postmenopausal osteoporosis with vertebral compression fracture","codes":["MS-13-E2"],"case":"A 71-year-old woman comes to the physician because of sudden midback pain that began 5 days ago when she lifted a bag of groceries. She has lost 4 cm of height over 10 years. Menopause occurred at the age of 47 years, and she has never taken hormone therapy. She has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years. Her body mass index is 19 kg/m2. Vital signs are within normal limits. Examination shows thoracic kyphosis and point tenderness over the T12 spinous process. Serum studies: calcium 9.4 mg/dL (normal 8.5 to 10.5), phosphorus 3.6 mg/dL (normal 2.5 to 4.5), alkaline phosphatase 82 U/L (normal 40 to 120), and PTH 38 pg/mL (normal 10 to 65). A radiograph shows anterior wedging of the T12 vertebral body. Diagnosis: postmenopausal osteoporosis with a vertebral compression fracture.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C20-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the accelerated bone loss that began after her menopause?","options":["Defective mineralization of osteoid from vitamin D deficiency","Less calcitonin secretion, which releases osteoclasts from inhibition","Lower PTH secretion, which slows osteoblast-driven bone formation","More RANKL and less osteoprotegerin, increasing osteoclast formation"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-E2","obj":"MS-13-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the hormonal change: loss of estrogen at menopause.","Recall that estrogen restrains bone resorption by lowering RANKL and raising osteoprotegerin.","Without estrogen, more osteoclasts form and live longer, so resorption outpaces formation."],"why":"Osteoblasts and stromal cells express RANKL, which binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their formation and activity. Osteoprotegerin is a decoy receptor that blocks RANKL. Estrogen lowers RANKL, raises osteoprotegerin, reduces bone-resorbing cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6, and TNF, and shortens osteoclast life span. After menopause these brakes are lost, and trabecular bone, as in the vertebrae, thins fastest. Her early menopause, smoking, and low body weight add to the risk, as do low peak bone mass and aging.","whyNot":["Defective mineralization is osteomalacia. In osteoporosis the bone that remains is normally mineralized, and her calcium, phosphorus, and alkaline phosphatase are normal.","Calcitonin plays only a small role in adult bone. People who lose it after thyroidectomy do not develop osteoporosis from that loss.","Low PTH would reduce bone resorption and would not explain bone loss. Her PTH is normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Estrogen and RANKL","Risk factors for osteoporosis"],"comps":["MS-13-E2","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C20-Q2","stem":"Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry shows a lumbar spine T-score of minus 3.1. Which of the following best describes the meaning of this value?","options":["3.1 standard deviations below the mean for women of her age","31 percent below the mean bone mass for women her age","31 percent below the young adult female mean bone mass","3.1 standard deviations below the young adult female mean"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E2","obj":"MS-13-E.4","dok":3,"approach":["A T-score compares bone density with the peak value of healthy young adults of the same sex.","It is expressed in standard deviations.","A Z-score is the measure that compares with people of the same age."],"why":"The T-score is the number of standard deviations a patient's bone density lies above or below the mean for healthy young adults of the same sex. A T-score of minus 1 or higher is normal, between minus 1 and minus 2.5 is osteopenia, and minus 2.5 or lower is osteoporosis. A fragility fracture of the spine or hip establishes osteoporosis on its own, whatever the T-score. Typical findings are vertebral compression, hip, and distal radius fractures, height loss, and kyphosis, with normal serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase.","whyNot":["Comparison with people of the same age and sex is the Z-score, which is used for premenopausal women, men under 50, and children.","This mixes two errors: the comparison group of the Z-score and a percentage instead of standard deviations.","T-scores are expressed in standard deviations. A percentage loss cannot be read directly from the score.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["DEXA T-score and Z-score","Osteoporosis findings"],"comps":["MS-13-E2"]}},{"id":"MS-C20-Q3","stem":"Which of the following sets of serum findings would have pointed to osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency instead of osteoporosis?","options":["Low calcium, low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH","Normal calcium, normal phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, normal PTH","High calcium, low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH","Low calcium, high phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, high PTH"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-C","obj":"MS-13-C.3","dok":3,"approach":["Vitamin D deficiency lowers intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate.","Low calcium triggers secondary hyperparathyroidism, and PTH makes the kidney waste phosphate.","Poorly mineralized osteoid stimulates osteoblasts, which raises alkaline phosphatase."],"why":"In osteoporosis, bone mass is low but its mineral is normal, so calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and PTH are all normal, as in this patient. In vitamin D deficiency osteomalacia, calcium is low or low-normal, phosphate is low, PTH is high, and alkaline phosphatase is high. Primary hyperparathyroidism raises calcium and lowers phosphate. Renal osteodystrophy lowers calcium and raises phosphate because the failing kidney cannot excrete phosphate or make calcitriol. Paget disease raises alkaline phosphatase alone.","whyNot":["Correct.","An isolated high alkaline phosphatase with normal minerals and PTH is the pattern of Paget disease of bone.","High calcium with low phosphate and high PTH is primary hyperparathyroidism, usually from a parathyroid adenoma.","Low calcium with high phosphate and high PTH is renal osteodystrophy from chronic kidney disease."],"study":{"topics":["Lab patterns in metabolic bone disease","Osteomalacia"],"comps":["MS-13-C","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C20-Q4","stem":"She has taken warfarin for 6 years for atrial fibrillation. Which of the following modifications of a bone matrix protein is most likely reduced by this drug?","options":["Lysyl oxidase cross-linking of type I collagen fibrils","Hydroxylation of proline residues in type I collagen","Gamma-carboxylation of glutamate in osteocalcin","Phosphorylation of serine residues in osteopontin"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, so active vitamin K is depleted.","Vitamin K is the cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues.","Apply this beyond clotting factors: osteoblasts make osteocalcin, a vitamin K dependent protein."],"why":"Vitamin K is needed by gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which adds a carboxyl group to specific glutamate residues. This creates gamma-carboxyglutamate (Gla), which binds calcium. Clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X depend on it, and so does osteocalcin, a bone matrix protein made by osteoblasts that binds hydroxyapatite. Warfarin and vitamin K deficiency leave osteocalcin undercarboxylated. Other cofactors matter for bone and muscle too: copper for lysyl oxidase, vitamin C for collagen hydroxylation, and magnesium for PTH secretion.","whyNot":["Lysyl oxidase needs copper and is impaired in Menkes disease and copper deficiency.","Proline hydroxylation needs vitamin C and fails in scurvy. Warfarin does not affect it.","Correct.","Osteopontin phosphorylation is done by kinases and does not depend on vitamin K."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin K and osteocalcin","Minerals and cofactors in bone"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-E2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C21","system":"MS","dx":"Paget disease of bone","codes":["MS-13-E3"],"case":"A 68-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of a deep, aching pain in his right hip and pelvis that is worse at night. He has also noticed that his hats no longer fit. Physical examination shows frontal bossing and warm skin over the right anterior thigh. Laboratory studies show: calcium 9.6 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 3.4 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), alkaline phosphatase 410 U/L (40 to 130), and parathyroid hormone 38 pg/mL (10 to 65). Radiographs show a thickened cortex and coarse trabeculae in the right hemipelvis and proximal femur, and patchy areas of increased density in the skull. Diagnosis: Paget disease of bone.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C21-Q1","stem":"A bone biopsy is taken from the right iliac crest. Which of the following histologic findings is most likely?","options":["Trabeculae with retained cores of calcified cartilage","Mosaic of lamellar bone joined by irregular cement lines","Wide seams of unmineralized osteoid covering normal trabeculae","Thin, widely spaced trabeculae with normal mineralization"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E3","obj":"MS-13-E.5","dok":2,"approach":["Notice an older man with bone pain, enlarging skull, and an isolated high alkaline phosphatase with normal calcium, phosphate, and PTH.","That pattern points to Paget disease, a disorder of disorganized remodeling.","Predict the tissue result: repeated rounds of resorption and hasty repair leave a mosaic of lamellar bone."],"why":"Paget disease starts with overactive, oversized osteoclasts that resorb bone. Osteoblasts then lay down new bone quickly and in a disorganized way. The many rounds of resorption and repair leave lamellar bone pieces joined by irregular cement lines, the classic mosaic pattern. The bone is thick but weak, and the high osteoblast activity raises alkaline phosphatase while calcium, phosphate, and PTH stay normal.","whyNot":["Retained cartilage cores reflect failed osteoclast resorption in osteopetrosis. Paget disease is the opposite at the start, with too much osteoclast activity.","Correct.","Excess unmineralized osteoid is the hallmark of osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency. That causes low or low-normal calcium and phosphate with high PTH, which this patient does not have.","This is osteoporosis: less bone of normal quality. Osteoporosis does not thicken the cortex, enlarge the skull, or raise alkaline phosphatase on its own."],"study":{"topics":["Paget disease phases and mosaic bone","Isolated alkaline phosphatase elevation"],"comps":["MS-13-E3","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C21-Q2","stem":"Two years later, he reports shortness of breath on exertion. His pulse is 104/min and bounding, and the skin over the affected pelvis and skull is warm. Echocardiography shows a cardiac output of 9.5 L/min (4 to 8) with normal valves. Which of the following best explains these cardiac findings?","options":["Anemia from replacement of marrow by fibrous tissue","Hypercalcemia shortening the ventricular action potential","Systemic hypertension from calcification of large arteries","Arteriovenous shunting through hypervascular pagetic bone"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-E3","obj":"MS-13-E.6","dok":3,"approach":["Link warm skin over pagetic bone to increased blood flow through that bone.","Recall that very vascular pagetic bone acts like many small arteriovenous fistulas.","Low resistance lowers afterload and raises venous return, so the heart must pump a high output, which can lead to high-output failure."],"why":"In active Paget disease the remodeling bone and its marrow become highly vascular. When much of the skeleton is involved, the blood flowing through these beds acts like arteriovenous shunts. Systemic vascular resistance falls, venous return rises, and the heart responds with a high output and wide pulse pressure. Over time this load can produce high-output heart failure.","whyNot":["Severe anemia can cause high-output failure, but Paget disease does not typically cause marrow failure. Fibrous marrow in Paget disease is local, not widespread enough to cause anemia.","His calcium is normal. Hypercalcemia shortens the QT interval but does not raise cardiac output.","Arterial stiffening raises afterload and causes pressure overload. It does not raise cardiac output or warm the skin over the bones.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["High-output heart failure","Complications of Paget disease"],"comps":["MS-13-E3"]}},{"id":"MS-C21-Q3","stem":"Over the next year he develops left-sided sensorineural hearing loss and weakness of all the muscles of facial expression on the left. Narrowing of which of the following openings by thickened bone best explains both findings?","options":["Superior orbital fissure","Jugular foramen","Internal acoustic meatus","Stylomastoid foramen"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the nerves involved: facial weakness points to cranial nerve VII and sensorineural hearing loss to cranial nerve VIII.","Ask which skull opening carries both nerves together.","Cranial nerves VII and VIII enter the petrous temporal bone together through the internal acoustic meatus."],"why":"Cranial nerves VII and VIII leave the posterior cranial fossa together through the internal acoustic meatus in the petrous temporal bone. Thickening of the temporal bone in Paget disease can narrow this canal and compress both nerves. Hearing loss in Paget disease can also be conductive from involvement of the ossicles, but the combination with facial weakness places the lesion at the meatus.","whyNot":["This fissure carries cranial nerves III, IV, V1, and VI to the orbit. Compression would cause eye movement problems and forehead numbness, not hearing loss or facial weakness.","The jugular foramen carries cranial nerves IX, X, and XI and the internal jugular vein. Compression would cause swallowing and voice problems and trapezius weakness.","Correct.","Only the facial nerve exits here. Compression could cause facial weakness, but it could not explain sensorineural hearing loss."],"study":{"topics":["Skull foramina and their contents","Hearing loss in Paget disease"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-13-E3"]}},{"id":"MS-C21-Q4","stem":"He falls and fractures his left femoral shaft and is kept on bed rest for 3 weeks. His serum calcium rises to 11.8 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5). Which of the following sets of hormone changes is most likely?","options":["Low PTH and low calcitriol","Low PTH and high calcitriol","High PTH and high calcitriol","High PTH and low calcitriol"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the source of the extra calcium: immobilization speeds osteoclast resorption, and pagetic bone already turns over fast.","Calcium-sensing receptors on the parathyroid glands respond to the high calcium by cutting PTH secretion.","Without PTH, renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase is not stimulated, so calcitriol falls."],"why":"Bed rest removes the mechanical loading that keeps bone formation and resorption balanced, and resorption wins. In a patient whose bone already turns over rapidly, this can release enough calcium to cause hypercalcemia. The parathyroid glands sense high calcium and lower PTH. PTH normally stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the proximal tubule, so calcitriol also falls. This is a PTH-independent hypercalcemia.","whyNot":["Correct.","High calcitriol with low PTH occurs when calcitriol is made outside the kidney, as in sarcoidosis. Bed rest does not do this, and without PTH the renal enzyme is not stimulated.","This pattern is seen in primary hyperparathyroidism, where PTH drives the hypercalcemia. Here the calcium comes from bone resorption, so PTH should be suppressed.","High PTH with low calcitriol occurs in chronic kidney disease, where the kidney cannot make calcitriol. Calcium is usually low or normal in that setting."],"study":{"topics":["PTH and calcitriol regulation","Hypercalcemia of immobilization"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-E3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C22","system":"MS","dx":"Avascular necrosis of the femoral head in SLE treated with glucocorticoids","codes":["MS-13-E4"],"case":"A 32-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of right groin pain that is worse with walking. For the past 2 years she has taken prednisone 20 to 40 mg daily for lupus nephritis. She does not drink alcohol. Physical examination shows pain and limited range of motion on internal rotation of the right hip. Radiographs of the hip are normal. MRI shows a band of low signal intensity outlining a wedge of subchondral bone in the anterosuperior right femoral head, with no collapse. Diagnosis: avascular necrosis of the right femoral head related to glucocorticoid therapy and SLE.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C22-Q1","stem":"A core decompression is performed, and tissue from the lesion is examined. Which of the following histologic findings is most likely?","options":["Mosaic lamellar bone with irregular cement lines and fibrovascular marrow","A fragment of dead bone surrounded by neutrophils and reactive new bone","Wide seams of unmineralized osteoid lining the trabeculae","Trabeculae with empty osteocyte lacunae and necrotic marrow fat"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E4","obj":"MS-13-E.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize avascular necrosis as an infarct of bone and marrow from interrupted blood flow.","Ask what dead bone looks like: osteocytes die and leave empty lacunae, and marrow fat undergoes necrosis.","Choose the option that shows ischemic cell death without infection or a remodeling disorder."],"why":"Avascular necrosis is ischemic death of bone and marrow. Osteocytes die and their lacunae are left empty, and the marrow fat becomes necrotic. The dead subchondral bone cannot remodel well, so it can fracture and collapse, flattening the joint surface. Glucocorticoids, alcohol, SLE, sickle cell disease, decompression sickness, and femoral neck or scaphoid fractures are classic causes, and the femoral head is the most common site because its retinacular arteries have few collaterals.","whyNot":["This is Paget disease, which comes from disordered remodeling, not loss of blood supply. It occurs in older adults with high alkaline phosphatase.","A sequestrum with an involucrum is the picture of chronic osteomyelitis. That needs infection, and she has no fever or other signs of infection.","Wide osteoid seams mean poor mineralization, as in osteomalacia. Bone cells stay alive in osteomalacia.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Avascular necrosis causes and sites","Bone infarct histology"],"comps":["MS-13-E4","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C22-Q2","stem":"Three years before her hip pain began, she had pain and swelling in the small joints of both hands. Which of the following findings in her hands is most likely?","options":["Sclerodactyly with fingertip calcinosis","Marginal bony erosions of the metacarpophalangeal joints","Reducible deformities without erosions on radiographs","Pencil-in-cup deformities of the distal joints"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the typical arthritis of SLE: symmetric small-joint polyarthritis.","Lupus synovitis does not erode bone; its deformities come from lax ligaments and joint capsules.","Pick the option that combines deformity with no erosions."],"why":"Arthritis is one of the most common features of SLE and usually involves the small joints of the hands symmetrically, like rheumatoid arthritis. Lupus synovitis does not form the invasive pannus that erodes cartilage and bone in rheumatoid arthritis. Some patients develop ulnar deviation and swan-neck deformities from lax tendons and capsules, which can be pushed back into place and show no erosions on radiographs (Jaccoud arthropathy).","whyNot":["Tight, thickened skin over the fingers with calcium deposits is a feature of systemic sclerosis, not SLE.","Marginal erosions come from invasive pannus in rheumatoid arthritis. Lupus arthritis does not form an erosive pannus.","Correct.","Pencil-in-cup erosions of the distal interphalangeal joints are typical of psoriatic arthritis, a seronegative spondyloarthropathy."],"study":{"topics":["SLE arthritis","Comparing inflammatory arthritides"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-B"]}},{"id":"MS-C22-Q3","stem":"During flares of her lupus, serum C3 and C4 fall and anti-double-stranded DNA titers rise, and her joint symptoms worsen. Which of the following mechanisms best explains her joint inflammation during these flares?","options":["Mast cell degranulation triggered by IgE cross-linking","Deposited circulating immune complexes that fix complement","Antibody binding to a fixed antigen on synovial lining cells","CD8+ T cells directly lysing muscle and synovial cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["Link low C3 and C4 to complement consumption.","Link anti-dsDNA antibodies to circulating antigen-antibody complexes.","Complexes that lodge in tissue and activate complement define type III hypersensitivity."],"why":"In SLE, antibodies bind nuclear antigens such as DNA to form circulating immune complexes. These complexes deposit in the synovium, skin, kidney, and vessel walls and activate the classical complement pathway, which consumes C3 and C4. Complement fragments such as C5a recruit neutrophils, which cause the inflammation. This is type III hypersensitivity, and falling complement with rising anti-dsDNA is a marker of active disease.","whyNot":["This is type I hypersensitivity, as in allergic reactions. It does not consume C3 and C4 or involve anti-DNA antibodies.","Correct.","Antibody against a fixed tissue antigen is type II hypersensitivity, as in the polyarthritis of rheumatic fever. Anti-DNA antibodies form circulating complexes instead.","Direct T-cell killing is type IV hypersensitivity, as in polymyositis. It does not lower complement levels."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types","Immune complexes in SLE"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-E4"]}},{"id":"MS-C22-Q4","stem":"She also reports difficulty rising from a chair and climbing stairs. Examination shows symmetric weakness of the hip flexors and shoulder abductors without tenderness. Serum creatine kinase is 85 U/L (30 to 170). Which of the following findings on a muscle biopsy is most likely?","options":["Perifascicular atrophy with perimysial inflammatory cells","Endomysial CD8+ T cells with necrotic muscle fibers","Fiber type grouping with groups of atrophic fibers","Selective atrophy of type II fibers without necrosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Notice proximal weakness with a normal creatine kinase in a patient on long-term glucocorticoids.","A normal CK argues against an inflammatory myopathy with fiber necrosis.","Glucocorticoid myopathy selectively shrinks type II (fast glycolytic) fibers."],"why":"Long-term glucocorticoids cause a proximal myopathy that mainly affects the hip and shoulder girdles. Steroids increase muscle protein breakdown and reduce synthesis, and type II fast glycolytic fibers are the most affected. Because the fibers shrink but do not die, creatine kinase stays normal. Disuse causes the same type II atrophy pattern.","whyNot":["Perifascicular atrophy is the hallmark of dermatomyositis, which raises CK and usually shows a skin rash.","This is polymyositis or lupus myositis. Active fiber necrosis releases creatine kinase, so CK would be high.","Fiber type grouping and grouped atrophy follow denervation and reinnervation, as in motor neuron disease. Neurogenic weakness also brings fasciculations and is often distal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Steroid myopathy","Neurogenic vs myopathic weakness"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C23","system":"MS","dx":"Chondrosarcoma arising from an osteochondroma","codes":["MS-13-F1"],"case":"A 41-year-old man comes to the physician because of an 8-month history of a slowly enlarging, painful mass over his left hip. Since childhood he has had multiple painless bony lumps near his knees, shoulders, and hips, and his father had similar lumps. Physical examination shows a firm, fixed mass over the left iliac wing and several hard, nontender bony prominences around both knees. Radiographs show multiple bony outgrowths from the metaphyses of long bones. MRI of the left ilium shows an outgrowth with a lobulated cartilage cap 4 cm thick. Biopsy shows a malignant cartilage-forming tumor. Diagnosis: secondary chondrosarcoma arising from an osteochondroma in hereditary multiple osteochondromas.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C23-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in the resected tumor would most strongly support chondrosarcoma rather than osteosarcoma?","options":["Malignant chondrocytes in hyaline matrix with no tumor osteoid","Mononuclear stromal cells mixed with osteoclast-like giant cells","Pleomorphic spindle cells laying down lacelike osteoid directly","Sheets of small round blue cells with membranous CD99 staining"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F1","obj":"MS-13-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that bone sarcomas are named for the matrix the malignant cells make.","Chondrosarcoma cells make cartilage only; any osteoid made directly by tumor cells makes the tumor an osteosarcoma.","Choose the option with malignant cartilage and no tumor osteoid."],"why":"Chondrosarcoma is a malignant tumor whose cells make hyaline or myxoid cartilage matrix but no osteoid. It is a tumor of middle-aged and older adults and favors the pelvis, proximal femur, and shoulder girdle. It may arise on its own, from an enchondroma, or in the cartilage cap of an osteochondroma, as here. A thick cap (more than about 2 cm in an adult) and new pain in an old lesion are warning signs of malignant change.","whyNot":["Correct.","This describes giant cell tumor of bone, an epiphyseal tumor of young adults that does not make cartilage.","Osteoid made directly by malignant cells defines osteosarcoma. Osteosarcoma can contain cartilage, but tumor osteoid places it in that category.","Small round blue cells with CD99 are Ewing sarcoma, a tumor of children and adolescents, usually in the diaphysis or pelvis."],"study":{"topics":["Chondrosarcoma features","Matrix-based classification of bone tumors"],"comps":["MS-13-F1","MS-13-F4"]}},{"id":"MS-C23-Q2","stem":"Genetic testing shows a germline loss-of-function mutation in EXT1. Which of the following best describes the lesions he has had around his knees since childhood?","options":["Expansile lytic lesions of the epiphysis with thin cortical shells","Intramedullary nodules of hyaline cartilage in the bones of the hands","Bony stalks continuous with the marrow cavity and capped by cartilage","Small cortical nidus of osteoid surrounded by dense reactive bone"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize EXT1 or EXT2 mutations as the cause of hereditary multiple osteochondromas.","Recall the structure of an osteochondroma.","The cortex and marrow of the stalk are continuous with the parent bone, and a hyaline cartilage cap sits on top."],"why":"EXT1 and EXT2 encode enzymes that make heparan sulfate, which helps control growth-plate signaling. Loss of both copies in growth-plate chondrocytes lets cartilage grow out sideways from the metaphysis, forming an osteochondroma. The lesion is a bony stalk whose cortex and marrow are continuous with the parent bone, covered by a cartilage cap. Solitary osteochondromas rarely turn malignant, but the risk of chondrosarcoma is higher in the hereditary multiple form.","whyNot":["Expansile epiphyseal lytic lesions suggest giant cell tumor of bone, which occurs in young adults after the growth plates close.","This is enchondroma. Many enchondromas occur in Ollier disease and, with hemangiomas, in Maffucci syndrome; these are not caused by EXT mutations.","Correct.","This is osteoid osteoma, a painful tumor of young people that is not hereditary and does not form a palpable bony lump."],"study":{"topics":["Osteochondroma vs enchondroma","EXT1 and EXT2"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C23-Q3","stem":"Which of the following is the main fibrillar collagen in the cartilage cap of the benign lesions around his knees?","options":["Type II","Type III","Type I","Type IV"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the tissue: the cap of an osteochondroma is hyaline cartilage, like a growth plate.","Match hyaline cartilage to its collagen type.","Hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus are made mainly of type II collagen."],"why":"The cap of an osteochondroma is hyaline cartilage that behaves like a growth plate, and its main collagen is type II. Type II collagen forms thin fibrils that hold proteoglycans such as aggrecan, giving cartilage its resistance to compression. Below the cap, the cartilage undergoes endochondral ossification into the type I collagen bone of the stalk.","whyNot":["Correct.","Type III collagen forms reticular fibers and is abundant in blood vessel walls and early granulation tissue.","Type I collagen is the main collagen of bone, tendon, ligament, and fibrocartilage. It forms the bony stalk of an osteochondroma, not the cap.","Type IV collagen is a network-forming collagen of basement membranes, not a fibrillar collagen of cartilage."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types by tissue","Hyaline cartilage matrix"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C23-Q4","stem":"A second outgrowth on the neck of his right fibula enlarges. He develops right foot drop. Which of the following additional findings is most likely?","options":["Weak knee extension and numbness on the medial leg","Weak foot eversion and numbness on the dorsum of the foot","Weak plantar flexion and numbness on the sole of the foot","Weak hip adduction and numbness on the medial thigh"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.5","dok":3,"approach":["Locate the lesion: the neck of the fibula.","The common fibular nerve wraps around the fibular neck, where it is exposed to compression and fracture.","Predict its deficits: the deep branch supplies dorsiflexors and the superficial branch supplies evertors and most of the dorsal foot skin."],"why":"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve winds around the lateral side of the fibular neck just under the skin, so masses, fractures, and casts there can compress it. Its deep branch supplies the anterior compartment dorsiflexors, so injury causes foot drop. Its superficial branch supplies the lateral compartment evertors and the skin of most of the dorsum of the foot, so eversion is weak and that skin is numb.","whyNot":["These point to the femoral nerve and its saphenous branch, which do not pass near the fibula.","Correct.","These are signs of tibial nerve injury. The tibial nerve runs deep in the posterior leg, away from the fibular neck.","These are obturator nerve signs. The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh and runs nowhere near the fibula."],"study":{"topics":["Common fibular nerve injury","Leg compartments and their nerves"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-5"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C24","system":"MS","dx":"Ewing sarcoma of the femur","codes":["MS-13-F2"],"case":"A 13-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a 6-week history of pain and swelling of the left thigh. He has had intermittent fevers and has lost 3 kg (6.6 lb). His temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F). Physical examination shows a tender, warm mass over the mid left thigh. Leukocyte count is 12,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000), and erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 58 mm/h (0 to 15). A radiograph shows a permeative lytic lesion in the femoral diaphysis with layered, onion-skin periosteal new bone and a large soft tissue mass. Blood cultures are negative. Biopsy shows sheets of uniform small round blue cells. Molecular testing confirms Ewing sarcoma.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C24-Q1","stem":"Which of the following immunohistochemical staining patterns is most likely in this tumor?","options":["Nuclear myogenin with strong cytoplasmic desmin","Strong membranous CD45 with negative CD99","Cytoplasmic synaptophysin with negative CD99","Membranous CD99 with negative myogenin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F2","obj":"MS-13-F.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the small round blue cell tumors of childhood: Ewing sarcoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, lymphoma, and neuroblastoma.","A diaphyseal bone tumor with onion-skin periosteum, fever, and high ESR in a teenager fits Ewing sarcoma.","Ewing sarcoma cells show strong membranous CD99 and lack muscle, leukocyte, and neuroendocrine markers."],"why":"Ewing sarcoma is a tumor of children and adolescents made of sheets of uniform small round blue cells. It usually arises in the diaphysis of long bones or in the pelvis, and the periosteum lifts in layers to form onion-skin new bone. Strong membranous CD99 staining supports the diagnosis, and the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion from t(11;22) confirms it. Fever, leukocytosis, and a high ESR are common and can mimic osteomyelitis, which is why biopsy and cultures matter.","whyNot":["Myogenin and desmin mark skeletal muscle differentiation in rhabdomyosarcoma, another small round blue cell tumor of children, usually in the head and neck or genitourinary tract.","CD45 is the common leukocyte antigen. A CD45-positive small round cell tumor is a lymphoma.","Synaptophysin marks neuroendocrine tumors such as neuroblastoma, which usually arises in the adrenal medulla of young children.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Small round blue cell tumors","Ewing sarcoma markers"],"comps":["MS-13-F2","MS-13-F5"]}},{"id":"MS-C24-Q2","stem":"Molecular testing shows a t(11;22)(q24;q12) translocation. Which of the following best describes the product of this rearrangement?","options":["An anti-apoptotic protein overexpressed from an immunoglobulin promoter","A constitutively active cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase","A chimeric transcription factor that activates abnormal target genes","A truncated tumor suppressor that can no longer bind E2F"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the genes at the breakpoints: EWSR1 on chromosome 22 and FLI1 on chromosome 11.","FLI1 is an ETS family transcription factor.","The fusion joins the EWSR1 activation domain to the FLI1 DNA-binding domain, making a new transcription factor."],"why":"The t(11;22) translocation fuses the EWSR1 gene on chromosome 22 to FLI1 on chromosome 11. The fusion protein keeps the DNA-binding domain of FLI1, an ETS family transcription factor, and gains a strong activation region from EWSR1. It binds DNA and turns on a set of genes that drive growth and block normal differentiation. Detecting this fusion confirms the diagnosis.","whyNot":["Moving BCL2 next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain promoter by t(14;18) causes follicular lymphoma.","A fused, always-on tyrosine kinase is the product of BCR-ABL1 from t(9;22) in chronic myeloid leukemia.","Correct.","Loss of RB function frees E2F and drives osteosarcoma and retinoblastoma. It is a loss of a tumor suppressor, not a fusion gene."],"study":{"topics":["EWSR1-FLI1 fusion","Translocations in cancer"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-F2"]}},{"id":"MS-C24-Q3","stem":"Which of the following embryonic tissues gives rise to the bone in which this tumor arose?","options":["Sclerotome of the paraxial mesoderm","Neural crest mesenchyme","Somatic layer of lateral plate mesoderm","Intermediate mesoderm of the nephrogenic cord"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the bone: the femur, part of the appendicular skeleton.","Recall that limb buds grow out from the body wall, which is lined by the somatic layer of lateral plate mesoderm.","Limb bones, cartilage, and tendons come from this layer; somites supply limb muscle instead."],"why":"The limb buds form when somatic lateral plate mesoderm and its overlying ectoderm grow out from the body wall. This mesoderm forms the limb bones, cartilages, tendons, and ligaments. Limb muscles come from myotome cells that migrate in from the somites. The axial skeleton comes from sclerotome, and most of the facial skeleton comes from neural crest.","whyNot":["Sclerotome forms the vertebrae and ribs, the axial skeleton. Limb bones do not come from somites.","Neural crest mesenchyme forms much of the bone and cartilage of the face and anterior skull, not the limbs.","Correct.","Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys and gonads and does not form bone."],"study":{"topics":["Embryonic origins of the skeleton","Limb bud development"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-1"]}},{"id":"MS-C24-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the region of the femur where this tumor arose first ossified during development?","options":["A primary center within a hyaline cartilage model","A secondary center within a hyaline cartilage model","Direct ossification within a sheet of mesenchyme","Cavitation of interzone mesenchyme"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.5","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the tumor: the femoral diaphysis.","Recall that long bones form by endochondral ossification on a hyaline cartilage model.","The first, or primary, ossification center appears in the diaphysis before birth."],"why":"The femur forms by endochondral ossification. Mesenchyme first makes a hyaline cartilage model. Early in development a periosteal bone collar forms around the middle of the shaft, the central cartilage calcifies, and blood vessels bring in osteoprogenitor cells to form the primary ossification center in the diaphysis. Secondary centers appear later in the epiphyses, and the growth plate between them allows lengthening.","whyNot":["Correct.","Secondary ossification centers form in the epiphyses, mostly around birth or after. The tumor is in the shaft.","Intramembranous ossification forms the flat bones of the skull vault and most of the clavicle, not the femoral shaft.","Cavitation of interzone mesenchyme creates synovial joint cavities. It does not make bone."],"study":{"topics":["Endochondral vs intramembranous ossification","Primary and secondary ossification centers"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C25","system":"MS","dx":"Osteoid osteoma of the tibia","codes":["MS-13-F3"],"case":"A 17-year-old boy comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of pain in his right shin. The pain is worst at night and wakes him from sleep. It goes away within an hour of taking ibuprofen. He has no fever or weight loss. Physical examination shows mild tenderness over the anterior right tibia without swelling or warmth. Leukocyte count and erythrocyte sedimentation rate are within normal limits. CT shows a round 1.2 cm lucent nidus within the anterior cortex of the tibial diaphysis, surrounded by dense sclerotic bone. Diagnosis: osteoid osteoma.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C25-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the pattern of his pain and its response to ibuprofen?","options":["Osteoclasts in the sclerotic rim release bradykinin locally","Prostaglandin E2 made by cyclooxygenase in the nidus","Mast cells around the nidus release histamine","Neutrophils in the nidus release leukotriene B4"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F3","obj":"MS-13-F.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the classic history: night pain from a small cortical lesion that is relieved by NSAIDs.","NSAIDs work by blocking cyclooxygenase.","So the pain must depend on a cyclooxygenase product, prostaglandin E2 made by the nidus."],"why":"Osteoid osteoma is a benign bone-forming tumor under about 2 cm, usually in the cortex of the femur or tibia of adolescents and young men. The nidus of osteoid and woven bone makes large amounts of prostaglandin E2, which causes the pain. NSAIDs block cyclooxygenase and so relieve the pain dramatically. Osteoblastoma has similar tissue but is larger than 2 cm, favors the vertebrae, and its pain is not relieved by NSAIDs.","whyNot":["Bradykinin is made from kininogen by kallikrein in plasma, and NSAIDs do not block its production.","Correct.","Histamine release is not blocked by NSAIDs, and the nidus is not a mast cell lesion.","Leukotrienes come from the lipoxygenase pathway, which NSAIDs do not block. The nidus is not a neutrophilic abscess."],"study":{"topics":["Osteoid osteoma vs osteoblastoma","Prostaglandins and pain"],"comps":["MS-13-F3","MS-13-F"]}},{"id":"MS-C25-Q2","stem":"The nidus is made of irregular trabeculae of woven bone rimmed by osteoblasts. Compared with the normal lamellar bone of the surrounding cortex, which of the following features best describes woven bone?","options":["Randomly oriented collagen with more osteocytes per volume","Concentric lamellae around central vascular canals","Parallel collagen sheets that change direction in each layer","Fewer osteocytes with greater resistance to bending"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that woven bone is the first bone made in development, fracture repair, and fast-growing lesions.","It is laid down quickly, so its collagen is disorganized and many osteoblasts are trapped as osteocytes.","Lamellar bone is laid down slowly in ordered layers and forms osteons in compact bone."],"why":"Woven bone is immature bone made quickly. Its collagen fibers run in random directions, it contains more osteocytes, and it is less mineralized and weaker than lamellar bone. Lamellar bone has collagen in orderly layers. In compact bone, these lamellae form osteons around Haversian canals, with osteocytes in lacunae connected by canaliculi and Volkmann canals linking the osteons. In adults, woven bone points to rapid bone formation such as a healing fracture, Paget disease, or a bone-forming tumor.","whyNot":["Correct.","Concentric lamellae around a Haversian canal form an osteon, the unit of mature compact lamellar bone.","Collagen in parallel sheets that alternate direction between layers is the structure of lamellar bone and gives it strength.","Woven bone is weaker than lamellar bone, not stronger, and it has more osteocytes, not fewer."],"study":{"topics":["Woven vs lamellar bone","Compact bone structure"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-F3"]}},{"id":"MS-C25-Q3","stem":"Giant cell tumor of bone was included in the radiologist's differential diagnosis. In giant cell tumor of bone, which of the following cell populations are the neoplastic cells?","options":["Multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells with RANK","Synovial cells that overexpress CSF1","Osteoblasts that secrete prostaglandin E2","Mononuclear stromal cells that express RANKL"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.8","dok":3,"approach":["Recall giant cell tumor of bone: an epiphyseal, soap-bubble lytic lesion in young adults.","The striking giant cells are osteoclast-like cells, but they are not the tumor cells.","The neoplastic mononuclear stromal cells express RANKL, which recruits and activates the giant cells."],"why":"Giant cell tumor of bone is a locally aggressive tumor of the epiphysis in young adults, usually around the knee, and appears as an expansile soap-bubble lytic lesion. The neoplastic cells are mononuclear stromal cells that express RANKL. RANKL binds RANK on monocyte precursors and drives them to fuse into the osteoclast-like giant cells that destroy bone. This is why the RANKL-blocking antibody denosumab can shrink these tumors.","whyNot":["The giant cells are reactive. They are recruited and activated by RANKL from the stromal cells, and they cause the bone destruction.","CSF1 overexpression drives tenosynovial giant cell tumor, which arises from synovium or tendon sheaths, not inside bone.","Prostaglandin-secreting osteoblasts describe the nidus of this patient's osteoid osteoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Giant cell tumor of bone","RANKL and osteoclasts"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C25-Q4","stem":"Osteoblasts in the nidus secrete osteocalcin, a protein that binds the mineral phase of bone. Which of the following post-translational modifications allows osteocalcin to bind this mineral?","options":["Oxidative deamination of lysine, requiring copper","Gamma-carboxylation of glutamate, requiring vitamin K","Hydroxylation of proline residues, requiring vitamin C","Phosphorylation of serine, requiring magnesium"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the mineral of bone: calcium hydroxyapatite.","Calcium-binding proteins such as clotting factors and osteocalcin use gamma-carboxyglutamate residues to bind calcium.","Gamma-carboxylation needs reduced vitamin K as a cofactor."],"why":"Osteocalcin is made by osteoblasts and is one of the most abundant noncollagen proteins in bone. Vitamin K dependent gamma-glutamyl carboxylase adds a second carboxyl group to specific glutamate residues. These gamma-carboxyglutamate residues bind calcium and let osteocalcin attach to hydroxyapatite. The same modification activates clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X, which is why warfarin can also lower carboxylated osteocalcin.","whyNot":["Copper-dependent lysyl oxidase forms the cross-links that strengthen collagen and elastin. It does not modify osteocalcin.","Correct.","Prolyl hydroxylation stabilizes the collagen triple helix. Its loss in vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, not a defect in calcium binding by osteocalcin.","Magnesium supports kinases and PTH secretion, but the calcium-binding residues of osteocalcin come from carboxylation, not phosphorylation."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin K and gamma-carboxylation","Bone matrix proteins"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-6"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C26","system":"MS","dx":"Osteosarcoma of the distal femur after hereditary retinoblastoma","codes":["MS-13-F4"],"case":"A 15-year-old girl comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of pain and swelling above her right knee. She was treated for bilateral retinoblastoma in infancy. Physical examination shows a firm, tender mass over the distal right thigh. Serum alkaline phosphatase is 520 U/L (for age, about 50 to 260). A radiograph shows a destructive, mixed lytic and sclerotic lesion in the distal femoral metaphysis with a triangle of elevated periosteum at its edge and radiating spicules of new bone in the soft tissue. Biopsy shows pleomorphic malignant cells producing lacelike osteoid. Diagnosis: osteosarcoma.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C26-Q1","stem":"This tumor has a second, smaller age peak later in life. Which of the following groups is at increased risk for this tumor in later adulthood?","options":["Adults with multiple enchondromas","Adults with hereditary multiple osteochondromas","Older adults with Paget disease of bone","Older adults with long-standing gout"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F4","obj":"MS-13-F.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the bimodal age pattern of osteosarcoma: adolescents during the growth spurt and older adults.","In older adults, osteosarcoma usually follows a condition with high bone turnover or DNA damage.","Paget disease and prior radiation are the classic predisposing conditions."],"why":"Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor apart from myeloma. Its main peak is in adolescents, where it arises in the metaphysis around the knee during the growth spurt. A second, smaller peak occurs in older adults, usually on a background of Paget disease or previous radiation. The defining feature is osteoid made directly by malignant cells.","whyNot":["Multiple enchondromas in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome raise the risk of chondrosarcoma.","Hereditary multiple osteochondromas raise the risk of chondrosarcoma in the cartilage caps, not osteosarcoma.","Correct.","Gout causes erosive arthritis and tophi but does not lead to bone sarcoma."],"study":{"topics":["Osteosarcoma epidemiology","Paget disease and sarcoma risk"],"comps":["MS-13-F4","MS-13-E3"]}},{"id":"MS-C26-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her increased risk for this tumor?","options":["A somatic EWSR1-FLI1 fusion created by prior radiation exposure","A germline TP53 mutation that removes apoptosis after DNA damage","A gain-of-function RB1 mutation that keeps E2F tightly bound","A second hit in RB1 that frees E2F to push cells into S phase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-13-F4","obj":"MS-13-F.5","dok":3,"approach":["Bilateral retinoblastoma in infancy means a germline loss-of-function RB1 mutation in every cell.","Tumors arise when the second RB1 copy is lost in a cell, the two-hit model.","Without RB protein, E2F is free to drive the G1 to S transition."],"why":"Children with hereditary retinoblastoma inherit or are born with one nonfunctional RB1 allele in every cell. When a bone cell loses the second allele, it has no working RB protein. RB normally binds E2F and holds the cell in G1 until cyclin D and CDK4/6 phosphorylate it. Without RB, E2F drives cells into S phase unchecked. Osteosarcoma is the most common second cancer in these patients. Its imaging shows a Codman triangle of lifted periosteum and a sunburst pattern of tumor bone, and it spreads through the blood to the lungs.","whyNot":["EWSR1-FLI1 defines Ewing sarcoma, a small round cell tumor that does not make osteoid.","Germline TP53 loss in Li-Fraumeni syndrome also raises osteosarcoma risk, but her history of bilateral retinoblastoma points to a germline RB1 mutation.","Bound E2F holds cells in G1, which would block growth. Retinoblastoma and osteosarcoma come from loss of RB function.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["RB1 and the two-hit hypothesis","Osteosarcoma imaging and spread"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-F4"]}},{"id":"MS-C26-Q3","stem":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of back pain. Radiographs show multiple dense, sclerotic lesions in the lumbar vertebrae and pelvis. Which of the following is the most likely primary tumor?","options":["Prostatic adenocarcinoma","Renal cell carcinoma","Squamous cell lung carcinoma","Follicular thyroid carcinoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.9","dok":2,"approach":["In an older adult with multiple bone lesions, think of metastases before a primary bone tumor.","Classify the lesions: dense and sclerotic means osteoblastic.","Prostate cancer is the classic cause of osteoblastic metastases in men."],"why":"Metastases are far more common than primary malignant bone tumors in adults, because carcinomas of the prostate, breast, lung, kidney, and thyroid often spread to the red marrow of the axial skeleton. Prostate cancer causes osteoblastic (sclerotic) lesions by releasing factors that stimulate osteoblasts. Kidney, thyroid, and lung cancers cause mainly lytic lesions, and breast cancer often causes mixed lesions. Metastases can cause pathologic fractures, and lytic lesions often cause hypercalcemia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Renal cell carcinoma produces purely lytic, often expansile and very vascular bone metastases.","Lung cancer metastases to bone are mainly lytic.","Thyroid carcinoma that spreads to bone causes lytic lesions."],"study":{"topics":["Bone metastases patterns","Hypercalcemia of malignancy"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-13-F4"]}},{"id":"MS-C26-Q4","stem":"The tumor arose in the metaphysis next to the distal femoral growth plate, where new bone is laid down on calcified cartilage. In a child with severe vitamin D deficiency, which of the following changes would be seen in this growth plate?","options":["Early fusion of the growth plate with loss of the reserve zone","A wide, disorganized hypertrophic zone that fails to mineralize","A thin proliferative zone with short, sparse chondrocyte columns","Thick cores of calcified cartilage retained within trabeculae"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.5","dok":3,"approach":["List the zones from epiphysis to metaphysis: reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, and ossification.","Vitamin D deficiency lowers calcium and phosphate, so cartilage matrix cannot calcify.","Hypertrophic chondrocytes are not cleared on time, so the plate widens and becomes irregular, which is rickets."],"why":"In the growth plate, chondrocytes rest, divide in columns, swell in the hypertrophic zone, and then the matrix around them calcifies. Calcified cartilage is invaded by vessels and used as a scaffold for new bone in the metaphysis. In rickets, low calcium and phosphate prevent this calcification. Hypertrophic chondrocytes pile up, the plate becomes wide and disorganized, and the ends of the bones flare. In achondroplasia, by contrast, the proliferative zone is underactive.","whyNot":["Early closure of the growth plate follows excess estrogen, as in precocious puberty, and limits final height.","Correct.","Too little chondrocyte proliferation is seen in achondroplasia, where activating FGFR3 mutations slow the proliferative zone.","Retained calcified cartilage within trabeculae happens when osteoclasts cannot resorb it, as in osteopetrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Growth plate zones","Rickets vs achondroplasia"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-A2"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C27","system":"MS","dx":"Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma (sarcoma botryoides)","codes":["MS-13-F5"],"case":"A 3-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a 1-week history of vaginal bleeding and a mass protruding from the vagina. Physical examination shows a soft, polypoid, grapelike mass at the vaginal opening. Biopsy shows a dense layer of primitive tumor cells just beneath the surface epithelium. Some tumor cells have abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm with cross-striations. The tumor cells stain for desmin and myogenin. Diagnosis: embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, botryoid type (sarcoma botryoides).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C27-Q1","stem":"The pathologist also considered alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma, which is more common in the extremities of adolescents. Which of the following genetic findings would indicate the alveolar type?","options":["PAX3 or PAX7 fused to FOXO1","SS18 fused to SSX1, SSX2, or SSX4","COL1A1 fused to PDGFB","EWSR1 fused to FLI1"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F5","obj":"MS-13-F.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the two main types of rhabdomyosarcoma in children: embryonal (younger children, head and neck or genitourinary tract) and alveolar (older children, extremities).","The alveolar type is defined by a fusion of a PAX gene with FOXO1.","Match each other fusion to its sarcoma to rule it out."],"why":"Rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood. The embryonal type affects young children, often in the head and neck or genitourinary tract, and its botryoid form grows as grapelike masses under the mucosa of hollow organs such as the vagina and bladder. The alveolar type affects adolescents, often in the extremities, and carries PAX3 or PAX7 fused to FOXO1 from t(2;13) or t(1;13). Both types stain for desmin and myogenin, which mark skeletal muscle differentiation.","whyNot":["Correct.","This fusion from t(X;18) defines synovial sarcoma, a soft tissue sarcoma of young adults near large joints.","This fusion drives dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans, a slow-growing sarcoma of the dermis.","This fusion from t(11;22) defines Ewing sarcoma, a CD99-positive small round cell tumor that lacks myogenin."],"study":{"topics":["Rhabdomyosarcoma types","Sarcoma fusion genes"],"comps":["MS-13-F5","MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C27-Q2","stem":"The tumor cells resemble embryonic skeletal myoblasts. In normal development, the deep intrinsic muscles of the back arise from which part of the somite, and which nerves supply them?","options":["Hypaxial myotome, supplied by dorsal rami","Epaxial myotome, supplied by ventral rami","Epaxial myotome, supplied by dorsal rami","Sclerotome, supplied by ventral rami"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that somites divide into sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome.","The myotome splits into a dorsal epaxial part and a ventral hypaxial part.","Epaxial muscles become the deep back muscles and are supplied by dorsal rami of spinal nerves."],"why":"Somites form from paraxial mesoderm and divide into sclerotome, which forms the vertebrae and ribs, myotome, which forms skeletal muscle, and dermatome, which forms the dermis of the back. The epaxial part of the myotome forms the deep (intrinsic) back muscles, supplied by dorsal rami. The hypaxial part forms the muscles of the body wall and limbs, supplied by ventral rami. Rhabdomyosarcoma cells copy this myoblast program, which is why they express desmin and myogenin even in sites such as the bladder and vagina, whose walls are made of smooth muscle.","whyNot":["Hypaxial myotome forms the body wall and limb muscles, and these are supplied by ventral rami.","Epaxial muscles are supplied by dorsal rami. Ventral rami supply the hypaxial muscles of the body wall and limbs.","Correct.","Sclerotome forms the vertebrae and ribs, not muscle."],"study":{"topics":["Somite derivatives","Epaxial vs hypaxial muscles"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C27-Q3","stem":"Her mother developed breast cancer at age 31 years, and a maternal uncle had adrenocortical carcinoma at age 8 years. Germline testing of the child is most likely to show a loss-of-function mutation in which of the following genes?","options":["APC","RB1","TP53","BRCA1"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.7","dok":3,"approach":["Look at the family pattern: several different cancers at young ages in one maternal line.","Childhood sarcoma, early breast cancer, and adrenocortical carcinoma are the core cancers of Li-Fraumeni syndrome.","Li-Fraumeni syndrome is caused by germline loss of TP53."],"why":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an autosomal dominant cancer syndrome caused by germline TP53 mutations. p53 normally senses DNA damage and halts the cell cycle in G1 through p21 or triggers apoptosis. Without it, damaged cells keep dividing and gain more mutations. Affected families have soft tissue sarcomas such as rhabdomyosarcoma, osteosarcoma, early breast cancer, brain tumors, leukemia, and adrenocortical carcinoma, often in childhood or young adulthood.","whyNot":["APC loss causes familial adenomatous polyposis with hundreds of colon polyps.","Germline RB1 loss causes hereditary retinoblastoma and later osteosarcoma. It does not explain early breast cancer and childhood adrenocortical carcinoma.","Correct.","BRCA1 raises the risk of breast and ovarian cancer but not childhood adrenocortical carcinoma or rhabdomyosarcoma."],"study":{"topics":["Li-Fraumeni syndrome","p53 function"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-13-F"]}},{"id":"MS-C27-Q4","stem":"A 58-year-old woman is found to have a large retroperitoneal mass. Biopsy shows intersecting fascicles of spindle cells with blunt-ended, cigar-shaped nuclei. Which of the following immunostaining profiles is most likely in this woman's tumor?","options":["Desmin and myogenin positive, smooth muscle actin negative","Smooth muscle actin and desmin positive, myogenin negative","CD99 positive, desmin and myogenin negative","S100 and SOX10 positive, desmin negative"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.10","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize spindle cells with cigar-shaped nuclei in the retroperitoneum of an older adult as leiomyosarcoma.","Leiomyosarcoma shows smooth muscle markers: smooth muscle actin and desmin.","Myogenin is a skeletal muscle transcription factor, present in rhabdomyosarcoma and absent in leiomyosarcoma."],"why":"Leiomyosarcoma is a malignant smooth muscle tumor of adults that arises most often in the uterus and retroperitoneum. Its spindle cells have blunt, cigar-shaped nuclei and stain for smooth muscle actin and desmin. Desmin is found in both smooth and skeletal muscle, so myogenin, a skeletal muscle transcription factor, is the marker that separates rhabdomyosarcoma from leiomyosarcoma. Both sarcomas spread mainly through the blood, most often to the lungs.","whyNot":["This profile marks skeletal muscle differentiation, as in the child's rhabdomyosarcoma.","Correct.","Strong CD99 without muscle markers suggests Ewing sarcoma, a small round cell tumor of young people.","S100 and SOX10 mark nerve sheath tumors and melanoma, which do not show muscle differentiation."],"study":{"topics":["Soft tissue sarcoma markers","Leiomyosarcoma"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-13-F5"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C28","system":"MS","dx":"Staphylococcus aureus septic arthritis of the knee in rheumatoid arthritis","codes":["MS-13-G1"],"case":"A 64-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of a 2-day history of severe pain and swelling of the right knee. She has had rheumatoid arthritis for 15 years and takes methotrexate and prednisone 7.5 mg daily. One week ago she had a draining, infected skin ulcer over her right ankle. She has had no joint injections or surgery. Her temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. The right knee is hot and swollen with a large effusion, and passive motion causes severe pain. Blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in clusters that are coagulase positive. Diagnosis: Staphylococcus aureus septic arthritis of the right knee.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C28-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains how infection became established in this joint?","options":["Spread from osteomyelitis of the adjacent distal femur","Direct inoculation through a penetrating wound of the knee","Deposition of staphylococcal antigen and antibody complexes","Bacteremia seeding a synovium damaged by inflammation"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G1","obj":"MS-13-G.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the likely source: an infected skin ulcer with Staphylococcus aureus now in the blood.","Most septic arthritis comes from hematogenous seeding, and the vascular synovium has no limiting basement membrane.","Rheumatoid arthritis adds risk through damaged, inflamed synovium and through immunosuppressive drugs."],"why":"Most septic arthritis in adults results from bacteremia. Synovial capillaries are rich and lack a limiting basement membrane, so circulating bacteria can enter the joint. Joints already damaged by rheumatoid arthritis are especially vulnerable, and methotrexate and glucocorticoids weaken host defense. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause overall, and its adhesins for collagen and fibronectin help it attach in the joint. Other risk factors are prosthetic joints, injection drug use, and diabetes.","whyNot":["Contiguous spread from bone does occur, but nothing in her history or exam suggests femoral osteomyelitis.","Direct inoculation follows trauma, surgery, or joint injection. She had none of these.","Immune complex deposition causes a sterile arthritis, as in SLE or serum sickness. Here live bacteria are growing in the blood.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Septic arthritis pathogenesis","Risk factors for septic arthritis"],"comps":["MS-13-G1","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C28-Q2","stem":"Arthrocentesis of the right knee is performed. Which of the following synovial fluid findings is most likely?","options":["WBC 14,000/mm3, 70% neutrophils, no crystals","WBC 96,000/mm3, 95% neutrophils, no crystals","WBC 22,000/mm3, 85% neutrophils, needle-shaped crystals","WBC 1,100/mm3, 20% neutrophils, no crystals"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G1","obj":"MS-13-G.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the synovial fluid categories by WBC count: normal under 200/mm3, noninflammatory under 2,000, inflammatory 2,000 to about 50,000, septic usually over 50,000.","Septic fluid is dominated by neutrophils and has no crystals; Gram stain and culture are often positive.","In a patient with rheumatoid arthritis, a very high count separates infection from a flare."],"why":"Synovial fluid analysis sorts an acute hot joint into categories. Normal fluid has fewer than 200 WBC/mm3, noninflammatory fluid fewer than 2,000, and inflammatory fluid from rheumatoid arthritis or crystals usually 2,000 to 50,000. Septic fluid usually has more than 50,000 WBC/mm3, mostly neutrophils, and Gram stain or culture is often positive. Crystal analysis is always done, because gout and infection can occur together.","whyNot":["This is an inflammatory fluid, as in a rheumatoid arthritis flare. It is too low for a typical bacterial joint infection.","Correct.","Needle-shaped crystals, negatively birefringent under polarized light, mean gout.","This is a noninflammatory fluid, under 2,000 WBC/mm3, as in osteoarthritis or trauma."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial fluid analysis","Acute monoarthritis evaluation"],"comps":["MS-13-G1","MS-13-B4"]}},{"id":"MS-C28-Q3","stem":"The orthopedic team drains the joint urgently. If drainage is delayed, which of the following mechanisms will most directly cause rapid loss of her articular cartilage?","options":["Urate crystals activating the NLRP3 inflammasome","Osteoclast resorption driven by synovial RANKL","Antibody-dependent killing of chondrocytes","Proteases and oxidants released by neutrophils"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize that a septic joint fills with huge numbers of neutrophils.","Neutrophils release elastase, collagenases, matrix metalloproteinases, and reactive oxygen species.","These enzymes digest the cartilage matrix within days, along with bacterial toxins and enzymes."],"why":"In septic arthritis, bacteria trigger a massive influx of neutrophils into the joint. Neutrophils release elastase, collagenases, and other proteases and reactive oxygen species. Avascular articular cartilage cannot repair itself, so its collagen and proteoglycans are destroyed within days. Pressure from the effusion also reduces blood flow to the joint. Prompt drainage and antibiotics are needed to save the joint.","whyNot":["Urate crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1 beta release in gout. No crystals are involved in this infection.","RANKL-driven osteoclasts cause the slow bone erosions of rheumatoid arthritis over months, not cartilage loss within days.","Antibodies against chondrocytes are not the mechanism of joint destruction in bacterial infection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neutrophil-mediated tissue damage","Septic arthritis complications"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-G1"]}},{"id":"MS-C28-Q4","stem":"The aspirated fluid is thin and watery and lacks the stringy quality of normal synovial fluid. Which of the following cells and secreted molecules normally give synovial fluid its viscosity?","options":["Fibroblast-like synoviocytes secreting hyaluronan","Macrophage-like synoviocytes secreting chondroitin sulfate","Chondrocytes secreting type II collagen","Mast cells secreting heparin"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the two cell types of the synovial lining: macrophage-like type A cells and fibroblast-like type B cells.","Type B cells secrete hyaluronan and lubricin into the joint space.","Hyaluronan makes the fluid viscous; in septic fluid, neutrophil enzymes and reactive oxygen species break it down."],"why":"Synovial joints are lined by a synovial membrane with two cell types. Macrophage-like type A synoviocytes remove debris, and fibroblast-like type B synoviocytes secrete hyaluronan and lubricin. Hyaluronan, a long glycosaminoglycan, gives the fluid its viscosity, and lubricin lowers friction between cartilage surfaces. Synovial fluid also feeds the avascular articular cartilage. In septic arthritis, enzymes and oxidants break down hyaluronan, so the fluid loses its viscosity.","whyNot":["Correct.","Macrophage-like (type A) synoviocytes clear debris from the joint. Chondroitin sulfate is a proteoglycan side chain of cartilage matrix, not the main viscous molecule of synovial fluid.","Chondrocytes make the collagen and proteoglycans of articular cartilage, which stay in the cartilage matrix.","Mast cells contain heparin, but it does not give synovial fluid its viscosity."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial joint histology","Synovial fluid composition"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-G1"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C29","system":"MS","dx":"Disseminated gonococcal infection with septic arthritis of the wrist","codes":["MS-13-G1"],"case":"A 22-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 5-day history of fever and joint pain. The pain moved between her knees, elbows, and wrists and is now in her right wrist, which has become swollen. She has a new sexual partner and has no genital symptoms. Her temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F). Physical examination shows a swollen, warm right wrist and pain along the dorsal wrist tendons with finger movement. There are four small pustules on red bases on her forearms and ankles. Synovial fluid from the wrist has 48,000 WBC/mm3 (normal under 200), 90% neutrophils, and no crystals; Gram stain is negative. Nucleic acid amplification testing of the fluid detects Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Diagnosis: disseminated gonococcal infection with septic arthritis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C29-Q1","stem":"She was treated for gonococcal cervicitis 18 months ago. Which of the following properties of the organism best explains why that infection did not protect her from this one?","options":["Antigenic variation of its pilus proteins","Survival within phagolysosomes of macrophages","Superantigen-induced anergy of helper T cells","A polysaccharide capsule that resists opsonization"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.2","dok":2,"approach":["Ask why a person can get gonorrhea again after infection.","Neisseria gonorrhoeae keeps changing its surface proteins, so antibodies from the last infection do not recognize the new strain.","Its pili undergo antigenic variation by gene recombination."],"why":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative diplococcus that attaches to mucosa with pili. By recombining silent pilin genes into the expressed site, it keeps changing the antigenic makeup of its pili, and it also varies its Opa proteins. Antibodies made during one infection therefore do not block the next, so reinfection is common and there is no vaccine. IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA and helps the organism attach. Disseminated infection causes the triad of tenosynovitis, pustular skin lesions, and migratory polyarthralgia, or a purulent monoarthritis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Intracellular survival in macrophages is the strategy of organisms such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis.","Superantigens are made by Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes, not by gonococci.","Neisseria gonorrhoeae has no polysaccharide capsule. Neisseria meningitidis does, and vaccines target it."],"study":{"topics":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae virulence factors","Disseminated gonococcal infection"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G1"]}},{"id":"MS-C29-Q2","stem":"Arthrocentesis of the right radiocarpal joint is performed from a dorsal approach. Which of the following carpal bones form the main articular surface that meets the distal radius in this joint?","options":["Lunate and pisiform","Scaphoid and lunate","Capitate and hamate","Scaphoid and trapezium"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-3","obj":"MS-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the proximal row from lateral to medial: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform.","The distal radius articulates with the scaphoid and lunate.","The triquetrum meets the articular disc of the ulna, not the radius directly."],"why":"The radiocarpal (wrist) joint is formed by the distal radius and the articular disc above, and the scaphoid, lunate, and triquetrum below. The distal radius itself articulates with the scaphoid and lunate, while the triquetrum contacts the articular disc. The ulna does not articulate directly with the carpal bones. A fall on an outstretched hand transmits force from the scaphoid to the radius, which explains why scaphoid and distal radius fractures are common.","whyNot":["The pisiform lies on the palmar surface of the triquetrum and does not reach the radius.","Correct.","The capitate and hamate are in the distal row and take part in the midcarpal and carpometacarpal joints.","The trapezium is in the distal row. It articulates with the scaphoid and the first metacarpal, not with the radius."],"study":{"topics":["Carpal bones and the wrist joint","Upper limb bones"],"comps":["MS-3","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C29-Q3","stem":"Early in her illness, pain at the radial side of the wrist was reproduced when she made a fist over her flexed thumb and the wrist was bent toward the ulnar side, and de Quervain tenosynovitis was considered. Inflammation of which of the following tendons produces that sign?","options":["Extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis","Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis","Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis","Flexor carpi radialis and flexor digitorum superficialis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B8","obj":"MS-13-B.12","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the maneuver as the Finkelstein test, which stretches the tendons of the first dorsal compartment.","The first dorsal compartment holds the abductor pollicis longus and the extensor pollicis brevis.","Pain over the radial styloid with this test means de Quervain tenosynovitis."],"why":"De Quervain tenosynovitis is inflammation and thickening of the sheath of the first dorsal compartment, which holds the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis at the radial styloid. Repetitive thumb and wrist use and late pregnancy or new parenthood are common settings. Folding the thumb into the palm and bending the wrist toward the ulna (Finkelstein test) stretches these tendons and reproduces pain. Gonococcal tenosynovitis, by contrast, is an infection and comes with fever and skin lesions.","whyNot":["These tendons run in the third and fourth dorsal compartments. The extensor pollicis longus can rupture after distal radius fractures.","These wrist extensors share the second dorsal compartment and do not move the thumb, so the Finkelstein maneuver does not mainly stress them.","Correct.","These are flexors on the palmar side of the wrist. Ulnar deviation with the thumb in the fist stretches the radial-side extensors, not these."],"study":{"topics":["De Quervain tenosynovitis","Dorsal compartments of the wrist"],"comps":["MS-13-B8","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C29-Q4","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely original site of the infection that spread to her wrist?","options":["Pustular lesions on the distal extremities","Asymptomatic endocervical infection","Dorsal tendon sheaths of the wrist","Osteomyelitis of the distal radius"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G1","obj":"MS-13-G.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gonococci first infect mucosa: cervix, urethra, rectum, or pharynx.","Mucosal infection in women is often silent, which lets bacteria reach the blood before treatment.","Bacteremia then seeds the joints, tendon sheaths, and skin."],"why":"Disseminated gonococcal infection starts as a mucosal infection, most often of the cervix in women, that is frequently asymptomatic. Gonococci invade the bloodstream and seed tendon sheaths, skin, and joints. Most septic arthritis arises this way, by hematogenous seeding of the vascular synovium, rather than by direct inoculation. In sexually active young adults, Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a leading cause of septic arthritis, while Staphylococcus aureus dominates in older adults and those with damaged joints.","whyNot":["The skin pustules come from bacteria spread through the blood. They are a result of dissemination, not its source.","Correct.","Tenosynovitis is part of disseminated infection. The tendon sheaths were seeded from the blood.","Contiguous spread from bone is a rare route here, and nothing suggests radial osteomyelitis."],"study":{"topics":["Disseminated gonococcal infection pathogenesis","Routes of joint infection"],"comps":["MS-13-G1","MS-12"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C30","system":"MS","dx":"Type II necrotizing fasciitis from Streptococcus pyogenes","codes":["MS-13-G2"],"case":"A 34-year-old previously healthy man comes to the emergency department because of a 1-day history of severe pain in the left lower leg. Two days ago he scraped his shin while playing soccer. His temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 88/52 mm Hg. The anterior left leg is swollen and tense, with dusky red skin and several bullae. Pain is far greater than the skin changes suggest, and the skin is numb at the center. Serum creatinine is 2.1 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). At surgery, the fascia is gray and necrotic and separates easily from the overlying tissue. Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in chains, and culture grows beta-hemolytic group A streptococci. Diagnosis: type II necrotizing fasciitis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C30-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his pain was far greater than the visible skin changes early in the illness?","options":["Infection of dermal lymphatics with a raised sharp border","Toxin cleavage of desmoglein 1 in the upper epidermis","Clostridial gas dissecting through skeletal muscle","Infection spreading along deep fascia under intact skin"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G2","obj":"MS-13-G.3","dok":3,"approach":["Place the infection: necrotizing fasciitis spreads along the deep fascia, beneath the skin.","The skin looks almost normal at first while vessels and nerves in the deeper layer are destroyed.","Later, thrombosis of perforating vessels causes dusky skin, bullae, and numbness from nerve death."],"why":"Necrotizing fasciitis spreads quickly along fascial planes, which have a poor blood supply. Early on the skin over the infection looks almost normal, so severe pain is out of proportion to the exam. As bacteria and toxins cause thrombosis of the vessels that perforate the fascia, the skin becomes dusky, forms bullae, and loses sensation as nerves die. Type I is polymicrobial and common in diabetes and the perineum (Fournier gangrene); type II is caused by Streptococcus pyogenes, often in healthy people after minor injury. Crepitus is more typical of type I with gas-forming organisms.","whyNot":["Infection of the upper dermis and lymphatics with a sharp, raised border is erysipelas. It is painful, but the visible skin changes match the pain.","Exfoliative toxin cleaving desmoglein 1 causes staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, with superficial peeling, not deep tissue destruction.","Gas gangrene is a clostridial infection of muscle with crepitus. His Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in chains, not large gram-positive rods.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Necrotizing fasciitis types","Skin and soft tissue infection depth"],"comps":["MS-13-G2","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C30-Q2","stem":"He becomes more hypotensive despite fluids and develops a diffuse red rash. Which of the following actions of this organism's pyrogenic exotoxins best explains this shock?","options":["Binding MHC II and T-cell receptors outside the peptide groove","Cleaving SNARE proteins to block release of neurotransmitters","Inserting into cholesterol in host membranes to form large pores","ADP-ribosylating elongation factor 2 to stop protein synthesis"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize streptococcal toxic shock syndrome: shock and organ failure with an invasive group A streptococcal infection.","Pyrogenic exotoxins act as superantigens.","They link MHC class II to T-cell receptor V beta regions without processing, activating many T cells and causing a cytokine storm."],"why":"Streptococcus pyogenes is a beta-hemolytic gram-positive coccus in chains. Its M protein resists phagocytosis by blocking complement deposition, and hyaluronidase and streptokinase help it spread through tissue planes. Its streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens. They bind MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells and the V beta region of T-cell receptors outside the normal peptide groove, activating a large share of T cells at once. The resulting flood of IL-2, TNF-alpha, and IL-1 causes fever, capillary leak, shock, and organ failure.","whyNot":["Correct.","SNARE cleavage is how tetanus and botulinum toxins act. They paralyze rather than cause shock.","Pore formation describes streptolysin O, which lyses cells and red blood cells but does not drive massive cytokine release.","This is how diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A kill cells, not the cause of streptococcal toxic shock."],"study":{"topics":["Superantigens","Streptococcus pyogenes virulence factors"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G2"]}},{"id":"MS-C30-Q3","stem":"During debridement, the surgeon opens the anterior compartment of the leg, which contains the dorsiflexors of the ankle. Which of the following developmental events explains why the extensor compartment of the leg lies anteriorly while the extensor compartment of the arm lies posteriorly?","options":["Medial rotation of both limbs by about 90 degrees","Lateral rotation of the lower limb and medial rotation of the upper limb","Medial rotation of the lower limb and lateral rotation of the upper limb","Lateral rotation of both limbs by about 90 degrees"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["Start with the early limb buds: flexor surfaces face ventrally and extensor surfaces face dorsally.","The upper limb rotates laterally about 90 degrees, so extensors end up posterior and the thumb lateral.","The lower limb rotates medially about 90 degrees, so extensors end up anterior and the big toe medial."],"why":"Early limb buds have a ventral flexor surface and a dorsal extensor surface. In the seventh and eighth weeks the upper limb rotates laterally about 90 degrees, so its extensors face posteriorly and the elbow points back. The lower limb rotates medially about 90 degrees, so its extensors face anteriorly, the knee points forward, and the big toe lies medially. This medial rotation also twists the dermatomes of the leg into a spiral pattern.","whyNot":["If both limbs rotated medially, both would have extensors facing anteriorly and thumbs facing medially.","This reverses the true directions. If the lower limb rotated laterally, its extensors would face posteriorly like the arm's.","Correct.","If both limbs rotated laterally, both would have extensors facing posteriorly, which is not true of the leg."],"study":{"topics":["Limb rotation in development","Leg compartments"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C30-Q4","stem":"Over the following months, the scar over his leg is remodeled and gains tensile strength as the collagen first laid down in his granulation tissue is replaced. Which collagen type mainly replaces it?","options":["Type II","Type III","Type IV","Type I"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the early matrix: granulation tissue fibroblasts first make mostly type III collagen, the collagen of reticular fibers.","Remodeling degrades this collagen and replaces it with the collagen of dense connective tissue such as dermis, tendon, and ligament.","That strong, cross-linked fibrillar collagen is type I."],"why":"Granulation tissue forms within days of injury and contains new capillaries, fibroblasts, macrophages, and a loose matrix. The first collagen deposited is mostly type III, the same type found in reticular fibers and vessel walls. During remodeling, matrix metalloproteinases break down this collagen and fibroblasts replace it with type I collagen, which is cross-linked to strengthen the scar. Even so, a mature scar reaches only about 70 to 80% of the strength of normal skin.","whyNot":["Type II collagen is found in hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus, not in healing skin.","Type III collagen is the early collagen of granulation tissue. It is the collagen being replaced, not the replacement.","Type IV collagen forms basement membranes under the new epithelium and around vessels. It does not give a scar its tensile strength.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Wound healing phases","Collagen types by tissue"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-12"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C31","system":"MS","dx":"Wet gangrene of the foot in diabetes with peripheral artery disease","codes":["MS-13-G4"],"case":"A 68-year-old man with a 20-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the emergency department because of a 4-day history of swelling, discoloration, and foul-smelling drainage from the second and third toes of his right foot. The symptoms began after a blister formed from new shoes. He has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years and has calf pain when walking one block. Temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 138/82 mm Hg. The toes are black, moist, and swollen, with purulent drainage, and erythema extends onto the forefoot without a clear line of demarcation. Pedal pulses are not palpable. Ankle-brachial index is 0.48 (normal 1.0 to 1.4). Hemoglobin A1c is 9.6% (normal below 5.7%), and leukocyte count is 16,800/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000). Diagnosis: wet gangrene of the right forefoot.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C31-Q1","stem":"In patients with arterial occlusion alone, an ischemic toe usually becomes dry, shrunken, and sharply demarcated from viable tissue. Which of the following processes most likely accounts for the moist, swollen, poorly demarcated appearance of this patient's toes?","options":["Fat necrosis from lipase release and calcium soap formation","Coagulative necrosis from arterial occlusion with protein denaturation","Caseous necrosis from a granulomatous response to mycobacteria","Liquefactive necrosis from bacterial infection and neutrophil enzymes"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G4","obj":"MS-13-G.6","dok":3,"approach":["Both dry and wet gangrene begin as ischemic coagulative necrosis, so look for what has been added on top.","Fever, pus, leukocytosis, and spreading erythema point to bacterial infection of the dead tissue.","Neutrophil and bacterial enzymes digest the tissue, which is liquefactive necrosis, so the lesion becomes wet and spreads."],"why":"Dry gangrene is coagulative necrosis after arterial occlusion. The tissue mummifies and a clear line separates dead from living tissue. Wet gangrene happens when bacteria invade that dead tissue. Neutrophils and bacterial enzymes then liquefy it, so it becomes moist, swollen, foul smelling, and poorly demarcated, and infection can spread up the limb. Diabetes adds poor blood flow, neuropathy that hides minor injuries, and impaired neutrophil function, which together make this complication more likely.","whyNot":["Fat necrosis follows lipase release, as in acute pancreatitis or trauma to breast tissue. It is not the process in an infected ischemic toe.","Coagulative necrosis is the ischemic process behind both forms of gangrene. On its own it produces dry gangrene, so it does not explain the wet appearance.","Caseous necrosis is the cheesy necrosis of granulomas in tuberculosis and some fungal infections. It does not cause a wet, purulent toe.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dry versus wet gangrene","Patterns of necrosis"],"comps":["MS-13-G4","MS-13-G"]}},{"id":"MS-C31-Q2","stem":"Three years earlier, this patient had osteomyelitis of the calcaneus after a nail went through the rubber sole of his athletic shoe while he was doing yard work. Bone culture at that time most likely grew which of the following organisms?","options":["Pasteurella multocida","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Salmonella enterica","Staphylococcus epidermidis"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the setting: a puncture wound of the foot through the sole of a shoe.","Recall that the moist foam and rubber inside athletic shoes harbor Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","Match the setting to the organism carried in on the nail."],"why":"A nail passing through a sneaker sole picks up Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which grows in the warm, damp foam of the shoe, and drives it straight into the calcaneus. This is the classic organism for osteomyelitis after a puncture through a shoe sole, and it is also linked to osteomyelitis in people who inject drugs. Pseudomonas is an aerobic, oxidase-positive, gram-negative rod.","whyNot":["Pasteurella multocida is the organism for osteomyelitis after a cat or dog bite, carried in from the animal's mouth.","Correct.","Salmonella is the classic cause of osteomyelitis in people with sickle cell disease, who have poor splenic function and infarcted bone.","Staphylococcus epidermidis causes infection on prosthetic joints and other implanted hardware, where its biofilm protects it."],"study":{"topics":["Osteomyelitis organisms by setting"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G3"]}},{"id":"MS-C31-Q3","stem":"Atherosclerosis and long-standing hyperglycemia have damaged the endothelium of this patient's leg arteries, so it releases less nitric oxide. In normal arteriolar smooth muscle, nitric oxide causes relaxation mainly through which of the following?","options":["cGMP-dependent activation of myosin light chain phosphatase","cAMP-dependent inhibition of troponin C calcium binding","IP3-mediated calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Calmodulin-dependent activation of myosin light chain kinase"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["Nitric oxide diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, which makes cGMP.","Smooth muscle contraction depends on phosphorylated myosin light chains, so relaxation needs those phosphates removed.","cGMP-dependent protein kinase lowers cytosolic calcium and activates myosin light chain phosphatase, which removes them."],"why":"Smooth muscle contracts when calcium binds calmodulin, the complex activates myosin light chain kinase, and the phosphorylated light chain lets myosin cycle on actin. Nitric oxide from endothelium raises cGMP in the smooth muscle cell. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers cytosolic calcium and activates myosin light chain phosphatase. The light chains are dephosphorylated and the vessel relaxes. Loss of this signal in diseased arteries favors vasoconstriction and adds to poor flow in peripheral artery disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Smooth muscle has no troponin. Troponin C is the calcium sensor in skeletal and cardiac muscle, and nitric oxide acts through cGMP rather than cAMP.","IP3 releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum after alpha-1 or other Gq receptor activation. That raises calcium and causes contraction.","Calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light chain kinase is the step that starts smooth muscle contraction, the opposite of what nitric oxide does."],"study":{"topics":["Smooth muscle contraction and relaxation","Nitric oxide and cGMP"],"comps":["MS-7","CV-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C31-Q4","stem":"He undergoes a transmetatarsal amputation. Two weeks later, the open stump is filled with red, granular, easily bleeding tissue. Which of the following collagen types is most abundant in the newly formed tissue at this stage?","options":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type VII"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the red, granular, bleeding tissue as granulation tissue in an early healing wound.","Recall that fibroblasts in granulation tissue first lay down a loose collagen meshwork.","That early collagen is type III, which is later replaced by stronger type I as the scar matures."],"why":"Granulation tissue is new capillaries, fibroblasts, and loose matrix that fills a wound in the first days to weeks. Its fibroblasts first make type III collagen, the thin, flexible collagen of reticular fibers and blood vessel walls. Over weeks to months the scar remodels, and type I collagen replaces most of the type III, which is why tensile strength rises slowly over time.","whyNot":["Type I collagen dominates the mature scar, bone, tendon, and ligament. It replaces type III later in healing.","Type II collagen is found in hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus. It is not made in skin wound healing.","Correct.","Type VII collagen forms the anchoring fibrils that attach the epidermis to the dermis. It is defective in dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa and is not the main collagen of granulation tissue."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types","Wound healing and granulation tissue"],"comps":["MS-6","IN-10"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C32","system":"MS","dx":"Gas gangrene after a contaminated crush injury","codes":["MS-13-G4"],"case":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 30 hours after his right lower leg was crushed under farm machinery. A deep laceration on the lateral leg, contaminated with soil, was cleaned and sutured at a local clinic. Since then he has had rapidly increasing, severe pain in the leg. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 88/52 mm Hg. The calf and lateral leg are tensely swollen with bronze-to-purple skin, hemorrhagic bullae, a thin, brown, foul-smelling discharge, and crepitus on palpation. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5), down from 14.6 g/dL at the time of injury. A radiograph shows gas dissecting along muscle planes. Gram stain of the discharge shows large gram-positive rods with blunt ends and few neutrophils. Diagnosis: gas gangrene (clostridial myonecrosis).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C32-Q1","stem":"Spores of the causative organism are common in soil, yet most contaminated surface wounds never develop this infection. Which of the following features of this wound most directly allowed the spores to germinate and multiply?","options":["Large numbers of neutrophils recruited into the injured muscle","Exposed collagen fibers in the torn fascial layers","Low oxidation-reduction potential in devitalized muscle","High glucose concentration in the wound exudate"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G4","obj":"MS-13-G.7","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the organism as Clostridium perfringens, a spore-forming obligate anaerobe.","Spores germinate only where oxygen tension and redox potential are low.","A deep crush wound with dead muscle, poor blood supply, and soil, then closed with sutures, creates exactly that environment."],"why":"Clostridium perfringens spores sit harmlessly in soil and on skin. They germinate only in an anaerobic setting. Crushed, devitalized muscle has no blood flow, foreign material uses up oxygen, and closing the wound seals it off. Once growing, the organism releases toxins that kill nearby muscle and lower the redox potential further, so the infection spreads through muscle within hours, with gas, severe pain, bronze skin, hemolysis, and shock.","whyNot":["Neutrophils fight infection. In gas gangrene the toxins actually destroy neutrophils, which is why the Gram stain shows few of them.","Exposed collagen helps organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus adhere. It does not create the anaerobic conditions spores need to germinate.","Correct.","Glucose supports bacterial growth in many settings, but it does not trigger germination of anaerobic spores. Low oxygen is the key requirement."],"study":{"topics":["Clostridial myonecrosis pathogenesis","Anaerobic wound infection"],"comps":["MS-13-G4","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C32-Q2","stem":"His hemoglobin fell from 14.6 to 8.9 g/dL in 30 hours without external bleeding. Which of the following actions of the organism's major toxin best explains both the hemolysis and the muscle necrosis?","options":["Hydrolysis of phosphatidylcholine in host cell membranes","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 in host cells","Cleavage of SNARE proteins at presynaptic nerve terminals","Cross-linking of MHC class II molecules and T-cell receptors"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the main toxin of Clostridium perfringens as alpha toxin.","Alpha toxin is a phospholipase C, also called lecithinase.","Breaking down membrane lecithin lyses any cell it reaches, including red cells and muscle fibers."],"why":"Clostridium perfringens alpha toxin is a phospholipase C that splits phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) and sphingomyelin in cell membranes. Red cells lyse, which explains the falling hemoglobin. Muscle fibers, endothelial cells, and leukocytes are destroyed, which explains the myonecrosis, the spreading edema, and the scarce neutrophils. The same lecithinase activity is the basis of the Nagler reaction on egg yolk agar in the laboratory.","whyNot":["Correct.","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 stops protein synthesis. This is how diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A kill cells.","SNARE cleavage blocks neurotransmitter release. This is how tetanus and botulinum toxins act, made by other clostridia.","Cross-linking MHC class II to T-cell receptors is the action of superantigens such as toxic shock syndrome toxin from Staphylococcus aureus."],"study":{"topics":["Clostridium perfringens alpha toxin","Bacterial exotoxin mechanisms"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G4"]}},{"id":"MS-C32-Q3","stem":"Surgical exploration shows that the laceration severed the nerve running through the lateral compartment of the leg, between the fibularis longus and fibularis brevis muscles. Which of the following deficits is most likely to result from this nerve injury?","options":["Weak plantarflexion with numbness over the sole of the foot","Weak dorsiflexion with numbness limited to the first web space","Weak knee extension with numbness over the medial leg","Weak eversion with numbness over the dorsum of the foot"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the nerve of the lateral compartment: the superficial fibular nerve.","Its muscles, fibularis longus and brevis, evert the foot.","Its skin territory is most of the dorsum of the foot, sparing the first web space."],"why":"The common fibular nerve splits at the neck of the fibula. The superficial branch runs in the lateral compartment and supplies fibularis longus and brevis, the main evertors of the foot. It then becomes cutaneous and supplies most of the dorsum of the foot. Cutting it weakens eversion and numbs the dorsum, while dorsiflexion is preserved because the deep fibular nerve in the anterior compartment is intact.","whyNot":["This is a tibial nerve injury. The tibial nerve runs in the posterior compartment and supplies the plantarflexors and the sole.","This is a deep fibular nerve injury, which affects the anterior compartment dorsiflexors and the skin of the first web space.","This is a femoral nerve injury in the thigh, which weakens the quadriceps and numbs the medial leg through the saphenous nerve.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Nerves of the leg compartments","Superficial fibular nerve"],"comps":["MS-5","NE-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C32-Q4","stem":"Serum creatine kinase (reference 40 to 200 U/L) and potassium (reference 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L) are measured on arrival. Which of the following sets of results is most likely?","options":["Creatine kinase 120 U/L; potassium 3.1 mEq/L","Creatine kinase 21,000 U/L; potassium 6.4 mEq/L","Creatine kinase 120 U/L; potassium 6.4 mEq/L","Creatine kinase 21,000 U/L; potassium 3.1 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize widespread muscle necrosis from the crush injury and the toxin as rhabdomyolysis.","Dying muscle releases its intracellular contents: creatine kinase, potassium, phosphate, and myoglobin.","Expect a very high creatine kinase with hyperkalemia."],"why":"Muscle cells hold large amounts of creatine kinase and most of the body's potassium. When many fibers die, as with a crush injury and clostridial myonecrosis, both leak into the blood. Creatine kinase rises to many times normal, and potassium climbs, which can cause fatal arrhythmias. Hemolysis here adds more potassium. Released myoglobin also reaches the kidney, where it can cause acute kidney injury, and it turns the urine dipstick positive for blood without red cells on microscopy.","whyNot":["A normal creatine kinase rules out major muscle necrosis, and a low potassium is the opposite of what cell breakdown produces.","Correct.","Hyperkalemia fits, but a normal creatine kinase is not possible with this much dead muscle.","The creatine kinase fits, but necrotic muscle and lysed red cells release potassium, so it rises rather than falls."],"study":{"topics":["Rhabdomyolysis","Hyperkalemia from cell breakdown"],"comps":["MS-13-H","MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C33","system":"MS","dx":"Salmonella osteomyelitis in a child with sickle cell disease","codes":["MS-13-G3"],"case":"A 6-year-old boy with sickle cell disease (hemoglobin SS) is brought to the physician because of a 5-day history of fever and pain above his left knee. He has refused to bear weight for 2 days. Two weeks ago he had 3 days of diarrhea. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 128/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. The distal left thigh is warm, swollen, and tender. Hemoglobin is 7.4 g/dL (his baseline is 8.0 g/dL), leukocyte count is 19,500/mm3 (normal 5,000 to 14,500), and C-reactive protein is 9.2 mg/dL (normal below 1.0). MRI shows marrow edema in the distal femoral metaphysis with a small subperiosteal collection. Blood cultures grow gram-negative rods that do not ferment lactose and produce hydrogen sulfide. Diagnosis: Salmonella osteomyelitis of the distal femur.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C33-Q1","stem":"In children, hematogenous osteomyelitis most often begins in the metaphysis of a long bone, as in this patient. Which of the following features of the growing bone best explains this location?","options":["Rich supply of epiphyseal arteries crossing the open growth plate","High density of osteoclasts along the diaphyseal endosteum","Exposure of the metaphyseal cortex inside the joint capsule","Slow flow in looping capillaries beneath the growth plate"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-G3","obj":"MS-13-G.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the blood supply of the metaphysis in a child: nutrient artery branches end in hairpin loops near the growth plate.","These loops open into wide sinusoidal veins where blood flow slows sharply.","Bacteria in the blood settle where flow is slow and phagocytes are few, so infection starts there."],"why":"In a growing long bone, branches of the nutrient artery run up to the growth plate, turn back in tight loops, and empty into large venous sinusoids. Blood slows down in these sinusoids, and the area has few phagocytes and gaps in the vessel lining. Circulating bacteria lodge there and multiply. After infancy the growth plate blocks spread into the epiphysis, so the infection stays in the metaphysis or tracks out under the periosteum, as in this boy. In sickle cell disease, bone infarcts can also seed infection in the diaphysis.","whyNot":["After infancy, vessels do not cross the growth plate. The plate acts as a barrier, which is why childhood infection stays on the metaphyseal side.","Osteoclasts resorb bone, but they do not trap circulating bacteria. Their activity does not explain where infection starts.","In some joints, such as the hip, the metaphysis lies inside the capsule. That explains spread into the joint, but not why infection starts in the metaphysis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hematogenous osteomyelitis in children","Metaphyseal blood supply"],"comps":["MS-13-G3","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C33-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why children with this patient's underlying disease are especially prone to invasive infection with this organism?","options":["Deficiency of terminal complement components","Selective deficiency of secretory IgA","Splenic dysfunction from repeated infarction","Thymic hypoplasia with low T-cell counts"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Link the setting: osteomyelitis caused by Salmonella in sickle cell disease.","Recall that sickled cells infarct the spleen early in childhood, leaving functional asplenia.","Without a working spleen, bacteremia from the gut is cleared poorly and seeds infarcted bone."],"why":"Repeated sickling in the spleen causes infarction, so most children with hemoglobin SS have little splenic function by early childhood. The spleen normally filters bacteria from the blood and makes opsonizing antibody. Salmonella can enter from the gut, sometimes through areas of bowel injured by sickling, and without splenic clearance it reaches the bone. Infarcted bone with poor blood flow gives it a place to grow. This is why Salmonella is a classic cause of osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease, alongside Staphylococcus aureus.","whyNot":["Terminal complement deficiency (C5 to C9) causes recurrent Neisseria infections. It is not part of sickle cell disease.","IgA deficiency causes sinus and lung infections and Giardia diarrhea. It does not explain Salmonella bone infection in sickle cell disease.","Correct.","Thymic hypoplasia, as in DiGeorge syndrome, causes viral, fungal, and opportunistic infections from low T cells. It is not linked to sickle cell disease."],"study":{"topics":["Functional asplenia in sickle cell disease","Osteomyelitis organisms by setting"],"comps":["MS-12","HE-9-B"]}},{"id":"MS-C33-Q3","stem":"In children without sickle cell disease, hematogenous osteomyelitis is most often caused by gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase and coagulase positive. Which of the following virulence factors most directly allows that organism to attach to exposed bone matrix?","options":["Protein A that binds the Fc portion of IgG antibodies","Surface adhesins that bind collagen and fibronectin","Superantigen toxin that activates T cells nonspecifically","M protein that blocks complement deposition on the cell"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism as Staphylococcus aureus.","Separate its virulence factors by job: attachment, immune evasion, or toxin effects.","Attachment to bone depends on surface adhesins that bind the matrix proteins collagen and fibronectin."],"why":"Staphylococcus aureus carries surface proteins that bind host matrix molecules, including collagen-binding and fibronectin-binding adhesins. These let it stick to bone matrix and to injured tissue, which is a key reason it is the most common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis. Once attached, it can form biofilm, especially on hardware, which shields it from antibiotics and phagocytes.","whyNot":["Protein A binds the Fc portion of IgG so the antibody cannot opsonize the bacterium. It helps evade phagocytosis, not attach to bone.","Correct.","Superantigens such as toxic shock syndrome toxin cause massive cytokine release and shock. They do not mediate attachment to bone.","M protein is a virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes, which is catalase negative and grows in chains."],"study":{"topics":["Staphylococcus aureus virulence factors"],"comps":["MS-12","MS-13-G3"]}},{"id":"MS-C33-Q4","stem":"Treatment is delayed, and 3 months later he has a draining sinus over the distal thigh. Radiographs show a dense, sharply separated fragment of cortical bone lying in a cavity, surrounded by a shell of new subperiosteal bone. Which of the following best describes the dense fragment and its significance?","options":["Brodie abscess; a walled-off cavity with a sclerotic bony rim","Bone infarct; sterile necrosis from sickling that later calcifies","Sequestrum; avascular dead bone that shields bacteria from drugs","Involucrum; reactive periosteal new bone that walls off the infected site"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G3","obj":"MS-13-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two structures in chronic osteomyelitis: the dead fragment and the new bone shell around it.","The dense, separated fragment is dead bone, the sequestrum, and the shell is the involucrum.","Dead bone has no blood supply, so antibiotics and leukocytes cannot reach bacteria inside it, and it usually must be removed."],"why":"In chronic osteomyelitis, pus under the periosteum strips away the blood supply of a piece of cortex, and that piece dies and separates. This dead fragment is the sequestrum. The lifted periosteum lays down a shell of new bone, the involucrum, around it. Because the sequestrum has no blood flow, antibiotics and immune cells cannot reach the bacteria living on it, so surgical removal is usually needed for cure. A draining sinus is common, and years of drainage can lead to squamous cell carcinoma in the tract.","whyNot":["A Brodie abscess is a localized subacute abscess, usually in the metaphysis, with a sclerotic rim and no separated dead fragment.","Sickle cell disease does cause bone infarcts, but a fragment lying in a cavity with an involucrum and a draining sinus is infected dead bone.","Correct.","The involucrum is the shell of new bone in this film, not the dense fragment inside it."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic osteomyelitis: sequestrum and involucrum"],"comps":["MS-13-G3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C34","system":"MS","dx":"Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott disease)","codes":["MS-13-G3"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman who moved to the United States 3 years ago from a region where tuberculosis is endemic comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of progressive mid-back pain, night sweats, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Examination shows a sharp, angular kyphosis at the thoracolumbar junction with point tenderness over T11 and T12. She holds the right hip slightly flexed, and passive extension of the hip is painful. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 64 mm/h (normal 0 to 20). An interferon-gamma release assay is positive. MRI shows destruction of the T11 and T12 vertebral bodies, narrowing of the disc between them, and a right psoas abscess. CT-guided biopsy shows caseating granulomas with acid-fast bacilli. Diagnosis: tuberculous spondylitis (Pott disease).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C34-Q1","stem":"A chest radiograph shows a small calcified peripheral lung nodule and a calcified hilar lymph node, with no cavities or active lung disease. Which of the following best describes how the organism most likely reached her vertebrae?","options":["Hematogenous spread from an earlier lung infection","Direct extension from a pulmonary cavity through the pleura","Ingestion of organisms with spread through the portal vein","Direct inoculation during an earlier spinal procedure"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the calcified nodule and hilar node as a healed primary (Ghon) complex.","During primary infection, Mycobacterium tuberculosis enters the blood and seeds distant organs, where it can stay dormant for years.","Vertebral bodies, with their rich marrow blood flow, are a common site, and reactivation later causes Pott disease."],"why":"In primary tuberculosis, bacilli spread from the lung to the hilar nodes and then into the blood, seeding many organs. Most foci are walled off by granulomas and stay dormant. Years later, a vertebral focus can reactivate. Caseating granulomas destroy the front of the vertebral bodies next to the disc, the infection spreads under the anterior longitudinal ligament to the next vertebra, and the bodies collapse forward into a gibbus. Pus tracking down along the psoas forms a psoas abscess, which explains her painful hip extension.","whyNot":["Correct.","Direct extension needs active disease next to the spine. Her lungs show only healed calcified lesions with no cavity.","Swallowed organisms cause intestinal tuberculosis. Portal blood drains to the liver and is not how the spine is seeded.","Direct inoculation causes infection only after surgery or injection into the spine, and she has had no such procedure."],"study":{"topics":["Pott disease pathogenesis","Primary and reactivation tuberculosis"],"comps":["MS-12","PU-13"]}},{"id":"MS-C34-Q2","stem":"The infection began in the T12 vertebral body next to the disc it shares with T11. During embryonic development, the T12 vertebral body formed from which of the following?","options":["Paraxial dermomyotome cells that migrate around the neural tube","Caudal half of one sclerotome joined to cranial half of the next","One whole sclerotome lying at the level of the twelfth spinal nerve","Cranial halves of two adjacent sclerotomes joined across a disc"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-1","obj":"MS-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that vertebral bodies come from sclerotome, while dermomyotome forms dermis and muscle.","Each sclerotome splits into a cranial and a caudal half.","In resegmentation, the caudal half of one sclerotome fuses with the cranial half of the next to form one vertebral body."],"why":"Sclerotome cells from the somites surround the notochord and neural tube. Each sclerotome splits into halves, and every vertebral body forms from the caudal half of one sclerotome joined to the cranial half of the one below. Because of this shift, each vertebra spans two body segments, the segmental muscles from the myotomes bridge the disc and can move one vertebra on the next, and the spinal nerves exit near the discs. The notochord between the bodies persists as the nucleus pulposus.","whyNot":["Dermomyotome forms dermis and skeletal muscle. Vertebrae come from sclerotome.","Correct.","A vertebral body does not come from a single whole sclerotome. Resegmentation combines halves of two sclerotomes.","The halves that fuse are a caudal half and a cranial half. Two cranial halves do not join."],"study":{"topics":["Somite derivatives","Vertebral resegmentation"],"comps":["MS-1","MS-3"]}},{"id":"MS-C34-Q3","stem":"Which of the following immune mechanisms is mainly responsible for the caseating granulomas and the destruction of her vertebral bodies?","options":["Type I, IgE-mediated degranulation of tissue mast cells and basophils","Type II, IgG antibodies binding antigens fixed on the cell surface","Type III, deposition of immune complexes that activate complement","Type IV, Th1 cell activation of macrophages by interferon-gamma"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Granulomas form when T cells repeatedly activate macrophages against an antigen they cannot clear.","This T cell driven response is delayed-type, or type IV, hypersensitivity.","The positive interferon-gamma release assay shows the same Th1 memory response."],"why":"Macrophages that ingest Mycobacterium tuberculosis present its antigens and release IL-12, which drives CD4 T cells to become Th1 cells. Th1 cells release interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages to kill bacilli and to form epithelioid cells and giant cells, building a granuloma. TNF helps hold the granuloma together. The central caseous necrosis and much of the bone destruction come from this host response rather than from bacterial toxins, which is the type IV hypersensitivity pattern.","whyNot":["Type I hypersensitivity causes allergy and anaphylaxis within minutes. It does not form granulomas.","Type II hypersensitivity is antibody against cell or tissue antigens, as in the polyarthritis of rheumatic fever and in Lambert-Eaton syndrome.","Type III hypersensitivity is immune complex disease, as in the arthritis of SLE. It causes vasculitis and complement-driven inflammation, not caseating granulomas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types","Granuloma formation"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-15-C"]}},{"id":"MS-C34-Q4","stem":"In adults, both tuberculous and pyogenic vertebral infections typically involve two adjacent vertebral bodies and the disc between them, as in this patient. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["A single segmental artery supplies endplates on both sides of a disc","Infection begins in the posterior arch and spreads forward into the bodies","The adult disc has a rich blood supply that seeds the bones on either side","Organisms spread through the spinal canal from the epidural space"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G3","obj":"MS-13-G.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the adult intervertebral disc is avascular, so bacteria cannot lodge in it first.","Blood-borne organisms settle in the richly supplied bone just under the vertebral endplates.","Branches of one segmental artery supply the endplates above and below a disc, so both vertebrae are seeded and the disc is destroyed between them."],"why":"In adults the disc has no blood vessels and gets nutrients by diffusion. Bacteria arriving in the blood therefore lodge in the vascular bone beside the endplates. A segmental artery divides to supply the lower endplate of one vertebra and the upper endplate of the next, so the infection usually involves both bodies and then spreads into the disc between them. This differs from children, where hematogenous osteomyelitis favors the metaphyses of long bones, and from diabetic foot osteomyelitis, which spreads directly from a skin ulcer.","whyNot":["Correct.","Vertebral osteomyelitis starts in the vascular vertebral bodies. The posterior arch is usually spared until late.","The adult disc is avascular. Infection reaches it from the bone on either side, not the reverse.","Epidural abscess is a complication that spreads from the infected bodies. It is not the starting point."],"study":{"topics":["Vertebral osteomyelitis and discitis","Blood supply of the vertebrae"],"comps":["MS-13-G3","MS-3"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C35","system":"MS","dx":"Trichinellosis","codes":["MS-13-G"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 10-day history of muscle pain, fever, and swelling around his eyes. Three weeks ago he ate homemade pork sausage made from a pig raised and slaughtered on a neighbor's farm, and a few days later he had abdominal cramps and diarrhea for 3 days. His calves, thighs, shoulders, and jaw muscles ache, and chewing is painful. Temperature is 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Examination shows bilateral periorbital edema, subconjunctival hemorrhages, splinter hemorrhages, and tender proximal and calf muscles. Leukocyte count is 14,200/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000) with 32% eosinophils (normal 1 to 4%). Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 46 mm/h (normal 0 to 20), and serum creatine kinase is 2,850 U/L (normal 40 to 200). Serologic testing confirms the diagnosis of trichinellosis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C35-Q1","stem":"Which of the following stages of the parasite's life cycle is mainly responsible for this patient's current muscle symptoms?","options":["Newborn larvae encysting within skeletal muscle cells","Eggs hatching in the duodenum with larvae migrating through the liver","Larvae penetrating the skin and migrating to the lungs","Adult worms in the intestinal mucosa releasing eggs in the stool"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-13-G","obj":"MS-13-G.8","dok":2,"approach":["Lay out the timeline: diarrhea in the first week (intestinal phase), then muscle pain from the second week on (muscle phase).","Recall that Trichinella spiralis females are viviparous and release live larvae, not eggs.","Those larvae travel in the blood to skeletal muscle, enter muscle cells, and encyst, causing myositis."],"why":"A person is infected by eating encysted larvae in undercooked pork or game. Stomach acid frees the larvae, which mature into adults in the small intestine and cause the early diarrhea. About a week later, females release newborn larvae that enter lymph and blood and invade striated muscle. Each larva enters a muscle cell and turns it into a nurse cell, where it coils and encysts. This muscle invasion causes myalgia, high creatine kinase, eosinophilia, and the periorbital edema and splinter hemorrhages of the systemic phase.","whyNot":["Correct.","Egg hatching with migration through the liver and lungs describes Ascaris. Trichinella has no free egg stage.","Skin penetration with lung migration is how hookworms and Strongyloides infect people. Trichinella is acquired by eating meat.","Trichinella females release live larvae, and eggs are not passed in the stool. The intestinal stage also explains the early diarrhea, not the muscle pain."],"study":{"topics":["Trichinella spiralis life cycle","Parasitic myositis"],"comps":["MS-13-G","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C35-Q2","stem":"A deltoid muscle biopsy shows coiled larvae inside host cells. The larvae also invade cardiac muscle, but they do not encyst there. Which of the following features of the surrounding uninfected fibers identifies the tissue as skeletal muscle rather than cardiac muscle?","options":["Branched cells joined end to end by gap junctions","Multinucleated fibers with nuclei at the periphery","Fusiform cells with dense bodies and no striations","Central nuclei with intercalated discs between cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the histologic features that separate the three muscle types.","Skeletal muscle fibers are long, unbranched, and multinucleated, with nuclei pushed to the edge.","Cardiac muscle has central nuclei, branching, and intercalated discs; smooth muscle has no striations."],"why":"Skeletal muscle fibers form by fusion of many myoblasts, so each fiber has many nuclei, and these sit just under the sarcolemma at the periphery. The fibers are striated and do not branch. Cardiac muscle cells are single cells with one or two central nuclei, they branch, and they join end to end at intercalated discs that contain gap junctions and desmosomes. Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped, with one central nucleus, dense bodies, and no striations.","whyNot":["Branching cells linked by gap junctions describe cardiac muscle, which conducts electrical impulses from cell to cell.","Correct.","Fusiform cells with dense bodies and no striations describe smooth muscle.","Central nuclei and intercalated discs identify cardiac muscle."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle"],"comps":["MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C35-Q3","stem":"Before serologic results were available, his physician considered polymyalgia rheumatica because of his muscle pain and stiffness. Which of the following findings from this case most strongly argues against polymyalgia rheumatica?","options":["Tenderness of the shoulder and thigh muscles","Age of 58 years","Serum creatine kinase of 2,850 U/L","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 46 mm/h"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B5","obj":"MS-13-B.8","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what polymyalgia rheumatica looks like: adults over 50, girdle pain and stiffness, high ESR, and normal muscle enzymes.","Its inflammation is in the bursae and synovium around the shoulders and hips, so muscle fibers are not damaged.","A high creatine kinase means myofiber necrosis, which points to myositis and away from polymyalgia rheumatica."],"why":"Creatine kinase leaks from damaged muscle fibers. In polymyalgia rheumatica the inflammation sits in the bursae and joints around the shoulder and hip girdles, so creatine kinase stays normal and strength is preserved. Inflammatory myositis, whether autoimmune or parasitic as here, damages muscle fibers and raises creatine kinase, often many times above normal. A muscle biopsy in myositis shows inflammatory cells and necrotic fibers, while biopsy is normal in polymyalgia rheumatica.","whyNot":["Pain around the shoulder and hip girdles is the main complaint in polymyalgia rheumatica, so this finding fits both diagnoses.","Polymyalgia rheumatica occurs in adults over 50, so his age fits the diagnosis.","Correct.","An elevated ESR is expected in polymyalgia rheumatica and does not argue against it."],"study":{"topics":["Polymyalgia rheumatica versus myositis","Creatine kinase in muscle disease"],"comps":["MS-13-B5","MS-13-B6"]}},{"id":"MS-C35-Q4","stem":"He is treated with albendazole and a 2-month tapering course of high-dose prednisone. Four months later, his muscle pain is gone, but he has trouble rising from a chair and climbing stairs. Serum creatine kinase is 95 U/L. Which of the following findings is most likely on a muscle biopsy now?","options":["Selective atrophy of type II fibers without inflammation","Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation","Endomysial CD8 T cells invading nonnecrotic fibers","Grouped atrophy with large clusters of a single fiber type"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note the new proximal weakness after weeks of high-dose glucocorticoids, with a normal creatine kinase.","Normal creatine kinase argues against active myofiber necrosis or inflammation.","Glucocorticoids cause a myopathy marked by atrophy of type II (fast, glycolytic) fibers."],"why":"High-dose glucocorticoids break down muscle protein and reduce protein synthesis, and type II fibers are hit hardest. The result is proximal weakness with a normal creatine kinase and a biopsy showing small type II fibers without inflammation or necrosis. Disuse causes the same type II atrophy. Neurogenic disease looks different: denervated fibers become reinnervated by neighboring motor units, producing fiber type grouping and grouped atrophy, often with fasciculations.","whyNot":["Correct.","Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation is the pattern of dermatomyositis, which has a high creatine kinase and skin findings.","CD8 T cells invading fibers is the pattern of polymyositis, which raises creatine kinase.","Grouped atrophy and fiber type grouping mean denervation and reinnervation, as in spinal muscular atrophy or motor neuron disease."],"study":{"topics":["Steroid myopathy","Neurogenic versus myopathic biopsy patterns"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-13-B5"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C36","system":"MS","dx":"Tibial shaft fracture with anterior compartment syndrome","codes":["MS-13-H1"],"case":"A 23-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a motorcycle collision. Radiographs show a closed, displaced fracture of the right tibial shaft, and the leg is splinted. Eight hours later she has severe, increasing pain in the front of the leg that is not relieved by intravenous opioids. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. The anterior leg is tense and firm. Passive plantarflexion of the toes causes severe pain. The dorsalis pedis pulse is 2+ and capillary refill is normal. Sensation is decreased in the first web space of the foot. Anterior compartment pressure is 48 mm Hg (normal below 10). Diagnosis: acute compartment syndrome of the anterior compartment of the leg.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C36-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why the muscles in this compartment are ischemic even though the dorsalis pedis pulse is palpable?","options":["Reflex spasm of the femoral artery has reduced inflow to the limb","Bone fragments have partly torn the anterior tibial artery wall","Marrow fat emboli have occluded arterioles within the muscle","Tissue pressure collapses capillaries and veins but not arteries"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.4","dok":3,"approach":["Compare pressures: compartment pressure 48 mm Hg, arterial systolic pressure about 128 mm Hg.","Capillary and venous pressures are far lower, roughly 10 to 30 mm Hg, so they are overcome first.","Blood still flows through the large artery to the foot, but the muscle capillaries collapse and the tissue becomes ischemic."],"why":"Bleeding and edema from the fracture fill a closed fascial compartment that cannot expand. As pressure rises above venous and capillary pressure, venous outflow and capillary flow stop, while the main artery, with far higher pressure, stays open. The muscle and nerves in the compartment become ischemic even though the distal pulse is normal. That is why severe pain, pain with passive stretch, and sensory changes appear early, and loss of pulse is a late and unreliable sign. Untreated, the dead muscle scars and contracts into a Volkmann ischemic contracture.","whyNot":["Femoral artery spasm would reduce flow to the entire limb and weaken the distal pulse. It would not single out the anterior compartment.","A torn artery would reduce or abolish the distal pulse and would cause ischemia of the whole foot, not just one compartment.","Fat embolism after long bone fractures mainly affects the lungs, brain, and skin. It does not produce a tense, painful compartment.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Compartment syndrome mechanism"],"comps":["MS-13-H1","CV-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C36-Q2","stem":"If the compartment pressure is not relieved, injury to the nerve running through this compartment is most likely to cause which of the following motor deficits?","options":["Loss of plantarflexion and foot inversion","Loss of foot eversion with intact dorsiflexion","Loss of ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension","Loss of knee extension and the patellar reflex"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the nerve of the anterior compartment: the deep fibular nerve, which runs with the anterior tibial artery.","Its numb skin area, the first web space, is already affected in this patient.","The muscles it supplies, tibialis anterior and the long toe extensors, dorsiflex the ankle and extend the toes, so their loss causes foot drop."],"why":"The deep fibular nerve runs in the anterior compartment and supplies tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, and fibularis tertius. These muscles dorsiflex the ankle and extend the toes. Its only skin area on the dorsum is the first web space. Ischemic damage to this nerve and these muscles leaves a foot drop with a high-stepping gait, which is a common lasting result of untreated anterior compartment syndrome.","whyNot":["Loss of plantarflexion and inversion follows tibial nerve injury in the posterior compartment.","Isolated loss of eversion follows superficial fibular nerve injury in the lateral compartment.","Correct.","Loss of knee extension and the patellar reflex follows femoral nerve injury in the thigh."],"study":{"topics":["Deep fibular nerve and foot drop","Compartments of the leg"],"comps":["MS-5","NE-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C36-Q3","stem":"She undergoes fasciotomy and intramedullary nailing of the tibia. She smokes one pack of cigarettes daily and continues to smoke after surgery. Five months later, radiographs show a persistent fracture line with little bridging bone. Which of the following steps of fracture healing was most likely impaired?","options":["Formation of the initial hematoma around the fracture ends","Vascular ingrowth that converts cartilage callus to bone","Recruitment of neutrophils and macrophages to the site","Osteoclast remodeling of woven bone into lamellar bone"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.1","dok":3,"approach":["Sequence healing: hematoma and inflammation, soft callus, hard callus, then remodeling.","No bridging bone at 5 months means the soft callus has not turned into hard bony callus.","That conversion needs new blood vessels, which smoking and damaged surrounding muscle both reduce."],"why":"Fracture healing starts with a hematoma and inflammation in the first days. Over the next weeks, a soft callus of granulation tissue, fibrocartilage, and cartilage bridges the gap. New blood vessels then grow into this callus, and osteoblasts replace the cartilage with woven bone, forming the hard callus. Months to years of remodeling turn woven bone into lamellar bone. Nicotine constricts vessels and slows angiogenesis, and the muscle injury from compartment syndrome reduces local blood supply, so the step that depends on blood vessels fails and union is delayed. Infection, instability, diabetes, and malnutrition also delay healing.","whyNot":["The hematoma forms within hours of the fracture. Smoking does not stop it from forming.","Correct.","Inflammatory cell recruitment happens in the first days and is not the step that leaves a gap without bone at 5 months.","Remodeling acts on bone that has already bridged the gap. Here no bridging bone has formed."],"study":{"topics":["Stages of fracture healing","Causes of delayed union"],"comps":["MS-13-H1","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C36-Q4","stem":"While the compartment pressure was high, oxygen delivery to the anterior compartment muscles fell sharply. After stored phosphocreatine was used up, which of the following processes allowed these muscle cells to keep making ATP?","options":["Beta-oxidation of fatty acids in mitochondria","Oxidation of ketone bodies through the citric acid cycle","Release of free glucose from glycogen by glucose-6-phosphatase","Conversion of pyruvate to lactate to regenerate NAD+"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Without oxygen, the electron transport chain stops, so all mitochondrial fuel oxidation stops.","Glycolysis can still make ATP, but it needs NAD+ to keep running.","Lactate dehydrogenase converts pyruvate to lactate and regenerates NAD+, so anaerobic glycolysis continues."],"why":"Muscle first uses its small store of phosphocreatine, which creatine kinase converts to ATP within seconds. After that, without oxygen, it relies on anaerobic glycolysis of glucose and its own glycogen. Glycolysis makes 2 ATP per glucose but uses NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step. Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and regenerates that NAD+, so glycolysis keeps going. Lactate builds up, the tissue becomes acidic, and the output is far too low to keep the muscle alive for long.","whyNot":["Beta-oxidation feeds acetyl-CoA and NADH to mitochondria, which need oxygen to make ATP. It stops in ischemia.","Ketone oxidation also depends on the citric acid cycle and oxidative phosphorylation, which require oxygen.","Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase. It breaks glycogen down to glucose-6-phosphate and uses it inside the cell rather than releasing free glucose.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis in muscle","Muscle fuel use"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C37","system":"MS","dx":"Supracondylar humerus fracture in a child","codes":["MS-13-H1"],"case":"A 7-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after she fell from playground monkey bars onto her outstretched right hand with the elbow extended. She has severe elbow pain and will not move the arm. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. The right elbow is markedly swollen with an S-shaped deformity of the distal arm. The right radial pulse is faint compared with the left, and capillary refill in the fingers is 3 seconds (normal 2 seconds or less). Radiographs show a completely displaced extension-type supracondylar fracture of the humerus with the distal fragment displaced posteriorly and laterally. Diagnosis: displaced extension-type supracondylar fracture of the right humerus.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C37-Q1","stem":"If the compression of the brachial artery is not relieved, which of the following is the most likely long-term complication in this patient's limb?","options":["Nonunion from interruption of a retrograde intraosseous blood supply","Fixed wrist and finger flexion from forearm muscle fibrosis","Avascular necrosis of the proximal humeral fracture fragment","Fat embolism syndrome with hypoxemia, confusion, and petechiae"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the faint radial pulse as compromise of the brachial artery by the displaced fracture.","Prolonged poor flow, often with swelling, causes ischemia of the deep forearm flexor muscles.","Dead muscle is replaced by scar that shortens, leaving the wrist and fingers fixed in flexion: Volkmann ischemic contracture."],"why":"In an extension-type supracondylar fracture, the sharp end of the proximal fragment can kink or tear the brachial artery in the front of the elbow. Ischemia, often with forearm compartment syndrome, kills the deep flexor muscles, especially flexor digitorum profundus and flexor pollicis longus. Fibrous scar then replaces and shortens them, producing Volkmann ischemic contracture with a clawed, flexed wrist and fingers. Restoring flow quickly prevents it.","whyNot":["Nonunion from a retrograde blood supply is the classic risk of scaphoid waist fractures. Supracondylar fractures in children heal well.","Correct.","Avascular necrosis from a disrupted blood supply is typical of femoral neck fractures and of the proximal scaphoid, not the distal humerus in a child.","Fat embolism syndrome follows fractures of large long bones such as the femur, mostly in adults. It does not follow arterial compression."],"study":{"topics":["Supracondylar fracture complications","Volkmann ischemic contracture"],"comps":["MS-13-H1","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C37-Q2","stem":"With the distal fragment displaced posterolaterally, the sharp end of the proximal fragment is driven forward and medially into the cubital fossa, where it compresses the brachial artery. Injury to the nerve that runs beside this artery would most likely cause which of the following deficits?","options":["Inability to oppose the thumb and flex the index fingertip","Weak elbow flexion with numbness of the lateral forearm","Inability to extend the wrist and fingers","Weak finger abduction with clawing of the ring and little fingers"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the contents of the cubital fossa from lateral to medial: biceps tendon, brachial artery, median nerve.","The nerve beside the brachial artery is the median nerve, so a medially driven spike threatens both.","Median nerve injury at the elbow weakens thumb opposition and flexion of the thumb and index finger."],"why":"In the cubital fossa, the median nerve lies just medial to the brachial artery, which lies medial to the biceps tendon. When the distal fragment shifts posterolaterally, the proximal fragment pushes into these medial structures. Median nerve injury at this level weakens the thenar muscles, so the thumb cannot oppose, and weakens flexor pollicis longus and the index part of flexor digitorum profundus through the anterior interosseous branch, so the child cannot make an OK sign. Posteromedial displacement instead puts the radial nerve at risk.","whyNot":["Correct.","Weak elbow flexion with lateral forearm numbness comes from musculocutaneous nerve injury, which is rare in this fracture.","Wrist drop comes from radial nerve injury, which is more likely when the distal fragment shifts posteromedially.","Clawing of the ring and little fingers with weak finger abduction comes from ulnar nerve injury, more typical of flexion-type fractures or medial pin placement."],"study":{"topics":["Cubital fossa contents","Median nerve injury at the elbow"],"comps":["MS-5","NE-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C37-Q3","stem":"At follow-up after reduction and pinning, the physician taps the biceps tendon to test the reflex. Which of the following receptors and spinal cord segments carry the afferent limb of this reflex?","options":["Golgi tendon organ, Ib afferent, C7","Golgi tendon organ, Ib afferent, C5 to C6","Muscle spindle, Ia afferent, C5 to C6","Muscle spindle, Ia afferent, C7"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["A tendon tap briefly stretches the muscle, which is the stimulus for the muscle spindle.","Spindle Ia afferents synapse directly on alpha motor neurons, producing a monosynaptic stretch reflex.","The biceps reflex is carried by C5 and C6; the triceps reflex by C7."],"why":"Tapping the biceps tendon stretches the muscle quickly. Muscle spindles, which lie parallel to the muscle fibers, sense this stretch and fire through large Ia afferents. These synapse directly on alpha motor neurons for the biceps in the C5 and C6 segments, and the muscle contracts. Golgi tendon organs sit in series with the muscle at the tendon junction and sense tension through Ib afferents, which inhibit the same muscle rather than produce a brisk jerk.","whyNot":["Golgi tendon organs do not mediate the stretch reflex, and C7 carries the triceps reflex.","The segments are right, but Golgi tendon organs sense tension and inhibit the muscle. They do not drive the tendon jerk.","Correct.","The receptor is right, but C7 is the root for the triceps reflex. The biceps reflex is C5 to C6."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs","Reflex roots"],"comps":["MS-10","NE-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C37-Q4","stem":"While the brachial artery was compressed, her forearm muscles relied on anaerobic glycolysis and released lactate. After blood flow is restored, which of the following best describes the main fate of this lactate?","options":["Converted to alanine in the liver and returned to muscle","Converted to glucose in the liver, at a net cost of ATP","Excreted unchanged by the kidneys as the main route of clearance","Converted to glucose in skeletal muscle for glycogen storage"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Lactate from muscle travels in the blood to the liver.","The liver turns it back into pyruvate and then into glucose by gluconeogenesis, which returns to muscle. This loop is the Cori cycle.","Making glucose costs more ATP in the liver than anaerobic glycolysis gave the muscle, so the cycle has a net energy cost."],"why":"In the Cori cycle, muscle converts glucose to lactate and gains 2 ATP per glucose. The liver takes up the lactate, converts it to pyruvate with lactate dehydrogenase, and builds glucose by gluconeogenesis at a cost of 6 ATP equivalents per glucose. The glucose returns to muscle. The cycle therefore costs the body about 4 ATP per turn, but it keeps glycolysis going in muscle and prevents lactate from piling up in the blood.","whyNot":["The alanine cycle runs the other way: muscle sends alanine to the liver, which uses it to make glucose.","Correct.","Only a small amount of lactate is lost in urine. The liver, and to a lesser degree the kidney cortex, clears most of it by metabolism.","Skeletal muscle does not carry out meaningful gluconeogenesis and lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so it cannot make free glucose from lactate."],"study":{"topics":["Cori cycle","Gluconeogenesis"],"comps":["MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C38","system":"MS","dx":"Myositis ossificans of the quadriceps","codes":["MS-13-H1"],"case":"A 19-year-old man who plays college soccer comes to the physician because of a firm, painful lump in his right thigh. Five weeks ago an opponent's knee struck the front of his thigh, causing a large bruise and swelling. Since then the pain has lessened, but the lump has grown harder and he cannot fully bend his knee. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 64/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. Examination shows a 6-cm firm, mildly tender mass deep in the anterior mid-thigh. Knee flexion is limited to 90 degrees (normal about 135 degrees). A radiograph shows a well-circumscribed mass within the quadriceps with a rim of mineralization at its periphery and a lucent center, separated from the femoral cortex by a thin radiolucent line. Diagnosis: myositis ossificans of the vastus intermedius.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C38-Q1","stem":"Because the lesion is growing in a young adult, a biopsy is considered to exclude osteosarcoma. Which of the following histologic patterns would most strongly support myositis ossificans?","options":["Mature bone centrally with atypical cells infiltrating at the margins","Sheets of small round blue cells with scant cytoplasm and glycogen","Malignant cells laying down osteoid that invades the surrounding muscle","Mature bone at the periphery and proliferating fibroblasts centrally"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H1","obj":"MS-13-H.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that myositis ossificans matures from the outside in, which is called the zonal pattern.","Its center holds immature, proliferating fibroblasts, the middle zone holds osteoid and woven bone, and the rim holds mature bone.","Osteosarcoma shows the reverse: its most immature, atypical cells are at the edge, invading the tissue around it."],"why":"Myositis ossificans is a benign reaction to muscle injury in which mesenchymal cells form bone in the muscle. It matures in zones. The center is cellular, with fibroblast-like cells that can look busy, the middle zone has osteoid and woven bone, and the periphery has the most mature bone, which is why the radiograph shows a mineralized rim. Osteosarcoma is the reverse: it may be most mineralized centrally, and its atypical cells make malignant osteoid that infiltrates the surrounding muscle. Recognizing this zonal pattern prevents an unnecessary radical operation.","whyNot":["Mature bone centrally with atypical cells at the edges is the reverse zonal pattern, which points to osteosarcoma.","Small round blue cells with glycogen describe Ewing sarcoma, which usually arises in the shaft of a long bone or the pelvis.","Malignant osteoid infiltrating the surrounding muscle is the hallmark of osteosarcoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Myositis ossificans versus osteosarcoma"],"comps":["MS-13-H","MS-13-F"]}},{"id":"MS-C38-Q2","stem":"A lesion of the nerve that supplies the injured muscle, just below the inguinal ligament, would most likely cause which of the following?","options":["Foot drop with numbness over the dorsum of the foot","Weak hip adduction with numbness over the medial thigh","Weak knee extension with numbness over the medial leg","Contralateral pelvic drop during stance on the affected leg"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["The vastus intermedius is part of the quadriceps, which is supplied by the femoral nerve.","The quadriceps extends the knee, so femoral nerve injury weakens knee extension and reduces the patellar reflex.","Its sensory branches cover the anterior thigh, and its saphenous branch covers the medial leg."],"why":"The femoral nerve (L2 to L4) enters the thigh under the inguinal ligament, lateral to the femoral artery, and supplies the quadriceps and sartorius. Injury there weakens knee extension, so the knee may buckle, and reduces the patellar reflex. Its anterior cutaneous branches supply the front of the thigh, and its saphenous branch supplies the skin of the medial leg and foot.","whyNot":["Foot drop with dorsal foot numbness follows common fibular nerve injury, often at the fibular neck.","Weak adduction with medial thigh numbness follows obturator nerve injury, which supplies the medial compartment of the thigh.","Correct.","A pelvic drop on the opposite side during stance is the Trendelenburg sign of superior gluteal nerve injury, from weak gluteus medius and minimus."],"study":{"topics":["Femoral nerve","Lower limb nerve injuries"],"comps":["MS-5","NE-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C38-Q3","stem":"In this lesion, mesenchymal progenitor cells in the injured muscle differentiate into osteoblasts under the control of a master transcription factor. Haploinsufficiency of that same transcription factor causes which of the following conditions?","options":["Cleidocranial dysplasia with absent clavicles","Osteogenesis imperfecta with blue sclerae and fractures","Campomelic dysplasia with bowed long bones","Achondroplasia with shortening of proximal limbs"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the master transcription factor for osteoblast differentiation: RUNX2.","Recall what happens when one copy of RUNX2 is lost.","Too little RUNX2 impairs intramembranous bone in particular, giving cleidocranial dysplasia."],"why":"RUNX2 switches mesenchymal cells into the osteoblast lineage and turns on bone matrix genes. Losing one copy (haploinsufficiency) leaves too little RUNX2 for normal bone formation, which shows most in bones formed by intramembranous ossification, such as the clavicles and skull vault. The result is cleidocranial dysplasia, with absent or small clavicles, delayed closure of the fontanelles and sutures, and dental anomalies. SOX9 plays the matching role for chondrocytes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Osteogenesis imperfecta comes from mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2, the genes for type I collagen.","Campomelic dysplasia comes from haploinsufficiency of SOX9, the master transcription factor for chondrocytes.","Achondroplasia comes from a gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3, which slows chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate."],"study":{"topics":["RUNX2 and SOX9","Cleidocranial dysplasia"],"comps":["MS-9","MS-1"]}},{"id":"MS-C38-Q4","stem":"A biopsy of the middle zone of the lesion shows osteoblasts rapidly laying down new bone on strands of osteoid. Which of the following best describes the structure of this newly formed bone?","options":["Hyaline cartilage with chondrocytes in lacunae and type II collagen","Lamellar bone with osteons around central Haversian canals","Spongy lamellar bone with trabeculae surrounding red marrow","Woven bone with haphazard collagen fibers and many osteocytes"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Bone that forms quickly, as in repair, the embryo, or reactive lesions, is laid down first as woven bone.","Woven bone has randomly arranged collagen and many large osteocytes and is weaker than lamellar bone.","Over months it is remodeled into lamellar bone, which is organized into osteons in compact bone."],"why":"Whenever bone forms fast, osteoblasts lay down woven bone: collagen fibers run in random directions, osteocytes are numerous and irregular, and the bone is mechanically weak. Later, it is remodeled into lamellar bone, in which collagen runs in parallel layers. In compact bone the lamellae wrap around Haversian canals to form osteons, linked by Volkmann canals, and in spongy bone they form trabeculae around marrow. The middle zone of a lesion only weeks old is still making woven bone.","whyNot":["Cartilage may appear in some lesions, but the stem describes bone being formed, and the earliest bone is woven.","Osteons with Haversian canals are the mature lamellar structure of compact bone. They take months to form by remodeling.","Spongy bone is also lamellar and mature. It is not the first bone laid down in a reactive lesion.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Woven versus lamellar bone","Compact and spongy bone"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-13-H"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C39","system":"MS","dx":"Rheumatoid arthritis with Felty syndrome","codes":["MS-11"],"case":"A 62-year-old woman with a 15-year history of rheumatoid arthritis comes to the physician because of two episodes of cellulitis in the past year and increasing fatigue. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. Examination shows symmetric swelling of the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints with sparing of the distal interphalangeal joints, ulnar deviation of the fingers, swan-neck deformities, and firm subcutaneous nodules over both olecranon processes. The spleen is palpable 5 cm below the left costal margin. Hemoglobin is 10.8 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0), leukocyte count is 2,600/mm3 (normal 4,500 to 11,000), absolute neutrophil count is 700/mm3 (normal 1,500 to 8,000), and platelet count is 198,000/mm3 (normal 150,000 to 400,000). Rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies are positive. Diagnosis: rheumatoid arthritis with Felty syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C39-Q1","stem":"She carries HLA-DRB1*04 (HLA-DR4) alleles, whose shared epitope binds certain modified self-peptides especially well and presents them to CD4 T cells. Her antibodies target the same modified peptides. Which of the following posttranslational modifications creates these self-peptides?","options":["Deamidation of glutamine residues by tissue transglutaminase","Hydroxylation of lysine residues by lysyl hydroxylase","Phosphorylation of tyrosine residues by receptor kinases","Enzymatic conversion of arginine residues to citrulline"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["Link the anti-CCP antibody to its target: proteins containing citrulline.","Citrulline is made after translation when peptidylarginine deiminase enzymes convert arginine residues.","HLA-DR4 shared epitope alleles present citrullinated peptides well, which drives T cell help for autoantibody production."],"why":"Inflammation, and in the lungs smoking, activates peptidylarginine deiminase enzymes that convert arginine residues in proteins such as fibrinogen, vimentin, and collagen into citrulline. In people with HLA-DR4 shared epitope alleles, these citrullinated peptides fit well into the MHC class II groove and activate CD4 T cells. The T cells help B cells make anti-CCP antibodies and drive synovitis through TNF, IL-1, and IL-6. The inflamed synovium becomes pannus, and RANKL-activated osteoclasts erode cartilage and bone.","whyNot":["Glutamine deamidation by tissue transglutaminase creates gliadin peptides that bind HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 in celiac disease.","Lysine hydroxylation is a normal step in collagen synthesis that allows cross-linking. It is not the target of rheumatoid arthritis autoantibodies.","Tyrosine phosphorylation is a signaling step in receptor tyrosine kinase pathways. It does not create the rheumatoid autoantigens.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","Citrullination and HLA-DR4"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-3"]}},{"id":"MS-C39-Q2","stem":"Her sister has hand osteoarthritis. Which of the following radiographic findings in this patient's hands would best distinguish her disease from her sister's?","options":["Pencil-in-cup deformities with fluffy periosteal new bone","Osteophytes and subchondral sclerosis with asymmetric joint space loss","Marginal bony erosions with periarticular osteopenia","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges of cortical bone"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.2","dok":3,"approach":["Rheumatoid arthritis is an inflammatory synovitis, and osteoarthritis is degeneration of cartilage with bony overgrowth.","Inflamed synovium erodes bone at the joint margins where cartilage does not cover it, and cytokines thin the nearby bone.","Osteoarthritis builds bone, with osteophytes and sclerosis, instead of eroding it."],"why":"In rheumatoid arthritis, pannus first attacks the bare areas at the joint margins, where bone sits inside the capsule without cartilage cover, producing marginal erosions. Cytokines and disuse thin the bone around the joint, which is periarticular osteopenia. Joint space narrowing is even across the joint, and the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints are hit symmetrically, with the distal joints spared. Osteoarthritis instead shows uneven joint space loss, subchondral sclerosis, cysts, and osteophytes, often at the distal interphalangeal joints.","whyNot":["Pencil-in-cup deformities with periosteal new bone are typical of psoriatic arthritis, which favors the distal interphalangeal joints.","Osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and uneven narrowing are the features of osteoarthritis, her sister's disease.","Correct.","Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges are the classic radiographic sign of chronic tophaceous gout."],"study":{"topics":["Rheumatoid arthritis versus osteoarthritis"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-13-E"]}},{"id":"MS-C39-Q3","stem":"She also reports 2 years of gritty, dry eyes and a dry mouth that makes it hard to swallow crackers. Which of the following autoantibodies, if present, would most support the additional diagnosis suggested by these symptoms?","options":["Anti-Scl-70","Anti-SSA/Ro","Anti-Smith","Anti-Jo-1"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Dry eyes and dry mouth point to Sjogren syndrome, which often occurs secondary to rheumatoid arthritis.","Match Sjogren syndrome to its autoantibodies: anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La).","Rule out antibodies tied to myositis, diffuse scleroderma, and SLE."],"why":"Sjogren syndrome is lymphocytic destruction of the lacrimal and salivary glands, causing dry eyes and dry mouth. It can occur alone or alongside rheumatoid arthritis and other connective tissue diseases. Anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La) antibodies support the diagnosis. Anti-SSA also matters in pregnancy, because it crosses the placenta and can cause neonatal lupus with congenital heart block.","whyNot":["Anti-Scl-70 (anti-topoisomerase I) is linked to diffuse systemic sclerosis.","Correct.","Anti-Smith is highly specific for SLE.","Anti-Jo-1 is linked to inflammatory myositis, often with interstitial lung disease."],"study":{"topics":["Autoantibodies in rheumatic disease","Sjogren syndrome"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-15-D"]}},{"id":"MS-C39-Q4","stem":"In her metacarpophalangeal joints, inflamed synovium has spread over the surface of the articular cartilage as pannus. The cartilage beneath it thins even in areas without direct erosion. Which of the following normally supplies nutrients to this articular cartilage?","options":["Capillaries running through the articular cartilage matrix","Lymphatic vessels in the deep calcified cartilage layer","Perichondrial vessels covering the articular surface","Diffusion from the synovial fluid in the joint cavity"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that articular hyaline cartilage has no blood vessels, nerves, lymphatics, or perichondrium.","Its chondrocytes get nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid, helped by joint loading and movement.","Pannus covering the surface blocks this supply and releases enzymes, so the cartilage starves and breaks down."],"why":"Synovial joints are lined by a synovial membrane whose cells make synovial fluid, rich in hyaluronan and lubricin. The articular surface is hyaline cartilage with no blood supply and no perichondrium, so its chondrocytes depend on diffusion of nutrients from this fluid, pumped in and out as the joint is loaded. In rheumatoid arthritis, pannus spreading over the cartilage cuts off this diffusion and releases proteases and cytokines, so the cartilage degrades even before the bone is eroded.","whyNot":["Articular cartilage is avascular. It has no capillaries in its matrix, which is one reason it heals poorly.","Cartilage has no lymphatic vessels. The calcified deep layer anchors it to bone.","Articular cartilage has no perichondrium, unlike most other hyaline cartilage, such as the cartilage of the trachea.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial joint structure","Articular cartilage nutrition"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-6"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C40","system":"MS","dx":"Limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)","codes":["MS-11"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-year history of heartburn and difficulty swallowing solid food. For 8 years, her fingers have turned white, then blue, then red when exposed to cold. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Examination shows thickened, tight, shiny skin of the fingers distal to the metacarpophalangeal joints and around the mouth, with no skin thickening on the arms or trunk. There are telangiectasias on the face and lips, small firm white nodules at the fingertips, and healed pitted scars on several fingertips. Antinuclear antibody testing is positive at a titer of 1:640. Esophageal manometry shows weak peristalsis in the distal esophagus and a low lower esophageal sphincter pressure. Diagnosis: limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (CREST syndrome).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C40-Q1","stem":"Over the next several years, which of the following changes in her hands is most likely to develop?","options":["Fixed flexion contractures of the fingers from skin fibrosis","Ulnar deviation with erosions at the metacarpophalangeal joints","Reducible deformities without erosions from lax tendons","Bony enlargement of the distal interphalangeal joints"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the disease process: fibrosis of the skin and tissues around the joints, with Raynaud phenomenon and calcinosis.","Tight, thickened skin and fibrotic tissue over the fingers cannot stretch.","The fingers become fixed in flexion: sclerodactyly with flexion contractures."],"why":"In systemic sclerosis, activated fibroblasts lay down excess collagen in the skin and in tendon sheaths and joint capsules. Over the fingers this produces sclerodactyly, and as the tissues tighten the fingers are pulled into fixed flexion contractures. Calcinosis deposits form at the fingertips, and ischemia from Raynaud phenomenon leaves pitted scars or ulcers. Joint involvement is usually from this fibrosis rather than erosive synovitis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Ulnar deviation with marginal erosions is the pattern of rheumatoid arthritis, an erosive synovitis.","Reducible deformities without erosions from lax tendons and capsules are typical of SLE arthritis (Jaccoud arthropathy).","Bony enlargement of the distal interphalangeal joints (Heberden nodes) is a sign of osteoarthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Musculoskeletal features of systemic sclerosis"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-15-D"]}},{"id":"MS-C40-Q2","stem":"Given the limited distribution of her skin thickening and her other findings, which of the following autoantibodies is most likely to be found on further testing?","options":["Anti-Jo-1","Anticentromere","Anti-topoisomerase I","Anti-U1 RNP"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the two forms of systemic sclerosis by skin extent: limited (distal limbs and face) and diffuse (proximal limbs and trunk).","Limited disease with calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasia is CREST.","Anticentromere antibodies go with the limited form; anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) goes with the diffuse form."],"why":"Systemic sclerosis is split by how far the skin thickening extends. In the limited form, skin changes stay distal to the elbows and knees and on the face, the course is slow, and anticentromere antibodies are typical. The major late risk is pulmonary arterial hypertension. In the diffuse form, skin thickens on the upper arms and trunk early, anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) antibodies are typical, and interstitial lung disease and scleroderma renal crisis are more common.","whyNot":["Anti-Jo-1 is linked to inflammatory myositis with interstitial lung disease and mechanic's hands.","Correct.","Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) is linked to diffuse systemic sclerosis, with skin thickening on the trunk and proximal limbs.","Anti-U1 RNP is linked to mixed connective tissue disease, which combines features of SLE, scleroderma, and myositis."],"study":{"topics":["Limited versus diffuse systemic sclerosis","Scleroderma autoantibodies"],"comps":["MS-11","IM-15-D"]}},{"id":"MS-C40-Q3","stem":"During an attack of Raynaud phenomenon, cold increases sympathetic alpha-1 adrenergic constriction of her digital arteries. Vascular smooth muscle can hold this contraction for long periods while using far less ATP than skeletal muscle needs to hold the same force. Which of the following best explains this economy of energy?","options":["Latch-state cross-bridges that detach slowly after dephosphorylation","Calcium binding to troponin C to keep myosin-binding sites exposed","Rigor cross-bridges that persist because cellular ATP is depleted","Rising myosin light chain kinase activity throughout the contraction"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the clue: force held for a long time with little ATP use.","Recall that smooth muscle can keep its force after the myosin light chains have been dephosphorylated.","Dephosphorylated myosin heads still bound to actin detach very slowly, which is the latch state, so tension is held at low energy cost."],"why":"Smooth muscle starts contracting when calcium binds calmodulin and the complex activates myosin light chain kinase. The kinase phosphorylates the regulatory light chain, and myosin begins cycling on actin. Once force has built up, myosin light chain phosphatase can remove the phosphate from heads that are still attached to actin. These heads detach very slowly, so they keep holding tension while using little ATP. This is the latch state, and it lets vascular smooth muscle maintain tone for long periods at a low energy cost. In systemic sclerosis, damaged and narrowed digital arteries make this normal cold response severe enough to cause ulcers and pitted scars.","whyNot":["Correct.","Smooth muscle has no troponin. Calcium acts through calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase instead.","Rigor bonds form only when ATP runs out, as after death. Her smooth muscle has ATP and relaxes when the stimulus ends.","Light chain kinase activity and light chain phosphorylation fall during a sustained contraction even though force is maintained. That fall is part of the latch state, so rising kinase activity does not explain the low ATP use."],"study":{"topics":["Latch state in smooth muscle","Raynaud phenomenon"],"comps":["MS-7","CV-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C40-Q4","stem":"A skin biopsy from her finger shows a thickened dermis packed with dense collagen bundles that have replaced hair follicles and sweat glands. Which of the following collagen types makes up the majority of this excess collagen?","options":["Type II","Type III","Type I","Type IV"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the dense dermal collagen as fibrosis from activated fibroblasts.","Recall which collagen makes up most of normal dermis, tendon, and mature scar: type I.","Fibroblasts in systemic sclerosis overproduce the same dermal collagen, so type I dominates."],"why":"Type I collagen is the main collagen of dermis, bone, tendon, ligament, and mature scar, and it forms thick, strong fibers. In systemic sclerosis, TGF-beta and other signals drive fibroblasts to make far too much collagen, and the dermis fills with dense type I bundles that crowd out skin appendages. Type III collagen is also increased, especially early, but type I makes up most of the excess.","whyNot":["Type II collagen is found in hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus. It is not a skin collagen.","Type III collagen rises in early fibrosis and in granulation tissue, but it makes up a smaller share than type I.","Correct.","Type IV collagen forms basement membranes, such as those under the epidermis and around vessels. It does not fill the dermis."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types","Fibrosis in systemic sclerosis"],"comps":["MS-6","IM-15-D"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C41","system":"MS","dx":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome with small cell lung cancer","codes":["MS-10"],"case":"A 62-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of progressive difficulty climbing stairs and rising from a chair. He also reports a dry mouth and erectile dysfunction. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years. Physical examination shows symmetric weakness of the hip and shoulder girdle muscles and mild bilateral ptosis. Patellar and biceps reflexes are absent at rest but can be elicited after 10 seconds of maximal voluntary contraction of the tested muscle. Sensation is intact. CT of the chest shows a large right hilar mass with mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and bronchoscopic biopsy shows small cell carcinoma. Diagnosis: Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome associated with small cell lung cancer.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C41-Q1","stem":"Nerve conduction studies are performed. Which of the following electrodiagnostic findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["Low resting motor amplitude that rises markedly after brief exercise","Normal resting motor amplitude with a decrement at 3 Hz stimulation","Reduced sensory nerve action potentials with slowed conduction velocity","Fibrillation potentials with large, long-duration motor units"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.2","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the clues: proximal weakness, autonomic symptoms, absent reflexes that return after exercise, and a smoker with small cell cancer.","Recall that this disorder reduces acetylcholine release, so each nerve impulse produces a small end plate potential.","Repeated activity builds up calcium in the nerve terminal and boosts release, so the response grows with exercise or fast stimulation."],"why":"In Lambert-Eaton syndrome, antibodies block presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, so too little acetylcholine is released with each impulse. The resting compound muscle action potential is small. After brief maximal exercise or fast repetitive stimulation, calcium accumulates in the terminal and release improves, so the amplitude rises, often by more than 100%. Myasthenia gravis is the opposite pattern: a normal resting amplitude that falls with repeated stimulation, because the problem is fewer postsynaptic receptors.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is the classic pattern of myasthenia gravis. Release is normal, but receptors are lost, so the safety margin fails as release falls with repeated 3 Hz stimulation.","Low sensory amplitudes and slowed conduction point to a peripheral neuropathy. Sensation is normal here, and Lambert-Eaton syndrome spares sensory nerves.","Fibrillations with large, long motor units are signs of denervation and reinnervation, as in motor neuron disease. They are not expected in a presynaptic junction disorder."],"study":{"topics":["Lambert-Eaton syndrome versus myasthenia gravis","Repetitive nerve stimulation patterns"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C41-Q2","stem":"Serum testing shows antibodies against P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels. Which of the following best classifies the immune mechanism of this patient's weakness?","options":["Type I hypersensitivity from IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","Type IV hypersensitivity from CD8+ T cells attacking muscle fibers","Type II hypersensitivity from IgG cross-reacting with a nerve antigen","Type III hypersensitivity from immune complexes deposited at end plates"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the target: an antibody against a fixed membrane protein on the nerve terminal.","Link the source: small cell carcinoma cells are neuroendocrine and express the same calcium channel, so the immune response to the tumor cross-reacts with nerve.","An antibody against a cell-surface antigen that changes cell function is type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"Small cell lung cancer cells express voltage-gated calcium channels. The immune response to the tumor makes IgG that also binds the channels on motor and autonomic nerve terminals. The antibodies cross-link and remove the channels, so less calcium enters and less acetylcholine is released. Antibody binding to a fixed cell-surface antigen is type II hypersensitivity, the same class as myasthenia gravis and the molecular mimicry of rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["Type I reactions are immediate IgE-mediated allergic responses such as anaphylaxis and play no role in this weakness.","Type IV injury by CD8+ T cells invading muscle fibers is the mechanism of polymyositis. Muscle fibers are normal in this disorder.","Correct.","Type III injury comes from circulating immune complexes that lodge in vessel walls, as in SLE arthritis and glomerulonephritis. Here the antibody binds a fixed membrane protein."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types in musculoskeletal disease","Paraneoplastic syndromes of small cell lung cancer"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-10"]}},{"id":"MS-C41-Q3","stem":"Six weeks later, he reports aching pain in both wrists and ankles. Examination shows clubbing of the fingers and warm, tender distal forearms and lower legs. Radiographs show smooth, symmetric periosteal new bone along the shafts of the distal radius, ulna, tibia, and fibula. Which of the following best explains these findings?","options":["PTH-related peptide causing subperiosteal bone resorption","Osteolytic metastases with a reactive periosteal bone response","Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition in the joints","Paraneoplastic new bone formation linked to the lung tumor"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-F","obj":"MS-13-F.11","dok":2,"approach":["Group the findings: clubbing, symmetric periosteal new bone on long bone shafts, and joint pain.","Recognize this triad as hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy.","Recall that it is a paraneoplastic syndrome of lung cancer and is not caused by tumor in the bone."],"why":"Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy is the triad of digital clubbing, periosteal new bone along the diaphyses of long bones, and arthritis of nearby joints. It is a paraneoplastic syndrome of lung cancer, more often non-small cell types, but it can occur with any lung tumor. The new bone is laid down symmetrically, which separates it from focal metastases. The leading explanation is that growth factors such as VEGF and PDGF act on the distal limbs.","whyNot":["PTH-related peptide is made mostly by squamous cell carcinoma and causes hypercalcemia. Subperiosteal resorption is a sign of hyperparathyroidism and removes bone rather than adding it.","Metastases are usually focal and asymmetric, often in the axial skeleton, and appear as lytic or blastic lesions within bone rather than smooth symmetric periosteal layers.","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition causes chondrocalcinosis and pseudogout attacks, often in the knee or wrist, but does not produce clubbing or periosteal new bone.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy","Paraneoplastic syndromes of lung cancer"],"comps":["MS-13-F","MS-13-C"]}},{"id":"MS-C41-Q4","stem":"Treatment with amifampridine (3,4-diaminopyridine), a blocker of voltage-gated potassium channels in the nerve terminal, improves his strength. Which of the following best explains this improvement?","options":["Prolonged terminal depolarization increases calcium entry","Increased number of postsynaptic nicotinic receptors","Protection of SNARE proteins from proteolytic cleavage","Inhibited acetylcholinesterase prolongs acetylcholine in the cleft"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.1","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the steps: the action potential depolarizes the terminal, voltage-gated calcium channels open, calcium triggers SNARE-mediated vesicle fusion, and acetylcholine binds nicotinic receptors.","Locate the defect in this disease at calcium entry.","Blocking potassium channels slows repolarization, so the remaining calcium channels stay open longer."],"why":"Potassium efflux repolarizes the nerve terminal after each action potential. When those channels are blocked, the terminal stays depolarized longer, and the calcium channels that survive the antibody attack stay open longer. More calcium enters, more vesicles fuse through the SNARE complex, and the end plate potential reaches threshold more often. This treats the exact step that fails in Lambert-Eaton syndrome.","whyNot":["Correct.","Potassium channel blockers do not change receptor number. Receptor loss is the defect in myasthenia gravis, and here the receptors are normal.","Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and blocks vesicle fusion. That is the defect in botulism, and SNARE proteins are intact here.","This is how pyridostigmine works. It helps most in myasthenia gravis, where the problem is postsynaptic, and it is not how a potassium channel blocker acts."],"study":{"topics":["Neuromuscular transmission steps","Localizing junction disorders"],"comps":["MS-10"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C42","system":"MS","dx":"McArdle disease","codes":["MS-8"],"case":"A 19-year-old woman comes to the physician because of lifelong exercise intolerance. Within a few minutes of intense exercise she develops muscle pain, stiffness, and fatigue. If she rests briefly and then resumes at a slower pace, she can continue with much less discomfort. Two days ago, after sprint drills, she had painful cramps and passed dark red-brown urine. Her parents are healthy and are first cousins. Physical examination today is normal. Serum creatine kinase is 8,400 U/L (reference 30 to 170). A forearm exercise test shows no rise in venous lactate, while venous ammonia rises. Genetic testing shows biallelic pathogenic variants in PYGM, the gene for muscle glycogen phosphorylase. Diagnosis: McArdle disease (glycogen storage disease type V).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C42-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her symptoms ease when she rests briefly and then continues exercising?","options":["Debranching enzyme releases free glucose from muscle glycogen","Muscle uptake of blood glucose and free fatty acids increases","Muscle glucose-6-phosphatase releases glucose into the cytosol","Lysosomal acid maltase begins degrading stored muscle glycogen"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the defect: muscle cannot break down its own glycogen.","Ask what other fuels muscle can use: glucose from the blood (supplied by the liver, whose phosphorylase is a different enzyme) and fatty acids.","After a few minutes, blood flow and fuel delivery rise, so muscle shifts to these outside fuels. This is the second wind."],"why":"McArdle disease is an autosomal recessive deficiency of muscle glycogen phosphorylase, so muscle cannot mobilize its glycogen at the start of intense exercise. After several minutes, exercising muscle receives more blood flow and takes up more glucose released by the liver and more free fatty acids from adipose tissue. With these outside fuels, she can continue, which is the second wind. The same block explains the flat lactate curve, the cramps, and myoglobinuria after intense exertion.","whyNot":["Debranching enzyme acts only after phosphorylase has shortened the outer chains, and it releases very little free glucose. It cannot bypass the missing phosphorylase.","Correct.","Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, which is why muscle glycogen cannot supply blood glucose. This enzyme is found in liver and kidney.","Acid maltase is the lysosomal enzyme missing in Pompe disease. Its small lysosomal pathway cannot supply energy for exercise."],"study":{"topics":["McArdle disease and the second wind","Glycogen storage diseases"],"comps":["MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C42-Q2","stem":"During one of her cramps, needle electromyography of the contracted muscle is electrically silent. The cramp is attributed to energy failure in the fibers. Which step in the contraction cycle most directly fails when muscle ATP is depleted?","options":["Depolarization of T tubules that activates DHP receptors","Calcium binding to troponin C to expose sites on actin","Release of ADP from myosin at the end of the stroke","Release of myosin heads from actin after ATP binding"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["An electrically silent contraction means the muscle is stuck in a contracted state without new action potentials.","Walk through the cross-bridge cycle and find the step that needs a fresh ATP molecule.","ATP binding releases myosin from actin. Without ATP, heads stay attached, as in rigor mortis."],"why":"In the cross-bridge cycle, ATP binding to the myosin head releases it from actin. Hydrolysis then cocks the head, the head binds actin, and phosphate release drives the power stroke. When ATP runs out, myosin stays bound to actin and the fiber cannot relax, which is the mechanism of rigor mortis. An electrically silent contracture in McArdle disease reflects this kind of energy failure, unlike an ordinary cramp, which shows electrical activity.","whyNot":["T tubule depolarization is electrical. The electromyogram is silent, so no new depolarizations are driving this contraction.","Calcium binding to troponin C does not use ATP. It starts contraction but does not explain why fibers stay locked once ATP is gone.","ADP release does not need new ATP. It happens at the end of the power stroke and leaves the head bound to actin, so it does not explain failure to relax.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle","Rigor and ATP depletion"],"comps":["MS-7","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C42-Q3","stem":"A muscle biopsy is fixed while the fibers are in a contracted state, and electron microscopy shows shortened sarcomeres. Compared with a relaxed sarcomere, which of the following keeps the same length?","options":["H zone","I band","A band","Distance between Z lines"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the sliding filament model: thin filaments slide past thick filaments, and neither filament changes length.","The A band equals the length of the thick filaments, so it stays the same.","Bands that contain only one kind of filament (I band, H zone) shrink as overlap increases, and the Z lines move closer together."],"why":"The A band is the full length of the thick myosin filaments, and thick filaments do not shorten. During contraction, thin filaments slide toward the M line, so the overlap grows. The I band (thin filaments only) and the H zone (thick filaments only) both shorten, and the Z lines are pulled closer, shortening the sarcomere.","whyNot":["The H zone contains only thick filaments and narrows, and can disappear, as thin filaments slide toward the M line.","The I band contains only thin filaments and shortens as they slide further into the A band.","Correct.","The Z line to Z line distance is the sarcomere length itself, which shortens with contraction."],"study":{"topics":["Sarcomere bands","Sliding filament model"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-7"]}},{"id":"MS-C42-Q4","stem":"She notes that she can perform a single 5-second maximal jump without any symptoms. Which of the following energy sources mainly supports this brief effort?","options":["Anaerobic breakdown of muscle glycogen to lactate","Phosphocreatine transfer of phosphate to ADP","Oxidation of free fatty acids in mitochondria","Oxidation of ketone bodies released by the liver"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Match exercise duration to fuel: the first seconds use stored ATP and phosphocreatine, the next minute or so relies on anaerobic glycolysis, and longer work uses oxidative metabolism.","Her glycogen breakdown is blocked, yet a 5-second effort is normal.","That points to the phosphocreatine system, which does not need glycogen."],"why":"In the first several seconds of maximal effort, muscle regenerates ATP from phosphocreatine through creatine kinase, which transfers phosphate to ADP. This system does not depend on glycogen, so it works normally in McArdle disease. Symptoms appear when the effort lasts long enough to need anaerobic glycolysis from muscle glycogen, the pathway she cannot start.","whyNot":["Anaerobic glycolysis from muscle glycogen powers efforts lasting from several seconds to about 2 minutes. It is the pathway blocked in her disease.","Correct.","Fatty acid oxidation is slow to start and supports prolonged, lower-intensity work, not a single maximal burst.","Ketone bodies are used mainly during fasting by heart, muscle, and brain. They are too slow and too scarce to fuel a 5-second maximal effort."],"study":{"topics":["Energy systems by exercise duration","Creatine kinase and phosphocreatine"],"comps":["MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C43","system":"MS","dx":"Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency","codes":["MS-8"],"case":"A 26-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 6 hours after finishing a 5-hour hike during which he ate nothing. He has diffuse muscle pain, weakness, and dark brown urine. He had two similar episodes, one after a long bicycle ride and one during a febrile illness. He can sprint without symptoms. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. Thigh and calf muscles are tender. Serum studies show creatine kinase 62,000 U/L (reference 30 to 200), potassium 6.1 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), and creatinine 2.4 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Urine dipstick is strongly positive for blood, and microscopy shows 0 to 2 red blood cells per high-power field. Genetic testing shows biallelic CPT2 variants. Diagnosis: carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency with exertional rhabdomyolysis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C43-Q1","stem":"Which of the following steps of fatty acid metabolism is deficient in this patient?","options":["Transfer of long-chain acyl groups from CoA to carnitine","Activation of long-chain fatty acids to acyl-CoA in the cytosol","Dehydrogenation of medium-chain acyl-CoA in the matrix","Return of acyl groups from carnitine to CoA in the matrix"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the carnitine shuttle: CPT I on the outer mitochondrial membrane moves long-chain acyl groups onto carnitine, a translocase carries acylcarnitine across the inner membrane, and CPT II on the inner membrane puts the acyl group back onto CoA.","Place CPT II at the last step, inside the matrix.","Link the defect to his triggers: prolonged exercise and fasting, when muscle depends most on long-chain fatty acid oxidation."],"why":"CPT II sits on the matrix side of the inner mitochondrial membrane and converts long-chain acylcarnitine back to acyl-CoA so beta-oxidation can begin. When it is deficient, long-chain fatty acids cannot be oxidized. At rest and during short bursts, muscle runs on glucose and glycogen, so he is well. During prolonged exercise, fasting, cold, or fever, muscle relies on fatty acids, energy fails, and fibers break down.","whyNot":["This is the CPT I reaction on the outer membrane. CPT I deficiency mainly affects the liver and causes fasting hypoketotic hypoglycemia, and his gene test points to CPT II.","Acyl-CoA synthetase activates fatty acids in the cytosol before the shuttle. It is upstream of the defect.","Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (MCAD) causes fasting hypoketotic hypoglycemia, mainly in infants, and is a beta-oxidation defect after the shuttle.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Carnitine shuttle","CPT II deficiency"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-13-H"]}},{"id":"MS-C43-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the rise in his serum creatinine?","options":["Interstitial nephritis from a drug hypersensitivity","Immune complex deposition in glomerular capillaries","Heme pigment toxicity and casts in renal tubules","Hemoglobin released by intravascular hemolysis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-H","obj":"MS-13-H.5","dok":3,"approach":["Read the urine: dipstick positive for blood but almost no red cells means a free heme pigment.","With a very high creatine kinase, the pigment is myoglobin from muscle breakdown.","Myoglobin filtered by the kidney injures tubules and forms casts, causing acute kidney injury."],"why":"In rhabdomyolysis, injured muscle releases potassium, creatine kinase, and myoglobin. Myoglobin is small enough to be filtered. In the tubules its heme causes oxidant injury, and it precipitates into casts, especially in acidic, concentrated urine, while volume depletion lowers renal blood flow. The dipstick detects heme, so it reads positive for blood even though few red cells are seen. Hyperkalemia comes from release of intracellular potassium and worsens as kidney function falls.","whyNot":["Drug-induced interstitial nephritis follows a new medication and often brings fever, rash, and white cells in the urine. Nothing in his history points to a drug, and the very high creatine kinase points to muscle breakdown.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis causes hematuria with red cells and red cell casts, not a heme-positive urine with no red cells.","Correct.","Hemolysis also gives a heme-positive urine with few red cells, but it would not raise creatine kinase to 62,000 U/L. The muscle pain and tenderness point to myoglobin."],"study":{"topics":["Rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuria","Pigment nephropathy"],"comps":["MS-13-H","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C43-Q3","stem":"Which of the following features of skeletal muscle fibers best explains why long, moderate-intensity exercise triggers his episodes while sprinting does not?","options":["Type I fibers have low myoglobin and few mitochondria","Type I fibers rely on fatty acid oxidation for steady work","Type II fibers are recruited first during low-intensity work","Type II fibers store glycogen that powers hours of steady work"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the two fiber types: type I are slow, red, oxidative, and fatigue resistant; type II are fast, glycolytic, and fatigable.","Long steady exercise mainly uses type I fibers, which burn fatty acids in their many mitochondria.","His defect blocks long-chain fatty acid oxidation, so the fibers doing endurance work run short of energy."],"why":"Type I fibers are slow, red, and rich in mitochondria and myoglobin. They resist fatigue because they generate ATP oxidatively, and during prolonged exercise much of that fuel is fatty acids. Small motor units with type I fibers are recruited first, so they carry most of the load in long, moderate work. When CPT II is missing, these fibers cannot use their main sustained fuel. Sprinting relies on type II fibers powered by phosphocreatine and glycolysis, which are unaffected.","whyNot":["Type I fibers are red because they are rich in myoglobin, and they have many mitochondria. This description is reversed.","Correct.","By the size principle, small motor units with type I fibers are recruited first. Type II units are added as force demands rise.","Type II fibers do store glycogen, but they use it for short, intense bursts and fatigue quickly. They do not power hours of steady work."],"study":{"topics":["Type I and type II muscle fibers","Motor unit recruitment"],"comps":["MS-7","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C43-Q4","stem":"His sprints are powered largely by anaerobic glycolysis, and the lactate produced is carried to the liver. Which of the following describes what the liver does with this lactate and the net energy cost of the cycle?","options":["Converts it to ketone bodies, with a net gain of 2 ATP","Converts it to glucose, with a net gain of 2 ATP per glucose","Converts it to alanine, with a net cost of 2 ATP","Converts it to glucose, with a net cost of 4 ATP per glucose"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the cycle: muscle lactate goes to the liver, is made into glucose, and glucose returns to muscle. This is the Cori cycle.","Count ATP: glycolysis in muscle yields 2 ATP per glucose, and gluconeogenesis from 2 lactate uses 6 high-energy phosphates.","The difference is a net cost of 4 ATP, paid by the liver."],"why":"In the Cori cycle, lactate from anaerobic glycolysis travels to the liver, is oxidized to pyruvate, and is rebuilt into glucose by gluconeogenesis. The glucose returns to muscle. Muscle gains 2 ATP per glucose, but the liver spends 6 ATP equivalents to make one glucose from 2 lactate, so the cycle costs 4 ATP overall. The liver can afford this because it burns fatty acids for energy, and the cycle lets muscle keep working anaerobically.","whyNot":["Ketone bodies are made from acetyl-CoA during fat oxidation, not from lactate, and their synthesis does not yield ATP in this way.","Glucose is the right product, but the cycle costs energy. Gluconeogenesis uses more ATP than glycolysis yields.","Alanine carries amino groups from muscle to the liver in the glucose-alanine cycle. The liver converts alanine to glucose, not lactate to alanine.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cori cycle","Gluconeogenesis energy cost"],"comps":["MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C44","system":"MS","dx":"MERRF mitochondrial myopathy","codes":["MS-9"],"case":"A 17-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of 2 years of sudden jerking movements of his arms, unsteady gait, and fatigue and weakness with exertion. Six months ago he had a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. His mother has bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, and a maternal uncle had seizures and died at the age of 34 years. Physical examination shows multifocal myoclonus, gait ataxia, and mild proximal weakness. Serum lactate at rest is 4.8 mmol/L (reference 0.5 to 2.2). Gomori trichrome stain of a quadriceps biopsy shows ragged red fibers. Genetic testing of muscle shows the m.8344A>G variant in the mitochondrial tRNA-lysine gene in 85% of mitochondrial DNA copies. Diagnosis: myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers (MERRF).","questions":[{"id":"MS-C44-Q1","stem":"His 20-year-old sister carries the same variant but has only mild exercise intolerance. Which of the following best explains the difference in severity between the siblings?","options":["Different proportions of mutant mitochondrial DNA in their tissues","Expansion of a trinucleotide repeat in the more severely affected child","Random inactivation of the X chromosome carrying the variant","Genomic imprinting that silences the maternal copy of the gene"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-9","obj":"MS-9.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note the pattern: the variant is in mitochondrial DNA and passes through the mother to all her children.","Each cell holds many mitochondrial genomes, some normal and some mutant. This mix is heteroplasmy.","The proportion of mutant copies varies between siblings and between tissues, and disease appears when it crosses a threshold."],"why":"Mitochondria come from the egg, so a mitochondrial DNA variant passes from a mother to all her children, while fathers do not transmit it. Each cell contains hundreds to thousands of mitochondrial genomes. When mutant and normal copies coexist (heteroplasmy), they are distributed randomly at cell division and into each egg. A sibling with a high mutant load in brain and muscle is severely affected, while one with a lower load may have mild or no symptoms. Ragged red fibers are muscle fibers packed with abnormal mitochondria that stain red under the sarcolemma on Gomori trichrome.","whyNot":["Correct.","Trinucleotide repeat expansion explains anticipation in disorders such as myotonic dystrophy and Huntington disease. It is a nuclear gene mechanism and does not apply to a mitochondrial point variant.","X inactivation explains variable expression in females with X-linked disorders. Mitochondrial genes are not on the X chromosome.","Imprinting silences one parental copy of certain nuclear genes, as in Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes. Mitochondrial DNA is not imprinted."],"study":{"topics":["Mitochondrial inheritance and heteroplasmy","Ragged red fibers"],"comps":["MS-9"]}},{"id":"MS-C44-Q2","stem":"His muscle fibers produce too little ATP, which slows the cross-bridge cycle. In a normal cycle, which of the following events directly produces the power stroke?","options":["Binding of ATP to the myosin head","Hydrolysis of ATP by the myosin head","Release of inorganic phosphate from myosin","Detachment of the myosin head from the actin filament"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the cycle in order: ATP binds and myosin detaches, ATP is hydrolyzed and the head is cocked, the head binds actin, phosphate is released and the power stroke occurs, then ADP leaves.","Separate the step that stores energy (hydrolysis cocks the head) from the step that uses it (phosphate release).","The power stroke follows phosphate release."],"why":"Hydrolysis of ATP stores energy in the myosin head by cocking it into a high-energy position while ADP and phosphate remain bound. When the cocked head binds actin, release of inorganic phosphate triggers the power stroke, which pulls the thin filament toward the M line. ADP is released at the end of the stroke, leaving the head tightly bound until a new ATP arrives. Low ATP supply in mitochondrial myopathy limits how fast this cycle can repeat, causing early fatigue.","whyNot":["ATP binding releases the head from actin. It ends one cycle and does not generate force.","Hydrolysis cocks the head and stores energy, but the head is not yet bound to actin, so no force is produced.","Correct.","Detachment follows ATP binding and allows the head to reset. It does not move the thin filament."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle sequence"],"comps":["MS-7"]}},{"id":"MS-C44-Q3","stem":"A supervised endurance training program is recommended. In healthy skeletal muscle, which of the following adaptations does sustained endurance training most characteristically produce?","options":["Hypertrophy of type II fibers with more myofibrils","More mitochondria and capillaries in trained fibers","Greater phosphocreatine stores in type II muscle fibers","Conversion of type I fibers into type II fibers"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Separate training types: endurance training improves oxidative capacity, and resistance training enlarges fibers.","Oxidative capacity depends on mitochondria and on oxygen delivery through capillaries.","Pick the option that increases both."],"why":"Endurance training increases the number and size of mitochondria, the amount of oxidative enzymes, and capillary density around muscle fibers. These changes raise oxidative capacity and fatigue resistance without much change in fiber size. Resistance training, by contrast, mainly causes hypertrophy of type II fibers by adding myofibrils. In mitochondrial myopathy, carefully supervised endurance training is used to build oxidative capacity in the fibers that remain.","whyNot":["Type II fiber hypertrophy is the main adaptation to resistance training, not endurance training.","Correct.","Phosphocreatine supports brief maximal efforts. Endurance training does not mainly work by enlarging this short-term store.","Training does not convert type I fibers to type II. Endurance training tends to shift fibers toward more oxidative properties."],"study":{"topics":["Training adaptations of muscle fibers"],"comps":["MS-7"]}},{"id":"MS-C44-Q4","stem":"Which of the following findings would most support a myopathic rather than a neurogenic cause of his weakness?","options":["Proximal weakness with elevated serum CK","Fasciculations in the tongue and thighs","Grouped atrophy of angular muscle fibers","Fiber type grouping on ATPase staining"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.4","dok":3,"approach":["Neurogenic disease damages the motor neuron or axon, so whole motor units lose their nerve supply.","Denervation leads to fasciculations, groups of atrophic fibers, and fiber type grouping after reinnervation.","Myopathic disease damages the fibers themselves, so weakness is proximal and the leaking fibers raise creatine kinase."],"why":"When a muscle fiber is damaged, creatine kinase leaks into the blood, and the large proximal muscles weaken first. This is the myopathic pattern, which fits a mitochondrial myopathy. Neurogenic disease, such as spinal muscular atrophy from SMN1 deletion, denervates whole motor units. Denervated fibers shrink in groups, surviving neurons reinnervate neighboring fibers and convert them to one type (fiber type grouping), and unstable motor units produce fasciculations.","whyNot":["Correct.","Fasciculations are spontaneous firing of whole motor units and point to lower motor neuron disease.","Grouped atrophy of small angular fibers means a whole motor unit lost its nerve. It is a neurogenic sign.","Fiber type grouping forms when surviving motor neurons reinnervate denervated fibers and convert them to their own type. It is a neurogenic sign."],"study":{"topics":["Neurogenic versus myopathic weakness"],"comps":["MS-10","MS-9"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C45","system":"MS","dx":"L4-L5 intervertebral disc herniation","codes":["MS-13-E"],"case":"A 44-year-old woman comes to the physician 3 days after she felt a sudden pain in her lower back while lifting a heavy box. The pain now radiates down the back of her left thigh and the lateral side of her leg to the top of her foot and big toe. Coughing worsens the pain. A straight-leg raise on the left reproduces the leg pain at 40 degrees. Neurologic examination shows weak extension of the left big toe and weak ankle dorsiflexion, with decreased sensation over the dorsum of the foot and the first web space. Patellar and Achilles reflexes are normal and symmetric. MRI of the lumbar spine shows a left posterolateral disc herniation at the L4-L5 level. Diagnosis: L4-L5 intervertebral disc herniation.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C45-Q1","stem":"Which of the following nerve roots is most likely compressed by this herniation?","options":["L5","L4","S1","L3"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-13-E","obj":"MS-13-E.9","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that a lumbar root exits below the pedicle of its own vertebra, so the L4 root leaves through the L4-L5 foramen above the disc.","A posterolateral herniation bulges into the lateral recess and hits the root traveling down to exit at the next level.","At L4-L5 that root is L5, which fits weak big toe extension, weak dorsiflexion, numbness of the foot dorsum, and normal reflexes."],"why":"With age, the nucleus pulposus dries out and the annulus fibrosus develops tears. The nucleus usually herniates posterolaterally, where the posterior longitudinal ligament is thinnest. In the lumbar spine, the root named for the upper vertebra has already exited above the disc, so a posterolateral L4-L5 herniation compresses the L5 root. L5 controls big toe extension and dorsiflexion and supplies skin on the dorsum of the foot, and it has no major reflex.","whyNot":["Correct.","L4 exits above the L4-L5 disc and is spared by a typical posterolateral herniation. Its compression would weaken knee extension and reduce the patellar reflex.","S1 is compressed by an L5-S1 herniation. It weakens plantarflexion, reduces the Achilles reflex, and causes numbness of the lateral foot.","L3 compression causes anterior thigh pain and weak knee extension and hip flexion. It would come from an L2-L3 herniation or a far lateral herniation at L3-L4."],"study":{"topics":["Lumbar disc herniation and root level","Spondylosis versus spondylolisthesis"],"comps":["MS-13-E","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C45-Q2","stem":"If the herniation had occurred one level lower, at L5-S1, which reflex would most likely be reduced, and which afferent fibers carry that reflex?","options":["Patellar reflex, carried by Ia afferents from muscle spindles","Achilles reflex, carried by Ia afferents from muscle spindles","Achilles reflex, carried by Ib afferents from Golgi tendon organs","Patellar reflex, carried by Ib afferents from Golgi tendon organs"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.3","dok":3,"approach":["An L5-S1 posterolateral herniation compresses the S1 root.","Match reflexes to roots: patellar is L4, Achilles is S1.","A tendon tap stretches the muscle, which activates spindle Ia afferents, so the stretch reflex runs through Ia fibers."],"why":"Tapping the Achilles tendon briefly stretches the gastrocnemius and soleus. Muscle spindles sense the stretch and fire Ia afferents that synapse directly on alpha motor neurons, mostly at S1, causing plantarflexion. An S1 root lesion interrupts this arc and reduces the reflex. Golgi tendon organs sense tension, signal through Ib afferents, and inhibit the same muscle; they do not drive the stretch reflex.","whyNot":["The patellar reflex runs through L4 and would be affected by an L3-L4 lesion or a far lateral L4-L5 herniation, not an L5-S1 herniation.","Correct.","The level is right, but Golgi tendon organs and Ib afferents sense tension and inhibit the muscle. The tendon jerk is a spindle Ia reflex.","Both parts are wrong: the patellar reflex is L4, and tendon reflexes use spindle Ia afferents, not Ib fibers."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle spindle versus Golgi tendon organ","Reflex root levels"],"comps":["MS-10"]}},{"id":"MS-C45-Q3","stem":"Her physician must distinguish this L5 radiculopathy from a common fibular nerve injury at the fibular neck. Which of the following findings would best support the radiculopathy?","options":["Weakness of foot eversion against resistance","Weakness of ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension","Contralateral pelvic drop in single-leg stance","Reduced sensation over the dorsum of the foot"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-5","obj":"MS-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Both lesions can cause foot drop, weak eversion, and numbness of the foot dorsum, so these do not separate them.","The L5 root also feeds nerves that the common fibular nerve does not carry, such as the superior gluteal nerve to gluteus medius and minimus.","Weak hip abduction shows as a Trendelenburg sign, with the pelvis dropping on the side opposite the weak muscle."],"why":"The common fibular nerve supplies the anterior and lateral leg compartments and the skin of the dorsum of the foot, so its injury causes foot drop, weak eversion, and dorsal foot numbness. An L5 root lesion causes the same findings but also weakens L5 muscles supplied by other nerves. The superior gluteal nerve (L4 to S1) innervates gluteus medius and minimus, which hold the pelvis level during single-leg stance. Weakness of these muscles lets the pelvis drop on the opposite side, which places the lesion above the fibular neck.","whyNot":["Weak eversion occurs with both lesions because the superficial fibular nerve supplies the fibularis muscles, which receive L5 and S1 fibers.","Weak dorsiflexion occurs with both lesions because the deep fibular nerve supplies tibialis anterior, which receives L4 and L5 fibers.","Correct.","Numbness of the foot dorsum occurs with both lesions because the superficial fibular nerve carries this L5 skin area."],"study":{"topics":["Lower limb nerve injuries","Root lesion versus peripheral nerve lesion"],"comps":["MS-5","MS-13-E"]}},{"id":"MS-C45-Q4","stem":"The herniated material passed through a radial tear in the annulus fibrosus. Which collagen types predominate in the outer annulus fibrosus and in the nucleus pulposus, respectively?","options":["Type I and type IV","Type II and type I","Type I and type II","Type III and type II"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-6","obj":"MS-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["The outer annulus fibrosus is fibrocartilage built to resist tension, like tendon and ligament.","Tension-resisting tissues use type I collagen.","The nucleus pulposus is a hydrated gel that resists compression, like hyaline cartilage, and uses type II collagen."],"why":"The outer annulus fibrosus is made of concentric lamellae of fibrocartilage that resist tension, and its main collagen is type I, as in tendon, ligament, and bone. The nucleus pulposus is a gel rich in proteoglycans with a fine network of type II collagen, the type found in hyaline cartilage. As the disc ages, the nucleus loses proteoglycans and water, and the annulus develops tears through which the nucleus can herniate.","whyNot":["Type IV collagen forms basement membranes. It is not the main collagen of the nucleus pulposus.","This reverses the two tissues. Type II collagen is the main collagen of the nucleus, not the outer annulus.","Correct.","Type III collagen forms reticular fibers and is prominent in vessels and early granulation tissue. It is not the main collagen of the annulus."],"study":{"topics":["Collagen types by tissue","Intervertebral disc structure"],"comps":["MS-6","MS-13-E"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C46","system":"MS","dx":"Oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis with anterior uveitis","codes":["MS-13-B"],"case":"A 4-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a 10-week history of swelling of her right knee and a limp that is worst in the morning. She has had no fever or rash. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F). Physical examination shows a warm right knee with a large effusion and a mild flexion contracture, and a swollen left ankle. No other joints are involved. Laboratory studies show a positive antinuclear antibody at a titer of 1:320, negative rheumatoid factor, a normal leukocyte count, and an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 28 mm/h (reference 0 to 20). Slit-lamp examination shows inflammatory cells in the anterior chamber of the right eye, although the eye is not red or painful. Diagnosis: oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis with anterior uveitis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C46-Q1","stem":"Her parents ask why she needs slit-lamp eye examinations every few months for several years. Which of the following features of this condition best explains this recommendation?","options":["Uveitis occurs mainly in the systemic form of the disease","Uveitis usually begins only after the arthritis remits","Uveitis is usually painful but responds to topical lubricants","Uveitis is often silent until it damages vision"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B","obj":"MS-13-B.14","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the subtype: arthritis in 4 or fewer joints for more than 6 weeks in a young ANA-positive girl is oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis.","Recall that this subtype carries the highest risk of chronic anterior uveitis, especially when ANA is positive.","The uveitis usually causes no redness or pain, so only regular slit-lamp screening finds it before damage occurs."],"why":"Juvenile idiopathic arthritis is arthritis that starts before age 16 and lasts at least 6 weeks with no other cause. The oligoarticular form affects 4 or fewer joints, often the knee, and is most common in young girls. ANA positivity marks a high risk of chronic anterior uveitis, which is typically silent, with no redness or pain. Untreated, it can cause cataract, band keratopathy, glaucoma, and vision loss, so children are screened regularly with slit-lamp examination.","whyNot":["Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis presents with daily spiking fever and a salmon-colored rash, and uveitis is uncommon in that form. The oligoarticular ANA-positive form has the highest risk.","Uveitis can appear before, with, or after the arthritis, and it can stay active when the joints are quiet. Its timing does not depend on remission.","The uveitis of oligoarticular disease is usually painless, and it needs anti-inflammatory treatment, not lubricants.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Juvenile idiopathic arthritis subtypes","Uveitis screening in JIA"],"comps":["MS-13-B","MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C46-Q2","stem":"At age 9 years, she develops pain in front of her right ear when chewing and a click when opening her mouth. MRI shows anterior displacement of the articular disc of the right temporomandibular joint. Which of the following nerves carries most of the pain sensation from this joint?","options":["Chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve","Great auricular nerve from C2 and C3","Buccal branch of the facial nerve","Auriculotemporal branch of CN V3"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the temporomandibular joint as a synovial joint with an articular disc that allows hinge and gliding motion.","Apply Hilton law: the nerve to the muscles that move a joint also supplies the joint. The muscles of mastication are supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3).","The auriculotemporal and masseteric branches of CN V3 supply the joint capsule."],"why":"The temporomandibular joint lies between the mandibular condyle and the temporal bone, with an articular disc dividing it into upper (gliding) and lower (hinge) compartments. When the disc slips forward, the condyle snaps back under it during opening, causing a click and pain. Sensation from the joint comes mainly from the auriculotemporal nerve, with help from the masseteric nerve, both branches of CN V3. Juvenile idiopathic arthritis often involves this joint and can disturb mandibular growth.","whyNot":["The chorda tympani carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular and sublingual glands. It does not supply the joint.","The great auricular nerve supplies skin over the parotid gland and earlobe, not the joint capsule.","The buccal branch of the facial nerve is motor to buccinator and muscles of the upper lip. It carries no joint sensation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Temporomandibular joint anatomy","Hilton law"],"comps":["MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C46-Q3","stem":"Her 35-year-old mother has had 4 months of symmetric pain and swelling of the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints of both hands, with 2 hours of morning stiffness. Which of the following antibodies is most specific for the mother's most likely disease?","options":["Anti-dsDNA antibody","Anti-CCP antibody","Anticentromere antibody","Anti-Jo-1 antibody"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Symmetric small joint arthritis of the hands with long morning stiffness in an adult suggests rheumatoid arthritis.","Rheumatoid factor is sensitive but not specific.","Antibodies to cyclic citrullinated peptides are the most specific marker."],"why":"Rheumatoid arthritis typically involves the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints symmetrically, with morning stiffness lasting more than an hour. Anti-CCP antibodies recognize citrullinated proteins and are highly specific for rheumatoid arthritis. They can appear years before symptoms and predict more erosive disease. Rheumatoid factor is also common in rheumatoid arthritis but appears in many other conditions.","whyNot":["Anti-dsDNA is specific for SLE, where arthritis is usually nonerosive and comes with rash, serositis, cytopenias, or nephritis.","Correct.","Anticentromere antibodies mark limited systemic sclerosis, with Raynaud phenomenon, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasias.","Anti-Jo-1 is a myositis antibody linked to interstitial lung disease, mechanic's hands, and arthritis in antisynthetase syndrome, with proximal muscle weakness."],"study":{"topics":["Autoantibodies in rheumatic disease"],"comps":["MS-11"]}},{"id":"MS-C46-Q4","stem":"Radiographs of the mother's hands show marginal erosions at the metacarpophalangeal joints. Which of the following mechanisms most directly produces these erosions?","options":["RANKL-driven osteoclasts at the edge of synovial pannus","Anti-dsDNA immune complexes in synovial vessels","Uric acid crystals activating the NLRP3 inflammasome","Mechanical wear of cartilage with subchondral sclerosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["In rheumatoid arthritis, CD4+ T cells reacting to citrullinated peptides (linked to HLA-DR4) drive synovial inflammation through TNF, IL-1, and IL-6.","The inflamed synovium grows into a pannus that spreads over cartilage and bone at the joint margins.","Synovial cells and T cells express RANKL, which activates osteoclasts that cut marginal erosions."],"why":"In rheumatoid arthritis, HLA-DR4 associated CD4+ T cells respond to citrullinated proteins and activate macrophages and B cells. Cytokines such as TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 cause synovial hyperplasia and inflammation, forming a pannus of proliferating synovium and granulation tissue. Pannus releases enzymes that destroy cartilage, and RANKL from synovial fibroblasts and T cells activates osteoclasts. Bone is eroded first at the joint margins, where synovium touches bone not covered by cartilage.","whyNot":["Correct.","Immune complexes cause the arthritis of SLE, which is typically nonerosive. Anti-dsDNA is not part of rheumatoid arthritis.","Uric acid crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome in gout. Gout erosions have overhanging edges and occur near tophi, often in the first metatarsophalangeal joint.","Mechanical wear with subchondral sclerosis and osteophytes describes osteoarthritis, which favors the distal interphalangeal joints and is not erosive in this way."],"study":{"topics":["Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","Pannus and RANKL"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C47","system":"MS","dx":"Lyme arthritis of the knee","codes":["MS-12"],"case":"An 11-year-old girl in Connecticut is brought to the physician because of intermittent swelling of her left knee over the past 2 months. Each episode lasts about a week, and the knee is only mildly painful. She can walk normally. Last summer she spent several weeks at a wooded camp in Wisconsin and had an expanding red rash on her thigh that faded without treatment. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Examination shows a large, mildly warm effusion of the left knee with full range of motion. Other joints are normal. Serum enzyme immunoassay for Borrelia burgdorferi is positive and confirmed by IgG immunoblot. Diagnosis: Lyme arthritis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C47-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes how she most likely acquired this infection?","options":["Bite of an adult Dermacentor tick attached for a few minutes","Bite of a larval Ixodes tick that hatched already infected","Bite of a body louse, with its feces rubbed into the skin","Bite of a nymphal Ixodes tick attached for 36 hours or more"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-12","obj":"MS-12.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete.","Recall the cycle: Ixodes larvae hatch uninfected and pick up the organism from white-footed mice, then molt into nymphs.","Nymphs are small, are active in late spring and summer, and must stay attached for about 36 hours or more before the spirochete is transmitted."],"why":"Borrelia burgdorferi is maintained in white-footed mice. Ixodes scapularis larvae hatch uninfected because the spirochete is rarely passed through the egg; they acquire it when feeding on infected mice. After molting, the nymph transmits the spirochete during its next blood meal. Spirochetes multiply in the tick gut and move to the salivary glands over a day or more, so transmission usually needs about 36 hours or more of attachment. Nymphs are tiny and often unnoticed, which is why many patients do not recall a bite.","whyNot":["Dermacentor ticks transmit Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Rickettsia rickettsii), not Lyme disease, and a few minutes of attachment is too short for Borrelia.","Ixodes larvae hatch uninfected because transovarial transmission of B. burgdorferi is rare. They acquire the spirochete by feeding on infected mice.","Body lice transmit Borrelia recurrentis (louse-borne relapsing fever) when crushed lice are rubbed into the skin, and also epidemic typhus. They do not transmit Lyme disease.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Borrelia burgdorferi transmission cycle","Ixodes life cycle"],"comps":["MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C47-Q2","stem":"Arthrocentesis of the left knee yields cloudy yellow fluid. Analysis shows a leukocyte count of 24,000/mm3 (normal under 200/mm3) with 75% neutrophils, a negative Gram stain, and no crystals on polarized light microscopy. Which of the following best classifies this fluid?","options":["Septic, as in staphylococcal joint infection","Inflammatory, as in rheumatoid arthritis","Hemorrhagic, as in traumatic hemarthrosis","Noninflammatory, as in osteoarthritis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-G","obj":"MS-13-G.2","dok":3,"approach":["Use leukocyte count cutoffs: under 2,000/mm3 is noninflammatory, 2,000 to about 50,000/mm3 is inflammatory, and septic fluid is usually over 50,000/mm3 with mostly neutrophils.","Her count of 24,000/mm3 falls in the inflammatory range, and the Gram stain is negative.","No crystals means this is not gout or pseudogout. Inflammatory fluid fits Lyme arthritis."],"why":"Synovial fluid is grouped by leukocyte count. Noninflammatory fluid (under 2,000/mm3) is seen in osteoarthritis and trauma. Inflammatory fluid (about 2,000 to 50,000/mm3) is seen in rheumatoid arthritis, crystal arthritis, reactive arthritis, and Lyme arthritis, which often has counts of 10,000 to 50,000/mm3. Septic fluid usually exceeds 50,000/mm3 with over 90% neutrophils and often a positive Gram stain or culture. The ranges overlap, so culture, crystal search, and the clinical picture are always used together.","whyNot":["Septic fluid usually has more than 50,000 leukocytes/mm3 with over 90% neutrophils, and bacteria are often seen on Gram stain. Her count is lower, the stain is negative, and she is nearly afebrile.","Correct.","Hemorrhagic fluid is bloody and follows trauma or a bleeding disorder. Her fluid is cloudy yellow, not bloody.","Noninflammatory fluid has fewer than 2,000 leukocytes/mm3 and is clear. Her count is far higher."],"study":{"topics":["Synovial fluid analysis","Approach to the hot swollen joint"],"comps":["MS-13-G","MS-12"]}},{"id":"MS-C47-Q3","stem":"Before the effusion was linked to Lyme disease, she was examined for a soccer injury of the knee. Which of the following findings would have indicated a tear of the anterior cruciate ligament?","options":["Lateral joint opening with varus stress at 30 degrees of flexion","Posterior tibial translation with the knee flexed 90 degrees","Anterior tibial translation with the knee flexed 20 to 30 degrees","Medial joint line opening with valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the ACL runs from the anterior tibia to the lateral femoral condyle and stops the tibia from sliding forward on the femur.","A torn ACL lets the tibia translate anteriorly, which the Lachman test (20 to 30 degrees of flexion) and the anterior drawer test (90 degrees) detect.","Match the other tests: valgus stress for MCL, varus stress for LCL, posterior drawer for PCL."],"why":"The anterior cruciate ligament attaches to the anterior intercondylar area of the tibia and the medial side of the lateral femoral condyle. It resists anterior translation of the tibia on the femur. The Lachman test, done at 20 to 30 degrees of flexion, is the most sensitive bedside test for an ACL tear, and the anterior drawer test at 90 degrees checks the same motion. The MCL is fused to the medial meniscus, which is why the two are often injured together.","whyNot":["Lateral opening with varus stress tests the lateral collateral ligament, which resists varus force.","Posterior translation at 90 degrees is a positive posterior drawer test, indicating a PCL tear.","Correct.","Medial opening with valgus stress tests the medial collateral ligament, which resists valgus force."],"study":{"topics":["Knee ligament tests","Knee ligaments and menisci"],"comps":["MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C47-Q4","stem":"Two months later, her 34-year-old mother develops symmetric swelling and stiffness of the small joints of both hands. One week earlier, the mother's 5-year-old son had bright red cheeks followed by a lacy rash on his arms. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the mother's arthritis?","options":["Parvovirus B19 associated arthritis","Early Lyme arthritis from a tick bite","Disseminated gonococcal arthritis","HLA-B27 associated reactive arthritis"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"MS-11","obj":"MS-11.7","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the child's illness: slapped-cheek rash followed by a lacy rash is erythema infectiosum, caused by parvovirus B19.","Recall that adults, especially women, infected with parvovirus B19 often develop symmetric small joint arthritis.","Distinguish it from reactive arthritis and Lyme arthritis, which are asymmetric and favor large joints of the legs."],"why":"Parvovirus B19 causes erythema infectiosum in children. Adults, especially women, often have little or no rash but develop acute symmetric polyarthritis of the hands, wrists, and knees that resembles rheumatoid arthritis. The arthritis is immune-mediated and usually resolves within weeks. Rubella infection and, less often, rubella vaccination can cause a similar transient arthritis or arthralgia in adult women.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lyme arthritis is a late finding that affects one or a few large joints, mainly the knee. It does not cause symmetric small joint arthritis after a child's rash.","Disseminated gonococcal infection causes migratory arthralgia, tenosynovitis, and pustular skin lesions, or a purulent monoarthritis. It does not follow a child's rash.","Reactive arthritis follows enteric or chlamydial infection, is linked to HLA-B27, and is usually an asymmetric arthritis of the large joints of the legs."],"study":{"topics":["Postinfectious arthritis","Parvovirus B19 in adults"],"comps":["MS-11","MS-12"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C48","system":"MS","dx":"Adhesive capsulitis with Dupuytren contracture in diabetes","codes":["MS-13-B"],"case":"A 56-year-old woman with a 12-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the physician because of a 5-month history of pain and progressive stiffness of the right shoulder. She can no longer reach behind her back or brush her hair. She has had no injury. Her hemoglobin A1c is 8.9% (reference under 5.7). Examination shows marked loss of both active and passive external rotation and abduction of the right shoulder; strength is normal within the available range. In the left palm there is a firm nodule with a cord extending to the ring finger, which cannot be fully extended at the metacarpophalangeal joint. Radiographs of the right shoulder are normal. Diagnosis: adhesive capsulitis of the right shoulder and Dupuytren contracture of the left hand.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C48-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her shoulder cannot be moved passively by the examiner?","options":["Full-thickness tear of the supraspinatus tendon","Denervation of the deltoid by axillary nerve injury","Calcium pyrophosphate deposits in the labrum","Inflammation and fibrosis of the joint capsule"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-B","obj":"MS-13-B.16","dok":3,"approach":["Compare active and passive motion: muscle, tendon, or nerve problems reduce active motion, but the examiner can still move the arm passively.","When passive motion is also lost, something inside the joint or capsule is mechanically blocking movement.","With normal radiographs and diabetes, the best fit is a thickened, contracted capsule."],"why":"Adhesive capsulitis is inflammation followed by fibrosis and contraction of the glenohumeral joint capsule. The capsule shrinks and loses its folds, so both active and passive motion are limited, especially external rotation and abduction. Radiographs are normal, which separates it from arthritis. Diabetes is a major risk factor, and the condition often coexists with other fibrosing hand disorders such as Dupuytren contracture.","whyNot":["A rotator cuff tear weakens active abduction, but the examiner can usually move the arm passively through a full range.","Axillary nerve injury weakens deltoid abduction but does not stop passive movement. Her strength is also normal.","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition causes acute pseudogout or chronic arthropathy, with chondrocalcinosis often visible on radiographs. Her radiographs are normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Adhesive capsulitis","Active versus passive range of motion"],"comps":["MS-13-B","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C48-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the contracture of her left ring finger?","options":["Synovial inflammation of the flexor tendon sheaths","Myofibroblast proliferation in the palmar fascia","Fibrosis of the metacarpophalangeal joint capsule","Thickening of the A1 pulley over the flexor tendons"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E","obj":"MS-13-E.11","dok":2,"approach":["Find the location of the lesion: a palpable nodule and cord in the palm, superficial to the tendons.","That tissue is the palmar aponeurosis.","Contractile myofibroblasts in this fascia form the nodules and cords that pull the ring and little fingers into flexion."],"why":"Dupuytren contracture is a fibroproliferative disorder of the palmar fascia. Myofibroblasts, which contain smooth muscle actin, form nodules and then cords of dense collagen that shorten over time. The cords pull the ring and little fingers into flexion at the metacarpophalangeal and then the proximal interphalangeal joints. Risk factors include diabetes, family history, alcohol use, and smoking.","whyNot":["Flexor tenosynovitis causes pain and swelling along the tendon sheath, as in rheumatoid arthritis or infection, without a fascial cord in the palm.","Correct.","Capsular fibrosis of the joint itself is not the source of a palpable palmar cord.","Thickening of the A1 pulley causes trigger finger, with catching or locking during finger flexion rather than a fixed palmar cord."],"study":{"topics":["Dupuytren contracture","Hand disorders in diabetes"],"comps":["MS-13-E","MS-13-B"]}},{"id":"MS-C48-Q3","stem":"Two years later, she falls on her outstretched, abducted, externally rotated right arm and sustains an anterior dislocation of the glenohumeral joint. After reduction, which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Winging of the scapula with weak protraction of the arm","Wrist drop with numbness over the dorsum of the hand","Weak abduction with numbness of the lateral shoulder","Weak elbow flexion with numbness of the lateral forearm"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["The glenohumeral joint has a shallow glenoid and depends on the rotator cuff and capsule for stability, so it dislocates easily, usually anteriorly and inferiorly.","The displaced humeral head stretches the axillary nerve as it winds around the surgical neck.","Predict loss of deltoid function (abduction from about 15 to 90 degrees) and numbness over the lateral shoulder."],"why":"The glenoid fossa is shallow and covers only part of the humeral head, so stability comes mainly from the rotator cuff muscles and the capsule and labrum. The anterior-inferior capsule is weakest, so most dislocations are anterior. The axillary nerve passes through the quadrangular space and around the surgical neck of the humerus, where the displaced head can stretch it. Injury weakens the deltoid and teres minor and causes numbness over the lateral shoulder (the regimental badge area). Supraspinatus can still start abduction for the first 15 degrees.","whyNot":["Scapular winging comes from long thoracic nerve injury, usually after surgery or trauma along the chest wall, not from anterior dislocation.","Wrist drop comes from radial nerve injury, typically at the spiral groove with a midshaft humeral fracture.","Correct.","Weak elbow flexion with lateral forearm numbness comes from musculocutaneous nerve injury, which is much less common with dislocation than axillary nerve injury."],"study":{"topics":["Glenohumeral stability","Axillary nerve injury"],"comps":["MS-4","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C48-Q4","stem":"The myofibroblasts in her palmar cords express alpha-smooth muscle actin and contract in a way that resembles smooth muscle. In smooth muscle, which of the following events directly allows myosin to begin cross-bridge cycling?","options":["Rise in cGMP after nitric oxide enters the cell","Dephosphorylation of myosin light chains by a phosphatase","Phosphorylation of myosin light chains by their kinase","Binding of calcium to troponin C on thin filaments"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-7","obj":"MS-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that smooth muscle has no troponin, so regulation is on the myosin side.","Calcium binds calmodulin, and calcium-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase.","Phosphorylated myosin light chains allow cross-bridge cycling. The phosphatase reverses this and relaxes the cell."],"why":"In smooth muscle, a rise in cytosolic calcium leads to calcium binding calmodulin. The calcium-calmodulin complex activates myosin light chain kinase, which phosphorylates the regulatory light chains of myosin and allows cross-bridges to form and cycle. Myosin light chain phosphatase removes the phosphate and causes relaxation. If myosin is dephosphorylated while still attached, the cross-bridges cycle slowly and hold tension at low energy cost, which is the latch state.","whyNot":["Nitric oxide raises cGMP, which activates protein kinase G and favors relaxation by lowering calcium and activating the phosphatase.","Myosin light chain phosphatase removes the phosphate and causes relaxation, not contraction.","Correct.","Troponin C regulates skeletal and cardiac muscle. Smooth muscle lacks troponin."],"study":{"topics":["Smooth muscle contraction and relaxation","Myosin light chain kinase"],"comps":["MS-7"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C49","system":"MS","dx":"Renal osteodystrophy in chronic kidney disease","codes":["MS-13-C"],"case":"A 67-year-old man with chronic kidney disease from diabetic nephropathy comes to the physician because of aching pain in his hips, lower back, and ribs for 6 months. He also has difficulty rising from a chair. He is not yet on dialysis. His estimated glomerular filtration rate is 14 mL/min/1.73 m2 (reference over 90). Serum studies show calcium 8.1 mg/dL (reference 8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 6.8 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), parathyroid hormone 640 pg/mL (10 to 65), alkaline phosphatase 310 U/L (40 to 130), 25-hydroxyvitamin D 34 ng/mL (30 to 100), and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D 9 pg/mL (18 to 72). Radiographs of the hands show subperiosteal bone resorption along the radial sides of the middle phalanges. Diagnosis: renal osteodystrophy with secondary hyperparathyroidism.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C49-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his elevated parathyroid hormone concentration?","options":["Phosphate retention and calcitriol deficiency","Excess calcitriol from extrarenal hydroxylase","Autonomous secretion by a parathyroid adenoma","Ectopic PTH-related peptide from a carcinoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-13-C","obj":"MS-13-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Look at the direction of each value: calcium is low, phosphate is high, PTH is high, and calcitriol is low.","High PTH with low calcium means the parathyroids are responding appropriately, which is secondary hyperparathyroidism.","Failing kidneys retain phosphate and make less calcitriol, and both lower serum calcium and stimulate PTH."],"why":"As kidney function falls, phosphate excretion drops and serum phosphate rises. High phosphate binds calcium and also stimulates the parathyroids directly. The diseased kidney makes less calcitriol, so intestinal calcium absorption falls, and FGF23 from osteocytes rises and further suppresses calcitriol. Low calcium, high phosphate, and low calcitriol all drive PTH up. Chronic high PTH increases bone turnover and resorption, causing osteitis fibrosa cystica with subperiosteal resorption, while low calcitriol impairs mineralization.","whyNot":["Correct.","Extrarenal calcitriol production, as in sarcoidosis, raises calcium and suppresses PTH. His calcitriol is low.","A parathyroid adenoma causes primary hyperparathyroidism, with high calcium and low or normal phosphate. His calcium is low and phosphate is high.","PTH-related peptide causes hypercalcemia and suppresses native PTH. His PTH is high and his calcium is low."],"study":{"topics":["Renal osteodystrophy","Secondary hyperparathyroidism"],"comps":["MS-13-C","MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C49-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes how parathyroid hormone increases osteoclast activity in his skeleton?","options":["Releases calcitonin, which stimulates osteoclast resorption","Raises osteoprotegerin secretion, which activates osteoclasts","Binds receptors on osteoclasts to directly increase resorption","Induces osteoblast RANKL, activating osteoclast precursors"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that PTH receptors are on osteoblasts and osteocytes, not osteoclasts.","PTH makes osteoblasts express RANKL and less osteoprotegerin.","RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their maturation and activity, so PTH acts on osteoclasts indirectly."],"why":"Osteoclasts do not have PTH receptors. PTH binds receptors on osteoblasts and osteocytes, which then increase RANKL and decrease osteoprotegerin. RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and, with M-CSF, drives them to fuse and resorb bone. Osteoprotegerin is a decoy receptor that binds RANKL and limits this process. PTH also increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule, increases phosphate excretion, and activates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, although the last effect fails in advanced kidney disease.","whyNot":["Calcitonin from thyroid C cells inhibits osteoclasts. PTH does not cause calcitonin release.","Osteoprotegerin is a decoy receptor that blocks RANKL and inhibits osteoclasts. PTH lowers it.","This is a common misconception. Osteoclasts lack PTH receptors, so the effect is indirect through osteoblasts.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["PTH action on bone","RANKL and osteoprotegerin"],"comps":["MS-8","MS-2"]}},{"id":"MS-C49-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his 25-hydroxyvitamin D concentration is normal while his 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D concentration is low?","options":["Reduced 1-alpha-hydroxylation in the kidney","Reduced intestinal absorption of dietary vitamin D","Reduced 25-hydroxylation of vitamin D in the liver","Reduced synthesis of vitamin D3 in sun-exposed skin"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"MS-8","obj":"MS-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace activation: 7-dehydrocholesterol in skin becomes vitamin D3 with UVB light, the liver adds a 25-hydroxyl group, and the kidney adds the 1-alpha-hydroxyl group.","A normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D means skin synthesis, intake, and liver conversion are adequate.","The block must be at the final renal step."],"why":"Vitamin D3 is made in the skin when UVB converts 7-dehydrocholesterol, or it comes from the diet. The liver converts it to 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the main circulating form, whose level reflects total vitamin D supply. The proximal tubule then adds a 1-alpha-hydroxyl group to form active calcitriol. In chronic kidney disease, loss of renal mass and high FGF23 reduce 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity, so calcitriol falls even when 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal.","whyNot":["Correct.","Poor intestinal absorption, as in fat malabsorption, reduces supply and would lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D.","Reduced hepatic 25-hydroxylation, as in severe liver disease, would lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D.","Reduced skin synthesis lowers the supply of vitamin D and would lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D, which is normal here."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation","Vitamin D status testing"],"comps":["MS-8"]}},{"id":"MS-C49-Q4","stem":"His serum alkaline phosphatase is elevated because bone turnover is high. Which of the following best describes the bone cells that are the main source of this enzyme?","options":["Multinucleated cells of monocyte and macrophage origin","Mesenchymal-derived cells that secrete osteoid","Mechanosensing cells within mineralized lacunae","Hypertrophic chondrocytes within a growth plate"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-2","obj":"MS-2.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that bone alkaline phosphatase is a marker of bone formation.","Bone formation is carried out by osteoblasts, which come from mesenchymal cells and secrete type I collagen osteoid.","In high-turnover bone disease, increased resorption is coupled with increased osteoblast activity, so alkaline phosphatase rises."],"why":"Osteoblasts arise from mesenchymal stem cells. They secrete type I collagen osteoid and alkaline phosphatase, which helps mineralize the matrix by raising local phosphate. In secondary hyperparathyroidism, resorption and formation both speed up, so osteoblast activity and serum alkaline phosphatase rise. Osteoclasts, the multinucleated resorbing cells, come from the monocyte and macrophage lineage and use acid and cathepsin K in Howship lacunae.","whyNot":["This describes osteoclasts, which resorb bone with acid and cathepsin K. They are a source of tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase, not alkaline phosphatase.","Correct.","This describes osteocytes, which sense mechanical load and secrete FGF23 and sclerostin. They are not the main source of serum alkaline phosphatase.","Growth plates are closed in adults. Hypertrophic chondrocytes are found only in growing bones."],"study":{"topics":["Bone cell types","Markers of bone turnover"],"comps":["MS-2","MS-8"]}}]},{"id":"MS-C50","system":"MS","dx":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis presenting as knee pain","codes":["MS-13-E"],"case":"A 13-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a 6-week history of dull pain in his left knee and a limp. The pain is worse after sports. He recalls no injury. His body mass index is at the 98th percentile for age and sex. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F). Examination of the left knee shows no swelling, tenderness, or instability. Passive flexion of the left hip produces obligatory external rotation, and internal rotation of the hip is markedly limited and painful. Anteroposterior and frog-leg lateral radiographs of the pelvis show posterior and inferior displacement of the left femoral head relative to the femoral neck. Diagnosis: slipped capital femoral epiphysis.","questions":[{"id":"MS-C50-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient best distinguishes his disorder from Legg-Calve-Perthes disease?","options":["Limited and painful internal rotation of the hip","Displacement of the femoral head at the physis","Hip disease presenting as pain in the knee","Limp without fever or a history of injury"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E","obj":"MS-13-E.8","dok":3,"approach":["List what the two disorders share: a limp, hip or knee pain, loss of internal rotation, and no fever.","Recall what differs: Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is idiopathic osteonecrosis of the femoral head in children about 4 to 8 years old, while this disorder is a slip of the epiphysis through the physis in an obese adolescent.","Pick the finding that reflects the slip itself: the femoral head displaced on the neck at the growth plate."],"why":"In slipped capital femoral epiphysis, the femoral neck moves anteriorly and superiorly while the epiphysis stays in the acetabulum, so the head looks displaced posteriorly and inferiorly on radiographs. The slip happens through the weak hypertrophic zone of the physis during the growth spurt, and obesity adds shear force. Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is idiopathic osteonecrosis of the femoral head, usually at about 4 to 8 years of age, and radiographs show a flattened or fragmented head that has not slipped. Developmental dysplasia of the hip is found in newborns with the Ortolani and Barlow maneuvers.","whyNot":["Loss of internal rotation is a sign of hip joint disease in general. It is also seen in Legg-Calve-Perthes disease, so it does not separate the two.","Correct.","Both disorders can present as knee pain, because nerves to the hip also supply the knee. It does not separate the two.","Both disorders usually cause a limp without fever or a clear injury. Fever with refusal to bear weight would instead suggest septic arthritis."],"study":{"topics":["Pediatric hip disorders by age","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis versus Legg-Calve-Perthes disease"],"comps":["MS-13-E"]}},{"id":"MS-C50-Q2","stem":"Before hip radiographs were obtained, his knee pain was thought to come from a common adolescent knee disorder. Which of the following findings would have instead supported Osgood-Schlatter disease?","options":["Tender swelling over the tibial tubercle","Knee locking with a loose fragment from the femoral condyle","Hip pain with limited internal rotation of the hip","Anterior knee pain worsened by sitting with the knees bent"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"MS-13-E","obj":"MS-13-E.10","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis where the patellar tendon pulls on the tibial tubercle apophysis.","It is common in active adolescents during growth spurts.","The key finding is a tender, sometimes enlarged, tibial tubercle."],"why":"In Osgood-Schlatter disease, repeated pull of the quadriceps through the patellar tendon irritates the tibial tubercle apophysis in growing adolescents. Running and jumping worsen the pain, and the tubercle is tender and often prominent. Chondromalacia patellae is softening of patellar cartilage with anterior knee pain, often worse after long sitting. Osteochondritis dissecans is separation of a fragment of subchondral bone and cartilage, often from the medial femoral condyle, which can cause locking.","whyNot":["Correct.","Locking with a loose fragment suggests osteochondritis dissecans, which separates subchondral bone and cartilage.","Limited hip internal rotation points to a hip disorder such as this patient's, not to Osgood-Schlatter disease.","Anterior knee pain after long sitting with bent knees suggests chondromalacia patellae, which involves the patellar cartilage, not the tibial tubercle."],"study":{"topics":["Adolescent knee disorders"],"comps":["MS-13-E","MS-4"]}},{"id":"MS-C50-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why disease of his hip was felt as pain in the knee?","options":["The superior and inferior gluteal nerves supply both joints","The lateral femoral cutaneous and saphenous nerves supply both joints","The pudendal and genitofemoral nerves supply both joints","The obturator and femoral nerves supply both joints"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"MS-4","obj":"MS-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Apply Hilton law: the nerves that supply the muscles moving a joint also supply that joint.","The femoral and obturator nerves send branches to the hip capsule and also to the knee.","Pain from the hip carried in these nerves can be perceived in the knee, their shared territory."],"why":"Hilton law states that a nerve supplying the muscles acting on a joint also supplies the joint and the skin over it. The hip capsule receives branches from the femoral and obturator nerves, and both nerves also send articular branches to the knee. Because the brain cannot always tell which end of the nerve's territory the signal came from, hip pain is often felt in the thigh or knee. This is why a child or adolescent with knee pain and a normal knee exam needs a hip exam.","whyNot":["The gluteal nerves supply the gluteal muscles and do not supply the knee joint.","These are cutaneous nerves to the skin of the thigh and medial leg. They do not carry pain from the hip joint.","The pudendal nerve supplies the perineum, and the genitofemoral nerve supplies the cremaster and skin of the upper anterior thigh. Neither supplies both joints.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hilton law","Referred pain from the hip"],"comps":["MS-4","MS-5"]}},{"id":"MS-C50-Q4","stem":"After surgical fixation, he begins physical therapy with active knee extension exercises. As the quadriceps shortens, which of the following keeps its muscle spindles able to detect further stretch?","options":["Ib afferent firing from Golgi tendon organs","Renshaw cell inhibition of alpha motor neurons","Gamma motor neuron activation of intrafusal fibers","Alpha motor neuron activation of extrafusal fibers"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"MS-10","obj":"MS-10.3","dok":2,"approach":["Muscle spindles lie parallel to extrafusal fibers and sense stretch through Ia afferents.","When the muscle shortens, the spindle would go slack and stop firing.","Gamma motor neurons contract the ends of intrafusal fibers, taking up the slack so the spindle stays sensitive."],"why":"Muscle spindles are made of intrafusal fibers lying in parallel with the main extrafusal fibers. When the muscle shortens, the spindle would go slack and its Ia afferents would fall silent. Gamma motor neurons fire along with alpha motor neurons (alpha-gamma coactivation), contracting the ends of the intrafusal fibers and keeping the central sensory region taut. This keeps the spindle able to signal length changes during movement.","whyNot":["Golgi tendon organs lie in series with the muscle and sense tension through Ib afferents. They do not reset spindle sensitivity.","Renshaw cells provide recurrent inhibition of alpha motor neurons. They do not act on intrafusal fibers.","Correct.","Alpha motor neurons contract the extrafusal fibers, which shortens the muscle and unloads the spindle rather than keeping it taut."],"study":{"topics":["Muscle spindle and gamma motor neurons"],"comps":["MS-10"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C01","system":"NE","dx":"Embolic left middle cerebral artery ischemic stroke from atrial fibrillation","codes":["NE-20-A1","NE-20-A2"],"case":"A 72-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 90 minutes after his wife noticed that he could not speak and could not lift his right arm. He has hypertension and intermittent palpitations for 1 year. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 112/min and irregularly irregular, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 164/92 mm Hg. Physical examination shows weakness of the right lower face, 1/5 strength in the right arm, and 4/5 strength in the right leg. His speech is sparse and effortful, and he cannot repeat a short phrase, but he follows two-step commands. An ECG shows atrial fibrillation. A noncontrast CT scan of the head shows no hemorrhage. CT angiography shows occlusion of the left middle cerebral artery. Diagnosis: embolic ischemic stroke in the left middle cerebral artery territory from atrial fibrillation.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C01-Q1","stem":"Emboli from the left atrium can travel to any branch of the cerebral circulation. If the embolus had instead lodged in the left posterior cerebral artery, which of the following deficits would be most likely?","options":["Weakness of the right leg greater than the right arm","Pure motor weakness of the right face, arm, and leg","Right homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing","Loss of pain and temperature sense on the left face"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-10","obj":"NE-10.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the main cortical territory of the posterior cerebral artery: the occipital lobe and inferior temporal lobe.","A left occipital lesion removes the right visual field of both eyes, a right homonymous hemianopia.","Macular sparing occurs because the occipital pole often receives collateral flow from the middle cerebral artery."],"why":"The posterior cerebral artery supplies the primary visual cortex along the calcarine sulcus. Each occipital lobe receives information about the opposite half of the visual field from both eyes, so a left PCA infarct produces a right homonymous hemianopia. The macula is often spared because its cortical representation at the occipital pole has a dual supply from the MCA and PCA.","whyNot":["Leg-predominant weakness is the anterior cerebral artery syndrome, because the leg area of the motor cortex lies on the medial surface of the hemisphere.","Pure motor hemiparesis is a lacunar syndrome from occlusion of a lenticulostriate artery supplying the posterior limb of the internal capsule.","Correct.","Ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss comes from the spinal trigeminal nucleus in the lateral medulla, supplied by the PICA or vertebral artery."],"study":{"topics":["Cerebral artery stroke syndromes","Visual field defects"],"comps":["NE-10","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C01-Q2","stem":"A repeat CT scan 2 days later shows patchy areas of blood within the infarcted territory. Which of the following best explains why this change is more typical of his type of infarct than of a thrombotic infarct?","options":["Venous outflow blocked by thrombus in a dural sinus","Reperfusion of injured vessels after the embolus breaks up","Rupture of a Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysm in the infarct","Amyloid deposits weakening cortical arteriole walls"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-A1","obj":"NE-20-A.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the change as hemorrhagic conversion of an ischemic infarct.","Recall that emboli often fragment and move distally, restoring flow to the area.","Blood returning into necrotic, leaky vessel walls leaks into the tissue, so embolic infarcts often turn hemorrhagic, while a fixed thrombus keeps the territory bloodless (pale infarct)."],"why":"Embolic infarcts are often hemorrhagic. The embolus lyses or breaks up and moves downstream, and blood flows back into vessels whose walls were damaged by ischemia. The blood leaks out and gives a petechial or confluent hemorrhagic infarct. A thrombus at an atherosclerotic plaque usually stays in place, so the infarct remains pale.","whyNot":["Dural sinus thrombosis can cause venous hemorrhagic infarcts, but this patient has an arterial occlusion proven on CT angiography.","Correct.","Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms form in small penetrating arteries in chronic hypertension and cause primary intraparenchymal hemorrhage in the basal ganglia, thalamus, pons, and cerebellum, not bleeding into an infarct.","Cerebral amyloid angiopathy causes recurrent lobar hemorrhages in older adults, unrelated to an embolic infarct."],"study":{"topics":["Hemorrhagic conversion of embolic infarcts","Thrombotic versus embolic stroke"],"comps":["NE-20-A1","NE-20-A2"]}},{"id":"NE-C01-Q3","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely site of the lesion causing his language deficit?","options":["Angular gyrus of the parietal lobe","Posterior superior temporal gyrus","Arcuate fasciculus","Posterior inferior frontal gyrus"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-11","obj":"NE-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Classify the aphasia: speech is nonfluent and repetition is impaired, but comprehension is intact.","Nonfluent speech with good comprehension is Broca aphasia.","Broca area lies in the posterior inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere, supplied by the superior division of the MCA."],"why":"Broca aphasia produces slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with poor repetition while comprehension is largely intact. The lesion is in the posterior inferior frontal gyrus (Broca area) of the dominant, usually left, hemisphere. It often comes with right face and arm weakness because the adjacent motor cortex is in the same MCA superior division territory.","whyNot":["A dominant angular gyrus lesion causes Gerstmann syndrome (agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, left-right confusion), not a nonfluent aphasia.","Wernicke area lesions give fluent speech with poor comprehension, the opposite of this patient.","Damage to the arcuate fasciculus causes conduction aphasia: fluent speech and good comprehension with poor repetition.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Aphasia types and lesion sites"],"comps":["NE-11","NE-3"]}},{"id":"NE-C01-Q4","stem":"His right face and arm are much weaker than his right leg. Which of the following best explains this pattern of weakness?","options":["Leg fibers cross in the pons, unlike arm fibers","The leg area of the precentral gyrus lies on the medial surface","The leg is represented mainly in the postcentral gyrus","Leg motor fibers run in the anterior limb of the internal capsule"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-3","obj":"NE-3.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the motor homunculus on the precentral gyrus: face and hand on the lateral convexity, leg on the medial surface.","The MCA supplies the lateral convexity; the ACA supplies the medial surface.","An MCA infarct therefore weakens the face and arm more than the leg."],"why":"The primary motor cortex lies in the precentral gyrus. Its face, hand, and arm areas are on the lateral surface of the hemisphere, and the leg and foot area wraps over onto the medial surface in the paracentral lobule. The MCA supplies the lateral surface, so an MCA infarct causes contralateral face and arm weakness with relative sparing of the leg.","whyNot":["All lateral corticospinal fibers cross at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla, not in the pons.","Correct.","The postcentral gyrus is primary somatosensory cortex. Its lesion causes loss of sensation, not weakness.","Corticospinal fibers for the arm and leg run in the posterior limb of the internal capsule. The anterior limb carries mainly frontal and thalamic connections."],"study":{"topics":["Motor homunculus","MCA versus ACA territory"],"comps":["NE-3","NE-10"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C02","system":"NE","dx":"Hypertensive intraparenchymal hemorrhage of the right putamen and internal capsule","codes":["NE-20-A4","NE-20-A2"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after the sudden onset of headache, vomiting, and weakness of her left side while gardening. She has a 15-year history of hypertension and stopped taking her medications 6 months ago. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 212/118 mm Hg. She is drowsy but arousable. Physical examination shows left lower facial weakness, 2/5 strength in the left arm and leg, decreased sensation on the entire left side, and an extensor plantar response on the left. Her eyes are deviated to the right. A noncontrast CT scan of the head shows a 3.5-cm hyperdense hematoma in the right putamen extending into the internal capsule. Diagnosis: hypertensive intraparenchymal hemorrhage.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C02-Q1","stem":"Which of the following vascular changes most likely preceded the rupture?","options":["Microaneurysms of small penetrating arteries","Saccular aneurysm at a branch point of the circle of Willis","Amyloid beta deposition in cortical arteriole walls","Direct artery-to-vein connections without a capillary bed"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-A4","obj":"NE-20-A.7","dok":3,"approach":["Note the setting: chronic uncontrolled hypertension and a deep hemorrhage in the putamen.","Recall that chronic hypertension damages small penetrating arteries and forms Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms.","These vessels supply the basal ganglia, thalamus, pons, and cerebellum, the classic sites of hypertensive hemorrhage."],"why":"Long-standing hypertension causes hyaline arteriolosclerosis and weakening of small penetrating arteries such as the lenticulostriates. Tiny Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms form and can rupture, producing a deep hematoma. The putamen is the most common site, followed by the thalamus, pons, and cerebellum.","whyNot":["Correct.","A ruptured saccular (berry) aneurysm bleeds mainly into the subarachnoid space, with sudden severe headache and blood in the basal cisterns.","Cerebral amyloid angiopathy causes lobar hemorrhages near the cortex in older adults, not deep basal ganglia bleeds.","Arteriovenous malformations can bleed into the brain, but they usually present in young adults with hemorrhage or seizures and are not caused by hypertension."],"study":{"topics":["Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms","Deep versus lobar hemorrhage"],"comps":["NE-20-A4","NE-10"]}},{"id":"NE-C02-Q2","stem":"Her left hemiparesis results from damage to corticospinal fibers in the posterior limb of the right internal capsule. Which of the following best describes the course most of these fibers normally take after leaving the internal capsule?","options":["Cerebral peduncle, then decussation in the caudal medulla","Cerebral peduncle, then decussation in the midbrain tegmentum","Uncrossed descent in the anterior funiculus to the lumbar cord","Middle cerebellar peduncle, then decussation in the pons"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-7","obj":"NE-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Follow the corticospinal tract downward: internal capsule, cerebral peduncle in the midbrain, basis pontis, medullary pyramid.","About 85 to 90 percent of the fibers cross at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla to form the lateral corticospinal tract.","A lesion above the decussation therefore causes contralateral weakness."],"why":"Corticospinal axons leave the precentral gyrus, pass through the posterior limb of the internal capsule, and descend in the middle portion of the cerebral peduncle and the basis pontis to the medullary pyramid. Most cross in the pyramidal decussation at the junction of the medulla and spinal cord and descend in the lateral corticospinal tract to anterior horn alpha motor neurons. Because the crossing is low, a right capsular lesion causes left-sided upper motor neuron weakness.","whyNot":["Correct.","The fibers pass through the cerebral peduncle, but they do not cross in the midbrain.","The anterior corticospinal tract stays uncrossed in the anterior funiculus, but it carries only a small fraction of fibers and ends mainly in the cervical and upper thoracic cord.","The middle cerebellar peduncle carries pontocerebellar fibers to the cerebellum, not corticospinal fibers."],"study":{"topics":["Corticospinal tract course"],"comps":["NE-7","NE-3"]}},{"id":"NE-C02-Q3","stem":"MRI 1 week later shows that the hemorrhage also disrupted white matter connections of the right inferior parietal lobule. She ignores people standing on her left, eats only from the right side of her plate, and denies that her left arm is weak. Which of the following best explains why this deficit follows right-sided rather than left-sided damage?","options":["The right somatosensory cortex receives bilateral input","The right parietal lobe holds the dominant language network","The right parietal lobe attends to both sides of space","The right optic radiation carries both visual fields"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-11","obj":"NE-11.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the behavior as hemispatial neglect with anosognosia (denial of the deficit).","Recall that the right parietal association cortex directs attention to both the left and right halves of space, while the left mainly attends to the right.","After a right-sided lesion, nothing attends to left space, so the left side is ignored."],"why":"Spatial attention is lateralized. The nondominant (usually right) parietal association cortex attends to both hemispaces, while the left parietal lobe covers mainly the right hemispace. A right parietal lesion leaves the left side of space without attention, causing hemispatial neglect. A left parietal lesion is compensated by the intact right side, so neglect after left-sided damage is rare and mild.","whyNot":["Primary somatosensory cortex receives input from the opposite side of the body. A lesion there causes sensory loss, not inattention to one side of space.","Language is lateralized to the left hemisphere in almost all right-handed people. A dominant parietal lesion at the angular gyrus causes Gerstmann syndrome, not neglect.","Correct.","Each optic radiation carries the opposite visual field only. Neglect also occurs when visual fields are intact."],"study":{"topics":["Hemispatial neglect","Parietal association cortex"],"comps":["NE-11"]}},{"id":"NE-C02-Q4","stem":"In patients with sudden focal deficits, a noncontrast CT scan of the head is obtained before any thrombolytic therapy is considered. Which of the following best explains why this study is used first?","options":["It shows ischemic infarcts within the first hour","It identifies the occluded artery and its collaterals","It maps the ischemic penumbra that can still be saved","Acute blood is hyperdense without contrast"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-A2","obj":"NE-20-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that ischemic and hemorrhagic strokes can look identical at the bedside.","Thrombolysis is dangerous if the stroke is hemorrhagic.","Fresh blood is bright (hyperdense) on CT right away, while early ischemia is often invisible for hours."],"why":"Ischemic stroke, hemorrhagic stroke, and even TIA can present with the same sudden focal deficits, and they share risk factors such as hypertension, atrial fibrillation, diabetes, smoking, and dyslipidemia. Treatment differs completely, and thrombolytics would worsen a hemorrhage. Acute blood is dense and appears bright on a noncontrast CT within minutes, so this fast test reliably excludes hemorrhage before treatment.","whyNot":["Early ischemic infarcts are often invisible on CT for the first several hours. MRI diffusion imaging detects them much sooner.","Locating the occluded vessel requires CT angiography or another vascular study, not a noncontrast scan.","The penumbra is assessed with perfusion imaging (CT or MRI perfusion), not a plain noncontrast scan.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ischemic versus hemorrhagic stroke","Stroke imaging"],"comps":["NE-20-A2"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C03","system":"NE","dx":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured anterior communicating artery aneurysm","codes":["NE-20-A4"],"case":"A 44-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 2 hours after the sudden onset of the worst headache of her life while lifting weights. She briefly lost consciousness and has vomited twice. She has autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 178/100 mm Hg. Physical examination shows nuchal rigidity and photophobia. She is oriented, and there are no focal neurologic deficits. A noncontrast CT scan of the head shows blood in the basal cisterns and the anterior interhemispheric fissure. CT angiography shows a 7-mm saccular aneurysm of the anterior communicating artery. Diagnosis: subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured anterior communicating artery aneurysm.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C03-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the formation of this type of aneurysm?","options":["Weak tunica media at arterial branch points","Septic emboli lodging in and weakening the vessel wall","Atherosclerotic thinning along a long arterial segment","Hyaline thickening of small penetrating arterioles"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-A4","obj":"NE-20-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion as a saccular (berry) aneurysm at a branch point of the circle of Willis.","Recall that the arterial wall at branch points has a deficient media and internal elastic lamina.","Link her ADPKD to the higher risk of saccular aneurysms; Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, hypertension, and smoking also raise the risk."],"why":"Saccular aneurysms form at branch points of the circle of Willis, most often near the anterior communicating artery, where the tunica media is thin or absent. Hemodynamic stress over years bulges the weak wall outward. ADPKD and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome raise the risk, and hypertension and smoking add to it. Rupture causes subarachnoid hemorrhage with sudden severe headache.","whyNot":["Correct.","Septic emboli from infective endocarditis cause mycotic aneurysms, usually in distal MCA branches. She has no signs of infection.","Atherosclerosis produces fusiform aneurysms, most often of the basilar artery, not small berry aneurysms at branch points.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of penetrating arterioles leads to lacunar infarcts and Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms, which bleed into deep brain tissue."],"study":{"topics":["Saccular aneurysm pathogenesis","ADPKD extrarenal features"],"comps":["NE-20-A4","NE-10"]}},{"id":"NE-C03-Q2","stem":"The parent vessel of her aneurysm links the two arteries that supply which of the following regions?","options":["Occipital cortex and the inferior temporal lobe","Medial frontal and parietal cortex, including the leg area","Lateral convexity of the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes","Internal capsule, basal ganglia, and thalamus"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-10","obj":"NE-10.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the anterior communicating artery joins the right and left anterior cerebral arteries.","Recall the ACA territory: the medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes.","That territory includes the leg area of the motor and sensory cortex."],"why":"The circle of Willis connects the internal carotid and vertebrobasilar systems. The anterior communicating artery joins the two anterior cerebral arteries, and the posterior communicating arteries join each internal carotid to a posterior cerebral artery. The ACAs run in the interhemispheric fissure and supply the medial frontal and parietal cortex, including the leg region of the motor and sensory strips. This is why vasospasm after an ACom aneurysm rupture can cause leg weakness.","whyNot":["The occipital and inferior temporal cortex belong to the posterior cerebral arteries, which come from the basilar artery.","Correct.","The lateral convexity is the middle cerebral artery territory. The MCAs are not joined by a communicating artery.","The internal capsule and basal ganglia are supplied mainly by lenticulostriate branches of the MCA, and the thalamus by small PCA branches."],"study":{"topics":["Circle of Willis","ACA territory"],"comps":["NE-10"]}},{"id":"NE-C03-Q3","stem":"Arteriovenous malformations are another common cause of intracranial bleeding in young adults. Which of the following angiographic findings would indicate an arteriovenous malformation rather than an aneurysm?","options":["Beaded narrowing of the internal carotid arteries","A spindle-shaped dilation of a long arterial segment","A rounded outpouching at an arterial branch point","Early venous filling through a tangle of vessels"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-A4","obj":"NE-20-A.9","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the defining structure of an AVM: arteries draining directly into veins with no capillary bed.","Without capillary resistance, contrast reaches the veins during the arterial phase.","Pick the finding of early venous filling through a vascular tangle."],"why":"An arteriovenous malformation is a tangle (nidus) of abnormal vessels in which arteries connect directly to veins without an intervening capillary bed. Blood shunts through at high flow and pressure, so draining veins fill early on angiography and their thin walls can rupture. AVMs typically present in young adults with intracerebral hemorrhage or seizures.","whyNot":["A string-of-beads pattern is typical of fibromuscular dysplasia, which narrows rather than shunts vessels.","A spindle-shaped dilation is a fusiform aneurysm, usually atherosclerotic.","A rounded outpouching at a branch point is a saccular aneurysm, the lesion in this patient.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Arteriovenous malformation"],"comps":["NE-20-A4"]}},{"id":"NE-C03-Q4","stem":"If her CT scan had been normal, a lumbar puncture 12 hours after onset would have been the next step. Which of the following cerebrospinal fluid findings would be most likely?","options":["Clear fluid with high protein and few cells","Clear fluid with lymphocytes and normal glucose","Xanthochromia with a constant red cell count","Turbid fluid with neutrophils and low glucose"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-4","obj":"NE-4.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that subarachnoid hemorrhage puts red cells directly into the CSF.","Red cells lyse over hours and release hemoglobin, which is broken down to bilirubin, giving a yellow tint (xanthochromia).","A traumatic tap shows red cells that fall from tube 1 to tube 4 and no xanthochromia; SAH shows a steady count."],"why":"In subarachnoid hemorrhage the CSF contains many red cells in every tube collected. Over the first hours, red cells lyse and release hemoglobin, which is converted to bilirubin. By about 12 hours the supernatant is reliably yellow (xanthochromia). A traumatic tap shows a falling red cell count across tubes and a clear supernatant. Opening pressure is often raised, and protein rises slightly from the blood.","whyNot":["High protein with few cells (albuminocytologic dissociation) is typical of Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Clear CSF with lymphocytes and normal glucose is the pattern of viral meningitis.","Correct.","Turbid CSF with neutrophils and low glucose is the pattern of bacterial meningitis."],"study":{"topics":["CSF findings in SAH","CSF patterns by cause"],"comps":["NE-4","NE-20-A4"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C04","system":"NE","dx":"Epidural hematoma from a temporal bone fracture with uncal herniation","codes":["NE-20-A4","NE-20-H1"],"case":"A 19-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 3 hours after he was struck on the left side of the head by a baseball. He lost consciousness briefly, then talked normally. Over the past hour, he has developed a severe headache, vomiting, and increasing drowsiness. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 54/min, respirations are 10/min and irregular, and blood pressure is 182/96 mm Hg. He opens his eyes only to pain. The left pupil is 7 mm and does not react to light; the right pupil is 3 mm and reactive. He has right-sided weakness and a right extensor plantar response. CT of the head shows a biconvex hyperdense collection over the left temporal lobe that does not cross sutures, a left squamous temporal bone fracture, and midline shift. Diagnosis: epidural hematoma with uncal herniation.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C04-Q1","stem":"The fracture line crosses the thin bone at the pterion. Laceration of which of the following vessels is the most likely source of the hematoma?","options":["Bridging cortical veins","Superior sagittal sinus","Middle cerebral artery","Middle meningeal artery"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-A4","obj":"NE-20-A.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the clues: temporal bone fracture, lucid interval, and a biconvex collection limited by sutures.","These describe an epidural hematoma, which forms between the skull and the periosteal dura.","The middle meningeal artery runs in a groove on the inner table under the pterion and tears with the fracture."],"why":"The middle meningeal artery, a branch of the maxillary artery, enters the skull through the foramen spinosum and runs between the dura and the inner table beneath the pterion, where the bone is thin. A temporal fracture tears it, and arterial blood strips the dura from the skull. The dura is attached firmly at sutures, so the collection is biconvex and does not cross suture lines. Arterial pressure makes it expand quickly after a lucid interval.","whyNot":["Torn bridging veins cause a subdural hematoma, which is crescent shaped and crosses suture lines.","The superior sagittal sinus runs in the midline, far from a temporal fracture. Sinus tears can cause epidural bleeding at the vertex, but not here.","The middle cerebral artery lies in the subarachnoid space. Its rupture would not collect blood between the dura and skull.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Epidural versus subdural hematoma","Middle meningeal artery"],"comps":["NE-20-A4","NE-20-H1"]}},{"id":"NE-C04-Q2","stem":"The medial temporal lobe has been pushed past the free edge of the tentorium cerebelli. As this herniation progresses, which of the following arteries is most likely to be compressed?","options":["Anterior cerebral artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Middle cerebral artery","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-4","obj":"NE-4.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify uncal (transtentorial) herniation of the medial temporal lobe through the tentorial notch.","List the structures at the tentorial notch: CN III, the cerebral peduncle, and the posterior cerebral artery.","PCA compression can cause an occipital infarct."],"why":"In uncal herniation the uncus of the temporal lobe slides medially and downward through the tentorial notch. There it compresses the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve (dilated pupil), the cerebral peduncle, and the posterior cerebral artery as it curves around the midbrain. PCA compression can infarct the occipital lobe and cause a contralateral homonymous hemianopia in survivors.","whyNot":["The anterior cerebral artery is compressed against the falx cerebri in subfalcine (cingulate) herniation.","Correct.","The middle cerebral artery runs in the lateral sulcus and is not trapped at the tentorial notch.","The posterior inferior cerebellar artery runs beside the medulla, far below the tentorial notch. The danger there comes from tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum."],"study":{"topics":["Brain herniation syndromes","Dural folds"],"comps":["NE-4","NE-20-H1"]}},{"id":"NE-C04-Q3","stem":"When a bright light is shone into his left eye, which of the following responses is expected?","options":["Only the right pupil constricts","Both pupils constrict normally","Neither of the pupils constricts","Only the left pupil constricts"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-6","obj":"NE-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two limbs of the reflex: CN II carries light information in, and CN III carries the constriction signal out.","Uncal herniation compresses the left CN III, so the efferent limb to the left pupil is lost while the left optic nerve still works.","Light in the left eye reaches both Edinger-Westphal nuclei, so the right pupil constricts (consensual response) and the left does not."],"why":"Light entering one eye travels in CN II to the pretectal nuclei, which project to both Edinger-Westphal nuclei. Parasympathetic fibers then travel in each CN III to the ciliary ganglion and the pupillary sphincter. With the left CN III compressed, the afferent signal from the left eye is still received, so the right pupil constricts, but the left pupil cannot constrict in response to light in either eye.","whyNot":["Correct.","Both pupils constricting normally means both pathways work, which does not fit a fixed, dilated left pupil.","Loss of both responses would require a lesion of the left optic nerve or both efferent pathways. His right CN III is intact.","This would occur with a right CN III lesion, the opposite side from his herniation."],"study":{"topics":["Pupillary light reflex","CN III compression"],"comps":["NE-6","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C04-Q4","stem":"If the hematoma is not evacuated and the brainstem continues to shift downward, which of the following is the most likely finding at autopsy?","options":["Hydrocephalus from blocked arachnoid granulations","Blood filling the ventricles from the hematoma","Pontine hemorrhages from torn basilar branches","Hemorrhagic conversion of a cerebellar infarct"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-H1","obj":"NE-20-H.3","dok":3,"approach":["Follow the sequence: expanding hematoma, rising intracranial pressure, uncal herniation, downward brainstem displacement.","The basilar artery is tethered, so its small paramedian branches stretch and tear as the brainstem moves down.","This produces secondary (Duret) hemorrhages in the midbrain and pons, which are usually fatal."],"why":"An expanding epidural hematoma raises intracranial pressure until the uncus herniates. Continued pressure pushes the brainstem downward while the basilar artery stays relatively fixed, so its paramedian perforating branches stretch and rupture. The resulting Duret hemorrhages in the midline midbrain and pons destroy vital centers and cause death unless the hematoma is removed promptly.","whyNot":["Blocked arachnoid granulations cause communicating hydrocephalus after subarachnoid hemorrhage or meningitis, a slower process.","An epidural hematoma lies outside the dura, so it cannot spread into the ventricles.","Correct.","A cerebellar infarct is not part of this process, and hemorrhagic conversion follows reperfusion of an ischemic infarct."],"study":{"topics":["Duret hemorrhages","Progression of raised intracranial pressure"],"comps":["NE-20-H1","NE-4"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C05","system":"NE","dx":"Acute subdural hematoma with contrecoup frontal contusions after a fall","codes":["NE-20-A4","NE-20-H1"],"case":"An 81-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 6 hours after he slipped and fell backward, striking the back of his head on a tile floor. He was initially confused, and over the next few hours he became increasingly difficult to arouse. He drinks about six beers daily. Temperature is 36.6 °C (97.9 °F), pulse is 64/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 168/88 mm Hg. He opens his eyes only to pain and withdraws his limbs from painful stimuli. There is a large occipital scalp hematoma. A CT scan of the head shows a crescent-shaped hyperdense collection over the left frontoparietal convexity that crosses the coronal suture, with 7 mm of midline shift, contusions of both frontal poles and the inferior frontal lobes, and diffuse cerebral atrophy. Diagnosis: acute subdural hematoma with contrecoup frontal contusions.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C05-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why older adults with brain atrophy are at increased risk for this type of hematoma?","options":["Hyaline thickening of small penetrating arterioles","Loss of CSF absorption by arachnoid granulations","Thinning of the squamous temporal bone over the pterion","Stretching of veins crossing from cortex to dural sinus"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-10","obj":"NE-10.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the lesion: a crescent-shaped collection crossing sutures is a subdural hematoma.","Recall the source: bridging veins that run from the cortical surface across the subdural space to the dural venous sinuses.","Atrophy pulls the brain away from the skull, stretching these veins so minor trauma tears them."],"why":"Cortical veins drain into the dural venous sinuses, such as the superior sagittal sinus, by crossing the subdural space as bridging veins. Blood then leaves through the transverse and sigmoid sinuses to the internal jugular veins. When the brain shrinks with age or chronic alcohol use, these bridging veins are stretched and tear easily with acceleration-deceleration forces. Venous blood spreads along the convexity under the dura and crosses suture lines.","whyNot":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of penetrating arterioles causes lacunar infarcts and deep hypertensive hemorrhage.","Impaired CSF absorption causes communicating hydrocephalus, not bleeding.","A thin pterion predisposes to middle meningeal artery tears and epidural hematoma, not subdural bleeding.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Bridging veins and dural sinuses","Subdural hematoma risk factors"],"comps":["NE-10","NE-20-A4"]}},{"id":"NE-C05-Q2","stem":"He struck the back of his head, yet his contusions are in the frontal lobes. Which of the following best explains the location of the contusions?","options":["Rupture of small arteries beneath the scalp hematoma","Brain striking the inner skull opposite the impact","Shearing of long axons at the gray-white junction","Spread of subdural blood into the frontal cortex"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-H1","obj":"NE-20-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that a moving head that stops suddenly lets the brain keep moving inside the skull.","A coup injury forms at the impact site; a contrecoup injury forms on the opposite side as the brain hits the skull there.","The frontal and temporal poles rest on rough bony floors, so they bruise most often."],"why":"When the moving head strikes a fixed surface, the brain continues forward and then rebounds within the skull. The surface opposite the impact strikes the inner table, producing a contrecoup contusion. Contusions are most common at the frontal poles, orbital frontal surfaces, and temporal poles because these areas lie against irregular bony ridges of the anterior and middle cranial fossae.","whyNot":["Scalp vessels are outside the skull. A scalp hematoma does not bruise brain tissue.","Correct.","Shearing of axons is diffuse axonal injury. It causes coma with axonal swellings at the gray-white junction and corpus callosum, not focal cortical bruises.","Subdural blood compresses the brain surface but does not infiltrate the cortex to form contusions."],"study":{"topics":["Coup and contrecoup contusions","Diffuse axonal injury"],"comps":["NE-20-H1"]}},{"id":"NE-C05-Q3","stem":"His hematoma involves only one cerebral hemisphere. Which of the following best explains his depressed level of consciousness?","options":["Pressure on the cerebellar hemispheres and vermis","Damage to the hippocampus of the left temporal lobe","Compression of the upper brainstem reticular formation","Injury limited to the left primary motor cortex and its fibers"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-3","obj":"NE-3.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that consciousness requires the ascending reticular activating system of the upper pons and midbrain plus at least one working cerebral hemisphere.","A lesion in one hemisphere alone does not cause coma.","A mass lesion causes coma when shift and herniation compress the upper brainstem and diencephalon."],"why":"Arousal depends on the ascending reticular activating system in the upper pons and midbrain, which projects through the thalamus to both hemispheres. Coma results from damage to this system or from widespread dysfunction of both hemispheres. A unilateral subdural hematoma causes coma when midline shift and herniation compress the upper brainstem. Brain death differs from coma: it is irreversible loss of all brain and brainstem function, including brainstem reflexes and respiratory drive.","whyNot":["Cerebellar lesions cause ataxia. Coma occurs only if a cerebellar mass compresses the brainstem.","Damage to one hippocampus impairs memory formation but does not reduce arousal.","Correct.","A primary motor cortex lesion causes contralateral weakness, not loss of consciousness."],"study":{"topics":["Reticular activating system","Coma versus brain death"],"comps":["NE-3","NE-4"]}},{"id":"NE-C05-Q4","stem":"His daughter reports that he boxed professionally for 15 years and has had slowly worsening memory, irritability, and unsteady gait for several years before this fall. Which of the following would most likely be found in his brain?","options":["TDP-43 inclusions in anterior horn motor neurons","Spongiform change from misfolded prion protein","Alpha-synuclein Lewy bodies in substantia nigra neurons","Perivascular tau tangles at the depths of sulci"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-H1","obj":"NE-20-H.1","dok":3,"approach":["Link years of repeated head impacts with progressive cognitive and behavioral decline.","This is chronic traumatic encephalopathy (dementia pugilistica).","Its marker is hyperphosphorylated tau, typically around small vessels at the depths of cortical sulci."],"why":"Repeated concussive and subconcussive blows can lead to chronic traumatic encephalopathy. A single concussion causes transient dysfunction with normal imaging and may leave postconcussion symptoms, but repeated injury over years causes progressive memory loss, mood and behavior change, and parkinsonian or gait features. Pathology shows hyperphosphorylated tau tangles clustered around small vessels at the depths of sulci, with brain atrophy.","whyNot":["TDP-43 inclusions in motor neurons are typical of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.","Spongiform change from prion protein is Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, a rapidly progressive dementia over months.","Lewy bodies of alpha-synuclein in the substantia nigra define Parkinson disease. The marker of repeated head trauma is tau, not alpha-synuclein.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic traumatic encephalopathy","Concussion"],"comps":["NE-20-H1","NE-20-B"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C06","system":"NE","dx":"Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome from vertebral artery dissection","codes":["NE-20-A1"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of sudden vertigo, nausea, hoarseness, and difficulty swallowing that began 4 hours ago. Three days ago she was in a rear-end motor vehicle collision, and since then she has had pain in the right side of the back of her neck. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg. Physical examination shows right ptosis and miosis, nystagmus, decreased pain and temperature sensation on the right side of the face and on the left trunk and limbs, right limb ataxia, and weakness of the right soft palate. Strength in all limbs is normal. MRI shows an infarct in the right lateral medulla. MR angiography shows a dissection of the right vertebral artery. Diagnosis: lateral medullary syndrome from vertebral artery dissection.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C06-Q1","stem":"Damage to which of the following structures best explains the loss of pain and temperature sensation on her left trunk and limbs?","options":["Lateral spinothalamic tract","Medullary pyramid","Spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract","Medial lemniscus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-10","obj":"NE-10.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the crossed sensory pattern: pain loss on the right face and the left body.","Pain fibers from the body cross in the spinal cord and ascend in the spinothalamic tract, which runs through the lateral medulla.","A right lateral medullary lesion therefore removes pain sensation from the left body."],"why":"The lateral medulla, supplied by the PICA and the vertebral artery, contains the spinothalamic tract, the spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract, the nucleus ambiguus, the vestibular nuclei, the inferior cerebellar peduncle, and descending sympathetic fibers. Spinothalamic fibers have already crossed in the spinal cord, so a right lesion causes left body pain and temperature loss. The trigeminal pain fibers have not yet crossed, so the face loses sensation on the right. The medial structures (pyramid, medial lemniscus, CN XII) are spared, which is why her strength is normal.","whyNot":["Correct.","The pyramid carries corticospinal fibers. Its damage causes contralateral weakness, which she does not have.","The spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract carry pain from the ipsilateral face. Their damage explains her right facial sensory loss, not the left body loss.","The medial lemniscus carries vibration and position sense and lies medially. It is damaged in medial medullary syndrome from anterior spinal artery occlusion."],"study":{"topics":["Lateral medullary syndrome","Brainstem vascular syndromes"],"comps":["NE-10","NE-5"]}},{"id":"NE-C06-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains how the arterial injury led to her infarct?","options":["Rupture of an atherosclerotic plaque at the carotid bifurcation","Reversed vertebral flow from proximal subclavian stenosis","Intimal tear causing an intramural hematoma and thrombus","Lipohyalinosis of small penetrating medullary arteries"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-A1","obj":"NE-20-A.10","dok":3,"approach":["Note the setting: a young adult with neck pain after neck trauma, then a posterior circulation stroke.","Recall that dissection begins with a tear in the intima, letting blood track into the wall.","The intramural hematoma narrows the lumen, and thrombus at the tear can occlude the artery or embolize."],"why":"Cervical artery dissection is a leading cause of stroke in young adults. Neck trauma, even minor, tears the intima, and blood enters the vessel wall to form an intramural hematoma. The lumen narrows, and thrombus forms on the exposed wall, which can occlude the vessel or send emboli downstream. Neck pain or headache on the side of the dissection often precedes the stroke. Carotid dissection classically adds an ipsilateral Horner syndrome from injury to the sympathetic plexus on the artery.","whyNot":["Carotid bifurcation plaque causes anterior circulation thrombotic or embolic strokes in older adults with atherosclerotic risk factors.","Subclavian steal reverses vertebral flow and causes arm claudication, unequal arm blood pressures, and transient vertebrobasilar symptoms with arm exercise, not dissection after trauma.","Correct.","Lipohyalinosis of penetrating arteries in hypertension causes small lacunar infarcts, not a dissection-related infarct in a young adult."],"study":{"topics":["Cervical artery dissection","Subclavian steal syndrome"],"comps":["NE-20-A1","NE-10"]}},{"id":"NE-C06-Q3","stem":"Her right ptosis and miosis result from interruption of which of the following neurons?","options":["Parasympathetic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus","Descending hypothalamic fibers in the lateral brainstem","Preganglionic fibers leaving the T1 ventral root","Postganglionic fibers in the internal carotid plexus"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-9","obj":"NE-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize Horner syndrome: ptosis, miosis, and often anhidrosis.","Trace the three-neuron path: hypothalamus down the lateral brainstem to C8 to T2, then to the superior cervical ganglion, then along the carotid plexus.","Her lesion is in the lateral medulla, so the first-order (central) neurons are interrupted."],"why":"The oculosympathetic pathway has three neurons. First-order neurons descend from the hypothalamus through the lateral brainstem and cervical cord to the ciliospinal center at C8 to T2. Second-order preganglionic neurons exit the upper thoracic roots and ascend over the lung apex to the superior cervical ganglion. Third-order postganglionic fibers travel on the internal carotid artery to the dilator pupillae and the superior tarsal muscle. A lateral medullary infarct interrupts the first-order fibers and causes an ipsilateral central Horner syndrome.","whyNot":["Loss of Edinger-Westphal parasympathetic output causes a dilated pupil, the opposite of miosis.","Correct.","Preganglionic fibers at T1 are injured by a Pancoast tumor at the lung apex.","Postganglionic fibers on the internal carotid artery are injured in carotid dissection, not in a medullary infarct."],"study":{"topics":["Oculosympathetic pathway","Horner syndrome"],"comps":["NE-9","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C06-Q4","stem":"Her vertigo and nystagmus result from damage to the right vestibular nuclei. Which of the following best explains why a one-sided lesion produces a false sense of rotation?","options":["Unequal tonic input from the two vestibular sides","Loss of linear acceleration signals from both utricles","Loss of cochlear input from the ipsilateral ear","Increased firing of the damaged nuclei during head turns"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-6","obj":"NE-6.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that each vestibular apparatus fires tonically at rest, and the brain compares the two sides.","Head rotation normally raises firing on one side and lowers it on the other.","A one-sided loss mimics this imbalance, so the brain senses rotation and the vestibulo-ocular reflex drives nystagmus."],"why":"The semicircular canals sense angular acceleration, and the utricle and saccule sense linear acceleration and gravity. Both sides fire at a steady rate at rest. Turning the head increases firing on one side and decreases it on the other, and the brain reads this difference as rotation. When one side's vestibular nuclei are damaged, the resulting imbalance is read as constant rotation, producing vertigo, and the vestibulo-ocular reflex drives the eyes slowly to one side with corrective fast phases (nystagmus).","whyNot":["Correct.","The utricles signal linear acceleration and head tilt, not rotation, and a one-sided lesion does not remove input from both.","Cochlear input carries hearing. Its loss causes deafness, as in AICA infarcts, but not vertigo.","Damaged nuclei fire less, not more. The symptom comes from the imbalance between the two sides."],"study":{"topics":["Vestibular physiology","Vestibulo-ocular reflex"],"comps":["NE-6","NE-8"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C07","system":"NE","dx":"Global hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy after cardiac arrest","codes":["NE-20-A3"],"case":"A 57-year-old man collapses while exercising at a gym. An automated external defibrillator shows ventricular fibrillation, and a return of spontaneous circulation is achieved after 22 minutes of resuscitation. During the next 24 hours, his systolic blood pressure repeatedly falls below 80 mm Hg. On day 5, temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 16/min on a ventilator, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. He does not open his eyes or respond to commands. Pupils react to light, and he has intermittent generalized myoclonic jerks. MRI of the brain shows restricted diffusion in the cerebral cortex, both hippocampi, and the border zones between the anterior and middle cerebral artery territories. Diagnosis: global hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy. He dies on day 9.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C07-Q1","stem":"At autopsy, which of the following neuronal populations is most likely to show the most severe loss?","options":["Pyramidal neurons of the hippocampal CA1 sector","Motor neurons of the spinal cord anterior horns","Granule cells of the hippocampal dentate gyrus","Motor neurons of the brainstem cranial nerve nuclei"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-A3","obj":"NE-20-A.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that neurons differ in their sensitivity to global hypoxia.","The most vulnerable are hippocampal CA1 pyramidal cells, cerebellar Purkinje cells, and pyramidal neurons of neocortical layers 3, 5, and 6.","Brainstem and spinal cord neurons are relatively resistant."],"why":"Global hypoxia-ischemia damages neurons selectively. Hippocampal CA1 (Sommer sector) pyramidal cells, cerebellar Purkinje cells, and neocortical pyramidal neurons are most vulnerable, in part because of high glutamate receptor density and high energy demand. Cortical damage produces laminar necrosis. Severe hypotension also causes watershed infarcts in border zones between arterial territories, as seen on his MRI.","whyNot":["Correct.","Spinal anterior horn cells are relatively resistant to global hypoxia.","Dentate granule cells lie in the same hippocampal formation but are relatively resistant to hypoxia.","Brainstem motor neurons are relatively resistant, which is why brainstem reflexes such as the pupillary response can persist after severe cortical injury."],"study":{"topics":["Selective neuronal vulnerability","Watershed infarcts"],"comps":["NE-20-A3","NE-2"]}},{"id":"NE-C07-Q2","stem":"Sections of the cerebral cortex show many neurons with shrunken cell bodies, intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm, and small dark pyknotic nuclei. Which of the following best describes these cells?","options":["Reactive gliosis from injury several weeks old","Irreversible injury at least 12 to 24 hours old","Chromatolysis from injury to the neuron's own axon","Reversible injury that began within minutes of arrest"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-2","obj":"NE-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cells as red neurons from the shrinkage, eosinophilia, and pyknosis.","Red neurons mark irreversible neuronal death.","They first appear about 12 to 24 hours after hypoxia or ischemia."],"why":"Red neurons are the earliest reliable histologic sign of irreversible neuronal injury. The cell body shrinks, the cytoplasm turns intensely eosinophilic as Nissl substance is lost, and the nucleus becomes pyknotic. They appear about 12 to 24 hours after the hypoxic or ischemic event, so their presence shows the injury is permanent and at least that old.","whyNot":["Reactive gliosis is an astrocyte response that becomes prominent over weeks. It describes glial cells, not shrunken neurons.","Correct.","Chromatolysis after axonal injury shows a swollen cell body with dispersed Nissl substance and a nucleus pushed to the edge, the opposite appearance.","Reversible injury in the first minutes to hours shows little or no change on light microscopy."],"study":{"topics":["Red neurons","Neuronal reactions to injury"],"comps":["NE-2","NE-20-A3"]}},{"id":"NE-C07-Q3","stem":"On day 3, while he is receiving an insulin infusion and his tube feeding is interrupted, serum glucose falls to 38 mg/dL (reference 70 to 100 mg/dL), and he has a generalized seizure. Which of the following best explains why his brain cannot compensate for the low glucose?","options":["The brain cannot use fatty acids and has little glycogen","Neuronal glucose uptake depends on insulin through GLUT4","Glucose crosses the blood-brain barrier by simple diffusion","Neuronal GLUT3 has too low an affinity to take up glucose"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-14","obj":"NE-14.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how glucose reaches neurons: GLUT1 across the blood-brain barrier and GLUT3 on neurons, both independent of insulin.","Ask what fuel the brain can use when glucose is low: it cannot oxidize fatty acids, and its glycogen store is tiny.","Ketone bodies can substitute only after days of fasting, so acute hypoglycemia causes neuroglycopenia."],"why":"The brain depends almost entirely on blood glucose. Glucose crosses the blood-brain barrier through GLUT1 and enters neurons through high-affinity GLUT3, neither of which needs insulin. The brain does not use fatty acids as a fuel, and its glycogen store (mostly in astrocytes) lasts only minutes. Ketone bodies become a major fuel only after several days of fasting. Acute hypoglycemia therefore causes neuroglycopenic symptoms: confusion, seizures, and coma.","whyNot":["Correct.","GLUT4 is the insulin-dependent transporter in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue. Neuronal glucose uptake does not depend on insulin.","Glucose is too polar to diffuse across the barrier. It needs facilitated transport through GLUT1.","GLUT3 has a high affinity for glucose, which helps neurons take up glucose even at modestly low levels."],"study":{"topics":["Brain energy metabolism","Neuroglycopenia"],"comps":["NE-14"]}},{"id":"NE-C07-Q4","stem":"During the arrest, ATP in his neurons fell within minutes. Which of the following best explains the resulting loss of the neuronal resting membrane potential?","options":["Loss of voltage-gated Na+ channel function","Increased Cl- entry through GABA-A channels","A fall in the extracellular K+ concentration","Pump failure with a rise in extracellular K+"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-13","obj":"NE-13.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the resting potential is set mainly by K+ leak channels and the K+ gradient, which the Na+/K+ ATPase maintains.","Without ATP the pump stops, K+ leaks out and builds up outside cells, and Na+ accumulates inside.","By the Nernst equation, higher extracellular K+ makes the K+ equilibrium potential less negative, so the membrane depolarizes."],"why":"The resting membrane potential, about -70 mV in neurons, is close to the K+ equilibrium potential because K+ leak channels dominate at rest. The Na+/K+ ATPase keeps K+ high inside and low outside. When ATP is lost, the pump fails, K+ accumulates in the extracellular space, and the Nernst potential for K+ shifts toward zero. The neurons depolarize, release glutamate, and enter a cycle of calcium overload and cell death.","whyNot":["Voltage-gated Na+ channels generate action potentials. They contribute little to the resting potential, so their loss would not depolarize the cell.","Chloride entry through GABA-A channels makes the inside more negative and hyperpolarizes the cell.","A fall in extracellular K+ would make the K+ equilibrium potential more negative and hyperpolarize the cell.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Resting membrane potential","Extracellular potassium and excitability"],"comps":["NE-13"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C08","system":"NE","dx":"Anterior spinal artery syndrome after thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm repair","codes":["NE-20-A5"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman undergoes open repair of a thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm that requires prolonged aortic cross-clamping. When she awakens 6 hours later, she cannot move her legs. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg. Physical examination shows flaccid paralysis of both legs with absent patellar and Achilles reflexes. Pain and temperature sensation are absent below the level of the umbilicus, but vibration and joint position sense in the toes are normal. She has urinary retention requiring a catheter. MRI of the spine shows increased T2 signal in the anterior two thirds of the spinal cord from T8 to T11. Diagnosis: anterior spinal artery syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C08-Q1","stem":"Loss of flow from which of the following arteries most likely caused her cord infarct?","options":["Inferior mesenteric artery","Posterior spinal artery","Artery of Adamkiewicz","Intracranial vertebral artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-A5","obj":"NE-20-A.8","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: paralysis and loss of pain and temperature, with vibration and position sense spared. This is the anterior two thirds of the cord.","The anterior spinal artery supplies that region, and in the lower thoracic cord it depends on the artery of Adamkiewicz.","This radicular artery usually arises from a lower intercostal or upper lumbar branch of the aorta, so aortic clamping or repair can cut off its flow."],"why":"The spinal cord has one anterior spinal artery supplying the anterior two thirds and two posterior spinal arteries supplying the dorsal columns. Along most of the thoracic cord, the anterior spinal artery is reinforced by few radicular feeders. The largest, the artery of Adamkiewicz, usually arises from the aorta between about T9 and T12 on the left. Aortic surgery or dissection can interrupt it, infarcting the anterior cord while the dorsal columns are spared.","whyNot":["The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut. Its loss causes colonic ischemia, not cord infarction.","Posterior spinal artery infarction damages the dorsal columns and would remove vibration and position sense, which she has kept.","Correct.","The vertebral arteries form the anterior spinal artery at the medulla and feed the upper cervical cord, not the lower thoracic cord."],"study":{"topics":["Spinal cord blood supply","Anterior spinal artery syndrome"],"comps":["NE-20-A5","NE-10"]}},{"id":"NE-C08-Q2","stem":"Her urinary retention reflects loss of descending control over the pathway that contracts the detrusor muscle during voiding. Which of the following best describes the motor innervation of the detrusor?","options":["One sacral neuron releasing ACh directly on muscarinic receptors","Two neurons, the second releasing norepinephrine on alpha-1 receptors","Two neurons, the second releasing ACh on muscarinic receptors","One anterior horn neuron releasing ACh on nicotinic receptors"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-7","obj":"NE-7.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the detrusor as smooth muscle, so its motor supply is visceral (autonomic), not somatic.","Visceral motor pathways use two neurons: a preganglionic neuron in the CNS and a postganglionic neuron in a ganglion.","Detrusor contraction is parasympathetic: S2 to S4 preganglionic fibers synapse in ganglia near the bladder, and postganglionic fibers release ACh on M3 receptors."],"why":"Somatic motor control uses one neuron: an anterior horn alpha motor neuron releasing acetylcholine on nicotinic receptors of skeletal muscle, such as the external urethral sphincter. Visceral motor control of smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands uses two neurons. For the detrusor, parasympathetic preganglionic neurons at S2 to S4 travel in the pelvic splanchnic nerves to ganglia in or near the bladder wall, and postganglionic neurons release acetylcholine on muscarinic (M3) receptors to contract the bladder.","whyNot":["Visceral motor pathways always use two neurons. No CNS neuron projects directly onto smooth muscle.","Sympathetic postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine on alpha-1 receptors to contract the internal urethral sphincter and help store urine, not to empty the bladder.","Correct.","This is the somatic pathway to skeletal muscle, such as the external urethral sphincter supplied by the pudendal nerve."],"study":{"topics":["Somatic versus visceral motor pathways","Bladder innervation"],"comps":["NE-7","NE-9"]}},{"id":"NE-C08-Q3","stem":"She dies of a pulmonary embolism 10 days after surgery. Which of the following is most likely to be seen in the infarcted segment of the cord?","options":["A fluid-filled cavity walled off by dense gliosis","Dense sheets of neutrophils in the gray matter","Red neurons with no inflammatory cells","Foamy macrophages in softened necrotic tissue"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-A5","obj":"NE-20-A.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the time course of CNS infarction: red neurons at 12 to 24 hours, neutrophils in the first few days, macrophages over the following weeks, then gliosis and a cyst.","At 10 days, liquefactive necrosis is under way and macrophages are clearing lipid-rich myelin debris.","Pick foamy (lipid-laden) macrophages."],"why":"CNS infarcts undergo liquefactive necrosis. Red neurons appear at about 12 to 24 hours, neutrophils infiltrate during the first few days, and by about the end of the first week macrophages (microglia and blood monocytes) become the main cells, filling with lipid from broken-down myelin. Over the following weeks and months, astrocytes form a glial scar around a fluid-filled cyst.","whyNot":["A cavity lined by gliosis is the end stage, seen weeks to months after the infarct.","Neutrophils predominate during roughly the first 1 to 3 days, not at 10 days.","Red neurons without inflammation describe the first day after the infarct.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Time course of CNS infarction","Liquefactive necrosis"],"comps":["NE-20-A","NE-2"]}},{"id":"NE-C08-Q4","stem":"Had she survived, her leg tone would be expected to increase and her patellar reflexes to become brisk over the following weeks. Which of the following best describes the circuit of the patellar reflex?","options":["Golgi tendon organ, Ib afferent, inhibitory interneuron","Muscle spindle, Ia afferent, direct synapse on alpha motor neuron","Muscle spindle, Ia afferent, interneuron, gamma motor neuron","Free nerve ending, A-delta afferent, interneurons, flexor motor neurons"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-5","obj":"NE-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that tapping the tendon stretches the quadriceps and its muscle spindles.","The spindle's Ia afferent synapses directly (one synapse) on alpha motor neurons to the same muscle.","The patellar reflex uses the L4 segment, below her lesion, so the arc is intact and becomes brisk once descending inhibition is lost."],"why":"The stretch (myotatic) reflex is monosynaptic. Stretch of muscle spindles excites Ia afferents, which synapse directly on alpha motor neurons of the same muscle and cause it to contract. The patellar reflex is mainly L4, the biceps C5, the triceps C7, and the Achilles S1. After a thoracic cord lesion, the reflex arcs below the lesion survive, and once spinal shock resolves the loss of descending inhibition makes them hyperactive.","whyNot":["The Golgi tendon organ Ib reflex senses tension and inhibits the same muscle, the opposite of a reflex jerk.","Correct.","Gamma motor neurons set spindle sensitivity but are not the efferent limb of the stretch reflex, and the reflex has no interneuron.","Free nerve endings and A-delta fibers drive the polysynaptic withdrawal reflex to painful stimuli."],"study":{"topics":["Stretch reflex","Reflex root levels"],"comps":["NE-5","NE-7"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C09","system":"NE","dx":"Alzheimer disease","codes":["NE-20-B1"],"case":"A 76-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her son because of a 3-year history of progressive forgetfulness. She repeats the same questions during conversations, misplaces items, and recently got lost while driving in her own neighborhood. Over the past 6 months, she has been unable to manage her bills. She clearly recalls events from her childhood. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows no tremor, rigidity, or gait abnormality. She scores 19 of 30 on the Mini-Mental State Examination (normal 24 or higher). Serum vitamin B12 and thyroid-stimulating hormone levels are within the reference ranges. MRI of the brain shows diffuse cortical atrophy, most marked in the medial temporal lobes, with enlarged lateral ventricles and widened sulci. Diagnosis: Alzheimer disease.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C09-Q1","stem":"Which of the following sequences of enzyme cleavage of amyloid precursor protein produces the main component of the extracellular plaques in her brain?","options":["Alpha secretase, then gamma secretase","Alpha secretase, then beta secretase","Beta secretase, then gamma secretase","Gamma secretase, then alpha secretase"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-B1","obj":"NE-20-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the plaque protein as amyloid beta, a fragment of amyloid precursor protein.","Recall the two processing routes: alpha secretase cuts inside the amyloid beta sequence and prevents its formation; beta secretase does not.","Beta secretase cleavage followed by gamma secretase cleavage releases amyloid beta."],"why":"Amyloid precursor protein is a transmembrane protein encoded on chromosome 21. Normally, alpha secretase cleaves it within the amyloid beta region, which prevents amyloid beta from forming. In the amyloidogenic pathway, beta secretase cuts first, then gamma secretase (which contains presenilin) cuts within the membrane, releasing amyloid beta that aggregates into plaques. Risk rises with age, APOE e4, Down syndrome (an extra APP gene copy), and mutations in APP, PSEN1, or PSEN2.","whyNot":["This is the normal, nonamyloidogenic pathway. Alpha secretase cuts through the amyloid beta sequence.","Alpha secretase cleavage destroys the amyloid beta sequence, and this order does not occur.","Correct.","Gamma secretase acts on the membrane stub left after alpha or beta secretase, not first."],"study":{"topics":["APP processing","Genetic risk in Alzheimer disease"],"comps":["NE-20-B1","NE-16"]}},{"id":"NE-C09-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the enlargement of her lateral ventricles?","options":["Filling of space left by lost brain tissue","Impaired CSF absorption at arachnoid granulations","Obstruction of CSF flow at the cerebral aqueduct","Overproduction of CSF by the choroid plexus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-B1","obj":"NE-20-B.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note that the ventricles are enlarged together with widened sulci and cortical atrophy.","This is hydrocephalus ex vacuo: CSF fills the space left by neuron loss, and pressure is normal.","Her lack of gait problems also argues against normal pressure hydrocephalus."],"why":"Alzheimer disease causes cortical atrophy with narrowed gyri, widened sulci, and compensatory enlargement of the ventricles (hydrocephalus ex vacuo). The atrophy is most marked in the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, matching her early memory loss. Microscopy shows neuritic plaques, neurofibrillary tangles, and often amyloid angiopathy.","whyNot":["Correct.","Impaired absorption at the arachnoid granulations causes communicating hydrocephalus, including normal pressure hydrocephalus with gait disturbance and urinary incontinence.","Aqueductal obstruction causes noncommunicating hydrocephalus with enlarged lateral and third ventricles, raised pressure, and narrowed sulci.","CSF overproduction is rare, caused mainly by a choroid plexus papilloma, and is not linked to neurodegeneration."],"study":{"topics":["Hydrocephalus ex vacuo","Gross and microscopic findings in Alzheimer disease"],"comps":["NE-20-B1","NE-20-C2"]}},{"id":"NE-C09-Q3","stem":"She is started on donepezil, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor. Which of the following enzymes and neuronal populations best match the transmitter deficit this drug addresses?","options":["Tyrosine hydroxylase in the substantia nigra","Tryptophan hydroxylase in the raphe nuclei","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase in the locus coeruleus","Choline acetyltransferase in the nucleus basalis"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-15","obj":"NE-15.5","dok":2,"approach":["An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor raises synaptic acetylcholine, so the deficit is cholinergic.","Acetylcholine is made from acetyl-CoA and choline by choline acetyltransferase.","The main cholinergic projection to the cortex comes from the nucleus basalis of Meynert, which degenerates early in Alzheimer disease."],"why":"Choline acetyltransferase joins acetyl-CoA and choline to form acetylcholine, and acetylcholinesterase breaks it down to acetate and choline in the synaptic cleft. The nucleus basalis of Meynert in the basal forebrain sends cholinergic fibers throughout the cortex, and its neurons are lost early in Alzheimer disease. Blocking acetylcholinesterase prolongs the action of the acetylcholine that remains.","whyNot":["Tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme for dopamine. Loss of dopaminergic nigral neurons causes Parkinson disease.","Tryptophan hydroxylase makes serotonin in the raphe nuclei. Donepezil does not act on serotonin.","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase makes norepinephrine in the locus coeruleus. Donepezil does not act on norepinephrine.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acetylcholine synthesis and breakdown","Nucleus basalis of Meynert"],"comps":["NE-15","NE-20-B1"]}},{"id":"NE-C09-Q4","stem":"Her earliest symptom was an inability to retain new information, while memories from decades ago remained intact. Damage to which of the following structures best explains this pattern?","options":["Anterior cingulate gyrus","Mammillary bodies","Amygdala","Hippocampal formation"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-12","obj":"NE-12.3","dok":3,"approach":["Classify the deficit: trouble forming new memories with old memories preserved is anterograde amnesia.","The hippocampus consolidates new declarative memories, and remote memories are stored in the neocortex.","Her MRI shows medial temporal atrophy, where the hippocampus lies."],"why":"The hippocampus is needed to convert new experiences into long-term declarative memories, but long-stored memories reside in the neocortex. Hippocampal damage therefore causes anterograde amnesia while remote memories are relatively preserved. In Alzheimer disease, neurofibrillary tangles and neuron loss begin in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus, which is why short-term memory loss is the first symptom.","whyNot":["The anterior cingulate gyrus is involved in emotion and motivation. Its damage causes apathy and reduced initiative, not anterograde amnesia.","Mammillary body damage occurs in Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome from thiamine deficiency. It is not the main site of damage in Alzheimer disease, and her MRI points to the medial temporal lobe.","Bilateral amygdala damage causes Kluver-Bucy syndrome with hyperorality, hypersexuality, and loss of fear, not isolated memory loss.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Limbic system and memory"],"comps":["NE-12","NE-20-B1"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C10","system":"NE","dx":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","codes":["NE-20-B2"],"case":"A 54-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 10-month history of progressive weakness. He first noticed he was dropping tools with his right hand, then his left leg became stiff, and over the past 2 months his speech has become slurred and he chokes on liquids. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows atrophy of the intrinsic muscles of the right hand, fasciculations in both arms and the tongue, and tongue atrophy. Muscle tone is increased in both legs. Deep tendon reflexes are brisk in all four limbs, including the atrophic right arm, and plantar responses are extensor bilaterally. Sensation, eye movements, and bladder function are normal. Electromyography shows active denervation with fasciculation potentials in the arms, legs, and tongue; sensory nerve conduction studies are normal. Diagnosis: amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings is most likely in his spinal cord at autopsy?","options":["Demyelinating plaques in the periventricular white matter","Loss of anterior horn cells with lateral column sclerosis","Degeneration of the dorsal columns and dorsal root ganglia","Cavitation of the central gray matter of the cervical cord"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-B2","obj":"NE-20-B.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the mix of lower motor neuron signs (atrophy, fasciculations) and upper motor neuron signs (spasticity, brisk reflexes, Babinski) with normal sensation.","This means degeneration of both anterior horn cells and corticospinal neurons.","In the cord, this appears as loss of anterior horn cells and scarring (sclerosis) of the lateral corticospinal tracts."],"why":"ALS destroys both upper and lower motor neurons. Anterior horn cells and brainstem motor neurons are lost, and degeneration of corticospinal axons leaves the lateral columns pale and gliotic, which gives the disease its name. Most cases are sporadic, and some familial cases carry SOD1 or C9orf72 mutations. Sensation, bladder control, and eye movements are usually spared, and most patients die of respiratory failure within a few years.","whyNot":["Demyelinating plaques in periventricular white matter are the hallmark of multiple sclerosis, which causes sensory and visual symptoms, not fasciculations.","Correct.","Dorsal column and dorsal root ganglion degeneration causes loss of position and vibration sense, as in tabes dorsalis or Friedreich ataxia. His sensation is normal.","Central cord cavitation is a syrinx, which causes a cape-like loss of pain and temperature sensation."],"study":{"topics":["ALS pathology","Motor neuron diseases"],"comps":["NE-20-B2","NE-7"]}},{"id":"NE-C10-Q2","stem":"His tongue atrophy and fasciculations indicate degeneration of lower motor neurons in which of the following nuclei?","options":["Hypoglossal nucleus in the medulla","Oculomotor nucleus in the midbrain","Trigeminal motor nucleus in the pons","Facial motor nucleus in the pons"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-3","obj":"NE-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the nerve to the tongue muscles: the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII).","Place the cranial nerve nuclei by level: III and IV in the midbrain, V to VIII in the pons, IX, X, and XII in the medulla.","Tongue atrophy with fasciculations means loss of hypoglossal motor neurons in the medulla."],"why":"The hypoglossal nucleus lies near the midline in the floor of the fourth ventricle in the medulla and supplies the intrinsic and most extrinsic tongue muscles. Its lower motor neurons degenerate in ALS, producing tongue atrophy and fasciculations. Medullary nuclei for CN IX and X (nucleus ambiguus) are also affected, explaining his dysphagia and dysarthria (bulbar palsy). The oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nuclei are typically spared, so eye movements are preserved.","whyNot":["Correct.","The oculomotor nucleus in the midbrain controls most eye movements, which are spared in ALS.","The trigeminal motor nucleus supplies the muscles of mastication, not the tongue.","The facial motor nucleus supplies muscles of facial expression, not the tongue."],"study":{"topics":["Cranial nerve nuclei by brainstem level"],"comps":["NE-3","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C10-Q3","stem":"His right hand is atrophic with fasciculations, yet the right biceps and triceps reflexes are brisk. Which of the following best explains this combination of findings?","options":["Demyelination of peripheral motor nerves and their roots","Loss of both cortical and spinal motor neurons","Infarction limited to the left precentral gyrus","Compression of the C8 and T1 nerve roots in the neck"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-7","obj":"NE-7.2","dok":3,"approach":["Sort the signs: atrophy and fasciculations are lower motor neuron signs; brisk reflexes are an upper motor neuron sign.","Finding both in the same limb means both motor neuron levels are failing.","Mixed UMN and LMN signs with normal sensation point to ALS."],"why":"Upper motor neuron lesions cause weakness with spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a Babinski sign, with little atrophy. Lower motor neuron lesions cause weakness with atrophy, fasciculations, low tone, and reduced reflexes. ALS damages both levels, so the same limb can be wasted and twitching yet hyperreflexic. Disorders limited to the anterior horn, such as poliomyelitis and spinal muscular atrophy, cause only lower motor neuron signs.","whyNot":["Peripheral demyelination, as in Guillain-Barre syndrome, causes weakness with reduced or absent reflexes.","Correct.","A cortical infarct causes upper motor neuron signs only, without atrophy or fasciculations, and would not explain the bulbar and widespread findings.","Root compression causes lower motor neuron signs and reduced reflexes in that root's distribution, usually with pain or sensory loss."],"study":{"topics":["Upper versus lower motor neuron signs"],"comps":["NE-7","NE-20-B2"]}},{"id":"NE-C10-Q4","stem":"A disorder of infancy also destroys anterior horn motor neurons but spares upper motor neurons. A 3-month-old girl has generalized hypotonia, tongue fasciculations, and absent deep tendon reflexes. Which of the following genetic defects is most likely?","options":["CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene","Dominant gain-of-function mutation in SOD1","Homozygous deletion of SMN1","Dystrophin gene deletion on the X chromosome"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-16","obj":"NE-16.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize a floppy infant with tongue fasciculations and areflexia: a pure lower motor neuron disease of infancy.","This is spinal muscular atrophy, the severe infantile form (type 1, Werdnig-Hoffmann disease).","SMA is autosomal recessive, most often from homozygous deletion of SMN1 (survival motor neuron 1)."],"why":"Spinal muscular atrophy is an autosomal recessive disease caused most often by homozygous deletion of SMN1 on chromosome 5. Without enough SMN protein, anterior horn motor neurons degenerate. The severe infantile form presents in the first months of life with profound hypotonia, areflexia, and tongue fasciculations, and death from respiratory failure is common without treatment. Upper motor neurons are spared, unlike in ALS.","whyNot":["CTG expansion in DMPK causes myotonic dystrophy. The congenital form causes hypotonia, but it is a muscle disease and does not cause tongue fasciculations.","SOD1 mutations cause some familial ALS cases, which present in adults with mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs.","Correct.","Dystrophin deletions cause Duchenne muscular dystrophy, a muscle disease in boys that presents in early childhood with proximal weakness and calf pseudohypertrophy."],"study":{"topics":["Spinal muscular atrophy genetics"],"comps":["NE-16","NE-7"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C11","system":"NE","dx":"Idiopathic Parkinson disease","codes":["NE-20-B3"],"case":"A 66-year-old man comes to the physician because of an 18-month history of shaking of his right hand and slowness in daily tasks. His handwriting has become small, and his wife says his face looks expressionless. He takes no medications. Physical examination shows a 4 to 5 Hz pill-rolling tremor of the right hand that is present at rest and lessens when he reaches for an object. There is cogwheel rigidity of the right arm, slow finger tapping, reduced arm swing on the right, and a stooped, shuffling gait. Strength, sensation, and reflexes are normal. MRI of the brain shows only age-appropriate changes. Diagnosis: idiopathic Parkinson disease.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C11-Q1","stem":"Eight years later, he dies of aspiration pneumonia. Which of the following findings is most likely on examination of his brain at autopsy?","options":["Caudate atrophy with intranuclear huntingtin aggregates","Anterior horn cell loss with TDP-43 cytoplasmic inclusions","Nigral depigmentation with alpha-synuclein inclusions","Frontotemporal atrophy with round tau inclusions in neurons"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-B3","obj":"NE-20-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Link resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia to loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta.","Recall the inclusion that marks these neurons: the Lewy body, made of alpha-synuclein.","Match the other options to Huntington disease, ALS, and frontotemporal dementia."],"why":"Parkinson disease destroys the pigmented dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta, so the midbrain looks pale at autopsy. Surviving neurons contain Lewy bodies, round eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions made mostly of alpha-synuclein. Symptoms appear after most nigral neurons are gone. Secondary parkinsonism looks similar clinically but has a different cause, such as the toxin MPTP, which kills nigral neurons, or dopamine D2 blockers such as haloperidol and metoclopramide.","whyNot":["This is Huntington disease, which causes chorea and dementia, with atrophy of the caudate nucleus.","This is amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, which causes weakness with mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs. His strength is normal.","Correct.","Round tau inclusions (Pick bodies) mark one form of frontotemporal dementia, which begins with behavior or language change instead of a movement disorder."],"study":{"topics":["Parkinson disease pathology","Lewy bodies and alpha-synuclein"],"comps":["NE-20-B3","NE-3"]}},{"id":"NE-C11-Q2","stem":"Which of the following changes in basal ganglia circuitry most directly explains his bradykinesia?","options":["Decreased activity of indirect pathway striatal neurons bearing D2 receptors","Increased inhibitory output from the internal globus pallidus to the thalamus","Increased activity of direct pathway striatal neurons bearing D1 receptors","Decreased activity of the subthalamic nucleus driving the internal globus pallidus"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-3","obj":"NE-3.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that nigral dopamine excites the direct pathway through D1 receptors and inhibits the indirect pathway through D2 receptors.","With dopamine lost, the direct pathway falls and the indirect pathway rises; both changes increase internal pallidal output.","More inhibitory pallidal output to the thalamus means less excitation of motor cortex, so movement is slow and small."],"why":"The internal globus pallidus (GPi) tonically inhibits the motor thalamus with GABA. The direct pathway (striatum to GPi) suppresses the GPi and releases movement, and dopamine strengthens it through D1 receptors. The indirect pathway (striatum to external pallidus to subthalamic nucleus to GPi) increases GPi output, and dopamine weakens it through D2 receptors. When nigral dopamine is lost, both effects push GPi output up, the thalamus is held down, and the cortex gets too little drive to start and scale movement.","whyNot":["Indirect pathway neurons lose their D2 inhibition, so their activity rises. Reduced indirect pathway activity is what happens in early Huntington disease and causes chorea.","Correct.","Direct pathway neurons lose their D1 excitation, so their activity falls. Increased direct pathway activity would produce excess movement.","Loss of dopamine releases the indirect pathway, so the subthalamic nucleus becomes overactive. That is why deep brain stimulation of this nucleus can help."],"study":{"topics":["Direct and indirect basal ganglia pathways","Dopamine D1 and D2 receptors"],"comps":["NE-3","NE-20-B3"]}},{"id":"NE-C11-Q3","stem":"The neurons lost in his disease share their neurotransmitter with a second group of midbrain neurons that projects to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex. In which of the following nuclei are those neurons located?","options":["Ventral tegmental area","Locus coeruleus","Nucleus basalis of Meynert","Dorsal raphe nucleus"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-15","obj":"NE-15.6","dok":2,"approach":["Name the transmitter of the neurons lost in Parkinson disease: dopamine.","Recall the two main dopamine source nuclei in the midbrain: substantia nigra pars compacta and the ventral tegmental area.","The ventral tegmental area supplies the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways."],"why":"Dopamine neurons sit in two neighboring midbrain groups. The substantia nigra pars compacta sends the nigrostriatal pathway to the caudate and putamen, and its loss causes the motor signs of Parkinson disease. The ventral tegmental area sends the mesolimbic pathway to the nucleus accumbens and the mesocortical pathway to prefrontal cortex, which handle reward and motivation. A third dopamine group, in the hypothalamic arcuate nucleus, inhibits prolactin release.","whyNot":["Correct.","The locus coeruleus in the pons is the main source of norepinephrine in the brain.","This basal forebrain nucleus is the main source of cortical acetylcholine. It degenerates in Alzheimer disease.","The raphe nuclei of the brainstem are the main source of serotonin."],"study":{"topics":["Monoamine source nuclei","Mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways"],"comps":["NE-15","NE-3"]}},{"id":"NE-C11-Q4","stem":"His 59-year-old sister has a 10-year history of tremor of both hands that makes it hard to hold a cup of coffee. The tremor lessens after a glass of wine. Their late mother had a similar tremor. Examination shows a symmetric 7 Hz tremor of the outstretched hands that is absent with the arms at rest and does not increase as her finger nears the target on finger-to-nose testing. Tone, finger tapping speed, and gait are normal. Serum TSH is normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in the sister?","options":["Cerebellar intention tremor","Essential tremor","Parkinsonian resting tremor","Enhanced physiologic tremor"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-B","obj":"NE-20-B.13","dok":3,"approach":["Classify the tremor by when it appears: with posture and action, absent at rest.","Check for features of other tremors: no increase near the target (cerebellar), no rigidity or bradykinesia (parkinsonian).","A long-standing, familial, alcohol-responsive action tremor is essential tremor."],"why":"Essential tremor is the most common movement disorder in adults. It is a postural and action tremor of both hands that often runs in families in an autosomal dominant pattern and often improves briefly with alcohol. It may also involve the head or voice. Parkinsonian tremor occurs at rest and comes with rigidity and bradykinesia, as in her brother. Cerebellar tremor gets worse as the hand approaches a target.","whyNot":["Cerebellar intention tremor grows as the finger approaches the target and comes with ataxia and dysmetria. Her tremor does not change near the target.","Correct.","Parkinsonian tremor is present at rest and lessens with action. Her tremor is absent at rest, and tone and finger tapping are normal.","Enhanced physiologic tremor is a fine postural tremor driven by adrenergic excess, such as hyperthyroidism, caffeine, or anxiety. A 10-year familial course with a normal TSH fits essential tremor better."],"study":{"topics":["Types of tremor","Essential tremor"],"comps":["NE-20-B","NE-20-B3"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C12","system":"NE","dx":"Huntington disease","codes":["NE-20-B3"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her husband because of a 2-year history of irritability, low mood, forgetfulness, and restless, fidgety movements. She has lost her job as a bookkeeper because of errors. Her father developed similar symptoms at the age of 48 years and died at the age of 60 years. Physical examination shows brief, irregular, flowing movements of the face, trunk, and fingers. She cannot keep her tongue protruded or maintain a steady hand grip. Saccadic eye movements are slow. MRI of the brain shows atrophy of the caudate nuclei with enlarged frontal horns of the lateral ventricles. Genetic testing shows 44 CAG repeats in one HTT allele (normal 26 or fewer). Diagnosis: Huntington disease.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C12-Q1","stem":"Which of the following neuronal populations is most severely lost in the atrophic structures seen on her MRI?","options":["Dopaminergic neurons of the pars compacta","Glutamatergic neurons of the subthalamic nucleus","Cholinergic neurons of the basal nucleus of Meynert","GABAergic medium spiny neurons of the striatum"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-B3","obj":"NE-20-B.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the atrophic structure: the caudate nucleus, part of the striatum.","Recall that most striatal neurons are GABAergic medium spiny neurons.","Link early loss of indirect pathway medium spiny neurons to chorea."],"why":"Huntington disease is an autosomal dominant CAG repeat expansion in HTT on chromosome 4p. Mutant huntingtin injures striatal GABAergic medium spiny neurons first, so the caudate and then the putamen atrophy and the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles look enlarged. Indirect pathway neurons are lost early, which releases the thalamus and produces chorea. Cortical neuron loss adds dementia, depression, and personality change, usually starting at the age of 30 to 50 years.","whyNot":["These neurons are lost in Parkinson disease, which causes resting tremor and bradykinesia instead of chorea.","A lesion of the subthalamic nucleus, usually a small stroke, causes hemiballismus on the opposite side. It is not the structure that atrophies in this disease.","These cholinergic neurons are lost in Alzheimer disease. They lie in the basal forebrain, not in the caudate.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Huntington disease pathology","Striatal medium spiny neurons"],"comps":["NE-20-B3","NE-3"]}},{"id":"NE-C12-Q2","stem":"Her father's symptoms began at the age of 48 years, and hers began at the age of 39 years. Which of the following best explains her earlier onset?","options":["Paternal meiotic expansion of the CAG repeat","Somatic mosaicism from a postzygotic mutation","Maternal imprinting of the normal HTT allele","Mitochondrial heteroplasmy in striatal neurons"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-16","obj":"NE-16.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize anticipation: earlier onset and more severe disease in each generation.","Recall that anticipation in trinucleotide repeat disorders comes from further expansion of the repeat when it is passed on.","In Huntington disease the CAG repeat is most unstable in spermatogenesis, so expansion is most likely when the father passes it on."],"why":"Huntington disease is one of the trinucleotide repeat disorders. A longer CAG repeat gives earlier onset, and the repeat tends to lengthen when transmitted, most of all through the father's sperm. This produces anticipation. Myotonic dystrophy (CTG in DMPK, autosomal dominant) and fragile X syndrome (CGG in FMR1, X-linked) also show anticipation, but there expansion happens mainly in maternal transmission. Friedreich ataxia (GAA in the frataxin gene) is autosomal recessive.","whyNot":["Correct.","Postzygotic mosaicism explains patchy disease in one person, such as McCune-Albright syndrome. It does not explain earlier onset across generations.","Imprinting silences one parent's allele and underlies Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes. HTT is not imprinted, and the disease is caused by a toxic gain of function from the mutant allele.","Heteroplasmy explains variable severity in mitochondrial DNA disorders, which pass only from the mother. Her disease came from her father and the gene is nuclear."],"study":{"topics":["Anticipation","Trinucleotide repeat disorders"],"comps":["NE-16","NE-20-B3"]}},{"id":"NE-C12-Q3","stem":"The neurons lost in her striatum make their main neurotransmitter from glutamate in a single enzymatic step. Which of the following cofactors is required for this reaction?","options":["Pyridoxal phosphate","Tetrahydrobiopterin","Thiamine pyrophosphate","S-adenosylmethionine"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-15","obj":"NE-15.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the transmitter of striatal medium spiny neurons: GABA.","Recall that GABA is made by decarboxylation of glutamate by glutamate decarboxylase.","Amino acid decarboxylases use pyridoxal phosphate, the active form of vitamin B6."],"why":"Glutamate decarboxylase removes the carboxyl group of glutamate to form GABA, and it needs pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6). Severe B6 deficiency, as from isoniazid without supplementation, lowers GABA and can cause seizures. Glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter and acts on NMDA and AMPA receptors, while GABA and glycine are inhibitory. Too much glutamate drives calcium into neurons through NMDA receptors and kills them, which is excitotoxicity, a mechanism in ischemic neuronal death and thought to contribute to striatal loss in Huntington disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tetrahydrobiopterin is the cofactor for phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan hydroxylases, which start catecholamine and serotonin synthesis.","Thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and transketolase.","S-adenosylmethionine donates methyl groups, as in the conversion of norepinephrine to epinephrine. It is not used to make GABA."],"study":{"topics":["GABA synthesis","Glutamate excitotoxicity"],"comps":["NE-15","NE-13"]}},{"id":"NE-C12-Q4","stem":"The structure that is atrophic on her MRI forms the lateral wall of the frontal horn of the lateral ventricle. From which of the following embryonic brain vesicles does this structure develop?","options":["Mesencephalon","Diencephalon","Metencephalon","Telencephalon"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-1","obj":"NE-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the structure: the head of the caudate nucleus.","Recall that the basal nuclei, cerebral hemispheres, and lateral ventricles all come from the telencephalon.","Exclude vesicles that form the thalamus, midbrain, and pons."],"why":"The prosencephalon divides into the telencephalon and diencephalon. The telencephalon forms the cerebral cortex, the basal nuclei including the caudate and putamen, and the lateral ventricles, so the caudate grows around the wall of the lateral ventricle. The diencephalon forms the thalamus, hypothalamus, and third ventricle. The mesencephalon forms the midbrain and cerebral aqueduct, the metencephalon forms the pons and cerebellum, and the myelencephalon forms the medulla, with the fourth ventricle spanning the last two.","whyNot":["The mesencephalon forms the midbrain, including the substantia nigra, and the cerebral aqueduct.","The diencephalon forms the thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus, and third ventricle. The thalamus lies medial to the internal capsule, not at the frontal horn.","The metencephalon forms the pons and cerebellum and the upper part of the fourth ventricle.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Primary and secondary brain vesicles","Basal nuclei anatomy"],"comps":["NE-1","NE-3"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C13","system":"NE","dx":"Guillain-Barre syndrome after Campylobacter jejuni enteritis","codes":["NE-20-B4"],"case":"A 34-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of 4 days of tingling in his feet and progressive weakness that began in his legs and now involves his arms. Three weeks ago he had 4 days of bloody diarrhea, and a stool culture grew Campylobacter jejuni. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 158/94 mm Hg; over the next hour, blood pressure falls to 102/60 mm Hg. Strength is 3/5 in the legs and 4/5 in the arms, symmetric. Deep tendon reflexes are absent. Forced vital capacity is 2.6 L (predicted 4.6 L). Cerebrospinal fluid shows a protein concentration of 160 mg/dL (15 to 45) and 2 leukocytes/µL (0 to 5). Nerve conduction studies show slowed conduction and conduction block. Diagnosis: Guillain-Barre syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C13-Q1","stem":"Over the next 24 hours, his forced vital capacity falls to 1.2 L, and he can no longer count to 10 in one breath. Which of the following best explains this change?","options":["Antibody blockade of diaphragmatic acetylcholine receptors","Degeneration of cervical anterior horn motor neurons","Demyelination of the phrenic and intercostal nerves","Demyelination of the medullary respiratory centers"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-B4","obj":"NE-20-B.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize that Guillain-Barre syndrome attacks peripheral nerve myelin, including nerve roots and proximal nerves.","Apply this to breathing: the diaphragm and intercostal muscles are driven by the phrenic and intercostal nerves.","A falling vital capacity means neuromuscular respiratory failure, the main cause of death in this disease."],"why":"Guillain-Barre syndrome is an acute inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy. It causes ascending, symmetric, flaccid weakness with lost reflexes. When the phrenic nerves (C3 to C5) and intercostal nerves are involved, the respiratory muscles weaken and vital capacity falls, so serial vital capacity is followed and patients are intubated before they tire. Involvement of autonomic fibers causes swings in blood pressure and heart rate, as in this patient, and arrhythmias.","whyNot":["Antibodies against acetylcholine receptors cause myasthenia gravis, which gives fatigable weakness with normal reflexes and normal CSF.","Loss of anterior horn cells occurs in ALS and poliomyelitis. It does not produce high CSF protein with slowed nerve conduction.","Correct.","Guillain-Barre syndrome affects the peripheral nervous system. Central demyelination of the brainstem occurs in multiple sclerosis and osmotic demyelination, not here."],"study":{"topics":["Guillain-Barre syndrome complications","Neuromuscular respiratory failure"],"comps":["NE-20-B4","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C13-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains how his recent infection led to his neurologic illness?","options":["Superantigen activation of T cells that attack central myelin","Direct bacterial invasion of dorsal root ganglia","Exotoxin carried retrogradely up motor axons","Antibodies to bacterial glycans that bind gangliosides"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-18","obj":"NE-18.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: weakness began weeks after the diarrhea cleared, which suggests an immune response, not ongoing infection.","Recall that Campylobacter jejuni lipooligosaccharide resembles gangliosides on peripheral nerve.","Antibodies made against the bacterium cross-react with the nerve, which is molecular mimicry."],"why":"Campylobacter jejuni is the infection most often linked to Guillain-Barre syndrome. Its lipooligosaccharide looks like human gangliosides such as GM1 and GD1a. Antibodies raised against the bacterium bind these gangliosides on peripheral nerve myelin or axons, fix complement, and recruit macrophages that strip myelin. The Miller Fisher variant, with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia, is tied to anti-GQ1b antibodies, because GQ1b is rich in the nerves to the eye muscles.","whyNot":["Superantigens from Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes cause toxic shock. This disease attacks peripheral myelin, not central myelin.","The infection was confined to the gut and had cleared. The CSF has almost no leukocytes, which argues against active infection near the nerve roots.","Retrograde axonal transport of a toxin describes tetanus toxin, which blocks inhibitory interneurons and causes spastic paralysis, not flaccid weakness.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular mimicry","Anti-ganglioside antibodies"],"comps":["NE-18","NE-20-B4"]}},{"id":"NE-C13-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why loss of myelin slows conduction along his motor nerves?","options":["Reduced axon diameter from loss of neurofilaments","Prolonged refractory period of nodal sodium channels","Current leak and higher capacitance along the internodes","Hyperpolarization of the axon resting membrane potential"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-13","obj":"NE-13.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that myelin raises membrane resistance and lowers capacitance between nodes of Ranvier.","This lets current spread quickly to the next node, which is saltatory conduction.","Without myelin, current leaks out and charges more membrane, so the next node reaches threshold late or not at all."],"why":"In a myelinated axon, sodium channels cluster at the nodes of Ranvier, and the insulated internode carries current passively and quickly to the next node. Demyelination lowers membrane resistance and raises capacitance, so current leaks away and more charge is needed to change the voltage. Conduction slows, and if too little current reaches the next node, conduction fails, which is conduction block. The bare internode has few sodium channels, so it cannot generate its own action potential well. Larger diameter axons conduct faster because their internal resistance is lower.","whyNot":["Axon diameter does affect speed, but demyelination does not shrink the axon. The defect here is in the myelin sheath.","The refractory period limits how fast an axon can fire repeatedly. It is not the reason a single impulse travels slowly after demyelination.","Correct.","Demyelination does not change the resting membrane potential. Hyperpolarization would reduce excitability by a different mechanism, as with low extracellular potassium."],"study":{"topics":["Saltatory conduction","Cable properties of axons"],"comps":["NE-13","NE-2"]}},{"id":"NE-C13-Q4","stem":"Electrodiagnostic testing 3 weeks later shows that some motor axons in his legs were also severed by the inflammation, with Wallerian degeneration distal to the injury. Which of the following best explains why these axons can regrow while severed axons in the spinal cord generally cannot?","options":["Oligodendrocytes clear myelin debris and guide new sprouts","Schwann cells form guiding tubes and secrete growth factors","Mature motor neurons divide to replace the injured neurons","Astrocytes form a permissive scar bridging the damaged segment"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-2","obj":"NE-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall Wallerian degeneration: the axon and myelin distal to the injury break down and are cleared by macrophages.","In peripheral nerve, Schwann cells survive inside the endoneurial tube and make growth factors and adhesion molecules.","In the CNS, oligodendrocyte myelin inhibitors and the astrocytic glial scar block regrowth."],"why":"After an axon is cut, the distal segment and its myelin degenerate (Wallerian degeneration), and the cell body swells with a displaced nucleus and dispersed Nissl substance (central chromatolysis). In peripheral nerve, Schwann cells multiply within the intact basal lamina tube, secrete nerve growth factors, and guide sprouts from the proximal stump at about 1 to 3 mm per day. In the CNS, macrophages clear debris slowly, oligodendrocyte myelin proteins such as Nogo inhibit growth, and astrocytes form a glial scar.","whyNot":["Oligodendrocytes are CNS cells. Their myelin contains proteins that inhibit axon growth, which is one reason CNS axons fail to regenerate.","Correct.","Neurons are postmitotic and do not divide. Recovery depends on regrowth of the axon from the surviving cell body.","Astrocytes are CNS cells, and their scar blocks regrowth. Peripheral nerves contain no astrocytes."],"study":{"topics":["Wallerian degeneration","Peripheral nerve regeneration"],"comps":["NE-2","NE-20-B4"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C14","system":"NE","dx":"Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis with trigeminal neuralgia","codes":["NE-20-B5","NE-20-I7"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 6 weeks of brief, electric shock-like pains in her left cheek and jaw that last a few seconds and are set off by brushing her teeth or chewing. She also has double vision when she looks to the right. Two years ago she had blurred, painful vision in her right eye that resolved over 6 weeks. Her symptoms feel worse after a hot shower. On rightward gaze, the left eye does not adduct, and the right eye shows nystagmus as it abducts. Convergence is normal. Facial sensation is normal between attacks. MRI shows multiple ovoid T2-hyperintense lesions oriented perpendicular to the lateral ventricles and a lesion in the left pons. Cerebrospinal fluid shows oligoclonal IgG bands absent from serum. Diagnosis: relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis with trigeminal neuralgia.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C14-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her symptoms worsen after a hot shower?","options":["Heat dilates cerebral vessels and raises intracranial pressure","Heat worsens conduction block in demyelinated axons","Heat triggers a new inflammatory plaque within hours","Heat lowers acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-B5","obj":"NE-20-B.8","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the Uhthoff phenomenon: brief worsening of old symptoms with a rise in body temperature.","Recall that demyelinated axons conduct with very little safety margin.","Higher temperature shortens sodium channel opening, so less current reaches the next node and conduction fails."],"why":"Multiple sclerosis most often affects women aged 20 to 40 years and is more common at higher latitudes. Its typical course is relapsing-remitting, with attacks such as optic neuritis, internuclear ophthalmoplegia, sensory loss, and weakness that improve over weeks. Heat speeds sodium channel inactivation, which reduces the current available at each node. A demyelinated axon already barely reaches threshold, so a small rise in temperature can block conduction. The worsening reverses with cooling and does not mean new disease activity.","whyNot":["Intracranial pressure is not raised in multiple sclerosis, and raised pressure would not reproduce her old focal symptoms.","Correct.","Heat-related worsening comes on within minutes and reverses with cooling. A true relapse from a new plaque lasts at least 24 hours and is not triggered by a shower.","Heat-sensitive neuromuscular weakness is not a feature of this disease. Multiple sclerosis affects central myelin, not the neuromuscular junction."],"study":{"topics":["Uhthoff phenomenon","Clinical features of multiple sclerosis"],"comps":["NE-20-B5","NE-13"]}},{"id":"NE-C14-Q2","stem":"Which of the following immune effectors is most directly responsible for initiating the lesions seen on her MRI?","options":["Antibodies against astrocyte aquaporin-4 channels","Antibodies against peripheral nerve gangliosides","CD4 Th1 and Th17 cells reactive to CNS myelin","Th2 cells driving IgE-mediated mast cell injury"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-18","obj":"NE-18.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesions as periventricular demyelinating plaques in the CNS.","Recall that multiple sclerosis is driven by autoreactive CD4 T cells against myelin proteins.","Th1 cells make interferon-gamma and Th17 cells make IL-17, which recruit macrophages that strip myelin."],"why":"In multiple sclerosis, CD4 T cells reactive to myelin antigens such as myelin basic protein cross the blood-brain barrier. Th1 cells secrete interferon-gamma and activate macrophages and microglia, and Th17 cells secrete IL-17 and recruit neutrophils and other inflammatory cells. Together they destroy myelin and oligodendrocytes around venules, which is why plaques line up perpendicular to the ventricles. B cells inside the CNS make the oligoclonal IgG bands. The strongest genetic risk is HLA-DR2 (DRB1*15:01).","whyNot":["Antibodies to aquaporin-4 cause neuromyelitis optica, with severe optic neuritis and long spinal cord lesions. That is a separate disease from multiple sclerosis.","Anti-ganglioside antibodies attack peripheral nerve in Guillain-Barre syndrome. Her lesions are in the brain.","Correct.","Th2 and IgE responses drive allergy and defense against parasites. They do not cause demyelinating plaques."],"study":{"topics":["Immunopathogenesis of multiple sclerosis","Th1 and Th17 cells"],"comps":["NE-18","NE-20-B5"]}},{"id":"NE-C14-Q3","stem":"Damage to which of the following structures best explains her abnormal eye movements?","options":["Left medial longitudinal fasciculus","Left oculomotor nerve","Right abducens nucleus","Right paramedian pontine reticular formation"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-8","obj":"NE-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: the left eye fails to adduct on right gaze, but convergence is intact.","Intact convergence shows the left medial rectus and its nerve work, so the defect is in the pathway that links the two eyes during lateral gaze.","This link is the medial longitudinal fasciculus, which runs from the right abducens nucleus to the left oculomotor nucleus."],"why":"For rightward gaze, the right paramedian pontine reticular formation activates the right abducens nucleus. Its motor neurons move the right lateral rectus, and its interneurons cross and ascend in the left medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) to the left oculomotor nucleus for the left medial rectus. A lesion of the left MLF blocks only this signal, so the left eye fails to adduct, and the right eye shows nystagmus as it abducts. Convergence uses a separate pathway, so it is preserved. This internuclear ophthalmoplegia is classic for multiple sclerosis in a young adult, and bilateral INO strongly suggests it.","whyNot":["Correct.","A left oculomotor nerve lesion would also impair convergence and would cause ptosis, a dilated pupil, and a down-and-out eye.","A right abducens nucleus lesion causes a right horizontal gaze palsy, with neither eye able to look to the right.","A right paramedian pontine reticular formation lesion prevents both eyes from moving to the right."],"study":{"topics":["Internuclear ophthalmoplegia","Horizontal gaze pathway"],"comps":["NE-8","NE-20-B5"]}},{"id":"NE-C14-Q4","stem":"Her facial pain is attributed to a demyelinating plaque at the trigeminal root entry zone in the pons. In a 68-year-old man without multiple sclerosis who has identical attacks, which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Reactivated varicella-zoster virus in the trigeminal ganglion","Tumor at the cerebellopontine angle","Arthritis of the temporomandibular joint","Arterial loop compressing the trigeminal root"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-I7","obj":"NE-20-I.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize trigeminal neuralgia: seconds-long, one-sided, stabbing pain in V2 or V3, triggered by touch or chewing, with normal sensation between attacks.","In older adults it is usually caused by a blood vessel pressing on the nerve root near the pons.","In a young adult, the same syndrome raises concern for multiple sclerosis, as in this patient."],"why":"Classic trigeminal neuralgia comes from compression of the trigeminal root near where it enters the pons, most often by a loop of the superior cerebellar artery. The pressure causes focal demyelination, and impulses jump between neighboring fibers, so light touch triggers bursts of pain. A plaque at the same site causes the same pain in multiple sclerosis. First-line drug treatment is carbamazepine, and decompression surgery can move the vessel off the nerve.","whyNot":["Herpes zoster in the trigeminal territory causes a vesicular rash, most often in V1, and steady burning pain afterward. It does not cause brief triggered shocks with normal skin.","A cerebellopontine angle tumor can cause facial pain, but it is a less common cause and usually brings sensory loss, hearing loss, or other cranial nerve signs.","Temporomandibular joint disease causes aching jaw pain with chewing and joint tenderness or clicking. It does not cause electric shock-like pain triggered by light touch.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Trigeminal neuralgia","Trigeminal nerve divisions"],"comps":["NE-20-I7","NE-8"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C15","system":"NE","dx":"Meniere disease (idiopathic endolymphatic hydrops)","codes":["NE-20-J8","NE-20-J10"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the physician because of recurrent episodes of spinning vertigo over the past 8 months. Each episode lasts 2 to 4 hours and comes with nausea, vomiting, a roaring sound, and a feeling of fullness in the left ear. Hearing in the left ear seems muffled during episodes and partly recovers between them. He feels well between episodes and has had no recent infections. Examination between episodes shows normal tympanic membranes and no nystagmus. Audiometry shows sensorineural hearing loss in the left ear, worst at low frequencies. MRI of the internal auditory canals is normal. Diagnosis: Meniere disease.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C15-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely underlying abnormality in his left inner ear?","options":["Overproduction of perilymph by the cochlear aqueduct","Loss of outer hair cells at the cochlear base","Poor endolymph resorption by the endolymphatic sac","Perilymph leakage through a round window fistula"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-20-J8","obj":"NE-20-J.8","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the two inner ear fluids: potassium-rich endolymph in the membranous labyrinth and sodium-rich perilymph around it.","Endolymph is made by the stria vascularis and absorbed by the endolymphatic sac.","Meniere disease is endolymphatic hydrops: too much endolymph, thought to come mainly from poor resorption, distends the cochlear duct and vestibular organs."],"why":"In Meniere disease, the volume of endolymph rises and distends the membranous labyrinth. Distension of the cochlear duct disturbs hair cell function at the low-frequency apex first, giving fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss, tinnitus, and fullness. Episodes of distension, and possibly small ruptures that let potassium-rich endolymph mix with perilymph, are thought to disturb the vestibular hair cells and cause vertigo lasting hours. With repeated attacks, hearing loss can become permanent.","whyNot":["Perilymph communicates with the subarachnoid space through the cochlear aqueduct. Excess perilymph is not the mechanism of this disease.","Loss of basal outer hair cells causes high-frequency loss, as in noise exposure and presbycusis, without episodic vertigo.","Correct.","A perilymph fistula usually follows barotrauma or straining and causes sudden hearing loss with vertigo triggered by pressure changes."],"study":{"topics":["Endolymphatic hydrops","Endolymph and perilymph"],"comps":["NE-20-J8","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C15-Q2","stem":"Which of the following tuning fork results is most likely in this patient?","options":["Weber lateralizes to the left ear; air conduction exceeds bone conduction in both ears","Weber lateralizes to the right ear; air conduction exceeds bone conduction in both ears","Weber is midline; bone conduction exceeds air conduction in both ears","Weber lateralizes to the left ear; bone conduction exceeds air conduction in the left ear"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-6","obj":"NE-6.5","dok":3,"approach":["Classify the loss: sensorineural, in the left ear.","In sensorineural loss, the Weber sound lateralizes to the better ear, here the right.","In sensorineural loss, the Rinne test stays normal, with air conduction better than bone conduction."],"why":"Sound normally travels through the tympanic membrane and ossicles to the oval window, which is why air conduction is louder than bone conduction (a positive Rinne test). In conductive loss, bone conduction exceeds air conduction in the affected ear, and the Weber tone lateralizes to that ear. In sensorineural loss, the affected cochlea or nerve responds poorly to both routes, so the Rinne test stays normal and the Weber tone lateralizes to the healthy ear. Hair cells in the base of the cochlea respond to high frequencies and those at the apex to low frequencies, so his low-frequency loss points to the apex.","whyNot":["Weber lateralizes to the affected ear in conductive loss, not in sensorineural loss.","Correct.","Bilateral negative Rinne tests with a midline Weber suggest symmetric conductive loss. His loss is one-sided and sensorineural.","This is left conductive hearing loss, as from cerumen, otitis media, or otosclerosis. His loss is sensorineural with normal tympanic membranes."],"study":{"topics":["Weber and Rinne tests","Conductive versus sensorineural hearing loss"],"comps":["NE-6","NE-20-J8"]}},{"id":"NE-C15-Q3","stem":"A 44-year-old woman comes to the same clinic because of 3 days of continuous, severe vertigo with nausea that began 1 week after an upper respiratory infection. The vertigo is worse with head movement but never stops. She has horizontal nystagmus beating to the right. Audiometry is normal, and there are no other neurologic findings. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Vestibular neuritis","Meniere disease","Viral labyrinthitis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-J10","obj":"NE-20-J.11","dok":3,"approach":["Sort peripheral vertigo by duration: seconds (positional vertigo), hours (Meniere disease), or days (neuritis or labyrinthitis).","Continuous vertigo for days after a viral illness points to inflammation of the vestibular nerve or labyrinth.","Normal hearing places the problem in the vestibular nerve alone."],"why":"Vestibular neuritis is inflammation of the vestibular nerve, thought to be viral, possibly from reactivated herpes simplex virus type 1. It causes sudden continuous vertigo lasting days, nausea, and horizontal nystagmus beating away from the affected side, with normal hearing. Labyrinthitis involves the inner ear itself, so hearing loss and tinnitus come with the vertigo. Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo comes from displaced otoconia, usually in the posterior semicircular canal, and causes brief vertigo with head position changes and a positive Dix-Hallpike test.","whyNot":["Positional vertigo lasts less than a minute with each change in head position. Her vertigo is continuous for days.","Correct.","Meniere disease causes recurrent episodes lasting hours with fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus, and fullness.","Labyrinthitis affects the cochlea as well as the vestibular organs, so it causes hearing loss. Her audiometry is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Peripheral causes of vertigo","Vestibular neuritis"],"comps":["NE-20-J10","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C15-Q4","stem":"His vertigo becomes disabling, and section of the left vestibular nerve is planned. The surgeon must protect a second cranial nerve that passes through the same bony canal. Injury to that nerve would most likely cause which of the following?","options":["Weakness of the whole ipsilateral face","Tongue deviation toward the injured side","Weak jaw closure on the injured side","Loss of the gag reflex with hoarseness"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-8","obj":"NE-8.1","dok":3,"approach":["The vestibular nerve, part of CN VIII, leaves the posterior cranial fossa through the internal acoustic meatus.","The facial nerve (CN VII) travels with it through the internal acoustic meatus.","Injury there causes lower motor neuron weakness of the entire face on that side."],"why":"The internal acoustic meatus carries the facial nerve (CN VII) and the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII), along with the labyrinthine artery. Damage to the facial nerve at this level paralyzes all the muscles of facial expression on that side, including the forehead, and can also reduce taste, tearing, and the stapedius reflex. A central lesion above the facial nucleus spares the forehead because the upper face receives cortical input from both hemispheres.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tongue deviation toward the lesion comes from CN XII injury. That nerve leaves through the hypoglossal canal.","Weak jaw closure comes from injury to the motor root of CN V3, which leaves through the foramen ovale.","Loss of the gag reflex with hoarseness points to CN IX and CN X, which leave through the jugular foramen."],"study":{"topics":["Cranial nerve foramina","Facial nerve lesions"],"comps":["NE-8","NE-6"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C16","system":"NE","dx":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus","codes":["NE-20-C2"],"case":"A 74-year-old man is brought to the physician by his daughter because of a 1-year history of difficulty walking and a 6-month history of urinary urgency with incontinence. She says he has become slow in his thinking and less interested in his hobbies. Physical examination shows a wide-based gait with short steps in which his feet seem stuck to the floor. There is no tremor, and tone in the arms is normal. Mini-Mental State Examination score is 23/30. MRI of the brain shows enlarged lateral and third ventricles out of proportion to sulcal enlargement, with no obstructing mass. Lumbar puncture shows an opening pressure of 15 cm H2O (normal about 10 to 20). His gait improves after removal of 40 mL of cerebrospinal fluid. Diagnosis: normal pressure hydrocephalus.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C16-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his gait disturbance and urinary incontinence?","options":["Loss of nigrostriatal dopamine neurons in the midbrain","Degeneration of sacral parasympathetic preganglionic neurons","Compression of the cerebellar vermis by the fourth ventricle","Stretched periventricular fibers from medial frontal cortex"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-20-C2","obj":"NE-20-C.3","dok":3,"approach":["Link his triad (gait disturbance, incontinence, cognitive decline) to enlarged lateral ventricles.","Recall which fibers lie next to the lateral ventricles: descending fibers from the medial frontal cortex that control the legs and bladder.","The enlarged ventricles stretch these fibers, so leg control and bladder inhibition suffer first."],"why":"In normal pressure hydrocephalus, CSF absorption is impaired, the ventricles enlarge, and the opening pressure is usually normal or only intermittently high. The enlarged lateral ventricles stretch nearby white matter of the corona radiata. Fibers from the medial frontal cortex and paracentral lobule, which control the legs and inhibit the bladder, run closest to the ventricle walls, so a magnetic gait and urge incontinence come early, followed by frontal slowing of thought. Symptoms that improve after a large-volume CSF tap suggest a shunt may help.","whyNot":["Nigral loss causes Parkinson disease, with resting tremor and rigidity. His tone is normal and he has no tremor.","Sacral parasympathetic damage, as in cauda equina syndrome, causes urinary retention with overflow and saddle anesthesia, not urge incontinence with a magnetic gait.","His MRI shows enlarged lateral and third ventricles. Vermis compression would cause truncal ataxia and would not explain incontinence.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Normal pressure hydrocephalus","Periventricular white matter"],"comps":["NE-20-C2","NE-4"]}},{"id":"NE-C16-Q2","stem":"For the CSF removal, a needle is inserted in the midline between the spinous processes at the level of a line joining the highest points of the iliac crests. When CSF flows from the needle, its tip lies within which of the following spaces?","options":["Subdural space between dura and arachnoid","Central canal of the conus medullaris","Lumbar cistern containing the cauda equina","Epidural space containing the vertebral venous plexus"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-4","obj":"NE-4.3","dok":3,"approach":["The line joining the iliac crests crosses at about the L4 spinous process.","In adults the spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near L1 to L2, so the needle at L3 to L5 enters below the cord.","Below the conus, the subarachnoid space widens into the lumbar cistern, filled with CSF and nerve roots of the cauda equina."],"why":"Lumbar puncture is done at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5, below the end of the spinal cord. The needle passes skin, supraspinous and interspinous ligaments, ligamentum flavum, the epidural space, dura, and arachnoid, then enters the lumbar cistern, an enlarged part of the subarachnoid space. The floating roots of the cauda equina usually slide away from the needle. CSF is normally returned to venous blood through arachnoid granulations into the dural venous sinuses, mainly the superior sagittal sinus.","whyNot":["The subdural space is only a potential space and holds no free CSF. Injecting into it by mistake is a known complication.","The conus medullaris lies above the puncture level, at about L1 to L2 in adults, and its central canal is too small to tap.","Correct.","The epidural space contains fat and the vertebral venous plexus. No CSF flows from it, and it is where epidural anesthetic is placed."],"study":{"topics":["Lumbar puncture anatomy","Subarachnoid cisterns"],"comps":["NE-4","NE-7"]}},{"id":"NE-C16-Q3","stem":"Before his MRI, his physician considered other causes of cognitive decline. Which of the following features would most strongly favor vascular dementia over Alzheimer disease?","options":["Gradual early loss of recent memory with normal gait","Recurrent visual hallucinations with fluctuating alertness","Early disinhibition and personality change with spared memory","Stepwise decline with focal neurologic signs"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-B","obj":"NE-20-B.11","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the course of Alzheimer disease: slow, steady decline that starts with recent memory.","Vascular dementia follows strokes or diffuse small vessel disease, so it often worsens in steps and comes with focal signs.","Match the other options to Lewy body and frontotemporal dementia."],"why":"Vascular dementia results from multiple infarcts or diffuse small vessel ischemia, usually in patients with hypertension, diabetes, or smoking. Its course is often stepwise, with sudden drops after new infarcts, and exam may show focal weakness, reflex changes, or gait problems. Alzheimer disease declines gradually and begins with trouble forming new memories. Mild cognitive impairment is measurable decline in memory or another domain with daily function still preserved, and it may progress to dementia.","whyNot":["Slow, steady loss of recent memory with normal gait and no focal signs is the classic picture of Alzheimer disease.","Visual hallucinations with fluctuating alertness suggest dementia with Lewy bodies.","Early disinhibition and personality change with relatively spared memory suggest frontotemporal dementia.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vascular dementia","Causes of dementia"],"comps":["NE-20-B","NE-20-B1"]}},{"id":"NE-C16-Q4","stem":"A 69-year-old woman in the same clinic has pain and numbness in both buttocks and legs after walking about two blocks. The symptoms ease within minutes when she sits or leans forward on a shopping cart, and she can ride a stationary bicycle without pain. Pedal pulses are normal. Which of the following best explains why bending forward relieves her symptoms?","options":["Flexion relaxes the piriformis muscle over the sciatic nerve","Flexion increases arterial inflow to calf muscles","Flexion lowers pressure within the intervertebral disc","Flexion widens the canal and eases cauda equina compression"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-I","obj":"NE-20-I.9","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize neurogenic claudication: leg symptoms with standing and walking, relieved by sitting or flexing forward, with normal pulses.","Recall that lumbar spinal stenosis narrows the canal through disc bulging, facet hypertrophy, and a thickened ligamentum flavum.","Extension narrows the canal further, and flexion opens it, which relieves pressure on the nerve roots."],"why":"Lumbar spinal stenosis, usually from degenerative change in older adults, narrows the central canal and lateral recesses. In extension, as in standing and walking upright, the ligamentum flavum buckles inward and the canal shrinks, compressing the cauda equina. Flexion enlarges the canal, so symptoms ease with sitting, leaning on a cart, cycling, or walking uphill. Vascular claudication, by contrast, is relieved by simply stopping and comes with weak pulses. Cervical stenosis compresses the cord itself and causes myelopathy with upper motor neuron signs.","whyNot":["Piriformis syndrome causes buttock pain with sitting and hip rotation. It does not cause bilateral leg symptoms with walking.","Vascular claudication depends on blood flow, so it is relieved by stopping activity regardless of posture, and pulses are weak. Her pulses are normal and cycling does not hurt.","Flexion raises intradiscal pressure, which is why disc herniation pain often worsens with sitting and bending.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lumbar spinal stenosis","Neurogenic versus vascular claudication"],"comps":["NE-20-I","NE-20-I3"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C17","system":"NE","dx":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","codes":["NE-20-C"],"case":"A 27-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 3 months of daily headaches that are worse in the morning and when lying down. She has brief episodes of vision going gray when she stands up, a whooshing sound in both ears, and horizontal double vision at distance. She began taking minocycline for acne 4 months ago. Body mass index is 38 kg/m2. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Funduscopic examination shows bilateral optic disc swelling. Her left eye does not abduct fully. Visual field testing shows enlarged blind spots. MRI of the brain and MR venography are normal. Lumbar puncture shows an opening pressure of 34 cm H2O (normal about 10 to 20) with normal CSF composition. Diagnosis: idiopathic intracranial hypertension.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C17-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her double vision?","options":["Stretch of the abducens nerve over the petrous apex","Inflammation of the lateral rectus muscle","Compression of the oculomotor nerve by the uncus","A demyelinating lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-C","obj":"NE-20-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Localize the deficit: failed abduction of the left eye means a CN VI problem.","Recall that CN VI has a long course along the clivus and over the petrous apex, where raised pressure can stretch it.","In raised intracranial pressure, a CN VI palsy is a false localizing sign; it does not mark the site of a lesion."],"why":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension typically affects obese women of childbearing age. Raised CSF pressure causes headache, papilledema, transient visual obscurations, pulsatile tinnitus, and sometimes a CN VI palsy, while imaging shows no mass and no venous sinus thrombosis. The abducens nerve is vulnerable because it runs a long path up the clivus and bends over the petrous apex. Known triggers include tetracyclines such as minocycline and excess vitamin A or retinoids. The main danger is permanent vision loss from chronic papilledema.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lateral rectus myositis is painful and would not explain papilledema or a raised opening pressure.","Uncal herniation compresses CN III, giving a dilated pupil and a down-and-out eye. She has normal imaging and failed abduction.","A medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion impairs adduction, not abduction, and her MRI shows no demyelinating lesion."],"study":{"topics":["Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","False localizing CN VI palsy"],"comps":["NE-20-C","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C17-Q2","stem":"Despite treatment, chronic papilledema leads to complete atrophy of the left optic nerve, while the right optic nerve recovers. Which of the following visual field defects is most likely?","options":["Right homonymous hemianopia","Bitemporal heteronymous hemianopia","Left monocular blindness","Right superior quadrantanopia"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-6","obj":"NE-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Place the lesion: the left optic nerve, in front of the chiasm.","Before the chiasm, each optic nerve carries all fibers from one eye.","So a complete left optic nerve lesion blinds the left eye and spares the right."],"why":"Fibers from each retina travel in that eye's optic nerve to the chiasm, where the nasal fibers cross. Behind the chiasm, each optic tract carries the opposite visual field from both eyes. A lesion of one optic nerve causes loss of vision in that eye. A chiasm lesion causes bitemporal hemianopia. A lesion of the optic tract or the whole radiation causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia. A lesion of the Meyer loop in the temporal lobe causes contralateral superior quadrantanopia.","whyNot":["Right homonymous hemianopia comes from a lesion behind the chiasm on the left, such as the left optic tract, radiation, or occipital cortex.","Bitemporal hemianopia comes from a lesion of the crossing fibers at the chiasm, classically a pituitary adenoma.","Correct.","Right superior quadrantanopia comes from a lesion of the left Meyer loop in the temporal lobe."],"study":{"topics":["Visual pathway lesions","Visual field defects"],"comps":["NE-6","NE-20-J"]}},{"id":"NE-C17-Q3","stem":"She is treated with acetazolamide, which inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the cells that secrete CSF. Which of the following forms the barrier between blood and CSF at this site?","options":["Choroid plexus epithelium sealed by tight junctions","Fenestrated endothelium of the area postrema","Arachnoid barrier cells beneath the dura","Brain capillary endothelium sealed by tight junctions"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-4","obj":"NE-4.4","dok":3,"approach":["CSF is secreted by the choroid plexus in the ventricles.","Choroid plexus capillaries are fenestrated and leaky, so the barrier must lie in the epithelium covering them.","Tight junctions between choroid plexus epithelial cells form the blood-CSF barrier."],"why":"The blood-brain barrier is formed by brain capillary endothelial cells joined by tight junctions, supported by their basement membrane and astrocyte foot processes. The blood-CSF barrier is in a different place: choroid plexus capillaries are fenestrated, and the barrier is formed by tight junctions between the choroid plexus epithelial cells, which secrete CSF. Carbonic anhydrase in these cells supplies bicarbonate and drives sodium and water secretion, so acetazolamide lowers CSF production. Circumventricular organs such as the area postrema and posterior pituitary lack a barrier so they can sense or release substances in the blood.","whyNot":["Correct.","The area postrema lacks a barrier, which lets it detect toxins in blood and trigger vomiting. It does not secrete CSF.","The arachnoid barrier layer separates the dura from CSF in the subarachnoid space. It is not where CSF is made.","Brain capillary endothelium forms the blood-brain barrier between blood and brain tissue, not the barrier at the site of CSF secretion."],"study":{"topics":["Blood-brain and blood-CSF barriers","Circumventricular organs"],"comps":["NE-4","NE-10"]}},{"id":"NE-C17-Q4","stem":"A 31-year-old woman who delivered a baby 10 days ago develops a severe headache, papilledema, and a seizure. Blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg. MRI shows hemorrhagic infarcts in the parasagittal cortex of both hemispheres that do not follow an arterial territory. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Rupture of a saccular aneurysm at the circle of Willis","Thrombosis of the superior sagittal sinus","Hypertensive vasogenic edema of posterior white matter","Embolic occlusion of the middle cerebral artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-A","obj":"NE-20-A.11","dok":3,"approach":["The postpartum state is hypercoagulable.","Bilateral parasagittal hemorrhagic infarcts outside arterial territories point to blocked venous drainage into the superior sagittal sinus.","Blocked venous outflow also impairs CSF absorption, which raises intracranial pressure and causes papilledema."],"why":"Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis occurs in hypercoagulable states such as pregnancy and the postpartum period, estrogen use, and inherited thrombophilias. When the superior sagittal sinus clots, venous pressure rises in the cortex it drains, causing edema and hemorrhagic venous infarcts in both parasagittal regions. CSF absorption through the arachnoid granulations also falls, so pressure rises. It can mimic idiopathic intracranial hypertension, which is why MR venography is done. Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome, by contrast, is vasogenic edema of posterior white matter during severe hypertension.","whyNot":["Aneurysm rupture causes subarachnoid hemorrhage with a sudden worst headache, not bilateral parasagittal infarcts.","Correct.","Vasogenic edema of posterior white matter describes posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome, which usually occurs with severe hypertension or eclampsia. Her blood pressure is normal.","A middle cerebral artery embolus causes a single infarct in that arterial territory on one side, not bilateral parasagittal lesions."],"study":{"topics":["Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome"],"comps":["NE-20-A","NE-10"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C18","system":"NE","dx":"Hepatic encephalopathy in alcoholic cirrhosis","codes":["NE-20-D1"],"case":"A 58-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis is brought to the emergency department by his wife because of 2 days of confusion and daytime sleepiness. He has passed black stools since yesterday. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. He is jaundiced and disoriented to time. Examination shows spider angiomas, ascites, and a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands. Rectal examination shows melena. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), sodium 124 mEq/L (135 to 145), potassium 3.1 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), and venous ammonia 112 µmol/L (about 10 to 40). Diagnosis: hepatic encephalopathy in alcoholic cirrhosis, precipitated by upper gastrointestinal bleeding.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C18-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains how the bleeding precipitated his encephalopathy?","options":["Blood loss reduced cerebral perfusion below autoregulatory limits","Bilirubin from hemoglobin breakdown crossed the blood-brain barrier","Bacteria digested blood protein, and the ammonia bypassed the liver","Iron from hemoglobin generated free radicals in the basal ganglia"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-20-D1","obj":"NE-20-D.1","dok":3,"approach":["Blood in the gut is a large protein load.","Colonic bacteria break down that protein and release ammonia, which is absorbed into the portal blood.","In cirrhosis with portosystemic shunting, the ammonia reaches the brain without being cleared by the liver."],"why":"Normally the liver removes gut-derived ammonia through the urea cycle. In cirrhosis, hepatocyte function is reduced, and portal blood flows through portosystemic shunts straight into the systemic circulation, so ammonia and other gut toxins reach the brain. Anything that raises ammonia production or absorption can tip a patient into encephalopathy: gastrointestinal bleeding, high protein intake, constipation, infection, and hypokalemia, which increases renal ammonia production. Confusion, sleep-wake reversal, and asterixis are the typical signs. Lactulose and rifaximin reduce ammonia production and absorption.","whyNot":["His blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, within the autoregulatory range. Low perfusion would not raise his ammonia.","Bilirubin enters the brain mainly in newborns, causing kernicterus. It does not cause encephalopathy in adult cirrhosis.","Correct.","Iron and free radical injury are not the mechanism of hepatic encephalopathy. Iron deposits in the basal ganglia occur in other disorders."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic encephalopathy precipitants","Portosystemic shunting"],"comps":["NE-20-D1","NE-14"]}},{"id":"NE-C18-Q2","stem":"Brain tissue from a patient who died of this disorder shows astrocytes with enlarged, pale nuclei (Alzheimer type II astrocytes). Which of the following reactions in these cells best explains their swelling?","options":["Glutamate plus ammonia forming glutamine","Glutamine hydrolysis releasing ammonia","Alpha-ketoglutarate transamination forming glutamate","Ornithine plus carbamoyl phosphate forming citrulline"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-14","obj":"NE-14.5","dok":3,"approach":["The brain has no urea cycle, so it detoxifies ammonia by another route.","Astrocytes use glutamine synthetase to combine ammonia with glutamate, forming glutamine.","Glutamine builds up inside astrocytes, draws in water osmotically, and the cells swell."],"why":"Astrocytes are the main cells in the brain that remove ammonia. Glutamine synthetase, an ATP-dependent enzyme, joins ammonia to glutamate to make glutamine. With high ammonia, glutamine accumulates in astrocytes, pulls water in, and the cells swell, which contributes to brain edema. The swollen astrocytes with large pale nuclei are called Alzheimer type II astrocytes. Ammonia also depletes glutamate and alpha-ketoglutarate, which disrupts neurotransmission and the TCA cycle in brain cells. Urea cycle enzyme defects cause the same brain injury in infants.","whyNot":["Correct.","Glutaminase releases ammonia from glutamine, mainly in the kidney and gut. That reaction would add to the ammonia load, not trap it.","Transamination moves an amino group between molecules but does not remove free ammonia, so it does not explain glutamine buildup.","This is a urea cycle step that occurs in liver mitochondria. The brain lacks a working urea cycle."],"study":{"topics":["Ammonia detoxification in the brain","Glutamine synthetase"],"comps":["NE-14","NE-20-D1"]}},{"id":"NE-C18-Q3","stem":"His serum sodium is raised from 124 mEq/L to 136 mEq/L within 24 hours. Three days later, he develops slurred speech, difficulty swallowing, and weakness of all four limbs. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his new findings?","options":["Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine depletion","Worsening hepatic encephalopathy from new bleeding","Cerebral edema from rapid water shift into neurons","Demyelination of the central basis pontis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-D","obj":"NE-20-D.7","dok":3,"approach":["Note the rise in sodium: 12 mEq/L in 24 hours, above the safe limit of about 8 mEq/L per day for a high-risk patient like him (about 10 to 12 for others).","In chronic hyponatremia, brain cells have shed solutes to avoid swelling; rapid correction pulls water out of them.","Glial cells in the central pons are injured, causing dysarthria, dysphagia, and quadriparesis a few days later."],"why":"In chronic hyponatremia, brain cells lose organic osmolytes to stay at normal volume. When sodium is corrected too quickly, water leaves the cells, and oligodendrocytes are injured, especially in the central basis pontis. This causes osmotic demyelination syndrome, which appears 2 to 6 days after correction with dysarthria, dysphagia, quadriparesis, and in severe cases locked-in syndrome. Alcohol use, malnutrition, liver disease, and hypokalemia raise the risk, so the correction limit is lower in patients like him.","whyNot":["Wernicke encephalopathy causes confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia. It does not cause dysarthria and quadriparesis after sodium correction.","Hepatic encephalopathy causes confusion and asterixis, not new quadriparesis with bulbar weakness.","Raising sodium moves water out of brain cells, so cells shrink. Cerebral edema results when hypernatremia is corrected too quickly.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Osmotic demyelination syndrome","Safe correction of hyponatremia"],"comps":["NE-20-D","NE-20-C1"]}},{"id":"NE-C18-Q4","stem":"Early in his illness, his wife noticed that he slept during the day and was awake at night. Which of the following normally keeps the sleep-wake cycle aligned with daylight?","options":["Retinal light input increasing melatonin secretion during the day","Suprachiasmatic nucleus timing nightly pineal melatonin release","Lateral hypothalamic orexin neurons secreting melatonin at night","Ventromedial nucleus timing nightly adrenal cortisol release"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-12","obj":"NE-12.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the master clock: the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus.","It receives light information directly from the retina through the retinohypothalamic tract.","It controls the pineal gland through a sympathetic pathway, so melatonin rises in darkness and falls in light."],"why":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus sits just above the optic chiasm and keeps a roughly 24-hour rhythm. Light reaching special retinal ganglion cells is relayed to it by the retinohypothalamic tract, which resets the clock each day. The nucleus signals the pineal gland through a pathway that passes through the superior cervical ganglion, and the pineal secretes melatonin at night, promoting sleep. Orexin (hypocretin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus keep a person awake, and their loss causes narcolepsy with cataplexy.","whyNot":["Light suppresses melatonin. Melatonin is high at night and low during the day.","Correct.","Orexin neurons promote wakefulness and do not secrete melatonin. Their loss causes narcolepsy.","The ventromedial nucleus is a satiety center. Cortisol peaks in the early morning, but it is not the signal that sets the sleep cycle."],"study":{"topics":["Circadian rhythm","Suprachiasmatic nucleus and melatonin"],"comps":["NE-12","NE-6"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C19","system":"NE","dx":"Distal symmetric diabetic polyneuropathy","codes":["NE-20-D2"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman with a 15-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the physician because of 2 years of burning pain and numbness in her feet that now reach just below the knees, with recent tingling in her fingertips. She also reports early satiety and lightheadedness when she stands. Her hemoglobin A1c is 9.4% (goal less than 7). Blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg supine and 98/64 mm Hg standing, with no change in pulse. Examination shows decreased pinprick, vibration, and position sense in a stocking-and-glove distribution and absent ankle reflexes. There is a painless ulcer under the first metatarsal head of the right foot. Nerve conduction studies show reduced sensory and motor response amplitudes with nearly normal conduction velocities. Diagnosis: distal symmetric diabetic polyneuropathy.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C19-Q1","stem":"Persistent hyperglycemia damages her nerves partly through an enzyme in Schwann cells that converts excess intracellular glucose to an osmotically active alcohol, using NADPH. Which of the following enzymes is this?","options":["Galactokinase","Glucokinase","Sorbitol dehydrogenase","Aldose reductase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-D2","obj":"NE-20-D.3","dok":2,"approach":["Schwann cells take up glucose without insulin, so their glucose rises with blood glucose.","Aldose reductase reduces glucose to sorbitol using NADPH.","Sorbitol is trapped in the cell, draws in water, and NADPH depletion weakens antioxidant defense."],"why":"In the polyol pathway, aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol, and sorbitol dehydrogenase converts sorbitol to fructose. Schwann cells, the lens, and the retina have little sorbitol dehydrogenase, so sorbitol accumulates, causing osmotic injury. Using up NADPH lowers glutathione regeneration and leaves cells exposed to oxidative stress. Advanced glycation end products and damage to the small vessels that supply nerves add to the injury. The results include stocking-and-glove sensory loss, autonomic dysfunction such as gastroparesis and orthostatic hypotension, neuropathic foot ulcers, and pupil-sparing CN III palsy from nerve infarction.","whyNot":["Galactokinase phosphorylates galactose. Its deficiency causes galactitol cataracts in infants.","Glucokinase phosphorylates glucose in liver and pancreatic beta cells. It does not form sorbitol.","Sorbitol dehydrogenase removes sorbitol by converting it to fructose. It is scarce in Schwann cells, which is why sorbitol builds up.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Polyol pathway","Mechanisms of diabetic neuropathy"],"comps":["NE-20-D2","NE-14"]}},{"id":"NE-C19-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the pattern and type of her nerve injury?","options":["Acute demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy","Single compressive mononeuropathy of the tibial nerve","Length-dependent axonal sensorimotor polyneuropathy","Mononeuritis multiplex from vasculitic nerve infarcts"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-D2","obj":"NE-20-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["Pattern: symmetric loss that starts in the feet and later reaches the fingertips, the longest nerves first.","Type: reduced response amplitudes with nearly normal conduction velocity means axons are lost while remaining myelin is intact.","Put them together: a length-dependent axonal polyneuropathy."],"why":"Peripheral neuropathy is sorted by pattern and by the part of the nerve injured. A distal symmetric polyneuropathy hits the longest axons first, so it begins in the toes and reaches the fingertips once leg symptoms are near the knees. Axonal injury lowers response amplitudes, while demyelination slows conduction and causes conduction block. Diabetes is the most common cause of distal symmetric axonal neuropathy. Others include alcohol, B12 and thiamine deficiency, HIV, and neurotoxic chemotherapy. Mononeuritis multiplex, with separate nerves hit in sequence, suggests vasculitis, and leprosy causes patchy sensory loss in cool skin.","whyNot":["An acute demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy, Guillain-Barre syndrome, develops over days and shows slowed conduction velocities. Her course is 2 years with near-normal velocities.","A single tibial nerve lesion would not explain symmetric loss in both feet and the hands.","Correct.","Mononeuritis multiplex affects separate named nerves in a patchy, stepwise pattern. Her loss is symmetric and length-dependent."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of peripheral neuropathy","Axonal versus demyelinating injury"],"comps":["NE-20-D2","NE-13"]}},{"id":"NE-C19-Q3","stem":"A sural nerve biopsy is stained for the tight junction protein claudin-1. A continuous ring of thin, flattened cells around each fascicle stains strongly. Which of the following layers has been stained?","options":["Perineurium","Schwann cell basal lamina","Epineurium","Endoneurium"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-2","obj":"NE-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the three connective tissue layers: endoneurium around each axon, perineurium around each fascicle, epineurium around the whole nerve.","The layer that wraps each fascicle is the perineurium.","Its cells are joined by tight junctions, so it forms the diffusion barrier of the blood-nerve barrier."],"why":"A peripheral nerve is built in layers. Endoneurium is loose connective tissue around each nerve fiber. Perineurium surrounds each fascicle and is made of layers of flattened cells joined by tight junctions with a basal lamina on each side. Epineurium is dense connective tissue that wraps the whole nerve and its blood vessels. The perineurium, together with tight junctions in endoneurial capillaries, forms the blood-nerve barrier that keeps the endoneurial fluid stable around axons.","whyNot":["Correct.","The Schwann cell basal lamina surrounds each nerve fiber, not each fascicle, and is extracellular matrix, not a layer of cells.","Epineurium is dense irregular connective tissue around the whole nerve. It does not form a ring of tight-junction cells around each fascicle.","Endoneurium is loose connective tissue around individual nerve fibers inside a fascicle."],"study":{"topics":["Peripheral nerve connective tissue","Blood-nerve barrier"],"comps":["NE-2","NE-20-D2"]}},{"id":"NE-C19-Q4","stem":"Which of the following is most likely to be seen on funduscopic examination of this patient?","options":["Microaneurysms, dot-blot hemorrhages, and hard exudates","Bilateral optic disc swelling with blurred margins","Cherry-red fovea within a pale, edematous retina","Arteriovenous nicking with copper-wire arteriolar reflexes"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-J","obj":"NE-20-J.13","dok":2,"approach":["Long-standing, poorly controlled diabetes causing neuropathy also damages small vessels in the retina.","The first retinal changes of diabetes are microaneurysms from capillary wall weakness, with leaks that leave hemorrhages and lipid exudates.","Match the other options to hypertension, raised intracranial pressure, and arterial occlusion."],"why":"Diabetic retinopathy begins as nonproliferative disease, with pericyte loss, basement membrane thickening, microaneurysms, dot-blot hemorrhages, and hard exudates from leaky capillaries. Retinal ischemia then drives VEGF release and neovascularization, which defines proliferative disease and can bleed or detach the retina. Hypertensive retinopathy shows arteriolar narrowing, AV nicking, flame hemorrhages, and cotton wool spots. Papilledema is swelling of both optic discs from raised intracranial pressure.","whyNot":["Correct.","Bilateral optic disc swelling is papilledema from raised intracranial pressure, which nothing in her case suggests.","A cherry-red fovea with a pale, swollen retina is the picture of central retinal artery occlusion, which causes sudden painless vision loss.","AV nicking and copper-wire arterioles are signs of hypertensive retinopathy. Her blood pressure is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Diabetic retinopathy","Funduscopic findings"],"comps":["NE-20-J","NE-20-D2"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C20","system":"NE","dx":"Subacute combined degeneration from pernicious anemia (vitamin B12 deficiency)","codes":["NE-20-D3"],"case":"A 61-year-old man comes to the physician because of 5 months of tingling in his feet and hands, unsteadiness when walking in the dark, and forgetfulness. He has autoimmune hypothyroidism treated with levothyroxine. Examination shows a smooth, red tongue. Vibration and joint position sense are reduced in both feet, and Romberg sign is positive. Muscle tone is increased in the legs, knee reflexes are brisk, ankle reflexes are absent, and plantar responses are extensor on both sides. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), mean corpuscular volume 112 fL (80 to 100), hypersegmented neutrophils, and serum vitamin B12 140 pg/mL (about 200 to 900). Antibodies to intrinsic factor are present. Diagnosis: subacute combined degeneration from pernicious anemia.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C20-Q1","stem":"His daughter asks why his diet, which includes meat and dairy daily, did not prevent the deficiency. Which of the following best explains his deficiency?","options":["Competition for cobalamin by an intestinal tapeworm","Loss of the gastric protein needed for ileal uptake","Loss of receptor-mediated uptake in a diseased ileum","Failure of pancreatic proteases to free cobalamin from haptocorrin"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-D3","obj":"NE-20-D.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the B12 absorption steps: release from food in the stomach, binding to haptocorrin, release by pancreatic proteases, binding to intrinsic factor, uptake in the terminal ileum.","Pernicious anemia is autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells, which make intrinsic factor.","Without intrinsic factor, dietary B12 cannot be taken up in the ileum, however much he eats."],"why":"Pernicious anemia is the most common cause of B12 deficiency in adults. Autoantibodies and T cells destroy parietal cells in the gastric body and fundus, and antibodies to intrinsic factor block the vitamin from binding it, so B12 is not absorbed in the terminal ileum. It often occurs with other autoimmune diseases such as autoimmune thyroiditis. Other causes of B12 deficiency include a strict vegan diet, gastrectomy, ileal disease or resection, and the fish tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum. Neurologic findings include subacute combined degeneration, peripheral neuropathy, and dementia.","whyNot":["Diphyllobothrium latum competes for B12 in people who eat raw freshwater fish. His intrinsic factor antibodies explain the deficiency.","Correct.","Crohn disease or resection of the terminal ileum removes the site of uptake. His ileum is not reported as diseased, and the antibodies point to intrinsic factor.","Pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 release from haptocorrin, but nothing suggests pancreatic disease, and intrinsic factor antibodies point to the stomach."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin B12 absorption","Pernicious anemia"],"comps":["NE-20-D3","NE-14"]}},{"id":"NE-C20-Q2","stem":"Which of the following pairs of laboratory results would best confirm vitamin B12 deficiency, rather than folate deficiency, as the cause of his megaloblastic anemia?","options":["Elevated homocysteine with normal methylmalonic acid","Elevated homocysteine and methylmalonic acid","Normal homocysteine with elevated methylmalonic acid","Normal homocysteine and methylmalonic acid"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-14","obj":"NE-14.3","dok":3,"approach":["B12 is the cofactor for two enzymes: methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.","Loss of methionine synthase raises homocysteine in both B12 and folate deficiency.","Only B12 deficiency also blocks methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, so methylmalonic acid rises as well."],"why":"Methionine synthase uses methylcobalamin to move a methyl group from N5-methyltetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, forming methionine and regenerating tetrahydrofolate for DNA synthesis. Deficiency of either B12 or folate stalls this step, raises homocysteine, and causes megaloblastic anemia. Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase uses adenosylcobalamin to convert methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, and it needs B12 only. Its failure raises methylmalonic acid and leads to abnormal fatty acids in myelin, and reduced S-adenosylmethionine impairs myelin methylation. This is why B12 deficiency damages the nervous system and folate deficiency does not.","whyNot":["This pattern fits folate deficiency, which blocks methionine synthase but leaves methylmalonyl-CoA mutase working.","Correct.","A B12 deficiency severe enough to cause anemia also blocks methionine synthase, so homocysteine would be high too.","Normal levels of both would argue against deficiency of either vitamin as the cause of his anemia."],"study":{"topics":["Methylmalonic acid and homocysteine","B12-dependent enzymes"],"comps":["NE-14","NE-20-D3"]}},{"id":"NE-C20-Q3","stem":"Which of the following pairs of spinal cord structures is most affected in this patient?","options":["Dorsal columns and dorsal roots","Lateral spinothalamic tracts and anterior horns","Dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts","Anterior horns and lateral corticospinal tracts"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-7","obj":"NE-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Lost vibration and position sense with a positive Romberg sign points to the dorsal columns.","Spasticity, brisk knee reflexes, and extensor plantar responses point to the lateral corticospinal tracts.","Both tracts together, from B12 deficiency, is subacute combined degeneration. The absent ankle reflexes come from the accompanying peripheral neuropathy."],"why":"Subacute combined degeneration is demyelination of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts, usually starting in the cervical and upper thoracic cord. Patients lose vibration and position sense, sway with eyes closed, and develop spastic weakness with extensor plantar responses. A coexisting peripheral neuropathy can abolish ankle reflexes. Tabes dorsalis affects dorsal columns and dorsal roots and gives sensory ataxia with lost reflexes but no upper motor neuron signs. Anterior spinal artery infarction spares the dorsal columns. Complete transection removes all function below the level.","whyNot":["Dorsal column and dorsal root damage is tabes dorsalis from neurosyphilis. It lacks upper motor neuron signs such as extensor plantar responses.","Spinothalamic tract and anterior horn damage with the dorsal columns spared is anterior spinal artery infarction, which causes weakness and loss of pain and temperature with vibration and position sense preserved. His dorsal column function is lost.","Correct.","Anterior horn and corticospinal tract damage is ALS, which causes no sensory loss."],"study":{"topics":["Subacute combined degeneration","Spinal cord syndromes"],"comps":["NE-7","NE-20-D3"]}},{"id":"NE-C20-Q4","stem":"The loss of vibration and position sense in his feet reflects damage to which of the following tracts, and where do the second-order fibers of that pathway cross the midline?","options":["Fasciculus cuneatus, crossing in the caudal medulla","Fasciculus cuneatus, crossing in the anterior white commissure","Fasciculus gracilis, crossing at the level of entry","Fasciculus gracilis, crossing in the caudal medulla"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-5","obj":"NE-5.2","dok":3,"approach":["Sensation from the legs ascends in the medial part of the dorsal columns, the fasciculus gracilis.","First-order fibers ascend on the same side and synapse in the nucleus gracilis in the medulla.","Second-order fibers cross there as internal arcuate fibers and ascend as the medial lemniscus to the thalamus."],"why":"The dorsal column medial lemniscus pathway carries vibration, position sense, and fine touch. First-order axons enter the cord and ascend on the same side, leg fibers medially in the fasciculus gracilis and arm fibers laterally in the fasciculus cuneatus above T6. They synapse in the gracile and cuneate nuclei of the caudal medulla, and second-order fibers cross as internal arcuate fibers, rise in the medial lemniscus to the ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus, and project to the postcentral gyrus. A cord lesion therefore causes loss on the same side below the lesion. Pain and temperature cross within a segment or two of entry in the anterior white commissure, so a cord lesion causes loss on the opposite side.","whyNot":["The fasciculus cuneatus carries sensation from the upper body and arms, not the feet.","The fasciculus cuneatus carries arm sensation, and crossing in the anterior white commissure describes the spinothalamic tract.","Fibers that cross near their level of entry belong to the spinothalamic tract, which carries pain and temperature.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dorsal column medial lemniscus pathway","Spinothalamic tract"],"comps":["NE-5","NE-7"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C21","system":"NE","dx":"Wernicke encephalopathy progressing to Korsakoff syndrome","codes":["NE-20-D4"],"case":"A 47-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by police after he was found confused in a park. He has alcohol use disorder and has eaten little for several weeks. His temperature is 36.4 °C (97.5 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. He is disoriented to place and time and cannot keep his attention on the examiner. There is horizontal nystagmus with bilateral weakness of abduction. He walks with a wide-based, staggering gait and cannot walk heel to toe. Finger-to-nose testing and rapid alternating hand movements are normal. Serum glucose is 68 mg/dL (reference 70 to 100 mg/dL). Diagnosis: Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C21-Q1","stem":"He receives thiamine but recovers only partly. Over the following months he cannot retain new information for more than a few minutes, and when asked what he did yesterday he gives detailed accounts of events that never happened. He dies 4 years later. Which of the following is most likely to be found at autopsy?","options":["Neuronal loss in the hippocampal CA1 sector","Pallor of the substantia nigra pars compacta","Shrunken, brown mammillary bodies","Atrophy of the head of the caudate nucleus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-D4","obj":"NE-20-D.5","dok":2,"approach":["Name the new syndrome: anterograde amnesia with confabulation after Wernicke encephalopathy is Korsakoff syndrome.","Recall where thiamine deficiency injures the brain: mammillary bodies, medial (dorsomedial) thalamus, and the gray matter around the third ventricle and aqueduct.","Pick the chronic lesion of those structures."],"why":"Thiamine deficiency damages the mammillary bodies, the dorsomedial thalamic nuclei, and periventricular and periaqueductal gray matter. Acute lesions show petechial hemorrhages and capillary proliferation. Over time the mammillary bodies shrink and turn brown from old hemosiderin. Damage to the mammillary bodies and medial thalamus breaks the memory circuit, which causes the lasting anterograde amnesia and confabulation of Korsakoff syndrome.","whyNot":["CA1 neurons die after global hypoxia, such as cardiac arrest. That causes amnesia, but confabulation after Wernicke encephalopathy points to mammillary and thalamic damage.","Nigral pallor is the gross finding in Parkinson disease, which causes tremor, rigidity, and slow movement.","Correct.","Caudate atrophy is the hallmark of Huntington disease, which causes chorea and dementia."],"study":{"topics":["Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome pathology","Papez memory circuit"],"comps":["NE-20-D4","NE-12"]}},{"id":"NE-C21-Q2","stem":"Because of his low glucose, emergency staff give an infusion of 5% dextrose before any thiamine is given. Over the next hour his confusion deepens, and serum lactate rises to 4.6 mmol/L (reference 0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). Reduced activity of which of the following enzymes best explains these changes?","options":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate carboxylase","Lactate dehydrogenase","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-14","obj":"NE-14.2","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the deficient cofactor: thiamine pyrophosphate.","List the enzymes that need it: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and transketolase.","A glucose load pushes more pyruvate toward pyruvate dehydrogenase. When that step is blocked, pyruvate is turned into lactate and the brain makes less ATP."],"why":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, which links glycolysis to the citric acid cycle. The brain runs almost entirely on glucose oxidation, so it depends heavily on this step. Giving glucose to a thiamine-deficient patient raises the flow of pyruvate into a blocked enzyme. Pyruvate backs up and is converted to lactate, and neurons in vulnerable areas run short of ATP. This is why thiamine is given before or with glucose in malnourished patients.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pyruvate carboxylase uses biotin, not thiamine. It makes oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis.","Lactate dehydrogenase converts pyruvate to lactate and needs no thiamine. It is working harder here, which is why lactate rises.","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase starts the pentose phosphate pathway and uses NADP+, not thiamine. Transketolase is the thiamine-dependent enzyme of that pathway."],"study":{"topics":["Thiamine-dependent enzymes","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex"],"comps":["NE-14","NE-20-D4"]}},{"id":"NE-C21-Q3","stem":"Position sense in his toes is normal, and he sways just as much with his eyes open as with them closed. Damage to which of the following structures best explains his gait disturbance?","options":["Dorsal columns of the spinal cord","Dentate nuclei","Lateral cerebellar hemispheres","Anterior cerebellar vermis"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-3","obj":"NE-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Separate sensory ataxia from cerebellar ataxia: normal position sense and no worsening with eyes closed rule out a dorsal column cause.","Note that the trunk and gait are affected while arm coordination is normal.","Midline (vermis) lesions cause truncal and gait ataxia; hemisphere lesions cause limb ataxia on the same side."],"why":"The cerebellar vermis controls the axial and proximal muscles used for posture and walking. Damage to it gives a wide-based, unsteady gait with truncal sway, while finger-to-nose testing can stay normal. In alcohol use disorder and thiamine deficiency, the anterior superior vermis is the part most often damaged. Lateral hemisphere lesions instead cause intention tremor, dysmetria, and dysdiadochokinesia in the limbs on the same side.","whyNot":["Dorsal column damage causes sensory ataxia with lost position sense and a positive Romberg sign, where sway worsens with the eyes closed. His position sense is normal.","The dentate nuclei receive output from the lateral hemispheres. Damage causes limb ataxia and intention tremor, but his hand coordination is normal.","Hemisphere lesions cause ipsilateral limb ataxia, intention tremor, and dysdiadochokinesia. His finger-to-nose and rapid alternating movements are normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cerebellar vermis versus hemisphere lesions","Sensory versus cerebellar ataxia"],"comps":["NE-3","NE-5"]}},{"id":"NE-C21-Q4","stem":"Three years later, he has chronic pancreatitis with greasy, foul-smelling stools. He now has worsening ataxia of his limbs and gait, loss of vibration and position sense in both feet, and absent ankle reflexes. Serum vitamin B12 and methylmalonic acid concentrations are within the reference ranges. Deficiency of which of the following is the most likely cause of these new findings?","options":["Vitamin E","Pyridoxine","Vitamin A","Niacin"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-14","obj":"NE-14.4","dok":3,"approach":["Greasy stools from pancreatic insufficiency mean fat malabsorption, so think of fat-soluble vitamins.","Ataxia with dorsal column loss and neuropathy, with normal B12 and methylmalonic acid, matches vitamin E deficiency.","Rule out the water-soluble vitamins by their own neurologic patterns."],"why":"Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) is a fat-soluble antioxidant that protects the lipid-rich membranes of neurons from oxidative injury. Fat malabsorption from chronic pancreatitis, cholestasis, or cystic fibrosis can deplete it. Deficiency damages the spinocerebellar tracts, dorsal columns, and peripheral nerves, giving ataxia, loss of vibration and position sense, and areflexia. It looks like B12 deficiency, but B12 and methylmalonic acid are normal and there is no megaloblastic anemia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pyridoxine deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy, as with isoniazid use, and seizures in infants. It is water soluble, so fat malabsorption is not its typical cause.","Vitamin A is also fat soluble, but deficiency causes night blindness, dry eyes, and dry skin, not ataxia.","Niacin deficiency causes pellagra: dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, diarrhea, and dementia. It does not cause dorsal column loss."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin E deficiency","Fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption"],"comps":["NE-14","NE-7"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C22","system":"NE","dx":"Down syndrome (trisomy 21) with early-onset Alzheimer disease","codes":["NE-20-E1"],"case":"A 46-year-old man with Down syndrome is brought to the physician by his sister because of 2 years of worsening memory. He used to work part time and dress himself, but he now gets lost in his own neighborhood and needs help with daily tasks. His karyotype at birth was 47,XY,+21, and an atrioventricular septal defect was repaired in infancy. Examination shows upslanting palpebral fissures, a flat facial profile, single palmar creases, and mild generalized hypotonia. He is oriented to person only. MRI of the brain shows diffuse cortical atrophy, most marked in the hippocampi. Diagnosis: Down syndrome with early-onset Alzheimer disease.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C22-Q1","stem":"He needs general anesthesia for a dental procedure. Before intubation, the anesthesiologist asks about neck symptoms and avoids forceful neck extension. Which of the following complications of his condition is the main concern?","options":["Syringomyelia of the cervical cord","Atlantoaxial instability from ligamentous laxity","Chiari I herniation of the cerebellar tonsils","Fusion of cervical vertebrae from failed segmentation"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-E1","obj":"NE-20-E.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the musculoskeletal features of Down syndrome: generalized ligament laxity and hypotonia.","Laxity of the transverse ligament lets C1 slide on C2.","Forced neck extension or flexion during intubation can then compress the upper cervical cord."],"why":"About 1 in 10 to 1 in 5 people with Down syndrome have extra movement between C1 and C2 because the transverse ligament that holds the dens is lax. Most have no symptoms, but sudden neck movement, as during intubation or contact sports, can compress the cord. Warning signs include neck pain, a new gait change, weakness, and bladder change. Down syndrome also brings intellectual disability, hypotonia, early Alzheimer disease, AV septal defects, duodenal atresia, and a higher risk of acute leukemia.","whyNot":["Syringomyelia is linked to Chiari I malformation and cord trauma, not to Down syndrome.","Correct.","Chiari I malformation is not part of Down syndrome. It presents with occipital headache worse with coughing and may cause a syrinx.","Congenital fusion of cervical vertebrae is Klippel-Feil syndrome, which is not a feature of Down syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Features of Down syndrome","Atlantoaxial instability"],"comps":["NE-20-E1","NE-16"]}},{"id":"NE-C22-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why he developed Alzheimer disease decades earlier than is typical in the general population?","options":["Prion protein gene mutation on chromosome 20","Apolipoprotein E4 allele on chromosome 19","Third copy of the APP gene on chromosome 21","Presenilin 1 mutation on chromosome 14"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-16","obj":"NE-16.2","dok":2,"approach":["Link his karyotype, 47,XY,+21, to a third copy of every gene on chromosome 21.","Recall that the amyloid precursor protein (APP) gene is on chromosome 21.","More APP means more beta-amyloid, so plaques form decades early."],"why":"The APP gene sits on chromosome 21. With three copies, cells make about 1.5 times the normal amount of APP, and more is cut into beta-amyloid. Plaques appear in most people with Down syndrome by about age 40, and dementia often follows in the 40s and 50s. Most trisomy 21 comes from maternal meiotic nondisjunction, and risk rises with maternal age. A smaller share comes from a Robertsonian translocation or from mosaicism.","whyNot":["Prion protein gene mutations cause familial prion diseases such as familial Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, which progress over months.","The E4 allele raises Alzheimer risk in the general population and is common in late-onset disease. It does not explain the near-universal early pathology in Down syndrome.","Correct.","Presenilin 1 mutations cause autosomal dominant early-onset Alzheimer disease, but they are not part of trisomy 21."],"study":{"topics":["Down syndrome genetics","APP gene dosage and Alzheimer disease"],"comps":["NE-16","NE-20-B1"]}},{"id":"NE-C22-Q3","stem":"Because thyroid disease is common in his condition and can worsen cognition, serum TSH is measured and is 14 μU/mL (reference 0.5 to 5.0 μU/mL). His ankle reflexes show a normal contraction followed by a slow return to the resting position. Which of the following best explains the slow relaxation phase?","options":["Reduced acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction","Slower calcium reuptake into the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Demyelination that slows the afferent limb of the reflex","Loss of upper motor neuron inhibition of the reflex arc"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-17","obj":"NE-17.5","dok":3,"approach":["Read the high TSH as primary hypothyroidism.","Note that only relaxation is slow, so the problem lies in the muscle after contraction, not in the nerve.","Thyroid hormone drives expression of the sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium pump; less pump activity means slower relaxation."],"why":"Muscle relaxes when calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum by the SERCA ATPase. Thyroid hormone increases SERCA expression and speeds both contraction and relaxation. In hypothyroidism, calcium is cleared slowly, so the reflex has a delayed relaxation phase. Hyperthyroidism does the reverse, with brisk reflexes and tremor. In infants, untreated congenital hypothyroidism impairs brain development, which is why newborns are screened.","whyNot":["Reduced acetylcholine release, as in Lambert-Eaton syndrome, weakens contraction. It does not slow relaxation.","Correct.","Slow afferent conduction would delay the start of the reflex and weaken it. The contraction here is normal.","Upper motor neuron loss makes reflexes brisk with spread and clonus. It does not slow relaxation."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid hormone and muscle","Hypothyroidism and reflexes"],"comps":["NE-17","NE-20-E1"]}},{"id":"NE-C22-Q4","stem":"He is also found to have bilateral cataracts, which are common in adults with his condition. Cataracts can also develop in the first weeks of life from an inherited metabolic disorder. A 3-week-old boy is brought to the physician because of vomiting and jaundice that began in his first week of breastfeeding. Physical examination shows hepatomegaly, and slit-lamp examination shows bilateral lens opacities. His urine contains a reducing substance that is not glucose. Accumulation of which of the following in the lens most directly causes the cataracts in this infant?","options":["Fructose-1-phosphate","Galactose-1-phosphate","Sorbitol","Galactitol"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-J2","obj":"NE-20-J.2","dok":3,"approach":["Link illness after starting milk, liver disease, and a non-glucose reducing sugar to classic galactosemia.","Excess galactose is reduced in the lens by aldose reductase.","The sugar alcohol formed is trapped in lens cells, draws in water, and makes the lens proteins opaque."],"why":"In classic galactosemia, galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase is missing, so galactose and galactose-1-phosphate build up. In the lens, aldose reductase reduces galactose to galactitol. Galactitol cannot leave the cell, so water follows it in, the fibers swell, and lens proteins lose their clear order. The same process with glucose and sorbitol explains cataracts in diabetes. Other cataract risks include age, corticosteroids, UV light, smoking, and congenital rubella.","whyNot":["Fructose-1-phosphate builds up in hereditary fructose intolerance, which causes hypoglycemia and liver injury after fruit or sucrose, not cataracts.","Galactose-1-phosphate builds up in classic galactosemia and injures the liver, kidney, and brain, but the lens damage comes from galactitol. Galactokinase deficiency causes cataracts without this metabolite.","Sorbitol is made from glucose by aldose reductase and causes cataracts in diabetes, not in galactosemia.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cataract risk factors","Galactosemia and galactitol"],"comps":["NE-20-J2","NE-20-E"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C23","system":"NE","dx":"X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy","codes":["NE-20-E2"],"case":"A 7-year-old boy is brought to the physician because his school performance has fallen over 6 months. His teacher reports trouble following directions and copying from the board, and he has become clumsy. He tires easily. His maternal uncle died at the age of 9 years of a progressive neurologic illness. Examination shows dark skin in the palmar creases and over the knuckles, brisk reflexes in the legs, and difficulty with fine motor tasks. MRI of the brain shows symmetric T2 hyperintensity in the parieto-occipital white matter with an enhancing leading edge. Plasma very long chain fatty acid levels are elevated. Diagnosis: X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C23-Q1","stem":"Which of the following defects most directly explains the accumulation of very long chain fatty acids in this patient?","options":["Lysosomal galactocerebrosidase deficiency","Peroxisomal membrane transporter defect","Mitochondrial carnitine shuttle defect","Lysosomal arylsulfatase A deficiency"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-E2","obj":"NE-20-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall where very long chain fatty acids are broken down: peroxisomes, not mitochondria.","X-linked inheritance through the mother's side and adrenal involvement point to the ABCD1 gene.","ABCD1 encodes a peroxisomal membrane transporter that brings these fatty acids in for beta-oxidation."],"why":"Fatty acids with 22 or more carbons are shortened by beta-oxidation inside peroxisomes. In X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy, mutations in ABCD1 disable the transporter that carries them across the peroxisomal membrane. The fatty acids build up in myelin, the adrenal cortex, and the testes. In the childhood cerebral form, this triggers inflammatory demyelination that usually starts in the parieto-occipital white matter, along with adrenal insufficiency.","whyNot":["Galactocerebrosidase deficiency is Krabbe disease, an autosomal recessive infantile leukodystrophy with globoid cells. It does not affect the adrenal glands.","Correct.","The carnitine shuttle moves long chain fatty acids into mitochondria. Defects cause hypoketotic hypoglycemia and muscle weakness with fasting.","Arylsulfatase A deficiency is metachromatic leukodystrophy, an autosomal recessive disease in which sulfatides build up. Fatty acid levels are normal."],"study":{"topics":["Leukodystrophies compared","Peroxisomal beta-oxidation"],"comps":["NE-20-E2","NE-14"]}},{"id":"NE-C23-Q2","stem":"His 8 AM serum cortisol is 2 μg/dL (reference 5 to 25 μg/dL), and his plasma ACTH is 480 pg/mL (reference 10 to 60 pg/mL). Which of the following best explains the darkening of his skin?","options":["Lost cortisol feedback raising ACTH output","Reduced dopamine inhibition of the pituitary","Excess aldosterone acting on skin melanocytes","Excess adrenal medullary catecholamine release"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-17","obj":"NE-17.4","dok":3,"approach":["Low cortisol with high ACTH means the problem is in the adrenal cortex (primary adrenal insufficiency).","Cortisol normally inhibits CRH release from the hypothalamus and ACTH release from the pituitary.","Without that feedback, ACTH rises, and ACTH stimulates melanocortin 1 receptors on melanocytes."],"why":"The HPA axis runs from CRH in the paraventricular nucleus to ACTH in the anterior pituitary to cortisol in the adrenal cortex, and cortisol feeds back to shut off the first two steps. When the adrenal cortex fails, feedback is lost and pituitary corticotrophs make large amounts of proopiomelanocortin (POMC), the precursor that is cut into ACTH. ACTH binds melanocortin 1 receptors on melanocytes and stimulates melanin production. The result is darkening of creases, knuckles, and mucosa. The other arm of the stress response, sympathetic output to the adrenal medulla, is not the cause of skin change.","whyNot":["Correct.","Dopamine from the hypothalamus inhibits prolactin, not ACTH. Loss of dopamine raises prolactin and does not darken the skin.","Aldosterone has no effect on melanocytes, and in primary adrenal insufficiency it is normal or low, not high.","The adrenal medulla releases epinephrine in response to stress through sympathetic preganglionic fibers. Catecholamines do not darken the skin."],"study":{"topics":["HPA axis feedback","Primary adrenal insufficiency"],"comps":["NE-17","NE-20-E2"]}},{"id":"NE-C23-Q3","stem":"His MRI changes reflect destruction of myelin within the cerebral white matter. The cells that formed this myelin differ from the myelinating cells of his peripheral nerves in which of the following ways?","options":["Each cell arises from the neural crest","Each cell myelinates one internode of one axon","Each cell is covered by a basal lamina","Each cell myelinates internodes on many axons"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-2","obj":"NE-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the myelinating cell of the brain white matter: the oligodendrocyte.","Compare it with the Schwann cell of peripheral nerve.","Oligodendrocytes extend many processes, each wrapping one internode on a different axon."],"why":"An oligodendrocyte sends out many processes, and each one wraps an internode on a separate axon, so one cell myelinates segments of many axons. A Schwann cell wraps a single internode of a single axon and is covered by a basal lamina that guides regrowth after injury. Oligodendrocytes come from neuroectoderm, while Schwann cells come from neural crest. Oligodendrocyte injury underlies multiple sclerosis, PML, and the leukodystrophies, while Schwann cell injury underlies Guillain-Barré syndrome.","whyNot":["Schwann cells arise from neural crest. Oligodendrocytes arise from neuroectoderm of the neural tube.","This describes a Schwann cell in a peripheral nerve.","Schwann cells have a basal lamina, which helps guide axon regrowth. Oligodendrocytes do not.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Oligodendrocytes versus Schwann cells","CNS and PNS myelin"],"comps":["NE-2","NE-1"]}},{"id":"NE-C23-Q4","stem":"His ACTH is driven by a hypothalamic hormone that reaches the anterior pituitary through the hypophyseal portal system. If this portal connection were cut by a transection of the pituitary stalk, the serum concentration of which of the following anterior pituitary hormones would most likely rise?","options":["Prolactin","Thyrotropin","Growth hormone","Corticotropin"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-17","obj":"NE-17.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that hypothalamic hormones reach the anterior pituitary only through portal capillaries in the stalk.","Most of these hormones are stimulatory (CRH, TRH, GnRH, GHRH), so cutting the stalk lowers the hormones they drive.","Prolactin is held down mainly by dopamine, so loss of portal dopamine lets prolactin rise."],"why":"Hypothalamic neurons release their hormones into a capillary bed in the median eminence. Portal veins carry this blood down the stalk to a second capillary bed in the anterior pituitary. CRH, TRH, GnRH, and GHRH stimulate their target cells, while somatostatin inhibits growth hormone and TSH. Prolactin is unique because its main control is inhibitory, from dopamine made in the arcuate nucleus. Cutting or compressing the stalk removes dopamine, so prolactin rises while the other anterior pituitary hormones fall.","whyNot":["Correct.","TSH depends on TRH. Losing portal flow would lower it.","Growth hormone depends mainly on GHRH. Losing portal flow would lower it.","ACTH depends on CRH from the hypothalamus. Losing portal flow would lower it."],"study":{"topics":["Hypophyseal portal system","Dopamine control of prolactin"],"comps":["NE-17","NE-12"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C24","system":"NE","dx":"Phenylketonuria (untreated, missed newborn screen)","codes":["NE-20-E3"],"case":"A 14-month-old girl is brought to the physician because she cannot sit without support and has had several seizures. She was born at home and had no newborn screening. Her parents are first cousins. Examination shows a head circumference below the 3rd percentile, eczema on her cheeks and arms, and hair and skin much lighter than those of her parents and siblings. Her urine and sweat have a musty odor. She does not babble or reach for toys. Plasma phenylalanine is 1,450 μmol/L (reference less than 120 μmol/L), and plasma tyrosine is low-normal. Phenylalanine hydroxylase gene testing shows two pathogenic variants. Diagnosis: phenylketonuria.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C24-Q1","stem":"She is started on a phenylalanine-restricted diet. At the age of 24 years, she stops the diet and becomes pregnant. Her plasma phenylalanine during pregnancy is 1,100 μmol/L. Which of the following is most likely in her infant?","options":["Neonatal hypoglycemia from fetal hyperinsulinism","Neural tube defect from folate antagonism","Microcephaly from fetal phenylalanine exposure","Musty odor from fetal phenylalanine hydroxylase loss"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-E3","obj":"NE-20-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize maternal phenylketonuria: the mother's high phenylalanine crosses the placenta.","The fetus is exposed to toxic levels whether or not it has PKU itself.","The main effects are microcephaly, intellectual disability, growth restriction, and congenital heart disease."],"why":"Phenylalanine is actively carried across the placenta, so fetal levels run even higher than the mother's. High levels harm the developing brain and heart. Infants of mothers with untreated PKU often have microcephaly, intellectual disability, low birth weight, and heart defects, even though most of them are only carriers. Restarting the diet before conception and keeping levels low through pregnancy prevents this. PKU itself is autosomal recessive loss of phenylalanine hydroxylase or its cofactor BH4, which makes tyrosine an essential amino acid.","whyNot":["Neonatal hypoglycemia from fetal hyperinsulinism occurs in infants of mothers with poorly controlled diabetes.","Neural tube defects follow folate deficiency or folate antagonists such as valproate. High phenylalanine damages the brain and heart instead.","Correct.","The infant is most likely a carrier with one working allele, so it can clear phenylalanine after birth. The harm comes from exposure in the womb."],"study":{"topics":["Maternal phenylketonuria","Phenylketonuria genetics"],"comps":["NE-20-E3","NE-1"]}},{"id":"NE-C24-Q2","stem":"A second infant has a similar plasma phenylalanine level but normal phenylalanine hydroxylase activity. He has deficient dihydropteridine reductase, which regenerates tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4). His cerebrospinal fluid shows low homovanillic acid. Which of the following enzymes in catecholamine synthesis is most directly impaired in this infant?","options":["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase","Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-15","obj":"NE-15.1","dok":3,"approach":["Low homovanillic acid means low dopamine production.","Find the catecholamine enzyme that, like phenylalanine hydroxylase, uses BH4.","Tyrosine hydroxylase converts tyrosine to L-DOPA, the rate-limiting step, and needs BH4."],"why":"Catecholamine synthesis runs tyrosine to L-DOPA (tyrosine hydroxylase, rate limiting, BH4 dependent), L-DOPA to dopamine (aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase, pyridoxal phosphate dependent), dopamine to norepinephrine (dopamine beta-hydroxylase, vitamin C and copper dependent), and norepinephrine to epinephrine (PNMT in the adrenal medulla, induced by cortisol). Phenylalanine hydroxylase, tyrosine hydroxylase, and tryptophan hydroxylase all share BH4. So BH4 defects cause high phenylalanine plus low dopamine and serotonin, and a low-phenylalanine diet alone does not treat them.","whyNot":["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase uses vitamin C and copper, not BH4. Its loss would leave dopamine and homovanillic acid normal or high.","Correct.","PNMT uses S-adenosylmethionine to make epinephrine from norepinephrine and is induced by cortisol. It does not need BH4.","This enzyme uses pyridoxal phosphate, not BH4."],"study":{"topics":["Catecholamine synthesis","Tetrahydrobiopterin-dependent enzymes"],"comps":["NE-15","NE-20-E3"]}},{"id":"NE-C24-Q3","stem":"Before treatment, her cerebrospinal fluid concentration of 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid was well below the reference range. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Increased monoamine oxidase activity in serotonergic terminals","Reduced conversion of tryptophan to niacin in the liver","Diversion of serotonin to melatonin in the pineal gland","Less tryptophan reaching raphe neurons for hydroxylation"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-15","obj":"NE-15.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid as the breakdown product of serotonin made by monoamine oxidase.","A low level means less serotonin is being made in the brain.","High phenylalanine competes with tryptophan for transport into the brain and inhibits tryptophan hydroxylase in the raphe nuclei."],"why":"Brain serotonin is made in the raphe nuclei. Tryptophan hydroxylase converts tryptophan to 5-hydroxytryptophan, which is then decarboxylated to serotonin. Monoamine oxidase breaks serotonin down to 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid, so the CSF level tracks serotonin turnover. In untreated PKU, phenylalanine crowds tryptophan off the shared large neutral amino acid carrier at the blood-brain barrier and also inhibits tryptophan hydroxylase, so less serotonin is made. The pineal gland converts a small share of serotonin to melatonin at night.","whyNot":["More monoamine oxidase activity would raise 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid, not lower it.","Tryptophan conversion to niacin happens in the liver and does not set brain serotonin levels.","The pineal gland uses only a tiny fraction of brain serotonin to make melatonin. This could not drain the whole pool.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Serotonin synthesis and breakdown","Brain amino acid transport in PKU"],"comps":["NE-15","NE-20-E3"]}},{"id":"NE-C24-Q4","stem":"Her mother describes the seizures. Each begins with rhythmic jerking of the right hand that spreads up the right arm over about 20 seconds. She then stops responding, and all four limbs stiffen and jerk for about 1 minute. Afterward she is sleepy for an hour. She has no fever with these episodes. Which of the following best describes these seizures?","options":["Generalized absence seizures","Simple febrile seizures","Focal to bilateral tonic-clonic","Generalized tonic-clonic from onset"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-13","obj":"NE-13.6","dok":2,"approach":["Find where the seizure starts: one hand, so it begins in one area of the left motor cortex.","Follow its spread: it moves along the motor strip, then involves both hemispheres with loss of awareness and tonic-clonic activity.","Classify by onset, then by spread: focal onset evolving to bilateral tonic-clonic."],"why":"A seizure is excessive, synchronous firing of a group of neurons when excitation outweighs inhibition. Focal seizures start in one network of one hemisphere, so the first sign points to the starting area, here the hand region of the left motor cortex. Spread along the motor strip gives a march of jerking up the limb. When the discharge spreads to both hemispheres, the patient loses awareness and has a tonic-clonic phase, followed by postictal drowsiness. Generalized seizures involve both hemispheres from the start.","whyNot":["Absence seizures are brief staring spells of a few seconds with 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharges, no aura, and no postictal state.","Simple febrile seizures occur with fever in children 6 months to 5 years old and are generalized from the start. She has no fever and a focal onset.","Correct.","Seizures that are generalized from onset begin with loss of awareness in both hemispheres at once. Her seizures start in one hand and spread, which marks a focal onset."],"study":{"topics":["Seizure classification","Focal seizures with spread"],"comps":["NE-13","NE-20-E3"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C25","system":"NE","dx":"Tay-Sachs disease","codes":["NE-20-E4"],"case":"An 8-month-old boy is brought to the physician because he has stopped rolling over and no longer reaches for toys. He developed normally until the age of 5 months. He startles strongly at ordinary household sounds. Both parents are healthy and are of Ashkenazi Jewish descent. Examination shows generalized hypotonia, poor head control, and brisk reflexes. Funduscopic examination shows a cherry-red spot in the center of each macula. The liver and spleen are not enlarged. Leukocyte hexosaminidase A activity is markedly reduced. Diagnosis: Tay-Sachs disease.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C25-Q1","stem":"A 6-month-old girl also has developmental regression and a cherry-red spot in each macula. She has marked hepatosplenomegaly, and a bone marrow aspirate shows lipid-laden foam cells. Deficiency of which of the following enzymes is most likely in this girl?","options":["Glucocerebrosidase","Hexosaminidase A","Galactocerebrosidase","Sphingomyelinase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-E4","obj":"NE-20-E.4","dok":3,"approach":["A cherry-red macula with regression narrows the choice to Tay-Sachs or Niemann-Pick disease.","Hepatosplenomegaly and foam cells mean lipid is stored in macrophages of the liver, spleen, and marrow.","That pattern fits Niemann-Pick disease, from sphingomyelinase deficiency."],"why":"In both Tay-Sachs and Niemann-Pick type A disease, lipid stored in retinal ganglion cells around the fovea makes the macula look pale, and the fovea, which has no ganglion cells, stands out as a cherry-red spot. Tay-Sachs stores GM2 ganglioside mainly in neurons, so the liver and spleen stay normal size. Niemann-Pick type A stores sphingomyelin in neurons and in macrophages throughout the body, which gives hepatosplenomegaly and foamy marrow cells. Both are autosomal recessive and more common in people of Ashkenazi Jewish descent.","whyNot":["Glucocerebrosidase deficiency is Gaucher disease. It causes hepatosplenomegaly with crumpled tissue-paper macrophages but not a cherry-red spot.","Hexosaminidase A deficiency is Tay-Sachs disease, as in the first infant, which spares the liver and spleen.","Galactocerebrosidase deficiency is Krabbe disease, an infantile leukodystrophy with globoid cells and peripheral neuropathy, without organ enlargement.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tay-Sachs versus Niemann-Pick disease","Cherry-red macula"],"comps":["NE-20-E4","NE-14"]}},{"id":"NE-C25-Q2","stem":"Electron microscopy of his cortical neurons shows lysosomes packed with concentric layers of membrane. Which of the following best explains the formation of this stored material?","options":["Blocked stepwise lysosomal breakdown of a sphingolipid","Impaired mitochondrial oxidation of myelin fatty acids","Excess ganglioside synthesis in the endoplasmic reticulum","Failed peroxisomal oxidation of very long chain fatty acids"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-14","obj":"NE-14.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the storage site: lysosomes, so the problem is a failed lysosomal enzyme.","Recall that sphingolipids are broken down one sugar or group at a time, each step by its own lysosomal enzyme.","When one step fails, the substrate for that step piles up in membrane whorls."],"why":"Gangliosides, cerebrosides, sulfatides, and sphingomyelin share a ceramide backbone and are broken down in lysosomes by a series of enzymes that each remove one piece. Synthesis is normal in these diseases. When one enzyme is missing, its substrate builds up as layered membrane whorls, and neurons swell and die. Myelin is rich in cholesterol, galactocerebroside, sulfatide, and sphingomyelin, so defects in their breakdown cause leukodystrophies, while ganglioside defects such as Tay-Sachs mainly damage neuronal cell bodies.","whyNot":["Correct.","Mitochondrial fatty acid oxidation defects cause fasting hypoglycemia and muscle disease, not lysosomal membrane whorls.","Synthesis is normal in the sphingolipidoses. The problem is breakdown, which is why reducing synthesis is a treatment strategy.","Failed peroxisomal oxidation of these fatty acids is X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy. The material is not stored in lysosomes."],"study":{"topics":["Sphingolipid breakdown","Lipid composition of myelin"],"comps":["NE-14","NE-20-E4"]}},{"id":"NE-C25-Q3","stem":"On light microscopy, his neuronal cell bodies are ballooned with stored lipid, and the normal basophilic Nissl substance is pushed to the edge of the cell. Which of the following best describes Nissl substance in a normal multipolar motor neuron?","options":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum absent from the axon hillock","Golgi stacks concentrated in the initial axon segment","Lysosome clusters filling the dendritic spines","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum concentrated in axon terminals"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-2","obj":"NE-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall what makes Nissl substance basophilic: RNA in ribosomes.","Nissl bodies are stacks of rough endoplasmic reticulum with free ribosomes.","They fill the soma and proximal dendrites but stop at the axon hillock, which is how the hillock is found on a slide."],"why":"Neurons make large amounts of protein, so their soma and proximal dendrites are full of rough endoplasmic reticulum and ribosomes that stain blue with basic dyes. These are Nissl bodies. The axon hillock and axon lack them, so proteins needed in the axon are made in the soma and moved by axonal transport. A multipolar motor neuron has one axon and many dendrites. Dorsal root ganglion neurons are pseudounipolar, with one process that splits, and retinal and olfactory neurons are bipolar.","whyNot":["Correct.","The Golgi apparatus sits in the soma near the nucleus. The initial axon segment is free of Nissl substance and holds clustered sodium channels.","Lysosomes are not basophilic, and dendritic spines contain actin and receptor clusters, not lysosome masses.","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum lacks ribosomes and does not stain blue. Axon terminals hold synaptic vesicles and mitochondria."],"study":{"topics":["Neuron structure","Nissl substance and axon hillock"],"comps":["NE-2","NE-13"]}},{"id":"NE-C25-Q4","stem":"A 31-year-old man has had burning pain in his hands and feet since childhood, reduced sweating, and clusters of small dark red papules between the umbilicus and knees. He has proteinuria and has just had an ischemic stroke. His maternal grandfather had kidney failure. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his disease?","options":["Autosomal recessive alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency","Autosomal recessive glucocerebrosidase deficiency","X-linked alpha-galactosidase A deficiency","X-linked iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-E4","obj":"NE-20-E.5","dok":3,"approach":["Group the clues: painful small fiber neuropathy, angiokeratomas, poor sweating, kidney disease, and early stroke.","This set is Fabry disease, where globotriaosylceramide builds up in nerves, vessel walls, and kidneys.","Fabry disease is X-linked, which fits disease passed through his mother's side."],"why":"Fabry disease is an X-linked deficiency of alpha-galactosidase A. Globotriaosylceramide builds up in small nerve fibers, sweat glands, endothelium, kidney, and heart. This gives burning pain in the hands and feet, reduced sweating, angiokeratomas in the bathing-trunk area, proteinuria leading to kidney failure, cardiomyopathy, and early stroke. Gaucher types 2 and 3 involve the brain. Hurler (alpha-L-iduronidase, autosomal recessive) causes corneal clouding, while Hunter (iduronate-2-sulfatase, X-linked) does not.","whyNot":["This is Hurler syndrome, with coarse facial features, corneal clouding, and developmental decline in early childhood.","Glucocerebrosidase deficiency is Gaucher disease, with hepatosplenomegaly, bone pain, and cytopenias. Neuronopathic types 2 and 3 cause brainstem signs, not painful neuropathy with angiokeratomas.","Correct.","This is Hunter syndrome, an X-linked mucopolysaccharidosis with coarse facial features, joint stiffness, and in severe forms behavior problems, without corneal clouding."],"study":{"topics":["Fabry disease","Mucopolysaccharidoses and Gaucher disease"],"comps":["NE-20-E4","NE-20-A"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C26","system":"NE","dx":"Wilson disease with neuropsychiatric presentation","codes":["NE-20-E5"],"case":"A 19-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her parents because of 6 months of irritability, impulsive spending, and falling grades. She now has a tremor of both arms that is worst when she holds them out, slurred speech, and drooling. Examination shows a coarse tremor of the outstretched arms, mild dystonic posturing of the left hand, and dysarthria. Slit-lamp examination shows a golden-brown ring at the edge of each cornea. Serum ceruloplasmin is 8 mg/dL (reference 20 to 50 mg/dL), 24-hour urine copper is 210 μg (reference less than 40 μg), and ALT is 78 U/L (reference 7 to 35 U/L). MRI shows bilateral T2 hyperintensity of the putamen. Diagnosis: Wilson disease.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C26-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the copper accumulation in her tissues?","options":["Excess copper absorption by intestinal enterocytes","Failed hepatocyte copper excretion into bile","Autoantibodies that clear ceruloplasmin","Renal loss of copper-bound ceruloplasmin"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-E5","obj":"NE-20-E.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that bile is the main route for removing copper from the body.","Wilson disease is autosomal recessive loss of ATP7B, the copper-transporting ATPase of hepatocytes.","Without it, copper is neither excreted into bile nor loaded onto ceruloplasmin, so copper builds up in the liver and then spills into brain, cornea, and other tissues."],"why":"ATP7B moves copper into the secretory pathway of hepatocytes, where it is loaded onto apoceruloplasmin and excreted into bile. When ATP7B fails, copper accumulates in the liver, causing hepatitis and cirrhosis. Free copper then leaks into the blood and deposits in the basal ganglia (tremor, dystonia, dysarthria, psychiatric change), the cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings), and the kidney. Apoceruloplasmin without copper is unstable, so serum ceruloplasmin is low, while urine copper rises.","whyNot":["Intestinal copper absorption is normal in Wilson disease. Defective copper transport out of enterocytes by ATP7A causes Menkes disease, which leads to copper deficiency.","Correct.","Wilson disease is not autoimmune. Ceruloplasmin is low because it is not loaded with copper and is broken down faster.","Protein loss in nephrotic syndrome can lower ceruloplasmin, but it does not cause copper deposits in the brain or cornea."],"study":{"topics":["Wilson disease pathogenesis","Copper handling by the liver"],"comps":["NE-20-E5","NE-3"]}},{"id":"NE-C26-Q2","stem":"Before her diagnosis, her physician considered another inherited disorder that causes psychiatric change and neuropathy in young adults. In that disorder, attacks bring severe abdominal pain, weakness, and anxiety, often after fasting or starting phenobarbital, without skin photosensitivity, and the urine darkens on standing in light. It is often inherited from one parent. Deficiency of which of the following enzymes causes that disorder?","options":["Ferrochelatase","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase","Porphobilinogen deaminase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-E","obj":"NE-20-E.9","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize acute attacks of abdominal pain, neuropathy, and psychiatric symptoms triggered by fasting or drugs as acute intermittent porphyria.","No photosensitivity means the porphyrin rings are not building up, so the block is early in heme synthesis.","Dominant inheritance and buildup of porphobilinogen point to porphobilinogen deaminase."],"why":"Acute intermittent porphyria is autosomal dominant partial deficiency of porphobilinogen deaminase (hydroxymethylbilane synthase). Triggers such as alcohol, fasting, and cytochrome P450 inducers like phenobarbital increase ALA synthase activity, so aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen pile up behind the block. These precursors are neurotoxic, causing abdominal pain, neuropathy, and psychiatric symptoms. Urine porphobilinogen is high and urine darkens in light. Treatment uses glucose and hemin, which suppress ALA synthase.","whyNot":["Ferrochelatase deficiency causes erythropoietic protoporphyria with painful skin photosensitivity. Lead also inhibits this enzyme.","This enzyme is deficient in porphyria cutanea tarda, which causes blistering skin on sun-exposed areas without neurologic attacks.","Lead poisoning inhibits this enzyme, and a rare recessive deficiency exists, but the dominant pattern and high porphobilinogen fit acute intermittent porphyria.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acute intermittent porphyria","Heme synthesis"],"comps":["NE-20-E","NE-20-D2"]}},{"id":"NE-C26-Q3","stem":"In a patient who died of untreated Wilson disease, the putamen shows loss of neurons, which have been replaced by a dense meshwork of cell processes that stain strongly for glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP). Which of the following is another normal function of the cell type that formed this meshwork?","options":["Lining of the ventricular cavities","Phagocytosis as resident CNS macrophages","Uptake of extracellular potassium","Formation of myelin around CNS axons"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-2","obj":"NE-2.4","dok":3,"approach":["GFAP is the intermediate filament of astrocytes, so the meshwork is a glial scar formed by reactive astrocytes.","List the other jobs of astrocytes: supporting the blood-brain barrier with their end-feet, buffering potassium, and recycling transmitters.","Match one of these to the options."],"why":"When neurons die, astrocytes enlarge and lay down dense GFAP-filled processes, forming a glial scar (gliosis). In the healthy brain, astrocytes take up the potassium released by firing neurons and spread it through gap junctions, which keeps extracellular potassium stable. Their end-feet surround capillaries and support the tight junctions of the blood-brain barrier. They also take up glutamate and convert it to glutamine. In Wilson disease and liver failure, some astrocytes become swollen Alzheimer type II cells.","whyNot":["Ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal, and modified ependymal cells form the choroid plexus epithelium.","Microglia are the resident macrophages of the CNS. They derive from mesoderm and do not express GFAP.","Correct.","Oligodendrocytes form CNS myelin. They do not form glial scars."],"study":{"topics":["Astrocyte functions","Gliosis"],"comps":["NE-2","NE-13"]}},{"id":"NE-C26-Q4","stem":"Her parents describe her as previously cautious and organized. Over the past year she has made reckless purchases, made crude remarks to strangers, and shown little concern about failing her classes. Dysfunction of which of the following cortical regions best explains these behavioral changes?","options":["Superior parietal lobule","Orbitofrontal cortex","Superior temporal gyrus","Precentral gyrus"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-11","obj":"NE-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Group the changes: disinhibition, poor judgment, and lack of concern are personality and executive changes.","These functions belong to the prefrontal cortex, especially its orbitofrontal part.","The basal ganglia, damaged in her disease, form loops with the prefrontal cortex, so striatal injury can produce frontal-type behavior."],"why":"The prefrontal cortex handles planning, judgment, impulse control, and social behavior. Orbitofrontal damage causes disinhibition and socially inappropriate behavior, while dorsolateral and medial damage cause poor planning and apathy. The caudate and putamen are part of loops that run from the prefrontal cortex through the basal ganglia and thalamus and back, so diseases of the striatum such as Wilson and Huntington disease often start with frontal-type behavior change. Frontotemporal dementia produces the same picture from direct frontal lobe degeneration.","whyNot":["Superior parietal damage disturbs spatial awareness and visually guided reaching, not personality.","Correct.","The superior temporal gyrus holds auditory cortex and, on the left, Wernicke area. Damage causes hearing or language comprehension problems.","Precentral gyrus damage causes weakness on the opposite side, not behavior change."],"study":{"topics":["Prefrontal cortex functions","Frontal-subcortical circuits"],"comps":["NE-11","NE-3"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C27","system":"NE","dx":"Lumbosacral myelomeningocele with Chiari II malformation and hydrocephalus","codes":["NE-20-C2","NE-1"],"case":"A newborn girl is examined shortly after delivery at 39 weeks' gestation. Her 23-year-old mother had no prenatal care and took no vitamins. A 4-cm open sac over the lower lumbar and sacral spine contains exposed neural tissue and leaks clear fluid. Both legs are flaccid with no movement, and the anal wink is absent. Her head circumference is 38 cm (above the 97th percentile), and the anterior fontanelle is full. MRI shows downward displacement of the cerebellar vermis and medulla through the foramen magnum, an elongated fourth ventricle, and enlarged lateral and third ventricles. Diagnosis: lumbosacral myelomeningocele with Chiari II malformation and hydrocephalus.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C27-Q1","stem":"Failure of which of the following developmental events most directly caused her spinal lesion?","options":["Segmentation of paraxial mesoderm into somites in week 5","Closure of the caudal neuropore in week 4","Closure of the cranial neuropore in week 4","Notochord induction of the neural plate in week 3"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-1","obj":"NE-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["An open sac with exposed neural tissue in the lumbosacral region is an open neural tube defect at the lower end.","Recall that the neural tube closes like a zipper from the middle, and the two ends close last.","The caudal (posterior) neuropore closes at about day 28, near the end of week 4."],"why":"In week 3, the notochord induces the overlying ectoderm to thicken into the neural plate. The edges rise as neural folds and fuse in the midline, starting near the future neck and moving in both directions. The cranial neuropore closes at about day 25 and the caudal neuropore at about day 28. Failure of the caudal neuropore leaves the lower cord open, giving a myelomeningocele. Failure of the cranial neuropore gives anencephaly. Both happen before many people know they are pregnant.","whyNot":["Somite segmentation errors cause vertebral malformations such as hemivertebrae, not an open neural tube.","Correct.","Failure of the cranial neuropore causes anencephaly, not a lumbosacral defect.","Failed induction of the neural plate would prevent the nervous system from forming. It is not the cause of a localized open lumbosacral defect."],"study":{"topics":["Neurulation","Neuropore closure timing"],"comps":["NE-1","NE-20-C2"]}},{"id":"NE-C27-Q2","stem":"Her mother plans another pregnancy in 2 years. Which of the following measures would most reduce the risk that a future child has a similar defect?","options":["Vitamin B12 supplements throughout pregnancy","Iron supplements throughout the first trimester","High-dose folic acid starting before conception","High-dose folic acid starting at 12 weeks of gestation"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-1","obj":"NE-1.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that low folate before and in early pregnancy raises the risk of open neural tube defects.","The neural tube closes by the end of week 4, often before pregnancy is recognized.","Folic acid must already be in place when the tube is closing, so it has to start before conception."],"why":"Folate supplies one-carbon units for nucleotide synthesis, which rapidly dividing neural tissue needs during neurulation. Women who have had a child with a neural tube defect are advised to take high-dose folic acid (about 4 mg a day) starting at least a month before conception and through the first trimester. All women who could become pregnant are advised to take about 0.4 mg a day. Open defects such as myelomeningocele and anencephaly leak alpha-fetoprotein into the amniotic fluid and maternal serum, so a high AFP is a screening clue. Spina bifida occulta is closed and does not raise AFP.","whyNot":["B12 is needed to recycle folate, but supplementation is not the standard way to prevent recurrence of neural tube defects.","Iron prevents anemia in pregnancy but does not affect neural tube closure.","Correct.","By 12 weeks the neural tube closed weeks earlier, so starting folic acid then is too late to prevent this defect."],"study":{"topics":["Folate and neural tube defects","Alpha-fetoprotein screening"],"comps":["NE-1","NE-14"]}},{"id":"NE-C27-Q3","stem":"A 32-year-old woman has occipital headaches that worsen with coughing and straining. Examination shows loss of pain and temperature sensation over both shoulders and upper arms, with normal touch and position sense. Which of the following findings is most likely on her MRI?","options":["Tonsils below the foramen magnum with a cervical syrinx","Stenosis of the cerebral aqueduct with enlarged third ventricle","Vermis agenesis with cystic enlargement of the fourth ventricle","Herniation of vermis and medulla with an elongated fourth ventricle"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-1","obj":"NE-1.6","dok":3,"approach":["Cape-like loss of pain and temperature with spared touch points to a syrinx damaging the crossing spinothalamic fibers in the cervical cord.","Headache worse with coughing in an adult suggests crowding at the foramen magnum.","Together these fit Chiari I malformation, which is often linked to syringomyelia."],"why":"Chiari I malformation is downward displacement of the cerebellar tonsils below the foramen magnum. It often presents in adults with occipital headache worsened by coughing or straining. It disturbs CSF flow and is commonly associated with a cervical syrinx that damages the fibers crossing in the anterior white commissure, causing cape-like loss of pain and temperature. Chiari II, as in the infant, involves the vermis and medulla, presents at birth, and comes with lumbosacral myelomeningocele and hydrocephalus. Dandy-Walker malformation is vermis agenesis with a cystic fourth ventricle.","whyNot":["Correct.","Aqueductal stenosis causes noncommunicating hydrocephalus with headache and papilledema, but it does not explain cape-like sensory loss.","This is Dandy-Walker malformation, which presents in infancy with an enlarging head and hydrocephalus.","This is Chiari II malformation, seen in infants with lumbosacral myelomeningocele."],"study":{"topics":["Chiari I, Chiari II, and Dandy-Walker malformations","Syringomyelia"],"comps":["NE-1","NE-7"]}},{"id":"NE-C27-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains her enlarged head and full anterior fontanelle?","options":["Overproduction of CSF by a choroid plexus tumor","Communicating hydrocephalus from scarred arachnoid granulations","Hydrocephalus ex vacuo from loss of brain tissue","Noncommunicating hydrocephalus in a skull that can expand"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-C2","obj":"NE-20-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["In Chiari II, the herniated hindbrain blocks outflow from the fourth ventricle, and aqueductal stenosis is common.","A block inside the ventricular system is noncommunicating hydrocephalus.","In an infant the sutures are not fused, so rising pressure enlarges the head and makes the fontanelle full."],"why":"Hydrocephalus is too much CSF with enlarged ventricles. In noncommunicating hydrocephalus, flow is blocked within the ventricles, as with aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II, Dandy-Walker malformation, or a tumor. In communicating hydrocephalus, CSF reaches the subarachnoid space but is poorly absorbed, often after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage scars the arachnoid granulations. Before the sutures fuse, raised pressure spreads the skull bones, so head circumference grows quickly. In older children and adults the skull is rigid, so pressure rises and causes headache, vomiting, and papilledema.","whyNot":["A choroid plexus papilloma can overproduce CSF, but her MRI shows a hindbrain malformation and no tumor.","Arachnoid granulation scarring follows meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage. Neither is present here, and her block is within the ventricles.","Hydrocephalus ex vacuo is ventricular enlargement after brain tissue loss, as in Alzheimer disease. Pressure is normal, and the head does not enlarge.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Types of hydrocephalus","Hydrocephalus in infants"],"comps":["NE-20-C2","NE-4"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C28","system":"NE","dx":"Medulloblastoma of the cerebellar vermis with obstructive hydrocephalus","codes":["NE-20-F1","NE-20-C2"],"case":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of 3 weeks of morning headaches and vomiting and a week of frequent falls. His temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 52/min, respirations are 10/min and irregular, and blood pressure is 156/94 mm Hg. Examination shows bilateral papilledema and a wide-based, unsteady gait. MRI shows a 4-cm enhancing midline mass arising from the cerebellar vermis and filling the fourth ventricle, with obstructive hydrocephalus. After urgent CSF diversion, the mass is resected. Microscopy shows sheets of small cells with scant cytoplasm and dark nuclei, some arranged in rings around central fibrillary material. Diagnosis: medulloblastoma.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C28-Q1","stem":"As part of staging, MRI of the entire spine and lumbar CSF cytology are obtained. Which of the following best explains why these studies are needed?","options":["Hematogenous spread to the vertebral marrow","Lymphatic spread to the cervical lymph nodes","Seeding through the CSF onto spinal meninges","Direct invasion down the spinal cord parenchyma"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-F1","obj":"NE-20-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that medulloblastoma grows into the fourth ventricle and is bathed in CSF.","Tumor cells shed into the CSF and settle on the leptomeninges of the spinal cord and cauda equina (drop metastases).","Staging therefore needs imaging of the whole neuraxis and CSF cytology."],"why":"Medulloblastoma is the most common malignant brain tumor of childhood. It is an embryonal small round blue cell tumor of the cerebellum, usually the vermis, and Homer Wright rosettes (tumor cells around a core of fibrils) are typical. Because it projects into the fourth ventricle, it can block CSF flow and shed cells into the CSF. These cells seed the spinal leptomeninges as drop metastases, which changes treatment to include craniospinal radiation. It is highly radiosensitive.","whyNot":["Spread outside the CNS is uncommon. Spinal disease comes from CSF seeding, not blood-borne marrow metastasis.","Brain tumors rarely spread through lymphatics to lymph nodes, so nodal disease is not the staging concern.","Correct.","Medulloblastoma does not grow down through cord tissue. It reaches the spine by floating in the CSF."],"study":{"topics":["Medulloblastoma","Drop metastases"],"comps":["NE-20-F1","NE-4"]}},{"id":"NE-C28-Q2","stem":"Before surgery, the main alternative diagnosis was the other common posterior fossa tumor of children, which often appears as a cyst with an enhancing mural nodule in a cerebellar hemisphere. Which of the following pairs of findings is typical of that tumor?","options":["Fried-egg cells with 1p/19q codeletion","Psammoma bodies with NF2 gene loss","Pseudopalisading necrosis with wildtype IDH","Rosenthal fibers with a BRAF alteration"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-F4","obj":"NE-20-F.5","dok":3,"approach":["A cystic cerebellar tumor with a mural nodule in a child is a pilocytic astrocytoma.","Recall its histology: piloid (hair-like) astrocytes, Rosenthal fibers, and eosinophilic granular bodies.","Recall its genetics: an activating BRAF alteration, most often a KIAA1549-BRAF fusion."],"why":"Pilocytic astrocytoma is the most common brain tumor of children overall. It is usually a well-circumscribed cystic tumor with a mural nodule in the cerebellum, and it has a good prognosis after resection. Microscopy shows bipolar hair-like astrocytes and Rosenthal fibers, which are dense eosinophilic GFAP-rich bodies. Most carry a BRAF alteration that activates MAPK signaling. Diffuse gliomas in adults differ: oligodendroglioma is IDH-mutant with 1p/19q codeletion and fried-egg cells, often in the frontal lobe with seizures and calcification.","whyNot":["These define oligodendroglioma, a diffuse glioma of adults that usually arises in the frontal lobe and presents with seizures.","These fit meningioma, a dural-based tumor of adults, often women. Bilateral vestibular schwannomas and meningiomas suggest NF2.","These fit glioblastoma, the most common primary malignant brain tumor of older adults, usually in the cerebral hemispheres.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pilocytic astrocytoma","Glioma molecular markers"],"comps":["NE-20-F4","NE-16"]}},{"id":"NE-C28-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains his slow pulse at presentation?","options":["Baroreflex slowing after a sympathetic pressor surge","Direct compression of the vagus nerve by the tumor","Hypercapnia depressing the sinoatrial node","Reduced venous return from raised intrathoracic pressure"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-10","obj":"NE-10.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the Cushing triad: hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular breathing with raised intracranial pressure.","Cerebral perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, so a high ICP lowers perfusion of the brainstem.","The ischemic brainstem triggers a sympathetic surge that raises blood pressure, and the baroreceptors then slow the heart through the vagus."],"why":"As intracranial pressure rises, cerebral perfusion pressure (MAP minus ICP) falls. The brainstem responds with intense sympathetic output that raises arterial pressure and restores perfusion. Carotid sinus and aortic baroreceptors sense the high pressure and increase vagal output to the heart, slowing it. Compression of the medulla also makes breathing irregular. Normally, autoregulation keeps cerebral blood flow steady across a wide range of pressures, and PaCO2 changes vessel tone, with high CO2 dilating and low CO2 constricting cerebral arterioles.","whyNot":["Correct.","Compression of the vagus nerve would interrupt vagal output and tend to speed the heart. Here the slowing is a reflex response to the high blood pressure.","Hypercapnia dilates cerebral vessels and raises ICP further, but it does not explain the combination of bradycardia with marked hypertension.","Low venous return would lower blood pressure and speed the heart. His blood pressure is high."],"study":{"topics":["Cushing reflex","Cerebral perfusion pressure"],"comps":["NE-10","NE-20-C1"]}},{"id":"NE-C28-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sets of CSF spaces is most likely dilated by the obstruction in this patient?","options":["One lateral ventricle only","Lateral and third ventricles only","Lateral and third ventricles plus the aqueduct","All four ventricles and the subarachnoid cisterns"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-10","obj":"NE-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["Trace CSF flow: lateral ventricles, interventricular foramina, third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle, then the foramina of Luschka and Magendie.","Locate the block: the tumor fills the fourth ventricle.","Every space upstream of the block dilates."],"why":"CSF is made mainly by the choroid plexus in the lateral ventricles. It passes through the interventricular foramina of Monro to the third ventricle, down the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle, and out through the median foramen of Magendie and the lateral foramina of Luschka into the subarachnoid space. A mass filling the fourth ventricle blocks flow at that level, so the lateral ventricles, third ventricle, and aqueduct all enlarge. Aqueductal stenosis spares the fourth ventricle, and a block at one foramen of Monro enlarges only one lateral ventricle.","whyNot":["Only one lateral ventricle dilates when one interventricular foramen is blocked, as by a colloid cyst or other mass at the foramen.","This pattern fits aqueductal stenosis. With the block in the fourth ventricle, the aqueduct also dilates.","Correct.","Enlargement of all four ventricles fits communicating hydrocephalus or a block at the fourth ventricle outflow foramina."],"study":{"topics":["CSF circulation","Level of obstruction in hydrocephalus"],"comps":["NE-10","NE-20-C2"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C29","system":"NE","dx":"Adrenal neuroblastoma","codes":["NE-20-F3"],"case":"A 2-year-old girl is brought to the physician because her parents felt a hard lump in her abdomen while bathing her. She has had poor appetite, weight loss, and bruising around both eyes for 3 weeks. Examination shows a firm, irregular, nontender mass in the left upper quadrant that extends past the midline, and bilateral periorbital ecchymoses. CT shows a 9-cm left adrenal mass with calcifications that surrounds the aorta. Urine homovanillic acid and vanillylmandelic acid are elevated. Biopsy shows sheets of small round blue cells with scattered rosettes around central fibrillary material. Diagnosis: neuroblastoma of the left adrenal gland.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C29-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in her tumor would most strongly predict aggressive behavior and a poor prognosis?","options":["Codeletion of chromosome arms 1p and 19q","Amplification of the MYCN oncogene","Activating fusion of the BRAF gene","Biallelic loss of the RB1 gene"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-F3","obj":"NE-20-F.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the tumor: a neural crest tumor of the adrenal medulla in a young child with high HVA and VMA is neuroblastoma.","Recall the key molecular marker used for risk grouping in neuroblastoma.","Many extra copies of the MYCN oncogene drive rapid growth and predict a poor outcome."],"why":"Neuroblastoma is the most common extracranial solid tumor of young children. It arises from neural crest cells of the adrenal medulla or sympathetic chain and often presents as an abdominal mass that crosses the midline, unlike Wilms tumor. Spread to the orbit gives periorbital bruising, and some children have opsoclonus-myoclonus. MYCN amplification, found in about a fifth of cases, marks high-risk disease regardless of stage. Younger age at diagnosis and favorable histology predict a better course, and some infant tumors regress on their own.","whyNot":["This codeletion, with IDH mutation, defines oligodendroglioma and predicts a better response to treatment.","Correct.","BRAF alterations are typical of pilocytic astrocytoma in children, which has a good prognosis.","Two-hit RB1 loss causes retinoblastoma, which presents with leukocoria."],"study":{"topics":["Neuroblastoma prognosis","MYCN amplification"],"comps":["NE-20-F3","NE-16"]}},{"id":"NE-C29-Q2","stem":"Her elevated urine homovanillic acid reflects breakdown of which of the following monoamines, by which enzymes?","options":["Dopamine, by MAO and COMT","Norepinephrine, by MAO and COMT","Epinephrine, by COMT alone","Serotonin, by MAO alone"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-15","obj":"NE-15.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the end product of each monoamine: dopamine gives homovanillic acid, norepinephrine and epinephrine give vanillylmandelic acid, and serotonin gives 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid.","Recall the enzymes: monoamine oxidase removes the amine group and catechol-O-methyltransferase methylates the ring.","Homovanillic acid needs both steps applied to dopamine."],"why":"Catecholamines are broken down by two enzymes that can act in either order. Monoamine oxidase (MAO) oxidizes the amine, and catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) adds a methyl group to the catechol ring. Dopamine is converted to homovanillic acid. Norepinephrine and epinephrine are converted to metanephrines by COMT and then to vanillylmandelic acid. Neuroblastoma cells make large amounts of dopamine and norepinephrine, so both urine HVA and VMA are high, which helps with diagnosis and follow-up.","whyNot":["Correct.","Norepinephrine broken down by MAO and COMT gives vanillylmandelic acid, not homovanillic acid.","COMT acting alone on epinephrine gives metanephrine, which is high in pheochromocytoma.","Serotonin is not a catecholamine. MAO converts it to 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid, which is high in carcinoid syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Catecholamine breakdown","Urine HVA and VMA"],"comps":["NE-15","NE-20-F3"]}},{"id":"NE-C29-Q3","stem":"Which of the following pairs of cells share the embryologic origin of the cells that gave rise to her tumor?","options":["Microglia and blood monocytes","Schwann cells and dorsal root ganglion neurons","Adrenal cortical cells and ovarian stromal cells","Oligodendrocytes and astrocytes"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-1","obj":"NE-1.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cell of origin: chromaffin precursors of the adrenal medulla.","Recall that the adrenal medulla comes from neural crest, while the adrenal cortex comes from mesoderm.","Find the pair of cells that also come from neural crest."],"why":"Neural crest cells leave the edges of the neural folds as the tube closes and migrate widely. They form dorsal root, cranial sensory, and autonomic ganglia, Schwann cells, the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, melanocytes, and parts of the face and heart outflow tract. The neural tube itself (neuroectoderm) forms CNS neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependyma, and the retina. Microglia come from mesoderm, from yolk sac precursors related to the monocyte line.","whyNot":["Microglia and monocytes come from mesoderm.","Correct.","The adrenal cortex and gonadal stroma both come from intermediate mesoderm. Only the adrenal medulla comes from neural crest.","Oligodendrocytes and astrocytes come from neuroectoderm of the neural tube, not from neural crest."],"study":{"topics":["Neural crest derivatives","Adrenal medulla development"],"comps":["NE-1","NE-9"]}},{"id":"NE-C29-Q4","stem":"Her tumor arose from precursors of adrenal chromaffin cells. In a healthy person, which of the following receptors on chromaffin cells triggers catecholamine release during sympathetic activation?","options":["Alpha-2 adrenergic receptor coupled to Gi","Muscarinic M3 receptor coupled to Gq protein","Beta-1 adrenergic receptor coupled to Gs","Nicotinic receptor with a cation channel"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-9","obj":"NE-9.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that chromaffin cells act as modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons.","They are directly innervated by preganglionic sympathetic fibers, which release acetylcholine.","At every autonomic ganglion-type synapse, acetylcholine acts on nicotinic receptors, which are ligand-gated cation channels."],"why":"All preganglionic autonomic neurons release acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors, which are ligand-gated channels that let sodium in and depolarize the cell. Chromaffin cells receive preganglionic sympathetic fibers directly from the splanchnic nerves, so they are stimulated the same way and release epinephrine and some norepinephrine into the blood. Postganglionic sympathetic neurons release norepinephrine onto adrenergic receptors: alpha-1 uses Gq, alpha-2 uses Gi, and beta receptors use Gs. Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons, and sympathetic neurons to sweat glands, release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors: M1 and M3 use Gq, and M2 uses Gi.","whyNot":["Alpha-2 receptors are mainly presynaptic and inhibit transmitter release through Gi.","M3 receptors are on parasympathetic targets such as glands and smooth muscle, and on sweat glands. They do not trigger adrenal medullary release.","Beta-1 receptors on the heart and kidney respond to catecholamines. They do not drive chromaffin cell secretion.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Autonomic transmitters and receptors","Adrenal medulla innervation"],"comps":["NE-9","NE-17"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C30","system":"NE","dx":"Glioblastoma","codes":["NE-20-F4"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 5 weeks of morning headaches and trouble recognizing people. She does not know her own husband or children until they speak, although she names objects and reads normally. Her vital signs are within normal limits. Examination shows mild papilledema and no weakness or sensory loss. MRI shows a 5-cm mass in the right inferior occipitotemporal lobe, including the fusiform gyrus, with an irregular enhancing rim around a necrotic center and extensive surrounding T2 hyperintensity in the white matter. Stereotactic biopsy shows a highly cellular astrocytic tumor that stains for GFAP. Molecular testing shows wildtype IDH. Diagnosis: glioblastoma.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C30-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional histologic findings is most likely in her biopsy?","options":["Whorled cells with psammoma bodies","Pseudopalisading cells bordering necrosis","Fried-egg cells with chicken-wire capillaries","Perivascular pseudorosettes of ependymal cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-F4","obj":"NE-20-F.4","dok":2,"approach":["A ring-enhancing, necrotic hemispheric astrocytic tumor in an older adult with wildtype IDH is glioblastoma.","Recall its defining features: necrosis with pseudopalisading tumor cells and microvascular proliferation.","Match the other options to meningioma, oligodendroglioma, and ependymoma."],"why":"Glioblastoma is the most common primary malignant brain tumor in adults. It is a diffuse astrocytic tumor, IDH-wildtype in most cases, that stains for GFAP. Its diagnostic features are necrosis bordered by lined-up tumor cells (pseudopalisading) and microvascular proliferation, which make the irregular enhancing rim seen on MRI. It infiltrates along white matter tracts and can cross the corpus callosum to the other hemisphere, giving a butterfly pattern. Median survival is only about 15 months with treatment.","whyNot":["Whorls and psammoma bodies are typical of meningioma, an extra-axial dural tumor of arachnoid cells that is GFAP negative.","Correct.","These are features of oligodendroglioma, which is IDH-mutant with 1p/19q codeletion.","Perivascular pseudorosettes mark ependymoma, which in children usually arises in the fourth ventricle."],"study":{"topics":["Glioblastoma histology","Brain tumor histologic clues"],"comps":["NE-20-F4","NE-2"]}},{"id":"NE-C30-Q2","stem":"A 38-year-old man has a slowly growing, calcified frontal lobe tumor that presents with a seizure. Its cells have round nuclei with clear cytoplasm, and the tumor lies among a network of branching capillaries. Which of the following molecular findings is most likely in his tumor, in contrast to hers?","options":["Biallelic loss of the RB1 tumor suppressor gene","Amplification of the MYCN oncogene","IDH mutation with 1p/19q codeletion","Activating fusion of the BRAF kinase gene"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-16","obj":"NE-16.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify his tumor: a calcified frontal tumor with fried-egg cells and chicken-wire capillaries in a younger adult with seizures is oligodendroglioma.","Recall that oligodendroglioma is defined by an IDH mutation plus whole-arm loss of 1p and 19q.","Her glioblastoma is IDH-wildtype, which separates the two."],"why":"Mutant IDH1 or IDH2 makes the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate, which alters DNA and histone methylation and drives some diffuse gliomas. Oligodendroglioma requires both an IDH mutation and codeletion of chromosome arms 1p and 19q. It grows slowly, responds better to treatment, and has a longer survival than glioblastoma. Other driver genes in nervous system tumors include two-hit RB1 loss in retinoblastoma and MYCN amplification in aggressive neuroblastoma.","whyNot":["Two-hit RB1 loss causes retinoblastoma in young children, not a frontal glioma in an adult.","MYCN amplification predicts aggressive neuroblastoma, a neural crest tumor of young children.","Correct.","BRAF fusions are typical of pilocytic astrocytoma, usually a cystic cerebellar tumor in children."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular markers of gliomas","Oligodendroglioma"],"comps":["NE-16","NE-20-F4"]}},{"id":"NE-C30-Q3","stem":"She can reach for and pick up objects accurately and can judge the direction of moving cars. Damage to which of the following pathways best explains her inability to recognize faces?","options":["Ventral occipitotemporal visual stream","Optic radiation through the parietal lobe","Arcuate fasciculus to the frontal lobe","Dorsal occipitoparietal visual stream"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-11","obj":"NE-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Name the deficit: inability to recognize familiar faces with normal vision is prosopagnosia.","Recall the two visual association streams leaving the occipital lobe: dorsal (where and how) and ventral (what and who).","Face recognition depends on the fusiform gyrus in the ventral stream, which her tumor involves."],"why":"From primary visual cortex, the dorsal stream runs to the parietal lobe and handles where objects are, how they move, and how to reach for them. The ventral stream runs along the inferior temporal lobe and handles recognizing objects, colors, and faces. The fusiform gyrus contains a face-selective area, and damage there, especially on the right or on both sides, causes prosopagnosia. Patients still know people by voice or gait. Her intact reaching and motion judgment show that the dorsal stream is spared.","whyNot":["Correct.","Damage to the upper optic radiation in the parietal lobe causes a contralateral inferior quadrantanopia, not loss of face recognition.","The arcuate fasciculus connects Wernicke and Broca areas. Damage causes conduction aphasia with poor repetition.","Dorsal stream damage disturbs spatial location, motion perception, and visually guided reaching, which are normal in her."],"study":{"topics":["Dorsal and ventral visual streams","Prosopagnosia"],"comps":["NE-11","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C30-Q4","stem":"She is started on dexamethasone, and within days her headache improves and the T2 hyperintensity around the tumor shrinks. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the fluid seen around her tumor?","options":["Failure of neuronal Na+/K+ ATPase from ischemia","Transependymal flow of CSF from blocked ventricles","Osmotic water shift after a rapid fall in serum sodium","Leaky tumor vessels letting plasma into white matter"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-C1","obj":"NE-20-C.1","dok":3,"approach":["Note the setting: edema around a tumor that spreads through the white matter and responds to corticosteroids.","Tumor vessels lack a normal blood-brain barrier, so plasma leaks out and tracks along white matter fibers.","This is vasogenic edema; corticosteroids tighten the leaky vessels."],"why":"Vasogenic edema forms when the blood-brain barrier breaks down, as with tumors, abscesses, and trauma. Protein-rich fluid leaks into the extracellular space and spreads easily through white matter, which is why it looks like finger-like T2 hyperintensity around the mass. Corticosteroids reduce this leak. Cytotoxic edema is swelling of cells when ATP-dependent pumps fail in ischemia, and it affects gray and white matter alike. Interstitial edema comes from CSF pushed across the ependyma in hydrocephalus. Any edema raises intracranial pressure and can cause papilledema, the Cushing triad, and herniation.","whyNot":["This is cytotoxic edema from ischemia or hypoxia. It does not respond well to corticosteroids.","This is interstitial edema around the ventricles in hydrocephalus. Her edema surrounds a hemispheric mass.","Rapid lowering of serum sodium draws water into brain cells, an osmotic cause of cell swelling. It is not linked to a tumor.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vasogenic, cytotoxic, and interstitial edema","Corticosteroids for tumor edema"],"comps":["NE-20-C1","NE-4"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C31","system":"NE","dx":"Olfactory groove meningioma","codes":["NE-20-F2"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her husband because of a 1-year history of apathy and loss of interest in her usual activities and a 6-month history of daily dull headaches. She says she has not been able to smell coffee or perfume for about 2 years. Physical examination shows she cannot identify coffee or peppermint by smell with either nostril. Funduscopic examination shows optic atrophy on the right and papilledema on the left. Strength, sensation, and reflexes are normal. MRI of the brain shows a 5-cm homogeneously enhancing extra-axial mass with a dural tail arising from the floor of the anterior cranial fossa over the cribriform plate and compressing both frontal lobes. Diagnosis: olfactory groove meningioma.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C31-Q1","stem":"The mass is resected. Which of the following is most likely to be found on microscopic examination of the tumor?","options":["Pseudopalisading tumor cells around necrosis","Round cells with perinuclear halos and branching capillaries","Small blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes","Whorled clusters of cells with psammoma bodies"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-F2","obj":"NE-20-F.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note the clues for an extra-axial tumor: attached to the dura, with a dural tail, pushing on the brain from outside.","A dural-based tumor in an adult woman is a meningioma, which arises from arachnoid cap cells.","Recall its histology: whorls of meningothelial cells and laminated calcifications called psammoma bodies."],"why":"Meningioma is a usually benign tumor of arachnoid cap (meningothelial) cells. It sits outside the brain, attaches to the dura, and often shows a dural tail on MRI. Under the microscope the cells form whorls, and some whorls calcify into layered psammoma bodies. It is more common in women and is linked to NF2 and prior head radiation. Symptoms come from compression; here the frontal lobes cause apathy, and the olfactory bulbs and optic nerve explain the anosmia and the eye findings.","whyNot":["Pseudopalisading necrosis is the mark of glioblastoma, an intra-axial tumor of the cerebral white matter that often crosses the corpus callosum.","Perinuclear halos (fried egg cells) with branching chicken-wire capillaries describe oligodendroglioma, an intra-axial frontal lobe tumor that often calcifies and causes seizures.","Homer Wright rosettes of small blue cells are seen in medulloblastoma, a cerebellar tumor of children, and in neuroblastoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Meningioma pathology","Extra-axial versus intra-axial brain tumors"],"comps":["NE-20-F2","NE-20-F"]}},{"id":"NE-C31-Q2","stem":"Her loss of smell results from compression of the olfactory bulbs and tracts. Which of the following best describes the normal central pathway of this sensation?","options":["Olfactory tract fibers relay in the VPM nucleus before reaching insular cortex","Olfactory tract fibers relay in the medial geniculate nucleus before reaching cortex","Olfactory tract fibers reach the piriform cortex without a thalamic relay","Olfactory nerve fibers relay in the nucleus solitarius before reaching cortex"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-6","obj":"NE-6.7","dok":2,"approach":["Trace smell from the receptor: olfactory neurons pass through the cribriform plate and synapse in the olfactory bulb.","Recall that the olfactory tract projects straight to primary olfactory (piriform) cortex and the amygdala.","Smell is the one sense that reaches cortex without first relaying in the thalamus."],"why":"Olfactory receptor neurons in the nasal roof send their axons (CN I) through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to synapse on mitral cells in the olfactory bulb. The olfactory tract then runs back under the frontal lobe to the piriform cortex and amygdala in the medial temporal lobe, with no thalamic relay. An olfactory groove mass presses on the bulbs and tracts from below. A fracture of the cribriform plate tears the olfactory nerve fibers and causes anosmia, often with CSF rhinorrhea.","whyNot":["VPM relays face sensation and taste to cortex. Taste reaches the insula and frontal operculum this way, but smell does not use VPM.","The medial geniculate nucleus relays hearing to the primary auditory cortex in the temporal lobe.","Correct.","The nucleus solitarius receives taste from CN VII, IX, and X and visceral input from CN IX and X. It plays no part in smell."],"study":{"topics":["Olfactory pathway","Taste pathway"],"comps":["NE-6","NE-3"]}},{"id":"NE-C31-Q3","stem":"Her 31-year-old daughter has had recurrent episodes of throbbing left-sided headache since adolescence. Each episode lasts about 12 hours, brings nausea and sensitivity to light and sound, and follows 20 minutes of flickering zigzag lines in her vision. Neurologic examination between episodes is normal. Which of the following mechanisms most directly produces the daughter's headache pain?","options":["Sustained contraction and tenderness of pericranial muscles","Trigeminovascular activation with release of CGRP","Hypothalamic activation driving a trigeminal autonomic reflex","Impaired CSF absorption raising intracranial pressure"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-5","obj":"NE-5.7","dok":3,"approach":["Classify the headache: unilateral, pulsating, hours long, with nausea, photophobia, phonophobia, and a visual aura. This is migraine with aura.","Recall the pain mechanism of migraine: trigeminal sensory fibers around meningeal vessels release CGRP and other peptides.","Match the other mechanisms to tension headache, cluster headache, and raised intracranial pressure."],"why":"Migraine is a recurrent unilateral pulsating headache lasting about 4 to 72 hours, with nausea, photophobia, and phonophobia. About a third of patients have an aura, often spreading visual zigzags, which reflects a wave of cortical spreading depression. The pain comes from activation of the trigeminovascular system: trigeminal afferents around dural and pial vessels release calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP), which dilates vessels and causes neurogenic inflammation and sensitization. This is why drugs that block CGRP prevent migraine.","whyNot":["Pericranial muscle tenderness goes with tension-type headache, a bilateral band-like pressure without nausea, aura, or marked photophobia.","Correct.","Hypothalamic activation with a trigeminal autonomic reflex underlies cluster headache: brief (15 to 180 minutes) severe periorbital pain with same-side tearing and rhinorrhea, often in men.","Raised intracranial pressure, as with her mother's mass or idiopathic intracranial hypertension, causes daily headache worse lying down, often with papilledema. Her headaches are episodic with a normal examination between attacks."],"study":{"topics":["Migraine pathophysiology","Primary headache types"],"comps":["NE-5","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C31-Q4","stem":"Eight months after surgery, she slips on ice and strikes the side of her head. She is unconscious for a minute, then talks normally for 2 hours before becoming drowsy. CT scan of the head shows a biconvex hyperdense collection over the left temporal lobe that does not cross suture lines, beneath a fracture of the squamous temporal bone. This blood has most likely collected in which of the following spaces?","options":["Between the dura and the arachnoid mater","Between the skull and the dura mater","Between the arachnoid and the pia mater","Within the parenchyma of the temporal lobe"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-4","obj":"NE-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Read the clues: temporal bone fracture, lucid interval, and a biconvex collection stopped by suture lines.","This is an epidural hematoma from a torn middle meningeal artery.","Arterial blood strips the dura off the skull, so it collects between the inner table of the skull and the dura."],"why":"The middle meningeal artery runs in a groove on the inner surface of the squamous temporal bone near the pterion, where the bone is thin. A fracture here can tear it, and arterial pressure peels the dura away from the skull. The dura is firmly fixed at the sutures, so the blood forms a lens-shaped (biconvex) collection that stops at suture lines. The classic course is brief loss of consciousness, a lucid interval, then rapid decline as the hematoma expands.","whyNot":["Blood between the dura and arachnoid is a subdural hematoma from torn bridging veins. It is crescent-shaped, crosses suture lines, and is common in older adults and people with alcohol use disorder.","Correct.","Blood between the arachnoid and pia is subarachnoid hemorrhage, most often from a ruptured berry aneurysm, with sudden thunderclap headache and blood in the basal cisterns.","Bleeding into the temporal lobe itself would be a contusion or intraparenchymal hemorrhage, seen inside the brain rather than as a lens-shaped collection under the skull."],"study":{"topics":["Meningeal spaces and hemorrhages","Epidural hematoma"],"comps":["NE-4","NE-20-H1"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C32","system":"NE","dx":"Vestibular schwannoma in neurofibromatosis type 2","codes":["NE-20-F6"],"case":"A 23-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of progressive hearing loss in both ears, constant ringing in his ears, and unsteadiness when walking in the dark. He had surgery for a cataract in his left eye at age 17 years. Physical examination shows decreased hearing to whispered voice bilaterally. On Rinne testing, air conduction is greater than bone conduction in both ears. He sways when standing with his eyes closed. Facial sensation and facial movement are normal. MRI of the brain with contrast shows bilateral enhancing masses at the cerebellopontine angles that extend into the internal acoustic canals, and a small dural-based mass over the right frontal convexity. Diagnosis: bilateral vestibular schwannomas in neurofibromatosis type 2.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C32-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the site where these tumors most likely originate?","options":["Vestibular nerve within the internal acoustic canal","Spiral ganglion in the modiolus of the cochlea","Vestibular nuclei at the pontomedullary junction of the brainstem","Facial nerve at the geniculate ganglion"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-F6","obj":"NE-20-F.7","dok":3,"approach":["A schwannoma is a tumor of Schwann cells, so it must arise where a nerve has peripheral myelin.","CN VIII is myelinated by oligodendrocytes near the brainstem and by Schwann cells farther out, with the transition near the internal acoustic canal.","Hearing loss, tinnitus, and imbalance with masses at the cerebellopontine angle point to the vestibular division of CN VIII."],"why":"Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma) almost always arises from the vestibular division of CN VIII, near the junction of central and peripheral myelin within the internal acoustic canal. As it grows, it fills the canal and pushes into the cerebellopontine angle. It compresses the cochlear nerve (hearing loss and tinnitus) and the vestibular nerve (imbalance), and large tumors later press on CN V (facial numbness, lost corneal reflex) and CN VII. Under the microscope it shows S-100 positive Schwann cells in dense Antoni A areas with Verocay bodies and loose Antoni B areas. Bilateral tumors define NF2.","whyNot":["Correct.","The spiral ganglion holds cochlear neuron cell bodies inside the cochlea. A lesion here could cause hearing loss but would not form an enhancing mass at the cerebellopontine angle.","The vestibular nuclei are CNS neurons inside the brainstem. There are no Schwann cells there, and a tumor there would be intra-axial.","Facial nerve schwannomas are rare and usually present with facial weakness. His facial movement is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Vestibular schwannoma","Cerebellopontine angle anatomy"],"comps":["NE-20-F6","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C32-Q2","stem":"Molecular analysis of the resected tumor shows loss of both copies of the gene that causes his disorder. The normal product of this gene most likely functions as which of the following?","options":["A Rheb GTPase-activating protein that restrains mTOR activity","A ubiquitin ligase subunit that targets HIF-1 alpha for breakdown","A cytoskeletal scaffold protein that mediates contact inhibition","A GTPase-activating protein that turns off Ras signaling"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-16","obj":"NE-16.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the disorder: bilateral vestibular schwannomas, meningioma, and juvenile cataract mean NF2.","Recall the NF2 gene on chromosome 22, which encodes the tumor suppressor merlin (schwannomin).","Merlin links membrane proteins to the actin cytoskeleton and stops growth when cells touch."],"why":"NF2 is caused by loss of the NF2 tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 22. Its product, merlin, is a cytoskeletal scaffold related to the ERM proteins. It links cell membrane receptors to actin and mediates contact inhibition, so its loss lets Schwann cells and meningothelial cells keep dividing. Like other tumor suppressor syndromes, NF2 is inherited as autosomal dominant, but a tumor forms only after the second allele is lost in a cell (two-hit model). About half of cases are new mutations.","whyNot":["This is tuberin (TSC2), which partners with hamartin (TSC1). Their loss activates mTOR and causes tuberous sclerosis.","This is the VHL protein, encoded on chromosome 3. Its loss stabilizes HIF and causes hemangioblastomas and renal cell carcinoma.","Correct.","This is neurofibromin, the NF1 gene product on chromosome 17. Its loss leaves Ras active and causes neurofibromas and café-au-lait macules."],"study":{"topics":["Neurocutaneous syndrome genes","Two-hit tumor suppressor model"],"comps":["NE-16","NE-20-F6"]}},{"id":"NE-C32-Q3","stem":"Another patient, an 8-year-old girl, has six café-au-lait macules larger than 5 mm, freckling in both axillae, and pigmented iris hamartomas on slit-lamp examination. She is at greatest risk for which of the following tumors?","options":["Optic pathway glioma","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas","Cerebellar hemangioblastoma","Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-F6","obj":"NE-20-F.8","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the syndrome: multiple café-au-lait macules, axillary freckling, and Lisch nodules (iris hamartomas) define NF1.","Recall the CNS tumor most typical of NF1 in children: a low-grade pilocytic glioma of the optic nerve or chiasm.","Assign the other tumors to NF2, von Hippel-Lindau disease, and tuberous sclerosis."],"why":"Neurofibromatosis type 1 shows café-au-lait macules, axillary or inguinal freckling, cutaneous neurofibromas, and Lisch nodules. Optic pathway glioma, usually a pilocytic astrocytoma, is the most common CNS tumor in children with NF1 and can cause vision loss or precocious puberty. Plexiform neurofibromas can turn into malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors, and adults have a higher risk of pheochromocytoma. NF1 comes from loss of neurofibromin on chromosome 17.","whyNot":["Correct.","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas define NF2, which has few skin findings and no Lisch nodules.","Cerebellar hemangioblastoma is the typical CNS tumor of von Hippel-Lindau disease.","Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma near the foramen of Monro is a feature of tuberous sclerosis, which has ash-leaf spots rather than café-au-lait macules."],"study":{"topics":["Neurofibromatosis type 1","NF1 versus NF2"],"comps":["NE-20-F6","NE-16"]}},{"id":"NE-C32-Q4","stem":"A 30-year-old woman has a cerebellar hemangioblastoma and two retinal hemangioblastomas. Genetic testing shows a germline loss-of-function mutation on chromosome 3p in a gene whose product targets hypoxia-inducible factor for degradation. She is at greatest risk for which of the following additional tumors?","options":["Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor","Renal angiomyolipoma","Cardiac rhabdomyoma","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-F","obj":"NE-20-F.11","dok":2,"approach":["Cerebellar and retinal hemangioblastomas with a chromosome 3p mutation identify von Hippel-Lindau disease.","Without VHL protein, HIF is not degraded, so cells keep making VEGF and other hypoxia genes, which drives vascular tumors.","The main malignant risk in this disease is clear cell renal cell carcinoma, often bilateral."],"why":"The VHL protein is part of a ubiquitin ligase that tags hypoxia-inducible factor for breakdown when oxygen is normal. When both copies are lost, HIF builds up and switches on VEGF, PDGF, and erythropoietin genes as if the cell were hypoxic. The result is highly vascular tumors: hemangioblastomas of the cerebellum, spinal cord, and retina; clear cell renal cell carcinoma, often multiple and bilateral; pheochromocytoma; and pancreatic cysts. Renal cell carcinoma is a leading cause of death in this disease.","whyNot":["Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor arises from plexiform neurofibromas in NF1.","Renal angiomyolipoma is also a feature of tuberous sclerosis, caused by TSC1 or TSC2 loss with mTOR activation.","Cardiac rhabdomyoma is a tumor of tuberous sclerosis, along with cortical tubers, subependymal nodules, and ash-leaf spots.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Von Hippel-Lindau disease","Tuberous sclerosis lesions"],"comps":["NE-20-F","NE-16"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C33","system":"NE","dx":"Pituitary macroadenoma compressing the optic chiasm","codes":["NE-17"],"case":"A 47-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of headaches and repeatedly bumping into door frames on both sides. He has had decreased libido and erectile dysfunction for 1 year. Visual field testing shows loss of the temporal half of the visual field in each eye. Visual acuity and pupillary light reflexes are normal. Serum LH and testosterone concentrations are decreased. MRI of the head shows a 2.8-cm sellar mass with suprasellar extension that elevates and compresses the optic chiasm and displaces the pituitary stalk. Diagnosis: nonfunctioning pituitary macroadenoma compressing the optic chiasm.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C33-Q1","stem":"Serum prolactin concentration is 58 ng/mL (N, 2 to 18 ng/mL). After transsphenoidal resection, immunohistochemical staining of the tumor is negative for prolactin and all other pituitary hormones. Which of the following best explains his increased serum prolactin?","options":["Interrupted dopamine delivery from the hypothalamus","Prolactin secretion by a subset of tumor cells","Increased TRH release from compressed hypothalamic neurons","Release of stored prolactin from the posterior pituitary"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-17","obj":"NE-17.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that prolactin is the one anterior pituitary hormone held down mainly by hypothalamic inhibition, through dopamine.","Dopamine from the arcuate nucleus reaches lactotrophs through the portal vessels in the pituitary stalk.","A mass that compresses the stalk cuts off dopamine, so normal lactotrophs release more prolactin (stalk effect)."],"why":"Dopamine made in the arcuate nucleus travels down the tuberoinfundibular tract and through the hypophyseal portal vessels of the stalk to inhibit lactotrophs. When a sellar mass compresses the stalk, this brake is lost and normal lactotrophs raise prolactin modestly, usually below about 100 to 150 ng/mL. A prolactin-secreting macroadenoma usually produces much higher levels. Telling the two apart matters because a prolactinoma shrinks with dopamine agonists, while a nonfunctioning adenoma does not. The same mass explains his bitemporal hemianopia, since it presses on the crossing nasal retinal fibers in the chiasm.","whyNot":["Correct.","The tumor stains negative for prolactin, so the adenoma cells are not the source.","TRH can stimulate prolactin release, but a sellar mass interferes with hypothalamic signals reaching the pituitary rather than increasing them.","The posterior pituitary stores ADH and oxytocin only. Prolactin is made and released by lactotrophs of the anterior pituitary."],"study":{"topics":["Stalk effect hyperprolactinemia","Pituitary mass effects"],"comps":["NE-17","NE-12"]}},{"id":"NE-C33-Q2","stem":"On the second day after surgery, he passes 6 L of dilute urine. Serum sodium concentration is 149 mEq/L (N, 135 to 145 mEq/L), plasma osmolality is 305 mOsm/kg H2O (N, 275 to 295 mOsm/kg H2O), and urine osmolality is 110 mOsm/kg H2O. Which of the following most likely accounts for these findings?","options":["Loss of ACTH secretion from anterior pituitary corticotrophs","Injured supraoptic and paraventricular axons in the stalk","Excess thirst with suppression of ADH secretion","Resistance of collecting duct principal cells to ADH"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-17","obj":"NE-17.2","dok":3,"approach":["Interpret the labs: large volumes of dilute urine while plasma is concentrated and sodium is high means too little ADH effect.","Recall where ADH comes from: supraoptic and paraventricular neurons send axons down the stalk to the posterior pituitary.","Surgery around the stalk injures these axons, causing central diabetes insipidus."],"why":"ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin are made in the cell bodies of supraoptic and paraventricular neurons, carried down their axons through the pituitary stalk, and stored in the posterior pituitary. Pituitary surgery or head trauma can injure these axons, so ADH release falls and the collecting ducts cannot reabsorb water. Urine becomes large in volume and dilute while plasma osmolality and sodium rise. Urine concentrates after desmopressin, which confirms a central cause. After surgery this can be transient or follow a triphasic course of diabetes insipidus, then SIADH as damaged terminals leak stored ADH, then permanent diabetes insipidus.","whyNot":["Cortisol deficiency from lost ACTH impairs free water excretion and tends to cause hyponatremia, the opposite of his findings.","Correct.","Primary polydipsia also gives dilute urine, but drinking too much water lowers plasma osmolality and sodium. His are high.","Collecting duct resistance to ADH is nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, caused by lithium, hypercalcemia, or receptor mutations. It would not start suddenly after pituitary surgery and would not respond to desmopressin."],"study":{"topics":["Central diabetes insipidus","Posterior pituitary physiology"],"comps":["NE-17","NE-12"]}},{"id":"NE-C33-Q3","stem":"The suprasellar part of the tumor reaches the floor of the third ventricle. A hypothalamic nucleus that lies just above the optic chiasm receives direct input from retinal ganglion cells. Damage to this nucleus would most likely disrupt which of the following functions?","options":["Heat loss through sweating and vasodilation","Satiety signaling after a meal","Circadian sleep-wake rhythm","Oxytocin release during lactation"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-12","obj":"NE-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the nucleus: the suprachiasmatic nucleus sits above the optic chiasm and receives the retinohypothalamic tract.","Recall its job: it is the master clock that sets circadian rhythms to the light-dark cycle.","Assign the other functions to the ventromedial, anterior, and paraventricular nuclei."],"why":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus receives light information directly from a small group of retinal ganglion cells through the retinohypothalamic tract. It sets the timing of sleep and wakefulness, body temperature cycles, and hormone rhythms such as cortisol and melatonin. It controls melatonin release from the pineal gland through a pathway that runs through the sympathetic system. Damage disrupts the daily sleep-wake cycle.","whyNot":["Heat loss is run by the anterior hypothalamus (preoptic area). A lesion there causes hyperthermia.","Satiety depends on the ventromedial nucleus. A lesion there causes hyperphagia and obesity.","Correct.","Oxytocin is made in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei and released from the posterior pituitary."],"study":{"topics":["Hypothalamic nuclei","Suprachiasmatic nucleus"],"comps":["NE-12","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C33-Q4","stem":"Most of the axons compressed by this tumor normally make their next synapse in which of the following structures?","options":["Ventral posterolateral nucleus","Lateral geniculate nucleus","Medial geniculate nucleus","Ventral posteromedial nucleus"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-3","obj":"NE-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["The tumor compresses the optic chiasm, which carries retinal ganglion cell axons.","These axons continue in the optic tract and most of them synapse in the thalamus.","The thalamic relay for vision is the lateral geniculate nucleus, which sends the optic radiations to visual cortex."],"why":"Retinal ganglion cell axons from both eyes meet in the optic chiasm, where nasal fibers cross. They continue in the optic tracts, and most synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, which projects through the optic radiations to the primary visual cortex. Each sensory system has its own thalamic relay: VPL for body sensation, VPM for face sensation and taste, LGN for vision, and MGN for hearing, with VL relaying motor signals. A thalamic infarct causes contralateral sensory loss and can later cause thalamic pain syndrome.","whyNot":["VPL relays touch, vibration, pain, and temperature from the body through the medial lemniscus and spinothalamic tract.","Correct.","The medial geniculate nucleus relays hearing from the inferior colliculus to auditory cortex.","VPM relays sensation from the face through the trigeminal pathways and taste from the nucleus solitarius."],"study":{"topics":["Thalamic relay nuclei","Visual pathway"],"comps":["NE-3","NE-6"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C34","system":"NE","dx":"Meningococcal meningitis","codes":["NE-20-G8"],"case":"A 19-year-old college student who lives in a dormitory is brought to the emergency department 10 hours after the onset of fever, severe headache, and vomiting. His temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. He is drowsy and has neck stiffness and photophobia. Nonblanching petechiae are present on his legs and trunk. Lumbar puncture shows an opening pressure of 32 cm H2O (N, 10 to 20 cm H2O). CSF analysis shows leukocytes 2,800/µL (N, 0 to 5/µL) with 90% neutrophils, glucose 18 mg/dL (N, 40 to 70 mg/dL), and protein 260 mg/dL (N, 15 to 45 mg/dL). Gram stain shows gram-negative diplococci. Diagnosis: meningococcal meningitis.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C34-Q1","stem":"Over the next 6 hours, the petechiae enlarge into purpura, and his blood pressure falls to 78/40 mm Hg. Which of the following bacterial components most directly triggers these changes?","options":["Pneumolysin","Polyribosylribitol phosphate capsule","Lipoteichoic acid","Lipooligosaccharide"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-19","obj":"NE-19.1","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-negative diplococci in CSF with a petechial rash identify Neisseria meningitidis.","Purpura with falling blood pressure means septic shock with disseminated intravascular coagulation.","The outer membrane endotoxin of N. meningitidis, lipooligosaccharide, drives this cytokine storm."],"why":"Neisseria meningitidis is a gram-negative diplococcus with two key virulence factors. Its polysaccharide capsule protects it from phagocytosis and complement. Its outer membrane lipooligosaccharide (LOS) is an endotoxin that it sheds in large amounts in membrane blebs. Lipid A binds TLR4 on macrophages and endothelium and triggers TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 release, endothelial damage, DIC, and shock. Severe cases can cause adrenal hemorrhage (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome).","whyNot":["Pneumolysin is a pore-forming toxin of Streptococcus pneumoniae, a gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococcus.","Polyribosylribitol phosphate is the capsule of Haemophilus influenzae type b, a gram-negative coccobacillus. Capsules block phagocytosis but do not directly cause shock.","Lipoteichoic acid is a cell wall component of gram-positive bacteria. N. meningitidis is gram-negative.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neisseria meningitidis virulence","Bacterial meningitis organisms"],"comps":["NE-19","NE-20-G8"]}},{"id":"NE-C34-Q2","stem":"His roommate received a meningococcal conjugate vaccine at age 16 years. In this vaccine, the capsular polysaccharide is linked to a protein carrier. Which of the following best explains why conjugation improves protection compared with plain polysaccharide?","options":["It recruits helper T cells, giving class switching and memory","It makes the polysaccharide a ligand for Toll-like receptor 4","It allows the polysaccharide to activate CD8 cytotoxic T cells","It lets the polysaccharide cross-link B cell receptors"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-18","obj":"NE-18.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that plain polysaccharide antigens activate B cells without T cell help, giving mostly IgM and little memory.","T cells recognize only peptides on MHC. Linking a protein carrier to the polysaccharide supplies those peptides.","The B cell that binds the polysaccharide presents carrier peptides on MHC class II and gets help from CD4 T cells."],"why":"A B cell whose receptor binds the capsular polysaccharide takes in the whole conjugate, breaks down the protein carrier, and presents its peptides on MHC class II. Carrier-specific CD4 helper T cells then give CD40 ligand and cytokine signals to that B cell. This produces class switching to IgG, affinity maturation, and long-lived memory B cells against the polysaccharide. Conjugate vaccines also work in infants under 2 years, who respond poorly to plain polysaccharide. The same principle is used for H. influenzae type b and pneumococcal conjugate vaccines.","whyNot":["Correct.","TLR4 recognizes lipid A of endotoxin. Conjugation to a protein does not turn polysaccharide into a TLR4 ligand.","CD8 T cells recognize peptides on MHC class I. Polysaccharides are not presented to CD8 T cells, and protection against capsule depends on antibody.","Plain polysaccharide already cross-links B cell receptors through its repeating units. That is how it causes a T cell-independent IgM response without memory."],"study":{"topics":["Conjugate vaccines","T cell-dependent and T cell-independent antigens"],"comps":["NE-18","NE-19"]}},{"id":"NE-C34-Q3","stem":"His 52-year-old uncle, who recently moved from a country where tuberculosis is endemic, develops 4 weeks of low-grade fever, headache, and confusion, followed by double vision from a right abducens nerve palsy. CSF shows 220 leukocytes/µL, mostly lymphocytes, glucose 22 mg/dL, and protein 310 mg/dL. Which of the following is most likely to be found in the uncle's brain?","options":["Spongiform vacuoles without inflammatory cells","Purulent neutrophilic exudate over the cerebral convexities","Hemorrhagic necrosis of the medial temporal lobes","Thick gelatinous basal exudate with caseating granulomas"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-G8","obj":"NE-20-G.10","dok":3,"approach":["Note the subacute course over weeks, a cranial nerve palsy, and CSF with lymphocytes, very low glucose, and very high protein.","This pattern in a person from an endemic area is tuberculous meningitis.","Recall its pathology: a thick exudate at the base of the brain with granulomas that traps cranial nerves and vessels."],"why":"Tuberculous meningitis develops over weeks. Mycobacterium tuberculosis seeds the meninges, and a thick gelatinous exudate with caseating granulomas collects around the base of the brain. It traps cranial nerves (CN VI, with its long course, and CN III and IV), inflames basal arteries and causes infarcts, and blocks CSF flow at the basal cisterns, causing hydrocephalus. CSF shows lymphocytes, very low glucose, and very high protein. Acute bacterial meningitis like the student's has other complications too: sensorineural hearing loss, seizures, and hydrocephalus.","whyNot":["Spongiform change without inflammation marks prion disease, a rapidly progressive dementia with myoclonus and no fever or CSF pleocytosis.","A purulent exudate over the convexities fits acute pyogenic meningitis, such as pneumococcal meningitis, with neutrophils in CSF and a course of hours to days.","Hemorrhagic necrosis of the temporal lobes is the pattern of herpes simplex encephalitis, with confusion, seizures, and a lymphocytic CSF with normal glucose.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tuberculous meningitis","Complications of meningitis"],"comps":["NE-20-G8","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C34-Q4","stem":"In the hour before he came to the hospital, he had shaking chills and felt cold even though his temperature was already 38.9 °C (102.0 °F). Which of the following best explains the chills?","options":["Cytokine-induced lowering of the hypothalamic set point","Endotoxin-induced uncoupling of mitochondria in muscle","A raised hypothalamic set point above core temperature","Loss of anterior hypothalamic heat-loss neurons"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-12","obj":"NE-12.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that pyrogenic cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF) induce prostaglandin E2 in the preoptic hypothalamus.","PGE2 raises the temperature set point.","Until core temperature reaches the new set point, the body senses itself as too cold and conserves and makes heat by vasoconstriction and shivering."],"why":"In infection, macrophages release IL-1, IL-6, and TNF. These cytokines induce COX-2 in the region of the preoptic hypothalamus, and the resulting PGE2 raises the thermoregulatory set point. While core temperature is still below the new set point, the hypothalamus triggers heat conservation and production: skin vasoconstriction, feeling cold, and shivering (chills). When the fever breaks and the set point falls, the opposite happens, with sweating and vasodilation. This is why fever is lowered by drugs that block prostaglandin synthesis.","whyNot":["Cytokines raise the set point. A lowered set point would trigger sweating and vasodilation, as happens when a fever breaks.","Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation generates heat in brown fat and in malignant hyperthermia or salicylate toxicity. It does not cause the cold feeling and shivering of a rising fever.","Correct.","Damage to the anterior hypothalamus impairs heat loss and causes hyperthermia, but it does not cause shivering in a feverish patient."],"study":{"topics":["Fever physiology","Hypothalamic thermoregulation"],"comps":["NE-12","NE-18"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C35","system":"NE","dx":"Neonatal group B streptococcal meningitis","codes":["NE-20-G8"],"case":"A 10-day-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of 1 day of fever, poor feeding, and irritability. She was born at term by vaginal delivery. Her mother received no prenatal care. Her temperature is 38.7 °C (101.7 °F), pulse is 182/min, and respirations are 56/min. She is lethargic, and the anterior fontanelle is bulging. Lumbar puncture is performed. CSF analysis shows leukocytes 1,600/µL (N, 0 to 20/µL for age) with 85% neutrophils, glucose 15 mg/dL with a simultaneous blood glucose of 78 mg/dL (CSF to blood ratio normally above about 0.6), and protein 310 mg/dL (N, 20 to 150 mg/dL for age). Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in chains. Diagnosis: group B streptococcal meningitis.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C35-Q1","stem":"Culture of the CSF grows the organism seen on Gram stain. Which of the following laboratory characteristics is this organism most likely to show?","options":["Alpha hemolysis and optochin sensitivity","Beta hemolysis and bacitracin sensitivity","Beta hemolysis and bacitracin resistance","Gamma hemolysis and growth in 6.5% NaCl"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-19","obj":"NE-19.2","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-positive cocci in chains causing meningitis in a newborn point to Streptococcus agalactiae (group B).","Recall how streptococci are separated in the lab: hemolysis first, then bacitracin or optochin.","Group B streptococci are beta-hemolytic and bacitracin resistant, and they are CAMP test positive."],"why":"Streptococcus agalactiae colonizes the vagina and rectum of many pregnant women and passes to the infant during delivery. It has a polysaccharide capsule that resists phagocytosis, which newborns handle poorly. In the lab it is beta-hemolytic, bacitracin resistant, CAMP test positive, and hydrolyzes hippurate. Screening pregnant women and giving intrapartum antibiotics prevents most early-onset disease. The other main neonatal meningitis pathogens are E. coli with the K1 capsule and Listeria monocytogenes, a gram-positive rod.","whyNot":["Alpha hemolysis with optochin sensitivity identifies Streptococcus pneumoniae, which appears as lancet-shaped diplococci and causes meningitis in older children and adults.","Beta hemolysis with bacitracin sensitivity identifies Streptococcus pyogenes (group A), which causes pharyngitis and skin infections, not neonatal meningitis.","Correct.","Gamma hemolysis with growth in 6.5% salt identifies Enterococcus, which is an uncommon cause of neonatal meningitis."],"study":{"topics":["Group B streptococcus","Streptococcal lab identification"],"comps":["NE-19","NE-20-G8"]}},{"id":"NE-C35-Q2","stem":"The organisms that cause bacterial meningitis change with age. Which of the following organisms is a classic cause in both neonates and adults older than 60 years but is uncommon in healthy young adults?","options":["Escherichia coli K1","Listeria monocytogenes","Neisseria meningitidis","Haemophilus influenzae type b"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-G8","obj":"NE-20-G.9","dok":2,"approach":["List the causes by age: neonates (group B streptococcus, E. coli, Listeria), children and adults (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis), older adults (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, Listeria).","Find the organism on both the neonatal and older adult lists.","Listeria targets people with weak cell-mediated immunity: newborns, pregnant women, older adults, and the immunosuppressed."],"why":"Listeria monocytogenes is a gram-positive rod with tumbling motility that lives inside cells and spreads from cell to cell using actin tails. Control depends on T cell-mediated immunity, which is immature in newborns and declines with age, pregnancy, and immunosuppression. It is acquired from unpasteurized dairy, soft cheese, and deli meats, and it grows at refrigerator temperature. That is why it appears on the neonatal list and again on the list for adults over about 60 years.","whyNot":["E. coli K1 is a major cause of neonatal meningitis but is not a typical cause of community meningitis in older adults.","Correct.","N. meningitidis is a leading cause in adolescents and young adults, so it is not uncommon in that group.","Haemophilus influenzae type b once caused much meningitis in children 6 months to 6 years old. The conjugate vaccine has made it rare, and it is not a typical cause in older adults."],"study":{"topics":["Meningitis organisms by age","Listeria monocytogenes"],"comps":["NE-20-G8","NE-19"]}},{"id":"NE-C35-Q3","stem":"Her bulging fontanelle reflects raised intracranial pressure from cerebral edema. Which of the following processes most directly increases brain water in this condition?","options":["Increased CSF secretion by the choroid plexus","Tearing of bridging veins with bleeding into the subdural space","Loss of astrocyte foot processes from viral lysis","Cytokine-mediated opening of endothelial tight junctions"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-18","obj":"NE-18.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the blood-brain barrier depends on tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells, supported by astrocyte foot processes.","In bacterial meningitis, bacterial cell wall products activate microglia and macrophages, which release IL-1 and TNF.","These cytokines loosen endothelial tight junctions, so plasma fluid and protein leak into the brain (vasogenic edema)."],"why":"The blood-brain barrier is formed by endothelial tight junctions with a basement membrane and astrocyte foot processes. It also keeps antibodies and complement low in the CSF, which lets bacteria multiply once they arrive. In meningitis, bacterial products such as lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycan activate microglia and macrophages through Toll-like receptors, and they release IL-1 and TNF. These cytokines activate the endothelium, bring in neutrophils, and open tight junctions, so fluid and protein leak into the brain. This vasogenic edema raises intracranial pressure, which in an infant with an open fontanelle makes the fontanelle bulge.","whyNot":["The choroid plexus makes CSF at a fairly steady rate, and inflammation does not raise brain water by increasing its output.","Torn bridging veins cause a subdural hematoma after trauma or shaking. This adds a blood collection rather than water in brain tissue.","Her infection is bacterial. Direct viral lysis of astrocytes is not how bacterial meningitis damages the barrier.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Blood-brain barrier","Cerebral edema in meningitis"],"comps":["NE-18","NE-20-C1"]}},{"id":"NE-C35-Q4","stem":"She recovers. At age 6 months, hearing testing shows bilateral hearing loss. The tympanic membranes look normal, and tympanometry shows normal middle ear pressure and movement. Which of the following is the most likely site of injury?","options":["Eustachian tube draining the middle ear","Ossicular chain of the middle ear","Cochlear hair cells of the inner ear","Primary auditory cortex of the temporal lobes"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-J","obj":"NE-20-J.16","dok":3,"approach":["Normal eardrums and normal tympanometry rule out a conductive problem in the middle ear.","So the loss is sensorineural.","Bacterial meningitis spreads from the subarachnoid space to the inner ear and destroys cochlear hair cells."],"why":"Sensorineural hearing loss is the most common lasting complication of bacterial meningitis. Bacteria and inflammation spread from the subarachnoid space into the cochlea through the cochlear aqueduct and internal acoustic canal, causing labyrinthitis that destroys hair cells and can later fill the cochlea with bone. Conductive loss comes from problems that block sound reaching the inner ear: cerumen, a perforated eardrum, middle ear fluid, eustachian tube dysfunction, otosclerosis, or cholesteatoma. Children who survive meningitis need prompt hearing testing.","whyNot":["Eustachian tube dysfunction causes negative middle ear pressure or fluid and a conductive loss, which would show on tympanometry.","Ossicular damage causes conductive loss, as in cholesteatoma or otosclerosis. Middle ear movement on tympanometry would usually be abnormal.","Correct.","Bilateral cortical deafness is very rare and needs damage to both temporal lobes. Meningitis-related hearing loss comes from the cochlea."],"study":{"topics":["Conductive versus sensorineural hearing loss","Complications of bacterial meningitis"],"comps":["NE-20-J","NE-20-G8"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C36","system":"NE","dx":"Herpes simplex virus type 1 encephalitis of the temporal lobe","codes":["NE-20-G6","NE-20-G4"],"case":"A 36-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by her husband because of 3 days of fever, headache, and increasingly strange behavior. She has complained of smelling burning rubber, and this morning she had a seizure that began with lip smacking. Her temperature is 38.8 °C (101.8 °F). She is disoriented and has difficulty naming objects. MRI of the brain shows T2 hyperintensity with small hemorrhages in the left medial temporal lobe and insula. CSF analysis shows leukocytes 180/µL (N, 0 to 5/µL), mostly lymphocytes; erythrocytes 400/µL (N, 0/µL); glucose 62 mg/dL (N, 40 to 70 mg/dL); and protein 95 mg/dL (N, 15 to 45 mg/dL). PCR of the CSF is positive for herpes simplex virus type 1 DNA. Diagnosis: HSV-1 encephalitis of the temporal lobe.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C36-Q1","stem":"A 2-week-old boy born by vaginal delivery to a mother who had active genital vesicles at the time of birth develops lethargy and seizures. He was well during his first week. Which of the following herpesviruses is the most likely cause of his encephalitis?","options":["Varicella-zoster virus","Epstein-Barr virus","Cytomegalovirus","Herpes simplex virus 2"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-G6","obj":"NE-20-G.7","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing and route: infection picked up during passage through a birth canal with active genital lesions.","Genital herpes is most often HSV-2, and neonatal HSV encephalitis appears in the first weeks of life.","Contrast congenital CMV, which crosses the placenta and is present at birth with periventricular calcifications."],"why":"Each herpesvirus has its own CNS pattern. HSV-1 causes hemorrhagic encephalitis of the temporal lobes in adults, like this woman's. HSV-2 causes aseptic meningitis in adults and, when passed from the birth canal, neonatal encephalitis that appears in the first weeks of life. CMV causes congenital infection with periventricular calcifications and, in advanced AIDS, encephalitis and ventriculitis. EBV is linked to primary CNS lymphoma in AIDS.","whyNot":["Varicella-zoster virus causes chickenpox and shingles. Congenital varicella causes limb and skin scarring, and it is not linked to genital vesicles.","EBV causes infectious mononucleosis and is linked to primary CNS lymphoma in AIDS. It is not a cause of neonatal encephalitis from the birth canal.","Congenital CMV is acquired across the placenta during pregnancy and shows at birth with periventricular calcifications, microcephaly, and hearing loss. It is not tied to genital vesicles at delivery.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["CNS herpesvirus diseases","Neonatal HSV"],"comps":["NE-20-G6","NE-19"]}},{"id":"NE-C36-Q2","stem":"Before this illness, the virus that caused her encephalitis most likely persisted in a latent state in which of the following locations?","options":["Trigeminal ganglion neurons","Memory B lymphocytes","Sacral dorsal root ganglia","Renal tubular epithelial cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-19","obj":"NE-19.4","dok":2,"approach":["HSV is an enveloped double-stranded DNA virus that establishes latency in sensory ganglion neurons.","HSV-1 usually infects the oral and facial region first, so it becomes latent in the trigeminal ganglion.","On reactivation it travels along axons, and in encephalitis it reaches the temporal and orbitofrontal lobes."],"why":"Herpesviruses are enveloped double-stranded DNA viruses that remain for life after the first infection. HSV-1 enters sensory nerve endings in the mouth or face, travels up the axon to the trigeminal ganglion, and stays there as a quiet episome. Reactivation sends virus back down the axon to cause cold sores, and rarely the virus reaches the brain along trigeminal or olfactory pathways and causes temporal lobe encephalitis. JC polyomavirus is a different nervous system DNA virus: nonenveloped, latent in the kidney, and reactivated in immunosuppression.","whyNot":["Correct.","Memory B lymphocytes are the latent site of Epstein-Barr virus.","Sacral dorsal root ganglia are the usual latent site of HSV-2 after genital infection.","Renal tubular epithelium is the latent site of JC and BK polyomaviruses."],"study":{"topics":["Herpesvirus latency","DNA viruses of the nervous system"],"comps":["NE-19","NE-20-G6"]}},{"id":"NE-C36-Q3","stem":"During her seizure, temporal lobe neurons fire action potentials at very high rates. Which of the following sets the shortest possible interval between two action potentials in one of these neurons?","options":["Closure of potassium leak channels at rest","Inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels","Activity of the sodium-potassium ATPase","Opening of delayed rectifier potassium channels"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-13","obj":"NE-13.2","dok":2,"approach":["The shortest interval between spikes is the absolute refractory period.","During this period no stimulus, however strong, can trigger a second spike.","This is because voltage-gated sodium channels are inactivated and cannot reopen until the membrane repolarizes."],"why":"An action potential starts when depolarization reaches threshold and voltage-gated sodium channels open. Within about a millisecond their inactivation gates close, and delayed potassium channels open to repolarize the membrane, often overshooting into an afterhyperpolarization. While the sodium channels are inactivated, no second action potential is possible (absolute refractory period). As they recover during repolarization, a stronger than normal stimulus can fire the cell (relative refractory period). The absolute refractory period sets the maximum firing rate.","whyNot":["Potassium leak channels set the resting membrane potential. They do not cause the refractory period.","Correct.","The sodium-potassium ATPase maintains ion gradients over time but is not what limits the interval between individual spikes.","Delayed potassium channels repolarize the membrane and cause the afterhyperpolarization, which underlies the relative refractory period. A strong stimulus can still fire the cell then."],"study":{"topics":["Action potential phases","Refractory periods"],"comps":["NE-13","NE-2"]}},{"id":"NE-C36-Q4","stem":"Two years later, she develops pain, redness, tearing, and photophobia of the right eye. Fluorescein staining shows a branching linear ulcer of the corneal epithelium with terminal bulbs. She does not wear contact lenses. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Herpes simplex virus","Adenovirus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Acanthamoeba castellanii"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-J5","obj":"NE-20-J.5","dok":2,"approach":["A branching (dendritic) ulcer that stains with fluorescein is the hallmark of HSV keratitis.","HSV-1 latent in the trigeminal ganglion can reactivate along the ophthalmic division to the cornea.","No contact lens use makes Pseudomonas and Acanthamoeba less likely."],"why":"Herpes simplex keratitis is usually reactivation of HSV-1 from the trigeminal ganglion along the ophthalmic nerve. It produces a dendritic ulcer of the corneal epithelium with terminal bulbs that stains with fluorescein. Repeated episodes can scar the stroma and are a major infectious cause of corneal blindness. Anterior uveitis (iritis) can also cause a painful red eye with photophobia, but it shows a ring of redness around the cornea (ciliary flush), a small irregular pupil, and cells in the anterior chamber, without an epithelial ulcer.","whyNot":["Correct.","Adenovirus causes epidemic keratoconjunctivitis with a watery discharge, preauricular nodes, and subepithelial infiltrates, not a branching ulcer.","Pseudomonas causes a rapidly spreading corneal ulcer with pus, mostly in contact lens wearers. It does not form a dendritic pattern.","Acanthamoeba keratitis occurs in contact lens wearers exposed to tap or pool water and causes severe pain with a ring-shaped stromal infiltrate."],"study":{"topics":["Herpes keratitis","Keratitis versus iritis"],"comps":["NE-20-J5","NE-20-G6"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C37","system":"NE","dx":"West Nile virus encephalitis with acute flaccid paralysis","codes":["NE-20-G1","NE-20-G4"],"case":"A 71-year-old man is brought to the emergency department in late August because of 4 days of fever, headache, and confusion and 1 day of weakness of his left leg. He lives in Colorado and spends his evenings gardening, and he recalls many mosquito bites. His temperature is 39.1 °C (102.4 °F). He is disoriented to place and time. The left leg has flaccid weakness with absent knee and ankle reflexes. Sensation is intact throughout. CSF analysis shows leukocytes 140/µL (N, 0 to 5/µL) with 70% lymphocytes, glucose 64 mg/dL (N, 40 to 70 mg/dL), and protein 110 mg/dL (N, 15 to 45 mg/dL). IgM antibody to West Nile virus is detected in the CSF. Diagnosis: West Nile virus encephalitis with acute flaccid paralysis.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C37-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the role of humans in the transmission cycle of this virus?","options":["Dead-end hosts with viremia too low to infect mosquitoes","Amplifying hosts whose viremia infects feeding mosquitoes","Reservoir hosts that maintain the virus between seasons","Primary hosts that spread virus by respiratory droplets"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-G1","obj":"NE-20-G.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the West Nile cycle: Culex mosquitoes pass the virus among birds, which are the reservoir and amplifying hosts.","Infected mosquitoes sometimes bite humans or horses.","Human viremia is brief and low, so a mosquito biting a person rarely picks up enough virus to continue the cycle."],"why":"West Nile virus and other arboviruses such as St. Louis encephalitis and eastern and western equine encephalitis viruses are maintained in a bird-mosquito-bird cycle. Birds develop high viremia and infect the Culex mosquitoes that feed on them. Humans and horses are incidental, dead-end hosts: their viremia is too low to infect mosquitoes. Most human infections cause no symptoms, and fewer than about 1 in 100 cause neuroinvasive disease, with older adults at highest risk of encephalitis and acute flaccid paralysis. Rare spread by transfusion or organ transplant still occurs.","whyNot":["Correct.","Birds are the amplifying hosts. Human viremia is too low to infect mosquitoes.","Birds, with mosquitoes, maintain the virus in nature. Humans do not carry it between seasons.","West Nile virus is not spread between people by respiratory droplets. It reaches humans through mosquito bites."],"study":{"topics":["Arbovirus transmission","West Nile virus"],"comps":["NE-20-G1","NE-19"]}},{"id":"NE-C37-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the structure and genome of the virus causing his illness?","options":["Nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA","Enveloped, negative-sense single-stranded RNA","Enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA","Enveloped, double-stranded linear DNA"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-19","obj":"NE-19.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the virus: a mosquito-borne encephalitis with bird reservoirs in late summer is West Nile virus.","Classify it: West Nile is a flavivirus.","Flaviviruses are enveloped and have a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome."],"why":"West Nile virus is a flavivirus. It has a lipid envelope and a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome that host ribosomes can translate directly. The envelope is easily damaged by drying and detergents, which fits its spread by mosquito bite rather than by contaminated surfaces. Compare the other nervous system RNA viruses: enteroviruses such as poliovirus are nonenveloped positive-sense picornaviruses spread by the fecal-oral route, and rabies is a bullet-shaped, enveloped, negative-sense rhabdovirus that travels up axons.","whyNot":["This describes enteroviruses such as poliovirus, coxsackievirus, and echovirus. Being nonenveloped lets them survive the gut and spread by the fecal-oral route.","This describes rabies virus, a rhabdovirus carried by bites from bats, raccoons, and dogs.","Correct.","This describes herpesviruses, such as HSV, which become latent in sensory ganglia."],"study":{"topics":["RNA viruses of the nervous system","Flaviviruses"],"comps":["NE-19","NE-20-G1"]}},{"id":"NE-C37-Q3","stem":"His leg weakness resembles that of paralytic poliomyelitis. In both conditions, the virus damages which of the following structures?","options":["Dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons","Anterior horn motor neurons","Lateral corticospinal tract axons","Motor end plates at the neuromuscular junction"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-G10","obj":"NE-20-G.12","dok":3,"approach":["Sort the signs: flaccid weakness with absent reflexes is a lower motor neuron pattern, and sensation is intact.","Asymmetric, purely motor lower motor neuron weakness with fever points to the cell bodies of motor neurons.","These cell bodies lie in the anterior (ventral) horn of the spinal cord gray matter."],"why":"Poliovirus is a fecal-oral enterovirus that multiplies in the gut and lymphoid tissue, then in a small fraction of people reaches the spinal cord and destroys anterior horn motor neurons. West Nile virus can attack the same neurons. Loss of the lower motor neuron causes asymmetric flaccid paralysis, absent reflexes, fasciculations, and later atrophy, with sensation spared because sensory neurons are not involved. Decades after polio, survivors can develop new weakness and fatigue (post-polio syndrome) as the remaining enlarged motor units fail.","whyNot":["Damage to dorsal root ganglia would cause sensory loss and sensory ataxia. His sensation is intact.","Correct.","Lateral corticospinal tract damage is an upper motor neuron lesion with spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a Babinski sign, not flaccid weakness with absent reflexes.","Disorders of the neuromuscular junction, such as botulism or myasthenia gravis, cause symmetric or fluctuating weakness that often begins in cranial muscles. His weakness is limited to one leg."],"study":{"topics":["Poliomyelitis","Upper versus lower motor neuron signs"],"comps":["NE-20-G10","NE-7"]}},{"id":"NE-C37-Q4","stem":"He dies 2 weeks later. Microscopic examination of his brainstem and spinal cord is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Perivascular lymphocytic cuffs and microglial nodules","Neutrophilic exudate filling the subarachnoid space","Eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions in Purkinje cells","Spongiform vacuoles in neuropil without inflammation"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-G4","obj":"NE-20-G.4","dok":2,"approach":["His CSF shows lymphocytes with normal glucose, which points to viral rather than bacterial infection.","Recall the general tissue pattern of viral encephalitis.","It includes lymphocytes around blood vessels, clusters of microglia, and microglia engulfing dying neurons (neuronophagia)."],"why":"Most viral encephalitides share a common tissue pattern. Lymphocytes and macrophages gather around small vessels (perivascular cuffs), microglia form small clusters called microglial nodules, and microglia engulf infected dying neurons (neuronophagia). Some viruses add a specific clue: HSV causes hemorrhagic necrosis of the temporal lobes, and rabies causes Negri bodies, eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions in hippocampal and Purkinje neurons. Rabies is acquired from bat, raccoon, or dog bites and causes agitation and hydrophobia.","whyNot":["Correct.","A neutrophilic exudate in the subarachnoid space marks bacterial meningitis, with low CSF glucose.","Negri bodies in Purkinje and hippocampal neurons mark rabies, which has a different history: an animal bite, agitation, and hydrophobia.","Spongiform change without inflammation marks prion disease, such as Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease."],"study":{"topics":["Viral encephalitis histology","Rabies"],"comps":["NE-20-G4","NE-2"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C38","system":"NE","dx":"Infant botulism","codes":["NE-20-G2"],"case":"A 4-month-old boy is brought to the physician because of 5 days of constipation followed by 2 days of poor feeding, a weak cry, and decreased movement. He was previously healthy and developing normally. His temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F). He has bilateral ptosis, dilated pupils that react sluggishly to light, a weak suck and gag, and generalized hypotonia with poor head control. Deep tendon reflexes are decreased. He follows faces with his eyes and responds to touch on all four limbs. Botulinum toxin is detected in his stool. Diagnosis: infant botulism.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C38-Q1","stem":"Stool culture also grows Clostridium botulinum. Which of the following best explains how this infant acquired the disease, in contrast with the usual adult foodborne form?","options":["Ingestion of preformed toxin in home-canned food","Spores germinating in a contaminated skin wound","Inhalation of toxin in contaminated household dust","Spores germinating in the colon and making toxin"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-G2","obj":"NE-20-G.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note that both the organism and its toxin are in the stool, so the bacteria are living in his gut.","Young infants lack the mature gut flora that normally keeps spores from germinating.","Swallowed spores, from honey or soil dust, germinate in the colon and make toxin there."],"why":"Botulism has three main forms with the same toxin and the same neurologic picture. In foodborne botulism, adults eat preformed toxin in improperly canned or preserved food. In infant botulism, babies under 1 year swallow spores from honey or environmental dust, and the spores germinate and colonize the immature colon, producing toxin in place. Constipation is often the first sign. In wound botulism, spores germinate in a contaminated wound, often from injection drug use. All forms cause descending symmetric flaccid paralysis that starts with cranial nerves, without fever or sensory loss.","whyNot":["Preformed toxin in food causes adult foodborne botulism. Organisms do not need to grow in the gut, and constipation is not the first sign.","Spores growing in a wound cause wound botulism, typically in people who inject drugs. He has no wound.","Spores, not toxin, are carried in household dust. Inhaled toxin is a bioterrorism concern and would not explain the organism growing in his stool.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Forms of botulism","Infant botulism"],"comps":["NE-20-G2","NE-19"]}},{"id":"NE-C38-Q2","stem":"Electromyography shows small compound muscle action potentials that increase in amplitude during rapid repetitive nerve stimulation at 50 Hz. Which of the following best explains this increase?","options":["Removal of bound toxin from terminals by nerve activity","Saturation of acetylcholinesterase prolonging ACh action","Recovery of postsynaptic receptors from desensitization","Presynaptic calcium buildup increasing vesicle release"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-13","obj":"NE-13.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the steps of transmission: an action potential opens presynaptic calcium channels, calcium triggers SNARE-mediated vesicle fusion, and ACh acts on nicotinic receptors.","Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins, so fewer vesicles are released and the muscle response is small.","With rapid stimulation, calcium builds up in the terminal and raises the chance of release from the remaining working machinery."],"why":"At the neuromuscular junction, an action potential opens voltage-gated calcium channels in the nerve terminal. Calcium triggers SNARE proteins to fuse vesicles with the membrane and release acetylcholine, which opens nicotinic receptors on the muscle. Acetylcholinesterase ends the signal. Botulinum toxin is a protease that cleaves SNARE proteins, so less ACh is released and the baseline response is small. During rapid stimulation, calcium accumulates in the terminal and increases release, so the response grows. Lambert-Eaton syndrome shows the same increment for a related reason, while myasthenia gravis shows a decrement with slow repetitive stimulation because of fewer postsynaptic receptors.","whyNot":["Botulinum toxin is internalized and its cleavage of SNARE proteins is not reversed by nerve activity. Recovery takes weeks to months while terminals sprout and SNAREs are replaced.","Acetylcholinesterase is not saturated at a normal junction, and the toxin's problem is too little ACh release, not faster breakdown.","The primary defect is presynaptic. Receptor desensitization would reduce, not increase, the response during rapid stimulation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neuromuscular transmission","Repetitive nerve stimulation patterns"],"comps":["NE-13","NE-19"]}},{"id":"NE-C38-Q3","stem":"A 33-year-old woman has drooping eyelids and double vision that worsen toward evening. Her serum contains antibodies to the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, and CT scan of the chest shows an anterior mediastinal mass. Which of the following immune mechanisms most directly causes her weakness?","options":["Immune complex deposition in muscle capillaries","IgG binding to a fixed cell surface antigen","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation at the end plate","T cell-mediated injury of the motor nerve terminal"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-18","obj":"NE-18.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the disease: fatiguable ptosis and diplopia with acetylcholine receptor antibodies and a thymic mass is myasthenia gravis.","Ask what the antibody targets: a receptor fixed on the muscle cell surface.","Antibody against a cell surface or matrix antigen is type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"In myasthenia gravis, IgG antibodies bind nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. They block the receptor, cross-link receptors so they are taken into the cell and destroyed, and activate complement that damages the end plate. This is type II hypersensitivity. It is associated with thymic hyperplasia and thymoma. Lambert-Eaton syndrome is also type II, with antibodies to presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, often with small cell lung cancer. Multiple sclerosis and ADEM are T cell-mediated. Botulism, in contrast, is caused by a bacterial toxin and involves no immune attack.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition is type III hypersensitivity, as in serum sickness and lupus nephritis. In myasthenia gravis the antibody binds a fixed tissue antigen.","Correct.","IgE-mediated mast cell release is type I hypersensitivity, as in anaphylaxis and asthma. It does not cause fatiguable weakness.","T cell-mediated injury is type IV hypersensitivity, as in multiple sclerosis. Myasthenia gravis is caused by antibodies against a postsynaptic receptor."],"study":{"topics":["Myasthenia gravis immunology","Hypersensitivity types in neurologic disease"],"comps":["NE-18","NE-13"]}},{"id":"NE-C38-Q4","stem":"The infant's dilated, sluggish pupils result from blocked acetylcholine release by postganglionic parasympathetic neurons. In which of the following ganglia are the cell bodies of these neurons located?","options":["Superior cervical ganglion","Otic ganglion","Ciliary ganglion","Pterygopalatine ganglion"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-9","obj":"NE-9.4","dok":2,"approach":["The muscle that constricts the pupil is the sphincter pupillae, supplied by parasympathetic fibers.","Preganglionic fibers come from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus and travel with CN III.","They synapse in the ciliary ganglion, whose postganglionic fibers reach the eye in the short ciliary nerves."],"why":"The four parasympathetic ganglia of the head each serve specific targets. The ciliary ganglion (CN III) supplies the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle. The pterygopalatine ganglion (CN VII) supplies the lacrimal gland and nasal glands. The submandibular ganglion (CN VII) supplies the submandibular and sublingual glands. The otic ganglion (CN IX) supplies the parotid. Botulinum toxin blocks ACh release at all cholinergic terminals, including these autonomic fibers, so it causes dilated pupils, dry mouth, and constipation as well as weakness.","whyNot":["The superior cervical ganglion is sympathetic. It supplies the pupillary dilator, and its loss causes a small pupil, as in Horner syndrome.","The otic ganglion relays CN IX fibers to the parotid gland. Blocking it causes dry mouth, not pupil changes.","Correct.","The pterygopalatine ganglion relays CN VII fibers to the lacrimal gland and nasal mucosa."],"study":{"topics":["Parasympathetic ganglia of the head","Pupillary innervation"],"comps":["NE-9","NE-8"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C39","system":"NE","dx":"Generalized tetanus after a puncture wound","codes":["NE-20-G14"],"case":"A 62-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 2 days of jaw stiffness and difficulty swallowing, followed by painful spasms of her neck and back muscles. Ten days ago she stepped on a nail while gardening and cleaned the wound herself. Her last tetanus immunization was in childhood. Her temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 148/88 mm Hg. She is alert and oriented. She cannot open her mouth more than 1 cm, and her face shows a fixed grin with raised eyebrows. A loud noise triggers a spasm that arches her back. Sensation is normal. A healing puncture wound is present on the sole of the right foot. Diagnosis: generalized tetanus.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C39-Q1","stem":"After recovery, she is given tetanus toxoid. Which of the following best describes how this vaccine prevents disease?","options":["IgG neutralizes toxin before it binds nerve terminals","Antibodies against the bacterium block spore germination","Transferred antitoxin gives lasting passive immunity","Live attenuated bacteria induce intestinal IgA"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-G14","obj":"NE-20-G.16","dok":2,"approach":["A toxoid is a toxin made harmless, usually with formaldehyde, that keeps its antigenic shape.","It induces active immunity: IgG and memory B cells against tetanospasmin.","Circulating IgG binds toxin released in a wound before it can enter nerve endings."],"why":"Tetanus is caused entirely by tetanospasmin, made by Clostridium tetani after spores enter a deep or dirty wound, or the umbilical stump in newborns. The toxoid vaccine induces IgG antitoxin, which neutralizes the toxin in the blood and tissues before it binds and enters motor nerve terminals. Once inside neurons, toxin is out of reach of antibody. Recovering from tetanus does not make a person immune, because the amount of toxin that causes disease is too small to stimulate antibodies, so she still needs the vaccine. Boosters are given about every 10 years, and vaccinating pregnant women protects newborns because IgG crosses the placenta.","whyNot":["Correct.","The toxoid targets the toxin, not the bacterium, and spore germination in a wound is not prevented by vaccination.","Passive immunity comes from tetanus immune globulin, given for wounds in poorly immunized people. It lasts only weeks. The toxoid produces active, long-lasting immunity.","Tetanus toxoid is an inactivated toxin given by injection. It is not a live vaccine, and gut IgA plays no part in protection."],"study":{"topics":["Tetanus prevention","Toxoid vaccines"],"comps":["NE-20-G14","NE-18"]}},{"id":"NE-C39-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the action of the toxin responsible for her spasms?","options":["Binds and activates nicotinic receptors on muscle","Blocks glycine and GABA release from spinal interneurons","Blocks acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction","Inhibits acetylcholinesterase at the motor end plate"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-19","obj":"NE-19.3","dok":3,"approach":["Her paralysis is spastic: sustained contraction and spasms, not weakness.","Spastic paralysis from a toxin means loss of inhibition of motor neurons.","Tetanospasmin travels retrogradely up motor axons to the spinal cord and blocks release of inhibitory transmitters from interneurons."],"why":"Tetanospasmin enters motor nerve terminals near the wound and travels up the axon by retrograde transport to the spinal cord and brainstem. It then crosses into inhibitory interneurons, including Renshaw cells, and cleaves the SNARE protein synaptobrevin, so these cells cannot release glycine and GABA. Motor neurons lose their inhibition and fire uncontrollably, causing trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonus, and spasms. Botulinum toxin uses the same type of SNARE cleavage but stays at the neuromuscular junction, so it blocks ACh release and causes flaccid paralysis.","whyNot":["Sustained activation of muscle nicotinic receptors causes brief fasciculations and then a depolarizing block with flaccid weakness. Tetanospasmin does not act on muscle receptors.","Correct.","Blocking ACh release at the neuromuscular junction is the action of botulinum toxin, which causes flaccid, not spastic, paralysis.","Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors, such as organophosphates, cause cholinergic excess with salivation, miosis, and muscle twitching followed by weakness. They are not bacterial toxins."],"study":{"topics":["Tetanospasmin mechanism","Botulinum versus tetanus toxin"],"comps":["NE-19","NE-20-G14"]}},{"id":"NE-C39-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her alpha motor neurons now fire in prolonged bursts after a sensory stimulus such as a loud noise?","options":["Action potential amplitude grows with stimulus strength","Graded potentials spread along the axon without decay","The motor neuron refractory period becomes shorter","EPSPs summate at the axon hillock without IPSPs"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-13","obj":"NE-13.3","dok":3,"approach":["A motor neuron fires when the sum of its synaptic inputs depolarizes the axon hillock to threshold.","Excitatory inputs give EPSPs, and inhibitory interneurons normally give IPSPs that offset them.","With inhibition removed by the toxin, EPSPs from a stimulus summate unopposed and drive repeated firing."],"why":"EPSPs and IPSPs are graded potentials. Their size varies with input, they fade with distance, and they add together in time (temporal summation) and across many synapses (spatial summation). The axon hillock has the highest density of sodium channels and the lowest threshold, so the net sum there decides whether an action potential fires. Glycine and GABA from inhibitory interneurons open chloride channels and create IPSPs. When tetanospasmin removes them, any excitatory input, such as a startle from a loud noise, pushes many motor neurons to threshold at once and keeps them firing.","whyNot":["Action potentials are all-or-none. A stronger stimulus raises firing frequency, not the size of each spike.","Graded potentials decay with distance. Only action potentials travel along the axon without losing size.","The refractory period depends on sodium channel inactivation and does not change in tetanus. The problem is loss of inhibitory input.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Graded potentials and summation","Inhibitory interneurons"],"comps":["NE-13","NE-15"]}},{"id":"NE-C39-Q4","stem":"On the fifth hospital day, she has profuse sweating, a pulse of 140/min, and blood pressure that swings between 190/110 and 90/50 mm Hg. This is attributed to loss of inhibition of sympathetic preganglionic neurons. In which of the following locations are the cell bodies of these neurons found?","options":["Dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus","Paravertebral chain ganglia from T1 to L2","Lateral horn of spinal segments T1 to L2","Gray matter of sacral spinal segments S2 to S4"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-9","obj":"NE-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the sympathetic system has thoracolumbar outflow and the parasympathetic system has craniosacral outflow.","Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies sit in the intermediolateral cell column, which forms the lateral horn of spinal segments T1 to L2.","Their axons leave through ventral roots and white rami to synapse in chain or prevertebral ganglia, where the postganglionic cell bodies lie."],"why":"Sympathetic preganglionic neurons lie in the lateral horn (intermediolateral cell column) of the spinal cord from T1 to L2. Their short axons synapse in paravertebral chain ganglia or prevertebral ganglia near the aorta, and long postganglionic axons reach the target organs. Parasympathetic preganglionic neurons lie in brainstem nuclei of CN III, VII, IX, and X and in sacral segments S2 to S4, with long preganglionic axons that synapse in ganglia near or in the target organ. In tetanus, toxin removes inhibition of the sympathetic neurons too, causing autonomic storms that are a major cause of death.","whyNot":["The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus holds parasympathetic preganglionic neurons for the thoracic and abdominal organs. Vagal activity slows the heart.","Paravertebral chain ganglia contain the sympathetic postganglionic cell bodies, not the preganglionic ones.","Correct.","Sacral segments S2 to S4 hold parasympathetic preganglionic neurons that supply the bladder, rectum, and genitals."],"study":{"topics":["Sympathetic and parasympathetic outflow","Autonomic dysfunction in tetanus"],"comps":["NE-9","NE-3"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C40","system":"NE","dx":"Cryptococcal meningitis in advanced HIV infection","codes":["NE-20-G5","NE-20-G12"],"case":"A 34-year-old man with HIV infection who stopped antiretroviral therapy 2 years ago comes to the emergency department because of 3 weeks of worsening headache, fever, and nausea and 2 days of confusion. His temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F). He is drowsy and has mild neck stiffness and bilateral papilledema. His CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 36/mm3 (N, 500 to 1,500/mm3). Lumbar puncture shows an opening pressure of 38 cm H2O (N, 10 to 20 cm H2O). CSF analysis shows leukocytes 22/µL (N, 0 to 5/µL), mostly lymphocytes; glucose 30 mg/dL (N, 40 to 70 mg/dL); and protein 85 mg/dL (N, 15 to 45 mg/dL). CSF cryptococcal antigen is positive. Diagnosis: cryptococcal meningitis in advanced HIV infection.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C40-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is most likely to be seen on microscopic examination of his CSF?","options":["Broad nonseptate hyphae with right-angle branching","Narrow septate hyphae with acute-angle branching","Spherules filled with endospores","Budding yeasts surrounded by wide clear halos"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-19","obj":"NE-19.6","dok":2,"approach":["A positive cryptococcal antigen identifies Cryptococcus neoformans.","Cryptococcus is a yeast with a thick polysaccharide capsule, and the antigen test detects that capsule.","India ink outlines the capsule as a clear halo around budding yeast cells."],"why":"Cryptococcus neoformans is an encapsulated yeast found in soil contaminated with pigeon droppings. It is inhaled, and in people with very low CD4 counts it spreads to the meninges. Its polysaccharide capsule blocks phagocytosis and is the antigen measured in CSF and serum. On India ink, the capsule excludes the ink and appears as a clear halo around round budding yeasts. Mucicarmine stains the capsule red in tissue.","whyNot":["Broad nonseptate hyphae branching at right angles are Mucor and Rhizopus, which invade the sinuses, orbit, and brain in diabetic ketoacidosis.","Narrow septate hyphae with acute-angle branching are Aspergillus, which causes invasive lung disease in neutropenia.","Spherules full of endospores are Coccidioides, from soil in the southwestern United States, which can cause chronic basilar meningitis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cryptococcus neoformans","Fungal morphology"],"comps":["NE-19","NE-20-G5"]}},{"id":"NE-C40-Q2","stem":"Which of the following most likely explains his markedly increased CSF opening pressure?","options":["Increased CSF production by inflamed choroid plexus","Obstruction of the cerebral aqueduct by a granuloma","Yeast and capsule blocking arachnoid granulations","Vasogenic edema from a ring-enhancing brain abscess"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-20-G5","obj":"NE-20-G.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that CSF is made by the choroid plexus and absorbed into venous sinuses through arachnoid granulations.","In cryptococcal meningitis, large numbers of yeasts and shed capsular polysaccharide fill the subarachnoid space.","They clog the arachnoid granulations, so CSF outflow falls and pressure rises, often with normal-sized ventricles."],"why":"Raised opening pressure is common and dangerous in cryptococcal meningitis in AIDS. Yeasts and capsular polysaccharide accumulate in the subarachnoid space and block CSF absorption through the arachnoid granulations. Pressure rises even though inflammation is mild, because a CD4 count this low allows little immune response. High pressure causes headache, papilledema, vision loss, cranial nerve palsies, and death, so it is relieved with repeated lumbar punctures. The other key fungal CNS infection to know is rhinocerebral mucormycosis in diabetic ketoacidosis, with a black necrotic nasal or palatal eschar that spreads to the orbit and brain.","whyNot":["The choroid plexus does not increase CSF output enough to explain this. The problem is reduced absorption.","Aqueduct obstruction causes noncommunicating hydrocephalus with enlarged lateral and third ventricles. Cryptococcal meningitis raises pressure through diffusely blocked absorption, often with normal-sized ventricles.","Correct.","A ring-enhancing abscess would show as a focal mass on imaging, as with toxoplasmosis. His findings fit diffuse meningitis."],"study":{"topics":["Cryptococcal meningitis","CSF absorption"],"comps":["NE-20-G5","NE-10"]}},{"id":"NE-C40-Q3","stem":"He again declines antiretroviral therapy after treatment. Four months later, he has a focal seizure followed by right-sided weakness. He has lived his whole life in Ohio and keeps two outdoor cats. MRI of the brain shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions with surrounding edema in the basal ganglia and at the gray-white junction. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Toxoplasma gondii","Taenia solium","Naegleria fowleri","Plasmodium falciparum"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-G4","obj":"NE-20-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the setting: AIDS with a CD4 count below 100/mm3.","Multiple ring-enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia in this setting are most often toxoplasmosis, from reactivation of tissue cysts.","Check the other parasites against the history: no travel, no freshwater exposure, and lesions that do not fit them."],"why":"Toxoplasma gondii is acquired from oocysts in cat feces or tissue cysts in undercooked meat, and it stays latent in brain and muscle for life. When the CD4 count falls below about 100/mm3, cysts reactivate and form multiple ring-enhancing abscesses, often in the basal ganglia and at the gray-white junction, causing seizures and focal deficits. Primary CNS lymphoma is the main alternative and is often a single lesion. Neurocysticercosis comes from swallowing Taenia solium eggs shed by a human tapeworm carrier, and Naegleria fowleri enters through the cribriform plate after swimming in warm fresh water.","whyNot":["Correct.","Neurocysticercosis causes brain cysts and seizures after swallowing T. solium eggs, usually in people from or exposed to endemic areas. It is not tied to AIDS, and cysts often show a scolex or calcify.","Naegleria fowleri causes a rapidly fatal meningoencephalitis within days of swimming in warm fresh water. It does not cause ring-enhancing lesions.","Cerebral malaria follows travel to an endemic area and causes coma from sequestered parasitized red cells, not ring-enhancing lesions."],"study":{"topics":["CNS toxoplasmosis","Parasitic CNS infections"],"comps":["NE-20-G4","NE-19"]}},{"id":"NE-C40-Q4","stem":"Two years later, still untreated, he develops aphasia, right arm weakness, and visual field loss that progress over 6 weeks. He is afebrile. MRI shows multiple asymmetric nonenhancing lesions of the subcortical white matter without mass effect. Which of the following is most likely to be seen in a brain biopsy specimen?","options":["Multinucleated giant cells with microglial nodules","Oligodendrocytes with enlarged glassy nuclei","Necrotic foci containing tachyzoites and cysts","Atypical B cells cuffing blood vessels"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-G12","obj":"NE-20-G.14","dok":3,"approach":["Note the setting of severe immunosuppression and the course over weeks.","Multifocal nonenhancing white matter lesions without mass effect suggest progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML).","PML is JC virus infection that kills oligodendrocytes, whose nuclei swell with viral inclusions."],"why":"Most adults carry JC polyomavirus latently in the kidney. In severe immunosuppression, such as AIDS with a very low CD4 count or treatment with natalizumab or rituximab, the virus reactivates and infects oligodendrocytes. It lyses them, causing patchy demyelination that grows and merges in the subcortical white matter. Infected oligodendrocytes have enlarged nuclei with glassy viral inclusions, and astrocytes become large and bizarre. Patients develop rapidly progressive focal deficits, and the lesions typically do not enhance or cause mass effect.","whyNot":["Multinucleated giant cells and microglial nodules mark HIV encephalitis, which causes slowly progressive dementia with diffuse, symmetric white matter changes rather than focal deficits.","Correct.","Necrotic foci with tachyzoites and cysts mark toxoplasmosis, which forms ring-enhancing lesions with edema.","Atypical B cells around vessels mark primary CNS lymphoma, which enhances and usually causes mass effect."],"study":{"topics":["Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy","CNS disorders in AIDS"],"comps":["NE-20-G12","NE-19"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C41","system":"NE","dx":"Thoracic herpes zoster with postherpetic neuralgia","codes":["NE-20-G15"],"case":"A 72-year-old woman comes to the physician because of burning pain on the left side of her trunk for 4 months. Four months ago she had 2 days of tingling pain, followed by clusters of painful vesicles on an erythematous base in a band that wrapped from the left side of her lower back around to the level of the umbilicus. The rash crusted and healed within 3 weeks, but the pain has continued. Light contact from clothing over the area causes severe burning pain. She had chickenpox as a child. Physical examination shows faint hypopigmented scars in the same band, stopping at the midline, with allodynia over the scarred area. Strength and reflexes are normal. Diagnosis: thoracic herpes zoster with postherpetic neuralgia.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C41-Q1","stem":"Before the rash appeared, the causative virus was most likely present in a latent state in which of the following cells?","options":["Dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons","Sympathetic chain ganglion neurons","Anterior horn motor neurons of the thoracic cord","Schwann cells of the intercostal nerve"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-G15","obj":"NE-20-G.17","dok":2,"approach":["Childhood chickenpox followed decades later by a painful one-sided band rash points to varicella-zoster virus (VZV) reactivation.","A rash that follows one dermatome tells you the virus came down one sensory nerve.","The cell bodies of those sensory fibers sit in the dorsal root ganglion, so that is where VZV stayed latent."],"why":"During chickenpox, VZV travels up sensory nerve fibers from the skin and stays latent in the cell bodies of sensory neurons in dorsal root ganglia and cranial nerve ganglia. When cell-mediated immunity wanes with age or immunosuppression, the virus reactivates in one ganglion and travels back down that nerve to its dermatome. That is why zoster is painful, one-sided, and stops at the midline. Reactivation in the trigeminal ganglion gives herpes zoster ophthalmicus, and reactivation in the geniculate ganglion gives Ramsay Hunt syndrome (facial palsy with ear vesicles).","whyNot":["Correct.","Sympathetic ganglion neurons are autonomic. VZV latency is in sensory ganglia, which explains the dermatomal pain and rash.","Anterior horn cells are motor neurons. Poliovirus targets them, causing flaccid weakness. Her strength is normal and her problem is sensory.","Schwann cells are the target of Mycobacterium leprae, which causes patchy sensory loss. Herpesviruses stay latent in neurons."],"study":{"topics":["VZV latency and reactivation","Herpes zoster variants"],"comps":["NE-20-G15","NE-19"]}},{"id":"NE-C41-Q2","stem":"She notices that firmly rubbing the skin just next to the painful band briefly lessens her pain. Which of the following mechanisms most likely explains this relief?","options":["Activation of C fibers that release substance P onto projection neurons","Activation of A-delta fibers that excite lateral spinothalamic neurons","Inhibition of periaqueductal gray neurons that project to the medulla","Activation of A-beta fibers that excite inhibitory interneurons"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-5","obj":"NE-5.6","dok":3,"approach":["Rubbing activates large myelinated touch fibers (A-beta), not pain fibers.","Recall the gate control theory: touch fibers excite inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn that quiet pain transmission.","Check the other options: anything that drives C or A-delta fibers or blocks descending inhibition would make pain worse."],"why":"In the gate control model, large A-beta touch fibers send collaterals into the dorsal horn that excite inhibitory interneurons, which then reduce the output of pain projection neurons. That is why rubbing a sore area helps. Pain is also reduced from above: the periaqueductal gray activates the rostral ventromedial medulla, whose descending fibers trigger enkephalin release in the dorsal horn. Her ongoing pain after the rash healed is postherpetic neuralgia, a neuropathic pain from damage to the sensory neurons themselves, like phantom limb pain and thalamic pain.","whyNot":["C fibers carry slow burning pain and release substance P and glutamate. Activating them would increase her pain.","A-delta fibers carry fast sharp pain into the spinothalamic tract. Activating them would add pain, not relieve it.","The periaqueductal gray is the start of descending pain inhibition. Inhibiting it would remove a brake on pain and make pain worse.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Gate control theory of pain","Descending pain modulation","Neuropathic pain"],"comps":["NE-5","NE-20-G15"]}},{"id":"NE-C41-Q3","stem":"Based on the location of her rash, reactivation most likely occurred in the ganglion of which of the following spinal nerves?","options":["T8","T10","T6","T12"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-8","obj":"NE-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["Find the landmark in the case: the band reached the front of the trunk at the umbilicus.","Recall the dermatome landmarks: T4 nipple, T6 xiphoid, T10 umbilicus, L1 inguinal ligament.","A thoracic dermatome wraps around from the back to the front, so the whole band belongs to one root, T10."],"why":"Each thoracic spinal nerve supplies a band of skin that runs from the back around to the front midline. The umbilicus sits in the T10 dermatome, so a zoster rash that reaches the umbilicus came from the T10 dorsal root ganglion. Other useful landmarks are C6 for the thumb, T4 for the nipple, L4 for the knee and medial leg, L5 for the dorsum of the foot, and S1 for the lateral foot. These same landmarks let you localize a root lesion from a band of sensory loss.","whyNot":["T8 supplies a band between the xiphoid and the umbilicus, above this rash.","Correct.","T6 supplies the band at the level of the xiphoid process, above the umbilicus.","T12 supplies the band just above the inguinal ligament and pubic area, below the umbilicus."],"study":{"topics":["Dermatome landmarks","Localizing a root lesion"],"comps":["NE-8","NE-5"]}},{"id":"NE-C41-Q4","stem":"During the 2 days of pain before the rash appeared, she was evaluated for an abdominal cause of her pain. Early visceral pain from which of the following organs is most likely to be felt in the same dermatome as her rash?","options":["Heart","Appendix","Esophagus","Central diaphragm"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-5","obj":"NE-5.5","dok":3,"approach":["Her rash is in the T10 dermatome, around the umbilicus.","Referred pain is felt in the dermatome whose spinal segment receives the visceral afferents from that organ.","The appendix is a midgut organ whose visceral pain afferents enter about T10, so early appendicitis pain is felt around the umbilicus."],"why":"Visceral pain fibers travel with sympathetic nerves and enter the spinal cord at specific segments. In the dorsal horn they converge on the same neurons that receive input from the skin of that segment, so the brain reads the pain as coming from the skin. Midgut organs, including the appendix, send pain afferents to about T10, so early appendicitis is felt around the umbilicus. Pain shifts to the right lower quadrant only when inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum, which has somatic innervation. Zoster in its pre-rash phase can mimic this kind of visceral pain.","whyNot":["Cardiac pain enters about T1 to T4 and is felt in the chest and the medial left arm.","Correct.","Esophageal pain is referred to the retrosternal chest, through segments of about T4 to T6, well above T10.","The central diaphragm is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5), so its irritation is felt in the shoulder."],"study":{"topics":["Referred pain","Visceral afferent segments"],"comps":["NE-5","NE-7"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C42","system":"NE","dx":"Complete spinal cord transection at T4 with autonomic dysreflexia","codes":["NE-20-H2"],"case":"A 27-year-old man with a complete spinal cord injury at T4 from a motorcycle collision 14 months ago is brought to the emergency department because of a severe pounding headache and blurred vision for 30 minutes. He uses an indwelling urinary catheter. His temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 46/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 214/118 mm Hg. His face and neck are flushed and sweaty; the skin of his legs is pale and cool. He has spastic paraplegia with hyperreflexia and extensor plantar responses, and no sensation below the nipples. The catheter tubing is kinked and the bladder is distended. Diagnosis: complete spinal cord transection at T4 with autonomic dysreflexia.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C42-Q1","stem":"On the day of his injury, which of the following findings was most likely present in his legs?","options":["Normal tone with intact tendon reflexes","Spastic tone with brisk tendon reflexes","Flaccid tone with absent tendon reflexes","Fasciculations with marked muscle atrophy"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-H2","obj":"NE-20-H.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that he now has upper motor neuron signs, the late phase of a cord transection.","Recall that right after transection the cord below the lesion goes silent (spinal shock).","Spinal shock gives flaccid paralysis and areflexia, which later changes to spasticity and hyperreflexia."],"why":"Spinal shock follows acute cord transection. The cord below the lesion suddenly loses descending excitatory input, so motor neurons become unresponsive. The result is flaccid paralysis, absent reflexes, and, with injuries above about T6, neurogenic hypotension from lost sympathetic tone. Over days to weeks, reflex circuits below the lesion recover and become overactive, producing spastic paralysis with hyperreflexia and Babinski signs. A thoracic injury causes paraplegia, while injury at or above C3 also cuts the phrenic outflow (C3 to C5) and requires a ventilator.","whyNot":["A complete transection removes all descending control, so tone and reflexes cannot stay normal on the day of injury.","Spasticity with brisk reflexes is the late phase, which he has now. It develops after spinal shock fades.","Correct.","Fasciculations and marked atrophy are lower motor neuron signs. A cord transection at T4 leaves the lumbar motor neurons intact."],"study":{"topics":["Spinal shock","Phases of cord transection"],"comps":["NE-20-H2","NE-2"]}},{"id":"NE-C42-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains his slow heart rate during this episode?","options":["Sympathetic discharge from spinal segments below the lesion","Release of epinephrine from the adrenal medulla","Baroreceptor reflex raising vagal output to the heart","Reduced venous return from dilation of leg veins"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-9","obj":"NE-9.5","dok":3,"approach":["The stimulus (a distended bladder) is below the lesion, so it triggers a spinal sympathetic reflex that cannot be damped from above.","Widespread vasoconstriction below the lesion causes severe hypertension.","The intact brainstem senses the high pressure through baroreceptors and slows the heart through the vagus nerve, which is not affected by the cord injury."],"why":"Autonomic dysreflexia occurs with cord injuries at or above about T6, above the splanchnic sympathetic outflow. A noxious stimulus below the lesion, most often a full bladder or bowel, sets off a large spinal sympathetic reflex. Descending inhibitory signals cannot reach the cord, so vessels below the lesion clamp down and blood pressure soars. Baroreceptors signal the brainstem, which raises vagal tone (slowing the heart) and dilates vessels above the lesion (flushing and sweating of the face). The vagus leaves the brainstem directly, so the cord lesion does not block it.","whyNot":["The sympathetic discharge below the lesion explains the hypertension and pale cold legs, but sympathetic input would speed the heart, not slow it.","Epinephrine from the adrenal medulla raises the heart rate through beta-1 receptors.","Correct.","The leg vessels constrict during dysreflexia, and reduced venous return would lower blood pressure and raise heart rate."],"study":{"topics":["Autonomic dysreflexia","Baroreceptor reflex"],"comps":["NE-9","NE-7"]}},{"id":"NE-C42-Q3","stem":"The rise in his blood pressure is first detected by stretch receptors in the carotid sinus. Which of the following describes the nerve that carries this signal and the nucleus where it first synapses?","options":["Vagus nerve to the nucleus ambiguus","Vagus nerve to the nucleus solitarius","Glossopharyngeal nerve to the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus","Glossopharyngeal nerve to the nucleus solitarius"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-7","obj":"NE-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the two baroreceptor sites: carotid sinus versus aortic arch.","Carotid sinus afferents travel in the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX); aortic arch afferents travel in the vagus (CN X).","All visceral sensory input from CN VII, IX, and X ends in the nucleus solitarius."],"why":"The carotid sinus sits at the bifurcation of the common carotid artery and is supplied by the carotid sinus nerve, a branch of CN IX. The aortic arch baroreceptors send their signals in CN X. Both nerves carry these visceral sensory fibers to the nucleus solitarius in the medulla, which then adjusts sympathetic and vagal output. Visceral pain afferents follow a different route: they travel with autonomic nerves to the spinal cord. His bladder afferents enter the cord below the T4 lesion, so the bladder distension never reaches conscious awareness.","whyNot":["The vagus does carry aortic arch signals, but the nucleus ambiguus is a motor nucleus for the pharynx, larynx, and part of the cardiac vagal output.","This is the route for aortic arch baroreceptors. The stem specifies the carotid sinus, which uses CN IX.","The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus is an efferent parasympathetic nucleus. It sends signals out, not in.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Baroreceptor afferent pathways","Nucleus solitarius"],"comps":["NE-7","NE-9"]}},{"id":"NE-C42-Q4","stem":"Another patient on the spinal injury unit has a stab wound that cut the right half of the spinal cord at T10. Which of the following findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["Loss of pain sense in both legs with vibration sense preserved","Right leg weakness with loss of pain sense in the left leg","Left leg weakness with loss of pain sense in the right leg","Right leg weakness with loss of vibration sense in the left leg"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-5","obj":"NE-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["A cut through half the cord is a Brown-Sequard hemisection.","The corticospinal tract and dorsal columns cross in the medulla, so their deficits are on the same side as the lesion.","The spinothalamic tract crosses within one or two segments of entry, so pain and temperature loss is on the opposite side."],"why":"In a hemisection, the lateral corticospinal tract and the dorsal columns are cut on the side of the lesion, and at the spinal level both tracts serve the same side of the body (they cross in the medulla). That gives weakness and loss of vibration and position sense on the right. Pain and temperature fibers from the left leg have already crossed to the right spinothalamic tract, so they are cut too, giving loss of pain and temperature in the left leg starting one or two segments below the lesion. Bilateral loss of pain with preserved touch is the pattern of syringomyelia, a central cord lesion.","whyNot":["Bilateral pain loss with preserved vibration fits a central cord lesion such as syringomyelia, which usually gives a cape-like pattern in the arms.","Correct.","This reverses the sides. Motor loss is on the side of the lesion, and pain loss is on the opposite side.","Weakness is ipsilateral, but vibration loss is also ipsilateral because the dorsal columns do not cross until the medulla."],"study":{"topics":["Brown-Sequard syndrome","Spinal tract crossing points"],"comps":["NE-5","NE-2"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C43","system":"NE","dx":"Tabes dorsalis (tertiary neurosyphilis)","codes":["NE-20-G9"],"case":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-year history of an unsteady gait that is worse in the dark and brief, stabbing pains in his legs. He has fallen three times. Physical examination shows a wide-based, stamping gait. He sways and falls when he closes his eyes while standing with feet together. Vibration and position sense are absent in the feet. Patellar and ankle reflexes are absent. Strength is normal. Both pupils are small and irregular; they constrict with near focus but not to light. Serum rapid plasma reagin is reactive. Cerebrospinal fluid shows 22 lymphocytes/mm3 (normal 0 to 5) and a reactive VDRL test. Diagnosis: tabes dorsalis (tertiary neurosyphilis).","questions":[{"id":"NE-C43-Q1","stem":"Degeneration of which of the following structures most directly explains his positive Romberg sign?","options":["Lateral corticospinal tracts","Anterior horn motor neurons","Cerebellar vermis Purkinje cells","Dorsal roots and dorsal columns"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-G9","obj":"NE-20-G.11","dok":2,"approach":["A positive Romberg sign means the patient can balance with vision but not without it, so the problem is lost proprioception.","Proprioception from the legs enters through the dorsal roots and ascends in the dorsal columns (fasciculus gracilis).","Tabes dorsalis destroys exactly these: the dorsal roots and the dorsal columns of the lower cord."],"why":"In tabes dorsalis, Treponema pallidum damages the dorsal roots, and the dorsal columns that carry those fibers then degenerate. Without position sense from the legs, the patient relies on vision to stay upright, so he falls when his eyes close (positive Romberg sign). Loss of the reflex afferents in the dorsal roots explains the absent reflexes, and irritation of the roots gives lightning pains. Other tertiary findings are Argyll Robertson pupils, Charcot joints, and, in other forms, general paresis and meningovascular stroke. CSF VDRL is specific for neurosyphilis.","whyNot":["Corticospinal tract damage causes weakness, spasticity, hyperreflexia, and Babinski signs. He has normal strength and absent reflexes.","Anterior horn cell loss causes weakness, atrophy, and fasciculations. His strength is normal.","Cerebellar ataxia is present with eyes open and does not worsen much with eye closure, so the Romberg test is usually negative.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tabes dorsalis","Romberg sign"],"comps":["NE-20-G9","NE-2"]}},{"id":"NE-C43-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the organism that caused his disease?","options":["Spirochete detected by darkfield microscopy","Acid-fast bacillus with an armadillo reservoir","Encapsulated yeast seen with India ink stain","Gram-positive bacillus that forms terminal spores"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-19","obj":"NE-19.7","dok":2,"approach":["Tabes dorsalis with a reactive CSF VDRL means tertiary syphilis.","The cause is Treponema pallidum, a thin spirochete.","It is too thin to see on Gram stain, so it is seen with darkfield microscopy or fluorescent antibody and confirmed by treponemal tests such as FTA-ABS."],"why":"Treponema pallidum is a thin, tightly coiled spirochete that does not grow in routine culture and does not show on Gram stain. In a primary chancre or secondary lesion it is seen by darkfield microscopy. Infection is screened with nontreponemal tests (VDRL, RPR), which detect antibody to cardiolipin, and confirmed with treponemal tests such as FTA-ABS. Borrelia burgdorferi is another spirochete, spread by Ixodes ticks. Mycobacterium leprae is an acid-fast rod that prefers cool body sites and infects Schwann cells.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is Mycobacterium leprae, which causes leprosy with patchy skin anesthesia and thickened nerves. Armadillos are its reservoir in the US.","This is Cryptococcus neoformans, a cause of chronic meningitis in people with AIDS. It does not cause tabes dorsalis.","This describes Clostridium tetani, whose toxin blocks inhibitory interneurons and causes spastic paralysis, not sensory ataxia."],"study":{"topics":["Treponema pallidum","Syphilis testing"],"comps":["NE-19","NE-20-G9"]}},{"id":"NE-C43-Q3","stem":"A 15-year-old girl has a 3-year history of progressive gait ataxia. Examination shows absent ankle reflexes, loss of vibration sense in the feet, a positive Romberg sign, extensor plantar responses, high foot arches, and scoliosis. Echocardiography shows hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Serologic testing for syphilis is nonreactive. Which of the following is the most likely genetic cause of her condition?","options":["CAG repeat expansion in the huntingtin gene","CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene","GAA repeat expansion in the frataxin gene","CGG repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-B3","obj":"NE-20-B.6","dok":2,"approach":["Like tabes, she has dorsal column loss and areflexia, but she also has Babinski signs, pes cavus, scoliosis, and cardiomyopathy in adolescence.","This combination is Friedreich ataxia.","Friedreich ataxia is autosomal recessive and caused by a GAA trinucleotide expansion in the frataxin gene."],"why":"Friedreich ataxia is the most common inherited ataxia. A GAA repeat expansion in an intron of the frataxin gene reduces frataxin, a mitochondrial protein needed for iron-sulfur cluster assembly. Iron builds up in mitochondria and oxidative damage kills neurons. The dorsal columns, spinocerebellar tracts, and corticospinal tracts degenerate, together with dorsal root ganglion cells, so the patient has sensory ataxia, absent reflexes, and Babinski signs at the same time. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (the usual cause of death), diabetes, kyphoscoliosis, and pes cavus complete the picture.","whyNot":["CAG expansion in huntingtin causes Huntington disease, with chorea and dementia in mid-adult life and autosomal dominant inheritance.","CTG expansion in DMPK causes myotonic dystrophy, with myotonia, distal weakness, cataracts, and frontal balding.","Correct.","CGG expansion in FMR1 causes fragile X syndrome, with intellectual disability, a long face, and large testes."],"study":{"topics":["Friedreich ataxia","Trinucleotide repeat disorders"],"comps":["NE-20-B3","NE-16"]}},{"id":"NE-C43-Q4","stem":"A 63-year-old man who has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years has 6 weeks of rapidly worsening numbness in all four limbs and an unsteady gait. Examination shows loss of vibration and position sense in the hands and feet, absent reflexes, a positive Romberg sign, and normal strength. Syphilis serology is nonreactive. CT of the chest shows a central hilar mass. Antibodies against which of the following are most likely present?","options":["Yo antigen in Purkinje cells","NMDA receptors on cortical neurons","Hu antigen in neuronal nuclei","Voltage-gated calcium channels on nerve terminals"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-18","obj":"NE-18.5","dok":3,"approach":["A smoker with a central hilar mass most likely has small cell lung cancer.","Rapid, purely sensory loss in all limbs with sensory ataxia means the dorsal root ganglion neurons are being destroyed (sensory neuronopathy).","Small cell lung cancer expresses the neuronal Hu antigen, and the immune response against it attacks dorsal root ganglion neurons."],"why":"Paraneoplastic neurologic syndromes occur when a tumor expresses a protein normally found only in neurons. The immune response against the tumor also attacks the matching neurons, mostly through cytotoxic T cells, with the antibody serving as a marker. Small cell lung cancer expresses Hu proteins, and anti-Hu disease destroys dorsal root ganglion neurons (sensory neuronopathy) and can cause limbic encephalitis. Anti-Yo antibodies, usually from ovarian or breast cancer, cause cerebellar degeneration. Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is linked to ovarian teratoma.","whyNot":["Anti-Yo antibodies cause subacute cerebellar degeneration, usually with ovarian or breast cancer. That gives cerebellar ataxia, not loss of position sense.","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis causes psychiatric symptoms, seizures, and dyskinesias, typically in young women with ovarian teratoma.","Correct.","Antibodies to presynaptic calcium channels cause Lambert-Eaton syndrome, also linked to small cell lung cancer, but it causes proximal weakness, not sensory loss."],"study":{"topics":["Paraneoplastic neurologic syndromes","Anti-Hu sensory neuronopathy"],"comps":["NE-18","NE-20-B"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C44","system":"NE","dx":"Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease","codes":["NE-20-G11"],"case":"A 64-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her husband because of rapidly worsening memory and confusion over 3 months. She now needs help dressing and walks unsteadily. She has no family history of neurologic disease, and she has had no surgery or transfusions. Her temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F). She is disoriented and cannot recall any of three words. Sudden noises cause brief myoclonic jerks of her arms. Gait is ataxic. Cerebrospinal fluid shows 2 cells/mm3 (normal 0 to 5), normal glucose and protein, and an elevated 14-3-3 protein. Diffusion-weighted MRI shows ribbon-like cortical and caudate hyperintensity. EEG shows periodic sharp wave complexes. Diagnosis: sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C44-Q1","stem":"Which of the following molecular changes most likely underlies her disease?","options":["Refolding of alpha-helical PrP into beta-pleated PrP","Hyperphosphorylation of tau into neurofibrillary tangles","Deposition of amyloid-beta in neuritic plaques","Polyglutamine expansion within huntingtin protein"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-G11","obj":"NE-20-G.13","dok":2,"approach":["Rapid dementia over months with myoclonus, elevated CSF 14-3-3, and periodic sharp waves on EEG points to prion disease.","Prion disease is caused by misfolded prion protein, not a virus or bacterium.","The misfolded protein turns normal alpha-helical PrP into beta-pleated PrP, which resists proteases and builds up."],"why":"Normal prion protein (PrPc) is a membrane protein rich in alpha helices. In prion disease, it changes into a beta-pleated form (PrPsc) that resists protease digestion and acts as a template that converts more normal PrP. The protein accumulates and kills neurons, leaving spongiform vacuoles in gray matter without inflammation. Most cases are sporadic, some are inherited (PRNP mutations), and some are acquired, such as variant CJD from eating beef contaminated with bovine prions and iatrogenic CJD from contaminated neurosurgical instruments or dura mater grafts.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tau tangles are part of Alzheimer disease, which progresses over years, without early myoclonus or periodic sharp waves.","Amyloid-beta plaques are the other hallmark of Alzheimer disease, a slowly progressive dementia.","Polyglutamine expansion in huntingtin causes Huntington disease, an autosomal dominant disorder with chorea. She has no family history."],"study":{"topics":["Prion protein misfolding","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease"],"comps":["NE-20-G11","NE-20-B1"]}},{"id":"NE-C44-Q2","stem":"For comparison, the laboratory records an EEG from a healthy 40-year-old volunteer who is lying awake and relaxed with her eyes closed. She is then asked to open her eyes and solve arithmetic problems. Which of the following changes over the occipital region is most likely?","options":["Alpha rhythm replaced by low-voltage beta activity","Theta rhythm replaced by alpha waves","Beta activity replaced by alpha waves","Alpha rhythm replaced by high-amplitude delta waves"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-11","obj":"NE-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the starting state: awake and relaxed with eyes closed gives a posterior alpha rhythm (8 to 13 Hz).","Identify the new state: eyes open and mentally alert gives low-voltage, fast beta activity (above 13 Hz).","So the alpha rhythm disappears and beta takes over (alpha blocking). Theta and delta belong to sleep, not to an alert adult."],"why":"The EEG frequency follows the level of arousal. Closing the eyes and relaxing brings out an alpha rhythm (8 to 13 Hz) over the occipital lobes. Opening the eyes or concentrating blocks the alpha rhythm and replaces it with low-voltage, fast beta activity. Drowsiness and stage N1 sleep show theta waves, N2 shows sleep spindles and K complexes, and N3 (deep sleep) shows high-amplitude delta waves. REM sleep returns to low-voltage, beta-like activity. The slow, disorganized background in an awake patient like her points to a widespread cortical disorder, such as prion disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Theta waves appear with drowsiness and N1 sleep. A relaxed adult with eyes closed shows alpha, and alerting her makes the EEG faster than alpha.","This is the reverse change, seen when an alert person closes the eyes and relaxes.","High-amplitude delta waves mark deep N3 sleep. Opening the eyes and doing mental work makes the EEG faster, not slower."],"study":{"topics":["EEG rhythms and arousal","Sleep stages"],"comps":["NE-11"]}},{"id":"NE-C44-Q3","stem":"A 12-year-old boy has had 8 months of declining school performance, personality change, and repetitive jerks of his arms. He had measles at 14 months of age, before any vaccination. Which of the following cerebrospinal fluid findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["JC virus DNA detected by PCR","High titer of antibody to measles virus","Albuminocytologic dissociation","Neutrophilic pleocytosis with low glucose"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-G13","obj":"NE-20-G.15","dok":3,"approach":["Progressive cognitive decline and myoclonus in a child years after early measles infection points to subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE).","SSPE is a persistent infection with a defective measles virus that cannot bud from neurons.","The immune system keeps responding, so CSF shows very high anti-measles antibody levels."],"why":"In SSPE, measles virus acquired early in life persists in the brain years later as a defective virus, often with mutations in the matrix (M) protein, so it spreads from cell to cell without forming new virus particles. The ongoing infection causes inflammation, demyelination, and neuron loss with inclusion bodies. Children develop behavior change, cognitive decline, and myoclonus, then progress to death. The diagnosis rests on high anti-measles antibody titers in CSF, often with periodic complexes on EEG. Measles infection before age 2 is a major risk factor.","whyNot":["JC virus causes progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy in severely immunosuppressed patients, not in a healthy child after measles.","Correct.","High protein with a normal cell count is the CSF pattern of Guillain-Barre syndrome, which causes ascending weakness.","Neutrophils with low glucose indicate acute bacterial meningitis, which develops over hours to days with fever and neck stiffness."],"study":{"topics":["Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis","Persistent measles infection"],"comps":["NE-20-G13","NE-20-G4"]}},{"id":"NE-C44-Q4","stem":"A 71-year-old man has 18 months of cognitive decline with marked day-to-day swings in alertness. He describes seeing small children playing in his living room. Examination shows mild rigidity and slow finger tapping. He dies 5 years later. Which of the following findings is most likely on examination of his cerebral cortex at autopsy?","options":["Spongiform vacuoles in gray matter without inflammation","Round tau-positive inclusions in frontal neurons","Tau aggregates in midbrain and basal ganglia neurons","Alpha-synuclein inclusions in cortical neurons"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-B","obj":"NE-20-B.10","dok":3,"approach":["Fluctuating cognition, well-formed visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism define dementia with Lewy bodies.","The slower course over years separates it from prion disease.","Lewy bodies are alpha-synuclein inclusions; in this disorder they are found in cortical neurons."],"why":"Dementia with Lewy bodies is a synucleinopathy, like Parkinson disease, but the alpha-synuclein inclusions are widespread in the cortex as well as the brainstem. Its core features are fluctuating attention, recurrent detailed visual hallucinations, parkinsonism, and REM sleep behavior disorder. Dementia appears before or within about a year of parkinsonism. Frontotemporal dementia starts with personality or language change and shows tau (Pick bodies) or TDP-43 inclusions. Progressive supranuclear palsy is a tauopathy with vertical gaze palsy and early falls.","whyNot":["Spongiform change without inflammation is prion disease, which progresses over months, not years, and lacks prominent hallucinations.","Round tau inclusions (Pick bodies) mark frontotemporal dementia, which begins with personality change or language loss.","Tau in the midbrain and basal ganglia fits progressive supranuclear palsy, with vertical gaze palsy and early falls.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dementia with Lewy bodies","Neurodegenerative inclusions"],"comps":["NE-20-B","NE-20-B3"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C45","system":"NE","dx":"Bell palsy (idiopathic facial nerve palsy)","codes":["NE-20-I1"],"case":"A 43-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the physician because he woke up this morning with drooping of the right side of his face. Yesterday he had aching pain behind his right ear. He reports that sounds seem too loud in his right ear and that food tastes strange. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows that he cannot wrinkle his right forehead, close his right eye fully, or raise the right corner of his mouth. The right nasolabial fold is flat. There are no vesicles in the ear canal or on the palate. The rest of the neurologic examination is normal. Diagnosis: Bell palsy.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C45-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the most commonly proposed cause of this patient's condition?","options":["Compression by a cerebellopontine angle tumor","Nerve swelling after HSV-1 reactivation","Invasion by a parotid gland carcinoma","Antibodies against GM1 ganglioside"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-I1","obj":"NE-20-I.1","dok":2,"approach":["Sudden whole-face weakness on one side with hyperacusis and taste change, developing overnight, fits Bell palsy.","Bell palsy is idiopathic, but the leading explanation is reactivation of herpes simplex virus in the geniculate ganglion.","The inflamed nerve swells inside the narrow facial canal, which compresses it."],"why":"Bell palsy is an acute lower motor neuron palsy of CN VII without another identified cause. Reactivation of latent HSV-1 in the geniculate ganglion is the most widely accepted trigger. The nerve becomes inflamed and swollen inside the bony facial canal, where it has little room. It is more common with diabetes and pregnancy, and Lyme disease must be excluded in endemic areas. Because the lesion is in the canal, branches to the stapedius (hyperacusis), chorda tympani (anterior tongue taste), and lacrimal gland (tearing) can be affected along with all facial muscles.","whyNot":["A tumor at the cerebellopontine angle, such as a vestibular schwannoma, causes slowly progressive hearing loss and tinnitus, not overnight facial palsy.","Correct.","Parotid carcinoma can invade the facial nerve, but weakness develops gradually and there would be a parotid mass. It also spares taste and hearing.","Anti-GM1 antibodies are linked to Guillain-Barre syndrome, which can cause facial weakness but usually bilateral and with ascending limb weakness."],"study":{"topics":["Bell palsy","Facial nerve branches"],"comps":["NE-20-I1","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C45-Q2","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient best distinguishes his lesion from an infarct in the left primary motor cortex?","options":["Drooping of the right corner of the mouth","Flattening of the right nasolabial fold","Weakness of the right frontalis muscle","Weak puffing of the right cheek"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-8","obj":"NE-8.2","dok":2,"approach":["A cortical lesion is an upper motor neuron lesion; Bell palsy is a lower motor neuron lesion of CN VII.","The part of the facial nucleus that controls the upper face receives input from both hemispheres; the part for the lower face gets input mainly from the opposite cortex.","So a cortical stroke spares the forehead, while a CN VII lesion weakens the forehead too."],"why":"Corticobulbar fibers to the facial motor neurons for the forehead come from both motor cortices, so one cortex can still move the forehead if the other is damaged. Neurons for the lower face receive mostly crossed input. A stroke in the left motor cortex therefore weakens only the right lower face and spares the forehead. A lesion of the facial nucleus or the nerve itself cuts the final common pathway to every muscle on that side, so the forehead is weak as well. This is the quickest bedside test to separate a central from a peripheral facial palsy.","whyNot":["Mouth droop is caused by lower face weakness, which occurs in both a cortical stroke and a facial nerve lesion.","A flat nasolabial fold is also a lower face sign and occurs with both lesions.","Correct.","Buccinator weakness affects the lower face and is present with both upper and lower motor neuron lesions."],"study":{"topics":["Upper versus lower motor neuron facial palsy","Corticobulbar innervation"],"comps":["NE-8","NE-2"]}},{"id":"NE-C45-Q3","stem":"A 26-year-old woman who lives in Connecticut has weakness of both sides of her face and headache for 4 days. Five weeks ago, after a hiking trip, she had an expanding red rash with central clearing on her thigh. Cerebrospinal fluid shows 110 cells/mm3 (normal 0 to 5), mostly lymphocytes. Her illness was most likely acquired by which of the following routes?","options":["Sexual contact with an infected partner","Bite of an Aedes mosquito","Exposure to water contaminated by rat urine","Bite of an Ixodes tick"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-G","obj":"NE-20-G.18","dok":3,"approach":["Bilateral facial palsy with lymphocytic meningitis after an expanding ring-shaped rash is early disseminated Lyme disease.","Lyme disease is caused by the spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi.","It is spread by Ixodes (deer) ticks, common in the northeastern US."],"why":"Lyme neuroborreliosis develops weeks after the tick bite and the erythema migrans rash. Its classic triad is facial nerve palsy (often bilateral), lymphocytic meningitis, and painful radiculopathy. Bilateral facial palsy is unusual for Bell palsy and should raise suspicion of Lyme disease. Borrelia burgdorferi is carried by Ixodes ticks, which acquire it from white-footed mice, while deer host the adult ticks. Leptospirosis, the other spirochetal cause of meningitis, comes from water contaminated by animal urine.","whyNot":["Sexual contact spreads Treponema pallidum. Syphilis can cause meningitis and cranial neuropathies, but the rash is not an expanding ring.","Aedes mosquitoes spread dengue, Zika, and chikungunya viruses, which do not cause erythema migrans.","Water contaminated by rodent or animal urine spreads Leptospira, which causes fever, myalgia, conjunctival suffusion, and sometimes meningitis, but not erythema migrans.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lyme neuroborreliosis","Spirochetal meningitis"],"comps":["NE-20-G","NE-19"]}},{"id":"NE-C45-Q4","stem":"Three days after his visit, the man with Bell palsy reports pain, a gritty feeling, and watering in his right eye. Which of the following findings is most likely on examination of the right eye?","options":["Blood layering in the anterior chamber","Corneal defect that stains with fluorescein","Painless bright red patch under the conjunctiva","Restricted upward gaze with double vision"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-J","obj":"NE-20-J.19","dok":3,"approach":["He cannot close the right eye fully, and Bell palsy can reduce tear production.","An eye that stays partly open and dry loses its protective tear film, so the corneal epithelium breaks down (exposure keratopathy).","A corneal epithelial defect causes pain and foreign body sensation and takes up fluorescein dye."],"why":"The orbicularis oculi closes the eye and spreads tears over the cornea with each blink. With CN VII weakness, the eye stays partly open, and the lacrimal gland may lose its parasympathetic drive through the greater petrosal nerve. The corneal epithelium dries and erodes, producing pain, a gritty sensation, reflex tearing, and redness. Fluorescein dye pools where the epithelium is missing and glows green under cobalt blue light. Corneal abrasion from a scratch or foreign body looks the same on staining.","whyNot":["Blood in the anterior chamber (hyphema) follows blunt eye trauma, which he did not have.","Correct.","Subconjunctival hemorrhage is a painless red patch from a ruptured conjunctival vessel, often after coughing or straining. It does not cause a gritty feeling.","Limited upward gaze with diplopia follows an orbital floor fracture that traps the inferior rectus after blunt facial trauma."],"study":{"topics":["Exposure keratopathy","Eye injuries"],"comps":["NE-20-J","NE-20-I1"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C46","system":"NE","dx":"Carpal tunnel syndrome in pregnancy","codes":["NE-20-I2"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman at 32 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of numbness and tingling in her right thumb, index finger, and middle finger for 6 weeks. The symptoms wake her at night and improve when she shakes her hand. She has mild swelling of both hands and ankles. Her blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows decreased sensation over the palmar surfaces of the right thumb, index finger, middle finger, and the lateral half of the ring finger. Sensation over the thenar eminence is normal. Thumb abduction is mildly weak. Tapping over the volar wrist and holding the wrists fully flexed for 60 seconds both reproduce the tingling. Diagnosis: carpal tunnel syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C46-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient best localizes the nerve compression to the wrist rather than to the forearm?","options":["Normal sensation over the thenar eminence","Weak pronation of the forearm","Numbness over the medial palm and little finger","Weak flexion at the thumb interphalangeal joint"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-I2","obj":"NE-20-I.2","dok":3,"approach":["Her symptoms follow the median nerve distribution, made worse at night and by wrist flexion.","The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve leaves the nerve above the wrist and passes over the flexor retinaculum, not through the carpal tunnel.","So spared sensation over the thenar eminence means the compression is inside the tunnel."],"why":"In carpal tunnel syndrome, the median nerve is squeezed under the flexor retinaculum along with nine flexor tendons. Anything that raises pressure in the tunnel can cause it: fluid retention in pregnancy, hypothyroidism, rheumatoid synovitis, diabetes, and dialysis-related amyloid. Symptoms are paresthesia of the thumb to the lateral ring finger, worse at night, with Tinel and Phalen signs and later thenar atrophy. The palmar cutaneous branch leaves before the tunnel, so sensation over the thenar palm is spared. A more proximal median nerve lesion would numb the palm too.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pronator teres and pronator quadratus are supplied in the forearm, so pronation stays normal in carpal tunnel syndrome.","The medial palm and little finger are supplied by the ulnar nerve, which runs outside the carpal tunnel.","Flexor pollicis longus is supplied in the forearm by the anterior interosseous branch, so its weakness points to a lesion above the wrist."],"study":{"topics":["Carpal tunnel syndrome","Median nerve branches"],"comps":["NE-20-I2","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C46-Q2","stem":"The compressed nerve in this patient is formed from which of the following parts of the brachial plexus?","options":["Posterior cord only","Lateral and posterior cords","Lateral and medial cords","Medial and posterior cords"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-8","obj":"NE-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the nerve: median nerve, from the sensory pattern and thenar weakness.","Recall the terminal branches: lateral cord gives musculocutaneous, medial cord gives ulnar, posterior cord gives axillary and radial.","The median nerve is formed by one root from the lateral cord and one from the medial cord, carrying C6 to T1 fibers (with some C5)."],"why":"The brachial plexus runs from roots (C5 to T1) to trunks, divisions, cords, and branches. The median nerve forms from a lateral root from the lateral cord and a medial root from the medial cord, which join in front of the axillary artery. The posterior cord gives the axillary and radial nerves. Upper trunk injury (Erb palsy, C5 to C6) causes the waiter's tip posture. Lower trunk injury (Klumpke palsy, C8 to T1) weakens the intrinsic hand muscles and can cause a claw hand.","whyNot":["The posterior cord alone gives rise to the radial and axillary nerves, which supply the extensors and deltoid.","The posterior cord does not contribute to the median nerve. It forms the radial and axillary nerves.","Correct.","The median nerve does get a medial cord root, but nothing from the posterior cord."],"study":{"topics":["Brachial plexus organization","Origins of the median nerve"],"comps":["NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C46-Q3","stem":"A 24-year-old woman who is a competitive swimmer has 5 months of tingling along the right medial forearm and little finger, right hand weakness, and coolness and fatigue of the right arm when she raises it overhead. Examination shows mild wasting of the intrinsic hand muscles. X-ray of the cervical spine shows a cervical rib on the right. Compression of which of the following structures most likely explains her neurologic findings?","options":["Median nerve beneath the flexor retinaculum","Ulnar nerve within the cubital tunnel","Upper trunk of the brachial plexus","Lower trunk of the brachial plexus"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-I6","obj":"NE-20-I.6","dok":3,"approach":["A cervical rib, overhead arm symptoms, and vascular signs point to thoracic outlet syndrome.","The lowest part of the brachial plexus lies on the first rib or cervical rib and is the part most often compressed.","Lower trunk fibers (C8 to T1) supply the medial forearm skin and the intrinsic hand muscles."],"why":"In thoracic outlet syndrome, the lower trunk of the brachial plexus (C8 to T1), the subclavian artery, or the subclavian vein is compressed as it passes over the first rib, usually by a cervical rib or tight scalene muscles. Lower trunk compression causes paresthesia along the ulnar side of the forearm and hand and weakness and wasting of the intrinsic hand muscles. Arterial compression causes coolness, pallor, and fatigue with arm elevation. The medial forearm sensory loss helps separate it from an ulnar nerve lesion.","whyNot":["The median nerve at the wrist causes symptoms in the thumb to the ring finger and spares the medial forearm.","Ulnar compression at the elbow numbs the little finger and weakens the hand intrinsics, but it spares the medial forearm and does not cause vascular symptoms.","Upper trunk (C5 to C6) compression weakens shoulder abduction and elbow flexion and causes lateral arm numbness.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Thoracic outlet syndrome","Lower trunk brachial plexus lesions"],"comps":["NE-20-I6","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C46-Q4","stem":"She reports that she can no longer feel a light object slipping across her right fingertips. Which of the following best describes the receptor most responsible for detecting this stimulus?","options":["Rapidly adapting corpuscle in the dermal papillae","Rapidly adapting corpuscle deep in the dermis and subcutis","Slowly adapting disc at the base of the epidermis","Slowly adapting spindle-shaped ending that senses skin stretch"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-5","obj":"NE-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["A light moving touch on glabrous skin is a change in stimulus, so it needs a rapidly adapting receptor near the surface.","Meissner corpuscles sit in the dermal papillae of fingertips, adapt rapidly, and respond to light touch, flutter, and slip.","All four skin mechanoreceptors send signals through large myelinated A-beta fibers, so fiber type does not separate them."],"why":"Skin mechanoreceptors differ by depth and adaptation. Meissner corpuscles are superficial in the dermal papillae of hairless skin, adapt rapidly, and detect light stroking, low-frequency vibration, and an object slipping in the grip. Merkel discs are superficial but slowly adapting and signal sustained pressure, edges, and texture. Pacinian corpuscles lie deep, adapt very rapidly, and sense high-frequency vibration. Ruffini endings are deep, slowly adapting, and sense skin stretch. Free nerve endings carry pain and temperature on A-delta and C fibers.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is the Pacinian corpuscle, which senses high-frequency vibration and deep pressure.","This is the Merkel disc, which signals steady pressure and fine form, not a moving stimulus.","This is the Ruffini ending, which senses sustained stretch of the skin and joint position."],"study":{"topics":["Cutaneous mechanoreceptors"],"comps":["NE-5"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C47","system":"NE","dx":"L5-S1 disc herniation with S1 radiculopathy and sciatica","codes":["NE-20-I3","NE-20-I4","NE-20-I5"],"case":"A 44-year-old man who works in a warehouse comes to the physician because of low back pain that radiates down the back of his left thigh and calf to the outer edge of his foot. The pain began 5 days ago while he was lifting a heavy box and worsens with coughing. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows that raising his straightened left leg to 40 degrees reproduces pain shooting below the knee. Sensation is decreased over the lateral left foot and sole. Plantar flexion of the left foot is weak, and the left ankle reflex is absent. Perianal sensation and bladder function are normal. MRI shows a posterolateral herniation of the L5-S1 disc compressing the left S1 nerve root. Diagnosis: L5-S1 disc herniation with S1 radiculopathy and sciatica.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C47-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why this posterolateral L5-S1 herniation compresses the S1 nerve root rather than the L5 nerve root?","options":["The ligamentum flavum shields the L5 root","The S1 root exits the canal above the L5-S1 disc","The posterior longitudinal ligament is widest laterally","The L5 root exits the canal above the L5-S1 disc"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-I3","obj":"NE-20-I.3","dok":2,"approach":["A herniated disc is nucleus pulposus pushing through a tear in the annulus fibrosus.","The posterior longitudinal ligament is strong in the midline, so most herniations go posterolateral.","Each lumbar root exits under the pedicle of its own vertebra, above the disc, so a posterolateral disc hits the next lower root as it passes by."],"why":"Lumbar nerve roots run down inside the canal and leave through the foramen below the pedicle of the same-numbered vertebra. The L5 root leaves through the L5-S1 foramen in its upper part, above the disc. The S1 root is still passing down in the lateral recess at the L5-S1 disc level, so a posterolateral L5-S1 herniation compresses S1. In the same way, an L4-L5 herniation affects L5. Most herniations occur at L4-L5 or L5-S1, where the spine bears the most load and motion.","whyNot":["The ligamentum flavum lines the back of the canal. It does not protect the L5 root; thickening of it can actually narrow the canal.","The S1 root exits through the first sacral foramen, below the L5-S1 disc. That is why it is still in the path of this herniation.","The posterior longitudinal ligament is strongest in the midline and narrow at the sides, which is why discs herniate posterolaterally. It does not decide which root is compressed.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lumbar disc herniation","Lumbar nerve root exits"],"comps":["NE-20-I3","NE-8"]}},{"id":"NE-C47-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why raising his straightened left leg reproduces his pain?","options":["It raises pressure in the lumbar cerebrospinal fluid","It stretches the compressed root over the disc","It contracts the piriformis around the sciatic nerve","It compresses the facet joints of the lower lumbar spine"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-20-I5","obj":"NE-20-I.5","dok":2,"approach":["Pain from the buttock down the back of the thigh to below the knee is sciatica, usually from L5 or S1 root compression.","Hip flexion with a straight knee pulls the sciatic nerve and its L4 to S1 roots downward.","A root tethered over a herniated disc cannot slide, so stretching it reproduces radiating pain, usually between about 30 and 70 degrees."],"why":"Sciatica is radiating pain along the course of the sciatic nerve, from the buttock down the back of the thigh and below the knee. It usually comes from compression of the L5 or S1 root by a herniated disc. In the straight leg raise test, flexing the hip with the knee straight stretches the sciatic nerve and pulls its roots through the foramina. If a root is already stretched over a disc, the extra tension produces pain that shoots below the knee. Pain felt only in the back or hamstrings does not count as a positive test.","whyNot":["Coughing and straining raise CSF and epidural pressure, which is why his pain worsens with coughing, but the straight leg raise works by stretching the root.","Correct.","Piriformis tightness can irritate the sciatic nerve in the buttock, but it would not explain the MRI finding or the absent ankle reflex.","Facet joint pain is local back pain that worsens with spinal extension. The straight leg raise does not load the facets."],"study":{"topics":["Sciatica","Straight leg raise test"],"comps":["NE-20-I5","NE-20-I4"]}},{"id":"NE-C47-Q3","stem":"A 52-year-old woman develops weakness lifting her right foot after sitting with her legs crossed for a long flight. She trips over her toes when she walks. Which of the following findings would most strongly favor a common fibular nerve injury over an L5 radiculopathy?","options":["Weak ankle dorsiflexion","Numbness of the dorsum of the foot","Normal strength of foot inversion","Weak extension of the great toe"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-8","obj":"NE-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["Foot drop can come from the common fibular nerve at the fibular neck or from the L5 root.","Both lesions weaken the dorsiflexors and toe extensors and numb the dorsum of the foot, so those findings do not separate them.","Foot inversion uses tibialis posterior, supplied by the tibial nerve but still from L5. It is weak in L5 radiculopathy and normal in fibular nerve injury."],"why":"The common fibular nerve wraps around the fibular neck, where leg crossing, casts, or fractures can compress it. It supplies the dorsiflexors and toe extensors (deep fibular) and the evertors (superficial fibular), and the skin of the dorsum of the foot. L5 supplies these same muscles but also tibialis posterior (inversion, through the tibial nerve) and gluteus medius (hip abduction, through the superior gluteal nerve). So weak inversion or hip abduction points to the root. Superior gluteal nerve injury alone gives a Trendelenburg sign.","whyNot":["Dorsiflexion weakness causes foot drop in both lesions, so it cannot tell them apart.","Dorsal foot numbness occurs with both, because the fibular nerve carries L5 fibers to that skin.","Correct.","Extensor hallucis longus is weak in both lesions. It is a sensitive test for L5 but not specific for the root."],"study":{"topics":["Common fibular nerve injury","Foot drop localization"],"comps":["NE-8","NE-20-I4"]}},{"id":"NE-C47-Q4","stem":"Two days later, he returns because he cannot urinate and has numbness of his buttocks and perineum. MRI shows a large central extrusion of the L5-S1 disc filling the spinal canal. Which of the following additional findings is most likely?","options":["Absent anal wink reflex","Brisk patellar reflexes in both legs","Extensor plantar responses in both feet","Sensory level at the umbilicus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-H","obj":"NE-20-H.5","dok":3,"approach":["Urinary retention with saddle anesthesia after a central disc extrusion means cauda equina syndrome.","The spinal cord ends at about L1 to L2, so at L5-S1 only nerve roots are compressed.","Root compression gives lower motor neuron signs: reduced or absent reflexes, including the anal wink reflex (S2 to S4)."],"why":"Below the conus medullaris, the canal contains only the lumbosacral roots of the cauda equina. A large central disc, tumor, or epidural abscess at this level compresses several roots on both sides. The result is saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, loss of anal sphincter tone and the anal wink reflex, and lower motor neuron weakness with reduced reflexes in the legs. Spinal cord compression higher up gives the opposite: a sensory level, upper motor neuron signs such as spasticity, brisk reflexes, and Babinski signs. Both are surgical emergencies.","whyNot":["Correct.","Brisk reflexes are an upper motor neuron sign. Cauda equina compression reduces reflexes.","Babinski signs are upper motor neuron signs. They come from cord compression above the conus, not root compression at L5-S1.","A sensory level at the umbilicus means the cord is compressed near T10, well above this lesion."],"study":{"topics":["Cauda equina syndrome","Cord compression versus root compression"],"comps":["NE-20-H2","NE-20-I3"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C48","system":"NE","dx":"Acute angle-closure glaucoma","codes":["NE-20-J4"],"case":"A 62-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 2 hours after the onset of severe pain in her left eye, which began while she was watching a film in a dark theater. She has blurred vision in that eye, sees colored halos around lights, and has vomited twice. She has worn glasses for farsightedness since childhood. Her temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 156/88 mm Hg. The left eye shows conjunctival redness around the cornea, a hazy cornea, a shallow anterior chamber, and a fixed mid-dilated pupil. Intraocular pressure is 58 mm Hg in the left eye (normal 10 to 21) and 17 mm Hg in the right. Diagnosis: acute angle-closure glaucoma.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C48-Q1","stem":"Which of the following most directly caused the rise in her intraocular pressure?","options":["Increased aqueous secretion by the ciliary body","Blood cells obstructing the trabecular meshwork","Iris pressed against the trabecular meshwork","Slowly rising resistance within the meshwork"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-J4","obj":"NE-20-J.4","dok":3,"approach":["In the dark the pupil dilates, and in a mid-dilated position the iris presses on the lens, blocking aqueous flow through the pupil (pupillary block).","Pressure builds behind the iris and pushes the peripheral iris forward against the trabecular meshwork.","With the angle closed, aqueous cannot drain and pressure rises within hours."],"why":"Aqueous humor is made behind the iris and must pass through the pupil to reach the drainage angle. In an eye with a shallow anterior chamber, mid-dilation in dim light increases contact between the iris and the lens. Aqueous is trapped behind the iris, which bows forward and seals the angle. Pressure rises fast, causing pain, corneal edema (halos), a fixed mid-dilated pupil from iris ischemia, and nausea. Open-angle glaucoma is different: the angle is open, outflow resistance rises slowly in the meshwork, and peripheral vision is lost without pain. Both damage the optic nerve and enlarge the cup.","whyNot":["Aqueous production is normal in glaucoma. The problem is blocked outflow.","Red cells clogging the meshwork occur after hyphema from trauma. She has no injury or blood in the anterior chamber.","Correct.","Gradually rising outflow resistance in an open angle is primary open-angle glaucoma, which is painless and develops over years."],"study":{"topics":["Angle-closure versus open-angle glaucoma","Pupillary block"],"comps":["NE-20-J4","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C48-Q2","stem":"In her left eye, aqueous humor continues to be secreted while its drainage is blocked. Which of the following correctly pairs the site where aqueous humor is secreted with the channel through which it normally drains toward the veins?","options":["Ciliary epithelium, then the canal of Schlemm","Corneal endothelium, then the vortex veins","Lens epithelium, then the canal of Schlemm","Iris pigment epithelium, then the central retinal vein"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-6","obj":"NE-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Trace aqueous from start to finish: production, flow, drainage.","The nonpigmented ciliary epithelium secretes aqueous into the posterior chamber; it flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber.","It drains at the angle through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm and then to episcleral veins."],"why":"The ciliary processes are covered by a two-layer epithelium, and the inner nonpigmented layer secretes aqueous humor into the posterior chamber. Aqueous passes through the pupil, nourishes the lens and cornea, and leaves mainly through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm, which empties into episcleral veins. A smaller amount leaves through the uveoscleral route. Tears are a separate system: the lacrimal gland, driven by CN VII parasympathetic fibers, makes the watery layer, and meibomian glands add the oily layer.","whyNot":["Correct.","The corneal endothelium pumps fluid out of the cornea to keep it clear; it does not make aqueous. Vortex veins drain the choroid.","The lens epithelium maintains the lens and does not secrete aqueous, although the canal of Schlemm is correct for drainage.","The iris does not secrete aqueous, and the central retinal vein drains the inner retina, not the anterior chamber."],"study":{"topics":["Aqueous humor production and drainage","Tear film"],"comps":["NE-6","NE-20-J4"]}},{"id":"NE-C48-Q3","stem":"Her lifelong refractive error increased her risk for this condition. Which of the following best describes her refractive error?","options":["Long eyeball with images focused in front of the retina","Short eyeball with images focused behind the retina","Unequal corneal curvature in different meridians","Stiffened lens with reduced ability to accommodate"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"NE-20-J","obj":"NE-20-J.15","dok":2,"approach":["Farsightedness since childhood is hyperopia.","In hyperopia the eye is too short for its focusing power, so parallel light focuses behind the retina; convex lenses correct it.","A short eye has a crowded front segment and a shallow anterior chamber, which predisposes to angle closure."],"why":"In hyperopia, the eye is short relative to the power of the cornea and lens, so light focuses behind the retina. Young people can overcome mild hyperopia by accommodating, but distance and near vision blur as accommodation fades. A short eye also has a shallow anterior chamber and a narrow angle, and the lens thickens with age, which makes angle closure more likely. Myopia is the opposite (eye too long, corrected with concave lenses). Astigmatism comes from uneven corneal curvature. Presbyopia is the age-related loss of lens elasticity that makes near work hard after about age 40.","whyNot":["This is myopia, which is corrected with concave lenses. A long eye has a deep anterior chamber and is a risk factor for retinal detachment, not angle closure.","Correct.","This is astigmatism, which blurs vision at all distances and does not narrow the angle.","This is presbyopia, an age-related change that affects everyone over about 40 and is not a lifelong refractive error."],"study":{"topics":["Refractive errors","Risk factors for angle closure"],"comps":["NE-20-J","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C48-Q4","stem":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden, painless, complete loss of vision in his right eye that began 5 hours ago and has not improved. Intraocular pressure is 15 mm Hg in both eyes (normal 10 to 21). Which of the following is most likely on funduscopic examination of the right eye?","options":["Normal retina with a bright plaque in an arteriole","Scattered retinal hemorrhages with dilated tortuous veins","Deep optic disc cupping with a thin neuroretinal rim","Whitened retina with a red spot at the fovea"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-J","obj":"NE-20-J.12","dok":3,"approach":["Sudden painless persistent monocular loss with an embolic source suggests central retinal artery occlusion.","Without blood flow, the inner retina becomes swollen and opaque white.","The fovea has no inner retinal layers, so the normal choroid shows through as a cherry-red spot."],"why":"The central retinal artery supplies the inner retina. When an embolus, for example from atrial fibrillation or carotid plaque, blocks it, the inner retina becomes ischemic and swollen and looks pale or white. The fovea is thin and receives its blood from the choroid, so it stands out as a cherry-red spot. Amaurosis fugax is the transient version, often from carotid emboli, with vision returning within minutes. Central retinal vein occlusion causes painless vision loss with widespread retinal hemorrhages, swollen tortuous veins, and disc swelling.","whyNot":["A refractile cholesterol plaque in an arteriole with vision returning fits amaurosis fugax. His vision loss has persisted.","Widespread hemorrhages with swollen tortuous veins describe central retinal vein occlusion, which is linked to hypertension and diabetes rather than emboli.","Disc cupping develops slowly in glaucoma and causes gradual peripheral loss. His pressure is normal and the loss was sudden.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Central retinal artery occlusion","Retinal vascular occlusions"],"comps":["NE-20-J","NE-20-A"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C49","system":"NE","dx":"Neovascular (wet) age-related macular degeneration","codes":["NE-20-J6"],"case":"A 79-year-old woman comes to the physician because of distorted vision in her left eye for 10 days. Straight door frames look wavy, and there is a dark smudge in the center of what she sees. She has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 50 years. Two years ago she was told that she had yellow deposits under the retina in both eyes. Visual acuity is 20/200 in the left eye and 20/40 in the right. Peripheral vision is intact. Funduscopic examination of the left eye shows subretinal fluid and hemorrhage at the macula; both eyes show drusen. Fluorescein angiography shows a leaking choroidal neovascular membrane beneath the left macula. Diagnosis: neovascular (wet) age-related macular degeneration.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C49-Q1","stem":"Which of the following most directly explains the rapid loss of central vision in her left eye?","options":["Tractional separation of the neurosensory retina","Opacification of the lens nucleus by protein aggregation","VEGF-driven choroidal vessel growth under the macula","Gradual atrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-J6","obj":"NE-20-J.6","dok":2,"approach":["Drusen in both eyes mean she has had dry macular degeneration.","Sudden distortion (metamorphopsia) and central loss with fluid and blood under the macula mean the disease has turned wet.","Wet disease is driven by VEGF, which makes fragile new choroidal vessels grow through Bruch membrane and leak."],"why":"Age-related macular degeneration damages the macula and spares peripheral vision. In the dry form, drusen (deposits between the retinal pigment epithelium and Bruch membrane) and gradual atrophy cause slow central loss. In the wet form, ischemia and damage of the outer retina raise vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), which drives new vessels from the choroid through Bruch membrane. These vessels leak fluid and bleed under the retina, so central vision distorts and falls quickly. Age and smoking are the main risk factors.","whyNot":["Separation of the retina causes flashes, floaters, and a peripheral curtain of vision loss, not leaking vessels at the macula.","Lens opacity is a cataract, which causes painless glare and blurred vision without metamorphopsia or subretinal blood.","Correct.","Slow atrophy of the pigment epithelium is dry macular degeneration, which causes gradual loss over years, not in days."],"study":{"topics":["Wet versus dry macular degeneration","VEGF and neovascularization"],"comps":["NE-20-J6","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C49-Q2","stem":"Her remaining photoreceptors still respond to light. When a photon is absorbed by 11-cis retinal in a photoreceptor, which of the following sequences of events follows?","options":["Increased cAMP, opened calcium channels, depolarization","Increased cGMP, opened sodium channels, depolarization","Decreased cAMP, closed potassium channels, depolarization","Decreased cGMP, closed sodium channels, hyperpolarization"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-6","obj":"NE-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["In the dark, high cGMP keeps sodium channels open (the dark current), so photoreceptors are depolarized and release glutamate.","Light converts 11-cis retinal to all-trans retinal, activating opsin, which activates transducin and then phosphodiesterase.","Phosphodiesterase breaks down cGMP, the channels close, and the cell hyperpolarizes, releasing less glutamate."],"why":"Photoreceptors work in reverse compared with most sensory receptors: light hyperpolarizes them. Light isomerizes 11-cis retinal to all-trans retinal in rhodopsin (rods) or cone opsins. The activated opsin turns on the G protein transducin, which activates cGMP phosphodiesterase. cGMP falls, cGMP-gated sodium channels close, and the cell hyperpolarizes, cutting glutamate release onto bipolar cells. Retinal is made from vitamin A, so vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness first, because rods depend most on rhodopsin regeneration.","whyNot":["Phototransduction uses cGMP, not cAMP, and light hyperpolarizes photoreceptors rather than depolarizing them.","This describes the photoreceptor in the dark. Light lowers cGMP and closes the channels.","cAMP is not the second messenger here, and light causes hyperpolarization, not depolarization.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Phototransduction","Vitamin A and night blindness"],"comps":["NE-6","NE-14"]}},{"id":"NE-C49-Q3","stem":"A 56-year-old man with high myopia has a sudden shower of new floaters and flashes of light in his right eye, followed by a dark curtain spreading across the upper part of his vision. Which of the following best explains his vision loss?","options":["Sensory retina separated from the pigment epithelium","Embolic occlusion of the central retinal artery","Inherited degeneration of rods beginning in the peripheral retina","New choroidal vessels leaking beneath the macula"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-J","obj":"NE-20-J.14","dok":2,"approach":["Flashes and floaters followed by a curtain of vision loss is the classic history of retinal detachment.","The neurosensory retina and the retinal pigment epithelium are attached only loosely, a remnant of the embryonic optic cup space.","A retinal tear lets fluid enter between these layers and lift the sensory retina away from its blood supply."],"why":"The neural retina develops from the inner layer of the optic cup and the pigment epithelium from the outer layer, and the space between them never fully seals. In a rhegmatogenous detachment, the vitreous pulls on the retina and tears it (flashes from traction, floaters from blood or pigment). Fluid then enters through the tear and separates the sensory retina from the pigment epithelium and choroid, which feed the photoreceptors. High myopia is a key risk because the long eye has a thin, stretched retina. Retinitis pigmentosa is different: an inherited degeneration with night blindness and peripheral loss over years.","whyNot":["Correct.","Central retinal artery occlusion causes sudden painless loss without flashes or floaters and shows a cherry-red spot.","Inherited rod degeneration is retinitis pigmentosa, which causes night blindness and tunnel vision over years with bone spicule pigment.","Choroidal neovascularization is wet macular degeneration. It distorts central vision without flashes or a curtain."],"study":{"topics":["Retinal detachment","Retinitis pigmentosa"],"comps":["NE-20-J","NE-6"]}},{"id":"NE-C49-Q4","stem":"A 28-year-old man has 1 day of pain, redness, and light sensitivity in his left eye. For 2 years he has had low back stiffness that is worst in the morning and improves with exercise. Slit-lamp examination shows inflammatory cells in the anterior chamber. Which of the following is most likely present in this patient?","options":["HLA-DR3","HLA-DQ2","HLA-DR4","HLA-B27"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-J7","obj":"NE-20-J.7","dok":2,"approach":["Inflammatory back pain in a young man suggests ankylosing spondylitis, a seronegative spondyloarthropathy.","Painful red eye with cells in the anterior chamber is anterior uveitis.","Both are tied to the MHC class I allele HLA-B27."],"why":"Anterior uveitis (iritis) is inflammation of the iris and ciliary body, causing pain, redness around the cornea, photophobia, and cells in the anterior chamber. It is the most common extra-articular feature of the HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathies, such as ankylosing spondylitis and reactive arthritis. Sarcoidosis and juvenile idiopathic arthritis also cause anterior uveitis. Posterior uveitis and chorioretinitis are more often infectious, such as toxoplasmosis and CMV retinitis in AIDS.","whyNot":["HLA-DR3 is linked to type 1 diabetes, Graves disease, and lupus, not spondyloarthropathy.","HLA-DQ2 is linked to celiac disease.","HLA-DR4 is linked to rheumatoid arthritis and type 1 diabetes. Rheumatoid arthritis spares the sacroiliac joints and lower spine.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anterior uveitis","HLA-B27 associations"],"comps":["NE-20-J7","IM-1"]}}]},{"id":"NE-C50","system":"NE","dx":"Temporal lobe brain abscess complicating chronic otitis media and mastoiditis","codes":["NE-20-G3","NE-20-J9"],"case":"A 22-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of worsening headache for 10 days and trouble finding words for 2 days. He has had recurrent drainage from his left ear since childhood. His temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 102/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows foul-smelling pus in the left ear canal, a perforated left tympanic membrane, and tenderness over the left mastoid. He has word-finding difficulty and a right upper quadrant visual field defect in both eyes. Contrast MRI of the brain shows a 3-cm ring-enhancing lesion in the left temporal lobe with surrounding edema. Aspirated pus grows streptococci and anaerobes. Diagnosis: temporal lobe brain abscess complicating chronic otitis media and mastoiditis.","questions":[{"id":"NE-C50-Q1","stem":"By which of the following routes did the infection most likely reach his brain?","options":["Extension along olfactory nerves through the cribriform plate","Extension through the bony roof of the middle ear","Extension through the posterior wall of the frontal sinus","Bacteremic seeding through a right-to-left shunt"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"NE-20-G3","obj":"NE-20-G.3","dok":2,"approach":["A single abscess next to a chronically infected ear and mastoid points to spread from a contiguous focus.","The roof of the middle ear and mastoid (tegmen tympani) is a thin plate of the temporal bone that lies directly under the temporal lobe.","Infection crosses this bone or travels in small veins to reach the temporal lobe; ear infections can also reach the cerebellum."],"why":"Brain abscesses come from contiguous spread or blood-borne seeding. Contiguous spread is usually from the ear, mastoid, sinuses, or teeth, and it gives one abscess near the source: ear and mastoid infections cause temporal lobe or cerebellar abscesses, and frontal sinusitis or dental infections cause frontal lobe abscesses. Hematogenous spread, from endocarditis, lung infection, or a right-to-left shunt that bypasses the lung filter, often causes multiple abscesses. Typical organisms are streptococci, staphylococci, and anaerobes. Imaging shows a ring-enhancing lesion with edema.","whyNot":["The cribriform plate links the nasal cavity to the frontal lobe and olfactory bulbs, far from the temporal lobe.","Correct.","Frontal sinus infection spreads to the frontal lobe, not the temporal lobe.","Right-to-left shunts allow bacteria to skip the lung filter and seed the brain, usually producing multiple abscesses. His source is next to the abscess."],"study":{"topics":["Brain abscess sources","Temporal bone relations"],"comps":["NE-20-G3","NE-20-J9"]}},{"id":"NE-C50-Q2","stem":"His ear problems began at age 2 years with fever, ear pain, and a red, bulging tympanic membrane. Which of the following organisms most likely caused that first episode?","options":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Escherichia coli"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-J9","obj":"NE-20-J.9","dok":2,"approach":["A bulging red tympanic membrane with fever in a toddler is acute otitis media.","Children are prone to it because their eustachian tubes are short, narrow, and more horizontal, so the middle ear does not drain well after a cold.","The usual organisms come from the nasopharynx: Streptococcus pneumoniae, nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis."],"why":"Acute otitis media follows eustachian tube dysfunction, usually after a viral upper respiratory infection. Fluid trapped in the middle ear becomes infected by bacteria that colonize the nasopharynx, most often Streptococcus pneumoniae, nontypeable H. influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis. Fluid that remains without signs of infection is otitis media with effusion and causes conductive hearing loss. Complications include perforation, mastoiditis, cholesteatoma, and spread into the skull, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pseudomonas often grows from a chronically draining ear and causes otitis externa, but it is not a usual cause of a first acute middle ear infection.","Staphylococcus epidermidis is a skin commensal that infects implanted devices. It is not a middle ear pathogen in a healthy child.","Escherichia coli causes neonatal meningitis and urinary infections, not acute otitis media in toddlers."],"study":{"topics":["Acute otitis media","Eustachian tube dysfunction"],"comps":["NE-20-J9","NE-20-G3"]}},{"id":"NE-C50-Q3","stem":"A 76-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus has had severe right ear pain and drainage for 6 weeks. His hemoglobin A1c is 10.4% (normal below 5.7%). Examination shows granulation tissue on the floor of the external ear canal and weakness of the entire right side of the face. CT shows erosion of the skull base. Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Haemophilus influenzae","Streptococcus pneumoniae"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"NE-20-J9","obj":"NE-20-J.10","dok":3,"approach":["An older diabetic patient with a painful draining ear canal, granulation tissue, and a cranial nerve palsy has malignant (necrotizing) otitis externa.","Infection spreads from the ear canal into the temporal bone and skull base, causing osteomyelitis that can damage CN VII and lower cranial nerves.","The classic organism is Pseudomonas aeruginosa."],"why":"Ordinary otitis externa (swimmer's ear) is infection of the ear canal skin, usually by Pseudomonas or Staphylococcus aureus, with pain when the tragus is pressed. In older patients with diabetes or immune compromise, Pseudomonas can invade through the canal floor into the temporal bone and skull base. This is malignant otitis externa, a skull base osteomyelitis that often causes facial nerve palsy first. Auricular perichondritis is a different Pseudomonas infection of the ear cartilage, often after a piercing through the upper ear.","whyNot":["S. aureus is a common cause of simple otitis externa, but skull base osteomyelitis in an older diabetic patient is classically Pseudomonas.","Correct.","Nontypeable H. influenzae causes acute otitis media in children, not necrotizing otitis externa.","S. pneumoniae causes acute otitis media and meningitis, not invasive infection starting in the ear canal."],"study":{"topics":["Malignant otitis externa","Otitis externa organisms"],"comps":["NE-20-J9"]}},{"id":"NE-C50-Q4","stem":"A 63-year-old woman who has smoked for 40 years has 3 weeks of headache and new left arm weakness. She has no fever. Contrast MRI of the brain shows four round, ring-enhancing lesions of different sizes, each at the junction of the gray and white matter, with surrounding edema. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Primary diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","Meningioma","Metastatic carcinoma","Glioblastoma, IDH-wildtype"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"NE-20-F","obj":"NE-20-F.9","dok":3,"approach":["Several round lesions at the gray-white junction suggest blood-borne spread.","At the gray-white junction, arterioles narrow abruptly, so tumor emboli lodge there.","In adults, metastases are the most common intracranial tumors, and lung cancer is the most common source, which fits her smoking history."],"why":"Brain metastases outnumber primary brain tumors in adults. Lung cancer, breast cancer, melanoma, kidney cancer, and colorectal cancer are the usual sources. Tumor cells arrive in arterial blood and lodge where vessels narrow at the gray-white junction, so metastases are often multiple, well circumscribed, and surrounded by edema. Ring enhancement can make them look like abscesses, but she has no fever and no infected source. Primary CNS lymphoma is a diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, often linked to EBV in AIDS, and usually involves deep periventricular regions.","whyNot":["Primary CNS lymphoma tends to involve deep periventricular areas and is most often ring-enhancing in patients with AIDS.","Meningioma is an extra-axial, dural-based tumor that enhances uniformly. It does not form multiple ring lesions in the brain tissue.","Correct.","Glioblastoma is usually a single ring-enhancing mass with central necrosis that can cross the corpus callosum, not several separate lesions at the gray-white junction."],"study":{"topics":["Brain metastases","Ring-enhancing lesions"],"comps":["NE-20-F","NE-20-G3"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C01","system":"PU","dx":"Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome in a 29-week preterm infant of a diabetic mother","codes":["PU-14-A1"],"case":"A male newborn is delivered by cesarean section at 29 weeks' gestation to a 31-year-old woman with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes mellitus. Her hemoglobin A1c was 9.4% (normal less than 5.7) during the pregnancy. She did not receive antenatal corticosteroids. Birth weight is 1450 g. Within 30 minutes of birth, he develops tachypnea, expiratory grunting, nasal flaring, and intercostal retractions. Temperature is 36.6 °C (97.9 °F), pulse is 168/min, respirations are 76/min, and blood pressure is 48/30 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 84%. A chest x-ray shows low lung volumes with diffuse, fine reticulogranular opacities and air bronchograms in both lungs. Diagnosis: neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C01-Q1","stem":"In the same unit, an infant delivered at 20 weeks' gestation could not be oxygenated despite full ventilatory support. Which of the following structural features, present in this 29-week infant's lungs and absent at 20 weeks, best explains why he can survive with support?","options":["Complete branching of the conducting airways to terminal bronchioles","Cartilage plates and submucosal glands in bronchial walls","Mature alveoli subdivided by secondary septa","Capillaries in close contact with thin respiratory epithelium"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-1","obj":"PU-1.3","dok":2,"approach":["Place each gestational age in its stage: 20 weeks is early canalicular; 29 weeks is the saccular stage (about week 26 to birth).","Ask what gas exchange needs: a thin epithelium with capillaries pressed against it.","Pick the feature that first appears near the end of the canalicular stage and is present at 29 weeks."],"why":"Lung development runs through four stages: pseudoglandular (about weeks 5 to 17, airway branching), canalicular (about weeks 16 to 26, respiratory bronchioles form and capillaries grow in), saccular (about week 26 to birth, terminal sacs form), and alveolar (about week 36 into childhood). Late in the canalicular stage, the airspace lining thins and capillaries bulge against it, forming the first blood-air barrier. That is why survival with support first becomes possible at about weeks 24 to 26. At 20 weeks this barrier is not yet in place, so ventilation cannot oxygenate the blood.","whyNot":["The conducting airways finish branching down to the terminal bronchioles by the end of the pseudoglandular stage, about week 16 to 17. The 20-week fetus already had them, so they cannot explain the difference. Conducting airways also do no gas exchange.","Bronchial cartilage and glands form from splanchnic mesoderm and endoderm early, during the pseudoglandular stage. They are present at 20 weeks and play no part in gas exchange.","Secondary septa and mature alveoli form in the alveolar stage, from about week 36 into childhood. A 29-week infant does not have them yet, so they cannot explain his survival.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Stages of lung development","Age of viability"],"comps":["PU-1","PU-14-A1"]}},{"id":"PU-C01-Q2","stem":"Amniotic fluid obtained 2 days before delivery showed a lecithin to sphingomyelin ratio of 1.2 (mature 2.0 or greater). Which of the following best explains why this result predicted his respiratory distress?","options":["Lecithin from type II pneumocytes was still too low relative to sphingomyelin","Sphingomyelin from fetal membranes had risen above its usual late-gestation level","Type I pneumocytes had not yet started secreting lecithin into fetal lung fluid","Fetal kidneys had not yet excreted enough lecithin into the amniotic fluid"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-1","obj":"PU-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall where amniotic lecithin comes from: surfactant made by type II pneumocytes, carried out with fetal lung fluid.","Recall that sphingomyelin stays fairly constant and acts as the yardstick.","A ratio under 2 means surfactant output is still immature, which predicts alveolar collapse after birth."],"why":"Type II pneumocytes begin making surfactant in the second trimester, but output reaches mature levels only at about week 35. Fetal lung fluid carries surfactant into the amniotic fluid, so amniotic lecithin tracks surfactant production. Sphingomyelin changes little, so the lecithin to sphingomyelin ratio rises as the lungs mature. A ratio of 2 or more signals mature lungs. His ratio of 1.2 showed that surfactant was still too low to keep his alveoli open.","whyNot":["Correct.","Sphingomyelin stays roughly constant through late gestation and serves as the reference value. The ratio is low because lecithin has not risen, not because sphingomyelin is high.","Type I pneumocytes are thin gas exchange cells and do not make surfactant. Type II pneumocytes make it.","Lecithin in amniotic fluid comes from fetal lung fluid that flows out through the trachea. It is not a kidney product."],"study":{"topics":["Lecithin to sphingomyelin ratio","Timing of surfactant production"],"comps":["PU-1","PU-9"]}},{"id":"PU-C01-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains how his mother's poorly controlled diabetes increased his risk of this condition?","options":["Maternal ketoacids crossing the placenta and degrading dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine","Fetal hyperinsulinemia opposing glucocorticoid induction of surfactant synthesis","Fetal hypoglycemia limiting glycerol supply for phospholipid synthesis","Maternal insulin crossing the placenta and suppressing type II pneumocytes"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-9","obj":"PU-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the glucose: maternal hyperglycemia crosses the placenta and makes the fetus hyperglycemic.","The fetal pancreas responds with high insulin output.","Fetal insulin blunts the cortisol signal that turns on surfactant synthesis in type II pneumocytes."],"why":"Surfactant is mostly phospholipid, with dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (lecithin) as the main surface-active lipid, plus phosphatidylglycerol and surfactant proteins. Fetal cortisol and thyroid hormone promote its synthesis, which is why antenatal corticosteroids are given before preterm birth. Glucose crosses the placenta but maternal insulin does not, so the fetus of a hyperglycemic mother makes large amounts of its own insulin. Fetal hyperinsulinemia opposes the effect of cortisol and delays surfactant production, so these infants can develop respiratory distress even at gestational ages that would otherwise be safe.","whyNot":["Ketoacids do not break down surfactant lipids. Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine is the main surfactant lipid, but this is not how diabetes affects it.","Correct.","Before birth the fetus of a hyperglycemic mother is hyperglycemic, not hypoglycemic. Neonatal hypoglycemia appears after the cord is cut, when the high fetal insulin is no longer matched by maternal glucose.","Maternal insulin does not cross the placenta. The insulin that delays surfactant is made by the fetal pancreas."],"study":{"topics":["Surfactant composition","Hormonal control of surfactant","Infant of a diabetic mother"],"comps":["PU-9","PU-14-A1"]}},{"id":"PU-C01-Q4","stem":"He is treated with intratracheal surfactant and mechanical ventilation. On day 3, he has bounding peripheral pulses, a widened pulse pressure, and a continuous murmur at the left upper sternal border. Which of the following best explains the persistence of the structure causing these findings?","options":["Raised left atrial pressure holding the foramen ovale open","Incomplete fusion of the septum primum with the endocardial cushions","Persistent high pulmonary vascular resistance with right-to-left ductal flow","Hypoxemia and high prostaglandin E2 levels keeping the ductus open"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-A1","obj":"PU-14-A.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the findings: a continuous murmur with bounding pulses and wide pulse pressure means a patent ductus arteriosus.","Recall what normally closes the ductus after birth: a rise in PaO2 and a fall in prostaglandin E2.","Link his prematurity and hypoxemic lung disease to failure of both signals."],"why":"In the fetus, low oxygen tension and prostaglandin E2 from the placenta keep the ductus arteriosus open. After birth, the rise in PaO2 and the fall in prostaglandin E2 normally close it. In a preterm infant with respiratory distress syndrome, hypoxemia persists and the immature ductus stays sensitive to prostaglandin, so it remains open. As surfactant improves the lungs and pulmonary resistance falls, blood flows from the aorta into the pulmonary artery throughout the cardiac cycle, giving a continuous murmur and bounding pulses. Other complications of this disease and its treatment are bronchopulmonary dysplasia and retinopathy of prematurity from oxygen therapy.","whyNot":["Raised left atrial pressure closes the foramen ovale flap. A small atrial shunt also causes no continuous murmur or bounding pulses.","This describes an ostium primum atrial septal defect, a structural defect present from early development. It causes a systolic flow murmur and fixed split S2, not a continuous murmur.","High pulmonary resistance with right-to-left flow causes cyanosis without a continuous murmur. A continuous murmur means aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary pressure throughout the cycle, so flow is left to right.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","Patent ductus arteriosus"],"comps":["PU-14-A1","PU-14-A"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C02","system":"PU","dx":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","codes":["PU-14-A2"],"case":"A 68-year-old man comes to the physician because of an 18-month history of progressive shortness of breath on exertion and a dry cough. He smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 25 years and quit 15 years ago. He worked as an accountant and has no known occupational or environmental exposures. He takes no medications. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows fine end-inspiratory crackles at both lung bases and clubbing of the fingers. Pulmonary function testing shows an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.88 (normal 0.70 or greater), a total lung capacity of 61% of predicted (normal 80 to 120), and a DLCO of 42% of predicted (normal 75 to 125). High-resolution CT shows subpleural, basal reticular opacities with honeycombing. Diagnosis: idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C02-Q1","stem":"A surgical lung biopsy shows patchy subpleural fibrosis that alternates with areas of normal lung, scattered fibroblastic foci, and honeycomb change. Which of the following best describes the process that produced these changes?","options":["Repeated alveolar epithelial injury with TGF-beta driven fibrosis","Th1 cell granulomatous reaction to repeatedly inhaled organic antigens","Neutrophil elastase destruction of alveolar walls distal to terminal bronchioles","Diffuse capillary endothelial injury with protein-rich exudate and hyaline membranes"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-A2","obj":"PU-14-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the biopsy pattern: patchy fibrosis of different ages, fibroblastic foci, and honeycombing is usual interstitial pneumonia.","Recall the current model of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis: repeated small injuries to alveolar epithelium with abnormal repair.","Name the main fibrogenic signal: TGF-beta activates fibroblasts and drives collagen deposition."],"why":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is thought to start with repeated injury to alveolar epithelial cells, with risk raised by smoking, older age, and genetic variants. Instead of normal repair, injured epithelium releases TGF-beta and other factors that recruit and activate fibroblasts. The result is the usual interstitial pneumonia pattern: patchy fibrosis that is worst at the bases and under the pleura, fibroblastic foci at the active edge, and honeycomb cysts where lung architecture is destroyed. Areas of different ages side by side reflect injury that happens again and again over time.","whyNot":["Correct.","This describes hypersensitivity pneumonitis, which shows poorly formed granulomas centered on bronchioles and a history of exposure to organic dust. He has no exposures and the biopsy shows no granulomas.","Elastase destruction of alveolar walls produces emphysema, an obstructive disease with increased compliance and a low FEV1/FVC ratio. His ratio is high and his lungs are small.","This is diffuse alveolar damage, the lesion of acute respiratory distress syndrome. It develops over days, involves the lungs uniformly, and does not show patchy fibrosis of different ages."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Usual interstitial pneumonia pattern"],"comps":["PU-14-A2","PU-14-A"]}},{"id":"PU-C02-Q2","stem":"His oxygen saturation is 94% at rest and falls to 86% after a 3-minute walk. Which of the following best explains the fall in saturation with exercise?","options":["Right-to-left shunting of blood through intrapulmonary arteriovenous channels","Alveolar hypoventilation from fatigue of the respiratory muscles","Faster red cell transit through capillaries across a thickened barrier","Left shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve during exercise"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-7","obj":"PU-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Use Fick law: diffusion falls when the barrier is thicker and the surface area is smaller, both present in fibrosis.","At rest, red cells spend about 0.75 second in the capillary, enough time to equilibrate even with slow diffusion.","With exercise, cardiac output rises and transit time shortens, so oxygen uptake becomes diffusion-limited."],"why":"Fick law states that gas transfer is proportional to surface area and the pressure difference, and inversely proportional to barrier thickness. In a healthy lung, oxygen uptake is perfusion-limited because blood equilibrates with alveolar gas within the first third of the capillary. Fibrosis thickens the blood-air barrier and destroys capillary surface area, which is why his DLCO is low. At rest there is still enough time to load oxygen, but exercise speeds blood through the capillaries, so blood leaves before it equilibrates and saturation falls. Carbon monoxide uptake is always diffusion-limited, which is why DLCO measures the diffusing capacity.","whyNot":["Intrapulmonary arteriovenous shunts occur in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia and hepatopulmonary syndrome. They are not part of pulmonary fibrosis.","Patients with fibrosis breathe rapidly and usually have a normal or low PaCO2. A 3-minute walk does not fatigue the respiratory muscles enough to cause hypoventilation.","Correct.","Exercise raises temperature, CO2, and acid in working muscle, which shift the curve to the right. A left shift would not cause desaturation in the lung."],"study":{"topics":["Fick law of diffusion","Perfusion-limited versus diffusion-limited exchange","DLCO"],"comps":["PU-7","PU-2"]}},{"id":"PU-C02-Q3","stem":"Which of the following changes in the mechanical properties and connective tissue of his lungs best accounts for his small lung volumes?","options":["Increased compliance from destruction of elastic fibers","Decreased compliance from excess interstitial collagen","Increased compliance from impaired elastin cross-linking","Normal compliance with increased chest wall stiffness"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-9","obj":"PU-9.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the restrictive pattern: low total lung capacity with a high FEV1/FVC ratio.","Ask what connective tissue change makes lungs stiffer: collagen replacing normal interstitium.","Link stiffness to low compliance, which limits how far the lungs can be inflated."],"why":"Lung compliance is the change in volume for a given change in pressure. Elastin, cross-linked by the copper-dependent enzyme lysyl oxidase, gives the lung stretch and recoil. Collagen is much less stretchy. In pulmonary fibrosis, fibroblasts lay down large amounts of collagen in the interstitium, so the lungs become stiff and compliance falls. The respiratory muscles cannot expand stiff lungs as far, so total lung capacity and the other volumes fall. Emphysema does the opposite: elastin is destroyed, compliance rises, and the lungs overinflate.","whyNot":["Loss of elastic fibers makes lungs floppy and easy to inflate, with high compliance and high total lung capacity. This is emphysema.","Correct.","Poor elastin cross-linking, as with copper deficiency reducing lysyl oxidase activity, weakens elastic recoil and raises compliance. His lungs are stiff, with low compliance.","A stiff chest wall, as in kyphoscoliosis, causes extrapulmonary restriction with normal DLCO. His low DLCO, crackles, and CT findings place the problem in the lung itself."],"study":{"topics":["Lung compliance","Elastin versus collagen in the lung"],"comps":["PU-9","PU-6"]}},{"id":"PU-C02-Q4","stem":"In the same clinic, a 50-year-old woman who has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 30 years has gradually progressive dyspnea. A lung biopsy shows alveoli uniformly filled with pigmented macrophages, mild uniform thickening of the alveolar septa, and no fibroblastic foci or honeycombing. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Fibrotic nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia","Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Usual interstitial pneumonia"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-A","obj":"PU-14-A.7","dok":2,"approach":["Note the key clues: a heavy smoker and airspaces uniformly filled with pigmented macrophages.","Contrast the patterns: macrophages in airspaces, uniform septal fibrosis, fibroblast plugs, or patchy fibrosis with honeycombing.","Match macrophage-filled alveoli in a smoker to desquamative interstitial pneumonia."],"why":"Desquamative interstitial pneumonia is a smoking-related interstitial lung disease. The name comes from an old belief that the cells in the alveoli were shed epithelial cells; they are actually macrophages, often containing brown smoker's pigment. The change is uniform throughout the biopsy, with only mild septal thickening. It usually improves with stopping smoking, and its prognosis is much better than that of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis.","whyNot":["Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia shows uniform interstitial inflammation and fibrosis of the same age. Its defining change is in the septa, not macrophages packing the airspaces.","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia shows polypoid plugs of loose fibroblastic tissue (Masson bodies) in alveolar ducts and alveoli. Macrophages do not dominate the picture.","Correct.","Usual interstitial pneumonia, the pattern of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, is patchy, with fibroblastic foci and honeycombing. Her biopsy has none of these."],"study":{"topics":["Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Patterns of interstitial pneumonia"],"comps":["PU-14-A","PU-14-A2"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C03","system":"PU","dx":"Chronic simple silicosis in a sandblaster","codes":["PU-14-A3"],"case":"A 57-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-year history of slowly worsening shortness of breath on exertion and a dry cough. He has worked as a sandblaster in a shipyard for 26 years and often did not wear respiratory protection. He has never smoked. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 82/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Lung examination shows a few scattered inspiratory crackles in the upper lung fields. A chest x-ray shows many small, rounded nodules concentrated in both upper lobes and thin rims of calcification around enlarged hilar lymph nodes. Diagnosis: chronic simple silicosis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C03-Q1","stem":"He has an increased risk of developing pulmonary tuberculosis. Which of the following best explains this risk?","options":["Silica-induced loss of secretory IgA in airway secretions","Silica-induced depletion of CD4+ helper T cells in hilar nodes","Replacement of ciliated epithelium in upper lobe bronchi by squamous cells","Impaired mycobacterial killing by silica-damaged macrophages"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-A3","obj":"PU-14-A.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the cell that eats silica: the alveolar macrophage.","Recall what silica does inside it: it damages the phagolysosome and activates the inflammasome, injuring or killing the cell.","Link this to tuberculosis, which is controlled mainly by macrophages that must kill the bacilli they ingest."],"why":"Inhaled silica particles are taken up by alveolar macrophages. Inside the phagolysosome, crystalline silica damages the membrane, releases enzymes, activates the inflammasome, and can kill the cell. The injured macrophages release cytokines and growth factors that drive the fibrotic nodules. These same macrophages are the main defense against Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and silica-damaged macrophages kill mycobacteria poorly. Silicosis therefore raises the risk of active tuberculosis. Other pneumoconiosis features to recall: coal dust also affects the upper lobes, asbestosis affects the lower lobes with ferruginous bodies, and berylliosis causes noncaseating granulomas.","whyNot":["Silica does not reduce secretory IgA. IgA deficiency causes recurrent sinus and lung infections with ordinary bacteria, not tuberculosis.","Systemic CD4+ T cell loss, as in HIV infection, raises tuberculosis risk, but silica does not deplete CD4+ T cells.","Squamous metaplasia from smoking impairs mucus clearance and favors bacterial bronchitis. It does not explain the specific link between silica and tuberculosis, and he has never smoked.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Silicosis and tuberculosis","Comparison of pneumoconioses"],"comps":["PU-14-A3","PU-12"]}},{"id":"PU-C03-Q2","stem":"His 45-year-old cousin works in a cotton textile mill. He has chest tightness, cough, and wheezing that are worst on the first day back at work after a weekend and improve as the work week goes on. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Bagassosis","Byssinosis","Silo-filler disease","Berylliosis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-A","obj":"PU-14-A.8","dok":2,"approach":["Note the exposure: cotton dust in a textile mill.","Note the timing: symptoms worst on the first workday after time away and easing as the week goes on.","Match cotton dust with this weekly pattern to byssinosis."],"why":"Byssinosis is an occupational airway disease caused by dust from cotton, flax, or hemp. Contaminants in the dust, including bacterial endotoxin, trigger bronchoconstriction and airway inflammation. The classic feature is chest tightness and wheezing on the first day back at work after a break, which lessens with continued exposure over the week. Years of exposure can cause chronic airflow obstruction.","whyNot":["Bagassosis is hypersensitivity pneumonitis from moldy sugarcane fiber (bagasse). It causes fever, cough, and interstitial disease after exposure, without the weekly pattern.","Correct.","Silo-filler disease follows inhalation of nitrogen dioxide in a freshly filled silo. It causes acute bronchiolitis and pulmonary edema, sometimes with later bronchiolitis obliterans.","Berylliosis follows exposure to beryllium in aerospace, electronics, or nuclear work. It causes noncaseating granulomas similar to sarcoidosis."],"study":{"topics":["Occupational lung diseases","Byssinosis"],"comps":["PU-14-A","PU-14-A3"]}},{"id":"PU-C03-Q3","stem":"A 52-year-old dairy farmer who lives nearby develops fever, chills, cough, and dyspnea 6 hours after forking moldy hay. The symptoms resolve over 2 days and recur with each new exposure. A lung biopsy shows lymphocytic infiltrates centered on the bronchioles and poorly formed noncaseating interstitial granulomas. Which of the following immune mechanisms best explains his illness?","options":["Immune complex and T cell reactions to inhaled antigens","Autoantibodies against type IV collagen in alveolar basement membranes","Th2 cytokine-driven eosinophilic inflammation of bronchial walls","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation within minutes of exposure"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-12","obj":"PU-12.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note the trigger: an organic dust (moldy hay with thermophilic actinomycetes).","Note the timing and pathology: fever hours after exposure, lymphocytes around bronchioles, and poorly formed granulomas.","Match this to hypersensitivity pneumonitis, which combines type III (immune complex) and type IV (T cell) reactions."],"why":"This is farmer lung, a form of hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused by spores of thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay. In the acute form, antibodies against the inhaled antigen form immune complexes in the alveolar walls (type III), which explains the onset 4 to 8 hours after exposure with fever and chills. With repeated exposure, T cell-mediated inflammation (type IV) produces lymphocytic infiltrates and poorly formed granulomas. Asthma differs in being a type I, IgE-mediated airway disease that begins within minutes and has eosinophils and no interstitial granulomas.","whyNot":["Correct.","Antibodies against type IV collagen cause Goodpasture syndrome, a type II reaction with lung hemorrhage and glomerulonephritis. Inhaled antigens are not involved.","Th2 cytokines and eosinophils drive allergic asthma. The biopsy here shows lymphocytes and granulomas in the interstitium, not eosinophilic bronchial inflammation.","This is a type I reaction, as in allergic asthma. It begins within minutes and causes wheezing, not fever, interstitial granulomas, and onset hours after exposure."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Hypersensitivity reaction types in the lung"],"comps":["PU-12","PU-14-A"]}},{"id":"PU-C03-Q4","stem":"Spirometry in the sandblaster shows an FEV1 of 62% of predicted (normal 80 or greater), an FVC of 60% of predicted (normal 80 or greater), and an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.84 (normal 0.70 or greater). Total lung capacity by body plethysmography is 64% of predicted (normal 80 to 120). Which of the following flow-volume loop patterns is most likely?","options":["Concave, scooped-out expiratory limb beginning at high lung volumes","Flat plateaus on both the inspiratory and the expiratory limbs","Narrow loop at low lung volumes with a normal expiratory shape","Flat plateau on the inspiratory limb with a normal expiratory limb"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-6","obj":"PU-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Read the numbers: FEV1 and FVC are both reduced, the ratio is normal, and total lung capacity is low.","Classify the pattern as restrictive.","Predict the loop: a small loop shifted toward low lung volumes, with a normal-shaped expiratory limb."],"why":"In restrictive disease, stiff lungs hold less air, so total lung capacity and FVC fall. FEV1 falls about in proportion, and the extra elastic recoil keeps airways open, so the FEV1/FVC ratio is normal or high. On a flow-volume loop, the whole loop is narrow and sits at lower lung volumes, but the expiratory limb keeps its normal shape. In obstructive disease, the expiratory limb is scooped out and the loop shifts to high lung volumes. Upper airway lesions flatten one or both limbs into plateaus.","whyNot":["A scooped-out expiratory limb at high lung volumes is the obstructive pattern of asthma or COPD. His FEV1/FVC ratio is normal and his total lung capacity is low.","Flattening of both limbs is the pattern of a fixed upper airway obstruction, such as tracheal stenosis. It limits peak flows without lowering total lung capacity.","Correct.","Flattening of only the inspiratory limb points to a variable obstruction outside the thorax, such as vocal cord paralysis. Lung volumes are normal."],"study":{"topics":["Flow-volume loops","Obstructive versus restrictive spirometry"],"comps":["PU-6","PU-14-A"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C04","system":"PU","dx":"Pulmonary sarcoidosis with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and hypercalcemia","codes":["PU-14-A4"],"case":"A 36-year-old Black woman comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of dry cough, shortness of breath on exertion, fatigue, and increased thirst and urination. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 86/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Lung examination shows a few scattered crackles. Serum studies show a calcium concentration of 11.6 mg/dL (normal 8.5 to 10.2), a parathyroid hormone concentration of 8 pg/mL (normal 10 to 65), and an angiotensin-converting enzyme activity of 98 U/L (normal 8 to 52). A chest x-ray shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy with reticular opacities in both lungs. Transbronchial biopsy shows noncaseating granulomas, and stains for acid-fast bacilli and fungi are negative. Diagnosis: pulmonary sarcoidosis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C04-Q1","stem":"Her serum 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D concentration is increased. Which of the following best explains her hypercalcemia?","options":["Unregulated 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity in granuloma macrophages","Increased hepatic 25-hydroxylation of dietary vitamin D","Parathyroid hormone-related peptide secreted by granuloma cells","Increased renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity driven by parathyroid hormone"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-14-A4","obj":"PU-14-A.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: high calcium, low parathyroid hormone, and high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D.","Low parathyroid hormone means the kidney is not the source of the extra active vitamin D.","Identify the source outside the kidney: activated macrophages in granulomas express 1-alpha-hydroxylase that escapes normal feedback."],"why":"Active vitamin D (calcitriol, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) is made by adding a hydroxyl group at carbon 1 of 25-hydroxyvitamin D. In the kidney, this step is driven by parathyroid hormone and shut off when calcium is high. Macrophages activated by IFN-gamma in sarcoid granulomas also express 1-alpha-hydroxylase, and this enzyme is not controlled by parathyroid hormone or calcium. Excess calcitriol increases intestinal calcium absorption, causing hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria, which suppress parathyroid hormone. High urine calcium explains her thirst and frequent urination.","whyNot":["Correct.","Liver 25-hydroxylation is not tightly regulated and is not the step that sets active vitamin D levels. It would not explain a high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D with a low parathyroid hormone.","Parathyroid hormone-related peptide causes hypercalcemia in squamous cell carcinoma and some other cancers. It usually leaves 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D low or normal, and granulomas do not make it.","Her parathyroid hormone is low, as expected when calcium is high. The kidney enzyme is turned down, not up."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation","Hypercalcemia in sarcoidosis"],"comps":["PU-14-A4","PU-12"]}},{"id":"PU-C04-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the immune process that forms and maintains her granulomas?","options":["Th17 cells releasing IL-17 that recruits neutrophils","Regulatory T cells releasing IL-10 that deactivates macrophages","Th2 cells releasing IL-4 that drives IgE class switching","Th1 cells releasing IFN-gamma to activate macrophages"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-12","obj":"PU-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion: a noncaseating granuloma made of epithelioid macrophages, giant cells, and surrounding lymphocytes.","Recall that granulomas are a Th1 response.","Link IFN-gamma from Th1 cells to macrophage activation, and TNF-alpha from macrophages to keeping the granuloma intact."],"why":"In sarcoidosis, an unknown antigen is presented to CD4+ T cells, which take on a Th1 profile. Macrophages release IL-12, which drives Th1 differentiation, and Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages and turns them into epithelioid cells and multinucleated giant cells. Activated macrophages release TNF-alpha, which recruits cells and holds the granuloma together. The same pathway contains tuberculosis, which is why blocking TNF-alpha can reactivate latent tuberculosis.","whyNot":["IL-17 recruits neutrophils and helps fight extracellular bacteria and fungi. Neutrophils are not the main cells of a sarcoid granuloma.","IL-10 suppresses macrophages and limits inflammation. Granulomas form when macrophages are activated, not deactivated.","IL-4 from Th2 cells drives IgE production and allergic inflammation, as in asthma. It does not build epithelioid granulomas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Granuloma formation","Th1 cytokines"],"comps":["PU-12","PU-14-A4"]}},{"id":"PU-C04-Q3","stem":"Endobronchial ultrasound-guided needle aspiration is planned for an enlarged lymph node at her left hilum. The node lies against the most superior structure in the root of the left lung. Which of the following structures is this most likely to be?","options":["Left superior pulmonary vein","Left pulmonary artery","Left inferior pulmonary vein","Left main bronchus"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-4","obj":"PU-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["Picture the root of the left lung from the medial side.","Recall the rule: on the left the pulmonary artery is the highest structure; on the right it lies in front of the bronchus, with the upper lobe bronchus above it.","The veins are anterior and inferior at both hila."],"why":"Each lung root contains a pulmonary artery, a main bronchus, two pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, nerves, and lymph nodes. On the left, the pulmonary artery arches over the main bronchus and is the most superior structure. On the right, the upper lobe (eparterial) bronchus branches off above the artery, and the right pulmonary artery lies in front of the bronchus. On both sides the pulmonary veins are the most anterior and inferior structures. Enlarged hilar nodes in sarcoidosis sit among these structures.","whyNot":["The superior pulmonary vein lies anterior and inferior to the pulmonary artery at each hilum.","Correct.","The inferior pulmonary vein is the lowest structure in the lung root, close to the pulmonary ligament.","At the left hilum the main bronchus lies below the pulmonary artery and toward the back. At the right hilum, the upper lobe bronchus is the highest structure."],"study":{"topics":["Root of the lung","Hilar relationships"],"comps":["PU-4"]}},{"id":"PU-C04-Q4","stem":"Pulmonary function testing shows a total lung capacity of 68% of predicted (normal 80 to 120) and a DLCO of 52% of predicted (normal 75 to 125). A 60-year-old man with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis has a similar total lung capacity because of respiratory muscle weakness, and his PaCO2 is 52 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45). Which of the following findings is most likely in the man?","options":["Reduced DLCO and widened A-a gradient","Normal DLCO and widened A-a gradient","Increased DLCO and widened A-a gradient","Normal DLCO and normal A-a gradient"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-14-A","obj":"PU-14-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Classify each restriction: hers is in the lung tissue; his comes from weak respiratory muscles.","In muscle weakness the alveolar walls and capillaries are normal, so diffusion is normal.","His low oxygen comes from hypoventilation alone, which lowers PAO2 and PaO2 together and keeps the A-a gradient normal."],"why":"Restriction can arise inside the lung or outside it. Intrinsic disease, such as sarcoidosis or fibrosis, damages the alveolar walls, so DLCO falls and ventilation-perfusion mismatch widens the A-a gradient. Extrapulmonary restriction comes from weak muscles, a deformed chest wall, or obesity. The lung tissue is healthy, so DLCO adjusted for lung volume is normal. If ventilation falls, PaCO2 rises and alveolar PO2 falls, but arterial PO2 falls by the same amount, so the A-a gradient stays normal.","whyNot":["This is her pattern. Granulomas and fibrosis thicken the blood-air barrier and disturb ventilation-perfusion matching, so DLCO falls and the A-a gradient widens.","A widened A-a gradient means a problem in gas exchange inside the lung. His lungs are normal, so his gradient stays normal.","DLCO rises with polycythemia or alveolar hemorrhage, which add hemoglobin to bind CO. Neither applies, and his gas exchange surface is normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Intrinsic versus extrapulmonary restriction","A-a gradient in hypoventilation"],"comps":["PU-14-A","PU-7"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C05","system":"PU","dx":"Allergic asthma with an acute exacerbation","codes":["PU-14-B1"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of 6 hours of increasing shortness of breath, wheezing, and cough that began while visiting a friend who has two cats. She has a history of allergic rhinitis and atopic dermatitis. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 30/min, and blood pressure is 124/76 mm Hg, with a pulsus paradoxus of 18 mm Hg (normal less than 10). She speaks in short phrases and uses her neck muscles to breathe. Lung examination shows diffuse expiratory wheezes with a prolonged expiratory phase. Peak expiratory flow is 40% of her personal best. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 91%. Diagnosis: acute exacerbation of allergic asthma.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C05-Q1","stem":"Microscopy of her sputum shows spiral-shaped mucus casts and colorless, bipyramidal crystals. Which of the following findings would most likely be seen on a biopsy of her bronchial wall?","options":["Thickened subepithelial basement membrane and goblet cell hyperplasia","Destruction of alveolar walls distal to the terminal bronchioles","Permanent bronchial dilation with necrotizing transmural inflammation","Noncaseating granulomas in the bronchial submucosa"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-B1","obj":"PU-14-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the sputum findings: Curschmann spirals (shed epithelium in mucus casts) and Charcot-Leyden crystals (from eosinophil protein).","These point to eosinophilic airway inflammation of asthma.","Recall the airway remodeling of asthma: thickened basement membrane, goblet cell hyperplasia, submucosal gland enlargement, and smooth muscle hypertrophy."],"why":"Allergic asthma is a chronic Th2 inflammatory disease of the bronchi and bronchioles. Charcot-Leyden crystals form from galectin-10 released by eosinophils, and Curschmann spirals are mucus casts of small airways with shed epithelial cells. Over time the airway wall remodels: collagen is laid down beneath the epithelium so the basement membrane looks thick, goblet cells and submucosal glands increase, and smooth muscle hypertrophies. Asthma can also be triggered by nonallergic factors such as viral infections, cold air, exercise, and aspirin.","whyNot":["Correct.","Alveolar wall destruction is emphysema. It affects the airspaces beyond the bronchioles, not the bronchial wall.","Necrotizing inflammation that destroys the wall and leaves the bronchus permanently dilated is bronchiectasis. Asthma airways are narrowed, and the obstruction is reversible.","Noncaseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis or berylliosis. They are not a feature of asthma."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of asthma","Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals"],"comps":["PU-14-B1","PU-12"]}},{"id":"PU-C05-Q2","stem":"On arrival, arterial blood gas analysis on room air showed a pH of 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45) and a PaCO2 of 31 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45). After 3 hours of treatment, she is drowsy, and breath sounds are faint with little wheezing. Repeat analysis shows a pH of 7.36, a PaCO2 of 45 mm Hg, and a PaO2 of 60 mm Hg (normal 75 to 100). Which of the following best explains the change in her blood gas values?","options":["Development of metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","Respiratory muscle fatigue with falling alveolar ventilation","Resolution of bronchospasm with a return to normal ventilation","Reduced ventilatory drive after correction of hypoxemia"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-14-B1","obj":"PU-14-B.2","dok":3,"approach":["In acute asthma, hypoxemia and airway receptors drive hyperventilation, so PaCO2 starts low.","A PaCO2 that climbs back to normal while she is still hypoxemic means ventilation is failing.","Drowsiness and a quiet chest confirm tiring muscles and little air movement."],"why":"Early in an asthma attack, patients breathe fast and blow off CO2, producing respiratory alkalosis. Air trapping forces the respiratory muscles to work at a mechanical disadvantage, and they eventually tire. As alveolar ventilation falls, PaCO2 rises toward normal and then above it. A normal PaCO2 in a patient who is still working hard to breathe is therefore an ominous sign of impending respiratory failure. A quiet chest means little air is moving, not that the obstruction has resolved.","whyNot":["Respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis lowers PaCO2. Her PaCO2 rose.","Correct.","Improving asthma brings better air movement, louder breath sounds, a clearer mind, and higher PaO2. Her drowsiness, faint breath sounds, and low PaO2 show the opposite.","Loss of hypoxic drive with oxygen therapy is a concern in some patients with chronic CO2 retention. She has no chronic lung disease, and her PaO2 is still low."],"study":{"topics":["Blood gases in acute asthma","Signs of impending respiratory failure"],"comps":["PU-14-B1","PU-8"]}},{"id":"PU-C05-Q3","stem":"Her symptoms improve, then return 6 hours later without further exposure, and her sputum now contains many eosinophils. Which of the following cytokines most directly drives the production and activation of the cells that dominate this second phase?","options":["Interferon-gamma from Th1 cells","Interleukin-4 from Th2 cells","Interleukin-13 from Th2 cells","Interleukin-5 from Th2 cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-12","obj":"PU-12.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the late phase response: symptoms return 4 to 8 hours after exposure, driven by recruited inflammatory cells.","Identify the dominant cell: eosinophils.","Match eosinophil production and activation to IL-5 from Th2 cells."],"why":"Allergic asthma is a type I hypersensitivity reaction. In the early phase, within minutes, allergen cross-links IgE on mast cells, which release histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins, causing bronchospasm. In the late phase, 4 to 8 hours later, recruited cells sustain the inflammation. Th2 cells release IL-4 (IgE class switching), IL-5 (eosinophil production in the marrow, recruitment, and activation), and IL-13 (mucus secretion and airway hyperresponsiveness). Eosinophils release major basic protein and other granule contents that injure the airway epithelium.","whyNot":["IFN-gamma activates macrophages in Th1 responses and tends to suppress Th2 inflammation.","IL-4 drives B cell class switching to IgE and Th2 differentiation. It sets up the allergy but does not directly produce and activate eosinophils.","IL-13 drives mucus production by goblet cells and airway hyperresponsiveness. It is not the main growth and activation factor for eosinophils.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Early and late phase allergic responses","Th2 cytokines"],"comps":["PU-12","PU-14-B1"]}},{"id":"PU-C05-Q4","stem":"During the attack, smooth muscle contraction reduces the radius of her medium-sized bronchi by half. Assuming laminar flow and no other changes, the resistance of these airways increases by approximately which of the following factors?","options":["4-fold","8-fold","16-fold","32-fold"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-5","obj":"PU-5.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall Poiseuille law: resistance equals 8 times viscosity times length, divided by pi times radius to the fourth power.","Halving the radius divides the denominator by 2 to the fourth power, which is 16.","Resistance rises 16-fold, so the same flow needs 16 times the pressure difference."],"why":"Poiseuille law shows that airway resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of the radius. Halving the radius raises resistance 16-fold, which is why modest bronchoconstriction causes severe obstruction. Lung volume also matters: at low lung volumes, airways lose radial traction and narrow, so resistance rises. During forced expiration, rising pleural pressure also compresses the airways, which is why wheezing is loudest in expiration. Gas density affects resistance where flow is turbulent, so breathing a low-density helium-oxygen mixture can lower resistance in large airways.","whyNot":["A 4-fold rise would follow if resistance depended on the square of the radius, as cross-sectional area does. Resistance depends on the fourth power.","An 8-fold rise would follow from a cube relation. Resistance depends on the fourth power of the radius.","Correct.","A 32-fold rise would follow from a fifth-power relation. Resistance depends on the fourth power of the radius."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille law","Determinants of airway resistance"],"comps":["PU-5","PU-14-B1"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C06","system":"PU","dx":"Bronchiectasis from primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome)","codes":["PU-14-B2"],"case":"A 26-year-old man comes to the physician because of a daily cough productive of large amounts of purulent sputum. He has had recurrent sinus infections and pneumonias since early childhood. He and his wife have been unable to conceive for 2 years. He has never smoked. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 120/74 mm Hg. Heart sounds are loudest to the right of the sternum. There is clubbing of the fingers, and coarse crackles are heard over both lower lung fields. CT shows dextrocardia, a left-sided liver, and dilated, thick-walled bronchi in both lower lobes. Semen analysis shows a normal sperm count with no motile sperm. Diagnosis: bronchiectasis due to primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome).","questions":[{"id":"PU-C06-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the reversed position of his heart and liver?","options":["Failed neural crest migration into the conotruncal septum","Abnormal fusion of the superior and inferior endocardial cushions","Absent nodal ciliary flow at left-right patterning","Displacement of the heart by a hypoplastic right lung"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-10","obj":"PU-10.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note that both the heart and the liver are reversed: this is situs inversus.","Recall that motile cilia at the embryonic node beat to create a leftward fluid flow that sets the left-right axis.","With immotile cilia, sidedness is decided by chance, so about half of patients have situs inversus."],"why":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia is usually autosomal recessive and most often affects the dynein arms of the ciliary axoneme. In the early embryo, rotating cilia at the primitive node push fluid to the left, which turns on left-sided genes and sets left-right patterning. When these cilia cannot move, organ sidedness becomes random, so about half of patients have situs inversus. Kartagener syndrome is the combination of situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis. Men are usually infertile because sperm flagella share the same axoneme structure and cannot swim.","whyNot":["Neural crest failure in the outflow tract causes defects such as tetralogy of Fallot and truncus arteriosus. It does not reverse the position of the organs.","Endocardial cushion defects cause atrioventricular septal defects, often in Down syndrome. They do not change the left-right position of organs.","Correct.","A small right lung can pull the heart to the right (dextroposition), but it would not move the liver to the left. His organs are mirror-imaged."],"study":{"topics":["Primary ciliary dyskinesia","Situs inversus"],"comps":["PU-10","PU-2"]}},{"id":"PU-C06-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains his recurrent airway infections?","options":["Abnormal CFTR chloride transport causing dehydrated mucus","Failed dynein-driven sliding of microtubule doublets","Squamous metaplasia replacing the ciliated epithelium","Hyperplasia of submucosal glands with excess mucus"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-2","obj":"PU-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Link the three clues: recurrent sinus and lung infections, immotile sperm, and situs inversus all depend on motile cilia or flagella.","Recall the axoneme: 9 outer microtubule doublets around a central pair, with dynein arms that make the doublets slide.","Without dynein, cilia cannot beat, so the mucociliary escalator stops and mucus and bacteria stay in the airways."],"why":"Airway cilia have a 9 plus 2 axoneme: nine outer doublets around two central microtubules. Dynein arms on each doublet use ATP to walk along the neighboring doublet, and this sliding bends the cilium. Coordinated beating moves the mucus layer up toward the pharynx at a few millimeters per minute, carrying trapped particles and bacteria out of the lung. When dynein is defective, mucus pools in the sinuses and bronchi, and repeated infection follows. Smoking causes a similar but acquired failure by paralyzing cilia and replacing ciliated cells with squamous epithelium.","whyNot":["Cystic fibrosis causes thick mucus and bronchiectasis, but the cilia and sperm tails are normal. Infertility in men with cystic fibrosis comes from absent vasa deferentia, which leaves no sperm in the semen. His sperm count is normal.","Correct.","Squamous metaplasia is a response to cigarette smoke and other irritants. He has never smoked, and metaplasia would not explain motionless sperm.","Mucous gland hyperplasia is the lesion of chronic bronchitis in smokers. It would not explain infertility with immotile sperm or situs inversus."],"study":{"topics":["Mucociliary clearance","Ciliary axoneme structure"],"comps":["PU-2","PU-10"]}},{"id":"PU-C06-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains the permanent dilation of his bronchi?","options":["Necrotizing infection destroying muscle and elastic tissue","Smooth muscle hypertrophy with episodic bronchospasm","Loss of radial traction after destruction of alveolar walls","Traction on airways from surrounding interstitial fibrosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-B2","obj":"PU-14-B.3","dok":3,"approach":["Bronchiectasis needs two things: obstruction that keeps secretions in the airway and infection that damages the wall.","Here, failed mucociliary clearance provides the retained secretions.","Repeated necrotizing infection destroys the muscle and elastic tissue of the wall, so the bronchus stays widened."],"why":"Bronchiectasis is permanent dilation of bronchi and bronchioles. It develops when secretions are retained and infected again and again. Neutrophils and bacterial enzymes destroy smooth muscle, elastic tissue, and cartilage, and the weakened wall dilates. Causes include cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, and blockage by a tumor or foreign body. Patients have large amounts of foul, purulent sputum, hemoptysis from enlarged bronchial arteries, clubbing, and, after years of chronic inflammation, risk of secondary (AA) amyloidosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Smooth muscle hypertrophy and spasm narrow the airways in asthma, and the narrowing is reversible.","Loss of alveolar attachments lets small airways collapse during expiration in emphysema. It does not permanently widen the bronchi.","Traction bronchiectasis occurs in advanced pulmonary fibrosis, where contracting scar pulls airways open. He has no interstitial fibrosis, and his disease is driven by infection."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of bronchiectasis","Causes and complications of bronchiectasis"],"comps":["PU-14-B2","PU-14-B"]}},{"id":"PU-C06-Q4","stem":"A resected segment of his lower lobe shows several dilated airways. The pathologist confirms that these airways are bronchi and not bronchioles. Which of the following wall features is the most reliable basis for this conclusion?","options":["Spiral smooth muscle beneath the mucosa","Club cells scattered among ciliated cells","Cartilage plates with submucosal glands","Ciliated columnar cells lining the lumen"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-2","obj":"PU-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the features that stop at the bronchus-bronchiole transition: cartilage plates and submucosal glands.","Note which features continue into bronchioles: smooth muscle and, in the larger ones, ciliated cells.","Choose the feature found only in bronchi."],"why":"Bronchi have plates of hyaline cartilage, submucosal seromucous glands, and many goblet cells. Bronchioles, about 1 mm across or smaller, have no cartilage and no glands, and their goblet cells thin out and are replaced by club cells near the terminal bronchioles. Because bronchioles lack cartilage, they stay open only through smooth muscle tone and the radial pull of attached alveolar walls. That is why they collapse in emphysema, when the alveolar attachments are destroyed.","whyNot":["Smooth muscle lines both bronchi and bronchioles. In bronchioles it makes up a larger share of the wall, so its presence does not separate the two.","Club cells are typical of bronchioles, especially terminal bronchioles. Their presence would point toward a bronchiole.","Correct.","Larger bronchioles are also lined by ciliated columnar cells, and the lining changes only gradually along the airway, so this cannot separate them."],"study":{"topics":["Bronchi versus bronchioles","Airway wall histology"],"comps":["PU-2","PU-14-B2"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C07","system":"PU","dx":"Chronic bronchitis with cor pulmonale","codes":["PU-14-B3"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of increasing shortness of breath and swelling of her ankles for 2 months. She has smoked 1.5 packs of cigarettes daily for 40 years. For the past 5 years, she has had a cough productive of mucoid sputum on most days for at least 3 months each year. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg. She is overweight and has central cyanosis. Examination shows scattered rhonchi and wheezes, jugular venous distention, a left parasternal heave, a loud pulmonic component of S2, an enlarged liver, and pitting edema of both ankles. Hemoglobin concentration is 17.8 g/dL (normal 12 to 16). Spirometry shows an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.55 (normal 0.70 or greater). Diagnosis: chronic bronchitis with cor pulmonale.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C07-Q1","stem":"Which of the following changes in her bronchial walls best explains her chronic productive cough?","options":["Thickened basement membrane with many eosinophils","Submucosal gland layer over half the bronchial wall thickness","Permanent bronchial dilation with destruction of the cartilage","Enlarged airspaces with alveolar wall destruction"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-B3","obj":"PU-14-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize chronic bronchitis by its clinical definition: productive cough for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years.","Ask what makes the extra mucus: enlarged submucosal mucous glands.","Recall how this is measured: the Reid index, gland thickness divided by wall thickness, is above 0.5."],"why":"Chronic bronchitis is defined clinically by a productive cough for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years. Smoke and other irritants cause hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the submucosal mucous glands of the large airways and more goblet cells in smaller airways. The Reid index compares the thickness of the gland layer with the thickness of the wall between epithelium and cartilage. It is normally under 0.4 and is above 0.5 in chronic bronchitis. Mucus plugging and inflammation of small airways cause the airflow obstruction.","whyNot":["A thick basement membrane with eosinophils is the remodeling of asthma, which causes episodic wheezing more than daily sputum.","Correct.","Destruction of the wall with permanent dilation is bronchiectasis. It produces large volumes of purulent sputum, often with hemoptysis and clubbing.","Alveolar wall destruction is emphysema, which affects the airspaces and causes dyspnea with little sputum."],"study":{"topics":["Reid index","Pathology of chronic bronchitis"],"comps":["PU-14-B3","PU-9"]}},{"id":"PU-C07-Q2","stem":"During a later exacerbation she needs noninvasive ventilation for hypercapnia, and enteral feeding is started. Her physician chooses a formula that supplies a larger share of calories as fat than as carbohydrate. Which of the following best explains this choice?","options":["Fat oxidation releases less CO2 for each liter of O2 consumed","Fat oxidation uses less O2 for each kilocalorie produced","Fat oxidation increases red cell 2,3-BPG and oxygen unloading","Carbohydrate oxidation lowers the respiratory quotient toward 0.7"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-11","obj":"PU-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Define the respiratory quotient: CO2 produced divided by O2 consumed.","Recall typical values: carbohydrate 1.0, protein about 0.8, fat about 0.7.","Apply it: less CO2 per unit of O2 means less CO2 that her weak ventilation must remove."],"why":"The respiratory quotient (RQ) is the ratio of CO2 produced to O2 consumed. For glucose, C6H12O6 plus 6 O2 gives 6 CO2, so RQ is 1.0. Fats are more reduced molecules that need more O2 for each carbon, so RQ is about 0.7, and protein is about 0.8. A patient with hypercapnic lung disease cannot easily raise ventilation. A carbohydrate-heavy diet, and especially overfeeding, raises CO2 production and the ventilation needed to clear it. Shifting calories toward fat lowers CO2 production and ventilatory demand.","whyNot":["Correct.","Fat actually uses slightly more O2 per kilocalorie than carbohydrate. The advantage of fat is the lower CO2 output.","2,3-BPG is made from a glycolytic intermediate in red cells. Burning fat does not raise it.","Carbohydrate oxidation has a respiratory quotient of 1.0, the highest of the three fuels. A value near 0.7 reflects fat."],"study":{"topics":["Respiratory quotient","Nutrition in hypercapnic lung disease"],"comps":["PU-11","PU-8"]}},{"id":"PU-C07-Q3","stem":"Echocardiography shows a dilated, hypertrophied right ventricle and an estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 55 mm Hg (normal less than 35). Which of the following best describes the main cause of her pulmonary hypertension and its vascular change?","options":["Organized thromboemboli obstructing the pulmonary arteries","Raised left atrial pressure transmitted back to pulmonary veins","BMPR2 mutation causing plexiform lesions in small arteries","Hypoxic vasoconstriction with medial hypertrophy of arterioles"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-E","obj":"PU-14-E.5","dok":3,"approach":["Place her in a pulmonary hypertension group: she has lung disease with chronic hypoxemia.","Recall the reflex: low alveolar oxygen constricts the pulmonary arterioles that supply it.","Chronic constriction causes muscle thickening in small arteries, raising pressure and overloading the right ventricle (cor pulmonale)."],"why":"Pulmonary hypertension is grouped by cause: pulmonary arterial hypertension (including BMPR2 mutations), left heart disease, lung disease or hypoxia, chronic thromboembolism, and other causes. In chronic bronchitis, poorly ventilated alveoli cause widespread hypoxic vasoconstriction. Over time, smooth muscle in small arteries and arterioles hypertrophies, the intima thickens, and resistance stays high. The right ventricle hypertrophies and then fails, which is cor pulmonale. Her raised hemoglobin, from erythropoietin released in response to hypoxemia, adds blood viscosity and makes it worse.","whyNot":["Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension follows repeated or unresolved emboli. Nothing in her history suggests emboli.","Pulmonary hypertension from left heart disease follows left ventricular failure or mitral valve disease. Her findings point to the right heart and lungs.","BMPR2 mutations cause heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension, with plexiform lesions in small arteries. This is a disease of the vessels themselves, not of airway disease.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of pulmonary hypertension","Cor pulmonale"],"comps":["PU-14-E","PU-5"]}},{"id":"PU-C07-Q4","stem":"Arterial blood gas analysis on room air shows a pH of 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), a PaCO2 of 60 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45), and a bicarbonate concentration of 31 mEq/L (normal 22 to 28). Which of the following best describes her acid-base status?","options":["Chronic respiratory acidosis with expected renal compensation","Metabolic alkalosis with compensatory hypoventilation","Chronic respiratory acidosis plus a superimposed metabolic acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis without renal compensation"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-8","obj":"PU-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["The pH is low and PaCO2 is high, so the primary problem is respiratory acidosis.","Estimate chronic compensation: bicarbonate rises about 3 to 4 mEq/L for each 10 mm Hg rise in PaCO2. A 20 mm Hg rise predicts about 24 + 7 = 31 mEq/L.","Her bicarbonate matches, so there is no second disorder."],"why":"In respiratory acidosis the kidneys compensate by making and retaining more bicarbonate. This takes 3 to 5 days. Acutely, bicarbonate rises only about 1 mEq/L for each 10 mm Hg rise in PaCO2, mostly from buffering. Chronically it rises about 3 to 4 mEq/L for each 10 mm Hg. Her PaCO2 is 20 mm Hg above normal, so a bicarbonate near 31 mEq/L is the expected chronic response, and her pH is close to normal. If the bicarbonate were well above or below the expected value, a mixed disorder would be present.","whyNot":["Correct.","A primary metabolic alkalosis would give an alkaline pH. Her pH is acidic, so the primary problem is respiratory acidosis.","An added metabolic acidosis would hold bicarbonate below the expected value. Her bicarbonate matches the expected chronic rise.","Acutely, bicarbonate rises only about 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in PaCO2, so it would be about 26 mEq/L, and the pH would be much lower, near 7.26."],"study":{"topics":["Compensation in respiratory acidosis","Mixed acid-base disorders"],"comps":["PU-8","PU-14-B3"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C08","system":"PU","dx":"Panacinar emphysema from alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (PiZZ)","codes":["PU-14-B4"],"case":"A 41-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-year history of progressive shortness of breath on exertion. He has never smoked. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. He is thin, with an increased anteroposterior chest diameter. He breathes out through pursed lips. The chest is hyperresonant, and breath sounds are decreased with a prolonged expiratory phase. A chest x-ray shows hyperinflated lungs with flattened diaphragms and increased lucency at both bases. Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin concentration is 18 mg/dL (normal 100 to 200), and genotyping shows the PiZZ genotype. Serum alanine aminotransferase activity is 64 U/L (normal 7 to 40). Diagnosis: panacinar emphysema due to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C08-Q1","stem":"A liver biopsy shows round, PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules in periportal hepatocytes. Which of the following best explains both his liver disease and his lung disease?","options":["Absent gene transcription with no protein made in any tissue","Normal secretion of a protein unable to bind neutrophil elastase","Retention of misfolded protein in the hepatocyte ER","Excess protein secretion that overwhelms hepatic clearance"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-10","obj":"PU-10.2","dok":3,"approach":["Link the globules to their content: misfolded Z protein polymerized in the endoplasmic reticulum of hepatocytes.","Trapped protein injures the liver (gain of toxic function).","Too little protein reaches the blood and lung, so neutrophil elastase acts unopposed (loss of function)."],"why":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin is made in hepatocytes and encoded by SERPINA1. Its alleles are codominant: each allele contributes to the plasma level, so PiMM is normal, PiMZ is intermediate, and PiZZ is about 10 to 15% of normal. The Z mutation (Glu342Lys) makes the protein misfold and polymerize in the endoplasmic reticulum, where it forms PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules that can cause hepatitis and cirrhosis. Because little protein is secreted, the lungs lack their main inhibitor of neutrophil elastase, and panacinar emphysema develops early, especially in smokers.","whyNot":["Null alleles that make no protein cause severe emphysema, but there is nothing to collect in the liver, so the liver is spared.","A secreted but nonfunctional protein could cause emphysema, but it would not build up as globules in hepatocytes.","Correct.","His plasma level is low, not high. The protein is trapped inside hepatocytes and not secreted."],"study":{"topics":["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Codominant inheritance"],"comps":["PU-10","PU-9"]}},{"id":"PU-C08-Q2","stem":"Which of the following patterns of airspace enlargement within the acinus is most likely present in his lungs?","options":["Irregular enlargement of airspaces adjacent to old scars","Uniform enlargement from respiratory bronchioles to alveoli","Enlarged distal acini along pleura and septa forming bullae","Enlarged respiratory bronchioles with sparing of distal alveoli"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-B4","obj":"PU-14-B.5","dok":2,"approach":["Link the cause to the pattern: a body-wide lack of antiprotease affects the whole acinus evenly.","Recall the three patterns: centriacinar (central, upper lobes, smoking), panacinar (whole acinus, lower lobes, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency), and paraseptal (edges of the acinus near pleura).","Match his basal lucency to panacinar emphysema."],"why":"Emphysema is permanent enlargement of airspaces distal to the terminal bronchiole with destruction of their walls. In smokers, damage starts where smoke particles land and inflammatory cells gather, in the respiratory bronchioles of the upper lobes, giving centriacinar emphysema. In alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, the antiprotease shield is low everywhere, so the entire acinus is destroyed evenly. It is usually most severe in the lower lobes. Air trapping produces the barrel chest, and pursed-lip breathing keeps airway pressure up to delay collapse during expiration.","whyNot":["Irregular emphysema occurs next to areas of scarring, such as healed infections. It is usually patchy and does not explain diffuse disease at both bases.","Correct.","Paraseptal emphysema affects the distal acinus next to the pleura and septa. Its subpleural bullae can rupture and cause spontaneous pneumothorax in young adults.","Centriacinar emphysema enlarges the central respiratory bronchioles and spares the distal alveoli. It is the smoking pattern and is worst in the upper lobes."],"study":{"topics":["Types of emphysema","Clinical signs of emphysema"],"comps":["PU-14-B4","PU-14-B"]}},{"id":"PU-C08-Q3","stem":"His FEV1/FVC ratio is 0.48 (normal 0.70 or greater). Which of the following sets of additional findings is most likely?","options":["Raised TLC, normal DLCO, raised PaCO2","Reduced TLC, normal DLCO, raised PaCO2","Reduced TLC, low DLCO, normal PaCO2","Raised TLC, low DLCO, normal PaCO2"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-14-B4","obj":"PU-14-B.6","dok":3,"approach":["The low FEV1/FVC ratio confirms obstruction; loss of elastic recoil and air trapping raise total lung capacity and residual volume.","Destroyed alveolar walls and their capillaries reduce the surface for diffusion, so DLCO falls.","Ventilation and perfusion are lost together, and he breathes harder, so blood gases stay near normal until late."],"why":"COPD is confirmed by an FEV1/FVC ratio below 0.7 after a bronchodilator. In emphysema, elastic recoil is lost, so airways collapse in expiration, air is trapped, and total lung capacity and residual volume rise. Loss of alveolar walls and their capillaries lowers DLCO. Because ventilation and blood flow are lost together, ventilation-perfusion matching is fairly well kept, and the patient works hard to breathe, so PaO2 and PaCO2 stay near normal until late (the pink puffer). In chronic bronchitis, DLCO is normal, but poorly ventilated, well-perfused areas cause hypoxemia and hypercapnia early (the blue bloater).","whyNot":["This is the pattern of predominant chronic bronchitis. The alveolar surface is preserved, so DLCO is normal, but ventilation-perfusion mismatch causes early hypoxemia and hypercapnia.","Low total lung capacity with normal DLCO and hypercapnia fits extrapulmonary restriction, such as neuromuscular weakness.","Low total lung capacity with low DLCO is the pattern of intrinsic restrictive disease, such as fibrosis. His low FEV1/FVC ratio and hyperinflation show obstruction.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary function in COPD","Emphysema versus chronic bronchitis"],"comps":["PU-14-B4","PU-14-B3"]}},{"id":"PU-C08-Q4","stem":"Over the past year he has lost 6 kg although his food intake has not changed. He has no diarrhea, and urinalysis is normal. Which of the following best explains his weight loss?","options":["Urinary protein loss from secondary amyloidosis","Fat malabsorption from reduced hepatic bile salt secretion","More resting energy spent on the work of breathing","Reduced intestinal absorption from bowel wall edema"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-11","obj":"PU-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the clues: weight loss with normal intake and no gut or kidney losses.","Ask where the extra energy goes: his respiratory muscles work against hyperinflated lungs.","Higher resting energy use, unmatched by intake, burns fat and muscle stores."],"why":"In severe emphysema, hyperinflation flattens the diaphragm and shortens its fibers, so each breath needs more effort. The respiratory muscles also work against air trapping and raised airway resistance. Their oxygen and energy use at rest is much higher than normal, which raises resting energy expenditure. If intake does not rise to match, the body burns fat and muscle, producing the thin build typical of predominant emphysema. Loss of muscle mass further weakens the respiratory muscles.","whyNot":["Amyloidosis from chronic inflammation, as in bronchiectasis, causes proteinuria. His urinalysis is normal.","Mild liver injury does not reduce bile salts enough to cause malabsorption, and he has no diarrhea or steatorrhea.","Correct.","Bowel wall edema can occur with right heart failure, but he has no signs of cor pulmonale or right heart failure."],"study":{"topics":["Work of breathing in emphysema","Weight loss in COPD"],"comps":["PU-11","PU-14-B4"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C09","system":"PU","dx":"Acute viral bronchitis in a smoker","codes":["PU-14-C1"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 10-day history of cough. It began with a sore throat and runny nose and was dry at first but now produces small amounts of clear to yellow sputum. She has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 15 years. Temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 98%. Lung examination shows scattered rhonchi that clear with coughing. There are no crackles, dullness to percussion, or egophony. A chest x-ray shows no consolidation. Diagnosis: acute viral bronchitis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C09-Q1","stem":"Her cough lasts a total of 3 weeks and then resolves without treatment. Which of the following best describes the pathologic process in her lungs during the illness?","options":["Transient inflammation of large airway mucosa","Mucous gland hyperplasia of bronchi lasting for years","Lymphocytic infiltrates widening the alveolar septa","Fibrinopurulent exudate filling the alveoli of a lobe"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-C1","obj":"PU-14-C.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the course: a cold followed by cough lasting up to about 3 weeks that clears on its own.","Note the negatives: no crackles, no signs of consolidation, and a clear x-ray, so the alveoli are not involved.","Match this to acute bronchitis, a self-limited inflammation of the trachea and bronchi."],"why":"Acute bronchitis is inflammation of the trachea and large bronchi, caused by viruses in most cases. Infected epithelium becomes swollen and sheds cells, and mucus production rises, producing cough that can last about 3 weeks. Sputum may turn yellow or green from shed cells and leukocyte enzymes, which does not by itself mean bacterial infection. The alveoli are spared, so there are no signs of consolidation. Chronic bronchitis is a different disease, defined by productive cough for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years.","whyNot":["Correct.","Long-standing gland hyperplasia defines chronic bronchitis, with productive cough for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years. Her illness is new and short.","Interstitial inflammation of the septa is the pattern of atypical (viral or mycoplasma) pneumonia. It involves the alveolar walls and often shows on x-ray.","Exudate filling alveoli is pneumonia. It causes crackles, dullness, egophony, and consolidation on x-ray, none of which she has."],"study":{"topics":["Acute bronchitis","Bronchitis versus pneumonia"],"comps":["PU-14-C1","PU-14-B3"]}},{"id":"PU-C09-Q2","stem":"The virus infects the epithelium of her trachea and bronchi. Which of the following linings would most likely be seen in a biopsy of the last purely conducting airway, just proximal to the first airway with alveoli opening from its wall?","options":["Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium with ciliated cells and club cells","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with many goblet cells","Thin squamous type I cells with scattered cuboidal type II cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-2","obj":"PU-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the airway: the last purely conducting airway before the respiratory zone is the terminal bronchiole.","Trace the airway lining from the trachea downward: pseudostratified columnar, then simple columnar, then simple cuboidal.","At the terminal bronchioles, the lining is simple cuboidal with some ciliated cells and many club cells, and goblet cells are absent."],"why":"The trachea and bronchi are lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells, which makes and moves mucus. In the bronchioles the epithelium becomes simple columnar and then simple cuboidal, and goblet cells give way to club cells. Club cells secrete protective proteins, detoxify inhaled chemicals with cytochrome P450 enzymes, and serve as progenitor cells. Beyond the terminal bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles lead to alveoli lined by type I pneumocytes for gas exchange and type II pneumocytes that make surfactant.","whyNot":["Stratified squamous epithelium lines the oropharynx and the true vocal folds, where friction is high.","Correct.","This lines the trachea and bronchi, the airways infected in acute bronchitis. Goblet cells become scarce in the bronchioles.","Type I and type II pneumocytes line the alveoli, which lie beyond the terminal bronchioles in the respiratory zone."],"study":{"topics":["Airway epithelium from trachea to alveoli","Club cells"],"comps":["PU-2","PU-5"]}},{"id":"PU-C09-Q3","stem":"She asks how continued smoking could lead to emphysema. Which of the following best explains how cigarette smoke shifts the lung toward destruction of alveolar walls?","options":["Carbon monoxide inhibition of elastin synthesis by fibroblasts","Nicotine-driven collagen deposition by alveolar fibroblasts","Oxidants inactivating antitrypsin as neutrophils release elastase","Smoke-induced SERPINA1 mutations that block antitrypsin secretion from liver"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-9","obj":"PU-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the normal balance: neutrophil elastase can digest elastin, and alpha-1 antitrypsin inhibits it.","Smoke adds protease: it recruits neutrophils and macrophages that release elastase and other enzymes.","Smoke removes antiprotease: its oxidants inactivate alpha-1 antitrypsin, so elastase acts unopposed."],"why":"Healthy lung tissue is protected by a balance between proteases, such as neutrophil elastase and macrophage metalloproteinases, and antiproteases, mainly alpha-1 antitrypsin. Cigarette smoke attracts neutrophils and activates macrophages, which raises protease activity. Oxidants in smoke and from activated inflammatory cells oxidize a methionine residue in the active site of alpha-1 antitrypsin, so it can no longer inhibit elastase. With protease activity unchecked, elastin in the alveolar walls is digested, and emphysema develops.","whyNot":["Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin and impairs oxygen delivery. It does not block elastin synthesis.","Collagen deposition causes fibrosis and stiff lungs. Emphysema results from breakdown of elastin, not from excess collagen.","Correct.","Smoke does not mutate the alpha-1 antitrypsin gene in the liver. Inherited mutations cause the deficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Protease and antiprotease balance","Pathogenesis of emphysema"],"comps":["PU-9","PU-14-B4"]}},{"id":"PU-C09-Q4","stem":"Which of the following effects of her smoking most increases her risk of developing bacterial pneumonia after this viral illness?","options":["Deficiency of C1 esterase inhibitor in the airway lining fluid","Impaired ciliary clearance and alveolar macrophage function","Depletion of CD4+ T cells in bronchus-associated lymphoid tissue","Reduced IgE class switching by B cells in bronchial mucosa"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-12","obj":"PU-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["List the innate lung defenses: nasal filtering, the mucociliary escalator, cough, secretory IgA, surfactant proteins A and D, and alveolar macrophages.","Ask which ones smoking damages: it slows ciliary beating, causes squamous metaplasia, and impairs macrophage killing.","Add the viral injury: damaged epithelium lets bacteria stick and multiply, so the two insults add up."],"why":"Most inhaled bacteria never cause infection because innate defenses clear them. Mucus traps particles, and cilia carry it up and out. Secretory IgA blocks attachment, surfactant proteins A and D coat microbes for uptake, and alveolar macrophages ingest and kill what reaches the alveoli. Smoking slows ciliary beating, replaces ciliated cells with squamous cells, and impairs macrophage killing. A viral infection strips ciliated cells further, so a smoker recovering from viral bronchitis is at high risk for bacterial pneumonia. Alcohol, which blunts cough and impairs neutrophils, and inherited ciliary defects raise the risk in similar ways.","whyNot":["C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency causes hereditary angioedema, with swelling episodes rather than pneumonia, and smoking does not cause it.","Correct.","Smoking does not deplete CD4+ T cells. Profound CD4+ loss occurs in HIV infection.","IgE drives allergic reactions and defense against helminths. Less IgE would not raise the risk of bacterial pneumonia."],"study":{"topics":["Innate defenses of the lung","Effects of smoking on lung defenses"],"comps":["PU-12","PU-2"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C10","system":"PU","dx":"Acute epiglottitis from Haemophilus influenzae type b in an unvaccinated child","codes":["PU-14-C2"],"case":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of a 6-hour history of high fever, severe sore throat, and trouble swallowing. He has not received any childhood vaccinations. He is sitting upright, leaning forward with his neck extended, and drooling. His voice is muffled, and he has no cough. Temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), pulse is 150/min, respirations are 34/min, and blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. Soft inspiratory stridor is heard. A lateral neck x-ray shows a swollen epiglottis (thumb sign). Blood cultures are obtained. Diagnosis: acute epiglottitis due to Haemophilus influenzae type b.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C10-Q1","stem":"The physician does not examine his throat with a tongue blade and keeps him sitting upright with a parent while the airway team is called. Which of the following complications is this approach mainly intended to prevent?","options":["Rupture of a peritonsillar abscess into the airway","Extension of infection into the cavernous sinus","Aspiration of a pseudomembrane into the trachea","Complete obstruction of the laryngeal inlet"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-C2","obj":"PU-14-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize epiglottitis: sudden fever, drooling, trouble swallowing, muffled voice, tripod posture, and the thumb sign.","Note the anatomy: the swollen epiglottis sits over the laryngeal inlet.","Predict the danger: crying, lying down, or a tongue blade can make the epiglottis close the airway completely."],"why":"Epiglottitis is a rapidly progressive cellulitis of the epiglottis and nearby supraglottic tissues. In unvaccinated children, Haemophilus influenzae type b is the classic cause. The swollen epiglottis can fall back over the laryngeal inlet, especially when the child lies flat, cries, or has the throat examined, causing sudden complete obstruction. Children lean forward with the neck extended to keep the airway open and drool because swallowing hurts. The airway is secured in a controlled setting before any other procedure.","whyNot":["Peritonsillar abscess causes trismus and deviation of the uvula. His swelling is in the epiglottis, not beside the tonsil.","Cavernous sinus thrombosis is a complication of sinus or facial infections. It is not prevented by keeping a child calm and upright.","A gray pseudomembrane that can come loose and block the airway is a feature of diphtheria, not epiglottitis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Epiglottitis","Upper airway obstruction in children"],"comps":["PU-14-C2","PU-3"]}},{"id":"PU-C10-Q2","stem":"Before fiberoptic intubation, a topical anesthetic is applied to the laryngeal mucosa above the vocal folds to blunt the cough reflex. Which of the following nerves carries the sensory impulses blocked by this anesthetic?","options":["External laryngeal nerve","Recurrent laryngeal nerve","Internal laryngeal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-3","obj":"PU-3.6","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the target: laryngeal mucosa above the vocal folds.","Recall the division: above the folds, sensation is from the internal laryngeal nerve; below, from the recurrent laryngeal nerve.","The internal laryngeal nerve is the sensory branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, from the vagus."],"why":"The superior laryngeal nerve, a branch of the vagus, divides into the internal laryngeal nerve, which pierces the thyrohyoid membrane and carries sensation from the laryngeal mucosa above the vocal folds, and the external laryngeal nerve, which supplies the cricothyroid. The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies all other intrinsic muscles and the mucosa below the folds. Sensation above the folds triggers the cough that protects the airway. If it is lost, from anesthesia or nerve injury, food and liquid can enter the larynx without a cough, leading to aspiration.","whyNot":["The external laryngeal nerve is motor to the cricothyroid muscle, which tenses the vocal folds. It carries no mucosal sensation.","The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies sensation below the vocal folds and all other intrinsic laryngeal muscles.","Correct.","The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies sensation to the oropharynx and the posterior third of the tongue, and carries the afferent limb of the gag reflex. It does not supply the laryngeal mucosa."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of the larynx","Internal laryngeal nerve and aspiration"],"comps":["PU-3","PU-4"]}},{"id":"PU-C10-Q3","stem":"His blood culture grows small gram-negative coccobacilli. Which of the following laboratory features would identify this organism?","options":["Growth only with both hemin (factor X) and NAD (factor V)","Large mucoid polysaccharide capsule and lactose fermentation","Growth on charcoal yeast extract agar with cysteine and iron","Oxidase positive with blue-green pigment production"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.2","dok":2,"approach":["Name the organism from the case: Haemophilus influenzae type b.","Recall its growth needs: factor X (hemin) and factor V (NAD).","Apply them in the lab: it grows on chocolate agar, or on blood agar only next to a Staphylococcus aureus streak (satellite growth)."],"why":"Haemophilus influenzae is a small gram-negative coccobacillus that needs both hemin (factor X) and NAD (factor V). Chocolate agar, made by heating blood to lyse red cells, releases both. On regular blood agar it grows only around colonies of Staphylococcus aureus, which lyse red cells and release NAD. Type b has a polyribosyl ribitol phosphate capsule that resists phagocytosis and causes invasive disease such as epiglottitis and meningitis. The conjugate vaccine against this capsule has made Hib epiglottitis rare.","whyNot":["Correct.","A thick mucoid capsule and lactose fermentation describe Klebsiella pneumoniae, which causes lobar pneumonia in people with alcohol use disorder.","Buffered charcoal yeast extract agar with cysteine and iron is used to grow Legionella, which spreads from contaminated water and causes pneumonia, not epiglottitis.","These features describe Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a cause of pneumonia in people with cystic fibrosis or on ventilators."],"study":{"topics":["Haemophilus influenzae","Gram-negative respiratory pathogens"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-12"]}},{"id":"PU-C10-Q4","stem":"His narrowed laryngeal inlet raised his airway resistance. In a normal lower respiratory tract, which of the following airway segments contributes the most to total airway resistance?","options":["Terminal bronchioles","Alveolar ducts","Respiratory bronchioles","Medium-sized bronchi"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-5","obj":"PU-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["Remember that airways branch into many parallel tubes, and parallel resistances combine to a lower total.","Small airways are individually narrow but so many that their combined resistance is low.","Medium-sized bronchi are still few in number and carry fast, often turbulent flow, so they contribute the most."],"why":"The conducting zone runs from the trachea to the terminal bronchioles and holds about 150 mL of air that takes no part in gas exchange (anatomic dead space). The respiratory zone, from respiratory bronchioles to alveoli, is where gas exchange occurs. Although single small airways are narrow, the total cross-sectional area grows rapidly with each branching, so the combined resistance of small airways is low. Most resistance is in the medium-sized bronchi, where airways are still relatively few and flow is fast and partly turbulent. This is why early small airway disease can be present before spirometry changes much.","whyNot":["Each terminal bronchiole is narrow, but there are thousands in parallel, so their combined resistance is low.","Alveolar ducts are part of the respiratory zone. Their combined cross-sectional area is huge, so gas moves mainly by diffusion and resistance is negligible.","Respiratory bronchioles begin the respiratory zone. Their total cross-sectional area is very large, so they add little resistance.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Conducting and respiratory zones","Distribution of airway resistance"],"comps":["PU-5","PU-6"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C11","system":"PU","dx":"Croup (parainfluenza laryngotracheobronchitis)","codes":["PU-14-C3"],"case":"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department at 2 AM because of a harsh, barking cough and noisy breathing that began a few hours earlier. For 2 days he has had rhinorrhea and a low-grade fever. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 140/min, respirations are 40/min, and blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 95%. He is alert, sits comfortably on his mother's lap, and is not drooling. His voice is hoarse. Inspiratory stridor is heard at rest, with mild suprasternal retractions. An anteroposterior neck radiograph shows tapering narrowing of the subglottic trachea. Diagnosis: croup (viral laryngotracheobronchitis), most likely caused by parainfluenza virus.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C11-Q1","stem":"He improves with treatment and is discharged. Four days later he returns with a temperature of 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), a toxic appearance, and worsening stridor that does not improve with nebulized epinephrine. He is not drooling. Laryngoscopy shows a normal epiglottis and thick purulent secretions in the trachea below the vocal folds. Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible for this new condition?","options":["Haemophilus influenzae type b","Respiratory syncytial virus","Staphylococcus aureus","Bordetella pertussis"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-C3","obj":"PU-14-C.3","dok":3,"approach":["High fever, a toxic look, and stridor that does not respond to epinephrine after a viral croup illness point away from simple croup.","A normal epiglottis rules out epiglottitis, and purulent secretions in the subglottic trachea define bacterial tracheitis.","Bacterial tracheitis is usually a secondary infection, most often by Staphylococcus aureus."],"why":"Bacterial tracheitis is a bacterial superinfection of the trachea, often after a viral illness such as croup. The child becomes toxic, with high fever and thick pus in the trachea that can block the airway. Stridor does not improve with nebulized epinephrine, because the problem is pus and sloughed mucosa rather than mucosal edema alone. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause. Simple croup, by contrast, causes a barking cough and stridor in a child who does not look toxic, and acute laryngitis in older children and adults causes hoarseness without stridor.","whyNot":["Haemophilus influenzae type b causes epiglottitis, with drooling, tripod posture, and a swollen cherry-red epiglottis. This child's epiglottis is normal.","Respiratory syncytial virus causes bronchiolitis in infants, with wheezing and crackles, and does not cause toxic illness with purulent tracheal secretions.","Correct.","Bordetella pertussis causes paroxysmal coughing with an inspiratory whoop and lymphocytosis, not acute toxic stridor with tracheal pus."],"study":{"topics":["Croup versus bacterial tracheitis versus epiglottitis","Causes of stridor in children"],"comps":["PU-14-C3","PU-14-C2"]}},{"id":"PU-C11-Q2","stem":"Inspiratory stridor similar to this child's can occur in an adult after total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy shows both vocal folds fixed near the midline. Injury to which of the following nerves most likely explains these findings?","options":["Recurrent laryngeal nerves","External branches of the superior laryngeal nerves","Internal branches of the superior laryngeal nerves","Glossopharyngeal nerves"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-3","obj":"PU-3.5","dok":3,"approach":["Vocal folds stuck near the midline means the muscle that opens the glottis is not working.","The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only muscle that abducts the vocal folds.","It is supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve, so bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury causes stridor."],"why":"The posterior cricoarytenoid muscle rotates the arytenoid cartilages so the vocal folds swing apart, and it is the only abductor of the folds. Like all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid, it is supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Injury to one recurrent nerve causes hoarseness. Injury to both leaves the folds near the midline, so the airway is narrow and inspiratory stridor results. The left nerve loops under the aortic arch, so it can also be injured by aortic arch aneurysm or tumors in the aortopulmonary window.","whyNot":["Correct.","The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid, which tenses the vocal folds. Injury causes a weak, low-pitched voice that tires easily, not stridor.","The internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve carries sensation from the larynx above the vocal folds. Injury blunts the cough reflex and raises aspiration risk but does not fix the folds near the midline.","The glossopharyngeal nerve carries sensation from the oropharynx and supplies the stylopharyngeus. It has no motor role in the larynx."],"study":{"topics":["Intrinsic laryngeal muscles and their nerves","Recurrent laryngeal nerve course"],"comps":["PU-3","PU-14-F4"]}},{"id":"PU-C11-Q3","stem":"A nasal swab PCR assay detects parainfluenza virus type 1. This virus shares which of the following features with respiratory syncytial virus?","options":["Segmented genome that allows reassortment","Positive-sense genome that acts as mRNA","Spike protein that binds ACE2","Fusion protein that forms syncytia"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.5","dok":2,"approach":["Parainfluenza virus is a paramyxovirus, and RSV is a pneumovirus that was formerly grouped with the paramyxoviruses.","Both are enveloped viruses with a nonsegmented, negative-sense RNA genome.","Both carry an F (fusion) protein that fuses infected cells into multinucleated syncytia."],"why":"Parainfluenza virus and respiratory syncytial virus both have a nonsegmented, negative-sense RNA genome inside an envelope, and both use an F protein to fuse the viral envelope with the host cell membrane. F protein on infected cells also fuses neighboring cells into giant multinucleated syncytia, which gives RSV its name. Influenza differs by having a segmented genome with separate hemagglutinin and neuraminidase, and coronaviruses differ by having a positive-sense RNA genome.","whyNot":["A segmented genome that can reassort belongs to influenza virus and allows antigenic shift. Parainfluenza and RSV genomes are not segmented.","A positive-sense RNA genome that can be translated directly belongs to coronaviruses and rhinoviruses. Parainfluenza and RSV carry negative-sense RNA and bring their own RNA polymerase.","Binding of the spike protein to ACE2 is the entry step of SARS-CoV-2, a coronavirus.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Paramyxoviruses and pneumoviruses","Influenza versus RSV versus coronavirus"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H8"]}},{"id":"PU-C11-Q4","stem":"As his work of breathing increases, he begins to contract his abdominal muscles forcefully during each expiration. Which of the following groups of brainstem neurons most directly drives this active expiration?","options":["Ventral respiratory group","Dorsal respiratory group","Pontine apneustic center","Pontine pneumotaxic center"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-6","obj":"PU-6.5","dok":2,"approach":["Quiet expiration is passive and needs no expiratory muscles.","When ventilatory demand rises, expiratory muscles such as the abdominal wall muscles are recruited.","The ventral respiratory group in the medulla contains the expiratory neurons that drive forced breathing."],"why":"In quiet breathing, the dorsal respiratory group in the medulla drives the inspiratory muscles, and expiration is passive recoil of the lungs and chest wall. When demand rises, as in this child with airway obstruction, the ventral respiratory group becomes active. It contains both inspiratory and expiratory neurons and drives accessory inspiratory muscles and the abdominal and internal intercostal muscles for forced expiration. The pontine centers fine-tune the timing of breaths rather than drive the expiratory muscles.","whyNot":["Correct.","The dorsal respiratory group, in the nucleus of the solitary tract, mainly drives inspiration and receives input from vagal and glossopharyngeal afferents. It is the main driver of quiet breathing.","The apneustic center in the lower pons promotes prolonged inspiration. Damage below the pneumotaxic center with vagal loss gives apneustic breathing.","The pneumotaxic center in the upper pons limits the length of inspiration and so raises respiratory rate. It does not drive the expiratory muscles."],"study":{"topics":["Medullary respiratory groups","Pontine respiratory centers and Hering-Breuer reflex"],"comps":["PU-6","PU-5"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C12","system":"PU","dx":"Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease with nasal polyps and allergic rhinitis","codes":["PU-14-F7","PU-14-C4","PU-14-B1"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman comes to the physician because of constant nasal congestion and loss of her sense of smell for 1 year. She developed asthma 3 years ago. On two occasions she had wheezing, worse nasal congestion, and facial flushing within 1 hour of taking ibuprofen. Each spring she has sneezing and itchy, watery eyes. Vital signs are within normal limits. The nasal mucosa is pale and boggy, and gray, glistening, soft masses fill both middle meatuses. Scattered expiratory wheezes are heard over both lungs. Blood eosinophil count is 850/μL (normal under 500). Diagnosis: aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease with bilateral nasal polyps and asthma, together with seasonal allergic rhinitis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C12-Q1","stem":"A 7-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of chronic nasal obstruction. He has a chronic productive cough and poor weight gain with bulky, foul-smelling stools. Physical examination shows bilateral nasal polyps. Which of the following is the most likely underlying disorder?","options":["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Celiac disease","Kartagener syndrome","Cystic fibrosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-F7","obj":"PU-14-F.7","dok":2,"approach":["In adults, nasal polyps usually come with chronic rhinosinusitis, allergy, or aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease.","In a child, nasal polyps should prompt testing for cystic fibrosis.","Chronic productive cough plus fat malabsorption from pancreatic insufficiency completes the picture of cystic fibrosis."],"why":"Nasal polyps are edematous outgrowths of inflamed sinonasal mucosa. In adults they come with chronic rhinosinusitis, allergy, and aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, and they cause nasal obstruction and loss of smell. In children they are unusual enough that cystic fibrosis should be suspected. Thick, dehydrated secretions block the sinuses and airways, and pancreatic duct blockage causes fat malabsorption with bulky, foul-smelling stools and poor weight gain.","whyNot":["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema in adults and liver disease. It does not cause nasal polyps or pancreatic insufficiency.","Celiac disease causes malabsorption and poor growth but does not cause nasal polyps or a chronic productive cough.","Kartagener syndrome causes chronic sinusitis and bronchiectasis from immotile cilia, often with situs inversus, but the pancreas works normally, so fat malabsorption does not fit.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of nasal polyps","Cystic fibrosis clinical features"],"comps":["PU-14-F7","PU-14-G1"]}},{"id":"PU-C12-Q2","stem":"In November her husband develops sneezing, a scratchy throat, and clear rhinorrhea without fever. His nasal mucosa is red and swollen rather than pale. Which of the following properties of the most likely causative virus explains why it infects the nose but does not cause intestinal infection?","options":["Lipid envelope disrupted by bile","Segmented double-stranded RNA genome","Inactivation by gastric acid","DNA genome replicated in the nucleus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-C4","obj":"PU-14-C.4","dok":2,"approach":["A common cold with red, swollen mucosa is infectious rhinitis, while her pale, boggy mucosa with eosinophilia reflects allergic rhinitis.","Rhinovirus is the main cause of the common cold.","Rhinovirus is a picornavirus that, unlike the enteroviruses in the same family, is destroyed by stomach acid."],"why":"Rhinovirus causes most common colds. It is a small, nonenveloped, positive-sense RNA picornavirus that binds ICAM-1 on nasal epithelium. Unlike enteroviruses such as poliovirus, it is acid labile, so swallowed virus is destroyed in the stomach and it does not infect the gut. Infectious rhinitis gives red, swollen mucosa with neutrophils, while allergic rhinitis gives pale, boggy mucosa with IgE-driven mast cell release and eosinophils. Vasomotor rhinitis has neither infection nor allergy and is triggered by things such as cold air or odors.","whyNot":["Rhinovirus has no envelope. Enveloped viruses are generally destroyed by bile, but that is not the reason rhinovirus spares the gut.","A segmented double-stranded RNA genome belongs to rotavirus, which infects the gut and causes diarrhea in young children.","Correct.","Rhinovirus has an RNA genome that is replicated in the cytoplasm. DNA viruses such as adenovirus can cause both colds and gastrointestinal infection."],"study":{"topics":["Rhinovirus properties","Allergic versus infectious versus vasomotor rhinitis"],"comps":["PU-14-C4","PU-13"]}},{"id":"PU-C12-Q3","stem":"CT of the sinuses confirms that polyps fill both middle meatuses. Drainage from which of the following is most likely to be obstructed?","options":["Maxillary sinus","Sphenoid sinus","Nasolacrimal duct","Posterior ethmoidal cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-3","obj":"PU-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["The polyps sit in the middle meatus, below the middle concha.","The frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoid sinuses drain into the middle meatus.","Among the options, only the maxillary sinus drains there."],"why":"The middle meatus receives the frontal sinus, the maxillary sinus through the semilunar hiatus, and the anterior ethmoidal cells. Polyps in this region therefore block these sinuses and cause chronic rhinosinusitis. The posterior ethmoidal cells drain into the superior meatus, the sphenoid sinus into the sphenoethmoidal recess, and the nasolacrimal duct into the inferior meatus.","whyNot":["Correct.","The sphenoid sinus drains into the sphenoethmoidal recess above the superior concha.","The nasolacrimal duct opens into the inferior meatus, below the inferior concha. Blockage there causes tearing (epiphora).","The posterior ethmoidal cells drain into the superior meatus."],"study":{"topics":["Paranasal sinus drainage sites","Nasal conchae and meatuses"],"comps":["PU-3","PU-14-C5"]}},{"id":"PU-C12-Q4","stem":"Her nasal and bronchial secretions are thick and tenacious. The main gel-forming component of this mucus is a family of large glycoproteins secreted by goblet cells and submucosal glands. Most of the carbohydrate chains on these proteins are attached to which of the following?","options":["Amide nitrogen of asparagine residues","Hydroxyl group of hydroxylysine residues","Hydroxyl of serine or threonine","Epsilon-amino group of lysine residues"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-9","obj":"PU-9.6","dok":2,"approach":["The gel-forming glycoproteins of mucus are mucins.","Mucins are heavily O-glycosylated, with dense sugar chains along their protein backbone.","O-linked chains are attached to the hydroxyl group of serine or threonine in the Golgi apparatus."],"why":"Mucins are long glycoproteins in which carbohydrate makes up most of the mass. The sugar chains start with N-acetylgalactosamine attached to the hydroxyl group of serine or threonine, an O-linkage added in the Golgi apparatus. These chains hold water and give mucus its gel properties. When goblet cells and submucosal glands enlarge, as in chronic bronchitis and chronic airway inflammation, more mucus is made. In cystic fibrosis the mucus is dehydrated, so it is too thick to clear.","whyNot":["N-linked glycosylation attaches sugars to the amide nitrogen of asparagine and begins in the rough endoplasmic reticulum. It is common in membrane and secreted proteins but is not the main linkage in mucins.","Sugars attached to hydroxylysine are a feature of collagen, not of mucins.","Correct.","Lysine amino groups are sites of nonenzymatic glycation and of other modifications, such as ubiquitination, not of mucin carbohydrate chains."],"study":{"topics":["Mucin structure and O-linked glycosylation","Airway mucus in chronic bronchitis and cystic fibrosis"],"comps":["PU-9","PU-14-B3"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C13","system":"PU","dx":"Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis of odontogenic origin","codes":["PU-14-C5"],"case":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of 10 days of left cheek pain and pressure with foul-smelling, purulent discharge from the left nostril. A tooth in his left upper jaw had been painful for several weeks before the facial symptoms began. Temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. There is tenderness over the left maxilla, and pus is seen draining from the left middle meatus. A carious upper left tooth is tender to percussion. CT shows an air-fluid level in the left maxillary sinus and a periapical lucency at the root of the diseased tooth, which projects into the sinus floor. Diagnosis: acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis of odontogenic origin.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C13-Q1","stem":"Despite antibiotic treatment, 5 days later he has a temperature of 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), severe headache, and swelling of both eyelids. Both eyes are proptotic, he cannot abduct either eye, and sensation over his forehead is reduced on both sides. Which of the following is the most likely complication?","options":["Orbital cellulitis","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Bacterial meningitis","Rhinocerebral mucormycosis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-C5","obj":"PU-14-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Bilateral eye signs from a one-sided facial infection mean the infection has reached a midline structure that connects both sides.","Loss of abduction (cranial nerve VI) and reduced forehead sensation (V1) point to structures in or beside the cavernous sinus.","Infection reaches the cavernous sinus through valveless facial and ophthalmic veins, and the two cavernous sinuses connect across the midline."],"why":"Cavernous sinus thrombosis is a septic clot in the cavernous sinus that follows infection of the face, sinuses, or teeth. Valveless facial and ophthalmic veins carry infection back to the sinus. Cranial nerves III, IV, V1, V2, and VI run through or beside the sinus, and VI is often affected first because it lies free in the sinus. Blocked orbital venous drainage causes proptosis and eyelid swelling, and spread through intercavernous connections makes the signs bilateral. Other sinusitis complications include orbital cellulitis, meningitis, brain abscess, and mucormycosis in diabetic ketoacidosis.","whyNot":["Orbital cellulitis causes proptosis and painful eye movements on the side of the infected sinus, usually the ethmoid sinus. It does not explain signs in both eyes or loss of forehead sensation.","Correct.","Bacterial meningitis causes fever, headache, and neck stiffness, but not proptosis or eyelid swelling.","Rhinocerebral mucormycosis occurs mainly in diabetic ketoacidosis or severe immunosuppression and shows black necrotic tissue in the nose or palate. This patient has neither risk factor."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of sinusitis","Cavernous sinus contents"],"comps":["PU-14-C5","PU-3"]}},{"id":"PU-C13-Q2","stem":"Which of the following teeth is most likely to be the source of his infection?","options":["Maxillary canine","Maxillary central incisor","Mandibular first molar","Maxillary first molar"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-3","obj":"PU-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Odontogenic sinusitis needs a tooth whose roots sit just under the floor of the maxillary sinus.","The roots of the maxillary molars, especially the first and second molars, lie closest to the sinus floor and may project into it.","Choose the maxillary first molar."],"why":"The floor of the maxillary sinus sits over the roots of the upper molars and premolars. The roots of the first and second molars are closest and may project into the sinus with only a thin bony layer or mucosa between them. A periapical infection or an extraction of these teeth can therefore spread into the sinus. The maxillary sinus is also the sinus most often infected overall, because its ostium opens high on its medial wall, so gravity does not help it drain.","whyNot":["The maxillary canine root lies in front of the maxillary sinus. Its infections usually spread into the soft tissues of the face (canine space) rather than through the sinus floor.","The roots of the maxillary central incisor lie under the floor of the nasal cavity, far in front of the maxillary sinus.","Mandibular molar infections spread into the submandibular and sublingual spaces, as in Ludwig angina, not into the maxillary sinus.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Maxillary sinus anatomy and drainage","Odontogenic sinusitis"],"comps":["PU-3","PU-14-C5"]}},{"id":"PU-C13-Q3","stem":"Culture of a sinus aspirate grows anaerobes and alpha-hemolytic, gram-positive cocci. Which of the following test results would show that the cocci are the species that most often causes community-acquired pneumonia, rather than oral viridans streptococci?","options":["Lysis by bile salts","Growth in 6.5% sodium chloride","Resistance to optochin","Susceptibility to bacitracin"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.1","dok":2,"approach":["The most common cause of community-acquired pneumonia is Streptococcus pneumoniae.","Both S. pneumoniae and viridans streptococci are alpha-hemolytic, so a further test is needed to tell them apart.","S. pneumoniae is bile soluble and optochin sensitive, while viridans streptococci are bile insoluble and optochin resistant."],"why":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is a lancet-shaped, gram-positive diplococcus that is alpha-hemolytic. Its autolysin is activated by bile salts, so colonies dissolve in bile, and it is inhibited by optochin. Viridans streptococci from the mouth are also alpha-hemolytic but resist both bile and optochin. S. pneumoniae is the most common cause of community-acquired and lobar pneumonia and a leading cause of bacterial sinusitis and otitis media. Its polysaccharide capsule is the main virulence factor, and it also makes IgA protease.","whyNot":["Correct.","Growth in 6.5% sodium chloride identifies Enterococcus, which is usually gamma-hemolytic and rarely causes community-acquired pneumonia.","Resistance to optochin is the result expected for viridans streptococci. S. pneumoniae is optochin sensitive.","Bacitracin susceptibility identifies Streptococcus pyogenes, which is beta-hemolytic and causes pharyngitis."],"study":{"topics":["Streptococcus pneumoniae identification","Alpha-hemolytic streptococci"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H5"]}},{"id":"PU-C13-Q4","stem":"Four months after dental treatment, his sinus symptoms persist. He now has crusting nasal ulcers, a cough with blood-streaked sputum, and red blood cell casts in his urine. Chest CT shows multiple cavitating nodules in both lungs. Which of the following serologic findings is most likely?","options":["Anti-glomerular basement membrane antibodies","Myeloperoxidase ANCA","Anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies","Proteinase 3 ANCA"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-12","obj":"PU-12.5","dok":3,"approach":["Persistent sinus disease with nasal ulcers, cavitating lung nodules, and glomerulonephritis is the triad of granulomatosis with polyangiitis.","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis is a necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis strongly linked to ANCA against proteinase 3.","Goodpasture syndrome also causes lung bleeding and glomerulonephritis but spares the upper airway and does not form cavitating nodules."],"why":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis causes necrotizing granulomas and vasculitis in the upper airway, lungs, and kidneys. Chronic sinusitis that does not resolve, nasal ulcers, and saddle nose deformity are typical upper airway signs. Lung nodules often cavitate, and the kidney lesion is a crescentic glomerulonephritis with red cell casts. Most patients have ANCA against proteinase 3, which gives a cytoplasmic (c-ANCA) staining pattern. Goodpasture syndrome, by contrast, is a type II hypersensitivity against the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes.","whyNot":["Anti-GBM antibodies against type IV collagen cause Goodpasture syndrome, with pulmonary hemorrhage and glomerulonephritis but no sinus disease or cavitating granulomas.","MPO-ANCA is typical of microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Microscopic polyangiitis lacks granulomas and upper airway disease.","Anti-dsDNA antibodies are specific for systemic lupus erythematosus, which can cause nephritis but not necrotizing granulomas of the sinuses and lungs.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Goodpasture syndrome"],"comps":["PU-12","CV-18-I"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C14","system":"PU","dx":"Recurrent streptococcal tonsillopharyngitis with tonsillar hypertrophy and obstructive sleep apnea","codes":["PU-14-C6","PU-14-C7"],"case":"A 6-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of 2 days of sore throat and fever. This is her seventh episode of sore throat in the past year. She has no cough or rhinorrhea. Her parents report loud snoring every night, pauses in her breathing followed by gasps, restless sleep, and poor attention at school. Temperature is 39.0 °C (102.2 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 98/62 mm Hg. Both palatine tonsils are red, covered with exudate, and so enlarged that they nearly touch in the midline. Tender anterior cervical lymph nodes are palpable. A rapid antigen test for group A streptococcus is positive. Diagnosis: recurrent streptococcal tonsillopharyngitis with tonsillar hypertrophy and obstructive sleep apnea.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C14-Q1","stem":"Her 9-year-old brother, whose streptococcal pharyngitis last month was not treated, now has migratory polyarthritis and a new apical holosystolic murmur. Which of the following best explains the injury to his heart?","options":["Direct invasion of the valve by streptococci","Antibodies to M protein that cross-react with heart","Immune complexes of streptococcal antigen in the valve","Superantigen activation of T cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-14-C6","obj":"PU-14-C.6","dok":3,"approach":["Arthritis and a new mitral regurgitation murmur weeks after untreated streptococcal pharyngitis is acute rheumatic fever.","Rheumatic fever is an immune reaction, and no bacteria are found in the heart.","Antibodies and T cells made against streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac proteins such as myosin (molecular mimicry)."],"why":"Acute rheumatic fever follows Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis by about 2 to 4 weeks. The M protein of the bacterium resembles proteins in heart muscle and valves, so antibodies and T cells made against it also attack the heart. This is molecular mimicry, a type II hypersensitivity. The mitral valve is affected most often, giving mitral regurgitation acutely and mitral stenosis years later. Treating the pharyngitis with antibiotics prevents rheumatic fever, while poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is an immune complex disease that antibiotics do not reliably prevent.","whyNot":["Direct invasion of the valve describes infective endocarditis, in which blood cultures are positive and vegetations contain bacteria. Rheumatic valves are sterile.","Correct.","Immune complex deposition is the mechanism of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, which causes hematuria, edema, and hypertension, not valve disease.","Superantigens, such as streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins, cause streptococcal toxic shock syndrome and scarlet fever rash, not delayed valvulitis."],"study":{"topics":["Acute rheumatic fever pathogenesis","Streptococcus pyogenes virulence factors"],"comps":["PU-14-C6","CV-18-K"]}},{"id":"PU-C14-Q2","stem":"She does not finish her antibiotic course. Four days later she has worsening throat pain on one side, difficulty opening her mouth, and a muffled voice. The uvula is deviated to the left. Which of the following is the most likely location of the abscess?","options":["Left peritonsillar space","Sublingual and submandibular spaces","Right peritonsillar space","Retropharyngeal space"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-C7","obj":"PU-14-C.7","dok":3,"approach":["One-sided throat pain, trismus, and a muffled voice after tonsillitis point to a peritonsillar abscess.","The abscess pushes the soft palate and uvula toward the opposite side.","A uvula deviated to the left means the abscess is on the right."],"why":"A peritonsillar abscess forms between the tonsillar capsule and the superior constrictor muscle, usually as a complication of tonsillitis. The swelling bulges the soft palate on that side and pushes the uvula away from the abscess. Irritation of the nearby medial pterygoid muscle causes trismus, and the voice sounds muffled. Streptococcal tonsillitis should also be told apart from infectious mononucleosis, which gives exudative tonsillitis with posterior cervical nodes, atypical lymphocytes, a positive heterophile antibody test, and splenomegaly.","whyNot":["A left-sided abscess would push the uvula to the right, away from the swelling.","Infection of the sublingual and submandibular spaces (Ludwig angina) usually comes from a lower molar and causes firm swelling of the floor of the mouth that pushes the tongue up.","Correct.","A retropharyngeal abscess causes neck stiffness, drooling, and bulging of the posterior pharyngeal wall, without uvular deviation. It occurs mostly in young children after nodal infection."],"study":{"topics":["Peritonsillar abscess","Streptococcal tonsillitis versus infectious mononucleosis"],"comps":["PU-14-C7","PU-3"]}},{"id":"PU-C14-Q3","stem":"She also breathes through her mouth during the day, has a hyponasal voice, and has had three episodes of otitis media with effusion this year. Enlargement of which of the following best explains these additional findings?","options":["Pharyngeal tonsil","Lingual tonsil","Jugulodigastric lymph nodes","Palatine tonsils"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-3","obj":"PU-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Hyponasal voice and mouth breathing mean the nasopharynx is blocked.","Recurrent middle ear effusions mean the pharyngotympanic tube opening, also in the nasopharynx, is blocked.","The lymphoid tissue in the roof and back wall of the nasopharynx is the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)."],"why":"Waldeyer ring is a ring of lymphoid tissue around the entrance to the pharynx: the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) in the nasopharynx, the tubal tonsils near the pharyngotympanic tube openings, the palatine tonsils in the oropharynx, and the lingual tonsil on the back of the tongue. Enlarged adenoids block airflow through the nose, so children breathe through the mouth, snore, and sound hyponasal. They also block the pharyngotympanic tube openings, so the middle ear cannot ventilate or drain, which leads to effusion and otitis media.","whyNot":["Correct.","The lingual tonsil lies on the posterior third of the tongue in the oropharynx, far from the pharyngotympanic tubes.","The jugulodigastric node drains the palatine tonsil and enlarges with tonsillitis, but it lies in the neck and cannot block the airway or the pharyngotympanic tube.","The palatine tonsils lie in the oropharynx between the palatine arches. Their enlargement narrows the oropharynx and adds to her sleep apnea, but it does not block the pharyngotympanic tubes or the nasal airway."],"study":{"topics":["Waldeyer ring","Regions of the pharynx"],"comps":["PU-3","PU-14-C7"]}},{"id":"PU-C14-Q4","stem":"Overnight polysomnography shows repeated episodes of absent airflow at the nose and mouth lasting 10 to 20 seconds, with continued and increasing chest and abdominal respiratory movements and falls in oxygen saturation. Which of the following best explains these events?","options":["Diaphragm weakness from phrenic nerve injury","Collapse of the pharyngeal airway during sleep","Daytime alveolar hypoventilation from obesity","Loss of brainstem respiratory drive during sleep"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-6","obj":"PU-6.7","dok":3,"approach":["Absent airflow with continued respiratory effort means the drive to breathe is intact but the airway is blocked.","This is obstructive sleep apnea, in which the pharynx collapses when muscle tone falls during sleep.","In this child, large tonsils and adenoids narrow the pharynx and make collapse more likely."],"why":"In obstructive sleep apnea the pharyngeal airway collapses during sleep, when the dilator muscles relax. The brainstem keeps sending signals, so the chest and abdomen keep moving and effort increases until the patient briefly arouses and the airway reopens. In children, enlarged tonsils and adenoids are the most common cause, and removing them usually cures it. Repeated hypoxemia over years causes hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, which can lead to pulmonary hypertension, and can stimulate erythropoietin, causing secondary polycythemia.","whyNot":["Diaphragm weakness reduces effort, especially when lying down. It does not cause complete cessation of airflow while effort increases.","Correct.","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is defined by daytime PaCO2 above 45 mm Hg with a BMI of 30 or more. It does not cause discrete apneas with rising effort, and this child is not described as obese.","Loss of brainstem drive is central sleep apnea. Airflow stops because chest and abdominal effort also stops."],"study":{"topics":["Obstructive versus central sleep apnea","Consequences of chronic hypoxemia"],"comps":["PU-6","PU-3"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C15","system":"PU","dx":"Chylothorax from mediastinal lymphoma","codes":["PU-14-D1"],"case":"A 27-year-old woman comes to the physician because of shortness of breath that has worsened over 6 weeks. She has had night sweats and a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Breath sounds are absent and percussion is dull over the lower half of the left hemithorax. Chest CT shows a large anterior mediastinal mass and a left pleural effusion. Thoracentesis yields milky white fluid with a triglyceride concentration of 420 mg/dL (chylous if above 110). Biopsy of the mass shows Reed-Sternberg cells among bands of collagen and mixed inflammatory cells. Diagnosis: chylothorax from obstruction of the thoracic duct by nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C15-Q1","stem":"Lipoprotein analysis shows that the particles giving the pleural fluid its milky color are chylomicrons. These particles were assembled in which cells, and which lipid do they mainly carry?","options":["Adipocytes; free fatty acids","Hepatocytes; cholesteryl esters","Hepatocytes; endogenous triglyceride","Enterocytes; dietary triglyceride"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-14-D1","obj":"PU-14-D.1","dok":2,"approach":["Chylous fluid is lymph from the intestine that has leaked out of the thoracic duct.","Intestinal lymph carries chylomicrons, made by enterocytes from absorbed dietary fat.","Chylomicrons are mostly triglyceride, which makes the fluid milky and gives a pleural triglyceride above 110 mg/dL."],"why":"After a fatty meal, enterocytes repackage absorbed fatty acids and monoglycerides into triglyceride and load it onto apolipoprotein B-48 to form chylomicrons. These are too large to enter blood capillaries, so they enter intestinal lacteals and travel through the cisterna chyli and thoracic duct to the left venous angle. When the duct is torn or blocked, as by trauma, surgery, or lymphoma, this lymph leaks into the pleural space. The fluid is milky, has triglycerides above 110 mg/dL, and contains chylomicrons.","whyNot":["Adipocytes release free fatty acids bound to albumin and do not assemble lipoproteins.","Particles rich in cholesteryl esters are LDL, which come from VLDL in the blood. Effusions rich in cholesterol (pseudochylothorax) form in old, long-standing effusions, not from duct obstruction.","Hepatocytes package endogenous triglyceride into VLDL, which enters the blood directly and is not carried in thoracic duct lymph in large amounts.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Chylomicron assembly and transport","Chylothorax fluid features"],"comps":["PU-14-D1","GI-9"]}},{"id":"PU-C15-Q2","stem":"The thoracic duct begins in the abdomen at the cisterna chyli. Which of the following describes where it passes through the diaphragm into the thorax?","options":["Esophageal hiatus, with the vagal trunks","Sternocostal triangle, with the superior epigastric vessels","Aortic hiatus, with the aorta and azygos vein","Caval opening, with the right phrenic nerve"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-4","obj":"PU-4.7","dok":2,"approach":["The thoracic duct runs up behind the aorta and beside the azygos vein.","It leaves the abdomen with the aorta through the aortic hiatus at about T12.","It then rises in the posterior mediastinum, crosses to the left at about T5, and ends at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins."],"why":"The thoracic duct starts at the cisterna chyli near L1 to L2 and passes through the aortic hiatus at about T12 with the aorta and usually the azygos vein. It ascends in the posterior mediastinum between the aorta and azygos vein, crosses from right to left at about the level of T5, and empties into the venous angle where the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins join. Injury below about T5 tends to cause a right chylothorax, and injury above it a left chylothorax. A mediastinal lymphoma can compress or invade the duct anywhere along this path.","whyNot":["The esophageal hiatus at about T10 transmits the esophagus and the anterior and posterior vagal trunks.","The sternocostal triangle (foramen of Morgagni) transmits the superior epigastric vessels and is a site of anterior diaphragmatic hernia.","Correct.","The caval opening at about T8 transmits the inferior vena cava and branches of the right phrenic nerve."],"study":{"topics":["Course of the thoracic duct","Diaphragmatic openings"],"comps":["PU-4","PU-14-D1"]}},{"id":"PU-C15-Q3","stem":"Additional studies show pleural fluid protein 4.0 g/dL, serum protein 6.5 g/dL, pleural fluid LDH 180 U/L, and serum LDH 210 U/L (upper limit of normal 220). Based on these results, the fluid falls into the same category as an effusion caused by which of the following?","options":["Cirrhosis with ascites","Nephrotic syndrome","Bacterial pneumonia","Left ventricular failure"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-D","obj":"PU-14-D.6","dok":3,"approach":["Apply Light criteria: an effusion is an exudate if any one criterion is met.","Protein ratio is 4.0/6.5, about 0.62 (above 0.5). LDH ratio is 180/210, about 0.86 (above 0.6). Pleural LDH is above two thirds of 220, which is about 147.","All three criteria are met, so the fluid is an exudate, like a parapneumonic effusion."],"why":"Light criteria separate exudates, which come from inflammation, infection, or tumor that makes capillaries leaky or blocks lymph flow, from transudates, which come from changes in hydrostatic or oncotic pressure. An effusion is an exudate if the pleural to serum protein ratio is above 0.5, the pleural to serum LDH ratio is above 0.6, or the pleural LDH is above two thirds of the upper limit of normal for serum. This fluid meets all three. Bacterial pneumonia causes an exudative parapneumonic effusion, while heart failure, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome cause transudates.","whyNot":["Cirrhosis causes a transudate, from low albumin and from ascitic fluid moving through small diaphragmatic defects (hepatic hydrothorax).","Nephrotic syndrome causes a transudate because urinary protein loss lowers plasma oncotic pressure.","Correct.","Left ventricular failure raises capillary hydrostatic pressure and causes a transudate with low protein and LDH. It is the most common cause of pleural effusion."],"study":{"topics":["Light criteria","Transudate versus exudate causes"],"comps":["PU-14-D","PU-14-H5"]}},{"id":"PU-C15-Q4","stem":"She is treated with doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine. Four months later she has a dry cough, exertional dyspnea, and fine bibasilar crackles. Pulmonary function tests show a restrictive pattern with a decreased diffusing capacity. Which of the following drugs most likely caused these findings?","options":["Bleomycin","Vinblastine","Dacarbazine","Doxorubicin"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-A","obj":"PU-14-A.9","dok":2,"approach":["Dry cough, crackles, restriction, and a low diffusing capacity after chemotherapy suggest drug-induced pulmonary fibrosis.","Bleomycin is the classic chemotherapy drug that damages the lung.","Lung and skin have little bleomycin hydrolase, the enzyme that inactivates bleomycin."],"why":"Bleomycin creates free radicals that break DNA. Most tissues inactivate it with bleomycin hydrolase, but the lungs and skin have little of this enzyme. Free radical injury damages pneumocytes and endothelium and leads to inflammation and then interstitial fibrosis, giving a restrictive pattern with a low diffusing capacity. Other drugs that cause pulmonary fibrosis include amiodarone, busulfan, and methotrexate. These must be ruled out before diagnosing idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Vinblastine mainly causes bone marrow suppression, while its relative vincristine is known for peripheral neuropathy.","Dacarbazine is an alkylating agent that causes marked nausea and myelosuppression. It is not a classic cause of pulmonary fibrosis.","Doxorubicin is known for dose-dependent dilated cardiomyopathy, not pulmonary fibrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Drug-induced pulmonary fibrosis","Bleomycin toxicity"],"comps":["PU-14-A","PU-14-A2"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C16","system":"PU","dx":"Traumatic hemopneumothorax with flail chest after a motor vehicle collision","codes":["PU-14-D2","PU-14-D5"],"case":"A 45-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 30 minutes after a high-speed motor vehicle collision in which he was the restrained driver. He has severe left chest pain and shortness of breath. Temperature is 36.6 °C (97.9 °F), pulse is 128/min, respirations are 32/min and shallow, and blood pressure is 88/54 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 87%. A segment of the left lateral chest wall moves inward during inspiration and outward during expiration. Breath sounds are decreased on the left. Chest radiograph shows fractures of the left fourth to seventh ribs, each broken in two places, and an air-fluid level in the left pleural space. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5). Diagnosis: left hemopneumothorax with flail chest.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C16-Q1","stem":"A chest tube drains 1,100 mL of blood, and the bleeding stops. CT on day 5 shows about 500 mL of clotted blood that remains in the left pleural space. Without evacuation of this clot, which of the following is the most likely long-term consequence?","options":["Bullous emphysema of the left lung","Malignant pleural mesothelioma","Chylothorax from thoracic duct injury","Fibrothorax with a trapped lung"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-D2","obj":"PU-14-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["Bloody pleural fluid with a hematocrit at least half the blood hematocrit is a hemothorax.","Retained clotted blood in the pleural space organizes, with fibroblasts laying down collagen.","The result is a thick fibrous peel over the visceral pleura that stops the lung from expanding (fibrothorax)."],"why":"A hemothorax is blood in the pleural space, defined by a pleural fluid hematocrit at least half the blood hematocrit. It usually follows chest trauma, and a large one causes hypovolemic shock as well as lung compression. Blood that is drained quickly causes little lasting harm. Retained clot organizes like any hematoma, and the fibrous peel it forms can encase the lung and fuse the pleural layers. This fibrothorax restricts lung expansion and may need surgical decortication. Retained blood can also become infected and form an empyema.","whyNot":["Bullous emphysema results from destruction of alveolar walls, usually from smoking, and is not a consequence of a hemothorax.","Malignant mesothelioma is linked to asbestos exposure decades earlier, not to retained blood.","Chylothorax follows injury to the thoracic duct, which lies in the posterior mediastinum. Lateral rib fractures and blood in the pleural space do not cause it.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hemothorax definition and complications","Fibrothorax"],"comps":["PU-14-D2","PU-14-D"]}},{"id":"PU-C16-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why the injured chest wall segment moves inward during inspiration?","options":["Greater elastic recoil of the injured lung","More negative pleural pressure","Contraction of the internal intercostals","Alveolar pressure rising above atmospheric"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-14-D","obj":"PU-14-D.8","dok":2,"approach":["Ribs broken in two places leave a segment that is no longer attached to the rest of the chest wall.","During inspiration, the diaphragm and intact chest wall expand the thorax, which makes pleural pressure more negative.","The free segment is sucked inward by this pressure instead of moving outward with the rest of the wall."],"why":"Flail chest occurs when three or more adjacent ribs are each broken in two or more places, so a segment floats free of the chest wall. In inspiration, pleural pressure falls below its resting value of about minus 5 cm H2O, and the free segment is pulled in. In expiration, pleural pressure rises and the segment moves out. This paradoxical motion makes breathing less effective and painful. The more important cause of hypoxemia is often the underlying pulmonary contusion, which bleeds into alveoli and worsens over the first day.","whyNot":["Lung elastic recoil pulls the lung inward all the time, but it does not change suddenly at each inspiration to move the segment.","Correct.","The internal intercostal muscles are expiratory muscles used in forced expiration. They do not explain inward movement during inspiration.","Alveolar pressure falls below atmospheric during inspiration so air can flow in. It rises above atmospheric during expiration."],"study":{"topics":["Flail chest and pulmonary contusion","Pleural pressure during the breathing cycle"],"comps":["PU-14-D","PU-6"]}},{"id":"PU-C16-Q3","stem":"He is intubated and started on positive-pressure ventilation before the chest tube is placed. Minutes later his blood pressure falls to 70/40 mm Hg. His neck veins are distended, the trachea is deviated to the right, and the left chest is hyperresonant with absent breath sounds. Which of the following best explains his hypotension?","options":["Loss of blood volume into the left pleural space","Impaired venous return from high intrathoracic pressure","Restricted ventricular filling from pericardial blood","Loss of sympathetic vascular tone from spinal cord injury"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-14-D5","obj":"PU-14-D.5","dok":3,"approach":["Hyperresonance, absent breath sounds, tracheal deviation away from the affected side, and distended neck veins describe a tension pneumothorax.","Positive-pressure ventilation pushes more air through the lung injury into the pleural space, and a one-way valve traps it there.","The rising pressure compresses the great veins and shifts the mediastinum, so venous return and cardiac output fall."],"why":"In a tension pneumothorax, air enters the pleural space with each breath but cannot leave. Pleural pressure rises above atmospheric, the lung collapses, and the mediastinum shifts to the opposite side. The high pressure compresses the venae cavae and the right heart, so venous return falls, neck veins distend, and blood pressure drops. Positive-pressure ventilation can quickly turn a simple traumatic pneumothorax into a tension pneumothorax. Needle decompression or a chest tube is lifesaving. Primary spontaneous pneumothorax occurs in tall, thin young men from ruptured apical blebs, and secondary pneumothorax occurs in lung diseases such as COPD.","whyNot":["Blood loss into the chest causes hypotension with flat neck veins and dullness to percussion, not distended neck veins and hyperresonance.","Correct.","Cardiac tamponade also causes hypotension with distended neck veins, but breath sounds are present and the trachea stays midline.","Neurogenic shock from spinal cord injury causes hypotension with bradycardia and warm skin, and it does not cause tracheal deviation or one-sided hyperresonance."],"study":{"topics":["Tension pneumothorax","Types of pneumothorax"],"comps":["PU-14-D5","CV-5"]}},{"id":"PU-C16-Q4","stem":"To place the chest tube, the physician makes an incision in the left midaxillary line and passes the instrument through the fifth intercostal space just over the upper border of the sixth rib. This technique mainly protects which of the following?","options":["Intercostal vein, artery, and nerve","Long thoracic nerve and lateral thoracic artery","Thoracodorsal nerve and artery","Internal thoracic artery and vein"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-4","obj":"PU-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Each intercostal space has a neurovascular bundle that runs in the costal groove on the lower border of the rib above.","A needle or tube passed just over the upper border of the rib below stays away from this bundle.","The bundle is ordered from top to bottom as vein, artery, and nerve."],"why":"The posterior intercostal vein, artery, and nerve run together in the costal groove along the inferior border of each rib, between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles. Entering the pleural space just above the upper border of a rib avoids injuring them. Chest tubes are placed in the midaxillary line at about the fourth or fifth intercostal space. In the midaxillary line the lower edge of the lung lies at about rib 8 and the pleura at about rib 10, so a needle placed too low can enter the costodiaphragmatic recess or pass through the diaphragm.","whyNot":["Correct.","The long thoracic nerve and lateral thoracic artery run on the surface of the serratus anterior. They can be injured by a cut that is too far posterior, but passing over a rib border does not protect them.","The thoracodorsal nerve and artery run along the posterior axillary wall to the latissimus dorsi, outside the intercostal space.","The internal thoracic vessels run vertically about 1 cm lateral to the sternum, far from a midaxillary approach."],"study":{"topics":["Intercostal neurovascular bundle","Surface projections of the lungs and pleura"],"comps":["PU-4","PU-14-D2"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C17","system":"PU","dx":"Bronchial carcinoid tumor with obstructive atelectasis","codes":["PU-14-F2","PU-14-D3"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman who has never smoked comes to the physician because of 4 months of cough, intermittent blood-streaked sputum, and wheezing on the right side of her chest. She has had two episodes of right-sided pneumonia in the past year. Vital signs are within normal limits. Breath sounds are decreased over the lower right chest, anteriorly and posteriorly. Bronchoscopy shows a smooth, red, polypoid mass that completely blocks the bronchus intermedius. Biopsy shows nests of uniform cells with round nuclei and finely stippled chromatin. Immunostaining is positive for chromogranin A and synaptophysin. Diagnosis: bronchial carcinoid tumor with obstructive atelectasis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C17-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional histologic findings in the resected tumor is most consistent with this diagnosis?","options":["Keratin pearls with intercellular bridges","Extensive necrosis with numerous mitoses","Rare mitoses without necrosis","Lepidic growth along alveolar walls"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-F2","obj":"PU-14-F.2","dok":2,"approach":["Chromogranin and synaptophysin show a neuroendocrine tumor, which could be a carcinoid or a small cell carcinoma.","A young never-smoker with a central polypoid mass and uniform cells fits a carcinoid tumor.","Carcinoid is low grade, so mitoses are rare and necrosis is absent."],"why":"Bronchial carcinoid is a low-grade neuroendocrine tumor that arises from neuroendocrine (Kulchitsky) cells of the bronchial mucosa. It forms nests and cords of uniform cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin, few mitoses, and no necrosis in the typical form. It is not linked to smoking and often grows as a central polypoid mass that blocks a bronchus, causing cough, hemoptysis, and repeated pneumonia beyond the block. Because most tumors are small and make little serotonin, carcinoid syndrome is rare.","whyNot":["Keratin pearls and intercellular bridges define squamous cell carcinoma, a central tumor strongly linked to smoking.","Extensive necrosis with many mitoses describes small cell carcinoma, a high-grade neuroendocrine tumor seen almost only in smokers.","Correct.","Lepidic growth along alveolar walls is the pattern of adenocarcinoma in situ, a peripheral tumor."],"study":{"topics":["Bronchial carcinoid tumor","Neuroendocrine lung tumors"],"comps":["PU-14-F2","PU-14-F8"]}},{"id":"PU-C17-Q2","stem":"Which of the following chest radiograph findings is most likely?","options":["Trachea shifted to the left with a flattened right hemidiaphragm","Trachea shifted to the left with a blunted right costophrenic angle","Trachea midline with air bronchograms in the right lower zone","Trachea shifted to the right with an elevated right hemidiaphragm"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-14-D3","obj":"PU-14-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["A complete bronchial block traps air in the lobes beyond it.","Blood flowing past these alveoli absorbs the trapped gas, so the lobes collapse and lose volume.","Loss of volume pulls the trachea and mediastinum toward the collapse and raises the hemidiaphragm on that side."],"why":"In obstructive (resorption) atelectasis, an airway is completely blocked by a tumor, mucus plug, or foreign body. Gas trapped in the alveoli beyond the block is gradually absorbed into the blood, so the lung tissue collapses. The volume loss pulls the mediastinum and trachea toward the affected side, raises the hemidiaphragm, and narrows the rib spaces. Compression atelectasis, from a large effusion or pneumothorax, does the opposite and pushes the mediastinum away.","whyNot":["Shift away with a flattened hemidiaphragm is the picture of a large tension pneumothorax or hyperinflation on the right, not volume loss.","Shift away with a blunted costophrenic angle describes a large pleural effusion that compresses the lung (compression atelectasis).","A midline trachea with air bronchograms describes consolidation, as in lobar pneumonia, in which the airways stay open and alveoli fill with fluid.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Obstructive versus compression atelectasis","Mediastinal shift"],"comps":["PU-14-D3","PU-4"]}},{"id":"PU-C17-Q3","stem":"Which part of the right lung most likely remains ventilated?","options":["Middle lobe","Medial basal segment of the lower lobe","Upper lobe","Superior segment of the lower lobe"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-4","obj":"PU-4.2","dok":3,"approach":["The right main bronchus gives off the upper lobe bronchus first.","The rest of the right main bronchus continues as the bronchus intermedius, which divides into the middle and lower lobe bronchi.","A block in the bronchus intermedius collapses the middle and lower lobes and spares the upper lobe."],"why":"The right lung has three lobes separated by the horizontal and oblique fissures. The right upper lobe bronchus arises from the right main bronchus very close to the carina. The remaining airway, the bronchus intermedius, supplies the middle lobe and all segments of the lower lobe, including its superior segment. A mass in the bronchus intermedius therefore collapses both the middle and lower lobes. The left lung has two lobes, with the lingula of the upper lobe matching the right middle lobe. The right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical, so aspirated material usually lodges on the right.","whyNot":["The middle lobe bronchus arises from the bronchus intermedius, so the middle lobe collapses.","The medial basal segment is supplied by the lower lobe bronchus beyond the block.","Correct.","The superior segment of the lower lobe is supplied by a branch of the lower lobe bronchus beyond the bronchus intermedius. It is the usual site of aspiration in a supine patient."],"study":{"topics":["Bronchial tree of the right lung","Lobes and fissures"],"comps":["PU-4","PU-14-H6"]}},{"id":"PU-C17-Q4","stem":"Arterial blood gas analysis on room air shows a PaO2 of 62 mm Hg (normal 75 to 100) and a PaCO2 of 37 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45). After 20 minutes of breathing 100% oxygen, the PaO2 rises only to 118 mm Hg. Which of the following best explains the poor response to oxygen?","options":["Diffusion across the alveolar wall is slowed","Alveolar ventilation is low in all lung regions","Hemoglobin affinity for oxygen is decreased","Shunted blood never meets the added oxygen"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-7","obj":"PU-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["Collapsed lobes are still perfused but receive no ventilation, so their V/Q ratio is zero (shunt).","Blood passing through them leaves with mixed venous gas levels and mixes with blood from normal lung.","Extra inspired oxygen cannot reach shunted blood, and normal lung blood is already nearly saturated, so PaO2 rises little."],"why":"A shunt is perfusion without ventilation, a V/Q of zero, as with a blocked airway or alveoli filled by pneumonia or edema. Blood from shunted areas stays deoxygenated, and because normal areas cannot carry much extra oxygen on already saturated hemoglobin, PaO2 stays low even on 100% oxygen. PaCO2 usually stays normal or low, because the respiratory drive raises ventilation of normal lung, which removes CO2 easily. Dead space is the opposite: ventilation without perfusion, a V/Q approaching infinity, as in pulmonary embolism, where wasted ventilation is the main problem.","whyNot":["Slowed diffusion, as in interstitial fibrosis, causes hypoxemia that improves well with supplemental oxygen, because a higher alveolar PO2 drives more oxygen across.","Global hypoventilation raises PaCO2 and lowers PaO2, and both correct readily with oxygen. Her PaCO2 is normal.","Lower hemoglobin affinity for oxygen changes how oxygen is unloaded in tissues. It does not lower PaO2 or block the rise of PaO2 with oxygen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Shunt versus dead space","Response of hypoxemia to oxygen"],"comps":["PU-7","PU-14-D3"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C18","system":"PU","dx":"High-altitude pulmonary edema in a climber","codes":["PU-14-E1"],"case":"A 29-year-old man is brought to a high-altitude clinic at 4,500 m (about 14,800 ft) because of breathlessness at rest. He flew in from sea level and climbed to this altitude over 2 days. Over the past day he has had a dry cough that now produces pink, frothy sputum, and he cannot keep up with his group. Temperature is 37.8 °C (100.0 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 30/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry shows an oxygen saturation of 68%. Crackles are heard over both mid lung fields. Chest radiograph shows patchy, asymmetric alveolar opacities with a normal heart size. Echocardiography shows high pulmonary artery pressure and normal left ventricular function. Diagnosis: high-altitude pulmonary edema.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C18-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the movement of fluid into his alveoli?","options":["Low plasma oncotic pressure from urinary protein loss","High flow through unconstricted capillary beds","Lymphatic obstruction by enlarged mediastinal nodes","Raised left atrial pressure transmitted backward"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-E1","obj":"PU-14-E.1","dok":3,"approach":["Normal left ventricular function and heart size make this a noncardiogenic edema, with a normal wedge pressure.","Alveolar hypoxia causes hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, which is patchy rather than even.","Blood is forced through the vessels that did not constrict, so capillary hydrostatic pressure there rises and fluid leaks out."],"why":"Starling forces govern fluid movement across pulmonary capillaries: capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out, and plasma oncotic pressure holds it in. In cardiogenic edema, a high left atrial pressure (wedge pressure above about 18 mm Hg) raises capillary pressure everywhere. In high-altitude pulmonary edema, the left heart is normal, but hypoxic vasoconstriction is uneven. Unconstricted areas receive very high flow and pressure, and capillary walls leak and can break (stress failure). This gives patchy, asymmetric edema and high pulmonary artery pressure with a normal wedge pressure.","whyNot":["Low oncotic pressure from protein loss occurs in nephrotic syndrome and causes generalized edema, not sudden lung edema on ascent.","Correct.","Lymphatic obstruction by tumor causes edema that develops slowly, and nothing suggests mediastinal disease in this healthy climber.","Raised left atrial pressure causes cardiogenic edema, as in left heart failure or mitral stenosis. His left ventricle and heart size are normal."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces in pulmonary edema","Cardiogenic versus noncardiogenic edema"],"comps":["PU-14-E1","PU-7"]}},{"id":"PU-C18-Q2","stem":"His climbing partner made the same ascent and feels well. Over the first several days at altitude, which of the following changes in the partner's kidney function best reflects normal acclimatization?","options":["Increased bicarbonate excretion","Decreased bicarbonate excretion","Increased ammonium excretion","Increased titratable acid excretion"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-7","obj":"PU-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Low inspired PO2 stimulates the peripheral chemoreceptors, so ventilation rises.","Hyperventilation lowers PaCO2 and causes a respiratory alkalosis.","Over days the kidneys compensate by excreting more bicarbonate, which lowers pH back toward normal."],"why":"At high altitude, the low PaO2 stimulates the carotid bodies, so ventilation increases. This raises PaO2 but blows off CO2, causing respiratory alkalosis, which at first limits the rise in ventilation. Over several days, the kidneys excrete more bicarbonate, pH returns toward normal, and ventilation can rise further. Other parts of acclimatization include more erythropoietin and red cell mass, more red cell 2,3-BPG, and hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction. Acetazolamide speeds bicarbonate loss and is used to prevent acute mountain sickness.","whyNot":["Correct.","Decreased bicarbonate excretion would worsen the alkalosis. It is the renal response to a respiratory acidosis.","Increased ammonium excretion is how the kidney excretes more acid, which is the response to acidosis, not alkalosis.","Increased titratable acid excretion also removes acid. It would make an alkalosis worse."],"study":{"topics":["Acclimatization to high altitude","Renal compensation for respiratory alkalosis"],"comps":["PU-7","PU-6"]}},{"id":"PU-C18-Q3","stem":"Within days, red cell 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG) rises in the partner, shifting the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve to the right. Which of the following inherited enzyme deficiencies also raises red cell 2,3-BPG through a direct effect on its synthetic pathway?","options":["Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Hexokinase deficiency","Pyruvate kinase deficiency","Cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-11","obj":"PU-11.4","dok":3,"approach":["2,3-BPG is made in a side branch of red cell glycolysis from 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate (the Rapoport-Luebering shunt).","A block below this branch point causes the intermediates above it to build up, so more 2,3-BPG forms.","Pyruvate kinase catalyzes the last step of glycolysis, so its deficiency raises 2,3-BPG."],"why":"In red cells, bisphosphoglycerate mutase converts some 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to 2,3-BPG. 2,3-BPG binds deoxyhemoglobin and stabilizes the T (tense) form, so hemoglobin gives up oxygen more easily in tissues. Hypoxia at altitude raises 2,3-BPG. In pyruvate kinase deficiency, glycolytic intermediates back up behind the block, more 2,3-BPG is made, and the curve shifts right, so patients tolerate their anemia relatively well. A block above the branch point, such as hexokinase deficiency, has the opposite effect.","whyNot":["G6PD deficiency affects the pentose phosphate pathway and NADPH production. It causes hemolysis under oxidant stress but does not directly raise 2,3-BPG.","Hexokinase acts at the first step of glycolysis, above the 2,3-BPG branch point. Its deficiency lowers 2,3-BPG and shifts the curve to the left.","Correct.","Cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency causes methemoglobinemia, which shifts the curve to the left. It does not raise 2,3-BPG."],"study":{"topics":["2,3-BPG synthesis and effect on hemoglobin","Red cell glycolytic enzyme deficiencies"],"comps":["PU-11","PU-8"]}},{"id":"PU-C18-Q4","stem":"At the clinic, his hemoglobin concentration is 15.0 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5), arterial oxygen saturation is 68%, and PaO2 is 36 mm Hg. Which of the following is closest to his arterial oxygen content, in mL O2/dL of blood?","options":["10.3","13.8","14.7","20.2"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-8","obj":"PU-8.1","dok":3,"approach":["Oxygen content equals bound oxygen (1.34 x Hb x SaO2) plus dissolved oxygen (0.003 x PaO2).","Bound oxygen: 1.34 x 15.0 x 0.68 is about 13.7 mL/dL. Dissolved oxygen: 0.003 x 36 is about 0.1 mL/dL.","The total is about 13.8 mL/dL."],"why":"Almost all oxygen in blood is carried on hemoglobin, with each gram binding about 1.34 mL of O2 when fully saturated. Dissolved oxygen adds only 0.003 mL/dL per mm Hg of PaO2. With a normal hemoglobin but a saturation of 68%, his oxygen content is about 13.8 mL/dL, far below the normal of about 20 mL/dL. Anemia lowers content with a normal PaO2 and SaO2. Polycythemia raises content. Carbon monoxide lowers SaO2 and content while PaO2 stays normal.","whyNot":["10.3 results from using 1.0 instead of 1.34 mL O2 per gram of hemoglobin.","Correct.","14.7 results from multiplying PaO2 by 0.03 instead of 0.003, which overstates the dissolved oxygen tenfold.","20.2 is the content if hemoglobin were fully saturated, which is about normal at sea level."],"study":{"topics":["Arterial oxygen content calculation","Effects of anemia and carbon monoxide on oxygen content"],"comps":["PU-8","PU-7"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C19","system":"PU","dx":"Pulmonary embolism with pulmonary infarction after hip surgery","codes":["PU-14-E2","PU-14-E3"],"case":"A 72-year-old woman develops sudden shortness of breath and right-sided chest pain that worsens with inspiration 6 days after surgical repair of a femoral neck fracture. She has coughed up small amounts of blood. She has chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. Her left calf is swollen and tender. Arterial blood gas analysis on room air shows pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), PaCO2 31 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45), and PaO2 62 mm Hg (normal 75 to 100). D-dimer concentration is increased. CT pulmonary angiography shows filling defects in segmental arteries of the right lower lobe and a peripheral, wedge-shaped opacity that touches the pleura. Diagnosis: pulmonary embolism with pulmonary infarction.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C19-Q1","stem":"She is breathing room air at sea level (inspired oxygen fraction 0.21, barometric pressure 760 mm Hg, water vapor pressure 47 mm Hg). Assuming a respiratory quotient of 0.8, which of the following is closest to her alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient?","options":["12 mm Hg","49 mm Hg","88 mm Hg","111 mm Hg"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-14-E2","obj":"PU-14-E.2","dok":3,"approach":["Alveolar PO2 = 0.21 x (760 minus 47) minus PaCO2/0.8 = 149.7 minus 38.8, which is about 111 mm Hg.","A-a gradient = alveolar PO2 minus PaO2 = 111 minus 62, which is about 49 mm Hg.","A normal gradient at her age is about 20 to 25 mm Hg, so this gradient is widened."],"why":"The alveolar gas equation gives alveolar PO2 from inspired oxygen and PaCO2. Her alveolar PO2 is about 111 mm Hg, so the A-a gradient is about 49 mm Hg, well above normal. Pulmonary embolism widens the gradient because blood is diverted to unblocked vessels, creating areas of low V/Q, and atelectasis adds some shunt. Hyperventilation from hypoxemia and receptor stimulation lowers PaCO2 and causes respiratory alkalosis. Most emboli start as deep vein thrombi in the legs, formed under the conditions of Virchow triad: stasis, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability, all present after hip surgery.","whyNot":["12 mm Hg is within the normal range, as seen when hypoxemia comes only from hypoventilation or low inspired oxygen. It does not fit a PE.","Correct.","88 mm Hg results from leaving out the PaCO2/0.8 term (149.7 minus 62).","111 mm Hg is the alveolar PO2 itself, before PaO2 is subtracted."],"study":{"topics":["Alveolar gas equation and A-a gradient","Pulmonary embolism findings"],"comps":["PU-14-E2","PU-7"]}},{"id":"PU-C19-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the gross appearance of the lung lesion seen as the peripheral opacity?","options":["Red wedge with its base on the pleura","Red wedge with its apex on the pleura","Pale cavity with a liquefied center","Pale wedge with its base on the pleura"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-E3","obj":"PU-14-E.4","dok":2,"approach":["A blocked artery supplies a cone of tissue that widens toward the periphery, so the infarct is wedge-shaped with its base at the pleura.","The lung has a dual blood supply and loose tissue, so blood from bronchial arteries leaks into the dead area.","Lung infarcts are therefore red (hemorrhagic)."],"why":"Pulmonary infarcts are wedge-shaped, with the apex pointing to the blocked vessel and the base on the pleural surface. They are hemorrhagic because bronchial arterial blood continues to flow into the necrotic area and the loose lung tissue lets blood collect. Infarction follows blockage of a medium or small artery, usually when bronchial flow is also reduced, as in heart failure. Hemoptysis comes from bleeding into the alveoli, and pleuritic pain comes from inflammation of the overlying pleura, often with a fibrinous exudate.","whyNot":["Correct.","The apex of the wedge points toward the blocked artery near the hilum, not toward the pleura.","A cavity with a liquefied center describes a lung abscess or a septic infarct, not a bland embolic infarct.","Pale (anemic) infarcts occur in solid organs with end-arterial blood supply, such as the heart, kidney, and spleen."],"study":{"topics":["Red versus pale infarcts","Pulmonary infarction"],"comps":["PU-14-E3","PU-5"]}},{"id":"PU-C19-Q3","stem":"Most pulmonary emboli in people with normal cardiac function do not cause infarction. Which of the following best explains this?","options":["Fibrinolysis clears every embolus within minutes","Bronchial arteries keep supplying the lung tissue","Hypoxic vasoconstriction diverts flow to the segment","Pulmonary veins refill the segment in reverse"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-5","obj":"PU-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["The lung has two blood supplies: the pulmonary arteries for gas exchange and the bronchial arteries for the lung tissue itself.","Bronchial arteries arise from the systemic circulation, mostly from the aorta, and continue to perfuse tissue beyond a pulmonary embolus.","Infarction happens mainly when bronchial flow is also poor, as in her heart failure."],"why":"The pulmonary circulation carries the whole cardiac output at low pressure (mean about 15 mm Hg) and low resistance, for gas exchange. The bronchial arteries are small systemic vessels from the aorta that nourish the bronchi and supporting tissue, and they connect with the pulmonary circulation. When a pulmonary artery is blocked, bronchial blood keeps the tissue alive, so fewer than about 10% of emboli cause infarction. In heart failure, low cardiac output and lung congestion reduce this backup flow, so infarction is more likely.","whyNot":["Fibrinolysis does break down emboli, but over days to weeks, not within minutes, and it does not explain why most emboli spare the tissue.","Correct.","Hypoxic vasoconstriction diverts blood away from poorly ventilated alveoli, not toward an area whose artery is blocked.","Pulmonary veins carry blood away from the alveoli toward the left atrium. They do not reverse flow to rescue a blocked segment."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary versus bronchial circulation","Why pulmonary infarction is uncommon"],"comps":["PU-5","PU-14-E3"]}},{"id":"PU-C19-Q4","stem":"On the same ward, a 22-year-old man with a femoral shaft fracture from a motorcycle crash becomes confused and short of breath 36 hours after the injury. He has petechiae over the upper chest, axillae, and conjunctivae. Which of the following is the most likely source of the embolic material?","options":["Air entering a central venous catheter","Vegetation from the tricuspid valve","Thrombus from deep veins of the calf","Fat globules from injured bone marrow"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-E","obj":"PU-14-E.3","dok":2,"approach":["A long bone fracture followed in 1 to 3 days by hypoxemia, confusion, and petechiae is the triad of fat embolism syndrome.","Marrow fat enters torn veins at the fracture site and lodges in the lung capillaries.","Fat droplets and their breakdown products also reach the brain and skin, causing confusion and petechiae."],"why":"Fat embolism syndrome follows long bone or pelvic fractures, usually 24 to 72 hours after injury. Marrow fat globules enter torn venules, block lung capillaries, and are broken down to free fatty acids that injure the endothelium. Some globules pass into the systemic circulation and block small vessels in the brain and skin, causing confusion and petechiae, typically on the upper body, axillae, and conjunctivae. Air embolism follows central line handling or rapid ascent in divers, and amniotic fluid embolism causes sudden collapse and DIC during delivery.","whyNot":["Air embolism occurs suddenly when air enters an open central line or during decompression in divers. There is no catheter event here.","Septic emboli from tricuspid endocarditis, as in injection drug use, cause multiple cavitating lung nodules with fever and a murmur.","A thromboembolus from the calf causes dyspnea and hypoxemia, but it does not cause petechiae and does not explain confusion in this young man.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Fat embolism syndrome","Types of embolism"],"comps":["PU-14-E","PU-14-E2"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C20","system":"PU","dx":"Lung adenocarcinoma with an EGFR mutation in a never-smoker","codes":["PU-14-F1"],"case":"A 49-year-old woman who has never smoked comes to the physician because of a dry cough for 3 months and a 4-kg (9-lb) weight loss. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Chest CT shows a 3.2-cm peripheral nodule with irregular, spiculated margins in the left upper lobe. CT-guided biopsy shows gland-forming tumor cells with intracellular mucin that stain positive for thyroid transcription factor 1 (TTF-1). Molecular testing of the tumor shows an exon 19 deletion in the EGFR gene. Diagnosis: lung adenocarcinoma with an EGFR mutation.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C20-Q1","stem":"The resected lobe also contains a separate 1.0-cm nodule. In this nodule, tumor cells grow along intact alveolar walls, with no invasion of the stroma, blood vessels, or pleura. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis of this second nodule?","options":["Large cell carcinoma","Invasive mucinous adenocarcinoma","Adenocarcinoma in situ","Squamous cell carcinoma in situ"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-F1","obj":"PU-14-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Growth of tumor cells along intact alveolar walls is called lepidic growth.","A small lesion with only lepidic growth and no invasion is adenocarcinoma in situ.","Any invasion of stroma, vessels, or pleura would make it an invasive adenocarcinoma."],"why":"Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer overall and the most common in never-smokers and women. It is usually peripheral and forms glands or makes mucin. Adenocarcinoma in situ is a small lesion, 3 cm or less, in which tumor cells spread along the alveolar walls (lepidic growth) without invading. It was formerly called bronchioloalveolar carcinoma. Complete resection of adenocarcinoma in situ is curative.","whyNot":["Large cell carcinoma is an invasive, poorly differentiated tumor with large cells and no gland or squamous differentiation.","Invasive mucinous adenocarcinoma has tall mucin-filled cells that invade tissue and often spread through the airways to many sites.","Correct.","Squamous cell carcinoma in situ arises in central bronchial epithelium after squamous metaplasia and dysplasia, usually in smokers, not along alveolar walls."],"study":{"topics":["Adenocarcinoma of the lung","Adenocarcinoma in situ and lepidic growth"],"comps":["PU-14-F1","PU-14-F9"]}},{"id":"PU-C20-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the effect of this mutation on the encoded protein?","options":["Loss of a nuclear protein that halts the cell cycle","Lost GTPase activity in a membrane-bound G protein","Gene fusion creating a constitutively active ALK kinase","Ligand-independent receptor tyrosine kinase signaling"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-10","obj":"PU-10.5","dok":2,"approach":["EGFR encodes the epidermal growth factor receptor, a transmembrane receptor tyrosine kinase.","Exon 19 deletions in its kinase domain are gain-of-function mutations.","The receptor signals without its ligand, driving growth through pathways such as RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT."],"why":"EGFR is a receptor tyrosine kinase that normally signals only after epidermal growth factor binds and two receptors pair up. Exon 19 deletions and the L858R point mutation activate the kinase domain so it signals all the time. These mutations are most common in adenocarcinomas of never-smokers, women, and people of East Asian ancestry. Tumors with them often respond to EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors. Other adenocarcinoma drivers are KRAS mutations (more common in smokers) and ALK rearrangements, while small cell carcinoma nearly always has TP53 and RB1 loss.","whyNot":["Loss of a nuclear brake on the cell cycle describes tumor suppressors such as RB1 and TP53, which are lost in small cell carcinoma.","Loss of GTPase activity describes activating KRAS mutations, which keep RAS in its GTP-bound active state. KRAS mutations occur mainly in smokers.","ALK rearrangements, such as EML4-ALK, are gene fusions that also activate a kinase, but they are a different alteration from an EGFR exon 19 deletion.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Driver mutations in lung cancer","Receptor tyrosine kinases as oncogenes"],"comps":["PU-10","PU-14-F8"]}},{"id":"PU-C20-Q3","stem":"Before biopsy, the differential diagnosis for the nodule included a benign hamartoma. Which of the following CT findings would most strongly have favored hamartoma?","options":["Multiple round nodules in both lungs","Popcorn calcification with fat density","Thick-walled cavity with an air-fluid level","Spiculated margins with pleural retraction"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-F","obj":"PU-14-F.12","dok":2,"approach":["A pulmonary hamartoma is a benign nodule made of mature cartilage, fat, and connective tissue.","Calcified cartilage gives a popcorn pattern on imaging, and fat gives low density.","These two features together strongly favor a hamartoma over a malignancy."],"why":"Pulmonary hamartoma is the most common benign lung tumor. It is a well-circumscribed nodule of disorganized mature cartilage, fat, and connective tissue, usually found by chance in adults. Popcorn-like calcification from the cartilage and fat within the nodule are its classic CT signs. Metastases are more common than primary lung cancer and usually appear as several round, well-circumscribed nodules in both lungs. Spiculated margins favor a primary lung cancer, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Multiple round, well-circumscribed nodules in both lungs are typical of metastases, which reach the lung by the blood.","Correct.","A thick-walled cavity suggests squamous cell carcinoma, lung abscess, or tuberculosis, not a hamartoma.","Spiculated margins and pleural retraction suggest an infiltrating primary lung cancer, as in this patient."],"study":{"topics":["Solitary pulmonary nodule","Pulmonary hamartoma versus metastases"],"comps":["PU-14-F","PU-14-F9"]}},{"id":"PU-C20-Q4","stem":"Electron microscopy of some tumor cells shows cytoplasmic lamellar bodies, a feature of one type of normal alveolar lining cell. In addition to secreting surfactant, that normal cell does which of the following?","options":["Regenerates type I cells after alveolar injury","Moves mucus toward the pharynx with its cilia","Phagocytoses inhaled particles in the alveoli","Forms most of the alveolar gas-exchange surface"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-2","obj":"PU-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Lamellar bodies store surfactant and are found in type II pneumocytes.","Type II pneumocytes are also the stem cells of the alveolar lining.","After injury they divide and replace type I pneumocytes."],"why":"Type II pneumocytes are cuboidal cells that make up a small share of the alveolar surface. They make surfactant, stored in lamellar bodies, which lowers surface tension and keeps alveoli open. They also divide after alveolar injury and differentiate into type I pneumocytes, so type II cell hyperplasia is a sign of repair. Type I pneumocytes are thin, flat cells that cover most of the alveolar surface for gas exchange. Club cells in the bronchioles secrete protective proteins, detoxify inhaled compounds with cytochrome P450, and act as local progenitors.","whyNot":["Correct.","Ciliated columnar cells move mucus up the airways, but they line the bronchi and bronchioles, not the alveoli.","Alveolar macrophages phagocytose inhaled particles and microbes. They come from blood monocytes and do not make surfactant.","Type I pneumocytes are thin, flat cells that form about 95% of the alveolar surface and the gas-exchange barrier. They do not make surfactant."],"study":{"topics":["Alveolar cell types and functions","Airway epithelium"],"comps":["PU-2","PU-14-A1"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C21","system":"PU","dx":"Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma","codes":["PU-14-F3"],"case":"A 63-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of difficulty swallowing. It began with solid foods and now occurs with soft foods. He has had a 9-kg (20-lb) weight loss during this period. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years and drinks 6 to 8 beers daily. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. He appears thin. Upper endoscopy shows an ulcerated, friable mass that narrows the lumen of the middle third of the esophagus, 28 cm from the incisors. Biopsy specimens show invasive nests of atypical squamous cells with keratin pearls. Diagnosis: squamous cell carcinoma of the middle third of the esophagus.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C21-Q1","stem":"Three months later, he has paroxysms of coughing each time he drinks liquids, and he develops right lower lobe pneumonia. His voice is unchanged. Which of the following most likely explains these new findings?","options":["Hematogenous spread of tumor to the right lower lobe","Aspiration of gastric acid refluxing above a distal stricture","Paralysis of a vocal fold from recurrent laryngeal nerve invasion","Erosion of the tumor into the adjacent airway"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-F3","obj":"PU-14-F.3","dok":3,"approach":["Place the tumor: the middle third of the esophagus lies directly behind the trachea and the left main bronchus.","Note the key clue: coughing that starts with every swallow of liquid points to a direct channel between the esophagus and the airway.","Use the normal voice to rule out recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy, then pick erosion into the airway (an acquired tracheoesophageal or bronchoesophageal fistula)."],"why":"Squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus favors the upper and middle thirds, where the esophagus touches the trachea and left main bronchus. The esophagus has no serosa, so the tumor invades neighboring structures early. When it erodes into the airway, swallowed liquid passes straight into the bronchi, which causes coughing with each swallow and aspiration pneumonia. Smoking and heavy alcohol use are the main risk factors for this tumor type, while adenocarcinoma arises in the distal third from Barrett esophagus.","whyNot":["Blood-borne metastasis to the lung forms nodules. It does not explain coughing that is triggered by drinking.","Reflux above a stricture can cause aspiration, but it tends to happen when lying down or at night. It does not start the moment liquid is swallowed, and his tumor is in the middle third, not at the gastroesophageal junction.","Invasion of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve can cause aspiration, but it also causes hoarseness. His voice is normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma versus adenocarcinoma","Acquired tracheoesophageal fistula"],"comps":["PU-14-F3","PU-14-F"]}},{"id":"PU-C21-Q2","stem":"He undergoes esophagectomy with removal of the distal esophagus through the diaphragm. After surgery, he has delayed gastric emptying. Division of which of the following structures, with its diaphragmatic opening and vertebral level, best explains this complication?","options":["Azygos vein, through the aortic hiatus at T12","Vagal trunks, through the esophageal hiatus at T10","Right phrenic nerve, through the caval opening at T8","Thoracic duct, through the aortic hiatus at T12"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-4","obj":"PU-4.6","dok":2,"approach":["Link delayed gastric emptying to loss of parasympathetic supply to the stomach.","Recall which structures pass through the diaphragm with the esophagus: the anterior and posterior vagal trunks at T10.","Check the other openings: inferior vena cava at T8; aorta, thoracic duct, and azygos vein at T12."],"why":"The anterior and posterior vagal trunks run on the esophagus and pass with it through the esophageal hiatus at about T10. They carry parasympathetic fibers that drive gastric motility and relaxation of the pylorus. Removing the distal esophagus divides both trunks, so the stomach empties slowly. A common memory aid is 'I ate ten eggs at twelve': IVC at T8, esophagus at T10, aorta at T12.","whyNot":["The azygos vein passes through the aortic hiatus at T12. It drains the posterior chest wall and has no role in gastric motility.","Correct.","The right phrenic nerve is associated with the caval opening at T8. It supplies the diaphragm, and injury paralyzes the right hemidiaphragm.","The thoracic duct passes through the aortic hiatus at T12. Injury causes chylothorax, a milky pleural effusion, not delayed gastric emptying."],"study":{"topics":["Diaphragmatic openings and their levels","Vagal supply of the stomach"],"comps":["PU-4"]}},{"id":"PU-C21-Q3","stem":"His tumor arose from esophageal surface epithelium. The trachea and esophagus both develop from the foregut in the fourth week. Which of the following components of the trachea arises from the same germ layer as the epithelium in which his tumor began?","options":["Smooth muscle of the trachealis","C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage","Seromucous glands of the submucosa","Fibroelastic connective tissue of the wall"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-1","obj":"PU-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the germ layer of esophageal epithelium: foregut endoderm.","Recall that the lung bud grows from the ventral foregut, so the airway lining and its glands are also endoderm.","Separate these from the wall tissues (cartilage, smooth muscle, connective tissue), which come from splanchnic mesoderm."],"why":"The respiratory diverticulum buds from the ventral wall of the foregut in week 4. Its endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the larynx, trachea, bronchi, and alveoli, and the glands that grow down from that lining. The surrounding splanchnic mesoderm forms the cartilage, smooth muscle, and connective tissue. The esophageal epithelium comes from the same foregut endoderm.","whyNot":["The trachealis muscle forms from splanchnic mesoderm. The same is true of bronchial smooth muscle.","Tracheal cartilage forms from splanchnic mesoderm around the lung bud, not from endoderm.","Correct.","The connective tissue of the airway wall comes from splanchnic mesoderm."],"study":{"topics":["Lung bud development","Endoderm versus splanchnic mesoderm derivatives"],"comps":["PU-1"]}},{"id":"PU-C21-Q4","stem":"Sequencing of his tumor shows an inactivating TP53 mutation with a G to T transversion, a change linked to benzo[a]pyrene in tobacco smoke. Loss of the normal product of this gene most directly allows which of the following in cells with DNA damage?","options":["Constitutive activation of RAS GTPase signaling","Stabilization of beta-catenin in the cytoplasm","Accumulation of replication errors in microsatellites","Entry into S phase before the damage is repaired"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-10","obj":"PU-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the job of p53: it senses DNA damage and halts the cell cycle at the G1 to S checkpoint.","Trace the pathway: p53 induces p21, which inhibits cyclin-CDK complexes, which keeps Rb active and holds E2F in check.","Predict the result of losing p53: damaged cells keep dividing and pass mutations on."],"why":"Normal p53 is stabilized when DNA is damaged. It turns on p21, a CDK inhibitor, so Rb stays underphosphorylated and keeps E2F bound, which blocks the G1 to S transition. This pause allows repair, and if the damage is too great, p53 triggers apoptosis. Without p53, cells with damaged DNA enter S phase and copy their mutations. Benzo[a]pyrene forms bulky adducts on guanine, and misrepair produces the G to T changes often found in TP53 of smoking-related cancers.","whyNot":["Constant RAS signaling comes from a gain-of-function mutation in a RAS gene, an oncogene. Losing p53 does not activate RAS.","Beta-catenin builds up when APC is lost, as in colorectal cancer. This is a separate pathway from p53.","Microsatellite errors build up when mismatch repair genes such as MLH1 or MSH2 are lost, as in Lynch syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["p53 and the G1 to S checkpoint","Tobacco carcinogens and DNA adducts"],"comps":["PU-10"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C22","system":"PU","dx":"Glottic laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma","codes":["PU-14-F4"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman comes to the physician because of hoarseness that has worsened steadily for 3 months. She has no pain or difficulty swallowing. She has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years and drinks 2 to 3 glasses of wine daily. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 78/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg. No cervical lymph nodes are palpable. Flexible laryngoscopy shows an irregular, white, exophytic lesion on the anterior two thirds of the right true vocal fold. Both vocal folds move normally. Biopsy specimens show invasive nests of atypical squamous cells with keratin pearls. Diagnosis: glottic squamous cell carcinoma of the larynx.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C22-Q1","stem":"Her 6-year-old grandson has a 1-year history of hoarseness. Laryngoscopy shows multiple soft, wartlike, exophytic lesions on both vocal folds, and the lesions have recurred twice after surgical removal. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the grandson's lesions?","options":["Human papillomavirus types 6 and 11","Latent Epstein-Barr virus infection","Repetitive phonotrauma from vocal overuse","Human papillomavirus types 16 and 18"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-F4","obj":"PU-14-F.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the features: a child, multiple wartlike lesions, and repeated recurrence after removal.","Name the lesion: juvenile (recurrent respiratory) laryngeal papillomatosis.","Match it to the low-risk HPV types that cause benign papillomas: types 6 and 11."],"why":"Juvenile laryngeal papillomatosis is caused by HPV types 6 and 11, usually acquired from the mother during birth. The lesions are benign squamous papillomas, often multiple, and they recur after removal. They rarely become malignant. In contrast, his grandmother's tumor is a squamous cell carcinoma driven by smoking and alcohol, and on the glottis it causes hoarseness early, while still small.","whyNot":["Correct.","Epstein-Barr virus is linked to nasopharyngeal carcinoma and some lymphomas, not to laryngeal papillomas.","Voice overuse causes vocal fold nodules, which are small, smooth, and usually bilateral at the junction of the anterior and middle thirds. They are not wartlike and do not keep recurring after removal if voice use changes.","HPV types 16 and 18 are high-risk types linked to oropharyngeal and cervical carcinoma. Benign papillomas come from the low-risk types."],"study":{"topics":["Laryngeal papillomatosis versus vocal fold nodules","Laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma"],"comps":["PU-14-F4","PU-14-F"]}},{"id":"PU-C22-Q2","stem":"Which of the following epithelia normally covers the site where her tumor arose?","options":["Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-2","obj":"PU-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the tumor: the glottis, meaning the true vocal fold.","Recall that surfaces exposed to friction (vocal folds, oropharynx) are lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","Connect this squamous lining to squamous cell carcinoma as the main laryngeal cancer. Keratin pearls in a tumor show squamous differentiation, not a keratinized normal lining."],"why":"The true vocal folds rub together thousands of times a day, so they are covered by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, like the oropharynx. Most of the rest of the larynx, including the false vocal folds, has pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium. Squamous cell carcinoma makes up the great majority of laryngeal cancers. Well-differentiated squamous carcinomas often form keratin pearls even when the lining they came from was nonkeratinized.","whyNot":["Correct.","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium covers the skin. The keratin pearls in her tumor reflect squamous differentiation of the cancer, not the normal fold lining.","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the false vocal folds, ventricles, and trachea. It is absent from the free edge of the true fold.","Simple cuboidal epithelium lines small ducts and the smallest bronchioles. It is not found on the vocal folds."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelium of the larynx","Laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma"],"comps":["PU-2","PU-14-F4"]}},{"id":"PU-C22-Q3","stem":"Cigarette smoke exposes her airway epithelium to superoxide and hydrogen peroxide. Her serum selenium concentration is low. Reduced activity of which of the following enzymes would most impair her epithelial cells' removal of hydrogen peroxide and lipid hydroperoxides?","options":["Copper-zinc superoxide dismutase","Glutathione peroxidase","Catalase","Myeloperoxidase"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-9","obj":"PU-9.5","dok":2,"approach":["List the main antioxidant enzymes: superoxide dismutase, catalase, and glutathione peroxidase.","Pick the one that needs selenium: glutathione peroxidase has selenocysteine in its active site.","Confirm that it handles both hydrogen peroxide and lipid hydroperoxides, using reduced glutathione."],"why":"Smoke and high inspired oxygen both increase reactive oxygen species in the lung. Superoxide dismutase turns superoxide into hydrogen peroxide. Glutathione peroxidase then reduces hydrogen peroxide and lipid hydroperoxides to water and alcohols, using reduced glutathione. This enzyme contains selenocysteine, so selenium deficiency lowers its activity and leaves membranes open to lipid peroxidation.","whyNot":["Copper-zinc superoxide dismutase converts superoxide to hydrogen peroxide. It needs copper and zinc.","Correct.","Catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide, but it is a heme enzyme that needs iron, not selenium, and it does not act on lipid hydroperoxides.","Myeloperoxidase in neutrophils makes hypochlorous acid from hydrogen peroxide. It creates oxidants to kill microbes, so it does not protect cells."],"study":{"topics":["Antioxidant enzymes and their cofactors","Oxidant injury in the lung"],"comps":["PU-9"]}},{"id":"PU-C22-Q4","stem":"She has also had frequent episodes of acute bronchitis. Which of the following defenses that normally clear inhaled particles of 2 to 10 micrometers from the bronchi is most directly impaired by smoking?","options":["Secretory IgA binding at the mucosal surface","Opsonization by surfactant proteins A and D","Mucociliary clearance by ciliated epithelium","Filtration of particles by the nasal turbinates"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-12","obj":"PU-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Match particle size to deposition site: particles of 2 to 10 micrometers settle in the trachea and bronchi.","Recall the defense that removes them there: mucus traps them and cilia sweep them up to the pharynx.","Link smoking to paralysis and loss of cilia and to goblet cell hyperplasia, which slows this escalator."],"why":"Larger particles are filtered in the nose, mid-size particles land in the bronchi, and particles under about 2 micrometers reach the alveoli. In the bronchi, the mucociliary escalator traps particles and microbes in mucus and moves them toward the pharynx. Smoke slows ciliary beating, destroys ciliated cells, and increases thick mucus. Retained mucus and microbes lead to repeated bronchitis and pneumonia.","whyNot":["Secretory IgA blocks microbes from attaching to the mucosa. It does not physically remove deposited particles, and smoking does not mainly act through it.","Surfactant proteins A and D act as opsonins in the alveoli. They handle the smallest particles that reach the gas-exchange surface, not those in the bronchi.","Correct.","The nasal turbinates filter particles larger than about 10 micrometers before they reach the lower airways."],"study":{"topics":["Mucociliary escalator","Particle size and deposition"],"comps":["PU-12","PU-2"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C23","system":"PU","dx":"Malignant pleural mesothelioma with asbestos pleural plaques","codes":["PU-14-F5","PU-14-D4"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of dull right-sided chest pain, increasing shortness of breath, and a 7-kg (15-lb) weight loss. He worked as a pipefitter in a shipyard from 1972 to 1985. He has never smoked. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. Examination shows dullness to percussion and decreased breath sounds over the lower right chest. CT of the chest shows a thick, nodular rind of pleural tissue encasing the right lung, a moderate right pleural effusion, and calcified plaques on the parietal pleura of both hemidiaphragms. Thoracentesis yields bloody exudative fluid. Pleural biopsy specimens show an infiltrating epithelioid malignancy. Diagnosis: malignant pleural mesothelioma with asbestos-related pleural plaques.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C23-Q1","stem":"The pathologist must distinguish his tumor from metastatic adenocarcinoma involving the pleura. Which of the following findings in the tumor cells would best support mesothelioma?","options":["Positive staining for CEA and TTF-1","Short, sparse microvilli on electron microscopy","Positive staining for calretinin and WT1","Intracellular mucin droplets on mucicarmine stain"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-F5","obj":"PU-14-F.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that both tumors can look glandular or epithelioid on routine stains.","List the markers of mesothelial cells: calretinin, WT1, and cytokeratin 5/6, with long, slender microvilli on electron microscopy.","List the markers of lung adenocarcinoma: CEA, TTF-1, mucin, and short microvilli. Pick the mesothelial pattern."],"why":"Malignant mesothelioma arises from the mesothelial cells lining the pleura. It keeps their markers: calretinin, WT1, and cytokeratins, and on electron microscopy it shows long, slender, abundant microvilli. Adenocarcinoma spreading to the pleura makes mucin, stains for CEA and, if from the lung, TTF-1, and has short, stubby microvilli. Mesothelioma follows asbestos exposure by decades and causes a recurrent bloody effusion and a rind of tumor around the lung.","whyNot":["CEA and TTF-1 positivity points to lung adenocarcinoma. Mesotheliomas are usually negative for both.","Short, sparse microvilli are typical of adenocarcinoma. Mesothelioma cells have long, slender, numerous microvilli.","Correct.","Mucin production is a feature of adenocarcinoma. Mesothelial cells do not make epithelial mucin."],"study":{"topics":["Mesothelioma versus metastatic adenocarcinoma","Immunohistochemical markers"],"comps":["PU-14-F5","PU-14-F1"]}},{"id":"PU-C23-Q2","stem":"His twin brother worked beside him in the shipyard for the same years and has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years. For which of the following malignancies does the combination of these two exposures most greatly increase the brother's risk?","options":["Urothelial carcinoma of the bladder","Malignant pleural mesothelioma","Bronchial carcinoid tumor","Bronchogenic carcinoma"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-D4","obj":"PU-14-D.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall which cancers asbestos causes: bronchogenic carcinoma and mesothelioma.","Recall which of these smoking also causes: bronchogenic carcinoma only.","Apply the principle: when both act on the same cancer, the risks multiply, so lung carcinoma risk rises far more than either exposure alone would cause."],"why":"Asbestos fibers injure the lung and pleura and increase the risk of both bronchogenic carcinoma and mesothelioma. Smoking adds to lung carcinoma risk in a synergistic way: the combined risk is much greater than the sum of the two separate risks. Smoking does not raise mesothelioma risk. Bronchogenic carcinoma is also far more common than mesothelioma among asbestos-exposed workers.","whyNot":["Smoking raises bladder cancer risk, but asbestos does not, so there is no combined effect.","Mesothelioma risk comes from asbestos alone. Smoking does not add to it, so the brother's mesothelioma risk is about the same as the patient's.","Bronchial carcinoids are neuroendocrine tumors with no strong link to smoking or asbestos.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Asbestos-related disease","Smoking and asbestos synergy"],"comps":["PU-14-D4","PU-14-A3"]}},{"id":"PU-C23-Q3","stem":"Over the next month, the tumor spreads across the central part of the right hemidiaphragm, and he develops pain at the tip of the right shoulder. Which of the following nerves carries the pain sensation that is referred to his shoulder?","options":["Lower intercostal nerves","Phrenic nerve","Thoracic splanchnic nerves","Vagus nerve"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-4","obj":"PU-4.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the irritated layer: parietal pleura on the central diaphragm.","Recall its sensory nerve: the phrenic nerve, from spinal segments C3 to C5.","Explain the referral: the same segments supply the skin over the shoulder through the supraclavicular nerves."],"why":"Parietal pleura is sensitive to pain. Its costal part and the peripheral diaphragm are supplied by intercostal nerves, so pain there is felt sharply in the chest wall or upper abdomen. The central diaphragmatic and mediastinal parietal pleura are supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5). The brain reads pain from these segments as coming from the shoulder, which shares the C3 and C4 dermatomes. Visceral pleura has only autonomic supply and is insensitive to pain.","whyNot":["The lower intercostal nerves supply the costal pleura and the peripheral rim of the diaphragm. Pain there is felt in the lower chest wall or abdomen, not the shoulder.","Correct.","The thoracic splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers to abdominal organs. They do not supply the diaphragmatic pleura.","The vagus nerve carries autonomic afferents from the viscera, including visceral pleura and lungs. It does not mediate sharp pleuritic pain referred to the shoulder."],"study":{"topics":["Nerve supply of the pleura","Referred shoulder pain"],"comps":["PU-4"]}},{"id":"PU-C23-Q4","stem":"Spirometry and lung volumes are measured before treatment. Which of the following patterns is most likely? (Normal FEV1/FVC ratio is 0.70 or greater.)","options":["Increased TLC with an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.55","Reduced TLC with an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.85","Reduced TLC with an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.55","Normal TLC with an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.55"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-6","obj":"PU-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the mechanical problem: a stiff rind of tumor and fluid prevents the lung from expanding.","Classify it as restrictive disease from outside the lung: the lung cannot fill, so TLC and FVC fall.","Predict the ratio: the airways are open, so FEV1 falls in proportion to FVC and the ratio stays normal or rises."],"why":"A pleural rind and effusion restrict lung expansion, which is a restrictive defect. TLC, vital capacity, and FVC all fall. Because the airways are not narrowed, air still leaves quickly, so FEV1 falls about as much as FVC, and the FEV1/FVC ratio is normal or high. Obstructive disease shows the reverse: a low ratio with normal or increased TLC.","whyNot":["An increased TLC with a low ratio is the obstructive pattern of emphysema, with air trapping and hyperinflation.","Correct.","A low TLC with a low ratio is a mixed pattern. It would need both restriction and airway obstruction, and he has no airway disease.","A normal TLC with a low ratio is obstruction without hyperinflation, as in asthma or chronic bronchitis."],"study":{"topics":["Obstructive versus restrictive spirometry","Extraparenchymal restriction"],"comps":["PU-6"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C24","system":"PU","dx":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","codes":["PU-14-F6"],"case":"A 46-year-old man who immigrated from Guangdong province in southern China comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of a painless lump in the left side of his neck. He also has a feeling of fullness and decreased hearing in the left ear and intermittent blood-tinged nasal discharge. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 124/80 mm Hg. Examination shows a firm, nontender, 3-cm left upper cervical lymph node. The left tympanic membrane is retracted with an amber middle ear effusion. Nasopharyngoscopy shows a mass in the left lateral nasopharyngeal recess. Biopsy specimens show sheets of undifferentiated, nonkeratinizing carcinoma cells mixed with many lymphocytes. Diagnosis: nasopharyngeal carcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C24-Q1","stem":"In situ hybridization is performed on the tumor cells. Which of the following is most likely to be detected?","options":["Epstein-Barr virus-encoded small RNAs","Human papillomavirus type 16 E6 and E7 mRNA","Human herpesvirus 8 latency-associated transcripts","Human T-lymphotropic virus type 1 proviral DNA"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-14-F6","obj":"PU-14-F.6","dok":2,"approach":["Combine the clues: a southern Chinese patient, a nasopharyngeal mass, and undifferentiated carcinoma rich in lymphocytes.","Match this to the virus that latently infects nasopharyngeal carcinoma cells: Epstein-Barr virus.","Recall the standard tissue test: in situ hybridization for EBV-encoded small RNAs (EBER)."],"why":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma, especially the nonkeratinizing and undifferentiated types, is almost always infected with Epstein-Barr virus. It is common in southern China and parts of Africa, where genetic, dietary, and viral factors combine. Latent EBV in tumor cells makes large amounts of small RNAs called EBERs, which in situ hybridization detects. The tumor spreads early to cervical lymph nodes, and blockage of the eustachian tube causes a one-sided serous middle ear effusion, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Correct.","HPV type 16 drives squamous carcinoma of the tonsil and base of tongue in the oropharynx, not the classic undifferentiated nasopharyngeal carcinoma seen in southern Chinese patients.","Human herpesvirus 8 causes Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, and multicentric Castleman disease.","HTLV-1 causes adult T-cell leukemia and lymphoma and a myelopathy. It is not linked to nasopharyngeal carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and EBV","Oncogenic viruses"],"comps":["PU-14-F6","PU-14-F"]}},{"id":"PU-C24-Q2","stem":"His 38-year-old sister has had recurrent nosebleeds since childhood. She has small red macules on her lips, tongue, and fingertips that blanch with pressure, and last year she had a brain abscess. Her son has the same skin lesions. Which of the following best explains the sister's condition?","options":["Autosomal dominant deficiency of von Willebrand factor","X-linked recessive deficiency of coagulation factor IX","Autosomal dominant loss of endoglin function","Autosomal dominant mutation in the fibrillin-1 gene"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-14-E","obj":"PU-14-E.6","dok":3,"approach":["Group the findings: recurrent epistaxis, mucocutaneous telangiectasias, and a brain abscess, passed from mother to son.","Explain the abscess: a pulmonary arteriovenous malformation shunts venous blood around the lung filter, so bacteria reach the brain.","Name the disorder and its genetics: hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia, autosomal dominant, with mutations in endoglin or ACVRL1 in TGF-beta signaling."],"why":"Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia is an autosomal dominant disorder, most often from loss of endoglin (ENG) or ACVRL1 (ALK1), both parts of TGF-beta family signaling in endothelial cells. Fragile, dilated vessels form telangiectasias in the nose, lips, and gut, and larger arteriovenous malformations form in the lungs, liver, and brain. A pulmonary AVM is a right-to-left shunt: it causes hypoxemia, and it lets clots or bacteria bypass the lung capillaries, leading to paradoxical stroke or brain abscess.","whyNot":["Von Willebrand disease causes nosebleeds and mucosal bleeding from impaired platelet adhesion, but it does not cause telangiectasias or a brain abscess.","Hemophilia B is X-linked and mainly affects males, with joint and deep muscle bleeding. It does not cause telangiectasias.","Correct.","Fibrillin-1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome, with lens dislocation and aortic root dilation, not telangiectasias or AVMs."],"study":{"topics":["Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia","Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations"],"comps":["PU-14-E","PU-7"]}},{"id":"PU-C24-Q3","stem":"A 52-year-old man who has never smoked has an enlarged right tonsil and a cystic right upper cervical lymph node. Biopsy shows nonkeratinizing squamous cell carcinoma with strong, diffuse nuclear and cytoplasmic staining for p16. Which of the following viral actions most directly explains the p16 staining?","options":["E7 binding and inactivation of Rb protein","Tax transactivation of host growth factor genes","HBx activation of host growth-promoting genes","LMP-1 signaling that mimics an active CD40 receptor"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-14-F","obj":"PU-14-F.13","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the tumor: tonsillar squamous cell carcinoma in a nonsmoker, which points to HPV type 16.","Recall the HPV oncoproteins: E6 degrades p53 and E7 binds Rb.","Link E7 to p16: with Rb inactivated, the cell loses the feedback that keeps p16 low, so p16 builds up and serves as a marker of HPV-driven tumors."],"why":"High-risk HPV, mainly type 16, causes squamous cell carcinoma of the tonsil and base of tongue, often in younger patients who do not smoke. Viral E7 binds Rb and releases E2F, so the cell enters S phase. Normally Rb activity keeps p16 expression low, so loss of Rb leads to strong p16 overexpression, which pathologists use as a surrogate marker for HPV. Tobacco and alcohol related carcinomas of the oral cavity and pharynx usually lack p16 overexpression and often carry TP53 mutations instead.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tax is the transforming protein of HTLV-1 in adult T-cell leukemia and lymphoma.","HBx is a hepatitis B virus protein that contributes to hepatocellular carcinoma.","LMP-1 is an Epstein-Barr virus protein that mimics CD40 signaling. It drives nasopharyngeal carcinoma, as in the man described in the case, and some B-cell lymphomas."],"study":{"topics":["HPV-related oropharyngeal carcinoma","Viral oncoproteins"],"comps":["PU-14-F","PU-10"]}},{"id":"PU-C24-Q4","stem":"Several months before his diagnosis, he had a nosebleed that began after forceful nose blowing in dry winter air and stopped after 10 minutes of pinching the soft part of his nose. A branch of which of the following arteries most likely contributed to this bleeding?","options":["Infraorbital branch of the maxillary artery","Ascending pharyngeal artery","Posterior lateral nasal branch of the sphenopalatine artery","Superior labial branch of the facial artery"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-3","obj":"PU-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the bleed: started with dry air and minor trauma, stopped with pressure on the front of the nose, so it is anterior.","Locate the anterior site: Kiesselbach area on the anterior nasal septum.","List the arteries that meet there: septal branches of the sphenopalatine, anterior ethmoidal, greater palatine, and superior labial arteries."],"why":"About 90% of nosebleeds come from Kiesselbach area on the anterior septum. Four arteries anastomose there: the septal branch of the sphenopalatine artery, the anterior ethmoidal artery, the greater palatine artery, and the septal branch of the superior labial artery from the facial artery. The mucosa is thin and dries easily, so minor trauma makes it bleed, and pinching the soft nose compresses the site. Posterior bleeds come from sphenopalatine branches far back in the nasal cavity, are heavier, and are not controlled by anterior pressure.","whyNot":["The infraorbital artery, a branch of the maxillary artery, supplies the lower eyelid, cheek, and side of the nose. It does not reach the nasal septum or Kiesselbach area.","The ascending pharyngeal artery supplies the pharynx and does not supply the anterior nasal septum.","Posterior lateral nasal branches of the sphenopalatine artery supply the back of the lateral nasal wall and are the source of posterior epistaxis, which pinching the nose does not stop.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Kiesselbach area","Anterior versus posterior epistaxis"],"comps":["PU-3"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C25","system":"PU","dx":"Small cell lung carcinoma with SIADH and Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","codes":["PU-14-F8"],"case":"A 64-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of difficulty rising from a chair and climbing stairs, dry mouth, and fatigue. Her strength improves briefly after repeated effort. She has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 50 years. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 82/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. She appears euvolemic. Hip flexors are weak. Deep tendon reflexes are decreased and increase after 10 seconds of maximal muscle contraction. Serum studies show sodium 121 mEq/L (135 to 145), potassium 4.0 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), and osmolality 252 mOsm/kg H2O (275 to 295). Urine osmolality is 460 mOsm/kg H2O. TSH and morning cortisol concentrations are within the reference ranges. CT shows a central hilar mass with bulky mediastinal lymph nodes. Diagnosis: small cell lung carcinoma with SIADH and Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C25-Q1","stem":"Bronchoscopic biopsy specimens show sheets of small cells with scant cytoplasm, finely granular chromatin, and nuclear molding. Immunohistochemical staining of these cells is most likely to be positive for which of the following?","options":["Chromogranin A and synaptophysin","Calretinin and Wilms tumor 1 protein","Leukocyte common antigen (CD45) and CD20","p40 and high-molecular-weight cytokeratin"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-F8","obj":"PU-14-F.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize small cell carcinoma from the histology: small cells, scant cytoplasm, salt-and-pepper chromatin, and nuclear molding.","Recall its origin: neuroendocrine (Kulchitsky-type) cells with dense-core secretory granules.","Choose the neuroendocrine markers: chromogranin A and synaptophysin."],"why":"Small cell carcinoma is a high-grade neuroendocrine tumor. It arises centrally in heavy smokers, grows fast, and spreads early, so it is usually treated as a systemic disease. Its cells contain neurosecretory granules, so they stain for chromogranin A, synaptophysin, and often CD56. The same neuroendocrine nature explains its paraneoplastic hormone secretion, such as ADH and ACTH.","whyNot":["Correct.","Calretinin and WT1 mark mesothelial cells and mesothelioma.","CD45 and CD20 mark B-cell lymphoma, another tumor of small round cells. Small cell carcinoma is epithelial and negative for CD45.","p40 and high-molecular-weight cytokeratin mark squamous cell carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Small cell carcinoma histology","Neuroendocrine markers"],"comps":["PU-14-F8"]}},{"id":"PU-C25-Q2","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms most likely explains her serum sodium concentration?","options":["Bilateral adrenal metastases abolishing aldosterone secretion","Ectopic ACTH driving cortisol excess and mineralocorticoid activity","PTHrP-induced hypercalcemia impairing urine concentration","Ectopic vasopressin acting on renal V2 receptors"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-14-F8","obj":"PU-14-F.10","dok":3,"approach":["Read the labs: low serum sodium and osmolality with inappropriately concentrated urine in a euvolemic patient with normal thyroid and adrenal function. This is SIADH.","Match SIADH to the lung cancer type that makes it: small cell carcinoma.","Trace the mechanism: ADH binds V2 receptors on collecting duct cells, aquaporin-2 channels insert in the apical membrane, and free water is retained."],"why":"Small cell carcinoma can secrete ADH (vasopressin) without regard to plasma osmolality. ADH binds V2 receptors, raises cAMP, and inserts aquaporin-2 water channels in collecting duct cells. The kidney keeps reabsorbing water, so serum sodium is diluted while urine stays concentrated. Lung cancer paraneoplastic syndromes follow tumor type: SIADH, ACTH, and Lambert-Eaton with small cell; PTHrP hypercalcemia with squamous cell; and hypertrophic osteoarthropathy mostly with adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["Loss of aldosterone would cause hyperkalemia and volume depletion. Her potassium and cortisol are normal and she is euvolemic.","Ectopic ACTH, also from small cell carcinoma, raises cortisol and tends to cause hypokalemia and sodium retention, not hyponatremia. Her cortisol is normal.","PTHrP comes mainly from squamous cell carcinoma. Hypercalcemia causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus with dilute urine and a tendency to high sodium.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["SIADH","Paraneoplastic syndromes by lung cancer type"],"comps":["PU-14-F8","PU-14-F"]}},{"id":"PU-C25-Q3","stem":"Her tumor cells express a neural protein that also appears on her motor nerve terminals, and her immune response against the tumor cross-reacts with the nerves. Which of the following best explains her weakness?","options":["Antibodies to postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors","Antibodies to muscle-specific tyrosine kinase","Antibodies to presynaptic P/Q-type calcium channels","Antibodies to the neuronal nuclear antigen Hu"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-12","obj":"PU-12.7","dok":3,"approach":["Use the clinical pattern: proximal weakness and low reflexes that improve after brief exercise, plus dry mouth.","Place the lesion presynaptically: repeated firing builds up calcium in the nerve terminal and releases more acetylcholine.","Name the antibody: against voltage-gated P/Q-type calcium channels shared by small cell tumor cells and nerve terminals."],"why":"Small cell carcinoma cells express voltage-gated calcium channels. Antibodies made against the tumor also bind the P/Q-type channels on motor and autonomic nerve terminals. Less calcium enters with each action potential, so less acetylcholine is released, which causes proximal weakness, low reflexes, and dry mouth. Brief exercise lets calcium build up in the terminal, so strength and reflexes improve for a moment. Lung tumors can still escape these responses by lowering MHC class I and expressing PD-L1.","whyNot":["Antibodies to acetylcholine receptors cause myasthenia gravis. Weakness worsens with repeated use, and eye muscles are usually involved first.","Antibodies to muscle-specific kinase cause a form of myasthenia gravis with fatigable bulbar weakness.","Correct.","Anti-Hu antibodies with small cell carcinoma cause sensory neuronopathy and encephalomyelitis, not weakness that improves with effort."],"study":{"topics":["Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","Paraneoplastic neurologic antibodies"],"comps":["PU-12","PU-14-F8"]}},{"id":"PU-C25-Q4","stem":"Myasthenia gravis was considered before the diagnosis was made. If she had myasthenia gravis instead, chest imaging would most likely have shown which of the following?","options":["Middle mediastinal pericardial cyst","Anterior mediastinal thymoma","Posterior mediastinal neurogenic tumor","Middle mediastinal bronchogenic cyst"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-4","obj":"PU-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the mass linked to myasthenia gravis: thymoma, or thymic hyperplasia.","Place the thymus: anterior mediastinum, behind the sternum.","Match other masses to their compartments: neurogenic tumors posterior, cysts and lymph nodes middle."],"why":"The thymus lies in the anterior (and superior) mediastinum. About 10 to 15% of patients with myasthenia gravis have a thymoma, and many more have thymic hyperplasia. Typical anterior masses are thymoma, teratoma, lymphoma, and retrosternal thyroid. The middle mediastinum holds the heart, hila, and lymph nodes, and the posterior mediastinum holds the sympathetic chain and nerve roots, where neurogenic tumors arise.","whyNot":["Pericardial cysts lie in the middle mediastinum, often at the right cardiophrenic angle, and are usually incidental findings.","Correct.","Neurogenic tumors such as schwannoma and neuroblastoma arise from nerves in the posterior mediastinum. They are not linked to myasthenia gravis.","Bronchogenic cysts come from abnormal foregut budding and usually sit near the carina. They are not linked to myasthenia gravis."],"study":{"topics":["Mediastinal compartments and masses","Thymoma and myasthenia gravis"],"comps":["PU-4"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C26","system":"PU","dx":"Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung as a superior sulcus (Pancoast) tumor with PTHrP hypercalcemia","codes":["PU-14-F9"],"case":"A 67-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of severe right shoulder pain that radiates down the inner arm to the fourth and fifth fingers, with weakness of the right hand. He also has constipation, frequent urination, and mild confusion. He has smoked 2 packs of cigarettes daily for 30 years. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 90/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. The right hand intrinsic muscles are weak. Serum studies show calcium 13.2 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), phosphorus 2.1 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), and PTH 6 pg/mL (10 to 65). PTH-related protein is increased. CT shows a mass in the right lung apex invading the chest wall and first rib. Diagnosis: squamous cell carcinoma of the right superior sulcus (Pancoast tumor) with humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C26-Q1","stem":"Which of the following findings on his biopsy specimen would most strongly support squamous cell carcinoma over other non-small-cell carcinomas?","options":["Small cells with scant cytoplasm and nuclear molding","Lepidic spread along intact alveolar walls","Sheets of anaplastic cells lacking glandular or squamous features","Keratin pearls and intercellular bridges"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-F9","obj":"PU-14-F.9","dok":2,"approach":["List the histologic signs of squamous differentiation: keratin production and cell-to-cell junctions.","Match the other choices to adenocarcinoma in situ, small cell carcinoma, and large cell carcinoma.","Pick keratin pearls with intercellular bridges (desmosomes)."],"why":"Squamous cell carcinoma shows keratinization, including keratin pearls, and intercellular bridges, which are desmosomes seen between cells. It is strongly linked to smoking, usually arises centrally from metaplastic bronchial epithelium, may cavitate, and is the lung tumor most often associated with PTHrP hypercalcemia. Large cell carcinoma is a poorly differentiated peripheral tumor with no glandular or squamous features and a poor prognosis. Adenocarcinoma is the most common type overall and forms glands or mucin.","whyNot":["Small cells with nuclear molding are the hallmark of small cell carcinoma, which is not a non-small-cell carcinoma.","Lepidic growth along intact alveolar walls is a pattern of adenocarcinoma, as in adenocarcinoma in situ. It does not show squamous differentiation.","Undifferentiated sheets of large cells without glands or keratin describe large cell carcinoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of non-small-cell carcinomas","Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung"],"comps":["PU-14-F9","PU-14-F1"]}},{"id":"PU-C26-Q2","stem":"Two weeks later, he has drooping of the right upper eyelid, a small right pupil, and absent sweating on the right side of the face. Invasion of which of the following structures best explains these new findings?","options":["Right phrenic nerve","Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","Cervicothoracic sympathetic chain","Lower trunk of the brachial plexus"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-14-F9","obj":"PU-14-F.11","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize Horner syndrome: ptosis, miosis, and facial anhidrosis.","Recall the sympathetic path to the face: it rises from the upper thoracic cord through the sympathetic chain at the lung apex, including the stellate (cervicothoracic) ganglion.","Relate this to the apical tumor, which also explains his C8 to T1 pain and hand weakness through the lower trunk of the brachial plexus."],"why":"A superior sulcus (Pancoast) tumor grows at the lung apex, where the lower trunk of the brachial plexus, the subclavian vessels, and the cervicothoracic sympathetic chain lie close together. Invasion of the sympathetic chain cuts the pathway to the face and eye, producing ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis on the same side. Invasion of the lower trunk (C8 and T1) causes the pain along the inner arm and weakness of the hand muscles he already has. Most Pancoast tumors are non-small-cell carcinomas.","whyNot":["Phrenic nerve injury paralyzes the hemidiaphragm, which shows as elevation on a radiograph.","Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury causes hoarseness from vocal fold paralysis.","Correct.","The lower trunk of the brachial plexus explains his arm pain and hand weakness, but it carries no sympathetic fibers to the face or eye."],"study":{"topics":["Pancoast tumor and Horner syndrome","Local spread of lung cancer"],"comps":["PU-14-F9","PU-14-F"]}},{"id":"PU-C26-Q3","stem":"Benzo[a]pyrene from his cigarette smoke forms bulky adducts on guanine in his bronchial epithelial cells. Which of the following steps is required before benzo[a]pyrene can bind DNA?","options":["UDP-glucuronosyltransferase conjugation to a glucuronide","Cytochrome P450 oxidation to a diol epoxide","Fenton reaction producing hydroxyl radicals from iron","Glutathione S-transferase conjugation to a thioether"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-10","obj":"PU-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that benzo[a]pyrene is a procarcinogen, inert until it is metabolized.","Identify the activating step: cytochrome P450 enzymes (such as CYP1A1, which smoke induces) oxidize it to a reactive diol epoxide.","Separate activation from detoxification: conjugation reactions make compounds water soluble so they can be excreted."],"why":"Benzo[a]pyrene is a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon in tobacco smoke. Cytochrome P450 enzymes and epoxide hydrolase convert it to benzo[a]pyrene diol epoxide, an electrophile that binds guanine and forms bulky DNA adducts. If these are not repaired, they cause mutations, often G to T transversions, in genes such as TP53. Smoke induces CYP1A1, which speeds this activation.","whyNot":["Glucuronidation is a phase II detoxifying reaction that makes metabolites water soluble for excretion. It prevents DNA binding.","Correct.","The Fenton reaction makes hydroxyl radicals from hydrogen peroxide and ferrous iron. It causes oxidative damage but is not how benzo[a]pyrene is activated.","Glutathione conjugation detoxifies reactive epoxides so they can be excreted. It removes the DNA-binding form."],"study":{"topics":["Activation of benzo[a]pyrene","Phase I and phase II metabolism"],"comps":["PU-10"]}},{"id":"PU-C26-Q4","stem":"He also has severe COPD. For several months his PaCO2 has been about 58 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45) with an arterial pH of 7.37 (normal 7.35 to 7.45). Which of the following best explains why his central chemoreceptors now drive relatively little extra ventilation at this PaCO2?","options":["CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier too slowly to reach them","Added CSF bicarbonate has returned CSF pH toward normal","They respond mainly to PaO2, which is only mildly reduced","Chronic hypercapnia destroys the medullary chemosensitive neurons"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-6","obj":"PU-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what central chemoreceptors sense: hydrogen ion in the CSF, which forms when CO2 diffuses in from the blood.","Notice that his arterial pH is near normal, so the kidneys have compensated, and over 1 to 2 days bicarbonate also rises in the CSF.","Conclude that CSF pH has returned toward normal, so the medullary stimulus fades and his breathing depends more on hypoxemia sensed by the carotid bodies."],"why":"CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier easily and forms carbonic acid in the CSF, and the hydrogen ion stimulates chemosensitive neurons in the medulla. With chronic CO2 retention, renal bicarbonate retention and transport of bicarbonate into the CSF buffer this acid, so CSF pH moves back toward normal within 1 to 2 days. The central drive then falls far below its acute level. The carotid and aortic bodies respond mainly to PaO2 below about 60 mm Hg, so hypoxemia becomes a larger share of his drive to breathe.","whyNot":["CO2 is lipid soluble and crosses the blood-brain barrier rapidly. That is why central chemoreceptors respond to acute hypercapnia within minutes.","Correct.","Central chemoreceptors do not sense oxygen. Hypoxemia is detected by the carotid and aortic bodies.","Chronic hypercapnia does not destroy these neurons. Their response returns if CSF pH falls again."],"study":{"topics":["Central versus peripheral chemoreceptors","Chronic CO2 retention"],"comps":["PU-6","PU-14-B"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C27","system":"PU","dx":"Cystic fibrosis","codes":["PU-14-G1"],"case":"A 14-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a chronic productive cough and three episodes of pneumonia in the past 2 years. He passes bulky, greasy, foul-smelling stools. He had meconium ileus as a newborn. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg. Height and weight are at the 5th percentile. Examination shows digital clubbing, nasal polyps, and coarse crackles over both upper lung fields. Sweat chloride concentration is 84 mmol/L on two occasions (normal less than 30; 60 or greater is diagnostic). Sputum culture grows mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Genetic testing shows homozygosity for the F508del variant of CFTR. Diagnosis: cystic fibrosis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C27-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes how his variant reduces CFTR function?","options":["Misfolded protein held in the ER and degraded","Premature stop codon causing mRNA decay and no protein","Normal amount of surface protein with defective channel opening","Splicing defect reducing the amount of normal protein made"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-10","obj":"PU-10.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the variant: F508del, a three-base deletion that removes phenylalanine at position 508.","Recall what this does to the protein: it folds abnormally and fails ER quality control.","Predict the result: the protein is ubiquitinated and destroyed by proteasomes, so little CFTR reaches the apical membrane."],"why":"Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in CFTR on chromosome 7. CFTR is a cAMP-regulated, ATP-gated chloride channel. The most common variant, F508del, is an in-frame deletion of three nucleotides. The protein misfolds, is held in the endoplasmic reticulum, and is degraded by the proteasome. Little chloride is secreted onto the airway surface, so the mucus becomes dehydrated and thick.","whyNot":["Correct.","Premature stop codons, such as G542X, are a different class of CFTR mutation that produces no protein. F508del is an in-frame deletion, not a nonsense mutation.","Gating mutations, such as G551D, put protein on the cell surface but the channel does not open normally. F508del mainly prevents the protein from reaching the surface.","Splicing variants reduce the amount of normal protein. They are much less common than F508del and work by a different mechanism."],"study":{"topics":["CFTR mutation classes","Protein misfolding and ER quality control"],"comps":["PU-10","PU-14-G1"]}},{"id":"PU-C27-Q2","stem":"His sweat chloride is high because chloride cannot be reabsorbed from the lumen of the sweat duct. In his airways, the same defect has a different effect on the luminal fluid. Which of the following best explains his dehydrated airway surface liquid?","options":["Excess sodium secretion through apical CFTR channels","Increased bicarbonate secretion that draws water from the lumen","Reduced chloride secretion with increased ENaC sodium absorption","Reduced chloride reabsorption, which leaves salt in the airway lumen"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-14-G1","obj":"PU-14-G.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the normal role of CFTR in each tissue: in the sweat duct it reabsorbs chloride, and in the airway it secretes chloride.","Predict the airway result: no chloride secretion, and loss of CFTR's restraint on ENaC lets sodium absorption increase.","Follow the water: salt leaves the lumen and water follows it, so the airway surface liquid shrinks and mucus thickens."],"why":"CFTR moves chloride in opposite directions in different tissues. In the sweat duct, it reabsorbs chloride from the lumen, and sodium follows. Without it, salt stays in the sweat, so sweat is salty. In the airway, CFTR secretes chloride into the lumen, and it also normally restrains the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC). Without CFTR, chloride secretion stops and sodium absorption increases, so salt and water are pulled out of the airway surface liquid. Cilia cannot beat in the thin, dry layer, and mucus builds up.","whyNot":["CFTR is a chloride (and bicarbonate) channel. It does not secrete sodium, and in CF the problem is too much sodium absorption.","CFTR also carries bicarbonate, and in CF bicarbonate secretion falls. Secreted bicarbonate would draw water into the lumen, not out of it.","Correct.","Failed reabsorption is the defect in the sweat duct. In the airway it would leave more salt and water in the lumen, which is the opposite of what happens."],"study":{"topics":["CFTR in sweat duct versus airway","Airway surface liquid"],"comps":["PU-14-G1","PU-10"]}},{"id":"PU-C27-Q3","stem":"He asks about having children in the future. Which of the following findings is he most likely to have as an adult?","options":["Azoospermia with absent vasa deferentia","Low testosterone with small, firm testes","Anosmia with low gonadotropin concentrations","Immotile sperm from absent dynein arms"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-G1","obj":"PU-14-G.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that CFTR is needed for normal development of the wolffian duct structures.","Predict the finding: congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens, so sperm cannot leave the testes.","Note that testes and testosterone are normal, so this is obstructive azoospermia."],"why":"About 95% of men with cystic fibrosis are infertile because the vasa deferentia are absent or blocked by thick secretions in fetal life. Their testes make sperm and testosterone normally, so semen has no sperm (obstructive azoospermia). Sperm can be retrieved from the testis for assisted reproduction. Other features of CF include meconium ileus, pancreatic insufficiency with steatorrhea, chronic Pseudomonas and Staphylococcus aureus infection, bronchiectasis, and nasal polyps.","whyNot":["Correct.","Small, firm testes with low testosterone suggest Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY), which is a testicular failure.","Anosmia with low gonadotropins is Kallmann syndrome, from failed migration of GnRH neurons.","Immotile sperm from missing dynein arms is primary ciliary dyskinesia. It also causes chronic sinus and lung infection, but sweat chloride is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Clinical features of cystic fibrosis","CF versus primary ciliary dyskinesia"],"comps":["PU-14-G1","PU-14-B2"]}},{"id":"PU-C27-Q4","stem":"Despite pancreatic enzyme replacement, he has trouble seeing at night, and his conjunctivae are dry with small foamy gray patches. Deficiency of this vitamin most likely contributes to his recurrent pulmonary infections through which of the following effects?","options":["Defective hydroxylation of proline residues in collagen","Impaired gamma-carboxylation of hepatic clotting factors","Squamous metaplasia of ciliated airway epithelium","Oxidative damage to neuronal and erythrocyte membranes"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-9","obj":"PU-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the vitamin: night blindness and Bitot spots point to vitamin A deficiency.","Explain why he lacks it: pancreatic insufficiency causes fat malabsorption, so fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K are poorly absorbed.","Recall the airway effect: without retinoic acid, ciliated and mucus-secreting epithelium turns into keratinizing squamous epithelium, which weakens mucociliary clearance."],"why":"Vitamin A, as retinoic acid, keeps specialized epithelia differentiated. In deficiency, the ciliated columnar epithelium of the airways undergoes squamous metaplasia and loses cilia and goblet cells, so mucociliary clearance fails and infections rise. In the eye, the same change causes xerophthalmia and Bitot spots, and too little rhodopsin forms in the rods, which causes night blindness. In cystic fibrosis, pancreatic insufficiency causes fat malabsorption and deficiency of all the fat-soluble vitamins.","whyNot":["Collagen proline hydroxylation needs vitamin C, which is water soluble and not lost through fat malabsorption.","Gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors needs vitamin K. Deficiency causes bleeding, not night blindness or infection.","Correct.","Membrane protection from oxidants needs vitamin E. Deficiency causes neuropathy, ataxia, and hemolysis."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin A and epithelial differentiation","Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency in CF"],"comps":["PU-9","PU-14-G1"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C28","system":"PU","dx":"Esophageal atresia with distal tracheoesophageal fistula","codes":["PU-14-G2"],"case":"A male newborn is evaluated in the nursery 3 hours after birth at 36 weeks' gestation because of excessive drooling and an episode of coughing, choking, and cyanosis during his first feed. The pregnancy was complicated by polyhydramnios. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 156/min, respirations are 58/min, and blood pressure is 64/40 mm Hg. Frothy secretions are present at the mouth. A nasogastric tube cannot be passed beyond 10 cm from the nostril. Chest and abdominal radiographs show the tube coiled in an upper esophageal pouch and air in the stomach and bowel. Diagnosis: esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C28-Q1","stem":"Before surgical repair, a careful examination is done to look for associated anomalies. Which of the following is most likely to be found?","options":["Macroglossia with an umbilical hernia","Bilateral cleft lip and palate","Hypertrophy of the pyloric muscle","Imperforate anus"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-G2","obj":"PU-14-G.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula is part of the VACTERL association.","List the components: vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, and limb (radial) anomalies.","Pick the option that belongs to this group: imperforate anus."],"why":"Esophageal atresia with a distal fistula is the most common form of tracheoesophageal fistula. The fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid into the gut, so polyhydramnios develops. After birth, the baby drools and chokes with feeds, the tube stops in the blind pouch, and air reaches the stomach through the distal fistula. About half of these infants have other anomalies, often from the VACTERL group, so the anus, spine, heart, kidneys, and radial limbs are checked.","whyNot":["Macroglossia with an umbilical hernia suggests Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, which is not part of VACTERL.","Cleft lip and palate come from failed fusion of facial processes. They are not part of VACTERL.","Pyloric stenosis presents at 2 to 6 weeks with projectile vomiting and is not part of VACTERL.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tracheoesophageal fistula types","VACTERL association"],"comps":["PU-14-G2","PU-14-G"]}},{"id":"PU-C28-Q2","stem":"Which of the following embryologic errors most likely caused his anomaly?","options":["Incomplete closure of the pleuroperitoneal canals","Faulty fusion of the tracheoesophageal ridges","Persistent communication of the vitelline duct","Failed recanalization of the second part of the duodenum"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-1","obj":"PU-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how the trachea and esophagus separate: tracheoesophageal ridges grow in and fuse to form the tracheoesophageal septum.","Predict the effect of faulty septation: the septum deviates posteriorly, the esophagus ends in a blind pouch, and the lower esophagus stays connected to the trachea.","Rule out errors in other foregut or midgut structures."],"why":"In weeks 4 and 5, the lung bud and foregut are partly separated by tracheoesophageal ridges, which fuse to form the tracheoesophageal septum. The trachea and lung bud lie ventral and the esophagus dorsal. If the septum deviates posteriorly or forms incompletely, the upper esophagus ends blindly and the lower esophagus stays connected to the trachea. This is esophageal atresia with a distal fistula.","whyNot":["Incomplete closure of the pleuroperitoneal canals causes a posterolateral congenital diaphragmatic hernia.","Correct.","A persistent vitelline duct causes a Meckel diverticulum or an umbilical fistula, not an airway connection.","Failed recanalization of the duodenum causes duodenal atresia with a double bubble sign and bilious vomiting, which is often seen with Down syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Tracheoesophageal septum","Foregut development"],"comps":["PU-1","PU-14-G2"]}},{"id":"PU-C28-Q3","stem":"Chest CT before surgery shows an incidental 3-cm mass of lung tissue in the posterior basal segment of the left lower lobe. It is supplied by an artery that arises from the descending thoracic aorta and has no connection to the bronchial tree. Which of the following is this lesion most likely to cause later in childhood?","options":["Stridor from compression of the trachea","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy","Paradoxical embolic stroke","Recurrent pneumonia in the lesion"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-14-G","obj":"PU-14-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion: lung tissue with a systemic arterial supply from the aorta and no bronchial connection is a pulmonary sequestration.","Separate it from a bronchogenic cyst, which is usually mediastinal, fluid filled, and has no aortic feeding vessel.","Predict the problem: tissue that cannot clear secretions through a bronchus becomes infected again and again."],"why":"Pulmonary sequestration is a mass of nonfunctioning lung tissue that develops from an accessory foregut bud. It has no normal connection to the airways and gets its blood from a systemic artery, usually from the aorta. Intralobar sequestration, which lies within a lower lobe, often becomes infected through small pores, and secretions cannot drain, so recurrent pneumonia in the same spot is typical. Bronchogenic cysts also come from abnormal foregut budding but are usually fluid-filled cysts in the mediastinum that may compress the airway.","whyNot":["Tracheal compression with stridor fits a mediastinal bronchogenic cyst near the carina, not a lesion in the lower lobe.","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy is mainly a paraneoplastic finding with lung cancer, especially adenocarcinoma.","Paradoxical embolism needs a right-to-left shunt, such as a pulmonary arteriovenous malformation. A sequestration is fed by systemic arterial blood and does not shunt venous blood to the systemic side.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary sequestration","Bronchogenic cyst"],"comps":["PU-14-G","PU-1"]}},{"id":"PU-C28-Q4","stem":"While awaiting surgery, he cries forcefully and gastric contents reflux through the distal fistula into his trachea. Within 2 hours, he becomes tachypneic and hypoxemic, and a chest radiograph shows new infiltrates in the right upper and both lower lung zones. Which of the following best explains this early lung injury?","options":["Infection with aspirated oral anaerobic bacteria","Hospital-acquired MRSA pneumonia","Chemical injury from acidic gastric contents","Transient tachypnea from retained fetal lung fluid"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-14-H","obj":"PU-14-H.12","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: lung injury within hours of aspirating gastric contents.","Separate chemical pneumonitis, which begins within hours from acid injury, from bacterial aspiration pneumonia, which develops over days.","Use the setting: hospital-acquired infections such as MRSA need about 48 hours or more in hospital."],"why":"Gastric acid that reaches the airways injures the alveolar epithelium and capillaries within minutes to hours. This chemical pneumonitis causes rapid hypoxemia and infiltrates in dependent lung segments, before bacteria have time to multiply. Bacterial aspiration pneumonia, from oral anaerobes and streptococci, develops over days and can form an abscess. Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia, after 48 hours or more, are usually caused by Pseudomonas, MRSA, and enteric gram-negative rods.","whyNot":["Bacterial aspiration pneumonia from oral anaerobes develops over days. A newborn also has little oral anaerobic flora.","Hospital-acquired pneumonia is defined as starting 48 hours or more after admission. Bacterial infection would not cause infiltrates within 2 hours.","Correct.","Transient tachypnea of the newborn starts at birth and clears in 1 to 2 days. It does not appear suddenly after a reflux event."],"study":{"topics":["Aspiration pneumonitis versus aspiration pneumonia","Hospital-acquired pneumonia organisms"],"comps":["PU-14-H","PU-14-H6"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C29","system":"PU","dx":"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia with pulmonary hypoplasia","codes":["PU-14-G"],"case":"A female newborn delivered at 39 weeks' gestation has severe respiratory distress and cyanosis within minutes of birth. Temperature is 36.6 °C (97.9 °F), pulse is 170/min, respirations are 72/min, and blood pressure is 52/30 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 92% in the right hand and 75% in the right foot on 100% oxygen. The abdomen is sunken. Breath sounds are absent over the left chest, and heart sounds are loudest to the right of the sternum. A chest radiograph shows gas-filled bowel loops in the left hemithorax, a small left lung, and shift of the mediastinum to the right. Echocardiography shows right-to-left shunting across the ductus arteriosus. Diagnosis: congenital diaphragmatic hernia with pulmonary hypoplasia.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C29-Q1","stem":"Her defect is in the posterolateral part of the left hemidiaphragm. Which of the following developmental errors most likely caused it?","options":["Failed closure of the left pleuroperitoneal membrane","Failed muscularization of an intact diaphragmatic dome","Widening of the esophageal hiatus around the stomach","Defect between the sternal and costal origins of the diaphragm"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-G","obj":"PU-14-G.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the parts that form the diaphragm: septum transversum, pleuroperitoneal membranes, dorsal mesentery of the esophagus, and body wall muscle.","Match the posterolateral location to the pleuroperitoneal membranes, which close the pleuroperitoneal canals.","Note why the left side is affected most: the left canal closes later than the right."],"why":"Most congenital diaphragmatic hernias are posterolateral (Bochdalek) defects, about 85% on the left. They occur when a pleuroperitoneal membrane fails to close its pleuroperitoneal canal. Abdominal organs move into the chest while the lungs are growing, compress the lung on that side, and push the mediastinum away. The abdomen looks sunken (scaphoid) because the bowel is in the chest.","whyNot":["Correct.","Failed muscularization leaves a thin but intact membrane, which is eventration of the diaphragm. Bowel stays below the membrane.","Widening of the esophageal hiatus is a hiatal hernia, an acquired condition usually seen in adults.","A defect between the sternal and costal origins is an anterior (Morgagni) hernia. It is less common and usually on the right."],"study":{"topics":["Development of the diaphragm","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia"],"comps":["PU-14-G","PU-1"]}},{"id":"PU-C29-Q2","stem":"In the same nursery, a newborn boy with bilateral renal agenesis has a similar degree of respiratory failure from small lungs. Which of the following is the shared mechanism of lung injury in these two infants?","options":["Restricted lung expansion during fetal development","Deficient surfactant from immature type II pneumocytes","Infection of the amniotic fluid before birth","Aspiration of meconium-stained fluid during delivery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-1","obj":"PU-1.5","dok":3,"approach":["Explain the boy's lungs: no kidneys means no fetal urine, so oligohydramnios develops and the uterus compresses the chest.","Explain the girl's lungs: abdominal organs in the chest take up the space the lung needs.","Find the common factor: the fetal lungs cannot expand with fetal breathing and lung fluid, so too few airways and alveoli form, which leads to respiratory failure at birth."],"why":"Fetal lungs need room to grow and need to be stretched by lung fluid and fetal breathing movements. In bilateral renal agenesis, no urine means oligohydramnios, so the chest is compressed and lung fluid is lost (the Potter sequence). In diaphragmatic hernia, bowel in the chest compresses the lung. Both cause pulmonary hypoplasia with fewer airway branches, alveoli, and vessels, so the newborn cannot exchange gas well even with normal surfactant.","whyNot":["Correct.","Surfactant deficiency causes respiratory distress in preterm infants. Both of these infants have lungs that are too small, which is a different problem.","Chorioamnionitis can cause neonatal pneumonia and sepsis, but it does not explain underdeveloped lungs in both infants.","Meconium aspiration occurs around birth in post-term or stressed infants and does not explain small lungs."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary hypoplasia","Potter sequence"],"comps":["PU-1","PU-14-G"]}},{"id":"PU-C29-Q3","stem":"Her hypoplastic left lung stays at a very low volume despite ventilation. Apart from any effect of low oxygen, which of the following best explains the high vascular resistance in that lung at this volume?","options":["Recruitment and distension of previously closed capillaries","Compression of alveolar capillaries by distended alveoli","Greater radial traction pulling open the larger vessels","Extra-alveolar vessel narrowing from lost radial traction"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-5","obj":"PU-5.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that pulmonary vascular resistance is lowest near functional residual capacity.","Separate the two vessel types: extra-alveolar vessels are held open by the pull of surrounding tissue, and alveolar capillaries are squeezed by expanding alveoli.","Apply to low volume: the tissue pull is lost, extra-alveolar vessels narrow, and resistance rises."],"why":"Pulmonary vascular resistance follows a U-shaped curve with lung volume, lowest near FRC. At low lung volumes, the lung tissue does not pull outward on the larger extra-alveolar vessels, so they narrow and resistance rises. At high lung volumes, the stretched alveoli compress the capillaries in their walls, which also raises resistance. Her small, poorly inflated lung sits on the low-volume side of this curve.","whyNot":["Recruitment and distension of capillaries lower resistance, as happens when pulmonary blood flow rises during exercise.","Compression of alveolar capillaries by stretched alveoli raises resistance at high lung volumes, not at low ones.","Radial traction increases as the lung inflates. At low volume it is reduced, so this choice describes the wrong direction.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary vascular resistance and lung volume","Alveolar versus extra-alveolar vessels"],"comps":["PU-5"]}},{"id":"PU-C29-Q4","stem":"Her oxygen saturation is higher in the right hand than in the foot because her pulmonary artery pressure exceeds aortic pressure. In addition to her small vascular bed, which of the following mechanisms in her lungs most likely raises the pulmonary arterial pressure?","options":["Increased prostacyclin synthesis by pulmonary endothelium","Excess endothelial nitric oxide release in the pulmonary bed","Hypoxia-induced dilation of pulmonary arterioles via KATP channels","Hypoxic constriction of pulmonary arteriolar smooth muscle"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-5","obj":"PU-5.2","dok":3,"approach":["Read the saturation gap: blood in the foot gets deoxygenated blood shunted right to left through the ductus, so pulmonary pressure is high.","Recall how pulmonary vessels respond to low alveolar oxygen: they constrict, the opposite of systemic arterioles.","Link poorly ventilated, hypoxic lung to widespread vasoconstriction and persistent pulmonary hypertension."],"why":"In the lung, low alveolar oxygen constricts the small pulmonary arteries. This normally shifts blood away from poorly ventilated areas. When much of the lung is hypoxic, the constriction is widespread and raises pulmonary artery pressure. In this newborn, the high pressure keeps fetal pathways open, so deoxygenated blood crosses the ductus arteriosus into the descending aorta and the foot saturation is lower than the hand. Over time, chronic alveolar hypoxia from lung disease causes pulmonary hypertension and right ventricular hypertrophy (cor pulmonale). Systemic arterioles do the opposite and dilate with hypoxia.","whyNot":["Prostacyclin is a pulmonary vasodilator. More of it would lower pulmonary pressure.","Nitric oxide relaxes pulmonary vessels. That is why inhaled nitric oxide is used to lower pulmonary pressure in newborns.","Hypoxic dilation through KATP channels is the response of systemic arterioles, such as coronary and skeletal muscle vessels, not pulmonary ones.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn"],"comps":["PU-5","PU-14-G"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C30","system":"PU","dx":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae atypical pneumonia","codes":["PU-14-H1"],"case":"A 19-year-old woman in basic military training comes to the clinic because of a 10-day history of headache, sore throat, malaise, and a persistent dry cough. Several recruits in her barracks have had similar symptoms. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg. She does not appear acutely ill. A few scattered crackles are heard at both lung bases. A chest radiograph shows patchy bilateral interstitial infiltrates that are more extensive than the physical findings suggest. Arterial blood gas analysis at sea level on room air shows pH 7.46 (7.35 to 7.45), PaCO2 32 mm Hg (35 to 45), and PaO2 64 mm Hg (75 to 100). Sputum Gram stain shows a few neutrophils and no organisms. PCR of a throat swab detects Mycoplasma pneumoniae. Diagnosis: Mycoplasma pneumoniae pneumonia.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C30-Q1","stem":"Two weeks later, she has fatigue, pallor, and dark urine, and her fingertips turn blue after outdoor drills in cold weather. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.6 g/dL (12 to 16), and the direct antiglobulin test is positive for C3 only. Which of the following best explains her anemia?","options":["Warm-reactive IgG against erythrocyte Rh antigens","Cold-reactive IgM to I antigen fixing complement","Shearing of erythrocytes by microthrombi in arterioles","Complement lysis from loss of CD55 and CD59 anchors"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-H1","obj":"PU-14-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Link the clues: hemolysis with symptoms in the cold and a direct antiglobulin test positive for complement only.","Recall the antibody that Mycoplasma pneumoniae induces: cold agglutinins, which are IgM against the I antigen on red cells.","Explain the C3 result: IgM binds in the cool periphery, fixes complement, and then falls off as blood warms, leaving C3 on the cell."],"why":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae often stimulates IgM antibodies that cross-react with the I antigen on red cells. These cold agglutinins bind in cool fingers and toes, fix complement, and then detach in the warm core, so the antiglobulin test shows C3 but no IgG. Complement causes hemolysis, mostly through liver macrophages, and sometimes directly in vessels. Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall, so it does not appear on Gram stain and is not killed by beta-lactam antibiotics. Its pneumonia is typically mild in young adults living in close quarters, and the radiograph looks worse than the patient.","whyNot":["Warm IgG autoantibodies cause warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, with a direct antiglobulin test positive for IgG. Symptoms do not depend on cold.","Correct.","Microangiopathic hemolysis shows schistocytes and a negative direct antiglobulin test, as in TTP or hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Loss of CD55 and CD59 is paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria. The direct antiglobulin test is negative."],"study":{"topics":["Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Cold agglutinin hemolysis"],"comps":["PU-14-H1","PU-14-H"]}},{"id":"PU-C30-Q2","stem":"She is given 40% oxygen by face mask, and her PaO2 rises to 150 mm Hg. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of her hypoxemia on room air?","options":["Reduced inspired oxygen tension","Ventilation-perfusion mismatch","Alveolar hypoventilation","Right-to-left intrapulmonary shunt"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-7","obj":"PU-7.2","dok":3,"approach":["Rule out low inspired oxygen: she is at sea level on room air.","Rule out hypoventilation: her PaCO2 is low, so she is breathing more, not less.","Use the oxygen response: a large rise in PaO2 with supplemental oxygen fits ventilation-perfusion mismatch, while a true shunt barely responds."],"why":"Patchy inflammation in pneumonia leaves some alveoli poorly ventilated but still perfused, which is ventilation-perfusion mismatch. These low V/Q units deliver poorly oxygenated blood, and the A-a gradient widens. Extra inspired oxygen raises the alveolar PO2 even in poorly ventilated units, so PaO2 rises sharply. In a true shunt, blood passes alveoli that receive no gas at all, so added oxygen cannot reach it and PaO2 barely changes. Hypoventilation and low inspired oxygen both keep the A-a gradient normal.","whyNot":["Low inspired oxygen occurs at high altitude or with a faulty gas supply. She is at sea level breathing room air.","Correct.","Hypoventilation raises PaCO2. Her PaCO2 is low.","A right-to-left shunt sends blood past completely unventilated alveoli, so supplemental oxygen raises PaO2 very little."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of hypoxemia","Response to supplemental oxygen"],"comps":["PU-7","PU-8"]}},{"id":"PU-C30-Q3","stem":"A second recruit returns from 2 weeks of leave on her family's sheep farm, where she helped deliver lambs. She develops high fever, severe headache, dry cough, and patchy infiltrates with a normal mediastinal width on chest radiograph. Serum ALT concentration is 160 U/L (10 to 40). Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause?","options":["Francisella tularensis","Bacillus anthracis","Coxiella burnetii","Burkholderia pseudomallei"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-14-H","obj":"PU-14-H.14","dok":2,"approach":["Pick out the exposure: contact with birth products of sheep.","Match it to the organism that is concentrated in placentas and birth fluids of farm animals: Coxiella burnetii, the cause of Q fever.","Confirm with the picture: atypical pneumonia with hepatitis and a normal mediastinum."],"why":"Coxiella burnetii causes Q fever. Infected sheep, goats, and cattle shed huge numbers of organisms in placentas and birth fluids, and people inhale contaminated dust or aerosols. Acute Q fever causes fever, headache, atypical pneumonia, and hepatitis. Coxiella forms a spore-like form that survives in the environment, and it does not need an arthropod vector to infect people.","whyNot":["Francisella tularensis is acquired from rabbits, ticks, or deer flies and causes tularemia, usually with an ulcer and swollen lymph nodes.","Bacillus anthracis is acquired from spores in wool or hides. Inhalational anthrax causes hemorrhagic mediastinitis with a widened mediastinum, and her mediastinum is normal.","Correct.","Burkholderia pseudomallei lives in soil and water in Southeast Asia and northern Australia and causes melioidosis."],"study":{"topics":["Zoonotic pneumonias","Q fever"],"comps":["PU-14-H"]}},{"id":"PU-C30-Q4","stem":"Using her room-air blood gas values at sea level (barometric pressure 760 mm Hg, water vapor pressure 47 mm Hg, respiratory quotient 0.8), which of the following is closest to her alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient?","options":["About 46 mm Hg","About 86 mm Hg","About 10 mm Hg","About 54 mm Hg"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-7","obj":"PU-7.1","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate PAO2: 0.21 times (760 minus 47) minus 32/0.8 = 149.7 minus 40 = about 110 mm Hg.","Subtract PaO2: 110 minus 64 = about 46 mm Hg.","Interpret: a normal gradient for a young adult is about 5 to 15 mm Hg, so hers is widened, which points to a problem in the lung itself."],"why":"The alveolar gas equation is PAO2 = FiO2 times (Patm minus 47) minus PaCO2/0.8. For her, 0.21 times 713 is about 150, and 32/0.8 is 40, so PAO2 is about 110 mm Hg. Subtracting her PaO2 of 64 gives an A-a gradient of about 46 mm Hg. A widened gradient means oxygen is not moving normally from alveoli into blood, as with V/Q mismatch, shunt, or diffusion limitation. Hypoventilation and high altitude lower PaO2 with a normal gradient.","whyNot":["Correct.","About 86 mm Hg comes from leaving out the PaCO2 term entirely.","About 10 mm Hg is a normal gradient. Her low PaO2 together with a low PaCO2 cannot give a normal gradient.","About 54 mm Hg comes from subtracting PaCO2 itself instead of PaCO2 divided by 0.8."],"study":{"topics":["Alveolar gas equation","A-a gradient"],"comps":["PU-7"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C31","system":"PU","dx":"Staphylococcus aureus bronchopneumonia after influenza","codes":["PU-14-H2"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of high fever, cough productive of purulent, blood-streaked sputum, and shortness of breath for 2 days. One week ago she had abrupt fever, myalgias, headache, and dry cough, and a nasopharyngeal swab was positive for influenza A. She improved for 2 days and then worsened. She did not receive an influenza vaccine this season. Her temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 88% on room air. Crackles are heard over both lungs. Leukocyte count is 17,800/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000) with 88% neutrophils. A chest x-ray shows patchy infiltrates in several lobes of both lungs with small thin-walled cavities. Sputum Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters, and culture grows coagulase-positive Staphylococcus aureus. The diagnosis is Staphylococcus aureus bronchopneumonia complicating influenza.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C31-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her recent influenza infection predisposed her to this bacterial pneumonia?","options":["Consumption of serum complement by viral immune complexes","Necrosis of ciliated airway epithelium impairing clearance","Selective depletion of CD4+ T cells in bronchial lymphoid tissue","Suppression of IgA class switching in mucosal plasma cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-14-H2","obj":"PU-14-H.13","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the timing: influenza, a brief improvement, then a new high fever with purulent sputum. This is the classic pattern of secondary bacterial pneumonia.","Influenza replicates in and kills ciliated columnar cells of the trachea and bronchi.","Without cilia, the mucociliary escalator stops working, so bacteria from the upper airway reach and colonize the lower airways."],"why":"Influenza virus infects the ciliated epithelium of the respiratory tract and causes necrosis and desquamation of these cells. Loss of ciliary clearance lets inhaled or aspirated bacteria stay in the lower airways. Viral damage also exposes basement membrane and other attachment sites, and the virus temporarily dampens alveolar macrophage and neutrophil function. Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pneumoniae are the classic secondary invaders, usually appearing several days into the illness or after brief improvement. Influenza A itself is a public health concern because avian and human strains can reassort in a shared host and produce a novel strain.","whyNot":["Influenza does not cause clinically important complement depletion. Complement consumption by immune complexes is seen in disorders such as lupus and poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis.","Correct.","Selective CD4+ T-cell depletion is the hallmark of HIV infection. It predisposes to Pneumocystis and other opportunists, which is a different pattern from acute post-influenza staphylococcal pneumonia.","Influenza does not block IgA class switching. A primary failure of IgA production is selective IgA deficiency, an inherited disorder with recurrent sinopulmonary infections over years."],"study":{"topics":["Secondary bacterial pneumonia after influenza","Mucociliary clearance"],"comps":["PU-14-H2","PU-13","PU-2"]}},{"id":"PU-C31-Q2","stem":"Which of the following organisms also characteristically causes this pattern of lung involvement?","options":["Chlamydophila pneumoniae","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Coxiella burnetii"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-H2","obj":"PU-14-H.1","dok":3,"approach":["Classify the pattern first: patchy infiltrates scattered through several lobes of both lungs fit bronchopneumonia, which is centered on bronchioles.","Lobar pneumonia fills an entire lobe uniformly, and atypical pneumonia is interstitial.","Match the pattern to organisms: Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa classically cause bronchopneumonia."],"why":"Bronchopneumonia begins in the bronchioles and spreads to the surrounding alveoli. The result is patchy foci of neutrophilic consolidation, often in several lobes and often bilateral and basal. Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa are typical causes. Pseudomonas bronchopneumonia is classic in hospitalized, ventilated, or cystic fibrosis patients. Staphylococcus aureus can also form abscesses and thin-walled pneumatoceles, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Chlamydophila (Chlamydia) pneumoniae also causes atypical interstitial pneumonia, usually a mild illness with patchy septal inflammation and no purulent alveolar exudate.","Mycoplasma pneumoniae causes atypical pneumonia with an interstitial, mononuclear infiltrate in the alveolar septa and little alveolar exudate.","Correct.","Coxiella burnetii, the cause of Q fever, is inhaled in dust from infected farm animals and their birth products. It causes atypical interstitial pneumonia, often with hepatitis, not patchy purulent bronchopneumonia."],"study":{"topics":["Bronchopneumonia versus lobar pneumonia","Causes of bronchopneumonia"],"comps":["PU-14-H2","PU-14-H5","PU-14-H1"]}},{"id":"PU-C31-Q3","stem":"She asks why a new influenza vaccine is needed every year even though the same influenza A subtype has circulated for several seasons. Which of the following properties of the virus best explains the need for annual reformulation?","options":["Gene conversion among silent antigen gene copies","Phase variation in expression of surface proteins","Reassortment of genome segments with avian strains","Point mutations in surface glycoprotein genes"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.5","dok":2,"approach":["Same subtype, gradual change each year: that describes antigenic drift.","Influenza is a segmented negative-sense RNA virus whose RNA-dependent RNA polymerase has no proofreading.","Point mutations accumulate in the hemagglutinin and neuraminidase genes and change the antibody-binding sites a little each season."],"why":"Influenza A has eight segments of negative-sense RNA. Its RNA polymerase makes frequent copying errors, so point mutations accumulate in the genes for hemagglutinin (which binds sialic acid on airway cells) and neuraminidase (which releases new virions). Small changes in these surface proteins let the virus partly escape antibodies from earlier infection or vaccination. This antigenic drift causes yearly epidemics within the same subtype and is why the vaccine is updated every season.","whyNot":["Gene conversion from silent gene copies drives antigenic variation in organisms such as Neisseria gonorrhoeae pilin and Trypanosoma brucei. Influenza does not carry silent antigen genes.","Phase variation, switching a surface protein on or off, is a bacterial strategy, for example with Neisseria pili and opacity proteins.","Reassortment of whole segments between human and animal strains is antigenic shift. It creates a new subtype, can cause pandemics, and happens only occasionally, so it does not explain yearly change within one subtype.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Antigenic drift and shift","Influenza virus structure"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H"]}},{"id":"PU-C31-Q4","stem":"As her blood passes through the pulmonary capillaries and hemoglobin binds oxygen, which of the following changes in the red cell most directly helps her eliminate carbon dioxide?","options":["Increased carbamino binding of CO2 to globin chains","Movement of chloride from plasma into the red cell","Increased binding of 2,3-BPG to deoxyhemoglobin","H+ release from hemoglobin, converting HCO3- to CO2"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-8","obj":"PU-8.4","dok":2,"approach":["Oxygen binding in the lung is the setting for the Haldane effect.","Oxyhemoglobin holds H+ and CO2 less tightly than deoxyhemoglobin.","Released H+ combines with bicarbonate to form carbonic acid, and carbonic anhydrase turns it into CO2 and water. The CO2 diffuses into the alveoli."],"why":"The Haldane effect states that oxygenation of hemoglobin lowers its affinity for CO2 and H+. In the lungs, hemoglobin releases H+ as it binds O2. The H+ combines with bicarbonate that has re-entered the red cell, and carbonic anhydrase converts the carbonic acid to CO2, which diffuses out to be exhaled. Carbamino CO2 is also released from oxyhemoglobin. The Bohr effect is the mirror image in the tissues: high CO2 and H+ lower hemoglobin's O2 affinity and help unload O2.","whyNot":["Carbamino binding increases in the tissues, where hemoglobin is deoxygenated. In the lungs oxygenation lowers carbamino binding and CO2 is released.","Chloride enters red cells in the systemic capillaries. In the lungs the chloride shift reverses: bicarbonate enters the red cell and chloride leaves.","2,3-BPG binds deoxyhemoglobin and shifts the curve right. Its binding falls as hemoglobin is oxygenated, and it does not carry CO2.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Haldane effect","Bohr effect"],"comps":["PU-8","PU-7"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C32","system":"PU","dx":"Diphtheria","codes":["PU-14-H3"],"case":"A 7-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of sore throat, malaise, and painful neck swelling for 3 days. His parents declined all childhood vaccinations. His temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. Examination shows a gray-white membrane covering both tonsils and the posterior pharyngeal wall; it is firmly adherent and bleeds when scraped. There is marked cervical lymphadenopathy with diffuse soft tissue swelling of the anterior neck. Gram stain of material from beneath the membrane shows gram-positive club-shaped rods. Culture on tellurite agar grows gray-black colonies, and an Elek immunodiffusion test of the isolate is positive. The diagnosis is respiratory diphtheria.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C32-Q1","stem":"Three weeks later, his throat has healed, but his speech is nasal and liquids come out of his nose when he swallows. The soft palate does not elevate during phonation. Which of the following best explains these findings?","options":["Autoantibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors","Toxin-mediated demyelination of cranial nerves","Retropharyngeal abscess compressing the vagus nerve","Enteroviral destruction of brainstem motor neurons"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-H3","obj":"PU-14-H.3","dok":3,"approach":["Palatal paralysis a few weeks after diphtheria is the classic first neurologic complication.","Diphtheria toxin travels in the blood and injures tissues away from the throat, mainly heart and nerves.","In nerves the toxin damages Schwann cells, causing demyelination that starts with the cranial nerves to the palate and pharynx."],"why":"Diphtheria toxin is absorbed from the pharynx into the blood. Cells that take it up stop making protein and die. In peripheral nerves, injury to Schwann cells causes demyelinating neuropathy. Palatal paralysis is typically the first sign, in the second or third week, and may be followed by paralysis of eye movement or accommodation and later by a peripheral polyneuropathy. Myocarditis with conduction block is the other major toxin-mediated complication, usually about 1 to 2 weeks into the illness, and it is the main cause of death.","whyNot":["Acetylcholine receptor antibodies cause myasthenia gravis, which produces fatigable weakness, often with ptosis and diplopia. It is not a complication of diphtheria.","Correct.","A retropharyngeal abscess causes fever, neck stiffness, and painful swallowing with a bulging posterior pharyngeal wall. His throat has healed, and the timing fits a toxin effect.","Poliovirus, an enterovirus, can destroy brainstem motor neurons in bulbar polio. He has no history of a new febrile viral illness, and the setting points to diphtheria toxin."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of diphtheria","Diphtheritic neuropathy and myocarditis"],"comps":["PU-14-H3","PU-13"]}},{"id":"PU-C32-Q2","stem":"A nontoxigenic strain of the same species is later isolated from a classmate's throat and causes only mild pharyngitis. The positive Elek test in this patient indicates production of a toxin that kills pharyngeal epithelial cells by which of the following mechanisms?","options":["Proteolytic cleavage of SNARE proteins","ADP-ribosylation of the Gi alpha subunit","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2","Cleavage of 28S ribosomal RNA"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.3","dok":2,"approach":["A strain that lacks the toxin causes only mild disease, so the toxin causes the membrane and the systemic damage.","The tox gene is carried by a lysogenic beta-prophage, so only lysogenized strains are toxigenic.","The toxin's A subunit ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2, halting protein synthesis and killing the cell."],"why":"Diphtheria toxin is an A-B exotoxin encoded by a gene carried on a beta-prophage. The B subunit binds a receptor on host cells and allows entry. The A subunit then ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2, which stops translation. Local epithelial death plus fibrin, neutrophils, and bacteria forms the adherent pseudomembrane. Absorbed toxin damages heart and nerves. The Elek test detects toxin production by a precipitin line between the isolate and antitoxin.","whyNot":["Cleavage of SNARE proteins is the action of tetanus and botulinum toxins, which block neurotransmitter release rather than kill epithelial cells.","ADP-ribosylation of Gi is the action of pertussis toxin. It raises cAMP and causes lymphocytosis, but it does not stop protein synthesis.","Correct.","Removing an adenine from 28S ribosomal RNA of the 60S subunit is how Shiga toxin and Shiga-like toxin stop protein synthesis. Those toxins come from Shigella and enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli."],"study":{"topics":["Diphtheria toxin mechanism","Lysogenic conversion"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H3"]}},{"id":"PU-C32-Q3","stem":"His 15-year-old cousin, who completed the recommended childhood immunization series, shares his bedroom. A throat culture from the cousin grows toxigenic Corynebacterium diphtheriae, but she has no symptoms. Which of the following best explains her protection from disease?","options":["Circulating IgG that neutralizes the toxin","Opsonizing IgG against the bacterial capsule","Cytotoxic T cells killing infected epithelial cells","Secretory IgA preventing colonization of the pharynx"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-12","obj":"PU-12.6","dok":3,"approach":["She is colonized but not sick, so her immunity blocks the toxin rather than the organism.","The diphtheria vaccine is a toxoid, a toxin inactivated with formaldehyde that keeps its antigenic structure.","Toxoid induces T-cell dependent IgG antitoxin that binds the toxin and prevents it from entering cells."],"why":"Toxoid vaccines (diphtheria, tetanus) present inactivated toxin protein. Because it is a protein, helper T cells support class switching and memory, giving long-lived IgG antitoxin. Antitoxin binds circulating toxin and blocks its attachment and entry, so the toxin cannot cause the membrane, myocarditis, or neuropathy. Toxoid immunity does not reliably prevent colonization, which is why vaccinated people can carry toxigenic strains. This contrasts with polysaccharide vaccines, which are T-cell independent and give weak, short-lived IgM responses unless the polysaccharide is conjugated to a protein carrier.","whyNot":["Correct.","Opsonizing anticapsular antibody is the goal of polysaccharide and conjugate vaccines such as those for pneumococcus and Haemophilus influenzae type b. The diphtheria vaccine contains no bacterial capsule.","Corynebacterium diphtheriae stays on the mucosal surface and does not invade cells, so cytotoxic T cells are not the defense. The vaccine is directed at the toxin.","Toxoid vaccination does not target the organism, so it does little to prevent colonization. Her positive culture shows the bacteria are present in her pharynx."],"study":{"topics":["Toxoid vaccines","T-cell dependent versus independent antibody responses"],"comps":["PU-12","PU-13"]}},{"id":"PU-C32-Q4","stem":"Indirect mirror examination shows that the membrane extends upward behind the soft palate onto the lateral walls of the nasopharynx. Spread of infection through openings in this region most directly places him at risk for which of the following?","options":["Maxillary sinusitis","Parotitis","Otitis media","Dacryocystitis"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-3","obj":"PU-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the region: above the soft palate and behind the nasal cavity is the nasopharynx.","The pharyngotympanic (auditory) tube opens on each lateral wall of the nasopharynx, behind the torus tubarius.","The tube connects to the middle ear, so infection or obstruction here leads to otitis media."],"why":"The pharynx has three parts: the nasopharynx above the soft palate, the oropharynx from the soft palate to the epiglottis, and the laryngopharynx below. The nasopharynx contains the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) on its posterior wall and the openings of the pharyngotympanic tubes on its lateral walls, with the tubal tonsils nearby. Swelling or infection here can block the tube or spread into the middle ear. This is also why enlarged adenoids in children cause otitis media, mouth breathing, and snoring. The palatine tonsils lie in the oropharynx between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches.","whyNot":["The maxillary sinus drains into the middle meatus of the nasal cavity, so nasopharyngeal spread would not reach it directly.","The parotid duct opens into the oral vestibule opposite the upper second molar, far from the nasopharynx.","Correct.","The nasolacrimal duct drains into the inferior meatus of the nasal cavity, not the nasopharynx. Dacryocystitis follows obstruction of that duct."],"study":{"topics":["Parts of the pharynx","Pharyngotympanic tube and Waldeyer ring"],"comps":["PU-3","PU-14-C7"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C33","system":"PU","dx":"Disseminated histoplasmosis in a man with AIDS","codes":["PU-14-H4"],"case":"A 41-year-old man with HIV infection who stopped antiretroviral therapy 2 years ago comes to the physician because of fever, cough, and 8-kg weight loss over 5 weeks. He lives in rural Kentucky and recently cleaned out an old chicken coop. His temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 106/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Examination shows a shallow ulcer on the tongue, generalized lymphadenopathy, and hepatosplenomegaly. CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 32/mm3 (500 to 1,500). Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), leukocyte count is 2,900/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000), and platelet count is 74,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000). A chest x-ray shows diffuse reticulonodular infiltrates. Urine Histoplasma antigen is positive. The diagnosis is disseminated histoplasmosis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C33-Q1","stem":"Most people in his region who inhale this organism have no symptoms or a brief, self-limited illness. Which of the following best explains why the infection disseminated in this patient?","options":["Absence of complement-mediated lysis of the yeast","Defective NADPH oxidase activity in neutrophils","Deficient secretory IgA at the bronchial mucosa","Absent interferon-gamma activation of macrophages"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-H4","obj":"PU-14-H.4","dok":3,"approach":["Histoplasma yeasts live inside macrophages, so control depends on cell-mediated immunity.","In healthy hosts, CD4+ Th1 cells release interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages to kill the yeasts and form granulomas that wall off the infection.","With a CD4 count of 32/mm3, this does not happen, so infected macrophages carry the organism to the liver, spleen, marrow, and lymph nodes."],"why":"Histoplasma capsulatum is inhaled as microconidia, which convert to yeasts inside alveolar macrophages. In healthy people, Th1 cells produce interferon-gamma that activates macrophages, and granulomas contain the infection. These granulomas can caseate and later calcify in the lung and hilar nodes, mimicking tuberculosis. When CD4+ T cells are severely depleted, macrophages cannot kill the yeasts and spread them through the reticuloendothelial system. This produces fever, weight loss, hepatosplenomegaly, pancytopenia from marrow involvement, and mucosal ulcers. Endemic fungi can also disseminate to skin, bone, and meninges.","whyNot":["Complement lysis is a key defense against Neisseria and other gram-negative bacteria. Fungal cell walls resist the membrane attack complex, and complement is not the defect in AIDS.","Defective NADPH oxidase is chronic granulomatous disease. It predisposes to catalase-positive organisms such as Aspergillus and Staphylococcus aureus, not to the loss of T-cell help seen here.","Secretory IgA deficiency causes recurrent sinopulmonary bacterial infections. It does not explain spread of an intracellular yeast through the macrophage system.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cell-mediated immunity to intracellular fungi","Disseminated endemic mycoses"],"comps":["PU-14-H4","PU-12"]}},{"id":"PU-C33-Q2","stem":"A bone marrow biopsy is performed. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Small oval yeasts within macrophages","Narrow-based budding yeasts with a thick capsule","Large yeasts with broad-based budding","Thick-walled spherules filled with endospores"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.6","dok":2,"approach":["Use the clues: Ohio and Mississippi River valley region, bird or bat droppings, and spread through the macrophage system point to Histoplasma.","Recall the tissue form of each dimorphic fungus.","Histoplasma appears as small (about 2 to 4 µm) oval yeasts packed inside macrophages."],"why":"The dimorphic fungi grow as molds in the environment and as yeasts or spherules in tissue. Histoplasma capsulatum is found in soil enriched with bird or bat droppings in the Ohio and Mississippi River valleys. In tissue it is a small intracellular yeast filling macrophages, which is why it spreads to marrow, liver, spleen, and nodes. Blastomyces (Great Lakes and Ohio and Mississippi basins) forms large broad-based budding yeasts, and Coccidioides (southwestern United States) forms spherules containing endospores.","whyNot":["Correct.","Narrow-based budding yeasts with a thick polysaccharide capsule are Cryptococcus neoformans, which causes meningitis in AIDS and is found in pigeon droppings worldwide.","Large yeasts with broad-based budding are Blastomyces dermatitidis, which often spreads to skin and bone. It is extracellular and much larger than Histoplasma.","Spherules with endospores are Coccidioides immitis, found in the desert soil of the southwestern United States."],"study":{"topics":["Dimorphic fungi tissue forms","Geography of endemic mycoses"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H4"]}},{"id":"PU-C33-Q3","stem":"His physician also notes that he stopped taking trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole prophylaxis when he stopped antiretroviral therapy. The opportunistic fungal pneumonia this drug was meant to prevent typically presents with which of the following?","options":["Mobile mass within an old upper lobe cavity","Bilateral ground-glass opacities, exertional hypoxemia","Nodules with surrounding halo during prolonged neutropenia","Fleeting infiltrates with eosinophilia in asthma"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-H4","obj":"PU-14-H.5","dok":3,"approach":["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole prophylaxis is given when the CD4 count falls below 200/mm3 to prevent Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia.","Pneumocystis fills alveoli with a foamy exudate and causes diffuse interstitial and alveolar disease.","The result is bilateral ground-glass infiltrates, a dry cough, oxygen desaturation with exertion, and a high serum LDH."],"why":"Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia occurs mainly when the CD4 count is below 200/mm3. It begins gradually with dry cough, fever, and dyspnea. Chest imaging shows bilateral, often perihilar, ground-glass opacities, and oxygen saturation falls sharply with exertion. Serum LDH is usually high. Silver stain of lavage fluid shows the cysts. Aspergillus causes different patterns that depend on the host: a fungus ball in an old cavity, angioinvasive disease in neutropenia, and allergic disease in asthma or cystic fibrosis.","whyNot":["A mobile fungus ball in a preexisting cavity is an aspergilloma. It needs no immune defect and often presents with hemoptysis.","Correct.","Nodules with a halo of hemorrhage during prolonged neutropenia suggest invasive aspergillosis. Neutrophils, not CD4+ T cells, are the main defense against Aspergillus hyphae.","Fleeting infiltrates with eosinophilia and high IgE in a patient with asthma or cystic fibrosis describe allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, a hypersensitivity reaction."],"study":{"topics":["Pneumocystis pneumonia","Forms of pulmonary aspergillosis"],"comps":["PU-14-H4","PU-13"]}},{"id":"PU-C33-Q4","stem":"A CT scan shows enlarged lymph nodes at both lung hila. Compared with the right hilum, which of the following arrangements is characteristic of the normal left hilum?","options":["Pulmonary artery superior to the main bronchus","Pulmonary veins superior to the main bronchus","Main bronchus anterior to the pulmonary veins","Eparterial lobar bronchus above the pulmonary artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-4","obj":"PU-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["At each hilum the pulmonary veins are the most anterior and inferior structures.","On the right, the superior lobar (eparterial) bronchus branches early and lies above the pulmonary artery, which runs anterior to the bronchus.","On the left there is no eparterial bronchus, so the pulmonary artery arches over the main bronchus and is the most superior structure."],"why":"The root of each lung contains the pulmonary artery, the main bronchus, two pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, nerves, and lymphatics, with hilar lymph nodes around them. In both hila the pulmonary veins lie anterior and inferior. On the left, the pulmonary artery lies superior to the main bronchus. On the right, the superior lobar bronchus comes off above the pulmonary artery, so the right pulmonary artery lies anterior to the bronchus. Enlarged hilar nodes from granulomatous infections or lymphoma sit among these structures.","whyNot":["Correct.","The pulmonary veins are the most inferior structures at both hila.","The pulmonary veins are anterior to the bronchus at both hila, so the bronchus is posterior to them.","An eparterial bronchus above the pulmonary artery describes the right hilum, where the superior lobar bronchus branches above the artery."],"study":{"topics":["Structures at the lung hilum","Right versus left hilum"],"comps":["PU-4"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C34","system":"PU","dx":"Lobar pneumococcal pneumonia","codes":["PU-14-H5"],"case":"A 72-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 3 days after the sudden onset of a shaking chill, fever, and sharp right-sided chest pain on inspiration. She now has a cough productive of rust-colored sputum. Her temperature is 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Over the right lower posterior chest there is dullness to percussion, increased tactile fremitus, bronchial breath sounds, and egophony. Leukocyte count is 19,200/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000) with 14% band forms. Arterial blood gas on room air shows pH 7.31 (7.35 to 7.45), PaCO2 33 mm Hg (35 to 45), and PaO2 61 mm Hg (75 to 100). A chest x-ray shows consolidation of the entire right lower lobe with air bronchograms. Sputum Gram stain shows gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci, and a urine pneumococcal antigen test is positive. The diagnosis is lobar pneumonia due to Streptococcus pneumoniae.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C34-Q1","stem":"Blood cultures grow alpha-hemolytic, catalase-negative gram-positive cocci. Which of the following results for this isolate would best distinguish it from viridans streptococci?","options":["Hydrolysis of esculin","Growth in 6.5% NaCl","Solubility in bile","Resistance to optochin"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.1","dok":2,"approach":["Alpha-hemolytic, catalase-negative cocci are either Streptococcus pneumoniae or viridans streptococci.","Streptococcus pneumoniae is optochin sensitive and bile soluble, and viridans streptococci are optochin resistant and bile insoluble.","Of the choices, only bile solubility points to pneumococcus."],"why":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is a gram-positive, lancet-shaped diplococcus that is catalase negative and alpha-hemolytic. It is lysed by bile salts because they activate its own autolysin, and it is inhibited by optochin. Viridans streptococci share the alpha-hemolysis but resist both. The pneumococcal polysaccharide capsule is its main virulence factor because it blocks phagocytosis, and IgA protease helps it colonize the nasopharynx. It is the most common cause of community-acquired and lobar pneumonia.","whyNot":["Esculin hydrolysis in bile is used to identify group D streptococci and enterococci, not to separate pneumococcus from viridans streptococci.","Growth in 6.5% NaCl identifies Enterococcus, which is usually gamma-hemolytic and causes urinary tract infections and endocarditis, not lobar pneumonia.","Correct.","Optochin resistance is the result viridans streptococci give. This isolate is pneumococcus, which is optochin sensitive."],"study":{"topics":["Identification of Streptococcus pneumoniae","Pneumococcal virulence factors"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H5"]}},{"id":"PU-C34-Q2","stem":"If the consolidated lobe were examined at this point in her illness, which of the following findings would most likely be seen?","options":["Disintegrating erythrocytes in a fibrinosuppurative exudate","Vascular engorgement with serous fluid and few neutrophils","Enzymatic digestion of exudate with restored architecture","Alveoli filled with neutrophils, erythrocytes, and fibrin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-H5","obj":"PU-14-H.1","dok":3,"approach":["Her illness began 3 days ago, so place her on the timeline of untreated lobar pneumonia.","Congestion lasts about the first 1 to 2 days, red hepatization about days 2 to 4, gray hepatization about days 4 to 8, then resolution.","At day 3 the lobe is in red hepatization: airless, red, firm, and liver-like, with alveoli packed with neutrophils, red cells, and fibrin."],"why":"Lobar pneumonia passes through four classic stages. In congestion the lobe is heavy and wet, with engorged capillaries, edema fluid, many bacteria, and few neutrophils. In red hepatization the alveoli fill with neutrophils, extravasated red cells, and fibrin, so the lobe looks red and firm like liver. The red cells explain the rust-colored sputum. In gray hepatization the red cells break down and a fibrinosuppurative exudate remains. In resolution enzymes digest the exudate and the architecture is restored. A uniformly consolidated lobe produces dullness, increased fremitus, bronchial breath sounds, and egophony.","whyNot":["Breakdown of red cells in a fibrinosuppurative exudate is gray hepatization, at about days 4 to 8.","Engorgement with serous fluid and few neutrophils is the congestion stage of about the first 1 to 2 days. It would not yet give the firm consolidation and rusty sputum she has.","Enzymatic digestion with restored architecture is resolution, which follows the hepatization stages.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Stages of lobar pneumonia","Physical findings of consolidation"],"comps":["PU-14-H5","PU-14-H2"]}},{"id":"PU-C34-Q3","stem":"Her fever and acidemia alter how hemoglobin releases oxygen in her tissues. Which of the following best describes this change?","options":["Stabilization of the relaxed (R) state, lowering P50","Stabilization of the taut (T) state, raising P50","Loss of cooperative binding, producing a hyperbolic curve","Lower oxygen-binding capacity with unchanged P50"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-8","obj":"PU-8.2","dok":2,"approach":["Fever and a low pH (high H+) both shift the oxyhemoglobin curve to the right.","A right shift means lower O2 affinity, so a higher PO2 is needed for 50% saturation (higher P50).","Lower affinity reflects stabilization of the deoxygenated taut (T) state, which favors O2 unloading in the tissues."],"why":"Hemoglobin has two conformations. The taut (T) state has low O2 affinity, and the relaxed (R) state has high affinity. Binding of one O2 favors the shift to R, which makes the next O2 bind more easily. This cooperative binding gives the sigmoid curve. Increased H+, CO2, temperature, and 2,3-BPG stabilize the T state, shift the curve right, and raise the P50 (normally about 27 mm Hg). This helps deliver O2 to hot, acidic, metabolically active tissues. Fetal hemoglobin, carbon monoxide, and methemoglobin shift the curve left.","whyNot":["Stabilizing the R state and lowering P50 is a left shift. It is caused by low temperature, alkalosis, low 2,3-BPG, fetal hemoglobin, or carbon monoxide, which is the opposite of her state.","Correct.","A hyperbolic curve without cooperativity is typical of myoglobin, which has one heme. Fever and acidemia shift hemoglobin's curve but keep its sigmoid shape.","Lower binding capacity with an unchanged P50 is the pattern of anemia, where there is less hemoglobin but each molecule behaves normally."],"study":{"topics":["Oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve shifts","T and R states of hemoglobin"],"comps":["PU-8","PU-7"]}},{"id":"PU-C34-Q4","stem":"In her systemic capillaries, carbon dioxide from her metabolically active tissues diffuses into red blood cells. Which of the following events then occurs in these cells?","options":["Bicarbonate influx in exchange for chloride","Chloride influx in exchange for bicarbonate","Water efflux causing red cell shrinkage","CO2 binding to the ferrous iron of heme"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-8","obj":"PU-8.3","dok":2,"approach":["Most CO2, about 70 percent, travels as bicarbonate, formed inside red cells by carbonic anhydrase.","CO2 plus water becomes carbonic acid, which splits into H+ (buffered by hemoglobin) and bicarbonate.","Bicarbonate leaves the cell through the band 3 anion exchanger, and chloride enters in exchange. This is the chloride shift."],"why":"CO2 is carried in blood in three forms: bicarbonate (about 70 percent), carbaminohemoglobin bound to amino groups of globin (about 20 percent), and dissolved CO2 (about 10 percent). Red cell carbonic anhydrase rapidly converts CO2 and water to carbonic acid, which dissociates into H+ and bicarbonate. Deoxyhemoglobin buffers the H+. Bicarbonate moves to the plasma in exchange for chloride through band 3, so venous red cells contain more chloride. In the lungs every step runs in reverse.","whyNot":["Bicarbonate enters red cells in exchange for chloride in the pulmonary capillaries, where CO2 is being released. This is the reverse chloride shift.","Correct.","The added chloride and bicarbonate raise osmotic content, so water enters and venous red cells swell slightly. They do not shrink.","CO2 binds the terminal amino groups of the globin chains to form carbaminohemoglobin. It does not bind heme iron, which is where O2 and carbon monoxide bind."],"study":{"topics":["CO2 transport in blood","Chloride shift"],"comps":["PU-8"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C35","system":"PU","dx":"Lung abscess after aspiration in a man with alcohol use disorder","codes":["PU-14-H6"],"case":"A 52-year-old man with alcohol use disorder comes to the physician because of 3 weeks of fever, night sweats, 5-kg weight loss, and cough productive of large amounts of foul-smelling, foul-tasting sputum. He reports several episodes of heavy drinking that ended with him waking on the floor. His temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 134/84 mm Hg. He has severe gingivitis and multiple carious teeth. Leukocyte count is 15,600/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000). A chest x-ray shows a 5-cm thick-walled cavity with an air-fluid level in the right lung. Sputum Gram stain shows many neutrophils and mixed bacterial flora. The diagnosis is lung abscess after aspiration.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C35-Q1","stem":"Which of the following is the most likely source of the organisms that caused this abscess?","options":["Aerosolized water from a contaminated cooling tower","Hematogenous seeding from right-sided endocarditis","Anaerobic bacteria from the gingival crevices","Inhaled droplet nuclei from an infected contact"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-14-H6","obj":"PU-14-H.6","dok":3,"approach":["Episodes of lost consciousness from alcohol plus poor dentition set up aspiration of mouth contents.","Putrid sputum with mixed flora points to anaerobes, which make foul-smelling fatty acids and other metabolic products.","The gingival crevices of a mouth with periodontal disease hold large numbers of anaerobes such as Bacteroides, Fusobacterium, Prevotella, and Peptostreptococcus."],"why":"Most lung abscesses follow aspiration of oropharyngeal contents when consciousness or swallowing is impaired, as with alcohol intoxication, seizures, anesthesia, or dysphagia. Poor dental hygiene raises the anaerobic load. The anaerobes and mixed flora cause necrotizing pneumonia that liquefies into a cavity, which shows an air-fluid level once it drains into a bronchus. Foul sputum is the clinical clue. Staphylococcus aureus and Klebsiella pneumoniae can also cause abscesses, usually after a necrotizing pneumonia.","whyNot":["Contaminated cooling towers and water systems transmit Legionella pneumophila, which causes pneumonia with hyponatremia and diarrhea, not a putrid anaerobic abscess.","Right-sided endocarditis, as in injection drug use, seeds the lungs with septic emboli. These appear as multiple peripheral nodules, often cavitating, and are usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus.","Correct.","Droplet nuclei from an infected contact transmit Mycobacterium tuberculosis, which forms apical cavities with chronic symptoms but does not produce putrid sputum or mixed flora."],"study":{"topics":["Aspiration and lung abscess","Oropharyngeal anaerobes"],"comps":["PU-14-H6","PU-13"]}},{"id":"PU-C35-Q2","stem":"He says he usually wakes from these episodes lying on his back. In which of the following bronchopulmonary segments is his abscess most likely located?","options":["Superior segment of the right lower lobe","Anterior segment of the right upper lobe","Medial segment of the right middle lobe","Lateral basal segment of the left lower lobe"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-4","obj":"PU-4.2","dok":3,"approach":["Aspirated material favors the right side because the right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical than the left.","Material then follows gravity to the most dependent segment for the body position.","Lying supine, the posteriorly directed superior segmental bronchus of the right lower lobe is dependent, so material collects there."],"why":"The right lung has three lobes separated by the horizontal and oblique fissures, and the left lung has two lobes with the lingula and cardiac notch. The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical, so aspirated material usually enters the right lung. Gravity then decides the segment. When upright, material reaches the basal segments of the right lower lobe. When supine, it enters the superior segment of the right lower lobe, and the posterior segment of the right upper lobe is the other classic supine site. When lying on the right side, the right upper lobe is favored.","whyNot":["Correct.","The anterior segment of the right upper lobe points forward and upward, so it is not dependent when lying on the back.","The right middle lobe medial segment points forward. It is not dependent in the supine position and is an uncommon site for aspiration.","Aspiration favors the right lung, and the left lower lobe basal segments are dependent mainly in the upright position."],"study":{"topics":["Aspiration sites by body position","Right versus left lung anatomy"],"comps":["PU-4","PU-14-H6"]}},{"id":"PU-C35-Q3","stem":"Ten days into treatment, he has new right-sided pleuritic pain and persistent fever. Ultrasound shows a loculated pleural collection. Thoracentesis yields thick yellow fluid with pH 6.9 (about 7.6 normally), glucose 22 mg/dL (serum 104 mg/dL), and LDH 2,400 U/L (serum upper limit 220 U/L). Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Chylothorax","Hemothorax","Hepatic hydrothorax","Empyema"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-D","obj":"PU-14-D.7","dok":2,"approach":["A pleural collection appearing during a lung infection raises concern for spread into the pleural space.","Very low pH and glucose with very high LDH mean intense neutrophil and bacterial metabolism in the fluid.","Thick, purulent, loculated fluid after pneumonia or abscess is empyema."],"why":"Empyema is pus in the pleural space. It usually follows pneumonia or a lung abscess that ruptures or spreads to the pleura. Neutrophils and bacteria consume glucose and generate acid, so the fluid has low pH and glucose and very high LDH. Fibrin deposition causes loculation. Without drainage, organization can lead to a thick pleural peel that restricts lung expansion.","whyNot":["Chylothorax is milky fluid with a high triglyceride level from thoracic duct injury or obstruction, as with trauma or lymphoma. It is not acidic or purulent.","Hemothorax is blood in the pleural space, usually after trauma, with a pleural fluid hematocrit at least half that of blood.","Hepatic hydrothorax is a transudate in cirrhosis from ascitic fluid crossing the diaphragm. It has normal pH and glucose and low LDH.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Empyema","Pleural fluid analysis"],"comps":["PU-14-D","PU-14-H6"]}},{"id":"PU-C35-Q4","stem":"The abscess heals, leaving a thin-walled residual cavity. Two years later he has recurrent small-volume hemoptysis. CT shows a round mass inside the cavity that moves when he changes position. Microscopy of the mass would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Cysts on methenamine silver stain","Septate hyphae with acute-angle branches","Broad nonseptate hyphae with right-angle branches","Budding yeasts with pseudohyphae"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.7","dok":2,"approach":["A mobile ball in an old cavity with hemoptysis is a fungus ball (aspergilloma).","Aspergillus colonizes the cavity without invading tissue, so it needs no immune defect.","Aspergillus forms septate hyphae that branch at acute angles, about 45 degrees."],"why":"An aspergilloma is a tangled mass of Aspergillus hyphae growing in a preexisting cavity from tuberculosis, sarcoidosis, or a healed abscess. It moves with position and can erode vessels in the cavity wall, causing hemoptysis. Microscopy shows narrow, septate hyphae with acute-angle branching. The host defect decides other Aspergillus forms: neutropenia allows angioinvasive disease, while CD4 counts below 200/mm3 allow Pneumocystis, whose cysts stain with methenamine silver.","whyNot":["Cysts on methenamine silver stain are Pneumocystis jirovecii, which causes diffuse pneumonia with CD4 counts below 200/mm3, not a ball in a cavity.","Correct.","Broad, ribbon-like, nonseptate hyphae with right-angle branching are Mucor and Rhizopus, which invade tissue in diabetic ketoacidosis and neutropenia.","Budding yeasts with pseudohyphae are Candida, which rarely forms a pulmonary fungus ball and is usually found in the mouth, esophagus, or blood of compromised hosts."],"study":{"topics":["Aspergilloma","Fungal morphology in tissue"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H4"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C36","system":"PU","dx":"Pertussis in a young infant","codes":["PU-14-H7"],"case":"A 7-week-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of spells of coughing followed by cyanosis and pauses in breathing. She had 10 days of runny nose and mild cough, and for the past 4 days she has had bursts of rapid coughs, often followed by vomiting. She was born at term and has not yet received any vaccines. Her mother has had a cough for 3 weeks and did not receive a pertussis vaccine during pregnancy. The infant's temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 160/min, and respirations are 48/min. Between spells she appears well, and the lungs are clear. Leukocyte count is 42,000/mm3 (5,000 to 19,500) with 76% lymphocytes. Nasopharyngeal PCR is positive for Bordetella pertussis. The diagnosis is pertussis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C36-Q1","stem":"Her mother's illness began with a week of rhinorrhea and mild cough, followed by 2 weeks of severe coughing fits. The mother was most likely to transmit the infection during which stage of her illness?","options":["Catarrhal","Incubation","Convalescent","Paroxysmal"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-H7","obj":"PU-14-H.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the three clinical stages: catarrhal (cold-like, 1 to 2 weeks), paroxysmal (coughing fits, whoop, posttussive vomiting, 2 to 6 weeks), and convalescent (slow fading of cough over weeks).","Bacterial numbers in the airway are highest early, in the catarrhal stage.","Because the symptoms then look like a common cold, the disease is rarely recognized while it is most contagious."],"why":"Bordetella pertussis attaches to ciliated respiratory epithelium and multiplies there. The catarrhal stage, with rhinorrhea, mild cough, and low-grade fever, carries the highest bacterial load and the greatest risk of spread. In the paroxysmal stage, toxin-mediated damage produces fits of rapid coughs followed by an inspiratory whoop and posttussive vomiting, while bacterial numbers fall. The convalescent stage brings slow recovery. Young infants often have no whoop and instead present with apnea, cyanosis, and a high risk of pneumonia and death.","whyNot":["Correct.","During incubation, about 7 to 10 days, the organism is establishing itself and there is little or no cough to spread it.","In the convalescent stage the cough slowly fades, and the organism is usually no longer recoverable, so transmission is unlikely.","The paroxysmal stage is still contagious, but bacterial numbers are falling, and transmission is greatest earlier."],"study":{"topics":["Stages of pertussis","Pertussis in infants"],"comps":["PU-14-H7","PU-13"]}},{"id":"PU-C36-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her leukocyte count?","options":["Superantigen-driven polyclonal T-cell proliferation","Viral transformation of circulating B lymphocytes","ADP-ribosylation of Gi blocking lymphocyte homing","Adenylate cyclase toxin stimulating marrow lymphopoiesis"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.3","dok":3,"approach":["Marked lymphocytosis is a classic finding in pertussis, especially in infants.","Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates the alpha subunit of Gi, so Gi can no longer inhibit adenylate cyclase.","Chemokine receptors on lymphocytes signal through Gi. Blocking this signal keeps lymphocytes from leaving the blood for lymph nodes and tissues, so they accumulate in circulation."],"why":"Pertussis toxin is an A-B toxin. Its A subunit ADP-ribosylates Gi alpha, which locks it in the inactive state, so adenylate cyclase is no longer inhibited and cAMP rises. Lymphocytes depend on Gi-coupled chemokine receptors to home out of the blood, so they stay in circulation and produce marked lymphocytosis. The toxin also contributes to airway damage, insulin release with hypoglycemia, and impaired phagocyte function. In contrast, diphtheria toxin ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 and kills cells by stopping protein synthesis.","whyNot":["Superantigens, such as toxic shock syndrome toxin, cross-link MHC class II and T-cell receptors outside the antigen groove. They cause cytokine storm and shock, not a sustained lymphocytosis.","Viral transformation of B cells is the action of Epstein-Barr virus, which causes infectious mononucleosis with atypical lymphocytes (mostly reactive T cells).","Correct.","Bordetella adenylate cyclase toxin enters phagocytes and raises their cAMP, which impairs killing. It does not stimulate the marrow to make lymphocytes."],"study":{"topics":["Pertussis toxin mechanism","Lymphocytosis in pertussis"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H7"]}},{"id":"PU-C36-Q3","stem":"If her mother had received the Tdap vaccine in the third trimester, which of the following would most likely have protected this infant during her first 2 months of life?","options":["Maternal memory T cells crossing the placenta","Live attenuated bacteria colonizing the infant's nasopharynx","Infant IgM produced against vaccine polysaccharide","Transplacental IgG against pertussis toxin and adhesins"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-12","obj":"PU-12.6","dok":3,"approach":["The infant is too young to have been vaccinated, so any protection must come from the mother.","Tdap contains protein antigens: pertussis toxoid, filamentous hemagglutinin, pertactin, and sometimes fimbriae. These give a T-cell dependent IgG response.","IgG is the only antibody class actively carried across the placenta, by the neonatal Fc receptor, mostly in the third trimester."],"why":"Acellular pertussis vaccines contain purified protein antigens, including inactivated pertussis toxin and adhesins such as filamentous hemagglutinin and pertactin. Because they are proteins, they drive T-cell dependent class switching to IgG with memory. When given at about 27 to 36 weeks of pregnancy, the mother's boosted IgG crosses the placenta through the neonatal Fc receptor. This passive antibody neutralizes toxin and blocks attachment, protecting the infant until her own DTaP series begins at 2 months. Tdap is recommended in every pregnancy for this reason.","whyNot":["Maternal T cells do not cross the placenta in useful numbers. Passive protection of the newborn is provided by antibody.","The acellular pertussis vaccine is not live. It contains purified, inactivated proteins and cannot colonize anyone.","Pertussis vaccines are protein vaccines, not polysaccharide vaccines. Polysaccharides give T-cell independent IgM responses, and infants under 2 years respond poorly to them.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acellular pertussis vaccine","Passive transfer of maternal IgG"],"comps":["PU-12","PU-14-H7"]}},{"id":"PU-C36-Q4","stem":"During a coughing paroxysm her oxygen saturation falls to 74%. When the paroxysm ends, she takes several rapid, deep breaths. Which of the following receptors most directly sense the fall in arterial PO2 that drives this response?","options":["Glomus cells of the carotid bodies","Central chemoreceptors in the ventral medulla","Juxtacapillary receptors in the alveolar walls","Pulmonary stretch receptors in airway smooth muscle"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-6","obj":"PU-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["A saturation of 74% means a PaO2 well below 60 mm Hg, which is the range where hypoxic drive becomes strong.","Only the peripheral chemoreceptors, the carotid and aortic bodies, respond to low arterial PO2.","Carotid body glomus cells depolarize when PO2 falls, and the glossopharyngeal nerve carries the signal to the medulla to increase ventilation."],"why":"Peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid bodies (glossopharyngeal nerve) and aortic bodies (vagus nerve) respond mainly to a fall in arterial PO2 below about 60 mm Hg, where the oxyhemoglobin curve becomes steep. They also respond to rising PaCO2 and H+. Central chemoreceptors in the ventral medulla respond to H+ in the cerebrospinal fluid, formed when CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier. They control most of the minute-to-minute ventilation but do not sense oxygen. Hypoxemia and hypercapnia together raise ventilation more than either does alone.","whyNot":["Correct.","Central chemoreceptors respond to CSF H+ produced from arterial CO2. They are not stimulated by low PO2, and severe hypoxia actually depresses the medullary centers.","Juxtacapillary (J) receptors sense interstitial fluid and congestion, as in pulmonary edema, and cause rapid shallow breathing. They do not detect arterial PO2.","Pulmonary stretch receptors sense lung inflation and terminate inspiration (the Hering-Breuer reflex). They do not measure blood gases."],"study":{"topics":["Central versus peripheral chemoreceptors","Hypoxic ventilatory drive"],"comps":["PU-6","PU-7"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C37","system":"PU","dx":"Respiratory syncytial virus bronchiolitis in a former preterm infant","codes":["PU-14-H8"],"case":"A 4-month-old boy is brought to the emergency department in January because of cough, fast breathing, and poor feeding for 2 days after 3 days of nasal congestion. He was born at 30 weeks' gestation and now weighs 5 kg. His temperature is 38.0 °C (100.4 °F), pulse is 172/min, and respirations are 70/min. Oxygen saturation is 89% on room air. He has nasal flaring, intercostal and subcostal retractions, and diffuse expiratory wheezes and fine crackles. A chest x-ray shows hyperinflation with patchy peribronchial thickening. Nasopharyngeal PCR is positive for respiratory syncytial virus. The diagnosis is respiratory syncytial virus bronchiolitis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C37-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why this virus causes severe airway obstruction in infants like him, while it usually causes only a cold in adults?","options":["Smooth muscle hypertrophy from chronic allergic inflammation","Edema and debris narrowing very small bronchioles","Surfactant deficiency causing diffuse alveolar collapse","Loss of alveolar walls reducing elastic recoil"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-H8","obj":"PU-14-H.8","dok":3,"approach":["Bronchiolitis means infection of the small airways, with epithelial necrosis, mucus, edema, and lymphocytic inflammation in the bronchioles.","Airway resistance rises steeply as radius falls (resistance is inversely proportional to radius to the fourth power).","Infant bronchioles are tiny, and a preterm infant's are smaller still, so the same amount of swelling and debris causes far more obstruction."],"why":"Respiratory syncytial virus is the most common cause of bronchiolitis and pneumonia in infants, with yearly winter epidemics. It infects bronchiolar epithelium, causing necrosis, sloughing, mucus plugs, submucosal edema, and peribronchiolar lymphocytes. In small airways these changes cause partial obstruction with air trapping and wheezing, or complete obstruction with atelectasis. Prematurity, chronic lung disease of prematurity, congenital heart disease, and age under about 6 months increase the risk of severe disease. Preterm infants also received less maternal IgG, which mostly crosses the placenta late in pregnancy.","whyNot":["Smooth muscle hypertrophy from chronic allergic inflammation is a feature of long-standing asthma. It develops over years and does not explain an acute viral illness in a 4-month-old.","Correct.","Surfactant deficiency causes neonatal respiratory distress syndrome in the first hours after preterm birth. At 4 months his surfactant system is mature, and his findings are airway obstruction rather than diffuse collapse.","Loss of alveolar walls with reduced elastic recoil is emphysema, a chronic disease of adults."],"study":{"topics":["Respiratory syncytial virus bronchiolitis","Airway radius and resistance"],"comps":["PU-14-H8","PU-5"]}},{"id":"PU-C37-Q2","stem":"Before the season, he could have received a preventive monoclonal antibody against this virus. This antibody neutralizes the virus by binding which of the following?","options":["Spike protein that binds ACE2 on host cells","Hemagglutinin that binds sialic acid on airway cells","Neuraminidase that releases newly formed virions","F protein that fuses viral and cell membranes"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.5","dok":2,"approach":["Respiratory syncytial virus is an enveloped, negative-sense RNA pneumovirus with two main surface glycoproteins, G (attachment) and F (fusion).","The F protein fuses the viral envelope with the host cell membrane and also fuses neighboring infected cells into syncytia, which gives the virus its name.","Preventive monoclonal antibodies such as palivizumab and nirsevimab bind the F protein and block entry and cell-to-cell spread."],"why":"Respiratory syncytial virus is a nonsegmented negative-sense RNA virus, now classed as a pneumovirus and formerly as a paramyxovirus. Its F protein drives fusion of viral and cell membranes and forms multinucleated syncytia. It has no hemagglutinin or neuraminidase. F is well conserved, which makes it the target of preventive monoclonal antibodies and of maternal and older-adult vaccines. Influenza, by contrast, uses hemagglutinin and neuraminidase, and SARS-CoV-2 uses its spike protein to bind ACE2.","whyNot":["Spike protein binding ACE2 is the entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2, an enveloped positive-sense RNA coronavirus.","Hemagglutinin binding sialic acid is how influenza attaches to cells. Respiratory syncytial virus lacks a hemagglutinin.","Neuraminidase, which cleaves sialic acid to release new virions, is an influenza protein and the target of oseltamivir. Respiratory syncytial virus does not have it.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Respiratory syncytial virus structure","Comparison of respiratory RNA viruses"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H8"]}},{"id":"PU-C37-Q3","stem":"Before this illness, at rest he breathed 40 times per minute with a tidal volume of 35 mL. He now breathes 70 times per minute with a tidal volume of 18 mL. Assume his dead space remains 10 mL. Which of the following is his current alveolar ventilation, in mL/min?","options":["560","700","1,000","1,260"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-6","obj":"PU-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Alveolar ventilation = (tidal volume minus dead space) x respiratory rate.","Now: (18 mL minus 10 mL) x 70/min = 560 mL/min.","Compare with before: (35 minus 10) x 40 = 1,000 mL/min. His minute ventilation fell only slightly, from 1,400 to 1,260 mL/min, but alveolar ventilation fell by almost half because more of each small breath is dead space."],"why":"Minute ventilation is tidal volume times rate. Alveolar ventilation subtracts the dead space from each breath, because dead space air never reaches gas exchange. With rapid shallow breathing, the fixed dead space takes up a larger share of each breath. Here the dead-space fraction rose from about 29% to about 56%, so alveolar ventilation fell from 1,000 to 560 mL/min. That fall raises PaCO2. Physiologic dead space can be measured with the Bohr equation: VD/VT = (PaCO2 minus PECO2) / PaCO2.","whyNot":["Correct.","700 mL/min is the dead space ventilation (10 mL x 70/min), the part of each minute's breathing that does not reach alveoli.","1,000 mL/min was his alveolar ventilation before the illness, at 40 breaths per minute with a 35-mL tidal volume.","1,260 mL/min is his current minute ventilation (18 mL x 70/min). It ignores the dead space."],"study":{"topics":["Alveolar ventilation calculation","Rapid shallow breathing and dead space"],"comps":["PU-6","PU-7"]}},{"id":"PU-C37-Q4","stem":"At age 8 years, he has recurrent wheezing, and air trapping is suspected. Which of the following lung volumes must be measured by helium dilution or body plethysmography rather than by spirometry?","options":["Vital capacity","Inspiratory reserve volume","Residual volume","Expiratory reserve volume"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-6","obj":"PU-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Air trapping means gas stays in the lungs after a full exhalation, so the key volume is residual volume.","A spirometer measures only air that moves in or out of the mouth.","Residual volume never leaves the lungs, so it needs gas dilution or plethysmography, and so do FRC and TLC, which contain it."],"why":"Tidal volume, inspiratory reserve volume, expiratory reserve volume, and vital capacity can all be measured with spirometry because they are exhaled. Residual volume is the air left after maximal exhalation. Functional residual capacity (expiratory reserve volume plus residual volume) and total lung capacity also include it. Helium dilution measures the gas volume that the helium mixes into, and body plethysmography uses Boyle's law to measure all thoracic gas. In obstructive disease with air trapping, residual volume and the ratio of residual volume to total lung capacity rise.","whyNot":["Vital capacity is the largest volume that can be exhaled after a maximal inspiration, so spirometry measures it directly.","Inspiratory reserve volume is extra air inhaled above a normal tidal breath, and a spirometer records it.","Correct.","Expiratory reserve volume is the extra air exhaled after a normal tidal exhalation. It leaves the lungs, so spirometry measures it."],"study":{"topics":["Lung volumes and capacities","Measuring residual volume"],"comps":["PU-6","PU-14-B1"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C38","system":"PU","dx":"Reactivation pulmonary tuberculosis","codes":["PU-14-H9"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis comes to the physician because of cough, low-grade fevers, drenching night sweats, and 6-kg weight loss over 2 months. For the past week her sputum has been streaked with blood. Five months ago she began treatment with a tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibitor. She received BCG vaccine as an infant. Her temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 94/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 124/76 mm Hg. Crackles are heard over the right upper chest. A chest x-ray shows a cavitary lesion in the apical-posterior right upper lobe. Sputum smear shows acid-fast bacilli, and a nucleic acid amplification test is positive for Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex. The diagnosis is reactivation pulmonary tuberculosis.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C38-Q1","stem":"Three weeks after starting treatment, she develops progressive midback pain. There is tenderness over the T10 spinous process. MRI shows destruction of the T9 and T10 vertebral bodies and the intervening disc, with a paraspinal fluid collection. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Osteoporotic compression fracture","Metastatic adenocarcinoma","Multiple myeloma","Tuberculous spondylitis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-H9","obj":"PU-14-H.10","dok":3,"approach":["Active tuberculosis plus immunosuppression raises the risk of hematogenous spread beyond the lung.","Destruction of two adjacent vertebral bodies and the disc between them, with a paraspinal (cold) abscess, is the classic pattern of spinal infection.","In this setting the diagnosis is tuberculous spondylitis (Pott disease), most common in the lower thoracic spine."],"why":"Reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis usually arises in the lung apices, where it forms caseating, cavitating lesions with fever, night sweats, weight loss, and hemoptysis. Bacilli can spread through the blood to the kidneys, meninges, adrenals, and vertebrae, especially when immunity is weakened by HIV, glucocorticoids, or TNF-alpha inhibitors. TNF-alpha helps keep granulomas intact, so blocking it allows latent foci to reactivate. In the spine, infection destroys vertebral bodies and the disc and may form a paraspinal or psoas cold abscess. Collapse can cause kyphosis and spinal cord compression.","whyNot":["Osteoporotic fractures collapse a vertebral body but spare the disc and do not form a paraspinal fluid collection.","Metastatic carcinoma destroys vertebral bodies and pedicles but characteristically spares the intervertebral disc.","Multiple myeloma causes punched-out lytic lesions and fractures without disc destruction or abscess, usually with anemia, renal injury, and a monoclonal protein.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Reactivation and miliary tuberculosis","Pott disease"],"comps":["PU-14-H9","PU-12"]}},{"id":"PU-C38-Q2","stem":"Before she began the TNF-alpha inhibitor, she should have been screened for latent tuberculosis infection. Given her vaccination history, which of the following tests would have been the most specific for latent infection?","options":["Tuberculin skin test","Sputum acid-fast bacilli smear","Interferon-gamma release assay","Serum antimycobacterial IgG titer"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-14-H9","obj":"PU-14-H.9","dok":3,"approach":["Latent tuberculosis means contained infection with memory T cells, no symptoms, and no bacilli in sputum.","The tuberculin skin test uses purified protein derivative, which shares antigens with BCG, so it can be positive from vaccination.","The interferon-gamma release assay measures T-cell interferon-gamma release to ESAT-6 and CFP-10, antigens absent from BCG, so it is not confounded by vaccination."],"why":"In primary tuberculosis, inhaled bacilli form a Ghon focus, usually in the lower part of an upper lobe or the upper part of a lower lobe, and spread to hilar nodes. Together these form the Ghon complex. Th1 cells then activate macrophages, and caseating granulomas contain the infection, which often calcifies (Ranke complex) and stays latent. Both screening tests detect this T-cell memory. The skin test is a delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction read as induration at 48 to 72 hours, and it can be falsely positive after BCG. The interferon-gamma release assay uses antigens found in M. tuberculosis but not in BCG, so it is more specific in vaccinated people. Neither test separates latent from active disease.","whyNot":["The tuberculin skin test detects the same T-cell memory, but BCG shares its antigens and can cause a false-positive result in vaccinated people.","Sputum smears detect active pulmonary disease with many bacilli. In latent infection there are no bacilli in the sputum, so the smear is negative.","Correct.","Antibody tests are unreliable for tuberculosis because protection and memory are cell mediated. Serologic tests are not recommended for diagnosis or screening."],"study":{"topics":["Primary tuberculosis and the Ghon complex","Tuberculin skin test versus IGRA"],"comps":["PU-14-H9","PU-12"]}},{"id":"PU-C38-Q3","stem":"After inhalation, the organism survives and multiplies inside alveolar macrophages. Which of the following mechanisms most directly allows this?","options":["Escape from the phagosome into the cytosol","Inhibition of phagosome-lysosome fusion","Polysaccharide capsule that prevents ingestion","Protein A binding the Fc portion of IgG"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-13","obj":"PU-13.4","dok":2,"approach":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis is readily ingested by alveolar macrophages, so the question is how it avoids being killed afterward.","It stays inside the phagosome but prevents it from fusing with lysosomes and becoming acidic.","The bacteria then multiply within the protected phagosome until Th1 cells arrive and activate the macrophage."],"why":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is spread by airborne droplet nuclei small enough to reach the alveoli. Its waxy cell wall, rich in mycolic acids, makes it acid-fast and resistant to drying and to many disinfectants. Sulfatides and other wall components prevent phagosome-lysosome fusion, so it survives inside unactivated macrophages. Cord factor (trehalose dimycolate) causes the serpentine growth pattern in culture, injures macrophages, and helps induce granuloma formation. Infection is controlled only after interferon-gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages.","whyNot":["Escape from the phagosome into the cytosol is used by Listeria monocytogenes (with listeriolysin O) and Shigella. Mycobacteria stay inside the phagosome.","Correct.","A polysaccharide capsule that blocks ingestion is the strategy of Streptococcus pneumoniae and Klebsiella. Mycobacteria are readily ingested and survive inside the cell.","Protein A binding the Fc portion of IgG is a Staphylococcus aureus virulence factor that blocks opsonization."],"study":{"topics":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis virulence factors","Intracellular survival strategies"],"comps":["PU-13","PU-14-H9"]}},{"id":"PU-C38-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains why reactivation tuberculosis favors the apices of the lungs in an upright person?","options":["Greatest blood flow delivering more bacilli per minute","Greatest ventilation per alveolus during tidal breathing","Highest ventilation-perfusion ratio and alveolar PO2","Lowest ventilation-perfusion ratio with alveolar hypoxia"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-5","obj":"PU-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis is an obligate aerobe, so it grows best where alveolar PO2 is highest.","When upright, gravity lowers arterial pressure at the apex, so perfusion falls much more than ventilation. The apex is usually in zone 2.","Apical V/Q is therefore the highest in the lung, about 3, and alveolar PO2 is highest, about 130 mm Hg."],"why":"West zones describe how perfusion varies with height in the upright lung. In zone 1 (only in abnormal states, such as low arterial pressure or high alveolar pressure), alveolar pressure exceeds arterial pressure and there is no flow. In zone 2, arterial pressure exceeds alveolar pressure, which exceeds venous pressure, so flow depends on the arterial minus alveolar difference. In zone 3 at the base, arterial and venous pressures both exceed alveolar pressure and flow is greatest. Both ventilation and perfusion are greater at the base, but perfusion changes more, so V/Q is highest at the apex and lowest at the base. The high apical PO2 suits the aerobic tubercle bacillus.","whyNot":["Blood flow is lowest at the apex in an upright person, not highest. Zone 3 at the base receives the most flow.","Ventilation per alveolus is greater at the base, because basal alveoli start smaller and sit on a steeper part of the pressure-volume curve.","Correct.","The lowest V/Q and lowest alveolar PO2 are found at the base, which is not where reactivation tuberculosis develops."],"study":{"topics":["West zones","Regional V/Q differences"],"comps":["PU-5","PU-7"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C39","system":"PU","dx":"COVID-19 pneumonia progressing to acute respiratory distress syndrome","codes":["PU-14-H10"],"case":"A 69-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus and obesity is brought to the emergency department because of worsening shortness of breath. Eight days ago he developed fever, dry cough, fatigue, and loss of smell. His temperature is 38.7 °C (101.7 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 32/min, and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 84% on room air. Crackles are heard over both lungs. He is intubated, and arterial PaO2 is 72 mm Hg on an FiO2 of 0.6. D-dimer is 3.8 µg/mL (below 0.5). A chest x-ray shows bilateral, mainly peripheral opacities. Echocardiography shows normal left ventricular function. Nasopharyngeal PCR is positive for SARS-CoV-2. The diagnosis is COVID-19 pneumonia complicated by acute respiratory distress syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C39-Q1","stem":"This virus infects alveolar type II pneumocytes and cells of the olfactory epithelium. Which of the following host molecules does it bind to enter these cells?","options":["Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2","CD4 glycoprotein","Sialic acid residues on glycoproteins","Intercellular adhesion molecule 1"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"PU-14-H10","obj":"PU-14-H.11","dok":2,"approach":["SARS-CoV-2 is an enveloped positive-sense RNA coronavirus whose spike protein mediates entry.","The spike receptor-binding domain attaches to ACE2, and host proteases such as TMPRSS2 then prime the spike for membrane fusion.","ACE2 is expressed on type II pneumocytes, nasal epithelium, and supporting cells of the olfactory epithelium, which explains pneumonia and loss of smell."],"why":"Coronaviruses are enveloped, positive-sense RNA viruses. Common cold strains cause mild upper respiratory infections. SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and SARS-CoV-2 cause severe pneumonia. SARS-CoV-2 binds ACE2 through its spike protein. Infection of olfactory supporting cells causes loss of smell, and infection of type II pneumocytes leads to diffuse alveolar damage. Endothelial injury and inflammation promote clotting, so D-dimer rises and venous and arterial thrombosis are common. Older age, diabetes, obesity, and heart and lung disease are major risk factors for severe disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","CD4, with CCR5 or CXCR4 as coreceptor, is the receptor for HIV on helper T cells and macrophages.","Sialic acid residues are the receptor for influenza hemagglutinin.","ICAM-1 is the receptor for most rhinoviruses, a common cause of colds."],"study":{"topics":["SARS-CoV-2 entry and ACE2","Coronavirus disease risk factors"],"comps":["PU-14-H10","PU-13"]}},{"id":"PU-C39-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the mechanism of his pulmonary edema?","options":["Increased alveolar-capillary permeability","Obstruction of pulmonary lymphatic drainage","Reduced plasma oncotic pressure","Elevated pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-14-A1","obj":"PU-14-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["His PaO2/FiO2 ratio is 72/0.6 = 120, well below 300, with bilateral opacities within a week of a known insult.","Normal left ventricular function argues against cardiogenic (hydrostatic) edema.","This is acute respiratory distress syndrome: injury to the alveolar-capillary membrane lets protein-rich fluid leak into the alveoli."],"why":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome follows a direct lung insult such as pneumonia or aspiration, or an indirect one such as sepsis, trauma, or pancreatitis. Injury to endothelium and type I pneumocytes, amplified by neutrophils and their proteases and oxygen radicals, makes the alveolar-capillary barrier leaky. Protein-rich fluid floods the alveoli, and fibrin and necrotic cell debris form hyaline membranes (diffuse alveolar damage). The result is bilateral infiltrates and severe hypoxemia from shunt, with PaO2/FiO2 of 300 or less, not explained by heart failure. Survivors may develop interstitial fibrosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lymphatic obstruction, as in lymphangitic carcinomatosis, causes slowly progressive interstitial thickening, not acute flooding of the alveoli.","Low plasma oncotic pressure, as in nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis, causes transudative edema. It would not produce acute, severe hypoxemia after a viral pneumonia.","High capillary hydrostatic pressure from left heart failure causes cardiogenic edema. His left ventricular function is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of ARDS","Cardiogenic versus noncardiogenic edema"],"comps":["PU-14-A1","PU-14-E1"]}},{"id":"PU-C39-Q3","stem":"Fluid and hyaline membranes have thickened the barrier that oxygen must cross to reach his blood. In the normal lung, which of the following layers make up the thinnest part of this barrier?","options":["Type I pneumocyte, smooth muscle, endothelium","Type II pneumocyte, interstitial fibroblast, endothelium","Ciliated epithelium, lamina propria, endothelium","Type I pneumocyte, fused basement membranes, endothelium"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"PU-2","obj":"PU-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Gas exchange happens across the thin portion of the alveolar wall.","Type I pneumocytes are flat cells covering about 95 percent of the alveolar surface, and type II cells are cuboidal and make surfactant.","At the thin part, the type I cell and capillary endothelium share one fused basement membrane, a barrier only about 0.2 to 0.6 µm thick."],"why":"The blood-air barrier at its thinnest consists of the type I pneumocyte cytoplasm, the fused basement membranes of epithelium and endothelium, and the capillary endothelial cell, with a thin surfactant layer on the alveolar side. By Fick's law, diffusion is inversely proportional to barrier thickness. Edema, hyaline membranes, inflammatory cells, or interstitial fibrosis thicken it and slow diffusion of O2 more than CO2, because CO2 is far more soluble. The thicker part of the wall holds interstitial cells and fibers but is not the main site of gas exchange.","whyNot":["Alveolar walls contain no smooth muscle layer. Smooth muscle ends at the level of the respiratory bronchioles and alveolar duct openings.","Type II pneumocytes and fibroblasts are found in the thicker part of the wall. Type II cells make surfactant and repair the epithelium, but gas exchange happens across type I cells.","Ciliated epithelium and lamina propria line the conducting airways, which do not exchange gas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Blood-air barrier","Diffusion and Fick's law"],"comps":["PU-2","PU-7"]}},{"id":"PU-C39-Q4","stem":"On the ventilator, a higher pressure than normal is needed to deliver each tidal volume, and many alveoli collapse at end expiration. Injury to type II pneumocytes contributes to this. Which of the following best explains the alveolar collapse?","options":["More negative intrapleural pressure at end expiration","Increased lung compliance from loss of elastic fibers","Lower surface tension from excess surfactant secretion","Surfactant loss raising surface tension"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-6","obj":"PU-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["Type II pneumocytes make surfactant, and injury plus protein in the alveoli inactivates what is left.","By the law of Laplace, collapsing pressure equals 2 x surface tension / radius.","With high surface tension, small alveoli have the highest collapsing pressure, so they empty and collapse. The lungs become stiff, and compliance falls."],"why":"Surfactant, mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine, lowers surface tension, especially as alveoli shrink. This keeps small alveoli open at end expiration and lowers the work of breathing. In ARDS, type II cell injury and plasma proteins in the alveoli reduce surfactant function. Surface tension rises, small alveoli collapse, and lung compliance falls, so higher pressure is needed for the same volume. Positive end-expiratory pressure holds collapsed alveoli open. Normally, the inward recoil of the lung and the outward recoil of the chest wall balance at FRC, giving an intrapleural pressure of about minus 5 cm H2O.","whyNot":["A more negative intrapleural pressure would hold alveoli more open, not collapse them.","Loss of elastic fibers increases compliance and is the change in emphysema. His lungs are stiffer, which is decreased compliance.","Excess surfactant would lower surface tension and stabilize alveoli. In ARDS surfactant is deficient and inactivated.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Surfactant and the law of Laplace","Lung compliance"],"comps":["PU-6","PU-14-A1"]}}]},{"id":"PU-C40","system":"PU","dx":"Carbon monoxide and cyanide poisoning from smoke inhalation","codes":["PU-11"],"case":"A 36-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after being rescued from a burning house where furniture and plastics were on fire. She has a headache and is confused. Her temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 124/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. There is soot in the nares and oropharynx. Pulse oximetry reads 98% on 100% oxygen by nonrebreather mask. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.27 (7.35 to 7.45), PaCO2 25 mm Hg (35 to 45), PaO2 410 mm Hg, and HCO3- 11 mEq/L (22 to 28). Carboxyhemoglobin is 28% (below 3%). Lactate is 11 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2). Central venous oxygen saturation is 88% (65 to 75). The diagnosis is combined carbon monoxide and cyanide poisoning from smoke inhalation.","questions":[{"id":"PU-C40-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her pulse oximetry reading is 98% despite a carboxyhemoglobin level of 28%?","options":["Cyanide raising capillary oxygen saturation at the fingertip","Carboxyhemoglobin absorbing light like oxyhemoglobin","Dissolved plasma oxygen detected by the sensor","Left shift of the curve raising true oxygen saturation"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"PU-8","obj":"PU-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["A standard pulse oximeter uses two wavelengths and assumes only oxyhemoglobin and deoxyhemoglobin are present.","Carboxyhemoglobin absorbs red light much like oxyhemoglobin, so the device counts it as oxygenated.","True oxygen saturation must be measured by co-oximetry, which here would be at most about 72%."],"why":"Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with about 200 times the affinity of oxygen. It occupies binding sites, lowering O2 content, and it shifts the remaining curve to the left so O2 is unloaded poorly in tissues. PaO2 reflects dissolved oxygen and stays normal or high, so it gives false reassurance. Standard pulse oximetry reads carboxyhemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin and overestimates saturation. Methemoglobin, with iron in the ferric state, also impairs delivery and makes pulse oximetry read about 85% regardless of the true value.","whyNot":["Cyanide raises venous O2 saturation by blocking tissue O2 use. It does not change arterial hemoglobin saturation or the oximeter's reading of carboxyhemoglobin.","Correct.","Pulse oximetry measures light absorption by hemoglobin in pulsatile blood. It does not detect dissolved oxygen, which is reflected in PaO2.","Carbon monoxide does shift the curve left, but that does not raise the fraction of hemoglobin carrying oxygen. Sites occupied by CO cannot carry O2."],"study":{"topics":["Carbon monoxide and oxygen delivery","Limits of pulse oximetry"],"comps":["PU-8","PU-7"]}},{"id":"PU-C40-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her high central venous oxygen saturation?","options":["Inhibition of cytochrome c oxidase","Uncoupling of electron transport from ATP synthesis","Increased oxygen extraction by hypermetabolic tissues","Reduced cardiac output prolonging capillary transit"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-11","obj":"PU-11.2","dok":3,"approach":["Normally tissues extract about 25 percent of delivered O2, so venous saturation is about 70 percent. A value of 88% means tissues are not using O2.","Burning plastics, wool, and silk release hydrogen cyanide.","Cyanide binds the ferric iron of cytochrome a3 in complex IV, so O2 cannot accept electrons and stays in the blood returning to the heart."],"why":"Cyanide blocks cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV), the final step of the electron transport chain. Oxygen delivery may be adequate, but cells cannot use it. Oxidative phosphorylation stops, ATP falls, and cells switch to anaerobic glycolysis, producing lactic acidosis. Venous blood returns with oxygen unused, so venous saturation is high and PaO2 is normal. Carbon monoxide also binds complex IV, adding to the injury. A high lactate with high venous oxygen saturation after a house fire suggests cyanide poisoning.","whyNot":["Correct.","Uncouplers such as 2,4-dinitrophenol let electron transport run faster without making ATP, so O2 use rises, which lowers venous saturation. Uncoupling also produces heat.","Greater extraction by hypermetabolic tissues would lower venous saturation, the opposite of her finding.","Low cardiac output gives tissues more time to extract O2, which lowers venous saturation. This is the pattern in circulatory shock."],"study":{"topics":["Cyanide toxicity","Inhibitors of the electron transport chain"],"comps":["PU-11","PU-7"]}},{"id":"PU-C40-Q3","stem":"Considering only her carbon monoxide exposure, which of the following patterns of arterial PaO2, oxygen saturation measured by co-oximetry, and arterial oxygen content is expected?","options":["Low PaO2, low saturation, low content","Normal PaO2, normal saturation, low content","Normal PaO2, low saturation, low content","Normal PaO2, normal saturation, normal content"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"PU-7","obj":"PU-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["PaO2 reflects dissolved oxygen, which depends on alveolar PO2 and lung function. CO does not change it.","Co-oximetry measures the true fraction of hemoglobin carrying O2. CO occupies sites, so saturation falls.","O2 content depends mainly on hemoglobin times saturation, so content falls."],"why":"Hypoxemia means a low PaO2. Tissue hypoxia means cells get too little usable oxygen and can occur with a normal PaO2. Hypoxemic hypoxia (high altitude, hypoventilation, V/Q mismatch, shunt) lowers PaO2, saturation, and content. Anemic hypoxia lowers content with normal PaO2 and saturation. Carbon monoxide is a functional form of anemic hypoxia, but because CO takes up binding sites, measured saturation also falls. Circulatory hypoxia (low flow) and histotoxic hypoxia (cyanide) leave PaO2, saturation, and content normal. Venous O2 tells them apart: it is low in circulatory hypoxia and high in histotoxic hypoxia.","whyNot":["Low PaO2, saturation, and content together are hypoxemic hypoxia, from lung disease, hypoventilation, or high altitude. Her PaO2 is high.","Normal PaO2 and saturation with low content is true anemia, where less hemoglobin is present but each molecule is normally saturated.","Correct.","Normal values for all three are seen in histotoxic hypoxia from cyanide and in circulatory hypoxia, where the problem is O2 use or flow rather than O2 carriage."],"study":{"topics":["Types of hypoxia","Oxygen content equation"],"comps":["PU-7","PU-8"]}},{"id":"PU-C40-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains the rise in her serum lactate concentration?","options":["Diversion of pyruvate to oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","Reduction of pyruvate to lactate regenerating NAD+","Increased fatty acid oxidation producing acetyl-CoA","Activation of pyruvate dehydrogenase by excess NAD+"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"PU-11","obj":"PU-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Oxygen is the final electron acceptor at complex IV. Without it, the electron transport chain stops, and NADH cannot be reoxidized to NAD+ in mitochondria.","Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step to continue making ATP.","Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and regenerates NAD+, so glycolysis can continue, at the cost of lactic acidosis."],"why":"Most ATP comes from oxidative phosphorylation, which needs O2 to accept electrons at cytochrome c oxidase. When O2 delivery fails (CO) or O2 use is blocked (cyanide), NADH builds up and the NADH/NAD+ ratio rises. Pyruvate can no longer enter the citric acid cycle efficiently, and lactate dehydrogenase converts it to lactate, regenerating NAD+ so glycolysis can keep making its 2 ATP per glucose. Lactate and H+ accumulate, producing an anion gap metabolic acidosis. Her low HCO3- and the compensating fall in PaCO2 reflect this.","whyNot":["Pyruvate carboxylase converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate in the liver for gluconeogenesis. This would consume pyruvate and lower lactate.","Correct.","Fatty acid oxidation needs a working electron transport chain to reoxidize NADH and FADH2, so it slows during tissue hypoxia. It does not produce lactate.","Pyruvate dehydrogenase is inhibited, not activated, when NADH is high. Its activity would lower lactate by sending pyruvate into the citric acid cycle."],"study":{"topics":["Oxygen and oxidative phosphorylation","Lactic acidosis"],"comps":["PU-11","PU-9"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C01","system":"RE","dx":"Turner syndrome (45,X) with primary amenorrhea","codes":["RE-13-C11","RE-13-A1"],"case":"A 16-year-old girl is brought to the physician because she has not had a menstrual period. She has always been the shortest student in her class. Height is 142 cm (below the 1st percentile). Pulse is 78/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg in both arms. Physical examination shows a broad chest with widely spaced nipples, a low posterior hairline, and webbing of the neck. Breasts are Tanner stage 1, and pubic hair is sparse. Serum studies show an FSH concentration of 92 mIU/mL (normal 3 to 10), LH concentration of 38 mIU/mL (normal 2 to 12), and estradiol concentration of less than 10 pg/mL (normal 30 to 100). Pelvic ultrasonography shows a small prepubertal uterus and bilateral streak gonads. Karyotype of peripheral blood lymphocytes is 45,X in all cells counted. Diagnosis: Turner syndrome with primary amenorrhea.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C01-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains this patient's serum FSH concentration?","options":["Autonomous secretion by a pituitary gonadotroph adenoma","Loss of negative feedback by ovarian estradiol and inhibin","Reduced hepatic clearance of circulating gonadotropins","Ectopic FSH production by a dysgerminoma in a streak gonad"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-C11","obj":"RE-13-C.11","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: high FSH and LH with very low estradiol. This is hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, which points to failure of the gonad itself.","Link 45,X to ovarian dysgenesis: oocytes are lost early, so the gonads become fibrous streaks with no follicles.","Without follicles there is no estradiol or inhibin to hold FSH down, so FSH rises."],"why":"In Turner syndrome the fetal ovary starts with germ cells, but they undergo accelerated atresia, and by puberty the ovaries are fibrous streaks without follicles. Granulosa cells of growing follicles make estradiol and inhibin, and both normally suppress FSH secretion. With no follicles, that feedback is gone, so the pituitary releases large amounts of FSH and LH. The low estradiol explains the absent breast development and the prepubertal uterus.","whyNot":["A gonadotroph adenoma is rare, would not cause the streak gonads, and usually presents with mass effects. The low estradiol here shows the problem is a lack of feedback, not a pituitary tumor.","Correct.","Liver disease does not raise gonadotropins to this degree. The pattern of high FSH with absent estradiol reflects gonadal failure.","Dysgerminoma risk rises in streak gonads that contain Y chromosome material, which this 45,X patient lacks. Germ cell tumors can make hCG, AFP, or LDH, not FSH."],"study":{"topics":["Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism","Feedback control of FSH"],"comps":["RE-13-C11","RE-7"]}},{"id":"RE-C01-Q2","stem":"Prenatal ultrasonography during her mother's pregnancy showed a large fluid-filled mass at the back of the fetal neck. Which of the following best explains how that finding relates to this patient's neck appearance now?","options":["Persistence of a second pharyngeal cleft remnant","Fibrosis of the sternocleidomastoid muscle in utero","Persistence of a remnant of the thyroglossal duct","Regression of a jugular lymphatic cystic hygroma"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-C11","obj":"RE-13-C.12","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the prenatal mass: a posterior neck fluid collection in a 45,X fetus is a cystic hygroma.","Recall that the lymphatic system develops abnormally in Turner syndrome, so lymph collects in the neck and limbs.","When the hygroma later drains and shrinks, the stretched skin remains as webbing."],"why":"Fetuses with Turner syndrome often have delayed or abnormal connection of the jugular lymph sacs to the venous system. Lymph pools at the back of the neck as a cystic hygroma. If the fetus survives, the fluid is resorbed, and the stretched skin folds remain as a webbed neck with a low posterior hairline. The same lymphatic problem causes the puffy hands and feet (lymphedema) seen in newborn girls with Turner syndrome. Short stature in Turner syndrome is a separate effect, from loss of one copy of the SHOX gene.","whyNot":["A second pharyngeal cleft remnant forms a cyst or sinus along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle, not posterior neck webbing.","Fibrosis of the sternocleidomastoid causes congenital torticollis, with the head tilted to one side, not bilateral webbing.","A thyroglossal duct cyst is a midline anterior neck mass that moves upward when the tongue protrudes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Associated findings in Turner syndrome","Cystic hygroma"],"comps":["RE-13-C11","CV-18-G"]}},{"id":"RE-C01-Q3","stem":"A buccal mucosal smear is prepared from this patient and from her 46,XX sister, and the nuclei are examined for sex chromatin. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["No Barr body in the patient and two Barr bodies in her sister","One Barr body in the patient and two Barr bodies in her sister","No Barr body in the patient and one Barr body in her sister","One Barr body in the patient and one Barr body in her sister"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-10","obj":"RE-10.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the rule: every X chromosome beyond the first is inactivated, so the number of Barr bodies equals the number of X chromosomes minus one.","Apply it to each karyotype: 45,X has one X, so zero Barr bodies. 46,XX has two X chromosomes, so one Barr body."],"why":"Early in female development, XIST RNA coats one X chromosome in each cell and silences most of its genes. The silenced X condenses into heterochromatin that is seen at the edge of the nucleus as a Barr body. A cell keeps only one active X, so the number of Barr bodies is the number of X chromosomes minus one. A 45,X cell has a single active X and no Barr body, and a 46,XX cell has one.","whyNot":["A 46,XX cell inactivates only one of its two X chromosomes, so it has one Barr body. Two Barr bodies are seen in 47,XXX cells.","Both counts are one too high. One Barr body would be expected in a 46,XX or 47,XXY cell, and two in a 47,XXX cell.","Correct.","A single X is never inactivated, because each cell needs one active X. A 45,X cell has no Barr body."],"study":{"topics":["X inactivation and XIST","Counting Barr bodies"],"comps":["RE-10","RE-13-C11"]}},{"id":"RE-C01-Q4","stem":"Laparoscopic biopsy of the left gonad shows wavy fibrous stroma resembling ovarian stroma. Which of the following structures, the most numerous follicle type in the ovary of a healthy girl her age, is most likely absent from this tissue?","options":["A primary oocyte within one layer of flat follicular cells","A collapsed structure of large lipid-rich luteinized granulosa cells","A primary oocyte with a zona pellucida and stratified granulosa","A fluid-filled antrum with the oocyte on a cumulus oophorus"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-2","obj":"RE-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that most follicles in the ovary at any age after birth are resting primordial follicles.","Match the description: a primordial follicle is a primary oocyte surrounded by one layer of flattened follicular (pregranulosa) cells.","Recognize that a streak gonad has lost its follicles, so even primordial follicles are missing."],"why":"At puberty each ovary still holds a large reserve of primordial follicles, far more than any other follicle type. A primordial follicle is a primary oocyte arrested in prophase I with a single layer of flat follicular cells around it. As a follicle grows, the cells become cuboidal (primary follicle), then stratified with a zona pellucida, then form an antrum (secondary follicle), and finally a large mature (Graafian) follicle. In Turner syndrome the oocytes undergo accelerated atresia during fetal life and early childhood, so the streak contains only stroma.","whyNot":["Correct.","Large lipid-rich luteinized cells describe the corpus luteum, which forms after ovulation and is not a follicle.","A zona pellucida with several layers of granulosa cells describes a growing preantral follicle. These are recruited in small numbers from the primordial pool.","An antrum with the oocyte sitting on a cumulus oophorus describes a mature (Graafian) follicle. Only one or a few are present in a cycle, so they are never the most numerous type."],"study":{"topics":["Follicle stages on ovarian histology","Ovarian reserve"],"comps":["RE-2","RE-3"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C02","system":"RE","dx":"Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) with infertility and gynecomastia","codes":["RE-13-C6"],"case":"A 27-year-old man comes to the physician with his wife because they have been unable to conceive after 2 years of regular unprotected intercourse. He shaves only once a week. Height is 191 cm, with long legs relative to his trunk. Pulse is 70/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows bilateral, symmetric breast enlargement and sparse body hair. Both testes are small and firm, each with a volume of 3 mL (normal 15 to 25). Semen analysis shows no sperm. Serum studies show an FSH concentration of 41 mIU/mL (normal 1.5 to 12.4), LH concentration of 24 mIU/mL (normal 1.7 to 8.6), and total testosterone concentration of 190 ng/dL (normal 300 to 1000). Karyotype is 47,XXY in all 30 cells counted. Diagnosis: Klinefelter syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C02-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional laboratory findings most directly reflects damage to this patient's seminiferous tubules?","options":["High serum estradiol concentration","High serum LH concentration","Low serum testosterone concentration","Low serum inhibin B concentration"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-C6","obj":"RE-13-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two compartments of the testis: seminiferous tubules (Sertoli and germ cells) and the interstitium (Leydig cells).","Recall that Sertoli cells make inhibin B, which suppresses FSH, while Leydig cells make testosterone, which mainly suppresses LH.","Pick the marker that comes from the tubules: inhibin B."],"why":"Klinefelter syndrome causes progressive hyalinization and fibrosis of the seminiferous tubules. Sertoli cells in the tubules make inhibin B, so its level falls, and FSH rises because inhibin B normally suppresses FSH. Leydig cell dysfunction is a separate problem that lowers testosterone and raises LH. That is why both gonadotropins are high in this patient, and why inhibin B is the marker that tracks tubule damage.","whyNot":["Estradiol rises because LH drives aromatase activity in the testis and because body fat converts androgens to estrogen. It does not measure tubule function.","High LH is the pituitary response to low testosterone, so it reflects Leydig cell failure.","Low testosterone reflects Leydig cell dysfunction in the interstitium, not tubule damage.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Sertoli versus Leydig cell markers","Hormone profile in Klinefelter syndrome"],"comps":["RE-13-C6","RE-2"]}},{"id":"RE-C02-Q2","stem":"DNA marker studies show that both of his X chromosomes are maternal in origin and are heterozygous for markers close to the centromere. Which of the following events most likely produced his karyotype?","options":["Nondisjunction in paternal meiosis I","Nondisjunction in mitosis after fertilization","Nondisjunction in maternal meiosis I","Nondisjunction in maternal meiosis II"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-10","obj":"RE-10.1","dok":3,"approach":["Both X chromosomes are maternal, so the error happened in the egg, not the sperm.","Markers near the centromere stay with their own homolog until meiosis I separates the homologs. Different (heterozygous) centromere markers mean both homologs went into the egg.","That pattern fits failure of homologs to separate in meiosis I. A uniform 47,XXY karyotype in all cells argues against a later mitotic error."],"why":"In meiosis I, the two homologous chromosomes separate. In meiosis II, the sister chromatids separate. Because crossing over rarely reaches the centromere, the region near the centromere keeps the identity of its original homolog. If an egg receives two X chromosomes that differ at the centromere, both homologs were kept together, which is nondisjunction in meiosis I. A meiosis II error would give two copies of the same homolog at the centromere. Nondisjunction after fertilization usually produces mosaicism, such as 46,XY/47,XXY, with a milder phenotype.","whyNot":["A paternal meiosis I error produces an XY sperm, so one of the two X chromosomes would be paternal. Here both are maternal.","A mitotic error after fertilization usually produces mosaicism. All 30 cells here were 47,XXY, so the error came before fertilization.","Correct.","A meiosis II error separates sister chromatids incorrectly, so both X chromosomes would be identical (homozygous) at centromeric markers."],"study":{"topics":["Meiosis I versus meiosis II nondisjunction","Mosaicism"],"comps":["RE-10","RE-3"]}},{"id":"RE-C02-Q3","stem":"Examination of the breasts shows a soft, rubbery, mobile disc of tissue centered under each areola. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Adipose tissue deposition without glandular tissue","Higher ratio of estrogen to androgen effect on the breast","Prolactin excess stimulating lobular milk production","Clonal proliferation of ductal epithelium after BRCA2 loss"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E","obj":"RE-13-E.15","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize gynecomastia: bilateral, symmetric, soft, mobile subareolar tissue in a man.","Link it to the hormone balance: estrogen stimulates ductal and stromal growth, and androgen opposes it.","In Klinefelter syndrome testosterone is low, and high LH drives testicular aromatase, so the estrogen to androgen ratio rises."],"why":"Gynecomastia is benign growth of breast ducts and surrounding stroma in a male. It occurs whenever estrogen effect outweighs androgen effect. In Klinefelter syndrome, testosterone is low and high LH increases aromatization of testosterone to estradiol in the testis, so the ratio shifts toward estrogen. Men with Klinefelter syndrome also have a much higher risk of male breast carcinoma than other men. A hard, eccentric, fixed mass would raise that concern and needs biopsy.","whyNot":["Fat without glandular tissue is pseudogynecomastia, seen in obesity. It feels soft and diffuse, with no firm disc under the areola.","Correct.","Prolactin causes galactorrhea and suppresses GnRH, but it does not explain soft bilateral glandular discs in this setting.","Clonal ductal proliferation describes carcinoma. Male breast carcinoma is linked to BRCA2 and Klinefelter syndrome, but it presents as a hard, usually one-sided mass, often eccentric and fixed."],"study":{"topics":["Gynecomastia mechanism","Male breast carcinoma risk factors"],"comps":["RE-13-E","RE-13-C6"]}},{"id":"RE-C02-Q4","stem":"A testicular biopsy shows hyalinized seminiferous tubules with no germ cells. Between the tubules are clusters of large polygonal cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm. These interstitial cells are the normal source of which of the following?","options":["Inhibin B","Testosterone","Androgen-binding protein","Anti-Mullerian hormone"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-2","obj":"RE-2.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the cells: they lie in the interstitium between tubules, not inside the tubules.","Large polygonal eosinophilic interstitial cells are Leydig cells.","Leydig cells make testosterone under LH stimulation. The other options are Sertoli cell products."],"why":"Leydig cells sit in the connective tissue between seminiferous tubules. They are large and polygonal, with eosinophilic, lipid-rich cytoplasm, and they make testosterone in response to LH. In Klinefelter syndrome, the tubules scar down and Leydig cells appear clustered (pseudohyperplasia), yet they produce too little testosterone. Sertoli cells sit inside the tubules on the basement membrane and make inhibin B, androgen-binding protein, and, in the fetus, anti-Mullerian hormone.","whyNot":["Inhibin B is made by Sertoli cells inside the tubules and feeds back to suppress FSH.","Correct.","Androgen-binding protein is made by Sertoli cells and keeps testosterone concentrated in the tubule lumen.","Anti-Mullerian hormone is made by fetal Sertoli cells and causes the paramesonephric ducts to regress."],"study":{"topics":["Testis histology","Leydig cell function"],"comps":["RE-2","RE-7"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C03","system":"RE","dx":"Fragile X syndrome in a teenage boy whose mother has primary ovarian insufficiency from an FMR1 premutation","codes":["RE-13-C3","RE-13-A5"],"case":"A 15-year-old boy is brought to the physician for evaluation of learning problems. He has moderate intellectual disability, avoids eye contact, and has hand-flapping behavior. Pulse is 74/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 116/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a long narrow face, a prominent jaw, large protruding ears, and hyperextensible finger joints. Pubertal development is Tanner stage 5, and each testis has a volume of 40 mL (normal 15 to 25). His 38-year-old mother has had no menstrual periods for 2 years, with hot flashes. Molecular testing shows more than 200 CGG repeats in the FMR1 gene of the boy (normal fewer than 45) and 85 repeats in his mother. Diagnosis: fragile X syndrome in the son and FMR1 premutation with primary ovarian insufficiency in the mother.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C03-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the absence of FMR1 protein in this patient's cells?","options":["Toxic gain of function from an expanded CAG polyglutamine tract","Premature stop codon from a nonsense point mutation in the gene","Abnormal mRNA processing from an intronic GAA repeat expansion","Methylation of the expanded repeat silencing transcription"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-13-C3","obj":"RE-13-C.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the mutation: more than 200 CGG repeats in the 5' untranslated region of FMR1 is a full mutation.","Recall what a full mutation does: the repeat and nearby promoter become hypermethylated, and transcription stops.","No mRNA means no FMR1 protein, which is needed for normal synapse function."],"why":"Fragile X syndrome is caused by expansion of a CGG repeat in the 5' untranslated region of FMR1 on the X chromosome. Above about 200 repeats, the repeat and the promoter become heavily methylated, the chromatin closes, and the gene is silenced. The repeat tends to expand further when passed through the mother, so each generation can be more severely affected (anticipation). The missing protein normally regulates translation at synapses, which explains the intellectual disability. The large testes appear after puberty.","whyNot":["An expanded CAG tract that adds polyglutamine to a protein causes Huntington disease and some spinocerebellar ataxias. These are toxic gain-of-function disorders.","A nonsense mutation can cause loss of function in many genes, but this patient's testing shows a repeat expansion, not a point mutation.","An intronic GAA expansion in the frataxin gene causes Friedreich ataxia by reducing transcription, not fragile X syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Trinucleotide repeat disorders","Gene silencing by DNA methylation"],"comps":["RE-13-C3","RE-10"]}},{"id":"RE-C03-Q2","stem":"The oocyte that gave rise to this patient was already present in his mother's ovary when she was born. At that time, the oocyte was arrested at which of the following stages?","options":["Prophase of meiosis I","Metaphase of meiosis II","Metaphase of meiosis I","Interphase before DNA replication"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-3","obj":"RE-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that all oogonia enter meiosis before birth, becoming primary oocytes.","Primary oocytes stop in prophase I (diplotene) and stay there until a surge of LH just before ovulation, which may be decades later.","Meiosis I finishes at ovulation, and the oocyte then arrests in metaphase II until fertilization."],"why":"Oogenesis starts before birth. Oogonia multiply in the fetal ovary, replicate their DNA, and enter meiosis I as primary oocytes, then stop in prophase I. Each one waits in a primordial follicle until it is selected for ovulation. Just before ovulation it completes meiosis I and releases the first polar body, then arrests again in metaphase II. Meiosis II finishes only if a sperm enters. Spermatogenesis differs: it starts at puberty, runs continuously, and each meiosis yields four sperm instead of one egg.","whyNot":["Correct.","Metaphase II is the second arrest point, reached at ovulation and held until fertilization. It occurs decades after birth.","Metaphase I is passed quickly during the hours before ovulation. It is not an arrest point.","Oogonia are in mitotic interphase, but by birth they have all entered meiosis. None remain as dividing oogonia."],"study":{"topics":["Oogenesis arrest points","Spermatogenesis versus oogenesis"],"comps":["RE-3","RE-2"]}},{"id":"RE-C03-Q3","stem":"Which of the following sets of serum findings is most likely in this patient's mother?","options":["Low FSH and high estradiol concentrations","Low FSH and low estradiol concentrations","High FSH and high estradiol concentrations","High FSH and low estradiol concentrations"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-A5","obj":"RE-13-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize primary ovarian insufficiency: loss of ovarian function with amenorrhea and hot flashes before age 40.","Link it to the FMR1 premutation (about 55 to 200 repeats), a known cause of early follicle loss.","Failure of the ovary itself means low estradiol, and loss of feedback means high FSH."],"why":"Primary ovarian insufficiency is loss of ovarian function before age 40. Causes include Turner syndrome, the FMR1 premutation, autoimmune oophoritis, and chemotherapy or radiation. Women with a premutation do not have fragile X syndrome, but about one in five develop ovarian insufficiency. With few follicles left, estradiol and inhibin fall, the pituitary loses negative feedback, and FSH rises, usually into the menopausal range. Low estradiol causes the hot flashes and amenorrhea.","whyNot":["Low FSH with high estradiol suggests an estrogen-secreting source, such as a granulosa cell tumor, suppressing the pituitary.","Low FSH with low estradiol is hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, from a hypothalamic or pituitary cause such as low body weight or a pituitary mass.","High FSH with high estradiol would mean the pituitary is overdriving a working ovary. That does not fit an ovary that has stopped producing follicles.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Primary ovarian insufficiency","FMR1 premutation"],"comps":["RE-13-A5","RE-13-C3"]}},{"id":"RE-C03-Q4","stem":"During measurement of testicular volume, the physician strokes the skin of the boy's upper medial thigh, and the testis on that side rises. The muscle layer of the spermatic cord responsible for this movement is continuous with which of the following structures?","options":["Internal oblique muscle","External oblique aponeurosis","Transversalis fascia","Parietal peritoneum"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-4","obj":"RE-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the reflex: stroking the medial thigh raises the testis, which is the cremasteric reflex.","The cremaster muscle and fascia form the middle covering of the spermatic cord.","Recall that each cord layer comes from an abdominal wall layer: the cremaster comes from the internal oblique."],"why":"As the testis descends through the inguinal canal, it carries a covering from each layer of the abdominal wall. The external spermatic fascia comes from the external oblique aponeurosis, the cremaster muscle and fascia come from the internal oblique, and the internal spermatic fascia comes from the transversalis fascia. The tunica vaginalis is a pouch of peritoneum around the testis. The cremaster contracts in the cremasteric reflex, and the motor limb of that reflex is the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve.","whyNot":["Correct.","The external oblique aponeurosis forms the external spermatic fascia, a thin outer layer with no muscle.","The transversalis fascia forms the internal spermatic fascia, the innermost covering of the cord.","The parietal peritoneum forms the tunica vaginalis around the testis, not a muscle layer of the cord."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the spermatic cord","Cremasteric reflex"],"comps":["RE-4","RE-1"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C04","system":"RE","dx":"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (46,XY)","codes":["RE-13-C9","RE-13-A1"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl is brought to the physician because she has never had a menstrual period. Breast development began at age 11. Height is 172 cm. Pulse is 68/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows Tanner stage 4 breasts and scant pubic and axillary hair. External genitalia appear female, and the vagina ends in a blind pouch 4 cm deep. A firm, mobile mass is palpated in each inguinal canal. Pelvic ultrasonography shows no uterus. Serum testosterone concentration is 640 ng/dL (normal adult female 15 to 70, adult male 300 to 1000), LH concentration is 19 mIU/mL (normal adult male 1.7 to 8.6), and estradiol concentration is 58 pg/mL (normal adult male 10 to 40). Karyotype is 46,XY. Diagnosis: complete androgen insensitivity syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C04-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains this patient's breast development?","options":["Estradiol secretion by residual ovarian tissue in a streak gonad","Adrenal estrogen secretion driven by increased ACTH stimulation","Aromatization of testosterone with no opposing androgen effect","Prolactin excess from a lactotroph adenoma of the pituitary gland"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-13-C9","obj":"RE-13-C.8","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the defect: a nonfunctional androgen receptor, so tissues cannot respond to testosterone or DHT.","The hypothalamus and pituitary also ignore testosterone, so LH and testosterone rise.","Aromatase in the testes and fat converts the extra testosterone to estradiol, which acts on the breast without androgen to oppose it."],"why":"Complete androgen insensitivity is an X-linked defect of the androgen receptor. Because androgen feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary does not work, LH rises and drives the testes to make more testosterone. Aromatase turns some of it into estradiol. Estrogen receptors work normally, and no androgen action opposes them, so breasts develop well at puberty. Pubic and axillary hair depend on androgen action, so they stay scant.","whyNot":["This patient has testes, not ovaries or streak gonads. The inguinal masses are the testes.","Adrenal estrogen excess with high ACTH is not part of this condition. Cortisol synthesis is normal.","Correct.","Prolactin causes milk secretion and suppresses gonadotropins. It would not raise LH and testosterone."],"study":{"topics":["Complete androgen insensitivity","Aromatase and estrogen effects"],"comps":["RE-13-C9","RE-8"]}},{"id":"RE-C04-Q2","stem":"A 46,XY newborn with 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency also has female-appearing external genitalia. Which of the following internal structures is most likely present in that newborn but absent in this patient?","options":["Upper third of the vagina","Uterus","Ductus deferens","Uterine tubes"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-7","obj":"RE-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Sort male internal structures by the hormone they need: Wolffian derivatives need testosterone, while external genitalia and prostate need DHT.","In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency testosterone acts normally, so Wolffian structures form. In androgen insensitivity neither hormone can act.","Both conditions have testes making anti-Mullerian hormone, so Mullerian structures are absent in both."],"why":"Testosterone acts directly on the androgen receptor to keep the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts and turn them into the epididymis, ductus deferens, and seminal vesicles. DHT, made from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase, is needed to form the penis, scrotum, and prostate. In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency the Wolffian structures form normally, but the external genitalia are undervirilized. In complete androgen insensitivity, the receptor fails, so neither Wolffian structures nor male external genitalia develop.","whyNot":["The upper third of the vagina is a paramesonephric derivative, so it is absent in both conditions.","The uterus is a paramesonephric derivative and regresses under anti-Mullerian hormone in both conditions.","Correct.","The uterine tubes come from the paramesonephric ducts. Anti-Mullerian hormone from the testes causes them to regress in both conditions."],"study":{"topics":["Testosterone versus DHT actions","5-alpha-reductase deficiency"],"comps":["RE-7","RE-8"]}},{"id":"RE-C04-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains the absence of a uterus in this patient?","options":["Testosterone from fetal Leydig cells","Anti-Mullerian hormone from fetal Sertoli cells","Dihydrotestosterone formed in the urogenital sinus","Failure of the paramesonephric ducts to form"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-1","obj":"RE-1.2","dok":2,"approach":["The uterus comes from the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts.","A 46,XY fetus with testes makes anti-Mullerian hormone from Sertoli cells, which causes those ducts to regress.","The androgen receptor defect does not affect anti-Mullerian hormone signaling, so the ducts regress normally."],"why":"SRY drives the gonad to become a testis with Sertoli cells. Fetal Sertoli cells secrete anti-Mullerian hormone, which acts through its own receptor to make the paramesonephric ducts regress. This pathway does not need the androgen receptor, so it works normally in androgen insensitivity. As a result, the uterus, tubes, and upper vagina are absent, and the vagina is a short blind pouch formed from the urogenital sinus.","whyNot":["Testosterone keeps the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts. It does not cause Mullerian regression, and it cannot act in this patient.","Correct.","DHT shapes the external genitalia and prostate. It has no role in Mullerian regression, and it cannot act here.","The paramesonephric ducts form in every embryo. Failure of the ducts to develop is the defect in Mullerian agenesis, which occurs in 46,XX individuals with ovaries."],"study":{"topics":["Anti-Mullerian hormone","Sexual differentiation of internal ducts"],"comps":["RE-1","RE-13-C9"]}},{"id":"RE-C04-Q4","stem":"The patient's 19-year-old cousin has had regular menstrual periods since age 12 but has had no menses for the past 4 months. She is sexually active and uses condoms inconsistently. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the cousin's amenorrhea?","options":["Pregnancy","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea","Hyperprolactinemia"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-A1","obj":"RE-13-A.1","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the amenorrhea: menses were regular and have now stopped for more than 3 months, so it is secondary.","Recall that pregnancy is the most common cause of secondary amenorrhea.","Contrast with the patient in the case, who has primary amenorrhea because she has never menstruated by age 15 despite breast development."],"why":"Primary amenorrhea means no menarche by age 15, or by age 13 with no breast development. Secondary amenorrhea means no menses for 3 months in someone who had regular cycles. The cousin has secondary amenorrhea, and pregnancy is the most common cause, so it is checked first with an hCG test. The case patient has primary amenorrhea from an absent uterus.","whyNot":["Correct.","Polycystic ovary syndrome usually causes irregular cycles from menarche onward, often with hirsutism, rather than sudden loss of regular cycles.","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea follows low energy intake, heavy exercise, or stress. Nothing in this history suggests it, and pregnancy is far more common.","Hyperprolactinemia can cause secondary amenorrhea, often with galactorrhea, but it is much less common than pregnancy."],"study":{"topics":["Primary versus secondary amenorrhea","Causes of secondary amenorrhea"],"comps":["RE-13-A1","RE-13-A"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C05","system":"RE","dx":"Mullerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome), with imperforate hymen as the key differential","codes":["RE-13-A1","RE-13-C5"],"case":"A 16-year-old girl comes to the physician because she has not had a menstrual period. She has no cyclic abdominal or pelvic pain. Breast development began at age 10. Height is 163 cm. Pulse is 72/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 110/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows Tanner stage 5 breasts and Tanner stage 5 pubic and axillary hair. External genitalia are normal, and the vagina is a blind pouch 2 cm deep with no bulging membrane. Pelvic ultrasonography shows normal ovaries and no uterus. Serum FSH concentration is 6 mIU/mL (normal 3 to 10), and testosterone concentration is 38 ng/dL (normal 15 to 70). Karyotype is 46,XX. Diagnosis: Mullerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome).","questions":[{"id":"RE-C05-Q1","stem":"Before the karyotype result was available, which of the following findings most strongly favored this diagnosis over complete androgen insensitivity?","options":["Tanner stage 5 pubic and axillary hair","Absent uterus on pelvic ultrasonography","Tanner stage 5 breast development","Vagina ending in a short blind pouch"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-C","obj":"RE-13-C.9","dok":3,"approach":["List what the two conditions share: primary amenorrhea, breast development, a blind vaginal pouch, and no uterus.","Find the difference: pubic and axillary hair need androgen action, which is absent in complete androgen insensitivity.","Normal hair growth means working androgen receptors, which fits a 46,XX patient with normal ovaries."],"why":"Both Mullerian agenesis and complete androgen insensitivity cause primary amenorrhea with breasts, a short blind vagina, and no uterus. In Mullerian agenesis the patient is 46,XX with normal ovaries and normal androgen action, so pubic and axillary hair are normal and testosterone is in the female range. In complete androgen insensitivity the patient is 46,XY with testes and no functioning androgen receptor, so body hair is scant and testosterone is in the male range.","whyNot":["Correct.","The uterus is absent in both: never formed in Mullerian agenesis, and regressed under anti-Mullerian hormone in androgen insensitivity.","Breasts develop in both conditions: from ovarian estrogen in Mullerian agenesis and from aromatized testosterone in androgen insensitivity.","A short blind vaginal pouch occurs in both, because the upper vagina is missing in each."],"study":{"topics":["Mullerian agenesis versus androgen insensitivity","Primary amenorrhea with a blind vaginal pouch"],"comps":["RE-13-C","RE-13-C9"]}},{"id":"RE-C05-Q2","stem":"The short vaginal pouch present in this patient most likely developed from which of the following embryonic structures?","options":["Mesonephric ducts","Urogenital sinus","Paramesonephric ducts","Genital tubercle"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-1","obj":"RE-1.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the paramesonephric ducts form the uterine tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina.","In this patient those ducts failed to develop, yet a short vagina is present, so it must have another source.","The lower vagina comes from the sinovaginal bulbs of the urogenital sinus."],"why":"The vagina has two origins. The paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts fuse in the midline to form the uterus, cervix, and upper vagina, while the uterine tubes come from their unfused upper parts. The lower vagina forms from the sinovaginal bulbs of the urogenital sinus, which grow up to meet the fused ducts. In Mullerian agenesis only the sinus-derived lower part forms, so the vagina is a short blind pouch. The ovaries come from the gonadal ridge, which is why they are normal.","whyNot":["The mesonephric ducts form the epididymis, ductus deferens, and seminal vesicles in males and regress in females.","Correct.","The paramesonephric ducts form the upper vagina, uterus, and tubes. These are the structures that failed to develop here.","The genital tubercle forms the clitoris in females and the glans penis in males."],"study":{"topics":["Paramesonephric duct derivatives","Development of the vagina"],"comps":["RE-1","RE-13-C"]}},{"id":"RE-C05-Q3","stem":"Which of the following sets of findings in a 14-year-old girl with no menses would instead indicate an imperforate hymen?","options":["Absent breast development, short stature, and a webbed neck","Clitoral enlargement, deepening voice, and increased muscle mass","Monthly pelvic pain and a bluish bulging introital membrane","Normal breasts, absent pubic hair, and bilateral inguinal masses"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-C5","obj":"RE-13-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["An imperforate hymen leaves the uterus and ovaries normal, so menses begin but cannot flow out.","Blood collects in the vagina (hematocolpos), stretching the hymen and causing pain each month.","Pick the option with cyclic pain and a bulging blue-tinged membrane."],"why":"The hymen is a thin membrane at the junction of the vaginal plate and the urogenital sinus that normally opens late in fetal life or around birth. If it stays closed, puberty proceeds normally and menstruation starts, but blood is trapped behind the membrane. The girl has primary amenorrhea with cyclic pelvic pain, and the membrane bulges and looks bluish from the blood behind it. A large hematocolpos can press on the bladder or rectum. Mullerian agenesis, by contrast, causes no cyclic pain because there is no endometrium.","whyNot":["Absent breasts, short stature, and a webbed neck describe Turner syndrome with streak gonads.","Clitoral enlargement, a deep voice, and more muscle mass are signs of virilization from androgen excess, not an outflow obstruction.","Correct.","Normal breasts with absent pubic hair and inguinal testes describe complete androgen insensitivity."],"study":{"topics":["Imperforate hymen","Outflow tract causes of primary amenorrhea"],"comps":["RE-13-C5","RE-13-A1"]}},{"id":"RE-C05-Q4","stem":"This patient's normal breast and pubic hair development shows that her hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis activated normally. The onset of gonadal puberty in this patient was most likely marked by which of the following?","options":["A midcycle surge of LH from estradiol feedback","Pulses of LH secretion occurring during sleep","A rise in pituitary secretion of prolactin","Continuous, nonpulsatile secretion of GnRH"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-7","obj":"RE-7.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that puberty begins when the hypothalamus restarts pulsatile GnRH release after the quiet years of childhood.","The first sign of this in the blood is LH pulses that appear at night during sleep.","Over time the pulses spread to the daytime, estradiol rises, and breast development begins."],"why":"GnRH secretion is active in infancy, then suppressed through childhood. At puberty, the GnRH pulse generator reawakens, and the earliest sign is sleep-associated pulses of LH. Rising FSH and LH stimulate the ovaries to make estradiol. In girls the usual order is breast budding (thelarche), then pubic hair (pubarche), then the growth spurt, then menarche. This patient followed that sequence except menarche, which cannot occur without a uterus.","whyNot":["The midcycle LH surge triggers ovulation in established cycles. It needs a mature follicle making high estradiol and comes late in puberty.","Correct.","Prolactin rises in pregnancy and with pituitary tumors. High prolactin suppresses GnRH and delays puberty.","Continuous GnRH desensitizes the gonadotroph receptors and suppresses LH and FSH. Puberty needs pulsatile GnRH."],"study":{"topics":["Onset of puberty","Sequence of female pubertal changes"],"comps":["RE-7","RE-6"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C06","system":"RE","dx":"Classic 21-hydroxylase deficiency with virilized genitalia in a 46,XX newborn","codes":["RE-13-C9"],"case":"A 10-day-old infant is brought to the emergency department because of poor feeding and vomiting for 2 days. At birth, the external genitalia were noted to be ambiguous. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 182/min, respirations are 48/min, and blood pressure is 58/32 mm Hg. Physical examination shows dry mucous membranes, an enlarged clitoris, and partially fused, rugated labioscrotal folds. No gonads are palpable. Serum sodium concentration is 126 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), and potassium concentration is 7.0 mEq/L (normal 3.7 to 5.9). Serum 17-hydroxyprogesterone concentration is more than 50 times the upper limit of normal for age. Pelvic ultrasonography shows a uterus and two ovaries. Karyotype is 46,XX. Diagnosis: classic salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C06-Q1","stem":"Which of the following conditions causes the same category of disorder of sex development as this infant's condition?","options":["Complete androgen insensitivity","5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency","45,X/46,XY mixed gonadal dysgenesis","Maternal luteoma of pregnancy"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-C9","obj":"RE-13-C.7","dok":3,"approach":["Classify this infant: 46,XX with ovaries and virilized external genitalia is a 46,XX disorder of sex development (formerly female pseudohermaphroditism).","The cause is androgen exposure of a female fetus, from the fetal adrenal here.","Pick the other source of androgen exposure: an androgen-secreting tumor in the mother."],"why":"In a 46,XX disorder of sex development, the gonads are ovaries and the internal organs are female, but the external genitalia are virilized by androgen during fetal life. The two classic sources are the fetal adrenal in congenital adrenal hyperplasia and the mother, for example an androgen-secreting luteoma of pregnancy or androgen medication. In a 46,XY disorder of sex development (formerly male pseudohermaphroditism), the gonads are testes, but the external genitalia are undervirilized, as in androgen insensitivity or 5-alpha-reductase deficiency.","whyNot":["Complete androgen insensitivity is a 46,XY disorder of sex development with testes and undervirilized, female-appearing genitalia.","5-alpha-reductase deficiency is a 46,XY disorder of sex development, with testes and undervirilized genitalia from low DHT.","Mixed gonadal dysgenesis is a sex chromosome disorder of sex development, usually with a testis on one side and a streak gonad on the other.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["46,XX versus 46,XY disorders of sex development","Causes of virilization in a female fetus"],"comps":["RE-13-C9","RE-1"]}},{"id":"RE-C06-Q2","stem":"Because of this infant's enzyme block, 17-hydroxylated 21-carbon precursors accumulate and are converted to 19-carbon androgens such as DHEA and androstenedione. Which of the following enzyme activities carries out this conversion?","options":["17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","Aromatase","17,20-lyase","11-beta-hydroxylase"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-8","obj":"RE-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["21-hydroxylase deficiency blocks production of cortisol and aldosterone, so precursors such as 17-hydroxyprogesterone build up.","Low cortisol raises ACTH, which pushes even more precursor into the pathway.","To become sex steroids, these 21-carbon precursors must lose their side chain, which is the 17,20-lyase step that forms androstenedione and DHEA."],"why":"Sex steroid synthesis begins with cholesterol side-chain cleavage to pregnenolone. 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase converts pregnenolone to progesterone. 17-alpha-hydroxylase adds a hydroxyl group at carbon 17, and 17,20-lyase then cuts off the side chain to make 19-carbon androgens such as androstenedione. 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase converts androstenedione to testosterone, and aromatase converts androgens to estrogens. In 21-hydroxylase deficiency the 17,20-lyase step carries the trapped precursors into androgens that virilize a female fetus.","whyNot":["17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase acts later, converting androstenedione to testosterone. It does not remove the side chain from 21-carbon precursors.","Aromatase converts androgens to estrogens. It acts after androstenedione is formed and would lower androgen, not raise it.","Correct.","11-beta-hydroxylase acts after 21-hydroxylase in the cortisol and aldosterone pathways. Its substrates are not formed when 21-hydroxylase is blocked."],"study":{"topics":["Sex steroid synthesis pathway","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia"],"comps":["RE-8","EN-8"]}},{"id":"RE-C06-Q3","stem":"A different infant is found to have a 46,XX karyotype in which the SRY gene has been translocated onto one X chromosome. Which of the following gonads is most likely present in that infant?","options":["Bilateral ovaries","One ovary and one ovotestis","Bilateral streak gonads","Bilateral testes"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-1","obj":"RE-1.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that SRY is the switch that makes the bipotential gonad become a testis.","It acts by turning on SOX9 in the supporting cells, which then become Sertoli cells.","If SRY is present, testes form, whatever the rest of the karyotype."],"why":"The early gonad can become either an ovary or a testis. SRY on the Y chromosome turns on SOX9 in supporting cells, which become Sertoli cells and organize testis cords. Once testes form, Sertoli cells make anti-Mullerian hormone and Leydig cells make testosterone, and male development follows. A 46,XX individual with SRY on an X chromosome develops testes and a male phenotype but is infertile, because other Y genes needed for spermatogenesis are missing. In this case's infant, with no SRY, the gonads became ovaries.","whyNot":["Ovaries form when SRY is absent, as in this case's 46,XX infant without an SRY translocation.","An ovary with an ovotestis describes ovotesticular disorder of sex development, which is usually 46,XX without detectable SRY.","Streak gonads form when germ cells are lost, as in Turner syndrome, or in 46,XY individuals with an SRY deletion or mutation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["SRY and gonadal determination","46,XX testicular disorder of sex development"],"comps":["RE-1","RE-10"]}},{"id":"RE-C06-Q4","stem":"A 31-year-old woman develops a deepening voice, temporal balding, and clitoral enlargement over 4 months. Imaging is pending. Reference ranges are testosterone 15 to 70 ng/dL and DHEA-S 35 to 430 µg/dL. Which of the following serum results most strongly localizes her androgen excess to the adrenal gland?","options":["Testosterone 45 ng/dL and DHEA-S 150 µg/dL","Testosterone 95 ng/dL and DHEA-S 210 µg/dL","Testosterone 230 ng/dL and DHEA-S 960 µg/dL","Testosterone 260 ng/dL and DHEA-S 190 µg/dL"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-13-A","obj":"RE-13-A.7","dok":3,"approach":["Separate hirsutism (excess hair only) from virilization (voice change, balding, clitoral enlargement, muscle gain). Rapid virilization suggests an androgen-secreting tumor.","Use the markers: DHEA-S is made almost entirely by the adrenal, while testosterone comes from both the ovary and adrenal.","A very high DHEA-S points to the adrenal. A very high testosterone with normal DHEA-S points to the ovary."],"why":"Hirsutism is male-pattern hair growth in a woman and is often caused by polycystic ovary syndrome. Virilization means more androgen effect: a deeper voice, temporal balding, clitoral enlargement, and more muscle mass. Rapid virilization suggests a tumor. DHEA-S is a sulfated androgen made almost only by the adrenal cortex, so a markedly raised level localizes the source to the adrenal, as in an adrenal carcinoma or congenital adrenal hyperplasia. A testosterone well above the female range with normal DHEA-S points to the ovary, as in a Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor.","whyNot":["Both values are normal. This fits idiopathic hirsutism and would not explain rapid virilization.","A mildly raised testosterone with normal DHEA-S is typical of polycystic ovary syndrome, which causes hirsutism but rarely virilization.","Correct.","A markedly raised testosterone with normal DHEA-S localizes the source to the ovary, such as a Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor."],"study":{"topics":["Hirsutism versus virilization","Localizing androgen excess"],"comps":["RE-13-A","RE-13-J"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C07","system":"RE","dx":"Testicular seminoma in a man with a history of cryptorchidism","codes":["RE-13-J9","RE-13-C1"],"case":"A 32-year-old man comes to the physician because of a painless, heavy feeling in his right testis for 3 months. He had orchiopexy for an undescended right testis at age 3 years. Pulse is 72/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 124/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a firm, nontender, enlarged right testis that does not transilluminate. Ultrasonography shows a solid, uniform hypoechoic mass within the testis. Serum studies show an AFP concentration of 4 ng/mL (normal less than 10), beta-hCG concentration of 14 mIU/mL (normal less than 5), and LDH concentration of 420 U/L (normal 100 to 250). He undergoes radical inguinal orchiectomy. Microscopy shows sheets of uniform cells with clear cytoplasm and central nuclei, separated by fibrous septa containing lymphocytes. The tumor cells stain for placental alkaline phosphatase. Diagnosis: classic seminoma.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C07-Q1","stem":"Which of the following serum findings, if present, would indicate a nonseminomatous component in this tumor?","options":["Increased lactate dehydrogenase","Increased alpha-fetoprotein","Mildly increased beta-hCG","Increased placental alkaline phosphatase"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J9","obj":"RE-13-J.18","dok":3,"approach":["Recall which germ cell tumors make which markers: yolk sac tumor makes AFP, choriocarcinoma makes high hCG, and seminoma makes neither AFP nor much hCG.","A mildly raised hCG can occur in pure seminoma from scattered syncytiotrophoblastic cells.","Any raised AFP means a nonseminomatous element, usually yolk sac tumor or embryonal carcinoma."],"why":"Seminoma is the most common testicular germ cell tumor. It has sheets of uniform clear cells with lymphocytes in fibrous septa and stains for placental alkaline phosphatase. It is very sensitive to radiation and chemotherapy and has a good prognosis. Pure seminoma does not make AFP. A raised AFP means the tumor contains a nonseminomatous part, such as yolk sac tumor, which changes staging and treatment. Choriocarcinoma makes very high hCG, embryonal carcinoma may raise AFP or hCG, and teratoma usually raises neither.","whyNot":["LDH reflects tumor bulk and is raised in many germ cell tumors, including seminoma, so it does not separate types.","Correct.","A small rise in hCG occurs in some pure seminomas because of scattered syncytiotrophoblastic giant cells. Very high hCG would suggest choriocarcinoma.","Placental alkaline phosphatase is a tissue stain that is positive in seminoma. It supports the diagnosis rather than pointing to another type."],"study":{"topics":["Testicular tumor markers","Seminoma versus nonseminomatous germ cell tumors"],"comps":["RE-13-J9","RE-13-J"]}},{"id":"RE-C07-Q2","stem":"Cytogenetic analysis of this tumor is most likely to show which of the following abnormalities?","options":["Translocation t(11;22)","Translocation t(14;18)","Amplification of MYCN","Isochromosome of 12p"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-13-J9","obj":"RE-13-J.17","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the tumor: seminoma is a germ cell tumor, the group that makes up most testicular tumors.","Recall the shared genetic marker of germ cell tumors: extra copies of the short arm of chromosome 12, most often as an isochromosome 12p."],"why":"About 95% of testicular tumors are germ cell tumors. Most arise from germ cell neoplasia in situ, abnormal germ cells within the seminiferous tubules. Nearly all of these tumors have extra copies of 12p, most often as an isochromosome in which both arms are 12p. Risk factors include cryptorchidism, even after orchiopexy, and Klinefelter syndrome. Sex cord-stromal tumors, such as Leydig cell tumors, are the other main group and do not share this marker.","whyNot":["t(11;22) joins EWSR1 and FLI1 and is the marker of Ewing sarcoma of bone and soft tissue.","t(14;18) places BCL2 next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain gene and is the marker of follicular lymphoma.","MYCN amplification is a marker of aggressive neuroblastoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Genetics of germ cell tumors","Classification of testicular tumors"],"comps":["RE-13-J9","RE-10"]}},{"id":"RE-C07-Q3","stem":"Before his orchiopexy, the undescended testis was at risk for impaired spermatogenesis. Which of the following best explains this risk?","options":["Exposure of germ cells to core body temperature","Ischemia from compression of the testicular artery","Lack of LH stimulation of the Leydig cells","Autoimmune injury after breakdown of the blood-testis barrier"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-C1","obj":"RE-13-C.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that spermatogenesis needs a temperature a few degrees below core body temperature, which the scrotum provides.","An undescended testis sits in the inguinal canal or abdomen at core temperature.","Germ cells are injured by heat, while Leydig cells are relatively spared."],"why":"The scrotum keeps the testes cooler than the body core through the pampiniform plexus countercurrent exchange, the dartos and cremaster muscles, and its thin skin. An undescended testis, most often in the inguinal canal, stays at core temperature, and its germ cells are damaged over time. Sertoli cell function also declines, so inhibin B falls and FSH rises. The cryptorchid testis also carries a higher risk of germ cell tumor, which persists after orchiopexy. Cryptorchidism is more common in preterm infants.","whyNot":["Correct.","Compression of the testicular artery is not the usual mechanism. Acute loss of blood flow happens with torsion, which causes sudden pain.","LH stimulation is not the problem. Leydig cells are relatively spared by heat, so testosterone is usually normal in unilateral cryptorchidism.","Breakdown of the blood-testis barrier can expose sperm antigens after trauma or vasectomy, but it is not the main problem in an undescended testis."],"study":{"topics":["Cryptorchidism","Temperature regulation of the testis"],"comps":["RE-13-C1","RE-4"]}},{"id":"RE-C07-Q4","stem":"The patient's 2-month-old son has a right scrotal swelling that transilluminates. His parents report that it is small on waking and larger by evening. Which of the following best explains the swelling?","options":["Patent processus vaginalis","Dilated pampiniform venous plexus","Torsion of the appendix testis","Persistent gubernacular cord"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-1","obj":"RE-1.5","dok":3,"approach":["A swelling that transilluminates is filled with clear fluid, so it is a hydrocele.","A change in size through the day means the fluid moves between the peritoneal cavity and the scrotum.","That connection is a processus vaginalis that never closed, which is a communicating hydrocele."],"why":"During descent, the gubernaculum guides the testis through the inguinal canal into the scrotum, and a pouch of peritoneum, the processus vaginalis, goes ahead of it. Normally the processus closes, leaving only the tunica vaginalis around the testis. If it stays open with a narrow channel, peritoneal fluid flows in and out, which is a communicating hydrocele that changes size with activity and position. If the channel is wide enough for bowel, the result is an indirect inguinal hernia.","whyNot":["Correct.","A dilated pampiniform plexus is a varicocele. It feels like a bag of worms, usually on the left, and does not transilluminate.","Torsion of the appendix testis causes sudden pain and a small tender nodule at the upper pole, sometimes with a blue dot sign.","The gubernaculum guides descent and becomes the scrotal ligament. It does not hold fluid."],"study":{"topics":["Testicular descent","Communicating hydrocele and indirect hernia"],"comps":["RE-1","RE-4"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C08","system":"RE","dx":"Hypospadias in a newborn boy","codes":["RE-13-C4","RE-13-C2"],"case":"A male newborn is examined in the nursery 1 day after an uncomplicated delivery at 38 weeks' gestation. Birth weight is 3100 g. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 140/min, and respirations are 44/min. Physical examination shows the urethral opening on the ventral surface of the penis at the junction of the glans and shaft. The foreskin is incomplete ventrally and forms a dorsal hood, and the penis curves ventrally. The scrotum is fused and normally formed. The left testis is in the scrotum, and the right testis is palpable in the inguinal canal. The remainder of the examination shows no abnormalities. Diagnosis: distal hypospadias with right cryptorchidism.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C08-Q1","stem":"A different male newborn has a urethral opening on the dorsal surface of the penis. Which of the following associated anomalies should be sought in that infant?","options":["Undescended testis","Horseshoe kidney","Posterior urethral valves","Exstrophy of the bladder"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-C2","obj":"RE-13-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["A dorsal urethral opening is epispadias, a different defect from hypospadias.","Epispadias comes from abnormal position of the genital tubercle, which is also part of the lower abdominal wall defect seen in bladder exstrophy.","Hypospadias, by contrast, goes with cryptorchidism and inguinal hernia."],"why":"Hypospadias is a ventral urethral opening caused by incomplete fusion of the urethral folds. It is common and is associated with undescended testes and inguinal hernia, as in this infant. Epispadias is a dorsal opening caused by abnormal positioning of the genital tubercle relative to the cloacal membrane. It is rare and is strongly associated with exstrophy of the bladder, in which the bladder is open on the lower abdominal wall.","whyNot":["Undescended testis is associated with hypospadias, as this infant shows, not specifically with epispadias.","Horseshoe kidney is fusion of the lower poles of the kidneys, seen with Turner syndrome and trisomy 18. It is not linked to a dorsal meatus.","Posterior urethral valves are folds in the prostatic urethra that obstruct urine flow in boys. They are not linked to a dorsal meatus.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypospadias versus epispadias","Bladder exstrophy"],"comps":["RE-13-C2","RE-13-C4"]}},{"id":"RE-C08-Q2","stem":"The embryonic structures whose incomplete fusion caused this infant's defect develop into which of the following in a female?","options":["Clitoris","Labia minora","Labia majora","Lower vagina"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-1","obj":"RE-1.4","dok":2,"approach":["Hypospadias results from incomplete fusion of the urethral (urogenital) folds on the underside of the developing penis.","In males these folds close to form the penile urethra and the ventral shaft.","In females they stay separate and become the labia minora."],"why":"The external genitalia start the same in both sexes. The genital tubercle becomes the glans penis or the clitoris. The urethral (urogenital) folds fuse in males to enclose the penile urethra and form the ventral shaft, and stay open in females as the labia minora. The labioscrotal swellings fuse to form the scrotum or stay apart as the labia majora. Fusion of the urethral folds depends on DHT, and when it stops early the urethra opens on the ventral surface.","whyNot":["The clitoris comes from the genital tubercle, which forms the glans penis in males.","Correct.","The labia majora come from the labioscrotal swellings, which form the scrotum in males. This infant's scrotum is normal.","The lower vagina comes from the urogenital sinus, not from the external genital folds."],"study":{"topics":["Homologues of the external genitalia","Urethral fold fusion"],"comps":["RE-1","RE-13-C4"]}},{"id":"RE-C08-Q3","stem":"Another infant, who is 46,XY, has perineal hypospadias, a bifid scrotum, and testes in the labioscrotal folds. Serum testosterone concentration is normal for age, and the ratio of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone is increased. If untreated, which of the following is most likely to occur in that child at puberty?","options":["Regression of the testes to streak gonads","Onset of menses from a hypoplastic uterus","Virilization with phallic growth","Breast development without body hair"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-8","obj":"RE-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["A normal testosterone with a high testosterone to DHT ratio means 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency.","Before birth, DHT is needed to form male external genitalia, so they are undervirilized.","At puberty, testosterone rises sharply and can act on the androgen receptor directly, and some DHT forms through the type 1 enzyme, so the body virilizes."],"why":"5-alpha-reductase type 2 converts testosterone to DHT in genital skin and the prostate. DHT binds the androgen receptor more strongly than testosterone, and it is needed to form the penis, scrotum, and prostate before birth. Without it, the infant has ambiguous or female-appearing genitalia, but normal Wolffian structures and testes. At puberty the large rise in testosterone, plus some DHT from the type 1 enzyme, causes marked virilization, with phallic growth, deepening voice, and muscle gain. Beard growth and male-pattern balding stay limited because they depend on DHT.","whyNot":["The testes in this condition are structurally normal and do not regress. Streak gonads occur with gonadal dysgenesis.","Anti-Mullerian hormone from the testes caused the uterus to regress before birth, so there is no uterus to menstruate.","Correct.","Breast development without body hair describes complete androgen insensitivity, where androgens cannot act at all."],"study":{"topics":["5-alpha-reductase deficiency","Testosterone versus DHT"],"comps":["RE-8","RE-7"]}},{"id":"RE-C08-Q4","stem":"Twenty years later, the patient hears a snapping sound during sexual intercourse, followed by immediate loss of erection and rapidly expanding swelling and bruising of the penile shaft. He voids normally with no blood at the meatus. Which of the following structures is most likely torn?","options":["Tunica albuginea of a corpus cavernosum","Deep dorsal vein of the penis","Suspensory ligament of the penis","Corpus spongiosum and the enclosed urethra"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-4","obj":"RE-4.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize penile fracture: a snap, sudden detumescence, and a swollen, bruised shaft after forceful bending of the erect penis.","During erection the tunica albuginea around each corpus cavernosum is stretched thin and tense, so it can tear.","Normal voiding with no blood at the meatus makes injury to the corpus spongiosum and urethra less likely."],"why":"The penis contains three erectile bodies. The paired corpora cavernosa lie dorsally and fill with blood during erection, and the corpus spongiosum lies ventrally around the urethra and expands to form the glans. A tough fibrous sheath, the tunica albuginea, surrounds the corpora cavernosa. In erection it becomes thin and rigid, so sudden bending can tear it. Blood escapes from the corpus, causing the swelling and bruising. A tear that extends into the corpus spongiosum can injure the urethra, which shows up as blood at the meatus or trouble voiding.","whyNot":["Correct.","A torn deep dorsal vein can cause bruising, but it does not produce the snap and sudden loss of erection of a corporal tear.","The suspensory ligament holds the penis to the pubic symphysis. Its injury does not cause sudden loss of erection with shaft hematoma.","Injury to the corpus spongiosum often involves the urethra, with blood at the meatus or difficulty voiding, which this patient does not have."],"study":{"topics":["Erectile tissue of the penis","Penile fracture"],"comps":["RE-4","RE-6"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C09","system":"RE","dx":"Tubal ectopic pregnancy in an Rh-negative woman with prior salpingitis","codes":["RE-13-B7"],"case":"A 28-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of left lower abdominal pain and vaginal spotting for 1 day. Her last menstrual period was 7 weeks ago. Three years ago she was treated for salpingitis caused by Chlamydia trachomatis. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Physical examination shows left adnexal tenderness. Hemoglobin concentration is 11.0 g/dL (normal 12 to 16). Serum beta-hCG concentration is 2400 mIU/mL, a level at which an intrauterine gestational sac is usually visible. Transvaginal ultrasonography shows no intrauterine gestational sac, a left adnexal mass, and a small amount of free pelvic fluid. Her blood type is O, Rh(D)-negative; her partner is Rh(D)-positive. Diagnosis: left ampullary tubal ectopic pregnancy.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C09-Q1","stem":"In a later pregnancy, the blastocyst implants in the lower uterine segment, and the placenta develops over the internal cervical os. Which of the following is the most likely complication of this placental position?","options":["Painless vaginal bleeding in the third trimester","Severe hypertension with new proteinuria","Uterine rupture early in the course of labor","Painful vaginal bleeding with a rigid, tender uterus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-3","obj":"RE-3.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the condition: a placenta covering the internal os is placenta previa, a form of abnormal implantation site, as is tubal ectopic pregnancy.","As the lower uterine segment stretches and the cervix begins to change late in pregnancy, the placenta separates from its attachment.","Separation exposes maternal vessels, causing painless, bright red bleeding."],"why":"Normal implantation occurs in the upper posterior wall of the uterine body. Implantation outside the uterus, most often in the ampulla of the uterine tube, is an ectopic pregnancy, and prior salpingitis is a major risk factor. Implantation low in the uterus can place the placenta over the internal os, which is placenta previa. In the third trimester the lower segment thins and stretches, the placenta shears off, and painless vaginal bleeding results. Placenta previa also raises the risk of placenta accreta, especially after a prior cesarean delivery.","whyNot":["Correct.","Severe hypertension with proteinuria after 20 weeks is preeclampsia, which relates to abnormal spiral artery remodeling, not placental position.","Uterine rupture is linked mainly to a prior uterine scar, such as a previous cesarean delivery, not to placenta previa alone.","Painful bleeding with a rigid, tender uterus describes placental abruption, a premature separation of a normally placed placenta."],"study":{"topics":["Placenta previa","Sites of ectopic implantation"],"comps":["RE-3","RE-6"]}},{"id":"RE-C09-Q2","stem":"At laparoscopy, thin fibrous adhesions are seen stretching between the anterior surface of the liver and the diaphragm. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Ascending cholangitis from biliary obstruction","Hematogenous seeding of the liver forming an abscess","Adhesions from leakage of blood from the ectopic pregnancy","Perihepatitis from spread of the earlier tubal infection"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-B7","obj":"RE-13-B.8","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the violin-string adhesions between liver capsule and diaphragm as Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome.","Link it to her earlier chlamydial salpingitis: organisms spread from the open end of the tube along the right paracolic gutter to the liver capsule.","The same past infection scarred the tube, which is why the embryo implanted there."],"why":"Salpingitis caused by Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae can damage the tube and the peritoneum. Scarring of the tubal lining and loss of cilia slow transport of the embryo, which raises the risk of ectopic pregnancy and tubal infertility. Infection can also form a hydrosalpinx or a tubo-ovarian abscess. When organisms spread up the right paracolic gutter, they inflame the liver capsule, causing right upper quadrant pain at the time and later leaving violin-string adhesions. This is Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome.","whyNot":["Ascending cholangitis is infection inside the bile ducts, with fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain. It does not form capsular adhesions to the diaphragm.","A pyogenic liver abscess is a collection inside the liver tissue, not thin adhesions on the surface.","Blood from a tubal pregnancy collects in the pelvis. It does not produce organized adhesions on the liver surface.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of salpingitis","Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome"],"comps":["RE-13-B7","RE-13-B6"]}},{"id":"RE-C09-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best describes the process that could harm a later Rh(D)-positive fetus if this patient becomes sensitized by fetal red cells during this ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Fetal anti-D IgG damaging maternal red cells in the placenta","Maternal anti-D IgM crossing the placenta to activate complement","Maternal cytotoxic T cells entering the fetus to lyse fetal red cells","Maternal anti-D IgG crossing the placenta to coat fetal red cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-11","obj":"RE-11.4","dok":3,"approach":["Fetal red cells that enter the circulation of an Rh-negative mother trigger an antibody response against the D antigen.","The first response is IgM, but memory B cells switch to IgG, and IgG crosses the placenta by FcRn transport.","In a later Rh-positive pregnancy, IgG coats fetal red cells, and fetal macrophages destroy them. This is type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"Fetal blood can enter the maternal circulation during delivery, miscarriage, abortion, ectopic pregnancy, or trauma. An Rh(D)-negative mother exposed to Rh(D)-positive fetal cells can form anti-D antibodies. The first pregnancy is usually spared because the early response is mainly IgM and is slow. In a later Rh-positive pregnancy, memory cells quickly make anti-D IgG, which crosses the placenta, binds fetal red cells, and leads to their destruction in the fetal spleen. Severe hemolysis causes anemia, heart failure, and hydrops fetalis. Anti-D immune globulin given after the ectopic pregnancy prevents sensitization.","whyNot":["The fetus does not make antibodies against its mother's red cells. The harmful antibody comes from the mother.","IgM is a large pentamer and does not cross the placenta, so it cannot reach the fetus.","Hemolytic disease of the fetus is antibody-mediated (type II). Maternal T cells do not cause it.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Rh alloimmunization","Type II hypersensitivity"],"comps":["RE-11","IM-15-C"]}},{"id":"RE-C09-Q4","stem":"Fertilization of the oocyte took place in the ampulla of the uterine tube. Immediately after the first sperm fused with the oocyte membrane, which of the following events prevented additional sperm from entering?","options":["Acrosomal release of enzymes from the sperm head","Exocytosis of cortical granules that alters the zona","Completion of meiosis I with release of a polar body","Capacitation of sperm within the uterine tube"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-3","obj":"RE-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the block to polyspermy comes from the oocyte, not the sperm.","Sperm fusion triggers a rise in calcium inside the oocyte.","Calcium releases cortical granules, whose enzymes change the zona pellucida so other sperm cannot bind or pass through."],"why":"Fertilization usually occurs in the ampulla, the widest part of the uterine tube. Capacitated sperm undergo the acrosome reaction, release enzymes, and penetrate the corona radiata and zona pellucida. When the first sperm fuses with the oocyte, a wave of calcium causes cortical granules to release their contents. Their enzymes modify the zona pellucida so it no longer binds sperm, which is the cortical (zona) reaction. The oocyte then completes meiosis II, releases the second polar body, and forms the female pronucleus.","whyNot":["The acrosome reaction lets a sperm penetrate the zona pellucida. It helps fertilization rather than blocking extra sperm.","Correct.","Meiosis I is completed before ovulation. At fertilization the oocyte completes meiosis II.","Capacitation happens before fertilization and prepares sperm to bind the zona. It does not block polyspermy."],"study":{"topics":["Fertilization and the cortical reaction","Block to polyspermy"],"comps":["RE-3","RE-2"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C10","system":"RE","dx":"Septate uterus with recurrent pregnancy loss","codes":["RE-13-C10"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of three consecutive pregnancy losses at 9 to 11 weeks' gestation. Her menstrual cycles are regular every 28 days. Pulse is 70/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 114/72 mm Hg. Pelvic examination shows a single cervix and a normal vagina. Three-dimensional ultrasonography shows a uterus with a smooth, convex outer fundal contour and a midline septum dividing the upper uterine cavity into two parts. Both kidneys are normal. Karyotypes of both partners are normal, antiphospholipid antibody tests are negative, and serum TSH concentration is 2.1 µU/mL (normal 0.5 to 5.0). Diagnosis: septate uterus with recurrent pregnancy loss.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following developmental errors best explains this patient's ultrasonographic findings?","options":["Complete failure of the paramesonephric ducts to fuse","Incomplete fusion of the paramesonephric ducts at the fundus","Failure to resorb the midline wall after duct fusion","Failure of one paramesonephric duct to develop"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-C10","obj":"RE-13-C.10","dok":3,"approach":["The uterus forms when the paramesonephric ducts fuse in the midline, and the wall between them is then resorbed.","A normal outer fundal contour means fusion was complete. A midline septum inside means the wall between the ducts did not resorb.","Bicornuate and didelphys uteri show a notched or doubled outer contour because fusion itself failed."],"why":"The two paramesonephric ducts first fuse side by side, which shapes the outer uterus, and then the septum between them is resorbed to create one cavity. In a septate uterus, fusion is complete, so the outer contour is smooth, but the septum persists. The septum is fibromuscular and poorly vascularized, so an embryo that implants on it is poorly supplied, which leads to pregnancy loss. Abnormal fusion or canalization lower down can leave a longitudinal or transverse vaginal septum.","whyNot":["Complete failure of fusion produces uterus didelphys, with two separate uterine bodies and two cervices. This patient has one cervix.","Partial failure of fusion at the top produces a bicornuate uterus, with a heart-shaped outer contour and a notch in the fundus.","Correct.","Failure of one duct produces a unicornuate uterus, often with a kidney anomaly on the side of the missing duct. Her kidneys are normal."],"study":{"topics":["Mullerian fusion anomalies","Septate versus bicornuate uterus"],"comps":["RE-13-C10","RE-1"]}},{"id":"RE-C10-Q2","stem":"During a later pregnancy, removal of the corpus luteum at 6 weeks' gestation would likely cause pregnancy loss, but removal at 14 weeks would not. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["hCG concentration peaks and directly supports the decidua","The placenta becomes the main source of progesterone","The fetal adrenal gland begins to secrete progesterone","Maternal pituitary LH output rises after 12 weeks"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-6","obj":"RE-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Early pregnancy needs progesterone to keep the decidua intact, and at first the corpus luteum supplies it.","hCG from the syncytiotrophoblast acts on LH receptors to keep the corpus luteum alive.","Near the end of the first trimester, the placenta makes enough progesterone by itself, so the corpus luteum is no longer needed."],"why":"After implantation, the syncytiotrophoblast secretes hCG, which acts like LH and keeps the corpus luteum making progesterone. Progesterone maintains the decidua and keeps the myometrium quiet. The placenta also makes progesterone from maternal cholesterol, and between about 7 and 10 weeks its output becomes large enough to replace the corpus luteum. By the end of the first trimester the corpus luteum is no longer needed. This is the luteal-placental shift. Placental estriol is different, because the placenta lacks 17-alpha-hydroxylase and needs DHEA-S from the fetal adrenal to make it.","whyNot":["hCG peaks at about 8 to 10 weeks and then falls. It supports the corpus luteum, not the decidua directly.","Correct.","The fetal adrenal supplies DHEA-S, which the placenta uses to make estriol. It is not a main source of progesterone.","Maternal LH is suppressed during pregnancy by high estrogen and progesterone, so it does not rise."],"study":{"topics":["Luteal-placental shift","Hormones of pregnancy"],"comps":["RE-6","RE-3"]}},{"id":"RE-C10-Q3","stem":"In each of her pregnancies, the conceptus began to implant about 6 days after fertilization. Which of the following layers of the conceptus invades the endometrium and secretes the hormone detected by a pregnancy test?","options":["Hypoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Extraembryonic mesoderm"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-3","obj":"RE-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the conceptus: zygote, morula, then blastocyst, with an inner cell mass and an outer trophoblast.","At implantation the trophoblast splits into an inner cytotrophoblast and an outer syncytiotrophoblast.","The syncytiotrophoblast erodes the endometrium and secretes hCG, the hormone measured in a pregnancy test."],"why":"The zygote divides as it moves down the uterine tube, becomes a morula, and then a blastocyst that enters the uterus. At about day 6 the blastocyst attaches to the endometrium. Its outer trophoblast forms the cytotrophoblast, a layer of dividing cells, and the syncytiotrophoblast, a multinucleated layer without cell borders. The syncytiotrophoblast invades the endometrium and makes hCG, which keeps the corpus luteum active. The inner cell mass forms the epiblast and hypoblast, which build the embryo and yolk sac.","whyNot":["The hypoblast is part of the inner cell mass and helps form the yolk sac.","The cytotrophoblast is the inner, dividing layer that supplies cells to the syncytiotrophoblast. It does not itself invade or secrete most hCG.","Correct.","Extraembryonic mesoderm forms in the second week between the trophoblast and the yolk sac and lines the chorionic cavity. It does not invade the endometrium."],"study":{"topics":["Implantation","Trophoblast layers"],"comps":["RE-3","RE-6"]}},{"id":"RE-C10-Q4","stem":"Before her next attempt at conception, she is advised to begin daily folic acid supplementation to lower the risk of a neural tube defect. A deficiency of folate most directly impairs which of the following reactions?","options":["Methylation of dUMP to form dTMP","Hydroxylation of proline residues in collagen","Methylmalonyl-CoA conversion to succinyl-CoA","Homocysteine conversion to cystathionine"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-9","obj":"RE-9.1","dok":3,"approach":["Folate carries one-carbon units as tetrahydrofolate derivatives.","Thymidylate synthase uses methylene-tetrahydrofolate to methylate dUMP to dTMP, which is needed to make DNA. Folate also supplies carbons for purine synthesis.","Rapidly dividing neural tube cells need a large supply of DNA precursors, and the tube must close by about day 28."],"why":"Folate in the form of tetrahydrofolate carries one-carbon groups for DNA synthesis. Thymidylate synthase uses N5,N10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate to convert dUMP to dTMP, and folate also donates two carbons to the purine ring. Low folate slows DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing cells. The neural tube closes by about day 28 after fertilization, often before a woman knows she is pregnant, so supplementation must begin before conception.","whyNot":["Correct.","Hydroxylation of proline in collagen needs vitamin C. Its deficiency causes scurvy.","Methylmalonyl-CoA conversion to succinyl-CoA needs vitamin B12. It is not affected by folate deficiency.","Homocysteine conversion to cystathionine is catalyzed by cystathionine beta-synthase and needs vitamin B6, not folate."],"study":{"topics":["Folate and DNA synthesis","Neural tube defect prevention"],"comps":["RE-9","RE-3"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C11","system":"RE","dx":"Squamous cell carcinoma of the penis in an uncircumcised man with phimosis and recurrent balanitis","codes":["RE-13-J8","RE-13-C8","RE-13-C7","RE-13-B1"],"case":"A 66-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of a foul-smelling discharge and a lump under his foreskin. He is uncircumcised. For the past 8 years his foreskin could not be retracted, after many episodes of inflammation of the glans. He has type 2 diabetes mellitus with a hemoglobin A1c of 9.4% (normal below 5.7%) and has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 78/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a scarred, nonretractable prepuce. After a dorsal slit, a 2.5-cm ulcerated, exophytic mass is seen on the glans. A firm, nontender 2-cm left inguinal lymph node is palpable. Biopsy shows invasive keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma. In situ hybridization of the tumor is positive for HPV 16. Diagnosis: squamous cell carcinoma of the penis.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C11-Q1","stem":"Ten years earlier, a sharply demarcated, shiny red, velvety plaque was noted on his glans. It persisted for 2 years despite topical antifungal treatment and was never biopsied. Which of the following histologic findings would that plaque most likely have shown?","options":["Pseudohyphae and neutrophils within the superficial keratin layer","Papillary squamous proliferation with koilocytes and no atypia","Homogenized dermal collagen beneath a band of lymphocytes","Full-thickness squamous atypia above an intact basement membrane"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J8","obj":"RE-13-J.16","dok":3,"approach":["Link the clues: a persistent red velvety plaque on the glans of an uncircumcised older man is erythroplasia of Queyrat.","Recall that erythroplasia of Queyrat, Bowen disease (shaft), and bowenoid papulosis are forms of squamous cell carcinoma in situ.","Carcinoma in situ means atypical cells through the full thickness of the epithelium with no breach of the basement membrane."],"why":"Erythroplasia of Queyrat is squamous cell carcinoma in situ of the glans or inner prepuce. It looks like a shiny red, velvety plaque and does not respond to antifungals. Bowen disease is the same lesion on the shaft or scrotum, and bowenoid papulosis is multiple small papules in younger men that rarely progress. Untreated, the in situ lesion can become invasive carcinoma. High-risk HPV (especially type 16), lack of circumcision, phimosis with trapped smegma and chronic inflammation, and smoking all raise the risk.","whyNot":["Pseudohyphae with neutrophils in the keratin layer are seen in candidal balanitis. That infection improves with antifungal treatment, which this plaque did not.","Papillary squamous growth with koilocytes and no atypia describes condyloma acuminatum, caused by low-risk HPV 6 and 11. It is a warty, cauliflower-like growth, not a flat red plaque.","Homogenized dermal collagen under a lymphocytic band is lichen sclerosus (balanitis xerotica obliterans). It produces white, atrophic plaques and can scar the foreskin, but it is not a red velvety plaque.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Penile carcinoma in situ: erythroplasia of Queyrat, Bowen disease, bowenoid papulosis","Risk factors for penile squamous cell carcinoma"],"comps":["RE-13-J8","RE-13-J"]}},{"id":"RE-C11-Q2","stem":"Nine years ago, before the foreskin scarred completely, a urinary catheter was placed during hip surgery. The narrowed foreskin was pulled back for the procedure and left behind the glans. Within hours, the glans became swollen and painful and the foreskin could not be returned to its normal position. Which of the following best explains the swelling of the glans at that time?","options":["Ballooning of the prepuce from urine trapped behind a pinhole preputial orifice on voiding","Thrombosis of the superficial dorsal vein of the penis along the shaft","Tight preputial ring proximal to the corona obstructing venous and lymphatic return","Rupture of the tunica albuginea surrounding the corpora cavernosa"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-13-C7","obj":"RE-13-C.6","dok":2,"approach":["A foreskin that is retracted and then cannot be brought forward is paraphimosis, not phimosis.","The narrow ring of foreskin sits behind the corona and acts like a tourniquet on the glans and distal prepuce.","Venous and lymphatic outflow is blocked first, so the tissue swells, and the swelling tightens the ring further."],"why":"Paraphimosis happens when a tight foreskin is retracted behind the glans and stays there. The constricting band at the coronal sulcus blocks venous and lymphatic drainage of the glans and distal prepuce, which causes edema. Edema makes the ring tighter, and if it is not reduced, arterial inflow can fail and the glans can become ischemic. Phimosis is the opposite problem: a foreskin that cannot be retracted over the glans. A partly narrowed foreskin, as he had then, is the usual setting for paraphimosis.","whyNot":["Ballooning of the foreskin while voiding happens in phimosis, when urine is trapped behind a pinhole opening with the foreskin forward. It swells the prepuce, not the glans, and does not follow retraction of the foreskin.","Thrombosis of the superficial dorsal vein (Mondor disease of the penis) causes a tender cord along the shaft. It does not trap the foreskin or swell the glans.","Correct.","Rupture of the tunica albuginea is penile fracture. It follows blunt trauma to an erect penis, with a snap, rapid detumescence, and a shaft hematoma."],"study":{"topics":["Phimosis versus paraphimosis","Venous and lymphatic drainage of the glans"],"comps":["RE-13-C7","RE-13-C8"]}},{"id":"RE-C11-Q3","stem":"His earlier episodes of inflammation of the glans caused itching, redness, white curdlike material under the foreskin, and small red satellite papules. Each episode resolved with topical treatment. Which of the following was the most likely cause of these episodes?","options":["Haemophilus ducreyi","Candida albicans","Treponema pallidum","Herpes simplex virus type 2"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-B1","obj":"RE-13-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify recurrent balanitis in a man with an uncircumcised, poorly retractable foreskin and poorly controlled diabetes.","Match the lesion: itching, white curdlike material, and satellite papules are the pattern of a yeast infection.","Explain the risk: the warm, moist space under the foreskin and glucose-rich urine favor Candida growth."],"why":"Balanitis is inflammation of the glans and is most often caused by Candida albicans or bacteria. Risk factors are an uncircumcised foreskin, poor hygiene, and diabetes mellitus, all of which he has. Glucose in urine and secretions feeds yeast trapped in the moist preputial space. Candidal balanitis gives a red, itchy glans with white material and satellite papules. Repeated balanitis scars the foreskin and is a common cause of acquired phimosis, which in turn keeps the space closed and makes the next episode more likely.","whyNot":["Haemophilus ducreyi causes chancroid, a painful ulcer with a ragged undermined border and tender inguinal nodes.","Correct.","Treponema pallidum causes a single painless, indurated chancre in primary syphilis. It is not itchy and does not recur in this pattern.","Herpes simplex virus type 2 causes painful grouped vesicles that break into shallow ulcers. It does not cause white curdlike material or satellite papules."],"study":{"topics":["Causes and risk factors of balanitis","Candida infection in diabetes"],"comps":["RE-13-B1","RE-13-C8"]}},{"id":"RE-C11-Q4","stem":"A 57-year-old man seen in the same clinic reports 8 months of painful erections with an upward bend of the penis. He also has a flexion contracture of the ring finger from Dupuytren disease. Examination of the flaccid penis shows a firm, nontender, 1.5-cm plaque on the dorsal shaft. The glans and foreskin are normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his penile curvature?","options":["Diffuse fibrosis of the cavernosal smooth muscle after prolonged ischemia","Localized fibrous plaque within the tunica albuginea","Infiltrating squamous cell carcinoma extending into the corpus spongiosum","Thrombosis of the superficial dorsal vein with surrounding inflammation"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-D","obj":"RE-13-D.8","dok":2,"approach":["Note the triad: a palpable plaque, curvature on erection, and pain, in a man who also has Dupuytren contracture.","Recall that Peyronie disease is a fibrous plaque in the tunica albuginea, a fibroproliferative disorder like Dupuytren disease.","The plaque does not stretch during erection, so the penis bends toward the plaque, here upward from a dorsal plaque."],"why":"Peyronie disease is a localized fibrous plaque in the tunica albuginea, the tough sheath around the corpora cavernosa. It is thought to follow repeated microtrauma with abnormal wound healing, and it is linked to Dupuytren contracture. Because the plaque cannot stretch, the erect penis curves toward it, and erections can be painful in the early phase. The glans and foreskin are normal, which separates it from penile cancer.","whyNot":["Diffuse cavernosal fibrosis after prolonged ischemic priapism causes poor erections and a firm, shortened penis. It is not a single dorsal plaque with curvature.","Correct.","Penile squamous cell carcinoma usually starts on the glans or inner prepuce as a visible mass or ulcer. His glans and foreskin are normal.","Superficial dorsal vein thrombosis (penile Mondor disease) causes a tender cord in the skin of the shaft. It does not involve the tunica albuginea or bend the erect penis."],"study":{"topics":["Peyronie disease","Tunica albuginea and erectile anatomy"],"comps":["RE-13-D","RE-6"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C12","system":"RE","dx":"Erectile dysfunction after ischemic priapism in sickle cell disease","codes":["RE-13-D1"],"case":"A 24-year-old man with sickle cell disease (hemoglobin SS) comes to the physician because of 4 years of erections too weak for intercourse. At age 20, he had a painful erection that lasted 30 hours before he sought care; it was treated by aspiration and irrigation of the corpora cavernosa. Since then he has had no morning erections. Libido, orgasm, and ejaculation are preserved. He takes hydroxyurea and folic acid. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. The penile shaft is firm and shortened with no discrete plaque. Testes are normal in size. Perineal sensation, the bulbocavernosus reflex, and lower limb reflexes are normal. Serum total testosterone is 540 ng/dL (normal 300 to 1,000). Nocturnal penile tumescence testing shows no erections during sleep. Diagnosis: erectile dysfunction after ischemic priapism.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C12-Q1","stem":"His physician explains why the sleep study was ordered. Which of the following findings on that study would have most strongly indicated a psychogenic cause of his erectile dysfunction?","options":["Normal rigid erections occurring during REM sleep","Absent erections during both REM and non-REM sleep","Brief partial erections that quickly lose rigidity","Erections only after a phosphodiesterase inhibitor dose"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-D1","obj":"RE-13-D.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that erections during REM sleep happen automatically and need intact nerves, arterial inflow, and functioning cavernosal tissue.","If those sleep erections are normal, the hardware works, so a failure during sexual activity points to a psychogenic cause.","Absent or poor sleep erections point to an organic cause: vascular, neurogenic, endocrine, drug-induced, or structural, as in this patient."],"why":"Erectile dysfunction is classified as vascular (atherosclerosis), neurogenic (diabetes, spinal cord injury, pelvic surgery), endocrine (hypogonadism, hyperprolactinemia), drug-induced, or psychogenic. Several erections occur during REM sleep each night without any sexual thought. Normal nocturnal erections show that nerves, blood supply, and erectile tissue all work, which points to a psychogenic cause. His absent nocturnal erections, normal testosterone, and normal neurologic examination fit an organic cause in the erectile tissue itself.","whyNot":["Correct.","Absent sleep erections, as in this patient, indicate an organic cause rather than a psychogenic one.","Erections that start but cannot be held point toward venous leak or structural damage to the cavernosal tissue, an organic cause.","A response to a phosphodiesterase inhibitor shows that some nitric oxide signaling and tissue function remain. It does not separate psychogenic from organic causes."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of erectile dysfunction","Nocturnal penile tumescence"],"comps":["RE-13-D1","RE-6"]}},{"id":"RE-C12-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why the 30-hour delay before treatment of his priapism led to permanent erectile dysfunction?","options":["Hypoxic, acidotic stasis causing smooth muscle fibrosis","Unregulated arterial inflow from a ruptured cavernosal artery","Infarction of the testes with loss of Leydig cell testosterone","Compression injury of the cavernous nerves by the engorged corpora"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-D","obj":"RE-13-D.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize ischemic (low-flow) priapism: a painful erection over 4 hours in a patient with sickle cell disease.","Sickled red cells block venous outflow from the corpora, so blood stagnates and becomes hypoxic and acidotic.","After many hours, the trabecular smooth muscle dies and is replaced by fibrous tissue, which cannot relax to make an erection."],"why":"Ischemic priapism is a compartment-syndrome-like state of the penis. In sickle cell disease, sickled cells obstruct venous drainage, so trapped blood loses oxygen and becomes acidotic, which causes pain. Smooth muscle injury begins within hours, and by about 24 to 48 hours, necrosis and fibrosis are likely. Fibrotic corpora cannot relax or compress the draining veins, which explains his permanent erectile dysfunction. Drugs that cause ischemic priapism include trazodone, alpha blockers, and injected vasoactive agents.","whyNot":["Correct.","Unregulated arterial inflow from a ruptured cavernosal artery causes high-flow (nonischemic) priapism, usually after perineal trauma. It is painless, the blood is well oxygenated, and fibrosis is uncommon.","Sickle cell disease can cause testicular infarction, but his testes are normal in size and his testosterone is normal.","The injury in ischemic priapism comes from hypoxia and acidosis in the trapped blood, which damages the cavernosal smooth muscle. Compression of the cavernous nerves is not the mechanism."],"study":{"topics":["Ischemic versus nonischemic priapism","Sickle cell disease complications"],"comps":["RE-13-D","RE-13-D1"]}},{"id":"RE-C12-Q3","stem":"He is given a phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitor, which increases cGMP in cavernosal smooth muscle, but his erections do not improve. Which of the following best explains the poor response?","options":["Absent nitric oxide release from the cavernous nerves","Inadequate testosterone support of nitric oxide synthase","Excess sympathetic outflow from performance anxiety","Loss of smooth muscle that relaxes to compress venules"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-6","obj":"RE-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Lay out the normal chain: nitric oxide raises cGMP, cGMP relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle, arterial blood fills the sinusoids, and the expanding sinusoids press the subtunical venules against the tunica albuginea.","A PDE5 inhibitor only prolongs cGMP, so it works only if the smooth muscle can still respond.","His nerves and testosterone are normal, so the failure lies in fibrotic tissue that cannot relax or trap blood."],"why":"Erection starts when parasympathetic nerves and endothelium release nitric oxide. Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase, cGMP rises, and cavernosal smooth muscle relaxes. Arterial inflow fills the sinusoids, which expand and squeeze the small draining veins against the stiff tunica albuginea, trapping blood. PDE5 inhibitors block cGMP breakdown, but they cannot help when the smooth muscle has been replaced by fibrous tissue. Without relaxing, expanding sinusoids, veno-occlusion fails.","whyNot":["Loss of nitric oxide release from damaged cavernous nerves causes neurogenic erectile dysfunction, as after radical prostatectomy. His sensation and reflexes are normal.","Testosterone supports libido and nitric oxide synthase expression, but his testosterone level is normal.","Performance anxiety raises sympathetic tone and is a psychogenic cause. He has no nocturnal erections, which points to an organic cause.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Nitric oxide and cGMP in erection","Veno-occlusive mechanism"],"comps":["RE-6","RE-13-D1"]}},{"id":"RE-C12-Q4","stem":"Rhythmic contraction of the bulbospongiosus and ischiocavernosus muscles expels semen during his ejaculation, which remains normal. These contractions depend on which of the following nerves?","options":["Hypogastric nerves","Genitofemoral nerve","Pudendal nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-5","obj":"RE-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the three phases: erection, emission, and ejaculation.","Erection is parasympathetic (pelvic splanchnic, S2 to S4), and emission into the posterior urethra is sympathetic (hypogastric).","Expulsion by skeletal perineal muscles is somatic, carried by the pudendal nerve."],"why":"The bulbospongiosus and ischiocavernosus are skeletal muscles of the perineum, so they get somatic motor fibers from the pudendal nerve (S2 to S4). Their rhythmic contractions expel semen from the urethra. Emission, the movement of sperm and gland secretions into the posterior urethra, is sympathetic through the hypogastric nerves. Erection is parasympathetic through the pelvic splanchnic nerves. His preserved ejaculation fits a tissue problem, not a nerve problem.","whyNot":["The hypogastric nerves carry sympathetic fibers that cause emission: contraction of the vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and prostate, and closure of the internal urethral sphincter.","The genitofemoral nerve supplies the cremaster muscle and skin of the anterior scrotum and upper thigh. It is the efferent limb of the cremasteric reflex.","Correct.","The pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic fibers (S2 to S4) that cause erection by releasing nitric oxide. They do not drive skeletal muscle."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of erection, emission, and ejaculation","Pudendal nerve"],"comps":["RE-5","RE-6"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C13","system":"RE","dx":"Traumatic hematocele of the scrotum","codes":["RE-13-D2","RE-13-D3"],"case":"A 19-year-old man comes to the emergency department 6 hours after a lacrosse ball struck his scrotum during a game. He has had aching right scrotal pain and swelling since the injury. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. Examination shows bruising of the right scrotal skin and a tense, tender right hemiscrotum. The testis cannot be palpated separately from the swelling, and its lie cannot be judged. The mass does not transilluminate. The right cremasteric reflex is present. Doppler ultrasonography shows a complex fluid collection with internal echoes surrounding an intact right testis with normal blood flow. Urinalysis is normal. Diagnosis: traumatic hematocele.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C13-Q1","stem":"On ultrasonography, the collection lies against the front and sides of the testis but not its posterior border, where the epididymis attaches. In which of the following spaces has the blood most likely collected?","options":["Beneath the tunica albuginea in the testicular parenchyma","Within the dartos layer of the scrotal wall","Between the external and internal spermatic fasciae","Between the two layers of the tunica vaginalis"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-13-D2","obj":"RE-13-D.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note the shape of the collection: it covers the front and sides of the testis but spares the back, where the epididymis attaches.","Recall that the tunica vaginalis has this shape, because it is a closed serous sac that covers the testis except along its posterior border.","Blood in the tunica vaginalis is a hematocele, which fits the trauma, the internal echoes, and the lack of transillumination."],"why":"The tunica vaginalis is the closed lower end of the processus vaginalis. Its visceral layer covers the front and sides of the testis, and its parietal layer lines the inner scrotal wall, with a thin potential space between them. The posterior border of the testis, where the epididymis and vessels attach, is left uncovered, so fluid in this sac wraps around the front and sides only. Blood in this space is a hematocele, and serous fluid in the same space is a hydrocele. Blood blocks light, so a hematocele does not transilluminate, while a hydrocele glows.","whyNot":["Blood beneath the tunica albuginea is an intratesticular hematoma, inside the testis itself. His testis is intact on ultrasound, and the collection surrounds it from outside.","Bleeding into the dartos layer is a scrotal wall hematoma, which thickens and bruises the skin. It does not form a collection wrapped around the testis that spares its posterior border.","Blood between the spermatic fasciae spreads through the coverings of the cord and testis. It is not held against the front and sides of the testis by a closed serous sac.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the scrotum and the tunica vaginalis","Hematocele versus hydrocele"],"comps":["RE-13-D2","RE-13-D3"]}},{"id":"RE-C13-Q2","stem":"His 6-month-old nephew has a soft, nontender right scrotal swelling that transilluminates. His mother reports that it is larger in the evening and after crying and smaller in the morning. Persistence of which of the following embryologic structures best explains the nephew's swelling?","options":["Gubernaculum","Processus vaginalis","Urachus","Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-13-D3","obj":"RE-13-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["A transilluminating fluid collection around the testis is a hydrocele.","A size that changes with activity and time of day means the fluid moves to and from the peritoneal cavity: a communicating hydrocele.","That connection is a processus vaginalis that failed to close."],"why":"The processus vaginalis is an outpouching of peritoneum that leads the testis into the scrotum. Normally it closes, and only its lower part stays as the tunica vaginalis. If it stays open, peritoneal fluid flows into the scrotum and back, so the swelling changes in size: a communicating hydrocele. A wide opening can let bowel through as an indirect inguinal hernia. A noncommunicating hydrocele has a closed processus and a fixed size, and in adults it may follow infection, trauma, or a tumor.","whyNot":["The gubernaculum guides testicular descent and becomes the scrotal ligament. It is a fibrous cord and does not carry fluid.","Correct.","A patent urachus connects the bladder to the umbilicus and leaks urine from the umbilicus. It does not reach the scrotum.","The mesonephric duct forms the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicle. It does not link the scrotum to the peritoneal cavity."],"study":{"topics":["Communicating versus noncommunicating hydrocele","Processus vaginalis"],"comps":["RE-13-D3","RE-2"]}},{"id":"RE-C13-Q3","stem":"Before ultrasonography, his physician considered testicular torsion triggered by the injury. Which of the following findings in this patient most strongly argued against torsion?","options":["Intact ipsilateral cremasteric reflex","Swelling that did not transilluminate","Pain that began during vigorous physical activity","Tachycardia with a pulse of 92/min"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-D","obj":"RE-13-D.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the torsion picture: sudden severe pain, a high-riding testis often lying horizontally, and an absent cremasteric reflex.","The cremasteric reflex is absent in almost all cases of torsion.","A preserved reflex makes torsion much less likely, though ultrasound with Doppler is still needed."],"why":"Testicular torsion is twisting of the spermatic cord, usually in a testis with a bell-clapper deformity that lets it rotate freely. The thin-walled veins close first, so the testis swells and becomes congested; then arterial inflow stops and the testis infarcts within hours. Typical findings are sudden severe pain, a high-riding testis, and an absent cremasteric reflex. A present cremasteric reflex is the single exam finding that argues most against torsion. His ultrasound later confirmed normal blood flow.","whyNot":["Correct.","Failure to transilluminate fits blood or a solid mass. It does not separate torsion from a hematocele.","Torsion often starts during exercise or sleep, and trauma can trigger it, so pain beginning with activity does not exclude it.","A fast pulse reflects pain and stress from any scrotal emergency. It does not help separate torsion from other causes."],"study":{"topics":["Testicular torsion","Cremasteric reflex"],"comps":["RE-13-D","RE-5"]}},{"id":"RE-C13-Q4","stem":"Six weeks later, after the hematocele resolves, follow-up ultrasonography shows a 1.5-cm solid hypoechoic mass within the right testis. He has developed tender bilateral breast enlargement. Serum estradiol is increased, and serum alpha-fetoprotein and beta-hCG are within the reference ranges. Orchiectomy shows sheets of large polygonal cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm and rod-shaped cytoplasmic crystals. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Classic seminoma","Choriocarcinoma","Leydig cell tumor","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J9","obj":"RE-13-J.19","dok":3,"approach":["Gynecomastia with a testicular mass means the tumor is making estrogen, making androgen that is aromatized, or making hCG.","A normal beta-hCG removes choriocarcinoma, and a normal alpha-fetoprotein argues against yolk sac elements.","Polygonal eosinophilic cells with rod-shaped crystals (Reinke crystals) identify a Leydig cell tumor."],"why":"Leydig cell tumors are sex cord-stromal tumors that can secrete androgens, estrogens, or both. In adults, estrogen excess causes gynecomastia and reduced libido; in boys, androgen excess causes precocious puberty. Histology shows large polygonal cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm, and about a quarter to a third contain Reinke crystals. Most are benign. Testicular tumors are one reason a hematocele can appear with little trauma, so a mass found after the blood clears must be evaluated.","whyNot":["Classic seminoma has sheets of clear cells with lymphocytes in fibrous septa. It does not usually make hormones that cause gynecomastia.","Choriocarcinoma can cause gynecomastia through very high hCG levels. His beta-hCG is normal, and the tumor would show cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast.","Correct.","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is the most common testicular tumor in men over 60. It is often bilateral, has sheets of atypical lymphoid cells, and does not secrete estrogen."],"study":{"topics":["Leydig cell tumor","Testicular tumors and gynecomastia"],"comps":["RE-13-J9","RE-8"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C14","system":"RE","dx":"Left varicocele with a coexisting spermatocele in an infertile man","codes":["RE-13-D5","RE-13-D4"],"case":"A 29-year-old man comes to the physician because he and his partner have not conceived after 18 months of regular unprotected intercourse. Her evaluation is normal. He reports a dull ache in the left scrotum after standing for long periods. Examination while standing shows a soft mass above the left testis that feels like a bag of worms, enlarges with a Valsalva maneuver, and empties when he lies supine. It does not transilluminate. A separate 1.5-cm smooth, nontender cyst sits at the head of the right epididymis, above and apart from the right testis, and transilluminates. The left testis measures 13 mL and the right 19 mL (normal 15 to 25). Semen analysis shows a sperm concentration of 9 million/mL (reference 15 million/mL or more), 24% progressive motility (reference 32% or more), and 2% normal forms (reference 4% or more). Diagnosis: left varicocele and right spermatocele.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C14-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his reduced sperm production?","options":["Heat injury to germ cells from poor countercurrent cooling","Obstruction of sperm transport by the epididymal cyst","Antisperm antibodies formed after rupture of the epididymal cyst","Suppression of FSH by excess testosterone from the left testis"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-D5","obj":"RE-13-D.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the varicocele: a bag of worms above the testis that fills on standing and empties on lying down.","Normally, cool venous blood in the pampiniform plexus cools incoming arterial blood, keeping the testis about 2 to 3 °C (3.6 to 5.4 °F) below core temperature.","Pooled warm blood in dilated veins blunts this exchange, and heat impairs spermatogenesis, which fits his smaller left testis and abnormal semen."],"why":"A varicocele is dilation of the pampiniform plexus. The plexus normally wraps around the testicular artery and acts as a countercurrent heat exchanger. When the veins are dilated and blood pools, the testis runs warmer, and heat harms developing germ cells. The result is lower sperm count, poor motility, abnormal shapes, and over time a smaller testis. Varicocele is the most common correctable cause of male infertility. A new left varicocele in an older man, especially one that does not empty when lying down, should raise concern for renal cell carcinoma blocking the left renal vein.","whyNot":["Correct.","A spermatocele usually does not block sperm transport, and his is on the right while the testicular damage is on the left.","Spermatoceles rarely rupture, and nothing suggests a rupture here. Antisperm antibodies are more typical after vasectomy, trauma, or orchitis.","A varicocele does not raise testosterone. Damage to Sertoli cells and germ cells tends to raise FSH, not suppress it."],"study":{"topics":["Varicocele and infertility","Countercurrent heat exchange in the spermatic cord"],"comps":["RE-13-D5","RE-13-D"]}},{"id":"RE-C14-Q2","stem":"The right epididymal cyst is aspirated during later surgery. Which of the following findings would identify it as a spermatocele rather than a simple epididymal cyst?","options":["Transmission of light through the mass","Location separate from the testis","Smooth wall lined by cuboidal cells","Milky fluid containing spermatozoa"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-D4","obj":"RE-13-D.4","dok":2,"approach":["Both a spermatocele and a simple epididymal cyst are smooth, transilluminating masses separate from the testis.","A spermatocele is a dilated part of the epididymal duct system, so its fluid contains sperm.","A simple epididymal cyst holds clear fluid without sperm."],"why":"A spermatocele is a cystic dilation of the epididymis, usually at the head, that contains sperm. On exam it is smooth, sits above and separate from the testis, and transilluminates. An epididymal cyst looks and feels the same but holds clear serous fluid without sperm. Aspirated spermatocele fluid is usually milky or cloudy and shows spermatozoa under the microscope. Both are benign and usually do not cause infertility.","whyNot":["Both spermatoceles and simple epididymal cysts contain fluid that lets light through, so transillumination does not separate them.","Both lesions arise from the epididymis and are felt apart from the testis. Separation from the testis mainly distinguishes them from a testicular tumor.","Both cysts have a smooth wall with a simple cuboidal to columnar epithelial lining, so the lining does not separate them.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Spermatocele versus epididymal cyst","Scrotal mass examination"],"comps":["RE-13-D4","RE-13-D"]}},{"id":"RE-C14-Q3","stem":"He asks why the dilated veins are only on the left side. Which of the following venous drainage patterns best explains this?","options":["Left testicular vein entering the left renal vein at a right angle","Left testicular vein entering the inferior vena cava at an acute angle","Right testicular vein entering the right renal vein at a right angle","Left testicular vein entering the left common iliac vein directly"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-5","obj":"RE-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the arteries: both testicular arteries arise from the abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries.","Trace the veins: the right testicular vein enters the inferior vena cava at an acute angle, and the left enters the left renal vein at a right angle.","The left vein is longer, meets higher pressure, and the left renal vein can be compressed between the superior mesenteric artery and aorta, so left varicoceles are far more common."],"why":"The testicular arteries come from the abdominal aorta, but the veins drain asymmetrically. The right testicular vein empties obliquely into the inferior vena cava. The left testicular vein runs a longer course and joins the left renal vein at a right angle, where pressure is higher and flow is less favorable. The left renal vein also passes between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta and can be compressed there. These features raise pressure in the left pampiniform plexus, so about 85 to 90% of varicoceles are on the left. A tumor in the left renal vein can cause a new left varicocele.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left testicular vein does not drain into the inferior vena cava. It is the right testicular vein that enters the inferior vena cava at an acute angle.","The right testicular vein normally drains into the inferior vena cava, not the right renal vein. An isolated right varicocele is uncommon and should prompt a search for a retroperitoneal mass or other venous obstruction.","The testicular veins do not drain into the common iliac veins. They ascend on the posterior abdominal wall to the renal vein level."],"study":{"topics":["Gonadal artery and vein anatomy","Why varicocele is left-sided"],"comps":["RE-5","RE-13-D5"]}},{"id":"RE-C14-Q4","stem":"Further morphology testing shows that many spermatozoa have round heads that lack the cap covering the anterior two-thirds of the nucleus. Which organelle of the developing spermatid normally forms this missing structure?","options":["Distal centriole","Mitochondria","Golgi apparatus","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-3","obj":"RE-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Name the missing cap: the acrosome, which covers the front of the sperm nucleus and holds enzymes.","In spermiogenesis, the acrosome forms from Golgi vesicles that fuse over the nucleus.","Without an acrosome, the sperm cannot complete the acrosome reaction needed to get through the zona pellucida."],"why":"Spermiogenesis turns a round spermatid into a spermatozoon. The Golgi apparatus makes the acrosome, a cap of hydrolytic enzymes over the nucleus. A centriole forms the axoneme of the flagellum, mitochondria pack into the midpiece, and most cytoplasm is shed as a residual body. After ejaculation, sperm undergo capacitation in the female tract, which changes the membrane and allows the acrosome reaction at the zona pellucida. Sperm without an acrosome cannot penetrate the zona and cannot fertilize the egg naturally.","whyNot":["The distal centriole gives rise to the axoneme of the flagellum, which makes the sperm motile. It does not form the head cap.","Mitochondria wrap around the midpiece and supply ATP for flagellar movement. They do not form the acrosome.","Correct.","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum makes steroids and lipids in Leydig cells. It does not form the acrosome in spermatids."],"study":{"topics":["Spermiogenesis","Capacitation and the acrosome reaction"],"comps":["RE-3","RE-12"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C15","system":"RE","dx":"Mumps orchitis with testicular atrophy","codes":["RE-13-B5"],"case":"A 26-year-old man comes to the physician because he and his partner have not conceived after 2 years of trying. Five years earlier, during a campus outbreak, he had fever and swelling of both parotid glands, followed 6 days later by painful swelling of both testes. He had never been vaccinated. Physical examination shows normal virilization and no gynecomastia. Both testes are small and soft, each about 8 mL (normal 15 to 25). Laboratory studies show FSH 28 mIU/mL (normal 1.5 to 12.4), LH 9.8 mIU/mL (normal 1.7 to 8.6), and total testosterone 340 ng/dL (normal 300 to 1,000). Semen analysis shows a normal volume with a sperm concentration of 2 million/mL (reference 15 million/mL or more). Karyotype is 46,XY. Diagnosis: testicular atrophy after bilateral mumps orchitis.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C15-Q1","stem":"During the same outbreak, his 22-year-old roommate, who is sexually active, developed acute pain and swelling of the right scrotum with dysuria and a scant mucoid urethral discharge. He had no parotid swelling. The tender swelling began at the back of the testis, and pain eased when the scrotum was elevated. Which of the following was the most likely cause of the roommate's illness?","options":["Mumps virus","Chlamydia trachomatis","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Escherichia coli"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-B5","obj":"RE-13-B.6","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two pictures: mumps orchitis follows parotitis and involves the testis itself, while epididymo-orchitis starts in the epididymis behind the testis.","Urethritis with dysuria and discharge in a sexually active young man points to a sexually transmitted cause.","In men under about 35, Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae cause most epididymitis; in older men, coliforms such as E. coli do."],"why":"Epididymo-orchitis in sexually active young men usually ascends from urethritis caused by C. trachomatis or N. gonorrhoeae. The epididymis at the back of the testis is tender, and lifting the scrotum often eases pain. A scant mucoid discharge fits chlamydia. In older men, E. coli and other coliforms from urinary tract infection or prostate obstruction are the usual cause. Mumps orchitis is a viral infection of the testis itself, usually about a week after parotitis in a postpubertal male. It can lead to atrophy and infertility, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Mumps orchitis follows parotitis and affects the testis directly. The roommate had no parotid swelling and had urethritis.","Correct.","Tuberculous epididymitis develops slowly with a firm, beaded epididymis and sterile pyuria. It is not an acute illness with urethral discharge.","Escherichia coli causes epididymitis mainly in older men with urinary tract infection or outlet obstruction, or in men who have anal intercourse. A young man with urethral discharge more likely has a sexually transmitted cause."],"study":{"topics":["Mumps orchitis versus epididymo-orchitis","Causes of epididymitis by age"],"comps":["RE-13-B5","RE-13-B"]}},{"id":"RE-C15-Q2","stem":"Male infertility is classified as pretesticular, testicular, or post-testicular. Which of the following causes of infertility belongs to the same category as his condition?","options":["Hyperprolactinemia","Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens","Cryptorchidism","Kallmann syndrome"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-D","obj":"RE-13-D.9","dok":3,"approach":["Use the gonadotropins to locate the problem: high FSH and LH with small testes mean the testis itself has failed.","Pretesticular causes have low gonadotropins, and post-testicular causes have normal testes and hormones with blocked outflow.","Testicular causes include Klinefelter syndrome, cryptorchidism, varicocele, and mumps orchitis."],"why":"Male infertility is grouped by where the problem lies. Pretesticular causes are hormonal, such as hypogonadotropic hypogonadism from Kallmann syndrome or hyperprolactinemia, with low LH and FSH. Testicular causes damage sperm production directly: Klinefelter syndrome, cryptorchidism, varicocele, toxins, and mumps orchitis. They raise FSH as feedback is lost. Post-testicular causes block sperm transport, such as congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens in cystic fibrosis, which gives a low semen volume with normal testes. His high FSH, small testes, and normal semen volume place him in the testicular group.","whyNot":["Hyperprolactinemia is a pretesticular cause. It suppresses GnRH and lowers LH and FSH, while his are high.","Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens is a post-testicular cause, with normal testes, normal FSH, and a low semen volume. His testes are atrophic and his semen volume is normal.","Correct.","Kallmann syndrome is a pretesticular cause. Absent GnRH makes LH and FSH low, while his are high."],"study":{"topics":["Pretesticular, testicular, and post-testicular infertility","Interpreting FSH and LH in men"],"comps":["RE-13-D","RE-7"]}},{"id":"RE-C15-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his FSH is raised more than his LH?","options":["Increased estradiol from peripheral aromatization","Reduced androgen-binding protein from Sertoli cells","Reduced testosterone secretion by Leydig cells","Reduced inhibin B secretion by Sertoli cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-7","obj":"RE-7.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the two arms: LH drives Leydig cell testosterone, and FSH drives Sertoli cells, which support sperm and make androgen-binding protein and inhibin B.","Testosterone feeds back mainly on LH, and inhibin B feeds back selectively on FSH.","His germ cells and Sertoli cell function are badly damaged while testosterone is near normal, so FSH rises most."],"why":"In the male axis, LH stimulates Leydig cells to make testosterone, and FSH stimulates Sertoli cells. Sertoli cells make androgen-binding protein, which keeps testosterone high in the tubules, and inhibin B, which suppresses FSH. Testosterone feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary mainly to lower LH. Mumps orchitis destroys seminiferous tubules, so inhibin B falls and FSH rises sharply. Leydig cells are more resistant, so testosterone stays near normal and LH rises only a little.","whyNot":["Raised estradiol would suppress both LH and FSH. It would not explain high gonadotropins.","Androgen-binding protein concentrates testosterone in the tubules, but it does not feed back on the pituitary to control FSH.","Loss of Leydig cell testosterone would mainly raise LH. His testosterone is in the low-normal range and his LH is only mildly raised.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis","Inhibin B and FSH"],"comps":["RE-7","RE-3"]}},{"id":"RE-C15-Q4","stem":"His semen shows clumped spermatozoa, and IgG and IgA antibodies are found bound to the surface of the few sperm present. Which of the following best explains how these antibodies formed?","options":["Polyclonal B-cell activation by a mumps virus superantigen","Disruption of the Sertoli cell barrier exposing sperm antigens","Deficiency of complement regulatory proteins on spermatozoa","Molecular mimicry between mumps nucleoprotein and sperm antigens"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-11","obj":"RE-11.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that sperm antigens appear at puberty, long after self-tolerance is set, so the immune system treats them as foreign.","Normally they stay hidden behind tight junctions between Sertoli cells (the blood-testis barrier) and local immunosuppression.","Inflammation from orchitis breaks the barrier, exposes sperm antigens, and triggers antisperm antibodies."],"why":"The testis is an immune-privileged site. Tight junctions between neighboring Sertoli cells form the blood-testis barrier, which hides meiotic and postmeiotic germ cells from the immune system. Local factors, such as regulatory T cells and immunosuppressive cytokines, add protection. Trauma, vasectomy, torsion, or orchitis can break the barrier and expose sperm antigens, leading to antisperm antibodies that clump sperm and block their movement and binding to the egg. Autoimmune orchitis with antibodies against testicular tissue can also cause hypogonadism.","whyNot":["Mumps virus does not act as a superantigen. Superantigens, such as staphylococcal toxic shock toxin, activate T cells broadly rather than causing sperm-specific antibodies.","Correct.","Antisperm antibodies arise from exposure of hidden antigens, not from loss of complement regulators on sperm.","Molecular mimicry between mumps proteins and sperm antigens is not an established mechanism. The antibodies follow barrier breakdown and exposure of hidden antigens."],"study":{"topics":["Blood-testis barrier and immune privilege","Antisperm antibodies"],"comps":["RE-11","RE-3"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C16","system":"RE","dx":"Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea in a distance runner","codes":["RE-13-A1","RE-13-A2"],"case":"A 20-year-old woman who runs collegiate cross-country comes to the physician because she has not had a menstrual period for 7 months. Menarche was at age 13, and her cycles were regular until this season. She now runs about 110 km (68 mi) a week and has lost 6 kg (13 lb). Three months ago she had a stress fracture of the second metatarsal. Temperature is 36.2 °C (97.2 °F), pulse is 48/min, respirations are 12/min, and blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg. BMI is 17.6 kg/m2. Breast and pubic hair development are normal, and there is no hirsutism or galactorrhea. Serum hCG is negative. Laboratory studies show FSH 2.1 mIU/mL (follicular phase 3.5 to 12.5), LH 1.0 mIU/mL (follicular phase 2.4 to 12.6), estradiol 15 pg/mL (follicular phase 30 to 120), prolactin 9 ng/mL (normal below 25), and TSH 1.8 μU/mL (normal 0.5 to 4.5). Diagnosis: functional hypothalamic amenorrhea.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C16-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains how her negative energy balance lowered her gonadotropin levels?","options":["Depleted ovarian follicles reducing inhibin and estradiol output","Raised prolactin from exercise stress inhibiting GnRH release","Low leptin from reduced fat mass decreasing GnRH pulsatility","Raised estradiol from adipose aromatase suppressing the pituitary"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-9","obj":"RE-9.6","dok":3,"approach":["Low FSH, LH, and estradiol with normal prolactin and TSH place the problem in the hypothalamus.","Leptin from fat cells signals energy stores to the hypothalamus, partly through kisspeptin neurons that drive GnRH.","Heavy training with too little intake lowers fat and leptin, so GnRH pulses slow and gonadotropins fall."],"why":"Leptin is made by adipocytes in proportion to fat mass and signals that energy stores are adequate for reproduction. When intake does not cover the energy cost of training, leptin falls, and kisspeptin and GnRH pulses slow. LH and FSH fall, follicles do not mature, estradiol stays low, and menses stop. Low estrogen speeds bone loss, which explains her stress fracture. High cortisol and low thyroid hormone from energy deficit add to the effect.","whyNot":["Follicle depletion is ovarian failure, which raises FSH and LH as feedback is lost. Her gonadotropins are low.","High prolactin can suppress GnRH, but her prolactin is normal.","Correct.","Her estradiol is low, not high. With a low fat mass, adipose aromatization would also be small."],"study":{"topics":["Leptin and GnRH","Low energy availability and bone loss"],"comps":["RE-9","RE-13-A1"]}},{"id":"RE-C16-Q2","stem":"Her physician considers restoring ovulation with an infusion pump that delivers GnRH. Which of the following regimens would produce a sustained rise in her LH and FSH?","options":["Pulses about every 60 to 90 minutes, keeping receptors responsive","Continuous infusion, which keeps GnRH receptors occupied and active","Once-daily large bolus, which reproduces the midcycle LH surge","Continuous infusion, which increases the number of GnRH receptors"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-7","obj":"RE-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the hypothalamus releases GnRH in pulses, roughly every 1 to 2 hours.","Pulses let pituitary GnRH receptors recover between exposures, so gonadotrophs keep releasing LH and FSH.","Continuous GnRH desensitizes and down-regulates the receptors, which suppresses LH and FSH after a brief flare."],"why":"Gonadotrophs respond to the pattern of GnRH, not just the amount. Pulsatile GnRH keeps receptors sensitive and drives LH and FSH release, and pulse frequency shapes the balance: faster pulses favor LH and slower pulses favor FSH. Continuous exposure causes an initial rise and then receptor desensitization and down-regulation, so gonadotropins fall. That is why long-acting GnRH agonists like leuprolide are used to shut down the axis in prostate cancer, endometriosis, and precocious puberty. A pulsatile pump can restore ovulation in hypothalamic amenorrhea.","whyNot":["Correct.","Continuous GnRH does occupy receptors, but sustained occupancy desensitizes them, so LH and FSH fall after an early flare.","A single daily dose is not the physiologic pattern and does not reproduce the midcycle surge, which is triggered by rising estradiol switching to positive feedback.","Continuous GnRH lowers receptor number through down-regulation. It does not increase it."],"study":{"topics":["Pulsatile versus continuous GnRH","GnRH agonist therapy"],"comps":["RE-7","RE-9"]}},{"id":"RE-C16-Q3","stem":"Her 31-year-old sister has had no menses for 6 months since a dilation and curettage for retained products of conception after a miscarriage. A pregnancy test is negative, and she had no bleeding after a course of estrogen followed by progestin. Which of the following laboratory patterns would be expected in the sister?","options":["Increased FSH and LH, decreased estradiol, normal prolactin","Decreased FSH and LH, decreased estradiol, normal prolactin","Decreased FSH and LH, decreased estradiol, increased prolactin","Normal FSH and LH, normal estradiol, normal prolactin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-A1","obj":"RE-13-A.2","dok":3,"approach":["Amenorrhea after curettage with no bleeding even after estrogen and progestin points to a scarred endometrium: Asherman syndrome.","In an outflow tract problem, the hypothalamus, pituitary, and ovaries all work normally.","So FSH, LH, estradiol, and prolactin are all normal, and the defect lies in the uterus."],"why":"Hormone levels localize amenorrhea. Low FSH and LH with low estradiol point to the hypothalamus or pituitary, as in this runner. High FSH and LH with low estradiol point to the ovary, such as primary ovarian insufficiency. High prolactin points to a prolactinoma or other cause of hyperprolactinemia, and TSH screens for thyroid disease. Normal hormones with no bleeding after estrogen and progestin point to the outflow tract. Asherman syndrome is scarring of the endometrial basal layer, usually after vigorous curettage, and the endometrium cannot respond to hormones.","whyNot":["High FSH and LH with low estradiol shows ovarian failure, such as primary ovarian insufficiency.","Low FSH, LH, and estradiol with normal prolactin is the pattern of hypothalamic amenorrhea, as in the runner.","Low gonadotropins and estradiol with high prolactin suggest a prolactinoma or other cause of hyperprolactinemia that suppresses GnRH.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hormonal localization of amenorrhea","Asherman syndrome"],"comps":["RE-13-A1","RE-13-A"]}},{"id":"RE-C16-Q4","stem":"Like women with polycystic ovary syndrome, she does not ovulate. Unlike them, she is not at increased risk of endometrial hyperplasia. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["Low estradiol gives little stimulus to endometrial growth","High androgen levels keep her endometrium thin and atrophic","Her endometrium lacks estrogen receptors during amenorrhea","A persistent corpus luteum supplies protective progesterone"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-A2","obj":"RE-13-A.3","dok":3,"approach":["Both conditions are anovulatory, so no corpus luteum forms and no progesterone is made.","In PCOS, estrogen levels stay normal or high, so the endometrium keeps proliferating without progesterone to oppose it.","In hypothalamic amenorrhea, estradiol is low, so the endometrium stays thin and does not grow into hyperplasia."],"why":"Anovulation means no LH surge and no corpus luteum, so the endometrium never gets the progesterone that turns it secretory and limits growth. Whether this matters depends on estrogen. In PCOS, estrogen keeps stimulating the endometrium, causing irregular heavy bleeding and raising the risk of hyperplasia. In hypothalamic amenorrhea from low energy availability, estrogen is low, so the endometrium is thin and there is no bleeding at all. Her main risk is bone loss rather than hyperplasia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Her androgens are not raised, and she has no hirsutism. Thin endometrium here comes from low estrogen.","The endometrium keeps its estrogen receptors. The issue is a lack of estrogen, not a lack of receptors.","She is anovulatory, so no corpus luteum forms and no progesterone is made."],"study":{"topics":["Anovulation and unopposed estrogen","Hypoestrogenic versus normoestrogenic anovulation"],"comps":["RE-13-A2","RE-13-J2"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C17","system":"RE","dx":"Polycystic ovary syndrome","codes":["RE-13-G3","RE-13-A2"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the physician because of irregular menses and unwanted hair growth. Since menarche at age 12, she has had 5 or 6 periods a year. She has coarse dark hair on her upper lip, chin, and lower abdomen and acne on her jaw. BMI is 33 kg/m2. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 80/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 132/84 mm Hg. Velvety hyperpigmented skin is present on the back of the neck. Serum hCG is negative. Laboratory studies show total testosterone 78 ng/dL (normal 15 to 70), SHBG 16 nmol/L (normal 20 to 130), LH 18 mIU/mL and FSH 5 mIU/mL (follicular phase LH 2.4 to 12.6, FSH 3.5 to 12.5), and fasting glucose 108 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99). DHEA sulfate, 17-hydroxyprogesterone, prolactin, and TSH are normal. Transvaginal ultrasonography shows more than 20 small follicles in each ovary. Diagnosis: polycystic ovary syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C17-Q1","stem":"The ovarian follicles on ultrasonography each measure 2 to 9 mm and are arranged around the periphery of enlarged ovaries. Which of the following best explains this appearance?","options":["Persistence of one unruptured dominant follicle after a missed LH surge","Arrest of antral follicles by androgen excess and low relative FSH","Luteinization of theca cells by very high concentrations of hCG","Implantation of endometrial glands and stroma on the ovarian surface"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-G3","obj":"RE-13-G.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note the raised LH to FSH ratio (18 to 5), the high testosterone, and the insulin resistance.","High LH and high insulin drive theca cells to make androgen, and the relative lack of FSH limits granulosa cell aromatase.","The androgen-rich follicles stop growing before one becomes dominant, so many small follicles line the cortex."],"why":"In PCOS, high LH pulse frequency raises the LH to FSH ratio, and LH drives theca cells to make androgens. Hyperinsulinemia adds to theca androgen output and lowers SHBG. With relatively low FSH and a high local androgen level, small antral follicles stop developing and none becomes dominant. They pile up under a thickened capsule as many small peripheral cysts, often called a string of pearls. No dominant follicle means no estradiol peak, no LH surge, and no ovulation.","whyNot":["A single dominant follicle that fails to rupture forms a follicular cyst, usually one cyst of 3 cm or more, not many small ones.","Correct.","Very high hCG from a molar pregnancy or choriocarcinoma causes theca lutein cysts. Her hCG is negative.","Endometrial tissue on the ovary forms an endometrioma, a single cyst of old blood (chocolate cyst)."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of PCOS","Ovarian cysts"],"comps":["RE-13-G3","RE-13-G"]}},{"id":"RE-C17-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains how her raised LH increases ovarian androgen production?","options":["Stimulation of granulosa cell aromatase activity","Stimulation of zona reticularis androgen synthesis","Stimulation of theca cell androgen synthesis from cholesterol","Stimulation of granulosa cell androgen synthesis from cholesterol"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-7","obj":"RE-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["Apply the two-cell model: theca cells have LH receptors and 17α-hydroxylase, so they make androstenedione from cholesterol.","Granulosa cells have FSH receptors and aromatase, so they convert androgens from the theca into estrogens, but they cannot make androgens themselves.","High LH with relatively low FSH pushes more androgen out of the theca than the granulosa cells can aromatize."],"why":"In the two-cell model, LH acts on theca cells, which take up cholesterol and make androstenedione. The androgen diffuses to granulosa cells, where FSH-induced aromatase converts it to estrogen. In PCOS, high LH drives theca androgen output, and low relative FSH limits aromatization, so androgens build up. In a normal cycle, a dominant follicle raises estradiol high enough for about 2 days that feedback switches from negative to positive and triggers the LH surge. Women with PCOS rarely reach that point.","whyNot":["Granulosa cell aromatase is driven by FSH and converts androgens to estrogens, which lowers androgen. It does not raise androgen production.","The zona reticularis makes DHEA and DHEA sulfate under ACTH control, not LH. Her DHEA sulfate is normal.","Correct.","Granulosa cells lack 17α-hydroxylase and cannot make androgens from cholesterol. They depend on theca cells for androgen substrate."],"study":{"topics":["Two-cell model of ovarian steroidogenesis","Positive feedback and the LH surge"],"comps":["RE-7","RE-9"]}},{"id":"RE-C17-Q3","stem":"Her total testosterone is only mildly raised, yet her hirsutism is marked. Which of the following best explains this?","options":["Raised hepatic SHBG synthesis from estrogen excess","Low hepatic SHBG synthesis from hyperinsulinemia","Tight testosterone binding to albumin in the plasma","Increased aromatization of testosterone to estradiol"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-7","obj":"RE-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that most testosterone is bound to SHBG (tightly) or albumin (loosely), and only about 1 to 2% is free.","Free and albumin-bound testosterone reach tissues such as hair follicles.","Insulin resistance, obesity, and androgens lower SHBG, so a larger share of her testosterone is free even when the total is only mildly high."],"why":"Sex steroids travel bound to SHBG and albumin. SHBG binds testosterone tightly, while albumin binds it loosely, so free plus albumin-bound testosterone is the active fraction. The liver makes SHBG; estrogen and thyroid hormone raise it, while insulin, obesity, and androgens lower it. Her low SHBG means more of her testosterone is free to act on hair follicles. That is why hirsutism can be marked when the total testosterone is only a little high.","whyNot":["Estrogen raises SHBG, which would bind more testosterone and lower the free fraction. Her SHBG is low.","Correct.","Albumin binds testosterone weakly, which is why albumin-bound testosterone is counted as available to tissues. Tight albumin binding is not the reason for her hirsutism.","Aromatization converts testosterone to estradiol, which would lower androgen effects, not increase them."],"study":{"topics":["SHBG and free testosterone","Insulin resistance in PCOS"],"comps":["RE-7","RE-13-G3"]}},{"id":"RE-C17-Q4","stem":"Her theca cells take up most of their cholesterol from circulating LDL by receptor-mediated endocytosis. Which of the following steps limits the rate of LH-stimulated androgen synthesis in these cells?","options":["Conversion of androstenedione to estrone by aromatase","Conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone by 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","Synthesis of mevalonate by HMG-CoA reductase","Transfer of cholesterol into mitochondria by StAR protein"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-9","obj":"RE-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Steroid-making cells in the gonads get most of their cholesterol from LDL, not from making it themselves.","The first enzyme of steroid synthesis, side-chain cleavage, sits on the inner mitochondrial membrane.","Moving cholesterol to that membrane by StAR protein is the slow, hormone-regulated step."],"why":"Gonadal and placental cells obtain cholesterol mainly from LDL by receptor-mediated endocytosis. LH, acting through cAMP, rapidly raises StAR protein, which carries cholesterol from the outer to the inner mitochondrial membrane. There, side-chain cleavage enzyme converts it to pregnenolone, the start of all steroid hormones. StAR transfer is the rate-limiting step, and mutations in StAR cause congenital lipoid adrenal hyperplasia with failure of adrenal and gonadal steroids.","whyNot":["Aromatase works in granulosa cells and converts androgens to estrogens. It is downstream of androgen synthesis.","3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase is a later enzyme in the pathway and does not limit the rate of LH-stimulated synthesis.","HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis, but theca cells rely mainly on LDL cholesterol rather than making their own.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["StAR protein and steroidogenesis","LDL as the cholesterol source for steroid hormones"],"comps":["RE-9","RE-7"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C18","system":"RE","dx":"Atypical endometrial hyperplasia in an obese woman with chronic anovulation","codes":["RE-13-J2","RE-13-A3"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 1 year of irregular, heavy vaginal bleeding. Her cycles vary from 45 to 90 days, and some episodes last 2 weeks. She was diagnosed with polycystic ovary syndrome at age 22 and has never been pregnant. She takes no hormones. BMI is 42 kg/m2. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 138/86 mm Hg. Examination shows facial hirsutism and acanthosis nigricans of the axillae. Serum hCG is negative. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0); TSH and prolactin are normal. Transvaginal ultrasonography shows a normal-sized uterus with homogeneous myometrium, an endometrial thickness of 18 mm, and no polyp or fibroid. Endometrial biopsy shows crowded back-to-back glands with nuclear atypia and no stromal invasion. Diagnosis: atypical endometrial hyperplasia.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C18-Q1","stem":"Immunohistochemistry shows loss of PTEN protein in the atypical glands. Which of the following best explains how this loss promotes growth of the glands?","options":["Failure of p53-mediated arrest after DNA damage","Loss of RB restraint of E2F at the G1 checkpoint","Loss of mismatch repair with microsatellite instability","Accumulation of PIP3 with constant AKT signaling"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-13-J2","obj":"RE-13-J.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that PTEN is a phosphatase that removes a phosphate from PIP3, turning it back into PIP2.","PIP3 recruits and activates AKT, which promotes cell growth and survival through mTOR.","Without PTEN, PIP3 builds up and AKT stays on, adding to the growth push from unopposed estrogen."],"why":"PTEN is a tumor suppressor that opposes PI3K by dephosphorylating PIP3. When PTEN is lost, PIP3 accumulates, AKT and mTOR stay active, and cells grow and resist apoptosis. PTEN loss is the most common early molecular change in endometrial hyperplasia and endometrioid carcinoma. Her hyperplasia began with unopposed estrogen from obesity and chronic anovulation, and PTEN loss helps turn that hormonal growth into a clonal, atypical process. Atypical hyperplasia is the direct precursor of endometrioid carcinoma.","whyNot":["Failure of p53-mediated arrest from TP53 mutation is typical of serous endometrial carcinoma, which arises in atrophic endometrium rather than hyperplasia.","Loss of RB control of E2F is the mechanism of HPV E7 in cervical neoplasia and of retinoblastoma. It is not the result of PTEN loss.","Mismatch repair loss with microsatellite instability is the mechanism in Lynch syndrome. It is a separate pathway from PTEN loss, though both can occur in endometrioid tumors.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["PTEN and the PI3K/AKT pathway","Endometrial hyperplasia"],"comps":["RE-13-J2","RE-10"]}},{"id":"RE-C18-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the irregular, heavy pattern of her bleeding?","options":["Synchronized shedding of secretory endometrium after luteal regression","Proliferative endometrium breaking down without progesterone","Endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium","Submucosal leiomyoma distorting the endometrial cavity"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-A3","obj":"RE-13-A.4","dok":3,"approach":["Her long, irregular cycles and PCOS point to chronic anovulation.","Without ovulation, there is no corpus luteum and no progesterone to make the endometrium secretory and stable.","Estrogen keeps thickening a fragile endometrium that outgrows its blood supply and breaks down at random, which is abnormal uterine bleeding from ovulatory dysfunction."],"why":"In anovulatory cycles, estrogen drives continued proliferation, but no progesterone arrives to convert the endometrium to a stable secretory state. The thick, fragile tissue outgrows its support and breaks down in patches at unpredictable times, so bleeding is irregular, prolonged, and sometimes heavy. In the PALM-COEIN system this is abnormal uterine bleeding from ovulatory dysfunction (formerly called dysfunctional uterine bleeding). Structural causes, such as polyps, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, and malignancy, must be excluded, as her ultrasound and biopsy did here.","whyNot":["Synchronized shedding after the corpus luteum regresses is a normal ovulatory period, which is regular and predictable.","Correct.","Endometrial tissue within the myometrium is adenomyosis, which gives a large, boggy uterus and painful heavy periods. Her uterus is normal in size with homogeneous myometrium.","A submucosal leiomyoma can cause heavy bleeding, but her ultrasound shows no fibroid."],"study":{"topics":["Abnormal uterine bleeding from ovulatory dysfunction","PALM-COEIN classification"],"comps":["RE-13-A3","RE-13-A2"]}},{"id":"RE-C18-Q3","stem":"Because of her polycystic ovary syndrome, which of the following conditions is she at greatest increased risk of developing?","options":["Osteoporosis","Primary ovarian insufficiency","Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Systemic lupus erythematosus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-G3","obj":"RE-13-G.5","dok":2,"approach":["Connect her acanthosis nigricans and obesity to insulin resistance, a core feature of PCOS.","Persistent insulin resistance leads over time to impaired glucose tolerance and type 2 diabetes.","Her estrogen levels are not low, so bone loss is not a main risk."],"why":"PCOS causes oligomenorrhea, hirsutism, acne, obesity, acanthosis nigricans, and infertility. Insulin resistance is central, so women with PCOS have a much higher risk of impaired glucose tolerance and type 2 diabetes, along with dyslipidemia and metabolic syndrome. Unopposed estrogen raises the risk of endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma, as in this patient. Estrogen is preserved and follicles are plentiful, so osteoporosis and early ovarian failure are not expected.","whyNot":["Osteoporosis results from low estrogen. Women with PCOS have normal or higher estrogen and are not at increased risk.","Women with PCOS have many follicles and a large follicle reserve, which is the opposite of ovarian insufficiency.","Correct.","PCOS is not linked to systemic lupus erythematosus. Lupus is an autoimmune disease that is more common in women but not tied to PCOS."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of PCOS","Insulin resistance and acanthosis nigricans"],"comps":["RE-13-G3","RE-13-J2"]}},{"id":"RE-C18-Q4","stem":"Her serum estrone is increased. Which of the following is the main source of this estrogen?","options":["Aromatization of androstenedione in adipose tissue","Hepatic synthesis of estrone from cholesterol","Conversion of DHEA sulfate to estrone in the kidney","Direct secretion of estrone by the adrenal cortex"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-8","obj":"RE-8.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that adipose stromal cells contain aromatase.","Aromatase converts androstenedione from the ovaries and adrenals into estrone.","More fat and more androgen substrate in PCOS mean more estrone, which stimulates the endometrium without progesterone to oppose it."],"why":"Adipose tissue contains aromatase, which converts androstenedione to estrone and testosterone to estradiol. In obesity, this peripheral conversion becomes a large source of estrogen, especially when ovarian and adrenal androgen output is high, as in PCOS. Without ovulation, there is no progesterone to oppose it, so the endometrium keeps growing and can become hyperplastic. The same process explains gynecomastia in obese men and is the main estrogen source after menopause.","whyNot":["Correct.","The liver does not make estrone from cholesterol. It metabolizes and conjugates estrogens.","The kidney does not convert DHEA sulfate to estrone. DHEA sulfate is an adrenal androgen precursor that can be used for estrogen synthesis in peripheral tissues such as fat.","The adrenal cortex secretes androgen precursors, not significant amounts of estrone."],"study":{"topics":["Peripheral aromatization in adipose tissue","Obesity and unopposed estrogen"],"comps":["RE-8","RE-13-J2"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C19","system":"RE","dx":"Endometrioid endometrial carcinoma in a woman with Lynch syndrome","codes":["RE-13-J2"],"case":"A 47-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 3 months of bleeding between her menstrual periods. At age 43, she had a right colon adenocarcinoma removed. Her father had colon cancer at age 45, and her paternal aunt had endometrial cancer at age 50. She has 2 children. BMI is 25 kg/m2. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Pelvic examination shows a normal cervix and a normal-sized uterus. Hemoglobin is 11.8 g/dL (normal 12.0 to 16.0). Endometrial biopsy shows a well-differentiated adenocarcinoma with back-to-back glands resembling proliferative endometrium. Immunohistochemistry of the tumor shows loss of MSH2 and MSH6 proteins, and germline testing shows a pathogenic MSH2 mutation. Diagnosis: endometrioid endometrial carcinoma with Lynch syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C19-Q1","stem":"Compared with serous carcinoma of the endometrium, her tumor type is characteristically associated with which of the following?","options":["Origin in a background of atrophic endometrium","TP53 mutation in nearly all tumor cells","Papillary tufts with psammoma bodies","Origin from endometrial hyperplasia"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J","obj":"RE-13-J.5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify her tumor as endometrioid: well-differentiated glands that resemble normal endometrium, in a younger woman with Lynch syndrome.","Recall the two-type model: endometrioid carcinoma is estrogen-related and arises from hyperplasia, while serous carcinoma arises in older women with atrophic endometrium.","Match each feature to its tumor type."],"why":"Endometrioid carcinoma is the more common type. It is driven by estrogen, arises from atypical hyperplasia, and occurs in perimenopausal women with obesity, anovulation, or Lynch syndrome. Its mutations include PTEN, and mismatch repair loss in Lynch syndrome. It is often low grade and has a good prognosis. Serous carcinoma affects older women, arises in atrophic endometrium from a precursor called serous endometrial intraepithelial carcinoma, carries TP53 mutations, and is aggressive. Both types usually present with abnormal or postmenopausal bleeding.","whyNot":["Origin in atrophic endometrium is typical of serous carcinoma in older postmenopausal women.","TP53 mutation in nearly all cells is the hallmark of serous carcinoma, not low-grade endometrioid carcinoma.","Papillary architecture with psammoma bodies and marked atypia describes serous carcinoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Endometrioid versus serous endometrial carcinoma","Molecular pathways in endometrial cancer"],"comps":["RE-13-J","RE-13-J2"]}},{"id":"RE-C19-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains how loss of MSH2 function leads to her cancers?","options":["Failure to correct replication base mismatches","Failure to degrade β-catenin in the WNT pathway","Failure to excise ultraviolet-induced pyrimidine dimers","Failure to repair double-strand breaks by recombination"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-10","obj":"RE-10.5","dok":2,"approach":["MSH2 and MSH6 are mismatch repair proteins that find mispaired bases left by DNA polymerase.","In Lynch syndrome, one mutant copy is inherited and the second is lost in the tissue, so mismatch repair fails.","Errors pile up, especially in short repeated sequences (microsatellite instability), and hit genes that control growth."],"why":"Lynch syndrome is caused by germline mutations in mismatch repair genes, mainly MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, and PMS2. It is autosomal dominant, and tumors form after the second copy is lost. Without mismatch repair, replication errors stay in the DNA, causing microsatellite instability and mutations in growth genes. The syndrome causes colorectal cancer, often right sided, and endometrial cancer, which in affected women is about as common as colon cancer. Ovarian, stomach, small bowel, urinary tract, and biliary cancers are also more frequent.","whyNot":["Correct.","Failure to degrade β-catenin results from APC loss in familial adenomatous polyposis, which causes hundreds of colon polyps through chromosomal instability.","Failure to remove ultraviolet-induced pyrimidine dimers is a nucleotide excision repair defect, as in xeroderma pigmentosum, which causes skin cancers.","Failure of homologous recombination repair of double-strand breaks is the defect in BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations, which raise breast and ovarian cancer risk."],"study":{"topics":["Lynch syndrome and mismatch repair","DNA repair defects and cancer syndromes"],"comps":["RE-10","RE-13-J"]}},{"id":"RE-C19-Q3","stem":"Before the biopsy, her physician considered an endometrial polyp as the cause of her intermenstrual bleeding. Which of the following findings would have characterized a benign endometrial polyp?","options":["Benign endometrial glands and stroma deep within the myometrium","A circumscribed mass of whorled smooth muscle bundles","Localized glands and fibrous stroma with thick-walled vessels","Infiltrating glands that invade the endometrial stroma"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J","obj":"RE-13-J.6","dok":2,"approach":["Picture an endometrial polyp: a sessile or stalked growth that projects into the uterine cavity.","Its structure is a local overgrowth of endometrial glands and a fibrous stroma, with thick-walled blood vessels in the core.","Separate it from leiomyoma (smooth muscle), adenomyosis (glands in myometrium), and carcinoma (invasion)."],"why":"Endometrial polyps are benign, localized overgrowths of endometrial glands and stroma that project into the uterine cavity. Their stroma is often fibrous and contains thick-walled vessels. They cause abnormal or intermenstrual bleeding, and they are more common around menopause and with tamoxifen use. Endocervical polyps are similar growths of the cervical canal that also cause spotting. Polyps rarely turn malignant, but bleeding with a polyp still needs tissue diagnosis, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Endometrial glands and stroma deep in the myometrium describe adenomyosis, which enlarges the uterus and causes painful, heavy periods.","Whorled smooth muscle bundles in a circumscribed mass describe a leiomyoma, which arises from the myometrium.","Correct.","Glands that invade the stroma define endometrial carcinoma, not a benign polyp."],"study":{"topics":["Endometrial and endocervical polyps","Structural causes of abnormal uterine bleeding"],"comps":["RE-13-J","RE-13-A3"]}},{"id":"RE-C19-Q4","stem":"Her 58-year-old neighbor, who has no family history of cancer, develops postmenopausal bleeding. Endometrial biopsy shows hyperplasia, and imaging shows a 10-cm solid right ovarian mass. The ovarian tumor has sheets of cells with grooved, coffee-bean nuclei arranged around small spaces filled with eosinophilic material. Serum inhibin is increased. Which of the following best explains the neighbor's endometrial hyperplasia?","options":["Estradiol secreted by a granulosa cell tumor","Thyroxine secreted by a struma ovarii","Testosterone secreted by a Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor","hCG secreted by an ovarian choriocarcinoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-J7","obj":"RE-13-J.14","dok":3,"approach":["Coffee-bean nuclei around small eosinophilic spaces (Call-Exner bodies) and high inhibin identify a granulosa cell tumor.","Granulosa cells have aromatase and make estrogen.","In a postmenopausal woman, that estrogen is unopposed, so it causes endometrial hyperplasia and bleeding."],"why":"Granulosa cell tumors are sex cord-stromal tumors that make estrogen, and inhibin is a useful tumor marker. Histology shows coffee-bean nuclei with grooves and Call-Exner bodies. In girls, the estrogen causes precocious puberty; in older women, it causes endometrial hyperplasia, bleeding, and sometimes endometrial carcinoma. Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors make androgens and cause virilization. Fibromas make no hormones but can cause Meigs syndrome, with ascites and a right pleural effusion.","whyNot":["Correct.","Struma ovarii is a teratoma made mostly of thyroid tissue and can cause hyperthyroidism, not estrogen excess.","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors make androgens and cause hirsutism, clitoromegaly, and voice deepening. They do not cause endometrial hyperplasia.","Ovarian choriocarcinoma makes hCG and is a germ cell tumor of young women. Its histology is trophoblast, not grooved nuclei around eosinophilic spaces."],"study":{"topics":["Hormonally active ovarian stromal tumors","Granulosa cell tumor"],"comps":["RE-13-J7","RE-13-J2"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C20","system":"RE","dx":"Postmenopausal pelvic organ prolapse with cystocele, urethrocele, and rectocele","codes":["RE-13-I2","RE-13-I3","RE-13-I4","RE-13-A4"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a bulge in her vagina and pelvic pressure that worsens through the day. She has a feeling of incomplete bladder emptying, leaks urine when she coughs, and must press on the back wall of her vagina to finish a bowel movement. She has had four vaginal deliveries, one a forceps delivery of a 4.5-kg (9.9-lb) infant. Menopause was at age 51, and she has never taken hormone therapy. She has chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with a daily cough. BMI is 31 kg/m2. Pelvic examination with straining shows the anterior vaginal wall bulging to the hymen, descent of the distal anterior wall with urethral hypermobility, a bulging posterior wall, and the cervix descending to the mid-vagina. The vaginal mucosa is thin and pale with loss of rugae. Postvoid residual volume is 180 mL (normal less than 50). Diagnosis: pelvic organ prolapse with cystocele, urethrocele, and rectocele.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C20-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains how her vaginal deliveries predisposed her to pelvic organ prolapse decades later?","options":["Hypertrophy of the detrusor muscle from bladder outlet obstruction","Stretch injury of the levator ani and its nerve supply","Weakening of the transversalis fascia at the deep inguinal ring","Separation of the rectus abdominis muscles along the linea alba"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-I","obj":"RE-13-I.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the pelvic organs are held up by the levator ani and by connective tissue (endopelvic fascia and its ligaments).","A large baby and forceps stretch and tear the levator ani and stretch the pudendal nerve and the nerves to the levator ani.","Later, estrogen loss, aging, obesity, and chronic cough add strain to the damaged support, and the organs herniate into the vagina."],"why":"Cystocele, urethrocele, and rectocele are hernias of pelvic organs through weakened pelvic floor muscle and endopelvic fascia. Vaginal childbirth is the main risk factor, especially with large babies, forceps, and many deliveries, because it stretches or tears the levator ani and injures its nerves. Aging and estrogen loss weaken connective tissue. Obesity, chronic cough, and chronic straining raise abdominal pressure that pushes the organs down. She has all of these risks.","whyNot":["Detrusor hypertrophy follows bladder outlet obstruction, as in benign prostatic hyperplasia. Childbirth does not cause it, and it does not cause prolapse.","Correct.","Weakness of the transversalis fascia at the deep inguinal ring leads to an indirect inguinal hernia, not to vaginal wall prolapse.","Separation of the rectus muscles (diastasis recti) affects the anterior abdominal wall after pregnancy. It does not support the pelvic organs."],"study":{"topics":["Risk factors for pelvic organ prolapse","Levator ani injury in childbirth"],"comps":["RE-13-I","RE-4"]}},{"id":"RE-C20-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why she must press on the back wall of her vagina to complete defecation?","options":["Bladder bulging into the anterior vaginal wall","Urethra descending with the distal anterior wall","Rectum bulging into the posterior vaginal wall","Cervix descending along the axis of the vagina"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-13-I","obj":"RE-13-I.3","dok":2,"approach":["Match each symptom to the wall that bulges: anterior wall for bladder and urethra, posterior wall for rectum.","When the rectum bulges into the posterior vaginal wall, stool pushes into the pouch instead of out of the anus.","Pressing on the posterior vaginal wall (splinting) straightens the path so stool can pass."],"why":"A rectocele is herniation of the rectum through a weak rectovaginal septum into the posterior vaginal wall. Stool collects in the pouch, so emptying is hard and many women splint the vagina or perineum to finish. A cystocele is the bladder bulging into the upper anterior wall and causes incomplete emptying and a high postvoid residual, as she has. A urethrocele is the urethra descending with the lower anterior wall and causes stress incontinence with coughing.","whyNot":["A bladder bulging into the anterior wall is a cystocele. It explains her incomplete bladder emptying and high postvoid residual, not her trouble with defecation.","Urethral descent with the lower anterior wall is a urethrocele. It explains her leaking with coughing.","Correct.","Cervical descent is uterine prolapse, which causes a sense of pressure and a bulge but does not trap stool."],"study":{"topics":["Cystocele, urethrocele, and rectocele","Symptoms of pelvic organ prolapse"],"comps":["RE-13-I","RE-4"]}},{"id":"RE-C20-Q3","stem":"Weakening of which of the following structures most directly allows her cervix to descend into the vagina?","options":["Broad ligament","Cardinal ligament","Round ligament of the uterus","Ovarian ligament"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-4","obj":"RE-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["Cervical descent is uterine prolapse, a failure of support at the top of the vagina.","The cervix and upper vagina are held by the cardinal (transverse cervical) and uterosacral ligaments, resting on the levator ani and perineal body below.","The round, broad, and ovarian ligaments hold position or carry vessels but give little support against descent."],"why":"The uterus and upper vagina are held up by condensations of endopelvic fascia, mainly the cardinal ligaments, which run from the cervix and lateral vaginal fornix to the lateral pelvic wall and contain the uterine vessels, and the uterosacral ligaments, which run to the sacrum. Below, the levator ani and perineal body form the floor. When these supports weaken, the cervix and uterus slide down the vagina. Childbirth injury, aging, and estrogen loss all contribute.","whyNot":["The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold over the uterus, tubes, and ovaries. It carries vessels and gives little support against descent.","Correct.","The round ligament runs from the uterine horn through the inguinal canal to the labium majus. It helps keep the uterus tipped forward but does not prevent descent.","The ovarian ligament connects the ovary to the uterus. It holds the ovary in place but does not support the cervix."],"study":{"topics":["Pelvic support structures","Uterine prolapse"],"comps":["RE-4","RE-13-I"]}},{"id":"RE-C20-Q4","stem":"Her serum FSH is 82 mIU/mL (follicular phase reference 3.5 to 12.5). Which of the following best explains this elevation?","options":["Increased hypothalamic GnRH release from falling prolactin","Gonadotroph hyperplasia driven by rising adrenal androgens","Reduced renal clearance of FSH with aging","Loss of ovarian inhibin and estradiol feedback"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-A4","obj":"RE-13-A.5","dok":3,"approach":["Menopause is the depletion of ovarian follicles.","Granulosa cells of those follicles make inhibin and estradiol, which hold FSH and LH down.","With no follicles, feedback is lost and FSH rises most, followed by LH. The same estrogen loss explains her thin, pale vaginal mucosa."],"why":"Menopause happens when the ovarian follicle pool runs out. Without follicles, the ovary stops making inhibin and estradiol, so negative feedback ends and FSH and LH rise, with FSH rising earlier and higher. Estrogen loss causes vasomotor symptoms, atrophic vaginitis with thin pale mucosa and loss of rugae, bone loss and osteoporosis, and higher cardiovascular risk. It also weakens pelvic connective tissue, which adds to her prolapse.","whyNot":["Prolactin changes do not drive the rise in FSH after menopause. The main cause is lost ovarian feedback.","Adrenal androgens fall with age, and they do not cause gonadotroph hyperplasia.","FSH levels rise from more secretion, not from slower clearance. Renal clearance changes do not explain this large rise.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hormonal changes of menopause","Consequences of estrogen loss"],"comps":["RE-13-A4","RE-7"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C21","system":"RE","dx":"Endometriosis with an ovarian endometrioma","codes":["RE-13-B3"],"case":"A 31-year-old nulligravid woman comes to the physician because of a 2-year history of worsening pelvic pain that begins 1 to 2 days before each menstrual period, deep pain with intercourse, and inability to conceive after 18 months of regular unprotected intercourse. Her partner's semen analysis is normal. Pelvic examination shows a normal-sized, fixed, retroverted uterus, tender nodularity along the uterosacral ligaments, and a 6-cm left adnexal mass. Transvaginal ultrasonography shows a 6-cm left ovarian cyst with homogeneous low-level internal echoes. At laparoscopy, the cyst contains thick dark brown fluid, red and blue-black implants cover the uterosacral ligaments and pelvic peritoneum, and dense adhesions bind the left ovary to the pelvic sidewall. Biopsy specimens show endometrial glands and stroma with hemosiderin-laden macrophages. Diagnosis: endometriosis with a left ovarian endometrioma.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C21-Q1","stem":"Her implants are concentrated on the ovaries, the uterosacral ligaments, and the dependent pelvic peritoneum. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this distribution of her lesions?","options":["Metaplasia of the pleural and peritoneal mesothelium","Direct downgrowth of the basalis into the myometrium","Hematogenous spread of endometrial cells to distant organs and tissues","Retrograde flow of menstrual tissue through the uterine tubes"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-B3","obj":"RE-13-B.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note where the implants sit: ovaries and the lowest parts of the pelvic peritoneum.","Ask which theory delivers endometrial fragments into the pelvis and lets them settle by gravity.","Match that pattern to retrograde menstruation, and keep the other theories for unusual sites."],"why":"Retrograde menstruation is the leading theory of endometriosis. During menses, fragments of viable endometrium pass backward through the uterine tubes, spill onto the ovaries, and settle in the dependent pelvis, where they implant and respond to ovarian hormones. That is why the ovaries, uterosacral ligaments, and pelvic floor peritoneum are the most common sites. The implants bleed with each cycle, which explains the chocolate cyst, the adhesions, the painful periods, the deep dyspareunia, and the infertility.","whyNot":["Coelomic metaplasia is the favored theory for endometriosis in sites menstrual flow cannot reach, such as the pleura, or in women who have never menstruated. It does not explain a pelvic, gravity-dependent pattern.","Growth of the basal endometrium down into the myometrium describes adenomyosis, which stays inside the uterine wall.","Vascular or lymphatic spread is the best explanation for rare distant lesions, such as endometriosis in the lung or a distant scar. It does not explain clustering in the dependent pelvis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Theories of endometriosis","Endometrioma (chocolate cyst)"],"comps":["RE-13-B3","RE-13-B"]}},{"id":"RE-C21-Q2","stem":"Her 46-year-old aunt has a 1-year history of heavy, prolonged menstrual bleeding and severe cramping with each period. Pelvic examination shows a symmetrically enlarged, globular, soft, tender uterus about the size of a 12-week gestation, with smooth contours and no adnexal masses. A hysterectomy is performed. Which of the following findings is most likely on examination of the uterus?","options":["Endometrial implants on the uterine serosa only","Well-circumscribed whorled nodules of smooth muscle in the wall","Endometrial glands and stroma deep within the myometrium","Crowded endometrial glands with cytologic atypia"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-B","obj":"RE-13-B.4","dok":3,"approach":["Pick out the key exam finding: a uniformly enlarged, soft, tender uterus with smooth contours.","Link heavy bleeding and dysmenorrhea with a diffusely enlarged uterus to adenomyosis.","Predict the histology: endometrial glands and stroma inside the myometrium."],"why":"Adenomyosis is endometrial tissue, glands and stroma, lying within the myometrium. The ectopic tissue bleeds and provokes smooth muscle hypertrophy, so the whole uterus becomes enlarged, globular, boggy, and tender. Women in their 40s with heavy menstrual bleeding and dysmenorrhea are the typical patients. Endometriosis places the same tissue outside the uterus, and a leiomyoma makes the uterus irregular and firm.","whyNot":["Implants on the serosa are endometriosis. That condition causes pain and adhesions, but it does not enlarge the uterus symmetrically.","Leiomyomas are firm, discrete nodules of smooth muscle that make the uterus irregular and lumpy. They are usually not tender, and they would be the answer for a firm, nodular uterus.","Correct.","Crowded glands with atypia describe atypical endometrial hyperplasia. It causes abnormal bleeding, usually without a tender, enlarged uterus."],"study":{"topics":["Adenomyosis vs endometriosis vs leiomyoma"],"comps":["RE-13-B","RE-13-J3"]}},{"id":"RE-C21-Q3","stem":"Two months later, she comes to the emergency department because of sudden severe lower abdominal pain while standing at work. Ultrasonography shows a collapsed left ovarian cyst and free fluid in the pelvis. In which of the following spaces has the fluid most likely collected?","options":["Hepatorenal recess","Rectouterine pouch","Vesicouterine pouch","Left paracolic gutter"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-4","obj":"RE-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize rupture of the endometrioma, which spills old blood and cyst fluid into the peritoneal cavity.","Use her position: upright, so fluid runs to the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity.","Recall that in a standing woman this is the peritoneal recess between the uterus and rectum."],"why":"The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas) is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity in a standing or sitting woman. It lies behind the uterus and in front of the rectum, so free blood, pus, or cyst fluid collects there first. It sits just above the posterior vaginal fornix, which is why fluid there can be seen on transvaginal ultrasonography and sampled through the posterior fornix. Endometriosis implants are also common on its peritoneum for the same gravity-dependent reason.","whyNot":["The hepatorenal recess is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity when a person lies supine. It is not the first collecting site in an upright patient.","Correct.","The vesicouterine pouch lies between the bladder and the uterus. It is shallower and higher than the pouch behind the uterus.","The paracolic gutters carry fluid between the upper abdomen and the pelvis. They are channels, and fluid drains through them toward the pelvis."],"study":{"topics":["Peritoneal recesses of the female pelvis","Broad ligament parts"],"comps":["RE-4"]}},{"id":"RE-C21-Q4","stem":"Her 16-year-old sister has crampy lower abdominal pain that begins with the onset of menstrual flow and lasts 1 to 2 days, with nausea. Her pelvic examination and pelvic ultrasonography are normal. Which of the following mechanisms most likely explains the sister's pain?","options":["Cyclic bleeding from endometrial implants on the peritoneum","Prostaglandin-induced myometrial contraction and ischemia","Peritoneal irritation from a ruptured ovarian follicle","Swelling of endometrial tissue within the myometrium"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-A","obj":"RE-13-A.8","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing: pain starts with menses in an adolescent with a normal pelvis.","Classify it as primary dysmenorrhea, which has no structural cause.","Recall the mediator: endometrial prostaglandins released as the endometrium sheds."],"why":"Primary dysmenorrhea is menstrual pain without pelvic disease. When progesterone falls at the end of the cycle, the shedding endometrium releases prostaglandins, mainly PGF2-alpha. These cause strong, uncoordinated myometrial contractions that compress uterine vessels and make the muscle ischemic, which produces crampy pain. Prostaglandins also cause the nausea and loose stools that often go with it, and NSAIDs relieve it by blocking prostaglandin synthesis.","whyNot":["Bleeding implants cause secondary dysmenorrhea from endometriosis, which is the explanation for this patient's own pain. Her sister has a normal examination and no sign of structural disease.","Correct.","A ruptured follicle causes mittelschmerz, which is one-sided pain at midcycle around ovulation. It does not start with menstrual flow.","Endometrial tissue within the myometrium is adenomyosis. It causes secondary dysmenorrhea in older women with an enlarged, tender uterus."],"study":{"topics":["Primary vs secondary dysmenorrhea","Mittelschmerz"],"comps":["RE-13-A","RE-7"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C22","system":"RE","dx":"Acute endometritis after cesarean delivery complicated by placenta accreta","codes":["RE-13-B4"],"case":"A 33-year-old woman, gravida 3, para 3, undergoes repeat cesarean delivery at 39 weeks' gestation after an unsuccessful trial of labor with membranes ruptured for 18 hours. She has had two previous cesarean deliveries. The placenta is implanted on the anterior lower uterine segment over the prior incision and does not separate; it is removed piecemeal by manual extraction, with blood loss of 1400 mL. Pathologic examination shows chorionic villi attached directly to myometrial smooth muscle. On postoperative day 2, her temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 110/68 mm Hg. The uterine fundus is markedly tender, and the lochia is foul smelling. Diagnosis: acute endometritis after cesarean delivery complicated by placenta accreta.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C22-Q1","stem":"She improves with intravenous antibiotics. Five months later, she has persistent intermenstrual spotting and dull pelvic ache. An endometrial biopsy is obtained. Which of the following findings would best establish a diagnosis of chronic endometritis?","options":["Plasma cells scattered in the endometrial stroma","Decidualized stroma with hemosiderin deposits","Neutrophils within the lumens of endometrial glands","Lymphoid aggregates in the basal endometrium"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-B4","obj":"RE-13-B.5","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two forms: acute endometritis follows delivery or retained products and shows neutrophils.","Recall the cell that defines chronic endometritis because it is absent from normal endometrium.","Exclude findings that also occur in a normal cycle or after a recent pregnancy."],"why":"Plasma cells are the diagnostic marker of chronic endometritis because the normal endometrium does not contain them. Chronic endometritis follows retained products of conception, prior pelvic infection, or an intrauterine device, and it causes irregular bleeding, pelvic pain, and infertility. Acute endometritis, like her postpartum infection, is a polymicrobial ascending infection with neutrophils, fever, and a tender uterus.","whyNot":["Correct.","Decidualized stroma reflects progesterone effect in pregnancy, and hemosiderin marks old bleeding. Neither defines chronic endometritis.","Neutrophils in glands fit acute endometritis, and they also appear normally in menstrual endometrium. They do not establish a chronic process.","Small lymphoid aggregates are a normal finding in the basal endometrium, so they do not define chronic inflammation."],"study":{"topics":["Acute vs chronic endometritis"],"comps":["RE-13-B4","RE-13-B"]}},{"id":"RE-C22-Q2","stem":"Blood cultures obtained on postoperative day 2 grow gram-positive cocci in chains. On blood agar, the colonies show a narrow zone of complete hemolysis. The isolate is resistant to bacitracin and enhances the hemolysis of Staphylococcus aureus in a CAMP test. Which of the following organisms is most likely?","options":["Enterococcus faecalis","Streptococcus pyogenes","Streptococcus agalactiae","Streptococcus pneumoniae"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-12","obj":"RE-12.7","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-positive cocci in chains point to streptococci or enterococci.","Complete (beta) hemolysis narrows it to group A or group B streptococci.","Bacitracin resistance and a positive CAMP test identify group B."],"why":"Streptococcus agalactiae (group B streptococcus) is a beta-hemolytic streptococcus that is bacitracin resistant and CAMP positive. It colonizes the vagina and rectum of many women and causes postpartum endometritis and bacteremia, especially after prolonged rupture of membranes. In the newborn it is a leading cause of early-onset sepsis, pneumonia, and meningitis, which is why pregnant women are screened late in the third trimester.","whyNot":["Enterococcus faecalis is usually nonhemolytic and is identified by growth in bile and 6.5% salt. It can take part in polymicrobial endometritis but does not fit these tests.","Streptococcus pyogenes is beta-hemolytic but is bacitracin sensitive and CAMP negative. It would be the answer for a sensitive isolate.","Correct.","Streptococcus pneumoniae is alpha-hemolytic and grows as lancet-shaped diplococci that are optochin sensitive."],"study":{"topics":["Group B streptococcus identification","Streptococcal lab tests"],"comps":["RE-12","RE-13-B4"]}},{"id":"RE-C22-Q3","stem":"During her earlier trial of labor, contractions grew stronger and more frequent as the fetal head descended and stretched the cervix. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this progressive strengthening of contractions?","options":["Cervical stretch increasing posterior pituitary oxytocin release","Cervical stretch increasing anterior pituitary prolactin release","Rising progesterone increasing myometrial gap junctions","Falling estrogen increasing myometrial oxytocin receptors"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-6","obj":"RE-6.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize a positive feedback loop: contraction causes stretch, and stretch causes more contraction.","Identify the afferent signal (cervical stretch) and the hormone it releases.","Recall that oxytocin comes from the posterior pituitary and acts with prostaglandins on the myometrium."],"why":"Labor is a positive feedback loop called the Ferguson reflex. Stretch of the cervix by the presenting part sends afferent signals to the hypothalamus, which increases oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary. Oxytocin and the prostaglandins it promotes make the myometrium contract harder, which pushes the head further and stretches the cervix more. The loop continues until the baby is delivered and the stretch stops.","whyNot":["Correct.","Prolactin from the anterior pituitary drives milk production. It does not increase uterine contraction.","Progesterone keeps the myometrium quiet in pregnancy. Its effective withdrawal, together with rising estrogen, increases gap junctions near term.","Estrogen rises toward term and increases oxytocin receptors and gap junctions. A fall in estrogen would weaken contractions."],"study":{"topics":["Ferguson reflex","Hormones of parturition"],"comps":["RE-6"]}},{"id":"RE-C22-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her placenta could not separate from the uterine wall at delivery?","options":["Implantation of the placenta over the internal cervical os","Failure of trophoblast to remodel the maternal spiral arteries","Overgrowth of syncytiotrophoblast secreting excess hCG","Deficient decidua basalis at the site of the uterine scar"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-13-H","obj":"RE-13-H.8","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pathology: villi attached directly to myometrium, which defines placenta accreta.","Ask what normally sits between villi and myometrium and allows a clean separation.","Connect the prior cesarean scar to a missing or thin decidua basalis."],"why":"The decidua basalis is the maternal layer under the placenta, and it forms the plane along which the placenta separates after delivery. Where the decidua is thin or absent, as over a cesarean scar, the villi attach directly to myometrium (accreta), invade into it (increta), or pass through it (percreta). Prior cesarean delivery and placenta previa are the major risk factors, and the risk rises with each cesarean. The result is a placenta that will not separate and severe postpartum hemorrhage.","whyNot":["Implantation over the cervical os is placenta previa, which causes painless bleeding. Previa raises the risk of accreta but does not by itself prevent separation.","Failed spiral artery remodeling causes preeclampsia and fetal growth restriction. It reflects too shallow an invasion rather than abnormal attachment.","Excess syncytiotrophoblast with very high hCG fits a hydatidiform mole or choriocarcinoma. It does not explain a normal placenta stuck to myometrium.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Placenta accreta spectrum","Decidua basalis"],"comps":["RE-13-H","RE-1"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C23","system":"RE","dx":"Pelvic inflammatory disease with salpingitis and a tubo-ovarian abscess","codes":["RE-13-B6","RE-13-B7","RE-13-G4"],"case":"A 22-year-old nulligravid woman comes to the emergency department because of a 5-day history of lower abdominal pain, fever, and vaginal discharge. Her last menstrual period ended 10 days ago. She has had a new sexual partner for 2 months and uses condoms inconsistently. Her temperature is 39.1 °C (102.4 °F), pulse is 110/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Speculum examination shows mucopurulent discharge from the cervical os. Bimanual examination shows cervical motion tenderness, bilateral adnexal tenderness, and a tender right adnexal mass. Urine hCG is negative. Leukocyte count is 17,800/mm3 (4500 to 11,000). Transvaginal ultrasonography shows a dilated, fluid-filled right uterine tube and a 6-cm thick-walled multiloculated mass involving the right ovary. Diagnosis: pelvic inflammatory disease with salpingitis and a right tubo-ovarian abscess.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C23-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes how infection most likely reached her uterine tubes?","options":["Ascent of cervical gonococci, chlamydiae, and vaginal flora","Direct extension from a ruptured, inflamed appendix","Hematogenous seeding of the tubes by Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Lymphatic spread through the parametrium from a postpartum uterus"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-B6","obj":"RE-13-B.7","dok":2,"approach":["Connect the cervical discharge and cervical motion tenderness to infection that began in the cervix.","Recall that PID spreads upward along mucosal surfaces from cervix to endometrium to tubes.","Name the usual organisms: N. gonorrhoeae, C. trachomatis, and polymicrobial vaginal flora."],"why":"Pelvic inflammatory disease is an ascending infection. Organisms that infect the endocervix, most often Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis, travel up through the endometrial cavity to the tubal mucosa, often after menses when the cervical mucus barrier is weakest. Anaerobes and other vaginal flora join in, so established PID and abscesses are polymicrobial. Cervical motion tenderness, adnexal tenderness, fever, and purulent cervical discharge are the classic findings.","whyNot":["Correct.","A ruptured appendix can involve the right adnexa, but it begins with periumbilical pain and does not cause mucopurulent cervicitis.","Tuberculous salpingitis does come from blood-borne spread, but it is chronic and granulomatous, without purulent cervicitis. It would fit a woman from an endemic area with infertility and weight loss.","Lymphatic spread through the parametrium is the route of postpartum and postabortion infections. She has not been pregnant."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of PID","Organisms of PID"],"comps":["RE-13-B6","RE-13-B7"]}},{"id":"RE-C23-Q2","stem":"She recovers with treatment. Three years later, she comes to the emergency department because of vaginal spotting and right lower quadrant pain after 7 weeks of amenorrhea. Serum hCG is elevated, and ultrasonography shows an empty uterus and a gestational sac in the right ampulla. Which of the following best explains the link between her earlier infection and this pregnancy?","options":["Antisperm antibodies that delayed fertilization in the tube","Luteal phase deficiency that prevented endometrial implantation","Persistent chlamydiae that attracted the embryo to the tubal wall","Scarring and loss of ciliated cells that slowed embryo transport"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-11","obj":"RE-11.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize a tubal ectopic pregnancy in a woman with prior salpingitis.","Recall what the host inflammatory response to chlamydial and gonococcal salpingitis does to the tube.","Link damaged cilia and partial obstruction to an embryo held in the tube."],"why":"Salpingitis damages the tube mainly through the host inflammatory response. Neutrophils and cytokines destroy ciliated cells, and healing causes fibrosis, adhesions, and partial or complete blockage. Chlamydial infection is especially harmful because it is often silent and repeated, and the immune response to chlamydial antigens such as heat shock protein 60 drives the scarring. A partly blocked tube with poor ciliary transport lets sperm through but holds the larger embryo, so it implants in the tube. A fully blocked tube causes tubal infertility.","whyNot":["Antisperm antibodies can reduce fertility, but they act before fertilization and do not cause an embryo to implant in the tube.","A luteal phase defect impairs implantation in the uterus and causes early loss. It does not move implantation into the tube.","Chlamydiae do not attract an embryo. The damage comes from scarring left by the immune response to infection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tubal damage after salpingitis","Ectopic pregnancy risk factors"],"comps":["RE-11","RE-13-B7"]}},{"id":"RE-C23-Q3","stem":"On the third hospital day, despite intravenous antibiotics, she suddenly has severe diffuse abdominal pain. Her temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), pulse is 132/min, and blood pressure is 82/50 mm Hg. The abdomen is rigid with diffuse rebound tenderness. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this change?","options":["Torsion of the right adnexa","Rupture of the tubo-ovarian abscess","Rupture of a tubal ectopic pregnancy","Perihepatitis with liver capsule adhesions"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-G4","obj":"RE-13-G.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the sudden change from localized pelvic pain to diffuse peritonitis with shock.","Recall that a tubo-ovarian abscess can rupture and spill pus into the peritoneal cavity.","Exclude causes that do not fit a negative hCG or a septic picture."],"why":"A tubo-ovarian abscess forms when an inflamed, pus-filled tube adheres to the ovary and the two form one walled-off cavity. If it ruptures, pus floods the peritoneal cavity and causes generalized peritonitis and septic shock, which is a surgical emergency. Women who survive often have adhesions and tubal damage that cause later infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain.","whyNot":["Adnexal torsion causes sudden one-sided pain and vomiting. It does not cause diffuse peritonitis with high fever and septic shock.","Correct.","Ectopic rupture causes hemorrhagic shock, but her urine hCG was negative, which rules it out.","Perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome) causes right upper quadrant pain from inflammation of the liver capsule. It does not cause sudden shock with a rigid abdomen."],"study":{"topics":["Tubo-ovarian abscess complications"],"comps":["RE-13-G4","RE-13-B6"]}},{"id":"RE-C23-Q4","stem":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis infect columnar epithelial cells. In an adult woman, which of the following epithelia lines the vagina and explains why these organisms cause cervicitis rather than vaginitis?","options":["Transitional epithelium","Mucus-secreting simple columnar epithelium","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Ciliated and secretory simple columnar epithelium"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-2","obj":"RE-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the epithelium of each segment: tube, endometrium, endocervix, ectocervix, and vagina.","Note that the vagina and ectocervix share a thick squamous lining.","Connect the columnar endocervix to the site these organisms infect."],"why":"The adult vagina and ectocervix are lined by thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which these organisms do not readily infect. The endocervix is lined by mucus-secreting simple columnar epithelium, so the discharge of gonococcal and chlamydial infection comes from the cervical os. The uterine tube has ciliated simple columnar epithelium, which is why ascending infection damages it. The junction between the squamous and columnar zones is the transformation zone, where cervical dysplasia arises.","whyNot":["Transitional epithelium lines the bladder and ureters. It is not found in the vagina.","Mucus-secreting simple columnar epithelium lines the endocervix, the main site of gonococcal and chlamydial cervicitis.","Correct.","Ciliated simple columnar epithelium lines the uterine tube, which these organisms do infect in salpingitis."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelia of the female tract","Transformation zone"],"comps":["RE-2","RE-13-B2"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C24","system":"RE","dx":"Preeclampsia with severe features and placental abruption","codes":["RE-13-H4"],"case":"A 19-year-old primigravid woman at 34 weeks' gestation comes to the labor unit because of a severe headache and flashing lights in her vision since the previous evening. Her blood pressure at 20 weeks was 114/72 mm Hg. Today her temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 166/112 mm Hg; 15 minutes later it is 164/110 mm Hg. She has 2+ pitting edema of the legs and brisk deep tendon reflexes. Fundal height is 30 cm. The urine protein-to-creatinine ratio is 1.1 (less than 0.3). Ultrasonography shows an estimated fetal weight below the 10th percentile. One hour later, she has sudden severe abdominal pain and dark vaginal bleeding; the uterus is firm and tender, and fetal heart tracing shows late decelerations. Diagnosis: preeclampsia with severe features complicated by placental abruption.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C24-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the maternal endothelial dysfunction that produced her hypertension and proteinuria?","options":["Placental hCG stimulating maternal thyrotropin receptors","Excess endothelial nitric oxide relaxing systemic arterioles","Antiphospholipid antibodies activating maternal platelets","Placental soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 binding VEGF"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-13-H4","obj":"RE-13-H.5","dok":3,"approach":["Start at the placenta: shallow trophoblast invasion leaves narrow, unremodeled spiral arteries.","Recall that the ischemic placenta releases antiangiogenic factors into the maternal blood.","Link loss of VEGF and placental growth factor signaling to leaky, constricted maternal vessels."],"why":"In preeclampsia, trophoblast fails to remodel the maternal spiral arteries into wide, low-resistance vessels. The underperfused placenta releases soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1), a decoy receptor that binds VEGF and placental growth factor, and soluble endoglin, which blocks TGF-beta signaling. Without these signals the maternal endothelium makes less nitric oxide and prostacyclin, so vessels constrict, leak protein, and activate platelets. That endothelial injury explains the hypertension, proteinuria, cerebral symptoms, and liver and kidney damage.","whyNot":["Very high hCG can stimulate the TSH receptor and cause hyperthyroidism, as in a molar pregnancy. It does not cause endothelial dysfunction.","Nitric oxide production falls in preeclampsia. More nitric oxide would lower blood pressure.","Antiphospholipid syndrome raises the risk of preeclampsia and thrombosis, but she has no history to suggest it, and it is not the general mechanism of the disease.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of preeclampsia","sFlt-1 and soluble endoglin"],"comps":["RE-13-H4","RE-1"]}},{"id":"RE-C24-Q2","stem":"Laboratory studies before delivery show: hemoglobin, 9.6 g/dL (11 to 14); platelet count, 78,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000); aspartate aminotransferase, 240 U/L (10 to 40); lactate dehydrogenase, 920 U/L (100 to 250). A peripheral blood smear shows schistocytes. Which of the following complications of her disorder do these findings indicate?","options":["Acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","HELLP syndrome","Dilutional anemia of pregnancy"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-H4","obj":"RE-13-H.6","dok":2,"approach":["Group the findings: hemolysis (schistocytes, high LDH), elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets.","Place them in context: a patient who already has preeclampsia with severe features.","Name the complication that combines all three."],"why":"HELLP syndrome is hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets, and it is a severe form of preeclampsia. Endothelial injury in small vessels shears red cells (schistocytes, high LDH), consumes platelets, and causes periportal hepatic necrosis that raises transaminases. Other complications of preeclampsia include eclamptic seizures, placental abruption, and fetal growth restriction, and she has two of these already. Delivery is the definitive treatment.","whyNot":["Acute fatty liver of pregnancy causes liver failure with hypoglycemia, high bilirubin, and coagulopathy. Schistocytic hemolysis with low platelets in a preeclamptic woman fits HELLP better.","TTP also causes microangiopathic hemolysis and low platelets, but it is caused by ADAMTS13 deficiency and does not explain high transaminases in a woman with preeclampsia.","Correct.","Dilutional anemia is the normal mild fall in hemoglobin from plasma volume expansion. It does not cause schistocytes, thrombocytopenia, or liver injury."],"study":{"topics":["HELLP syndrome","Complications of preeclampsia"],"comps":["RE-13-H4"]}},{"id":"RE-C24-Q3","stem":"Over the next 2 hours, she continues to bleed and develops oozing from intravenous sites. Which of the following sets of laboratory changes is most likely?","options":["High fibrinogen, normal D-dimer, normal prothrombin time","Low fibrinogen, high D-dimer, prolonged prothrombin time","Normal fibrinogen, normal D-dimer, prolonged bleeding time","Normal fibrinogen, high D-dimer, shortened prothrombin time"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-H","obj":"RE-13-H.7","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the abruption: painful bleeding with a firm, tender uterus, unlike the painless bleeding of placenta previa.","Recall that the separating placenta releases tissue factor into the maternal circulation.","Predict consumptive coagulopathy: clotting factors and fibrinogen are used up while fibrin is broken down."],"why":"Placental abruption is premature separation of a normally placed placenta, with painful bleeding and a tender, firm uterus. Hypertension, cocaine use, and abdominal trauma are its main risk factors. Damaged decidua and placenta release large amounts of tissue factor, which triggers disseminated intravascular coagulation. Fibrinogen and clotting factors are consumed, so the prothrombin time lengthens and fibrinogen falls, while breakdown of fibrin raises D-dimer. Placenta previa, by contrast, causes painless bright red bleeding and rarely causes DIC.","whyNot":["High fibrinogen with normal clotting tests is the normal hypercoagulable state of late pregnancy. It does not explain oozing from intravenous sites.","Correct.","An isolated long bleeding time points to a platelet function defect. It does not explain consumptive bleeding after abruption.","A high D-dimer can occur with DIC, but consumption lengthens the prothrombin time rather than shortening it, and fibrinogen falls."],"study":{"topics":["Placental abruption vs placenta previa","DIC in obstetrics"],"comps":["RE-13-H","HE-9-C1"]}},{"id":"RE-C24-Q4","stem":"At her 28-week prenatal visit, before preeclampsia developed, which of the following sets of changes compared with her nonpregnant state would most likely have been present?","options":["Cardiac output increased, vascular resistance increased, GFR decreased","Cardiac output increased, vascular resistance decreased, GFR increased","Cardiac output unchanged, vascular resistance increased, GFR unchanged","Cardiac output decreased, vascular resistance decreased, GFR increased"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-6","obj":"RE-6.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that normal pregnancy expands plasma volume and dilates systemic vessels.","Predict the effect on each variable: more volume and heart rate raise cardiac output, and vasodilation lowers resistance.","Apply the higher renal plasma flow to glomerular filtration."],"why":"In normal pregnancy, progesterone, nitric oxide, and the low-resistance placental circulation lower systemic vascular resistance. Plasma volume and heart rate rise, so cardiac output increases by about 30 to 50 percent. Renal plasma flow and GFR rise by about 50 percent, so serum creatinine falls. Other normal changes include dilutional anemia, respiratory alkalosis from progesterone-driven breathing, and hypercoagulability. Preeclampsia reverses the vascular part of this picture with high resistance and vasoconstriction.","whyNot":["High resistance and reduced GFR describe preeclampsia, not normal pregnancy.","Correct.","Pregnancy changes all three variables. Unchanged output and GFR with high resistance fit none of its normal adaptations.","Cardiac output rises in normal pregnancy. A falling output would suggest heart disease or severe volume loss."],"study":{"topics":["Maternal cardiovascular and renal adaptations"],"comps":["RE-6"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C25","system":"RE","dx":"Candidal vaginitis in a pregnant woman with gestational diabetes","codes":["RE-13-B8"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman, gravida 2, para 1, at 30 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of a 1-week history of intense vulvar itching, burning with urination, and a white vaginal discharge. Gestational diabetes was diagnosed at 26 weeks, and her fasting glucose levels have been 105 to 120 mg/dL (target less than 95) on diet alone. She has taken no antibiotics. Pelvic examination shows erythematous, excoriated, edematous vulvar skin and a thick, white, curd-like discharge adherent to the vaginal walls. Vaginal pH is 4.2 (3.8 to 4.5). The whiff test is negative. A potassium hydroxide preparation confirms the diagnosis. Diagnosis: candidal vulvovaginitis.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C25-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why this patient is at increased risk for her vaginal infection?","options":["Loss of vaginal lactobacilli from recent antibiotic use","Neutropenia impairing phagocytosis of fungal elements","Increased vaginal glycogen and glucose available to yeast","Rise in vaginal pH that favors anaerobic bacterial overgrowth"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-B8","obj":"RE-13-B.9","dok":3,"approach":["List her risk factors: pregnancy and poorly controlled gestational diabetes, with no antibiotic exposure.","Recall how high estrogen changes the vaginal epithelium and how hyperglycemia changes secretions.","Link more glycogen and glucose to better yeast growth and adherence."],"why":"Candida albicans is part of normal vaginal flora and overgrows when local conditions favor it. High estrogen in pregnancy increases glycogen in the vaginal epithelium, and hyperglycemia adds glucose to vaginal secretions, so the yeast has more fuel and adheres better. Antibiotics that kill lactobacilli and impaired cell-mediated immunity are the other classic risk factors. The typical result is itching, a thick white curd-like discharge, a normal vaginal pH, and pseudohyphae on KOH preparation.","whyNot":["Antibiotic use is a classic risk factor, but she has taken none.","Neutropenia predisposes to invasive fungal infection, but nothing suggests it here, and it is not the usual setting for vaginal candidiasis.","Correct.","A rise in pH with anaerobic overgrowth describes bacterial vaginosis. Her pH is normal, which fits candidiasis."],"study":{"topics":["Risk factors for vulvovaginal candidiasis"],"comps":["RE-13-B8","RE-6"]}},{"id":"RE-C25-Q2","stem":"Which of the following microscopic findings is most likely on her potassium hydroxide preparation?","options":["Budding yeast cells with pseudohyphae","Epithelial cells covered with adherent coccobacilli","Motile pear-shaped organisms with flagella","Septate hyphae branching at acute angles"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-12","obj":"RE-12.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism from the clinical picture: curd-like discharge, itching, normal pH.","Recall why KOH is used: it dissolves epithelial cells and leaves fungal elements.","Pick the microscopic form of Candida albicans."],"why":"Candida albicans is a dimorphic fungus. In tissue and on KOH preparation it appears as oval budding yeast cells and pseudohyphae, which are chains of elongated buds with constrictions at the joins. KOH dissolves epithelial cells and debris so these fungal elements stand out. A thick white discharge with intense itching and a normal pH fits this finding.","whyNot":["Correct.","Epithelial cells studded with coccobacilli are clue cells, which identify bacterial vaginosis from Gardnerella vaginalis.","Motile, flagellated pear-shaped protozoa on a saline wet mount identify Trichomonas vaginalis.","Septate hyphae branching at acute angles describe Aspergillus, which does not cause vaginitis."],"study":{"topics":["Microscopy of vaginal infections"],"comps":["RE-12","IN-13-H2"]}},{"id":"RE-C25-Q3","stem":"Three months after delivery, she returns because of a thin, gray, homogeneous vaginal discharge with a fishy odor that is worse after intercourse. She has no itching. Which of the following sets of findings would best distinguish this new condition from candidiasis and trichomoniasis?","options":["pH 4.5 or lower, positive whiff test, abundant lactobacilli","pH 4.5 or lower, negative whiff test, pseudohyphae on KOH","pH above 4.5, positive whiff test, motile flagellates on wet mount","pH above 4.5, positive whiff test, clue cells on wet mount"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-K2","obj":"RE-13-K.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the clinical picture of bacterial vaginosis: thin gray discharge, fishy odor, little irritation.","Use pH to separate the infections: candidiasis keeps a normal pH, while BV and trichomoniasis raise it.","Separate BV from trichomoniasis by the wet mount: clue cells versus motile flagellates."],"why":"Bacterial vaginosis is a shift from lactobacilli to Gardnerella vaginalis and anaerobes. With fewer lactobacilli, less lactic acid is made and the pH rises above 4.5. Anaerobes produce amines, which give a fishy odor when KOH is added (positive whiff test). The wet mount shows clue cells, epithelial cells coated with bacteria. Trichomoniasis also raises pH and can give a positive whiff test, so the wet mount is what separates the two. Candidiasis has a normal pH and a negative whiff test.","whyNot":["Abundant lactobacilli keep the pH low and make a positive whiff test unlikely. This set describes no single infection.","Normal pH, negative whiff test, and pseudohyphae describe candidiasis, which was her earlier infection.","High pH, a positive whiff test, and motile flagellates fit trichomoniasis, which causes a frothy yellow-green discharge with irritation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Comparing BV, trichomoniasis, and candidiasis"],"comps":["RE-13-K","RE-13-K2","RE-13-K10"]}},{"id":"RE-C25-Q4","stem":"Her glucose tolerance was normal at 12 weeks and abnormal at 26 weeks. Which of the following best explains why her glucose intolerance appeared in the second half of pregnancy?","options":["Placental lactogen increasing maternal insulin resistance","Placental insulin clearance lowering circulating insulin levels","Estrogen suppressing hepatic glycogen storage","Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-6","obj":"RE-6.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: glucose intolerance appears as the placenta grows.","Recall which placental hormone rises with placental mass and opposes insulin action.","Link insulin resistance to gestational diabetes when beta cells cannot raise insulin output enough."],"why":"Human placental lactogen (human chorionic somatomammotropin) rises with placental mass through the second and third trimesters. It acts like growth hormone, increasing maternal insulin resistance and lipolysis so more glucose and fatty acids reach the fetus. Progesterone, cortisol, and other placental hormones add to this effect. Most women raise insulin secretion to compensate; when beta cells cannot keep up, gestational diabetes develops, which is why screening is done at 24 to 28 weeks.","whyNot":["Correct.","The placenta does break down some insulin, but most women respond by secreting more, and this does not explain the timing as well as rising placental lactogen.","This is not a recognized mechanism of gestational diabetes, and it would not explain insulin resistance that rises as the placenta grows.","Autoimmune beta cell destruction causes type 1 diabetes. Gestational diabetes is a state of insulin resistance with inadequate compensation."],"study":{"topics":["Human placental lactogen","Gestational diabetes physiology"],"comps":["RE-6","EN-12-D"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C26","system":"RE","dx":"Chlamydial nongonococcal urethritis with reactive arthritis","codes":["RE-13-K3","RE-13-K7"],"case":"A 26-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-week history of a painful, swollen left knee and right ankle, pain in both heels, and red eyes. Three weeks ago, after unprotected intercourse with a new partner, he had burning with urination and a scant clear urethral discharge that he did not treat. His temperature is 37.8 °C (100.0 °F). Examination shows bilateral conjunctival injection, a warm left knee effusion, and tenderness at both Achilles tendon insertions. A Gram stain of urethral discharge shows many neutrophils and no organisms. Synovial fluid shows a leukocyte count of 18,000/mm3 (less than 200), no crystals, and a negative culture. A urine nucleic acid amplification test is positive for Chlamydia trachomatis and negative for Neisseria gonorrhoeae. HLA-B27 is positive. Diagnosis: chlamydial nongonococcal urethritis with reactive arthritis.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C26-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his joint disease?","options":["Sterile immune-mediated synovitis triggered by infection","Autoantibodies against the Fc portion of IgG","Monosodium urate crystal deposition provoked by fever","Hematogenous seeding of the joints by viable urethral bacteria"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-K7","obj":"RE-13-K.9","dok":3,"approach":["Note the sequence: urethritis, then arthritis, conjunctivitis, and enthesitis weeks later.","Use the sterile synovial fluid and HLA-B27 to place it among the seronegative spondyloarthropathies.","Choose an immune mechanism triggered by infection elsewhere."],"why":"Reactive arthritis is a sterile, immune-mediated inflammation of joints, entheses, and eyes that follows a genitourinary or enteric infection. Nongonococcal urethritis is most often caused by Chlamydia trachomatis, followed by Mycoplasma genitalium, Ureaplasma urealyticum, and Trichomonas vaginalis. In people with HLA-B27, the immune response to bacterial antigens, which can persist in the synovium, drives inflammation of joints and entheses. The classic picture is arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis, with asymmetric large-joint arthritis and heel pain.","whyNot":["Correct.","Antibodies to the Fc portion of IgG are rheumatoid factor. Reactive arthritis is seronegative.","Gout comes from urate crystals, which are absent from his fluid, and it does not explain the conjunctivitis or urethritis.","Bacteria seeding the joint cause septic arthritis, as in disseminated gonococcal infection. His synovial culture is negative and his urethral Gram stain shows no gonococci."],"study":{"topics":["Reactive arthritis","Causes of nongonococcal urethritis"],"comps":["RE-13-K7","MS-13-B7"]}},{"id":"RE-C26-Q2","stem":"His partner is 24 weeks pregnant. Her cervical nucleic acid amplification test is positive for the same organism, but she has no symptoms. If she is not treated and delivers vaginally, her infant is at greatest risk for which of the following?","options":["Early-onset sepsis with meningitis in the first days of life","Vesicles of the skin, eyes, and mouth in the first weeks","Conjunctivitis at 5 to 14 days, then afebrile pneumonia","Purulent conjunctivitis with corneal ulceration at 2 to 5 days"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-K3","obj":"RE-13-K.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize that genital chlamydial infection is often silent in women.","Recall that the newborn acquires it in the birth canal and that it infects conjunctival and respiratory epithelium.","Match the timing: conjunctivitis in the first 2 weeks, then a staccato-cough pneumonia at 1 to 3 months."],"why":"Most women with genital chlamydial infection have no symptoms, so it often goes untreated. An infant born through an infected cervix can develop inclusion conjunctivitis about 5 to 14 days after birth and an afebrile pneumonia with a staccato cough, often with eosinophilia, at 1 to 3 months. In adults the same organism causes urethritis, cervicitis, epididymitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, and reactive arthritis. Testing and treating pregnant women prevents neonatal infection.","whyNot":["Early-onset sepsis and meningitis are typical of group B streptococcus, Escherichia coli, and Listeria.","Vesicles of the skin, eyes, and mouth are a form of neonatal herpes simplex infection.","Correct.","Hyperacute purulent conjunctivitis at 2 to 5 days that can perforate the cornea is gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum."],"study":{"topics":["Chlamydial infection in women and newborns"],"comps":["RE-13-K3","RE-12"]}},{"id":"RE-C26-Q3","stem":"The organism cannot be grown on cell-free culture media. Which of the following best describes the serovar and the form of the organism that was transmitted to him during intercourse?","options":["Serovar A to C reticulate body","Serovar D to K reticulate body","Serovar A to C elementary body","Serovar D to K elementary body"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-12","obj":"RE-12.2","dok":2,"approach":["Match the disease to the serovar group: genital and neonatal infections are serovars D to K.","Recall the two forms in the chlamydial life cycle and which one survives outside cells.","Pick the small, metabolically inactive form that enters host cells."],"why":"Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium, so it cannot grow on cell-free media. It has two forms. The elementary body is small, metabolically inactive, and infectious, and it enters columnar cells by endocytosis. Inside a membrane-bound inclusion it converts to the reticulate body, which divides by binary fission and then converts back to elementary bodies that are released to infect new cells. Serovars D to K cause genital and neonatal infection, A to C cause trachoma, and L1 to L3 cause lymphogranuloma venereum.","whyNot":["Serovars A to C cause trachoma, an eye infection spread by hands and flies, and the reticulate body is the replicating intracellular form.","Serovars D to K fit his genital infection, but the reticulate body lives inside cells and does not transmit infection.","The elementary body is the infectious form, but serovars A to C cause trachoma rather than urethritis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Chlamydial life cycle","Chlamydia trachomatis serovars"],"comps":["RE-12","RE-13-K3"]}},{"id":"RE-C26-Q4","stem":"Two weeks later, he develops pain and swelling at the posterior aspect of the left testis, and the epididymis is tender and enlarged. Infection has spread backward from the urethra. Which of the following best describes the most likely route?","options":["Spongy urethra, bulbourethral gland, ductus deferens, epididymis","Prostatic urethra, ejaculatory duct, ductus deferens, epididymis","Prostatic urethra, seminal vesicle, efferent ductules, epididymis","Membranous urethra, ejaculatory duct, rete testis, epididymis"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-2","obj":"RE-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Write out the forward path of sperm from the seminiferous tubules to the external meatus.","Reverse it from the urethra to the epididymis.","Check which option keeps every structure in the correct order and skips no step."],"why":"Sperm travel from the seminiferous tubules to the rete testis, efferent ductules, epididymis, ductus deferens, and ejaculatory duct, which opens into the prostatic urethra. The urethra then continues as the membranous and spongy urethra. Urethral organisms that ascend reach the epididymis by running this path backward: prostatic urethra, ejaculatory duct, ductus deferens, and the tail of the epididymis. In sexually active men under about 35, C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae are the main causes of epididymitis.","whyNot":["The bulbourethral glands drain into the spongy urethra but have no connection to the ductus deferens.","Correct.","The seminal vesicle joins the ductus deferens to form the ejaculatory duct, and the efferent ductules lie between the rete testis and the epididymis. This route skips the ductus deferens.","The ejaculatory duct opens into the prostatic urethra, not the membranous urethra, and the rete testis lies beyond the epididymis on the path."],"study":{"topics":["Path of sperm through the male tract","Epididymitis"],"comps":["RE-2","RE-13-K3"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C27","system":"RE","dx":"Gonococcal Bartholin gland abscess with mucopurulent cervicitis","codes":["RE-13-I1","RE-13-K4","RE-13-B2"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-day history of a painful swelling of the left labium that makes walking and sitting difficult. She also has had a yellow vaginal discharge and spotting after intercourse for 1 week. Her temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F). Examination shows a 4-cm tender, warm, fluctuant mass in the posterior left vulvar vestibule at the 4 o'clock position, with surrounding erythema. Speculum examination shows a friable cervix with yellow mucopurulent discharge from the endocervical canal. There is no cervical motion tenderness. Gram stain of fluid drained from the mass shows gram-negative diplococci within neutrophils. An endocervical nucleic acid amplification test is positive for Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Diagnosis: gonococcal Bartholin gland abscess with mucopurulent cervicitis.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C27-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains how the vulvar mass formed?","options":["Cystic dilation of a mesonephric duct remnant in the lateral vagina","Obstruction of a paraurethral gland duct beside the urethral meatus","Herniation of the bladder through the anterior vaginal wall","Obstruction of the greater vestibular gland duct with infection"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-13-I1","obj":"RE-13-I.1","dok":2,"approach":["Use the location: posterolateral vestibule at the 4 o'clock position.","Name the gland whose duct opens there: the greater vestibular (Bartholin) gland.","Explain the abscess: a blocked duct traps secretions, which then become infected."],"why":"The Bartholin (greater vestibular) glands lie deep to the posterior labia, and their ducts open into the posterolateral vestibule at about the 4 and 8 o'clock positions. When a duct is blocked, mucus builds up and forms a cyst; if it becomes infected, it forms a painful abscess. Most abscesses are polymicrobial, from skin and vaginal flora, and Neisseria gonorrhoeae causes some of them, as it did here. These glands are active in reproductive-age women, so a new Bartholin mass after menopause raises concern for carcinoma.","whyNot":["A mesonephric duct remnant forms a Gartner duct cyst in the lateral vaginal wall, inside the vagina rather than in the vestibule.","Paraurethral (Skene) glands open beside the urethral meatus in the anterior vestibule, not at the 4 o'clock position.","Bladder herniation is a cystocele, a soft anterior vaginal wall bulge that is not tender or fluctuant.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Bartholin cyst and abscess","Vulvar anatomy"],"comps":["RE-13-I1","RE-4"]}},{"id":"RE-C27-Q2","stem":"Two years later, she is hospitalized because of fever, migratory joint pain, tenosynovitis of the right wrist, and a few hemorrhagic pustules on her fingers. Blood cultures grow the same organism. This is her third episode of disseminated infection with this organism. Which of the following immune defects is most likely?","options":["Deficiency of C1 esterase inhibitor","Deficiency of NADPH oxidase in phagocytic cells","Deficiency of terminal complement components","Selective deficiency of serum IgA"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-K4","obj":"RE-13-K.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize disseminated gonococcal infection: arthritis, tenosynovitis, and pustular skin lesions.","Note that repeated systemic Neisseria infection points to a host defect.","Recall that Neisseria species are killed mainly by the membrane attack complex."],"why":"Disseminated gonococcal infection causes fever, migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis, and a few pustules on the extremities, and it can progress to septic arthritis. Neisseria species are cleared largely by complement-mediated lysis through the membrane attack complex, C5 to C9. People who lack a terminal complement component have repeated disseminated gonococcal and meningococcal infections. Other forms of gonorrhea include purulent urethritis with intracellular gram-negative diplococci, cervicitis, PID, and neonatal conjunctivitis.","whyNot":["C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency causes hereditary angioedema from excess bradykinin, without recurrent infection.","NADPH oxidase deficiency (chronic granulomatous disease) causes infections with catalase-positive organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus and Aspergillus.","Correct.","Selective IgA deficiency causes sinopulmonary and gastrointestinal infections, and many patients have no symptoms."],"study":{"topics":["Disseminated gonococcal infection","Terminal complement deficiency"],"comps":["RE-13-K4","IM-7"]}},{"id":"RE-C27-Q3","stem":"The organism is oxidase positive, ferments glucose but not maltose, and was isolated on Thayer-Martin agar. She has had gonorrhea before, yet her earlier infection did not protect her. Which of the following properties of the organism best explains this lack of lasting immunity?","options":["M protein that inhibits complement activation","Antigenic variation of its pilus proteins","Polysaccharide capsule that blocks phagocytosis","Protein A that binds the Fc portion of IgG"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-12","obj":"RE-12.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify Neisseria gonorrhoeae from the biochemical results and the selective medium.","Ask which virulence factor lets it escape antibodies made during a previous infection.","Pick the factor that keeps changing its surface antigens."],"why":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is an oxidase-positive gram-negative diplococcus that ferments glucose but not maltose and is grown on Thayer-Martin agar. Its pili attach to columnar epithelium, and the bacterium constantly rearranges its pilin genes so the pili change their antigens. Antibodies from one infection do not recognize the next strain, so reinfection is common and no vaccine exists. Its other virulence factors are IgA protease, Opa proteins, and lipooligosaccharide.","whyNot":["M protein is a virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes.","Correct.","Gonococci lack a polysaccharide capsule. Neisseria meningitidis has one, which is the target of meningococcal vaccines.","Protein A is a virulence factor of Staphylococcus aureus."],"study":{"topics":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae virulence factors"],"comps":["RE-12","RE-13-K4"]}},{"id":"RE-C27-Q4","stem":"Her roommate has postcoital spotting and a yellow mucopurulent endocervical discharge with a friable cervix. Her nucleic acid amplification test for Neisseria gonorrhoeae is negative. Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause of the roommate's cervicitis?","options":["Candida albicans","Trichomonas vaginalis","Herpes simplex virus type 2","Chlamydia trachomatis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-B2","obj":"RE-13-B.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize mucopurulent cervicitis: purulent discharge from the endocervical canal and a friable cervix.","Recall the two main causes, which both infect columnar endocervical cells.","With gonorrhea excluded, choose the other main cause, and contrast the cervical pictures of the distractors."],"why":"Mucopurulent cervicitis is caused mainly by Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae, which both infect the columnar cells of the endocervix. With a negative gonococcal test, chlamydia is the most likely cause, and it is the more common of the two. Because coinfection is frequent, women with cervicitis are tested for both. Trichomoniasis gives a strawberry cervix with punctate hemorrhages, and HSV gives an ulcerated cervix.","whyNot":["Candida albicans causes vulvovaginitis with a thick white discharge. It does not cause mucopurulent cervicitis.","Trichomonas vaginalis can inflame the cervix, but its classic picture is a strawberry cervix with punctate hemorrhages and a frothy, malodorous vaginal discharge. It is a much less common cause of mucopus from the endocervical canal.","Herpes simplex virus causes ulcerative, necrotic cervicitis, usually with painful vulvar vesicles and ulcers.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of cervicitis"],"comps":["RE-13-B2","RE-13-K3"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C28","system":"RE","dx":"Early congenital syphilis","codes":["RE-13-K8"],"case":"A 5-week-old boy is brought to the physician because of a 1-week history of persistent bloody nasal discharge and rash. He was born at term after no prenatal care. His mother recalls a painless genital sore during the second trimester that healed without treatment. His temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F). Examination shows profuse blood-tinged rhinorrhea, a copper-colored maculopapular rash with peeling on the palms and soles, hepatosplenomegaly, and generalized lymphadenopathy. Radiographs of the long bones show periostitis and metaphyseal lucencies. The infant's rapid plasma reagin (RPR) titer is 1:64, and the mother's is 1:16. Diagnosis: early congenital syphilis.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C28-Q1","stem":"If he is not treated, which of the following findings is most likely to appear later in childhood?","options":["Notched, peg-shaped upper central incisors","Cataracts with a patent ductus arteriosus","Hydrocephalus with diffuse intracranial calcifications","Microcephaly with periventricular calcifications"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-K8","obj":"RE-13-K.12","dok":2,"approach":["Separate early congenital syphilis (first 2 years) from late congenital syphilis (after 2 years).","Recall that late findings come from scarring and abnormal growth of teeth, bone, and cartilage.","Pick the late syphilitic finding and exclude the signature findings of other TORCH infections."],"why":"Early congenital syphilis appears in the first 2 years with snuffles, a rash that involves the palms and soles, hepatosplenomegaly, and bone lesions. Untreated children develop late findings from damage to developing teeth, bone, and cartilage. These include Hutchinson teeth (notched, peg-shaped upper central incisors), mulberry molars, saddle nose, saber shins, interstitial keratitis, and sensorineural deafness.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cataracts with a patent ductus arteriosus and deafness are the classic triad of congenital rubella.","Hydrocephalus with diffuse calcifications and chorioretinitis is the classic triad of congenital toxoplasmosis.","Microcephaly with periventricular calcifications is classic for congenital cytomegalovirus infection."],"study":{"topics":["Early vs late congenital syphilis","TORCH infections"],"comps":["RE-13-K8","RE-12"]}},{"id":"RE-C28-Q2","stem":"The mother is treated with penicillin. One year later, her RPR is nonreactive. Which of the following results is expected on her fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption (FTA-ABS) test, and why?","options":["Reactive, because anticardiolipin antibodies persist after treatment","Nonreactive, because the test detects only IgM antibodies","Reactive, because treponemal antibodies usually persist for life","Nonreactive, because treponemal antibodies clear after treatment"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-K8","obj":"RE-13-K.11","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two test types: nontreponemal (RPR, VDRL) and treponemal (FTA-ABS).","Recall what each measures: antibodies to cardiolipin versus antibodies to treponemal antigens.","Predict the effect of treatment: RPR titers fall, while treponemal tests usually stay positive."],"why":"Nontreponemal tests (RPR and VDRL) measure antibodies that react with cardiolipin. Their titers track disease activity and fall after treatment, often to nonreactive, so they are used to screen and to follow treatment. They can be falsely positive in pregnancy, SLE, and viral infections. Treponemal tests such as FTA-ABS detect antibodies to the organism itself and usually remain reactive for life, so they confirm infection but cannot show whether it was treated.","whyNot":["Anticardiolipin antibodies are what the RPR measures, and her RPR is now nonreactive. FTA-ABS detects treponemal antibodies.","FTA-ABS detects IgG as well as IgM. A reactive result in adults often reflects long-lasting IgG.","Correct.","Treponemal antibodies usually persist after successful treatment, so the FTA-ABS stays reactive."],"study":{"topics":["Syphilis serology"],"comps":["RE-13-K8"]}},{"id":"RE-C28-Q3","stem":"Fluid from a moist skin lesion on the infant's sole is examined directly for the causative organism. Which of the following methods is most likely to show it?","options":["Dark-field microscopy of the fluid","Culture on blood and chocolate agar","Culture on Thayer-Martin agar","Gram stain of the lesion fluid"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-12","obj":"RE-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: Treponema pallidum.","Recall two key facts: it is too thin to see with ordinary stains, and it cannot be grown on standard media.","Pick the method that shows live spirochetes directly."],"why":"Treponema pallidum is a thin, tightly coiled spirochete. It is too thin to be seen on a Gram stain and cannot be cultured on standard laboratory media. Dark-field microscopy of fluid from a moist lesion shows motile corkscrew organisms. It is transmitted sexually and across the placenta, which is how this infant was infected.","whyNot":["Correct.","Treponema pallidum does not grow on blood or chocolate agar or on any standard medium.","Thayer-Martin agar is used to culture Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Treponema pallidum does not grow on it.","T. pallidum is too thin to resolve with a Gram stain under ordinary light microscopy."],"study":{"topics":["Treponema pallidum"],"comps":["RE-12","RE-13-K8"]}},{"id":"RE-C28-Q4","stem":"The organism crossed from maternal blood in the intervillous space into the fetal circulation. Which of the following layers forms the outer surface of the chorionic villi in direct contact with that maternal blood?","options":["Cytotrophoblast layer","Syncytiotrophoblast","Decidua basalis","Amniotic epithelium"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-1","obj":"RE-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how the placenta forms: fetal chorion frondosum plus maternal decidua basalis.","Picture a mature villus from outside in: syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast, connective tissue, fetal capillaries.","Pick the layer bathed by maternal blood in the intervillous space."],"why":"The fetal part of the placenta is the chorion frondosum, made of villi, and the maternal part is the decidua basalis. Each villus is covered by syncytiotrophoblast, a multinucleated layer that sits in maternal blood in the intervillous space and makes hCG. Under it lies cytotrophoblast, then connective tissue and fetal capillaries that drain into the single umbilical vein. Organisms that cross the placenta must pass this barrier to reach the fetus.","whyNot":["Cytotrophoblast lies under the syncytiotrophoblast and thins out later in pregnancy. It is not the outer surface.","Correct.","The decidua basalis is the maternal part of the placenta. It forms the floor of the intervillous space.","The amnion lines the amniotic cavity on the fetal side and does not contact maternal blood."],"study":{"topics":["Placental structure"],"comps":["RE-1"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C29","system":"RE","dx":"Chancroid","codes":["RE-13-K1"],"case":"A 32-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-week history of painful genital sores and a 3-day history of painful swelling in his right groin. The sores began as tender papules 5 days after unprotected intercourse with a new partner. Examination shows two 1.5-cm deep ulcers on the coronal sulcus with ragged, undermined borders and soft edges. The bases are friable, bleed easily, and are covered by a gray-yellow exudate. The right inguinal lymph nodes are matted, tender, and fluctuant. Dark-field examination of ulcer fluid is negative, the RPR is nonreactive, and PCR of ulcer fluid is negative for herpes simplex virus. Gram stain of ulcer exudate shows gram-negative coccobacilli in parallel chains. Diagnosis: chancroid due to Haemophilus ducreyi.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C29-Q1","stem":"Another man seen the same day has a single genital ulcer. Which of the following additional findings would favor primary syphilis rather than chancroid in that patient?","options":["Ragged, undermined edges with a gray exudate","Tender inguinal nodes that suppurate and drain","Clustered painful ulcers that began as vesicles","Painless indurated ulcer with rubbery nodes"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-K1","obj":"RE-13-K.1","dok":3,"approach":["List the features that separate genital ulcers: pain, induration, base, and lymph nodes.","Recall chancroid: painful soft ulcer with ragged edges and painful suppurating nodes.","Recall primary syphilis: painless, firm, clean-based ulcer with painless firm nodes."],"why":"The primary chancre of syphilis is a painless ulcer with a firm, indurated border and a clean base, often with painless, rubbery inguinal nodes. Chancroid, caused by Haemophilus ducreyi, is the opposite: a painful, soft ulcer with ragged, undermined edges and a gray exudate, followed by painful inguinal nodes that can suppurate and drain. Herpes forms clusters of vesicles that break into shallow painful ulcers.","whyNot":["Ragged, undermined edges with a gray exudate describe chancroid.","Tender nodes that suppurate and drain describe chancroid buboes.","Clustered painful ulcers that start as vesicles describe genital herpes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Genital ulcer differential"],"comps":["RE-13-K1","RE-13-K8"]}},{"id":"RE-C29-Q2","stem":"A 28-year-old man who has sex with men had a small painless genital papule that healed within a week. Three weeks later, he has fever, painful fluctuant inguinal lymph nodes above and below the inguinal ligament, rectal pain, and bloody rectal discharge. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":["Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","Haemophilus ducreyi","Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1 to L3","Chlamydia trachomatis serovars D to K"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-K3","obj":"RE-13-K.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the sequence: a brief painless lesion, then painful buboes and proctocolitis.","Recognize lymphogranuloma venereum, an invasive lymphatic infection.","Match it to the invasive Chlamydia trachomatis serovars."],"why":"Lymphogranuloma venereum is caused by Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1 to L3, which invade lymphatic tissue rather than staying in the mucosa. A small painless papule or ulcer heals quickly and is often missed. Weeks later, painful inguinal nodes enlarge into buboes that can rupture, and nodes on both sides of the inguinal ligament make the groove sign. Receptive anal exposure can cause proctocolitis with rectal pain and bloody discharge.","whyNot":["Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus causes skin abscesses and can cause suppurative lymphadenitis, but it does not follow a painless genital papule or cause proctocolitis.","Haemophilus ducreyi causes chancroid, in which the genital ulcer is painful and persistent rather than small and quickly healed.","Correct.","Serovars D to K cause urethritis, cervicitis, and neonatal infection. They stay in the mucosa and do not cause buboes."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphogranuloma venereum"],"comps":["RE-13-K3","RE-12"]}},{"id":"RE-C29-Q3","stem":"Because coinfection is common, he is retested 3 months later. He now has a generalized nonpruritic rash, and his RPR is reactive at a titer of 1:64. The FTA-ABS is reactive. He does not recall any new sore. Which of the following additional findings is most likely?","options":["Loss of vibration sense and proprioception in the legs","Moist, flat, gray plaques in the perianal area","Dilation of the ascending aorta with aortic regurgitation","Granulomatous necrotic nodules of skin and bone"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-K8","obj":"RE-13-K.10","dok":3,"approach":["Use the timing and the high RPR titer with a diffuse rash to place him in secondary syphilis.","Recall the other lesions of the secondary stage.","Exclude findings that belong to tertiary syphilis, which takes years to develop."],"why":"Secondary syphilis begins weeks to a few months after the chancre, which is often painless and unnoticed. Spirochetes spread through the blood and cause a diffuse rash that often involves the palms and soles, generalized lymphadenopathy, and condylomata lata, moist, flat, gray plaques in warm, moist areas such as the perianal skin. Nontreponemal titers are highest in this stage. Untreated disease then enters latency, and years later tertiary syphilis can cause gummas, aortitis, and tabes dorsalis.","whyNot":["Loss of vibration and position sense is tabes dorsalis, a tertiary form of neurosyphilis.","Correct.","Ascending aortitis with aortic regurgitation is a tertiary finding caused by endarteritis of the vasa vasorum.","Gummas are granulomatous necrotic lesions of tertiary syphilis that appear years after infection."],"study":{"topics":["Stages of syphilis"],"comps":["RE-13-K8"]}},{"id":"RE-C29-Q4","stem":"His enlarged nodes are in the superficial inguinal group, which drains the skin of the penis. A malignancy at which of the following sites would spread first to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes instead of to this group?","options":["Perianal skin below the pectinate line","Skin of the penile shaft","Testis","Skin of the scrotum"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-5","obj":"RE-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall which structures drain to the superficial inguinal nodes: skin of the perineum, scrotum, penis, and lower anal canal.","Recall that the gonads develop in the abdomen and keep their lymphatic drainage there.","Pick the gonad, whose lymph follows the testicular vessels to the para-aortic nodes."],"why":"Lymph follows the blood supply. The testis develops near the kidneys and descends, bringing its testicular artery, veins, and lymphatics from the abdomen, so testicular tumors spread first to the para-aortic nodes. The scrotal skin, penile skin, and perianal skin below the pectinate line drain to the superficial inguinal nodes. The same rule applies in women: the ovary drains to para-aortic nodes, and the vulva and distal vagina drain to superficial inguinal nodes.","whyNot":["Perianal skin below the pectinate line drains to the superficial inguinal nodes.","Penile shaft skin drains to the superficial inguinal nodes.","Correct.","Scrotal skin drains to the superficial inguinal nodes, so scrotal cancer spreads to the groin."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic drainage of the pelvis and perineum"],"comps":["RE-5","RE-13-J9"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C30","system":"RE","dx":"Primary genital herpes (HSV-2) late in pregnancy","codes":["RE-13-K5"],"case":"A 23-year-old primigravid woman at 37 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of a 5-day history of painful genital sores, burning with urination, headache, and malaise. Her temperature is 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Examination shows multiple clusters of vesicles and shallow, painful ulcers on erythematous bases on the labia majora and minora, and bilateral tender inguinal lymphadenopathy. PCR of fluid from a vesicle is positive for herpes simplex virus type 2. Serum IgG antibodies to herpes simplex virus types 1 and 2 are not detected. Diagnosis: primary genital herpes simplex virus type 2 infection late in pregnancy.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C30-Q1","stem":"Scrapings from the base of a fresh vesicle are stained and examined. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions","Keratinocytes with large eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions","Histiocytes containing intracytoplasmic bacteria","Squamous cells with perinuclear halos and wrinkled nuclei"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-K5","obj":"RE-13-K.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize genital herpes: painful grouped vesicles that ulcerate, with fever and malaise in a primary infection.","Recall how herpesviruses change infected epithelial cells.","Pick the cytologic finding of a Tzanck smear."],"why":"Herpes simplex virus causes infected keratinocytes to fuse into multinucleated giant cells, and the nuclei show ground-glass chromatin and intranuclear inclusions. A Tzanck smear of a vesicle base shows these cells, though PCR is now the preferred test. Primary infection causes many grouped vesicles that ulcerate, with fever, malaise, and tender nodes. The virus then becomes latent in sacral ganglia and can reactivate to cause milder recurrences. A newborn is usually infected during delivery.","whyNot":["Correct.","Large eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions in keratinocytes are molluscum bodies of molluscum contagiosum.","Bacteria inside histiocytes (Donovan bodies) are the sign of granuloma inguinale from Klebsiella granulomatis.","Squamous cells with perinuclear halos and wrinkled nuclei are koilocytes, the sign of human papillomavirus infection."],"study":{"topics":["Genital herpes","Tzanck smear"],"comps":["RE-13-K5","RE-12"]}},{"id":"RE-C30-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the virus causing her infection?","options":["Enveloped ssRNA retrovirus integrated in CD4 T cells","Enveloped dsDNA virus latent in sacral sensory ganglia","Nonenveloped dsDNA virus persisting in basal keratinocytes","Enveloped dsDNA virus latent in trigeminal ganglia"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-12","obj":"RE-12.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the virus from the PCR result: HSV-2.","Recall its structure: an enveloped double-stranded DNA herpesvirus.","Recall where genital HSV hides between outbreaks and contrast it with HPV."],"why":"Herpes simplex virus type 2 is an enveloped, double-stranded DNA herpesvirus. After the primary genital infection, it travels up sensory nerves and stays latent in the sacral dorsal root ganglia, then reactivates and travels back down to cause recurrent lesions. Human papillomavirus is a nonenveloped double-stranded DNA virus that persists in basal keratinocytes. Low-risk types 6 and 11 cause genital warts, and high-risk types 16 and 18 cause cervical and anogenital cancers.","whyNot":["An enveloped ssRNA retrovirus that integrates in CD4 T cells describes HIV.","Correct.","A nonenveloped dsDNA virus in basal keratinocytes describes human papillomavirus.","Latency in the trigeminal ganglia is typical of HSV-1 after oral infection, not of genital HSV-2."],"study":{"topics":["HSV vs HPV virology"],"comps":["RE-12","RE-13-K6"]}},{"id":"RE-C30-Q3","stem":"Three years later, she delivers her second child vaginally, with no herpetic lesions present, and receives a transvaginal pudendal nerve block for perineal anesthesia. Toward which of the following palpable landmarks is the needle directed?","options":["Ischial spine","Pubic tubercle","Sacral promontory","Ischial tuberosity"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-5","obj":"RE-5.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the origin of the pudendal nerve: ventral rami of S2 to S4.","Trace its path: out the greater sciatic foramen, around the ischial spine and sacrospinous ligament, then into the lesser sciatic foramen.","Pick the point where it is closest to a bony landmark felt through the vagina."],"why":"The pudendal nerve (S2 to S4) leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, curves around the ischial spine and sacrospinous ligament, and enters the perineum through the lesser sciatic foramen in the pudendal canal. The ischial spine can be felt through the lateral vaginal wall, so anesthetic is injected just medial and posterior to it. The block numbs most of the perineum, the lower vagina, and the posterior vulva.","whyNot":["Correct.","The pubic tubercle is the attachment of the inguinal ligament and is used as a landmark for inguinal hernias.","The sacral promontory is the upper edge of S1 at the pelvic inlet and is far from the pudendal nerve.","The ischial tuberosity lies below the point where the nerve curves around the spine. It is the landmark for the transperineal approach, not for a transvaginal block."],"study":{"topics":["Course of the pudendal nerve"],"comps":["RE-5","RE-4"]}},{"id":"RE-C30-Q4","stem":"Her obstetrician explains that an infant exposed at delivery is at much higher risk of neonatal herpes during this primary infection than during a recurrence in a woman infected long before pregnancy. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["Lack of maternal HSV-specific IgM for transfer across the placenta","Lack of secretory IgA in colostrum at the time of delivery","Lack of maternal cytotoxic T cells crossing into the fetus","Lack of maternal HSV-specific IgG for FcRn-mediated transfer"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-11","obj":"RE-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note her serology: no HSV IgG, so she has not yet made a mature antibody response.","Recall that the only antibody class that crosses the placenta is IgG, carried by the neonatal Fc receptor.","Link the absence of transferred IgG to an unprotected newborn exposed to large amounts of virus."],"why":"Newborns depend on maternal IgG, which the syncytiotrophoblast moves across the placenta using the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), mostly in the third trimester. A woman with long-standing HSV infection has HSV-specific IgG that protects her baby, and she sheds less virus during a recurrence. In a primary infection near term, she has not yet made IgG that can be transferred, and she sheds large amounts of virus, so the newborn is far more likely to be infected. IgM is too large to cross the placenta, and secretory IgA in breast milk protects mainly the infant's gut.","whyNot":["IgM is a pentamer that cannot cross the placenta, so it never provides passive protection to the fetus.","Secretory IgA in colostrum protects the infant's gut mucosa after birth. It does not prevent infection acquired in the birth canal.","Maternal T cells do not cross the placenta to protect the fetus. Passive protection of the newborn is antibody based.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Passive immunity of the newborn","Neonatal herpes"],"comps":["RE-11","RE-13-K5"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C31","system":"RE","dx":"Trichomoniasis with coexisting bacterial vaginosis","codes":["RE-13-K10","RE-13-K2"],"case":"A 27-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 1-week history of malodorous vaginal discharge, vulvar itching, and pain with intercourse. She has had two new sexual partners in the past 3 months and does not use condoms. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F). Speculum examination shows a frothy, yellow-green discharge and punctate hemorrhages on the ectocervix. Vaginal pH is 5.8 (3.8 to 4.5). Adding potassium hydroxide to the discharge releases a fishy odor. Saline wet mount shows motile, pear-shaped, flagellated organisms, many neutrophils, and squamous epithelial cells whose borders are obscured by adherent coccobacilli. Gram stain shows few lactobacilli. The diagnosis is trichomoniasis with coexisting bacterial vaginosis.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C31-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the life cycle and transmission of the motile organism seen on this patient's wet mount?","options":["Cyst form that survives in contaminated water","Trophozoite form only, passed by sexual contact","Yeast form that overgrows after antibiotic use","Elementary body form that enters epithelial cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-K10","obj":"RE-13-K.14","dok":2,"approach":["Motile, pear-shaped, flagellated organisms on a saline wet mount identify Trichomonas vaginalis.","Recall its life cycle: it exists only as a trophozoite and has no cyst stage.","Without a hardy cyst it cannot survive long outside the body, so it spreads by sexual contact."],"why":"Trichomonas vaginalis is an anaerobic flagellated protozoan that exists only as a trophozoite. It has no cyst stage, so it does not survive long in the environment and spreads mainly by sexual contact. The trophozoite attaches to squamous epithelium of the vagina, ectocervix, and male urethra and causes inflammation with many neutrophils, a frothy yellow-green discharge, a pH above 4.5, and punctate cervical hemorrhages (strawberry cervix). Men often carry it without symptoms, so sexual partners are treated as well.","whyNot":["A hardy cyst passed in water or food is the infectious form of Giardia lamblia and Entamoeba histolytica. T. vaginalis has no cyst stage.","Correct.","Candida albicans overgrows after antibiotics or with diabetes and causes a thick white discharge with a normal pH. It is not flagellated and does not swim.","The elementary body is the infectious form of Chlamydia trachomatis, an obligate intracellular bacterium. It is far too small to be seen moving on a wet mount."],"study":{"topics":["Trichomonas vaginalis life cycle","Causes of vaginitis on wet mount"],"comps":["RE-13-K10","RE-12"]}},{"id":"RE-C31-Q2","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient is most specifically attributable to the bacterial vaginosis rather than to the trichomonal infection?","options":["Numerous neutrophils on wet mount","Punctate hemorrhages on the ectocervix","Vaginal pH greater than 4.5","Clue cells on saline wet mount"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-K2","obj":"RE-13-K.2","dok":3,"approach":["List what each condition can cause.","A pH above 4.5 and an amine odor occur in both, while neutrophils and cervical hemorrhages point to trichomoniasis.","Clue cells form when Gardnerella and anaerobes coat squamous cells, so they are specific for bacterial vaginosis."],"why":"Bacterial vaginosis is a shift in the vaginal flora. Lactobacilli are replaced by Gardnerella vaginalis and anaerobes, which stick to squamous cells so their borders look stippled and blurred. These are clue cells. Bacterial vaginosis causes little inflammation, so neutrophils are few. Trichomoniasis also raises the pH above 4.5 and can give an amine odor, but it does not produce clue cells, so clue cells are the finding that shows bacterial vaginosis is present as well.","whyNot":["Many neutrophils point to trichomoniasis, which is an inflammatory infection. Bacterial vaginosis typically has few neutrophils, which is why it is called a vaginosis.","Punctate cervical hemorrhages (strawberry cervix) come from the inflammation caused by T. vaginalis. Bacterial vaginosis does not damage the cervix in this way.","Both conditions raise vaginal pH above 4.5, so pH cannot separate them. Candidiasis is the common vaginitis with a normal pH.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Bacterial vaginosis findings","Comparing the three common vaginitides"],"comps":["RE-13-K2","RE-13-K10"]}},{"id":"RE-C31-Q3","stem":"The Gram stain in this patient shows few lactobacilli. Loss of which of the following lactobacillus functions best explains the overgrowth of Gardnerella vaginalis and anaerobes?","options":["Production of lactic acid that keeps pH at 4.5 or below","Secretion of IgA that coats bacteria in the vaginal lumen","Release of volatile amines that kill anaerobic bacteria","Storage of glycogen within superficial squamous cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-11","obj":"RE-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Few lactobacilli means the normal protective flora has been lost.","Lactobacilli ferment epithelial glycogen products to lactic acid, which keeps the pH at 4.5 or below.","Without that acid the pH rises, and Gardnerella and anaerobes overgrow."],"why":"Estrogen makes vaginal squamous cells store glycogen. Lactobacilli ferment the breakdown products of this glycogen into lactic acid, which keeps the vaginal pH at about 3.8 to 4.5. This acid environment holds back Gardnerella and anaerobes. When lactobacilli are lost, for example with new sexual partners or douching, the pH rises and these organisms overgrow. They then make amines that cause the fishy odor and push the pH higher still.","whyNot":["Correct.","Secretory IgA is made by plasma cells and carried across the epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor. It is a host defense, and lactobacilli do not make it.","Volatile amines such as trimethylamine are made by the anaerobes of bacterial vaginosis. They cause the fishy odor and raise the pH further.","Glycogen is stored by the squamous cells under the influence of estrogen. It is the substrate that lactobacilli use, and the bacteria themselves do not store it."],"study":{"topics":["Normal vaginal flora and pH","Pathogenesis of bacterial vaginosis"],"comps":["RE-11","RE-13-K2"]}},{"id":"RE-C31-Q4","stem":"Trichomonads attach to the epithelium that lines this patient's vagina and ectocervix, and clue cells are shed from the same lining. Which of the following describes this epithelium?","options":["Stratified squamous, nonkeratinized","Stratified squamous, keratinized","Simple columnar, mucus secreting","Simple columnar, ciliated with peg cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-2","obj":"RE-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Clue cells are shed vaginal epithelial cells, and trichomonads attach to this same lining.","The vagina and ectocervix must resist friction, so they have a stratified squamous lining.","They are moist internal surfaces, so the epithelium is nonkeratinized."],"why":"The vagina and ectocervix are lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium whose cells store glycogen under estrogen. This is the surface that T. vaginalis attaches to and the source of clue cells in bacterial vaginosis. The endocervical canal is lined by mucus-secreting simple columnar epithelium. The two meet at the transformation zone, where columnar cells are replaced by squamous metaplasia and where cervical dysplasia begins.","whyNot":["Correct.","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium covers the skin, including the labia majora. The vaginal lining stays moist and does not keratinize under normal conditions.","Mucus-secreting simple columnar epithelium lines the endocervical canal. It meets the squamous epithelium of the ectocervix at the transformation zone.","Ciliated simple columnar epithelium with secretory peg cells lines the uterine tube, where the cilia help move the oocyte toward the uterus."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelium of the female tract","Cervical transformation zone"],"comps":["RE-2","RE-13-K10"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C32","system":"RE","dx":"Menstrual toxic shock syndrome","codes":["RE-13-K9"],"case":"A 19-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of a 1-day history of fever, vomiting, watery diarrhea, muscle aches, and lightheadedness on standing. She is on day 3 of her menstrual period and has used high-absorbency tampons; the most recent was placed about 20 hours ago. Temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), pulse is 128/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 78/44 mmHg. She has a diffuse, blanching, erythematous macular rash and bilateral conjunctival hyperemia. A retained tampon is removed during pelvic examination. Laboratory studies show a platelet count of 88,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000), serum creatinine of 2.1 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1), and creatine kinase of 1,850 U/L (30 to 170). Blood cultures show no growth. Vaginal culture grows Staphylococcus aureus. The diagnosis is menstrual toxic shock syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C32-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains this patient's hypotension and multiorgan injury?","options":["Immune complex deposition in small blood vessels","Lipopolysaccharide binding to TLR4 on macrophages","Bloodstream spread of bacteria into many organs","Cytokine surge from mass T cell activation"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-K9","obj":"RE-13-K.13","dok":3,"approach":["Note fever, shock, a diffuse rash, and injury to several organs, with negative blood cultures and a retained tampon.","This pattern fits a toxin absorbed from a local S. aureus infection.","TSST-1 is a superantigen, so it activates huge numbers of T cells and causes a cytokine storm."],"why":"TSST-1 is a superantigen made by S. aureus growing on the retained tampon. It activates a large fraction of T cells at once, and they release large amounts of IL-2, interferon-gamma, and TNF-alpha, which push macrophages to release IL-1 and more TNF-alpha. This cytokine storm causes fever, vasodilation, capillary leak, and hypotension, with injury to the kidneys and muscle and a fall in the platelet count. Blood cultures are usually negative because the toxin is absorbed from the vagina and bacteremia is not needed. A diffuse rash appears early, and the palms and soles peel 1 to 2 weeks later.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition (type III hypersensitivity) causes vasculitis and glomerulonephritis that develop over days to weeks, as in serum sickness. It does not cause shock within a day.","Lipopolysaccharide binding to TLR4 drives septic shock from gram-negative bacteria. S. aureus is gram-positive and has no lipopolysaccharide.","Her blood cultures are negative. Menstrual toxic shock syndrome comes from toxin absorbed from a local vaginal infection, without spread of bacteria through the blood.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Toxic shock syndrome pathogenesis","Superantigen cytokine storm"],"comps":["RE-13-K9","RE-12"]}},{"id":"RE-C32-Q2","stem":"Which of the following binding interactions of the toxin explains why it activates such a large fraction of this patient's T cells?","options":["CD28 on T cells, in place of the B7 costimulatory molecule","MHC class II and TCR V-beta, outside the peptide groove","Peptide bound in the MHC class I groove presented to CD8 T cells","Peptide bound in the MHC class II groove after endocytosis"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-12","obj":"RE-12.6","dok":2,"approach":["Activation of a huge share of T cells at once means the toxin bypasses antigen specificity.","Specificity comes from the peptide in the MHC groove, so the toxin must bind outside it.","TSST-1 links MHC class II to the V-beta region of the T cell receptor."],"why":"A conventional antigen is processed into peptides that sit in the MHC groove, and only the rare T cells with a matching receptor respond, far fewer than 1 in 1,000. TSST-1 acts as an intact protein and is never processed into peptides. It binds the outside of MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells and the V-beta region of the T cell receptor, away from the peptide groove. Every T cell that uses a matching V-beta family is activated, which can be up to about 20% of all T cells, so cytokine release is massive.","whyNot":["CD28 binding to B7 provides the second signal for T cell activation. A costimulatory signal on its own does not activate a T cell and does not explain why so many clones respond. The huge response comes from binding the V-beta region of the T cell receptor.","Correct.","MHC class I presents processed peptides from inside the cell to CD8 T cells. This is antigen specific and activates only a few clones.","This is normal presentation of an extracellular protein to CD4 T cells. Only T cells specific for that peptide respond, so the number activated is small."],"study":{"topics":["Superantigens versus conventional antigens","MHC class II antigen presentation"],"comps":["RE-12","RE-13-K9"]}},{"id":"RE-C32-Q3","stem":"Which of the following hormonal events most directly started the menstrual bleeding during which this patient used the tampons?","options":["Rise in FSH that recruits the next cohort of follicles","Rise in estradiol secreted by the dominant follicle","Fall in progesterone as the corpus luteum regresses","Surge in LH caused by positive feedback from estradiol"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-6","obj":"RE-6.1","dok":3,"approach":["Menses is shedding of the functional layer of the endometrium.","The secretory endometrium depends on progesterone from the corpus luteum.","Without hCG the corpus luteum regresses, progesterone falls, and the lining is shed."],"why":"After ovulation, the corpus luteum secretes progesterone and estrogen, which convert the proliferative endometrium into secretory endometrium. Without pregnancy and hCG, the corpus luteum regresses after about 14 days. The fall in progesterone causes spiral artery constriction, ischemia, and shedding of the functional layer, which is menses. The luteal phase stays near 14 days because the corpus luteum has a fixed lifespan, so differences in cycle length come from the follicular phase.","whyNot":["FSH rises late in the luteal phase as estrogen and progesterone fall, recruiting follicles for the next cycle. It accompanies menses but does not cause the endometrium to shed.","Rising estradiol in the follicular phase builds the proliferative endometrium. It thickens the lining and does not shed it.","Correct.","The LH surge occurs at mid cycle and triggers ovulation about a day later. It starts the luteal phase and does not cause menses."],"study":{"topics":["Ovarian and endometrial cycles","Corpus luteum lifespan"],"comps":["RE-6","RE-7"]}},{"id":"RE-C32-Q4","stem":"Which of the following changes in the vaginal environment during menstruation most favored growth of S. aureus and toxin production in this patient?","options":["Greater secretion of IgA into cervical mucus","Rise in vaginal pH from menstrual blood","Rise in epithelial glycogen from high estrogen","Increase in lactic acid made by lactobacilli"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-11","obj":"RE-11.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the normal vaginal defense: lactobacilli make lactic acid that keeps the pH at 4.5 or below.","Menstrual blood has a pH near 7.4 and buffers this acid.","A near-neutral pH, with oxygen brought in by the tampon, lets S. aureus grow and make TSST-1."],"why":"Lactobacilli keep vaginal pH at about 3.8 to 4.5 with lactic acid, and this acid limits growth of S. aureus and many other organisms. Menstrual blood has a pH near 7.4 and buffers that acid, so the vaginal pH rises toward neutral during menses. A high-absorbency tampon left in place also brings in oxygen and holds blood as a growth medium. A near-neutral pH and oxygen favor S. aureus growth and TSST-1 production.","whyNot":["Secretory IgA is a mucosal defense that blocks microbial attachment. An increase would help protect her from infection.","Correct.","Epithelial glycogen rises with estrogen in the follicular and ovulatory phases. Estrogen is low during menses, and more glycogen would feed lactobacilli and keep the pH low.","More lactic acid would lower the pH and protect against S. aureus. Menstrual blood has the opposite effect."],"study":{"topics":["Vaginal defenses and pH","Risk factors for menstrual toxic shock"],"comps":["RE-11","RE-13-K9"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C33","system":"RE","dx":"Lactational mastitis with a galactocele","codes":["RE-13-E4","RE-13-E2"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician 3 weeks after delivering her first child because of a 2-day history of pain and redness in the right breast, fever, and chills. She is breastfeeding and has had a painful crack in the right nipple for 1 week. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mmHg. The upper outer quadrant of the right breast has a warm, tender, wedge-shaped area of erythema without fluctuance. A smooth, mobile, nontender, 2-cm cystic mass is present beneath the left areola, and aspiration yields thick, milky fluid. Leukocyte count is 14,200/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000). Culture of milk from the right breast is pending. The diagnosis is acute lactational mastitis of the right breast with a galactocele of the left breast.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C33-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the organism most likely to grow from the milk culture and its route into the breast?","options":["Catalase-negative cocci in chains carried in blood from the uterus","Lactose-fermenting gram-negative rods spread from the infant's gut","Anaerobic organisms growing behind keratin-plugged lactiferous ducts","Coagulase-positive cocci in clusters entering a nipple fissure"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-E4","obj":"RE-13-E.4","dok":2,"approach":["A breastfeeding woman with a cracked nipple, fever, and a red, warm, wedge-shaped area has acute mastitis.","Organisms from the skin and the infant's mouth enter through the fissure.","The usual cause is S. aureus: gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase and coagulase positive."],"why":"Acute mastitis in the first weeks of breastfeeding is usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus, a gram-positive coccus that grows in clusters and is catalase and coagulase positive. Organisms from the skin or the infant's mouth enter through cracks and fissures in the nipple. Milk stasis in the affected lobes helps them grow. The breast becomes red, warm, and painful with fever, and untreated infection can form an abscess that feels fluctuant.","whyNot":["Streptococci can cause postpartum uterine infection, but lactational mastitis comes from skin and mouth organisms entering through the nipple. Spread through the blood from the uterus is not the usual route.","Gram-negative rods such as E. coli are uncommon causes of lactational mastitis. They are the usual cause of urinary tract infections.","Keratin-plugged ducts with squamous metaplasia describe periductal mastitis in smokers, which often involves mixed flora. It is a subareolar process unrelated to breastfeeding.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Acute lactational mastitis","Staphylococcus aureus identification"],"comps":["RE-13-E4","RE-12"]}},{"id":"RE-C33-Q2","stem":"If the cystic mass beneath this patient's left areola ruptured, which of the following would most likely result?","options":["Squamous metaplasia with keratin plugging of the duct","Malignant change in the epithelium lining the cyst","Local inflammation from milk leaking into the stroma","Hemorrhagic infarction of the surrounding lobules"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E2","obj":"RE-13-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["A milky aspirate from a smooth subareolar cyst in a lactating woman identifies a galactocele.","Its contents are milk, which irritates tissue once it leaves the duct.","Rupture therefore causes a local inflammatory reaction."],"why":"A galactocele is a cystic dilation of a duct filled with milk. It forms when a duct is blocked during lactation or weaning. Milk is irritating once it leaves the duct. If the cyst ruptures, milk lipids and proteins enter the stroma and set off a local inflammatory reaction, which can leave a firm area or scar. A galactocele is benign and does not become malignant.","whyNot":["Squamous metaplasia with keratin plugs is the cause of periductal mastitis, which is linked to smoking.","A galactocele is a benign retention cyst with no precancerous potential.","Correct.","Rupture of a milk cyst does not block the blood supply of the lobules. Infarction of breast tissue is rare and is not a result of a galactocele."],"study":{"topics":["Galactocele","Benign breast cysts"],"comps":["RE-13-E2","RE-13-E4"]}},{"id":"RE-C33-Q3","stem":"Which of the following is required for the alveolar cells of this patient's breasts to synthesize the main carbohydrate in her milk?","options":["Alpha-lactalbumin bound to galactosyltransferase","Aldose reductase converting glucose into galactose","Sucrase-isomaltase linking glucose to galactose","Lactase on the apical membrane acting in reverse"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-9","obj":"RE-9.3","dok":2,"approach":["The main carbohydrate in milk is lactose, made from UDP-galactose and glucose.","Galactosyltransferase on its own prefers proteins as acceptors.","Alpha-lactalbumin binds it to form lactose synthase, which uses glucose."],"why":"Lactose is made in the Golgi of mammary alveolar cells from UDP-galactose and glucose. The enzyme galactosyltransferase normally adds galactose to glycoproteins. Alpha-lactalbumin, a whey protein whose synthesis rises under prolactin, binds the enzyme and greatly lowers its Km for glucose, so the pair makes lactose. Together they are called lactose synthase. Human milk is high in lactose, whey proteins, and secretory IgA but low in vitamin D and vitamin K, which is why newborns receive vitamin K at birth and breastfed infants receive vitamin D.","whyNot":["Correct.","Aldose reductase reduces glucose to sorbitol and galactose to galactitol. The conversion between glucose and galactose uses UDP-glucose 4-epimerase.","Sucrase-isomaltase is an intestinal brush border enzyme that digests sucrose and alpha-limit dextrins. It has no role in milk synthesis.","Lactase on the small intestinal brush border splits lactose into glucose and galactose. It does not make lactose in the breast."],"study":{"topics":["Lactose synthase","Composition of human milk"],"comps":["RE-9","RE-6"]}},{"id":"RE-C33-Q4","stem":"This patient notices milk dripping from her left breast when she hears her infant cry, before the infant begins to nurse. Which of the following best explains this response?","options":["Prolactin release causing myoepithelial cell contraction","Oxytocin release causing myoepithelial cell contraction","Dopamine release relaxing smooth muscle of the sinuses","Prolactin release increasing alveolar milk synthesis"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-6","obj":"RE-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["Milk leaving the breast within seconds is ejection of milk that is already stored.","Ejection needs contraction of the myoepithelial cells around the alveoli.","Oxytocin from the posterior pituitary does this, and the reflex can become conditioned to the infant's cry."],"why":"Milk ejection (let-down) is a neuroendocrine reflex. Suckling, and after conditioning the sight or sound of the infant, activates hypothalamic neurons in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei, which release oxytocin from the posterior pituitary. Oxytocin contracts the myoepithelial cells around the alveoli and pushes stored milk into the ducts within about a minute. Prolactin from the anterior pituitary rises with suckling and drives milk synthesis for later feedings. High prolactin during regular suckling also suppresses GnRH, which delays the return of ovulation.","whyNot":["Prolactin acts on alveolar epithelial cells to make milk. It has no contractile effect on myoepithelial cells.","Correct.","Hypothalamic dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin release from the anterior pituitary. It does not act on the breast to release milk.","Prolactin-driven synthesis refills the alveoli over hours. It does not explain milk dripping within seconds of a cry, before any suckling."],"study":{"topics":["Milk ejection reflex","Prolactin and oxytocin in lactation"],"comps":["RE-6","RE-7"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C34","system":"RE","dx":"Traumatic fat necrosis of the breast","codes":["RE-13-E5","RE-13-E3"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a firm lump in her left breast that she noticed 2 weeks ago. Eight weeks ago she was the restrained driver in a motor vehicle collision, and the seat belt left a large bruise across the left breast. She has no nipple discharge. Examination shows a 2-cm firm, irregular, nontender mass in the upper inner quadrant of the left breast with dimpling of the overlying skin. There is no axillary lymphadenopathy. Mammography shows an irregular density with calcifications. Core needle biopsy shows necrotic adipocytes surrounded by lipid-laden foamy macrophages, multinucleated giant cells, and calcium deposits, with early fibrosis and no atypical epithelial cells. The diagnosis is traumatic fat necrosis of the breast.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C34-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the calcifications seen on this patient's mammogram?","options":["Metastatic calcification from high serum calcium","Calcium oxalate crystals in apocrine cyst fluid","Calcification of necrotic tumor debris in ducts","Dystrophic calcification of necrotic fat cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E5","obj":"RE-13-E.6","dok":2,"approach":["The biopsy shows dead fat cells with calcium deposits in a woman with a recent breast injury.","Calcium that settles in dead tissue while serum calcium is normal is dystrophic calcification.","In fat necrosis, fatty acids from the dead fat cells bind calcium."],"why":"Trauma ruptures fat cells, and the released lipid and dead cells draw in foamy macrophages and multinucleated giant cells. Fatty acids from the dead fat cells combine with calcium, which is dystrophic calcification: calcium deposits in dead tissue while the serum calcium is normal. Fibrosis follows. In the breast this is called nonenzymatic fat necrosis because it is started by trauma, unlike the enzymatic fat necrosis of acute pancreatitis. The firm mass, skin dimpling, and calcifications can look like carcinoma on exam and mammography, so a biopsy is needed.","whyNot":["Metastatic calcification occurs in normal tissues such as the kidney, lung, and gastric mucosa when serum calcium or phosphate is high. Nothing here suggests hypercalcemia, and the calcium sits in dead fat.","Calcium oxalate crystals can form in benign apocrine cysts. The biopsy here shows necrotic fat and no cysts.","Calcified necrotic debris inside ducts is typical of comedo-type ductal carcinoma in situ. The biopsy shows no atypical epithelial cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Traumatic fat necrosis of the breast","Dystrophic versus metastatic calcification"],"comps":["RE-13-E5","RE-13-E6"]}},{"id":"RE-C34-Q2","stem":"Mammary duct ectasia was in the differential diagnosis. In a 67-year-old multiparous woman with that condition, biopsy shows dilated subareolar ducts filled with thick secretions and a periductal infiltrate rich in plasma cells. Which of the following best explains the nipple retraction that often develops in that condition?","options":["Fibrosis that follows chronic periductal inflammation","Dermal edema from blocked lymphatic vessels","Carcinoma cells infiltrating the suspensory ligaments","Contraction of hypertrophied areolar smooth muscle"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E3","obj":"RE-13-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Dilated ducts with thick secretions and plasma cell-rich inflammation describe mammary duct ectasia.","Chronic inflammation heals by fibrosis.","Contracting periductal scar tissue pulls the nipple inward and can mimic carcinoma."],"why":"In duct ectasia, thick, lipid-rich secretions collect in dilated subareolar ducts and leak into the surrounding tissue. This causes chronic inflammation with many plasma cells, lymphocytes, and macrophages. The inflammation heals by fibrosis, which shortens the tissue around the ducts and pulls the nipple inward. Nipple retraction, a subareolar mass, and thick discharge can mimic carcinoma, so tissue diagnosis is needed. The condition is most often seen in older multiparous women.","whyNot":["Correct.","Blocked dermal lymphatics cause skin edema with peau d'orange, as in inflammatory carcinoma. Edema swells the skin and does not pull the nipple inward.","Carcinoma involving the suspensory ligaments causes skin dimpling and can retract the nipple, but this biopsy shows inflammation and no tumor.","Areolar smooth muscle contracts to make the nipple erect. It does not hypertrophy in duct ectasia, and its contraction does not invert the nipple."],"study":{"topics":["Mammary duct ectasia","Benign mimics of breast carcinoma"],"comps":["RE-13-E3","RE-13-E6"]}},{"id":"RE-C34-Q3","stem":"Periductal mastitis was also considered. A 36-year-old woman who smokes one pack of cigarettes daily and is not pregnant or breastfeeding has a painful, red subareolar mass that drains pus at the edge of the areola. Which of the following changes in her lactiferous ducts most likely began this process?","options":["Infection entering through nipple fissures","Dilation with thick lipid-rich secretions","Papillary growth on fibrovascular cores","Squamous metaplasia with keratin plugging"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E4","obj":"RE-13-E.5","dok":2,"approach":["A smoker with a painful subareolar mass and a draining tract at the areolar edge has periductal mastitis.","Smoking is linked to squamous metaplasia of the lactiferous duct lining.","Keratin plugs the duct, and rupture releases keratin that provokes inflammation and abscess."],"why":"Periductal mastitis is strongly linked to smoking. The lining of the lactiferous ducts undergoes squamous metaplasia, and the squamous cells shed keratin that plugs and dilates the duct. When the duct ruptures, keratin leaks into the stroma and causes intense inflammation, which forms a painful subareolar mass or abscess. The abscess can drain through a fistula that opens at the edge of the areola.","whyNot":["Infection through nipple fissures causes acute lactational mastitis in breastfeeding women. This woman is not breastfeeding, and her risk factor is smoking.","Dilated ducts with thick secretions and plasma cell-rich inflammation describe mammary duct ectasia, which is seen mainly in older multiparous women.","Papillary fronds on fibrovascular cores inside a duct form an intraductal papilloma, which causes bloody nipple discharge without signs of infection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Periductal mastitis","Squamous metaplasia"],"comps":["RE-13-E4","RE-13-E3"]}},{"id":"RE-C34-Q4","stem":"The dimpling of the skin over this patient's mass is most likely caused by fibrous scarring that shortens which of the following structures?","options":["Pectoral fascia under the retromammary space","Lactiferous ducts converging on the nipple","Suspensory ligaments of the breast","Subdermal lymphatic plexus of the breast"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-4","obj":"RE-4.5","dok":2,"approach":["Skin dimpling means something is pulling the dermis toward the mass.","The suspensory ligaments connect the deep fascia to the dermis.","Fibrosis from fat necrosis or carcinoma shortens these ligaments and tethers the skin."],"why":"The suspensory (Cooper) ligaments are bands of connective tissue that run from the deep fascia through the breast to the dermis. When scar tissue or tumor involves them, they shorten and pull the skin inward, which produces dimpling. Both carcinoma and benign scarring such as fat necrosis can do this, so dimpling alone cannot separate them. The breast lies on the pectoralis major from about rib 2 to rib 6, separated from the pectoral fascia by the retromammary space, and has an axillary tail that extends toward the axilla.","whyNot":["Involvement of the pectoral fascia fixes a mass to the chest wall so it does not move when the pectoralis major contracts. It does not pull on the skin.","Shortening of the lactiferous ducts near the nipple causes nipple retraction. It does not dimple skin away from the nipple.","Correct.","Blocked subdermal lymphatics cause edema with peau d'orange, a pitted, thickened skin. They do not produce focal dimpling."],"study":{"topics":["Suspensory ligaments of the breast","Breast location and boundaries"],"comps":["RE-4","RE-13-E5"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C35","system":"RE","dx":"Fibroadenoma in a young woman, with phyllodes tumor as the differential","codes":["RE-13-E6"],"case":"A 23-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a painless lump in her left breast that she noticed 2 months ago while showering. It has not changed in size. She has no nipple discharge and no family history of breast cancer. Examination shows a 2.5-cm firm, rubbery, smooth, nontender mass in the upper outer quadrant of the left breast that moves freely under the examining fingers. There is no skin change or axillary lymphadenopathy. Ultrasonography shows an oval, well-circumscribed, solid mass. Core needle biopsy shows proliferating stroma of low cellularity compressing ducts into slit-like spaces, with no stromal mitoses. The ducts are lined by an inner luminal cell layer and an outer cell layer. The diagnosis is fibroadenoma.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C35-Q1","stem":"This patient becomes pregnant 1 year later. Which of the following changes in the mass is most likely during the pregnancy?","options":["Regression as prolactin suppresses estrogen","Enlargement driven by rising estrogen","Involution with stromal calcification","Transformation into invasive carcinoma"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E6","obj":"RE-13-E.7","dok":2,"approach":["Fibroadenoma contains hormone-responsive stroma and ducts.","Pregnancy brings very high estrogen from the placenta.","The tumor therefore tends to enlarge, while it shrinks and calcifies after menopause."],"why":"A fibroadenoma is a benign proliferation of the specialized intralobular stroma together with ducts. Both parts respond to ovarian hormones, so fibroadenomas can grow during pregnancy and lactation, when estrogen levels are very high. After menopause, as estrogen falls, they tend to shrink and often calcify. Fibroadenoma is the most common benign breast tumor in young women and almost never becomes malignant.","whyNot":["Prolactin rises in pregnancy, but the placenta makes very large amounts of estrogen. Suppression of the ovarian axis does not lower estrogen while the placenta is producing it.","Correct.","Involution with coarse calcification happens after menopause, when estrogen is low.","Fibroadenoma has little or no malignant potential, and pregnancy does not convert it into carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Fibroadenoma","Hormone effects on benign breast tumors"],"comps":["RE-13-E6","RE-7"]}},{"id":"RE-C35-Q2","stem":"Which of the following biopsy findings, had it been present, would have favored a phyllodes tumor over fibroadenoma?","options":["Ducts compressed into slits by bland stroma","Papillae with fibrovascular cores in a duct","Leaflike fronds of hypercellular stroma","Single-file strands of discohesive cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E6","obj":"RE-13-E.8","dok":3,"approach":["Phyllodes tumor and fibroadenoma both mix stroma and ducts, so look at the stroma.","Phyllodes stroma is hypercellular and outgrows the epithelium.","The overgrown stroma bulges into clefts as leaf-like fronds, which is the key feature."],"why":"Fibroadenoma and phyllodes tumor are both fibroepithelial tumors, and in both the stroma is the neoplastic part. In phyllodes tumor, the stroma is more cellular, may show mitoses and atypia, and grows faster than the epithelium, so it bulges into epithelium-lined clefts as leaf-like projections. Phyllodes tumors usually occur in older women, most often in the sixth decade, and they can grow large, recur after excision, and in high-grade cases spread through the blood. Fibroadenoma has bland stroma of low cellularity, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Ducts compressed into slits by bland, low-cellularity stroma are the pattern of fibroadenoma, which this patient already has.","Papillary fronds with fibrovascular cores inside a large duct describe an intraductal papilloma.","Correct.","Single-file strands of discohesive cells are typical of invasive lobular carcinoma, an epithelial cancer."],"study":{"topics":["Phyllodes tumor versus fibroadenoma","Fibroepithelial breast tumors"],"comps":["RE-13-E6"]}},{"id":"RE-C35-Q3","stem":"The epithelial part of this patient's tumor arises from the same structure in which most breast carcinomas begin. Which of the following describes that structure?","options":["Terminal duct joined to a cluster of acini","Lactiferous duct opening at the nipple surface","Lactiferous sinus just beneath the areola","Areolar gland that secretes oily sebum"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-2","obj":"RE-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Fibroadenomas contain both specialized stroma and epithelium of the lobule.","The unit where most breast cancers start is the terminal duct lobular unit.","It consists of a terminal duct and the cluster of small acini it drains."],"why":"The terminal duct lobular unit is a terminal duct with its cluster of small ductules (acini) and the loose specialized stroma around them. Its epithelium has two layers: inner luminal cells and outer myoepithelial cells on the basement membrane. Most breast carcinomas, both ductal and lobular, begin in this unit, and fibroadenomas arise from its stroma and epithelium. The large ducts and lactiferous sinuses near the nipple are the usual site of intraductal papillomas.","whyNot":["Correct.","Large lactiferous ducts carry milk to the nipple. They are the usual site of intraductal papilloma and periductal mastitis, and few carcinomas or fibroadenomas begin there.","The lactiferous sinus is the widened part of a large duct under the areola, where milk collects. Fibroadenomas do not arise here.","Areolar (Montgomery) glands are modified sebaceous glands that lubricate the nipple. They are not the source of these tumors."],"study":{"topics":["Terminal duct lobular unit","Breast histology"],"comps":["RE-2","RE-13-E6"]}},{"id":"RE-C35-Q4","stem":"Fibroadenoma contains ducts and stroma that respond to ovarian steroids in the same way as normal breast tissue. Which of the following correctly pairs each hormone with its main effect on normal breast development?","options":["Estrogen drives duct growth, progesterone drives lobular growth","Progesterone drives duct growth, estrogen drives lobular growth","Estrogen drives duct growth, testosterone drives lobular growth","Testosterone drives duct growth, progesterone drives lobular growth"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-7","obj":"RE-7.5","dok":2,"approach":["At puberty the breast first grows ducts under rising estrogen.","Lobules and alveoli expand with progesterone in luteal phases and in pregnancy.","Androgens oppose breast growth, so they fit neither role."],"why":"At puberty, estrogen stimulates growth and branching of the ducts and deposition of fat and stroma. Progesterone, made by the corpus luteum each cycle and in large amounts in pregnancy, adds growth of the lobules and alveoli. Full development of milk-making alveoli also needs prolactin. Androgens oppose breast growth, which is why breast enlargement in men follows a rise in the estrogen to androgen ratio.","whyNot":["Correct.","This reverses the two roles. Estrogen is the main driver of duct growth, and progesterone is needed for development of lobules and alveoli.","Testosterone does not promote lobular growth. Androgens oppose breast growth.","Duct growth is driven by estrogen. Androgens oppose breast growth and do not drive duct development."],"study":{"topics":["Estrogen and progesterone effects on the breast","Breast development at puberty"],"comps":["RE-7","RE-6"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C36","system":"RE","dx":"Intraductal papilloma with bloody nipple discharge in a woman with fibrocystic breast change","codes":["RE-13-E1"],"case":"A 44-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of spontaneous bloody discharge from the right nipple. For several years both breasts have felt lumpy and tender in the week before menses. Her menses are regular. Pressure at the edge of the right areola at the 9 o'clock position produces bloody discharge from a single duct orifice. No discrete mass is palpable. Serum prolactin is 12 ng/mL (less than 25). Ultrasonography shows a 6-mm solid mass inside a dilated subareolar duct and several simple cysts in both breasts. Excision of the duct shows papillary fronds with fibrovascular cores. Biopsy of one cyst shows apocrine metaplasia of its lining and surrounding stromal fibrosis without epithelial hyperplasia. The diagnosis is intraductal papilloma with nonproliferative fibrocystic change.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C36-Q1","stem":"Which of the following histologic features of this patient's duct lesion best distinguishes it from papillary carcinoma?","options":["Red blood cells within the duct lumen","Fibrovascular cores within the papillae","Myoepithelial cells along the papillae","Location within a large subareolar duct"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E6","obj":"RE-13-E.9","dok":3,"approach":["Both papilloma and papillary carcinoma form fronds with fibrovascular cores inside a duct.","Benign duct lesions keep the normal two-layer lining of luminal and myoepithelial cells.","A myoepithelial layer along the papillae therefore points to papilloma."],"why":"An intraductal papilloma is a benign papillary growth inside a large subareolar duct. It is a common cause of spontaneous bloody discharge from a single duct, often with no palpable mass. Each papilla has a fibrovascular core covered by the same two cell layers as a normal duct, luminal cells and myoepithelial cells. Papillary carcinoma also has fibrovascular cores but has lost the myoepithelial layer along its papillae, so finding that layer supports a benign lesion.","whyNot":["Bleeding into the duct occurs with both papilloma and papillary carcinoma, so it cannot tell them apart.","Fibrovascular cores are present in both papilloma and papillary carcinoma. They show that the lesion is papillary without showing whether it is benign.","Correct.","Both benign and malignant papillary lesions can occur in large ducts, so location does not separate them."],"study":{"topics":["Intraductal papilloma","Causes of bloody nipple discharge"],"comps":["RE-13-E6","RE-2"]}},{"id":"RE-C36-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the breast cancer risk associated with the type of fibrocystic change found in this patient's cyst biopsy, compared with the general population?","options":["About 8 to 10 times higher","No increase in risk","About 1.5 to 2 times higher","About 4 to 5 times higher"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E1","obj":"RE-13-E.1","dok":3,"approach":["Classify the change by what the epithelium is doing: cysts, apocrine metaplasia, and fibrosis are nonproliferative.","Nonproliferative change carries no added cancer risk.","Risk rises only with epithelial proliferation, and more with atypia."],"why":"Fibrocystic change is graded by how much the epithelium proliferates. Nonproliferative change, which includes cysts, apocrine metaplasia, and stromal fibrosis as in this patient, does not raise cancer risk. Proliferative change without atypia, such as epithelial hyperplasia, sclerosing adenosis, and intraductal papilloma, raises risk slightly, about 1.5 to 2 times. Atypical ductal or lobular hyperplasia raises risk about 4 to 5 times.","whyNot":["A risk of about 8 to 10 times goes with carcinoma in situ, such as lobular or ductal carcinoma in situ.","Correct.","A slight increase of about 1.5 to 2 times goes with proliferative change without atypia, such as epithelial hyperplasia, sclerosing adenosis, or an intraductal papilloma.","A moderate increase of about 4 to 5 times goes with atypical ductal or atypical lobular hyperplasia."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrocystic change and cancer risk","Proliferative breast disease"],"comps":["RE-13-E1","RE-13-E6"]}},{"id":"RE-C36-Q3","stem":"Hyperprolactinemia was considered before her prolactin level was measured. If a prolactin-secreting pituitary adenoma were present, which of the following would most likely disrupt her menstrual cycles?","options":["Prolactin blockade of endometrial estrogen receptors","Stalk compression raising gonadotropin release","Prolactin-induced destruction of ovarian follicles","Suppression of pulsatile GnRH secretion"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-7","obj":"RE-7.7","dok":3,"approach":["Prolactin is under tonic dopamine inhibition, and a prolactinoma secretes it without that control.","High prolactin suppresses pulsatile GnRH release from the hypothalamus.","Low LH and FSH stop ovulation and disrupt menses."],"why":"Prolactin is the anterior pituitary hormone held mainly under inhibition, by hypothalamic dopamine acting on lactotrophs. High prolactin suppresses pulsatile GnRH release from the hypothalamus. LH and FSH fall, follicles do not mature, ovulation stops, and estrogen is low, which causes oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea, often with galactorrhea. Galactorrhea from hyperprolactinemia is usually milky and from both breasts, while this patient's bloody discharge from a single duct points to a duct lesion.","whyNot":["Prolactin does not block estrogen receptors in the endometrium. Menses become irregular because ovulation stops.","Stalk compression interrupts dopamine delivery and raises prolactin. Gonadotropin release falls, because it depends on GnRH reaching the pituitary through the same portal vessels.","Follicles are preserved in hyperprolactinemia. They fail to mature because gonadotropins are low, and cycles return once prolactin falls.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Prolactin regulation","Hyperprolactinemia and amenorrhea"],"comps":["RE-7","RE-6"]}},{"id":"RE-C36-Q4","stem":"Examination also shows a 4-mm pigmented papule with a small central nipple-like projection on the left upper abdomen, directly below the left nipple. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Migration of neural crest melanocytes into dermis","Partial persistence of the ectodermal mammary ridge","Failed fusion of paired mesodermal breast primordia","Persistence of a remnant of the vitelline duct"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-1","obj":"RE-1.7","dok":2,"approach":["A small nipple-like papule in line with the nipple suggests a supernumerary nipple.","The breast forms from the ectodermal mammary ridge that runs from the axilla to the groin.","Parts of the ridge that fail to regress can form extra nipples or breast tissue along this line."],"why":"During the embryonic period, a band of thickened ectoderm called the mammary ridge (milk line) runs on each side from the axilla to the inguinal region. Normally it disappears except in the pectoral region, where the breast develops. If other parts persist, a supernumerary nipple (polythelia) or extra breast tissue can form anywhere along this line, most often just below the normal breast.","whyNot":["Neural crest melanocytes in the dermis form pigmented lesions such as a blue nevus or a congenital dermal melanocytosis. They do not form a nipple-like structure along the milk line.","Correct.","Each breast forms from a single ectodermal bud on the mammary ridge. Breasts do not form by fusion of paired primordia.","A vitelline duct remnant can leave an umbilical polyp, sinus, or Meckel diverticulum. It appears at the umbilicus and not in line with the nipple."],"study":{"topics":["Mammary ridge development","Supernumerary nipple"],"comps":["RE-1","RE-2"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C37","system":"RE","dx":"Invasive ductal carcinoma with Paget disease of the nipple","codes":["RE-13-E6"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of an itchy, red, scaly, crusted rash on the left nipple and areola that has not improved with a topical corticosteroid. Examination shows erythema, scaling, and a small erosion of the left nipple and areola. A 2.5-cm hard, irregular, fixed mass is palpated in the lower inner quadrant of the left breast, and a firm 1.5-cm left axillary lymph node is palpable. Mammography shows a spiculated mass and fine linear branching calcifications extending toward the nipple. Biopsy of the nipple shows large cells with abundant pale cytoplasm and prominent nucleoli, single and in clusters, within the epidermis. Core biopsy of the mass shows invasive ductal carcinoma with adjacent ductal carcinoma in situ. The tumor is negative for estrogen and progesterone receptors and strongly positive (3+) for HER2. The diagnosis is invasive ductal carcinoma with Paget disease of the nipple.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C37-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the origin of the large cells in this patient's nipple epidermis?","options":["Ductal carcinoma cells spreading through lactiferous ducts","Epidermal keratinocytes undergoing malignant change in place","Langerhans cells infiltrating the epidermis from dermis","Melanocytes proliferating along the epidermal basal layer"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E6","obj":"RE-13-E.13","dok":3,"approach":["An eczema-like nipple rash that does not respond to steroids, with a breast mass, suggests Paget disease.","Large pale cells within the epidermis are Paget cells.","They are carcinoma cells that reached the skin by spreading through the lactiferous ducts from the underlying ductal cancer."],"why":"In Paget disease of the nipple, cells from an underlying ductal carcinoma spread along the lactiferous ducts into the skin of the nipple without crossing the epidermal basement membrane. Paget cells are large, with abundant pale or clear cytoplasm, and lie within the epidermis. They disrupt the skin barrier and cause an eczema-like rash with scaling and crusting that does not respond to steroids. Nearly all cases have an underlying carcinoma, either ductal carcinoma in situ or invasive ductal carcinoma, and the Paget cells usually share its HER2 overexpression.","whyNot":["Correct.","Malignant change of keratinocytes in place is squamous cell carcinoma in situ (Bowen disease). Its atypical keratinocytes are not linked to an underlying ductal carcinoma.","Langerhans cells are dendritic antigen-presenting cells that normally live in the epidermis. They increase in some forms of dermatitis and in Langerhans cell histiocytosis and are not malignant breast epithelial cells.","Melanoma in situ spreads along the basal layer as atypical melanocytes. It does not come with an underlying ductal carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Paget disease of the nipple","Eczema-like nipple lesions"],"comps":["RE-13-E6"]}},{"id":"RE-C37-Q2","stem":"Which of the following findings would best show that this patient's breast tumor has progressed beyond carcinoma in situ?","options":["Comedo necrosis in involved ducts","Fine linear branching calcifications","Tumor nests in desmoplastic stroma","High-grade nuclei with many mitoses"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E6","obj":"RE-13-E.11","dok":3,"approach":["What separates in situ from invasive carcinoma is the basement membrane.","Comedo necrosis, linear calcifications, and high grade can all occur in ductal carcinoma in situ.","Tumor cells in stroma with a desmoplastic reaction prove invasion."],"why":"Ductal carcinoma in situ stays within ducts, bounded by the myoepithelial cells and basement membrane. It often shows comedo necrosis and fine linear or branching calcifications on mammography, and it may be high grade, but it cannot spread through lymphatics or blood. Invasive ductal carcinoma has broken through the basement membrane into the stroma, where tumor cells provoke a dense fibrous (desmoplastic) reaction. This reaction is why the mass is hard, irregular, and spiculated. Invasive ductal carcinoma is the most common type of invasive breast cancer.","whyNot":["Comedo necrosis is central necrosis in ducts filled with high-grade ductal carcinoma in situ. It is a feature of in situ disease.","Fine linear branching calcifications form in necrotic debris inside ducts and are the classic mammographic sign of ductal carcinoma in situ.","Correct.","High nuclear grade and frequent mitoses occur in high-grade ductal carcinoma in situ as well as in invasive tumors. Grade describes how abnormal the cells look and does not show whether they have crossed the basement membrane."],"study":{"topics":["Ductal carcinoma in situ versus invasive ductal carcinoma","Desmoplasia"],"comps":["RE-13-E6"]}},{"id":"RE-C37-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains the strong HER2 staining in this patient's tumor?","options":["Loss of both alleles of a DNA repair gene","Translocation of a kinase gene to a strong promoter","Point mutation in a nuclear receptor gene","Amplification of a receptor tyrosine kinase gene"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-10","obj":"RE-10.6","dok":2,"approach":["Strong (3+) HER2 staining means the cells make far more receptor than normal.","HER2 is a receptor tyrosine kinase encoded by ERBB2.","In breast cancer, overexpression comes from amplification of ERBB2."],"why":"HER2 is a transmembrane receptor tyrosine kinase in the EGF receptor family, encoded by ERBB2 on chromosome 17. In about 15 to 20% of breast cancers the gene is amplified into many copies, so the cells overexpress the receptor and receive constant growth signals. Breast cancers are grouped by estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and HER2 status: ER-positive tumors form the luminal groups, HER2-amplified tumors form the HER2 group, and tumors negative for all three are triple-negative, a basal-like group linked to BRCA1 mutation. This tumor is ER and PR negative and HER2 positive.","whyNot":["Loss of both BRCA1 alleles impairs DNA repair and is linked to triple-negative, basal-like tumors. It does not cause HER2 overexpression.","A translocation that activates a kinase, such as BCR-ABL in chronic myeloid leukemia, is a different mechanism. HER2 is activated by gene amplification.","Estrogen and progesterone receptors are nuclear receptors, and both are negative in this tumor. HER2 is a cell-surface receptor.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["HER2 amplification","Molecular classes of breast carcinoma"],"comps":["RE-10","RE-13-E6"]}},{"id":"RE-C37-Q4","stem":"Lymph from the region of this patient's mass drains mainly to the axillary nodes. Which other lymph node group most directly receives lymph from this region?","options":["Deep cervical nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes","Posterior mediastinal nodes","Parasternal nodes"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-5","obj":"RE-5.6","dok":2,"approach":["The mass is in the lower inner quadrant, a medial quadrant.","Most breast lymph goes to the axilla, but the medial quadrants also drain medially.","Medial drainage goes to parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels."],"why":"About 75% of breast lymph, especially from the lateral quadrants, drains to the axillary nodes. Lymph from the medial quadrants, like this lower inner quadrant tumor, also drains to parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels. The blood supply follows a similar pattern: the internal thoracic artery sends perforating branches to the medial breast, and the lateral thoracic and thoracoacromial arteries supply the lateral and upper parts.","whyNot":["Deep cervical nodes drain the head and neck. Breast lymph reaches the neck only indirectly, through the apical axillary and supraclavicular nodes.","Superficial inguinal nodes drain the lower limb, the perineum, and the skin of the abdominal wall below the umbilicus.","Posterior mediastinal nodes drain the esophagus and posterior mediastinum. They do not receive breast lymph directly.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic drainage of the breast","Blood supply of the breast"],"comps":["RE-5","RE-4"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C38","system":"RE","dx":"Invasive lobular carcinoma of the breast in a BRCA2 carrier","codes":["RE-13-E6"],"case":"A 56-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a vague thickening in her right breast that she first noticed 3 months ago. She carries a pathogenic BRCA2 variant, identified after her mother developed ovarian cancer at age 52. Menarche occurred at age 10 and menopause at age 55. She has never been pregnant. Her body mass index is 33 kg/m2. Examination shows an ill-defined area of firmness without a discrete mass in the upper outer quadrant of the right breast. Mammography shows subtle architectural distortion. MRI shows several separate areas of enhancement in the right breast and an 8-mm focus in the left breast. Biopsy of the right breast shows small, uniform cells infiltrating the stroma in single-file strands. The tumor cells do not stain for E-cadherin, are positive for estrogen and progesterone receptors, and are negative for HER2. The diagnosis is invasive lobular carcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C38-Q1","stem":"Loss of which of the following functions of the protein absent on immunostaining best explains the growth pattern of this patient's tumor?","options":["Calcium-dependent adhesion at adherens junctions","Attachment of cells to laminin in basement membrane","Sealing of the paracellular space at tight junctions","Linkage of keratin filaments at desmosomes"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-13-E6","obj":"RE-13-E.12","dok":3,"approach":["Single-file strands of discohesive cells that lack E-cadherin define invasive lobular carcinoma.","E-cadherin is the calcium-dependent adhesion protein of adherens junctions.","Without it, tumor cells separate and invade the stroma one by one."],"why":"E-cadherin, encoded by CDH1, is a calcium-dependent transmembrane protein that binds E-cadherin on neighboring cells at adherens junctions and links to the actin cytoskeleton through catenins. Lobular carcinoma loses E-cadherin, so its cells do not stick together. They infiltrate the stroma one by one in single-file lines, form little mass, and are hard to feel or see on mammography. Lobular carcinoma is more often multifocal and bilateral than ductal carcinoma, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Correct.","Attachment to laminin in the basement membrane is done by integrins. Integrins are not the protein lost in lobular carcinoma.","Claudins and occludin seal the paracellular space at tight junctions. Their loss makes an epithelium leaky without causing single-file invasion.","Desmogleins and desmocollins link keratin filaments at desmosomes. Antibodies against desmogleins cause pemphigus."],"study":{"topics":["E-cadherin and lobular carcinoma","Cell junctions"],"comps":["RE-13-E6","RE-10"]}},{"id":"RE-C38-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains how this patient's body mass index contributed to her breast cancer risk after menopause?","options":["Higher hepatic SHBG production binding more estradiol","Insulin-driven increase in ovarian progesterone secretion","Conversion of androgens to estrogen by adipose aromatase","Leptin-driven suppression of hypothalamic GnRH release"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-E6","obj":"RE-13-E.10","dok":3,"approach":["After menopause, the main estrogen source is peripheral aromatization.","Adipose tissue contains aromatase, so more fat means more estrogen.","Longer and higher estrogen exposure of breast epithelium raises cancer risk."],"why":"After menopause the ovaries make little estradiol, and most estrogen comes from aromatase in adipose tissue, which converts androstenedione to estrone and testosterone to estradiol. More fat therefore means more estrogen, and obesity also lowers SHBG, which raises free estrogen. Prolonged estrogen stimulation of breast epithelium raises cancer risk, which is why early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, and late first pregnancy are also risk factors. This patient has several of these as well as a BRCA2 variant. Most breast cancers arise in the upper outer quadrant, where most breast tissue lies.","whyNot":["Obesity lowers SHBG production by the liver. Higher SHBG would bind more estradiol and reduce its effect.","Ovarian progesterone comes from the corpus luteum after ovulation. After menopause there is no ovulation, so obesity cannot raise it.","Correct.","Leptin signals adequate energy stores and permits GnRH release. After menopause, GnRH cannot raise ovarian estrogen much because few follicles remain."],"study":{"topics":["Estrogen exposure and breast cancer risk","Peripheral aromatization"],"comps":["RE-13-E6","RE-7"]}},{"id":"RE-C38-Q3","stem":"Two years later, this patient's left breast becomes diffusely red, warm, and swollen over several weeks, and its skin develops a pitted, orange-peel texture. She is afebrile, and her leukocyte count is within the reference range. Which of the following best explains these skin changes?","options":["Tumor emboli in dermal lymphatic vessels","Necrosis of breast fat with foamy macrophages","Staphylococcal infection of the breast lobules","Tumor traction on the suspensory ligaments"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-E6","obj":"RE-13-E.14","dok":3,"approach":["A diffusely red, warm, swollen breast with peau d'orange, no fever, and a normal leukocyte count points to inflammatory carcinoma over mastitis.","Peau d'orange means dermal edema from blocked lymph drainage.","Tumor emboli in dermal lymphatics block that drainage."],"why":"Inflammatory carcinoma is defined by its clinical picture: a red, warm, swollen breast with peau d'orange. The cause is tumor cells blocking lymphatic vessels in the dermis. Lymph cannot drain, so the skin becomes edematous while staying tethered at the hair follicles, which gives the pitted orange-peel look. There is no infection, abscess, or fever, and the prognosis is poor. Acute mastitis also causes a red, warm breast, but it usually occurs in breastfeeding women with fever, pain, and leukocytosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Fat necrosis forms a firm mass after trauma, sometimes with dimpling or bruising. It does not cause weeks of spreading redness with peau d'orange.","S. aureus mastitis usually occurs during breastfeeding and causes fever, pain, and leukocytosis. This patient has none of these.","Traction on the suspensory ligaments causes focal skin dimpling over a mass. It does not cause diffuse redness and edema."],"study":{"topics":["Inflammatory breast carcinoma","Inflammatory carcinoma versus mastitis"],"comps":["RE-13-E6","RE-13-E4"]}},{"id":"RE-C38-Q4","stem":"This patient's 30-year-old son asks about his own risk. Which of the following best describes his chance of having inherited her BRCA2 variant and the cancers for which a male carrier has increased risk?","options":["25%; breast and prostate cancer","25%; testicular cancer and lymphoma","50%; breast and prostate cancer","50%; testicular cancer and lymphoma"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-10","obj":"RE-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["BRCA2 cancer predisposition is autosomal dominant, so each child has a 50% chance of inheriting the variant.","BRCA2 is a homologous recombination repair gene, and a tumor forms when the second allele is lost.","In men, BRCA2 raises the risk of breast and prostate cancer."],"why":"BRCA2 cancer predisposition is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait, so each child of a carrier has a 50% chance of inheriting the variant, whatever the child's sex. BRCA2 helps repair double-strand DNA breaks by homologous recombination, loading RAD51 onto damaged DNA, and a tumor forms when the remaining normal allele is lost in a cell. BRCA1 and BRCA2 carriers have high risks of breast and ovarian cancer. BRCA2 carriers, including men, also have increased risks of male breast cancer, prostate cancer, and pancreatic cancer.","whyNot":["A 25% chance fits a child of two carriers of an autosomal recessive trait. BRCA2 cancer predisposition is autosomal dominant.","Both parts are wrong. BRCA2 predisposition is autosomal dominant, and testicular cancer and lymphoma are not BRCA2-related cancers.","Correct.","The 50% chance is right, but testicular cancer and lymphoma are not BRCA2-related cancers."],"study":{"topics":["BRCA1 and BRCA2","Hereditary breast and ovarian cancer"],"comps":["RE-10","RE-13-E6"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C39","system":"RE","dx":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia with urinary retention","codes":["RE-13-F1"],"case":"A 71-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because he has been unable to urinate for 14 hours and has lower abdominal pain. For 3 years he has had a hesitant, weak urinary stream, a feeling of incomplete emptying, and nocturia three times nightly. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 152/88 mmHg. A tender, distended bladder is palpable above the pubic symphysis. Digital rectal examination shows a smooth, firm, rubbery, symmetrically enlarged prostate without nodules. Catheterization drains 1,100 mL of urine. Serum urea nitrogen is 34 mg/dL (7 to 20), and creatinine is 1.9 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is 5.1 ng/mL (less than 4.0). Ultrasonography shows bilateral mild hydronephrosis and a thick-walled bladder. The diagnosis is benign prostatic hyperplasia with acute urinary retention.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C39-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the process that enlarged this patient's prostate?","options":["Scarring of the gland after chronic bacterial infection","DHT-driven nodules of periurethral glands and stroma","Enlargement of existing cells without new cell division","Clonal growth of malignant acini in the peripheral zone"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-F1","obj":"RE-13-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["An older man with obstructive voiding symptoms and a smooth, rubbery, enlarged prostate has benign prostatic hyperplasia.","It is hyperplasia of both glands and stroma, beginning in the periurethral transition zone.","Its growth is driven by DHT made locally from testosterone."],"why":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia is an increase in the number of both glandular and stromal cells, which form nodules. It begins in the transition zone and periurethral glands around the prostatic urethra, so even modest growth narrows the urethra and causes obstructive symptoms. Growth depends on DHT, made from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase in prostatic stromal cells, which stimulates growth factors and reduces cell death. It is very common with age and is not a precursor of prostate carcinoma. PSA can be mildly raised because there is more prostatic tissue.","whyNot":["Chronic prostatitis can cause scarring, but it does not produce a diffusely enlarged, rubbery gland made of hyperplastic nodules.","Correct.","Enlargement of existing cells without division is hypertrophy, as in this patient's thickened bladder wall. The prostate in this condition grows by adding new cells.","Prostate carcinoma usually arises in the peripheral zone and feels hard and nodular on rectal examination. This prostate is smooth and rubbery."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of benign prostatic hyperplasia","Hyperplasia versus hypertrophy"],"comps":["RE-13-F1","RE-8"]}},{"id":"RE-C39-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains this patient's elevated serum creatinine concentration?","options":["Reduced renal blood flow from volume depletion","Raised hydrostatic pressure in Bowman space","Immune complex deposits in the glomeruli","Ascending infection destroying renal tubules"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-F1","obj":"RE-13-F.2","dok":3,"approach":["Bilateral hydronephrosis with a large retained bladder volume points to obstruction below the bladder.","Backed-up urine raises pressure in the tubules and Bowman space.","That pressure opposes filtration, lowering GFR and raising creatinine (postrenal injury)."],"why":"Prostatic obstruction keeps the bladder from emptying. Pressure builds back through the ureters and renal pelvis (hydronephrosis) into the tubules and Bowman space. Higher pressure in Bowman space opposes filtration, so GFR falls and creatinine and urea rise. This is postrenal kidney injury, and it improves once the obstruction is relieved. Chronic obstruction also thickens the bladder wall through smooth muscle hypertrophy and raises the risk of infection and stones from urinary stasis.","whyNot":["Volume depletion lowers glomerular capillary pressure and causes prerenal injury. Nothing suggests volume loss here, and he has hydronephrosis from obstruction.","Correct.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis causes hematuria, proteinuria, and kidney injury without hydronephrosis.","Pyelonephritis can complicate urinary stasis, but he has no fever, and infection would not explain bilateral hydronephrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of benign prostatic hyperplasia","Postrenal kidney injury"],"comps":["RE-13-F1","UR-6"]}},{"id":"RE-C39-Q3","stem":"During this patient's digital rectal examination, the examiner's finger palpates the prostate through which of the following?","options":["Perineal membrane in the urogenital triangle","Posterior wall of the rectum","Anterior wall of the rectum","Peritoneum of the rectovesical pouch"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-4","obj":"RE-4.7","dok":2,"approach":["Picture a sagittal section: bladder on top, prostate below it, rectum behind both.","The finger enters through the anus and presses forward.","It meets the prostate through the anterior rectal wall."],"why":"The prostate lies below the bladder, above the perineal membrane, and directly in front of the rectum, separated from it only by the rectoprostatic (Denonvilliers) fascia. A finger in the rectum presses forward through the anterior rectal wall onto the posterior surface of the prostate. The finger enters through the anus in the anal triangle, the posterior part of the diamond-shaped perineum bounded by the pubic symphysis, ischiopubic rami, ischial tuberosities, sacrotuberous ligaments, and coccyx.","whyNot":["The perineal membrane lies below the prostate in the urogenital triangle. A rectal examination enters through the anal triangle.","The posterior rectal wall faces the sacrum and coccyx. The prostate lies in front of the rectum.","Correct.","The rectovesical pouch is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity in men and lies above the prostate, between the bladder and rectum. The prostate is below the peritoneum."],"study":{"topics":["Relations of the prostate","Boundaries of the perineum"],"comps":["RE-4","RE-2"]}},{"id":"RE-C39-Q4","stem":"Growth of this patient's prostate depends on an androgen formed from testosterone within the gland. A 46,XY individual with congenital deficiency of the enzyme that forms this androgen would most likely have which of the following?","options":["Female external genitalia, no uterus, and sparse hair","Normal male genitalia and absent vasa deferentia","Male genitalia with a uterus and uterine tubes","Ambiguous genitalia at birth, virilizing at puberty"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-8","obj":"RE-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["The androgen made in the prostate from testosterone is DHT, formed by 5-alpha-reductase.","In the fetus, DHT masculinizes the external genitalia and prostate, while testosterone drives the Wolffian ducts.","Without the enzyme, the genitalia are ambiguous at birth, and the large rise in testosterone at puberty causes virilization."],"why":"5-alpha-reductase type 2 converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT) in the prostate and genital skin. In the fetus, DHT forms the penis, scrotum, and prostate, while testosterone itself drives the Wolffian ducts, so the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles form normally. A 46,XY infant with 5-alpha-reductase deficiency has ambiguous or female-appearing external genitalia at birth. At puberty, the large rise in testosterone causes virilization with muscle growth and phallic enlargement. The prostate stays small, showing that prostate growth depends on DHT.","whyNot":["Female external genitalia, no uterus, and sparse pubic and axillary hair describe complete androgen insensitivity from a defective androgen receptor. Anti-Mullerian hormone still causes Mullerian regression.","Absent vasa deferentia with otherwise normal male genitalia occur with CFTR mutations in cystic fibrosis.","A uterus and uterine tubes in a 46,XY male with normal external genitalia result from a lack of anti-Mullerian hormone or its receptor (persistent Mullerian duct syndrome).","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["5-alpha-reductase deficiency","Testosterone versus DHT actions"],"comps":["RE-8","RE-1"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C40","system":"RE","dx":"Acute bacterial prostatitis","codes":["RE-13-F2"],"case":"A 64-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 2-day history of fever, shaking chills, painful urination, urinary frequency, and aching pain in the perineum and lower back. Temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 126/76 mmHg. Gentle digital rectal examination shows a warm, swollen, exquisitely tender, boggy prostate. Leukocyte count is 17,600/mm3 (4,500 to 11,000) with increased neutrophils. Urinalysis shows positive leukocyte esterase, positive nitrite, and more than 50 leukocytes per high-power field. Serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is 18 ng/mL (less than 4.0). Urine and blood cultures are pending. The diagnosis is acute bacterial prostatitis.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C40-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the organism most likely to grow from this patient's urine culture?","options":["Coagulase-negative gram-positive coccus","Obligate intracellular gram-negative bacterium","Lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod","Gram-negative intracellular diplococcus"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-F2","obj":"RE-13-F.3","dok":3,"approach":["An older man with fever, dysuria, and a tender, boggy prostate has acute bacterial prostatitis.","In older men the cause is the usual urinary pathogens, and a positive nitrite test points to Enterobacterales.","The most likely isolate is E. coli, a lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod."],"why":"In older men, acute bacterial prostatitis is usually caused by the same organisms that cause urinary tract infections, most often E. coli and other gram-negative rods. They reach the prostate by reflux of infected urine into the prostatic ducts. A positive nitrite test fits, because Enterobacterales such as E. coli reduce urinary nitrate to nitrite. In younger, sexually active men, N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis are more likely causes.","whyNot":["Coagulase-negative staphylococci such as S. epidermidis infect catheters and prosthetic devices. They are uncommon causes of acute prostatitis.","C. trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium that causes urethritis and prostatitis mainly in younger sexually active men. It does not grow on routine urine culture.","Correct.","N. gonorrhoeae, a gram-negative intracellular diplococcus, causes prostatitis mainly in younger sexually active men. A 64-year-old man with a positive nitrite test most likely has a gut-derived gram-negative rod."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of acute bacterial prostatitis","Escherichia coli features"],"comps":["RE-13-F2","RE-12"]}},{"id":"RE-C40-Q2","stem":"Over the next 2 years, this patient has three episodes of cystitis caused by the same strain of E. coli, with sterile urine between episodes. He has no pelvic pain between episodes. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Autoimmune inflammation of the prostate without bacteria","Hematogenous seeding of the bladder from a distant focus","Reinfection with new strains from a sexual partner","Bacteria persisting in prostatic ducts that reseed urine"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-F2","obj":"RE-13-F.4","dok":3,"approach":["The same organism in repeated episodes, with sterile urine in between, means a hidden reservoir is reseeding the urine.","In men the classic reservoir is the prostate.","This pattern defines chronic bacterial prostatitis, which differs from acute prostatitis and from nonbacterial pelvic pain."],"why":"Chronic bacterial prostatitis develops when bacteria survive in the prostate, often in ducts or small prostatic stones where they are hard to clear. They periodically reseed the bladder, so the man has recurrent urinary tract infections with the same organism and sterile urine in between. Acute bacterial prostatitis is a sudden febrile illness with a tender, boggy gland. Chronic nonbacterial prostatitis (chronic pelvic pain syndrome) causes pelvic or perineal pain for months with no bacteria found.","whyNot":["Chronic pelvic pain with no bacteria found fits chronic nonbacterial prostatitis. This patient has proven bacterial infections and no pain between episodes.","The bladder is rarely seeded through the blood. Urinary infections in men usually ascend from the urethra or come from a prostatic source.","Reinfection would bring different strains over time. Repeated infection with the identical strain points to a persistent source.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Types of prostatitis","Chronic bacterial prostatitis"],"comps":["RE-13-F2"]}},{"id":"RE-C40-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best describes the normal function of the protein measured in this patient's serum and the reason its level is high?","options":["Liquefies semen; leaks from inflamed glands","Liquefies semen; made only by carcinoma cells","Supplies fructose to sperm; leaks from inflamed glands","Coagulates semen; made by the seminal vesicles"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-8","obj":"RE-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["PSA is a serine protease from prostatic epithelium that liquefies the semen coagulum.","It normally stays in the ducts, and little reaches the blood.","Inflammation breaks the epithelial barrier, so PSA leaks into serum."],"why":"PSA is a serine protease made by prostatic epithelial cells and secreted into the ducts. It breaks down semenogelin, the clotting protein from the seminal vesicles, and liquefies the semen coagulum after ejaculation. Normally little PSA crosses into the blood. Inflammation disrupts the epithelium and basement membrane, so PSA leaks into the circulation, which is why PSA rises in prostatitis and benign hyperplasia as well as in carcinoma. Prostatic fluid also adds citrate and zinc, and the seminal vesicles supply fructose.","whyNot":["Correct.","Benign prostatic epithelium makes PSA too. It is specific to the prostate but not to cancer, so infection and benign hyperplasia also raise it.","Fructose for sperm comes from the seminal vesicles. PSA is a protease.","Semenogelin from the seminal vesicles forms the coagulum. PSA comes from the prostate and dissolves the coagulum."],"study":{"topics":["Prostate-specific antigen","Components of semen"],"comps":["RE-8","RE-13-F2"]}},{"id":"RE-C40-Q4","stem":"After the infection resolves, his PSA will be rechecked because prostate carcinoma is a concern at his age. Which of the following describes the prostatic zone where most carcinomas arise?","options":["Transition zone, around the urethra","Peripheral zone, toward the rectum","Central zone, around the ejaculatory ducts","Anterior fibromuscular stroma, lacking glands"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-2","obj":"RE-2.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the three glandular zones: peripheral, transition, and central.","Most carcinomas arise in the peripheral zone, which lies posteriorly and laterally.","That position is why carcinomas can be felt on rectal examination and cause urinary symptoms late."],"why":"The prostate has three glandular zones. The peripheral zone is the largest, holding about 70% of the glandular tissue, and lies posteriorly and laterally, next to the rectum. Most carcinomas begin here, so they can be felt as hard nodules on rectal examination and often cause few urinary symptoms early. The transition zone surrounds the proximal prostatic urethra and is where benign prostatic hyperplasia begins. The central zone surrounds the ejaculatory ducts and is rarely the origin of disease.","whyNot":["The transition zone surrounds the proximal prostatic urethra and is the site of benign prostatic hyperplasia, which is why hyperplasia causes urinary obstruction early.","Correct.","The central zone surrounds the ejaculatory ducts and is an uncommon site of both hyperplasia and carcinoma.","The anterior fibromuscular stroma has no glands, so it does not give rise to adenocarcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Zones of the prostate","Prostate carcinoma location"],"comps":["RE-2","RE-13-F1"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C41","system":"RE","dx":"Prostate adenocarcinoma with bone metastases","codes":["RE-13-J6"],"case":"A 71-year-old Black man comes to the physician because of low back pain for 3 months that is worse at night. His father was diagnosed with prostate cancer at age 62, and his sister had breast cancer at age 44. Digital rectal examination shows a hard, irregular nodule in the left lobe of the prostate. Serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is 48 ng/mL (less than 4.0), alkaline phosphatase is 310 U/L (40 to 130), and calcium is 9.6 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5). Radiographs show sclerotic lesions in the L3 and L4 vertebral bodies and both iliac bones. Needle biopsy shows infiltrating small glands lined by a single layer of cells with prominent nucleoli. Diagnosis: prostate adenocarcinoma with bone metastases.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C41-Q1","stem":"The pathologist reports a Gleason score of 4 + 4 = 8. Which of the following features of the tumor is the basis of this score?","options":["Architectural pattern of the malignant glands","Depth of invasion through the prostatic capsule","Degree of nuclear pleomorphism and nucleolar size","Number of mitotic figures per high-power field"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J6","obj":"RE-13-J.9","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that most tumor grades rest on nuclear features or mitoses, and prostate cancer is the classic exception.","Gleason grading looks at gland architecture at low power: well-formed glands (pattern 3), fused or cribriform glands (pattern 4), and sheets or single cells (pattern 5).","The two most common patterns are added to give the score."],"why":"Gleason grading is based only on the architectural pattern of the tumor glands seen at low magnification. Each pattern gets a number, and the most common and second most common patterns are added together. Pattern 4 means fused, poorly formed, or cribriform glands, so a score of 8 is high grade. That fits a tumor that has already reached bone. Grade is separate from stage, which describes how far the tumor has spread.","whyNot":["Correct.","Extension through the capsule is a feature of stage (the T category), not grade.","Nuclear pleomorphism is the basis of nuclear grading in other carcinomas, such as breast cancer. Prostate cancer nuclei are often fairly uniform, so the Gleason system does not use nuclear detail.","Mitotic count is part of grading for breast carcinoma and for sarcomas. It is not part of the Gleason system."],"study":{"topics":["Gleason grading","Grade versus stage"],"comps":["RE-13-J6"]}},{"id":"RE-C41-Q2","stem":"A bone scan shows multiple lesions in the lumbar vertebrae and pelvis, and CT of the chest shows no lung nodules. Which of the following best explains how tumor cells reached the lumbar spine without first lodging in the lungs?","options":["Lymphatic spread through the para-aortic lymph nodes","Flow through the valveless vertebral venous plexus","Arterial spread through the lumbar segmental arteries","Portal venous drainage through the inferior mesenteric vein"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-13-J6","obj":"RE-13-J.10","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the pattern: lumbar spine and pelvis involved, lungs spared.","Blood from the prostatic venous plexus normally drains to the internal iliac veins and then through the lungs.","The valveless vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus) links the prostatic and pelvic veins directly to the vertebral bodies, bypassing the lungs."],"why":"The prostatic venous plexus connects with the vertebral venous plexus, a network of valveless veins around the spine. When abdominal pressure rises, as with coughing or straining, blood can flow backward from the pelvis into these veins and carry tumor cells straight to the lumbar vertebrae and pelvis. This route skips the lung capillary filter. Prostate cancer cells in bone then stimulate osteoblasts, which produce sclerotic lesions, bone pain, and a high serum alkaline phosphatase, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Prostate cancer does spread to pelvic and para-aortic lymph nodes, but lymph returns to the venous blood through the thoracic duct and then passes through the lungs. Nodal spread does not give a direct route to the vertebral bodies.","Correct.","Tumor cells that enter the systemic veins must pass through the pulmonary capillaries before they can reach any systemic artery, so arterial spread would usually leave lung lesions first.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains the left colon into the portal system, which carries tumor cells to the liver. This is a route for colorectal cancer, not prostate cancer."],"study":{"topics":["Vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus)","Osteoblastic bone metastases"],"comps":["RE-13-J6","RE-5"]}},{"id":"RE-C41-Q3","stem":"The nodule was easily felt on digital rectal examination, and he had no urinary hesitancy or weak stream. Which of the following regions of the prostate is the most likely site of origin of this tumor?","options":["Anterior fibromuscular stroma","Transition zone","Peripheral zone","Central zone"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-2","obj":"RE-2.6","dok":3,"approach":["Combine the clues: a hard nodule felt through the rectal wall and no obstructive urinary symptoms.","The peripheral zone lies posterior and lateral, against the rectum and away from the urethra.","Benign prostatic hyperplasia arises in the transition zone around the urethra, which is why it causes obstructive symptoms early."],"why":"About 70% of prostate carcinomas arise in the peripheral zone, which forms most of the posterior and lateral gland. This zone lies against the rectum, so tumors there are often felt as hard nodules on rectal examination. Because the peripheral zone is away from the prostatic urethra, these tumors seldom cause early obstructive symptoms. Benign prostatic hyperplasia arises in the transition zone, which surrounds the proximal prostatic urethra.","whyNot":["The anterior fibromuscular stroma is mostly smooth muscle and fibrous tissue with few glands, and it faces away from the rectum. Carcinoma rarely starts here.","The transition zone surrounds the urethra and is the site of benign prostatic hyperplasia. Enlargement here compresses the urethra and causes hesitancy and a weak stream early.","Correct.","The central zone surrounds the ejaculatory ducts at the base of the gland. Only a small share of carcinomas start here."],"study":{"topics":["Zones of the prostate","Benign prostatic hyperplasia versus carcinoma"],"comps":["RE-2","RE-13-J6"]}},{"id":"RE-C41-Q4","stem":"Because of his sister's early breast cancer and his own diagnosis, germline testing is performed and shows a pathogenic BRCA2 variant. The normal protein product of this gene is required for which of the following DNA repair processes?","options":["Base excision repair of deaminated or oxidized bases","Nucleotide excision repair of bulky DNA adducts","Homologous recombination at double-strand breaks","Mismatch repair of errors left after replication"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-10","obj":"RE-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["Link the family pattern (prostate cancer and early breast cancer) to hereditary BRCA2 mutation.","Recall that BRCA1 and BRCA2 are tumor suppressor genes in homologous recombination repair.","A cell loses repair only after a second hit in the normal allele, so inheritance is autosomal dominant while the cell defect is recessive."],"why":"BRCA2 loads RAD51 onto single-stranded DNA so that a broken strand can copy the matching sequence on the sister chromatid. This is homologous recombination, the error-free repair of double-strand breaks. Carriers inherit one faulty copy in an autosomal dominant pattern. After a second, somatic hit, cells must use error-prone repair, and mutations build up. BRCA2 carriers have a higher risk of breast and ovarian cancer and, in men, of breast and prostate cancer, which fits this family.","whyNot":["Base excision repair uses glycosylases to remove single damaged bases, such as uracil formed by cytosine deamination. It is not the BRCA2 pathway.","Nucleotide excision repair removes bulky lesions such as UV-induced pyrimidine dimers. Its defect causes xeroderma pigmentosum and skin cancers.","Correct.","Mismatch repair genes (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) are defective in Lynch syndrome, which raises the risk of colon and endometrial cancer."],"study":{"topics":["BRCA1 and BRCA2","DNA repair pathways"],"comps":["RE-10","RE-13-J6"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C42","system":"RE","dx":"Ovarian torsion from a mature cystic teratoma, with a paraovarian cyst as the differential","codes":["RE-13-G2"],"case":"A 19-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of sudden, severe right lower quadrant pain that began 4 hours ago during exercise, with nausea and two episodes of vomiting. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. A urine pregnancy test is negative. Pelvic ultrasound shows an enlarged right ovary containing a 7-cm cystic mass with echogenic fat and a focus of calcification, and Doppler shows reduced flow to the ovary. The differential diagnosis includes a twisted paraovarian cyst. At laparoscopy, the right adnexa is twisted twice on its pedicle. The ovary is untwisted and the cyst is removed; it contains sebaceous material, hair, and a tooth. Diagnosis: ovarian torsion caused by a mature cystic teratoma.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C42-Q1","stem":"At laparoscopy, the twisted ovary is enlarged, edematous, and dark blue-black, but it turns pink within minutes of being untwisted. Which of the following best explains these findings?","options":["Immediate arterial occlusion with pale coagulative infarction","Cyst rupture with chemical peritonitis and serosal edema","Hemorrhage from a corpus luteum into the ovarian stroma","Venous outflow blocked first, with arterial inflow preserved"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-G","obj":"RE-13-G.2","dok":3,"approach":["Notice the color: a dark, swollen ovary means blood is trapped in it, not simply cut off.","Thin-walled, low-pressure veins collapse under twisting before thick-walled arteries do.","Arteries keep filling a congested ovary until tissue pressure stops them, so prompt untwisting can save it."],"why":"When the ovary twists on its ligaments, the thin-walled, low-pressure veins collapse first. Arteries keep pumping blood in, so the ovary becomes engorged, edematous, and dark from hemorrhagic congestion. If the twist persists, rising tissue pressure finally stops arterial inflow and the ovary infarcts. Here the ovary turned pink after untwisting, which shows the arterial supply was still working. An ovary enlarged by a cyst or tumor, like this teratoma, is the usual setup for torsion.","whyNot":["Pure arterial occlusion gives a pale infarct with no time for congestion. Torsion squeezes the low-pressure veins first, so the ovary turns dark before it infarcts.","Rupture of a teratoma can spill sebaceous material and cause chemical peritonitis, but this cyst was intact and twisted. Rupture would not explain a dark ovary that recovers after untwisting.","Bleeding into a corpus luteum cyst can cause sudden pain, but this ovary held a teratoma and was twisted on its pedicle.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ovarian torsion","Venous versus arterial occlusion"],"comps":["RE-13-G","RE-4"]}},{"id":"RE-C42-Q2","stem":"Histologic examination of the cyst shows skin with hair follicles and sebaceous glands, cartilage, and respiratory epithelium. If the tumor had instead been composed almost entirely of mature thyroid follicles, which of the following would most likely have been found before surgery?","options":["Suppressed TSH with raised free thyroxine","Raised serum lactate dehydrogenase","Raised serum alpha-fetoprotein","Raised serum testosterone with hirsutism and clitoromegaly"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J7","obj":"RE-13-J.13","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize mature tissues from all three germ layers: a mature cystic teratoma.","A teratoma made mostly of thyroid tissue is struma ovarii, a monodermal teratoma.","Functioning thyroid tissue can make hormone on its own, and the high T4 suppresses pituitary TSH."],"why":"Mature cystic teratoma is the most common ovarian germ cell tumor and contains mature tissues from ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm. Struma ovarii is a teratoma made mostly of thyroid tissue. That tissue can secrete thyroid hormone outside normal control, causing hyperthyroidism with a high free T4 and, through negative feedback on the pituitary, a suppressed TSH. Removing the tumor cures the hyperthyroidism.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lactate dehydrogenase is the marker for dysgerminoma, which is made of sheets of large clear cells. It is not a feature of struma ovarii.","Alpha-fetoprotein is raised in yolk sac tumor, a malignant germ cell tumor with Schiller-Duval bodies. Mature thyroid tissue does not make AFP.","Testosterone excess with virilization points to a Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor, a sex cord-stromal tumor, not a teratoma."],"study":{"topics":["Ovarian germ cell tumors","Struma ovarii"],"comps":["RE-13-J7"]}},{"id":"RE-C42-Q3","stem":"Which of the following laparoscopic findings would have favored torsion of a paraovarian cyst over torsion of an ovarian mass?","options":["Ovary studded with cysts of dark brown, chocolate-like fluid","Thin-walled cyst in the broad ligament beside a normal ovary","Bilateral multiloculated ovarian cysts filled with clear fluid","Hemorrhagic cyst within the ovary lined by luteinized cells"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-G2","obj":"RE-13-G.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall where paraovarian cysts arise: remnants of the Mullerian or Wolffian (mesonephric) ducts in the broad ligament.","The key feature is a cyst separate from the ovary, which looks normal.","The other choices are all cysts inside the ovary itself."],"why":"Paraovarian cysts are thin-walled, fluid-filled cysts in the broad ligament, between the ovary and the uterine tube. They arise from embryonic remnants of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) or mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts. Because they sit apart from the ovary, the ovary itself looks normal at surgery. Like ovarian masses, a large paraovarian cyst can twist or rupture and cause acute pelvic pain.","whyNot":["Ovarian cysts with chocolate-colored fluid are endometriomas from endometriosis. They lie within the ovary, not beside it.","Correct.","Bilateral multiloculated ovarian cysts are theca lutein cysts, which form under very high hCG, as in molar pregnancy. Her pregnancy test was negative.","A hemorrhagic cyst lined by luteinized cells is a corpus luteum cyst inside the ovary. It can rupture and bleed into the peritoneum."],"study":{"topics":["Paraovarian cysts","Types of ovarian cysts"],"comps":["RE-13-G2","RE-13-G1"]}},{"id":"RE-C42-Q4","stem":"The twisted pedicle compressed the main artery and vein of the right ovary. In which of the following structures do these vessels reach the ovary?","options":["Transverse cervical (cardinal) ligament","Round ligament of the uterus","Proper ligament of the ovary","Suspensory ligament of the ovary"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-4","obj":"RE-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the ovarian artery comes directly from the abdominal aorta and descends to the pelvic brim.","It enters the pelvis inside the suspensory (infundibulopelvic) ligament, with the ovarian vein and lymphatics.","Torsion twists the adnexa around this ligament and the ovarian ligament, kinking these vessels."],"why":"The ovarian artery arises from the abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries and reaches the ovary through the suspensory ligament of the ovary, also called the infundibulopelvic ligament. The ovarian vein and lymphatics run with it. In torsion, the adnexa rotate around this ligament and the ovarian ligament, so these vessels are kinked. Surgeons also clamp the suspensory ligament when they remove an ovary.","whyNot":["The cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament carries the uterine artery and vein at the base of the broad ligament, near the cervix.","The round ligament runs from the uterine horn through the inguinal canal to the labium majus. It carries only a small artery and is not the ovarian supply.","The proper ovarian ligament joins the ovary to the uterus. The ovarian branch of the uterine artery runs nearby and joins the ovarian artery, but the main ovarian vessels arrive through the suspensory ligament.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ligaments of the uterus and ovary","Ovarian blood supply"],"comps":["RE-4","RE-5"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C43","system":"RE","dx":"High-grade serous carcinoma of the ovary","codes":["RE-13-J7"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman comes to the physician because of abdominal bloating, early satiety, and a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss over 3 months. Her mother had breast cancer at age 46. Physical examination shows shifting dullness and bilateral adnexal masses. Serum CA-125 is 840 U/mL (less than 35). CT shows ascites, bilateral solid and cystic adnexal masses, and a thickened, nodular omentum. At surgery, both ovaries have surface papillary growths, and tumor nodules cover the omentum and peritoneum. Histologic examination shows papillae and slit-like spaces lined by highly atypical cells with frequent mitoses and scattered laminated calcifications (psammoma bodies). Diagnosis: high-grade serous carcinoma of the ovary.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C43-Q1","stem":"Sequencing of tumor DNA is performed. A mutation in which of the following genes is most likely to be found?","options":["KRAS","ARID1A","CTNNB1","TP53"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-13-J7","obj":"RE-13-J.12","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the subtype: high-grade serous carcinoma, the most common and most lethal ovarian cancer.","Recall that high-grade serous carcinoma carries TP53 mutations in nearly every case, and many also have BRCA1 or BRCA2 loss.","Match the other genes to other ovarian subtypes."],"why":"Almost all high-grade serous carcinomas carry TP53 mutations, which appear early, even in the precursor lesion. Many of these tumors are now thought to begin in the fimbria of the uterine tube as serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma and then seed the ovary and peritoneum. Women with germline BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutations, such as those with a family history like this patient's, have a much higher risk. Psammoma bodies, a high CA-125, ascites, and omental spread complete the classic picture.","whyNot":["KRAS mutations are typical of low-grade serous and mucinous tumors, which grow slowly from borderline precursors.","ARID1A loss is typical of clear cell and endometrioid carcinomas, which are often linked to endometriosis.","CTNNB1 (beta-catenin) mutations are typical of endometrioid carcinoma of the ovary.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular pathways of ovarian carcinoma","Tubal origin of serous carcinoma"],"comps":["RE-13-J7","RE-10"]}},{"id":"RE-C43-Q2","stem":"Which of the following ovarian tumors arises from the same category of cells as this patient's tumor?","options":["Dysgerminoma","Granulosa cell tumor","Brenner tumor","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J7","obj":"RE-13-J.11","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the patient's tumor: serous carcinoma is a surface epithelial tumor.","List the surface epithelial group: serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, and Brenner (transitional cell).","Sort the remaining choices into germ cell and sex cord-stromal tumors."],"why":"Ovarian tumors are grouped by the cells they come from. Surface epithelial tumors are the largest group and include most ovarian cancers in older women: serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, and Brenner tumors. Brenner tumors contain nests of transitional (urothelial-like) cells in a fibrous stroma and are usually benign. Germ cell tumors are most common in young women, and sex cord-stromal tumors often make hormones.","whyNot":["Dysgerminoma is a germ cell tumor, the ovarian counterpart of testicular seminoma.","Granulosa cell tumor is a sex cord-stromal tumor that often makes estrogen.","Correct.","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor is a sex cord-stromal tumor that often makes androgens."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of ovarian tumors"],"comps":["RE-13-J7"]}},{"id":"RE-C43-Q3","stem":"Before surgery, metastatic disease to both ovaries was considered. If an ovarian biopsy had instead shown cellular stroma infiltrated by mucin-filled cells with nuclei pushed to one side, the primary tumor would most likely have been in which of the following organs?","options":["Stomach","Urinary bladder","Kidney","Endometrium"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J7","obj":"RE-13-J.15","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize signet ring cells: mucin pushes the nucleus to the edge of the cell.","Signet ring cells in both ovaries define a Krukenberg tumor.","The classic primary is diffuse-type gastric adenocarcinoma."],"why":"A Krukenberg tumor is a metastasis to the ovaries, usually both, made of mucin-filled signet ring cells within a reactive, cellular ovarian stroma. The most common primary is diffuse-type adenocarcinoma of the stomach, followed by other gastrointestinal sites such as the colon and appendix. Bilateral ovarian masses should always raise the question of a metastasis. Finding one should prompt a search for the primary tumor.","whyNot":["Correct.","Bladder carcinoma is a urothelial tumor and a rare source of ovarian metastasis. It is not a classic source of signet ring cell metastases.","Renal cell carcinoma is made of clear cells full of glycogen and lipid, not mucin, and it rarely spreads to the ovary.","Endometrial carcinoma can spread to the ovaries, but it forms glands, not signet ring cells."],"study":{"topics":["Krukenberg tumor","Metastases to the ovary"],"comps":["RE-13-J7"]}},{"id":"RE-C43-Q4","stem":"Postoperative CT shows thrombus in the left ovarian vein extending to the point where this vein drains. Into which of the following vessels does the thrombus most likely extend?","options":["Left internal iliac vein","Left renal vein","Inferior vena cava","Left common iliac vein"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-5","obj":"RE-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the gonadal veins drain differently on the two sides.","The right ovarian vein enters the inferior vena cava directly; the left enters the left renal vein at a right angle.","A left ovarian vein thrombus therefore extends into the left renal vein."],"why":"The ovarian arteries arise from the abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries. The right ovarian vein drains straight into the inferior vena cava, but the left ovarian vein drains into the left renal vein. The same pattern in men explains why varicocele is more common on the left: the left testicular vein is longer, joins the renal vein at a right angle, and can be compressed where the left renal vein passes between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery.","whyNot":["The internal iliac vein drains the uterus, bladder, and rectum. The ovarian vein leaves the pelvis in the suspensory ligament and does not join it.","Correct.","The right ovarian vein drains into the inferior vena cava. The left one drains into the left renal vein.","The common iliac veins drain the pelvis and legs. The ovarian veins run up past them to the renal level."],"study":{"topics":["Gonadal vessels","Left-sided varicocele"],"comps":["RE-5"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C44","system":"RE","dx":"Complete hydatidiform mole with theca lutein cysts","codes":["RE-13-H2","RE-13-G1"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman, gravida 1, para 0, at 13 weeks' gestation by dates comes to the physician because of intermittent vaginal bleeding and severe nausea and vomiting for 2 weeks. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 154/100 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a uterus enlarged to the level of the umbilicus, consistent with about 20 weeks' gestation. No fetal heart tones are heard. Urinalysis shows 2+ protein. Serum beta-hCG is 480,000 mIU/mL, far above the range expected at 13 weeks. Ultrasound shows a uterus filled with a heterogeneous mass containing many small cystic spaces, no fetus, and bilateral multiloculated ovarian cysts 7 and 8 cm in diameter. Suction evacuation yields diffusely swollen villi with circumferential trophoblast proliferation. Diagnosis: complete hydatidiform mole with theca lutein cysts.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C44-Q1","stem":"The karyotype of the molar tissue is 46,XX, and immunostaining for p57 is absent in the villous cytotrophoblast. Which of the following best explains the absent p57 staining?","options":["Absence of any maternal chromosomes","Triploidy with one maternal haploid set","Maternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 11","Somatic deletion of the p57 gene in trophoblast"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-13-H2","obj":"RE-13-H.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that p57 (CDKN1C) is paternally imprinted, so only the maternal copy is expressed.","A complete mole forms when an ovum with no maternal nucleus is fertilized, usually by one sperm that duplicates to give 46,XX.","With no maternal genome, no active p57 gene exists, so the stain is negative."],"why":"In a complete mole, an ovum that has lost its own nucleus is fertilized, most often by one sperm that duplicates its DNA. The result is a 46,XX genome that is entirely paternal. The p57 gene is imprinted: the paternal copy is silenced and only the maternal copy is expressed. With no maternal chromosomes, the villous cytotrophoblast and stroma cannot make p57. A partial mole is triploid with one maternal set, so it stains positive, which is how pathologists tell the two apart.","whyNot":["Correct.","A triploid conceptus with one maternal set is a partial mole. The maternal copy of p57 is present, so its cells stain positive.","Maternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 11 gives two maternal copies of the region, which would increase p57 expression.","Molar trophoblast does not lose p57 by deletion. The absent stain reflects the missing maternal genome, which also fits the all-paternal 46,XX karyotype."],"study":{"topics":["Complete versus partial mole","Genomic imprinting"],"comps":["RE-13-H2","RE-10"]}},{"id":"RE-C44-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the hypertension and proteinuria in this patient at 13 weeks' gestation?","options":["Essential hypertension present before this pregnancy","Thyrotoxicosis from hCG stimulation of the thyroid gland","Early preeclampsia driven by excess abnormal trophoblast","Immune complex glomerulonephritis triggered by pregnancy"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-H2","obj":"RE-13-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: preeclampsia normally appears after 20 weeks.","Hypertension with proteinuria before 20 weeks should suggest a molar pregnancy.","The large mass of abnormal trophoblast drives the same endothelial injury as typical preeclampsia, only earlier."],"why":"Preeclampsia is new hypertension with proteinuria or organ damage after 20 weeks. When it appears earlier, a hydatidiform mole is the classic cause. The mole's large mass of abnormal trophoblast releases antiangiogenic factors such as soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1), which injure the maternal endothelium. This patient also has the other classic findings of a complete mole: bleeding, a uterus large for dates, very high hCG, hyperemesis, and theca lutein cysts.","whyNot":["Chronic hypertension would be present before pregnancy, and it would not explain new proteinuria arising with a molar pregnancy.","Very high hCG can stimulate the thyroid and raise systolic pressure, but hyperthyroidism does not cause proteinuria.","Correct.","Glomerulonephritis usually shows hematuria and red cell casts, and pregnancy is not a trigger for it. The molar pregnancy explains her findings."],"study":{"topics":["Clinical features of complete mole","Preeclampsia before 20 weeks"],"comps":["RE-13-H2","RE-13-H4"]}},{"id":"RE-C44-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains the ovarian cysts seen on ultrasound?","options":["Failure of a dominant follicle to rupture at ovulation","Luteinization of theca cells stimulated by excess hCG","Hemorrhage into a persistent corpus luteum","Endometrial tissue implanted on the ovarian surface"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-G1","obj":"RE-13-G.1","dok":2,"approach":["Notice the pattern: large, bilateral, multiloculated cysts with very high hCG.","hCG is structurally similar to LH and acts on LH receptors on theca cells.","Overstimulation makes the theca cells luteinize and the follicles fill with fluid."],"why":"Theca lutein cysts form when very high hCG, from a mole, choriocarcinoma, or multiple pregnancy, acts on LH receptors in both ovaries. The theca cells luteinize and the follicles swell into many fluid-filled cysts. Because they depend on hCG, the cysts shrink over weeks to months after the mole is evacuated and hCG falls. They can occasionally twist or rupture.","whyNot":["A follicle that fails to rupture forms a follicular cyst, which is usually single and one-sided and may make estrogen.","Correct.","Bleeding into a corpus luteum gives a single cyst that can rupture into the peritoneum. It does not give bilateral multiloculated cysts.","Endometrial tissue on the ovary forms an endometrioma filled with old blood (a chocolate cyst), linked to cyclic pain, not to high hCG."],"study":{"topics":["Theca lutein cysts","Functional ovarian cysts"],"comps":["RE-13-G1","RE-8"]}},{"id":"RE-C44-Q4","stem":"A partial mole, in contrast, usually forms when two sperm fertilize a single ovum. Which of the following events normally prevents a second sperm from fertilizing the ovum?","options":["Acrosomal enzyme release that digests the corona radiata","Capacitation of sperm in the female reproductive tract","Completion of the second meiotic division of the oocyte","Cortical granule release that alters the zona pellucida"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-3","obj":"RE-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the problem in a partial mole: two sperm entered one ovum (dispermy).","Recall the block to polyspermy that follows fusion of the first sperm with the oocyte.","A calcium wave makes cortical granules empty their enzymes, which changes the zona pellucida."],"why":"Fertilization usually takes place in the ampulla of the uterine tube. When the first sperm fuses with the oocyte membrane, a wave of calcium release triggers the cortical reaction. Cortical granules release enzymes that modify zona pellucida proteins, so no other sperm can bind or pass through. The same calcium signal prompts the oocyte to finish its second meiotic division and release the second polar body. If this block fails and two sperm enter, the result is a triploid conceptus such as a partial mole.","whyNot":["The acrosome reaction releases enzymes that help a sperm get through the corona radiata and zona pellucida. It helps fertilization and does not block extra sperm.","Capacitation in the female tract prepares sperm for the acrosome reaction. It happens before fertilization and does not prevent polyspermy.","Completion of meiosis II is triggered by sperm entry and forms the female pronucleus. It does not stop other sperm from entering.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Fertilization and the cortical reaction","Origin of partial mole"],"comps":["RE-3","RE-13-H2"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C45","system":"RE","dx":"Gestational choriocarcinoma after an invasive mole","codes":["RE-13-H1","RE-13-H3"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician because of cough with blood-streaked sputum for 2 weeks. Five months ago, she had suction evacuation of a complete hydatidiform mole. Her serum beta-hCG fell at first and then plateaued, and pelvic imaging 2 months ago showed molar tissue within the myometrium, but she did not return for treatment. She also reports palpitations and heat intolerance. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 112/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 132/76 mm Hg. A 2-cm dark red nodule is seen in the anterior vaginal wall. Serum beta-hCG is 260,000 mIU/mL (less than 5 in nonpregnant women). Chest CT shows multiple nodules in both lungs. Uterine curettage yields hemorrhagic, necrotic tissue with sheets of atypical cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast and no chorionic villi. Diagnosis: gestational choriocarcinoma after an invasive mole.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C45-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the early appearance of lung metastases in this patient?","options":["Vascular invasion by malignant trophoblast","Transcoelomic seeding across the peritoneal cavity","Lymphatic spread to pelvic and para-aortic nodes","Retrograde venous flow through the vertebral plexus"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-H1","obj":"RE-13-H.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall normal trophoblast behavior: it invades maternal spiral arteries to build the placenta.","Malignant trophoblast keeps this ability, so it enters the blood very early.","Uterine venous blood reaches the lungs first, so lung metastases are most common, followed by the vagina, brain, and liver."],"why":"Gestational choriocarcinoma is a malignant tumor of cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast with no chorionic villi. Like normal trophoblast, it invades blood vessels, so it spreads through the blood early, often while the uterine tumor is still small. Venous blood from the uterus reaches the lungs first, which is why lung nodules and hemoptysis are common at presentation. Metastases to the vagina, brain, and liver follow, and they bleed easily. The tumor can follow a mole, a miscarriage, an ectopic pregnancy, or a normal pregnancy, and it secretes very high hCG.","whyNot":["Correct.","Transcoelomic seeding across the peritoneum is the typical route of ovarian surface epithelial carcinoma. It does not explain lung nodules.","Lymphatic spread is the usual early route of carcinomas, such as cervical cancer. Choriocarcinoma spreads mainly through the blood.","Retrograde flow through the valveless vertebral venous plexus carries prostate cancer to the spine. It does not explain lung metastases."],"study":{"topics":["Gestational choriocarcinoma","Routes of metastasis"],"comps":["RE-13-H1"]}},{"id":"RE-C45-Q2","stem":"Two months before this visit, imaging showed molar tissue within the myometrium. Which of the following histologic findings most likely characterized that earlier lesion?","options":["Intermediate trophoblast forming a mass at the placental site","Hydropic villi penetrating into the myometrium","Sheets of trophoblast without villi invading the myometrium","Swollen villi confined to the endometrial cavity"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-H3","obj":"RE-13-H.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall what a plateau in hCG after evacuation means: molar tissue has persisted or invaded.","Molar tissue in the myometrium means the swollen villi have grown into the uterine wall, which defines an invasive mole.","Villi are still present, which separates invasive mole from choriocarcinoma."],"why":"An invasive mole is a mole whose hydropic villi grow into the myometrium and sometimes into its blood vessels. It can perforate the uterus and cause hemorrhage, and it keeps hCG elevated after evacuation. Chorionic villi are still present, which separates an invasive mole from choriocarcinoma, where only sheets of malignant trophoblast are seen, as in this patient's current lesion.","whyNot":["A mass of intermediate trophoblast at the implantation site is a placental site trophoblastic tumor. It makes only modest amounts of hCG and makes human placental lactogen.","Correct.","Sheets of malignant trophoblast without villi describe choriocarcinoma, the lesion she has now.","Swollen villi limited to the endometrial cavity describe the original complete mole, before it invaded the uterine wall."],"study":{"topics":["Invasive mole","Spectrum of gestational trophoblastic disease"],"comps":["RE-13-H3","RE-13-H1"]}},{"id":"RE-C45-Q3","stem":"The thyroid gland is not enlarged and has no nodules. Serum TSH is 0.05 μU/mL (0.4 to 4.0), free thyroxine is 3.1 ng/dL (0.8 to 1.8), and thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins are absent. Which of the following best explains her thyroid findings?","options":["Release of stored hormone from a destructive thyroiditis","Autonomous hormone production by a toxic thyroid nodule","Activation of TSH receptors by structurally similar hCG","Increased thyroxine-binding globulin from high estrogen"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-8","obj":"RE-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["Read the labs: suppressed TSH with high free T4 means primary hyperthyroidism.","With no stimulating antibodies, no nodule, and very high hCG, think of hCG acting on the thyroid.","hCG, LH, FSH, and TSH share an alpha subunit, and hCG is similar enough to TSH to activate the TSH receptor at high levels."],"why":"hCG, LH, FSH, and TSH are glycoprotein hormones with a common alpha subunit and a hormone-specific beta subunit. hCG is similar enough to TSH that, at very high levels, it binds and activates the TSH receptor on thyroid follicular cells. Gestational trophoblastic disease can produce hCG levels high enough to cause clinical hyperthyroidism, and the high T4 then suppresses pituitary TSH. The hyperthyroidism resolves as hCG falls with treatment.","whyNot":["Destructive thyroiditis releases stored hormone for a few weeks, but it has no link to a very high hCG, which explains her findings directly.","A toxic nodule can cause this lab pattern, but her gland has no nodules, and her very high hCG accounts for the thyroid stimulation.","Correct.","High estrogen raises thyroxine-binding globulin and total T4, but free T4 and TSH stay normal."],"study":{"topics":["Glycoprotein hormone structure","hCG-induced hyperthyroidism"],"comps":["RE-8","RE-13-H1"]}},{"id":"RE-C45-Q4","stem":"Because this tumor arose from a complete mole, its cells carry only paternal chromosomes and are fully foreign to the mother. Which of the following properties of trophoblast normally limits attack by maternal T cells and natural killer cells?","options":["Secretion of IgG that blocks maternal T cell receptors","Loss of all HLA class I molecules from the cell surface","Expression of HLA-G in place of HLA-A and HLA-B","High expression of HLA class II molecules"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-11","obj":"RE-11.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that cells lacking class I MHC are killed by NK cells (the missing-self response).","Trophoblast avoids T cell attack by not displaying the highly polymorphic HLA-A and HLA-B molecules.","It avoids NK cell attack by displaying HLA-G, a nonclassical class I molecule that engages inhibitory NK receptors."],"why":"The fetus carries paternal antigens, so it is semiallogeneic to the mother. Trophoblast does not express the highly polymorphic HLA-A and HLA-B molecules or class II molecules, so maternal T cells have little foreign HLA to recognize. It does express HLA-G, a nonclassical class I molecule with very little variation between people. HLA-G binds inhibitory receptors on uterine NK cells and prevents the missing-self response that would otherwise kill a cell lacking class I.","whyNot":["Trophoblast does not make antibodies. Only plasma cells secrete immunoglobulin.","Cells that lose all class I molecules become targets for NK cells, which kill cells missing self. Trophoblast avoids this by keeping HLA-G.","Correct.","Class II molecules present antigen to CD4 T cells and would increase recognition. Trophoblast lacks class II."],"study":{"topics":["Maternal-fetal immune tolerance","HLA-G and NK cells"],"comps":["RE-11"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C46","system":"RE","dx":"Gestational diabetes with polyhydramnios and fetal macrosomia","codes":["RE-13-H"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman, gravida 2, para 1, at 32 weeks' gestation comes for a prenatal visit. Her hemoglobin A1c at 8 weeks was 5.2% (less than 5.7). At 26 weeks, a 50-g glucose challenge test and a follow-up 100-g oral glucose tolerance test were abnormal, and gestational diabetes was diagnosed. She has had difficulty following her diet. Fasting plasma glucose today is 118 mg/dL (target less than 95 in pregnancy). Fundal height is 38 cm (expected about 32 cm). Ultrasound shows an estimated fetal weight above the 97th percentile, a large fetal abdominal circumference, normal fetal anatomy, and an amniotic fluid index of 29 cm (about 5 to 24). Diagnosis: gestational diabetes with polyhydramnios and fetal macrosomia.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C46-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the fetal overgrowth?","options":["Human placental lactogen acting directly on fetal tissue","Fetal growth hormone excess from pituitary stimulation","Transfer of maternal insulin across the placenta to the fetus","Fetal insulin secreted in response to maternal glucose"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-9","obj":"RE-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what crosses the placenta: glucose does, by facilitated diffusion through GLUT1, and maternal insulin does not.","High maternal glucose means high fetal glucose, so the fetal pancreas makes more insulin.","Insulin is a major fetal growth hormone, so fat and organ mass increase."],"why":"Glucose crosses the placenta by facilitated diffusion down its concentration gradient, so fetal glucose follows maternal glucose. Maternal insulin is too large to cross. The fetal beta cells, active from about the end of the first trimester, respond to the high glucose with hyperplasia and high insulin output. Fetal insulin acts as an anabolic growth factor, increasing fat, liver, and heart mass and causing macrosomia. After birth, the maternal glucose supply stops while insulin stays high, so the newborn is at risk of hypoglycemia.","whyNot":["Human placental lactogen acts on the mother, raising her insulin resistance and so her glucose. It does not drive fetal growth directly.","Fetal growth in late pregnancy depends mainly on insulin and insulin-like growth factors. Pituitary growth hormone plays a minor role.","Maternal insulin does not cross the placenta in meaningful amounts. Fetal insulin comes only from the fetal pancreas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Placental glucose transfer","Infant of a diabetic mother"],"comps":["RE-9","RE-13-H"]}},{"id":"RE-C46-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains the increased amniotic fluid volume?","options":["Fetal osmotic diuresis that increases urine output","Impaired fetal swallowing from esophageal atresia","Reduced fetal urine output from renal agenesis","Transudation across the amnion from maternal edema"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-H","obj":"RE-13-H.9","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the balance: fetal urine adds amniotic fluid, and fetal swallowing removes it.","Fetal hyperglycemia exceeds renal tubular reabsorption, so glucose pulls water into the urine.","More fetal urine means more amniotic fluid; the normal anatomy scan argues against a swallowing defect."],"why":"In the second half of pregnancy, most amniotic fluid is fetal urine, and fetal swallowing removes it. When the fetus is hyperglycemic, glucose spills into the urine and causes an osmotic diuresis, so fluid builds up. Polyhydramnios also follows anything that prevents swallowing, such as esophageal atresia or anencephaly, but this fetus has normal anatomy. Oligohydramnios is the opposite pattern and comes from low urine output, as in bilateral renal agenesis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Esophageal atresia causes polyhydramnios because the fetus cannot swallow fluid, but the ultrasound showed normal anatomy, and maternal diabetes explains the finding.","Renal agenesis stops fetal urine production and causes oligohydramnios with Potter sequence, the opposite of this finding.","Maternal edema does not move fluid into the amniotic cavity. Amniotic volume depends mainly on fetal urine and swallowing."],"study":{"topics":["Amniotic fluid balance","Polyhydramnios and oligohydramnios"],"comps":["RE-13-H"]}},{"id":"RE-C46-Q3","stem":"Infants of mothers with poorly controlled diabetes before conception have higher rates of congenital heart defects and caudal regression. Which of the following best explains why this patient's fetus does not share that higher risk of major malformations?","options":["Placental glucose transport begins only after 20 weeks","Her hyperglycemia began after organogenesis was complete","Maternal insulin protects the embryo early in gestation","The embryonic pancreas buffers glucose during weeks 3 to 8"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-3","obj":"RE-3.6","dok":3,"approach":["Use her early A1c: it was normal at 8 weeks, so her glucose was normal during early development.","Recall that organs form in the embryonic period, weeks 3 to 8 after fertilization.","Gestational diabetes appears in the second half of pregnancy, after organogenesis, so it affects growth more than structure."],"why":"The embryonic period runs from gastrulation in week 3, when the trilaminar disc forms, to the end of week 8, when the organ primordia have been laid down. Teratogens act most strongly in this window because a disruption can derail a whole organ system. Maternal hyperglycemia during these weeks, as in poorly controlled pregestational diabetes, raises the risk of heart defects, neural tube defects, and caudal regression. Gestational diabetes develops later, as placental hormones raise insulin resistance, so its effects fall mainly on fetal size and metabolism.","whyNot":["Glucose passes from mother to embryo from the earliest weeks of pregnancy, so an embryo exposed to maternal hyperglycemia during organogenesis is affected.","Correct.","Maternal insulin does not cross the placenta, so it cannot protect the embryo directly.","The fetal pancreas begins secreting insulin only near the end of the first trimester, and it cannot shield the embryo from maternal glucose."],"study":{"topics":["Embryonic period and teratogen sensitivity","Pregestational versus gestational diabetes"],"comps":["RE-3","RE-9"]}},{"id":"RE-C46-Q4","stem":"At her first visit, she reported stopping oral isotretinoin for acne 3 months before conception. Exposure to this drug during early pregnancy is most likely to cause which of the following patterns of defects?","options":["Phocomelia and other limb reduction defects of the arms","Discolored teeth and impaired fetal long bone growth","Ebstein anomaly with downward tricuspid valve displacement","Craniofacial, heart, and central nervous system defects"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-9","obj":"RE-9.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize isotretinoin as a vitamin A derivative (a retinoid).","Recall that retinoic acid sets HOX gene patterns, especially in the hindbrain and neural crest.","Excess retinoid disrupts structures that depend on neural crest: the face and ears, the cardiac outflow tract, and the central nervous system."],"why":"Isotretinoin and excess vitamin A act through retinoic acid receptors that control HOX gene expression along the body axis. Cranial neural crest cells are especially sensitive, so exposure causes small or absent ears, cleft palate and other facial defects, conotruncal heart defects, and brain malformations such as hydrocephalus. Because the drug is so teratogenic, people who can become pregnant must use reliable contraception during treatment and for 1 month afterward.","whyNot":["Limb reduction defects such as phocomelia are the classic effect of thalidomide.","Tooth discoloration and impaired bone growth are linked to tetracyclines, which bind calcium in forming teeth and bone.","Ebstein anomaly is linked to lithium exposure in the first trimester.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin A and retinoid teratogenicity","Classic teratogens"],"comps":["RE-9","RE-3"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C47","system":"RE","dx":"Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia grade 3 (HSIL) in a woman exposed to DES in utero, with clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina as the related risk","codes":["RE-13-J1","RE-13-K6","RE-13-J10"],"case":"A 56-year-old woman comes to the physician for follow-up of an abnormal cervical screening test. Her mother took diethylstilbestrol (DES) during the pregnancy with her. She smokes one pack of cigarettes daily. Cervical cytology shows a high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion, and testing is positive for HPV 16. Colposcopy shows a dense acetowhite lesion at the transformation zone and patches of red glandular mucosa in the upper vagina (vaginal adenosis). Cervical biopsy shows atypical, immature basaloid cells with a high nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio occupying the full thickness of the squamous epithelium, with an intact basement membrane. Diagnosis: cervical intraepithelial neoplasia grade 3 (HSIL) in a woman exposed to DES in utero.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C47-Q1","stem":"Eight years ago, a cervical biopsy showed koilocytes in the upper layers of the epithelium, with atypical immature cells limited to the lower third. Which of the following was the most likely classification of that earlier lesion?","options":["CIN 2, high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion","CIN 1, low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion","CIN 3, high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion","Microinvasive squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J1","obj":"RE-13-J.1","dok":3,"approach":["Grade CIN by how much of the epithelial thickness is replaced by atypical immature cells.","Lower third is CIN 1, up to two thirds is CIN 2, and more than two thirds or full thickness is CIN 3.","Koilocytes in the upper layers reflect productive HPV infection, typical of low-grade lesions."],"why":"High-risk HPV infects immature basal cells at the transformation zone. In CIN 1, atypical immature cells fill only the lower third of the epithelium, and the upper layers still mature and show koilocytes, cells with wrinkled nuclei and a clear halo around the nucleus from viral replication. CIN 1 is reported as a low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (LSIL). As the atypical cells extend higher, the lesion becomes CIN 2 and then CIN 3, which together are called HSIL, as she has now.","whyNot":["CIN 2 means atypical cells extend into the middle third of the epithelium.","Correct.","CIN 3 means atypical cells fill more than two thirds or the full thickness of the epithelium, as in her current biopsy.","Microinvasive carcinoma requires tumor cells breaking through the basement membrane into the stroma."],"study":{"topics":["CIN grading","LSIL and HSIL"],"comps":["RE-13-J1","RE-13-K6"]}},{"id":"RE-C47-Q2","stem":"Lesional cells contain integrated HPV 16 DNA with high expression of the viral E7 protein. Which of the following effects of E7 most directly drives these cells into S phase?","options":["Binding of Rb, which releases E2F transcription factors","Promoter methylation that silences the p16 gene","Constitutive activation of RAS by a point mutation","Ubiquitin-mediated degradation of the p53 tumor suppressor"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-10","obj":"RE-10.3","dok":3,"approach":["Separate the two HPV oncoproteins: E6 targets p53, and E7 targets Rb.","Rb normally holds E2F inactive until cyclin D-CDK4/6 phosphorylates it.","E7 binding frees E2F, so S-phase genes turn on without growth signals."],"why":"Hypophosphorylated Rb binds E2F and blocks the G1 to S transition. High-risk HPV E7 binds Rb and releases E2F, which turns on the genes needed for DNA synthesis. E6 adds to this by tagging p53 for ubiquitin-mediated breakdown, so damaged cells neither arrest nor undergo apoptosis. Integration of viral DNA often disrupts the E2 regulatory gene and raises E6 and E7 expression. Because Rb is inactive, the cells overexpress p16, which pathologists use as a stain for high-risk HPV lesions.","whyNot":["Correct.","In HPV lesions, p16 is strongly overexpressed because Rb is inactive, so silencing of p16 is not part of this pathway.","RAS point mutations drive pancreatic, colon, and lung cancers. HPV oncoproteins do not act through RAS mutation.","Degradation of p53 is the action of E6. It removes the DNA damage checkpoint and apoptosis."],"study":{"topics":["HPV E6 and E7","Rb and the G1 to S checkpoint"],"comps":["RE-10","RE-13-K6"]}},{"id":"RE-C47-Q3","stem":"Her 11-year-old granddaughter is scheduled to receive the HPV vaccine. Which of the following best explains how this vaccine prevents infection?","options":["Cytotoxic T cells that kill cells expressing E6 and E7","Neutralizing antibody to L1 virus-like particles","Mucosal IgA from replication of live attenuated virus","Antibodies to the E2 protein that block DNA integration"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-11","obj":"RE-11.6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the vaccine content: the L1 major capsid protein, which self-assembles into virus-like particles with no viral DNA.","A noninfectious particle vaccine works mainly through antibodies.","High-titer neutralizing IgG reaches the genital mucosa and blocks virions before they infect basal cells."],"why":"HPV vaccines contain the L1 capsid protein, which assembles into virus-like particles that look like the virus but carry no DNA. They induce high levels of neutralizing IgG that reaches the cervical mucosa and blocks infection of basal cells at sites of small injuries. The vaccine prevents new infection but does not clear an established one. Once infection occurs, cell-mediated immunity clears most HPV infections and low-grade lesions within about 1 to 2 years, which is why people with weak T cell immunity, such as those with HIV, have more persistent infection and more cervical cancer.","whyNot":["T cell responses against E6 and E7 are the goal of therapeutic vaccines under study. Current preventive vaccines contain only L1 and do not target infected cells.","Correct.","HPV vaccines are not live. Virus-like particles contain no viral genome and cannot replicate.","E2 is a viral regulatory protein made inside infected cells and is not in the vaccine. Antibodies cannot reach it to block integration."],"study":{"topics":["HPV vaccine","Immune clearance of HPV"],"comps":["RE-11","RE-13-K6"]}},{"id":"RE-C47-Q4","stem":"Which of the following malignancies can arise from the glandular epithelium of her vaginal adenosis?","options":["Malignant melanoma","Keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma","Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma","Clear cell adenocarcinoma"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J10","obj":"RE-13-J.20","dok":2,"approach":["Link DES exposure in utero to vaginal adenosis: Mullerian glandular tissue left in the upper vagina.","Recall the malignancy that arises from this glandular tissue: clear cell adenocarcinoma.","Match the other vaginal tumors to their usual settings."],"why":"DES was given to pregnant women in the United States from about 1940 to 1971. It disrupted the normal replacement of Mullerian glandular epithelium by squamous epithelium in the developing vagina, so many exposed daughters have vaginal adenosis, patches of glandular tissue in the upper vagina. A small fraction develop clear cell adenocarcinoma from this tissue, with peak risk in young adulthood. Vaginal squamous cell carcinoma is mostly HPV-related and arises from vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia in older women, and embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma is a grape-like mass in young girls.","whyNot":["Vaginal melanoma arises from melanocytes, is rare, and is not linked to DES.","Vaginal squamous cell carcinoma arises from squamous epithelium through HPV-related vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia, not from glandular adenosis.","Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma (sarcoma botryoides) is a grape-like, desmin-positive tumor of skeletal muscle origin in young girls. It is not related to DES.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["DES exposure and vaginal adenosis","Vaginal neoplasms"],"comps":["RE-13-J10","RE-3"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C48","system":"RE","dx":"Invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix","codes":["RE-13-J4"],"case":"A 47-year-old woman comes to the physician because of bleeding after intercourse and a foul-smelling vaginal discharge for 4 months, and bilateral flank pain for 2 weeks. She has HIV infection and has not had cervical screening for 15 years. She has smoked for 30 years. Pelvic examination shows a friable, exophytic cervical mass that extends through both parametria to the pelvic sidewalls. Serum creatinine is 2.9 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1), urea nitrogen is 48 mg/dL (7 to 20), and calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5). CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 210/μL (500 to 1500). Ultrasound shows bilateral hydronephrosis. Biopsy shows nests of keratinizing malignant squamous cells invading the cervical stroma. Diagnosis: invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C48-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains this patient's kidney dysfunction?","options":["Collapsing glomerulopathy from HIV infection","Hypercalcemia from tumor-secreted PTH-related peptide","Ureteral compression by tumor in the parametria","Immune complex glomerulonephritis from tumor antigens"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J4","obj":"RE-13-J.3","dok":3,"approach":["Pair the high creatinine with bilateral hydronephrosis: this is postrenal (obstructive) kidney failure.","Cervical cancer spreads laterally into the parametria, where the ureters pass close to the cervix.","Her normal calcium and the imaging rule out the other causes."],"why":"Invasive cervical carcinoma grows directly into the parametrial tissue beside the cervix. The ureters run through this tissue about 1 to 2 cm lateral to the cervix, so lateral spread can compress both of them. The result is bilateral hydronephrosis and postrenal kidney failure, a major cause of death in advanced cervical cancer. Bleeding after intercourse, from a friable tumor surface, is the classic early symptom.","whyNot":["HIV-associated nephropathy causes heavy proteinuria and large, echogenic kidneys. It does not cause hydronephrosis.","Squamous cell carcinomas can secrete PTH-related peptide, but her calcium is normal.","Correct.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis causes hematuria and proteinuria with normal collecting systems. It does not explain hydronephrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Spread of cervical carcinoma","Postrenal kidney failure"],"comps":["RE-13-J4","RE-5"]}},{"id":"RE-C48-Q2","stem":"HPV genotyping of the tumor is performed. Which of the following patterns of infection most likely led to this cancer?","options":["Transient infection with HPV 16","Persistent infection with HPV 11","Persistent infection with HPV 6","Persistent infection with HPV 16"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-J1","obj":"RE-13-J.2","dok":3,"approach":["Separate high-risk types (16, 18, and others) from low-risk types (6 and 11), which cause warts.","Most HPV infections, even with high-risk types, clear within 1 to 2 years.","Cancer needs a high-risk type plus years of persistence, which her HIV infection and smoking promote."],"why":"HPV 16 and 18 cause about 70% of cervical cancers. Most infections, including those with high-risk types, are cleared, and most CIN 1 regresses on its own. Cancer develops when a high-risk infection persists for years, which allows viral integration and steady E6 and E7 expression. Risk rises with early first intercourse, many sexual partners, smoking, and immunodeficiency such as HIV. Her lack of screening meant precursor lesions were never found and treated.","whyNot":["A transient HPV 16 infection is cleared by the immune system and does not lead to cancer. Persistence is required.","HPV 11, like HPV 6, is a low-risk type linked to genital warts and laryngeal papillomas, not cervical cancer.","HPV 6 is a low-risk type that causes genital warts. Its E6 and E7 proteins bind p53 and Rb only weakly.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["High-risk versus low-risk HPV","Risk factors for cervical neoplasia"],"comps":["RE-13-J1","RE-13-K6"]}},{"id":"RE-C48-Q3","stem":"Two weeks later, she has heavy bleeding from the tumor. If the uterine artery were ligated close to the cervix to control the bleeding, which of the following structures would be at greatest risk of injury?","options":["Ureter","Internal pudendal artery","Inferior epigastric artery","Obturator nerve"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-5","obj":"RE-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the course of the uterine artery: from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery, medially through the base of the broad ligament.","About 1 to 2 cm lateral to the cervix, it crosses above the ureter (water under the bridge).","A clamp placed here can catch the ureter."],"why":"The uterine artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and runs medially in the base of the broad ligament toward the cervix. Near the lateral fornix, about 1 to 2 cm from the cervix, it passes above the ureter, which is heading forward to the bladder. A clamp or ligature placed here can cut, crush, or tie off the ureter, so surgeons identify the ureter before ligating the uterine vessels. The same closeness explains why parametrial tumor obstructs the ureters.","whyNot":["Correct.","The internal pudendal artery leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen to supply the perineum. It does not lie beside the uterine artery at the cervix.","The inferior epigastric artery arises from the external iliac artery and runs up the anterior abdominal wall.","The obturator nerve runs along the lateral pelvic wall to the obturator canal, well away from the uterine artery near the cervix."],"study":{"topics":["Uterine artery and ureter","Internal iliac artery branches"],"comps":["RE-5","RE-4"]}},{"id":"RE-C48-Q4","stem":"The tumor arose at the cervical transformation zone. Which of the following epithelial changes at this site creates the cell population most vulnerable to HPV-driven neoplasia?","options":["Atrophy of nonkeratinized ectocervical epithelium","Glandular metaplasia of ectocervical squamous epithelium","Squamous metaplasia of endocervical columnar epithelium","Keratinization of the stratified squamous vaginal lining"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-2","obj":"RE-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the two linings: simple columnar in the endocervix, nonkeratinized stratified squamous on the ectocervix and vagina.","In the acidic vagina after puberty, exposed columnar epithelium is replaced by squamous epithelium (squamous metaplasia).","Immature metaplastic cells in this zone are the main target of HPV."],"why":"The endocervical canal is lined by mucus-secreting simple columnar epithelium, and the ectocervix and vagina by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium. At puberty and in pregnancy, columnar epithelium is pushed out onto the ectocervix, where the acidic environment drives reserve cells to replace it with squamous epithelium. This area of ongoing squamous metaplasia is the transformation zone. Its immature, dividing metaplastic cells are especially vulnerable to high-risk HPV, so nearly all CIN and cervical carcinoma begin here.","whyNot":["Atrophy after menopause thins the squamous lining but does not create the immature metaplastic cells that HPV targets.","Glandular metaplasia of squamous epithelium is the reverse of what happens at the transformation zone.","Correct.","The vaginal lining is normally nonkeratinized, and keratinization, as with prolapse, is not the source of cervical carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Epithelium of the female tract","Cervical transformation zone"],"comps":["RE-2","RE-13-J1"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C49","system":"RE","dx":"Uterine leiomyoma with heavy menstrual bleeding and iron deficiency anemia","codes":["RE-13-J3","RE-13-J5"],"case":"A 36-year-old Black woman comes to the physician because of heavy menstrual bleeding with clots, pelvic pressure, and urinary frequency for 2 years. She and her partner have been trying to conceive for 18 months. Her cycles are regular, and a midluteal progesterone level confirms ovulation. Hysterosalpingography shows patent uterine tubes and a filling defect that distorts the uterine cavity. Pelvic examination shows an irregularly enlarged uterus the size of a 14-week gestation. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL (12.0 to 15.5), mean corpuscular volume is 72 fL (80 to 100), and ferritin is 6 ng/mL (15 to 150). Ultrasound shows several well-circumscribed hypoechoic myometrial masses, including a 3-cm submucosal mass. The larger masses are removed by myomectomy and are firm, white, and whorled on cut section. Diagnosis: uterine leiomyomas with heavy menstrual bleeding and iron deficiency anemia.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C49-Q1","stem":"Several small intramural leiomyomas were left in place. Which of the following best predicts their course after menopause?","options":["Shrinkage as ovarian estrogen production falls","Continued growth driven by rising gonadotropin levels","Progression to diffuse adenomyosis of the myometrium","Dystrophic calcification followed by renewed rapid growth"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J3","obj":"RE-13-J.7","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that leiomyomas are benign smooth muscle tumors whose growth depends on estrogen and progesterone.","Ovarian hormone production falls after menopause.","So fibroids usually stop growing and shrink."],"why":"Leiomyomas are benign, well-circumscribed clonal tumors of myometrial smooth muscle with a whorled cut surface. Their cells carry more estrogen and progesterone receptors than normal myometrium, so they grow during the reproductive years, may enlarge in pregnancy, and usually shrink after menopause. They are more common, and appear earlier, in Black women. A uterine mass that grows after menopause should raise concern for a different tumor.","whyNot":["Correct.","FSH and LH rise after menopause, but fibroid growth depends on estrogen and progesterone, not gonadotropins.","Adenomyosis is endometrial tissue within the myometrium, a separate condition. Leiomyomas do not turn into it.","Old fibroids often calcify after menopause, but calcified fibroids do not start growing again."],"study":{"topics":["Leiomyoma hormone dependence","Clinical features of fibroids"],"comps":["RE-13-J3","RE-13-A4"]}},{"id":"RE-C49-Q2","stem":"Her 63-year-old aunt was recently found to have a single, rapidly enlarging uterine mass 3 years after menopause. Which of the following histologic findings would most strongly indicate leiomyosarcoma rather than leiomyoma?","options":["Hyaline degeneration with dystrophic calcification","Tumor cell necrosis, marked atypia, and many mitoses","Whorled bundles of uniform spindle cells, rare mitoses","Strong nuclear estrogen receptor and desmin staining"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J5","obj":"RE-13-J.8","dok":3,"approach":["Note the setting: one large, growing mass in a postmenopausal woman, unlike multiple fibroids that shrink.","Leiomyosarcoma is diagnosed by a combination of tumor cell necrosis, nuclear atypia, and a high mitotic rate.","It arises de novo and does not come from a preexisting fibroid."],"why":"Leiomyosarcoma is a malignant smooth muscle tumor that usually arises de novo in perimenopausal or postmenopausal women. It typically forms a single, large, soft, poorly circumscribed mass with hemorrhage and necrosis. Under the microscope, the key features are coagulative tumor cell necrosis, marked nuclear atypia, and a high mitotic rate. Leiomyomas, by contrast, are usually multiple, firm, and well circumscribed, and they do not transform into leiomyosarcoma.","whyNot":["Hyaline degeneration and calcification are common benign changes in older leiomyomas.","Correct.","Whorled bundles of uniform spindle cells with rare mitoses describe an ordinary leiomyoma.","Both leiomyomas and leiomyosarcomas are smooth muscle tumors, so both can stain for desmin and express estrogen receptors. Staining does not separate them."],"study":{"topics":["Leiomyosarcoma versus leiomyoma"],"comps":["RE-13-J5","RE-13-J3"]}},{"id":"RE-C49-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains this patient's infertility?","options":["Intrauterine adhesions after uterine curettage","Chronic anovulation from excess prolactin secretion","Cavity distortion that impairs embryo implantation","Tubal blockage from scarring after pelvic infection"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"RE-13-A","obj":"RE-13-A.9","dok":3,"approach":["Classify infertility as ovulatory, tubal, or uterine.","She ovulates and her tubes are patent, so the cause is uterine.","A submucosal leiomyoma bulges into the cavity and disrupts the endometrium where the embryo must implant."],"why":"Female infertility is grouped into ovulatory causes (PCOS, hyperprolactinemia, ovarian insufficiency), tubal causes (scarring from prior pelvic inflammatory disease), and uterine causes. This patient has regular ovulatory cycles and patent tubes, so the problem is uterine. A submucosal leiomyoma distorts the cavity and the overlying endometrium and interferes with implantation, and it also causes heavy bleeding. Removing it often improves fertility.","whyNot":["Intrauterine adhesions (Asherman syndrome) follow curettage and cause scant or absent menses, the opposite of her heavy bleeding.","Hyperprolactinemia suppresses GnRH and causes anovulation, but she has regular cycles with confirmed ovulation.","Correct.","Tubal scarring from pelvic inflammatory disease blocks egg and sperm transport, but her tubes are patent."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of female infertility","Submucosal leiomyoma"],"comps":["RE-13-A","RE-13-J3"]}},{"id":"RE-C49-Q4","stem":"After myomectomy and iron replacement, she conceives. At 24 weeks' gestation, her hemoglobin has fallen to 10.8 g/dL. Which of the following combinations of findings would best indicate that this fall reflects normal plasma volume expansion rather than recurrent iron deficiency?","options":["Raised MCV and normal ferritin","Reduced MCV and normal ferritin","Normal MCV and normal ferritin","Reduced MCV and reduced ferritin"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"RE-9","obj":"RE-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that plasma volume rises about 40 to 50% in pregnancy, more than red cell mass, so hemoglobin falls by dilution.","Dilution lowers concentration but does not change red cell size or iron stores.","Iron deficiency lowers ferritin first and then produces small cells (low MCV)."],"why":"In pregnancy, plasma volume expands more than red cell mass, so hemoglobin concentration falls even when iron supply is adequate. This physiologic dilution leaves red cells normal in size and iron stores intact, so MCV and ferritin stay normal. Pregnancy needs roughly 1 g of extra iron for the larger maternal red cell mass, the fetus, and the placenta, so iron deficiency is common, especially after heavy menstrual losses like hers. Iron deficiency shows a low ferritin first and a low MCV as it advances.","whyNot":["Large red cells with normal ferritin suggest folate or vitamin B12 deficiency.","Small red cells with normal iron stores point to thalassemia trait or another cause, not dilution.","Correct.","Low ferritin with small red cells is the pattern of iron deficiency."],"study":{"topics":["Iron needs in pregnancy","Physiologic anemia of pregnancy"],"comps":["RE-9","RE-6"]}}]},{"id":"RE-C50","system":"RE","dx":"Vulvar squamous cell carcinoma arising in lichen sclerosus","codes":["RE-13-J11","RE-13-J12"],"case":"A 74-year-old woman comes to the physician because of vulvar itching for many years and a painful sore on the vulva for 3 months. Physical examination shows thin, white, wrinkled, parchment-like plaques over the labia minora and perianal skin, with narrowing of the introitus. A 2-cm ulcerated, indurated mass is present on the right labium majus, and a firm 2-cm right inguinal lymph node is palpable. A biopsy of the mass shows invasive keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma with keratin pearls. Diagnosis: vulvar squamous cell carcinoma arising in lichen sclerosus.","questions":[{"id":"RE-C50-Q1","stem":"Molecular studies are performed on the tumor and the adjacent epithelium. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Integrated HPV 16 DNA with diffuse p16 staining","Activating KRAS mutation in basal keratinocytes","Loss of mismatch repair protein MLH1","TP53 mutation with no high-risk HPV DNA"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J11","obj":"RE-13-J.21","dok":3,"approach":["Note the setting: an older woman with long-standing lichen sclerosus.","Vulvar carcinoma follows two pathways: HPV-related (younger women, usual VIN) and HPV-independent (older women, lichen sclerosus, differentiated VIN).","The HPV-independent pathway is driven by TP53 mutation."],"why":"Most vulvar carcinomas in older women arise without HPV, in skin damaged by lichen sclerosus or other chronic inflammation. The precursor is differentiated VIN, which often carries TP53 mutations. HPV-related vulvar carcinoma occurs in younger women, arises from usual (HPV-associated) VIN, contains integrated high-risk HPV, and stains strongly for p16. HPV-independent tumors tend to have a worse prognosis.","whyNot":["Integrated HPV 16 with strong p16 staining is the profile of HPV-related vulvar cancer, which affects younger women and arises from usual VIN.","KRAS mutations drive pancreatic, colon, and lung cancers. They are not a defining step in either vulvar carcinoma pathway.","Mismatch repair loss is the pathway of Lynch syndrome tumors, such as colon and endometrial cancer, and is not the usual vulvar pathway.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Two pathways of vulvar carcinoma","Differentiated VIN"],"comps":["RE-13-J11","RE-13-J12"]}},{"id":"RE-C50-Q2","stem":"Before the mass appeared, her physician had considered extramammary Paget disease as a cause of the itching. If a vulvar biopsy had shown large pale cells with mucin-rich cytoplasm scattered through the epidermis, which of the following would have been the most likely interpretation?","options":["Intraepithelial adenocarcinoma, usually with no underlying tumor","HPV-infected koilocytes of usual vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia","Malignant melanocytes confined to the epidermal basal layer","Ductal carcinoma cells spreading up from an invasive tumor"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"RE-13-J12","obj":"RE-13-J.22","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize Paget cells: large pale cells with mucin, spread singly and in clusters through the epidermis.","In the breast, Paget disease of the nipple almost always signals an underlying carcinoma.","On the vulva, extramammary Paget disease is usually an intraepithelial adenocarcinoma with no underlying tumor."],"why":"Extramammary Paget disease of the vulva is an adenocarcinoma confined to the epidermis. It presents as a red, scaly, itchy plaque. Paget disease of the nipple nearly always marks an underlying ductal carcinoma of the breast, but vulvar Paget disease usually has no underlying invasive tumor. Its mucin-containing Paget cells separate it from melanoma in situ and from vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Usual VIN shows immature basaloid cells with koilocytes, not large mucin-filled cells.","Melanoma in situ shows atypical melanocytes that lack mucin and stain for melanocytic markers such as S100.","Paget cells spreading from an underlying ductal carcinoma describe Paget disease of the nipple."],"study":{"topics":["Extramammary Paget disease","Usual versus differentiated VIN"],"comps":["RE-13-J12"]}},{"id":"RE-C50-Q3","stem":"A biopsy is taken from one of the white plaques. Which of the following histologic findings best accounts for their thin, parchment-like appearance?","options":["Absent melanocytes in a normal epidermis","Thickened epidermis with marked surface hyperkeratosis","Noncaseating granulomas in the superficial dermis","Thinned epidermis over homogenized dermal collagen"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"RE-13-B","obj":"RE-13-B.10","dok":2,"approach":["Match appearance to histology: thin, white, wrinkled skin means a thin epidermis.","The pale color and stiffness come from dense, hyalinized collagen in the upper dermis.","A band of lymphocytes often lies below this layer."],"why":"Lichen sclerosus thins the epidermis, flattens the rete ridges, and replaces the upper dermis with pale, homogenized, fibrotic collagen above a band of lymphocytes. This produces white, parchment-like plaques that can shrink the labia and narrow the introitus. It occurs mostly in postmenopausal women, likely has an autoimmune basis, and carries a small increased risk of vulvar squamous cell carcinoma, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Loss of melanocytes is vitiligo, which makes white skin of normal texture and thickness.","A thickened epidermis with hyperkeratosis describes lichen simplex chronicus (squamous hyperplasia), caused by chronic rubbing and scratching.","Noncaseating granulomas suggest Crohn disease or sarcoidosis involving the vulva.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lichen sclerosus","Non-neoplastic vulvar disorders"],"comps":["RE-13-B","RE-13-J11"]}},{"id":"RE-C50-Q4","stem":"The initial differential diagnosis of her vulvar mass included a large condyloma. Which of the following findings would identify condyloma acuminatum rather than condyloma latum?","options":["Flat, moist, broad-based papules","Koilocytes from HPV 6 or 11 infection","Spirochetes seen on dark-field microscopy","Reactive serum rapid plasma reagin test"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"RE-13-K6","obj":"RE-13-K.8","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the two condylomas by cause: acuminatum from HPV, latum from secondary syphilis.","HPV 6 and 11 produce koilocytes in a raised, papillary wart.","Condyloma latum is full of treponemes and comes with positive syphilis serology."],"why":"Condyloma acuminatum is a benign, raised, cauliflower-like wart caused mainly by low-risk HPV 6 and 11. Histology shows papillary squamous proliferation with koilocytes, cells with wrinkled nuclei and a clear halo around the nucleus. Condyloma latum is a flat, moist, highly infectious lesion of secondary syphilis that contains many spirochetes. High-risk HPV types, mainly 16 and 18, cause carcinomas of the cervix, vulva, vagina, penis, anus, and oropharynx.","whyNot":["Flat, moist, broad-based papules describe condyloma latum. Condyloma acuminatum is raised and papillary.","Correct.","Spirochetes on dark-field microscopy point to condyloma latum of secondary syphilis.","A reactive RPR supports syphilis, which is the cause of condyloma latum."],"study":{"topics":["Condyloma acuminatum versus condyloma latum","HPV-related cancers"],"comps":["RE-13-K6","RE-13-K8"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C01","system":"UR","dx":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis after impetigo","codes":["UR-12-A1"],"case":"A 7-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of dark brown urine and puffiness around his eyes for 2 days. Three weeks ago he had crusted, honey-colored sores around his nose and mouth that healed without treatment. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 138/90 mm Hg (95th percentile for age and height, about 110/74). Physical examination shows periorbital edema and 1+ pitting edema of the ankles. Urinalysis shows cola-colored urine with 3+ blood and 2+ protein. Laboratory studies show serum creatinine 1.1 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7), C3 38 mg/dL (90 to 180), C4 24 mg/dL (10 to 40), and an elevated anti-DNase B titer. Diagnosis: poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C01-Q1","stem":"Plasma renin activity and atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) are measured. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely?","options":["Increased renin and decreased atrial natriuretic peptide","Decreased renin and increased atrial natriuretic peptide","Increased renin and increased atrial natriuretic peptide","Decreased renin and decreased atrial natriuretic peptide"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Inflamed glomeruli filter less, so the kidney retains sodium and water and the extracellular volume expands.","Expanded volume and high pressure at the afferent arteriole suppress renin release from the juxtaglomerular cells.","Stretched atria release ANP, so this is a high-volume, low-renin form of hypertension."],"why":"In acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis the GFR falls and the tubules keep reabsorbing sodium, so salt and water are retained. The hypertension and edema come from this volume expansion. Higher perfusion pressure at the juxtaglomerular cells and less sympathetic drive turn renin down, so renin and aldosterone are low. Atrial stretch raises ANP, which would normally promote natriuresis, but the injured kidney responds poorly to it.","whyNot":["This pattern fits volume depletion or renal artery stenosis, where low perfusion pressure drives renin up and the atria are underfilled.","Correct.","Renin and ANP rarely rise together. High renin with high ANP would need low renal perfusion and atrial stretch at the same time, as in some heart failure states.","Low ANP means the atria are not stretched. This child is volume expanded, with edema and hypertension."],"study":{"topics":["Volume-dependent hypertension in nephritis","Control of renin release","Actions of atrial natriuretic peptide"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C01-Q2","stem":"Which of the following additional findings is most likely in this patient?","options":["Selective albuminuria with normal urine output","Oval fat bodies and fatty casts in the urine","Serum albumin under 2.5 g/dL with hyperlipidemia","Urine protein under 3.5 g/day with oliguria"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.1","dok":3,"approach":["Hematuria, hypertension, edema, and a rising creatinine after an infection point to a nephritic syndrome.","Nephritic disease comes from inflammation that injures the capillary wall and lowers GFR, so urine output falls and proteinuria is usually modest.","Pick the finding that belongs to the nephritic picture and reject the features of nephrotic syndrome."],"why":"Nephritic syndrome is driven by glomerular inflammation. Red cells leak into the urine, GFR falls, and salt and water retention cause hypertension, oliguria, and azotemia. Proteinuria is present but usually below 3.5 g/day. Nephrotic syndrome is defined by proteinuria above 3.5 g/day with hypoalbuminemia, edema, hyperlipidemia, and lipiduria.","whyNot":["Selective albuminuria with preserved output is typical of minimal change disease in a child. It does not fit hematuria, hypertension, and azotemia.","Oval fat bodies and fatty casts come from heavy lipoprotein loss in nephrotic syndrome.","Severe hypoalbuminemia (reference 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL) with hyperlipidemia defines nephrotic syndrome. Postinfectious glomerulonephritis only rarely reaches nephrotic-range protein loss.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Nephritic versus nephrotic syndrome"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-12-A3"]}},{"id":"UR-C01-Q3","stem":"Culture of the skin lesions 3 weeks ago would most likely have grown which of the following organisms?","options":["Catalase-positive, coagulase-positive cocci in clusters","Alpha-hemolytic, optochin-sensitive lancet-shaped diplococci","Beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-sensitive cocci in chains","Beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-resistant, CAMP-positive cocci"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Nephritis about 3 weeks after impetigo, with low C3 and a raised anti-DNase B titer, means a preceding infection with a nephritogenic strain of group A streptococcus.","Identify group A streptococcus in the laboratory: gram-positive cocci in chains, catalase negative, beta-hemolytic, and bacitracin sensitive."],"why":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis follows infection with certain nephritogenic strains of Streptococcus pyogenes (group A). After pharyngitis the latent period is about 1 to 2 weeks; after skin infection it is about 3 to 6 weeks. Group A streptococci are catalase-negative, beta-hemolytic cocci in chains that are sensitive to bacitracin and PYR positive. After skin infection the antistreptolysin O titer often stays low, so anti-DNase B is the better marker.","whyNot":["Staphylococcus aureus is a common cause of impetigo, but it does not cause poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. Staphylococcal infection is linked to a different, IgA-dominant glomerulonephritis that occurs during active infection.","This describes Streptococcus pneumoniae, which causes pneumonia, otitis, and meningitis and does not cause impetigo.","Correct.","This describes Streptococcus agalactiae (group B), which causes neonatal sepsis and meningitis and is not a cause of postinfectious nephritis after impetigo."],"study":{"topics":["Nephritogenic group A streptococci","Laboratory identification of streptococci"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C01-Q4","stem":"If a renal biopsy were performed, which of the following findings on immunofluorescence and electron microscopy would most likely be seen?","options":["Linear IgG along the basement membrane, no dense deposits","Granular IgG and C3 with subepithelial humps","Mesangial IgA with mesangial dense deposits","Granular IgG, C3, and C1q with subendothelial deposits"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.3","dok":2,"approach":["Low C3 with normal C4 after a streptococcal infection points to immune complex disease that activates complement mainly through the alternative pathway.","Immune complexes give a granular (lumpy-bumpy) pattern of IgG and C3.","In this disease the large deposits sit on the outer side of the basement membrane, under the podocytes."],"why":"Streptococcal antigens such as SpeB and nephritis-associated plasmin receptor deposit in the glomerulus and bind antibody, forming immune complexes in place and from the circulation. Immunofluorescence shows granular IgG and C3 along capillary walls and in the mesangium. Electron microscopy shows the classic subepithelial humps. Complement activation lowers serum C3, which returns to normal within about 6 to 8 weeks.","whyNot":["Linear IgG with no deposits is anti-GBM disease, where antibodies bind the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen directly.","Correct.","Mesangial IgA deposits define IgA nephropathy and IgA vasculitis, which have normal complement levels.","Full-house granular staining with C1q and subendothelial wire-loop deposits suggests lupus nephritis, which lowers both C3 and C4."],"study":{"topics":["Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis pathology","Immunofluorescence patterns in glomerular disease"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-10"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C02","system":"UR","dx":"IgA nephropathy with gross hematuria after a viral pharyngitis","codes":["UR-12-A1"],"case":"A 24-year-old man comes to the physician because of red-brown urine that began yesterday, 1 day after the onset of a sore throat and runny nose. He had a similar episode of dark urine during a cold 1 year ago that cleared within a week. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows pharyngeal erythema and no edema. Urinalysis shows 3+ blood, 1+ protein, and dysmorphic red blood cells. Serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Serum C3 and C4 concentrations are within the reference ranges. A renal biopsy shows mesangial proliferation with dominant mesangial IgA deposits on immunofluorescence. Diagnosis: IgA nephropathy.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C02-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the immunologic mechanism of this patient's glomerular injury?","options":["Antibodies binding the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","Cytokine release from T cells injuring podocytes","ANCA-mediated neutrophil activation without immune deposits","Deposition of circulating IgA1-containing immune complexes"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.1","dok":3,"approach":["Granular mesangial IgA deposits mean antigen-antibody complexes are lodging in the glomerulus.","Tissue injury from immune complexes that activate complement and recruit inflammatory cells is type III hypersensitivity.","Match the mechanism to IgA nephropathy and reject type II and pauci-immune disease."],"why":"IgA nephropathy is a type III immune complex disease. Patients make IgA1 with too little galactose in its hinge region. Autoantibodies of the IgG or IgA class bind this abnormal IgA1, and the complexes deposit in the mesangium. They stimulate mesangial cells to proliferate and activate complement through the alternative and lectin pathways. Mucosal infections boost IgA1 production, which is why flares follow colds.","whyNot":["Antibody to a fixed antigen in the basement membrane is type II hypersensitivity. It is the mechanism of anti-GBM disease and gives linear IgG staining.","T cell cytokine injury to podocytes is the proposed mechanism of minimal change disease, which causes nephrotic syndrome with no immune deposits.","ANCA vasculitis is pauci-immune, with little or no immunoglobulin in the glomerulus. This biopsy shows strong IgA deposits.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types in glomerular disease","Galactose-deficient IgA1"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C02-Q2","stem":"Urine protein electrophoresis is performed on a 24-hour collection. Which of the following patterns is most likely?","options":["Mostly albumin","Mostly monoclonal free light chains","Mostly uromodulin from the thick ascending limb","Mostly beta-2 microglobulin and lysozyme"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.2","dok":2,"approach":["Dysmorphic red cells show that the leak is in the glomerulus.","A damaged filtration barrier lets through plasma proteins that are normally held back, and albumin is the most plentiful of these.","Separate this glomerular pattern from overflow and tubular patterns."],"why":"Proteinuria has three main sources. In glomerular proteinuria a damaged barrier lets albumin and sometimes larger proteins through, so albumin dominates. In overflow proteinuria small proteins such as free light chains are made in excess and swamp tubular reabsorption. In tubular proteinuria the barrier is intact, but the proximal tubule fails to take back the small proteins that are normally filtered.","whyNot":["Correct.","A monoclonal light chain peak is overflow proteinuria from a plasma cell disorder such as multiple myeloma.","Uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) is secreted by the thick ascending limb and is the main protein in normal urine. It forms the matrix of casts but does not cause disease-level proteinuria.","Excess small proteins such as beta-2 microglobulin and lysozyme with little albumin is tubular proteinuria, seen in Fanconi syndrome and tubulointerstitial disease."],"study":{"topics":["Glomerular, overflow, and tubular proteinuria"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C02-Q3","stem":"Which of the following other glomerular diseases also characteristically presents with normal serum complement concentrations?","options":["Diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Anti-GBM disease","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.2","dok":3,"approach":["Complement falls when immune complexes in or around the glomerulus activate it faster than the liver can replace it.","Sort the options into diseases that consume complement and diseases that do not.","Anti-GBM antibody binds a fixed antigen, and serum complement stays normal."],"why":"Serum complement helps narrow the cause of nephritis. Low complement points to poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (low C3), membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis (low C3), and lupus nephritis (low C3 and C4). Normal complement points to IgA nephropathy, anti-GBM disease, and ANCA-associated vasculitis. In anti-GBM disease complement is activated locally at the basement membrane, but not enough to lower serum levels.","whyNot":["Lupus nephritis consumes complement through the classical pathway, so both C3 and C4 are low during active disease.","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis typically lowers C3, from immune complexes or from C3 nephritic factor.","Correct.","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis lowers C3 through alternative pathway activation, with recovery over about 6 to 8 weeks."],"study":{"topics":["Serum complement in glomerulonephritis"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C02-Q4","stem":"Which of the following features of this patient's presentation most strongly favors IgA nephropathy over poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis?","options":["Dysmorphic red cells on urine microscopy","Hematuria within a day of the sore throat","Red blood cell casts in the urine sediment","Pharyngitis as the preceding infection"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.4","dok":3,"approach":["Both diseases can follow an upper respiratory infection and both cause glomerular hematuria.","The timing differs: IgA nephropathy flares during or within a few days of the infection, while poststreptococcal disease follows after a latent period of weeks.","Choose the feature that one disease has and the other does not."],"why":"In IgA nephropathy, gross hematuria starts during or within 1 to 3 days of a mucosal infection, so it is called synpharyngitic. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis appears about 1 to 2 weeks after pharyngitis or 3 to 6 weeks after impetigo, because antibody must form and immune complexes must build up first. Complement also separates them: normal in IgA nephropathy, low C3 in poststreptococcal disease. IgA nephropathy often recurs, while poststreptococcal disease rarely does.","whyNot":["Dysmorphic red cells show that blood passed through a glomerulus. They are present in both diseases.","Correct.","Red cell casts mark glomerular bleeding in any glomerulonephritis, so they appear in both diseases.","Pharyngitis precedes both diseases. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis can also follow skin infection."],"study":{"topics":["IgA nephropathy versus poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-10"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C03","system":"UR","dx":"Anti-glomerular basement membrane disease (Goodpasture syndrome)","codes":["UR-12-A1"],"case":"A 27-year-old man who smokes cigarettes is brought to the emergency department because of coughing up blood for 1 week and reduced urine output with dark urine for 3 days. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 108/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 150/94 mm Hg. Crackles are heard over both lung fields. A chest x-ray shows bilateral alveolar infiltrates. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), urea nitrogen 62 mg/dL (7 to 20), and creatinine 4.6 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Urinalysis shows 3+ blood, 2+ protein, and red blood cell casts. Serum anti-GBM antibodies are positive and ANCA is negative. A renal biopsy shows crescents in 80% of glomeruli with linear IgG along the basement membranes. Diagnosis: anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture syndrome).","questions":[{"id":"UR-C03-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the lesions seen in most of his glomeruli on light microscopy?","options":["Nodules of mesangial matrix with hyaline material","Proliferating mesangial cells with duplication of the basement membrane","Segmental sclerosis of the capillary tuft with adhesion to Bowman capsule","Parietal epithelial cells, macrophages, and fibrin in Bowman space"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.5","dok":2,"approach":["Crescents in most glomeruli define rapidly progressive (crescentic) glomerulonephritis.","Severe injury tears the glomerular capillary wall, and fibrin and inflammatory cells pour into Bowman space.","Fibrin and cytokines drive parietal epithelial cells to proliferate, and macrophages join them to form the crescent."],"why":"A crescent forms when severe injury ruptures the glomerular capillary wall. Fibrinogen leaks into Bowman space and becomes fibrin. Fibrin and inflammatory cytokines make the parietal epithelial cells proliferate, and monocytes and macrophages move in, so the crescent compresses the tuft. Untreated cellular crescents become fibrous scars, which is why rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis can cause renal failure within weeks.","whyNot":["Nodular mesangial matrix (Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules) is the lesion of diabetic nephropathy.","Mesangial proliferation with a split basement membrane is membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis.","Segmental sclerosis with adhesions is focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, a podocyte disease that causes nephrotic syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Crescent formation in rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-10"]}},{"id":"UR-C03-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism of tissue injury in this patient?","options":["Type II, with antibody against a fixed tissue antigen","Type I, with IgE-triggered mast cell degranulation","Type III, with granular deposits of circulating immune complexes","Type IV, with sensitized T cells releasing cytokines"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.1","dok":3,"approach":["Linear IgG along the basement membrane means antibody is binding an antigen built into the membrane itself.","Antibody against a fixed tissue antigen, followed by complement activation and neutrophil injury, is type II hypersensitivity.","The same antigen is present in alveolar basement membranes, which explains the lung hemorrhage."],"why":"In anti-GBM disease, IgG autoantibodies bind the noncollagenous (NC1) domain of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen. Because the antigen is spread evenly along the membrane, immunofluorescence shows a smooth linear pattern. The bound antibody activates complement and recruits neutrophils and macrophages, which tear the capillary wall. Alveolar basement membranes carry the same antigen. Smoking and other lung injury expose it, and pulmonary hemorrhage results.","whyNot":["Correct.","IgE-mediated reactions cause anaphylaxis, urticaria, and asthma. They do not cause glomerulonephritis.","Immune complexes give granular deposits, as in poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis and lupus nephritis.","Delayed T cell injury is seen in contact dermatitis and tuberculin reactions and does not produce linear IgG staining."],"study":{"topics":["Type II hypersensitivity","Goodpasture syndrome"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C03-Q3","stem":"The target of his antibodies lies in the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen, encoded by COL4A3. A heterozygous loss-of-function mutation in this gene in another person is most likely to cause which of the following?","options":["Hyperextensible skin with widened atrophic scars","Spontaneous rupture of large arteries and colon","Isolated hematuria with a thin basement membrane","Blue sclerae with fractures after minor trauma"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.2","dok":2,"approach":["Type IV collagen forms the sheet-like network of basement membranes, and the alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 chains make the mature glomerular basement membrane.","One normal COL4A3 allele still makes some network, so a heterozygous defect gives a thin but working membrane.","The result is persistent microscopic hematuria, usually with normal kidney function."],"why":"The mature glomerular basement membrane is built from a network of alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 chains of type IV collagen. Mutations in COL4A3 or COL4A4 on both alleles, or in X-linked COL4A5, cause Alport syndrome with a split, laminated membrane. A heterozygous COL4A3 or COL4A4 mutation causes thin basement membrane disease, with persistent microscopic hematuria and usually a benign course, though some carriers later develop proteinuria and loss of function.","whyNot":["Hyperextensible skin and wide atrophic scars are classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, most often from type V collagen mutations (COL5A1 or COL5A2).","Arterial and bowel rupture are vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, from COL3A1 mutations in type III collagen.","Correct.","Blue sclerae and fragile bones are osteogenesis imperfecta, most often from COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations in type I collagen."],"study":{"topics":["Type IV collagen genetics","Thin basement membrane disease"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-E1"]}},{"id":"UR-C03-Q4","stem":"Arterial blood gas analysis on room air shows: pH 7.42 (7.35 to 7.45), PaCO2 16 mm Hg (35 to 45), PaO2 56 mm Hg (75 to 100), and HCO3- 10 mEq/L (22 to 28). Serum sodium is 136 mEq/L and chloride is 104 mEq/L. Which of the following best describes his acid-base status?","options":["Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","Metabolic acidosis with a concurrent respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis with a concurrent respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis with appropriate renal compensation"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.2","dok":3,"approach":["The bicarbonate of 10 mEq/L and the anion gap of 22 (136 minus 104 minus 10) show an anion gap metabolic acidosis from renal failure.","Winters formula predicts PaCO2 = 1.5 x 10 + 8 = 23, give or take 2, so 21 to 25 mm Hg.","His PaCO2 of 16 is lower than predicted, so a second, respiratory alkalosis is present, driven here by hypoxemia from alveolar hemorrhage."],"why":"Renal failure retains acids such as sulfate and phosphate, which lowers bicarbonate and raises the anion gap. Winters formula gives the PaCO2 expected from respiratory compensation alone. A PaCO2 below that range means the patient is breathing off more CO2 than compensation explains, which is a concurrent respiratory alkalosis. His hypoxemia from pulmonary hemorrhage drives this extra ventilation. A normal pH with clearly abnormal PaCO2 and bicarbonate is a strong clue to a mixed disorder.","whyNot":["Appropriate compensation would put PaCO2 at about 21 to 25 mm Hg. His PaCO2 of 16 is too low.","A concurrent respiratory acidosis would leave PaCO2 above the predicted range, and the pH would be well below 7.35.","Correct.","Renal compensation for chronic respiratory alkalosis rarely lowers bicarbonate below about 12 to 15 mEq/L, and his illness is too recent for full compensation. The high anion gap also shows a separate metabolic acidosis."],"study":{"topics":["Winters formula","Mixed acid-base disorders"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-12-A4"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C04","system":"UR","dx":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis with crescentic glomerulonephritis","codes":["UR-12-A1"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of nasal crusting, bloody nasal discharge, and sinus pain for 2 months, and cough with blood-streaked sputum for 2 weeks. Temperature is 37.8 °C (100.0 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 146/88 mm Hg. Examination shows ulcerated, crusted nasal mucosa. A chest x-ray shows several cavitating nodules. Serum creatinine is 3.2 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1); it was 0.9 mg/dL 4 months ago. Urinalysis shows 3+ blood and 2+ protein. Serum C3 and C4 concentrations are within the reference ranges. PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) is positive at a high titer. A renal biopsy shows necrotizing crescentic glomerulonephritis. Diagnosis: granulomatosis with polyangiitis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C04-Q1","stem":"Before the antibody results were available, multiple myeloma was considered as a cause of her renal failure. Which of the following urine findings would have most strongly suggested overflow proteinuria from that disorder?","options":["Albumin accounting for most of the protein in the urine","Protein detected by dipstick but not by sulfosalicylic acid","Uromodulin as the main protein in the urine","Protein detected by sulfosalicylic acid but not dipstick"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.2","dok":3,"approach":["In myeloma, plasma cells make excess free light chains that are small enough to be filtered and overwhelm proximal tubular reabsorption.","Urine dipsticks react mainly with albumin and miss light chains, while sulfosalicylic acid precipitates all proteins.","A positive sulfosalicylic acid test with a negative dipstick points to a non-albumin protein such as Bence Jones protein."],"why":"Overflow proteinuria happens when the plasma holds too much of a small, freely filtered protein. In multiple myeloma, free light chains (Bence Jones protein) are filtered faster than the proximal tubule can take them back, so they appear in the urine. The dipstick pad is sensitive to albumin and poorly detects light chains. Sulfosalicylic acid precipitates any protein, so a gap between the two tests suggests light chains. Small tubular proteins can give the same gap, so urine electrophoresis and immunofixation are used to confirm a monoclonal light chain.","whyNot":["Albumin-dominant proteinuria is glomerular. It fits her glomerulonephritis and argues against light chain overflow.","A positive dipstick with a negative sulfosalicylic acid test is the reverse pattern. It suggests a false-positive dipstick, as in highly alkaline urine, and does not point to light chains.","Uromodulin is the main protein of normal urine, secreted by the thick ascending limb, and does not point to any disease.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Overflow proteinuria","Dipstick versus sulfosalicylic acid testing"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-A4"]}},{"id":"UR-C04-Q2","stem":"Immunofluorescence microscopy of the biopsy specimen is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane","Little or no immunoglobulin or complement","Dominant IgA deposits in the mesangium","Granular IgG and C3 along capillary walls"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.5","dok":2,"approach":["Crescentic glomerulonephritis is sorted into three groups by immunofluorescence: linear, granular, and pauci-immune.","Positive ANCA with normal complement places her in the pauci-immune group.","In ANCA disease, neutrophils activated by the antibodies damage vessels without leaving immune deposits."],"why":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis has three immunologic types. Anti-GBM disease shows linear IgG. Immune complex disease, such as lupus or postinfectious glomerulonephritis, shows granular deposits. Pauci-immune disease shows little or no immunoglobulin or complement and is usually ANCA positive. In granulomatosis with polyangiitis, ANCA binds proteinase 3 on primed neutrophils and makes them release enzymes and reactive oxygen species into the vessel wall. This damage leaves no immune deposits behind.","whyNot":["Linear IgG is anti-GBM disease. She is ANCA positive and has upper airway disease, which fits granulomatosis with polyangiitis.","Correct.","Mesangial IgA is IgA nephropathy or IgA vasculitis, which do not cause cavitating lung nodules or a positive PR3-ANCA.","Granular IgG and C3 indicate immune complex disease, which usually lowers complement. Her complement is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Types of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","Pauci-immune glomerulonephritis"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-10"]}},{"id":"UR-C04-Q3","stem":"Microscopic examination of her urine sediment is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Dysmorphic erythrocytes and red blood cell casts","Leukocytes and white blood cell casts with bacteria","Renal tubular cells and muddy brown granular casts","Broad waxy casts in a bland sediment"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.2","dok":2,"approach":["Necrotizing glomerulonephritis breaks glomerular capillaries, and red cells enter the tubules.","Red cells squeezed through the capillary wall become misshapen, and red cells trapped in uromodulin within the tubule form casts.","Red cell casts place the bleeding in the glomerulus."],"why":"Casts form in the tubules when uromodulin gels and traps whatever is in the lumen, so a cast shows where in the nephron the problem started. Red cell casts and dysmorphic red cells mean bleeding from inflamed glomeruli. White cell casts point to pyelonephritis or interstitial nephritis. Muddy brown granular casts point to acute tubular necrosis. Fatty casts point to nephrotic syndrome, and waxy casts point to advanced chronic kidney disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","White cell casts with bacteria indicate pyelonephritis, an infection of the tubules and interstitium.","Muddy brown granular casts and shed tubular cells indicate acute tubular necrosis, such as after ischemia or toxins.","Broad waxy casts form in dilated, slow-flowing tubules of advanced chronic kidney disease. Her decline happened over weeks."],"study":{"topics":["Urinary casts and what they localize"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-12-A4"]}},{"id":"UR-C04-Q4","stem":"Which of the following findings best distinguishes her disease from microscopic polyangiitis?","options":["Microaneurysms of the renal arteries on angiography","Granulomatous inflammation of the airways","Necrotizing glomerulonephritis with crescents","Palpable purpura of the lower legs"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.3","dok":3,"approach":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis are both ANCA-associated small vessel vasculitides that cause pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis.","They differ in that granulomatosis with polyangiitis forms necrotizing granulomas in the upper and lower respiratory tract and is usually PR3-ANCA positive.","Microscopic polyangiitis has no granulomas and is usually MPO-ANCA positive."],"why":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis has a classic triad of necrotizing granulomas of the upper airway (sinusitis, nasal ulcers, saddle nose), lung disease (nodules that may cavitate), and necrotizing glomerulonephritis. It is most often PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) positive. Microscopic polyangiitis causes the same kind of glomerulonephritis and lung capillaritis but no granulomas, and it is usually MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA) positive. Polyarteritis nodosa affects medium arteries, spares glomeruli, and is ANCA negative.","whyNot":["Renal artery microaneurysms are a feature of polyarteritis nodosa, a medium vessel vasculitis that spares the glomeruli.","Correct.","Both ANCA vasculitides cause necrotizing crescentic glomerulonephritis, so this does not separate them.","Palpable purpura from small vessel vasculitis of the skin can occur in both diseases."],"study":{"topics":["ANCA-associated vasculitis","Polyarteritis nodosa"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-A1"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C05","system":"UR","dx":"Diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis","codes":["UR-12-A1"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician because of swelling of her legs, painful joints, and a facial rash for 3 weeks. Temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F), pulse is 90/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 158/98 mm Hg. Examination shows an erythematous rash over the cheeks and nasal bridge that spares the nasolabial folds, tender swollen wrists, and 2+ pitting edema of the legs. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.2 g/dL (12 to 16), creatinine 1.8 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1), and a positive antinuclear antibody test at 1:1280. Urine protein excretion is 2.4 g/24 h. A renal biopsy shows endocapillary proliferation in more than half of the glomeruli, wire-loop thickening of capillary walls, and granular IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q deposits. Diagnosis: diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C05-Q1","stem":"Four years later, after treatment, she has new swelling of her legs. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL, serum albumin is 2.2 g/dL (3.5 to 5.5), and urine protein excretion is 7.5 g/24 h. Urinalysis shows 3+ protein, no blood, and no red blood cell casts. A repeat renal biopsy is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Mesangial hypercellularity with mesangial deposits only","Global sclerosis of more than 90 percent of glomeruli","Subepithelial deposits with basement membrane spikes","Subendothelial wire-loop deposits with endocapillary proliferation"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Heavy proteinuria with low albumin, edema, and no blood or casts in the urine is a nephrotic picture.","In lupus, the class that gives a nephrotic picture with a quiet sediment is membranous lupus nephritis (class V).","Membranous disease places immune deposits under the podocytes, and new basement membrane grows up around them as spikes."],"why":"Lupus nephritis is classified by where the immune deposits sit and how much the glomeruli proliferate. Subendothelial deposits with proliferation (classes III and IV) give a nephritic picture with hematuria and red cell casts. Subepithelial deposits (class V, membranous lupus nephritis) injure podocytes and give nephrotic syndrome with little hematuria, like primary membranous nephropathy. A patient can move from one class to another over time, so a change in the urine picture is a reason to repeat the biopsy.","whyNot":["Mesangial lupus nephritis (class II) usually causes mild hematuria or low-grade proteinuria, not nephrotic syndrome.","Advanced sclerosing lupus nephritis (class VI) means most glomeruli are scarred, so GFR would be markedly reduced. Her creatinine is normal.","Correct.","Active diffuse proliferative disease would give hematuria and red cell casts. Her sediment is bland."],"study":{"topics":["Classes of lupus nephritis","Membranous nephropathy"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-12-A3"]}},{"id":"UR-C05-Q2","stem":"Which of the following sets of serum findings is most likely during this flare?","options":["Low C3, low C4, high anti-dsDNA","Low C3, normal C4, high anti-dsDNA","Normal C3, normal C4, high anti-dsDNA","Normal C3, low C4, normal anti-dsDNA"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.6","dok":3,"approach":["Full-house granular deposits with C1q mean immune complexes are activating complement through the classical pathway.","Classical pathway activation uses up C1, C4, and C2 before C3, so both C4 and C3 fall.","Anti-dsDNA antibodies help form these complexes, and their titer rises with active nephritis."],"why":"Lupus nephritis is a type III immune complex disease. Complexes of anti-dsDNA and nuclear antigens deposit beneath the endothelium and activate the classical pathway, which consumes C4 and C3. Low C3 and C4 with a rising anti-dsDNA titer signal active disease, and both are followed during treatment. Anti-dsDNA is highly specific for lupus and is closely linked to renal involvement.","whyNot":["Correct.","Low C3 with normal C4 points to alternative pathway activation, as in poststreptococcal or C3 glomerulopathy.","Normal complement would be unusual with active diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis and its heavy immune complex load.","A normal anti-dsDNA with isolated low C4 does not fit an active lupus flare and suggests problems such as C1 inhibitor deficiency or cryoglobulinemia."],"study":{"topics":["Complement and anti-dsDNA in lupus nephritis"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-10"]}},{"id":"UR-C05-Q3","stem":"The wire-loop lesions are formed by immune deposits between the capillary endothelium and the glomerular basement membrane. Which feature of this location best explains why these deposits cause intense glomerular inflammation?","options":["Separation from blood by the basement membrane","Direct contact with slit diaphragm proteins of podocytes","Location within the mesangial matrix","Contact with blood through endothelial fenestrae"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.1","dok":3,"approach":["The filtration barrier has three layers from the blood side outward: fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte foot processes.","Subendothelial deposits sit on the blood side of the basement membrane, behind only a thin endothelium with open pores.","Complement fragments made there reach the blood, and circulating leukocytes can reach the deposits, so inflammation and proliferation follow."],"why":"Where an immune deposit sits in the barrier shapes the disease. Subendothelial deposits are separated from the capillary lumen only by fenestrated endothelium. Chemotactic complement fragments such as C5a diffuse into the blood, and neutrophils and monocytes can reach the deposits, so the result is a proliferative, nephritic lesion. Subepithelial deposits lie outside the basement membrane, away from circulating leukocytes, so they mainly injure podocytes and cause nephrotic syndrome, as in membranous nephropathy.","whyNot":["Deposits separated from blood by the basement membrane are subepithelial. That position shields them from leukocytes and gives a less inflammatory, nephrotic picture.","The slit diaphragms lie on the urinary side of the basement membrane. Subendothelial deposits do not touch them.","Mesangial deposits are a separate site, typical of IgA nephropathy and milder lupus classes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the glomerular filtration barrier","Location of immune deposits"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C05-Q4","stem":"Her edema results from renal sodium retention. Her serum sodium concentration is 139 mEq/L (136 to 145). Compared with before her illness, which of the following best describes her body fluid compartments?","options":["ECF unchanged, ICF increased, osmolality decreased","ECF increased, ICF decreased, osmolality increased","ECF increased, ICF unchanged, osmolality unchanged","ECF increased, ICF increased, osmolality decreased"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["A normal serum sodium means the fluid she gained has the same osmolality as plasma, so this is an isotonic gain.","Sodium stays mostly in the extracellular fluid, so isotonic fluid expands only the extracellular compartment.","No osmotic gradient forms, so water does not move into or out of cells."],"why":"Sodium is the main extracellular solute, so retained sodium with an equal share of water adds isotonic fluid to the extracellular space. Plasma and interstitial volume increase, and the interstitial excess shows as edema. Osmolality is unchanged, so intracellular volume stays the same. A hypotonic gain would swell cells, and a hypertonic gain would shrink them.","whyNot":["Expansion of cells alone with an unchanged extracellular volume does not fit sodium retention with edema.","This is hypertonic gain, as after a hypertonic saline infusion, which would raise serum sodium.","Correct.","This is hypotonic fluid gain, as in excess water intake or SIADH, which would lower serum sodium."],"study":{"topics":["Isotonic, hypotonic, and hypertonic volume changes"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-12-A1"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C06","system":"UR","dx":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis with hepatitis C cryoglobulinemia","codes":["UR-12-A1"],"case":"A 61-year-old man comes to the physician because of swelling of his legs, a rash on his shins, joint aches, and fatigue for 2 months. He has untreated chronic hepatitis C infection acquired through injection drug use 30 years ago. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 84/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 164/96 mm Hg. Examination shows palpable purpura on both lower legs and 2+ pitting edema. Laboratory studies show creatinine 1.9 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3), albumin 2.6 g/dL (3.5 to 5.5), C3 72 mg/dL (90 to 180), C4 4 mg/dL (10 to 40), and a high rheumatoid factor titer. Cryoglobulins are detected. Urinalysis shows 2+ blood and red blood cell casts, and urine protein is 4.1 g/24 h. Diagnosis: membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis from hepatitis C-associated cryoglobulinemia.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C06-Q1","stem":"A silver stain of the renal biopsy shows double contours of the glomerular capillary walls. Which of the following processes best explains this appearance?","options":["Subepithelial spikes of new matrix around deposits","Mesangial interposition with new basement membrane","Fibrin and parietal cells crowding Bowman space","Splitting of the GBM from defective type IV collagen"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.7","dok":2,"approach":["A double contour, or tram track, on silver stain means there are two basement membrane layers in the capillary wall.","In membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, subendothelial deposits draw mesangial cells and leukocytes into the capillary wall.","The endothelium then lays down new basement membrane over the cells it has taken in, which creates the second layer."],"why":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis is named for two changes: thickened capillary walls and proliferating mesangial and endothelial cells. Subendothelial immune deposits draw mesangial cell processes in between the endothelium and the original basement membrane. New basement membrane forms on the inner side, so the silver stain shows two parallel lines. The immune complex form is linked to hepatitis B and C, and dense deposit disease is a C3 glomerulopathy linked to C3 nephritic factor.","whyNot":["Spikes of new basement membrane around subepithelial deposits are the spike-and-dome pattern of membranous nephropathy.","Correct.","Fibrin and proliferating parietal cells in Bowman space are crescents, the lesion of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.","Split and laminated basement membranes from type IV collagen defects are seen in Alport syndrome, on electron microscopy."],"study":{"topics":["Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis pathology"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-11"]}},{"id":"UR-C06-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes the cryoglobulins most likely responsible for his kidney disease?","options":["Monoclonal IgG from a clonal plasma cell disorder","IgG antibodies to double-stranded DNA with nucleosomes","Polyclonal IgA1 complexes with a galactose-deficient hinge","Monoclonal IgM rheumatoid factor with polyclonal IgG"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Chronic hepatitis C keeps stimulating B cells and can lead to a clonal B-cell population.","This clone often makes an IgM with rheumatoid factor activity, meaning it binds the Fc part of IgG, which explains his high rheumatoid factor.","The IgM and IgG complexes, often with viral particles, precipitate in the cold and deposit in glomeruli and small vessels of the skin."],"why":"Hepatitis C is the leading cause of mixed cryoglobulinemia. Long-term viral stimulation of B cells produces a monoclonal IgM, usually kappa, with rheumatoid factor activity. This IgM binds polyclonal IgG, often with hepatitis C particles, forming type II mixed cryoglobulins. The complexes cause small vessel vasculitis (palpable purpura, arthralgia) and deposit beneath the endothelium of glomeruli, producing membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis. Hepatitis B, by contrast, is linked mainly to membranous nephropathy.","whyNot":["A lone monoclonal immunoglobulin is type I cryoglobulinemia, seen in multiple myeloma and Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia. It does not explain a high rheumatoid factor.","Anti-dsDNA immune complexes are the cause of lupus nephritis, not of hepatitis C cryoglobulinemia.","IgA1 complexes with abnormal hinge glycosylation cause IgA nephropathy and IgA vasculitis, which do not lower complement.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis C and mixed cryoglobulinemia","Infections linked to glomerular disease"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C06-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains his pattern of serum complement levels?","options":["Classical pathway activation by circulating immune complexes","Loss of complement proteins in nephrotic-range proteinuria","Autoantibody stabilizing the alternative pathway C3 convertase","Lectin pathway activation by mannose on bacterial walls"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.2","dok":3,"approach":["His C4 is very low and his C3 is mildly low.","C4 is used only by the classical and lectin pathways, so a deep drop in C4 points to classical activation by antibody-containing complexes.","IgM-IgG cryoglobulins fix C1 strongly and consume C4 more than C3."],"why":"Complement levels show which pathway is being used up. The classical pathway starts when C1q binds antibody in immune complexes, then cleaves C4 and C2 before C3. Cryoglobulins with IgM are strong C1 activators, so C4 falls very low while C3 is normal or mildly low. Alternative pathway disease, such as C3 nephritic factor in dense deposit disease or poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, lowers C3 with a normal C4.","whyNot":["Correct.","Urinary loss in nephrotic syndrome does not explain such a marked and selective fall in C4, and his proteinuria is only modestly above 3.5 g/day.","C3 nephritic factor stabilizes the alternative pathway C3 convertase in dense deposit disease. It lowers C3 but leaves C4 normal.","Lectin pathway activation by microbial sugars does not fit chronic hepatitis C with circulating cryoglobulins and rheumatoid factor."],"study":{"topics":["Complement pathways","Complement in cryoglobulinemic glomerulonephritis"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C06-Q4","stem":"Considered alone, which of the following best describes the effect of his low serum albumin on glomerular filtration?","options":["Higher glomerular capillary oncotic pressure, opposing filtration","Lower glomerular capillary oncotic pressure, favoring filtration","Higher Bowman space hydrostatic pressure, opposing filtration","Lower glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, opposing filtration"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.1","dok":3,"approach":["GFR depends on the filtration coefficient times net filtration pressure: glomerular hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space hydrostatic pressure minus glomerular oncotic pressure.","Plasma albumin provides most of the oncotic pressure in the glomerular capillary.","Less albumin means less oncotic pressure pulling fluid back, so net filtration pressure rises."],"why":"Net filtration pressure equals glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space hydrostatic pressure minus glomerular capillary oncotic pressure. Hypoalbuminemia lowers the oncotic term, which by itself favors filtration. Ureteral obstruction works the other way by raising Bowman space pressure, and dehydration raises oncotic pressure, so both lower GFR. In this patient the GFR is still reduced because proliferation and deposits lower the filtration coefficient, which outweighs the oncotic effect.","whyNot":["Oncotic pressure rises when albumin is concentrated, as in dehydration. Low albumin lowers it.","Correct.","Higher Bowman space pressure is the effect of urinary tract obstruction, not of low albumin.","Lower capillary hydrostatic pressure follows afferent arteriolar constriction or hypotension. Albumin does not set hydrostatic pressure."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces in the glomerulus"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-5"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C07","system":"UR","dx":"IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura) with nephritis","codes":["UR-12-A1"],"case":"A 6-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a rash on her buttocks and legs for 4 days, cramping abdominal pain, and painful swollen ankles. Two weeks ago she had a cough and runny nose. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 102/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Examination shows raised purpuric lesions over the buttocks and the backs of the legs, diffuse abdominal tenderness, and swelling and tenderness of both ankles. Laboratory studies show platelet count 310,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000), normal prothrombin and partial thromboplastin times, and creatinine 0.5 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7). Urinalysis shows 2+ blood, 1+ protein, and red blood cell casts. A stool test for occult blood is positive. Diagnosis: IgA vasculitis with nephritis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C07-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains her skin lesions?","options":["Autoantibodies against platelet glycoprotein IIb/IIIa","Toxin-mediated endothelial injury with platelet thrombi","IgA immune complexes in small-vessel walls","Consumption of clotting factors in microthrombi"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["Purpura with a normal platelet count and normal clotting times points to a problem in the vessel wall.","Raised (palpable) purpura means inflammation of small vessels, called leukocytoclastic vasculitis.","In a child after an upper respiratory infection, with arthritis, abdominal pain, and nephritis, the cause is IgA immune complex deposition."],"why":"IgA vasculitis is the most common systemic vasculitis of childhood. After an upper respiratory infection, IgA1-containing immune complexes deposit in the walls of small vessels in the skin, gut, joints, and glomeruli. Complement activation and neutrophil influx damage the vessels, and red cells leak out, so the purpura can be felt. Gut involvement causes colicky pain and bleeding and can lead to intussusception, and the kidney lesion is a mesangial IgA glomerulonephritis.","whyNot":["Antiplatelet antibodies cause immune thrombocytopenia, which gives flat petechiae with a low platelet count.","Shiga toxin endothelial injury is hemolytic uremic syndrome, which causes thrombocytopenia and hemolytic anemia.","Correct.","Consumption of clotting factors is disseminated intravascular coagulation, which prolongs PT and PTT and lowers platelets."],"study":{"topics":["IgA vasculitis","Palpable purpura versus petechiae"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C07-Q2","stem":"Two days later, her abdominal pain becomes severe and intermittent, she vomits, and she passes a stool with blood and mucus. Ultrasonography shows an ileoileal intussusception. Which of the following best explains this complication?","options":["Submucosal hemorrhage and edema as a lead point","Hypertrophy of the circular muscle of the pylorus","Embolic occlusion of the superior mesenteric artery","Absence of ganglion cells in the distal colon"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["IgA vasculitis affects small vessels of the bowel wall as well as the skin.","Bleeding and swelling in the submucosa create a mass that peristalsis pulls forward.","The mass acts as a lead point, and the bowel telescopes into the segment below it."],"why":"IgA immune complexes deposit in small vessels of the gut wall, causing vasculitis with submucosal hemorrhage and edema. This explains her colicky pain and occult blood. A swollen, hemorrhagic segment can act as a lead point that is pulled into the next segment of bowel. In IgA vasculitis the intussusception is often confined to the small bowel, while idiopathic intussusception in infants is usually ileocolic. Intussusception is the most important gastrointestinal complication of IgA vasculitis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pyloric muscle hypertrophy is pyloric stenosis, which causes nonbilious projectile vomiting in the first weeks of life.","Superior mesenteric artery embolism causes acute mesenteric ischemia, usually in older adults with atrial fibrillation. It does not telescope the bowel.","Absent ganglion cells in the distal colon is Hirschsprung disease, which presents in the newborn with delayed passage of meconium and constipation."],"study":{"topics":["Gastrointestinal involvement in IgA vasculitis","Intussusception"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-A1"]}},{"id":"UR-C07-Q3","stem":"A renal biopsy is performed. Which of the following immunofluorescence findings is most likely, and would distinguish her renal lesion from poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis?","options":["Granular C3 along capillary loops","Linear IgG along the basement membrane","Granular IgG in a starry-sky pattern","Dominant or codominant mesangial IgA"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A1","obj":"UR-12-A.4","dok":2,"approach":["IgA vasculitis and IgA nephropathy share the same kidney lesion, mesangial IgA deposits.","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis shows granular IgG and C3, with subepithelial humps.","Pick the finding present in her disease and absent in poststreptococcal disease."],"why":"The renal lesion of IgA vasculitis looks like IgA nephropathy: mesangial proliferation with IgA as the dominant or codominant immunoglobulin in the mesangium, often with C3. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis shows granular IgG and C3 along capillary walls and subepithelial humps. Timing also helps: her nephritis appeared with the rash, while poststreptococcal disease follows a latent period of weeks and lowers C3.","whyNot":["C3 staining can appear in both diseases, so it does not separate them.","Linear IgG is anti-GBM disease, which does not fit a child with purpura and arthritis.","Granular IgG in a starry-sky pattern is a pattern of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Renal lesion of IgA vasculitis"],"comps":["UR-12-A1","UR-10"]}},{"id":"UR-C07-Q4","stem":"Her glomerular IgA deposits lodge in the mesangium, and the mesangial cells proliferate in response. Which of the following is a normal function of these cells?","options":["Production of erythropoietin in response to hypoxia","Uptake and removal of trapped macromolecules","Detection of sodium chloride delivery to the distal tubule","Secretion of renin in response to low perfusion pressure"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Intraglomerular mesangial cells sit between the capillary loops inside the glomerulus.","They support the loops, can contract, make matrix, and take up macromolecules and immune complexes caught in the filtration barrier.","This clearing role is why circulating complexes, such as IgA complexes, collect in the mesangium and trigger proliferation."],"why":"Intraglomerular mesangial cells support the capillary tuft, contract to adjust the filtration surface area, make mesangial matrix, and phagocytose macromolecules trapped in the filtration barrier. Because they clear filtered complexes, the mesangium is a common site for immune deposits. IgA complexes that lodge there stimulate the cells to proliferate and release cytokines. Mesangial cells outside the glomerulus (extraglomerular, or lacis, cells) form part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus.","whyNot":["Erythropoietin is made by peritubular interstitial cells of the renal cortex in response to low oxygen delivery.","Correct.","The macula densa, in the distal thick ascending limb, senses sodium chloride delivery and signals the afferent arteriole and juxtaglomerular cells.","Renin is secreted by juxtaglomerular cells, which are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole."],"study":{"topics":["Mesangial cells","Juxtaglomerular apparatus"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-10"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C08","system":"UR","dx":"X-linked Alport syndrome","codes":["UR-12-E1"],"case":"A 16-year-old boy is referred because of microscopic hematuria first found at age 6 and recent trouble hearing in class. His mother has had microscopic hematuria with normal kidney function for years. Her brother began dialysis at age 30 and wears hearing aids. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 72/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg. Audiometry shows bilateral high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss. Slit-lamp examination shows anterior lenticonus. Urinalysis shows 2+ blood, 2+ protein, and dysmorphic red blood cells. Serum creatinine is 1.6 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.2). Electron microscopy of a renal biopsy specimen shows irregular thinning, thickening, and splitting of the glomerular basement membrane with a basket-weave appearance. Diagnosis: X-linked Alport syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C08-Q1","stem":"A 24-hour urine collection has a volume of 1440 mL and a creatinine concentration of 100 mg/dL. His plasma creatinine concentration that day is 1.6 mg/dL. Which of the following is closest to his creatinine clearance?","options":["63 mL/min","90 mL/min","100 mL/min","160 mL/min"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.2","dok":2,"approach":["Convert the urine flow: 1440 mL over 1440 minutes is 1.0 mL/min.","Clearance equals urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration: 100 x 1.0 / 1.6.","The result is about 63 mL/min, a clear fall from a normal value of about 90 to 120 mL/min."],"why":"Clearance is the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per minute, calculated as U times V divided by P. Creatinine is freely filtered and only slightly secreted, so its clearance approximates GFR. Because a small amount is secreted by the proximal tubule, creatinine clearance slightly overestimates the true GFR. Inulin is filtered and neither secreted nor reabsorbed, so its clearance equals GFR exactly. A substance with clearance below GFR is reabsorbed on balance, and one with clearance above GFR is secreted.","whyNot":["Correct.","This value uses 1.44, the daily volume in liters, as if it were the flow in mL/min. Convert first: 1440 mL over 1440 minutes is 1.0 mL/min.","This value is U times V without dividing by plasma creatinine.","This value comes from multiplying by plasma creatinine instead of dividing by it."],"study":{"topics":["Calculating clearance","Creatinine clearance and GFR"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-12-E1"]}},{"id":"UR-C08-Q2","stem":"In a healthy glomerulus, negatively charged albumin is filtered far less than a neutral dextran molecule of the same size. Which of the following structures is chiefly responsible for this difference?","options":["Heparan sulfate proteoglycans in the basement membrane","Nephrin bridging the podocyte filtration slit diaphragm","Contractile mesangial cells between capillary loops","Open fenestrae of the glomerular capillary endothelium"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.1","dok":3,"approach":["Two molecules of the same size are filtered differently, so the difference is due to charge, not size.","The filtration barrier carries fixed negative charges that repel anionic proteins such as albumin.","The best-known source of these charges is heparan sulfate proteoglycan in the basement membrane."],"why":"The glomerular filtration barrier has three layers: fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte foot processes joined by slit diaphragms. Size selectivity comes mainly from the basement membrane mesh and the slit diaphragm. Charge selectivity comes from negatively charged heparan sulfate proteoglycans in the basement membrane and from the anionic coat on endothelial and podocyte surfaces. Loss of these charges, or loss of foot processes, lets albumin through and causes albuminuria.","whyNot":["Correct.","Nephrin forms the slit diaphragm, which acts mainly as a size barrier. It does not explain a difference between molecules of the same size.","Mesangial cells support the capillary loops and can contract to change surface area, but they do not form part of the filtration barrier.","Endothelial fenestrae are about 70 to 100 nm wide, far larger than albumin, so they do not hold it back by size and the open pores themselves do not repel it."],"study":{"topics":["Size and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-8"]}},{"id":"UR-C08-Q3","stem":"Which of the following genes and inheritance patterns best explain this family's disease?","options":["COL4A3, autosomal recessive","PKD1, autosomal dominant","COL4A5, X-linked","COL1A1, autosomal dominant"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Hematuria, sensorineural hearing loss, anterior lenticonus, and a split basement membrane identify Alport syndrome, a type IV collagen disorder.","Severe disease in a boy and his maternal uncle, with only mild disease in his mother, fits X-linked inheritance through a carrier mother.","X-linked Alport syndrome, about 80% of cases, is caused by COL4A5 mutations."],"why":"Alport syndrome is caused by mutations in the genes for the alpha-3, alpha-4, or alpha-5 chains of type IV collagen. These chains form the network of the mature glomerular basement membrane, the cochlea, and the lens capsule, which explains the kidney, ear, and eye findings. About 80% of cases are X-linked, from COL4A5 mutations. The rest are mostly autosomal recessive, from mutations in both copies of COL4A3 or COL4A4, which would rarely give an affected uncle and nephew linked through the mother.","whyNot":["Autosomal recessive Alport syndrome affects males and females equally and needs mutations from both parents. The pattern through the maternal line points to X-linked disease.","PKD1 mutations cause autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, with enlarged cystic kidneys and no hearing or lens changes.","Correct.","COL1A1 mutations cause osteogenesis imperfecta, with bone fragility and blue sclerae, and do not cause nephritis."],"study":{"topics":["Genetics of Alport syndrome","X-linked inheritance patterns"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-E1"]}},{"id":"UR-C08-Q4","stem":"His 13-year-old sister has persistent microscopic hematuria, normal hearing, and normal kidney function. Genetic testing shows she carries the same mutation. Which of the following best explains her milder disease?","options":["Anticipation from trinucleotide repeat expansion","Random inactivation of one X in each cell","Reduced penetrance of an autosomal dominant allele","Maternal uniparental disomy of the X chromosome"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-12-E1","obj":"UR-12-E.1","dok":2,"approach":["The mutation is X-linked, so a male with one mutant X has no normal copy, while a female has a second, normal X.","Early in development each female cell silences one X at random (lyonization).","Podocytes that keep the normal X active make normal type IV collagen, so her basement membrane is partly normal and her disease is milder."],"why":"Males with X-linked Alport syndrome usually develop progressive renal failure, often by early adulthood, along with hearing loss and eye changes. Heterozygous females are a mosaic because each cell inactivates one X chromosome at random. The share of podocytes expressing the normal allele sets disease severity. Most carrier females have only hematuria, though skewed X inactivation can cause proteinuria and kidney failure later in life.","whyNot":["Anticipation from repeat expansion occurs in disorders such as Huntington disease and myotonic dystrophy, not in collagen point mutations.","Correct.","The disease in this family is X-linked, not autosomal dominant, and reduced penetrance does not explain the consistent sex difference.","Maternal uniparental disomy would give her two copies of her mother's X chromosome. That would not make a carrier milder."],"study":{"topics":["X inactivation in female carriers","Clinical features of Alport syndrome"],"comps":["UR-12-E1","UR-9"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C09","system":"UR","dx":"Diabetic nephropathy with nodular glomerulosclerosis","codes":["UR-12-A2"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman with a 15-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension comes to the physician because of swelling of her legs for 2 months. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 80/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 152/90 mm Hg. Funduscopic examination shows dot-blot hemorrhages and hard exudates. There is 2+ pitting edema of the legs. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin A1c 9.1% (4.0 to 5.6), creatinine 2.4 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1), estimated GFR 23 mL/min/1.73 m2 (over 90), and urine protein 4.2 g/24 h. A renal biopsy shows diffuse mesangial expansion with nodular PAS-positive mesangial lesions, thickened glomerular basement membranes, and hyaline arteriolosclerosis of afferent and efferent arterioles. Diagnosis: diabetic nephropathy.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C09-Q1","stem":"Which of the following processes contributes most directly to the basement membrane thickening and hyaline arteriolar changes in this patient?","options":["Granular deposition of circulating immune complexes","Glycogen accumulation in proximal tubular cells","Deposition of fibrils of misfolded light chains","Nonenzymatic glycation of matrix proteins"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-12-A2","obj":"UR-12-A.8","dok":3,"approach":["Years of high glucose let glucose attach to proteins without enzymes, forming Amadori products and then advanced glycation end products (AGEs).","AGEs cross-link collagen and other matrix proteins and trap plasma proteins, so basement membranes thicken and hyaline builds up in arteriolar walls.","Pick nonenzymatic glycation and reject amyloid and immune complex mechanisms."],"why":"In chronic hyperglycemia, glucose binds the amino groups of long-lived proteins without an enzyme. After rearrangement these become advanced glycation end products. AGEs cross-link type IV collagen and other matrix proteins, trap leaked plasma proteins, and act on the receptor for AGEs (RAGE) to promote fibrosis. The results are basement membrane thickening, mesangial expansion with Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules, and hyaline arteriolosclerosis. Early hyperfiltration adds to the injury, and disease moves from microalbuminuria to nephrotic proteinuria and renal failure.","whyNot":["Granular immune complexes cause immune complex glomerulonephritis. Diabetic nephropathy has no immune deposits, though IgG and albumin can stick along the membranes.","Glycogen in tubular cells (Armanni-Ebstein change) is a real diabetic finding, but it affects tubules and does not thicken basement membranes or arterioles.","Misfolded light chain fibrils are AL amyloid, which forms Congo red-positive deposits and can mimic nodules, but the cause here is diabetes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Advanced glycation end products","Pathogenesis of diabetic nephropathy"],"comps":["UR-12-A2","UR-8"]}},{"id":"UR-C09-Q2","stem":"As her kidney function declines further, which of the following sets of serum findings is most likely to develop?","options":["High phosphate, high calcium, low PTH","Low phosphate, low calcium, low PTH","Low phosphate, high calcium, low PTH","High phosphate, low calcium, high PTH"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A4","obj":"UR-12-A.15","dok":3,"approach":["Falling GFR means less phosphate is excreted, so serum phosphate rises.","Damaged kidneys make less calcitriol, so the gut absorbs less calcium, and phosphate also binds calcium, so serum calcium falls.","Low calcium, high phosphate, and low calcitriol all stimulate the parathyroid glands, causing secondary hyperparathyroidism."],"why":"Chronic kidney disease, led by diabetes and hypertension, causes a mineral and bone disorder. Phosphate is retained, FGF23 rises, and the renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase falls, so calcitriol drops. Less calcitriol means less intestinal calcium absorption and weaker suppression of PTH. Secondary hyperparathyroidism follows and produces renal osteodystrophy, including high-turnover bone disease. Other consequences of advanced disease include hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, anemia from low erythropoietin, volume overload, and uremic pericarditis and encephalopathy.","whyNot":["High calcium with low PTH is uncommon in untreated chronic kidney disease and would suggest excess calcium or vitamin D intake.","Low phosphate with low calcium and low PTH does not fit a kidney that cannot excrete phosphate.","Low phosphate, high calcium, and low PTH would suggest non-PTH hypercalcemia, such as vitamin D excess or malignancy, not kidney failure.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Mineral and bone disorder of chronic kidney disease","Secondary hyperparathyroidism"],"comps":["UR-12-A4","UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C09-Q3","stem":"Two years earlier, when albuminuria was first found, she was started on lisinopril. Shortly after starting the drug, which of the following changes in renal plasma flow (RPF), glomerular filtration rate (GFR), and filtration fraction (FF) would be expected?","options":["Decreased RPF, decreased GFR, unchanged FF","Increased RPF, increased GFR, increased FF","Increased RPF, decreased GFR, decreased FF","Decreased RPF, increased GFR, increased FF"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole more than the afferent, so an ACE inhibitor mainly dilates the efferent arteriole.","Less resistance downstream raises flow through the glomerulus but lowers glomerular capillary pressure.","RPF rises, GFR falls, and filtration fraction (GFR divided by RPF) falls."],"why":"Glomerular pressure depends on the balance of afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance. Efferent dilation lowers total resistance, so renal plasma flow rises, but it also lets pressure drain out of the glomerulus, so GFR falls and filtration fraction falls. In diabetic nephropathy this lower intraglomerular pressure reduces hyperfiltration injury and proteinuria, which is why ACE inhibitors slow progression. A small early rise in creatinine is expected.","whyNot":["Afferent constriction, as from NSAIDs blocking prostaglandins or sympathetic stimulation, lowers both RPF and GFR, leaving FF about the same.","This pattern does not fit the drug. Efferent dilation lowers glomerular pressure, so GFR and FF fall instead of rising.","Correct.","Efferent constriction, as from angiotensin II, lowers RPF but raises GFR and FF. An ACE inhibitor has the opposite effect."],"study":{"topics":["Afferent and efferent arteriolar effects on GFR"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C09-Q4","stem":"Ten years ago, before she had kidney disease, she had polyuria and thirst. At that time her plasma glucose was 290 mg/dL (70 to 99 fasting) and her urine glucose was 3+. Which of the following best explains the glucose in her urine at that time?","options":["Increased filtration of glucose through a damaged glomerular barrier","Active secretion of glucose by collecting duct principal cells","Saturation of proximal tubular sodium-glucose cotransport","Reduced glucose reabsorption in the thick ascending limb"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.1","dok":3,"approach":["Glucose is freely filtered, so its filtered load rises in step with plasma glucose.","The proximal tubule reabsorbs glucose through SGLT2 and SGLT1, which have a transport maximum.","Once plasma glucose passes about 200 mg/dL, some nephrons are saturated and glucose spills into the urine, pulling water with it."],"why":"Filtered glucose is almost all reclaimed in the proximal tubule. SGLT2 in the early segment does most of the work, using the sodium gradient set up by the basolateral Na-K ATPase, and glucose leaves the cell through GLUT2. The carriers have a transport maximum. Glucose first appears in urine at about 200 mg/dL (the renal threshold), because nephrons differ in capacity. Unreabsorbed glucose acts as an osmotic diuretic and causes polyuria and thirst. SGLT2 inhibitor drugs work by producing this glucosuria on purpose.","whyNot":["Glucose is freely filtered by a normal glomerulus, so barrier damage does not increase its filtration.","The collecting duct does not secrete glucose. Glucose is handled by filtration and proximal reabsorption only.","Correct.","The thick ascending limb reabsorbs sodium, potassium, and chloride through NKCC2 and has no role in glucose reabsorption."],"study":{"topics":["Renal glucose handling and transport maximum","Osmotic diuresis"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-6"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C10","system":"UR","dx":"HIV-associated nephropathy (collapsing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis)","codes":["UR-12-A2","UR-12-A3"],"case":"A 38-year-old Black man comes to the physician because of leg swelling and foamy urine for 1 month. HIV infection was diagnosed 6 months ago, and he has not started antiretroviral therapy. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse is 80/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. There is 3+ pitting edema of the legs. Laboratory studies show CD4+ T-lymphocyte count 180/mm3 (500 to 1500), albumin 2.2 g/dL (3.5 to 5.5), total cholesterol 310 mg/dL (under 200), and creatinine 2.8 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Urine protein is 9.8 g/24 h. Ultrasonography shows enlarged, echogenic kidneys. A renal biopsy shows collapse of glomerular capillary tufts with enlarged overlying podocytes, dilated tubules, and no immune deposits on immunofluorescence. Diagnosis: HIV-associated nephropathy (collapsing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis).","questions":[{"id":"UR-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following conditions is also a recognized secondary cause of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis?","options":["Sickle cell disease","Long-standing rheumatoid arthritis","Hodgkin lymphoma","Chronic hepatitis B infection"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A2","obj":"UR-12-A.9","dok":2,"approach":["Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis is a podocyte injury that leaves scars in parts of some glomeruli.","Secondary causes either injure podocytes directly (HIV, heroin) or overload the remaining nephrons through hyperfiltration (sickle cell disease, obesity, reduced nephron mass).","Match each option to the glomerular disease it is classically linked with."],"why":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis is a leading cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults, especially Black adults, and it responds poorly to corticosteroids. Secondary causes include HIV infection, heroin use, sickle cell disease, obesity, and loss of nephron mass. In sickle cell disease, chronic anemia and hyperfiltration strain the podocytes. The collapsing variant, typical of HIV, has the worst prognosis and progresses quickly to renal failure.","whyNot":["Correct.","Long-standing inflammatory disease such as rheumatoid arthritis causes AA amyloidosis, which deposits Congo red-positive fibrils in glomeruli.","Hodgkin lymphoma is linked to minimal change disease, likely through T cell cytokines that injure podocytes.","Chronic hepatitis B is linked mainly to membranous nephropathy and also to membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis."],"study":{"topics":["Secondary causes of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Glomerular diseases linked to systemic conditions"],"comps":["UR-12-A2","UR-12-A3"]}},{"id":"UR-C10-Q2","stem":"Urine sediment examined under polarized light shows round bodies with a Maltese cross pattern. These structures are most likely composed of which of the following?","options":["Cholesterol esters in lipid-laden tubular cells","Calcium oxalate monohydrate crystals from oxalate excess","Uric acid crystals in acidic urine","Myoglobin pigment within coarse granular casts"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.7","dok":2,"approach":["Heavy proteinuria and a high cholesterol mean nephrotic syndrome.","In nephrotic syndrome the liver makes more lipoproteins and their breakdown slows, and lipoproteins leak through the damaged barrier.","Tubular cells take up the filtered lipid and are shed as oval fat bodies, whose cholesterol esters show a Maltese cross under polarized light."],"why":"Low oncotic pressure in nephrotic syndrome stimulates hepatic synthesis of lipoproteins, including LDL and lipoprotein(a). Lipoprotein lipase activity falls, so VLDL breakdown also slows, and cholesterol and triglycerides rise. Lipoproteins that cross the injured barrier are taken up by tubular cells. These cells shed into the urine as oval fat bodies, or the lipid is trapped in fatty casts. Cholesterol esters are birefringent and show a Maltese cross under polarized light.","whyNot":["Correct.","Calcium oxalate monohydrate crystals are dumbbell or needle shaped, as in ethylene glycol poisoning. They do not form Maltese crosses.","Uric acid crystals are yellow rhomboid or rosette shaped. They form in acidic urine, as in gout and tumor lysis.","Myoglobin pigment gives dark granular casts in rhabdomyolysis and does not show a Maltese cross."],"study":{"topics":["Hyperlipidemia of nephrotic syndrome","Oval fat bodies and fatty casts"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-A3"]}},{"id":"UR-C10-Q3","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains this patient's kidney injury?","options":["Opportunistic cytomegalovirus infection of glomeruli","Proximal tubule toxicity of antiretroviral drugs","Granular immune complexes of HIV antigens","Viral gene expression in infected podocytes"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.6","dok":3,"approach":["Collapsing glomerulosclerosis with enlarged podocytes and no immune deposits means the podocytes are injured directly.","HIV infects podocytes and tubular epithelial cells, and viral proteins such as Nef and Vpr make podocytes lose their mature features and proliferate.","Untreated infection with a falling CD4 count and inherited APOL1 risk variants raise the risk."],"why":"HIV-associated nephropathy is caused by direct infection of renal epithelial cells. HIV genes expressed in podocytes, especially nef and vpr, make the cells lose their mature features and proliferate, so the capillary tuft collapses. Infected tubular cells cause microcystic tubular dilation, which helps make the kidneys large and echogenic. The disease is far more common in people of African ancestry who carry two APOL1 risk variants. Antiretroviral therapy reduces the risk and slows progression.","whyNot":["Cytomegalovirus infection occurs at very low CD4 counts and causes retinitis, colitis, and pneumonitis. It does not cause collapsing glomerulosclerosis.","Drugs such as tenofovir can injure the proximal tubule, but he has not started antiretroviral therapy, and tubular injury does not cause nephrotic-range proteinuria.","HIV immune complex kidney disease exists, but it shows granular deposits on immunofluorescence. His biopsy shows none.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of HIV-associated nephropathy","Infections linked to glomerular disease"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-A2"]}},{"id":"UR-C10-Q4","stem":"Three weeks later, he develops sudden left flank pain and gross hematuria. Doppler ultrasonography shows thrombosis of the left renal vein. He has not received heparin. Which of the following most likely contributed to this complication?","options":["Reduced plasma factor VIII activity","Urinary loss of antithrombin III","Decreased hepatic synthesis of fibrinogen","Antibodies to heparin and platelet factor 4"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A3","obj":"UR-12-A.10","dok":3,"approach":["Nephrotic syndrome is a hypercoagulable state, and renal vein thrombosis is a classic complication.","Antithrombin III is a midsize protein that leaks through the damaged barrier, while large clotting factors stay in the blood and rise with increased hepatic synthesis.","Losing a natural anticoagulant while procoagulants rise tips the balance toward clotting."],"why":"Nephrotic syndrome changes several systems at once. Albumin loss lowers oncotic pressure and causes edema. The liver responds by making more proteins, including lipoproteins, fibrinogen, and factors V and VIII, so lipids and procoagulants rise. Antithrombin III, about the size of albumin, is lost in the urine, which removes a key brake on thrombin and factor Xa. Renal vein thrombosis is the classic result, and it is most common in membranous nephropathy. Loss of immunoglobulins also raises the risk of infection.","whyNot":["Factor VIII is a large protein that is kept in the blood and rises in nephrotic syndrome. Low factor VIII would cause bleeding.","Correct.","Fibrinogen synthesis increases in nephrotic syndrome. Less fibrinogen would reduce clotting.","Heparin-PF4 antibodies cause heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, which needs prior heparin exposure. He has not received heparin."],"study":{"topics":["Hypercoagulability in nephrotic syndrome","Complications of nephrotic syndrome"],"comps":["UR-12-A3","UR-8"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C11","system":"UR","dx":"Fabry disease nephropathy","codes":["UR-12-A2"],"case":"A 34-year-old man comes to the physician because of swelling of his ankles for 2 months. Since childhood he has had episodes of burning pain in his hands and feet, and he sweats very little during exercise. His maternal grandfather required dialysis. Blood pressure is 138/86 mm Hg. Physical examination shows clusters of small dark red papules between the umbilicus and the knees. Urine protein excretion is 2.4 g/24 h (less than 0.15). Serum creatinine is 1.6 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Leukocyte alpha-galactosidase A activity is markedly decreased. Kidney biopsy shows podocytes with vacuolated, foamy cytoplasm and segmental scarring of some glomerular tufts. Electron microscopy shows lamellated inclusions in podocytes. Diagnosis: Fabry disease nephropathy.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C11-Q1","stem":"A timed urine collection is performed. His urine creatinine concentration is 96 mg/dL, urine flow is 1.25 mL/min, and plasma creatinine is 1.6 mg/dL. Inulin clearance measured on the same day is 62 mL/min. Which of the following best explains the difference between his creatinine clearance and his inulin clearance?","options":["Proximal tubular secretion of creatinine","Distal tubular reabsorption of creatinine","Collecting duct reabsorption of inulin","Binding of inulin to plasma albumin"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate creatinine clearance: (96 mg/dL x 1.25 mL/min) / 1.6 mg/dL = 75 mL/min.","Compare it with inulin clearance, which equals GFR: 75 is above 62.","A clearance above GFR means the kidney adds the substance to urine by net secretion."],"why":"Clearance is urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration. Inulin clearance equals GFR because inulin is freely filtered and not reabsorbed or secreted. Creatinine is freely filtered and also secreted by the proximal tubule through organic cation transporters, so its clearance runs above the true GFR. The gap widens as GFR falls, because secretion makes up a larger share of the creatinine excreted. A clearance below GFR means net reabsorption, and a clearance above GFR means net secretion.","whyNot":["Correct.","Net reabsorption would make creatinine clearance lower than GFR. His creatinine clearance (75 mL/min) is higher than his inulin clearance.","Inulin is freely filtered and is neither reabsorbed nor secreted. That is why its clearance equals GFR.","Inulin is not protein bound, so all of it in plasma is filterable. Protein binding would lower its clearance, which is not the reason creatinine clearance is higher."],"study":{"topics":["Renal clearance calculation","Inulin versus creatinine clearance"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-12-A4"]}},{"id":"UR-C11-Q2","stem":"He and his wife, who is unaffected and has no family history of the disease, have two daughters and one son. Which of the following best describes which of his children inherited his disease-causing allele?","options":["Half of his sons and half of his daughters","All of his daughters and none of his sons","All of his sons and none of his daughters","Half of his sons and none of his daughters"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.7","dok":2,"approach":["Fabry disease is caused by mutations in GLA, an X-linked gene for alpha-galactosidase A.","An affected man has one X, which he passes to every daughter, and his Y, which he passes to every son.","So every daughter is a heterozygote and no son is affected."],"why":"Fabry disease is an X-linked lysosomal storage disease. Deficient alpha-galactosidase A lets globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) build up in vascular endothelium, podocytes, tubular cells, nerves, and heart. This produces angiokeratomas, burning neuropathic pain, reduced sweating, and progressive proteinuric kidney failure. An affected father passes his X to all daughters, who are heterozygotes. Because of X inactivation, many heterozygous women develop symptoms, usually milder and later than in men.","whyNot":["This pattern fits autosomal dominant inheritance. The GLA gene is on the X chromosome, so a father passes it to his daughters only.","Correct.","A father gives his Y chromosome to every son, never his X. Sons cannot inherit his X-linked allele.","A father never passes his X chromosome to a son, so no son can inherit the allele from him. Every daughter receives his X, so none of them can escape it."],"study":{"topics":["X-linked inheritance","Fabry disease and globotriaosylceramide"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-A2"]}},{"id":"UR-C11-Q3","stem":"The segmental scarring seen on his biopsy is a pattern of glomerular injury that also occurs in HIV infection, heroin use, sickle cell disease, and severe obesity. Loss of which of the following glomerular cells most directly leads to this pattern?","options":["Mesangial cells","Glomerular endothelial cells","Podocytes","Parietal epithelial cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A2","obj":"UR-12-A.9","dok":3,"approach":["Name the pattern: segmental sclerosis in some glomeruli is focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS).","Recall the common target in primary and secondary FSGS: the podocyte.","In Fabry disease, Gb3 storage injures podocytes, which detach and leave bare basement membrane that scars."],"why":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis starts with podocyte injury. When podocytes detach, the bare basement membrane sticks to Bowman capsule and the segment scars. Secondary causes include HIV (often the collapsing variant), heroin use, sickle cell disease, obesity, and loss of nephron mass. Storage of Gb3 in podocytes produces the same pattern in Fabry disease. FSGS causes nephrotic-range proteinuria, responds poorly to corticosteroids, and often progresses to kidney failure.","whyNot":["Mesangial cells support the capillary loops and clear trapped material. Their expansion and proliferation mark IgA nephropathy and diabetic nodules, not podocyte-driven segmental sclerosis.","Endothelial injury drives thrombotic microangiopathy, as in hemolytic uremic syndrome, with fibrin thrombi in capillaries rather than segmental scarring.","Correct.","Parietal cells line Bowman capsule. They can proliferate to form crescents in rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, but the starting lesion in this pattern is loss of visceral epithelial cells."],"study":{"topics":["Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Podocyte injury"],"comps":["UR-12-A2","UR-12-A3"]}},{"id":"UR-C11-Q4","stem":"The biopsy also shows Gb3 inclusions in a tubule segment that lies next to the vascular pole of its own glomerulus. Its cuboidal cells have no brush border, have a smaller cross-section than the adjacent proximal tubule, and contain a cluster of tightly packed nuclei facing the afferent arteriole. Which of the following segments is this?","options":["Distal tubule","Proximal tubule","Cortical collecting duct","Thin descending limb"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Use the brush border first: its absence rules out the proximal tubule.","Use the location: the distal tubule returns to the vascular pole of its own glomerulus, where its wall forms the macula densa.","The packed nuclei facing the afferent arteriole are the macula densa."],"why":"The proximal tubule has tall cuboidal cells with a brush border and many mitochondria. Thin limbs are simple squamous. The distal tubule (the end of the thick ascending limb and the distal convoluted tubule) is cuboidal without a brush border. It returns to the vascular pole of its own glomerulus, where the end of the thick ascending limb forms the macula densa. Collecting ducts have principal and intercalated cells with clear cell borders. In Fabry disease, Gb3 collects prominently in distal tubule and loop of Henle epithelium as well as in podocytes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Proximal tubule cells are tall cuboidal cells with a prominent brush border and abundant mitochondria. This segment has no brush border.","Collecting ducts are lined by principal and intercalated cells with distinct lateral borders. They run in medullary rays and do not contact the vascular pole of a glomerulus.","The thin limbs are lined by simple squamous epithelium and lie in the medulla, not at the vascular pole."],"study":{"topics":["Nephron epithelium by segment","Macula densa"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-9"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C12","system":"UR","dx":"Minimal change disease","codes":["UR-12-A3"],"case":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of puffiness around his eyes for 1 week. It began a few days after an upper respiratory infection. His temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 102/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg. Physical examination shows periorbital edema, pitting edema of the legs, and scrotal swelling. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no blood. The urine protein to creatinine ratio is 7.5 mg/mg (less than 0.2). Serum albumin is 1.7 g/dL (3.5 to 5.5), total cholesterol is 360 mg/dL (less than 170), and creatinine is 0.3 mg/dL (0.2 to 0.4). Serum C3 is normal. Diagnosis: minimal change disease.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C12-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his elevated serum cholesterol?","options":["Defective hepatic LDL receptor gene","Increased intestinal cholesterol absorption","Impaired renal excretion of cholesterol","Increased hepatic lipoprotein synthesis"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-12-A3","obj":"UR-12-A.10","dok":2,"approach":["Link the low serum albumin and low plasma oncotic pressure to the liver's response.","The liver raises synthesis of albumin and, with it, apolipoprotein B lipoproteins such as VLDL and LDL.","Reduced lipoprotein lipase activity also slows triglyceride-rich lipoprotein clearance."],"why":"In nephrotic syndrome, the liver responds to low albumin and low oncotic pressure by making more protein, including apolipoprotein B lipoproteins, so VLDL and LDL rise. Catabolism also falls, partly because lipoprotein lipase activity is reduced. The other consequences of heavy proteinuria are edema from low oncotic pressure, a clotting tendency from urinary loss of antithrombin III, and infection from urinary loss of immunoglobulin.","whyNot":["Familial hypercholesterolemia causes lifelong high LDL from birth. His cholesterol rose together with heavy proteinuria and low albumin.","Hypoalbuminemia does not increase intestinal absorption. The lipid rise in nephrotic syndrome comes from the liver and reduced clearance.","The kidney is not a route for cholesterol excretion. Cholesterol leaves the body mainly in bile.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hyperlipidemia of nephrotic syndrome","Consequences of nephrotic syndrome"],"comps":["UR-12-A3","UR-8"]}},{"id":"UR-C12-Q2","stem":"If a kidney biopsy were performed, which of the following findings would be most likely?","options":["Mesangial IgA deposits with mesangial proliferation","Diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes","Subepithelial humps with granular IgG and C3","Linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A3","obj":"UR-12-A.11","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the classic child: nephrotic syndrome, no hematuria, normal blood pressure, normal C3.","Minimal change disease looks normal on light microscopy and immunofluorescence.","The only lesion is podocyte foot process effacement on electron microscopy."],"why":"Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in young children. Light microscopy and immunofluorescence are normal, and electron microscopy shows effacement of podocyte foot processes. Loss of the glomerular charge barrier gives selective albuminuria. Most children respond well to corticosteroids, so biopsy is usually reserved for those who do not. In adults it can be associated with Hodgkin lymphoma and with NSAID use.","whyNot":["Mesangial IgA marks IgA nephropathy, which usually presents with hematuria during or right after a mucosal infection.","Correct.","Subepithelial humps with granular IgG and C3 are the pattern of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, which presents with hematuria, hypertension, and low C3.","Linear IgG marks anti-GBM disease, a nephritic process with hematuria and often lung hemorrhage."],"study":{"topics":["Minimal change disease pathology","Glomerular disease patterns on biopsy"],"comps":["UR-12-A3","UR-10"]}},{"id":"UR-C12-Q3","stem":"His edema develops as the kidneys retain sodium and water in about the same proportion as in plasma, and his serum sodium stays normal. Compared with before his illness, which of the following best describes the change in his body fluid compartments?","options":["ECF increased, ICF increased, osmolality decreased","ECF unchanged, ICF increased, osmolality decreased","ECF increased, ICF unchanged, osmolality unchanged","ECF increased, ICF decreased, osmolality increased"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.6","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the fluid gained: sodium and water in plasma proportion is an isotonic gain.","Isotonic fluid does not change osmolality, so no water shifts across cell membranes.","All of the gain stays in the ECF, much of it in the interstitium as edema."],"why":"Water moves between ICF and ECF only when ECF osmolality changes. An isotonic gain adds volume to the ECF without changing its osmolality, so cell volume stays the same. A hypotonic gain lowers osmolality, and water enters cells, so both compartments expand. A hypertonic gain raises osmolality and pulls water out of cells. In nephrotic syndrome the extra ECF collects in the interstitium because low plasma oncotic pressure favors filtration out of capillaries.","whyNot":["This is a hypotonic gain, such as retained free water. His serum sodium is normal, so osmolality has not fallen.","Water alone would spread into both compartments. Retained isotonic fluid stays in the extracellular space.","Correct.","This is a hypertonic gain, such as hypertonic saline. Water would leave cells, and serum sodium would rise."],"study":{"topics":["Body fluid compartment shifts","Isotonic volume expansion"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-12-A3"]}},{"id":"UR-C12-Q4","stem":"In his disease, immunofluorescence shows no immunoglobulin or complement in the glomeruli. A rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis that also shows few or no immune deposits on immunofluorescence is most characteristic of which of the following?","options":["Diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis","Anti-glomerular basement membrane disease","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.1","dok":3,"approach":["Sort immune glomerular injury by immunofluorescence pattern: linear, granular, or pauci-immune.","Linear IgG is anti-GBM (type II). Granular deposits are immune complexes (type III).","Pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis is ANCA-associated vasculitis, such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis."],"why":"Immune glomerular injury falls into three groups. Type II hypersensitivity is anti-GBM disease, with linear IgG and, in Goodpasture syndrome, lung hemorrhage. Type III is immune complex disease with granular deposits, as in poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis and lupus. The third group is pauci-immune disease, in which ANCA-activated neutrophils injure the capillaries and immunofluorescence shows little or no immunoglobulin. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (PR3-ANCA) and microscopic polyangiitis (MPO-ANCA) are examples. Minimal change disease also lacks deposits, but it is a podocyte disease, not an inflammatory glomerulonephritis.","whyNot":["Lupus nephritis is type III immune complex disease with granular deposits of many immunoglobulins and complement, a full-house pattern.","Anti-GBM disease is type II hypersensitivity, with antibodies to the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen. It shows bright linear IgG on immunofluorescence.","This is type III immune complex disease with granular IgG and C3 deposits.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hypersensitivity types in glomerular disease","ANCA-associated vasculitis"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-A1"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C13","system":"UR","dx":"Hepatitis B associated membranous nephropathy","codes":["UR-12-A3"],"case":"A 38-year-old man comes to the physician because of foamy urine and swelling of his legs for 6 weeks. Chronic hepatitis B was diagnosed 10 years ago, and he has not been treated. Blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Physical examination shows pitting edema to the knees. Urine protein excretion is 6.8 g/24 h (less than 0.15). Serum albumin is 2.2 g/dL (3.5 to 5.5), total cholesterol is 320 mg/dL (less than 200), and creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). HBsAg and HBeAg are positive. Serum anti-phospholipase A2 receptor (anti-PLA2R) antibody is negative. Kidney biopsy shows diffusely thickened glomerular capillary walls without cell proliferation, and immunofluorescence shows granular IgG and C3 along the capillary walls. Diagnosis: hepatitis B associated membranous nephropathy.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C13-Q1","stem":"During his hospital stay he drinks large volumes of water, and much of it is retained. His serum sodium falls from 138 mEq/L to 127 mEq/L (136 to 145). Compared with admission, which of the following best describes the change in his body fluid compartments?","options":["ECF increased, ICF increased, osmolality decreased","ECF decreased, ICF increased, osmolality decreased","ECF increased, ICF decreased, osmolality increased","ECF increased, ICF unchanged, osmolality unchanged"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.6","dok":3,"approach":["Classify the change: retained free water is a hypotonic gain.","Lower ECF osmolality draws water into cells until osmolality is equal on both sides.","Both compartments enlarge, and osmolality in both falls."],"why":"Free water distributes across both compartments in proportion to their size, about two thirds into cells. A hypotonic gain therefore expands both ECF and ICF and lowers osmolality everywhere, which shows up as a lower serum sodium. In the brain, this cell swelling explains the confusion and seizures of severe acute hyponatremia. An isotonic gain stays in the ECF, and a hypertonic gain shrinks cells.","whyNot":["Correct.","This pattern follows loss of salt in excess of water, as with heavy sweating replaced by water alone. He has gained water, so ECF volume rises.","This is a hypertonic gain. Serum sodium would rise, and water would move out of cells.","This is an isotonic gain, such as normal saline. His falling serum sodium shows that osmolality has dropped."],"study":{"topics":["Body fluid compartment shifts","Hypotonic fluid gain"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-12-A3"]}},{"id":"UR-C13-Q2","stem":"Electron microscopy of his biopsy is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Mesangial deposits with mesangial cell proliferation","Subendothelial deposits with basement membrane duplication","Foot process effacement without immune deposits","Subepithelial deposits between basement membrane spikes"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A3","obj":"UR-12-A.12","dok":2,"approach":["Nephrotic syndrome in an adult with thick capillary walls, no proliferation, and granular IgG is membranous nephropathy.","In membranous nephropathy, immune complexes form on the outer (subepithelial) side of the basement membrane.","New basement membrane grows between the deposits, giving spikes on silver stain and a spike-and-dome look."],"why":"Membranous nephropathy is a common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults. Immune complexes sit in the subepithelial space, and the basement membrane grows up around them, producing spikes on silver stain. Most primary cases are caused by autoantibodies to the podocyte phospholipase A2 receptor (anti-PLA2R). Secondary causes include lupus, hepatitis B, solid tumors, and drugs such as NSAIDs. His negative anti-PLA2R and active hepatitis B point to a secondary form.","whyNot":["Mesangial deposits with proliferation mark IgA nephropathy. His biopsy shows thick capillary walls without proliferation.","Subendothelial deposits with a split, tram-track basement membrane mark membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis. Hepatitis C is a classic cause of that pattern.","Effacement without deposits is minimal change disease. His immunofluorescence shows granular IgG and C3, so deposits are present.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Membranous nephropathy","Location of glomerular immune deposits"],"comps":["UR-12-A3","UR-10"]}},{"id":"UR-C13-Q3","stem":"His 7-year-old daughter is tested. Her results show negative HBsAg, negative anti-HBc, and positive anti-HBs. Which of the following best explains these results?","options":["Recovery from a past natural infection","Immunity from hepatitis B vaccination","Window period of acute infection","Chronic hepatitis B carrier state"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.7","dok":2,"approach":["Note that anti-HBs is present but anti-HBc is absent.","The vaccine contains only recombinant surface antigen, so it induces anti-HBs and never anti-HBc.","Anti-HBs alone therefore means immunity from vaccination."],"why":"The hepatitis B vaccine is made of recombinant HBsAg. It induces neutralizing anti-HBs antibodies that protect against infection. Because the vaccine has no core antigen, vaccinated people are anti-HBc negative. Natural infection produces anti-HBc, and recovery adds anti-HBs. Vaccination prevents chronic hepatitis B and so prevents its immune complex complications, including membranous nephropathy.","whyNot":["Natural infection exposes the immune system to the core antigen, so anti-HBc would be positive along with anti-HBs.","Correct.","In the window period HBsAg has cleared but anti-HBs is not yet detectable, and IgM anti-HBc is positive. She has anti-HBs and no anti-HBc.","A carrier has persistent HBsAg and positive anti-HBc. Her HBsAg is negative."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis B serology","Recombinant subunit vaccines"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-A3"]}},{"id":"UR-C13-Q4","stem":"Urine sediment shows tubular cells filled with refractile droplets and casts that show a Maltese cross pattern under polarized light. Which of the following lipids is responsible for this birefringence?","options":["Free fatty acids","Cholesteryl esters","Triglycerides","Conjugated bile acids"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.7","dok":2,"approach":["Link heavy proteinuria to leakage of lipoproteins across the damaged glomerular barrier.","Tubular cells take up the filtered lipid, becoming oval fat bodies, and shed into fatty casts.","The droplets that polarize as a Maltese cross are cholesteryl esters."],"why":"In nephrotic syndrome, lipoproteins leak through the injured glomerular barrier. Tubular cells absorb the lipid and are shed as oval fat bodies, or they form fatty casts. Under polarized light, cholesteryl ester droplets are birefringent and show a Maltese cross. This lipiduria accompanies the hyperlipidemia of nephrotic syndrome, and fatty casts point to a nephrotic process.","whyNot":["Free fatty acids travel bound to albumin and do not form the anisotropic droplets that make a Maltese cross.","Correct.","Triglycerides stain with fat stains but are not birefringent under polarized light.","Bile acids are not part of the lipid droplets in urine sediment. Bile pigments color urine in cholestasis but do not form these droplets."],"study":{"topics":["Lipiduria and fatty casts","Urine sediment in nephrotic syndrome"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-A"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C14","system":"UR","dx":"AL amyloidosis with nephrotic syndrome and renal vein thrombosis","codes":["UR-12-A3","UR-12-F"],"case":"A 63-year-old man comes to the physician because of progressive leg swelling and fatigue for 3 months. Physical examination shows an enlarged tongue with indentations from his teeth and pitting edema to the thighs. Urine protein excretion is 9.5 g/24 h (less than 0.15). Serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL (3.5 to 5.5). The serum free kappa to lambda light chain ratio is 0.08 (0.26 to 1.65), and bone marrow biopsy shows 9% clonal lambda plasma cells. A percutaneous kidney biopsy shows glomeruli filled with amorphous pink extracellular material. Three weeks later he develops sudden left flank pain and gross hematuria, and CT shows a thrombus in the left renal vein. Diagnosis: AL amyloidosis with nephrotic syndrome complicated by left renal vein thrombosis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C14-Q1","stem":"Two days after the flank pain begins, he notices a new soft mass of dilated veins in the left side of his scrotum. Which of the following anatomic features best explains why this developed on the left side?","options":["The left renal vein passes behind the abdominal aorta","The left testicular vein drains into the inferior vena cava","The left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein","The left testicular artery arises from the left renal artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the scrotal mass of dilated veins as a varicocele.","Recall venous drainage: left testicular vein to left renal vein, right testicular vein to the IVC.","Blockage of the left renal vein raises pressure in the left testicular vein and pampiniform plexus."],"why":"The left renal vein is long. It crosses in front of the aorta just below the origin of the superior mesenteric artery, where it can be compressed (nutcracker phenomenon). It receives the left testicular (or ovarian) vein and the left suprarenal vein. Thrombosis of the left renal vein, or invasion by renal cell carcinoma, raises pressure in the left testicular vein and causes a new left varicocele. The right testicular vein drains straight into the IVC, so this does not happen on the right.","whyNot":["The left renal vein normally crosses in front of the aorta, just below the superior mesenteric artery. A retroaortic vein is an uncommon variant.","This describes the right testicular vein. On the left, the testicular vein drains into the left renal vein, so a renal vein clot backs up into it.","Correct.","The testicular arteries arise from the aorta. Arterial anatomy would not cause venous dilation."],"study":{"topics":["Left renal vein anatomy","Varicocele"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-3"]}},{"id":"UR-C14-Q2","stem":"Congo red stain of the kidney biopsy shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light. Which of the following proteins most likely forms these deposits?","options":["Serum amyloid A acute phase protein","Beta-2 microglobulin","Wild-type transthyretin","Immunoglobulin light chains"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-12-A3","obj":"UR-12-A.13","dok":3,"approach":["Apple-green birefringence with Congo red confirms amyloid, a beta-pleated sheet protein.","Use the clues to the source: abnormal light chain ratio and clonal plasma cells.","Misfolded light chains from the plasma cell clone form AL amyloid."],"why":"Amyloid is misfolded protein arranged in beta-pleated sheets. It binds Congo red and shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light. AL amyloid comes from monoclonal light chains, most often lambda, made by a plasma cell clone. AA amyloid comes from serum amyloid A in chronic inflammation. In the kidney, amyloid fills the mesangium and capillary walls and disrupts the filtration barrier, which causes heavy proteinuria. An enlarged tongue is a classic sign of AL amyloidosis.","whyNot":["AA amyloid comes from serum amyloid A, an acute phase protein made in chronic inflammation such as rheumatoid arthritis or chronic infection. He has a clonal plasma cell disorder.","Beta-2 microglobulin amyloid forms in patients on long-term dialysis and deposits in joints and the carpal tunnel.","Transthyretin amyloid usually affects the heart and nerves in older adults or in hereditary forms, and does not explain his abnormal light chain ratio.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Amyloid types","Congo red staining"],"comps":["UR-12-A3","UR-8"]}},{"id":"UR-C14-Q3","stem":"Which of the following findings in the left kidney is most likely on his CT scan?","options":["Enlarged, swollen kidney","Small kidney with cortical scars","Dilated pelvis and calyces","Wedge-shaped cortical infarct"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-F","obj":"UR-12-F.6","dok":2,"approach":["Venous outflow is blocked while arterial inflow continues.","Blood and fluid back up into the kidney, causing congestion, swelling, flank pain, and hematuria.","Nephrotic syndrome is a leading cause, because urinary loss of antithrombin III makes blood hypercoagulable."],"why":"Renal vein thrombosis causes flank pain, hematuria, and an enlarged, congested kidney. The main causes are nephrotic syndrome, especially membranous nephropathy, renal cell carcinoma growing into the vein, and dehydration in infants. In nephrotic syndrome, antithrombin III is small enough to be lost in urine, while the liver makes more clotting factors such as fibrinogen. Both shifts favor thrombosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Small, scarred kidneys reflect chronic damage such as chronic pyelonephritis or vascular disease. Acute venous blockage makes the kidney swell.","A dilated collecting system means urinary obstruction, such as a stone or tumor, not venous thrombosis.","A wedge-shaped infarct follows blockage of an artery branch, usually by an embolus. His clot is in the vein."],"study":{"topics":["Renal vein thrombosis","Hypercoagulability in nephrotic syndrome"],"comps":["UR-12-F","UR-12-A3"]}},{"id":"UR-C14-Q4","stem":"A CT scan obtained after his kidney biopsy showed a hematoma surrounding the kidney within the perirenal fat. Which of the following structures limited the spread of this hematoma?","options":["Transversalis fascia","Fibrous renal capsule","Renal fascia","Parietal peritoneum"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.3","dok":3,"approach":["List the coverings from inside out: fibrous capsule, perirenal fat, renal fascia, pararenal fat.","Blood in the perirenal fat lies outside the capsule but inside the renal fascia.","So the renal fascia contains a perinephric hematoma."],"why":"The kidney is wrapped in layers. The fibrous capsule sits on the kidney surface. Around it is perirenal fat, enclosed by the renal (Gerota) fascia, which also encloses the adrenal gland. Outside the fascia is pararenal fat. A perinephric hematoma or abscess stays within the renal fascia. At the hilum, structures run from front to back as renal vein, renal artery, and renal pelvis.","whyNot":["Transversalis fascia lines the inner abdominal wall. It lies outside the pararenal fat and does not enclose the perirenal space.","The fibrous capsule clings to the kidney surface. Blood under it forms a thin subcapsular hematoma that hugs the kidney, not one within the perirenal fat.","Correct.","The peritoneum lies in front of the renal fascia and pararenal fat. It would limit blood only after the blood had escaped the renal fascia."],"study":{"topics":["Renal coverings","Perirenal space"],"comps":["UR-3","UR-4"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C15","system":"UR","dx":"Myeloma cast nephropathy with acquired Fanconi syndrome","codes":["UR-12-B1","UR-12-A4"],"case":"A 67-year-old woman comes to the physician because of low back pain and fatigue for 4 months. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL (12 to 16), serum calcium 11.6 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), creatinine 2.9 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1), fasting plasma glucose 92 mg/dL (70 to 99), phosphate 1.9 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), bicarbonate 17 mEq/L (22 to 28), and chloride 112 mEq/L (98 to 106). Urinalysis shows 2+ glucose. Urine studies show generalized aminoaciduria and large amounts of free kappa light chains. Bone marrow biopsy shows 35% clonal kappa plasma cells. Kidney biopsy shows tubules plugged with dense, fractured casts surrounded by multinucleated giant cells. Diagnosis: multiple myeloma cast nephropathy with acquired Fanconi syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C15-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the glucose in her urine?","options":["Plasma glucose above the renal threshold","Reduced proximal glucose transport capacity","Increased collecting duct glucose secretion","Impaired distal tubule glucose reabsorption"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.1","dok":3,"approach":["Note glucosuria with a normal plasma glucose, so the filtered load is normal.","Glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule by SGLT2 and SGLT1 with a transport maximum.","Injury to proximal cells lowers that transport maximum, so normal filtered glucose spills into urine."],"why":"Filtered glucose is reclaimed almost entirely in the proximal tubule. SGLT2 in the early proximal tubule does most of the work, using the sodium gradient made by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase. Because the carriers can be saturated, glucose appears in urine when plasma glucose exceeds about 200 mg/dL, as in uncontrolled diabetes, and the extra solute causes an osmotic diuresis. Here the plasma glucose is normal, so the glucosuria must come from damaged proximal cells with a lower transport maximum.","whyNot":["The renal threshold is about 200 mg/dL. Her plasma glucose is 92 mg/dL, so filtered glucose is well within normal reabsorptive capacity.","Correct.","The kidney does not secrete glucose. Glucose appears in urine only when filtered glucose escapes proximal reabsorption.","Almost all filtered glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule. The distal nephron has little capacity to reclaim it."],"study":{"topics":["SGLT2 and glucose transport maximum","Renal glucosuria"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-B"]}},{"id":"UR-C15-Q2","stem":"Filtered free light chains are normally taken up by endocytosis and broken down in the tubule segment whose failure explains her glucosuria, aminoaciduria, and phosphaturia. Which of the following best describes the epithelium of that segment?","options":["Simple squamous cells with few organelles and no brush border","Cuboidal cells with lateral interdigitations but no brush border","Principal cells mixed with acid-secreting intercalated cells","Cuboidal cells with a brush border and many mitochondria"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Glucosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and bicarbonate loss point to the proximal tubule.","The proximal tubule reclaims filtered small proteins, including light chains, by endocytosis.","Its cells are cuboidal with a brush border for surface area and many mitochondria for active transport."],"why":"Proximal tubule cells have a dense microvillous brush border, a busy endocytic and lysosomal system, and many basal mitochondria that power Na+/K+ ATPase. They reclaim filtered low molecular weight proteins such as light chains. When light chains flood these cells, lysosomal handling fails and transport is impaired, causing Fanconi syndrome. The same high energy demand makes the proximal tubule very sensitive to ischemia.","whyNot":["This describes the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, which do little active transport or protein uptake.","This describes the distal tubule. It lacks the brush border and endocytic machinery used to reclaim filtered proteins.","These line the collecting duct, where sodium, potassium, water, and acid are fine-tuned. They do not reclaim filtered proteins.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Proximal tubule histology","Tubular handling of filtered proteins"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-12-B"]}},{"id":"UR-C15-Q3","stem":"Which of the following conditions also causes this pattern of tubular dysfunction?","options":["Bartter syndrome","Lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Wilson disease","Liddle syndrome"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-12-B","obj":"UR-12-B.4","dok":2,"approach":["Name her pattern: glucosuria with normal plasma glucose, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and bicarbonate wasting is Fanconi syndrome.","Fanconi syndrome is generalized proximal tubule failure.","Its causes include cystinosis, Wilson disease, multiple myeloma, and some drugs and heavy metals."],"why":"Fanconi syndrome is generalized failure of proximal tubule reabsorption. Glucose, amino acids, phosphate, bicarbonate, and uric acid are lost in urine. Loss of bicarbonate causes a proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis, and loss of phosphate causes rickets or osteomalacia. In children, cystinosis is the classic inherited cause. Acquired causes include multiple myeloma light chains, Wilson disease (copper injury), heavy metals such as lead, and drugs such as tenofovir and expired tetracycline.","whyNot":["Bartter syndrome is a defect in thick ascending limb salt transport. It causes hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, not proximal wasting of glucose and amino acids.","Lithium blocks ADH action in the collecting duct, causing dilute polyuria. It does not cause glucosuria or aminoaciduria.","Correct.","Liddle syndrome is overactivity of the collecting duct sodium channel ENaC, causing hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis."],"study":{"topics":["Fanconi syndrome","Proximal renal tubular acidosis"],"comps":["UR-12-B","UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C15-Q4","stem":"Her serum bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L, and her urine pH is 5.4. When an infusion of sodium bicarbonate raises her serum bicarbonate to 22 mEq/L, her urine pH rises to 7.6 and more than 15% of filtered bicarbonate appears in the urine. Which of the following transport functions is most likely impaired?","options":["Collecting duct aldosterone response","Distal H+ ATPase secretion","Thick ascending limb NKCC2 transport","Proximal bicarbonate reclamation"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.1","dok":3,"approach":["Normally the proximal tubule reclaims about 80% of filtered bicarbonate.","Na+/H+ exchange secretes H+, which joins filtered bicarbonate; carbonic anhydrase turns it into CO2 and water, which enter the cell and regenerate bicarbonate.","When this fails, bicarbonate spills into urine whenever serum bicarbonate is above a new, lower threshold, as during the infusion."],"why":"In the proximal tubule, the apical Na+/H+ exchanger secretes H+. Brush border carbonic anhydrase converts H+ and filtered bicarbonate to CO2 and water, which diffuse into the cell. Inside, carbonic anhydrase rebuilds bicarbonate, which leaves across the basolateral membrane with sodium. The kidney also makes new bicarbonate by excreting H+ as titratable acid (mainly phosphate) and as ammonium made from glutamine in the proximal tubule. Proximal (type 2) RTA lowers the threshold for bicarbonate reabsorption, causing a normal anion gap acidosis. Once serum bicarbonate falls low enough, the distal nephron can still acidify the urine.","whyNot":["Aldosterone deficiency or resistance causes type 4 RTA with hyperkalemia, and urine can still be acidified.","Defective distal H+ secretion is distal (type 1) RTA. It does not cause glucosuria or aminoaciduria.","Loss of NKCC2 transport causes salt wasting with hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, as with loop diuretics or Bartter syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Proximal bicarbonate reabsorption","Renal tubular acidosis types"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-12-B"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C16","system":"UR","dx":"Chronic kidney disease from diabetes and hypertension with renal osteodystrophy","codes":["UR-12-A4"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman with a 20-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension comes to the physician because of fatigue, itching, and aching bones for 6 months. Blood pressure is 162/96 mm Hg. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.1 g/dL (12 to 16), urea nitrogen 68 mg/dL (7 to 20), creatinine 3.4 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1), potassium 5.6 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), bicarbonate 17 mEq/L (22 to 28), calcium 7.9 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), phosphate 6.3 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), and parathyroid hormone 420 pg/mL (10 to 65). Estimated GFR is 16 mL/min/1.73 m2. Ultrasound shows two small, echogenic kidneys. Diagnosis: chronic kidney disease due to diabetic and hypertensive nephropathy, with renal osteodystrophy.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C16-Q1","stem":"Which of the following most directly contributes to her low serum calcium?","options":["Reduced renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity","Suppressed secretion of parathyroid hormone","Reduced hepatic 25-hydroxylase activity","Increased fractional excretion of calcium"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: low calcium, high phosphate, high PTH in advanced CKD.","The proximal tubule makes calcitriol through 1-alpha-hydroxylase, and lost nephron mass plus high phosphate and FGF23 reduce this.","Low calcitriol cuts intestinal calcium absorption, and retained phosphate binds calcium, so calcium falls and PTH rises."],"why":"Proximal tubule 1-alpha-hydroxylase converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol. PTH and low phosphate stimulate it, and high phosphate and FGF23 suppress it. In CKD, nephron loss and phosphate retention reduce calcitriol, so calcium falls and PTH rises (secondary hyperparathyroidism). The result is renal osteodystrophy. Primary hyperparathyroidism gives high calcium, low phosphate, and high PTH. Vitamin D deficiency gives low or normal calcium, low phosphate, and high PTH. Low magnesium blocks PTH release and action, so hypocalcemia does not correct until magnesium is replaced.","whyNot":["Correct.","Her PTH is high, not suppressed. Low calcium, high phosphate, and low calcitriol all drive PTH up.","The liver adds the 25-hydroxyl group, and that step is usually preserved in kidney disease. The step that fails is in the kidney.","With a GFR of 16 mL/min/1.73 m2, less calcium is filtered. Urinary calcium loss is not what lowers her serum calcium."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation","Mineral changes in CKD"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-A4"]}},{"id":"UR-C16-Q2","stem":"She reports getting up three times each night to urinate. After an overnight water restriction, her urine osmolality is 300 mOsm/kg (normal response greater than 600 after restriction), and it does not rise after desmopressin. Which of the following best explains her inability to concentrate urine?","options":["Excess water intake suppressing ADH release","Reduced medullary interstitial osmotic gradient","Excess ADH secretion with free water retention","Deficient ADH release from the posterior pituitary"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.5","dok":3,"approach":["Urine that cannot rise above plasma osmolality despite restriction and desmopressin points to a kidney problem, not an ADH problem.","ADH can concentrate urine only as far as the medullary interstitium is hypertonic.","In CKD, fewer nephrons each carry a high solute load and medullary structure is damaged, so the countercurrent gradient is lost."],"why":"The thick ascending limb pumps NaCl into the medulla without water, and countercurrent flow in the loop multiplies this into a gradient up to about 1200 mOsm/kg. ADH inserts aquaporin-2 into collecting duct cells so water follows that gradient. In advanced CKD, each remaining nephron carries a high solute load and medullary structure is damaged. The gradient is lost, and urine stays near plasma osmolality (isosthenuria) whatever the ADH level. Central diabetes insipidus responds to desmopressin, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus does not, SIADH gives concentrated urine with low serum sodium, and primary polydipsia concentrates with water restriction.","whyNot":["In primary polydipsia, urine concentrates after water restriction. Her urine stays at about plasma osmolality.","Correct.","Excess ADH, as in SIADH, makes urine inappropriately concentrated. Her urine stays dilute.","In central diabetes insipidus, urine osmolality rises clearly after desmopressin. Hers does not change."],"study":{"topics":["Countercurrent multiplication","Water deprivation test"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-12-A4"]}},{"id":"UR-C16-Q3","stem":"She takes extra doses of oxycodone for bone pain and is brought to the emergency department because she is drowsy. Arterial blood gas analysis on room air shows pH 7.25, PaCO2 40 mm Hg, and bicarbonate 17 mEq/L. Which of the following best describes her acid-base status?","options":["Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","Metabolic acidosis with concurrent respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis with concurrent respiratory acidosis","Respiratory acidosis with appropriate renal compensation"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.2","dok":3,"approach":["pH below 7.35 with low bicarbonate is a primary metabolic acidosis.","Apply Winters formula: expected PaCO2 = 1.5 x 17 + 8 = 33.5, plus or minus 2.","Her PaCO2 of 40 mm Hg is higher than expected, so she also has a respiratory acidosis from opioid hypoventilation."],"why":"In metabolic acidosis the lungs normally lower PaCO2 to the value predicted by Winters formula (1.5 x bicarbonate + 8, plus or minus 2). A PaCO2 above that range means a second process, respiratory acidosis, is present. Here, opioids depress her breathing on top of the metabolic acidosis of CKD. A PaCO2 below the range would mean an added respiratory alkalosis. A normal PaCO2 in a patient with low bicarbonate is a warning sign of a mixed disorder.","whyNot":["Winters formula predicts a PaCO2 of 1.5 x 17 + 8 = 33.5, plus or minus 2. Her PaCO2 of 40 mm Hg is too high to be compensation alone.","A concurrent respiratory alkalosis would push PaCO2 below the expected range of about 31.5 to 35.5 mm Hg. Hers is above it.","Correct.","Renal compensation for respiratory acidosis raises bicarbonate. Her bicarbonate is low, so the main process is metabolic acidosis."],"study":{"topics":["Winters formula","Mixed acid-base disorders"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-12-A4"]}},{"id":"UR-C16-Q4","stem":"One month later she has bleeding gums and large bruises after minor bumps. Platelet count is 210,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000), and prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time are normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause of her bleeding?","options":["Vitamin K deficiency","Thrombocytopenia from marrow failure","Uremic platelet dysfunction","Acquired factor VIII inhibitor"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A4","obj":"UR-12-A.15","dok":2,"approach":["Mucocutaneous bleeding with normal platelet count, PT, and PTT points to a problem with platelet function.","Retained uremic toxins impair platelet adhesion and aggregation.","This uremic platelet dysfunction is a complication of advanced CKD."],"why":"Diabetes and hypertension are the leading causes of CKD. As GFR falls, uremic toxins build up and cause pericarditis, encephalopathy, itching, and platelet dysfunction with a prolonged bleeding time. Other consequences include hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, volume overload, hypertension, anemia from low erythropoietin, and renal osteodystrophy. Dialysis improves uremic platelet dysfunction, and desmopressin helps short term by releasing von Willebrand factor.","whyNot":["Vitamin K deficiency lowers factors II, VII, IX, and X and prolongs the prothrombin time. Her PT is normal.","Her platelet count is normal, so platelet number is not the problem.","Correct.","A factor VIII inhibitor prolongs the partial thromboplastin time. Her PTT is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of chronic kidney disease","Uremic bleeding"],"comps":["UR-12-A4","UR-8"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C17","system":"UR","dx":"Prerenal acute kidney injury from persistent vomiting","codes":["UR-12-A4"],"case":"A 45-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of repeated vomiting of undigested food for 5 days. She has a long history of peptic ulcer disease, and endoscopy later shows pyloric channel narrowing. Her temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 114/min, respirations are 12/min, and blood pressure is 94/58 mm Hg. Mucous membranes are dry. Laboratory studies show sodium 134 mEq/L (136 to 145), potassium 2.8 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), chloride 84 mEq/L (98 to 106), bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (22 to 28), urea nitrogen 46 mg/dL (7 to 20), and creatinine 2.0 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1); her creatinine was 0.8 mg/dL a year ago. Arterial pH is 7.52. Urine sodium is 9 mEq/L (less than 20 in prerenal states), and the fractional excretion of sodium is 0.4% (less than 1% in prerenal states). Diagnosis: prerenal acute kidney injury due to volume depletion from vomiting.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C17-Q1","stem":"An ECG is obtained. Which of the following findings is most likely?","options":["Prominent U waves","Peaked T waves","Shortened QT interval","Widened QRS complexes"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note her potassium of 2.8 mEq/L and her alkalemia.","Alkalosis moves potassium into cells, and volume-driven aldosterone and high distal bicarbonate delivery increase urinary potassium loss.","Hypokalemia slows repolarization and produces U waves and flattened T waves."],"why":"Potassium moves into cells with insulin, beta-adrenergic stimulation, and alkalosis. It moves out with acidosis, cell lysis, insulin deficiency, and hyperosmolality. In vomiting, the larger potassium loss is through the kidney, driven by aldosterone and by bicarbonate reaching the distal nephron, and alkalosis shifts more into cells. Hypokalemia causes flattened T waves, ST depression, and U waves, with a risk of arrhythmia. Hyperkalemia causes peaked T waves and then QRS widening.","whyNot":["Correct.","Peaked T waves are an early sign of hyperkalemia. Her potassium is low.","A short QT is typical of hypercalcemia. It is not a feature of hypokalemia.","QRS widening appears in more severe hyperkalemia, as conduction slows."],"study":{"topics":["Potassium shifts","ECG in potassium disorders"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C17-Q2","stem":"Her urine pH is 5.5 despite her arterial pH of 7.52. Which of the following best explains the acidic urine?","options":["Hypoventilation raising PaCO2 to compensate","Aldosterone-driven distal H+ secretion","Decreased renal ammonium production from hypokalemia","Loss of bicarbonate-rich fluid in the vomitus"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Vomiting removes HCl, which raises bicarbonate and lowers chloride (the generation phase).","Volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and makes the kidney hold on to sodium (the maintenance phase).","With little chloride to reabsorb with sodium, sodium is reclaimed in exchange for H+ and K+, so the urine becomes acidic."],"why":"Each H+ lost in vomitus leaves a new bicarbonate in blood. Normally the kidney would excrete the extra bicarbonate, but volume depletion prevents this. Angiotensin II increases proximal Na+/H+ exchange and bicarbonate reabsorption. Aldosterone drives distal H+ and K+ secretion, so urine is acidic even though blood is alkaline (paradoxical aciduria). Low chloride limits sodium reabsorption with chloride. Giving normal saline restores volume and chloride and lets the kidney excrete the extra bicarbonate.","whyNot":["Respiratory compensation raises PaCO2 to bring blood pH down. It does not make the urine acidic.","Correct.","Hypokalemia increases proximal ammonium production. It does not lower acid excretion.","Vomitus is rich in hydrochloric acid, not bicarbonate. Losing it raises serum bicarbonate."],"study":{"topics":["Contraction metabolic alkalosis","Paradoxical aciduria"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-8"]}},{"id":"UR-C17-Q3","stem":"Her plasma renin activity is markedly increased. Which of the following cells release renin into her circulation?","options":["Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole","Extraglomerular mesangial cells between the two arterioles","Tubular epithelial cells forming the macula densa plaque","Endothelial cells lining the efferent arteriole lumen"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["Volume depletion lowers afferent arteriole stretch, increases renal sympathetic activity, and lowers NaCl delivery to the macula densa.","All three signals converge on the juxtaglomerular apparatus.","Renin is stored and released by juxtaglomerular cells, which are modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole wall."],"why":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus has three parts. Juxtaglomerular cells are modified, granule-containing smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole that secrete renin. The macula densa is a plaque of cells at the end of the thick ascending limb that senses NaCl. Extraglomerular mesangial cells sit between the two arterioles. Renin release rises with low afferent pressure, beta-1 sympathetic stimulation, and low macula densa NaCl. Intraglomerular mesangial cells support the capillary tuft and clear trapped material by phagocytosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Extraglomerular mesangial (lacis) cells lie between the arterioles and may relay signals, but they do not secrete renin.","Macula densa cells sense NaCl delivery and signal renin release, but they do not secrete renin themselves.","The efferent arteriole is a site of angiotensin II action, not of renin secretion."],"study":{"topics":["Juxtaglomerular apparatus","Renin release"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C17-Q4","stem":"Her mean arterial pressure is about 70 mm Hg. Before her blood pressure fell this low, falling NaCl delivery to the macula densa had triggered a feedback response that helped hold her GFR steady. Which of the following arteriolar changes is the direct result of this feedback?","options":["Constriction of the renal artery","Afferent arteriolar constriction","Efferent arteriolar dilation","Afferent arteriolar dilation"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.4","dok":3,"approach":["Low perfusion lowers GFR, so less NaCl reaches the macula densa.","The macula densa reduces adenosine signaling, and the afferent arteriole dilates.","Dilation raises glomerular capillary pressure and supports GFR, but only within the autoregulatory range of mean pressure (about 80 to 180 mm Hg)."],"why":"Renal autoregulation keeps blood flow and GFR fairly constant over a mean arterial pressure of about 80 to 180 mm Hg. In the myogenic response, the afferent arteriole constricts when stretched and relaxes when pressure falls. In tubuloglomerular feedback, the macula densa senses NaCl. High NaCl causes afferent constriction, largely through adenosine, and low NaCl causes afferent dilation and renin release. Her mean pressure of about 70 mm Hg is below the autoregulatory range, so GFR falls and prerenal azotemia develops.","whyNot":["Tubuloglomerular feedback acts on the afferent arteriole of each nephron, not on the main renal artery.","Constriction is the response to high NaCl delivery, which signals that GFR is too high.","Efferent dilation would lower glomerular pressure and GFR. Angiotensin II acts the other way, constricting the efferent arteriole.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Renal autoregulation","Tubuloglomerular feedback"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C18","system":"UR","dx":"Ischemic acute tubular necrosis after hemorrhagic shock","codes":["UR-12-B1"],"case":"A 52-year-old man undergoes emergency surgery for a bleeding duodenal ulcer. Before transfusion, his blood pressure stayed near 70/40 mm Hg for about 90 minutes. Over the next 2 days, his urine output is 300 mL/day. His serum creatinine rises from 0.9 mg/dL to 4.1 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Urine sodium is 58 mEq/L, the fractional excretion of sodium is 4% (less than 1% in prerenal states), and urine osmolality is 310 mOsm/kg. Urine microscopy shows many muddy brown granular casts and renal tubular epithelial cells. Diagnosis: ischemic acute tubular necrosis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C18-Q1","stem":"The outer medulla of his kidneys is especially vulnerable to this injury. Which of the following features of the renal circulation best explains this vulnerability?","options":["Slow vasa recta flow with countercurrent oxygen exchange","High-pressure glomerular capillaries in the cortex","Efferent arterioles supplying cortical peritubular capillaries","Arcuate arteries running along the corticomedullary border"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.5","dok":3,"approach":["The medulla gets only a small share of renal blood flow, through the vasa recta.","The vasa recta are arranged as a countercurrent loop, so oxygen diffuses from descending to ascending vessels and bypasses the deep medulla.","Slow flow and oxygen shunting keep medullary oxygen tension low, while thick ascending limb cells there use a lot of oxygen."],"why":"The vasa recta carry a small, slow flow through the medulla. This protects the medullary osmotic gradient, because slow countercurrent flow lets solutes exchange without being washed out. The same arrangement shunts oxygen from descending to ascending vessels, so medullary tissue lives at a low oxygen tension. The straight proximal tubule and the medullary thick ascending limb, both active transporters, are the first to die when perfusion falls. Low medullary oxygen also explains papillary necrosis and sickling of red cells in the vasa recta in sickle cell disease and trait.","whyNot":["Correct.","Glomerular capillaries are part of the cortex, which receives most of renal blood flow and has a higher oxygen tension than the medulla.","Cortical peritubular capillaries carry a high flow of blood. The oxygen supply problem is in the medulla.","Arcuate arteries are large distributing vessels. They do not create a low-oxygen zone."],"study":{"topics":["Vasa recta","Medullary hypoxia"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-12-B1"]}},{"id":"UR-C18-Q2","stem":"On day 3 his serum potassium is 6.7 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0) and his arterial pH is 7.22. Which of the following mechanisms links his acidemia to his rising potassium?","options":["Increased Na+/K+ ATPase activity in muscle cells","Increased aldosterone-driven distal K+ secretion","Movement of K+ out of cells as H+ moves in","Increased insulin release from pancreatic beta cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the shift factor: acidemia.","Cells buffer extra H+ by taking it up, and K+ leaves to keep electrical balance.","This shift adds to the reduced renal potassium excretion of oliguric acute tubular necrosis."],"why":"In acidemia, H+ enters cells to be buffered, and K+ leaves to keep the charge balance, so serum potassium rises. This effect is strongest in mineral (normal anion gap) acidosis. Cell lysis, insulin deficiency, beta blockade, and hyperosmolality also move potassium out of cells. Insulin, beta-2 stimulation, and alkalosis move it in. In oliguric acute tubular necrosis, low distal flow and tubular damage also reduce potassium excretion. Hyperkalemia first causes peaked T waves, then a wide QRS and arrhythmias.","whyNot":["Increased pump activity, as with insulin or beta-2 agonists, moves potassium into cells and lowers serum potassium.","More distal secretion would lower serum potassium. His failing tubules secrete less potassium.","Correct.","Insulin drives potassium into cells. Insulin deficiency, not excess, moves potassium out."],"study":{"topics":["Potassium shifts","Hyperkalemia in acute kidney injury"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-B1"]}},{"id":"UR-C18-Q3","stem":"As he recovers, his serum magnesium is 1.3 mg/dL (1.7 to 2.2) with inappropriately high urinary magnesium losses. In the normal kidney, paracellular reabsorption of Mg2+ in the thick ascending limb depends most directly on which of the following?","options":["Solvent drag from transcellular water reabsorption","Apical Na+/H+ exchange with bicarbonate","A lumen-positive voltage from K+ recycling","Basolateral Na+/Ca2+ exchange in tubular cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["The thick ascending limb takes up Na+, K+, and 2 Cl- through NKCC2.","K+ leaks back into the lumen through ROMK channels, making the lumen positive.","This positive voltage pushes Mg2+ and Ca2+ between cells through tight junctions."],"why":"The thick ascending limb reabsorbs about 25% of filtered sodium through apical NKCC2, and it is impermeable to water, which dilutes the tubular fluid. Potassium recycled through ROMK makes the lumen positive, and this voltage drives Mg2+ and Ca2+ through the paracellular path. The proximal tubule reabsorbs about two thirds of filtered sodium and water and nearly all glucose and amino acids, using Na+/H+ exchange and sodium cotransporters. Ischemic injury to these segments explains the high fractional excretion of sodium in acute tubular necrosis.","whyNot":["The thick ascending limb is impermeable to water, so there is no solvent drag in this segment.","Na+/H+ exchange drives bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule. It does not create the voltage that pulls Mg2+ through the paracellular path.","Correct.","This exchanger moves calcium across distal convoluted tubule cells under PTH control. It does not drive paracellular Mg2+ movement."],"study":{"topics":["Thick ascending limb transport","Proximal tubule reabsorption"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-12-B1"]}},{"id":"UR-C18-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains his low urine output during the first days of his illness?","options":["Excess ADH from the posterior pituitary","Glomerular immune complex deposition","Bilateral ureteral obstruction by clots","Tubular obstruction by sloughed cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-B1","obj":"UR-12-B.1","dok":2,"approach":["Ischemia kills cells in the straight proximal tubule and the medullary thick ascending limb.","Dead cells detach, form casts, and plug the tubules, raising intratubular pressure and lowering GFR.","Filtrate also leaks back through the bare basement membrane, further reducing urine flow."],"why":"In ischemic acute tubular necrosis, the energy-hungry straight proximal tubule and medullary thick ascending limb are injured first. Injured cells lose polarity and detach. They clump with Tamm-Horsfall protein into muddy brown granular casts that block the tubules. The obstruction raises tubular pressure and lowers net filtration, and filtrate leaks back through the denuded basement membrane. Afferent vasoconstriction lowers GFR further. Because the tubules cannot reabsorb sodium or concentrate urine, FENa is above 2% and urine osmolality is near 300 mOsm/kg.","whyNot":["Excess ADH would make concentrated urine. His urine osmolality of 310 mOsm/kg shows he cannot concentrate.","Immune complexes cause glomerulonephritis with RBC casts and hematuria. His sediment shows granular casts and tubular cells.","Postrenal obstruction would cause hydronephrosis. His granular casts and high FENa point to tubular injury.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of acute tubular necrosis","Oliguria in acute kidney injury"],"comps":["UR-12-B1","UR-12-A4"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C19","system":"UR","dx":"Rhabdomyolysis-induced acute tubular necrosis after a crush injury with pelvic fracture","codes":["UR-12-B1"],"case":"A 29-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after being pinned under a collapsed wall for 6 hours. His temperature is 36.4 °C (97.5 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Both thighs are swollen and tender. Pelvic radiographs show fractures of the pubic rami. There is blood at the urethral meatus, and rectal examination shows a high-riding prostate. Serum creatine kinase is 52,000 U/L (40 to 200), potassium is 6.1 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), and creatinine is 2.6 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). A suprapubic catheter drains dark brown urine. Diagnosis: rhabdomyolysis with acute tubular necrosis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C19-Q1","stem":"Which of the following urinalysis results is most likely?","options":["Positive leukocyte esterase with WBC casts","Positive dipstick for blood with few RBCs","Negative dipstick for blood with many RBCs","Many dysmorphic RBCs with RBC casts"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-B1","obj":"UR-12-B.3","dok":3,"approach":["Crush injury and very high creatine kinase mean muscle breakdown releases myoglobin.","Myoglobin is filtered and contains heme, which turns the dipstick blood pad positive.","Microscopy finds few or no red cells, because the heme is not inside red cells."],"why":"The dipstick blood pad detects the peroxidase-like activity of heme. Myoglobin from muscle breakdown and free hemoglobin from intravascular hemolysis both turn it positive, but microscopy shows few RBCs. Myoglobin injures tubules by direct heme toxicity and oxidant stress, by forming casts, and by causing vasoconstriction. Other nephrotoxic causes of acute tubular necrosis include aminoglycosides, iodinated contrast, cisplatin, and ethylene glycol.","whyNot":["WBC casts point to pyelonephritis or interstitial nephritis, not pigment injury.","Correct.","The dipstick reacts with the heme in intact red cells, so many RBCs would also give a positive dipstick.","Dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts mean glomerulonephritis, which bleeds through the glomerulus."],"study":{"topics":["Pigment nephropathy","Urine dipstick for blood"],"comps":["UR-12-B1","UR-12-A"]}},{"id":"UR-C19-Q2","stem":"A urethral catheter was not passed because of the findings at the meatus and on rectal examination. Which of the following parts of the urethra is most likely injured?","options":["Membranous urethra","Spongy urethra in the bulb of the penis","Intramural urethra at the bladder neck","Penile urethra distal to the bulb"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.6","dok":2,"approach":["Pelvic fracture with blood at the meatus and a high-riding prostate means the urethra has been torn.","The membranous urethra passes through the fixed urogenital diaphragm just below the prostate.","Shearing forces tear it there, and the prostate is displaced upward."],"why":"The male urethra has prostatic, membranous, and spongy parts. The membranous urethra is short and thin and passes through the external urethral sphincter, held in place by the perineal membrane. In pelvic fracture, the prostate and bladder move while the membranous urethra stays fixed, so it tears at that junction. Urine then leaks into the deep pelvis. A straddle injury instead crushes the bulbous spongy urethra against the pubic arch, and urine collects in the superficial perineal pouch and spreads into the scrotum and abdominal wall.","whyNot":["Correct.","The bulbous spongy urethra is injured by straddle injuries, which crush it against the pubic arch. Urine and blood then spread under the superficial perineal fascia.","Bladder neck injury from pelvic fracture is uncommon in adults, and it would not explain a high-riding prostate.","The pendulous penile urethra is injured by direct penile trauma or instrumentation, not by pelvic fracture."],"study":{"topics":["Male urethral injury","Parts of the male urethra"],"comps":["UR-3","UR-4"]}},{"id":"UR-C19-Q3","stem":"He receives 9 L of 0.9% saline over 24 hours. Laboratory studies then show sodium 141 mEq/L (136 to 145), chloride 115 mEq/L (98 to 106), and bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (22 to 28). The normal anion gap is 8 to 12 mEq/L. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his acidosis at this point?","options":["Ketoacids from prolonged fasting","Large-volume 0.9% saline infusion","Lactic acid from ischemic muscle","Retained anions from kidney failure"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate the anion gap: 141 - (115 + 16) = 10 mEq/L, which is normal.","A normal gap acidosis means bicarbonate was lost or replaced by chloride, not that an unmeasured acid was added.","Saline has much more chloride than plasma and dilutes bicarbonate, causing a hyperchloremic acidosis."],"why":"The anion gap is sodium minus chloride plus bicarbonate, normally about 8 to 12 mEq/L. A high gap means an unmeasured acid anion was added: lactate, ketoacids, uremic anions, or toxins such as methanol, ethylene glycol, and salicylate. A normal gap acidosis has high chloride. It comes from bicarbonate loss in diarrhea or renal tubular acidosis, or from large amounts of chloride-rich 0.9% saline. Saline contains 154 mEq/L of chloride and no bicarbonate.","whyNot":["Ketoacids are unmeasured anions that raise the anion gap. His gap is normal.","Correct.","Lactic acidosis adds an unmeasured anion and raises the anion gap. His gap is normal.","Retained sulfate, phosphate, and urate in kidney failure raise the anion gap. His gap is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Anion gap","Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-12-B1"]}},{"id":"UR-C19-Q4","stem":"Twelve days later his urine output rises to 4.5 L/day while his creatinine begins to fall. Which of the following electrolyte problems is most likely during this phase of his illness?","options":["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hyperphosphatemia","Hypermagnesemia"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-B1","obj":"UR-12-B.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the phase: rising urine output with falling creatinine is the recovery phase.","GFR is returning, but the new tubular cells cannot yet reabsorb solutes well.","Large volumes of dilute urine carry away potassium and sodium."],"why":"Acute tubular necrosis has three phases. Initiation is the period of injury. Maintenance is oliguric, with retention of potassium, H+, phosphate, and water, so hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and volume overload are the dangers. Recovery begins as tubular cells regrow. GFR returns before reabsorption does, so urine output rises sharply and potassium, sodium, and water are lost. Hypokalemia and volume depletion are the main risks in this polyuric phase.","whyNot":["Hyperkalemia belongs to the oliguric maintenance phase, when potassium cannot be excreted. In the polyuric phase potassium is lost.","Correct.","Phosphate retention belongs to the oliguric phase, especially with muscle breakdown. Recovery tubules lose solutes rather than retain them.","Retention of magnesium occurs when GFR is very low. In the polyuric phase, magnesium is wasted rather than retained."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of acute tubular necrosis","Polyuric recovery phase"],"comps":["UR-12-B1","UR-8"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C20","system":"UR","dx":"Ethylene glycol poisoning with acute tubular necrosis","codes":["UR-12-B1"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 10 hours after drinking an unknown amount of antifreeze in a suicide attempt. She is confused. Her temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 110/min, respirations are 28/min and deep, and blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg. Laboratory studies show sodium 140 mEq/L (136 to 145), chloride 102 mEq/L (98 to 106), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (22 to 28), glucose 90 mg/dL (70 to 99), urea nitrogen 14 mg/dL (7 to 20), and creatinine 2.4 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1). Measured serum osmolality is 322 mOsm/kg (275 to 295), and the calculated osmolality is 290 mOsm/kg. Blood ethanol is undetectable. Diagnosis: ethylene glycol poisoning with acute tubular necrosis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C20-Q1","stem":"Which of the following casts is most likely to be seen on microscopy of her urine sediment?","options":["Fatty casts with oval fat bodies","Red blood cell casts","White blood cell casts","Muddy brown granular casts"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A","obj":"UR-12-A.2","dok":2,"approach":["Her kidney injury is acute tubular necrosis.","Casts take the shape of the tubule and carry whatever was inside it.","Dead tubular cells breaking down inside casts give muddy brown granular casts."],"why":"The type of cast tells where the kidney is injured. RBC casts and dysmorphic RBCs mean glomerulonephritis. WBC casts mean pyelonephritis or interstitial nephritis. Muddy brown granular casts made of dead tubular cells mean acute tubular necrosis. Fatty casts mean nephrotic syndrome, and broad waxy casts mean chronic kidney disease. Hyaline casts, made of Tamm-Horsfall protein alone, are nonspecific and are often seen in concentrated urine.","whyNot":["Fatty casts and oval fat bodies come from lipid leaking through a damaged glomerulus. They mark nephrotic syndrome.","RBC casts form when red cells cross a damaged glomerulus. They mark glomerulonephritis.","WBC casts mark inflammation in the interstitium, as in pyelonephritis or interstitial nephritis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Urinary casts","Urine sediment in acute kidney injury"],"comps":["UR-12-A","UR-12-B1"]}},{"id":"UR-C20-Q2","stem":"Measurements over the next few hours, before treatment, show her osmolal gap falling while her anion gap rises. Which of the following processes best explains this change?","options":["Renal excretion of unchanged ethylene glycol","Lactate production from tissue hypoperfusion","Hepatic glucuronide conjugation of the toxin","Oxidation by hepatic alcohol dehydrogenase"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Unmetabolized ethylene glycol is an uncharged, osmotically active molecule, so it causes the osmolal gap.","NAD+-dependent alcohol dehydrogenase starts its conversion to glycoaldehyde, then glycolic, glyoxylic, and oxalic acids.","As parent alcohol turns into acids, the osmolal gap falls and the anion gap rises."],"why":"Ethylene glycol itself is not very toxic, but it adds unmeasured osmoles, which explains the early osmolal gap. Alcohol dehydrogenase, using NAD+, starts its oxidation to glycoaldehyde, which aldehyde dehydrogenase turns into glycolic acid. Glycolic acid causes most of the acidosis. Further metabolism produces oxalic acid, which binds calcium to form calcium oxalate crystals that injure tubules. Fomepizole and ethanol block alcohol dehydrogenase and stop toxic acids from forming.","whyNot":["Excretion would lower the osmolal gap, but it would not create new acids. The anion gap would not rise.","She is not hypotensive. Lactate would raise the anion gap but would not lower the osmolal gap.","Glucuronidation is not the main route for ethylene glycol and would not make the organic acids that raise the anion gap.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ethylene glycol metabolism","Osmolal gap"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C20-Q3","stem":"Which of the following crystals is most likely to be found in her urine?","options":["Calcium oxalate crystals","Cystine crystals","Magnesium ammonium phosphate crystals","Uric acid crystals"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-B1","obj":"UR-12-B.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify ethylene glycol as a nephrotoxic cause of acute tubular necrosis.","Its end product, oxalic acid, binds calcium.","Calcium oxalate crystals, often needle-shaped or envelope-shaped, appear in urine and lodge in tubules."],"why":"Ethylene glycol is a nephrotoxic cause of acute tubular necrosis. Oxalate from its breakdown binds calcium and forms calcium oxalate crystals that deposit in proximal tubule cells and lumens. Glycolic acid and glycoaldehyde also injure tubular cells directly. Binding of calcium by oxalate can lower serum calcium. Other nephrotoxic causes of acute tubular necrosis include aminoglycosides, iodinated contrast, cisplatin, and myoglobin or hemoglobin pigment.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cystine crystals are hexagonal and form in cystinuria, an inherited defect in dibasic amino acid reabsorption.","Struvite crystals form in alkaline urine infected by urease-producing bacteria such as Proteus.","Uric acid crystals form in acidic urine with high uric acid, as in gout or tumor lysis syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Nephrotoxic acute tubular necrosis","Urine crystals"],"comps":["UR-12-B1","UR-8"]}},{"id":"UR-C20-Q4","stem":"Her anion gap is 30 mEq/L (8 to 12), and her osmolal gap is 32 mOsm/kg (less than 10). Which of the following conditions would also be expected to produce both a high anion gap and a high osmolal gap?","options":["Isopropyl alcohol poisoning","Salicylate poisoning","Methanol poisoning","Severe diarrhea"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.3","dok":3,"approach":["Confirm the anion gap: 140 - (102 + 8) = 30 mEq/L, which is high.","A high osmolal gap means an unmeasured, osmotically active substance, usually a toxic alcohol.","A toxic alcohol that is metabolized to an acid gives both gaps: ethylene glycol or methanol."],"why":"The anion gap is sodium minus chloride plus bicarbonate. High anion gap acidosis comes from lactic acidosis, ketoacidosis, uremia, methanol, ethylene glycol, and salicylates. Normal anion gap acidosis comes from diarrhea and renal tubular acidosis. A high osmolal gap means an unmeasured osmole such as a toxic alcohol. Methanol, like ethylene glycol, is converted by alcohol dehydrogenase to an acid (formic acid), so both gaps rise. Methanol damages the retina and optic nerve, while ethylene glycol damages the kidney.","whyNot":["Isopropyl alcohol raises the osmolal gap, but it is metabolized to acetone, which is not an acid. The anion gap stays normal.","Salicylates cause a high anion gap acidosis, often with respiratory alkalosis, but do not cause a significant osmolal gap.","Correct.","Diarrhea removes bicarbonate and causes a normal anion gap, hyperchloremic acidosis without an osmolal gap."],"study":{"topics":["Anion gap","Toxic alcohols"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-8"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C21","system":"UR","dx":"Distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis with calcium phosphate stones in Sjogren syndrome","codes":["UR-12-C2"],"case":"A 38-year-old woman comes to the physician because of two episodes of left flank pain over the past year and increasing muscle weakness. She has had dry eyes and a dry mouth for 3 years. Serum anti-Ro/SSA antibodies are positive. Serum studies show sodium 139 mEq/L (136 to 145), potassium 2.9 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), chloride 114 mEq/L (98 to 106), and bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (22 to 28). Arterial pH is 7.30 (7.35 to 7.45). Urine pH is 6.5, and urine culture shows no growth. CT scan shows bilateral medullary nephrocalcinosis and a 6-mm stone in the left renal pelvis. Analysis of a previously passed stone shows calcium phosphate. Diagnosis: distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis due to Sjogren syndrome, with calcium phosphate stones.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C21-Q1","stem":"Her hypokalemia results largely from increased potassium secretion by principal cells of the cortical collecting duct, enhanced by aldosterone released in response to mild volume depletion. Which of the following most directly drives this potassium secretion?","options":["Apical potassium reabsorption by H+/K+-ATPase","Paracellular potassium flux driven by a lumen-positive voltage","Aquaporin-2 insertion that concentrates luminal potassium","Lumen-negative voltage from sodium entry through ENaC"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the cell: principal cells of the cortical collecting duct secrete potassium.","Recall what aldosterone does there: more ENaC channels in the apical membrane and more Na+/K+-ATPase in the basolateral membrane.","Link the two: sodium entering through ENaC makes the lumen negative, which pulls potassium out of the cell through apical K+ channels."],"why":"Aldosterone binds a cytoplasmic mineralocorticoid receptor in principal cells and increases apical ENaC channels and basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps. Sodium moves into the cell through ENaC without an accompanying anion, so the lumen becomes electrically negative. The pump keeps intracellular potassium high, and the negative lumen pulls potassium out through apical ROMK channels. In distal RTA, failed H+ secretion and volume-driven aldosterone both increase this potassium loss.","whyNot":["The H+/K+-ATPase sits in alpha-intercalated cells and reabsorbs potassium. It works against potassium loss and becomes important in potassium depletion.","A lumen-positive voltage exists in the thick ascending limb and drives paracellular Mg2+ and Ca2+ reabsorption. It does not drive potassium secretion in the collecting duct.","ADH inserts aquaporin-2 to reabsorb water. Water reabsorption does not create the electrical gradient that drives potassium secretion.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Aldosterone action on principal cells","Renal potassium secretion"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-8"]}},{"id":"UR-C21-Q2","stem":"Which of the following features of her urine most directly accounts for the composition of her stones?","options":["Alkaline pH from urease-producing bacteria","Acidic pH with high cystine excretion","Alkaline pH with low citrate excretion","Acidic pH with high urate excretion"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-C2","obj":"UR-12-C.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the stone type: calcium phosphate.","Recall that calcium phosphate precipitates in alkaline urine, and that citrate normally binds calcium and keeps it in solution.","Apply to distal RTA: the urine stays alkaline, and chronic acidosis increases proximal citrate reabsorption, so urinary citrate falls."],"why":"Calcium phosphate is much less soluble in alkaline urine. In distal RTA the collecting duct cannot secrete H+, so urine pH stays above 5.5 even during acidemia. Chronic acidosis also increases proximal tubule reabsorption of citrate, so little citrate is left to chelate urinary calcium, and acidosis releases calcium from bone, adding hypercalciuria. Together these drive calcium phosphate stones and nephrocalcinosis.","whyNot":["Urease-producing bacteria such as Proteus alkalinize urine and form struvite stones. Her urine culture is sterile, and her stones are calcium phosphate.","Cystine stones form in cystinuria, an inherited transport defect, and are more likely in acidic urine. They show hexagonal crystals.","Correct.","Acidic urine with high urate favors uric acid stones, which are radiolucent and seen in gout and tumor lysis."],"study":{"topics":["Stone types and urine pH","Hypocitraturia in distal RTA"],"comps":["UR-12-C2","UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C21-Q3","stem":"Which of the following findings in this patient most reliably distinguishes her disorder from proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis?","options":["Calculated serum anion gap of 10 mEq/L","Urine pH 6.5 with arterial pH 7.30","Serum chloride concentration of 114 mEq/L","Serum potassium concentration of 2.9 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.4","dok":3,"approach":["List what types 1 and 2 share: normal anion gap, hyperchloremia, and hypokalemia.","Find the feature that differs: the ability to acidify urine when serum bicarbonate is low.","In proximal RTA, once serum bicarbonate falls below the lowered threshold, the intact distal nephron acidifies urine below 5.5. In distal RTA it cannot."],"why":"Both proximal and distal RTA cause a normal anion gap, hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia. In proximal RTA, the proximal tubule wastes bicarbonate until serum bicarbonate drops to a new, lower threshold, and then the healthy distal nephron can lower urine pH below 5.5. In distal RTA, alpha-intercalated cells cannot secrete H+, so the urine stays above 5.5 despite acidemia. A urine pH of 6.5 with an arterial pH of 7.30 therefore points to the distal type.","whyNot":["A normal anion gap is shared by all types of RTA. It separates RTA from ketoacidosis or lactic acidosis, not one RTA from another.","Correct.","Hyperchloremia accompanies any normal anion gap acidosis, so it is present in both proximal and distal RTA.","Hypokalemia occurs in both type 1 and type 2 RTA. It separates them from type 4, which causes hyperkalemia."],"study":{"topics":["Types of renal tubular acidosis","Urine pH in acidemia"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-12-C2"]}},{"id":"UR-C21-Q4","stem":"Eight months later, she has severe left flank pain. CT shows a 5-mm stone impacted in the left ureter at the point where the ureter enters the true pelvis. Which of the following structures lies immediately posterior to the ureter at this site?","options":["Uterine artery lateral to the cervix","Gonadal artery and vein in the abdomen","Psoas major muscle at the level of the L3 vertebra","Bifurcation of the common iliac artery"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the site: where the ureter enters the true pelvis is the pelvic brim.","Trace the ureter: it descends on the psoas, then crosses the bifurcation of the common iliac artery at the pelvic brim.","This crossing is one of the three narrow points where stones lodge."],"why":"The ureter runs down on the psoas major in the abdomen, then crosses anterior to the bifurcation of the common iliac artery (the start of the external iliac artery) as it enters the pelvis. The ureter narrows at three points: the ureteropelvic junction, this crossing at the pelvic brim, and the ureterovesical junction. Stones commonly lodge at each of them.","whyNot":["The uterine artery crosses above and in front of the distal ureter near the cervix, deep in the pelvis. That site is at risk during hysterectomy.","The gonadal vessels cross anterior to the ureter in the abdomen, not behind it, and they do this above the pelvic brim.","The psoas lies behind the ureter in its abdominal course, above the pelvic brim. A stone stuck on the psoas would be in the mid ureter.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Course of the ureter","Sites of ureteral narrowing"],"comps":["UR-3","UR-12-C2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C22","system":"UR","dx":"Calcium oxalate ureteral stone with renal colic","codes":["UR-12-C2"],"case":"A 45-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 3 hours after the sudden onset of severe right flank pain that comes in waves and spreads into the right groin and scrotum. He has vomited twice and cannot find a comfortable position. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 148/90 mm Hg. There is right costovertebral angle tenderness. Testicular examination is normal. Urinalysis shows 3+ blood, 20 to 30 RBCs/hpf, and is negative for leukocyte esterase and nitrite. Noncontrast CT shows a 6-mm radiopaque stone at the right ureterovesical junction with mild right hydronephrosis. Diagnosis: calcium oxalate ureteral stone with renal colic.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C22-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his pain is felt in the groin?","options":["Shared T11 to L2 segments of ureteral and groin afferents","Irritation of the obturator nerve on the lateral pelvic wall","Stretch of parietal peritoneum innervated by the phrenic nerve","Sacral parasympathetic afferents from the bladder trigone"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the pain as visceral: it is colicky, poorly localized, and comes from stretch of the ureter and pelvis.","Recall that ureteral visceral afferents enter the cord at lower thoracic and upper lumbar segments, about T11 to L2.","Apply referred pain: the brain assigns the pain to skin supplied by the same segments, including the groin and scrotum (L1)."],"why":"Pain from a distended ureter travels in visceral afferent fibers that run with sympathetic nerves and enter the spinal cord at about T11 to L2. Somatic afferents from the flank, groin, and anterior scrotum enter those same segments. Because the signals converge on the same dorsal horn neurons, the brain reads the pain as coming from that skin. As the stone moves down the ureter, the pain tends to shift from the flank toward the groin.","whyNot":["Correct.","The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh. Irritation by a pelvic mass or hernia causes medial thigh pain, not flank to groin colic.","The phrenic nerve (C3 to C5) carries pain from diaphragmatic peritoneum to the shoulder, as in splenic rupture. It plays no part in ureteral pain.","Afferents from the bladder trigone and neck run with pelvic splanchnic nerves to S2 to S4 and refer pain to the perineum and tip of the penis."],"study":{"topics":["Referred pain of renal colic","Visceral afferent pathways"],"comps":["UR-4","UR-12-C2"]}},{"id":"UR-C22-Q2","stem":"Six weeks later, metabolic evaluation shows serum calcium 11.1 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.2), serum phosphorus 2.2 mg/dL (2.5 to 4.5), an elevated serum parathyroid hormone (PTH) concentration, and a 24-hour urine calcium of 360 mg (under 300). Which of the following most directly explains his increased intestinal calcium absorption?","options":["PTH-induced renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity","PTH-stimulated 25-hydroxylation of vitamin D in the liver","Increased renal 24-hydroxylation of calcitriol","Calcitonin-stimulated calcium uptake in the duodenum"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.3","dok":3,"approach":["Read the pattern: high calcium, low phosphate, and high PTH means primary hyperparathyroidism.","Recall that PTH and low phosphate both stimulate 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the proximal tubule.","Follow the result: more calcitriol increases calcium absorption in the gut, which raises the filtered calcium load and the urine calcium."],"why":"In primary hyperparathyroidism, PTH acts on the proximal tubule to increase 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which turns 25-hydroxyvitamin D into 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol). The low serum phosphate, caused by PTH blocking proximal phosphate reabsorption, also stimulates this enzyme. Calcitriol raises intestinal calcium absorption. PTH increases distal calcium reabsorption, but the filtered calcium load rises so much that urine calcium still goes up, which promotes calcium stones.","whyNot":["Correct.","Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is not under tight hormonal control. It depends mainly on the supply of vitamin D.","24-hydroxylation inactivates vitamin D and is favored when PTH is low and calcitriol is high. PTH suppresses it.","Calcitonin lowers serum calcium by inhibiting osteoclasts. It does not drive intestinal calcium absorption."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin D activation in the kidney","Laboratory pattern of primary hyperparathyroidism"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-C2"]}},{"id":"UR-C22-Q3","stem":"Which of the following sets of features is most consistent with the stone recovered from this patient?","options":["Radiolucent stone with rhomboid or rosette crystals","Radiopaque stone with envelope-shaped crystals","Radiopaque stone with coffin-lid prism crystals","Faintly opaque stone with hexagonal crystals"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-C2","obj":"UR-12-C.3","dok":2,"approach":["Start from the stone type: calcium oxalate, the most common stone.","Recall its features: calcium makes it radiopaque, and the dihydrate crystals look like envelopes (bipyramids).","Match each other option to its stone: rhomboid for uric acid, hexagonal for cystine, coffin-lid for struvite."],"why":"Calcium oxalate makes up most kidney stones, and hypercalciuria is the usual underlying cause. Calcium-containing stones are radiopaque on plain films and CT. Calcium oxalate dihydrate crystals look like envelopes, and the monohydrate form looks like dumbbells. The appearance of crystals and stones lets you predict composition before formal analysis.","whyNot":["Uric acid stones are radiolucent on plain films and show rhomboid or rosette crystals. They form in acidic urine, as in gout.","Correct.","Struvite stones are radiopaque and show coffin-lid crystals, but they form in alkaline urine with urease-producing bacteria.","Cystine stones are faintly opaque and show flat hexagonal crystals. They come from inherited cystinuria."],"study":{"topics":["Kidney stone types","Urine crystal shapes"],"comps":["UR-12-C2"]}},{"id":"UR-C22-Q4","stem":"Thirty-six hours later, the stone has not passed. He develops rigors, temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 88/54 mm Hg. Repeat CT shows the stone still at the right ureterovesical junction with worsening right hydronephrosis. Which of the following most likely explains his deterioration?","options":["Ascending infection limited to the bladder mucosa","Infection of stagnant urine above the obstructing stone","Hematogenous seeding of the renal cortex from skin flora","Intraperitoneal urine leak from rupture of the bladder dome"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-C2","obj":"UR-12-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Note the combination: fever and hypotension in a patient with a stone that still blocks the ureter.","Recall that urine trapped above an obstruction is not flushed out, so bacteria multiply there.","Conclude that he has an infected obstructed kidney (obstructive pyelonephritis) with sepsis, a complication that needs urgent drainage."],"why":"Stones cause harm mainly by obstruction. Urine above the stone stagnates, the pelvis and calyces dilate, and bacteria that reach this static urine multiply without being washed out. An infected, obstructed kidney can progress quickly to pyonephrosis and urosepsis with fever and hypotension. Other complications of calculi include hydronephrosis, loss of renal function with long obstruction, and hematuria.","whyNot":["Cystitis alone causes dysuria and frequency without high fever, rigors, or hypotension. It also would not explain worsening hydronephrosis.","Correct.","Hematogenous spread, often from Staphylococcus aureus, causes renal abscesses. It is much less common than ascending infection and does not follow from his obstruction.","Dome rupture follows blunt trauma to a full bladder and causes urinary ascites. Nothing in his course suggests bladder injury."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of kidney stones","Infected obstructed kidney"],"comps":["UR-12-C2","UR-12-C1"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C23","system":"UR","dx":"Cystinuria with recurrent cystine stones","codes":["UR-12-C2"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl comes to the physician because of left flank pain for 2 days. She has passed kidney stones four times since the age of 9 years. Her 20-year-old brother also has recurrent stones, and her parents have never had stones. Vital signs are within normal limits. Urinalysis shows urine pH 5.5, 2+ blood, and flat hexagonal crystals. Urine glucose is negative. The urine cyanide-nitroprusside test is positive. CT scan shows an 8-mm stone in the left renal pelvis and a 4-mm stone in the proximal left ureter, both faintly opaque. Diagnosis: cystinuria with recurrent cystine stones.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C23-Q1","stem":"Pain from her proximal ureteral stone is carried by visceral afferent fibers. Which of the following nerves do these fibers travel with to reach the central nervous system?","options":["Pelvic splanchnic nerves from S2 to S4","Vagal fibers passing through the celiac plexus","Lumbar and lower thoracic splanchnic nerves","Subcostal and iliohypogastric nerves"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the pain: ureteral distension is visceral pain.","Recall the rule: visceral pain from organs above the pelvic pain line travels back with sympathetic fibers.","Apply it to the ureter: the fibers run back through the lower thoracic and lumbar splanchnic nerves and enter the cord at about T11 to L2."],"why":"Visceral pain fibers from the kidney and the abdominal ureter run backward along the sympathetic nerves (the lesser and least splanchnic and lumbar splanchnic nerves). They reach the spinal cord through the dorsal roots of about T11 to L2. This explains why the pain is felt in the flank, lower abdomen, and groin. Pelvic organs below the peritoneum send pain mainly with parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic nerves instead.","whyNot":["Pelvic splanchnic nerves carry visceral pain from organs below the pelvic pain line, such as the bladder neck and cervix. The proximal ureter lies far above that level.","Vagal afferents carry physiological signals such as stretch and chemoreception. They do not carry pain from the ureter.","Correct.","The subcostal and iliohypogastric nerves carry somatic sensation from the skin and muscles of the flank and lower abdominal wall. Ureteral distension is visceral pain."],"study":{"topics":["Visceral afferents of the ureter","Pelvic pain line"],"comps":["UR-4"]}},{"id":"UR-C23-Q2","stem":"On CT, her 4-mm proximal ureteral stone lies below the ureteropelvic junction, where the ureter descends on the psoas major. Which of the following structures crosses directly anterior to the left ureter in this segment?","options":["Left gonadal artery and vein","Left renal vein anterior to the aorta","Lumbar sympathetic trunk on the vertebral bodies","Genitofemoral nerve on the psoas major"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Place the stone: the abdominal ureter, descending on the psoas major.","Recall the ureter's relations there: muscle and nerve behind, gonadal vessels crossing in front.","Pick the structure that crosses anterior to it."],"why":"In the abdomen the ureter runs down on the psoas major, with the genitofemoral nerve behind it on the muscle. The gonadal vessels run obliquely downward and cross anterior to the ureter in this segment. Lower down, the ureter crosses the common iliac bifurcation at the pelvic brim and then runs along the pelvic wall to the bladder. Knowing these relations helps you read CT and understand surgical risk to the ureter.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left renal vein crosses anterior to the aorta to reach the inferior vena cava. It lies above and medial to the abdominal ureter.","The lumbar sympathetic trunk lies along the vertebral bodies, posterior and medial to the ureter.","The genitofemoral nerve lies on the anterior surface of the psoas, behind the ureter."],"study":{"topics":["Relations of the abdominal ureter","Course of the ureter"],"comps":["UR-3"]}},{"id":"UR-C23-Q3","stem":"In a healthy person, which of the following best describes how the nephron normally handles the filtered cystine and dibasic amino acids that are lost in this patient's urine?","options":["About two thirds reabsorbed in the collecting duct","About two thirds reabsorbed in the proximal tubule","Nearly all reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb","Nearly all reabsorbed in the proximal tubule"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the segment: amino acids, like glucose, are reclaimed in the proximal tubule.","Separate the fractions: about two thirds applies to sodium and water, while nearly all filtered glucose and amino acids are reabsorbed.","Link to the case: a missing proximal transporter lets cystine reach the urine in large amounts."],"why":"The proximal tubule reabsorbs about two thirds of filtered sodium and water and nearly all filtered glucose and amino acids. Amino acids enter the cell through specific apical transporters, many of them sodium-coupled. In cystinuria, the transporter for cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine is defective, so these amino acids escape into the urine. Cystine is poorly soluble and forms stones.","whyNot":["The collecting duct fine-tunes sodium, potassium, acid, and water. Amino acids are reabsorbed well before they reach it.","About two thirds is the fraction for sodium and water in the proximal tubule. Amino acid reabsorption there is nearly complete.","The thick ascending limb reabsorbs Na+, K+, and Cl- through Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransport and Mg2+ and Ca2+ by the paracellular route. It does not reclaim amino acids.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Proximal tubule reabsorption","Renal handling of amino acids"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-9"]}},{"id":"UR-C23-Q4","stem":"Both of her parents have normal urinalyses. Which of the following additional findings is most likely in her urine?","options":["Increased urinary tryptophan and other neutral amino acids","Increased urinary glucose, phosphate, and bicarbonate","Increased urinary homocystine and methionine","Increased urinary lysine, ornithine, and arginine"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.6","dok":3,"approach":["Use the pedigree: an affected sibling with unaffected parents fits autosomal recessive inheritance.","Recall the defect: a proximal tubule transporter shared by cystine and the dibasic amino acids.","Predict the urine: cystine plus ornithine, lysine, and arginine (COLA)."],"why":"Cystinuria is an autosomal recessive defect in a proximal tubule (and intestinal) transporter that carries cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine. All four appear in excess in the urine. Only cystine causes trouble, because it is poorly soluble, especially in acidic urine, and forms hexagonal crystals and stones. The cyanide-nitroprusside test turns positive when urinary cystine is high.","whyNot":["Loss of neutral amino acids such as tryptophan is Hartnup disease, which can cause pellagra-like skin findings. It does not cause cystine stones.","Glucose, phosphate, and bicarbonate wasting is Fanconi syndrome, a global proximal tubule failure. Her urine glucose is negative.","Homocystinuria is a defect in methionine metabolism, usually cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency. It causes lens dislocation and thrombosis, not kidney stones.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cystinuria genetics and transport","Inherited aminoacidurias"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-C2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C24","system":"UR","dx":"Struvite staghorn calculus from Proteus mirabilis infection","codes":["UR-12-C2"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the physician because of dull left flank pain for 2 months. She has had four urinary tract infections in the past year. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows pH 8.5 (4.5 to 8.0), positive leukocyte esterase, positive nitrite, and coffin-lid crystals. Urine culture grows more than 100,000 CFU/mL of a gram-negative rod that spreads in waves across the blood agar plate and is identified as Proteus mirabilis. CT scan shows a branching stone that fills the left renal pelvis and calyces. Diagnosis: struvite staghorn calculus due to Proteus mirabilis infection.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C24-Q1","stem":"Most of the ammonia in her urine comes from bacterial urease. In a healthy person, urinary ammonium comes from the kidney itself. Which of the following best describes this normal renal process?","options":["Loop of Henle carbonic anhydrase activity that reclaims bicarbonate","Collecting duct urea hydrolysis that consumes filtered bicarbonate","Proximal tubule glutamine metabolism that yields new bicarbonate","Distal tubule glycine metabolism that generates titratable acid"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the two jobs of the kidney in acid-base balance: reclaim filtered bicarbonate and make new bicarbonate.","Recall that new bicarbonate is made when H+ leaves as titratable acid (mainly phosphate) or as ammonium.","Recall the source of ammonium: proximal tubule cells break down glutamine, yielding ammonium for excretion and bicarbonate for the blood."],"why":"Proximal tubule cells metabolize glutamine into two ammonium ions and two bicarbonate ions. The bicarbonate goes to the blood as new bicarbonate, and the ammonium is secreted and eventually excreted in the urine. Each ammonium excreted represents H+ removed from the body. The kidney does not make urease, so in a healthy person urea passes into the urine intact. In her case, bacterial urease splits urea in the urine itself, producing ammonia that raises urine pH.","whyNot":["The thick ascending limb does reclaim some filtered bicarbonate with carbonic anhydrase, and the proximal tubule reclaims most of it. Reclaiming filtered bicarbonate makes no new bicarbonate and no ammonium.","Human kidneys have no urease. Urea hydrolysis in urine happens only with urease-producing bacteria, as in this patient.","Correct.","Titratable acid is mostly H+ buffered by filtered phosphate. It is not made by metabolizing glycine."],"study":{"topics":["Renal ammoniagenesis","New bicarbonate generation"],"comps":["UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C24-Q2","stem":"Percutaneous nephrolithotomy is planned through the posterior flank. After passing through the posterior abdominal wall muscles and transversalis fascia, the access needle traverses which of the following layers, in order, before reaching the renal parenchyma?","options":["Renal fascia, pararenal fat, fibrous capsule, perirenal fat","Pararenal fat, perirenal fat, renal fascia, fibrous capsule","Pararenal fat, renal fascia, perirenal fat, fibrous capsule","Perirenal fat, pararenal fat, renal fascia, fibrous capsule"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.3","dok":2,"approach":["List the coverings from outside in: pararenal fat, renal fascia, perirenal fat, fibrous capsule.","Remember that the renal fascia separates the two fat layers: para (outside) and peri (inside).","The fibrous capsule sticks directly to the kidney surface."],"why":"The kidney is wrapped in four layers. From outside in, they are the pararenal fat, the renal (Gerota) fascia, the perirenal fat, and the fibrous capsule. The renal fascia encloses the kidney, the perirenal fat, and the suprarenal gland. A posterior approach crosses these layers in that order. The renal fascia also confines perirenal collections, such as a perinephric abscess or hematoma, around the kidney.","whyNot":["The pararenal fat lies outside the renal fascia, and the fibrous capsule lies directly on the kidney beneath the perirenal fat.","The two fat layers are separated by the renal fascia, so the needle cannot pass from pararenal fat straight into perirenal fat.","Correct.","Perirenal fat lies inside the renal fascia. It cannot be reached before the pararenal fat and the fascia."],"study":{"topics":["Renal coverings","Retroperitoneal spaces"],"comps":["UR-3"]}},{"id":"UR-C24-Q3","stem":"Which of the following bacterial activities most directly allows the magnesium ammonium phosphate crystals in her stone to form?","options":["Hydrolysis of urea to ammonia, raising urine pH","Reduction of nitrate to nitrite, chelating urinary magnesium","Release of endotoxin that injures the renal papillae","Acidification of urine by bacterial lactate production"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the stone: struvite is magnesium ammonium phosphate.","Recall Proteus urease: it splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide.","Link the result: ammonia raises urine pH and supplies ammonium, and struvite precipitates in alkaline urine."],"why":"Proteus mirabilis makes large amounts of urease. Urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, and ammonia combines with H+ to form ammonium, raising the urine pH. In alkaline urine, ammonium, magnesium, and phosphate precipitate as struvite, often with carbonate apatite. The stone grows into the calyces as a staghorn, and bacteria live inside it, which keeps the infection going. Proteus also shows swarming motility on agar.","whyNot":["Correct.","Nitrate reductase makes the nitrite seen on the dipstick. Nitrite does not chelate magnesium or cause stones.","Endotoxin drives inflammation and sepsis but does not supply the ammonium or the high pH needed for struvite.","Struvite needs alkaline urine. Acidifying the urine would dissolve it."],"study":{"topics":["Urease and struvite stones","Proteus mirabilis"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-C2"]}},{"id":"UR-C24-Q4","stem":"After nephrolithotomy, she remains hospitalized with an indwelling urinary catheter for 10 days and develops fever. Urine culture grows an oxidase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod that produces a blue-green pigment and a grape-like odor. Which of the following is the most likely organism?","options":["Staphylococcus saprophyticus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Proteus mirabilis"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the clues: oxidase positive, non-lactose fermenter, blue-green pigment, grape-like odor.","Add the setting: a long-term indwelling catheter in a hospitalized patient.","Put them together: Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a common catheter-associated urinary pathogen."],"why":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is an oxidase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod. It makes pyocyanin (blue-green) and has a sweet, grape-like odor. It thrives in moist hospital equipment and forms biofilms on catheters, so it is a classic cause of catheter-associated urinary infection. Many strains resist common oral antibiotics.","whyNot":["Staphylococcus saprophyticus is a gram-positive, novobiocin-resistant coccus that causes cystitis in young sexually active women.","Correct.","Klebsiella is a lactose-fermenting, oxidase-negative rod with a thick mucoid capsule.","Proteus is a non-lactose fermenter but is oxidase negative and shows swarming motility rather than blue-green pigment."],"study":{"topics":["Catheter-associated urinary pathogens","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-10"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C25","system":"UR","dx":"Posterior urethral valves with bilateral hydronephrosis and patent urachus in a newborn boy","codes":["UR-12-C1"],"case":"A male newborn is delivered at 38 weeks' gestation. Prenatal ultrasonography at 30 weeks showed oligohydramnios, bilateral hydronephrosis, and a distended, thick-walled bladder. At birth he has mild respiratory distress. Physical examination shows a palpable suprapubic mass, a weak, dribbling urinary stream, and clear fluid leaking from the umbilicus; the fluid has a creatinine concentration similar to urine. Serum creatinine rises over the first 3 days instead of falling. Voiding cystourethrography shows a dilated posterior urethra with obstructing membranous folds just below the bladder neck. Diagnosis: posterior urethral valves with bilateral hydronephrosis and a patent urachus.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C25-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains his respiratory distress at birth?","options":["Herniation of bowel through a posterolateral diaphragm defect","Aspiration of meconium-stained amniotic fluid","Surfactant deficiency from immature type II pneumocytes","Pulmonary hypoplasia from reduced amniotic fluid"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-1","obj":"UR-1.6","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the chain: urethral valves block urine outflow from the fetal bladder.","After about 16 weeks, fetal urine is the main source of amniotic fluid, so blocked outflow causes oligohydramnios.","Too little amniotic fluid limits lung growth, causing pulmonary hypoplasia."],"why":"Posterior urethral valves are folds of tissue in the posterior (prostatic) urethra of males that block urine outflow. The bladder distends and thickens, and back-pressure dilates both ureters and both kidneys. From the second trimester on, amniotic fluid is mostly fetal urine, so the obstruction causes oligohydramnios. Without enough fluid, the lungs do not develop fully, and the fetus may show the Potter sequence with flat facies and limb deformities.","whyNot":["Congenital diaphragmatic hernia also causes lung hypoplasia, but by direct compression. Prenatal imaging here pointed to the urinary tract.","Meconium aspiration occurs in post-term or stressed infants. Nothing in this pregnancy points to it.","Surfactant deficiency causes respiratory distress in preterm infants. He was born at 38 weeks, and his lungs are small, not immature.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Posterior urethral valves","Oligohydramnios and Potter sequence"],"comps":["UR-1","UR-12-C1"]}},{"id":"UR-C25-Q2","stem":"If the obstruction were left untreated for months, which of the following changes would most likely develop in his kidneys?","options":["Nodular mesangial sclerosis with hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Papillary blunting and thinning of the renal cortex","Bilateral enlargement from many cortical and medullary cysts","Proliferation of parietal epithelium forming glomerular crescents"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-C1","obj":"UR-12-C.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize persistent hydronephrosis from outlet obstruction.","Recall the effect of back-pressure: high pressure in the pelvis compresses the papillae and medulla and reduces blood flow.","Predict the lesion: papillary atrophy followed by thinning of the cortex, with a falling GFR."],"why":"Lasting obstruction raises pressure in the pelvis and calyces. The pressure is transmitted back into the tubules and compresses the renal parenchyma and its blood vessels. The papillae flatten first, then the medulla and cortex thin, and over time the kidney becomes a dilated sac with a thin rim of tissue. GFR falls as nephrons are lost. Posterior urethral valves are the most common cause of bilateral hydronephrosis in male infants.","whyNot":["Nodular mesangial sclerosis (Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules) with hyaline arteriolosclerosis is diabetic nephropathy.","Correct.","Massive bilateral enlargement from many cysts is the picture of polycystic kidney disease. Obstruction early in fetal life can cause cystic dysplasia, but continued back-pressure after birth thins the parenchyma instead.","Crescents form in rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis from severe glomerular injury, not from back-pressure."],"study":{"topics":["Pathology of hydronephrosis","Causes of hydronephrosis by age"],"comps":["UR-12-C1"]}},{"id":"UR-C25-Q3","stem":"Which of the following embryologic processes failed, leading to the drainage from his umbilicus?","options":["Obliteration of the distal umbilical arteries","Obliteration of the vitelline duct","Obliteration of the allantois","Fusion of the urorectal septum with the cloacal membrane"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-1","obj":"UR-1.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the fluid as urine because its creatinine matches urine.","Find the connection between bladder and umbilicus: the allantois, which becomes the urachus.","Normally the urachus closes into the median umbilical ligament. If it stays open, urine drains from the umbilicus."],"why":"The allantois extends from the top of the urogenital sinus (the future bladder) into the umbilical cord. Its intra-abdominal part, the urachus, normally closes into a fibrous cord, the median umbilical ligament. If it stays open, urine leaks from the umbilicus. High bladder pressure from outlet obstruction, as with posterior urethral valves, can keep the urachus open. Partial closure leaves a urachal cyst or sinus instead.","whyNot":["The distal umbilical arteries become the medial umbilical ligaments. They carry no urine.","A patent vitelline duct connects the umbilicus with the ileum and drains intestinal contents, not urine.","Correct.","Failure of the urorectal septum leaves fistulas between the rectum and the urinary tract. It does not connect the bladder to the umbilicus."],"study":{"topics":["Allantois and urachus","Umbilical remnants"],"comps":["UR-1"]}},{"id":"UR-C25-Q4","stem":"Voiding cystourethrography also shows contrast refluxing up both ureters into dilated renal pelves. At 3 weeks of age, he develops fever and Escherichia coli bacteriuria. Which of the following normal defenses has failed most directly?","options":["Flap-valve closure of the intramural ureter","Neutrophil adhesion to the bladder urothelium","Complement-mediated lysis of gram-negative rods","Placental transfer of maternal IgG"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.5","dok":3,"approach":["Link the reflux to the defense: the ureter runs obliquely through the bladder wall, so bladder pressure normally squeezes it shut.","Explain the failure: high bladder pressure from the valves distorts the ureterovesical junction, so the flap valve no longer closes.","Predict the consequence: infected bladder urine is pushed up to the kidneys."],"why":"The ureter passes obliquely through the bladder wall. When the detrusor contracts, the rising pressure flattens this intramural segment and acts as a one-way flap valve. In posterior urethral valves, chronically high bladder pressure and wall thickening distort this junction, so urine refluxes up the ureters with each void. Reflux carries bacteria to the kidneys and raises the risk of pyelonephritis and scarring.","whyNot":["Correct.","Neutrophil adhesion fails in leukocyte adhesion deficiency, which causes infections without pus and delayed cord separation. It does not cause vesicoureteral reflux.","Complement deficiencies predispose to Neisseria and encapsulated organisms. They do not explain reflux of urine into the kidneys.","Maternal IgG crosses the placenta normally in a term infant, and antibody levels do not explain urine flowing back up the ureters."],"study":{"topics":["Urinary tract defenses","Vesicoureteral reflux"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-C1"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C26","system":"UR","dx":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction with hydronephrosis in a horseshoe kidney","codes":["UR-12-C1"],"case":"A 27-year-old woman comes to the physician because of episodes of dull left flank pain for 6 months. The pain often starts after she drinks large amounts of fluid and resolves within a few hours. Vital signs are within normal limits. Abdominal examination shows mild left-sided deep tenderness. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1). CT scan shows a horseshoe kidney with its lower poles joined by an isthmus anterior to the aorta at the level of L4. The left renal pelvis and calyces are markedly dilated, with an abrupt narrowing where the pelvis meets the ureter and a normal-caliber ureter below. Diagnosis: ureteropelvic junction obstruction with hydronephrosis in a horseshoe kidney.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C26-Q1","stem":"Which of the following structures most likely prevented normal ascent of her kidneys during development?","options":["Superior mesenteric artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk","Bifurcation of the common iliac artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-1","obj":"UR-1.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that the metanephric kidneys form in the pelvis and ascend to the lumbar region between about weeks 6 and 9.","Note that fused lower poles form an isthmus that crosses in front of the aorta.","Identify the vessel that the isthmus catches on during ascent: the inferior mesenteric artery."],"why":"The permanent kidneys arise in the pelvis and rise toward the lumbar region as the body grows. If the lower poles fuse early, the joined kidney forms a U-shaped horseshoe kidney with an isthmus in front of the aorta. As it rises, the isthmus catches under the inferior mesenteric artery, so the kidney stays lower than normal, usually at the level of L3 to L5. Horseshoe kidneys carry a higher risk of ureteropelvic junction obstruction, stones, and infection.","whyNot":["The superior mesenteric artery arises at about L1, above the inferior mesenteric artery. The isthmus is caught before it gets that high.","Correct.","The celiac trunk arises at about T12, far above the level a horseshoe kidney ever reaches.","The common iliac bifurcation lies at the pelvic brim. The kidneys normally ascend past it, and a horseshoe kidney is caught higher, at the inferior mesenteric artery."],"study":{"topics":["Renal ascent and horseshoe kidney","Congenital renal anomalies"],"comps":["UR-1","UR-12-C1"]}},{"id":"UR-C26-Q2","stem":"Two years later, she is pregnant. At 30 weeks' gestation, ultrasonography shows new mild dilation of the right renal pelvis and right ureter down to the pelvic brim, with a normal-caliber ureter below that point. Which of the following best explains this new finding?","options":["Compression of the ureter by the enlarged uterus at the pelvic brim","Progression of her congenital ureteropelvic junction narrowing","Vesicoureteral reflux through an incompetent ureterovesical junction","A ureteral stone impacted at the ureterovesical junction"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-C1","obj":"UR-12-C.1","dok":3,"approach":["Find the level: the ureter is dilated only above the pelvic brim.","Match the level to a cause: in late pregnancy the uterus compresses the ureter where it crosses the pelvic brim, more often on the right.","Exclude the others by side or level: her known narrowing is on the left, and a distal stone or reflux would dilate the whole ureter."],"why":"Hydronephrosis has typical causes by age and sex. Infants and children have ureteropelvic junction obstruction and posterior urethral valves; young adults have stones; pregnant women have compression by the uterus; older men have prostatic enlargement; and older women have pelvic tumors such as cervical cancer. In pregnancy the enlarged uterus presses on the ureter at the pelvic brim, and progesterone relaxes ureteral smooth muscle. The right side is affected more often because the uterus usually rotates to the right.","whyNot":["Correct.","Her ureteropelvic junction narrowing is on the left, and it dilates only the pelvis and calyces, not the ureter.","Reflux dilates the full length of the ureter from the bladder upward, not only the part above the pelvic brim.","A stone at the ureterovesical junction would dilate the ureter all the way down to the bladder."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of hydronephrosis by age and sex","Hydronephrosis of pregnancy"],"comps":["UR-12-C1"]}},{"id":"UR-C26-Q3","stem":"After delivery, she develops fever of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F), left flank pain, and Escherichia coli bacteriuria. Voiding cystourethrography shows no reflux, and the left renal pelvis remains dilated. Which of the following best explains why the left kidney is more prone to infection than the right?","options":["Reduced shedding of urothelial cells in the renal pelvis","Bacterial entry through a defective ureterovesical flap valve","Stagnant urine that is not cleared by antegrade flow","Loss of Tamm-Horsfall protein secretion by the left kidney"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the asymmetry: only the left side has a dilated, poorly draining pelvis.","Recall the most important defense: steady one-way flow of urine washes bacteria out.","Apply it: stagnant urine in the obstructed pelvis lets bacteria multiply."],"why":"The urinary tract is protected mostly by flow. Urine moves steadily from kidney to bladder and is emptied completely, which flushes bacteria out before they can multiply. Above an obstruction, urine pools in a dilated pelvis and turns over slowly, giving bacteria time to grow. Obstruction anywhere in the tract therefore raises the risk of pyelonephritis on that side.","whyNot":["Urothelial shedding helps clear attached bacteria, but obstruction does not selectively stop it. Stasis is the main problem.","The study showed no reflux, so the ureterovesical flap valve is working.","Correct.","Tamm-Horsfall protein (uromodulin) is made by the thick ascending limb of both kidneys and binds bacteria. There is no reason for it to be lost on one side."],"study":{"topics":["Urinary tract defenses","Obstruction and infection"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-C1"]}},{"id":"UR-C26-Q4","stem":"A pyeloplasty is performed. In a normally positioned left kidney, which of the following structures lies directly anterior to the renal hilum?","options":["Visceral surface of the liver","Hepatic flexure of the colon","Second part of the duodenum","Tail of the pancreas"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.2","dok":2,"approach":["Work out the side: this question is about the left kidney.","List the anterior relations of the left kidney: stomach, spleen, tail of the pancreas, jejunum, and the left colic flexure.","Choose the structure at the hilum: the pancreatic tail lies across it."],"why":"The left kidney is related anteriorly to the stomach above, the spleen laterally, the tail of the pancreas across the hilum, the jejunum below and medially, and the left colic flexure and descending colon laterally. The right kidney is related to the liver, the descending (second) part of the duodenum near its hilum, and the right colic (hepatic) flexure. These relations matter in trauma and surgery near either kidney.","whyNot":["The liver lies in front of the upper part of the right kidney.","The hepatic (right colic) flexure lies in front of the lower pole of the right kidney.","The second part of the duodenum lies in front of the hilum of the right kidney.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anterior relations of the kidneys"],"comps":["UR-3"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C27","system":"UR","dx":"Ureteral ligation with hydronephrosis after hysterectomy","codes":["UR-12-C1"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman has right flank pain 4 days after an abdominal hysterectomy for uterine leiomyomas. Temperature is 37.8 °C (100.0 °F), pulse is 90/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Her urine output is normal. Serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1), unchanged from before surgery. Ultrasonography shows moderate right hydronephrosis and a dilated right ureter; the left kidney is normal. CT urography shows that contrast in the right ureter stops abruptly in the distal ureter, with no leakage. At repeat operation, a suture is found around the right ureter. Diagnosis: inadvertent ligation of the right ureter during hysterectomy, with hydronephrosis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C27-Q1","stem":"Immediately after the ligation, which of the following changes most directly reduced the glomerular filtration rate of the right kidney?","options":["Increased hydrostatic pressure in Bowman space","Increased oncotic pressure in the glomerular capillaries","Decreased hydrostatic pressure in the glomerular capillaries","Decreased oncotic pressure in Bowman space"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Write the Starling balance: GFR depends on capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space hydrostatic pressure minus capillary oncotic pressure.","Ask which term the blockage changes: urine backs up from the ureter into the tubules and Bowman space.","Raised Bowman space pressure opposes filtration, so GFR falls."],"why":"Net filtration pressure equals glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space hydrostatic pressure minus glomerular capillary oncotic pressure (Bowman space oncotic pressure is near zero). A blocked ureter raises pressure in the pelvis, tubules, and Bowman space, which pushes against filtration. Hypoalbuminemia does the opposite by lowering capillary oncotic pressure, and dehydration lowers GFR mainly by reducing renal blood flow and capillary pressure.","whyNot":["Correct.","Capillary oncotic pressure rises with dehydration or when filtration fraction increases. A ureteral ligature does not change plasma proteins.","Glomerular capillary pressure falls with afferent arteriolar constriction or low blood pressure, as in prerenal states. The ligature acts downstream of the glomerulus.","Bowman space oncotic pressure is already close to zero. A fall would favor filtration, not reduce it."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces and GFR","GFR in urinary obstruction"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-12-C1"]}},{"id":"UR-C27-Q2","stem":"Years later, she is the restrained driver in a motor vehicle collision while her bladder is full. CT cystography shows contrast outlining loops of small bowel. Which of the following best explains the location of the contrast?","options":["Rupture of the bladder neck below the peritoneal reflection","Rupture of the peritoneum-covered bladder dome","Rupture of the anterior wall into the retropubic space","Rupture of the pelvic ureter at the iliac vessels"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Read the image: contrast around bowel loops means it is inside the peritoneal cavity.","Recall the bladder's relation to peritoneum: only the dome (superior surface) is covered by peritoneum.","A sudden blow to a full bladder bursts the dome, its weakest part, and urine spills into the peritoneal cavity."],"why":"The kidneys and ureters lie behind the peritoneum, and the bladder lies below it, with peritoneum covering only its superior surface (the dome). When a full bladder is struck, pressure spikes and the dome ruptures, so urine leaks into the peritoneal cavity. Pelvic fractures more often tear the bladder near its neck or anterior wall, and urine then leaks into the extraperitoneal tissues around the bladder.","whyNot":["Rupture of the bladder neck occurs below the peritoneum, so contrast collects in the extraperitoneal pelvic tissues.","Correct.","Anterior wall tears, often from pelvic fracture fragments, leak into the retropubic space, which lies outside the peritoneum.","The ureter is retroperitoneal. A ureteral tear leaks into the retroperitoneum, not around bowel loops."],"study":{"topics":["Bladder rupture patterns","Peritoneal relations of the urinary tract"],"comps":["UR-4"]}},{"id":"UR-C27-Q3","stem":"At which of the following sites was the ureter most likely ligated during the hysterectomy?","options":["At the ureteropelvic junction near the renal hilum","At the pelvic brim anterior to the iliac vessels","Lateral to the cervix beneath the uterine artery","Within the bladder wall at the ureteral orifice"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the step of hysterectomy where the ureter is most at risk: clamping and tying the uterine artery.","Recall the anatomy: in the base of the broad ligament, about 2 cm lateral to the cervix, the uterine artery passes over the ureter.","A suture placed around the artery here can catch the ureter."],"why":"In the base of the broad ligament, the uterine artery crosses above and in front of the ureter about 2 cm lateral to the cervix, a relation often remembered as water under the bridge. When the uterine vessels are clamped and tied, the ureter can be caught in the ligature or cut. Ligation causes hydronephrosis above the tie, while transection causes a urine leak and urinoma or a fistula.","whyNot":["The ureteropelvic junction lies near the kidney, far from the operative field of a hysterectomy.","The ureter does cross the iliac vessels at the pelvic brim, but this site is above the uterine vessels and is not where the uterine artery is tied.","Correct.","The intramural ureter lies within the bladder wall. Injury there would come from bladder surgery, and it is not the classic site in hysterectomy."],"study":{"topics":["Ureter and uterine artery","Ureteral injury in pelvic surgery"],"comps":["UR-4","UR-12-C1"]}},{"id":"UR-C27-Q4","stem":"Her serum creatinine remained normal despite complete obstruction of the right ureter. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Dilution of serum creatinine by intravenous fluids","Adequate filtration by the unobstructed left kidney","Lower creatinine generation from postoperative muscle loss","Tubular secretion of creatinine by the obstructed right kidney"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-C1","obj":"UR-12-C.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note that only one ureter is blocked.","Recall that one healthy kidney can clear enough creatinine to keep serum creatinine near normal.","Contrast with bilateral obstruction (or obstruction of a single kidney), which causes postrenal acute kidney injury."],"why":"Unilateral obstruction often causes few signs beyond flank pain, because the other kidney compensates. Serum creatinine and urine output stay near normal, and the problem can be missed until imaging shows hydronephrosis. Postrenal acute kidney injury with oliguria or anuria requires obstruction of both kidneys, of a single functioning kidney, or of the bladder outlet.","whyNot":["Intravenous fluids lower serum creatinine only slightly and briefly. They do not explain a stable value over several days.","Correct.","Creatinine production falls only slowly with muscle loss and would not mask the loss of a kidney's function after a few days.","Secretion into a tubule that cannot drain does not remove creatinine from the body. The blocked kidney contributes little clearance."],"study":{"topics":["Unilateral versus bilateral obstruction","Postrenal acute kidney injury"],"comps":["UR-12-C1","UR-12-A4"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C28","system":"UR","dx":"Postrenal acute kidney injury from benign prostatic hyperplasia with postobstructive diuresis","codes":["UR-12-C1","UR-12-A4"],"case":"A 74-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because he has passed only small amounts of urine for 3 days. He has had nocturia, hesitancy, and a weak stream for several years. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 156/88 mm Hg. The bladder is palpable to the umbilicus. Digital rectal examination shows a smooth, symmetrically enlarged, rubbery prostate. Serum studies show urea nitrogen (BUN) 68 mg/dL (7 to 20), creatinine 4.2 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3), potassium 6.1 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), and bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (22 to 28). Ultrasonography shows bilateral hydronephrosis. A urethral catheter drains 1600 mL. Over the next 24 hours, his urine output is about 400 mL/h. Diagnosis: postrenal acute kidney injury from benign prostatic hyperplasia, with postobstructive diuresis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C28-Q1","stem":"An ECG shows peaked T waves. He is given intravenous insulin with glucose, and his serum potassium falls within 30 minutes. Which of the following best explains this effect?","options":["Increased potassium secretion by collecting duct principal cells","Increased potassium loss through colonic epithelium","Stabilization of cardiac membranes against potassium","Stimulation of Na+/K+-ATPase in muscle and liver cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the speed: a fall within 30 minutes means a shift into cells, not removal from the body.","Recall what insulin does to potassium: it activates the Na+/K+-ATPase, mainly in skeletal muscle and liver.","Conclude that potassium moves from the blood into cells while total body potassium stays the same."],"why":"Insulin activates the Na+/K+-ATPase, which pumps potassium into cells. Beta-2 agonists and alkalosis also shift potassium inward. Acidosis, cell lysis, insulin deficiency, and hyperosmolality shift it out, and his metabolic acidosis adds to his hyperkalemia. Hyperkalemia causes peaked T waves and, when severe, a wide QRS. A shift buys time, but the potassium still has to be removed from the body, here by relieving the obstruction.","whyNot":["Aldosterone, not insulin, is the main driver of potassium secretion by principal cells, and that effect takes hours.","The colon secretes some potassium, and binding resins work there, but insulin does not act this way.","Calcium, not insulin, stabilizes the cardiac membrane. It protects the heart without lowering serum potassium.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Potassium shifts between compartments","ECG changes of hyperkalemia"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-A4"]}},{"id":"UR-C28-Q2","stem":"He requires an indwelling urethral catheter for 2 weeks while awaiting surgery. Which of the following best explains why the catheter raises his risk of urinary tract infection?","options":["It suppresses neutrophil migration into the bladder wall","It neutralizes the acidity of the urine within the bladder","It lets bacteria ascend in a biofilm along its surface","It blocks secretion of IgA by the urothelium"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.5","dok":2,"approach":["List the normal defenses: a closed urethra, regular flushing by voiding, and a urothelium that sheds attached bacteria.","Ask what a catheter does: it holds the urethra open and gives bacteria a surface to grow on.","Conclude that bacteria climb along the catheter in a biofilm that the defenses cannot reach."],"why":"A catheter bypasses the urethral sphincter and the flushing action of voiding. Bacteria from the perineum or the drainage system attach to the catheter surface and form a biofilm that protects them from antibodies, neutrophils, and many antibiotics. They then climb along the outside or inside of the catheter into the bladder. The risk rises each day the catheter stays in place, which is why catheters are removed as soon as possible.","whyNot":["Catheters do not suppress neutrophil function. Neutrophils still enter the bladder wall, but they cannot clear bacteria inside a biofilm.","Urine pH is not the main defense, and catheters do not neutralize urine acidity.","Correct.","Secretory IgA plays only a small role in the bladder, and a catheter does not block its secretion."],"study":{"topics":["Catheter-associated urinary infection","Urinary tract defenses"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-11"]}},{"id":"UR-C28-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains his large urine output after the catheter is placed?","options":["Urea diuresis plus impaired tubular concentrating ability","Glucose-driven osmotic diuresis after insulin treatment","Central suppression of ADH release after bladder decompression","Afferent arteriolar dilation that raises glomerular filtration"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-C1","obj":"UR-12-C.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize postobstructive diuresis after relief of bilateral obstruction.","Identify the retained solute: urea and sodium built up while both kidneys were blocked, and they are now filtered and excreted.","Add the tubular injury: back-pressure damaged the tubules and washed out the medullary gradient, so the kidney cannot concentrate urine."],"why":"Bilateral obstruction (here at the bladder outlet) causes postrenal acute kidney injury. Unilateral obstruction usually does not, because the other kidney compensates. After relief, the kidneys excrete the urea, sodium, and water retained during the obstruction. Back-pressure has also injured the tubules, reduced the medullary concentration gradient, and blunted the response to ADH, so the urine is dilute and large in volume. Severe diuresis can cause volume depletion and electrolyte losses.","whyNot":["Correct.","His glucose was given with insulin and would not reach levels high enough to cause glucosuria and osmotic diuresis.","Draining the bladder does not suppress ADH release. The problem lies in the kidney's response to ADH, not in its secretion.","Relief of obstruction lowers Bowman space pressure, but this restores filtration toward normal. It does not explain urine output far above normal."],"study":{"topics":["Postobstructive diuresis","Bilateral obstruction and AKI"],"comps":["UR-12-C1","UR-12-A4"]}},{"id":"UR-C28-Q4","stem":"Before catheterization, he had constant dribbling of small volumes from a large bladder. A similar overflow pattern without any outlet obstruction is most likely to result from which of the following lesions?","options":["Infarction of the medial frontal cortex above the pons","Demyelinating plaque in the cervical spinal cord","Complete transection of the spinal cord at the T6 level","Destruction of the sacral cord segments S2 to S4"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.7","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the bladder type: large, overfilled, and dribbling means a flaccid overflow bladder.","Recall the reflex: detrusor contraction depends on parasympathetic efferents in the pelvic splanchnic nerves from S2 to S4.","A lesion that destroys the sacral reflex center leaves the detrusor unable to contract."],"why":"Micturition is a spinal reflex at S2 to S4 that is coordinated by the pontine micturition center and controlled by the frontal cortex. Parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic nerves contract the detrusor, sympathetic fibers from about T11 to L2 relax it and close the internal sphincter, and the pudendal nerve controls the external sphincter. Damage at the sacral cord or cauda equina abolishes the reflex, so the bladder fills until it overflows. Lesions above the sacral cord leave the reflex intact but uncoordinated, giving a small, spastic bladder.","whyNot":["A frontal lesion removes cortical inhibition, causing urgency and uninhibited voiding of normal or small volumes.","A cervical plaque in multiple sclerosis is above the sacral cord and causes a spastic bladder, often with detrusor and sphincter dyssynergia.","A T6 transection spares the sacral reflex arc but cuts pontine control. After spinal shock, the bladder becomes small and spastic and empties reflexly.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Micturition reflex","Spastic versus flaccid bladder"],"comps":["UR-6"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C29","system":"UR","dx":"Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis from a proton pump inhibitor","codes":["UR-12-D1"],"case":"A 67-year-old woman comes to the physician because of fatigue, low-grade fever, and a rash for 5 days. She started omeprazole for gastroesophageal reflux 3 weeks ago. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 86/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. A maculopapular rash covers her trunk. Serum creatinine is 2.6 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1); 2 months ago it was 0.9 mg/dL. Leukocyte count is 9800/mm3 with 9% eosinophils (1 to 4). Urinalysis shows positive leukocyte esterase, negative nitrite, trace protein, 20 to 30 WBCs/hpf, and WBC casts. Urine culture shows no growth. Diagnosis: acute interstitial nephritis caused by omeprazole.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C29-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best interprets her urinalysis?","options":["Sterile pyuria arising from inflammation within the kidney","Lower urinary tract inflammation from urethritis","Bacterial cystitis with nitrate-reducing gram-negative rods","Contamination of the specimen by vaginal leukocytes"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Read each result: leukocyte esterase means pyuria; a negative nitrite and a negative culture mean no usual bacterial infection.","Localize the source: WBC casts form only inside renal tubules.","Conclude that this is sterile pyuria from inflammation in the kidney."],"why":"Leukocyte esterase detects white cells in the urine. Nitrite appears when Enterobacterales such as Escherichia coli reduce dietary nitrate, so a negative nitrite with a sterile culture argues against a common bacterial infection. Casts are molded in the tubules, so WBC casts place the inflammation in the kidney, as in interstitial nephritis or pyelonephritis. Sterile pyuria without casts suggests urethritis, as from Chlamydia, or renal tuberculosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Urethritis causes sterile pyuria, but the inflammation is below the kidney, so there would be no WBC casts.","Bacterial cystitis would usually give a positive nitrite and a positive culture, and it would not produce WBC casts.","Contamination brings squamous cells and leukocytes but cannot produce casts, which form only in renal tubules."],"study":{"topics":["Urine dipstick interpretation","Sterile pyuria and WBC casts"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-D1"]}},{"id":"UR-C29-Q2","stem":"A renal biopsy shows interstitial edema with an infiltrate of lymphocytes, macrophages, and eosinophils invading the tubules; the glomeruli are normal. Which of the following mechanisms is most likely responsible for her kidney injury?","options":["Direct dose-dependent toxicity to proximal tubular cells","Delayed hypersensitivity to a drug-modified tubular antigen","Antibodies against type IV collagen in the glomerular basement membrane","Immune complex deposition along the glomerular basement membrane"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-D1","obj":"UR-12-D.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the timing and systemic signs: fever, rash, and eosinophilia beginning weeks after a new drug.","Read the biopsy: interstitial inflammation with eosinophils and tubulitis, but normal glomeruli.","Conclude that this is an immune hypersensitivity reaction to the drug in the interstitium, mainly T cell mediated."],"why":"Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis is an immune reaction, not a toxic one. The drug or its metabolite binds to tubular or interstitial proteins and acts as a hapten, and T cells react against the new antigen (a delayed, type IV reaction), often with features of IgE-mediated hypersensitivity such as eosinophilia. It begins days to weeks after starting drugs such as penicillins, NSAIDs, proton pump inhibitors, and diuretics, and it does not depend on the dose. Stopping the drug usually leads to recovery.","whyNot":["Direct dose-dependent toxicity, as with aminoglycosides, causes acute tubular necrosis without fever, rash, or eosinophilia.","Correct.","Anti-type IV collagen antibodies cause anti-GBM disease with crescents and linear IgG staining, and often lung hemorrhage.","Immune complex deposition causes glomerulonephritis with granular deposits. Her glomeruli are normal."],"study":{"topics":["Drug-induced interstitial nephritis","Hypersensitivity mechanisms in the kidney"],"comps":["UR-12-D1"]}},{"id":"UR-C29-Q3","stem":"Her BUN is 34 mg/dL (7 to 20), fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) is 2.6 percent, and urine osmolality is 320 mOsm/kg H2O. Volume depletion from poor oral intake had been suspected at first. Which of the following sets of findings would have supported volume depletion as the cause of her rising creatinine?","options":["BUN to creatinine ratio above 20, FENa above 2 percent, urine osmolality below 350 mOsm/kg","BUN to creatinine ratio below 15, FENa above 2 percent, urine osmolality below 350 mOsm/kg","BUN to creatinine ratio above 20, FENa below 1 percent, urine osmolality above 500 mOsm/kg","BUN to creatinine ratio below 15, FENa below 1 percent, urine osmolality above 500 mOsm/kg"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A4","obj":"UR-12-A.14","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that in prerenal injury the tubules work normally and hold on to sodium, water, and urea.","Predict the indices: FENa under 1 percent, concentrated urine above 500 mOsm/kg, and a BUN to creatinine ratio over 20 because urea is reabsorbed with water.","Compare her values: a ratio of about 13, FENa 2.6 percent, and dilute urine point to intrinsic injury."],"why":"Acute kidney injury is classified as prerenal, intrinsic, or postrenal. In prerenal states, low perfusion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and ADH, so the healthy tubules keep sodium and water, and urea follows water back into the blood. This gives a FENa below 1 percent, a high urine osmolality, and a BUN to creatinine ratio above 20. In intrinsic disease such as acute tubular necrosis or interstitial nephritis, damaged tubules lose sodium and cannot concentrate urine, giving a FENa above 2 percent and urine osmolality near plasma.","whyNot":["A high ratio with a high FENa and dilute urine is a mixed pattern that does not fit healthy tubules responding to low perfusion.","This set fits intrinsic tubular injury, which matches her actual values, not volume depletion.","Correct.","A low FENa with concentrated urine suggests intact tubules, but a ratio below 15 argues against the extra urea reabsorption of a prerenal state."],"study":{"topics":["Classification of acute kidney injury","FENa and BUN to creatinine ratio"],"comps":["UR-12-A4","UR-12-D1"]}},{"id":"UR-C29-Q4","stem":"Two weeks after the drug is stopped, her serum creatinine is 1.8 mg/dL. Serum studies show sodium 138 mEq/L (136 to 145), potassium 5.9 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), chloride 112 mEq/L (98 to 106), and bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (22 to 28). Urine pH is 5.0. Which of the following best describes her acid-base disorder?","options":["Proximal (type 2) RTA with bicarbonate wasting","Type 4 RTA from reduced aldosterone effect","Distal (type 1) RTA with impaired H+ secretion","Anion gap acidosis from uremic acid retention"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["Calculate the anion gap: 138 minus 112 minus 18 equals 8, which is normal.","Use potassium to sort the RTAs: hyperkalemia points to type 4, while types 1 and 2 cause hypokalemia.","Check the urine pH: it is below 5.5, which fits type 4 and rules out type 1."],"why":"Type 4 RTA comes from low aldosterone or from tubules that do not respond to it, which is common in tubulointerstitial disease. Without aldosterone action, principal cells secrete less potassium and intercalated cells secrete less H+. The hyperkalemia also suppresses ammonium production from glutamine in the proximal tubule, so the kidney excretes less acid. The result is a normal anion gap acidosis with hyperkalemia, and the urine can still be acidified below 5.5.","whyNot":["Proximal RTA causes hypokalemia from bicarbonate and sodium delivery to the distal nephron.","Correct.","Distal RTA causes hypokalemia and a urine pH above 5.5 despite acidemia.","Retained uremic anions widen the anion gap, mostly when GFR is much lower. Her anion gap is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Type 4 renal tubular acidosis","Comparing RTA types"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-12-D1"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C30","system":"UR","dx":"Analgesic nephropathy with papillary necrosis","codes":["UR-12-D1"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman comes to the physician because of left flank pain and red urine for 1 day. She passed a small piece of tissue in her urine this morning. She has taken daily combination analgesic tablets containing aspirin, acetaminophen, and caffeine for chronic headaches for 20 years. She has never smoked. Vital signs are within normal limits. Serum creatinine is 1.9 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1). Urinalysis shows many RBCs, 10 to 15 WBCs/hpf, and no bacteria; urine culture shows no growth. CT scan shows small kidneys with irregular contours and calcified renal papillae. Diagnosis: analgesic nephropathy with chronic interstitial nephritis and papillary necrosis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C30-Q1","stem":"Which of the following conditions also predisposes to the papillary lesion seen in this patient?","options":["Membranous nephropathy with nephrotic syndrome","Minimal change disease in a child","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","Long-standing type 2 diabetes mellitus"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-D1","obj":"UR-12-D.2","dok":2,"approach":["Name the lesion: papillary necrosis, ischemic death of the tips of the medullary pyramids.","List its causes: analgesics, diabetes, sickle cell disease, severe pyelonephritis, and obstruction.","Pick the option on that list."],"why":"Papillary necrosis is ischemic necrosis of the renal papillae. Its classic causes are long-term combination analgesic use, diabetes mellitus, sickle cell disease or trait, severe acute pyelonephritis, and urinary obstruction. Diabetes contributes through small-vessel disease and a higher risk of infection. A sloughed papilla can block the ureter and cause colic and hematuria, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Membranous nephropathy is an immune complex disease of the glomerular basement membrane. It does not cause papillary necrosis.","Minimal change disease causes nephrotic syndrome with podocyte foot process effacement and spares the papillae.","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is an immune complex glomerular disease that causes the nephritic syndrome, not papillary necrosis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Causes of papillary necrosis","Analgesic nephropathy"],"comps":["UR-12-D1","UR-12-D2"]}},{"id":"UR-C30-Q2","stem":"Long-term aspirin use inhibits prostaglandin synthesis and reduces vasodilation in the renal medulla. Which of the following best explains why the papillae are more vulnerable to this effect than the cortex?","options":["Cortical peritubular capillaries supply the papillae","Juxtamedullary glomeruli receive most of the renal blood flow","Papillary cells rely mainly on oxidative fatty acid metabolism","Slow vasa recta flow keeps papillary oxygen tension low"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the medullary blood supply: the vasa recta, which carry only a small share of renal blood flow.","Recall the countercurrent arrangement: slow flow and oxygen diffusion from descending to ascending vessels keep oxygen tension at the papillary tip low.","Apply it: tissue that is already near hypoxia dies first when prostaglandin-driven vasodilation is lost."],"why":"The medulla receives only a small share of renal blood flow, carried by the vasa recta. Their slow flow and hairpin, countercurrent arrangement preserve medullary hypertonicity by keeping solute from being washed out. The same arrangement lets oxygen diffuse from descending to ascending vessels, so the papillary tip has a very low oxygen tension. Prostaglandins help keep medullary flow up, so blocking them can tip the papillae into ischemic necrosis. Low oxygen tension also explains why red cells sickle in the vasa recta.","whyNot":["The papillae are supplied by the vasa recta, which come from efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons, not by cortical peritubular capillaries.","Most renal blood flow goes to the cortex, not to the juxtamedullary glomeruli or the medulla.","Medullary cells depend more on anaerobic glycolysis because oxygen there is scarce, not on oxidative fatty acid metabolism.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vasa recta and countercurrent exchange","Medullary hypoxia"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-12-D1"]}},{"id":"UR-C30-Q3","stem":"Five years later, she has painless gross hematuria. CT urography shows a papillary filling defect within the left renal pelvis, and the renal parenchyma is not expanded. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Urothelial carcinoma","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","Oncocytoma","Angiomyolipoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-G","obj":"UR-12-G.5","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the mass: it arises from the lining of the renal pelvis, not from the renal parenchyma.","Recall the tumor of that lining: urothelial carcinoma.","Link the risk factor: long-term analgesic use, especially phenacetin-containing mixtures, raises the risk of urothelial carcinoma of the renal pelvis."],"why":"The renal pelvis, ureters, and bladder are lined by urothelium, and urothelial carcinoma is the main tumor of that lining. It usually presents with painless hematuria and tends to be multifocal and to recur. Major risk factors are smoking, aromatic amine dyes, cyclophosphamide, and long-term analgesic use with phenacetin. Patients with analgesic nephropathy have a clearly increased risk of urothelial carcinoma of the renal pelvis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma arises from proximal tubule cells in the cortex and expands the renal parenchyma.","Oncocytoma is a benign parenchymal tumor from intercalated cells, with a central scar on imaging.","Angiomyolipoma is a parenchymal tumor of vessels, smooth muscle, and fat, linked to tuberous sclerosis."],"study":{"topics":["Urothelial carcinoma risk factors","Renal pelvis tumors"],"comps":["UR-12-G","UR-12-D1"]}},{"id":"UR-C30-Q4","stem":"Years before her flank pain began, she had nocturia, and her urine osmolality stayed close to that of plasma. Loss of which of the following nephron populations most directly impairs urine concentration?","options":["Cortical nephrons with short loops of Henle and peritubular capillaries","Cortical nephrons with long loops and vasa recta","Juxtamedullary nephrons with long loops and vasa recta","Juxtamedullary nephrons with short loops and peritubular capillaries"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the early sign: a concentrating defect, which reflects medullary and papillary injury.","Recall the two nephron types: juxtamedullary nephrons have glomeruli near the medulla, long loops reaching the papilla, and vasa recta.","Conclude that loss of the long loops removes the deep medullary gradient needed to concentrate urine."],"why":"Cortical nephrons make up most nephrons. Their glomeruli lie in the outer cortex, their loops of Henle are short, and their tubules are surrounded by peritubular capillaries. Juxtamedullary nephrons have glomeruli near the corticomedullary junction, long loops that reach deep into the papilla, and efferent arterioles that form the vasa recta. Their long loops build the steep medullary gradient that lets ADH concentrate urine. Papillary damage destroys these loops early, causing nocturia and urine that cannot be concentrated.","whyNot":["Cortical nephrons do have short loops and peritubular capillaries, but they add little to the deep medullary gradient, and they are spared early in papillary disease.","Cortical nephrons have short loops and peritubular capillaries, not long loops and vasa recta.","Correct.","Juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops and vasa recta, not short loops and peritubular capillaries."],"study":{"topics":["Cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons","Urine concentrating mechanism"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-5"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C31","system":"UR","dx":"Chronic pyelonephritis with reflux nephropathy","codes":["UR-12-D2"],"case":"A 26-year-old woman comes to the physician for evaluation of high blood pressure found at a pre-employment examination. Between ages 2 and 7 years, she had repeated febrile urinary tract infections. She takes no medications. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 76/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 152/96 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 1.3 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1). Urinalysis shows 2+ protein with negative leukocyte esterase and nitrite. Ultrasonography shows a small left kidney (8.2 cm) with coarse, irregular cortical scars overlying dilated, blunted calyces; the right kidney measures 12.0 cm and appears normal. Diagnosis: chronic pyelonephritis due to reflux nephropathy.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C31-Q1","stem":"Records show that cystoscopy at age 5 years found the left ureteral orifice displaced laterally on the bladder base, with a short submucosal ureteral tunnel. Which of the following developmental events most likely produced this orifice position?","options":["Persistent obstruction of the ureteric lumen during fetal recanalization","Origin of the ureteric bud at an abnormally cranial site on the mesonephric duct","Early division of the ureteric bud into two separate branches","Origin of the ureteric bud at an abnormally caudal site on the mesonephric duct"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-1","obj":"UR-1.4","dok":3,"approach":["Link the finding to the problem: a lateral orifice with a short tunnel is the classic setup for reflux.","Recall how the orifice gets its position: the caudal mesonephric duct is absorbed into the bladder wall, and the ureteric orifice then migrates cranially and laterally.","A bud that starts too low is absorbed early and migrates too far, so it ends up lateral with a short tunnel."],"why":"The ureteric bud grows out of the mesonephric duct just above where the duct enters the urogenital sinus, the future bladder. As the end of the mesonephric duct is absorbed into the bladder wall, the ureteric orifice moves cranially and laterally. A bud that arises too caudally is absorbed early and travels too far, so its orifice sits lateral on the bladder base with a short intravesical segment. A short tunnel is not squeezed shut when the bladder contracts, so urine refluxes up the ureter.","whyNot":["Incomplete recanalization of the ureter has been proposed as a cause of congenital ureteral narrowing, such as ureteropelvic junction obstruction. It narrows the lumen and does not move the orifice laterally.","A bud that arises too cranially is absorbed late and stays caudal and medial, so its orifice lands near the bladder neck or in the urethra. That ectopic position tends to cause obstruction, or continuous dribbling in girls when the orifice lies below the sphincter.","Early splitting of a single bud gives a bifid ureter that joins before the bladder and drains through one orifice in a normal position.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ureteric bud origin and orifice position","Ectopic ureteral orifices"],"comps":["UR-1","UR-12-E4"]}},{"id":"UR-C31-Q2","stem":"Her reflux was never documented during childhood. Which of the following studies would have most directly demonstrated the abnormality that led to her renal scarring?","options":["Contrast-enhanced abdominal CT","Voiding cystourethrography","Technetium-99m DMSA cortical scan","Renal ultrasonography with Doppler flow"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-E4","obj":"UR-12-E.5","dok":2,"approach":["Name the underlying problem: childhood febrile infections plus scarring over blunted calyces point to vesicoureteral reflux.","Ask what reflux requires to be seen: contrast inside the bladder, imaged while the bladder contracts.","Only a voiding cystourethrogram fills the bladder with contrast and watches it move up the ureter during voiding."],"why":"Primary vesicoureteral reflux comes from a short intravesical ureteral segment. Normally the ureter runs obliquely through the bladder wall, so rising bladder pressure during voiding flattens it like a flap valve. When the segment is short, the valve fails and urine is pushed up toward the kidney, carrying bacteria with it. A voiding cystourethrogram fills the bladder with contrast through a catheter and images the patient while voiding, so it shows the reflux itself and its grade.","whyNot":["CT shows renal scarring and complications such as abscess. It exposes a child to radiation and does not image the bladder during voiding.","Correct.","A DMSA scan shows cortical scars and acute pyelonephritis. It documents the damage, not the reflux that caused it.","Ultrasonography shows kidney size, scars, and hydronephrosis, but a normal ultrasound does not exclude reflux because it does not image urine moving backward during voiding."],"study":{"topics":["Primary vesicoureteral reflux","Voiding cystourethrogram"],"comps":["UR-12-E4","UR-12-D2"]}},{"id":"UR-C31-Q3","stem":"Her blood pressure remains elevated even though her daily sodium excretion matches her sodium intake and her plasma volume is stable. Which of the following best explains how her scarred kidney sustains the hypertension?","options":["Excess aldosterone secretion that does not depend on renin release","Sustained rise in cardiac output from a continually expanding plasma volume","A higher arterial pressure is needed to excrete her daily sodium load","Resetting of carotid sinus baroreceptors to a higher pressure"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.7","dok":3,"approach":["Note that she is in sodium balance: output equals intake and volume is steady.","Recall pressure natriuresis: the kidney excretes more sodium as arterial pressure rises, and long-term pressure settles where output equals intake.","Apply it: a scarred kidney with fewer working nephrons reaches that balance only at a higher pressure."],"why":"Pressure natriuresis is the kidney's long-term control of blood pressure. When arterial pressure rises, the kidney excretes more sodium and water, which lowers volume and brings pressure back down. Over weeks, arterial pressure settles at the point where sodium output equals intake. Scarring with nephron loss, and renin release from ischemic areas, shift this pressure natriuresis curve to the right, so balance is reached only at a higher arterial pressure.","whyNot":["Primary hyperaldosteronism raises pressure with low renin and hypokalemia. Nothing here suggests an adrenal source, and renal scarring tends to raise renin rather than suppress it.","Her plasma volume is stable, so volume is not still expanding. Once balance is restored, the hypertension is maintained by the kidney's higher set point.","Correct.","Baroreceptors reset to a new pressure within days. They buffer short-term changes and cannot set the long-term level of arterial pressure."],"study":{"topics":["Pressure natriuresis","Long-term blood pressure control"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-12-D2"]}},{"id":"UR-C31-Q4","stem":"Her kidney function continues to decline, and the left kidney is eventually removed. Microscopic examination of the scarred regions is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Patchy tubular necrosis with muddy brown granular casts","Atrophic tubules filled with eosinophilic hyaline casts","Concentric onion-skin thickening of arteriolar walls","Fibrocellular crescents filling Bowman space"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-D2","obj":"UR-12-D.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the lesion: chronic pyelonephritis from reflux, with scars over blunted calyces.","Recall its microscopy: chronic interstitial inflammation, interstitial fibrosis, and atrophic, dilated tubules.","Pick the classic picture: tubules packed with pink casts that look like thyroid follicles (thyroidization)."],"why":"Chronic pyelonephritis is a tubulointerstitial disease. Scarred areas show interstitial fibrosis, lymphocytes and plasma cells, and atrophic tubules that are dilated and filled with eosinophilic hyaline casts. Rows of these tubules look like thyroid follicles, so the pattern is called thyroidization. Glomeruli are spared early, though some later develop periglomerular fibrosis and secondary segmental sclerosis, which explains her proteinuria.","whyNot":["Muddy brown casts and patchy tubular necrosis are the signs of acute tubular necrosis, an acute injury. Her disease is chronic and scarring.","Correct.","Onion-skin arteriolar thickening is hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis from malignant hypertension. Her hypertension is moderate and secondary to the scarring.","Crescents form in rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis when fibrin and cells fill Bowman space. Reflux nephropathy damages tubules and interstitium first."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic pyelonephritis morphology","Thyroidization of tubules"],"comps":["UR-12-D2","UR-12-E4"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C32","system":"UR","dx":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis with an obstructing stone","codes":["UR-12-D2","UR-12-C2"],"case":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of dull right flank pain, intermittent low-grade fever, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 92/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (12 to 16), and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1). Urine pH is 8.5 (4.5 to 8.0), and urine culture grows Proteus mirabilis. CT shows an enlarged, poorly functioning right kidney with a branching staghorn calculus filling the renal pelvis and calyces, and dilated calyces surrounded by low-density masses. The left kidney is normal. Right nephrectomy is performed. Diagnosis: xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis with an obstructing staghorn calculus.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C32-Q1","stem":"Which of the following properties of the infecting organism most directly explains the composition of her calculus?","options":["Urease activity that splits urea into ammonia","Swarming motility that spreads it along the urothelium","Nitrate reductase activity that generates urinary nitrite","Lactose fermentation that produces organic acids in urine"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.3","dok":2,"approach":["Connect the clues: Proteus, alkaline urine, and a staghorn calculus.","Recall that staghorn stones in infected, alkaline urine are struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate).","Ask what makes urine alkaline and supplies ammonium: urease splitting urea."],"why":"Proteus mirabilis makes urease, which splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide. Ammonia takes up hydrogen ions to form ammonium, and the urine pH rises above about 7. In alkaline urine, magnesium, ammonium, and phosphate precipitate as struvite, often mixed with calcium phosphate. These stones grow quickly into a staghorn shape and shelter bacteria, which keeps the infection going.","whyNot":["Correct.","Swarming motility is a laboratory clue, a spreading film of growth on agar plates. It helps identify Proteus but has no role in stone chemistry.","Nitrate reductase gives a positive nitrite dipstick in Enterobacterales infections. Nitrite does not change urine pH or form stones.","Proteus does not ferment lactose. Acid production would lower urine pH, which favors uric acid stones rather than struvite."],"study":{"topics":["Proteus urease and struvite stones","Urine pH and stone type"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-C2"]}},{"id":"UR-C32-Q2","stem":"Preoperative imaging raised concern for renal cell carcinoma. The cut surface of the resected kidney shows yellow-orange nodules surrounding the dilated calyces. Microscopic examination of these nodules is most likely to show sheets of which of the following?","options":["Lipid-laden foamy macrophages","Polygonal clear cells in nests with delicate vessels","Epithelioid histiocytes in caseating granulomas","Neutrophils within tubules and the interstitium"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-D2","obj":"UR-12-D.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the setting: a chronic obstructed infection with Proteus and a staghorn stone.","Recall the host response in xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis: macrophages take up lipid from destroyed tissue and fail to clear the bacteria.","Yellow nodules made of foamy macrophages explain why it can look like a clear cell tumor."],"why":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis is a chronic destructive infection, usually behind an obstructing stone and often with Proteus or E. coli. Macrophages flood the kidney, ingest lipid released from damaged tissue, and become foamy. Mixed with plasma cells, lymphocytes, and giant cells, they form yellow nodules that replace the parenchyma. On imaging and grossly this can mimic renal cell carcinoma, and the foamy macrophages can resemble tumor clear cells under the microscope.","whyNot":["Correct.","Nests of clear epithelial cells with a fine vascular network describe clear cell renal cell carcinoma, the lesion this condition mimics. The infection and staghorn stone favor xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis.","Caseating granulomas suggest renal tuberculosis, which gives sterile pyuria on routine culture. Her culture grew Proteus.","Neutrophils in tubules and interstitium are the picture of acute pyelonephritis. Her months-long destructive process with yellow nodules is chronic."],"study":{"topics":["Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","Mimics of renal cell carcinoma"],"comps":["UR-12-D2","UR-12-G1"]}},{"id":"UR-C32-Q3","stem":"CT also shows that the inflammation has spread through the anterior surface of the medial part of the right kidney, near the hilum, and formed a fistula into adjacent bowel. Which of the following structures is most likely involved?","options":["Right colic flexure","Horizontal part of the duodenum","Right lobe of the liver","Descending part of the duodenum"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.2","dok":3,"approach":["Locate the leak: anterior, medial, at the level of the hilum of the right kidney.","List the anterior relations of the right kidney: liver above, right colic flexure at the lower lateral part, and the descending duodenum medially.","The only bowel lying on the medial, hilar part is the descending (second) part of the duodenum."],"why":"The front of the right kidney touches three main structures. The right lobe of the liver covers the upper part, separated by the hepatorenal recess. The right colic flexure lies on the lower lateral part. The descending part of the duodenum lies on the medial part near the hilum. Inflammation breaking through the anteromedial surface at the hilum would therefore reach the descending duodenum, and renoduodenal fistulas are a recognized complication of this infection.","whyNot":["The right colic flexure lies against the lower lateral part of the right kidney. A fistula from that region would enter the colon.","The horizontal part of the duodenum crosses the midline at about L3, below the kidney and in front of the inferior vena cava and aorta. It does not lie on the renal hilum.","The liver lies in front of the upper part of the right kidney and is not bowel. Spread there would form a hepatic abscess.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anterior relations of the kidneys","Duodenum and right kidney"],"comps":["UR-3","UR-12-D2"]}},{"id":"UR-C32-Q4","stem":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma, which this lesion resembled, can raise the hematocrit by producing erythropoietin. In a normal kidney, which of the following best describes how erythropoietin production is regulated?","options":["High tubular sodium chloride signals the macula densa to release it","Low perfusion pressure triggers its release from juxtaglomerular cells","Hypoxia stabilizes HIF in peritubular interstitial fibroblasts","Hypoxia increases HIF hydroxylation in proximal tubule cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.5","dok":3,"approach":["Name the stimulus: low oxygen delivery to the kidney, not low pressure or salt.","Recall the molecular switch: with normal oxygen, HIF-alpha is hydroxylated and destroyed; with hypoxia, it survives and turns on the erythropoietin gene.","Place it in the right cells: fibroblast-like interstitial cells around the peritubular capillaries."],"why":"Erythropoietin is made by fibroblast-like interstitial cells next to the peritubular capillaries of the cortex and outer medulla. With normal oxygen, prolyl hydroxylases use oxygen to hydroxylate HIF-alpha, and the VHL protein tags it for destruction. In hypoxia, hydroxylation stops, HIF-alpha builds up, and it switches on the erythropoietin gene. Clear cell carcinoma usually loses VHL, so HIF stays active and can drive erythropoietin release and polycythemia. Loss of these interstitial cells in chronic kidney disease causes normocytic anemia, while her own anemia reflects chronic inflammation because her left kidney is normal.","whyNot":["The macula densa senses tubular sodium chloride and adjusts afferent arteriolar tone and renin release. It does not release erythropoietin.","Juxtaglomerular cells release renin when afferent arteriolar pressure falls. They do not make erythropoietin.","Correct.","HIF hydroxylation happens when oxygen is plentiful and leads to HIF breakdown. Hypoxia stops hydroxylation, and the producing cells are interstitial cells rather than tubular epithelium."],"study":{"topics":["Erythropoietin and HIF","Anemia of chronic kidney disease"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-G1"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C33","system":"UR","dx":"Interstitial cystitis (bladder pain syndrome)","codes":["UR-12-D"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman comes to the physician because of an 18-month history of suprapubic pain that worsens as her bladder fills and eases after voiding. She urinates about 16 times daily and twice nightly and has a near-constant urge to void. Five urine cultures obtained during symptomatic periods showed no growth, and several courses of antibiotics did not help. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse is 72/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Pelvic examination shows suprapubic and anterior vaginal wall tenderness. Urine cytology is negative. Cystoscopy with hydrodistention shows pinpoint submucosal hemorrhages and a reddened mucosal patch with radiating vessels on the bladder dome. Diagnosis: interstitial cystitis (bladder pain syndrome) with a Hunner lesion.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C33-Q1","stem":"At her first visit, she was treated for presumed bacterial cystitis. Which of the following urine dipstick results would most specifically have indicated infection with Escherichia coli or another member of the Enterobacterales?","options":["Positive leukocyte esterase","Positive nitrite","Elevated specific gravity","Positive blood"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.4","dok":2,"approach":["Ask what each dipstick pad detects: white cells, red cells, concentration, or a bacterial product.","Recall that Enterobacterales carry nitrate reductase, which turns dietary nitrate in urine into nitrite.","Only the nitrite pad detects a product made by the bacteria themselves."],"why":"Gram-negative enteric rods such as E. coli, Klebsiella, and Proteus reduce urinary nitrate to nitrite with nitrate reductase. A positive nitrite test therefore points to these organisms specifically. The test needs a few hours of bladder dwell time, so frequent voiding, as in this patient, can cause false negatives. Enterococci and Staphylococcus saprophyticus usually give a negative nitrite test.","whyNot":["Leukocyte esterase detects neutrophils, so it shows pyuria from any cause. Sterile pyuria occurs in urethritis from Chlamydia and in renal tuberculosis.","Correct.","Specific gravity reflects urine concentration and hydration. It says nothing about infection.","A positive blood pad detects hemoglobin or myoglobin. It occurs with stones, tumors, glomerular disease, and inflamed mucosa, so it does not point to a bacterial cause."],"study":{"topics":["Urine dipstick in infection","Nitrate reductase"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-H2"]}},{"id":"UR-C33-Q2","stem":"She also reports leaking small amounts of urine when she coughs, sneezes, or lifts her children, with no preceding urge. She has had three vaginal deliveries. Which of the following best explains this leakage?","options":["Involuntary detrusor contractions during bladder filling","Bladder overdistension from impaired detrusor contraction","Weakened pelvic floor support of the proximal urethra","Fistula between the bladder and the anterior vagina"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.6","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the leak: small volumes with coughing or lifting and no urge is stress incontinence.","Recall what normally prevents it: the levator ani and endopelvic fascia hold the proximal urethra so abdominal pressure compresses it as much as the bladder.","Vaginal deliveries can weaken that support, so the urethra drops and opens under strain."],"why":"Normally the bladder neck and proximal urethra sit on a hammock of levator ani and endopelvic fascia. When abdominal pressure rises, it squeezes the urethra as well as the bladder, so the urethra stays closed. Stretching or tearing of this support during vaginal delivery lets the urethra become hypermobile and drop below it. Then a cough raises bladder pressure above urethral closing pressure, and urine leaks without any detrusor contraction.","whyNot":["Involuntary detrusor contractions cause urge incontinence, with a sudden strong need to void before leakage. Her leakage has no urge.","Poor detrusor contraction leads to retention and overflow incontinence, with constant dribbling from an overfull bladder, as in diabetic neuropathy or outlet obstruction.","Correct.","A vesicovaginal fistula causes continuous leakage from the vagina regardless of effort. It typically follows pelvic surgery, radiation, or obstructed labor."],"study":{"topics":["Types of urinary incontinence","Pelvic floor support of the urethra"],"comps":["UR-4","UR-12-D"]}},{"id":"UR-C33-Q3","stem":"One proposed mechanism of her disorder is a defect in the bladder lining that lets urinary solutes, such as potassium, reach suburothelial nerves. During normal bladder filling, which of the following allows the lining to stretch while staying impermeable?","options":["Tight junctions between surface cells open to relieve tension","Keratinized squamous surface cells slide over each other","Intermediate cells divide rapidly to cover the larger surface","Umbrella cells flatten and insert stored membrane vesicles"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the tissue: urothelium (transitional epithelium) with large surface umbrella cells.","Recall the barrier: umbrella cells have tight junctions and an apical membrane of stiff uroplakin plaques.","Recall how it stretches: the cells flatten, and stored vesicles fuse with the apical membrane to add surface area without opening any gaps."],"why":"The urothelium lines the bladder with several layers of cells topped by large umbrella cells. Their apical membrane is built of rigid uroplakin plaques sealed by tight junctions, which makes a strong barrier to urine. As the bladder fills, the umbrella cells flatten and fuse stored membrane vesicles into their apical surface, so the lining enlarges while the barrier stays intact. When the bladder empties, the extra membrane is taken back in. Damage to this barrier is one theory for the pain of interstitial cystitis.","whyNot":["Opening tight junctions would let urine leak into the bladder wall. The junctions stay sealed during filling.","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium covers skin. The bladder has a nonkeratinized transitional epithelium.","Bladder filling happens over minutes to hours, far too fast for cell division. The lining enlarges by changing cell shape and adding membrane.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Urothelium and umbrella cells","Bladder wall histology"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-12-D"]}},{"id":"UR-C33-Q4","stem":"A biopsy of the reddened patch is taken to exclude carcinoma in situ. Which of the following microscopic findings is most consistent with her diagnosis?","options":["Full-thickness urothelial atypia with loss of polarity","Sheets of macrophages containing Michaelis-Gutmann bodies","Nests of urothelium in the lamina propria with central lumens","Mucosal ulceration with chronic inflammation and mast cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-D","obj":"UR-12-D.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that interstitial cystitis is a diagnosis of exclusion: cultures are negative and cytology rules out tumor.","Recall the Hunner lesion: a reddened area of mucosa with radiating vessels that may ulcerate.","Match the histology: ulceration with chronic inflammation and fibrosis through the wall, with prominent mast cells."],"why":"Interstitial cystitis is chronic bladder pain with urgency and frequency in the absence of infection, and it is much more common in women. Some patients have Hunner lesions, patches of reddened mucosa that ulcerate. Biopsy shows mucosal ulceration, chronic inflammation, and fibrosis that can extend through the wall, and mast cells are characteristically increased. The biopsy matters because carcinoma in situ of the bladder can cause the same urgency and frequency.","whyNot":["Full-thickness atypia with loss of polarity is urothelial carcinoma in situ, the lesion this biopsy is meant to exclude. Her cytology was negative.","Macrophages with laminated Michaelis-Gutmann bodies define malakoplakia, a reaction to chronic bacterial infection, usually E. coli. Her cultures are sterile.","Nests of urothelium with central lumens are cystitis cystica, a common reactive change. It does not cause the chronic pain syndrome described.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Interstitial cystitis","Hunner lesion"],"comps":["UR-12-D","UR-12-H2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C34","system":"UR","dx":"Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease with a ruptured berry aneurysm","codes":["UR-12-E2"],"case":"A 44-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after the sudden onset of a severe headache that he describes as the worst of his life. He has a 6-year history of hypertension and has had two episodes of flank pain with gross hematuria. His father began dialysis at age 52 years. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse is 88/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 182/104 mm Hg. He has nuchal rigidity. Large, nodular masses are palpable in both flanks. Serum creatinine is 2.1 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Noncontrast CT of the head shows blood in the basal cisterns. Abdominal CT shows bilaterally enlarged kidneys replaced by numerous cysts. Diagnosis: autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease with subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured berry aneurysm.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C34-Q1","stem":"His sister's newborn son has a palpable left flank mass. Ultrasonography shows a left kidney replaced by noncommunicating cysts of varying size with no recognizable normal parenchyma. The right kidney and amniotic fluid volume were normal. Which of the following best describes the infant's condition?","options":["A sporadic malformation of metanephric differentiation","An early presentation of his uncle's inherited disease","Autosomal recessive cystic disease with hepatic fibrosis","Acquired cysts from dilated tubules after tubular injury"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-E2","obj":"UR-12-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: one kidney replaced by cysts of varying size, with a normal opposite kidney and normal amniotic fluid.","Compare cystic diseases: polycystic disease of either inheritance pattern involves both kidneys, and acquired cystic disease follows years of dialysis.","A unilateral cystic, nonfunctioning kidney in a newborn is multicystic dysplastic kidney, a sporadic developmental defect."],"why":"Multicystic dysplastic kidney comes from abnormal differentiation of the metanephros. It is usually unilateral and not inherited, and the kidney is replaced by cysts with immature ducts and sometimes cartilage. Because the other kidney works, amniotic fluid is normal and the prognosis is good. Both forms of polycystic kidney disease are bilateral, and autosomal recessive disease shows huge kidneys in infancy with Potter sequence and congenital hepatic fibrosis. Acquired cystic disease develops in adults after years of dialysis and carries a risk of renal cell carcinoma.","whyNot":["Correct.","Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is bilateral and usually becomes evident in adulthood. A single cystic kidney with a normal partner does not fit.","Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease enlarges both kidneys, causes oligohydramnios, and comes with congenital hepatic fibrosis. This infant's right kidney and amniotic fluid were normal.","Acquired cystic disease develops in scarred kidneys of adults on long-term dialysis. It does not occur in a newborn."],"study":{"topics":["Multicystic dysplastic kidney","Cystic kidney diseases compared"],"comps":["UR-12-E2","UR-1"]}},{"id":"UR-C34-Q2","stem":"His 39-year-old brother carries the same mutation and has mildly enlarged cystic kidneys. Which of the following extrarenal lesions is the brother most likely to have?","options":["Hemangioblastoma of the cerebellum","Multiple cysts in the liver","Facial angiofibromas","Fibrosis of portal tracts in the liver"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-E2","obj":"UR-12-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the syndrome: bilateral cystic kidneys in a family with dominant inheritance is autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease.","List its extrarenal features: hepatic cysts, berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse, colonic diverticula, and hernias.","Separate it from other genetic cystic syndromes and pick its most common extrarenal lesion."],"why":"Hepatic cysts are the most common extrarenal lesion in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, and they become more frequent with age. Polycystins are also expressed in bile duct epithelium, so cysts form there as well. Other associations include berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse, colonic diverticula, and abdominal wall hernias. Several other genetic syndromes also cause renal cysts, so the extrarenal lesion helps identify the disease.","whyNot":["Cerebellar hemangioblastoma belongs to von Hippel-Lindau disease, which causes renal cysts and clear cell renal carcinoma from loss of the VHL gene on chromosome 3.","Correct.","Facial angiofibromas belong to tuberous sclerosis (TSC1 or TSC2), which causes renal cysts and angiomyolipomas.","Congenital hepatic fibrosis with portal fibrosis accompanies autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease and leads to portal hypertension in childhood."],"study":{"topics":["Extrarenal features of ADPKD","Genetic syndromes with renal cysts"],"comps":["UR-12-E2","UR-9"]}},{"id":"UR-C34-Q3","stem":"On hospital day 4, he is alert and appears euvolemic. Serum sodium is 127 mEq/L (136 to 145), serum osmolality is 262 mOsm/kg H2O (275 to 295), urine osmolality is 560 mOsm/kg H2O, and urine sodium is 62 mEq/L. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his hyponatremia?","options":["Inappropriate ADH release after brain injury","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from cystic kidneys","Central diabetes insipidus from hypothalamic injury","Primary polydipsia from increased water intake"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.5","dok":3,"approach":["Read the plasma: low sodium and low osmolality mean too much water relative to solute.","Read the urine: an osmolality of 560 means the collecting duct is reabsorbing water, so ADH is acting despite hypotonic plasma.","Euvolemia with concentrated urine after subarachnoid hemorrhage points to inappropriate ADH secretion."],"why":"The loop of Henle uses countercurrent multiplication to build a hyperosmotic medulla, and ADH inserts aquaporin-2 into collecting duct cells so water can follow that gradient. Normally, hypotonic plasma shuts off ADH and the urine becomes dilute. Here the urine is concentrated while the plasma is dilute, so ADH is being released inappropriately. Brain injury such as subarachnoid hemorrhage is a common cause, and urine sodium stays high because the patient is euvolemic and taking in salt.","whyNot":["Correct.","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus also gives dilute urine and high or high-normal sodium, with no response to desmopressin. Cystic kidneys can impair concentration, but his urine is concentrated.","Central diabetes insipidus removes ADH, so the urine is dilute and serum sodium rises. Urine osmolality rises after desmopressin.","Primary polydipsia lowers sodium, but ADH is suppressed and the urine is maximally dilute, often below 100 mOsm/kg H2O."],"study":{"topics":["Countercurrent multiplication and ADH","SIADH versus diabetes insipidus"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C34-Q4","stem":"Genetic testing shows a mutation in the gene on chromosome 16 that is responsible for most cases of his disease. Which of the following correctly identifies the gene product and the probability that his 16-year-old daughter inherited the mutation?","options":["Polycystin-2; 50 percent","Fibrocystin; 25 percent","Polycystin-1; 50 percent","Polycystin-1; 25 percent"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.1","dok":2,"approach":["Match the chromosome to the gene: PKD1 on chromosome 16 encodes polycystin-1.","Recall the inheritance: autosomal dominant, so an affected heterozygous parent passes the mutant allele to each child half the time.","Combine the two."],"why":"About 85 percent of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is due to PKD1 on chromosome 16, which encodes polycystin-1. Most of the rest is due to PKD2 on chromosome 4, which encodes polycystin-2 and causes a milder disease with later kidney failure. Polycystins work together in the primary cilia of tubular cells, and their loss lets cysts grow. Because inheritance is autosomal dominant, each child of an affected heterozygous parent has a 50 percent chance of inheriting the mutation.","whyNot":["Polycystin-2 is encoded by PKD2 on chromosome 4. It causes a minority of cases.","Fibrocystin is encoded by PKHD1, the gene of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease. A 25 percent risk applies when both parents are carriers.","Correct.","Polycystin-1 is the right protein, but 25 percent is the recurrence risk for a recessive disease. A dominant mutation passes to half of an affected parent's children."],"study":{"topics":["PKD1 and PKD2 genetics","Autosomal dominant inheritance"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-E2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C35","system":"UR","dx":"Unilateral renal agenesis with compensatory hypertrophy","codes":["UR-12-E3"],"case":"A 27-year-old woman is evaluated in the emergency department after a low-speed motor vehicle collision. She has no significant injuries. Abdominal CT shows no right kidney and an enlarged left kidney measuring 13.8 cm (normal adult length about 10 to 12 cm) with normal contrast enhancement. She has no history of urinary tract disease. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse is 80/min, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1), and urinalysis is normal. Cystoscopy performed later shows no right ureteral orifice and no right half of the trigone. Diagnosis: right unilateral renal agenesis with compensatory hypertrophy of the left kidney.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C35-Q1","stem":"Which of the following embryonic structures normally gives rise to the organ that is missing on her right side, and when does that structure first appear?","options":["Metanephros, in the fifth week","Mesonephros, in the fourth week","Pronephros, in the fourth week","Metanephros, in the tenth week"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-1","obj":"UR-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the missing organ: the permanent kidney.","Recall the three kidney systems in order: pronephros, mesonephros, metanephros.","Only the metanephros persists as the adult kidney, and it appears in the fifth week."],"why":"Three kidney systems form in sequence from intermediate mesoderm. The pronephros appears early in the fourth week and is rudimentary and nonfunctional. The mesonephros forms late in the fourth week and works briefly as an interim kidney, and its duct gives rise to the ureteric bud. The metanephros appears in the fifth week and becomes the permanent kidney, starting to make urine around the end of the first trimester.","whyNot":["Correct.","The mesonephros functions briefly and then mostly regresses, though its duct contributes to the male genital ducts and gives rise to the ureteric bud.","The pronephros is a transient, nonfunctional structure that regresses. It never forms the adult kidney.","The metanephros is the right structure, but it appears in the fifth week. By about the end of the first trimester it is already producing urine."],"study":{"topics":["Pronephros, mesonephros, metanephros","Timeline of kidney development"],"comps":["UR-1","UR-12-E3"]}},{"id":"UR-C35-Q2","stem":"If her renal agenesis had been bilateral, which of the following would most likely have caused death shortly after birth?","options":["Hypoplastic left heart syndrome","Pulmonary hypoplasia","Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest","Intraventricular hemorrhage"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-E3","obj":"UR-12-E.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the first consequence: with no kidneys, the fetus makes no urine, so amniotic fluid is very low.","Recall what amniotic fluid does: it is needed for lung growth and gives the fetus room to move.","Oligohydramnios causes Potter sequence, and the lethal part is underdeveloped lungs."],"why":"From the second trimester onward, fetal urine provides most of the amniotic fluid. Without kidneys, oligohydramnios develops, and the compressed fetus forms Potter sequence: flattened facies, limb and positional deformities, and pulmonary hypoplasia. The placenta clears wastes before birth, so uremia is not the immediate threat. Newborns die of respiratory failure from small, underdeveloped lungs.","whyNot":["Hypoplastic left heart is a separate cardiac malformation. It is not a consequence of absent fetal urine.","Correct.","Without kidneys, potassium would rise over days after birth, but respiratory failure from small lungs occurs within hours.","Intraventricular hemorrhage is a complication of prematurity due to a fragile germinal matrix. It does not follow from renal agenesis."],"study":{"topics":["Potter sequence","Amniotic fluid and lung development"],"comps":["UR-12-E3","UR-1"]}},{"id":"UR-C35-Q3","stem":"In her solitary kidney, each remaining nephron shows increased glomerular plasma flow and increased single-nephron GFR, while filtration fraction stays about the same. Which of the following arteriolar changes best explains these findings?","options":["Dilation of the efferent arteriole","Constriction of the afferent arteriole","Dilation of the afferent arteriole","Constriction of the efferent arteriole"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Write down the pattern: plasma flow up, GFR up, filtration fraction unchanged.","Predict each change: afferent dilation raises both flow and glomerular pressure; efferent constriction raises GFR but lowers flow, so filtration fraction rises.","Only afferent dilation raises both by similar amounts."],"why":"Filtration fraction is GFR divided by renal plasma flow. Dilating the afferent arteriole lets more blood into the glomerulus and transmits more pressure to its capillaries, so plasma flow and GFR both rise and filtration fraction changes little. This adaptive hyperfiltration lets one kidney do the work of two. Over decades, the higher glomerular pressure may contribute to proteinuria and glomerulosclerosis in some people with a single kidney.","whyNot":["Efferent dilation raises plasma flow but lowers glomerular pressure and GFR, so filtration fraction falls. This is the effect of ACE inhibitors.","Afferent constriction lowers both plasma flow and GFR, as with NSAIDs blocking vasodilating prostaglandins or in sympathetic activation.","Correct.","Efferent constriction raises glomerular pressure and GFR but reduces plasma flow, so filtration fraction rises. This is the angiotensin II effect."],"study":{"topics":["Arteriolar control of GFR and RPF","Hyperfiltration in a solitary kidney"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-6"]}},{"id":"UR-C35-Q4","stem":"Which of the following developmental failures best accounts for her absent right kidney, ureteral orifice, and hemitrigone?","options":["Failure of the right kidney to ascend from the pelvis","Failure of metanephric mesenchyme to form nephrons","Absence of the right ureteric bud","Fusion of the lower poles of both kidneys"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-1","obj":"UR-1.2","dok":3,"approach":["Note what is missing: kidney, ureter, ureteral orifice, and half of the trigone.","Recall what each tissue builds: the ureteric bud forms the ureter, pelvis, calyces, and collecting ducts; the metanephric mesenchyme forms nephrons, but only when the bud induces it.","Missing ureter structures plus missing kidney means the bud never formed, so induction never happened."],"why":"The kidney forms by reciprocal induction. The metanephric mesenchyme signals the ureteric bud to grow and branch into the ureter, pelvis, calyces, and collecting ducts. The bud in turn induces the mesenchyme to form nephrons from Bowman capsule to the distal convoluted tubule. With no bud, there is no ureter, no ureteral orifice, and no ipsilateral trigone, and the mesenchyme is never induced, so no kidney forms.","whyNot":["Failed ascent produces a pelvic kidney, which is present and has a ureter. Her kidney is absent altogether.","If the bud formed but the mesenchyme failed to respond, a ureter and ureteral orifice would still be present, sometimes ending blindly. She has neither.","Correct.","Fusion of the lower poles produces a horseshoe kidney trapped below the inferior mesenteric artery. Both kidneys and both ureters are present."],"study":{"topics":["Reciprocal induction in kidney development","Renal agenesis"],"comps":["UR-1","UR-12-E3"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C36","system":"UR","dx":"Duplex collecting system with vesicoureteral reflux and acute pyelonephritis in a toddler","codes":["UR-12-E4","UR-12-H1"],"case":"A 2-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of fever, vomiting, and irritability for 2 days. Temperature is 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), pulse is 150/min, respirations are 30/min, and blood pressure is 94/56 mm Hg. She cries when pressure is applied at the left costovertebral angle. Urinalysis of a catheterized specimen shows positive leukocyte esterase and nitrite, numerous neutrophils, and white blood cell casts. Urine culture grows more than 100,000 CFU/mL of Escherichia coli. Ultrasonography shows a left kidney with two separate collecting systems. Voiding cystourethrography shows reflux into the ureter draining the left lower pole, which enters the bladder separately from the upper pole ureter. Diagnosis: complete left duplex collecting system with vesicoureteral reflux and acute pyelonephritis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C36-Q1","stem":"Ultrasonography also shows that the upper pole collecting system and its ureter are dilated, with the ureter ending in a cystic bulge within the bladder near the bladder neck. Which of the following best explains why the upper pole moiety is obstructed while the lower pole moiety refluxes?","options":["Upper pole ureter lacks a muscular coat along its length","Upper pole ureter is compressed by an aberrant lower pole artery","Upper pole ureter has a short tunnel that opens laterally","Upper pole ureter opens caudal and medial to the other"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-1","obj":"UR-1.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the rule for complete duplication: the two ureters cross so that the upper pole ureter inserts lower and more medially, and the lower pole ureter inserts higher and more laterally.","Relate position to function: a low, medial, ectopic orifice often has a ureterocele and obstructs; a high, lateral orifice has a short tunnel and refluxes.","Match to her findings: upper pole dilated with a ureterocele, lower pole refluxing."],"why":"In a complete duplex system, the upper pole ureter enters the bladder caudal and medial to the lower pole ureter. This is the Weigert-Meyer rule. The ectopic upper pole orifice may sit near the bladder neck, often with a ureterocele, and it tends to obstruct. In girls it can even open below the urethral sphincter and cause constant dribbling. The lower pole ureter opens cranial and lateral with a short intravesical tunnel, so it tends to reflux, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Absent or abnormal muscle in the distal ureter is a cause of primary megaureter. It does not explain the paired pattern of an obstructed upper pole and a refluxing lower pole.","A lower pole crossing vessel can kink the ureteropelvic junction and cause obstruction there. The obstruction here is at a ureterocele inside the bladder.","A lateral orifice with a short tunnel describes the lower pole ureter, which refluxes. It does not explain obstruction of the upper pole.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Duplex collecting system","Ectopic ureter and ureterocele"],"comps":["UR-1","UR-12-E4"]}},{"id":"UR-C36-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the organism most likely reached her kidney?","options":["Direct extension from a perinephric abscess","Ascent from the bladder up the ureter","Hematogenous seeding during transient bacteremia","Lymphatic spread from the adjacent colon"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-12-H1","obj":"UR-12-H.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism and its source: E. coli from fecal flora that colonizes the periurethral area.","Identify the anatomic setup: reflux carries bladder urine up to the renal pelvis.","Most acute pyelonephritis is ascending, and reflux makes ascent easy."],"why":"Most acute pyelonephritis is an ascending infection. E. coli from the bowel colonizes the periurethral area, enters the bladder through the short female urethra, and reflux carries infected urine to the renal pelvis and into the collecting ducts. Neutrophils then fill the interstitium and tubules, and white blood cell casts form inside the tubules, which proves the kidney is involved. Clinically this produces fever, flank pain, and costovertebral angle tenderness.","whyNot":["A perinephric abscess is a complication of pyelonephritis that spreads outward from the kidney. It is not the source.","Correct.","Hematogenous pyelonephritis is uncommon and is usually due to Staphylococcus aureus or Candida during sepsis or endocarditis. E. coli with reflux points to ascent.","Lymphatic spread from the bowel is not a recognized main route to the kidney. Enteric organisms reach the kidney by ascending the urinary tract."],"study":{"topics":["Ascending pyelonephritis","White blood cell casts"],"comps":["UR-12-H1","UR-11"]}},{"id":"UR-C36-Q3","stem":"If the reflux persists with repeated infections, coarse scars are most likely to form at the upper and lower poles of the kidney. Which of the following best explains this distribution?","options":["Calyces at the poles empty more slowly into the renal pelvis","Cortex at the poles has the highest density of nephrons","The poles receive the least blood flow from segmental arteries","Compound papillae at the poles have widely open duct orifices"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-D2","obj":"UR-12-D.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how reflux injures the kidney: infected urine must enter the collecting ducts, which is called intrarenal reflux.","Recall papilla shape: simple papillae have slit-like openings that close under pressure; compound papillae, found mainly at the poles, have round openings that stay open.","Scars form where intrarenal reflux occurs, so they cluster at the poles."],"why":"Reflux damages the kidney when infected urine enters the collecting ducts through the papillae. Simple papillae are cone-shaped with slit-like duct openings that close when pelvic pressure rises. Compound papillae, made of fused papillae and concentrated at the upper and lower poles, are flat or concave with round, gaping openings that allow urine to flow back into the ducts. That is why reflux nephropathy produces coarse polar scars over blunted, dilated calyces.","whyNot":["Calyceal emptying is not slower at the poles. The key factor is whether the papilla lets urine back into the collecting ducts.","Nephron density is not highest at the poles, and nephron number would not decide where refluxed urine enters.","Polar regions are supplied by their own segmental arteries and are not relatively ischemic. Uneven blood flow does not explain where reflux scars form.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Intrarenal reflux and compound papillae","Reflux nephropathy scarring"],"comps":["UR-12-D2","UR-12-E4"]}},{"id":"UR-C36-Q4","stem":"Despite intravenous antibiotics, she remains febrile on hospital day 5. Ultrasonography shows a fluid collection in the perirenal fat around the lower pole of the left kidney. Along which of the following paths is this collection most likely to spread?","options":["Inferiorly within the renal fascia toward the pelvis","Through the parietal peritoneum into the greater sac","Laterally through the abdominal wall into the flank","Anteriorly through the renal fascia into the lesser sac"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the space: a perinephric abscess lies in the perirenal fat inside the renal fascia, behind the peritoneum.","Recall how the renal fascia is closed: its layers are fused above and laterally but only loosely joined below.","Fluid follows the path of least resistance, which is downward toward the pelvis."],"why":"The kidney sits in the retroperitoneum inside perirenal fat, which is enclosed by the renal fascia. The anterior and posterior layers of this fascia are fused above the suprarenal gland and along the lateral border, but they are loosely joined below. A perinephric abscess is therefore contained in the retroperitoneum and tends to track inferiorly toward the pelvis. The same posterior position of the kidney, against the 12th rib and posterior abdominal wall, is why pressure at the costovertebral angle hurts in pyelonephritis.","whyNot":["Correct.","The kidney and its fat are retroperitoneal and enclosed by renal fascia, so spread into the peritoneal cavity is uncommon.","The fascial layers are fused along the lateral border, which blocks lateral spread. Pus would have to break through fascia and muscle to reach the flank.","The anterior layer of renal fascia and the parietal peritoneum separate the kidney from the lesser sac. Spread there is not the usual path."],"study":{"topics":["Renal fascia and perirenal space","Perinephric abscess"],"comps":["UR-4","UR-12-H1"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C37","system":"UR","dx":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome after Escherichia coli O157:H7 diarrhea","codes":["UR-12-F1"],"case":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of pallor and decreased urine output for 1 day. Seven days ago, 3 days after eating an undercooked hamburger, he developed abdominal cramps and bloody diarrhea, which have since resolved. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 132/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 112/72 mm Hg. Hemoglobin is 7.6 g/dL (11.5 to 13.5), and platelet count is 38,000/mm3 (150,000 to 400,000). A peripheral blood smear shows schistocytes. Serum creatinine is 2.6 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7). Stool culture grows sorbitol-nonfermenting Escherichia coli O157:H7. Diagnosis: hemolytic uremic syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C37-Q1","stem":"A kidney biopsy shows fibrin-platelet thrombi in glomerular capillaries and in the arterioles that supply them. These arterioles branch directly from which of the following vessels?","options":["Arcuate arteries at the corticomedullary junction","Segmental arteries within the renal sinus","Cortical radiate arteries within the cortex","Interlobar arteries between the renal pyramids"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Name the arterioles that supply glomeruli: afferent arterioles.","Trace the arterial tree: renal, segmental, interlobar, arcuate, cortical radiate, then afferent arterioles.","Afferent arterioles come straight off the cortical radiate (interlobular) arteries."],"why":"Blood runs from the renal artery to segmental arteries in the sinus, interlobar arteries between the pyramids, and arcuate arteries along the corticomedullary junction. Cortical radiate arteries rise from the arcuate arteries into the cortex and give off afferent arterioles, each feeding one glomerulus. The efferent arteriole then forms peritubular capillaries or, near the medulla, the vasa recta. Thrombi in afferent arterioles and glomeruli cut off flow to the tubules downstream, which is why HUS causes acute kidney injury.","whyNot":["Arcuate arteries run along the base of the pyramids and give off the cortical radiate arteries, not afferent arterioles.","Segmental arteries lie in the renal sinus and give rise to interlobar arteries. They are several branchings away from the glomeruli.","Correct.","Interlobar arteries run between the pyramids toward the cortex and branch into arcuate arteries."],"study":{"topics":["Renal arterial tree","Thrombotic microangiopathy in the kidney"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-12-F1"]}},{"id":"UR-C37-Q2","stem":"The toxin made by this organism enters endothelial cells after binding a glycolipid receptor on their surface. Which of the following is its intracellular action?","options":["ADP-ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2","Activation of guanylate cyclase in enterocytes","Removal of an adenine from 28S ribosomal RNA"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-12-F1","obj":"UR-12-F.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the toxin: E. coli O157:H7 makes Shiga toxin.","Recall its structure: the B subunit binds the glycolipid Gb3 on endothelium, and the A subunit enters the cell.","Recall what the A subunit does: it cuts an adenine from 28S rRNA of the 60S ribosomal subunit, stopping protein synthesis."],"why":"Shiga toxin has one A subunit and five B subunits. The B subunits bind globotriaosylceramide (Gb3), which is plentiful on glomerular endothelium, and the A subunit removes an adenine from 28S rRNA in the 60S ribosomal subunit. Protein synthesis stops and endothelial cells are injured, so platelets and fibrin deposit in small vessels. The thrombi consume platelets and shear red cells into schistocytes, giving the triad of thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, and acute kidney injury. TTP produces a similar microangiopathy from ADAMTS13 deficiency, which leaves unusually large von Willebrand factor multimers in the blood.","whyNot":["ADP-ribosylation of Gs keeps adenylate cyclase on. Cholera toxin and the heat-labile toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli act this way and cause watery diarrhea.","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 is the action of diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A. It also stops protein synthesis, by a different target.","Activation of guanylate cyclase is the action of the heat-stable toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli, which causes watery diarrhea without endothelial injury.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Shiga toxin mechanism","HUS versus TTP"],"comps":["UR-12-F1","UR-11"]}},{"id":"UR-C37-Q3","stem":"Urine studies show sodium 64 mEq/L and creatinine 40 mg/dL. Serum sodium is 134 mEq/L (135 to 145). Which of the following best classifies his acute kidney injury?","options":["Prerenal injury; FENa about 0.3 percent","Prerenal injury; FENa about 3 percent","Intrinsic injury; FENa about 0.3 percent","Intrinsic injury; FENa about 3 percent"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-A4","obj":"UR-12-A.14","dok":3,"approach":["Use FENa = (urine Na x plasma creatinine) / (plasma Na x urine creatinine) x 100.","Calculate: (64 x 2.6) / (134 x 40) x 100 = 166 / 5,360 x 100, which is about 3 percent.","A FENa above 2 percent means the tubules are failing to reabsorb sodium, which indicates intrinsic injury."],"why":"Fractional excretion of sodium compares the sodium the kidney excretes with the sodium it filters. In prerenal injury, the tubules are intact and hold on to sodium, so FENa is below 1 percent. When tubules are damaged, as when ischemia downstream of thrombosed glomeruli injures them, sodium is lost and FENa rises above 2 percent. His value of about 3 percent fits intrinsic injury from the thrombotic microangiopathy, even though his earlier diarrhea could have caused a prerenal component.","whyNot":["A FENa of 0.3 percent would fit prerenal injury, but his calculated value is about ten times higher.","A FENa of about 3 percent does not fit prerenal injury, because intact tubules would conserve sodium and keep it below 1 percent.","Intrinsic injury is the right category, but 0.3 percent comes from a calculation error. His FENa is about 3 percent.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Fractional excretion of sodium","Classifying acute kidney injury"],"comps":["UR-12-A4","UR-12-F1"]}},{"id":"UR-C37-Q4","stem":"Two years later, his 7-year-old sister has fever, left flank pain, and white blood cell casts in her urine. Culture grows an Escherichia coli strain that does not produce Shiga toxin. Which of the following virulence factors most likely allowed this strain to colonize her upper urinary tract?","options":["P fimbriae binding Gal-Gal on uroepithelium","Heat-stable toxin activating guanylate cyclase","Intimin binding enterocyte Tir receptors","Type 1 fimbriae binding mannosylated uroplakin"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the infection: fever, flank pain, and white blood cell casts mean acute pyelonephritis.","Recall the adhesins of uropathogenic E. coli: type 1 fimbriae for the bladder, P fimbriae for the upper tract.","Pick the adhesin linked to pyelonephritis."],"why":"Uropathogenic E. coli comes from fecal flora and is the most common cause of urinary tract infection. Type 1 fimbriae bind mannose residues on bladder uroplakins and are mainly linked to cystitis. P fimbriae bind galactose-galactose (Gal-Gal) residues on glycolipids of the upper urinary tract and kidney, which lets the bacteria hold on against urine flow. P fimbriae are strongly associated with pyelonephritis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Heat-stable toxin belongs to enterotoxigenic E. coli and causes watery traveler's diarrhea. It plays no part in urinary infection.","Intimin binds Tir on enterocytes to form attaching and effacing lesions in enteropathogenic and enterohemorrhagic E. coli. It is an intestinal adhesin.","Type 1 fimbriae bind mannose on bladder uroplakins and are associated mostly with cystitis."],"study":{"topics":["Uropathogenic E. coli adhesins","P fimbriae and pyelonephritis"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-H1"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C38","system":"UR","dx":"Malignant hypertension with malignant nephrosclerosis","codes":["UR-12-F2"],"case":"A 39-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a severe headache and blurred vision for 2 days. Three years ago, he was told that he had high blood pressure but did not begin treatment. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 238/136 mm Hg. Funduscopic examination shows papilledema, flame-shaped hemorrhages, and cotton-wool spots. Serum creatinine is 3.8 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3); it was 1.0 mg/dL 1 year ago. Hemoglobin is 10.8 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), and a blood smear shows schistocytes. Urinalysis shows 2+ protein and red blood cells. Plasma renin activity is markedly increased. Diagnosis: malignant hypertension with malignant nephrosclerosis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C38-Q1","stem":"A kidney biopsy is performed. Which of the following arteriolar findings is most likely?","options":["Granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells","Concentric smooth muscle layering with fibrinoid necrosis","Medial calcification without luminal narrowing","Homogeneous pink hyaline thickening of the wall"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-F2","obj":"UR-12-F.2","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the two forms of hypertensive kidney disease: benign (slow, long-standing) and malignant (abrupt, severe).","Recall the arteriolar lesion of each: hyaline arteriolosclerosis in benign; hyperplastic onion-skin arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis in malignant.","His pressure, papilledema, rapid creatinine rise, and schistocytes mark malignant hypertension."],"why":"In malignant hypertension, very high pressure injures arteriolar endothelium. Smooth muscle cells proliferate in concentric layers with reduplicated basement membrane, giving an onion-skin appearance, and the wall can undergo fibrinoid necrosis. The narrowed lumens cause ischemia, which drives renin release and raises pressure further. The kidney surface shows pinpoint hemorrhages (the flea-bitten kidney), and acute renal failure follows.","whyNot":["Granulomatous inflammation with giant cells describes giant cell or Takayasu arteritis. These affect larger arteries, not renal arterioles.","Correct.","Medial calcification without narrowing is Monckeberg sclerosis of muscular arteries. It is usually clinically silent.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis is the lesion of benign nephrosclerosis and of diabetes. It gives symmetrically shrunken kidneys with finely granular surfaces over years."],"study":{"topics":["Benign versus malignant nephrosclerosis","Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis"],"comps":["UR-12-F2","UR-5"]}},{"id":"UR-C38-Q2","stem":"In healthy kidneys, a rise in mean arterial pressure from 90 to 140 mm Hg causes little change in GFR. His blood pressure exceeded the upper limit of this protection. Which of the following normally keeps a rise in arterial pressure from reaching the glomerular capillaries?","options":["Efferent arteriolar constriction caused by angiotensin II","Afferent arteriolar dilation signaled by the macula densa","Afferent arteriolar constriction in response to wall stretch","Mesangial cell contraction in response to natriuretic peptide"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.4","dok":2,"approach":["To protect the glomerulus from a pressure rise, the vessel upstream of it must narrow.","Recall the two autoregulatory mechanisms: the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback. Both constrict the afferent arteriole.","Choose the option that constricts the afferent arteriole: the myogenic response to stretch."],"why":"Autoregulation keeps renal blood flow and GFR nearly constant over a mean arterial pressure of about 75 to 160 mm Hg. In the myogenic response, higher pressure stretches the afferent arteriole, stretch-activated channels depolarize its smooth muscle, calcium enters, and the vessel constricts. Tubuloglomerular feedback adds to this, because more filtered sodium chloride reaching the macula densa triggers afferent constriction through adenosine. Above the upper limit, as in this patient, pressure reaches the arterioles and glomeruli and damages them.","whyNot":["Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole when pressure falls, which keeps GFR up. Efferent constriction would raise glomerular pressure further when arterial pressure rises.","When pressure and filtration rise, the macula densa senses more sodium chloride and signals afferent constriction. Dilation would let even more pressure through.","Correct.","Natriuretic peptides relax mesangial cells and dilate the afferent arteriole, which increases GFR. They do not protect the glomerulus from high pressure."],"study":{"topics":["Renal autoregulation","Myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-2"]}},{"id":"UR-C38-Q3","stem":"Before this presentation, his physician had considered screening him for primary hyperaldosteronism. Which of the following sets of findings would most strongly have supported that diagnosis?","options":["Low renin, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis","High renin, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis","High renin, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis","Low renin, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.7","dok":3,"approach":["Start with aldosterone's actions in the collecting duct: sodium reabsorption with potassium and hydrogen ion secretion.","Predict the electrolytes: hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis.","Predict renin: the extra sodium and volume raise pressure, which suppresses renin from the juxtaglomerular cells."],"why":"An aldosterone-producing adenoma or adrenal hyperplasia makes aldosterone without renin. Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption through ENaC and drives potassium and hydrogen ion secretion, causing hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. The added sodium raises blood pressure, and pressure natriuresis then increases sodium excretion until intake and output balance again. This escape limits edema but leaves the pressure high, and the expanded volume suppresses renin. Malignant hypertension, as in this patient now, has high renin and secondary hyperaldosteronism.","whyNot":["Correct.","High renin with hypokalemia and alkalosis is secondary hyperaldosteronism, as in renovascular or malignant hypertension. It is the pattern expected with his high renin, not the primary adrenal disorder.","High renin with hyperkalemia and acidosis fits primary adrenal insufficiency, where the adrenal cannot make aldosterone despite high renin.","Low renin with hyperkalemia and acidosis is hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism (type 4 renal tubular acidosis), common in diabetic kidney disease."],"study":{"topics":["Primary hyperaldosteronism","Pressure natriuresis and aldosterone escape"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-6"]}},{"id":"UR-C38-Q4","stem":"His markedly increased plasma renin activity reflects ischemia from narrowed renal arterioles. Which of the following cells sense the reduced perfusion pressure and secrete renin?","options":["Extraglomerular mesangial cells near the vascular pole","Modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole","Macula densa cells of the thick ascending limb","Intraglomerular mesangial cells between capillary loops"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.2","dok":2,"approach":["List the parts of the juxtaglomerular apparatus: juxtaglomerular cells, macula densa, and extraglomerular mesangial cells.","Assign the jobs: juxtaglomerular cells sense stretch and secrete renin; the macula densa senses tubular sodium chloride.","Low perfusion pressure is sensed in the arteriolar wall, so the answer is the juxtaglomerular cells."],"why":"Juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole that contain renin granules. They act as baroreceptors, releasing renin when stretch of the arteriole falls, and they also respond to sympathetic beta-1 stimulation and macula densa signals. In malignant nephrosclerosis, narrowed arterioles lower the pressure the juxtaglomerular cells sense, so renin rises and drives the pressure even higher.","whyNot":["Extraglomerular (lacis) mesangial cells lie between the arterioles and the macula densa and likely relay signals between them. They do not store renin.","Correct.","Macula densa cells sit in the distal thick ascending limb and sense tubular sodium chloride. They signal the juxtaglomerular cells but do not secrete renin.","Intraglomerular mesangial cells support the capillary loops, contract, and phagocytose trapped proteins. They are not the main source of renin."],"study":{"topics":["Juxtaglomerular apparatus","Renin release"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-7"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C39","system":"UR","dx":"Fibromuscular dysplasia with renal artery stenosis","codes":["UR-12-F3"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician for follow-up of high blood pressure first noted 4 months ago. She takes no medications and has no family history of hypertension. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse is 78/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 174/106 mm Hg. An epigastric bruit with systolic and diastolic components is heard. Serum potassium is 3.2 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.0), bicarbonate is 30 mEq/L (22 to 28), and creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1). Plasma renin activity and aldosterone are both increased. CT angiography shows alternating narrowing and dilation of the middle and distal right main renal artery. The right kidney measures 9.0 cm, and the left kidney measures 11.5 cm. Diagnosis: fibromuscular dysplasia with right renal artery stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C39-Q1","stem":"An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor is started. Two weeks later, renal scintigraphy shows a sharp fall in the GFR of the right kidney. Which of the following changes in the right kidney best explains this?","options":["Efferent constriction, raising filtration fraction","Afferent constriction, lowering renal plasma flow","Efferent dilation, lowering glomerular pressure","Afferent dilation, raising glomerular pressure"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the setting: low perfusion pressure beyond the stenosis.","Recall how the kidney protects GFR there: angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole and keeps glomerular pressure up.","Predict the drug effect: removing angiotensin II dilates the efferent arteriole, glomerular pressure drops, and GFR falls."],"why":"Beyond a stenosis, pressure in the afferent arteriole is low. Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole more than the afferent one, which raises glomerular capillary pressure and keeps GFR up while plasma flow falls, so filtration fraction rises. An ACE inhibitor removes this support, so the efferent arteriole dilates, glomerular pressure falls, and GFR in the affected kidney drops. This is the basis of the fall in GFR seen with ACE inhibitors in renal artery stenosis.","whyNot":["Efferent constriction is what angiotensin II does. The drug removes this effect, so GFR falls.","ACE inhibitors do not constrict the afferent arteriole. Afferent constriction is caused by NSAIDs, sympathetic activity, or the myogenic response.","Correct.","Afferent dilation would raise glomerular pressure and GFR, the opposite of what happened."],"study":{"topics":["Angiotensin II and the efferent arteriole","GFR in renal artery stenosis"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-12-F3"]}},{"id":"UR-C39-Q2","stem":"Which of the following histologic changes most likely underlies the angiographic appearance of her renal artery?","options":["Granulomatous panarteritis with multinucleated giant cells","Medial fibroplasia alternating with thinned arterial wall","Lipid-rich intimal plaque at the arterial ostium","Concentric medial calcification without stenosis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-F3","obj":"UR-12-F.3","dok":3,"approach":["Read the clues: a young woman, a beaded middle to distal renal artery, and renin-driven hypertension.","Separate the two main causes of renal artery stenosis: atherosclerosis at the ostium in older adults, and fibromuscular dysplasia farther along the artery in young women.","Recall the histology of the common form of fibromuscular dysplasia: ridges of thickened fibromuscular media alternate with thin, dilated segments."],"why":"Fibromuscular dysplasia is a noninflammatory, nonatherosclerotic disease of medium arteries, most often the renal and carotid arteries of young to middle-aged women. In the most common form, medial fibroplasia, thickened ridges of fibrous tissue and smooth muscle alternate with thinned segments that bulge outward, giving a string-of-beads appearance in the middle and distal renal artery. The stenosis lowers pressure at the juxtaglomerular cells, renin rises, and secondary hyperaldosteronism causes hypokalemia. The underperfused kidney gradually shrinks, and turbulent flow produces a bruit.","whyNot":["Granulomatous panarteritis with giant cells is Takayasu or giant cell arteritis. It causes long, smooth narrowing and systemic inflammation rather than beading.","Correct.","An ostial lipid plaque is atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis, seen in older adults with vascular risk factors. It narrows the origin of the artery without beading.","Medial calcification without stenosis is Monckeberg sclerosis. It does not narrow the lumen or cause hypertension."],"study":{"topics":["Fibromuscular dysplasia","Causes of renal artery stenosis"],"comps":["UR-12-F3","UR-7"]}},{"id":"UR-C39-Q3","stem":"Reduced flow through her right renal artery lowers perfusion of the renal medulla as well as the cortex. Most blood reaching the medulla arrives through which of the following vessels?","options":["Arcuate arteries entering the renal pyramids directly","Peritubular capillaries of superficial cortical nephrons","Afferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons","Vasa recta from juxtamedullary efferent arterioles"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that most medullary blood has already passed through a glomerulus.","Identify which glomeruli lie next to the medulla: those of juxtamedullary nephrons.","Their efferent arterioles descend as the vasa recta, which supply the medulla."],"why":"The medulla receives blood mainly from the efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary glomeruli. These efferent arterioles dip into the medulla as long, hairpin vessels called the vasa recta, which run beside the loops of Henle and then drain back to arcuate and interlobar veins. Because this blood has already passed through two capillary beds and flows slowly, medullary oxygen tension is low. That slow countercurrent flow also helps preserve the concentration gradient.","whyNot":["Arcuate arteries run along the corticomedullary junction and give off cortical radiate arteries. The medulla is supplied mainly after blood passes through juxtamedullary glomeruli.","Efferent arterioles of superficial cortical nephrons form peritubular capillaries around cortical tubules. They do not supply the medulla.","Afferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons feed their glomeruli. The blood reaches the medulla only after leaving the glomerulus through the efferent arteriole.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Vasa recta","Medullary blood supply"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-6"]}},{"id":"UR-C39-Q4","stem":"Her hypokalemia results from secondary hyperaldosteronism. Which of the following best describes how aldosterone acts on its target cells in the collecting duct?","options":["Binds a cytosolic receptor that increases ENaC synthesis","Binds a V2 receptor and inserts aquaporin-2 channels","Binds a membrane receptor and increases distal calcium reabsorption","Binds a cytosolic receptor that increases NKCC2 synthesis"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the hormone type: aldosterone is a steroid, so it acts through an intracellular receptor that changes gene expression.","Identify the target: principal cells of the collecting duct.","Recall the products: more apical ENaC channels and more basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase, which drive sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion."],"why":"Aldosterone is a steroid that enters principal cells and binds the cytosolic mineralocorticoid receptor. The complex moves to the nucleus and increases synthesis of apical ENaC channels and basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps. More sodium is reabsorbed, the lumen becomes more negative, and more potassium is secreted through apical channels. In renal artery stenosis, high renin drives this process, which explains her hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","ADH binds the V2 receptor, a membrane receptor that raises cAMP and inserts aquaporin-2 into the apical membrane. That changes water reabsorption, not potassium secretion.","PTH acts on a membrane receptor in the distal convoluted tubule to increase calcium reabsorption. It is a peptide hormone with a different target.","NKCC2 is the cotransporter of the thick ascending limb, which loop diuretics block. Aldosterone's main sodium effect is on ENaC in the collecting duct."],"study":{"topics":["Aldosterone action on principal cells","Hormones acting on the distal nephron"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-7"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C40","system":"UR","dx":"Embolic renal infarction from atrial fibrillation","codes":["UR-12-F4"],"case":"A 74-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 3 hours after the sudden onset of severe, constant left flank pain with nausea. He has atrial fibrillation and stopped taking warfarin 2 months ago. Temperature is 37.6 °C (99.7 °F), pulse is 118/min and irregularly irregular, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 158/92 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows 2+ blood with 10 to 20 RBC/hpf and no crystals. Serum lactate dehydrogenase is 920 U/L (100 to 250), and serum creatinine is 1.4 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Contrast-enhanced CT shows a wedge-shaped, nonenhancing region in the upper pole of the left kidney, with its broad base at the capsule. Diagnosis: embolic renal infarction due to atrial fibrillation.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C40-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the shape of the affected region?","options":["Venous outflow from the upper pole is obstructed","Cortical capillaries have the lowest oxygen tension","Interlobar arteries lack functional anastomoses","Collateral flow from capsular arteries spares the medulla"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.2","dok":2,"approach":["Describe the lesion: a wedge with its apex toward the hilum and its base at the capsule.","Recall the arterial pattern: segmental and interlobar arteries branch outward like a tree and do not connect with their neighbors.","Block one branch and the whole fan-shaped territory it supplies dies, giving a wedge."],"why":"The segmental and interlobar arteries of the kidney are end arteries, with no meaningful anastomoses between neighboring branches. Each artery supplies a territory that widens as it branches toward the cortex. When an embolus blocks one, the whole territory dies, producing a wedge with its apex at the occlusion and its base at the capsule. A thin rim of outer cortex can survive on capsular collaterals.","whyNot":["Venous occlusion, as in renal vein thrombosis, causes a swollen, congested kidney. It does not produce a sharply wedge-shaped arterial infarct.","Oxygen tension is lowest in the medulla, not the cortex. Low oxygen tension would not explain a wedge-shaped zone.","Correct.","Capsular collaterals preserve a thin rim of outer cortex, not the medulla. The medulla lies inside the infarct, toward its apex."],"study":{"topics":["Renal end arteries","Wedge-shaped infarcts"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-12-F4"]}},{"id":"UR-C40-Q2","stem":"The upper pole of his left kidney lies at about the level of the T11 to T12 vertebrae. Which of the following structures lies directly posterior to this part of the kidney?","options":["Diaphragm","Psoas major muscle","Transversus abdominis aponeurosis","Quadratus lumborum muscle"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.1","dok":2,"approach":["Place the kidney: retroperitoneal, from about T12 to L3, with the left slightly higher than the right.","Recall the posterior relations from top to bottom: diaphragm and lower ribs above; psoas major, quadratus lumborum, and transversus abdominis below.","The upper pole rests on the diaphragm, with the pleural recess behind it."],"why":"The kidneys lie in the retroperitoneum from about T12 to L3. The right kidney sits a little lower because the liver lies above it, so the left upper pole reaches about the 11th rib. Behind the upper pole lies the diaphragm, which separates it from the costodiaphragmatic recess of the pleura and the lower ribs. Lower down, the kidney rests on the psoas major medially, the quadratus lumborum, and the transversus abdominis aponeurosis laterally.","whyNot":["Correct.","The psoas major lies behind the medial part of the lower kidney. It is too low and too medial to sit behind the upper pole.","The transversus abdominis aponeurosis lies behind the lateral border of the lower kidney.","The quadratus lumborum lies behind the middle and lower parts of the kidney, below the diaphragm."],"study":{"topics":["Position of the kidneys","Posterior relations of the kidney"],"comps":["UR-3","UR-4"]}},{"id":"UR-C40-Q3","stem":"If the infarcted tissue were examined 3 days after the event, which of the following findings would most likely be seen?","options":["Liquefactive necrosis forming a fluid-filled cavity in the cortex","Red hemorrhagic necrosis from dual blood supply","Caseous necrosis with surrounding granulomas","Pale coagulative necrosis with ghost cell outlines"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-F4","obj":"UR-12-F.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the tissue and the cause: a solid organ with end arteries, blocked by an embolus.","Recall the infarct type: arterial occlusion in a solid organ gives a pale (white) infarct.","Recall the necrosis pattern for ischemia outside the brain: coagulative, with cell outlines preserved for days."],"why":"Arterial occlusion in a solid organ with end arteries, such as the kidney, heart, or spleen, produces a pale infarct because little blood seeps into the dead tissue. Ischemia denatures structural proteins and enzymes, so the cells die but keep their outlines as ghost cells for several days. A rim of hyperemia and inflammation forms at the edge of the wedge. Over weeks the area is replaced by a depressed fibrous scar.","whyNot":["Liquefactive necrosis is the pattern of ischemia in the brain and of abscesses. Ischemic kidney tissue undergoes coagulative necrosis.","Red infarcts occur in tissues with a dual blood supply or loose structure, such as the lung and small bowel, or after venous occlusion or reperfusion. The kidney has end arteries.","Caseous necrosis with granulomas is the pattern of tuberculosis and some fungal infections. It is not caused by ischemia.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pale versus red infarcts","Coagulative necrosis"],"comps":["UR-12-F4","UR-5"]}},{"id":"UR-C40-Q4","stem":"Three months later, his serum creatinine is stable at 1.5 mg/dL. A 24-hour urine collection has a volume of 1,440 mL and a creatinine concentration of 90 mg/dL. Which of the following best describes his creatinine clearance and its relation to his true GFR?","options":["About 60 mL/min, slightly below true GFR","About 60 mL/min, slightly above true GFR","About 90 mL/min, slightly above true GFR","About 135 mL/min, slightly above true GFR"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Convert urine flow: 1,440 mL over 1,440 minutes is 1.0 mL/min.","Apply clearance = (U x V) / P: (90 mg/dL x 1.0 mL/min) / 1.5 mg/dL = 60 mL/min.","Recall that creatinine is filtered and also slightly secreted, so its clearance runs a little above the true GFR."],"why":"Clearance is the volume of plasma cleared of a substance each minute, equal to urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration. Here that is 90 x 1.0 / 1.5, or 60 mL/min. Inulin is filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals GFR exactly. Creatinine is filtered and also secreted in small amounts by the proximal tubule, so creatinine clearance overestimates GFR by roughly 10 to 20 percent, more so as kidney function falls.","whyNot":["The value is right, but creatinine secretion makes its clearance higher than GFR, not lower. A clearance below GFR means net reabsorption, as with urea.","Correct.","90 mL/min comes from multiplying urine concentration by urine flow without dividing by plasma creatinine.","135 mL/min comes from multiplying by plasma creatinine instead of dividing by it."],"study":{"topics":["Clearance calculation","Creatinine clearance versus GFR"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-12-A4"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C41","system":"UR","dx":"Sickle cell trait with renal papillary necrosis","codes":["UR-12-F5"],"case":"A 22-year-old man comes to the physician because of 2 days of painless red urine. He plays college basketball and has had no trauma. He takes no regular medications and does not use analgesics. Temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pulse 72/min, respirations 14/min, and blood pressure 118/74 mm Hg. Hemoglobin is 14.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Urinalysis shows many red blood cells and no casts, and urine culture shows no growth. After 12 hours of fluid restriction, urine osmolality is 310 mOsm/kg (expected above 800). Hemoglobin electrophoresis shows HbA 58% and HbS 40%. CT urography shows a sloughed left renal papilla. Diagnosis: sickle cell trait with renal papillary necrosis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C41-Q1","stem":"His concentrating defect reflects injury to the nephrons whose loops of Henle reach the inner medulla, and to the capillaries that run beside those loops. Which of the following features characterizes these nephrons?","options":["Glomeruli in the outer cortex and efferent arterioles forming vasa recta","Glomeruli near the corticomedullary junction and short loops ending in the outer medulla","Glomeruli in the outer cortex and long loops reaching the tips of the papillae","Glomeruli near the corticomedullary junction and efferent arterioles forming vasa recta"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.4","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the nephrons with loops that reach the inner medulla: juxtamedullary nephrons.","Recall their three features: deep glomeruli, long loops, and efferent arterioles that form the vasa recta.","Link to the case: sickling in the vasa recta damages the countercurrent system these nephrons build, so urine cannot be concentrated."],"why":"Juxtamedullary nephrons make up a minority of nephrons. Their glomeruli sit near the corticomedullary junction, their loops of Henle dip deep into the inner medulla, and their efferent arterioles give rise to the vasa recta, which run alongside the loops. The long loops build the steep medullary osmotic gradient, and the vasa recta preserve it by countercurrent exchange. When sickled cells block the vasa recta, the gradient is lost and urine osmolality stays near that of plasma (isosthenuria).","whyNot":["Outer cortical (superficial) nephrons have efferent arterioles that form peritubular capillaries around cortical tubules. They do not give rise to the vasa recta.","Short loops that turn back in the outer medulla belong to cortical nephrons. Juxtamedullary nephrons have the long loops.","Outer cortical nephrons have short loops. The long loops that reach the papillary tips come from glomeruli deep in the cortex.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cortical versus juxtamedullary nephrons","Vasa recta and countercurrent exchange"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-6"]}},{"id":"UR-C41-Q2","stem":"Chronic analgesic nephropathy produces the same papillary lesion. In that disorder, which of the following best explains why the injury centers on the renal papillae?","options":["Immune complexes deposited along the papillary basement membrane","Hypertonic medullary blood promoting polymerization of hemoglobin S","Concentrated toxic metabolites with loss of prostaglandin vasodilation","Drug-specific T cells infiltrating the interstitium along with eosinophils"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-D1","obj":"UR-12-D.2","dok":3,"approach":["Name the two parts of classic combination analgesics: phenacetin (or acetaminophen) and aspirin.","Ask why the papilla is vulnerable: drugs are concentrated there, and its blood supply is already marginal.","Combine them: toxic metabolites concentrated in the papilla plus loss of prostaglandin-driven blood flow cause ischemic and oxidative necrosis."],"why":"The countercurrent system concentrates acetaminophen, the main metabolite of phenacetin, in the inner medulla, where its reactive products deplete glutathione and cause oxidative cell injury. Aspirin and other NSAIDs block prostaglandin synthesis, and the medulla depends on prostaglandins to keep its low blood flow adequate. Together these cause papillary necrosis and chronic interstitial nephritis after years of combination analgesic use. Other causes of papillary necrosis include diabetes mellitus, sickle cell disease and trait, severe acute pyelonephritis, and urinary tract obstruction.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition injures glomeruli and causes glomerulonephritis. It does not explain selective papillary necrosis.","This is the mechanism in this patient. Analgesic nephropathy occurs in people without hemoglobin S.","Correct.","This describes acute drug-induced interstitial nephritis, a hypersensitivity reaction with fever, rash, and acute kidney injury. It is not the chronic papillary injury of analgesic abuse."],"study":{"topics":["Analgesic nephropathy","Causes of papillary necrosis"],"comps":["UR-12-D1","UR-12-F5"]}},{"id":"UR-C41-Q3","stem":"His hemoglobin concentration is normal. If repeated injury later progresses to chronic kidney disease with widespread scarring, his anemia would develop mainly from loss of which of the following cells?","options":["Peritubular interstitial fibroblasts that sense low oxygen","Juxtaglomerular cells that secrete renin into afferent arterioles","Proximal tubule cells that hydroxylate calcidiol to calcitriol","Macula densa cells that sense sodium chloride in the tubule"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-8","obj":"UR-8.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the hormone whose loss causes anemia in CKD: erythropoietin.","Recall where it is made: fibroblast-like interstitial cells around the peritubular capillaries of the deep cortex and outer medulla.","Recall the trigger: low tissue oxygen stabilizes HIF, which switches on the erythropoietin gene."],"why":"Erythropoietin is made by peritubular interstitial fibroblast-like cells in the deep cortex and outer medulla. When oxygen delivery falls, hypoxia-inducible factor is no longer degraded and drives erythropoietin transcription. Erythropoietin keeps erythroid progenitors in the marrow alive and dividing. As CKD destroys these cells, erythropoietin falls and a normocytic, normochromic anemia develops. The opposite happens when a renal cell carcinoma makes erythropoietin, causing secondary polycythemia.","whyNot":["Correct.","Juxtaglomerular cells release renin. Their loss or dysfunction affects blood pressure and aldosterone, not red cell production.","Loss of 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity in CKD causes low calcitriol, hypocalcemia, and secondary hyperparathyroidism. It is not the main cause of CKD anemia.","The macula densa senses tubular NaCl for tubuloglomerular feedback and renin release. It does not make erythropoietin."],"study":{"topics":["Erythropoietin production","Anemia of chronic kidney disease"],"comps":["UR-8","UR-12-F5"]}},{"id":"UR-C41-Q4","stem":"Which of the following features of the renal medulla most directly explains why red cells sickle there even though he has only about 40% hemoglobin S?","options":["Glomerular fenestrae that trap deformed red cells in Bowman space","Rapid blood flow with high shear stress that fragments rigid red blood cells","High oxygen tension that oxidizes heme iron from the ferrous to ferric form","Low oxygen, high osmolality, and low pH that favor HbS polymerization"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-12-F5","obj":"UR-12-F.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what makes HbS polymerize: deoxygenation, a higher HbS concentration inside the cell, and acidosis.","Match each to the medulla: countercurrent shunting lowers oxygen, the hypertonic interstitium dehydrates red cells, and the pH is low.","Predict the result: sickling in the vasa recta causes papillary ischemia, hematuria, and loss of concentrating ability."],"why":"The valine substitution in HbS creates a hydrophobic patch that lets deoxygenated HbS molecules polymerize into rigid fibers. The renal medulla is the one site where conditions favor this even in sickle cell trait. Oxygen tension is low because oxygen diffuses across the hairpin vasa recta, the hypertonic interstitium pulls water out of red cells and raises their HbS concentration, and the acidic pH lowers hemoglobin oxygen affinity. Slow flow keeps cells in this setting longer. Sickled cells occlude the vasa recta, causing hematuria, papillary necrosis, and isosthenuria.","whyNot":["The papillae have no glomeruli. Red cells in the urine here come from infarcted papillae, not from trapping in Bowman space.","Blood flow in the vasa recta is slow, not rapid. Shear fragmentation of red cells produces schistocytes in microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.","The medulla has low oxygen tension. Oxidation of heme iron to the ferric state produces methemoglobin, which is unrelated to sickling.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Sickle cell nephropathy","Hemoglobin S polymerization"],"comps":["UR-12-F5","UR-2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C42","system":"UR","dx":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma invading the left renal vein","codes":["UR-12-G1"],"case":"A 61-year-old man comes to the physician because of 3 weeks of intermittent hematuria, a dull left flank ache, and a new left scrotal swelling. He has a 40-pack-year smoking history, hypertension, and obesity. Temperature is 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pulse 84/min, respirations 16/min, and blood pressure 152/94 mm Hg. A soft mass of dilated veins is palpable above the left testis and does not decompress when he lies supine. Hemoglobin is 18.4 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5), serum calcium is 12.1 mg/dL (8.5 to 10.5), and serum parathyroid hormone is 6 pg/mL (10 to 65). CT shows an 8-cm left renal mass with tumor thrombus extending into the left renal vein. Skeletal imaging shows no bone lesions. Nephrectomy shows a golden-yellow tumor composed of clear cells. Diagnosis: clear cell renal cell carcinoma invading the left renal vein.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C42-Q1","stem":"Tumor sequencing shows inactivation of both VHL alleles. Which of the following is the most direct consequence of VHL loss in the tumor cells?","options":["Loss of tuberin restraint on mTOR growth signaling","Accumulation of HIF that increases VEGF transcription","Failure of WT1 to direct normal nephron differentiation","Release of E2F from retinoblastoma protein control"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what VHL protein does: it is part of an E3 ubiquitin ligase that marks HIF-alpha for destruction when oxygen is normal.","Predict the effect of losing it: HIF builds up as if the cell were hypoxic.","Name the target genes: VEGF (angiogenesis), plus PDGF, TGF-alpha, and erythropoietin."],"why":"VHL is a tumor suppressor on chromosome 3p. In normal oxygen, HIF-alpha is hydroxylated and the VHL ubiquitin ligase complex sends it to the proteasome. When both VHL copies are lost (often by 3p deletion plus a mutation), HIF accumulates and drives transcription of hypoxia genes, most importantly VEGF. This explains why clear cell carcinomas are so vascular. Overproduction of erythropoietin by the same pathway can explain his polycythemia.","whyNot":["Tuberin is the TSC2 gene product. Its loss activates mTOR and causes angiomyolipomas in tuberous sclerosis.","Correct.","WT1 loss causes Wilms tumor in children, a tumor of persistent metanephric blastema.","Release of E2F follows loss or inactivation of RB, as in retinoblastoma and osteosarcoma."],"study":{"topics":["VHL and HIF signaling","Molecular basis of clear cell carcinoma"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-G1"]}},{"id":"UR-C42-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains his scrotal finding?","options":["Blocked testicular lymph drainage to the para-aortic lymph nodes","Blocked left testicular vein drainage into the left renal vein","Blocked left testicular vein drainage into the left internal iliac vein","Blocked left testicular vein drainage into the inferior vena cava"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the finding: a left varicocele, a dilated pampiniform plexus, that does not empty when supine.","Trace venous drainage: the left testicular vein empties into the left renal vein, which passes between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta.","Apply to the case: tumor thrombus in the left renal vein raises pressure in the testicular vein and the plexus dilates."],"why":"The left testicular (gonadal) vein drains into the left renal vein, which passes between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery to reach the inferior vena cava. Tumor thrombus in the left renal vein raises back pressure in the left testicular vein and dilates the pampiniform plexus. A varicocele that appears suddenly in an older man, or that fails to decompress lying down, suggests venous obstruction by a retroperitoneal or renal mass. Clear cell carcinoma is known for growing into the renal vein.","whyNot":["Testicular lymph does drain to para-aortic nodes, but lymphatic obstruction causes scrotal edema or hydrocele, not dilated veins.","Correct.","The testicular veins do not drain into the internal iliac veins. They ascend through the inguinal canal into the abdomen.","The right testicular vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava. The left does not."],"study":{"topics":["Left renal vein anatomy","Varicocele from renal cell carcinoma"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-12-G1"]}},{"id":"UR-C42-Q3","stem":"If the tumor had instead been composed of papillae with foamy macrophages in their fibrovascular cores, which of the following cytogenetic findings would most likely be present?","options":["Deletion of the short arm of chromosome 3","Deletion of chromosome 11p13 including WT1","Trisomy of chromosomes 7 and 17","Hypodiploidy with loss of multiple whole chromosomes"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-G1","obj":"UR-12-G.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the tumor from the histology: papillae with foamy macrophages means papillary renal cell carcinoma.","Recall its typical cytogenetics: trisomy 7 and 17 (and loss of Y in men).","Contrast the other subtypes: clear cell with 3p loss, chromophobe with hypodiploidy."],"why":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma is the second most common type. It grows as papillae with fibrovascular cores that often contain foamy macrophages and psammoma bodies, and it is more often multifocal than clear cell carcinoma. Its typical cytogenetic changes are trisomies of chromosomes 7 and 17, and hereditary cases carry activating MET mutations. Clear cell carcinoma shows 3p loss, and chromophobe carcinoma shows loss of whole chromosomes.","whyNot":["Loss of 3p with VHL inactivation is typical of clear cell carcinoma, the tumor in this patient.","11p13 deletion including WT1 is the lesion of Wilms tumor in WAGR syndrome.","Correct.","Extreme hypodiploidy with loss of whole chromosomes characterizes chromophobe carcinoma, which arises from intercalated cells and has pale cells with perinuclear halos."],"study":{"topics":["Subtypes of renal cell carcinoma","Papillary renal cell carcinoma"],"comps":["UR-12-G1","UR-9"]}},{"id":"UR-C42-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains his serum calcium concentration?","options":["Tumor secretion of parathyroid hormone-related protein","Lytic bone metastases that release calcium from the skeleton","Tumor conversion of calcidiol to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","Autonomous hormone secretion from a parathyroid adenoma"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-G1","obj":"UR-12-G.2","dok":3,"approach":["Read the PTH: it is low, so the hypercalcemia does not come from the parathyroid glands.","Rule out local bone destruction: skeletal imaging shows no bone lesions.","Conclude: humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy from PTHrP, a classic paraneoplastic product of renal cell carcinoma."],"why":"Hypercalcemia with suppressed PTH and no bone metastases points to a humoral cause. Renal cell carcinoma can secrete PTH-related protein, which binds the PTH receptor in bone and kidney, increasing bone resorption and renal calcium reabsorption. Standard PTH assays do not detect PTHrP, and the high calcium suppresses native PTH. Renal cell carcinoma also causes polycythemia (erythropoietin) and hypertension (renin), and it spreads by blood to lung and bone. The classic presentation is hematuria, flank pain, and a flank mass.","whyNot":["Correct.","Renal cell carcinoma does produce lytic bone metastases that can raise calcium, but his skeletal imaging shows no bone lesions.","Excess 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D production explains hypercalcemia in lymphomas and granulomatous diseases such as sarcoidosis, not renal cell carcinoma.","A parathyroid adenoma would raise serum PTH. His PTH is suppressed, which points away from primary hyperparathyroidism."],"study":{"topics":["Paraneoplastic syndromes of renal cell carcinoma","Humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy"],"comps":["UR-12-G1","UR-8"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C43","system":"UR","dx":"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome with bilateral renal cell carcinoma","codes":["UR-12-G1"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician for evaluation of kidney masses found on imaging for abdominal pain. Five years ago, a cerebellar hemangioblastoma was resected. Her father died of kidney cancer at age 44. Temperature is 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pulse 76/min, respirations 14/min, and blood pressure 124/78 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (0.6 to 1.1). MRI shows three solid enhancing masses in the right kidney and two in the left, multiple bilateral renal cysts, and several pancreatic cysts. Partial nephrectomy specimens show clear cell renal cell carcinoma. Germline testing identifies a pathogenic VHL variant. Diagnosis: von Hippel-Lindau syndrome with bilateral renal cell carcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C43-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains why she developed multiple tumors in both kidneys at a young age?","options":["Germline loss of one VHL allele, so each tumor needs one more hit","Germline gain-of-function VHL allele acting as a dominant oncogene","Germline loss of both VHL alleles present in every cell from birth","Germline mismatch repair defect that raises the somatic mutation rate"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.3","dok":3,"approach":["Apply the two-hit model: a tumor suppressor must lose both copies before a cell becomes a tumor.","In the inherited syndrome, every renal tubular cell already carries the first hit.","Any one of millions of cells needs only one somatic hit (often a 3p deletion), so tumors are multiple, bilateral, and early."],"why":"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome is autosomal dominant, but at the cellular level the gene acts recessively. Each cell in the body carries one inactive VHL allele from conception. A single somatic event, often deletion of 3p, removes the remaining copy and stabilizes HIF. Because this can happen in many cells, patients develop bilateral, multifocal clear cell carcinomas decades earlier than people with sporadic tumors, who need two somatic hits in the same cell. Hemangioblastomas, pheochromocytomas, and pancreatic cysts arise the same way.","whyNot":["Correct.","VHL is a tumor suppressor. Tumors arise from loss of its function, not from a gain of function.","Affected people inherit one mutant allele. Their normal cells keep one working copy and make normal VHL protein until a second hit occurs.","Inherited mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, with colon, endometrial, and upper urinary tract urothelial cancers, not VHL-type clear cell tumors."],"study":{"topics":["Two-hit hypothesis","Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-G1"]}},{"id":"UR-C43-Q2","stem":"Immunostaining shows that many of her tumor cells have lost surface MHC class I molecules. These tumor cells are most likely to be killed by which of the following?","options":["Eosinophils releasing major basic protein","Natural killer cells","CD4+ T helper 1 lymphocytes","CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.6","dok":2,"approach":["Note the escape strategy: lost MHC class I hides tumor antigens from CD8+ T cells.","Recall which cell watches for missing MHC class I: the NK cell, whose inhibitory receptors bind MHC class I.","Without that inhibitory signal, activating receptors that bind stress ligands trigger killing."],"why":"CD8+ T cells kill tumor cells by recognizing tumor peptides on MHC class I, so many renal carcinomas downregulate MHC class I to escape them. NK cells cover this gap. Their inhibitory receptors bind self MHC class I, and when MHC class I is missing, signals from activating receptors (such as NKG2D binding stress-induced ligands) dominate. The NK cell then releases perforin and granzymes to kill the target. This is called missing-self recognition.","whyNot":["Eosinophils mainly defend against helminths and drive allergic inflammation. They are not a main defense against tumor cells.","Correct.","CD4+ T cells recognize peptides on MHC class II, mainly on antigen-presenting cells. They help other cells rather than directly killing MHC class I-negative targets.","CD8+ T cells recognize tumor peptides only when they are presented on MHC class I. Losing MHC class I is a way tumors escape these cells."],"study":{"topics":["NK cell missing-self recognition","Tumor immune escape"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-G1"]}},{"id":"UR-C43-Q3","stem":"Her kidneys contain many cysts as well as carcinomas. Which of the following other cystic kidney conditions also carries a well-established increased risk of renal cell carcinoma?","options":["Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease of infancy","Unilateral multicystic dysplastic kidney of the newborn","Acquired cystic disease of long-term dialysis","Medullary sponge kidney with collecting duct ectasia"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-E2","obj":"UR-12-E.3","dok":2,"approach":["List the cystic kidney conditions and the setting of each: infancy, congenital malformation, or acquired.","Recall which one develops after years of end-stage kidney disease: acquired cystic disease in dialysis patients.","Recall its key complication: a several-fold increase in renal cell carcinoma."],"why":"Acquired cystic kidney disease develops in shrunken end-stage kidneys after years of dialysis. Multiple cortical and medullary cysts form, some with atypical lining cells, and the risk of renal cell carcinoma rises several-fold. ARPKD presents in infancy with large spongy kidneys, oligohydramnios, and hepatic fibrosis. Multicystic dysplastic kidney is a noninherited malformation of one kidney with disorganized tissue and cartilage.","whyNot":["ARPKD causes bilateral enlarged kidneys in infancy, with Potter sequence and congenital hepatic fibrosis. Kidney cancer is not one of its typical outcomes.","Multicystic dysplastic kidney is a noninherited, usually unilateral, developmental malformation. It does not carry a meaningful carcinoma risk, and the other kidney often enlarges to compensate.","Correct.","Medullary sponge kidney has dilated medullary collecting ducts that predispose to stones and nephrocalcinosis, not to carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Cystic kidney diseases","Acquired cystic disease of dialysis"],"comps":["UR-12-E2","UR-12-G1"]}},{"id":"UR-C43-Q4","stem":"The resected tumors have a golden-yellow cut surface. Which of the following best explains this gross appearance?","options":["Mitochondria-packed cells derived from intercalated cells","Hemosiderin-laden cells derived from medullary collecting ducts","Lipid-rich cytoplasm in cells derived from proximal tubules","Mucin-filled cells derived from the transitional epithelium"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-G1","obj":"UR-12-G.1","dok":2,"approach":["Connect the color to cell contents: yellow means lipid.","Connect the clear cytoplasm to lipid and glycogen that dissolve during tissue processing.","Recall the cell of origin of clear cell carcinoma: the proximal tubule."],"why":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma arises from proximal tubule epithelium. Its cells store large amounts of lipid and glycogen, which dissolve out during processing and leave clear cytoplasm on slides. The lipid gives the cut surface its golden-yellow color, often mixed with red hemorrhage and gray necrosis. These tumors tend to grow into the renal vein and inferior vena cava. Risk factors include smoking, obesity, hypertension, acquired cystic disease, and VHL syndrome.","whyNot":["Mitochondria-packed cells from intercalated cells form oncocytoma, which has a mahogany-brown cut surface.","Hemosiderin causes brown discoloration in areas of old hemorrhage. Clear cell carcinoma does not arise from medullary collecting ducts.","Correct.","Mucin-producing glandular tumors of the bladder are adenocarcinomas. Tumors of transitional epithelium are urothelial carcinomas, not yellow renal masses."],"study":{"topics":["Gross and microscopic features of clear cell carcinoma"],"comps":["UR-12-G1","UR-2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C44","system":"UR","dx":"Wilms tumor in WAGR syndrome","codes":["UR-12-G2"],"case":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the physician because his mother felt a firm mass in his right abdomen while bathing him. He has had absent irises in both eyes since birth, had surgical repair of hypospadias and an undescended testis, and has delayed speech and motor milestones. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse 110/min, respirations 24/min, and blood pressure 128/84 mm Hg (above the 95th percentile for age and height). A smooth, nontender right flank mass does not cross the midline. Urinalysis shows 15 RBC/hpf (0 to 2). Urine catecholamine metabolites are within normal limits. CT shows a large solid mass arising within the right kidney. Diagnosis: Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma) in WAGR syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C44-Q1","stem":"Which of the following histologic patterns is most likely in the resected tumor?","options":["Spindle cells, mature fat, and thick-walled vessels","Small round blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes","Clear cells filled with lipid and glycogen","Blastemal, stromal, and epithelial elements"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-G2","obj":"UR-12-G.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the tumor: an intrarenal mass in a 3-year-old with WAGR features is Wilms tumor.","Recall that Wilms tumor recapitulates the developing kidney.","Name its three components: blastema, stroma, and epithelium (tubules and abortive glomeruli)."],"why":"Wilms tumor is the most common primary renal tumor of childhood, usually seen at 2 to 5 years of age as a large unilateral flank mass, sometimes with hematuria or hypertension. It arises from persistent metanephric tissue and shows a triphasic pattern: sheets of small blue blastemal cells, spindle-cell stroma, and epithelial tubules or glomerulus-like structures. Syndromes that raise the risk include WAGR, Denys-Drash, and Beckwith-Wiedemann.","whyNot":["Spindle cells, fat, and thick-walled vessels describe angiomyolipoma, which is linked to tuberous sclerosis.","Small round blue cells in Homer Wright rosettes describe neuroblastoma, a tumor of the adrenal medulla or sympathetic ganglia that often crosses the midline and raises urine catecholamine metabolites.","Clear cells filled with lipid and glycogen describe clear cell renal cell carcinoma, a tumor of older adults.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Wilms tumor histology","Pediatric abdominal masses"],"comps":["UR-12-G2","UR-1"]}},{"id":"UR-C44-Q2","stem":"Which of the following genetic findings best explains the combination of features in this child?","options":["Loss of imprinting at 11p15 with excess IGF2 expression","Deletion of 11p13 removing WT1 and PAX6","Dominant-negative point mutation in one WT1 allele","Two-hit inactivation of VHL on chromosome 3p"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.4","dok":3,"approach":["List his features: Wilms tumor, aniridia, genitourinary anomalies, and developmental delay (WAGR).","Ask what single event could cause all of them: loss of several neighboring genes.","Recall the region: 11p13, which holds both WT1 (kidney and gonad) and PAX6 (eye)."],"why":"WAGR syndrome is a contiguous gene deletion at chromosome 11p13. Loss of PAX6 causes aniridia, and loss of WT1 causes genitourinary malformations and a high risk of Wilms tumor once the second WT1 copy is lost in a kidney cell. Additional deleted genes contribute to developmental delay. WT1 encodes a transcription factor needed for normal kidney and gonad development.","whyNot":["Loss of imprinting at 11p15 causes Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, with organomegaly, macroglossia, hemihypertrophy, and omphalocele, not aniridia.","Correct.","A dominant-negative WT1 mutation causes Denys-Drash syndrome, with early nephropathy (diffuse mesangial sclerosis) and gonadal dysgenesis, but not aniridia or developmental delay.","VHL loss causes clear cell renal carcinoma and hemangioblastomas, not childhood Wilms tumor."],"study":{"topics":["WAGR syndrome","WT1 and Wilms tumor genetics"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-G2"]}},{"id":"UR-C44-Q3","stem":"His hypertension is attributed to renin released by the tumor. Which of the following sets of plasma findings is most likely?","options":["High renin, high aldosterone, and low potassium","Low renin, high aldosterone, and low potassium","Low renin, low aldosterone, and high potassium","High renin, low aldosterone, and high potassium"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-7","obj":"UR-7.6","dok":3,"approach":["Start at renin: tumor renin raises it regardless of normal feedback.","Follow the cascade: renin forms angiotensin I, ACE converts it to angiotensin II, and angiotensin II stimulates aldosterone.","Predict aldosterone's effect on principal cells: more sodium reabsorption and more potassium secretion, so potassium falls."],"why":"Juxtaglomerular cells normally release renin in response to low afferent arteriolar pressure, low NaCl delivery to the macula densa, and beta-1 sympathetic stimulation. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, and ACE converts it to angiotensin II, which constricts arterioles and stimulates aldosterone release from the zona glomerulosa. Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion in the collecting duct. Atrial natriuretic peptide opposes these effects by inhibiting renin and aldosterone and promoting natriuresis, but it cannot stop renin that a tumor releases on its own.","whyNot":["Correct.","Low renin with high aldosterone is primary hyperaldosteronism, where the adrenal makes aldosterone on its own and suppresses renin.","Low renin with low aldosterone and hyperkalemia is hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism (type 4 renal tubular acidosis), often seen in diabetic nephropathy.","High renin with low aldosterone and hyperkalemia is primary adrenal insufficiency, where the adrenal cannot respond to angiotensin II."],"study":{"topics":["Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Atrial natriuretic peptide"],"comps":["UR-7","UR-12-G2"]}},{"id":"UR-C44-Q4","stem":"Wilms tumor arises from nephrogenic rests, which are persistent remnants of metanephric blastema. Which of the following best describes when the metanephros first appears and what its blastema normally forms?","options":["Week 4; nonfunctional cervical nephrotomes that regress completely","Week 4; a transient kidney whose tubules form the efferent ductules","Week 5; collecting ducts, minor and major calyces, and renal pelvis","Week 5; nephrons from Bowman capsule to distal convoluted tubule"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-1","obj":"UR-1.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the three kidney systems in order: pronephros, mesonephros, then metanephros.","Place the metanephros: it appears in week 5 and becomes the permanent kidney.","Split its two parts: metanephric blastema forms the nephron, and the ureteric bud forms the collecting system."],"why":"Three kidney systems form in sequence. The pronephros appears early in week 4 in the cervical region, never functions, and regresses. The mesonephros forms next and works briefly as an interim kidney; its duct and some tubules persist in the male genital tract. The metanephros appears in week 5 and becomes the permanent kidney. Its metanephric blastema forms the nephron from Bowman capsule to the distal convoluted tubule, while the ureteric bud forms the collecting ducts, calyces, pelvis, and ureter.","whyNot":["This describes the pronephros, a rudimentary structure in the cervical region that never functions and disappears.","This describes the mesonephros, the interim kidney. In males its tubules persist as the efferent ductules of the testis.","Collecting ducts, calyces, and renal pelvis come from the ureteric bud, which grows into the metanephric blastema at about the same time.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Kidney development","Metanephric blastema and ureteric bud"],"comps":["UR-1","UR-12-G2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C45","system":"UR","dx":"Urothelial carcinoma of the bladder in a former dye worker","codes":["UR-12-G"],"case":"A 66-year-old man comes to the physician because of three episodes of painless gross hematuria over 2 months. For 25 years he worked at a plant that made aniline dyes, handling benzidine and 2-naphthylamine. He smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 30 years and quit 10 years ago. Temperature is 36.7 °C (98.1 °F), pulse 78/min, respirations 14/min, and blood pressure 134/82 mm Hg. Urine cytology shows malignant cells, and urine culture shows no growth. Cystoscopy shows a 3-cm papillary tumor at the bladder dome and separate flat, red, velvety patches on the lateral walls. Biopsies show high-grade papillary urothelial carcinoma invading the detrusor muscle and flat carcinoma in situ in the lateral wall patches. Diagnosis: urothelial carcinoma of the bladder in a former dye worker.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C45-Q1","stem":"Months later, imaging shows that the tumor at the dome has grown through the bladder wall and its peritoneal covering. When he is upright, tumor cells shed into the peritoneal cavity would most likely collect first in which of the following spaces?","options":["Retropubic space","Left paracolic gutter","Hepatorenal recess","Rectovesical pouch"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the peritoneum: it covers the superior surface of the bladder, then reflects back onto the rectum.","Name the recess this forms in males: the rectovesical pouch.","Apply gravity: upright, the rectovesical pouch is the lowest point of the male peritoneal cavity."],"why":"Only the superior surface of the bladder is covered by peritoneum. In males, the peritoneum passes from the bladder back onto the anterior rectum and forms the rectovesical pouch, the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity in the upright position. Fluid, blood, pus, or shed tumor cells collect there first. In females, the uterus sits between bladder and rectum, creating a vesicouterine pouch in front and a rectouterine pouch behind, and the rectouterine pouch is lowest.","whyNot":["The retropubic space lies in front of the bladder, outside the peritoneum. Cells shed into the peritoneal cavity cannot reach it.","The paracolic gutters carry fluid between the upper abdomen and pelvis. They are pathways, not the lowest collecting point.","The hepatorenal recess is the lowest part of the upper abdominal peritoneal cavity when a person lies supine. Upright, fluid falls into the pelvis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Peritoneal reflections of the pelvis"],"comps":["UR-4","UR-3"]}},{"id":"UR-C45-Q2","stem":"During transurethral resection of the dome tumor, the bladder is distended with irrigation fluid, and the resectoscope perforates the full thickness of the dome. Where will the irrigation fluid most likely collect?","options":["Perirenal fat within renal fascia","Superficial perineal space","Peritoneal cavity","Retropubic space of Retzius"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.1","dok":3,"approach":["Locate the perforation: the dome, the only bladder surface covered by peritoneum.","Recall that a distended bladder rises into the abdomen, so the dome lies against the peritoneal cavity.","Predict: a dome breach leaks into the peritoneal cavity, while breaches elsewhere stay extraperitoneal."],"why":"The kidneys and ureters are retroperitoneal, and the bladder is extraperitoneal with only its superior surface (the dome) covered by peritoneum. When the bladder is full, the dome rises above the pubis into the abdomen. A tear through the dome, whether from instruments or a blow to a full bladder, leaks urine or fluid into the peritoneal cavity. By contrast, bladder injuries with pelvic fractures usually tear the anterior or lateral wall and leak into the extraperitoneal perivesical and retropubic spaces.","whyNot":["The perirenal space surrounds the kidney within renal fascia. Fluid from the bladder dome does not track there.","The superficial perineal space fills when the spongy urethra is torn, as in a straddle injury. A bladder dome injury does not reach it.","Correct.","Fluid enters the retropubic space when the anterior or lateral bladder wall is breached below the peritoneum, as in extraperitoneal rupture with pelvic fracture."],"study":{"topics":["Intraperitoneal versus extraperitoneal bladder rupture"],"comps":["UR-4","UR-3"]}},{"id":"UR-C45-Q3","stem":"The invasive tumor is surrounded by CD8+ T cells, and the tumor cells strongly express PD-L1. Which of the following best explains how this expression allows the tumor to escape the T cells?","options":["Replaces MHC class II, which CD8+ T cells need for recognition","Binds PD-1 on T cells and delivers an inhibitory signal","Binds CTLA-4 on T cells and delivers an activating signal","Binds CD28 on T cells and delivers a costimulatory signal"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-10","obj":"UR-10.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the ligand-receptor pair: PD-L1 on tumor cells binds PD-1 on activated T cells.","Recall what PD-1 signaling does: it dampens T-cell receptor signaling and leads to T-cell exhaustion.","Apply: tumor-specific CD8+ T cells are present but switched off."],"why":"Chronically stimulated T cells express PD-1. When PD-1 binds PD-L1 on tumor cells, it recruits phosphatases that dampen T-cell receptor signaling, so CD8+ T cells stop killing and become exhausted. Tumors can also escape by reducing MHC class I so CD8+ T cells cannot see their antigens. Antibodies that block PD-1 or PD-L1 release this brake, which is why urothelial and renal carcinomas can respond to checkpoint blockade.","whyNot":["CD8+ T cells recognize peptide on MHC class I, not MHC class II.","Correct.","CTLA-4 binds B7 molecules and is an inhibitory receptor, not an activating one. PD-L1 does not use CTLA-4.","CD28 binds B7 molecules (CD80 and CD86) on antigen-presenting cells and provides the second signal that activates T cells. This would strengthen the attack."],"study":{"topics":["PD-1 and PD-L1 checkpoint","Tumor immune evasion"],"comps":["UR-10","UR-12-G"]}},{"id":"UR-C45-Q4","stem":"After complete resection, he is scheduled for repeat cystoscopy every few months for years. Which of the following best explains the need for this surveillance?","options":["Aromatic amines remain stored within the detrusor muscle for decades","Carcinogens in urine have injured the entire urothelial surface","Low-grade papillary tumors almost always progress to invasive disease","Urothelial carcinoma typically recurs as squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-G","obj":"UR-12-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note his exposures: aromatic amine dyes and smoking, both excreted as carcinogens in urine.","Recall that urine bathes the whole urothelium from renal pelvis to urethra.","Conclude: the whole lining is at risk (field effect), so new tumors keep appearing in other sites."],"why":"Urothelial carcinoma presents with painless hematuria and is often multifocal. Carcinogens from smoking, aromatic amines such as benzidine and 2-naphthylamine, cyclophosphamide, and phenacetin are excreted in urine and expose the entire urothelium. This field effect explains frequent new tumors after resection, sometimes in the ureter or renal pelvis. Two precursor routes exist: papillary tumors, which recur often, and flat carcinoma in situ, which is high grade and more likely to invade.","whyNot":["Aromatic amines act through contact with urine. They are not stored in the bladder muscle.","Correct.","Low-grade papillary tumors recur often but seldom invade. Progression is the main risk of high-grade tumors and flat carcinoma in situ.","Recurrences are usually urothelial carcinoma again. Squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder is linked to chronic irritation such as schistosomiasis."],"study":{"topics":["Urothelial carcinoma risk factors","Field effect and recurrence"],"comps":["UR-12-G","UR-2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C46","system":"UR","dx":"Squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder with Schistosoma haematobium infection","codes":["UR-12-G"],"case":"A 47-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 4 months of intermittent blood in the urine, urinary frequency, and burning with urination. She grew up in a farming village in the Nile Delta and moved to the United States 3 years ago. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse 80/min, respirations 14/min, and blood pressure 126/80 mm Hg. Hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0). Urine microscopy shows red blood cells and large oval eggs with a sharp terminal spine. A plain radiograph shows calcification outlining the bladder wall. Cystoscopy shows an ulcerated mass on the posterosuperior bladder wall. Biopsy shows keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma, with calcified eggs in the surrounding bladder wall. Diagnosis: squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder with Schistosoma haematobium infection.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C46-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best explains the histologic type of her tumor?","options":["Squamous metaplasia from chronic egg-induced inflammation","Glandular metaplasia arising in a persistent urachal remnant","Intestinal-type epithelium persisting after bladder exstrophy","Aromatic amine injury to otherwise normal urothelial cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-G","obj":"UR-12-G.6","dok":3,"approach":["Note the tumor type: keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma, which is uncommon in the bladder.","Find the chronic irritant: schistosome eggs trapped in the bladder wall.","Link irritation to cancer: chronic inflammation leads to squamous metaplasia, then dysplasia, then carcinoma."],"why":"Schistosoma haematobium eggs lodge in the bladder wall and provoke granulomatous inflammation, fibrosis, and calcification. Years of chronic irritation convert the urothelium to squamous epithelium (metaplasia), which can become dysplastic and then invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Other chronic irritants such as stones and long-term catheters do the same. Bladder adenocarcinoma instead arises from urachal remnants or exstrophy.","whyNot":["Correct.","Urachal remnants give rise to adenocarcinoma at the bladder dome, not keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma.","Bladder exstrophy predisposes to adenocarcinoma, not squamous cell carcinoma.","Aromatic amines cause urothelial carcinoma, the most common bladder cancer, rather than squamous cell carcinoma."],"study":{"topics":["Bladder squamous cell carcinoma","Metaplasia to carcinoma sequence"],"comps":["UR-12-G","UR-11"]}},{"id":"UR-C46-Q2","stem":"The mass extends through the posterosuperior wall of her bladder. Which of the following spaces lies directly against this surface of her bladder?","options":["Retropubic space of Retzius","Rectouterine pouch","Rectovesical pouch","Vesicouterine pouch"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.2","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the female peritoneum from front to back: over the bladder, onto the uterus, then onto the rectum.","Name the two recesses: vesicouterine in front of the uterus, rectouterine behind it.","Apply: the posterosuperior bladder surface faces the vesicouterine pouch."],"why":"In females, the peritoneum covering the superior bladder surface reflects onto the anterior uterus, forming the shallow vesicouterine pouch. It then passes over the uterus and down onto the rectum, forming the deeper rectouterine pouch, the lowest part of the female peritoneal cavity. A bladder tumor growing through the posterosuperior wall reaches the vesicouterine pouch and the uterus first.","whyNot":["The retropubic space lies in front of the bladder, between it and the pubic bones, and is outside the peritoneum.","The rectouterine pouch lies between the uterus and the rectum, behind the uterus, not against the bladder.","The rectovesical pouch exists only in males, where no uterus separates the bladder from the rectum.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Female pelvic peritoneal pouches"],"comps":["UR-4","UR-3"]}},{"id":"UR-C46-Q3","stem":"CT also shows a 2-cm fluid-filled cyst in the midline of the anterior abdominal wall, between the bladder dome and the umbilicus. This cyst most likely arises from a remnant of which of the following embryonic structures?","options":["Allantois","Vitelline duct","Urorectal septum","Mesonephric duct"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-1","obj":"UR-1.5","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the cyst: on the path from the bladder apex to the umbilicus.","Recall what runs there in the embryo: the allantois, which becomes the urachus and then the median umbilical ligament.","Predict: incomplete closure leaves a urachal cyst, or a patent urachus that drains urine from the umbilicus."],"why":"The urorectal septum divides the cloaca into the urogenital sinus in front (which forms the bladder and urethra) and the anorectal canal behind. The allantois connects the bladder apex to the umbilicus and normally closes into a fibrous cord, the urachus, which becomes the median umbilical ligament. If a segment stays open, it forms a urachal cyst. If the whole channel stays open, urine drains from the umbilicus (patent urachus). Urachal remnants are also the source of bladder adenocarcinoma at the dome.","whyNot":["Correct.","A persistent vitelline duct causes Meckel diverticulum or a fistula that drains intestinal contents from the umbilicus.","The urorectal septum divides the cloaca. Its failure causes fistulas between the rectum and the urinary tract, not a midline cyst above the bladder.","The mesonephric duct gives rise to the ureteric bud and male genital ducts. Remnants in females form Gartner duct cysts beside the vagina."],"study":{"topics":["Partition of the cloaca","Urachal anomalies"],"comps":["UR-1","UR-12-G"]}},{"id":"UR-C46-Q4","stem":"She most likely acquired this infection by which of the following routes?","options":["Bite of a blackfly that deposits infective larvae","Ingestion of metacercariae in undercooked freshwater fish","Skin penetration by cercariae from freshwater snails","Ingestion of eggs in food contaminated with feces"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.7","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the parasite: a terminal-spined egg in urine is Schistosoma haematobium.","Recall the life cycle: eggs hatch in fresh water, miracidia infect snails, and snails release cercariae.","Apply: cercariae penetrate the skin of people who wade or bathe in that water."],"why":"Schistosoma haematobium eggs pass in urine and hatch in fresh water into miracidia, which infect specific snails. The snails release free-swimming cercariae that penetrate human skin during contact with the water. The larvae travel through the circulation, mature in the portal system, and the adult worm pairs settle in the vesical venous plexus. Their terminal-spined eggs work through the bladder wall, causing hematuria, fibrosis, calcification, and in time squamous cell carcinoma.","whyNot":["Blackfly bites transmit Onchocerca volvulus, which causes river blindness.","Metacercariae in raw or undercooked freshwater fish transmit the liver fluke Clonorchis sinensis.","Correct.","Ingesting eggs from fecal contamination transmits Ascaris, Trichuris, and Taenia solium cysticercosis, not schistosomes."],"study":{"topics":["Schistosoma haematobium life cycle"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-G"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C47","system":"UR","dx":"Renal angiomyolipomas in tuberous sclerosis","codes":["UR-12-G"],"case":"A 32-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of sudden severe left flank pain for 3 hours. She has had seizures since early childhood. Temperature is 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pulse 112/min, respirations 20/min, and blood pressure 98/62 mm Hg. Examination shows facial angiofibromas, several hypopigmented macules on the trunk, and a periungual fibroma. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0). CT shows multiple bilateral renal masses containing fat density, the largest a 9-cm left renal mass with surrounding retroperitoneal hemorrhage, and numerous bilateral renal cysts. Biopsy of a smaller mass shows thick-walled blood vessels, smooth muscle, and mature fat. Diagnosis: renal angiomyolipomas in tuberous sclerosis complex.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C47-Q1","stem":"Her renal cysts are unusually numerous and enlarging. Genetic testing shows a large deletion on chromosome 16p that removes TSC2 and the gene immediately next to it. The neighboring gene most likely encodes which of the following proteins?","options":["Hamartin","Polycystin-2","Fibrocystin","Polycystin-1"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.1","dok":3,"approach":["Locate TSC2: chromosome 16p13.3.","Recall which polycystic kidney gene lies on the same arm: PKD1, encoding polycystin-1.","Apply: a deletion removing both genes causes tuberous sclerosis with severe, early polycystic kidney disease."],"why":"PKD1 lies right next to TSC2 on chromosome 16p13.3, so a large deletion can remove both, producing tuberous sclerosis plus severe early polycystic kidneys. Polycystin-1 (PKD1) and polycystin-2 (PKD2, chromosome 4) work together in primary cilia of tubular cells to regulate calcium signaling and cell growth, and loss leads to cyst formation. Both are autosomal dominant: each child of an affected parent has a 50% risk. PKD1 accounts for most families and causes earlier kidney failure than PKD2. ARPKD from PKHD1 (fibrocystin) skips generations and appears in infancy.","whyNot":["Hamartin is the TSC1 product, encoded on chromosome 9, not 16.","Polycystin-2 is encoded by PKD2 on chromosome 4. PKD2 disease is autosomal dominant but milder and later in onset.","Fibrocystin is encoded by PKHD1 on chromosome 6 and causes autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease in infants.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Genetics of polycystic kidney disease","TSC2 and PKD1 contiguous deletion"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-E2"]}},{"id":"UR-C47-Q2","stem":"To stop the bleeding, the segmental artery branch supplying the largest tumor is embolized. Which of the following is most likely to develop in the kidney tissue supplied by that branch?","options":["Diffuse bilateral cortical necrosis sparing the medulla","Wedge-shaped cortical infarct with its base at the capsule","Normal perfusion through arcuate artery anastomoses","Patchy necrosis of the papillae sparing the overlying cortex"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-5","obj":"UR-5.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that segmental, interlobar, and arcuate arteries do not anastomose: they are end arteries.","Predict that occluding one branch kills all the tissue it supplies.","Picture the shape: the territory fans out from the hilum, so the infarct is a wedge with its base at the capsule."],"why":"Each renal segmental artery supplies its own segment with no meaningful collateral flow from neighbors, and the interlobar and arcuate arteries behave the same way. Embolic occlusion, whether therapeutic or from a cardiac embolus, therefore causes a pale coagulative infarct of the whole territory. Because the vessels fan out toward the surface, the infarct is wedge-shaped, with its apex toward the hilum and its base at the capsule. A thin rim just under the capsule may survive on capsular collateral vessels.","whyNot":["Diffuse bilateral cortical necrosis follows shock or disseminated intravascular coagulation, which affect both kidneys at once. A single embolized branch affects one territory.","Correct.","Arcuate arteries do not form effective anastomoses with one another, so they cannot keep the tissue perfused.","Papillary necrosis follows analgesic abuse, sickle cell disease, diabetes, or obstruction. Arterial occlusion damages the whole wedge of tissue that the artery supplies."],"study":{"topics":["Renal segmental arteries as end arteries","Renal infarction"],"comps":["UR-5","UR-12-F4"]}},{"id":"UR-C47-Q3","stem":"A separate 4-cm mass without fat is removed from her right kidney. It is mahogany brown with a central stellate scar, and electron microscopy shows tumor cells packed with mitochondria. Which of the following best describes this lesion?","options":["Malignant papillary tumor with trisomy of chromosomes 7 and 17","Malignant tumor of intercalated cells with perinuclear halos","Benign tumor of collecting duct intercalated cells","Malignant tumor of lipid-laden proximal tubule cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-G1","obj":"UR-12-G.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the clues: mahogany brown, central stellate scar, and cells packed with mitochondria.","Name the tumor: oncocytoma.","Recall its behavior: benign, arising from intercalated cells of the collecting duct."],"why":"Oncocytoma is a benign renal tumor of large eosinophilic cells whose cytoplasm is packed with mitochondria. Grossly it is tan to mahogany brown, often with a central stellate scar. It arises from intercalated cells of the collecting duct, like chromophobe carcinoma, but does not metastasize. Chromophobe carcinoma has pale cells with prominent borders and perinuclear halos, papillary carcinoma shows trisomy 7 and 17, and clear cell carcinoma has lipid-rich proximal tubule cells.","whyNot":["Papillary renal cell carcinoma forms papillae with foamy macrophages, not a brown mass with a central scar.","Pale cells with distinct cell borders and perinuclear halos describe chromophobe carcinoma, a malignant tumor with a good prognosis.","Correct.","Lipid-laden proximal tubule cells form clear cell carcinoma, which has a golden-yellow cut surface."],"study":{"topics":["Renal oncocytoma","Renal tumor subtypes"],"comps":["UR-12-G1","UR-12-G"]}},{"id":"UR-C47-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best explains the overgrowth of her renal tumors at the molecular level?","options":["Stabilization of HIF after loss of VHL ubiquitin ligase","Activating mutation in the MET receptor tyrosine kinase","Loss of WT1 control of nephron differentiation","Loss of tuberin-hamartin inhibition of mTOR signaling"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"UR-9","obj":"UR-9.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the syndrome: tuberous sclerosis, from TSC1 (hamartin) or TSC2 (tuberin) mutation.","Recall what the complex does: it inactivates Rheb, the activator of mTORC1.","Predict loss: mTORC1 is always on, driving cell growth and hamartomas such as angiomyolipomas."],"why":"Hamartin (TSC1, chromosome 9) and tuberin (TSC2, chromosome 16) form a complex that acts as a GTPase-activating protein for Rheb. Keeping Rheb in its inactive GDP-bound state holds mTORC1 in check. When the second allele of either gene is lost in a cell, Rheb stays active and mTORC1 drives protein synthesis and cell growth. This produces hamartomas: renal angiomyolipomas (blood vessels, smooth muscle, fat), cortical tubers, cardiac rhabdomyomas, and renal cysts.","whyNot":["HIF stabilization from VHL loss drives clear cell renal carcinoma and hemangioblastomas in von Hippel-Lindau syndrome.","Activating MET mutations cause hereditary papillary renal cell carcinoma.","WT1 loss causes Wilms tumor, a childhood tumor of nephrogenic blastema.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tuberous sclerosis and mTOR","Angiomyolipoma"],"comps":["UR-9","UR-12-G"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C48","system":"UR","dx":"Acute cystitis from Staphylococcus saprophyticus","codes":["UR-12-H2"],"case":"A 20-year-old woman comes to the student health clinic because of 2 days of burning with urination, urinary frequency, urgency, and lower abdominal discomfort. She became sexually active with a new partner 1 week ago. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pulse 78/min, respirations 14/min, and blood pressure 112/70 mm Hg. Mild suprapubic tenderness is present, and there is no costovertebral angle tenderness. Urinalysis shows positive leukocyte esterase, negative nitrite, 40 WBC/hpf (0 to 5), and 8 RBC/hpf (0 to 2). Urine culture grows more than 100,000 CFU/mL of catalase-positive, coagulase-negative, gram-positive cocci in clusters that are resistant to novobiocin. Diagnosis: acute cystitis due to Staphylococcus saprophyticus.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C48-Q1","stem":"Which of the following additional findings would most strongly indicate that her infection has ascended to the kidney?","options":["Positive nitrite reaction on urine dipstick","White blood cell casts in the urine sediment","Gross hematuria with small blood clots","Tenderness to palpation above the pubic symphysis"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-H2","obj":"UR-12-H.2","dok":3,"approach":["Separate lower from upper tract findings: dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain without fever fit cystitis.","Ask which urine finding can only form in the kidney: casts, which are molded in renal tubules.","Conclude: white cell casts show inflammation in the renal parenchyma, as in pyelonephritis."],"why":"Casts form when Tamm-Horsfall protein gels inside renal tubules and traps cells, so a white cell cast proves that leukocytes were in the tubules. In a patient with urinary tract infection, this means pyelonephritis. Clinically, pyelonephritis adds fever, flank pain, and costovertebral angle tenderness to bladder symptoms. Risk factors for cystitis include female sex, sexual intercourse, pregnancy, catheters, diabetes, and obstruction.","whyNot":["Nitrite shows nitrate-reducing gram-negative bacteria such as E. coli. It is common in simple cystitis and does not tell where the infection is.","Correct.","Gross hematuria can occur in hemorrhagic cystitis. Blood in the urine does not place the infection in the kidney.","Suprapubic tenderness is a sign of bladder inflammation, which she already has."],"study":{"topics":["Cystitis versus pyelonephritis","Urine casts"],"comps":["UR-12-H2","UR-12-H1"]}},{"id":"UR-C48-Q2","stem":"As her bladder fills between voids, its lining stretches while still keeping urine out of the bladder wall. Which of the following changes in the epithelium makes this possible?","options":["Umbrella cells flatten and add uroplakin plaques to their apical membrane","Basal cells divide rapidly to add extra layers of epithelium during filling","A surface layer of keratin thickens to resist the increase in wall tension","Tight junctions between surface cells open to allow passive stretching"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-2","obj":"UR-2.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the epithelium: transitional epithelium (urothelium) with large surface umbrella cells.","Recall the two jobs: stretch during filling and block urine from leaking in.","Link structure to function: umbrella cells flatten and insert stored uroplakin plaques into their surface, while tight junctions and plaques keep the barrier."],"why":"The urothelium has basal cells, intermediate cells, and a surface layer of large umbrella cells. The apical membrane of umbrella cells contains stiff uroplakin plaques that are very impermeable to water and solutes, and tight junctions seal the gaps between cells. During filling, umbrella cells flatten and insert extra plaque-containing membrane from cytoplasmic vesicles, enlarging their surface without breaking the barrier. Beneath lie the lamina propria and the smooth muscle detrusor.","whyNot":["Correct.","Cell division is far too slow to follow minute-to-minute filling. The urothelium actually becomes thinner as it stretches.","Normal urothelium is not keratinized. Keratinization appears only after squamous metaplasia, as with chronic irritation.","Opening tight junctions would let urine leak into the bladder wall. The tight junctions stay sealed and are part of the barrier."],"study":{"topics":["Urothelium and umbrella cells","Bladder wall layers"],"comps":["UR-2","UR-3"]}},{"id":"UR-C48-Q3","stem":"Which of the following anatomic features most directly explains why women have a higher risk of this infection than men?","options":["Short urethra that opens near the vaginal introitus","Wider ureteric orifices at the corners of the trigone","Ureters that enter the bladder wall at a right angle","Absence of a trigone at the base of the bladder"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the route of most cystitis: bacteria from the perineum ascend through the urethra.","Compare lengths: the female urethra is about 4 cm, and the male urethra is much longer, with prostatic, membranous, and spongy parts.","Conclude: a short urethra opening near the vagina and anus gives bacteria an easy path, and intercourse pushes them inward."],"why":"The female urethra is about 4 cm long and opens into the vestibule just in front of the vaginal opening, close to perineal and fecal flora. Bacteria colonize the periurethral area and reach the bladder over a short distance, helped by intercourse. The male urethra runs much farther through the prostate, the external sphincter (membranous part), and the corpus spongiosum, so ascending infection is far less common in men.","whyNot":["Correct.","The ureteric orifices are similar in both sexes. Their shape affects reflux, not entry of bacteria from the perineum.","The ureters pass obliquely through the bladder wall in both sexes. A short or perpendicular course causes vesicoureteral reflux, not a sex difference in cystitis.","Both sexes have a trigone, the smooth triangle between the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice."],"study":{"topics":["Female versus male urethra","Bladder trigone"],"comps":["UR-3","UR-12-H2"]}},{"id":"UR-C48-Q4","stem":"Escherichia coli, the most common cause of this condition, attaches to bladder urothelium mainly through which of the following?","options":["K1 capsule blocking complement deposition","P fimbriae binding glycolipids of the renal pelvis","Type 1 fimbriae binding mannose on uroplakins","Shiga-like toxin binding endothelial Gb3"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall uropathogenic E. coli: a gram-negative, lactose-fermenting rod from fecal flora.","Match the adhesin to the site: type 1 fimbriae for the bladder, P fimbriae for the kidney.","Name the receptor: the FimH tip binds mannose on uroplakins of umbrella cells."],"why":"Uropathogenic E. coli comes from the patient's own fecal flora and causes most urinary tract infections. Its type 1 fimbriae carry the FimH adhesin, which binds mannose residues on uroplakin proteins of umbrella cells and lets the bacteria resist being flushed out, causing cystitis. Strains with P fimbriae bind glycolipids on upper urinary tract epithelium and are more likely to cause pyelonephritis. Staphylococcus saprophyticus is the second most common cause in young sexually active women.","whyNot":["The K1 capsule resists phagocytosis and complement and is linked to neonatal meningitis. It is not an adhesin.","P fimbriae bind galactose-containing glycolipids on upper tract epithelium. They are linked to pyelonephritis rather than cystitis.","Correct.","Shiga-like toxin from enterohemorrhagic E. coli damages endothelium in hemolytic uremic syndrome. It does not attach bacteria to the bladder."],"study":{"topics":["Uropathogenic E. coli virulence factors"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-H2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C49","system":"UR","dx":"Catheter-associated Pseudomonas cystitis with neurogenic bladder after spinal cord injury","codes":["UR-12-H2"],"case":"A 34-year-old man with a complete spinal cord injury at the T6 level from a motorcycle crash 2 years ago comes to the clinic because of cloudy, foul-smelling urine and increased leg spasms for 3 days. He has used an indwelling urethral catheter since an episode of urinary retention 6 months ago. Earlier urodynamic testing showed involuntary detrusor contractions at low bladder volumes with simultaneous contraction of the external urethral sphincter. Temperature is 37.3 °C (99.1 °F), pulse 76/min, respirations 14/min, and blood pressure 122/78 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows positive leukocyte esterase and 60 WBC/hpf (0 to 5). Urine culture grows more than 100,000 CFU/mL of Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Diagnosis: catheter-associated Pseudomonas cystitis with neurogenic bladder after spinal cord injury.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C49-Q1","stem":"His urodynamic findings result from loss of descending input from which of the following structures?","options":["Sympathetic neurons of the T11 to L2 cord","Pudendal nerve motor fibers to the sphincter","Sacral parasympathetic nucleus at S2 to S4","Pontine micturition center"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"UR-6","obj":"UR-6.7","dok":3,"approach":["Place the lesion: T6, above the sacral cord, so the sacral reflex arc still works.","Explain the contractions: bladder stretch triggers a sacral reflex to the detrusor, giving a reflex (spastic) bladder.","Explain the sphincter contracting at the same time: the pons normally relaxes the sphincter when the detrusor contracts, and that signal is cut off."],"why":"Normal voiding is coordinated by the pontine micturition center. When it fires, sacral parasympathetic neurons (S2 to S4, pelvic splanchnic nerves) contract the detrusor while pudendal motor neurons and sympathetic outflow to the bladder neck are inhibited, so the sphincters relax. A cord lesion above the sacral segments leaves the sacral reflex intact but cuts the pons off, producing a reflex bladder with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia, high pressures, and incomplete emptying. A lesion at the sacral cord or cauda equina destroys the reflex arc and causes a flaccid bladder with overflow.","whyNot":["These sympathetic neurons lie below his injury and are intact. They relax the detrusor and close the internal sphincter during filling.","The pudendal nerve is a peripheral nerve from the sacral cord. It is intact, which is why the external sphincter can contract.","The sacral parasympathetic neurons lie below his injury and are intact. They are producing the reflex detrusor contractions.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Micturition reflex","Spastic versus flaccid neurogenic bladder"],"comps":["UR-6","UR-4"]}},{"id":"UR-C49-Q2","stem":"The involuntary detrusor contractions on his earlier study share their mechanism with which of the following types of incontinence in neurologically intact adults?","options":["Overflow incontinence","Stress incontinence","Urge incontinence","Functional incontinence"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-4","obj":"UR-4.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the mechanism: involuntary detrusor contractions, also called detrusor overactivity.","Recall which incontinence type has this mechanism: urge incontinence, with sudden urgency and leakage.","Contrast the others: stress means weak urethral support, and overflow means poor emptying."],"why":"Urge incontinence results from detrusor overactivity: the bladder contracts before the person chooses to void, producing sudden urgency and leakage. Stress incontinence results from weak pelvic floor and urethral support, often after childbirth and with a cystocele, so rises in abdominal pressure push urine out. Overflow incontinence results from a bladder that cannot empty because of an underactive detrusor or an obstruction.","whyNot":["Overflow incontinence comes from an underactive detrusor or outlet obstruction, as with prostatic enlargement or a sacral cord lesion. The overfull bladder dribbles.","Stress incontinence comes from weak urethral support or sphincter weakness, as after vaginal deliveries or with a cystocele. Leakage happens with coughing or lifting, without detrusor contraction.","Correct.","Functional incontinence occurs when a person with a normal bladder cannot reach the toilet in time because of limited mobility or cognition."],"study":{"topics":["Types of urinary incontinence","Pelvic floor and urethral support"],"comps":["UR-4","UR-6"]}},{"id":"UR-C49-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why an indwelling catheter allows this infection to persist?","options":["Biofilm on the catheter that resists antibiotics and phagocytes","Catheter-induced loss of IgA secretion by the bladder urothelium","Hematogenous seeding of the bladder wall from a distant skin site","Continuous bladder distension that disrupts the urothelial barrier"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-12-H2","obj":"UR-12-H.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall why catheters are a major cystitis risk factor: they bypass urethral defenses and provide a surface for bacteria.","Recall what Pseudomonas does on plastic: it makes an alginate-rich biofilm.","Apply: bacteria inside biofilm grow slowly and are shielded, so the infection resists clearance."],"why":"Catheters let bacteria climb along the inner and outer surfaces of the tube, past the urethral defenses. Pseudomonas and other organisms attach and secrete a slimy matrix (for Pseudomonas, largely alginate) that forms a biofilm. Bacteria within biofilm are shielded from antibodies, phagocytes, and many antibiotics and keep reseeding the urine. The risk of bacteriuria rises with each day the catheter stays in. Incomplete emptying from neurogenic bladder adds to the risk.","whyNot":["Correct.","There is no established loss of IgA secretion from catheters. The main problem is the foreign surface and the bypassed urethral defenses.","Bacteria reach the catheterized bladder by ascending along the catheter, not through the bloodstream.","An indwelling catheter drains the bladder continuously, so it prevents distension rather than causing it."],"study":{"topics":["Catheter-associated urinary tract infection","Bacterial biofilm"],"comps":["UR-12-H2","UR-11"]}},{"id":"UR-C49-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best describes the laboratory features of the organism isolated from his urine?","options":["Coagulase-negative gram-positive coccus that resists novobiocin","Oxidase-positive gram-negative rod that does not ferment lactose","Oxidase-negative gram-negative rod with a mucoid lactose-fermenting colony","Catalase-negative gram-positive coccus that grows in 6.5% sodium chloride"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","Recall its key traits: gram-negative rod, oxidase-positive, and non-lactose-fermenting, often with blue-green pigment and a grape-like odor.","Rule out the other urinary pathogens by their traits."],"why":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is an aerobic gram-negative rod that is oxidase-positive and does not ferment lactose, which separates it from E. coli and Klebsiella. Colonies often make blue-green pigments such as pyocyanin and smell like grapes. It thrives in moist hospital settings and on devices, so it is a classic cause of catheter-associated urinary infection. It is also a major pathogen in burns and cystic fibrosis.","whyNot":["A coagulase-negative, novobiocin-resistant coccus describes Staphylococcus saprophyticus, which causes cystitis in young sexually active women.","Correct.","A mucoid, lactose-fermenting, oxidase-negative rod describes Klebsiella pneumoniae, which has a thick polysaccharide capsule.","A catalase-negative coccus that grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile describes Enterococcus, a common cause of urinary infection in catheterized and hospitalized patients."],"study":{"topics":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Identifying urinary pathogens"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-H2"]}}]},{"id":"UR-C50","system":"UR","dx":"Gonococcal urethritis with epididymitis","codes":["UR-12-H3"],"case":"A 23-year-old man comes to the physician because of 4 days of burning with urination and a thick yellow urethral discharge, followed by 1 day of right scrotal pain and swelling. He has had two new female sexual partners in the past month and uses condoms inconsistently. He was treated for gonorrhea 2 years ago. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse 92/min, respirations 16/min, and blood pressure 124/76 mm Hg. The right epididymis is swollen and tender, the right testis lies in a normal position, and the cremasteric reflex is present. Nucleic acid amplification testing of urine is positive for Neisseria gonorrhoeae and negative for Chlamydia trachomatis. Diagnosis: gonococcal urethritis with right epididymitis.","questions":[{"id":"UR-C50-Q1","stem":"Four years ago, he fell astride a metal fence rail and developed blood at the urethral meatus and bruising of the perineum. Which part of the urethra was most likely torn in that injury?","options":["Bulbous part of the spongy urethra","Pendulous part of the spongy urethra","Membranous urethra","Prostatic urethra"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the mechanism: a straddle injury crushes the perineum against the pubic arch.","Find the urethral segment fixed in the perineum below the pubic arch: the bulbous part of the spongy urethra.","Contrast with pelvic fracture, which tears the membranous urethra."],"why":"In a straddle injury, the bulbous part of the spongy urethra is crushed against the inferior pubic rami. Blood and urine escape into the superficial perineal space, deep to the membranous layer of superficial fascia (Colles fascia), and spread into the scrotum, penis, and lower abdominal wall but not the thighs. Pelvic fractures instead tear the posterior urethra at the membranous segment, often with a high-riding prostate and extravasation into the retropubic space.","whyNot":["Correct.","The pendulous (penile) urethra is mobile and lies in front of the pubic arch, so it is not crushed against bone in a straddle fall.","The membranous urethra is torn by pelvic fractures, which shear it where it passes through the deep perineal pouch below the prostate.","The prostatic urethra is protected within the prostate and is rarely torn by blunt injury."],"study":{"topics":["Male urethral injuries","Superficial perineal space"],"comps":["UR-3","UR-4"]}},{"id":"UR-C50-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains why his infection 2 years ago did not protect him from this one?","options":["Obligate intracellular replication within host cells","Polysaccharide capsule that blocks opsonization","Antigenic variation of pilin proteins","Protein A binding the Fc portion of IgG"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"UR-11","obj":"UR-11.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: Neisseria gonorrhoeae, a gram-negative diplococcus.","Ask what lets it reinfect the same person: changing its main surface antigens.","Recall the mechanism: recombination of pilin genes changes pilus antigens, so old antibodies no longer bind."],"why":"Gonococcal pili attach the organism to urethral and cervical epithelium. The pilin gene recombines with many silent copies, so the pili keep changing their antigens, and outer membrane Opa proteins switch on and off. Antibodies from an earlier infection no longer match, which allows repeated infections and has prevented a vaccine. Gonococci also make IgA protease, which cleaves mucosal IgA. Nucleic acid amplification testing is the standard diagnostic test for both gonorrhea and chlamydia.","whyNot":["Obligate intracellular growth with elementary and reticulate bodies describes Chlamydia trachomatis, not Neisseria gonorrhoeae.","Neisseria gonorrhoeae lacks a polysaccharide capsule. Neisseria meningitidis has one, which is why a meningococcal vaccine works.","Correct.","Protein A is a Staphylococcus aureus surface protein. Gonococci do not make it."],"study":{"topics":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae virulence factors","Gonorrhea versus chlamydia"],"comps":["UR-11","UR-12-H3"]}},{"id":"UR-C50-Q3","stem":"Bacteria most likely reached his epididymis by ascending through ducts that open into which segment of the urethra?","options":["Membranous urethra","Prostatic urethra","Bulbous part of the spongy urethra","Navicular fossa of the spongy urethra"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"UR-3","obj":"UR-3.5","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the path in reverse: epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra.","Recall where the ejaculatory ducts open: on the seminal colliculus in the prostatic urethra.","Conclude: urethral organisms reach the epididymis by climbing back up through the ejaculatory duct and ductus deferens."],"why":"The male urethra has prostatic, membranous, and spongy segments. The ejaculatory ducts, formed by the ductus deferens joining the seminal vesicle duct, open on the seminal colliculus of the prostatic urethra, along with the prostatic ducts. Organisms from urethritis can ascend this route to cause epididymitis. In sexually active men younger than about 35 years, epididymitis is usually caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae or Chlamydia trachomatis.","whyNot":["The membranous urethra is the short segment passing through the external sphincter. No genital ducts open into it.","Correct.","The ducts of the bulbourethral glands open into the bulbous part of the spongy urethra. These glands do not connect to the epididymis.","The navicular fossa is the dilated end of the urethra in the glans. No ducts from the epididymis open there."],"study":{"topics":["Segments of the male urethra","Route to epididymitis"],"comps":["UR-3","UR-12-H3"]}},{"id":"UR-C50-Q4","stem":"One of his partners has an untreated endocervical infection with the same organism. Which of the following complications is she at greatest risk of developing?","options":["Bacterial vaginosis with clue cells","Squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix","Toxic shock syndrome","Salpingitis with tubal scarring"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"UR-12-H3","obj":"UR-12-H.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gonococcal cervicitis can ascend through the uterus to the fallopian tubes.","Name the result: pelvic inflammatory disease with salpingitis.","Predict the long-term effect: tubal scarring, causing infertility and ectopic pregnancy."],"why":"Untreated gonococcal or chlamydial cervicitis can ascend to the endometrium and fallopian tubes, causing pelvic inflammatory disease with salpingitis and sometimes tubo-ovarian abscess. Scarring of the tubes leads to infertility and ectopic pregnancy. Gonococcal urethritis gives a purulent discharge with gram-negative intracellular diplococci, while nongonococcal urethritis (often Chlamydia trachomatis) gives scant discharge. Other complications include epididymitis, disseminated gonococcal infection with septic arthritis, and reactive arthritis after chlamydial infection.","whyNot":["Bacterial vaginosis is an overgrowth of Gardnerella and anaerobes with clue cells and a fishy odor. It is not caused by gonococci.","Cervical squamous cell carcinoma is caused by high-risk human papillomavirus, not gonorrhea.","Toxic shock syndrome comes from Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes toxins acting as superantigens.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pelvic inflammatory disease","Gonococcal versus nongonococcal urethritis"],"comps":["UR-12-H3","UR-11"]}}]}];
window.NPLEX_DIAGNOSTIC = [{"id":"DX-CV-C01","system":"CV","dx":"Acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction from left anterior descending artery occlusion","codes":["CV-18-C3","CV-8"],"case":"A 61-year-old man with hypertension and a 40 pack-year smoking history comes to the emergency department 4 hours after crushing substernal chest pain began. The pain radiates to his left arm, and he is diaphoretic. Pulse is 102/min, and blood pressure is 104/68 mm Hg. The ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V4. Serum troponin I is 8.2 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). Echocardiography shows no motion of the anterior wall, anterior septum, and apex of the left ventricle, with an ejection fraction of 40% (normal 55 to 70%). Diagnosis: acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q1","from":[4],"stem":"Occlusion of which of the following arteries most likely explains the ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V4 and the loss of anterior wall, septal, and apical motion?","options":["Left anterior descending artery","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Posterior interventricular artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-8","obj":"CV-8.1","dok":2,"approach":["Match the leads to the wall: V1 and V2 face the septum, and V3 and V4 face the anterior wall.","Recall which artery runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex.","That artery, the LAD, supplies the anterior left ventricle, the apex, and the anterior two-thirds of the septum."],"why":"The left anterior descending artery is a branch of the left main coronary artery that runs in the anterior interventricular groove toward the apex. It supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, the apex, and the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum through its septal branches. ST elevation in V1 to V4 with anterior and apical wall motion loss is the classic pattern of LAD occlusion, the most common site of coronary occlusion in MI.","whyNot":["Correct.","The right coronary artery supplies the right ventricle and, in most people, the inferior wall, the SA node, and the AV node. Its occlusion causes ST elevation in the inferior leads II, III, and aVF.","The left circumflex artery runs in the left atrioventricular groove and supplies the lateral and posterior left ventricle. Its occlusion shows up in the lateral leads I, aVL, V5, and V6.","The posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery usually arises from the right coronary artery and supplies the inferior wall and the posterior third of the septum, which leads V1 to V4 do not face."],"study":{"topics":["Coronary artery territories","ECG leads and the walls they face"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q2","from":[17],"stem":"Troponin I is a structural protein of the myocyte contractile apparatus. Which of the following processes is most likely responsible for its increased serum concentration in this patient?","options":["Caspase-driven apoptosis of myocytes in the ischemic zone","Hepatic synthesis of troponin as an acute phase reactant","Exocytosis of troponin by viable myocytes under stress","Membrane rupture in myocytes that have died by necrosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note that troponin normally sits inside the myocyte, bound to the thin filaments.","Ask what lets an intracellular protein reach the blood: loss of plasma membrane integrity.","Membrane breakdown is the hallmark of irreversible injury and necrosis."],"why":"After about 20 to 40 minutes of severe ischemia, myocyte injury becomes irreversible. ATP loss, calcium influx, and activation of phospholipases and proteases damage the plasma membrane, so the cell contents leak out. Troponin I and troponin T are cardiac-specific, so their leakage into blood marks myocyte necrosis. Troponin begins to rise within about 2 to 4 hours and stays elevated for about 7 to 10 days.","whyNot":["Apoptosis keeps the plasma membrane intact, and the cell fragments are eaten by macrophages without spilling their contents. The cell death of an infarct is mainly coagulative necrosis.","The liver does not make cardiac troponin. Acute phase reactants made by the liver include C-reactive protein and fibrinogen.","Myocytes do not package troponin into vesicles for secretion. A large rise like his reflects leakage from dead cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Reversible and irreversible cell injury","Cardiac biomarkers after MI"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-5"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q3","from":[26],"stem":"Two days after the onset of pain, he dies of ventricular fibrillation. Which of the following microscopic findings is most likely in the infarcted anterior wall at autopsy?","options":["Granulation tissue with new capillaries and fibroblasts","Dense collagen scar replacing the lost muscle fibers","Macrophages clearing dead myocytes at the infarct border","Coagulative necrosis with a dense neutrophil infiltrate"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","obj":"CV-18-C.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: about 48 hours after the infarct began.","Recall the Robbins sequence: neutrophils peak at about 1 to 3 days, macrophages at about 3 to 7 days, granulation tissue at about 7 to 14 days, then scar.","At 2 days, expect dead myocytes with preserved outlines and many neutrophils."],"why":"Myocardial infarcts undergo coagulative necrosis: dead myocytes keep their outlines while their nuclei fade. Neutrophils are drawn into the dead tissue and peak at about 1 to 3 days. Macrophages take over at about 3 to 7 days, granulation tissue forms at about 7 to 14 days, and a collagen scar matures over the following weeks. At 2 days the expected picture is coagulative necrosis with a dense neutrophil infiltrate.","whyNot":["Granulation tissue forms at the infarct margins at about 7 to 14 days.","A collagen scar replaces the infarct over several weeks and is largely complete by about 2 months.","Macrophages arrive and begin removing dead myocytes at about 3 to 7 days. At 2 days the infiltrate is still mainly neutrophils.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Timeline of changes after MI","Coagulative necrosis"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q4","from":[8],"stem":"During the first hours, potassium released from injured cells depolarizes the resting membrane potential of myocytes at the infarct border from about -90 mV to about -65 mV. Which of the following best explains the slowed impulse conduction through these ventricular myocytes?","options":["L-type calcium channels close early, so the phase 2 plateau is lost","Many fast sodium channels stay inactivated, so phase 0 is slower","Funny current channels open, so phase 4 depolarization speeds up","Delayed rectifier potassium channels fail, so phase 3 is prolonged"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the ventricular myocyte upstroke (phase 0): rapid sodium influx through voltage-gated fast sodium channels.","Recall that these channels inactivate with sustained depolarization and recover only at a negative resting potential.","A less negative resting potential leaves fewer channels available, so the upstroke is smaller and slower, and conduction slows."],"why":"The rapid upstroke of the ventricular myocyte action potential comes from sodium rushing in through voltage-gated fast sodium channels. These channels close an inactivation gate when the membrane stays depolarized and need a negative resting potential to reset. When extracellular potassium rises in ischemic tissue, the resting potential becomes less negative, many sodium channels stay inactivated, and phase 0 becomes smaller and slower. Slow conduction in the border zone sets up reentry, which is why ventricular fibrillation is a common cause of early death after MI.","whyNot":["Calcium entry through L-type channels holds up the plateau and triggers contraction. It does not set how fast the upstroke spreads in ventricular myocytes.","Correct.","The funny current drives spontaneous phase 4 depolarization in SA node cells. Working ventricular myocytes have a stable phase 4 and rely on sodium channels for their upstroke.","Potassium efflux in phase 3 repolarizes the cell and sets the refractory period. It has no role in the speed of the upstroke."],"study":{"topics":["Ventricular myocyte action potential phases","Sodium channel inactivation"],"comps":["CV-5","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C02","system":"CV","dx":"Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia with coronary atherosclerosis and stable angina","codes":["CV-18-I4","CV-18-I3","CV-13"],"case":"A 32-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of chest pressure that occurs when he climbs stairs and resolves with rest within 5 minutes. His father had a myocardial infarction at age 44 years. Physical examination shows firm nodules on both Achilles tendons. Fasting serum studies show LDL cholesterol 310 mg/dL (desirable below 100 mg/dL), HDL cholesterol 44 mg/dL (40 or higher), and triglycerides 120 mg/dL (below 150 mg/dL). An exercise stress test shows ischemic ST-segment depression. Coronary angiography shows 75% atherosclerotic narrowing of the proximal right coronary artery. Genetic testing shows one pathogenic variant in the LDL receptor gene and one normal copy. Diagnosis: heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia with coronary atherosclerosis and stable angina.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q1","from":[16],"stem":"His LDL cholesterol is markedly increased, but his triglyceride concentration is normal. Which of the following is the direct consequence of his LDL receptor defect?","options":["The liver makes and secretes more VLDL than usual","Lipoprotein lipase in capillaries is less active","Less LDL is taken up and cleared by hepatocytes","HDL can no longer carry cholesterol back to the liver"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-I4","obj":"CV-18-I.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the pattern: isolated high LDL with normal triglycerides.","Recall that most LDL leaves the blood by receptor-mediated endocytosis into hepatocytes through the LDL receptor.","A defective receptor means LDL stays in the blood longer and its level rises."],"why":"Hepatocytes remove most LDL from the blood by binding its apolipoprotein B-100 to the LDL receptor and taking it in by receptor-mediated endocytosis. In familial hypercholesterolemia, an autosomal dominant defect in the LDL receptor slows this clearance, so plasma LDL rises, often 2 to 3 times normal in heterozygotes. The excess LDL deposits in tendons as xanthomas and in arteries as atheroma, which explains his early coronary disease.","whyNot":["More VLDL output would raise fasting triglycerides as well as LDL. His triglycerides are normal, and an LDL receptor defect does not drive VLDL synthesis.","Low lipoprotein lipase activity causes high triglycerides from retained chylomicrons and VLDL (familial chylomicronemia). It does not raise LDL alone.","Correct.","Reverse cholesterol transport by HDL uses other receptors, such as scavenger receptor B1. His HDL is normal."],"study":{"topics":["LDL receptor and receptor-mediated endocytosis","Familial hypercholesterolemia"],"comps":["CV-18-I4","CV-13","CV-14"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q2","from":[14],"stem":"He begins statin therapy, and 8 weeks later his LDL cholesterol concentration has decreased by 45%. Which of the following best explains this decrease?","options":["It blocks 7-alpha-hydroxylase, so the liver converts more cholesterol to bile","It blocks HMG-CoA reductase, so his normal allele makes more LDL receptors","It blocks cholesterol esterase in the gut, so less dietary cholesterol is absorbed","It blocks lipoprotein lipase, so less LDL is formed from VLDL in the blood"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the statin target: HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis (HMG-CoA to mevalonate).","Follow the effect in the hepatocyte: less cholesterol inside the cell activates SREBP, which turns on LDL receptor gene transcription.","Apply it to him: his one normal LDL receptor allele can still respond, so LDL clearance rises."],"why":"Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate in hepatic cholesterol synthesis. When hepatocyte cholesterol falls, the transcription factor SREBP increases expression of the LDL receptor gene, so the liver pulls more LDL out of the blood. In heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia the normal allele can be upregulated, so statins work well. Patients with two nonfunctional LDL receptor alleles respond poorly for this reason.","whyNot":["7-alpha-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis. Blocking it would leave more cholesterol in the liver, and statins do not target it.","Correct.","Reducing intestinal cholesterol absorption is how ezetimibe works, through the NPC1L1 transporter. Statins act on hepatic cholesterol synthesis.","Blocking lipoprotein lipase would raise triglycerides and is not how statins work. Statins inhibit the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis."],"study":{"topics":["HMG-CoA reductase and SREBP regulation","How statins lower LDL"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I4"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q3","from":[15],"stem":"Which of the following best describes the lipoprotein particle and the route by which dietary triglyceride leaves his small intestine after a fatty meal?","options":["Chylomicrons, through lacteals and the thoracic duct","Chylomicrons, through the portal vein to the liver","VLDL particles, through lacteals and the thoracic duct","LDL particles, through the portal vein to the liver"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-10","obj":"CV-10.2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the particle the enterocyte builds for dietary triglyceride: the chylomicron, with apolipoprotein B-48.","Recall that chylomicrons are too large for blood capillaries and enter the central lacteal of each villus.","Trace the lymph: lacteals, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct, and the left venous angle."],"why":"Enterocytes package absorbed dietary triglyceride and cholesterol into chylomicrons containing apolipoprotein B-48. Chylomicrons are too large to pass into blood capillaries, so they enter the lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. In the blood, lipoprotein lipase removes their triglyceride for muscle and fat, and the remnants are taken up by the liver.","whyNot":["Correct.","Chylomicrons are too large to enter intestinal capillaries, so they do not travel in the portal vein. Short- and medium-chain fatty acids are the dietary fats that go by the portal route.","VLDL is made by the liver to export triglyceride the liver itself synthesizes. It does not carry dietary fat out of the intestine.","LDL is formed in the blood from VLDL after its triglyceride is removed. It does not carry dietary fat from the gut."],"study":{"topics":["Chylomicron formation and lymphatic transport","Lipoprotein classes"],"comps":["CV-10","CV-13"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q4","from":[25],"stem":"Macrophages in his coronary plaque are filled with cholesterol despite his defective LDL receptors. Which of the following best explains how cholesterol accumulated in his plaque?","options":["Oxidized intimal LDL is taken up by macrophage scavenger receptors","Adventitial fibroblasts multiply, and LDL enters the wall from the vasa vasorum","Medial smooth muscle calcifies, and the stiff wall traps LDL from the plasma within it","Fibrin forms on normal endothelium, and LDL is trapped in the clot as it organizes"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that atherosclerosis begins with endothelial injury or dysfunction in the intima.","LDL enters the intima and is oxidized; monocytes enter and become macrophages.","Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors, which are separate from the LDL receptor, so foam cells form even in familial hypercholesterolemia."],"why":"Atherosclerosis starts when injured or dysfunctional endothelium lets LDL into the intima, where it is oxidized. Recruited monocytes become macrophages that take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors, which do not depend on the LDL receptor and are not turned off by cholesterol in the cell. Engorged macrophages become foam cells, and smooth muscle cells migrate from the media into the intima and lay down collagen. The result is a fibrous cap over a lipid core. High LDL speeds up every step.","whyNot":["Correct.","Atherosclerosis is a disease of the intima. LDL enters from the lumen through injured endothelium, and the adventitia is not the site of plaque.","Calcification of the media without lipid or inflammation is Monckeberg medial sclerosis. It does not narrow the lumen or form foam cells.","Thrombus usually forms on a plaque after its cap is disrupted. It does not start atherosclerosis on healthy endothelium."],"study":{"topics":["Atherosclerosis pathogenesis","Scavenger receptors and foam cells"],"comps":["CV-18-I3","CV-2","CV-18-I4"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C03","system":"CV","dx":"Rheumatic mitral stenosis with a past subacute endocarditis and current acute Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis","codes":["CV-18-D5","CV-18-D3","CV-18-K1"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of a 4-day history of fever. At age 9 years, she had fever, migratory polyarthritis, and a new heart murmur about 3 weeks after an untreated sore throat. She has known mitral stenosis with a valve area of 1.3 cm2 (normal 4 to 6 cm2). Three years ago, 2 weeks after a tooth extraction, she had 6 weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue, and a vegetation was found on her mitral valve; that infection was cured. She currently injects heroin. Her temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F). Physical examination shows a new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases in intensity with inspiration. Blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Diagnosis: rheumatic mitral stenosis, a past subacute endocarditis, and acute S. aureus endocarditis.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q1","from":[19],"stem":"Which of the following organisms most likely caused the pharyngitis that preceded her childhood illness?","options":["Streptococcus agalactiae (group B)","Streptococcus pyogenes (group A)","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-D5","obj":"CV-18-D.8","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the childhood picture as acute rheumatic fever: carditis and migrating polyarthritis a few weeks after pharyngitis.","Recall that rheumatic fever follows throat infection with group A beta-hemolytic streptococci.","Name the organism: Streptococcus pyogenes."],"why":"Acute rheumatic fever is a delayed, nonsuppurative complication of pharyngitis caused by group A beta-hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes). It usually starts about 2 to 4 weeks after the sore throat, most often in children aged 5 to 15. Repeated or severe attacks scar the valves, and the mitral valve is affected most often. Prompt antibiotic treatment of streptococcal pharyngitis prevents rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["Group B streptococci colonize the vagina and cause sepsis and meningitis in newborns. They do not cause pharyngitis followed by rheumatic fever.","Correct.","Pneumococcus causes pneumonia, otitis media, and meningitis. It is not linked to rheumatic fever.","S. aureus can cause many infections, but it does not trigger rheumatic fever. Her current endocarditis is a separate, later problem."],"study":{"topics":["Acute rheumatic fever","Group A streptococcus"],"comps":["CV-18-D5","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q2","from":[22],"stem":"No bacteria were present in her heart during the childhood illness, yet her mitral valve was damaged and later scarred. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this valve damage?","options":["Circulating immune complexes deposit in the valve and fix complement","IgE on mast cells triggers degranulation within the valve tissue","Streptococci invade the valve leaflets and form a small abscess","Antibodies to streptococcal M protein bind valve proteins"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","obj":"CV-16.2","dok":2,"approach":["Note that the valve is sterile, so the damage is immune mediated.","Recall molecular mimicry: streptococcal M protein resembles proteins in heart valves and myocardium.","Antibodies made against the bacterium bind the patient's own tissue, which is type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"Rheumatic fever is an example of molecular mimicry. Antibodies made against the streptococcal M protein cross-react with similar proteins in the heart, including valve glycoproteins and myosin, which is classified as type II (antibody-mediated) hypersensitivity. CD4 T cells that react to the same antigens add to the inflammation. Aschoff bodies in the myocardium are the characteristic lesion, and repeated attacks leave the mitral valve thick, fused, and stenotic.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition is type III hypersensitivity. After streptococcal infection it explains poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, not rheumatic carditis.","IgE and mast cell degranulation is type I hypersensitivity, as in anaphylaxis and allergic asthma. It does not scar heart valves.","Rheumatic fever is nonsuppurative: the valve is sterile. Bacteria growing on a valve would be infective endocarditis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular mimicry","Hypersensitivity types I to IV"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D5"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q3","from":[20],"stem":"Her endocarditis 3 years ago began 2 weeks after a tooth extraction and followed an indolent 6-week course on her scarred mitral valve. Which of the following best explains that infection?","options":["Enterococcus faecalis from the bowel reached the valve after a colonoscopy","Candida albicans from a central line grew on the surface of the valve","Oral viridans streptococci adhered to fibrin on the scarred valve","Staphylococcus aureus from the skin invaded the normal leaflets of the valve"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D3","obj":"CV-18-D.5","dok":3,"approach":["Note the clues: a slow, low-grade illness (subacute), a previously damaged valve, and recent dental work.","Recall that oral viridans streptococci enter the blood during dental procedures.","They adhere to the sterile fibrin and platelet deposits that form on damaged valve endothelium, using surface dextran and adhesins."],"why":"Viridans streptococci, such as Streptococcus sanguinis and Streptococcus mutans, are normal mouth flora that enter the blood during dental work. They are low in virulence, so they usually infect valves that are already damaged, where small sterile fibrin and platelet deposits give them a place to attach. Dextran on their surface helps them stick. The illness develops over weeks to months, which is the classic subacute endocarditis pattern.","whyNot":["Enterococci cause subacute endocarditis after gastrointestinal or genitourinary procedures. Her infection followed dental work.","Fungal endocarditis is linked to central lines, prosthetic valves, and injection drug use. She had none of these at that time, and the trigger was a tooth extraction.","Correct.","S. aureus is highly virulent, can infect normal valves, and causes an acute illness over days. Her infection was slow and sat on a valve that was already damaged."],"study":{"topics":["Acute and subacute endocarditis","Viridans streptococci"],"comps":["CV-18-D3","CV-18-K1","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q4","from":[24],"stem":"Which of the following valves is most likely the site of her current vegetation?","options":["Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-K1","obj":"CV-18-K.1","dok":3,"approach":["Note that injected bacteria enter the venous blood and reach the right heart valves first.","A holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration is right-sided regurgitation.","Inspiration raises venous return to the right heart, so right-sided murmurs get louder. The valve is the tricuspid."],"why":"In people who inject drugs, bacteria and particles enter the veins and strike the tricuspid valve first, and S. aureus is the most common organism. Damage to the leaflets causes tricuspid regurgitation, a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border. Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, which increases venous return to the right heart and makes right-sided murmurs louder. Pieces of a tricuspid vegetation travel to the lungs as septic emboli.","whyNot":["Pulmonary valve endocarditis is rare. Its murmurs are heard at the left upper sternal border.","Correct.","Aortic regurgitation gives a diastolic murmur along the left sternal border, and left-sided murmurs do not get louder with inspiration.","The mitral valve is the most common site of endocarditis in people who do not inject drugs. A mitral regurgitation murmur is loudest at the apex and does not increase with inspiration."],"study":{"topics":["Endocarditis in people who inject drugs","Murmurs and their locations"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-4"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C04","system":"CV","dx":"Hypertensive heart disease with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, hypertensive nephrosclerosis, and an aneurysm of the ascending aorta and arch","codes":["CV-18-A2","CV-18-I1"],"case":"A 71-year-old man with 30 years of poorly controlled hypertension comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of hoarseness and difficulty swallowing solid food. Blood pressure is 172/98 mm Hg. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.7 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3 mg/dL) with mild proteinuria. Echocardiography shows a non-dilated left ventricle with a wall thickness of 15 mm (normal 6 to 11 mm), an ejection fraction of 62% (normal 55 to 70%), and impaired relaxation in diastole. CT shows a 6.2 cm aneurysm of the ascending aorta and aortic arch. Diagnosis: hypertensive heart disease with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, hypertensive nephrosclerosis, and an aneurysm of the ascending aorta and arch.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q1","from":[11],"stem":"Which of the following processes best explains his echocardiographic findings?","options":["Dilated cardiomyopathy with failing contraction","Eccentric hypertrophy from chronic volume overload","Concentric hypertrophy from pressure overload","Constrictive pericarditis that limits filling"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the load: long-standing hypertension raises afterload, which is a pressure overload.","Apply the Laplace relationship: wall stress rises with pressure and falls as the wall thickens.","Myocytes add sarcomeres in parallel, the wall thickens without dilation, and the stiff wall relaxes poorly."],"why":"Chronic hypertension forces the left ventricle to generate high pressure. By the Laplace relationship, wall stress rises with pressure and radius and falls with wall thickness, so myocytes add sarcomeres in parallel and the wall thickens to bring stress back down. This is concentric hypertrophy. The thick, stiff wall fills poorly in diastole, so ejection fraction stays normal at first while relaxation is impaired. Over many years, the hypertrophied heart can dilate and fail.","whyNot":["Dilated cardiomyopathy gives a large, thin-walled ventricle with a low ejection fraction. His ejection fraction is normal and the cavity is not dilated.","Volume overload, as in mitral or aortic regurgitation, adds sarcomeres in series. The chamber dilates and the wall is not much thicker than normal.","Correct.","A stiff pericardium limits filling, but the myocardium itself is not thickened. His problem is in the ventricular wall."],"study":{"topics":["Concentric and eccentric hypertrophy","Law of Laplace"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-6"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q2","from":[6],"stem":"Compression of which of the following pairs of structures by the aneurysm most likely explains his hoarseness and dysphagia?","options":["Thoracic duct and left sympathetic trunk","Left brachiocephalic vein and left vagus nerve","Left phrenic nerve and trachea","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve and esophagus"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.4","dok":2,"approach":["Link hoarseness to the vocal cords, which are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerves.","Recall that the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch next to the ligamentum arteriosum.","Link trouble swallowing to the esophagus, which lies just to the right of the arch."],"why":"The left vagus nerve gives off the left recurrent laryngeal nerve as it crosses the aortic arch. The nerve hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum and climbs back up in the groove between the trachea and esophagus to supply most of the muscles of the larynx. An arch aneurysm can stretch or compress it, causing left vocal cord paralysis and hoarseness. The esophagus lies right next to the arch and the descending aorta, so a large aneurysm can also compress it and cause dysphagia.","whyNot":["Compression of the sympathetic trunk can cause Horner syndrome, and duct injury can cause chylothorax. Neither causes hoarseness or trouble swallowing.","A compressed brachiocephalic vein causes swelling of the left arm and face. It does not explain trouble swallowing solid food.","The phrenic nerve controls the diaphragm, so compressing it paralyzes a hemidiaphragm. It does not cause hoarseness, and tracheal compression causes cough and stridor, not trouble swallowing.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Relations of the aortic arch","Recurrent laryngeal nerve course"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q3","from":[27],"stem":"His increased serum creatinine concentration and mild proteinuria are attributed to long-standing hypertension without a malignant phase. Which of the following changes is most likely present in the walls of his small renal arterioles?","options":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis","Hyperplastic (onion-skin) arteriolosclerosis","Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A2","obj":"CV-18-A.3","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: years of moderate (benign) hypertension, not a hypertensive emergency.","Recall that chronic pressure pushes plasma proteins into arteriolar walls and stimulates extra matrix.","The result is pink, glassy wall thickening that narrows the lumen: hyaline arteriolosclerosis, which causes nephrosclerosis."],"why":"In long-standing benign hypertension, and also in diabetes, plasma proteins leak through injured endothelium into the walls of small arterioles, and smooth muscle cells lay down extra basement membrane. The wall becomes thick, pink, and glassy on H&E stain, and the lumen narrows. In the kidney, the resulting ischemia shrinks glomeruli and tubules. This is benign nephrosclerosis, which causes a slow rise in creatinine and mild proteinuria.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomatous inflammation that destroys vessel walls is seen in vasculitis, such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis. It is not caused by hypertension.","Concentric layers of smooth muscle and basement membrane, often with fibrinoid necrosis, are seen in malignant hypertension, usually with pressures above 180/120 mm Hg and acute organ damage.","Monckeberg sclerosis is calcification in the media of medium-sized muscular arteries in older people. It does not narrow the lumen or damage the kidney."],"study":{"topics":["Arteriolosclerosis types","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q4","from":[3],"stem":"Three weeks later, he develops sudden tearing chest pain and collapses. Blood pressure is 76/50 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention and muffled heart sounds. The ascending aorta has dissected and ruptured. Into which of the following spaces is blood most likely accumulating?","options":["Left pleural cavity","Superior mediastinum","Posterior mediastinum","Pericardial cavity"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","obj":"CV-3.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize Beck triad: low blood pressure, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds point to cardiac tamponade.","Recall that the ascending aorta begins inside the pericardial sac.","Blood from an ascending aortic rupture fills the pericardial cavity, between the parietal and visceral layers of serous pericardium."],"why":"The pericardial cavity is the thin space between the parietal and visceral layers of serous pericardium. Because the fibrous pericardium does not stretch quickly, sudden bleeding into this space raises its pressure and squeezes the heart, especially the thin-walled right side, so the ventricles cannot fill in diastole. Stroke volume and blood pressure fall, venous pressure rises, and the fluid muffles the heart sounds. The same picture follows a stab wound to the heart or rupture of the free wall after a myocardial infarction.","whyNot":["Rupture of the descending thoracic aorta often bleeds into the left pleural cavity. That causes hypotension and a hemothorax, but not distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds.","The aortic arch and great vessels lie in the superior mediastinum, outside the pericardial sac. Bleeding there does not produce Beck triad.","Bleeding there collects around the descending aorta and esophagus, outside the pericardial sac, so it does not cause tamponade. His rupture is in the ascending aorta, which lies inside the sac.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pericardium layers","Cardiac tamponade and Beck triad"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F2","CV-18-I2"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C05","system":"CV","dx":"Tetralogy of Fallot","codes":["CV-18-G4","CV-1"],"case":"A 4-month-old girl is brought to the physician because of episodes of perioral cyanosis during crying and feeding. She was born at term after a normal pregnancy. Physical examination shows a harsh systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border. Oxygen saturation is 82% on room air (normal 95 to 100%). Chest x-ray shows a boot-shaped heart with reduced lung markings. Echocardiography shows a large ventricular septal defect, an aorta that sits over the defect, narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract below the pulmonary valve, and right ventricular hypertrophy. Diagnosis: tetralogy of Fallot.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q1","from":[29],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains her cyanosis?","options":["Two parallel circuits with no mixing because the great arteries are switched","Right-to-left shunt across the VSD because flow to the lungs is obstructed","Left-to-right shunt across the VSD because left ventricular pressure is higher","Left-to-right shunt across a patent ductus because aortic pressure is higher"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-G4","obj":"CV-18-G.5","dok":2,"approach":["Note the low saturation with reduced lung markings: less blood is reaching the lungs.","Identify the obstruction: right ventricular outflow narrowing raises right ventricular pressure.","With a large VSD, deoxygenated blood takes the easier path into the overriding aorta, a right-to-left shunt."],"why":"In tetralogy of Fallot, narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract makes it hard for blood to reach the lungs. Right ventricular pressure rises to match or exceed left ventricular pressure, so deoxygenated blood crosses the large VSD into the overriding aorta. This right-to-left shunt sends unoxygenated blood to the body and causes cyanosis. The more severe the outflow obstruction, the larger the shunt and the worse the cyanosis.","whyNot":["Parallel circuits are the problem in transposition of the great arteries, where the aorta arises from the right ventricle. Her echo shows an overriding aorta, not transposition.","Correct.","A left-to-right shunt sends oxygenated blood back to the lungs. It increases lung markings and does not cause cyanosis early on, as in an isolated VSD.","Flow from the aorta into the pulmonary artery through a patent ductus returns oxygenated blood to the lungs. It does not cause cyanosis unless pulmonary hypertension later reverses it."],"study":{"topics":["Tetralogy of Fallot","Right-to-left and left-to-right shunts"],"comps":["CV-18-G4","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q2","from":[],"stem":"During an episode of severe cyanosis, her mother draws the infant's knees up to her chest, and the cyanosis improves. Older children with this defect squat for the same reason. Which of the following best explains this improvement?","options":["It relaxes the outflow tract muscle, so pulmonary resistance falls","It raises systemic resistance, so less blood crosses the VSD","It lowers venous return, so the right ventricle ejects less blood","It lowers systemic vascular resistance, so more blood enters the aorta"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-G4","obj":"CV-18-G.5","dok":3,"approach":["See the VSD as a fork: right ventricular blood goes to the lungs or into the aorta, whichever path resists less.","Flexing the legs kinks the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance.","Higher aortic resistance pushes more right ventricular blood through the narrowed outflow tract to the lungs, so oxygenation improves."],"why":"The direction of flow across her VSD depends on the balance between resistance to pulmonary flow and resistance to systemic flow. Pulling the knees to the chest, or squatting, compresses the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance. With a higher pressure in the aorta, less deoxygenated blood crosses from the right ventricle into the aorta and more goes to the lungs. Anything that lowers systemic resistance, such as exercise or a hot bath, does the opposite.","whyNot":["Spasm of the outflow tract can trigger a spell, but leg position does not relax it. The benefit comes from the systemic side of the circulation.","Correct.","Squatting tends to increase venous return from the legs. Less right ventricular filling would also not send more blood to the lungs.","Lower systemic resistance makes the aorta an easier path for right ventricular blood, which increases the right-to-left shunt and worsens cyanosis."],"study":{"topics":["Tet spells and squatting","Systemic vascular resistance and shunt direction"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-G4"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q3","from":[1],"stem":"Before surgery, a catheter is advanced from the inferior vena cava into the right atrium and then directly into the hypertrophied ventricle with the narrowed outflow tract. Which of the following valves does the catheter cross?","options":["Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","obj":"CV-4.1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the chamber: the hypertrophied chamber with a narrowed outflow tract in tetralogy of Fallot is the right ventricle.","The catheter is moving from the right atrium to the right ventricle.","Name the right atrioventricular valve: the tricuspid valve."],"why":"The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, with three leaflets held by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles of the right ventricle. A venous catheter passes from the inferior vena cava into the right atrium and through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. In tetralogy of Fallot, right ventricular pressure equals left ventricular and aortic pressure because the large VSD joins the two ventricles and the outflow tract is narrow.","whyNot":["Correct.","The aortic valve guards the exit from the left ventricle. It is reached from the arterial side or by crossing the septum.","The mitral valve lies between the left atrium and left ventricle. A catheter from the inferior vena cava would reach it only by crossing the atrial septum.","The pulmonary valve guards the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. The catheter would cross it only after leaving the right ventricle."],"study":{"topics":["Heart valves and chambers","Right heart catheterization path"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q4","from":[5],"stem":"Before birth, oxygenated blood from the placenta entered her right atrium through the inferior vena cava and passed directly to the left atrium. Which of the following correctly names this structure and the reason it normally closes after birth?","options":["Ductus arteriosus; rising oxygen and falling prostaglandin E2 constrict it","Foramen ovale; falling oxygen levels constrict the septum primum","Foramen ovale; left atrial pressure rises above right atrial pressure","Ductus venosus; flow in the umbilical vein stops when the cord is clamped"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","obj":"CV-1.6","dok":2,"approach":["Trace fetal blood: inferior vena cava to right atrium, then across the atrial septum to the left atrium.","Name the opening in the atrial septum: the foramen ovale.","At birth, lung blood flow rises, more blood returns to the left atrium, and left atrial pressure pushes the septum primum flap shut."],"why":"In the fetus, the crista dividens (the lower edge of the septum secundum) directs oxygen-rich inferior vena cava blood through the foramen ovale into the left atrium, so it bypasses the airless lungs. At birth the lungs expand, pulmonary resistance falls, pulmonary venous return to the left atrium rises, and placental flow into the right side stops. Left atrial pressure then exceeds right atrial pressure and presses the septum primum against the septum secundum. The opening later seals to form the fossa ovalis.","whyNot":["This is how the ductus arteriosus closes, but the ductus joins the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. It does not carry blood between the atria.","The foramen ovale is the correct structure, but it closes by pressure, with no muscle to constrict. Oxygen level is the trigger for closing the ductus arteriosus.","Correct.","The ductus venosus carries umbilical vein blood past the liver into the inferior vena cava. It does not connect the two atria."],"study":{"topics":["Fetal circulation","Changes in the circulation at birth"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G3"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C06","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy (dilated) with left and then right heart failure","codes":["CV-18-K2","CV-18-E1","CV-18-B"],"case":"A 47-year-old woman who grew up in a mud-walled house in rural Bolivia comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of worsening dyspnea with paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea and a 1-month history of leg swelling. Physical examination shows bibasilar crackles, an S3 gallop, jugular venous distention, tender hepatomegaly, and pitting edema of the legs. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25% (normal 55 to 70%) and an estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 58 mm Hg (normal 15 to 30 mm Hg). Coronary angiography is normal. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0 g/dL). Two serologic tests for Trypanosoma cruzi are positive. Diagnosis: chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy with heart failure.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q1","from":[21],"stem":"By which of the following routes was Trypanosoma cruzi most likely transmitted to her during childhood?","options":["Tsetse fly saliva injected during a bite","Sand fly saliva injected during a bite","Triatomine bug feces rubbed into a bite","Anopheles mosquito saliva injected during a bite"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","obj":"CV-17.3","dok":2,"approach":["Link the setting, a rural home with mud walls in Latin America, to the insects that live in wall cracks.","Name the vector of Trypanosoma cruzi: triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bugs.","Recall the route: the bug defecates while feeding, and the person rubs infected feces into the bite or the conjunctiva."],"why":"Trypanosoma cruzi is carried by triatomine (reduviid or kissing) bugs that hide in cracks in mud walls and thatched roofs and feed at night. The parasite is in the bug's feces, not its saliva, and enters through the bite wound or mucous membranes when the person scratches or rubs the area. Years later, about 20 to 30% of infected people develop chronic disease, most often a dilated cardiomyopathy, with megaesophagus or megacolon in some.","whyNot":["Tsetse flies in Africa carry Trypanosoma brucei, which causes African sleeping sickness. They do not live in the Americas.","Sand flies transmit Leishmania species, which cause cutaneous and visceral leishmaniasis.","Correct.","Female Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium species, which cause malaria."],"study":{"topics":["Chagas disease","Arthropod vectors of protozoa"],"comps":["CV-18-K2","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q2","from":[12],"stem":"Her dyspnea preceded the leg edema, jugular venous distention, and hepatomegaly by several months. Which of the following mechanisms best explains how her left ventricular failure led to these right-sided findings?","options":["Pulmonary venous congestion raised right ventricular afterload","Occlusion of the right coronary artery caused a right ventricular infarct","Low plasma oncotic pressure from protein loss let fluid leave the capillaries","A stiff, thickened pericardium limited filling of the right ventricle"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B","obj":"CV-18-B.2","dok":3,"approach":["Start on the left: a failing left ventricle raises left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure, which causes her breathlessness and crackles.","Follow the pressure backward: pulmonary artery pressure rises (58 mm Hg), raising right ventricular afterload.","The right ventricle fails, so systemic venous pressure rises, causing raised jugular venous pressure, liver congestion, and leg edema."],"why":"Left ventricular failure is the most common cause of right ventricular failure. When the left ventricle cannot empty, left atrial and pulmonary venous pressures rise, and the pulmonary arteries must work at higher pressure to push blood through the lungs. The thin-walled right ventricle tolerates this afterload poorly and eventually fails. Systemic venous pressure then rises, causing raised jugular venous pressure, a congested liver, and dependent edema.","whyNot":["Correct.","Her coronary angiogram is normal, so there was no right coronary occlusion.","Her albumin is normal. Low oncotic pressure, as in nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis, causes edema without raised jugular venous pressure.","Constrictive pericarditis limits filling of both ventricles and would not explain a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25%."],"study":{"topics":["Left-sided and right-sided heart failure","Pulmonary hypertension from left heart disease"],"comps":["CV-18-B","CV-18-A1","CV-15"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q3","from":[7],"stem":"In a healthy heart, an increase in end-diastolic volume increases stroke volume, but her dilated ventricle gains little stroke volume from the same increase in filling. Which of the following is the main cellular basis of this length-tension relationship in a normal ventricle?","options":["Stretch shortens the sarcomeres, so more myosin heads bind to the thin filaments","Stretch releases norepinephrine from cardiac nerve endings, which raises contractility","Stretch lowers wall tension, so afterload falls and the ventricle empties more fully","Stretch sets better filament overlap and makes troponin C more sensitive to calcium"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","obj":"CV-5.5","dok":3,"approach":["Name the principle: the Frank-Starling mechanism, where preload sets stroke volume.","Go to the sarcomere: stretch moves resting sarcomere length toward the optimum and increases the calcium sensitivity of the myofilaments.","Apply it to her: a failing ventricle has a flattened curve, so extra filling mostly raises filling pressure and congestion."],"why":"When the ventricle fills more, each myocyte is stretched. In the normal working range this brings the sarcomere closer to its optimal length, and it increases the calcium sensitivity of troponin C, so the same calcium transient produces more force. The ventricle therefore ejects more, matching output to venous return beat by beat. In a dilated, failing ventricle the curve is flat, so extra preload adds congestion with little gain in stroke volume.","whyNot":["Filling stretches the sarcomeres, it does not shorten them. In the normal working range, stretch brings sarcomere length closer to the optimum for force.","Norepinephrine raises contractility through beta-1 receptors, but stretch does not release it. The Frank-Starling effect works in a denervated, transplanted heart.","By the Laplace relationship, a larger chamber has higher wall tension for the same pressure, so afterload on each fiber rises with dilation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Frank-Starling mechanism","Length-dependent activation"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-5"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q4","from":[30],"stem":"Her low cardiac output decreases perfusion pressure in the renal afferent arterioles, and juxtaglomerular cells release renin. Which of the following sequences correctly describes the subsequent steps leading to aldosterone release?","options":["Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, then ACE converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II","Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin II, then ACE converts angiotensin II to aldosterone","Renin converts angiotensin I to angiotensinogen, then ACE converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin II","ACE converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, then renin converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the trigger: low afferent arteriolar pressure and less sodium chloride at the macula densa cause renin release.","Place each enzyme: renin cleaves liver-made angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, and ACE, mainly on lung endothelium, converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II.","Angiotensin II acts on the zona glomerulosa to release aldosterone."],"why":"Juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole release renin when renal perfusion pressure falls, when the macula densa senses less sodium chloride, or with sympathetic stimulation through beta-1 receptors. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen, made by the liver, to angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme, abundant on pulmonary capillary endothelium, converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II constricts arterioles and stimulates the zona glomerulosa to release aldosterone, which increases sodium and water retention and worsens edema in heart failure.","whyNot":["Correct.","Renin makes angiotensin I, not angiotensin II. Aldosterone is a steroid made from cholesterol in the adrenal cortex, not a product of ACE.","Angiotensinogen is the starting substrate made by the liver. It is not a product of renin.","The enzymes are reversed. Renin acts first, on angiotensinogen, and ACE acts second."],"study":{"topics":["Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Neurohormonal activation in heart failure"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-B"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C07","system":"CV","dx":"Lyme carditis with complete (third-degree) atrioventricular block","codes":["CV-18-K3","CV-7"],"case":"A 26-year-old man from Connecticut comes to the emergency department in August because of a 2-day history of lightheadedness and one episode of syncope. Five weeks ago, after hiking, he had an expanding erythematous rash with central clearing on his thigh that resolved without treatment. Pulse is 42/min and blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg. The ECG shows regular P waves at 84/min and regular, narrow QRS complexes (0.08 s; normal below 0.12 s) at 42/min, with no fixed relationship between them. Serologic testing confirms Lyme disease. Diagnosis: Lyme carditis with complete (third-degree) atrioventricular block.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q1","from":[23],"stem":"Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause of his condition?","options":["Ehrlichia chaffeensis","Treponema pallidum","Borrelia burgdorferi","Rickettsia rickettsii"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-K3","obj":"CV-18-K.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize erythema migrans: an expanding red rash with central clearing after a tick exposure.","Link it to Lyme disease, which in the northeastern United States is carried by Ixodes ticks.","Recall that early disseminated Lyme disease can cause carditis, most often as AV block."],"why":"Lyme disease is caused by the spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi, carried by Ixodes ticks in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States. Erythema migrans marks early local infection. Weeks later the organism can spread to the heart, nerves, and joints. Lyme carditis most often causes AV block that can change quickly in degree, and it usually resolves with antibiotics, sometimes after temporary pacing.","whyNot":["Ehrlichia is spread by the lone star tick and causes fever, low white cell and platelet counts, and raised liver enzymes. It does not cause erythema migrans or heart block.","T. pallidum causes syphilis. Its main cardiovascular effect is tertiary syphilitic aortitis, which damages the vasa vasorum of the ascending aorta years after infection.","Correct.","R. rickettsii causes Rocky Mountain spotted fever: fever, headache, and a rash that starts on the wrists and ankles and spreads to the trunk, including the palms and soles."],"study":{"topics":["Lyme disease stages","Spirochetes"],"comps":["CV-18-K3","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q2","from":[2],"stem":"His P waves continue at a regular rate of 84/min although none are conducted to the ventricles. Which of the following correctly names the structure that sets this atrial rate and its location?","options":["AV node, in the interatrial septum near the coronary sinus opening","Bundle of His, in the membranous part of the interventricular septum","Purkinje fibers, under the endocardium of the right ventricle","SA node, in the right atrial wall near the superior vena cava"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.1","dok":2,"approach":["Note that each P wave is an atrial depolarization, so something above the block is firing at 84/min.","Recall the fastest intrinsic pacemaker: the SA node, at about 60 to 100/min.","Locate it: in the wall of the right atrium where the superior vena cava enters, at the top of the crista terminalis."],"why":"The SA node lies in the wall of the right atrium where the superior vena cava enters, at the upper end of the crista terminalis. Its cells depolarize on their own faster than any other cardiac tissue, at about 60 to 100/min, so they set the rate of the whole heart in health. In complete heart block, the SA node keeps driving the atria normally, but its impulses cannot reach the ventricles.","whyNot":["The AV node is the structure most often blocked in Lyme carditis. It does not set the atrial rate, and its own rate is only about 40 to 60/min.","The bundle of His carries impulses from the AV node to the bundle branches. It does not drive atrial depolarization.","Purkinje fibers spread the impulse through the ventricles. Their intrinsic rate is about 20 to 40/min, and they do not drive the atria.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Conduction system anatomy","Intrinsic pacemaker rates"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q3","from":[9],"stem":"His QRS complexes are narrow (0.08 s) and occur at 42/min, independent of the P waves. Which of the following best describes the origin and spread of this escape rhythm?","options":["Atrial depolarization spreads backward from a focus in the AV node","Ventricular depolarization spreads from a focus above the bundle branch split","Ventricular depolarization spreads cell to cell from a Purkinje fiber focus","Ventricular repolarization spreads from a focus above the bundle branch split"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the QRS complex records ventricular depolarization.","A narrow QRS means the impulse entered the ventricles through the His bundle and spread rapidly through both bundle branches and the Purkinje system.","A rate of about 40 to 60/min with a narrow QRS fits a junctional escape pacemaker in the AV junction or His bundle."],"why":"The QRS complex records depolarization of the ventricles. When the impulse starts at or above the His bundle, it spreads through the fast His-Purkinje system and the QRS stays narrow, below 0.12 s. An escape focus in the ventricular muscle or distal Purkinje fibers has to spread slowly from cell to cell and gives a wide QRS. His narrow QRS at 42/min points to a junctional escape rhythm below a block in the AV node, the usual site of block in Lyme carditis.","whyNot":["Atrial depolarization is shown by the P wave. His P waves come from the SA node and are unrelated to the QRS complexes.","Correct.","An escape beat from the Purkinje fibers or ventricular muscle spreads slowly through muscle, giving a wide QRS of 0.12 s or more and a rate of about 20 to 40/min.","Ventricular repolarization is shown by the T wave. The QRS complex records ventricular depolarization."],"study":{"topics":["ECG waves and intervals","Escape rhythms"],"comps":["CV-7"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q4","from":[10],"stem":"Intravenous atropine is administered, and his P-wave rate increases from 84/min to 112/min. Atropine blocked which of the following effects of vagal acetylcholine on SA node cells?","options":["Activation of nicotinic receptors that open sodium channels in nodal cells","Activation of M2 receptors that open potassium channels and slow phase 4","Activation of M3 receptors that raise IP3 and release stored calcium","Activation of beta-1 receptors that raise cAMP and speed up phase 4"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-7","obj":"CV-7.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that atropine blocks muscarinic receptors, so the rate rise means vagal tone was slowing the SA node.","Name the cardiac muscarinic receptor: M2, coupled to Gi.","M2 activation opens acetylcholine-sensitive potassium channels and lowers cAMP, which reduces the funny current and slows phase 4 depolarization."],"why":"Vagal fibers release acetylcholine onto M2 muscarinic receptors in the SA and AV nodes. Through Gi, the beta-gamma subunits open acetylcholine-sensitive potassium channels, which make the resting potential more negative, and the alpha subunit lowers cAMP, which reduces the funny current. Both effects slow phase 4 depolarization, so the node takes longer to reach threshold and the rate falls. Atropine blocks M2 receptors and removes this brake.","whyNot":["Nicotinic receptors are found at autonomic ganglia and the neuromuscular junction. Postganglionic vagal fibers act on muscarinic receptors at the SA node.","Correct.","M3 receptors act through Gq and IP3 in smooth muscle and glands. The heart uses M2 receptors, which act through Gi.","Beta-1 activation by norepinephrine is the sympathetic effect, and it speeds the heart. Atropine does not block beta-1 receptors."],"study":{"topics":["Autonomic control of heart rate","SA node action potential"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-6"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C08","system":"CV","dx":"Wet beriberi (thiamine deficiency) with high-output heart failure, and deep vein thrombosis, in a woman with pancreatic cancer","codes":["CV-13","CV-18-I10","CV-14"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman with metastatic adenocarcinoma of the pancreas has had poor oral intake and frequent vomiting for 3 months. She now has dyspnea, leg edema, and numbness and burning pain in both feet. Her pulse is 110/min, and blood pressure is 112/48 mm Hg. Physical examination shows warm skin and bounding peripheral pulses. Echocardiography shows an end-diastolic volume of 160 mL and an end-systolic volume of 60 mL. Serum lactate is 4.1 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). She is admitted to the hospital and confined to bed. On hospital day 4, her left calf becomes swollen. Ultrasound shows a deep vein thrombosis in the left femoral and popliteal veins. She is later found to be heterozygous for factor V Leiden. Diagnosis: wet beriberi with high-output heart failure, and a deep vein thrombosis.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q1","from":[13],"stem":"Which of the following is closest to her cardiac output?","options":["1.1 L/min","6.6 L/min","11.0 L/min","17.6 L/min"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","obj":"CV-6.1","dok":2,"approach":["Find stroke volume: end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume, 160 mL minus 60 mL, which is 100 mL.","Multiply by heart rate: 100 mL times 110/min is 11,000 mL/min.","Convert and judge: 11.0 L/min is about twice the normal resting output of about 5 L/min, which fits high-output failure."],"why":"Cardiac output equals stroke volume times heart rate. Stroke volume is the end-diastolic volume minus the end-systolic volume, here 100 mL, so her output is 100 mL times 110/min, or 11.0 L/min. Her ejection fraction is 100 divided by 160, about 63%, so the pump itself is not weak. In wet beriberi, peripheral vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance and the heart must pump a very high output, which over time leads to failure with congestion.","whyNot":["This misplaces the decimal when converting units. Stroke volume is 100 mL, and 100 mL times 110/min is 11,000 mL/min, which is 11.0 L/min.","This multiplies the end-systolic volume (60 mL) by the heart rate. End-systolic volume is the blood left in the ventricle after the beat, not the amount ejected.","Correct.","This multiplies the end-diastolic volume (160 mL) by the heart rate. Not all of the end-diastolic volume is ejected."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac output and stroke volume","High-output heart failure"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-11"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q2","from":[18],"stem":"Her increased serum lactate and her cardiac and neurologic findings result from deficiency of a cofactor required by several enzymes of energy metabolism. Which of the following reactions is most directly impaired?","options":["Pyruvate to acetyl-CoA","Alanine to pyruvate by transamination","Pyruvate to oxaloacetate","Methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","obj":"CV-13.5","dok":3,"approach":["Name the missing cofactor in wet beriberi: thiamine pyrophosphate, made from vitamin B1.","List the enzymes that need it: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase, and transketolase.","Link pyruvate dehydrogenase failure to her findings: pyruvate is shunted to lactate, and aerobic ATP production falls in heart and nerve."],"why":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, which converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA so it can enter the citric acid cycle. It is also needed by alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and transketolase. Without it, pyruvate builds up and is turned into lactate, and tissues that rely on glucose oxidation, such as nerves and heart, cannot make enough ATP. Poor intake, vomiting, and alcohol use are common causes of deficiency, and giving glucose before thiamine can make it worse.","whyNot":["Correct.","Transamination needs pyridoxal phosphate from vitamin B6. B6 deficiency causes neuropathy, cheilosis, and sideroblastic anemia.","Pyruvate carboxylase needs biotin. Biotin deficiency is rare and causes dermatitis and hair loss.","This reaction needs vitamin B12 (adenosylcobalamin). B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and spinal cord disease, not high-output heart failure."],"study":{"topics":["Thiamine-dependent enzymes","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q3","from":[28],"stem":"Which of the following components of Virchow triad best explain the formation of her deep vein thrombosis?","options":["Endothelial injury and turbulent flow","Endothelial injury and venous stasis","Venous stasis and hypercoagulability","Turbulent flow and hypercoagulability"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I10","obj":"CV-18-I.8","dok":2,"approach":["List the triad: endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow (stasis or turbulence), and hypercoagulability.","Match her risks: bed rest causes venous stasis; pancreatic cancer and factor V Leiden cause hypercoagulability.","Pick the pair that matches the case."],"why":"Virchow triad names three factors that lead to thrombosis: endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow, and hypercoagulability. In the deep leg veins, slow flow from immobility is the main flow problem, because the calf muscle pump stops working. Mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas such as pancreatic cancer release procoagulant factors, including tissue factor, and factor V Leiden adds an inherited hypercoagulable state. Her deep vein thrombosis therefore reflects stasis and hypercoagulability.","whyNot":["These two drive thrombosis in arteries and the heart, such as on a ruptured plaque or an aneurysm. In the deep leg veins of an immobile patient, slow flow is the main problem.","Stasis applies to her, but nothing in the case points to injury of the vein wall, such as trauma, surgery, or a catheter. This pair also leaves out her cancer and factor V Leiden.","Correct.","Her cancer and factor V Leiden make her blood hypercoagulable, but blood in the deep leg veins of a bedbound patient moves slowly, which is stasis, not turbulence."],"study":{"topics":["Virchow triad","Deep vein thrombosis risk factors"],"comps":["CV-18-I10","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q4","from":[],"stem":"Factor V Leiden is a single amino acid substitution in factor V. Which of the following best explains how this variant increases her risk of thrombosis?","options":["Factor V is made in greater amounts by the liver","Factor V is no longer able to bind antithrombin","Factor Va binds tissue factor more tightly","Factor Va resists activated protein C"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-H6","obj":"CV-18-H.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the normal brake: activated protein C, with protein S, cleaves and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa.","Identify the variant: factor V Leiden changes arginine 506, the site where activated protein C cuts factor Va.","Factor Va stays active longer, so more thrombin is made and clots form more easily."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is a point mutation in the factor V gene that replaces arginine with glutamine at position 506, one of the sites where activated protein C cleaves factor Va. Factor Va therefore resists inactivation, keeps supporting the prothrombinase complex, and more thrombin is made. It is the most common inherited thrombophilia in people of European descent and is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Heterozygotes have a several-fold higher risk of venous thrombosis, which adds to risks such as cancer and immobility.","whyNot":["The Leiden variant does not raise the amount of factor V. Increased levels of a clotting factor describe other risks, such as high factor VIII.","Antithrombin mainly inhibits thrombin and factor Xa, and heparin speeds this up. Antithrombin deficiency is a separate inherited thrombophilia.","Tissue factor binds factor VII to start the extrinsic pathway. Factor V does not bind tissue factor.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Factor V Leiden","Protein C pathway"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C01","system":"GI","dx":"MASH cirrhosis with portal hypertension, ascites, esophageal varices, and Barrett esophagus","codes":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-C5","GI-13-J1","GI-13-G2"],"case":"A 57-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of increasing abdominal girth. He has obesity (BMI 38 kg/m2), type 2 diabetes, 20 years of heartburn, and known cirrhosis from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH). He does not drink alcohol. Physical examination shows shifting dullness, spider angiomas, and a palpable spleen tip. Laboratory studies show albumin 2.6 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), platelet count 82,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), and INR 1.8 (0.8 to 1.2). Paracentesis yields clear, straw-colored fluid with a serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) of 1.8 g/dL (1.1 or higher points to portal hypertension) and no increase in neutrophils. Screening endoscopy shows large distal esophageal varices and a tongue of salmon-colored mucosa extending 4 cm above the gastroesophageal junction. Diagnosis: MASH cirrhosis with portal hypertension, ascites, esophageal varices, and Barrett esophagus.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q1","from":[32],"stem":"His varices form where portal blood is diverted into systemic veins at the lower esophagus. Which of the following portal tributaries carries this blood backward into the esophageal submucosal veins?","options":["Left gastric vein","Superior rectal vein","Paraumbilical veins","Inferior mesenteric vein"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-13-J1","obj":"GI-13-J.1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the varices: distal esophagus.","Name the anastomosis there: portal left gastric (coronary) vein meets systemic esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system.","With portal hypertension, blood flows backward through the left gastric vein into the thin-walled submucosal veins."],"why":"The left gastric (coronary) vein drains the lesser curvature of the stomach into the portal vein, and its esophageal branches connect with esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system. When portal pressure rises, blood is pushed backward through the left gastric vein into these submucosal esophageal veins. They dilate into varices that can rupture and cause massive hematemesis, a leading cause of death in cirrhosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","The superior rectal vein is the portal side of the anorectal anastomosis with the middle and inferior rectal veins. In portal hypertension it feeds anorectal varices, not esophageal ones.","The paraumbilical veins run in the falciform ligament and connect with superficial veins of the abdominal wall. When they dilate, they produce caput medusae around the umbilicus.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains the left colon and upper rectum into the splenic vein. It does not reach the esophagus."],"study":{"topics":["Portosystemic anastomoses","Esophageal varices"],"comps":["GI-13-J1","GI-5","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q2","from":[56],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his ascites?","options":["Blocked thoracic duct releasing milky, fat-rich lymph","Tumor implants on the peritoneum leaking protein-rich fluid","Bacterial infection of the peritoneum producing an exudate","High sinusoidal pressure with low plasma albumin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-I1","obj":"GI-13-I.1","dok":2,"approach":["Read the SAAG: 1.1 g/dL or higher means portal hypertension drives the fluid.","Add the low albumin from reduced hepatic synthesis, which lowers plasma oncotic pressure.","Combine the two Starling forces: high hydrostatic pressure out, low oncotic pressure holding fluid in."],"why":"Scarring in cirrhosis raises resistance in the sinusoids, so hydrostatic pressure in the sinusoids and splanchnic capillaries rises and fluid leaks into the peritoneal cavity. The failing liver also makes less albumin, which lowers plasma oncotic pressure. Splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial volume and activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, so the kidneys retain sodium and water that keep the ascites coming back. A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more confirms that portal hypertension is the driver.","whyNot":["Chylous ascites from lymphatic disruption (lymphoma, trauma, surgery) is milky and high in triglycerides. His fluid is clear and straw colored.","Peritoneal carcinomatosis produces protein-rich fluid with a SAAG below 1.1 g/dL. His SAAG of 1.8 g/dL points to portal hypertension instead.","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is an infection of ascites that is already present, and it raises the ascitic neutrophil count. His fluid has no increase in neutrophils, and infection does not explain why the ascites formed.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ascites and SAAG","Starling forces"],"comps":["GI-13-I1","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q3","from":[46],"stem":"His INR of 1.8 reflects falling hepatic synthesis of clotting factors. Deficiency of which of the following factors prolongs the prothrombin time (PT) earliest as liver function declines?","options":["Factor VIII, made mostly by sinusoidal endothelium","Factor IX, which the prothrombin time measures directly","Factor VII, which has the shortest half-life","Factor XII, which begins the extrinsic coagulation pathway"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C2","obj":"GI-13-C.4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what the PT measures: the extrinsic and common pathways (VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen).","Recall that the liver makes these factors, including the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX, and X.","Pick the factor that falls first when synthesis drops: the one with the shortest half-life."],"why":"Hepatocytes make most clotting factors, including the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX, and X and the anticoagulant proteins C and S. When synthesis fails, the factor with the shortest plasma half-life falls first. Factor VII lasts only about 4 to 6 hours, and because it starts the extrinsic pathway, the PT and INR rise early in liver failure. Vitamin K does not fully correct this, because the problem is synthesis, not vitamin supply.","whyNot":["Factor VIII is made mainly by sinusoidal and other endothelial cells, and its level is often normal or high in cirrhosis. It is measured by the aPTT, not the PT.","Factor IX belongs to the intrinsic pathway and is measured by the aPTT. A low factor IX level prolongs the aPTT, as in hemophilia B.","Correct.","Factor XII starts the intrinsic (contact) pathway, and its deficiency prolongs the aPTT without bleeding. The extrinsic pathway begins with tissue factor and factor VII."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic synthesis of clotting factors","PT versus aPTT"],"comps":["GI-13-C2","GI-9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q4","from":[57],"stem":"Biopsy of the salmon-colored segment shows columnar epithelium with goblet cells where stratified squamous epithelium should be. This change is a precursor of which of the following tumors?","options":["Primary lymphoma of the esophageal wall","Small cell carcinoma of the esophagus","Adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus","Squamous cell carcinoma of the mid esophagus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G2","obj":"GI-13-G.2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize intestinal metaplasia (goblet cells) in the distal esophagus: Barrett esophagus from chronic reflux.","Follow the sequence: metaplasia, then dysplasia, then carcinoma.","A glandular precursor gives a glandular cancer: adenocarcinoma near the gastroesophageal junction."],"why":"Years of acid and bile reflux replace the distal esophageal squamous lining with intestinal-type columnar epithelium containing goblet cells. This metaplastic epithelium can become dysplastic and then progress to adenocarcinoma, which arises in the distal esophagus and gastroesophageal junction. Obesity adds to his risk because it promotes reflux. Patients with Barrett esophagus are followed with surveillance endoscopy to catch dysplasia early.","whyNot":["Primary esophageal lymphoma is very rare and does not arise from intestinal metaplasia.","Small cell carcinoma of the esophagus is a rare neuroendocrine tumor with no link to Barrett metaplasia.","Correct.","Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma arises from the native squamous lining, usually in the middle third. Its main risks are tobacco, alcohol, very hot drinks, and achalasia, not columnar metaplasia."],"study":{"topics":["Barrett esophagus","Esophageal cancer types"],"comps":["GI-13-G2","GI-13-K1","GI-13-G8"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C02","system":"GI","dx":"Adenocarcinoma of the head of the pancreas with obstructive jaundice","codes":["GI-13-K6","GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of itching, dark urine, pale greasy stools, and a 7-kg weight loss. She has no abdominal pain. Physical examination shows jaundice and a smooth, nontender, pear-shaped mass below the right costal margin. Laboratory studies show total bilirubin 9.4 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2) with direct bilirubin 7.9 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), alkaline phosphatase 610 U/L (40 to 120), and CA 19-9 1,250 U/mL (under 37). CT shows a 3-cm mass in the head of the pancreas, dilated common bile and pancreatic ducts, a distended gallbladder without stones, and tumor contact with the portal vein. Biopsy confirms ductal adenocarcinoma of the pancreatic head.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q1","from":[58],"stem":"Which of the following best explains why her gallbladder is enlarged and nontender?","options":["Slow tumor blockage of the distal bile duct stretches a healthy gallbladder","A stone lodged in the cystic duct inflames and swells the gallbladder wall","Years of stone-related inflammation have thickened and enlarged the gallbladder","Tumor has spread into the gallbladder wall and formed a bulky mass there"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K6","obj":"GI-13-K.9","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the sign: painless jaundice with a palpable, nontender gallbladder (Courvoisier sign).","Ask what lets a gallbladder distend: a healthy, compliant wall and slowly rising pressure below it.","Match the cause: gradual distal bile duct blockage by a pancreatic head tumor."],"why":"The pancreatic head surrounds the distal common bile duct, so a tumor there slowly narrows the duct. Bile backs up gradually, and a gallbladder with a normal, compliant wall stretches until it can be felt. Because the process is slow and not inflammatory, the gallbladder is not tender. Gallstone disease scars the wall, so obstruction from a stone rarely produces a large palpable gallbladder.","whyNot":["Correct.","A stone impacted in the cystic duct causes acute cholecystitis, which makes the gallbladder tender (Murphy sign) and causes pain and fever. Her gallbladder is painless and has no stones.","Chronic stone disease scars the gallbladder wall, so it becomes small and stiff and cannot distend. This is why a palpable, painless gallbladder points away from stones.","Pancreatic head cancer blocks the bile duct; it does not usually invade the gallbladder. CT shows a distended gallbladder without a wall mass."],"study":{"topics":["Courvoisier sign","Obstructive jaundice"],"comps":["GI-13-K6","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q2","from":[34],"stem":"At surgery, the surgeon places a finger in the omental (epiploic) foramen and the thumb on its anterior border. Which of the following groups of structures runs in this free edge of the lesser omentum?","options":["Inferior vena cava, hepatic veins, and cystic duct","Hepatic artery proper, portal vein, and bile duct","Splenic artery, splenic vein, and pancreatic duct","Left gastric artery, portal vein, and thoracic duct"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-4","obj":"GI-4.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the free edge of the lesser omentum: the hepatoduodenal ligament, the anterior border of the omental foramen.","Recall its contents, the portal triad: bile duct (right), hepatic artery proper (left), portal vein (posterior).","Check each option against that list."],"why":"The hepatoduodenal ligament is the free right edge of the lesser omentum and forms the front wall of the omental foramen. It carries the common bile duct on the right, the hepatic artery proper on the left, and the portal vein behind both. Surgeons use this relation to find the bile duct, and squeezing the ligament (the Pringle maneuver) stops inflow to the liver during bleeding.","whyNot":["The inferior vena cava lies behind the omental foramen, forming its posterior wall, and the hepatic veins drain into it above the liver. Neither runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament.","Correct.","The splenic artery and vein run along the upper and back surface of the pancreas, and the pancreatic duct runs inside the gland. None of them lies in the lesser omentum.","The left gastric artery runs in the lesser omentum along the lesser curvature, not in its free edge, and the thoracic duct is in the posterior mediastinum."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatoduodenal ligament","Omental foramen"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-4","GI-5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q3","from":[31],"stem":"If the tumor occludes the portal vein, venous pressure will rise in which of the following groups of organs?","options":["Lower limbs, pelvic walls, and bladder","Kidneys, adrenal glands, and gonads","Stomach, spleen, and intestines","Head, neck, and upper limbs"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","obj":"GI-5.3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how the portal vein forms: the superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein behind the neck of the pancreas.","List what those veins drain: the abdominal gut from stomach to upper rectum, plus the spleen, pancreas, and gallbladder.","Choose the option made only of portal territory."],"why":"The portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas, where the superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein, so pancreatic head tumors often touch or block it. It carries blood from the stomach, small and large intestines, spleen, pancreas, and gallbladder to the liver, which processes absorbed nutrients and drugs before they reach the systemic circulation. Blockage raises pressure throughout this territory and can cause splenomegaly, bowel congestion, and gastric varices.","whyNot":["The lower limbs, pelvic walls, and bladder drain through the iliac veins into the inferior vena cava.","The kidneys, adrenal glands, and gonads drain into the inferior vena cava or the left renal vein, which are systemic veins.","Correct.","The head, neck, and upper limbs drain into the superior vena cava."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous system","Portal vein formation"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q4","from":[38],"stem":"Her pale, greasy stools reflect blocked flow of bile and pancreatic juice. Normally, fat and protein entering the duodenum release a hormone that contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic acinar enzyme secretion. Which of the following cells releases this hormone?","options":["S cells of the duodenal mucosa","I cells of the duodenum and jejunum","G cells of the gastric antrum","K cells of the duodenum and upper jejunum"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the hormone from its two actions: gallbladder contraction plus acinar enzyme secretion is cholecystokinin (CCK).","Recall its trigger: fatty acids and amino acids in the duodenum.","Recall its source: I cells of the duodenum and jejunum."],"why":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is released by I cells in the duodenal and jejunal mucosa when fatty acids and amino acids arrive. It contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates acinar cells to secrete lipase, amylase, and proteases, and slows gastric emptying. In this patient the hormone still works, but the tumor blocks both ducts, so bile and enzymes cannot reach the gut and fat is poorly digested.","whyNot":["S cells release secretin when acid enters the duodenum. Secretin stimulates bicarbonate and water secretion from pancreatic duct cells, not gallbladder contraction.","Correct.","G cells release gastrin, which stimulates gastric acid secretion. Gastrin does not contract the gallbladder.","K cells release glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP), which raises insulin release after a meal. It does not drive gallbladder contraction or enzyme secretion."],"study":{"topics":["CCK","GI hormones and their cells"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-7"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C03","system":"GI","dx":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a duodenal gastrinoma","codes":["GI-13-K3","GI-13-G5"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the physician because of watery diarrhea with greasy, foul-smelling stools. He has had three duodenal ulcers in 2 years. Helicobacter pylori was found on gastric biopsy at his first endoscopy and treated, and a negative stool antigen test confirmed eradication, yet ulcers returned, including one in the proximal jejunum. Off acid-suppressing drugs, fasting serum gastrin is 1,150 pg/mL (under 100) with a gastric pH of 1.2. Endoscopy shows prominent, thickened gastric folds. Imaging finds a 1.5-cm mass in the duodenal wall. Diagnosis: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a duodenal gastrinoma.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q1","from":[39,51],"stem":"His first gastric biopsy showed curved organisms in the mucus layer of the antrum. Which of the following best describes this organism and how it survives gastric acid?","options":["Gram-positive, facultative anaerobic rod; catalase removes peroxide","Gram-positive coccus in chains; a toxin increases acid output","Gram-negative, strictly anaerobic rod; spores resist the acid","Gram-negative, microaerophilic spiral rod; urease makes ammonia"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: curved rods in the antral mucus layer of an ulcer patient are Helicobacter pylori.","Recall its features: gram negative, microaerophilic, helical or spiral rod with flagella.","Recall its acid survival tool: urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, buffering the acid around it."],"why":"Helicobacter pylori is a gram-negative, microaerophilic, flagellated spiral rod that lives in the mucus layer over the gastric epithelium, mostly in the antrum. Its urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, and the ammonia neutralizes acid in its immediate surroundings while also injuring epithelial cells. Flagella let it swim through mucus, and the CagA and VacA proteins add to tissue damage. The same urease is the basis of the urea breath test and the rapid urease biopsy test.","whyNot":["Helicobacter pylori is gram negative, not gram positive. It does make catalase, but catalase protects against neutrophil peroxide, not against gastric acid.","Helicobacter pylori is a curved rod, not a coccus, and it does not survive by raising acid output. Gram-positive cocci in chains are streptococci.","Helicobacter pylori is microaerophilic, not a strict anaerobe, and it does not form spores. Spore formation is a feature of gram-positive rods such as Clostridium and Bacillus.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Helicobacter pylori virulence","Gram-negative spiral organisms"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-G5","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q2","from":[43],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his greasy, foul-smelling stools?","options":["Excess acid in the duodenum inactivates pancreatic lipase","Gastrin acts on enterocytes and drives chloride secretion","Gastrin overstimulates the gallbladder and causes bile acid loss","Gastrin blocks CCK receptors on pancreatic acinar cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-K3","obj":"GI-13-K.4","dok":3,"approach":["Start with the cause: autonomous gastrin secretion drives huge acid output.","Follow the acid: it overwhelms pancreatic bicarbonate, so the duodenal pH stays low.","Apply enzyme pH limits: lipase is inactivated in acid, and bile acids precipitate, so fat is not digested."],"why":"The gastrinoma releases gastrin continuously, so parietal cells secrete large volumes of acid. This acid overwhelms the bicarbonate from the pancreas, and the duodenal pH stays low. Pancreatic lipase is inactivated at low pH, and bile acids precipitate, so fewer micelles form and fat is poorly digested and absorbed. The result is steatorrhea, alongside the watery diarrhea caused by the sheer volume of gastric secretion.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gastrin does not stimulate intestinal chloride secretion. Secretory diarrhea from chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera toxin and VIP-secreting tumors, and it does not cause fat malabsorption.","Gastrin is a weak stimulus for gallbladder contraction at most. Bile acid diarrhea follows ileal disease or resection, which he does not have.","Gastrin and CCK share a receptor family, but gastrin does not block acinar CCK receptors. Enzyme secretion is still present; the enzymes are inactivated after they reach the acidic duodenum."],"study":{"topics":["Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","Fat digestion and pH"],"comps":["GI-13-K3","GI-7"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q3","from":[37],"stem":"Gastrin is the main hormonal stimulus of gastric acid secretion. Besides acting on parietal cells directly, it raises acid output by making a neighboring cell release a paracrine mediator that binds H2 receptors on parietal cells. Which of the following cells releases this mediator?","options":["D cell of the oxyntic mucosa","Chief cell at the gland base","Mucous neck cell of the gland","Enterochromaffin-like cell"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.3","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the mediator from the receptor: H2 receptors bind histamine.","Name the gastric cell that releases histamine: the enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cell.","Link it to gastrin: gastrin binds CCK2 receptors on ECL cells and triggers histamine release."],"why":"Gastrin from antral G cells, or here from the tumor, binds CCK2 (CCK-B) receptors on parietal cells and on enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells. ECL cells respond by releasing histamine, which binds H2 receptors on nearby parietal cells and raises cAMP, strongly increasing H+/K+-ATPase activity. This is why H2 blockers reduce gastrin-driven acid secretion. Long-term high gastrin also makes ECL cells and parietal cells multiply.","whyNot":["D cells release somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release and acid secretion. Somatostatin does not act on H2 receptors.","Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase. They do not release histamine.","Mucous neck cells secrete mucus and are stem-like precursors. They do not release histamine.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Regulation of gastric acid secretion","Gastrin and ECL cells"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-2"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q4","from":[33],"stem":"To confirm that his thickened folds come from hyperplasia of the acid-secreting cells, the endoscopist biopsies the region where those cells are concentrated. Which of the following regions is most likely biopsied?","options":["Antrum near the pylorus","Gastric body and fundus","Cardia near the esophagus","First part of the duodenum"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","obj":"GI-2.3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the acid-secreting cell: the parietal cell.","Recall where parietal cells live: the oxyntic glands of the gastric body and fundus.","Remember that gastrin is trophic to this mucosa, so chronic hypergastrinemia thickens its folds."],"why":"Parietal cells, which secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, are concentrated in the oxyntic glands of the gastric body and fundus, along with chief cells that secrete pepsinogen. The antrum holds G cells that make gastrin, and the cardia holds mainly mucous glands. Constant high gastrin from a gastrinoma makes parietal cells and ECL cells multiply, so the body and fundus mucosa thickens into prominent folds.","whyNot":["The antral glands contain G cells and mucous cells and have few parietal cells. Gastrin from the antrum acts on the body and fundus.","Correct.","The cardiac glands near the gastroesophageal junction are mostly mucus secreting and contain few parietal cells.","The duodenum has no parietal cells. Its Brunner glands secrete alkaline mucus."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types","Regions of the stomach"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C04","system":"GI","dx":"Celiac disease with iron deficiency anemia and coexisting primary biliary cholangitis","codes":["GI-13-E2","GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman comes to the physician because of an 8-month history of bloating, loose bulky stools, fatigue, and a 5-kg weight loss, and a 1-year history of generalized itching. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), MCV 71 fL (80 to 100), and ferritin 5 ng/mL (15 to 150). Tissue transglutaminase IgA is strongly positive. Duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes. Alkaline phosphatase is 390 U/L (40 to 120) with normal bilirubin, antimitochondrial antibody (AMA) is positive, and ultrasound shows no bile duct dilation. Diagnosis: celiac disease with iron deficiency anemia and coexisting primary biliary cholangitis (PBC), an autoimmune liver disease that is more common in people with celiac disease.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q1","from":[41],"stem":"Because of where celiac disease damages the bowel, which of the following is she most likely to malabsorb early in her disease?","options":["Fat and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K","Vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor","Short-chain fatty acids from colonic fermentation","Conjugated bile acids returning to the liver"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E2","obj":"GI-13-E.2","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the injury: celiac disease is worst in the duodenum and proximal jejunum.","Recall what those segments absorb: most dietary fat and fat-soluble vitamins, plus iron, calcium, and folate.","Exclude options absorbed in the terminal ileum or colon."],"why":"Gluten-driven immune injury flattens the villi most severely in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, where most dietary fat and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K are absorbed. The loss of surface area causes bulky, fatty stools and deficiencies such as low vitamin D with bone loss and low vitamin K with easy bruising. Her PBC can add to fat malabsorption by reducing bile flow late in its course. The terminal ileum and colon are usually spared early in celiac disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","The vitamin B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed in the terminal ileum, which celiac disease usually spares early on. B12 deficiency suggests ileal disease, as in Crohn disease.","Short-chain fatty acids are made and absorbed in the colon, which celiac disease does not damage.","Conjugated bile acids are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. Their loss follows ileal disease or resection."],"study":{"topics":["Celiac disease malabsorption","Fat-soluble vitamins"],"comps":["GI-13-E2","GI-6","GI-8"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q2","from":[40],"stem":"Her iron deficiency persists even though her diet contains enough iron. Which of the following best explains why celiac disease often causes iron deficiency?","options":["Iron is absorbed mostly in the terminal ileum, where gluten acts","Iron absorption needs intrinsic factor made by parietal cells","Iron is absorbed mostly in the duodenum, where damage is worst","Iron absorption needs bile acid micelles to cross the mucosa"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the absorption site for iron (and calcium): the duodenum and proximal jejunum.","Recall the site of maximal celiac injury: the same proximal segment.","Connect them: proximal villous atrophy causes iron deficiency that does not respond well to oral iron."],"why":"Dietary nonheme iron is reduced from Fe3+ to Fe2+ at the brush border and enters duodenal enterocytes through DMT1, then leaves through ferroportin. Active, calcitriol-driven calcium absorption also happens mainly in the duodenum. Because celiac disease damages the duodenum most, iron deficiency anemia is one of its most common presentations, sometimes without diarrhea. Unexplained iron deficiency that does not respond to oral iron is a classic reason to test for celiac disease.","whyNot":["The terminal ileum absorbs vitamin B12 and bile acids, not most of the iron. Celiac damage is also worst proximally.","Intrinsic factor is needed for vitamin B12 absorption, not iron absorption.","Correct.","Bile acid micelles carry fats and fat-soluble vitamins. Iron is taken up by a membrane transporter without micelles."],"study":{"topics":["Iron absorption","Nutrient absorption sites"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-8","GI-13-E2"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q3","from":[47],"stem":"Her liver adjusts iron absorption to her depleted iron stores by changing its output of a peptide hormone. Which of the following changes occurs, and what is its effect?","options":["Hepcidin rises, so ferroportin is degraded and absorption falls","Hepcidin falls, so more ferroportin stays in cell membranes","Transferrin falls, so less iron is carried away from enterocytes","Erythropoietin falls, so the marrow's demand for iron decreases"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","obj":"GI-8.6","dok":3,"approach":["Name the liver hormone that controls iron: hepcidin.","Recall its action: it binds ferroportin and causes its internalization and degradation.","Apply to low stores: hepcidin falls, ferroportin stays, and enterocytes and macrophages release more iron into blood."],"why":"Hepcidin, made by hepatocytes, is the main regulator of body iron. It binds ferroportin, the only known iron exporter, on enterocytes and macrophages and causes it to be degraded, which traps iron inside those cells. When iron stores are low, hepcidin falls, ferroportin stays in the membrane, and absorption rises. In hereditary hemochromatosis, hepcidin is inappropriately low despite iron overload, so absorption continues unchecked.","whyNot":["Hepcidin rises when iron stores are full or with inflammation, which lowers iron absorption. With a ferritin of 5 ng/mL, her stores are empty, so hepcidin is suppressed.","Correct.","Transferrin is a carrier protein, not a regulating hormone, and its level rises in iron deficiency (high TIBC).","Erythropoietin is made mainly by the kidney, not the liver, and anemia raises it."],"study":{"topics":["Hepcidin and ferroportin","Iron homeostasis"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q4","from":[60],"stem":"Her itching and high alkaline phosphatase come from autoimmune injury in the liver. Which of the following cells are the main target of this injury?","options":["Kupffer cells lining the hepatic sinusoids","Stellate cells in the space of Disse","Hepatocytes around the central veins","Small intrahepatic bile duct cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C1","obj":"GI-13-C.2","dok":2,"approach":["Read the pattern: itching and high alkaline phosphatase with normal duct imaging is intrahepatic cholestasis.","Add the AMA: this marks primary biliary cholangitis.","Name the target: epithelial cells (cholangiocytes) of small intrahepatic bile ducts."],"why":"Primary biliary cholangitis is an autoimmune disease in which T cells destroy the epithelial cells (cholangiocytes) of small intrahepatic bile ducts. Bile backs up, causing itching, high alkaline phosphatase, and later jaundice, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, and cirrhosis. Antimitochondrial antibodies against the E2 subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex are found in more than 90% of patients. It mainly affects middle-aged women and often occurs with other autoimmune diseases.","whyNot":["Kupffer cells are resident macrophages. They take part in liver inflammation but are not the target of PBC.","Stellate cells store vitamin A and become collagen-producing myofibroblasts in fibrosis. They cause scarring but are not the target of the autoimmune attack.","Hepatocytes are the main target in autoimmune and viral hepatitis, which raise ALT and AST far more than alkaline phosphatase.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Primary biliary cholangitis","Cholestasis patterns"],"comps":["GI-13-C1","GI-11"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C05","system":"GI","dx":"Crohn disease of the terminal ileum and colon","codes":["GI-13-G9","GI-13-K7"],"case":"A 54-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of right lower quadrant cramping, nonbloody diarrhea, and an 8-kg weight loss. Her temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F). Physical examination shows a tender mass in the right lower quadrant. Colonoscopy shows deep linear ulcers and a cobblestone pattern in the terminal ileum and in patches of the ascending colon, with normal mucosa between them and a normal rectum. A 1.4-cm tubular adenoma is removed from her sigmoid colon, which is not inflamed. Ileal biopsies show transmural inflammation with non-caseating granulomas. Diagnosis: Crohn disease of the terminal ileum and colon.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q1","from":[53],"stem":"Which of the following best describes the main cells in her ileal granulomas and the cytokine that drives their formation?","options":["Plasma cells and B cells, activated by IL-4 from Th2 cells","Neutrophils and mast cells, activated by IL-5 from Th2 cells","Epithelioid macrophages, driven by IFN-gamma from Th1 cells","Eosinophils and NK cells, activated by IL-17 from Th17 cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","obj":"GI-11.3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize a granuloma: a collection of activated macrophages.","Recall the cell types: epithelioid macrophages, often with multinucleated giant cells, ringed by CD4 T cells.","Recall the signal: Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages (type IV hypersensitivity)."],"why":"Granulomas form through a type IV, cell-mediated response. Macrophages present antigen and release IL-12, which drives CD4 T cells toward the Th1 type. Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which turns macrophages into epithelioid cells that can fuse into multinucleated giant cells, surrounded by a cuff of lymphocytes. In Crohn disease the granulomas are non-caseating, which helps separate it from tuberculosis.","whyNot":["Plasma cells and B cells make antibodies, and IL-4 drives class switching to IgE. Antibody responses do not build granulomas.","Neutrophils form abscesses and mast cells drive immediate hypersensitivity. IL-5 recruits and activates eosinophils, and none of these form granulomas.","Correct.","Eosinophils and NK cells are not the core of granulomas. IL-17 recruits neutrophils and contributes to Crohn inflammation, but the granuloma itself is organized by Th1 cells."],"study":{"topics":["Granuloma formation","Type IV hypersensitivity"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q2","from":[55],"stem":"Six months later she has pneumaturia and recurrent urinary tract infections with enteric organisms. Which of the following features of her disease, in contrast to ulcerative colitis, best explains this complication?","options":["Continuous mucosal inflammation spreading up from the rectum","Crypt abscesses confined to the mucosa and submucosa","Loss of haustra that leaves a short, tube-shaped colon","Transmural inflammation that tunnels into nearby organs"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G9","obj":"GI-13-G.10","dok":3,"approach":["Read the new clue: pneumaturia with enteric organisms means a fistula from bowel to bladder.","Ask what lets inflammation reach the bladder: inflammation through the full thickness of the bowel wall.","Match to the disease: transmural, patchy (skip) inflammation is Crohn disease; mucosal, continuous inflammation from the rectum is ulcerative colitis."],"why":"Crohn disease inflames the full thickness of the bowel wall in patches (skip lesions) anywhere from mouth to anus, most often the terminal ileum. Deep fissuring ulcers can tunnel through the wall and form fistulas to bowel, bladder, vagina, or skin, and they can also cause abscesses and strictures. Ulcerative colitis involves only the mucosa and submucosa, starting at the rectum and spreading continuously, so fistulas are not a feature of it.","whyNot":["Continuous inflammation spreading upward from the rectum is the pattern of ulcerative colitis. Her rectum is normal and her disease is patchy.","Inflammation limited to the mucosa and submucosa is typical of ulcerative colitis, and it does not reach other organs.","A shortened colon without haustra (lead-pipe colon) is a late feature of ulcerative colitis and does not create tracts to the bladder.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","Fistulas in Crohn disease"],"comps":["GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q3","from":[35],"stem":"Two years later, about 80 cm of her terminal ileum is removed because of a stricture. For which of the following vitamins will she most likely need lifelong monitoring and replacement?","options":["Vitamin B12, taken up with intrinsic factor by ileal receptors","Vitamin C, taken up only after binding gastric intrinsic factor","Folate, taken up mainly by the terminal ileum after digestion","Thiamine, taken up mainly by the terminal ileum with bile acids"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-7","obj":"GI-7.4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify what was removed: the terminal ileum.","Recall what only the terminal ileum absorbs: the vitamin B12 and intrinsic factor complex (and bile acids).","Predict the result: B12 deficiency and megaloblastic anemia once liver stores run out over years."],"why":"Parietal cells secrete intrinsic factor, which binds vitamin B12 in the duodenum after pancreatic proteases free it from haptocorrin. The B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed only in the terminal ileum through cubilin receptors. Removing a long stretch of terminal ileum, or severe ileal Crohn disease, therefore causes B12 malabsorption. Liver stores last years, so deficiency appears late, which is why monitoring and replacement are lifelong.","whyNot":["Correct.","Vitamin C does not bind intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor is specific for vitamin B12.","Folate is absorbed mainly in the duodenum and jejunum, so it is not lost with ileal resection.","Thiamine is absorbed mainly in the jejunum and does not depend on bile acids."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin B12 absorption","Ileal resection effects"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-8"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q4","from":[59],"stem":"The sigmoid tubular adenoma arose in a segment free of colitis, so it follows the usual sporadic pathway. Which of the following sequences of genetic changes drives that pathway from normal mucosa to carcinoma?","options":["TP53 loss early, with APC loss only late in the process","APC loss, then KRAS activation, then TP53 loss","MLH1 or MSH2 loss, causing microsatellite instability","BRCA2 loss, causing failed homologous recombination repair"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","obj":"GI-10.4","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion: a tubular adenoma in uninflamed colon follows the adenoma-carcinoma sequence.","Recall the order: APC loss starts the adenoma, KRAS activation enlarges it, and TP53 loss allows invasive cancer.","Separate it from the mismatch repair pathway and from colitis-associated cancer."],"why":"Most sporadic colorectal cancers follow the chromosomal instability pathway. Loss of both APC alleles turns on Wnt signaling and starts the adenoma, activating KRAS mutations let it grow, and loss of SMAD4 and TP53 allows the shift to invasive carcinoma. Adenomas take years to progress, which is why removing them prevents cancer. Cancers arising in colitis, as can happen in long-standing Crohn colitis, tend to lose TP53 early instead.","whyNot":["Early TP53 loss with late APC loss is the order seen in colitis-associated cancer, which arises in chronically inflamed mucosa without a preceding adenoma. Her adenoma arose outside the colitis.","Correct.","Loss of mismatch repair genes causes microsatellite instability. This happens in Lynch syndrome and in sporadic cancers with MLH1 promoter silencing, together about 15% of colorectal cancers, so it is not the usual pathway.","BRCA2 loss is linked to breast, ovarian, prostate, and pancreatic cancers. It is not a step in the colorectal adenoma sequence."],"study":{"topics":["Adenoma-carcinoma sequence","Colorectal cancer pathways"],"comps":["GI-13-K7","GI-10"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C06","system":"GI","dx":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome from enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli O157:H7","codes":["GI-13-L1","GI-13-L3"],"case":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of pallor and decreased urine output. Six days ago he developed abdominal cramps and watery diarrhea that became bloody; the illness began 4 days after he ate an undercooked hamburger at a barbecue. He has had no fever. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.8 g/dL (11.5 to 15.5), platelet count 42,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), creatinine 2.6 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7), and a high LDH. The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is negative, and the blood smear shows schistocytes. Stool testing detects Shiga toxin, and culture grows Escherichia coli O157:H7. Diagnosis: hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) caused by enterohemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC).","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q1","from":[52],"stem":"The toxin in his stool binds the glycolipid Gb3 on endothelial cells of the colon and glomeruli and enters them. Which of the following is the action of its A subunit inside the cell?","options":["ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 in the cytosol","Links MHC class II to T-cell receptors outside the groove","Cleaves synaptobrevin and blocks vesicle release","Removes one adenine from 28S rRNA in the 60S subunit"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the toxin: EHEC O157:H7 makes Shiga toxin (Shiga-like toxin, verotoxin).","Recall its enzyme action: the A subunit is an N-glycosidase that removes a single adenine from 28S rRNA of the 60S ribosomal subunit.","Connect to disease: protein synthesis stops, endothelial cells die, and microthrombi form in glomeruli."],"why":"Shiga toxin is an AB5 toxin. Its B pentamer binds Gb3 (globotriaosylceramide), which is plentiful on glomerular and colonic endothelial cells. Inside the cell, the A subunit acts as an N-glycosidase that removes one specific adenine from the 28S rRNA of the 60S ribosomal subunit, so aminoacyl-tRNA can no longer bind and protein synthesis stops. Endothelial injury exposes a surface that triggers platelet-fibrin microthrombi, producing HUS.","whyNot":["ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 is how diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A stop protein synthesis.","Linking MHC class II to T-cell receptors outside the peptide groove is the action of superantigens such as TSST-1, which cause toxic shock.","Cleaving synaptobrevin is the action of tetanus toxin and some botulinum toxins, which block neurotransmitter release.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Shiga toxin mechanism","EHEC and HUS"],"comps":["GI-13-L1","GI-12"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q2","from":[54],"stem":"While his culture was pending, the health department asked whether the family keeps backyard poultry or pet reptiles. That question screens for a different cause of bloody diarrhea. Which of the following identifies that organism and explains why the question fits?","options":["Shigella, which lives only in humans and spreads person to person","Vibrio cholerae, which lives in poultry and causes bloody stools","Salmonella, which has animal hosts and can enter the blood","Shigella, which has animal hosts and spreads mainly through eggs"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L3","obj":"GI-13-L.2","dok":3,"approach":["Spot the exposure clue: poultry, eggs, and reptiles are animal reservoirs.","Recall which enteric pathogen has those reservoirs: nontyphoidal Salmonella.","Contrast with Shigella: humans are the only reservoir, a very small dose causes infection, and spread is person to person."],"why":"Nontyphoidal Salmonella lives in animals, especially poultry, eggs, and reptiles, so public health teams ask about these exposures. It invades the intestinal mucosa and can enter the bloodstream, particularly in infants, older adults, and people with sickle cell disease or weak immunity. Shigella has no animal reservoir, spreads person to person with a very small infectious dose, and usually stays in the colon. Both are gram-negative rods that do not ferment lactose.","whyNot":["This statement about Shigella is true, but it explains why Shigella spreads in daycares and households. It does not explain a question about poultry and reptiles.","Vibrio cholerae lives in brackish and salt water and in shellfish, and it causes watery, not bloody, diarrhea.","Correct.","Shigella has no animal reservoir, so poultry and eggs are not its usual source."],"study":{"topics":["Salmonella versus Shigella","Reservoirs of enteric pathogens"],"comps":["GI-13-L3","GI-12"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q3","from":[42],"stem":"Shiga toxin and cholera toxin are both AB5 toxins whose B pentamer binds a cell-surface glycolipid. Cholera toxin, however, causes profuse rice-water diarrhea without blood. Which of the following is the mechanism of its A subunit?","options":["ADP-ribosylates Gs alpha, so cAMP rises and CFTR secretes chloride","Activates guanylyl cyclase, so cGMP rises and NaCl absorption falls","ADP-ribosylates Gi alpha, so adenylyl cyclase loses its inhibition","Forms pores in enterocyte membranes, so the cells lyse"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the cholera toxin target: the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein (Gs).","Follow the signal: ADP-ribosylation blocks GTP hydrolysis, so adenylyl cyclase stays on and cAMP rises.","Follow the effect: protein kinase A opens CFTR, chloride and water pour into the lumen, and the cells survive, so the stool is watery without blood."],"why":"Cholera toxin's B pentamer binds the ganglioside GM1 on enterocytes, and its A1 fragment ADP-ribosylates the Gs alpha subunit. This blocks GTP hydrolysis, so Gs stays active and adenylyl cyclase keeps making cAMP. Protein kinase A then opens CFTR chloride channels and reduces NaCl absorption, and water follows into the lumen. The enterocytes stay alive and intact, so the diarrhea is watery and noninflammatory, unlike the bloody diarrhea of EHEC.","whyNot":["Correct.","Raising cGMP through guanylyl cyclase is the action of the heat-stable toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli, not cholera toxin.","ADP-ribosylation of Gi alpha is the action of pertussis toxin. It raises cAMP in airway cells and lymphocytes, not by locking Gs on.","Forming pores in enterocyte membranes is the action of Clostridium perfringens enterotoxin, which damages the cells. Cholera toxin leaves enterocytes alive and intact."],"study":{"topics":["Cholera toxin mechanism","Secretory diarrhea"],"comps":["GI-13-L3","GI-12","GI-6"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q4","from":[],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his anemia?","options":["IgG coats red cells, which splenic macrophages then remove","Red cells are sliced by microthrombi in small vessels","Toxin in the blood suppresses red cell production in marrow","Complement lyses red cells that lack CD55 and CD59"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-L1","obj":"GI-13-L.2","dok":2,"approach":["Read the clues: schistocytes, high LDH, low platelets, negative Coombs test.","Name the pattern: microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.","Link to the toxin: endothelial injury triggers platelet-fibrin microthrombi that shear red cells, mainly in glomeruli."],"why":"Shiga toxin injures endothelial cells, especially in the glomeruli. Platelets stick to the damaged surface and form platelet-fibrin microthrombi, which use up platelets and narrow small vessels. Red cells are sliced as they squeeze past these thrombi, producing schistocytes, high LDH, and anemia, while blocked glomerular capillaries cause acute kidney injury. This triad of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury defines HUS.","whyNot":["IgG-coated red cells cause warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, which gives a positive direct Coombs test and spherocytes. His Coombs test is negative.","Correct.","Marrow suppression lowers red cell production without fragmented cells or a high LDH. His schistocytes and high LDH point to destruction in the circulation.","Red cells lacking CD55 and CD59 are found in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, an acquired stem cell disorder unrelated to EHEC."],"study":{"topics":["Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia","HUS pathogenesis"],"comps":["GI-13-L1"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C07","system":"GI","dx":"Acute hepatitis A in a man with Gilbert syndrome","codes":["GI-13-L7","GI-13-C3"],"case":"A 23-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 5-day history of fever, nausea, right upper quadrant pain, and dark urine. Three weeks ago he ate raw oysters on a trip, and two friends from the trip are also ill. His temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F). Physical examination shows jaundice and a tender liver edge. Laboratory studies show ALT 2,150 U/L (7 to 56), total bilirubin 6.8 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2) with direct bilirubin 4.1 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), and INR 1.1 (0.8 to 1.2). IgM anti-HAV is positive. HBsAg and anti-HBc are negative, and anti-HBs is positive. His records show three episodes of mild jaundice since age 19 during fasting or illness, each with unconjugated bilirubin of 2.4 to 3.0 mg/dL, normal ALT, and normal hemoglobin and reticulocyte counts. Diagnosis: acute hepatitis A.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q1","from":[49],"stem":"Which of the following is the most likely course of his hepatitis A infection?","options":["Full recovery with lasting immunity and no carrier state","Chronic infection in most patients, with slow scarring over decades","Chronic infection in about 5% of adults, with later cirrhosis","Latent virus in hepatocytes that reactivates during later stress"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L7","obj":"GI-13-L.9","dok":2,"approach":["Use the clues: raw shellfish and a cluster of ill travelers point to fecal-oral spread, and IgM anti-HAV confirms acute hepatitis A.","Recall the natural history of HAV: acute, self-limited illness.","Exclude options describing chronic hepatitis B or C or latent viruses."],"why":"Hepatitis A is a nonenveloped single-stranded RNA picornavirus spread by the fecal-oral route through contaminated food, water, and shellfish. It causes an acute illness that resolves in nearly all patients, with no chronic infection and no carrier state, and recovery gives lifelong immunity (IgG anti-HAV). Rare patients develop fulminant liver failure, mostly older adults or people with existing liver disease. Hepatitis E is also spread fecal-orally but can become chronic in people with weak immunity.","whyNot":["Correct.","Chronic infection in most patients describes hepatitis C, which spreads mainly through blood.","Chronic infection in about 5% of adults infected in adulthood describes hepatitis B, which spreads through blood and sexual contact.","Hepatitis A, an RNA virus, does not establish latency. Latency and reactivation are features of herpesviruses."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis A natural history","Comparison of hepatitis viruses"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-12"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q2","from":[45],"stem":"Which of the following defects best explains the jaundice episodes recorded since age 19?","options":["Short red cell survival from a membrane skeleton defect","Promoter variant lowering UGT1A1 activity to about 30%","Defective MRP2 transport of conjugated bilirubin into bile","Complete absence of UGT1A1 activity from a coding mutation"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C3","obj":"GI-13-C.6","dok":3,"approach":["Characterize the episodes: mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia triggered by fasting or illness, with normal ALT.","Exclude hemolysis: normal hemoglobin and reticulocytes.","Name the condition and defect: Gilbert syndrome, reduced (not absent) UGT1A1 activity, most often from an extra TA repeat in the promoter."],"why":"Gilbert syndrome is the most common inherited cause of hyperbilirubinemia. In most people of European descent it comes from an extra TA repeat in the UGT1A1 promoter, which lowers expression of bilirubin UDP-glucuronosyltransferase to about 30% of normal. Bilirubin stays normal or mildly high and rises with fasting, illness, stress, or exertion. Liver enzymes and liver structure are normal, and no treatment is needed.","whyNot":["A red cell membrane defect such as hereditary spherocytosis causes hemolysis with anemia and a high reticulocyte count. His hemoglobin and reticulocytes were normal.","Correct.","An MRP2 defect is Dubin-Johnson syndrome, which raises conjugated bilirubin and darkens the liver. His past episodes were unconjugated.","Complete absence of UGT1A1 activity is Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I, which causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and kernicterus in the newborn period."],"study":{"topics":["Gilbert syndrome","Inherited hyperbilirubinemias"],"comps":["GI-13-C3","GI-9","GI-10"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q3","from":[44],"stem":"During his earlier episodes his urine stayed normal in color, but now it is dark with bilirubin. Which of the following changes allows bilirubin to appear in his urine now?","options":["Albumin binding increased in plasma, so it passes the glomerulus","Glycine was attached in hepatocytes, so it became lipid soluble","Glucuronic acid was added in hepatocytes, so it dissolves in water","Sulfate was attached by gut bacteria, so it is reabsorbed from bowel"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","obj":"GI-9.2","dok":3,"approach":["Compare the episodes: before, unconjugated bilirubin; now, a large conjugated (direct) fraction.","Recall the change: UGT1A1 attaches glucuronic acid, making bilirubin water soluble.","Apply to the kidney: conjugated bilirubin that leaks back into blood from injured hepatocytes is filtered and appears in urine."],"why":"Unconjugated bilirubin is lipid soluble and tightly bound to albumin, so it is not filtered and never appears in urine, which is why his Gilbert episodes left his urine normal. In the hepatocyte, UDP-glucuronosyltransferase attaches glucuronic acid, making conjugated (direct) bilirubin that is water soluble and normally secreted into bile. Hepatitis injures hepatocytes and bile canaliculi, so conjugated bilirubin leaks back into blood, is filtered by the kidney, and darkens the urine.","whyNot":["Albumin binding keeps unconjugated bilirubin out of the urine, because the albumin complex is too large to pass the glomerular barrier.","Glycine and taurine are attached to bile acids, not bilirubin, and conjugation makes molecules more water soluble, not lipid soluble.","Correct.","Gut bacteria turn conjugated bilirubin into urobilinogen; they do not sulfate it. Urobilinogen reabsorbed from the gut is a small, normal urine component that does not make urine dark with bilirubin."],"study":{"topics":["Bilirubin conjugation","Bilirubinuria"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C3"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q4","from":[50],"stem":"He asks whether he could also acquire hepatitis D. Based on his serologic results, which of the following best describes his risk?","options":["At risk, because HDV replicates on its own once inside hepatocytes","Protected, because HDV needs HBsAg made by HBV for its envelope","Protected, because HDV spreads only by the fecal-oral route","At risk, because antibodies against HBsAg do not neutralize HDV"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","obj":"GI-12.5","dok":3,"approach":["Read the serology: anti-HBs positive with HBsAg and anti-HBc negative means vaccinated against HBV.","Recall what HDV needs: HBsAg from an active HBV infection to build its envelope.","Conclude: no HBV infection, so no HDV infection."],"why":"Hepatitis D virus is a small, defective RNA virus. It can replicate its genome inside hepatocytes, but it uses HBsAg made by hepatitis B virus as its envelope, so it can only infect people who also have HBV. It can arrive at the same time as HBV (coinfection, which usually clears) or infect someone with chronic HBV (superinfection, which causes the most severe and rapidly progressive hepatitis). Because he is immune to HBV from vaccination, he is protected from HDV as well.","whyNot":["HDV can copy its own RNA genome using host RNA polymerase, but it cannot make an envelope or leave the cell without HBsAg from HBV. Without HBV it cannot spread.","Correct.","HDV spreads like HBV, through blood, sexual contact, and from mother to child. The fecal-oral route belongs to hepatitis A and E.","The HDV envelope is made of HBsAg, so anti-HBs neutralizes HDV particles. HBV vaccination therefore also prevents HDV infection."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis D","Hepatitis B serology"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-12"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C08","system":"GI","dx":"Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon)","codes":["GI-13-F5","NE-20-E3"],"case":"A 2-day-old boy born at term is evaluated because he has not passed meconium. He now has abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. His mother has phenylketonuria (PKU) and kept a phenylalanine-restricted diet before and during the pregnancy; his father has no family history of PKU. Rectal examination shows a tight rectum, and withdrawal of the finger releases an explosive gush of stool and gas. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectum and sigmoid colon below a cone-shaped transition zone and a dilated colon above it. Suction rectal biopsy shows no ganglion cells in the submucosal plexus and thickened nerve fibers. Diagnosis: Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon).","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q1","from":[36],"stem":"Which of the following developmental failures best explains the missing ganglion cells?","options":["Splanchnic mesoderm did not form the muscularis externa","Endoderm did not recanalize the gut lumen after its solid stage","Neural crest cells did not migrate all the way to the distal colon","The midgut loop did not rotate around the superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-13-F5","obj":"GI-13-F.5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify what is missing: ganglion cells of the enteric nervous system.","Recall their origin: neural crest cells that enter the foregut early in development and migrate in a head-to-tail wave toward the end of the hindgut.","Apply: migration that stops early leaves the most distal segment (rectum and sigmoid) without ganglia."],"why":"The enteric nervous system comes from neural crest cells, mainly from the vagal region, that enter the foregut and migrate down the gut toward the anus. In Hirschsprung disease this migration stops short, so a continuous distal segment starting at the rectum has no ganglion cells in the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. That segment cannot relax, so it acts as a functional obstruction, and the normal bowel above it dilates. Risk is higher in boys and in Down syndrome.","whyNot":["Splanchnic mesoderm forms the gut muscle and connective tissue. A muscle defect does not explain absent ganglion cells.","Failed recanalization of the gut lumen, best known in the duodenum, causes stenosis or atresia. That is a structural blockage, and ganglion cells are normal.","Correct.","Failed midgut rotation causes malrotation with a risk of volvulus. It does not affect ganglion cells in the rectum."],"study":{"topics":["Enteric nervous system development","Hirschsprung disease"],"comps":["GI-13-F5","GI-1"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q2","from":[],"stem":"The aganglionic segment stays tightly contracted. Loss of which of the following neurons best explains why it cannot relax?","options":["Sympathetic fibers that release norepinephrine onto the bowel","Excitatory motor neurons that release acetylcholine and substance P","Vagal preganglionic fibers that release acetylcholine onto ganglia","Inhibitory motor neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","obj":"GI-6.2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gut smooth muscle relaxes in front of a bolus by an enteric reflex.","Identify the neurons that make it relax: intrinsic inhibitory motor neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP.","Apply: with no ganglion cells, these neurons are absent, so the segment cannot relax and stays narrow."],"why":"Peristalsis needs the muscle ahead of a bolus to relax while the muscle behind it contracts. Relaxation comes from inhibitory motor neurons in the myenteric plexus that release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP). Without ganglion cells, these neurons are missing, so the segment cannot relax, and thick extrinsic cholinergic nerve fibers in the area add to its tone. The internal anal sphincter also fails to relax when the rectum fills.","whyNot":["Sympathetic fibers come from outside the gut and are still present in the aganglionic segment, so their loss does not explain the problem.","Losing excitatory motor neurons would reduce contraction and cause a flaccid segment, not a tightly contracted one.","Vagal fibers do not supply the rectum or sigmoid colon; pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic input there.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Enteric motor neurons","Peristaltic reflex"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q3","from":[],"stem":"Genetic testing finds a heterozygous variant in the gene most often mutated in this disease. Which of the following variants is most likely?","options":["Loss-of-function variant in RET","Gain-of-function variant in RET","Loss-of-function variant in APC","Gain-of-function variant in KRAS"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-1","obj":"GI-1.5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the receptor that guides enteric neural crest cells: the RET receptor tyrosine kinase, activated by GDNF.","Ask which direction of change blocks migration: loss of function.","Separate it from the gain-of-function RET variants of MEN2."],"why":"Enteric neural crest cells need signaling through the RET receptor tyrosine kinase, activated by glial cell line-derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF), to survive, multiply, and migrate along the gut. Loss-of-function RET variants are the most common genetic finding in Hirschsprung disease, especially in familial and long-segment cases, and penetrance is incomplete. Gain-of-function RET variants have the opposite effect and cause MEN2.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gain-of-function RET variants cause multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 and medullary thyroid cancer. A few MEN2A variants occur with Hirschsprung disease, but the usual cause is loss of RET signaling, which impairs neural crest migration into the gut.","Loss-of-function APC variants cause familial adenomatous polyposis and start the colorectal adenoma sequence.","Gain-of-function KRAS variants drive growth in many cancers, including colorectal and pancreatic cancer, and are not a cause of Hirschsprung disease."],"study":{"topics":["RET and Hirschsprung disease","Loss versus gain of function"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q4","from":[48],"stem":"His newborn screen shows a normal phenylalanine level. Which of the following best describes him with respect to his mother's disorder?","options":["Obligate carrier of one tyrosine hydroxylase variant; PKU is X-linked","Obligate carrier of one homogentisate oxidase variant; PKU is autosomal recessive","50% chance of carrying one PAH variant; PKU is autosomal recessive","Obligate carrier of one PAH variant; PKU is autosomal recessive"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-E3","obj":"NE-20-E.3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the defect: PKU is deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), which converts phenylalanine to tyrosine.","Recall the inheritance: autosomal recessive, so his affected mother has two PAH variants.","Apply: he must inherit one maternal variant, and his normal screen means he is not affected, so he is a carrier."],"why":"Classic PKU is an autosomal recessive deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), which needs tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) to convert phenylalanine to tyrosine. An affected mother passes one PAH variant to every child, so this baby is an obligate carrier, and his normal screen shows he is not affected. Untreated PKU causes intellectual disability, seizures, light skin and hair, and a musty odor, and tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid. Keeping maternal phenylalanine low before and during pregnancy prevents maternal PKU embryopathy (microcephaly, heart defects, growth restriction).","whyNot":["Tyrosine hydroxylase makes L-DOPA from tyrosine and is not the PKU enzyme, and PKU is autosomal, not X-linked.","Homogentisate oxidase deficiency causes alkaptonuria, not PKU.","His mother has two PAH variants, so every child inherits one of them. His chance of carrying one is 100%, not 50%.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Phenylketonuria","Autosomal recessive inheritance"],"comps":["NE-20-E3","GI-8"]}}]}];
